if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-2860"]="# CHAPTER I. “OMNES OMNIA BONA DICERE.”\n\nWhen young Mark Robarts was leaving college, his father might well declare that all men began to say all good things to him, and to extol his fortune in that he had a son blessed with so excellent a disposition.\n\nThis father was a physician living at Exeter. He was a gentleman possessed of no private means, but enjoying a lucrative practice, which had enabled him to maintain and educate a family with all the advantages which money can give in this country. Mark was his eldest son and second child; and the first page or two of this narrative must be consumed in giving a catalogue of the good things which chance and conduct together had heaped upon this young man’s head.\n\nHis first step forward in life had arisen from his having been sent, while still very young, as a private pupil to the house of a clergyman, who was an old friend and intimate friend of his father’s. This clergyman had one other, and only one other, pupil—the young Lord Lufton; and between the two boys, there had sprung up a close alliance.\n\nWhile they were both so placed, Lady Lufton had visited her son, and then invited young Robarts to pass his next holidays at Framley Court. This visit was made; and it ended in Mark going back to Exeter with a letter full of praise from the widowed peeress. She had been delighted, she said, in having such a companion for her son, and expressed a hope that the boys might remain together during the course of their education. Dr. Robarts was a man who thought much of the breath of peers and peeresses, and was by no means inclined to throw away any advantage which might arise to his child from such a friendship. When, therefore, the young lord was sent to Harrow, Mark Robarts went there also.\n\nThat the lord and his friend often quarrelled, and occasionally fought,—the fact even that for one period of three months they never spoke to each other—by no means interfered with the doctor’s hopes. Mark again and again stayed a fortnight at Framley Court, and Lady Lufton always wrote about him in the highest terms.\n\nAnd then the lads went together to Oxford, and here Mark’s good fortune followed him, consisting rather in the highly respectable manner in which he lived, than in any wonderful career of collegiate success. His family was proud of him, and the doctor was always ready to talk of him to his patients; not because he was a prizeman, and had gotten medals and scholarships, but on account of the excellence of his general conduct. He lived with the best set—he incurred no debts—he was fond of society, but able to avoid low society—liked his glass of wine, but was never known to be drunk; and, above all things, was one of the most popular men in the university.\n\nThen came the question of a profession for this young Hyperion, and on this subject, Dr. Robarts was invited himself to go over to Framley Court to discuss the matter with Lady Lufton. Dr. Robarts returned with a very strong conception that the Church was the profession best suited to his son.\n\nLady Lufton had not sent for Dr. Robarts all the way from Exeter for nothing. The living of Framley was in the gift of the Lufton family, and the next presentation would be in Lady Lufton’s hands, if it should fall vacant before the young lord was twenty-five years of age, and in the young lord’s hands if it should fall afterwards. But the mother and the heir consented to give a joint promise to Dr. Robarts. Now, as the present incumbent was over seventy, and as the living was worth £900 a year, there could be no doubt as to the eligibility of the clerical profession.\n\nAnd I must further say, that the dowager and the doctor were justified in their choice by the life and principles of the young man—as far as any father can be justified in choosing such a profession for his son, and as far as any lay impropriator can be justified in making such a promise. Had Lady Lufton had a second son, that second son would probably have had the living, and no one would have thought it wrong;—certainly not if that second son had been such a one as Mark Robarts.\n\nLady Lufton herself was a woman who thought much on religious matters, and would by no means have been disposed to place any one in a living, merely because such a one had been her son’s friend. Her tendencies were High Church, and she was enabled to perceive that those of young Mark Robarts ran in the same direction. She was very desirous that her son should make an associate of his clergyman, and by this step she would insure, at any rate, that. She was anxious that the parish vicar should be one with whom she could herself fully co-operate, and was perhaps unconsciously wishful that he might in some measure be subject to her influence. Should she appoint an elder man, this might probably not be the case to the same extent; and should her son have the gift, it might probably not be the case at all. And therefore it was resolved that the living should be given to young Robarts.\n\nHe took his degree—not with any brilliancy, but quite in the manner that his father desired; he then travelled for eight or ten months with Lord Lufton and a college don, and almost immediately after his return home was ordained.\n\nThe living of Framley is in the diocese of Barchester; and, seeing what were Mark’s hopes with reference to that diocese, it was by no means difficult to get him a curacy within it. But this curacy he was not allowed long to fill. He had not been in it above a twelvemonth, when poor old Dr. Stopford, the then vicar of Framley, was gathered to his fathers, and the full fruition of his rich hopes fell upon his shoulders.\n\nBut even yet more must be told of his good fortune before we can come to the actual incidents of our story. Lady Lufton, who, as I have said, thought much of clerical matters, did not carry her High Church principles so far as to advocate celibacy for the clergy. On the contrary, she had an idea that a man could not be a good parish parson without a wife. So, having given to her favourite a position in the world, and an income sufficient for a gentleman’s wants, she set herself to work to find him a partner in those blessings.\n\nAnd here also, as in other matters, he fell in with the views of his patroness—not, however, that they were declared to him in that marked manner in which the affair of the living had been broached. Lady Lufton was much too highly gifted with woman’s craft for that. She never told the young vicar that Miss Monsell accompanied her ladyship’s married daughter to Framley Court expressly that he, Mark, might fall in love with her; but such was in truth the case.\n\nLady Lufton had but two children. The eldest, a daughter, had been married some four or five years to Sir George Meredith, and this Miss Monsell was a dear friend of hers. And now looms before me the novelist’s great difficulty. Miss Monsell,—or, rather, Mrs. Mark Robarts,—must be described. As Miss Monsell, our tale will have to take no prolonged note of her. And yet we will call her Fanny Monsell, when we declare that she was one of the pleasantest companions that could be brought near to a man, as the future partner of his home, and owner of his heart. And if high principles without asperity, female gentleness without weakness, a love of laughter without malice, and a true loving heart, can qualify a woman to be a parson’s wife, then was Fanny Monsell qualified to fill that station.\n\nIn person she was somewhat larger than common. Her face would have been beautiful but that her mouth was large. Her hair, which was copious, was of a bright brown; her eyes also were brown, and, being so, were the distinctive feature of her face, for brown eyes are not common. They were liquid, large, and full either of tenderness or of mirth. Mark Robarts still had his accustomed luck, when such a girl as this was brought to Framley for his wooing.\n\nAnd he did woo her—and won her. For Mark himself was a handsome fellow. At this time the vicar was about twenty-five years of age, and the future Mrs. Robarts was two or three years younger. Nor did she come quite empty-handed to the vicarage. It cannot be said that Fanny Monsell was an heiress, but she had been left with a provision of some few thousand pounds. This was so settled, that the interest of his wife’s money paid the heavy insurance on his life which young Robarts effected, and there was left to him, over and above, sufficient to furnish his parsonage in the very best style of clerical comfort,—and to start him on the road of life rejoicing.\n\nSo much did Lady Lufton do for her protégé, and it may well be imagined that the Devonshire physician, sitting meditative over his parlour fire, looking back, as men will look back on the upshot of their life, was well contented with that upshot, as regarded his eldest offshoot, the Rev. Mark Robarts, the vicar of Framley.\n\nBut little has as yet been said, personally, as to our hero himself, and perhaps it may not be necessary to say much. Let us hope that by degrees he may come forth upon the canvas, showing to the beholder the nature of the man inwardly and outwardly. Here it may suffice to say that he was no born heaven’s cherub, neither was he a born fallen devil’s spirit. Such as his training made him, such he was. He had large capabilities for good—and aptitudes also for evil, quite enough: quite enough to make it needful that he should repel temptation as temptation only can be repelled. Much had been done to spoil him, but in the ordinary acceptation of the word he was not spoiled. He had too much tact, too much common sense, to believe himself to be the paragon which his mother thought him. Self-conceit was not, perhaps, his greatest danger. Had he possessed more of it, he might have been a less agreeable man, but his course before him might on that account have been the safer.\n\nIn person he was manly, tall, and fair-haired, with a square forehead, denoting intelligence rather than thought, with clear white hands, filbert nails, and a power of dressing himself in such a manner that no one should ever observe of him that his clothes were either good or bad, shabby or smart.\n\nSuch was Mark Robarts when at the age of twenty-five, or a little more, he married Fanny Monsell. The marriage was celebrated in his own church, for Miss Monsell had no home of her own, and had been staying for the last three months at Framley Court. She was given away by Sir George Meredith, and Lady Lufton herself saw that the wedding was what it should be, with almost as much care as she had bestowed on that of her own daughter. The deed of marrying, the absolute tying of the knot, was performed by the Very Reverend the Dean of Barchester, an esteemed friend of Lady Lufton’s. And Mrs. Arabin, the dean’s wife, was of the party, though the distance from Barchester to Framley is long, and the roads deep, and no railway lends its assistance. And Lord Lufton was there of course; and people protested that he would surely fall in love with one of the four beautiful bridesmaids, of whom Blanche Robarts, the vicar’s second sister, was by common acknowledgment by far the most beautiful.\n\nAnd there was there another and a younger sister of Mark’s—who did not officiate at the ceremony, though she was present—and of whom no prediction was made, seeing that she was then only sixteen, but of whom mention is made here, as it will come to pass that my readers will know her hereafter. Her name was Lucy Robarts.\n\nAnd then the vicar and his wife went off on their wedding tour, the old curate taking care of the Framley souls the while.\n\nAnd in due time they returned; and after a further interval, in due course, a child was born to them; and then another; and after that came the period at which we will begin our story. But before doing so, may I not assert that all men were right in saying all manner of good things to the Devonshire physician, and in praising his luck in having such a son?\n\n“You were up at the house to-day, I suppose?” said Mark to his wife, as he sat stretching himself in an easy chair in the drawing-room, before the fire, previously to his dressing for dinner. It was a November evening, and he had been out all day, and on such occasions the aptitude for delay in dressing is very powerful. A strong-minded man goes direct from the hall-door to his chamber without encountering the temptation of the drawing-room fire.\n\n“No; but Lady Lufton was down here.”\n\n“Full of arguments in favour of Sarah Thompson?”\n\n“Exactly so, Mark.”\n\n“And what did you say about Sarah Thompson?”\n\n“Very little as coming from myself; but I did hint that you thought, or that I thought that you thought, that one of the regular trained schoolmistresses would be better.”\n\n“But her ladyship did not agree?”\n\n“Well, I won’t exactly say that;—though I think that perhaps she did not.”\n\n“I am sure she did not. When she has a point to carry, she is very fond of carrying it.”\n\n“But then, Mark, her points are generally so good.”\n\n“But, you see, in this affair of the school she is thinking more of her protégée than she does of the children.”\n\n“Tell her that, and I am sure she will give way.”\n\nAnd then again they were both silent. And the vicar having thoroughly warmed himself, as far as this might be done by facing the fire, turned round and began the operation à tergo.\n\n“Come, Mark, it is twenty minutes past six. Will you go and dress?”\n\n“I’ll tell you what, Fanny: she must have her way about Sarah Thompson. You can see her to-morrow and tell her so.”\n\n“I am sure, Mark, I would not give way, if I thought it wrong. Nor would she expect it.”\n\n“If I persist this time, I shall certainly have to yield the next; and then the next may probably be more important.”\n\n“But if it’s wrong, Mark?”\n\n“I didn’t say it was wrong. Besides, if it is wrong, wrong in some infinitesimal degree, one must put up with it. Sarah Thompson is very respectable; the only question is whether she can teach.”\n\nThe young wife, though she did not say so, had some idea that her husband was in error. It is true that one must put up with wrong, with a great deal of wrong. But no one need put up with wrong that he can remedy. Why should he, the vicar, consent to receive an incompetent teacher for the parish children, when he was able to procure one that was competent? In such a case,—so thought Mrs. Robarts to herself,—she would have fought the matter out with Lady Lufton.\n\nOn the next morning, however, she did as she was bid, and signified to the dowager that all objection to Sarah Thompson would be withdrawn.\n\n“Ah! I was sure he would agree with me,” said her ladyship, “when he learned what sort of person she is. I know I had only to explain;”—and then she plumed her feathers, and was very gracious; for, to tell the truth, Lady Lufton did not like to be opposed in things which concerned the parish nearly.\n\n“And, Fanny,” said Lady Lufton, in her kindest manner, “you are not going anywhere on Saturday, are you?”\n\n“No, I think not.”\n\n“Then you must come to us. Justinia is to be here, you know”—Lady Meredith was named Justinia—“and you and Mr. Robarts had better stay with us till Monday. He can have the little book-room all to himself on Sunday. The Merediths go on Monday; and Justinia won’t be happy if you are not with her.”\n\nIt would be unjust to say that Lady Lufton had determined not to invite the Robartses if she were not allowed to have her own way about Sarah Thompson. But such would have been the result. As it was, however, she was all kindness; and when Mrs. Robarts made some little excuse, saying that she was afraid she must return home in the evening, because of the children, Lady Lufton declared that there was room enough at Framley Court for baby and nurse, and so settled the matter in her own way, with a couple of nods and three taps of her umbrella.\n\nThis was on a Tuesday morning, and on the same evening, before dinner, the vicar again seated himself in the same chair before the drawing-room fire, as soon as he had seen his horse led into the stable.\n\n“Mark,” said his wife, “the Merediths are to be at Framley on Saturday and Sunday; and I have promised that we will go up and stay over till Monday.”\n\n“You don’t mean it! Goodness gracious, how provoking!”\n\n“Why? I thought you wouldn’t mind it. And Justinia would think it unkind if I were not there.”\n\n“You can go, my dear, and of course will go. But as for me, it is impossible.”\n\n“But why, love?”\n\n“Why? Just now, at the school-house, I answered a letter that was brought to me from Chaldicotes. Sowerby insists on my going over there for a week or so; and I have said that I would.”\n\n“Go to Chaldicotes for a week, Mark?”\n\n“I believe I have even consented to ten days.”\n\n“And be away two Sundays?”\n\n“No, Fanny, only one. Don’t be so censorious.”\n\n“Don’t call me censorious, Mark; you know I am not so. But I am so sorry. It is just what Lady Lufton won’t like. Besides, you were away in Scotland two Sundays last month.”\n\n“In September, Fanny. And that is being censorious.”\n\n“Oh, but, Mark, dear Mark; don’t say so. You know I don’t mean it. But Lady Lufton does not like those Chaldicotes people. You know Lord Lufton was with you the last time you were there; and how annoyed she was!”\n\n“Lord Lufton won’t be with me now, for he is still in Scotland. And the reason why I am going is this: Harold Smith and his wife will be there, and I am very anxious to know more of them. I have no doubt that Harold Smith will be in the government some day, and I cannot afford to neglect such a man’s acquaintance.”\n\n“But, Mark, what do you want of any government?”\n\n“Well, Fanny, of course I am bound to say that I want nothing; neither in one sense do I; but nevertheless, I shall go and meet the Harold Smiths.”\n\n“Could you not be back before Sunday?”\n\n“I have promised to preach at Chaldicotes. Harold Smith is going to lecture at Barchester, about the Australasian archipelago, and I am to preach a charity sermon on the same subject. They want to send out more missionaries.”\n\n“A charity sermon at Chaldicotes!”\n\n“And why not? The house will be quite full, you know; and I dare say the Arabins will be there.”\n\n“I think not; Mrs. Arabin may get on with Mrs. Harold Smith, though I doubt that; but I’m sure she’s not fond of Mrs. Smith’s brother. I don’t think she would stay at Chaldicotes.”\n\n“And the bishop will probably be there for a day or two.”\n\n“That is much more likely, Mark. If the pleasure of meeting Mrs. Proudie is taking you to Chaldicotes, I have not a word more to say.”\n\n“I am not a bit more fond of Mrs. Proudie than you are, Fanny,” said the vicar, with something like vexation in the tone of his voice, for he thought that his wife was hard upon him. “But it is generally thought that a parish clergyman does well to meet his bishop now and then. And as I was invited there, especially to preach while all these people are staying at the place, I could not well refuse.” And then he got up, and taking his candlestick, escaped to his dressing-room.\n\n“But what am I to say to Lady Lufton?” his wife said to him, in the course of the evening.\n\n“Just write her a note, and tell her that you find I had promised to preach at Chaldicotes next Sunday. You’ll go, of course?”\n\n“Yes: but I know she’ll be annoyed. You were away the last time she had people there.”\n\n“It can’t be helped. She must put it down against Sarah Thompson. She ought not to expect to win always.”\n\n“I should not have minded it, if she had lost, as you call it, about Sarah Thompson. That was a case in which you ought to have had your own way.”\n\n“And this other is a case in which I shall have it. It’s a pity that there should be such a difference; isn’t it?”\n\nThen the wife perceived that, vexed as she was, it would be better that she should say nothing further; and before she went to bed, she wrote the note to Lady Lufton, as her husband recommended.\n\n# CHAPTER II. THE FRAMLEY SET, AND THE CHALDICOTES SET.\n\nIt will be necessary that I should say a word or two of some of the people named in the few preceding pages, and also of the localities in which they lived.\n\nOf Lady Lufton herself enough, perhaps, has been written to introduce her to my readers. The Framley property belonged to her son; but as Lufton Park—an ancient ramshackle place in another county—had heretofore been the family residence of the Lufton family, Framley Court had been apportioned to her for her residence for life. Lord Lufton himself was still unmarried; and as he had no establishment at Lufton Park—which indeed had not been inhabited since his grandfather died—he lived with his mother when it suited him to live anywhere in that neighbourhood. The widow would fain have seen more of him than he allowed her to do. He had a shooting-lodge in Scotland, and apartments in London, and a string of horses in Leicestershire—much to the disgust of the county gentry around him, who held that their own hunting was as good as any that England could afford. His lordship, however, paid his subscription to the East Barsetshire pack, and then thought himself at liberty to follow his own pleasure as to his own amusement.\n\nFramley itself was a pleasant country place, having about it nothing of seignorial dignity or grandeur, but possessing everything necessary for the comfort of country life. The house was a low building of two stories, built at different periods, and devoid of all pretensions to any style of architecture; but the rooms, though not lofty, were warm and comfortable, and the gardens were trim and neat beyond all others in the county. Indeed, it was for its gardens only that Framley Court was celebrated.\n\nVillage there was none, properly speaking. The high road went winding about through the Framley paddocks, shrubberies, and wood-skirted home fields, for a mile and a half, not two hundred yards of which ran in a straight line; and there was a cross-road which passed down through the domain, whereby there came to be a locality called Framley Cross. Here stood the “Lufton Arms,” and here, at Framley Cross, the hounds occasionally would meet; for the Framley woods were drawn in spite of the young lord’s truant disposition; and then, at the Cross also, lived the shoemaker, who kept the post-office.\n\nFramley church was distant from this just a quarter of a mile, and stood immediately opposite to the chief entrance to Framley Court. It was but a mean, ugly building, having been erected about a hundred years since, when all churches then built were made to be mean and ugly; nor was it large enough for the congregation, some of whom were thus driven to the dissenting chapels, the Sions and Ebenezers, which had got themselves established on each side of the parish, in putting down which Lady Lufton thought that her pet parson was hardly as energetic as he might be. It was, therefore, a matter near to Lady Lufton’s heart to see a new church built, and she was urgent in her eloquence, both with her son and with the vicar, to have this good work commenced.\n\nBeyond the church, but close to it, were the boys’ school and girls’ school, two distinct buildings, which owed their erection to Lady Lufton’s energy; then came a neat little grocer’s shop, the neat grocer being the clerk and sexton, and the neat grocer’s wife, the pew-opener in the church. Podgens was their name, and they were great favourites with her ladyship, both having been servants up at the house.\n\nAnd here the road took a sudden turn to the left, turning, as it were, away from Framley Court; and just beyond the turn was the vicarage, so that there was a little garden path running from the back of the vicarage grounds into the churchyard, cutting the Podgenses off into an isolated corner of their own;—from whence, to tell the truth, the vicar would have been glad to banish them and their cabbages, could he have had the power to do so. For has not the small vineyard of Naboth been always an eyesore to neighbouring potentates?\n\nThe potentate in this case had as little excuse as Ahab, for nothing in the parsonage way could be more perfect than his parsonage. It had all the details requisite for the house of a moderate gentleman with moderate means, and none of those expensive superfluities which immoderate gentlemen demand, or which themselves demand—immoderate means. And then the gardens and paddocks were exactly suited to it; and everything was in good order;—not exactly new, so as to be raw and uncovered, and redolent of workmen; but just at that era of their existence in which newness gives way to comfortable homeliness.\n\nOther village at Framley there was none. At the back of the Court, up one of those cross-roads, there was another small shop or two, and there was a very neat cottage residence, in which lived the widow of a former curate, another protégé of Lady Lufton’s; and there was a big, staring brick house, in which the present curate lived; but this was a full mile distant from the church, and farther from Framley Court, standing on that cross-road which runs from Framley Cross in a direction away from the mansion. This gentleman, the Rev. Evan Jones, might, from his age, have been the vicar’s father; but he had been for many years curate of Framley; and though he was personally disliked by Lady Lufton, as being Low Church in his principles, and unsightly in his appearance, nevertheless, she would not urge his removal. He had two or three pupils in that large brick house, and if turned out from these and from his curacy, might find it difficult to establish himself elsewhere. On this account mercy was extended to the Rev. E. Jones, and, in spite of his red face and awkward big feet, he was invited to dine at Framley Court, with his plain daughter, once in every three months.\n\nOver and above these, there was hardly a house in the parish of Framley, outside the bounds of Framley Court, except those of farmers and farm labourers; and yet the parish was of large extent.\n\nFramley is in the eastern division of the county of Barsetshire, which, as all the world knows, is, politically speaking, as true blue a county as any in England. There have been backslidings even here, it is true; but then, in what county have there not been such backslidings? Where, in these pinchbeck days, can we hope to find the old agricultural virtue in all its purity? But, among those backsliders, I regret to say, that men now reckon Lord Lufton. Not that he is a violent Whig, or perhaps that he is a Whig at all. But he jeers and sneers at the old county doings; declares, when solicited on the subject, that, as far as he is concerned, Mr. Bright may sit for the county, if he pleases; and alleges, that being unfortunately a peer, he has no right even to interest himself in the question. All this is deeply regretted, for, in the old days, there was no portion of the county more decidedly true blue than that Framley district; and, indeed, up to the present day, the dowager is able to give an occasional helping hand.\n\nChaldicotes is the seat of Nathaniel Sowerby, Esq., who, at the moment supposed to be now present, is one of the members for the Western Division of Barsetshire. But this Western Division can boast none of the fine political attributes which grace its twin brother. It is decidedly Whig, and is almost governed in its politics by one or two great Whig families.\n\nIt has been said that Mark Robarts was about to pay a visit to Chaldicotes, and it has been hinted that his wife would have been as well pleased had this not been the case. Such was certainly the fact; for she, dear, prudent, excellent wife as she was, knew that Mr. Sowerby was not the most eligible friend in the world for a young clergyman, and knew, also, that there was but one other house in the whole county the name of which was so distasteful to Lady Lufton. The reasons for this were, I may say, manifold. In the first place, Mr. Sowerby was a Whig, and was seated in Parliament mainly by the interest of that great Whig autocrat the Duke of Omnium, whose residence was more dangerous even than that of Mr. Sowerby, and whom Lady Lufton regarded as an impersonation of Lucifer upon earth. Mr. Sowerby, too, was unmarried—as indeed, also, was Lord Lufton, much to his mother’s grief. Mr. Sowerby, it is true, was fifty, whereas the young lord was as yet only twenty-six, but, nevertheless, her ladyship was becoming anxious on the subject. In her mind, every man was bound to marry as soon as he could maintain a wife; and she held an idea—a quite private tenet, of which she was herself but imperfectly conscious—that men in general were inclined to neglect this duty for their own selfish gratifications, that the wicked ones encouraged the more innocent in this neglect, and that many would not marry at all, were not an unseen coercion exercised against them by the other sex. The Duke of Omnium was the very head of all such sinners, and Lady Lufton greatly feared that her son might be made subject to the baneful Omnium influence, by means of Mr. Sowerby and Chaldicotes.\n\nAnd then Mr. Sowerby was known to be a very poor man, with a very large estate. He had wasted, men said, much on electioneering, and more in gambling. A considerable portion of his property had already gone into the hands of the duke, who, as a rule, bought up everything around him that was to be purchased. Indeed it was said of him by his enemies, that so covetous was he of Barsetshire property, that he would lead a young neighbour on to his ruin, in order that he might get his land. What—oh! what if he should come to be possessed in this way of any of the fair acres of Framley Court? What if he should become possessed of them all? It can hardly be wondered at that Lady Lufton should not like Chaldicotes.\n\nThe Chaldicotes set, as Lady Lufton called them, were in every way opposed to what a set should be according to her ideas. She liked cheerful, quiet, well-to-do people, who loved their Church, their country, and their Queen, and who were not too anxious to make a noise in the world. She desired that all the farmers round her should be able to pay their rents without trouble, that all the old women should have warm flannel petticoats, that the working men should be saved from rheumatism by healthy food and dry houses, that they should all be obedient to their pastors and masters—temporal as well as spiritual. That was her idea of loving her country. She desired also that the copses should be full of pheasants, the stubble-field of partridges, and the gorse covers of foxes;—in that way, also, she loved her country. She had ardently longed, during that Crimean war, that the Russians might be beaten—but not by the French, to the exclusion of the English, as had seemed to her to be too much the case; and hardly by the English under the dictatorship of Lord Palmerston. Indeed, she had had but little faith in that war after Lord Aberdeen had been expelled. If, indeed, Lord Derby could have come in!\n\nBut now as to this Chaldicotes set. After all, there was nothing so very dangerous about them; for it was in London, not in the country, that Mr. Sowerby indulged, if he did indulge, his bachelor mal-practices. Speaking of them as a set, the chief offender was Mr. Harold Smith, or perhaps his wife. He also was a member of Parliament, and, as many thought, a rising man. His father had been for many years a debater in the House, and had held high office. Harold, in early life, had intended himself for the cabinet; and if working hard at his trade could ensure success, he ought to obtain it sooner or later. He had already filled more than one subordinate station, had been at the Treasury, and for a month or two at the Admiralty, astonishing official mankind by his diligence. Those last-named few months had been under Lord Aberdeen, with whom he had been forced to retire. He was a younger son, and not possessed of any large fortune. Politics as a profession was therefore of importance to him. He had in early life married a sister of Mr. Sowerby; and as the lady was some six or seven years older than himself, and had brought with her but a scanty dowry, people thought that in this matter Mr. Harold Smith had not been perspicacious. Mr. Harold Smith was not personally a popular man with any party, though some judged him to be eminently useful. He was laborious, well-informed, and, on the whole, honest; but he was conceited, long-winded, and pompous.\n\nMrs. Harold Smith was the very opposite of her lord. She was a clever, bright woman, good-looking for her time of life—and she was now over forty—with a keen sense of the value of all worldly things, and a keen relish for all the world’s pleasures. She was neither laborious, nor well-informed, nor perhaps altogether honest—what woman ever understood the necessity or recognized the advantage of political honesty?—but then she was neither dull nor pompous, and if she was conceited, she did not show it. She was a disappointed woman, as regards her husband; seeing that she had married him on the speculation that he would at once become politically important; and as yet Mr. Smith had not quite fulfilled the prophecies of his early life.\n\nAnd Lady Lufton, when she spoke of the Chaldicotes set, distinctly included, in her own mind, the Bishop of Barchester, and his wife and daughter. Seeing that Bishop Proudie was, of course, a man much addicted to religion and to religious thinking, and that Mr. Sowerby himself had no peculiar religious sentiments whatever, there would not at first sight appear to be ground for much intercourse, and perhaps there was not much of such intercourse; but Mrs. Proudie and Mrs. Harold Smith were firm friends of four or five years’ standing—ever since the Proudies came into the diocese; and therefore the bishop was usually taken to Chaldicotes whenever Mrs. Smith paid her brother a visit. Now Bishop Proudie was by no means a High Church dignitary, and Lady Lufton had never forgiven him for coming into that diocese. She had, instinctively, a high respect for the episcopal office; but of Bishop Proudie himself she hardly thought better than she did of Mr. Sowerby, or of that fabricator of evil, the Duke of Omnium. Whenever Mr. Robarts would plead that in going anywhere he would have the benefit of meeting the bishop, Lady Lufton would slightly curl her upper lip. She could not say in words, that Bishop Proudie—bishop as he certainly must be called—was no better than he ought to be; but by that curl of her lip she did explain to those who knew her that such was the inner feeling of her heart.\n\nAnd then it was understood—Mark Robarts, at least, had so heard, and the information soon reached Framley Court—that Mr. Supplehouse was to make one of the Chaldicotes party. Now Mr. Supplehouse was a worse companion for a gentlemanlike, young, High Church, conservative county parson than even Harold Smith. He also was in Parliament, and had been extolled during the early days of that Russian war by some portion of the metropolitan daily press, as the only man who could save the country. Let him be in the ministry, the Jupiter had said, and there would be some hope of reform, some chance that England’s ancient glory would not be allowed in these perilous times to go headlong to oblivion. And upon this the ministry, not anticipating much salvation from Mr. Supplehouse, but willing, as they usually are, to have the Jupiter at their back, did send for that gentleman, and gave him some footing among them. But how can a man born to save a nation, and to lead a people, be content to fill the chair of an under-secretary? Supplehouse was not content, and soon gave it to be understood that his place was much higher than any yet tendered to him. The seals of high office, or war to the knife, was the alternative which he offered to a much-belaboured Head of Affairs—nothing doubting that the Head of Affairs would recognize the claimant’s value, and would have before his eyes a wholesome fear of the Jupiter. But the Head of Affairs, much belaboured as he was, knew that he might pay too high even for Mr. Supplehouse and the Jupiter; and the saviour of the nation was told that he might swing his tomahawk. Since that time he had been swinging his tomahawk, but not with so much effect as had been anticipated. He also was very intimate with Mr. Sowerby, and was decidedly one of the Chaldicotes set.\n\nAnd there were many others included in the stigma whose sins were political or religious rather than moral. But they were gall and wormwood to Lady Lufton, who regarded them as children of the Lost One, and who grieved with a mother’s grief when she knew that her son was among them, and felt all a patron’s anger when she heard that her clerical protégé was about to seek such society. Mrs. Robarts might well say that Lady Lufton would be annoyed.\n\n“You won’t call at the house before you go, will you?” the wife asked on the following morning. He was to start after lunch on that day, driving himself in his own gig, so as to reach Chaldicotes, some twenty-four miles distant, before dinner.\n\n“No, I think not. What good should I do?”\n\n“Well, I can’t explain; but I think I should call: partly, perhaps, to show her that as I had determined to go, I was not afraid of telling her so.”\n\n“Afraid! That’s nonsense, Fanny. I’m not afraid of her. But I don’t see why I should bring down upon myself the disagreeable things she will say. Besides, I have not time. I must walk up and see Jones about the duties; and then, what with getting ready, I shall have enough to do to get off in time.”\n\nHe paid his visit to Mr. Jones, the curate, feeling no qualms of conscience there, as he rather boasted of all the members of Parliament he was going to meet, and of the bishop who would be with them. Mr. Evan Jones was only his curate, and in speaking to him on the matter he could talk as though it were quite the proper thing for a vicar to meet his bishop at the house of a county member. And one would be inclined to say that it was proper: only why could he not talk of it in the same tone to Lady Lufton? And then, having kissed his wife and children, he drove off, well pleased with his prospect for the coming ten days, but already anticipating some discomfort on his return.\n\nOn the three following days, Mrs. Robarts did not meet her ladyship. She did not exactly take any steps to avoid such a meeting, but she did not purposely go up to the big house. She went to her school as usual, and made one or two calls among the farmers’ wives, but put no foot within the Framley Court grounds. She was braver than her husband, but even she did not wish to anticipate the evil day.\n\nOn the Saturday, just before it began to get dusk, when she was thinking of preparing for the fatal plunge, her friend, Lady Meredith, came to her.\n\n“So, Fanny, we shall again be so unfortunate as to miss Mr. Robarts,” said her ladyship.\n\n“Yes. Did you ever know anything so unlucky? But he had promised Mr. Sowerby before he heard that you were coming. Pray do not think that he would have gone away had he known it.”\n\n“We should have been sorry to keep him from so much more amusing a party.”\n\n“Now, Justinia, you are unfair. You intend to imply that he has gone to Chaldicotes, because he likes it better than Framley Court; but that is not the case. I hope Lady Lufton does not think that it is.”\n\nLady Meredith laughed as she put her arm round her friend’s waist. “Don’t lose your eloquence in defending him to me,” she said. “You’ll want all that for my mother.”\n\n“But is your mother angry?” asked Mrs. Robarts, showing by her countenance, how eager she was for true tidings on the subject.\n\n“Well, Fanny, you know her ladyship as well as I do. She thinks so very highly of the vicar of Framley, that she does begrudge him to those politicians at Chaldicotes.”\n\n“But, Justinia, the bishop is to be there, you know.”\n\n“I don’t think that that consideration will at all reconcile my mother to the gentleman’s absence. He ought to be very proud, I know, to find that he is so much thought of. But come, Fanny, I want you to walk back with me, and you can dress at the house. And now we’ll go and look at the children.”\n\nAfter that, as they walked together to Framley Court, Mrs. Robarts made her friend promise that she would stand by her if any serious attack were made on the absent clergyman.\n\n“Are you going up to your room at once?” said the vicar’s wife, as soon as they were inside the porch leading into the hall. Lady Meredith immediately knew what her friend meant, and decided that the evil day should not be postponed. “We had better go in and have it over,” she said, “and then we shall be comfortable for the evening.” So the drawing-room door was opened, and there was Lady Lufton alone upon the sofa.\n\n“Now, mamma,” said the daughter, “you mustn’t scold Fanny much about Mr. Robarts. He has gone to preach a charity sermon before the bishop, and under those circumstances, perhaps, he could not refuse.” This was a stretch on the part of Lady Meredith—put in with much good nature, no doubt; but still a stretch; for no one had supposed that the bishop would remain at Chaldicotes for the Sunday.\n\n“How do you do, Fanny?” said Lady Lufton, getting up. “I am not going to scold her; and I don’t know how you can talk such nonsense, Justinia. Of course, we are very sorry not to have Mr. Robarts; more especially as he was not here the last Sunday that Sir George was with us. I do like to see Mr. Robarts in his own church, certainly; and I don’t like any other clergyman there as well. If Fanny takes that for scolding, why—”\n\n“Oh! no, Lady Lufton; and it’s so kind of you to say so. But Mr. Robarts was so sorry that he had accepted this invitation to Chaldicotes, before he heard that Sir George was coming, and—”\n\n“Oh, I know that Chaldicotes has great attractions which we cannot offer,” said Lady Lufton.\n\n“Indeed, it was not that. But he was asked to preach, you know; and Mr. Harold Smith—” Poor Fanny was only making it worse. Had she been worldly wise, she would have accepted the little compliment implied in Lady Lufton’s first rebuke, and then have held her peace.\n\n“Oh, yes; the Harold Smiths! They are irresistible, I know. How could any man refuse to join a party, graced both by Mrs. Harold Smith and Mrs. Proudie—even though his duty should require him to stay away?”\n\n“Now, mamma—” said Justinia.\n\n“Well, my dear, what am I to say? You would not wish me to tell a fib. I don’t like Mrs. Harold Smith—at least, what I hear of her; for it has not been my fortune to meet her since her marriage. It may be conceited; but to own the truth, I think that Mr. Robarts would be better off with us at Framley than with the Harold Smiths at Chaldicotes,—even though Mrs. Proudie be thrown into the bargain.”\n\nIt was nearly dark, and therefore the rising colour in the face of Mrs. Robarts could not be seen. She, however, was too good a wife to hear these things said without some anger within her bosom. She could blame her husband in her own mind; but it was intolerable to her that others should blame him in her hearing.\n\n“He would undoubtedly be better off,” she said; “but then, Lady Lufton, people can’t always go exactly where they will be best off. Gentlemen sometimes must—”\n\n“Well—well, my dear, that will do. He has not taken you, at any rate; and so we will forgive him.” And Lady Lufton kissed her. “As it is,”—and she affected a low whisper between the two young wives—“as it is, we must e’en put up with poor old Evan Jones. He is to be here to-night, and we must go and dress to receive him.”\n\nAnd so they went off. Lady Lufton was quite good enough at heart to like Mrs. Robarts all the better for standing up for her absent lord.\n\n# CHAPTER III. CHALDICOTES.\n\nChaldicotes is a house of much more pretension than Framley Court. Indeed, if one looks at the ancient marks about it, rather than at those of the present day, it is a place of very considerable pretension. There is an old forest, not altogether belonging to the property, but attached to it, called the Chace of Chaldicotes. A portion of this forest comes up close behind the mansion, and of itself gives a character and celebrity to the place. The Chace of Chaldicotes—the greater part of it, at least—is, as all the world knows, Crown property, and now, in these utilitarian days, is to be disforested. In former times it was a great forest, stretching half across the country, almost as far as Silverbridge; and there are bits of it, here and there, still to be seen at intervals throughout the whole distance; but the larger remaining portion, consisting of aged hollow oaks, centuries old, and wide-spreading withered beeches, stands in the two parishes of Chaldicotes and Uffley. People still come from afar to see the oaks of Chaldicotes, and to hear their feet rustle among the thick autumn leaves. But they will soon come no longer. The giants of past ages are to give way to wheat and turnips; a ruthless Chancellor of the Exchequer, disregarding old associations and rural beauty, requires money returns from the lands; and the Chace of Chaldicotes is to vanish from the earth’s surface.\n\nSome part of it, however, is the private property of Mr. Sowerby, who hitherto, through all his pecuniary distresses, has managed to save from the axe and the auction-mart that portion of his paternal heritage. The house of Chaldicotes is a large stone building, probably of the time of Charles the Second. It is approached on both fronts by a heavy double flight of stone steps. In the front of the house a long, solemn, straight avenue through a double row of lime-trees, leads away to lodge-gates, which stand in the centre of the village of Chaldicotes; but to the rear the windows open upon four different vistas, which run down through the forest: four open green rides, which all converge together at a large iron gateway, the barrier which divides the private grounds from the Chace. The Sowerbys, for many generations, have been rangers of the Chace of Chaldicotes, thus having almost as wide an authority over the Crown forest as over their own. But now all this is to cease, for the forest will be disforested.\n\nIt was nearly dark as Mark Robarts drove up through the avenue of lime-trees to the hall-door; but it was easy to see that the house, which was dead and silent as the grave through nine months of the year, was now alive in all its parts. There were lights in many of the windows, and a noise of voices came from the stables, and servants were moving about, and dogs barked, and the dark gravel before the front steps was cut up with many a coach-wheel.\n\n“Oh, be that you, sir, Mr. Robarts?” said a groom, taking the parson’s horse by the head, and touching his own hat. “I hope I see your reverence well?”\n\n“Quite well, Bob, thank you. All well at Chaldicotes?”\n\n“Pretty bobbish, Mr. Robarts. Deal of life going on here now, sir. The bishop and his lady came this morning.”\n\n“Oh—ah—yes! I understood they were to be here. Any of the young ladies?”\n\n“One young lady. Miss Olivia, I think they call her, your reverence.”\n\n“And how’s Mr. Sowerby?”\n\n“Very well, your reverence. He, and Mr. Harold Smith, and Mr. Fothergill—that’s the duke’s man of business, you know—is getting off their horses now in the stable-yard there.”\n\n“Home from hunting—eh, Bob?”\n\n“Yes, sir, just home, this minute.” And then Mr. Robarts walked into the house, his portmanteau following on a footboy’s shoulder.\n\nIt will be seen that our young vicar was very intimate at Chaldicotes; so much so that the groom knew him, and talked to him about the people in the house. Yes; he was intimate there: much more than he had given the Framley people to understand. Not that he had wilfully and overtly deceived any one; not that he had ever spoken a false word about Chaldicotes. But he had never boasted at home that he and Sowerby were near allies. Neither had he told them there how often Mr. Sowerby and Lord Lufton were together in London. Why trouble women with such matters? Why annoy so excellent a woman as Lady Lufton?\n\nAnd then Mr. Sowerby was one whose intimacy few young men would wish to reject. He was fifty, and had lived, perhaps, not the most salutary life; but he dressed young, and usually looked well. He was bald, with a good forehead, and sparkling moist eyes. He was a clever man, and a pleasant companion, and always good-humoured when it so suited him. He was a gentleman, too, of high breeding and good birth, whose ancestors had been known in that county—longer, the farmers around would boast, than those of any other landowner in it, unless it be the Thornes of Ullathorne, or perhaps the Greshams of Greshamsbury—much longer than the De Courcys at Courcy Castle. As for the Duke of Omnium, he, comparatively speaking, was a new man.\n\nAnd then he was a member of Parliament, a friend of some men in power, and of others who might be there; a man who could talk about the world as one knowing the matter of which he talked. And moreover, whatever might be his ways of life at other times, when in the presence of a clergyman he rarely made himself offensive to clerical tastes. He neither swore, nor brought his vices on the carpet, nor sneered at the faith of the Church. If he was no churchman himself, he at least knew how to live with those who were.\n\nHow was it possible that such a one as our vicar should not relish the intimacy of Mr. Sowerby? It might be very well, he would say to himself, for a woman like Lady Lufton to turn up her nose at him—for Lady Lufton, who spent ten months of the year at Framley Court, and who during those ten months, and for the matter of that, during the two months also which she spent in London, saw no one out of her own set. Women did not understand such things, the vicar said to himself; even his own wife—good, and nice, and sensible, and intelligent as she was—even she did not understand that a man in the world must meet all sorts of men; and that in these days it did not do for a clergyman to be a hermit.\n\n’Twas thus that Mark Robarts argued when he found himself called upon to defend himself before the bar of his own conscience for going to Chaldicotes and increasing his intimacy with Mr. Sowerby. He did know that Mr. Sowerby was a dangerous man; he was aware that he was over head and ears in debt, and that he had already entangled young Lord Lufton in some pecuniary embarrassment; his conscience did tell him that it would be well for him, as one of Christ’s soldiers, to look out for companions of a different stamp. But nevertheless he went to Chaldicotes, not satisfied with himself indeed, but repeating to himself a great many arguments why he should be so satisfied.\n\nHe was shown into the drawing-room at once, and there he found Mrs. Harold Smith, with Mrs. and Miss Proudie, and a lady whom he had never before seen, and whose name he did not at first hear mentioned.\n\n“Is that Mr. Robarts?” said Mrs. Harold Smith, getting up to greet him, and screening her pretended ignorance under the veil of the darkness. “And have you really driven over four-and-twenty miles of Barsetshire roads on such a day as this to assist us in our little difficulties? Well, we can promise you gratitude at any rate.”\n\nAnd then the vicar shook hands with Mrs. Proudie, in that deferential manner which is due from a vicar to his bishop’s wife; and Mrs. Proudie returned the greeting with all that smiling condescension which a bishop’s wife should show to a vicar. Miss Proudie was not quite so civil. Had Mr. Robarts been still unmarried, she also could have smiled sweetly; but she had been exercising smiles on clergymen too long to waste them now on a married parish parson.\n\n“And what are the difficulties, Mrs. Smith, in which I am to assist you?”\n\n“We have six or seven gentlemen here, Mr. Robarts, and they always go out hunting before breakfast, and they never come back—I was going to say—till after dinner. I wish it were so, for then we should not have to wait for them.”\n\n“Excepting Mr. Supplehouse, you know,” said the unknown lady, in a loud voice.\n\n“And he is generally shut up in the library, writing articles.”\n\n“He’d be better employed if he were trying to break his neck like the others,” said the unknown lady.\n\n“Only he would never succeed,” said Mrs. Harold Smith. “But perhaps, Mr. Robarts, you are as bad as the rest; perhaps you, too, will be hunting to-morrow.”\n\n“My dear Mrs. Smith!” said Mrs. Proudie, in a tone denoting slight reproach, and modified horror.\n\n“Oh! I forgot. No, of course, you won’t be hunting, Mr. Robarts; you’ll only be wishing that you could.”\n\n“Why can’t he?” said the lady with the loud voice.\n\n“My dear Miss Dunstable! a clergyman hunt, while he is staying in the same house with the bishop? Think of the proprieties!”\n\n“Oh—ah! The bishop wouldn’t like it—wouldn’t he? Now, do tell me, sir, what would the bishop do to you if you did hunt?”\n\n“It would depend upon his mood at the time, madam,” said Mr. Robarts. “If that were very stern, he might perhaps have me beheaded before the palace gates.”\n\nMrs. Proudie drew herself up in her chair, showing that she did not like the tone of the conversation; and Miss Proudie fixed her eyes vehemently on her book, showing that Miss Dunstable and her conversation were both beneath her notice.\n\n“If these gentlemen do not mean to break their necks to-night,” said Mrs. Harold Smith, “I wish they’d let us know it. It’s half-past six already.”\n\nAnd then Mr. Robarts gave them to understand that no such catastrophe could be looked for that day, as Mr. Sowerby and the other sportsmen were within the stable-yard when he entered the door.\n\n“Then, ladies, we may as well dress,” said Mrs. Harold Smith. But as she moved towards the door, it opened, and a short gentleman, with a slow, quiet step, entered the room; but was not yet to be distinguished through the dusk by the eyes of Mr. Robarts. “Oh! bishop, is that you?” said Mrs. Smith. “Here is one of the luminaries of your diocese.” And then the bishop, feeling through the dark, made his way up to the vicar and shook him cordially by the hand. “He was delighted to meet Mr. Robarts at Chaldicotes,” he said—“quite delighted. Was he not going to preach on behalf of the Papuan Mission next Sunday? Ah! so he, the bishop, had heard. It was a good work, an excellent work.” And then Dr. Proudie expressed himself as much grieved that he could not remain at Chaldicotes, and hear the sermon. It was plain that his bishop thought no ill of him on account of his intimacy with Mr. Sowerby. But then he felt in his own heart that he did not much regard his bishop’s opinion.\n\n“Ah, Robarts, I’m delighted to see you,” said Mr. Sowerby, when they met on the drawing-room rug before dinner. “You know Harold Smith? Yes, of course you do. Well, who else is there? Oh! Supplehouse. Mr. Supplehouse, allow me to introduce to you my friend Mr. Robarts. It is he who will extract the five-pound note out of your pocket next Sunday for these poor Papuans whom we are going to Christianize. That is, if Harold Smith does not finish the work out of hand at his Saturday lecture. And, Robarts, you have seen the bishop, of course:” this he said in a whisper. “A fine thing to be a bishop, isn’t it? I wish I had half your chance. But, my dear fellow, I’ve made such a mistake; I haven’t got a bachelor parson for Miss Proudie. You must help me out, and take her in to dinner.” And then the great gong sounded, and off they went in pairs.\n\nAt dinner Mark found himself seated between Miss Proudie and the lady whom he had heard named as Miss Dunstable. Of the former he was not very fond, and, in spite of his host’s petition, was not inclined to play bachelor parson for her benefit. With the other lady he would willingly have chatted during the dinner, only that everybody else at table seemed to be intent on doing the same thing. She was neither young, nor beautiful, nor peculiarly ladylike; yet she seemed to enjoy a popularity which must have excited the envy of Mr. Supplehouse, and which certainly was not altogether to the taste of Mrs. Proudie—who, however, fêted her as much as did the others. So that our clergyman found himself unable to obtain more than an inconsiderable share of the lady’s attention.\n\n“Bishop,” said she, speaking across the table, “we have missed you so all day! we have had no one on earth to say a word to us.”\n\n“My dear Miss Dunstable, had I known that— But I really was engaged on business of some importance.”\n\n“I don’t believe in business of importance; do you, Mrs. Smith?”\n\n“Do I not?” said Mrs. Smith. “If you were married to Mr. Harold Smith for one week, you’d believe in it.”\n\n“Should I, now? What a pity that I can’t have that chance of improving my faith! But you are a man of business, also, Mr. Supplehouse; so they tell me.” And she turned to her neighbour on her right hand.\n\n“I cannot compare myself to Harold Smith,” said he. “But perhaps I may equal the bishop.”\n\n“What does a man do, now, when he sits himself down to business? How does he set about it? What are his tools? A quire of blotting paper, I suppose, to begin with?”\n\n“That depends, I should say, on his trade. A shoemaker begins by waxing his thread.”\n\n“And Mr. Harold Smith—?”\n\n“By counting up his yesterday’s figures, generally, I should say; or else by unrolling a ball of red tape. Well-docketed papers and statistical facts are his forte.”\n\n“And what does a bishop do? Can you tell me that?”\n\n“He sends forth to his clergy either blessings or blowings-up, according to the state of his digestive organs. But Mrs. Proudie can explain all that to you with the greatest accuracy.”\n\n“Can she, now? I understand what you mean, but I don’t believe a word of it. The bishop manages his own affairs himself, quite as much as you do, or Mr. Harold Smith.”\n\n“I, Miss Dunstable?”\n\n“Yes, you.”\n\n“But I, unluckily, have not a wife to manage them for me.”\n\n“Then you should not laugh at those who have, for you don’t know what you may come to yourself, when you’re married.”\n\nMr. Supplehouse began to make a pretty speech, saying that he would be delighted to incur any danger in that respect to which he might be subjected by the companionship of Miss Dunstable. But before he was half through it, she had turned her back upon him, and begun a conversation with Mark Robarts.\n\n“Have you much work in your parish, Mr. Robarts?” she asked. Now, Mark was not aware that she knew his name, or the fact of his having a parish, and was rather surprised by the question. And he had not quite liked the tone in which she had seemed to speak of the bishop and his work. His desire for her further acquaintance was therefore somewhat moderated, and he was not prepared to answer her question with much zeal.\n\n“All parish clergymen have plenty of work, if they choose to do it.”\n\n“Ah, that is it; is it not, Mr. Robarts? If they choose to do it? A great many do—many that I know, do; and see what a result they have. But many neglect it—and see what a result they have. I think it ought to be the happiest life that a man can lead, that of a parish clergyman, with a wife and family, and a sufficient income.”\n\n“I think it is,” said Mark Robarts, asking himself whether the contentment accruing to him from such blessings had made him satisfied at all points. He had all these things of which Miss Dunstable spoke, and yet he had told his wife, the other day, that he could not afford to neglect the acquaintance of a rising politician like Harold Smith.\n\n“What I find fault with is this,” continued Miss Dunstable, “that we expect clergymen to do their duty, and don’t give them a sufficient income—give them hardly any income at all. Is it not a scandal, that an educated gentleman with a family should be made to work half his life, and perhaps the whole, for a pittance of seventy pounds a year?”\n\nMark said that it was a scandal, and thought of Mr. Evan Jones and his daughter;—and thought also of his own worth, and his own house, and his own nine hundred a year.\n\n“And yet you clergymen are so proud—aristocratic would be the genteel word, I know—that you won’t take the money of common, ordinary poor people. You must be paid from land and endowments, from tithe and church property. You can’t bring yourself to work for what you earn, as lawyers and doctors do. It is better that curates should starve than undergo such ignominy as that.”\n\n“It is a long subject, Miss Dunstable.”\n\n“A very long one; and that means that I am not to say any more about it.”\n\n“I did not mean that exactly.”\n\n“Oh! but you did though, Mr. Robarts. And I can take a hint of that kind when I get it. You clergymen like to keep those long subjects for your sermons, when no one can answer you. Now if I have a longing heart’s desire for anything at all in this world, it is to be able to get up into a pulpit, and preach a sermon.”\n\n“You can’t conceive how soon that appetite would pall upon you, after its first indulgence.”\n\n“That would depend upon whether I could get people to listen to me. It does not pall upon Mr. Spurgeon, I suppose.” Then her attention was called away by some question from Mr. Sowerby, and Mark Robarts found himself bound to address his conversation to Miss Proudie. Miss Proudie, however, was not thankful, and gave him little but monosyllables for his pains.\n\n“Of course you know Harold Smith is going to give us a lecture about these islanders,” Mr. Sowerby said to him, as they sat round the fire over their wine after dinner. Mark said that he had been so informed, and should be delighted to be one of the listeners.\n\n“You are bound to do that, as he is going to listen to you the day afterwards—or, at any rate, to pretend to do so, which is as much as you will do for him. It’ll be a terrible bore—the lecture, I mean, not the sermon.” And he spoke very low into his friend’s ear. “Fancy having to drive ten miles after dusk, and ten miles back, to hear Harold Smith talk for two hours about Borneo! One must do it, you know.”\n\n“I daresay it will be very interesting.”\n\n“My dear fellow, you haven’t undergone so many of these things as I have. But he’s right to do it. It’s his line of life; and when a man begins a thing he ought to go on with it. Where’s Lufton all this time?”\n\n“In Scotland, when I last heard from him; but he’s probably at Melton now.”\n\n“It’s deuced shabby of him, not hunting here in his own county. He escapes all the bore of going to lectures, and giving feeds to the neighbours; that’s why he treats us so. He has no idea of his duty, has he?”\n\n“Lady Lufton does all that, you know.”\n\n“I wish I’d a Mrs. Sowerby mère to do it for me. But then Lufton has no constituents to look after—lucky dog! By-the-by, has he spoken to you about selling that outlying bit of land of his in Oxfordshire? It belongs to the Lufton property, and yet it doesn’t. In my mind it gives more trouble than it’s worth.”\n\nLord Lufton had spoken to Mark about this sale, and had explained to him that such a sacrifice was absolutely necessary, in consequence of certain pecuniary transactions between him, Lord Lufton, and Mr. Sowerby. But it was found impracticable to complete the business without Lady Lufton’s knowledge, and her son had commissioned Mr. Robarts not only to inform her ladyship, but to talk her over, and to appease her wrath. This commission he had not yet attempted to execute, and it was probable that this visit to Chaldicotes would not do much to facilitate the business.\n\n“They are the most magnificent islands under the sun,” said Harold Smith to the bishop.\n\n“Are they, indeed!” said the bishop, opening his eyes wide, and assuming a look of intense interest.\n\n“And the most intelligent people.”\n\n“Dear me!” said the bishop.\n\n“All they want is guidance, encouragement, instruction—”\n\n“And Christianity,” suggested the bishop.\n\n“And Christianity, of course,” said Mr. Smith, remembering that he was speaking to a dignitary of the Church. It was well to humour such people, Mr. Smith thought. But the Christianity was to be done in the Sunday sermon, and was not part of his work.\n\n“And how do you intend to begin with them?” asked Mr. Supplehouse, the business of whose life it had been to suggest difficulties.\n\n“Begin with them—oh—why—it’s very easy to begin with them. The difficulty is to go on with them, after the money is all spent. We’ll begin by explaining to them the benefits of civilization.”\n\n“Capital plan!” said Mr. Supplehouse. “But how do you set about it, Smith?”\n\n“How do we set about it? How did we set about it with Australia and America? It is very easy to criticize; but in such matters the great thing is to put one’s shoulder to the wheel.”\n\n“We sent our felons to Australia,” said Supplehouse, “and they began the work for us. And as to America, we exterminated the people instead of civilizing them.”\n\n“We did not exterminate the inhabitants of India,” said Harold Smith, angrily.\n\n“Nor have we attempted to Christianize them, as the bishop so properly wishes to do with your islanders.”\n\n“Supplehouse, you are not fair,” said Mr. Sowerby, “neither to Harold Smith nor to us;—you are making him rehearse his lecture, which is bad for him; and making us hear the rehearsal, which is bad for us.”\n\n“Supplehouse belongs to a clique which monopolizes the wisdom of England,” said Harold Smith; “or, at any rate, thinks that it does. But the worst of them is that they are given to talk leading articles.”\n\n“Better that, than talk articles which are not leading,” said Mr. Supplehouse. “Some first-class official men do that.”\n\n“Shall I meet you at the duke’s next week, Mr. Robarts?” said the bishop to him, soon after they had gone into the drawing-room.\n\nMeet him at the duke’s!—the established enemy of Barsetshire mankind, as Lady Lufton regarded his grace! No idea of going to the duke’s had ever entered our hero’s mind; nor had he been aware that the duke was about to entertain any one.\n\n“No, my lord; I think not. Indeed, I have no acquaintance with his grace.”\n\n“Oh—ah! I did not know. Because Mr. Sowerby is going; and so are the Harold Smiths, and, I think, Mr. Supplehouse. An excellent man is the duke;—that is, as regards all the county interests,” added the bishop, remembering that the moral character of his bachelor grace was not the very best in the world.\n\nAnd then his lordship began to ask some questions about the church affairs of Framley, in which a little interest as to Framley Court was also mixed up, when he was interrupted by a rather sharp voice, to which he instantly attended.\n\n“Bishop,” said the rather sharp voice; and the bishop trotted across the room to the back of the sofa, on which his wife was sitting. “Miss Dunstable thinks that she will be able to come to us for a couple of days, after we leave the duke’s.”\n\n“I shall be delighted above all things,” said the bishop, bowing low to the dominant lady of the day. For be it known to all men, that Miss Dunstable was the great heiress of that name.\n\n“Mrs. Proudie is so very kind as to say that she will take me in, with my poodle, parrot, and pet old woman.”\n\n“I tell Miss Dunstable that we shall have quite room for any of her suite,” said Mrs. Proudie. “And that it will give us no trouble.”\n\n“‘The labour we delight in physics pain,’” said the gallant bishop, bowing low, and putting his hand upon his heart.\n\nIn the meantime, Mr. Fothergill had got hold of Mark Robarts. Mr. Fothergill was a gentleman, and a magistrate of the county, but he occupied the position of managing man on the Duke of Omnium’s estates. He was not exactly his agent; that is to say, he did not receive his rents; but he “managed” for him, saw people, went about the county, wrote letters, supported the electioneering interest, did popularity when it was too much trouble for the duke to do it himself, and was, in fact, invaluable. People in West Barsetshire would often say that they did not know what on earth the duke would do, if it were not for Mr. Fothergill. Indeed, Mr. Fothergill was useful to the duke.\n\n“Mr. Robarts,” he said, “I am very happy to have the pleasure of meeting you—very happy, indeed. I have often heard of you from our friend Sowerby.”\n\nMark bowed, and said that he was delighted to have the honour of making Mr. Fothergill’s acquaintance.\n\n“I am commissioned by the Duke of Omnium,” continued Mr. Fothergill, “to say how glad he will be if you will join his grace’s party at Gatherum Castle, next week. The bishop will be there, and indeed nearly the whole set who are here now. The duke would have written when he heard that you were to be at Chaldicotes; but things were hardly quite arranged then, so his grace has left it for me to tell you how happy he will be to make your acquaintance in his own house. I have spoken to Sowerby,” continued Mr. Fothergill, “and he very much hopes that you will be able to join us.”\n\nMark felt that his face became red when this proposition was made to him. The party in the county to which he properly belonged—he and his wife, and all that made him happy and respectable—looked upon the Duke of Omnium with horror and amazement; and now he had absolutely received an invitation to the duke’s house! A proposition was made to him that he should be numbered among the duke’s friends!\n\nAnd though in one sense he was sorry that the proposition was made to him, yet in another he was proud of it. It is not every young man, let his profession be what it may, who can receive overtures of friendship from dukes without some elation. Mark, too, had risen in the world, as far as he had yet risen, by knowing great people; and he certainly had an ambition to rise higher. I will not degrade him by calling him a tuft-hunter; but he undoubtedly had a feeling that the paths most pleasant for a clergyman’s feet were those which were trodden by the great ones of the earth.\n\nNevertheless, at the moment he declined the duke’s invitation. He was very much flattered, he said, but the duties of his parish would require him to return direct from Chaldicotes to Framley.\n\n“You need not give me an answer to-night, you know,” said Mr. Fothergill. “Before the week is past, we will talk it over with Sowerby and the bishop. It will be a thousand pities, Mr. Robarts, if you will allow me to say so, that you should neglect such an opportunity of knowing his grace.”\n\nWhen Mark went to bed, his mind was still set against going to the duke’s; but, nevertheless, he did feel that it was a pity that he should not do so. After all, was it necessary that he should obey Lady Lufton in all things?\n\n# CHAPTER IV. A MATTER OF CONSCIENCE.\n\nIt is no doubt very wrong to long after a naughty thing. But nevertheless we all do so. One may say that hankering after naughty things is the very essence of the evil into which we have been precipitated by Adam’s fall. When we confess that we are all sinners, we confess that we all long after naughty things.\n\nAnd ambition is a great vice—as Mark Antony told us a long time ago—a great vice, no doubt, if the ambition of the man be with reference to his own advancement, and not to the advancement of others. But then, how many of us are there who are not ambitious in this vicious manner?\n\nAnd there is nothing viler than the desire to know great people—people of great rank, I should say; nothing worse than the hunting of titles and worshipping of wealth. We all know this, and say it every day of our lives. But presuming that a way into the society of Park Lane was open to us, and a way also into that of Bedford Row, how many of us are there who would prefer Bedford Row because it is so vile to worship wealth and title?\n\nI am led into these rather trite remarks by the necessity of putting forward some sort of excuse for that frame of mind in which the Rev. Mark Robarts awoke on the morning after his arrival at Chaldicotes. And I trust that the fact of his being a clergyman will not be allowed to press against him unfairly. Clergymen are subject to the same passions as other men; and, as far as I can see, give way to them, in one line or in another, almost as frequently. Every clergyman should, by canonical rule, feel a personal disinclination to a bishopric; but yet we do not believe that such personal disinclination is generally very strong.\n\nMark’s first thoughts when he woke on that morning flew back to Mr. Fothergill’s invitation. The duke had sent a special message to say how peculiarly glad he, the duke, would be to make acquaintance with him, the parson! How much of this message had been of Mr. Fothergill’s own manufacture, that Mark Robarts did not consider.\n\nHe had obtained a living at an age when other young clergymen are beginning to think of a curacy, and he had obtained such a living as middle-aged parsons in their dreams regard as a possible Paradise for their old years. Of course he thought that all these good things had been the results of his own peculiar merits. Of course he felt that he was different from other parsons,—more fitted by nature for intimacy with great persons, more urbane, more polished, and more richly endowed with modern clerical well-to-do aptitudes. He was grateful to Lady Lufton for what she had done for him; but perhaps not so grateful as he should have been.\n\nAt any rate he was not Lady Lufton’s servant, nor even her dependant. So much he had repeated to himself on many occasions, and had gone so far as to hint the same idea to his wife. In his career as parish priest he must in most things be the judge of his own actions—and in many also it was his duty to be the judge of those of his patroness. The fact of Lady Lufton having placed him in the living, could by no means make her the proper judge of his actions. This he often said to himself; and he said as often that Lady Lufton certainly had a hankering after such a judgment-seat.\n\nOf whom generally did prime ministers and official bigwigs think it expedient to make bishops and deans? Was it not, as a rule, of those clergymen who had shown themselves able to perform their clerical duties efficiently, and able also to take their place with ease in high society? He was very well off certainly at Framley; but he could never hope for anything beyond Framley, if he allowed himself to regard Lady Lufton as a bugbear. Putting Lady Lufton and her prejudices out of the question, was there any reason why he ought not to accept the duke’s invitation? He could not see that there was any such reason. If any one could be a better judge on such a subject than himself, it must be his bishop. And it was clear that the bishop wished him to go to Gatherum Castle.\n\nThe matter was still left open to him. Mr. Fothergill had especially explained that; and therefore his ultimate decision was as yet within his own power. Such a visit would cost him some money, for he knew that a man does not stay at great houses without expense; and then, in spite of his good income, he was not very flush of money. He had been down this year with Lord Lufton in Scotland. Perhaps it might be more prudent for him to return home.\n\nBut then an idea came to him that it behoved him as a man and a priest to break through that Framley thraldom under which he felt that he did to a certain extent exist. Was it not the fact that he was about to decline this invitation from fear of Lady Lufton? and if so, was that a motive by which he ought to be actuated? It was incumbent on him to rid himself of that feeling. And in this spirit he got up and dressed.\n\nThere was hunting again on that day; and as the hounds were to meet near Chaldicotes, and to draw some coverts lying on the verge of the Chace, the ladies were to go in carriages through the drives of the forest, and Mr. Robarts was to escort them on horseback. Indeed it was one of those hunting-days got up rather for the ladies than for the sport. Great nuisances they are to steady, middle-aged hunting men; but the young fellows like them because they have thereby an opportunity of showing off their sporting finery, and of doing a little flirtation on horseback. The bishop, also, had been minded to be of the party; so, at least, he had said on the previous evening; and a place in one of the carriages had been set apart for him: but since that, he and Mrs. Proudie had discussed the matter in private, and at breakfast his lordship declared that he had changed his mind.\n\nMr. Sowerby was one of those men who are known to be very poor—as poor as debt can make a man—but who, nevertheless, enjoy all the luxuries which money can give. It was believed that he could not live in England out of jail but for his protection as a member of Parliament; and yet it seemed that there was no end to his horses and carriages, his servants and retinue. He had been at this work for a great many years, and practice, they say, makes perfect. Such companions are very dangerous. There is no cholera, no yellow-fever, no small-pox more contagious than debt. If one lives habitually among embarrassed men, one catches it to a certainty. No one had injured the community in this way more fatally than Mr. Sowerby. But still he carried on the game himself; and now on this morning carriages and horses thronged at his gate, as though he were as substantially rich as his friend the Duke of Omnium.\n\n“Robarts, my dear fellow,” said Mr. Sowerby, when they were well under way down one of the glades of the forest,—for the place where the hounds met was some four or five miles from the house of Chaldicotes,—“ride on with me a moment. I want to speak to you; and if I stay behind we shall never get to the hounds.” So Mark, who had come expressly to escort the ladies, rode on alongside of Mr. Sowerby in his pink coat.\n\n“My dear fellow, Fothergill tells me that you have some hesitation about going to Gatherum Castle.”\n\n“Well, I did decline, certainly. You know I am not a man of pleasure, as you are. I have some duties to attend to.”\n\n“Gammon!” said Mr. Sowerby; and as he said it he looked with a kind of derisive smile into the clergyman’s face.\n\n“It is easy enough to say that, Sowerby; and perhaps I have no right to expect that you should understand me.”\n\n“Ah, but I do understand you; and I say it is gammon. I would be the last man in the world to ridicule your scruples about duty, if this hesitation on your part arose from any such scruple. But answer me honestly, do you not know that such is not the case?”\n\n“I know nothing of the kind.”\n\n“Ah, but I think you do. If you persist in refusing this invitation will it not be because you are afraid of making Lady Lufton angry? I do not know what there can be in that woman that she is able to hold both you and Lufton in leading-strings.”\n\nRobarts, of course, denied the charge and protested that he was not to be taken back to his own parsonage by any fear of Lady Lufton. But though he made such protest with warmth, he knew that he did so ineffectually. Sowerby only smiled and said that the proof of the pudding was in the eating.\n\n“What is the good of a man keeping a curate if it be not to save him from that sort of drudgery?” he asked.\n\n“Drudgery! If I were a drudge how could I be here to-day?”\n\n“Well, Robarts, look here. I am speaking now, perhaps, with more of the energy of an old friend than circumstances fully warrant; but I am an older man than you, and as I have a regard for you I do not like to see you throw up a good game when it is in your hands.”\n\n“Oh, as far as that goes, Sowerby, I need hardly tell you that I appreciate your kindness.”\n\n“If you are content,” continued the man of the world, “to live at Framley all your life, and to warm yourself in the sunshine of the dowager there, why, in such case, it may perhaps be useless for you to extend the circle of your friends; but if you have higher ideas than these, I think you will be very wrong to omit the present opportunity of going to the duke’s. I never knew the duke go so much out of his way to be civil to a clergyman as he has done in this instance.”\n\n“I am sure I am very much obliged to him.”\n\n“The fact is, that you may, if you please, make yourself popular in the county; but you cannot do it by obeying all Lady Lufton’s behests. She is a dear old woman, I am sure.”\n\n“She is, Sowerby; and you would say so, if you knew her.”\n\n“I don’t doubt it; but it would not do for you or me to live exactly according to her ideas. Now, here, in this case, the bishop of the diocese is to be one of the party, and he has, I believe, already expressed a wish that you should be another.”\n\n“He asked me if I were going.”\n\n“Exactly; and Archdeacon Grantly will be there.”\n\n“Will he?” asked Mark. Now, that would be a great point gained, for Archdeacon Grantly was a close friend of Lady Lufton.\n\n“So I understand from Fothergill. Indeed, it will be very wrong of you not to go, and I tell you so plainly; and what is more, when you talk about your duty—you having a curate as you have—why, it is gammon.” These last words he spoke looking back over his shoulder as he stood up in his stirrups, for he had caught the eye of the huntsman, who was surrounded by his hounds, and was now trotting on to join him.\n\nDuring a great portion of the day, Mark found himself riding by the side of Mrs. Proudie, as that lady leaned back in her carriage. And Mrs. Proudie smiled on him graciously, though her daughter would not do so. Mrs. Proudie was fond of having an attendant clergyman; and as it was evident that Mr. Robarts lived among nice people—titled dowagers, members of Parliament, and people of that sort—she was quite willing to instal him as a sort of honorary chaplain pro tem.\n\n“I’ll tell you what we have settled, Mrs. Harold Smith and I,” said Mrs. Proudie to him. “This lecture at Barchester will be so late on Saturday evening, that you had all better come and dine with us.”\n\nMark bowed and thanked her, and declared that he should be very happy to make one of such a party. Even Lady Lufton could not object to this, although she was not especially fond of Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“And then they are to sleep at the hotel. It will really be too late for ladies to think of going back so far at this time of the year. I told Mrs. Harold Smith, and Miss Dunstable, too, that we could manage to make room at any rate for them. But they will not leave the other ladies; so they go to the hotel for that night. But, Mr. Robarts, the bishop will never allow you to stay at the inn, so of course you will take a bed at the palace.”\n\nIt immediately occurred to Mark that as the lecture was to be given on Saturday evening, the next morning would be Sunday; and, on that Sunday, he would have to preach at Chaldicotes. “I thought they were all going to return the same night,” said he.\n\n“Well, they did intend it; but you see Mrs. Smith is afraid.”\n\n“I should have to get back here on the Sunday morning, Mrs. Proudie.”\n\n“Ah, yes, that is bad—very bad, indeed. No one dislikes any interference with the Sabbath more than I do. Indeed, if I am particular about anything it is about that. But some works are works of necessity, Mr. Robarts; are they not? Now you must necessarily be back at Chaldicotes on Sunday morning!” And so the matter was settled. Mrs. Proudie was very firm in general in the matter of Sabbath-day observances; but when she had to deal with such persons as Mrs. Harold Smith, it was expedient that she should give way a little. “You can start as soon as it’s daylight, you know, if you like it, Mr. Robarts,” said Mrs. Proudie.\n\nThere was not much to boast of as to the hunting, but it was a very pleasant day for the ladies. The men rode up and down the grass roads through the Chace, sometimes in the greatest possible hurry as though they never could go quick enough; and then the coachmen would drive very fast also, though they did not know why, for a fast pace of movement is another of those contagious diseases. And then again the sportsmen would move at an undertaker’s pace, when the fox had traversed and the hounds would be at a loss to know which was the hunt and which was the heel; and then the carriage also would go slowly, and the ladies would stand up and talk. And then the time for lunch came; and altogether the day went by pleasantly enough.\n\n“And so that’s hunting, is it?” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n“Yes, that’s hunting,” said Mr. Sowerby.\n\n“I did not see any gentleman do anything that I could not do myself, except there was one young man slipped off into the mud; and I shouldn’t like that.”\n\n“But there was no breaking of bones, was there, my dear?” said Mrs. Harold Smith.\n\n“And nobody caught any foxes,” said Miss Dunstable. “The fact is, Mrs. Smith, that I don’t think much more of their sport than I do of their business. I shall take to hunting a pack of hounds myself after this.”\n\n“Do, my dear, and I’ll be your whipper-in. I wonder whether Mrs. Proudie would join us.”\n\n“I shall be writing to the duke to-night,” said Mr. Fothergill to Mark, as they were all riding up to the stable-yard together. “You will let me tell his grace that you will accept his invitation—will you not?”\n\n“Upon my word, the duke is very kind,” said Mark.\n\n“He is very anxious to know you, I can assure you,” said Fothergill.\n\nWhat could a young flattered fool of a parson do, but say that he would go? Mark did say that he would go; and in the course of the evening his friend Mr. Sowerby congratulated him, and the bishop joked with him and said that he knew that he would not give up good company so soon; and Miss Dunstable said she would make him her chaplain as soon as Parliament would allow quack doctors to have such articles—an allusion which Mark did not understand, till he learned that Miss Dunstable was herself the proprietress of the celebrated Oil of Lebanon, invented by her late respected father, and patented by him with such wonderful results in the way of accumulated fortune; and Mrs. Proudie made him quite one of their party, talking to him about all manner of church subjects; and then at last, even Miss Proudie smiled on him, when she learned that he had been thought worthy of a bed at a duke’s castle. And all the world seemed to be open to him.\n\nBut he could not make himself happy that evening. On the next morning he must write to his wife; and he could already see the look of painful sorrow which would fall upon his Fanny’s brow when she learned that her husband was going to be a guest at the Duke of Omnium’s. And he must tell her to send him money, and money was scarce. And then, as to Lady Lufton, should he send her some message, or should he not? In either case he must declare war against her. And then did he not owe everything to Lady Lufton? And thus in spite of all his triumphs he could not get himself to bed in a happy frame of mind.\n\nOn the next day, which was Friday, he postponed the disagreeable task of writing. Saturday would do as well; and on Saturday morning, before they all started for Barchester, he did write. And his letter ran as follows:—\n\nChaldicotes, — November, 185—.\n\nDEAREST LOVE,—You will be astonished when I tell you how gay we all are here, and what further dissipations are in store for us. The Arabins, as you supposed, are not of our party; but the Proudies are,—as you supposed also. Your suppositions are always right. And what will you think when I tell you that I am to sleep at the palace on Saturday? You know that there is to be a lecture in Barchester on that day. Well; we must all go, of course, as Harold Smith, one of our set here, is to give it. And now it turns out that we cannot get back the same night because there is no moon; and Mrs. Bishop would not allow that my cloth should be contaminated by an hotel;—very kind and considerate, is it not?\n\nBut I have a more astounding piece of news for you than this. There is to be a great party at Gatherum Castle next week, and they have talked me over into accepting an invitation which the duke sent expressly to me. I refused at first; but everybody here said that my doing so would be so strange; and then they all wanted to know my reason. When I came to render it, I did not know what reason I had to give. The bishop is going, and he thought it very odd that I should not go also, seeing that I was asked.\n\nI know what my own darling will think, and I know that she will not be pleased, and I must put off my defence till I return to her from this ogre-land,—if ever I do get back alive. But joking apart, Fanny, I think that I should have been wrong to stand out, when so much was said about it. I should have been seeming to take upon myself to sit in judgment upon the duke. I doubt if there be a single clergyman in the diocese, under fifty years of age, who would have refused the invitation under such circumstances,—unless it be Crawley, who is so mad on the subject that he thinks it almost wrong to take a walk out of his own parish.\n\nI must stay at Gatherum Castle over Sunday week—indeed, we only go there on Friday. I have written to Jones about the duties. I can make it up to him, as I know he wishes to go into Wales at Christmas. My wanderings will all be over then, and he may go for a couple of months if he pleases. I suppose you will take my classes in the school on Sunday, as well as your own; but pray make them have a good fire. If this is too much for you, make Mrs. Podgens take the boys. Indeed I think that will be better.\n\nOf course you will tell her ladyship of my whereabouts. Tell her from me, that as regards the bishop, as well as regarding another great personage, the colour has been laid on perhaps a little too thickly. Not that Lady Lufton would ever like him. Make her understand that my going to the duke’s has almost become a matter of conscience with me. I have not known how to make it appear that it would be right for me to refuse, without absolutely making a party matter of it. I saw that it would be said, that I, coming from Lady Lufton’s parish, could not go to the Duke of Omnium’s. This I did not choose.\n\nI find that I shall want a little more money before I leave here, five or ten pounds—say ten pounds. If you cannot spare it, get it from Davis. He owes me more than that, a good deal.\n\nAnd now, God bless and preserve you, my own love. Kiss my darling bairns for papa, and give them my blessing.\n\nAlways and ever your own,\n\nAnd then there was written, on an outside scrap which was folded round the full-written sheet of paper, “Make it as smooth at Framley Court as possible.”\n\nHowever strong, and reasonable, and unanswerable the body of Mark’s letter may have been, all his hesitation, weakness, doubt, and fear, were expressed in this short postscript.\n\n# CHAPTER V. AMANTIUM IRÆ AMORIS INTEGRATIO.\n\nAnd now, with my reader’s consent, I will follow the postman with that letter to Framley; not by its own circuitous route indeed, or by the same mode of conveyance; for that letter went into Barchester by the Courcy night mail-cart, which, on its road, passes through the villages of Uffley and Chaldicotes, reaching Barchester in time for the up mail-train to London. By that train, the letter was sent towards the metropolis as far as the junction of the Barset branch line, but there it was turned in its course, and came down again by the main line as far as Silverbridge; at which place, between six and seven in the morning, it was shouldered by the Framley footpost messenger, and in due course delivered at the Framley Parsonage exactly as Mrs. Robarts had finished reading prayers to the four servants. Or, I should say rather, that such would in its usual course have been that letter’s destiny. As it was, however, it reached Silverbridge on Sunday, and lay there till the Monday, as the Framley people have declined their Sunday post. And then again, when the letter was delivered at the parsonage, on that wet Monday morning, Mrs. Robarts was not at home. As we are all aware, she was staying with her ladyship at Framley Court.\n\n“Oh, but it’s mortial wet,” said the shivering postman as he handed in that and the vicar’s newspaper. The vicar was a man of the world, and took the Jupiter.\n\n“Come in, Robin postman, and warm theeself awhile,” said Jemima the cook, pushing a stool a little to one side, but still well in front of the big kitchen fire.\n\n“Well, I dudna jist know how it’ll be. The wery ’edges ’as eyes and tells on me in Silverbridge, if I so much as stops to pick a blackberry.”\n\n“There bain’t no hedges here, mon, nor yet no blackberries; so sit thee down and warm theeself. That’s better nor blackberries I’m thinking,” and she handed him a bowl of tea with a slice of buttered toast.\n\nRobin postman took the proffered tea, put his dripping hat on the ground, and thanked Jemima cook. “But I dudna jist know how it’ll be,” said he; “only it do pour so tarnation heavy.” Which among us, O my readers, could have withstood that temptation?\n\nSuch was the circuitous course of Mark’s letter; but as it left Chaldicotes on Saturday evening, and reached Mrs. Robarts on the following morning, or would have done, but for that intervening Sunday, doing all its peregrinations during the night, it may be held that its course of transport was not inconveniently arranged. We, however, will travel by a much shorter route.\n\nRobin, in the course of his daily travels, passed, first the post-office at Framley, then the Framley Court back entrance, and then the vicar’s house, so that on this wet morning Jemima cook was not able to make use of his services in transporting this letter back to her mistress; for Robin had got another village before him, expectant of its letters.\n\n“Why didn’t thee leave it, mon, with Mr. Applejohn at the Court?” Mr. Applejohn was the butler who took the letter-bag. “Thee know’st as how missus was there.”\n\nAnd then Robin, mindful of the tea and toast, explained to her courteously how the law made it imperative on him to bring the letter to the very house that was indicated, let the owner of the letter be where she might; and he laid down the law very satisfactorily with sundry long-worded quotations. Not to much effect, however, for the housemaid called him an oaf; and Robin would decidedly have had the worst of it had not the gardener come in and taken his part. “They women knows nothin’, and understands nothin’,” said the gardener. “Give us hold of the letter. I’ll take it up to the house. It’s the master’s fist.” And then Robin postman went on one way, and the gardener, he went the other. The gardener never disliked an excuse for going up to the Court gardens, even on so wet a day as this.\n\nMrs. Robarts was sitting over the drawing-room fire with Lady Meredith, when her husband’s letter was brought to her. The Framley Court letter-bag had been discussed at breakfast; but that was now nearly an hour since, and Lady Lufton, as was her wont, was away in her own room writing her own letters, and looking after her own matters: for Lady Lufton was a person who dealt in figures herself, and understood business almost as well as Harold Smith. And on that morning she also had received a letter which had displeased her not a little. Whence arose this displeasure neither Mrs. Robarts nor Lady Meredith knew; but her ladyship’s brow had grown black at breakfast time; she had bundled up an ominous-looking epistle into her bag without speaking of it, and had left the room immediately that breakfast was over.\n\n“There’s something wrong,” said Sir George.\n\n“Mamma does fret herself so much about Ludovic’s money matters,” said Lady Meredith. Ludovic was Lord Lufton,—Ludovic Lufton, Baron Lufton of Lufton, in the county of Oxfordshire.\n\n“And yet I don’t think Lufton gets much astray,” said Sir George, as he sauntered out of the room. “Well, Justy; we’ll put off going then till to-morrow; but remember, it must be the first train.” Lady Meredith said she would remember, and then they went into the drawing-room, and there Mrs. Robarts received her letter.\n\nFanny, when she read it, hardly at first realized to herself the idea that her husband, the clergyman of Framley, the family clerical friend of Lady Lufton’s establishment, was going to stay with the Duke of Omnium. It was so thoroughly understood at Framley Court that the duke and all belonging to him was noxious and damnable. He was a Whig, he was a bachelor, he was a gambler, he was immoral in every way, he was a man of no church principle, a corrupter of youth, a sworn foe of young wives, a swallower up of small men’s patrimonies; a man whom mothers feared for their sons, and sisters for their brothers; and worse again, whom fathers had cause to fear for their daughters, and brothers for their sisters;—a man who, with his belongings, dwelt, and must dwell, poles asunder from Lady Lufton and her belongings!\n\nAnd it must be remembered that all these evil things were fully believed by Mrs. Robarts. Could it really be that her husband was going to dwell in the halls of Apollyon, to shelter himself beneath the wings of this very Lucifer? A cloud of sorrow settled upon her face, and then she read the letter again very slowly, not omitting the tell-tale postscript.\n\n“Oh, Justinia!” at last she said.\n\n“What, have you got bad news, too?”\n\n“I hardly know how to tell you what has occurred. There; I suppose you had better read it;” and she handed her husband’s epistle to Lady Meredith,—keeping back, however, the postscript.\n\n“What on earth will her ladyship say now?” said Lady Meredith, as she folded the paper, and replaced it in the envelope.\n\n“What had I better do, Justinia? how had I better tell her?” And then the two ladies put their heads together, bethinking themselves how they might best deprecate the wrath of Lady Lufton. It had been arranged that Mrs. Robarts should go back to the parsonage after lunch, and she had persisted in her intention after it had been settled that the Merediths were to stay over that evening. Lady Meredith now advised her friend to carry out this determination without saying anything about her husband’s terrible iniquities, and then to send the letter up to Lady Lufton as soon as she reached the parsonage. “Mamma will never know that you received it here,” said Lady Meredith.\n\nBut Mrs. Robarts would not consent to this. Such a course seemed to her to be cowardly. She knew that her husband was doing wrong; she felt that he knew it himself; but still it was necessary that she should defend him. However terrible might be the storm, it must break upon her own head. So she at once went up and tapped at Lady Lufton’s private door; and as she did so Lady Meredith followed her.\n\n“Come in,” said Lady Lufton, and the voice did not sound soft and pleasant. When they entered, they found her sitting at her little writing table, with her head resting on her arm, and that letter which she had received that morning was lying open on the table before her. Indeed there were two letters now there, one from a London lawyer to herself, and the other from her son to that London lawyer. It needs only be explained that the subject of those letters was the immediate sale of that outlying portion of the Lufton property in Oxfordshire, as to which Mr. Sowerby once spoke. Lord Lufton had told the lawyer that the thing must be done at once, adding that his friend Robarts would have explained the whole affair to his mother. And then the lawyer had written to Lady Lufton, as indeed was necessary; but unfortunately Lady Lufton had not hitherto heard a word of the matter.\n\nIn her eyes the sale of family property was horrible; the fact that a young man with some fifteen or twenty thousand a year should require subsidiary money was horrible; that her own son should have not written to her himself was horrible; and it was also horrible that her own pet, the clergyman whom she had brought there to be her son’s friend, should be mixed up in the matter,—should be cognizant of it while she was not cognizant,—should be employed in it as a go-between and agent in her son’s bad courses. It was all horrible, and Lady Lufton was sitting there with a black brow and an uneasy heart. As regarded our poor parson, we may say that in this matter he was blameless, except that he had hitherto lacked the courage to execute his friend’s commission.\n\n“What is it, Fanny?” said Lady Lufton as soon as the door was opened; “I should have been down in half-an-hour, if you wanted me, Justinia.”\n\n“Fanny has received a letter which makes her wish to speak to you at once,” said Lady Meredith. “What letter, Fanny?”\n\nPoor Fanny’s heart was in her mouth; she held it in her hand, but had not yet quite made up her mind whether she would show it bodily to Lady Lufton.\n\n“From Mr. Robarts,” she said.\n\n“Well; I suppose he is going to stay another week at Chaldicotes. For my part I should be as well pleased;” and Lady Lufton’s voice was not friendly, for she was thinking of that farm in Oxfordshire. The imprudence of the young is very sore to the prudence of their elders. No woman could be less covetous, less grasping than Lady Lufton; but the sale of a portion of the old family property was to her as the loss of her own heart’s blood.\n\n“Here is the letter, Lady Lufton; perhaps you had better read it;” and Fanny handed it to her, again keeping back the postscript. She had read and re-read the letter downstairs, but could not make out whether her husband had intended her to show it. From the line of the argument she thought that he must have done so. At any rate he said for himself more than she could say for him, and so, probably, it was best that her ladyship should see it.\n\nLady Lufton took it, and read it, and her face grew blacker and blacker. Her mind was set against the writer before she began it, and every word in it tended to make her feel more estranged from him. “Oh, he is going to the palace, is he?—well; he must choose his own friends. Harold Smith one of his party! It’s a pity, my dear, he did not see Miss Proudie before he met you, he might have lived to be the bishop’s chaplain. Gatherum Castle! You don’t mean to tell me that he is going there? Then I tell you fairly, Fanny, that I have done with him.”\n\n“Oh, Lady Lufton, don’t say that,” said Mrs. Robarts, with tears in her eyes.\n\n“Mamma, mamma, don’t speak in that way,” said Lady Meredith.\n\n“But my dear, what am I to say? I must speak in that way. You would not wish me to speak falsehoods, would you? A man must choose for himself, but he can’t live with two different sets of people; at least, not if I belong to one and the Duke of Omnium to the other. The bishop going indeed! If there be anything that I hate it is hypocrisy.”\n\n“There is no hypocrisy in that, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“But I say there is, Fanny. Very strange, indeed! ‘Put off his defence!’ Why should a man need any defence to his wife if he acts in a straightforward way? His own language condemns him: ‘Wrong to stand out!’ Now, will either of you tell me that Mr. Robarts would really have thought it wrong to refuse that invitation? I say that that is hypocrisy. There is no other word for it.”\n\nBy this time the poor wife, who had been in tears, was wiping them away and preparing for action. Lady Lufton’s extreme severity gave her courage. She knew that it behoved her to fight for her husband when he was thus attacked. Had Lady Lufton been moderate in her remarks Mrs. Robarts would not have had a word to say.\n\n“My husband may have been ill-judged,” she said, “but he is no hypocrite.”\n\n“Very well, my dear, I dare say you know better than I; but to me it looks extremely like hypocrisy: eh, Justinia?”\n\n“Oh, mamma, do be moderate.”\n\n“Moderate! That’s all very well. How is one to moderate one’s feelings when one has been betrayed?”\n\n“You do not mean that Mr. Robarts has betrayed you?” said the wife.\n\n“Oh, no; of course not.” And then she went on reading the letter: “‘Seem to have been standing in judgment upon the duke.’ Might he not use the same argument as to going into any house in the kingdom, however infamous? We must all stand in judgment one upon another in that sense. ‘Crawley!’ Yes; if he were a little more like Mr. Crawley it would be a good thing for me, and for the parish, and for you too, my dear. God forgive me for bringing him here; that’s all.”\n\n“Lady Lufton, I must say that you are very hard upon him—very hard. I did not expect it from such a friend.”\n\n“My dear, you ought to know me well enough to be sure that I shall speak my mind. ‘Written to Jones’—yes; it is easy enough to write to poor Jones. He had better write to Jones, and bid him do the whole duty. Then he can go and be the duke’s domestic chaplain.”\n\n“I believe my husband does as much of his own duty as any clergyman in the whole diocese,” said Mrs. Robarts, now again in tears.\n\n“And you are to take his work in the school; you and Mrs. Podgens. What with his curate and his wife and Mrs. Podgens, I don’t see why he should come back at all.”\n\n“Oh, mamma,” said Justinia, “pray, pray don’t be so harsh to her.”\n\n“Let me finish it, my dear;—oh, here I come. ‘Tell her ladyship my whereabouts.’ He little thought you’d show me this letter.”\n\n“Didn’t he?” said Mrs. Robarts, putting out her hand to get it back, but in vain. “I thought it was for the best; I did indeed.”\n\n“I had better finish it now, if you please. What is this? How does he dare send his ribald jokes to me in such a matter? No, I do not suppose I ever shall like Dr. Proudie; I have never expected it. A matter of conscience with him! Well—well, well. Had I not read it myself, I could not have believed it of him. I would not positively have believed it. ‘Coming from my parish he could not go to the Duke of Omnium!’ And it is what I would wish to have said. People fit for this parish should not be fit for the Duke of Omnium’s house. And I had trusted that he would have this feeling more strongly than any one else in it. I have been deceived—that’s all.”\n\n“He has done nothing to deceive you, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“I hope he will not have deceived you, my dear. ‘More money;’ yes, it is probable that he will want more money. There is your letter, Fanny. I am very sorry for it. I can say nothing more.” And she folded up the letter and gave it back to Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“I thought it right to show it you,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“It did not much matter whether you did or no; of course I must have been told.”\n\n“He especially begs me to tell you.”\n\n“Why, yes; he could not very well have kept me in the dark in such a matter. He could not neglect his own work, and go and live with gamblers and adulterers at the Duke of Omnium’s without my knowing it.”\n\nAnd now Fanny Robarts’s cup was full, full to the overflowing. When she heard these words she forgot all about Lady Lufton, all about Lady Meredith, and remembered only her husband,—that he was her husband, and, in spite of his faults, a good and loving husband;—and that other fact also she remembered, that she was his wife.\n\n“Lady Lufton,” she said, “you forget yourself in speaking in that way of my husband.”\n\n“What!” said her ladyship; “you are to show me such a letter as that, and I am not to tell you what I think?”\n\n“Not if you think such hard things as that. Even you are not justified in speaking to me in that way, and I will not hear it.”\n\n“Heighty-tighty!” said her ladyship.\n\n“Whether or no he is right in going to the Duke of Omnium’s, I will not pretend to judge. He is the judge of his own actions, and neither you nor I.”\n\n“And when he leaves you with the butcher’s bill unpaid and no money to buy shoes for the children, who will be the judge then?”\n\n“Not you, Lady Lufton. If such bad days should ever come—and neither you nor I have a right to expect them—I will not come to you in my troubles; not after this.”\n\n“Very well, my dear. You may go to the Duke of Omnium if that suits you better.”\n\n“Fanny, come away,” said Lady Meredith. “Why should you try to anger my mother?”\n\n“I don’t want to anger her; but I won’t hear him abused in that way without speaking up for him. If I don’t defend him, who will? Lady Lufton has said terrible things about him; and they are not true.”\n\n“Oh, Fanny!” said Justinia.\n\n“Very well, very well!” said Lady Lufton. “This is the sort of return that one gets.”\n\n“I don’t know what you mean by return, Lady Lufton: but would you wish me to stand by quietly and hear such things said of my husband? He does not live with such people as you have named. He does not neglect his duties. If every clergyman were as much in his parish, it would be well for some of them. And in going to such a house as the Duke of Omnium’s it does make a difference that he goes there in company with the bishop. I can’t explain why, but I know that it does.”\n\n“Especially when the bishop is coupled up with the devil, as Mr. Robarts has done,” said Lady Lufton; “he can join the duke with them and then they’ll stand for the three Graces, won’t they, Justinia?” And Lady Lufton laughed a bitter little laugh at her own wit.\n\n“I suppose I may go now, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“Oh, yes, certainly, my dear.”\n\n“I am sorry if I have made you angry with me; but I will not allow any one to speak against Mr. Robarts without answering them. You have been very unjust to him; and even though I do anger you, I must say so.”\n\n“Come, Fanny; this is too bad,” said Lady Lufton. “You have been scolding me for the last half-hour because I would not congratulate you on this new friend that your husband has made, and now you are going to begin it all over again. That is more than I can stand. If you have nothing else particular to say, you might as well leave me.” And Lady Lufton’s face as she spoke was unbending, severe, and harsh.\n\nMrs. Robarts had never before been so spoken to by her old friend; indeed she had never been so spoken to by any one, and she hardly knew how to bear herself.\n\n“Very well, Lady Lufton,” she said; “then I will go. Good-bye.”\n\n“Good-bye,” said Lady Lufton, and turning herself to her table she began to arrange her papers. Fanny had never before left Framley Court to go back to her own parsonage without a warm embrace. Now she was to do so without even having her hand taken. Had it come to this, that there was absolutely to be a quarrel between them,—a quarrel for ever?\n\n“Fanny is going, you know, mamma,” said Lady Meredith. “She will be home before you are down again.”\n\n“I cannot help it, my dear. Fanny must do as she pleases. I am not to be the judge of her actions. She has just told me so.”\n\nMrs. Robarts had said nothing of the kind, but she was far too proud to point this out. So with a gentle step she retreated through the door, and then Lady Meredith, having tried what a conciliatory whisper with her mother would do, followed her. Alas, the conciliatory whisper was altogether ineffectual!\n\nThe two ladies said nothing as they descended the stairs, but when they had regained the drawing-room they looked with blank horror into each other’s faces. What were they to do now? Of such a tragedy as this they had had no remotest preconception. Was it absolutely the case that Fanny Robarts was to walk out of Lady Lufton’s house as a declared enemy,—she who, before her marriage as well as since, had been almost treated as an adopted daughter of the family?\n\n“Oh, Fanny, why did you answer my mother in that way?” said Lady Meredith. “You saw that she was vexed. She had other things to vex her besides this about Mr. Robarts.”\n\n“And would not you answer any one who attacked Sir George?”\n\n“No, not my own mother. I would let her say what she pleased, and leave Sir George to fight his own battles.”\n\n“Ah, but it is different with you. You are her daughter, and Sir George—she would not dare to speak in that way as to Sir George’s doings.”\n\n“Indeed she would, if it pleased her. I am sorry I let you go up to her.”\n\n“It is as well that it should be over, Justinia. As those are her thoughts about Mr. Robarts, it is quite as well that we should know them. Even for all that I owe to her, and all the love I bear to you, I will not come to this house if I am to hear my husband abused;—not into any house.”\n\n“My dearest Fanny, we all know what happens when two angry people get together.”\n\n“I was not angry when I went up to her; not in the least.”\n\n“It is no good looking back. What are we to do now, Fanny?”\n\n“I suppose I had better go home,” said Mrs. Robarts. “I will go and put my things up, and then I will send James for them.”\n\n“Wait till after lunch, and then you will be able to kiss my mother before you leave us.”\n\n“No, Justinia; I cannot wait. I must answer Mr. Robarts by this post, and I must think what I have to say to him. I could not write that letter here, and the post goes at four.” And Mrs. Robarts got up from her chair, preparatory to her final departure.\n\n“I shall come to you before dinner,” said Lady Meredith; “and if I can bring you good tidings, I shall expect you to come back here with me. It is out of the question that I should go away from Framley leaving you and my mother at enmity with each other.”\n\nTo this Mrs. Robarts made no answer; and in a very few minutes afterwards she was in her own nursery, kissing her children, and teaching the elder one to say something about papa. But, even as she taught him, the tears stood in her eyes, and the little fellow knew that everything was not right.\n\nAnd there she sat till about two, doing little odds and ends of things for the children, and allowing that occupation to stand as an excuse to her for not commencing her letter. But then there remained only two hours to her, and it might be that the letter would be difficult in the writing—would require thought and changes, and must needs be copied, perhaps more than once. As to the money, that she had in the house—as much, at least, as Mark now wanted, though the sending of it would leave her nearly penniless. She could, however, in case of personal need, resort to Davis as desired by him.\n\nSo she got out her desk in the drawing-room and sat down and wrote her letter. It was difficult, though she found that it hardly took so long as she expected. It was difficult, for she felt bound to tell him the truth; and yet she was anxious not to spoil all his pleasure among his friends. She told him, however, that Lady Lufton was very angry, “unreasonably angry, I must say,” she put in, in order to show that she had not sided against him. “And indeed we have quite quarrelled, and this has made me unhappy, as it will you, dearest; I know that. But we both know how good she is at heart, and Justinia thinks that she had other things to trouble her; and I hope it will all be made up before you come home; only, dearest Mark, pray do not be longer than you said in your last letter.” And then there were three or four paragraphs about the babies and two about the schools, which I may as well omit.\n\nShe had just finished her letter, and was carefully folding it for its envelope, with the two whole five-pound notes imprudently placed within it, when she heard a footstep on the gravel path which led up from a small wicket to the front-door. The path ran near the drawing-room window, and she was just in time to catch a glimpse of the last fold of a passing cloak. “It is Justinia,” she said to herself; and her heart became disturbed at the idea of again discussing the morning’s adventure. “What am I to do,” she had said to herself before, “if she wants me to beg her pardon? I will not own before her that he is in the wrong.”\n\nAnd then the door opened—for the visitor made her entrance without the aid of any servant—and Lady Lufton herself stood before her. “Fanny,” she said at once, “I have come to beg your pardon.”\n\n“Oh, Lady Lufton!”\n\n“I was very much harassed when you came to me just now;—by more things than one, my dear. But, nevertheless, I should not have spoken to you of your husband as I did, and so I have come to beg your pardon.”\n\nMrs. Robarts was past answering by the time that this was said,—past answering at least in words; so she jumped up and, with her eyes full of tears, threw herself into her old friend’s arms. “Oh, Lady Lufton!” she sobbed forth again.\n\n“You will forgive me, won’t you?” said her ladyship, as she returned her young friend’s caress. “Well, that’s right. I have not been at all happy since you left my den this morning, and I don’t suppose you have. But, Fanny, dearest, we love each other too well and know each other too thoroughly, to have a long quarrel, don’t we?”\n\n“Oh, yes, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“Of course we do. Friends are not to be picked up on the road-side every day; nor are they to be thrown away lightly. And now sit down, my love, and let us have a little talk. There, I must take my bonnet off. You have pulled the strings so that you have almost choked me.” And Lady Lufton deposited her bonnet on the table and seated herself comfortably in the corner of the sofa.\n\n“My dear,” she said, “there is no duty which any woman owes to any other human being at all equal to that which she owes to her husband, and, therefore, you were quite right to stand up for Mr. Robarts this morning.”\n\nUpon this Mrs. Robarts said nothing, but she got her hand within that of her ladyship and gave it a slight squeeze.\n\n“And I loved you for what you were doing all the time. I did, my dear; though you were a little fierce, you know. Even Justinia admits that, and she has been at me ever since you went away. And indeed, I did not know that it was in you to look in that way out of those pretty eyes of yours.”\n\n“Oh, Lady Lufton!”\n\n“But I looked fierce enough too myself, I dare say; so we’ll say nothing more about that; will we? But now, about this good man of yours?”\n\n“Dear Lady Lufton, you must forgive him.”\n\n“Well: as you ask me, I will. We’ll have nothing more said about the duke, either now or when he comes back; not a word. Let me see—he’s to be back;—when is it?”\n\n“Wednesday week, I think.”\n\n“Ah, Wednesday. Well, tell him to come and dine up at the house on Wednesday. He’ll be in time, I suppose, and there shan’t be a word said about this horrid duke.”\n\n“I am so much obliged to you, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“But look here, my dear; believe me, he’s better off without such friends.”\n\n“Oh, I know he is; much better off.”\n\n“Well, I’m glad you admit that, for I thought you seemed to be in favour of the duke.”\n\n“Oh, no, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“That’s right, then. And now, if you’ll take my advice, you’ll use your influence, as a good, dear sweet wife as you are, to prevent his going there any more. I’m an old woman and he is a young man, and it’s very natural that he should think me behind the times. I’m not angry at that. But he’ll find that it’s better for him, better for him in every way, to stick to his old friends. It will be better for his peace of mind, better for his character as a clergyman, better for his pocket, better for his children and for you,—and better for his eternal welfare. The duke is not such a companion as he should seek;—nor if he is sought, should he allow himself to be led away.”\n\nAnd then Lady Lufton ceased, and Fanny Robarts kneeling at her feet sobbed, with her face hidden on her friend’s knees. She had not a word now to say as to her husband’s capability of judging for himself.\n\n“And now I must be going again; but Justinia has made me promise,—promise, mind you, most solemnly, that I would have you back to dinner to-night,—by force if necessary. It was the only way I could make my peace with her; so you must not leave me in the lurch.” Of course, Fanny said that she would go and dine at Framley Court.\n\n“And you must not send that letter, by any means,” said her ladyship as she was leaving the room, poking with her umbrella at the epistle, which lay directed on Mrs. Robarts’s desk. “I can understand very well what it contains. You must alter it altogether, my dear.” And then Lady Lufton went.\n\nMrs. Robarts instantly rushed to her desk and tore open her letter. She looked at her watch and it was past four. She had hardly begun another when the postman came. “Oh, Mary,” she said, “do make him wait. If he’ll wait a quarter of an hour I’ll give him a shilling.”\n\n“There’s no need of that, ma’am. Let him have a glass of beer.”\n\n“Very well, Mary; but don’t give him too much, for fear he should drop the letters about. I’ll be ready in ten minutes.”\n\nAnd in five minutes she had scrawled a very different sort of a letter. But he might want the money immediately, so she would not delay it for a day.\n\n# CHAPTER VI. MR. HAROLD SMITH’S LECTURE.\n\nOn the whole the party at Chaldicotes was very pleasant, and the time passed away quickly enough. Mr. Robarts’s chief friend there, independently of Mr. Sowerby, was Miss Dunstable, who seemed to take a great fancy to him, whereas she was not very accessible to the blandishments of Mr. Supplehouse, nor more specially courteous even to her host than good manners required of her. But then Mr. Supplehouse and Mr. Sowerby were both bachelors, while Mark Robarts was a married man.\n\nWith Mr. Sowerby Robarts had more than one communication respecting Lord Lufton and his affairs, which he would willingly have avoided had it been possible. Sowerby was one of those men who are always mixing up business with pleasure, and who have usually some scheme in their mind which requires forwarding. Men of this class have, as a rule, no daily work, no regular routine of labour; but it may be doubted whether they do not toil much more incessantly than those who have.\n\n“Lufton is so dilatory,” Mr. Sowerby said. “Why did he not arrange this at once, when he promised it? And then he is so afraid of that old woman at Framley Court. Well, my dear fellow, say what you will; she is an old woman and she’ll never be younger. But do write to Lufton and tell him that this delay is inconvenient to me; he’ll do anything for you, I know.”\n\nMark said that he would write, and, indeed, did do so; but he did not at first like the tone of the conversation into which he was dragged. It was very painful to him to hear Lady Lufton called an old woman, and hardly less so to discuss the propriety of Lord Lufton’s parting with his property. This was irksome to him, till habit made it easy. But by degrees his feelings became less acute, and he accustomed himself to his friend Sowerby’s mode of talking.\n\nAnd then on the Saturday afternoon they all went over to Barchester. Harold Smith during the last forty-eight hours had become crammed to overflowing with Sarawak, Labuan, New Guinea, and the Salomon Islands. As is the case with all men labouring under temporary specialities, he for the time had faith in nothing else, and was not content that any one near him should have any other faith. They called him Viscount Papua and Baron Borneo; and his wife, who headed the joke against him, insisted on having her title. Miss Dunstable swore that she would wed none but a South Sea islander; and to Mark was offered the income and duties of Bishop of Spices. Nor did the Proudie family set themselves against these little sarcastic quips with any overwhelming severity. It is sweet to unbend oneself at the proper opportunity, and this was the proper opportunity for Mrs. Proudie’s unbending. No mortal can be seriously wise at all hours; and in these happy hours did that usually wise mortal, the bishop, lay aside for awhile his serious wisdom.\n\n“We think of dining at five to-morrow, my Lady Papua,” said the facetious bishop; “will that suit his lordship and the affairs of State? he! he! he!” And the good prelate laughed at the fun.\n\nHow pleasantly young men and women of fifty or thereabouts can joke and flirt and poke their fun about, laughing and holding their sides, dealing in little innuendoes and rejoicing in nicknames when they have no Mentors of twenty-five or thirty near them to keep them in order. The vicar of Framley might perhaps have been regarded as such a Mentor, were it not for that capability of adapting himself to the company immediately around him on which he so much piqued himself. He therefore also talked to my Lady Papua, and was jocose about the Baron,—not altogether to the satisfaction of Mr. Harold Smith himself.\n\nFor Mr. Harold Smith was in earnest and did not quite relish these jocundities. He had an idea that he could in about three months talk the British world into civilizing New Guinea, and that the world of Barsetshire would be made to go with him by one night’s efforts. He did not understand why others should be less serious, and was inclined to resent somewhat stiffly the amenities of our friend Mark.\n\n“We must not keep the Baron waiting,” said Mark, as they were preparing to start for Barchester.\n\n“I don’t know what you mean by the Baron, sir,” said Harold Smith. “But perhaps the joke will be against you, when you are getting up into your pulpit to-morrow and sending the hat round among the clodhoppers of Chaldicotes.”\n\n“Those who live in glass houses shouldn’t throw stones; eh, Baron?” said Miss Dunstable. “Mr. Robarts’s sermon will be too near akin to your lecture to allow of his laughing.”\n\n“If we can do nothing towards instructing the outer world till it’s done by the parsons,” said Harold Smith, “the outer world will have to wait a long time, I fear.”\n\n“Nobody can do anything of that kind short of a member of Parliament and a would-be minister,” whispered Mrs. Harold.\n\nAnd so they were all very pleasant together, in spite of a little fencing with edge-tools; and at three o’clock the cortége of carriages started for Barchester, that of the bishop, of course, leading the way. His lordship, however, was not in it.\n\n“Mrs. Proudie, I’m sure you’ll let me go with you,” said Miss Dunstable, at the last moment, as she came down the big stone steps. “I want to hear the rest of that story about Mr. Slope.”\n\nNow this upset everything. The bishop was to have gone with his wife, Mrs. Smith, and Mark Robarts; and Mr. Sowerby had so arranged matters that he could have accompanied Miss Dunstable in his phaeton. But no one ever dreamed of denying Miss Dunstable anything. Of course Mark gave way; but it ended in the bishop declaring that he had no special predilection for his own carriage, which he did in compliance with a glance from his wife’s eye. Then other changes of course followed, and, at last, Mr. Sowerby and Harold Smith were the joint occupants of the phaeton.\n\nThe poor lecturer, as he seated himself, made some remark such as those he had been making for the last two days—for out of a full heart the mouth speaketh. But he spoke to an impatient listener. “D—— the South Sea islanders,” said Mr. Sowerby. “You’ll have it all your own way in a few minutes, like a bull in a china-shop; but for Heaven’s sake let us have a little peace till that time comes.” It appeared that Mr. Sowerby’s little plan of having Miss Dunstable for his companion was not quite insignificant; and, indeed, it may be said that but few of his little plans were so. At the present moment he flung himself back in the carriage and prepared for sleep. He could further no plan of his by a tête-à-tête conversation with his brother-in-law.\n\nAnd then Mrs. Proudie began her story about Mr. Slope, or rather recommenced it. She was very fond of talking about this gentleman, who had once been her pet chaplain but was now her bitterest foe; and in telling the story, she had sometimes to whisper to Miss Dunstable, for there were one or two fie-fie little anecdotes about a married lady, not altogether fit for young Mr. Robarts’s ears. But Mrs. Harold Smith insisted on having them out loud, and Miss Dunstable would gratify that lady in spite of Mrs. Proudie’s winks.\n\n“What, kissing her hand, and he a clergyman!” said Miss Dunstable. “I did not think they ever did such things, Mr. Robarts.”\n\n“Still waters run deepest,” said Mrs. Harold Smith.\n\n“Hush-h-h,” looked, rather than spoke, Mrs. Proudie. “The grief of spirit which that bad man caused me nearly broke my heart, and all the while, you know, he was courting—” and then Mrs. Proudie whispered a name.\n\n“What, the dean’s wife!” shouted Miss Dunstable, in a voice which made the coachman of the next carriage give a chuck to his horses as he overheard her.\n\n“The archdeacon’s sister-in-law!” screamed Mrs. Harold Smith.\n\n“What might he not have attempted next?” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n“She wasn’t the dean’s wife then, you know,” said Mrs. Proudie, explaining.\n\n“Well, you’ve a gay set in the chapter, I must say,” said Miss Dunstable. “You ought to make one of them in Barchester, Mr. Robarts.”\n\n“Only perhaps Mrs. Robarts might not like it,” said Mrs. Harold Smith.\n\n“And then the schemes which he tried on with the bishop!” said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“It’s all fair in love and war, you know,” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n“But he little knew whom he had to deal with when he began that,” said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“The bishop was too many for him,” suggested Mrs. Harold Smith, very maliciously.\n\n“If the bishop was not, somebody else was; and he was obliged to leave Barchester in utter disgrace. He has since married the wife of some tallow-chandler.”\n\n“The wife!” said Miss Dunstable. “What a man!”\n\n“Widow, I mean; but it’s all one to him.”\n\n“The gentleman was clearly born when Venus was in the ascendant,” said Mrs. Smith. “You clergymen usually are, I believe, Mr. Robarts.” So that Mrs. Proudie’s carriage was by no means the dullest as they drove into Barchester that day; and by degrees our friend Mark became accustomed to his companions, and before they reached the palace he acknowledged to himself that Miss Dunstable was very good fun.\n\nWe cannot linger over the bishop’s dinner, though it was very good of its kind; and as Mr. Sowerby contrived to sit next to Miss Dunstable, thereby overturning a little scheme made by Mr. Supplehouse, he again shone forth in unclouded good humour. But Mr. Harold Smith became impatient immediately on the withdrawal of the cloth. The lecture was to begin at seven, and according to his watch that hour had already come. He declared that Sowerby and Supplehouse were endeavouring to delay matters in order that the Barchesterians might become vexed and impatient; and so the bishop was not allowed to exercise his hospitality in true episcopal fashion.\n\n“You forget, Sowerby,” said Supplehouse, “that the world here for the last fortnight has been looking forward to nothing else.”\n\n“The world shall be gratified at once,” said Mrs. Harold, obeying a little nod from Mrs. Proudie. “Come, my dear,” and she took hold of Miss Dunstable’s arm, “don’t let us keep Barchester waiting. We shall be ready in a quarter-of-an-hour, shall we not, Mrs. Proudie?” and so they sailed off.\n\n“And we shall have time for one glass of claret,” said the bishop.\n\n“There; that’s seven by the cathedral,” said Harold Smith, jumping up from his chair as he heard the clock. “If the people have come it would not be right in me to keep them waiting, and I shall go.”\n\n“Just one glass of claret, Mr. Smith, and we’ll be off,” said the bishop.\n\n“Those women will keep me an hour,” said Harold, filling his glass, and drinking it standing. “They do it on purpose.” He was thinking of his wife, but it seemed to the bishop as though his guest were actually speaking of Mrs. Proudie.\n\nIt was rather late when they all found themselves in the big room of the Mechanics’ Institute; but I do not know whether this on the whole did them any harm. Most of Mr. Smith’s hearers, excepting the party from the palace, were Barchester tradesmen with their wives and families; and they waited, not impatiently, for the big people. And then the lecture was gratis, a fact which is always borne in mind by an Englishman when he comes to reckon up and calculate the way in which he is treated. When he pays his money, then he takes his choice; he may be impatient or not as he likes. His sense of justice teaches him so much, and in accordance with that sense he usually acts.\n\nSo the people on the benches rose graciously when the palace party entered the room. Seats for them had been kept in the front. There were three arm-chairs, which were filled, after some little hesitation, by the bishop, Mrs. Proudie, and Miss Dunstable—Mrs. Smith positively declining to take one of them; though, as she admitted, her rank as Lady Papua of the islands did give her some claim. And this remark, as it was made quite out loud, reached Mr. Smith’s ears as he stood behind a little table on a small raised dais, holding his white kid gloves; and it annoyed him and rather put him out. He did not like that joke about Lady Papua.\n\nAnd then the others of the party sat upon a front bench covered with red cloth. “We shall find this very hard and very narrow about the second hour,” said Mr. Sowerby, and Mr. Smith on his dais again overheard the words, and dashed his gloves down to the table. He felt that all the room would hear it.\n\nAnd there were one or two gentlemen on the second seat who shook hands with some of our party. There was Mr. Thorne of Ullathorne, a good-natured old bachelor, whose residence was near enough to Barchester to allow of his coming in without much personal inconvenience; and next to him was Mr. Harding, an old clergyman of the chapter, with whom Mrs. Proudie shook hands very graciously, making way for him to seat himself close behind her if he would so please. But Mr. Harding did not so please. Having paid his respects to the bishop he returned quietly to the side of his old friend Mr. Thorne, thereby angering Mrs. Proudie, as might easily be seen by her face. And Mr. Chadwick also was there, the episcopal man of business for the diocese; but he also adhered to the two gentlemen above named.\n\nAnd now that the bishop and the ladies had taken their places, Mr. Harold Smith relifted his gloves and again laid them down, hummed three times distinctly, and then began.\n\n“It was,” he said, “the most peculiar characteristic of the present era in the British islands that those who were high placed before the world in rank, wealth, and education were willing to come forward and give their time and knowledge without fee or reward, for the advantage and amelioration of those who did not stand so high in the social scale.” And then he paused for a moment, during which Mrs. Smith remarked to Miss Dunstable that that was pretty well for a beginning; and Miss Dunstable replied, “that as for herself she felt very grateful to rank, wealth, and education.” Mr. Sowerby winked to Mr. Supplehouse, who opened his eyes very wide and shrugged his shoulders. But the Barchesterians took it all in good part and gave the lecturer the applause of their hands and feet.\n\nAnd then, well pleased, he recommenced—“I do not make these remarks with reference to myself—”\n\n“I hope he’s not going to be modest,” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n“It will be quite new if he is,” replied Mrs. Smith.\n\n“—so much as to many noble and talented lords and members of the Lower House who have lately from time to time devoted themselves to this good work.” And then he went through a long list of peers and members of Parliament, beginning, of course, with Lord Boanerges, and ending with Mr. Green Walker, a young gentleman who had lately been returned by his uncle’s interest for the borough of Crewe Junction, and had immediately made his entrance into public life by giving a lecture on the grammarians of the Latin language as exemplified at Eton school.\n\n“On the present occasion,” Mr. Smith continued, “our object is to learn something as to those grand and magnificent islands which lie far away, beyond the Indies, in the Southern Ocean; the lands of which produce rich spices and glorious fruits, and whose seas are imbedded with pearls and corals,—Papua and the Philippines, Borneo and the Moluccas. My friends, you are familiar with your maps, and you know the track which the equator makes for itself through those distant oceans.” And then many heads were turned down, and there was a rustle of leaves; for not a few of those “who stood not so high in the social scale” had brought their maps with them, and refreshed their memories as to the whereabouts of these wondrous islands.\n\nAnd then Mr. Smith also, with a map in his hand, and pointing occasionally to another large map which hung against the wall, went into the geography of the matter. “We might have found that out from our atlases, I think, without coming all the way to Barchester,” said that unsympathizing helpmate, Mrs. Harold, very cruelly—most illogically too, for there be so many things which we could find out ourselves by search, but which we never do find out unless they be specially told us; and why should not the latitude and longitude of Labuan be one—or rather two of these things?\n\nAnd then, when he had duly marked the path of the line through Borneo, Celebes, and Gilolo, through the Macassar strait and the Molucca passage, Mr. Harold Smith rose to a higher flight. “But what,” said he, “avails all that God can give to man, unless man will open his hand to receive the gift? And what is this opening of the hand but the process of civilization—yes, my friends, the process of civilization? These South Sea islanders have all that a kind Providence can bestow on them; but that all is as nothing without education. That education and that civilization it is for you to bestow upon them—yes, my friends, for you; for you, citizens of Barchester as you are.” And then he paused again, in order that the feet and hands might go to work. The feet and hands did go to work, during which Mr. Smith took a slight drink of water.\n\nHe was now quite in his element and had got into the proper way of punching the table with his fists. A few words dropping from Mr. Sowerby did now and again find their way to his ears, but the sound of his own voice had brought with it the accustomed charm, and he ran on from platitude to truism, and from truism back to platitude, with an eloquence that was charming to himself.\n\n“Civilization,” he exclaimed, lifting up his eyes and hands to the ceiling. “Oh, civilization—”\n\n“There will not be a chance for us now for the next hour and a half,” said Mr. Supplehouse, groaning.\n\nHarold Smith cast one eye down at him, but it immediately flew back to the ceiling.\n\n“Oh, civilization! thou that ennoblest mankind and makest him equal to the gods, what is like unto thee?” Here Mrs. Proudie showed evident signs of disapprobation, which no doubt would have been shared by the bishop, had not that worthy prelate been asleep. But Mr. Smith continued unobservant; or, at any rate, regardless.\n\n“What is like unto thee? Thou art the irrigating stream which makest fertile the barren plain. Till thou comest all is dark and dreary; but at thy advent the noontide sun shines out, the earth gives forth her increase; the deep bowels of the rocks render up their tribute. Forms which were dull and hideous become endowed with grace and beauty, and vegetable existence rises to the scale of celestial life. Then, too, Genius appears clad in a panoply of translucent armour, grasping in his hand the whole terrestrial surface, and making every rood of earth subservient to his purposes;—Genius, the child of civilization, the mother of the Arts!”\n\nThe last little bit, taken from the Pedigree of Progress, had a great success, and all Barchester went to work with its hands and feet;—all Barchester, except that ill-natured aristocratic front-row together with the three arm-chairs at the corner of it. The aristocratic front-row felt itself to be too intimate with civilization to care much about it; and the three arm-chairs, or rather that special one which contained Mrs. Proudie, considered that there was a certain heathenness, a pagan sentimentality almost amounting to infidelity, contained in the lecturer’s remarks, with which she, a pillar of the Church, could not put up, seated as she was now in public conclave.\n\n“It is to civilization that we must look,” continued Mr. Harold Smith, descending from poetry to prose as a lecturer well knows how, and thereby showing the value of both—“for any material progress in these islands; and—”\n\n“And to Christianity,” shouted Mrs. Proudie, to the great amazement of the assembled people and to the thorough wakening of the bishop, who, jumping up in his chair at the sound of the well-known voice, exclaimed, “Certainly, certainly.”\n\n“Hear, hear, hear,” said those on the benches who particularly belonged to Mrs. Proudie’s school of divinity in the city, and among the voices was distinctly heard that of a new verger in whose behalf she had greatly interested herself.\n\n“Oh, yes, Christianity of course,” said Harold Smith, upon whom the interruption did not seem to operate favourably.\n\n“Christianity and Sabbath-day observance,” exclaimed Mrs. Proudie, who, now that she had obtained the ear of the public, seemed well inclined to keep it. “Let us never forget that these islanders can never prosper unless they keep the Sabbath holy.”\n\nPoor Mr. Smith, having been so rudely dragged from his high horse, was never able to mount it again, and completed the lecture in a manner not at all comfortable to himself. He had there, on the table before him, a huge bundle of statistics with which he had meant to convince the reason of his hearers after he had taken full possession of their feelings. But they fell very dull and flat. And at the moment when he was interrupted he was about to explain that that material progress to which he had alluded could not be attained without money; and that it behoved them, the people of Barchester before him, to come forward with their purses like men and brothers. He did also attempt this; but from the moment of that fatal onslaught from the arm-chair, it was clear to him and to every one else, that Mrs. Proudie was now the hero of the hour. His time had gone by, and the people of Barchester did not care a straw for his appeal.\n\nFrom these causes the lecture was over full twenty minutes earlier than any one had expected, to the great delight of Messrs. Sowerby and Supplehouse, who, on that evening, moved and carried a vote of thanks to Mrs. Proudie. For they had gay doings yet before they went to their beds.\n\n“Robarts, here one moment,” Mr. Sowerby said, as they were standing at the door of the Mechanics’ Institute. “Don’t you go off with Mr. and Mrs. Bishop. We are going to have a little supper at the Dragon of Wantly, and after what we have gone through upon my word we want it. You can tell one of the palace servants to let you in.”\n\nMark considered the proposal wistfully. He would fain have joined the supper-party had he dared; but he, like many others of his cloth, had the fear of Mrs. Proudie before his eyes.\n\nAnd a very merry supper they had; but poor Mr. Harold Smith was not the merriest of the party.\n\n# CHAPTER VII. SUNDAY MORNING.\n\nIt was, perhaps, quite as well on the whole for Mark Robarts, that he did not go to that supper party. It was eleven o’clock before they sat down, and nearly two before the gentlemen were in bed. It must be remembered that he had to preach, on the coming Sunday morning, a charity sermon on behalf of a mission to Mr. Harold Smith’s islanders; and, to tell the truth, it was a task for which he had now very little inclination.\n\nWhen first invited to do this, he had regarded the task seriously enough, as he always did regard such work, and he completed his sermon for the occasion before he left Framley; but, since that, an air of ridicule had been thrown over the whole affair, in which he had joined without much thinking of his own sermon, and this made him now heartily wish that he could choose a discourse upon any other subject.\n\nHe knew well that the very points on which he had most insisted, were those which had drawn most mirth from Miss Dunstable and Mrs. Smith, and had oftenest provoked his own laughter; and how was he now to preach on those matters in a fitting mood, knowing, as he would know, that those two ladies would be looking at him, would endeavour to catch his eye, and would turn him into ridicule as they had already turned the lecturer?\n\nIn this he did injustice to one of the ladies, unconsciously. Miss Dunstable, with all her aptitude for mirth, and we may almost fairly say for frolic, was in no way inclined to ridicule religion or anything which she thought to appertain to it. It may be presumed that among such things she did not include Mrs. Proudie, as she was willing enough to laugh at that lady; but Mark, had he known her better, might have been sure that she would have sat out his sermon with perfect propriety.\n\nAs it was, however, he did feel considerable uneasiness; and in the morning he got up early with the view of seeing what might be done in the way of emendation. He cut out those parts which referred most specially to the islands,—he rejected altogether those names over which they had all laughed together so heartily,—and he inserted a string of general remarks, very useful, no doubt, which he flattered himself would rob his sermon of all similarity to Harold Smith’s lecture. He had, perhaps, hoped, when writing it, to create some little sensation; but now he would be quite satisfied if it passed without remark.\n\nBut his troubles for that Sunday were destined to be many. It had been arranged that the party at the hotel should breakfast at eight, and start at half-past eight punctually, so as to enable them to reach Chaldicotes in ample time to arrange their dresses before they went to church. The church stood in the grounds, close to that long formal avenue of lime-trees, but within the front gates. Their walk, therefore, after reaching Mr. Sowerby’s house, would not be long.\n\nMrs. Proudie, who was herself an early body, would not hear of her guest—and he a clergyman—going out to the inn for his breakfast on a Sunday morning. As regarded that Sabbath-day journey to Chaldicotes, to that she had given her assent, no doubt with much uneasiness of mind; but let them have as little desecration as possible. It was, therefore, an understood thing that he was to return with his friends; but he should not go without the advantage of family prayers and family breakfast. And so Mrs. Proudie on retiring to rest gave the necessary orders, to the great annoyance of her household.\n\nTo the great annoyance, at least, of her servants! The bishop himself did not make his appearance till a much later hour. He in all things now supported his wife’s rule; in all things, now, I say; for there had been a moment, when in the first flush and pride of his episcopacy other ideas had filled his mind. Now, however, he gave no opposition to that good woman with whom Providence had blessed him; and in return for such conduct that good woman administered in all things to his little personal comforts. With what surprise did the bishop now look back upon that unholy war which he had once been tempted to wage against the wife of his bosom?\n\nNor did any of the Miss Proudies show themselves at that early hour. They, perhaps, were absent on a different ground. With them Mrs. Proudie had not been so successful as with the bishop. They had wills of their own which became stronger and stronger every day. Of the three with whom Mrs. Proudie was blessed one was already in a position to exercise that will in a legitimate way over a very excellent young clergyman in the diocese, the Rev. Optimus Grey; but the other two, having as yet no such opening for their powers of command, were perhaps a little too much inclined to keep themselves in practice at home.\n\nBut at half-past seven punctually Mrs. Proudie was there, and so was the domestic chaplain; so was Mr. Robarts, and so were the household servants,—all excepting one lazy recreant. “Where is Thomas?” said she of the Argus eyes, standing up with her book of family prayers in her hand. “So please you, ma’am, Tummas be bad with the tooth-ache.” “Tooth-ache!” exclaimed Mrs. Proudie; but her eyes said more terrible things than that. “Let Thomas come to me before church.” And then they proceeded to prayers. These were read by the chaplain, as it was proper and decent that they should be; but I cannot but think that Mrs. Proudie a little exceeded her office in taking upon herself to pronounce the blessing when the prayers were over. She did it, however, in a clear, sonorous voice, and perhaps with more personal dignity than was within the chaplain’s compass.\n\nMrs. Proudie was rather stern at breakfast, and the vicar of Framley felt an unaccountable desire to get out of the house. In the first place she was not dressed with her usual punctilious attention to the proprieties of her high situation. It was evident that there was to be a further toilet before she sailed up the middle of the cathedral choir. She had on a large loose cap with no other strings than those which were wanted for tying it beneath her chin, a cap with which the household and the chaplain were well acquainted, but which seemed ungracious in the eyes of Mr. Robarts after all the well-dressed holiday doings of the last week. She wore also a large, loose, dark-coloured wrapper, which came well up round her neck, and which was not buoyed out, as were her dresses in general, with an under mechanism of petticoats. It clung to her closely, and added to the inflexibility of her general appearance. And then she had encased her feet in large carpet slippers, which no doubt were comfortable, but which struck her visitor as being strange and unsightly.\n\n“Do you find a difficulty in getting your people together for early morning prayers?” she said, as she commenced her operations with the teapot.\n\n“I can’t say that I do,” said Mark. “But then we are seldom so early as this.”\n\n“Parish clergymen should be early, I think,” said she. “It sets a good example in the village.”\n\n“I am thinking of having morning prayers in the church,” said Mr. Robarts.\n\n“That’s nonsense,” said Mrs. Proudie, “and usually means worse than nonsense. I know what that comes to. If you have three services on Sunday and domestic prayers at home, you do very well.” And so saying she handed him his cup.\n\n“But I have not three services on Sunday, Mrs. Proudie.”\n\n“Then I think you should have. Where can the poor people be so well off on Sundays as in church? The bishop intends to express a very strong opinion on this subject in his next charge; and then I am sure you will attend to his wishes.”\n\nTo this Mark made no answer, but devoted himself to his egg.\n\n“I suppose you have not a very large establishment at Framley?” asked Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“What, at the parsonage?”\n\n“Yes; you live at the parsonage, don’t you?”\n\n“Certainly—well; not very large, Mrs. Proudie; just enough to do the work, make things comfortable, and look after the children.”\n\n“It is a very fine living,” said she; “very fine. I don’t remember that we have anything so good ourselves,—except it is Plumstead, the archdeacon’s place. He has managed to butter his bread pretty well.”\n\n“His father was Bishop of Barchester.”\n\n“Oh, yes, I know all about him. Only for that he would barely have risen to be an archdeacon, I suspect. Let me see; yours is £800, is it not, Mr. Robarts? And you such a young man! I suppose you have insured your life highly.”\n\n“Pretty well, Mrs. Proudie.”\n\n“And then, too, your wife had some little fortune, had she not? We cannot all fall on our feet like that; can we, Mr. White?” and Mrs. Proudie in her playful way appealed to the chaplain.\n\nMrs. Proudie was an imperious woman; but then so also was Lady Lufton; and it may therefore be said that Mr. Robarts ought to have been accustomed to feminine domination; but as he sat there munching his toast he could not but make a comparison between the two. Lady Lufton in her little attempts sometimes angered him; but he certainly thought, comparing the lay lady and the clerical together, that the rule of the former was the lighter and the pleasanter. But then Lady Lufton had given him a living and a wife, and Mrs. Proudie had given him nothing.\n\nImmediately after breakfast Mr. Robarts escaped to the Dragon of Wantly, partly because he had had enough of the matutinal Mrs. Proudie, and partly also in order that he might hurry his friends there. He was already becoming fidgety about the time, as Harold Smith had been on the preceding evening, and he did not give Mrs. Smith credit for much punctuality. When he arrived at the inn he asked if they had done breakfast, and was immediately told that not one of them was yet down. It was already half-past eight, and they ought to be now under way on the road.\n\nHe immediately went to Mr. Sowerby’s room, and found that gentleman shaving himself. “Don’t be a bit uneasy,” said Mr. Sowerby. “You and Smith shall have my phaeton, and those horses will take you there in an hour. Not, however, but what we shall all be in time. We’ll send round to the whole party and ferret them out.” And then Mr. Sowerby, having evoked manifold aid with various peals of the bell, sent messengers, male and female, flying to all the different rooms.\n\n“I think I’ll hire a gig and go over at once,” said Mark. “It would not do for me to be late, you know.”\n\n“It won’t do for any of us to be late; and it’s all nonsense about hiring a gig. It would be just throwing a sovereign away, and we should pass you on the road. Go down and see that the tea is made, and all that; and make them have the bill ready; and, Robarts, you may pay it too, if you like it. But I believe we may as well leave that to Baron Borneo—eh?”\n\nAnd then Mark did go down and make the tea, and he did order the bill; and then he walked about the room, looking at his watch, and nervously waiting for the footsteps of his friends. And as he was so employed, he bethought himself whether it was fit that he should be so doing on a Sunday morning; whether it was good that he should be waiting there, in painful anxiety, to gallop over a dozen miles in order that he might not be too late with his sermon; whether his own snug room at home, with Fanny opposite to him, and his bairns crawling on the floor, with his own preparations for his own quiet service, and the warm pressure of Lady Lufton’s hand when that service should be over, was not better than all this.\n\nHe could not afford not to know Harold Smith, and Mr. Sowerby, and the Duke of Omnium, he had said to himself. He had to look to rise in the world, as other men did. But what pleasure had come to him as yet from these intimacies? How much had he hitherto done towards his rising? To speak the truth he was not over well pleased with himself, as he made Mrs. Harold Smith’s tea and ordered Mr. Sowerby’s mutton-chops on that Sunday morning.\n\nAt a little after nine they all assembled; but even then he could not make the ladies understand that there was any cause for hurry; at least Mrs. Smith, who was the leader of the party, would not understand it. When Mark again talked of hiring a gig, Miss Dunstable indeed said that she would join him; and seemed to be so far earnest in the matter that Mr. Sowerby hurried through his second egg in order to prevent such a catastrophe. And then Mark absolutely did order the gig; whereupon Mrs. Smith remarked that in such case she need not hurry herself; but the waiter brought up word that all the horses of the hotel were out, excepting one pair, neither of which could go in single harness. Indeed, half of their stable establishment was already secured by Mr. Sowerby’s own party.\n\n“Then let me have the pair,” said Mark, almost frantic with delay.\n\n“Nonsense, Robarts; we are ready now. He won’t want them, James. Come, Supplehouse, have you done?”\n\n“Then I am to hurry myself, am I?” said Mrs. Harold Smith. “What changeable creatures you men are! May I be allowed half a cup more tea, Mr. Robarts?”\n\nMark, who was now really angry, turned away to the window. There was no charity in these people, he said to himself. They knew the nature of his distress, and yet they only laughed at him. He did not, perhaps, reflect that he had assisted in the joke against Harold Smith on the previous evening.\n\n“James,” said he, turning to the waiter, “let me have that pair of horses immediately, if you please.”\n\n“Yes, sir; round in fifteen minutes, sir: only Ned, sir, the post-boy, sir; I fear he’s at his breakfast, sir; but we’ll have him here in less than no time, sir!”\n\nBut before Ned and the pair were there, Mrs. Smith had absolutely got her bonnet on, and at ten they started. Mark did share the phaeton with Harold Smith, but the phaeton did not go any faster than the other carriages. They led the way, indeed, but that was all; and when the vicar’s watch told him that it was eleven, they were still a mile from Chaldicotes’ gate, although the horses were in a lather of steam; and they had only just entered the village when the church bells ceased to be heard.\n\n“Come, you are in time, after all,” said Harold Smith. “Better time than I was last night.” Robarts could not explain to him that the entry of a clergyman into church, of a clergyman who is going to assist in the service, should not be made at the last minute, that it should be staid and decorous, and not done in scrambling haste, with running feet and scant breath.\n\n“I suppose we’ll stop here, sir,” said the postilion, as he pulled up his horses short at the church-door, in the midst of the people who were congregated together ready for the service. But Mark had not anticipated being so late, and said at first that it was necessary that he should go on to the house; then, when the horses had again begun to move, he remembered that he could send for his gown, and as he got out of the carriage he gave his orders accordingly. And now the other two carriages were there, and so there was a noise and confusion at the door—very unseemly, as Mark felt it; and the gentlemen spoke in loud voices, and Mrs. Harold Smith declared that she had no prayer-book, and was much too tired to go in at present;—she would go home and rest herself, she said. And two other ladies of the party did so also, leaving Miss Dunstable to go alone;—for which, however, she did not care one button. And then one of the party, who had a nasty habit of swearing, cursed at something as he walked in close to Mark’s elbow; and so they made their way up the church as the absolution was being read, and Mark Robarts felt thoroughly ashamed of himself. If his rising in the world brought him in contact with such things as these, would it not be better for him that he should do without rising?\n\nHis sermon went off without any special notice. Mrs. Harold Smith was not there, much to his satisfaction; and the others who were did not seem to pay any special attention to it. The subject had lost its novelty, except with the ordinary church congregation, the farmers and labourers of the parish; and the “quality” in the squire’s great pew were content to show their sympathy by a moderate subscription. Miss Dunstable, however, gave a ten-pound note, which swelled up the sum total to a respectable amount—for such a place as Chaldicotes.\n\n“And now I hope I may never hear another word about New Guinea,” said Mr. Sowerby, as they all clustered round the drawing-room fire after church. “That subject may be regarded as having been killed and buried; eh, Harold?”\n\n“Certainly murdered last night,” said Mrs. Harold, “by that awful woman, Mrs. Proudie.”\n\n“I wonder you did not make a dash at her and pull her out of the arm-chair,” said Miss Dunstable. “I was expecting it, and thought that I should come to grief in the scrimmage.”\n\n“I never knew a lady do such a brazen-faced thing before,” said Miss Kerrigy, a travelling friend of Miss Dunstable’s.\n\n“Nor I—never; in a public place, too,” said Dr. Easyman, a medical gentleman, who also often accompanied her.\n\n“As for brass,” said Mr. Supplehouse, “she would never stop at anything for want of that. It is well that she has enough, for the poor bishop is but badly provided.”\n\n“I hardly heard what it was she did say,” said Harold Smith; “so I could not answer her, you know. Something about Sundays, I believe.”\n\n“She hoped you would not put the South Sea islanders up to Sabbath travelling,” said Mr. Sowerby.\n\n“And specially begged that you would establish Lord’s-day schools,” said Mrs. Smith; and then they all went to work and picked Mrs. Proudie to pieces from the top ribbon of her cap down to the sole of her slipper.\n\n“And then she expects the poor parsons to fall in love with her daughters. That’s the hardest thing of all,” said Miss Dunstable.\n\nBut, on the whole, when our vicar went to bed he did not feel that he had spent a profitable Sunday.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII. GATHERUM CASTLE.\n\nOn the Tuesday morning Mark did receive his wife’s letter and the ten-pound note, whereby a strong proof was given of the honesty of the post-office people in Barsetshire. That letter, written as it had been in a hurry, while Robin post-boy was drinking a single mug of beer,—well, what of it if it was half filled a second time?—was nevertheless eloquent of his wife’s love and of her great triumph.\n\nI have only half a moment to send you the money [she said], for the postman is here waiting. When I see you I’ll explain why I am so hurried. Let me know that you get it safe. It is all right now, and Lady Lufton was here not a minute ago. She did not quite like it; about Gatherum Castle I mean; but you’ll hear nothing about it. Only remember that you must dine at Framley Court on Wednesday week. I have promised for you. You will: won’t you, dearest? I shall come and fetch you away if you attempt to stay longer than you have said. But I’m sure you won’t. God bless you, my own one! Mr. Jones gave us the same sermon he preached the second Sunday after Easter. Twice in the same year is too often. God bless you! The children are quite well. Mark sends a big kiss.—Your own F.\n\nRobarts, as he read this letter and crumpled the note up into his pocket, felt that it was much more satisfactory than he deserved. He knew that there must have been a fight, and that his wife, fighting loyally on his behalf, had got the best of it; and he knew also that her victory had not been owing to the goodness of her cause. He frequently declared to himself that he would not be afraid of Lady Lufton; but nevertheless these tidings that no reproaches were to be made to him afforded him great relief.\n\nOn the following Friday they all went to the duke’s, and found that the bishop and Mrs. Proudie were there before them; as were also sundry other people, mostly of some note, either in the estimation of the world at large or of that of West Barsetshire. Lord Boanerges was there, an old man who would have his own way in everything, and who was regarded by all men—apparently even by the duke himself—as an intellectual king, by no means of the constitutional kind,—as an intellectual emperor, rather, who took upon himself to rule all questions of mind without the assistance of any ministers whatever. And Baron Brawl was of the party, one of her Majesty’s puisne judges, as jovial a guest as ever entered a country house; but given to be rather sharp withal in his jovialities. And there was Mr. Green Walker, a young but rising man, the same who lectured not long since on a popular subject to his constituents at the Crewe Junction. Mr. Green Walker was a nephew of the Marchioness of Hartletop, and the Marchioness of Hartletop was a friend of the Duke of Omnium’s. Mr. Mark Robarts was certainly elated when he ascertained who composed the company of which he had been so earnestly pressed to make a portion. Would it have been wise in him to forego this on account of the prejudices of Lady Lufton?\n\nAs the guests were so many and so great, the huge front portals of Gatherum Castle were thrown open, and the vast hall, adorned with trophies—with marble busts from Italy and armour from Wardour Street,—was thronged with gentlemen and ladies, and gave forth unwonted echoes to many a footstep. His grace himself, when Mark arrived there with Sowerby and Miss Dunstable—for in this instance Miss Dunstable did travel in the phaeton while Mark occupied a seat in the dicky—his grace himself was at this moment in the drawing-room, and nothing could exceed his urbanity.\n\n“Oh, Miss Dunstable,” he said, taking that lady by the hand, and leading her up to the fire, “now I feel for the first time that Gatherum Castle has not been built for nothing.”\n\n“Nobody ever supposed it was, your grace,” said Miss Dunstable. “I am sure the architect did not think so when his bill was paid.” And Miss Dunstable put her toes up on the fender to warm them with as much self-possession as though her father had been a duke also, instead of a quack doctor.\n\n“We have given the strictest orders about the parrot,” said the duke—\n\n“Ah! but I have not brought him after all,” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n—“and I have had an aviary built on purpose,—just such as parrots are used to in their own country. Well, Miss Dunstable, I do call that unkind. Is it too late to send for him?”\n\n“He and Dr. Easyman are travelling together. The truth was, I could not rob the doctor of his companion.”\n\n“Why? I have had another aviary built for him. I declare, Miss Dunstable, the honour you are doing me is shorn of half its glory. But the poodle—I still trust in the poodle.”\n\n“And your grace’s trust shall not in that respect be in vain. Where is he, I wonder?” And Miss Dunstable looked round as though she expected that somebody would certainly have brought her dog in after her. “I declare I must go and look for him,—only think if they were to put him among your grace’s dogs,—how his morals would be destroyed!”\n\n“Miss Dunstable, is that intended to be personal?” But the lady had turned away from the fire, and the duke was able to welcome his other guests.\n\nThis he did with much courtesy. “Sowerby,” he said, “I am glad to find that you have survived the lecture. I can assure you I had fears for you.”\n\n“I was brought back to life after considerable delay by the administration of tonics at the Dragon of Wantly. Will your grace allow me to present to you Mr. Robarts, who on that occasion was not so fortunate. It was found necessary to carry him off to the palace, where he was obliged to undergo very vigorous treatment.”\n\nAnd then the duke shook hands with Mr. Robarts, assuring him that he was most happy to make his acquaintance. He had often heard of him since he came into the county; and then he asked after Lord Lufton, regretting that he had been unable to induce his lordship to come to Gatherum Castle.\n\n“But you had a diversion at the lecture, I am told,” continued the duke. “There was a second performer, was there not, who almost eclipsed poor Harold Smith?” And then Mr. Sowerby gave an amusing sketch of the little Proudie episode.\n\n“It has, of course, ruined your brother-in-law for ever as a lecturer,” said the duke, laughing.\n\n“If so, we shall feel ourselves under the deepest obligations to Mrs. Proudie,” said Mr. Sowerby. And then Harold Smith himself came up and received the duke’s sincere and hearty congratulations on the success of his enterprise at Barchester.\n\nMark Robarts had now turned away, and his attention was suddenly arrested by the loud voice of Miss Dunstable, who had stumbled across some very dear friends in her passage through the rooms, and who by no means hid from the public her delight upon the occasion.\n\n“Well—well—well!” she exclaimed, and then she seized upon a very quiet-looking, well-dressed, attractive young woman who was walking towards her, in company with a gentleman. The gentleman and lady, as it turned out, were husband and wife. “Well—well—well! I hardly hoped for this.” And then she took hold of the lady and kissed her enthusiastically, and after that grasped both the gentleman’s hands, shaking them stoutly.\n\n“And what a deal I shall have to say to you!” she went on. “You’ll upset all my other plans. But, Mary, my dear, how long are you going to stay here? I go—let me see—I forget when, but it’s all put down in a book upstairs. But the next stage is at Mrs. Proudie’s. I shan’t meet you there, I suppose. And now, Frank, how’s the governor?”\n\nThe gentleman called Frank declared that the governor was all right—“mad about the hounds, of course, you know.”\n\n“Well, my dear, that’s better than the hounds being mad about him, like the poor gentleman they’ve put into a statue. But talking of hounds, Frank, how badly they manage their foxes at Chaldicotes! I was out hunting all one day—”\n\n“You out hunting!” said the lady called Mary.\n\n“And why shouldn’t I go out hunting? I’ll tell you what, Mrs. Proudie was out hunting, too. But they didn’t catch a single fox; and, if you must have the truth, it seemed to me to be rather slow.”\n\n“You were in the wrong division of the county,” said the gentleman called Frank.\n\n“Of course I was. When I really want to practise hunting I’ll go to Greshamsbury; not a doubt about that.”\n\n“Or to Boxall Hill,” said the lady; “you’ll find quite as much zeal there as at Greshamsbury.”\n\n“And more discretion, you should add,” said the gentleman.\n\n“Ha! ha! ha!” laughed Miss Dunstable; “your discretion indeed! But you have not told me a word about Lady Arabella.”\n\n“My mother is quite well,” said the gentleman.\n\n“And the doctor? By-the-by, my dear, I’ve had such a letter from the doctor; only two days ago. I’ll show it you upstairs to-morrow. But mind, it must be a positive secret. If he goes on in this way he’ll get himself into the Tower, or Coventry, or a blue-book, or some dreadful place.”\n\n“Why; what has he said?”\n\n“Never you mind, Master Frank: I don’t mean to show you the letter, you may be sure of that. But if your wife will swear three times on a poker and tongs that she won’t reveal, I’ll show it to her. And so you are quite settled at Boxall Hill, are you?”\n\n“Frank’s horses are settled; and the dogs nearly so,” said Frank’s wife; “but I can’t boast much of anything else yet.”\n\n“Well, there’s a good time coming. I must go and change my things now. But, Mary, mind you get near me this evening; I have such a deal to say to you.” And then Miss Dunstable marched out of the room.\n\nAll this had been said in so loud a voice that it was, as a matter of course, overheard by Mark Robarts—that part of the conversation of course I mean which had come from Miss Dunstable. And then Mark learned that this was young Frank Gresham of Boxall Hill, son of old Mr. Gresham of Greshamsbury. Frank had lately married a great heiress; a greater heiress, men said, even than Miss Dunstable; and as the marriage was hardly as yet more than six months old the Barsetshire world was still full of it.\n\n“The two heiresses seem to be very loving, don’t they?” said Mr. Supplehouse. “Birds of a feather flock together, you know. But they did say some little time ago that young Gresham was to have married Miss Dunstable himself.”\n\n“Miss Dunstable! why she might almost be his mother,” said Mark.\n\n“That makes but little difference. He was obliged to marry money, and I believe there is no doubt that he did at one time propose to Miss Dunstable.”\n\n“I have had a letter from Lufton,” Mr. Sowerby said to him the next morning. “He declares that the delay was all your fault. You were to have told Lady Lufton before he did anything, and he was waiting to write about it till he heard from you. It seems that you never said a word to her ladyship on the subject.”\n\n“I never did, certainly. My commission from Lufton was to break the matter to her when I found her in a proper humour for receiving it. If you knew Lady Lufton as well as I do, you would know that it is not every day that she would be in a humour for such tidings.”\n\n“And so I was to be kept waiting indefinitely because you two between you were afraid of an old woman! However I have not a word to say against her, and the matter is settled now.”\n\n“Has the farm been sold?”\n\n“Not a bit of it. The dowager could not bring her mind to suffer such profanation for the Lufton acres, and so she sold five thousand pounds out of the funds and sent the money to Lufton as a present;—sent it to him without saying a word, only hoping that it would suffice for his wants. I wish I had a mother, I know.”\n\nMark found it impossible at the moment to make any remark upon what had been told him, but he felt a sudden qualm of conscience and a wish that he was at Framley instead of at Gatherum Castle at the present moment. He knew a good deal respecting Lady Lufton’s income and the manner in which it was spent. It was very handsome for a single lady, but then she lived in a free and open-handed style; her charities were noble; there was no reason why she should save money, and her annual income was usually spent within the year. Mark knew this, and he knew also that nothing short of an impossibility to maintain them would induce her to lessen her charities. She had now given away a portion of her principal to save the property of her son—her son, who was so much more opulent than herself,—upon whose means, too, the world made fewer effectual claims.\n\nAnd Mark knew, too, something of the purpose for which this money had gone. There had been unsettled gambling claims between Sowerby and Lord Lufton, originating in affairs of the turf. It had now been going on for four years, almost from the period when Lord Lufton had become of age. He had before now spoken to Robarts on the matter with much bitter anger, alleging that Mr. Sowerby was treating him unfairly, nay, dishonestly—that he was claiming money that was not due to him; and then he declared more than once that he would bring the matter before the Jockey Club. But Mark, knowing that Lord Lufton was not clear-sighted in these matters, and believing it to be impossible that Mr. Sowerby should actually endeavour to defraud his friend, had smoothed down the young lord’s anger, and recommended him to get the case referred to some private arbiter. All this had afterwards been discussed between Robarts and Mr. Sowerby himself, and hence had originated their intimacy. The matter was so referred, Mr. Sowerby naming the referee; and Lord Lufton, when the matter was given against him, took it easily. His anger was over by that time. “I’ve been clean done among them,” he said to Mark, laughing; “but it does not signify; a man must pay for his experience. Of course, Sowerby thinks it all right; I am bound to suppose so.” And then there had been some further delay as to the amount, and part of the money had been paid to a third person, and a bill had been given, and heaven and the Jews only know how much money Lord Lufton had paid in all; and now it was ended by his handing over to some wretched villain of a money-dealer, on behalf of Mr. Sowerby, the enormous sum of five thousand pounds, which had been deducted from the means of his mother, Lady Lufton!\n\nMark, as he thought of all this, could not but feel a certain animosity against Mr. Sowerby—could not but suspect that he was a bad man. Nay, must he not have known that he was very bad? And yet he continued walking with him through the duke’s grounds, still talking about Lord Lufton’s affairs, and still listening with interest to what Sowerby told him of his own.\n\n“No man was ever robbed as I have been,” said he. “But I shall win through yet, in spite of them all. But those Jews, Mark”—he had become very intimate with him in these latter days—“whatever you do, keep clear of them. Why, I could paper a room with their signatures; and yet I never had a claim upon one of them, though they always have claims on me!”\n\nI have said above that this affair of Lord Lufton’s was ended; but it now appeared to Mark that it was not quite ended. “Tell Lufton, you know,” said Sowerby, “that every bit of paper with his name has been taken up, except what that ruffian Tozer has. Tozer may have one bill, I believe,—something that was not given up when it was renewed. But I’ll make my lawyer Gumption get that up. It may cost ten pounds or twenty pounds, not more. You’ll remember that when you see Lufton, will you?”\n\n“You’ll see Lufton in all probability before I shall.”\n\n“Oh, did I not tell you? He’s going to Framley Court at once; you’ll find him there when you return.”\n\n“Find him at Framley!”\n\n“Yes; this little cadeau from his mother has touched his filial heart. He is rushing home to Framley to pay back the dowager’s hard moidores in soft caresses. I wish I had a mother; I know that.”\n\nAnd Mark still felt that he feared Mr. Sowerby, but he could not make up his mind to break away from him.\n\nAnd there was much talk of politics just then at the castle. Not that the duke joined in it with any enthusiasm. He was a Whig—a huge mountain of a colossal Whig—all the world knew that. No opponent would have dreamed of tampering with his whiggery, nor would any brother Whig have dreamed of doubting it. But he was a Whig who gave very little practical support to any set of men, and very little practical opposition to any other set. He was above troubling himself with such sublunar matters. At election time he supported, and always carried, Whig candidates; and in return he had been appointed lord lieutenant of the county by one Whig minister, and had received the Garter from another. But these things were matters of course to a Duke of Omnium. He was born to be a lord lieutenant and a knight of the Garter.\n\nBut not the less on account of his apathy, or rather quiescence, was it thought that Gatherum Castle was a fitting place in which politicians might express to each other their present hopes and future aims, and concoct together little plots in a half-serious and half-mocking way. Indeed it was hinted that Mr. Supplehouse and Harold Smith, with one or two others, were at Gatherum for this express purpose. Mr. Fothergill, too, was a noted politician, and was supposed to know the duke’s mind well; and Mr. Green Walker, the nephew of the marchioness, was a young man whom the duke desired to have brought forward. Mr. Sowerby also was the duke’s own member, and so the occasion suited well for the interchange of a few ideas.\n\nThe then prime minister, angry as many men were with him, had not been altogether unsuccessful. He had brought the Russian war to a close, which, if not glorious, was at any rate much more so than Englishmen at one time had ventured to hope. And he had had wonderful luck in that Indian mutiny. It is true that many of those even who voted with him would declare that this was in no way attributable to him. Great men had risen in India and done all that. Even his minister there, the governor whom he had sent out, was not allowed in those days any credit for the success which was achieved under his orders. There was great reason to doubt the man at the helm. But nevertheless he had been lucky. There is no merit in a public man like success!\n\nBut now, when the evil days were well nigh over, came the question whether he had not been too successful. When a man has nailed fortune to his chariot-wheels he is apt to travel about in rather a proud fashion. There are servants who think that their masters cannot do without them; and the public also may occasionally have some such servant. What if this too successful minister were one of them!\n\nAnd then a discreet, commonplace, zealous member of the Lower House does not like to be jeered at, when he does his duty by his constituents and asks a few questions. An all-successful minister who cannot keep his triumph to himself, but must needs drive about in a proud fashion, laughing at commonplace zealous members—laughing even occasionally at members who are by no means commonplace, which is outrageous!—may it not be as well to ostracize him for awhile?\n\n“Had we not better throw in our shells against him?” says Mr. Harold Smith.\n\n“Let us throw in our shells, by all means,” says Mr. Supplehouse, mindful as Juno of his despised charms. And when Mr. Supplehouse declares himself an enemy, men know how much it means. They know that that much-belaboured head of affairs must succumb to the terrible blows which are now in store for him. “Yes, we will throw in our shells.” And Mr. Supplehouse rises from his chair with gleaming eyes. “Has not Greece as noble sons as him? ay, and much nobler, traitor that he is. We must judge a man by his friends,” says Mr. Supplehouse; and he points away to the East, where our dear allies the French are supposed to live, and where our head of affairs is supposed to have too close an intimacy.\n\nThey all understand this, even Mr. Green Walker. “I don’t know that he is any good to any of us at all, now,” says the talented member for the Crewe Junction. “He’s a great deal too uppish to suit my book; and I know a great many people that think so too. There’s my uncle—”\n\n“He’s the best fellow in the world,” said Mr. Fothergill, who felt, perhaps, that that coming revelation about Mr. Green Walker’s uncle might not be of use to them; “but the fact is one gets tired of the same man always. One does not like partridge every day. As for me, I have nothing to do with it myself; but I would certainly like to change the dish.”\n\n“If we’re merely to do as we are bid, and have no voice of our own, I don’t see what’s the good of going to the shop at all,” said Mr. Sowerby.\n\n“Not the least use,” said Mr. Supplehouse. “We are false to our constituents in submitting to such a dominion.”\n\n“Let’s have a change, then,” said Mr. Sowerby. “The matter’s pretty much in our own hands.”\n\n“Altogether,” said Mr. Green Walker. “That’s what my uncle always says.”\n\n“The Manchester men will only be too happy for the chance,” said Harold Smith.\n\n“And as for the high and dry gentlemen,” said Mr. Sowerby, “it’s not very likely that they will object to pick up the fruit when we shake the tree.”\n\n“As to picking up the fruit, that’s as may be,” said Mr. Supplehouse. Was he not the man to save the nation; and if so, why should he not pick up the fruit himself? Had not the greatest power in the country pointed him out as such a saviour? What though the country at the present moment needed no more saving, might there not, nevertheless, be a good time coming? Were there not rumours of other wars still prevalent—if indeed the actual war then going on was being brought to a close without his assistance by some other species of salvation? He thought of that country to which he had pointed, and of that friend of his enemies, and remembered that there might be still work for a mighty saviour. The public mind was now awake, and understood what it was about. When a man gets into his head an idea that the public voice calls for him, it is astonishing how great becomes his trust in the wisdom of the public. Vox populi vox Dei. “Has it not been so always?” he says to himself, as he gets up and as he goes to bed. And then Mr. Supplehouse felt that he was the master mind there at Gatherum Castle, and that those there were all puppets in his hand. It is such a pleasant thing to feel that one’s friends are puppets, and that the strings are in one’s own possession. But what if Mr. Supplehouse himself were a puppet?\n\nSome months afterwards, when the much-belaboured head of affairs was in very truth made to retire, when unkind shells were thrown in against him in great numbers, when he exclaimed, “Et tu, Brute!” till the words were stereotyped upon his lips, all men in all places talked much about the great Gatherum Castle confederation. The Duke of Omnium, the world said, had taken into his high consideration the state of affairs, and seeing with his eagle’s eye that the welfare of his countrymen at large required that some great step should be initiated, he had at once summoned to his mansion many members of the Lower House, and some also of the House of Lords,—mention was here especially made of the all-venerable and all-wise Lord Boanerges; and men went on to say that there, in deep conclave, he had made known to them his views. It was thus agreed that the head of affairs, Whig as he was, must fall. The country required it, and the duke did his duty. This was the beginning, the world said, of that celebrated confederation, by which the ministry was overturned, and—as the Goody Twoshoes added,—the country saved. But the Jupiter took all the credit to itself; and the Jupiter was not far wrong. All the credit was due to the Jupiter—in that, as in everything else.\n\nIn the meantime the Duke of Omnium entertained his guests in the quiet princely style, but did not condescend to have much conversation on politics either with Mr. Supplehouse or with Mr. Harold Smith. And as for Lord Boanerges, he spent the morning on which the above-described conversation took place in teaching Miss Dunstable to blow soap-bubbles on scientific principles.\n\n“Dear, dear!” said Miss Dunstable, as sparks of knowledge came flying in upon her mind. “I always thought that a soap-bubble was a soap-bubble, and I never asked the reason why. One doesn’t, you know, my lord.”\n\n“Pardon me, Miss Dunstable,” said the old lord, “one does; but nine hundred and ninety-nine do not.”\n\n“And the nine hundred and ninety-nine have the best of it,” said Miss Dunstable. “What pleasure can one have in a ghost after one has seen the phosphorus rubbed on?”\n\n“Quite true, my dear lady. ‘If ignorance be bliss, ’tis folly to be wise.’ It all lies in the ‘if.’”\n\nThen Miss Dunstable began to sing:—\n\n“‘What tho’ I trace each herb and flower That sips the morning dew—’\n\n—you know the rest, my lord.”\n\nLord Boanerges did know almost everything, but he did not know that; and so Miss Dunstable went on:—\n\n“‘Did I not own Jehovah’s power How vain were all I knew.’”\n\n“Exactly, exactly, Miss Dunstable,” said his lordship; “but why not own the power and trace the flower as well? perhaps one might help the other.”\n\nUpon the whole I am afraid that Lord Boanerges got the best of it. But then that is his line. He has been getting the best of it all his life.\n\nIt was observed by all that the duke was especially attentive to young Mr. Frank Gresham, the gentleman on whom and on whose wife Miss Dunstable had seized so vehemently. This Mr. Gresham was the richest commoner in the county, and it was rumoured that at the next election he would be one of the members for the East Riding. Now the duke had little or nothing to do with the East Riding, and it was well known that young Gresham would be brought forward as a strong conservative. But, nevertheless, his acres were so extensive and his money so plentiful that he was worth a duke’s notice. Mr. Sowerby also was almost more than civil to him, as was natural, seeing that this very young man by a mere scratch of his pen could turn a scrap of paper into a bank-note of almost fabulous value.\n\n“So you have the East Barsetshire hounds at Boxall Hill; have you not?” said the duke.\n\n“The hounds are there,” said Frank. “But I am not the master.”\n\n“Oh! I understood—”\n\n“My father has them. But he finds Boxall Hill more centrical than Greshamsbury. The dogs and horses have to go shorter distances.”\n\n“Boxall Hill is very centrical.”\n\n“Oh, exactly!”\n\n“And your young gorse coverts are doing well?”\n\n“Pretty well—gorse won’t thrive everywhere, I find. I wish it would.”\n\n“That’s just what I say to Fothergill; and then where there’s much woodland you can’t get the vermin to leave it.”\n\n“But we haven’t a tree at Boxall Hill,” said Mrs. Gresham.\n\n“Ah, yes; you’re new there, certainly; you’ve enough of it at Greshamsbury in all conscience. There’s a larger extent of wood there than we have; isn’t there, Fothergill?”\n\nMr. Fothergill said that the Greshamsbury woods were very extensive, but that, perhaps, he thought—\n\n“Oh, ah! I know,” said the duke. “The Black Forest in its old days was nothing to Gatherum woods, according to Fothergill. And then again, nothing in East Barsetshire could be equal to anything in West Barsetshire. Isn’t that it; eh, Fothergill?”\n\nMr. Fothergill professed that he had been brought up in that faith and intended to die in it.\n\n“Your exotics at Boxall Hill are very fine, magnificent!” said Mr. Sowerby.\n\n“I’d sooner have one full-grown oak standing in its pride alone,” said young Gresham, rather grandiloquently, “than all the exotics in the world.”\n\n“They’ll come in due time,” said the duke.\n\n“But the due time won’t be in my days. And so they’re going to cut down Chaldicotes forest, are they, Mr. Sowerby?”\n\n“Well, I can’t tell you that. They are going to disforest it. I have been ranger since I was twenty-two, and I don’t yet know whether that means cutting down.”\n\n“Not only cutting down, but rooting up,” said Mr. Fothergill.\n\n“It’s a murderous shame,” said Frank Gresham; “and I will say one thing, I don’t think any but a Whig government would do it.”\n\n“Ha, ha, ha!” laughed his grace. “At any rate I’m sure of this,” he said, “that if a conservative government did do so, the Whigs would be just as indignant as you are now.”\n\n“I’ll tell you what you ought to do, Mr. Gresham,” said Sowerby: “put in an offer for the whole of the West Barsetshire crown property; they will be very glad to sell it.”\n\n“And we should be delighted to welcome you on this side of the border,” said the duke.\n\nYoung Gresham did feel rather flattered. There were not many men in the county to whom such an offer could be made without an absurdity. It might be doubted whether the duke himself could purchase the Chace of Chaldicotes with ready money; but that he, Gresham, could do so—he and his wife between them—no man did doubt. And then Mr. Gresham thought of a former day when he had once been at Gatherum Castle. He had been poor enough then, and the duke had not treated him in the most courteous manner in the world. How hard it is for a rich man not to lean upon his riches! harder, indeed, than for a camel to go through the eye of a needle.\n\nAll Barsetshire knew—at any rate all West Barsetshire—that Miss Dunstable had been brought down in those parts in order that Mr. Sowerby might marry her. It was not surmised that Miss Dunstable herself had had any previous notice of this arrangement, but it was supposed that the thing would turn out as a matter of course. Mr. Sowerby had no money, but then he was witty, clever, good-looking, and a member of Parliament. He lived before the world, represented an old family, and had an old place. How could Miss Dunstable possibly do better? She was not so young now, and it was time that she should look about her.\n\nThe suggestion as regarded Mr. Sowerby was certainly true, and was not the less so as regarded some of Mr. Sowerby’s friends. His sister, Mrs. Harold Smith, had devoted herself to the work, and with this view had run up a dear friendship with Miss Dunstable. The bishop had intimated, nodding his head knowingly, that it would be a very good thing. Mrs. Proudie had given in her adherence. Mr. Supplehouse had been made to understand that it must be a case of “Paws off” with him, as long as he remained in that part of the world; and even the duke himself had desired Fothergill to manage it.\n\n“He owes me an enormous sum of money,” said the duke, who held all Mr. Sowerby’s title-deeds, “and I doubt whether the security will be sufficient.”\n\n“Your grace will find the security quite sufficient,” said Mr. Fothergill; “but nevertheless it would be a good match.”\n\n“Very good,” said the duke. And then it became Mr. Fothergill’s duty to see that Mr. Sowerby and Miss Dunstable became man and wife as speedily as possible.\n\nSome of the party, who were more wide awake than others, declared that he had made the offer; others, that he was just going to do so; and one very knowing lady went so far at one time as to say that he was making it at that moment. Bets also were laid as to the lady’s answer, as to the terms of the settlement, and as to the period of the marriage,—of all which poor Miss Dunstable of course knew nothing.\n\nMr. Sowerby, in spite of the publicity of his proceedings, proceeded in the matter very well. He said little about it to those who joked with him, but carried on the fight with what best knowledge he had in such matters. But so much it is given to us to declare with certainty, that he had not proposed on the evening previous to the morning fixed for the departure of Mark Robarts.\n\nDuring the last two days Mr. Sowerby’s intimacy with Mark had grown warmer and warmer. He had talked to the vicar confidentially about the doings of these bigwigs now present at the castle, as though there were no other guest there with whom he could speak in so free a manner. He confided, it seemed, much more in Mark than in his brother-in-law, Harold Smith, or in any of his brother members of Parliament, and had altogether opened his heart to him in this affair of his anticipated marriage. Now Mr. Sowerby was a man of mark in the world, and all this flattered our young clergyman not a little.\n\nOn that evening before Robarts went away Sowerby asked him to come up into his bedroom when the whole party was breaking up, and there got him into an easy-chair while he, Sowerby, walked up and down the room.\n\n“You can hardly tell, my dear fellow,” said he, “the state of nervous anxiety in which this puts me.”\n\n“Why don’t you ask her and have done with it? She seems to me to be fond of your society.”\n\n“Ah, it is not that only; there are wheels within wheels;” and then he walked once or twice up and down the room, during which Mark thought that he might as well go to bed.\n\n“Not that I mind telling you everything,” said Sowerby. “I am infernally hard up for a little ready money just at the present moment. It may be, and indeed I think it will be, the case that I shall be ruined in this matter for the want of it.”\n\n“Could not Harold Smith give it you?”\n\n“Ha, ha, ha! you don’t know Harold Smith. Did you ever hear of his lending a man a shilling in his life?”\n\n“Or Supplehouse?”\n\n“Lord love you! You see me and Supplehouse together here, and he comes and stays at my house, and all that; but Supplehouse and I are no friends. Look you here, Mark—I would do more for your little finger than for his whole hand, including the pen which he holds in it. Fothergill indeed might—but then I know Fothergill is pressed himself at the present moment. It is deuced hard, isn’t it? I must give up the whole game if I can’t put my hand upon £400 within the next two days.”\n\n“Ask her for it, herself.”\n\n“What, the woman I wish to marry! No, Mark, I’m not quite come to that. I would sooner lose her than that.”\n\nMark sat silent, gazing at the fire and wishing that he was in his own bedroom. He had an idea that Mr. Sowerby wished him to produce this £400; and he knew also that he had not £400 in the world, and that if he had he would be acting very foolishly to give it to Mr. Sowerby. But nevertheless he felt half fascinated by the man, and half afraid of him.\n\n“Lufton owes it to me to do more than this,” continued Mr. Sowerby; “but then Lufton is not here.”\n\n“Why, he has just paid five thousand pounds for you.”\n\n“Paid five thousand pounds for me! Indeed he has done no such thing: not a sixpence of it came into my hands. Believe me, Mark, you don’t know the whole of that yet. Not that I mean to say a word against Lufton. He is the soul of honour; though so deucedly dilatory in money matters. He thought he was right all through that affair, but no man was ever so confoundedly wrong. Why, don’t you remember that that was the very view you took of it yourself?”\n\n“I remember saying that I thought he was mistaken.”\n\n“Of course he was mistaken. And dearly the mistake cost me. I had to make good the money for two or three years. And my property is not like his—I wish it were.”\n\n“Marry Miss Dunstable, and that will set it all right for you.”\n\n“Ah! so I would if I had this money. At any rate I would bring it to the point. Now, I tell you what, Mark; if you’ll assist me at this strait I’ll never forget it. And the time will come round when I may be able to do something for you.”\n\n“I have not got a hundred, no, not fifty pounds by me in the world.”\n\n“Of course you’ve not. Men don’t walk about the streets with £400 in their pockets. I don’t suppose there’s a single man here in the house with such a sum at his bankers’, unless it be the duke.”\n\n“What is it you want then?”\n\n“Why, your name, to be sure. Believe me, my dear fellow, I would not ask you really to put your hand into your pocket to such a tune as that. Allow me to draw on you for that amount at three months. Long before that time I shall be flush enough.” And then, before Mark could answer, he had a bill stamp and pen and ink out on the table before him, and was filling in the bill as though his friend had already given his consent.\n\n“Upon my word, Sowerby, I had rather not do that.”\n\n“Why! what are you afraid of?”—Mr. Sowerby asked this very sharply. “Did you ever hear of my having neglected to take up a bill when it fell due?” Robarts thought that he had heard of such a thing; but in his confusion he was not exactly sure, and so he said nothing.\n\n“No, my boy; I have not come to that. Look here: just you write, ‘Accepted, Mark Robarts,’ across that, and then you shall never hear of the transaction again;—and you will have obliged me for ever.”\n\n“As a clergyman it would be wrong of me,” said Robarts.\n\n“As a clergyman! Come, Mark! If you don’t like to do as much as that for a friend, say so; but don’t let us have that sort of humbug. If there be one class of men whose names would be found more frequent on the backs of bills in the provincial banks than another, clergymen are that class. Come, old fellow, you won’t throw me over when I am so hard pushed.”\n\nMark Robarts took the pen and signed the bill. It was the first time in his life that he had ever done such an act. Sowerby then shook him cordially by the hand, and he walked off to his own bedroom a wretched man.\n\n# CHAPTER IX. THE VICAR’S RETURN.\n\nThe next morning Mr. Robarts took leave of all his grand friends with a heavy heart. He had lain awake half the night thinking of what he had done and trying to reconcile himself to his position. He had not well left Mr. Sowerby’s room before he felt certain that at the end of three months he would again be troubled about that £400. As he went along the passage all the man’s known antecedents crowded upon him much quicker than he could remember them when seated in that arm-chair with the bill stamp before him, and the pen and ink ready to his hand. He remembered what Lord Lufton had told him—how he had complained of having been left in the lurch; he thought of all the stories current through the entire county as to the impossibility of getting money from Chaldicotes; he brought to mind the known character of the man, and then he knew that he must prepare himself to make good a portion at least of that heavy payment.\n\nWhy had he come to this horrid place? Had he not everything at home at Framley which the heart of man could desire? No; the heart of man can desire deaneries—the heart, that is, of the man vicar; and the heart of the man dean can desire bishoprics; and before the eyes of the man bishop does there not loom the transcendental glory of Lambeth? He had owned to himself that he was ambitious; but he had to own to himself now also that he had hitherto taken but a sorry path towards the object of his ambition.\n\nOn the next morning at breakfast-time, before his horse and gig arrived for him, no one was so bright as his friend Sowerby. “So you are off, are you?” said he.\n\n“Yes, I shall go this morning.”\n\n“Say everything that’s kind from me to Lufton. I may possibly see him out hunting; otherwise we shan’t meet till the spring. As to my going to Framley, that’s out of the question. Her ladyship would look for my tail, and swear that she smelt brimstone. By-bye, old fellow!”\n\nThe German student when he first made his bargain with the devil felt an indescribable attraction to his new friend; and such was the case now with Robarts. He shook Sowerby’s hand very warmly, said that he hoped he should meet him soon somewhere, and professed himself specially anxious to hear how that affair with the lady came off. As he had made his bargain—as he had undertaken to pay nearly half-a-year’s income for his dear friend—ought he not to have as much value as possible for his money? If the dear friendship of this flash member of Parliament did not represent that value, what else did do so? But then he felt, or fancied that he felt, that Mr. Sowerby did not care for him so much this morning as he had done on the previous evening. “By-bye,” said Mr. Sowerby, but he spoke no word as to such future meetings, nor did he even promise to write. Mr. Sowerby probably had many things on his mind; and it might be that it behoved him, having finished one piece of business, immediately to look to another.\n\nThe sum for which Robarts had made himself responsible—which he so much feared that he would be called upon to pay—was very nearly half-a-year’s income; and as yet he had not put by one shilling since he had been married. When he found himself settled in his parsonage, he found also that all the world regarded him as a rich man. He had taken the dictum of all the world as true, and had set himself to work to live comfortably. He had no absolute need of a curate; but he could afford the £70—as Lady Lufton had said rather injudiciously; and by keeping Jones in the parish he would be acting charitably to a brother clergyman, and would also place himself in a more independent position. Lady Lufton had wished to see her pet clergyman well-to-do and comfortable; but now, as matters had turned out, she much regretted this affair of the curate. Mr. Jones, she said to herself, more than once, must be made to depart from Framley.\n\nHe had given his wife a pony-carriage, and for himself he had a saddle-horse, and a second horse for his gig. A man in his position, well-to-do as he was, required as much as that. He had a footman also, and a gardener, and a groom. The two latter were absolutely necessary, but about the former there had been a question. His wife had been decidedly hostile to the footman; but, in all such matters as that, to doubt is to be lost. When the footman had been discussed for a week it became quite clear to the master that he also was a necessary.\n\nAs he drove home that morning he pronounced to himself the doom of that footman, and the doom also of that saddle-horse. They at any rate should go. And then he would spend no more money in trips to Scotland; and above all, he would keep out of the bedrooms of impoverished members of Parliament at the witching hour of midnight. Such resolves did he make to himself as he drove home; and bethought himself wearily how that £400 might be made to be forthcoming. As to any assistance in the matter from Sowerby,—of that he gave himself no promise.\n\nBut he almost felt himself happy again as his wife came out into the porch to meet him, with a silk shawl over her head, and pretending to shiver as she watched him descending from his gig.\n\n“My dear old man,” she said, as she led him into the warm drawing-room with all his wrappings still about him, “you must be starved.” But Mark during the whole drive had been thinking too much of that transaction in Mr. Sowerby’s bedroom to remember that the air was cold. Now he had his arm round his own dear Fanny’s waist; but was he to tell her of that transaction? At any rate he would not do it now, while his two boys were in his arms, rubbing the moisture from his whiskers with their kisses. After all, what is there equal to that coming home?\n\n“And so Lufton is here. I say, Frank, gently, old boy,”—Frank was his eldest son—“you’ll have baby into the fender.”\n\n“Let me take baby; it’s impossible to hold the two of them, they are so strong,” said the proud mother. “Oh, yes, he came home early yesterday.”\n\n“Have you seen him?”\n\n“He was here yesterday, with her ladyship; and I lunched there to-day. The letter came, you know, in time to stop the Merediths. They don’t go till to-morrow, so you will meet them after all. Sir George is wild about it, but Lady Lufton would have her way. You never saw her in such a state as she is.”\n\n“Good spirits, eh?”\n\n“I should think so. All Lord Lufton’s horses are coming, and he’s to be here till March.”\n\n“Till March!”\n\n“So her ladyship whispered to me. She could not conceal her triumph at his coming. He’s going to give up Leicestershire this year altogether. I wonder what has brought it all about?” Mark knew very well what had brought it about; he had been made acquainted, as the reader has also, with the price at which Lady Lufton had purchased her son’s visit. But no one had told Mrs. Robarts that the mother had made her son a present of five thousand pounds.\n\n“She’s in a good humour about everything now,” continued Fanny; “so you need say nothing at all about Gatherum Castle.”\n\n“But she was very angry when she first heard it; was she not?”\n\n“Well, Mark, to tell the truth, she was; and we had quite a scene there up in her own room up-stairs,—Justinia and I. She had heard something else that she did not like at the same time; and then—but you know her way. She blazed up quite hot.”\n\n“And said all manner of horrid things about me.”\n\n“About the duke she did. You know she never did like the duke; and for the matter of that, neither do I. I tell you that fairly, Master Mark!”\n\n“The duke is not so bad as he’s painted.”\n\n“Ah, that’s what you say about another great person. However, he won’t come here to trouble us, I suppose. And then I left her, not in the best temper in the world; for I blazed up too, you must know.”\n\n“I am sure you did,” said Mark, pressing his arm round her waist.\n\n“And then we were going to have a dreadful war, I thought; and I came home and wrote such a doleful letter to you. But what should happen when I had just closed it, but in came her ladyship—all alone, and—. But I can’t tell you what she did or said, only she behaved beautifully; just like herself too; so full of love and truth and honesty. There’s nobody like her, Mark; and she’s better than all the dukes that ever wore—whatever dukes do wear.”\n\n“Horns and hoofs; that’s their usual apparel, according to you and Lady Lufton,” said he, remembering what Mr. Sowerby had said of himself.\n\n“You may say what you like about me, Mark, but you shan’t abuse Lady Lufton. And if horns and hoofs mean wickedness and dissipation, I believe it’s not far wrong. But get off your big coat and make yourself comfortable.” And that was all the scolding that Mark Robarts got from his wife on the occasion of his great iniquity.\n\n“I will certainly tell her about this bill transaction,” he said to himself; “but not to-day; not till after I have seen Lufton.”\n\nThat evening they dined at Framley Court, and there they met the young lord; they found also Lady Lufton still in high good-humour. Lord Lufton himself was a fine, bright-looking young man; not so tall as Mark Robarts, and with perhaps less intelligence marked on his face; but his features were finer, and there was in his countenance a thorough appearance of good-humour and sweet temper. It was, indeed, a pleasant face to look upon, and dearly Lady Lufton loved to gaze at it.\n\n“Well, Mark, so you have been among the Philistines?” that was his lordship’s first remark. Robarts laughed as he took his friend’s hands, and bethought himself how truly that was the case; that he was, in very truth, already “himself in bonds under Philistian yoke.” Alas, alas, it is very hard to break asunder the bonds of the latter-day Philistines. When a Samson does now and then pull a temple down about their ears, is he not sure to be engulfed in the ruin with them? There is no horse-leech that sticks so fast as your latter-day Philistine.\n\n“So you have caught Sir George, after all,” said Lady Lufton; and that was nearly all she did say in allusion to his absence. There was afterwards some conversation about the lecture, and from her ladyship’s remarks, it certainly was apparent that she did not like the people among whom the vicar had been lately staying; but she said no word that was personal to him himself, or that could be taken as a reproach. The little episode of Mrs. Proudie’s address in the lecture-room had already reached Framley, and it was only to be expected that Lady Lufton should enjoy the joke. She would affect to believe that the body of the lecture had been given by the bishop’s wife; and afterwards, when Mark described her costume at that Sunday morning breakfast-table, Lady Lufton would assume that such had been the dress in which she had exercised her faculties in public.\n\n“I would have given a five-pound note to have heard it,” said Sir George.\n\n“So would not I,” said Lady Lufton. “When one hears of such things described so graphically as Mr. Robarts now tells it, one can hardly help laughing. But it would give me great pain to see the wife of one of our bishops place herself in such a situation. For he is a bishop after all.”\n\n“Well, upon my word, my lady, I agree with Meredith,” said Lord Lufton. “It must have been good fun. As it did happen, you know,—as the Church was doomed to the disgrace, I should like to have heard it.”\n\n“I know you would have been shocked, Ludovic.”\n\n“I should have got over that in time, mother. It would have been like a bull-fight I suppose—horrible to see no doubt, but extremely interesting. And Harold Smith, Mark; what did he do all the while?”\n\n“It didn’t take so very long, you know,” said Robarts.\n\n“And the poor bishop,” said Lady Meredith; “how did he look? I really do pity him.”\n\n“Well, he was asleep, I think.”\n\n“What, slept through it all?” said Sir George.\n\n“It awakened him; and then he jumped up and said something.”\n\n“What, out loud too?”\n\n“Only one word or so.”\n\n“What a disgraceful scene!” said Lady Lufton. “To those who remember the good old man who was in the diocese before him it is perfectly shocking. He confirmed you, Ludovic, and you ought to remember him. It was over at Barchester, and you went and lunched with him afterwards.”\n\n“I do remember; and especially this, that I never ate such tarts in my life, before or since. The old man particularly called my attention to them, and seemed remarkably pleased that I concurred in his sentiments. There are no such tarts as those going in the palace now, I’ll be bound.”\n\n“Mrs. Proudie will be very happy to do her best for you if you will go and try,” said Sir George.\n\n“I beg that he will do no such thing,” said Lady Lufton, and that was the only severe word she said about any of Mark’s visitings.\n\nAs Sir George Meredith was there, Robarts could say nothing then to Lord Lufton about Mr. Sowerby and Mr. Sowerby’s money affairs; but he did make an appointment for a tête-à-tête on the next morning.\n\n“You must come down and see my nags, Mark; they came to-day. The Merediths will be off at twelve, and then we can have an hour together.” Mark said he would, and then went home with his wife under his arm.\n\n“Well, now, is not she kind?” said Fanny, as soon as they were out on the gravel together.\n\n“She is kind; kinder than I can tell you just at present. But did you ever know anything so bitter as she is to the poor bishop? And really the bishop is not so bad.”\n\n“Yes; I know something much more bitter; and that is what she thinks of the bishop’s wife. And you know, Mark, it was so unladylike, her getting up in that way. What must the people of Barchester think of her?”\n\n“As far as I could see the people of Barchester liked it.”\n\n“Nonsense, Mark; they could not. But never mind that now. I want you to own that she is good.” And then Mrs. Robarts went on with another long eulogy on the dowager. Since that affair of the pardon-begging at the parsonage Mrs. Robarts hardly knew how to think well enough of her friend. And the evening had been so pleasant after the dreadful storm and threatenings of hurricanes; her husband had been so well received after his lapse of judgment; the wounds that had looked so sore had been so thoroughly healed, and everything was so pleasant. How all of this would have been changed had she known of that little bill!\n\nAt twelve the next morning the lord and the vicar were walking through the Framley stables together. Quite a commotion had been made there, for the larger portion of these buildings had of late years seldom been used. But now all was crowding and activity. Seven or eight very precious animals had followed Lord Lufton from Leicestershire, and all of them required dimensions that were thought to be rather excessive by the Framley old-fashioned groom. My lord, however, had a head man of his own who took the matter quite into his own hands.\n\nMark, priest as he was, was quite worldly enough to be fond of a good horse; and for some little time allowed Lord Lufton to descant on the merit of this four-year-old filly, and that magnificent Rattlebones colt, out of a Mousetrap mare; but he had other things that lay heavy on his mind, and after bestowing half an hour on the stud, he contrived to get his friend away to the shrubbery walks.\n\n“So you have settled with Sowerby,” Robarts began by saying.\n\n“Settled with him; yes, but do you know the price?”\n\n“I believe that you have paid five thousand pounds.”\n\n“Yes, and about three before; and that in a matter in which I did not really owe one shilling. Whatever I do in future, I’ll keep out of Sowerby’s grip.”\n\n“But you don’t think he has been unfair to you.”\n\n“Mark, to tell you the truth I have banished the affair from my mind, and don’t wish to take it up again. My mother has paid the money to save the property, and of course I must pay her back. But I think I may promise that I will not have any more money dealings with Sowerby. I will not say that he is dishonest, but at any rate he is sharp.”\n\n“Well, Lufton; what will you say when I tell you that I have put my name to a bill for him, for four hundred pounds?”\n\n“Say; why I should say—; but you’re joking; a man in your position would never do such a thing.”\n\n“But I have done it.”\n\nLord Lufton gave a long low whistle.\n\n“He asked me the last night that I was there, making a great favour of it, and declaring that no bill of his had ever yet been dishonoured.”\n\nLord Lufton whistled again. “No bill of his dishonoured! Why the pocket-books of the Jews are stuffed full of his dishonoured papers! And you have really given him your name for four hundred pounds?”\n\n“I have certainly.”\n\n“At what date?”\n\n“Three months.”\n\n“And have you thought where you are to get the money?”\n\n“I know very well that I can’t get it; not at least by that time. The bankers must renew it for me, and I must pay it by degrees. That is, if Sowerby really does not take it up.”\n\n“It is just as likely that he will take up the national debt.”\n\nRobarts then told him about the projected marriage with Miss Dunstable, giving it as his opinion that the lady would probably accept the gentleman.\n\n“Not at all improbable,” said his lordship, “for Sowerby is an agreeable fellow; and if it be so, he will have all that he wants for life. But his creditors will gain nothing. The duke, who has his title-deeds, will doubtless get his money, and the estate will in fact belong to the wife. But the small fry, such as you, will not get a shilling.”\n\nPoor Mark! He had had an inkling of this before; but it had hardly presented itself to him in such certain terms. It was, then, a positive fact, that in punishment for his weakness in having signed that bill he would have to pay, not only four hundred pounds, but four hundred pounds with interest, and expenses of renewal, and commission, and bill stamps. Yes; he had certainly got among the Philistines during that visit of his to the duke. It began to appear to him pretty clearly that it would have been better for him to have relinquished altogether the glories of Chaldicotes and Gatherum Castle.\n\nAnd now, how was he to tell his wife?\n\n# CHAPTER X. LUCY ROBARTS.\n\nAnd now how was he to tell his wife? That was the consideration heavy on Mark Robarts’ mind when last we left him; and he turned the matter often in his thoughts before he could bring himself to a resolution. At last he did do so, and one may say that it was not altogether a bad one, if only he could carry it out.\n\nHe would ascertain in what bank that bill of his had been discounted. He would ask Sowerby, and if he could not learn from him, he would go to the three banks in Barchester. That it had been taken to one of them he felt tolerably certain. He would explain to the manager his conviction that he would have to make good the amount, his inability to do so at the end of the three months, and the whole state of his income; and then the banker would explain to him how the matter might be arranged. He thought that he could pay £50 every three months with interest. As soon as this should have been concerted with the banker, he would let his wife know all about it. Were he to tell her at the present moment, while the matter was all unsettled, the intelligence would frighten her into illness.\n\nBut on the next morning there came to him tidings by the hands of Robin postman, which for a long while upset all his plans. The letter was from Exeter. His father had been taken ill, and had very quickly been pronounced to be in danger. That evening—the evening on which his sister wrote—the old man was much worse, and it was desirable that Mark should go off to Exeter as quickly as possible. Of course he went to Exeter—again leaving the Framley souls at the mercy of the Welsh Low Churchman. Framley is only four miles from Silverbridge, and at Silverbridge he was on the direct road to the west. He was therefore at Exeter before nightfall on that day.\n\nBut nevertheless he arrived there too late to see his father again alive. The old man’s illness had been sudden and rapid, and he expired without again seeing his eldest son. Mark arrived at the house of mourning just as they were learning to realize the full change in their position.\n\nThe doctor’s career had been on the whole successful, but nevertheless he did not leave behind him as much money as the world had given him credit for possessing. Who ever does? Dr. Robarts had educated a large family, had always lived with every comfort, and had never possessed a shilling but what he had earned himself. A physician’s fees come in, no doubt, with comfortable rapidity as soon as rich old gentlemen and middle-aged ladies begin to put their faith in him; but fees run out almost with equal rapidity when a wife and seven children are treated to everything that the world considers most desirable. Mark, we have seen, had been educated at Harrow and Oxford, and it may be said, therefore, that he had received his patrimony early in life. For Gerald Robarts, the second brother, a commission had been bought in a crack regiment. He also had been lucky, having lived and become a captain in the Crimea; and the purchase-money was lodged for his majority. And John Robarts, the youngest, was a clerk in the Petty Bag Office, and was already assistant private secretary to the Lord Petty Bag himself—a place of considerable trust, if not hitherto of large emolument; and on his education money had been spent freely, for in these days a young man cannot get into the Petty Bag Office without knowing at least three modern languages; and he must be well up in trigonometry too, in bible theology, or in one dead language—at his option.\n\nAnd the doctor had four daughters. The two elder were married, including that Blanche with whom Lord Lufton was to have fallen in love at the vicar’s wedding. A Devonshire squire had done this in the lord’s place; but on marrying her it was necessary that he should have a few thousand pounds, two or three perhaps, and the old doctor had managed that they should be forthcoming. The elder also had not been sent away from the paternal mansion quite empty-handed. There were, therefore, at the time of the doctor’s death two children left at home, of whom one only, Lucy, the younger, will come much across us in the course of our story.\n\nMark stayed for ten days at Exeter, he and the Devonshire squire having been named as executors in the will. In this document it was explained that the doctor trusted that provision had been made for most of his children. As for his dear son Mark, he said, he was aware that he need be under no uneasiness. On hearing this read Mark smiled sweetly, and looked very gracious; but, nevertheless, his heart did sink somewhat within him, for there had been a hope that a small windfall, coming now so opportunely, might enable him to rid himself at once of that dreadful Sowerby incubus. And then the will went on to declare that Mary, and Gerald, and Blanche, had also, by God’s providence, been placed beyond want. And here, looking into the squire’s face, one might have thought that his heart fell a little also; for he had not so full a command of his feelings as his brother-in-law, who had been so much more before the world. To John, the assistant private secretary, was left a legacy of a thousand pounds; and to Jane and Lucy certain sums in certain four per cents., which were quite sufficient to add an efficient value to the hands of those young ladies in the eyes of most prudent young would-be Benedicts. Over and beyond this there was nothing but the furniture, which he desired might be sold, and the proceeds divided among them all. It might come to sixty or seventy pounds a piece, and pay the expenses incidental on his death.\n\nAnd then all men and women there and thereabouts said that old Dr. Robarts had done well. His life had been good and prosperous, and his will was just. And Mark, among others, so declared,—and was so convinced in spite of his own little disappointment. And on the third morning after the reading of the will Squire Crowdy, of Creamclotted Hall, altogether got over his grief, and said that it was all right. And then it was decided that Jane should go home with him,—for there was a brother squire who, it was thought, might have an eye to Jane;—and Lucy, the younger, should be taken to Framley Parsonage. In a fortnight from the receipt of that letter Mark arrived at his own house with his sister Lucy under his wing.\n\nAll this interfered greatly with Mark’s wise resolution as to the Sowerby-bill incubus. In the first place he could not get to Barchester as soon as he had intended, and then an idea came across him that possibly it might be well that he should borrow the money of his brother John, explaining the circumstances, of course, and paying him due interest. But he had not liked to broach the subject when they were there in Exeter, standing, as it were, over their father’s grave, and so the matter was postponed. There was still ample time for arrangement before the bill would come due, and he would not tell Fanny till he had made up his mind what that arrangement would be. It would kill her, he said to himself over and over again, were he to tell her of it without being able to tell her also that the means of liquidating the debt were to be forthcoming.\n\nAnd now I must say a word about Lucy Robarts. If one might only go on without those descriptions, how pleasant it would all be! But Lucy Robarts has to play a forward part in this little drama, and those who care for such matters must be made to understand something of her form and likeness. When last we mentioned her as appearing, though not in any prominent position, at her brother’s wedding, she was only sixteen; but now, at the time of her father’s death, somewhat over two years having since elapsed, she was nearly nineteen. Laying aside for the sake of clearness that indefinite term of girl—for girls are girls from the age of three up to forty-three, if not previously married—dropping that generic word, we may say that then, at that wedding of her brother, she was a child; and now, at the death of her father, she was a woman.\n\nNothing, perhaps, adds so much to womanhood, turns the child so quickly into a woman, as such death-bed scenes as these. Hitherto but little had fallen to Lucy to do in the way of woman’s duties. Of money transactions she had known nothing, beyond a jocose attempt to make her annual allowance of twenty-five pounds cover all her personal wants—an attempt which was made jocose by the loving bounty of her father. Her sister, who was three years her elder—for John came in between them—had managed the house; that is, she had made the tea and talked to the housekeeper about the dinners. But Lucy had sat at her father’s elbow, had read to him of evenings when he went to sleep, had brought him his slippers and looked after the comforts of his easy-chair. All this she had done as a child; but when she stood at the coffin head, and knelt at the coffin side, then she was a woman.\n\nShe was smaller in stature than either of her three sisters, to all of whom had been acceded the praise of being fine women—a eulogy which the people of Exeter, looking back at the elder sisters, and the general remembrance of them which pervaded the city, were not willing to extend to Lucy. “Dear—dear!” had been said of her; “poor Lucy is not like a Robarts at all; is she, now, Mrs. Pole?”—for as the daughters had become fine women, so had the sons grown into stalwart men. And then Mrs. Pole had answered: “Not a bit; is she, now? Only think what Blanche was at her age. But she has fine eyes, for all that; and they do say she is the cleverest of them all.”\n\nAnd that, too, is so true a description of her, that I do not know that I can add much to it. She was not like Blanche; for Blanche had a bright complexion, and a fine neck, and a noble bust, et vera incessu patuit Dea—a true goddess, that is, as far as the eye went. She had a grand idea, moreover, of an apple-pie, and had not reigned eighteen months at Creamclotted Hall before she knew all the mysteries of pigs and milk, and most of those appertaining to cider and green geese.\n\nLucy had no neck at all worth speaking of,—no neck, I mean, that ever produced eloquence; she was brown, too, and had addicted herself in nowise, as she undoubtedly should have done, to larder utility. In regard to the neck and colour, poor girl, she could not help herself; but in that other respect she must be held as having wasted her opportunities.\n\nBut then what eyes she had! Mrs. Pole was right there. They flashed upon you—not always softly; indeed not often softly, if you were a stranger to her; but whether softly or savagely, with a brilliancy that dazzled you as you looked at them. And who shall say of what colour they were? Green probably, for most eyes are green—green or grey, if green be thought uncomely for an eye-colour. But it was not their colour, but their fire, which struck one with such surprise.\n\nLucy Robarts was thoroughly a brunette. Sometimes the dark tint of her cheek was exquisitely rich and lovely, and the fringes of her eyes were long and soft, and her small teeth, which one so seldom saw, were white as pearls, and her hair, though short, was beautifully soft—by no means black, but yet of so dark a shade of brown. Blanche, too, was noted for fine teeth. They were white and regular and lofty as a new row of houses in a French city. But then when she laughed she was all teeth; as she was all neck when she sat at the piano. But Lucy’s teeth!—it was only now and again, when in some sudden burst of wonder she would sit for a moment with her lips apart, that the fine finished lines and dainty pearl-white colour of that perfect set of ivory could be seen. Mrs. Pole would have said a word of her teeth also, but that to her they had never been made visible.\n\n“But they do say that she is the cleverest of them all,” Mrs. Pole had added, very properly. The people of Exeter had expressed such an opinion, and had been quite just in doing so. I do not know how it happens, but it always does happen, that everybody in every small town knows which is the brightest-witted in every family. In this respect Mrs. Pole had only expressed public opinion, and public opinion was right. Lucy Robarts was blessed with an intelligence keener than that of her brothers or sisters.\n\n“To tell the truth, Mark, I admire Lucy more than I do Blanche.” This had been said by Mrs. Robarts within a few hours of her having assumed that name. “She’s not a beauty, I know, but yet I do.”\n\n“My dearest Fanny!” Mark had answered in a tone of surprise.\n\n“I do then; of course people won’t think so; but I never seem to care about regular beauties. Perhaps I envy them too much.”\n\nWhat Mark said next need not be repeated, but everybody may be sure that it contained some gross flattery for his young bride. He remembered this, however, and had always called Lucy his wife’s pet. Neither of the sisters had since that been at Framley; and though Fanny had spent a week at Exeter on the occasion of Blanche’s marriage, it could hardly be said that she was very intimate with them. Nevertheless, when it became expedient that one of them should go to Framley, the remembrance of what his wife had said immediately induced Mark to make the offer to Lucy; and Jane, who was of a kindred soul with Blanche, was delighted to go to Creamclotted Hall. The acres of Heavybed House, down in that fat Totnes country, adjoined those of Creamclotted Hall, and Heavybed House still wanted a mistress.\n\nFanny was delighted when the news reached her. It would of course be proper that one of his sisters should live with Mark under their present circumstances, and she was happy to think that that quiet little bright-eyed creature was to come and nestle with her under the same roof. The children should so love her—only not quite so much as they loved mamma; and the snug little room that looks out over the porch, in which the chimney never smokes, should be made ready for her; and she should be allowed her share of driving the pony—which was a great sacrifice of self on the part of Mrs. Robarts—and Lady Lufton’s best good-will should be bespoken. In fact, Lucy was not unfortunate in the destination that was laid out for her.\n\nLady Lufton had of course heard of the doctor’s death, and had sent all manner of kind messages to Mark, advising him not to hurry home by any means until everything was settled at Exeter. And then she was told of the new-comer that was expected in the parish. When she heard that it was Lucy, the younger, she also was satisfied; for Blanche’s charms, though indisputable, had not been altogether to her taste. If a second Blanche were to arrive there what danger might there not be for young Lord Lufton!\n\n“Quite right,” said her ladyship, “just what he ought to do. I think I remember the young lady; rather small, is she not, and very retiring?”\n\n“Rather small and very retiring. What a description!” said Lord Lufton.\n\n“Never mind, Ludovic; some young ladies must be small, and some at least ought to be retiring. We shall be delighted to make her acquaintance.”\n\n“I remember your other sister-in-law very well,” said Lord Lufton. “She was a beautiful woman.”\n\n“I don’t think you will consider Lucy a beauty,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“Small, retiring, and—” so far Lord Lufton had gone, when Mrs. Robarts finished by the word, “plain.” She had liked Lucy’s face, but she had thought that others probably did not do so.\n\n“Upon my word,” said Lady Lufton, “you don’t deserve to have a sister-in-law. I remember her very well, and can say that she is not plain. I was very much taken with her manner at your wedding, my dear; and thought more of her than I did of the beauty, I can tell you.”\n\n“I must confess I do not remember her at all,” said his lordship. And so the conversation ended.\n\nAnd then at the end of the fortnight Mark arrived with his sister. They did not reach Framley till long after dark—somewhere between six and seven—and by this time it was December. There was snow on the ground, and frost in the air, and no moon, and cautious men when they went on the roads had their horses’ shoes cocked. Such being the state of the weather Mark’s gig had been nearly filled with cloaks and shawls when it was sent over to Silverbridge. And a cart was sent for Lucy’s luggage, and all manner of preparations had been made. Three times had Fanny gone herself to see that the fire burned brightly in the little room over the porch, and at the moment that the sound of the wheels was heard she was engaged in opening her son’s mind as to the nature of an aunt. Hitherto papa and mamma and Lady Lufton were all that he had known, excepting, of course, the satellites of the nursery.\n\nAnd then in three minutes Lucy was standing by the fire. Those three minutes had been taken up in embraces between the husband and the wife. Let who would be brought as a visitor to the house, after a fortnight’s absence, she would kiss him before she welcomed any one else. But then she turned to Lucy, and began to assist her with her cloaks.\n\n“Oh, thank you,” said Lucy; “I’m not cold,—not very at least. Don’t trouble yourself: I can do it.” But here she had made a false boast, for her fingers had been so numbed that she could not do nor undo anything.\n\nThey were all in black, of course; but the sombreness of Lucy’s clothes struck Fanny much more than her own. They seemed to have swallowed her up in their blackness, and to have made her almost an emblem of death. She did not look up, but kept her face turned towards the fire, and seemed almost afraid of her position.\n\n“She may say what she likes, Fanny,” said Mark, “but she is very cold. And so am I,—cold enough. You had better go up with her to her room. We won’t do much in the dressing way to-night; eh, Lucy?”\n\nIn the bedroom Lucy thawed a little, and Fanny, as she kissed her, said to herself that she had been wrong as to that word “plain.” Lucy, at any rate, was not plain.\n\n“You will be used to us soon,” said Fanny, “and then I hope we shall make you comfortable.” And she took her sister-in-law’s hand and pressed it.\n\nLucy looked up at her, and her eyes then were tender enough. “I am sure I shall be happy here,” she said, “with you. But—but—dear papa!” And then they got into each other’s arms, and had a great bout of kissing and crying. “Plain,” said Fanny to herself, as at last she got her guest’s hair smoothed and the tears washed from her eyes—“plain! She has the loveliest countenance that I ever looked at in my life!”\n\n“Your sister is quite beautiful,” she said to Mark, as they talked her over alone before they went to sleep that night.\n\n“No, she’s not beautiful; but she’s a very good girl, and clever enough too, in her sort of way.”\n\n“I think her perfectly lovely. I never saw such eyes in my life before.”\n\n“I’ll leave her in your hands then; you shall get her a husband.”\n\n“That mayn’t be so easy. I don’t think she’d marry anybody.”\n\n“Well, I hope not. But she seems to me to be exactly cut out for an old maid;—to be aunt Lucy for ever and ever to your bairns.”\n\n“And so she shall, with all my heart. But I don’t think she will, very long. I have no doubt she will be hard to please; but if I were a man I should fall in love with her at once. Did you ever observe her teeth, Mark?”\n\n“I don’t think I ever did.”\n\n“You wouldn’t know whether any one had a tooth in their head, I believe.”\n\n“No one except you, my dear; and I know all yours by heart.”\n\n“You are a goose.”\n\n“And a very sleepy one; so, if you please, I’ll go to roost.” And thus there was nothing more said about Lucy’s beauty on that occasion.\n\nFor the first two days Mrs. Robarts did not make much of her sister-in-law. Lucy, indeed, was not demonstrative; and she was, moreover, one of those few persons—for they are very few—who are contented to go on with their existence without making themselves the centre of any special outward circle. To the ordinary run of minds it is impossible not to do this. A man’s own dinner is to himself so important that he cannot bring himself to believe that it is a matter utterly indifferent to every one else. A lady’s collection of baby-clothes, in early years, and of house linen and curtain-fringes in later life, is so very interesting to her own eyes, that she cannot believe but what other people will rejoice to behold it. I would not, however, be held as regarding this tendency as evil. It leads to conversation of some sort among people, and perhaps to a kind of sympathy. Mrs. Jones will look at Mrs. White’s linen-chest, hoping that Mrs. White may be induced to look at hers. One can only pour out of a jug that which is in it. For the most of us, if we do not talk of ourselves, or at any rate of the individual circles of which we are the centres, we can talk of nothing. I cannot hold with those who wish to put down the insignificant chatter of the world. As for myself, I am always happy to look at Mrs. Jones’s linen, and never omit an opportunity of giving her the details of my own dinners.\n\nBut Lucy Robarts had not this gift. She had come there as a stranger into her sister-in-law’s house, and at first seemed as though she would be contented in simply having her corner in the drawing-room and her place at the parlour-table. She did not seem to need the comforts of condolence and open-hearted talking. I do not mean to say that she was moody, that she did not answer when she was spoken to, or that she took no notice of the children; but she did not at once throw herself and all her hopes and sorrows into Fanny’s heart, as Fanny would have had her do.\n\nMrs. Robarts herself was what we call demonstrative. When she was angry with Lady Lufton she showed it. And as since that time her love and admiration for Lady Lufton had increased, she showed that also. When she was in any way displeased with her husband, she could not hide it, even though she tried to do so, and fancied herself successful;—no more than she could hide her warm, constant, overflowing woman’s love. She could not walk through a room hanging on her husband’s arm without seeming to proclaim to every one there that she thought him the best man in it. She was demonstrative, and therefore she was the more disappointed in that Lucy did not rush at once with all her cares into her open heart.\n\n“She is so quiet,” Fanny said to her husband.\n\n“That’s her nature,” said Mark. “She always was quiet as a child. While we were smashing everything, she would never crack a teacup.”\n\n“I wish she would break something now,” said Fanny, “and then perhaps we should get to talk about it.” But she did not on this account give over loving her sister-in-law. She probably valued her the more, unconsciously, for not having those aptitudes with which she herself was endowed.\n\nAnd then after two days Lady Lufton called; of course it may be supposed that Fanny had said a good deal to her new inmate about Lady Lufton. A neighbour of that kind in the country exercises so large an influence upon the whole tenor of one’s life, that to abstain from such talk is out of the question. Mrs. Robarts had been brought up almost under the dowager’s wing, and of course she regarded her as being worthy of much talking. Do not let persons on this account suppose that Mrs. Robarts was a tuft-hunter, or a toad-eater. If they do not see the difference they have yet got to study the earliest principles of human nature.\n\nLady Lufton called, and Lucy was struck dumb. Fanny was particularly anxious that her ladyship’s first impression should be favourable, and to effect this, she especially endeavoured to throw the two together during that visit. But in this she was unwise. Lady Lufton, however, had woman-craft enough not to be led into any egregious error by Lucy’s silence.\n\n“And what day will you come and dine with us?” said Lady Lufton, turning expressly to her old friend Fanny.\n\n“Oh, do you name the day. We never have many engagements, you know.”\n\n“Will Thursday do, Miss Robarts? You will meet nobody you know, only my son; so you need not regard it as going out. Fanny here will tell you that stepping over to Framley Court is no more going out, than when you go from one room to another in the parsonage. Is it, Fanny?”\n\nFanny laughed and said that that stepping over to Framley Court certainly was done so often that perhaps they did not think so much about it as they ought to do.\n\n“We consider ourselves a sort of happy family here, Miss Robarts, and are delighted to have the opportunity of including you in the ménage.”\n\nLucy gave her ladyship one of her sweetest smiles, but what she said at that moment was inaudible. It was plain, however, that she could not bring herself even to go as far as Framley Court for her dinner just at present. “It was very kind of Lady Lufton,” she said to Fanny; “but it was so very soon, and—and—and if they would only go without her, she would be so happy.” But as the object was to go with her—expressly to take her there—the dinner was adjourned for a short time—sine die.\n\n# CHAPTER XI. GRISELDA GRANTLY.\n\nIt was nearly a month after this that Lucy was first introduced to Lord Lufton, and then it was brought about only by accident. During that time Lady Lufton had been often at the parsonage, and had in a certain degree learned to know Lucy; but the stranger in the parish had never yet plucked up courage to accept one of the numerous invitations that had reached her. Mr. Robarts and his wife had frequently been at Framley Court, but the dreaded day of Lucy’s initiation had not yet arrived.\n\nShe had seen Lord Lufton in church, but hardly so as to know him, and beyond that she had not seen him at all. One day, however—or rather, one evening, for it was already dusk—he overtook her and Mrs. Robarts on the road walking towards the vicarage. He had his gun on his shoulder, three pointers were at his heels, and a gamekeeper followed a little in the rear.\n\n“How are you, Mrs. Robarts?” he said, almost before he had overtaken them. “I have been chasing you along the road for the last half mile. I never knew ladies walk so fast.”\n\n“We should be frozen if we were to dawdle about as you gentlemen do,” and then she stopped and shook hands with him. She forgot at the moment that Lucy and he had not met, and therefore she did not introduce them.\n\n“Won’t you make me known to your sister-in-law?” said he, taking off his hat, and bowing to Lucy. “I have never yet had the pleasure of meeting her, though we have been neighbours for a month and more.”\n\nFanny made her excuses and introduced them, and then they went on till they came to Framley Gate, Lord Lufton talking to them both, and Fanny answering for the two, and there they stopped for a moment.\n\n“I am surprised to see you alone,” Mrs. Robarts had just said; “I thought that Captain Culpepper was with you.”\n\n“The captain has left me for this one day. If you’ll whisper I’ll tell you where he has gone. I dare not speak it out loud, even to the woods.”\n\n“To what terrible place can he have taken himself? I’ll have no whisperings about such horrors.”\n\n“He has gone to—to—but you’ll promise not to tell my mother?”\n\n“Not tell your mother! Well, now you have excited my curiosity! where can he be?”\n\n“Do you promise, then?”\n\n“Oh, yes! I will promise, because I am sure Lady Lufton won’t ask me as to Captain Culpepper’s whereabouts. We won’t tell; will we, Lucy?”\n\n“He has gone to Gatherum Castle for a day’s pheasant-shooting. Now, mind, you must not betray us. Her ladyship supposes that he is shut up in his room with a toothache. We did not dare to mention the name to her.”\n\nAnd then it appeared that Mrs. Robarts had some engagement which made it necessary that she should go up and see Lady Lufton, whereas Lucy was intending to walk on to the parsonage alone.\n\n“And I have promised to go to your husband,” said Lord Lufton; “or rather to your husband’s dog, Ponto. And I will do two other good things—I will carry a brace of pheasants with me, and protect Miss Robarts from the evil spirits of the Framley roads.” And so Mrs. Robarts turned in at the gate, and Lucy and his lordship walked off together.\n\nLord Lufton, though he had never before spoken to Miss Robarts, had already found out that she was by no means plain. Though he had hardly seen her except at church, he had already made himself certain that the owner of that face must be worth knowing, and was not sorry to have the present opportunity of speaking to her. “So you have an unknown damsel shut up in your castle,” he had once said to Mrs. Robarts. “If she be kept a prisoner much longer, I shall find it my duty to come and release her by force of arms.” He had been there twice with the object of seeing her, but on both occasions Lucy had managed to escape. Now we may say she was fairly caught, and Lord Lufton, taking a pair of pheasants from the gamekeeper, and swinging them over his shoulder, walked off with his prey.\n\n“You have been here a long time,” he said, “without our having had the pleasure of seeing you.”\n\n“Yes, my lord,” said Lucy. Lords had not been frequent among her acquaintance hitherto.\n\n“I tell Mrs. Robarts that she has been confining you illegally, and that we shall release you by force or stratagem.”\n\n“I—I—I have had a great sorrow lately.”\n\n“Yes, Miss Robarts; I know you have; and I am only joking, you know. But I do hope that now you will be able to come amongst us. My mother is so anxious that you should do so.”\n\n“I am sure she is very kind, and you also—my lord.”\n\n“I never knew my own father,” said Lord Lufton, speaking gravely. “But I can well understand what a loss you have had.” And then, after pausing a moment, he continued, “I remember Dr. Robarts well.”\n\n“Do you, indeed?” said Lucy, turning sharply towards him, and speaking now with some animation in her voice. Nobody had yet spoken to her about her father since she had been at Framley. It had been as though the subject were a forbidden one. And how frequently is this the case! When those we love are dead, our friends dread to mention them, though to us who are bereaved no subject would be so pleasant as their names. But we rarely understand how to treat our own sorrow or those of others.\n\nThere was once a people in some land—and they may be still there for what I know—who thought it sacrilegious to stay the course of a raging fire. If a house were being burned, burn it must, even though there were facilities for saving it. For who would dare to interfere with the course of the god? Our idea of sorrow is much the same. We think it wicked, or at any rate heartless, to put it out. If a man’s wife be dead, he should go about lugubrious, with long face, for at least two years, or perhaps with full length for eighteen months, decreasing gradually during the other six. If he be a man who can quench his sorrow—put out his fire as it were—in less time than that, let him at any rate not show his power!\n\n“Yes: I remember him,” continued Lord Lufton. “He came twice to Framley while I was a boy, consulting with my mother about Mark and myself,—whether the Eton floggings were not more efficacious than those at Harrow. He was very kind to me, foreboding all manner of good things on my behalf.”\n\n“He was very kind to every one,” said Lucy.\n\n“I should think he would have been—a kind, good, genial man—just the man to be adored by his own family.”\n\n“Exactly; and so he was. I do not remember that I ever heard an unkind word from him. There was not a harsh tone in his voice. And he was generous as the day.” Lucy, we have said, was not generally demonstrative, but now, on this subject, and with this absolute stranger, she became almost eloquent.\n\n“I do not wonder that you should feel his loss, Miss Robarts.”\n\n“Oh, I do feel it. Mark is the best of brothers, and, as for Fanny, she is too kind and too good to me. But I had always been specially my father’s friend. For the last year or two we had lived so much together!”\n\n“He was an old man when he died, was he not?”\n\n“Just seventy, my lord.”\n\n“Ah, then he was old. My mother is only fifty, and we sometimes call her the old woman. Do you think she looks older than that? We all say that she makes herself out to be so much more ancient than she need do.”\n\n“Lady Lufton does not dress young.”\n\n“That is it. She never has, in my memory. She always used to wear black when I first recollect her. She has given that up now; but she is still very sombre; is she not?”\n\n“I do not like ladies to dress very young, that is, ladies of—of—”\n\n“Ladies of fifty, we will say?”\n\n“Very well; ladies of fifty, if you like it.”\n\n“Then I am sure you will like my mother.”\n\nThey had now turned up through the parsonage wicket, a little gate that opened into the garden at a point on the road nearer than the chief entrance.\n\n“I suppose I shall find Mark up at the house?” said he.\n\n“I daresay you will, my lord.”\n\n“Well, I’ll go round this way, for my business is partly in the stable. You see I am quite at home here, though you never have seen me before. But, Miss Robarts, now that the ice is broken, I hope that we may be friends.” He then put out his hand, and when she gave him hers he pressed it almost as an old friend might have done.\n\nAnd, indeed, Lucy had talked to him almost as though he were an old friend. For a minute or two she had forgotten that he was a lord and a stranger—had forgotten also to be stiff and guarded as was her wont. Lord Lufton had spoken to her as though he had really cared to know her; and she, unconsciously, had been taken by the compliment. Lord Lufton, indeed, had not thought much about it—excepting as thus, that he liked the glance of a pair of bright eyes, as most other young men do like it. But, on this occasion, the evening had been so dark, that he had hardly seen Lucy’s eyes at all.\n\n“Well, Lucy, I hope you liked your companion,” Mrs. Robarts said, as the three of them clustered round the drawing-room fire before dinner.\n\n“Oh, yes; pretty well,” said Lucy.\n\n“That is not at all complimentary to his lordship.”\n\n“I did not mean to be complimentary, Fanny.”\n\n“Lucy is a great deal too matter-of-fact for compliments,” said Mark.\n\n“What I meant was, that I had no great opportunity for judging, seeing that I was only with Lord Lufton for about ten minutes.”\n\n“Ah! but there are girls here who would give their eyes for ten minutes of Lord Lufton to themselves. You do not know how he’s valued. He has the character of being always able to make himself agreeable to ladies at half a minute’s warning.”\n\n“Perhaps he had not the half-minute’s warning in this case,” said Lucy,—hypocrite that she was.\n\n“Poor Lucy,” said her brother; “he was coming up to see Ponto’s shoulder, and I am afraid he was thinking more about the dog than you.”\n\n“Very likely,” said Lucy; and then they went in to dinner.\n\nLucy had been a hypocrite, for she had confessed to herself, while dressing, that Lord Lufton had been very pleasant; but then it is allowed to young ladies to be hypocrites when the subject under discussion is the character of a young gentleman.\n\nSoon after that, Lucy did dine at Framley Court. Captain Culpepper, in spite of his enormity with reference to Gatherum Castle, was still staying there, as was also a clergyman from the neighbourhood of Barchester with his wife and daughter. This was Archdeacon Grantly, a gentleman whom we have mentioned before, and who was as well known in the diocese as the bishop himself,—and more thought about by many clergymen than even that illustrious prelate.\n\nMiss Grantly was a young lady not much older than Lucy Robarts, and she also was quiet, and not given to much talking in open company. She was decidedly a beauty, but somewhat statuesque in her loveliness. Her forehead was high and white, but perhaps too like marble to gratify the taste of those who are fond of flesh and blood. Her eyes were large and exquisitely formed, but they seldom showed much emotion. She, indeed, was impassive herself, and betrayed but little of her feelings. Her nose was nearly Grecian, not coming absolutely in a straight line from her forehead, but doing so nearly enough to entitle it to be considered as classical. Her mouth, too, was very fine—artists, at least, said so, and connoisseurs in beauty; but to me she always seemed as though she wanted fulness of lip. But the exquisite symmetry of her cheek and chin and lower face no man could deny. Her hair was light, and being always dressed with considerable care, did not detract from her appearance; but it lacked that richness which gives such luxuriance to feminine loveliness. She was tall and slight, and very graceful in her movements; but there were those who thought that she wanted the ease and abandon of youth. They said that she was too composed and stiff for her age, and that she gave but little to society beyond the beauty of her form and face.\n\nThere can be no doubt, however, that she was considered by most men and women to be the beauty of Barsetshire, and that gentlemen from neighbouring counties would come many miles through dirty roads on the mere hope of being able to dance with her. Whatever attractions she may have lacked, she had at any rate created for herself a great reputation. She had spent two months of the last spring in London, and even there she had made a sensation; and people had said that Lord Dumbello, Lady Hartletop’s eldest son, had been peculiarly struck with her.\n\nIt may be imagined that the archdeacon was proud of her, and so indeed was Mrs. Grantly—more proud, perhaps, of her daughter’s beauty, than so excellent a woman should have allowed herself to be of such an attribute. Griselda—that was her name—was now an only daughter. One sister she had had, but that sister had died. There were two brothers also left, one in the Church and the other in the army. That was the extent of the archdeacon’s family, and as the archdeacon was a very rich man—he was the only child of his father, who had been Bishop of Barchester for a great many years; and in those years it had been worth a man’s while to be Bishop of Barchester—it was supposed that Miss Grantly would have a large fortune. Mrs. Grantly, however, had been heard to say, that she was in no hurry to see her daughter established in the world;—ordinary young ladies are merely married, but those of real importance are established:—and this, if anything, added to the value of the prize. Mothers sometimes depreciate their wares by an undue solicitude to dispose of them.\n\nBut to tell the truth openly and at once—a virtue for which a novelist does not receive very much commendation—Griselda Grantly was, to a certain extent, already given away. Not that she, Griselda, knew anything about it, or that the thrice happy gentleman had been made aware of his good fortune; nor even had the archdeacon been told. But Mrs. Grantly and Lady Lufton had been closeted together more than once, and terms had been signed and sealed between them. Not signed on parchment, and sealed with wax, as is the case with treaties made by kings and diplomats,—to be broken by the same; but signed with little words, and sealed with certain pressings of the hand,—a treaty which between two such contracting parties would be binding enough. And by the terms of this treaty Griselda Grantly was to become Lady Lufton.\n\nLady Lufton had hitherto been fortunate in her matrimonial speculations. She had selected Sir George for her daughter, and Sir George, with the utmost good nature, had fallen in with her views. She had selected Fanny Monsell for Mr. Robarts, and Fanny Monsell had not rebelled against her for a moment. There was a prestige of success about her doings, and she felt almost confident that her dear son Ludovic must fall in love with Griselda.\n\nAs to the lady herself, nothing, Lady Lufton thought, could be much better than such a match for her son. Lady Lufton, I have said, was a good churchwoman, and the archdeacon was the very type of that branch of the Church which she venerated. The Grantlys, too, were of a good family,—not noble, indeed; but in such matters Lady Lufton did not want everything. She was one of those persons who, in placing their hopes at a moderate pitch, may fairly trust to see them realized. She would fain that her son’s wife should be handsome; this she wished for his sake, that he might be proud of his wife, and because men love to look on beauty. But she was afraid of vivacious beauty, of those soft, sparkling feminine charms which are spread out as lures for all the world, soft dimples, laughing eyes, luscious lips, conscious smiles, and easy whispers. What if her son should bring her home a rattling, rapid-spoken, painted piece of Eve’s flesh such as this? Would not the glory and joy of her life be over, even though such child of their first mother should have come forth to the present day ennobled by the blood of two dozen successive British peers?\n\nAnd then, too, Griselda’s money would not be useless. Lady Lufton, with all her high-flown ideas, was not an imprudent woman. She knew that her son had been extravagant, though she did not believe that he had been reckless; and she was well content to think that some balsam from the old bishop’s coffers should be made to cure the slight wounds which his early imprudence might have inflicted on the carcase of the family property. And thus, in this way, and for these reasons, Griselda Grantly had been chosen out from all the world to be the future Lady Lufton.\n\nLord Lufton had met Griselda more than once already; had met her before these high contracting parties had come to any terms whatsoever, and had evidently admired her. Lord Dumbello had remained silent one whole evening in London with ineffable disgust, because Lord Lufton had been rather particular in his attentions; but then Lord Dumbello’s muteness was his most eloquent mode of expression. Both Lady Hartletop and Mrs. Grantly, when they saw him, knew very well what he meant. But that match would not exactly have suited Mrs. Grantly’s views. The Hartletop people were not in her line. They belonged altogether to another set, being connected, as we have heard before, with the Omnium interest—“those horrid Gatherum people,” as Lady Lufton would say to her, raising her hands and eyebrows, and shaking her head. Lady Lufton probably thought that they ate babies in pies during their midnight orgies at Gatherum Castle; and that widows were kept in cells, and occasionally put on racks for the amusement of the duke’s guests.\n\nWhen the Robarts’s party entered the drawing-room the Grantlys were already there, and the archdeacon’s voice sounded loud and imposing in Lucy’s ears, as she heard him speaking while she was yet on the threshold of the door.\n\n“My dear Lady Lufton, I would believe anything on earth about her—anything. There is nothing too outrageous for her. Had she insisted on going there with the bishop’s apron on, I should not have been surprised.” And then they all knew that the archdeacon was talking about Mrs. Proudie, for Mrs. Proudie was his bugbear.\n\nLady Lufton after receiving her guests introduced Lucy to Griselda Grantly. Miss Grantly smiled graciously, bowed slightly, and then remarked in the lowest voice possible that it was exceedingly cold. A low voice, we know, is an excellent thing in woman.\n\nLucy, who thought that she was bound to speak, said that it was cold, but that she did not mind it when she was walking. And then Griselda smiled again, somewhat less graciously than before, and so the conversation ended. Miss Grantly was the elder of the two, and having seen most of the world, should have been the best able to talk, but perhaps she was not very anxious for a conversation with Miss Robarts.\n\n“So, Robarts, I hear that you have been preaching at Chaldicotes,” said the archdeacon, still rather loudly. “I saw Sowerby the other day, and he told me that you gave them the fag end of Mrs. Proudie’s lecture.”\n\n“It was ill-natured of Sowerby to say the fag end,” said Robarts. “We divided the matter into thirds. Harold Smith took the first part, I the last—”\n\n“And the lady the intervening portion. You have electrified the county between you; but I am told that she had the best of it.”\n\n“I was so sorry that Mr. Robarts went there,” said Lady Lufton, as she walked into the dining-room leaning on the archdeacon’s arm.\n\n“I am inclined to think he could not very well have helped himself,” said the archdeacon, who was never willing to lean heavily on a brother parson, unless on one who had utterly and irrevocably gone away from his side of the Church.\n\n“Do you think not, archdeacon?”\n\n“Why, no: Sowerby is a friend of Lufton’s—”\n\n“Not particularly,” said poor Lady Lufton, in a deprecating tone.\n\n“Well, they have been intimate; and Robarts, when he was asked to preach at Chaldicotes, could not well refuse.”\n\n“But then he went afterwards to Gatherum Castle. Not that I am vexed with him at all now, you understand. But it is such a dangerous house, you know.”\n\n“So it is.—But the very fact of the duke’s wishing to have a clergyman there, should always be taken as a sign of grace, Lady Lufton. The air was impure, no doubt; but it was less impure with Robarts there than it would have been without him. But, gracious heavens! what blasphemy have I been saying about impure air? Why, the bishop was there!”\n\n“Yes, the bishop was there,” said Lady Lufton, and they both understood each other thoroughly.\n\nLord Lufton took out Mrs. Grantly to dinner, and matters were so managed that Miss Grantly sat on his other side. There was no management apparent in this to anybody; but there she was, while Lucy was placed between her brother and Captain Culpepper. Captain Culpepper was a man with an enormous moustache, and a great aptitude for slaughtering game; but as he had no other strong characteristics, it was not probable that he would make himself very agreeable to poor Lucy.\n\nShe had seen Lord Lufton once, for two minutes, since the day of that walk, and then he had addressed her quite like an old friend. It had been in the parsonage drawing-room, and Fanny had been there. Fanny now was so well accustomed to his lordship, that she thought but little of this, but to Lucy it had been very pleasant. He was not forward or familiar, but kind, and gentle, and pleasant; and Lucy did feel that she liked him.\n\nNow, on this evening, he had hitherto hardly spoken to her; but then she knew that there were other people in the company to whom he was bound to speak. She was not exactly humble-minded in the usual sense of the word; but she did recognize the fact that her position was less important than that of other people there, and that therefore it was probable that to a certain extent she would be overlooked. But not the less would she have liked to occupy the seat to which Miss Grantly had found her way. She did not want to flirt with Lord Lufton; she was not such a fool as that; but she would have liked to have heard the sound of his voice close to her ear, instead of that of Captain Culpepper’s knife and fork.\n\nThis was the first occasion on which she had endeavoured to dress herself with care since her father had died; and now, sombre though she was in her deep mourning, she did look very well.\n\n“There is an expression about her forehead that is full of poetry,” Fanny had said to her husband.\n\n“Don’t you turn her head, Fanny, and make her believe that she is a beauty,” Mark had answered.\n\n“I doubt it is not so easy to turn her head, Mark. There is more in Lucy than you imagine, and so you will find out before long.” It was thus that Mrs. Robarts prophesied about her sister-in-law. Had she been asked she might perhaps have said that Lucy’s presence would be dangerous to the Grantly interest at Framley Court.\n\nLord Lufton’s voice was audible enough as he went on talking to Miss Grantly—his voice, but not his words. He talked in such a way that there was no appearance of whispering, and yet the person to whom he spoke, and she only, could hear what he said. Mrs. Grantly the while conversed constantly with Lucy’s brother, who sat at Lucy’s left hand. She never lacked for subjects on which to speak to a country clergyman of the right sort, and thus Griselda was left quite uninterrupted.\n\nBut Lucy could not but observe that Griselda herself seemed to have very little to say,—or at any rate to say very little. Every now and then she did open her mouth, and some word or brace of words would fall from it. But for the most part she seemed to be content in the fact that Lord Lufton was paying her attention. She showed no animation, but sat there still and graceful, composed and classical, as she always was. Lucy, who could not keep her ears from listening or her eyes from looking, thought that had she been there she would have endeavoured to take a more prominent part in the conversation. But then Griselda Grantly probably knew much better than Lucy did how to comport herself in such a situation. Perhaps it might be that young men, such as Lord Lufton, liked to hear the sound of their own voices.\n\n“Immense deal of game about here,” Captain Culpepper said to her towards the end of the dinner. It was the second attempt he had made; on the former he had asked her whether she knew any of the fellows of the 9th.\n\n“Is there?” said Lucy. “Oh! I saw Lord Lufton the other day with a great armful of pheasants.”\n\n“An armful! Why we had seven cartloads the other day at Gatherum.”\n\n“Seven carts full of pheasants!” said Lucy, amazed.\n\n“That’s not so much. We had eight guns, you know. Eight guns will do a deal of work when the game has been well got together. They manage all that capitally at Gatherum. Been at the duke’s, eh?”\n\nLucy had heard the Framley report as to Gatherum Castle, and said with a sort of shudder that she had never been at that place. After this, Captain Culpepper troubled her no further.\n\nWhen the ladies had taken themselves to the drawing-room Lucy found herself hardly better off than she had been at the dinner-table. Lady Lufton and Mrs. Grantly got themselves on to a sofa together, and there chatted confidentially into each other’s ears. Her ladyship had introduced Lucy and Miss Grantly, and then she naturally thought that the young people might do very well together. Mrs. Robarts did attempt to bring about a joint conversation, which should include the three, and for ten minutes or so she worked hard at it. But it did not thrive. Miss Grantly was monosyllabic, smiling, however, at every monosyllable; and Lucy found that nothing would occur to her at that moment worthy of being spoken. There she sat, still and motionless, afraid to take up a book, and thinking in her heart how much happier she would have been at home at the parsonage. She was not made for society; she felt sure of that; and another time she would let Mark and Fanny come to Framley Court by themselves.\n\nAnd then the gentlemen came in, and there was another stir in the room. Lady Lufton got up and bustled about; she poked the fire and shifted the candles, spoke a few words to Dr. Grantly, whispered something to her son, patted Lucy on the cheek, told Fanny, who was a musician, that they would have a little music, and ended by putting her two hands on Griselda’s shoulders and telling her that the fit of her frock was perfect. For Lady Lufton, though she did dress old herself, as Lucy had said, delighted to see those around her neat and pretty, jaunty and graceful.\n\n“Dear Lady Lufton!” said Griselda, putting up her hand so as to press the end of her ladyship’s fingers. It was the first piece of animation she had shown, and Lucy Robarts watched it all.\n\nAnd then there was music. Lucy neither played nor sang; Fanny did both, and for an amateur did both well. Griselda did not sing, but she played; and did so in a manner that showed that neither her own labour nor her father’s money had been spared in her instruction. Lord Lufton sang also, a little, and Captain Culpepper a very little; so that they got up a concert among them. In the meantime the doctor and Mark stood talking together on the rug before the fire; the two mothers sat contented, watching the billings and the cooings of their offspring—and Lucy sat alone, turning over the leaves of a book of pictures. She made up her mind fully, then and there, that she was quite unfitted by disposition for such work as this. She cared for no one, and no one cared for her. Well, she must go through with it now; but another time she would know better. With her own book and a fireside she never felt herself to be miserable as she was now.\n\nShe had turned her back to the music, for she was sick of seeing Lord Lufton watch the artistic motion of Miss Grantly’s fingers, and was sitting at a small table as far away from the piano as a long room would permit, when she was suddenly roused from a reverie of self-reproach by a voice close behind her: “Miss Robarts,” said the voice, “why have you cut us all?” and Lucy felt that though she heard the words plainly, nobody else did. Lord Lufton was now speaking to her as he had before spoken to Miss Grantly.\n\n“I don’t play, my lord,” said Lucy, “nor yet sing.”\n\n“That would have made your company so much more valuable to us, for we are terribly badly off for listeners. Perhaps you don’t like music?”\n\n“I do like it,—sometimes very much.”\n\n“And when are the sometimes? But we shall find it all out in time. We shall have unravelled all your mysteries, and read all your riddles, by—when shall I say?—by the end of the winter. Shall we not?”\n\n“I do not know that I have got any mysteries.”\n\n“Oh, but you have! It is very mysterious in you to come and sit here, with your back to us all—”\n\n“Oh, Lord Lufton; if I have done wrong—!” and poor Lucy almost started from her chair, and a deep flush came across her dark cheek.\n\n“No—no; you have done no wrong. I was only joking. It is we who have done wrong in leaving you to yourself—you who are the greatest stranger among us.”\n\n“I have been very well, thank you. I don’t care about being left alone. I have always been used to it.”\n\n“Ah! but we must break you of the habit. We won’t allow you to make a hermit of yourself. But the truth is, Miss Robarts, you don’t know us yet, and therefore you are not quite happy among us.”\n\n“Oh! yes, I am; you are all very good to me.”\n\n“You must let us be good to you. At any rate, you must let me be so. You know, don’t you, that Mark and I have been dear friends since we were seven years old. His wife has been my sister’s dearest friend almost as long; and now that you are with them, you must be a dear friend too. You won’t refuse the offer; will you?”\n\n“Oh, no,” she said, quite in a whisper; and, indeed, she could hardly raise her voice above a whisper, fearing that tears would fall from her tell-tale eyes.\n\n“Dr. and Mrs. Grantly will have gone in a couple of days, and then we must get you down here. Miss Grantly is to remain for Christmas, and you two must become bosom friends.”\n\nLucy smiled, and tried to look pleased, but she felt that she and Griselda Grantly could never be bosom friends—could never have anything in common between them. She felt sure that Griselda despised her, little, brown, plain, and unimportant as she was. She herself could not despise Griselda in turn; indeed she could not but admire Miss Grantly’s great beauty and dignity of demeanour; but she knew that she could never love her. It is hardly possible that the proud-hearted should love those who despise them; and Lucy Robarts was very proud-hearted.\n\n“Don’t you think she is very handsome?” said Lord Lufton.\n\n“Oh, very,” said Lucy. “Nobody can doubt that.”\n\n“Ludovic,” said Lady Lufton—not quite approving of her son’s remaining so long at the back of Lucy’s chair—“won’t you give us another song? Mrs. Robarts and Miss Grantly are still at the piano.”\n\n“I have sung away all that I knew, mother. There’s Culpepper has not had a chance yet. He has got to give us his dream—how he ‘dreamt that he dwelt in marble halls!’”\n\n“I sang that an hour ago,” said the captain, not over pleased.\n\n“But you certainly have not told us how ‘your little lovers came!’”\n\nThe captain, however, would not sing any more. And then the party was broken up, and the Robartses went home to their parsonage.\n\n# CHAPTER XII. THE LITTLE BILL.\n\nLucy, during those last fifteen minutes of her sojourn in the Framley Court drawing-room, somewhat modified the very strong opinion she had before formed as to her unfitness for such society. It was very pleasant sitting there in that easy chair, while Lord Lufton stood at the back of it saying nice, soft, good-natured words to her. She was sure that in a little time she could feel a true friendship for him, and that she could do so without any risk of falling in love with him. But then she had a glimmering of an idea that such a friendship would be open to all manner of remarks, and would hardly be compatible with the world’s ordinary ways. At any rate it would be pleasant to be at Framley Court, if he would come and occasionally notice her. But she did not admit to herself that such a visit would be intolerable if his whole time were devoted to Griselda Grantly. She neither admitted it, nor thought it; but nevertheless, in a strange unconscious way, such a feeling did find entrance in her bosom.\n\nAnd then the Christmas holidays passed away. How much of this enjoyment fell to her share, and how much of this suffering she endured, we will not attempt accurately to describe. Miss Grantly remained at Framley Court up to Twelfth Night, and the Robartses also spent most of the season at the house. Lady Lufton, no doubt, had hoped that everything might have been arranged on this occasion in accordance with her wishes, but such had not been the case. Lord Lufton had evidently admired Miss Grantly very much; indeed, he had said so to his mother half-a-dozen times; but it may almost be questioned whether the pleasure Lady Lufton derived from this was not more than neutralized by an opinion he once put forward that Griselda Grantly wanted some of the fire of Lucy Robarts.\n\n“Surely, Ludovic, you would never compare the two girls,” said Lady Lufton.\n\n“Of course not. They are the very antipodes to each other. Miss Grantly would probably be more to my taste; but then I am wise enough to know that it is so because my taste is a bad taste.”\n\n“I know no man with a more accurate or refined taste in such matters,” said Lady Lufton. Beyond this she did not dare to go. She knew very well that her strategy would be vain should her son once learn that she had a strategy. To tell the truth, Lady Lufton was becoming somewhat indifferent to Lucy Robarts. She had been very kind to the little girl; but the little girl seemed hardly to appreciate the kindness as she should do—and then Lord Lufton would talk to Lucy, “which was so unnecessary, you know;” and Lucy had got into a way of talking quite freely with Lord Lufton, having completely dropped that short, spasmodic, ugly exclamation of “my lord.”\n\nAnd so the Christmas festivities were at an end, and January wore itself away. During the greater part of this month Lord Lufton did not remain at Framley, but was nevertheless in the county, hunting with the hounds of both divisions, and staying at various houses. Two or three nights he spent at Chaldicotes; and one—let it only be told in an under voice—at Gatherum Castle! Of this he said nothing to Lady Lufton. “Why make her unhappy?” as he said to Mark. But Lady Lufton knew it, though she said not a word to him—knew it, and was unhappy. “If he would only marry Griselda, there would be an end of that danger,” she said to herself.\n\nBut now we must go back for a while to the vicar and his little bill. It will be remembered, that his first idea with reference to that trouble, after the reading of his father’s will, was to borrow the money from his brother John. John was down at Exeter at the time, and was to stay one night at the parsonage on his way to London. Mark would broach the matter to him on the journey, painful though it would be to him to tell the story of his own folly to a brother so much younger than himself, and who had always looked up to him, clergyman and full-blown vicar as he was, with a deference greater than that which such difference in age required.\n\nThe story was told, however; but was told all in vain, as Mark found out before he reached Framley. His brother John immediately declared that he would lend him the money, of course—eight hundred, if his brother wanted it. He, John, confessed that, as regarded the remaining two, he should like to feel the pleasure of immediate possession. As for interest, he would not take any—take interest from a brother! of course not. Well, if Mark made such a fuss about it, he supposed he must take it; but would rather not. Mark should have his own way, and do just what he liked.\n\nThis was all very well, and Mark had fully made up his mind that his brother should not be kept long out of his money. But then arose the question, how was that money to be reached? He, Mark, was executor, or one of the executors under his father’s will, and, therefore, no doubt, could put his hand upon it; but his brother wanted five months of being of age, and could not therefore as yet be put legally in possession of the legacy.\n\n“That’s a bore,” said the assistant private secretary to the Lord Petty Bag, thinking, perhaps, as much of his own immediate wish for ready cash as he did of his brother’s necessities. Mark felt that it was a bore, but there was nothing more to be done in that direction. He must now find out how far the bankers could assist him.\n\nSome week or two after his return to Framley he went over to Barchester, and called there on a certain Mr. Forrest, the manager of one of the banks, with whom he was acquainted; and with many injunctions as to secrecy told this manager the whole of his story. At first he concealed the name of his friend Sowerby, but it soon appeared that no such concealment was of any avail. “That’s Sowerby, of course,” said Mr. Forrest. “I know you are intimate with him; and all his friends go through that, sooner or later.”\n\nIt seemed to Mark as though Mr. Forrest made very light of the whole transaction.\n\n“I cannot possibly pay the bill when it falls due,” said Mark.\n\n“Oh, no, of course not,” said Mr. Forrest. “It’s never very convenient to hand out four hundred pounds at a blow. Nobody will expect you to pay it!”\n\n“But I suppose I shall have to do it sooner or later?”\n\n“Well, that’s as may be. It will depend partly on how you manage with Sowerby, and partly on the hands it gets into. As the bill has your name on it, they’ll have patience as long as the interest is paid, and the commissions on renewal. But no doubt it will have to be met some day by somebody.”\n\nMr. Forrest said that he was sure that the bill was not in Barchester; Mr. Sowerby would not, he thought, have brought it to a Barchester bank. The bill was probably in London, but doubtless would be sent to Barchester for collection. “If it comes in my way,” said Mr. Forrest, “I will give you plenty of time, so that you may manage about the renewal with Sowerby. I suppose he’ll pay the expense of doing that.”\n\nMark’s heart was somewhat lighter as he left the bank. Mr. Forrest had made so little of the whole transaction that he felt himself justified in making little of it also. “It may be as well,” said he to himself, as he drove home, “not to tell Fanny anything about it till the three months have run round. I must make some arrangement then.” And in this way his mind was easier during the last of those three months than it had been during the two former. That feeling of over-due bills, of bills coming due, of accounts overdrawn, of tradesmen unpaid, of general money cares, is very dreadful at first; but it is astonishing how soon men get used to it. A load which would crush a man at first becomes, by habit, not only endurable, but easy and comfortable to the bearer. The habitual debtor goes along jaunty and with elastic step, almost enjoying the excitement of his embarrassments. There was Mr. Sowerby himself; who ever saw a cloud on his brow? It made one almost in love with ruin to be in his company. And even now, already, Mark Robarts was thinking to himself quite comfortably about this bill;—how very pleasantly those bankers managed these things. Pay it! No; no one will be so unreasonable as to expect you to do that! And then Mr. Sowerby certainly was a pleasant fellow, and gave a man something in return for his money. It was still a question with Mark whether Lord Lufton had not been too hard on Sowerby. Had that gentleman fallen across his clerical friend at the present moment, he might no doubt have gotten from him an acceptance for another four hundred pounds.\n\nOne is almost inclined to believe that there is something pleasurable in the excitement of such embarrassments, as there is also in the excitement of drink. But then, at last, the time does come when the excitement is over, and when nothing but the misery is left. If there be an existence of wretchedness on earth it must be that of the elderly, worn-out roué, who has run this race of debt and bills of accommodation and acceptances,—of what, if we were not in these days somewhat afraid of good broad English, we might call lying and swindling, falsehood and fraud—and who, having ruined all whom he should have loved, having burnt up every one who would trust him much, and scorched all who would trust him a little, is at last left to finish his life with such bread and water as these men get, without one honest thought to strengthen his sinking heart, or one honest friend to hold his shivering hand! If a man could only think of that, as he puts his name to the first little bill, as to which he is so good-naturedly assured that it can easily be renewed!\n\nWhen the three months had nearly run out, it so happened that Robarts met his friend Sowerby. Mark had once or twice ridden with Lord Lufton as far as the meet of the hounds, and may, perhaps, have gone a field or two farther on some occasions. The reader must not think that he had taken to hunting, as some parsons do; and it is singular enough that whenever they do so they always show a special aptitude for the pursuit, as though hunting were an employment peculiarly congenial with a cure of souls in the country. Such a thought would do our vicar injustice. But when Lord Lufton would ask him what on earth could be the harm of riding along the roads to look at the hounds, he hardly knew what sensible answer to give his lordship. It would be absurd to say that his time would be better employed at home in clerical matters, for it was notorious that he had not clerical pursuits for the employment of half his time. In this way, therefore, he had got into a habit of looking at the hounds, and keeping up his acquaintance in the county, meeting Lord Dumbello, Mr. Green Walker, Harold Smith, and other such like sinners; and on one such occasion, as the three months were nearly closing, he did meet Mr. Sowerby.\n\n“Look here, Sowerby; I want to speak to you for half a moment. What are you doing about that bill?”\n\n“Bill—bill! what bill?—which bill? The whole bill, and nothing but the bill. That seems to be the conversation now-a-days of all men, morning, noon, and night.”\n\n“Don’t you know the bill I signed for you for four hundred pounds?”\n\n“Did you, though? Was not that rather green of you?”\n\nThis did seem strange to Mark. Could it really be the fact that Mr. Sowerby had so many bills flying about that he had absolutely forgotten that occurrence in the Gatherum Castle bedroom? And then to be called green by the very man whom he had obliged!\n\n“Perhaps I was,” said Mark, in a tone that showed that he was somewhat piqued. “But all the same I should be glad to know how it will be taken up.”\n\n“Oh, Mark, what a ruffian you are to spoil my day’s sport in this way. Any man but a parson would be too good a Christian for such intense cruelty. But let me see—four hundred pounds? Oh, yes—Tozer has it.”\n\n“And what will Tozer do with it?”\n\n“Make money of it; whatever way he may go to work he will do that.”\n\n“But will Tozer bring it to me on the 20th?”\n\n“Oh, Lord, no! Upon my word, Mark, you are deliciously green. A cat would as soon think of killing a mouse directly she got it into her claws. But, joking apart, you need not trouble yourself. Maybe you will hear no more about it; or, perhaps, which no doubt is more probable, I may have to send it to you to be renewed. But you need do nothing till you hear from me or somebody else.”\n\n“Only do not let any one come down upon me for the money.”\n\n“There is not the slightest fear of that. Tally-ho, old fellow! He’s away. Tally-ho! right over by Gossetts’ barn. Come along, and never mind Tozer—‘Sufficient for the day is the evil thereof.’” And away they both went together, parson and member of Parliament.\n\nAnd then again on that occasion Mark went home with a sort of feeling that the bill did not matter. Tozer would manage it somehow; and it was quite clear that it would not do to tell his wife of it just at present.\n\nOn the 21st of that month of February, however, he did receive a reminder that the bill and all concerning it had not merely been a farce. This was a letter from Mr. Sowerby, dated from Chaldicotes, though not bearing the Barchester post-mark, in which that gentleman suggested a renewal—not exactly of the old bill, but of a new one. It seemed to Mark that the letter had been posted in London. If I give it entire, I shall, perhaps, most quickly explain its purport:\n\nChaldicotes,—20th February, 185—.\n\nMY DEAR MARK,—“Lend not thy name to the money-dealers, for the same is a destruction and a snare.” If that be not in the Proverbs, it ought to be. Tozer has given me certain signs of his being alive and strong this cold weather. As we can neither of us take up that bill for £400 at the moment, we must renew it, and pay him his commission and interest, with all the rest of his perquisites, and pickings, and stealings—from all which, I can assure you, Tozer does not keep his hands as he should do.\n\nTo cover this and some other little outstanding trifles, I have filled in the new bill for £500, making it due 23rd of May next. Before that time, a certain accident will, I trust, have occurred to your impoverished friend. By-the-by, I never told you how she went off from Gatherum Castle, the morning after you left us, with the Greshams. Cart-ropes would not hold her, even though the duke held them; which he did, with all the strength of his ducal hands. She would go to meet some doctor of theirs, and so I was put off for that time; but I think that the matter stands in a good train.\n\nDo not lose a post in sending back the bill accepted, as Tozer may annoy you—nay, undoubtedly will, if the matter be not in his hand, duly signed by both of us, the day after to-morrow. He is an ungrateful brute; he has lived on me for these eight years, and would not let me off a single squeeze now to save my life. But I am specially anxious to save you from the annoyance and cost of lawyers’ letters; and if delayed, it might get into the papers.\n\nPut it under cover to me, at No. 7, Duke Street, St. James’s. I shall be in town by that time.\n\nGood-bye, old fellow. That was a decent brush we had the other day from Cobbold’s Ashes. I wish I could get that brown horse from you. I would not mind going to a hundred and thirty.\n\nYours ever,\n\nWhen Mark had read it through he looked down on his table to see whether the old bill had fallen from the letter; but no, there was no enclosure, and had been no enclosure but the new bill. And then he read the letter through again, and found that there was no word about the old bill,—not a syllable, at least, as to its whereabouts. Sowerby did not even say that it would remain in his own hands.\n\nMark did not in truth know much about such things. It might be that the very fact of his signing this second document would render that first document null and void; and from Sowerby’s silence on the subject, it might be argued that this was so well known to be the case, that he had not thought of explaining it. But yet Mark could not see how this should be so.\n\nBut what was he to do? That threat of cost and lawyers, and specially of the newspapers, did have its effect upon him—as no doubt it was intended to do. And then he was utterly dumfounded by Sowerby’s impudence in drawing on him for £500 instead of £400, “covering,” as Sowerby so good-humouredly said, “sundry little outstanding trifles.”\n\nBut at last he did sign the bill, and sent it off, as Sowerby had directed. What else was he to do?\n\nFool that he was. A man always can do right, even though he has done wrong before. But that previous wrong adds so much difficulty to the path—a difficulty which increases in tremendous ratio, till a man at last is choked in his struggling, and is drowned beneath the waters.\n\nAnd then he put away Sowerby’s letter carefully, locking it up from his wife’s sight. It was a letter that no parish clergyman should have received. So much he acknowledged to himself. But nevertheless it was necessary that he should keep it. And now again for a few hours this affair made him very miserable.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII. DELICATE HINTS.\n\nLady Lufton had been greatly rejoiced at that good deed which her son did in giving up his Leicestershire hunting, and coming to reside for the winter at Framley. It was proper, and becoming, and comfortable in the extreme. An English nobleman ought to hunt in the county where he himself owns the fields over which he rides; he ought to receive the respect and honour due to him from his own tenants; he ought to sleep under a roof of his own, and he ought also—so Lady Lufton thought—to fall in love with a young embryo bride of his own mother’s choosing.\n\nAnd then it was so pleasant to have him there in the house. Lady Lufton was not a woman who allowed her life to be what people in common parlance call dull. She had too many duties, and thought too much of them, to allow of her suffering from tedium and ennui. But nevertheless the house was more joyous to her when he was there. There was a reason for some little gaiety, which would never have been attracted thither by herself, but which, nevertheless, she did enjoy when it was brought about by his presence. She was younger and brighter when he was there, thinking more of the future and less of the past. She could look at him, and that alone was happiness to her. And then he was pleasant-mannered with her; joking with her on her little old-world prejudices in a tone that was musical to her ear as coming from him; smiling on her, reminding her of those smiles which she had loved so dearly when as yet he was all her own, lying there in his little bed beside her chair. He was kind and gracious to her, behaving like a good son, at any rate while he was there in her presence. When we add, to this, her fears that he might not be so perfect in his conduct when absent, we may well imagine that Lady Lufton was pleased to have him there at Framley Court.\n\nShe had hardly said a word to him as to that five thousand pounds. Many a night, as she lay thinking on her pillow, she said to herself that no money had ever been better expended, since it had brought him back to his own house. He had thanked her for it in his own open way, declaring that he would pay it back to her during the coming year, and comforting her heart by his rejoicing that the property had not been sold.\n\n“I don’t like the idea of parting with an acre of it,” he had said.\n\n“Of course not, Ludovic. Never let the estate decrease in your hands. It is only by such resolutions as that that English noblemen and English gentlemen can preserve their country. I cannot bear to see property changing hands.”\n\n“Well, I suppose it’s a good thing to have land in the market sometimes, so that the millionnaires may know what to do with their money.”\n\n“God forbid that yours should be there!” And the widow made a little mental prayer that her son’s acres might be protected from the millionnaires and other Philistines.\n\n“Why, yes: I don’t exactly want to see a Jew tailor investing his earnings at Lufton,” said the lord.\n\n“Heaven forbid!” said the widow.\n\nAll this, as I have said, was very nice. It was manifest to her ladyship, from his lordship’s way of talking, that no vital injury had as yet been done: he had no cares on his mind, and spoke freely about the property: but nevertheless there were clouds even now, at this period of bliss, which somewhat obscured the brilliancy of Lady Lufton’s sky. Why was Ludovic so slow in that affair of Griselda Grantly? why so often in these latter winter days did he saunter over to the parsonage? And then that terrible visit to Gatherum Castle!\n\nWhat actually did happen at Gatherum Castle, she never knew. We, however, are more intrusive, less delicate in our inquiries, and we can say. He had a very bad day’s sport with the West Barsetshire. The county is altogether short of foxes, and some one who understands the matter must take that point up before they can do any good. And after that he had had rather a dull dinner with the duke. Sowerby had been there, and in the evening he and Sowerby had played billiards. Sowerby had won a pound or two, and that had been the extent of the damage done.\n\nBut those saunterings over to the parsonage might be more dangerous. Not that it ever occurred to Lady Lufton as possible that her son should fall in love with Lucy Robarts. Lucy’s personal attractions were not of a nature to give ground for such a fear as that. But he might turn the girl’s head with his chatter; she might be fool enough to fancy any folly; and, moreover, people would talk. Why should he go to the parsonage now more frequently than he had ever done before Lucy came there?\n\nAnd then her ladyship, in reference to the same trouble, hardly knew how to manage her invitations to the parsonage. These hitherto had been very frequent, and she had been in the habit of thinking that they could hardly be too much so; but now she was almost afraid to continue the custom. She could not ask the parson and his wife without Lucy; and when Lucy was there, her son would pass the greater part of the evening in talking to her, or playing chess with her. Now this did disturb Lady Lufton not a little.\n\nAnd then Lucy took it all so quietly. On her first arrival at Framley she had been so shy, so silent, and so much awestruck by the grandeur of Framley Court, that Lady Lufton had sympathized with her and encouraged her. She had endeavoured to moderate the blaze of her own splendour, in order that Lucy’s unaccustomed eyes might not be dazzled. But all this was changed now. Lucy could listen to the young lord’s voice by the hour together—without being dazzled in the least.\n\nUnder these circumstances two things occurred to her. She would speak either to her son or to Fanny Robarts, and by a little diplomacy have this evil remedied. And then she had to determine on which step she would take.\n\n“Nothing could be more reasonable than Ludovic.” So at least she said to herself over and over again. But then Ludovic understood nothing about such matters; and he had, moreover, a habit, inherited from his father, of taking the bit between his teeth whenever he suspected interference. Drive him gently without pulling his mouth about, and you might take him anywhere, almost at any pace; but a smart touch, let it be ever so slight, would bring him on his haunches, and then it might be a question whether you could get him another mile that day. So that on the whole Lady Lufton thought that the other plan would be the best. I have no doubt that Lady Lufton was right.\n\nShe got Fanny up into her own den one afternoon, and seated her discreetly in an easy arm-chair, making her guest take off her bonnet, and showing by various signs that the visit was regarded as one of great moment.\n\n“Fanny,” she said, “I want to speak to you about something that is important and necessary to mention, and yet it is a very delicate affair to speak of.” Fanny opened her eyes, and said that she hoped that nothing was wrong.\n\n“No, my dear, I think nothing is wrong: I hope so, and I think I may say I’m sure of it; but then it’s always well to be on one’s guard.”\n\n“Yes, it is,” said Fanny, who knew that something unpleasant was coming—something as to which she might probably be called upon to differ from her ladyship. Mrs. Robarts’ own fears, however, were running entirely in the direction of her husband;—and, indeed, Lady Lufton had a word or two to say on that subject also, only not exactly now. A hunting parson was not at all to her taste; but that matter might be allowed to remain in abeyance for a few days.\n\n“Now, Fanny, you know that we have all liked your sister-in-law, Lucy, very much.” And then Mrs. Robarts’ mind was immediately opened, and she knew the rest as well as though it had all been spoken. “I need hardly tell you that, for I am sure we have shown it.”\n\n“You have, indeed, as you always do.”\n\n“And you must not think that I am going to complain,” continued Lady Lufton.\n\n“I hope there is nothing to complain of,” said Fanny, speaking by no means in a defiant tone, but humbly as it were, and deprecating her ladyship’s wrath. Fanny had gained one signal victory over Lady Lufton, and on that account, with a prudence equal to her generosity, felt that she could afford to be submissive. It might, perhaps, not be long before she would be equally anxious to conquer again.\n\n“Well, no; I don’t think there is,” said Lady Lufton. “Nothing to complain of; but a little chat between you and me may, perhaps, set matters right, which, otherwise, might become troublesome.”\n\n“Is it about Lucy?”\n\n“Yes, my dear—about Lucy. She is a very nice, good girl, and a credit to her father—”\n\n“And a great comfort to us,” said Fanny.\n\n“I am sure she is: she must be a very pleasant companion to you, and so useful about the children; but—” And then Lady Lufton paused for a moment; for she, eloquent and discreet as she always was, felt herself rather at a loss for words to express her exact meaning.\n\n“I don’t know what I should do without her,” said Fanny, speaking with the object of assisting her ladyship in her embarrassment.\n\n“But the truth is this: she and Lord Lufton are getting into the way of being too much together—of talking to each other too exclusively. I am sure you must have noticed it, Fanny. It is not that I suspect any evil. I don’t think that I am suspicious by nature.”\n\n“Oh! no,” said Fanny.\n\n“But they will each of them get wrong ideas about the other, and about themselves. Lucy will, perhaps, think that Ludovic means more than he does, and Ludovic will—” But it was not quite so easy to say what Ludovic might do or think; but Lady Lufton went on: “I am sure that you understand me, Fanny, with your excellent sense and tact. Lucy is clever, and amusing, and all that; and Ludovic, like all young men, is perhaps ignorant that his attentions may be taken to mean more than he intends—”\n\n“You don’t think that Lucy is in love with him?”\n\n“Oh dear, no—nothing of the kind. If I thought it had come to that, I should recommend that she should be sent away altogether. I am sure she is not so foolish as that.”\n\n“I don’t think there is anything in it at all, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“I don’t think there is, my dear, and therefore I would not for worlds make any suggestion about it to Lord Lufton. I would not let him suppose that I suspected Lucy of being so imprudent. But still, it may be well that you should just say a word to her. A little management now and then, in such matters, is so useful.”\n\n“But what shall I say to her?”\n\n“Just explain to her that any young lady who talks so much to the same young gentleman will certainly be observed—that people will accuse her of setting her cap at Lord Lufton. Not that I suspect her—I give her credit for too much proper feeling: I know her education has been good, and her principles are upright. But people will talk of her. You must understand that, Fanny, as well as I do.”\n\nFanny could not help meditating whether proper feeling, education, and upright principles did forbid Lucy Robarts to fall in love with Lord Lufton; but her doubts on this subject, if she held any, were not communicated to her ladyship. It had never entered into her mind that a match was possible between Lord Lufton and Lucy Robarts, nor had she the slightest wish to encourage it now that the idea was suggested to her. On such a matter she could sympathize with Lady Lufton, though she did not completely agree with her as to the expediency of any interference. Nevertheless, she at once offered to speak to Lucy.\n\n“I don’t think that Lucy has any idea in her head upon the subject,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“I dare say not—I don’t suppose she has. But young ladies sometimes allow themselves to fall in love, and then to think themselves very ill-used, just because they have had no idea in their head.”\n\n“I will put her on her guard if you wish it, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“Exactly, my dear; that is just it. Put her on her guard—that is all that is necessary. She is a dear, good, clever girl, and it would be very sad if anything were to interrupt our comfortable way of getting on with her.”\n\nMrs. Robarts knew to a nicety the exact meaning of this threat. If Lucy would persist in securing to herself so much of Lord Lufton’s time and attention, her visits to Framley Court must become less frequent. Lady Lufton would do much, very much, indeed, for her friends at the parsonage; but not even for them could she permit her son’s prospects in life to be endangered.\n\nThere was nothing more said between them, and Mrs. Robarts got up to take her leave, having promised to speak to Lucy.\n\n“You manage everything so perfectly,” said Lady Lufton, as she pressed Mrs. Robarts’ hand, “that I am quite at ease now that I find you will agree with me.” Mrs. Robarts did not exactly agree with her ladyship, but she hardly thought it worth her while to say so.\n\nMrs. Robarts immediately started off on her walk to her own home, and when she had got out of the grounds into the road, where it makes a turn towards the parsonage, nearly opposite to Podgens’ shop, she saw Lord Lufton on horseback, and Lucy standing beside him. It was already nearly five o’clock, and it was getting dusk; but as she approached, or rather as she came suddenly within sight of them, she could see that they were in close conversation. Lord Lufton’s face was towards her, and his horse was standing still; he was leaning over towards his companion, and the whip, which he held in his right hand, hung almost over her arm and down her back, as though his hand had touched and perhaps rested on her shoulder. She was standing by his side, looking up into his face, with one gloved hand resting on the horse’s neck. Mrs. Robarts, as she saw them, could not but own that there might be cause for Lady Lufton’s fears.\n\nBut then Lucy’s manner, as Mrs. Robarts approached, was calculated to dissipate any such fears, and to prove that there was no ground for them. She did not move from her position, or allow her hand to drop, or show that she was in any way either confused or conscious. She stood her ground, and when her sister-in-law came up was smiling and at her ease.\n\n“Lord Lufton wants me to learn to ride,” said she.\n\n“To learn to ride!” said Fanny, not knowing what answer to make to such a proposition.\n\n“Yes,” said he. “This horse would carry her beautifully: he is as quiet as a lamb, and I made Gregory go out with him yesterday with a sheet hanging over him like a lady’s habit, and the man got up into a lady’s saddle.”\n\n“I think Gregory would make a better hand of it than Lucy.”\n\n“The horse cantered with him as though he had carried a lady all his life, and his mouth is like velvet; indeed, that is his fault—he is too soft-mouthed.”\n\n“I suppose that’s the same sort of thing as a man being soft-hearted,” said Lucy.\n\n“Exactly: you ought to ride them both with a very light hand. They are difficult cattle to manage, but very pleasant when you know how to do it.”\n\n“But you see I don’t know how to do it,” said Lucy.\n\n“As regards the horse, you will learn in two days, and I do hope you will try. Don’t you think it will be an excellent thing for her, Mrs. Robarts?”\n\n“Lucy has got no habit,” said Mrs. Robarts, making use of the excuse common on all such occasions.\n\n“There is one of Justinia’s in the house, I know. She always leaves one here, in order that she may be able to ride when she comes.”\n\n“She would not think of taking such a liberty with Lady Meredith’s things,” said Fanny, almost frightened at the proposal.\n\n“Of course it is out of the question, Fanny,” said Lucy, now speaking rather seriously. “In the first place, I would not take Lord Lufton’s horse; in the second place, I would not take Lady Meredith’s habit; in the third place, I should be a great deal too much frightened; and, lastly, it is quite out of the question for a great many other very good reasons.”\n\n“Nonsense,” said Lord Lufton.\n\n“A great deal of nonsense,” said Lucy, laughing, “but all of it of Lord Lufton’s talking. But we are getting cold—are we not, Fanny?—so we will wish you good-night.” And then the two ladies shook hands with him, and walked on towards the parsonage.\n\nThat which astonished Mrs. Robarts the most in all this was the perfectly collected manner in which Lucy spoke and conducted herself. This connected, as she could not but connect it, with the air of chagrin with which Lord Lufton received Lucy’s decision, made it manifest to Mrs. Robarts that Lord Lufton was annoyed because Lucy would not consent to learn to ride; whereas she, Lucy herself, had given her refusal in a firm and decided tone, as though resolved that nothing more should be said about it.\n\nThey walked on in silence for a minute or two, till they reached the parsonage gate, and then Lucy said, laughing, “Can’t you fancy me sitting on that great big horse? I wonder what Lady Lufton would say if she saw me there, and his lordship giving me my first lesson?”\n\n“I don’t think she would like it,” said Fanny.\n\n“I’m sure she would not. But I will not try her temper in that respect. Sometimes I fancy that she does not even like seeing Lord Lufton talking to me.”\n\n“She does not like it, Lucy, when she sees him flirting with you.”\n\nThis Mrs. Robarts said rather gravely, whereas Lucy had been speaking in a half-bantering tone. As soon as even the word flirting was out of Fanny’s mouth, she was conscious that she had been guilty of an injustice in using it. She had wished to say something which would convey to her sister-in-law an idea of what Lady Lufton would dislike; but in doing so, she had unintentionally brought against her an accusation.\n\n“Flirting, Fanny!” said Lucy, standing still in the path, and looking up into her companion’s face with all her eyes. “Do you mean to say that I have been flirting with Lord Lufton?”\n\n“I did not say that.”\n\n“Or that I have allowed him to flirt with me?”\n\n“I did not mean to shock you, Lucy.”\n\n“What did you mean, Fanny?”\n\n“Why, just this: that Lady Lufton would not be pleased if he paid you marked attentions, and if you received them;—just like that affair of the riding; it was better to decline it.”\n\n“Of course I declined it; of course I never dreamt of accepting such an offer. Go riding about the country on his horses! What have I done, Fanny, that you should suppose such a thing?”\n\n“You have done nothing, dearest.”\n\n“Then why did you speak as you did just now?”\n\n“Because I wished to put you on your guard. You know, Lucy, that I do not intend to find fault with you; but you may be sure, as a rule, that intimate friendships between young gentlemen and young ladies are dangerous things.”\n\nThey then walked up to the hall-door in silence. When they had reached it, Lucy stood in the doorway instead of entering it, and said, “Fanny, let us take another turn together, if you are not tired.”\n\n“No, I’m not tired.”\n\n“It will be better that I should understand you at once,”—and then they again moved away from the house. “Tell me truly now, do you think that Lord Lufton and I have been flirting?”\n\n“I do think that he is a little inclined to flirt with you.”\n\n“And Lady Lufton has been asking you to lecture me about it?”\n\nPoor Mrs. Robarts hardly knew what to say. She thought well of all the persons concerned, and was very anxious to behave well by all of them;—was particularly anxious to create no ill feeling, and wished that everybody should be comfortable, and on good terms with everybody else. But yet the truth was forced out of her when this question was asked so suddenly.\n\n“Not to lecture you, Lucy,” she said at last.\n\n“Well, to preach to me, or to talk to me, or to give me a lesson; to say something that shall drive me to put my back up against Lord Lufton?”\n\n“To caution you, dearest. Had you heard what she said, you would hardly have felt angry with Lady Lufton.”\n\n“Well, to caution me. It is such a pleasant thing for a girl to be cautioned against falling in love with a gentleman, especially when the gentleman is very rich, and a lord, and all that sort of thing!”\n\n“Nobody for a moment attributes anything wrong to you, Lucy.”\n\n“Anything wrong—no. I don’t know whether it would be anything wrong, even if I were to fall in love with him. I wonder whether they cautioned Griselda Grantly when she was here? I suppose when young lords go about, all the girls are cautioned as a matter of course. Why do they not label him ‘dangerous’?” And then again they were silent for a moment, as Mrs. Robarts did not feel that she had anything further to say on the matter.\n\n“‘Poison’ should be the word with any one so fatal as Lord Lufton; and he ought to be made up of some particular colour, for fear he should be swallowed in mistake.”\n\n“You will be safe, you see,” said Fanny, laughing, “as you have been specially cautioned as to this individual bottle.”\n\n“Ah! but what’s the use of that after I have had so many doses? It is no good telling me about it now, when the mischief is done,—after I have been taking it for I don’t know how long. Dear! dear! dear! and I regarded it as a mere commonplace powder, good for the complexion. I wonder whether it’s too late, or whether there’s any antidote?”\n\nMrs. Robarts did not always quite understand her sister-in-law, and now she was a little at a loss. “I don’t think there’s much harm done yet on either side,” she said, cheerily.\n\n“Ah! you don’t know, Fanny. But I do think that if I die—as I shall—I feel I shall;—and if so, I do think it ought to go very hard with Lady Lufton. Why didn’t she label him ‘dangerous’ in time?” And then they went into the house and up to their own rooms.\n\nIt was difficult for any one to understand Lucy’s state of mind at present, and it can hardly be said that she understood it herself. She felt that she had received a severe blow in having been thus made the subject of remark with reference to Lord Lufton. She knew that her pleasant evenings at Framley Court were now over, and that she could not again talk to him in an unrestrained tone and without embarrassment. She had felt the air of the whole place to be very cold before her intimacy with him, and now it must be cold again. Two homes had been open to her, Framley Court and the parsonage; and now, as far as comfort was concerned, she must confine herself to the latter. She could not again be comfortable in Lady Lufton’s drawing-room.\n\nBut then she could not help asking herself whether Lady Lufton was not right. She had had courage enough, and presence of mind, to joke about the matter when her sister-in-law spoke to her, and yet she was quite aware that it was no joking matter. Lord Lufton had not absolutely made love to her, but he had latterly spoken to her in a manner which she knew was not compatible with that ordinary comfortable masculine friendship with the idea of which she had once satisfied herself. Was not Fanny right when she said that intimate friendships of that nature were dangerous things?\n\nYes, Lucy, very dangerous. Lucy, before she went to bed that night, had owned to herself that they were so; and lying there with sleepless eyes and a moist pillow, she was driven to confess that the label would in truth be now too late, that the caution had come to her after the poison had been swallowed. Was there any antidote? That was all that was left for her to consider. But, nevertheless, on the following morning she could appear quite at her ease. And when Mark had left the house after breakfast, she could still joke with Fanny as to Lady Lufton’s poison cupboard.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV. MR. CRAWLEY OF HOGGLESTOCK.\n\nAnd then there was that other trouble in Lady Lufton’s mind, the sins, namely, of her selected parson. She had selected him, and she was by no means inclined to give him up, even though his sins against parsondom were grievous. Indeed she was a woman not prone to give up anything, and of all things not prone to give up a protégé. The very fact that she herself had selected him was the strongest argument in his favour.\n\nBut his sins against parsondom were becoming very grievous in her eyes, and she was at a loss to know what steps to take. She hardly dared to take him to task, him himself. Were she to do so, and should he then tell her to mind her own business—as he probably might do, though not in those words—there would be a schism in the parish; and almost anything would be better than that. The whole work of her life would be upset, all the outlets of her energy would be impeded if not absolutely closed, if a state of things were to come to pass in which she and the parson of her parish should not be on good terms.\n\nBut what was to be done? Early in the winter he had gone to Chaldicotes and to Gatherum Castle, consorting with gamblers, Whigs, atheists, men of loose pleasure, and Proudieites. That she had condoned; and now he was turning out a hunting parson on her hands. It was all very well for Fanny to say that he merely looked at the hounds as he rode about his parish. Fanny might be deceived. Being his wife, it might be her duty not to see her husband’s iniquities. But Lady Lufton could not be deceived. She knew very well in what part of the county Cobbold’s Ashes lay. It was not in Framley parish, nor in the next parish to it. It was half-way across to Chaldicotes—in the western division; and she had heard of that run in which two horses had been killed, and in which Parson Robarts had won such immortal glory among West Barsetshire sportsmen. It was not easy to keep Lady Lufton in the dark as to matters occurring in her own county.\n\nAll these things she knew, but as yet had not noticed, grieving over them in her own heart the more on that account. Spoken grief relieves itself; and when one can give counsel, one always hopes at least that that counsel will be effective. To her son she had said, more than once, that it was a pity that Mr. Robarts should follow the hounds.—“The world has agreed that it is unbecoming in a clergyman,” she would urge, in her deprecatory tone. But her son would by no means give her any comfort. “He doesn’t hunt, you know—not as I do,” he would say. “And if he did, I really don’t see the harm of it. A man must have some amusement, even if he be an archbishop.” “He has amusement at home,” Lady Lufton would answer. “What does his wife do—and his sister?” This allusion to Lucy, however, was very soon dropped.\n\nLord Lufton would in no wise help her. He would not even passively discourage the vicar, or refrain from offering to give him a seat in going to the meets. Mark and Lord Lufton had been boys together, and his lordship knew that Mark in his heart would enjoy a brush across the country quite as well as he himself; and then what was the harm of it?\n\nLady Lufton’s best aid had been in Mark’s own conscience. He had taken himself to task more than once, and had promised himself that he would not become a sporting parson. Indeed, where would be his hopes of ulterior promotion, if he allowed himself to degenerate so far as that? It had been his intention, in reviewing what he considered to be the necessary proprieties of clerical life, in laying out his own future mode of living, to assume no peculiar sacerdotal strictness; he would not be known as a denouncer of dancing or of card-tables, of theatres or of novel-reading; he would take the world around him as he found it, endeavouring by precept and practice to lend a hand to the gradual amelioration which Christianity is producing; but he would attempt no sudden or majestic reforms. Cake and ale would still be popular, and ginger be hot in the mouth, let him preach ever so—let him be never so solemn a hermit; but a bright face, a true trusting heart, a strong arm, and an humble mind, might do much in teaching those around him that men may be gay and yet not profligate, that women may be devout and yet not dead to the world.\n\nSuch had been his ideas as to his own future life; and though many would think that as a clergyman he should have gone about his work with more serious devotion of thought, nevertheless there was some wisdom in them;—some folly also, undoubtedly, as appeared by the troubles into which they led him.\n\n“I will not affect to think that to be bad,” said he to himself, “which in my heart of hearts does not seem to be bad.” And thus he resolved that he might live without contamination among hunting squires. And then, being a man only too prone by nature to do as others did around him, he found by degrees that that could hardly be wrong for him which he admitted to be right for others.\n\nBut still his conscience upbraided him, and he declared to himself more than once that after this year he would hunt no more. And then his own Fanny would look at him on his return home on those days in a manner that cut him to the heart. She would say nothing to him. She never inquired in a sneering tone, and with angry eyes, whether he had enjoyed his day’s sport; but when he spoke of it, she could not answer him with enthusiasm; and in other matters which concerned him she was always enthusiastic.\n\nAfter a while, too, he made matters worse, for about the end of March he did another very foolish thing. He almost consented to buy an expensive horse from Sowerby—an animal which he by no means wanted, and which, if once possessed, would certainly lead him into further trouble. A gentleman, when he has a good horse in his stable, does not like to leave him there eating his head off. If he be a gig-horse, the owner of him will be keen to drive a gig; if a hunter, the happy possessor will wish to be with a pack of hounds.\n\n“Mark,” said Sowerby to him one day, when they were out together, “this brute of mine is so fresh, I can hardly ride him; you are young and strong; change with me for an hour or so.” And then they did change, and the horse on which Robarts found himself mounted went away with him beautifully.\n\n“He’s a splendid animal,” said Mark, when they again met.\n\n“Yes, for a man of your weight. He’s thrown away upon me;—too much of a horse for my purposes. I don’t get along now quite as well as I used to do. He is a nice sort of hunter; just rising six, you know.”\n\nHow it came to pass that the price of the splendid animal was mentioned between them, I need not describe with exactness. But it did come to pass that Mr. Sowerby told the parson that the horse should be his for £130.\n\n“And I really wish you’d take him,” said Sowerby. “It would be the means of partially relieving my mind of a great weight.”\n\nMark looked up into his friend’s face with an air of surprise, for he did not at the moment understand how this should be the case.\n\n“I am afraid, you know, that you will have to put your hand into your pocket sooner or later about that accursed bill—” Mark shrank as the profane word struck his ears—“and I should be glad to think that you had got something in hand in the way of value.”\n\n“Do you mean that I shall have to pay the whole sum of £500?”\n\n“Oh! dear, no; nothing of the kind. But something I dare say you will have to pay: if you like to take Dandy for a hundred and thirty, you can be prepared for that amount when Tozer comes to you. The horse is dog cheap, and you will have a long day for your money.”\n\nMark at first declared, in a quiet, determined tone, that he did not want the horse; but it afterwards appeared to him that if it were so fated that he must pay a portion of Mr. Sowerby’s debts, he might as well repay himself to any extent within his power. It would be as well perhaps that he should take the horse and sell him. It did not occur to him that by so doing he would put it in Mr. Sowerby’s power to say that some valuable consideration had passed between them with reference to this bill, and that he would be aiding that gentleman in preparing an inextricable confusion of money-matters between them. Mr. Sowerby well knew the value of this. It would enable him to make a plausible story, as he had done in that other case of Lord Lufton.\n\n“Are you going to have Dandy?” Sowerby said to him again.\n\n“I can’t say that I will just at present,” said the parson. “What should I want of him now the season’s over?”\n\n“Exactly, my dear fellow; and what do I want of him now the season’s over? If it were the beginning of October instead of the end of March, Dandy would be up at two hundred and thirty instead of one: in six months’ time that horse will be worth anything you like to ask for him. Look at his bone.”\n\nThe vicar did look at his bones, examining the brute in a very knowing and unclerical manner. He lifted the animal’s four feet, one after another, handling the frogs, and measuring with his eye the proportion of the parts; he passed his hand up and down the legs, spanning the bones of the lower joint; he peered into his eyes, took into consideration the width of his chest, the dip of his back, the form of his ribs, the curve of his haunches, and his capabilities for breathing when pressed by work. And then he stood away a little, eyeing him from the side, and taking in a general idea of the form and make of the whole. “He seems to stand over a little, I think,” said the parson.\n\n“It’s the lie of the ground. Move him about, Bob. There now, let him stand there.”\n\n“He’s not perfect,” said Mark. “I don’t quite like his heels; but no doubt he’s a nicish cut of a horse.”\n\n“I rather think he is. If he were perfect, as you say, he would not be going into your stables for a hundred and thirty. Do you ever remember to have seen a perfect horse?”\n\n“Your mare Mrs. Gamp was as nearly perfect as possible.”\n\n“Even Mrs. Gamp had her faults. In the first place she was a bad feeder. But one certainly doesn’t often come across anything much better than Mrs. Gamp.” And thus the matter was talked over between them with much stable conversation, all of which tended to make Sowerby more and more oblivious of his friend’s sacred profession, and perhaps to make the vicar himself too frequently oblivious of it also. But no: he was not oblivious of it. He was even mindful of it; but mindful of it in such a manner that his thoughts on the subject were nowadays always painful.\n\nThere is a parish called Hogglestock lying away quite in the northern extremity of the eastern division of the county—lying also on the borders of the western division. I almost fear that it will become necessary, before this history be completed, to provide a map of Barsetshire for the due explanation of all these localities. Framley is also in the northern portion of the county, but just to the south of the grand trunk line of railway from which the branch to Barchester strikes off at a point some thirty miles nearer to London. The station for Framley Court is Silverbridge, which is, however, in the western division of the county. Hogglestock is to the north of the railway, the line of which, however, runs through a portion of the parish, and it adjoins Framley, though the churches are as much as seven miles apart. Barsetshire, taken altogether, is a pleasant green tree-becrowded county, with large bosky hedges, pretty damp deep lanes, and roads with broad grass margins running along them. Such is the general nature of the county; but just up in its northern extremity this nature alters. There it is bleak and ugly, with low artificial hedges and without wood; not uncultivated, as it is all portioned out into new-looking large fields, bearing turnips and wheat and mangel, all in due course of agricultural rotation; but it has none of the special beauties of English cultivation. There is not a gentleman’s house in the parish of Hogglestock besides that of the clergyman; and this, though it is certainly the house of a gentleman, can hardly be said to be fit to be so. It is ugly, and straight, and small. There is a garden attached to the house, half in front of it and half behind; but this garden, like the rest of the parish, is by no means ornamental, though sufficiently useful. It produces cabbages, but no trees: potatoes of, I believe, an excellent description, but hardly any flowers, and nothing worthy of the name of a shrub. Indeed the whole parish of Hogglestock should have been in the adjoining county, which is by no means so attractive as Barsetshire;—a fact well known to those few of my readers who are well acquainted with their own country.\n\nMr. Crawley, whose name has been mentioned in these pages, was the incumbent of Hogglestock. On what principle the remuneration of our parish clergymen was settled when the original settlement was made, no deepest, keenest lover of middle-aged ecclesiastical black-letter learning can, I take it, now say. That the priests were to be paid from tithes of the parish produce, out of which tithes certain other good things were to be bought and paid for, such as church repairs and education, of so much the most of us have an inkling. That a rector, being a big sort of parson, owned the tithes of his parish in full,—or at any rate that part of them intended for the clergyman,—and that a vicar was somebody’s deputy, and therefore entitled only to little tithes, as being a little body: of so much we that are simple in such matters have a general idea. But one cannot conceive that even in this way any approximation could have been made, even in those old mediæval days, towards a fair proportioning of the pay to the work. At any rate, it is clear enough that there is no such approximation now.\n\nAnd what a screech would there not be among the clergy of the Church, even in these reforming days, if any over-bold reformer were to suggest that such an approximation should be attempted? Let those who know clergymen, and like them, and have lived with them, only fancy it! Clergymen to be paid, not according to the temporalities of any living which they may have acquired either by merit or favour, but in accordance with the work to be done! O Doddington! and O Stanhope, think of this, if an idea so sacrilegious can find entrance into your warm ecclesiastical bosoms! Ecclesiastical work to be bought and paid for according to its quantity and quality!\n\nBut, nevertheless, one may prophesy that we Englishmen must come to this, disagreeable as the idea undoubtedly is. Most pleasant-minded churchmen feel, I think, on this subject pretty much in the same way. Our present arrangement of parochial incomes is beloved as being time-honoured, gentlemanlike, English, and picturesque. We would fain adhere to it closely as long as we can, but we know that we do so by the force of our prejudices, and not by that of our judgment. A time-honoured, gentlemanlike, English, picturesque arrangement is so far very delightful. But are there not other attributes very desirable—nay, absolutely necessary—in respect to which this time-honoured, picturesque arrangement is so very deficient?\n\nHow pleasant it was, too, that one bishop should be getting fifteen thousand a year and another with an equal cure of parsons only four! That a certain prelate could get twenty thousand one year and his successor in the same diocese only five the next! There was something in it pleasant, and picturesque; it was an arrangement endowed with feudal charms, and the change which they have made was distasteful to many of us. A bishop with a regular salary, and no appanage of land and land-bailiffs, is only half a bishop. Let any man prove to me the contrary ever so thoroughly—let me prove it to my own self ever so often—my heart in this matter is not thereby a whit altered. One liked to know that there was a dean or two who got his three thousand a year, and that old Dr. Purple held four stalls, one of which was golden, and the other three silver-gilt! Such knowledge was always pleasant to me! A golden stall! How sweet is the sound thereof to church-loving ears!\n\nBut bishops have been shorn of their beauty, and deans are in their decadence. A utilitarian age requires the fatness of the ecclesiastical land, in order that it may be divided out into small portions of provender, on which necessary working clergymen may live,—into portions so infinitesimally small that working clergymen can hardly live. And the full-blown rectors and vicars, with full-blown tithes—with tithes when too full-blown for strict utilitarian principles—will necessarily follow. Stanhope and Doddington must bow their heads, with such compensation for temporal rights as may be extracted,—but probably without such compensation as may be desired. In other trades, professions, and lines of life, men are paid according to their work. Let it be so in the Church. Such will sooner or later be the edict of a utilitarian, reforming, matter-of-fact House of Parliament.\n\nI have a scheme of my own on the subject, which I will not introduce here, seeing that neither men nor women would read it. And with reference to this matter, I will only here further explain that all these words have been brought about by the fact, necessary to be here stated, that Mr. Crawley only received one hundred and thirty pounds a year for performing the whole parochial duty of the parish of Hogglestock. And Hogglestock is a large parish. It includes two populous villages, abounding in brickmakers, a race of men very troublesome to a zealous parson who won’t let men go rollicking to the devil without interference. Hogglestock has full work for two men; and yet all the funds therein applicable to parson’s work is this miserable stipend of one hundred and thirty pounds a year. It is a stipend neither picturesque, nor time-honoured, nor feudal, for Hogglestock takes rank only as a perpetual curacy.\n\nMr. Crawley has been mentioned before as a clergyman of whom Mr. Robarts said, that he almost thought it wrong to take a walk out of his own parish. In so saying Mark Robarts of course burlesqued his brother parson; but there can be no doubt that Mr. Crawley was a strict man,—a strict, stern, unpleasant man, and one who feared God and his own conscience. We must say a word or two of Mr. Crawley and his concerns.\n\nHe was now some forty years of age, but of these he had not been in possession even of his present benefice for more than four or five. The first ten years of his life as a clergyman had been passed in performing the duties and struggling through the life of a curate in a bleak, ugly, cold parish on the northern coast of Cornwall. It had been a weary life and a fearful struggle, made up of duties ill requited and not always satisfactorily performed, of love and poverty, of increasing cares, of sickness, debt, and death. For Mr. Crawley had married almost as soon as he was ordained, and children had been born to him in that chill, comfortless Cornish cottage. He had married a lady well educated and softly nurtured, but not dowered with worldly wealth. They two had gone forth determined to fight bravely together; to disregard the world and the world’s ways, looking only to God and to each other for their comfort. They would give up ideas of gentle living, of soft raiment, and delicate feeding. Others,—those that work with their hands, even the bettermost of such workers—could live in decency and health upon even such provision as he could earn as a clergyman. In such manner would they live, so poorly and so decently, working out their work, not with their hands but with their hearts.\n\nAnd so they had established themselves, beginning the world with one bare-footed little girl of fourteen to aid them in their small household matters; and for a while they had both kept heart, loving each other dearly, and prospering somewhat in their work. But a man who has once walked the world as a gentleman knows not what it is to change his position, and place himself lower down in the social rank. Much less can he know what it is so to put down the woman whom he loves. There are a thousand things, mean and trifling in themselves, which a man despises when he thinks of them in his philosophy, but to dispense with which puts his philosophy to so stern a proof. Let any plainest man who reads this think of his usual mode of getting himself into his matutinal garments, and confess how much such a struggle would cost him.\n\nAnd then children had come. The wife of the labouring man does rear her children, and often rears them in health, without even so many appliances of comfort as found their way into Mrs. Crawley’s cottage; but the task to her was almost more than she could accomplish. Not that she ever fainted or gave way: she was made of the sterner metal of the two, and could last on while he was prostrate.\n\nAnd sometimes he was prostrate—prostrate in soul and spirit. Then would he complain with bitter voice, crying out that the world was too hard for him, that his back was broken with his burden, that his God had deserted him. For days and days, in such moods, he would stay within his cottage, never darkening the door or seeing other face than those of his own inmates. Those days were terrible both to him and her. He would sit there unwashed, with his unshorn face resting on his hand, with an old dressing-gown hanging loose about him, hardly tasting food, seldom speaking, striving to pray, but striving so frequently in vain. And then he would rise from his chair, and, with a burst of frenzy, call upon his Creator to remove him from this misery.\n\nIn these moments she never deserted him. At one period they had had four children, and though the whole weight of this young brood rested on her arms, on her muscles, on her strength of mind and body, she never ceased in her efforts to comfort him. Then at length, falling utterly upon the ground, he would pour forth piteous prayers for mercy, and, after a night of sleep, would once more go forth to his work.\n\nBut she never yielded to despair: the struggle was never beyond her powers of endurance. She had possessed her share of woman’s loveliness, but that was now all gone. Her colour quickly faded, and the fresh, soft tints soon deserted her face and forehead. She became thin, and rough, and almost haggard: thin, till her cheek-bones were nearly pressing through her skin, till her elbows were sharp, and her finger-bones as those of a skeleton. Her eye did not lose its lustre, but it became unnaturally bright, prominent, and too large for her wan face. The soft brown locks which she had once loved to brush back, scorning, as she would boast to herself, to care that they should be seen, were now sparse enough and all untidy and unclean. It was matter of little thought now whether they were seen or no. Whether he could be made fit to go into his pulpit—whether they might be fed—those four innocents—and their backs kept from the cold wind—that was now the matter of her thought.\n\nAnd then two of them died, and she went forth herself to see them laid under the frost-bound sod, lest he should faint in his work over their graves. For he would ask aid from no man—such at least was his boast through all.\n\nTwo of them died, but their illness had been long; and then debts came upon them. Debt, indeed, had been creeping on them with slow but sure feet during the last five years. Who can see his children hungry, and not take bread if it be offered? Who can see his wife lying in sharpest want, and not seek a remedy if there be a remedy within reach? So debt had come upon them, and rude men pressed for small sums of money—for sums small to the world, but impossibly large to them. And he would hide himself within there, in that cranny of an inner chamber—hide himself with deep shame from the world, with shame, and a sinking heart, and a broken spirit.\n\nBut had such a man no friend? it will be said. Such men, I take it, do not make many friends. But this man was not utterly friendless. Almost every year one visit was paid to him in his Cornish curacy by a brother clergyman, an old college friend, who, as far as might in him lie, did give aid to the curate and his wife. This gentleman would take up his abode for a week at a farmer’s in the neighbourhood, and though he found Mr. Crawley in despair, he would leave him with some drops of comfort in his soul. Nor were the benefits in this respect all on one side. Mr. Crawley, though at some periods weak enough for himself, could be strong for others; and, more than once, was strong to the great advantage of this man whom he loved. And then, too, pecuniary assistance was forthcoming—in those earlier years not in great amount, for this friend was not then among the rich ones of the earth—but in amount sufficient for that moderate hearth, if only its acceptance could have been managed. But in that matter there were difficulties without end. Of absolute money tenders Mr. Crawley would accept none. But a bill here and there was paid, the wife assisting; and shoes came for Kate—till Kate was placed beyond the need of shoes; and cloth for Harry and Frank found its way surreptitiously in beneath the cover of that wife’s solitary trunk—cloth with which those lean fingers worked garments for the two boys, to be worn—such was God’s will—only by the one.\n\nSuch were Mr. and Mrs. Crawley in their Cornish curacy, and during their severest struggles. To one who thinks that a fair day’s work is worth a fair day’s wages, it seems hard enough that a man should work so hard and receive so little. There will be those who think that the fault was all his own in marrying so young. But still there remains that question, Is not a fair day’s work worth a fair day’s wages? This man did work hard—at a task perhaps the hardest of any that a man may do; and for ten years he earned some seventy pounds a year. Will any one say that he received fair wages for his fair work, let him be married or single? And yet there are so many who would fain pay their clergy, if they only knew how to apply their money! But that is a long subject, as Mr. Robarts had told Miss Dunstable.\n\nSuch was Mr. Crawley in his Cornish curacy.\n\n# CHAPTER XV. LADY LUFTON’S AMBASSADOR.\n\nAnd then, in the days which followed, that friend of Mr. Crawley’s, whose name, by-the-by, is yet to be mentioned, received quick and great promotion. Mr. Arabin by name he was then;—Dr. Arabin afterwards, when that quick and great promotion reached its climax. He had been simply a Fellow of Lazarus in those former years. Then he became Vicar of St. Ewold’s, in East Barsetshire, and had not yet got himself settled there when he married the Widow Bold, a widow with belongings in land and funded money, and with but one small baby as an encumbrance. Nor had he even yet married her,—had only engaged himself so to do, when they made him Dean of Barchester—all which may be read in the diocesan and county chronicles.\n\nAnd now that he was wealthy, the new dean did contrive to pay the debts of his poor friend, some lawyer of Camelford assisting him. It was but a paltry schedule after all, amounting in the total to something not much above a hundred pounds. And then, in the course of eighteen months, this poor piece of preferment fell in the dean’s way, this incumbency of Hogglestock with its stipend reaching one hundred and thirty pounds a year. Even that was worth double the Cornish curacy, and there was, moreover, a house attached to it. Poor Mrs. Crawley, when she heard of it, thought that their struggles of poverty were now well nigh over. What might not be done with a hundred and thirty pounds by people who had lived for ten years on seventy?\n\nAnd so they moved away out of that cold, bleak country, carrying with them their humble household gods, and settled themselves in another country, cold and bleak also, but less terribly so than the former. They settled themselves, and again began their struggles against man’s hardness and the devil’s zeal. I have said that Mr. Crawley was a stern, unpleasant man; and it certainly was so. The man must be made of very sterling stuff, whom continued and undeserved misfortune does not make unpleasant. This man had so far succumbed to grief, that it had left upon him its marks, palpable and not to be effaced. He cared little for society, judging men to be doing evil who did care for it. He knew as a fact, and believed with all his heart, that these sorrows had come to him from the hand of God, and that they would work for his weal in the long run; but not the less did they make him morose, silent, and dogged. He had always at his heart a feeling that he and his had been ill-used, and too often solaced himself, at the devil’s bidding, with the conviction that eternity would make equal that which life in this world had made so unequal;—the last bait that with which the devil angles after those who are struggling to elude his rod and line.\n\nThe Framley property did not run into the parish of Hogglestock; but nevertheless Lady Lufton did what she could in the way of kindness to these new-comers. Providence had not supplied Hogglestock with a Lady Lufton, or with any substitute in the shape of lord or lady, squire or squiress. The Hogglestock farmers, male and female, were a rude, rough set, not bordering in their social rank on the farmer gentle; and Lady Lufton, knowing this, and hearing something of these Crawleys from Mrs. Arabin, the dean’s wife, trimmed her lamps, so that they should shed a wider light, and pour forth some of their influence on that forlorn household.\n\nAnd as regards Mrs. Crawley, Lady Lufton by no means found that her work and good-will were thrown away. Mrs. Crawley accepted her kindness with thankfulness, and returned to some of the softnesses of life under her hand. As for dining at Framley Court, that was out of the question. Mr. Crawley, she knew, would not hear of it, even if other things were fitting and appliances were at command. Indeed Mrs. Crawley at once said that she felt herself unfit to go through such a ceremony with anything like comfort. The dean, she said, would talk of their going to stay at the deanery; but she thought it quite impossible that either of them should endure even that. But, all the same, Lady Lufton was a comfort to her; and the poor woman felt that it was well to have a lady near her in case of need.\n\nThe task was much harder with Mr. Crawley, but even with him it was not altogether unsuccessful. Lady Lufton talked to him of his parish and of her own; made Mark Robarts go to him, and by degrees did something towards civilizing him. Between him and Robarts too there grew up an intimacy rather than a friendship. Robarts would submit to his opinion on matters of ecclesiastical and even theological law, would listen to him with patience, would agree with him where he could, and differ from him mildly when he could not. For Robarts was a man who made himself pleasant to all men. And thus, under Lady Lufton’s wing, there grew up a connection between Framley and Hogglestock, in which Mrs. Robarts also assisted.\n\nAnd now that Lady Lufton was looking about her, to see how she might best bring proper clerical influence to bear upon her own recreant fox-hunting parson, it occurred to her that she might use Mr. Crawley in the matter. Mr. Crawley would certainly be on her side as far as opinion went, and would have no fear as to expressing his opinion to his brother clergyman. So she sent for Mr. Crawley.\n\nIn appearance he was the very opposite to Mark Robarts. He was a lean, slim, meagre man, with shoulders slightly curved, and pale, lank, long locks of ragged hair; his forehead was high, but his face was narrow; his small grey eyes were deeply sunken in his head, his nose was well-formed, his lips thin, and his mouth expressive. Nobody could look at him without seeing that there was a purpose and a meaning in his countenance. He always wore, in summer and winter, a long dusky gray coat, which buttoned close up to his neck and descended almost to his heels. He was full six feet high, but being so slight in build, he looked as though he were taller.\n\nHe came at once at Lady Lufton’s bidding, putting himself into the gig beside the servant, to whom he spoke no single word during the journey. And the man, looking into his face, was struck with taciturnity. Now Mark Robarts would have talked with him the whole way from Hogglestock to Framley Court; discoursing partly as to horses and land, but partly also as to higher things.\n\nAnd then Lady Lufton opened her mind and told her griefs to Mr. Crawley, urging, however, through the whole length of her narrative, that Mr. Robarts was an excellent parish clergyman,—“just such a clergyman in his church as I would wish him to be,” she explained, with the view of saving herself from an expression of any of Mr. Crawley’s special ideas as to church teaching, and of confining him to the one subject-matter in hand; “but he got this living so young, Mr. Crawley, that he is hardly quite as steady as I could wish him to be. It has been as much my fault as his own in placing him in such a position so early in life.”\n\n“I think it has,” said Mr. Crawley, who might perhaps be a little sore on such a subject.\n\n“Quite so, quite so,” continued her ladyship, swallowing down with a gulp a certain sense of anger. “But that is done now, and is past cure. That Mr. Robarts will become a credit to his profession, I do not doubt, for his heart is in the right place and his sentiments are good; but I fear that at present he is succumbing to temptation.”\n\n“I am told that he hunts two or three times a week. Everybody round us is talking about it.”\n\n“No, Mr. Crawley; not two or three times a week; very seldom above once, I think. And then I do believe he does it more with the view of being with Lord Lufton than anything else.”\n\n“I cannot see that that would make the matter better,” said Mr. Crawley.\n\n“It would show that he was not strongly imbued with a taste which I cannot but regard as vicious in a clergyman.”\n\n“It must be vicious in all men,” said Mr. Crawley. “It is in itself cruel, and leads to idleness and profligacy.”\n\nAgain Lady Lufton made a gulp. She had called Mr. Crawley thither to her aid, and felt that it would be inexpedient to quarrel with him. But she did not like to be told that her son’s amusement was idle and profligate. She had always regarded hunting as a proper pursuit for a country gentleman. It was, indeed, in her eyes one of the peculiar institutions of country life in England, and it may be almost said that she looked upon the Barsetshire hunt as something sacred. She could not endure to hear that a fox was trapped, and allowed her turkeys to be purloined without a groan. Such being the case, she did not like being told that it was vicious, and had by no means wished to consult Mr. Crawley on that matter. But nevertheless, she swallowed down her wrath.\n\n“It is at any rate unbecoming in a clergyman,” she said; “and as I know that Mr. Robarts places a high value on your opinion, perhaps you will not object to advise him to discontinue it. He might possibly feel aggrieved were I to interfere personally on such a question.”\n\n“I have no doubt he would,” said Mr. Crawley. “It is not within a woman’s province to give counsel to a clergyman on such a subject, unless she be very near and very dear to him—his wife, or mother, or sister.”\n\n“As living in the same parish, you know, and being, perhaps—” the leading person in it, and the one who naturally rules the others. Those would have been the fitting words for the expression of her ladyship’s ideas; but she remembered herself, and did not use them. She had made up her mind that, great as her influence ought to be, she was not the proper person to speak to Mr. Robarts as to his pernicious, unclerical habits, and she would not now depart from her resolve by attempting to prove that she was the proper person.\n\n“Yes,” said Mr. Crawley, “just so. All that would entitle him to offer you his counsel if he thought that your mode of life was such as to require it, but could by no means justify you in addressing yourself to him.”\n\nThis was very hard upon Lady Lufton. She was endeavouring with all her woman’s strength to do her best, and endeavouring so to do it that the feelings of the sinner might be spared; and yet the ghostly comforter whom she had evoked to her aid, treated her as though she were arrogant and overbearing. She acknowledged the weakness of her own position with reference to her parish clergyman by calling in the aid of Mr. Crawley; and under such circumstances, he might, at any rate, have abstained from throwing that weakness in her teeth.\n\n“Well, sir; I hope my mode of life may not require it; but that is not exactly to the point: what I wish to know is, whether you will speak to Mr. Robarts?”\n\n“Certainly I will,” said he.\n\n“Then I shall be much obliged to you. But, Mr. Crawley, pray—pray, remember this: I would not on any account wish that you should be harsh with him. He is an excellent young man, and—”\n\n“Lady Lufton, if I do this, I can only do it in my own way, as best I may, using such words as God may give me at the time. I hope that I am harsh to no man; but it is worse than useless, in all cases, to speak anything but the truth.”\n\n“Of course—of course.”\n\n“If the ears be too delicate to hear the truth, the mind will be too perverse to profit by it.” And then Mr. Crawley got up to take his leave.\n\nBut Lady Lufton insisted that he should go with her to luncheon. He hummed and ha’d and would fain have refused, but on this subject she was peremptory. It might be that she was unfit to advise a clergyman as to his duties, but in a matter of hospitality she did know what she was about. Mr. Crawley should not leave the house without refreshment. As to this, she carried her point; and Mr. Crawley—when the matter before him was cold roast-beef and hot potatoes, instead of the relative position of a parish priest and his parishioner—became humble, submissive, and almost timid. Lady Lufton recommended Madeira instead of Sherry, and Mr. Crawley obeyed at once, and was, indeed, perfectly unconscious of the difference. Then there was a basket of seakale in the gig for Mrs. Crawley; that he would have left behind had he dared, but he did not dare. Not a word was said to him as to the marmalade for the children which was hidden under the seakale, Lady Lufton feeling well aware that that would find its way to its proper destination without any necessity for his co-operation. And then Mr. Crawley returned home in the Framley Court gig.\n\nThree or four days after this he walked over to Framley Parsonage. This he did on a Saturday, having learned that the hounds never hunted on that day; and he started early, so that he might be sure to catch Mr. Robarts before he went out on his parish business. He was quite early enough to attain this object, for when he reached the parsonage door at about half-past nine, the vicar, with his wife and sister, were just sitting down to breakfast.\n\n“Oh, Crawley,” said Robarts, before the other had well spoken, “you are a capital fellow;” and then he got him into a chair, and Mrs. Robarts had poured him out tea, and Lucy had surrendered to him a knife and plate, before he knew under what guise to excuse his coming among them.\n\n“I hope you will excuse this intrusion,” at last he muttered; “but I have a few words of business to which I will request your attention presently.”\n\n“Certainly,” said Robarts, conveying a broiled kidney on to the plate before Mr. Crawley; “but there is no preparation for business like a good breakfast. Lucy, hand Mr. Crawley the buttered toast. Eggs, Fanny; where are the eggs?” And then John, in livery, brought in the fresh eggs. “Now we shall do. I always eat my eggs while they’re hot, Crawley, and I advise you to do the same.”\n\nTo all this Mr. Crawley said very little, and he was not at all at home under the circumstances. Perhaps a thought did pass across his brain, as to the difference between the meal which he had left on his own table, and that which he now saw before him; and as to any cause which might exist for such difference. But, if so, it was a very fleeting thought, for he had far other matter now fully occupying his mind. And then the breakfast was over, and in a few minutes the two clergymen found themselves together in the parsonage study.\n\n“Mr. Robarts,” began the senior, when he had seated himself uncomfortably on one of the ordinary chairs at the farther side of the well-stored library table, while Mark was sitting at his ease in his own arm-chair by the fire, “I have called upon you on an unpleasant business.”\n\nMark’s mind immediately flew off to Mr. Sowerby’s bill, but he could not think it possible that Mr. Crawley could have had anything to do with that.\n\n“But as a brother clergyman, and as one who esteems you much and wishes you well, I have thought myself bound to take this matter in hand.”\n\n“What matter is it, Crawley?”\n\n“Mr. Robarts, men say that your present mode of life is one that is not befitting a soldier in Christ’s army.”\n\n“Men say so! what men?”\n\n“The men around you, of your own neighbourhood; those who watch your life, and know all your doings; those who look to see you walking as a lamp to guide their feet, but find you consorting with horse-jockeys and hunters, galloping after hounds, and taking your place among the vainest of worldly pleasure-seekers. Those who have a right to expect an example of good living, and who think that they do not see it.”\n\nMr. Crawley had gone at once to the root of the matter, and in doing so had certainly made his own task so much the easier. There is nothing like going to the root of the matter at once when one has on hand an unpleasant piece of business.\n\n“And have such men deputed you to come here?”\n\n“No one has or could depute me. I have come to speak my own mind, not that of any other. But I refer to what those around you think and say, because it is to them that your duties are due. You owe it to those around you to live a godly, cleanly life;—as you owe it also, in a much higher way, to your Father who is in heaven. I now make bold to ask you whether you are doing your best to lead such a life as that?” And then he remained silent, waiting for an answer.\n\nHe was a singular man; so humble and meek, so unutterably inefficient and awkward in the ordinary intercourse of life, but so bold and enterprising, almost eloquent, on the one subject which was the work of his mind! As he sat there, he looked into his companion’s face from out his sunken grey eyes with a gaze which made his victim quail. And then repeated his words: “I now make bold to ask you, Mr. Robarts, whether you are doing your best to lead such a life as may become a parish clergyman among his parishioners?” And again he paused for an answer.\n\n“There are but few of us,” said Mark in a low tone, “who could safely answer that question in the affirmative.”\n\n“But are there many, think you, among us who would find the question so unanswerable as yourself? And even were there many, would you, young, enterprising, and talented as you are, be content to be numbered among them? Are you satisfied to be a castaway after you have taken upon yourself Christ’s armour? If you will say so, I am mistaken in you, and will go my way.” There was again a pause, and then he went on. “Speak to me, my brother, and open your heart if it be possible.” And rising from his chair, he walked across the room, and laid his hand tenderly on Mark’s shoulder.\n\nMark had been sitting lounging in his chair, and had at first, for a moment only, thought to brazen it out. But all idea of brazening had now left him. He had raised himself from his comfortable ease, and was leaning forward with his elbow on the table; but now, when he heard these words, he allowed his head to sink upon his arms, and he buried his face between his hands.\n\n“It is a terrible falling off,” continued Crawley: “terrible in the fall, but doubly terrible through that difficulty of returning. But it cannot be that it should content you to place yourself as one among those thoughtless sinners, for the crushing of whose sin you have been placed here among them. You become a hunting parson, and ride with a happy mind among blasphemers and mocking devils—you, whose aspirations were so high, who have spoken so often and so well of the duties of a minister of Christ; you, who can argue in your pride as to the petty details of your Church, as though the broad teachings of its great and simple lessons were not enough for your energies! It cannot be that I have had a hypocrite beside me in all those eager controversies!”\n\n“Not a hypocrite—not a hypocrite,” said Mark, in a tone which was almost reduced to sobbing.\n\n“But a castaway! Is it so that I must call you? No, Mr. Robarts, not a castaway; neither a hypocrite, nor a castaway; but one who in walking has stumbled in the dark and bruised his feet among the stones. Henceforth let him take a lantern in his hand, and look warily to his path, and walk cautiously among the thorns and rocks,—cautiously, but yet boldly, with manly courage, but Christian meekness, as all men should walk on their pilgrimage through this vale of tears.” And then without giving his companion time to stop him he hurried out of the room, and from the house, and without again seeing any others of the family, stalked back on his road to Hogglestock, thus tramping fourteen miles through the deep mud in performance of the mission on which he had been sent.\n\nIt was some hours before Mr. Robarts left his room. As soon as he found that Crawley was really gone, and that he should see him no more, he turned the lock of his door, and sat himself down to think over his present life. At about eleven his wife knocked, not knowing whether that other strange clergyman were there or no, for none had seen his departure. But Mark, answering cheerily, desired that he might be left to his studies.\n\nLet us hope that his thoughts and mental resolves were then of service to him.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI. MRS. PODGENS’ BABY.\n\nThe hunting season had now nearly passed away, and the great ones of the Barsetshire world were thinking of the glories of London. Of these glories Lady Lufton always thought with much inquietude of mind. She would fain have remained throughout the whole year at Framley Court, did not certain grave considerations render such a course on her part improper in her own estimation. All the Lady Luftons of whom she had heard, dowager and ante-dowager, had always had their seasons in London, till old age had incapacitated them for such doings—sometimes for clearly long after the arrival of such period. And then she had an idea, perhaps not altogether erroneous, that she annually imported back with her into the country somewhat of the passing civilization of the times:—may we not say an idea that certainly was not erroneous? for how otherwise is it that the forms of new caps and remodelled shapes for women’s waists find their way down into agricultural parts, and that the rural eye learns to appreciate grace and beauty? There are those who think that remodelled waists and new caps had better be kept to the towns; but such people, if they would follow out their own argument, would wish to see ploughboys painted with ruddle and milkmaids covered with skins.\n\nFor these and other reasons Lady Lufton always went to London in April, and stayed there till the beginning of June. But for her this was usually a period of penance. In London she was no very great personage. She had never laid herself out for greatness of that sort, and did not shine as a lady-patroness or state secretary in the female cabinet of fashion. She was dull and listless, and without congenial pursuits in London, and spent her happiest moments in reading accounts of what was being done at Framley, and in writing orders for further local information of the same kind.\n\nBut on this occasion there was a matter of vital import to give an interest of its own to her visit to town. She was to entertain Griselda Grantly, and as far as might be possible to induce her son to remain in Griselda’s society. The plan of the campaign was to be as follows:—Mrs. Grantly and the archdeacon were in the first place to go up to London for a month, taking Griselda with them; and then, when they returned to Plumstead, Griselda was to go to Lady Lufton. This arrangement was not at all points agreeable to Lady Lufton, for she knew that Mrs. Grantly did not turn her back on the Hartletop people quite as cordially as she should do, considering the terms of the Lufton-Grantly family treaty. But then Mrs. Grantly might have alleged in excuse the slow manner in which Lord Lufton proceeded in the making and declaring of his love, and the absolute necessity which there is for two strings to one’s bow, when one string may be in any way doubtful. Could it be possible that Mrs. Grantly had heard anything of that unfortunate Platonic friendship with Lucy Robarts?\n\nThere came a letter from Mrs. Grantly just about the end of March, which added much to Lady Lufton’s uneasiness, and made her more than ever anxious to be herself on the scene of action and to have Griselda in her own hands. After some communications of mere ordinary importance with reference to the London world in general and the Lufton-Grantly world in particular, Mrs. Grantly wrote confidentially about her daughter:\n\n“It would be useless to deny,” she said, with a mother’s pride and a mother’s humility, “that she is very much admired. She is asked out a great deal more than I can take her, and to houses to which I myself by no means wish to go. I could not refuse her as to Lady Hartletop’s first ball, for there will be nothing else this year like them; and of course when with you, dear Lady Lufton, that house will be out of the question. So indeed would it be with me, were I myself only concerned. The duke was there, of course, and I really wonder Lady Hartletop should not be more discreet in her own drawing-room when all the world is there. It is clear to me that Lord Dumbello admires Griselda much more than I could wish. She, dear girl, has such excellent sense that I do not think it likely that her head should be turned by it; but with how many girls would not the admiration of such a man be irresistible? The marquis, you know, is very feeble, and I am told that since this rage for building has come on, the Lancashire property is over two hundred thousand a year!! I do not think that Lord Dumbello has said much to her. Indeed it seems to me that he never does say much to any one. But he always stands up to dance with her, and I see that he is uneasy and fidgety when she stands up with any other partner whom he could care about. It was really embarrassing to see him the other night at Miss Dunstable’s, when Griselda was dancing with a certain friend of ours. But she did look very well that evening, and I have seldom seen her more animated!”\n\nAll this, and a great deal more of the same sort in the same letter, tended to make Lady Lufton anxious to be in London. It was quite certain—there was no doubt of that, at any rate—that Griselda would see no more of Lady Hartletop’s meretricious grandeur when she had been transferred to Lady Lufton’s guardianship. And she, Lady Lufton, did wonder that Mrs. Grantly should have taken her daughter to such a house. All about Lady Hartletop was known to all the world. It was known that it was almost the only house in London at which the Duke of Omnium was constantly to be met. Lady Lufton herself would almost as soon think of taking a young girl to Gatherum Castle; and on these accounts she did feel rather angry with her friend Mrs. Grantly. But then perhaps she did not sufficiently calculate that Mrs. Grantly’s letter had been written purposely to produce such feelings—with the express view of awakening her ladyship to the necessity of action. Indeed in such a matter as this Mrs. Grantly was a more able woman than Lady Lufton—more able to see her way and to follow it out. The Lufton-Grantly alliance was in her mind the best, seeing that she did not regard money as everything. But failing that, the Hartletop-Grantly alliance was not bad. Regarding it as a second string to her bow, she thought that it was not at all bad.\n\nLady Lufton’s reply was very affectionate. She declared how happy she was to know that Griselda was enjoying herself; she insinuated that Lord Dumbello was known to the world as a fool, and his mother as—being not a bit better than she ought to be; and then she added that circumstances would bring herself up to town four days sooner than she had expected, and that she hoped her dear Griselda would come to her at once. Lord Lufton, she said, though he would not sleep in Bruton Street—Lady Lufton lived in Bruton Street—had promised to pass there as much of his time as his parliamentary duties would permit.\n\nO Lady Lufton! Lady Lufton! did it not occur to you, when you wrote those last words, intending that they should have so strong an effect on the mind of your correspondent, that you were telling a—tarradiddle? Was it not the case that you had said to your son, in your own dear, kind, motherly way: “Ludovic, we shall see something of you in Bruton Street this year, shall we not? Griselda Grantly will be with me, and we must not let her be dull—must we?” And then had he not answered, “Oh, of course, mother,” and sauntered out of the room, not altogether graciously? Had he, or you, said a word about his parliamentary duties? Not a word! O Lady Lufton! have you not now written a tarradiddle to your friend?\n\nIn these days we are becoming very strict about truth with our children; terribly strict occasionally, when we consider the natural weakness of the moral courage at the ages of ten, twelve, and fourteen. But I do not know that we are at all increasing the measure of strictness with which we, grown-up people, regulate our own truth and falsehood. Heaven forbid that I should be thought to advocate falsehood in children; but an untruth is more pardonable in them than in their parents. Lady Lufton’s tarradiddle was of a nature that is usually considered excusable—at least with grown people; but, nevertheless, she would have been nearer to perfection could she have confined herself to the truth. Let us suppose that a boy were to write home from school, saying that another boy had promised to come and stay with him, that other having given no such promise—what a very naughty boy would that first boy be in the eyes of his pastors and masters!\n\nThat little conversation between Lord Lufton and his mother—in which nothing was said about his lordship’s parliamentary duties—took place on the evening before he started for London. On that occasion he certainly was not in his best humour, nor did he behave to his mother in his kindest manner. He had then left the room when she began to talk about Miss Grantly; and once again in the course of the evening, when his mother, not very judiciously, said a word or two about Griselda’s beauty, he had remarked that she was no conjuror, and would hardly set the Thames on fire.\n\n“If she were a conjuror!” said Lady Lufton, rather piqued, “I should not now be going to take her out in London. I know many of those sort of girls whom you call conjurors; they can talk for ever, and always talk either loudly or in a whisper. I don’t like them, and I am sure that you do not in your heart.”\n\n“Oh, as to liking them in my heart—that is being very particular.”\n\n“Griselda Grantly is a lady, and as such I shall be happy to have her with me in town. She is just the girl that Justinia will like to have with her.”\n\n“Exactly,” said Lord Lufton. “She will do exceedingly well for Justinia.”\n\nNow this was not good-natured on the part of Lord Lufton; and his mother felt it the more strongly, inasmuch as it seemed to signify that he was setting his back up against the Lufton-Grantly alliance. She had been pretty sure that he would do so in the event of his suspecting that a plot was being laid to catch him; and now it almost appeared that he did suspect such a plot. Why else that sarcasm as to Griselda doing very well for his sister?\n\nAnd now we must go back and describe a little scene at Framley which will account for his lordship’s ill-humour and suspicions, and explain how it came to pass that he so snubbed his mother. This scene took place about ten days after the evening on which Mrs. Robarts and Lucy were walking together in the parsonage garden, and during those ten days Lucy had not once allowed herself to be entrapped into any special conversation with the young peer. She had dined at Framley Court during that interval, and had spent a second evening there; Lord Lufton had also been up at the parsonage on three or four occasions, and had looked for her in her usual walks; but, nevertheless, they had never come together in their old familiar way, since the day on which Lady Lufton had hinted her fears to Mrs. Robarts.\n\nLord Lufton had very much missed her. At first he had not attributed this change to a purposed scheme of action on the part of any one; nor, indeed, had he much thought about it, although he had felt himself to be annoyed. But as the period fixed for his departure grew near, it did occur to him as very odd that he should never hear Lucy’s voice unless when she said a few words to his mother, or to her sister-in-law. And then he made up his mind that he would speak to her before he went, and that the mystery should be explained to him.\n\nAnd he carried out his purpose, calling at the parsonage on one special afternoon; and it was on the evening of the same day that his mother sang the praises of Griselda Grantly so inopportunely. Robarts, he knew, was then absent from home, and Mrs. Robarts was with his mother down at the house, preparing lists of the poor people to be specially attended to in Lady Lufton’s approaching absence. Taking advantage of this, he walked boldly in through the parsonage garden; asked the gardener, with an indifferent voice, whether either of the ladies were at home, and then caught poor Lucy exactly on the doorstep of the house.\n\n“Were you going in or out, Miss Robarts?”\n\n“Well, I was going out,” said Lucy; and she began to consider how best she might get quit of any prolonged encounter.\n\n“Oh, going out, were you? I don’t know whether I may offer to—”\n\n“Well, Lord Lufton, not exactly, seeing that I am about to pay a visit to our near neighbour, Mrs. Podgens. Perhaps you have no particular call towards Mrs. Podgens’ just at present, or to her new baby?”\n\n“And have you any very particular call that way?”\n\n“Yes, and especially to Baby Podgens. Baby Podgens is a real little duck—only just two days old.” And Lucy, as she spoke, progressed a step or two, as though she were determined not to remain there talking on the doorstep.\n\nA slight cloud came across his brow as he saw this, and made him resolve that she should not gain her purpose. He was not going to be foiled in that way by such a girl as Lucy Robarts. He had come there to speak to her, and speak to her he would. There had been enough of intimacy between them to justify him in demanding, at any rate, as much as that.\n\n“Miss Robarts,” he said, “I am starting for London to-morrow, and if I do not say good-bye to you now, I shall not be able to do so at all.”\n\n“Good-bye, Lord Lufton,” she said, giving him her hand, and smiling on him with her old genial, good-humoured, racy smile. “And mind you bring into Parliament that law which you promised me for defending my young chickens.”\n\nHe took her hand, but that was not all that he wanted. “Surely Mrs. Podgens and her baby can wait ten minutes. I shall not see you again for months to come, and yet you seem to begrudge me two words.”\n\n“Not two hundred if they can be of any service to you,” said she, walking cheerily back into the drawing-room; “only I did not think it worth while to waste your time, as Fanny is not here.”\n\nShe was infinitely more collected, more master of herself than he was. Inwardly, she did tremble at the idea of what was coming, but outwardly she showed no agitation—none as yet; if only she could so possess herself as to refrain from doing so, when she heard what he might have to say to her.\n\nHe hardly knew what it was for the saying of which he had so resolutely come thither. He had by no means made up his mind that he loved Lucy Robarts; nor had he made up his mind that, loving her, he would, or that, loving her, he would not, make her his wife. He had never used his mind in the matter in any way, either for good or evil. He had learned to like her and to think that she was very pretty. He had found out that it was very pleasant to talk to her; whereas, talking to Griselda Grantly, and, indeed, to some other young ladies of his acquaintance, was often hard work. The half-hours which he had spent with Lucy had always been satisfactory to him. He had found himself to be more bright with her than with other people, and more apt to discuss subjects worth discussing; and thus it had come about that he thoroughly liked Lucy Robarts. As to whether his affection was Platonic or anti-Platonic he had never asked himself; but he had spoken words to her, shortly before that sudden cessation of their intimacy, which might have been taken as anti-Platonic by any girl so disposed to regard them. He had not thrown himself at her feet, and declared himself to be devoured by a consuming passion; but he had touched her hand as lovers touch those of women whom they love; he had had his confidences with her, talking to her of his own mother, of his sister, and of his friends; and he had called her his own dear friend Lucy.\n\nAll this had been very sweet to her, but very poisonous also. She had declared to herself very frequently that her liking for this young nobleman was as purely a feeling of mere friendship as was that of her brother; and she had professed to herself that she would give the lie to the world’s cold sarcasms on such subjects. But she had now acknowledged that the sarcasms of the world on that matter, cold though they may be, are not the less true; and having so acknowledged, she had resolved that all close alliance between herself and Lord Lufton must be at an end. She had come to a conclusion, but he had come to none; and in this frame of mind he was now there with the object of reopening that dangerous friendship which she had had the sense to close.\n\n“And so you are going to-morrow?” she said, as soon as they were both within the drawing-room.\n\n“Yes: I’m off by the early train to-morrow morning, and Heaven knows when we may meet again.”\n\n“Next winter, shall we not?”\n\n“Yes, for a day or two, I suppose. I do not know whether I shall pass another winter here. Indeed, one can never say where one will be.”\n\n“No, one can’t; such as you, at least, cannot. I am not of a migratory tribe myself.”\n\n“I wish you were.”\n\n“I’m not a bit obliged to you. Your nomade life does not agree with young ladies.”\n\n“I think they are taking to it pretty freely, then. We have unprotected young women all about the world.”\n\n“And great bores you find them, I suppose?”\n\n“No; I like it. The more we can get out of old-fashioned grooves the better I am pleased. I should be a radical to-morrow—a regular man of the people,—only I should break my mother’s heart.”\n\n“Whatever you do, Lord Lufton, do not do that.”\n\n“That is why I have liked you so much,” he continued, “because you get out of the grooves.”\n\n“Do I?”\n\n“Yes; and go along by yourself, guiding your own footsteps; not carried hither and thither, just as your grandmother’s old tramway may chance to take you.”\n\n“Do you know I have a strong idea that my grandmother’s old tramway will be the safest and the best after all? I have not left it very far, and I certainly mean to go back to it.”\n\n“That’s impossible! An army of old women, with coils of ropes made out of time-honoured prejudices, could not drag you back.”\n\n“No, Lord Lufton, that is true. But one—” and then she stopped herself. She could not tell him that one loving mother, anxious for her only son, had sufficed to do it. She could not explain to him that this departure from the established tramway had already broken her own rest, and turned her peaceful happy life into a grievous battle.\n\n“I know that you are trying to go back,” he said. “Do you think that I have eyes and cannot see? Come, Lucy, you and I have been friends, and we must not part in this way. My mother is a paragon among women. I say it in earnest;—a paragon among women: and her love for me is the perfection of motherly love.”\n\n“It is, it is; and I am so glad that you acknowledge it.”\n\n“I should be worse than a brute did I not do so; but, nevertheless, I cannot allow her to lead me in all things. Were I to do so, I should cease to be a man.”\n\n“Where can you find any one who will counsel you so truly?”\n\n“But, nevertheless, I must rule myself. I do not know whether my suspicions may be perfectly just, but I fancy that she has created this estrangement between you and me. Has it not been so?”\n\n“Certainly not by speaking to me,” said Lucy, blushing ruby-red through every vein of her deep-tinted face. But though she could not command her blood, her voice was still under her control—her voice and her manner.\n\n“But has she not done so? You, I know, will tell me nothing but the truth.”\n\n“I will tell you nothing on this matter, Lord Lufton, whether true or false. It is a subject on which it does not concern me to speak.”\n\n“Ah! I understand,” he said; and rising from his chair, he stood against the chimney-piece with his back to the fire. “She cannot leave me alone to choose for myself my own friends, and my own—;” but he did not fill up the void.\n\n“But why tell me this, Lord Lufton?”\n\n“No! I am not to choose my own friends, though they be among the best and purest of God’s creatures. Lucy, I cannot think that you have ceased to have a regard for me. That you had a regard for me, I am sure.”\n\nShe felt that it was almost unmanly of him thus to seek her out, and hunt her down, and then throw upon her the whole weight of the explanation that his coming thither made necessary. But, nevertheless, the truth must be told, and with God’s help she would find strength for the telling of it.\n\n“Yes, Lord Lufton, I had a regard for you—and have. By that word you mean something more than the customary feeling of acquaintance which may ordinarily prevail between a gentleman and lady of different families, who have known each other so short a time as we have done.”\n\n“Yes, something much more,” said he, with energy.\n\n“Well, I will not define the much—something closer than that.”\n\n“Yes, and warmer, and dearer, and more worthy of two human creatures who value each other’s minds and hearts.”\n\n“Some such closer regard I have felt for you—very foolishly. Stop! You have made me speak, and do not interrupt me now. Does not your conscience tell you that in doing so I have unwisely deserted those wise old grandmother’s tramways of which you spoke just now? It has been pleasant to me to do so. I have liked the feeling of independence with which I have thought that I might indulge in an open friendship with such as you are. And your rank, so different from my own, has doubtless made this more attractive.”\n\n“Nonsense!”\n\n“Ah! but it has. I know it now. But what will the world say of me as to such an alliance?”\n\n“The world!”\n\n“Yes, the world! I am not such a philosopher as to disregard it, though you may afford to do so. The world will say that I, the parson’s sister, set my cap at the young lord, and that the young lord had made a fool of me.”\n\n“The world shall say no such thing!” said Lord Lufton, very imperiously.\n\n“Ah! but it will. You can no more stop it, than King Canute could the waters. Your mother has interfered wisely to spare me from this; and the only favour that I can ask you is, that you will spare me also.” And then she got up as though she intended at once to walk forth to her visit to Mrs. Podgens’ baby.\n\n“Stop, Lucy!” he said, putting himself between her and the door.\n\n“It must not be Lucy any longer, Lord Lufton; I was madly foolish when I first allowed it.”\n\n“By heavens! but it shall be Lucy—Lucy before all the world. My Lucy, my own Lucy—my heart’s best friend, and chosen love. Lucy, there is my hand. How long you may have had my heart, it matters not to say now.”\n\nThe game was at her feet now, and no doubt she felt her triumph. Her ready wit and speaking lip, not her beauty, had brought him to her side; and now he was forced to acknowledge that her power over him had been supreme. Sooner than leave her he would risk all. She did feel her triumph; but there was nothing in her face to tell him that she did so.\n\nAs to what she would now do she did not for a moment doubt. He had been precipitated into the declaration he had made, not by his love, but by his embarrassment. She had thrown in his teeth the injury which he had done her, and he had then been moved by his generosity to repair that injury by the noblest sacrifice which he could make. But Lucy Robarts was not the girl to accept a sacrifice.\n\nHe had stepped forward as though he were going to clasp her round the waist, but she receded, and got beyond the reach of his hand. “Lord Lufton!” she said, “when you are more cool you will know that this is wrong. The best thing for both of us now is to part.”\n\n“Not the best thing, but the very worst, till we perfectly understand each other.”\n\n“Then perfectly understand me, that I cannot be your wife.”\n\n“Lucy! do you mean that you cannot learn to love me?”\n\n“I mean that I shall not try. Do not persevere in this, or you will have to hate yourself for your own folly.”\n\n“But I will persevere till you accept my love, or say with your hand on your heart that you cannot and will not love me.”\n\n“Then I must beg you to let me go,” and having so said, she paused while he walked once or twice hurriedly up and down the room. “And, Lord Lufton,” she continued, “if you will leave me now, the words that you have spoken shall be as though they had never been uttered.”\n\n“I care not who knows that they have been uttered. The sooner that they are known to all the world, the better I shall be pleased, unless indeed—”\n\n“Think of your mother, Lord Lufton.”\n\n“What can I do better than give her as a daughter the best and sweetest girl I have ever met? When my mother really knows you, she will love you as I do. Lucy, say one word to me of comfort.”\n\n“I will say no word to you that shall injure your future comfort. It is impossible that I should be your wife.”\n\n“Do you mean that you cannot love me?”\n\n“You have no right to press me any further,” she said; and sat down upon the sofa, with an angry frown upon her forehead.\n\n“By heavens,” he said, “I will take no such answer from you till you put your hand upon your heart, and say that you cannot love me.”\n\n“Oh, why should you press me so, Lord Lufton?”\n\n“Why! because my happiness depends upon it; because it behoves me to know the very truth. It has come to this, that I love you with my whole heart, and I must know how your heart stands towards me.”\n\nShe had now again risen from the sofa, and was looking steadily in his face.\n\n“Lord Lufton,” she said, “I cannot love you,” and as she spoke she did put her hand, as he had desired, upon her heart.\n\n“Then God help me! for I am very wretched. Good-bye, Lucy,” and he stretched out his hand to her.\n\n“Good-bye, my lord. Do not be angry with me.”\n\n“No, no, no!” and without further speech he left the room and the house, and hurried home. It was hardly surprising that he should that evening tell his mother that Griselda Grantly would be a companion sufficiently good for his sister. He wanted no such companion.\n\nAnd when he was well gone—absolutely out of sight from the window—Lucy walked steadily up to her room, locked the door, and then threw herself on the bed. Why—oh! why had she told such a falsehood? Could anything justify her in a lie? Was it not a lie—knowing as she did that she loved him with all her loving heart?\n\nBut, then, his mother! and the sneers of the world, which would have declared that she had set her trap, and caught the foolish young lord! Her pride would not have submitted to that. Strong as her love was, yet her pride was, perhaps, stronger—stronger at any rate during that interview.\n\nBut how was she to forgive herself the falsehood she had told?\n\n# CHAPTER XVII. MRS. PROUDIE’S CONVERSAZIONE.\n\nIt was grievous to think of the mischief and danger into which Griselda Grantly was brought by the worldliness of her mother in those few weeks previous to Lady Lufton’s arrival in town—very grievous, at least, to her ladyship, as from time to time she heard of what was done in London. Lady Hartletop’s was not the only objectionable house at which Griselda was allowed to reap fresh fashionable laurels. It had been stated openly in the Morning Post that that young lady had been the most admired among the beautiful at one of Miss Dunstable’s celebrated soirées, and then she was heard of as gracing the drawing-room at Mrs. Proudie’s conversazione.\n\nOf Miss Dunstable herself Lady Lufton was not able openly to allege any evil. She was acquainted, Lady Lufton knew, with very many people of the right sort, and was the dear friend of Lady Lufton’s highly conservative and not very distant neighbours, the Greshams. But then she was also acquainted with so many people of the bad sort. Indeed, she was intimate with everybody, from the Duke of Omnium to old Dowager Lady Goodygaffer, who had represented all the cardinal virtues for the last quarter of a century. She smiled with equal sweetness on treacle and on brimstone; was quite at home at Exeter Hall, having been consulted—so the world said, probably not with exact truth—as to the selection of more than one disagreeably Low Church bishop; and was not less frequent in her attendance at the ecclesiastical doings of a certain terrible prelate in the Midland counties, who was supposed to favour stoles and vespers, and to have no proper Protestant hatred for auricular confession and fish on Fridays. Lady Lufton, who was very staunch, did not like this, and would say of Miss Dunstable that it was impossible to serve both God and Mammon.\n\nBut Mrs. Proudie was much more objectionable to her. Seeing how sharp was the feud between the Proudies and the Grantlys down in Barsetshire, how absolutely unable they had always been to carry a decent face towards each other in church matters, how they headed two parties in the diocese, which were, when brought together, as oil and vinegar, in which battles the whole Lufton influence had always been brought to bear on the Grantly side;—seeing all this, I say, Lady Lufton was surprised to hear that Griselda had been taken to Mrs. Proudie’s evening exhibition. “Had the archdeacon been consulted about it,” she said to herself, “this would never have happened.” But there she was wrong, for in matters concerning his daughter’s introduction to the world the archdeacon never interfered.\n\nOn the whole, I am inclined to think that Mrs. Grantly understood the world better than did Lady Lufton. In her heart of hearts Mrs. Grantly hated Mrs. Proudie—that is, with that sort of hatred one Christian lady allows herself to feel towards another. Of course Mrs. Grantly forgave Mrs. Proudie all her offences, and wished her well, and was at peace with her, in the Christian sense of the word, as with all other women. But under this forbearance and meekness, and perhaps, we may say, wholly unconnected with it, there was certainly a current of antagonistic feeling which, in the ordinary unconsidered language of every day, men and women do call hatred. This raged and was strong throughout the whole year in Barsetshire, before the eyes of all mankind. But, nevertheless, Mrs. Grantly took Griselda to Mrs. Proudie’s evening parties in London.\n\nIn these days Mrs. Proudie considered herself to be by no means the least among bishops’ wives. She had opened the season this year in a new house in Gloucester Place, at which the reception rooms, at any rate, were all that a lady bishop could desire. Here she had a front drawing-room of very noble dimensions, a second drawing-room rather noble also, though it had lost one of its back corners awkwardly enough, apparently in a jostle with the neighbouring house; and then there was a third—shall we say drawing-room, or closet?—in which Mrs. Proudie delighted to be seen sitting, in order that the world might know that there was a third room; altogether a noble suite, as Mrs. Proudie herself said in confidence to more than one clergyman’s wife from Barsetshire. “A noble suite, indeed, Mrs. Proudie!” the clergymen’s wives from Barsetshire would usually answer.\n\nFor some time Mrs. Proudie was much at a loss to know by what sort of party or entertainment she would make herself famous. Balls and suppers were of course out of the question. She did not object to her daughters dancing all night at other houses—at least, of late she had not objected, for the fashionable world required it, and the young ladies had perhaps a will of their own—but dancing at her house—absolutely under the shade of the bishop’s apron—would be a sin and a scandal. And then as to suppers—of all modes in which one may extend one’s hospitality to a large acquaintance, they are the most costly.\n\n“It is horrid to think that we should go out among our friends for the mere sake of eating and drinking,” Mrs. Proudie would say to the clergymen’s wives from Barsetshire. “It shows such a sensual propensity.”\n\n“Indeed it does, Mrs. Proudie; and is so vulgar too!” those ladies would reply.\n\nBut the elder among them would remember with regret the unsparing, open-handed hospitality of Barchester palace in the good old days of Bishop Grantly—God rest his soul! One old vicar’s wife there was whose answer had not been so courteous—\n\n“When we are hungry, Mrs. Proudie,” she had said, “we do all have sensual propensities.”\n\n“It would be much better, Mrs. Athill, if the world would provide for all that at home,” Mrs. Proudie had rapidly replied; with which opinion I must here profess that I cannot by any means bring myself to coincide.\n\nBut a conversazione would give play to no sensual propensity, nor occasion that intolerable expense which the gratification of sensual propensities too often produces. Mrs. Proudie felt that the word was not all that she could have desired. It was a little faded by old use and present oblivion, and seemed to address itself to that portion of the London world that is considered blue, rather than fashionable. But, nevertheless, there was a spirituality about it which suited her, and one may also say an economy. And then as regarded fashion, it might perhaps not be beyond the power of a Mrs. Proudie to regild the word with a newly burnished gilding. Some leading person must produce fashion at first hand, and why not Mrs. Proudie?\n\nHer plan was to set the people by the ears talking, if talk they would, or to induce them to show themselves there inert if no more could be got from them. To accommodate with chairs and sofas as many as the furniture of her noble suite of rooms would allow, especially with the two chairs and padded bench against the wall in the back closet—the small inner drawing-room, as she would call it to the clergymen’s wives from Barsetshire—and to let the others stand about upright, or “group themselves,” as she described it. Then four times during the two hours’ period of her conversazione tea and cake were to be handed round on salvers. It is astonishing how far a very little cake will go in this way, particularly if administered tolerably early after dinner. The men can’t eat it, and the women, having no plates and no table, are obliged to abstain. Mrs. Jones knows that she cannot hold a piece of crumbly cake in her hand till it be consumed without doing serious injury to her best dress. When Mrs. Proudie, with her weekly books before her, looked into the financial upshot of her conversazione, her conscience told her that she had done the right thing.\n\nGoing out to tea is not a bad thing, if one can contrive to dine early, and then be allowed to sit round a big table with a tea urn in the middle. I would, however, suggest that breakfast cups should always be provided for the gentlemen. And then with pleasant neighbours,—or more especially with a pleasant neighbour,—the affair is not, according to my taste, by any means the worst phase of society. But I do dislike that handing round, unless it be of a subsidiary thimbleful when the business of the social intercourse has been dinner.\n\nAnd indeed this handing round has become a vulgar and an intolerable nuisance among us second-class gentry with our eight hundred a year—there or thereabouts;—doubly intolerable as being destructive of our natural comforts, and a wretchedly vulgar aping of men with large incomes. The Duke of Omnium and Lady Hartletop are undoubtedly wise to have everything handed round. Friends of mine who occasionally dine at such houses tell me that they get their wine quite as quickly as they can drink it, that their mutton is brought to them without delay, and that the potato-bearer follows quick upon the heels of carnifer. Nothing can be more comfortable, and we may no doubt acknowledge that these first-class grandees do understand their material comforts. But we of the eight hundred can no more come up to them in this than we can in their opera-boxes and equipages. May I not say that the usual tether of this class, in the way of carnifers, cup-bearers, and the rest, does not reach beyond neat-handed Phyllis and the greengrocer? and that Phyllis, neat-handed as she probably is, and the greengrocer, though he be ever so active, cannot administer a dinner to twelve people who are prohibited by a Medo-Persian law from all self-administration whatever? And may I not further say that the lamentable consequence to us eight hundreders dining out among each other is this, that we too often get no dinner at all. Phyllis, with the potatoes, cannot reach us till our mutton is devoured, or in a lukewarm state past our power of managing; and Ganymede, the greengrocer, though we admire the skill of his necktie and the whiteness of his unexceptionable gloves, fails to keep us going in Sherry.\n\nSeeing a lady the other day in this strait, left without a small modicum of stimulus which was no doubt necessary for her good digestion, I ventured to ask her to drink wine with me. But when I bowed my head at her, she looked at me with all her eyes, struck with amazement. Had I suggested that she should join me in a wild Indian war-dance, with nothing on but my paint, her face could not have shown greater astonishment. And yet I should have thought she might have remembered the days when Christian men and women used to drink wine with each other.\n\nGod be with the good old days when I could hobnob with my friend over the table as often as I was inclined to lift my glass to my lips, and make a long arm for a hot potato whenever the exigencies of my plate required it.\n\nI think it may be laid down as a rule in affairs of hospitality, that whatever extra luxury or grandeur we introduce at our tables when guests are with us, should be introduced for the advantage of the guest and not for our own. If, for instance, our dinner be served in a manner different from that usual to us, it should be so served in order that our friends may with more satisfaction eat our repast than our everyday practice would produce on them. But the change should by no means be made to their material detriment in order that our fashion may be acknowledged. Again, if I decorate my sideboard and table, wishing that the eyes of my visitors may rest on that which is elegant and pleasant to the sight, I act in that matter with a becoming sense of hospitality; but if my object be to kill Mrs. Jones with envy at the sight of all my silver trinkets, I am a very mean-spirited fellow. This, in a broad way, will be acknowledged; but if we would bear in mind the same idea at all times,—on occasions when the way perhaps may not be so broad, when more thinking may be required to ascertain what is true hospitality,—I think we of the eight hundred would make a greater advance towards really entertaining our own friends than by any rearrangement of the actual meats and dishes which we set before them.\n\nKnowing as we do, that the terms of the Lufton-Grantly alliance had been so solemnly ratified between the two mothers, it is perhaps hardly open to us to suppose that Mrs. Grantly was induced to take her daughter to Mrs. Proudie’s by any knowledge which she may have acquired that Lord Dumbello had promised to grace the bishop’s assembly. It is certainly the fact that high contracting parties do sometimes allow themselves a latitude which would be considered dishonest by contractors of a lower sort; and it may be possible that the archdeacon’s wife did think of that second string with which her bow was furnished. Be that as it may, Lord Dumbello was at Mrs. Proudie’s, and it did so come to pass that Griselda was seated at the corner of a sofa close to which was a vacant space in which his lordship could—“group himself.”\n\nThey had not been long there before Lord Dumbello did group himself. “Fine day,” he said, coming up and occupying the vacant position by Miss Grantly’s elbow.\n\n“We were driving to-day, and we thought it rather cold,” said Griselda.\n\n“Deuced cold,” said Lord Dumbello, and then he adjusted his white cravat and touched up his whiskers. Having got so far, he did not proceed to any other immediate conversational efforts; nor did Griselda. But he grouped himself again as became a marquis, and gave very intense satisfaction to Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“This is so kind of you, Lord Dumbello,” said that lady, coming up to him and shaking his hand warmly; “so very kind of you to come to my poor little tea-party.”\n\n“Uncommonly pleasant, I call it,” said his lordship. “I like this sort of thing—no trouble, you know.”\n\n“No; that is the charm of it: isn’t it? no trouble, or fuss, or parade. That’s what I always say. According to my ideas, society consists in giving people facility for an interchange of thoughts—what we call conversation.”\n\n“Aw, yes, exactly.”\n\n“Not in eating and drinking together—eh, Lord Dumbello? And yet the practice of our lives would seem to show that the indulgence of those animal propensities can alone suffice to bring people together. The world in this has surely made a great mistake.”\n\n“I like a good dinner all the same,” said Lord Dumbello.\n\n“Oh, yes, of course—of course. I am by no means one of those who would pretend to preach that our tastes have not been given to us for our enjoyment. Why should things be nice if we are not to like them?”\n\n“A man who can really give a good dinner has learned a great deal,” said Lord Dumbello, with unusual animation.\n\n“An immense deal. It is quite an art in itself; and one which I, at any rate, by no means despise. But we cannot always be eating—can we?”\n\n“No,” said Lord Dumbello, “not always.” And he looked as though he lamented that his powers should be so circumscribed.\n\nAnd then Mrs. Proudie passed on to Mrs. Grantly. The two ladies were quite friendly in London; though down in their own neighbourhood they waged a war so internecine in its nature. But nevertheless Mrs. Proudie’s manner might have showed to a very close observer that she knew the difference between a bishop and an archdeacon. “I am so delighted to see you,” said she. “No, don’t mind moving; I won’t sit down just at present. But why didn’t the archdeacon come?”\n\n“It was quite impossible; it was indeed,” said Mrs. Grantly. “The archdeacon never has a moment in London that he can call his own.”\n\n“You don’t stay up very long, I believe.”\n\n“A good deal longer than we either of us like, I can assure you. London life is a perfect nuisance to me.”\n\n“But people in a certain position must go through with it, you know,” said Mrs. Proudie. “The bishop, for instance, must attend the House.”\n\n“Must he?” asked Mrs. Grantly, as though she were not at all well informed with reference to this branch of a bishop’s business. “I am very glad that archdeacons are under no such liability.”\n\n“Oh, no; there’s nothing of that sort,” said Mrs. Proudie, very seriously. “But how uncommonly well Miss Grantly is looking! I do hear that she has quite been admired.”\n\nThis phrase certainly was a little hard for the mother to bear. All the world had acknowledged, so Mrs. Grantly had taught herself to believe, that Griselda was undoubtedly the beauty of the season. Marquises and lords were already contending for her smiles, and paragraphs had been written in newspapers as to her profile. It was too hard to be told, after that, that her daughter had been “quite admired.” Such a phrase might suit a pretty little red-cheeked milkmaid of a girl.\n\n“She cannot, of course, come near your girls in that respect,” said Mrs. Grantly, very quietly. Now the Miss Proudies had not elicited from the fashionable world any very loud encomiums on their beauty. Their mother felt the taunt in its fullest force, but she would not essay to do battle on the present arena. She jotted down the item in her mind, and kept it over for Barchester and the chapter. Such debts as those she usually paid on some day, if the means of doing so were at all within her power.\n\n“But there is Miss Dunstable, I declare,” she said, seeing that that lady had entered the room; and away went Mrs. Proudie to welcome her distinguished guest.\n\n“And so this is a conversazione, is it?” said that lady, speaking, as usual, not in a suppressed voice. “Well, I declare, it’s very nice. It means conversation, don’t it, Mrs. Proudie?”\n\n“Ha, ha, ha! Miss Dunstable, there is nobody like you, I declare.”\n\n“Well, but don’t it? and tea and cake? and then, when we’re tired of talking, we go away,—isn’t that it?”\n\n“But you must not be tired for these three hours yet.”\n\n“Oh, I’m never tired of talking; all the world knows that. How do, bishop? A very nice sort of thing this conversazione, isn’t it now?”\n\nThe bishop rubbed his hands together and smiled, and said that he thought it was rather nice.\n\n“Mrs. Proudie is so fortunate in all her little arrangements,” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n“Yes, yes,” said the bishop. “I think she is happy in these matters. I do flatter myself that she is so. Of course, Miss Dunstable, you are accustomed to things on a much grander scale.”\n\n“I! Lord bless you, no! Nobody hates grandeur so much as I do. Of course I must do as I am told. I must live in a big House, and have three footmen six feet high. I must have a coachman with a top-heavy wig, and horses so big that they frighten me. If I did not, I should be made out a lunatic and declared unable to manage my own affairs. But as for grandeur, I hate it. I certainly think that I shall have some of these conversaziones. I wonder whether Mrs. Proudie will come and put me up to a wrinkle or two.”\n\nThe bishop again rubbed his hands, and said that he was sure she would. He never felt quite at his ease with Miss Dunstable, as he rarely could ascertain whether or no she was earnest in what she was saying. So he trotted off, muttering some excuse as he went, and Miss Dunstable chuckled with an inward chuckle at his too evident bewilderment. Miss Dunstable was by nature kind, generous, and open-hearted; but she was living now very much with people on whom kindness, generosity, and open-heartedness were thrown away. She was clever also, and could be sarcastic; and she found that those qualities told better in the world around her than generosity and an open heart. And so she went on from month to month, and year to year, not progressing in a good spirit as she might have done, but still carrying within her bosom a warm affection for those she could really love. And she knew that she was hardly living as she should live,—that the wealth which she affected to despise was eating into the soundness of her character, not by its splendour, but by the style of life which it had seemed to produce as a necessity. She knew that she was gradually becoming irreverent, scornful, and prone to ridicule; but yet, knowing this and hating it, she hardly knew how to break from it.\n\nShe had seen so much of the blacker side of human nature that blackness no longer startled her as it should do. She had been the prize at which so many ruined spendthrifts had aimed; so many pirates had endeavoured to run her down while sailing in the open waters of life, that she had ceased to regard such attempts on her money-bags as unmanly or over-covetous. She was content to fight her own battle with her own weapons, feeling secure in her own strength of purpose and strength of wit.\n\nSome few friends she had whom she really loved,—among whom her inner self could come out and speak boldly what it had to say with its own true voice. And the woman who thus so spoke was very different from that Miss Dunstable whom Mrs. Proudie courted, and the Duke of Omnium fêted, and Mrs. Harold Smith claimed as her bosom friend. If only she could find among such one special companion on whom her heart might rest, who would help her to bear the heavy burdens of her world! But where was she to find such a friend?—she with her keen wit, her untold money, and loud laughing voice. Everything about her was calculated to attract those whom she could not value, and to scare from her the sort of friend to whom she would fain have linked her lot.\n\nAnd then she met Mrs. Harold Smith, who had taken Mrs. Proudie’s noble suite of rooms in her tour for the evening, and was devoting to them a period of twenty minutes. “And so I may congratulate you,” Miss Dunstable said eagerly to her friend.\n\n“No, in mercy’s name do no such thing, or you may too probably have to uncongratulate me again; and that will be so unpleasant.”\n\n“But they told me that Lord Brock had sent for him yesterday.” Now at this period Lord Brock was Prime Minister.\n\n“So he did, and Harold was with him backwards and forwards all the day. But he can’t shut his eyes and open his mouth, and see what God will send him, as a wise and prudent man should do. He is always for bargaining, and no Prime Minister likes that.”\n\n“I would not be in his shoes if, after all, he has to come home and say that the bargain is off.”\n\n“Ha, ha, ha! Well, I should not take it very quietly. But what can we poor women do, you know? When it is settled, my dear, I’ll send you a line at once.” And then Mrs. Harold Smith finished her course round the rooms, and regained her carriage within the twenty minutes.\n\n“Beautiful profile, has she not?” said Miss Dunstable, somewhat later in the evening, to Mrs. Proudie. Of course, the profile spoken of belonged to Miss Grantly.\n\n“Yes, it is beautiful, certainly,” said Mrs. Proudie. “The pity is that it means nothing.”\n\n“The gentlemen seem to think that it means a good deal.”\n\n“I am not sure of that. She has no conversation, you see; not a word. She has been sitting there with Lord Dumbello at her elbow for the last hour, and yet she has hardly opened her mouth three times.”\n\n“But, my dear Mrs. Proudie, who on earth could talk to Lord Dumbello?”\n\nMrs. Proudie thought that her own daughter Olivia would undoubtedly be able to do so, if only she could get the opportunity. But, then, Olivia had so much conversation.\n\nAnd while the two ladies were yet looking at the youthful pair, Lord Dumbello did speak again. “I think I have had enough of this now,” said he, addressing himself to Griselda.\n\n“I suppose you have other engagements,” said she.\n\n“Oh, yes; and I believe I shall go to Lady Clantelbrocks.” And then he took his departure. No other word was spoken that evening between him and Miss Grantly beyond those given in this chronicle, and yet the world declared that he and that young lady had passed the evening in so close a flirtation as to make the matter more than ordinarily particular; and Mrs. Grantly, as she was driven home to her lodgings, began to have doubts in her mind whether it would be wise to discountenance so great an alliance as that which the head of the great Hartletop family now seemed so desirous to establish. The prudent mother had not yet spoken a word to her daughter on these subjects, but it might soon become necessary to do so. It was all very well for Lady Lufton to hurry up to town, but of what service would that be, if Lord Lufton were not to be found in Bruton Street?\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII. THE NEW MINISTER’S PATRONAGE.\n\nAt that time, just as Lady Lufton was about to leave Framley for London, Mark Robarts received a pressing letter, inviting him also to go up to the metropolis for a day or two—not for pleasure, but on business. The letter was from his indefatigable friend Sowerby.\n\n“My dear Robarts,” the letter ran:—\n\nI have just heard that poor little Burslem, the Barsetshire prebendary, is dead. We must all die some day, you know,—as you have told your parishioners from the Framley pulpit more than once, no doubt. The stall must be filled up, and why should not you have it as well as another? It is six hundred a year and a house. Little Burslem had nine, but the good old times are gone. Whether the house is letable or not under the present ecclesiastical régime, I do not know. It used to be so, for I remember Mrs. Wiggins, the tallow-chandler’s widow, living in old Stanhope’s house.\n\nHarold Smith has just joined the Government as Lord Petty Bag, and could, I think, at the present moment get this for asking. He cannot well refuse me, and, if you will say the word, I will speak to him. You had better come up yourself; but say the word “Yes,” or “No,” by the wires.\n\nIf you say “Yes,” as of course you will, do not fail to come up. You will find me at the “Travellers,” or at the House. The stall will just suit you,—will give you no trouble, improve your position, and give some little assistance towards bed and board, and rack and manger.\n\nYours ever faithfully,\n\nSingularly enough, I hear that your brother is private secretary to the new Lord Petty Bag. I am told that his chief duty will consist in desiring the servants to call my sister’s carriage. I have only seen Harold once since he accepted office; but my Lady Petty Bag says that he has certainly grown an inch since that occurrence.\n\nThis was certainly very good-natured on the part of Mr. Sowerby, and showed that he had a feeling within his bosom that he owed something to his friend the parson for the injury he had done him. And such was in truth the case. A more reckless being than the member for West Barsetshire could not exist. He was reckless for himself, and reckless for all others with whom he might be concerned. He could ruin his friends with as little remorse as he had ruined himself. All was fair game that came in the way of his net. But, nevertheless, he was good-natured, and willing to move heaven and earth to do a friend a good turn, if it came in his way to do so.\n\nHe did really love Mark Robarts as much as it was given him to love any among his acquaintance. He knew that he had already done him an almost irreparable injury, and might very probably injure him still deeper before he had done with him. That he would undoubtedly do so, if it came in his way, was very certain. But then, if it also came in his way to repay his friend by any side blow, he would also undoubtedly do that. Such an occasion had now come, and he had desired his sister to give the new Lord Petty Bag no rest till he should have promised to use all his influence in getting the vacant prebend for Mark Robarts.\n\nThis letter of Sowerby’s Mark immediately showed to his wife. How lucky, thought he to himself, that not a word was said in it about those accursed money transactions! Had he understood Sowerby better he would have known that that gentleman never said anything about money transactions until it became absolutely necessary. “I know you don’t like Mr. Sowerby,” he said; “but you must own that this is very good-natured.”\n\n“It is the character I hear of him that I don’t like,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“But what shall I do now, Fanny? As he says, why should not I have the stall as well as another?”\n\n“I suppose it would not interfere with your parish?” she asked.\n\n“Not in the least, at the distance at which we are. I did think of giving up old Jones; but if I take this, of course I must keep a curate.”\n\nHis wife could not find it in her heart to dissuade him from accepting promotion when it came in his way—what vicar’s wife would have so persuaded her husband? But yet she did not altogether like it. She feared that Greek from Chaldicotes, even when he came with the present of a prebendal stall in his hands. And then what would Lady Lufton say?\n\n“And do you think that you must go up to London, Mark?”\n\n“Oh, certainly; that is, if I intend to accept Harold Smith’s kind offices in the matter.”\n\n“I suppose it will be better to accept them,” said Fanny, feeling perhaps that it would be useless in her to hope that they should not be accepted.\n\n“Prebendal stalls, Fanny, don’t generally go begging long among parish clergymen. How could I reconcile it to the duty I owe to my children to refuse such an increase to my income?” And so it was settled that he should at once drive to Silverbridge and send off a message by telegraph, and that he should himself proceed to London on the following day. “But you must see Lady Lufton first, of course,” said Fanny, as soon as all this was settled.\n\nMark would have avoided this if he could have decently done so, but he felt that it would be impolitic, as well as indecent. And why should he be afraid to tell Lady Lufton that he hoped to receive this piece of promotion from the present government? There was nothing disgraceful in a clergyman becoming a prebendary of Barchester. Lady Lufton herself had always been very civil to the prebendaries, and especially to little Dr. Burslem, the meagre little man who had just now paid the debt of nature. She had always been very fond of the chapter, and her original dislike to Bishop Proudie had been chiefly founded on his interference with the cathedral clergy,—on his interference, or on that of his wife or chaplain. Considering these things Mark Robarts tried to make himself believe that Lady Lufton would be delighted at his good fortune. But yet he did not believe it. She at any rate would revolt from the gift of the Greek of Chaldicotes.\n\n“Oh, indeed,” she said, when the vicar had with some difficulty explained to her all the circumstances of the case. “Well, I congratulate you, Mr. Robarts, on your powerful new patron.”\n\n“You will probably feel with me, Lady Lufton, that the benefice is one which I can hold without any detriment to me in my position here at Framley,” said he, prudently resolving to let the slur upon his friends pass by unheeded.\n\n“Well, I hope so. Of course, you are a very young man, Mr. Robarts, and these things have generally been given to clergymen more advanced in life.”\n\n“But you do not mean to say that you think I ought to refuse it?”\n\n“What my advice to you might be if you really came to me for advice, I am hardly prepared to say at so very short a notice. You seem to have made up your mind, and therefore I need not consider it. As it is, I wish you joy, and hope that it may turn out to your advantage in every way.”\n\n“You understand, Lady Lufton, that I have by no means got it as yet.”\n\n“Oh, I thought it had been offered to you: I thought you spoke of this new minister as having all that in his own hand.”\n\n“Oh, dear, no. What may be the amount of his influence in that respect I do not at all know. But my correspondent assures me—”\n\n“Mr. Sowerby, you mean. Why don’t you call him by his name?”\n\n“Mr. Sowerby assures me that Mr. Smith will ask for it; and thinks it most probable that his request will be successful.”\n\n“Oh, of course. Mr. Sowerby and Mr. Harold Smith together would no doubt be successful in anything. They are the sort of men who are successful nowadays. Well, Mr. Robarts, I wish you joy.” And she gave him her hand in token of her sincerity.\n\nMark took her hand, resolving to say nothing further on that occasion. That Lady Lufton was not now cordial with him, as she used to be, he was well aware; and sooner or later he was determined to have the matter out with her. He would ask her why she now so constantly met him with a taunt, and so seldom greeted him with that kind old affectionate smile which he knew and appreciated so well. That she was honest and true, he was quite sure. If he asked her the question plainly, she would answer him openly. And if he could induce her to say that she would return to her old ways, return to them she would in a hearty manner. But he could not do this just at present. It was but a day or two since Mr. Crawley had been with him; and was it not probable that Mr. Crawley had been sent thither by Lady Lufton? His own hands were not clean enough for a remonstrance at the present moment. He would cleanse them, and then he would remonstrate.\n\n“Would you like to live part of the year in Barchester?” he said to his wife and sister that evening.\n\n“I think that two houses are only a trouble,” said his wife. “And we have been very happy here.”\n\n“I have always liked a cathedral town,” said Lucy; “and I am particularly fond of the close.”\n\n“And Barchester-close is the closest of all closes,” said Mark. “There is not a single house within the gateways that does not belong to the chapter.”\n\n“But if we are to keep up two houses, the additional income will soon be wasted,” said Fanny prudently.\n\n“The thing would be, to let the house furnished every summer,” said Lucy.\n\n“But I must take my residence as the terms come,” said the vicar; “and I certainly should not like to be away from Framley all the winter; I should never see anything of Lufton.” And perhaps he thought of his hunting, and then thought again of that cleansing of his hands.\n\n“I should not a bit mind being away during the winter,” said Lucy, thinking of what the last winter had done for her.\n\n“But where on earth should we find money to furnish one of those large, old-fashioned houses? Pray, Mark, do not do anything rash.” And the wife laid her hand affectionately on her husband’s arm. In this manner the question of the prebend was discussed between them on the evening before he started for London.\n\nSuccess had at last crowned the earnest effort with which Harold Smith had carried on the political battle of his life for the last ten years. The late Lord Petty Bag had resigned in disgust, having been unable to digest the Prime Minister’s ideas on Indian Reform, and Mr. Harold Smith, after sundry hitches in the business, was installed in his place. It was said that Harold Smith was not exactly the man whom the Premier would himself have chosen for that high office; but the Premier’s hands were a good deal tied by circumstances. The last great appointment he had made had been terribly unpopular,—so much so as to subject him, popular as he undoubtedly was himself, to a screech from the whole nation. The Jupiter, with withering scorn, had asked whether vice of every kind was to be considered, in these days of Queen Victoria, as a passport to the cabinet. Adverse members of both Houses had arrayed themselves in a pure panoply of morality, and thundered forth their sarcasms with the indignant virtue and keen discontent of political Juvenals; and even his own friends had held up their hands in dismay. Under these circumstances he had thought himself obliged in the present instance to select a man who would not be especially objectionable to any party. Now Harold Smith lived with his wife, and his circumstances were not more than ordinarily embarrassed. He kept no race-horses; and, as Lord Brock now heard for the first time, gave lectures in provincial towns on popular subjects. He had a seat which was tolerably secure, and could talk to the House by the yard if required to do so. Moreover, Lord Brock had a great idea that the whole machinery of his own ministry would break to pieces very speedily. His own reputation was not bad, but it was insufficient for himself and that lately selected friend of his. Under all these circumstances combined, he chose Harold Smith to fill the vacant office of Lord Petty Bag.\n\nAnd very proud the Lord Petty Bag was. For the last three or four months, he and Mr. Supplehouse had been agreeing to consign the ministry to speedy perdition. “This sort of dictatorship will never do,” Harold Smith had himself said, justifying that future vote of his as to want of confidence in the Queen’s government. And Mr. Supplehouse in this matter had fully agreed with him. He was a Juno whose form that wicked old Paris had utterly despised, and he, too, had quite made up his mind as to the lobby in which he would be found when that day of vengeance should arrive. But now things were much altered in Harold Smith’s views. The Premier had shown his wisdom in seeking for new strength where strength ought to be sought, and introducing new blood into the body of his ministry. The people would now feel fresh confidence, and probably the House also. As to Mr. Supplehouse—he would use all his influence on Supplehouse. But, after all, Mr. Supplehouse was not everything.\n\nOn the morning after our vicar’s arrival in London he attended at the Petty Bag office. It was situated in the close neighbourhood of Downing Street and the higher governmental gods; and though the building itself was not much, seeing that it was shored up on one side, that it bulged out in the front, was foul with smoke, dingy with dirt, and was devoid of any single architectural grace or modern scientific improvement, nevertheless its position gave it a status in the world which made the clerks in the Lord Petty Bag’s office quite respectable in their walk in life. Mark had seen his friend Sowerby on the previous evening, and had then made an appointment with him for the following morning at the new minister’s office. And now he was there a little before his time, in order that he might have a few moments’ chat with his brother.\n\nWhen Mark found himself in the private secretary’s room he was quite astonished to see the change in his brother’s appearance which the change in his official rank had produced. Jack Robarts had been a well-built, straight-legged, lissome young fellow, pleasant to the eye because of his natural advantages, but rather given to a harum-skarum style of gait, and occasionally careless, not to say slovenly, in his dress. But now he was the very pink of perfection. His jaunty frock-coat fitted him to perfection; not a hair of his head was out of place; his waistcoat and trousers were glossy and new, and his umbrella, which stood in the umbrella-stand in the corner, was tight, and neat, and small, and natty.\n\n“Well, John, you’ve become quite a great man,” said his brother.\n\n“I don’t know much about that,” said John; “but I find that I have an enormous deal of fagging to go through.”\n\n“Do you mean work? I thought you had about the easiest berth in the whole Civil Service.”\n\n“Ah! that’s just the mistake that people make. Because we don’t cover whole reams of foolscap paper at the rate of fifteen lines to a page, and five words to a line, people think that we private secretaries have got nothing to do. Look here,” and he tossed over scornfully a dozen or so of little notes. “I tell you what, Mark; it is no easy matter to manage the patronage of a cabinet minister. Now I am bound to write to every one of these fellows a letter that will please him; and yet I shall refuse to every one of them the request which he asks.”\n\n“That must be difficult.”\n\n“Difficult is no word for it. But, after all, it consists chiefly in the knack of the thing. One must have the wit ‘from such a sharp and waspish word as No to pluck the sting.’ I do it every day, and I really think that the people like it.”\n\n“Perhaps your refusals are better than other people’s acquiescences.”\n\n“I don’t mean that at all. We private secretaries have all to do the same thing. Now, would you believe it? I have used up three lifts of note-paper already in telling people that there is no vacancy for a lobby messenger in the Petty Bag office. Seven peeresses have asked for it for their favourite footmen. But there—there’s the Lord Petty Bag!”\n\nA bell rang and the private secretary, jumping up from his note-paper, tripped away quickly to the great man’s room.\n\n“He’ll see you at once,” said he, returning. “Buggins, show the Reverend Mr. Robarts to the Lord Petty Bag.”\n\nBuggins was the messenger for whose not vacant place all the peeresses were striving with so much animation. And then Mark, following Buggins for two steps, was ushered into the next room.\n\nIf a man be altered by becoming a private secretary, he is much more altered by being made a cabinet minister. Robarts, as he entered the room, could hardly believe that this was the same Harold Smith whom Mrs. Proudie bothered so cruelly in the lecture-room at Barchester. Then he was cross, and touchy, and uneasy, and insignificant. Now, as he stood smiling on the hearthrug of his official fireplace, it was quite pleasant to see the kind, patronizing smile which lighted up his features. He delighted to stand there, with his hands in his trousers’ pocket, the great man of the place, conscious of his lordship, and feeling himself every inch a minister. Sowerby had come with him, and was standing a little in the background, from which position he winked occasionally at the parson over the minister’s shoulder.\n\n“Ah, Robarts, delighted to see you. How odd, by-the-by, that your brother should be my private secretary!” Mark said that it was a singular coincidence.\n\n“A very smart young fellow, and, if he minds himself, he’ll do well.”\n\n“I’m quite sure he’ll do well,” said Mark.\n\n“Ah! well, yes; I think he will. And now, what can I do for you, Robarts?”\n\nHereupon Mr. Sowerby struck in, making it apparent by his explanation that Mr. Robarts himself by no means intended to ask for anything; but that, as his friends had thought that this stall at Barchester might be put into his hands with more fitness than in those of any other clergyman of the day, he was willing to accept the piece of preferment from a man whom he respected so much as he did the new Lord Petty Bag.\n\nThe minister did not quite like this, as it restricted him from much of his condescension, and robbed him of the incense of a petition which he had expected Mark Robarts would make to him. But, nevertheless, he was very gracious.\n\n“He could not take upon himself to declare,” he said, “what might be Lord Brock’s pleasure with reference to the preferment at Barchester which was vacant. He had certainly already spoken to his lordship on the subject, and had perhaps some reason to believe that his own wishes would be consulted. No distinct promise had been made, but he might perhaps go so far as to say that he expected such result. If so, it would give him the greatest pleasure in the world to congratulate Mr. Robarts on the possession of the stall—a stall which he was sure Mr. Robarts would fill with dignity, piety, and brotherly love.” And then, when he had finished, Mr. Sowerby gave a final wink, and said that he regarded the matter as settled.\n\n“No, not settled, Nathaniel,” said the cautious minister.\n\n“It’s the same thing,” rejoined Sowerby. “We all know what all that flummery means. Men in office, Mark, never do make a distinct promise,—not even to themselves of the leg of mutton which is roasting before their kitchen fires. It is so necessary in these days to be safe; is it not, Harold?”\n\n“Most expedient,” said Harold Smith, shaking his head wisely. “Well, Robarts, who is it now?” This he said to his private secretary, who came to notice the arrival of some bigwig. “Well, yes. I will say good morning, with your leave, for I am a little hurried. And remember, Mr. Robarts, I will do what I can for you; but you must distinctly understand that there is no promise.”\n\n“Oh, no promise at all,” said Sowerby—“of course not.” And then, as he sauntered up Whitehall towards Charing Cross, with Robarts on his arm, he again pressed upon him the sale of that invaluable hunter, who was eating his head off his shoulders in the stable at Chaldicotes.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX. MONEY DEALINGS.\n\nMr. Sowerby, in his resolution to obtain this good gift for the Vicar of Framley, did not depend quite alone on the influence of his near connection with the Lord Petty Bag. He felt the occasion to be one on which he might endeavour to move even higher powers than that, and therefore he had opened the matter to the duke—not by direct application, but through Mr. Fothergill. No man who understood matters ever thought of going direct to the duke in such an affair as that. If one wanted to speak about a woman or a horse or a picture the duke could, on occasions, be affable enough.\n\nBut through Mr. Fothergill the duke was approached. It was represented, with some cunning, that this buying over of the Framley clergyman from the Lufton side would be a praiseworthy spoiling of the Amalekites. The doing so would give the Omnium interest a hold even in the cathedral close. And then it was known to all men that Mr. Robarts had considerable influence over Lord Lufton himself. So guided, the Duke of Omnium did say two words to the Prime Minister, and two words from the duke went a great way, even with Lord Brock. The upshot of all this was, that Mark Robarts did get the stall; but he did not hear the tidings of his success till some days after his return to Framley.\n\nMr. Sowerby did not forget to tell him of the great effort—the unusual effort, as he of Chaldicotes called it—which the duke had made on the subject. “I don’t know when he has done such a thing before,” said Sowerby; “and you may be quite sure of this, he would not have done it now, had you not gone to Gatherum Castle when he asked you: indeed, Fothergill would have known that it was vain to attempt it. And I’ll tell you what, Mark—it does not do for me to make little of my own nest, but I truly believe the duke’s word will be more efficacious than the Lord Petty Bag’s solemn adjuration.”\n\nMark, of course, expressed his gratitude in proper terms, and did buy the horse for a hundred and thirty pounds. “He’s as well worth it,” said Sowerby, “as any animal that ever stood on four legs; and my only reason for pressing him on you is, that when Tozer’s day does come round, I know you will have to stand to us to something about that tune.” It did not occur to Mark to ask him why the horse should not be sold to some one else, and the money forthcoming in the regular way. But this would not have suited Mr. Sowerby.\n\nMark knew that the beast was good, and as he walked to his lodgings was half proud of his new possession. But then, how would he justify it to his wife, or how introduce the animal into his stables without attempting any justification in the matter? And yet, looking to the absolute amount of his income, surely he might feel himself entitled to buy a new horse when it suited him. He wondered what Mr. Crawley would say when he heard of the new purchase. He had lately fallen into a state of much wondering as to what his friends and neighbours would say about him.\n\nHe had now been two days in town, and was to go down after breakfast on the following morning so that he might reach home by Friday afternoon. But on that evening, just as he was going to bed, he was surprised by Lord Lufton coming into the coffee-room at his hotel. He walked in with a hurried step, his face was red, and it was clear that he was very angry.\n\n“Robarts,” said he, walking up to his friend and taking the hand that was extended to him, “do you know anything about this man, Tozer?”\n\n“Tozer—what Tozer? I have heard Sowerby speak of such a man.”\n\n“Of course you have. If I do not mistake you have written to me about him yourself.”\n\n“Very probably. I remember Sowerby mentioning the man with reference to your affairs. But why do you ask me?”\n\n“This man has not only written to me, but has absolutely forced his way into my rooms when I was dressing for dinner; and absolutely had the impudence to tell me that if I did not honour some bill which he holds for eight hundred pounds he would proceed against me.”\n\n“But you settled all that matter with Sowerby?”\n\n“I did settle it at a very great cost to me. Sooner than have a fuss I paid him through the nose—like a fool that I was—everything that he claimed. This is an absolute swindle, and if it goes on I will expose it as such.”\n\nRobarts looked round the room, but luckily there was not a soul in it but themselves. “You do not mean to say that Sowerby is swindling you?” said the clergyman.\n\n“It looks very like it,” said Lord Lufton; “and I tell you fairly that I am not in a humour to endure any more of this sort of thing. Some years ago I made an ass of myself through that man’s fault. But four thousand pounds should have covered the whole of what I really lost. I have now paid more than three times that sum; and, by heavens! I will not pay more without exposing the whole affair.”\n\n“But, Lufton, I do not understand. What is this bill?—has it your name to it?”\n\n“Yes, it has: I’ll not deny my name, and if there be absolute need I will pay it; but if I do so, my lawyer shall sift it, and it shall go before a jury.”\n\n“But I thought all those bills were paid?”\n\n“I left it to Sowerby to get up the old bills when they were renewed, and now one of them that has in truth been already honoured is brought against me.”\n\nMark could not but think of the two documents which he himself had signed, and both of which were now undoubtedly in the hands of Tozer, or of some other gentleman of the same profession;—which both might be brought against him, the second as soon as he should have satisfied the first. And then he remembered that Sowerby had said something to him about an outstanding bill, for the filling up of which some trifle must be paid, and of this he reminded Lord Lufton.\n\n“And do you call eight hundred pounds a trifle? If so, I do not.”\n\n“They will probably make no such demand as that.”\n\n“But I tell you they do make such a demand, and have made it. The man whom I saw, and who told me that he was Tozer’s friend, but who was probably Tozer himself, positively swore to me that he would be obliged to take legal proceedings if the money were not forthcoming within a week or ten days. When I explained to him that it was an old bill that had been renewed, he declared that his friend had given full value for it.”\n\n“Sowerby said that you would probably have to pay ten pounds to redeem it. I should offer the man some such sum as that.”\n\n“My intention is to offer the man nothing, but to leave the affair in the hands of my lawyer with instructions to him to spare none;—neither myself nor any one else. I am not going to allow such a man as Sowerby to squeeze me like an orange.”\n\n“But, Lufton, you seem as though you were angry with me.”\n\n“No, I am not. But I think it is as well to caution you about this man; my transactions with him lately have chiefly been through you, and therefore—”\n\n“But they have only been so through his and your wish: because I have been anxious to oblige you both. I hope you don’t mean to say that I am concerned in these bills.”\n\n“I know that you are concerned in bills with him.”\n\n“Why, Lufton, am I to understand, then, that you are accusing me of having any interest in these transactions which you have called swindling?”\n\n“As far as I am concerned there has been swindling, and there is swindling going on now.”\n\n“But you do not answer my question. Do you bring any accusation against me? If so, I agree with you that you had better go to your lawyer.”\n\n“I think that is what I shall do.”\n\n“Very well. But upon the whole, I never heard of a more unreasonable man, or of one whose thoughts are more unjust than yours. Solely with the view of assisting you, and solely at your request, I spoke to Sowerby about these money transactions of yours. Then at his request, which originated out of your request, he using me as his ambassador to you, as you had used me as yours to him, I wrote and spoke to you. And now this is the upshot.”\n\n“I bring no accusation against you, Robarts; but I know you have dealings with this man. You have told me so yourself.”\n\n“Yes, at his request to accommodate him, I have put my name to a bill.”\n\n“Only to one?”\n\n“Only to one; and then to that same renewed, or not exactly to that same, but to one which stands for it. The first was for four hundred pounds; the last for five hundred.”\n\n“All which you will have to make good, and the world will of course tell you that you have paid that price for this stall at Barchester.”\n\nThis was terrible to be borne. He had heard much lately which had frightened and scared him, but nothing so terrible as this; nothing which so stunned him, or conveyed to his mind so frightful a reality of misery and ruin. He made no immediate answer, but standing on the hearthrug with his back to the fire, looked up the whole length of the room. Hitherto his eyes had been fixed upon Lord Lufton’s face, but now it seemed to him as though he had but little more to do with Lord Lufton. Lord Lufton and Lord Lufton’s mother were neither now to be counted among those who wished him well. Upon whom indeed could he now count, except that wife of his bosom upon whom he was bringing all this wretchedness?\n\nIn that moment of agony ideas ran quickly through his brain. He would immediately abandon this preferment at Barchester, of which it might be said with so much colour that he had bought it. He would go to Harold Smith, and say positively that he declined it. Then he would return home and tell his wife all that had occurred;—tell the whole also to Lady Lufton, if that might still be of any service. He would make arrangement for the payment of both those bills as they might be presented, asking no questions as to the justice of the claim, making no complaint to any one, not even to Sowerby. He would put half his income, if half were necessary, into the hands of Forrest the banker, till all was paid. He would sell every horse he had. He would part with his footman and groom, and at any rate strive like a man to get again a firm footing on good ground. Then, at that moment, he loathed with his whole soul the position in which he found himself placed, and his own folly which had placed him there. How could he reconcile it to his conscience that he was there in London with Sowerby and Harold Smith, petitioning for Church preferment to a man who should have been altogether powerless in such a matter, buying horses, and arranging about past due bills? He did not reconcile it to his conscience. Mr. Crawley had been right when he told him that he was a castaway.\n\nLord Lufton, whose anger during the whole interview had been extreme, and who had become more angry the more he talked, had now walked once or twice up and down the room; and as he so walked the idea did occur to him that he had been unjust. He had come there with the intention of exclaiming against Sowerby, and of inducing Robarts to convey to that gentleman, that if he, Lord Lufton, were made to undergo any further annoyance about this bill, the whole affair should be thrown into the lawyer’s hands; but instead of doing this, he had brought an accusation against Robarts. That Robarts had latterly become Sowerby’s friend rather than his own in all these horrid money dealings, had galled him; and now he had expressed himself in terms much stronger than he had intended to use.\n\n“As to you personally, Mark,” he said, coming back to the spot on which Robarts was standing, “I do not wish to say anything that shall annoy you.”\n\n“You have said quite enough, Lord Lufton.”\n\n“You cannot be surprised that I should be angry and indignant at the treatment I have received.”\n\n“You might, I think, have separated in your mind those who have wronged you, if there has been such wrong, from those who have only endeavoured to do your will and pleasure for you. That I, as a clergyman, have been very wrong in taking any part whatsoever in these matters, I am well aware. That as a man I have been outrageously foolish in lending my name to Mr. Sowerby, I also know well enough: it is perhaps as well that I should be told of this somewhat rudely; but I certainly did not expect the lesson to come from you.”\n\n“Well, there has been mischief enough. The question is, what we had better now both do?”\n\n“You have said what you mean to do. You will put the affair into the hands of your lawyer.”\n\n“Not with any object of exposing you.”\n\n“Exposing me, Lord Lufton! Why, one would think that I had had the handling of your money.”\n\n“You will misunderstand me. I think no such thing. But do you not know yourself that if legal steps be taken in this wretched affair, your arrangements with Sowerby will be brought to light?”\n\n“My arrangements with Sowerby will consist in paying or having to pay, on his account, a large sum of money, for which I have never had and shall never have any consideration whatever.”\n\n“And what will be said about this stall at Barchester?”\n\n“After the charge which you brought against me just now, I shall decline to accept it.”\n\nAt this moment three or four other gentlemen entered the room, and the conversation between our two friends was stopped. They still remained standing near the fire, but for a few minutes neither of them said anything. Robarts was waiting till Lord Lufton should go away, and Lord Lufton had not yet said that which he had come to say. At last he spoke again, almost in a whisper: “I think it will be best to ask Sowerby to come to my rooms to-morrow, and I think also that you should meet him there.”\n\n“I do not see any necessity for my presence,” said Robarts. “It seems probable that I shall suffer enough for meddling with your affairs, and I will do so no more.”\n\n“Of course I cannot make you come; but I think it will be only just to Sowerby, and it will be a favour to me.”\n\nRobarts again walked up and down the room for half-a-dozen times, trying to resolve what it would most become him to do in the present emergency. If his name were dragged before the courts,—if he should be shown up in the public papers as having been engaged in accommodation bills, that would certainly be ruinous to him. He had already learned from Lord Lufton’s innuendoes what he might expect to hear as the public version of his share in these transactions! And then his wife,—how would she bear such exposure?\n\n“I will meet Mr. Sowerby at your rooms to-morrow, on one condition,” he at last said.\n\n“And what is that?”\n\n“That I receive your positive assurance that I am not suspected by you of having had any pecuniary interest whatever in any money matters with Mr. Sowerby, either as concerns your affairs or those of anybody else.”\n\n“I have never suspected you of any such thing. But I have thought that you were compromised with him.”\n\n“And so I am—I am liable for these bills. But you ought to have known, and do know, that I have never received a shilling on account of such liability. I have endeavoured to oblige a man whom I regarded first as your friend, and then as my own; and this has been the result.”\n\nLord Lufton did at last give him the assurance that he desired, as they sat with their heads together over one of the coffee-room tables; and then Robarts promised that he would postpone his return to Framley till the Saturday, so that he might meet Sowerby at Lord Lufton’s chambers in the Albany on the following afternoon. As soon as this was arranged, Lord Lufton took his leave and went his way.\n\nAfter that poor Mark had a very uneasy night of it. It was clear enough that Lord Lufton had thought, if he did not still think, that the stall at Barchester was to be given as pecuniary recompense in return for certain money accommodation to be afforded by the nominee to the dispenser of this patronage. Nothing on earth could be worse than this. In the first place it would be simony; and then it would be simony beyond all description mean and simoniacal. The very thought of it filled Mark’s soul with horror and dismay. It might be that Lord Lufton’s suspicions were now at rest; but others would think the same thing, and their suspicions it would be impossible to allay; those others would consist of the outer world, which is always so eager to gloat over the detected vice of a clergyman.\n\nAnd then that wretched horse which he had purchased, and the purchase of which should have prohibited him from saying that nothing of value had accrued to him in these transactions with Mr. Sowerby! what was he to do about that? And then of late he had been spending, and had continued to spend, more money than he could well afford. This very journey of his up to London would be most imprudent, if it should become necessary for him to give up all hope of holding the prebend. As to that he had made up his mind; but then again he unmade it, as men always do in such troubles. That line of conduct which he had laid down for himself in the first moments of his indignation against Lord Lufton, by adopting which he would have to encounter poverty, and ridicule, and discomfort, the annihilation of his high hopes, and the ruin of his ambition—that, he said to himself over and over again, would now be the best for him. But it is so hard for us to give up our high hopes, and willingly encounter poverty, ridicule, and discomfort!\n\nOn the following morning, however, he boldly walked down to the Petty Bag office, determined to let Harold Smith know that he was no longer desirous of the Barchester stall. He found his brother there, still writing artistic notes to anxious peeresses on the subject of Buggins’ non-vacant situation; but the great man of the place, the Lord Petty Bag himself, was not there. He might probably look in when the House was beginning to sit, perhaps at four or a little after; but he certainly would not be at the office in the morning. The functions of the Lord Petty Bag he was no doubt performing elsewhere. Perhaps he had carried his work home with him—a practice which the world should know is not uncommon with civil servants of exceeding zeal.\n\nMark did think of opening his heart to his brother, and of leaving his message with him. But his courage failed him, or perhaps it might be more correct to say that his prudence prevented him. It would be better for him, he thought, to tell his wife before he told any one else. So he merely chatted with his brother for half an hour and then left him.\n\nThe day was very tedious till the hour came at which he was to attend at Lord Lufton’s rooms; but at last it did come, and just as the clock struck, he turned out of Piccadilly into the Albany. As he was going across the court before he entered the building, he was greeted by a voice just behind him.\n\n“As punctual as the big clock on Barchester tower,” said Mr. Sowerby. “See what it is to have a summons from a great man, Mr. Prebendary.”\n\nHe turned round and extended his hand mechanically to Mr. Sowerby, and as he looked at him he thought he had never before seen him so pleasant in appearance, so free from care, and so joyous in demeanour.\n\n“You have heard from Lord Lufton,” said Mark in a voice that was certainly very lugubrious.\n\n“Heard from him! oh, yes, of course I have heard from him. I’ll tell you what it is, Mark,” and he now spoke almost in a whisper as they walked together along the Albany passage, “Lufton is a child in money matters—a perfect child. The dearest, finest fellow in the world, you know; but a very baby in money matters.” And then they entered his lordship’s rooms.\n\nLord Lufton’s countenance also was lugubrious enough, but this did not in the least abash Sowerby, who walked quickly up to the young lord with his gait perfectly self-possessed and his face radiant with satisfaction.\n\n“Well, Lufton, how are you?” said he. “It seems that my worthy friend Tozer has been giving you some trouble?”\n\nThen Lord Lufton with a face by no means radiant with satisfaction again began the story of Tozer’s fraudulent demand upon him. Sowerby did not interrupt him, but listened patiently to the end;—quite patiently, although Lord Lufton, as he made himself more and more angry by the history of his own wrongs, did not hesitate to pronounce certain threats against Mr. Sowerby, as he had pronounced them before against Mark Robarts. He would not, he said, pay a shilling, except through his lawyer; and he would instruct his lawyer, that before he paid anything, the whole matter should be exposed openly in court. He did not care, he said, what might be the effect on himself or any one else. He was determined that the whole case should go to a jury.\n\n“To grand jury, and special jury, and common jury, and Old Jewry, if you like,” said Sowerby. “The truth is, Lufton, you lost some money, and as there was some delay in paying it, you have been harassed.”\n\n“I have paid more than I lost three times over,” said Lord Lufton, stamping his foot.\n\n“I will not go into that question now. It was settled, as I thought, some time ago by persons to whom you yourself referred it. But will you tell me this: Why on earth should Robarts be troubled in this matter? What has he done?”\n\n“Well, I don’t know. He arranged the matter with you.”\n\n“No such thing. He was kind enough to carry a message from you to me, and to convey back a return message from me to you. That has been his part in it.”\n\n“You don’t suppose that I want to implicate him: do you?”\n\n“I don’t think you want to implicate any one, but you are hot-headed and difficult to deal with, and very irrational into the bargain. And, what is worse, I must say you are a little suspicious. In all this matter I have harassed myself greatly to oblige you, and in return I have got more kicks than halfpence.”\n\n“Did not you give this bill to Tozer—the bill which he now holds?”\n\n“In the first place he does not hold it; and in the next place I did not give it to him. These things pass through scores of hands before they reach the man who makes the application for payment.”\n\n“And who came to me the other day?”\n\n“That, I take it, was Tom Tozer, a brother of our Tozer’s.”\n\n“Then he holds the bill, for I saw it with him.”\n\n“Wait a moment; that is very likely. I sent you word that you would have to pay for taking it up. Of course they don’t abandon those sort of things without some consideration.”\n\n“Ten pounds, you said,” observed Mark.\n\n“Ten or twenty; some such sum as that. But you were hardly so soft as to suppose that the man would ask for such a sum. Of course he would demand the full payment. There is the bill, Lord Lufton,” and Sowerby, producing a document, handed it across the table to his lordship. “I gave five-and-twenty pounds for it this morning.”\n\nLord Lufton took the paper and looked at it. “Yes,” said he, “that’s the bill. What am I to do with it now?”\n\n“Put it with the family archives,” said Sowerby,—“or behind the fire, just which you please.”\n\n“And is this the last of them? Can no other be brought up?”\n\n“You know better than I do what paper you may have put your hand to. I know of no other. At the last renewal that was the only outstanding bill of which I was aware.”\n\n“And you have paid five-and-twenty pounds for it?”\n\n“I have. Only that you have been in such a tantrum about it, and would have made such a noise this afternoon if I had not brought it, I might have had it for fifteen or twenty. In three or four days they would have taken fifteen.”\n\n“The odd ten pounds does not signify, and I’ll pay you the twenty-five, of course,” said Lord Lufton, who now began to feel a little ashamed of himself.\n\n“You may do as you please about that.”\n\n“Oh! it’s my affair, as a matter of course. Any amount of that kind I don’t mind,” and he sat down to fill in a cheque for the money.\n\n“Well, now, Lufton, let me say a few words to you,” said Sowerby, standing with his back against the fireplace, and playing with a small cane which he held in his hand. “For heaven’s sake try and be a little more charitable to those around you. When you become fidgety about anything, you indulge in language which the world won’t stand, though men who know you as well as Robarts and I may consent to put up with it. You have accused me, since I have been here, of all manner of iniquity—”\n\n“Now, Sowerby—”\n\n“My dear fellow, let me have my say out. You have accused me, I say, and I believe that you have accused him. But it has never occurred to you, I daresay, to accuse yourself.”\n\n“Indeed it has.”\n\n“Of course you have been wrong in having to do with such men as Tozer. I have also been very wrong. It wants no great moral authority to tell us that. Pattern gentlemen don’t have dealings with Tozer, and very much the better they are for not having them. But a man should have back enough to bear the weight which he himself puts on it. Keep away from Tozer, if you can, for the future; but if you do deal with him, for heaven’s sake keep your temper.”\n\n“That’s all very fine, Sowerby; but you know as well as I do—”\n\n“I know this,” said the devil, quoting Scripture, as he folded up the check for twenty-five pounds, and put it in his pocket, “that when a man sows tares, he won’t reap wheat, and it’s no use to expect it. I am tough in these matters, and can bear a great deal—that is, if I be not pushed too far,” and he looked full into Lord Lufton’s face as he spoke; “but I think you have been very hard upon Robarts.”\n\n“Never mind me, Sowerby; Lord Lufton and I are very old friends.”\n\n“And may therefore take a liberty with each other. Very well. And now I’ve done my sermon. My dear dignitary, allow me to congratulate you. I hear from Fothergill that that little affair of yours has been definitely settled.”\n\nMark’s face again became clouded. “I rather think,” said he, “that I shall decline the presentation.”\n\n“Decline it!” said Sowerby, who, having used his utmost efforts to obtain it, would have been more absolutely offended by such vacillation on the vicar’s part than by any personal abuse which either he or Lord Lufton could heap upon him.\n\n“I think I shall,” said Mark.\n\n“And why?”\n\nMark looked up at Lord Lufton, and then remained silent for a moment.\n\n“There can be no occasion for such a sacrifice under the present circumstances,” said his lordship.\n\n“And under what circumstances could there be occasion for it?” asked Sowerby. “The Duke of Omnium has used some little influence to get the place for you as a parish clergyman belonging to his county, and I should think it monstrous if you were now to reject it.”\n\nAnd then Robarts openly stated the whole of his reasons, explaining exactly what Lord Lufton had said with reference to the bill transactions, and to the allegation which would be made as to the stall having been given in payment for the accommodation.\n\n“Upon my word that’s too bad,” said Sowerby.\n\n“Now, Sowerby, I won’t be lectured,” said Lord Lufton.\n\n“I have done my lecture,” said he, aware, perhaps, that it would not do for him to push his friend too far, “and I shall not give a second. But, Robarts, let me tell you this: as far as I know, Harold Smith has had little or nothing to do with the appointment. The duke has told the Prime Minister that he was very anxious that a parish clergyman from the county should go into the chapter, and then, at Lord Brock’s request, he named you. If under those circumstances you talk of giving it up, I shall believe you to be insane. As for the bill which you accepted for me, you need have no uneasiness about it. The money will be ready; but of course, when that time comes, you will let me have the hundred and thirty for—”\n\nAnd then Mr. Sowerby took his leave, having certainly made himself master of the occasion. If a man of fifty have his wits about him, and be not too prosy, he can generally make himself master of the occasion, when his companions are under thirty.\n\nRobarts did not stay at the Albany long after him, but took his leave, having received some assurances of Lord Lufton’s regret for what had passed and many promises of his friendship for the future. Indeed Lord Lufton was a little ashamed of himself. “And as for the prebend, after what has passed, of course you must accept it.” Nevertheless his lordship had not omitted to notice Mr. Sowerby’s hint about the horse and the hundred and thirty pounds.\n\nRobarts, as he walked back to his hotel, thought that he certainly would accept the Barchester promotion, and was very glad that he had said nothing on the subject to his brother. On the whole his spirits were much raised. That assurance of Sowerby’s about the bill was very comforting to him; and strange to say, he absolutely believed it. In truth Sowerby had been so completely the winning horse at the late meeting, that both Lord Lufton and Robarts were inclined to believe almost anything he said;—which was not always the case with either of them.\n\n# CHAPTER XX. HAROLD SMITH IN THE CABINET.\n\nFor a few days the whole Harold Smith party held their heads very high. It was not only that their man had been made a cabinet minister; but a rumour had got abroad that Lord Brock, in selecting him, had amazingly strengthened his party, and done much to cure the wounds which his own arrogance and lack of judgment had inflicted on the body politic of his government. So said the Harold-Smithians, much elated. And when we consider what Harold had himself achieved, we need not be surprised that he himself was somewhat elated also.\n\nIt must be a proud day for any man when he first walks into a cabinet. But when a humble-minded man thinks of such a phase of life, his mind becomes lost in wondering what a cabinet is. Are they gods that attend there or men? Do they sit on chairs, or hang about on clouds? When they speak, is the music of the spheres audible in their Olympian mansion, making heaven drowsy with its harmony? In what way do they congregate? In what order do they address each other? Are the voices of all the deities free and equal? Is plodding Themis from the Home Department, or Ceres from the Colonies, heard with as rapt attention as powerful Pallas of the Foreign Office, the goddess that is never seen without her lance and helmet? Does our Whitehall Mars make eyes there at bright young Venus of the Privy Seal, disgusting that quaint tinkering Vulcan, who is blowing his bellows at our Exchequer, not altogether unsuccessfully? Old Saturn of the Woolsack sits there mute, we will say, a relic of other days, as seated in this divan. The hall in which he rules is now elsewhere. Is our Mercury of the Post Office ever ready to fly nimbly from globe to globe, as great Jove may order him, while Neptune, unaccustomed to the waves, offers needful assistance to the Apollo of the India Board? How Juno sits apart, glum and huffy, uncared for, Council President though she be, great in name, but despised among gods—that we can guess. If Bacchus and Cupid share Trade and the Board of Works between them, the fitness of things will have been as fully consulted as is usual. And modest Diana of the Petty Bag, latest summoned to these banquets of ambrosia,—does she not cling retiring near the doors, hardly able as yet to make her low voice heard among her brother deities? But Jove, great Jove—old Jove, the King of Olympus, hero among gods and men, how does he carry himself in these councils summoned by his voice? Does he lie there at his ease, with his purple cloak cut from the firmament around his shoulders? Is his thunderbolt ever at his hand to reduce a recreant god to order? Can he proclaim silence in that immortal hall? Is it not there, as elsewhere, in all places, and among all nations, that a king of gods and a king of men is and will be king, rules and will rule, over those who are smaller than himself?\n\nHarold Smith, when he was summoned to the august hall of divine councils, did feel himself to be a proud man; but we may perhaps conclude that at the first meeting or two he did not attempt to take a very leading part. Some of my readers may have sat at vestries, and will remember how mild, and for the most part, mute, is a new-comer at their board. He agrees generally, with abated enthusiasm; but should he differ, he apologizes for the liberty. But anon, when the voices of his colleagues have become habitual in his ears—when the strangeness of the room is gone, and the table before him is known and trusted—he throws off his awe and dismay, and electrifies his brotherhood by the vehemence of his declamation and the violence of his thumping. So let us suppose it will be with Harold Smith, perhaps in the second or third season of his cabinet practice. Alas! alas! that such pleasures should be so fleeting!\n\nAnd then, too, there came upon him a blow which somewhat modified his triumph—a cruel, dastard blow, from a hand which should have been friendly to him, from one to whom he had fondly looked to buoy him up in the great course that was before him. It had been said by his friends that in obtaining Harold Smith’s services the Prime Minister had infused new young healthy blood into his body. Harold himself had liked the phrase, and had seen at a glance how it might have been made to tell by some friendly Supplehouse or the like. But why should a Supplehouse out of Elysium be friendly to a Harold Smith within it? Men lapped in Elysium, steeped to the neck in bliss, must expect to see their friends fall off from them. Human nature cannot stand it. If I want to get anything from my old friend Jones, I like to see him shoved up into a high place. But if Jones, even in his high place, can do nothing for me, then his exaltation above my head is an insult and an injury. Who ever believes his own dear intimate companion to be fit for the highest promotion? Mr. Supplehouse had known Mr. Smith too closely to think much of his young blood.\n\nConsequently, there appeared an article in the Jupiter, which was by no means complimentary to the ministry in general. It harped a good deal on the young blood view of the question, and seemed to insinuate that Harold Smith was not much better than diluted water. “The Prime Minister,” the article said, “having lately recruited his impaired vigour by a new infusion of aristocratic influence of the highest moral tone, had again added to himself another tower of strength chosen from among the people. What might he not hope, now that he possessed the services of Lord Brittleback and Mr. Harold Smith! Renovated in a Medea’s caldron of such potency, all his effete limbs—and it must be acknowledged that some of them had become very effete—would come forth young and round and robust. A new energy would diffuse itself through every department; India would be saved and quieted; the ambition of France would be tamed; even-handed reform would remodel our courts of law and parliamentary elections; and Utopia would be realized. Such, it seems, is the result expected in the ministry from Mr. Harold Smith’s young blood!”\n\nThis was cruel enough, but even this was hardly so cruel as the words with which the article ended. By that time irony had been dropped, and the writer spoke out earnestly his opinion upon the matter. “We beg to assure Lord Brock,” said the article, “that such alliances as these will not save him from the speedy fall with which his arrogance and want of judgment threaten to overwhelm it. As regards himself we shall be sorry to hear of his resignation. He is in many respects the best statesman that we possess for the emergencies of the present period. But if he be so ill-judged as to rest on such men as Mr. Harold Smith and Lord Brittleback for his assistants in the work which is before him, he must not expect that the country will support him. Mr. Harold Smith is not made of the stuff from which cabinet ministers should be formed.”\n\nMr. Harold Smith, as he read this, seated at his breakfast-table, recognized, or said that he recognized, the hand of Mr. Supplehouse in every touch. That phrase about the effete limbs was Supplehouse all over, as was also the realization of Utopia. “When he wants to be witty, he always talks about Utopia,” said Mr. Harold Smith—to himself: for Mrs. Harold was not usually present in the flesh at these matutinal meals.\n\nAnd then he went down to his office, and saw in the glance of every man that he met an announcement that that article in the Jupiter had been read. His private secretary tittered in evident allusion to the article, and the way in which Buggins took his coat made it clear that it was well known in the messengers’ lobby. “He won’t have to fill up my vacancy when I go,” Buggins was saying to himself. And then in the course of the morning came the cabinet council, the second that he had attended, and he read in the countenance of every god and goddess there assembled that their chief was thought to have made another mistake. If Mr. Supplehouse could have been induced to write in another strain, then indeed that new blood might have been felt to have been efficacious.\n\nAll this was a great drawback to his happiness, but still it could not rob him of the fact of his position. Lord Brock could not ask him to resign because the Jupiter had written against him; nor was Lord Brock the man to desert a new colleague for such a reason. So Harold Smith girded his loins, and went about the duties of the Petty Bag with new zeal. “Upon my word, the Jupiter is right,” said young Robarts to himself, as he finished his fourth dozen of private notes explanatory of everything in and about the Petty Bag Office. Harold Smith required that his private secretary’s notes should be so terribly precise.\n\nBut nevertheless, in spite of his drawbacks, Harold Smith was happy in his new honours, and Mrs. Harold Smith enjoyed them also. She certainly, among her acquaintance, did quiz the new cabinet minister not a little, and it may be a question whether she was not as hard upon him as the writer in the Jupiter. She whispered a great deal to Miss Dunstable about new blood, and talked of going down to Westminster Bridge to see whether the Thames were really on fire. But though she laughed, she triumphed, and though she flattered herself that she bore her honours without any outward sign, the world knew that she was triumphing, and ridiculed her elation.\n\nAbout this time she also gave a party—not a pure-minded conversazione like Mrs. Proudie, but a downright wicked worldly dance, at which there were fiddles, ices, and champagne sufficient to run away with the first quarter’s salary accruing to Harold from the Petty Bag Office. To us this ball is chiefly memorable from the fact that Lady Lufton was among the guests. Immediately on her arrival in town she received cards from Mrs. H. Smith for herself and Griselda, and was about to send back a reply at once declining the honour. What had she to do at the house of Mr. Sowerby’s sister? But it so happened that at that moment her son was with her, and as he expressed a wish that she should go, she yielded. Had there been nothing in his tone of persuasion more than ordinary,—had it merely had reference to herself,—she would have smiled on him for his kind solicitude, have made out some occasion for kissing his forehead as she thanked him, and would still have declined. But he had reminded her both of himself and Griselda. “You might as well go, mother, for the sake of meeting me,” he said; “Mrs. Harold caught me the other day, and would not liberate me till I had given her a promise.”\n\n“That is an attraction certainly,” said Lady Lufton. “I do like going to a house when I know that you will be there.”\n\n“And now that Miss Grantly is with you—you owe it to her to do the best you can for her.”\n\n“I certainly do, Ludovic; and I have to thank you for reminding me of my duty so gallantly.” And so she said that she would go to Mrs. Harold Smith’s. Poor lady! She gave much more weight to those few words about Miss Grantly than they deserved. It rejoiced her heart to think that her son was anxious to meet Griselda—that he should perpetrate this little ruse in order to gain his wish. But he had spoken out of the mere emptiness of his mind, without thought of what he was saying, excepting that he wished to please his mother.\n\nBut nevertheless he went to Mrs. Harold Smith’s, and when there he did dance more than once with Griselda Grantly—to the manifest discomfiture of Lord Dumbello. He came in late, and at the moment Lord Dumbello was moving slowly up the room, with Griselda on his arm, while Lady Lufton was sitting near looking on with unhappy eyes. And then Griselda sat down, and Lord Dumbello stood mute at her elbow.\n\n“Ludovic,” whispered his mother, “Griselda is absolutely bored by that man, who follows her like a ghost. Do go and rescue her.”\n\nHe did go and rescue her, and afterwards danced with her for the best part of an hour consecutively. He knew that the world gave Lord Dumbello the credit of admiring the young lady, and was quite alive to the pleasure of filling his brother nobleman’s heart with jealousy and anger. Moreover, Griselda was in his eyes very beautiful, and had she been one whit more animated, or had his mother’s tactics been but a thought better concealed, Griselda might have been asked that night to share the vacant throne at Lufton, in spite of all that had been said and sworn in the drawing-room of Framley Parsonage.\n\nIt must be remembered that our gallant, gay Lothario had passed some considerable number of days with Miss Grantly in his mother’s house, and the danger of such contiguity must be remembered also. Lord Lufton was by no means a man capable of seeing beauty unmoved or of spending hours with a young lady without some approach to tenderness. Had there been no such approach, it is probable that Lady Lufton would not have pursued the matter. But, according to her ideas on such subjects, her son Ludovic had on some occasions shown quite sufficient partiality for Miss Grantly to justify her in her hopes, and to lead her to think that nothing but opportunity was wanted. Now, at this ball of Mrs. Smith’s, he did, for a while, seem to be taking advantage of such opportunity, and his mother’s heart was glad. If things should turn out well on this evening she would forgive Mrs. Harold Smith all her sins.\n\nAnd for a while it looked as though things would turn out well. Not that it must be supposed that Lord Lufton had come there with any intention of making love to Griselda, or that he ever had any fixed thought that he was doing so. Young men in such matters are so often without any fixed thoughts! They are such absolute moths. They amuse themselves with the light of the beautiful candle, fluttering about, on and off, in and out of the flame with dazzled eyes, till in a rash moment they rush in too near the wick, and then fall with singed wings and crippled legs, burnt up and reduced to tinder by the consuming fire of matrimony. Happy marriages, men say, are made in heaven, and I believe it. Most marriages are fairly happy, in spite of Sir Cresswell Cresswell; and yet how little care is taken on earth towards such a result!\n\n“I hope my mother is using you well?” said Lord Lufton to Griselda, as they were standing together in a doorway between the dances.\n\n“Oh, yes: she is very kind.”\n\n“You have been rash to trust yourself in the hands of so very staid and demure a person. And, indeed, you owe your presence here at Mrs. Harold Smith’s first cabinet ball altogether to me. I don’t know whether you are aware of that.”\n\n“Oh, yes: Lady Lufton told me.”\n\n“And are you grateful or otherwise? Have I done you an injury or a benefit? Which do you find best, sitting with a novel in the corner of a sofa in Bruton Street, or pretending to dance polkas here with Lord Dumbello?”\n\n“I don’t know what you mean. I haven’t stood up with Lord Dumbello all the evening. We were going to dance a quadrille, but we didn’t.”\n\n“Exactly; just what I say;—pretending to do it. Even that’s a good deal for Lord Dumbello; isn’t it?” And then Lord Lufton, not being a pretender himself, put his arm round her waist, and away they went up and down the room, and across and about, with an energy which showed that what Griselda lacked in her tongue she made up with her feet. Lord Dumbello, in the meantime, stood by, observant, thinking to himself that Lord Lufton was a glib-tongued, empty-headed ass, and reflecting that if his rival were to break the tendons of his leg in one of those rapid evolutions, or suddenly come by any other dreadful misfortune, such as the loss of all his property, absolute blindness, or chronic lumbago, it would only serve him right. And in that frame of mind he went to bed, in spite of the prayer which no doubt he said as to his forgiveness of other people’s trespasses.\n\nAnd then, when they were again standing, Lord Lufton, in the little intervals between his violent gasps for fresh breath, asked Griselda if she liked London. “Pretty well,” said Griselda, gasping also a little herself.\n\n“I am afraid—you were very dull—down at Framley.”\n\n“Oh, no;—I liked it—particularly.”\n\n“It was a great bore when you went—away, I know. There wasn’t a soul—about the house worth speaking to.” And they remained silent for a minute till their lungs had become quiescent.\n\n“Not a soul,” he continued—not of falsehood prepense, for he was not in fact thinking of what he was saying. It did not occur to him at the moment that he had truly found Griselda’s going a great relief, and that he had been able to do more in the way of conversation with Lucy Robarts in one hour than with Miss Grantly during a month of intercourse in the same house. But, nevertheless, we should not be hard upon him. All is fair in love and war; and if this was not love, it was the usual thing that stands as a counterpart for it.\n\n“Not a soul,” said Lord Lufton. “I was very nearly hanging myself in the park next morning;—only it rained.”\n\n“What nonsense! You had your mother to talk to.”\n\n“Oh, my mother,—yes; and you may tell me too, if you please, that Captain Culpepper was there. I do love my mother dearly; but do you think that she could make up for your absence?” And his voice was very tender, and so were his eyes.\n\n“And Miss Robarts; I thought you admired her very much?”\n\n“What, Lucy Robarts?” said Lord Lufton, feeling that Lucy’s name was more than he at present knew how to manage. Indeed that name destroyed all the life there was in that little flirtation. “I do like Lucy Robarts, certainly. She is very clever; but it so happened that I saw little or nothing of her after you were gone.”\n\nTo this Griselda made no answer, but drew herself up, and looked as cold as Diana when she froze Orion in the cave. Nor could she be got to give more than monosyllabic answers to the three or four succeeding attempts at conversation which Lord Lufton made. And then they danced again, but Griselda’s steps were by no means so lively as before.\n\nWhat took place between them on that occasion was very little more than what has been here related. There may have been an ice or a glass of lemonade into the bargain, and perhaps the faintest possible attempt at hand-pressing. But if so, it was all on one side. To such overtures as that Griselda Grantly was as cold as any Diana.\n\nBut little as all this was, it was sufficient to fill Lady Lufton’s mind and heart. No mother with six daughters was ever more anxious to get them off her hands, than Lady Lufton was to see her son married,—married, that is, to some girl of the right sort. And now it really did seem as though he were actually going to comply with her wishes. She had watched him during the whole evening, painfully endeavouring not to be observed in doing so. She had seen Lord Dumbello’s failure and wrath, and she had seen her son’s victory and pride. Could it be the case that he had already said something, which was still allowed to be indecisive only through Griselda’s coldness? Might it not be the case, that by some judicious aid on her part, that indecision might be turned into certainty, and that coldness into warmth? But then any such interference requires so delicate a touch,—as Lady Lufton was well aware.\n\n“Have you had a pleasant evening?” Lady Lufton said, when she and Griselda were seated together with their feet on the fender of her ladyship’s dressing-room. Lady Lufton had especially invited her guest into this, her most private sanctum, to which as a rule none had admittance but her daughter, and sometimes Fanny Robarts. But to what sanctum might not such a daughter-in-law as Griselda have admittance?\n\n“Oh, yes—very,” said Griselda.\n\n“It seemed to me that you bestowed most of your smiles upon Ludovic.” And Lady Lufton put on a look of good pleasure that such should have been the case.\n\n“Oh! I don’t know,” said Griselda: “I did dance with him two or three times.”\n\n“Not once too often to please me, my dear. I like to see Ludovic dancing with my friends.”\n\n“I am sure I am very much obliged to you, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“Not at all, my dear. I don’t know where he could get so nice a partner.” And then she paused a moment, not feeling how far she might go. In the meantime Griselda sat still, staring at the hot coals. “Indeed, I know that he admires you very much,” continued Lady Lufton.\n\n“Oh! no, I am sure he doesn’t,” said Griselda; and then there was another pause.\n\n“I can only say this,” said Lady Lufton, “that if he does do so—and I believe he does—it would give me very great pleasure. For you know, my dear, that I am very fond of you myself.”\n\n“Oh! thank you,” said Griselda, and stared at the coals more perseveringly than before.\n\n“He is a young man of a most excellent disposition—though he is my own son, I will say that—and if there should be anything between you and him—”\n\n“There isn’t, indeed, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“But if there ever should be, I should be delighted to think that Ludovic had made so good a choice.”\n\n“But there will never be anything of the sort, I’m sure, Lady Lufton. He is not thinking of such a thing in the least.”\n\n“Well, perhaps he may, some day. And now, good-night, my dear.”\n\n“Good-night, Lady Lufton.” And Griselda kissed her with the utmost composure, and betook herself to her own bedroom. Before she retired to sleep she looked carefully to her different articles of dress, discovering what amount of damage the evening’s wear and tear might have inflicted.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI. WHY PUCK, THE PONY, WAS BEATEN.\n\nMark Robarts returned home the day after the scene at the Albany, considerably relieved in spirit. He now felt that he might accept the stall without discredit to himself as a clergyman in doing so. Indeed, after what Mr. Sowerby had said, and after Lord Lufton’s assent to it, it would have been madness, he considered, to decline it. And then, too, Mr. Sowerby’s promise about the bills was very comfortable to him. After all, might it not be possible that he might get rid of all these troubles with no other drawback than that of having to pay £130 for a horse that was well worth the money?\n\nOn the day after his return he received proper authentic tidings of his presentation to the prebend. He was, in fact, already prebendary, or would be as soon as the dean and chapter had gone through the form of instituting him in his stall. The income was already his own; and the house also would be given up to him in a week’s time—a part of the arrangement with which he would most willingly have dispensed had it been at all possible to do so. His wife congratulated him nicely, with open affection, and apparent satisfaction at the arrangement. The enjoyment of one’s own happiness at such windfalls depends so much on the free and freely expressed enjoyment of others! Lady Lufton’s congratulations had nearly made him throw up the whole thing; but his wife’s smiles re-encouraged him; and Lucy’s warm and eager joy made him feel quite delighted with Mr. Sowerby and the Duke of Omnium. And then that splendid animal, Dandy, came home to the parsonage stables, much to the delight of the groom and gardener, and of the assistant stable boy who had been allowed to creep into the establishment, unawares as it were, since “master” had taken so keenly to hunting. But this satisfaction was not shared in the drawing-room. The horse was seen on his first journey round to the stable gate, and questions were immediately asked. It was a horse, Mark said, “which he had bought from Mr. Sowerby some little time since with the object of obliging him. He, Mark, intended to sell him again, as soon as he could do so judiciously.” This, as I have said above, was not satisfactory. Neither of the two ladies at Framley Parsonage knew much about horses, or of the manner in which one gentleman might think it proper to oblige another by purchasing the superfluities of his stable; but they did both feel that there were horses enough in the parsonage stable without Dandy, and that the purchasing of a hunter with the view of immediately selling him again, was, to say the least of it, an operation hardly congenial with the usual tastes and pursuits of a clergyman.\n\n“I hope you did not give very much money for him, Mark,” said Fanny.\n\n“Not more than I shall get again,” said Mark; and Fanny saw from the form of his countenance that she had better not pursue the subject any further at that moment.\n\n“I suppose I shall have to go into residence almost immediately,” said Mark, recurring to the more agreeable subject of the stall.\n\n“And shall we all have to go and live at Barchester at once?” asked Lucy.\n\n“The house will not be furnished, will it, Mark?” said his wife. “I don’t know how we shall get on.”\n\n“Don’t frighten yourselves. I shall take lodgings in Barchester.”\n\n“And we shall not see you all the time,” said Mrs. Robarts with dismay. But the prebendary explained that he would be backwards and forwards at Framley every week, and that in all probability he would only sleep at Barchester on the Saturdays and Sundays—and, perhaps, not always then.\n\n“It does not seem very hard work, that of a prebendary,” said Lucy.\n\n“But it is very dignified,” said Fanny. “Prebendaries are dignitaries of the Church—are they not, Mark?”\n\n“Decidedly,” said he; “and their wives also, by special canon law. The worst of it is that both of them are obliged to wear wigs.”\n\n“Shall you have a hat, Mark, with curly things at the side, and strings through to hold them up?” asked Lucy.\n\n“I fear that does not come within my perquisites.”\n\n“Nor a rosette? Then I shall never believe that you are a dignitary. Do you mean to say that you will wear a hat like a common parson—like Mr. Crawley, for instance?”\n\n“Well—I believe I may give a twist to the leaf; but I am by no means sure till I shall have consulted the dean in chapter.”\n\nAnd thus at the parsonage they talked over the good things that were coming to them, and endeavoured to forget the new horse, and the hunting boots that had been used so often during the last winter, and Lady Lufton’s altered countenance. It might be that the evils would vanish away, and the good things alone remain to them.\n\nIt was now the month of April, and the fields were beginning to look green, and the wind had got itself out of the east and was soft and genial, and the early spring flowers were showing their bright colours in the parsonage garden, and all things were sweet and pleasant. This was a period of the year that was usually dear to Mrs. Robarts. Her husband was always a better parson when the warm months came than he had been during the winter. The distant county friends whom she did not know and of whom she did not approve went away when the spring came, leaving their houses innocent and empty. The parish duty was better attended to, and perhaps domestic duties also. At such period he was a pattern parson and a pattern husband, atoning to his own conscience for past shortcomings by present zeal. And then, though she had never acknowledged it to herself, the absence of her dear friend Lady Lufton was perhaps in itself not disagreeable. Mrs. Robarts did love Lady Lufton heartily; but it must be acknowledged of her ladyship, that, with all her good qualities, she was inclined to be masterful. She liked to rule, and she made people feel that she liked it. Mrs. Robarts would never have confessed that she laboured under a sense of thraldom; but perhaps she was mouse enough to enjoy the temporary absence of her kind-hearted cat. When Lady Lufton was away Mrs. Robarts herself had more play in the parish.\n\nAnd Mark also was not unhappy, though he did not find it practicable immediately to turn Dandy into money. Indeed, just at this moment, when he was a good deal over at Barchester, going through those deep mysteries and rigid ecclesiastical examinations which are necessary before a clergyman can become one of a chapter, Dandy was rather a thorn in his side. Those wretched bills were to come due early in May, and before the end of April Sowerby wrote to him saying that he was doing his utmost to provide for the evil day; but that if the price of Dandy could be remitted to him at once, it would greatly facilitate his object. Nothing could be more different than Mr. Sowerby’s tone about money at different times. When he wanted to raise the wind, everything was so important; haste and superhuman efforts, and men running to and fro with blank acceptances in their hands, could alone stave off the crack of doom; but at other times, when retaliatory applications were made to him, he could prove with the easiest voice and most jaunty manner that everything was quite serene. Now, at this period, he was in that mood of superhuman efforts, and he called loudly for the hundred and thirty pounds for Dandy. After what had passed, Mark could not bring himself to say that he would pay nothing till the bills were safe; and therefore with the assistance of Mr. Forrest of the Bank, he did remit the price of Dandy to his friend Sowerby in London.\n\nAnd Lucy Robarts—we must now say a word of her. We have seen how, on that occasion, when the world was at her feet, she had sent her noble suitor away, not only dismissed, but so dismissed that he might be taught never again to offer to her the sweet incense of his vows. She had declared to him plainly that she did not love him and could not love him, and had thus thrown away not only riches and honour and high station, but more than that—much worse than that—she had flung away from her the lover to whose love her warm heart clung. That her love did cling to him, she knew even then, and owned more thoroughly as soon as he was gone. So much her pride had done for her, and that strong resolve that Lady Lufton should not scowl on her and tell her that she had entrapped her son.\n\nI know it will be said of Lord Lufton himself that, putting aside his peerage and broad acres, and handsome, sonsy face, he was not worth a girl’s care and love. That will be said because people think that heroes in books should be so much better than heroes got up for the world’s common wear and tear. I may as well confess that of absolute, true heroism there was only a moderate admixture in Lord Lufton’s composition; but what would the world come to if none but absolute true heroes were to be thought worthy of women’s love? What would the men do? and what—oh! what would become of the women? Lucy Robarts in her heart did not give her dismissed lover credit for much more heroism than did truly appertain to him;—did not, perhaps, give him full credit for a certain amount of heroism which did really appertain to him; but, nevertheless, she would have been very glad to take him could she have done so without wounding her pride.\n\nThat girls should not marry for money we are all agreed. A lady who can sell herself for a title or an estate, for an income or a set of family diamonds, treats herself as a farmer treats his sheep and oxen—makes hardly more of herself, of her own inner self, in which are comprised a mind and soul, than the poor wretch of her own sex who earns her bread in the lowest stage of degradation. But a title, and an estate, and an income, are matters which will weigh in the balance with all Eve’s daughters—as they do with all Adam’s sons. Pride of place, and the power of living well in front of the world’s eye, are dear to us all;—are, doubtless, intended to be dear. Only in acknowledging so much, let us remember that there are prices at which these good things may be too costly. Therefore, being desirous, too, of telling the truth in this matter, I must confess that Lucy did speculate with some regret on what it would have been to be Lady Lufton. To have been the wife of such a man, the owner of such a heart, the mistress of such a destiny—what more or what better could the world have done for her? And now she had thrown all that aside because she would not endure that Lady Lufton should call her a scheming, artful girl! Actuated by that fear she had repulsed him with a falsehood, though the matter was one on which it was so terribly expedient that she should tell the truth.\n\nAnd yet she was cheerful with her brother and sister-in-law. It was when she was quite alone, at night in her own room, or in her solitary walks, that a single silent tear would gather in the corner of her eye and gradually moisten her eyelids. “She never told her love,” nor did she allow concealment to “feed on her damask cheek.” In all her employments, in her ways about the house, and her accustomed quiet mirth, she was the same as ever. In this she showed the peculiar strength which God had given her. But not the less did she in truth mourn for her lost love and spoiled ambition.\n\n“We are going to drive over to Hogglestock this morning,” Fanny said one day at breakfast. “I suppose, Mark, you won’t go with us?”\n\n“Well, no; I think not. The pony-carriage is wretched for three.”\n\n“Oh, as for that, I should have thought the new horse might have been able to carry you as far as that. I heard you say you wanted to see Mr. Crawley.”\n\n“So I do; and the new horse, as you call him, shall carry me there to-morrow. Will you say that I’ll be over about twelve o’clock?”\n\n“You had better say earlier, as he is always out about the parish.”\n\n“Very well, say eleven. It is parish business about which I am going, so it need not irk his conscience to stay in for me.”\n\n“Well, Lucy, we must drive ourselves, that’s all. You shall be charioteer going, and then we’ll change coming back.” To all which Lucy agreed, and as soon as their work in the school was over they started.\n\nNot a word had been spoken between them about Lord Lufton since that evening, now more than a month ago, on which they had been walking together in the garden. Lucy had so demeaned herself on that occasion as to make her sister-in-law quite sure that there had been no love passages up to that time; and nothing had since occurred which had created any suspicion in Mrs. Robarts’ mind. She had seen at once that all the close intimacy between them was over, and thought that everything was as it should be.\n\n“Do you know, I have an idea,” she said in the pony-carriage that day, “that Lord Lufton will marry Griselda Grantly.” Lucy could not refrain from giving a little check at the reins which she was holding, and she felt that the blood rushed quickly to her heart. But she did not betray herself. “Perhaps he may,” she said, and then gave the pony a little touch with her whip.\n\n“Oh, Lucy, I won’t have Puck beaten. He was going very nicely.”\n\n“I beg Puck’s pardon. But you see when one is trusted with a whip one feels such a longing to use it.”\n\n“Oh, but you should keep it still. I feel almost certain that Lady Lufton would like such a match.”\n\n“I daresay she might. Miss Grantly will have a large fortune, I believe.”\n\n“It is not that altogether: but she is the sort of young lady that Lady Lufton likes. She is ladylike and very beautiful—”\n\n“Come, Fanny!”\n\n“I really think she is; not what I should call lovely, you know, but very beautiful. And then she is quiet and reserved; she does not require excitement, and I am sure is conscientious in the performance of her duties.”\n\n“Very conscientious, I have no doubt,” said Lucy, with something like a sneer in her tone. “But the question, I suppose, is, whether Lord Lufton likes her.”\n\n“I think he does,—in a sort of way. He did not talk to her so much as he did to you—”\n\n“Ah! that was all Lady Lufton’s fault, because she didn’t have him properly labelled.”\n\n“There does not seem to have been much harm done?”\n\n“Oh! by God’s mercy, very little. As for me, I shall get over it in three or four years I don’t doubt—that’s if I can get ass’s milk and change of air.”\n\n“We’ll take you to Barchester for that. But as I was saying, I really do think Lord Lufton likes Griselda Grantly.”\n\n“Then I really do think that he has uncommon bad taste,” said Lucy, with a reality in her voice differing much from the tone of banter she had hitherto used.\n\n“What, Lucy!” said her sister-in-law, looking at her. “Then I fear we shall really want the ass’s milk.”\n\n“Perhaps, considering my position, I ought to know nothing of Lord Lufton, for you say that it is very dangerous for young ladies to know young gentlemen. But I do know enough of him to understand that he ought not to like such a girl as Griselda Grantly. He ought to know that she is a mere automaton, cold, lifeless, spiritless, and even vapid. There is, I believe, nothing in her mentally, whatever may be her moral excellences. To me she is more absolutely like a statue than any other human being I ever saw. To sit still and be admired is all that she desires; and if she cannot get that, to sit still and not be admired would almost suffice for her. I do not worship Lady Lufton as you do; but I think quite well enough of her to wonder that she should choose such a girl as that for her son’s wife. That she does wish it, I do not doubt. But I shall indeed be surprised if he wishes it also.” And then as she finished her speech, Lucy again flogged the pony. This she did in vexation, because she felt that the tell-tale blood had suffused her face.\n\n“Why, Lucy, if he were your brother you could not be more eager about it.”\n\n“No, I could not. He is the only man friend with whom I was ever intimate, and I cannot bear to think that he should throw himself away. It’s horridly improper to care about such a thing, I have no doubt.”\n\n“I think we might acknowledge that if he and his mother are both satisfied, we may be satisfied also.”\n\n“I shall not be satisfied. It’s no use your looking at me, Fanny. You will make me talk of it, and I won’t tell a lie on the subject. I do like Lord Lufton very much; and I do dislike Griselda Grantly almost as much. Therefore I shall not be satisfied if they become man and wife. However, I do not suppose that either of them will ask my consent; nor is it probable that Lady Lufton will do so.” And then they went on for perhaps a quarter of a mile without speaking.\n\n“Poor Puck!” at last Lucy said. “He shan’t be whipped any more, shall he, because Miss Grantly looks like a statue? And, Fanny, don’t tell Mark to put me into a lunatic asylum. I also know a hawk from a heron, and that’s why I don’t like to see such a very unfitting marriage.” There was then nothing more said on the subject, and in two minutes they arrived at the house of the Hogglestock clergyman.\n\nMrs. Crawley had brought two children with her when she came from the Cornish curacy to Hogglestock, and two other babies had been added to her cares since then. One of these was now ill with croup, and it was with the object of offering to the mother some comfort and solace, that the present visit was made. The two ladies got down from their carriage, having obtained the services of a boy to hold Puck, and soon found themselves in Mrs. Crawley’s single sitting-room. She was sitting there with her foot on the board of a child’s cradle, rocking it, while an infant about three months old was lying in her lap. For the elder one, who was the sufferer, had in her illness usurped the baby’s place. Two other children, considerably older, were also in the room. The eldest was a girl, perhaps nine years of age, and the other a boy three years her junior. These were standing at their father’s elbow, who was studiously endeavouring to initiate them in the early mysteries of grammar. To tell the truth Mrs. Robarts would much have preferred that Mr. Crawley had not been there, for she had with her and about her certain contraband articles, presents for the children, as they were to be called, but in truth relief for that poor, much-tasked mother, which they knew it would be impossible to introduce in Mr. Crawley’s presence.\n\nShe, as we have said, was not quite so gaunt, not altogether so haggard as in the latter of those dreadful Cornish days. Lady Lufton and Mrs. Arabin between them, and the scanty comfort of their improved, though still wretched income, had done something towards bringing her back to the world in which she had lived in the soft days of her childhood. But even the liberal stipend of a hundred and thirty pounds a-year—liberal according to the scale by which the incomes of clergymen in some of our new districts are now apportioned—would not admit of a gentleman with his wife and four children living with the ordinary comforts of an artisan’s family. As regards the mere eating and drinking, the amounts of butcher’s meat and tea and butter, they of course were used in quantities which any artisan would have regarded as compatible only with demi-starvation. Better clothing for her children was necessary, and better clothing for him. As for her own raiment, the wives of few artisans would have been content to put up with Mrs. Crawley’s best gown. The stuff of which it was made had been paid for by her mother when she with much difficulty bestowed upon her daughter her modest wedding trousseau.\n\nLucy had never seen Mrs. Crawley. These visits to Hogglestock were not frequent, and had generally been made by Lady Lufton and Mrs. Robarts together. It was known that they were distasteful to Mr. Crawley, who felt a savage satisfaction in being left to himself. It may almost be said of him that he felt angry with those who relieved him, and he had certainly never as yet forgiven the Dean of Barchester for paying his debts. The dean had also given him his present living; and consequently his old friend was not now so dear to him as when in old days he would come down to that farm-house, almost as penniless as the curate himself. Then they would walk together for hours along the rock-bound shore, listening to the waves, discussing deep polemical mysteries, sometimes with hot fury, then again with tender, loving charity, but always with a mutual acknowledgment of each other’s truth. Now they lived comparatively near together, but no opportunities arose for such discussions. At any rate once a quarter Mr. Crawley was pressed by his old friend to visit him at the deanery, and Dr. Arabin had promised that no one else should be in the house if Mr. Crawley objected to society. But this was not what he wanted. The finery and grandeur of the deanery, and the comfort of that warm, snug library, would silence him at once. Why did not Dr. Arabin come out there to Hogglestock, and tramp with him through the dirty lanes as they used to tramp? Then he could have enjoyed himself; then he could have talked; then old days would have come back to them. But now!—“Arabin always rides on a sleek, fine horse, now-a-days,” he once said to his wife with a sneer. His poverty had been so terrible to himself that it was not in his heart to love a rich friend.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII. HOGGLESTOCK PARSONAGE.\n\nAt the end of the last chapter, we left Lucy Robarts waiting for an introduction to Mrs. Crawley, who was sitting with one baby in her lap while she was rocking another who lay in a cradle at her feet. Mr. Crawley, in the meanwhile, had risen from his seat with his finger between the leaves of an old grammar out of which he had been teaching his two elder children. The whole Crawley family was thus before them when Mrs. Robarts and Lucy entered the sitting-room.\n\n“This is my sister-in-law, Lucy,” said Mrs. Robarts. “Pray don’t move now, Mrs. Crawley; or if you do, let me take baby.” And she put out her arms and took the infant into them, making him quite at home there; for she had work of this kind of her own, at home, which she by no means neglected, though the attendance of nurses was more plentiful with her than at Hogglestock.\n\nMrs. Crawley did get up, and told Lucy that she was glad to see her, and Mr. Crawley came forward, grammar in hand, looking humble and meek. Could we have looked into the innermost spirit of him and his life’s partner, we should have seen that mixed with the pride of his poverty there was some feeling of disgrace that he was poor, but that with her, regarding this matter, there was neither pride nor shame. The realities of life had become so stern to her that the outward aspects of them were as nothing. She would have liked a new gown because it would have been useful; but it would have been nothing to her if all the county knew that the one in which she went to church had been turned three times. It galled him, however, to think that he and his were so poorly dressed.\n\n“I am afraid you can hardly find a chair, Miss Robarts,” said Mr. Crawley.\n\n“Oh, yes; there is nothing here but this young gentleman’s library,” said Lucy, moving a pile of ragged, coverless books on to the table. “I hope he’ll forgive me for moving them.”\n\n“They are not Bob’s,—at least, not the most of them,—but mine,” said the girl.\n\n“But some of them are mine,” said the boy; “ain’t they, Grace?”\n\n“And are you a great scholar?” asked Lucy, drawing the child to her.\n\n“I don’t know,” said Grace, with a sheepish face. “I am in Greek Delectus and the irregular verbs.”\n\n“Greek Delectus and the irregular verbs!” And Lucy put up her hands with astonishment.\n\n“And she knows an ode of Horace all by heart,” said Bob.\n\n“An ode of Horace!” said Lucy, still holding the young shamefaced female prodigy close to her knees.\n\n“It is all that I can give them,” said Mr. Crawley, apologetically. “A little scholarship is the only fortune that has come in my way, and I endeavour to share that with my children.”\n\n“I believe men say that it is the best fortune any of us can have,” said Lucy, thinking, however, in her own mind, that Horace and the irregular Greek verbs savoured too much of precocious forcing in a young lady of nine years old. But, nevertheless, Grace was a pretty, simple-looking girl, and clung to her ally closely, and seemed to like being fondled. So that Lucy anxiously wished that Mr. Crawley could be got rid of and the presents produced.\n\n“I hope you have left Mr. Robarts quite well,” said Mr. Crawley, with a stiff, ceremonial voice, differing very much from that in which he had so energetically addressed his brother clergyman when they were alone together in the study at Framley.\n\n“He is quite well, thank you. I suppose you have heard of his good fortune?”\n\n“Yes; I have heard of it,” said Mr. Crawley, gravely. “I hope that his promotion may tend in every way to his advantage here and hereafter.”\n\nIt seemed, however, to be manifest from the manner in which he expressed his kind wishes, that his hopes and expectations did not go hand-in-hand together.\n\n“By-the-by, he desired us to say that he will call here to-morrow; at about eleven, didn’t he say, Fanny?”\n\n“Yes; he wishes to see you about some parish business, I think,” said Mrs. Robarts, looking up for a moment from the anxious discussion in which she was already engaged with Mrs. Crawley on nursery matters.\n\n“Pray tell him,” said Mr. Crawley, “that I shall be happy to see him; though, perhaps, now that new duties have been thrown upon him, it will be better that I should visit him at Framley.”\n\n“His new duties do not disturb him much as yet,” said Lucy. “And his riding over here will be no trouble to him.”\n\n“Yes; there he has the advantage over me. I unfortunately have no horse.”\n\nAnd then Lucy began petting the little boy, and by degrees slipped a small bag of gingerbread-nuts out of her muff into his hands. She had not the patience necessary for waiting, as had her sister-in-law.\n\nThe boy took the bag, peeped into it, and then looked up into her face.\n\n“What is that, Bob?” said Mr. Crawley.\n\n“Gingerbread,” faltered Bobby, feeling that a sin had been committed, though, probably, feeling also that he himself could hardly as yet be accounted as deeply guilty.\n\n“Miss Robarts,” said the father, “we are very much obliged to you; but our children are hardly used to such things.”\n\n“I am a lady with a weak mind, Mr. Crawley, and always carry things of this sort about with me when I go to visit children; so you must forgive me, and allow your little boy to accept them.”\n\n“Oh, certainly. Bob, my child, give the bag to your mamma, and she will let you and Grace have them, one at a time.” And then the bag in a solemn manner was carried over to their mother, who, taking it from her son’s hands, laid it high on a bookshelf.\n\n“And not one now?” said Lucy Robarts, very piteously. “Don’t be so hard, Mr. Crawley,—not upon them, but upon me. May I not learn whether they are good of their kind?”\n\n“I am sure they are very good; but I think their mamma will prefer their being put by for the present.”\n\nThis was very discouraging to Lucy. If one small bag of gingerbread-nuts created so great a difficulty, how was she to dispose of the pot of guava jelly and box of bonbons, which were still in her muff; or how distribute the packet of oranges with which the pony-carriage was laden? And there was jelly for the sick child, and chicken broth, which was, indeed, another jelly; and, to tell the truth openly, there was also a joint of fresh pork and a basket of eggs from the Framley Parsonage farmyard, which Mrs. Robarts was to introduce, should she find herself capable of doing so; but which would certainly be cast out with utter scorn by Mr. Crawley, if tendered in his immediate presence. There had also been a suggestion as to adding two or three bottles of port; but the courage of the ladies had failed them on that head, and the wine was not now added to their difficulties.\n\nLucy found it very difficult to keep up a conversation with Mr. Crawley—the more so, as Mrs. Robarts and Mrs. Crawley presently withdrew into a bedroom, taking the two younger children with them. “How unlucky,” thought Lucy, “that she has not got my muff with her!” But the muff lay in her lap, ponderous with its rich enclosures.\n\n“I suppose you will live in Barchester for a portion of the year now,” said Mr. Crawley.\n\n“I really do not know as yet; Mark talks of taking lodgings for his first month’s residence.”\n\n“But he will have the house, will he not?”\n\n“Oh, yes; I suppose so.”\n\n“I fear he will find it interfere with his own parish—with his general utility there: the schools, for instance.”\n\n“Mark thinks that, as he is so near, he need not be much absent from Framley, even during his residence. And then Lady Lufton is so good about the schools.”\n\n“Ah! yes; but Lady Lufton is not a clergyman, Miss Robarts.”\n\nIt was on Lucy’s tongue to say that her ladyship was pretty nearly as bad, but she stopped herself.\n\nAt this moment Providence sent great relief to Miss Robarts in the shape of Mrs. Crawley’s red-armed maid-of-all-work, who, walking up to her master, whispered into his ear that he was wanted. It was the time of day at which his attendance was always required in his parish school; and that attendance being so punctually given, those who wanted him looked for him there at this hour, and if he were absent, did not scruple to send for him.\n\n“Miss Robarts, I am afraid you must excuse me,” said he, getting up and taking his hat and stick. Lucy begged that she might not be at all in the way, and already began to speculate how she might best unload her treasures. “Will you make my compliments to Mrs. Robarts, and say that I am sorry to miss the pleasure of wishing her good-bye? But I shall probably see her as she passes the school-house.” And then, stick in hand, he walked forth, and Lucy fancied that Bobby’s eyes immediately rested on the bag of gingerbread-nuts.\n\n“Bob,” said she, almost in a whisper, “do you like sugar-plums?”\n\n“Very much indeed,” said Bob, with exceeding gravity, and with his eye upon the window to see whether his father had passed.\n\n“Then come here,” said Lucy. But as she spoke the door again opened, and Mr. Crawley reappeared. “I have left a book behind me,” he said; and, coming back through the room, he took up the well-worn prayer-book which accompanied him in all his wanderings through the parish. Bobby, when he saw his father, had retreated a few steps back, as also did Grace, who, to confess the truth, had been attracted by the sound of sugar-plums, in spite of the irregular verbs. And Lucy withdrew her hand from her muff, and looked guilty. Was she not deceiving the good man—nay, teaching his own children to deceive him? But there are men made of such stuff that an angel could hardly live with them without some deceit.\n\n“Papa’s gone now,” whispered Bobby; “I saw him turn round the corner.” He, at any rate, had learned his lesson—as it was natural that he should do.\n\nSome one else, also, had learned that papa was gone; for while Bob and Grace were still counting the big lumps of sugar-candy, each employed the while for inward solace with an inch of barley-sugar, the front-door opened, and a big basket, and a bundle done up in a kitchen-cloth, made surreptitious entrance into the house, and were quickly unpacked by Mrs. Robarts herself on the table in Mrs. Crawley’s bedroom.\n\n“I did venture to bring them,” said Fanny, with a look of shame, “for I know how a sick child occupies the whole house.”\n\n“Ah! my friend,” said Mrs. Crawley, taking hold of Mrs. Robarts’ arm and looking into her face, “that sort of shame is over with me. God has tried us with want, and for my children’s sake I am glad of such relief.”\n\n“But will he be angry?”\n\n“I will manage it. Dear Mrs. Robarts, you must not be surprised at him. His lot is sometimes very hard to bear: such things are so much worse for a man than for a woman.”\n\nFanny was not quite prepared to admit this in her own heart, but she made no reply on that head. “I am sure I hope we may be able to be of use to you,” she said, “if you will only look upon me as an old friend, and write to me if you want me. I hesitate to come frequently for fear that I should offend him.”\n\nAnd then, by degrees, there was confidence between them, and the poverty-stricken helpmate of the perpetual curate was able to speak of the weight of her burden to the well-to-do young wife of the Barchester prebendary. “It was hard,” the former said, “to feel herself so different from the wives of other clergymen around her—to know that they lived softly, while she, with all the work of her hands, and unceasing struggle of her energies, could hardly manage to place wholesome food before her husband and children. It was a terrible thing—a grievous thing to think of, that all the work of her mind should be given up to such subjects as these. But, nevertheless, she could bear it,” she said, “as long as he would carry himself like a man, and face his lot boldly before the world.” And then she told how he had been better there at Hogglestock than in their former residence down in Cornwall, and in warm language she expressed her thanks to the friend who had done so much for them.\n\n“Mrs. Arabin told me that she was so anxious you should go to them,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“Ah, yes; but that I fear is impossible. The children, you know, Mrs. Robarts.”\n\n“I would take care of two of them for you.”\n\n“Oh, no; I could not punish you for your goodness in that way. But he would not go. He could go and leave me at home. Sometimes I have thought that it might be so, and I have done all in my power to persuade him. I have told him that if he could mix once more with the world, with the clerical world, you know, that he would be better fitted for the performance of his own duties. But he answers me angrily, that it is impossible—that his coat is not fit for the dean’s table,” and Mrs. Crawley almost blushed as she spoke of such a reason.\n\n“What! with an old friend like Dr. Arabin? Surely that must be nonsense.”\n\n“I know that it is. The dean would be glad to see him with any coat. But the fact is that he cannot bear to enter the house of a rich man unless his duty calls him there.”\n\n“But surely that is a mistake?”\n\n“It is a mistake. But what can I do? I fear that he regards the rich as his enemies. He is pining for the solace of some friend to whom he could talk—for some equal, with a mind educated like his own, to whose thoughts he could listen, and to whom he could speak his own thoughts. But such a friend must be equal, not only in mind, but in purse; and where can he ever find such a man as that?”\n\n“But you may get better preferment.”\n\n“Ah, no; and if he did, we are hardly fit for it now. If I could think that I could educate my children; if I could only do something for my poor Grace—”\n\nIn answer to this Mrs. Robarts said a word or two, but not much. She resolved, however, that if she could get her husband’s leave, something should be done for Grace. Would it not be a good work? and was it not incumbent on her to make some kindly use of all the goods with which Providence had blessed herself?\n\nAnd then they went back to the sitting-room, each again with a young child in her arms, Mrs. Crawley having stowed away in the kitchen the chicken broth and the leg of pork and the supply of eggs. Lucy had been engaged the while with the children, and when the two married ladies entered, they found that a shop had been opened at which all manner of luxuries were being readily sold and purchased at marvellously easy prices; the guava jelly was there, and the oranges, and the sugar-plums, red and yellow and striped; and, moreover, the gingerbread had been taken down in the audacity of their commercial speculations, and the nuts were spread out upon a board, behind which Lucy stood as shop-girl, disposing of them for kisses.\n\n“Mamma, mamma,” said Bobby, running up to his mother, “you must buy something of her,” and he pointed with his fingers at the shop-girl. “You must give her two kisses for that heap of barley-sugar.” Looking at Bobby’s mouth at the time, one would have said that his kisses might be dispensed with.\n\nWhen they were again in the pony-carriage, behind the impatient Puck, and were well away from the door, Fanny was the first to speak.\n\n“How very different those two are,” she said; “different in their minds and in their spirit!”\n\n“But how much higher toned is her mind than his! How weak he is in many things, and how strong she is in everything! How false is his pride, and how false his shame!”\n\n“But we must remember what he has to bear. It is not every one that can endure such a life as his without false pride and false shame.”\n\n“But she has neither,” said Lucy.\n\n“Because you have one hero in a family, does that give you a right to expect another?” said Mrs. Robarts. “Of all my own acquaintance, Mrs. Crawley, I think, comes nearest to heroism.”\n\nAnd then they passed by the Hogglestock school, and Mr. Crawley, when he heard the noise of the wheels, came out.\n\n“You have been very kind,” said he, “to remain so long with my poor wife.”\n\n“We had a great many things to talk about, after you went.”\n\n“It is very kind of you, for she does not often see a friend, now-a-days. Will you have the goodness to tell Mr. Robarts that I shall be here at the school, at eleven o’clock to-morrow?”\n\nAnd then he bowed, taking off his hat to them, and they drove on.\n\n“If he really does care about her comfort, I shall not think so badly of him,” said Lucy.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII. THE TRIUMPH OF THE GIANTS.\n\nAnd now about the end of April news arrived almost simultaneously in all quarters of the habitable globe that was terrible in its import to one of the chief persons of our history;—some may think to the chief person in it. All high parliamentary people will doubtless so think, and the wives and daughters of such. The Titans warring against the gods had been for awhile successful. Typhœus and Mimas, Porphyrion and Rhœcus, the giant brood of old, steeped in ignorance and wedded to corruption, had scaled the heights of Olympus, assisted by that audacious flinger of deadly ponderous missiles, who stands ever ready armed with his terrific sling—Supplehouse, the Enceladus of the press. And in this universal cataclasm of the starry councils, what could a poor Diana do, Diana of the Petty Bag, but abandon her pride of place to some rude Orion? In other words, the ministry had been compelled to resign, and with them Mr. Harold Smith.\n\n“And so poor Harold is out, before he has well tasted the sweets of office,” said Sowerby, writing to his friend the parson; “and as far as I know, the only piece of Church patronage which has fallen in the way of the ministry since he joined it, has made its way down to Framley—to my great joy and contentment.” But it hardly tended to Mark’s joy and contentment on the same subject that he should be so often reminded of the benefit conferred upon him.\n\nTerrible was this break-down of the ministry, and especially to Harold Smith, who to the last had had confidence in that theory of new blood. He could hardly believe that a large majority of the House should vote against a government which he had only just joined. “If we are to go on in this way,” he said to his young friend Green Walker, “the Queen’s government cannot be carried on.” That alleged difficulty as to carrying on the Queen’s government has been frequently mooted in late years since a certain great man first introduced the idea. Nevertheless, the Queen’s government is carried on, and the propensity and aptitude of men for this work seems to be not at all on the decrease. If we have but few young statesmen, it is because the old stagers are so fond of the rattle of their harness.\n\n“I really do not see how the Queen’s government is to be carried on,” said Harold Smith to Green Walker, standing in a corner of one of the lobbies of the House of Commons on the first of those days of awful interest, in which the Queen was sending for one crack statesman after another; and some anxious men were beginning to doubt whether or no we should, in truth, be able to obtain the blessing of another cabinet. The gods had all vanished from their places. Would the giants be good enough to do anything for us or no? There were men who seemed to think that the giants would refuse to do anything for us. “The House will now be adjourned over till Monday, and I would not be in her Majesty’s shoes for something,” said Mr. Harold Smith.\n\n“By Jove! no,” said Green Walker, who in these days was a stanch Harold Smithian, having felt a pride in joining himself on as a substantial support to a cabinet minister. Had he contented himself with being merely a Brockite, he would have counted as nobody. “By Jove! no,” and Green Walker opened his eyes and shook his head, as he thought of the perilous condition in which her Majesty must be placed. “I happen to know that Lord —— won’t join them unless he has the Foreign Office,” and he mentioned some hundred-handed Gyas supposed to be of the utmost importance to the counsels of the Titans.\n\n“And that, of course, is impossible. I don’t see what on earth they are to do. There’s Sidonia; they do say that he’s making some difficulty now.” Now Sidonia was another giant, supposed to be very powerful.\n\n“We all know that the Queen won’t see him,” said Green Walker, who, being a member of Parliament for the Crewe Junction, and nephew to Lady Hartletop, of course had perfectly correct means of ascertaining what the Queen would do, and what she would not.\n\n“The fact is,” said Harold Smith, recurring again to his own situation as an ejected god, “that the House does not in the least understand what it is about;—doesn’t know what it wants. The question I should like to ask them is this: do they intend that the Queen shall have a government, or do they not? Are they prepared to support such men as Sidonia and Lord De Terrier? If so, I am their obedient humble servant; but I shall be very much surprised, that’s all.” Lord De Terrier was at this time recognized by all men as the leader of the giants.\n\n“And so shall I,—deucedly surprised. They can’t do it, you know. There are the Manchester men. I ought to know something about them down in my country; and I say they can’t support Lord De Terrier. It wouldn’t be natural.”\n\n“Natural! Human nature has come to an end, I think,” said Harold Smith, who could hardly understand that the world should conspire to throw over a government which he had joined, and that, too, before the world had waited to see how much he would do for it; “the fact is this, Walker, we have no longer among us any strong feeling of party.”\n\n“No, not a d——,” said Green Walker, who was very energetic in his present political aspirations.\n\n“And till we can recover that, we shall never be able to have a government firm-seated and sure-handed. Nobody can count on men from one week to another. The very members who in one month place a minister in power, are the very first to vote against him in the next.”\n\n“We must put a stop to that sort of thing, otherwise we shall never do any good.”\n\n“I don’t mean to deny that Brock was wrong with reference to Lord Brittleback. I think that he was wrong, and I said so all through. But, heavens on earth—!” and instead of completing his speech Harold Smith turned away his head, and struck his hands together in token of his astonishment at the fatuity of the age. What he probably meant to express was this: that if such a good deed as that late appointment made at the Petty Bag Office were not held sufficient to atone for that other evil deed to which he had alluded, there would be an end of all justice in sublunary matters. Was no offence to be forgiven, even when so great virtue had been displayed?\n\n“I attribute it all to Supplehouse,” said Green Walker, trying to console his friend.\n\n“Yes,” said Harold Smith, now verging on the bounds of parliamentary eloquence, although he still spoke with bated breath, and to one solitary hearer. “Yes; we are becoming the slaves of a mercenary and irresponsible press—of one single newspaper. There is a man endowed with no great talent, enjoying no public confidence, untrusted as a politician, and unheard of even as a writer by the world at large, and yet, because he is on the staff of the Jupiter, he is able to overturn the government and throw the whole country into dismay. It is astonishing to me that a man like Lord Brock should allow himself to be so timid.” And nevertheless it was not yet a month since Harold Smith had been counselling with Supplehouse how a series of strong articles in the Jupiter, together with the expected support of the Manchester men, might probably be effective in hurling the minister from his seat. But at that time the minister had not revigorated himself with young blood. “How the Queen’s government is to be carried on, that is the question now,” Harold Smith repeated. A difficulty which had not caused him much dismay at that period, about a month since, to which we have alluded.\n\nAt this moment Sowerby and Supplehouse together joined them, having come out of the House, in which some unimportant business had been completed after the minister’s notice of adjournment.\n\n“Well, Harold,” said Sowerby, “what do you say to your governor’s statement?”\n\n“I have nothing to say to it,” said Harold Smith, looking up very solemnly from under the penthouse of his hat, and, perhaps, rather savagely. Sowerby had supported the government at the late crisis; but why was he now seen herding with such a one as Supplehouse?\n\n“He did it pretty well, I think,” said Sowerby.\n\n“Very well, indeed,” said Supplehouse; “as he always does those sort of things. No man makes so good an explanation of circumstances, or comes out with so telling a personal statement. He ought to keep himself in reserve for those sort of things.”\n\n“And who in the meantime is to carry on the Queen’s government?” said Harold Smith, looking very stern.\n\n“That should be left to men of lesser mark,” said he of the Jupiter. “The points as to which one really listens to a minister, the subjects about which men really care, are always personal. How many of us are truly interested as to the best mode of governing India? But in a question touching the character of a prime minister we all muster together like bees round a sounding cymbal.”\n\n“That arises from envy, malice, and all uncharitableness,” said Harold Smith.\n\n“Yes; and from picking and stealing, evil speaking, lying, and slandering,” said Mr. Sowerby.\n\n“We are so prone to desire and covet other men’s places,” said Supplehouse.\n\n“Some men are so,” said Sowerby; “but it is the evil speaking, lying, and slandering, which does the mischief. Is it not, Harold?”\n\n“And in the meantime how is the Queen’s government to be carried on?” said Mr. Green Walker.\n\nOn the following morning it was known that Lord De Terrier was with the Queen at Buckingham Palace, and at about twelve a list of the new ministry was published, which must have been in the highest degree satisfactory to the whole brood of giants. Every son of Tellus was included in it, as were also very many of the daughters. But then, late in the afternoon, Lord Brock was again summoned to the palace, and it was thought in the West End among the clubs that the gods had again a chance. “If only,” said the Purist, an evening paper which was supposed to be very much in the interest of Mr. Harold Smith, “if only Lord Brock can have the wisdom to place the right men in the right places. It was only the other day that he introduced Mr. Smith into his government. That this was a step in the right direction every one has acknowledged, though unfortunately it was made too late to prevent the disturbance which has since occurred. It now appears probable that his lordship will again have an opportunity of selecting a list of statesmen with the view of carrying on the Queen’s government; and it is to be hoped that such men as Mr. Smith may be placed in situations in which their talents, industry, and acknowledged official aptitudes, may be of permanent service to the country.”\n\nSupplehouse, when he read this at the club with Mr. Sowerby at his elbow, declared that the style was too well marked to leave any doubt as to the author; but we ourselves are not inclined to think that Mr. Harold Smith wrote the article himself, although it may be probable that he saw it in type.\n\nBut the Jupiter the next morning settled the whole question, and made it known to the world that, in spite of all the sendings and re-sendings, Lord Brock and the gods were permanently out, and Lord De Terrier and the giants permanently in. That fractious giant who would only go to the Foreign Office had, in fact, gone to some sphere of much less important duty, and Sidonia, in spite of the whispered dislike of an illustrious personage, opened the campaign with all the full appanages of a giant of the highest standing. “We hope,” said the Jupiter, “that Lord Brock may not yet be too old to take a lesson. If so, the present decision of the House of Commons, and we may say of the country also, may teach him not to put his trust in such princes as Lord Brittleback, or such broken reeds as Mr. Harold Smith.” Now this parting blow we always thought to be exceedingly unkind, and altogether unnecessary, on the part of Mr. Supplehouse.\n\n“My dear,” said Mrs. Harold, when she first met Miss Dunstable after the catastrophe was known, “how am I possibly to endure this degradation?” And she put her deeply-laced handkerchief up to her eyes.\n\n“Christian resignation,” suggested Miss Dunstable.\n\n“Fiddlestick!” said Mrs. Harold Smith. “You millionnaires always talk of Christian resignation, because you never are called on to resign anything. If I had any Christian resignation, I shouldn’t have cared for such pomps and vanities. Think of it, my dear; a cabinet minister’s wife for only three weeks!”\n\n“How does poor Mr. Smith endure it?”\n\n“What? Harold? He only lives on the hope of vengeance. When he has put an end to Mr. Supplehouse, he will be content to die.”\n\nAnd then there were further explanations in both Houses of Parliament, which were altogether satisfactory. The high-bred, courteous giants assured the gods that they had piled Pelion on Ossa and thus climbed up into power, very much in opposition to their own good-wills; for they, the giants themselves, preferred the sweets of dignified retirement. But the voice of the people had been too strong for them; the effort had been made, not by themselves, but by others, who were determined that the giants should be at the head of affairs. Indeed, the spirit of the times was so clearly in favour of giants that there had been no alternative. So said Briareus to the Lords, and Orion to the Commons. And then the gods were absolutely happy in ceding their places; and so far were they from any uncelestial envy or malice which might not be divine, that they promised to give the giants all the assistance in their power in carrying on the work of government; upon which the giants declared how deeply indebted they would be for such valuable counsel and friendly assistance. All this was delightful in the extreme; but not the less did ordinary men seem to expect that the usual battle would go on in the old customary way. It is easy to love one’s enemy when one is making fine speeches; but so difficult to do so in the actual everyday work of life.\n\nBut there was and always has been this peculiar good point about the giants, that they are never too proud to follow in the footsteps of the gods. If the gods, deliberating painfully together, have elaborated any skilful project, the giants are always willing to adopt it as their own, not treating the bantling as a foster-child, but praising it and pushing it so that men should regard it as the undoubted offspring of their own brains. Now just at this time there had been a plan much thought of for increasing the number of the bishops. Good active bishops were very desirable, and there was a strong feeling among certain excellent churchmen that there could hardly be too many of them. Lord Brock had his measure cut and dry. There should be a Bishop of Westminster to share the Herculean toils of the metropolitan prelate, and another up in the North to Christianize the mining interests and wash white the blackamoors of Newcastle: Bishop of Beverley he should be called. But, in opposition to this, the giants, it was known, had intended to put forth the whole measure of their brute force. More curates, they said, were wanting, and district incumbents; not more bishops rolling in carriages. That bishops should roll in carriages was very good; but of such blessings the English world for the present had enough. And therefore Lord Brock and the gods had had much fear as to their little project.\n\nBut now, immediately on the accession of the giants, it was known that the bishop bill was to be gone on with immediately. Some small changes would be effected so that the bill should be gigantic rather than divine; but the result would be altogether the same. It must, however, be admitted that bishops appointed by ourselves may be very good things, whereas those appointed by our adversaries will be anything but good. And, no doubt, this feeling went a long way with the giants. Be that as it may, the new bishop bill was to be their first work of government, and it was to be brought forward and carried, and the new prelates selected and put into their chairs all at once,—before the grouse should begin to crow and put an end to the doings of gods as well as giants.\n\nAmong other minor effects arising from this decision was the following, that Archdeacon and Mrs. Grantly returned to London, and again took the lodgings in which they had before been staying. On various occasions also during the first week of this second sojourn, Dr. Grantly might be seen entering the official chambers of the First Lord of the Treasury. Much counsel was necessary among high churchmen of great repute before any fixed resolution could wisely be made in such a matter as this; and few churchmen stood in higher repute than the Archdeacon of Barchester. And then it began to be rumoured in the world that the minister had disposed at any rate of the see of Westminster.\n\nThis present time was a very nervous one for Mrs. Grantly. What might be the aspirations of the archdeacon himself, we will not stop to inquire. It may be that time and experience had taught him the futility of earthly honours, and made him content with the comfortable opulence of his Barsetshire rectory. But there is no theory of church discipline which makes it necessary that a clergyman’s wife should have an objection to a bishopric. The archdeacon probably was only anxious to give a disinterested aid to the minister, but Mrs. Grantly did long to sit in high places, and be at any rate equal to Mrs. Proudie. It was for her children, she said to herself, that she was thus anxious,—that they should have a good position before the world, and the means of making the best of themselves. “One is able to do nothing, you know, shut up there, down at Plumstead,” she had remarked to Lady Lufton on the occasion of her first visit to London, and yet the time was not long past when she had thought that rectory house at Plumstead to be by no means insufficient or contemptible.\n\nAnd then there came a question whether or no Griselda should go back to her mother; but this idea was very strongly opposed by Lady Lufton, and ultimately with success. “I really think the dear girl is very happy with me,” said Lady Lufton; “and if ever she is to belong to me more closely, it will be so well that we should know and love one another.”\n\nTo tell the truth, Lady Lufton had been trying hard to know and love Griselda, but hitherto she had scarcely succeeded to the full extent of her wishes. That she loved Griselda was certain,—with that sort of love which springs from a person’s volition and not from the judgment. She had said all along to herself and others that she did love Griselda Grantly. She had admired the young lady’s face, liked her manner, approved of her fortune and family, and had selected her for a daughter-in-law in a somewhat impetuous manner. Therefore she loved her. But it was by no means clear to Lady Lufton that she did as yet know her young friend. The match was a plan of her own, and therefore she stuck to it as warmly as ever, but she began to have some misgivings whether or no the dear girl would be to her herself all that she had dreamed of in a daughter-in-law.\n\n“But, dear Lady Lufton,” said Mrs. Grantly, “is it not possible that we may put her affections to too severe a test? What, if she should learn to regard him, and then—”\n\n“Ah! if she did, I should have no fear of the result. If she showed anything like love for Ludovic, he would be at her feet in a moment. He is impulsive, but she is not.”\n\n“Exactly, Lady Lufton. It is his privilege to be impulsive and to sue for her affection, and hers to have her love sought for without making any demonstration. It is perhaps the fault of young ladies of the present day that they are too impulsive. They assume privileges which are not their own, and thus lose those which are.”\n\n“Quite true! I quite agree with you. It is probably that very feeling that has made me think so highly of Griselda. But then—” But then a young lady, though she need not jump down a gentleman’s throat, or throw herself into his face, may give some signs that she is made of flesh and blood; especially when her papa and mamma and all belonging to her are so anxious to make the path of her love run smooth. That was what was passing through Lady Lufton’s mind; but she did not say it all; she merely looked it.\n\n“I don’t think she will ever allow herself to indulge in an unauthorized passion,” said Mrs. Grantly.\n\n“I am sure she will not,” said Lady Lufton, with ready agreement, fearing perhaps in her heart that Griselda would never indulge in any passion, authorized or unauthorized.\n\n“I don’t know whether Lord Lufton sees much of her now,” said Mrs. Grantly, thinking perhaps of that promise of Lady Lufton’s with reference to his lordship’s spare time.\n\n“Just lately, during these changes, you know, everybody has been so much engaged. Ludovic has been constantly at the House, and then men find it so necessary to be at their clubs just now.”\n\n“Yes, yes, of course,” said Mrs. Grantly, who was not at all disposed to think little of the importance of the present crisis, or to wonder that men should congregate together when such deeds were to be done as those which now occupied the breasts of the Queen’s advisers. At last, however, the two mothers perfectly understood each other. Griselda was still to remain with Lady Lufton; and was to accept her ladyship’s son, if he could only be induced to exercise his privilege of asking her; but in the meantime, as this seemed to be doubtful, Griselda was not to be debarred from her privilege of making what use she could of any other string which she might have to her bow.\n\n“But, mamma,” said Griselda, in a moment of unwatched intercourse between the mother and daughter, “is it really true that they are going to make papa a bishop?”\n\n“We can tell nothing as yet, my dear. People in the world are talking about it. Your papa has been a good deal with Lord De Terrier.”\n\n“And isn’t he prime minister?”\n\n“Oh, yes; I am happy to say that he is.”\n\n“I thought the prime minister could make any one a bishop that he chooses,—any clergyman, that is.”\n\n“But there is no see vacant,” said Mrs. Grantly.\n\n“Then there isn’t any chance,” said Griselda, looking very glum.\n\n“They are going to have an Act of Parliament for making two more bishops. That’s what they are talking about at least. And if they do—”\n\n“Papa will be Bishop of Westminster—won’t he? And we shall live in London?”\n\n“But you must not talk about it, my dear.”\n\n“No, I won’t. But, mamma, a Bishop of Westminster will be higher than a Bishop of Barchester; won’t he? I shall so like to be able to snub those Miss Proudies.” It will therefore be seen that there were matters on which even Griselda Grantly could be animated. Like the rest of her family she was devoted to the Church.\n\nLate on that afternoon the archdeacon returned home to dine in Mount Street, having spent the whole of the day between the Treasury chambers, a meeting of Convocation, and his club. And when he did get home it was soon manifest to his wife that he was not laden with good news.\n\n“It is almost incredible,” he said, standing with his back to the drawing-room fire.\n\n“What is incredible?” said his wife, sharing her husband’s anxiety to the full.\n\n“If I had not learned it as fact, I would not have believed it, even of Lord Brock,” said the archdeacon.\n\n“Learned what?” said the anxious wife.\n\n“After all, they are going to oppose the bill.”\n\n“Impossible!” said Mrs. Grantly.\n\n“But they are.”\n\n“The bill for the two new bishops, archdeacon? oppose their own bill!”\n\n“Yes—oppose their own bill. It is almost incredible; but so it is. Some changes have been forced upon us; little things which they had forgotten—quite minor matters; and they now say that they will be obliged to divide against us on these twopenny-halfpenny, hair-splitting points. It is Lord Brock’s own doing too, after all that he said about abstaining from factious opposition to the government.”\n\n“I believe there is nothing too bad or too false for that man,” said Mrs. Grantly.\n\n“After all they said, too, when they were in power themselves, as to the present government opposing the cause of religion! They declare now that Lord De Terrier cannot be very anxious about it, as he had so many good reasons against it a few weeks ago. Is it not dreadful that there should be such double-dealing in men in such positions?”\n\n“It is sickening,” said Mrs. Grantly.\n\nAnd then there was a pause between them as each thought of the injury that was done to them.\n\n“But, archdeacon—”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Could you not give up those small points and shame them into compliance?”\n\n“Nothing would shame them.”\n\n“But would it not be well to try?”\n\nThe game was so good a one, and the stake so important, that Mrs. Grantly felt that it would be worth playing for to the last.\n\n“It is no good.”\n\n“But I certainly would suggest it to Lord De Terrier. I am sure the country would go along with him; at any rate the Church would.”\n\n“It is impossible,” said the archdeacon. “To tell the truth, it did occur to me. But some of them down there seemed to think that it would not do.”\n\nMrs. Grantly sat awhile on the sofa, still meditating in her mind whether there might not yet be some escape from so terrible a downfall.\n\n“But, archdeacon—”\n\n“I’ll go upstairs and dress,” said he, in despondency.\n\n“But, archdeacon, surely the present ministry may have a majority on such a subject as that; I thought they were sure of a majority now.”\n\n“No; not sure.”\n\n“But at any rate the chances are in their favour? I do hope they’ll do their duty, and exert themselves to keep their members together.”\n\nAnd then the archdeacon told out the whole of the truth.\n\n“Lord De Terrier says that under the present circumstances he will not bring the matter forward this session at all. So we had better go back to Plumstead.”\n\nMrs. Grantly then felt that there was nothing further to be said, and it will be proper that the historian should drop a veil over their sufferings.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV. MAGNA EST VERITAS.\n\nIt was made known to the reader that in the early part of the winter Mr. Sowerby had a scheme for retrieving his lost fortunes, and setting himself right in the world, by marrying that rich heiress, Miss Dunstable. I fear my friend Sowerby does not, at present, stand high in the estimation of those who have come on with me thus far in this narrative. He has been described as a spendthrift and gambler, and as one scarcely honest in his extravagance and gambling. But nevertheless there are worse men than Mr. Sowerby, and I am not prepared to say that, should he be successful with Miss Dunstable, that lady would choose by any means the worst of the suitors who are continually throwing themselves at her feet. Reckless as this man always appeared to be, reckless as he absolutely was, there was still within his heart a desire for better things, and in his mind an understanding that he had hitherto missed the career of an honest English gentleman. He was proud of his position as member for his county, though hitherto he had done so little to grace it; he was proud of his domain at Chaldicotes, though the possession of it had so nearly passed out of his own hands; he was proud of the old blood that flowed in his veins; and he was proud also of that easy, comfortable, gay manner, which went so far in the world’s judgment to atone for his extravagance and evil practices. If only he could get another chance, as he now said to himself, things should go very differently with him. He would utterly forswear the whole company of Tozers. He would cease to deal in bills, and to pay heaven only knows how many hundred per cent. for his moneys. He would no longer prey upon his friends, and would redeem his title-deeds from the clutches of the Duke of Omnium. If only he could get another chance!\n\nMiss Dunstable’s fortune would do all this and ever so much more, and then, moreover, Miss Dunstable was a woman whom he really liked. She was not soft, feminine, or pretty, nor was she very young; but she was clever, self-possessed, and quite able to hold her own in any class; and as to age, Mr. Sowerby was not very young himself. In making such a match he would have no cause of shame. He could speak of it before his friends without fear of their grimaces, and ask them to his house, with the full assurance that the head of his table would not disgrace him. And then as the scheme grew clearer and clearer to him, he declared to himself that if he should be successful, he would use her well, and not rob her of her money—beyond what was absolutely necessary.\n\nHe had intended to have laid his fortunes at her feet at Chaldicotes; but the lady had been coy. Then the deed was to have been done at Gatherum Castle, but the lady ran away from Gatherum Castle just at the time on which he had fixed. And since that, one circumstance after another had postponed the affair in London, till now at last he was resolved that he would know his fate, let it be what it might. If he could not contrive that things should speedily be arranged, it might come to pass that he would be altogether debarred from presenting himself to the lady as Mr. Sowerby of Chaldicotes. Tidings had reached him, through Mr. Fothergill, that the duke would be glad to have matters arranged; and Mr. Sowerby well knew the meaning of that message.\n\nMr. Sowerby was not fighting this campaign alone, without the aid of any ally. Indeed, no man ever had a more trusty ally in any campaign than he had in this. And it was this ally, the only faithful comrade that clung to him through good and ill during his whole life, who first put it into his head that Miss Dunstable was a woman and might be married.\n\n“A hundred needy adventurers have attempted it, and failed already,” Mr. Sowerby had said, when the plan was first proposed to him.\n\n“But, nevertheless, she will some day marry some one; and why not you as well as another?” his sister had answered. For Mrs. Harold Smith was the ally of whom I have spoken.\n\nMrs. Harold Smith, whatever may have been her faults, could boast of this virtue—that she loved her brother. He was probably the only human being that she did love. Children she had none; and as for her husband, it had never occurred to her to love him. She had married him for a position; and being a clever woman, with a good digestion and command of her temper, had managed to get through the world without much of that unhappiness which usually follows ill-assorted marriages. At home she managed to keep the upper hand, but she did so in an easy, good-humoured way that made her rule bearable; and away from home she assisted her lord’s political standing, though she laughed more keenly than any one else at his foibles. But the lord of her heart was her brother; and in all his scrapes, all his extravagances, and all his recklessness, she had ever been willing to assist him. With the view of doing this she had sought the intimacy of Miss Dunstable, and for the last year past had indulged every caprice of that lady. Or rather, she had had the wit to learn that Miss Dunstable was to be won, not by the indulgence of caprices, but by free and easy intercourse, with a dash of fun, and, at any rate, a semblance of honesty. Mrs. Harold Smith was not, perhaps, herself very honest by disposition; but in these latter days she had taken up a theory of honesty for the sake of Miss Dunstable—not altogether in vain, for Miss Dunstable and Mrs. Harold Smith were certainly very intimate.\n\n“If I am to do it at all, I must not wait any longer,” said Mr. Sowerby to his sister a day or two after the final break-down of the gods. The affection of the sister for the brother may be imagined from the fact that at such a time she could give up her mind to such a subject. But, in truth, her husband’s position as a cabinet minister was as nothing to her compared with her brother’s position as a county gentleman.\n\n“One time is as good as another,” said Mrs. Harold Smith.\n\n“You mean that you would advise me to ask her at once.”\n\n“Certainly. But you must remember, Nat, that you will have no easy task. It will not do for you to kneel down and swear that you love her.”\n\n“If I do it at all, I shall certainly do it without kneeling—you may be sure of that, Harriet.”\n\n“Yes, and without swearing that you love her. There is only one way in which you can be successful with Miss Dunstable—you must tell her the truth.”\n\n“What!—tell her that I am ruined, horse, foot, and dragoons, and then bid her help me out of the mire?”\n\n“Exactly: that will be your only chance, strange as it may appear.”\n\n“This is very different from what you used to say, down at Chaldicotes.”\n\n“So it is; but I know her much better than I did when we were there. Since then I have done but little else than study the freaks of her character. If she really likes you—and I think she does—she could forgive you any other crime but that of swearing that you loved her.”\n\n“I should hardly know how to propose without saying something about it.”\n\n“But you must say nothing—not a word; you must tell her that you are a gentleman of good blood and high station, but sadly out at elbows.”\n\n“She knows that already.”\n\n“Of course she does; but she must know it as coming directly from your own mouth. And then tell her that you propose to set yourself right by marrying her—by marrying her for the sake of her money.”\n\n“That will hardly win her, I should say.”\n\n“If it does not, no other way, that I know of, will do so. As I told you before, it will be no easy task. Of course you must make her understand that her happiness shall be cared for; but that must not be put prominently forward as your object. Your first object is her money, and your only chance for success is in telling the truth.”\n\n“It is very seldom that a man finds himself in such a position as that,” said Sowerby, walking up and down his sister’s room; “and, upon my word, I don’t think I am up to the task. I should certainly break down. I don’t believe there’s a man in London could go to a woman with such a story as that, and then ask her to marry him.”\n\n“If you cannot, you may as well give it up,” said Mrs. Harold Smith. “But if you can do it—if you can go through with it in that manner—my own opinion is that your chance of success would not be bad. The fact is,” added the sister after awhile, during which her brother was continuing his walk and meditating on the difficulties of his position—“the fact is, you men never understand a woman; you give her credit neither for her strength, nor for her weakness. You are too bold, and too timid: you think she is a fool and tell her so, and yet never can trust her to do a kind action. Why should she not marry you with the intention of doing you a good turn? After all, she would lose very little: there is the estate, and if she redeemed it, it would belong to her as well as to you.”\n\n“It would be a good turn, indeed. I fear I should be too modest to put it to her in that way.”\n\n“Her position would be much better as your wife than it is at present. You are good-humoured and good-tempered, you would intend to treat her well, and, on the whole, she would be much happier as Mrs. Sowerby, of Chaldicotes, than she can be in her present position.”\n\n“If she cared about being married, I suppose she could be a peer’s wife to-morrow.”\n\n“But I don’t think she cares about being a peer’s wife. A needy peer might perhaps win her in the way that I propose to you; but then a needy peer would not know how to set about it. Needy peers have tried—half a dozen I have no doubt—and have failed, because they have pretended that they were in love with her. It may be difficult, but your only chance is to tell her the truth.”\n\n“And where shall I do it?”\n\n“Here if you choose; but her own house will be better.”\n\n“But I never can see her there—at least, not alone. I believe that she never is alone. She always keeps a lot of people round her in order to stave off her lovers. Upon my word, Harriet, I think I’ll give it up. It is impossible that I should make such a declaration to her as that you propose.”\n\n“Faint heart, Nat—you know the rest.”\n\n“But the poet never alluded to such wooing as that you have suggested. I suppose I had better begin with a schedule of my debts, and make reference, if she doubts me, to Fothergill, the sheriff’s officers, and the Tozer family.”\n\n“She will not doubt you, on that head; nor will she be a bit surprised.”\n\nThen there was again a pause, during which Mr. Sowerby still walked up and down the room, thinking whether or no he might possibly have any chance of success in so hazardous an enterprise.\n\n“I tell you what, Harriet,” at last he said; “I wish you’d do it for me.”\n\n“Well,” said she, “if you really mean it, I will make the attempt.”\n\n“I am sure of this, that I shall never make it myself. I positively should not have the courage to tell her in so many words, that I wanted to marry her for her money.”\n\n“Well, Nat, I will attempt it. At any rate, I am not afraid of her. She and I are excellent friends, and, to tell the truth, I think I like her better than any other woman that I know; but I never should have been intimate with her, had it not been for your sake.”\n\n“And now you will have to quarrel with her, also for my sake?”\n\n“Not at all. You’ll find that whether she accedes to my proposition or not, we shall continue friends. I do not think that she would die for me—nor I for her. But as the world goes we suit each other. Such a little trifle as this will not break our loves.”\n\nAnd so it was settled. On the following day Mrs. Harold Smith was to find an opportunity of explaining the whole matter to Miss Dunstable, and was to ask that lady to share her fortune—some incredible number of thousands of pounds—with the bankrupt member for West Barsetshire, who in return was to bestow on her—himself and his debts.\n\nMrs. Harold Smith had spoken no more than the truth in saying that she and Miss Dunstable suited one another. And she had not improperly described their friendship. They were not prepared to die, one for the sake of the other. They had said nothing to each other of mutual love and affection. They never kissed, or cried, or made speeches, when they met or when they parted. There was no great benefit for which either had to be grateful to the other; no terrible injury which either had forgiven. But they suited each other; and this, I take it, is the secret of most of our pleasantest intercourse in the world.\n\nAnd it was almost grievous that they should suit each other, for Miss Dunstable was much the worthier of the two, had she but known it herself. It was almost to be lamented that she should have found herself able to live with Mrs. Harold Smith on terms that were perfectly satisfactory to herself. Mrs. Harold Smith was worldly, heartless—to all the world but her brother—and, as has been above hinted, almost dishonest. Miss Dunstable was not worldly, though it was possible that her present style of life might make her so; she was affectionate, fond of truth, and prone to honesty, if those around would but allow her to exercise it. But she was fond of ease and humour, sometimes of wit that might almost be called broad, and she had a thorough love of ridiculing the world’s humbugs. In all these propensities Mrs. Harold Smith indulged her.\n\nUnder these circumstances they were now together almost every day. It had become quite a habit with Mrs. Harold Smith to have herself driven early in the forenoon to Miss Dunstable’s house; and that lady, though she could never be found alone by Mr. Sowerby, was habitually so found by his sister. And after that they would go out together, or each separately, as fancy or the business of the day might direct them. Each was easy to the other in this alliance, and they so managed that they never trod on each other’s corns.\n\nOn the day following the agreement made between Mr. Sowerby and Mrs. Harold Smith, that lady as usual called on Miss Dunstable, and soon found herself alone with her friend in a small room which the heiress kept solely for her own purposes. On special occasions persons of various sorts were there admitted; occasionally a parson who had a church to build, or a dowager laden with the last morsel of town slander, or a poor author who could not get due payment for the efforts of his brain, or a poor governess on whose feeble stamina the weight of the world had borne too hardly. But men who by possibility could be lovers did not make their way thither, nor women who could be bores. In these latter days, that is, during the present London season, the doors of it had been oftener opened to Mrs. Harold Smith than to any other person.\n\nAnd now the effort was to be made with the object of which all this intimacy had been effected. As she came thither in her carriage, Mrs. Harold Smith herself was not altogether devoid of that sinking of the heart which is so frequently the forerunner of any difficult and hazardous undertaking. She had declared that she would feel no fear in making the little proposition. But she did feel something very like it; and when she made her entrance into the little room she certainly wished that the work was done and over.\n\n“How is poor Mr. Smith to-day?” asked Miss Dunstable, with an air of mock condolence, as her friend seated herself in her accustomed easy-chair. The downfall of the gods was as yet a history hardly three days old, and it might well be supposed that the late lord of the Petty Bag had hardly recovered from his misfortune.\n\n“Well, he is better, I think, this morning; at least I should judge so from the manner in which he confronted his eggs. But still I don’t like the way he handles the carving-knife. I am sure he is always thinking of Mr. Supplehouse at those moments.”\n\n“Poor man! I mean Supplehouse. After all, why shouldn’t he follow his trade as well as another? Live and let live, that’s what I say.”\n\n“Ay, but it’s kill and let kill with him. That is what Horace says. However, I am tired of all that now, and I came here to-day to talk about something else.”\n\n“I rather like Mr. Supplehouse myself,” exclaimed Miss Dunstable. “He never makes any bones about the matter. He has a certain work to do, and a certain cause to serve—namely, his own; and in order to do that work, and serve that cause, he uses such weapons as God has placed in his hands.”\n\n“That’s what the wild beasts do.”\n\n“And where will you find men honester than they? The tiger tears you up because he is hungry and wants to eat you. That’s what Supplehouse does. But there are so many among us tearing up one another without any excuse of hunger. The mere pleasure of destroying is reason enough.”\n\n“Well, my dear, my mission to you to-day is certainly not one of destruction, as you will admit when you hear it. It is one, rather, very absolutely of salvation. I have come to make love to you.”\n\n“Then the salvation, I suppose, is not for myself,” said Miss Dunstable.\n\nIt was quite clear to Mrs. Harold Smith that Miss Dunstable had immediately understood the whole purport of this visit, and that she was not in any great measure surprised. It did not seem from the tone of the heiress’s voice, or from the serious look which at once settled on her face, that she would be prepared to give a very ready compliance. But then great objects can only be won with great efforts.\n\n“That’s as may be,” said Mrs. Harold Smith. “For you and another also, I hope. But I trust, at any rate, that I may not offend you?”\n\n“Oh, laws, no; nothing of that kind ever offends me now.”\n\n“Well, I suppose you’re used to it.”\n\n“Like the eels, my dear. I don’t mind it the least in the world—only sometimes, you know, it is a little tedious.”\n\n“I’ll endeavour to avoid that, so I may as well break the ice at once. You know enough of Nathaniel’s affairs to be aware that he is not a very rich man.”\n\n“Since you do ask me about it, I suppose there’s no harm in saying that I believe him to be a very poor man.”\n\n“Not the least harm in the world, but just the reverse. Whatever may come of this, my wish is that the truth should be told scrupulously on all sides; the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth.”\n\n“Magna est veritas,” said Miss Dunstable. “The Bishop of Barchester taught me as much Latin as that at Chaldicotes; and he did add some more, but there was a long word, and I forgot it.”\n\n“The bishop was quite right, my dear, I’m sure. But if you go to your Latin, I’m lost. As we were just now saying, my brother’s pecuniary affairs are in a very bad state. He has a beautiful property of his own, which has been in the family for I can’t say how many centuries—long before the Conquest, I know.”\n\n“I wonder what my ancestors were then?”\n\n“It does not much signify to any of us,” said Mrs. Harold Smith, with a moral shake of her head, “what our ancestors were; but it’s a sad thing to see an old property go to ruin.”\n\n“Yes, indeed; we none of us like to see our property going to ruin, whether it be old or new. I have some of that sort of feeling already, although mine was only made the other day out of an apothecary’s shop.”\n\n“God forbid that I should ever help you to ruin it,” said Mrs. Harold Smith. “I should be sorry to be the means of your losing a ten-pound note.”\n\n“Magna est veritas, as the dear bishop said,” exclaimed Miss Dunstable. “Let us have the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth, as we agreed just now.”\n\nMrs. Harold Smith did begin to find that the task before her was difficult. There was a hardness about Miss Dunstable when matters of business were concerned on which it seemed almost impossible to make any impression. It was not that she had evinced any determination to refuse the tender of Mr. Sowerby’s hand; but she was so painfully resolute not to have dust thrown in her eyes! Mrs. Harold Smith had commenced with a mind fixed upon avoiding what she called humbug; but this sort of humbug had become so prominent a part of her usual rhetoric, that she found it very hard to abandon it.\n\n“And that’s what I wish,” said she. “Of course my chief object is to secure my brother’s happiness.”\n\n“That’s very unkind to poor Mr. Harold Smith.”\n\n“Well, well, well—you know what I mean.”\n\n“Yes, I think I do know what you mean. Your brother is a gentleman of good family, but of no means.”\n\n“Not quite so bad as that.”\n\n“Of embarrassed means, then, or anything that you will; whereas I am a lady of no family, but of sufficient wealth. You think that if you brought us together and made a match of it, it would be a very good thing for—for whom?” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n“Yes, exactly,” said Mrs. Harold Smith.\n\n“For which of us? Remember the bishop now and his nice little bit of Latin.”\n\n“For Nathaniel then,” said Mrs. Harold Smith, boldly. “It would be a very good thing for him.” And a slight smile came across her face as she said it. “Now that’s honest, or the mischief is in it.”\n\n“Yes, that’s honest enough. And did he send you here to tell me this?”\n\n“Well, he did that, and something else.”\n\n“And now let’s have the something else. The really important part, I have no doubt, has been spoken.”\n\n“No, by no means, by no means all of it. But you are so hard on one, my dear, with your running after honesty, that one is not able to tell the real facts as they are. You make one speak in such a bald, naked way.”\n\n“Ah, you think that anything naked must be indecent; even truth.”\n\n“I think it is more proper-looking, and better suited, too, for the world’s work, when it goes about with some sort of a garment on it. We are so used to a leaven of falsehood in all we hear and say, now-a-days, that nothing is more likely to deceive us than the absolute truth. If a shopkeeper told me that his wares were simply middling, of course, I should think that they were not worth a farthing. But all that has nothing to do with my poor brother. Well, what was I saying?”\n\n“You were going to tell me how well he would use me, no doubt.”\n\n“Something of that kind.”\n\n“That he wouldn’t beat me; or spend all my money if I managed to have it tied up out of his power; or look down on me with contempt because my father was an apothecary! Was not that what you were going to say?”\n\n“I was going to tell you that you might be more happy as Mrs. Sowerby of Chaldicotes than you can be as Miss Dunstable—”\n\n“Of Mount Lebanon. And had Mr. Sowerby no other message to send?—nothing about love, or anything of that sort? I should like, you know, to understand what his feelings are before I take such a leap.”\n\n“I do believe he has as true a regard for you as any man of his age ever does have—”\n\n“For any woman of mine. That is not putting it in a very devoted way certainly; but I am glad to see that you remember the bishop’s maxim.”\n\n“What would you have me say? If I told you that he was dying for love, you would say, I was trying to cheat you; and now because I don’t tell you so, you say that he is wanting in devotion. I must say you are hard to please.”\n\n“Perhaps I am, and very unreasonable into the bargain. I ought to ask no questions of the kind when your brother proposes to do me so much honour. As for my expecting the love of a man who condescends to wish to be my husband, that, of course, would be monstrous. What right can I have to think that any man should love me? It ought to be enough for me to know that as I am rich, I can get a husband. What business can such as I have to inquire whether the gentleman who would so honour me really would like my company, or would only deign to put up with my presence in his household?”\n\n“Now, my dear Miss Dunstable—”\n\n“Of course I am not such an ass as to expect that any gentleman should love me; and I feel that I ought to be obliged to your brother for sparing me the string of complimentary declarations which are usual on such occasions. He, at any rate, is not tedious—or rather you on his behalf; for no doubt his own time is so occupied with his parliamentary duties that he cannot attend to this little matter himself. I do feel grateful to him; and perhaps nothing more will be necessary than to give him a schedule of the property, and name an early day for putting him in possession.”\n\nMrs. Smith did feel that she was rather badly used. This Miss Dunstable, in their mutual confidences, had so often ridiculed the love-making grimaces of her mercenary suitors—had spoken so fiercely against those who had persecuted her, not because they had desired her money, but on account of their ill-judgment in thinking her to be a fool—that Mrs. Smith had a right to expect that the method she had adopted for opening the negotiation would be taken in a better spirit. Could it be possible, after all, thought Mrs. Smith to herself, that Miss Dunstable was like other women, and that she did like to have men kneeling at her feet? Could it be the case that she had advised her brother badly, and that it would have been better for him to have gone about his work in the old-fashioned way? “They are very hard to manage,” said Mrs. Harold Smith to herself, thinking of her own sex.\n\n“He was coming here himself,” said she, “but I advised him not to do so.”\n\n“That was so kind of you.”\n\n“I thought that I could explain to you more openly and more freely, what his intentions really are.”\n\n“Oh! I have no doubt that they are honourable,” said Miss Dunstable. “He does not want to deceive me in that way, I am quite sure.”\n\nIt was impossible to help laughing, and Mrs. Harold Smith did laugh. “Upon my word, you would provoke a saint,” said she.\n\n“I am not likely to get into any such company by the alliance that you are now suggesting to me. There are not many saints usually at Chaldicotes, I believe;—always excepting my dear bishop and his wife.”\n\n“But, my dear, what am I to say to Nathaniel?”\n\n“Tell him, of course, how much obliged to him I am.”\n\n“Do listen to me one moment. I daresay that I have done wrong to speak to you in such a bold, unromantic way.”\n\n“Not at all. The truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth. That’s what we agreed upon. But one’s first efforts in any line are always apt to be a little uncouth.”\n\n“I will send Nathaniel to you himself.”\n\n“No, do not do so. Why torment either him or me? I do like your brother; in a certain way I like him much. But no earthly consideration would induce me to marry him. Is it not so glaringly plain that he would marry me for my money only, that you have not even dared to suggest any other reason?”\n\n“Of course it would have been nonsense to say that he had no regard whatever towards your money.”\n\n“Of course it would—absolute nonsense. He is a poor man with a good position, and he wants to marry me because I have got that which he wants. But, my dear, I do not want that which he has got, and therefore the bargain would not be a fair one.”\n\n“But he would do his very best to make you happy.”\n\n“I am so much obliged to him; but you see, I am very happy as I am. What should I gain?”\n\n“A companion whom you confess that you like.”\n\n“Ah! but I don’t know that I should like too much even of such a companion as your brother. No, my dear—it won’t do. Believe me when I tell you, once for all, that it won’t do.”\n\n“Do you mean, then, Miss Dunstable, that you’ll never marry?”\n\n“To-morrow—if I met any one that I fancied, and he would have me. But I rather think that any that I may fancy won’t have me. In the first place, if I marry any one, the man must be quite indifferent to money.”\n\n“Then you’ll not find him in this world, my dear.”\n\n“Very possibly not,” said Miss Dunstable.\n\nAll that was further said upon the subject need not be here repeated. Mrs. Harold Smith did not give up her cause quite at once, although Miss Dunstable had spoken so plainly. She tried to explain how eligible would be her friend’s situation as mistress of Chaldicotes, when Chaldicotes should owe no penny to any man: and went so far as to hint that the master of Chaldicotes, if relieved of his embarrassments and known as a rich man, might in all probability be found worthy of a peerage when the gods should return to Olympus. Mr. Harold Smith, as a cabinet minister, would, of course, do his best. But it was all of no use. “It’s not my destiny,” said Miss Dunstable, “and therefore do not press it any longer.”\n\n“But we shall not quarrel,” said Mrs. Harold Smith, almost tenderly.\n\n“Oh, no—why should we quarrel?”\n\n“And you won’t look glum at my brother?”\n\n“Why should I look glum at him? But, Mrs. Smith, I’ll do more than not looking glum at him. I do like you, and I do like your brother, and if I can in any moderate way assist him in his difficulties, let him tell me so.”\n\nSoon after this, Mrs. Harold Smith went her way. Of course, she declared in a very strong manner that her brother could not think of accepting from Miss Dunstable any such pecuniary assistance as that offered—and, to give her her due, such was the feeling of her mind at the moment; but as she went to meet her brother and gave him an account of this interview, it did occur to her that possibly Miss Dunstable might be a better creditor than the Duke of Omnium for the Chaldicotes property.\n\n# CHAPTER XXV. NON-IMPULSIVE.\n\nIt cannot be held as astonishing, that that last decision on the part of the giants in the matter of the two bishoprics should have disgusted Archdeacon Grantly. He was a politician, but not a politician as they were. As is the case with all exoteric men, his political eyes saw a short way only, and his political aspirations were as limited. When his friends came into office, that bishop bill, which as the original product of his enemies had been regarded by him as being so pernicious—for was it not about to be made law in order that other Proudies and such like might be hoisted up into high places and large incomes, to the terrible detriment of the Church?—that bishop bill, I say, in the hands of his friends, had appeared to him to be a means of almost national salvation. And then, how great had been the good fortune of the giants in this matter! Had they been the originators of such a measure they would not have had a chance of success; but now—now that the two bishops were falling into their mouths out of the weak hands of the gods, was not their success ensured? So Dr. Grantly had girded up his loins and marched up to the fight, almost regretting that the triumph would be so easy. The subsequent failure was very trying to his temper as a party man.\n\nIt always strikes me that the supporters of the Titans are in this respect much to be pitied. The giants themselves, those who are actually handling Pelion and breaking their shins over the lower rocks of Ossa, are always advancing in some sort towards the councils of Olympus. Their highest policy is to snatch some ray from heaven. Why else put Pelion on Ossa, unless it be that a furtive hand, making its way through Jove’s windows, may pluck forth a thunderbolt or two, or some article less destructive, but of manufacture equally divine? And in this consists the wisdom of the higher giants—that, in spite of their mundane antecedents, theories, and predilections, they can see that articles of divine manufacture are necessary. But then they never carry their supporters with them. Their whole army is an army of martyrs. “For twenty years I have stuck to them, and see how they have treated me!” Is not that always the plaint of an old giant-slave? “I have been true to my party all my life, and where am I now?” he says. Where, indeed, my friend? Looking about you, you begin to learn that you cannot describe your whereabouts. I do not marvel at that. No one finds himself planted at last in so terribly foul a morass, as he would fain stand still for ever on dry ground.\n\nDr. Grantly was disgusted; and although he was himself too true and thorough in all his feelings, to be able to say aloud that any Giant was wrong, still he had a sad feeling within his heart that the world was sinking from under him. He was still sufficiently exoteric to think that a good stand-up fight in a good cause was a good thing. No doubt he did wish to be Bishop of Westminster, and was anxious to compass that preferment by any means that might appear to him to be fair. And why not? But this was not the end of his aspirations. He wished that the giants might prevail in everything, in bishoprics as in all other matters; and he could not understand that they should give way on the very first appearance of a skirmish. In his open talk he was loud against many a god; but in his heart of hearts he was bitter enough against both Porphyrion and Orion.\n\n“My dear doctor, it would not do;—not in this session; it would not indeed.” So had spoken to him a half-fledged but especially esoteric young monster-cub at the Treasury, who considered himself as up to all the dodges of his party, and regarded the army of martyrs who supported it as a rather heavy, but very useful collection of fogeys. Dr. Grantly had not cared to discuss the matter with the half-fledged monster-cub. The best licked of all the monsters, the Giant most like a god of them all, had said a word or two to him; and he also had said a word or two to that Giant. Porphyrion had told him that the bishop bill would not do; and he, in return, speaking with warm face, and blood in his cheeks, had told Porphyrion that he saw no reason why the bill should not do. The courteous Giant had smiled as he shook his ponderous head, and then the archdeacon had left him, unconsciously shaking some dust from his shoes, as he paced the passages of the Treasury chambers for the last time. As he walked back to his lodgings in Mount Street, many thoughts, not altogether bad in their nature, passed through his mind. Why should he trouble himself about a bishopric? Was he not well as he was, in his rectory down at Plumstead? Might it not be ill for him at his age to transplant himself into new soil, to engage in new duties, and live among new people? Was he not useful at Barchester, and respected also; and might it not be possible, that up there at Westminster, he might be regarded merely as a tool with which other men could work? He had not quite liked the tone of that specially esoteric young monster-cub, who had clearly regarded him as a distinguished fogey from the army of martyrs. He would take his wife back to Barsetshire, and there live contented with the good things which Providence had given him.\n\nThose high political grapes had become sour, my sneering friends will say. Well? Is it not a good thing that grapes should become sour which hang out of reach? Is he not wise who can regard all grapes as sour which are manifestly too high for his hand? Those grapes of the Treasury bench, for which gods and giants fight, suffering so much when they are forced to abstain from eating, and so much more when they do eat,—those grapes are very sour to me. I am sure that they are indigestible, and that those who eat them undergo all the ills which the Revalenta Arabica is prepared to cure. And so it was now with the archdeacon. He thought of the strain which would have been put on his conscience had he come up there to sit in London as Bishop of Westminster; and in this frame of mind he walked home to his wife.\n\nDuring the first few moments of his interview with her all his regrets had come back upon him. Indeed, it would have hardly suited for him then to have preached this new doctrine of rural contentment. The wife of his bosom, whom he so fully trusted—had so fully loved—wished for grapes that hung high upon the wall, and he knew that it was past his power to teach her at the moment to drop her ambition. Any teaching that he might effect in that way, must come by degrees. But before many minutes were over he had told her of her fate and of his own decision. “So we had better go back to Plumstead,” he said; and she had not dissented.\n\n“I am sorry for poor Griselda’s sake,” Mrs. Grantly had remarked later in the evening, when they were again together.\n\n“But I thought she was to remain with Lady Lufton?”\n\n“Well; so she will, for a little time. There is no one with whom I would so soon trust her out of my own care as with Lady Lufton. She is all that one can desire.”\n\n“Exactly; and as far as Griselda is concerned, I cannot say that I think she is to be pitied.”\n\n“Not to be pitied, perhaps,” said Mrs. Grantly. “But, you see, archdeacon, Lady Lufton, of course, has her own views.”\n\n“Her own views?”\n\n“It is hardly any secret that she is very anxious to make a match between Lord Lufton and Griselda. And though that might be a very proper arrangement if it were fixed—”\n\n“Lord Lufton marry Griselda!” said the archdeacon, speaking quick and raising his eyebrows. His mind had as yet been troubled by but few thoughts respecting his child’s future establishment. “I had never dreamt of such a thing.”\n\n“But other people have done more than dream of it, archdeacon. As regards the match itself, it would, I think, be unobjectionable. Lord Lufton will not be a very rich man, but his property is respectable, and as far as I can learn his character is on the whole good. If they like each other, I should be contented with such a marriage. But, I must own, I am not quite satisfied at the idea of leaving her all alone with Lady Lufton. People will look on it as a settled thing, when it is not settled—and very probably may not be settled; and that will do the poor girl harm. She is very much admired; there can be no doubt of that; and Lord Dumbello—”\n\nThe archdeacon opened his eyes still wider. He had had no idea that such a choice of sons-in-law was being prepared for him; and, to tell the truth, was almost bewildered by the height of his wife’s ambition. Lord Lufton, with his barony and twenty thousand a year, might be accepted as just good enough; but failing him there was an embryo marquis, whose fortune would be more than ten times as great, all ready to accept his child! And then he thought, as husbands sometimes will think, of Susan Harding as she was when he had gone a-courting to her under the elms before the house in the warden’s garden at Barchester, and of dear old Mr. Harding, his wife’s father, who still lived in humble lodgings in that city; and as he thought, he wondered at and admired the greatness of that lady’s mind.\n\n“I never can forgive Lord De Terrier,” said the lady, connecting various points together in her own mind.\n\n“That’s nonsense,” said the archdeacon. “You must forgive him.”\n\n“And I must confess that it annoys me to leave London at present.”\n\n“It can’t be helped,” said the archdeacon, somewhat gruffly; for he was a man who, on certain points, chose to have his own way—and had it.\n\n“Oh, no: I know it can’t be helped,” said Mrs. Grantly, in a tone which implied a deep injury. “I know it can’t be helped. Poor Griselda!” And then they went to bed.\n\nOn the next morning Griselda came to her, and in an interview that was strictly private, her mother said more to her than she had ever yet spoken, as to the prospects of her future life. Hitherto, on this subject, Mrs. Grantly had said little or nothing. She would have been well pleased that her daughter should have received the incense of Lord Lufton’s vows—or, perhaps, as well pleased had it been the incense of Lord Dumbello’s vows—without any interference on her part. In such case her child, she knew, would have told her with quite sufficient eagerness, and the matter in either case would have been arranged as a very pretty love match. She had no fear of any impropriety or of any rashness on Griselda’s part. She had thoroughly known her daughter when she boasted that Griselda would never indulge in an unauthorized passion. But as matters now stood, with those two strings to her bow, and with that Lufton-Grantly alliance treaty in existence—of which she, Griselda herself, knew nothing—might it not be possible that the poor child should stumble through want of adequate direction? Guided by these thoughts, Mrs. Grantly had resolved to say a few words before she left London. So she wrote a line to her daughter, and Griselda reached Mount Street at two o’clock in Lady Lufton’s carriage, which, during the interview, waited for her at the beer-shop round the corner.\n\n“And papa won’t be Bishop of Westminster?” said the young lady, when the doings of the giants had been sufficiently explained to make her understand that all those hopes were over.\n\n“No, my dear; at any rate not now.”\n\n“What a shame! I thought it was all settled. What’s the good, mamma, of Lord De Terrier being prime minister, if he can’t make whom he likes a bishop?”\n\n“I don’t think that Lord De Terrier has behaved at all well to your father. However, that’s a long question, and we can’t go into it now.”\n\n“How glad those Proudies will be!”\n\nGriselda would have talked by the hour on this subject had her mother allowed her, but it was necessary that Mrs. Grantly should go to other matters. She began about Lady Lufton, saying what a dear woman her ladyship was; and then went on to say that Griselda was to remain in London as long as it suited her friend and hostess to stay there with her; but added, that this might probably not be very long, as it was notorious that Lady Lufton, when in London, was always in a hurry to get back to Framley.\n\n“But I don’t think she is in such a hurry this year, mamma,” said Griselda, who in the month of May preferred Bruton Street to Plumstead, and had no objection whatever to the coronet on the panels of Lady Lufton’s coach.\n\nAnd then Mrs. Grantly commenced her explanation—very cautiously. “No, my dear, I daresay she is not in such a hurry this year,—that is, as long as you remain with her.”\n\n“I am sure she is very kind.”\n\n“She is very kind, and you ought to love her very much. I know I do. I have no friend in the world for whom I have a greater regard than for Lady Lufton. It is that which makes me so happy to leave you with her.”\n\n“All the same, I wish that you and papa had remained up; that is, if they had made papa a bishop.”\n\n“It’s no good thinking of that now, my dear. What I particularly wanted to say to you was this: I think you should know what are the ideas which Lady Lufton entertains.”\n\n“Her ideas!” said Griselda, who had never troubled herself much in thinking about other people’s thoughts.\n\n“Yes, Griselda. While you were staying down at Framley Court, and also, I suppose, since you have been up here in Bruton Street, you must have seen a good deal of—Lord Lufton.”\n\n“He doesn’t come very often to Bruton Street,—that is to say, not very often.”\n\n“H-m,” ejaculated Mrs. Grantly, very gently. She would willingly have repressed the sound altogether, but it had been too much for her. If she found reason to think that Lady Lufton was playing her false, she would immediately take her daughter away, break up the treaty, and prepare for the Hartletop alliance. Such were the thoughts that ran through her mind. But she knew all the while that Lady Lufton was not false. The fault was not with Lady Lufton; nor, perhaps, altogether with Lord Lufton. Mrs. Grantly had understood the full force of the complaint which Lady Lufton had made against her daughter; and though she had of course defended her child, and on the whole had defended her successfully, yet she confessed to herself that Griselda’s chance of a first-rate establishment would be better if she were a little more impulsive. A man does not wish to marry a statue, let the statue be ever so statuesque. She could not teach her daughter to be impulsive, any more than she could teach her to be six feet high; but might it not be possible to teach her to seem so? The task was a very delicate one, even for a mother’s hand.\n\n“Of course he cannot be at home now as much as he was down in the country, when he was living in the same house,” said Mrs. Grantly, whose business it was to take Lord Lufton’s part at the present moment. “He must be at his club, and at the House of Lords, and in twenty places.”\n\n“He is very fond of going to parties, and he dances beautifully.”\n\n“I am sure he does. I have seen as much as that myself, and I think I know some one with whom he likes to dance.” And the mother gave her daughter a loving little squeeze.\n\n“Do you mean me, mamma?”\n\n“Yes, I do mean you, my dear. And is it not true? Lady Lufton says that he likes dancing with you better than with any one else in London.”\n\n“I don’t know,” said Griselda, looking down upon the ground.\n\nMrs. Grantly thought that this upon the whole was rather a good opening. It might have been better. Some point of interest more serious in its nature than that of a waltz might have been found on which to connect her daughter’s sympathies with those of her future husband. But any point of interest was better than none; and it is so difficult to find points of interest in persons who by their nature are not impulsive.\n\n“Lady Lufton says so, at any rate,” continued Mrs. Grantly, ever so cautiously. “She thinks that Lord Lufton likes no partner better. What do you think yourself, Griselda?”\n\n“I don’t know, mamma.”\n\n“But young ladies must think of such things, must they not?”\n\n“Must they, mamma?”\n\n“I suppose they do, don’t they? The truth is, Griselda, that Lady Lufton thinks that if— Can you guess what it is she thinks?”\n\n“No, mamma.” But that was a fib on Griselda’s part.\n\n“She thinks that my Griselda would make the best possible wife in the world for her son; and I think so too. I think that her son will be a very fortunate man if he can get such a wife. And now what do you think, Griselda?”\n\n“I don’t think anything, mamma.”\n\nBut that would not do. It was absolutely necessary that she should think, and absolutely necessary that her mother should tell her so. Such a degree of unimpulsiveness as this would lead to—heaven knows what results! Lufton-Grantly treaties and Hartletop interests would be all thrown away upon a young lady who would not think anything of a noble suitor sighing for her smiles. Besides, it was not natural. Griselda, as her mother knew, had never been a girl of headlong feeling; but still she had had her likes and her dislikes. In that matter of the bishopric she was keen enough; and no one could evince a deeper interest in the subject of a well-made new dress than Griselda Grantly. It was not possible that she should be indifferent as to her future prospects, and she must know that those prospects depended mainly on her marriage. Her mother was almost angry with her, but nevertheless she went on very gently:\n\n“You don’t think anything! But, my darling, you must think. You must make up your mind what would be your answer if Lord Lufton were to propose to you. That is what Lady Lufton wishes him to do.”\n\n“But he never will, mamma.”\n\n“And if he did?”\n\n“But I’m sure he never will. He doesn’t think of such a thing at all—and—and—”\n\n“And what, my dear?”\n\n“I don’t know, mamma.”\n\n“Surely you can speak out to me, dearest! All I care about is your happiness. Both Lady Lufton and I think that it would be a happy marriage if you both cared for each other enough. She thinks that he is fond of you. But if he were ten times Lord Lufton I would not tease you about it if I thought that you could not learn to care about him. What was it you were going to say, my dear?”\n\n“Lord Lufton thinks a great deal more of Lucy Robarts than he does of—of—of any one else, I believe,” said Griselda, showing now some little animation by her manner, “dumpy little black thing that she is.”\n\n“Lucy Robarts!” said Mrs. Grantly, taken by surprise at finding that her daughter was moved by such a passion as jealousy, and feeling also perfectly assured that there could not be any possible ground for jealousy in such a direction as that. “Lucy Robarts, my dear! I don’t suppose Lord Lufton ever thought of speaking to her, except in the way of civility.”\n\n“Yes, he did, mamma! Don’t you remember at Framley?”\n\nMrs. Grantly began to look back in her mind, and she thought she did remember having once observed Lord Lufton talking in rather a confidential manner with the parson’s sister. But she was sure that there was nothing in it. If that was the reason why Griselda was so cold to her proposed lover, it would be a thousand pities that it should not be removed.\n\n“Now you mention her, I do remember the young lady,” said Mrs. Grantly, “a dark girl, very low, and without much figure. She seemed to me to keep very much in the background.”\n\n“I don’t know much about that, mamma.”\n\n“As far as I saw her, she did. But, my dear Griselda, you should not allow yourself to think of such a thing. Lord Lufton, of course, is bound to be civil to any young lady in his mother’s house, and I am quite sure that he has no other idea whatever with regard to Miss Robarts. I certainly cannot speak as to her intellect, for I do not think she opened her mouth in my presence; but—”\n\n“Oh! she has plenty to say for herself, when she pleases. She’s a sly little thing.”\n\n“But, at any rate, my dear, she has no personal attractions whatever, and I do not at all think that Lord Lufton is a man to be taken by—by—by anything that Miss Robarts might do or say.”\n\nAs those words “personal attractions” were uttered, Griselda managed so to turn her neck as to catch a side view of herself in one of the mirrors on the wall, and then she bridled herself up, and made a little play with her eyes, and looked, as her mother thought, very well. “It is all nothing to me, mamma, of course,” she said.\n\n“Well, my dear, perhaps not. I don’t say that it is. I do not wish to put the slightest constraint upon your feelings. If I did not have the most thorough dependence on your good sense and high principles, I should not speak to you in this way. But as I have, I thought it best to tell you that both Lady Lufton and I should be well pleased if we thought that you and Lord Lufton were fond of each other.”\n\n“I am sure he never thinks of such a thing, mamma.”\n\n“And as for Lucy Robarts, pray get that idea out of your head; if not for your sake, then for his. You should give him credit for better taste.”\n\nBut it was not so easy to take anything out of Griselda’s head that she had once taken into it. “As for tastes, mamma, there is no accounting for them,” she said; and then the colloquy on that subject was over. The result of it on Mrs. Grantly’s mind was a feeling amounting almost to a conviction in favour of the Dumbello interest.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVI. IMPULSIVE.\n\nI trust my readers will all remember how Puck the pony was beaten during that drive to Hogglestock. It may be presumed that Puck himself on that occasion did not suffer much. His skin was not so soft as Mrs. Robarts’s heart. The little beast was full of oats and all the good things of this world, and therefore, when the whip touched him, he would dance about and shake his little ears, and run on at a tremendous pace for twenty yards, making his mistress think that he had endured terrible things. But, in truth, during those whippings Puck was not the chief sufferer.\n\nLucy had been forced to declare—forced by the strength of her own feelings, and by the impossibility of assenting to the propriety of a marriage between Lord Lufton and Miss Grantly—, she had been forced to declare that she did care about Lord Lufton as much as though he were her brother. She had said all this to herself,—nay, much more than this—very often. But now she had said it out loud to her sister-in-law; and she knew that what she had said was remembered, considered, and had, to a certain extent, become the cause of altered conduct. Fanny alluded very seldom to the Luftons in casual conversation, and never spoke about Lord Lufton, unless when her husband made it impossible that she should not speak of him. Lucy had attempted on more than one occasion to remedy this, by talking about the young lord in a laughing and, perhaps, half-jeering way; she had been sarcastic as to his hunting and shooting, and had boldly attempted to say a word in joke about his love for Griselda. But she felt that she had failed; that she had failed altogether as regarded Fanny; and that as to her brother, she would more probably be the means of opening his eyes, than have any effect in keeping them closed. So she gave up her efforts and spoke no further word about Lord Lufton. Her secret had been told, and she knew that it had been told.\n\nAt this time the two ladies were left a great deal alone together in the drawing-room at the parsonage; more, perhaps, than had ever yet been the case since Lucy had been there. Lady Lufton was away, and therefore the almost daily visit to Framley Court was not made; and Mark in these days was a great deal at Barchester, having, no doubt, very onerous duties to perform before he could be admitted as one of that chapter. He went into, what he was pleased to call residence, almost at once. That is, he took his month of preaching, aiding also in some slight and very dignified way, in the general Sunday morning services. He did not exactly live at Barchester, because the house was not ready. That at least was the assumed reason. The chattels of Dr. Stanhope, the late prebendary, had not been as yet removed, and there was likely to be some little delay, creditors asserting their right to them. This might have been very inconvenient to a gentleman anxiously expecting the excellent house which the liberality of past ages had provided for his use; but it was not so felt by Mr. Robarts. If Dr. Stanhope’s family or creditors would keep the house for the next twelve months, he would be well pleased. And by this arrangement he was enabled to get through his first month of absence from the church of Framley without any notice from Lady Lufton, seeing that Lady Lufton was in London all the time. This also was convenient, and taught our young prebendary to look on his new preferment more favourably than he had hitherto done.\n\nFanny and Lucy were thus left much alone: and as out of the full head the mouth speaks, so is the full heart more prone to speak at such periods of confidence as these. Lucy, when she first thought of her own state, determined to endow herself with a powerful gift of reticence. She would never tell her love, certainly; but neither would she let concealment feed on her damask cheek, nor would she ever be found for a moment sitting like Patience on a monument. She would fight her own fight bravely within her own bosom, and conquer her enemy altogether. She would either preach, or starve, or weary her love into subjection, and no one should be a bit the wiser. She would teach herself to shake hands with Lord Lufton without a quiver, and would be prepared to like his wife amazingly—unless indeed that wife should be Griselda Grantly. Such were her resolutions; but at the end of the first week they were broken into shivers and scattered to the winds.\n\nThey had been sitting in the house together the whole of one wet day; and as Mark was to dine in Barchester with the dean, they had had dinner early, eating with the children almost in their laps. It is so that ladies do, when their husbands leave them to themselves. It was getting dusk towards evening, and they were still sitting in the drawing-room, the children now having retired, when Mrs. Robarts for the fifth time since her visit to Hogglestock began to express her wish that she could do some good to the Crawleys,—to Grace Crawley in particular, who, standing up there at her father’s elbow, learning Greek irregular verbs, had appeared to Mrs. Robarts to be an especial object of pity.\n\n“I don’t know how to set about it,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\nNow any allusion to that visit to Hogglestock always drove Lucy’s mind back to the consideration of the subject which had most occupied it at the time. She at such moments remembered how she had beaten Puck, and how in her half-bantering but still too serious manner she had apologized for doing so, and had explained the reason. And therefore she could not interest herself about Grace Crawley as vividly as she should have done.\n\n“No; one never does,” she said.\n\n“I was thinking about it all that day as I drove home,” said Fanny. “The difficulty is this: What can we do with her?”\n\n“Exactly,” said Lucy, remembering the very point of the road at which she had declared that she did like Lord Lufton very much.\n\n“If we could have her here for a month or so and then send her to school;—but I know Mr. Crawley would not allow us to pay for her schooling.”\n\n“I don’t think he would,” said Lucy, with her thoughts far removed from Mr. Crawley and his daughter Grace.\n\n“And then we should not know what to do with her; should we?”\n\n“No; you would not.”\n\n“It would never do to have the poor girl about the house here, with no one to teach her anything. Mark would not teach her Greek verbs, you know.”\n\n“I suppose not.”\n\n“Lucy, you are not attending to a word I say to you, and I don’t think you have for the last hour. I don’t believe you know what I am talking about.”\n\n“Oh, yes, I do—Grace Crawley; I’ll try and teach her if you like, only I don’t know anything myself.”\n\n“That’s not what I mean at all, and you know I would not ask you to take such a task as that on yourself. But I do think you might talk it over with me.”\n\n“Might I? very well; I will. What is it? Oh, Grace Crawley—you want to know who is to teach her the irregular Greek verbs. Oh dear, Fanny, my head does ache so: pray don’t be angry with me.” And then Lucy, throwing herself back on the sofa, put one hand up painfully to her forehead, and altogether gave up the battle.\n\nMrs. Robarts was by her side in a moment. “Dearest Lucy, what is it makes your head ache so often now? you used not to have those headaches.”\n\n“It’s because I’m growing stupid: never mind. We will go on about poor Grace. It would not do to have a governess, would it?”\n\n“I can see that you are not well, Lucy,” said Mrs. Robarts, with a look of deep concern. “What is it, dearest? I can see that something is the matter.”\n\n“Something the matter! No, there’s not; nothing worth talking of. Sometimes I think I’ll go back to Devonshire and live there. I could stay with Blanche for a time, and then get a lodging in Exeter.”\n\n“Go back to Devonshire!” and Mrs. Robarts looked as though she thought that her sister-in-law was going mad. “Why do you want to go away from us? This is to be your own, own home, always now.”\n\n“Is it? Then I am in a bad way. Oh dear, oh dear, what a fool I am! What an idiot I’ve been! Fanny, I don’t think I can stay here; and I do so wish I’d never come. I do—I do—I do, though you look at me so horribly,” and jumping up she threw herself into her sister-in-law’s arms and began kissing her violently. “Don’t pretend to be wounded, for you know that I love you. You know that I could live with you all my life, and think you were perfect—as you are; but—”\n\n“Has Mark said anything?”\n\n“Not a word,—not a ghost of a syllable. It is not Mark; oh, Fanny!”\n\n“I am afraid I know what you mean,” said Mrs. Robarts in a low tremulous voice, and with deep sorrow painted on her face.\n\n“Of course you do; of course you know; you have known it all along: since that day in the pony-carriage. I knew that you knew it. You do not dare to mention his name: would not that tell me that you know it? And I, I am hypocrite enough for Mark; but my hypocrisy won’t pass muster before you. And, now, had I not better go to Devonshire?”\n\n“Dearest, dearest Lucy.”\n\n“Was I not right about that labelling? O heavens! what idiots we girls are! That a dozen soft words should have bowled me over like a ninepin, and left me without an inch of ground to call my own. And I was so proud of my own strength; so sure that I should never be missish, and spoony, and sentimental! I was so determined to like him as Mark does, or you—”\n\n“I shall not like him at all if he has spoken words to you that he should not have spoken.”\n\n“But he has not.” And then she stopped a moment to consider. “No, he has not. He never said a word to me that would make you angry with him if you knew of it. Except, perhaps, that he called me Lucy; and that was my fault, not his.”\n\n“Because you talked of soft words.”\n\n“Fanny, you have no idea what an absolute fool I am, what an unutterable ass. The soft words of which I tell you were of the kind which he speaks to you when he asks you how the cow gets on which he sent you from Ireland, or to Mark about Ponto’s shoulder. He told me that he knew papa, and that he was at school with Mark, and that as he was such good friends with you here at the parsonage, he must be good friends with me too. No; it has not been his fault. The soft words which did the mischief were such as those. But how well his mother understood the world! In order to have been safe, I should not have dared to look at him.”\n\n“But, dearest Lucy—”\n\n“I know what you are going to say, and I admit it all. He is no hero. There is nothing on earth wonderful about him. I never heard him say a single word of wisdom, or utter a thought that was akin to poetry. He devotes all his energies to riding after a fox or killing poor birds, and I never heard of his doing a single great action in my life. And yet—”\n\nFanny was so astounded by the way her sister-in-law went on, that she hardly knew how to speak. “He is an excellent son, I believe,” at last she said.\n\n“Except when he goes to Gatherum Castle. I’ll tell you what he has: he has fine straight legs, and a smooth forehead, and a good-humoured eye, and white teeth. Was it possible to see such a catalogue of perfections, and not fall down, stricken to the very bone? But it was not that that did it all, Fanny. I could have stood against that. I think I could at least. It was his title that killed me. I had never spoken to a lord before. Oh, me! what a fool, what a beast I have been!” And then she burst out into tears.\n\nMrs. Robarts, to tell the truth, could hardly understand poor Lucy’s ailment. It was evident enough that her misery was real; but yet she spoke of herself and her sufferings with so much irony, with so near an approach to joking, that it was very hard to tell how far she was in earnest. Lucy, too, was so much given to a species of badinage which Mrs. Robarts did not always quite understand, that the latter was afraid sometimes to speak out what came uppermost to her tongue. But now that Lucy was absolutely in tears, and was almost breathless with excitement, she could not remain silent any longer. “Dearest Lucy, pray do not speak in that way; it will all come right. Things always do come right when no one has acted wrongly.”\n\n“Yes, when nobody has done wrongly. That’s what papa used to call begging the question. But I’ll tell you what, Fanny; I will not be beaten. I will either kill myself or get through it. I am so heartily self-ashamed that I owe it to myself to fight the battle out.”\n\n“To fight what battle, dearest?”\n\n“This battle. Here, now, at the present moment, I could not meet Lord Lufton. I should have to run like a scared fowl if he were to show himself within the gate; and I should not dare to go out of the house, if I knew that he was in the parish.”\n\n“I don’t see that, for I am sure you have not betrayed yourself.”\n\n“Well, no; as for myself, I believe I have done the lying and the hypocrisy pretty well. But, dearest Fanny, you don’t know half; and you cannot and must not know.”\n\n“But I thought you said there had been nothing whatever between you.”\n\n“Did I? Well, to you I have not said a word that was not true. I said that he had spoken nothing that it was wrong for him to say. It could not be wrong—. But never mind. I’ll tell you what I mean to do. I have been thinking of it for the last week—only I shall have to tell Mark.”\n\n“If I were you I would tell him all.”\n\n“What, Mark! If you do, Fanny, I’ll never, never, never speak to you again. Would you—when I have given you all my heart in true sisterly love?”\n\nMrs. Robarts had to explain that she had not proposed to tell anything to Mark herself, and was persuaded, moreover, to give a solemn promise that she would not tell anything to him unless specially authorized to do so.\n\n“I’ll go into a home, I think,” continued Lucy. “You know what those homes are?” Mrs. Robarts assured her that she knew very well, and then Lucy went on: “A year ago I should have said that I was the last girl in England to think of such a life, but I do believe now that it would be the best thing for me. And then I’ll starve myself, and flog myself, and in that way I’ll get back my own mind and my own soul.”\n\n“Your own soul, Lucy!” said Mrs. Robarts, in a tone of horror.\n\n“Well, my own heart, if you like it better; but I hate to hear myself talking about hearts. I don’t care for my heart. I’d let it go—with this young popinjay lord or anyone else, so that I could read, and talk, and walk, and sleep, and eat, without always feeling that I was wrong here—here—here,” and she pressed her hand vehemently against her side. “What is it that I feel, Fanny? Why am I so weak in body that I cannot take exercise? Why cannot I keep my mind on a book for one moment? Why can I not write two sentences together? Why should every mouthful that I eat stick in my throat? Oh, Fanny, is it his legs, think you, or is it his title?”\n\nThrough all her sorrow,—and she was very sorrowful,—Mrs. Robarts could not help smiling. And, indeed, there was every now and then something even in Lucy’s look that was almost comic. She acted the irony so well with which she strove to throw ridicule on herself! “Do laugh at me,” she said. “Nothing on earth will do me so much good as that; nothing, unless it be starvation and a whip. If you would only tell me that I must be a sneak and an idiot to care for a man because he is good-looking and a lord!”\n\n“But that has not been the reason. There is a great deal more in Lord Lufton than that; and since I must speak, dear Lucy, I cannot but say that I should not wonder at your being in love with him, only—only that—”\n\n“Only what? Come, out with it. Do not mince matters, or think that I shall be angry with you because you scold me.”\n\n“Only that I should have thought that you would have been too guarded to have—have cared for any gentleman till—till he had shown that he cared for you.”\n\n“Guarded! Yes, that’s it; that’s just the word. But it’s he that should have been guarded. He should have had a fire-guard hung before him—or a love-guard, if you will. Guarded! Was I not guarded, till you all would drag me out? Did I want to go there? And when I was there, did I not make a fool of myself, sitting in a corner, and thinking how much better placed I should have been down in the servants’ hall. Lady Lufton—she dragged me out, and then cautioned me, and then, then— Why is Lady Lufton to have it all her own way? Why am I to be sacrificed for her? I did not want to know Lady Lufton, or any one belonging to her.”\n\n“I cannot think that you have any cause to blame Lady Lufton, nor, perhaps, to blame anybody very much.”\n\n“Well, no, it has been all my own fault; though for the life of me, Fanny, going back and back, I cannot see where I took the first false step. I do not know where I went wrong. One wrong thing I did, and it is the only thing that I do not regret.”\n\n“What was that, Lucy?”\n\n“I told him a lie.”\n\nMrs. Robarts was altogether in the dark, and feeling that she was so, she knew that she could not give counsel as a friend or a sister. Lucy had begun by declaring—so Mrs. Robarts thought—that nothing had passed between her and Lord Lufton but words of most trivial import, and yet she now accused herself of falsehood, and declared that that falsehood was the only thing which she did not regret!\n\n“I hope not,” said Mrs. Robarts. “If you did, you were very unlike yourself.”\n\n“But I did, and were he here again, speaking to me in the same way, I should repeat it. I know I should. If I did not, I should have all the world on me. You would frown on me, and be cold. My darling Fanny, how would you look if I really displeasured you?”\n\n“I don’t think you will do that, Lucy.”\n\n“But if I told him the truth I should, should I not? Speak now. But no, Fanny, you need not speak. It was not the fear of you; no, nor even of her: though Heaven knows that her terrible glumness would be quite unendurable.”\n\n“I cannot understand you, Lucy. What truth or what untruth can you have told him if, as you say, there has been nothing between you but ordinary conversation?”\n\nLucy then got up from the sofa, and walked twice the length of the room before she spoke. Mrs. Robarts had all the ordinary curiosity—I was going to say, of a woman, but I mean to say, of humanity; and she had, moreover, all the love of a sister. She was both curious and anxious, and remained sitting where she was, silent, and with her eyes fixed on her companion.\n\n“Did I say so?” Lucy said at last. “No, Fanny; you have mistaken me: I did not say that. Ah, yes, about the cow and the dog. All that was true. I was telling you of what his soft words had been while I was becoming such a fool. Since that he has said more.”\n\n“What more has he said, Lucy?”\n\n“I yearn to tell you, if only I can trust you;” and Lucy knelt down at the feet of Mrs. Robarts, looking up into her face and smiling through the remaining drops of her tears. “I would fain tell you, but I do not know you yet,—whether you are quite true. I could be true,—true against all the world, if my friend told me. I will tell you, Fanny, if you say that you can be true. But if you doubt yourself, if you must whisper all to Mark—then let us be silent.”\n\nThere was something almost awful in this to Mrs. Robarts. Hitherto, since their marriage, hardly a thought had passed through her mind which she had not shared with her husband. But now all this had come upon her so suddenly, that she was unable to think whether it would be well that she should become the depositary of such a secret,—not to be mentioned to Lucy’s brother, not to be mentioned to her own husband. But who ever yet was offered a secret and declined it? Who at least ever declined a love secret? What sister could do so? Mrs. Robarts therefore gave the promise, smoothing Lucy’s hair as she did so, and kissing her forehead and looking into her eyes, which, like a rainbow, were the brighter for her tears. “And what has he said to you, Lucy?”\n\n“What? Only this, that he asked me to be his wife.”\n\n“Lord Lufton proposed to you?”\n\n“Yes; proposed to me. It is not credible, is it? You cannot bring yourself to believe that such a thing happened, can you?” And Lucy rose again to her feet, as the idea of the scorn with which she felt that others would treat her—with which she herself treated herself—made the blood rise to her cheek. “And yet it is not a dream. I think that it is not a dream. I think that he really did.”\n\n“Think, Lucy!”\n\n“Well, I may say that I am sure.”\n\n“A gentleman would not make you a formal proposal, and leave you in doubt as to what he meant.”\n\n“Oh dear, no. There was no doubt at all of that kind; none in the least. Mr. Smith in asking Miss Jones to do him the honour of becoming Mrs. Smith never spoke more plainly. I was alluding to the possibility of having dreamt it all.”\n\n“Lucy!”\n\n“Well, it was not a dream. Here, standing here, on this very spot—on that flower of the carpet—he begged me a dozen times to be his wife. I wonder whether you and Mark would let me cut it out and keep it.”\n\n“And what answer did you make to him?”\n\n“I lied to him and told him that I did not love him.”\n\n“You refused him?”\n\n“Yes; I refused a live lord. There is some satisfaction in having that to think of, is there not? Fanny, was I wicked to tell that falsehood?”\n\n“And why did you refuse him?”\n\n“Why? Can you ask? Think what it would have been to go down to Framley Court, and to tell her ladyship in the course of conversation that I was engaged to her son. Think of Lady Lufton. But yet it was not that, Fanny. Had I thought that it was good for him, that he would not have repented, I would have braved anything—for his sake. Even your frown, for you would have frowned. You would have thought it sacrilege for me to marry Lord Lufton! You know you would.”\n\nMrs. Robarts hardly knew how to say what she thought, or indeed what she ought to think. It was a matter on which much meditation would be required before she could give advice, and there was Lucy expecting counsel from her at that very moment. If Lord Lufton really loved Lucy Robarts, and was loved by Lucy Robarts, why should not they two become man and wife? And yet she did feel that it would be—perhaps not sacrilege, as Lucy had said, but something almost as troublesome. What would Lady Lufton say, or think, or feel? What would she say, and think, and feel as to that parsonage from which so deadly a blow would fall upon her? Would she not accuse the vicar and the vicar’s wife of the blackest ingratitude? Would life be endurable at Framley under such circumstances as those?\n\n“What you tell me so surprises me, that I hardly as yet know how to speak about it,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“It was amazing, was it not? He must have been insane at the time; there can be no other excuse made for him. I wonder whether there is anything of that sort in the family?”\n\n“What; madness?” said Mrs. Robarts, quite in earnest.\n\n“Well, don’t you think he must have been mad when such an idea as that came into his head? But you don’t believe it; I can see that. And yet it is as true as heaven. Standing exactly here, on this spot, he said that he would persevere till I accepted his love. I wonder what made me specially observe that both his feet were within the lines of that division.”\n\n“And you would not accept his love?”\n\n“No; I would have nothing to say to it. Look you, I stood here, and putting my hand upon my heart,—for he bade me to do that,—I said that I could not love him.”\n\n“And what then?”\n\n“He went away,—with a look as though he were heart-broken. He crept away slowly, saying that he was the most wretched soul alive. For a minute I believed him, and could almost have called him back. But, no, Fanny; do not think that I am over proud, or conceited about my conquest. He had not reached the gate before he was thanking God for his escape.”\n\n“That I do not believe.”\n\n“But I do; and I thought of Lady Lufton too. How could I bear that she should scorn me, and accuse me of stealing her son’s heart? I know that it is better as it is; but tell me—is a falsehood always wrong, or can it be possible that the end should justify the means? Ought I to have told him the truth, and to have let him know that I could almost kiss the ground on which he stood?”\n\nThis was a question for the doctors which Mrs. Robarts would not take upon herself to answer. She would not make that falsehood matter of accusation, but neither would she pronounce for it any absolution. In that matter Lucy must regulate her own conscience. “And what shall I do next?” said Lucy, still speaking in a tone that was half tragic and half jeering.\n\n“Do?” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“Yes, something must be done. If I were a man I should go to Switzerland, of course; or, as the case is a bad one, perhaps as far as Hungary. What is it that girls do? they don’t die now-a-days, I believe.”\n\n“Lucy, I do not believe that you care for him one jot. If you were in love you would not speak of it like that.”\n\n“There, there. That’s my only hope. If I could laugh at myself till it had become incredible to you, I also, by degrees, should cease to believe that I had cared for him. But, Fanny, it is very hard. If I were to starve, and rise before daybreak, and pinch myself, or do some nasty work,—clean the pots and pans and the candlesticks; that I think would do the most good. I have got a piece of sack-cloth, and I mean to wear that, when I have made it up.”\n\n“You are joking now, Lucy, I know.”\n\n“No, by my word; not in the spirit of what I am saying. How shall I act upon my heart, if I do not do it through the blood and the flesh?”\n\n“Do you not pray that God will give you strength to bear these troubles?”\n\n“But how is one to word one’s prayer, or how even to word one’s wishes? I do not know what is the wrong that I have done. I say it boldly; in this matter I cannot see my own fault. I have simply found that I have been a fool.”\n\nIt was now quite dark in the room, or would have been so to any one entering it afresh. They had remained there talking till their eyes had become accustomed to the gloom, and would still have remained, had they not suddenly been disturbed by the sound of a horse’s feet.\n\n“There is Mark,” said Fanny, jumping up and running to the bell, that lights might be ready when he should enter.\n\n“I thought he remained in Barchester to-night.”\n\n“And so did I; but he said it might be doubtful. What shall we do if he has not dined?”\n\nThat, I believe, is always the first thought in the mind of a good wife when her husband returns home. Has he had his dinner? What can I give him for dinner? Will he like his dinner? Oh dear, oh dear! there is nothing in the house but cold mutton. But on this occasion the lord of the mansion had dined, and came home radiant with good-humour, and owing, perhaps, a little of his radiance to the dean’s claret. “I have told them,” said he, “that they may keep possession of the house for the next two months, and they have agreed to that arrangement.”\n\n“That is very pleasant,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“And I don’t think we shall have so much trouble about the dilapidations after all.”\n\n“I am very glad of that,” said Mrs. Robarts. But nevertheless she was thinking much more of Lucy than of the house in Barchester Close.\n\n“You won’t betray me,” said Lucy, as she gave her sister-in-law a parting kiss at night.\n\n“No; not unless you give me permission.”\n\n“Ah; I shall never do that.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXVII. SOUTH AUDLEY STREET.\n\nThe Duke of Omnium had notified to Mr. Fothergill his wish that some arrangement should be made about the Chaldicotes mortgages, and Mr. Fothergill had understood what the duke meant as well as though his instructions had been written down with all a lawyer’s verbosity. The duke’s meaning was this, that Chaldicotes was to be swept up and garnered, and made part and parcel of the Gatherum property. It had seemed to the duke that that affair between his friend and Miss Dunstable was hanging fire, and, therefore, it would be well that Chaldicotes should be swept up and garnered. And, moreover, tidings had come into the western division of the county that young Frank Gresham of Boxall Hill was in treaty with the Government for the purchase of all that Crown property called the Chace of Chaldicotes. It had been offered to the duke, but the duke had given no definite answer. Had he got his money back from Mr. Sowerby, he could have forestalled Mr. Gresham; but now that did not seem to be probable, and his grace was resolved that either the one property or the other should be duly garnered. Therefore Mr. Fothergill went up to town, and therefore Mr. Sowerby was, most unwillingly, compelled to have a business interview with Mr. Fothergill. In the meantime, since last we saw him, Mr. Sowerby had learned from his sister the answer which Miss Dunstable had given to his proposition, and knew that he had no further hope in that direction.\n\nThere was no further hope thence of absolute deliverance, but there had been a tender of money services. To give Mr. Sowerby his due, he had at once declared that it would be quite out of the question that he should now receive any assistance of that sort from Miss Dunstable; but his sister had explained to him that it would be a mere business transaction; that Miss Dunstable would receive her interest; and that, if she would be content with four per cent., whereas the duke received five, and other creditors six, seven, eight, ten, and heaven only knows how much more, it might be well for all parties. He, himself, understood, as well as Fothergill had done, what was the meaning of the duke’s message. Chaldicotes was to be gathered up and garnered, as had been done with so many another fair property lying in those regions. It was to be swallowed whole, and the master was to walk out from his old family hall, to leave the old woods that he loved, to give up utterly to another the parks and paddocks and pleasant places which he had known from his earliest infancy, and owned from his earliest manhood.\n\nThere can be nothing more bitter to a man than such a surrender. What, compared to this, can be the loss of wealth to one who has himself made it, and brought it together, but has never actually seen it with his bodily eyes? Such wealth has come by one chance, and goes by another: the loss of it is part of the game which the man is playing; and if he cannot lose as well as win, he is a poor, weak, cowardly creature. Such men, as a rule, do know how to bear a mind fairly equal to adversity. But to have squandered the acres which have descended from generation to generation; to be the member of one’s family that has ruined that family; to have swallowed up in one’s own maw all that should have graced one’s children, and one’s grandchildren! It seems to me that the misfortunes of this world can hardly go beyond that!\n\nMr. Sowerby, in spite of his recklessness and that dare-devil gaiety which he knew so well how to wear and use, felt all this as keenly as any man could feel it. It had been absolutely his own fault. The acres had come to him all his own, and now, before his death, every one of them would have gone bodily into that greedy maw. The duke had bought up nearly all the debts which had been secured upon the property, and now could make a clean sweep of it. Sowerby, when he received that message from Mr. Fothergill, knew well that this was intended; and he knew well also, that when once he should cease to be Mr. Sowerby of Chaldicotes, he need never again hope to be returned as member for West Barsetshire. This world would for him be all over. And what must such a man feel when he reflects that this world is for him all over?\n\nOn the morning in question he went to his appointment, still bearing a cheerful countenance. Mr. Fothergill, when in town on such business as this, always had a room at his service in the house of Messrs. Gumption and Gagebee, the duke’s London law agents, and it was thither that Mr. Sowerby had been summoned. The house of business of Messrs. Gumption and Gagebee was in South Audley Street; and it may be said that there was no spot on the whole earth which Mr. Sowerby so hated as he did the gloomy, dingy back sitting-room up-stairs in that house. He had been there very often, but had never been there without annoyance. It was a horrid torture-chamber, kept for such dread purposes as these, and no doubt had been furnished, and papered, and curtained with the express object of finally breaking down the spirits of such poor country gentlemen as chanced to be involved. Everything was of a brown crimson,—of a crimson that had become brown. Sunlight, real genial light of the sun, never made its way there, and no amount of candles could illumine the gloom of that brownness. The windows were never washed; the ceiling was of a dark brown; the old Turkey carpet was thick with dust, and brown withal. The ungainly office-table, in the middle of the room, had been covered with black leather, but that was now brown. There was a bookcase full of dingy brown law books in a recess on one side of the fireplace, but no one had touched them for years, and over the chimney-piece hung some old legal pedigree table, black with soot. Such was the room which Mr. Fothergill always used in the business house of Messrs. Gumption and Gagebee, in South Audley Street, near to Park Lane.\n\nI once heard this room spoken of by an old friend of mine, one Mr. Gresham of Greshamsbury, the father of Frank Gresham, who was now about to purchase that part of the Chace of Chaldicotes which belonged to the Crown. He also had had evil days, though now happily they were past and gone; and he, too, had sat in that room, and listened to the voice of men who were powerful over his property, and intended to use that power. The idea which he left on my mind was much the same as that which I had entertained, when a boy, of a certain room in the castle of Udolpho. There was a chair in that Udolpho room in which those who sat were dragged out limb by limb, the head one way and the legs another; the fingers were dragged off from the hands, and the teeth out from the jaws, and the hair off the head, and the flesh from the bones, and the joints from their sockets, till there was nothing left but a lifeless trunk seated in the chair. Mr. Gresham, as he told me, always sat in the same seat, and the tortures he suffered when so seated, the dislocations of his property which he was forced to discuss, the operations on his very self which he was forced to witness, made me regard that room as worse than the chamber of Udolpho. He, luckily—a rare instance of good fortune—had lived to see all his bones and joints put together again, and flourishing soundly; but he never could speak of the room without horror.\n\n“No consideration on earth,” he once said to me, very solemnly,—“I say none, should make me again enter that room.” And indeed this feeling was so strong with him, that from the day when his affairs took a turn he would never even walk down South Audley Street. On the morning in question into this torture-chamber Mr. Sowerby went, and there, after some two or three minutes, he was joined by Mr. Fothergill.\n\nMr. Fothergill was, in one respect, like to his friend Sowerby. He enacted two altogether different persons on occasions which were altogether different. Generally speaking, with the world at large, he was a jolly, rollicking, popular man, fond of eating and drinking, known to be devoted to the duke’s interests, and supposed to be somewhat unscrupulous, or at any rate hard, when they were concerned; but in other respects a good-natured fellow; and there was a report about that he had once lent somebody money, without charging him interest or taking security. On the present occasion Sowerby saw at a glance that he had come thither with all the aptitudes and appurtenances of his business about him. He walked into the room with a short, quick step; there was no smile on his face as he shook hands with his old friend; he brought with him a box laden with papers and parchments, and he had not been a minute in the room before he was seated in one of the old dingy chairs.\n\n“How long have you been in town, Fothergill?” said Sowerby, still standing with his back against the chimney. He had resolved on only one thing—that nothing should induce him to touch, look at, or listen to any of those papers. He knew well enough that no good would come of that. He also had his own lawyer, to see that he was pilfered according to rule.\n\n“How long? Since the day before yesterday. I never was so busy in my life. The duke, as usual, wants to have everything done at once.”\n\n“If he wants to have all that I owe him paid at once, he is like to be out in his reckoning.”\n\n“Ah, well; I’m glad you are ready to come quickly to business, because it’s always best. Won’t you come and sit down here?”\n\n“No, thank you; I’ll stand.”\n\n“But we shall have to go through these figures, you know.”\n\n“Not a figure, Fothergill. What good would it do? None to me, and none to you either, as I take it; if there is anything wrong, Potter’s fellows will find it out. What is it the duke wants?”\n\n“Well; to tell the truth, he wants his money.”\n\n“In one sense, and that the main sense, he has got it. He gets his interest regularly, does not he?”\n\n“Pretty well for that, seeing how times are. But, Sowerby, that’s nonsense. You understand the duke as well as I do, and you know very well what he wants. He has given you time, and if you had taken any steps towards getting the money, you might have saved the property.”\n\n“A hundred and eighty thousand pounds! What steps could I take to get that? Fly a bill, and let Tozer have it to get cash on it in the City!”\n\n“We hoped you were going to marry.”\n\n“That’s all off.”\n\n“Then I don’t think you can blame the duke for looking for his own. It does not suit him to have so large a sum standing out any longer. You see, he wants land, and will have it. Had you paid off what you owed him, he would have purchased the Crown property; and now, it seems, young Gresham has bid against him, and is to have it. This has riled him, and I may as well tell you fairly, that he is determined to have either money or marbles.”\n\n“You mean that I am to be dispossessed.”\n\n“Well, yes; if you choose to call it so. My instructions are to foreclose at once.”\n\n“Then I must say the duke is treating me most uncommonly ill.”\n\n“Well, Sowerby, I can’t see it.”\n\n“I can, though. He has his money like clock-work; and he has bought up these debts from persons who would have never disturbed me as long as they got their interest.”\n\n“Haven’t you had the seat?”\n\n“The seat! and is it expected that I am to pay for that?”\n\n“I don’t see that any one is asking you to pay for it. You are like a great many other people that I know. You want to eat your cake and have it. You have been eating it for the last twenty years, and now you think yourself very ill-used because the duke wants to have his turn.”\n\n“I shall think myself very ill-used if he sells me out—worse than ill-used. I do not want to use strong language, but it will be more than ill-usage. I can hardly believe that he really means to treat me in that way.”\n\n“It is very hard that he should want his own money!”\n\n“It is not his money that he wants. It is my property.”\n\n“And has he not paid for it? Have you not had the price of your property? Now, Sowerby, it is of no use for you to be angry; you have known for the last three years what was coming on you as well as I did. Why should the duke lend you money without an object? Of course he has his own views. But I do say this; he has not hurried you; and had you been able to do anything to save the place you might have done it. You have had time enough to look about you.”\n\nSowerby still stood in the place in which he had first fixed himself, and now for awhile he remained silent. His face was very stern, and there was in his countenance none of those winning looks which often told so powerfully with his young friends,—which had caught Lord Lufton and had charmed Mark Robarts. The world was going against him, and things around him were coming to an end. He was beginning to perceive that he had in truth eaten his cake, and that there was now little left for him to do,—unless he chose to blow out his brains. He had said to Lord Lufton that a man’s back should be broad enough for any burden with which he himself might load it. Could he now boast that his back was broad enough and strong enough for this burden? But he had even then, at that bitter moment, a strong remembrance that it behoved him still to be a man. His final ruin was coming on him, and he would soon be swept away out of the knowledge and memory of those with whom he had lived. But, nevertheless, he would bear himself well to the last. It was true that he had made his own bed, and he understood the justice which required him to lie upon it.\n\nDuring all this time Fothergill occupied himself with the papers. He continued to turn over one sheet after another, as though he were deeply engaged in money considerations and calculations. But, in truth, during all that time he did not read a word. There was nothing there for him to read. The reading and the writing, and the arithmetic in such matters, are done by underlings—not by such big men as Mr. Fothergill. His business was to tell Sowerby that he was to go. All those records there were of very little use. The duke had the power; Sowerby knew that the duke had the power; and Fothergill’s business was to explain that the duke meant to exercise his power. He was used to the work, and went on turning over the papers and pretending to read them, as though his doing so were of the greatest moment.\n\n“I shall see the duke myself,” Mr. Sowerby said at last, and there was something almost dreadful in the sound of his voice.\n\n“You know that the duke won’t see you on a matter of this kind. He never speaks to anyone about money; you know that as well as I do.”\n\n“By ——, but he shall speak to me. Never speak to anyone about money! Why is he ashamed to speak of it when he loves it so dearly? He shall see me.”\n\n“I have nothing further to say, Sowerby. Of course I shan’t ask his grace to see you; and if you force your way in on him you know what will happen. It won’t be my doing if he is set against you. Nothing that you say to me in that way,—nothing that anybody ever says,—goes beyond myself.”\n\n“I shall manage the matter through my own lawyer,” said Sowerby; and then he took his hat, and, without uttering another word, left the room.\n\nWe know not what may be the nature of that eternal punishment to which those will be doomed who shall be judged to have been evil at the last; but methinks that no more terrible torment can be devised than the memory of self-imposed ruin. What wretchedness can exceed that of remembering from day to day that the race has been all run, and has been altogether lost; that the last chance has gone, and has gone in vain; that the end has come, and with it disgrace, contempt, and self-scorn—disgrace that never can be redeemed, contempt that never can be removed, and self-scorn that will eat into one’s vitals for ever?\n\nMr. Sowerby was now fifty; he had enjoyed his chances in life; and as he walked back, up South Audley Street, he could not but think of the uses he had made of them. He had fallen into the possession of a fine property on the attainment of his manhood; he had been endowed with more than average gifts of intellect; never-failing health had been given to him, and a vision fairly clear in discerning good from evil; and now to what a pass had he brought himself!\n\nAnd that man Fothergill had put all this before him in so terribly clear a light! Now that the day for his final demolishment had arrived, the necessity that he should be demolished—finished away at once, out of sight and out of mind—had not been softened, or, as it were, half hidden, by any ambiguous phrase. “You have had your cake, and eaten it—eaten it greedily. Is not that sufficient for you? Would you eat your cake twice? Would you have a succession of cakes? No, my friend; there is no succession of these cakes for those who eat them greedily. Your proposition is not a fair one, and we who have the whip-hand of you will not listen to it. Be good enough to vanish. Permit yourself to be swept quietly into the dunghill. All that there was about you of value has departed from you; and allow me to say that you are now—rubbish.” And then the ruthless besom comes with irresistible rush, and the rubbish is swept into the pit, there to be hidden for ever from the sight.\n\nAnd the pity of it is this—that a man, if he will only restrain his greed, may eat his cake and yet have it; ay, and in so doing will have twice more the flavour of the cake than he who with gourmandizing maw will devour his dainty all at once. Cakes in this world will grow by being fed on, if only the feeder be not too insatiate. On all which wisdom Mr. Sowerby pondered with sad heart and very melancholy mind as he walked away from the premises of Messrs. Gumption and Gagebee.\n\nHis intention had been to go down to the House after leaving Mr. Fothergill, but the prospect of immediate ruin had been too much for him, and he knew that he was not fit to be seen at once among the haunts of men. And he had intended also to go down to Barchester early on the following morning—only for a few hours, that he might make further arrangements respecting that bill which Robarts had accepted for him. That bill—the second one—had now become due, and Mr. Tozer had been with him.\n\n“Now it ain’t no use in life, Mr. Sowerby,” Tozer had said. “I ain’t got the paper myself, nor didn’t ’old it, not two hours. It went away through Tom Tozer; you knows that, Mr. Sowerby, as well as I do.”\n\nNow, whenever Tozer, Mr. Sowerby’s Tozer, spoke of Tom Tozer, Mr. Sowerby knew that seven devils were being evoked, each worse than the first devil. Mr. Sowerby did feel something like sincere regard, or rather love, for that poor parson whom he had inveigled into mischief, and would fain save him, if it were possible, from the Tozer fang. Mr. Forrest, of the Barchester bank, would probably take up that last five hundred pound bill, on behalf of Mr. Robarts,—only it would be needful that he, Sowerby, should run down and see that this was properly done. As to the other bill—the former and lesser one—as to that, Mr. Tozer would probably be quiet for a while.\n\nSuch had been Sowerby’s programme for these two days; but now—what further possibility was there now that he should care for Robarts, or any other human being; he that was to be swept at once into the dung-heap?\n\nIn this frame of mind he walked up South Audley Street, and crossed one side of Grosvenor Square, and went almost mechanically into Green Street. At the farther end of Green Street, near to Park Lane, lived Mr. and Mrs. Harold Smith.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII. DR. THORNE.\n\nWhen Miss Dunstable met her friends, the Greshams—young Frank Gresham and his wife—at Gatherum Castle, she immediately asked after one Dr. Thorne, who was Mrs. Gresham’s uncle. Dr. Thorne was an old bachelor, in whom both as a man and a doctor Miss Dunstable was inclined to place much confidence. Not that she had ever entrusted the cure of her bodily ailments to Dr. Thorne—for she kept a doctor of her own, Dr. Easyman, for this purpose—and it may moreover be said that she rarely had bodily ailments requiring the care of any doctor. But she always spoke of Dr. Thorne among her friends as a man of wonderful erudition and judgment; and had once or twice asked and acted on his advice in matters of much moment. Dr. Thorne was not a man accustomed to the London world; he kept no house there, and seldom even visited the metropolis; but Miss Dunstable had known him at Greshamsbury, where he lived, and there had for some months past grown up a considerable intimacy between them. He was now staying at the house of his niece, Mrs. Gresham; but the chief reason of his coming up had been a desire expressed by Miss Dunstable, that he should do so. She had wished for his advice; and at the instigation of his niece he had visited London and given it.\n\nThe special piece of business as to which Dr. Thorne had thus been summoned from the bedsides of his country patients, and especially from the bedside of Lady Arabella Gresham, to whose son his niece was married, related to certain large money interests, as to which one might have imagined that Dr. Thorne’s advice would not be peculiarly valuable. He had never been much versed in such matters on his own account, and was knowing neither in the ways of the share market, nor in the prices of land. But Miss Dunstable was a lady accustomed to have her own way, and to be indulged in her own wishes without being called on to give adequate reasons for them.\n\n“My dear,” she had said to young Mrs. Gresham, “if your uncle don’t come up to London now, when I make such a point of it, I shall think that he is a bear and a savage; and I certainly will never speak to him again,—or to Frank—or to you; so you had better see to it.” Mrs. Gresham had not probably taken her friend’s threat as meaning quite all that it threatened. Miss Dunstable habitually used strong language; and those who knew her well, generally understood when she was to be taken as expressing her thoughts by figures of speech. In this instance she had not meant it all; but, nevertheless, Mrs. Gresham had used violent influence in bringing the poor doctor up to London.\n\n“Besides,” said Miss Dunstable, “I have resolved on having the doctor at my conversazione, and if he won’t come of himself, I shall go down and fetch him. I have set my heart on trumping my dear friend Mrs. Proudie’s best card; so I mean to get everybody!”\n\nThe upshot of all this was, that the doctor did come up to town, and remained the best part of a week at his niece’s house in Portman Square—to the great disgust of the Lady Arabella, who conceived that she must die if neglected for three days. As to the matter of business, I have no doubt but that he was of great use. He was possessed of common sense and an honest purpose; and I am inclined to think that they are often a sufficient counterpoise to a considerable amount of worldly experience. If one could have the worldly experience also—! True! but then it is so difficult to get everything. But with that special matter of business we need not have any further concern. We will presume it to have been discussed and completed, and will now dress ourselves for Miss Dunstable’s conversazione.\n\nBut it must not be supposed that she was so poor in genius as to call her party openly by a name borrowed for the nonce from Mrs. Proudie. It was only among her specially intimate friends, Mrs. Harold Smith and some few dozen others, that she indulged in this little joke. There had been nothing in the least pretentious about the card with which she summoned her friends to her house on this occasion. She had merely signified in some ordinary way, that she would be glad to see them as soon after nine o’clock on Thursday evening, the —— instant, as might be convenient. But all the world understood that all the world was to be gathered together at Miss Dunstable’s house on the night in question,—that an effort was to be made to bring together people of all classes, gods and giants, saints and sinners, those rabid through the strength of their morality, such as our dear friend Lady Lufton, and those who were rabid in the opposite direction, such as Lady Hartletop, the Duke of Omnium, and Mr. Sowerby. An orthodox martyr had been caught from the East, and an oily latter-day St. Paul from the other side of the water—to the horror and amazement of Archdeacon Grantly, who had come up all the way from Plumstead to be present on the occasion. Mrs. Grantly also had hankered to be there; but when she heard of the presence of the latter-day St. Paul, she triumphed loudly over her husband, who had made no offer to take her. That Lords Brock and De Terrier were to be at the gathering was nothing. The pleasant king of the gods and the courtly chief of the giants could shake hands with each other in any house with the greatest pleasure; but men were to meet who, in reference to each other, could shake nothing but their heads or their fists. Supplehouse was to be there, and Harold Smith, who now hated his enemy with a hatred surpassing that of women—or even of politicians. The minor gods, it was thought, would congregate together in one room, very bitter in their present state of banishment; and the minor giants in another, terribly loud in their triumph. That is the fault of the giants, who, otherwise, are not bad fellows; they are unable to endure the weight of any temporary success. When attempting Olympus—and this work of attempting is doubtless their natural condition—they scratch and scramble, diligently using both toes and fingers, with a mixture of good-humoured virulence and self-satisfied industry that is gratifying to all parties. But whenever their efforts are unexpectedly, and for themselves unfortunately successful, they are so taken aback that they lose the power of behaving themselves with even gigantesque propriety.\n\nSuch, so great and so various, was to be the intended gathering at Miss Dunstable’s house. She herself laughed, and quizzed herself—speaking of the affair to Mrs. Harold Smith as though it were an excellent joke, and to Mrs. Proudie as though she were simply emulous of rivalling those world-famous assemblies in Gloucester Place; but the town at large knew that an effort was being made, and it was supposed that even Miss Dunstable was somewhat nervous. In spite of her excellent joking it was presumed that she would be unhappy if she failed.\n\nTo Mrs. Frank Gresham she did speak with some little seriousness. “But why on earth should you give yourself all this trouble?” that lady had said, when Miss Dunstable owned that she was doubtful, and unhappy in her doubts, as to the coming of one of the great colleagues of Mr. Supplehouse. “When such hundreds are coming, big wigs and little wigs of all shades, what can it matter whether Mr. Towers be there or not?”\n\nBut Miss Dunstable had answered almost with a screech,—“My dear, it will be nothing without him. You don’t understand; but the fact is, that Tom Towers is everybody and everything at present.”\n\nAnd then, by no means for the first time, Mrs. Gresham began to lecture her friend as to her vanity; in answer to which lecture Miss Dunstable mysteriously hinted, that if she were only allowed her full swing on this occasion,—if all the world would now indulge her, she would— She did not quite say what she would do, but the inference drawn by Mrs. Gresham was this: that if the incense now offered on the altar of Fashion were accepted, Miss Dunstable would at once abandon the pomps and vanities of this wicked world, and all the sinful lusts of the flesh.\n\n“But the doctor will stay, my dear? I hope I may look on that as fixed.”\n\nMiss Dunstable, in making this demand on the doctor’s time, showed an energy quite equal to that with which she invoked the gods that Tom Towers might not be absent. Now, to tell the truth, Dr. Thorne had at first thought it very unreasonable that he should be asked to remain up in London in order that he might be present at an evening party, and had for a while pertinaciously refused; but when he learned that three or four prime ministers were expected, and that it was possible that even Tom Towers might be there in the flesh, his philosophy also had become weak, and he had written to Lady Arabella to say that his prolonged absence for two days further must be endured, and that the mild tonics, morning and evening, might be continued.\n\nBut why should Miss Dunstable be so anxious that Dr. Thorne should be present on this grand occasion? Why, indeed, should she be so frequently inclined to summon him away from his country practice, his compounding board, and his useful ministrations to rural ailments? The doctor was connected with her by no ties of blood. Their friendship, intimate as it was, had as yet been but of short date. She was a very rich woman, capable of purchasing all manner of advice and good counsel, whereas he was so far from being rich, that any continued disturbance to his practice might be inconvenient to him. Nevertheless, Miss Dunstable seemed to have no more compunction in making calls upon his time, than she might have felt had he been her brother. No ideas on this matter suggested themselves to the doctor himself. He was a simple-minded man, taking things as they came, and especially so taking things that came pleasantly. He liked Miss Dunstable, and was gratified by her friendship, and did not think of asking himself whether she had a right to put him to trouble and inconvenience. But such ideas did occur to Mrs. Gresham, the doctor’s niece. Had Miss Dunstable any object, and if so, what object? Was it simply veneration for the doctor, or was it caprice? Was it eccentricity—or could it possibly be love?\n\nIn speaking of the ages of these two friends it may be said in round terms that the lady was well past forty, and that the gentleman was well past fifty. Under such circumstances could it be love? The lady, too, was one who had had offers almost by the dozen,—offers from men of rank, from men of fashion, and from men of power; from men endowed with personal attractions, with pleasant manners, with cultivated tastes, and with eloquent tongues. Not only had she loved none such, but by none such had she been cajoled into an idea that it was possible that she could love them. That Dr. Thorne’s tastes were cultivated, and his manners pleasant, might probably be admitted by three or four old friends in the country who valued him; but the world in London, that world to which Miss Dunstable was accustomed, and which was apparently becoming dearer to her day by day, would not have regarded the doctor as a man likely to become the object of a lady’s passion.\n\nBut nevertheless the idea did occur to Mrs. Gresham. She had been brought up at the elbow of this country practitioner; she had lived with him as though she had been his daughter; she had been for years the ministering angel of his household; and, till her heart had opened to the natural love of womanhood, all her closest sympathies had been with him. In her eyes the doctor was all but perfect; and it did not seem to her to be out of the question that Miss Dunstable should have fallen in love with her uncle.\n\nMiss Dunstable once said to Mrs. Harold Smith that it was possible that she might marry, the only condition then expressed being this, that the man elected should be one who was quite indifferent as to money. Mrs. Harold Smith, who, by her friends, was presumed to know the world with tolerable accuracy, had replied that such a man Miss Dunstable would never find in this world. All this had passed in that half comic vein of banter which Miss Dunstable so commonly used when conversing with such friends as Mrs. Harold Smith; but she had spoken words of the same import more than once to Mrs. Gresham; and Mrs. Gresham, putting two and two together as women do, had made four of the little sum; and, as the final result of the calculation, determined that Miss Dunstable would marry Dr. Thorne if Dr. Thorne would ask her.\n\nAnd then Mrs. Gresham began to bethink herself of two other questions. Would it be well that her uncle should marry Miss Dunstable? and if so, would it be possible to induce him to make such a proposition? After the consideration of many pros and cons, and the balancing of very various arguments, Mrs. Gresham thought that the arrangement on the whole might not be a bad one. For Miss Dunstable she herself had a sincere affection, which was shared by her husband. She had often grieved at the sacrifices Miss Dunstable made to the world, thinking that her friend was falling into vanity, indifference, and an ill mode of life; but such a marriage as this would probably cure all that. And then as to Dr. Thorne himself, to whose benefit were of course applied Mrs. Gresham’s most earnest thoughts in this matter, she could not but think that he would be happier married than he was single. In point of temper, no woman could stand higher than Miss Dunstable; no one had ever heard of her being in an ill humour; and then though Mrs. Gresham was gifted with a mind which was far removed from being mercenary, it was impossible not to feel that some benefit must accrue from the bride’s wealth. Mary Thorne, the present Mrs. Frank Gresham, had herself been a great heiress. Circumstances had weighted her hand with enormous possessions, and hitherto she had not realized the truth of that lesson which would teach us to believe that happiness and riches are incompatible. Therefore she resolved that it might be well if the doctor and Miss Dunstable were brought together.\n\nBut could the doctor be induced to make such an offer? Mrs. Gresham acknowledged a terrible difficulty in looking at the matter from that point of view. Her uncle was fond of Miss Dunstable; but she was sure that an idea of such a marriage had never entered his head; that it would be very difficult—almost impossible—to create such an idea; and that if the idea were there, the doctor could hardly be instigated to make the proposition. Looking at the matter as a whole, she feared that the match was not practicable.\n\nOn the day of Miss Dunstable’s party, Mrs. Gresham and her uncle dined together alone in Portman Square. Mr. Gresham was not yet in Parliament, but an almost immediate vacancy was expected in his division of the county, and it was known that no one could stand against him with any chance of success. This threw him much among the politicians of his party—those giants, namely, whom it would be his business to support—and on this account he was a good deal away from his own house at the present moment.\n\n“Politics make a terrible demand on a man’s time,” he said to his wife; and then went down to dine at his club in Pall Mall with sundry other young philogeants. On men of that class politics do make a great demand—at the hour of dinner and thereabouts.\n\n“What do you think of Miss Dunstable?” said Mrs. Gresham to her uncle, as they sat together over their coffee. She added nothing to the question, but asked it in all its baldness.\n\n“Think about her!” said the doctor. “Well, Mary; what do you think about her? I dare say we think the same.”\n\n“But that’s not the question. What do you think about her? Do you think she’s honest?”\n\n“Honest? Oh, yes, certainly—very honest, I should say.”\n\n“And good-tempered?”\n\n“Uncommonly good-tempered.”\n\n“And affectionate?”\n\n“Well; yes,—and affectionate. I should certainly say that she is affectionate.”\n\n“I’m sure she’s clever.”\n\n“Yes, I think she’s clever.”\n\n“And, and—and womanly in her feelings.” Mrs. Gresham felt that she could not quite say lady-like, though she would fain have done so had she dared.\n\n“Oh, certainly,” said the doctor. “But, Mary, why are you dissecting Miss Dunstable’s character with so much ingenuity?”\n\n“Well, uncle, I will tell you why; because—” and Mrs. Gresham, while she was speaking, got up from her chair, and going round the table to her uncle’s side, put her arm round his neck till her face was close to his, and then continued speaking as she stood behind him out of his sight—“because—I think that Miss Dunstable is—is very fond of you; and that it would make her happy if you would—ask her to be your wife.”\n\n“Mary!” said the doctor, turning round with an endeavour to look his niece in the face.\n\n“I am quite in earnest, uncle—quite in earnest. From little things that she has said, and little things that I have seen, I do believe what I now tell you.”\n\n“And you want me to—”\n\n“Dear uncle; my own one darling uncle, I want you only to do that which will make you—make you happy. What is Miss Dunstable to me compared to you?” And then she stooped down and kissed him.\n\nThe doctor was apparently too much astounded by the intimation given him to make any further immediate reply. His niece, seeing this, left him that she might go and dress; and when they met again in the drawing-room Frank Gresham was with them.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX. MISS DUNSTABLE AT HOME.\n\nMiss Dunstable did not look like a love-lorn maiden, as she stood in a small ante-chamber at the top of her drawing-room stairs receiving her guests. Her house was one of those abnormal mansions, which are to be seen here and there in London, built in compliance rather with the rules of rural architecture, than with those which usually govern the erection of city streets and town terraces. It stood back from its brethren, and alone, so that its owner could walk round it. It was approached by a short carriageway; the chief door was in the back of the building; and the front of the house looked on to one of the parks. Miss Dunstable in procuring it had had her usual luck. It had been built by an eccentric millionnaire at an enormous cost; and the eccentric millionnaire, after living in it for twelve months, had declared that it did not possess a single comfort, and that it was deficient in most of those details which, in point of house accommodation, are necessary to the very existence of man. Consequently the mansion was sold, and Miss Dunstable was the purchaser. Cranbourn House it had been named, and its present owner had made no change in this respect; but the world at large very generally called it Ointment Hall, and Miss Dunstable herself as frequently used that name for it as any other. It was impossible to quiz Miss Dunstable with any success, because she always joined in the joke herself.\n\nNot a word further had passed between Mrs. Gresham and Dr. Thorne on the subject of their last conversation; but the doctor as he entered the lady’s portals amongst a tribe of servants and in a glare of light, and saw the crowd before him and the crowd behind him, felt that it was quite impossible that he should ever be at home there. It might be all right that a Miss Dunstable should live in this way, but it could not be right that the wife of Dr. Thorne should so live. But all this was a matter of the merest speculation, for he was well aware—as he said to himself a dozen times—that his niece had blundered strangely in her reading of Miss Dunstable’s character.\n\nWhen the Gresham party entered the ante-room into which the staircase opened, they found Miss Dunstable standing there surrounded by a few of her most intimate allies. Mrs. Harold Smith was sitting quite close to her; Dr. Easyman was reclining on a sofa against the wall, and the lady who habitually lived with Miss Dunstable was by his side. One or two others were there also, so that a little running conversation was kept up, in order to relieve Miss Dunstable of the tedium which might otherwise be engendered by the work she had in hand. As Mrs. Gresham, leaning on her husband’s arm, entered the room, she saw the back of Mrs. Proudie, as that lady made her way through the opposite door, leaning on the arm of the bishop.\n\nMrs. Harold Smith had apparently recovered from the annoyance which she must no doubt have felt when Miss Dunstable so utterly rejected her suit on behalf of her brother. If any feeling had existed, even for a day, calculated to put a stop to the intimacy between the two ladies, that feeling had altogether died away, for Mrs. Harold Smith was conversing with her friend, quite in the old way. She made some remark on each of the guests as they passed by, and apparently did so in a manner satisfactory to the owner of the house, for Miss Dunstable answered with her kindest smiles, and in that genial, happy tone of voice which gave its peculiar character to her good humour:\n\n“She is quite convinced that you are a mere plagiarist in what you are doing,” said Mrs. Harold Smith, speaking of Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“And so I am. I don’t suppose there can be anything very original now-a-days about an evening party.”\n\n“But she thinks you are copying her.”\n\n“And why not? I copy everybody that I see, more or less. You did not at first begin to wear big petticoats out of your own head? If Mrs. Proudie has any such pride as that, pray don’t rob her of it. Here’s the doctor and the Greshams. Mary, my darling, how are you?” and in spite of all her grandeur of apparel, Miss Dunstable took hold of Mrs. Gresham and kissed her—to the disgust of the dozen-and-a-half of the distinguished fashionable world who were passing up the stairs behind.\n\nThe doctor was somewhat repressed in his mode of address by the communication which had so lately been made to him. Miss Dunstable was now standing on the very top of the pinnacle of wealth, and seemed to him to be not only so much above his reach, but also so far removed from his track in life, that he could not in any way put himself on a level with her. He could neither aspire so high nor descend so low; and thinking of this he spoke to Miss Dunstable as though there were some great distance between them,—as though there had been no hours of intimate friendship down at Greshamsbury. There had been such hours, during which Miss Dunstable and Dr. Thorne had lived as though they belonged to the same world: and this at any rate may be said of Miss Dunstable, that she had no idea of forgetting them.\n\nDr. Thorne merely gave her his hand, and then prepared to pass on.\n\n“Don’t go, doctor,” she said; “for heaven’s sake, don’t go yet. I don’t know when I may catch you if you get in there. I shan’t be able to follow you for the next two hours. Lady Meredith, I am so much obliged to you for coming—your mother will be here, I hope. Oh, I am so glad! From her you know that is quite a favour. You, Sir George, are half a sinner yourself, so I don’t think so much about it.”\n\n“Oh, quite so,” said Sir George; “perhaps rather the largest half.”\n\n“The men divide the world into gods and giants,” said Miss Dunstable. “We women have our divisions also. We are saints or sinners according to our party. The worst of it is, that we rat almost as often as you do.” Whereupon Sir George laughed and passed on.\n\n“I know, doctor, you don’t like this kind of thing,” she continued, “but there is no reason why you should indulge yourself altogether in your own way, more than another—is there, Frank?”\n\n“I am not so sure but he does like it,” said Mr. Gresham. “There are some of your reputed friends whom he owns that he is anxious to see.”\n\n“Are there? Then there is some hope of his ratting too. But he’ll never make a good staunch sinner; will he, Mary? You’re too old to learn new tricks; eh, doctor?”\n\n“I am afraid I am,” said the doctor, with a faint laugh.\n\n“Does Dr. Thorne rank himself among the army of saints?” asked Mrs. Harold Smith.\n\n“Decidedly,” said Miss Dunstable. “But you must always remember that there are saints of different orders; are there not, Mary? and nobody supposes that the Franciscans and the Dominicans agree very well together. Dr. Thorne does not belong to the school of St. Proudie, of Barchester; he would prefer the priestess whom I see coming round the corner of the staircase, with a very famous young novice at her elbow.”\n\n“From all that I can hear, you will have to reckon Miss Grantly among the sinners,” said Mrs. Harold Smith—seeing that Lady Lufton with her young friend was approaching—“unless, indeed, you can make a saint of Lady Hartletop.”\n\nAnd then Lady Lufton entered the room, and Miss Dunstable came forward to meet her with more quiet respect in her manner than she had as yet shown to many of her guests. “I am much obliged to you for coming, Lady Lufton,” she said, “and the more so, for bringing Miss Grantly with you.”\n\nLady Lufton uttered some pretty little speech, during which Dr. Thorne came up and shook hands with her; as did also Frank Gresham and his wife. There was a county acquaintance between the Framley people and the Greshamsbury people, and therefore there was a little general conversation before Lady Lufton passed out of the small room into what Mrs. Proudie would have called the noble suite of apartments. “Papa will be here,” said Miss Grantly; “at least so I understand. I have not seen him yet myself.”\n\n“Oh, yes, he has promised me,” said Miss Dunstable; “and the archdeacon, I know, will keep his word. I should by no means have the proper ecclesiastical balance without him.”\n\n“Papa always does keep his word,” said Miss Grantly, in a tone that was almost severe. She had not at all understood poor Miss Dunstable’s little joke, or at any rate she was too dignified to respond to it.\n\n“I understand that old Sir John is to accept the Chiltern Hundreds at once,” said Lady Lufton, in a half whisper to Frank Gresham. Lady Lufton had always taken a keen interest in the politics of East Barsetshire, and was now desirous of expressing her satisfaction that a Gresham should again sit for the county. The Greshams had been old county members in Barsetshire, time out of mind.\n\n“Oh, yes; I believe so,” said Frank, blushing. He was still young enough to feel almost ashamed of putting himself forward for such high honours.\n\n“There will be no contest, of course,” said Lady Lufton, confidentially. “There seldom is in East Barsetshire, I am happy to say. But if there were, every tenant at Framley would vote on the right side; I can assure you of that. Lord Lufton was saying so to me only this morning.”\n\nFrank Gresham made a pretty little speech in reply, such as young sucking politicians are expected to make; and this, with sundry other small courteous murmurings, detained the Lufton party for a minute or two in the ante-chamber. In the meantime the world was pressing on and passing through to the four or five large reception-rooms—the noble suite, which was already piercing poor Mrs. Proudie’s heart with envy to the very core. “These are the sort of rooms,” she said to herself unconsciously, “which ought to be provided by the country for the use of its bishops.”\n\n“But the people are not brought enough together,” she said to her lord.\n\n“No, no; I don’t think they are,” said the bishop.\n\n“And that is so essential for a conversazione,” continued Mrs. Proudie. “Now in Gloucester Place—.” But we will not record all her adverse criticisms, as Lady Lufton is waiting for us in the ante-room.\n\nAnd now another arrival of moment had taken place;—an arrival indeed of very great moment. To tell the truth, Miss Dunstable’s heart had been set upon having two special persons; and though no stone had been left unturned,—no stone which could be turned with discretion,—she was still left in doubt as to both these two wondrous potentates. At the very moment of which we are now speaking, light and airy as she appeared to be—for it was her character to be light and airy—her mind was torn with doubts. If the wished-for two would come, her evening would be thoroughly successful; but if not, all her trouble would have been thrown away, and the thing would have been a failure; and there were circumstances connected with the present assembly which made Miss Dunstable very anxious that she should not fail. That the two great ones of the earth were Tom Towers of the Jupiter, and the Duke of Omnium, need hardly be expressed in words.\n\nAnd now, at this very moment, as Lady Lufton was making her civil speeches to young Gresham, apparently in no hurry to move on, and while Miss Dunstable was endeavouring to whisper something into the doctor’s ear, which would make him feel himself at home in this new world, a sound was heard which made that lady know that half her wish had at any rate been granted to her. A sound was heard—but only by her own and one other attentive pair of ears. Mrs. Harold Smith had also caught the name, and knew that the duke was approaching.\n\nThere was great glory and triumph in this; but why had his grace come at so unchancy a moment? Miss Dunstable had been fully aware of the impropriety of bringing Lady Lufton and the Duke of Omnium into the same house at the same time; but when she had asked Lady Lufton, she had been led to believe that there was no hope of obtaining the duke; and then, when that hope had dawned upon her, she had comforted herself with the reflection that the two suns, though they might for some few minutes be in the same hemisphere, could hardly be expected to clash, or come across each other’s orbits. Her rooms were large and would be crowded; the duke would probably do little more than walk through them once, and Lady Lufton would certainly be surrounded by persons of her own class. Thus Miss Dunstable had comforted herself. But now all things were going wrong, and Lady Lufton would find herself in close contiguity to the nearest representative of Satanic agency, which, according to her ideas, was allowed to walk this nether English world of ours. Would she scream? or indignantly retreat out of the house?—or would she proudly raise her head, and with outstretched hand and audible voice, boldly defy the devil and all his works? In thinking of these things as the duke approached Miss Dunstable almost lost her presence of mind.\n\nBut Mrs. Harold Smith did not lose hers. “So here at last is the duke,” she said, in a tone intended to catch the express attention of Lady Lufton.\n\nMrs. Smith had calculated that there might still be time for her ladyship to pass on and avoid the interview. But Lady Lufton, if she heard the words, did not completely understand them. At any rate they did not convey to her mind at the moment the meaning they were intended to convey. She paused to whisper a last little speech to Frank Gresham, and then looking round, found that the gentleman who was pressing against her dress was—the Duke of Omnium!\n\nOn this great occasion, when the misfortune could no longer be avoided, Miss Dunstable was by no means beneath herself or her character. She deplored the calamity, but she now saw that it was only left to her to make the best of it. The duke had honoured her by coming to her house, and she was bound to welcome him, though in doing so she should bring Lady Lufton to her last gasp.\n\n“Duke,” she said, “I am greatly honoured by this kindness on the part of your grace. I hardly expected that you would be so good to me.”\n\n“The goodness is all on the other side,” said the duke, bowing over her hand.\n\nAnd then in the usual course of things this would have been all. The duke would have walked on and shown himself, would have said a word or two to Lady Hartletop, to the bishop, to Mr. Gresham, and such like, and would then have left the rooms by another way, and quietly escaped. This was the duty expected from him, and this he would have done, and the value of the party would have been increased thirty per cent. by such doing; but now, as it was, the news-mongers of the West End were likely to get much more out of him.\n\nCircumstances had so turned out that he had absolutely been pressed close against Lady Lufton, and she, when she heard the voice, and was made positively acquainted with the fact of the great man’s presence by Miss Dunstable’s words, turned round quickly, but still with much feminine dignity, removing her dress from the contact. In doing this she was brought absolutely face to face with the duke, so that each could not but look full at the other. “I beg your pardon,” said the duke. They were the only words that had ever passed between them, nor have they spoken to each other since; but simple as they were, accompanied by the little by-play of the speakers, they gave rise to a considerable amount of ferment in the fashionable world. Lady Lufton, as she retreated back on to Dr. Easyman, curtseyed low; she curtseyed low and slowly, and with a haughty arrangement of her drapery that was all her own; but the curtsey, though it was eloquent, did not say half so much,—did not reprobate the habitual iniquities of the duke with a voice nearly as potent as that which was expressed in the gradual fall of her eye and the gradual pressure of her lips. When she commenced her curtsey she was looking full in her foe’s face. By the time that she had completed it her eyes were turned upon the ground, but there was an ineffable amount of scorn expressed in the lines of her mouth. She spoke no word, and retreated, as modest virtue and feminine weakness must ever retreat, before barefaced vice and virile power; but nevertheless she was held by all the world to have had the best of the encounter. The duke, as he begged her pardon, wore in his countenance that expression of modified sorrow which is common to any gentleman who is supposed by himself to have incommoded a lady. But over and above this,—or rather under it,—there was a slight smile of derision, as though it were impossible for him to look upon the bearing of Lady Lufton without some amount of ridicule. All this was legible to eyes so keen as those of Miss Dunstable and Mrs. Harold Smith, and the duke was known to be a master of this silent inward sarcasm; but even by them,—by Miss Dunstable and Mrs. Harold Smith,—it was admitted that Lady Lufton had conquered. When her ladyship again looked up, the duke had passed on; she then resumed the care of Miss Grantly’s hand, and followed in among the company.\n\n“That is what I call unfortunate,” said Miss Dunstable, as soon as both belligerents had departed from the field of battle. “The fates sometimes will be against one.”\n\n“But they have not been at all against you here,” said Mrs. Harold Smith. “If you could arrive at her ladyship’s private thoughts to-morrow morning, you would find her to be quite happy in having met the duke. It will be years before she has done boasting of her triumph, and it will be talked of by the young ladies of Framley for the next three generations.”\n\nThe Gresham party, including Dr. Thorne, had remained in the ante-chamber during the battle. The whole combat did not occupy above two minutes, and the three of them were hemmed off from escape by Lady Lufton’s retreat into Dr. Easyman’s lap; but now they, too, essayed to pass on.\n\n“What, you will desert me,” said Miss Dunstable. “Very well; but I shall find you out by-and-by. Frank, there is to be some dancing in one of the rooms,—just to distinguish the affair from Mrs. Proudie’s conversazione. It would be stupid, you know, if all conversaziones were alike; wouldn’t it? So I hope you will go and dance.”\n\n“There will, I presume, be another variation at feeding time,” said Mrs. Harold Smith.\n\n“Oh, yes; certainly; I am the most vulgar of all wretches in that respect. I do love to set people eating and drinking.—Mr. Supplehouse, I am delighted to see you; but do tell me—” and then she whispered with great energy into the ear of Mr. Supplehouse, and Mr. Supplehouse again whispered into her ear. “You think he will, then?” said Miss Dunstable.\n\nMr. Supplehouse assented; he did think so; but he had no warrant for stating the circumstance as a fact. And then he passed on, hardly looking at Mrs. Harold Smith as he passed.\n\n“What a hang-dog countenance he has,” said that lady.\n\n“Ah! you’re prejudiced, my dear, and no wonder; as for myself I always liked Supplehouse. He means mischief; but then mischief is his trade, and he does not conceal it. If I were a politician I should as soon think of being angry with Mr. Supplehouse for turning against me as I am now with a pin for pricking me. It’s my own awkwardness, and I ought to have known how to use the pin more craftily.”\n\n“But you must detest a man who professes to stand by his party, and then does his best to ruin it.”\n\n“So many have done that, my dear; and with much more success than Mr. Supplehouse! All is fair in love and war,—why not add politics to the list? If we could only agree to do that, it would save us from such a deal of heartburning, and would make none of us a bit the worse.”\n\nMiss Dunstable’s rooms, large as they were—“a noble suite of rooms certainly, though perhaps a little too—too—too scattered, we will say, eh, bishop?”—were now nearly full, and would have been inconveniently crowded, were it not that many who came only remained for half-an-hour or so. Space, however, had been kept for the dancers—much to Mrs. Proudie’s consternation. Not that she disapproved of dancing in London, as a rule; but she was indignant that the laws of a conversazione, as re-established by herself in the fashionable world, should be so violently infringed.\n\n“Conversaziones will come to mean nothing,” she said to the bishop, putting great stress on the latter word, “nothing at all, if they are to be treated in this way.”\n\n“No, they won’t; nothing in the least,” said the bishop.\n\n“Dancing may be very well in its place,” said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“I have never objected to it myself; that is, for the laity,” said the bishop.\n\n“But when people profess to assemble for higher objects,” said Mrs. Proudie, “they ought to act up to their professions.”\n\n“Otherwise they are no better than hypocrites,” said the bishop.\n\n“A spade should be called a spade,” said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“Decidedly,” said the bishop, assenting.\n\n“And when I undertook the trouble and expense of introducing conversaziones,” continued Mrs. Proudie, with an evident feeling that she had been ill-used, “I had no idea of seeing the word so—so—so misinterpreted;” and then observing certain desirable acquaintances at the other side of the room, she went across, leaving the bishop to fend for himself.\n\nLady Lufton, having achieved her success, passed on to the dancing, whither it was not probable that her enemy would follow her, and she had not been there very long before she was joined by her son. Her heart at the present moment was not quite satisfied at the state of affairs with reference to Griselda. She had gone so far as to tell her young friend what were her own wishes; she had declared her desire that Griselda should become her daughter-in-law; but in answer to this Griselda herself had declared nothing. It was, to be sure, no more than natural that a young lady so well brought up as Miss Grantly should show no signs of a passion till she was warranted in showing them by the proceedings of the gentleman; but notwithstanding this—fully aware as she was of the propriety of such reticence—Lady Lufton did think that to her Griselda might have spoken some word evincing that the alliance would be satisfactory to her. Griselda, however, had spoken no such word, nor had she uttered a syllable to show that she would accept Lord Lufton if he did offer. Then again she had uttered no syllable to show that she would not accept him; but, nevertheless, although she knew that the world had been talking about her and Lord Dumbello, she stood up to dance with the future marquess on every possible occasion. All this did give annoyance to Lady Lufton, who began to bethink herself that if she could not quickly bring her little plan to a favourable issue, it might be well for her to wash her hands of it. She was still anxious for the match on her son’s account. Griselda would, she did not doubt, make a good wife; but Lady Lufton was not so sure as she once had been that she herself would be able to keep up so strong a feeling for her daughter-in-law as she had hitherto hoped to do.\n\n“Ludovic, have you been here long?” she said, smiling as she always did smile when her eyes fell upon her son’s face.\n\n“This instant arrived; and I hurried on after you, as Miss Dunstable told me that you were here. What a crowd she has! Did you see Lord Brock?”\n\n“I did not observe him.”\n\n“Or Lord De Terrier? I saw them both in the centre room.”\n\n“Lord De Terrier did me the honour of shaking hands with me as I passed through.”\n\n“I never saw such a mixture of people. There is Mrs. Proudie going out of her mind because you are all going to dance.”\n\n“The Miss Proudies dance,” said Griselda Grantly.\n\n“But not at conversaziones. You don’t see the difference. And I saw Spermoil there, looking as pleased as Punch. He had quite a circle of his own round him, and was chattering away as though he were quite accustomed to the wickednesses of the world.”\n\n“There certainly are people here whom one would not have wished to meet, had one thought of it,” said Lady Lufton, mindful of her late engagement.\n\n“But it must be all right, for I walked up the stairs with the archdeacon. That is an absolute proof, is it not, Miss Grantly?”\n\n“I have no fears. When I am with your mother I know I must be safe.”\n\n“I am not so sure of that,” said Lord Lufton, laughing. “Mother, you hardly know the worst of it yet. Who is here, do you think?”\n\n“I know whom you mean; I have seen him,” said Lady Lufton, very quietly.\n\n“We came across him just at the top of the stairs,” said Griselda, with more animation in her face than ever Lord Lufton had seen there before.\n\n“What; the duke?”\n\n“Yes, the duke,” said Lady Lufton. “I certainly should not have come had I expected to be brought in contact with that man. But it was an accident, and on such an occasion as this it could not be helped.”\n\nLord Lufton at once perceived, by the tone of his mother’s voice and by the shades of her countenance that she had absolutely endured some personal encounter with the duke, and also that she was by no means so indignant at the occurrence as might have been expected. There she was, still in Miss Dunstable’s house, and expressing no anger as to Miss Dunstable’s conduct. Lord Lufton could hardly have been more surprised had he seen the duke handing his mother down to supper; he said, however, nothing further on the subject.\n\n“Are you going to dance, Ludovic?” said Lady Lufton.\n\n“Well, I am not sure that I do not agree with Mrs. Proudie in thinking that dancing would contaminate a conversazione. What are your ideas, Miss Grantly?”\n\nGriselda was never very good at a joke, and imagined that Lord Lufton wanted to escape the trouble of dancing with her. This angered her. For the only species of love-making, or flirtation, or sociability between herself as a young lady, and any other self as a young gentleman, which recommended itself to her taste, was to be found in the amusement of dancing. She was altogether at variance with Mrs. Proudie on this matter, and gave Miss Dunstable great credit for her innovation. In society Griselda’s toes were more serviceable to her than her tongue, and she was to be won by a rapid twirl much more probably than by a soft word. The offer of which she would approve would be conveyed by two all but breathless words during a spasmodic pause in a waltz; and then as she lifted up her arm to receive the accustomed support at her back, she might just find power enough to say, “You—must ask—papa.” After that she would not care to have the affair mentioned till everything was properly settled.\n\n“I have not thought about it,” said Griselda, turning her face away from Lord Lufton.\n\nIt must not, however, be supposed that Miss Grantly had not thought about Lord Lufton, or that she had not considered how great might be the advantage of having Lady Lufton on her side if she made up her mind that she did wish to become Lord Lufton’s wife. She knew well that now was her time for a triumph, now in this very first season of her acknowledged beauty; and she knew also that young, good-looking bachelor lords do not grow on hedges like blackberries. Had Lord Lufton offered to her, she would have accepted him at once without any remorse as to the greater glories which might appertain to a future Marchioness of Hartletop. In that direction she was not without sufficient wisdom. But then Lord Lufton had not offered to her, nor given any signs that he intended to do so; and to give Griselda Grantly her due, she was not a girl to make a first overture. Neither had Lord Dumbello offered; but he had given signs,—dumb signs, such as birds give to each other, quite as intelligible as verbal signs to a girl who preferred the use of her toes to that of her tongue.\n\n“I have not thought about it,” said Griselda, very coldly, and at that moment a gentleman stood before her and asked her hand for the next dance. It was Lord Dumbello; and Griselda, making no reply except by a slight bow, got up and put her hand within her partner’s arm.\n\n“Shall I find you here, Lady Lufton, when we have done?” she said; and then started off among the dancers. When the work before one is dancing the proper thing for a gentleman to do is, at any rate, to ask a lady; this proper thing Lord Lufton had omitted, and now the prize was taken away from under his very nose.\n\nThere was clearly an air of triumph about Lord Dumbello as he walked away with the beauty. The world had been saying that Lord Lufton was to marry her, and the world had also been saying that Lord Dumbello admired her. Now this had angered Lord Dumbello, and made him feel as though he walked about, a mark of scorn, as a disappointed suitor. Had it not been for Lord Lufton, perhaps he would not have cared so much for Griselda Grantly; but circumstances had so turned out that he did care for her, and felt it to be incumbent upon him as the heir to a marquisate to obtain what he wanted, let who would have a hankering after the same article. It is in this way that pictures are so well sold at auctions; and Lord Dumbello regarded Miss Grantly as being now subject to the auctioneer’s hammer, and conceived that Lord Lufton was bidding against him. There was, therefore, an air of triumph about him as he put his arm round Griselda’s waist and whirled her up and down the room in obedience to the music.\n\nLady Lufton and her son were left together looking at each other. Of course he had intended to ask Griselda to dance, but it cannot be said that he very much regretted his disappointment. Of course also Lady Lufton had expected that her son and Griselda would stand up together, and she was a little inclined to be angry with her protégée.\n\n“I think she might have waited a minute,” said Lady Lufton.\n\n“But why, mother? There are certain things for which no one ever waits: to give a friend, for instance, the first passage through a gate out hunting, and such like. Miss Grantly was quite right to take the first that offered.”\n\nLady Lufton had determined to learn what was to be the end of this scheme of hers. She could not have Griselda always with her, and if anything were to be arranged it must be arranged now, while both of them were in London. At the close of the season Griselda would return to Plumstead, and Lord Lufton would go—nobody as yet knew where. It would be useless to look forward to further opportunities. If they did not contrive to love each other now, they would never do so. Lady Lufton was beginning to fear that her plan would not work, but she made up her mind that she would learn the truth then and there,—at least as far as her son was concerned.\n\n“Oh, yes; quite so;—if it is equal to her with which she dances,” said Lady Lufton.\n\n“Quite equal, I should think—unless it be that Dumbello is longer-winded than I am.”\n\n“I am sorry to hear you speak of her in that way, Ludovic.”\n\n“Why sorry, mother?”\n\n“Because I had hoped—that you and she would have liked each other.” This she said in a serious tone of voice, tender and sad, looking up into his face with a plaintive gaze, as though she knew that she were asking of him some great favour.\n\n“Yes, mother, I have known that you have wished that.”\n\n“You have known it, Ludovic!”\n\n“Oh, dear, yes; you are not at all sharp at keeping your secrets from me. And, mother, at one time, for a day or so, I thought that I could oblige you. You have been so good to me, that I would almost do anything for you.”\n\n“Oh, no, no, no,” she said, deprecating his praise, and the sacrifice which he seemed to offer of his own hopes and aspirations. “I would not for worlds have you do so for my sake. No mother ever had a better son, and my only ambition is for your happiness.”\n\n“But, mother, she would not make me happy. I was mad enough for a moment to think that she could do so—for a moment I did think so. There was one occasion on which I would have asked her to take me, but—”\n\n“But what, Ludovic?”\n\n“Never mind; it passed away; and now I shall never ask her. Indeed I do not think she would have me. She is ambitious, and flying at higher game than I am. And I must say this for her, that she knows well what she is doing, and plays her cards as though she had been born with them in her hand.”\n\n“You will never ask her?”\n\n“No, mother; had I done so, it would have been for love of you—only for love of you.”\n\n“I would not for worlds that you should do that.”\n\n“Let her have Dumbello; she will make an excellent wife for him, just the wife that he will want. And you, you will have been so good to her in assisting her to such a matter.”\n\n“But, Ludovic, I am so anxious to see you settled.”\n\n“All in good time, mother!”\n\n“Ah, but the good time is passing away. Years run so very quickly. I hope you think about marrying, Ludovic.”\n\n“But, mother, what if I brought you a wife that you did not approve?”\n\n“I will approve of any one that you love; that is—”\n\n“That is, if you love her also; eh, mother?”\n\n“But I rely with such confidence on your taste. I know that you can like no one that is not lady-like and good.”\n\n“Lady-like and good! Will that suffice?” said he, thinking of Lucy Robarts.\n\n“Yes; it will suffice, if you love her. I don’t want you to care for money. Griselda will have a fortune that would have been convenient; but I do not wish you to care for that.” And thus, as they stood together in Miss Dunstable’s crowded room, the mother and son settled between themselves that the Lufton-Grantly alliance treaty was not to be ratified. “I suppose I must let Mrs. Grantly know,” said Lady Lufton to herself, as Griselda returned to her side. There had not been above a dozen words spoken between Lord Dumbello and his partner, but that young lady also had now fully made up her mind that the treaty above mentioned should never be brought into operation.\n\nWe must go back to our hostess, whom we should not have left for so long a time, seeing that this chapter is written to show how well she could conduct herself in great emergencies. She had declared that after awhile she would be able to leave her position near the entrance door, and find out her own peculiar friends among the crowd; but the opportunity for doing so did not come till very late in the evening. There was a continuation of arrivals; she was wearied to death with making little speeches, and had more than once declared that she must depute Mrs. Harold Smith to take her place.\n\nThat lady stuck to her through all her labours with admirable constancy, and made the work bearable. Without some such constancy on a friend’s part, it would have been unbearable. And it must be acknowledged that this was much to the credit of Mrs. Harold Smith. Her own hopes with reference to the great heiress had all been shattered, and her answer had been given to her in very plain language. But, nevertheless, she was true to her friendship, and was almost as willing to endure fatigue on the occasion as though she had a sister-in-law’s right in the house.\n\nAt about one o’clock her brother came. He had not yet seen Miss Dunstable since the offer had been made, and had now with difficulty been persuaded by his sister to show himself.\n\n“What can be the use?” said he. “The game is up with me now;”—meaning, poor, ruined ne’er-do-well, not only that that game with Miss Dunstable was up, but that the great game of his whole life was being brought to an uncomfortable termination.\n\n“Nonsense,” said his sister. “Do you mean to despair because a man like the Duke of Omnium wants his money? What has been good security for him will be good security for another;” and then Mrs. Harold Smith made herself more agreeable than ever to Miss Dunstable.\n\nWhen Miss Dunstable was nearly worn out, but was still endeavouring to buoy herself up by a hope of the still-expected great arrival—for she knew that the hero would show himself only at a very late hour if it were to be her good fortune that he showed himself at all—Mr. Sowerby walked up the stairs. He had schooled himself to go through this ordeal with all the cool effrontery which was at his command; but it was clearly to be seen that all his effrontery did not stand him in sufficient stead, and that the interview would have been embarrassing had it not been for the genuine good-humour of the lady.\n\n“Here is my brother,” said Mrs. Harold Smith, showing by the tremulousness of the whisper that she looked forward to the meeting with some amount of apprehension.\n\n“How do you do, Mr. Sowerby?” said Miss Dunstable, walking almost into the doorway to welcome him. “Better late than never.”\n\n“I have only just got away from the House,” said he, as he gave her his hand.\n\n“Oh, I know well that you are sans reproche among senators;—as Mr. Harold Smith is sans peur;—eh, my dear?”\n\n“I must confess that you have contrived to be uncommonly severe upon them both,” said Mrs. Harold, laughing; “and as regards poor Harold, most undeservedly so: Nathaniel is here, and may defend himself.”\n\n“And no one is better able to do so on all occasions. But, my dear Mr. Sowerby, I am dying of despair. Do you think he’ll come?”\n\n“He? who?”\n\n“You stupid man—as if there were more than one he! There were two, but the other has been.”\n\n“Upon my word, I don’t understand,” said Mr. Sowerby, now again at his ease. “But can I do anything? shall I go and fetch any one? Oh, Tom Towers! I fear I can’t help you. But here he is at the foot of the stairs!” And then Mr. Sowerby stood back with his sister to make way for the great representative man of the age.\n\n“Angels and ministers of grace, assist me!” said Miss Dunstable. “How on earth am I to behave myself? Mr. Sowerby, do you think that I ought to kneel down? My dear, will he have a reporter at his back in the royal livery?” And then Miss Dunstable advanced two or three steps—not into the doorway, as she had done for Mr. Sowerby—put out her hand, and smiled her sweetest on Mr. Towers, of the Jupiter.\n\n“Mr. Towers,” she said, “I am delighted to have this opportunity of seeing you in my own house.”\n\n“Miss Dunstable, I am immensely honoured by the privilege of being here,” said he.\n\n“The honour done is all conferred on me,” and she bowed and curtseyed with very stately grace. Each thoroughly understood the badinage of the other; and then, in a few moments, they were engaged in very easy conversation.\n\n“By-the-by, Sowerby, what do you think of this threatened dissolution?” said Tom Towers.\n\n“We are all in the hands of Providence,” said Mr. Sowerby, striving to take the matter without any outward show of emotion. But the question was one of terrible import to him, and up to this time he had heard of no such threat. Nor had Mrs. Harold Smith, nor Miss Dunstable, nor had a hundred others who now either listened to the vaticinations of Mr. Towers, or to the immediate report made of them. But it is given to some men to originate such tidings, and the performance of the prophecy is often brought about by the authority of the prophet. On the following morning the rumour that there would be a dissolution was current in all high circles. “They have no conscience in such matters; no conscience whatever,” said a small god, speaking of the giants,—a small god, whose constituency was expensive.\n\nMr. Towers stood there chatting for about twenty minutes, and then took his departure without making his way into the room. He had answered the purpose for which he had been invited, and left Miss Dunstable in a happy frame of mind.\n\n“I am very glad that he came,” said Mrs. Harold Smith, with an air of triumph.\n\n“Yes, I am glad,” said Miss Dunstable, “though I am thoroughly ashamed that I should be so. After all, what good has he done to me or to any one?” And having uttered this moral reflection, she made her way into the rooms, and soon discovered Dr. Thorne standing by himself against the wall.\n\n“Well, doctor,” she said, “where are Mary and Frank? You do not look at all comfortable, standing here by yourself.”\n\n“I am quite as comfortable as I expected, thank you,” said he. “They are in the room somewhere, and, as I believe, equally happy.”\n\n“That’s spiteful in you, doctor, to speak in that way. What would you say if you were called on to endure all that I have gone through this evening?”\n\n“There is no accounting for tastes, but I presume you like it.”\n\n“I am not so sure of that. Give me your arm, and let me get some supper. One always likes the idea of having done hard work, and one always likes to have been successful.”\n\n“We all know that virtue is its own reward,” said the doctor.\n\n“Well, that is something hard upon me,” said Miss Dunstable, as she sat down to table. “And you really think that no good of any sort can come from my giving such a party as this?”\n\n“Oh, yes; some people, no doubt, have been amused.”\n\n“It is all vanity in your estimation,” said Miss Dunstable; “vanity and vexation of spirit. Well; there is a good deal of the latter, certainly. Sherry, if you please. I would give anything for a glass of beer, but that is out of the question. Vanity and vexation of spirit! And yet I meant to do good.”\n\n“Pray, do not suppose that I am condemning you, Miss Dunstable.”\n\n“Ah, but I do suppose it. Not only you, but another also, whose judgment I care for perhaps more than yours; and that, let me tell you, is saying a great deal. You do condemn me, Dr. Thorne, and I also condemn myself. It is not that I have done wrong, but the game is not worth the candle.”\n\n“Ah; that’s the question.”\n\n“The game is not worth the candle. And yet it was a triumph to have both the duke and Tom Towers. You must confess that I have not managed badly.”\n\nSoon after that the Greshams went away, and in an hour’s time or so, Miss Dunstable was allowed to drag herself to her own bed.\n\nThat is the great question to be asked on all such occasions, “Is the game worth the candle?”\n\n# CHAPTER XXX. THE GRANTLY TRIUMPH.\n\nIt has been mentioned cursorily—the reader, no doubt, will have forgotten it—that Mrs. Grantly was not specially invited by her husband to go up to town with a view of being present at Miss Dunstable’s party. Mrs. Grantly said nothing on the subject, but she was somewhat chagrined; not on account of the loss she sustained with reference to that celebrated assembly, but because she felt that her daughter’s affairs required the supervision of a mother’s eye. She also doubted the final ratification of that Lufton-Grantly treaty, and, doubting it, she did not feel quite satisfied that her daughter should be left in Lady Lufton’s hands. She had said a word or two to the archdeacon before he went up, but only a word or two, for she hesitated to trust him in so delicate a matter. She was, therefore, not a little surprised at receiving, on the second morning after her husband’s departure, a letter from him desiring her immediate presence in London. She was surprised; but her heart was filled rather with hope than dismay, for she had full confidence in her daughter’s discretion.\n\nOn the morning after the party, Lady Lufton and Griselda had breakfasted together as usual, but each felt that the manner of the other was altered. Lady Lufton thought that her young friend was somewhat less attentive, and perhaps less meek in her demeanour, than usual; and Griselda felt that Lady Lufton was less affectionate. Very little, however, was said between them, and Lady Lufton expressed no surprise when Griselda begged to be left alone at home, instead of accompanying her ladyship when the carriage came to the door.\n\nNobody called in Bruton Street that afternoon—no one, at least, was let in—except the archdeacon. He came there late in the day, and remained with his daughter till Lady Lufton returned. Then he took his leave, with more abruptness than was usual with him, and without saying anything special to account for the duration of his visit. Neither did Griselda say anything special; and so the evening wore away, each feeling in some unconscious manner that she was on less intimate terms with the other than had previously been the case.\n\nOn the next day also Griselda would not go out, but at four o’clock a servant brought a letter to her from Mount Street. Her mother had arrived in London and wished to see her at once. Mrs. Grantly sent her love to Lady Lufton, and would call at half-past five, or at any later hour at which it might be convenient for Lady Lufton to see her. Griselda was to stay and dine in Mount Street; so said the letter. Lady Lufton declared that she would be very happy to see Mrs. Grantly at the hour named; and then, armed with this message, Griselda started for her mother’s lodgings.\n\n“I’ll send the carriage for you,” said Lady Lufton. “I suppose about ten will do.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Griselda, “that will do very nicely;” and then she went.\n\nExactly at half-past five Mrs. Grantly was shown into Lady Lufton’s drawing-room. Her daughter did not come with her, and Lady Lufton could see by the expression of her friend’s face that business was to be discussed. Indeed, it was necessary that she herself should discuss business, for Mrs. Grantly must now be told that the family treaty could not be ratified. The gentleman declined the alliance, and poor Lady Lufton was uneasy in her mind at the nature of the task before her.\n\n“Your coming up has been rather unexpected,” said Lady Lufton, as soon as her friend was seated on the sofa.\n\n“Yes, indeed; I got a letter from the archdeacon only this morning, which made it absolutely necessary that I should come.”\n\n“No bad news, I hope?” said Lady Lufton.\n\n“No; I can’t call it bad news. But, dear Lady Lufton, things won’t always turn out exactly as one would have them.”\n\n“No, indeed,” said her ladyship, remembering that it was incumbent on her to explain to Mrs. Grantly now at this present interview the tidings with which her mind was fraught. She would, however, let Mrs. Grantly first tell her own story, feeling, perhaps, that the one might possibly bear upon the other.\n\n“Poor dear Griselda!” said Mrs. Grantly, almost with a sigh. “I need not tell you, Lady Lufton, what my hopes were regarding her.”\n\n“Has she told you anything—anything that—”\n\n“She would have spoken to you at once—and it was due to you that she should have done so—but she was timid; and not unnaturally so. And then it was right that she should see her father and me before she quite made up her own mind. But I may say that it is settled now.”\n\n“What is settled?” asked Lady Lufton.\n\n“Of course it is impossible for any one to tell beforehand how these things will turn out,” continued Mrs. Grantly, beating about the bush rather more than was necessary. “The dearest wish of my heart was to see her married to Lord Lufton. I should so much have wished to have her in the same county with me, and such a match as that would have fully satisfied my ambition.”\n\n“Well, I should rather think it might!” Lady Lufton did not say this out loud, but she thought it. Mrs. Grantly was absolutely speaking of a match between her daughter and Lord Lufton as though she would have displayed some amount of Christian moderation in putting up with it! Griselda Grantly might be a very nice girl; but even she—so thought Lady Lufton at the moment—might possibly be priced too highly.\n\n“Dear Mrs. Grantly,” she said, “I have foreseen for the last few days that our mutual hopes in this respect would not be gratified. Lord Lufton, I think;—but perhaps it is not necessary to explain— Had you not come up to town I should have written to you,—probably to-day. Whatever may be dear Griselda’s fate in life, I sincerely hope that she may be happy.”\n\n“I think she will,” said Mrs. Grantly, in a tone that expressed much satisfaction.\n\n“Has—has anything—”\n\n“Lord Dumbello proposed to Griselda the other night, at Miss Dunstable’s party,” said Mrs. Grantly, with her eyes fixed upon the floor, and assuming on the sudden much meekness in her manner; “and his lordship was with the archdeacon yesterday, and again this morning. I fancy he is in Mount Street at the present moment.”\n\n“Oh, indeed!” said Lady Lufton. She would have given worlds to have possessed at the moment sufficient self-command to have enabled her to express in her tone and manner unqualified satisfaction at the tidings. But she had not such self-command, and was painfully aware of her own deficiency.\n\n“Yes,” said Mrs. Grantly. “And as it is all so far settled, and as I know you are so kindly anxious about dear Griselda, I thought it right to let you know at once. Nothing can be more upright, honourable, and generous, than Lord Dumbello’s conduct; and, on the whole, the match is one with which I and the archdeacon cannot but be contented.”\n\n“It is certainly a great match,” said Lady Lufton. “Have you seen Lady Hartletop yet?”\n\nNow Lady Hartletop could not be regarded as an agreeable connection, but this was the only word which escaped from Lady Lufton that could be considered in any way disparaging, and, on the whole, I think that she behaved well.\n\n“Lord Dumbello is so completely his own master that that has not been necessary,” said Mrs. Grantly. “The marquis has been told, and the archdeacon will see him either to-morrow or the day after.”\n\nThere was nothing left for Lady Lufton but to congratulate her friend, and this she did in words perhaps not very sincere, but which, on the whole, were not badly chosen.\n\n“I am sure I hope she will be very happy,” said Lady Lufton, “and I trust that the alliance”—the word was very agreeable to Mrs. Grantly’s ear—“will give unalloyed gratification to you and to her father. The position which she is called to fill is a very splendid one, but I do not think that it is above her merits.”\n\nThis was very generous, and so Mrs. Grantly felt it. She had expected that her news would be received with the coldest shade of civility, and she was quite prepared to do battle if there were occasion. But she had no wish for war, and was almost grateful to Lady Lufton for her cordiality.\n\n“Dear Lady Lufton,” she said, “it is so kind of you to say so. I have told no one else, and of course would tell no one till you knew it. No one has known her and understood her so well as you have done. And I can assure you of this: that there is no one to whose friendship she looks forward in her new sphere of life with half so much pleasure as she does to yours.”\n\nLady Lufton did not say much further. She could not declare that she expected much gratification from an intimacy with the future Marchioness of Hartletop. The Hartletops and Luftons must, at any rate for her generation, live in a world apart, and she had now said all that her old friendship with Mrs. Grantly required. Mrs. Grantly understood all this quite as well as did Lady Lufton; but then Mrs. Grantly was much the better woman of the world.\n\nIt was arranged that Griselda should come back to Bruton Street for that night, and that her visit should then be brought to a close.\n\n“The archdeacon thinks that for the present I had better remain up in town,” said Mrs. Grantly, “and under the very peculiar circumstances Griselda will be—perhaps more comfortable with me.”\n\nTo this Lady Lufton entirely agreed; and so they parted, excellent friends, embracing each other in a most affectionate manner.\n\nThat evening Griselda did return to Bruton Street, and Lady Lufton had to go through the further task of congratulating her. This was the more disagreeable of the two, especially so as it had to be thought over beforehand. But the young lady’s excellent good sense and sterling qualities made the task comparatively an easy one. She neither cried, nor was impassioned, nor went into hysterics, nor showed any emotion. She did not even talk of her noble Dumbello—her generous Dumbello. She took Lady Lufton’s kisses almost in silence, thanked her gently for her kindness, and made no allusion to her own future grandeur.\n\n“I think I should like to go to bed early,” she said, “as I must see to my packing up.”\n\n“Richards will do all that for you, my dear.”\n\n“Oh, yes, thank you, nothing can be kinder than Richards. But I’ll just see to my own dresses.” And so she went to bed early.\n\nLady Lufton did not see her son for the next two days, but when she did, of course she said a word or two about Griselda.\n\n“You have heard the news, Ludovic?” she asked.\n\n“Oh, yes: it’s at all the clubs. I have been overwhelmed with presents of willow branches.”\n\n“You, at any rate, have got nothing to regret,” she said.\n\n“Nor you either, mother. I am sure that you do not think you have. Say that you do not regret it. Dearest mother, say so for my sake. Do you not know in your heart of hearts that she was not suited to be happy as my wife,—or to make me happy?”\n\n“Perhaps not,” said Lady Lufton, sighing. And then she kissed her son, and declared to herself that no girl in England could be good enough for him.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI. SALMON FISHING IN NORWAY.\n\nLord Dumbello’s engagement with Griselda Grantly was the talk of the town for the next ten days. It formed, at least, one of two subjects which monopolized attention, the other being that dreadful rumour, first put in motion by Tom Towers at Miss Dunstable’s party, as to a threatened dissolution of Parliament.\n\n“Perhaps, after all, it will be the best thing for us,” said Mr. Green Walker, who felt himself to be tolerably safe at Crewe Junction.\n\n“I regard it as a most wicked attempt,” said Harold Smith, who was not equally secure in his own borough, and to whom the expense of an election was disagreeable. “It is done in order that they may get time to tide over the autumn. They won’t gain ten votes by a dissolution, and less than forty would hardly give them a majority. But they have no sense of public duty—none whatever. Indeed, I don’t know who has.”\n\n“No, by Jove; that’s just it. That’s what my aunt Lady Hartletop says; there is no sense of duty left in the world. By-the-by, what an uncommon fool Dumbello is making himself!” And then the conversation went off to that other topic.\n\nLord Lufton’s joke against himself about the willow branches was all very well, and nobody dreamed that his heart was sore in that matter. The world was laughing at Lord Dumbello for what it chose to call a foolish match, and Lord Lufton’s friends talked to him about it as though they had never suspected that he could have made an ass of himself in the same direction; but, nevertheless, he was not altogether contented. He by no means wished to marry Griselda; he had declared to himself a dozen times since he had first suspected his mother’s manœuvres, that no consideration on earth should induce him to do so; he had pronounced her to be cold, insipid, and unattractive in spite of her beauty; and yet he felt almost angry that Lord Dumbello should have been successful. And this, too, was the more inexcusable, seeing that he had never forgotten Lucy Robarts, had never ceased to love her, and that, in holding those various conversations within his own bosom, he was as loud in Lucy’s favour as he was in dispraise of Griselda.\n\n“Your hero, then,” I hear some well-balanced critic say, “is not worth very much.”\n\nIn the first place Lord Lufton is not my hero; and in the next place, a man may be very imperfect and yet worth a great deal. A man may be as imperfect as Lord Lufton, and yet worthy of a good mother and a good wife. If not, how many of us are unworthy of the mothers and wives we have! It is my belief that few young men settle themselves down to the work of the world, to the begetting of children, and carving and paying and struggling and fretting for the same, without having first been in love with four or five possible mothers for them, and probably with two or three at the same time. And yet these men are, as a rule, worthy of the excellent wives that ultimately fall to their lot. In this way Lord Lufton had, to a certain extent, been in love with Griselda. There had been one moment in his life in which he would have offered her his hand, had not her discretion been so excellent; and though that moment never returned, still he suffered from some feeling akin to disappointment when he learned that Griselda had been won and was to be worn. He was, then, a dog in the manger, you will say. Well; and are we not all dogs in the manger, more or less actively? Is not that manger-doggishness one of the most common phases of the human heart?\n\nBut not the less was Lord Lufton truly in love with Lucy Robarts. Had he fancied that any Dumbello was carrying on a siege before that fortress, his vexation would have manifested itself in a very different manner. He could joke about Griselda Grantly with a frank face and a happy tone of voice; but had he heard of any tidings of a similar import with reference to Lucy, he would have been past all joking, and I much doubt whether it would not even have affected his appetite.\n\n“Mother,” he said to Lady Lufton a day or two after the declaration of Griselda’s engagement, “I am going to Norway to fish.”\n\n“To Norway,—to fish!”\n\n“Yes. We’ve got rather a nice party. Clontarf is going, and Culpepper—”\n\n“What, that horrid man!”\n\n“He’s an excellent hand at fishing;—and Haddington Peebles, and—and—there’ll be six of us altogether; and we start this day week.”\n\n“That’s rather sudden, Ludovic.”\n\n“Yes, it is sudden; but we’re sick of London. I should not care to go so soon myself, but Clontarf and Culpepper say that the season is early this year. I must go down to Framley before I start—about my horses; and therefore I came to tell you that I shall be there to-morrow.”\n\n“At Framley to-morrow! If you could put it off for three days I should be going myself.”\n\nBut Lord Lufton could not put it off for three days. It may be that on this occasion he did not wish for his mother’s presence at Framley while he was there; that he conceived that he should be more at his ease in giving orders about his stable if he were alone while so employed. At any rate he declined her company, and on the following morning did go down to Framley by himself.\n\n“Mark,” said Mrs. Robarts, hurrying into her husband’s book-room about the middle of the day, “Lord Lufton is at home. Have you heard it?”\n\n“What! here at Framley?”\n\n“He is over at Framley Court; so the servants say. Carson saw him in the paddock with some of the horses. Won’t you go and see him?”\n\n“Of course I will,” said Mark, shutting up his papers. “Lady Lufton can’t be here, and if he is alone he will probably come and dine.”\n\n“I don’t know about that,” said Mrs. Robarts, thinking of poor Lucy.\n\n“He is not in the least particular. What does for us will do for him. I shall ask him, at any rate.” And without further parley the clergyman took up his hat and went off in search of his friend.\n\nLucy Robarts had been present when the gardener brought in tidings of Lord Lufton’s arrival at Framley, and was aware that Fanny had gone to tell her husband.\n\n“He won’t come here, will he?” she said, as soon as Mrs. Robarts returned.\n\n“I can’t say,” said Fanny. “I hope not. He ought not to do so, and I don’t think he will. But Mark says that he will ask him to dinner.”\n\n“Then, Fanny, I must be taken ill. There is nothing else for it.”\n\n“I don’t think he will come. I don’t think he can be so cruel. Indeed, I feel sure that he won’t; but I thought it right to tell you.”\n\nLucy also conceived that it was improbable that Lord Lufton should come to the parsonage under the present circumstances; and she declared to herself that it would not be possible that she should appear at table if he did do so; but, nevertheless, the idea of his being at Framley was, perhaps, not altogether painful to her. She did not recognize any pleasure as coming to her from his arrival, but still there was something in his presence which was, unconsciously to herself, soothing to her feelings. But that terrible question remained;—how was she to act if it should turn out that he was coming to dinner?\n\n“If he does come, Fanny,” she said, solemnly, after a pause, “I must keep to my own room, and leave Mark to think what he pleases. It will be better for me to make a fool of myself there, than in his presence in the drawing-room.”\n\nMark Robarts took his hat and stick and went over at once to the home paddock, in which he knew that Lord Lufton was engaged with the horses and grooms. He also was in no supremely happy frame of mind, for his correspondence with Mr. Tozer was on the increase. He had received notice from that indefatigable gentleman that certain “overdue bills” were now lying at the bank in Barchester, and were very desirous of his, Mr. Robarts’s, notice. A concatenation of certain peculiarly unfortunate circumstances made it indispensably necessary that Mr. Tozer should be repaid, without further loss of time, the various sums of money which he had advanced on the credit of Mr. Robarts’s name, &c. &c. &c. No absolute threat was put forth, and, singular to say, no actual amount was named. Mr. Robarts, however, could not but observe, with a most painfully accurate attention, that mention was made, not of an overdue bill, but of overdue bills. What if Mr. Tozer were to demand from him the instant repayment of nine hundred pounds? Hitherto he had merely written to Mr. Sowerby, and he might have had an answer from that gentleman this morning, but no such answer had as yet reached him. Consequently he was not, at the present moment, in a very happy frame of mind.\n\nHe soon found himself with Lord Lufton and the horses. Four or five of them were being walked slowly about the paddock in the care of as many men or boys, and the sheets were being taken off them—off one after another, so that their master might look at them with the more accuracy and satisfaction. But though Lord Lufton was thus doing his duty, and going through his work, he was not doing it with his whole heart,—as the head groom perceived very well. He was fretful about the nags, and seemed anxious to get them out of his sight as soon as he had made a decent pretext of looking at them.\n\n“How are you, Lufton?” said Robarts, coming forward. “They told me that you were down, and so I came across at once.”\n\n“Yes; I only got here this morning, and should have been over with you directly. I am going to Norway for six weeks or so, and it seems that the fish are so early this year, that we must start at once. I have a matter on which I want to speak to you before I leave; and, indeed, it was that which brought me down more than anything else.”\n\nThere was something hurried and not altogether easy about his manner as he spoke, which struck Robarts, and made him think that this promised matter to be spoken of would not be agreeable in discussion. He did not know whether Lord Lufton might not again be mixed up with Tozer and the bills.\n\n“You will dine with us to-day,” he said, “if, as I suppose, you are all alone.”\n\n“Yes, I am all alone.”\n\n“Then you’ll come?”\n\n“Well, I don’t quite know. No, I don’t think I can go over to dinner. Don’t look so disgusted. I’ll explain it all to you just now.”\n\nWhat could there be in the wind; and how was it possible that Tozer’s bill should make it inexpedient for Lord Lufton to dine at the parsonage? Robarts, however, said nothing further about it at the moment, but turned off to look at the horses.\n\n“They are an uncommonly nice set of animals,” said he.\n\n“Well, yes; I don’t know. When a man has four or five horses to look at, somehow or other he never has one fit to go. That chestnut mare is a picture, now that nobody wants her; but she wasn’t able to carry me well to hounds a single day last winter. Take them in, Pounce; that’ll do.”\n\n“Won’t your lordship run your eye over the old black ’oss?” said Pounce, the head groom, in a melancholy tone; “he’s as fine, sir—as fine as a stag.”\n\n“To tell you the truth, I think they’re too fine; but that’ll do; take them in. And now, Mark, if you’re at leisure, we’ll take a turn round the place.”\n\nMark, of course, was at leisure, and so they started on their walk.\n\n“You’re too difficult to please about your stable,” Robarts began.\n\n“Never mind the stable now,” said Lord Lufton. “The truth is, I am not thinking about it. Mark,” he then said, very abruptly, “I want you to be frank with me. Has your sister ever spoken to you about me?”\n\n“My sister; Lucy?”\n\n“Yes; your sister Lucy.”\n\n“No, never; at least nothing especial; nothing that I can remember at this moment.”\n\n“Nor your wife?”\n\n“Spoken about you!—Fanny? Of course she has, in an ordinary way. It would be impossible that she should not. But what do you mean?”\n\n“Have either of them told you that I made an offer to your sister?”\n\n“That you made an offer to Lucy?”\n\n“Yes, that I made an offer to Lucy.”\n\n“No; nobody has told me so. I have never dreamed of such a thing; nor, as far as I believe, have they. If anybody has spread such a report, or said that either of them have hinted at such a thing, it is a base lie. Good heavens! Lufton, for what do you take them?”\n\n“But I did,” said his lordship.\n\n“Did what?” said the parson.\n\n“I did make your sister an offer.”\n\n“You made Lucy an offer of marriage!”\n\n“Yes, I did;—in as plain language as a gentleman could use to a lady.”\n\n“And what answer did she make?”\n\n“She refused me. And now, Mark, I have come down here with the express purpose of making that offer again. Nothing could be more decided than your sister’s answer. It struck me as being almost uncourteously decided. But still it is possible that circumstances may have weighed with her, which ought not to weigh with her. If her love be not given to any one else, I may still have a chance of it. It’s the old story of faint heart, you know: at any rate, I mean to try my luck again; and thinking over it with deliberate purpose, I have come to the conclusion that I ought to tell you before I see her.”\n\nLord Lufton in love with Lucy! As these words repeated themselves over and over again within Mark Robarts’s mind, his mind added to them notes of surprise without end. How had it possibly come about,—and why? In his estimation his sister Lucy was a very simple girl—not plain indeed, but by no means beautiful; certainly not stupid, but by no means brilliant. And then, he would have said, that of all men whom he knew, Lord Lufton would have been the last to fall in love with such a girl as his sister. And now, what was he to say or do? What views was he bound to hold? In what direction should he act? There was Lady Lufton on the one side, to whom he owed everything. How would life be possible to him in that parsonage—within a few yards of her elbow—if he consented to receive Lord Lufton as the acknowledged suitor of his sister? It would be a great match for Lucy, doubtless; but— Indeed, he could not bring himself to believe that Lucy could in truth become the absolute reigning queen of Framley Court.\n\n“Do you think that Fanny knows anything of all this?” he said, after a moment or two.\n\n“I cannot possibly tell. If she does it is not with my knowledge. I should have thought that you could best answer that.”\n\n“I cannot answer it at all,” said Mark. “I, at least, have had no remotest idea of such a thing.”\n\n“Your ideas of it now need not be at all remote,” said Lord Lufton, with a faint smile; “and you may know it as a fact. I did make her an offer of marriage; I was refused; I am going to repeat it; and I am now taking you into my confidence, in order that, as her brother, and as my friend, you may give me such assistance as you can.” They then walked on in silence for some yards, after which Lord Lufton added: “And now I’ll dine with you to-day if you wish it.”\n\nMr. Robarts did not know what to say; he could not bethink himself what answer duty required of him. He had no right to interfere between his sister and such a marriage, if she herself should wish it; but still there was something terrible in the thought of it! He had a vague conception that it must come to evil; that the project was a dangerous one; and that it could not finally result happily for any of them. What would Lady Lufton say? That undoubtedly was the chief source of his dismay.\n\n“Have you spoken to your mother about this?” he said.\n\n“My mother? no; why speak to her till I know my fate? A man does not like to speak much of such matters if there be a probability of his being rejected. I tell you because I do not like to make my way into your house under a false pretence.”\n\n“But what would Lady Lufton say?”\n\n“I think it probable that she would be displeased on the first hearing it; that in four-and-twenty hours she would be reconciled; and that after a week or so Lucy would be her dearest favourite and the prime minister of all her machinations. You don’t know my mother as well as I do. She would give her head off her shoulders to do me a pleasure.”\n\n“And for that reason,” said Mark Robarts, “you ought, if possible, to do her pleasure.”\n\n“I cannot absolutely marry a wife of her choosing, if you mean that,” said Lord Lufton.\n\nThey went on walking about the garden for an hour, but they hardly got any farther than the point to which we have now brought them. Mark Robarts could not make up his mind on the spur of the moment; nor, as he said more than once to Lord Lufton, could he be at all sure that Lucy would in any way be guided by him. It was, therefore, at last settled between them that Lord Lufton should come to the parsonage immediately after breakfast on the following morning. It was agreed also that the dinner had better not come off, and Robarts promised that he would, if possible, have determined by the morning as to what advice he would give his sister.\n\nHe went direct home to the parsonage from Framley Court, feeling that he was altogether in the dark till he should have consulted his wife. How would he feel if Lucy were to become Lady Lufton? and how would he look Lady Lufton in the face in telling her that such was to be his sister’s destiny? On returning home he immediately found his wife, and had not been closeted with her five minutes before he knew, at any rate, all that she knew.\n\n“And you mean to say that she does love him?” said Mark.\n\n“Indeed she does; and is it not natural that she should? When I saw them so much together I feared that she would. But I never thought that he would care for her.”\n\nEven Fanny did not as yet give Lucy credit for half her attractiveness. After an hour’s talking the interview between the husband and wife ended in a message to Lucy, begging her to join them both in the book-room.\n\n“Aunt Lucy,” said a chubby little darling, who was taken up into his aunt’s arms as he spoke, “papa and mamma ’ant ’oo in te tuddy, and I musn’t go wis ’oo.”\n\nLucy, as she kissed the boy and pressed his face against her own, felt that her blood was running quick to her heart.\n\n“Musn’t ’oo go wis me, my own one?” she said, as she put her playfellow down; but she played with the child only because she did not wish to betray even to him that she was hardly mistress of herself. She knew that Lord Lufton was at Framley; she knew that her brother had been to him; she knew that a proposal had been made that he should come there that day to dinner. Must it not therefore be the case that this call to a meeting in the study had arisen out of Lord Lufton’s arrival at Framley? and yet, how could it have done so? Had Fanny betrayed her in order to prevent the dinner invitation? It could not be possible that Lord Lufton himself should have spoken on the subject! And then she again stooped to kiss the child, rubbed her hands across her forehead to smooth her hair, and erase, if that might be possible, the look of care which she wore, and then descended slowly to her brother’s sitting-room.\n\nHer hand paused for a second on the door ere she opened it, but she had resolved that, come what might, she would be brave. She pushed it open and walked in with a bold front, with eyes wide open, and a slow step.\n\n“Frank says that you want me,” she said.\n\nMr. Robarts and Fanny were both standing up by the fireplace, and each waited a second for the other to speak when Lucy entered the room; and then Fanny began,—\n\n“Lord Lufton is here, Lucy.”\n\n“Here! Where? At the parsonage?”\n\n“No, not at the parsonage; but over at Framley Court,” said Mark.\n\n“And he promises to call here after breakfast to-morrow,” said Fanny. And then again there was a pause. Mrs. Robarts hardly dared to look Lucy in the face. She had not betrayed her trust, seeing that the secret had been told to Mark, not by her, but by Lord Lufton; but she could not but feel that Lucy would think that she had betrayed it.\n\n“Very well,” said Lucy, trying to smile; “I have no objection in life.”\n\n“But, Lucy, dear,”—and now Mrs. Robarts put her arm round her sister-in-law’s waist,—“he is coming here especially to see you.”\n\n“Oh; that makes a difference. I am afraid that I shall be—engaged.”\n\n“He has told everything to Mark,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\nLucy now felt that her bravery was almost deserting her. She hardly knew which way to look or how to stand. Had Fanny told everything also? There was so much that Fanny knew that Lord Lufton could not have known. But, in truth, Fanny had told all—the whole story of Lucy’s love, and had described the reasons which had induced her to reject her suitor; and had done so in words which, had Lord Lufton heard them, would have made him twice as passionate in his love.\n\nAnd then it certainly did occur to Lucy to think why Lord Lufton should have come to Framley and told all this history to her brother. She attempted for a moment to make herself believe that she was angry with him for doing so. But she was not angry. She had not time to argue much about it, but there came upon her a gratified sensation of having been remembered, and thought of, and—loved. Must it not be so? Could it be possible that he himself would have told this tale to her brother, if he did not still love her? Fifty times she had said to herself that his offer had been an affair of the moment, and fifty times she had been unhappy in so saying. But this new coming of his could not be an affair of the moment. She had been the dupe, she had thought, of an absurd passion on her own part; but now—how was it now? She did not bring herself to think that she should ever be Lady Lufton. She had still, in some perversely obstinate manner, made up her mind against that result. But yet, nevertheless, it did in some unaccountable manner satisfy her to feel that Lord Lufton had himself come down to Framley and himself told this story.\n\n“He has told everything to Mark,” said Mrs. Robarts; and then again there was a pause for a moment, during which these thoughts passed through Lucy’s mind.\n\n“Yes,” said Mark, “he has told me all, and he is coming here to-morrow morning that he may receive an answer from yourself.”\n\n“What answer?” said Lucy, trembling.\n\n“Nay, dearest; who can say that but yourself?” and her sister-in-law, as she spoke, pressed close against her. “You must say that yourself.”\n\nMrs. Robarts in her long conversation with her husband had pleaded strongly on Lucy’s behalf, taking, as it were, a part against Lady Lufton. She had said that if Lord Lufton persevered in his suit, they at the parsonage could not be justified in robbing Lucy of all that she had won for herself, in order to do Lady Lufton’s pleasure.\n\n“But she will think,” said Mark, “that we have plotted and intrigued for this. She will call us ungrateful, and will make Lucy’s life wretched.” To which the wife had answered, that all that must be left in God’s hands. They had not plotted or intrigued. Lucy, though loving the man in her heart of hearts, had already once refused him, because she would not be thought to have snatched at so great a prize. But if Lord Lufton loved her so warmly that he had come down there in this manner, on purpose, as he himself had put it, that he might learn his fate, then—so argued Mrs. Robarts—they two, let their loyalty to Lady Lufton be ever so strong, could not justify it to their consciences to stand between Lucy and her lover. Mark had still somewhat demurred to this, suggesting how terrible would be their plight if they should now encourage Lord Lufton, and if he, after such encouragement, when they should have quarrelled with Lady Lufton, should allow himself to be led away from his engagement by his mother. To which Fanny had answered that justice was justice, and that right was right. Everything must be told to Lucy, and she must judge for herself.\n\n“But I do not know what Lord Lufton wants,” said Lucy, with her eyes fixed upon the ground, and now trembling more than ever. “He did come to me, and I did give him an answer.”\n\n“And is that answer to be final?” said Mark,—somewhat cruelly, for Lucy had not yet been told that her lover had made any repetition of his proposal. Fanny, however, determined that no injustice should be done, and therefore she at last continued the story.\n\n“We know that you did give him an answer, dearest; but gentlemen sometimes will not put up with one answer on such a subject. Lord Lufton has declared to Mark that he means to ask again. He has come down here on purpose to do so.”\n\n“And Lady Lufton—” said Lucy, speaking hardly above a whisper, and still hiding her face as she leaned against her sister’s shoulder.\n\n“Lord Lufton has not spoken to his mother about it,” said Mark; and it immediately became clear to Lucy, from the tone of her brother’s voice, that he, at least, would not be pleased, should she accept her lover’s vow.\n\n“You must decide out of your own heart, dear,” said Fanny, generously. “Mark and I know how well you have behaved, for I have told him everything.” Lucy shuddered and leaned closer against her sister as this was said to her. “I had no alternative, dearest, but to tell him. It was best so; was it not? But nothing has been told to Lord Lufton. Mark would not let him come here to-day, because it would have flurried you, and he wished to give you time to think. But you can see him to-morrow morning,—can you not? and then answer him.”\n\nLucy now stood perfectly silent, feeling that she dearly loved her sister-in-law for her sisterly kindness—for that sisterly wish to promote a sister’s love; but still there was in her mind a strong resolve not to allow Lord Lufton to come there under the idea that he would be received as a favoured lover. Her love was powerful, but so also was her pride; and she could not bring herself to bear the scorn which would lay in Lady Lufton’s eyes. “His mother will despise me, and then he will despise me too,” she said to herself; and with a strong gulp of disappointed love and ambition she determined to persist.\n\n“Shall we leave you now, dear; and speak of it again to-morrow morning, before he comes?” said Fanny.\n\n“That will be the best,” said Mark. “Turn it in your mind every way to-night. Think of it when you have said your prayers—and, Lucy, come here to me;”—then, taking her in his arms, he kissed her with a tenderness that was not customary with him towards her. “It is fair,” said he, “that I should tell you this: that I have perfect confidence in your judgment and feeling; and that I will stand by you as your brother in whatever decision you may come to. Fanny and I both think that you have behaved excellently, and are both of us sure that you will do what is best. Whatever you do I will stick to you;—and so will Fanny.”\n\n“Dearest, dearest Mark!”\n\n“And now we will say nothing more about it till to-morrow morning,” said Fanny.\n\nBut Lucy felt that this saying nothing more about it till to-morrow morning would be tantamount to an acceptance on her part of Lord Lufton’s offer. Mrs. Robarts knew, and Mr. Robarts also now knew, the secret of her heart; and if, such being the case, she allowed Lord Lufton to come there with the acknowledged purpose of pleading his own suit, it would be impossible for her not to yield. If she were resolved that she would not yield, now was the time for her to stand her ground and make her fight.\n\n“Do not go, Fanny; at least not quite yet,” she said.\n\n“Well, dear?”\n\n“I want you to stay while I tell Mark. He must not let Lord Lufton come here to-morrow.”\n\n“Not let him!” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\nMr. Robarts said nothing, but he felt that his sister was rising in his esteem from minute to minute.\n\n“No; Mark must bid him not come. He will not wish to pain me when it can do no good. Look here, Mark;” and she walked over to her brother, and put both her hands upon his arm. “I do love Lord Lufton. I had no such meaning or thought when I first knew him. But I do love him—I love him dearly;—almost as well as Fanny loves you, I suppose. You may tell him so if you think proper—nay, you must tell him so, or he will not understand me. But tell him this, as coming from me: that I will never marry him, unless his mother asks me.”\n\n“She will not do that, I fear,” said Mark, sorrowfully.\n\n“No; I suppose not,” said Lucy, now regaining all her courage. “If I thought it probable that she should wish me to be her daughter-in-law, it would not be necessary that I should make such a stipulation. It is because she will not wish it; because she would regard me as unfit to—to—to mate with her son. She would hate me, and scorn me; and then he would begin to scorn me, and perhaps would cease to love me. I could not bear her eye upon me, if she thought that I had injured her son. Mark, you will go to him now; will you not? and explain this to him;—as much of it as is necessary. Tell him, that if his mother asks me I will—consent. But that as I know that she never will, he is to look upon all that he has said as forgotten. With me it shall be the same as though it were forgotten.”\n\nSuch was her verdict, and so confident were they both of her firmness—of her obstinacy Mark would have called it on any other occasion,—that they, neither of them, sought to make her alter it.\n\n“You will go to him now,—this afternoon; will you not?” she said; and Mark promised that he would. He could not but feel that he himself was greatly relieved. Lady Lufton might probably hear that her son had been fool enough to fall in love with the parson’s sister, but under existing circumstances she could not consider herself aggrieved either by the parson or by his sister. Lucy was behaving well, and Mark was proud of her. Lucy was behaving with fierce spirit, and Fanny was grieving for her.\n\n“I’d rather be by myself till dinner-time,” said Lucy, as Mrs. Robarts prepared to go with her out of the room. “Dear Fanny, don’t look unhappy; there’s nothing to make us unhappy. I told you I should want goat’s milk, and that will be all.”\n\nRobarts, after sitting for an hour with his wife, did return again to Framley Court; and, after a considerable search, found Lord Lufton returning home to a late dinner.\n\n“Unless my mother asks her,” said he, when the story had been told him. “That is nonsense. Surely you told her that such is not the way of the world.”\n\nRobarts endeavoured to explain to him that Lucy could not endure to think that her husband’s mother should look on her with disfavour.\n\n“Does she think that my mother dislikes her—her specially?” asked Lord Lufton.\n\nNo; Robarts could not suppose that that was the case; but Lady Lufton might probably think that a marriage with a clergyman’s sister would be a mésalliance.\n\n“That is out of the question,” said Lord Lufton; “as she has especially wanted me to marry a clergyman’s daughter for some time past. But, Mark, it is absurd talking about my mother. A man in these days is not to marry as his mother bids him.”\n\nMark could only assure him, in answer to all this, that Lucy was very firm in what she was doing, that she had quite made up her mind, and that she altogether absolved Lord Lufton from any necessity to speak to his mother, if he did not think well of doing so. But all this was to very little purpose.\n\n“She does love me then?” said Lord Lufton.\n\n“Well,” said Mark, “I will not say whether she does or does not. I can only repeat her own message. She cannot accept you, unless she does so at your mother’s request.” And having said that again, he took his leave, and went back to the parsonage.\n\nPoor Lucy, having finished her interview with so much dignity, having fully satisfied her brother, and declined any immediate consolation from her sister-in-law, betook herself to her own bed-room. She had to think over what she had said and done, and it was necessary that she should be alone to do so. It might be that, when she came to reconsider the matter, she would not be quite so well satisfied as was her brother. Her grandeur of demeanour and slow propriety of carriage lasted her till she was well into her own room. There are animals who, when they are ailing in any way, contrive to hide themselves, ashamed, as it were, that the weakness of their suffering should be witnessed. Indeed, I am not sure whether all dumb animals do not do so more or less; and in this respect Lucy was like a dumb animal. Even in her confidences with Fanny she made a joke of her own misfortunes, and spoke of her heart ailments with self-ridicule. But now, having walked up the staircase with no hurried step, and having deliberately locked the door, she turned herself round to suffer in silence and solitude—as do the beasts and birds.\n\nShe sat herself down on a low chair, which stood at the foot of her bed, and, throwing back her head, held her handkerchief across her eyes and forehead, holding it tight in both her hands; and then she began to think. She began to think and also to cry, for the tears came running down from beneath the handkerchief; and low sobs were to be heard,—only that the animal had taken itself off, to suffer in solitude.\n\nHad she not thrown from her all her chances of happiness? Was it possible that he should come to her yet again,—a third time? No; it was not possible. The very mode and pride of this, her second rejection of him, made it impossible. In coming to her determination, and making her avowal, she had been actuated by the knowledge that Lady Lufton would regard such a marriage with abhorrence. Lady Lufton would not and could not ask her to condescend to be her son’s bride. Her chance of happiness, of glory, of ambition, of love, was all gone. She had sacrificed everything, not to virtue, but to pride; and she had sacrificed not only herself, but him. When first he came there—when she had meditated over his first visit—she had hardly given him credit for deep love; but now—there could be no doubt that he loved her now. After his season in London, his days and nights passed with all that was beautiful, he had returned there, to that little country parsonage, that he might again throw himself at her feet. And she—she had refused to see him, though she loved him with all her heart; she had refused to see him, because she was so vile a coward that she could not bear the sour looks of an old woman!\n\n“I will come down directly,” she said, when Fanny at last knocked at the door, begging to be admitted. “I won’t open it, love, but I will be with you in ten minutes; I will, indeed.” And so she was; not, perhaps, without traces of tears, discernible by the experienced eye of Mrs. Robarts, but yet with a smooth brow, and voice under her own command.\n\n“I wonder whether she really loves him,” Mark said to his wife that night.\n\n“Love him!” his wife had answered; “indeed she does; and, Mark, do not be led away by the stern quiet of her demeanour. To my thinking she is a girl who might almost die for love.”\n\nOn the next day Lord Lufton left Framley; and started, according to his arrangements, for the Norway salmon fishing.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII. THE GOAT AND COMPASSES.\n\nHarold Smith had been made unhappy by that rumour of a dissolution; but the misfortune to him would be as nothing compared to the severity with which it would fall on Mr. Sowerby. Harold Smith might or might not lose his borough, but Mr. Sowerby would undoubtedly lose his county; and, in losing that, he would lose everything. He felt very certain now that the duke would not support him again, let who would be master of Chaldicotes; and as he reflected on these things he found it very hard to keep up his spirits.\n\nTom Towers, it seems, had known all about it, as he always does. The little remark which had dropped from him at Miss Dunstable’s, made, no doubt, after mature deliberation, and with profound political motives, was the forerunner, only by twelve hours, of a very general report that the giants were going to the country. It was manifest that the giants had not a majority in Parliament, generous as had been the promises of support disinterestedly made to them by the gods. This indeed was manifest, and therefore they were going to the country, although they had been deliberately warned by a very prominent scion of Olympus that if they did do so that disinterested support must be withdrawn. This threat did not seem to weigh much, and by two o’clock on the day following Miss Dunstable’s party, the fiat was presumed to have gone forth. The rumour had begun with Tom Towers, but by that time it had reached Buggins at the Petty Bag Office.\n\n“It won’t make no difference to hus, sir; will it, Mr. Robarts?” said Buggins, as he leaned respectfully against the wall near the door, in the room of the private secretary at that establishment.\n\nA good deal of conversation, miscellaneous, special, and political, went on between young Robarts and Buggins in the course of the day; as was natural, seeing that they were thrown in these evil times very much upon each other. The Lord Petty Bag of the present ministry was not such a one as Harold Smith. He was a giant indifferent to his private notes, and careless as to the duties even of patronage; he rarely visited the office, and as there were no other clerks in the establishment—owing to a root and branch reform carried out in the short reign of Harold Smith—to whom could young Robarts talk, if not to Buggins?\n\n“No; I suppose not,” said Robarts, as he completed on his blotting-paper an elaborate picture of a Turk seated on his divan.\n\n“’Cause, you see, sir, we’re in the Upper ’Ouse, now;—as I always thinks we hought to be. I don’t think it ain’t constitutional for the Petty Bag to be in the Commons, Mr. Robarts. Hany ways, it never usen’t.”\n\n“They’re changing all those sort of things now-a-days, Buggins,” said Robarts, giving the final touch to the Turk’s smoke.\n\n“Well; I’ll tell you what it is, Mr. Robarts: I think I’ll go. I can’t stand all these changes. I’m turned of sixty now, and don’t want any ’stifflicates. I think I’ll take my pension and walk. The hoffice ain’t the same place at all since it come down among the Commons.” And then Buggins retired sighing, to console himself with a pot of porter behind a large open office ledger, set up on end on a small table in the little lobby outside the private secretary’s room. Buggins sighed again as he saw that the date made visible in the open book was almost as old as his own appointment; for such a book as this lasted long in the Petty Bag Office. A peer of high degree had been Lord Petty Bag in those days; one whom a messenger’s heart could respect with infinite veneration, as he made his unaccustomed visits to the office with much solemnity—perhaps four times during the season. The Lord Petty Bag then was highly regarded by his staff, and his coming among them was talked about for some hours previously and for some days afterwards; but Harold Smith had bustled in and out like the managing clerk in a Manchester house. “The service is going to the dogs,” said Buggins to himself, as he put down the porter pot and looked up over the book at a gentleman who presented himself at the door.\n\n“Mr. Robarts in his room?” said Buggins, repeating the gentleman’s words. “Yes, Mr. Sowerby; you’ll find him there; first door to the left.” And then, remembering that the visitor was a county member—a position which Buggins regarded as next to that of a peer—he got up, and, opening the private secretary’s door, ushered in the visitor.\n\nYoung Robarts and Mr. Sowerby had, of course, become acquainted in the days of Harold Smith’s reign. During that short time the member for East Barset had on most days dropped in at the Petty Bag Office for a minute or two, finding out what the energetic cabinet minister was doing, chatting on semi-official subjects, and teaching the private secretary to laugh at his master. There was nothing, therefore, in his present visit which need appear to be singular, or which required any immediate special explanation. He sat himself down in his ordinary way, and began to speak of the subject of the day.\n\n“We’re all to go,” said Sowerby.\n\n“So I hear,” said the private secretary. “It will give me no trouble, for, as the respectable Buggins says, we’re in the Upper House now.”\n\n“What a delightful time those lucky dogs of lords do have!” said Sowerby. “No constituents, no turning out, no fighting, no necessity for political opinions,—and, as a rule, no such opinions at all!”\n\n“I suppose you’re tolerably safe in East Barsetshire?” said Robarts. “The duke has it pretty much his own way there.”\n\n“Yes; the duke does have it pretty much his own way. By-the-by, where is your brother?”\n\n“At home,” said Robarts; “at least I presume so.”\n\n“At Framley or at Barchester? I believe he was in residence at Barchester not long since.”\n\n“He’s at Framley now, I know. I got a letter only yesterday from his wife, with a commission. He was there, and Lord Lufton had just left.”\n\n“Yes; Lufton was down. He started for Norway this morning. I want to see your brother. You have not heard from him yourself, have you?”\n\n“No; not lately. Mark is a bad correspondent. He would not do at all for a private secretary.”\n\n“At any rate, not to Harold Smith. But you are sure I should not catch him at Barchester?”\n\n“Send down by telegraph, and he would meet you.”\n\n“I don’t want to do that. A telegraph message makes such a fuss in the country, frightening people’s wives, and setting all the horses about the place galloping.”\n\n“What is it about?”\n\n“Nothing of any great consequence. I didn’t know whether he might have told you. I’ll write down by to-night’s post, and then he can meet me at Barchester to-morrow. Or do you write. There’s nothing I hate so much as letter-writing;—just tell him that I called, and that I shall be much obliged if he can meet me at the Dragon of Wantly—say at two to-morrow. I will go down by the express.”\n\nMark Robarts, in talking over this coming money trouble with Sowerby, had once mentioned that if it were necessary to take up the bill for a short time he might be able to borrow the money from his brother. So much of the father’s legacy still remained in the hands of the private secretary as would enable him to produce the amount of the latter bill, and there could be no doubt that he would lend it if asked. Mr. Sowerby’s visit to the Petty Bag Office had been caused by a desire to learn whether any such request had been made,—and also by a half-formed resolution to make the request himself if he should find that the clergyman had not done so. It seemed to him to be a pity that such a sum should be lying about, as it were, within reach, and that he should not stoop to put his hands upon it. Such abstinence would be so contrary to all the practice of his life that it was as difficult to him as it is for a sportsman to let pass a cock-pheasant. But yet something like remorse touched his heart as he sat there balancing himself on his chair in the private secretary’s room, and looking at the young man’s open face.\n\n“Yes; I’ll write to him,” said John Robarts; “but he hasn’t said anything to me about anything particular.”\n\n“Hasn’t he? It does not much signify. I only mentioned it because I thought I understood him to say that he would.” And then Mr. Sowerby went on swinging himself. How was it that he felt so averse to mention that little sum of £500 to a young man like John Robarts, a fellow without wife or children or calls on him of any sort, who would not even be injured by the loss of the money, seeing that he had an ample salary on which to live? He wondered at his own weakness. The want of the money was urgent on him in the extreme. He had reasons for supposing that Mark would find it very difficult to renew the bills, but he, Sowerby, could stop their presentation if he could get this money at once into his own hands.\n\n“Can I do anything for you?” said the innocent lamb, offering his throat to the butcher.\n\nBut some unwonted feeling numbed the butcher’s fingers, and blunted his knife. He sat still for half a minute after the question, and then jumping from his seat, declined the offer. “No, no; nothing, thank you. Only write to Mark, and say that I shall be there to-morrow,” and then, taking his hat, he hurried out of the office. “What an ass I am,” he said to himself as he went: “as if it were of any use now to be particular!”\n\nHe then got into a cab and had himself driven half way up Portman Street towards the New Road, and walking from thence a few hundred yards down a cross-street he came to a public-house. It was called the “Goat and Compasses,”—a very meaningless name, one would say; but the house boasted of being a place of public entertainment very long established on that site, having been a tavern out in the country in the days of Cromwell. At that time the pious landlord, putting up a pious legend for the benefit of his pious customers, had declared that—“God encompasseth us.” The “Goat and Compasses” in these days does quite as well; and, considering the present character of the house, was perhaps less unsuitable than the old legend.\n\n“Is Mr. Austen here?” asked Mr. Sowerby of the man at the bar.\n\n“Which on ’em? Not Mr. John; he ain’t here. Mr. Tom is in—the little room on the left-hand side.” The man whom Mr. Sowerby would have preferred to see was the elder brother, John; but as he was not to be found, he did go into the little room. In that room he found—Mr. Austen, Junior, according to one arrangement of nomenclature, and Mr. Tom Tozer according to another. To gentlemen of the legal profession he generally chose to introduce himself as belonging to the respectable family of the Austens; but among his intimates he had always been—Tozer.\n\nMr. Sowerby, though he was intimate with the family, did not love the Tozers; but he especially hated Tom Tozer. Tom Tozer was a bull-necked, beetle-browed fellow, the expression of whose face was eloquent with acknowledged roguery. “I am a rogue,” it seemed to say. “I know it; all the world knows it: but you’re another. All the world don’t know that, but I do. Men are all rogues, pretty nigh. Some are soft rogues, and some are ’cute rogues. I am a ’cute one; so mind your eye.” It was with such words that Tom Tozer’s face spoke out; and though a thorough liar in his heart, he was not a liar in his face.\n\n“Well, Tozer,” said Mr. Sowerby, absolutely shaking hands with the dirty miscreant, “I wanted to see your brother.”\n\n“John ain’t here, and ain’t like; but it’s all as one.”\n\n“Yes, yes; I suppose it is. I know you two hunt in couples.”\n\n“I don’t know what you mean about hunting, Mr. Sowerby. You gents ’as all the hunting, and we poor folk ’as all the work. I hope you’re going to make up this trifle of money we’re out of so long.”\n\n“It’s about that I’ve called. I don’t know what you call long, Tozer; but the last bill was only dated in February.”\n\n“It’s overdue; ain’t it?”\n\n“Oh, yes; it’s overdue. There’s no doubt about that.”\n\n“Well; when a bit of paper is come round, the next thing is to take it up. Them’s my ideas. And to tell you the truth, Mr. Sowerby, we don’t think as ’ow you’ve been treating us just on the square lately. In that matter of Lord Lufton’s you was down on us uncommon.”\n\n“You know I couldn’t help myself.”\n\n“Well; and we can’t help ourselves now. That’s where it is, Mr. Sowerby. Lord love you; we know what’s what, we do. And so, the fact is we’re uncommon low as to the ready just at present, and we must have them few hundred pounds. We must have them at once, or we must sell up that clerical gent. I’m dashed if it ain’t as hard to get money from a parson as it is to take a bone from a dog. ’E’s ’ad ’is account, no doubt, and why don’t ’e pay?”\n\nMr. Sowerby had called with the intention of explaining that he was about to proceed to Barchester on the following day with the express view of “making arrangements” about this bill; and had he seen John Tozer, John would have been compelled to accord to him some little extension of time. Both Tom and John knew this; and, therefore, John—the soft-hearted one—kept out of the way. There was no danger that Tom would be weak; and, after some half-hour of parley, he was again left by Mr. Sowerby, without having evinced any symptom of weakness.\n\n“It’s the dibs as we want, Mr. Sowerby; that’s all,” were the last words which he spoke as the member of Parliament left the room.\n\nMr. Sowerby then got into another cab, and had himself driven to his sister’s house. It is a remarkable thing with reference to men who are distressed for money—distressed as was now the case with Mr. Sowerby—that they never seem at a loss for small sums, or deny themselves those luxuries which small sums purchase. Cabs, dinners, wine, theatres, and new gloves are always at the command of men who are drowned in pecuniary embarrassments, whereas those who don’t owe a shilling are so frequently obliged to go without them! It would seem that there is no gratification so costly as that of keeping out of debt. But then it is only fair that, if a man has a hobby, he should pay for it.\n\nAny one else would have saved his shilling, as Mrs. Harold Smith’s house was only just across Oxford Street, in the neighbourhood of Hanover Square; but Mr. Sowerby never thought of this. He had never saved a shilling in his life, and it did not occur to him to begin now. He had sent word to her to remain at home for him, and he now found her waiting.\n\n“Harriet,” said he, throwing himself back into an easy chair, “the game is pretty well up at last.”\n\n“Nonsense,” said she. “The game is not up at all if you have the spirit to carry it on.”\n\n“I can only say that I got a formal notice this morning from the duke’s lawyer, saying that he meant to foreclose at once;—not from Fothergill, but from those people in South Audley Street.”\n\n“You expected that,” said his sister.\n\n“I don’t see how that makes it any better; besides, I am not quite sure that I did expect it; at any rate I did not feel certain. There is no doubt now.”\n\n“It is better that there should be no doubt. It is much better that you should know on what ground you have to stand.”\n\n“I shall soon have no ground to stand on, none at least of my own,—not an acre,” said the unhappy man, with great bitterness in his tone.\n\n“You can’t in reality be poorer now than you were last year. You have not spent anything to speak of. There can be no doubt that Chaldicotes will be ample to pay all you owe the duke.”\n\n“It’s as much as it will; and what am I to do then? I almost think more of the seat than I do of Chaldicotes.”\n\n“You know what I advise,” said Mrs. Smith. “Ask Miss Dunstable to advance the money on the same security which the duke holds. She will be as safe then as he is now. And if you can arrange that, stand for the county against him; perhaps you may be beaten.”\n\n“I shouldn’t have a chance.”\n\n“But it would show that you are not a creature in the duke’s hands. That’s my advice,” said Mrs. Smith, with much spirit; “and if you wish, I’ll broach it to Miss Dunstable, and ask her to get her lawyer to look into it.”\n\n“If I had done this before I had run my head into that other absurdity!”\n\n“Don’t fret yourself about that; she will lose nothing by such an investment, and therefore you are not asking any favour of her. Besides, did she not make the offer? and she is just the woman to do this for you now, because she refused to do that other thing for you yesterday. You understand most things, Nathaniel; but I am not sure that you understand women; not, at any rate, such a woman as her.”\n\nIt went against the grain with Mr. Sowerby, this seeking of pecuniary assistance from the very woman whose hand he had attempted to gain about a fortnight since; but he allowed his sister to prevail. What could any man do in such straits that would not go against the grain? At the present moment he felt in his mind an infinite hatred against the duke, Mr. Fothergill, Gumption and Gagebee, and all the tribes of Gatherum Castle and South Audley Street; they wanted to rob him of that which had belonged to the Sowerbys before the name of Omnium had been heard of in the county, or in England! The great leviathan of the deep was anxious to swallow him up as a prey! He was to be swallowed up, and made away with, and put out of sight, without a pang of remorse! Any measure which could now present itself as the means of staving off so evil a day would be acceptable; and therefore he gave his sister the commission of making this second proposal to Miss Dunstable. In cursing the duke—for he did curse the duke lustily—it hardly occurred to him to think that, after all, the duke only asked for his own.\n\nAs for Mrs. Harold Smith, whatever may be the view taken of her general character as a wife and a member of society, it must be admitted that as a sister she had virtues.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII. CONSOLATION.\n\nOn the next day, at two o’clock punctually, Mark Robarts was at the “Dragon of Wantly,” walking up and down the very room in which the party had breakfasted after Harold Smith’s lecture, and waiting for the arrival of Mr. Sowerby. He had been very well able to divine what was the business on which his friend wished to see him, and he had been rather glad than otherwise to receive the summons. Judging of his friend’s character by what he had hitherto seen, he thought that Mr. Sowerby would have kept out of the way, unless he had it in his power to make some provision for these terrible bills. So he walked up and down the dingy room, impatient for the expected arrival, and thought himself wickedly ill-used in that Mr. Sowerby was not there when the clock struck a quarter to three. But when the clock struck three, Mr. Sowerby was there, and Mark Robarts’s hopes were nearly at an end.\n\n“Do you mean that they will demand nine hundred pounds?” said Robarts, standing up and glaring angrily at the member of Parliament.\n\n“I fear that they will,” said Sowerby. “I think it is best to tell you the worst, in order that we may see what can be done.”\n\n“I can do nothing, and will do nothing,” said Robarts. “They may do what they choose—what the law allows them.”\n\nAnd then he thought of Fanny and his nursery, and Lucy refusing in her pride Lord Lufton’s offer, and he turned away his face that the hard man of the world before him might not see the tear gathering in his eye.\n\n“But, Mark, my dear fellow—” said Sowerby, trying to have recourse to the power of his cajoling voice.\n\nRobarts, however, would not listen.\n\n“Mr. Sowerby,” said he, with an attempt at calmness which betrayed itself at every syllable, “it seems to me that you have robbed me. That I have been a fool, and worse than a fool, I know well; but—but—but I thought that your position in the world would guarantee me from such treatment as this.”\n\nMr. Sowerby was by no means without feeling, and the words which he now heard cut him very deeply—the more so because it was impossible that he should answer them with an attempt at indignation. He had robbed his friend, and, with all his wit, knew no words at the present moment sufficiently witty to make it seem that he had not done so.\n\n“Robarts,” said he, “you may say what you like to me now; I shall not resent it.”\n\n“Who would care for your resentment?” said the clergyman, turning on him with ferocity. “The resentment of a gentleman is terrible to a gentleman; and the resentment of one just man is terrible to another. Your resentment!”—and then he walked twice the length of the room, leaving Sowerby dumb in his seat. “I wonder whether you ever thought of my wife and children when you were plotting this ruin for me!” And then again he walked the room.\n\n“I suppose you will be calm enough presently to speak of this with some attempt to make a settlement?”\n\n“No; I will make no such attempt. These friends of yours, you tell me, have a claim on me for nine hundred pounds, of which they demand immediate payment. You shall be asked in a court of law how much of that money I have handled. You know that I have never touched—have never wanted to touch—one shilling. I will make no attempt at any settlement. My person is here, and there is my house. Let them do their worst.”\n\n“But, Mark—”\n\n“Call me by my name, sir, and drop that affectation of regard. What an ass I have been to be so cozened by a sharper!”\n\nSowerby had by no means expected this. He had always known that Robarts possessed what he, Sowerby, would have called the spirit of a gentleman. He had regarded him as a bold, open, generous fellow, able to take his own part when called on to do so, and by no means disinclined to speak his own mind; but he had not expected from him such a torrent of indignation, or thought that he was capable of such a depth of anger.\n\n“If you use such language as that, Robarts, I can only leave you.”\n\n“You are welcome. Go. You tell me that you are the messenger of these men who intend to work nine hundred pounds out of me. You have done your part in the plot, and have now brought their message. It seems to me that you had better go back to them. As for me, I want my time to prepare my wife for the destiny before her.”\n\n“Robarts, you will be sorry some day for the cruelty of your words.”\n\n“I wonder whether you will ever be sorry for the cruelty of your doings, or whether these things are really a joke to you.”\n\n“I am at this moment a ruined man,” said Sowerby. “Everything is going from me,—my place in the world, the estate of my family, my father’s house, my seat in Parliament, the power of living among my countrymen, or, indeed, of living anywhere;—but all this does not oppress me now so much as the misery which I have brought upon you.” And then Sowerby also turned away his face, and wiped from his eyes tears which were not artificial.\n\nRobarts was still walking up and down the room, but it was not possible for him to continue his reproaches after this. This is always the case. Let a man endure to heap contumely on his own head, and he will silence the contumely of others—for the moment. Sowerby, without meditating on the matter, had had some inkling of this, and immediately saw that there was at last an opening for conversation.\n\n“You are unjust to me,” said he, “in supposing that I have now no wish to save you. It is solely in the hope of doing so that I have come here.”\n\n“And what is your hope? That I should accept another brace of bills, I suppose.”\n\n“Not a brace; but one renewed bill for—”\n\n“Look here, Mr. Sowerby. On no earthly consideration that can be put before me will I again sign my name to any bill in the guise of an acceptance. I have been very weak, and am ashamed of my weakness; but so much strength as that, I hope, is left to me. I have been very wicked, and am ashamed of my wickedness; but so much right principle as that, I hope, remains. I will put my name to no other bill; not for you, not even for myself.”\n\n“But, Robarts, under your present circumstances that will be madness.”\n\n“Then I will be mad.”\n\n“Have you seen Forrest? If you will speak to him I think you will find that everything can be accommodated.”\n\n“I already owe Mr. Forrest a hundred and fifty pounds, which I obtained from him when you pressed me for the price of that horse, and I will not increase the debt. What a fool I was again there. Perhaps you do not remember that, when I agreed to buy the horse, the price was to be my contribution to the liquidation of these bills.”\n\n“I do remember it; but I will tell you how that was.”\n\n“It does not signify. It has been all of a piece.”\n\n“But listen to me. I think you would feel for me if you knew all that I have gone through. I pledge you my solemn word that I had no intention of asking you for the money when you took the horse;—indeed I had not. But you remember that affair of Lufton’s, when he came to you at your hotel in London and was so angry about an outstanding bill.”\n\n“I know that he was very unreasonable as far as I was concerned.”\n\n“He was so; but that makes no difference. He was resolved, in his rage, to expose the whole affair; and I saw that, if he did so, it would be most injurious to you, seeing that you had just accepted your stall at Barchester.” Here the poor prebendary winced terribly. “I moved heaven and earth to get up that bill. Those vultures stuck to their prey when they found the value which I attached to it, and I was forced to raise above a hundred pounds at the moment to obtain possession of it, although every shilling absolutely due on it had long since been paid. Never in my life did I wish to get money as I did to raise that hundred and twenty pounds; and as I hope for mercy in my last moments, I did that for your sake. Lufton could not have injured me in that matter.”\n\n“But you told him that you got it for twenty-five pounds.”\n\n“Yes, I told him so. I was obliged to tell him that, or I should have apparently condemned myself by showing how anxious I was to get it. And you know I could not have explained all this before him and you. You would have thrown up the stall in disgust.”\n\nWould that he had! That was Mark’s wish now,—his futile wish. In what a slough of despond had he come to wallow in consequence of his folly on that night at Gatherum Castle! He had then done a silly thing, and was he now to rue it by almost total ruin? He was sickened also with all these lies. His very soul was dismayed by the dirt through which he was forced to wade. He had become unconsciously connected with the lowest dregs of mankind, and would have to see his name mingled with theirs in the daily newspapers. And for what had he done this? Why had he thus filed his mind and made himself a disgrace to his cloth? In order that he might befriend such a one as Mr. Sowerby!\n\n“Well,” continued Sowerby, “I did get the money, but you would hardly believe the rigour of the pledge which was exacted from me for repayment. I got it from Harold Smith, and never, in my worst straits, will I again look to him for assistance. I borrowed it only for a fortnight; and in order that I might repay it, I was obliged to ask you for the price of the horse. Mark, it was on your behalf that I did all this,—indeed it was.”\n\n“And now I am to repay you for your kindness by the loss of all that I have in the world.”\n\n“If you will put the affair into the hands of Mr. Forrest, nothing need be touched,—not a hair of a horse’s back; no, not though you should be obliged to pay the whole amount yourself, gradually out of your income. You must execute a series of bills, falling due quarterly, and then—”\n\n“I will execute no bill, I will put my name to no paper in the matter; as to that my mind is fully made up. They may come and do their worst.”\n\nMr. Sowerby persevered for a long time, but he was quite unable to move the parson from this position. He would do nothing towards making what Mr. Sowerby called an arrangement, but persisted that he would remain at home at Framley, and that any one who had a claim upon him might take legal steps.\n\n“I shall do nothing myself,” he said; “but if proceedings against me be taken, I shall prove that I have never had a shilling of the money.” And in this resolution he quitted the Dragon of Wantly.\n\nMr. Sowerby at one time said a word as to the expediency of borrowing that sum of money from John Robarts; but as to this Mark would say nothing. Mr. Sowerby was not the friend with whom he now intended to hold consultation in such matters. “I am not at present prepared,” he said, “to declare what I may do; I must first see what steps others take.” And then he took his hat and went off; and mounting his horse in the yard of the Dragon of Wantly—that horse which he had now so many reasons to dislike—he slowly rode back home.\n\nMany thoughts passed through his mind during that ride, but only one resolution obtained for itself a fixture there. He must now tell his wife everything. He would not be so cruel as to let it remain untold until a bailiff were at the door, ready to walk him off to the county gaol, or until the bed on which they slept was to be sold from under them. Yes, he would tell her everything,—immediately, before his resolution could again have faded away. He got off his horse in the yard, and seeing his wife’s maid at the kitchen door, desired her to beg her mistress to come to him in the book-room. He would not allow one half-hour to pass towards the waning of his purpose. If it be ordained that a man shall drown, had he not better drown and have done with it?\n\nMrs. Robarts came to him in his room, reaching him in time to touch his arm as he entered it.\n\n“Mary says you want me. I have been gardening, and she caught me just as I came in.”\n\n“Yes, Fanny, I do want you. Sit down for a moment.” And walking across the room, he placed his whip in its proper place.\n\n“Oh, Mark, is there anything the matter?”\n\n“Yes, dearest; yes. Sit down, Fanny; I can talk to you better if you will sit.”\n\nBut she, poor lady, did not wish to sit. He had hinted at some misfortune, and therefore she felt a longing to stand by him and cling to him.\n\n“Well, there; I will if I must; but, Mark, do not frighten me. Why is your face so very wretched?”\n\n“Fanny, I have done very wrong,” he said. “I have been very foolish. I fear that I have brought upon you great sorrow and trouble.” And then he leaned his head upon his hand and turned his face away from her.\n\n“Oh, Mark, dearest Mark, my own Mark! what is it?” and then she was quickly up from her chair, and went down on her knees before him. “Do not turn from me. Tell me, Mark! tell me, that we may share it.”\n\n“Yes, Fanny, I must tell you now; but I hardly know what you will think of me when you have heard it.”\n\n“I will think that you are my own husband, Mark; I will think that—that chiefly, whatever it may be.” And then she caressed his knees, and looked up in his face, and, getting hold of one of his hands, pressed it between her own. “Even if you have been foolish, who should forgive you if I cannot?”\n\nAnd then he told it her all, beginning from that evening when Mr. Sowerby had got him into his bedroom, and going on gradually, now about the bills, and now about the horses, till his poor wife was utterly lost in the complexity of the accounts. She could by no means follow him in the details of his story; nor could she quite sympathize with him in his indignation against Mr. Sowerby, seeing that she did not comprehend at all the nature of the renewing of a bill. The only part to her of importance in the matter was the amount of money which her husband would be called upon to pay;—that and her strong hope, which was already a conviction, that he would never again incur such debts.\n\n“And how much is it, dearest, altogether?”\n\n“These men claim nine hundred pounds of me.”\n\n“Oh, dear! that is a terrible sum.”\n\n“And then there is the hundred and fifty which I have borrowed from the bank—the price of the horse, you know; and there are some other debts,—not a great deal, I think; but people will now look for every shilling that is due to them. If I have to pay it all, it will be twelve or thirteen hundred pounds.”\n\n“That will be as much as a year’s income, Mark; even with the stall.”\n\nThat was the only word of reproach she said,—if that could be called a reproach.\n\n“Yes,” he said; “and it is claimed by men who will have no pity in exacting it at any sacrifice, if they have the power. And to think that I should have incurred all this debt without having received anything for it. Oh, Fanny, what will you think of me!”\n\nBut she swore to him that she would think nothing of it;—that she would never bear it in her mind against him,—that it could have no effect in lessening her trust in him. Was he not her husband? She was so glad she knew it, that she might comfort him. And she did comfort him, making the weight seem lighter and lighter on his shoulders as he talked of it. And such weights do thus become lighter. A burden that will crush a single pair of shoulders will, when equally divided—when shared by two, each of whom is willing to take the heavier part—become light as a feather. Is not that sharing of the mind’s burdens one of the chief purposes for which a man wants a wife? For there is no folly so great as keeping one’s sorrows hidden.\n\nAnd this wife cheerfully, gladly, thankfully took her share. To endure with her lord all her lord’s troubles was easy to her; it was the work to which she had pledged herself. But to have thought that her lord had troubles not communicated to her—that would have been to her the one thing not to be borne.\n\nAnd then they discussed their plans;—what mode of escape they might have out of this terrible money difficulty. Like a true woman, Mrs. Robarts proposed at once to abandon all superfluities. They would sell all their horses; they would not sell their cows, but would sell the butter that came from them; they would sell the pony-carriage, and get rid of the groom. That the footman must go was so much a matter of course, that it was hardly mentioned. But then, as to that house at Barchester, the dignified prebendal mansion in the close—might they not be allowed to leave it unoccupied for one year longer,—perhaps to let it? The world of course must know of their misfortune; but if that misfortune was faced bravely, the world would be less bitter in its condemnation. And then, above all things, everything must be told to Lady Lufton.\n\n“You may, at any rate, believe this, Fanny,” said he, “that for no consideration which can be offered to me will I ever put my name to another bill.”\n\nThe kiss with which she thanked him for this was as warm and generous as though he had brought to her that day news of the brightest; and when he sat, as he did that evening, discussing it all, not only with his wife, but with Lucy, he wondered how it was that his troubles were now so light.\n\nWhether or no a man should have his own private pleasures, I will not now say; but it never can be worth his while to keep his sorrows private.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIV. LADY LUFTON IS TAKEN BY SURPRISE.\n\nLord Lufton, as he returned to town, found some difficulty in resolving what step he would next take. Sometimes, for a minute or two, he was half inclined to think—or rather to say to himself—that Lucy was perhaps not worth the trouble which she threw in his way. He loved her very dearly, and would willingly make her his wife, he thought or said at such moments; but— Such moments, however, were only moments. A man in love seldom loves less because his love becomes difficult. And thus, when those moments were over, he would determine to tell his mother at once, and urge her to signify her consent to Miss Robarts. That she would not be quite pleased he knew; but if he were firm enough to show that he had a will of his own in this matter, she would probably not gainsay him. He would not ask this humbly, as a favour, but request her ladyship to go through the ceremony as though it were one of those motherly duties which she as a good mother could not hesitate to perform on behalf of her son. Such was the final resolve with which he reached his chambers in the Albany.\n\nOn the next day he did not see his mother. It would be well, he thought, to have his interview with her immediately before he started for Norway, so that there might be no repetition of it; and it was on the day before he did start that he made his communication, having invited himself to breakfast in Brook Street on the occasion.\n\n“Mother,” he said, quite abruptly, throwing himself into one of the dining-room arm-chairs, “I have a thing to tell you.”\n\nHis mother at once knew that the thing was important, and with her own peculiar motherly instinct imagined that the question to be discussed had reference to matrimony. Had her son desired to speak to her about money, his tone and look would have been different; as would also have been the case—in a different way—had he entertained any thought of a pilgrimage to Pekin, or a prolonged fishing excursion to the Hudson Bay territories.\n\n“A thing, Ludovic! well; I am quite at liberty.”\n\n“I want to know what you think of Lucy Robarts?”\n\nLady Lufton became pale and frightened, and the blood ran cold to her heart. She had feared more than rejoiced in conceiving that her son was about to talk of love, but she had feared nothing so bad as this. “What do I think of Lucy Robarts?” she said, repeating her son’s words in a tone of evident dismay.\n\n“Yes, mother; you have said once or twice lately that you thought I ought to marry, and I am beginning to think so too. You selected one clergyman’s daughter for me, but that lady is going to do much better with herself—”\n\n“Indeed she is not,” said Lady Lufton sharply.\n\n“And therefore I rather think I shall select for myself another clergyman’s sister. You don’t dislike Miss Robarts, I hope?”\n\n“Oh, Ludovic!”\n\nIt was all that Lady Lufton could say at the spur of the moment.\n\n“Is there any harm in her? Have you any objection to her? Is there anything about her that makes her unfit to be my wife?”\n\nFor a moment or two Lady Lufton sat silent, collecting her thoughts. She thought that there was very great objection to Lucy Robarts, regarding her as the possible future Lady Lufton. She could hardly have stated all her reasons, but they were very cogent. Lucy Robarts had, in her eyes, neither beauty, nor style, nor manner, nor even the education which was desirable. Lady Lufton was not herself a worldly woman. She was almost as far removed from being so as a woman could be in her position. But, nevertheless, there were certain worldly attributes which she regarded as essential to the character of any young lady who might be considered fit to take the place which she herself had so long filled. It was her desire in looking for a wife for her son to combine these with certain moral excellences which she regarded as equally essential. Lucy Robarts might have the moral excellences, or she might not; but as to the other attributes Lady Lufton regarded her as altogether deficient. She could never look like a Lady Lufton, or carry herself in the county as a Lady Lufton should do. She had not that quiet personal demeanour—that dignity of repose—which Lady Lufton loved to look upon in a young married woman of rank. Lucy, she would have said, could be nobody in a room except by dint of her tongue, whereas Griselda Grantly would have held her peace for a whole evening, and yet would have impressed everybody by the majesty of her presence. Then again Lucy had no money—and, again, Lucy was only the sister of her own parish clergyman. People are rarely prophets in their own country, and Lucy was no prophet at Framley; she was none, at least, in the eyes of Lady Lufton. Once before, as may be remembered, she had had fears on this subject—fears, not so much for her son, whom she could hardly bring herself to suspect of such a folly, but for Lucy, who might be foolish enough to fancy that the lord was in love with her. Alas! alas! her son’s question fell upon the poor woman at the present moment with the weight of a terrible blow.\n\n“Is there anything about her which makes her unfit to be my wife?”\n\nThose were her son’s last words.\n\n“Dearest Ludovic, dearest Ludovic!” and she got up and came over to him, “I do think so; I do, indeed.”\n\n“Think what?” said he, in a tone that was almost angry.\n\n“I do think that she is unfit to be your wife. She is not of that class from which I would wish to see you choose.”\n\n“She is of the same class as Griselda Grantly.”\n\n“No, dearest. I think you are in error there. The Grantlys have moved in a different sphere of life. I think you must feel that they are—”\n\n“Upon my word, mother, I don’t. One man is Rector of Plumstead, and the other is Vicar of Framley. But it is no good arguing that. I want you to take to Lucy Robarts. I have come to you on purpose to ask it of you as a favour.”\n\n“Do you mean as your wife, Ludovic?”\n\n“Yes; as my wife.”\n\n“Am I to understand that you are—are engaged to her?”\n\n“Well, I cannot say that I am—not actually engaged to her. But you may take this for granted, that, as far as it lies in my power, I intend to become so. My mind is made up, and I certainly shall not alter it.”\n\n“And the young lady knows all this?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“Horrid, sly, detestable, underhand girl,” Lady Lufton said to herself, not being by any means brave enough to speak out such language before her son. What hope could there be if Lord Lufton had already committed himself by a positive offer? “And her brother, and Mrs. Robarts; are they aware of it?”\n\n“Yes; both of them.”\n\n“And both approve of it?”\n\n“Well, I cannot say that. I have not seen Mrs. Robarts, and do not know what may be her opinion. To speak my mind honestly about Mark, I do not think he does cordially approve. He is afraid of you, and would be desirous of knowing what you think.”\n\n“I am glad, at any rate, to hear that,” said Lady Lufton, gravely. “Had he done anything to encourage this, it would have been very base.” And then there was another short period of silence.\n\nLord Lufton had determined not to explain to his mother the whole state of the case. He would not tell her that everything depended on her word—that Lucy was ready to marry him only on condition that she, Lady Lufton, would desire her to do so. He would not let her know that everything depended on her—according to Lucy’s present verdict. He had a strong disinclination to ask his mother’s permission to get married; and he would have to ask it were he to tell her the whole truth. His object was to make her think well of Lucy, and to induce her to be kind, and generous, and affectionate down at Framley. Then things would all turn out comfortably when he again visited that place, as he intended to do on his return from Norway. So much he thought it possible he might effect, relying on his mother’s probable calculation that it would be useless for her to oppose a measure which she had no power of stopping by authority. But were he to tell her that she was to be the final judge, that everything was to depend on her will, then, so thought Lord Lufton, that permission would in all probability be refused.\n\n“Well, mother, what answer do you intend to give me?” he said. “My mind is positively made up. I should not have come to you had not that been the case. You will now be going down home, and I would wish you to treat Lucy as you yourself would wish to treat any girl to whom you knew that I was engaged.”\n\n“But you say that you are not engaged.”\n\n“No, I am not; but I have made my offer to her, and I have not been rejected. She has confessed that she—loves me,—not to myself, but to her brother. Under these circumstances, may I count upon your obliging me?”\n\nThere was something in his manner which almost frightened his mother, and made her think that there was more behind than was told to her. Generally speaking, his manner was open, gentle, and unguarded; but now he spoke as though he had prepared his words, and was resolved on being harsh as well as obstinate.\n\n“I am so much taken by surprise, Ludovic, that I can hardly give you an answer. If you ask me whether I approve of such a marriage, I must say that I do not; I think that you would be throwing yourself away in marrying Miss Robarts.”\n\n“That is because you do not know her.”\n\n“May it not be possible that I know her better than you do, dear Ludovic? You have been flirting with her—”\n\n“I hate that word; it always sounds to me to be vulgar.”\n\n“I will say making love to her, if you like it better; and gentlemen under these circumstances will sometimes become infatuated.”\n\n“You would not have a man marry a girl without making love to her. The fact is, mother, that your tastes and mine are not exactly the same; you like silent beauty, whereas I like talking beauty, and then—”\n\n“Do you call Miss Robarts beautiful?”\n\n“Yes, I do; very beautiful; she has the beauty that I admire. Good-bye now, mother; I shall not see you again before I start. It will be no use writing, as I shall be away so short a time, and I don’t quite know where we shall be. I shall come down to Framley immediately I return, and shall learn from you how the land lies. I have told you my wishes, and you will consider how far you think it right to fall in with them.” He then kissed her, and without waiting for her reply he took his leave.\n\nPoor Lady Lufton, when she was left to herself, felt that her head was going round and round. Was this to be the end of all her ambition,—of all her love for her son? and was this to be the result of all her kindness to the Robartses? She almost hated Mark Robarts as she reflected that she had been the means of bringing him and his sister to Framley. She thought over all his sins, his absences from the parish, his visit to Gatherum Castle, his dealings with reference to that farm which was to have been sold, his hunting, and then his acceptance of that stall, given, as she had been told, through the Omnium interest. How could she love him at such a moment as this? And then she thought of his wife. Could it be possible that Fanny Robarts, her own friend Fanny, would be so untrue to her as to lend any assistance to such a marriage as this; as not to use all her power in preventing it? She had spoken to Fanny on this very subject,—not fearing for her son, but with a general idea of the impropriety of intimacies between such girls as Lucy and such men as Lord Lufton, and then Fanny had agreed with her. Could it be possible that even she must be regarded as an enemy?\n\nAnd then by degrees Lady Lufton began to reflect what steps she had better take. In the first place, should she give in at once, and consent to the marriage? The only thing quite certain to her was this, that life would be not worth having if she were forced into a permanent quarrel with her son. Such an event would probably kill her. When she read of quarrels in other noble families—and the accounts of such quarrels will sometimes, unfortunately, force themselves upon the attention of unwilling readers—she would hug herself, with a spirit that was almost pharisaical, reflecting that her destiny was not like that of others. Such quarrels and hatreds between fathers and daughters, and mothers and sons, were in her eyes disreputable to all the persons concerned. She had lived happily with her husband, comfortably with her neighbours, respectably with the world, and, above all things, affectionately with her children. She spoke everywhere of Lord Lufton as though he were nearly perfect,—and in so speaking, she had not belied her convictions. Under these circumstances, would not any marriage be better than a quarrel?\n\nBut then, again, how much of the pride of her daily life would be destroyed by such a match as that! And might it not be within her power to prevent it without any quarrel? That her son would be sick of such a chit as Lucy before he had been married to her six months—of that Lady Lufton entertained no doubt, and therefore her conscience would not be disquieted in disturbing the consummation of an arrangement so pernicious. It was evident that the matter was not considered as settled even by her son; and also evident that he regarded the matter as being in some way dependent on his mother’s consent. On the whole, might it not be better for her—better for them all—that she should think wholly of her duty, and not of the disagreeable results to which that duty might possibly lead? It could not be her duty to accede to such an alliance; and therefore she would do her best to prevent it. Such, at least, should be her attempt in the first instance.\n\nHaving so decided, she next resolved on her course of action. Immediately on her arrival at Framley, she would send for Lucy Robarts, and use all her eloquence—and perhaps also a little of that stern dignity for which she was so remarkable—in explaining to that young lady how very wicked it was on her part to think of forcing herself into such a family as that of the Luftons. She would explain to Lucy that no happiness could come of it, that people placed by misfortune above their sphere are always miserable; and, in short, make use of all those excellent moral lessons which are so customary on such occasions. The morality might, perhaps, be thrown away; but Lady Lufton depended much on her dignified sternness. And then, having so resolved, she prepared for her journey home.\n\nVery little had been said at Framley Parsonage about Lord Lufton’s offer after the departure of that gentleman; very little, at least, in Lucy’s presence. That the parson and his wife should talk about it between themselves was a matter of course; but very few words were spoken on the matter either by or to Lucy. She was left to her own thoughts, and possibly to her own hopes.\n\nAnd then other matters came up at Framley which turned the current of interest into other tracks. In the first place there was the visit made by Mr. Sowerby to the Dragon of Wantly, and the consequent revelation made by Mark Robarts to his wife. And while that latter subject was yet new, before Fanny and Lucy had as yet made up their minds as to all the little economies which might be practised in the household without serious detriment to the master’s comfort, news reached them that Mrs. Crawley of Hogglestock had been stricken with fever. Nothing of the kind could well be more dreadful than this. To those who knew the family it seemed impossible that their most ordinary wants could be supplied if that courageous head were even for a day laid low; and then the poverty of poor Mr. Crawley was such that the sad necessities of a sick bed could hardly be supplied without assistance.\n\n“I will go over at once,” said Fanny.\n\n“My dear!” said her husband, “it is typhus, and you must first think of the children. I will go.”\n\n“What on earth could you do, Mark?” said his wife. “Men on such occasions are almost worse than useless; and then they are so much more liable to infection.”\n\n“I have no children, nor am I a man,” said Lucy, smiling; “for both of which exemptions I am thankful. I will go, and when I come back I will keep clear of the bairns.”\n\nSo it was settled, and Lucy started in the pony-carriage, carrying with her such things from the parsonage storehouse as were thought to be suitable to the wants of the sick lady at Hogglestock. When she arrived there, she made her way into the house, finding the door open, and not being able to obtain the assistance of the servant girl in ushering her in. In the parlour she found Grace Crawley, the eldest child, sitting demurely in her mother’s chair nursing an infant. She, Grace herself, was still a young child, but not the less, on this occasion of well-understood sorrow, did she go through her task not only with zeal but almost with solemnity. Her brother, a boy of six years old, was with her, and he had the care of another baby. There they sat in a cluster, quiet, grave, and silent, attending on themselves, because it had been willed by fate that no one else should attend on them.\n\n“How is your mamma, dear Grace?” said Lucy, walking up to her, and holding out her hand.\n\n“Poor mamma is very ill, indeed,” said Grace.\n\n“And papa is very unhappy,” said Bobby, the boy.\n\n“I can’t get up because of baby,” said Grace; “but Bobby can go and call papa out.”\n\n“I will knock at the door,” said Lucy, and so saying she walked up to the bedroom door, and tapped against it lightly. She repeated this for the third time before she was summoned in by a low hoarse voice, and then on entering she saw Mr. Crawley standing by the bedside with a book in his hand. He looked at her uncomfortably, in a manner which seemed to show that he was annoyed by this intrusion, and Lucy was aware that she had disturbed him while at prayers by the bedside of his wife. He came across the room, however, and shook hands with her, and answered her inquiries in his ordinary grave and solemn voice.\n\n“Mrs. Crawley is very ill,” he said, “very ill. God has stricken us heavily, but His will be done. But you had better not go to her, Miss Robarts. It is typhus.”\n\nThe caution, however, was too late; for Lucy was already by the bedside, and had taken the hand of the sick woman, which had been extended on the coverlid to greet her. “Dear Miss Robarts,” said a weak voice; “this is very good of you; but it makes me unhappy to see you here.”\n\nLucy lost no time in taking sundry matters into her own hands, and ascertaining what was most wanted in that wretched household. For it was wretched enough. Their only servant, a girl of sixteen, had been taken away by her mother as soon as it became known that Mrs. Crawley was ill with fever. The poor mother, to give her her due, had promised to come down morning and evening herself, to do such work as might be done in an hour or so; but she could not, she said, leave her child to catch the fever. And now, at the period of Lucy’s visit, no step had been taken to procure a nurse, Mr. Crawley having resolved to take upon himself the duties of that position. In his absolute ignorance of all sanatory measures, he had thrown himself on his knees to pray; and if prayers—true prayers—might succour his poor wife, of such succour she might be confident. Lucy, however, thought that other aid also was wanting to her.\n\n“If you can do anything for us,” said Mrs. Crawley, “let it be for the poor children.”\n\n“I will have them all moved from this till you are better,” said Lucy, boldly.\n\n“Moved!” said Mr. Crawley, who even now—even in his present strait—felt a repugnance to the idea that any one should relieve him of any portion of his burden.\n\n“Yes,” said Lucy; “I am sure it will be better that you should lose them for a week or two, till Mrs. Crawley may be able to leave her room.”\n\n“But where are they to go?” said he, very gloomily.\n\nAs to this Lucy was not as yet able to say anything. Indeed when she left Framley Parsonage there had been no time for discussion. She would go back and talk it all over with Fanny, and find out in what way the children might be best put out of danger. Why should they not all be harboured at the parsonage, as soon as assurance could be felt that they were not tainted with the poison of the fever? An English lady of the right sort will do all things but one for a sick neighbour; but for no neighbour will she wittingly admit contagious sickness within the precincts of her own nursery.\n\nLucy unloaded her jellies and her febrifuges, Mr. Crawley frowning at her bitterly the while. It had come to this with him, that food had been brought into his house, as an act of charity, in his very presence, and in his heart of hearts he disliked Lucy Robarts in that she had brought it. He could not cause the jars and the pots to be replaced in the pony-carriage, as he would have done had the position of his wife been different. In her state it would have been barbarous to refuse them, and barbarous also to have created the fracas of a refusal; but each parcel that was introduced was an additional weight laid on the sore withers of his pride, till the total burden became almost intolerable. All this his wife saw and recognized even in her illness, and did make some slight ineffectual efforts to give him ease; but Lucy in her new power was ruthless, and the chicken to make the chicken-broth was taken out of the basket under his very nose.\n\nBut Lucy did not remain long. She had made up her mind what it behoved her to do herself, and she was soon ready to return to Framley. “I shall be back again, Mr. Crawley,” she said, “probably this evening, and I shall stay with her till she is better.” “Nurses don’t want rooms,” she went on to say, when Mr. Crawley muttered something as to there being no bed-chamber. “I shall make up some sort of a litter near her; you’ll see that I shall be very snug.” And then she got into the pony-chaise, and drove herself home.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV. THE STORY OF KING COPHETUA.\n\nLucy as she drove herself home had much as to which it was necessary that she should arouse her thoughts. That she would go back and nurse Mrs. Crawley through her fever she was resolved. She was free agent enough to take so much on herself, and to feel sure that she could carry it through. But how was she to redeem her promise about the children? Twenty plans ran through her mind, as to farm-houses in which they might be placed, or cottages which might be hired for them; but all these entailed the want of money; and at the present moment, were not all the inhabitants of the parsonage pledged to a dire economy? This use of the pony-carriage would have been illicit under any circumstances less pressing than the present, for it had been decided that the carriage, and even poor Puck himself, should be sold. She had, however, given her promise about the children, and though her own stock of money was very low, that promise should be redeemed.\n\nWhen she reached the parsonage she was of course full of her schemes, but she found that another subject of interest had come up in her absence, which prevented her from obtaining the undivided attention of her sister-in-law to her present plans. Lady Lufton had returned that day, and immediately on her return had sent up a note addressed to Miss Lucy Robarts, which note was in Fanny’s hands when Lucy stepped out of the pony-carriage. The servant who brought it had asked for an answer, and a verbal answer had been sent, saying that Miss Robarts was away from home, and would herself send a reply when she returned. It cannot be denied that the colour came to Lucy’s face, and that her hand trembled when she took the note from Fanny in the drawing-room. Everything in the world to her might depend on what that note contained; and yet she did not open it at once, but stood with it in her hand, and when Fanny pressed her on the subject, still endeavoured to bring back the conversation to the subject of Mrs. Crawley.\n\nBut yet her mind was intent on the letter, and she had already augured ill from the handwriting and even from the words of the address. Had Lady Lufton intended to be propitious, she would have directed her letter to Miss Robarts, without the Christian name; so at least argued Lucy,—quite unconsciously, as one does argue in such matters. One forms half the conclusions of one’s life without any distinct knowledge that the premises have even passed through one’s mind.\n\nThey were now alone together, as Mark was out.\n\n“Won’t you open her letter?” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“Yes, immediately; but, Fanny, I must speak to you about Mrs. Crawley first. I must go back there this evening, and stay there; I have promised to do so, and shall certainly keep my promise. I have promised also that the children shall be taken away, and we must arrange about that. It is dreadful, the state she is in. There is no one to see to her but Mr. Crawley, and the children are altogether left to themselves.”\n\n“Do you mean that you are going back to stay?”\n\n“Yes, certainly; I have made a distinct promise that I would do so. And about the children; could not you manage for the children, Fanny,—not perhaps in the house; at least not at first perhaps?” And yet during all the time that she was thus speaking and pleading for the Crawleys, she was endeavouring to imagine what might be the contents of that letter which she held between her fingers.\n\n“And is she so very ill?” asked Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“I cannot say how ill she may be, except this, that she certainly has typhus fever. They have had some doctor or doctor’s assistant from Silverbridge; but it seems to me that they are greatly in want of better advice.”\n\n“But, Lucy, will you not read your letter? It is astonishing to me that you should be so indifferent about it.”\n\nLucy was anything but indifferent, and now did proceed to tear the envelope. The note was very short, and ran in these words,—\n\nMY DEAR MISS ROBARTS,—I am particularly anxious to see you, and shall feel much obliged to you if you can step over to me here, at Framley Court. I must apologize for taking this liberty with you, but you will probably feel that an interview here would suit us both better than one at the parsonage. Truly yours,\n\n“There; I am in for it now,” said Lucy, handing the note over to Mrs. Robarts. “I shall have to be talked to as never poor girl was talked to before; and when one thinks of what I have done, it is hard.”\n\n“Yes; and of what you have not done.”\n\n“Exactly; and of what I have not done. But I suppose I must go,” and she proceeded to re-tie the strings of her bonnet, which she had loosened.\n\n“Do you mean that you are going over at once?”\n\n“Yes; immediately. Why not? it will be better to have it over, and then I can go to the Crawleys. But, Fanny, the pity of it is that I know it all as well as though it had been already spoken; and what good can there be in my having to endure it? Can’t you fancy the tone in which she will explain to me the conventional inconveniences which arose when King Cophetua would marry the beggar’s daughter? how she will explain what Griselda went through;—not the archdeacon’s daughter, but the other Griselda?”\n\n“But it all came right with her.”\n\n“Yes; but then I am not Griselda, and she will explain how it would certainly all go wrong with me. But what’s the good when I know it all beforehand? Have I not desired King Cophetua to take himself and sceptre elsewhere?”\n\nAnd then she started, having first said another word or two about the Crawley children, and obtained a promise of Puck and the pony-carriage for the afternoon. It was also almost agreed that Puck on his return to Framley should bring back the four children with him; but on this subject it was necessary that Mark should be consulted. The present scheme was to prepare for them a room outside the house, once the dairy, at present occupied by the groom and his wife; and to bring them into the house as soon as it was manifest that there was no danger from infection. But all this was to be matter for deliberation.\n\nFanny wanted her to send over a note, in reply to Lady Lufton’s, as harbinger of her coming; but Lucy marched off, hardly answering this proposition.\n\n“What’s the use of such a deal of ceremony?” she said. “I know she’s at home; and if she is not, I shall only lose ten minutes in going.” And so she went, and on reaching the door of Framley Court house found that her ladyship was at home. Her heart almost came to her mouth as she was told so, and then, in two minutes’ time, she found herself in the little room upstairs. In that little room we found ourselves once before,—you, and I, O my reader;—but Lucy had never before visited that hallowed precinct. There was something in its air calculated to inspire awe in those who first saw Lady Lufton sitting bolt upright in the cane-bottomed arm-chair, which she always occupied when at work at her books and papers; and this she knew when she determined to receive Lucy in that apartment. But there was there another arm-chair, an easy, cozy chair, which stood by the fireside; and for those who had caught Lady Lufton napping in that chair of an afternoon, some of this awe had perhaps been dissipated.\n\n“Miss Robarts,” she said, not rising from her chair, but holding out her hand to her visitor; “I am much obliged to you for having come over to me here. You, no doubt, are aware of the subject on which I wish to speak to you, and will agree with me that it is better that we should meet here than over at the parsonage.”\n\nIn answer to which Lucy merely bowed her head, and took her seat on the chair which had been prepared for her.\n\n“My son,” continued her ladyship, “has spoken to me on the subject of—I think I understand, Miss Robarts, that there has been no engagement between you and him?”\n\n“None whatever,” said Lucy. “He made me an offer and I refused him.” This she said very sharply;—more so undoubtedly than the circumstances required; and with a brusqueness that was injudicious as well as uncourteous. But at the moment, she was thinking of her own position with reference to Lady Lufton—not to Lord Lufton; and of her feelings with reference to the lady—not to the gentleman.\n\n“Oh,” said Lady Lufton, a little startled by the manner of the communication. “Then I am to understand that there is nothing now going on between you and my son;—that the whole affair is over?”\n\n“That depends entirely upon you.”\n\n“On me! does it?”\n\n“I do not know what your son may have told you, Lady Lufton. For myself, I do not care to have any secrets from you in this matter; and as he has spoken to you about it, I suppose that such is his wish also. Am I right in presuming that he has spoken to you on the subject?”\n\n“Yes, he has; and it is for that reason that I have taken the liberty of sending for you.”\n\n“And may I ask what he has told you? I mean, of course, as regards myself,” said Lucy.\n\nLady Lufton, before she answered this question, began to reflect that the young lady was taking too much of the initiative in this conversation, and was, in fact, playing the game in her own fashion, which was not at all in accordance with those motives which had induced Lady Lufton to send for her.\n\n“He has told me that he made you an offer of marriage,” replied Lady Lufton; “a matter which, of course, is very serious to me, as his mother; and I have thought, therefore, that I had better see you, and appeal to your own good sense and judgment and high feeling. Of course you are aware—”\n\nNow was coming the lecture to be illustrated by King Cophetua and Griselda, as Lucy had suggested to Mrs. Robarts; but she succeeded in stopping it for awhile.\n\n“And did Lord Lufton tell you what was my answer?”\n\n“Not in words. But you yourself now say that you refused him; and I must express my admiration for your good—”\n\n“Wait half a moment, Lady Lufton. Your son did make me an offer. He made it to me in person, up at the parsonage, and I then refused him;—foolishly, as I now believe, for I dearly love him. But I did so from a mixture of feelings which I need not, perhaps, explain; that most prominent, no doubt, was a fear of your displeasure. And then he came again, not to me but to my brother, and urged his suit to him. Nothing can have been kinder to me, more noble, more loving, more generous, than his conduct. At first I thought, when he was speaking to myself, that he was led on thoughtlessly to say all that he did say. I did not trust his love, though I saw that he did trust it himself. But I could not but trust it when he came again—to my brother, and made his proposal to him. I don’t know whether you will understand me, Lady Lufton; but a girl placed as I am feels ten times more assurance in such a tender of affection as that, than in one made to herself, at the spur of the moment, perhaps. And then you must remember that I—I myself—I loved him from the first. I was foolish enough to think that I could know him and not love him.”\n\n“I saw all that going on,” said Lady Lufton, with a certain assumption of wisdom about her; “and took steps which I hoped would have put a stop to it in time.”\n\n“Everybody saw it. It was a matter of course,” said Lucy, destroying her ladyship’s wisdom at a blow. “Well; I did learn to love him, not meaning to do so; and I do love him with all my heart. It is no use my striving to think that I do not; and I could stand with him at the altar to-morrow and give him my hand, feeling that I was doing my duty by him, as a woman should do. And now he has told you of his love, and I believe in that as I do in my own—” And then for a moment she paused.\n\n“But, my dear Miss Robarts—” began Lady Lufton.\n\nLucy, however, had now worked herself up into a condition of power, and would not allow her ladyship to interrupt her in her speech.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Lady Lufton; I shall have done directly, and then I will hear you. And so my brother came to me, not urging this suit, expressing no wish for such a marriage, but allowing me to judge for myself, and proposing that I should see your son again on the following morning. Had I done so, I could not but have accepted him. Think of it, Lady Lufton. How could I have done other than accept him, seeing that in my heart I had accepted his love already?”\n\n“Well?” said Lady Lufton, not wishing now to put in any speech of her own.\n\n“I did not see him—I refused to do so—because I was a coward. I could not endure to come into this house as your son’s wife, and be coldly looked on by your son’s mother. Much as I loved him, much as I do love him, dearly as I prize the generous offer which he came down here to repeat to me, I could not live with him to be made the object of your scorn. I sent him word, therefore, that I would have him when you would ask me, and not before.”\n\nAnd then, having thus pleaded her cause—and pleaded as she believed the cause of her lover also—she ceased from speaking, and prepared herself to listen to the story of King Cophetua.\n\nBut Lady Lufton felt considerable difficulty in commencing her speech. In the first place she was by no means a hard-hearted or a selfish woman; and were it not that her own son was concerned, and all the glory which was reflected upon her from her son, her sympathies would have been given to Lucy Robarts. As it was, she did sympathize with her, and admire her, and to a certain extent like her. She began also to understand what it was that had brought about her son’s love, and to feel that but for certain unfortunate concomitant circumstances the girl before her might have made a fitting Lady Lufton. Lucy had grown bigger in her eyes while sitting there and talking, and had lost much of that missish want of importance—that lack of social weight which Lady Lufton in her own opinion had always imputed to her. A girl that could thus speak up and explain her own position now, would be able to speak up and explain her own, and perhaps some other positions at any future time.\n\nBut not for all or any of these reasons did Lady Lufton think of giving way. The power of making or marring this marriage was placed in her hands, as was very fitting, and that power it behoved her to use, as best she might use it, to her son’s advantage. Much as she might admire Lucy, she could not sacrifice her son to that admiration. The unfortunate concomitant circumstances still remained, and were of sufficient force, as she thought, to make such a marriage inexpedient. Lucy was the sister of a gentleman, who by his peculiar position as parish clergyman of Framley was unfitted to be the brother-in-law of the owner of Framley. Nobody liked clergymen better than Lady Lufton, or was more willing to live with them on terms of affectionate intimacy, but she could not get over the feeling that the clergyman of her own parish,—or of her son’s,—was a part of her own establishment, of her own appanage,—or of his,—and that it could not be well that Lord Lufton should marry among his own—dependants. Lady Lufton would not have used the word, but she did think it. And then, too, Lucy’s education had been so deficient. She had had no one about her in early life accustomed to the ways of,—of what shall I say, without making Lady Lufton appear more worldly than she was? Lucy’s wants in this respect, not to be defined in words, had been exemplified by the very way in which she had just now stated her case. She had shown talent, good temper, and sound judgment; but there had been no quiet, no repose about her. The species of power in young ladies which Lady Lufton most admired was the vis inertiæ belonging to beautiful and dignified reticence; of this poor Lucy had none. Then, too, she had no fortune, which, though a minor evil, was an evil; and she had no birth, in the high-life sense of the word, which was a greater evil. And then, though her eyes had sparkled when she confessed her love, Lady Lufton was not prepared to admit that she was possessed of positive beauty. Such were the unfortunate concomitant circumstances which still induced Lady Lufton to resolve that the match must be marred.\n\nBut the performance of her part in this play was much more difficult than she had imagined, and she found herself obliged to sit silent for a minute or two, during which, however, Miss Robarts made no attempt at further speech.\n\n“I am greatly struck,” Lady Lufton said at last, “by the excellent sense you have displayed in the whole of this affair; and you must allow me to say, Miss Robarts, that I now regard you with very different feelings from those which I entertained when I left London.” Upon this Lucy bowed her head, slightly but very stiffly; acknowledging rather the former censure implied than the present eulogium expressed.\n\n“But my feelings,” continued Lady Lufton, “my strongest feelings in this matter, must be those of a mother. What might be my conduct if such a marriage did take place, I need not now consider. But I must confess that I should think such a marriage very—very ill-judged. A better hearted young man than Lord Lufton does not exist, nor one with better principles, or a deeper regard for his word; but he is exactly the man to be mistaken in any hurried outlook as to his future life. Were you and he to become man and wife, such a marriage would tend to the happiness neither of him nor of you.”\n\nIt was clear that the whole lecture was now coming; and as Lucy had openly declared her own weakness, and thrown all the power of decision into the hands of Lady Lufton, she did not see why she should endure this.\n\n“We need not argue about that, Lady Lufton,” she said. “I have told you the only circumstances under which I would marry your son; and you, at any rate, are safe.”\n\n“No; I was not wishing to argue,” answered Lady Lufton, almost humbly; “but I was desirous of excusing myself to you, so that you should not think me cruel in withholding my consent. I wished to make you believe that I was doing the best for my son.”\n\n“I am sure that you think you are, and therefore no excuse is necessary.”\n\n“No; exactly; of course it is a matter of opinion, and I do think so. I cannot believe that this marriage would make either of you happy, and therefore I should be very wrong to express my consent.”\n\n“Then, Lady Lufton,” said Lucy, rising from her chair, “I suppose we have both now said what is necessary, and I will therefore wish you good-bye.”\n\n“Good-bye, Miss Robarts. I wish I could make you understand how very highly I regard your conduct in this matter. It has been above all praise, and so I shall not hesitate to say when speaking of it to your relatives.” This was disagreeable enough to Lucy, who cared but little for any praise which Lady Lufton might express to her relatives in this matter. “And pray,” continued Lady Lufton, “give my best love to Mrs. Robarts, and tell her that I shall hope to see her over here very soon, and Mr. Robarts also. I would name a day for you all to dine, but perhaps it will be better that I should have a little talk with Fanny first.”\n\nLucy muttered something, which was intended to signify that any such dinner-party had better not be made up with the intention of including her, and then took her leave. She had decidedly had the best of the interview, and there was a consciousness of this in her heart as she allowed Lady Lufton to shake hands with her. She had stopped her antagonist short on each occasion on which an attempt had been made to produce the homily which had been prepared, and during the interview had spoken probably three words for every one which her ladyship had been able to utter. But, nevertheless, there was a bitter feeling of disappointment about her heart as she walked back home; and a feeling, also, that she herself had caused her own unhappiness. Why should she have been so romantic and chivalrous and self-sacrificing, seeing that her romance and chivalry had all been to his detriment as well as to hers,—seeing that she sacrificed him as well as herself? Why should she have been so anxious to play into Lady Lufton’s hands? It was not because she thought it right, as a general social rule, that a lady should refuse a gentleman’s hand, unless the gentleman’s mother were a consenting party to the marriage. She would have held any such doctrine as absurd. The lady, she would have said, would have had to look to her own family and no further. It was not virtue but cowardice which had influenced her, and she had none of that solace which may come to us in misfortune from a consciousness that our own conduct has been blameless. Lady Lufton had inspired her with awe, and any such feeling on her part was mean, ignoble, and unbecoming the spirit with which she wished to think that she was endowed. That was the accusation which she brought against herself, and it forbade her to feel any triumph as to the result of her interview.\n\nWhen she reached the parsonage, Mark was there, and they were of course expecting her. “Well,” said she, in her short, hurried manner, “is Puck ready again? I have no time to lose, and I must go and pack up a few things. Have you settled about the children, Fanny?”\n\n“Yes; I will tell you directly; but you have seen Lady Lufton?”\n\n“Seen her! Oh, yes, of course I have seen her. Did she not send for me? and in that case it was not on the cards that I should disobey her.”\n\n“And what did she say?”\n\n“How green you are, Mark; and not only green, but impolite also, to make me repeat the story of my own disgrace. Of course she told me that she did not intend that I should marry my lord, her son; and of course I said that under those circumstances I should not think of doing such a thing.”\n\n“Lucy, I cannot understand you,” said Fanny, very gravely. “I am sometimes inclined to doubt whether you have any deep feeling in the matter or not. If you have, how can you bring yourself to joke about it?”\n\n“Well, it is singular; and sometimes I doubt myself whether I have. I ought to be pale, ought I not? and very thin, and to go mad by degrees? I have not the least intention of doing anything of the kind, and, therefore, the matter is not worth any further notice.”\n\n“But was she civil to you, Lucy?” asked Mark; “civil in her manner, you know?”\n\n“Oh, uncommonly so. You will hardly believe it, but she actually asked me to dine. She always does, you know, when she wants to show her good-humour. If you’d broken your leg, and she wished to commiserate you, she’d ask you to dinner.”\n\n“I suppose she meant to be kind,” said Fanny, who was not disposed to give up her old friend, though she was quite ready to fight Lucy’s battle, if there were any occasion for a battle to be fought.\n\n“Lucy is so perverse,” said Mark, “that it is impossible to learn from her what really has taken place.”\n\n“Upon my word, then, you know it all as well as I can tell you. She asked me if Lord Lufton had made me an offer. I said, yes. She asked next, if I meant to accept it. Not without her approval, I said. And then she asked us all to dinner. That is exactly what took place, and I cannot see that I have been perverse at all.” After that she threw herself into a chair, and Mark and Fanny stood looking at each other.\n\n“Mark,” she said, after a while, “don’t be unkind to me. I make as little of it as I can, for all our sakes. It is better so, Fanny, than that I should go about moaning, like a sick cow;” and then they looked at her, and saw that the tears were already brimming over from her eyes.\n\n“Dearest, dearest Lucy,” said Fanny, immediately going down on her knees before her, “I won’t be unkind to you again.” And then they had a great cry together.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVI. KIDNAPPING AT HOGGLESTOCK.\n\nThe great cry, however, did not take long, and Lucy was soon in the pony-carriage again. On this occasion her brother volunteered to drive her, and it was now understood that he was to bring back with him all the Crawley children. The whole thing had been arranged; the groom and his wife were to be taken into the house, and the big bedroom across the yard, usually occupied by them, was to be converted into a quarantine hospital until such time as it might be safe to pull down the yellow flag. They were about half way on their road to Hogglestock when they were overtaken by a man on horseback, whom, when he came up beside them, Mr. Robarts recognized as Dr. Arabin, Dean of Barchester, and head of the chapter to which he himself belonged. It immediately appeared that the dean also was going to Hogglestock, having heard of the misfortune that had befallen his friends there; he had, he said, started as soon as the news reached him, in order that he might ascertain how best he might render assistance. To effect this he had undertaken a ride of nearly forty miles, and explained that he did not expect to reach home again much before midnight.\n\n“You pass by Framley?” said Robarts.\n\n“Yes, I do,” said the dean.\n\n“Then of course you will dine with us as you go home; you and your horse also, which will be quite as important.” This having been duly settled, and the proper ceremony of introduction having taken place between the dean and Lucy, they proceeded to discuss the character of Mr. Crawley.\n\n“I have known him all my life,” said the dean, “having been at school and college with him, and for years since that I was on terms of the closest intimacy with him; but in spite of that, I do not know how to help him in his need. A prouder-hearted man I never met, or one less willing to share his sorrows with his friends.”\n\n“I have often heard him speak of you,” said Mark.\n\n“One of the bitterest feelings I have is that a man so dear to me should live so near to me, and that I should see so little of him. But what can I do? He will not come to my house; and when I go to his he is angry with me because I wear a shovel hat and ride on horseback.”\n\n“I should leave my hat and my horse at the borders of the last parish,” said Lucy, timidly.\n\n“Well; yes, certainly; one ought not to give offence even in such matters as that; but my coat and waistcoat would then be equally objectionable. I have changed,—in outward matters I mean,—and he has not. That irritates him, and unless I could be what I was in the old days, he will not look at me with the same eyes;” and then he rode on, in order, as he said, that the first pang of the interview might be over before Robarts and his sister came upon the scene.\n\nMr. Crawley was standing before his door, leaning over the little wooden railing, when the dean trotted up on his horse. He had come out after hours of close watching to get a few mouthfuls of the sweet summer air, and as he stood there he held the youngest of his children in his arms. The poor little baby sat there, quiet indeed, but hardly happy. This father, though he loved his offspring with an affection as intense as that which human nature can supply, was not gifted with the knack of making children fond of him; for it is hardly more than a knack, that aptitude which some men have of gaining the good graces of the young. Such men are not always the best fathers or the safest guardians; but they carry about with them a certain duc ad me which children recognize, and which in three minutes upsets all the barriers between five and five-and-forty. But Mr. Crawley was a stern man, thinking ever of the souls and minds of his bairns—as a father should do; and thinking also that every season was fitted for operating on these souls and minds—as, perhaps, he should not have done either as a father or as a teacher. And consequently his children avoided him when the choice was given them, thereby adding fresh wounds to his torn heart, but by no means quenching any of the great love with which he regarded them.\n\nHe was standing there thus with a placid little baby in his arms—a baby placid enough, but one that would not kiss him eagerly, and stroke his face with her soft little hands, as he would have had her do—when he saw the dean coming towards him. He was sharp-sighted as a lynx out in the open air, though now obliged to pore over his well-fingered books with spectacles on his nose; and thus he knew his friend from a long distance, and had time to meditate the mode of his greeting. He too doubtless had come, if not with jelly and chicken, then with money and advice;—with money and advice such as a thriving dean might offer to a poor brother clergyman; and Mr. Crawley, though no husband could possibly be more anxious for a wife’s safety than he was, immediately put his back up and began to bethink himself how these tenders might be rejected.\n\n“How is she?” were the first words which the dean spoke as he pulled up his horse close to the little gate, and put out his hand to take that of his friend.\n\n“How are you, Arabin?” said he. “It is very kind of you to come so far, seeing how much there is to keep you at Barchester. I cannot say that she is any better, but I do not know that she is worse. Sometimes I fancy that she is delirious, though I hardly know. At any rate her mind wanders, and then after that she sleeps.”\n\n“But is the fever less?”\n\n“Sometimes less and sometimes more, I imagine.”\n\n“And the children?”\n\n“Poor things; they are well as yet.”\n\n“They must be taken from this, Crawley, as a matter of course.”\n\nMr. Crawley fancied that there was a tone of authority in the dean’s advice, and immediately put himself into opposition.\n\n“I do not know how that may be; I have not yet made up my mind.”\n\n“But, my dear Crawley—”\n\n“Providence does not admit of such removals in all cases,” said he. “Among the poorer classes the children must endure such perils.”\n\n“In many cases it is so,” said the dean, by no means inclined to make an argument of it at the present moment; “but in this case they need not. You must allow me to make arrangements for sending for them, as of course your time is occupied here.”\n\nMiss Robarts, though she had mentioned her intention of staying with Mrs. Crawley, had said nothing of the Framley plan with reference to the children.\n\n“What you mean is that you intend to take the burden off my shoulders—in fact, to pay for them. I cannot allow that, Arabin. They must take the lot of their father and their mother, as it is proper that they should do.”\n\nAgain the dean had no inclination for arguing, and thought it might be well to let the question of the children drop for a little while.\n\n“And is there no nurse with her?” said he.\n\n“No, no; I am seeing to her myself at the present moment. A woman will be here just now.”\n\n“What woman?”\n\n“Well; her name is Mrs. Stubbs; she lives in the parish. She will put the younger children to bed, and—and—but it’s no use troubling you with all that. There was a young lady talked of coming, but no doubt she has found it too inconvenient. It will be better as it is.”\n\n“You mean Miss Robarts; she will be here directly; I passed her as I came here;” and as Dr. Arabin was yet speaking, the noise of the carriage wheels was heard upon the road.\n\n“I will go in now,” said Mr. Crawley, “and see if she still sleeps;” and then he entered the house, leaving the dean at the door still seated upon his horse. “He will be afraid of the infection, and I will not ask him to come in,” said Mr. Crawley to himself.\n\n“I shall seem to be prying into his poverty, if I enter unasked,” said the dean to himself. And so he remained there till Puck, now acquainted with the locality, stopped at the door.\n\n“Have you not been in?” said Robarts.\n\n“No; Crawley has been at the door talking to me; he will be here directly, I suppose;” and then Mark Robarts also prepared himself to wait till the master of the house should reappear.\n\nBut Lucy had no such punctilious misgivings; she did not much care now whether she offended Mr. Crawley or no. Her idea was to place herself by the sick woman’s bedside, and to send the four children away;—with their father’s consent if it might be; but certainly without it if that consent were withheld. So she got down from the carriage, and taking certain packages in her hand made her way direct into the house.\n\n“There’s a big bundle under the seat, Mark,” she said; “I’ll come and fetch it directly, if you’ll drag it out.”\n\nFor some five minutes the two dignitaries of the Church remained at the door, one on his cob and the other in his low carriage, saying a few words to each other and waiting till some one should again appear from the house. “It is all arranged, indeed it is,” were the first words which reached their ears, and these came from Lucy. “There will be no trouble at all, and no expense, and they shall all come back as soon as Mrs. Crawley is able to get out of bed.”\n\n“But, Miss Robarts, I can assure—” That was Mr. Crawley’s voice, heard from him as he followed Miss Robarts to the door; but one of the elder children had then called him into the sick room, and Lucy was left to do her worst.\n\n“Are you going to take the children back with you?” said the dean.\n\n“Yes; Mrs. Robarts has prepared for them.”\n\n“You can take greater liberties with my friend here than I can.”\n\n“It is all my sister’s doing,” said Robarts. “Women are always bolder in such matters than men.” And then Lucy reappeared, bringing Bobby with her, and one of the younger children.\n\n“Do not mind what he says,” said she, “but drive away when you have got them all. Tell Fanny I have put into the basket what things I could find, but they are very few. She must borrow things for Grace from Mrs. Granger’s little girl”—(Mrs. Granger was the wife of a Framley farmer);—“and, Mark, turn Puck’s head round, so that you may be off in a moment. I’ll have Grace and the other one here directly.” And then, leaving her brother to pack Bobby and his little sister on the back part of the vehicle, she returned to her business in the house. She had just looked in at Mrs. Crawley’s bed, and finding her awake, had smiled on her, and deposited her bundle in token of her intended stay, and then, without speaking a word, had gone on her errand about the children. She had called to Grace to show her where she might find such things as were to be taken to Framley, and having explained to the bairns, as well as she might, the destiny which immediately awaited them, prepared them for their departure without saying a word to Mr. Crawley on the subject. Bobby and the elder of the two infants were stowed away safely in the back part of the carriage, where they allowed themselves to be placed without saying a word. They opened their eyes and stared at the dean, who sat by on his horse, and assented to such orders as Mr. Robarts gave them,—no doubt with much surprise, but nevertheless in absolute silence.\n\n“Now, Grace, be quick, there’s a dear,” said Lucy, returning with the infant in her arms. “And, Grace, mind you are very careful about baby; and bring the basket; I’ll give it you when you are in.” Grace and the other child were then packed on to the other seat, and a basket with children’s clothes put in on the top of them. “That’ll do, Mark; good-bye; tell Fanny to be sure and send the day after to-morrow, and not to forget—” and then she whispered into her brother’s ear an injunction about certain dairy comforts which might not be spoken of in the hearing of Mr. Crawley. “Good-bye, dears; mind you are good children; you shall hear about mamma the day after to-morrow,” said Lucy; and Puck, admonished by a sound from his master’s voice, began to move just as Mr. Crawley reappeared at the house door.\n\n“Oh, oh, stop!” he said. “Miss Robarts, you really had better not—”\n\n“Go on, Mark,” said Lucy, in a whisper, which, whether audible or not by Mr. Crawley, was heard very plainly by the dean. And Mark, who had slightly arrested Puck by the reins on the appearance of Mr. Crawley, now touched the impatient little beast with his whip; and the vehicle with its freight darted off rapidly, Puck shaking his head and going away with a tremendously quick short trot which soon separated Mr. Crawley from his family.\n\n“Miss Robarts,” he began, “this step has been taken altogether without—”\n\n“Yes,” said she, interrupting him. “My brother was obliged to return at once. The children, you know, will remain all together at the parsonage; and that, I think, is what Mrs. Crawley will best like. In a day or two they will be under Mrs. Robarts’s own charge.”\n\n“But, my dear Miss Robarts, I had no intention whatever of putting the burden of my family on the shoulders of another person. They must return to their own home immediately—that is, as soon as they can be brought back.”\n\n“I really think Miss Robarts has managed very well,” said the dean. “Mrs. Crawley must be so much more comfortable to think that they are out of danger.”\n\n“And they will be quite comfortable at the parsonage,” said Lucy.\n\n“I do not at all doubt that,” said Mr. Crawley; “but too much of such comforts will unfit them for their home; and—and I could have wished that I had been consulted more at leisure before the proceeding had been taken.”\n\n“It was arranged, Mr. Crawley, when I was here before, that the children had better go away,” pleaded Lucy.\n\n“I do not remember agreeing to such a measure, Miss Robarts; however— I suppose they cannot be had back to-night?”\n\n“No, not to-night,” said Lucy. “And now I will go in to your wife.” And then she returned to the house, leaving the two gentlemen at the door. At this moment a labourer’s boy came sauntering by, and the dean, obtaining possession of his services for the custody of his horse, was able to dismount and put himself on a more equal footing for conversation with his friend.\n\n“Crawley,” said he, putting his hand affectionately on his friend’s shoulder, as they both stood leaning on the little rail before the door; “that is a good girl—a very good girl.”\n\n“Yes,” said he slowly; “she means well.”\n\n“Nay, but she does well; she does excellently. What can be better than her conduct now? While I was meditating how I might possibly assist your wife in this strait—”\n\n“I want no assistance; none, at least, from man,” said Crawley, bitterly.\n\n“Oh, my friend, think of what you are saying! Think of the wickedness which must accompany such a state of mind! Have you ever known any man able to walk alone, without assistance from his brother men?”\n\nMr. Crawley did not make any immediate answer, but putting his arms behind his back and closing his hands, as was his wont when he walked alone thinking of the general bitterness of his lot in life, began to move slowly along the road in front of his house. He did not invite the other to walk with him, but neither was there anything in his manner which seemed to indicate that he had intended to be left to himself. It was a beautiful summer afternoon, at that delicious period of the year when summer has just burst forth from the growth of spring; when the summer is yet but three days old, and all the various shades of green which nature can put forth are still in their unsoiled purity of freshness. The apple blossoms were on the trees, and the hedges were sweet with May. The cuckoo at five o’clock was still sounding his soft summer call with unabated energy, and even the common grasses of the hedgerows were sweet with the fragrance of their new growth. The foliage of the oaks was complete, so that every bough and twig was clothed; but the leaves did not yet hang heavy in masses, and the bend of every bough and the tapering curve of every twig were visible through their light green covering. There is no time of the year equal in beauty to the first week in summer; and no colour which nature gives, not even the gorgeous hues of autumn, which can equal the verdure produced by the first warm suns of May.\n\nHogglestock, as has been explained, has little to offer in the way of landskip beauty, and the clergyman’s house at Hogglestock was not placed on a green slopy bank of land, retired from the road, with its windows opening on to a lawn, surrounded by shrubs, with a view of the small church tower seen through them; it had none of that beauty which is so common to the cozy houses of our spiritual pastors in the agricultural parts of England. Hogglestock Parsonage stood bleak beside the road, with no pretty paling lined inside by hollies and laburnum, Portugal laurels and rose-trees. But, nevertheless, even Hogglestock was pretty now. There were apple-trees there covered with blossom, and the hedgerows were in full flower. There were thrushes singing, and here and there an oak-tree stood in the roadside, perfect in its solitary beauty.\n\n“Let us walk on a little,” said the dean. “Miss Robarts is with her now, and you will be better for leaving the room for a few minutes.”\n\n“No,” said he; “I must go back; I cannot leave that young lady to do my work.”\n\n“Stop, Crawley!” And the dean, putting his hand upon him, stayed him in the road. “She is doing her own work, and if you were speaking of her with reference to any other household than your own, you would say so. Is it not a comfort to you to know that your wife has a woman near her at such a time as this; and a woman, too, who can speak to her as one lady does to another?”\n\n“These are comforts which we have no right to expect. I could not have done much for poor Mary; but what a man could have done should not have been wanting.”\n\n“I am sure of it; I know it well. What any man could do by himself you would do—excepting one thing.” And the dean as he spoke looked full into the other’s face.\n\n“And what is there I would not do?” said Crawley.\n\n“Sacrifice your own pride.”\n\n“My pride?”\n\n“Yes; your own pride.”\n\n“I have had but little pride this many a day. Arabin, you do not know what my life has been. How is a man to be proud who—” And then he stopped himself, not wishing to go through the catalogue of those grievances, which, as he thought, had killed the very germs of pride within him, or to insist by spoken words on his poverty, his wants, and the injustice of his position. “No; I wish I could be proud; but the world has been too heavy to me, and I have forgotten all that.”\n\n“How long have I known you, Crawley?”\n\n“How long? Ah dear! a life-time nearly, now.”\n\n“And we were like brothers once.”\n\n“Yes; we were equal as brothers then—in our fortunes, our tastes, and our modes of life.”\n\n“And yet you would begrudge me the pleasure of putting my hand in my pocket, and relieving the inconveniences which have been thrown on you, and those you love better than yourself, by the chances of your fate in life.”\n\n“I will live on no man’s charity,” said Crawley, with an abruptness which amounted almost to an expression of anger.\n\n“And is not that pride?”\n\n“No—yes;—it is a species of pride, but not that pride of which you spoke. A man cannot be honest if he have not some pride. You yourself;—would you not rather starve than become a beggar?”\n\n“I would rather beg than see my wife starve,” said Arabin.\n\nCrawley when he heard these words turned sharply round, and stood with his back to the dean, with his hands still behind him, and with his eyes fixed upon the ground.\n\n“But in this case there is no question of begging,” continued the dean. “I, out of those superfluities which it has pleased God to put at my disposal, am anxious to assist the needs of those whom I love.”\n\n“She is not starving,” said Crawley, in a voice very bitter, but still intended to be exculpatory of himself.\n\n“No, my dear friend; I know she is not, and do not you be angry with me because I have endeavoured to put the matter to you in the strongest language I could use.”\n\n“You look at it, Arabin, from one side only; I can only look at it from the other. It is very sweet to give; I do not doubt that. But the taking of what is given is very bitter. Gift bread chokes in a man’s throat and poisons his blood, and sits like lead upon the heart. You have never tried it.”\n\n“But that is the very fault for which I blame you. That is the pride which I say you ought to sacrifice.”\n\n“And why should I be called on to do so? Is not the labourer worthy of his hire? Am I not able to work, and willing? Have I not always had my shoulder to the collar, and is it right that I should now be contented with the scraps from a rich man’s kitchen? Arabin, you and I were equal once and we were then friends, understanding each other’s thoughts and sympathizing with each other’s sorrows. But it cannot be so now.”\n\n“If there be such inability, it is all with you.”\n\n“It is all with me,—because in our connection the pain would all be on my side. It would not hurt you to see me at your table with worn shoes and a ragged shirt. I do not think so meanly of you as that. You would give me your feast to eat though I were not clad a tithe as well as the menial behind your chair. But it would hurt me to know that there were those looking at me who thought me unfit to sit in your rooms.”\n\n“That is the pride of which I speak;—false pride.”\n\n“Call it so if you will; but, Arabin, no preaching of yours can alter it. It is all that is left to me of my manliness. That poor broken reed who is lying there sick,—who has sacrificed all the world to her love for me,—who is the mother of my children, and the partner of my sorrows and the wife of my bosom,—even she cannot change me in this, though she pleads with the eloquence of all her wants. Not even for her can I hold out my hand for a dole.”\n\nThey had now come back to the door of the house, and Mr. Crawley, hardly conscious of what he was doing, was preparing to enter.\n\n“Will Mrs. Crawley be able to see me if I come in?” said the dean.\n\n“Oh, stop; no; you had better not do so,” said Mr. Crawley. “You, no doubt, might be subject to infection, and then Mrs. Arabin would be frightened.”\n\n“I do not care about it in the least,” said the dean.\n\n“But it is of no use; you had better not. Her room, I fear, is quite unfit for you to see; and the whole house, you know, may be infected.”\n\nDr. Arabin by this time was in the sitting-room; but seeing that his friend was really anxious that he should not go farther, he did not persist.\n\n“It will be a comfort to us, at any rate, to know that Miss Robarts is with her.”\n\n“The young lady is very good—very good indeed,” said Crawley; “but I trust she will return to her home to-morrow. It is impossible that she should remain in so poor a house as mine. There will be nothing here of all the things that she will want.”\n\nThe dean thought that Lucy Robarts’s wants during her present occupation of nursing would not be so numerous as to make her continued sojourn in Mrs. Crawley’s sick room impossible, and therefore took his leave with a satisfied conviction that the poor lady would not be left wholly to the somewhat unskilful nursing of her husband.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII. MR. SOWERBY WITHOUT COMPANY.\n\nAnd now there were going to be wondrous doings in West Barsetshire, and men’s minds were much disturbed. The fiat had gone forth from the high places, and the Queen had dissolved her faithful Commons. The giants, finding that they could effect little or nothing with the old House, had resolved to try what a new venture would do for them, and the hubbub of a general election was to pervade the country. This produced no inconsiderable irritation and annoyance, for the House was not as yet quite three years old; and members of Parliament, though they naturally feel a constitutional pleasure in meeting their friends and in pressing the hands of their constituents, are, nevertheless, so far akin to the lower order of humanity that they appreciate the danger of losing their seats; and the certainty of a considerable outlay in their endeavours to retain them is not agreeable to the legislative mind.\n\nNever did the old family fury between the gods and giants rage higher than at the present moment. The giants declared that every turn which they attempted to take in their country’s service had been thwarted by faction, in spite of those benign promises of assistance made to them only a few weeks since by their opponents; and the gods answered by asserting that they were driven to this opposition by the Bœotian fatuity of the giants. They had no doubt promised their aid, and were ready to give it to measures that were decently prudent; but not to a bill enabling government at its will to pension aged bishops! No; there must be some limit to their tolerance, and when such attempts as these were made that limit had been clearly passed.\n\nAll this had taken place openly only a day or two after that casual whisper dropped by Tom Towers at Miss Dunstable’s party—by Tom Towers, that most pleasant of all pleasant fellows. And how should he have known it,—he who flutters from one sweetest flower of the garden to another,\n\n“Adding sugar to the pink, and honey to the rose, So loved for what he gives, but taking nothing as he goes”?\n\nBut the whisper had grown into a rumour, and the rumour into a fact, and the political world was in a ferment. The giants, furious about their bishops’ pension bill, threatened the House—most injudiciously; and then it was beautiful to see how indignant members got up, glowing with honesty, and declared that it was base to conceive that any gentleman in that House could be actuated in his vote by any hopes or fears with reference to his seat. And so matters grew from bad to worse, and these contending parties never hit at each other with such envenomed wrath as they did now;—having entered the ring together so lately with such manifold promises of good-will, respect, and forbearance!\n\nBut going from the general to the particular, we may say that nowhere was a deeper consternation spread than in the electoral division of West Barsetshire. No sooner had the tidings of the dissolution reached the county than it was known that the duke intended to change his nominee. Mr. Sowerby had now sat for the division since the Reform Bill! He had become one of the county institutions, and by the dint of custom and long establishment had been borne with and even liked by the county gentlemen, in spite of his well-known pecuniary irregularities. Now all this was to be changed. No reason had as yet been publicly given, but it was understood that Lord Dumbello was to be returned, although he did not own an acre of land in the county. It is true that rumour went on to say that Lord Dumbello was about to form close connections with Barsetshire. He was on the eve of marrying a young lady, from the other division indeed, and was now engaged, so it was said, in completing arrangements with the government for the purchase of that noble crown property usually known as the Chace of Chaldicotes. It was also stated—this statement, however, had hitherto been only announced in confidential whispers—that Chaldicotes House itself would soon become the residence of the marquis. The duke was claiming it as his own—would very shortly have completed his claims and taken possession;—and then, by some arrangement between them, it was to be made over to Lord Dumbello.\n\nBut very contrary rumours to these got abroad also. Men said—such as dared to oppose the duke, and some few also who did not dare to oppose him when the day of battle came—that it was beyond his grace’s power to turn Lord Dumbello into a Barsetshire magnate. The crown property—such men said—was to fall into the hands of young Mr. Gresham, of Boxall Hill, in the other division, and that the terms of purchase had been already settled. And as to Mr. Sowerby’s property and the house of Chaldicotes—these opponents of the Omnium interest went on to explain—it was by no means as yet so certain that the duke would be able to enter it and take possession. The place was not to be given up to him quietly. A great fight would be made, and it was beginning to be believed that the enormous mortgages would be paid off by a lady of immense wealth. And then a dash of romance was not wanting to make these stories palatable. This lady of immense wealth had been courted by Mr. Sowerby, had acknowledged her love,—but had refused to marry him on account of his character. In testimony of her love, however, she was about to pay all his debts.\n\nIt was soon put beyond a rumour, and became manifest enough, that Mr. Sowerby did not intend to retire from the county in obedience to the duke’s behests. A placard was posted through the whole division in which no allusion was made by name to the duke, but in which Mr. Sowerby warned his friends not to be led away by any report that he intended to retire from the representation of West Barsetshire. “He had sat,” the placard said, “for the same county during the full period of a quarter of a century, and he would not lightly give up an honour that had been extended to him so often and which he prized so dearly. There were but few men now in the House whose connection with the same body of constituents had remained unbroken so long as had that which bound him to West Barsetshire; and he confidently hoped that that connection might be continued through another period of coming years till he might find himself in the glorious position of being the father of the county members of the House of Commons.” The placard said much more than this, and hinted at sundry and various questions, all of great interest to the county; but it did not say one word of the Duke of Omnium, though every one knew what the duke was supposed to be doing in the matter. He was, as it were, a great Llama, shut up in a holy of holies, inscrutable, invisible, inexorable,—not to be seen by men’s eyes or heard by their ears, hardly to be mentioned by ordinary men at such periods as these without an inward quaking. But nevertheless, it was he who was supposed to rule them. Euphemism required that his name should be mentioned at no public meetings in connection with the coming election; but, nevertheless, most men in the county believed that he could send his dog up to the House of Commons as member for West Barsetshire if it so pleased him.\n\nIt was supposed, therefore, that our friend Sowerby would have no chance; but he was lucky in finding assistance in a quarter from which he certainly had not deserved it. He had been a staunch friend of the gods during the whole of his political life,—as, indeed, was to be expected, seeing that he had been the duke’s nominee; but, nevertheless, on the present occasion, all the giants connected with the county came forward to his rescue. They did not do this with the acknowledged purpose of opposing the duke; they declared that they were actuated by a generous disinclination to see an old county member put from his seat;—but the world knew that the battle was to be waged against the great Llama. It was to be a contest between the powers of aristocracy and the powers of oligarchy, as those powers existed in West Barsetshire,—and, it may be added, that democracy would have very little to say to it, on one side or on the other. The lower order of voters, the small farmers and tradesmen, would no doubt range themselves on the side of the duke, and would endeavour to flatter themselves that they were thereby furthering the views of the liberal side; but they would in fact be led to the poll by an old-fashioned, time-honoured adherence to the will of their great Llama; and by an apprehension of evil if that Llama should arise and shake himself in his wrath. What might not come to the county if the Llama were to walk himself off, he with his satellites and armies and courtiers? There he was, a great Llama; and though he came among them but seldom, and was scarcely seen when he did come, nevertheless—and not the less but rather the more—was obedience to him considered as salutary and opposition regarded as dangerous. A great rural Llama is still sufficiently mighty in rural England.\n\nBut the priest of the temple, Mr. Fothergill, was frequent enough in men’s eyes, and it was beautiful to hear with how varied a voice he alluded to the things around him and to the changes which were coming. To the small farmers, not only on the Gatherum property but on others also, he spoke of the duke as a beneficent influence, shedding prosperity on all around him, keeping up prices by his presence, and forbidding the poor rates to rise above one and fourpence in the pound by the general employment which he occasioned. Men must be mad, he thought, who would willingly fly in the duke’s face. To the squires from a distance he declared that no one had a right to charge the duke with any interference;—as far, at least, as he knew the duke’s mind. People would talk of things of which they understood nothing. Could any one say that he had traced a single request for a vote home to the duke? All this did not alter the settled conviction on men’s minds; but it had its effect, and tended to increase the mystery in which the duke’s doings were enveloped. But to his own familiars, to the gentry immediately around him, Mr. Fothergill merely winked his eye. They knew what was what, and so did he. The duke had never been bit yet in such matters, and Mr. Fothergill did not think that he would now submit himself to any such operation.\n\nI never heard in what manner and at what rate Mr. Fothergill received remuneration for the various services performed by him with reference to the duke’s property in Barsetshire; but I am very sure that, whatever might be the amount, he earned it thoroughly. Never was there a more faithful partisan, or one who, in his partisanship, was more discreet. In this matter of the coming election he declared that he himself,—personally, on his own hook,—did intend to bestir himself actively on behalf of Lord Dumbello. Mr. Sowerby was an old friend of his, and a very good fellow. That was true. But all the world must admit that Sowerby was not in the position which a county member ought to occupy. He was a ruined man, and it would not be for his own advantage that he should be maintained in a position which was fit only for a man of property. He knew—he, Fothergill—that Mr. Sowerby must abandon all right and claim to Chaldicotes; and if so, what would be more absurd than to acknowledge that he had a right and claim to the seat in Parliament? As to Lord Dumbello, it was probable that he would soon become one of the largest landowners in the county; and, as such, who could be more fit for the representation? Beyond this, Mr. Fothergill was not ashamed to confess—so he said—that he hoped to hold Lord Dumbello’s agency. It would be compatible with his other duties, and therefore, as a matter of course, he intended to support Lord Dumbello;—he himself, that is. As to the duke’s mind in the matter—! But I have already explained how Mr. Fothergill disposed of that.\n\nIn these days, Mr. Sowerby came down to his own house—for ostensibly it was still his own house—but he came very quietly, and his arrival was hardly known in his own village. Though his placard was stuck up so widely, he himself took no electioneering steps; none, at least, as yet. The protection against arrest which he derived from Parliament would soon be over, and those who were most bitter against the duke averred that steps would be taken to arrest him, should he give sufficient opportunity to the myrmidons of the law. That he would, in such case, be arrested was very likely; but it was not likely that this would be done in any way at the duke’s instance. Mr. Fothergill declared indignantly that this insinuation made him very angry; but he was too prudent a man to be very angry at anything, and he knew how to make capital on his own side of charges such as these which overshot their own mark.\n\nMr. Sowerby came down very quietly to Chaldicotes, and there he remained for a couple of days, quite alone. The place bore a very different aspect now to that which we noticed when Mark Robarts drove up to it, in the early pages of this little narrative. There were no lights in the windows now, and no voices came from the stables; no dogs barked, and all was dead and silent as the grave. During the greater portion of those two days he sat alone within the house, almost unoccupied. He did not even open his letters, which lay piled on a crowded table in the small breakfast parlour in which he sat; for the letters of such men come in piles, and there are few of them which are pleasant in the reading. There he sat, troubled with thoughts which were sad enough, now and then moving to and fro the house, but for the most part occupied in thinking over the position to which he had brought himself. What would he be in the world’s eye, if he ceased to be the owner of Chaldicotes, and ceased also to be the member for his county? He had lived ever before the world, and, though always harassed by encumbrances, had been sustained and comforted by the excitement of a prominent position. His debts and difficulties had hitherto been bearable, and he had borne them with ease so long that he had almost taught himself to think that they would never be unendurable. But now,—\n\nThe order for foreclosing had gone forth, and the harpies of the law, by their present speed in sticking their claws into the carcase of his property, were atoning to themselves for the delay with which they had hitherto been compelled to approach their prey. And the order as to his seat had gone forth also. That placard had been drawn up by the combined efforts of his sister, Miss Dunstable, and a certain well-known electioneering agent, named Closerstill, presumed to be in the interest of the giants. But poor Sowerby had but little confidence in the placard. No one knew better than he how great was the duke’s power.\n\nHe was hopeless, therefore, as he walked about through those empty rooms, thinking of his past life and of that life which was to come. Would it not be well for him that he were dead, now that he was dying to all that had made the world pleasant! We see and hear of such men as Mr. Sowerby, and are apt to think that they enjoy all that the world can give, and that they enjoy that all without payment either in care or labour; but I doubt that, with even the most callous of them, their periods of wretchedness must be frequent, and that wretchedness very intense. Salmon and lamb in February and green pease and new potatoes in March can hardly make a man happy, even though nobody pays for them; and the feeling that one is an antecedentem scelestum after whom a sure, though lame, Nemesis is hobbling, must sometimes disturb one’s slumbers. On the present occasion Scelestus felt that his Nemesis had overtaken him. Lame as she had been, and swift as he had run, she had mouthed him at last, and there was nothing left for him but to listen to the “whoop” set up at the sight of his own death-throes.\n\nIt was a melancholy, dreary place now, that big house of Chaldicotes; and though the woods were all green with their early leaves, and the gardens thick with flowers, they also were melancholy and dreary. The lawns were untrimmed and weeds were growing through the gravel, and here and there a cracked Dryad, tumbled from her pedestal and sprawling in the grass, gave a look of disorder to the whole place. The wooden trellis-work was shattered here and bending there, the standard rose-trees were stooping to the ground, and the leaves of the winter still encumbered the borders. Late in the evening of the second day Mr. Sowerby strolled out, and went through the gardens into the wood. Of all the inanimate things of the world this wood of Chaldicotes was the dearest to him. He was not a man to whom his companions gave much credit for feelings or thoughts akin to poetry, but here, out in the Chace, his mind would be almost poetical. While wandering among the forest trees, he became susceptible of the tenderness of human nature: he would listen to the birds singing, and pick here and there a wild flower on his path. He would watch the decay of the old trees and the progress of the young, and make pictures in his eyes of every turn in the wood. He would mark the colour of a bit of road as it dipped into a dell, and then, passing through a water-course, rose brown, rough, irregular, and beautiful against the bank on the other side. And then he would sit and think of his old family: how they had roamed there time out of mind in those Chaldicotes woods, father and son and grandson in regular succession, each giving them over, without blemish or decrease, to his successor. So he would sit; and so he did sit even now, and, thinking of these things, wished that he had never been born.\n\nIt was dark night when he returned to the house, and as he did so he resolved that he would quit the place altogether, and give up the battle as lost. The duke should take it and do as he pleased with it; and as for the seat in Parliament, Lord Dumbello, or any other equally gifted young patrician, might hold it for him. He would vanish from the scene and betake himself to some land from whence he would be neither heard nor seen, and there—starve. Such were now his future outlooks into the world; and yet, as regards health and all physical capacities, he knew that he was still in the prime of his life. Yes; in the prime of his life! But what could he do with what remained to him of such prime? How could he turn either his mind or his strength to such account as might now be serviceable? How could he, in his sore need, earn for himself even the barest bread? Would it not be better for him that he should die? Let not any one covet the lot of a spendthrift, even though the days of his early pease and champagne seem to be unnumbered; for that lame Nemesis will surely be up before the game has been all played out.\n\nWhen Mr. Sowerby reached his house he found that a message by telegraph had arrived for him in his absence. It was from his sister, and it informed him that she would be with him that night. She was coming down by the mail train, had telegraphed to Barchester for post-horses, and would be at Chaldicotes about two hours after midnight. It was therefore manifest enough that her business was of importance.\n\nExactly at two the Barchester post-chaise did arrive, and Mrs. Harold Smith, before she retired to her bed, was closeted for about an hour with her brother.\n\n“Well,” she said, the following morning, as they sat together at the breakfast-table, “what do you say to it now? If you accept her offer you should be with her lawyer this afternoon.”\n\n“I suppose I must accept it,” said he.\n\n“Certainly, I think so. No doubt it will take the property out of your own hands as completely as though the duke had it, but it will leave you the house, at any rate, for your life.”\n\n“What good will the house be, when I can’t keep it up?”\n\n“But I am not so sure of that. She will not want more than her fair interest; and as it will be thoroughly well managed, I should think that there would be something over—something enough to keep up the house. And then, you know, we must have some place in the country.”\n\n“I tell you fairly, Harriet, that I will have nothing further to do with Harold in the way of money.”\n\n“Ah! that was because you would go to him. Why did you not come to me? And then, Nathaniel, it is the only way in which you can have a chance of keeping the seat. She is the queerest woman I ever met, but she seems resolved on beating the duke.”\n\n“I do not quite understand it, but I have not the slightest objection.”\n\n“She thinks that he is interfering with young Gresham about the crown property. I had no idea that she had so much business at her fingers’ ends. When I first proposed the matter she took it up quite as a lawyer might, and seemed to have forgotten altogether what occurred about that other matter.”\n\n“I wish I could forget it also,” said Mr. Sowerby.\n\n“I really think that she does. When I was obliged to make some allusion to it—at least I felt myself obliged, and was sorry afterwards that I did—she merely laughed—a great loud laugh as she always does, and then went on about the business. However, she was clear about this, that all the expenses of the election should be added to the sum to be advanced by her, and that the house should be left to you without any rent. If you choose to take the land round the house you must pay for it, by the acre, as the tenants do. She was as clear about it all as though she had passed her life in a lawyer’s office.”\n\nMy readers will now pretty well understand what last step that excellent sister, Mrs. Harold Smith, had taken on her brother’s behalf, nor will they be surprised to learn that in the course of the day Mr. Sowerby hurried back to town and put himself into communication with Miss Dunstable’s lawyer.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVIII. IS THERE CAUSE OR JUST IMPEDIMENT?\n\nI now purpose to visit another country house in Barsetshire, but on this occasion our sojourn shall be in the eastern division, in which, as in every other county in England, electioneering matters are paramount at the present moment. It has been mentioned that Mr. Gresham, junior, young Frank Gresham as he was always called, lived at a place called Boxall Hill. This property had come to his wife by will, and he was now settled there,—seeing that his father still held the family seat of the Greshams at Greshamsbury.\n\nAt the present moment Miss Dunstable was staying at Boxall Hill with Mrs. Frank Gresham. They had left London,—as, indeed, all the world had done, to the terrible dismay of the London tradesmen. This dissolution of Parliament was ruining everybody except the country publicans, and had of course destroyed the London season among other things.\n\nMrs. Harold Smith had only just managed to catch Miss Dunstable before she left London; but she did do so, and the great heiress had at once seen her lawyers, and instructed them how to act with reference to the mortgages on the Chaldicotes property. Miss Dunstable was in the habit of speaking of herself and her own pecuniary concerns as though she herself were rarely allowed to meddle in their management; but this was one of those small jokes which she ordinarily perpetrated; for in truth few ladies, and perhaps not many gentlemen, have a more thorough knowledge of their own concerns or a more potent voice in their own affairs, than was possessed by Miss Dunstable. Circumstances had lately brought her much into Barsetshire and she had there contracted very intimate friendships. She was now disposed to become, if possible, a Barsetshire proprietor, and with this view had lately agreed with young Mr. Gresham that she would become the purchaser of the Crown property. As, however, the purchase had been commenced in his name, it was so to be continued; but now, as we are aware, it was rumoured that, after all, the duke, or, if not the duke, then the Marquis of Dumbello, was to be the future owner of the Chace. Miss Dunstable, however, was not a person to give up her object if she could attain it, nor, under the circumstances, was she at all displeased at finding herself endowed with the power of rescuing the Sowerby portion of the Chaldicotes property from the duke’s clutches. Why had the duke meddled with her, or with her friend, as to the other property? Therefore it was arranged that the full amount due to the duke on mortgage should be ready for immediate payment; but it was arranged also that the security as held by Miss Dunstable should be very valid.\n\nMiss Dunstable, at Boxall Hill or at Greshamsbury, was a very different person from Miss Dunstable in London; and it was this difference which so much vexed Mrs. Gresham; not that her friend omitted to bring with her into the country her London wit and aptitude for fun, but that she did not take with her up to town the genuine goodness and love of honesty which made her loveable in the country. She was as it were two persons, and Mrs. Gresham could not understand that any lady should permit herself to be more worldly at one time of the year than at another—or in one place than in any other.\n\n“Well, my dear, I am heartily glad we’ve done with that,” Miss Dunstable said to her, as she sat herself down to her desk in the drawing-room on the first morning after her arrival at Boxall Hill.\n\n“What does ‘that’ mean?” said Mrs. Gresham.\n\n“Why, London and smoke and late hours, and standing on one’s legs for four hours at a stretch on the top of one’s own staircase, to be bowed at by any one who chooses to come. That’s all done—for one year, at any rate.”\n\n“You know you like it.”\n\n“No, Mary; that’s just what I don’t know. I don’t know whether I like it or not. Sometimes, when the spirit of that dearest of all women, Mrs. Harold Smith, is upon me, I think that I do like it; but then again, when other spirits are on me, I think that I don’t.”\n\n“And who are the owners of the other spirits?”\n\n“Oh! you are one, of course. But you are a weak little thing, by no means able to contend with such a Samson as Mrs. Harold. And then you are a little given to wickedness yourself, you know. You’ve learned to like London well enough since you sat down to the table of Dives. Your uncle,—he’s the real impracticable, unapproachable Lazarus who declares that he can’t come down because of the big gulf. I wonder how he’d behave, if somebody left him ten thousand a year?”\n\n“Uncommonly well, I am sure.”\n\n“Oh, yes; he is a Lazarus now, so of course we are bound to speak well of him; but I should like to see him tried. I don’t doubt but what he’d have a house in Belgrave Square, and become noted for his little dinners before the first year of his trial was over.”\n\n“Well, and why not? You would not wish him to be an anchorite?”\n\n“I am told that he is going to try his luck,—not with ten thousand a year, but with one or two.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“Jane tells me that they all say at Greshamsbury that he is going to marry Lady Scatcherd.” Now Lady Scatcherd was a widow living in those parts; an excellent woman, but one not formed by nature to grace society of the highest order.\n\n“What!” exclaimed Mrs. Gresham, rising up from her chair while her eyes flashed with anger at such a rumour.\n\n“Well, my dear, don’t eat me. I don’t say it is so; I only say that Jane said so.”\n\n“Then you ought to send Jane out of the house.”\n\n“You may be sure of this, my dear: Jane would not have told me if somebody had not told her.”\n\n“And you believed it?”\n\n“I have said nothing about that.”\n\n“But you look as if you had believed it.”\n\n“Do I? Let us see what sort of a look it is, this look of faith.” And Miss Dunstable got up and went to the glass over the fire-place. “But, Mary, my dear, ain’t you old enough to know that you should not credit people’s looks? You should believe nothing now-a-days; and I did not believe the story about poor Lady Scatcherd. I know the doctor well enough to be sure that he is not a marrying man.”\n\n“What a nasty, hackneyed, false phrase that is—that of a marrying man! It sounds as though some men were in the habit of getting married three or four times a month.”\n\n“It means a great deal all the same. One can tell very soon whether a man is likely to marry or no.”\n\n“And can one tell the same of a woman?”\n\n“The thing is so different. All unmarried women are necessarily in the market; but if they behave themselves properly they make no signs. Now there was Griselda Grantly; of course she intended to get herself a husband, and a very grand one she has got; but she always looked as though butter would not melt in her mouth. It would have been very wrong to call her a marrying girl.”\n\n“Oh, of course she was,” says Mrs. Gresham, with that sort of acrimony which one pretty young woman so frequently expresses with reference to another. “But if one could always tell of a woman, as you say you can of a man, I should be able to tell of you. Now, I wonder whether you are a marrying woman? I have never been able to make up my mind yet.”\n\nMiss Dunstable remained silent for a few moments, as though she were at first minded to take the question as being, in some sort, one made in earnest; but then she attempted to laugh it off. “Well, I wonder at that,” said she, “as it was only the other day I told you how many offers I had refused.”\n\n“Yes; but you did not tell me whether any had been made that you meant to accept.”\n\n“None such was ever made to me. Talking of that, I shall never forget your cousin, the Honourable George.”\n\n“He is not my cousin.”\n\n“Well, your husband’s. It would not be fair to show a man’s letters; but I should like to show you his.”\n\n“You are determined, then, to remain single?”\n\n“I didn’t say that. But why do you cross-question me so?”\n\n“Because I think so much about you. I am afraid that you will become so afraid of men’s motives as to doubt that any one can be honest. And yet sometimes I think you would be a happier woman and a better woman, if you were married.”\n\n“To such an one as the Honourable George, for instance?”\n\n“No, not to such an one as him; you have probably picked out the worst.”\n\n“Or to Mr. Sowerby?”\n\n“Well, no; not to Mr. Sowerby, either. I would not have you marry any man that looked to you for your money principally.”\n\n“And how is it possible that I should expect any one to look to me principally for anything else? You don’t see my difficulty, my dear? If I had only five hundred a year, I might come across some decent middle-aged personage, like myself, who would like me, myself, pretty well, and would like my little income—pretty well also. He would not tell me any violent lie, and perhaps no lie at all. I should take to him in the same sort of way, and we might do very well. But, as it is, how is it possible that any disinterested person should learn to like me? How could such a man set about it? If a sheep have two heads, is not the fact of the two heads the first and, indeed, only thing which the world regards in that sheep? Must it not be so as a matter of course? I am a sheep with two heads. All this money which my father put together, and which has been growing since like grass under May showers, has turned me into an abortion. I am not the giantess eight feet high, or the dwarf that stands in the man’s hand,—”\n\n“Or the two-headed sheep—”\n\n“But I am the unmarried woman with—half a dozen millions of money—as I believe some people think. Under such circumstances have I a fair chance of getting my own sweet bit of grass to nibble, like any ordinary animal with one head? I never was very beautiful, and I am not more so now than I was fifteen years ago.”\n\n“I am quite sure it is not that which hinders it. You would not call yourself plain; and even plain women are married every day, and are loved, too, as well as pretty women.”\n\n“Are they? Well, we won’t say more about that; but I don’t expect a great many lovers on account of my beauty. If ever you hear of such an one, mind you tell me.”\n\nIt was almost on Mrs. Gresham’s tongue to say that she did know of one such—meaning her uncle. But in truth, she did not know any such thing; nor could she boast to herself that she had good grounds for feeling that it was so—certainly none sufficient to justify her in speaking of it. Her uncle had said no word to her on the matter, and had been confused and embarrassed when the idea of such a marriage was hinted to him. But, nevertheless, Mrs. Gresham did think that each of these two was well inclined to love the other, and that they would be happier together than they would be single. The difficulty, however, was very great, for the doctor would be terribly afraid of being thought covetous in regard to Miss Dunstable’s money; and it would hardly be expected that she should be induced to make the first overture to the doctor.\n\n“My uncle would be the only man that I can think of that would be at all fit for you,” said Mrs. Gresham, boldly.\n\n“What, and rob poor Lady Scatcherd!” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n“Oh, very well. If you choose to make a joke of his name in that way, I have done.”\n\n“Why, God bless the girl! what does she want me to say? And as for joking, surely that is innocent enough. You’re as tender about the doctor as though he were a girl of seventeen.”\n\n“It’s not about him; but it’s such a shame to laugh at poor dear Lady Scatcherd. If she were to hear it she’d lose all comfort in having my uncle near her.”\n\n“And I’m to marry him, so that she may be safe with her friend!”\n\n“Very well; I have done.” And Mrs. Gresham, who had already got up from her seat, employed herself very sedulously in arranging flowers which had been brought in for the drawing-room tables. Thus they remained silent for a minute or two, during which she began to reflect that, after all, it might probably be thought that she also was endeavouring to catch the great heiress for her uncle.\n\n“And now you are angry with me,” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n“No, I am not.”\n\n“Oh, but you are. Do you think I’m such a fool as not to see when a person’s vexed? You wouldn’t have twitched that geranium’s head off if you’d been in a proper frame of mind.”\n\n“I don’t like that joke about Lady Scatcherd.”\n\n“And is that all, Mary? Now do try and be true, if you can. You remember the bishop? Magna est veritas.”\n\n“The fact is you’ve got into such a way of being sharp, and saying sharp things among your friends up in London, that you can hardly answer a person without it.”\n\n“Can’t I? Dear, dear, what a Mentor you are, Mary! No poor lad that ever ran up from Oxford for a spree in town got so lectured for his dissipation and iniquities as I do. Well, I beg Dr. Thorne’s pardon, and Lady Scatcherd’s, and I won’t be sharp any more; and I will—let me see, what was it I was to do? Marry him myself, I believe; was not that it?”\n\n“No; you’re not half good enough for him.”\n\n“I know that. I’m quite sure of that. Though I am so sharp, I’m very humble. You can’t accuse me of putting any very great value on myself.”\n\n“Perhaps not as much as you ought to do—on yourself.”\n\n“Now, what do you mean, Mary? I won’t be bullied and teased, and have innuendos thrown out at me, because you’ve got something on your mind, and don’t quite dare to speak it out. If you have got anything to say, say it.”\n\nBut Mrs. Gresham did not choose to say it at that moment. She held her peace, and went on arranging her flowers—now with a more satisfied air, and without destruction to the geraniums. And when she had grouped her bunches properly she carried the jar from one part of the room to another, backwards and forwards, trying the effect of the colours, as though her mind was quite intent upon her flowers, and was for the moment wholly unoccupied with any other subject.\n\nBut Miss Dunstable was not the woman to put up with this. She sat silent in her place, while her friend made one or two turns about the room; and then she got up from her seat also. “Mary,” she said, “give over about those wretched bits of green branches and leave the jars where they are. You’re trying to fidget me into a passion.”\n\n“Am I?” said Mrs. Gresham, standing opposite to a big bowl, and putting her head a little on one side, as though she could better look at her handiwork in that position.\n\n“You know you are; and it’s all because you lack courage to speak out. You didn’t begin at me in this way for nothing.”\n\n“I do lack courage. That’s just it,” said Mrs. Gresham, still giving a twist here and a set there to some of the small sprigs which constituted the background of her bouquet. “I do lack courage—to have ill motives imputed to me. I was thinking of saying something, and I am afraid, and therefore I will not say it. And now, if you like, I will be ready to take you out in ten minutes.”\n\nBut Miss Dunstable was not going to be put off in this way. And to tell the truth, I must admit that her friend Mrs. Gresham was not using her altogether well. She should either have held her peace on the matter altogether,—which would probably have been her wiser course,—or she should have declared her own ideas boldly, feeling secure in her own conscience as to her own motives. “I shall not stir from this room,” said Miss Dunstable, “till I have had this matter out with you. And as for imputations,—my imputing bad motives to you,—I don’t know how far you may be joking, and saying what you call sharp things to me; but you have no right to think that I should think evil of you. If you really do think so, it is treason to the love I have for you. If I thought that you thought so, I could not remain in the house with you. What! you are not able to know the difference which one makes between one’s real friends and one’s mock friends! I don’t believe it of you, and I know you are only striving to bully me.” And Miss Dunstable now took her turn of walking up and down the room.\n\n“Well, she shan’t be bullied,” said Mrs. Gresham, leaving her flowers, and putting her arm round her friend’s waist;—“at least, not here, in this house, although she is sometimes such a bully herself.”\n\n“Mary, you have gone too far about this to go back. Tell me what it was that was on your mind, and as far as it concerns me, I will answer you honestly.”\n\nMrs. Gresham now began to repent that she had made her little attempt. That uttering of hints in a half-joking way was all very well, and might possibly bring about the desired result, without the necessity of any formal suggestion on her part; but now she was so brought to book that she must say something formal. She must commit herself to the expression of her own wishes, and to an expression also of an opinion as to what had been the wishes of her friend; and this she must do without being able to say anything as to the wishes of that third person.\n\n“Well,” she said, “I suppose you know what I meant.”\n\n“I suppose I did,” said Miss Dunstable; “but it is not at all the less necessary that you should say it out. I am not to commit myself by my interpretation of your thoughts, while you remain perfectly secure in having only hinted your own. I hate hints, as I do—the mischief. I go in for the bishop’s doctrine. Magna est veritas.”\n\n“Well, I don’t know,” said Mrs. Gresham.\n\n“Ah! but I do,” said Miss Dunstable. “And therefore go on, or for ever hold your peace.”\n\n“That’s just it,” said Mrs. Gresham.\n\n“What’s just it?” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n“The quotation out of the Prayer Book which you finished just now. ‘If any of you know cause or just impediment why these two persons should not be joined together in holy matrimony, ye are to declare it. This is the first time of asking.’ Do you know any cause, Miss Dunstable?”\n\n“Do you know any, Mrs. Gresham?”\n\n“None, on my honour!” said the younger lady, putting her hand upon her breast.\n\n“Ah! but do you not?” and Miss Dunstable caught hold of her arm, and spoke almost abruptly in her energy.\n\n“No, certainly not. What impediment? If I did, I should not have broached the subject. I declare I think you would both be very happy together. Of course, there is one impediment; we all know that. That must be your look out.”\n\n“What do you mean? What impediment?”\n\n“Your own money.”\n\n“Psha! Did you find that an impediment in marrying Frank Gresham?”\n\n“Ah! the matter was so different there. He had much more to give than I had, when all was counted. And I had no money when we—when we were first engaged.” And the tears came into her eyes as she thought of the circumstances of her early love;—all of which have been narrated in the county chronicles of Barsetshire, and may now be read by men and women interested therein.\n\n“Yes; yours was a love match. I declare, Mary, I often think that you are the happiest woman of whom I ever heard; to have it all to give, when you were so sure that you were loved while you yet had nothing.”\n\n“Yes; I was sure,” and she wiped the sweet tears from her eyes, as she remembered a certain day when a certain youth had come to her, claiming all kinds of privileges in a very determined manner. She had been no heiress then. “Yes; I was sure. But now with you, dear, you can’t make yourself poor again. If you can trust no one—”\n\n“I can. I can trust him. As regards that I do trust him altogether. But how can I tell that he would care for me?”\n\n“Do you not know that he likes you?”\n\n“Ah, yes; and so he does Lady Scatcherd.”\n\n“Miss Dunstable!”\n\n“And why not Lady Scatcherd, as well as me? We are of the same kind—come from the same class.”\n\n“Not quite that, I think.”\n\n“Yes, from the same class; only I have managed to poke myself up among dukes and duchesses, whereas she has been content to remain where God placed her. Where I beat her in art, she beats me in nature.”\n\n“You know you are talking nonsense.”\n\n“I think that we are both doing that—absolute nonsense; such as schoolgirls of eighteen talk to each other. But there is a relief in it; is there not? It would be a terrible curse to have to talk sense always. Well, that’s done; and now let us go out.”\n\nMrs. Gresham was sure after this that Miss Dunstable would be a consenting party to the little arrangement which she contemplated. But of that she had felt but little doubt for some considerable time past. The difficulty lay on the other side, and all that she had as yet done was to convince herself that she would be safe in assuring her uncle of success if he could be induced to take the enterprise in hand. He was to come to Boxall Hill that evening, and to remain there for a day or two. If anything could be done in the matter, now would be the time for doing it. So at least thought Mrs. Gresham.\n\nThe doctor did come, and did remain for the allotted time at Boxall Hill; but when he left, Mrs. Gresham had not been successful. Indeed, he did not seem to enjoy his visit as was usual with him; and there was very little of that pleasant friendly intercourse which for some time past had been customary between him and Miss Dunstable. There were no passages of arms between them; no abuse from the doctor against the lady’s London gaiety; no raillery from the lady as to the doctor’s country habits. They were very courteous to each other, and, as Mrs. Gresham thought, too civil by half; nor, as far as she could see, did they ever remain alone in each other’s company for five minutes at a time during the whole period of the doctor’s visit. What, thought Mrs. Gresham to herself,—what if she had set these two friends at variance with each other, instead of binding them together in the closest and most durable friendship!\n\nBut still she had an idea that, as she had begun to play this game, she must play it out. She felt conscious that what she had done must do evil, unless she could so carry it on as to make it result in good. Indeed, unless she could so manage, she would have done a manifest injury to Miss Dunstable in forcing her to declare her thoughts and feelings. She had already spoken to her uncle in London, and though he had said nothing to show that he approved of her plan, neither had he said anything to show that he disapproved it. Therefore she had hoped through the whole of those three days that he would make some sign,—at any rate to her; that he would in some way declare what were his own thoughts on this matter. But the morning of his departure came, and he had declared nothing.\n\n“Uncle,” she said, in the last five minutes of his sojourn there, after he had already taken leave of Miss Dunstable and shaken hands with Mrs. Gresham, “have you ever thought of what I said to you up in London?”\n\n“Yes, Mary; of course I have thought about it. Such an idea as that, when put into a man’s head, will make itself thought about.”\n\n“Well; and what next? Do talk to me about it. Do not be so hard and unlike yourself.”\n\n“I have very little to say about it.”\n\n“I can tell you this for certain, you may if you like.”\n\n“Mary! Mary!”\n\n“I would not say so if I were not sure that I should not lead you into trouble.”\n\n“You are foolish in wishing this, my dear; foolish in trying to tempt an old man into a folly.”\n\n“Not foolish if I know that it will make you both happier.”\n\nHe made her no further reply, but stooping down that she might kiss him, as was his wont, went his way, leaving her almost miserable in the thought that she had troubled all these waters to no purpose. What would Miss Dunstable think of her? But on that afternoon Miss Dunstable seemed to be as happy and even-tempered as ever.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIX. HOW TO WRITE A LOVE LETTER.\n\nDr. Thorne, in the few words which he spoke to his niece before he left Boxall Hill, had called himself an old man; but he was as yet on the right side of sixty by five good years, and bore about with him less of the marks of age than most men of fifty-five do bear. One would have said in looking at him that there was no reason why he should not marry if he found that such a step seemed good to him; and looking at the age of the proposed bride, there was nothing unsuitable in that respect.\n\nBut nevertheless he felt almost ashamed of himself, in that he allowed himself even to think of the proposition which his niece had made. He mounted his horse that day at Boxall Hill—for he made all his journeys about the county on horseback—and rode slowly home to Greshamsbury, thinking not so much of the suggested marriage as of his own folly in thinking of it. How could he be such an ass at his time of life as to allow the even course of his way to be disturbed by any such idea? Of course he could not propose to himself such a wife as Miss Dunstable without having some thoughts as to her wealth; and it had been the pride of his life so to live that the world might know that he was indifferent about money. His profession was all in all to him,—the air which he breathed as well as the bread which he ate; and how could he follow his profession if he made such a marriage as this? She would expect him to go to London with her; and what would he become, dangling at her heels there, known only to the world as the husband of the richest woman in the town? The kind of life was one which would be unsuitable to him;—and yet, as he rode home, he could not resolve to rid himself of the idea. He went on thinking of it, though he still continued to condemn himself for keeping it in his thoughts. That night at home he would make up his mind, so he declared to himself; and would then write to his niece begging her to drop the subject. Having so far come to a resolution he went on meditating what course of life it might be well for him to pursue if he and Miss Dunstable should, after all, become man and wife.\n\nThere were two ladies whom it behoved him to see on the day of his arrival—whom, indeed, he generally saw every day except when absent from Greshamsbury. The first of these—first in the general consideration of the people of the place—was the wife of the squire, Lady Arabella Gresham, a very old patient of the doctor’s. Her it was his custom to visit early in the afternoon; and then, if he were able to escape the squire’s daily invitation to dinner, he customarily went to the other, Lady Scatcherd, when the rapid meal in his own house was over. Such, at least, was his summer practice.\n\n“Well, doctor, how are they at Boxall Hill?” said the squire, waylaying him on the gravel sweep before the door. The squire was very hard set for occupation in these summer months.\n\n“Quite well, I believe.”\n\n“I don’t know what’s come to Frank. I think he hates this place now. He’s full of the election, I suppose.”\n\n“Oh, yes; he told me to say he should be over here soon. Of course there’ll be no contest, so he need not trouble himself.”\n\n“Happy dog, isn’t he, doctor? to have it all before him instead of behind him. Well, well; he’s as good a lad as ever lived,—as ever lived. And let me see; Mary’s time—” And then there were a few very important words spoken on that subject.\n\n“I’ll just step up to Lady Arabella now,” said the doctor.\n\n“She’s as fretful as possible,” said the squire. “I’ve just left her.”\n\n“Nothing special the matter, I hope?”\n\n“No, I think not; nothing in your way, that is; only specially cross, which always comes in my way. You’ll stop and dine to-day, of course?”\n\n“Not to-day, squire.”\n\n“Nonsense; you will. I have been quite counting on you. I have a particular reason for wanting to have you to-day,—a most particular reason.” But the squire always had his particular reasons.\n\n“I’m very sorry, but it is impossible to-day. I shall have a letter to write that I must sit down to seriously. Shall I see you when I come down from her ladyship?”\n\nThe squire turned away sulkily, almost without answering him, for he now had no prospect of any alleviation to the tedium of the evening; and the doctor went up-stairs to his patient.\n\nFor Lady Arabella, though it cannot be said that she was ill, was always a patient. It must not be supposed that she kept her bed and swallowed daily doses, or was prevented from taking her share in such prosy gaieties as came from time to time in the way of her prosy life; but it suited her turn of mind to be an invalid and to have a doctor; and as the doctor whom her good fates had placed at her elbow thoroughly understood her case, no great harm was done.\n\n“It frets me dreadfully that I cannot get to see Mary,” Lady Arabella said, as soon as the first ordinary question as to her ailments had been asked and answered.\n\n“She’s quite well and will be over to see you before long.”\n\n“Now I beg that she won’t. She never thinks of coming when there can be no possible objection, and travelling, at the present moment, would be—” Whereupon the Lady Arabella shook her head very gravely. “Only think of the importance of it, doctor,” she said. “Remember the enormous stake there is to be considered.”\n\n“It would not do her a ha’porth of harm if the stake were twice as large.”\n\n“Nonsense, doctor, don’t tell me; as if I didn’t know myself. I was very much against her going to London this spring, but of course what I said was overruled. It always is. I do believe Mr. Gresham went over to Boxall Hill, on purpose to induce her to go. But what does he care? He’s fond of Frank; but he never thinks of looking beyond the present day. He never did, as you know well enough, doctor.”\n\n“The trip did her all the good in the world,” said Dr. Thorne, preferring anything to a conversation respecting the squire’s sins.\n\n“I very well remember that when I was in that way it wasn’t thought that such trips would do me any good. But, perhaps, things are altered since then.”\n\n“Yes, they are,” said the doctor. “We don’t interfere so much now-a-days.”\n\n“I know I never asked for such amusements when so much depended on quietness. I remember before Frank was born—and, indeed, when all of them were born— But as you say, things were different then; and I can easily believe that Mary is a person quite determined to have her own way.”\n\n“Why, Lady Arabella, she would have stayed at home without wishing to stir if Frank had done so much as hold up his little finger.”\n\n“So did I always. If Mr. Gresham made the slightest hint I gave way. But I really don’t see what one gets in return for such implicit obedience. Now this year, doctor, of course I should have liked to have been up in London for a week or two. You seemed to think yourself that I might as well see Sir Omicron.”\n\n“There could be no possible objection, I said.”\n\n“Well; no; exactly; and as Mr. Gresham knew I wished it, I think he might as well have offered it. I suppose there can be no reason now about money.”\n\n“But I understood that Mary specially asked you and Augusta?”\n\n“Yes; Mary was very good. She did ask me. But I know very well that Mary wants all the room she has got in London. The house is not at all too large for herself. And, for the matter of that, my sister, the countess, was very anxious that I should be with her. But one does like to be independent if one can, and for one fortnight I do think that Mr. Gresham might have managed it. When I knew that he was so dreadfully out at elbows I never troubled him about it,—though, goodness knows, all that was never my fault.”\n\n“The squire hates London. A fortnight there in warm weather would nearly be the death of him.”\n\n“He might at any rate have paid me the compliment of asking me. The chances are ten to one I should not have gone. It is that indifference that cuts me so. He was here just now, and, would you believe it?—”\n\nBut the doctor was determined to avoid further complaint for the present day. “I wonder what you would feel, Lady Arabella, if the squire were to take it into his head to go away and amuse himself, leaving you at home. There are worse men than Mr. Gresham, if you will believe me.” All this was an allusion to Earl de Courcy, her ladyship’s brother, as Lady Arabella very well understood; and the argument was one which was very often used to silence her.\n\n“Upon my word, then, I should like it better than his hanging about here doing nothing but attend to those nasty dogs. I really sometimes think that he has no spirit left.”\n\n“You are mistaken there, Lady Arabella,” said the doctor, rising with his hat in his hand and making his escape without further parley.\n\nAs he went home he could not but think that that phase of married life was not a very pleasant one. Mr. Gresham and his wife were supposed by the world to live on the best of terms. They always inhabited the same house, went out together when they did go out, always sat in their respective corners in the family pew, and in their wildest dreams after the happiness of novelty never thought of Sir Cresswell Cresswell. In some respects—with regard, for instance, to the continued duration of their joint domesticity at the family mansion of Greshamsbury—they might have been taken for a pattern couple. But yet, as far as the doctor could see, they did not seem to add much to the happiness of each other. They loved each other, doubtless, and had either of them been in real danger, that danger would have made the other miserable; but yet it might well be a question whether either would not be more comfortable without the other.\n\nThe doctor, as was his custom, dined at five, and at seven he went up to the cottage of his old friend Lady Scatcherd. Lady Scatcherd was not a refined woman, having in her early days been a labourer’s daughter and having then married a labourer. But her husband had risen in the world—as has been told in those chronicles before mentioned,—and his widow was now Lady Scatcherd with a pretty cottage and a good jointure. She was in all things the very opposite to Lady Arabella Gresham; nevertheless, under the doctor’s auspices, the two ladies were in some measure acquainted with each other. Of her married life, also, Dr. Thorne had seen something, and it may be questioned whether the memory of that was more alluring than the reality now existing at Greshamsbury.\n\nOf the two women Dr. Thorne much preferred his humbler friend, and to her he made his visits not in the guise of a doctor, but as a neighbour. “Well, my lady,” he said, as he sat down by her on a broad garden seat—all the world called Lady Scatcherd “my lady,”—“and how do these long summer days agree with you? Your roses are twice better out than any I see up at the big house.”\n\n“You may well call them long, doctor. They’re long enough surely.”\n\n“But not too long. Come, now, I won’t have you complaining. You don’t mean to tell me that you have anything to make you wretched? You had better not, for I won’t believe you.”\n\n“Eh; well; wretched! I don’t know as I’m wretched. It’d be wicked to say that, and I with such comforts about me.”\n\n“I think it would, almost.” The doctor did not say this harshly, but in a soft, friendly tone, and pressing her hand gently as he spoke.\n\n“And I didn’t mean to be wicked. I’m very thankful for everything—leastways, I always try to be. But, doctor, it is so lonely like.”\n\n“Lonely! not more lonely than I am.”\n\n“Oh, yes; you’re different. You can go everywheres. But what can a lone woman do? I’ll tell you what, doctor; I’d give it all up to have Roger back with his apron on and his pick in his hand. How well I mind his look when he’d come home o’ nights.”\n\n“And yet it was a hard life you had then, eh, old woman? It would be better for you to be thankful for what you’ve got.”\n\n“I am thankful. Didn’t I tell you so before?” said she, somewhat crossly. “But it’s a sad life, this living alone. I declares I envy Hannah, ’cause she’s got Jemima to sit in the kitchen with her. I want her to sit with me sometimes, but she won’t.”\n\n“Ah! but you shouldn’t ask her. It’s letting yourself down.”\n\n“What do I care about down or up? It makes no difference, as he’s gone. If he had lived one might have cared about being up, as you call it. Eh, deary; I’ll be going after him before long, and it will be no matter then.”\n\n“We shall all be going after him, sooner or later; that’s sure enough.”\n\n“Eh, dear, that’s true, surely. It’s only a span long, as Parson Oriel tells us when he gets romantic in his sermons. But it’s a hard thing, doctor, when two is married, as they can’t have their span, as he calls it, out together. Well, I must only put up with it, I suppose, as others does. Now, you’re not going, doctor? You’ll stop and have a dish of tea with me. You never see such cream as Hannah has from the Alderney cow. Do’ey now, doctor.”\n\nBut the doctor had his letter to write, and would not allow himself to be tempted even by the promise of Hannah’s cream. So he went his way, angering Lady Scatcherd by his departure as he had before angered the squire, and thinking as he went which was most unreasonable in her wretchedness, his friend Lady Arabella, or his friend Lady Scatcherd. The former was always complaining of an existing husband who never refused her any moderate request; and the other passed her days in murmuring at the loss of a dead husband, who in his life had ever been to her imperious and harsh, and had sometimes been cruel and unjust.\n\nThe doctor had his letter to write, but even yet he had not quite made up his mind what he would put into it; indeed, he had not hitherto resolved to whom it should be written. Looking at the matter as he had endeavoured to look at it, his niece, Mrs. Gresham, would be his correspondent; but if he brought himself to take this jump in the dark, in that case he would address himself direct to Miss Dunstable.\n\nHe walked home, not by the straightest road, but taking a considerable curve, round by narrow lanes, and through thick flower-laden hedges,—very thoughtful. He was told that she wished to marry him; and was he to think only of himself? And as to that pride of his about money, was it in truth a hearty, manly feeling; or was it a false pride, of which it behoved him to be ashamed as it did of many cognate feelings? If he acted rightly in this matter, why should he be afraid of the thoughts of any one? A life of solitude was bitter enough, as poor Lady Scatcherd had complained. But then, looking at Lady Scatcherd, and looking also at his other near neighbour, his friend the squire, there was little thereabouts to lead him on to matrimony. So he walked home slowly through the lanes, very meditative, with his hands behind his back.\n\nNor when he got home was he much more inclined to any resolute line of action. He might have drunk his tea with Lady Scatcherd, as well as have sat there in his own drawing-room, drinking it alone; for he got no pen and paper, and he dawdled over his teacup with the utmost dilatoriness, putting off, as it were, the evil day. To only one thing was he fixed—to this, namely, that that letter should be written before he went to bed.\n\nHaving finished his tea, which did not take place till near eleven, he went downstairs to an untidy little room which lay behind his depôt of medicines, and in which he was wont to do his writing; and herein he did at last set himself down to his work. Even at that moment he was in doubt. But he would write his letter to Miss Dunstable and see how it looked. He was almost determined not to send it; so, at least, he said to himself: but he could do no harm by writing it. So he did write it, as follows:—\n\nGreshamsbury, — June, 185—.\n\nWhen he had got so far, he leaned back in his chair and looked at the paper. How on earth was he to find words to say that which he now wished to have said? He had never written such a letter in his life, or anything approaching to it, and now found himself overwhelmed with a difficulty of which he had not previously thought. He spent another half-hour in looking at the paper, and was at last nearly deterred by this new difficulty. He would use the simplest, plainest language, he said to himself over and over again; but it is not always easy to use simple, plain language,—by no means so easy as to mount on stilts, and to march along with sesquipedalian words, with pathos, spasms, and notes of interjection. But the letter did at last get itself written, and there was not a note of interjection in it.\n\nMY DEAR MISS DUNSTABLE,—I think it right to confess that I should not be now writing this letter to you, had I not been led to believe by other judgment than my own that the proposition which I am going to make would be regarded by you with favour. Without such other judgment I should, I own, have feared that the great disparity between you and me in regard to money would have given to such a proposition an appearance of being false and mercenary. All I ask of you now, with confidence, is to acquit me of such fault as that.\n\nWhen you have read so far you will understand what I mean. We have known each other now somewhat intimately, though indeed not very long, and I have sometimes fancied that you were almost as well pleased to be with me as I have been to be with you. If I have been wrong in this, tell me so simply, and I will endeavour to let our friendship run on as though this letter had not been written. But if I have been right, and if it be possible that you can think that a union between us will make us both happier than we are single, I will plight you my word and troth with good faith, and will do what an old man may do to make the burden of the world lie light upon your shoulders. Looking at my age I can hardly keep myself from thinking that I am an old fool: but I try to reconcile myself to that by remembering that you yourself are no longer a girl. You see that I pay you no compliments, and that you need expect none from me.\n\nI do not know that I could add anything to the truth of this, if I were to write three times as much. All that is necessary is, that you should know what I mean. If you do not believe me to be true and honest already, nothing that I can write will make you believe it.\n\nGod bless you. I know you will not keep me long in suspense for an answer.\n\nAffectionately your friend,\n\nWhen he had finished he meditated again for another half-hour whether it would not be right that he should add something about her money. Would it not be well for him to tell her—it might be said in a postscript—that with regard to all her wealth she would be free to do what she chose? At any rate he owed no debts for her to pay, and would still have his own income, sufficient for his own purposes. But about one o’clock he came to the conclusion that it would be better to leave the matter alone. If she cared for him, and could trust him, and was worthy also that he should trust her, no omission of such a statement would deter her from coming to him: and if there were no such trust, it would not be created by any such assurance on his part. So he read the letter over twice, sealed it, and took it up, together with his bed candle, into his bed-room. Now that the letter was written it seemed to be a thing fixed by fate that it must go. He had written it that he might see how it looked when written; but now that it was written, there remained no doubt but that it must be sent. So he went to bed, with the letter on the toilette-table beside him; and early in the morning—so early as to make it seem that the importance of the letter had disturbed his rest—he sent it off by a special messenger to Boxall Hill.\n\n“I’se wait for an answer?” said the boy.\n\n“No,” said the doctor: “leave the letter, and come away.”\n\nThe breakfast hour was not very early at Boxall Hill in these summer months. Frank Gresham, no doubt, went round his farm before he came in for prayers, and his wife was probably looking to the butter in the dairy. At any rate, they did not meet till near ten, and therefore, though the ride from Greshamsbury to Boxall Hill was nearly two hours’ work, Miss Dunstable had her letter in her own room before she came down.\n\nShe read it in silence as she was dressing, while the maid was with her in the room; but she made no sign which could induce her Abigail to think that the epistle was more than ordinarily important. She read it, and then quietly refolding it and placing it in the envelope, she put it down on the table at which she was sitting. It was full fifteen minutes afterwards that she begged her servant to see if Mrs. Gresham were still in her own room. “Because I want to see her for five minutes, alone, before breakfast,” said Miss Dunstable.\n\n“You traitor; you false, black traitor!” were the first words which Miss Dunstable spoke when she found herself alone with her friend.\n\n“Why, what’s the matter?”\n\n“I did not think there was so much mischief in you, nor so keen and commonplace a desire for match-making. Look here. Read the first four lines; not more, if you please; the rest is private. Whose is the other judgment of whom your uncle speaks in his letter?”\n\n“Oh, Miss Dunstable! I must read it all.”\n\n“Indeed you’ll do no such thing. You think it’s a love-letter, I dare say; but indeed there’s not a word about love in it.”\n\n“I know he has offered. I shall be so glad, for I know you like him.”\n\n“He tells me that I am an old woman, and insinuates that I may probably be an old fool.”\n\n“I am sure he does not say that.”\n\n“Ah! but I’m sure that he does. The former is true enough, and I never complain of the truth. But as to the latter, I am by no means so certain that it is true—not in the sense that he means it.”\n\n“Dear, dearest woman, don’t go on in that way now. Do speak out to me, and speak without jesting.”\n\n“Whose was the other judgment to whom he trusts so implicitly? Tell me that.”\n\n“Mine, mine, of course. No one else can have spoken to him about it. Of course I talked to him.”\n\n“And what did you tell him?”\n\n“I told him—”\n\n“Well, out with it. Let me have the real facts. Mind, I tell you fairly that you had no right to tell him anything. What passed between us, passed in confidence. But let us hear what you did say.”\n\n“I told him that you would have him if he offered.” And Mrs. Gresham, as she spoke, looked into her friend’s face doubtingly, not knowing whether in very truth Miss Dunstable were pleased with her or displeased. If she were displeased, then how had her uncle been deceived!\n\n“You told him that as a fact?”\n\n“I told him that I thought so.”\n\n“Then I suppose I am bound to have him,” said Miss Dunstable, dropping the letter on to the floor in mock despair.\n\n“My dear, dear, dearest woman!” said Mrs. Gresham, bursting into tears, and throwing herself on to her friend’s neck.\n\n“Mind you are a dutiful niece,” said Miss Dunstable. “And now let me go and finish dressing.”\n\nIn the course of the afternoon, an answer was sent back to Greshamsbury, in these words:—\n\nDEAR DR. THORNE,—I do and will trust you in everything; and it shall be as you would have it. Mary writes to you; but do not believe a word she says. I never will again, for she has behaved so bad in this matter.\n\nYours affectionately and very truly,\n\n“And so I am going to marry the richest woman in England,” said Dr. Thorne to himself, as he sat down that day to his mutton-chop.\n\n# CHAPTER XL. INTERNECINE.\n\nIt must be conceived that there was some feeling of triumph at Plumstead Episcopi, when the wife of the rector returned home with her daughter, the bride elect of the Lord Dumbello. The heir of the Marquis of Hartletop was, in wealth, the most considerable unmarried young nobleman of the day; he was noted, too, as a man difficult to be pleased, as one who was very fine and who gave himself airs,—and to have been selected as the wife of such a man as this was a great thing for the daughter of a parish clergyman. We have seen in what manner the happy girl’s mother communicated the fact to Lady Lufton, hiding, as it were, her pride under a veil; and we have seen also how meekly the happy girl bore her own great fortune, applying herself humbly to the packing of her clothes, as though she ignored her own glory.\n\nBut nevertheless there was triumph at Plumstead Episcopi. The mother, when she returned home, began to feel that she had been thoroughly successful in the great object of her life. While she was yet in London she had hardly realized her satisfaction, and there were doubts then whether the cup might not be dashed from her lips before it was tasted. It might be that even the son of the Marquis of Hartletop was subject to parental authority, and that barriers should spring up between Griselda and her coronet; but there had been nothing of the kind. The archdeacon had been closeted with the marquis, and Mrs. Grantly had been closeted with the marchioness; and though neither of those noble persons had expressed themselves gratified by their son’s proposed marriage, so also neither of them had made any attempt to prevent it. Lord Dumbello was a man who had a will of his own,—as the Grantlys boasted amongst themselves. Poor Griselda! the day may perhaps come when this fact of her lord’s masterful will may not to her be matter of much boasting. But in London, as I was saying, there had been no time for an appreciation of the family joy. The work to be done was nervous in its nature, and self-glorification might have been fatal; but now, when they were safe at Plumstead, the great truth burst upon them in all its splendour.\n\nMrs. Grantly had but one daughter, and the formation of that child’s character and her establishment in the world had been the one main object of the mother’s life. Of Griselda’s great beauty the Plumstead household had long been conscious; of her discretion also, of her conduct, and of her demeanour there had been no doubt. But the father had sometimes hinted to the mother that he did not think that Grizzy was quite so clever as her brothers. “I don’t agree with you at all,” Mrs. Grantly had answered. “Besides, what you call cleverness is not at all necessary in a girl; she is perfectly ladylike; even you won’t deny that.” The archdeacon had never wished to deny it, and was now fain to admit that what he had called cleverness was not necessary in a young lady.\n\nAt this period of the family glory the archdeacon himself was kept a little in abeyance, and was hardly allowed free intercourse with his own magnificent child. Indeed, to give him his due, it must be said of him that he would not consent to walk in the triumphal procession which moved with stately step, to and fro, through the Barchester regions. He kissed his daughter and blessed her, and bade her love her husband and be a good wife; but such injunctions as these, seeing how splendidly she had done her duty in securing to herself a marquis, seemed out of place and almost vulgar. Girls about to marry curates or sucking barristers should be told to do their duty in that station of life to which God might be calling them; but it seemed to be almost an impertinence in a father to give such an injunction to a future marchioness.\n\n“I do not think that you have any ground for fear on her behalf,” said Mrs. Grantly, “seeing in what way she has hitherto conducted herself.”\n\n“She has been a good girl,” said the archdeacon, “but she is about to be placed in a position of great temptation.”\n\n“She has a strength of mind suited for any position,” replied Mrs. Grantly, vain-gloriously.\n\nBut nevertheless even the archdeacon moved about through the close at Barchester with a somewhat prouder step since the tidings of this alliance had become known there. The time had been—in the latter days of his father’s lifetime—when he was the greatest man of the close. The dean had been old and infirm, and Dr. Grantly had wielded the bishop’s authority. But since that things had altered. A new bishop had come there, absolutely hostile to him. A new dean had also come, who was not only his friend, but the brother-in-law of his wife; but even this advent had lessened the authority of the archdeacon. The vicars choral did not hang upon his words as they had been wont to do, and the minor canons smiled in return to his smile less obsequiously when they met him in the clerical circles of Barchester. But now it seemed that his old supremacy was restored to him. In the minds of many men an archdeacon, who was the father-in-law of a marquis, was himself as good as any bishop. He did not say much of his new connection to others beside the dean, but he was conscious of the fact, and conscious also of the reflected glory which shone around his own head.\n\nBut as regards Mrs. Grantly it may be said that she moved in an unending procession of stately ovation. It must not be supposed that she continually talked to her friends and neighbours of Lord Dumbello and the marchioness. She was by far too wise for such folly as that. The coming alliance having been once announced, the name of Hartletop was hardly mentioned by her out of her own domestic circle. But she assumed, with an ease that was surprising even to herself, the airs and graces of a mighty woman. She went through her work of morning calls as though it were her business to be affable to the country gentry. She astonished her sister, the dean’s wife, by the simplicity of her grandeur; and condescended to Mrs. Proudie in a manner which nearly broke that lady’s heart. “I shall be even with her yet,” said Mrs. Proudie to herself, who had contrived to learn various very deleterious circumstances respecting the Hartletop family since the news about Lord Dumbello and Griselda had become known to her.\n\nGriselda herself was carried about in the procession, taking but little part in it of her own, like an Eastern god. She suffered her mother’s caresses and smiled in her mother’s face as she listened to her own praises, but her triumph was apparently within. To no one did she say much on the subject, and greatly disgusted the old family housekeeper by declining altogether to discuss the future Dumbello ménage. To her aunt, Mrs. Arabin, who strove hard to lead her into some open-hearted speech as to her future aspirations, she was perfectly impassive. “Oh, yes, aunt, of course,” and “I’ll think about it, aunt Eleanor,” or “Of course I shall do that if Lord Dumbello wishes it.” Nothing beyond this could be got from her; and so, after half-a-dozen ineffectual attempts, Mrs. Arabin abandoned the matter.\n\nBut then there arose the subject of clothes—of the wedding trousseau! Sarcastic people are wont to say that the tailor makes the man. Were I such a one, I might certainly assert that the milliner makes the bride. As regarding her bridehood, in distinction either to her girlhood or her wifehood—as being a line of plain demarcation between those two periods of a woman’s life—the milliner does do much to make her. She would be hardly a bride if the trousseau were not there. A girl married without some such appendage would seem to pass into the condition of a wife without any such line of demarcation. In that moment in which she finds herself in the first fruition of her marriage finery she becomes a bride; and in that other moment, when she begins to act upon the finest of these things as clothes to be packed up, she becomes a wife.\n\nWhen this subject was discussed Griselda displayed no lack of a becoming interest. She went to work steadily, slowly, and almost with solemnity, as though the business in hand were one which it would be wicked to treat with impatience. She even struck her mother with awe by the grandeur of her ideas and the depth of her theories. Nor let it be supposed that she rushed away at once to the consideration of the great fabric which was to be the ultimate sign and mark of her status, the quintessence of her briding, the outer veil, as it were, of the tabernacle—namely, her wedding-dress. As a great poet works himself up by degrees to that inspiration which is necessary for the grand turning point of his epic, so did she slowly approach the hallowed ground on which she would sit, with her ministers around her, when about to discuss the nature, the extent, the design, the colouring, the structure, and the ornamentation of that momentous piece of apparel. No; there was much indeed to be done before she came to this; and as the poet, to whom I have already alluded, first invokes his muse, and then brings his smaller events gradually out upon his stage, so did Miss Grantly with sacred fervour ask her mother’s aid, and then prepare her list of all those articles of under-clothing which must be the substratum for the visible magnificence of her trousseau.\n\nMoney was no object. We all know what that means; and frequently understand, when the words are used, that a blaze of splendour is to be attained at the cheapest possible price. But, in this instance, money was no object;—such an amount of money, at least, as could by any possibility be spent on a lady’s clothes, independently of her jewels. With reference to diamonds and such like, the archdeacon at once declared his intention of taking the matter into his own hands—except in so far as Lord Dumbello, or the Hartletop interest, might be pleased to participate in the selection. Nor was Mrs. Grantly sorry for such a decision. She was not an imprudent woman, and would have dreaded the responsibility of trusting herself on such an occasion among the dangerous temptations of a jeweller’s shop. But as far as silks and satins went—in the matter of French bonnets, muslins, velvets, hats, riding-habits, artificial flowers, head-gilding, curious nettings, enamelled buckles, golden tagged bobbins, and mechanical petticoats—as regarded shoes, and gloves, and corsets, and stockings, and linen, and flannel, and calico—money, I may conscientiously assert, was no object. And, under these circumstances, Griselda Grantly went to work with a solemn industry and a steady perseverance that was beyond all praise.\n\n“I hope she will be happy,” Mrs. Arabin said to her sister, as the two were sitting together in the dean’s drawing-room.\n\n“Oh, yes; I think she will. Why should she not?” said the mother.\n\n“Oh, no; I know of no reason. But she is going up into a station so much above her own in the eyes of the world that one cannot but feel anxious for her.”\n\n“I should feel much more anxious if she were going to marry a poor man,” said Mrs. Grantly. “It has always seemed to me that Griselda was fitted for a high position; that nature intended her for rank and state. You see that she is not a bit elated. She takes it all as if it were her own by right. I do not think that there is any danger that her head will be turned, if you mean that.”\n\n“I was thinking rather of her heart,” said Mrs. Arabin.\n\n“She never would have taken Lord Dumbello without loving him,” said Mrs. Grantly, speaking rather quickly.\n\n“That is not quite what I mean either, Susan. I am sure she would not have accepted him had she not loved him. But it is so hard to keep the heart fresh among all the grandeurs of high rank; and it is harder for a girl to do so who has not been born to it, than for one who has enjoyed it as her birthright.”\n\n“I don’t quite understand about fresh hearts,” said Mrs. Grantly, pettishly. “If she does her duty, and loves her husband, and fills the position in which God has placed her with propriety, I don’t know that we need look for anything more. I don’t at all approve of the plan of frightening a young girl when she is making her first outset into the world.”\n\n“No; I would not frighten her. I think it would be almost difficult to frighten Griselda.”\n\n“I hope it would. The great matter with a girl is whether she has been brought up with proper notions as to a woman’s duty. Of course it is not for me to boast on this subject. Such as she is, I, of course, am responsible. But I must own that I do not see occasion to wish for any change.” And then the subject was allowed to drop.\n\nAmong those of her relations who wondered much at the girl’s fortune, but allowed themselves to say but little, was her grandfather, Mr. Harding. He was an old clergyman, plain and simple in his manners, and not occupying a very prominent position, seeing that he was only precentor to the chapter. He was loved by his daughter, Mrs. Grantly, and was treated by the archdeacon, if not invariably with the highest respect, at least always with consideration and regard. But, old and plain as he was, the young people at Plumstead did not hold him in any great reverence. He was poorer than their other relatives, and made no attempt to hold his head high in Barsetshire circles. Moreover, in these latter days, the home of his heart had been at the deanery. He had, indeed, a lodging of his own in the city, but was gradually allowing himself to be weaned away from it. He had his own bedroom in the dean’s house, his own arm-chair in the dean’s library, and his own corner on a sofa in Mrs. Dean’s drawing-room. It was not, therefore, necessary that he should interfere greatly in this coming marriage; but still it became his duty to say a word of congratulation to his granddaughter,—and perhaps to say a word of advice.\n\n“Grizzy, my dear,” he said to her—he always called her Grizzy, but the endearment of the appellation had never been appreciated by the young lady—“come and kiss me, and let me congratulate you on your great promotion. I do so very heartily.”\n\n“Thank you, grandpapa,” she said, touching his forehead with her lips, thus being, as it were, very sparing with her kiss. But those lips now were august and reserved for nobler foreheads than that of an old cathedral hack. For Mr. Harding still chanted the Litany from Sunday to Sunday, unceasingly, standing at that well-known desk in the cathedral choir; and Griselda had a thought in her mind that when the Hartletop people should hear of the practice they would not be delighted. Dean and archdeacon might be very well, and if her grandfather had even been a prebendary, she might have put up with him; but he had, she thought, almost disgraced his family in being, at his age, one of the working menial clergy of the cathedral. She kissed him, therefore, sparingly, and resolved that her words with him should be few.\n\n“You are going to be a great lady, Grizzy,” said he.\n\n“Umph!” said she.\n\nWhat was she to say when so addressed?\n\n“And I hope you will be happy,—and make others happy.”\n\n“I hope I shall,” said she.\n\n“But always think most about the latter, my dear. Think about the happiness of those around you, and your own will come without thinking. You understand that; do you not?”\n\n“Oh, yes, I understand,” she said.\n\nAs they were speaking Mr. Harding still held her hand, but Griselda left it with him unwillingly, and therefore ungraciously, looking as though she were dragging it from him.\n\n“And Grizzy—I believe it is quite as easy for a rich countess to be happy, as for a dairymaid—”\n\nGriselda gave her head a little chuck which was produced by two different operations of her mind. The first was a reflection that her grandpapa was robbing her of her rank. She was to be a rich marchioness. And the second was a feeling of anger at the old man for comparing her lot to that of a dairymaid.\n\n“Quite as easy, I believe,” continued he; “though others will tell you that it is not so. But with the countess as with the dairymaid, it must depend on the woman herself. Being a countess—that fact alone won’t make you happy.”\n\n“Lord Dumbello at present is only a viscount,” said Griselda. “There is no earl’s title in the family.”\n\n“Oh! I did not know,” said Mr. Harding, relinquishing his granddaughter’s hand; and, after that, he troubled her with no further advice.\n\nBoth Mrs. Proudie and the bishop had called at Plumstead since Mrs. Grantly had come back from London, and the ladies from Plumstead, of course, returned the visit. It was natural that the Grantlys and Proudies should hate each other. They were essentially church people, and their views on all church matters were antagonistic. They had been compelled to fight for supremacy in the diocese, and neither family had so conquered the other as to have become capable of magnanimity and good-humour. They did hate each other, and this hatred had, at one time, almost produced an absolute disseverance of even the courtesies which are so necessary between a bishop and his clergy. But the bitterness of this rancour had been overcome, and the ladies of the families had continued on visiting terms.\n\nBut now this match was almost more than Mrs. Proudie could bear. The great disappointment which, as she well knew, the Grantlys had encountered in that matter of the proposed new bishopric had for the moment mollified her. She had been able to talk of poor dear Mrs. Grantly! “She is heartbroken, you know, in this matter, and the repetition of such misfortunes is hard to bear,” she had been heard to say, with a complacency which had been quite becoming to her. But now that complacency was at an end. Olivia Proudie had just accepted a widowed preacher at a district church in Bethnal Green,—a man with three children, who was dependent on pew-rents; and Griselda Grantly was engaged to the eldest son of the Marquis of Hartletop! When women are enjoined to forgive their enemies it cannot be intended that such wrongs as these should be included.\n\nBut Mrs. Proudie’s courage was nothing daunted. It may be boasted of her that nothing could daunt her courage. Soon after her return to Barchester, she and Olivia—Olivia being very unwilling—had driven over to Plumstead, and, not finding the Grantlys at home, had left their cards; and now, at a proper interval, Mrs. Grantly and Griselda returned the visit. It was the first time that Miss Grantly had been seen by the Proudie ladies since the fact of her engagement had become known.\n\nThe first bevy of compliments that passed might be likened to a crowd of flowers on a hedge rosebush. They were beautiful to the eye but were so closely environed by thorns that they could not be plucked without great danger. As long as the compliments were allowed to remain on the hedge—while no attempt was made to garner them and realize their fruits for enjoyment—they did no mischief; but the first finger that was put forth for such a purpose was soon drawn back, marked with spots of blood.\n\n“Of course it is a great match for Griselda,” said Mrs. Grantly, in a whisper the meekness of which would have disarmed an enemy whose weapons were less firmly clutched than those of Mrs. Proudie; “but, independently of that, the connection is one which is gratifying in many ways.”\n\n“Oh, no doubt,” said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“Lord Dumbello is so completely his own master,” continued Mrs. Grantly, and a slight, unintended semi-tone of triumph mingled itself with the meekness of that whisper.\n\n“And is likely to remain so, from all I hear,” said Mrs. Proudie, and the scratched hand was at once drawn back.\n\n“Of course the estab—,” and then Mrs. Proudie, who was blandly continuing her list of congratulations, whispered her sentence close into the ear of Mrs. Grantly, so that not a word of what she said might be audible by the young people.\n\n“I never heard a word of it,” said Mrs. Grantly, gathering herself up, “and I don’t believe it.”\n\n“Oh, I may be wrong; and I’m sure I hope so. But young men will be young men, you know;—and children will take after their parents. I suppose you will see a great deal of the Duke of Omnium now.”\n\nBut Mrs. Grantly was not a woman to be knocked down and trampled on without resistance; and though she had been lacerated by the rosebush she was not as yet placed altogether hors de combat. She said some word about the Duke of Omnium very tranquilly, speaking of him merely as a Barsetshire proprietor, and then, smiling with her sweetest smile, expressed a hope that she might soon have the pleasure of becoming acquainted with Mr. Tickler; and as she spoke she made a pretty little bow towards Olivia Proudie. Now Mr. Tickler was the worthy clergyman attached to the district church at Bethnal Green.\n\n“He’ll be down here in August,” said Olivia, boldly, determined not to be shamefaced about her love affairs.\n\n“You’ll be starring it about the Continent by that time, my dear,” said Mrs. Proudie to Griselda. “Lord Dumbello is well known at Homburg and Ems, and places of that sort; so you will find yourself quite at home.”\n\n“We are going to Rome,” said Griselda, majestically.\n\n“I suppose Mr. Tickler will come into the diocese soon,” said Mrs. Grantly. “I remember hearing him very favourably spoken of by Mr. Slope, who was a friend of his.”\n\nNothing short of a fixed resolve on the part of Mrs. Grantly that the time had now come in which she must throw away her shield and stand behind her sword, declare war to the knife, and neither give nor take quarter, could have justified such a speech as this. Any allusion to Mr. Slope acted on Mrs. Proudie as a red cloth is supposed to act on a bull; but when that allusion connected the name of Mr. Slope in a friendly bracket with that of Mrs. Proudie’s future son-in-law it might be certain that the effect would be terrific. And there was more than this: for that very Mr. Slope had once entertained audacious hopes—hopes not thought to be audacious by the young lady herself—with reference to Miss Olivia Proudie. All this Mrs. Grantly knew, and, knowing it, still dared to mention his name.\n\nThe countenance of Mrs. Proudie became darkened with black anger, and the polished smile of her company manners gave place before the outraged feelings of her nature.\n\n“The man you speak of, Mrs. Grantly,” said she, “was never known as a friend by Mr. Tickler.”\n\n“Oh, indeed,” said Mrs. Grantly. “Perhaps I have made a mistake. I am sure I have heard Mr. Slope mention him.”\n\n“When Mr. Slope was running after your sister, Mrs. Grantly, and was encouraged by her as he was, you perhaps saw more of him than I did.”\n\n“Mrs. Proudie, that was never the case.”\n\n“I have reason to know that the archdeacon conceived it to be so, and that he was very unhappy about it.” Now this, unfortunately, was a fact which Mrs. Grantly could not deny.\n\n“The archdeacon may have been mistaken about Mr. Slope,” she said, “as were some other people at Barchester. But it was you, I think, Mrs. Proudie, who were responsible for bringing him here.”\n\nMrs. Grantly, at this period of the engagement, might have inflicted a fatal wound by referring to poor Olivia’s former love affairs, but she was not destitute of generosity. Even in the extremest heat of the battle she knew how to spare the young and tender.\n\n“When I came here, Mrs. Grantly, I little dreamed what a depth of wickedness might be found in the very close of a cathedral city,” said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“Then, for dear Olivia’s sake, pray do not bring poor Mr. Tickler to Barchester.”\n\n“Mr. Tickler, Mrs. Grantly, is a man of assured morals and of a highly religious tone of thinking. I wish every one could be so safe as regards their daughters’ future prospects as I am.”\n\n“Yes, I know he has the advantage of being a family man,” said Mrs. Grantly, getting up. “Good morning, Mrs. Proudie; good day, Olivia.”\n\n“A great deal better that than—” But the blow fell upon the empty air; for Mrs. Grantly had already escaped on to the staircase while Olivia was ringing the bell for the servant to attend the front-door.\n\nMrs. Grantly, as she got into her carriage, smiled slightly, thinking of the battle, and as she sat down she gently pressed her daughter’s hand. But Mrs. Proudie’s face was still dark as Acheron when her enemy withdrew, and with angry tone she sent her daughter to her work. “Mr. Tickler will have great reason to complain if, in your position, you indulge such habits of idleness,” she said. Therefore I conceive that I am justified in saying that in that encounter Mrs. Grantly was the conqueror.\n\n# CHAPTER XLI. DON QUIXOTE.\n\nOn the day on which Lucy had her interview with Lady Lufton the dean dined at Framley Parsonage. He and Robarts had known each other since the latter had been in the diocese, and now, owing to Mark’s preferment in the chapter, had become almost intimate. The dean was greatly pleased with the manner in which poor Mr. Crawley’s children had been conveyed away from Hogglestock, and was inclined to open his heart to the whole Framley household. As he still had to ride home he could only allow himself to remain half an hour after dinner, but in that half-hour he said a great deal about Crawley, complimented Robarts on the manner in which he was playing the part of the Good Samaritan, and then by degrees informed him that it had come to his, the dean’s, ears, before he left Barchester, that a writ was in the hands of certain persons in the city, enabling them to seize—he did not know whether it was the person or the property of the vicar of Framley.\n\nThe fact was that these tidings had been conveyed to the dean with the express intent that he might put Robarts on his guard; but the task of speaking on such a subject to a brother clergyman had been so unpleasant to him that he had been unable to introduce it till the last five minutes before his departure.\n\n“I hope you will not put it down as an impertinent interference,” said the dean, apologizing.\n\n“No,” said Mark; “no, I do not think that.” He was so sad at heart that he hardly knew how to speak of it.\n\n“I do not understand much about such matters,” said the dean; “but I think, if I were you, I should go to a lawyer. I should imagine that anything so terribly disagreeable as an arrest might be avoided.”\n\n“It is a hard case,” said Mark, pleading his own cause. “Though these men have this claim against me I have never received a shilling either in money or money’s worth.”\n\n“And yet your name is to the bills!” said the dean.\n\n“Yes, my name is to the bills, certainly, but it was to oblige a friend.”\n\nAnd then the dean, having given his advice, rode away. He could not understand how a clergyman, situated as was Mr. Robarts, could find himself called upon by friendship to attach his name to accommodation bills which he had not the power of liquidating when due!\n\nOn that evening they were both wretched enough at the parsonage. Hitherto Mark had hoped that perhaps, after all, no absolutely hostile steps would be taken against him with reference to these bills. Some unforeseen chance might occur in his favour, or the persons holding them might consent to take small instalments of payment from time to time; but now it seemed that the evil day was actually coming upon him at a blow. He had no longer any secrets from his wife. Should he go to a lawyer? and if so, to what lawyer? And when he had found his lawyer, what should he say to him? Mrs. Robarts at one time suggested that everything should be told to Lady Lufton. Mark, however, could not bring himself to do that. “It would seem,” he said, “as though I wanted her to lend me the money.”\n\nOn the following morning Mark did ride into Barchester, dreading, however, lest he should be arrested on his journey, and he did see a lawyer. During his absence two calls were made at the parsonage—one by a very rough-looking individual, who left a suspicious document in the hands of the servant, purporting to be an invitation—not to dinner—from one of the judges of the land; and the other call was made by Lady Lufton in person.\n\nMrs. Robarts had determined to go down to Framley Court on that day. In accordance with her usual custom she would have been there within an hour or two of Lady Lufton’s return from London, but things between them were not now as they usually had been. This affair of Lucy’s must make a difference, let them both resolve to the contrary as they might. And, indeed, Mrs. Robarts had found that the closeness of her intimacy with Framley Court had been diminishing from day to day since Lucy had first begun to be on friendly terms with Lord Lufton. Since that she had been less at Framley Court than usual; she had heard from Lady Lufton less frequently by letter during her absence than she had done in former years, and was aware that she was less implicitly trusted with all the affairs of the parish. This had not made her angry, for she was in a manner conscious that it must be so. It made her unhappy, but what could she do? She could not blame Lucy, nor could she blame Lady Lufton. Lord Lufton she did blame, but she did so in the hearing of no one but her husband.\n\nHer mind, however, was made up to go over and bear the first brunt of her ladyship’s arguments, when she was stopped by her ladyship’s arrival. If it were not for this terrible matter of Lucy’s love—a matter on which they could not now be silent when they met—there would be twenty subjects of pleasant, or, at any rate, not unpleasant conversation. But even then there would be those terrible bills hanging over her conscience, and almost crushing her by their weight. At the moment in which Lady Lufton walked up to the drawing-room window, Mrs. Robarts held in her hand that ominous invitation from the judge. Would it not be well that she should make a clean breast of it all, disregarding what her husband had said? It might be well: only this—she had never yet done anything in opposition to her husband’s wishes. So she hid the slip within her desk, and left the matter open to consideration.\n\nThe interview commenced with an affectionate embrace, as was a matter of course. “Dear Fanny,” and “Dear Lady Lufton,” was said between them with all the usual warmth. And then the first inquiry was made about the children, and the second about the school. For a minute or two Mrs. Robarts thought that, perhaps, nothing was to be said about Lucy. If it pleased Lady Lufton to be silent she, at least, would not commence the subject.\n\nThen there was a word or two spoken about Mrs. Podgens’ baby, after which Lady Lufton asked whether Fanny were alone.\n\n“Yes,” said Mrs. Robarts. “Mark has gone over to Barchester.”\n\n“I hope he will not be long before he lets me see him. Perhaps he can call to-morrow. Would you both come and dine to-morrow?”\n\n“Not to-morrow, I think, Lady Lufton; but Mark, I am sure, will go over and call.”\n\n“And why not come to dinner? I hope there is to be no change among us, eh, Fanny?” and Lady Lufton as she spoke looked into the other’s face in a manner which almost made Mrs. Robarts get up and throw herself on her old friend’s neck. Where was she to find a friend who would give her such constant love as she had received from Lady Lufton? And who was kinder, better, more honest than she?\n\n“Change! no, I hope not, Lady Lufton;” and as she spoke the tears stood in her eyes.\n\n“Ah, but I shall think there is if you will not come to me as you used to do. You always used to come and dine with me the day I came home, as a matter of course.”\n\nWhat could she say, poor woman, to this?\n\n“We were all in confusion yesterday about poor Mrs. Crawley, and the dean dined here; he had been over at Hogglestock to see his friend.”\n\n“I have heard of her illness, and will go over and see what ought to be done. Don’t you go, do you hear, Fanny? You with your young children! I should never forgive you if you did.”\n\nAnd then Mrs. Robarts explained how Lucy had gone there, had sent the four children back to Framley, and was herself now staying at Hogglestock with the object of nursing Mrs. Crawley. In telling the story she abstained from praising Lucy with all the strong language which she would have used had not Lucy’s name and character been at the present moment of peculiar import to Lady Lufton; but nevertheless she could not tell it without dwelling much on Lucy’s kindness. It would have been ungenerous to Lady Lufton to make much of Lucy’s virtue at this present moment, but unjust to Lucy to make nothing of it.\n\n“And she is actually with Mrs. Crawley now?” asked Lady Lufton.\n\n“Oh, yes; Mark left her there yesterday afternoon.”\n\n“And the four children are all here in the house?”\n\n“Not exactly in the house—that is, not as yet. We have arranged a sort of quarantine hospital over the coach-house.”\n\n“What, where Stubbs lives?”\n\n“Yes; Stubbs and his wife have come into the house, and the children are to remain up there till the doctor says that there is no danger of infection. I have not even seen my visitors myself as yet,” said Mrs. Robarts with a slight laugh.\n\n“Dear me!” said Lady Lufton. “I declare you have been very prompt. And so Miss Robarts is over there! I should have thought Mr. Crawley would have made a difficulty about the children.”\n\n“Well, he did; but they kidnapped them,—that is, Lucy and Mark did. The dean gave me such an account of it. Lucy brought them out by twos and packed them in the pony-carriage, and then Mark drove off at a gallop while Mr. Crawley stood calling to them in the road. The dean was there at the time and saw it all.”\n\n“That Miss Lucy of yours seems to be a very determined young lady when she takes a thing into her head,” said Lady Lufton, now sitting down for the first time.\n\n“Yes, she is,” said Mrs. Robarts, having laid aside all her pleasant animation, for the discussion which she dreaded was now at hand.\n\n“A very determined young lady,” continued Lady Lufton. “Of course, my dear Fanny, you know all this about Ludovic and your sister-in-law?”\n\n“Yes, she has told me about it.”\n\n“It is very unfortunate—very.”\n\n“I do not think Lucy has been to blame,” said Mrs. Robarts; and as she spoke the blood was already mounting to her cheeks.\n\n“Do not be too anxious to defend her, my dear, before any one accuses her. Whenever a person does that it looks as though their cause were weak.”\n\n“But my cause is not weak as far as Lucy is concerned; I feel quite sure that she has not been to blame.”\n\n“I know how obstinate you can be, Fanny, when you think it necessary to dub yourself any one’s champion. Don Quixote was not a better knight-errant than you are. But is it not a pity to take up your lance and shield before an enemy is within sight or hearing? But that was ever the way with your Don Quixotes.”\n\n“Perhaps there may be an enemy in ambush.” That was Mrs. Robarts’ thought to herself, but she did not dare to express it, so she remained silent.\n\n“My only hope is,” continued Lady Lufton, “that when my back is turned you fight as gallantly for me.”\n\n“Ah, you are never under a cloud, like poor Lucy.”\n\n“Am I not? But, Fanny, you do not see all the clouds. The sun does not always shine for any of us, and the down-pouring rain and the heavy wind scatter also my fairest flowers,—as they have done hers, poor girl. Dear Fanny, I hope it may be long before any cloud comes across the brightness of your heaven. Of all the creatures I know you are the one most fitted for quiet continued sunshine.”\n\nAnd then Mrs. Robarts did get up and embrace her friend, thus hiding the tears which were running down her face. Continued sunshine indeed! A dark spot had already gathered on her horizon which was likely to fall in a very waterspout of rain. What was to come of that terrible notice which was now lying in the desk under Lady Lufton’s very arm?\n\n“But I am not come here to croak like an old raven,” continued Lady Lufton, when she had brought this embrace to an end. “It is probable that we all may have our sorrows; but I am quite sure of this,—that if we endeavour to do our duties honestly, we shall all find our consolation and all have our joys also. And now, my dear, let you and I say a few words about this unfortunate affair. It would not be natural if we were to hold our tongues to each other; would it?”\n\n“I suppose not,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“We should always be conceiving worse than the truth,—each as to the other’s thoughts. Now, some time ago, when I spoke to you about your sister-in-law and Ludovic—I daresay you remember—”\n\n“Oh, yes, I remember.”\n\n“We both thought then that there would really be no danger. To tell you the plain truth I fancied, and indeed hoped, that his affections were engaged elsewhere; but I was altogether wrong then; wrong in thinking it, and wrong in hoping it.”\n\nMrs. Robarts knew well that Lady Lufton was alluding to Griselda Grantly, but she conceived that it would be discreet to say nothing herself on that subject at present. She remembered, however, Lucy’s flashing eye when the possibility of Lord Lufton making such a marriage was spoken of in the pony-carriage, and could not but feel glad that Lady Lufton had been disappointed.\n\n“I do not at all impute any blame to Miss Robarts for what has occurred since,” continued her ladyship. “I wish you distinctly to understand that.”\n\n“I do not see how any one could blame her. She has behaved so nobly.”\n\n“It is of no use inquiring whether any one can. It is sufficient that I do not.”\n\n“But I think that is hardly sufficient,” said Mrs. Robarts, pertinaciously.\n\n“Is it not?” asked her ladyship, raising her eyebrows.\n\n“No. Only think what Lucy has done and is doing. If she had chosen to say that she would accept your son I really do not know how you could have justly blamed her. I do not by any means say that I would have advised such a thing.”\n\n“I am glad of that, Fanny.”\n\n“I have not given any advice; nor is it needed. I know no one more able than Lucy to see clearly, by her own judgment, what course she ought to pursue. I should be afraid to advise one whose mind is so strong, and who, of her own nature, is so self-denying as she is. She is sacrificing herself now, because she will not be the means of bringing trouble and dissension between you and your son. If you ask me, Lady Lufton, I think you owe her a deep debt of gratitude. I do, indeed. And as for blaming her—what has she done that you possibly could blame?”\n\n“Don Quixote on horseback!” said Lady Lufton. “Fanny, I shall always call you Don Quixote, and some day or other I will get somebody to write your adventures. But the truth is this, my dear: there has been imprudence. You may call it mine, if you will—though I really hardly see how I am to take the blame. I could not do other than ask Miss Robarts to my house, and I could not very well turn my son out of it. In point of fact, it has been the old story.”\n\n“Exactly; the story that is as old as the world, and which will continue as long as people are born into it. It is a story of God’s own telling!”\n\n“But, my dear child, you do not mean that every young gentleman and every young lady should fall in love with each other directly they meet! Such a doctrine would be very inconvenient.”\n\n“No, I do not mean that. Lord Lufton and Miss Grantly did not fall in love with each other, though you meant them to do so. But was it not quite as natural that Lord Lufton and Lucy should do so instead?”\n\n“It is generally thought, Fanny, that young ladies should not give loose to their affections until they have been certified of their friends’ approval.”\n\n“And that young gentlemen of fortune may amuse themselves as they please! I know that is what the world teaches, but I cannot agree to the justice of it. The terrible suffering which Lucy has to endure makes me cry out against it. She did not seek your son. The moment she began to suspect that there might be danger she avoided him scrupulously. She would not go down to Framley Court, though her not doing so was remarked by yourself. She would hardly go out about the place lest she should meet him. She was contented to put herself altogether in the background till he should have pleased to leave the place. But he—he came to her here, and insisted on seeing her. He found her when I was out, and declared himself determined to speak to her. What was she to do? She did try to escape, but he stopped her at the door. Was it her fault that he made her an offer?”\n\n“My dear, no one has said so.”\n\n“Yes, but you do say so when you tell me that young ladies should not give play to their affections without permission. He persisted in saying to her, here, all that it pleased him, though she implored him to be silent. I cannot tell the words she used, but she did implore him.”\n\n“I do not doubt that she behaved well.”\n\n“But he—he persisted, and begged her to accept his hand. She refused him then, Lady Lufton—not as some girls do, with a mock reserve, not intending to be taken at their words—but steadily, and, God forgive her, untruly. Knowing what your feelings would be, and knowing what the world would say, she declared to him that he was indifferent to her. What more could she do in your behalf?” And then Mrs. Robarts paused.\n\n“I shall wait till you have done, Fanny.”\n\n“You spoke of girls giving loose to their affections. She did not do so. She went about her work exactly as she had done before. She did not even speak to me of what had passed—not then, at least. She determined that it should all be as though it had never been. She had learned to love your son; but that was her misfortune and she would get over it as she might. Tidings came to us here that he was engaged, or about to engage himself, to Miss Grantly.”\n\n“Those tidings were untrue.”\n\n“Yes, we know that now; but she did not know it then. Of course she could not but suffer; but she suffered within herself.” Mrs. Robarts, as she said this, remembered the pony-carriage and how Puck had been beaten. “She made no complaint that he had ill-treated her—not even to herself. She had thought it right to reject his offer; and there, as far as he was concerned, was to be an end of it.”\n\n“That would be a matter of course, I should suppose.”\n\n“But it was not a matter of course, Lady Lufton. He returned from London to Framley on purpose to repeat his offer. He sent for her brother— You talk of a young lady waiting for her friends’ approval. In this matter who would be Lucy’s friends?”\n\n“You and Mr. Robarts, of course.”\n\n“Exactly; her only friends. Well, Lord Lufton sent for Mark and repeated his offer to him. Mind you, Mark had never heard a word of this before, and you may guess whether or no he was surprised. Lord Lufton repeated his offer in the most formal manner and claimed permission to see Lucy. She refused to see him. She has never seen him since that day when, in opposition to all her efforts, he made his way into this room. Mark,—as I think very properly,—would have allowed Lord Lufton to come up here. Looking at both their ages and position he could have had no right to forbid it. But Lucy positively refused to see your son, and sent him a message instead, of the purport of which you are now aware—that she would never accept him unless she did so at your request.”\n\n“It was a very proper message.”\n\n“I say nothing about that. Had she accepted him I would not have blamed her:—and so I told her, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“I cannot understand your saying that, Fanny.”\n\n“Well; I did say so. I don’t want to argue now about myself,—whether I was right or wrong, but I did say so. Whatever sanction I could give she would have had. But she again chose to sacrifice herself, although I believe she regards him with as true a love as ever a girl felt for a man. Upon my word I don’t know that she is right. Those considerations for the world may perhaps be carried too far.”\n\n“I think that she was perfectly right.”\n\n“Very well, Lady Lufton; I can understand that. But after such sacrifice on her part—a sacrifice made entirely to you—how can you talk of ‘not blaming her’? Is that the language in which you speak of those whose conduct from first to last has been superlatively excellent? If she is open to blame at all, it is—it is—”\n\nBut here Mrs. Robarts stopped herself. In defending her sister she had worked herself almost into a passion; but such a state of feeling was not customary to her, and now that she had spoken her mind she sank suddenly into silence.\n\n“It seems to me, Fanny, that you almost regret Miss Robarts’ decision,” said Lady Lufton.\n\n“My wish in this matter is for her happiness, and I regret anything that may mar it.”\n\n“You think nothing then of our welfare, and yet I do not know to whom I might have looked for hearty friendship and for sympathy in difficulties, if not to you?”\n\nPoor Mrs. Robarts was almost upset by this. A few months ago, before Lucy’s arrival, she would have declared that the interests of Lady Lufton’s family would have been paramount with her, after and next to those of her own husband. And even now, it seemed to argue so black an ingratitude on her part—this accusation that she was indifferent to them! From her childhood upwards she had revered and loved Lady Lufton, and for years had taught herself to regard her as an epitome of all that was good and gracious in woman. Lady Lufton’s theories of life had been accepted by her as the right theories, and those whom Lady Lufton had liked she had liked. But now it seemed that all these ideas which it had taken a life to build up were to be thrown to the ground, because she was bound to defend a sister-in-law whom she had only known for the last eight months. It was not that she regretted a word that she had spoken on Lucy’s behalf. Chance had thrown her and Lucy together, and, as Lucy was her sister, she should receive from her a sister’s treatment. But she did not the less feel how terrible would be the effect of any disseverance from Lady Lufton.\n\n“Oh, Lady Lufton,” she said, “do not say that.”\n\n“But, Fanny, dear, I must speak as I find. You were talking about clouds just now, and do you think that all this is not a cloud in my sky? Ludovic tells me that he is attached to Miss Robarts, and you tell me that she is attached to him; and I am called upon to decide between them. Her very act obliges me to do so.”\n\n“Dear Lady Lufton,” said Mrs. Robarts, springing from her seat. It seemed to her at the moment as though the whole difficulty were to be solved by an act of grace on the part of her old friend.\n\n“And yet I cannot approve of such a marriage,” said Lady Lufton.\n\nMrs. Robarts returned to her seat, saying nothing further.\n\n“Is not that a cloud on one’s horizon?” continued her ladyship. “Do you think that I can be basking in the sunshine while I have such a weight upon my heart as that? Ludovic will soon be home, but instead of looking to his return with pleasure I dread it. I would prefer that he should remain in Norway. I would wish that he should stay away for months. And, Fanny, it is a great addition to my misfortune to feel that you do not sympathize with me.”\n\nHaving said this, in a slow, sorrowful, and severe tone, Lady Lufton got up and took her departure. Of course Mrs. Robarts did not let her go without assuring her that she did sympathize with her,—did love her as she ever had loved her. But wounds cannot be cured as easily as they may be inflicted, and Lady Lufton went her way with much real sorrow at her heart. She was proud and masterful, fond of her own way, and much too careful of the worldly dignities to which her lot had called her: but she was a woman who could cause no sorrow to those she loved without deep sorrow to herself.\n\n# CHAPTER XLII. TOUCHING PITCH.\n\nIn these hot midsummer days, the end of June and the beginning of July, Mr. Sowerby had but an uneasy time of it. At his sister’s instance, he had hurried up to London, and there had remained for days in attendance on the lawyers. He had to see new lawyers, Miss Dunstable’s men of business, quiet old cautious gentlemen whose place of business was in a dark alley behind the Bank, Messrs. Slow and Bideawhile by name, who had no scruple in detaining him for hours while they or their clerks talked to him about anything or about nothing. It was of vital consequence to Mr. Sowerby that this business of his should be settled without delay, and yet these men, to whose care this settling was now confided, went on as though law processes were a sunny bank on which it delighted men to bask easily. And then, too, he had to go more than once to South Audley Street, which was a worse infliction; for the men in South Audley Street were less civil now than had been their wont. It was well understood there that Mr. Sowerby was no longer a client of the duke’s, but his opponent; no longer his nominee and dependant, but his enemy in the county. “Chaldicotes,” as old Mr. Gumption remarked to young Mr. Gagebee; “Chaldicotes, Gagebee, is a cooked goose, as far as Sowerby is concerned. And what difference could it make to him whether the duke is to own it or Miss Dunstable? For my part I cannot understand how a gentleman like Sowerby can like to see his property go into the hands of a gallipot wench whose money still smells of bad drugs. And nothing can be more ungrateful,” he said, “than Sowerby’s conduct. He has held the county for five-and-twenty years without expense; and now that the time for payment has come, he begrudges the price.” He called it no better than cheating, he did not—he, Mr. Gumption. According to his ideas Sowerby was attempting to cheat the duke. It may be imagined, therefore, that Mr. Sowerby did not feel any very great delight in attending at South Audley Street.\n\nAnd then rumour was spread about among all the bill-discounting leeches that blood was once more to be sucked from the Sowerby carcase. The rich Miss Dunstable had taken up his affairs; so much as that became known in the purlieus of the Goat and Compasses. Tom Tozer’s brother declared that she and Sowerby were going to make a match of it, and that any scrap of paper with Sowerby’s name on it would become worth its weight in bank-notes; but Tom Tozer himself—Tom, who was the real hero of the family—pooh-poohed at this, screwing up his nose, and alluding in most contemptuous terms to his brother’s softness. He knew better—as was indeed the fact. Miss Dunstable was buying up the squire, and by jingo she should buy them up—them, the Tozers, as well as others! They knew their value, the Tozers did;—whereupon they became more than ordinarily active.\n\nFrom them and all their brethren Mr. Sowerby at this time endeavoured to keep his distance, but his endeavours were not altogether effectual. Whenever he could escape for a day or two from the lawyers he ran down to Chaldicotes; but Tom Tozer in his perseverance followed him there, and boldly sent in his name by the servant at the front-door.\n\n“Mr. Sowerby is not just at home at the present moment,” said the well-trained domestic.\n\n“I’ll wait about then,” said Tom, seating himself on an heraldic stone griffin which flanked the big stone steps before the house. And in this way Mr. Tozer gained his purpose. Sowerby was still contesting the county, and it behoved him not to let his enemies say that he was hiding himself. It had been a part of his bargain with Miss Dunstable that he should contest the county. She had taken it into her head that the duke had behaved badly, and she had resolved that he should be made to pay for it. “The duke,” she said, “had meddled long enough;” she would now see whether the Chaldicotes interest would not suffice of itself to return a member for the county, even in opposition to the duke. Mr. Sowerby himself was so harassed at the time, that he would have given way on this point if he had had the power; but Miss Dunstable was determined, and he was obliged to yield to her. In this manner Mr. Tom Tozer succeeded and did make his way into Mr. Sowerby’s presence—of which intrusion one effect was the following letter from Mr. Sowerby to his friend Mark Robarts:—\n\nChaldicotes, July, 185—.\n\nMY DEAR ROBARTS,—I am so harassed at the present moment by an infinity of troubles of my own that I am almost callous to those of other people. They say that prosperity makes a man selfish. I have never tried that, but I am quite sure that adversity does so. Nevertheless I am anxious about those bills of yours—\n\n“Bills of mine!” said Robarts to himself, as he walked up and down the shrubbery path at the parsonage, reading this letter. This happened a day or two after his visit to the lawyer at Barchester.\n\n—and would rejoice greatly if I thought that I could save you from any further annoyance about them. That kite, Tom Tozer, has just been with me, and insists that both of them shall be paid. He knows—no one better—that no consideration was given for the latter. But he knows also that the dealing was not with him, nor even with his brother, and he will be prepared to swear that he gave value for both. He would swear anything for five hundred pounds—or for half the money, for that matter. I do not think that the father of mischief ever let loose upon the world a greater rascal than Tom Tozer.\n\nHe declares that nothing shall induce him to take one shilling less than the whole sum of nine hundred pounds. He has been brought to this by hearing that my debts are about to be paid. Heaven help me! The meaning of that is that these wretched acres, which are now mortgaged to one millionnaire, are to change hands and be mortgaged to another instead. By this exchange I may possibly obtain the benefit of having a house to live in for the next twelve months, but no other. Tozer, however, is altogether wrong in his scent; and the worst of it is that his malice will fall on you rather than on me.\n\nWhat I want you to do is this: let us pay him one hundred pounds between us. Though I sell the last sorry jade of a horse I have, I will make up fifty; and I know you can, at any rate, do as much as that. Then do you accept a bill, conjointly with me, for eight hundred. It shall be done in Forrest’s presence, and handed to him; and you shall receive back the two old bills into your own hands at the same time. This new bill should be timed to run ninety days; and I will move heaven and earth during that time to have it included in the general schedule of my debts which are to be secured on the Chaldicotes property.\n\nThe meaning of which was that Miss Dunstable was to be cozened into paying the money under an idea that it was part of the sum covered by the existing mortgage.\n\nWhat you said the other day at Barchester, as to never executing another bill, is very well as regards future transactions. Nothing can be wiser than such a resolution. But it would be folly—worse than folly—if you were to allow your furniture to be seized when the means of preventing it are so ready to your hand. By leaving the new bill in Forrest’s hands you may be sure that you are safe from the claws of such birds of prey as these Tozers. Even if I cannot get it settled when the three months are over, Forrest will enable you to make any arrangement that may be most convenient.\n\nFor Heaven’s sake, my dear fellow, do not refuse this. You can hardly conceive how it weighs upon me, this fear that bailiffs should make their way into your wife’s drawing-room. I know you think ill of me, and I do not wonder at it. But you would be less inclined to do so if you knew how terribly I am punished. Pray let me hear that you will do as I counsel you.\n\nYours always faithfully,\n\nIn answer to which the parson wrote a very short reply:—\n\nFramley, July, 185—.\n\nI will sign no more bills on any consideration.\n\nYours truly,\n\nAnd then having written this, and having shown it to his wife, he returned to the shrubbery walk and paced it up and down, looking every now and then to Sowerby’s letter as he thought over all the past circumstances of his friendship with that gentleman.\n\nThat the man who had written this letter should be his friend—that very fact was a disgrace to him. Sowerby so well knew himself and his own reputation, that he did not dare to suppose that his own word would be taken for anything,—not even when the thing promised was an act of the commonest honesty. “The old bills shall be given back into your own hands,” he had declared with energy, knowing that his friend and correspondent would not feel himself secure against further fraud under any less stringent guarantee. This gentleman, this county member, the owner of Chaldicotes, with whom Mark Robarts had been so anxious to be on terms of intimacy, had now come to such a phase of life that he had given over speaking of himself as an honest man. He had become so used to suspicion that he argued of it as of a thing of course. He knew that no one could trust either his spoken or his written word, and he was content to speak and to write without attempt to hide this conviction.\n\nAnd this was the man whom he had been so glad to call his friend; for whose sake he had been willing to quarrel with Lady Lufton, and at whose instance he had unconsciously abandoned so many of the best resolutions of his life. He looked back now, as he walked there slowly, still holding the letter in his hand, to the day when he had stopped at the school-house and written his letter to Mr. Sowerby, promising to join the party at Chaldicotes. He had been so eager then to have his own way, that he would not permit himself to go home and talk the matter over with his wife. He thought also of the manner in which he had been tempted to the house of the Duke of Omnium, and the conviction on his mind at the time that his giving way to that temptation would surely bring him to evil. And then he remembered the evening in Sowerby’s bedroom, when the bill had been brought out, and he had allowed himself to be persuaded to put his name upon it;—not because he was willing in this way to assist his friend, but because he was unable to refuse. He had lacked the courage to say, “No,” though he knew at the time how gross was the error which he was committing. He had lacked the courage to say, “No,” and hence had come upon him and on his household all this misery and cause for bitter repentance.\n\nI have written much of clergymen, but in doing so I have endeavoured to portray them as they bear on our social life rather than to describe the mode and working of their professional careers. Had I done the latter I could hardly have steered clear of subjects on which it has not been my intention to pronounce an opinion, and I should either have laden my fiction with sermons or I should have degraded my sermons into fiction. Therefore I have said but little in my narrative of this man’s feelings or doings as a clergyman.\n\nBut I must protest against its being on this account considered that Mr. Robarts was indifferent to the duties of his clerical position. He had been fond of pleasure and had given way to temptation,—as is so customarily done by young men of six-and-twenty, who are placed beyond control and who have means at command. Had he remained as a curate till that age, subject in all his movements to the eye of a superior, he would, we may say, have put his name to no bills, have ridden after no hounds, have seen nothing of the iniquities of Gatherum Castle. There are men of twenty-six as fit to stand alone as ever they will be—fit to be prime ministers, heads of schools, judges on the bench—almost fit to be bishops; but Mark Robarts had not been one of them. He had within him many aptitudes for good, but not the strengthened courage of a man to act up to them. The stuff of which his manhood was to be formed had been slow of growth, as it is with many men; and, consequently, when temptation was offered to him, he had fallen.\n\nBut he deeply grieved over his own stumbling, and from time to time, as his periods of penitence came upon him, he resolved that he would once more put his shoulder to the wheel as became one who fights upon earth that battle for which he had put on his armour. Over and over again did he think of those words of Mr. Crawley, and now as he walked up and down the path, crumpling Mr. Sowerby’s letter in his hand, he thought of them again—“It is a terrible falling off; terrible in the fall, but doubly terrible through that difficulty of returning.” Yes; that is a difficulty which multiplies itself in a fearful ratio as one goes on pleasantly running down the path—whitherward? Had it come to that with him that he could not return—that he could never again hold up his head with a safe conscience as the pastor of his parish! It was Sowerby who had led him into this misery, who had brought on him this ruin? But then had not Sowerby paid him? Had not that stall which he now held in Barchester been Sowerby’s gift? He was a poor man now—a distressed, poverty-stricken man; but nevertheless he wished with all his heart that he had never become a sharer in the good things of the Barchester chapter.\n\n“I shall resign the stall,” he said to his wife that night. “I think I may say that I have made up my mind as to that.”\n\n“But, Mark, will not people say that it is odd?”\n\n“I cannot help it—they must say it. Fanny, I fear that we shall have to bear the saying of harder words than that.”\n\n“Nobody can ever say that you have done anything that is unjust or dishonourable. If there are such men as Mr. Sowerby—”\n\n“The blackness of his fault will not excuse mine.” And then again he sat silent, hiding his eyes, while his wife, sitting by him, held his hand.\n\n“Don’t make yourself wretched, Mark. Matters will all come right yet. It cannot be that the loss of a few hundred pounds should ruin you.”\n\n“It is not the money—it is not the money!”\n\n“But you have done nothing wrong, Mark.”\n\n“How am I to go into the church, and take my place before them all, when every one will know that bailiffs are in the house?” And then, dropping his head on to the table, he sobbed aloud.\n\nMark Robarts’ mistake had been mainly this,—he had thought to touch pitch and not to be defiled. He, looking out from his pleasant parsonage into the pleasant upper ranks of the world around him, had seen that men and things in those quarters were very engaging. His own parsonage, with his sweet wife, were exceedingly dear to him, and Lady Lufton’s affectionate friendship had its value; but were not these things rather dull for one who had lived in the best sets at Harrow and Oxford;—unless, indeed, he could supplement them with some occasional bursts of more lively life? Cakes and ale were as pleasant to his palate as to the palates of those with whom he had formerly lived at college. He had the same eye to look at a horse, and the same heart to make him go across a country, as they. And then, too, he found that men liked him,—men and women also; men and women who were high in worldly standing. His ass’s ears were tickled, and he learned to fancy that he was intended by nature for the society of high people. It seemed as though he were following his appointed course in meeting men and women of the world at the houses of the fashionable and the rich. He was not the first clergyman that had so lived and had so prospered. Yes, clergymen had so lived, and had done their duties in their sphere of life altogether to the satisfaction of their countrymen—and of their sovereigns. Thus Mark Robarts had determined that he would touch pitch, and escape defilement if that were possible. With what result those who have read so far will have perceived.\n\nLate on the following afternoon who should drive up to the parsonage door but Mr. Forrest, the bank manager from Barchester—Mr. Forrest, to whom Sowerby had always pointed as the Deus ex machinâ who, if duly invoked, could relieve them all from their present troubles, and dismiss the whole Tozer family—not howling into the wilderness, as one would have wished to do with that brood of Tozers, but so gorged with prey that from them no further annoyance need be dreaded? All this Mr. Forrest could do; nay, more, most willingly would do! Only let Mark Robarts put himself into the banker’s hand, and blandly sign what documents the banker might desire.\n\n“This is a very unpleasant affair,” said Mr. Forrest as soon as they were closeted together in Mark’s book-room. In answer to which observation the parson acknowledged that it was a very unpleasant affair.\n\n“Mr. Sowerby has managed to put you into the hands of about the worst set of rogues now existing, in their line of business, in London.”\n\n“So I supposed; Curling told me the same.” Curling was the Barchester attorney whose aid he had lately invoked.\n\n“Curling has threatened them that he will expose their whole trade; but one of them who was down here, a man named Tozer, replied, that you had much more to lose by exposure than he had. He went further and declared that he would defy any jury in England to refuse him his money. He swore that he discounted both bills in the regular way of business; and, though this is of course false, I fear that it will be impossible to prove it so. He well knows that you are a clergyman, and that, therefore, he has a stronger hold on you than on other men.”\n\n“The disgrace shall fall on Sowerby,” said Robarts, hardly actuated at the moment by any strong feeling of Christian forgiveness.\n\n“I fear, Mr. Robarts, that he is somewhat in the condition of the Tozers. He will not feel it as you will do.”\n\n“I must bear it, Mr. Forrest, as best I may.”\n\n“Will you allow me, Mr. Robarts, to give you my advice? Perhaps I ought to apologize for intruding it upon you; but as the bills have been presented and dishonoured across my counter, I have, of necessity, become acquainted with the circumstances.”\n\n“I am sure I am very much obliged to you,” said Mark.\n\n“You must pay this money, or, at any rate, the most considerable portion of it;—the whole of it, indeed, with such deduction as a lawyer may be able to induce these hawks to make on the sight of the ready money. Perhaps £750 or £800 may see you clear of the whole affair.”\n\n“But I have not a quarter of that sum lying by me.”\n\n“No, I suppose not; but what I would recommend is this: that you should borrow the money from the bank, on your own responsibility,—with the joint security of some friend who may be willing to assist you with his name. Lord Lufton probably would do it.”\n\n“No, Mr. Forrest—”\n\n“Listen to me first, before you make up your mind. If you took this step, of course you would do so with the fixed intention of paying the money yourself,—without any further reliance on Sowerby or on any one else.”\n\n“I shall not rely on Mr. Sowerby again; you may be sure of that.”\n\n“What I mean is that you must teach yourself to recognize the debt as your own. If you can do that, with your income you can surely pay it, with interest, in two years. If Lord Lufton will assist you with his name I will so arrange the bills that the payments shall be made to fall equally over that period. In that way the world will know nothing about it, and in two years’ time you will once more be a free man. Many men, Mr. Robarts, have bought their experience much dearer than that, I can assure you.”\n\n“Mr. Forrest, it is quite out of the question.”\n\n“You mean that Lord Lufton will not give you his name.”\n\n“I certainly shall not ask him; but that is not all. In the first place my income will not be what you think it, for I shall probably give up the prebend at Barchester.”\n\n“Give up the prebend! give up six hundred a year!”\n\n“And, beyond this, I think I may say that nothing shall tempt me to put my name to another bill. I have learned a lesson which I hope I may never forget.”\n\n“Then what do you intend to do?”\n\n“Nothing!”\n\n“Then those men will sell every stick of furniture about the place. They know that your property here is enough to secure all that they claim.”\n\n“If they have the power, they must sell it.”\n\n“And all the world will know the facts.”\n\n“So it must be. Of the faults which a man commits he must bear the punishment. If it were only myself!”\n\n“That’s where it is, Mr. Robarts. Think what your wife will have to suffer in going through such misery as that! You had better take my advice. Lord Lufton, I am sure—”\n\nBut the very name of Lord Lufton, his sister’s lover, again gave him courage. He thought, too, of the accusations which Lord Lufton had brought against him on that night when he had come to him in the coffee-room of the hotel, and he felt that it was impossible that he should apply to him for such aid. It would be better to tell all to Lady Lufton! That she would relieve him, let the cost to herself be what it might, he was very sure. Only this;—that in looking to her for assistance he would be forced to bite the dust in very deed.\n\n“Thank you, Mr. Forrest, but I have made up my mind. Do not think that I am the less obliged to you for your disinterested kindness,—for I know that it is disinterested; but this I think I may confidently say, that not even to avert so terrible a calamity will I again put my name to any bill. Even if you could take my own promise to pay without the addition of any second name, I would not do it.”\n\nThere was nothing for Mr. Forrest to do under such circumstances but simply to drive back to Barchester. He had done the best for the young clergyman according to his lights, and perhaps, in a worldly view, his advice had not been bad. But Mark dreaded the very name of a bill. He was as a dog that had been terribly scorched, and nothing should again induce him to go near the fire.\n\n“Was not that the man from the bank?” said Fanny, coming into the room when the sound of the wheels had died away.\n\n“Yes; Mr. Forrest.”\n\n“Well, dearest?”\n\n“We must prepare ourselves for the worst.”\n\n“You will not sign any more papers, eh, Mark?”\n\n“No; I have just now positively refused to do so.”\n\n“Then I can bear anything. But, dearest, dearest Mark, will you not let me tell Lady Lufton?”\n\nLet them look at the matter in any way the punishment was very heavy.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIII. IS SHE NOT INSIGNIFICANT?\n\nAnd now a month went by at Framley without any increase of comfort to our friends there, and also without any absolute development of the ruin which had been daily expected at the parsonage. Sundry letters had reached Mr. Robarts from various personages acting in the Tozer interest, all of which he referred to Mr. Curling, of Barchester. Some of these letters contained prayers for the money, pointing out how an innocent widow lady had been induced to invest her all on the faith of Mr. Robarts’ name, and was now starving in a garret, with her three children, because Mr. Robarts would not make good his own undertakings. But the majority of them were filled with threats;—only two days longer would be allowed and then the sheriff’s officers would be enjoined to do their work; then one day of grace would be added, at the expiration of which the dogs of war would be unloosed. These, as fast as they came, were sent to Mr. Curling, who took no notice of them individually, but continued his endeavour to prevent the evil day. The second bill Mr. Robarts would take up—such was Mr. Curling’s proposition; and would pay by two instalments of £250 each, the first in two months, and the second in four. If this were acceptable to the Tozer interest—well; if it were not, the sheriff’s officers must do their worst and the Tozer interest must look for what it could get. The Tozer interest would not declare itself satisfied with these terms, and so the matter went on. During which the roses faded from day to day on the cheeks of Mrs. Robarts, as under such circumstances may easily be conceived.\n\nIn the meantime Lucy still remained at Hogglestock and had there become absolute mistress of the house. Poor Mrs. Crawley had been at death’s door; for some days she was delirious, and afterwards remained so weak as to be almost unconscious; but now the worst was over, and Mr. Crawley had been informed, that as far as human judgment might pronounce, his children would not become orphans nor would he become a widower. During these weeks Lucy had not once been home nor had she seen any of the Framley people. “Why should she incur the risk of conveying infection for so small an object?” as she herself argued, writing by letters, which were duly fumigated before they were opened at the parsonage. So she remained at Hogglestock, and the Crawley children, now admitted to all the honours of the nursery, were kept at Framley. They were kept at Framley, although it was expected from day to day that the beds on which they lay would be seized for the payment of Mr. Sowerby’s debts.\n\nLucy, as I have said, became mistress of the house at Hogglestock and made herself absolutely ascendant over Mr. Crawley. Jellies, and broth, and fruit, and even butter, came from Lufton Court, which she displayed on the table, absolutely on the cloth before him, and yet he bore it. I cannot say that he partook of these delicacies with any freedom himself, but he did drink his tea when it was given to him although it contained Framley cream;—and, had he known it, Bohea itself from the Framley chest. In truth, in these days, he had given himself over to the dominion of this stranger; and he said nothing beyond, “Well, well,” with two uplifted hands, when he came upon her as she was sewing the buttons on to his own shirts—sewing on the buttons and perhaps occasionally applying her needle elsewhere,—not without utility.\n\nHe said to her at this period very little in the way of thanks. Some protracted conversations they did have, now and again, during the long evenings; but even in these he did not utter many words as to their present state of life. It was on religion chiefly that he spoke, not lecturing her individually, but laying down his ideas as to what the life of a Christian should be, and especially what should be the life of a minister. “But though I can see this, Miss Robarts,” he said, “I am bound to say that no one has fallen off so frequently as myself. I have renounced the devil and all his works; but it is by word of mouth only—by word of mouth only. How shall a man crucify the old Adam that is within him, unless he throw himself prostrate in the dust and acknowledge that all his strength is weaker than water?” To this, often as it might be repeated, she would listen patiently, comforting him by such words as her theology would supply; but then, when this was over, she would again resume her command and enforce from him a close obedience to her domestic behests.\n\nAt the end of the month Lord Lufton came back to Framley Court. His arrival there was quite unexpected; though, as he pointed out when his mother expressed some surprise, he had returned exactly at the time named by him before he started.\n\n“I need not say, Ludovic, how glad I am to have you,” said she, looking to his face and pressing his arm; “the more so, indeed, seeing that I hardly expected it.”\n\nHe said nothing to his mother about Lucy the first evening, although there was some conversation respecting the Robarts family.\n\n“I am afraid Mr. Robarts has embarrassed himself,” said Lady Lufton, looking very seriously. “Rumours reach me which are most distressing. I have said nothing to anybody as yet—not even to Fanny; but I can see in her face, and hear in the tones of her voice, that she is suffering some great sorrow.”\n\n“I know all about it,” said Lord Lufton.\n\n“You know all about it, Ludovic?”\n\n“Yes; it is through that precious friend of mine, Mr. Sowerby, of Chaldicotes. He has accepted bills for Sowerby; indeed, he told me so.”\n\n“What business had he at Chaldicotes? What had he to do with such friends as that? I do not know how I am to forgive him.”\n\n“It was through me that he became acquainted with Sowerby. You must remember that, mother.”\n\n“I do not see that that is any excuse. Is he to consider that all your acquaintances must necessarily be his friends also? It is reasonable to suppose that you in your position must live occasionally with a great many people who are altogether unfit companions for him as a parish clergyman. He will not remember this, and he must be taught it. What business had he to go to Gatherum Castle?”\n\n“He got his stall at Barchester by going there.”\n\n“He would be much better without his stall, and Fanny has the sense to know this. What does he want with two houses? Prebendal stalls are for older men than he—for men who have earned them, and who at the end of their lives want some ease. I wish with all my heart that he had never taken it.”\n\n“Six hundred a year has its charms all the same,” said Lufton, getting up and strolling out of the room.\n\n“If Mark really be in any difficulty,” he said, later in the evening, “we must put him on his legs.”\n\n“You mean, pay his debts?”\n\n“Yes; he has no debts except these acceptances of Sowerby’s.”\n\n“How much will it be, Ludovic?”\n\n“A thousand pounds, perhaps, more or less. I’ll find the money, mother; only I shan’t be able to pay you quite as soon as I intended.” Whereupon his mother got up, and throwing her arms round his neck declared that she would never forgive him if he ever said a word more about her little present to him. I suppose there is no pleasure a mother can have more attractive than giving away her money to an only son.\n\nLucy’s name was first mentioned at breakfast the next morning. Lord Lufton had made up his mind to attack his mother on the subject early in the morning—before he went up to the parsonage; but as matters turned out Miss Robarts’ doings were necessarily brought under discussion without reference to Lord Lufton’s special aspirations regarding her. The fact of Mrs. Crawley’s illness had been mentioned, and Lady Lufton had stated how it had come to pass that all the Crawleys’ children were at the parsonage.\n\n“I must say that Fanny has behaved excellently,” said Lady Lufton. “It was just what might have been expected from her. And indeed,” she added, speaking in an embarrassed tone, “so has Miss Robarts. Miss Robarts has remained at Hogglestock and nursed Mrs. Crawley through the whole.”\n\n“Remained at Hogglestock—through the fever!” exclaimed his lordship.\n\n“Yes, indeed,” said Lady Lufton.\n\n“And is she there now?”\n\n“Oh, yes; I am not aware that she thinks of leaving just yet.”\n\n“Then I say that it is a great shame—a scandalous shame!”\n\n“But, Ludovic, it was her own doing.”\n\n“Oh, yes; I understand. But why should she be sacrificed? Were there no nurses in the country to be hired, but that she must go and remain there for a month at the bedside of a pestilent fever? There is no justice in it.”\n\n“Justice, Ludovic? I don’t know about justice, but there was great Christian charity. Mrs. Crawley has probably owed her life to Miss Robarts.”\n\n“Has she been ill? Is she ill? I insist upon knowing whether she is ill. I shall go over to Hogglestock myself immediately after breakfast.”\n\nTo this Lady Lufton made no reply. If Lord Lufton chose to go to Hogglestock she could not prevent him. She thought, however, that it would be much better that he should stay away. He would be quite as open to the infection as Lucy Robarts; and, moreover, Mrs. Crawley’s bedside would be as inconvenient a place as might be selected for any interview between two lovers. Lady Lufton felt at the present moment that she was cruelly treated by circumstances with reference to Miss Robarts. Of course it would have been her part to lessen, if she could do so without injustice, that high idea which her son entertained of the beauty and worth of the young lady; but, unfortunately, she had been compelled to praise her and to load her name with all manner of eulogy. Lady Lufton was essentially a true woman, and not even with the object of carrying out her own views in so important a matter would she be guilty of such deception as she might have practised by simply holding her tongue; but nevertheless she could hardly reconcile herself to the necessity of singing Lucy’s praises.\n\nAfter breakfast Lady Lufton got up from her chair, but hung about the room without making any show of leaving. In accordance with her usual custom she would have asked her son what he was going to do; but she did not dare so to inquire now. Had he not declared, only a few minutes since, whither he would go? “I suppose I shall see you at lunch?” at last she said.\n\n“At lunch? Well, I don’t know. Look here, mother. What am I to say to Miss Robarts when I see her?” and he leaned with his back against the chimney-piece as he interrogated his mother.\n\n“What are you to say to her, Ludovic?”\n\n“Yes; what am I to say,—as coming from you? Am I to tell her that you will receive her as your daughter-in-law?”\n\n“Ludovic, I have explained all that to Miss Robarts herself.”\n\n“Explained what?”\n\n“I have told her that I did not think that such a marriage would make either you or her happy.”\n\n“And why have you told her so? Why have you taken upon yourself to judge for me in such a matter, as though I were a child? Mother, you must unsay what you have said.”\n\nLord Lufton, as he spoke, looked full into his mother’s face; and he did so, not as though he were begging from her a favour, but issuing to her a command. She stood near him, with one hand on the breakfast-table, gazing at him almost furtively, not quite daring to meet the full view of his eye. There was only one thing on earth which Lady Lufton feared, and that was her son’s displeasure. The sun of her earthly heaven shone upon her through the medium of his existence. If she were driven to quarrel with him, as some ladies of her acquaintance were driven to quarrel with their sons, the world to her would be over. Not but what facts might be so strong as to make it absolutely necessary that she should do this. As some people resolve that, under certain circumstances, they will commit suicide, so she could see that, under certain circumstances, she must consent even to be separated from him. She would not do wrong,—not that which she knew to be wrong,—even for his sake. If it were necessary that all her happiness should collapse and be crushed in ruin around her, she must endure it, and wait God’s time to relieve her from so dark a world. The light of the sun was very dear to her, but even that might be purchased at too dear a cost.\n\n“I told you before, mother, that my choice was made, and I asked you then to give your consent; you have now had time to think about it, and therefore I have come to ask you again. I have reason to know that there will be no impediment to my marriage if you will frankly hold out your hand to Lucy.”\n\nThe matter was altogether in Lady Lufton’s hands, but, fond as she was of power, she absolutely wished that it were not so. Had her son married without asking her and then brought Lucy home as his wife, she would undoubtedly have forgiven him; and much as she might have disliked the match, she would, ultimately, have embraced the bride. But now she was compelled to exercise her judgment. If he married imprudently, it would be her doing. How was she to give her expressed consent to that which she believed to be wrong?\n\n“Do you know anything against her; any reason why she should not be my wife?” continued he.\n\n“If you mean as regards her moral conduct, certainly not,” said Lady Lufton. “But I could say as much as that in favour of a great many young ladies whom I should regard as very ill suited for such a marriage.”\n\n“Yes; some might be vulgar, some might be ill-tempered, some might be ugly; others might be burdened with disagreeable connections. I can understand that you should object to a daughter-in-law under any of these circumstances. But none of these things can be said of Miss Robarts. I defy you to say that she is not in all respects what a lady should be.”\n\nBut her father was a doctor of medicine, she is the sister of the parish clergyman, she is only five feet two in height, and is so uncommonly brown! Had Lady Lufton dared to give a catalogue of her objections, such would have been its extent and nature. But she did not dare to do this.\n\n“I cannot say, Ludovic, that she is possessed of all that you should seek in a wife.” Such was her answer.\n\n“Do you mean that she has not got money?”\n\n“No, not that; I should be very sorry to see you making money your chief object, or indeed any essential object. If it chanced that your wife did have money, no doubt you would find it a convenience. But pray understand me, Ludovic; I would not for a moment advise you to subject your happiness to such a necessity as that. It is not because she is without fortune—”\n\n“Then why is it? At breakfast you were singing her praises, and saying how excellent she is.”\n\n“If I were forced to put my objection into one word, I should say—” and then she paused, hardly daring to encounter the frown which was already gathering itself on her son’s brow.\n\n“You would say what?” said Lord Lufton, almost roughly.\n\n“Don’t be angry with me, Ludovic; all that I think, and all that I say on this subject, I think and say with only one object—that of your happiness. What other motive can I have for anything in this world?” And then she came close to him and kissed him.\n\n“But tell me, mother, what is this objection; what is this terrible word that is to sum up the list of all poor Lucy’s sins, and prove that she is unfit for married life?”\n\n“Ludovic, I did not say that. You know that I did not.”\n\n“What is the word, mother?”\n\nAnd then at last Lady Lufton spoke it out. “She is—insignificant. I believe her to be a very good girl, but she is not qualified to fill the high position to which you would exalt her.”\n\n“Insignificant!”\n\n“Yes, Ludovic, I think so.”\n\n“Then, mother, you do not know her. You must permit me to say that you are talking of a girl whom you do not know. Of all the epithets of opprobrium which the English language could give you, that would be nearly the last which she would deserve.”\n\n“I have not intended any opprobrium.”\n\n“Insignificant!”\n\n“Perhaps you do not quite understand me, Ludovic.”\n\n“I know what insignificant means, mother.”\n\n“I think that she would not worthily fill the position which your wife should take in the world.”\n\n“I understand what you say.”\n\n“She would not do you honour at the head of your table.”\n\n“Ah, I understand. You want me to marry some bouncing Amazon, some pink and white giantess of fashion who would frighten the little people into their proprieties.”\n\n“Oh, Ludovic! you are intending to laugh at me now.”\n\n“I was never less inclined to laugh in my life—never, I can assure you. And now I am more certain than ever that your objection to Miss Robarts arises from your not knowing her. You will find, I think, when you do know her, that she is as well able to hold her own as any lady of your acquaintance;—ay, and to maintain her husband’s position, too. I can assure you that I shall have no fear of her on that score.”\n\n“I think, dearest, that perhaps you hardly—”\n\n“I think this, mother, that in such a matter as this I must choose for myself. I have chosen; and I now ask you, as my mother, to go to her and bid her welcome. Dear mother, I will own this, that I should not be happy if I thought that you did not love my wife.” These last words he said in a tone of affection that went to his mother’s heart, and then he left the room.\n\nPoor Lady Lufton, when she was alone, waited till she heard her son’s steps retreating through the hall, and then betook herself up-stairs to her customary morning work. She sat down at last as though about so to occupy herself; but her mind was too full to allow of her taking up her pen. She had often said to herself, in days which to her were not as yet long gone by, that she would choose a bride for her son, and that then she would love the chosen one with all her heart. She would dethrone herself in favour of this new queen, sinking with joy into her dowager state, in order that her son’s wife might shine with the greater splendour. The fondest day-dreams of her life had all had reference to the time when her son should bring home a new Lady Lufton, selected by herself from the female excellence of England, and in which she might be the first to worship her new idol. But could she dethrone herself for Lucy Robarts? Could she give up her chair of state in order to place thereon the little girl from the parsonage? Could she take to her heart, and treat with absolute loving confidence, with the confidence of an almost idolatrous mother, that little chit who, a few months since, had sat awkwardly in one corner of her drawing-room, afraid to speak to any one? And yet it seemed that it must come to this—to this—or else those day-dreams of hers would in nowise come to pass.\n\nShe sat herself down, trying to think whether it were possible that Lucy might fill the throne; for she had begun to recognize it as probable that her son’s will would be too strong for her; but her thoughts would fly away to Griselda Grantly. In her first and only matured attempt to realize her day-dreams, she had chosen Griselda for her queen. She had failed there, seeing that the fates had destined Miss Grantly for another throne;—for another and a higher one, as far as the world goes. She would have made Griselda the wife of a baron, but fate was about to make that young lady the wife of a marquis. Was there cause of grief in this? Did she really regret that Miss Grantly, with all her virtues, should be made over to the house of Hartletop? Lady Lufton was a woman who did not bear disappointment lightly; but nevertheless she did almost feel herself to have been relieved from a burden when she thought of the termination of the Lufton-Grantly marriage treaty. What if she had been successful, and, after all, the prize had been other than she had expected? She was sometimes prone to think that that prize was not exactly all that she had once hoped. Griselda looked the very thing that Lady Lufton wanted for a queen;—but how would a queen reign who trusted only to her looks? In that respect it was perhaps well for her that destiny had interposed. Griselda, she was driven to admit, was better suited to Lord Dumbello than to her son.\n\nBut still—such a queen as Lucy! Could it ever come to pass that the lieges of the kingdom would bow the knee in proper respect before so puny a sovereign? And then there was that feeling which, in still higher quarters, prevents the marriage of princes with the most noble of their people. Is it not a recognized rule of these realms that none of the blood royal shall raise to royal honours those of the subjects who are by birth un-royal! Lucy was a subject of the house of Lufton in that she was the sister of the parson and a resident denizen of the parsonage. Presuming that Lucy herself might do for queen—granting that she might have some faculty to reign, the crown having been duly placed on her brow—how, then, about that clerical brother near the throne? Would it not come to this, that there would no longer be a queen at Framley?\n\nAnd yet she knew that she must yield. She did not say so to herself. She did not as yet acknowledge that she must put out her hand to Lucy, calling her by name as her daughter. She did not absolutely say as much to her own heart;—not as yet. But she did begin to bethink herself of Lucy’s high qualities, and to declare to herself that the girl, if not fit to be a queen, was at any rate fit to be a woman. That there was a spirit within that body, insignificant though the body might be, Lady Lufton was prepared to admit. That she had acquired the power—the chief of all powers in this world—of sacrificing herself for the sake of others; that, too, was evident enough. That she was a good girl, in the usual acceptation of the word good, Lady Lufton had never doubted. She was ready-witted too, prompt in action, gifted with a certain fire. It was that gift of fire which had won for her, so unfortunately, Lord Lufton’s love. It was quite possible for her also to love Lucy Robarts; Lady Lufton admitted that to herself;—but then who could bow the knee before her, and serve her as a queen? Was it not a pity that she should be so insignificant?\n\nBut, nevertheless, we may say that as Lady Lufton sate that morning in her own room for two hours without employment, the star of Lucy Robarts was gradually rising in the firmament. After all, love was the food chiefly necessary for the nourishment of Lady Lufton,—the only food absolutely necessary. She was not aware of this herself, nor probably would those who knew her best have so spoken of her. They would have declared that family pride was her daily pabulum, and she herself would have said so too, calling it, however, by some less offensive name. Her son’s honour, and the honour of her house!—of those she would have spoken as the things dearest to her in this world. And this was partly true, for had her son been dishonoured, she would have sunk with sorrow to the grave. But the one thing necessary to her daily life was the power of loving those who were near to her.\n\nLord Lufton, when he left the dining-room, intended at once to go up to the parsonage, but he first strolled round the garden in order that he might make up his mind what he would say there. He was angry with his mother, having not had the wit to see that she was about to give way and yield to him, and he was determined to make it understood that in this matter he would have his own way. He had learned that which it was necessary that he should know as to Lucy’s heart, and such being the case he would not conceive it possible that he should be debarred by his mother’s opposition. “There is no son in England loves his mother better than I do,” he said to himself; “but there are some things which a man cannot stand. She would have married me to that block of stone if I would have let her; and now, because she is disappointed there— Insignificant! I never in my life heard anything so absurd, so untrue, so uncharitable, so— She’d like me to bring a dragon home, I suppose. It would serve her right if I did,—some creature that would make the house intolerable to her.” “She must do it though,” he said again, “or she and I will quarrel,” and then he turned off towards the gate, preparing to go to the parsonage.\n\n“My lord, have you heard what has happened?” said the gardener, coming to him at the gate. The man was out of breath and almost overwhelmed by the greatness of his own tidings.\n\n“No; I have heard nothing. What is it?”\n\n“The bailiffs have taken possession of everything at the parsonage.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLIV. THE PHILISTINES AT THE PARSONAGE.\n\nIt has been already told how things went on between the Tozers, Mr. Curling, and Mark Robarts during that month. Mr. Forrest had drifted out of the business altogether, as also had Mr. Sowerby, as far as any active participation in it went. Letters came frequently from Mr. Curling to the parsonage, and at last came a message by special mission to say that the evil day was at hand. As far as Mr. Curling’s professional experience would enable him to anticipate or foretell the proceedings of such a man as Tom Tozer, he thought that the sheriff’s officers would be at Framley Parsonage on the following morning. Mr. Curling’s experience did not mislead him in this respect.\n\n“And what will you do, Mark?” said Fanny, speaking through her tears, after she had read the letter which her husband handed to her.\n\n“Nothing. What can I do? They must come.”\n\n“Lord Lufton came to-day. Will you not go to him?”\n\n“No. If I were to do so it would be the same as asking him for the money.”\n\n“Why not borrow it of him, dearest? Surely it would not be so much for him to lend.”\n\n“I could not do it. Think of Lucy, and how she stands with him. Besides I have already had words with Lufton about Sowerby and his money matters. He thinks that I am to blame, and he would tell me so; and then there would be sharp things said between us. He would advance me the money if I pressed for it, but he would do so in a way that would make it impossible that I should take it.”\n\nThere was nothing more then to be said. If she had had her own way Mrs. Robarts would have gone at once to Lady Lufton, but she could not induce her husband to sanction such a proceeding. The objection to seeking assistance from her ladyship was as strong as that which prevailed as to her son. There had already been some little beginning of ill-feeling, and under such circumstances it was impossible to ask for pecuniary assistance. Fanny, however, had a prophetic assurance that assistance out of these difficulties must in the end come to them from that quarter, or not come at all; and she would fain, had she been allowed, make everything known at the big house.\n\nOn the following morning they breakfasted at the usual hour, but in great sadness. A maid-servant, whom Mrs. Robarts had brought with her when she married, told her that a rumour of what was to happen had reached the kitchen. Stubbs, the groom, had been in Barchester on the preceding day, and, according to his account—so said Mary—everybody in the city was talking about it. “Never mind, Mary,” said Mrs. Robarts, and Mary replied, “Oh, no, of course not, ma’am.”\n\nIn these days Mrs. Robarts was ordinarily very busy, seeing that there were six children in the house, four of whom had come to her but ill supplied with infantine belongings; and now, as usual, she went about her work immediately after breakfast. But she moved about the house very slowly, and was almost unable to give her orders to the servants, and spoke sadly to the children who hung about her wondering what was the matter. Her husband at the same time took himself to his book-room, but when there did not attempt any employment. He thrust his hands into his pockets, and, leaning against the fire-place, fixed his eyes upon the table before him without looking at anything that was on it; it was impossible for him to betake himself to his work. Remember what is the ordinary labour of a clergyman in his study, and think how fit he must have been for such employment! What would have been the nature of a sermon composed at such a moment, and with what satisfaction could he have used the sacred volume in referring to it for his arguments? He, in this respect, was worse off than his wife; she did employ herself, but he stood there without moving, doing nothing, with fixed eyes, thinking what men would say of him.\n\nLuckily for him this state of suspense was not long, for within half an hour of his leaving the breakfast-table the footman knocked at his door—that footman with whom at the beginning of his difficulties he had made up his mind to dispense, but who had been kept on because of the Barchester prebend.\n\n“If you please, your reverence, there are two men outside,” said the footman.\n\nTwo men! Mark knew well enough what men they were, but he could hardly take the coming of two such men to his quiet country parsonage quite as a matter of course.\n\n“Who are they, John?” said he, not wishing any answer, but because the question was forced upon him.\n\n“I’m afeard they’re—bailiffs, sir.”\n\n“Very well, John; that will do; of course they must do what they please about the place.”\n\nAnd then, when the servant left him, he still stood without moving, exactly as he had stood before. There he remained for ten minutes, but the time went by very slowly. When about noon some circumstance told him what was the hour, he was astonished to find that the day had not nearly passed away.\n\nAnd then another tap was struck on the door,—a sound which he well recognized,—and his wife crept silently into the room. She came close up to him before she spoke, and put her arm within his:\n\n“Mark,” she said, “the men are here; they are in the yard.”\n\n“I know it,” he answered gruffly.\n\n“Will it be better that you should see them, dearest?”\n\n“See them; no; what good can I do by seeing them? But I shall see them soon enough; they will be here, I suppose, in a few minutes.”\n\n“They are taking an inventory, cook says; they are in the stable now.”\n\n“Very well; they must do as they please; I cannot help them.”\n\n“Cook says that if they are allowed their meals and some beer, and if nobody takes anything away, they will be quite civil.”\n\n“Civil! But what does it matter? Let them eat and drink what they please, as long as the food lasts. I don’t suppose the butcher will send you more.”\n\n“But, Mark, there’s nothing due to the butcher,—only the regular monthly bill.”\n\n“Very well; you’ll see.”\n\n“Oh, Mark, don’t look at me in that way. Do not turn away from me. What is to comfort us if we do not cling to each other now?”\n\n“Comfort us! God help you! I wonder, Fanny, that you can bear to stay in the room with me.”\n\n“Mark, dearest Mark, my own dear, dearest husband! who is to be true to you, if I am not? You shall not turn from me. How can anything like this make a difference between you and me?” And then she threw her arms round his neck and embraced him.\n\nIt was a terrible morning to him, and one of which every incident will dwell on his memory to the last day of his life. He had been so proud in his position—had assumed to himself so prominent a standing—had contrived, by some trick which he had acquired, to carry his head so high above the heads of neighbouring parsons. It was this that had taken him among great people, had introduced him to the Duke of Omnium, had procured for him the stall at Barchester. But how was he to carry his head now? What would the Arabins and Grantlys say? How would the bishop sneer at him, and Mrs. Proudie and her daughters tell of him in all their quarters? How would Crawley look at him—Crawley, who had already once had him on the hip? The stern severity of Crawley’s face loomed upon him now. Crawley, with his children half naked, and his wife a drudge, and himself half starved, had never had a bailiff in his house at Hogglestock! And then his own curate, Evans, whom he had patronized, and treated almost as a dependant—how was he to look his curate in the face and arrange with him for the sacred duties of the next Sunday?\n\nHis wife still stood by him, gazing into his face; and as he looked at her and thought of her misery, he could not control his heart with reference to the wrongs which Sowerby had heaped on him. It was Sowerby’s falsehood and Sowerby’s fraud which had brought upon him and his wife this terrible anguish. “If there be justice on earth he will suffer for it yet,” he said at last, not speaking intentionally to his wife, but unable to repress his feelings.\n\n“Do not wish him evil, Mark; you may be sure he has his own sorrows.”\n\n“His own sorrows! No; he is callous to such misery as this. He has become so hardened in dishonesty that all this is mirth to him. If there be punishment in heaven for falsehood—”\n\n“Oh, Mark, do not curse him!”\n\n“How am I to keep myself from cursing when I see what he has brought upon you?”\n\n“‘Vengeance is mine, saith the Lord,’” answered the young wife, not with solemn, preaching accent, as though bent on reproof, but with the softest whisper into his ear. “Leave that to Him, Mark; and for us, let us pray that He may soften the hearts of us all;—of him who has caused us to suffer, and of our own.”\n\nMark was not called upon to reply to this, for he was again disturbed by a servant at the door. It was the cook this time herself, who had come with a message from the men of the law. And she had come, be it remembered, not from any necessity that she as cook should do this line of work; for the footman, or Mrs. Robarts’ maid, might have come as well as she. But when things are out of course servants are always out of course also. As a rule, nothing will induce a butler to go into a stable, or persuade a housemaid to put her hand to a frying-pan. But now that this new excitement had come upon the household—seeing that the bailiffs were in possession, and that the chattels were being entered in a catalogue, everybody was willing to do everything—everything but his or her own work. The gardener was looking after the dear children; the nurse was doing the rooms before the bailiffs should reach them; the groom had gone into the kitchen to get their lunch ready for them; and the cook was walking about with an inkstand, obeying all the orders of these great potentates. As far as the servants were concerned, it may be a question whether the coming of the bailiffs had not hitherto been regarded as a treat.\n\n“If you please, ma’am,” said Jemima cook, “they wishes to know in which room you’d be pleased to have the inmin-tory took fust. ’Cause, ma’am, they wouldn’t disturb you nor master more than can be avoided. For their line of life, ma’am, they is very civil—very civil indeed.”\n\n“I suppose they may go into the drawing-room,” said Mrs. Robarts, in a sad low voice. All nice women are proud of their drawing-rooms, and she was very proud of hers. It had been furnished when money was plenty with them, immediately after their marriage, and everything in it was pretty, good, and dear to her. O ladies, who have drawing-rooms in which the things are pretty, good, and dear to you, think of what it would be to have two bailiffs rummaging among them with pen and inkhorn, making a catalogue preparatory to a sheriff’s auction; and all without fault or extravagance of your own! There were things there that had been given to her by Lady Lufton, by Lady Meredith, and other friends, and the idea did occur to her that it might be possible to save them from contamination; but she would not say a word, lest by so saying she might add to Mark’s misery.\n\n“And then the dining-room,” said Jemima cook, in a tone almost of elation.\n\n“Yes; if they please.”\n\n“And then master’s book-room here; or perhaps the bedrooms, if you and master be still here.”\n\n“Any way they please, cook; it does not much signify,” said Mrs. Robarts. But for some days after that Jemima was by no means a favourite with her.\n\nThe cook was hardly out of the room before a quick footstep was heard on the gravel before the window, and the hall door was immediately opened.\n\n“Where is your master?” said the well-known voice of Lord Lufton; and then in half a minute he also was in the book-room.\n\n“Mark, my dear fellow, what’s all this?” said he, in a cheery tone and with a pleasant face. “Did not you know that I was here? I came down yesterday; landed from Hamburg only yesterday morning. How do you do, Mrs. Robarts? This is a terrible bore, isn’t it?”\n\nRobarts, at the first moment, hardly knew how to speak to his old friend. He was struck dumb by the disgrace of his position; the more so as his misfortune was one which it was partly in the power of Lord Lufton to remedy. He had never yet borrowed money since he had filled a man’s position, but he had had words about money with the young peer, in which he knew that his friend had wronged him; and for this double reason he was now speechless.\n\n“Mr. Sowerby has betrayed him,” said Mrs. Robarts, wiping the tears from her eyes. Hitherto she had said no word against Sowerby, but now it was necessary to defend her husband.\n\n“No doubt about it. I believe he has always betrayed every one who has ever trusted him. I told you what he was, some time since; did I not? But, Mark, why on earth have you let it go so far as this? Would not Forrest help you?”\n\n“Mr. Forrest wanted him to sign more bills, and he would not do that,” said Mrs. Robarts, sobbing.\n\n“Bills are like dram-drinking,” said the discreet young lord: “when one once begins, it is very hard to leave off. Is it true that the men are here now, Mark?”\n\n“Yes, they are in the next room.”\n\n“What, in the drawing-room?”\n\n“They are making out a list of the things,” said Mrs. Robarts.\n\n“We must stop that at any rate,” said his lordship, walking off towards the scene of the operations; and as he left the room Mrs. Robarts followed him, leaving her husband by himself.\n\n“Why did you not send down to my mother?” said he, speaking hardly above a whisper, as they stood together in the hall.\n\n“He would not let me.”\n\n“But why not go yourself? or why not have written to me,—considering how intimate we are?”\n\nMrs. Robarts could not explain to him that the peculiar intimacy between him and Lucy must have hindered her from doing so, even if otherwise it might have been possible; but she felt such was the case.\n\n“Well, my men, this is bad work you’re doing here,” said he, walking into the drawing-room. Whereupon the cook curtseyed low, and the bailiffs, knowing his lordship, stopped from their business and put their hands to their foreheads. “You must stop this, if you please,—at once. Come, let’s go out into the kitchen, or some place outside. I don’t like to see you here with your big boots and the pen and ink among the furniture.”\n\n“We ain’t a-done no harm, my lord, so please your lordship,” said Jemima cook.\n\n“And we is only a-doing our bounden dooties,” said one of the bailiffs.\n\n“As we is sworn to do, so please your lordship,” said the other.\n\n“And is wery sorry to be unconwenient, my lord, to any gen’leman or lady as is a gen’leman or lady. But accidents will happen, and then what can the likes of us do?” said the first.\n\n“Because we is sworn, my lord,” said the second. But, nevertheless, in spite of their oaths, and in spite also of the stern necessity which they pleaded, they ceased their operations at the instance of the peer. For the name of a lord is still great in England.\n\n“And now leave this, and let Mrs. Robarts go into her drawing-room.”\n\n“And, please your lordship, what is we to do? Who is we to look to?”\n\nIn satisfying them absolutely on this point Lord Lufton had to use more than his influence as a peer. It was necessary that he should have pen and paper. But with pen and paper he did satisfy them;—satisfy them so far that they agreed to return to Stubbs’ room, the former hospital, due stipulation having been made for the meals and beer, and there await the order to evacuate the premises which would no doubt, under his lordship’s influence, reach them on the following day. The meaning of all which was that Lord Lufton had undertaken to bear upon his own shoulder the whole debt due by Mr. Robarts.\n\nAnd then he returned to the book-room where Mark was still standing almost on the spot in which he had placed himself immediately after breakfast. Mrs. Robarts did not return, but went up among the children to counter-order such directions as she had given for the preparation of the nursery for the Philistines. “Mark,” he said, “do not trouble yourself about this more than you can help. The men have ceased doing anything, and they shall leave the place to-morrow morning.”\n\n“And how will the money—be paid?” said the poor clergyman.\n\n“Do not bother yourself about that at present. It shall so be managed that the burden shall fall ultimately on yourself—not on any one else. But I am sure it must be a comfort to you to know that your wife need not be driven out of her drawing-room.”\n\n“But, Lufton, I cannot allow you—after what has passed—and at the present moment—”\n\n“My dear fellow, I know all about it, and I am coming to that just now. You have employed Curling, and he shall settle it; and upon my word, Mark, you shall pay the bill. But, for the present emergency, the money is at my banker’s.”\n\n“But, Lufton—”\n\n“And to deal honestly, about Curling’s bill I mean, it ought to be as much my affair as your own. It was I that brought you into this mess with Sowerby, and I know now how unjust about it I was to you up in London. But the truth is that Sowerby’s treachery had nearly driven me wild. It has done the same to you since, I have no doubt.”\n\n“He has ruined me,” said Robarts.\n\n“No, he has not done that. No thanks to him though; he would not have scrupled to do it had it come in his way. The fact is, Mark, that you and I cannot conceive the depth of fraud in such a man as that. He is always looking for money; I believe that in all his hours of most friendly intercourse,—when he is sitting with you over your wine, and riding beside you in the field,—he is still thinking how he can make use of you to tide him over some difficulty. He has lived in that way till he has a pleasure in cheating, and has become so clever in his line of life that if you or I were with him again to-morrow he would again get the better of us. He is a man that must be absolutely avoided; I, at any rate, have learned to know so much.”\n\nIn the expression of which opinion Lord Lufton was too hard upon poor Sowerby; as indeed we are all apt to be too hard in forming an opinion upon the rogues of the world. That Mr. Sowerby had been a rogue, I cannot deny. It is roguish to lie, and he had been a great liar. It is roguish to make promises which the promiser knows he cannot perform, and such had been Mr. Sowerby’s daily practice. It is roguish to live on other men’s money, and Mr. Sowerby had long been doing so. It is roguish, at least so I would hold it, to deal willingly with rogues; and Mr. Sowerby had been constant in such dealings. I do not know whether he had not at times fallen even into more palpable roguery than is proved by such practices as those enumerated. Though I have for him some tender feeling, knowing that there was still a touch of gentle bearing round his heart, an abiding taste for better things within him, I cannot acquit him from the great accusation. But, for all that, in spite of his acknowledged roguery, Lord Lufton was too hard upon him in his judgment. There was yet within him the means of repentance, could a locus penitentiæ have been supplied to him. He grieved bitterly over his own ill doings, and knew well what changes gentlehood would have demanded from him. Whether or no he had gone too far for all changes—whether the locus penitentiæ was for him still a possibility—that was between him and a higher power.\n\n“I have no one to blame but myself,” said Mark, still speaking in the same heart-broken tone and with his face averted from his friend.\n\nThe debt would now be paid, and the bailiffs would be expelled; but that would not set him right before the world. It would be known to all men—to all clergymen in the diocese—that the sheriff’s officers had been in charge of Framley Parsonage, and he could never again hold up his head in the close of Barchester.\n\n“My dear fellow, if we were all to make ourselves miserable for such a trifle as this—” said Lord Lufton, putting his arm affectionately on his friend’s shoulder.\n\n“But we are not all clergymen,” said Mark, and as he spoke he turned away to the window and Lord Lufton knew that the tears were on his cheek.\n\nNothing was then said between them for some moments, after which Lord Lufton again spoke,—\n\n“Mark, my dear fellow!”\n\n“Well,” said Mark, with his face still turned towards the window.\n\n“You must remember one thing; in helping you over this stile, which will be really a matter of no inconvenience to me, I have a better right than that even of an old friend; I look upon you now as my brother-in-law.”\n\nMark turned slowly round, plainly showing the tears upon his face.\n\n“Do you mean,” said he, “that anything more has taken place?”\n\n“I mean to make your sister my wife; she sent me word by you to say that she loved me, and I am not going to stand upon any nonsense after that. If she and I are both willing no one alive has a right to stand between us; and, by heavens, no one shall. I will do nothing secretly, so I tell you that, exactly as I have told her ladyship.”\n\n“But what does she say?”\n\n“She says nothing; but it cannot go on like that. My mother and I cannot live here together if she opposes me in this way. I do not want to frighten your sister by going over to her at Hogglestock, but I expect you to tell her so much as I now tell you, as coming from me; otherwise she will think that I have forgotten her.”\n\n“She will not think that.”\n\n“She need not; good-bye, old fellow. I’ll make it all right between you and her ladyship about this affair of Sowerby’s.”\n\nAnd then he took his leave and walked off to settle about the payment of the money.\n\n“Mother,” said he to Lady Lufton that evening, “you must not bring this affair of the bailiffs up against Robarts. It has been more my fault than his.”\n\nHitherto not a word had been spoken between Lady Lufton and her son on the subject. She had heard with terrible dismay of what had happened, and had heard also that Lord Lufton had immediately gone to the parsonage. It was impossible, therefore, that she should now interfere. That the necessary money would be forthcoming she was aware, but that would not wipe out the terrible disgrace attached to an execution in a clergyman’s house. And then, too, he was her clergyman,—her own clergyman, selected, and appointed, and brought to Framley by herself, endowed with a wife of her own choosing, filled with good things by her own hand! It was a terrible misadventure, and she began to repent that she had ever heard the name of Robarts. She would not, however, have been slow to put forth the hand to lessen the evil by giving her own money, had this been either necessary or possible. But how could she interfere between Robarts and her son, especially when she remembered the proposed connection between Lucy and Lord Lufton?\n\n“Your fault, Ludovic?”\n\n“Yes, mother. It was I who introduced him to Mr. Sowerby; and, to tell the truth, I do not think he would ever have been intimate with Sowerby if I had not given him some sort of a commission with reference to money matters then pending between Mr. Sowerby and me. They are all over now,—thanks to you, indeed.”\n\n“Mr. Robarts’ character as a clergyman should have kept him from such troubles, if no other feeling did so.”\n\n“At any rate, mother, oblige me by letting it pass by.”\n\n“Oh, I shall say nothing to him.”\n\n“You had better say something to her, or otherwise it will be strange; and even to him I would say a word or two,—a word in kindness, as you so well know how. It will be easier to him in that way, than if you were to be altogether silent.”\n\nNo further conversation took place between them at the time, but later in the evening she brushed her hand across her son’s forehead, sweeping the long silken hairs into their place, as she was wont to do when moved by any special feeling of love. “Ludovic,” she said, “no one, I think, has so good a heart as you. I will do exactly as you would have me about this affair of Mr. Robarts and the money.” And then there was nothing more said about it.\n\n# CHAPTER XLV. PALACE BLESSINGS.\n\nAnd now, at this period, terrible rumours found their way into Barchester, and flew about the cathedral towers and round the cathedral door; ay, and into the canons’ houses and the humbler sitting-rooms of the vicars choral. Whether they made their way from thence up to the bishop’s palace, or whether they descended from the palace to the close, I will not pretend to say. But they were shocking, unnatural, and no doubt grievous to all those excellent ecclesiastical hearts which cluster so thickly in those quarters.\n\nThe first of these had reference to the new prebendary, and to the disgrace which he had brought on the chapter; a disgrace, as some of them boasted, which Barchester had never known before. This, however, like most other boasts, was hardly true; for within but a very few years there had been an execution in the house of a late prebendary, old Dr. Stanhope; and on that occasion the doctor himself had been forced to fly away to Italy, starting in the night, lest he also should fall into the hands of the Philistines, as well as his chairs and tables.\n\n“It is a scandalous shame,” said Mrs. Proudie, speaking not of the old doctor, but of the new offender; “a scandalous shame: and it would only serve him right if the gown were stripped from his back.”\n\n“I suppose his living will be sequestrated,” said a young minor canon who attended much to the ecclesiastical injunctions of the lady of the diocese, and was deservedly held in high favour. If Framley were sequestrated, why should not he, as well as another, undertake the duty—with such stipend as the bishop might award?\n\n“I am told that he is over head and ears in debt,” said the future Mrs. Tickler, “and chiefly for horses which he has bought and not paid for.”\n\n“I see him riding very splendid animals when he comes over for the cathedral duties,” said the minor canon.\n\n“The sheriff’s officers are in the house at present, I am told,” said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“And is not he in jail?” said Mrs. Tickler.\n\n“If not, he ought to be,” said Mrs. Tickler’s mother.\n\n“And no doubt soon will be,” said the minor canon; “for I hear that he is linked up with a most discreditable gang of persons.”\n\nThis was what was said in the palace on that heading; and though, no doubt, more spirit and poetry was displayed there than in the houses of the less gifted clergy, this shows the manner in which the misfortune of Mr. Robarts was generally discussed. Nor, indeed, had he deserved any better treatment at their hands. But his name did not run the gauntlet for the usual nine days; nor, indeed, did his fame endure at its height for more than two. This sudden fall was occasioned by other tidings of a still more distressing nature; by a rumour which so affected Mrs. Proudie that it caused, as she said, her blood to creep. And she was very careful that the blood of others should creep also, if the blood of others was equally sensitive. It was said that Lord Dumbello had jilted Miss Grantly.\n\nFrom what adverse spot in the world these cruel tidings fell upon Barchester I have never been able to discover. We know how quickly rumour flies, making herself common through all the cities. That Mrs. Proudie should have known more of the facts connected with the Hartletop family than any one else in Barchester was not surprising, seeing that she was so much more conversant with the great world in which such people lived. She knew, and was therefore correct enough in declaring, that Lord Dumbello had already jilted one other young lady—the Lady Julia Mac Mull, to whom he had been engaged three seasons back, and that therefore his character in such matters was not to be trusted. That Lady Julia had been a terrible flirt and greatly given to waltzing with a certain German count, with whom she had since gone off—that, I suppose, Mrs. Proudie did not know, much as she was conversant with the great world,—seeing that she said nothing about it to any of her ecclesiastical listeners on the present occasion.\n\n“It will be a terrible warning, Mrs. Quiverful, to us all; a most useful warning to us—not to trust to the things of this world. I fear they made no inquiry about this young nobleman before they agreed that his name should be linked with that of their daughter.” This she said to the wife of the present warden of Hiram’s Hospital, a lady who had received favours from her, and was therefore bound to listen attentively to her voice.\n\n“But I hope it may not be true,” said Mrs. Quiverful, who, in spite of the allegiance due by her to Mrs. Proudie, had reasons of her own for wishing well to the Grantly family.\n\n“I hope so, indeed,” said Mrs. Proudie, with a slight tinge of anger in her voice; “but I fear that there is no doubt. And I must confess that it is no more than we had a right to expect. I hope that it may be taken by all of us as a lesson, and an ensample, and a teaching of the Lord’s mercy. And I wish you would request your husband—from me, Mrs. Quiverful—to dwell on this subject in morning and evening lecture at the hospital on Sabbath next, showing how false is the trust which we put in the good things of this world;” which behest, to a certain extent, Mr. Quiverful did obey, feeling that a quiet life in Barchester was of great value to him; but he did not go so far as to caution his hearers, who consisted of the aged bedesmen of the hospital, against matrimonial projects of an ambitious nature.\n\nIn this case, as in all others of the kind, the report was known to all the chapter before it had been heard by the archdeacon or his wife. The dean heard it, and disregarded it; as did also the dean’s wife—at first; and those who generally sided with the Grantlys in the diocesan battles pooh-poohed the tidings, saying to each other that both the archdeacon and Mrs. Grantly were very well able to take care of their own affairs. But dripping water hollows a stone; and at last it was admitted on all sides that there was ground for fear,—on all sides, except at Plumstead.\n\n“I am sure there is nothing in it; I really am sure of it,” said Mrs. Arabin, whispering to her sister; “but after turning it over in my mind, I thought it right to tell you. And yet I don’t know now but I am wrong.”\n\n“Quite right, dearest Eleanor,” said Mrs. Grantly. “And I am much obliged to you. But we understand it, you know. It comes, of course, like all other Christian blessings, from the palace.” And then there was nothing more said about it between Mrs. Grantly and her sister.\n\nBut on the following morning there arrived a letter by post, addressed to Mrs. Grantly, bearing the postmark of Littlebath. The letter ran:—\n\nIt is known to the writer that Lord Dumbello has arranged with certain friends how he may escape from his present engagement. I think, therefore, that it is my duty as a Christian to warn you of this.\n\nYours truly,\n\nNow it had happened that the embryo Mrs. Tickler’s most intimate bosom friend and confidante was known at Plumstead to live at Littlebath, and it had also happened—most unfortunately—that the embryo Mrs. Tickler, in the warmth of her neighbourly regard, had written a friendly line to her friend Griselda Grantly, congratulating her with all female sincerity on her splendid nuptials with the Lord Dumbello.\n\n“It is not her natural hand,” said Mrs. Grantly, talking the matter over with her husband, “but you may be sure it has come from her. It is a part of the new Christianity which we learn day by day from the palace teaching.”\n\nBut these things had some effect on the archdeacon’s mind. He had learned lately the story of Lady Julia Mac Mull, and was not sure that his son-in-law—as ought to be about to be—had been entirely blameless in that matter. And then in these days Lord Dumbello made no great sign. Immediately on Griselda’s return to Plumstead he had sent her a magnificent present of emeralds, which, however, had come to her direct from the jewellers, and might have been—and probably was—ordered by his man of business. Since that he had neither come, nor sent, nor written. Griselda did not seem to be in any way annoyed by this absence of the usual sign of love, and went on steadily with her great duties. “Nothing,” as she told her mother, “had been said about writing, and, therefore, she did not expect it.” But the archdeacon was not quite at his ease. “Keep Dumbello up to his P’s and Q’s, you know,” a friend of his had whispered to him at his club. By heavens, yes. The archdeacon was not a man to bear with indifference a wrong in such a quarter. In spite of his clerical profession, few men were more inclined to fight against personal wrongs—and few men more able.\n\n“Can there be anything wrong, I wonder?” said he to his wife. “Is it worth while that I should go up to London?” But Mrs. Grantly attributed it all to the palace doctrine. What could be more natural, looking at all the circumstances of the Tickler engagement? She therefore gave her voice against any steps being taken by the archdeacon.\n\nA day or two after that Mrs. Proudie met Mrs. Arabin in the close and condoled with her openly on the termination of the marriage treaty;—quite openly, for Mrs. Tickler—as she was to be—was with her mother, and Mrs. Arabin was accompanied by her sister-in-law, Mary Bold.\n\n“It must be very grievous to Mrs. Grantly, very grievous indeed,” said Mrs. Proudie, “and I sincerely feel for her. But, Mrs. Arabin, all these lessons are sent to us for our eternal welfare.”\n\n“Of course,” said Mrs. Arabin. “But as to this special lesson, I am inclined to doubt that it—”\n\n“Ah-h! I fear it is too true. I fear there is no room for doubt. Of course you are aware that Lord Dumbello is off for the Continent.”\n\nMrs. Arabin was not aware of it, and she was obliged to admit as much.\n\n“He started four days ago, by way of Boulogne,” said Mrs. Tickler, who seemed to be very well up in the whole affair. “I am so sorry for poor dear Griselda. I am told she has got all her things. It is such a pity, you know.”\n\n“But why should not Lord Dumbello come back from the Continent?” said Miss Bold, very quietly.\n\n“Why not, indeed? I’m sure I hope he may,” said Mrs. Proudie. “And no doubt he will, some day. But if he be such a man as they say he is, it is really well for Griselda that she should be relieved from such a marriage. For, after all, Mrs. Arabin, what are the things of this world?—dust beneath our feet, ashes between our teeth, grass cut for the oven, vanity, vexation, and nothing more!”—well pleased with which variety of Christian metaphors Mrs. Proudie walked on, still muttering, however, something about worms and grubs, by which she intended to signify her own species and the Dumbello and Grantly sects of it in particular.\n\nThis now had gone so far that Mrs. Arabin conceived herself bound in duty to see her sister, and it was then settled in consultation at Plumstead that the archdeacon should call officially at the palace and beg that the rumour might be contradicted. This he did early on the next morning and was shown into the bishop’s study, in which he found both his lordship and Mrs. Proudie. The bishop rose to greet him with special civility, smiling his very sweetest on him, as though of all his clergy the archdeacon were the favourite; but Mrs. Proudie wore something of a gloomy aspect, as though she knew that such a visit at such an hour must have reference to some special business. The morning calls made by the archdeacon at the palace in the way of ordinary civility were not numerous.\n\nOn the present occasion he dashed at once into his subject. “I have called this morning, Mrs. Proudie,” said he, “because I wish to ask a favour from you.” Whereupon Mrs. Proudie bowed.\n\n“Mrs. Proudie will be most happy, I am sure,” said the bishop.\n\n“I find that some foolish people have been talking in Barchester about my daughter,” said the archdeacon; “and I wish to ask Mrs. Proudie—”\n\nMost women under such circumstances would have felt the awkwardness of their situation, and would have prepared to eat their past words with wry faces. But not so Mrs. Proudie. Mrs. Grantly had had the imprudence to throw Mr. Slope in her face—there, in her own drawing-room, and she was resolved to be revenged. Mrs. Grantly, too, had ridiculed the Tickler match, and no too great niceness should now prevent Mrs. Proudie from speaking her mind about the Dumbello match.\n\n“A great many people are talking about her, I am sorry to say,” said Mrs. Proudie; “but, poor dear, it is not her fault. It might have happened to any girl; only, perhaps, a little more care—; you’ll excuse me, Dr. Grantly.”\n\n“I have come here to allude to a report which has been spread about in Barchester, that the match between Lord Dumbello and my daughter has been broken off; and—”\n\n“Everybody in Barchester knows it, I believe,” said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n—“and,” continued the archdeacon, “to request that that report may be contradicted.”\n\n“Contradicted! Why, he has gone right away,—out of the country!”\n\n“Never mind where he has gone to, Mrs. Proudie; I beg that the report may be contradicted.”\n\n“You’ll have to go round to every house in Barchester then,” said she.\n\n“By no means,” replied the archdeacon. “And perhaps it may be right that I should explain to the bishop that I came here because—”\n\n“The bishop knows nothing about it,” said Mrs. Proudie.\n\n“Nothing in the world,” said his lordship. “And I am sure I hope that the young lady may not be disappointed.”\n\n—“because the matter was so distinctly mentioned to Mrs. Arabin by yourself yesterday.”\n\n“Distinctly mentioned! Of course it was distinctly mentioned. There are some things which can’t be kept under a bushel, Dr. Grantly; and this seems to be one of them. Your going about in this way won’t make Lord Dumbello marry the young lady.”\n\nThat was true; nor would it make Mrs. Proudie hold her tongue. Perhaps the archdeacon was wrong in his present errand, and so he now began to bethink himself. “At any rate,” said he, “when I tell you that there is no ground whatever for such a report you will do me the kindness to say that, as far as you are concerned, it shall go no further. I think, my lord, I am not asking too much in asking that.”\n\n“The bishop knows nothing about it,” said Mrs. Proudie again.\n\n“Nothing at all,” said the bishop.\n\n“And as I must protest that I believe the information which has reached me on this head,” said Mrs. Proudie, “I do not see how it is possible that I should contradict it. I can easily understand your feelings, Dr. Grantly. Considering your daughter’s position the match was, as regards earthly wealth, a very great one. I do not wonder that you should be grieved at its being broken off; but I trust that this sorrow may eventuate in a blessing to you and to Miss Griselda. These worldly disappointments are precious balms, and I trust you know how to accept them as such.”\n\nThe fact was that Dr. Grantly had done altogether wrong in coming to the palace. His wife might have some chance with Mrs. Proudie, but he had none. Since she had come to Barchester he had had only two or three encounters with her, and in all of these he had gone to the wall. His visits to the palace always resulted in his leaving the presence of the inhabitants in a frame of mind by no means desirable, and he now found that he had to do so once again. He could not compel Mrs. Proudie to say that the report was untrue; nor could he condescend to make counter hits at her about her own daughter, as his wife would have done. And thus, having utterly failed, he got up and took his leave.\n\nBut the worst of the matter was, that, in going home, he could not divest his mind of the idea that there might be some truth in the report. What if Lord Dumbello had gone to the Continent resolved to send back from thence some reason why it was impossible that he should make Miss Grantly his wife? Such things had been done before now by men in his rank. Whether or no Mrs. Tickler had been the letter-writing wellwisher from Littlebath, or had induced her friend to be so, it did seem manifest to him, Dr. Grantly, that Mrs. Proudie absolutely believed the report which she promulgated so diligently. The wish might be father to the thought, no doubt; but that the thought was truly there, Dr. Grantly could not induce himself to disbelieve.\n\nHis wife was less credulous, and to a certain degree comforted him; but that evening he received a letter which greatly confirmed the suspicions set on foot by Mrs. Proudie, and even shook his wife’s faith in Lord Dumbello. It was from a mere acquaintance, who in the ordinary course of things would not have written to him. And the bulk of the letter referred to ordinary things, as to which the gentleman in question would hardly have thought of giving himself the trouble to write a letter. But at the end of the note he said,—\n\n“Of course you are aware that Dumbello is off to Paris; I have not heard whether the exact day of his return is fixed.”\n\n“It is true then,” said the archdeacon, striking the library table with his hand, and becoming absolutely white about the mouth and jaws.\n\n“It cannot be,” said Mrs. Grantly; but even she was now trembling.\n\n“If it be so I’ll drag him back to England by the collar of his coat, and disgrace him before the steps of his father’s hall.”\n\nAnd the archdeacon as he uttered the threat looked his character as an irate British father much better than he did his other character as a clergyman of the Church of England. The archdeacon had been greatly worsted by Mrs. Proudie, but he was a man who knew how to fight his battles among men,—sometimes without too close a regard to his cloth.\n\n“Had Lord Dumbello intended any such thing he would have written, or got some friend to write by this time,” said Mrs. Grantly. “It is quite possible that he might wish to be off, but he would be too chary of his name not to endeavour to do so with decency.”\n\nThus the matter was discussed, and it appeared to them both to be so serious that the archdeacon resolved to go at once to London. That Lord Dumbello had gone to France he did not doubt; but he would find some one in town acquainted with the young man’s intentions, and he would, no doubt, be able to hear when his return was expected. If there were real reason for apprehension he would follow the runagate to the Continent, but he would not do this without absolute knowledge. According to Lord Dumbello’s present engagements he was bound to present himself in August next at Plumstead Episcopi, with the view of then and there taking Griselda Grantly in marriage; but if he kept his word in this respect no one had a right to quarrel with him for going to Paris in the meantime. Most expectant bridegrooms would, no doubt, under such circumstances have declared their intentions to their future brides; but if Lord Dumbello were different from others, who had a right on that account to be indignant with him? He was unlike other men in other things; and especially unlike other men in being the eldest son of the Marquis of Hartletop. It would be all very well for Tickler to proclaim his whereabouts from week to week; but the eldest son of a marquis might find it inconvenient to be so precise! Nevertheless the archdeacon thought it only prudent to go up to London.\n\n“Susan,” said the archdeacon to his wife, just as he was starting;—at this moment neither of them were in the happiest spirits,—“I think I would say a word of caution to Griselda.”\n\n“Do you feel so much doubt about it as that?” said Mrs. Grantly. But even she did not dare to put a direct negative to this proposal, so much had she been moved by what she had heard!\n\n“I think I would do so, not frightening her more than I could help. It will lessen the blow if it be that the blow is to fall.”\n\n“It will kill me,” said Mrs. Grantly; “but I think that she will be able to bear it.”\n\nOn the next morning Mrs. Grantly, with much cunning preparation, went about the task which her husband had left her to perform. It took her long to do, for she was very cunning in the doing of it; but at last it dropped from her in words that there was a possibility—a bare possibility—that some disappointment might even yet be in store for them.\n\n“Do you mean, mamma, that the marriage will be put off?”\n\n“I don’t mean to say that I think it will; God forbid! but it is just possible. I daresay that I am very wrong to tell you of this, but I know that you have sense enough to bear it. Papa has gone to London and we shall hear from him soon.”\n\n“Then, mamma, I had better give them orders not to go on with the marking.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLVI. LADY LUFTON’S REQUEST.\n\nThe bailiffs on that day had their meals regular,—and their beer, which state of things, together with an absence of all duty in the way of making inventories and the like, I take to be the earthly paradise of bailiffs; and on the next morning they walked off with civil speeches and many apologies as to their intrusion. “They was very sorry,” they said, “to have troubled a gen’leman as were a gen’leman, but in their way of business what could they do?” To which one of them added a remark that, “business is business.” This statement I am not prepared to contradict, but I would recommend all men in choosing a profession to avoid any that may require an apology at every turn;—either an apology or else a somewhat violent assertion of right. Each younger male reader may perhaps reply that he has no thought of becoming a sheriff’s officer; but then are there not other cognate lines of life to which perhaps the attention of some such may be attracted?\n\nOn the evening of the day on which they went Mark received a note from Lady Lufton begging him to call early on the following morning, and immediately after breakfast he went across to Framley Court. It may be imagined that he was not in a very happy frame of mind, but he felt the truth of his wife’s remark that the first plunge into cold water was always the worst. Lady Lufton was not a woman who would continually throw his disgrace into his teeth, however terribly cold might be the first words with which she spoke of it. He strove hard as he entered her room to carry his usual look and bearing, and to put out his hand to greet her with his customary freedom, but he knew that he failed. And it may be said that no good man who has broken down in his goodness can carry the disgrace of his fall without some look of shame. When a man is able to do that, he ceases to be in any way good.\n\n“This has been a distressing affair,” said Lady Lufton after her first salutation.\n\n“Yes, indeed,” said he. “It has been very sad for poor Fanny.”\n\n“Well; we must all have our little periods of grief; and it may perhaps be fortunate if none of us have worse than this. She will not complain, herself, I am sure.”\n\n“She complain!”\n\n“No, I am sure she will not. And now all I’ve got to say, Mr. Robarts, is this: I hope you and Lufton have had enough to do with black sheep to last you your lives; for I must protest that your late friend Mr. Sowerby is a black sheep.”\n\nIn no possible way could Lady Lufton have alluded to the matter with greater kindness than in thus joining Mark’s name with that of her son. It took away all the bitterness of the rebuke, and made the subject one on which even he might have spoken without difficulty. But now, seeing that she was so gentle to him, he could not but lean the more hardly on himself.\n\n“I have been very foolish,” said he, “very foolish and very wrong, and very wicked.”\n\n“Very foolish, I believe, Mr. Robarts—to speak frankly and once for all; but, as I also believe, nothing worse. I thought it best for both of us that we should just have one word about it, and now I recommend that the matter be never mentioned between us again.”\n\n“God bless you, Lady Lufton,” he said. “I think no man ever had such a friend as you are.”\n\nShe had been very quiet during the interview, and almost subdued, not speaking with the animation that was usual to her; for this affair with Mr. Robarts was not the only one she had to complete that day, nor, perhaps, the one most difficult of completion. But she cheered up a little under the praise now bestowed on her, for it was the sort of praise she loved best. She did hope, and, perhaps, flatter herself, that she was a good friend.\n\n“You must be good enough, then, to gratify my friendship by coming up to dinner this evening; and Fanny, too, of course. I cannot take any excuse, for the matter is completely arranged. I have a particular reason for wishing it.” These last violent injunctions had been added because Lady Lufton had seen a refusal rising in the parson’s face. Poor Lady Lufton! Her enemies—for even she had enemies—used to declare of her, that an invitation to dinner was the only method of showing itself of which her good-humour was cognizant. But let me ask of her enemies whether it is not as good a method as any other known to be extant? Under such orders as these obedience was of course a necessity, and he promised that he, with his wife, would come across to dinner. And then, when he went away, Lady Lufton ordered her carriage.\n\nDuring these doings at Framley, Lucy Robarts still remained at Hogglestock, nursing Mrs. Crawley. Nothing occurred to take her back to Framley, for the same note from Fanny which gave her the first tidings of the arrival of the Philistines told her also of their departure—and also of the source from whence relief had reached them. “Don’t come, therefore, for that reason,” said the note, “but, nevertheless, do come as quickly as you can, for the whole house is sad without you.”\n\nOn the morning after the receipt of this note Lucy was sitting, as was now usual with her, beside an old arm-chair to which her patient had lately been promoted. The fever had gone, and Mrs. Crawley was slowly regaining her strength—very slowly, and with frequent caution from the Silverbridge doctor that any attempt at being well too fast might again precipitate her into an abyss of illness and domestic inefficiency.\n\n“I really think I can get about to-morrow,” said she; “and then, dear Lucy, I need not keep you longer from your home.”\n\n“You are in a great hurry to get rid of me, I think. I suppose Mr. Crawley has been complaining again about the cream in his tea.” Mr. Crawley had on one occasion stated his assured conviction that surreptitious daily supplies were being brought into the house, because he had detected the presence of cream instead of milk in his own cup. As, however, the cream had been going for sundry days before this, Miss Robarts had not thought much of his ingenuity in making the discovery.\n\n“Ah, you do not know how he speaks of you when your back is turned.”\n\n“And how does he speak of me? I know you would not have the courage to tell me the whole.”\n\n“No, I have not; for you would think it absurd coming from one who looks like him. He says that if he were to write a poem about womanhood, he would make you the heroine.”\n\n“With a cream-jug in my hand, or else sewing buttons on to a shirt-collar. But he never forgave me about the mutton broth. He told me, in so many words, that I was a—storyteller. And for the matter of that, my dear, so I was.”\n\n“He told me that you were an angel.”\n\n“Goodness gracious!”\n\n“A ministering angel. And so you have been. I can almost feel it in my heart to be glad that I have been ill, seeing that I have had you for my friend.”\n\n“But you might have had that good fortune without the fever.”\n\n“No, I should not. In my married life I have made no friends till my illness brought you to me; nor should I ever really have known you but for that. How should I get to know any one?”\n\n“You will now, Mrs. Crawley; will you not? Promise that you will. You will come to us at Framley when you are well? You have promised already, you know.”\n\n“You made me do so when I was too weak to refuse.”\n\n“And I shall make you keep your promise too. He shall come, also, if he likes; but you shall come whether he likes or no. And I won’t hear a word about your old dresses. Old dresses will wear as well at Framley as at Hogglestock.”\n\nFrom all which it will appear that Mrs. Crawley and Lucy Robarts had become very intimate during this period of the nursing; as two women always will, or, at least should do, when shut up for weeks together in the same sick room.\n\nThe conversation was still going on between them when the sound of wheels was heard upon the road. It was no highway that passed before the house, and carriages of any sort were not frequent there.\n\n“It is Fanny, I am sure,” said Lucy, rising from her chair.\n\n“There are two horses,” said Mrs. Crawley, distinguishing the noise with the accurate sense of hearing which is always attached to sickness; “and it is not the noise of the pony-carriage.”\n\n“It is a regular carriage,” said Lucy, speaking from the window, “and stopping here. It is somebody from Framley Court, for I know the servant.”\n\nAs she spoke a blush came to her forehead. Might it not be Lord Lufton, she thought to herself,—forgetting at the moment that Lord Lufton did not go about the country in a close chariot with a fat footman. Intimate as she had become with Mrs. Crawley she had said nothing to her new friend on the subject of her love affair.\n\nThe carriage stopped and down came the footman, but nobody spoke to him from the inside.\n\n“He has probably brought something from Framley,” said Lucy, having cream and such like matters in her mind; for cream and such like matters had come from Framley Court more than once during her sojourn there. “And the carriage, probably, happened to be coming this way.”\n\nBut the mystery soon elucidated itself partially, or, perhaps, became more mysterious in another way. The red-armed little girl who had been taken away by her frightened mother in the first burst of the fever had now returned to her place, and at the present moment entered the room, with awestruck face, declaring that Miss Robarts was to go at once to the big lady in the carriage.\n\n“I suppose it’s Lady Lufton,” said Mrs. Crawley.\n\nLucy’s heart was so absolutely in her mouth that any kind of speech was at the moment impossible to her. Why should Lady Lufton have come thither to Hogglestock, and why should she want to see her, Lucy Robarts, in the carriage? Had not everything between them been settled? And yet—! Lucy, in the moment for thought that was allowed to her, could not determine what might be the probable upshot of such an interview. Her chief feeling was a desire to postpone it for the present instant. But the red-armed little girl would not allow that.\n\n“You are to come at once,” said she.\n\nAnd then Lucy, without having spoken a word, got up and left the room. She walked downstairs, along the little passage, and out through the small garden, with firm steps, but hardly knowing whither she went, or why. Her presence of mind and self-possession had all deserted her. She knew that she was unable to speak as she should do; she felt that she would have to regret her present behaviour, but yet she could not help herself. Why should Lady Lufton have come to her there? She went on, and the big footman stood with the carriage door open. She stepped up almost unconsciously, and, without knowing how she got there, she found herself seated by Lady Lufton.\n\nTo tell the truth her ladyship also was a little at a loss to know how she was to carry through her present plan of operations. The duty of beginning, however, was clearly with her, and therefore, having taken Lucy by the hand, she spoke.\n\n“Miss Robarts,” she said, “my son has come home. I don’t know whether you are aware of it.”\n\nShe spoke with a low, gentle voice, not quite like herself, but Lucy was much too confused to notice this.\n\n“I was not aware of it,” said Lucy.\n\nShe had, however, been so informed in Fanny’s letter, but all that had gone out of her head.\n\n“Yes; he has come back. He has been in Norway, you know,—fishing.”\n\n“Yes,” said Lucy.\n\n“I am sure you will remember all that took place when you came to me, not long ago, in my little room upstairs at Framley Court.”\n\nIn answer to which, Lucy, quivering in every nerve, and wrongly thinking that she was visibly shaking in every limb, timidly answered that she did remember. Why was it that she had then been so bold, and now was so poor a coward?\n\n“Well, my dear, all that I said to you then I said to you thinking that it was for the best. You, at any rate, will not be angry with me for loving my own son better than I love any one else.”\n\n“Oh, no,” said Lucy.\n\n“He is the best of sons, and the best of men, and I am sure that he will be the best of husbands.”\n\nLucy had an idea, by instinct, however, rather than by sight, that Lady Lufton’s eyes were full of tears as she spoke. As for herself she was altogether blinded and did not dare to lift her face or to turn her head. As for the utterance of any sound, that was quite out of the question.\n\n“And now I have come here, Lucy, to ask you to be his wife.”\n\nShe was quite sure that she heard the words. They came plainly to her ears, leaving on her brain their proper sense, but yet she could not move or make any sign that she had understood them. It seemed as though it would be ungenerous in her to take advantage of such conduct and to accept an offer made with so much self-sacrifice. She had not time at the first moment to think even of his happiness, let alone her own, but she thought only of the magnitude of the concession which had been made to her. When she had constituted Lady Lufton the arbiter of her destiny she had regarded the question of her love as decided against herself. She had found herself unable to endure the position of being Lady Lufton’s daughter-in-law while Lady Lufton would be scorning her, and therefore she had given up the game. She had given up the game, sacrificing herself, and, as far as it might be a sacrifice, sacrificing him also. She had been resolute to stand to her word in this respect, but she had never allowed herself to think it possible that Lady Lufton should comply with the conditions which she, Lucy, had laid upon her. And yet such was the case, as she so plainly heard. “And now I have come here, Lucy, to ask you to be his wife.”\n\nHow long they sat together silent, I cannot say; counted by minutes the time would not probably have amounted to many, but to each of them the duration seemed considerable. Lady Lufton, while she was speaking, had contrived to get hold of Lucy’s hand, and she sat, still holding it, trying to look into Lucy’s face,—which, however, she could hardly see, so much was it turned away. Neither, indeed, were Lady Lufton’s eyes perfectly dry. No answer came to her question, and therefore, after a while, it was necessary that she should speak again.\n\n“Must I go back to him, Lucy, and tell him that there is some other objection—something besides a stern old mother; some hindrance, perhaps, not so easily overcome?”\n\n“No,” said Lucy, and it was all which at the moment she could say.\n\n“What shall I tell him, then? Shall I say yes—simply yes?”\n\n“Simply yes,” said Lucy.\n\n“And as to the stern old mother who thought her only son too precious to be parted with at the first word—is nothing to be said to her?”\n\n“Oh, Lady Lufton!”\n\n“No forgiveness to be spoken, no sign of affection to be given? Is she always to be regarded as stern and cross, vexatious and disagreeable?”\n\nLucy slowly turned round her head and looked up into her companion’s face. Though she had as yet no voice to speak of affection she could fill her eyes with love, and in that way make to her future mother all the promises that were needed.\n\n“Lucy, dearest Lucy, you must be very dear to me now.” And then they were in each other’s arms, kissing each other.\n\nLady Lufton now desired her coachman to drive up and down for some little space along the road while she completed her necessary conversation with Lucy. She wanted at first to carry her back to Framley that evening, promising to send her again to Mrs. Crawley on the following morning—“till some permanent arrangement could be made,” by which Lady Lufton intended the substitution of a regular nurse for her future daughter-in-law, seeing that Lucy Robarts was now invested in her eyes with attributes which made it unbecoming that she should sit in attendance at Mrs. Crawley’s bedside. But Lucy would not go back to Framley on that evening; no, nor on the next morning. She would be so glad if Fanny would come to her there, and then she would arrange about going home.\n\n“But, Lucy, dear, what am I to say to Ludovic? Perhaps you would feel it awkward if he were to come to see you here.”\n\n“Oh, yes, Lady Lufton; pray tell him not to do that.”\n\n“And is that all that I am to tell him?”\n\n“Tell him—tell him—He won’t want you to tell him anything;—only I should like to be quiet for a day, Lady Lufton.”\n\n“Well, dearest, you shall be quiet; the day after to-morrow then.—Mind we must not spare you any longer, because it will be right that you should be at home now. He would think it very hard if you were to be so near, and he was not to be allowed to look at you. And there will be some one else who will want to see you. I shall want to have you very near to me, for I shall be wretched, Lucy, if I cannot teach you to love me.” In answer to which Lucy did find voice enough to make sundry promises.\n\nAnd then she was put out of the carriage at the little wicket gate, and Lady Lufton was driven back to Framley. I wonder whether the servant when he held the door for Miss Robarts was conscious that he was waiting on his future mistress. I fancy that he was, for these sort of people always know everything, and the peculiar courtesy of his demeanour as he let down the carriage steps was very observable.\n\nLucy felt almost beside herself as she returned upstairs, not knowing what to do, or how to look, and with what words to speak. It behoved her to go at once to Mrs. Crawley’s room, and yet she longed to be alone. She knew that she was quite unable either to conceal her thoughts or express them; nor did she wish at the present moment to talk to any one about her happiness,—seeing that she could not at the present moment talk to Fanny Robarts. She went, however, without delay into Mrs. Crawley’s room, and with that little eager way of speaking quickly which is so common with people who know that they are confused, said that she feared she had been a very long time away.\n\n“And was it Lady Lufton?”\n\n“Yes; it was Lady Lufton.”\n\n“Why, Lucy; I did not know that you and her ladyship were such friends.”\n\n“She had something particular she wanted to say,” said Lucy, avoiding the question, and avoiding also Mrs. Crawley’s eyes; and then she sate down in her usual chair.\n\n“It was nothing unpleasant, I hope.”\n\n“No, nothing at all unpleasant; nothing of that kind.—Oh, Mrs. Crawley, I’ll tell you some other time, but pray do not ask me now.” And then she got up and escaped, for it was absolutely necessary that she should be alone.\n\nWhen she reached her own room—that in which the children usually slept—she made a great effort to compose herself, but not altogether successfully. She got out her paper and blotting-book intending, as she said to herself, to write to Fanny, knowing, however, that the letter when written would be destroyed; but she was not able even to form a word. Her hand was unsteady and her eyes were dim and her thoughts were incapable of being fixed. She could only sit, and think, and wonder, and hope; occasionally wiping the tears from her eyes, and asking herself why her present frame of mind was so painful to her? During the last two or three months she had felt no fear of Lord Lufton, had always carried herself before him on equal terms, and had been signally capable of doing so when he made his declaration to her at the parsonage; but now she looked forward with an undefined dread to the first moment in which she should see him.\n\nAnd then she thought of a certain evening she had passed at Framley Court, and acknowledged to herself that there was some pleasure in looking back to that. Griselda Grantly had been there, and all the constitutional powers of the two families had been at work to render easy a process of love-making between her and Lord Lufton. Lucy had seen and understood it all, without knowing that she understood it, and had, in a certain degree, suffered from beholding it. She had placed herself apart, not complaining—painfully conscious of some inferiority, but, at the same time, almost boasting to herself that in her own way she was the superior. And then he had come behind her chair, whispering to her, speaking to her his first words of kindness and good-nature, and she had resolved that she would be his friend—his friend, even though Griselda Grantly might be his wife. What those resolutions were worth had soon become manifest to her. She had soon confessed to herself the result of that friendship, and had determined to bear her punishment with courage. But now—\n\nShe sate so for about an hour, and would fain have so sat out the day. But as this could not be, she got up, and having washed her face and eyes returned to Mrs. Crawley’s room. There she found Mr. Crawley also, to her great joy, for she knew that while he was there no questions would be asked of her. He was always very gentle to her, treating her with an old-fashioned polished respect—except when compelled on that one occasion by his sense of duty to accuse her of mendacity respecting the purveying of victuals—, but he had never become absolutely familiar with her as his wife had done; and it was well for her now that he had not done so, for she could not have talked about Lady Lufton.\n\nIn the evening, when the three were present, she did manage to say that she expected Mrs. Robarts would come over on the following day.\n\n“We shall part with you, Miss Robarts, with the deepest regret,” said Mr. Crawley; “but we would not on any account keep you longer. Mrs. Crawley can do without you now. What she would have done, had you not come to us, I am at a loss to think.”\n\n“I did not say that I should go,” said Lucy.\n\n“But you will,” said Mrs. Crawley. “Yes, dear, you will. I know that it is proper now that you should return. Nay, but we will not have you any longer. And the poor dear children, too,—they may return. How am I to thank Mrs. Robarts for what she has done for us?”\n\nIt was settled that if Mrs. Robarts came on the following day Lucy should go back with her; and then, during the long watches of the night—for on this last night Lucy would not leave the bed-side of her new friend till long after the dawn had broken—she did tell Mrs. Crawley what was to be her destiny in life. To herself there seemed nothing strange in her new position; but to Mrs. Crawley it was wonderful that she—she, poor as she was—should have an embryo peeress at her bedside, handing her her cup to drink, and smoothing her pillow that she might be at rest. It was strange, and she could hardly maintain her accustomed familiarity. Lucy felt this, at the moment.\n\n“It must make no difference, you know,” said she, eagerly; “none at all, between you and me. Promise me that it shall make no difference.”\n\nThe promise was, of course, exacted; but it was not possible that such a promise should be kept.\n\nVery early on the following morning—so early that it woke her while still in her first sleep—there came a letter for her from the parsonage. Mrs. Robarts had written it, after her return home from Lady Lufton’s dinner.\n\nThe letter said:—\n\nHow am I to congratulate you, and be eager enough in wishing you joy? I do wish you joy, and am so very happy. I write now chiefly to say that I shall be over with you about twelve to-morrow, and that I must bring you away with me. If I did not some one else, by no means so trustworthy, would insist on doing it.\n\nBut this, though it was thus stated to be the chief part of the letter, and though it might be so in matter, was by no means so in space. It was very long, for Mrs. Robarts had sat writing it till past midnight.\n\nI will not say anything about him [she went on to say, after two pages had been filled with his name], but I must tell you how beautifully she has behaved. You will own that she is a dear woman; will you not?\n\nLucy had already owned it many times since the visit of yesterday, and had declared to herself, as she has continued to declare ever since, that she had never doubted it.\n\nShe took us by surprise when we got into the drawing-room before dinner, and she told us first of all that she had been to see you at Hogglestock. Lord Lufton, of course, could not keep the secret, but brought it out instantly. I can’t tell you now how he told it all, but I am sure you will believe that he did it in the best possible manner. He took my hand and pressed it half a dozen times, and I thought he was going to do something else; but he did not, so you need not be jealous. And she was so nice to Mark, saying such things in praise of you, and paying all manner of compliments to your father. But Lord Lufton scolded her immensely for not bringing you. He said it was lackadaisical and nonsensical; but I could see how much he loved her for what she had done; and she could see it too, for I know her ways, and know that she was delighted with him. She could not keep her eyes off him all the evening, and certainly I never did see him look so well.\n\nAnd then while Lord Lufton and Mark were in the dining-room, where they remained a terribly long time, she would make me go through the house that she might show me your rooms, and explain how you were to be mistress there. She has got it all arranged to perfection, and I am sure she has been thinking about it for years. Her great fear at present is that you and he should go and live at Lufton. If you have any gratitude in you, either to her or me, you will not let him do this. I consoled her by saying that there are not two stones upon one another at Lufton as yet; and I believe such is the case. Besides, everybody says that it is the ugliest spot in the world. She went on to declare, with tears in her eyes, that if you were content to remain at Framley, she would never interfere in anything. I do think that she is the best woman that ever lived.\n\nSo much as I have given of this letter formed but a small portion of it, but it comprises all that it is necessary that we should know. Exactly at twelve o’clock on that day Puck the pony appeared, with Mrs. Robarts and Grace Crawley behind him, Grace having been brought back as being capable of some service in the house. Nothing that was confidential, and very little that was loving, could be said at the moment, because Mr. Crawley was there, waiting to bid Miss Robarts adieu; and he had not as yet been informed of what was to be the future fate of his visitor. So they could only press each other’s hands and embrace, which to Lucy was almost a relief; for even to her sister-in-law she hardly as yet knew how to speak openly on this subject.\n\n“May God Almighty bless you, Miss Robarts,” said Mr. Crawley, as he stood in his dingy sitting-room ready to lead her out to the pony-carriage. “You have brought sunshine into this house, even in the time of sickness, when there was no sunshine; and He will bless you. You have been the Good Samaritan, binding up the wounds of the afflicted, pouring in oil and balm. To the mother of my children you have given life, and to me you have brought light, and comfort, and good words,—making my spirit glad within me as it had not been gladdened before. All this hath come of charity, which vaunteth not itself and is not puffed up. Faith and hope are great and beautiful, but charity exceedeth them all.” And having so spoken, instead of leading her out, he went away and hid himself.\n\nHow Puck behaved himself as Fanny drove him back to Framley, and how those two ladies in the carriage behaved themselves—of that, perhaps, nothing further need be said.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII. NEMESIS.\n\nBut in spite of all these joyful tidings it must, alas! be remembered that Pœna, that just but Rhadamanthine goddess, whom we moderns ordinarily call Punishment, or Nemesis when we wish to speak of her goddess-ship, very seldom fails to catch a wicked man though she have sometimes a lame foot of her own, and though the wicked man may possibly get a start of her. In this instance the wicked man had been our unfortunate friend Mark Robarts; wicked in that he had wittingly touched pitch, gone to Gatherum Castle, ridden fast mares across the country to Cobbold’s Ashes, and fallen very imprudently among the Tozers; and the instrument used by Nemesis was Mr. Tom Towers of the Jupiter, than whom, in these our days, there is no deadlier scourge in the hands of that goddess.\n\nIn the first instance, however, I must mention, though I will not relate, a little conversation which took place between Lady Lufton and Mr. Robarts. That gentleman thought it right to say a few words more to her ladyship respecting those money transactions. He could not but feel, he said, that he had received that prebendal stall from the hands of Mr. Sowerby; and under such circumstances, considering all that had happened, he could not be easy in his mind as long as he held it. What he was about to do would, he was aware, delay considerably his final settlement with Lord Lufton; but Lufton, he hoped, would pardon that, and agree with him as to the propriety of what he was about to do.\n\nOn the first blush of the thing Lady Lufton did not quite go along with him. Now that Lord Lufton was to marry the parson’s sister it might be well that the parson should be a dignitary of the Church; and it might be well, also, that one so nearly connected with her son should be comfortable in his money matters. There loomed also, in the future, some distant possibility of higher clerical honours for a peer’s brother-in-law; and the top rung of the ladder is always more easily attained when a man has already ascended a step or two. But, nevertheless, when the matter came to be fully explained to her, when she saw clearly the circumstances under which the stall had been conferred, she did agree that it had better be given up.\n\nAnd well for both of them it was—well for them all at Framley—that this conclusion had been reached before the scourge of Nemesis had fallen. Nemesis, of course, declared that her scourge had produced the resignation; but it was generally understood that this was a false boast, for all clerical men at Barchester knew that the stall had been restored to the chapter, or, in other words, into the hands of the Government, before Tom Towers had twirled the fatal lash above his head. But the manner of the twirling was as follows:—\n\nIt is with difficulty enough [said the article in the Jupiter], that the Church of England maintains at the present moment that ascendancy among the religious sects of this country which it so loudly claims. And perhaps it is rather from an old-fashioned and time-honoured affection for its standing than from any intrinsic merits of its own that some such general acknowledgment of its ascendancy is still allowed to prevail. If, however, the patrons and clerical members of this Church are bold enough to disregard all general rules of decent behaviour, we think we may predict that this chivalrous feeling will be found to give way. From time to time we hear of instances of such imprudence, and are made to wonder at the folly of those who are supposed to hold the State Church in the greatest reverence.\n\nAmong those positions of dignified ease to which fortunate clergymen may be promoted are the stalls of the canons or prebendaries in our cathedrals. Some of these, as is well known, carry little or no emolument with them, but some are rich in the good things of this world. Excellent family houses are attached to them, with we hardly know what domestic privileges, and clerical incomes, moreover, of an amount which, if divided, would make glad the hearts of many a hard-working clerical slave. Reform has been busy even among these stalls, attaching some amount of work to the pay, and paring off some superfluous wealth from such of them as were over full; but reform has been lenient with them, acknowledging that it was well to have some such places of comfortable and dignified retirement for those who have worn themselves out in the hard work of their profession. There has of late prevailed a taste for the appointment of young bishops, produced no doubt by a feeling that bishops should be men fitted to get through really hard work; but we have never heard that young prebendaries were considered desirable. A clergyman selected for such a position should, we have always thought, have earned an evening of ease by a long day of work, and should, above all things, be one whose life has been, and therefore in human probability will be, so decorous as to be honourable to the cathedral of his adoption.\n\nWe were, however, the other day given to understand that one of these luxurious benefices, belonging to the cathedral of Barchester, had been bestowed on the Rev. Mark Robarts, the vicar of a neighbouring parish, on the understanding that he should hold the living and the stall together; and on making further inquiry we were surprised to learn that this fortunate gentleman is as yet considerably under thirty years of age. We were desirous, however, of believing that his learning, his piety, and his conduct, might be of a nature to add peculiar grace to his chapter, and therefore, though almost unwillingly, we were silent. But now it has come to our ears, and, indeed, to the ears of all the world, that this piety and conduct are sadly wanting; and judging of Mr. Robarts by his life and associates, we are inclined to doubt even the learning. He has at this moment, or at any rate had but a few days since, an execution in his parsonage house at Framley, on the suit of certain most disreputable bill discounters in London; and probably would have another execution in his other house in Barchester close, but for the fact that he has never thought it necessary to go into residence.\n\nThen followed some very stringent, and, no doubt, much-needed advice to those clerical members of the Church of England who are supposed to be mainly responsible for the conduct of their brethren; and the article ended as follows:—\n\nMany of these stalls are in the gift of the respective deans and chapters, and in such cases the dean and chapters are bound to see that proper persons are appointed; but in other instances the power of selection is vested in the Crown, and then an equal responsibility rests on the government of the day. Mr. Robarts, we learn, was appointed to the stall in Barchester by the late Prime Minister, and we really think that a grave censure rests on him for the manner in which his patronage has been exercised. It may be impossible that he should himself in all such cases satisfy himself by personal inquiry. But our government is altogether conducted on the footing of vicarial responsibility. Quod facit per alium, facit per se, is in a special manner true of our ministers, and any man who rises to high position among them must abide by the danger thereby incurred. In this peculiar case we are informed that the recommendation was made by a very recently admitted member of the Cabinet, to whose appointment we alluded at the time as a great mistake. The gentleman in question held no high individual office of his own; but evil such as this which has now been done at Barchester, is exactly the sort of mischief which follows the exaltation of unfit men to high positions, even though no great scope for executive failure may be placed within their reach.\n\nIf Mr. Robarts will allow us to tender to him our advice, he will lose no time in going through such ceremony as may be necessary again to place the stall at the disposal of the Crown!\n\nI may here observe that poor Harold Smith, when he read this, writhing in agony, declared it to be the handiwork of his hated enemy, Mr. Supplehouse. He knew the mark; so, at least, he said; but I myself am inclined to believe that his animosity misled him. I think that one greater than Mr. Supplehouse had taken upon himself the punishment of our poor vicar.\n\nThis was very dreadful to them all at Framley, and, when first read, seemed to crush them to atoms. Poor Mrs. Robarts, when she heard it, seemed to think that for them the world was over. An attempt had been made to keep it from her, but such attempts always fail, as did this. The article was copied into all the good-natured local newspapers, and she soon discovered that something was being hidden. At last it was shown to her by her husband, and then for a few hours she was annihilated; for a few days she was unwilling to show herself; and for a few weeks she was very sad. But after that the world seemed to go on much as it had done before; the sun shone upon them as warmly as though the article had not been written; and not only the sun of heaven, which, as a rule, is not limited in his shining by any display of pagan thunder, but also the genial sun of their own sphere, the warmth and light of which were so essentially necessary to their happiness. Neighbouring rectors did not look glum, nor did the rectors’ wives refuse to call. The people in the shops at Barchester did not regard her as though she were a disgraced woman, though it must be acknowledged that Mrs. Proudie passed her in the close with the coldest nod of recognition.\n\nOn Mrs. Proudie’s mind alone did the article seem to have any enduring effect. In one respect it was, perhaps, beneficial; Lady Lufton was at once induced by it to make common cause with her own clergyman, and thus the remembrance of Mr. Robarts’ sins passed away the quicker from the minds of the whole Framley Court household.\n\nAnd, indeed, the county at large was not able to give to the matter that undivided attention which would have been considered its due at periods of no more than ordinary interest. At the present moment preparations were being made for a general election, and although no contest was to take place in the eastern division, a very violent fight was being carried on in the west; and the circumstances of that fight were so exciting that Mr. Robarts and his article were forgotten before their time. An edict had gone forth from Gatherum Castle directing that Mr. Sowerby should be turned out, and an answering note of defiance had been sounded from Chaldicotes, protesting on behalf of Mr. Sowerby, that the duke’s behest would not be obeyed.\n\nThere are two classes of persons in this realm who are constitutionally inefficient to take any part in returning members to Parliament—peers, namely, and women; and yet it was soon known through the whole length and breadth of the county that the present electioneering fight was being carried on between a peer and a woman. Miss Dunstable had been declared the purchaser of the Chace of Chaldicotes, as it were, just in the very nick of time; which purchase—so men in Barsetshire declared, not knowing anything of the facts—would have gone altogether the other way, had not the giants obtained temporary supremacy over the gods. The duke was a supporter of the gods, and therefore, so Mr. Fothergill hinted, his money had been refused. Miss Dunstable was prepared to beard this ducal friend of the gods in his own county, and therefore her money had been taken. I am inclined, however, to think that Mr. Fothergill knew nothing about it, and to opine that Miss Dunstable, in her eagerness for victory, offered to the Crown more money than the property was worth in the duke’s opinion, and that the Crown took advantage of her anxiety, to the manifest profit of the public at large.\n\nAnd it soon became known also that Miss Dunstable was, in fact, the proprietor of the whole Chaldicotes estate, and that in promoting the success of Mr. Sowerby as a candidate for the county, she was standing by her own tenant. It also became known, in the course of the battle, that Miss Dunstable had herself at last succumbed, and that she was about to marry Dr. Thorne of Greshamsbury, or the “Greshamsbury apothecary,” as the adverse party now delighted to call him. “He has been little better than a quack all his life,” said Dr. Fillgrave, the eminent physician of Barchester, “and now he is going to marry a quack’s daughter.” By which, and the like to which, Dr. Thorne did not allow himself to be much annoyed.\n\nBut all this gave rise to a very pretty series of squibs arranged between Mr. Fothergill and Mr. Closerstill, the electioneering agent. Mr. Sowerby was named “the lady’s pet,” and descriptions were given of the lady who kept this pet, which were by no means flattering to Miss Dunstable’s appearance, or manners, or age. And then the western division of the county was asked in a grave tone—as counties and boroughs are asked by means of advertisements stuck up on blind walls and barn doors—whether it was fitting and proper that it should be represented by a woman. Upon which the county was again asked whether it was fitting and proper that it should be represented by a duke. And then the question became more personal as against Miss Dunstable, and inquiry was urged whether the county would not be indelibly disgraced if it were not only handed over to a woman, but handed over to a woman who sold the oil of Lebanon. But little was got by this move, for an answering placard explained to the unfortunate county how deep would be its shame if it allowed itself to become the appanage of any peer, but more especially of a peer who was known to be the most immoral lord that ever disgraced the benches of the upper house.\n\nAnd so the battle went on very prettily, and, as money was allowed to flow freely, the West Barsetshire world at large was not ill satisfied. It is wonderful how much disgrace of that kind a borough or county can endure without flinching; and wonderful, also, seeing how supreme is the value attached to the constitution by the realm at large, how very little the principles of that constitution are valued by the people in detail. The duke, of course, did not show himself. He rarely did on any occasion, and never on such occasions as this; but Mr. Fothergill was to be seen everywhere. Miss Dunstable, also, did not hide her light under a bushel; though I here declare, on the faith of an historian, that the rumour spread abroad of her having made a speech to the electors from the top of the porch over the hotel-door at Courcy was not founded on fact. No doubt she was at Courcy, and her carriage stopped at the hotel; but neither there nor elsewhere did she make any public exhibition. “They must have mistaken me for Mrs. Proudie,” she said, when the rumour reached her ears.\n\nBut there was, alas! one great element of failure on Miss Dunstable’s side of the battle. Mr. Sowerby himself could not be induced to fight it as became a man. Any positive injunctions that were laid upon him he did, in a sort, obey. It had been a part of the bargain that he should stand the contest, and from that bargain he could not well go back; but he had not the spirit left to him for any true fighting on his own part. He could not go up on the hustings, and there defy the duke. Early in the affair Mr. Fothergill challenged him to do so, and Mr. Sowerby never took up the gauntlet.\n\n“We have heard,” said Mr. Fothergill, in that great speech which he made at the Omnium Arms at Silverbridge—“we have heard much during this election of the Duke of Omnium, and of the injuries which he is supposed to have inflicted on one of the candidates. The duke’s name is very frequent in the mouths of the gentlemen,—and of the lady,—who support Mr. Sowerby’s claims. But I do not think that Mr. Sowerby himself has dared to say much about the duke. I defy Mr. Sowerby to mention the duke’s name upon the hustings.”\n\nAnd it so happened that Mr. Sowerby never did mention the duke’s name.\n\nIt is ill fighting when the spirit is gone, and Mr. Sowerby’s spirit for such things was now well nigh broken. It is true that he had escaped from the net in which the duke, by Mr. Fothergill’s aid, had entangled him; but he had only broken out of one captivity into another. Money is a serious thing; and when gone cannot be had back by a shuffle in the game, or a fortunate blow with the battledore, as may political power, or reputation, or fashion. One hundred thousand pounds gone, must remain as gone, let the person who claims to have had the honour of advancing it be Mrs. B. or my Lord C. No lucky dodge can erase such a claim from the things that be—unless, indeed, such dodge be possible as Mr. Sowerby tried with Miss Dunstable. It was better for him, undoubtedly, to have the lady for a creditor than the duke, seeing that it was possible for him to live as a tenant in his own old house under the lady’s reign. But this he found to be a sad enough life, after all that was come and gone.\n\nThe election on Miss Dunstable’s part was lost. She carried on the contest nobly, fighting it to the last moment, and sparing neither her own money nor that of her antagonist; but she carried it on unsuccessfully. Many gentlemen did support Mr. Sowerby because they were willing enough to emancipate their county from the duke’s thraldom; but Mr. Sowerby was felt to be a black sheep, as Lady Lufton had called him, and at the close of the election he found himself banished from the representation of West Barsetshire;—banished for ever, after having held the county for five-and-twenty years.\n\nUnfortunate Mr. Sowerby! I cannot take leave of him here without some feeling of regret, knowing that there was that within him which might, under better guidance, have produced better things. There are men, even of high birth, who seem as though they were born to be rogues; but Mr. Sowerby was, to my thinking, born to be a gentleman. That he had not been a gentleman—that he had bolted from his appointed course, going terribly on the wrong side of the posts—let us all acknowledge. It is not a gentlemanlike deed, but a very blackguard action, to obtain a friend’s acceptance to a bill in an unguarded hour of social intercourse. That and other similar doings have stamped his character too plainly. But, nevertheless, I claim a tear for Mr. Sowerby, and lament that he has failed to run his race discreetly, in accordance with the rules of the Jockey Club.\n\nHe attempted that plan of living as a tenant in his old house at Chaldicotes and of making a living out of the land which he farmed; but he soon abandoned it. He had no aptitude for such industry, and could not endure his altered position in the county. He soon relinquished Chaldicotes of his own accord, and has vanished away, as such men do vanish—not altogether without necessary income; to which point in the final arrangement of their joint affairs, Mrs. Thorne’s man of business—if I may be allowed so far to anticipate—paid special attention.\n\nAnd thus Lord Dumbello, the duke’s nominee, got in, as the duke’s nominee had done for very many years past. There was no Nemesis here—none as yet. Nevertheless, she with the lame foot will assuredly catch him, the duke, if it be that he deserve to be caught. With us his grace’s appearance has been so unfrequent that I think we may omit to make any further inquiry as to his concerns.\n\nOne point, however, is worthy of notice, as showing the good sense with which we manage our affairs here in England. In an early portion of this story the reader was introduced to the interior of Gatherum Castle, and there saw Miss Dunstable entertained by the duke in the most friendly manner. Since those days the lady has become the duke’s neighbour, and has waged a war with him, which he probably felt to be very vexatious. But, nevertheless, on the next great occasion at Gatherum Castle, Doctor and Mrs. Thorne were among the visitors, and to no one was the duke more personally courteous than to his opulent neighbour, the late Miss Dunstable.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII. HOW THEY WERE ALL MARRIED, HAD TWO CHILDREN, AND LIVED HAPPY EVER AFTER.\n\nDear, affectionate, sympathetic readers, we have four couple of sighing lovers with whom to deal in this our last chapter, and I, as leader of the chorus, disdain to press you further with doubts as to the happiness of any of that quadrille. They were all made happy, in spite of that little episode which so lately took place at Barchester; and in telling of their happiness—shortly, as is now necessary—we will take them chronologically, giving precedence to those who first appeared at the hymeneal altar.\n\nIn July, then, at the cathedral, by the father of the bride, assisted by his examining chaplain, Olivia Proudie, the eldest daughter of the Bishop of Barchester, was joined in marriage to the Rev. Tobias Tickler, incumbent of the Trinity district church in Bethnal Green. Of the bridegroom, in this instance, our acquaintance has been so short, that it is not, perhaps, necessary to say much. When coming to the wedding he proposed to bring his three darling children with him; but in this measure he was, I think prudently, stopped by advice, rather strongly worded, from his future valued mother-in-law. Mr. Tickler was not an opulent man, nor had he hitherto attained any great fame in his profession; but, at the age of forty-three he still had sufficient opportunity before him, and now that his merit has been properly viewed by high ecclesiastical eyes the refreshing dew of deserved promotion will no doubt fall upon him. The marriage was very smart, and Olivia carried herself through the trying ordeal with an excellent propriety of conduct.\n\nUp to that time, and even for a few days longer, there was doubt at Barchester as to that strange journey which Lord Dumbello undoubtedly did take to France. When a man so circumstanced will suddenly go to Paris, without notice given even to his future bride, people must doubt; and grave were the apprehensions expressed on this occasion by Mrs. Proudie, even at her child’s wedding-breakfast. “God bless you, my dear children,” she said, standing up at the head of her table as she addressed Mr. Tickler and his wife; “when I see your perfect happiness—perfect, that is, as far as human happiness can be made perfect in this vale of tears—and think of the terrible calamity which has fallen on our unfortunate neighbours, I cannot but acknowledge His infinite mercy and goodness. The Lord giveth, and the Lord taketh away.” By which she intended, no doubt, to signify that whereas Mr. Tickler had been given to her Olivia, Lord Dumbello had been taken away from the archdeacon’s Griselda. The happy couple then went in Mrs. Proudie’s carriage to the nearest railway station but one, and from thence proceeded to Malvern, and there spent the honeymoon.\n\nAnd a great comfort it was, I am sure, to Mrs. Proudie when authenticated tidings reached Barchester that Lord Dumbello had returned from Paris, and that the Hartletop-Grantly alliance was to be carried to its completion. She still, however, held her opinion—whether correctly or not, who shall say?—that the young lord had intended to escape. “The archdeacon has shown great firmness in the way in which he has done it,” said Mrs. Proudie; “but whether he has consulted his child’s best interests in forcing her into a marriage with an unwilling husband, I for one must take leave to doubt. But then, unfortunately, we all know how completely the archdeacon is devoted to worldly matters.”\n\nIn this instance the archdeacon’s devotion to worldly matters was rewarded by that success which he no doubt desired. He did go up to London, and did see one or two of Lord Dumbello’s friends. This he did, not obtrusively, as though in fear of any falsehood or vacillation on the part of the viscount, but with that discretion and tact for which he has been so long noted. Mrs. Proudie declares that during the few days of his absence from Barsetshire he himself crossed to France and hunted down Lord Dumbello at Paris. As to this I am not prepared to say anything; but I am quite sure, as will be all those who knew the archdeacon, that he was not a man to see his daughter wronged as long as any measure remained by which such wrong might be avoided.\n\nBut, be that as it may—that mooted question as to the archdeacon’s journey to Paris—Lord Dumbello was forthcoming at Plumstead on the 5th of August, and went through his work like a man. The Hartletop family, when the alliance was found to be unavoidable, endeavoured to arrange that the wedding should be held at Hartletop Priory, in order that the clerical dust and dinginess of Barchester Close might not soil the splendour of the marriage gala doings; for, to tell the truth, the Hartletopians, as a rule, were not proud of their new clerical connections. But on this subject Mrs. Grantly was very properly inexorable; nor, when an attempt was made on the bride to induce her to throw over her mamma at the last moment and pronounce for herself that she would be married at the priory, was it attended with any success. The Hartletopians knew nothing of the Grantly fibre and calibre, or they would have made no such attempt. The marriage took place at Plumstead, and on the morning of the day Lord Dumbello posted over from Barchester to the rectory. The ceremony was performed by the archdeacon, without assistance, although the dean, and the precentor, and two other clergymen, were at the ceremony. Griselda’s propriety of conduct was quite equal to that of Olivia Proudie; indeed, nothing could exceed the statuesque grace and fine aristocratic bearing with which she carried herself on the occasion. The three or four words which the service required of her she said with ease and dignity; there was neither sobbing nor crying to disturb the work or embarrass her friends, and she signed her name in the church books as “Griselda Grantly” without a tremor—and without a regret.\n\nMrs. Grantly kissed her and blessed her in the hall as she was about to step forward to her travelling carriage, leaning on her father’s arm, and the child put up her face to her mother for a last whisper. “Mamma,” she said, “I suppose Jane can put her hand at once on the moire antique when we reach Dover?” Mrs. Grantly smiled and nodded, and again blessed her child. There was not a tear shed—at least, not then—nor a sign of sorrow to cloud for a moment the gay splendour of the day. But the mother did bethink herself, in the solitude of her own room, of those last words, and did acknowledge a lack of something for which her heart had sighed. She had boasted to her sister that she had nothing to regret as to her daughter’s education; but now, when she was alone after her success, did she feel that she could still support herself with that boast? For, be it known, Mrs. Grantly had a heart within her bosom and a faith within her heart. The world, it is true, had pressed upon her sorely with all its weight of accumulated clerical wealth, but it had not utterly crushed her—not her, but only her child. For the sins of the father, are they not visited on the third and fourth generation?\n\nBut if any such feeling of remorse did for awhile mar the fulness of Mrs. Grantly’s joy, it was soon dispelled by the perfect success of her daughter’s married life. At the end of the autumn the bride and bridegroom returned from their tour, and it was evident to all the circle at Hartletop Priory that Lord Dumbello was by no means dissatisfied with his bargain. His wife had been admired everywhere to the top of his bent. All the world at Ems, and at Baden, and at Nice, had been stricken by the stately beauty of the young viscountess. And then, too, her manner, style, and high dignity of demeanour altogether supported the reverential feeling which her grace and form at first inspired. She never derogated from her husband’s honour by the fictitious liveliness of gossip, or allowed any one to forget the peeress in the woman. Lord Dumbello soon found that his reputation for discretion was quite safe in her hands, and that there were no lessons as to conduct in which it was necessary that he should give instruction.\n\nBefore the winter was over she had equally won the hearts of all the circle at Hartletop Priory. The duke was there and declared to the marchioness that Dumbello could not possibly have done better. “Indeed, I do not think he could,” said the happy mother. “She sees all that she ought to see, and nothing that she ought not.”\n\nAnd then, in London, when the season came, all men sang all manner of praises in her favour, and Lord Dumbello was made aware that he was reckoned among the wisest of his age. He had married a wife who managed everything for him, who never troubled him, whom no woman disliked, and whom every man admired. As for feast of reason and for flow of soul, is it not a question whether any such flows and feasts are necessary between a man and his wife? How many men can truly assert that they ever enjoy connubial flows of soul, or that connubial feasts of reason are in their nature enjoyable? But a handsome woman at the head of your table, who knows how to dress, and how to sit, and how to get in and out of her carriage—who will not disgrace her lord by her ignorance, or fret him by her coquetry, or disparage him by her talent—how beautiful a thing it is! For my own part I think that Griselda Grantly was born to be the wife of a great English peer.\n\n“After all, then,” said Miss Dunstable, speaking of Lady Dumbello—she was Mrs. Thorne at this time—“after all, there is some truth in what our quaint latter-day philosopher tells us—‘Great are thy powers, O Silence!’”\n\nThe marriage of our old friends Dr. Thorne and Miss Dunstable was the third on the list, but that did not take place till the latter end of September. The lawyers on such an occasion had no inconsiderable work to accomplish, and though the lady was not coy, nor the gentleman slow, it was not found practicable to arrange an earlier wedding. The ceremony was performed at St. George’s, Hanover Square, and was not brilliant in any special degree. London at the time was empty, and the few persons whose presence was actually necessary were imported from the country for the occasion. The bride was given away by Dr. Easyman, and the two bridesmaids were ladies who had lived with Miss Dunstable as companions. Young Mr. Gresham and his wife were there, as was also Mrs. Harold Smith, who was not at all prepared to drop her old friend in her new sphere of life.\n\n“We shall call her Mrs. Thorne instead of Miss Dunstable, and I really think that that will be all the difference,” said Mrs. Harold Smith.\n\nTo Mrs. Harold Smith that probably was all the difference, but it was not so to the persons most concerned.\n\nAccording to the plan of life arranged between the doctor and his wife she was still to keep up her house in London, remaining there during such period of the season as she might choose, and receiving him when it might appear good to him to visit her; but he was to be the master in the country. A mansion at the Chace was to be built, and till such time as that was completed, they would keep on the old house at Greshamsbury. Into this, small as it was, Mrs. Thorne,—in spite of her great wealth,—did not disdain to enter. But subsequent circumstances changed their plans. It was found that Mr. Sowerby could not or would not live at Chaldicotes; and, therefore, in the second year of their marriage, that place was prepared for them. They are now well known to the whole county as Dr. and Mrs. Thorne of Chaldicotes,—of Chaldicotes, in distinction to the well-known Thornes of Ullathorne in the eastern division. Here they live respected by their neighbours, and on terms of alliance both with the Duke of Omnium and with Lady Lufton. “Of course those dear old avenues will be very sad to me,” said Mrs. Harold Smith, when at the end of a London season she was invited down to Chaldicotes; and as she spoke she put her handkerchief up to her eyes.\n\n“Well, dear, what can I do?” said Mrs. Thorne. “I can’t cut them down; the doctor would not let me.”\n\n“Oh, no,” said Mrs. Harold Smith, sighing; and in spite of her feelings she did visit Chaldicotes.\n\nBut it was October before Lord Lufton was made a happy man;—that is, if the fruition of his happiness was a greater joy than the anticipation of it. I will not say that the happiness of marriage is like the Dead Sea fruit,—an apple which, when eaten, turns to bitter ashes in the mouth. Such pretended sarcasm would be very false. Nevertheless, is it not the fact that the sweetest morsel of love’s feast has been eaten, that the freshest, fairest blush of the flower has been snatched and has passed away, when the ceremony at the altar has been performed, and legal possession has been given? There is an aroma of love, an undefinable delicacy of flavour, which escapes and is gone before the church portal is left, vanishing with the maiden name, and incompatible with the solid comfort appertaining to the rank of wife. To love one’s own spouse, and to be loved by her, is the ordinary lot of man, and is a duty exacted under penalties. But to be allowed to love youth and beauty that is not one’s own—to know that one is loved by a soft being who still hangs cowering from the eye of the world as though her love were all but illicit—can it be that a man is made happy when a state of anticipation such as this is brought to a close? No; when the husband walks back from the altar, he has already swallowed the choicest dainties of his banquet. The beef and pudding of married life are then in store for him;—or perhaps only the bread and cheese. Let him take care lest hardly a crust remain,—or perhaps not a crust.\n\nBut before we finish, let us go back for one moment to the dainties,—to the time before the beef and pudding were served,—while Lucy was still at the parsonage, and Lord Lufton still staying at Framley Court. He had come up one morning, as was now frequently his wont, and, after a few minutes’ conversation, Mrs. Robarts had left the room,—as not unfrequently on such occasions was her wont. Lucy was working and continued her work, and Lord Lufton for a moment or two sat looking at her; then he got up abruptly, and standing before her, thus questioned her:—\n\n“Lucy,” said he.\n\n“Well, what of Lucy now? Any particular fault this morning?”\n\n“Yes, a most particular fault. When I asked you, here, in this room, on this very spot, whether it was possible that you should love me—why did you say that it was impossible?”\n\nLucy, instead of answering at the moment, looked down upon the carpet, to see if his memory were as good as hers. Yes; he was standing on the exact spot where he had stood before. No spot in all the world was more frequently clear before her own eyes.\n\n“Do you remember that day, Lucy?” he said again.\n\n“Yes, I remember it,” she said.\n\n“Why did you say it was impossible?”\n\n“Did I say impossible?”\n\nShe knew that she had said so. She remembered how she had waited till he had gone, and that then, going to her own room, she had reproached herself with the cowardice of the falsehood. She had lied to him then; and now—how was she punished for it?\n\n“Well, I suppose it was possible,” she said.\n\n“But why did you say so when you knew it would make me so miserable?”\n\n“Miserable! nay, but you went away happy enough! I thought I had never seen you look better satisfied.”\n\n“Lucy!”\n\n“You had done your duty and had had such a lucky escape! What astonishes me is that you should have ever come back again. But the pitcher may go to the well once too often, Lord Lufton.”\n\n“But will you tell me the truth now?”\n\n“What truth?”\n\n“That day, when I came to you,—did you love me at all then?”\n\n“We’ll let bygones be bygones, if you please.”\n\n“But I swear you shall tell me. It was such a cruel thing to answer me as you did, unless you meant it. And yet you never saw me again till after my mother had been over for you to Mrs. Crawley’s.”\n\n“It was absence that made me—care for you.”\n\n“Lucy, I swear I believe you loved me then.”\n\n“Ludovic, some conjuror must have told you that.”\n\nShe was standing as she spoke, and, laughing at him, she held up her hands and shook her head. But she was now in his power, and he had his revenge,—his revenge for her past falsehood and her present joke. How could he be more happy when he was made happy by having her all his own, than he was now?\n\nAnd in these days there again came up that petition as to her riding—with very different result now than on that former occasion. There were ever so many objections, then. There was no habit, and Lucy was—or said that she was—afraid; and then, what would Lady Lufton say? But now Lady Lufton thought it would be quite right; only were they quite sure about the horse? Was Ludovic certain that the horse had been ridden by a lady? And Lady Meredith’s habits were dragged out as a matter of course, and one of them chipped and snipped and altered, without any compunction. And as for fear, there could be no bolder horsewoman than Lucy Robarts. It was quite clear to all Framley that riding was the very thing for her. “But I never shall be happy, Ludovic, till you have got a horse properly suited for her,” said Lady Lufton.\n\nAnd then, also, came the affair of her wedding garments, of her trousseau,—as to which I cannot boast that she showed capacity or steadiness at all equal to that of Lady Dumbello. Lady Lufton, however, thought it a very serious matter; and as, in her opinion, Mrs. Robarts did not go about it with sufficient energy, she took the matter mainly into her own hands, striking Lucy dumb by her frowns and nods, deciding on everything herself, down to the very tags of the boot-ties.\n\n“My dear, you really must allow me to know what I am about;” and Lady Lufton patted her on the arm as she spoke. “I did it all for Justinia, and she never had reason to regret a single thing that I bought. If you’ll ask her, she’ll tell you so.”\n\nLucy did not ask her future sister-in-law, seeing that she had no doubt whatever as to her future mother-in-law’s judgment on the articles in question. Only the money! And what could she want with six dozen pocket-handkerchiefs all at once? There was no question of Lord Lufton’s going out as governor-general to India! But twelve dozen pocket-handkerchiefs had not been too many for Griselda’s imagination.\n\nAnd Lucy would sit alone in the drawing-room at Framley Court, filling her heart with thoughts of that evening when she had first sat there. She had then resolved, painfully, with inward tears, with groanings of her spirit, that she was wrongly placed in being in that company. Griselda Grantly had been there, quite at her ease, petted by Lady Lufton, admired by Lord Lufton; while she had retired out of sight, sore at heart, because she felt herself to be no fit companion to those around her. Then he had come to her, making matters almost worse by talking to her, bringing the tears into her eyes by his good-nature, but still wounding her by the feeling that she could not speak to him at her ease.\n\nBut things were at a different pass with her now. He had chosen her—her out of all the world, and brought her there to share with him his own home, his own honours, and all that he had to give. She was the apple of his eye, and the pride of his heart. And the stern mother, of whom she had stood so much in awe, who at first had passed her by as a thing not to be noticed, and had then sent out to her that she might be warned to keep herself aloof, now hardly knew in what way she might sufficiently show her love, regard, and solicitude.\n\nI must not say that Lucy was not proud in these moments—that her heart was not elated at these thoughts. Success does beget pride, as failure begets shame. But her pride was of that sort which is in no way disgraceful to either man or woman, and was accompanied by pure true love, and a full resolution to do her duty in that state of life to which it had pleased her God to call her. She did rejoice greatly to think that she had been chosen, and not Griselda. Was it possible that having loved she should not so rejoice, or that, rejoicing, she should not be proud of her love?\n\nThey spent the whole winter abroad, leaving the dowager Lady Lufton to her plans and preparations for their reception at Framley Court; and in the following spring they appeared in London, and there set up their staff. Lucy had some inner tremblings of the spirit, and quiverings about the heart, at thus beginning her duty before the great world, but she said little or nothing to her husband on the matter. Other women had done as much before her time, and by courage had gone through with it. It would be dreadful enough, that position in her own house with lords and ladies bowing to her, and stiff members of Parliament for whom it would be necessary to make small talk; but, nevertheless, it was to be endured. The time came, and she did endure it. The time came, and before the first six weeks were over she found that it was easy enough. The lords and ladies got into their proper places and talked to her about ordinary matters in a way that made no effort necessary, and the members of Parliament were hardly more stiff than the clergymen she had known in the neighbourhood of Framley.\n\nShe had not been long in town before she met Lady Dumbello. At this interview also she had to overcome some little inward emotion. On the few occasions on which she had met Griselda Grantly at Framley they had not much progressed in friendship, and Lucy had felt that she had been despised by the rich beauty. She also in her turn had disliked, if she had not despised, her rival. But how would it be now? Lady Dumbello could hardly despise her, and yet it did not seem possible that they should meet as friends. They did meet, and Lucy came forward with a pretty eagerness to give her hand to Lady Lufton’s late favourite. Lady Dumbello smiled slightly—the same old smile which had come across her face when they two had been first introduced in the Framley drawing-room; the same smile without the variation of a line,—took the offered hand, muttered a word or two, and then receded. It was exactly as she had done before. She had never despised Lucy Robarts. She had accorded to the parson’s sister the amount of cordiality with which she usually received her acquaintance; and now she could do no more for the peer’s wife. Lady Dumbello and Lady Lufton have known each other ever since, and have occasionally visited at each other’s houses, but the intimacy between them has never gone beyond this.\n\nThe dowager came up to town for about a month, and while there was contented to fill a second place. She had no desire to be the great lady in London. But then came the trying period when they commenced their life together at Framley Court. The elder lady formally renounced her place at the top of the table,—formally persisted in renouncing it though Lucy with tears implored her to resume it. She said also, with equal formality—repeating her determination over and over again to Mrs. Robarts with great energy—that she would in no respect detract by interference of her own from the authority of the proper mistress of the house; but, nevertheless, it is well known to every one at Framley that old Lady Lufton still reigns paramount in the parish.\n\n“Yes, my dear; the big room looking into the little garden to the south was always the nursery; and if you ask my advice, it will still remain so. But, of course, any room you please—”\n\nAnd the big room looking into the little garden to the south is still the nursery at Framley Court.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-2860"]="# 제1장. 「옴네스 옴니아 보나 디케레」\n\n젊은 마크 로바츠가 대학을 떠날 무렵, 그의 아버지는 세상 모든 사람이 자기에게 온갖 좋은 말을 하기 시작했다고, 그토록 훌륭한 성품을 타고난 아들을 두었으니 복이 많다고 저마다 칭송한다고, 얼마든지 그렇게 말할 만했다.\n\n이 아버지는 엑서터에 사는 의사였다. 그는 사유 재산이라고는 없는 신사였으나 벌이가 좋은 개업을 누리고 있었고, 그 덕에 이 나라에서 돈이 줄 수 있는 온갖 이점을 갖추어 가족을 부양하고 교육시킬 수 있었다. 마크는 그의 맏아들이자 둘째 아이였다. 그리고 이 이야기의 첫 한두 쪽은, 우연과 처신이 함께 이 젊은이의 머리 위에 쌓아 올린 좋은 것들의 목록을 적는 데 써야 하겠다.\n\n그가 인생에서 앞으로 내디딘 첫걸음은, 아직 아주 어릴 때 아버지의 오래고 가까운 벗이던 어느 성직자의 집으로 사숙 학생으로 보내진 데서 비롯되었다. 이 성직자에게는 다른 학생이 딱 한 명, 오직 한 명만 더 있었다. 어린 러프턴 경이었다. 그리고 두 소년 사이에는 긴밀한 동맹이 싹텄다.\n\n두 아이가 함께 그 집에 있는 동안 레이디 러프턴이 아들을 보러 왔고, 그러고는 어린 로바츠에게 다음 방학을 프램리 코트에서 보내라고 청했다. 이 방문은 실행되었다. 그리고 그 끝에 마크는 미망인이 된 그 귀족 부인의 칭찬으로 가득한 편지를 들고 엑서터로 돌아갔다. 아들에게 그런 동무가 생겨 무척 기뻤다고 그녀는 썼고, 두 소년이 학업을 마칠 때까지 줄곧 함께 지낼 수 있기를 바란다는 희망을 밝혔다. 로바츠 선생은 귀족 남녀의 입김을 대단히 중히 여기는 사람이었고, 그런 우정에서 제 자식에게 돌아올 수 있는 이점이라면 무엇 하나 내던질 생각이 결코 없었다. 그러므로 어린 경이 해로로 보내지자 마크 로바츠도 그곳으로 갔다.\n\n그 경과 그의 벗이 자주 다투고 이따금 주먹질까지 했다는 것, 심지어 석 달 동안 서로 한마디도 하지 않은 시기가 있었다는 사실조차, 의사의 희망을 조금도 방해하지 않았다. 마크는 몇 번이고 다시 프램리 코트에서 보름씩 머물렀고, 레이디 러프턴은 언제나 그를 두고 가장 좋은 말로 편지를 썼다.\n\n그러고 나서 두 소년은 함께 옥스퍼드로 갔고, 여기서도 마크의 좋은 운은 그를 따라다녔는데, 그 운이란 학업에서 놀라운 성취를 이룬 데 있다기보다 지극히 존경스러운 방식으로 생활했다는 데 있었다. 가족은 그를 자랑스러워했고, 의사는 늘 환자들에게 그의 이야기를 할 준비가 되어 있었다. 그가 수상자여서, 메달이며 장학금을 받아서가 아니라, 전반적인 처신이 훌륭해서였다. 그는 가장 좋은 무리와 어울렸다. 빚을 지지 않았다. 사교를 좋아하면서도 저열한 교제는 피할 줄 알았다. 포도주 한 잔을 즐겼으나 취한 모습을 보인 적이 없었다. 그리고 무엇보다도 그는 대학에서 가장 인기 있는 사람 가운데 하나였다.\n\n그다음에는 이 젊은 히페리온의 직업 문제가 나왔다. 그리고 이 문제를 두고 로바츠 선생은 몸소 프램리 코트로 건너와 레이디 러프턴과 상의하자는 초청을 받았다. 로바츠 선생은 성직이 아들에게 가장 알맞은 직업이라는 아주 강한 확신을 안고 돌아왔다.\n\n레이디 러프턴이 로바츠 선생을 엑서터에서 그 먼 길을 불러들인 것은 아무 뜻 없이 한 일이 아니었다. 프램리의 성직록은 러프턴 가문의 증여권에 속해 있었고, 다음 추천권은 어린 경이 스물다섯 살이 되기 전에 자리가 비면 레이디 러프턴의 손에 있고, 그 뒤에 비면 어린 경의 손에 있었다. 그러나 어머니와 상속자는 로바츠 선생에게 공동으로 약속해 주기로 뜻을 모았다. 그런데 현직자는 일흔이 넘었고 성직록은 연 900파운드의 값이 나갔으니, 성직이라는 직업이 얼마나 유리한지는 의심할 여지가 없었다.\n\n그리고 나는 한마디 더 해 두어야 하겠다. 아버지가 아들의 직업을 그렇게 골라 줄 때 정당할 수 있는 한에서, 또 성직록을 쥔 속인이 그런 약속을 할 때 정당할 수 있는 한에서, 그 미망인과 의사는 그 젊은이의 생활과 원칙으로 자기들의 선택을 정당화할 수 있었다. 레이디 러프턴에게 둘째 아들이 있었다면 그 둘째 아들이 아마 그 성직록을 받았을 것이고, 아무도 그것을 잘못이라 여기지 않았을 것이다. 적어도 그 둘째 아들이 마크 로바츠 같은 사람이었다면 분명히 그랬을 것이다.\n\n레이디 러프턴 자신은 종교 문제를 많이 생각하는 여자였고, 단지 아들의 벗이라는 이유만으로 누구를 성직록에 앉힐 마음은 결코 없었을 것이다. 그녀의 성향은 고교회파였고, 젊은 마크 로바츠의 성향도 같은 방향으로 흐른다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그녀는 아들이 자기 교구의 성직자를 벗으로 삼기를 몹시 바랐고, 이렇게 하면 적어도 그것만은 확실히 해 둘 수 있었다. 교구 사제가 자기와 온전히 협력할 수 있는 사람이기를 원했고, 어쩌면 자기도 모르는 채, 그가 어느 정도는 자기 영향 아래 있어 주었으면 하고 바랐다. 나이 든 사람을 앉히면 아마 같은 정도로는 그렇게 되지 않을 것이고, 아들이 추천권을 쥐게 되면 아마 아예 그렇게 되지 않을 것이었다. 그러므로 성직록은 젊은 로바츠에게 주기로 결정되었다.\n\n그는 학위를 받았다. 무슨 빛나는 성적은 아니었으나 아버지가 바라던 그대로였다. 그러고 나서 여덟 달인가 열 달 동안 러프턴 경과 대학 교원 한 사람과 함께 여행했고, 집에 돌아오자 거의 곧바로 성직에 서품되었다.\n\n프램리의 성직록은 바체스터 교구에 속한다. 그리고 마크가 그 교구를 두고 어떤 희망을 품고 있었는지를 보면, 그 안에서 부목사 자리를 얻는 일은 조금도 어렵지 않았다. 그러나 이 부목사 자리를 그는 오래 채우도록 허락받지 못했다. 그 자리에 앉은 지 열두 달을 넘기지 않았을 때, 당시 프램리의 교구 사제였던 불쌍한 늙은 스톱퍼드 박사가 조상들에게로 돌아갔고, 그 풍성한 희망의 온전한 열매가 그의 어깨 위에 떨어졌다.\n\n그러나 우리 이야기의 실제 사건에 이르기 전에 그의 좋은 운에 대해 아직 더 말해 두어야 할 것이 있다. 앞서 말했듯 성직에 관한 문제를 많이 생각한 레이디 러프턴은, 고교회파 원칙을 성직자의 독신을 주장할 만큼 멀리까지 밀고 나가지는 않았다. 오히려 그녀는 아내 없이는 좋은 교구 목사가 될 수 없다는 생각을 갖고 있었다. 그래서 자기가 아끼는 사람에게 세상에서의 지위와 신사의 필요를 채울 만한 수입을 준 다음, 그녀는 그 축복을 함께 나눌 짝을 찾아 주는 일에 나섰다.\n\n그리고 여기서도 다른 일에서와 마찬가지로 그는 후원자의 뜻과 맞아떨어졌다. 다만 성직록 일이 꺼내졌을 때처럼 그렇게 드러내 놓고 그에게 말해진 것은 아니었다. 레이디 러프턴은 그런 식으로 하기에는 여자의 재간을 너무나 높이 타고난 사람이었다. 몬셀 양이 마님의 결혼한 딸을 따라 프램리 코트에 온 것은 다름 아니라 그, 마크가 그녀에게 반하게 하려는 것이라고, 그녀는 젊은 교구 사제에게 한 번도 말하지 않았다. 그러나 사실은 그러했다.\n\n레이디 러프턴에게는 아이가 둘뿐이었다. 맏이인 딸은 사오 년 전에 조지 메러디스 경과 결혼했고, 이 몬셀 양은 그 딸의 소중한 벗이었다. 그리고 이제 소설가의 큰 난관이 내 앞에 어른거린다. 몬셀 양을, 아니 차라리 마크 로바츠 부인을 묘사해야 한다. 몬셀 양으로서라면 우리 이야기가 그녀를 길게 다룰 일이 없을 것이다. 그러나 그래도 우리는 그녀를 패니 몬셀이라 부르겠다. 한 남자의 앞날의 가정을 함께 꾸리고 그 마음의 주인이 될 사람으로서 그에게 가까이 데려올 수 있는 가장 유쾌한 벗 가운데 하나였다고 말할 때 말이다. 그리고 모질지 않은 높은 원칙, 약하지 않은 여자다운 부드러움, 악의 없이 웃기를 좋아하는 마음, 그리고 참으로 사랑하는 심장이 한 여자에게 목사의 아내가 될 자격을 준다면, 패니 몬셀은 그 자리를 채울 자격을 갖추고 있었다.\n\n몸은 보통보다 조금 컸다. 입이 크지 않았다면 얼굴은 아름다웠을 것이다. 머리카락은 숱이 많고 밝은 갈색이었다. 눈도 갈색이었는데, 갈색 눈은 흔하지 않으므로 그 눈이 얼굴에서 두드러진 특징이었다. 물기 어리고 크고, 다정함이나 즐거움으로 가득한 눈이었다. 이런 처녀가 구애를 받으라고 프램리로 데려와졌을 때에도 마크 로바츠에게는 여전히 늘 따르던 운이 있었다.\n\n그리고 그는 정말로 구애했고, 그녀를 얻었다. 마크 자신도 잘생긴 사내였으니까. 이때 교구 사제는 스물다섯 살쯤이었고, 앞날의 로바츠 부인은 두세 살 아래였다. 게다가 그녀는 목사관에 아주 빈손으로 오지도 않았다. 패니 몬셀이 상속녀였다고는 할 수 없지만, 몇천 파운드쯤의 재산이 그녀에게 남겨져 있었다. 이것이 이렇게 정리되어, 아내 몫 돈의 이자로 젊은 로바츠가 든 무거운 생명 보험료를 냈고, 그러고도 남는 것이 있어 성직자의 안락에 가장 어울리는 최상의 방식으로 목사관을 꾸미고, 기쁨 속에 인생길로 나서게 하기에 충분했다.\n\n레이디 러프턴은 자기 피후견인을 위해 그만큼을 해 주었다. 그러니 데번셔의 그 의사가 응접실 난롯불 앞에 앉아 명상에 잠긴 채, 사람들이 흔히 제 인생의 결산을 돌아보듯 돌아보면서, 맏자식인 프램리의 교구 사제 마크 로바츠 목사에 관한 한 그 결산에 매우 흡족했으리라는 것은 넉넉히 상상할 수 있다.\n\n그러나 우리 주인공 자신에 대해서는 아직 개인적으로 이야기한 것이 거의 없다. 어쩌면 많이 말할 필요가 없을지도 모른다. 차츰차츰 그가 화폭 위로 나와 보는 이에게 그 사람의 안팎을 다 보여 주기를 바라자. 여기서는 이렇게만 말해 두면 될 것이다. 그는 하늘의 천사로 태어난 것도 아니었고, 타락한 악마의 영으로 태어난 것도 아니었다. 그의 교육이 그를 만든 대로 그는 그러했다. 그에게는 선을 향한 큰 역량이 있었고, 악을 향한 소질도 넉넉히 있었다. 유혹은 오직 유혹을 물리치는 방식으로만 물리쳐야 한다는 것이 그에게 꼭 필요할 만큼은 넉넉히 있었다. 그를 망칠 만한 일이 많이 있었지만, 흔히 쓰는 뜻으로 그는 망쳐지지 않았다. 어머니가 여기는 만큼 자기가 완벽한 사람이라고 믿기에는 그에게 재치와 상식이 너무 많았다. 아마 자만심이 그의 가장 큰 위험은 아니었을 것이다. 자만심이 좀 더 많았더라면 그는 덜 유쾌한 사람이 되었을지 모르나, 그 덕에 그의 앞에 놓인 길은 더 안전했을 수도 있다.\n\n겉모습으로 보면 그는 남자다웠고, 키가 크고 머리는 금빛이었으며, 이마는 각이 져서 사색보다는 지성을 말해 주었고, 손은 희고 깨끗했으며 손톱은 개암 열매처럼 도톰했고, 옷을 입는 솜씨가 있어 그의 옷이 좋다거나 나쁘다거나, 낡았다거나 멋지다거나 하는 말을 누구도 그에 대해 한 적이 없었다.\n\n스물다섯 살, 혹은 그보다 조금 더 되었을 때 패니 몬셀과 결혼한 마크 로바츠는 그러한 사람이었다. 결혼식은 그의 교회에서 치러졌다. 몬셀 양에게는 제 집이 없었고 지난 석 달을 프램리 코트에서 지냈기 때문이다. 신부를 넘겨준 것은 조지 메러디스 경이었고, 레이디 러프턴은 자기 딸의 결혼에 쏟았던 것과 거의 다름없는 정성으로 이 결혼식이 마땅한 모습이 되도록 직접 살폈다. 결혼시키는 예식, 곧 매듭을 실제로 묶는 일은 레이디 러프턴의 존경하는 벗인 바체스터의 수석 사제께서 맡아 행했다. 그리고 바체스터에서 프램리까지 길이 멀고 도로가 험하고 철도의 도움도 없는데도 수석 사제의 부인 애러빈 부인이 일행에 있었다. 물론 러프턴 경도 왔다. 그리고 사람들은 그가 네 명의 아름다운 신부 들러리 가운데 누군가에게 반하고 말 것이라고 입을 모았는데, 그 넷 중에서 교구 사제의 둘째 누이인 블랜치 로바츠가 누구나 인정하듯 단연 가장 아름다웠다.\n\n그리고 거기에는 마크의 또 다른, 더 어린 누이도 있었다. 예식에서 소임을 맡지는 않았으나 자리에는 있었고, 그때 겨우 열여섯 살이었으므로 그녀를 두고는 아무런 예측도 나오지 않았지만, 훗날 내 독자들이 그녀를 알게 될 터이므로 여기에 언급해 둔다. 그녀의 이름은 루시 로바츠였다.\n\n그러고 나서 교구 사제와 그의 아내는 신혼여행을 떠났고, 그동안 늙은 부목사가 프램리의 영혼들을 돌보았다.\n\n그리고 때가 되어 그들은 돌아왔다. 얼마간의 세월이 더 지나 때가 차자 그들에게 아이가 태어났고, 그다음에 또 하나 태어났다. 그러고 나서 우리가 이야기를 시작하려는 시기가 왔다. 그러나 시작하기 전에, 사람들이 모두 데번셔의 그 의사에게 온갖 좋은 말을 하고 그런 아들을 둔 그의 운을 칭송한 것이 옳았다고 내가 주장해도 되지 않겠는가?\n\n“오늘 그 집에 올라갔었지?” 마크가 아내에게 말했다. 그는 저녁 식사 옷을 갈아입기 전에 응접실 난롯불 앞 안락의자에 몸을 길게 뻗고 앉아 있었다. 십일월 저녁이었고 그는 하루 종일 밖에 있었으니, 그런 때에 옷 갈아입기를 미루려는 마음은 아주 강하다. 마음이 굳센 사람은 현관문에서 곧장 제 방으로 올라가 응접실 난롯불의 유혹을 만나지 않는다.\n\n“아니요, 그런데 레이디 러프턴이 여기 내려오셨어요.”\n\n“세라 톰프슨을 편드는 논거로 잔뜩 무장하고?”\n\n“그래요, 딱 그랬어요, 마크.”\n\n“그래서 세라 톰프슨에 대해 당신은 뭐라고 했어?”\n\n“제 뜻으로는 별말 안 했어요. 하지만 당신이 그렇게 생각한다고, 아니 당신이 그렇게 생각하는 것 같다고, 정식으로 훈련받은 여교사가 더 나을 거라고 슬쩍 비추기는 했지요.”\n\n“그런데 마님은 동의하지 않았지?”\n\n“글쎄, 꼭 그렇다고는 하지 않겠어요. 하기야 아마 동의하지 않으신 것 같기는 해요.”\n\n“동의하지 않은 게 분명해. 그분은 관철할 뜻이 있으면 관철하기를 아주 좋아하니까.”\n\n“하지만 마크, 그분의 뜻은 대체로 훌륭하잖아요.”\n\n“그래도 알겠지, 이 학교 문제에서 그분은 아이들보다 자기 피후견인을 더 생각하고 있어.”\n\n“그 말씀을 그분께 하세요. 그러면 분명히 물러서실 거예요.”\n\n그러고 나서 두 사람은 다시 말이 없었다. 그리고 교구 사제는 난롯불을 마주하는 것으로 할 수 있는 만큼 몸을 충분히 데우고 나서, 몸을 돌려 이번에는 뒤쪽으로 같은 작업을 시작했다.\n\n“여보, 마크, 여섯 시 이십 분이에요. 가서 옷 갈아입으시겠어요?”\n\n“내 생각을 말해 줄게, 패니. 세라 톰프슨 일은 그분 뜻대로 하시게 해야 해. 내일 그분을 만나서 그렇게 말씀드려.”\n\n“정말이지 마크, 저라면 잘못된 일이라고 생각되면 물러서지 않겠어요. 그분도 그러기를 바라지는 않으실 거예요.”\n\n“이번에 내가 고집을 부리면 다음번에는 틀림없이 양보해야 할 텐데, 그다음 일은 아마 더 중요할지도 몰라.”\n\n“그렇지만 그게 잘못된 일이라면요, 마크?”\n\n“잘못됐다고는 하지 않았어. 게다가 잘못됐다 해도, 아주 미미한 정도로 잘못됐다면 참아야지. 세라 톰프슨은 아주 점잖은 사람이야. 문제는 오직 그녀가 가르칠 수 있느냐 하는 것뿐이지.”\n\n젊은 아내는 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 남편이 틀렸다는 생각이 좀 들었다. 잘못은 참아야 하는 것이 사실이고, 그것도 아주 많은 잘못을 참아야 하는 것이 사실이다. 그러나 자기가 고칠 수 있는 잘못을 참아야 할 사람은 아무도 없다. 유능한 교사를 구할 수 있는데 왜 그가, 교구 사제가, 교구 아이들에게 무능한 교사를 받아들이는 데 동의해야 하는가? 그런 경우라면, 로바츠 부인은 혼자 그렇게 생각했다, 자기는 레이디 러프턴과 끝까지 싸웠을 것이다.\n\n그러나 이튿날 아침 그녀는 시킨 대로 했고, 세라 톰프슨에 대한 반대는 모두 철회하겠다고 그 미망인에게 알렸다.\n\n“아! 그이가 그녀가 어떤 사람인지 알게 되면 내 뜻에 동의할 줄 알았어요.” 마님이 말했다. “설명만 하면 된다고 생각했지요.” 그러고 나서 그녀는 깃을 다듬으며 아주 너그러워졌다. 사실을 말하자면 레이디 러프턴은 교구에 깊이 관계되는 일에서 반대당하는 것을 좋아하지 않았다.\n\n“그리고 패니,” 레이디 러프턴이 가장 다정한 어조로 말했다. “토요일에 어디 갈 일은 없지요?”\n\n“네, 없을 것 같아요.”\n\n“그러면 우리에게 와야 해요. 저스티니아가 여기 올 거예요, 알지요” — 레이디 메러디스의 이름이 저스티니아였다 — “그러니 당신과 로바츠 씨는 월요일까지 우리와 함께 지내는 게 좋겠어요. 그이는 일요일에 작은 서재를 혼자 다 쓰면 돼요. 메러디스 부부는 월요일에 떠나요. 그리고 저스티니아는 당신이 함께 있지 않으면 즐겁지 않을 거예요.”\n\n세라 톰프슨 일에서 자기 뜻대로 하도록 허락받지 못했다면 레이디 러프턴이 로바츠네를 초대하지 않기로 마음먹었을 것이라고 말하는 것은 부당하겠다. 그러나 결과는 그리 되었을 것이다. 어쨌든 지금은 그녀가 온통 친절했다. 그래서 로바츠 부인이 아이들 때문에 저녁에는 집에 돌아가야 할 것 같다며 조금 사양의 말을 하자, 레이디 러프턴은 프램리 코트에는 아기와 유모가 있을 방도 넉넉하다고 단언하고, 고개를 두 번 끄덕이고 우산으로 세 번 톡톡 두드려 그 일을 자기 방식대로 정리해 버렸다.\n\n이것은 화요일 아침의 일이었고, 같은 날 저녁 식사 전에 교구 사제는 자기 말이 마구간으로 끌려 들어가는 것을 보자마자 다시 응접실 난롯불 앞 그 의자에 앉았다.\n\n“마크,” 아내가 말했다. “메러디스 부부가 토요일과 일요일에 프램리에 와 있을 거예요. 그래서 우리가 올라가서 월요일까지 지내겠다고 약속했어요.”\n\n“그럴 리가! 아이고, 이런 난처한 일이!”\n\n“왜요? 당신도 싫어하지 않을 줄 알았어요. 그리고 제가 거기 없으면 저스티니아가 서운하게 여길 거예요.”\n\n“당신은 가면 돼, 물론 가야지. 하지만 나는 안 돼, 그건 불가능해.”\n\n“그렇지만 왜요, 여보?”\n\n“왜냐고? 방금 학교에서, 콜디코츠에서 온 편지에 답장을 썼어. 사워비가 한 주쯤 거기 와 있으라고 우기더군. 그래서 그러겠다고 했어.”\n\n“콜디코츠에 한 주씩이나 가요, 마크?”\n\n“열흘까지도 승낙한 것 같아.”\n\n“그러면 일요일을 두 번 비우게요?”\n\n“아니야, 패니, 한 번뿐이야. 그렇게 책잡지 마.”\n\n“저를 책잡는 사람이라고 하지 마세요, 마크. 제가 그렇지 않다는 걸 아시잖아요. 하지만 정말 안타까워요. 하필 레이디 러프턴이 싫어하실 일이잖아요. 게다가 지난달에도 스코틀랜드에 가 계셔서 일요일을 두 번 비우셨고요.”\n\n“구월이었어, 패니. 그리고 그게 바로 책잡는 거야.”\n\n“아, 하지만 마크, 마크, 그렇게 말씀하지 마세요. 제가 그런 뜻이 아니라는 걸 아시잖아요. 하지만 레이디 러프턴은 그 콜디코츠 사람들을 좋아하지 않으세요. 지난번에 거기 가셨을 때는 러프턴 경도 함께 계셨잖아요. 그때 그분이 얼마나 언짢아하셨는지 아시죠!”\n\n“이번에는 러프턴 경이 나와 함께 있지 않아. 아직 스코틀랜드에 있으니까. 그리고 내가 가는 이유는 이거야. 해럴드 스미스와 그 부인이 거기 올 텐데, 나는 그들을 더 알아 두고 싶어. 해럴드 스미스는 틀림없이 언젠가 정부에 들어갈 텐데, 그런 사람과의 교분을 소홀히 할 수는 없지.”\n\n“하지만 마크, 정부가 당신에게 무슨 소용이에요?”\n\n“글쎄, 패니, 물론 나는 아무것도 바라지 않는다고 말해야 하겠지. 어떤 뜻에서는 정말 바라지도 않아. 그렇지만 그래도 나는 가서 해럴드 스미스 부부를 만날 거야.”\n\n“일요일 전에 돌아오실 수는 없어요?”\n\n“콜디코츠에서 설교하겠다고 약속했어. 해럴드 스미스가 바체스터에서 오스트랄라시아 군도에 대해 강연을 할 거고, 나는 같은 주제로 자선 설교를 하는 거야. 선교사를 더 내보내고 싶어 하거든.”\n\n“콜디코츠에서 자선 설교라니!”\n\n“왜 안 되지? 집이 손님으로 꽉 찰 텐데, 알지, 애러빈 부부도 아마 올 거야.”\n\n“아닐 것 같아요. 애러빈 부인이 해럴드 스미스 부인과는 잘 지낼지 몰라도, 그것도 의심스럽지만, 스미스 부인의 오라비를 좋아하지 않는 건 분명해요. 콜디코츠에 머물지는 않으실 거예요.”\n\n“그리고 주교도 아마 하루 이틀은 거기 계실 거야.”\n\n“그건 훨씬 더 그럴듯하네요, 마크. 프라우디 부인을 만나는 즐거움 때문에 콜디코츠에 가시는 거라면, 저는 더 말할 것이 없어요.”\n\n“나도 프라우디 부인을 좋아하는 정도가 당신보다 조금도 더하지 않아, 패니.” 교구 사제가 목소리에 짜증 같은 것을 담아 말했다. 아내가 자기를 심하게 대한다고 생각한 것이다. “하지만 교구 성직자가 주교를 이따금 만나 두는 것이 좋다는 게 일반적인 생각이야. 그리고 그 사람들이 다 그곳에 머무는 동안 특히 설교를 하라고 초청을 받았으니, 딱 거절할 수도 없었어.” 그러고 나서 그는 일어나 촛대를 들고 옷 갈아입는 방으로 달아났다.\n\n“하지만 레이디 러프턴께는 뭐라고 말씀드려야 해요?” 저녁나절에 아내가 그에게 말했다.\n\n“그냥 짧게 편지를 써서, 내가 다음 일요일에 콜디코츠에서 설교하겠다고 약속한 것을 당신이 알게 되었다고 말해. 당신은 물론 가는 거지?”\n\n“네, 하지만 그분이 언짢아하실 걸 알아요. 지난번에 그분이 손님을 모았을 때도 당신은 나가 계셨잖아요.”\n\n“어쩔 수 없어. 그분은 그걸 세라 톰프슨 건과 상쇄해야 해. 언제나 이기기를 바라서는 안 되지.”\n\n“세라 톰프슨 일에서, 당신 말대로 그분이 지셨다면 저는 마음에 두지 않았을 거예요. 그건 당신이 뜻을 관철해야 했던 일이에요.”\n\n“그리고 이 다른 일은 내가 뜻을 관철할 일이야. 이렇게 차이가 나는 게 딱하지, 그렇지 않아?”\n\n그러자 아내는, 속이 상하기는 했으나 더 말하지 않는 게 낫겠다고 깨달았다. 그리고 잠자리에 들기 전에 남편이 권한 대로 레이디 러프턴에게 편지를 썼다.\n\n# 제2장. 프램리 패와 콜디코츠 패\n\n앞의 몇 페이지에서 이름을 든 사람들 가운데 몇몇에 대해, 그리고 그들이 살던 고장에 대해서도, 내가 한두 마디 해 두어야 하겠다.\n\n레이디 러프턴 자신에 대해서는 독자에게 그녀를 소개할 만큼은 이미 적었을 것이다. 프램리의 재산은 그녀의 아들 것이었다. 그러나 러프턴 파크—다른 주에 있는, 오래되어 다 낡아 빠진 집—가 여태까지 러프턴 집안의 본가였으므로, 프램리 코트는 그녀가 살아 있는 동안 머물 거처로 그녀에게 배정되어 있었다. 러프턴 경 자신은 아직 미혼이었다. 그리고 러프턴 파크에는—실은 조부가 죽은 뒤로 사람이 든 적이 없었다—살림을 차려 놓지 않았으므로, 그 근처에 머무는 것이 마음에 맞을 때면 그는 어머니와 함께 지냈다. 미망인은 아들이 허락해 주는 것보다 훨씬 더 자주 그를 보고 싶어 했다. 그는 스코틀랜드에 사냥용 별장을 두었고, 런던에 아파트를 두었고, 레스터셔에 말 여러 필을 두었다—자기들 사냥이 영국의 어느 사냥에도 뒤지지 않는다고 여기던 주변 시골 지주들의 비위를 몹시 거스르면서. 그러나 러프턴 경은 동 바셋주 사냥회에 회비를 냈고, 그러고 나면 자기가 무엇을 즐길지는 자기 뜻대로 해도 좋다고 여겼다.\n\n프램리 자체는 기분 좋은 시골이었다. 영주다운 위엄이나 웅장함이라 할 것은 아무것도 없었으나, 시골 생활의 안락에 필요한 것은 다 갖추고 있었다. 집은 이층으로 된 낮은 건물로, 시기를 달리하여 지어졌고, 무슨 건축 양식이라 내세울 만한 것은 전혀 없었다. 그래도 방들은 천장이 높지는 않아도 따뜻하고 편안했으며, 정원은 주 안의 다른 어느 정원보다도 손질이 잘 되어 단정했다. 실상 프램리 코트가 이름난 것은 오직 그 정원 때문이었다.\n\n마을이라 할 것은, 엄밀히 말하면 없었다. 큰길이 프램리의 방목장과 관목숲과 숲이 둘러싼 안쪽 들판 사이를 한 마일 반 동안 구불구불 지나갔는데, 그 가운데 이백 야드도 곧게 뻗은 데가 없었다. 그리고 영지를 가로질러 내려가는 갈림길이 하나 있어서, 그 덕에 프램리 크로스라 불리는 곳이 생겼다. 여기에 “러프턴 암스” 여관이 서 있었고, 또 여기 프램리 크로스에서 이따금 사냥개들이 모였다. 젊은 러프턴 경이 제 고장 사냥을 등한히 하는 성미였음에도 프램리 숲에서는 몰이가 이루어졌기 때문이다. 그리고 이 크로스에는 우편취급소를 맡고 있는 구두장이도 살았다.\n\n프램리 교회는 여기서 딱 사분의 일 마일 떨어져 있었고, 프램리 코트의 정문 바로 맞은편에 서 있었다. 그것은 초라하고 못생긴 건물에 지나지 않았는데, 백 년쯤 전에 세워진 것이라 그때 지어진 교회는 다 초라하고 못생기게 만들어졌던 것이다. 게다가 신자를 다 들일 만큼 크지도 못해서, 더러는 그 때문에 교구 양쪽에 자리를 잡은 비국교회 예배당, 곧 시온이니 에벤에셀이니 하는 데로 밀려갔다. 레이디 러프턴은 그것들을 눌러 없애는 일에 자기가 아끼는 목사가 낼 수 있는 만큼의 열심을 내지 않는다고 여겼다. 그러므로 새 교회가 세워지는 것을 보는 일은 레이디 러프턴의 마음에 가까이 놓인 문제였고, 그녀는 아들에게나 교구 사제에게나 이 좋은 일을 시작하라고 간곡히 말을 쏟았다.\n\n교회를 지나 그 가까이에 남학교와 여학교가 있었는데, 따로 떨어진 두 채로, 레이디 러프턴이 힘을 써서 세워진 것이었다. 그다음에는 아담한 식료품 가게가 나왔다. 그 아담한 식료품 장수는 교회 서기 겸 교회지기였고, 아담한 식료품 장수의 아내는 교회에서 신자석 문을 열어 주는 사람이었다. 포진스가 그들의 성이었고, 둘 다 전에 큰 집에서 하인으로 있었던 터라 레이디 러프턴이 몹시 아끼는 사람들이었다.\n\n그리고 여기서 길은 갑자기 왼쪽으로 꺾여, 말하자면 프램리 코트에서 등을 돌리며 돌아갔다. 그 꺾인 데를 막 지나면 목사관이 있어서, 목사관 뜰 뒤에서 교회 묘지로 이어지는 작은 정원 길이 하나 났고, 그 길이 포진스네를 그들만의 외딴 구석으로 떼어 놓았다. 사실을 말하면, 교구 사제는 힘만 있었다면 그들과 그들의 양배추를 그 구석에서 기꺼이 몰아냈을 것이다. 나봇의 작은 포도밭은 예부터 이웃한 권세가의 눈에 늘 가시였지 않은가?\n\n이 경우의 권세가에게는 아합만큼이나 변명거리가 없었다. 목사관이라는 점에서 그의 목사관보다 더 완전할 수 있는 것은 없었기 때문이다. 그것은 적당한 재산을 가진 적당한 신사의 집에 필요한 세목을 다 갖추었고, 적당함을 넘어선 신사들이 요구하는—또는 그것들 자신이 적당함을 넘어선 재산을 요구하는—값비싼 사치는 하나도 없었다. 그리고 정원과 방목장은 집에 꼭 어울렸으며, 모든 것이 잘 정돈되어 있었다. 아주 새것이어서 설익고 헐벗고 인부 냄새가 나는 것은 아니었고, 새것다움이 편안한 소박함으로 넘어가는 바로 그 시기에 놓여 있었다.\n\n프램리에 다른 마을은 없었다. 코트 뒤편, 그 갈림길 가운데 하나를 따라 올라가면 작은 가게가 한둘 더 있었고, 아주 아담한 시골집 한 채가 있어서 전임 부목사의 미망인이 살았는데, 그녀 또한 레이디 러프턴이 거둔 사람이었다. 그리고 크고 눈에 거슬리는 벽돌집이 하나 있어 지금의 부목사가 살았다. 그러나 이 집은 교회에서 꼬박 한 마일이나 떨어져 있었고 프램리 코트에서는 더 멀었으며, 프램리 크로스에서 저택과 반대 방향으로 뻗어 가는 그 갈림길에 서 있었다. 이 신사, 곧 에번 존스 목사는 나이로 보면 교구 사제의 아버지라 해도 될 만했다. 그러나 그는 여러 해 동안 프램리의 부목사였고, 신조가 저교회파이고 생김새가 보기 좋지 않아 레이디 러프턴이 개인적으로 그를 싫어하기는 했으나, 그래도 그녀는 그를 내보내라고 다그치지는 않았다. 그는 그 큰 벽돌집에서 두세 명의 학생을 가르쳤는데, 그 일에서도 부목사 자리에서도 밀려난다면 다른 데서 자리를 잡기가 어려울 것이었다. 그런 까닭에 에번 존스 목사에게는 자비가 베풀어졌고, 붉은 얼굴과 어색하게 큰 발에도 불구하고 그는 석 달에 한 번씩 그 수수한 딸과 함께 프램리 코트의 만찬에 초대되었다.\n\n이 밖에는, 프램리 코트의 경계 밖 프램리 교구에 농부와 농사꾼의 집 말고는 집이라 할 것이 거의 없었다. 그러면서도 교구는 넓이가 컸다.\n\n프램리는 바셋주의 동부에 있는데, 이 주가 세상이 다 알듯이 정치로 말하면 영국의 어느 주에도 못지않은 골수 보수의 땅이다. 여기서도 등을 돌린 자가 있었던 것은 사실이다. 그러나 그렇다면 그렇게 등을 돌린 자가 없었던 주가 어디 있는가? 이 싸구려 도금 같은 시대에 옛 농촌의 덕을 조금도 섞이지 않은 채로 찾아보기를 어디서 바랄 수 있겠는가? 그런데 유감이지만 그 배신자들 가운데 사람들이 이제 러프턴 경을 넣는다. 그가 과격한 휘그라거나, 아니 휘그이기라도 하다는 말은 아니다. 다만 그는 이 주의 오래된 일들을 두고 비웃고 코웃음을 친다. 이 문제로 청을 받으면, 자기로서는 브라이트 씨가 원한다면 이 주의 의원으로 앉아도 좋다고 선언한다. 그리고 불행히도 자기는 귀족이니 이 문제에 관심을 가질 권리조차 없다고 내세운다. 이 모든 일이 깊이 유감스러운 것은, 옛날에는 그 프램리 지역보다 더 확실하게 골수 보수인 데가 이 주에 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 실은 오늘날까지도 노부인은 이따금 손을 보태 줄 수 있다.\n\n콜디코츠는 나다니엘 사워비 씨의 본거지인데, 그는 지금이라고 가정된 이 시점에 바셋주 서부의 하원 의원 가운데 한 사람이다. 그러나 이 서부는 그 쌍둥이 형제를 빛내 주는 훌륭한 정치적 미덕이라면 하나도 자랑할 것이 없다. 이곳은 확실히 휘그이며, 그 정치는 한둘의 대단한 휘그 집안에 거의 지배당하고 있다.\n\n마크 로바츠가 콜디코츠를 찾아가려 한다는 말은 이미 했고, 그렇지 않았더라면 그의 아내가 더 기뻐했을 것이라는 암시도 했다. 그것은 분명히 사실이었다. 그녀는 사랑스럽고 사려 깊고 훌륭한 아내였던 만큼, 사워비 씨가 젊은 성직자에게 세상에서 가장 알맞은 친구는 아니라는 것을 알았고, 또 이 주 전체에서 레이디 러프턴에게 그만큼 이름이 거슬리는 집은 딱 한 채밖에 더 없다는 것도 알았다. 그 이유는, 말하자면 여러 겹이었다. 첫째로 사워비 씨는 휘그였고, 주로 저 대단한 휘그 독재자 옴니엄 공작의 후원으로 의석을 얻고 있었다. 공작의 집은 사워비 씨의 집보다도 더 위험했고, 레이디 러프턴은 그를 이 땅에 나타난 루시퍼의 화신으로 여겼다. 사워비 씨 또한 미혼이었다. 실은 러프턴 경도 그러했고, 그것이 어머니에게는 큰 걱정거리였다. 사워비 씨가 쉰 살인데 젊은 러프턴 경은 아직 스물여섯밖에 되지 않은 것은 사실이지만, 그래도 레이디 러프턴은 그 일로 마음이 조여 오고 있었다. 그녀의 생각으로는 남자란 아내를 먹여 살릴 수 있게 되면 곧 결혼해야 마땅했다. 그리고 그녀는 한 가지 생각을 품고 있었다—아주 사사로운 신념이어서 그녀 자신도 온전히 의식하지는 못했다—남자들이 대체로 제 이기적인 즐거움 때문에 이 의무를 소홀히 하려 든다는 것, 못된 자들이 더 순진한 자들을 부추겨 그렇게 소홀히 하게 만든다는 것, 그리고 다른 쪽 성이 보이지 않는 강제를 그들에게 가하지 않는다면 많은 남자가 아예 결혼하지 않을 것이라는 생각이었다. 옴니엄 공작은 그런 죄인들 가운데 바로 우두머리였고, 레이디 러프턴은 사워비 씨와 콜디코츠를 통해 자기 아들이 그 해로운 옴니엄의 영향 아래 놓이게 될까 몹시 두려워했다.\n\n게다가 사워비 씨는 아주 큰 영지를 가지고도 아주 가난한 사람으로 알려져 있었다. 사람들 말로는 그가 선거 운동에 많이 쏟아 버렸고, 도박에는 더 많이 쏟아 버렸다고 했다. 그의 재산 가운데 상당한 몫이 이미 공작의 손에 넘어가 있었는데, 공작은 으레 제 주변에서 팔 만한 것이면 무엇이든 사들이는 사람이었다. 실상 그의 적들은 그를 두고, 바셋주의 땅을 어찌나 탐내는지 젊은 이웃의 땅을 얻으려고 그 이웃을 파멸로 끌고 갈 사람이라고 했다. 그런데—아! 만일 그가 그런 식으로 프램리 코트의 좋은 땅 얼마간을 차지하게 된다면? 만일 그것을 다 차지하게 된다면? 레이디 러프턴이 콜디코츠를 좋아하지 않은 것을 이상하게 여길 수는 없는 일이다.\n\n레이디 러프턴이 콜디코츠 패라고 부르던 사람들은, 패라면 마땅히 이러해야 한다는 그녀의 생각에 어느 모로나 어긋났다. 그녀는 명랑하고 조용하고 살림이 넉넉한 사람들, 자기 교회와 자기 나라와 자기 여왕을 사랑하고 세상에서 시끄러운 소리를 내는 데 너무 마음을 쓰지 않는 사람들을 좋아했다. 그녀는 주변의 농부들이 모두 어려움 없이 소작료를 낼 수 있기를, 노파들이 모두 따뜻한 플란넬 속치마를 갖기를, 일하는 사람들이 몸에 좋은 음식과 마른 집으로 류머티즘을 면하기를, 그리고 그들이 모두 자기네 목자와 주인에게—영적인 쪽으로나 세속적인 쪽으로나—순종하기를 바랐다. 그것이 나라를 사랑한다는 그녀의 생각이었다. 그녀는 또한 잡목숲에 꿩이 그득하고, 그루터기 밭에 자고가 그득하고, 가시금작화 덤불에 여우가 그득하기를 바랐다. 그런 식으로도 그녀는 나라를 사랑했다. 저 크림 전쟁 동안 그녀는 러시아가 지기를 간절히 바랐다—그러나 그녀가 보기에는 너무 그런 쪽으로 흐르는 듯했지만, 영국을 제쳐 놓고 프랑스가 이겨서는 안 되었고, 파머스턴 경의 독재 아래 영국이 이기는 것도 별로 마땅치 않았다. 실상 애버딘 경이 쫓겨난 뒤로 그녀는 그 전쟁에 믿음이 거의 없었다. 아, 정말이지 더비 경이 들어올 수만 있었다면!\n\n그런데 이제 이 콜디코츠 패에 대해 말해 보자. 결국 그들에게 그리 위험할 것은 아무것도 없었다. 사워비 씨가 총각의 그릇된 짓을 즐겼다면 그것은 시골이 아니라 런던에서였으니까. 그들을 하나의 패로 놓고 말하면, 가장 큰 죄인은 해럴드 스미스 씨, 아니면 어쩌면 그의 아내였다. 그도 하원 의원이었고, 많은 사람이 보기에 떠오르는 인물이었다. 그의 아버지는 여러 해 동안 의회의 논객이었고 높은 관직을 지냈다. 해럴드는 젊었을 때부터 자신을 내각에 앉힐 작정이었다. 그리고 제 일을 부지런히 하는 것으로 성공이 보장된다면 그는 이르든 늦든 그것을 얻어야 마땅했다. 그는 이미 하급 자리를 한 자리 이상 채워 보았고, 재무부에 있었고, 한두 달은 해군부에 있어서 부지런함으로 관청 사람들을 놀라게 했다. 마지막에 말한 그 몇 달은 애버딘 경 아래였고, 그와 함께 물러날 수밖에 없었다. 그는 차남이어서 큰 재산을 가지지 못했다. 그러므로 직업으로서의 정치는 그에게 중요했다. 그는 젊었을 때 사워비 씨의 누이와 결혼했는데, 그 부인이 그보다 여섯 일곱 살쯤 위였고 가져온 지참금도 빈약했으므로, 사람들은 이 일에서 해럴드 스미스 씨가 눈이 밝지 못했다고 생각했다. 해럴드 스미스 씨는 어느 당에서도 개인적으로 인기 있는 사람이 아니었으나, 더러는 그가 대단히 유용하다고 판단했다. 그는 부지런하고 아는 것이 많고 대체로 정직했다. 그러나 자만하고 말이 길고 뻐겼다.\n\n해럴드 스미스 부인은 그 남편과 정반대였다. 그녀는 영리하고 재기 있는 여자로, 나이에 비하면 보기 좋았다—이제 마흔이 넘었다—세상 온갖 것의 값을 날카롭게 알아보았고, 세상 온갖 즐거움을 날카롭게 맛보았다. 그녀는 부지런하지도, 아는 것이 많지도, 어쩌면 온전히 정직하지도 않았다—정치적 정직의 필요를 이해하거나 그 이로움을 알아본 여자가 여태 어디 있었던가?—그러나 그 대신 둔하지도 뻐기지도 않았고, 자만이 있었다 해도 그것을 드러내지는 않았다. 그녀는 남편에 관해서는 실망한 여자였다. 그가 당장 정치적으로 중요한 인물이 될 것이라는 셈으로 그와 결혼했는데, 스미스 씨는 아직 젊은 시절의 예언을 다 채우지 못했으니 말이다.\n\n그리고 레이디 러프턴은 콜디코츠 패를 말할 때 마음속으로 바체스터의 주교와 그 아내와 딸을 분명히 거기에 넣었다. 프라우디 주교는 물론 종교와 종교적 사색에 크게 몸을 담근 사람이고 사워비 씨 자신은 종교적 감정이라 할 것이 아무것도 없었으니, 언뜻 보면 서로 많이 오갈 근거가 있을 것 같지 않고, 어쩌면 그런 왕래가 많지도 않았다. 그러나 프라우디 부인과 해럴드 스미스 부인은 네다섯 해 된 굳은 벗이었으니—프라우디 부부가 이 교구로 온 뒤로 줄곧 그러했다—그래서 스미스 부인이 오라비를 찾아올 때면 으레 주교도 콜디코츠로 끌려왔다. 그런데 프라우디 주교는 결코 고교회파 고위 성직자가 아니었고, 레이디 러프턴은 그가 이 교구로 들어온 것을 끝내 용서하지 않았다. 그녀는 본능적으로 주교라는 직분에 높은 경의를 품고 있었다. 그러나 프라우디 주교 그 사람에 대해서는 사워비 씨나 저 악의 조작자 옴니엄 공작에 대해서보다 나을 것 없이 생각했다. 로바츠 씨가 어디를 가면 주교를 만나는 이로움이 있다고 변명할 때마다 레이디 러프턴은 윗입술을 살짝 비틀었다. 프라우디 주교가—분명히 주교라고 불러야 하는 사람이지만—마땅히 그러해야 할 만큼도 못 된다고 말로는 할 수 없었다. 그러나 그 입술의 비틂으로 그녀는 자기를 아는 이들에게 자기 마음속 느낌이 그렇다는 것을 똑똑히 알렸다.\n\n그리고 또—적어도 마크 로바츠는 그렇게 들었고, 그 소식은 곧 프램리 코트에까지 닿았는데—서플하우스 씨가 콜디코츠 손님 가운데 한 사람이 될 것이라고 알려졌다. 그런데 서플하우스 씨는 신사답고 젊고 고교회파인 보수적 시골 목사에게 해럴드 스미스보다도 더 나쁜 벗이었다. 그도 하원에 있었고, 저 러시아 전쟁 초기에 수도의 일간 신문 일부로부터 나라를 구할 수 있는 유일한 사람이라고 떠받들렸다. 그를 내각에 앉히라고 《주피터》는 말했다. 그러면 개혁에 다소 희망이 있고, 이 위태로운 때에 영국의 오랜 영광이 곤두박질쳐 잊힘 속으로 떨어지도록 방치되지 않을 가망도 얼마간 있으리라고 했다. 그러자 내각은 서플하우스 씨에게서 큰 구원을 기대한 것은 아니지만, 으레 그러하듯 《주피터》를 등에 두고자 하여, 그 신사를 불러들여 자기들 사이에 자리를 얼마간 마련해 주었다. 그러나 나라를 구하고 백성을 이끌도록 태어난 사람이 어떻게 차관 자리를 채우는 것으로 만족할 수 있겠는가? 서플하우스는 만족하지 않았고, 곧 자기 자리는 여태 자기에게 내밀어진 어떤 자리보다도 훨씬 높은 데 있다는 뜻을 알려 놓았다. 높은 관직의 직인이냐 아니면 칼을 뽑는 싸움이냐, 이것이 그가 몹시 두들겨 맞고 있던 국정의 수반에게 내민 양자택일이었다—국정의 수반이 이 청구자의 값을 알아볼 것이고 《주피터》에 대한 건전한 두려움을 눈앞에 둘 것이라고 조금도 의심하지 않은 채였다. 그러나 국정의 수반은 그렇게 두들겨 맞고 있으면서도, 서플하우스 씨와 《주피터》를 얻는 데조차 값을 너무 많이 치를 수 있다는 것은 알았다. 그리하여 나라의 구원자는 제 토마호크를 마음대로 휘둘러도 좋다는 말을 들었다. 그때부터 그는 토마호크를 휘둘러 왔으나, 기대되었던 만큼 효험을 내지는 못했다. 그도 사워비 씨와 아주 가까웠고, 확실히 콜디코츠 패의 한 사람이었다.\n\n그리고 그 낙인 안에 든 사람이 많았는데, 그들의 죄는 도덕보다는 정치나 종교의 죄였다. 그러나 그들은 레이디 러프턴에게 쓰디쓴 쓸개즙이요 쑥이었다. 그녀는 그들을 타락한 자의 자식들로 여겼고, 자기 아들이 그들 가운데 있음을 알았을 때는 어머니의 슬픔으로 슬퍼했으며, 자기가 거둔 성직자가 그런 자리를 찾아가려 한다는 말을 들었을 때는 후원자의 노여움을 다 느꼈다. 레이디 러프턴이 언짢아할 것이라고 로바츠 부인이 말한 것도 무리가 아니었다.\n\n“떠나기 전에 큰 집에 들르지는 않을 거죠?” 이튿날 아침 아내가 물었다. 그는 그날 점심을 먹고 나서, 자기 이륜마차를 스스로 몰아 이십사 마일쯤 떨어진 콜디코츠에 만찬 전에 닿도록 떠날 예정이었다.\n\n“아니, 안 갈 생각이야. 가서 무슨 소용이 있겠어?”\n\n“글쎄요, 설명은 못 하겠어요. 그래도 저라면 들를 것 같아요. 어쩌면 얼마쯤은, 가기로 정했으니 그렇게 말씀드리기가 두렵지 않다는 것을 그분에게 보여 드리려고요.”\n\n“두렵다니! 말도 안 되는 소리야, 패니. 나는 그분이 두렵지 않아. 다만 그분이 할 언짢은 소리를 내가 왜 스스로 불러들여야 하는지 모르겠어. 게다가 시간도 없어. 예배 일을 두고 존스한테 걸어가 봐야 하고, 그러고 나서 떠날 준비까지 하려면 시간에 맞춰 나서기도 빠듯할 거야.”\n\n그는 부목사 존스 씨를 찾아갔는데, 거기서는 양심에 거리끼는 것을 조금도 느끼지 않고 자기가 만나게 될 하원 의원들과 그들과 함께 있을 주교를 오히려 자랑삼아 말했다. 에번 존스 씨는 그의 부목사일 뿐이었으니, 그 일을 두고 그에게 말할 때는 교구 사제가 주의 하원 의원 집에서 자기 주교를 만나는 것이 지극히 마땅한 일인 양 이야기할 수 있었다. 그리고 마땅한 일이라고 말하고 싶어질 법도 하다. 다만, 그렇다면 왜 그는 레이디 러프턴에게는 같은 어조로 그 이야기를 할 수 없었을까? 그러고 나서 아내와 아이들에게 입을 맞춘 뒤 그는 마차를 몰고 떠났다. 앞으로 열흘의 전망에 아주 흡족했으나, 돌아왔을 때의 불편함도 벌써 미리 헤아리고 있었다.\n\n그 뒤 사흘 동안 로바츠 부인은 레이디 러프턴을 만나지 않았다. 그런 만남을 피하려고 딱히 무슨 수를 쓴 것은 아니었으나, 일부러 큰 집으로 올라가지도 않았다. 그녀는 여느 때처럼 학교에 갔고 농부의 아내들 집에 한두 번 들렀으나, 프램리 코트의 뜰 안에는 발을 들이지 않았다. 그녀는 남편보다 용감했으나, 그런 그녀도 궂은 날을 미리 앞당기고 싶지는 않았다.\n\n토요일, 어스름이 깔리기 바로 전, 그 운명적인 걸음을 내디딜 준비를 생각하고 있을 때 그녀의 벗 레이디 메러디스가 찾아왔다.\n\n“그래서 패니, 우리는 또 로바츠 씨를 못 만나는 불운을 겪게 되었네요.” 레이디 메러디스가 말했다.\n\n“네. 이렇게 운이 안 맞는 일이 또 있을까요? 하지만 그이는 당신이 온다는 말을 듣기 전에 사워비 씨에게 약속을 해 버렸어요. 알고서도 떠났으리라고는 제발 생각하지 말아요.”\n\n“그렇게 훨씬 더 재미난 모임에서 그이를 붙들어 두었다면 우리가 미안했겠지요.”\n\n“이봐요, 저스티니아, 그건 공정하지 않아요. 그이가 프램리 코트보다 콜디코츠를 더 좋아해서 거기 갔다는 뜻으로 말하는 거지요. 하지만 그렇지 않아요. 레이디 러프턴께서 그렇게 생각하지 않으시면 좋겠어요.”\n\n레이디 메러디스는 벗의 허리에 팔을 두르며 웃었다. “나한테 그이를 변호하느라 말솜씨를 다 써 버리지 말아요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그건 우리 어머니한테 다 쓸 데가 있을 거예요.”\n\n“그런데 어머님께서 노하셨나요?” 로바츠 부인이 물으며, 그 일에 대해 참된 소식을 얼마나 간절히 알고 싶어 하는지를 얼굴에 드러냈다.\n\n“글쎄요, 패니, 어머니를 나만큼 당신도 잘 알잖아요. 어머니는 프램리의 교구 사제를 아주 높이 여기시니까, 그이를 콜디코츠의 그 정치가들에게 내주기를 아까워하시는 거지요.”\n\n“하지만 저스티니아, 주교님도 거기 오신다잖아요.”\n\n“그 점을 든다고 해서 어머니가 그 신사의 부재를 조금이라도 봐주실 것 같지는 않아요. 그이는 자기를 그렇게까지 중히 여겨 준다는 것을 알면 아주 자랑스러워해야 마땅하지요. 하지만 자, 패니, 나와 함께 걸어서 돌아가요. 옷은 큰 집에서 갈아입으면 되고요. 그럼 이제 아이들을 보러 가요.”\n\n그런 뒤에, 프램리 코트로 함께 걸어가면서 로바츠 부인은 자리에 없는 성직자에게 어떤 심각한 공격이 가해지면 자기 편에 서 주겠다는 약속을 벗에게서 받아 냈다.\n\n“곧 방으로 올라가시나요?” 현관으로 이어지는 문간에 들어서자마자 교구 사제의 아내가 말했다. 레이디 메러디스는 벗이 무슨 뜻으로 그러는지 곧 알아차렸고, 궂은 날을 미루지는 말아야겠다고 마음을 정했다. “들어가서 끝내 버리는 게 좋겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그러면 저녁은 마음 편히 지낼 수 있을 테니까요.” 그래서 응접실 문이 열렸고, 거기 레이디 러프턴이 혼자 소파에 앉아 있었다.\n\n“어머니,” 딸이 말했다. “로바츠 씨 일로 패니를 너무 꾸짖으시면 안 돼요. 그이는 주교님 앞에서 자선 설교를 하러 간 거예요. 그런 처지라면 어쩌면 거절할 수 없었겠지요.” 이것은 레이디 메러디스가 좀 늘려 말한 것이었다—의심할 것 없이 아주 선한 마음으로 끼워 넣은 말이었지만, 그래도 늘려 말한 것이었다. 주교가 일요일까지 콜디코츠에 머물 것이라고는 아무도 생각하지 않았으니까.\n\n“잘 지냈어요, 패니?” 레이디 러프턴이 일어서며 말했다. “나는 이 사람을 꾸짖을 생각이 없어요. 그리고 저스티니아, 너는 어쩌면 그런 말도 안 되는 소리를 하는지 모르겠구나. 물론 로바츠 씨가 없는 것은 우리에게 몹시 서운한 일이지요. 더구나 조지 경이 우리와 함께 있던 지난 일요일에도 그이가 여기 없었으니 더 그래요. 나는 로바츠 씨가 자기 교회에 서 있는 것을 보는 게 참으로 좋아요. 그리고 거기 다른 성직자가 서 있는 것은 그만큼 좋지가 않아요. 패니가 그것을 꾸짖는 말로 여긴다면, 뭐—”\n\n“아, 아닙니다, 레이디 러프턴. 그렇게 말씀해 주시니 참 고맙습니다. 하지만 로바츠 씨는 조지 경이 오신다는 말을 듣기 전에 콜디코츠의 이 초대를 받아들인 것을 어찌나 미안해했는지, 그래서—”\n\n“아, 콜디코츠에는 우리가 내놓을 수 없는 큰 매력이 있다는 것을 나도 알아요.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“정말 그런 것이 아닙니다. 그이는 설교를 부탁받았어요. 그리고 해럴드 스미스 씨가—” 가엾은 패니는 일을 더 나쁘게만 만들고 있었다. 세상 물정에 밝았다면 그녀는 레이디 러프턴의 첫 꾸중에 담긴 그 작은 칭찬을 받아들이고 입을 다물었을 것이다.\n\n“아, 그렇지요. 해럴드 스미스 부부라! 그 사람들은 뿌리칠 수 없다는 것을 나도 알아요. 해럴드 스미스 부인과 프라우디 부인이 함께 빛내 주는 모임에 끼는 것을 어느 남자가 마다할 수 있겠어요—의무가 그더러 가지 말라고 요구한다 해도 말이지요?”\n\n“어머니, 그러지 마세요—” 저스티니아가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 얘야, 내가 무슨 말을 하랴? 너도 내가 거짓말을 하기를 바라지는 않겠지. 나는 해럴드 스미스 부인이 마음에 들지 않는다—적어도 그 사람에 대해 내가 듣는 말이 그렇다. 결혼한 뒤로는 그 사람을 만나 볼 복이 내게 없었으니까. 자만하는 소리일지 모르지만, 사실을 털어놓자면 나는 로바츠 씨가 콜디코츠에서 해럴드 스미스 부부와 함께 있기보다 프램리에서 우리와 함께 있는 편이 더 나을 거라고 생각한다—프라우디 부인을 덤으로 얹어 준다 해도 말이야.”\n\n거의 어두웠으므로 로바츠 부인의 얼굴에 올라오는 붉은빛은 보이지 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 이런 말을 들으면서 가슴속에 노여움이 일지 않을 만큼 무른 아내가 아니었다. 그녀는 마음속으로는 남편을 나무랄 수 있었다. 그러나 남들이 자기가 듣는 데서 그를 나무라는 것은 참을 수 없었다.\n\n“그이가 더 나을 것은 의심할 것도 없습니다.” 그녀가 말했다. “하지만 레이디 러프턴, 사람이 늘 자기가 가장 나을 곳으로만 갈 수는 없지 않습니까. 신사라면 때로는 어쩔 수 없이—”\n\n“됐어요, 됐어요, 그만하면 됐어요. 아무튼 그이가 당신을 데려가지는 않았으니, 그것으로 우리가 그를 용서해 주기로 하지요.” 그러고서 레이디 러프턴은 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다. “이렇게 된 바에는”—그러면서 그녀는 두 젊은 아내 사이에서 낮은 속삭임을 짓는 척했다—“이렇게 된 바에는 가엾은 늙은 에번 존스로 그냥 견뎌야 해요. 그이가 오늘 밤 여기 올 테니, 우리는 가서 옷을 갈아입고 그를 맞아야지요.”\n\n그리하여 그들은 갔다. 레이디 러프턴은 마음이 넉넉히 좋은 사람이어서, 로바츠 부인이 자리에 없는 남편을 감싸고 나선 것 때문에 그녀를 오히려 더 좋아하게 되었다.\n\n# 제3장. 콜디코츠\n\n콜디코츠는 프램리 코트보다 훨씬 더 위세를 부리는 집이다. 실로, 오늘날의 흔적이 아니라 그 집에 남은 옛 흔적을 들여다본다면, 이곳은 대단히 위세 있는 곳이다. 오래된 숲이 하나 있는데, 전부가 그 영지에 속한 것은 아니나 그에 딸려 있어 콜디코츠 수렵림이라 불린다. 이 숲의 한 부분은 저택 바로 뒤까지 바싹 올라와 있고, 그것만으로도 이곳에 하나의 성격과 명성을 부여한다. 콜디코츠 수렵림은—적어도 그 대부분은—세상이 다 아는 바와 같이 왕실 소유이며, 이제 이 실리 위주의 시대에 이르러 숲의 지정이 해제될 처지다. 옛날에는 나라의 절반을 가로질러 거의 실버브리지에까지 뻗은 큰 숲이었고, 그 전 구간에 걸쳐 여기저기 띄엄띄엄 그 조각들이 아직도 눈에 띈다. 그러나 남아 있는 큰 부분, 곧 몇백 년 묵어 속이 텅 빈 늙은 참나무들과 넓게 가지를 벌린 시든 너도밤나무들로 이루어진 그 부분은 콜디코츠와 어플리 두 교구에 걸쳐 서 있다. 사람들은 아직도 먼 데서 찾아와 콜디코츠의 참나무들을 보고, 두껍게 깔린 가을 낙엽 사이에서 제 발이 내는 바스락 소리를 듣는다. 그러나 머지않아 더는 오지 않을 것이다. 지나간 시대의 거인들이 밀과 순무에게 자리를 내주게 되어 있다. 무자비한 재무 대신이 오래된 인연과 시골의 아름다움을 아랑곳하지 않고 그 땅에서 돈이 나오기를 요구하는 것이니, 콜디코츠 수렵림은 지상에서 사라질 것이다.\n\n그러나 그 일부는 사워비 씨의 사유지다. 그는 여태껏 온갖 금전상의 곤경을 겪으면서도 아버지에게서 물려받은 그 몫만은 도끼와 경매장에서 용케 지켜 냈다. 콜디코츠의 저택은 큰 석조 건물로, 아마 찰스 2세 시대의 것이다. 앞뒤 양쪽 정면 모두 묵직한 두 갈래 돌계단으로 오르게 되어 있다. 집 앞쪽으로는 보리수 두 줄 사이로 길고 엄숙하고 곧은 가로수길이 뻗어 문지기 집 대문까지 이어지는데, 그 대문은 콜디코츠 마을 한가운데 서 있다. 그러나 뒤쪽으로는 창문들이 서로 다른 네 개의 전망을 향해 열리고, 그 전망들은 숲을 따라 내리 뻗는다. 초록이 열린 네 개의 승마길인데, 모두 큰 철제 대문 하나에서 한데 모이며, 그 대문이 사유지와 수렵림을 가르는 경계다. 사워비 집안은 여러 대에 걸쳐 콜디코츠 수렵림의 산림관 노릇을 해 왔으니, 왕실 숲에 대해서도 제 숲에 대해서와 거의 같은 권한을 지녀 온 셈이다. 그러나 이제 이 모든 것이 끝나게 되어 있다. 숲의 지정이 해제될 것이기 때문이다.\n\n마크 로바츠가 보리수 가로수길을 지나 현관 앞까지 마차를 몰고 올라갔을 때는 거의 어두워져 있었다. 그러나 한 해 열두 달 중 아홉 달은 무덤처럼 죽어 조용하던 그 집이 지금은 구석구석 살아 있음을 알아보기는 어렵지 않았다. 여러 창문에 불빛이 있었고, 마구간에서는 사람들 목소리가 들려왔으며, 하인들이 오락가락하고, 개들이 짖고, 현관 앞 계단 앞의 어두운 자갈밭은 수많은 마차 바퀴에 파여 있었다.\n\n“아, 로바츠 목사님이십니까요?” 마부 하나가 목사의 말 머리를 붙들고 제 모자에 손을 대며 말했다. “목사님, 별고 없으신지요?”\n\n“아주 잘 있네, 밥, 고맙군. 콜디코츠는 다 잘 있나?”\n\n“다들 기운이 펄펄합니다요, 로바츠 목사님. 지금 여기는 아주 북적북적합니다요. 주교님하고 사모님이 오늘 아침에 오셨고요.”\n\n“오—아—그렇지! 두 분이 여기 오신다고 들었네. 따님들도 왔나?”\n\n“아가씨는 한 분요. 올리비아 양이라고 부르는 것 같습니다요, 목사님.”\n\n“사워비 씨는 어떠신가?”\n\n“아주 잘 계십니다요, 목사님. 그분하고, 해럴드 스미스 씨하고, 포더길 씨하고—그 공작님 일 봐주는 분 아시죠—지금 저 마구간 마당에서 말에서 내리고 있습니다요.”\n\n“사냥에서 돌아온 거지, 밥?”\n\n“예, 목사님, 방금 막 돌아왔습니다요.” 그러고 나서 로바츠 씨는 집 안으로 들어갔고, 그의 큰 가죽 가방은 하인 아이의 어깨에 실려 뒤따랐다.\n\n보다시피 우리의 젊은 교구 사제는 콜디코츠와 무척 가까웠다. 마부가 그를 알아보고 집 안의 사람들에 대해 그에게 이야기를 늘어놓을 정도로 가까웠다. 그렇다, 그는 그 집과 가까웠다. 프램리 사람들에게 짐작하게 해 둔 것보다 훨씬 더 가까웠다. 그가 누구를 고의로 드러내어 속인 것은 아니다. 콜디코츠에 대해 거짓말을 한 적도 결코 없다. 그러나 그는 집에서 자기와 사워비가 가까운 한편이라고 자랑한 적이 한 번도 없었다. 사워비 씨와 러프턴 경이 런던에서 얼마나 자주 함께 지내는지도 그들에게 말한 적이 없었다. 그런 일로 여자들을 어지럽힐 이유가 어디 있는가? 레이디 러프턴처럼 훌륭한 부인을 성가시게 할 이유가 어디 있는가?\n\n게다가 사워비 씨는 젊은 사내라면 그와 가까이 사귈 기회를 마다하고 싶지 않을 그런 사람이었다. 그는 쉰이었고, 아마도 가장 건전한 생활을 해 온 것은 아니었다. 그러나 젊게 차려입었고 대개 보기 좋았다. 대머리였으나 이마가 훤했고 눈은 반짝반짝 젖어 있었다. 머리가 잘 돌아가는 사람이었고, 함께 있으면 즐거운 상대였으며, 마음만 그리 먹으면 언제나 기분이 좋았다. 또한 가정 교육이 높고 태생이 좋은 신사였다. 그의 조상들은 그 주에서 알려져 온 지가—주변의 농부들이 자랑하기로는—울라손의 손 집안이나 어쩌면 그레셤스베리의 그레셤 집안을 빼면 그 주의 다른 어느 지주보다도 오래되었고, 코시 성의 드 코시 집안보다야 훨씬 오래되었다. 옴니엄 공작으로 치면, 그는 상대적으로 말해서 새 사람이었다.\n\n또한 그는 하원 의원이었고, 권력을 쥔 사람들 몇과 앞으로 쥘지도 모르는 사람들과 벗이었으며, 자기가 말하는 사안을 아는 자로서 세상 이야기를 할 줄 아는 사람이었다. 더욱이 다른 때에 그의 사는 방식이 어떠하든, 성직자 앞에 있을 때에는 성직자의 취향에 거슬리는 짓을 좀처럼 하지 않았다. 욕설을 하지도 않았고, 자기 악덕을 상 위에 올려놓지도 않았고, 교회의 신앙을 비웃지도 않았다. 그 자신이 교회의 사람이 아니라면, 적어도 그런 사람들과 어울려 지내는 법은 알았다.\n\n우리의 교구 사제 같은 이가 어떻게 사워비 씨와의 교분을 즐기지 않을 수 있었겠는가? 레이디 러프턴 같은 부인이야 그를 코웃음 치며 볼 수도 있으리라고 그는 스스로에게 말했다. 한 해에 열 달을 프램리 코트에서 지내고, 그 열 달 동안은 물론이거니와 런던에서 지내는 나머지 두 달 동안에도 제 무리 밖의 사람은 아무도 만나지 않는 레이디 러프턴이야 그럴 수 있으리라고. 여자들은 그런 것을 이해하지 못한다고 교구 사제는 스스로에게 말했다. 착하고 다정하고 분별 있고 총명한 자기 아내조차도, 세상에 나가 사는 남자는 온갖 종류의 남자들을 만나야 한다는 것, 그리고 이런 시대에 성직자가 은자로 사는 것은 통하지 않는다는 것을 이해하지 못한다고.\n\n콜디코츠에 가서 사워비 씨와의 교분을 더 깊게 하는 일을 두고 제 양심의 법정 앞에서 스스로를 변호해야 하는 처지에 놓였을 때, 마크 로바츠가 논한 논리는 이러했다. 그는 사워비 씨가 위험한 사람임을 알고 있었다. 그가 빚에 목까지 잠겨 있음을, 그리고 이미 젊은 러프턴 경을 금전상의 곤경에 끌어들여 놓았음을 알고 있었다. 그리스도의 병사 가운데 하나로서, 다른 품새의 벗들을 찾아보는 편이 자기에게 좋을 것이라고 그의 양심이 일러 주기는 했다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 콜디코츠로 갔다. 자기 자신이 정말로 만족스러운 것은 아니었으나, 왜 만족해도 되는지에 대한 논거를 아주 많이 스스로에게 되풀이하면서.\n\n그는 곧바로 응접실로 안내되었고, 거기서 해럴드 스미스 부인과, 프라우디 부인 및 프라우디 양과, 그리고 전에 한 번도 본 적 없는 한 부인을 만났다. 그 부인의 이름은 처음에는 거명되는 것을 듣지 못했다.\n\n“로바츠 씨 아니세요?” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 그를 맞으러 일어나며, 실은 알고 있으면서도 모르는 척하는 것을 어둠의 장막 아래 감추고 말했다. “이런 날에 바셋주의 길을 이십사 마일이나 마차로 달려오셔서, 우리의 자그마한 곤경을 도와주시겠다는 건가요? 어쨌든 감사만은 약속드릴 수 있어요.”\n\n그러고서 교구 사제는 프라우디 부인과, 교구 사제가 자기 주교의 부인에게 응당 갖추어야 할 그 공손한 태도로 악수를 나누었다. 프라우디 부인은 주교의 부인이 교구 사제에게 마땅히 보여야 할 저 미소 띤 겸양을 다하여 인사를 되받았다. 프라우디 양은 그만큼 정중하지 않았다. 로바츠 씨가 아직 미혼이었다면 그녀도 달콤하게 웃어 줄 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 성직자들에게 미소를 지어 보이는 일을 너무 오래 해 온 터라, 아내 있는 교구 목사에게 그것을 낭비할 수는 없었다.\n\n“그런데 제가 도와드려야 할 곤경이란 무엇입니까, 스미스 부인?”\n\n“여기 신사분이 여섯인가 일곱인가 계신데요, 로바츠 씨, 이분들은 늘 아침 식사 전에 사냥을 나가서, 저녁 식사 뒤가 되도록—이렇게 말하려고 했어요—돌아오지 않아요. 정말 그랬으면 좋겠어요. 그러면 우리가 기다릴 일이 없을 테니까요.”\n\n“서플하우스 씨는 빼고요.” 낯모를 부인이 큰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“그리고 그분은 대개 서고에 갇혀서 논설을 쓰고 계시죠.”\n\n“다른 분들처럼 목이라도 부러뜨려 보려고 애쓰는 편이 그분한테는 더 나은 일거리일 텐데요.” 낯모를 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그런데 그분은 절대로 성공하지 못하실 거예요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. “하지만 로바츠 씨, 어쩌면 당신도 나머지 분들만큼 못됐을지 모르지요. 어쩌면 당신도 내일 사냥을 나가실지 모르고요.”\n\n“아니, 스미스 부인!” 프라우디 부인이 가벼운 질책과 절제된 경악이 담긴 어조로 말했다.\n\n“아! 내가 잊었네요. 아니지요, 물론 당신은 사냥을 나가지 않으실 거예요, 로바츠 씨. 다만 나갈 수 있으면 좋겠다고 바라기만 하시겠지요.”\n\n“왜 못 나가시는데요?” 목소리 큰 부인이 말했다.\n\n“어머, 던스터블 양! 주교님과 같은 집에 머무는 동안에 성직자가 사냥을 한다니요? 체면을 생각하셔야지요!”\n\n“오—아! 주교님이 좋아하지 않으실 거란 말이죠? 어디 좀 말씀해 보세요, 선생님. 정말로 사냥을 나가시면 주교님이 당신을 어떻게 하실까요?”\n\n“그때 주교님의 심기에 달렸겠지요, 부인.” 로바츠 씨가 말했다. “심기가 아주 험하시다면, 어쩌면 주교관 문 앞에서 제 목을 베게 하실지도 모르겠습니다.”\n\n프라우디 부인은 의자에 앉은 몸을 곧추세워, 이런 투의 대화가 마음에 들지 않는다는 것을 드러내었다. 그리고 프라우디 양은 제 책에 눈을 맹렬히 붙여, 던스터블 양이든 그 대화든 자기가 눈길을 줄 만한 것이 아님을 드러내었다.\n\n“이 신사분들이 오늘 밤에는 목을 부러뜨릴 뜻이 없으시다면,” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. “우리한테 그걸 알려 주셨으면 좋겠어요. 벌써 여섯 시 반이니까요.”\n\n그러자 로바츠 씨가, 자기가 문에 들어설 때 사워비 씨와 다른 사냥꾼들이 마구간 마당에 있었으므로 오늘은 그런 참변을 기대할 수 없다는 뜻을 그들에게 알려 주었다.\n\n“그럼 부인들, 우리도 옷을 갈아입는 게 좋겠어요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. 그러나 그녀가 문을 향해 움직이는데 그 문이 열리며, 키가 작은 신사 한 분이 느리고 조용한 걸음으로 방에 들어섰다. 그러나 로바츠 씨의 눈으로는 어스름 속에서 아직 그가 누구인지 가려낼 수 없었다. “아! 주교님, 오셨어요?” 스미스 부인이 말했다. “여기 주교님 교구의 빛나는 별 한 분이 계세요.” 그러자 주교는 어둠 속을 더듬어 교구 사제에게 다가와 그의 손을 다정하게 잡고 흔들었다. “콜디코츠에서 로바츠 씨를 만나 아주 기쁘다”고 그는 말했다—“참으로 기쁘다. 다음 일요일에 파푸아 선교를 위해 설교를 하기로 되어 있지 않은가? 아! 그러니까 자기, 곧 주교가 그렇게 들었다. 좋은 사업이고, 훌륭한 사업이다.” 그러고서 프라우디 박사는 자기가 콜디코츠에 남아 그 설교를 듣지 못하는 것이 무척 애석하다는 뜻을 말했다. 자기 주교가 사워비 씨와의 교분을 두고 자기를 나쁘게 여기지 않는 것이 분명했다. 그러나 그러면서도 그는 제 마음속으로, 자기가 주교의 의견을 그리 대수롭게 여기지 않는다고 느꼈다.\n\n“아, 로바츠, 만나서 정말 반가워요.” 저녁 식사 전에 응접실 깔개 위에서 마주쳤을 때 사워비 씨가 말했다. “해럴드 스미스는 알지요? 그럼, 물론 알겠지. 그러면 또 누가 있나? 아! 서플하우스. 서플하우스 씨, 제 친구 로바츠 씨를 소개하겠습니다. 우리가 기독교로 이끌려는 저 가엾은 파푸아인들을 위해 다음 일요일에 당신 주머니에서 5파운드 지폐를 끄집어낼 사람이 바로 이 사람이오. 그야 해럴드 스미스가 토요일 강연에서 그 일을 대번에 끝장내 버리지만 않는다면 말이지만. 그리고 로바츠, 주교님은 물론 봤겠지요.” 이 말은 속삭이듯 했다. “주교가 된다는 건 좋은 일이지요? 나한테 당신 몫의 절반만이라도 기회가 있었으면 좋겠어요. 그런데 여보게, 내가 이런 실수를 했군. 프라우디 양 몫으로 총각 목사를 하나도 구해 두지 못했어요. 나를 좀 도와서 그녀를 식탁으로 데려가 줘야겠어요.” 그러고서 큰 징이 울렸고, 그들은 둘씩 짝을 지어 나갔다.\n\n식탁에서 마크는 프라우디 양과, 던스터블 양이라고 불리는 것을 들은 그 부인 사이에 앉게 되었다. 앞의 사람은 그가 그리 좋아하지 않는 상대였고, 주인의 청이 있었음에도 그녀를 위해 총각 목사 노릇을 해 줄 마음은 들지 않았다. 뒤의 부인과는 식사 동안 기꺼이 이야기를 나누고 싶었으나, 식탁의 다른 모든 사람이 하나같이 같은 일에 열심인 듯했다. 그녀는 젊지도, 아름답지도, 유별나게 귀부인답지도 않았다. 그런데도 서플하우스 씨의 시샘을 돋우었음이 틀림없을 만한 인기를 누리는 듯했고, 그 인기는 프라우디 부인의 취향에도 결코 온전히 맞지 않는 것이었다—하기야 프라우디 부인도 남들만큼이나 그녀를 떠받들었다. 그리하여 우리의 성직자는 그 부인의 관심 가운데 하찮은 몫밖에 얻어 낼 수 없었다.\n\n“주교님,” 그녀가 식탁 건너로 말을 건넸다. “저희가 온종일 주교님이 안 계셔서 얼마나 서운했는지요! 말 한마디 붙일 사람이 이 세상에 하나도 없었어요.”\n\n“아, 던스터블 양, 내가 그걸 알았더라면— 그러나 나는 참으로 좀 중요한 일에 붙들려 있었소.”\n\n“저는 중요한 일이라는 걸 믿지 않아요. 스미스 부인, 부인은 믿으세요?”\n\n“내가 안 믿겠어요?” 스미스 부인이 말했다. “해럴드 스미스 씨와 결혼해서 한 주만 살아 보시면, 그건 믿게 될 거예요.”\n\n“그럴까요? 제 믿음을 키울 그런 기회를 가질 수 없다니 참 아쉬운 일이네요! 하지만 서플하우스 씨도 일하는 분이시죠. 사람들이 그러던데요.” 그러면서 그녀는 오른쪽 옆자리 사람을 향해 돌아앉았다.\n\n“나를 해럴드 스미스에 견줄 수야 없지요.” 그가 말했다. “그러나 주교님과는 어쩌면 맞먹을지도 모르겠소.”\n\n“남자가 일을 하려고 자리에 앉으면 대체 무엇을 하나요? 어떻게 시작하죠? 도구가 뭐예요? 우선은 압지 한 첩쯤 있어야 하나요?”\n\n“그건 업종에 달렸다고 해야겠지요. 구두장이는 실에 밀을 먹이는 것으로 시작하오.”\n\n“그럼 해럴드 스미스 씨는요?”\n\n“대개 어제 자 숫자를 합쳐 보는 것으로 시작하겠지요. 그게 아니면 붉은 끈 뭉치를 풀어 놓는 것으로. 꼬리표 잘 달린 서류와 통계상의 사실이 그이의 장기니까.”\n\n“그럼 주교는 무엇을 하죠? 그건 말해 주실 수 있어요?”\n\n“소화 기관의 상태에 따라 자기 성직자들에게 축복을 내려보내거나 호통을 내려보내오. 그러나 그 모든 것은 프라우디 부인이 아주 정확하게 설명해 줄 수 있을 거요.”\n\n“그럴까요? 무슨 말씀인지는 알겠지만, 저는 한마디도 믿지 않아요. 주교님은 자기 일을 자기가 처리하세요. 당신이나 해럴드 스미스 씨만큼은 충분히요.”\n\n“내가 말이오, 던스터블 양?”\n\n“그래요, 당신이요.”\n\n“그러나 나에게는 안타깝게도 그 일을 대신 처리해 줄 아내가 없소.”\n\n“그러면 아내가 있는 분들을 비웃지 마셔야죠. 당신도 결혼하고 나면 어떤 처지가 될지 모르는 일이니까요.”\n\n서플하우스 씨는 던스터블 양과 짝을 이룸으로써 자기에게 닥칠 그 방면의 어떤 위험이든 기꺼이 무릅쓰겠다며 그럴싸한 말을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그러나 그가 그 말을 절반쯤 하기도 전에 그녀는 그에게 등을 돌리고, 마크 로바츠와 이야기를 시작해 버렸다.\n\n“교구에 일이 많으세요, 로바츠 씨?” 그녀가 물었다. 그런데 마크는 그녀가 자기 이름을 안다는 것도, 자기에게 교구가 있다는 사실을 안다는 것도 몰랐던 터라 그 질문에 다소 놀랐다. 게다가 그녀가 주교와 주교의 일을 두고 말하던 어조가 그의 마음에 그리 들지 않았다. 그래서 그녀와 더 알고 지내고 싶은 마음이 조금 식었고, 그녀의 질문에 그리 열을 내어 답할 준비가 되어 있지 않았다.\n\n“교구 성직자라면 누구나 하려고만 하면 일이 넘칩니다.”\n\n“아, 그거예요. 그렇지 않아요, 로바츠 씨? 하려고만 하면. 하는 분이 많지요. 제가 아는 분들 가운데도 많고요. 그런데 그분들이 어떤 결과를 얻는지 보세요. 그러나 소홀히 하는 분도 많아요. 그리고 그분들이 어떤 결과를 얻는지도 보세요. 저는 아내와 자식이 있고 넉넉한 수입이 있는 교구 성직자의 삶이야말로 사람이 누릴 수 있는 가장 행복한 삶이어야 한다고 생각해요.”\n\n“저도 그렇게 생각합니다.” 마크 로바츠는 그렇게 말하면서, 그러한 복에서 나오는 넉넉함이 자기를 모든 점에서 만족시켰던가를 스스로에게 물었다. 던스터블 양이 말한 그 모든 것을 그는 가지고 있었다. 그런데도 그는 며칠 전에 아내에게, 해럴드 스미스처럼 떠오르는 정치가와의 교분을 소홀히 할 처지는 못 된다고 말했던 것이다.\n\n“제가 못마땅해하는 건 이거예요.” 던스터블 양이 말을 이었다. “우리는 성직자에게 제 본분을 다하라고 하면서, 넉넉한 수입을 주지 않는다는 거요—수입이랄 것을 거의 하나도 주지 않지요. 교육받은 신사가 딸린 식구를 두고서 제 인생의 절반을, 어쩌면 평생을, 연 70파운드라는 쥐꼬리를 받고 일하도록 만들어진다는 게 창피한 일이 아닌가요?”\n\n마크는 그것이 창피한 일이라고 말하고서, 에번 존스 씨와 그의 딸을 생각했다.—그리고 자기 자신의 값과, 자기 자신의 집과, 자기 자신의 연 900파운드도 생각했다.\n\n“게다가 성직자분들은 어찌나 오만하신지—고상하게 말하자면 귀족적이라고 해야 할 줄은 알아요—보통의, 평범한 가난한 사람들의 돈은 받으려고 하지 않으세요. 땅과 기부 재산에서, 십일조와 교회 재산에서 받으셔야 하죠. 변호사나 의사가 하듯이 벌이만큼 일한다는 데에는 스스로를 들여놓을 수가 없으신 거예요. 그런 수모를 당하기보다는 부목사들이 굶어 죽는 편이 낫다는 거죠.”\n\n“그건 긴 이야기입니다, 던스터블 양.”\n\n“아주 긴 이야기죠. 그리고 그 말은 제가 더는 그 이야기를 하지 말라는 뜻이고요.”\n\n“꼭 그런 뜻은 아니었습니다.”\n\n“아니에요! 그런 뜻이셨어요, 로바츠 씨. 그런 눈치는 제가 받으면 알아채요. 성직자분들은 그런 긴 이야기는 아무도 대꾸할 수 없는 설교 시간을 위해 남겨 두기를 좋아하시죠. 자, 제가 이 세상에서 무엇이든 하나 마음속으로 간절히 바라는 게 있다면, 그건 설교단에 올라가서 설교를 한번 해 보는 거예요.”\n\n“그 식욕이 한 번 채워진 뒤에 얼마나 빨리 물려 버릴지는 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다.”\n\n“그건 사람들이 제 말을 듣게 만들 수 있느냐에 달렸겠죠. 스퍼전 씨는 물리지 않으신 것 같은데요.” 그러고서 그녀의 관심은 사워비 씨의 어떤 질문에 이끌려 딴 데로 갔고, 마크 로바츠는 프라우디 양에게 말을 붙여야 하는 처지가 되었다. 그러나 프라우디 양은 고마워하지 않았고, 애쓴 값으로 그에게 준 것은 외마디 대답 정도가 거의 다였다.\n\n“해럴드 스미스가 그 섬사람들에 관해 우리한테 강연을 하기로 한 건 물론 알고 있지요?” 저녁 식사 뒤에 포도주를 놓고 난롯가에 둘러앉았을 때 사워비 씨가 그에게 말했다. 마크는 그렇게 들었으며 청중의 하나가 되는 것이 기쁠 것이라고 말했다.\n\n“그야 해야 할 일이지요. 그 사람은 그다음 날 당신 설교를 들으러 갈 테니까요—적어도 듣는 척은 할 테고, 당신이 그 사람한테 해 줄 것도 그만큼일 테니까. 그건 지독한 골칫거리일 겁니다—강연 말이오, 설교가 아니라.” 그러고서 그는 벗의 귀에 아주 낮게 말했다. “해럴드 스미스가 보르네오에 관해 두 시간 떠드는 것을 들으러, 해 저문 뒤에 십 마일을 마차로 가고 십 마일을 되돌아와야 한다고 생각해 보세요! 그래도 해야 하는 일이니까요.”\n\n“꽤 흥미로울 것 같은데요.”\n\n“여보게, 당신은 이런 일을 나만큼 겪어 보지 않았지요. 그러나 그 사람은 그걸 하는 게 옳아요. 그게 그 사람의 밥벌이 길이니까. 그리고 사람이 어떤 일을 시작했으면 계속해 나가야 하는 법이지요. 그런데 러프턴은 이 시간에 어디 있소?”\n\n“마지막으로 소식을 들었을 때는 스코틀랜드에 있었는데, 지금은 아마 멜턴에 있을 겁니다.”\n\n“제 고향 주에서 사냥을 하지 않다니 그 사람 참 지독하게 야박하군. 강연에 가는 골칫거리며 이웃들에게 대접을 차려 내는 골칫거리를 다 피해 버리니, 그래서 우리를 그렇게 대하는 거지요. 제 본분이라는 걸 도무지 모르지 않소?”\n\n“그건 다 레이디 러프턴이 하시니까요.”\n\n“나한테도 그 일을 대신 해 줄 어머니 사워비 부인이 하나 있었으면 좋겠어요. 하지만 러프턴에게는 챙겨야 할 선거구민이 없으니—운 좋은 놈이지! 그런데 말이오, 그 사람이 옥스퍼드셔에 떨어져 있는 땅뙈기를 팔겠다는 이야기를 당신한테 했소? 러프턴 소유에 딸린 것이면서 딸리지 않은 것 같은 땅인데. 내 생각에는 값보다 골치를 더 안기는 땅이오.”\n\n러프턴 경은 그 매각에 관해 마크에게 이야기한 바 있었고, 자기와 사워비 씨 사이의 어떤 금전 거래 때문에 그런 희생이 절대로 불가피하다고 설명한 바 있었다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴이 모르는 채로 그 일을 마무리하기는 불가능하다는 것이 드러났고, 그 아들은 로바츠 씨에게 마님께 그 사실을 알리는 것뿐 아니라 그녀를 설득하고 그 노여움을 달래는 일까지 맡겨 두었다. 이 소임을 그는 아직 실행에 옮겨 보려는 시도조차 하지 않았고, 이번 콜디코츠 방문이 그 일을 수월하게 하는 데 별 도움이 되지 않을 것은 뻔한 일이었다.\n\n“그것은 해 아래 가장 훌륭한 섬들이오.” 해럴드 스미스가 주교에게 말했다.\n\n“정말 그렇습니까!” 주교가 눈을 크게 뜨고 몹시 흥미롭다는 표정을 지으며 말했다.\n\n“그리고 가장 총명한 사람들이오.”\n\n“이런!” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“그들에게 필요한 것은 지도와 격려와 가르침뿐이니—”\n\n“그리고 기독교지요.” 주교가 거들었다.\n\n“그리고 물론 기독교요.” 스미스 씨가 자기가 교회의 고위 인사에게 말하고 있음을 떠올리고서 말했다. 그런 사람들은 맞춰 주는 것이 좋다고 스미스 씨는 생각했다. 그러나 기독교는 일요일 설교에서 할 일이고, 자기 일의 몫은 아니었다.\n\n“그러면 그들에게 어떻게 착수하실 작정이오?” 서플하우스 씨가 물었다. 어려운 점을 들이대는 것이 평생의 일거리였던 사람이다.\n\n“그들에게 착수한다—오—왜—그들에게 착수하는 것은 아주 쉬운 일이오. 어려운 것은 돈이 다 떨어진 뒤에 그들과 함께 계속 나아가는 일이지. 우리는 그들에게 문명의 이로움을 설명하는 것으로 시작할 것이오.”\n\n“기막힌 계획이오!” 서플하우스 씨가 말했다. “그런데 그 일에 어떻게 손을 대오, 스미스?”\n\n“그 일에 어떻게 손을 대느냐고? 오스트레일리아와 아메리카에서는 그 일에 어떻게 손을 댔소? 비판하기야 아주 쉽지. 그러나 그런 일에서 중요한 것은 수레바퀴에 어깨를 대고 미는 것이오.”\n\n“오스트레일리아에는 우리가 중죄인들을 보냈고,” 서플하우스가 말했다. “그자들이 우리 대신 그 일을 시작해 주었소. 그리고 아메리카로 말하면, 우리는 그 사람들을 문명화하는 대신 몰살했지.”\n\n“우리가 인도의 주민들을 몰살하지는 않았소.” 해럴드 스미스가 화를 내며 말했다.\n\n“그들을 기독교로 이끌려고 하지도 않았고. 주교님이 당신의 그 섬사람들을 두고 그토록 지당하게 바라시는 것처럼 말이오.”\n\n“서플하우스, 당신은 공정하지 않소.” 사워비 씨가 말했다. “해럴드 스미스에게도, 우리에게도 말이오.—당신은 저 사람에게 강연을 미리 연습하게 만들고 있는데 그건 저 사람에게 나쁜 일이고, 우리에게 그 연습을 듣게 만들고 있는데 그건 우리에게 나쁜 일이오.”\n\n“서플하우스는 영국의 지혜를 독차지하는 패거리에 속해 있소.” 해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “적어도 저들 생각으로는 그렇다는 거지. 그런데 그 무리의 가장 나쁜 점은 사설을 읊어 대는 버릇이 들었다는 것이오.”\n\n“남을 이끌지 못하는 글을 읊어 대는 것보다야 낫지요.” 서플하우스 씨가 말했다. “일류 관리라는 분들 가운데 더러 그런 이가 있소.”\n\n“다음 주에 공작님 댁에서 뵙게 되겠소, 로바츠 씨?” 그들이 응접실로 들어간 지 얼마 되지 않아 주교가 그에게 말했다.\n\n공작의 댁에서 만난다니!—레이디 러프턴이 여기던 대로라면 각하는 바셋주 인류의 공인된 원수였다! 공작의 댁에 간다는 생각은 우리 주인공의 머릿속에 한 번도 들어온 적이 없었다. 공작이 누구를 접대할 참이라는 것도 그는 알지 못했다.\n\n“아닙니다, 주교님. 그럴 일은 없을 것 같습니다. 실은 저는 각하와 아무 안면이 없습니다.”\n\n“오—아! 나는 몰랐소. 사워비 씨가 가고, 해럴드 스미스 부부도 가고, 내가 알기로는 서플하우스 씨도 가기에 말이오. 공작은 훌륭한 사람이오.—그러니까, 주의 온갖 이해에 관련해서는 그렇다는 말이오.” 주교는 그 총각 각하의 도덕적 품성이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 것은 아님을 떠올리고서 그렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n그러고서 주교님은 프램리의 교회 사정에 관해 몇 가지 질문을 하기 시작했는데, 그 안에는 프램리 코트에 대한 약간의 관심도 섞여 있었다. 그러다가 그는 다소 날카로운 목소리에 말이 끊겼고, 곧바로 그 목소리에 응했다.\n\n“주교.” 다소 날카로운 그 목소리가 말했다. 그러자 주교는 방을 가로질러 종종걸음으로 건너가, 자기 아내가 앉아 있는 소파의 등 뒤에 섰다. “던스터블 양이 공작님 댁을 떠난 뒤에 우리 집에 이틀쯤 와 주실 수 있을 것 같다고 하시오.”\n\n“무엇보다도 기쁜 일이오.” 주교가 그날의 지배자인 그 부인에게 허리를 깊이 숙이며 말했다. 모든 사람이 알아 둘 일이거니와, 던스터블 양은 그 이름으로 알려진 굴지의 상속녀였다.\n\n“프라우디 부인이 아주 친절하게도, 제 푸들이며 앵무새며 아끼는 노파까지 함께 받아 주시겠다고 하시네요.”\n\n“던스터블 양의 수행하는 이들이라면 누구든 넉넉히 자리가 있다고 내가 말씀드렸소.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그리고 우리에게 아무 수고도 되지 않는다고요.”\n\n“‘우리가 즐거이 하는 수고는 아픔을 고쳐 주지요.’” 늠름한 주교가 허리를 깊이 숙이고 제 가슴에 손을 얹으며 말했다.\n\n그러는 동안 포더길 씨가 마크 로바츠를 붙잡았다. 포더길 씨는 신사였고 그 주의 치안 판사였으나, 옴니엄 공작 영지의 살림을 맡아 보는 사람의 자리를 차지하고 있었다. 정확히 공작의 대리인은 아니었다. 다시 말해 지대를 받아들이지는 않았다. 그러나 공작을 위해 “관리”를 했고, 사람들을 만났고, 주 안을 돌아다녔고, 편지를 썼고, 선거 운동의 이해를 뒷받침했고, 공작이 몸소 나서기에 너무 번거로운 인기 관리 노릇도 해 주었으니, 실로 값을 매길 수 없는 사람이었다. 서바셋주 사람들은 포더길 씨가 없다면 공작이 대체 무엇을 어떻게 할지 도무지 모르겠다고 흔히 말했다. 과연 포더길 씨는 공작에게 요긴한 사람이었다.\n\n“로바츠 씨,” 그가 말했다. “뵙게 되어 정말 반갑습니다—정말로 반갑습니다. 우리 친구 사워비에게서 선생 말씀을 자주 들었지요.”\n\n마크는 고개를 숙이고, 포더길 씨와 알게 되는 영광을 얻어 기쁘다고 말했다.\n\n“옴니엄 공작 각하께서 저에게 소임을 맡기셨습니다.” 포더길 씨가 말을 이었다. “다음 주에 개서럼 성에서 열리는 각하의 모임에 선생께서 함께해 주시면 각하께서 얼마나 기뻐하실지 전해 달라고요. 주교님도 오실 것이고, 실은 지금 여기 모인 이들 거의 전부가 옵니다. 선생께서 콜디코츠에 오신다는 말을 들으셨을 때 각하께서 직접 편지를 쓰려 하셨지만, 그때는 일이 아직 다 정해지지 않았던 터라, 각하께서 저에게 남겨 두시고 선생을 자기 집에서 알게 되면 얼마나 기쁠지 대신 말씀드리라 하셨지요. 사워비에게도 말해 두었는데,” 포더길 씨가 말을 이었다. “그도 선생이 우리와 함께해 주시기를 몹시 바라고 있습니다.”\n\n이런 제안을 받고서 마크는 자기 얼굴이 붉어지는 것을 느꼈다. 그가 원래 속해 있는 그 주의 무리는—그 자신과 그의 아내, 그리고 그를 행복하고 떳떳하게 만들어 주는 그 모든 것은—옴니엄 공작을 경악과 공포로 바라보았다. 그런데 이제 그가 공작의 집으로 오라는 초대를 실제로 받은 것이다! 그가 공작의 벗들 가운데 하나로 헤아려지도록 하겠다는 제안이 그에게 온 것이다!\n\n그리고 그 제안이 자기에게 온 것이 한편으로는 안타까웠으나, 다른 한편으로는 그것이 자랑스러웠다. 직업이 무엇이든, 공작에게서 우정의 제안을 받고서 조금도 우쭐하지 않을 젊은 남자는 세상에 흔하지 않다. 게다가 마크는 여기까지 올라온 만큼은 훌륭한 사람들을 알아서 올라온 것이었고, 더 높이 올라가고 싶은 야심이 분명히 있었다. 그를 깎아내려 귀인 쫓아다니는 자라고 부르지는 않겠다. 그러나 성직자의 발에 가장 유쾌한 길은 세상의 위대한 이들이 밟는 길이라는 느낌을 그가 지녔던 것은 틀림없다.\n\n그렇지만 그 자리에서는 그는 공작의 초대를 사양했다. 대단히 과분한 말씀이지만 교구의 본분 때문에 콜디코츠에서 곧장 프램리로 돌아가야 한다고 그는 말했다.\n\n“오늘 밤에 저에게 답을 주실 필요는 없습니다.” 포더길 씨가 말했다. “이번 주가 지나기 전에 사워비와 주교님과 함께 그 이야기를 해 보지요. 이런 말씀을 드려도 된다면, 로바츠 씨, 각하를 알게 될 이런 기회를 그냥 흘려보내신다면 그건 천 번 아까운 일일 겁니다.”\n\n마크가 잠자리에 들 때에도 그의 마음은 여전히 공작의 댁에 가지 않겠다고 굳어져 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 가지 않는 것이 아깝다는 느낌을 지녔다. 결국, 그가 레이디 러프턴에게 모든 일에서 복종해야 할 이유가 있는가?\n\n# 제4장. 양심에 걸리는 일\n\n못된 것을 갈망하는 일은 의심할 여지 없이 아주 나쁘다. 그러나 그럼에도 우리는 모두 그렇게 한다. 못된 것을 탐내는 마음이야말로 아담의 타락으로 우리가 곤두박질쳐 떨어진 그 악의 본질이라고 말할 수도 있다. 우리가 모두 죄인이라고 고백할 때, 우리는 우리 모두가 못된 것을 갈망한다고 고백하는 것이다.\n\n그리고 야망은 큰 악덕이다 — 오래전에 안토니우스가 우리에게 일러 주었듯이 — 그 사람의 야망이 남의 영달이 아니라 제 자신의 영달을 향한 것이라면, 그것은 의심할 여지 없이 큰 악덕이다. 그러나 그렇다면 우리 가운데 이 악덕의 방식으로 야망을 품지 않은 자가 몇이나 되는가?\n\n그리고 지체 높은 사람들과 알고 지내려는 욕망보다 더 천한 것은 없다 — 신분이 높은 사람들이라고 해야 하겠다. 작위를 사냥하고 재물을 숭배하는 짓보다 더 나쁜 것은 없다. 우리는 모두 이것을 알고 있고, 살아가는 하루하루 그렇게 말한다. 그러나 파크 레인의 사교계로 드는 길이 우리에게 열려 있고 베드퍼드 로의 사교계로 드는 길 또한 열려 있다고 가정한다면, 재물과 작위를 숭배하는 것이 하도 천한 일이라 하여 베드퍼드 로를 택할 사람이 우리 가운데 몇이나 되겠는가?\n\n내가 이런 다소 진부한 언설로 끌려 들어온 것은, 콜디코츠에 도착한 이튿날 아침 마크 로바츠 목사가 눈을 뜰 때 그가 놓여 있던 그 마음의 상태에 대하여 무언가 변명 같은 것을 앞세워 둘 필요가 있기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 성직자라는 사실이 그에게 부당하게 불리하도록 작용하지는 않기를 바란다. 성직자도 다른 사람들과 똑같은 정념에 지배된다. 그리고 내가 보는 한, 이런 방면으로든 저런 방면으로든 그 정념에 굴복하는 일도 거의 같은 빈도로 있다. 교회법의 규칙에 따르면 모든 성직자는 주교직에 대하여 개인적으로는 마음이 끌리지 않아야 마땅하지만, 그러나 그런 개인적인 무심함이 대체로 아주 강하다고는 우리는 믿지 않는다.\n\n그날 아침 잠에서 깬 마크의 첫 생각은 포더길 씨의 초대로 날아갔다. 공작은 특별히 전갈을 보내어, 그 자신, 곧 공작이 그와, 곧 목사와 알게 되는 것을 얼마나 각별히 기쁘게 여기는지를 전해 왔던 것이다! 그 전갈 가운데 얼마만큼이 포더길 씨 자신의 손에서 만들어진 것인지는 마크 로바츠가 헤아려 보지 않았다.\n\n다른 젊은 성직자들이 부목사 자리나 생각하기 시작할 나이에 그는 성직록을 얻었고, 그것도 중년의 목사들이 꿈속에서나 노년의 낙원으로 여길 만한 성직록을 얻었다. 물론 그는 이 모든 좋은 것들이 자기 자신의 남다른 공덕의 결과라고 생각했다. 물론 그는 자기가 다른 목사들과는 다르다고 느꼈다. 지체 높은 사람들과 가까이 지내기에 천성이 더 알맞고, 더 도회적이고, 더 세련되고, 요즘 성직자에게 어울리는 유복한 재간을 더 넉넉히 타고났다고. 그는 레이디 러프턴이 자기에게 해 준 일에 감사하고 있었다. 그러나 아마 마땅히 감사해야 할 만큼은 아니었다.\n\n어쨌든 그는 레이디 러프턴의 하인이 아니었고, 그녀에게 매여 사는 사람조차 아니었다. 그만한 말은 그가 여러 번 스스로에게 되뇌었고, 아내에게까지 그런 뜻을 슬쩍 내비칠 정도로 나아간 바 있었다. 교구 사제로서의 이력에서 그는 대부분의 일에 대하여 자기 행동의 심판자여야 했다. 그리고 많은 일에서는 후원자인 그 부인의 행동을 심판하는 것 또한 그의 의무였다. 레이디 러프턴이 그를 그 성직록에 앉혔다는 사실이 그녀를 그의 행동에 대한 온당한 심판자로 만들어 줄 수는 결코 없었다. 이 말을 그는 자주 스스로에게 했다. 그리고 레이디 러프턴이 분명히 그런 심판석을 탐내고 있다는 말도 그만큼 자주 했다.\n\n대체로 총리와 관가의 높은 분들은 누구를 주교와 수석 사제로 만드는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼던가? 대개 성직자의 직무를 유능하게 수행할 수 있음을 보여 준 데다 상류 사교계에서도 편안하게 제자리를 잡을 수 있는 성직자들이 아니었던가? 그는 프램리에서 분명히 아주 잘 지내고 있었다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴을 무서운 도깨비로 여기도록 자신을 내버려 둔다면 프램리 너머의 무엇도 결코 바랄 수 없을 것이었다. 레이디 러프턴과 그녀의 편견을 문제 밖으로 밀어 둔다면, 그가 공작의 초대를 받아들여서는 안 될 이유가 있는가? 그런 이유가 있다고는 그로서는 볼 수 없었다. 이런 문제에 대하여 자기보다 더 나은 심판자가 있을 수 있다면 그것은 그의 주교여야 했다. 그리고 주교가 그더러 개서럼 성에 가기를 바란다는 것은 분명했다.\n\n그 문제는 아직 그에게 열린 채로 남아 있었다. 포더길 씨가 특별히 그렇게 설명해 두었다. 그러므로 마지막 결정은 아직 그 자신의 손안에 있었다. 그런 방문에는 돈이 좀 들 것이었다. 큰 집에 머무는 데 비용이 들지 않을 수 없음을 그는 알고 있었다. 그리고 수입이 넉넉한데도 그는 수중에 현금이 그리 넉넉하지 않았다. 그해에 그는 러프턴 경과 함께 스코틀랜드에 다녀왔다. 아마 집으로 돌아가는 편이 더 신중한 처사일지도 몰랐다.\n\n그러나 그때 한 가지 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다. 어느 정도는 자기가 그 안에서 살고 있다고 느끼는 프램리의 예속을 한 사람의 남자로서 또 한 사람의 사제로서 깨뜨리는 것이 마땅하다는 생각이었다. 그가 이 초대를 사양하려 하는 것은 레이디 러프턴이 두려워서가 아닌가? 그렇다면 그것이 그를 움직여야 할 동기인가? 그런 감정을 스스로 떨어내는 것이 그의 책무였다. 그리고 그런 마음가짐으로 그는 일어나 옷을 입었다.\n\n그날도 사냥이 있었다. 그리고 사냥개들이 콜디코츠 근처에서 모여 체이스 가장자리에 놓인 덤불숲을 뒤지기로 되어 있었으므로, 부인들은 마차를 타고 그 숲의 길들을 지나가고, 로바츠 씨가 말을 타고 그들을 호위하기로 되어 있었다. 사실 그날은 사냥 자체보다는 부인들을 위해 마련된 그런 사냥날 가운데 하나였다. 그런 날은 착실한 중년의 사냥꾼들에게는 대단한 골칫거리다. 그러나 젊은 친구들은 그런 날을 좋아한다. 사냥 나들이옷의 멋을 뽐내고 말 위에서 약간의 연애 놀음을 할 기회가 생기기 때문이다. 주교도 그 일행에 끼일 마음이 있었다. 적어도 전날 저녁에는 그렇게 말했다. 그리고 마차 하나에 그의 자리가 따로 마련되어 있었다. 그러나 그 뒤에 그와 프라우디 부인이 그 문제를 따로 의논했고, 아침 식사 자리에서 주교께서는 마음이 바뀌었다고 선언했다.\n\n사워비 씨는 몹시 가난한 사람으로 알려진 — 빚이 사람을 가난하게 만들 수 있는 그만큼 가난한 — 그러면서도 돈이 줄 수 있는 온갖 사치를 다 누리는 그런 사람들 가운데 하나였다. 국회의원이라는 신분의 보호가 없다면 그는 감옥 밖에서 영국에 살 수 없을 것이라고들 믿었다. 그런데도 그의 말과 마차, 하인과 시종들에는 끝이 없는 것 같았다. 그는 아주 여러 해 이 일에 종사해 왔고, 익히면 완벽해진다고 사람들은 말한다. 그런 동료는 몹시 위험하다. 빚보다 더 잘 옮는 콜레라도, 황열도, 천연두도 없다. 궁색한 사람들 사이에서 늘 살다 보면 반드시 그것에 걸린다. 이런 방식으로 사워비 씨보다 더 치명적으로 그 사회를 해친 사람은 없었다. 그러나 여전히 그 자신은 그 판을 이어 나갔다. 그리고 이제 이날 아침, 마치 그가 그의 벗 옴니엄 공작만큼이나 실속 있게 부유하기라도 한 듯이 마차와 말들이 그의 문간에 몰려들었다.\n\n“로바츠, 이 친구,” 사워비 씨가 말했다. 그들이 숲의 빈터 길 하나를 따라 한참 나아갔을 때였다 — 사냥개들이 모이는 곳은 콜디코츠의 집에서 사오 마일 떨어져 있었으므로. “나와 잠깐 앞서 갑시다. 할 말이 있어요. 그리고 내가 뒤에 남으면 우리는 사냥개들한테 영영 못 갈 겁니다.” 그리하여 부인들을 호위하려고 일부러 나온 마크는 붉은 사냥 상의를 입은 사워비 씨와 나란히 말을 몰았다.\n\n“이 친구, 포더길이 그러던데, 개서럼 성에 가는 일을 좀 망설인다고요.”\n\n“글쎄, 사양하기는 했지요, 분명히. 나는 당신처럼 향락을 좇는 사람이 아니잖아요. 나에게는 돌봐야 할 직무가 좀 있습니다.”\n\n“허튼소리!” 사워비 씨가 말했다. 그리고 그 말을 하면서 그는 일종의 비웃는 미소를 띠고 성직자의 얼굴을 들여다보았다.\n\n“그런 말은 하기야 쉽지요, 사워비. 그리고 당신이 나를 이해해 주기를 기대할 권리도 아마 나에게는 없겠고요.”\n\n“아니, 나는 당신을 이해합니다. 그러니까 허튼소리라고 하는 거예요. 당신이 이렇게 망설이는 것이 정말로 그런 양심의 거리낌에서 나온 것이라면, 직무에 대한 당신의 거리낌을 비웃는 일에 나는 세상에서 가장 마지막에 나설 사람입니다. 하지만 정직하게 대답해 봐요. 사정이 그렇지 않다는 것을 당신도 알고 있지 않나요?”\n\n“나는 그런 건 전혀 모릅니다.”\n\n“아니, 내 생각에는 알고 있어요. 이 초대를 끝까지 거절한다면, 그건 레이디 러프턴을 노엽게 만드는 것이 두려워서가 아닐까요? 그 부인에게 무엇이 있어서 당신과 러프턴을 둘 다 걸음마 끈에 매어 둘 수 있는지 나는 모르겠군요.”\n\n로바츠는 물론 그 혐의를 부인하며, 레이디 러프턴이 두려워서 자기 목사관으로 되돌아갈 사람이 아니라고 항변했다. 그러나 그렇게 열을 내어 항변하면서도 그 항변에 힘이 없다는 것을 그 자신이 알고 있었다. 사워비는 그저 미소를 지으며, 푸딩의 맛은 먹어 봐야 안다고 말했다.\n\n“그런 고된 잡일에서 벗어나려는 것이 아니라면, 사람이 부목사를 두는 것이 무슨 소용이 있습니까?” 그가 물었다.\n\n“잡일이라니! 내가 잡일에 매인 사람이라면 오늘 여기 있을 수 있겠어요?”\n\n“자, 로바츠, 들어 보세요. 지금 내가 하는 말은 아마 사정이 온전히 허락하는 것보다 더 오랜 벗의 기세로 하는 말일 겁니다. 하지만 나는 당신보다 나이가 많은 사람이고, 당신을 마음에 두고 있으니 손안에 든 좋은 판을 당신이 내던지는 것을 보고 싶지 않아요.”\n\n“아, 그 점이라면, 사워비, 당신의 호의를 고맙게 여긴다고는 새삼 말할 필요도 없겠지요.”\n\n“당신이 평생 프램리에 살면서 그곳 노부인의 햇볕이나 쬐고 있는 것으로 만족한다면,” 세상 물정에 밝은 그 사나이가 말을 이었다. “왜, 그런 경우라면 벗의 범위를 넓히는 일이 당신에게 아마 소용없을지도 모르지요. 하지만 그보다 더 높은 생각을 품고 있다면, 공작의 집에 가는 이번 기회를 빠뜨리는 것은 크게 잘못하는 일이라고 나는 생각합니다. 공작이 이번처럼 성직자에게 예를 갖추려고 그렇게까지 일부러 나서는 것은 나도 여태 본 적이 없어요.”\n\n“정말이지 그분께 대단히 감사하고 있습니다.”\n\n“사실은, 당신이 마음만 먹으면 이 주에서 인기를 얻을 수 있어요. 하지만 레이디 러프턴의 분부를 다 따르면서 그럴 수는 없습니다. 참 좋은 노부인이지요, 그건 나도 압니다.”\n\n“그렇습니다, 사워비. 당신도 그분을 알게 되면 그렇게 말할 겁니다.”\n\n“의심하지 않아요. 하지만 당신이나 내가 딱 그분의 생각대로 사는 것은 될 일이 아니지요. 그런데 이번 경우에는 교구의 주교가 그 일행 가운데 한 사람이고, 그분은 내가 알기로 당신도 또 한 사람이 되기를 이미 바란다고 말했어요.”\n\n“가는지 나에게 물으셨습니다.”\n\n“바로 그겁니다. 그리고 그랜틀리 부주교도 갈 거예요.”\n\n“그래요?” 마크가 물었다. 그건 크게 얻는 점이 될 것이었다. 그랜틀리 부주교는 레이디 러프턴의 가까운 벗이었으니까.\n\n“포더길에게 그렇게 들었어요. 정말이지 당신이 가지 않는다면 그건 아주 잘못하는 일입니다. 나는 이 말을 툭 터놓고 하는 거예요. 게다가 당신처럼 부목사를 두고 있으면서 직무 이야기를 한다면 — 그거야, 허튼소리지요.” 이 마지막 말은 그가 등자를 밟고 일어선 채로 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 한 말이었다. 사냥개들에 둘러싸여 있던 사냥개 몰이꾼이 눈에 들어왔고, 그가 이제 그에게 합류하려고 속보로 달려오고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n낮의 상당 부분 동안 마크는 프라우디 부인의 마차 옆에서 말을 타고 가는 자신을 발견했다. 그 부인은 마차에 등을 기대고 앉아 있었다. 그리고 프라우디 부인은 그에게 정중하게 미소를 보냈다. 그 딸은 그렇게 하지 않았지만. 프라우디 부인은 성직자를 하나 곁에 딸리고 다니기를 좋아했다. 그리고 로바츠 씨가 좋은 사람들 사이에서 — 작위를 가진 노부인들, 국회의원들, 그런 부류의 사람들 사이에서 — 살고 있음이 분명했으므로, 그녀는 그를 일종의 임시 명예 전속 사제로 앉히는 데 아주 기꺼웠다.\n\n“우리가 어떻게 정했는지 말씀드리지요, 해럴드 스미스 부인과 내가,” 프라우디 부인이 그에게 말했다. “바체스터에서 하는 그 강연이 토요일 저녁 아주 늦게까지 갈 테니, 여러분 모두 우리 집에 와서 저녁을 드시는 것이 좋겠소.”\n\n마크는 고개를 숙여 감사를 표하고, 그런 자리에 한몫 끼는 것이 아주 기쁘겠다고 말했다. 이 일에는 레이디 러프턴도 반대할 수 없을 것이었다. 그녀가 프라우디 부인을 특별히 좋아하지는 않았지만.\n\n“그리고 그다음에 그분들은 호텔에서 자기로 되어 있소. 해가 이맘때인데 부인들이 그렇게 먼 길을 되돌아갈 생각을 하기에는 정말 너무 늦은 시각일 테니까. 나는 해럴드 스미스 부인에게, 던스터블 양에게도, 그분들만은 어떻게든 방을 마련할 수 있다고 말했소. 그러나 다른 부인들을 두고 가려 하지 않으니, 그 밤은 호텔로 가는 거요. 하지만 로바츠 씨, 주교는 결코 당신을 여관에 묵게 두지 않을 테니, 물론 당신은 주교관에서 침대를 하나 쓰게 될 거요.”\n\n강연이 토요일 저녁에 열린다면 그 이튿날 아침은 일요일이고, 그 일요일에 자기는 콜디코츠에서 설교를 해야 한다는 생각이 곧바로 마크에게 떠올랐다. “모두 그날 밤에 돌아오시는 줄 알았습니다,” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그야 그럴 생각이었지요. 하지만 보시오, 스미스 부인이 겁을 내니.”\n\n“저는 일요일 아침에 여기로 돌아와 있어야 합니다, 프라우디 부인.”\n\n“아, 그렇군, 그건 곤란하오 — 정말이지 아주 곤란하오. 안식일을 침범하는 일이라면 나만큼 싫어하는 사람도 없소. 정말이지, 내가 무엇 하나에 까다롭다면 바로 그것에 까다롭지요. 그러나 어떤 일은 부득이한 일이 아니겠소, 로바츠 씨? 그런데 지금 당신은 일요일 아침에 부득이 콜디코츠로 돌아와 있어야 하오!” 그리하여 그 문제는 그렇게 정해졌다. 프라우디 부인은 대체로 안식일을 지키는 문제에서 아주 단호했다. 그러나 해럴드 스미스 부인 같은 사람들을 상대해야 할 때에는 조금 물러 주는 것이 마땅했다. “날이 밝는 대로 곧 떠나셔도 되오, 그러고 싶으시면 말이오, 로바츠 씨,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n사냥으로 자랑할 만한 것은 별로 없었으나, 부인들에게는 아주 즐거운 하루였다. 남자들은 체이스를 가로지르는 풀길을 오르내리며 말을 달렸다. 때로는 아무리 빨리 가도 성에 차지 않는다는 듯이 있을 수 있는 가장 큰 서두름으로 달렸고, 그러면 마부들도 왜 그러는지는 모르면서 몹시 빠르게 마차를 몰았다. 움직임의 빠른 속도라는 것도 옮는 병 가운데 하나이기 때문이다. 그러다가 또 여우가 자취를 되짚어 사냥개들이 어느 쪽이 나아간 자취이고 어느 쪽이 되짚어 온 자취인지 알지 못해 헤맬 때에는, 사냥꾼들이 장의사의 걸음으로 움직였다. 그러면 마차도 천천히 갔고, 부인들은 일어서서 이야기를 했다. 그러고 나서 점심때가 왔다. 그리고 다 해서 그날은 흡족하게 지나갔다.\n\n“그래서 저것이 사냥이라는 거군요?” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n“그렇습니다, 저것이 사냥이지요,” 사워비 씨가 말했다.\n\n“내가 직접 못 할 일을 하는 신사는 하나도 못 봤어요. 진흙탕으로 미끄러져 떨어진 젊은 사람 하나만 빼면요. 그건 나도 하고 싶지 않네요.”\n\n“그래도 뼈가 부러진 사람은 없었잖아요, 그렇지요?” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그리고 여우를 잡은 사람도 없어요,” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “실은 말이지요, 스미스 부인, 나는 저 사람들의 놀이도 저 사람들의 일만큼밖에는 대단하게 여기지 않아요. 이걸 보고 나니 나도 사냥개를 한 무리 데리고 사냥을 해 볼까 싶네요.”\n\n“그러세요, 그러면 내가 부몰이꾼이 되어 드리지요. 프라우디 부인도 우리와 함께해 주실지 모르겠네요.”\n\n“오늘 밤에 공작께 편지를 쓸 겁니다,” 그들이 다 함께 마구간 마당으로 올라가고 있을 때 포더길 씨가 마크에게 말했다. “초대를 받아들이시겠다고 각하께 전해도 되겠지요 — 그렇지 않습니까?”\n\n“정말이지 공작께서 아주 친절하시군요,” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“각하께서 당신을 몹시 알고 싶어 하십니다, 내가 보증하지요,” 포더길이 말했다.\n\n추어올림을 받아 얼떨떨해진 젊고 어리석은 목사가 가겠다고 말하는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 마크는 가겠다고 말했다. 그리고 그날 저녁이 흘러가는 동안 그의 벗 사워비 씨는 그에게 축하를 했고, 주교는 그를 놀리며 그가 좋은 벗들을 그렇게 쉽게 포기하지 않을 줄 알고 있었다고 말했고, 던스터블 양은 국회가 돌팔이 의사에게도 그런 물건을 두는 것을 허락하는 대로 그를 자기 전속 사제로 삼겠다고 말했다 — 마크는 이 빗댐을 알아듣지 못했고, 던스터블 양 자신이 그 이름난 레바논 연고의 소유주라는 것을 알게 된 뒤에야 알아들었다. 그것은 그녀의 작고한 존경받는 아버지가 발명하여 특허를 낸 것이었고, 그리하여 재산이 쌓이는 방면으로 놀라운 결과를 낳은 것이었다. 그리고 프라우디 부인은 온갖 교회 문제를 그에게 이야기하며 그를 아주 그들 편의 한 사람으로 만들었다. 그리고 마침내 프라우디 양까지도, 그가 공작의 성에서 침대 하나를 받을 만하다고 여겨졌음을 알게 되자 그에게 미소를 보냈다. 그리고 온 세상이 그에게 열려 있는 것 같았다.\n\n그러나 그날 저녁 그는 스스로 행복해질 수 없었다. 이튿날 아침에는 아내에게 편지를 써야 했다. 그리고 자기 남편이 옴니엄 공작의 손님이 되려 한다는 것을 알게 될 때 그의 패니의 이마에 내려앉을 그 괴로운 슬픔의 표정을 그는 벌써 볼 수 있었다. 그리고 돈을 보내 달라고 그녀에게 말해야 했고, 돈은 넉넉하지 않았다. 그리고 또 레이디 러프턴에게는 무슨 말을 전해야 하는가, 아니면 전하지 말아야 하는가? 어느 쪽이든 그는 그녀에게 선전을 포고해야 했다. 그리고 또 자기가 모든 것을 레이디 러프턴에게 힘입지 않았는가? 그리하여 온갖 승리에도 불구하고 그는 행복한 마음으로 잠자리에 들 수 없었다.\n\n이튿날, 그러니까 금요일에 그는 그 언짢은 일을 뒤로 미루었다. 토요일도 마찬가지로 괜찮을 것이었다. 그리고 토요일 아침, 다들 바체스터로 떠나기 전에 그는 정말로 썼다. 그리고 그의 편지는 이러했다 : —\n\n콜디코츠, 185─년 11월 ─일.\n\n가장 사랑하는 이에게, — 우리가 여기서 얼마나 흥청거리고 있는지, 또 어떤 방탕이 앞으로 우리를 기다리고 있는지 말하면 당신은 놀랄 거야. 애러빈 부부는 당신이 짐작한 대로 우리 일행이 아니야. 그러나 프라우디 부부는 우리 일행이지 — 이것도 당신이 짐작한 대로야. 당신의 짐작은 언제나 옳아. 그리고 내가 토요일에 주교관에서 잔다고 말하면 당신은 어떻게 생각할까? 그날 바체스터에서 강연이 있다는 건 당신도 알지. 그런데, 우리는 물론 다 가야 해. 여기 우리 패의 한 사람인 해럴드 스미스가 그 강연을 하기로 되어 있으니까. 그리고 이제 보니 달이 없어서 우리는 그날 밤에 돌아올 수 없다는 거야. 그리고 주교 부인께서는 내 성직자의 옷이 호텔로 더럽혀지는 것을 허락하지 않으시겠다는 거지 — 아주 친절하고 사려 깊지 않아?\n\n그러나 이보다 더 놀라운 소식이 당신에게 있어. 다음 주에 개서럼 성에서 큰 모임이 있는데, 공작이 나에게 특별히 보낸 초대를 받아들이도록 다들 나를 설득해 버렸어. 처음에는 거절했지. 그런데 여기 있는 사람들이 다 내가 그러는 것은 아주 이상한 일이라고 했고, 그러고는 다들 내 이유를 알고 싶어 했어. 그 이유를 대려고 하니 내가 댈 이유가 무엇인지 알 수 없었어. 주교가 가는데, 내가 청함을 받았는데도 가지 않는 것을 그분은 아주 괴상하게 여겼어.\n\n내 사랑하는 사람이 무엇을 생각할지 나는 알아. 그리고 그이가 기뻐하지 않을 것도 알아. 그래서 내 변명은 이 도깨비 나라에서 그이에게 돌아갈 때까지 미뤄 두어야겠어 — 살아서 돌아가기라도 한다면 말이지. 하지만 농담은 접어 두고, 패니, 그렇게 말이 많이 나온 판에 내가 끝까지 버티는 것은 잘못된 일이었으리라고 나는 생각해. 내가 공작을 심판하는 자리에 앉겠다고 나서는 꼴로 보였을 거야. 이 교구에서 쉰 살이 안 된 성직자 가운데 그런 사정에서 그 초대를 거절했을 사람이 한 사람이라도 있을지 의심스러워 — 크롤리라면 몰라도. 그 사람은 이 문제에 하도 미쳐 있어서 자기 교구 밖으로 산책을 나가는 것조차 거의 잘못이라고 여기니까.\n\n나는 다음 주 일요일이 지날 때까지 개서럼 성에 머물러야 해 — 실은 우리가 그리로 가는 것이 금요일이니까. 직무에 대해서는 존스에게 편지를 써 두었어. 그에게 갚아 줄 수 있어. 그가 성탄절에 웨일스로 가고 싶어 하는 것을 내가 아니까. 그때면 내 떠돌이 노릇도 다 끝날 테고, 그가 원하면 두어 달 다녀와도 좋아. 일요일에 학교에서 당신 반과 함께 내 반도 맡아 주겠지. 그런데 아이들에게 불은 잘 지펴 주도록 해. 이것이 당신에게 너무 무거우면 포진스 부인에게 사내아이들을 맡겨. 실은 그게 더 나을 것 같아.\n\n물론 당신은 마님께 내가 어디 있는지 알려 드릴 테지. 내 말로 이렇게 전해 줘. 주교에 관해서도, 또 다른 한 분 지체 높은 인물에 관해서도, 색을 아마 좀 너무 두껍게 칠해 놓은 것 같다고. 레이디 러프턴이 그 사람을 좋아하게 되지야 않겠지만. 공작 집에 가는 일이 나에게는 거의 양심에 걸리는 일이 되어 버렸다는 것을 그분이 알아주시게 해 줘. 이 일을 아주 당파의 문제로 만들어 버리지 않고서는, 내가 거절하는 것이 옳다고 보이게 할 방도를 나는 알 수 없었어. 내가 레이디 러프턴의 교구에서 온 사람이니 옴니엄 공작의 집에는 갈 수 없다는 말이 나올 것이 눈에 보였어. 이것은 내가 택하지 않았어.\n\n여기를 떠나기 전에 돈이 좀 더 필요할 것 같아. 5파운드나 10파운드 — 10파운드라고 하자. 당신이 내놓을 수 없으면 데이비스에게서 받아. 그가 나에게 갚을 것이 그보다 훨씬 많아.\n\n그러면 이제, 하느님께서 당신을 축복하시고 지켜 주시기를, 내 사랑. 아빠를 대신해 내 귀여운 아이들에게 입 맞춰 주고, 내 축복을 전해 줘.\n\n언제나 영원히 당신의 사람,\n\n그리고 그다음에, 빽빽하게 쓴 종이를 감싸는 바깥쪽 쪽지에 이렇게 적혀 있었다. “프램리 코트에서 될 수 있는 대로 매끄럽게 해 줘.”\n\n마크의 편지 본문이 아무리 강하고 조리 있고 반박할 수 없는 것이었다 해도, 그의 온갖 망설임과 약함과 의심과 두려움은 이 짧은 추신에 다 드러나 있었다.\n\n# 제5장. 「아만티움 이래 아모리스 인테그라티오」\n\n이제 독자의 양해를 얻어, 나는 그 편지를 든 우체부를 따라 프램리로 가 보려 한다. 물론 편지가 지나온 그 구불구불한 길을 그대로 따라가거나 같은 운송 수단을 타고 가겠다는 말은 아니다. 그 편지는 코시 야간 우편 마차에 실려 바체스터로 들어갔는데, 이 마차는 도중에 어플리와 콜디코츠 두 마을을 지나 런던행 상행 우편 열차 시간에 맞추어 바체스터에 닿는다. 편지는 그 열차에 실려 수도를 향해 바셋 지선의 분기점까지 갔다가, 거기서 방향을 돌려 다시 본선을 타고 실버브리지까지 내려왔다. 그리고 그곳에서 아침 여섯 시와 일곱 시 사이에 프램리 도보 우편 배달부의 어깨에 얹혔고, 로바츠 부인이 하인 넷에게 기도문을 읽어 주기를 막 끝냈을 무렵 어김없이 프램리 목사관에 배달되었다. 아니, 이렇게 말하는 편이 옳겠다. 일이 여느 때처럼 흘러갔다면 그 편지의 운명은 그러했으리라는 것이다. 그러나 실제로는 편지가 일요일에 실버브리지에 닿았고, 프램리 사람들이 일요일 우편을 사양했던 터라 월요일까지 거기 놓여 있었다. 게다가 비 내리는 그 월요일 아침에 편지가 목사관에 배달되었을 때, 로바츠 부인은 집에 없었다. 우리 모두가 아는 대로 그녀는 프램리 코트에서 마님과 함께 머물고 있었다.\n\n“허, 지독히도 젖었수,” 그 편지와 교구 사제의 신문을 건네주며 몸을 떨던 우체부가 말했다. 교구 사제는 세상 물정을 아는 사람이라 《주피터》를 받아 보았다.\n\n“들어오시우, 로빈 우체부. 잠깐 몸이나 녹이시구려,” 요리사 제마이마가 등받이 없는 의자를 한쪽으로 조금 밀어 주면서 말했다. 그래도 의자는 여전히 큰 부엌 불 바로 앞에 있었다.\n\n“글쎄, 어찌 될지 나도 잘 모르겠수. 실버브리지에선 울타리란 놈들까지 눈이 달려서, 내가 블랙베리 하나 따려고 멈추기만 해도 죄다 고해바친다니까.”\n\n“여기는 울타리도 없고 블랙베리도 없수, 이 사람아. 그러니 앉아서 몸이나 녹이시우. 그게 블랙베리보다야 낫지 싶은데,” 그러면서 그녀는 버터 바른 토스트 한 조각과 함께 차 한 그릇을 그에게 건넸다.\n\n로빈 우체부는 내미는 차를 받아 들고, 물이 뚝뚝 떨어지는 모자를 바닥에 내려놓고, 요리사 제마이마에게 고맙다고 했다. “그래도 어찌 될지는 나도 잘 모르겠수,” 그가 말했다. “다만 비가 억수로 쏟아지니 말이우.” 아, 독자들이여, 우리 가운데 누가 그런 유혹을 물리칠 수 있었겠는가?\n\n마크의 편지가 지나온 길은 그처럼 구불구불했다. 그러나 편지는 토요일 저녁에 콜디코츠를 떠나 이튿날 아침에 로바츠 부인에게 닿았고—중간에 그 일요일이 끼어들지 않았다면 닿았을 것이며—그 모든 여정을 밤 사이에 마쳤으니, 운송 경로가 그리 불편하게 짜여 있었다고는 할 수 없겠다. 그러나 우리는 훨씬 짧은 길로 가겠다.\n\n로빈은 날마다 도는 길에서 먼저 프램리 우체국을 지나고, 다음으로 프램리 코트의 뒷문을 지나고, 그다음에 교구 사제의 집을 지났다. 그래서 이 비 오는 아침에 요리사 제마이마는 이 편지를 안주인에게 되돌려 보내는 일에 그의 발을 빌릴 수가 없었다. 로빈에게는 편지를 기다리는 또 다른 마을이 앞에 남아 있었기 때문이다.\n\n“왜 그 편지를 코트의 애플존 씨한테 맡기지 않았수, 이 사람아?” 애플존 씨는 우편 행낭을 받는 집사였다. “안주인이 거기 계신 걸 알고 있었잖수.”\n\n그러자 로빈은 차와 토스트를 잊지 않고, 편지 주인이 어디에 있든 편지에 적힌 바로 그 집까지 편지를 가져가는 것이 법이 자기에게 지운 의무라고 그녀에게 정중히 설명했다. 그러고는 여러 가지 긴 낱말을 인용해 가며 법을 아주 만족스럽게 늘어놓았다. 그러나 큰 효험은 없었다. 하녀가 그를 얼간이라 불렀으니 말이다. 정원사가 들어와 그의 편을 들어 주지 않았다면 로빈은 영락없이 지고 말았을 것이다. “저 여편네들은 아무것도 모르고 아무것도 못 알아듣는다니까,” 정원사가 말했다. “편지 이리 주우. 내가 저택으로 가져가겠수. 주인 나리 글씨로군.” 그러고 로빈 우체부는 한쪽으로 갔고, 정원사는 다른 쪽으로 갔다. 정원사는 이렇게 비 오는 날에도 코트의 정원으로 올라갈 구실이라면 싫어한 적이 없었다.\n\n남편의 편지가 로바츠 부인에게 전해졌을 때, 그녀는 응접실 난롯가에 레이디 메러디스와 함께 앉아 있었다. 프램리 코트의 우편 행낭은 아침 식사 자리에서 다 뒤진 터였지만 그것은 이미 한 시간쯤 전 일이었고, 레이디 러프턴은 늘 하는 대로 자기 방에 들어가 자기 편지를 쓰고 자기 일을 살피고 있었다. 레이디 러프턴은 숫자를 스스로 다루는 사람이었고, 해럴드 스미스에 못지않게 사업을 잘 알았기 때문이다. 그리고 그날 아침 그녀 역시 적잖이 마음을 상하게 하는 편지 한 통을 받았다. 그 불쾌함이 어디서 비롯되었는지는 로바츠 부인도 레이디 메러디스도 알지 못했다. 다만 마님의 이마는 아침 식사 때부터 어두워졌고, 그녀는 불길해 보이는 서한을 아무 말 없이 손가방에 쑤셔 넣고는 식사가 끝나자마자 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n“무슨 일이 있으신가 보군,” 조지 경이 말했다.\n\n“어머니는 루도빅의 돈 문제로 어찌나 마음을 태우시는지 몰라요,” 레이디 메러디스가 말했다. 루도빅은 러프턴 경이었다. 옥스퍼드셔주 러프턴의 러프턴 남작, 루도빅 러프턴.\n\n“그래도 러프턴이 그리 크게 어긋나는 것 같지는 않은데,” 방에서 어슬렁어슬렁 나가면서 조지 경이 말했다. “그럼 저스티, 떠나는 건 내일로 미루기로 하지. 하지만 첫차라야 하는 걸 잊지 말아.” 레이디 메러디스는 잊지 않겠다고 했고, 그러고 나서 두 여인은 응접실로 들어갔으며, 거기서 로바츠 부인이 편지를 받았다.\n\n패니는 편지를 읽고도 처음에는 자기 남편이, 프램리의 성직자이자 레이디 러프턴 집안의 가까운 성직자 친구인 그이가 옴니엄 공작의 집에 머물려 한다는 사실을 좀처럼 실감하지 못했다. 프램리 코트에서는 공작과 그에게 딸린 모든 것이 해롭고 저주스럽다는 것이 철저하게 합의된 바였다. 그는 휘그였고, 독신이었고, 도박꾼이었고, 무슨 일에서나 부도덕했고, 교회의 원칙이라고는 없는 사람이었고, 젊은이를 타락시키는 자였고, 젊은 아내들의 맹세한 원수였고, 자잘한 사람들의 세습 재산을 삼켜 버리는 자였다. 어머니들이 아들을 위해 두려워하고 누이들이 오라비를 위해 두려워하는 사람, 그리고 더 나쁘게는 아버지들이 딸을 위해, 오라비들이 누이를 위해 두려워할 까닭이 있는 사람이었다. 그에게 딸린 것들과 더불어 레이디 러프턴과 그녀에게 딸린 것들로부터 양극처럼 멀리 떨어져 살았고, 또 그래야만 하는 사람이었다!\n\n그리고 이 모든 나쁜 소문을 로바츠 부인이 고스란히 믿고 있었다는 것을 잊지 말아야 한다. 정말로 자기 남편이 아폴리온의 궁에 들어가 살고, 바로 그 루시퍼의 날개 밑에 몸을 숨기려 한다는 말인가? 슬픔의 구름이 그녀의 얼굴에 내려앉았다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 그 편지를 아주 천천히 다시 읽었는데, 속내를 다 드러내는 추신도 빼놓지 않았다.\n\n“오, 저스티니아!” 마침내 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“왜요, 패니도 나쁜 소식을 받았어요?”\n\n“무슨 일이 생겼는지 어떻게 말해야 할지 모르겠어요. 자, 그냥 읽어 보는 게 나을 것 같아요.” 그러고 그녀는 남편의 서한을 레이디 메러디스에게 건네주었다. 다만 추신은 접어서 감추었다.\n\n“마님이 이번엔 대체 무슨 말씀을 하실까요?” 종이를 접어 봉투에 다시 넣으면서 레이디 메러디스가 말했다.\n\n“어쩌면 좋아요, 저스티니아? 어떻게 말씀드리는 게 좋을까요?” 그러고 나서 두 여인은 머리를 맞대고, 레이디 러프턴의 노여움을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 달랠 수 있을지 궁리했다. 로바츠 부인은 점심을 먹고 목사관으로 돌아가기로 되어 있었고, 메러디스 부부가 그날 저녁까지 머물기로 정해진 뒤에도 그 뜻을 굽히지 않았다. 이제 레이디 메러디스는 남편의 끔찍한 죄악에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않은 채 그 결심대로 떠나고, 목사관에 닿는 대로 편지를 레이디 러프턴에게 올려 보내라고 친구에게 권했다. “패니가 그 편지를 여기서 받았다는 건 어머니가 결코 모르실 거예요,” 레이디 메러디스가 말했다.\n\n그러나 로바츠 부인은 그렇게 하는 데 동의하지 않았다. 그런 처사는 그녀에게 비겁해 보였다. 남편이 잘못하고 있다는 것을 그녀는 알았고, 그이 자신도 그것을 안다고 느꼈다. 그럼에도 그녀가 그를 변호해야 한다는 것 또한 사실이었다. 폭풍이 아무리 무섭게 몰아친다 해도 그것은 자기 머리 위에서 터져야 했다. 그래서 그녀는 곧 위층으로 올라가 레이디 러프턴의 사실(私室) 문을 두드렸고, 그렇게 하는 그녀를 레이디 메러디스가 뒤따랐다.\n\n“들어오세요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했는데, 그 목소리는 부드럽지도 상냥하지도 않았다. 두 사람이 들어가 보니 그녀는 작은 필기 탁자 앞에 앉아 팔에 머리를 기대고 있었고, 그날 아침에 받은 그 편지가 탁자 위에 펼쳐진 채 놓여 있었다. 실은 지금 거기에 편지가 두 통 있었다. 하나는 런던의 어느 변호사가 그녀에게 보낸 것이고, 다른 하나는 그녀의 아들이 그 런던 변호사에게 보낸 것이었다. 그 편지들의 주제가 언젠가 사워비 씨가 말했던, 옥스퍼드셔에 있는 러프턴 가문 재산의 외딴 일부를 곧 팔아 치우는 일이었다는 것만 밝혀 두면 되겠다. 러프턴 경은 변호사에게 그 일을 당장 처리해야 한다고 말했고, 친구 로바츠가 그 일의 전말을 어머니에게 설명해 두었을 것이라고 덧붙였다. 그리고 변호사는, 실로 그래야 마땅한 대로 레이디 러프턴에게 편지를 썼다. 그러나 불행히도 레이디 러프턴은 그때까지 그 일에 대해 한마디도 듣지 못했던 것이다.\n\n그녀의 눈에는 집안 재산을 파는 일이 끔찍했다. 연 수입이 1만 5천이나 2만 파운드나 되는 젊은이가 곁돈을 필요로 한다는 사실이 끔찍했다. 자기 아들이 자기에게 직접 편지를 쓰지 않았다는 것이 끔찍했다. 그리고 자기가 아끼는 사람, 아들의 친구가 되라고 자기가 이곳으로 데려온 그 성직자가 이 일에 얽혀 있다는 것—자기는 모르는 일을 그가 알고 있었다는 것, 아들의 나쁜 행로에서 중개인이자 대리인으로 부려졌다는 것—역시 끔찍했다. 모든 것이 끔찍했고, 레이디 러프턴은 이마를 어둡게 하고 마음을 불편해하며 거기 앉아 있었다. 우리의 가난한 목사에 대해 말한다면, 이 일에서 그는 친구의 심부름을 실행할 용기를 그때까지 내지 못했다는 것 말고는 잘못이 없다고 해야 할 것이다.\n\n“무슨 일이에요, 패니?” 문이 열리자마자 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “나를 찾았으면 반 시간 안에 내려갔을 텐데, 저스티니아.”\n\n“패니가 편지를 받았는데, 어머니께 당장 말씀드리고 싶어 해서요,” 레이디 메러디스가 말했다. “무슨 편지예요, 패니?”\n\n가엾은 패니는 심장이 목까지 올라와 있었다. 편지를 손에 들고 있었지만, 그것을 레이디 러프턴에게 실물로 보여 줄지 아직 마음을 정하지 못했다.\n\n“로바츠 씨한테서 왔습니다,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그래요. 콜디코츠에 한 주 더 머물겠다는 거겠지요. 나로서야 그러는 게 더 좋겠지만요.” 그러면서 레이디 러프턴의 목소리는 다정하지 않았다. 그녀는 옥스퍼드셔의 그 농장을 생각하고 있었기 때문이다. 젊은이들의 무분별은 나이 든 이들의 분별에 몹시 쓰라리다. 레이디 러프턴만큼 욕심 없고 움키려 들지 않는 여인은 없었다. 그러나 오래된 집안 재산의 한 조각을 파는 일은 그녀에게 제 심장의 피를 잃는 것과 같았다.\n\n“편지가 여기 있습니다, 레이디 러프턴. 읽어 보시는 게 좋겠습니다.” 그러고 패니는 이번에도 추신은 감춘 채 그것을 그녀에게 건넸다. 그녀는 아래층에서 편지를 읽고 또 읽었지만, 남편이 그것을 보여 주라는 뜻이었는지는 알 수 없었다. 글의 흐름으로 보아 그런 뜻이었으리라 생각했다. 어쨌든 그가 자기를 위해 한 말이 그녀가 그를 위해 할 수 있는 말보다 많았으니, 마님이 그것을 보는 편이 아마 가장 나을 것이었다.\n\n레이디 러프턴은 그것을 받아 읽었고, 그녀의 얼굴은 갈수록 어두워졌다. 읽기 시작하기도 전에 그녀의 마음은 이미 그 글을 쓴 사람에게 등을 돌리고 있었고, 편지 속의 모든 낱말이 그를 더욱 멀게 느끼도록 만들었다. “아, 주교관에 간다는 거군요. 그래요, 친구는 자기가 골라야지요. 해럴드 스미스가 한패라니! 얘야, 그이가 패니를 만나기 전에 프라우디 양을 보지 못한 것이 안타깝군요. 그랬으면 주교의 전속 사제가 되고도 남았을 텐데. 개서럼 성이라니! 그이가 거기까지 간다는 말은 아니겠지요? 그렇다면 나도 솔직히 말하겠어요, 패니. 나는 그이와는 이제 끝이에요.”\n\n“오, 레이디 러프턴, 그런 말씀 하지 마십시오,” 눈에 눈물을 담고 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“어머니, 어머니, 그렇게 말씀하지 마세요,” 레이디 메러디스가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 얘야, 내가 무슨 말을 해야 하니? 나는 그렇게 말해야 해. 네가 나더러 거짓을 말하기를 바라지는 않겠지? 사람은 스스로 골라야 하지만, 서로 다른 두 무리와 함께 살 수는 없어. 적어도 한쪽에 내가 있고 다른 쪽에 옴니엄 공작이 있다면 그럴 수 없지. 주교까지 간다니 참! 내가 무엇을 싫어한다면 그건 위선이야.”\n\n“그건 위선이 아닙니다, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“나는 위선이라고 말해요, 패니. 정말이지 아주 괴상하군요! ‘변명은 나중으로 미루겠소’라니! 사람이 곧게 행동한다면 아내에게 무슨 변명이 필요하겠어요? 그이 자신의 말이 그이를 벌하고 있어요. ‘버티는 것은 잘못이었을 것이오’라니! 자, 두 사람 가운데 누구라도 로바츠 씨가 그 초대를 거절하는 것을 정말로 잘못이라 여겼으리라고 말해 주겠어요? 나는 그것이 위선이라고 말하겠어요. 달리 부를 말이 없어요.”\n\n그때쯤 눈물을 흘리던 가엾은 아내는 눈물을 닦고 싸울 준비를 하고 있었다. 레이디 러프턴의 지나친 혹독함이 그녀에게 용기를 주었다. 남편이 이렇게 공격당할 때는 그를 위해 싸우는 것이 마땅하다는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 레이디 러프턴이 온건하게만 말했더라면 로바츠 부인은 한마디도 하지 못했을 것이다.\n\n“제 남편이 분별없이 판단했을지는 모릅니다,” 그녀가 말했다. “그러나 그이는 위선자가 아닙니다.”\n\n“좋아요, 패니. 내가 아는 것보다야 패니가 더 잘 알겠지요. 그러나 내 눈에는 몹시도 위선처럼 보여요. 그렇지 않니, 저스티니아?”\n\n“어머니, 좀 누그러지세요.”\n\n“누그러지라고! 그거야 말은 좋지. 배신을 당한 사람이 어떻게 제 감정을 누그러뜨리겠니?”\n\n“로바츠 씨가 마님을 배신했다는 말씀은 아니시겠지요?” 아내가 말했다.\n\n“아, 아니에요. 물론 아니지요.” 그러고 나서 그녀는 편지를 계속 읽어 갔다. “‘공작을 심판하는 자리에 앉는 것처럼 보였을 것이오.’ 이 나라의 어떤 집에 들어가든, 그 집이 아무리 악명 높든 같은 논리를 쓸 수 있지 않겠어요? 그런 뜻이라면 우리는 모두 서로를 심판하는 자리에 서야 해요. ‘크롤리!’ 그래요. 그이가 크롤리 씨를 조금이라도 닮았다면 나에게도, 이 교구에도, 그리고 패니에게도 좋은 일일 거예요. 그이를 여기 데려온 것을 하느님이 용서하시기를. 그뿐이에요.”\n\n“레이디 러프턴, 마님이 그이에게 너무 모지시다고 말씀드리지 않을 수 없습니다. 너무 모지십니다. 그런 친구분께서 그러실 줄은 몰랐습니다.”\n\n“얘야, 내가 마음에 있는 말을 하는 사람이라는 건 알 만큼 알 텐데요. ‘존스에게 편지를 썼소’—그래요, 가엾은 존스에게 편지 쓰는 건 쉬운 일이지요. 존스에게 편지를 써서 의무를 다 맡기는 게 낫겠어요. 그러고 나서 공작의 집안 전속 사제나 되면 되겠군요.”\n\n“제 남편은 온 주교구의 어느 성직자에게도 뒤지지 않게 자기 의무를 다한다고 저는 믿습니다,” 다시 눈물을 흘리며 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그리고 학교에서 그이가 할 일을 패니가 맡으라고요. 패니와 포진스 부인이 말이지요. 부목사와 아내와 포진스 부인이 있으니, 그이가 대체 왜 돌아와야 하는지 나로서는 알 수가 없군요.”\n\n“어머니,” 저스티니아가 말했다. “제발, 제발 패니에게 그렇게 매정하게 굴지 마세요.”\n\n“끝까지 읽게 해 주렴, 얘야. 아, 여기 내가 나오는군. ‘내가 어디 있는지 마님께 알려 주시오.’ 그이는 패니가 이 편지를 내게 보일 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐겠지요.”\n\n“몰랐을까요?” 편지를 되찾으려고 손을 내밀었으나 헛수고였던 로바츠 부인이 말했다. “저는 그게 가장 좋다고 생각했습니다. 정말 그랬습니다.”\n\n“이제 내가 끝까지 읽는 게 좋겠어요. 이건 뭔가요? 이런 일에 어떻게 감히 나에게 상스러운 농담을 보낼 수가 있지요? 아니에요, 나는 프라우디 박사를 좋아하게 되리라고는 생각하지 않아요. 그런 걸 바란 적도 없어요. 그이에게는 양심의 문제라니! 원, 원, 원. 내 눈으로 읽지 않았다면 그이가 이랬으리라고 믿지 못했을 거예요. 정말로 믿지 못했을 거예요. ‘내 교구에서 온 사람이 옴니엄 공작에게 갈 수는 없다!’ 그리고 그건 내가 하고 싶었던 말이기도 해요. 이 교구에 어울리는 사람이라면 옴니엄 공작의 집에는 어울리지 않아야 하지요. 그리고 나는 그이가 이 교구의 누구보다도 그 감정을 더 강하게 품고 있으리라 믿었어요. 나는 속았어요. 그뿐이에요.”\n\n“그이는 마님을 속인 일이 없습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“그이가 패니를 속이지 않기를 바라요, 얘야. ‘돈이 더 필요하오.’ 그래요, 그이가 돈을 더 원하게 될 것도 그럴 법한 일이지요. 편지 여기 있어요, 패니. 참 안타까운 일이에요. 더는 할 말이 없어요.” 그러고 그녀는 편지를 접어 로바츠 부인에게 돌려주었다.\n\n“보여 드리는 것이 옳다고 생각했습니다,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“보이든 안 보이든 큰 상관은 없었어요. 어차피 나는 들어야 했을 테니까요.”\n\n“그이가 마님께 꼭 알려 드리라고 했습니다.”\n\n“그렇겠지요. 이런 일에 나를 어둠 속에 놓아둘 수야 없었겠지요. 제 일을 팽개친 채 옴니엄 공작의 집에서 도박꾼과 간음하는 자들과 어울려 지내면서 내가 모르게 할 수는 없었을 테니까요.”\n\n그리고 이제 패니 로바츠의 잔은 가득 찼다. 넘칠 만큼 가득 찼다. 이 말을 들었을 때 그녀는 레이디 러프턴도 레이디 메러디스도 다 잊고 오직 남편만을 떠올렸다. 그이가 자기 남편이라는 것, 결점이 있어도 착하고 사랑이 깊은 남편이라는 것을. 그리고 또 하나의 사실도 떠올렸다. 자기가 그이의 아내라는 것을.\n\n“레이디 러프턴,” 그녀가 말했다. “제 남편을 그런 식으로 말씀하시는 것은 도를 넘으시는 일입니다.”\n\n“무엇이라고요!” 마님이 말했다. “나에게 그런 편지를 보여 주고서, 내가 생각하는 바는 말하지 말라는 거예요?”\n\n“그렇게 모진 것을 생각하신다면 안 됩니다. 마님이시라도 저에게 그런 식으로 말씀하실 권리는 없습니다. 저는 그것을 듣지 않겠습니다.”\n\n“어허, 이거 참 대단하시군!” 마님이 말했다.\n\n“그이가 옴니엄 공작의 집에 가는 것이 옳은지 그른지는 제가 감히 판단하지 않겠습니다. 그이의 행동을 판단하는 이는 그이 자신이고, 마님도 저도 아닙니다.”\n\n“그러다 그이가 정육점 외상값도 갚지 않고 아이들 신발 살 돈도 남기지 않은 채 패니를 두고 떠나면, 그때는 누가 판단하지요?”\n\n“마님은 아니십니다, 레이디 러프턴. 그런 나쁜 날이 온다 해도—마님도 저도 그런 날을 예상할 권리는 없습니다만—저는 어려울 때 마님께 오지 않겠습니다. 이런 일이 있은 뒤에는요.”\n\n“좋아요, 얘야. 그게 더 좋다면 옴니엄 공작에게 가도 되겠지요.”\n\n“패니, 그만 가요,” 레이디 메러디스가 말했다. “왜 우리 어머니를 화나게 하려 해요?”\n\n“화나게 하려는 게 아니에요. 하지만 그이가 그런 식으로 욕을 듣는 것을 두고 보면서 그이를 위해 말하지 않을 수는 없어요. 내가 변호하지 않으면 누가 하겠어요? 레이디 러프턴이 그이에 대해 끔찍한 말을 하셨어요. 그리고 그건 사실이 아니에요.”\n\n“오, 패니!” 저스티니아가 말했다.\n\n“좋아요, 좋아요!” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “이런 게 사람이 받는 보답이군요.”\n\n“보답이라는 말씀이 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠습니다, 레이디 러프턴. 하지만 제 남편이 그런 말을 듣는데도 저더러 잠자코 서 있으라는 말씀입니까? 그이는 마님이 말씀하신 그런 사람들과 함께 살지 않습니다. 자기 의무를 소홀히 하지도 않습니다. 모든 성직자가 그이만큼 자기 교구에 머문다면 그들 가운데 몇 사람에게는 잘된 일일 것입니다. 그리고 옴니엄 공작의 집 같은 곳에 갈 때, 그이가 주교와 함께 간다는 것은 분명 차이가 있습니다. 왜 그런지 설명할 수는 없지만, 차이가 있다는 것은 압니다.”\n\n“로바츠 씨가 한 것처럼 주교를 악마와 한데 묶어 놓았을 때는 특히 그렇겠지요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “거기에 공작까지 붙여 놓으면 셋이 삼미신 노릇을 하겠구나, 그렇지 않니, 저스티니아?” 그러고 나서 레이디 러프턴은 자기 재담에 씁쓸한 웃음을 조금 웃었다.\n\n“이제 가도 되겠습니까, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“아, 그래요, 물론이지요, 얘야.”\n\n“제가 마님을 노엽게 했다면 죄송합니다. 하지만 누가 로바츠 씨를 두고 나쁜 말을 하는데 대꾸하지 않고 있을 수는 없습니다. 마님은 그이에게 매우 부당하셨습니다. 마님을 노엽게 하더라도 저는 그렇게 말해야 합니다.”\n\n“이봐요, 패니. 이건 너무하군요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “패니는 반 시간 내내 나를 꾸짖었어요. 남편이 새로 사귄 이 친구를 두고 내가 축하해 주지 않았다고 말이지요. 그런데 이제 또 그 모든 것을 처음부터 다시 시작하려 하는군요. 그건 내가 견딜 수 있는 정도를 넘어요. 달리 특별히 할 말이 없다면 이만 나가 주는 게 좋겠어요.” 그렇게 말하는 레이디 러프턴의 얼굴은 굽힘이 없고 엄하고 매서웠다.\n\n로바츠 부인은 전에는 오랜 친구에게서 그런 말을 들어 본 적이 없었다. 실은 누구에게서도 그런 말을 들어 본 적이 없었기에, 어떻게 처신해야 할지 알 수 없었다.\n\n“좋습니다, 레이디 러프턴,” 그녀가 말했다. “그러면 저는 가겠습니다. 안녕히 계십시오.”\n\n“잘 가요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말하고, 탁자를 향해 몸을 돌려 서류를 정리하기 시작했다. 패니는 전에는 프램리 코트를 떠나 자기 목사관으로 돌아갈 때 따뜻한 포옹 없이 나선 적이 없었다. 이제 그녀는 손조차 잡히지 못한 채 그렇게 하게 되었다. 일이 여기까지 와서, 정말로 두 사람 사이에 다툼이—영원한 다툼이—생기게 되었단 말인가?\n\n“패니가 가요, 어머니,” 레이디 메러디스가 말했다. “어머니가 다시 내려오시기 전에 집에 가 있을 거예요.”\n\n“어쩔 수 없구나, 얘야. 패니는 하고 싶은 대로 해야지. 나는 그 사람의 행동을 판단하는 사람이 아니야. 방금 그 사람이 나에게 그렇게 말했으니.”\n\n로바츠 부인은 그런 말을 하지 않았지만, 그것을 짚어 말하기에는 자존심이 너무 높았다. 그래서 그녀는 조용한 걸음으로 문을 나섰고, 그러고 나서 레이디 메러디스는 어머니에게 화해의 귀엣말을 시도해 본 뒤 그녀를 따라 나왔다. 아, 그 화해의 귀엣말은 아무런 소용이 없었다!\n\n두 여인은 계단을 내려오는 동안 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 응접실에 다시 들어서자 새하얗게 질린 얼굴로 서로를 마주 보았다. 이제 어떻게 해야 하는가? 이런 비극은 두 사람이 조금도 예상하지 못한 일이었다. 정말로 패니 로바츠가, 결혼 전이나 후로나 이 집안의 양녀처럼 대접받아 온 그녀가, 선포된 원수가 되어 레이디 러프턴의 집에서 걸어 나가야 하는 것인가?\n\n“오, 패니, 왜 우리 어머니에게 그런 식으로 대답했어요?” 레이디 메러디스가 말했다. “어머니가 속상해하시는 게 보였잖아요. 로바츠 씨 일 말고도 어머니를 속상하게 하는 일이 또 있었어요.”\n\n“그럼 저스티니아는 누가 조지 경을 공격하는데도 대답하지 않겠어요?”\n\n“그럼요, 우리 어머니라면 안 하지요. 어머니가 무슨 말씀을 하시든 하시게 두고, 조지 경은 스스로 싸우게 놔둘 거예요.”\n\n“아, 하지만 저스티니아는 사정이 달라요. 저스티니아는 어머니의 딸이고, 조지 경은—어머니는 조지 경이 하는 일을 두고 그런 식으로 말할 엄두를 내지 않으실 거예요.”\n\n“아니에요, 마음만 있으시면 하실 거예요. 패니를 어머니께 올려 보낸 게 후회돼요.”\n\n“이렇게 끝난 게 오히려 잘된 일이에요, 저스티니아. 그게 로바츠 씨에 대한 어머니의 생각이라면, 우리가 그것을 아는 게 차라리 나아요. 제가 어머니께 입은 은혜가 아무리 크고 저스티니아를 사랑하는 마음이 아무리 깊어도, 남편이 욕을 듣는다면 저는 이 집에 오지 않겠어요. 어느 집에도요.”\n\n“내 사랑하는 패니, 화난 두 사람이 마주 앉으면 어떻게 되는지는 우리 모두 알아요.”\n\n“어머니께 올라갈 때 저는 화나지 않았어요. 조금도요.”\n\n“돌아보는 건 소용없어요. 이제 우리가 어떻게 해야 할까요, 패니?”\n\n“집으로 돌아가는 게 좋겠어요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. “가서 물건을 챙겨 두고, 제임스를 보내 가져오게 하겠어요.”\n\n“점심때까지 기다려요. 그러면 떠나기 전에 우리 어머니에게 입을 맞출 수 있을 거예요.”\n\n“아니에요, 저스티니아. 기다릴 수 없어요. 이번 우편으로 로바츠 씨에게 답장을 써야 하는데, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 생각해 봐야 해요. 여기서는 그 편지를 쓸 수 없고, 우편은 네 시에 나가요.” 그러고 로바츠 부인은 마지막으로 떠날 준비를 하며 의자에서 일어섰다.\n\n“저녁 식사 전에 찾아갈게요,” 레이디 메러디스가 말했다. “그리고 좋은 소식을 가져갈 수 있으면, 패니가 나와 함께 이리로 돌아오기를 바라겠어요. 패니와 우리 어머니가 서로 원수가 된 채로 내가 프램리를 떠난다는 건 있을 수 없는 일이에요.”\n\n이에 로바츠 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그리고 아주 얼마 지나지 않아 그녀는 자기 아이 방에서 아이들에게 입을 맞추며, 큰아이에게 아빠에 대한 말을 가르치고 있었다. 그러나 가르치는 동안에도 눈에는 눈물이 고였고, 어린 녀석은 모든 것이 온전하지 않다는 것을 알았다.\n\n그리고 그녀는 두 시쯤까지 거기 앉아 아이들을 위해 자잘한 일들을 하며, 그 일을 편지를 시작하지 않는 핑계로 삼았다. 그러나 그러고 나니 남은 시간은 두 시간뿐이었고, 편지는 쓰기 어려울지도 몰랐다. 생각이 필요하고 고쳐 쓸 곳도 있어서 어쩌면 한 번 이상 옮겨 적어야 할지도 몰랐다. 돈이라면 집에 있었다. 적어도 마크가 지금 원하는 만큼은 있었다. 다만 그것을 보내면 그녀에게는 거의 한 푼도 남지 않을 것이었다. 그러나 정말 필요할 때는 그이가 말한 대로 데이비스에게 부탁할 수 있었다.\n\n그래서 그녀는 응접실에서 필기함을 꺼내 앉아 편지를 썼다. 쓰기 어려웠지만, 생각했던 만큼 오래 걸리지는 않았다. 어려웠던 것은 그이에게 사실을 말해야 한다고 느꼈으면서도, 친구들 사이에서 누리는 그이의 즐거움을 다 망쳐 놓고 싶지는 않았기 때문이다. 그러면서도 그녀는 레이디 러프턴이 매우 화가 나 있다고 그에게 알렸다. “정말이지 터무니없이 화가 나셨어요,” 그녀는 자기가 그의 편에 서지 않은 것이 아님을 보이려고 그렇게 끼워 넣었다. “그래서 우리는 아주 다투고 말았어요. 이 일이 저를 불행하게 만들어요. 당신도 그러실 줄 알아요, 여보. 하지만 우리는 그분이 마음속으로는 얼마나 좋은 분인지 알고, 저스티니아는 그분에게 다른 걱정거리가 있었다고 해요. 당신이 집에 돌아오기 전에 다 풀어지기를 바라요. 다만 사랑하는 마크, 지난 편지에서 말한 것보다 더 오래 있지는 말아 주세요.” 그러고는 아기들에 대한 서너 문단과 학교에 대한 두 문단이 있었는데, 그건 내가 빼도 좋을 것이다.\n\n편지를 막 끝내고, 5파운드짜리 지폐 두 장을 고스란히, 무모하게도 그 안에 넣은 채 봉투에 넣으려고 조심스레 접고 있을 때, 그녀는 작은 쪽문에서 현관으로 이어지는 자갈길에 나는 발소리를 들었다. 그 길은 응접실 창 가까이로 지나갔고, 그녀는 스쳐 가는 외투의 마지막 자락을 가까스로 볼 수 있었다. “저스티니아구나,” 그녀는 마음속으로 말했다. 그러고 아침의 그 사건을 또 이야기하게 될 생각에 마음이 어지러워졌다. “어떻게 해야 하지,” 그녀는 앞서도 마음속으로 이렇게 말했었다. “저스티니아가 나더러 어머니께 용서를 빌라고 하면? 그이가 잘못했다고 그 앞에서 인정하지는 않겠어.”\n\n그러고 문이 열렸다—손님은 하인의 도움 없이 몸소 들어섰던 것이다—그리고 레이디 러프턴이 그녀 앞에 서 있었다. “패니,” 그녀가 곧바로 말했다. “내가 용서를 빌러 왔어요.”\n\n“오, 레이디 러프턴!”\n\n“조금 전 패니가 나에게 왔을 때 나는 몹시 시달리고 있었어요. 한 가지 일만이 아니었지요, 얘야. 그렇더라도 남편에 대해 그런 식으로 말하지는 않아야 했어요. 그래서 용서를 빌러 왔어요.”\n\n이 말이 끝났을 때 로바츠 부인은 이미 대답할 수 있는 상태를 넘어섰다. 적어도 말로는 대답할 수 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 눈에 눈물을 가득 담고 오랜 친구의 품에 몸을 던졌다. “오, 레이디 러프턴!” 그녀가 다시 흐느끼며 말했다.\n\n“나를 용서해 주겠지요?” 젊은 친구의 포옹에 답하며 마님이 말했다. “자, 됐어요. 오늘 아침 패니가 내 골방을 나간 뒤로 나도 조금도 행복하지 않았어요. 패니도 그랬으리라 생각해요. 하지만 패니, 우리는 서로를 너무 사랑하고 서로를 너무 잘 알아서 오래 다툴 수는 없지요. 그렇지 않아요?”\n\n“오, 그렇습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“물론 그렇지요. 친구란 날마다 길가에서 주울 수 있는 것이 아니고, 가볍게 버릴 수 있는 것도 아니에요. 이제 앉아요, 얘야. 우리 조금 이야기를 해요. 아, 모자를 벗어야겠어요. 패니가 끈을 어찌나 당겼는지 숨이 막힐 지경이었어요.” 그러고 레이디 러프턴은 모자를 탁자에 내려놓고 소파 구석에 편안히 앉았다.\n\n“얘야,” 그녀가 말했다. “어떤 여자가 다른 사람에게 지는 의무 가운데 남편에게 지는 의무만 한 것은 없어요. 그러니 오늘 아침 로바츠 씨를 위해 나선 것은 아주 옳았어요.”\n\n이 말에 로바츠 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 마님의 손 안에 자기 손을 넣고 가볍게 쥐었다.\n\n“그리고 나는 패니가 그렇게 하는 동안 줄곧 패니를 사랑했어요. 정말이에요, 얘야. 조금 사납기는 했지만요. 저스티니아도 그건 인정해요. 그 애는 패니가 떠난 뒤로 줄곧 나를 몰아세웠어요. 그리고 정말이지, 그 예쁜 눈으로 그런 눈빛을 낼 줄은 몰랐어요.”\n\n“오, 레이디 러프턴!”\n\n“하지만 나도 어지간히 사나운 얼굴을 했겠지요. 그러니 그 이야기는 더 하지 않기로 해요. 그럴까요? 그런데 이제 이 착한 남편 이야기를 해 봐요.”\n\n“레이디 러프턴, 그이를 용서해 주십시오.”\n\n“그래요, 패니가 그렇게 청하니 용서하지요. 공작에 대해서는 더 아무 말도 하지 않겠어요. 지금도, 그이가 돌아온 뒤에도요. 한마디도요. 어디 보자, 그이가 돌아오는 건—언제였지요?”\n\n“다음 주 수요일인 것 같습니다.”\n\n“아, 수요일. 그럼 수요일에 저택으로 저녁을 먹으러 오라고 전해요. 그때면 시간이 맞겠지요. 그리고 이 끔찍한 공작에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않겠어요.”\n\n“정말 감사합니다, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“하지만 이걸 알아 둬요, 얘야. 내 말을 믿어요. 그이는 그런 친구들이 없는 편이 나아요.”\n\n“오, 저도 압니다. 훨씬 낫습니다.”\n\n“그렇게 인정해 주니 반갑군요. 나는 패니가 공작 편을 드는 것 같다고 생각했어요.”\n\n“오, 아닙니다, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“그러면 됐어요. 그리고 이제 내 말을 들어 준다면, 지금처럼 착하고 사랑스럽고 다정한 아내로서 패니의 영향력을 써서 그이가 다시는 거기 가지 않게 해요. 나는 늙은 여자고 그이는 젊은 남자니, 그이가 나를 시대에 뒤진 사람이라 여기는 것도 아주 자연스러운 일이에요. 그 일로 화를 내는 게 아니에요. 그러나 그이는 오랜 친구들을 지키는 것이 자기에게 더 낫다는 것을, 모든 면에서 더 낫다는 것을 알게 될 거예요. 마음의 평안에도 더 낫고, 성직자로서 인격에도 더 낫고, 주머니에도 더 낫고, 아이들과 패니에게도 더 낫고—그리고 영원한 복락에도 더 나아요. 공작은 그이가 찾아 나설 벗이 아니에요. 또 저쪽에서 찾아온다 해도 이끌려 가서는 안 되지요.”\n\n그러고 나서 레이디 러프턴이 말을 그쳤고, 패니 로바츠는 그녀의 발 앞에 무릎을 꿇은 채 친구의 무릎에 얼굴을 감추고 흐느꼈다. 남편이 스스로 판단할 능력이 있다는 말은 이제 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n“그리고 이제 나는 또 가 봐야 해요. 그런데 저스티니아가 나에게서 약속을 받아 냈어요. 그것도 아주 엄숙하게 받아 냈지요. 오늘 저녁 식사에 패니를 데려오겠다고, 필요하다면 억지로라도 데려오겠다고요. 그 애와 화해할 길이 그것뿐이었으니, 나를 곤경에 빠뜨리지 말아요.” 물론 패니는 프램리 코트에 가서 저녁을 먹겠다고 했다.\n\n“그리고 그 편지는 절대로 보내지 말아요,” 마님이 방을 나서면서, 로바츠 부인의 책상 위에 주소가 적힌 채 놓인 그 서한을 우산으로 찌르며 말했다. “거기 무엇이 들었는지 나는 아주 잘 알겠어요. 처음부터 다 고쳐 써야 해요, 얘야.” 그러고 나서 레이디 러프턴은 갔다.\n\n로바츠 부인은 곧 책상으로 달려가 자기 편지를 찢어 열었다. 시계를 보니 네 시가 지나 있었다. 다른 편지를 겨우 시작했을 때 우체부가 왔다. “오, 메리,” 그녀가 말했다. “그 사람을 좀 기다리게 해 다오. 십오 분만 기다려 주면 1실링을 주겠다고 해.”\n\n“그러실 것 없습니다, 마님. 맥주 한 잔 주시면 됩니다.”\n\n“그래, 메리. 하지만 너무 많이는 주지 마라. 편지들을 흘리고 다니면 안 되니까. 십 분이면 준비될 거야.”\n\n그리고 오 분 만에 그녀는 아주 다른 종류의 편지를 급히 갈겨썼다. 그러나 그이가 돈을 곧 원할지도 몰랐으므로, 그녀는 그것을 하루라도 늦추려 하지 않았다.\n\n# 제6장. 해럴드 스미스 씨의 강연\n\n전체로 보아 콜디코츠의 모임은 매우 유쾌했고, 시간도 충분히 빠르게 흘러갔다. 로바츠 씨가 그곳에서 사귄 으뜸 벗은, 사워비 씨를 빼고 말한다면 던스터블 양이었다. 그녀는 그에게 대단한 호감을 품은 듯했으나, 서플하우스 씨의 아첨에는 그다지 마음을 열지 않았고, 주인에게조차 좋은 예의가 요구하는 정도 이상으로 각별히 정중하지는 않았다. 하지만 서플하우스 씨와 사워비 씨는 둘 다 독신이었고, 마크 로바츠는 아내가 있는 사람이었다.\n\n로바츠는 사워비 씨와 러프턴 경과 그의 재정에 관하여 한 번 이상 이야기를 나누었는데, 할 수만 있었다면 기꺼이 피하고 싶은 이야기였다. 사워비는 일과 즐거움을 늘 뒤섞어 놓는 부류의 사람, 그리고 머릿속에 밀고 나가야 할 무슨 계획을 언제나 하나쯤 품고 있는 부류의 사람이었다. 이런 부류의 사람들은 대체로 날마다 할 일이 없고 정해진 노동의 일과도 없다. 그러나 그런 것을 가진 사람들보다 오히려 훨씬 더 끊임없이 애를 쓰고 있는 것이 아닌지는 의심해 볼 만하다.\n\n“러프턴은 어찌나 굼뜬지,” 사워비 씨가 말했다. “약속했을 때 왜 그 일을 단번에 처리해 두지 않았을까요? 게다가 프램리 코트의 그 노부인을 어찌나 무서워하는지. 뭐, 이보세요, 무슨 말을 하셔도 좋습니다. 그분은 노부인이고, 앞으로 더 젊어지실 일도 없지요. 아무튼 러프턴에게 편지를 써서 이렇게 늦어지면 내가 곤란하다고 전해 주세요. 그는 당신 말이라면 무엇이든 들을 사람이니까요.”\n\n마크는 쓰겠다고 말했고, 실제로 썼다. 그러나 처음에는 자신이 끌려 들어간 대화의 어조가 마음에 들지 않았다. 레이디 러프턴이 노부인이라 불리는 것을 듣기가 몹시 괴로웠고, 러프턴 경이 자기 재산을 떼어 내는 일이 옳은지 아닌지를 따지는 것도 그에 못지않게 괴로웠다. 습관이 그것을 쉽게 만들어 주기까지 이것은 그에게 거북한 일이었다. 그러나 차츰 그의 감각은 무디어졌고, 그는 벗 사워비의 말투에 스스로를 익숙하게 만들어 갔다.\n\n그러고 나서 토요일 오후에 그들은 모두 바체스터로 건너갔다. 해럴드 스미스는 지난 사십팔 시간 동안 사라왁과 라부안, 뉴기니와 솔로몬 제도로 넘칠 만큼 꽉 채워진 사람이 되어 있었다. 일시적인 전문 분야에 짓눌린 모든 사람이 그렇듯, 그는 당장은 그 밖의 어떤 것도 믿지 않았고, 자기 주변의 누구라도 다른 무엇을 믿는 것을 참지 못했다. 사람들은 그를 파푸아 자작이라, 보르네오 남작이라 불렀다. 그를 놀리는 농담에 앞장선 아내는 자기 칭호를 달라고 우겼다. 던스터블 양은 남태평양 섬사람이 아니면 누구와도 혼인하지 않겠노라 맹세했고, 마크에게는 향료 주교의 수입과 직무가 제안되었다. 프라우디 집안 사람들도 이런 조그만 비꼬는 농담에 압도적인 엄격함으로 맞서지는 않았다. 알맞은 기회에 스스로를 풀어놓는 것은 달콤한 일이고, 이때가 프라우디 부인이 스스로를 풀어놓기에 알맞은 기회였다. 어떤 인간도 하루의 온 시간을 진지하게 지혜로울 수는 없다. 그리하여 이 즐거운 시간에는 평소 지혜로운 그 인간, 곧 주교도 자신의 진지한 지혜를 얼마간 내려놓았다.\n\n“내일은 다섯 시에 만찬을 할까 하는데, 레이디 파푸아,” 익살맞은 주교가 말했다. “그것이 각하와 국사(國事)에 맞겠소? 허! 허! 허!” 그러고서 이 선량한 고위 성직자는 그 농담에 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n쉰 살쯤 되는 젊은 신사와 숙녀들은, 자기들을 단속할 스물다섯이나 서른 살의 멘토가 곁에 없을 때면 얼마나 유쾌하게 농담을 하고 희롱을 하고 서로를 찔러 대며, 옆구리를 붙잡고 웃고, 조그만 빈정거림을 주고받고, 별명을 즐거워하는가. 프램리의 교구 사제도 아마 그런 멘토로 여겨졌을지 모른다. 자기 바로 주변의 사람들에게 스스로를 맞추는 그 능력을 그가 그토록 자랑스러워하지 않았다면 말이다. 그리하여 그도 레이디 파푸아에게 말을 걸고 남작을 두고 익살을 부렸는데, 해럴드 스미스 씨 본인에게는 그것이 온전히 만족스럽지는 않았다.\n\n해럴드 스미스 씨는 진지했으므로 이런 희롱들이 그리 달갑지 않았다. 그는 석 달쯤이면 영국 세상을 설득해서 뉴기니를 문명화하게 만들 수 있고, 바셋주의 세상은 하룻밤의 노력으로 자기 편에 서게 할 수 있다고 여겼다. 그는 왜 남들이 자기보다 덜 진지해야 하는지를 이해하지 못했고, 우리 벗 마크의 상냥한 농담을 다소 딱딱하게 못마땅해하는 쪽으로 기울었다.\n\n“남작을 기다리게 해서는 안 되겠지요,” 바체스터로 떠날 준비를 하면서 마크가 말했다.\n\n“남작이라는 말이 무슨 뜻인지 나는 모르겠소, 선생,” 해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “하지만 내일 당신이 설교단에 올라가 콜디코츠의 촌뜨기들 사이로 모금 모자를 돌릴 때에는, 아마 그 농담이 당신에게로 돌아갈 거요.”\n\n“유리 집에 사는 사람은 돌을 던지면 안 되지요. 그렇지 않아요, 남작?” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “로바츠 씨의 설교는 당신 강연과 너무 가까운 친척이라, 그분도 웃을 처지가 못 될 텐데요.”\n\n“바깥 세상을 가르치는 일이 목사들 손으로 이루어질 때까지 우리가 아무것도 할 수 없다면,” 해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “바깥 세상은 아무래도 아주 오래 기다려야 할 거요.”\n\n“그런 일은 하원 의원이자 장관이 되려는 사람이 아니면 아무도 할 수 없지요,” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 속삭였다.\n\n그리하여 날 선 도구로 조금씩 찌르고 막는 일이 있었음에도 그들은 다 함께 아주 유쾌했다. 그리고 세 시에 마차 행렬이 바체스터를 향해 출발했는데, 물론 주교의 마차가 앞장을 섰다. 그러나 주교 각하는 그 안에 없었다.\n\n“프라우디 부인, 저를 함께 태워 주시겠지요,” 커다란 돌계단을 내려오면서 마지막 순간에 던스터블 양이 말했다. “슬로프 씨에 관한 그 이야기의 남은 대목을 듣고 싶어서요.”\n\n이 말이 모든 것을 뒤집어 놓았다. 주교는 아내와 스미스 부인, 마크 로바츠와 함께 가기로 되어 있었고, 사워비 씨는 자기 사륜 경마차에 던스터블 양을 태울 수 있도록 일을 짜 놓았던 터였다. 그러나 던스터블 양에게 무엇이든 안 된다고 하는 것은 아무도 꿈에조차 생각하지 못하는 일이었다. 물론 마크가 자리를 내주었다. 그러나 결국에는 주교가 자기 마차에 특별한 애착은 없노라고 선언하는 데까지 이르렀는데, 그가 그렇게 한 것은 아내의 눈에서 날아온 눈짓에 따른 것이었다. 그러자 물론 다른 변경들이 잇따랐고, 마침내 사워비 씨와 해럴드 스미스가 그 경마차의 공동 탑승자가 되었다.\n\n가엾은 강연자는 자리에 앉으면서 지난 이틀 동안 해 오던 것과 같은 말을 몇 마디 했다. 마음이 가득하면 입이 말하는 법이니까. 그러나 그가 말을 건 상대는 참을성이 없는 청자였다. “남태평양 섬사람들 따위 빌어먹을,” 사워비 씨가 말했다. “몇 분 뒤면 도자기 가게에 들어간 황소처럼 다 자네 마음대로 하게 될 텐데, 제발 그때까지는 좀 조용히 있게 해 주게.” 던스터블 양을 동행으로 삼으려던 사워비 씨의 조그만 계획이 그리 대수롭지 않은 것은 아니었던 모양이다. 아니, 그의 조그만 계획들 가운데 대수롭지 않은 것은 거의 없었다고 해도 좋다. 지금 이 순간 그는 마차에 몸을 던져 기대고 잠들 준비를 했다. 매부와 단둘이 마주 앉아 이야기해서는 자기 계획을 하나도 밀고 나갈 수 없었다.\n\n그러고서 프라우디 부인이 슬로프 씨에 관한 이야기를 시작했다. 아니, 다시 이어 갔다. 그녀는 이 신사에 관하여 말하기를 아주 좋아했다. 한때는 그녀가 아끼던 전속 사제였으나 이제는 가장 지독한 원수가 된 사람이었다. 이야기를 하는 동안 그녀는 이따금 던스터블 양에게 귓속말을 해야 했다. 어느 유부녀에 관한, 어머 어머 하게 되는 조그만 일화가 한둘 있었는데, 젊은 로바츠 씨의 귀에 온전히 어울리는 것은 아니었기 때문이다. 그러나 해럴드 스미스 부인은 그것을 큰 소리로 하라고 우겼고, 던스터블 양은 프라우디 부인이 눈짓을 하는데도 그 부인의 뜻을 채워 주었다.\n\n“뭐라고요, 그 부인의 손에 입을 맞추었다고요, 성직자가?” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “성직자들이 그런 짓을 하는 줄은 몰랐어요, 로바츠 씨.”\n\n“고요한 물이 가장 깊지요,” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“쉬이이,” 프라우디 부인은 말했다기보다 그런 얼굴을 지었다. “그 못된 사람이 내 마음에 안긴 괴로움은 하마터면 내 가슴을 무너뜨릴 지경이었소. 그런데 그 내내, 알겠소, 그이가 구애하고 있던 상대는—” 그러고서 프라우디 부인은 어떤 이름을 속삭였다.\n\n“뭐라고요, 수석 사제 부인이라니!” 던스터블 양이 어찌나 큰 소리로 외쳤던지, 그 말을 얻어들은 뒷마차의 마부가 말들에게 혀를 차 댔다.\n\n“부주교의 처제라니!” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 소리를 질렀다.\n\n“그다음에는 무엇을 노렸을지 모를 일이네요,” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n“그때는 그 사람이 수석 사제 부인이 아니었소, 알겠소,” 프라우디 부인이 설명하듯 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 그 성당 참사회에는 참 흥겨운 패가 모였다고 해야겠네요,” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “로바츠 씨도 바체스터에서 그 한 사람이 되셔야겠어요.”\n\n“다만 로바츠 부인이 좋아하지 않을지도 모르지요,” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“게다가 그이가 주교에게 걸었던 그 계략들이란!” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“사랑과 전쟁에서는 무엇이든 정당하다잖아요,” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 그 일을 시작할 때 자기가 누구를 상대해야 하는지는 조금도 몰랐소,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“주교님이 그이에게는 너무 벅찬 상대였겠지요,” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 아주 짓궂게 말을 보탰다.\n\n“주교가 아니었다면 다른 누군가가 그러했소. 그이는 온전한 망신을 지고 바체스터를 떠나야 했지. 그 뒤로는 어느 양초 장수의 아내와 혼인했다오.”\n\n“아내라고요!” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “참 대단한 사람이네요!”\n\n“과부 말이오. 하지만 그이에게는 그게 그거지.”\n\n“그 신사는 분명 금성이 하늘에 오르는 때에 태어났군요,” 스미스 부인이 말했다. “성직자들은 대개 그렇다고 하더군요, 로바츠 씨.” 그리하여 그날 바체스터로 들어가는 길에 프라우디 부인의 마차는 결코 가장 심심한 마차가 아니었다. 그리고 우리 벗 마크는 차츰 동행들에게 익숙해져서, 주교관에 이르기 전에는 던스터블 양이 아주 재미있는 사람이라고 스스로 인정하고 있었다.\n\n주교의 만찬에 오래 머물러 있을 수는 없다. 물론 그 나름으로는 아주 훌륭한 만찬이었다. 사워비 씨는 던스터블 양 옆자리에 앉는 데 성공하여 서플하우스 씨가 세워 둔 조그만 계획을 뒤집어 놓았고, 그 덕에 다시금 구름 없이 좋은 기분으로 빛났다. 그러나 해럴드 스미스 씨는 식탁보가 걷히자마자 곧바로 안절부절못하기 시작했다. 강연은 일곱 시에 시작해야 했고, 그의 시계로는 그 시각이 이미 와 있었다. 그는 사워비와 서플하우스가 바체스터 사람들을 애가 타고 조바심 나게 만들려고 일을 일부러 늦추고 있다고 단언했다. 그래서 주교는 참으로 주교다운 방식으로 자기 환대를 베풀 기회를 얻지 못했다.\n\n“사워비, 자네는 잊고 있군,” 서플하우스가 말했다. “여기 세상이 지난 두 주 동안 그것 말고는 아무것도 기다리지 않았다는 것을.”\n\n“그럼 세상을 당장 만족시켜 드려야지요,” 프라우디 부인의 조그만 고갯짓에 따르며 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. “가요, 그대,” 그러면서 그녀는 던스터블 양의 팔을 붙잡았다. “바체스터를 기다리게 하지 맙시다. 십오 분이면 준비가 되겠지요, 프라우디 부인?” 그러고서 그들은 미끄러져 나갔다.\n\n“그러면 우리는 클라레 한 잔 할 시간이 있겠구려,” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“저기, 대성당 종이 일곱 시를 치는군,” 시계 소리를 듣고 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “사람들이 왔다면 내가 그들을 기다리게 하는 것은 옳지 않소. 나는 가겠소.”\n\n“클라레 딱 한 잔만 하고 곧 떠납시다, 스미스 씨,” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“저 여자들이 나를 한 시간은 붙잡아 둘 거요,” 해럴드가 잔을 채우고 선 채로 마시면서 말했다. “일부러 그러는 거지.” 그는 자기 아내를 생각하고 있었으나, 주교에게는 손님이 실은 프라우디 부인을 두고 말하는 것처럼 들렸다.\n\n그들이 모두 직공 학원의 큰 방에 자리를 잡은 것은 꽤 늦은 시각이었다. 그러나 전체로 보아 이것이 그들에게 무슨 해가 되었는지는 나도 모르겠다. 주교관에서 온 일행을 빼면 스미스 씨의 청중은 대부분 바체스터의 상인들과 그 아내와 아이들이었고, 그들은 조바심 내지 않고 높은 분들을 기다렸다. 게다가 강연은 무료였다. 영국 사람은 자기가 어떤 대접을 받고 있는지 셈하고 따질 때에 언제나 이 사실을 마음에 새겨 둔다. 돈을 낼 때에는 자기 몫을 고르게 된다. 그때는 마음대로 조바심을 내도 좋고 내지 않아도 좋다. 그의 정의감이 그에게 그만큼을 가르쳐 주고, 그는 대개 그 감각에 따라 행동한다.\n\n그리하여 주교관 일행이 방에 들어서자 긴 의자에 앉아 있던 사람들이 정중하게 일어섰다. 그들의 자리는 앞줄에 마련되어 있었다. 팔걸이의자가 셋 있었는데, 얼마간 망설임이 있은 뒤 주교와 프라우디 부인과 던스터블 양이 그 자리를 채웠다. 스미스 부인은 그중 하나에 앉기를 딱 잘라 사양했다. 물론 그녀 자신이 인정한 대로, 그 섬들의 레이디 파푸아라는 지위는 그녀에게 얼마간 권리를 주기는 했다. 그리고 이 말이 아주 큰 소리로 나왔던 까닭에, 조그맣게 높인 단 위의 조그만 탁자 뒤에 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 들고 서 있던 스미스 씨의 귀에까지 닿았다. 그것이 그를 짜증나게 하고 적잖이 흐트러뜨렸다. 그는 레이디 파푸아에 관한 그 농담이 마음에 들지 않았다.\n\n그러고서 일행의 나머지 사람들은 붉은 천을 덮은 앞줄 긴 의자에 앉았다. “두 시간째쯤 되면 이게 아주 딱딱하고 아주 좁게 느껴질 걸세,” 사워비 씨가 말했고, 단 위의 스미스 씨는 또 그 말을 얻어듣고 장갑을 탁자에 내던졌다. 방 안 사람들이 다 그 말을 들었을 것 같았다.\n\n그리고 둘째 줄 자리에는 우리 일행의 몇몇과 악수를 나눈 신사가 한둘 있었다. 울라손의 손 씨가 있었는데, 성품 좋은 늙은 독신 신사로, 그의 저택은 바체스터에서 가까워 몸소 큰 불편을 겪지 않고도 들어올 수 있었다. 그 옆에는 성당 참사회의 노(老) 성직자 하딩 씨가 있었다. 프라우디 부인은 그와 아주 정중하게 악수를 하고, 그가 원한다면 자기 바로 뒤에 앉을 수 있도록 자리를 비켜 주었다. 그러나 하딩 씨는 그러기를 원하지 않았다. 주교에게 인사를 드린 뒤 그는 조용히 오랜 벗 손 씨의 옆으로 돌아갔고, 그로써 프라우디 부인을 노엽게 했는데, 그것은 그녀의 얼굴만 보아도 쉽게 알 수 있었다. 그리고 채드윅 씨도 그 자리에 있었으니 교구의 주교청 사무를 맡아보는 사람이었으나, 그 역시 앞에 말한 두 신사에게 붙어 있었다.\n\n이제 주교와 숙녀들이 자리를 잡았으므로, 해럴드 스미스 씨는 장갑을 다시 들었다가 또 내려놓고, 또렷하게 세 번 흠, 하고 목청을 가다듬은 뒤 시작했다.\n\n“오늘날 영국 제도(諸島)에서 이 시대가 지닌 가장 특이한 성격은,” 그가 말했다. “지위와 부와 교육에서 세상 앞에 높이 놓인 이들이, 사회의 계단에서 그만큼 높이 서지 못한 이들의 이익과 향상을 위하여, 보수도 대가도 없이 스스로 나서서 자기 시간과 지식을 내어 주려 한다는 점이었소.” 그러고서 그는 잠시 말을 멈추었는데, 그 사이에 스미스 부인은 시작치고는 꽤 괜찮다고 던스터블 양에게 말했고, 던스터블 양은 “저로서는 지위와 부와 교육에 아주 감사하는 마음이 든답니다” 하고 대답했다. 사워비 씨가 서플하우스 씨에게 눈을 찡긋했고, 서플하우스 씨는 눈을 아주 크게 뜨고는 어깨를 으쓱했다. 그러나 바체스터 사람들은 그것을 모두 좋게 받아들이고 강연자에게 손과 발의 박수를 보냈다.\n\n그러고서 그는 흡족해하며 다시 말을 이었다. “나는 이런 말을 나 자신을 두고 하는 것이 아니오—”\n\n“이제 와서 겸손을 부리려는 건 아니었으면 좋겠네요,” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 아주 새로운 일이지요,” 스미스 부인이 대답했다.\n\n“—그보다는 근래에 이따금 이 선한 일에 몸을 바쳐 온 고귀하고 재능 있는 여러 귀족과 하원 의원들을 두고 하는 말이오.” 그러고서 그는 귀족과 국회의원들의 긴 명단을 훑어 나갔는데, 물론 보아네르게스 경으로 시작해서 그린 워커 씨로 끝을 맺었다. 그린 워커 씨는 근래에 숙부의 힘으로 크루 정션 선거구에서 당선된 젊은 신사로, 이튼 학교에서 본을 보이는 대로 라틴어 문법학자들에 관한 강연을 한 것으로 곧바로 공적 생활에 발을 들인 사람이었다.\n\n“오늘 이 자리에서,” 스미스 씨가 말을 이었다. “우리의 목적은 저 멀리, 인도 제도 너머 남쪽 대양에 놓인 그 크고 웅장한 섬들에 관하여 무엇인가를 배우는 것이오. 그 땅은 풍성한 향료와 영광스러운 열매를 내고, 그 바다에는 진주와 산호가 박혀 있소. 파푸아와 필리핀, 보르네오와 몰루카 제도가 그것이오. 벗들이여, 여러분은 지도에 익숙하고, 적도가 그 먼 바다들을 지나며 스스로 그려 내는 길을 알고 있소.” 그러자 많은 머리가 아래로 숙여지고 종이 넘기는 소리가 부스럭거렸다. “사회의 계단에서 그만큼 높이 서지 못한” 이들 가운데 적잖은 수가 지도를 가지고 와서, 이 놀라운 섬들이 어디쯤 있는지 기억을 되살렸기 때문이다.\n\n그러고서 스미스 씨도 손에 지도를 들고, 이따금 벽에 걸린 다른 큰 지도를 가리키며 이 문제의 지리로 들어갔다. “그런 건 바체스터까지 오지 않고도 우리 지도책에서 찾아낼 수 있었을 텐데요,” 저 매정한 조력자 해럴드 스미스 부인이 아주 잔인하게 말했다. 게다가 몹시 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 말이었다. 우리가 찾아보면 스스로 알아낼 수 있는 것이 그토록 많으나, 누가 따로 말해 주지 않으면 결코 알아내지 않는 것도 그만큼 많으니 말이다. 그러니 라부안의 위도와 경도가 그런 것들 가운데 하나, 아니 오히려 둘이 되지 못할 이유가 무엇인가?\n\n그러고서 보르네오와 셀레베스와 길롤로를 지나, 마카사르 해협과 몰루카 항로를 지나는 그 선의 길을 빠짐없이 짚어 보인 뒤, 해럴드 스미스 씨는 더 높은 비상으로 올라갔다. “그러나,” 그가 말했다. “사람이 손을 펴서 그 선물을 받으려 하지 않는다면, 하느님이 사람에게 주실 수 있는 그 모든 것이 무슨 소용이오? 그리고 이 손을 펴는 일이 문명화의 과정이 아니면 무엇이겠소. 그렇소, 벗들이여, 문명화의 과정이오. 저 남태평양 섬사람들은 자비로운 섭리가 그들에게 내려 줄 수 있는 모든 것을 가지고 있소. 그러나 그 모든 것은 교육이 없으면 아무것도 아니오. 그 교육과 그 문명을 그들에게 내려 줄 사람은 바로 여러분이오. 그렇소, 벗들이여, 여러분이오. 바로 여러분, 바체스터의 시민인 여러분 말이오.” 그러고서 그는 발과 손이 일하러 나설 수 있도록 다시 말을 멈추었다. 발과 손은 과연 일하러 나섰고, 그동안 스미스 씨는 물을 조금 마셨다.\n\n이제 그는 완전히 제 물을 만났고, 주먹으로 탁자를 두드리는 알맞은 방식도 찾아냈다. 사워비 씨에게서 흘러나온 몇 마디가 이따금 그의 귀에까지 길을 찾아들기는 했다. 그러나 자기 목소리의 울림이 익숙한 매력을 함께 데려왔으므로, 그는 진부한 소리에서 뻔한 소리로, 뻔한 소리에서 다시 진부한 소리로, 스스로에게는 매혹적인 웅변을 타고 달려 나갔다.\n\n“문명이여,” 그가 눈과 손을 천장으로 들어 올리며 외쳤다. “오, 문명이여—”\n\n“이제 앞으로 한 시간 반은 우리에게 기회가 없겠군,” 서플하우스 씨가 신음하며 말했다.\n\n해럴드 스미스가 한쪽 눈을 그에게로 내리떴으나, 그 눈은 곧바로 천장으로 되돌아갔다.\n\n“오, 문명이여! 인류를 고귀하게 하고 사람을 신들과 같게 만드는 너여, 무엇이 너와 같으랴?” 이 대목에서 프라우디 부인은 뚜렷한 못마땅함의 표시를 드러냈고, 그 훌륭한 고위 성직자가 잠들어 있지 않았다면 주교도 틀림없이 그 마음을 같이했을 것이다. 그러나 스미스 씨는 알아차리지 못한 채, 아니 적어도 아랑곳하지 않은 채 계속했다.\n\n“무엇이 너와 같으랴? 너는 메마른 들판을 기름지게 하는 물길이다. 네가 오기까지는 모든 것이 어둡고 쓸쓸하다. 그러나 네가 이르면 한낮의 해가 비쳐 나오고 땅은 그 소출을 내어 주며, 바위의 깊은 창자가 그 조공을 바친다. 무디고 흉하던 형상들이 우아함과 아름다움을 입고, 식물의 존재가 천상의 생명의 반열로 올라선다. 그때 또한 천재가 반투명한 갑주를 온몸에 두르고 나타나, 그 손에 온 땅의 표면을 잡아쥐고 땅 한 뙈기 한 뙈기를 자기 목적에 복종시킨다. 천재, 문명의 아이요 예술의 어머니여!”\n\n「진보의 계보」에서 따온 마지막 조그만 대목은 큰 성공을 거두어, 바체스터 전체가 손과 발로 일하러 나섰다. 심사 고약한 저 귀족적 앞줄과 그 끝에 놓인 팔걸이의자 셋만은 빼고 말이다. 그 귀족적 앞줄은 스스로가 문명과 너무 가까운 사이라서 문명 따위에 크게 마음을 쓸 것이 없다고 느꼈다. 그리고 팔걸이의자 셋은, 아니 그중에서도 프라우디 부인을 담고 있던 그 특별한 하나는, 강연자의 말 속에 어떤 이교스러움, 거의 불신앙에 이르는 이교도적 감상이 들어 있다고 여겼으니, 교회의 기둥인 그녀로서는 더구나 이렇게 공개된 회중 가운데 앉은 처지로서는 참아 넘길 수 없는 것이었다.\n\n“우리가 바라보아야 할 것은 문명이오,” 강연자라면 잘 아는 방식으로 시에서 산문으로 내려오면서, 그럼으로써 둘 모두의 값을 보여 주면서 해럴드 스미스 씨가 말을 이었다. “이 섬들의 어떠한 물질적 진보를 위해서든 말이오. 그리고—”\n\n“그리고 기독교요,” 프라우디 부인이 외쳤고, 모인 사람들은 크게 놀랐으며 주교는 완전히 깨어났다. 주교는 익숙한 그 목소리에 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 “물론이오, 물론이오” 하고 소리쳤다.\n\n“옳소, 옳소, 옳소,” 이 도시에서 특히 프라우디 부인의 신학 학파에 속한 사람들이 긴 의자에서 말했고, 그 목소리들 가운데는 그녀가 몸소 크게 애를 써 준 새 성당 관리인의 목소리가 또렷이 들렸다.\n\n“아, 그렇소, 물론 기독교요,” 해럴드 스미스가 말했으나, 그 끼어듦이 그에게 좋게 작용한 것 같지는 않았다.\n\n“기독교와 안식일 준수요,” 프라우디 부인이 외쳤다. 이제 대중의 귀를 얻은 그녀는 그것을 놓지 않으려는 기색이 뚜렷했다. “저 섬사람들은 안식일을 거룩하게 지키지 않고서는 결코 번영할 수 없다는 것을 결코 잊지 맙시다.”\n\n가엾은 스미스 씨는 자기 높은 말에서 그렇게 무례하게 끌어내려진 뒤 다시는 그 위에 올라앉을 수 없었고, 자신에게는 조금도 편안하지 않은 방식으로 강연을 마쳤다. 그의 앞 탁자에는 통계 뭉치가 커다랗게 놓여 있었는데, 청중의 감정을 온전히 사로잡은 다음에 그것으로 그들의 이성을 설득할 작정이었다. 그러나 그 통계들은 아주 지루하고 맥없이 떨어졌다. 그리고 말이 끊긴 그 순간에 그는, 자기가 언급한 그 물질적 진보는 돈 없이는 이룰 수 없으며, 그러므로 자기 앞에 있는 바체스터 사람들이 남자답게 형제답게 지갑을 들고 나서야 마땅하다고 설명하려는 참이었다. 그것도 시도해 보기는 했다. 그러나 팔걸이의자에서 날아온 그 치명적인 습격의 순간부터, 이제 이 시간의 주인공은 프라우디 부인이라는 것이 그에게도 다른 모든 사람에게도 분명했다. 그의 때는 지나갔고, 바체스터 사람들은 그의 호소에 지푸라기만큼도 마음을 쓰지 않았다.\n\n이런 까닭으로 강연은 누구도 예상하지 못한 만큼, 꼬박 이십 분이나 일찍 끝났다. 사워비 씨와 서플하우스 씨는 크게 기뻐하여 그날 저녁 프라우디 부인에게 감사를 표하는 결의를 발의하고 통과시켰다. 그들에게는 잠자리에 들기 전에 아직 흥겨운 일이 남아 있었기 때문이다.\n\n“로바츠, 잠깐 이리 오세요,” 직공 학원 문간에 서 있을 때 사워비 씨가 말했다. “주교 씨와 주교 부인을 따라가 버리지 마세요. 우리는 원틀리의 용에서 조그맣게 밤참을 하려는데, 우리가 겪은 일을 생각하면 정말이지 그게 꼭 필요하지 않겠어요. 주교관 하인 아무에게나 문을 열어 달라고 일러 두면 되지요.”\n\n마크는 그 제안을 아쉬운 마음으로 곱씹었다. 용기가 있었다면 그 밤참 자리에 기꺼이 끼었을 것이다. 그러나 그도 같은 옷을 입은 다른 많은 이들처럼 눈앞에 프라우디 부인에 대한 두려움을 두고 있었다.\n\n그리고 그들은 아주 흥겨운 밤참을 했다. 그러나 가엾은 해럴드 스미스 씨는 그 자리에서 가장 흥겨운 사람이 아니었다.\n\n# 제7장. 일요일 아침\n\n대체로 보아 마크 로바츠가 그 밤참 자리에 가지 않은 것은 아마도 잘된 일이었다. 그들이 자리에 앉은 것이 열한 시였고, 신사들이 잠자리에 든 것은 두 시가 가까워서였다. 기억해 두어야 할 것은, 그가 다가오는 일요일 아침에 해럴드 스미스 씨의 섬사람들에게 보내는 선교 사업을 위하여 자선 설교를 해야 했다는 사실이다. 그리고 사실을 말하자면, 그것은 이제 그에게 마음이 거의 가지 않는 일거리였다.\n\n처음 이 일을 청탁받았을 때, 그는 그런 일을 늘 진지하게 여겨 왔듯이 이 일도 충분히 진지하게 여겼고, 프램리를 떠나기 전에 그 자리를 위한 설교문을 완성해 두었다. 그러나 그 뒤로 이 일 전체에 조롱의 기운이 덮였고, 그는 자기 설교문 따위는 그다지 생각하지 않은 채 그 조롱에 함께 끼었으니, 이것이 이제 그로 하여금 다른 어떤 주제로든 강론을 골랐으면 좋겠다고 진심으로 바라게 만들었다.\n\n그는 잘 알고 있었다. 자기가 가장 힘주어 강조한 바로 그 대목들이 던스터블 양과 스미스 부인에게서 가장 큰 웃음을 끌어냈고, 자기 자신의 웃음도 가장 자주 터뜨리게 했던 것들이라는 사실을. 그러니 저 두 부인이 자기를 바라보고 있을 것이며, 자기 눈길을 붙잡으려 애쓸 것이며, 이미 강연자를 조롱거리로 만들었듯이 자기도 조롱거리로 만들 것이라는 사실을 알면서, 아니 알 수밖에 없으면서, 그가 어떻게 그 문제들을 걸맞은 마음가짐으로 설교할 수 있겠는가?\n\n이 점에서 그는 자기도 모르게 두 부인 가운데 한 사람에게 부당했다. 던스터블 양은 웃음에, 아니 거의 공정하게 말하자면 장난에까지 소질이 있었으나, 종교나 종교에 속한다고 자기가 여기는 것을 조롱할 마음은 조금도 없었다. 그런 것들 가운데 프라우디 부인을 넣지는 않았으리라고 짐작해도 좋을 것이다. 그 부인을 두고는 얼마든지 웃을 뜻이 있었으니 말이다. 그러나 마크가 그녀를 더 잘 알았더라면, 그녀가 완벽하게 예의를 지키며 자기 설교를 끝까지 앉아 들었으리라는 것을 확실히 알 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n그러나 실상 그는 상당한 불안을 느꼈다. 그래서 아침에 일찍 일어나 손질할 여지가 없는지 보기로 했다. 그는 그 섬들을 특별히 언급한 대목들을 잘라 냈고, 그들이 다 함께 그토록 신나게 웃어 댔던 그 이름들은 아예 빼 버렸으며, 의심할 바 없이 매우 유익한 일반론을 줄줄이 끼워 넣었다. 이렇게 하면 자기 설교가 해럴드 스미스의 강연과 조금도 닮지 않게 되리라고 그는 스스로 만족스레 여겼다. 설교문을 쓸 때는 아마도 얼마간 반향을 일으키기를 바랐을 것이다. 그러나 이제는 아무 말 없이 지나가 준다면 그것만으로도 아주 만족할 터였다.\n\n그러나 그 일요일에 그가 겪을 곤란은 여러 가지가 되도록 정해져 있었다. 여관에 묵은 일행은 여덟 시에 아침을 먹고 여덟 시 반에 어김없이 출발하여, 콜디코츠에 넉넉히 도착해 교회에 가기 전에 옷을 갖추어 입을 수 있게 하기로 이야기가 되어 있었다. 교회는 저 길고 격식 있는 보리수 가로수길 가까이, 그러나 앞쪽 대문 안쪽의 영지 안에 서 있었다. 그러니 사워비 씨의 집에 닿은 뒤의 걸음은 길지 않을 것이었다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 자신이 아침에 일찍 일어나는 사람이었으므로, 자기 손님이 — 게다가 성직자가 — 일요일 아침에 아침 식사를 하러 여관으로 나가는 것은 들으려 하지 않았다. 콜디코츠로 가는 그 안식일 여행에 관해서는, 의심할 바 없이 마음이 몹시 불편하기는 했으나 승낙을 해 주었다. 그러나 신성을 더럽히는 일은 되도록 적게 하자는 것이었다. 그러므로 그가 벗들과 함께 돌아가는 것은 양해된 일이었으되, 가족 기도와 가족 아침 식사의 은덕을 받지 않고서 가서는 안 되었다. 그리하여 프라우디 부인은 잠자리에 들면서 필요한 지시를 내렸고, 그것이 집안 사람들에게는 크게 짜증스러운 일이었다.\n\n적어도 그녀의 하인들에게는 크게 짜증스러운 일이었다! 주교 자신은 훨씬 늦은 시각까지 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그는 이제 모든 일에서 아내의 통치를 뒷받침했다. 이제는 모든 일에서 그렇다고 나는 말한다. 주교직의 첫 흥분과 자랑 속에서 다른 생각들이 그의 마음을 채웠던 한때가 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 그는 섭리가 자기에게 축복으로 내려 준 그 착한 여인에게 아무런 반대도 하지 않았다. 그리고 그런 처신의 보답으로 그 착한 여인은 모든 일에서 그의 자그마한 개인적 안락을 돌보아 주었다. 한때 품속의 아내를 상대로 벌이려는 유혹에 빠졌던 그 불경한 전쟁을, 주교는 이제 얼마나 놀란 마음으로 돌아보았던가?\n\n프라우디 자매 가운데 누구도 그 이른 시각에 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그들은 아마 다른 이유로 자리에 없었을 것이다. 프라우디 부인은 그들에게는 주교만큼 성공을 거두지 못했다. 그들에게는 저마다 제 뜻이 있었고, 그 뜻은 날마다 더욱더 강해졌다. 프라우디 부인이 축복으로 받은 세 딸 가운데 하나는 이미 교구의 아주 훌륭한 젊은 성직자 옵티머스 그레이 목사를 상대로 그 뜻을 정당한 방식으로 행사할 자리에 있었다. 그러나 나머지 둘은 아직 자기들의 명령권을 펼칠 그런 통로가 없었으므로, 아마도 집에서 연습을 계속하려는 쪽으로 조금 지나치게 기울어 있었다.\n\n그러나 일곱 시 반에는 프라우디 부인이 어김없이 그 자리에 있었고, 전속 사제도 그러했으며, 로바츠 씨도 그러했고, 집안 하인들도 다 그러했다 — 게으른 배교자 하나만 빼고. “토머스는 어디 있느냐?” 아르고스의 눈을 가진 그 여인이 가족 기도서를 손에 들고 일어서서 말했다. “황송하오나 마님, 터머스는 이가 아파 앓고 있습니다요.” “이가 아프다니!” 프라우디 부인이 소리쳤다. 그러나 그녀의 눈은 그보다 훨씬 무서운 것들을 말하고 있었다. “교회에 가기 전에 토머스를 내게 보내라.” 그러고서 그들은 기도로 넘어갔다. 기도는 전속 사제가 읽었으니, 그것이 마땅하고 온당한 일이었다. 그러나 기도가 끝났을 때 프라우디 부인이 축도를 스스로 맡아 읊은 것은 자기 직분을 조금 넘어선 일이었다고 나는 생각하지 않을 수 없다. 하기야 그녀는 맑고 낭랑한 목소리로 그것을 해냈으며, 어쩌면 전속 사제가 미칠 수 없는 위엄을 몸소 드러내었다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 아침 식사 자리에서 다소 엄했고, 프램리의 교구 사제는 이 집에서 나가고 싶다는 까닭 모를 마음에 사로잡혔다. 우선 그녀는 자기 높은 지위의 격식에 늘 기울이던 그 꼼꼼한 주의로 차려입고 있지 않았다. 대성당 성가대석 가운데를 미끄러져 올라가기 전에 다시 한 번 몸단속이 있을 것이 분명했다. 그녀는 턱 아래로 매는 데 필요한 끈 말고는 다른 끈이 없는 크고 헐렁한 모자를 쓰고 있었는데, 집안 사람들과 전속 사제에게는 익숙한 모자였으나 지난 한 주간의 잘 차려입은 놀이 뒤인 로바츠 씨의 눈에는 볼품없어 보였다. 또 그녀는 목까지 잘 올라오는 크고 헐렁한 짙은 색 겉옷을 입고 있었는데, 그것은 그녀의 옷들이 대개 그러하듯 속치마의 장치로 부풀려져 있지 않았다. 그것은 몸에 착 달라붙어 그녀의 전체적인 모습에 딱딱함을 한층 더했다. 그리고 그녀는 두 발을 커다란 카펫 슬리퍼에 넣고 있었으니, 그것은 의심할 바 없이 편안한 물건이겠으나 손님에게는 이상하고 보기 흉하게 느껴졌다.\n\n“이른 아침 기도에 사람들을 모으기가 어렵소?” 그녀가 찻주전자를 들고 일을 시작하며 말했다.\n\n“그렇다고는 못 하겠습니다.” 마크가 말했다. “하기야 저희는 이렇게 이른 적이 좀처럼 없지요.”\n\n“교구 성직자는 일찍 일어나야 한다고 나는 생각하오.” 그녀가 말했다. “그것이 마을에 좋은 본이 되지요.”\n\n“교회에서 아침 기도를 드릴까 생각 중입니다.” 로바츠 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그건 부질없는 일이오.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그리고 대개는 부질없는 것보다 더 나쁜 일을 뜻하지요. 그게 어떻게 되는지 나는 알고 있소. 일요일에 예배를 세 번 드리고 집에서 가족 기도를 드린다면 아주 잘하는 것이오.” 그러고서 그녀는 그에게 찻잔을 건넸다.\n\n“그런데 저는 일요일에 예배를 세 번 드리지 않습니다, 프라우디 부인.”\n\n“그렇다면 그렇게 해야 한다고 생각하오. 가난한 사람들이 일요일에 교회만큼 편히 있을 곳이 어디 있겠소? 주교께서는 다음 훈시에서 이 문제에 대해 매우 강한 의견을 밝히실 참이오. 그러면 로바츠 씨도 그분의 뜻을 분명 따르겠지요.”\n\n이 말에 마크는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 자기 달걀에 전념했다.\n\n“프램리에서는 살림을 그리 크게 하지는 않겠지요?” 프라우디 부인이 물었다.\n\n“목사관 말씀입니까?”\n\n“그렇소. 목사관에 살지 않소?”\n\n“물론입니다 — 글쎄요, 그리 크지는 않습니다, 프라우디 부인. 일을 해내고, 살림을 편하게 하고, 아이들을 돌볼 만큼만 두고 있지요.”\n\n“아주 좋은 성직록이오.” 그녀가 말했다. “아주 좋지요. 우리 쪽에도 그만큼 좋은 것이 있는지는 기억이 나지 않소 — 부주교의 자리인 플럼스테드만 빼고는. 그 사람은 제 빵에 버터를 꽤 잘 발라 놓았지요.”\n\n“그분의 아버님이 바체스터 주교였습니다.”\n\n“아, 그렇지요, 그 사람 일은 다 알고 있소. 그것이 아니었다면 부주교 자리까지도 겨우 올라갔을까 싶소. 어디 보자, 로바츠 씨 것은 800파운드지요, 그렇지 않소? 그런데 그렇게 젊은 나이에! 생명 보험은 넉넉히 들어 두었겠지요.”\n\n“그럭저럭 들었습니다, 프라우디 부인.”\n\n“그리고 부인도 얼마간 재산이 있었지요, 그렇지 않소? 우리가 다 그렇게 두 발로 떨어질 수는 없지요. 그렇지 않소, 화이트 씨?” 그러고서 프라우디 부인은 장난스러운 투로 전속 사제에게 말을 붙였다.\n\n프라우디 부인은 위세를 부리는 여인이었다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴 또한 그러했다. 그러니 로바츠 씨는 여인의 지배에 익숙해져 있어야 했다고 말할 만도 하다. 그렇지만 그는 거기 앉아 토스트를 씹으면서 두 사람을 견주어 보지 않을 수 없었다. 레이디 러프턴은 그 자그마한 시도들로 이따금 그를 노엽게 했다. 그러나 세속의 부인과 교회의 부인을 나란히 놓고 견주자면, 앞쪽의 통치가 더 가볍고 더 즐겁다고 그는 분명히 생각했다. 하기야 레이디 러프턴은 그에게 성직록과 아내를 주었고, 프라우디 부인은 그에게 아무것도 주지 않았다.\n\n아침 식사가 끝나자마자 로바츠 씨는 원틀리의 용으로 빠져나갔다. 한편으로는 아침나절의 프라우디 부인을 충분히 겪었기 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 거기 있는 벗들을 재촉하려는 뜻도 있었다. 그는 전날 저녁 해럴드 스미스가 그랬듯 벌써 시간에 조바심을 내고 있었고, 스미스 부인이 시간을 잘 지킬 것이라고는 별로 믿지 않았다. 여관에 닿아 그들이 아침을 먹었느냐고 물으니, 아직 아무도 내려오지 않았다는 대답이 곧 돌아왔다. 벌써 여덟 시 반이었으니, 그들은 이제 길에 나서 있어야 했다.\n\n그는 곧 사워비 씨의 방으로 갔고, 그 신사가 수염을 깎고 있는 것을 보았다. “조금도 걱정하지 마세요.” 사워비 씨가 말했다. “당신과 스미스가 내 페이턴을 타면 되고, 그 말들이 한 시간 안에 거기까지 데려다 줄 겁니다. 하기야 우리 모두 시간에 맞추게 될 테지만요. 온 일행에게 사람을 돌려서 하나씩 다 몰아내지요.” 그러고서 사워비 씨는 종을 여러 차례 울려 온갖 도움을 불러내어, 남녀 심부름꾼들을 여러 방으로 날려 보냈다.\n\n“저는 이륜마차를 하나 빌려서 지금 곧 건너갈까 합니다.” 마크가 말했다. “제가 늦으면 안 되는 것은 아시지요.”\n\n“우리 가운데 누구도 늦으면 안 되지요. 그리고 이륜마차를 빌린다는 건 다 부질없는 소리예요. 소버린 한 닢을 그냥 버리는 셈이고, 우리가 길에서 당신을 지나쳐 갈 텐데요. 내려가서 차가 다 되었는지 보고, 그런 것들도 챙기고, 계산서를 준비해 두게 하세요. 그리고 로바츠, 원한다면 그 계산도 당신이 치러도 좋아요. 하지만 그건 보르네오 남작에게 맡겨 두어도 되겠지요, 안 그래요?”\n\n그리하여 마크는 정말로 내려가 차를 준비시켰고, 계산서도 시켜 두었다. 그러고서 그는 방 안을 서성이며 시계를 보고, 벗들의 발소리를 초조하게 기다렸다. 그렇게 하고 있는 동안 그는 문득 생각했다. 일요일 아침에 자기가 이런 일을 하고 있는 것이 마땅한가? 설교에 너무 늦지 않으려고 열두 마일을 달려가려고 여기서 괴로운 불안에 싸여 기다리고 있는 것이 좋은 일인가? 패니가 마주 앉아 있고 아이들이 바닥을 기어 다니는 집의 아늑한 자기 방, 자기 조용한 예배를 위한 자기 준비, 그리고 그 예배가 끝났을 때 레이디 러프턴이 따뜻하게 잡아 주는 손, 그것이 이 모든 것보다 낫지 않았던가?\n\n자기는 해럴드 스미스와 사워비 씨와 옴니엄 공작을 모르고 지낼 수는 없다고 그는 스스로에게 말해 왔다. 다른 사람들이 하듯 세상에서 올라갈 길을 찾아야 했다. 그러나 이 친교에서 그에게 지금까지 어떤 즐거움이 왔던가? 올라가는 일을 위해 그가 이제까지 얼마나 해냈던가? 사실을 말하자면, 그 일요일 아침에 해럴드 스미스 부인의 차를 끓이고 사워비 씨의 양갈비를 주문하면서 그는 자기 자신이 그리 마땅치 않았다.\n\n아홉 시가 조금 지나 그들은 모두 모였다. 그러나 그때에도 그는 서둘러야 할 까닭이 있다는 것을 부인들에게 납득시킬 수 없었다. 적어도 일행의 우두머리인 스미스 부인은 그것을 납득하려 하지 않았다. 마크가 다시 이륜마차를 빌리는 이야기를 하자 던스터블 양이 자기도 함께 타겠다고 말했고, 그 일에 어찌나 진심인 듯 보였던지 사워비 씨는 그런 참사를 막으려고 두 번째 달걀을 서둘러 먹어 치웠다. 그러고서 마크는 정말로 이륜마차를 시켰다. 그러자 스미스 부인은 그렇다면 자기는 서두를 필요가 없겠다고 말했다. 그러나 급사가 올라와 전한 말은, 여관의 말이 한 쌍만 빼고 다 나가 있으며 그 한 쌍은 둘 다 외마차에 맬 수 없다는 것이었다. 사실 여관 마구간의 절반은 이미 사워비 씨 일행이 잡아 두고 있었다.\n\n“그럼 그 한 쌍을 내가 쓰겠네.” 지체 때문에 거의 미칠 지경이 된 마크가 말했다.\n\n“부질없는 소리예요, 로바츠. 우리 이제 준비되었어요. 이분은 그것들이 필요 없겠네, 제임스. 자, 서플하우스, 다 됐나?”\n\n“그러면 이제 내가 서둘러야 하는 건가요?” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. “남자들이란 어찌나 변덕스러운 것들인지! 차를 반 잔만 더 주셔도 될까요, 로바츠 씨?”\n\n이제 정말로 화가 난 마크는 창가로 돌아섰다. 이 사람들에게는 자애라는 것이 없다고 그는 스스로에게 말했다. 그들은 그의 곤란이 어떤 것인지 알면서도 그를 비웃기만 했다. 아마도 그는 전날 저녁에 자기가 해럴드 스미스를 놀리는 농담에 한몫 거들었다는 것은 떠올리지 않았다.\n\n“제임스,” 그가 급사를 돌아보며 말했다. “미안하지만 그 말 한 쌍을 지금 곧 내주게.”\n\n“예, 나리. 십오 분 안에 돌려 대겠습니다, 나리. 다만 마부 아이 네드가 말이지요, 나리, 아침을 먹고 있는 게 아닌가 싶습니다, 나리. 하지만 눈 깜짝할 사이도 안 되어 데려오겠습니다요, 나리!”\n\n그러나 네드와 그 한 쌍이 오기 전에 스미스 부인이 어김없이 모자를 다 쓰고 나섰고, 열 시에 그들은 출발했다. 마크는 해럴드 스미스와 페이턴을 함께 탔으나, 페이턴은 다른 마차들보다 조금도 빠르지 않았다. 과연 앞장은 섰으되 그뿐이었다. 그리고 교구 사제의 시계가 열한 시라고 알려 주었을 때 그들은 아직 콜디코츠 대문에서 한 마일 떨어져 있었으니, 말들은 땀에 흠뻑 젖어 김을 내뿜고 있었다. 그리고 교회 종소리가 그치는 것이 들릴 때에야 그들은 겨우 마을에 들어섰다.\n\n“보시오, 결국 시간에 맞췄구려.” 해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “어젯밤의 내 시간보다는 나은 편이오.” 성직자가, 그것도 예배를 도우러 가는 성직자가 교회에 들어서는 일은 마지막 순간에 이루어져서는 안 되며, 차분하고 점잖아야 하고, 뛰는 걸음과 가쁜 숨으로 허둥지둥 해서는 안 된다는 것을 로바츠는 그에게 설명해 줄 수 없었다.\n\n“여기서 세우면 되겠지요, 나리.” 마부 아이가 예배를 위해 모여 선 사람들 가운데서 교회 문 앞에 말을 딱 세우며 말했다. 그러나 마크는 자기가 이토록 늦을 줄은 미리 헤아리지 못했던 터라, 처음에는 집까지 가야 한다고 말했다. 그러다 말들이 다시 움직이기 시작했을 때, 사람을 보내 법의를 가져오게 할 수 있다는 것을 떠올렸고, 마차에서 내리면서 그대로 지시를 내렸다. 그리고 이제 다른 두 마차도 와 있었으므로 문간에는 소란과 혼잡이 일었으니 — 마크가 느끼기에 매우 볼품없는 일이었다. 신사들은 큰 목소리로 이야기했고, 해럴드 스미스 부인은 자기에게 기도서가 없으며 지금은 너무 지쳐서 들어갈 수 없다고 밝혔다. 집으로 가서 쉬겠다고 그녀는 말했다. 일행 가운데 다른 두 부인도 그렇게 하여, 던스터블 양만 혼자 들어가게 되었다. 하기야 그녀는 그런 일 따위는 조금도 개의치 않았다. 그리고 일행 가운데 욕하는 못된 버릇이 있는 한 사람이 마크의 팔꿈치에 바싹 붙어 걸어 들어오면서 무엇엔가 욕설을 퍼부었다. 그리하여 그들은 사죄 기도가 낭독되는 중에 교회 안을 걸어 올라갔고, 마크 로바츠는 자기 자신이 몹시 부끄러웠다. 세상에서 올라가는 일이 그를 이런 것들과 맞닿게 한다면, 올라가지 않고 지내는 편이 그에게 더 낫지 않겠는가?\n\n그의 설교는 별다른 주목 없이 지나갔다. 해럴드 스미스 부인은 그 자리에 없었으니 그로서는 크게 만족스러운 일이었고, 자리에 있던 다른 이들도 설교에 별다른 주의를 기울이는 것 같지 않았다. 그 주제는 새로움을 잃어버렸다. 다만 여느 교회 신자들, 곧 교구의 농부들과 일꾼들에게는 그렇지 않았다. 그리고 지주의 큰 신도석에 앉은 “높은 분들”은 어지간한 헌금으로 공감을 보이는 데 만족했다. 그러나 던스터블 양이 10파운드 지폐를 내놓아, 총액이 콜디코츠 같은 곳으로서는 남 앞에 내놓을 만한 액수까지 불어났다.\n\n“그래, 이제는 뉴기니라는 말을 다시 듣지 않게 되기를 바라네.” 예배가 끝난 뒤 모두 응접실 난롯가에 둘러 모였을 때 사워비 씨가 말했다. “그 주제는 죽어서 묻힌 것으로 여겨도 되겠지. 그렇지 않은가, 해럴드?”\n\n“어젯밤에 확실히 살해되었지요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. “저 무서운 여자, 프라우디 부인의 손에.”\n\n“부인이 그 여자에게 달려들어 안락의자에서 끌어내지 않은 게 이상하네요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “저는 그러기를 기다리고 있었고, 그 난투 속에서 제가 봉변을 당하겠구나 생각했지요.”\n\n“숙녀가 그처럼 낯가죽 두꺼운 짓을 하는 건 여태 본 적이 없어요.” 던스터블 양의 여행 동무인 케리지 양이 말했다.\n\n“나도 그렇습니다 — 여태 한 번도. 게다가 사람들이 모인 자리에서 말이지요.” 역시 그녀와 자주 동행하는 의사 이지먼 선생이 말했다.\n\n“낯가죽으로 말하자면,” 서플하우스 씨가 말했다. “그 부인은 그것이 모자라서 못 하는 일이란 없을 거요. 넉넉히 가지고 있으니 다행이지요. 불쌍한 주교는 그 점에서 아주 빈약하게 타고났으니 말이오.”\n\n“그 부인이 무슨 말을 했는지 나는 거의 듣지도 못했소.” 해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “그러니 대답을 할 수도 없었지요, 아시겠소. 일요일에 관한 무슨 이야기였던 것 같소.”\n\n“남태평양 섬사람들에게 안식일 여행을 가르치지 말아 달라고 바라더군.” 사워비 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 주일 학교를 꼭 세워 달라고 특별히 청했지요.” 스미스 부인이 말했다. 그러고서 그들은 모두 일에 착수하여 프라우디 부인을 모자의 맨 위 리본에서 슬리퍼 밑바닥까지 낱낱이 헐어 놓았다.\n\n“그러고 나서는 가난한 목사들이 자기 딸들과 사랑에 빠져 주기를 바라지요. 그게 무엇보다 딱한 일이에요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n그러나 대체로 보아, 우리 교구 사제가 잠자리에 들 때 그는 자기가 유익한 일요일을 보냈다고 느끼지 못했다.\n\n# 제8장. 개서럼 성\n\n화요일 아침에 마크는 과연 아내의 편지와 10파운드짜리 지폐를 받았고, 이로써 바셋주 우체국 사람들의 정직함이 강력하게 증명된 셈이었다. 우편배달 소년 로빈이 맥주를 딱 한 잔 들이켜는 동안 서둘러 쓴 그 편지는—뭐, 두 번째 잔을 반쯤 더 채웠다 한들 그게 무슨 상관이랴?—그럼에도 아내의 사랑과 그녀의 큰 승리를 웅변하고 있었다.\n\n돈만 보낼 시간밖에 없어요[그녀는 이렇게 적었다]. 우편배달부가 여기서 기다리고 있으니까요. 만나면 왜 이렇게 서둘렀는지 설명해 드릴게요. 무사히 받으셨다고 알려 주세요. 이제 다 잘됐어요. 레이디 러프턴이 방금 전까지 여기 계셨어요. 그분은 영 마음에 들지 않으셨어요. 개서럼 성 이야기 말이에요. 하지만 당신은 그 일로 아무 말도 듣지 않으실 거예요. 다만 다음 주 수요일에는 반드시 프램리 코트에서 저녁을 드셔야 한다는 것만 기억하세요. 제가 당신 대신 약속했어요. 그렇게 해 주실 거죠, 여보? 말씀하신 날보다 더 머무르려 하시면 제가 가서 끌고 올 거예요. 하지만 그러지 않으실 거라고 믿어요. 하느님의 축복이 있기를, 내 하나뿐인 사람! 존스 씨는 부활절 후 둘째 주일에 하셨던 설교를 또 하셨어요. 한 해에 두 번은 너무 잦지요. 하느님의 축복이 있기를! 아이들은 아주 잘 있어요. 마크가 큰 입맞춤을 보냅니다.—당신의 F.\n\n로바츠는 이 편지를 읽고 지폐를 구겨 주머니에 넣으면서, 자기가 받아 마땅한 것보다 훨씬 만족스러운 결과라고 느꼈다. 한바탕 싸움이 있었을 것이고, 자기를 위해 충실히 싸운 아내가 그 싸움에서 이겼다는 것을 그는 알았다. 그리고 그녀의 승리가 그 명분이 훌륭했던 덕은 아니라는 것도 알았다. 그는 레이디 러프턴을 두려워하지 않겠다고 스스로에게 자주 선언했지만, 그럼에도 자기에게 아무 책망도 돌아오지 않는다는 이 소식은 그에게 큰 안도를 주었다.\n\n다음 금요일에 그들은 모두 공작의 집으로 갔고, 주교와 프라우디 부인이 이미 와 있는 것을 보았다. 그 밖에도 여러 사람들이 있었는데, 대개 세상 일반의 평가로든 서부 바셋주의 평가로든 어느 정도 이름 있는 이들이었다. 보아네르게스 경이 와 있었다. 무슨 일에서든 제 뜻을 관철하는 노인으로, 모든 사람에게—보아하니 공작 자신에게까지도—지성의 왕으로 여겨지는 사람이었다. 그것도 결코 입헌군주 같은 왕이 아니라, 어떤 대신의 도움도 없이 정신에 관한 모든 문제를 홀로 다스리기를 자임하는 지성의 황제로 여겨졌다. 그리고 브롤 남작도 일행에 있었다. 여왕 폐하의 배석 판사 가운데 한 사람으로, 시골 저택에 들어선 손님 중에 그만큼 유쾌한 이가 없었다. 다만 그 유쾌함 속에 다소 날카로운 데가 있는 것이 흠이었다. 또 그린 워커 씨가 있었다. 젊지만 떠오르는 인물로, 얼마 전 크루 정션의 자기 선거구민들에게 대중적인 주제로 강연을 한 바로 그 사람이었다. 그린 워커 씨는 하틀탑 후작부인의 조카였고, 하틀탑 후작부인은 옴니엄 공작의 벗이었다. 마크 로바츠 씨는 자기가 그토록 간곡히 한몫 끼라고 권유받은 그 일행이 누구로 이루어졌는지 알게 되자 분명 우쭐해졌다. 레이디 러프턴의 편견 때문에 이것을 포기하는 것이 현명한 일이었겠는가?\n\n손님이 그렇게 많고 그렇게 대단했으므로 개서럼 성의 거대한 정면 문짝이 활짝 열렸고, 이탈리아에서 온 대리석 흉상과 워도어 스트리트에서 온 갑주 같은 전리품으로 장식된 광대한 홀은 신사 숙녀로 붐볐으며, 수많은 발소리에 낯선 메아리를 돌려주었다. 마크가 사워비와 던스터블 양과 함께 그곳에 도착했을 때—이번 경우에는 던스터블 양이 페이턴에 앉아 오고 마크가 뒷자리를 차지했다—각하 자신은 바로 그 순간 응접실에 있었고, 그의 정중함은 더할 나위가 없었다.\n\n“오, 던스터블 양,” 그가 그 부인의 손을 잡고 난롯가로 이끌면서 말했다. “개서럼 성이 헛되이 지어진 것이 아니라는 것을 이제야 처음으로 느끼오.”\n\n“헛되이 지어졌다고 생각한 사람은 아무도 없었을 텐데요, 각하,” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “건축가가 청구서를 받았을 때 분명 그렇게는 생각하지 않았을 거예요.” 그러고는 던스터블 양은 발을 난로 울에 올려 데웠는데, 그 태연자약함이 마치 자기 아버지도 돌팔이 의사가 아니라 공작이었던 것 같았다.\n\n“앵무새에 대해서는 아주 엄중하게 일러두었소,” 공작이 말했다—\n\n“아! 하지만 결국 데려오지 못했어요,” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n—“게다가 새장도 일부러 지어 두었소. 앵무새들이 자기 나라에서 익숙하게 지내는 바로 그런 것으로 말이오. 그런데, 던스터블 양, 그건 정말 매정하다고 하겠소. 지금 사람을 보내 데려오게 하기에는 너무 늦었소?”\n\n“그 애는 이지먼 선생과 함께 여행하고 있어요. 실은 선생한테서 그 벗을 빼앗을 수가 없었지요.”\n\n“어째서? 그 사람을 위해서도 새장을 하나 더 지어 두었는데. 던스터블 양, 단언하지만 당신이 내게 베푸는 영광이 반쯤은 빛을 잃었소. 하지만 푸들은—푸들만은 아직 믿고 있소.”\n\n“그 점에서는 각하의 믿음이 헛되지 않을 거예요. 그런데 그 애가 어디 있을까요?” 그러면서 던스터블 양은 누군가가 자기 뒤를 따라 개를 안고 들어왔으리라 기대하는 듯 주위를 둘러보았다. “정말이지 가서 찾아봐야겠어요. 생각만 해도 그래요, 사람들이 그 애를 각하의 개들 사이에 넣어 놓았다면—그 애 품행이 어떻게 망가질까요!”\n\n“던스터블 양, 그건 나를 두고 하는 말이오?” 그러나 그 부인은 이미 난롯가에서 몸을 돌렸고, 공작은 다른 손님들을 맞이할 수 있었다.\n\n이 일을 그는 매우 정중하게 해냈다. “사워비,” 그가 말했다. “강연에서 살아 돌아온 것을 보니 반갑소. 정말이지 나는 자네가 걱정스러웠다오.”\n\n“원틀리의 용에서 한참 지체된 뒤에 강장제를 투여받고서야 겨우 되살아났습니다. 각하께 이 사람 로바츠 씨를 소개해 드려도 될까요. 그날 이 사람은 저만큼 운이 좋지 못했습니다. 결국 주교관으로 실려 가서 아주 격렬한 치료를 받아야 했지요.”\n\n그러고서 공작은 로바츠 씨와 악수를 하며, 알게 되어 무척 기쁘다고 말했다. 그가 이 주에 들어온 뒤로 그에 대한 이야기를 여러 번 들었노라고 했다. 그리고 러프턴 경의 안부를 물으면서, 그를 개서럼 성으로 오게 하지 못한 것이 아쉽다고 했다.\n\n“그런데 강연에서 여흥이 하나 있었다고 들었소,” 공작이 말을 이었다. “두 번째 연사가 있어서 딱한 해럴드 스미스를 거의 무색하게 만들었다지요?” 그러자 사워비 씨가 그 작은 프라우디 삽화를 재미있게 그려 보였다.\n\n“그렇다면 물론 자네 매부는 강연자로서는 영영 망한 것이오,” 공작이 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 저희는 프라우디 부인께 이루 말할 수 없이 깊은 은혜를 입은 셈이지요,” 사워비 씨가 말했다. 그러고서 해럴드 스미스 자신이 다가왔고, 바체스터에서 벌인 자기 사업의 성공에 대해 공작의 진심 어리고 따뜻한 축하를 받았다.\n\n마크 로바츠는 이제 몸을 돌렸는데, 그의 주의가 갑자기 던스터블 양의 큰 목소리에 붙들렸다. 그녀는 방들을 지나가다가 아주 각별한 벗들과 우연히 마주친 참이었고, 그 기쁨을 조금도 세상에 감추지 않았다.\n\n“어머—어머—어머!” 그녀가 소리쳤다. 그러고는 한 신사와 함께 자기 쪽으로 걸어오던, 아주 조용해 보이고 잘 차려입은 매력적인 젊은 부인을 붙잡았다. 알고 보니 그 신사와 부인은 부부였다. “어머—어머—어머! 이건 바라지도 못했는데.” 그러고 그녀는 그 부인을 붙들고 열렬히 입을 맞추었고, 이어 신사의 두 손을 잡아 힘차게 흔들었다.\n\n“그리고 내가 너한테 할 얘기가 얼마나 많은지!” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “네가 내 다른 계획들을 다 뒤엎겠구나. 그런데 메리, 얘, 여기 얼마나 있을 거니? 나는—어디 보자—언제 떠나는지 잊어버렸지만, 다 위층 수첩에 적어 두었어. 아무튼 다음 행선지는 프라우디 부인 댁이야. 거기서는 너를 못 만나겠지. 그래, 프랭크, 아버님은 어떠셔?”\n\n프랭크라고 불린 신사는 아버지가 아주 잘 계시다고 말했다.—“물론 사냥개들에 미쳐 계시지요, 아시겠지만.”\n\n“그래, 얘, 그게 동상으로 만들어져 버린 그 딱한 신사처럼 사냥개들이 그분에게 미쳐 있는 것보다는 낫지. 하지만 사냥개 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 프랭크, 콜디코츠에서는 여우를 얼마나 형편없이 다루는지 몰라! 나도 하루는 사냥에 나가 봤는데—”\n\n“사냥에 나가셨다고요!” 메리라고 불린 부인이 말했다.\n\n“내가 사냥에 나가면 안 될 이유가 어디 있니? 얘기해 주지, 프라우디 부인도 사냥에 나왔더라. 그런데 여우는 한 마리도 못 잡았어. 그리고 사실을 말하자면 나한테는 좀 시시해 보였다.”\n\n“그 주에서 잘못된 쪽으로 가셨네요,” 프랭크라고 불린 신사가 말했다.\n\n“당연히 그랬지. 사냥을 정말로 해 보고 싶어지면 그레셤스베리로 갈 거야. 그건 의심할 나위가 없지.”\n\n“아니면 박솔 힐로요,” 부인이 말했다. “거기서도 그레셤스베리만큼 열성을 보실 거예요.”\n\n“게다가 분별도 더 있다고 덧붙여야지,” 신사가 말했다.\n\n“하! 하! 하!” 던스터블 양이 웃었다. “너희 분별력이라니, 참! 그런데 레이디 애러벨라 얘기는 한마디도 안 해 주는구나.”\n\n“어머니는 아주 잘 계세요,” 신사가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 선생님은? 그러고 보니, 얘, 내가 선생님한테서 그런 편지를 받았단다. 겨우 이틀 전에. 내일 위층에서 보여 주지. 하지만 명심해, 절대 비밀이어야 해. 그 사람이 이런 식으로 계속하다간 런던 탑이나 코번트리나 청서(靑書)나 무슨 끔찍한 데로 들어가고 말 거야.”\n\n“어머, 무슨 말씀을 하셨는데요?”\n\n“넌 알 것 없다, 프랭크 도련님. 너한테 그 편지를 보여 줄 마음은 없으니 그건 확실히 알아 두렴. 하지만 네 아내가 부지깽이와 부집게에 세 번 맹세해서 발설하지 않겠다고 하면 그 사람에게는 보여 주지. 그래, 그럼 박솔 힐에 완전히 자리를 잡았니?”\n\n“프랭크의 말들은 자리를 잡았고 개들도 거의 잡았어요,” 프랭크의 아내가 말했다. “하지만 그 밖의 것은 아직 뭐라고 자랑할 게 없네요.”\n\n“그래, 좋은 시절이 오고 있지. 나는 이제 가서 옷을 갈아입어야겠다. 그런데 메리, 오늘 저녁에는 꼭 내 옆에 붙어 있어. 너한테 할 얘기가 아주 많으니까.” 그러고서 던스터블 양은 성큼성큼 방을 나섰다.\n\n이 모든 말이 어찌나 큰 목소리로 이루어졌던지, 당연한 일로 마크 로바츠에게도 다 들렸다—물론 대화 가운데 던스터블 양의 입에서 나온 부분을 말하는 것이다. 그리고 마크는 이 사람이 박솔 힐의 젊은 프랭크 그레셤이며, 그레셤스베리의 늙은 그레셤 씨의 아들이라는 것을 알게 되었다. 프랭크는 얼마 전 큰 상속녀와 결혼했는데, 사람들 말로는 던스터블 양보다도 더 큰 상속녀였다. 그리고 결혼한 지 겨우 여섯 달이 될까 말까 했으므로 바셋주 세상은 아직도 그 이야기로 가득했다.\n\n“두 상속녀가 아주 다정해 보이지 않소?” 서플하우스 씨가 말했다. “깃이 같은 새는 함께 모인다지요. 하지만 얼마 전만 해도 저 젊은 그레셤이 던스터블 양과 결혼하기로 되어 있었다는 말이 있었소.”\n\n“던스터블 양이라니요! 그분은 저 사람의 어머니라 해도 될 나이인데요,” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“그런 건 별 차이가 없소. 그는 돈과 결혼해야 할 처지였고, 한때 던스터블 양에게 청혼한 것은 의심할 여지가 없다고 믿소.”\n\n“러프턴한테서 편지를 받았어요,” 이튿날 아침 사워비 씨가 그에게 말했다. “지체된 것이 다 당신 탓이라고 하더군요. 그가 무슨 일을 하기 전에 당신이 레이디 러프턴에게 말해 두어야 했는데, 그는 당신에게서 소식이 올 때까지 편지 쓰는 것을 기다리고 있었다는 거예요. 당신이 그 일에 대해 마님께 한마디도 하지 않은 모양이던데요.”\n\n“분명히 한마디도 하지 않았지요. 러프턴이 내게 맡긴 일은 그분이 그 얘기를 받아들일 만한 기분일 때 꺼내라는 것이었어요. 당신이 나만큼 레이디 러프턴을 잘 안다면, 그분이 그런 소식을 받아들일 기분이 되는 날이 아무 날이나 오는 것이 아니라는 걸 알 텐데요.”\n\n“그래서 당신들 둘이 늙은 부인 하나를 무서워하는 바람에 나는 언제까지고 기다려야 했군요! 하지만 그분께 한마디도 나쁘게 말할 생각은 없고, 그 일은 이제 끝났어요.”\n\n“농장이 팔렸나요?”\n\n“전혀 아니에요. 노부인은 러프턴 땅에 그런 신성모독이 벌어지는 것을 차마 견디지 못하고, 공채에서 오천 파운드를 팔아 그 돈을 선물로 러프턴에게 보냈어요. 한마디 말도 없이 보냈지요. 그저 그것으로 그가 쓸 데에 넉넉하기를 바란다면서요. 나도 어머니가 있었으면 좋겠어요, 정말이지.”\n\n마크는 지금 들은 이야기에 대해 그 순간 아무 말도 할 수 없었지만, 갑작스러운 양심의 통증을 느꼈고, 지금 개서럼 성이 아니라 프램리에 있었으면 좋겠다고 생각했다. 그는 레이디 러프턴의 수입과 그것이 쓰이는 방식을 꽤 잘 알고 있었다. 홀로 사는 부인에게는 아주 넉넉한 액수였지만, 그녀는 거리낌 없이 손이 큰 방식으로 살았다. 그녀의 자선은 훌륭했다. 그녀가 돈을 모아 둘 이유는 없었고, 해마다의 수입은 보통 그 해 안에 다 쓰였다. 마크는 이것을 알고 있었고, 또한 그것을 도저히 계속할 수 없게 되는 처지가 아니라면 그녀가 자선을 줄이는 일은 결코 없으리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 그런데 그녀는 이제 아들의 재산을 지키기 위해 자기 원금의 일부를 내놓은 것이다—자기보다 훨씬 부유한 아들, 게다가 세상이 그 재산에 대해 실제로 요구하는 것이 더 적은 아들을 위해서.\n\n그리고 마크는 이 돈이 어떤 목적에 쓰였는지도 얼마간 알고 있었다. 사워비와 러프턴 경 사이에는 결말이 나지 않은 도박 채권이 있었는데, 경마에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그것은 이제 사 년째 이어져 왔으니, 거의 러프턴 경이 성년이 된 무렵부터였다. 그는 전에도 이 문제로 로바츠에게 몹시 씁쓸한 노여움을 터뜨리며, 사워비 씨가 자기를 부당하게, 아니 부정직하게 대하고 있다고—받을 권리가 없는 돈을 요구하고 있다고 주장한 적이 있었다. 그리고 그때 그는 이 일을 자키 클럽에 부치겠다고 여러 번 선언했다. 그러나 마크는 러프턴 경이 이런 문제에 밝지 못하다는 것을 알았고, 사워비 씨가 실제로 자기 벗을 속이려 할 리는 없다고 믿었으므로, 젊은 경의 노여움을 달래고 사건을 어떤 사사로운 중재자에게 맡기라고 권했다. 이 모든 일이 나중에 로바츠와 사워비 씨 자신 사이에서 논의되었고, 거기서 두 사람의 친분이 시작되었다. 사건은 그렇게 부쳐졌고, 중재자는 사워비 씨가 지명했다. 그리고 판정이 자기에게 불리하게 나왔을 때 러프턴 경은 그것을 담담히 받아들였다. 그때는 이미 노여움이 가셔 있었다. “저 사람들 사이에서 나는 깨끗이 털렸어요,” 그는 마크에게 웃으며 말했다. “하지만 대수롭지 않아요. 사람은 자기 경험에 값을 치러야 하는 법이지요. 물론 사워비는 다 옳다고 생각할 테고, 나도 그렇다고 여길 수밖에요.” 그러고 나서 금액을 두고 또 지체가 있었고, 돈의 일부는 제삼자에게 지불되었고, 어음이 하나 발행되었으며, 러프턴 경이 도대체 모두 얼마를 지불했는지는 하늘과 유대인들만이 알 것이다. 그리고 이제 그것은, 사워비 씨를 위해 어떤 형편없는 고리대금업자 악당에게 오천 파운드라는 엄청난 금액을 넘겨주는 것으로 끝이 났다. 그 돈은 그의 어머니 레이디 러프턴의 재산에서 떼어 낸 것이었다!\n\n마크는 이 모든 것을 생각하면서 사워비 씨에 대해 어떤 반감을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다—그가 나쁜 사람이라고 의심하지 않을 수 없었다. 아니, 그가 매우 나쁘다는 것을 그는 알고 있었던 것이 아닐까? 그런데도 그는 공작의 정원을 그와 함께 계속 거닐며, 여전히 러프턴 경의 일에 대해 이야기하고, 사워비가 자기 일에 대해 들려주는 것에 여전히 관심을 갖고 귀를 기울였다.\n\n“나처럼 강탈당한 사람은 없어요,” 그가 말했다. “하지만 나는 그자들 모두를 제치고 끝내 이겨 낼 겁니다. 그렇지만 그 유대인들 말이오, 마크”—요 며칠 사이 그는 그와 아주 친해져 있었다—“당신은 무슨 일이 있어도 그자들과는 얽히지 마세요. 아니, 나는 그자들 서명으로 방 하나를 도배할 수도 있어요. 그런데 나는 그자들 가운데 누구에게도 청구할 것이 하나도 없었고, 그자들은 늘 내게 청구할 것이 있지요!”\n\n위에서 나는 러프턴 경의 이 일이 끝났다고 말했다. 그러나 이제 마크에게는 그것이 아주 끝난 것이 아닌 듯이 보였다. “러프턴에게 전해 주세요,” 사워비가 말했다. “그의 이름이 적힌 종이는 저 토저라는 악당이 가진 것만 빼고 다 회수되었다고요. 토저가 어음 한 장은 갖고 있을 겁니다. 갱신할 때 넘겨받지 못한 것이지요. 하지만 내 변호사 검션에게 그것도 회수하게 하겠어요. 십 파운드나 이십 파운드쯤 들 테고, 그 이상은 아닐 거예요. 러프턴을 만나면 그것을 기억해 주겠지요?”\n\n“러프턴은 아마 나보다 당신이 먼저 만날 텐데요.”\n\n“아, 말하지 않았던가요? 그는 곧 프램리 코트로 갑니다. 당신이 돌아가면 거기 있을 거예요.”\n\n“프램리에 와 있다니요!”\n\n“그렇다니까요. 어머니가 보낸 이 작은 선물이 그의 효심을 건드린 거지요. 노부인의 딱딱한 금화를 부드러운 애교로 갚으러 프램리로 달려가는 겁니다. 나도 어머니가 있었으면 좋겠어요. 정말 그래요.”\n\n그리고 마크는 여전히 사워비 씨가 무섭다고 느끼면서도, 그와 관계를 끊기로 마음을 정할 수는 없었다.\n\n그리고 그때 성에서는 정치 이야기가 많았다. 공작이 무슨 열의를 갖고 거기 끼어든 것은 아니었다. 그는 휘그였다—거대한 산더미 같은 어마어마한 휘그였다. 그것은 온 세상이 알고 있었다. 어떤 반대파도 그의 휘그주의를 건드릴 꿈을 꾸지 않았을 것이고, 같은 휘그 형제라도 그것을 의심할 꿈을 꾸지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그는 어떤 무리에게도 실제로는 거의 지원을 하지 않고, 다른 어떤 무리에게도 실제로는 거의 반대를 하지 않는 휘그였다. 그는 그런 지상의 일들로 자신을 번거롭게 하는 것 위에 있었다. 선거 때면 그는 휘그 후보를 지원했고 언제나 당선시켰다. 그 대가로 그는 어느 휘그 대신에게서 주 지사로 임명되었고, 또 다른 대신에게서 가터 훈장을 받았다. 그러나 이런 것들은 옴니엄 공작에게는 당연한 일이었다. 그는 주 지사와 가터 훈장 기사가 되도록 태어난 사람이었다.\n\n그러나 그의 무심함, 아니 오히려 조용함 때문에 오히려 더욱, 개서럼 성은 정치가들이 서로 지금의 희망과 앞날의 목표를 털어놓고 반은 진지하고 반은 조롱하는 식으로 함께 작은 음모를 꾸미기에 알맞은 장소로 여겨졌다. 실제로 서플하우스 씨와 해럴드 스미스, 그리고 한둘 다른 이들이 바로 이 목적으로 개서럼에 와 있다는 말이 나돌았다. 포더길 씨도 이름난 정치가였고 공작의 마음을 잘 안다고 알려져 있었다. 그리고 후작부인의 조카인 그린 워커 씨는 공작이 앞으로 내세우고 싶어 하는 젊은이였다. 사워비 씨도 공작이 앉힌 하원 의원이었으니, 몇 가지 생각을 주고받기에 이 기회는 잘 맞았다.\n\n당시의 총리는 많은 사람이 그에게 화를 내고 있었지만 아주 실패한 것은 아니었다. 그는 러시아 전쟁을 끝맺었는데, 영광스럽지는 않았다 해도 적어도 한때 영국인들이 감히 바랐던 것보다는 훨씬 나았다. 그리고 그는 인도의 그 반란에서 놀랄 만한 운을 누렸다. 물론 그와 함께 표를 던진 이들조차 그것이 결코 그의 공이 아니라고 말했다. 인도에서 위인들이 일어나 그 모든 일을 해냈다는 것이다. 그가 파견한 총독, 곧 그곳에 있는 그의 대신조차도 그의 명령 아래 이루어진 성공에 대해 당시에는 아무 공로도 인정받지 못했다. 배의 키를 잡은 사람을 의심할 큰 이유가 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 운이 좋았다. 공적 인물에게 성공만 한 공적은 없다!\n\n그러나 이제 험한 날들이 거의 지나갔을 때, 그가 너무 성공한 것이 아니냐는 문제가 나왔다. 사람이 행운을 자기 전차 바퀴에 못 박아 두면 자못 오만한 모습으로 돌아다니기 쉬운 법이다. 주인이 자기 없이는 아무것도 못 한다고 여기는 하인들이 있다. 그리고 대중에게도 이따금 그런 하인이 있을 수 있다. 만일 이 너무 성공한 대신이 그런 하인 가운데 하나라면?\n\n그리고 신중하고 평범하고 열심인 하원 의원은 자기 선거구민에 대한 의무를 다하며 몇 가지 질문을 할 때 비웃음을 받는 것을 좋아하지 않는다. 모든 일에 성공한 대신이 그 승리를 혼자 삭이지 못하고 굳이 오만한 모습으로 돌아다니면서 평범하고 열심인 의원들을 비웃는다면—때로는 결코 평범하지 않은 의원들까지 비웃는다면, 이건 괘씸한 일이다!—잠시 그를 추방해 두는 것이 좋지 않을까?\n\n“그에게 우리 포탄을 던져 넣는 게 좋지 않겠나?” 해럴드 스미스 씨가 말한다.\n\n“무슨 일이 있어도 우리 포탄을 던져 넣자고,” 유노처럼 자기가 무시당한 매력을 잊지 않고 있는 서플하우스 씨가 말한다. 그리고 서플하우스 씨가 스스로 적이라고 선언하면 사람들은 그것이 얼마나 큰 뜻인지 안다. 그 숱하게 두들겨 맞은 국정의 수장이 이제 준비되어 있는 무서운 타격에 굴복하지 않을 수 없다는 것을 안다. “그렇지, 우리 포탄을 던져 넣는 거야.” 그러면서 서플하우스 씨는 눈을 번득이며 의자에서 일어난다. “그리스에 그만한 아들이 없단 말인가? 아니, 훨씬 더 훌륭한 아들이 있지. 저 사람은 배신자야. 사람은 그 벗을 보고 판단해야 하는 법이지,” 서플하우스 씨가 말한다. 그리고 그는 동쪽을 가리킨다. 우리의 친애하는 동맹인 프랑스인들이 산다고 하는 곳이자, 우리 국정의 수장이 너무 가까운 친교를 맺고 있다고 하는 곳이다.\n\n이것은 모두가, 그린 워커 씨까지도 알아듣는다. “그가 이제 우리 누구에게도 조금이라도 좋은 구실을 한다고는 생각하지 않네,” 크루 정션의 그 재능 있는 의원이 말한다. “내 취향에는 너무 거만해. 그렇게 생각하는 사람을 나는 아주 많이 알고 있지. 우리 삼촌만 해도—”\n\n“그분은 세상에서 가장 좋은 분이지요,” 포더길 씨가 말했다. 그린 워커 씨의 삼촌에 관해 나오려는 그 폭로가 어쩌면 자기들에게 쓸모없을지도 모른다고 느꼈던 것이다. “하지만 사실은 같은 사람만 늘 있으면 싫증이 나는 법이에요. 자고새를 매일 먹고 싶은 사람은 없거든요. 나로서야 이 일에 아무 관계도 없지만, 요리를 바꾸는 것은 확실히 마음에 들 것 같군요.”\n\n“우리가 그저 시키는 대로만 하고 우리 목소리를 낼 수 없다면, 아예 그 가게에 갈 이유가 뭔지 모르겠네,” 사워비 씨가 말했다.\n\n“조금도 쓸모없지,” 서플하우스 씨가 말했다. “그런 지배에 굴복하는 것은 우리 선거구민에 대한 배신이야.”\n\n“그럼 한번 바꿔 보자고,” 사워비 씨가 말했다. “일은 꽤 우리 손안에 있으니.”\n\n“전적으로 그렇지,” 그린 워커 씨가 말했다. “우리 삼촌도 늘 그렇게 말한다네.”\n\n“맨체스터 파는 그 기회를 너무나 기뻐할 걸세,” 해럴드 스미스가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 고답파 신사들이야,” 사워비 씨가 말했다. “우리가 나무를 흔들면 떨어진 열매를 주워 가는 것을 마다할 것 같지 않네.”\n\n“열매를 주워 가는 문제라면, 그건 어떻게 될지 모르지,” 서플하우스 씨가 말했다. 그가 나라를 구할 사람이 아니던가? 그렇다면 왜 그가 스스로 열매를 주워서는 안 되는가? 이 나라의 가장 큰 권력이 그를 바로 그런 구원자로 지목하지 않았던가? 지금 이 순간 나라가 더 구원받을 것이 없다 해도, 그럼에도 좋은 시절이 오고 있는 것은 아닐까? 다른 전쟁들의 소문이 아직 돌고 있지 않은가—지금 진행 중인 저 전쟁이 그의 도움 없이 다른 종류의 구원으로 끝맺어지고 있다면 말이지만. 그는 자기가 가리켰던 그 나라를, 그리고 자기 적들의 그 벗을 생각하고, 위대한 구원자가 할 일이 아직 남아 있을지 모른다고 떠올렸다. 대중의 마음은 이제 깨어 있었고 무슨 일인지 알고 있었다. 사람이 대중의 목소리가 자기를 부른다는 생각을 머릿속에 넣게 되면, 대중의 지혜에 대한 그의 신뢰가 얼마나 커지는지 놀라울 정도다. 「복스 포풀리 복스 데이」. “언제나 그러지 않았던가?” 그는 일어나면서도, 잠자리에 들면서도 스스로에게 그렇게 말한다. 그러고서 서플하우스 씨는 자기가 여기 개서럼 성의 주인 되는 두뇌이며 여기 있는 자들은 모두 자기 손안의 인형이라고 느꼈다. 자기 벗들이 인형이고 그 줄이 자기 손에 있다고 느끼는 것은 참 즐거운 일이다. 그러나 만일 서플하우스 씨 자신이 인형이라면?\n\n몇 달 뒤에 그 숱하게 두들겨 맞은 국정의 수장이 정말로 물러나게 되었을 때, 매정한 포탄이 그에게 무수히 던져졌을 때, 그가 「에트 투, 브루테!」를 입술에 새겨질 만큼 되풀이해 외쳤을 때, 어디서나 모든 사람이 저 위대한 개서럼 성 연합에 대해 많은 이야기를 했다. 세상 말로는, 옴니엄 공작이 나라의 사정을 높이 헤아려 보고 나라의 동포 전체의 복리를 위해 어떤 큰 조치를 시작할 필요가 있음을 그 독수리 같은 눈으로 알아보고서, 곧 자기 저택으로 하원 의원 여러 명과 상원 의원 몇 명을 불러 모았다는 것이었다—이 대목에서 특히 온 세상이 존경하고 온 세상이 지혜롭다 하는 보아네르게스 경이 언급되었다. 그리고 사람들은 이어서 말하기를, 거기서 그가 깊은 밀회 가운데 자기 견해를 그들에게 알렸다고 했다. 그리하여 국정의 수장이 휘그이기는 하나 무너져야 한다는 데 합의가 이루어졌다. 나라가 그것을 요구했고, 공작은 자기 의무를 다한 것이다. 세상 말로는 이것이 저 유명한 연합의 시작이었으며, 그 연합에 의해 내각이 뒤집히고—《구디 투슈즈》가 덧붙인 대로—나라가 구원되었다. 그러나 《주피터》는 그 공을 다 자기 것으로 돌렸다. 그리고 《주피터》가 아주 틀린 것도 아니었다. 그 공은 모두 《주피터》의 것이었다—이 일에서나 다른 모든 일에서나.\n\n그동안 옴니엄 공작은 조용하고 제후다운 방식으로 손님들을 대접했으나, 서플하우스 씨나 해럴드 스미스 씨와 정치 이야기를 많이 나누는 데까지 몸을 낮추지는 않았다. 그리고 보아네르게스 경은, 위에서 그려 놓은 대화가 있던 그날 아침을 던스터블 양에게 비눗방울을 과학적 원리에 따라 부는 법을 가르치며 보냈다.\n\n“어머나, 어머나!” 지식의 불꽃이 마음속으로 날아 들어오는 것을 느끼며 던스터블 양이 말했다. “저는 늘 비눗방울은 그저 비눗방울이라고 생각했고, 그 이유를 물어본 적이 없어요. 사람들은 안 묻잖아요, 경.”\n\n“미안하지만, 던스터블 양,” 늙은 경이 말했다. “묻는 사람은 있소. 다만 구백구십구 명은 묻지 않지요.”\n\n“그리고 그 구백구십구 명이 더 낫게 사는 거예요,” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “인(燐)을 문질러 바르는 것을 본 뒤에 유령이 무슨 재미가 있겠어요?”\n\n“지당한 말이오, 부인. ‘무지가 축복이라면 현명해지는 것은 어리석은 짓이다.’ 모든 것은 그 ‘라면’에 달려 있소.”\n\n그러자 던스터블 양이 노래를 부르기 시작했다.—\n\n“‘아침 이슬을 마시는 / 풀과 꽃을 낱낱이 더듬는다 해도—’\n\n—나머지는 아시죠, 경.\n\n보아네르게스 경은 거의 모든 것을 알고 있었으나 그것은 몰랐다. 그래서 던스터블 양이 이어 불렀다.—\n\n“‘여호와의 권능을 내 것으로 알지 못한다면 / 내가 아는 모든 것이 얼마나 헛되랴.’”\n\n“정확하오, 정확하오, 던스터블 양,” 경이 말했다. “그런데 권능을 내 것으로 알면서 꽃도 더듬으면 안 될 이유가 무엇이오? 어쩌면 하나가 다른 하나를 도울지도 모르지요.”\n\n전체적으로 보아 나는 보아네르게스 경이 이겼다고 생각한다. 하지만 그것이야 그의 본업이다. 그는 평생 이기기만 해 왔다.\n\n공작이 젊은 프랭크 그레셤 씨에게 특별히 친절하다는 것을 모두가 알아보았다. 던스터블 양이 그토록 격렬하게 붙들었던 그 신사와 그 아내의 그 신사다. 이 그레셤 씨는 이 주에서 가장 부유한 평민이었고, 다음 선거에서는 이스트 라이딩의 의원 중 한 사람이 될 것이라는 소문이 있었다. 그런데 공작은 이스트 라이딩과는 관계가 거의 또는 전혀 없었고, 젊은 그레셤이 강경한 보수파로 나설 것이라는 것도 잘 알려져 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그의 땅이 그토록 넓고 그의 돈이 그토록 넉넉했으므로 그는 공작이 눈여겨볼 만한 값이 있었다. 사워비 씨도 그에게 거의 지나칠 만큼 예의를 다했는데, 그것도 당연한 일이었다. 이 아주 젊은 사람이 펜을 한 번 긁는 것만으로 종이 한 조각을 거의 신화적인 값의 은행권으로 바꿀 수 있었으니 말이다.\n\n“그래서 동부 바셋주 사냥개들을 박솔 힐에 두고 있다지요?” 공작이 말했다.\n\n“사냥개들은 거기 있습니다,” 프랭크가 말했다. “하지만 제가 주인은 아닙니다.”\n\n“오! 나는 알기로는—”\n\n“아버지가 가지고 계십니다. 다만 아버지는 그레셤스베리보다 박솔 힐이 더 중심에 있다고 여기시지요. 개와 말이 더 짧은 거리를 가면 되니까요.”\n\n“박솔 힐은 아주 중심에 있소.”\n\n“오, 그렇습니다!”\n\n“그리고 새로 심은 가시금작화 은신처는 잘되고 있소?”\n\n“그저 그렇습니다—가시금작화가 어디서나 잘 자라는 것은 아니더군요. 그랬으면 좋겠는데요.”\n\n“내가 포더길에게 늘 하는 말이 바로 그것이오. 그리고 삼림이 많은 곳에서는 해로운 짐승들을 거기서 나오게 할 수가 없소.”\n\n“하지만 박솔 힐에는 나무가 한 그루도 없어요,” 그레셤 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아, 그렇지. 거기는 아직 새로 자리 잡은 곳이니. 그레셤스베리에는 아무리 봐도 넉넉하지요. 거기 숲이 우리 것보다 넓고. 그렇지 않소, 포더길?”\n\n포더길 씨는 그레셤스베리 숲이 매우 넓다고 하면서도, 다만 아마 자기 생각으로는—\n\n“오, 아! 알고 있소,” 공작이 말했다. “포더길에 따르면 옛날의 검은 숲도 개서럼 숲에 대면 아무것도 아니었지. 그리고 또, 동부 바셋주에 있는 어떤 것도 서부 바셋주에 있는 것과 같을 수 없고. 그렇지 않소, 응, 포더길?”\n\n포더길 씨는 자기가 그 믿음 속에서 자랐고 그 믿음 속에서 죽을 작정이라고 밝혔다.\n\n“박솔 힐의 외래 식물들은 아주 훌륭합니다. 장관이지요!” 사워비 씨가 말했다.\n\n“저는 세상의 모든 외래 식물보다 다 자란 참나무 한 그루가 홀로 당당히 서 있는 것을 택하겠습니다,” 젊은 그레셤이 다소 호언장담하듯 말했다.\n\n“때가 되면 그것들도 자랄 거요,” 공작이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 그 때는 제 생전에 오지 않을 겁니다. 그런데 콜디코츠 숲을 베어 낸다지요, 사워비 씨?”\n\n“글쎄, 그건 나도 모르겠어요. 삼림 지정을 해제한다고 하더군요. 나는 스물두 살 때부터 산림 감독관이었는데도 그것이 베어 낸다는 뜻인지 아직 모르겠어요.”\n\n“베어 내는 것만이 아니라 뿌리째 뽑아냅니다,” 포더길 씨가 말했다.\n\n“살인 같은 짓입니다,” 프랭크 그레셤이 말했다. “그리고 한마디 하자면, 휘그 정부가 아니면 어떤 정부도 그런 일을 하지 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“하, 하, 하!” 각하가 웃었다. “적어도 이것만은 분명하오,” 그가 말했다. “보수 정부가 그렇게 했다면 휘그도 지금 당신만큼 분개했을 거라는 것 말이오.”\n\n“당신이 어떻게 하면 좋을지 말해 드리지요, 그레셤 씨,” 사워비가 말했다. “서부 바셋주 왕실 소유지 전체에 값을 매겨 사겠다고 내놓으세요. 그들은 아주 기뻐하며 팔 겁니다.”\n\n“그리고 우리는 국경 이쪽으로 당신을 맞아들이는 것을 기쁘게 여기겠소,” 공작이 말했다.\n\n젊은 그레셤은 자못 우쭐한 기분이 들었다. 이 주에서 그런 제안을 터무니없는 소리로 들리지 않게 할 수 있는 사람은 많지 않았다. 공작 자신이 현금으로 콜디코츠의 체이스를 살 수 있는지도 의심스러울 수 있었지만, 그, 그레셤이 그렇게 할 수 있다는 것은—그와 그의 아내가 함께라면—아무도 의심하지 않았다. 그리고 그러다가 그레셤 씨는 언젠가 전에 개서럼 성에 왔던 때를 떠올렸다. 그때 그는 무척 가난했고, 공작은 그를 세상에서 가장 정중한 태도로 대해 주지 않았다. 부자가 자기 부에 기대지 않는 것이 얼마나 어려운가! 낙타가 바늘귀를 지나가는 것보다 정말로 더 어렵다.\n\n바셋주 전체가—적어도 서부 바셋주 전체가—던스터블 양이 사워비 씨와 결혼하도록 하기 위해 이 지방으로 데려와졌다는 것을 알고 있었다. 던스터블 양 자신이 이 계획을 미리 알고 있었으리라고는 아무도 짐작하지 않았지만, 일이 저절로 그렇게 될 것이라고 여겨졌다. 사워비 씨에게는 돈이 없었으나, 그는 재치 있고 영리하고 잘생겼으며 하원 의원이었다. 그는 세상 앞에서 살았고, 오래된 가문을 대표했으며, 오래된 저택을 가지고 있었다. 던스터블 양이 이보다 더 나은 짝을 어떻게 얻겠는가? 그녀는 이제 그렇게 젊지 않았고, 주위를 살펴볼 때가 되었다.\n\n사워비 씨에 관한 이 추측은 분명히 사실이었고, 사워비 씨의 몇몇 벗들에 관해서도 그에 못지않게 사실이었다. 그의 누이 해럴드 스미스 부인은 이 일에 헌신했고, 그 목적으로 던스터블 양과 각별한 우정을 급히 쌓아 올렸다. 주교는 아는 척 고개를 끄덕이며 그것이 아주 좋은 일이 될 것이라고 시사했다. 프라우디 부인도 찬성을 표했다. 서플하우스 씨는 자기가 이 지방에 머무는 동안은 「손 떼시오」의 경우여야 한다는 것을 이해하게 되었다. 그리고 공작 자신조차 포더길에게 그 일을 잘 처리하라고 지시했다.\n\n“그가 내게 엄청난 금액을 빚지고 있소,” 사워비 씨의 모든 권리증서를 쥐고 있는 공작이 말했다. “그리고 그 담보가 넉넉할지 의심스럽소.”\n\n“각하께서는 담보가 아주 넉넉하다는 것을 아시게 될 겁니다,” 포더길 씨가 말했다. “하지만 그렇더라도 좋은 혼사가 되겠지요.”\n\n“아주 좋지,” 공작이 말했다. 그리하여 사워비 씨와 던스터블 양이 되도록 빨리 부부가 되게 하는 것이 포더길 씨의 임무가 되었다.\n\n일행 가운데 남들보다 눈치가 밝은 이들은 그가 이미 청혼을 했다고 했고, 다른 이들은 그가 이제 막 하려는 참이라고 했다. 그리고 아주 잘 안다는 어떤 부인은 한때 그가 바로 그 순간에 청혼하고 있다고까지 말했다. 그 부인의 대답에 대해서도, 재산 약정의 조건에 대해서도, 결혼 시기에 대해서도 돈이 걸렸다. 물론 딱한 던스터블 양은 그런 것을 아무것도 몰랐다.\n\n사워비 씨는 자기 일이 그렇게 공개되어 있었음에도 그 일을 아주 잘 처리해 나갔다. 그를 두고 농담하는 이들에게는 그 일에 대해 거의 말하지 않으면서, 이런 일에 자기가 가진 최선의 지식으로 싸움을 이어 갔다. 그러나 이것만은 확실히 밝혀 둘 수 있다. 마크 로바츠가 떠나기로 정해진 날 아침의 전날 저녁까지, 그는 청혼을 하지 않았다.\n\n지난 이틀 동안 사워비 씨의 마크에 대한 친밀함은 갈수록 더 따뜻해졌다. 그는 지금 성에 와 있는 이 거물들의 움직임에 대해 교구 사제에게, 마치 그렇게 거리낌 없이 말할 수 있는 다른 손님이 하나도 없는 것처럼 속을 털어놓았다. 보아하니 그는 자기 매부 해럴드 스미스나 다른 어떤 하원 동료보다도 마크를 더 믿었고, 이 예상되는 결혼 문제에서도 그에게 온 마음을 열어 놓았다. 그런데 사워비 씨는 세상에서 이름 있는 사람이었으니, 이 모든 것이 우리의 젊은 성직자를 적잖이 우쭐하게 했다.\n\n로바츠가 떠나기 전날 저녁, 일행이 모두 흩어질 때 사워비는 그에게 자기 침실로 올라오라고 청했고, 거기서 그를 안락의자에 앉혀 놓고 자기는, 곧 사워비는 방 안을 오르내리며 걸었다.\n\n“당신은 짐작도 못 할 거예요, 마크,” 그가 말했다. “이 일이 나를 어떤 신경 곤두선 불안 속에 몰아넣는지.”\n\n“그냥 물어보고 끝내지 그러세요? 그분은 당신과 함께 있는 걸 좋아하는 것 같은데요.”\n\n“아, 그것만이 아니에요. 바퀴 속에 또 바퀴가 있지요.” 그러고서 그는 한두 번 방 안을 오르내리며 걸었고, 그동안 마크는 이제 자리에 들어도 되겠다고 생각했다.\n\n“당신에게 다 털어놓는 것이 싫다는 건 아니에요,” 사워비가 말했다. “바로 지금 이 순간 나는 얼마 안 되는 현금이 없어서 지독하게 곤궁해요. 그리고 그것이 없어서 이 일에서 파멸할 수도 있어요. 아니, 그렇게 될 것 같아요.”\n\n“해럴드 스미스가 주지 못하나요?”\n\n“하, 하, 하! 당신은 해럴드 스미스를 몰라요. 그가 평생 누구에게 한 실링이라도 빌려 주었다는 얘기를 들어 본 적 있어요?”\n\n“그럼 서플하우스는요?”\n\n“어이구, 참! 당신은 나와 서플하우스가 여기 함께 있는 것을 보고, 그가 우리 집에 와서 지내기도 하고 그러니 그렇게 생각하겠지요. 하지만 서플하우스와 나는 벗이 아니에요. 여기 보세요, 마크—나는 그의 손 전체를 위해서보다, 거기 들린 펜까지 다 합쳐서보다, 당신의 새끼손가락 하나를 위해서 더 많은 일을 할 거예요. 포더길이라면 혹시 해 줄지도 모르지만, 나는 포더길이 지금 이 순간 자기도 쪼들리고 있다는 걸 알아요. 정말 죽겠지요, 그렇지 않아요? 앞으로 이틀 안에 400파운드를 손에 넣지 못하면 나는 이 판을 다 포기해야 해요.”\n\n“그분에게 직접 청해 보세요.”\n\n“뭐라고요, 내가 아내로 맞고 싶은 여자에게? 아니에요, 마크, 나는 아직 그 지경까지 가지는 않았어요. 그러기보다는 그녀를 잃는 것이 낫지요.”\n\n마크는 잠자코 앉아 불을 바라보며, 자기 침실에 있었으면 좋겠다고 생각했다. 그는 사워비 씨가 이 400파운드를 자기가 내놓기를 바란다는 짐작이 들었다. 그리고 자기에게 400파운드가 세상에 없다는 것도, 있다 해도 그것을 사워비 씨에게 주는 것은 지극히 어리석은 짓이라는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 이 사람에게 반쯤 매혹되고 반쯤 겁을 먹고 있었다.\n\n“러프턴은 이보다 더한 일을 내게 해 줄 의무가 있어요,” 사워비 씨가 말을 이었다. “하지만 러프턴은 여기 없지요.”\n\n“아니, 그는 방금 당신을 위해 오천 파운드를 지불했잖아요.”\n\n“나를 위해 오천 파운드를 지불했다고요! 정말이지 그는 그런 일을 하지 않았어요. 그중 육 펜스도 내 손에 들어오지 않았어요. 믿어 주세요, 마크, 당신은 그 일의 전모를 아직 몰라요. 그렇다고 러프턴을 두고 한마디라도 나쁘게 말하려는 것은 아니에요. 그는 명예 그 자체지요. 다만 돈 문제에서는 지독하게 느리고요. 그는 그 일 전체에서 자기가 옳다고 생각했지만, 그렇게까지 어처구니없이 틀린 사람은 없었어요. 아니, 당신 자신도 그 일을 바로 그렇게 보았던 것을 기억하지 못해요?”\n\n“그가 잘못 생각하는 것 같다고 말한 건 기억해요.”\n\n“물론 그가 잘못 생각한 거지요. 그리고 그 잘못이 내게 비싸게 값을 물렸어요. 나는 이삼 년 동안 그 돈을 메워야 했어요. 그리고 내 재산은 그의 것 같지가 않아요—그랬으면 좋겠지만.”\n\n“던스터블 양과 결혼하세요. 그러면 그것이 다 해결될 텐데요.”\n\n“아! 이 돈이 있으면 그러겠어요. 적어도 일을 결말까지 끌고 가겠지요. 그러니 이렇게 하면 어때요, 마크. 당신이 이 곤경에서 나를 도와준다면 나는 그것을 결코 잊지 않겠어요. 그리고 언젠가는 내가 당신을 위해 무언가 할 수 있는 때가 돌아올 거예요.”\n\n“나는 세상에 백 파운드도, 아니 오십 파운드도 가진 것이 없어요.”\n\n“물론 없겠지요. 사람들이 주머니에 400파운드를 넣고 거리를 돌아다니지는 않지요. 이 집에 있는 사람 가운데 은행에 그런 금액을 둔 사람이 하나라도 있을 것 같지 않아요. 공작이라면 몰라도.”\n\n“그럼 원하는 게 뭐예요?”\n\n“그야 당신의 이름이지요, 당연히. 믿어 주세요, 내 좋은 벗, 정말로 당신에게 그만한 액수를 주머니에서 꺼내라고 청하지는 않을 거예요. 석 달 기한으로 그 금액을 당신 앞으로 발행하게 해 주세요. 그때가 오기 훨씬 전에 나는 넉넉해질 거예요.” 그러고서 마크가 대답도 하기 전에 그는 어음 용지와 펜과 잉크를 탁자에 꺼내 놓고, 마치 벗이 이미 승낙한 것처럼 어음을 채워 넣고 있었다.\n\n“정말이지, 사워비, 나는 그렇게 하고 싶지 않아요.”\n\n“왜요! 무엇이 무서워요?”—사워비 씨는 이것을 아주 날카롭게 물었다. “내가 만기가 된 어음을 인수하지 않고 내버려 두었다는 얘기를 들어 본 적 있어요?” 로바츠는 그런 얘기를 들은 적이 있다고 생각했지만, 당황한 나머지 확실하게 짚어 낼 수가 없었고, 그래서 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n“아니에요, 마크. 나는 그 지경까지 가지 않았어요. 여기 보세요. 그저 여기에 ‘인수함, 마크 로바츠’라고 써 주기만 하면, 당신은 이 거래에 대해 다시는 듣지 않을 거예요.—그리고 나에게 영원히 은혜를 베푸는 거지요.”\n\n“성직자로서 그렇게 하는 것은 옳지 않아요,” 로바츠가 말했다.\n\n“성직자로서라니! 이봐요, 마크! 벗을 위해 그만한 일도 하고 싶지 않다면 그렇다고 말하세요. 하지만 그런 식의 수작은 그만둡시다. 지방 은행의 어음 뒷면에서 다른 어떤 계층보다 더 자주 이름이 발견되는 계층이 하나 있다면, 성직자가 바로 그 계층이에요. 어서요, 마크, 내가 이렇게 몰려 있는데 나를 내던지지는 않을 거지요.”\n\n마크 로바츠는 펜을 들어 어음에 서명했다. 그가 평생 그런 일을 한 것은 그때가 처음이었다. 사워비는 그러고서 그의 손을 다정하게 흔들었고, 그는 참담한 사람이 되어 자기 침실로 걸어갔다.\n\n# 제9장. 교구 사제의 귀환\n\n이튿날 아침 로바츠 씨는 무거운 마음으로 그 지체 높은 벗들에게 작별을 알렸다. 그는 밤의 절반을 뜬눈으로 누워 자기가 저지른 일을 생각하며 제 처지와 화해해 보려 애썼다. 사워비 씨의 방을 나서기가 무섭게 그는, 석 달이 지나면 그 400파운드 때문에 다시 시달리게 되리라는 것을 확신했다. 복도를 걸어가는 동안, 그 사내에 관해 알려진 온갖 전력이 어찌나 빠르게 밀려들었던지, 어음 인지를 앞에 놓고 펜과 잉크를 손 닿는 데 갖춘 채 그 안락의자에 앉아 있을 때에는 도무지 떠오르지 않던 것들이었다. 러프턴 경이 그에게 했던 말이 떠올랐다 — 곤경에 버려졌다고 하소연하던 그 말이. 콜디코츠에서 돈을 받아 내는 것은 불가능하다는, 주 전체에 퍼져 있는 온갖 이야기들이 생각났다. 그 사내의 익히 알려진 됨됨이가 되살아났다. 그러자 그는, 그 무거운 지불의 적어도 일부는 자기가 갚아야 할 각오를 해야 한다는 것을 알았다.\n\n무엇 하러 그는 이 끔찍한 곳에 왔던가? 프램리의 제 집에, 사람의 마음이 바랄 만한 것이 다 있지 않았던가? 아니다. 사람의 마음은 수석 사제 자리를 바랄 수 있다 — 즉 교구 사제인 사람의 마음이 그렇다. 그리고 수석 사제인 사람의 마음은 주교 자리를 바랄 수 있다. 또 주교인 사람의 눈앞에는 램버스의 초월적인 영광이 어른거리지 않겠는가? 그는 자기가 야심가임을 스스로 인정해 왔다. 그러나 이제는 또 하나, 여태 그 야심의 목적을 향해 걸어온 길이 참으로 딱한 길이었음도 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 식사 시간, 그를 태울 말과 이륜마차가 도착하기 전에, 벗 사워비만큼 환한 사람은 없었다. “그래, 떠나시는군요?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“네, 오늘 아침에 떠나려고요.”\n\n“러프턴에게 제 다정한 인사를 다 전해 주세요. 사냥터에서라면 마주칠 수도 있겠지만, 그러지 않으면 봄까지는 못 만날 겁니다. 제가 프램리에 가는 건 어림도 없는 일이고요. 마님은 제 꼬리를 찾으실 테고, 유황 냄새가 난다고 단언하실 테니까요. 그럼 잘 가요, 이 친구!”\n\n그 독일인 학생도 악마와 처음 거래를 맺었을 때 새 친구에게 말로 다 할 수 없는 매력을 느꼈다. 지금 로바츠의 경우도 그러했다. 그는 사워비의 손을 아주 따뜻하게 잡고, 어디서든 곧 다시 만나기를 바란다고 말했으며, 그 숙녀와의 일이 어떻게 되었는지 꼭 알려 달라며 유별난 관심을 내보였다. 거래를 맺은 이상 — 이 소중한 벗을 위해 반년치 수입에 가까운 돈을 물어 주기로 한 이상 — 그 돈만큼의 값어치는 되도록 많이 챙겨야 하지 않겠는가? 이 화려한 하원 의원의 소중한 우정이 그 값어치가 아니라면, 대체 무엇이 그 값어치겠는가? 그러나 그때 그는, 사워비 씨가 지난 저녁만큼 오늘 아침에는 자기를 아껴 주지 않는다고 느꼈다. 아니면 느꼈다고 여겼다. “그럼 잘 가요.” 사워비 씨는 그렇게 말했으나, 앞으로 만나자는 말은 한마디도 하지 않았고, 편지를 쓰겠다는 약속조차 하지 않았다. 사워비 씨는 아마 마음에 걸린 일이 많았을 것이다. 한 건의 볼일을 끝냈으니 곧바로 다음 건을 챙기는 것이 그로서는 마땅한 일이었을지도 모른다.\n\n로바츠가 스스로 책임을 지고 만 그 금액은 — 자기가 갚으라는 소리를 듣게 되지 않을까 그토록 두려워한 그 금액은 — 반년치 수입에 거의 맞먹었다. 그런데 그는 결혼한 뒤로 아직 한 실링도 모아 두지 못했다. 목사관에 자리를 잡고 보니, 세상 사람 모두가 자기를 부유한 사람으로 여긴다는 것도 알게 되었다. 그는 세상 모두의 그 판정을 참으로 받아들여, 편안하게 사는 일에 스스로 힘을 쏟았다. 부목사가 꼭 있어야 할 형편은 아니었다. 그러나 70파운드쯤은 감당할 수 있었다 — 레이디 러프턴이 다소 분별없이 그렇게 말한 대로였다. 게다가 존스 씨를 교구에 붙들어 두면 같은 성직자 형제에게 자선을 베푸는 일이 되고, 자기 처지도 더 홀로 설 수 있는 자리에 놓이게 되었다. 레이디 러프턴은 자기가 아끼는 성직자가 넉넉하고 편안하게 지내는 것을 보고 싶어 했다. 그러나 이제 일이 이렇게 되고 보니, 그녀는 이 부목사 일을 몹시 후회했다. 존스 씨는 프램리에서 떠나게 만들어야 한다고, 그녀는 혼자서 한 번 이상 되뇌었다.\n\n그는 아내에게 조랑말 마차를 사 주었고, 자기 몫으로는 승용마 한 마리와 이륜마차용 말 한 마리를 더 두었다. 그만한 처지에, 그만큼 넉넉한 사람이라면 그 정도는 있어야 했다. 하인도 한 명 두었고, 정원사와 마부도 두었다. 뒤의 둘은 없어서는 안 될 사람이었으나, 앞의 하나를 두고는 말이 있었다. 아내는 그 하인을 두는 데 단연 반대였다. 그러나 그런 일에서는 무릇 망설이는 쪽이 지는 법이다. 하인 문제를 한 주 동안 의논하고 나니, 그 하인 역시 없어서는 안 될 사람이라는 것이 주인에게는 아주 분명해졌다.\n\n그날 아침 마차를 몰아 집으로 가면서 그는 그 하인에게 파면을 선고했고, 그 승용마에게도 함께 선고했다. 적어도 그 둘은 내보내야 했다. 그리고 앞으로는 스코틀랜드 여행에 돈을 쓰지 않을 것이며, 무엇보다도 한밤중 그 마법 같은 시각에 빈털터리 하원 의원의 침실에는 얼씬거리지 않을 것이었다. 집으로 마차를 몰며 그는 그런 결심들을 스스로 세웠다. 그러고는 그 400파운드를 어디서 마련해야 하나 지친 마음으로 곰곰이 생각했다. 그 일에 사워비가 도움을 주는 것에 대해서는 — 그는 스스로 아무 기대도 걸지 않았다.\n\n그러나 아내가 머리에 명주 숄을 쓰고 현관 밖으로 나와 그를 맞으며, 그가 이륜마차에서 내리는 것을 지켜보면서 짐짓 몸을 떠는 시늉을 할 때, 그는 다시 거의 행복해진 기분이었다.\n\n“여보, 우리 영감님,” 아내는 그가 걸친 것을 그대로 두른 채인 그를 따뜻한 응접실로 이끌면서 말했다. “꽁꽁 얼었겠어요.” 그러나 마크는 오는 길 내내 사워비 씨 침실에서의 그 거래를 너무 골똘히 생각한 나머지 바깥 공기가 찬 줄도 몰랐다. 이제 그는 사랑하는 패니의 허리에 팔을 감고 있었다. 그런데 그 거래를 아내에게 말해야 할까? 적어도 지금은 아니었다. 두 아들이 그의 품에 안겨, 입맞춤으로 그의 구레나룻에 서린 물기를 문질러 닦아 내는 이 순간에는. 아무리 생각해도, 집에 돌아오는 그 순간에 견줄 만한 것이 어디 있겠는가?\n\n“그래서 러프턴이 와 있단 말이지. 얘, 프랭크, 살살 하렴,” — 프랭크는 그의 맏아들이었다 — “그러다 아기를 벽난로 울에 처박겠구나.”\n\n“아기는 제가 안을게요. 둘을 한꺼번에 안기는 무리예요, 어찌나 힘이 센지,” 자랑스러운 어머니가 말했다. “아, 네, 어제 이른 시각에 돌아오셨어요.”\n\n“당신은 만나 봤어?”\n\n“어제 마님과 함께 여기 오셨어요. 그리고 저는 오늘 그 댁에서 점심을 먹었고요. 편지가 때맞춰 닿아서 메러디스 부부를 붙들어 두었잖아요. 그분들은 내일이나 떠나실 테니 당신도 결국 만나게 될 거예요. 조지 경은 그 일로 몹시 성이 났지만, 레이디 러프턴이 뜻을 굽히지 않으셨죠. 마님이 저러시는 건 당신도 여태 본 적 없을 거예요.”\n\n“기분이 아주 좋으시다는 거지?”\n\n“그럼요. 러프턴 경의 말들이 다 오는 중이고, 경께서 3월까지 여기 계실 거래요.”\n\n“3월까지!”\n\n“마님이 저한테 그렇게 속삭이셨어요. 아들이 온다는 데 대한 승리감을 감추지 못하시더군요. 경께서는 올해 레스터셔를 아예 접으실 거래요. 대체 무슨 일로 이렇게 됐을까요?” 마크는 무슨 일로 그렇게 되었는지 아주 잘 알고 있었다. 독자도 이미 알게 되었듯이, 레이디 러프턴이 아들의 방문을 사들인 값이 얼마였는지 그는 들어 알고 있었다. 그러나 어머니가 아들에게 5,000파운드를 선물했다는 것은 아무도 로바츠 부인에게 말해 주지 않았다.\n\n“이제는 무슨 일에든 기분이 좋으세요,” 패니가 말을 이었다. “그러니 개서럼 성 이야기는 아무것도 하실 필요 없어요.”\n\n“하지만 처음 그 얘기를 들었을 때는 몹시 화가 났었지. 그렇지 않아?”\n\n“글쎄요, 마크, 사실대로 말하면 그러셨어요. 위층 그분 방에서 한바탕 난리가 났죠 — 저스티니아와 저요. 마음에 안 드는 다른 소식도 같은 때에 들으셨거든요. 그래서 — 하지만 그분 성미는 당신도 아시잖아요. 아주 뜨겁게 확 타오르셨어요.”\n\n“그리고 나에 대해 온갖 험한 말을 하셨겠지.”\n\n“공작에 대해서라면 그러셨죠. 그분이 공작을 좋아한 적이 없다는 건 당신도 아시고요. 그리고 그 문제라면 저도 마찬가지예요. 미리 분명히 말해 두는데요, 마크 도련님!”\n\n“공작은 소문만큼 나쁜 사람은 아니야.”\n\n“아, 그건 또 다른 어떤 위대한 분에 대해서도 당신이 하는 말이잖아요. 어쨌든 그이가 우리를 괴롭히러 여기 오지는 않겠죠. 아무튼 저는 그분 기분이 세상에서 가장 좋다고는 할 수 없는 상태로 그 댁을 나왔어요. 저도 확 타올랐거든요. 그것도 아셔야 해요.”\n\n“그랬을 테지,” 마크는 아내의 허리를 팔로 감아 안으며 말했다.\n\n“그래서 이제 끔찍한 전쟁이 벌어지겠구나 싶었어요. 집에 돌아와서 당신에게 그렇게 처량한 편지를 썼고요. 그런데 봉투를 막 봉하자마자 무슨 일이 있었겠어요, 마님이 들어오셨어요 — 혼자서, 그리고 —. 하지만 그분이 무엇을 하고 무슨 말을 하셨는지는 당신에게 말할 수 없어요. 다만 참으로 아름답게 행동하셨어요. 딱 그분다우셨죠. 사랑과 진실과 정직으로 가득해서요. 그분 같은 사람은 없어요, 마크. 그리고 그분은 여태 무엇을 걸쳤든 그 모든 공작들보다 훌륭한 분이에요 — 공작들이 무엇을 걸치는지야 모르겠지만요.”\n\n“뿔과 굽이지. 당신과 레이디 러프턴에 따르면 그게 그들이 늘 걸치는 것이고,” 그는 사워비 씨가 자기 자신에 대해 했던 말을 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n“저에 대해서는 무슨 말이든 하셔도 돼요, 마크. 하지만 레이디 러프턴을 험담하시면 안 돼요. 그리고 뿔과 굽이 사악함과 방탕을 뜻하는 거라면, 그리 틀린 말도 아닐 거예요. 자, 그 두꺼운 외투는 벗고 편히 앉으세요.” 마크 로바츠가 그 큰 죄악을 지은 일로 아내에게서 들은 꾸중은 그것이 전부였다.\n\n“이 어음 거래는 아내에게 반드시 말하겠다,” 그는 혼자 되뇌었다. “그러나 오늘은 아니다. 러프턴을 만나 본 뒤가 아니면 안 된다.”\n\n그날 저녁 그들은 프램리 코트에서 만찬을 했고, 거기서 그 젊은 경을 만났다. 레이디 러프턴 역시 여전히 기분이 아주 좋은 것을 보았다. 러프턴 경은 준수하고 환한 인상의 젊은이였다. 마크 로바츠만큼 키가 크지는 않았고, 얼굴에 새겨진 지력은 아마 덜했다. 그러나 이목구비가 더 고왔고, 그 얼굴에는 좋은 기분과 부드러운 성미가 그대로 드러나 있었다. 참으로 보기에 즐거운 얼굴이었으니, 레이디 러프턴은 그 얼굴을 바라보는 것을 몹시 사랑했다.\n\n“그래, 마크, 자네 블레셋 사람들 틈에 있었다지?” 그것이 경의 첫마디였다. 로바츠는 벗의 두 손을 잡으며 웃었고, 그것이 얼마나 참말인지를 속으로 생각했다. 그는 정말로 이미 “블레셋의 멍에 아래 스스로 사슬에 묶인” 몸이었다. 아, 슬프도다, 오늘날의 블레셋 사람들이 채운 사슬은 끊어 내기가 참으로 어렵다. 삼손이 이따금 그들의 머리 위로 신전을 무너뜨린다 해도, 그 자신도 그들과 함께 그 폐허에 묻히고 마는 것이 아닌가? 오늘날의 블레셋 사람만큼 끈덕지게 달라붙는 말거머리는 없다.\n\n“그래서 결국 조지 경을 붙잡았군요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. 그의 부재에 대해 그녀가 한 말은 그것이 거의 전부였다. 그 뒤에 강연에 관한 이야기가 조금 오갔는데, 마님의 말에서 그 교구 사제가 근래 머물렀던 사람들을 그녀가 좋아하지 않는다는 것은 분명히 드러났다. 그러나 그 자신을 겨냥한 말이나 책망으로 받아들일 만한 말은 한마디도 하지 않았다. 강연장에서 프라우디 부인이 연설을 했다는 그 작은 일화는 이미 프램리까지 닿아 있었으니, 레이디 러프턴이 그 우스개를 즐거워하는 것은 당연한 일이었다. 그녀는 강연의 본문 자체가 주교 부인의 입에서 나온 것이라고 짐짓 믿는 척했다. 그리고 나중에 마크가 그 일요일 아침 식탁에서 보았던 부인의 옷차림을 이야기하자, 레이디 러프턴은 부인이 바로 그 옷을 입은 채로 대중 앞에서 제 재능을 뽐낸 것으로 여기려 했다.\n\n“그 이야기를 직접 들었다면 5파운드 지폐라도 냈을 거요,” 조지 경이 말했다.\n\n“나는 아니에요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “로바츠 씨가 지금처럼 생생하게 그려 주는 이야기로 들으면 웃지 않기가 어렵죠. 하지만 우리 주교 가운데 한 분의 부인이 스스로 그런 처지에 서는 것을 직접 보는 일은 나로서는 몹시 마음 아플 거예요. 그래도 그는 어쨌든 주교니까요.”\n\n“정말이지, 마님, 저는 메러디스 생각에 찬성이에요,” 러프턴 경이 말했다. “꽤나 볼만했겠죠. 어차피 일이 벌어진 이상 — 교회가 그런 망신을 당할 운명이었던 이상, 저도 그 이야기를 직접 들었으면 좋았을 겁니다.”\n\n“너는 충격을 받았을 게다, 루도빅.”\n\n“시간이 지나면 그건 넘겼을 거예요, 어머니. 아마 투우 같았겠죠 — 보기에는 틀림없이 끔찍하지만 몹시 흥미로운. 그런데 해럴드 스미스는, 마크, 그 내내 무얼 하고 있었나?”\n\n“그리 오래 걸리지는 않았네,” 로바츠가 말했다.\n\n“그 딱한 주교님은,” 레이디 메러디스가 말했다. “어떤 모습이었나요? 저는 정말로 그분이 안됐어요.”\n\n“글쎄요, 주교님은 잠들어 계셨던 것 같습니다.”\n\n“뭐요, 그 내내 자고 있었단 말이오?” 조지 경이 말했다.\n\n“그 소리에 깨셨습니다. 그러고는 벌떡 일어나 뭐라고 말씀하셨죠.”\n\n“뭐요, 소리 내어서 말이오?”\n\n“한마디쯤이었습니다.”\n\n“참 볼썽사나운 광경이군요!” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그 앞에 그 교구에 계셨던 선량한 노인을 기억하는 사람에게는 정말이지 충격적인 일이에요. 그분이 네게 견진을 주셨지, 루도빅. 너도 그분을 기억해야 한다. 바체스터에서였고, 그 뒤에 너는 그분과 점심을 먹었어.”\n\n“기억해요. 특히 이건 기억나죠. 그런 타르트는 그전에도 그 뒤로도 평생 먹어 본 적이 없다는 것. 그 노인께서 특별히 제게 그것을 권하셨고, 제가 그 뜻에 동의하니 유난히 기뻐하시는 것 같았어요. 지금 주교관에서는 그만한 타르트가 나오지 않을 겁니다. 틀림없어요.”\n\n“가서 한번 청해 보겠다면 프라우디 부인이 기꺼이 최선을 다해 줄 거요,” 조지 경이 말했다.\n\n“그 애가 그런 짓은 하지 않기를 바라요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. 마크의 그 방문들에 관해 그녀가 한 매서운 말은 그것이 전부였다.\n\n조지 메러디스 경이 그 자리에 있었으므로, 로바츠는 사워비 씨와 사워비 씨의 돈 문제에 대해서 러프턴 경에게 그때는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다. 그러나 이튿날 아침에 단둘이 만나기로 약속은 해 두었다.\n\n“내려와서 내 말들을 좀 보아야 하네, 마크. 오늘 도착했어. 메러디스 부부는 열두 시에 떠날 테니, 그다음에 우리 한 시간쯤 함께 있을 수 있겠지.” 마크는 그러겠다고 했고, 그러고는 아내를 팔에 끼고 집으로 갔다.\n\n“자, 이제, 그분이 다정하지 않으세요?” 자갈길에 함께 나서기가 무섭게 패니가 말했다.\n\n“다정하지. 지금 당장은 내가 당신에게 말해 줄 수 있는 것보다 더 다정하고. 그런데 그분이 그 딱한 주교에게 그토록 매서운 걸 당신은 여태 알고 있었어? 정말이지 그 주교는 그리 나쁜 사람이 아니야.”\n\n“네, 저는 훨씬 더 매서운 것도 알아요. 그분이 주교 부인을 어떻게 여기시는지 말이에요. 그리고 마크, 그렇게 일어나서 나서는 건 정말 숙녀답지 못한 일이었어요. 바체스터 사람들이 그 부인을 어떻게 생각하겠어요?”\n\n“내가 본 바로는 바체스터 사람들은 그걸 좋아했어.”\n\n“말도 안 돼요, 마크. 그럴 수가 없죠. 하지만 그건 지금 접어 두고요. 저는 당신이 그분이 훌륭한 분이라고 인정하시길 바라요.” 그러고 나서 로바츠 부인은 그 노마님에 대한 또 한 차례 긴 찬사를 이어 갔다. 목사관에서 용서를 청하러 온 그 일이 있은 뒤로, 로바츠 부인은 자기 벗을 어떻게 더 좋게 생각해야 할지 알 수 없을 만큼 좋게 생각하고 있었다. 게다가 그 끔찍한 폭풍과 태풍의 위협이 지나간 뒤의 저녁이 참으로 즐거웠다. 남편이 판단을 그르친 뒤에도 그토록 잘 맞아들여졌다. 그토록 쓰라려 보였던 상처가 그토록 깨끗이 아물었고, 모든 것이 그토록 즐거웠다. 그 작은 어음 이야기를 알았더라면 이 모든 것이 얼마나 달라졌겠는가!\n\n이튿날 아침 열두 시에 경과 교구 사제는 프램리의 마구간을 함께 거닐고 있었다. 그곳은 한바탕 소동이 벌어져 있었으니, 이 건물의 큰 부분은 근래 여러 해 동안 좀처럼 쓰이지 않았기 때문이다. 그런데 이제는 온통 붐비고 부산했다. 아주 값진 말 일곱인가 여덟 마리가 레스터셔에서 러프턴 경을 따라왔고, 그것들은 모두 프램리의 구식 마부가 보기에는 다소 과하다 싶은 넓이를 요구했다. 그러나 경에게는 이 일을 제 손에 온전히 거머쥔 제 사람이 따로 있었다.\n\n마크는 성직자이면서도 좋은 말을 좋아할 만큼은 넉넉히 세속적이었다. 그래서 한동안은 러프턴 경이 이 네 살배기 암말의 장점을, 그리고 마우스트랩 어미에서 난 저 훌륭한 래틀본스 수망아지의 장점을 늘어놓게 두었다. 그러나 그에게는 마음을 무겁게 누르는 다른 일이 있었으므로, 말들에게 반 시간을 바친 뒤에 그는 벗을 관목 산책길로 데려가는 데 성공했다.\n\n“그래, 사워비와는 정리했다고,” 로바츠가 먼저 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“정리했지. 그래, 그런데 그 값이 얼마인지 아나?”\n\n“5,000파운드를 냈다고 들었네.”\n\n“그렇지. 그리고 그전에도 3,000쯤. 그것도 내가 실제로는 한 실링도 빚지지 않은 일에서 말이야. 앞으로 무슨 일을 하든, 사워비의 손아귀에는 들어가지 않겠네.”\n\n“그렇다고 그가 자네에게 부당하게 했다고 생각하는 건 아니지.”\n\n“마크, 솔직히 말하면 나는 그 일을 머리에서 쫓아냈고 다시 꺼내고 싶지 않네. 어머니가 재산을 지키려고 그 돈을 내주셨으니, 물론 내가 되갚아야 하지. 하지만 앞으로 사워비와는 돈 거래를 더 하지 않겠다고는 약속할 수 있을 것 같네. 그가 부정직하다고까지는 말하지 않겠지만, 어쨌든 약삭빠른 사람이야.”\n\n“그런데 러프턴, 내가 그를 위해 400파운드 어음에 내 이름을 적었다고 말하면 자네는 뭐라고 하겠나?”\n\n“뭐라고 하겠느냐고. 그야 나는 이렇게 말하겠지 —. 아니, 자네 농담하는 거지. 자네 같은 처지의 사람이 그런 일을 할 리가 없어.”\n\n“하지만 나는 했네.”\n\n러프턴 경이 길고 낮게 휘파람을 불었다.\n\n“내가 거기 있던 마지막 밤에 그가 청했네. 대단한 청이라도 되는 듯이 굴면서, 자기 어음이 부도난 적은 여태 한 번도 없다고 못 박더군.”\n\n러프턴 경이 또 휘파람을 불었다. “자기 어음이 부도난 적이 없다니! 유대인들 지갑에는 그가 부도낸 종이가 잔뜩 들어차 있는데! 그런데 자네가 정말로 400파운드에 이름을 내주었단 말인가?”\n\n“분명히 그랬네.”\n\n“기한은 언제인가?”\n\n“석 달.”\n\n“그러면 그 돈을 어디서 구할지는 생각해 보았나?”\n\n“구할 수 없다는 건 아주 잘 알고 있네. 적어도 그때까지는 안 되지. 은행에서 갱신해 주어야 하고, 나는 조금씩 나누어 갚아야 할 걸세. 사워비가 정말로 그것을 떠맡지 않는다면 말이지.”\n\n“그가 국채를 떠맡을 가능성이나 마찬가지일 걸세.”\n\n그러고 나서 로바츠는 던스터블 양과 하려는 그 혼인 이야기를 그에게 전하며, 그 숙녀가 아마 그 신사를 받아들일 것이라는 제 생각을 덧붙였다.\n\n“전혀 있을 수 없는 일은 아니지,” 경이 말했다. “사워비는 사람 마음에 드는 자이니까. 그렇게만 되면 그는 평생 원하는 것을 다 갖게 될 걸세. 하지만 그의 채권자들은 아무것도 얻지 못하지. 그의 권리 증서를 쥐고 있는 공작은 틀림없이 자기 돈을 받아 낼 테고, 영지는 사실상 아내의 것이 될 테지. 그러나 자네 같은 잔챙이들은 한 실링도 못 받네.”\n\n딱한 마크! 그는 전에도 이런 짐작은 하고 있었다. 그러나 이토록 확실한 말로 눈앞에 들이닥친 적은 없었다. 그러니 그 어음에 서명한 자신의 약함에 대한 벌로, 그는 400파운드만이 아니라 이자와 갱신 비용과 수수료와 어음 인지값까지 얹은 400파운드를 물어야 한다는 것이 명백한 사실이었다. 그렇다. 공작을 찾아간 그 방문 동안 그는 분명히 블레셋 사람들 틈에 들어가 버렸다. 콜디코츠와 개서럼 성의 영광을 아예 포기하는 편이 자기에게 나았으리라는 것이 이제 그에게 꽤 분명하게 보이기 시작했다.\n\n그런데 이제, 아내에게는 어떻게 말해야 하는가?\n\n# 제10장. 루시 로바츠\n\n그런데 이제 이 일을 아내에게 어떻게 말한다? 우리가 마크 로바츠를 마지막으로 두고 떠나올 때 그의 마음을 무겁게 짓누르던 고민이 바로 그것이었다. 그는 결심에 이르기까지 그 문제를 여러 번 마음속에서 뒤집어 보았다. 마침내 결심을 세웠는데, 그것을 실행에 옮길 수만 있다면 아주 나쁜 결심은 아니었다고 말할 만하다.\n\n자기 어음이 어느 은행에서 할인되었는지를 알아내기로 했다. 사워비 씨에게 물어보고, 그에게서 알아낼 수 없으면 바체스터의 은행 세 곳을 찾아가 볼 참이었다. 그 어음이 그중 한 곳으로 넘어갔으리라는 것은 꽤 확실하게 느끼고 있었다. 지배인에게 자기가 결국 그 금액을 물어내야 할 것이라는 확신과, 석 달이 끝나는 날에는 그럴 능력이 없다는 사정과, 자기 수입의 형편을 통틀어 설명하면, 은행가가 이 일을 어떻게 처리할 수 있는지 알려 줄 것이었다. 이자를 붙여 석 달마다 50파운드씩은 낼 수 있으리라 생각했다. 은행가와 이 일을 맞추어 놓는 즉시 아내에게 모든 것을 알릴 것이었다. 만사가 이렇게 미정인 지금 이 순간에 아내에게 말한다면, 그 소식은 아내를 겁에 질려 병이 나게 만들 터였다.\n\n그러나 이튿날 아침 우체부 로빈의 손을 거쳐 소식이 하나 닿았고, 그 소식은 오랫동안 그의 모든 계획을 헝클어 놓았다. 편지는 엑서터에서 온 것이었다. 아버지가 병으로 누웠고, 이내 위태롭다는 진단이 내려졌다는 것이었다. 그 저녁—누이가 편지를 쓴 그 저녁—에 노인은 훨씬 더 나빠졌으며, 마크가 되도록 빨리 엑서터로 떠나오는 것이 좋겠다고 했다. 물론 그는 엑서터로 갔다. 프램리의 영혼들은 또 한 번 웨일스 출신 저교회파 목사의 처분에 맡겨졌다. 프램리는 실버브리지에서 4마일밖에 떨어져 있지 않고, 실버브리지에서는 서쪽으로 가는 직행로에 오르게 된다. 그래서 그는 그날 해가 지기 전에 엑서터에 닿았다.\n\n그러나 그것으로도 아버지의 살아 있는 얼굴을 다시 보기에는 너무 늦었다. 노인의 병은 갑작스럽고 빨랐고, 그는 맏아들을 다시 보지 못한 채 숨을 거두었다. 마크가 상가에 이르렀을 때, 식구들은 마침 자기들의 처지가 온통 달라졌음을 실감하기 시작하던 참이었다.\n\n로바츠 선생의 이력은 대체로 성공적이었지만, 그럼에도 세상이 그가 가졌으리라 여겨 준 만큼의 돈을 남기지는 못했다. 누가 그러기나 하던가? 로바츠 선생은 대가족을 교육시켰고, 언제나 온갖 안락을 누리며 살았으며, 스스로 벌지 않은 1실링은 가져 본 적이 없었다. 부유한 노신사들과 중년 부인들이 그를 신뢰하기 시작하면 의사의 진료비란 과연 흐뭇할 만큼 빠르게 들어온다. 그러나 아내 하나와 아이 일곱에게 세상이 가장 바람직하다고 여기는 것을 다 해 주다 보면, 그 진료비는 들어오던 것과 거의 같은 속도로 빠져나간다. 우리가 보았듯이 마크는 해로와 옥스퍼드에서 교육을 받았으니, 그는 제 몫의 유산을 인생의 이른 시기에 미리 받았다고 해도 되겠다. 둘째 형제인 제럴드 로바츠에게는 정예 연대의 장교직이 사들여졌다. 그 역시 운이 좋아 크림반도에서 살아남아 대위가 되었고, 소령 계급을 사들일 대금까지 예치되어 있었다. 그리고 아들 가운데 막내인 존 로바츠는 프티백 청의 서기로, 벌써 프티백 경 본인의 개인 비서 보좌관 자리에까지 올라 있었다. 지금까지 봉급이 많은 자리는 아니었을지언정 신임이 상당히 두터운 자리였다. 그의 교육에도 돈이 아낌없이 들어갔는데, 요즘은 젊은 사람이 현대 어학을 적어도 세 가지는 알지 못하면 프티백 청에 들어갈 수 없기 때문이다. 게다가 삼각법이나 성서 신학, 또는 사어 하나에도 정통해야 한다—그 셋 중 무엇을 택하느냐는 본인의 재량이다.\n\n그리고 선생에게는 딸이 넷 있었다. 위의 두 딸은 이미 시집을 갔는데, 그중에는 교구 사제의 결혼식에서 러프턴 경이 반해 버렸어야 마땅했던 그 블랜치도 있었다. 경이 할 일을 데번셔의 어느 지주가 대신 해 주었다. 다만 그가 블랜치와 결혼하려면 몇천 파운드, 아마 2, 3천 파운드는 있어야 했고, 늙은 선생이 그 돈이 나오도록 마련해 주었다. 위의 딸도 아버지의 집을 아주 빈손으로 떠난 것은 아니었다. 그러므로 선생이 세상을 떠날 무렵 집에 남아 있는 자식은 둘이었고, 그중 아래인 루시 하나만이 우리 이야기가 흘러가는 동안 우리 앞에 자주 나서게 될 것이다.\n\n마크는 엑서터에 열흘을 머물렀다. 그와 데번셔의 지주가 유언장에서 집행자로 지명되어 있었기 때문이다. 그 문서에는 선생이 자기 자식들 대부분에 대해서는 이미 대비가 되어 있다고 믿는다는 뜻이 적혀 있었다. 사랑하는 아들 마크에 대해서는, 그가 조금도 불안해할 필요가 없음을 자기는 알고 있다고 했다. 그 대목을 읽어 주는 것을 듣고 마크는 곱게 웃으며 아주 너그러운 표정을 지었다. 그러면서도 가슴은 속으로 다소 내려앉았다. 마침맞은 때에 떨어지는 자그마한 횡재가 저 끔찍한 사워비 씨의 몽마를 단번에 떨어낼 수 있게 해 주지 않을까 하는 바람이 있었던 것이다. 이어 유언장은 메리와 제럴드와 블랜치도 하느님의 섭리로 궁핍을 면할 자리에 놓였음을 선언했다. 그리고 이 대목에서 지주의 얼굴을 들여다본 사람이라면 그의 가슴도 조금 내려앉았다고 여겼을 것이다. 세상에 훨씬 더 많이 나서 본 처남만큼 자기 감정을 잘 다스리지는 못했으니까. 개인 비서 보좌관인 존에게는 1,000파운드의 유산이 남겨졌고, 제인과 루시에게는 4퍼센트 공채로 얼마씩이 남겨졌는데, 이는 장가들 마음을 품은 신중한 젊은 베네딕들의 눈에 그 아가씨들의 손 값을 실속 있게 올려 주기에 충분한 액수였다. 그 밖에는 가구뿐이었고, 선생은 그것을 팔아 그 돈을 모두에게 나누어 주기를 바랐다. 한 사람 앞에 60이나 70파운드쯤은 될 터였으니, 장례에 딸린 비용을 치를 만했다.\n\n그러고 나서 그곳과 그 근방의 남녀노소가 다들 늙은 로바츠 선생이 잘했다고 말했다. 그의 삶은 훌륭하고 넉넉했으며, 그의 유언은 공정했다. 마크도 여러 사람과 함께 그렇게 말했고, 제 나름의 작은 실망에도 불구하고 정말로 그렇게 믿었다. 그리고 유언장을 읽은 지 사흘째 아침에 크림클로티드 홀의 크라우디 지주는 슬픔을 완전히 털어 버리고, 다 잘된 일이라고 말했다. 이어 제인은 그와 함께 그의 집으로 가는 것으로 정해졌다. 제인에게 마음을 두었을지도 모른다고들 여기는 이웃 지주가 하나 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 아래인 루시는 프램리 목사관으로 데려가기로 했다. 그 편지를 받은 지 보름 만에 마크는 누이 루시를 날개 밑에 품고 자기 집에 이르렀다.\n\n이 모든 일이 사워비 씨의 어음이라는 몽마에 관한 마크의 현명한 결심을 크게 방해했다. 우선 그는 작정했던 만큼 빨리 바체스터에 갈 수 없었다. 그러다 어쩌면 동생 존에게서 그 돈을 빌리는 것이 좋을지도 모른다는 생각이 스쳤다. 물론 사정을 설명하고 응당한 이자를 물면서 말이다. 그러나 엑서터에서, 말하자면 아버지의 무덤 앞에 서 있는 동안에는 그 이야기를 꺼내고 싶지 않았고, 그래서 그 일은 뒤로 밀렸다. 어음의 기일이 오기까지는 처리할 시간이 아직 넉넉했고, 그는 그 처리를 어떻게 할지 마음을 정하기 전까지는 패니에게 말하지 않을 작정이었다. 빚을 청산할 방도도 함께 말해 줄 수 없는 채로 그 이야기를 꺼낸다면 아내는 죽고 말 것이라고, 그는 몇 번이고 스스로에게 되뇌었다.\n\n그러니 이제 나는 루시 로바츠에 대해 한마디 해야 한다. 이런 묘사 없이 그냥 넘어갈 수만 있다면 얼마나 즐거운 일이겠는가! 그러나 루시 로바츠는 이 작은 극에서 앞자리를 맡아야 하고, 그런 것에 마음을 쓰는 분들에게는 그녀의 모양과 됨됨이를 어느 만큼 알려 드리지 않을 수 없다. 지난번에 우리가 그녀를 언급했을 때, 그러니까 두드러진 자리는 아니었으나 오라비의 결혼식에 나타났을 때 그녀는 겨우 열여섯이었다. 그러나 이제 아버지가 세상을 떠난 시점에는 그 뒤로 두 해가 좀 넘게 지나 열아홉에 가까워져 있었다. 분명하게 하기 위해 ‘소녀’라는 저 애매한 말은 치워 두자—미리 시집을 가지 않는다면 소녀란 세 살부터 마흔세 살까지 소녀이니 말이다. 그 두루뭉술한 낱말을 버리고 말한다면, 오라비의 그 결혼식에서 그녀는 아이였고, 아버지가 세상을 떠난 지금 그녀는 여자였다.\n\n이런 임종의 자리만큼 여자다움을 보태 주는 것, 아이를 그토록 빨리 여자로 바꾸어 놓는 것은 아마 없을 것이다. 그때까지 루시가 여자의 소임이라 할 만한 일로 해 온 것은 별로 없었다. 돈거래에 관해서는 해마다 받는 25파운드의 용돈으로 자기 씀씀이를 다 감당해 보겠다는 장난스러운 시도밖에는 아는 것이 없었다. 그 시도를 장난이 되게 만든 것은 아버지의 사랑 넘치는 후함이었다. 살림은 그녀보다 세 살 위인 언니가 맡았는데—두 사람 사이에 존이 끼어 있었다—말하자면 차를 끓이고 가정부와 저녁 식탁에 대해 이야기하는 일이었다. 그러나 루시는 아버지의 팔꿈치 곁에 앉아 있었고, 저녁에 아버지가 잠들 때까지 책을 읽어 드렸고, 실내화를 가져다 드렸고, 안락의자가 편안한지 살폈다. 이 모든 것을 그녀는 아이로서 했다. 그러나 관 머리에 서고 관 옆에 무릎을 꿇었을 때, 그녀는 여자였다.\n\n그녀는 세 언니 누구보다도 키가 작았다. 언니들에게는 모두 훌륭한 여인이라는 칭찬이 돌아갔지만, 엑서터 사람들은 언니들을 돌아보고 도시에 두루 남아 있는 그들의 기억을 떠올리면서, 그 칭찬만은 루시에게까지 넓혀 줄 마음이 없었다. “아유, 아유!” 하는 말이 그녀를 두고 나왔다. “가엾은 루시는 도무지 로바츠 집안 사람 같지가 않아요. 그렇지 않아요, 폴 부인?” 딸들이 훌륭한 여인이 되었듯이 아들들도 늠름한 사내로 자랐기 때문이다. 그러면 폴 부인은 이렇게 대답했다. “털끝만큼도 안 그렇지요, 안 그래요? 블랜치가 저 아이 나이에 어땠는지 한번 생각해 보세요. 그래도 눈만은 고운 눈을 가졌어요. 그리고 그 집에서 제일 똑똑한 아이라는 말이 있기는 하지요.”\n\n그리고 그것은 그녀에 대한 참으로 정확한 묘사여서, 내가 여기에 무엇을 더 보탤 수 있을지 모르겠다. 그녀는 블랜치와 같지 않았다. 블랜치는 환한 얼굴빛과 고운 목덜미와 당당한 가슴을 가졌으니, 「에트 베라 인케수 파투이트 데아」—즉, 눈으로 보는 한에서는 진짜 여신이었다. 게다가 그녀는 애플파이에 대해 훌륭한 식견을 지녔고, 크림클로티드 홀에서 군림한 지 열여덟 달이 되기도 전에 돼지와 우유에 관한 온갖 비밀과 사과술과 어린 거위에 관한 비밀 대부분을 알아 버렸다.\n\n루시에게는 말할 만한 목덜미라는 것이 도무지 없었다—내 말은, 누구의 입에서 찬사를 끌어낸 적이 있는 목덜미가 없었다는 뜻이다. 게다가 살빛이 검었고, 마땅히 그러했어야 함이 분명한데도 식품 저장실에서 쓸모 있는 일에는 조금도 몸을 담그지 않았다. 목덜미와 살빛에 관해서라면, 가엾은 아이, 저로서는 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 그러나 그 다른 한 가지에 관해서라면 기회를 헛되이 썼다고 할 수밖에 없다.\n\n그러나 그 눈이라니! 폴 부인의 말이 옳았다. 그 눈은 사람을 향해 번쩍였다—언제나 부드럽게는 아니었다. 그녀와 낯선 사이라면 부드럽게 번쩍이는 일은 실로 드물었다. 그러나 부드럽든 거칠든, 바라보는 사람의 눈이 부실 만큼 빛났다. 그리고 그 눈이 무슨 색이었다고 누가 말할 수 있으랴? 아마 초록이었을 것이다. 대개의 눈은 초록이니까. 초록이 눈 색으로 어울리지 않는다고 여겨진다면 초록이나 회색이라고 해 두자. 그러나 그렇게 사람을 놀라게 한 것은 그 색이 아니라 그 안의 불이었다.\n\n루시 로바츠는 철저하게 갈색 머리 여인이었다. 어떤 때는 뺨의 짙은 빛깔이 말할 수 없이 그윽하고 사랑스러웠고, 눈시울의 속눈썹은 길고 부드러웠고, 좀처럼 보이지 않는 작은 이는 진주처럼 희었고, 머리카락은 짧았어도 아름답게 부드러웠다—결코 검지는 않고, 그러면서도 아주 짙은 갈색이었다. 블랜치도 고운 이로 이름이 났었다. 프랑스 어느 도시에 새로 들어선 집들처럼 희고 고르고 높직한 이였다. 그러나 그녀가 웃으면 온몸이 이뿐이었다. 피아노 앞에 앉으면 온몸이 목덜미뿐이었던 것처럼. 그런데 루시의 이라니!—어쩌다 한 번, 문득 놀라움이 터져 나와 입술을 벌린 채 잠깐 앉아 있을 때에만, 그 완벽한 상아 한 벌의 곱게 다듬어진 선과 우아한 진주빛 흰색을 볼 수 있었다. 폴 부인도 그녀의 이에 대해서 한마디 했을 것이다. 다만 폴 부인 앞에서는 그 이가 한 번도 드러난 적이 없었다.\n\n“그래도 그 집에서 제일 똑똑한 아이라는 말이 있기는 하지요.” 폴 부인은 아주 온당하게 그렇게 덧붙였다. 엑서터 사람들이 그런 의견을 내놓았고, 그렇게 한 것은 지극히 옳았다. 어떻게 그렇게 되는지는 모르겠으나, 작은 고을에서는 어느 집에서 누가 가장 머리가 밝은지를 누구나 다 아는 일이 언제나 벌어진다. 이 점에서 폴 부인은 여론을 그대로 옮긴 것일 뿐이었고, 그 여론은 옳았다. 루시 로바츠는 오라비들이나 언니들보다 예리한 지성을 타고났다.\n\n“사실을 말하면요, 마크, 저는 블랜치보다 루시가 더 마음에 들어요.” 로바츠 부인이 그 이름을 얻은 지 몇 시간도 되지 않아 한 말이었다. “미인이 아닌 건 저도 알아요. 그래도 그런걸요.”\n\n“내 사랑 패니!” 마크가 놀란 어조로 대답했다.\n\n“정말 그래요. 물론 남들은 그렇게 생각하지 않겠지요. 그런데 저는 반듯한 미인들에게는 도무지 마음이 가지 않는 것 같아요. 어쩌면 그들을 너무 부러워하기 때문인지도 모르지요.”\n\n마크가 그다음에 한 말은 되풀이할 필요가 없겠으나, 그 안에 젊은 신부를 향한 낯 두꺼운 아첨이 얼마간 들어 있었으리라는 것은 누구든 확실히 짐작해도 좋다. 다만 그는 이 일을 기억해 두었고, 그 뒤로 늘 루시를 아내가 아끼는 아이라고 불렀다. 그때 이후로 누이들 가운데 프램리에 와 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그리고 패니가 블랜치의 결혼식 때문에 엑서터에서 한 주를 보낸 적은 있으나, 그녀가 시누이들과 아주 가까운 사이였다고는 하기 어려웠다. 그럼에도 둘 중 하나가 프램리로 가는 것이 마땅해졌을 때, 아내가 했던 말의 기억이 마크로 하여금 곧바로 루시에게 그 제안을 하게 만들었다. 그리고 블랜치와 마음이 닮은 제인은 크림클로티드 홀로 가는 것을 기뻐했다. 저 기름진 토트니스 지방 아래쪽에 있는 헤비베드 하우스의 땅이 크림클로티드 홀의 땅과 맞닿아 있었고, 헤비베드 하우스에는 아직 안주인이 없었던 것이다.\n\n소식이 닿았을 때 패니는 몹시 기뻐했다. 지금의 사정으로 보면 시누이들 가운데 하나가 마크와 함께 사는 것이 물론 온당한 일이었고, 그 조용하고 자그마한 눈 밝은 아이가 와서 같은 지붕 아래 저와 함께 깃들게 된다는 생각에 그녀는 마음이 흐뭇했다. 아이들도 그 고모를 몹시 사랑할 것이다—다만 엄마를 사랑하는 만큼까지는 아니고. 그리고 현관 위로 내다보이는, 굴뚝이 도무지 연기를 내지 않는 그 아늑한 작은 방을 그녀를 위해 마련해 주어야지. 그리고 조랑말을 몰 차례도 나누어 주어야지—이것은 로바츠 부인 쪽에서는 큰 자기희생이었다. 그리고 레이디 러프턴의 가장 좋은 호의도 미리 청해 두어야지. 사실 루시는 자기에게 정해진 갈 곳에 관해서라면 불운하지 않았다.\n\n레이디 러프턴도 물론 선생의 죽음을 들어 알고 있었고, 마크에게 온갖 자애로운 말을 전하며 엑서터의 일이 다 정리되기 전에는 무슨 일이 있어도 서둘러 돌아오지 말라고 일러 주었다. 그러고 나서 교구에 새 사람이 오기로 되어 있다는 이야기를 들었다. 그 사람이 아래인 루시라는 말을 듣고 그분도 마음이 놓였다. 블랜치의 매력은 나무랄 데가 없었지만 아무래도 그분의 취향에는 맞지 않았기 때문이다. 두 번째 블랜치가 그곳에 온다면 젊은 러프턴 경에게 어떤 위험이 닥치지 않겠는가!\n\n“아주 잘했어요.” 마님이 말했다. “마땅히 그렇게 해야 하지요. 그 아가씨를 기억하는 것 같아요. 몸집이 좀 작지 않았나요? 그리고 아주 내성적이었지요?”\n\n“몸집이 좀 작고 아주 내성적이라니. 그게 무슨 묘사예요!” 러프턴 경이 말했다.\n\n“신경 쓰지 마라, 루도빅. 아가씨들 중에도 몸집이 작은 사람은 있어야 하고, 적어도 몇은 내성적이어야 하는 거야. 우리는 그 아가씨와 알게 되어 기쁠 거예요.”\n\n“저는 부인의 다른 시누이를 아주 잘 기억해요.” 러프턴 경이 말했다. “아름다운 여인이었지요.”\n\n“루시를 미인으로 보시지는 않을 거예요.” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“몸집이 작고, 내성적이고, 그리고—” 러프턴 경이 여기까지 갔을 때 로바츠 부인이 “못생겼다”는 말로 끝을 맺어 주었다. 그녀는 루시의 얼굴이 마음에 들었으나, 다른 사람들은 아마 그렇지 않을 것이라고 생각했던 것이다.\n\n“어머나, 정말이지.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그런 말을 하니 시누이를 둘 자격이 없어요. 나는 그 아가씨를 아주 잘 기억하는데, 못생기지 않았다고 말할 수 있어요. 나는 그대 결혼식에서 그 아가씨의 몸가짐이 무척 마음에 들었어요. 그리고 솔직히 말하면 그 미인보다 그 아가씨를 더 높이 보았지요.”\n\n“저는 그 아가씨가 전혀 기억나지 않는다고 고백해야겠군요.” 경이 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 대화는 끝났다.\n\n그리고 보름이 끝날 무렵 마크가 누이를 데리고 도착했다. 두 사람이 프램리에 이른 것은 어두워진 지 한참 뒤인 여섯 시와 일곱 시 사이 어름이었고, 이때는 벌써 12월이었다. 땅에는 눈이 있었고 공기에는 서릿발이 섰고 달은 없었으며, 조심성 있는 사람들은 길을 나설 때 말굽에 미끄럼막이 징을 박았다. 날씨가 그런 형편이었으므로 마크의 이륜마차는 실버브리지로 보내질 때 외투와 숄로 거의 가득 찼다. 그리고 루시의 짐을 실을 수레도 보냈고, 온갖 준비가 다 되어 있었다. 패니는 현관 위 작은 방의 불이 환하게 타고 있는지 몸소 세 번이나 가서 보았고, 바퀴 소리가 들려온 순간에는 고모라는 것이 어떤 존재인지를 아들의 머리에 열어 주는 일에 매달려 있었다. 그때까지 아들이 알고 있던 사람은 아빠와 엄마와 레이디 러프턴뿐이었다. 물론 아기방의 위성들은 빼고서.\n\n그러고서 삼 분 뒤에 루시는 난롯가에 서 있었다. 그 삼 분은 남편과 아내가 서로 껴안는 데 쓰였다. 누구를 손님으로 집에 데려왔든, 보름을 떠나 있었다면 그녀는 다른 누구를 맞이하기 전에 먼저 남편에게 입을 맞출 사람이었다. 그러나 곧 루시에게로 돌아서서 외투를 벗는 것을 돕기 시작했다.\n\n“아, 고마워요.” 루시가 말했다. “저는 춥지 않아요. 적어도 아주 춥지는 않아요. 수고하지 마세요. 제가 할 수 있어요.” 그러나 여기서 그녀는 거짓 호언을 한 것이었다. 손가락이 너무 얼어서 무엇을 채우지도 풀지도 못했으니 말이다.\n\n물론 모두 검은 옷을 입고 있었다. 그러나 루시의 옷이 지닌 침울함은 자기 옷의 침울함보다 훨씬 더 패니의 마음을 쳤다. 그 옷은 검은 빛으로 그 아이를 통째로 삼켜 버린 듯했고, 그 아이를 거의 죽음의 표상으로 만들어 놓은 듯했다. 루시는 눈을 들지 않고 얼굴을 난롯불 쪽으로 돌린 채, 자기가 놓인 자리를 거의 두려워하는 듯이 보였다.\n\n“저 아이가 뭐라고 하든, 패니.” 마크가 말했다. “저 아이는 몹시 추워. 나도 그렇고—꽤 춥다. 저 아이를 데리고 방으로 올라가는 게 좋겠어. 오늘 밤은 옷 갈아입는 일로 애쓰지 말자. 그렇지, 루시?”\n\n침실에서 루시는 조금 녹았고, 패니는 그녀에게 입을 맞추면서 “못생겼다”는 그 말에 관해서는 자기가 틀렸다고 속으로 말했다. 적어도 루시는 못생기지 않았다.\n\n“곧 우리에게 익숙해질 거예요.” 패니가 말했다. “그러고 나면 우리가 아가씨를 편안하게 해 줄 수 있으면 좋겠어요.” 그러고서 시누이의 손을 잡아 꼭 눌렀다.\n\n루시가 그녀를 올려다보았고, 그때 그 눈은 넉넉히 부드러웠다. “저는 여기서 틀림없이 행복할 거예요.” 그녀가 말했다. “이렇게 함께라면요. 하지만—하지만—아버지가!” 그러고서 두 사람은 서로의 품에 안겨 한바탕 입을 맞추고 울었다. “못생겼다니.” 마침내 손님의 머리를 매만져 주고 눈에서 눈물을 씻어 준 뒤에 패니는 속으로 말했다. “못생겼다니! 내 평생 본 얼굴 가운데 가장 사랑스러운 얼굴인데!”\n\n“당신 누이는 아주 아름다워요.” 그날 밤 잠자리에 들기 전에 둘이서만 그 아이에 대해 이야기하며 그녀가 마크에게 말했다.\n\n“아니야, 아름답지는 않아. 하지만 아주 착한 아이지. 그리고 제 나름으로는 꽤 영리하기도 하고.”\n\n“저는 그 아이가 완벽하게 사랑스러워요. 저는 평생 그런 눈은 본 적이 없어요.”\n\n“그럼 그 아이는 당신 손에 맡기지. 당신이 남편을 하나 구해 줘.”\n\n“그게 그리 쉽지는 않을 거예요. 그 아이는 아무하고도 결혼하지 않을 것 같아요.”\n\n“글쎄, 나도 그러기를 바라. 하지만 내가 보기에 그 아이는 딱 노처녀로 마름질된 사람 같아. 영원토록 당신 꼬맹이들의 루시 고모로 지낼 사람이지.”\n\n“그렇게 되기를 저도 진심으로 바라요. 하지만 그리 오래 그러지는 않을 것 같아요. 그 아이 마음에 들기가 어려우리라는 건 저도 의심하지 않아요. 그래도 제가 남자라면 그 아이에게 단번에 사랑에 빠졌을 거예요. 그 아이 이를 눈여겨본 적 있어요, 마크?”\n\n“본 적이 없는 것 같군.”\n\n“당신은 누가 머리에 이를 하나라도 달고 있는지 없는지도 모를 사람이에요, 정말이지.”\n\n“당신만 빼고 아무도 모르지. 그리고 당신 이는 하나하나 다 외고 있다고.”\n\n“당신은 거위예요.”\n\n“그것도 아주 졸린 거위지. 그러니 미안하지만 나는 홰에 올라가겠어.” 그리하여 그날은 루시의 아름다움에 대해 더 이야기되는 것이 없었다.\n\n처음 이틀 동안 로바츠 부인은 시누이와 그다지 가까워지지 못했다. 루시는 과연 감정을 드러내는 사람이 아니었다. 게다가 그녀는—참으로 아주 드문 사람들이지만—어떤 특별한 바깥 동그라미의 중심에 자기를 앉히지 않고도 그저 살아가는 것으로 만족하는 그 몇 안 되는 사람 가운데 하나였다. 보통 사람들의 마음으로는 그러지 않기가 불가능하다. 사람은 자기 저녁 식사가 자기에게 너무나 중요해서, 그것이 다른 모든 사람에게는 조금도 상관없는 일이라는 것을 도무지 믿을 수 없다. 젊은 시절에는 아기 옷을, 나이가 든 뒤에는 집안 리넨과 커튼 술 장식을 모아 놓은 것이 부인네 자신의 눈에는 너무나 흥미로워서, 다른 사람들도 그것을 보고 기뻐하지 않으리라고는 믿을 수 없는 것이다. 그렇다고 내가 이 성향을 나쁜 것으로 여긴다고 받아들여지지는 않기를 바란다. 그것은 사람들 사이에 어떻게든 이야기를 만들어 내고, 아마 일종의 공감도 만들어 낸다. 존스 부인은 화이트 부인의 리넨 장을 들여다볼 것이다. 화이트 부인도 자기 것을 들여다보게 되기를 바라면서. 항아리에서는 그 안에 든 것만 쏟아 낼 수 있다. 우리 대다수는 자기 이야기를, 적어도 자기가 그 중심인 저마다의 동그라미 이야기를 하지 않으면 아무 이야기도 할 수 없다. 그러니 세상의 대수롭지 않은 잡담을 눌러 없애고 싶어 하는 사람들에게 나는 동의할 수 없다. 나로서는 존스 부인의 리넨을 들여다보는 일이 언제나 즐겁고, 그 부인에게 내 저녁 식사의 세목을 늘어놓을 기회는 한 번도 빼놓지 않는다.\n\n그러나 루시 로바츠에게는 이 재주가 없었다. 그녀는 낯선 사람으로 올케의 집에 들어왔고, 처음에는 응접실의 한 구석과 거실 식탁의 한 자리를 얻은 것만으로 만족할 사람처럼 보였다. 위로의 말과 속을 터놓는 이야기가 주는 편안함을 필요로 하지 않는 듯했다. 그녀가 침울했다거나, 말을 걸어도 대답하지 않았다거나, 아이들을 아랑곳하지 않았다는 뜻은 아니다. 다만 그녀는 패니가 바랐을 만큼 자기 자신과 자기의 모든 희망과 슬픔을 곧바로 패니의 가슴에 던져 넣지는 않았다.\n\n로바츠 부인 자신은 우리가 이르는바 감정을 드러내는 사람이었다. 레이디 러프턴에게 화가 났을 때 그녀는 그것을 드러냈다. 그리고 그때 이후로 레이디 러프턴에 대한 사랑과 흠모가 커졌으니, 그것 또한 드러냈다. 남편에게 어떤 식으로든 마음이 언짢을 때에는 감추려 애쓰고 스스로 잘 감추었다고 여기면서도 감출 수가 없었다. 따뜻하고 한결같고 넘쳐흐르는 여자의 사랑을 감출 수 없는 것과 마찬가지였다. 남편의 팔에 매달려 방을 지나가면서, 자기는 이 방에서 이 사람이 가장 훌륭한 남자라고 여긴다는 것을 거기 있는 모든 사람에게 알리는 듯이 보이지 않을 수가 없었다. 그녀는 감정을 드러내는 사람이었고, 그러므로 루시가 자기 근심을 다 안고 활짝 열린 자기 가슴으로 단번에 달려들지 않는 것이 그만큼 더 실망스러웠다.\n\n“그 아이는 너무 조용해요.” 패니가 남편에게 말했다.\n\n“그게 저 아이 천성이야.” 마크가 말했다. “어릴 때도 늘 조용했지. 우리가 온갖 것을 깨부수고 다닐 때 저 아이는 찻잔 하나도 금 가게 하는 일이 없었어.”\n\n“지금이라도 뭘 좀 깨뜨려 주면 좋겠어요.” 패니가 말했다. “그러면 어쩌면 그 이야기라도 하게 될 텐데요.” 그러나 그렇다고 해서 그녀가 시누이를 사랑하는 일을 그만둔 것은 아니었다. 아마 자기 자신에게 주어진 그 재주들을 그 아이가 갖고 있지 않다는 바로 그 점 때문에, 그녀는 자기도 모르게 그 아이를 더 소중히 여겼을 것이다.\n\n그리고 이틀이 지난 뒤 레이디 러프턴이 찾아왔다. 물론 패니가 새 식구에게 레이디 러프턴에 대해 어지간히 많이 이야기해 두었으리라고 짐작해도 좋다. 시골에서 그런 이웃은 한 사람의 삶의 흐름 전체에 그토록 큰 영향을 미치므로, 그런 이야기를 삼가는 것은 애초에 있을 수 없는 일이다. 로바츠 부인은 거의 그 미망인 마님의 날개 밑에서 자랐으니, 물론 그분을 많이 이야기할 만한 가치가 있는 분으로 여겼다. 그렇다고 로바츠 부인을 귀족 뒤꽁무니나 쫓는 사람, 알랑쇠로 여기는 이는 없기를 바란다. 그 차이를 알아보지 못하는 사람이라면 인간 본성의 가장 기초적인 원리부터 아직 더 공부해야 한다.\n\n레이디 러프턴이 찾아왔고, 루시는 벙어리가 되었다. 패니는 마님의 첫인상이 호의적이기를 유난히 바랐고, 그렇게 되도록 하려고 그 방문 동안 두 사람을 한데 붙여 놓으려고 특히 애를 썼다. 그러나 이 점에서 그녀는 지혜롭지 못했다. 그렇더라도 레이디 러프턴에게는 루시의 침묵 때문에 터무니없는 오판으로 끌려가지 않을 만한 여자의 안목이 넉넉히 있었다.\n\n“그래, 어느 날에 우리 집에 와서 저녁을 먹겠어요?” 레이디 러프턴이 일부러 오랜 벗 패니 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n“아유, 날은 마님이 정해 주세요. 저희야 약속이 많은 적이 없다는 걸 아시잖아요.”\n\n“목요일이면 괜찮겠어요, 로바츠 양? 아는 사람은 아무도 만나지 않을 거예요. 내 아들뿐이니까요. 그러니 나들이라고 여길 필요가 없어요. 여기 패니가 말해 줄 거예요. 프램리 코트에 올라가는 것은 목사관에서 이 방에서 저 방으로 건너가는 것보다 더 나들이일 것이 없다고요. 그렇지 않아요, 패니?”\n\n패니는 웃으면서, 프램리 코트에 올라가는 일은 과연 너무 자주 하는 일이어서 어쩌면 자기들이 마땅히 여겨야 할 만큼 대단하게 여기지 않는지도 모르겠다고 말했다.\n\n“우리는 이곳에서 우리끼리를 일종의 행복한 한 식구로 여기고 있어요, 로바츠 양. 그러니 그 살림 안에 아가씨를 끼워 넣을 기회를 얻게 되어 기뻐요.”\n\n루시는 마님에게 자기의 가장 고운 미소 가운데 하나를 보냈으나, 그 순간 그녀가 한 말은 알아들을 수 없었다. 그렇더라도 당장은 저녁을 먹기 위해 프램리 코트까지 올라가는 일조차 스스로 감당할 수 없다는 것은 분명했다. “레이디 러프턴께서 참 자애로우세요.” 그녀가 패니에게 말했다. “그런데 너무 이르고, 그리고—그리고—그리고 저를 빼놓고 두 분만 가 주신다면 저는 참 좋겠어요.” 그러나 목적이 그녀와 함께 가는 것—바로 그녀를 거기에 데려가는 것—이었으므로, 만찬은 잠깐 동안 미루어졌다—「시네 디에」, 곧 기약 없이.\n\n# 제11장. 그리젤다 그랜틀리\n\n루시가 러프턴 경에게 처음으로 소개된 것은 그로부터 거의 한 달이 지난 뒤였고, 그것도 순전히 우연으로 이루어진 일이었다. 그동안 레이디 러프턴은 자주 목사관에 왔고, 어느 정도는 루시를 알게 되었다. 그러나 이 교구의 낯선 사람은 자기에게 온 수많은 초대 가운데 하나라도 받아들일 용기를 아직 내지 못했다. 로바츠 씨와 그의 아내는 프램리 코트에 자주 갔지만, 루시가 첫걸음을 떼는 그 두려운 날은 아직 오지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 교회에서 러프턴 경을 본 적이 있었으나 얼굴을 안다고 하기도 어려웠고, 그 밖에는 그를 전혀 보지 못했다. 그런데 어느 날—아니 차라리 어느 저녁, 벌써 어스름이 깔린 때였으니—그가 목사관 쪽으로 걸어가는 그녀와 로바츠 부인을 길에서 뒤따라 앞질렀다. 그는 총을 어깨에 걸치고 있었고, 포인터 세 마리가 발뒤꿈치를 따랐으며, 사냥터 관리인이 조금 뒤에서 따라오고 있었다.\n\n“안녕하세요, 로바츠 부인?” 그는 두 사람을 따라잡기도 전에 벌써 그렇게 말했다. “지난 반 마일 동안 길에서 두 분을 쫓아왔어요. 부인들이 이렇게 빨리 걷는 줄은 몰랐네요.”\n\n“우리가 신사분들처럼 어슬렁거리다가는 얼어 죽을걸요.” 그러고서 그녀는 멈춰 서서 그와 악수했다. 그 순간 그녀는 루시와 그가 아직 만난 적이 없다는 것을 잊고 있었고, 그래서 두 사람을 소개하지 않았다.\n\n“시누이를 저에게 소개해 주지 않으시겠어요?” 그가 모자를 벗고 루시에게 고개를 숙이며 말했다. “한 달 남짓 이웃으로 지냈는데도 아직 뵐 기쁨을 누리지 못했지요.”\n\n패니는 미안하다고 하고서 두 사람을 소개했고, 그러고서 그들은 프램리 문에 이를 때까지 걸었다. 러프턴 경은 두 사람에게 다 말을 걸었고 패니가 둘을 대신해 대답했다. 그리고 거기서 그들은 잠시 멈추어 섰다.\n\n“혼자 계신 걸 보니 놀랍네요.” 로바츠 부인이 바로 앞서 그렇게 말했었다. “컬페퍼 대위가 함께 있는 줄 알았어요.”\n\n“대위는 오늘 하루 저를 두고 갔어요. 귓속말로 하시겠다면 어디로 갔는지 알려 드리지요. 숲을 향해서도 큰 소리로는 말할 수가 없어서요.”\n\n“대체 어떤 무서운 곳으로 가 버렸다는 거예요? 그런 끔찍한 일로 귓속말은 하지 않겠어요.”\n\n“그가 간 곳은—간 곳은—하지만 어머니께 말하지 않겠다고 약속해 주시겠어요?”\n\n“어머님께 말하지 말라니! 이제 정말 호기심이 이는군요! 어디에 있을 수 있을까요?”\n\n“그럼 약속하시는 거지요?”\n\n“네, 약속하겠어요. 레이디 러프턴께서 컬페퍼 대위가 어디 있는지 저에게 물으실 일은 없을 테니까요. 우리는 말하지 않을 거예요. 그렇죠, 루시?”\n\n“하루 꿩 사냥을 하러 개서럼 성에 갔어요. 자, 명심하세요, 저희를 배신하시면 안 돼요. 마님께서는 그가 치통으로 방에 갇혀 있는 줄로 알고 계세요. 저희는 감히 그 이름을 어머니께 입에 올리지 못했지요.”\n\n그러고 나서 로바츠 부인에게는 레이디 러프턴을 뵈러 올라가야 할 약속이 있는 것으로 드러났고, 루시는 혼자 목사관까지 걸어갈 참이었다.\n\n“그리고 저는 부인의 남편께 가겠다고 약속했지요.” 러프턴 경이 말했다. “아니, 남편의 개 폰토에게라고 하는 편이 맞겠네요. 그리고 좋은 일 두 가지를 더 하겠어요—꿩 한 쌍을 들고 가고, 프램리 길의 악령들로부터 로바츠 양을 지켜 드리지요.” 그리하여 로바츠 부인은 문 안으로 들어섰고, 루시와 러프턴 경은 함께 걸어 나갔다.\n\n러프턴 경은 로바츠 양과 전에 말을 나눈 적이 한 번도 없었으나, 그녀가 결코 못생기지 않았다는 것은 이미 알아차리고 있었다. 교회에서 말고는 거의 본 적이 없었지만, 저 얼굴의 주인은 알아 둘 만한 사람이라고 그는 벌써 확신하고 있었고, 그래서 지금 그녀와 이야기할 기회가 생긴 것이 싫지 않았다. “그러니까 성 안에 이름 모를 처녀를 가둬 두고 계신 거군요.” 언젠가 그는 로바츠 부인에게 그렇게 말했다. “그녀를 더 오래 죄수로 두신다면 저는 무력으로 쳐들어가 구해 내는 것이 제 의무라고 여기겠어요.” 그는 그녀를 볼 셈으로 두 번 그곳에 갔으나, 두 번 다 루시가 용케 몸을 피했다. 이제 우리는 그녀가 제대로 붙잡혔다고 말할 수 있고, 러프턴 경은 사냥터 관리인에게서 꿩 한 쌍을 받아 어깨에 걸쳐 메고서 제 먹이를 들고 걸어 나갔다.\n\n“여기 오신 지 오래되었는데도,” 그가 말했다. “저희는 아직 뵐 기쁨을 누리지 못했지요.”\n\n“예, 그렇습니다, 러프턴 경.” 루시가 말했다. 여태껏 그녀가 알고 지낸 사람들 가운데 경이라 불리는 이는 흔하지 않았다.\n\n“저는 로바츠 부인께 이렇게 말하지요. 부인이 당신을 불법으로 가두어 두고 있으니 저희가 힘으로든 꾀로든 풀어 드리겠다고요.”\n\n“저는—저는—저는 얼마 전에 큰 슬픔을 겪었습니다.”\n\n“예, 로바츠 양. 알고 있어요. 그리고 저는 그저 농담을 하는 거예요, 아시겠지요. 하지만 이제는 저희와 어울리실 수 있게 되기를 정말 바라요. 어머니께서 그러시기를 몹시 바라고 계세요.”\n\n“마님께서 무척 친절하시다는 걸 잘 알고 있습니다. 그리고 당신께서도—러프턴 경.”\n\n“저는 제 아버지를 알지 못했어요.” 러프턴 경이 무겁게 말했다. “하지만 당신이 무엇을 잃었는지는 잘 헤아릴 수 있지요.” 그러고서 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그는 말을 이었다. “로바츠 선생을 잘 기억해요.”\n\n“정말이십니까?” 루시가 그를 향해 홱 돌아서며 말했고, 이제 그 목소리에는 어느 정도 생기가 실려 있었다. 프램리에 온 뒤로 아무도 아버지 이야기를 그녀에게 꺼내지 않았다. 그 화제가 금기이기라도 한 듯했다. 그런데 이런 일이 얼마나 흔한가! 우리가 사랑하는 이들이 죽으면 벗들은 그 이름을 입에 올리기를 두려워한다. 사별한 우리에게는 그 이름만큼 반가운 화제가 없는데도 그렇다. 그러나 우리는 제 슬픔도 남의 슬픔도 어떻게 다루어야 하는지 좀처럼 알지 못한다.\n\n어느 땅에 한 백성이 있었다—내가 아는 한 아직도 거기 있을지 모른다—그들은 타오르는 불의 진행을 막는 것이 신성을 모독하는 짓이라고 여겼다. 집이 불타고 있다면, 구할 방도가 있더라도 타야 한다는 것이었다. 감히 누가 신의 진행에 끼어들겠는가? 슬픔에 대한 우리의 생각도 그와 거의 같다. 우리는 그것을 꺼 버리는 일이 못된 짓이거나, 적어도 몰인정한 짓이라고 여긴다. 아내가 죽었으면 남자는 적어도 두 해는 긴 얼굴로 침울하게 지내야 하고, 아니면 열여덟 달은 그 길이를 온전히 채우고서 남은 여섯 달 동안 차츰 줄여 가야 한다. 그보다 짧은 시간에 제 슬픔을 삭일 수 있는—이를테면 제 불을 끌 수 있는—사람이라면, 적어도 그 능력을 남에게 보이지는 말아야 한다!\n\n“예, 그분을 기억해요.” 러프턴 경이 말을 이었다. “제가 소년일 때 두 번 프램리에 오셔서 마크와 제 일로 어머니와 의논하셨지요—이튼의 매질이 해로의 매질보다 더 효험이 있지 않겠느냐 하고요. 저에게 무척 친절하셨고, 제 앞날에 온갖 좋은 일을 점쳐 주셨어요.”\n\n“아버지는 누구에게나 친절하셨습니다.” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“그러셨을 것 같아요—친절하고 선하고 너그러운 분, 바로 제 식구들에게서 흠모받을 만한 분이었지요.”\n\n“바로 그렇습니다. 정말 그러셨어요. 저는 아버지에게서 모진 말을 한 마디라도 들은 기억이 없습니다. 그 목소리에는 거친 가락이 없었습니다. 그리고 너그럽기가 한없었습니다.” 루시는 앞서 말한 대로 좀처럼 감정을 드러내지 않는 사람이었으나, 지금 이 화제 앞에서, 그리고 이 온전한 낯선 사람 앞에서 거의 웅변가처럼 되었다.\n\n“아버님을 잃은 것을 그렇게 느끼시는 게 이상하지 않아요, 로바츠 양.”\n\n“네, 정말 그렇게 느낍니다. 마크는 세상에서 가장 좋은 오라비이고, 패니는 저에게 너무나 친절하고 너무나 좋은 사람입니다. 하지만 저는 늘 각별히 아버지의 벗이었습니다. 지난 한두 해는 그렇게 함께 지냈으니까요!”\n\n“돌아가실 때 연로하셨지요, 그렇지 않아요?”\n\n“막 일흔이었습니다, 러프턴 경.”\n\n“아, 그러면 연로하셨네요. 저희 어머니는 겨우 쉰인데도 저희는 이따금 노부인이라고 불러요. 그보다 더 늙어 보이시나요? 저희는 다들 어머니가 굳이 그럴 필요도 없는데 훨씬 더 늙은 사람처럼 스스로를 꾸며 놓는다고 말해요.”\n\n“레이디 러프턴께서는 젊게 차려입지 않으십니다.”\n\n“그렇지요. 제 기억으로는 한 번도 그러신 적이 없어요. 제가 처음 기억하는 무렵에는 늘 검정을 입으셨어요. 이제는 그건 그만두셨지만, 여전히 아주 침침하시지요, 그렇지 않아요?”\n\n“저는 부인들이 아주 젊게 차려입는 것을 좋아하지 않습니다. 그러니까, 그—그런 나이의 부인들이—”\n\n“쉰 살 부인들이라고 해 두지요?”\n\n“좋습니다. 그렇게 하시겠다면, 쉰 살 부인들이라고요.”\n\n“그러면 저희 어머니를 분명 마음에 들어 하시겠어요.”\n\n이제 그들은 목사관 쪽문을 지나 올라섰다. 큰 정문보다 길에서 더 가까운 지점에서 정원으로 들어가는 작은 문이었다.\n\n“마크는 저 위 집에 있겠지요?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그러실 겁니다, 러프턴 경.”\n\n“그럼 저는 이쪽으로 돌아갈게요. 제 볼일이 한편으로는 마구간에 있으니까요. 보시다시피 저는 여기서 아주 제집처럼 굴지요. 당신은 저를 전에 한 번도 본 적이 없지만요. 하지만 로바츠 양, 이제 얼음이 깨졌으니 우리가 벗이 될 수 있기를 바라요.” 그러고서 그는 손을 내밀었고, 그녀가 손을 내주자 오래된 벗이라도 되는 것처럼 거의 그렇게 꽉 잡았다.\n\n그리고 실제로 루시도 그가 오래된 벗이기라도 한 듯이 그에게 이야기했다. 일이 분 동안 그녀는 그가 경이며 낯선 사람이라는 것을 잊었고, 늘 그러던 대로 딱딱하게 경계하는 것도 잊었다. 러프턴 경은 정말로 그녀를 알고 싶어 하는 것처럼 말을 걸었고, 그녀는 자기도 모르게 그 호의에 마음이 끌렸다. 러프턴 경 자신은 사실 그 일을 그리 깊이 생각하지 않았다—다만 이 정도, 다른 젊은 사내들이 대개 그렇듯 그도 반짝이는 두 눈의 눈길을 좋아했다는 것뿐이었다. 그러나 이번에는 저녁이 너무 어두워서 그는 루시의 눈을 거의 보지도 못했다.\n\n“그래, 루시, 함께 온 분이 마음에 들었어요?” 저녁 식사 전에 세 사람이 응접실 난로 앞에 모여 앉았을 때 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“네, 그럭저럭요.” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“그건 그분께 조금도 칭찬이 못 되네요.”\n\n“칭찬할 뜻은 없었어요, 패니.”\n\n“루시는 칭찬을 하기에는 너무 사실만 따지지.” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“제 말은 판단할 기회가 별로 없었다는 뜻이에요. 러프턴 경과 십 분쯤밖에 같이 있지 않았으니까요.”\n\n“아! 하지만 여기에는 러프턴 경을 십 분 독차지하려고 두 눈이라도 내줄 아가씨들이 있어요. 그분이 얼마나 값비싼 분인지 루시는 몰라요. 그분은 삼십 초만 예고를 받으면 언제든 부인들의 마음에 들 수 있는 사람이라는 평판을 얻고 있지요.”\n\n“어쩌면 이번에는 삼십 초 예고도 못 받았겠지요.” 루시가 말했다—그녀는 위선자였다.\n\n“가엾은 루시,” 오라비가 말했다. “그는 폰토의 어깨를 보러 올라오던 길이었고, 안타깝지만 너보다 개를 더 생각하고 있었을 거야.”\n\n“그랬겠지요.” 루시가 말했다. 그러고서 그들은 저녁 식사를 하러 들어갔다.\n\n루시는 위선자였다. 옷을 갈아입으면서 러프턴 경이 무척 유쾌한 사람이었다고 스스로 인정했으니 말이다. 그러나 논의되는 화제가 젊은 신사의 사람됨일 때는 젊은 아가씨들에게 위선자가 되는 것이 허락되어 있다.\n\n그로부터 얼마 지나지 않아 루시는 정말로 프램리 코트에서 저녁 식사를 했다. 컬페퍼 대위는 개서럼 성에 관한 그 대죄에도 불구하고 아직 그곳에 머물고 있었고, 바체스터 근교에서 온 한 성직자도 부인과 딸과 함께 머물고 있었다. 이 사람이 바로 그랜틀리 부주교로, 우리가 앞서 언급한 신사이며, 주교 자신만큼이나 교구에 널리 알려진 사람이었다—그리고 많은 성직자에게는 저 이름 높은 고위 성직자보다도 더 자주 마음에 오르는 사람이었다.\n\n그랜틀리 양은 루시 로바츠보다 그리 많이 나이 들지 않은 젊은 아가씨였고, 그녀 또한 조용하며 여러 사람 앞에서 말을 많이 하는 편이 아니었다. 그녀는 분명히 미인이었으나, 그 아름다움에는 어딘가 조각상 같은 데가 있었다. 이마는 높고 희었으나, 살과 피를 좋아하는 이들의 취향을 만족시키기에는 아마 너무 대리석 같았다. 눈은 크고 그 모양이 나무랄 데 없었으나, 감정을 많이 드러내는 일이 드물었다. 사실 그녀 자신이 무표정했고, 제 마음을 좀처럼 내보이지 않았다. 코는 거의 그리스식이었다. 이마에서 딱 곧은 선으로 내려오는 것은 아니었으나, 고전적이라 할 만하다고 여길 만큼은 거의 그러했다. 입도 아주 훌륭했다—적어도 화가들과 아름다움의 감식가들은 그렇게 말했다. 그러나 내게는 늘 입술의 도톰함이 모자라 보였다. 그래도 뺨과 턱과 아래쪽 얼굴의 그 나무랄 데 없는 균형은 어떤 남자도 부정할 수 없었다. 머리털은 밝은 빛이었고 늘 적잖은 정성으로 손질되어 있어서 그 모습을 깎아내리지 않았으나, 여성의 아름다움에 그토록 풍요로움을 주는 그 그윽함이 없었다. 키가 크고 날씬했으며 움직임이 아주 우아했으나, 젊음의 편안함과 거침없음이 그녀에게 모자란다고 여기는 이들도 있었다. 그들은 그녀가 나이에 걸맞지 않게 너무 침착하고 딱딱하며, 제 몸매와 얼굴의 아름다움 말고는 사교에 내주는 것이 거의 없다고 말했다.\n\n그렇지만 그녀가 대개의 남자와 여자에게 바셋주의 미인으로 여겨졌고, 이웃 여러 주의 신사들이 그녀와 춤을 출 수 있으리라는 기대만으로 진창길을 여러 마일씩 달려왔다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 어떤 매력이 모자랐든, 적어도 그녀는 스스로 큰 명성을 만들어 냈다. 그녀는 지난봄에 두 달을 런던에서 지냈고, 거기서도 화제를 일으켰다. 그리고 레이디 하틀탑의 맏아들 덤벨로 경이 그녀에게 유별나게 마음을 빼앗겼다는 말이 사람들 사이에 돌았다.\n\n부주교가 딸을 자랑스러워한 것은 짐작할 만하고, 그랜틀리 부인도 그러했다—어쩌면 그토록 훌륭한 여인이 그런 속성을 두고 스스로에게 허락해도 좋을 정도보다 더 딸의 아름다움을 자랑스러워했다. 그리젤다—그것이 그녀의 이름이었다—는 이제 외딸이었다. 자매가 하나 있었으나 그 자매는 죽었다. 남자 형제도 둘 남아 있었는데, 하나는 교회에, 다른 하나는 군에 있었다. 그것이 부주교 집안의 전부였다. 그리고 부주교는 아주 부유한 사람이었으므로—그는 오래도록 바체스터 주교를 지낸 아버지의 외아들이었고, 그 시절에는 바체스터 주교를 지낼 값이 있었다—그랜틀리 양이 큰 재산을 갖게 될 것이라고 사람들은 짐작했다. 그러나 그랜틀리 부인은 딸을 세상에 자리 잡게 하는 일을 서두를 생각이 없다고 말한 적이 있다고 전해졌다—보통 아가씨들은 그저 결혼하지만, 정말로 중요한 아가씨들은 자리를 잡는다—그리고 이것은 오히려 그 상품의 값을 더해 주었다. 어머니들은 이따금 물건을 처분하려고 지나치게 애를 씀으로써 제 물건의 값을 떨어뜨린다.\n\n그러나 솔직히 그리고 단번에 진실을 말한다면—소설가가 그리 많은 칭찬을 받지 못하는 미덕이지만—그리젤다 그랜틀리는 어느 정도 이미 남에게 주어져 있었다. 그녀 자신, 그리젤다가 그것을 알고 있었다는 것도 아니고, 세 겹으로 복된 그 신사가 제 행운을 알게 되었다는 것도 아니다. 부주교조차 듣지 못했다. 그러나 그랜틀리 부인과 레이디 러프턴은 한 번 이상 단둘이 문을 닫고 마주 앉았고, 두 사람 사이에 조건들이 서명되고 봉인되었다. 왕과 외교관들이 맺는 조약처럼 양피지에 서명하고 밀랍으로 봉인한 것은 아니었다—그런 조약은 같은 사람들 손에 깨지기도 한다—그러나 자잘한 말로 서명하고 손을 몇 번 지그시 잡는 것으로 봉인했으니, 이런 두 계약 당사자 사이에서라면 충분히 구속력이 있는 조약이었다. 그리고 이 조약의 조건에 따라 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 레이디 러프턴이 될 것이었다.\n\n레이디 러프턴은 여태 혼사 투기에서 운이 좋았다. 그녀는 딸을 위해 조지 경을 골랐고, 조지 경은 너그러움을 다해 그 뜻에 따라 주었다. 그녀는 로바츠 씨를 위해 패니 몬셀을 골랐고, 패니 몬셀은 한순간도 그녀에게 반항하지 않았다. 그녀가 하는 일에는 성공의 위세가 감돌았고, 그래서 그녀는 사랑하는 아들 루도빅이 그리젤다에게 반하고 말 것이라고 거의 확신하고 있었다.\n\n그 아가씨 자신을 두고 말하면, 레이디 러프턴의 생각으로는 아들에게 그만한 혼처보다 나은 것이 그리 있을 수 없었다. 레이디 러프턴은 앞서 말했듯 훌륭한 교회의 여인이었고, 부주교는 그녀가 우러르는 그 교회 갈래의 바로 전형이었다. 그랜틀리 집안도 좋은 가문이었다—과연 귀족은 아니었지만, 그런 일에서 레이디 러프턴이 모든 것을 다 바란 것은 아니었다. 그녀는 바람을 알맞은 높이에 두어 그것이 이루어지는 것을 볼 만하게 기대할 수 있는 사람들 가운데 하나였다. 아들의 아내가 아름답기를 그녀는 바라 마지않았다. 이것은 아들을 위해 바란 것이니, 아들이 아내를 자랑스러워할 수 있도록, 또 사내들이 아름다움을 바라보기를 좋아하니 그러했다. 그러나 그녀는 발랄한 아름다움, 온 세상을 향해 미끼로 펼쳐 놓는 저 부드럽고 반짝이는 여성스러운 매력을 두려워했다. 부드러운 보조개, 웃는 눈, 물오른 입술, 뜻을 담은 미소, 거침없는 귓속말 같은 것을. 만일 아들이 그런 종류를, 재잘거리고 빠르게 지껄이며 분칠한 이브의 살점을 집으로 데려온다면? 우리 첫 어머니의 그런 자식이 스물네 명의 잇따른 영국 귀족의 피로 고귀해져서 오늘에 이르렀다 해도, 그녀 인생의 영광과 기쁨은 끝나 버리지 않겠는가?\n\n그리고 또한 그리젤다의 돈도 소용없지 않을 것이었다. 레이디 러프턴은 그 온갖 드높은 생각에도 불구하고 분별없는 여자가 아니었다. 아들이 헤프게 썼다는 것은 알았다. 아무렇게나 마구 썼다고는 믿지 않았지만 그랬다. 그리고 노주교의 금고에서 나온 향유가 아들의 이른 무분별이 집안 재산의 몸통에 입혔을 그 작은 상처들을 낫게 하는 데 쓰이리라는 생각에 그녀는 흡족했다. 그리하여 이런 식으로, 이런 이유들로, 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 온 세상에서 뽑혀 앞날의 레이디 러프턴이 되기로 정해진 것이었다.\n\n러프턴 경은 이미 한 번 이상 그리젤다를 만난 적이 있었다. 이 드높은 계약 당사자들이 아무런 조건에 이르기도 전에 그녀를 만났고, 분명히 그녀에게 감탄했다. 런던에서 어느 저녁 내내 덤벨로 경은 말로 할 수 없는 역겨움에 잠겨 입을 다물고 있었는데, 러프턴 경이 그녀에게 좀 유별나게 마음을 쏟았기 때문이었다. 그러나 덤벨로 경의 침묵은 그가 뜻을 드러내는 가장 웅변적인 방식이었다. 레이디 하틀탑도 그랜틀리 부인도 그를 보면 그가 무슨 뜻인지 아주 잘 알았다. 그러나 그 혼사는 그랜틀리 부인의 뜻에 꼭 맞지 않았을 것이다. 하틀탑 사람들은 그녀의 줄이 아니었다. 그들은 앞서 들은 대로 옴니엄 쪽 이해와 얽혀 아주 다른 패에 속해 있었다—“저 끔찍한 개서럼 사람들”이라고 레이디 러프턴은 그녀에게 손과 눈썹을 치켜올리고 고개를 저으며 말했다. 레이디 러프턴은 아마도 그들이 개서럼 성에서 한밤중에 흥청거리며 아기를 파이에 넣어 먹고, 과부들을 감방에 가둬 두었다가 이따금 공작의 손님들을 즐겁게 하려고 형틀에 올린다고 생각했을 것이다.\n\n로바츠 씨네 일행이 응접실에 들어섰을 때 그랜틀리 사람들은 이미 거기 있었고, 부주교의 목소리가 루시의 귀에 크고 위엄 있게 울렸다. 그녀가 아직 문지방에 서 있는데도 그가 말하는 소리가 들려왔던 것이다.\n\n“친애하는 레이디 러프턴, 나는 그 여자에 대해서라면 세상 무엇이든 믿을 수 있소—무엇이든지. 그 여자에게 너무 터무니없는 일이란 없소. 주교의 앞치마를 두르고 거기 가겠다고 우겼다 해도 나는 놀라지 않았을 것이오.” 그러고서 그들은 모두 부주교가 프라우디 부인 이야기를 하고 있음을 알았다. 프라우디 부인은 그의 골칫거리였으니까.\n\n레이디 러프턴은 손님들을 맞은 뒤 루시를 그리젤다 그랜틀리에게 소개했다. 그랜틀리 양은 우아하게 미소 짓고 살짝 고개를 숙인 뒤, 날이 몹시 춥다고 낼 수 있는 가장 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 낮은 목소리는 우리가 알듯 여자에게 훌륭한 것이다.\n\n루시는 자기가 무슨 말을 해야 한다고 생각해서, 날이 춥지만 걸을 때는 개의치 않는다고 말했다. 그러자 그리젤다는 앞서보다 다소 덜 우아하게 다시 미소 지었고, 그렇게 대화는 끝났다. 그랜틀리 양은 둘 중 나이가 위였고 세상을 가장 많이 보았으니 말을 가장 잘할 수 있어야 했겠지만, 어쩌면 그녀는 로바츠 양과 이야기를 나누고 싶은 마음이 별로 없었다.\n\n“그래, 로바츠, 콜디코츠에서 설교를 했다고 들었소.” 부주교가 여전히 좀 큰 소리로 말했다. “며칠 전에 사워비를 만났는데, 자네가 프라우디 부인 강연의 꼬리 토막을 들려주었다더군.”\n\n“꼬리 토막이라니 사워비도 심술궂었습니다.” 로바츠가 말했다. “우리는 그 일을 셋으로 나누었지요. 해럴드 스미스가 첫 몫을, 제가 마지막 몫을—”\n\n“그리고 그 부인이 가운데 토막을 맡았군. 자네들이 함께 이 주를 감전시켰지. 하지만 내가 듣기로는 그 부인이 가장 잘했다더군.”\n\n“로바츠 씨가 거기 간 것이 참 안타까웠어요.” 레이디 러프턴이 부주교의 팔에 기대어 식당으로 걸어 들어가며 말했다.\n\n“그로서는 어쩔 도리가 별로 없었으리라는 쪽으로 나는 기울고 있소.” 부주교가 말했다. 그는 자기 쪽 교회에서 아주 그리고 돌이킬 수 없이 떠나 버린 사람이 아니라면 형제 목사를 무겁게 짓누를 마음이 결코 없는 사람이었다.\n\n“어쩔 도리가 없었다고 보세요, 부주교님?”\n\n“그야, 그렇소. 사워비는 러프턴의 벗이니—”\n\n“딱히 그렇지는 않아요.” 가엾은 레이디 러프턴이 손사래를 치는 듯한 어조로 말했다.\n\n“그래도 두 사람은 가깝게 지냈소. 그러니 로바츠가 콜디코츠에서 설교해 달라는 청을 받았을 때 마다할 수는 없었지.”\n\n“하지만 그러고서 개서럼 성에 갔지요. 이제는 제가 그이에게 조금도 화가 나 있지 않다는 걸 아셔야 해요. 그렇지만 그건 아시다시피 그렇게 위험한 집이니까요.”\n\n“그렇소.—하지만 공작이 성직자를 거기 두고 싶어 했다는 바로 그 사실은 언제나 은총의 표시로 받아들여야 하오, 레이디 러프턴. 공기가 더러웠던 것은 의심할 것 없소. 그러나 로바츠가 거기 있었으니 그가 없을 때보다는 덜 더러웠지. 그런데, 맙소사! 더러운 공기라니 내가 무슨 망언을 하고 있었소? 아니, 주교가 거기 있었는데!”\n\n“네, 주교님이 거기 있었지요.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했고, 두 사람은 서로를 아주 잘 알아들었다.\n\n러프턴 경은 그랜틀리 부인을 이끌고 식당으로 갔고, 그랜틀리 양이 그의 다른 쪽에 앉도록 일이 짜여 있었다. 이것이 누구에게도 짜인 일로 보이지는 않았으나, 어쨌든 그녀는 거기 있었고, 루시는 오라비와 컬페퍼 대위 사이에 놓였다. 컬페퍼 대위는 엄청난 콧수염을 기르고 사냥감을 잡는 데 큰 재간이 있는 사내였다. 그러나 그 밖에 두드러진 성질이 없었으니, 그가 가엾은 루시에게 아주 유쾌한 사람이 되어 줄 것 같지는 않았다.\n\n그 산책의 날 뒤로 그녀는 러프턴 경을 딱 한 번, 이 분쯤 보았고, 그때 그는 오래된 벗에게 하듯 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. 목사관 응접실에서였고 패니도 거기 있었다. 이제 패니는 그분에게 아주 익숙해져서 그것을 대수롭게 여기지 않았으나, 루시에게는 아주 반가운 일이었다. 그는 앞으로 나서지도 않고 지나치게 허물없지도 않았으며, 친절하고 부드럽고 유쾌했다. 그리고 루시는 정말로 그가 마음에 들었다.\n\n그런데 이 저녁에 그는 여태 그녀에게 거의 말을 걸지 않았다. 그러나 그가 말을 걸어야 할 다른 사람들이 자리에 있다는 것은 그녀도 알았다. 그녀는 흔히 말하는 뜻으로 마음이 겸허한 사람은 딱히 아니었다. 그러나 자기 위치가 거기 있는 다른 사람들의 위치보다 덜 중요하고, 그러니 어느 정도는 자기가 지나쳐질 법하다는 사실은 알아보고 있었다. 그렇더라도 그랜틀리 양이 찾아 들어간 그 자리를 차지하고 싶은 마음이 덜하지는 않았다. 그녀는 러프턴 경과 시시덕거리고 싶은 것이 아니었다. 그렇게 바보는 아니었다. 그러나 컬페퍼 대위의 나이프와 포크 소리 대신 그의 목소리가 귀 가까이서 들리기를 바랐다.\n\n아버지가 죽은 뒤로 정성을 들여 옷을 차려입어 보려 한 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 그리고 이제 깊은 상복 차림으로 침침했지만 그녀는 정말 아주 보기 좋았다.\n\n“저 아이 이마에는 시로 가득 찬 표정이 있어요.” 패니가 남편에게 말했다.\n\n“패니, 저 애 머리를 돌려 놓아서 자기가 미인이라고 믿게 하지는 마.” 마크가 대답했다.\n\n“저 아이 머리는 그렇게 쉽게 돌려지지 않을 거예요, 마크. 루시에게는 당신이 생각하는 것보다 더 많은 것이 있고, 당신도 오래지 않아 알게 될 거예요.” 로바츠 부인은 이렇게 시누이를 두고 예언했다. 물어보았다면 그녀는 어쩌면 루시가 있는 것이 프램리 코트에서 그랜틀리 쪽 이해에 위험하리라고 말했을지도 모른다.\n\n러프턴 경이 그랜틀리 양에게 계속 말하는 동안 그의 목소리는 넉넉히 들렸다—목소리는 들렸으나 말은 들리지 않았다. 그는 귓속말을 하는 티가 조금도 없이 말했으면서도, 그가 말을 거는 사람만이, 오직 그 사람만이 그가 하는 말을 들을 수 있었다. 그러는 동안 그랜틀리 부인은 루시의 왼편에 앉은 루시의 오라비와 끊임없이 이야기했다. 그녀는 알맞은 종류의 시골 성직자에게 말할 화제가 모자란 적이 없었고, 그리하여 그리젤다는 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 남겨졌다.\n\n그러나 루시는 그리젤다 자신이 할 말이 아주 적은—아니 적어도 하는 말이 아주 적은—듯 보이는 것을 눈치채지 않을 수 없었다. 이따금 그녀가 입을 열면 한 마디, 혹은 두어 마디가 떨어지기는 했다. 그러나 대체로 그녀는 러프턴 경이 자기에게 마음을 쏟고 있다는 사실만으로 흡족한 듯했다. 그녀는 생기를 보이지 않았고, 늘 그렇듯 고요하고 우아하게, 침착하고 고전적으로 그저 앉아 있었다. 귀를 기울이지 않을 수도 눈길을 두지 않을 수도 없었던 루시는, 자기가 거기 있었다면 그 대화에서 더 앞장선 몫을 맡으려 애썼을 것이라고 생각했다. 그러나 그런 자리에서 어떻게 처신해야 하는지는 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 아마 루시보다 훨씬 잘 알았다. 어쩌면 러프턴 경 같은 젊은 사내들은 제 목소리 듣기를 좋아하는지도 몰랐다.\n\n“여기 사냥감이 엄청나게 많소.” 저녁 식사가 끝날 무렵 컬페퍼 대위가 그녀에게 말했다. 그가 시도한 두 번째였다. 앞서 그는 제9연대 사람들 가운데 아는 이가 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n“그런가요?” 루시가 말했다. “아! 며칠 전에 러프턴 경이 꿩을 한 아름 안고 있는 걸 보았어요.”\n\n“한 아름이라! 우리는 며칠 전 개서럼에서 일곱 수레를 채웠소.”\n\n“꿩 일곱 수레라니요!” 루시가 놀라 말했다.\n\n“그건 그리 많은 것도 아니오. 우리는 총이 여덟 자루였으니까. 사냥감을 잘 모아 놓으면 총 여덟 자루가 일을 아주 많이 해내지요. 개서럼에서는 그런 일을 다 훌륭하게 해내오. 공작 댁에 가 보았소?”\n\n루시는 개서럼 성에 관한 프램리 쪽 이야기를 들은 바 있어서, 몸을 떨듯이 그런 곳에는 한 번도 가 본 적이 없다고 말했다. 이 뒤로 컬페퍼 대위는 그녀를 더 이상 괴롭히지 않았다.\n\n부인들이 응접실로 물러난 뒤에도 루시의 처지는 식탁에 있을 때보다 나을 것이 거의 없었다. 레이디 러프턴과 그랜틀리 부인은 함께 소파에 자리를 잡고 거기서 서로 귀에 대고 다정하게 이야기했다. 마님은 루시와 그랜틀리 양을 소개해 놓았으니, 젊은 사람들끼리 잘 어울릴 만하다고 자연히 생각한 것이었다. 로바츠 부인은 셋이 함께하는 대화를 이끌어 보려 했고, 십 분쯤은 애를 썼다. 그러나 잘되지 않았다. 그랜틀리 양은 한 마디씩만 했고, 그러면서도 그 한 마디마다 미소를 지었다. 그리고 루시는 그 순간 입에 올릴 만한 이야기가 아무것도 떠오르지 않았다. 그녀는 거기 고요히 꼼짝없이 앉아, 책을 들 용기도 없이, 목사관 집에 있었더라면 얼마나 더 행복했을까 마음속으로 생각했다. 자기는 사교에 맞게 태어나지 않았다. 그것만은 분명하다고 느꼈다. 그리고 다음에는 마크와 패니만 프램리 코트에 오게 하리라고 생각했다.\n\n그러고서 신사들이 들어왔고, 방에는 또 한 번 움직임이 일었다. 레이디 러프턴은 일어나 부산히 돌아다녔다. 난롯불을 쑤시고 촛대를 옮기고, 그랜틀리 박사에게 몇 마디 하고, 아들에게 무언가 귓속말을 하고, 루시의 뺨을 두드리고, 음악을 아는 패니에게 음악을 조금 하자고 말했다. 그리고 끝으로 그리젤다의 두 어깨에 손을 얹고 옷이 몸에 꼭 맞는다고 말해 주었다. 레이디 러프턴은 루시가 말했듯 자기는 늙게 차려입었지만, 주위 사람들이 말끔하고 예쁘고 멋들어지고 우아한 것을 보기를 좋아했으니까.\n\n“친애하는 레이디 러프턴!” 그리젤다가 손을 들어 마님의 손끝을 지그시 누르며 말했다. 그것은 그녀가 보인 첫 생기였고, 루시 로바츠는 그것을 다 지켜보았다.\n\n그러고서 음악이 있었다. 루시는 연주도 노래도 하지 않았다. 패니는 둘 다 했고, 아마추어로서는 둘 다 잘했다. 그리젤다는 노래는 하지 않았으나 연주는 했다. 그리고 그 연주는 제 수고도 아버지의 돈도 배움에 아끼지 않았음을 보여 주는 것이었다. 러프턴 경도 조금 노래했고, 컬페퍼 대위는 아주 조금 했다. 그래서 그들끼리 연주회가 차려졌다. 그동안 박사와 마크는 난로 앞 깔개에 서서 함께 이야기했고, 두 어머니는 흡족하게 앉아 제 자식들이 서로 다정하게 속살거리며 어르는 모습을 지켜보았다—그리고 루시는 혼자 앉아 그림책의 낱장을 넘겼다. 그녀는 그때 거기서, 자기가 이런 일에 성품으로 도무지 맞지 않는다고 완전히 마음을 정했다. 그녀는 아무도 아끼지 않았고, 아무도 그녀를 아끼지 않았다. 그래, 이제는 이것을 끝까지 견뎌야 한다. 그러나 다음에는 더 잘 알 것이다. 제 책과 난롯가만 있으면 그녀는 지금처럼 스스로 비참하다고 느낀 적이 없었다.\n\n그녀는 음악에 등을 돌렸다. 러프턴 경이 그랜틀리 양의 손가락이 예술적으로 움직이는 것을 지켜보는 모습이 지겨웠기 때문이다. 그리고 긴 방이 허락하는 한 피아노에서 멀리 떨어진 작은 탁자에 앉아 있었는데, 바로 등 뒤에서 들린 목소리에 자책의 생각에서 갑자기 깨어났다. “로바츠 양,” 그 목소리가 말했다. “왜 저희를 다 저버리셨어요?” 그리고 루시는 자기에게는 그 말이 똑똑히 들렸으나 다른 누구에게도 들리지 않았음을 느꼈다. 러프턴 경은 앞서 그랜틀리 양에게 말할 때처럼 이제 그녀에게 말하고 있었다.\n\n“저는 연주를 하지 않습니다, 러프턴 경.” 루시가 말했다. “노래도 하지 않습니다.”\n\n“그렇다면 함께해 주시는 것이 저희에게 훨씬 더 값진 일이 되었겠네요. 저희는 들어 줄 사람이 몹시 모자라니까요. 어쩌면 음악을 좋아하지 않으시나요?”\n\n“좋아합니다—어떤 때는 아주 많이요.”\n\n“그 어떤 때는 언제인가요? 하지만 그건 차차 다 알아내겠지요. 당신의 신비를 다 풀고 수수께끼를 다 읽어 낼 거예요. 언제라고 할까요?—겨울이 끝날 무렵쯤. 그렇지 않겠어요?”\n\n“저에게 무슨 신비가 있는지 모르겠습니다.”\n\n“아, 있어요! 우리 모두에게 등을 돌리고 와서 여기 앉아 있는 것부터가 아주 신비로운데요—”\n\n“아, 러프턴 경. 제가 잘못했다면—!” 그러면서 가엾은 루시는 의자에서 거의 벌떡 일어날 듯했고, 짙은 뺨에 깊은 홍조가 번졌다.\n\n“아니에요—아니에요. 잘못하신 게 없어요. 저는 그저 농담을 한 거예요. 잘못한 것은 저희지요, 당신을 혼자 두었으니까요—저희 가운데 가장 낯선 손님인 당신을요.”\n\n“저는 아주 잘 있었습니다, 고맙습니다. 혼자 남겨지는 것은 개의치 않습니다. 늘 그렇게 지내는 데 익숙했습니다.”\n\n“아! 하지만 저희가 그 버릇을 고쳐 드려야겠어요. 스스로 은자가 되게 놓아두지 않을 거예요. 그렇지만 사실은요, 로바츠 양, 아직 저희를 모르시니까 저희 사이에서 그리 행복하지 않으신 거예요.”\n\n“아! 저는 행복합니다. 다들 저에게 아주 좋게 대해 주십니다.”\n\n“저희가 좋게 대하도록 놓아두셔야 해요. 어쨌든 저만은 그러도록 놓아두셔야 해요. 아시지요, 마크와 저는 일곱 살 때부터 소중한 벗이었어요. 그의 아내는 거의 그만큼 오래 제 누이의 가장 소중한 벗이었지요. 그리고 이제 당신이 그들과 함께 있으니 당신도 소중한 벗이 되어야 해요. 이 제안을 마다하지는 않으시겠지요?”\n\n“아, 그러지 않겠습니다.” 그녀는 아주 귓속말로 말했다. 그리고 실제로 귓속말 이상으로 목소리를 높이기가 어려웠다. 속을 다 드러내는 눈에서 눈물이 떨어질까 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n“그랜틀리 박사 부부는 이틀쯤 뒤에 떠나실 거고, 그러면 저희가 당신을 이리로 불러들여야겠어요. 그랜틀리 양은 성탄절까지 머물 예정이니, 두 분이 마음을 나누는 벗이 되어야 해요.”\n\n루시는 미소 지으며 기뻐하는 표정을 지어 보이려 했으나, 자기와 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 결코 마음을 나누는 벗이 될 수 없다고—둘 사이에 통하는 것이 결코 있을 수 없다고 느꼈다. 그녀는 자기가 작고 가무잡잡하고 못생기고 대수롭지 않은 사람이니 그리젤다가 자기를 업신여긴다고 확신했다. 자기는 거꾸로 그리젤다를 업신여길 수 없었다. 실은 그랜틀리 양의 대단한 아름다움과 몸가짐의 품위에 감탄하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 자기가 그녀를 사랑할 수는 결코 없다는 것을 알았다. 마음이 도도한 사람이 자기를 업신여기는 이들을 사랑하는 일은 좀처럼 있을 수 없다. 그리고 루시 로바츠는 마음이 몹시 도도했다.\n\n“저분이 아주 아름답다고 생각하지 않으세요?” 러프턴 경이 말했다.\n\n“아, 아주 그렇습니다.” 루시가 말했다. “누구도 의심할 수 없습니다.”\n\n“루도빅,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다—아들이 루시의 의자 뒤에 그렇게 오래 머물러 있는 것이 그리 마땅치 않았던 것이다—“노래 한 곡 더 들려주지 않으련? 로바츠 부인과 그랜틀리 양이 아직 피아노 앞에 있구나.”\n\n“제가 아는 건 다 불러 버렸어요, 어머니. 컬페퍼는 아직 기회가 없었네요. 그가 자기 꿈을 들려주어야 해요—어떻게 ‘대리석 궁전에 사는 꿈을 꾸었네’ 하는 그것을요.”\n\n“그건 한 시간 전에 불렀소.” 대위가 그리 반갑지 않은 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n“하지만 어떻게 ‘그대의 어린 연인들이 찾아왔네’ 하는 건 분명 아직 안 들려주셨지요.”\n\n그러나 대위는 더는 노래하려 하지 않았다. 그러고서 모임은 흩어졌고, 로바츠 씨네 사람들은 목사관으로 돌아갔다.\n\n# 제12장. 자그마한 어음\n\n루시는 프램리 코트 응접실에 머문 그 마지막 십오 분 동안, 자기가 그런 사교계에 맞지 않는다고 앞서 품었던 그 아주 굳은 생각을 다소 고쳐 잡았다. 러프턴 경이 안락의자 등 뒤에 서서 그녀에게 다정하고 부드럽고 마음씨 좋은 말을 건네는 동안, 그 의자에 그렇게 앉아 있는 것은 아주 즐거웠다. 조금만 지나면 그에게 참된 우정을 느낄 수 있을 것이고, 게다가 그를 사랑하게 될 위험 없이 그렇게 할 수 있을 것이라고 그녀는 확신했다. 그러나 그러면서도 그런 우정이란 온갖 종류의 뒷말에 열려 있을 것이고 세상의 여느 방식과는 좀처럼 어울리지 않으리라는 생각이 어렴풋이 그녀에게 비쳤다. 어쨌든 그가 와서 이따금 자기를 알아봐 준다면 프램리 코트에 있는 것은 즐거울 것이었다. 그러나 그의 시간이 온통 그리젤다 그랜틀리에게 바쳐진다면 그런 방문은 견딜 수 없으리라는 것은 스스로 인정하지 않았다. 인정하지도 않았고 생각하지도 않았다. 그러나 그럼에도 이상하게, 저도 모르는 방식으로 그런 감정이 그녀의 가슴에 자리를 얻은 것은 사실이었다.\n\n그리고 성탄 휴일이 지나갔다. 이 즐거움 가운데 얼마만큼이 그녀의 몫으로 떨어졌고 이 고통 가운데 얼마만큼을 그녀가 견뎌 냈는지는 정확하게 그려 보려 하지 않겠다. 그랜틀리 양은 십이야까지 프램리 코트에 머물렀고, 로바츠 집안 사람들도 그 시절의 대부분을 그 집에서 보냈다. 레이디 러프턴은 의심할 여지 없이 이번 기회에 모든 것이 자기 소망대로 정돈되기를 바랐으나, 그렇게 되지는 않았다. 러프턴 경은 분명히 그랜틀리 양을 매우 찬탄했다. 실은 어머니에게 그렇게 여섯 번은 말했다. 그러나 그리젤다 그랜틀리에게는 루시 로바츠의 불기운이 좀 모자란다고 그가 한번 내놓은 의견 때문에, 레이디 러프턴이 그 찬탄에서 얻은 기쁨이 도리어 상쇄되고도 남지 않았는지 의심스러울 지경이었다.\n\n“루도빅, 설마 그 두 아가씨를 견주려는 것은 아니겠지,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“물론 아니에요. 그 두 사람은 서로 정반대에 놓여 있는걸요. 아마 그랜틀리 양이 제 취향에는 더 맞겠지요. 하지만 그것이 제 취향이 나쁜 취향이기 때문이라는 것쯤은 저도 알 만큼은 아는 사람입니다.”\n\n“그런 일에 너보다 더 정확하고 세련된 취향을 가진 남자는 나도 알지 못한다,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. 이 이상으로 그녀는 감히 나아가지 못했다. 자기에게 계략이 있다는 것을 아들이 한번 알아차리기만 하면 그 계략이 헛일이 되어 버릴 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다. 사실을 말하자면 레이디 러프턴은 루시 로바츠에게 어느 정도 무심해지고 있었다. 그녀는 그 어린 아가씨에게 아주 친절했다. 그런데 그 어린 아가씨는 그 친절을 마땅히 여겨야 할 만큼 여기는 것 같지 않았고 — 게다가 러프턴 경이 루시에게 말을 걸려 하는 것이었으니, “그건 그렇게까지 할 필요가 없는 일이잖아요” 하는 마음이었다. 그리고 루시는 그 짧고 발작적이고 보기 흉한 “러프턴 경” 소리를 완전히 떨어 버린 채 러프턴 경과 아주 거침없이 이야기하는 버릇이 들었다.\n\n그리하여 성탄의 잔치는 끝이 났고, 일월도 저물어 갔다. 이 달의 대부분 동안 러프턴 경은 프램리에 머물지 않았으나, 그럼에도 주 안에 있으면서 두 지구의 사냥개들과 함께 사냥을 하고 여러 집에 머물렀다. 두세 밤은 콜디코츠에서 보냈다. 그리고 한 밤은 — 이것은 목소리를 낮추어서만 말하기로 하자 — 개서럼 성에서 보냈다! 이 일에 대해 그는 레이디 러프턴에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 마크에게 말한 대로, “어머니를 왜 불행하게 만들겠나?” 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 그것을 알고 있었다. 그에게 한마디도 하지 않았지만 — 알고 있었고, 불행했다. “그이가 그리젤다와 결혼만 해 준다면 저 위험도 끝이 날 텐데,” 그녀는 혼자 그렇게 말했다.\n\n그러나 이제 우리는 잠시 그 교구 사제와 그의 자그마한 어음으로 돌아가야 한다. 기억하시겠지만, 아버지의 유언장을 읽은 뒤 그 곤경에 관하여 그가 처음 떠올린 생각은 아우 존에게서 그 돈을 빌리는 것이었다. 존은 그때 엑서터에 내려와 있었고, 런던으로 가는 길에 하룻밤을 목사관에서 지내기로 되어 있었다. 마크는 그 길에서 아우에게 이 문제를 꺼내 볼 작정이었다. 자기보다 그렇게 어리고, 게다가 성직자요 버젓한 교구 사제인 자기를 나이 차이가 요구하는 것보다 더 큰 공경으로 언제나 우러러보아 온 아우에게 자기 자신의 어리석음을 이야기하는 것이 그에게는 괴로운 일이었지만 말이다.\n\n그러나 이야기는 하고야 말았다. 다만 마크가 프램리에 이르기 전에 알아낸 대로, 온전히 헛되게 한 이야기였다. 아우 존은 당장에 돈을 빌려주겠다고 선언했다. 물론 빌려주겠다고 — 형이 원한다면 800파운드를. 자기, 곧 존은 남은 200파운드에 관해서는 곧바로 손에 넣는 기쁨을 맛보고 싶다고 털어놓았다. 이자에 관해서라면 받지 않겠다고 — 형에게서 이자를 받는다니! 그럴 수 없었다. 좋다, 마크가 그 일로 그렇게 야단을 떤다면 받아야 하겠지만, 그래도 받지 않는 편이 좋겠다고 했다. 마크가 하고 싶은 대로 해야 하고, 그저 마음에 드는 대로 하라고 했다.\n\n이것은 다 아주 좋았고, 마크는 아우를 오래 자기 돈에서 떼어 놓지는 않겠다고 마음을 굳게 정했다. 그러나 그다음에 문제가 생겼다. 그 돈에 어떻게 손이 닿는가? 그, 마크는 아버지의 유언장에 따른 유언 집행자였고, 아니면 집행자 가운데 하나였으니, 의심할 것 없이 그 돈에 손을 얹을 수는 있었다. 그러나 아우는 성년이 되기까지 다섯 달이 남아 있었고, 그러므로 아직은 법적으로 그 유산의 소유자가 될 수 없었다.\n\n“그건 난처하군요,” 프티백 경의 개인 비서보가 말했다. 아마 아우의 궁핍만큼이나 현금이 지금 당장 있으면 좋겠다는 자기 자신의 바람을 생각하면서 한 말이었을 것이다. 마크도 그것이 난처한 일이라고 느꼈으나, 그 방면으로는 더 할 일이 없었다. 이제 그는 은행이 어디까지 자기를 도울 수 있는지 알아봐야 했다.\n\n프램리로 돌아온 뒤 한두 주가 지나 그는 바체스터로 건너가, 그곳 은행 가운데 하나의 지배인이며 자기와 안면이 있는 포레스트 씨라는 사람을 찾아갔다. 그리고 비밀을 지켜 달라고 여러 번 당부하면서 이 지배인에게 자기 이야기를 다 털어놓았다. 처음에는 벗 사워비의 이름을 감추었으나, 그런 감추기가 아무 소용이 없다는 것이 곧 드러났다. “그거야 사워비지요, 뻔한 일입니다,” 포레스트 씨가 말했다. “선생이 그 사람과 가깝게 지내는 건 나도 압니다. 그리고 그의 벗은 누구나 이르든 늦든 그 일을 겪지요.”\n\n포레스트 씨는 그 거래 전체를 아주 가볍게 여기는 것처럼 마크에게는 보였다.\n\n“만기가 되어도 저로서는 그 어음을 도저히 갚을 수 없습니다,” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“아, 아니지요, 그야 그렇지요,” 포레스트 씨가 말했다. “400파운드를 한꺼번에 내놓는 일이야 언제라도 그리 만만치 않습니다. 그걸 갚으라고 기대할 사람은 아무도 없어요!”\n\n“하지만 이르든 늦든 제가 갚아야 하는 것이겠지요?”\n\n“글쎄요, 그건 그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있지요. 얼마쯤은 선생이 사워비와 어떻게 처리하는가에 달렸고, 얼마쯤은 그 어음이 누구 손에 들어가는가에 달렸습니다. 어음에 선생 이름이 적혀 있으니, 이자가 지불되고 갱신 수수료가 지불되는 동안에는 그쪽에서 참고 기다릴 겁니다. 하지만 언젠가는 누군가가 그것을 결제해야 하는 것이 틀림없지요.”\n\n포레스트 씨는 그 어음이 바체스터에 있지 않을 것이 분명하다고 말했다. 사워비 씨가 그것을 바체스터의 은행으로 들고 오지는 않았을 것이라고 그는 생각했다. 어음은 아마 런던에 있을 것이나, 추심을 위해 바체스터로 보내질 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. “그것이 내 손에 들어오면,” 포레스트 씨가 말했다. “시간을 넉넉히 드리지요. 그래서 사워비와 갱신 일을 처리하실 수 있게요. 그 비용은 그가 물겠지요.”\n\n은행을 나설 때 마크의 마음은 다소 가벼워졌다. 포레스트 씨가 그 거래 전체를 하도 가볍게 여겼으므로 자기도 그것을 가볍게 여겨도 된다는 생각이 들었다. “아마 이렇게 하는 게 좋겠지,” 마차를 몰아 집으로 돌아가면서 그는 혼자 생각했다. “석 달이 다 돌아가기까지는 패니에게 아무 말도 하지 않는 것이. 그때는 무슨 방도를 마련해야겠지.” 그리하여 그 석 달 가운데 마지막 달 동안 그의 마음은 앞의 두 달 동안보다 편했다. 기한이 지난 어음, 다가오는 만기, 초과 인출된 계좌, 값을 치르지 못한 상인들, 돈에 관한 온갖 걱정 — 그런 느낌은 처음에는 몹시 끔찍하다. 그러나 사람이 그것에 익숙해지는 속도는 놀랍기만 하다. 처음에는 사람을 짓눌러 부수었을 짐이 습관이 되고 나면 견딜 만한 것이 되고, 나아가 짊어진 사람에게 편하고 안락한 것까지 된다. 상습적인 채무자는 경쾌하게, 탄력 있는 걸음으로 걸어 다니며 제 곤경이 주는 흥분을 거의 즐긴다. 사워비 씨가 바로 그러했다. 그의 이마에 구름이 낀 것을 누가 본 적이 있는가? 그와 함께 있으면 파산과 사랑에 빠질 지경이었다. 그리고 지금 벌써 마크 로바츠도 이 어음에 관하여 아주 편안하게 생각하고 있었다. 은행가들이 이런 일을 얼마나 유쾌하게 다루는가! 갚아라? 아니, 그런 것을 하라고 기대할 만큼 사리에 어긋난 사람은 아무도 없다! 게다가 사워비 씨는 분명히 유쾌한 친구였고, 사람이 낸 돈에 대해 무엇인가는 돌려주었다. 러프턴 경이 사워비에게 너무 모지게 굴지 않았느냐 하는 것이 마크에게는 여전히 물음거리였다. 그 신사가 마침 이 무렵에 그 성직자 벗과 마주쳤더라면, 그로부터 또 400파운드의 인수를 얻어 낼 수도 있었을 것이다.\n\n그런 곤경이 주는 흥분에는 술이 주는 흥분과 마찬가지로 무엇인가 즐거운 것이 있다고 거의 믿고 싶어진다. 그러나 그러다가 마침내 흥분이 다 지나가고 오직 비참만 남는 때가 온다. 이 땅에 참담함의 삶이라는 것이 있다면 그것은 빚과 융통 어음과 인수의 경주를 달려온 늙고 지쳐 빠진 난봉꾼의 삶일 것이다 — 요즘 우리가 넓고 시원한 영어를 다소 두려워하지 않는다면 거짓말이요 사기요 허위요 협잡이라 불렀을 그것의 경주를 달려온 사람 말이다. 그리고 사랑해야 마땅했던 모든 이를 망쳐 놓고, 자기를 크게 믿어 준 사람은 누구나 태워 버리고 조금이라도 믿어 준 사람은 누구나 그슬려 놓은 끝에, 마침내 그런 자들이 얻는 빵과 물로 남은 목숨을 마치도록 버려진 사람 — 가라앉는 가슴을 붙들어 줄 정직한 생각 하나 없이, 떨리는 손을 잡아 줄 정직한 벗 하나 없이! 사람이 첫 자그마한 어음에 자기 이름을 적을 때, 갱신이야 얼마든지 쉽게 할 수 있다고 그렇게 마음씨 좋게 다짐받으면서 그 이름을 적을 때, 그것만이라도 생각할 수 있다면!\n\n석 달이 거의 다 되었을 무렵, 마침 로바츠가 벗 사워비를 만나게 되었다. 마크는 한두 번 러프턴 경과 함께 사냥개들이 모이는 곳까지 말을 타고 갔고, 어떤 경우에는 아마 들판 한두 개쯤 더 나아갔을 것이다. 어떤 목사들이 그러듯이 그가 사냥에 손을 댔다고 독자는 생각하지 마시라. 그리고 참으로 이상한 일이지만, 목사들이 그럴 때면 언제나 그 놀이에 유별난 재간을 보인다. 마치 사냥이 시골에서 영혼을 돌보는 일과 각별히 어울리는 소일거리라도 되는 듯이. 그런 생각은 우리 교구 사제에게 부당한 것이겠다. 그러나 러프턴 경이 길을 따라 말을 타고 가서 사냥개들을 구경하는 것이 대체 무슨 해가 되느냐고 물을 때, 그는 그 경에게 어떤 분별 있는 대답을 해야 할지 좀처럼 알지 못했다. 집에서 성직의 일에 시간을 쓰는 편이 더 낫겠다고 말하는 것은 우스운 일이었을 터이다. 그는 자기 시간의 절반을 쓸 만큼의 성직 업무조차 가지고 있지 않다는 것이 널리 알려진 일이었으니 말이다. 그리하여 이런 식으로 그는 사냥개들을 구경하고 주 안의 교분을 이어 가는 버릇이 들었고, 덤벨로 경, 그린 워커 씨, 해럴드 스미스, 그리고 그런 부류의 다른 죄인들과 만나게 되었다. 그리고 석 달이 거의 다 되어 가던 그런 자리 가운데 하나에서 그는 사워비 씨를 만난 것이다.\n\n“이봐요, 사워비. 잠깐만 이야기 좀 합시다. 그 어음은 어떻게 하고 있어요?”\n\n“어음 — 어음! 무슨 어음? 어느 어음? 어음의 전부, 오직 어음만을. 요즘은 아침이고 낮이고 밤이고 모든 사람의 이야기가 그것뿐인 것 같군요.”\n\n“내가 당신을 위해 400파운드로 서명해 준 그 어음을 모른다는 겁니까?”\n\n“정말 그랬어요? 그건 좀 풋내 나는 짓이 아니었나요?”\n\n이것은 정말이지 마크에게 이상하게 보였다. 사워비 씨에게 날아다니는 어음이 하도 많아서 개서럼 성 침실에서 있었던 그 일을 아주 잊어버렸다는 것이 사실일 수 있는가? 게다가 자기가 은혜를 베풀어 준 바로 그 사람에게서 풋내기라는 소리를 듣다니!\n\n“아마 그랬겠지요,” 마크가 말했다. 좀 부아가 났음을 드러내는 목소리였다. “하지만 어쨌든 그것이 어떻게 결제될지는 알면 좋겠군요.”\n\n“아, 마크, 이런 식으로 내 하루 사냥을 망쳐 놓다니 당신은 정말 무뢰한이군요. 목사가 아닌 사람이라면 누구라도 그렇게 지독하게 잔인할 만큼 훌륭한 기독교인은 못 될 겁니다. 하지만 어디 봅시다 — 400파운드? 아, 그래요 — 토저가 가지고 있어요.”\n\n“그러면 토저는 그것을 어떻게 할 겁니까?”\n\n“그걸로 돈을 만들지요. 어떤 식으로 일을 벌이든 그것만은 할 겁니다.”\n\n“하지만 토저가 20일에 그것을 내게 들고 오겠지요?”\n\n“아이고, 천만에요! 정말이지 마크, 당신은 어여쁘게 풋내기군요. 고양이가 쥐를 발톱에 넣자마자 곧바로 죽여 버릴 생각을 할 리가 없지요. 하지만 농담은 접어 두고, 당신은 애를 태울 필요가 없어요. 어쩌면 그 일에 대해 더는 아무 말도 못 듣게 될지 모르고, 아니면 아마도, 그쪽이 훨씬 더 그럴 법하기는 하지만, 갱신하시라고 내가 그것을 당신에게 보내야 할지도 모르지요. 하지만 나에게서든 다른 누구에게서든 소식을 듣기까지는 당신은 아무것도 할 필요가 없습니다.”\n\n“다만 아무도 그 돈 때문에 나를 덮치게 하지는 마세요.”\n\n“그럴 염려는 조금도 없어요. 탤리호, 이 사람아! 놈이 달아났군. 탤리호! 바로 저기 고셋네 헛간 쪽으로. 갑시다, 토저는 잊어버리고 — ‘그날의 괴로움은 그날에 족하니라.’” 그리하여 두 사람은 함께 달려 나갔다. 목사와 국회의원이.\n\n그리하여 이번에도 마크는 그 어음이 별일 아니라는 듯한 느낌을 품고 집으로 돌아갔다. 토저가 어떻게든 처리해 줄 것이다. 그리고 지금 당장 아내에게 그것을 이야기해서는 안 될 것이 아주 분명했다.\n\n그러나 그 이월 21일에 그는 그 어음과 그에 관한 모든 일이 한갓 익살극이 아니었음을 일러 주는 것을 받았다. 그것은 사워비 씨에게서 온 편지였다. 콜디코츠에서 날짜가 적혀 있었으나 바체스터의 소인은 찍혀 있지 않았고, 그 안에서 그 신사는 갱신을 제안하고 있었다 — 꼭 낡은 어음의 갱신이 아니라 새 어음의 갱신을. 그 편지는 런던에서 부쳐진 것 같다고 마크에게는 보였다. 그것을 온전히 옮겨 놓으면 아마 그 뜻이 가장 빨리 설명되겠다 : —\n\n콜디코츠, 185─년 2월 20일.\n\n내 사랑하는 마크에게, — “네 이름을 돈 장사꾼에게 빌려주지 말라, 그것은 멸망이요 올무니라.” 이 말이 잠언에 없다면 마땅히 있어야 할 겁니다. 토저가 이 추운 날씨에도 자기가 살아서 기운차다는 표시를 몇 가지 내게 보내 왔어요. 우리 둘 다 지금 당장은 그 400파운드 어음을 결제할 수 없으니, 그것을 갱신하고 그에게 수수료와 이자를 물어야 합니다. 그 밖에 그의 온갖 부수입과 곁수입과 도둑질까지 다 얹어서요 — 그 모든 일에서 토저가 마땅히 지켜야 할 만큼 제 손을 깨끗이 지키지 않는다는 것은 내가 보증할 수 있어요.\n\n이것과 그 밖의 자잘하게 밀린 것들을 덮으려고 새 어음은 500파운드로 채워 넣었고, 만기는 오는 5월 23일로 했습니다. 그때가 오기 전에 당신의 궁색한 벗에게 어떤 사고가 하나 일어나 있을 것이라고 나는 믿어요. 그러고 보니, 당신이 우리를 떠난 이튿날 아침에 그 여인이 그레셤네와 함께 개서럼 성을 떠나 버린 이야기를 아직 안 했군요. 짐수레 밧줄로도 그이를 붙들지 못했을 겁니다. 공작이 그 밧줄을 붙들었는데도요. 그리고 공작은 정말로 붙들었지요, 그 공작다운 손의 온 힘을 다해서. 그이가 그 집안의 어떤 의사를 만나러 가겠다고 하니 나는 그때는 물러날 수밖에 없었어요. 하지만 그 일은 좋은 궤도에 올라 있다고 생각합니다.\n\n인수 서명을 한 어음을 되돌려 보내는 데 우편 한 편도 놓치지 마세요. 토저가 당신을 괴롭힐 수 있으니까요 — 아니, 모레까지 그 서류가 우리 둘의 서명을 제대로 받아 그의 손에 들어가 있지 않으면 틀림없이 괴롭힐 겁니다. 그는 은혜를 모르는 짐승이에요. 여덟 해 동안 나를 뜯어먹고 살았는데도 지금 내 목숨을 살리려고 한 번의 짜내기조차 면해 주려 하지 않을 사람입니다. 하지만 나는 변호사 편지의 번거로움과 비용에서 당신을 지켜 주고 싶은 마음이 각별해요. 그리고 늦어지면 그 일이 신문에까지 오를 수도 있습니다.\n\n세인트 제임스 듀크 스트리트 7번지의 나에게로 봉해 보내세요. 그때면 나는 런던에 있을 겁니다.\n\n그럼 잘 있어요, 이 사람아. 요전 날 코볼즈 애시스에서 우리가 한 그 몰이는 제법이었지요. 그 갈색 말을 당신에게서 얻을 수 있으면 좋겠군요. 130까지는 갈 마음이 있습니다.\n\n언제나 당신의 벗,\n\n마크가 그것을 다 읽고 나서, 낡은 어음이 편지에서 떨어져 나왔는지 보려고 책상을 내려다보았다. 그러나 아니었다. 동봉된 것은 없었고, 새 어음 말고는 애초에 동봉된 것이 없었다. 그러고 나서 그는 편지를 다시 처음부터 읽어 보았고, 낡은 어음에 관해서는 한마디도 없다는 것을 알아냈다 — 적어도 그것이 어디 있는지에 관해서는 한 음절도 없었다. 사워비는 그것이 자기 손에 남아 있으리라는 말조차 하지 않았다.\n\n마크는 사실 그런 일에 관해 아는 것이 많지 않았다. 이 두 번째 서류에 그가 서명한다는 바로 그 사실이 저 첫 번째 서류를 무효로 만들어 버리는 것일 수도 있었다. 그리고 사워비가 그 문제에 대해 잠자코 있는 것을 보면, 그것이 워낙 뻔한 일로 알려져 있어서 그가 설명할 생각조차 하지 않았다고 볼 수도 있었다. 그러나 그러면서도 마크는 어째서 그렇게 되는지 알 수가 없었다.\n\n그러나 그가 어떻게 해야 하는가? 비용과 변호사에 대한, 특히 신문에 대한 그 위협은 정말로 그에게 효험을 발휘했다 — 의심할 것 없이 그렇게 하려고 한 것이었다. 그리고 400파운드가 아니라 500파운드로 자기에게 어음을 발행한 사워비의 뻔뻔함에 그는 아주 어안이 벙벙해졌다. 사워비가 그렇게 마음씨 좋게 말한 대로 “자잘하게 밀린 여러 가지”를 “덮기” 위해서라니.\n\n그러나 마침내 그는 그 어음에 서명을 하고, 사워비가 지시한 대로 그것을 부쳐 보냈다. 그 밖에 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n어리석은 사람. 사람은 앞서 그릇된 일을 했더라도 언제나 옳은 일을 할 수 있다. 그러나 그 앞서의 그릇된 일이 그 길에 그토록 많은 어려움을 더한다 — 무서운 비율로 늘어나는 어려움을. 그리하여 마침내 사람은 몸부림 속에서 숨이 막히고 물 아래로 가라앉아 죽는다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 사워비의 편지를 조심스럽게 치워 두었다. 아내의 눈에 닿지 않도록 자물쇠를 채워서. 그것은 어떤 교구 성직자도 받아서는 안 될 편지였다. 그만큼은 그도 스스로 인정했다. 그러나 그럼에도 그것을 보관해 두는 것은 필요한 일이었다. 그리하여 이번에도 몇 시간 동안 이 일은 그를 몹시 비참하게 만들었다.\n\n# 제13장. 조심스러운 암시\n\n레이디 러프턴은 아들이 레스터셔의 사냥을 접고 겨울을 지내러 프램리로 온 그 착한 처사에 크게 기뻐하고 있었다. 그것은 온당하고, 어울리고, 더할 나위 없이 마음 편한 일이었다. 영국의 귀인이라면 자기가 말을 달리는 그 들판을 스스로 소유한 주에서 사냥을 해야 하고, 자기 소작인들로부터 마땅히 자기에게 돌아올 존경과 예우를 받아야 하고, 자기 것인 지붕 아래에서 잠을 자야 하며, 또한 — 레이디 러프턴의 생각으로는 — 자기 어머니가 골라 놓은 어린 신붓감과 사랑에 빠져야 하는 것이었다.\n\n그리고 그가 집에 있다는 것은 참으로 즐거운 일이었다. 레이디 러프턴은 세상에서 흔히 무료하다고 부르는 그런 삶을 스스로에게 허락하는 여자가 아니었다. 그녀에게는 해야 할 일이 너무 많았고 그 일들을 너무 무겁게 여겼으므로, 지루함과 권태에 시달릴 틈이 없었다. 그럼에도 그가 있을 때면 집은 그녀에게 더 흥겨웠다. 그녀 혼자서는 결코 이 집으로 끌어들이지 못했을, 그러나 그가 있어서 생겨난 것이라면 그녀도 정말로 즐기게 되는 약간의 흥겨움 — 그런 것에 이유가 생기는 것이었다. 그가 있을 때 그녀는 더 젊고 더 밝았으며, 지난 일보다 앞일을 더 많이 생각했다. 그녀는 그를 바라볼 수 있었고, 그것만으로도 그녀에게는 행복이었다. 게다가 그는 그녀에게 다정하게 굴었다. 그녀의 조그만 구시대적 편견을 두고 농을 걸어도, 그의 입에서 나오면 그 어조가 그녀의 귀에는 음악이었다. 그가 그녀에게 미소를 지어 보일 때면, 그가 아직 온전히 그녀만의 것이던 시절, 그녀의 의자 옆 작은 침대에 누워 있던 시절에 그토록 애틋하게 사랑했던 그 미소들이 떠올랐다. 그는 그녀에게 친절하고 너그러웠으며, 적어도 그녀 앞에 있는 동안에는 착한 아들답게 행동했다. 여기에, 눈앞에 없을 때에는 그의 행실이 그만큼 온전하지 못할지도 모른다는 그녀의 두려움까지 보태면, 레이디 러프턴이 그를 프램리 코트에 두고 있어서 기뻐한 것을 우리도 넉넉히 짐작할 수 있다.\n\n그 5천 파운드에 관하여 그녀는 그에게 거의 한마디도 하지 않았다. 여러 밤 베개에 누워 생각에 잠기면서 그녀는, 그 돈이 그를 그의 집으로 돌아오게 했으니 그보다 더 잘 쓰인 돈은 여태 없었다고 혼자 되뇌었다. 그는 자기다운 시원한 태도로 그 일을 고마워했고, 다음 해 안에 갚겠다고 언명했으며, 재산이 팔리지 않은 것을 기뻐함으로써 그녀의 마음을 위로해 주었다.\n\n“그 땅에서 한 에이커라도 떼어 낸다는 건 생각만 해도 싫어요,” 그가 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“그럼 그렇지, 루도빅. 네 손에서 영지가 줄어들게 하는 일은 절대로 없어야 한다. 영국의 귀인들과 영국의 신사들이 자기 나라를 지켜 낼 수 있는 것은 오직 그런 결심으로써다. 나는 땅이 남의 손으로 넘어가는 것을 차마 보지 못하겠구나.”\n\n“글쎄요, 백만장자들이 자기 돈을 어떻게 해야 할지 알 수 있도록 이따금 땅이 시장에 나오는 것도 좋은 일이 아닐까요.”\n\n“하느님이 막아 주시기를, 네 땅이 거기 나가는 일만은!” 그러고서 이 미망인은 아들의 땅이 백만장자들과 그 밖의 블레셋 사람들로부터 지켜지기를 마음속으로 짧게 기도했다.\n\n“하기야 그렇지요. 유대인 재봉사가 벌어들인 돈을 러프턴에 투자하는 것을 보고 싶지는 않으니까요,” 나리가 말했다.\n\n“하늘이 막아 주시기를!” 이 미망인이 말했다.\n\n앞에서도 말했듯이, 이 모든 것이 참 좋았다. 나리의 이야기하는 태도로 보아 아직 치명적인 손상은 없었다는 것이 마님에게는 분명했다. 그의 마음에는 걱정이 없었고 재산에 관해서도 거침없이 말했다. 그러나 그럼에도 이 복된 시절에조차 레이디 러프턴의 하늘의 빛을 다소 흐리게 하는 구름이 있었다. 루도빅은 왜 그리젤다 그랜틀리의 일에 그렇게 더딘가? 겨울이 저물어 가는 이 며칠 동안 그는 왜 그렇게 자주 목사관으로 어슬렁어슬렁 내려가는가? 그리고 개서럼 성의 그 끔찍한 방문!\n\n개서럼 성에서 실제로 무슨 일이 있었는지 그녀는 끝내 알지 못했다. 그러나 우리는 더 뻔뻔하고 캐묻는 데에 덜 조심스러우니, 우리는 말할 수 있다. 그는 서바셋주 사냥회와 함께 대단히 형편없는 하루를 보냈다. 이 주에는 도무지 여우가 모자라니, 사정을 아는 누군가가 그 점을 손보지 않고서는 아무 성과도 볼 수 없을 것이다. 그런 다음 그는 공작과 함께 다소 심심한 만찬을 했다. 사워비가 거기 있었고, 저녁에 그와 사워비는 당구를 쳤다. 사워비가 한두 파운드를 땄고, 그것이 입은 손해의 전부였다.\n\n그러나 목사관으로 어슬렁거려 내려가는 그 걸음은 더 위험할 수 있었다. 아들이 루시 로바츠와 사랑에 빠질 수도 있다는 생각이 레이디 러프턴의 머리에 떠오른 적은 결코 없었다. 루시의 됨됨이가 지닌 매력은 그런 두려움에 근거를 줄 만한 것이 아니었다. 그러나 그가 재잘거리는 말로 그 아이의 머리를 돌려놓을 수는 있었다. 그 아이가 어떤 어리석은 일이라도 상상할 만큼 어리석을 수도 있었다. 게다가 사람들이 입을 놀릴 것이었다. 루시가 그곳에 오기 전에 여태 그랬던 것보다 지금 더 자주 목사관에 갈 까닭이 그에게 무엇인가?\n\n그리고 마님은 바로 그 걱정 때문에, 목사관 사람들을 초대하는 일을 어떻게 다뤄야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그때까지 그 초대는 대단히 빈번했고, 아무리 잦아도 지나칠 것이 없다고 그녀는 늘 생각해 왔다. 그러나 이제는 그 관례를 이어 가기가 거의 두려웠다. 목사와 그 아내를 부르면서 루시를 빼놓을 수는 없었다. 그리고 루시가 오면 아들은 저녁의 대부분을 그 아이와 이야기하거나 그 아이와 체스를 두며 보내는 것이었다. 이것이 레이디 러프턴의 마음을 적지 않게 어지럽혔다.\n\n게다가 루시는 그 모든 것을 너무나 태연하게 받아들였다. 프램리에 처음 왔을 때 그 아이는 어찌나 수줍고, 어찌나 말이 없고, 프램리 코트의 위세에 어찌나 압도되어 있었던지, 레이디 러프턴은 그 아이를 딱하게 여겨 기운을 돋우어 주었다. 익숙하지 못한 루시의 눈이 부시지 않도록 그녀는 자기 화려함의 불빛을 누그러뜨리려 애쓰기까지 했다. 그러나 이제 그 모든 것이 달라졌다. 루시는 젊은 나리의 목소리를 몇 시간이고 내리 들을 수 있었다 — 조금도 눈이 부시지 않은 채로.\n\n이런 사정 아래에서 그녀에게 두 가지 방도가 떠올랐다. 아들에게 말하든지 패니 로바츠에게 말하든지 하여, 약간의 수완으로 이 폐단을 고쳐 놓겠다는 것이었다. 그러고서 그녀는 그 둘 가운데 어느 쪽을 택할지 정해야 했다.\n\n“루도빅만큼 말이 통하는 아이는 없지.” 적어도 그녀는 몇 번이고 스스로에게 그렇게 말했다. 그러나 루도빅은 그런 일에 대해서는 아무것도 몰랐다. 게다가 그에게는 아버지에게서 물려받은 버릇이 하나 있었으니, 남이 간섭한다는 낌새만 채면 재갈을 이로 물어 버리는 버릇이었다. 입을 마구 당기지 않고 부드럽게 몰아가면 어디로든 데려갈 수 있고 거의 어떤 걸음으로든 갈 수 있다. 그러나 아무리 가벼운 것이라도 한 번 날카롭게 채기만 하면 그는 뒷다리로 주저앉아 버릴 것이고, 그러면 그날 그를 한 마일이라도 더 나아가게 할 수 있을지가 문제가 될 것이었다. 그러니 이러저러 따져 보아 레이디 러프턴은 다른 쪽 방도가 제일 낫겠다고 생각했다. 레이디 러프턴이 옳았다는 데에 나는 의심을 두지 않는다.\n\n어느 날 오후 그녀는 패니를 자기만의 방으로 올라오게 하여 안락의자에 조심스레 앉히고, 손님에게 보닛을 벗게 하고, 이 방문이 대단히 중대한 것으로 여겨진다는 것을 여러 가지 표시로 드러냈다.\n\n“패니,” 그녀가 말했다. “중요하고 꼭 말해 두어야 할 일이 있어서 이야기를 하려는데, 그런데 그게 말을 꺼내기에는 아주 조심스러운 일이에요.” 패니는 눈을 크게 뜨고, 아무 일도 잘못된 게 없기를 바란다고 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 얘야, 잘못된 건 없다고 생각해요. 그러기를 바라고, 확실히 그렇다고 말해도 된다고 생각해요. 그렇지만 조심해 두는 것은 언제나 좋은 일이지요.”\n\n“예, 그렇습니다,” 패니가 말했다. 그녀는 무언가 달갑지 않은 것이 오고 있다는 것을, 아마도 마님과 뜻을 달리해야 할 무엇인가가 오고 있다는 것을 알았다. 그러나 로바츠 부인 자신의 두려움은 온통 남편 쪽으로 달려가고 있었다. 그리고 사실 레이디 러프턴도 그 문제에 관하여 한두 마디 할 말이 있었으나, 바로 지금은 아니었다. 사냥을 다니는 목사는 그녀의 취향에 도무지 맞지 않았다. 그러나 그 문제는 며칠쯤 미뤄 두어도 될 일이었다.\n\n“자, 패니, 우리가 다들 패니의 시누이 루시를 아주 좋아했다는 건 알고 있겠지요.” 그러자 로바츠 부인의 마음은 즉시 열렸고, 나머지 말이 다 나온 것이나 다름없이 그녀는 그것을 알아차렸다. “그 말은 새삼 할 필요도 없겠지요. 우리가 그것을 보여 주었으니까요.”\n\n“정말 그러셨습니다. 언제나 그러시듯이요.”\n\n“그리고 내가 무슨 불평을 하려는 거라고 생각하면 안 돼요,” 레이디 러프턴이 이어 말했다.\n\n“불평하실 일이 없기를 바랍니다,” 패니가 말했다. 대드는 어조와는 조금도 거리가 멀었고, 오히려 겸허하게, 마님의 노여움을 미리 달래는 어조였다. 패니는 레이디 러프턴을 상대로 한 번 뚜렷한 승리를 거둔 바 있었으므로, 그 너그러움에 못지않은 신중함으로, 자기가 고분고분해도 될 만하다고 느꼈다. 어쩌면 다시 한번 이겨야 하겠다고 애태울 날이 머지않을지도 몰랐다.\n\n“아니, 그럼요. 없다고 생각해요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “불평할 일은 없어요. 그렇지만 우리 둘이 조금 이야기를 나눠 두면, 그러지 않았을 때 골칫거리가 될 수도 있는 일들을 바로잡을 수 있을지도 모르지요.”\n\n“루시 이야기입니까?”\n\n“그래요, 얘야 — 루시 이야기예요. 참 착하고 좋은 아이이고, 아버지에게 자랑이 될 아이지요 —”\n\n“그리고 저희에게는 큰 위안입니다,” 패니가 말했다.\n\n“그렇겠지요. 아주 즐거운 벗일 테고, 아이들 일에도 그렇게 도움이 되겠지요. 그런데 —” 그러고서 레이디 러프턴은 잠시 말을 멈추었다. 늘 그렇듯 능변이고 조심스러운 그녀였으나, 자기가 뜻하는 바를 정확히 나타낼 말이 얼른 떠오르지 않았던 것이다.\n\n“그 아이가 없으면 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠습니다,” 패니가 말했다. 난처해하는 마님을 도와줄 셈으로 한 말이었다.\n\n“그런데 사실은 이렇답니다. 그 아이와 러프턴 경이 너무 자주 함께 있는 버릇이 들고 있어요 — 서로만 붙들고 이야기하는 버릇이요. 패니도 분명 눈치를 챘을 거예요. 내가 무슨 나쁜 일을 의심한다는 게 아니에요. 나는 천성이 의심 많은 사람은 아니라고 생각해요.”\n\n“아! 그러실 리가요,” 패니가 말했다.\n\n“그렇지만 두 사람은 각기 상대에 대해서도, 자기 자신에 대해서도 잘못된 생각을 품게 될 거예요. 루시는 어쩌면 루도빅이 실제보다 더 깊은 뜻을 두고 있다고 여기게 되겠지요. 그리고 루도빅은 —” 그러나 루도빅이 무엇을 할지, 무엇을 생각할지를 말하기는 그렇게 쉽지 않았다. 그래도 레이디 러프턴은 말을 이었다. “패니는 그 훌륭한 분별과 재치로 내 말을 알아들을 거예요. 루시는 똑똑하고, 재미있고, 그런 아이지요. 그리고 루도빅은 젊은 남자들이 다 그렇듯이, 자기가 보이는 관심이 자기가 뜻한 것보다 더 깊은 것으로 받아들여질 수 있다는 걸 모르는지도 몰라요 —”\n\n“루시가 러프턴 경을 사랑하고 있다고 여기시는 것은 아니시겠지요?”\n\n“아이고, 아니에요 — 그런 게 아니에요. 일이 그렇게까지 되었다고 생각한다면 나는 그 아이를 아주 내보내라고 권했을 거예요. 그 아이가 그만큼 어리석지는 않다고 믿어요.”\n\n“저는 거기에 아무것도 없다고 생각합니다, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“없다고 나도 생각해요, 얘야. 그러니 이 일에 관해서 러프턴 경에게 무슨 말을 꺼내는 짓은 무슨 일이 있어도 하지 않을 거예요. 내가 루시를 그렇게 분별없는 아이로 의심한다고 그가 여기게 할 수는 없어요. 그렇지만 그래도, 패니가 그 아이에게 한마디쯤 해 두는 것은 좋은 일일 거예요. 그런 일에는 이따금 조금씩 손을 써 두는 것이 아주 쓸모가 있지요.”\n\n“그런데 그 아이에게 무슨 말을 해야 하겠습니까?”\n\n“같은 젊은 신사와 그렇게 많이 이야기하는 아가씨는 반드시 남의 눈에 띈다는 것 — 사람들이 그 아이가 러프턴 경을 노린다고 흉을 잡을 거라는 것만 일러 주면 돼요. 내가 그 아이를 의심한다는 게 아니에요. 나는 그 아이에게 온당한 마음가짐이 넉넉하다고 믿어요. 좋은 교육을 받았고 품성이 곧다는 것도 알아요. 그러나 사람들은 그 아이 이야기를 할 거예요. 패니도 나만큼 그것을 알고 있을 거예요.”\n\n온당한 마음가짐과 교육과 곧은 품성이 정말로 루시 로바츠에게 러프턴 경을 사랑하는 일을 금하는 것인지, 패니는 생각해 보지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 이 문제에 관한 의심이 그녀에게 있었다 해도 그것이 마님에게 전해지지는 않았다. 러프턴 경과 루시 로바츠 사이에 혼사가 가능하다는 생각은 그녀의 머리에 한 번도 들어온 적이 없었고, 이제 그 생각이 들이밀어졌다 해서 그것을 거들어 주고 싶은 마음도 조금도 없었다. 그런 문제에 관해서라면 그녀도 레이디 러프턴과 마음을 같이할 수 있었다. 다만 무슨 간섭을 하는 것이 온당하냐 하는 데에는 완전히 뜻을 같이하지 않았다. 그럼에도 그녀는 곧 루시에게 말해 보겠다고 나섰다.\n\n“루시는 그 일에 관해서 머릿속에 아무 생각도 없을 것입니다,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그렇겠지요 — 없을 거라고 나도 짐작해요. 그런데 아가씨들은 머릿속에 아무 생각도 없었다는 바로 그 까닭으로, 이따금 저도 모르게 사랑에 빠져 버리고, 그러고는 자기가 몹시 부당한 대접을 받았다고 여기게 되지요.”\n\n“원하신다면 그 아이에게 조심하라고 일러 두겠습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“그래요, 얘야. 바로 그거예요. 조심하라고 일러 두는 것 — 그것으로 다 됐어요. 사랑스럽고 착하고 똑똑한 아이인데, 무슨 일로 그 아이와 편안히 지내 온 이 모양새가 끊긴다면 참 서운한 일이겠지요.”\n\n로바츠 부인은 이 위협의 정확한 뜻을 털끝만큼도 틀리지 않게 알아들었다. 루시가 러프턴 경의 시간과 관심을 그렇게 많이 자기 몫으로 붙들어 두기를 그치지 않는다면, 그 아이가 프램리 코트에 올라오는 일이 드물어져야 한다는 것이었다. 레이디 러프턴은 목사관의 벗들을 위하여 많은 일을, 참으로 많은 일을 해 줄 사람이었다. 그러나 그들을 위해서라도 아들의 앞날이 위태로워지는 것을 허락할 수는 없었다.\n\n두 사람 사이에 더 오간 말은 없었고, 로바츠 부인은 루시에게 말하겠다고 약속한 뒤 물러가려고 일어섰다.\n\n“패니는 무슨 일이든 그렇게 완벽하게 처리하니,” 레이디 러프턴이 로바츠 부인의 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했다. “나와 뜻이 같다는 것을 알고 나니 이제 아주 마음이 놓여요.” 로바츠 부인은 마님과 뜻이 딱 같지는 않았으나, 그렇다고 말할 만한 값이 있다고는 좀처럼 여기지 않았다.\n\n로바츠 부인은 곧바로 걸어서 제 집으로 향했다. 그리고 저택의 정원을 벗어나 길로 나와, 포진스네 가게 거의 맞은편에서 길이 목사관 쪽으로 굽어지는 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 말을 탄 러프턴 경과 그 옆에 서 있는 루시를 보았다. 벌써 다섯 시가 가까웠고 어스름이 내리고 있었다. 그러나 다가가면서, 아니 그보다는 불현듯 두 사람이 보이는 데에 이르면서, 그녀는 두 사람이 바싹 붙어 이야기하고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 러프턴 경의 얼굴은 그녀 쪽을 향해 있었고 말은 가만히 서 있었다. 그는 곁에 선 이쪽으로 몸을 굽히고 있었고, 오른손에 든 채찍은 마치 그의 손이 그 아이의 어깨에 닿았다가 어쩌면 그대로 얹혀 있기라도 한 듯이 그 아이의 팔 위로, 등 아래로 거의 드리워져 있었다. 그 아이는 그의 옆에 서서 그의 얼굴을 올려다보고 있었고, 장갑 낀 한 손은 말의 목에 얹혀 있었다. 두 사람을 본 로바츠 부인은 레이디 러프턴의 두려움에 까닭이 있을 수도 있다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 로바츠 부인이 다가올 때 루시가 보인 태도는 그런 두려움을 흩어 버리고 그것에 근거가 없다는 것을 증명하기에 알맞은 것이었다. 그녀는 서 있던 자리에서 움직이지도, 손을 내려놓지도, 무슨 당황한 낌새나 남의 눈을 의식하는 낌새를 보이지도 않았다. 그녀는 그 자리에 그대로 서 있었고, 올케가 다가왔을 때에는 웃고 있었으며 태연했다.\n\n“러프턴 경께서 저더러 말 타는 법을 배우라고 하시네요,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“말 타는 법을 배우라고!” 패니가 말했다. 그런 제안에 무슨 대답을 해야 할지 알 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n“그렇습니다,” 그가 말했다. “이 말이라면 아주 훌륭하게 태워 줄 거예요. 양처럼 순한 놈이고요. 어제는 그레고리에게 부인용 승마복처럼 이 놈에게 천을 늘어뜨려 두르게 하고 끌고 나가라고 시켰지요. 그래서 그 사람이 부인용 안장에 올라앉았습니다.”\n\n“그레고리가 루시보다 더 잘 해내겠군요.”\n\n“그 말은 평생 부인을 태우고 다닌 놈처럼 그를 태우고 캔터로 달렸어요. 그리고 입이 벨벳 같지요. 실은 그게 그 놈의 흠이에요 — 입이 너무 무릅니다.”\n\n“사람이 마음이 무른 것과 같은 종류의 일인 듯합니다,” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“바로 그렇습니다. 둘 다 아주 가벼운 손으로 다루어야 해요. 다루기 까다로운 짐승들이지만, 다루는 법을 알고 나면 아주 흡족하지요.”\n\n“그런데 보시다시피 저는 다루는 법을 모릅니다,” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“말에 관해서라면 이틀이면 배울 겁니다. 그리고 정말 한번 해 보시기를 바랍니다. 로바츠 양에게 아주 좋은 일이 되지 않겠어요, 로바츠 부인?”\n\n“루시에게는 승마복이 없어요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. 그런 자리에서 흔히 쓰는 핑계를 끌어댄 것이었다.\n\n“저희 집에 저스티니아의 승마복이 하나 있는 걸 압니다. 올 때 말을 탈 수 있도록 늘 여기에 하나 두고 가지요.”\n\n“레이디 메러디스의 물건을 그렇게 마음대로 쓰는 무례는 이 아이가 꿈에도 생각하지 못할 거예요,” 패니가 말했다. 그 제안에 거의 겁을 먹은 듯했다.\n\n“물론 있을 수 없는 일이에요, 패니,” 루시가 말했다. 이제는 다소 진지한 어조였다. “첫째로, 저는 러프턴 경의 말을 받지 않겠어요. 둘째로, 레이디 메러디스의 승마복을 받지 않겠어요. 셋째로, 저는 너무나 많이 무서울 거예요. 그리고 끝으로, 그 밖의 아주 훌륭한 여러 까닭으로 그것은 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이에요.”\n\n“터무니없는 소리,” 러프턴 경이 말했다.\n\n“터무니없는 소리가 아주 많습니다,” 루시가 웃으며 말했다. “그런데 그게 다 러프턴 경께서 하신 소리지요. 그런데 우리 춥잖아요 — 그렇지요, 패니? — 그러니 안녕히 계시라고 인사드리겠습니다.” 그러고서 두 사람은 그와 악수를 하고 목사관을 향해 걸어갔다.\n\n이 모든 일에서 로바츠 부인을 가장 놀라게 한 것은 루시가 말하고 처신하는 태도가 완전히 침착했다는 점이었다. 이것을, 루시의 결정을 받아들이는 러프턴 경의 낙담한 낯빛과 이어 놓고 보지 않을 수 없었으니, 로바츠 부인에게는 러프턴 경이 루시가 말 타는 법을 배우겠다고 승낙하지 않아서 속이 상해 있다는 것이 분명해졌다. 그런데 루시 자신은, 그 일에 관해서 더는 말이 나오지 않게 하겠다고 작심한 듯이 단단하고 딱 잘린 어조로 거절을 내놓은 것이었다.\n\n두 사람은 목사관 문에 이르기까지 한두 순간 말없이 걸었다. 그러다가 루시가 웃으며 말했다. “제가 그 커다란 말 위에 올라앉은 걸 상상할 수 있어요? 레이디 러프턴께서, 제가 그 위에 앉아 있고 그 나리께서 첫 수업을 해 주시는 것을 보셨다면 무슨 말씀을 하셨을까요?”\n\n“좋아하지 않으실 것 같아요,” 패니가 말했다.\n\n“좋아하지 않으실 게 분명해요. 그렇지만 그 점으로 그분의 심기를 건드릴 생각은 없어요. 러프턴 경이 저와 이야기하는 것을 보시는 것조차 그분은 좋아하지 않으신다는 생각이 이따금 들어요.”\n\n“좋아하지 않으시지요, 루시. 그분이 루시하고 희롱하는 걸 보시면요.”\n\n로바츠 부인은 이 말을 다소 무겁게 했다. 루시는 반쯤 놀리는 어조로 말하고 있었는데 말이다. 희롱이라는 말이 입에서 나오자마자 패니는 그 말을 쓴 것이 부당한 짓이었음을 깨달았다. 그녀는 레이디 러프턴이 무엇을 싫어할지를 시누이에게 전해 줄 무슨 말을 하고 싶었을 뿐이었다. 그런데 그렇게 하는 가운데 뜻하지 않게 그 아이에게 죄를 씌운 것이었다.\n\n“희롱이라니요, 패니!” 루시가 길에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 온 눈으로 옆 사람의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다. “제가 러프턴 경과 희롱을 했다는 말인가요?”\n\n“그런 말이 아니에요.”\n\n“아니면 제가 그분이 저를 희롱하도록 내버려 두었다는 말인가요?”\n\n“놀라게 하려던 건 아니었어요, 루시.”\n\n“그러면 무슨 뜻이었어요, 패니?”\n\n“그저 이런 뜻이에요. 그분이 루시에게 눈에 띄게 관심을 쏟고 루시가 그것을 받아들이면 레이디 러프턴께서 좋아하지 않으실 거라는 뜻이지요. 저 승마 이야기와 똑같아요. 거절하는 편이 나았던 거예요.”\n\n“물론 저는 거절했어요. 물론 그런 제안을 받아들이는 건 꿈에도 생각하지 않았어요. 그분의 말을 타고 시골을 돌아다니다니요! 제가 무슨 짓을 했다고 패니가 그런 생각을 해요?”\n\n“아무 짓도 하지 않았어요, 우리 루시.”\n\n“그러면 왜 아까 그렇게 말했어요?”\n\n“조심하라고 일러 주고 싶었기 때문이에요. 루시도 알잖아요, 루시를 흠잡을 뜻은 없어요. 그렇지만 대체로, 젊은 신사와 젊은 아가씨 사이의 가까운 우정은 위험한 것이라고 믿어도 좋아요.”\n\n그러고서 두 사람은 말없이 현관문까지 걸어 올라갔다. 문에 이르자 루시는 안으로 들어서는 대신 문간에 선 채로 말했다. “패니, 피곤하지 않으면 우리 한 바퀴 더 걸어요.”\n\n“아니, 피곤하지 않아요.”\n\n“패니 뜻을 지금 당장 알아 두는 게 좋겠어요.” 그러고서 두 사람은 다시 집에서 멀어져 갔다. “이제 사실대로 말해 주세요. 러프턴 경과 제가 희롱을 하고 있었다고 생각해요?”\n\n“그분이 루시를 조금 희롱하려는 기색이 있다고는 생각해요.”\n\n“그래서 레이디 러프턴께서 그 일로 저를 훈계하라고 부탁하신 거예요?”\n\n가엾은 로바츠 부인은 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그녀는 관련된 모든 사람을 좋게 여겼고 그들 모두에게 잘 처신하고 싶어 애가 탔다. 특히 나쁜 감정이 생기지 않기를 애타게 바랐고, 누구나 마음 편하고 서로 사이가 좋기를 바랐다. 그러나 이 물음이 이렇게 불현듯 날아들었을 때 진실은 그녀의 입에서 밀려 나오고 말았다.\n\n“훈계하라고는 하지 않으셨어요, 루시,” 마침내 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그럼 저에게 설교를 하라거나, 이야기를 하라거나, 한 수 가르치라거나요. 러프턴 경에게 등을 돌리게 만들 무슨 말을 하라고요?”\n\n“조심하라고 일러 주라고요, 우리 루시. 그분이 하신 말씀을 직접 들었다면 레이디 러프턴께 좀처럼 화가 나지 않았을 거예요.”\n\n“그럼, 조심하라고요. 아가씨가 어떤 신사와 사랑에 빠지지 말라고 주의를 받는 건 참 유쾌한 일이지요. 더구나 그 신사가 아주 부자이고, 경이고, 그런 온갖 것을 다 갖추었을 때에는요!”\n\n“아무도 한순간이라도 루시에게 잘못이 있다고 하지 않아요, 루시.”\n\n“잘못이라 — 그래요, 없지요. 제가 그분과 사랑에 빠진다 해도 그것이 잘못일지 어떨지는 저도 모르겠어요. 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 여기 있을 때에도 사람들이 그 아가씨에게 조심하라고 했을까요? 젊은 경들이 돌아다닐 때에는 아가씨들이 다 으레 주의를 받는 모양이지요. 왜 그분에게 ‘위험’이라고 딱지를 붙여 두지 않는 걸까요?” 그러고서 두 사람은 또 잠시 말이 없었다. 로바츠 부인은 이 일에 관하여 더 할 말이 있다고 느끼지 못했던 것이다.\n\n“러프턴 경처럼 그렇게 치명적인 사람이라면 ‘독약’이라고 적어야 마땅하지요. 그리고 잘못 삼키는 일이 없도록 눈에 확 띄는 색으로 조제해 두어야 하고요.”\n\n“보다시피 루시는 안전하겠네요,” 패니가 웃으며 말했다. “바로 이 병 하나에 관해서는 특별히 주의를 받았으니까요.”\n\n“아! 그렇지만 이미 그렇게 여러 번 복용한 뒤에 그게 무슨 쓸모가 있어요? 이미 화를 다 입은 지금에 와서, 얼마나 오래 그것을 먹어 왔는지도 모를 지금에 와서 말해 주는 건 아무 도움이 안 되지요. 아이고! 아이고! 아이고! 그런데 저는 그것을 안색에 좋은 흔해빠진 가루약 정도로 여겼답니다. 너무 늦었는지, 아니면 해독제라도 있는지 궁금해요.”\n\n로바츠 부인은 시누이의 말을 늘 다 알아듣지는 못했고, 지금도 조금 막막했다. “아직 어느 쪽에도 큰 화는 없다고 생각해요,” 그녀가 밝게 말했다.\n\n“아! 패니는 모르잖아요. 그렇지만 제가 죽는다면 — 저는 죽을 거예요, 죽을 것 같은 느낌이 들어요 — 그렇게 되면 레이디 러프턴께서 아주 호되게 당하셔야 마땅하다고 생각해요. 왜 제때에 그분에게 ‘위험’이라고 딱지를 붙여 두지 않으셨을까요?” 그러고서 두 사람은 집으로 들어가 각자의 방으로 올라갔다.\n\n지금 루시의 마음 상태를 이해하기는 누구에게든 어려운 일이었고, 그녀 자신이 그것을 이해했다고도 좀처럼 말할 수 없었다. 러프턴 경과 관련해서 이렇게 남의 입에 오르는 처지가 된 것이 그녀에게는 호된 타격으로 느껴졌다. 프램리 코트에서 보내던 즐거운 저녁들이 이제 끝났다는 것을, 그리고 다시는 거리낌 없는 어조로, 어색함 없이 그와 이야기할 수 없다는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그와 친해지기 전에는 그곳의 공기가 온통 아주 차갑게 느껴졌는데, 이제 다시 차가워질 수밖에 없었다. 그녀에게는 두 집이 열려 있었다. 프램리 코트와 목사관이었다. 그런데 이제 마음 편함을 두고 말하자면 그녀는 뒤의 것에만 머물러야 했다. 레이디 러프턴의 응접실에서 그녀는 다시 마음이 편할 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 그러면서도 그녀는 레이디 러프턴이 옳지 않은가 하고 스스로에게 묻지 않을 수 없었다. 올케가 말을 걸어왔을 때 그 일을 두고 농을 할 만한 용기와 침착함은 있었으나, 그것이 결코 농담거리가 아니라는 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다. 러프턴 경이 딱 잘라 그녀에게 구애를 한 것은 아니었다. 그러나 요즈음 그가 그녀에게 말하는 태도는, 언젠가 그녀가 스스로를 달래며 만족해했던 그 예사롭고 마음 편한 사내 벗의 우정이라는 생각과는 어울리지 않는다는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그런 성질의 가까운 우정은 위험한 것이라고 패니가 말했을 때, 패니는 옳지 않았던가?\n\n그렇다, 루시야, 아주 위험한 것이다. 루시는 그날 밤 잠자리에 들기 전에 그것이 그렇다는 것을 스스로에게 인정했다. 그리고 잠들지 못하는 눈과 젖은 베개를 안고 누워, 이제 그 딱지가 사실 너무 늦었다는 것을, 독약을 이미 삼킨 뒤에야 주의가 그녀에게 왔다는 것을 고백하지 않을 수 없었다. 해독제가 있는가? 그것만이 그녀가 헤아려 볼 거리로 남아 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 이튿날 아침 그녀는 아주 태연한 낯으로 나타날 수 있었다. 그리고 아침 식사 뒤에 마크가 집을 나선 다음, 그녀는 여전히 레이디 러프턴의 독약 장을 두고 패니와 농을 할 수 있었다.\n\n# 제14장. 호글스톡의 크롤리 씨\n\n그리고 레이디 러프턴의 마음에는 또 다른 근심이 있었다. 다름 아니라 그녀가 골라 앉힌 목사의 죄과였다. 그를 고른 것은 그녀였고, 목사의 도리를 거스른 그의 죄가 아무리 무겁다 해도 그녀는 결코 그를 놓을 마음이 없었다. 실로 그녀는 무엇이든 놓아 버리는 데 익숙한 여자가 아니었고, 그 무엇보다도 자기가 후원하는 사람을 놓아 버리는 데는 더욱 익숙하지 않았다. 그를 고른 사람이 바로 자기 자신이라는 사실이야말로 그에게 유리한 가장 강력한 논거였다.\n\n그러나 목사의 도리를 거스른 그의 죄는 그녀의 눈에 점점 심각해져 갔고, 그녀는 어떤 조치를 취해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 그를, 바로 그 사람 본인을 붙잡고 따진다는 것은 엄두가 나지 않았다. 만약 그렇게 했다가 그가 남의 일에 참견하지 말라고 — 그런 말 그대로는 아니겠지만 아마 그런 뜻으로 — 대답한다면 교구에 분열이 생길 것이었다. 그리고 그것보다는 무슨 일이라도 나았다. 그녀와 자기 교구의 목사가 서로 좋게 지내지 못하는 사태가 벌어진다면, 그녀 일생의 사업 전체가 뒤집히고 그녀의 활력이 흘러 나가는 모든 출구가 아주 막히지는 않더라도 막힘을 당할 터였다.\n\n그러나 어찌해야 하는가? 겨울 초에 그는 콜디코츠와 개서럼 성에 가서 도박꾼, 휘그, 무신론자, 방탕한 향락꾼, 그리고 프라우디 파 사람들과 어울렸다. 그것은 그녀가 눈감아 주었다. 그런데 이제 그는 그녀가 보는 앞에서 사냥하는 목사가 되어 가고 있었다. 그가 교구를 돌아다니다가 사냥개들을 그저 구경한 것뿐이라고 패니가 말하는 것은 그럴 수 있는 일이었다. 패니는 속을 수도 있었다. 그의 아내이니, 남편의 죄악을 보지 않는 것이 그녀의 의무일 수도 있었다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 속을 수 없었다. 코볼즈 애시스가 주의 어느 편에 있는지 그녀는 아주 잘 알고 있었다. 그곳은 프램리 교구도 아니고 그 옆 교구도 아니었다. 콜디코츠까지 가는 길의 절반쯤 — 주의 서부 지구에 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 말 두 마리가 죽어 나간 그 몰이 이야기를, 그 몰이에서 로바츠 목사가 서바셋주 수렵꾼들 사이에 불멸의 영광을 얻었다는 이야기를 들어 알고 있었다. 자기 주 안에서 일어나는 일을 레이디 러프턴에게 감춰 두기란 쉽지 않았다.\n\n이 모든 일을 그녀는 알고 있었으나 아직 아무 말도 꺼내지 않았고, 그런 까닭에 마음속으로 더욱 애를 태웠다. 입으로 나온 슬픔은 스스로 덜어지는 법이요, 충고를 할 수 있을 때에는 적어도 그 충고가 효험이 있으리라는 희망이라도 품게 되는 법이다. 아들에게는 로바츠 씨가 사냥개를 따라다니는 것이 안타까운 일이라고 여러 번 말한 바 있었다. — “성직자에게 어울리지 않는 일이라고 세상이 다 그러잖니.” 그녀는 못마땅하다는 어조로 이렇게 다그쳤다. 그러나 아들은 그녀에게 아무런 위로도 주지 않았다. “그는 사냥을 하는 게 아니에요, 어머니. 저처럼 하는 건 아니지요.” 그는 이렇게 말했다. “설령 한다 해도 무슨 해가 되는지 저는 정말 모르겠어요. 사람이란 대주교라 해도 소일거리 하나쯤은 있어야 하는 법이지요.” “소일거리는 집에 있잖니.” 레이디 러프턴은 이렇게 대답했다. “그 아내는 무얼 하고, 그 누이는 무얼 한단 말이냐?” 그러나 루시를 두고 하는 이런 말은 곧 자취를 감췄다.\n\n러프턴 경은 어머니를 도울 마음이 조금도 없었다. 그 교구 사제를 소극적으로라도 말리지 않았고, 사냥 집합 장소로 갈 때 마차에 자리를 내주겠다는 말을 삼가지도 않았다. 마크와 러프턴 경은 어릴 때부터 함께 자란 사이였고, 마크도 속으로는 자기와 똑같이 들판을 가로지르는 한바탕 질주를 즐긴다는 것을 경은 알고 있었다. 그렇다면 그게 무슨 해가 되겠는가?\n\n레이디 러프턴의 가장 든든한 조력자는 마크 자신의 양심이었다. 그는 한 번 이상 스스로를 나무랐고, 사냥이나 다니는 목사가 되지는 않겠다고 스스로에게 약속했다. 실로 그만큼까지 타락하도록 자신을 내버려 둔다면 앞날의 승진에 무슨 희망을 걸 수 있겠는가? 성직자의 생활에 필요하다고 여겨지는 예절을 되짚어 보고 앞으로 자신이 살아갈 방식을 그려 볼 때, 그가 품은 뜻은 사제 특유의 유난한 엄격함은 취하지 않겠다는 것이었다. 춤이나 카드판을, 극장이나 소설 읽기를 규탄하는 사람으로 이름나지는 않겠다는 것이었다. 그는 주위의 세상을 있는 그대로 받아들이면서, 가르침과 몸가짐으로 기독교가 이루어 가는 점진적인 개선에 손을 보태려 애쓸 것이었다. 그러나 갑작스럽거나 거창한 개혁은 시도하지 않을 것이었다. 그가 아무리 설교를 한들, 아무리 엄숙한 은자가 된들, 과자와 맥주는 여전히 인기가 있을 것이고 생강은 여전히 입안을 얼얼하게 할 것이었다. 그러나 밝은 얼굴, 참되고 믿음직한 마음, 튼튼한 팔, 겸손한 정신이라면, 사람이 유쾌하면서도 방탕하지 않을 수 있고 여자가 신앙이 깊으면서도 세상에 죽어 있지 않을 수 있다는 것을 주위 사람들에게 가르치는 데 큰 몫을 할지도 몰랐다.\n\n자기 앞날의 삶에 대한 그의 생각은 그러했다. 성직자라면 좀 더 진지하게 마음을 바쳐 자기 일에 임했어야 한다고 여길 사람이 많겠지만, 그래도 그 생각에는 얼마간의 지혜가 있었다. 물론 얼마간의 어리석음도 있었으니, 그 생각이 그를 끌고 들어간 곤경이 그것을 드러냈다.\n\n“내 마음 깊은 곳에서 나쁘게 보이지 않는 것을 나쁘다고 여기는 척하지는 않겠다.” 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 말했다. 그리하여 그는 사냥하는 지주들 사이에서도 물들지 않고 살 수 있으리라고 마음을 정했다. 그런데 그는 본래 주위 사람들이 하는 대로 따라 하기가 너무 쉬운 사람이었으므로, 남에게 옳다고 인정한 일이 자기에게 그르기는 어렵겠다는 생각에 차츰 이르렀다.\n\n그래도 양심은 그를 꾸짖었고, 그는 올해가 지나면 다시는 사냥을 하지 않겠다고 한 번 이상 스스로에게 선언했다. 그리고 그런 날 집에 돌아오면 그의 패니가 그를 바라보는 눈길이 그의 가슴을 찔렀다. 그녀는 그에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 비웃는 어조로, 노한 눈으로 오늘 사냥이 즐거웠느냐고 물은 적도 없었다. 그러나 그가 사냥 이야기를 꺼내면 그녀는 신이 나서 대꾸해 주지 못했다. 그와 관련된 다른 일이라면 그녀는 언제나 신이 나서 대꾸하는 사람이었다.\n\n게다가 얼마 뒤에는 사태를 더 나쁘게 만들었으니, 삼월 말께에 또 하나 아주 어리석은 일을 저지른 것이다. 그는 사워비에게서 값비싼 말을 사겠다고 거의 승낙해 버렸다 — 조금도 필요하지 않은 짐승이요, 한번 손에 넣으면 반드시 그를 더 깊은 곤경으로 끌고 갈 짐승이었다. 신사란 마구간에 좋은 말이 있으면 그 말을 거기 세워 두고 밥값이나 먹어 치우게 하고 싶지 않은 법이다. 이륜마차 말이라면 주인은 이륜마차를 몰고 싶어 안달할 것이고, 사냥말이라면 그 흐뭇한 임자는 사냥개 무리와 함께 나서고 싶어질 것이다.\n\n“마크,” 어느 날 함께 나가 있을 때 사워비가 그에게 말했다. “내 이놈이 어찌나 기운이 넘치는지 도무지 탈 수가 없어요. 당신은 젊고 튼튼하니 한 시간쯤 나와 바꿔 타 봐요.” 그래서 두 사람은 말을 바꿔 탔고, 로바츠가 올라앉은 그 말은 그를 태우고 훌륭하게 달려 나갔다.\n\n“훌륭한 짐승이군요.” 다시 만났을 때 마크가 말했다.\n\n“그래요, 당신만큼 몸무게가 나가는 사람에게는 그렇지요. 나에게는 아까운 말이에요. 내 용도에는 너무 과한 말이지요. 나는 이제 예전만큼 잘 따라가지 못하니까요. 사냥말로는 좋은 축이에요. 이제 여섯 살로 접어들고 있고요.”\n\n그 훌륭한 짐승의 값이 두 사람 사이에서 거론되기에 이른 경위를 내가 정확히 그려 보일 필요는 없다. 다만 사워비 씨가 그 목사에게 그 말을 130파운드에 넘기겠다고 말한 일은 실제로 벌어졌다.\n\n“그리고 정말이지 당신이 그 말을 가져갔으면 좋겠어요.” 사워비가 말했다. “그러면 내 마음을 짓누르는 큰 짐이 얼마쯤은 덜릴 테니까요.”\n\n마크는 놀란 얼굴로 벗의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 어째서 그렇게 되는지 그 순간에는 알 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n“당신도 알다시피 나는 그 저주받을 어음 때문에 당신이 조만간 주머니에 손을 넣어야 하지 않을까 걱정이거든요 —” 그 불경한 말이 귀에 들어오자 마크는 몸을 움츠렸다 — “그러니 당신이 값이 될 만한 것을 손에 쥐고 있다고 생각할 수 있으면 나로서는 기쁘겠어요.”\n\n“내가 500파운드를 통째로 물어야 한다는 말인가요?”\n\n“아니, 천만에요. 그런 게 아니에요. 하지만 얼마쯤은 물어야 할 겁니다. 댄디를 백삼십에 가져가겠다면, 토저가 당신에게 찾아올 때 그만한 액수는 준비된 셈이 되지요. 그 말은 헐값이나 다름없어요. 낸 돈으로 두고두고 재미를 볼 겁니다.”\n\n마크는 처음에는 조용하고 단호한 어조로 그 말이 필요하지 않다고 잘라 말했다. 그러나 나중에는, 사워비 씨의 빚 가운데 얼마를 자기가 물어야 할 운명이라면 힘이 미치는 한 얼마라도 되받아 두는 편이 낫지 않겠느냐는 생각이 들었다. 그 말을 받아 두었다가 팔아 버리는 것도 어쩌면 괜찮은 일이었다. 그렇게 하면 이 어음과 관련하여 두 사람 사이에 값나가는 대가가 오갔다고 사워비 씨가 말할 수 있게 되고, 두 사람 사이의 금전 관계를 풀 수 없는 뒤엉킴으로 만들어 놓으려는 그 신사의 일을 자기가 돕는 셈이 된다는 것은 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다. 사워비 씨는 이것의 값을 잘 알고 있었다. 그것이 있으면 러프턴 경의 그 다른 경우에 그랬듯이 그럴싸한 이야기를 꾸며 낼 수 있었다.\n\n“댄디를 가져가겠어요?” 사워비가 그에게 다시 말했다.\n\n“당장은 그러겠다고 말할 수 없군요.” 목사가 말했다. “철도 다 끝났는데 이제 그 말을 어디에 쓰겠어요?”\n\n“바로 그거예요, 마크. 철이 끝난 지금 나 역시 그 말을 어디에 쓰겠어요? 지금이 삼월 말이 아니라 시월 초라면 댄디는 백이 아니라 이백삼십까지 올라 있을 겁니다. 여섯 달만 지나면 그 말은 당신이 부르고 싶은 값이 무엇이든 그만한 값이 나갈 거예요. 그 골격을 좀 봐요.”\n\n그 교구 사제는 정말로 골격을 들여다보았으니, 아주 능숙하고 도무지 성직자답지 않은 태도로 그 짐승을 살폈다. 그는 말의 네 발을 하나씩 차례로 들어 굽바닥의 삼각 살을 만져 보고 각 부분의 균형을 눈으로 재었다. 다리를 위아래로 손으로 훑으며 아래 관절의 뼈를 손으로 감아 보았다. 눈을 들여다보았고, 가슴통의 너비, 등의 처짐, 갈비의 모양, 엉덩이의 곡선, 그리고 일을 심하게 시켰을 때의 호흡 능력을 헤아렸다. 그리고 조금 물러서서 옆에서 바라보며 전체의 모양과 됨됨이를 대강 가늠했다. “무릎이 조금 앞으로 넘어간 것 같은데요.” 목사가 말했다.\n\n“땅이 기울어서 그래요. 밥, 말을 좀 걸려 봐. 그래, 이제 거기 세워 둬.”\n\n“완전하지는 않군요.” 마크가 말했다. “뒷굽이 그다지 마음에 들지 않아요. 하지만 제법 괜찮은 축의 말인 건 틀림없네요.”\n\n“나는 꽤 괜찮다고 보는데요. 당신 말대로 완전하기까지 하다면 백삼십에 당신 마구간으로 들어가지는 않겠지요. 완전한 말을 본 기억이 있어요?”\n\n“당신의 암말 갬프 부인은 거의 완전하다 할 만했지요.”\n\n“갬프 부인에게도 흠은 있었어요. 우선 먹성이 좋지 않았지요. 하지만 갬프 부인보다 훨씬 나은 말을 만나는 일이 흔하지 않은 건 분명해요.” 이런 식으로 두 사람 사이에서 그 일이 이야기되었고 마구간 이야기가 길게 오갔으니, 그 모든 것이 사워비로 하여금 벗의 성스러운 직분을 점점 더 잊게 만들었고, 어쩌면 그 교구 사제 자신도 그것을 너무 자주 잊게 만들었을 것이다. 그러나 아니었다. 그는 그것을 잊지 않았다. 오히려 마음에 두고 있었다. 다만 마음에 두는 방식이 그러하여, 요즘 그 문제에 대한 그의 생각은 언제나 괴로웠다.\n\n주의 동부 지구 북쪽 맨 끝에, 그러면서 서부 지구와 맞닿은 경계에도 걸쳐 있는 호글스톡이라는 교구가 있다. 이 이야기가 끝나기 전에 이 모든 지명을 제대로 설명하려면 바셋주의 지도를 하나 붙여야 하지 않을까 나는 은근히 걱정이 된다. 프램리도 주의 북부에 있으나, 런던 쪽으로 삼십 마일쯤 더 가까운 지점에서 바체스터로 갈라져 나가는 지선이 뻗어 나오는 그 간선 철도의 바로 남쪽이다. 프램리 코트로 가는 역은 실버브리지인데, 그 역은 주의 서부 지구에 있다. 호글스톡은 철도의 북쪽이지만 그 철도 노선은 교구의 일부를 지나가며, 교회끼리는 칠 마일이나 떨어져 있어도 교구는 프램리와 맞붙어 있다. 바셋주는 전체로 보아 나무가 빽빽한 정겨운 초록 고을이니, 우거진 큰 산울타리와 촉촉하고 깊숙한 예쁜 시골길, 그리고 넓은 풀밭 가장자리를 끼고 뻗은 도로가 있다. 이 주의 일반적인 성격은 그러하다. 그러나 북쪽 맨 끝에 이르면 그 성격이 달라진다. 거기는 황량하고 보기 흉하며, 산울타리는 낮게 인공으로 다듬어져 있고 숲이 없다. 그렇다고 농사를 짓지 않는 것은 아니어서, 온 땅이 새로 낸 듯한 큰 밭으로 갈라져 순무와 밀과 사료 무를 윤작의 순서에 따라 내고 있다. 그러나 영국식 농지 특유의 아름다움은 조금도 없다. 호글스톡 교구에는 성직자의 집 말고는 신사의 집이 한 채도 없다. 그리고 그 집도 신사의 집이기는 하나 신사의 집으로 걸맞다고는 도무지 말할 수 없다. 보기 흉하고, 멋없이 각지고, 작다. 집에는 정원이 딸려 있어 절반은 앞에, 절반은 뒤에 있다. 그러나 이 정원도 교구의 다른 데와 마찬가지로 쓸모는 충분하나 볼거리는 전혀 없다. 양배추는 나지만 나무는 없고, 감자는 아주 훌륭한 종류라고 나는 알고 있으나 꽃은 거의 없으며, 관목이라는 이름에 걸맞은 것은 하나도 없다. 실로 호글스톡 교구 전체는 바셋주만큼 매력이 있다고는 결코 할 수 없는 이웃 주에 들어가 있어야 했다. 이는 자기 나라를 잘 아는 몇 안 되는 내 독자들이라면 익히 아는 사실이다.\n\n이 지면에서 이름이 언급된 바 있는 크롤리 씨가 호글스톡의 담임 성직자였다. 애초에 제도가 정해질 때 우리 교구 성직자의 보수가 어떤 원리로 정해졌는지는, 중세 교회의 검은 고딕 활자 학문을 가장 깊고 가장 열렬히 사랑하는 사람이라도 이제는 말할 수 없으리라고 나는 본다. 사제들이 교구에서 나는 것의 십일조로 보수를 받고, 그 십일조에서 교회 수리며 교육 같은 다른 좋은 일들의 비용도 대게 되어 있었다는 것 정도는 우리 대부분이 어렴풋이 안다. 주임 사제는 큰 축의 목사이므로 자기 교구의 십일조를 전부 — 적어도 성직자에게 돌아갈 몫은 전부 — 차지했고, 교구 사제는 남의 대리인이니 작은 몸인 만큼 소십일조에만 권리가 있었다는 것 정도는, 이런 일에 어두운 우리도 대강 알고 있다. 그러나 그 옛 중세에도 그런 식으로나마 일에 대한 보수를 공정하게 배분하는 데 조금이라도 가까이 갔다고는 생각할 수 없다. 아무튼 지금은 그런 근사치조차 없다는 것만은 충분히 분명하다.\n\n그리고 개혁의 시대라는 이 시절에조차, 어떤 지나치게 대담한 개혁가가 그런 근사치를 한번 시도해 보자고 제안한다면 교회의 성직자들 사이에서 얼마나 큰 비명이 터져 나올 것인가? 성직자를 알고, 성직자를 좋아하고, 성직자와 더불어 살아 본 사람들은 한번 상상이나 해 보시라! 성직자가 공로로든 은고로든 얻어 낸 성직록의 세속 수입에 따라 보수를 받는 것이 아니라, 해야 할 일에 따라 보수를 받는다니! 오 도딩턴이여! 오 스탠호프여, 이토록 신성을 모독하는 생각이 그대들의 따뜻한 성직자의 품에 들어갈 수 있다면, 이것을 한번 생각해 보시라! 교회의 일을 그 양과 질에 따라 사고팔고 값을 치른다니!\n\n그러나 그럼에도 우리 영국인은 결국 여기에 이르지 않을 수 없으리라고 예언해 둘 수 있다. 그 생각이 아무리 언짢은 것임에 틀림없더라도 그렇다. 마음이 너그러운 교인들도 이 문제에 대해서는 대개 나와 비슷하게 느낀다고 나는 생각한다. 교구 수입에 관한 우리의 지금 제도는 오래되었다는 이유로, 신사답고 영국답고 그림 같다는 이유로 사랑을 받는다. 우리는 될 수 있는 한 그것에 바싹 매달리고 싶어 하나, 우리가 그러는 것이 판단의 힘이 아니라 편견의 힘 때문임을 우리도 안다. 오래되고, 신사답고, 영국답고, 그림 같은 제도라는 것은 거기까지는 아주 흐뭇하다. 그러나 그 밖에도 몹시 바람직한 — 아니, 반드시 있어야 할 — 성질들이 있는데, 이 오래되고 그림 같은 제도는 바로 그 점에서 지독히 모자라지 않은가?\n\n게다가 어떤 주교는 한 해에 만 오천을 받고, 목사를 거느린 정도가 똑같은 다른 주교는 겨우 사천을 받는다는 것이 또 얼마나 흐뭇한 일이었던가! 어느 고위 성직자는 한 해에 이만을 받는데 같은 교구의 후임자는 이듬해에 겨우 오천을 받을 수 있었다는 것이! 거기에는 흐뭇하고 그림 같은 무엇이 있었다. 봉건의 매력이 깃든 제도였고, 그것을 바꿔 놓은 일은 우리 가운데 많은 사람의 마음에 들지 않았다. 정해진 봉급을 받고 영지도 토지 관리인도 딸리지 않은 주교는 반쪽 주교일 뿐이다. 누가 나에게 그 반대를 아무리 철저히 증명해 보인다 한들 — 내가 나 자신에게 아무리 자주 증명해 본다 한들 — 이 문제에 관한 내 마음은 그로써 눈곱만큼도 달라지지 않는다. 한 해에 삼천을 받는 수석 사제가 하나둘 있고, 늙은 퍼플 박사는 참사회원 자리를 네 개나 쥐고 있었으며 그중 하나는 황금 자리요 나머지 셋은 은에 금박을 씌운 자리였다는 것을 아는 게 좋았다! 그런 것을 아는 일은 나에게 언제나 흐뭇했다! 황금 자리라니! 교회를 사랑하는 귀에 그 울림이 얼마나 달가운가!\n\n그러나 주교들은 그 아름다움을 깎였고 수석 사제들은 쇠락의 길에 있다. 공리주의의 시대는 교회 땅의 기름진 몫을 요구한다. 그것을 잘게 나누어, 꼭 필요한 일하는 성직자들이 먹고살 만한 작은 여물로 만들자는 것이다 — 일하는 성직자가 도무지 먹고살 수 없을 만큼 터무니없이 잘게 나눈 몫으로. 그리고 십일조가 활짝 만개한 주임 사제와 교구 사제들도 — 엄격한 공리주의 원칙에 비추어 너무 만개한 십일조를 쥔 이들도 — 반드시 그 뒤를 따를 것이다. 스탠호프와 도딩턴은 머리를 숙여야 하리라. 세속의 권리에 대해서는 받아 낼 수 있는 만큼의 보상을 받겠으나, 바라는 만큼의 보상은 아마 받지 못할 것이다. 다른 장사와 직업과 생업에서는 사람이 일한 만큼 보수를 받는다. 교회에서도 그렇게 되게 하라. 공리주의적이고 개혁적이고 실무적인 의회의 명령이 조만간 그렇게 나올 것이다.\n\n이 문제에 대해서는 나에게도 나름의 방안이 있으나, 남자도 여자도 읽지 않을 터이니 여기 내놓지는 않겠다. 그리고 이 일과 관련하여 여기서 더 밝혀 둘 것은 다만, 이 모든 말이 나오게 된 계기가 여기 반드시 적어 두어야 할 사실, 곧 크롤리 씨가 호글스톡 교구의 교구 업무 전부를 도맡아 하면서 한 해에 백삼십 파운드밖에 받지 못했다는 사실이라는 점이다. 게다가 호글스톡은 큰 교구다. 벽돌 굽는 이들이 잔뜩 사는 인구 많은 마을이 둘 들어 있는데, 사람이 흥청거리며 마귀에게로 걸어가는 것을 그냥 두고 보지 못하는 열심한 목사에게 이 무리는 몹시 골칫거리다. 호글스톡에는 두 사람이 꼬박 매달릴 만한 일이 있다. 그런데도 그 안에서 목사의 일에 쓸 수 있는 재원이라고는 이 한 해 백삼십 파운드라는 초라한 봉급이 전부다. 그림 같지도, 오래되지도, 봉건적이지도 않은 봉급이다. 호글스톡은 종신 부목사직으로만 등급이 매겨져 있기 때문이다.\n\n크롤리 씨는 앞서, 자기 교구 밖으로 산책 나가는 것조차 옳지 않게 여기는 사람이라고 로바츠 씨가 말한 그 성직자로 언급된 바 있다. 그렇게 말할 때 마크 로바츠는 물론 같은 목사인 그를 우스갯거리로 삼은 것이다. 그러나 크롤리 씨가 엄격한 사람이었다는 것에는 의심의 여지가 없다 — 엄격하고 준엄하고 사람을 편하게 해 주지 않는 사람, 하느님과 자기 양심을 두려워하는 사람이었다. 우리는 크롤리 씨와 그의 처지에 대해 한두 마디 해 두어야 한다.\n\n그는 이제 마흔 살쯤 되었으나, 그 가운데 지금의 성직록을 차지한 것은 사오 년밖에 되지 않았다. 성직자로서 그의 삶의 첫 십 년은 콘월 북쪽 해안의 황량하고 보기 흉하고 추운 교구에서 부목사의 직무를 치르며 그 삶을 헤쳐 나가는 데 다 들어갔다. 그것은 지치는 삶이요 무서운 싸움이었으니, 제대로 보답받지 못하고 늘 만족스럽게 해내지도 못한 직무로, 사랑과 가난으로, 늘어 가는 근심으로, 병과 빚과 죽음으로 이루어진 삶이었다. 크롤리 씨는 서품을 받자마자 거의 곧바로 혼인했고, 그 차고 아늑함이라곤 없는 콘월의 오두막에서 그에게 아이들이 태어났던 것이다. 그가 혼인한 여자는 교육을 잘 받고 곱게 자랐으나 세상의 재물은 지니고 오지 못한 사람이었다. 두 사람은 함께 용감히 싸우기로 마음을 굳히고 나섰다. 세상과 세상의 방식은 아예 거들떠보지 않고, 위로를 얻을 곳으로는 오직 하느님과 서로만을 바라보기로 했다. 점잖은 살림이며 부드러운 옷가지며 고운 음식 따위의 생각은 버릴 것이었다. 다른 사람들, 곧 손으로 일하는 사람들, 그중에서도 형편이 나은 축이라 해도, 그가 성직자로 벌 수 있는 만큼의 살림만으로도 체면을 지키며 건강하게 살아가고 있었다. 그렇게 그들도 살 것이었다. 그토록 가난하게, 그토록 체면을 지키며, 손이 아니라 마음으로 자기 일을 해내며 살 것이었다.\n\n그리하여 그들은 자리를 잡았으니, 조그만 살림을 거들어 줄 사람으로 맨발의 열네 살 난 계집아이 하나를 두고 세상을 시작했다. 그리고 한동안은 둘 다 마음을 잃지 않고 서로를 깊이 사랑하며 자기 일에서도 어느 만큼 잘되어 갔다. 그러나 한번 신사로서 세상을 걸어 본 사람은 자기 처지를 바꾸어 사회의 서열에서 더 아래로 내려앉는다는 것이 어떤 일인지 알지 못한다. 자기가 사랑하는 여자를 그렇게 끌어내린다는 것이 어떤 일인지는 더욱 알지 못한다. 그 자체로는 하잘것없고 사소한 일이 천 가지나 있어서, 사람이 자기 철학으로 그것들을 생각할 때에는 업신여기지만, 정작 그것들 없이 지내야 할 때에는 그 철학이 준엄한 시험을 받는다. 이 글을 읽는 아무리 소박한 사람이라도, 아침에 옷을 갖춰 입는 자기 평소의 방식을 한번 떠올려 보고 그런 싸움이 자기에게 얼마나 비싼 값을 물릴지 고백해 보시라.\n\n그러고서 아이들이 태어났다. 막일하는 사람의 아내도 자기 아이들을 길러 내며, 크롤리 부인의 오두막에 들어와 있던 것만큼의 편의조차 없이도 흔히 건강하게 길러 낸다. 그러나 그녀에게 그 일은 거의 감당할 수 없을 만큼의 짐이었다. 그렇다고 그녀가 기절하거나 주저앉았다는 말은 아니다. 그녀는 두 사람 가운데 더 단단한 쇠로 만들어진 사람이어서, 그가 엎어져 있는 동안에도 버텨 낼 수 있었다.\n\n그리고 이따금 그는 정말로 엎어졌다 — 넋과 얼이 엎어졌다. 그럴 때면 그는 쓰라린 목소리로 하소연하며, 세상이 자기에게 너무 모질다고, 짐에 짓눌려 등이 부러졌다고, 자기의 하느님이 자기를 버렸다고 외쳤다. 그런 심사에 빠지면 그는 며칠이고 며칠이고 오두막 안에 들어박혀, 문간에 그림자도 비치지 않고 집안 식구 아닌 다른 얼굴은 보지도 않았다. 그런 날들은 그에게나 그녀에게나 끔찍했다. 그는 세수도 하지 않은 채, 깎지 않은 얼굴을 손에 기대고, 낡은 실내 가운을 헐렁하게 걸치고 거기 앉아, 음식은 거의 입에 대지 않고, 말은 좀처럼 하지 않고, 기도하려 애쓰다가 그 애씀이 하도 자주 헛되이 끝나곤 했다. 그러다가 그는 의자에서 일어나 미친 듯이 터져 나오는 소리로 자기 창조주를 불러, 이 참담함에서 자기를 데려가 달라고 부르짖었다.\n\n그런 순간에도 그녀는 결코 그를 버리지 않았다. 한때 그들에게는 아이가 넷 있었고, 이 어린 무리의 무게가 온전히 그녀의 팔에, 그녀의 근육에, 그녀의 마음과 몸의 힘에 얹혀 있었으나, 그녀는 그를 위로하려는 애씀을 한 번도 그치지 않았다. 그러면 마침내 그는 땅바닥에 아주 엎드려 자비를 구하는 애처로운 기도를 쏟아 놓았고, 하룻밤 잠을 자고 나면 다시 한번 일하러 나섰다.\n\n그러나 그녀는 절망에 굽히지 않았다. 그 싸움이 그녀가 견딜 수 있는 힘을 넘어선 적은 한 번도 없었다. 여자로서의 고운 모습도 제 몫만큼은 지녔던 사람이었으나 그것은 이제 다 사라졌다. 혈색은 빠르게 시들었고, 싱싱하고 부드러운 빛깔은 이내 그 얼굴과 이마에서 물러갔다. 그녀는 마르고 거칠어져 거의 파리해졌다. 광대뼈가 살갗을 뚫고 나올 지경으로, 팔꿈치가 뾰족해질 지경으로, 손가락뼈가 해골의 것처럼 될 지경으로 말랐다. 눈은 빛을 잃지 않았으나 부자연스럽게 반짝이고 튀어나와, 핏기 없는 얼굴에 어울리지 않게 너무 커 보였다. 한때는 뒤로 빗어 넘기기를 좋아하던, 남이 보든 말든 마음 쓰지 않는다고 스스로 자랑하곤 했던 그 부드러운 갈색 머리채는 이제 성글기도 하고 온통 헝클어지고 깨끗하지도 않았다. 그것이 남의 눈에 보이는지 아닌지는 이제 별로 생각할 거리가 아니었다. 그를 설교단에 올려보낼 만한 사람으로 만들 수 있을지 — 그 네 어린것들이 먹을 것은 있을지 — 그 등을 찬 바람에서 지켜 줄 수 있을지, 그것이 이제 그녀가 생각하는 거리였다.\n\n그러고서 그 가운데 둘이 죽었고, 그가 아이들의 무덤 앞에서 자기 직무를 치르다 쓰러지지 않게 하려고 그녀가 스스로 나가 아이들을 얼어붙은 땅 아래 누이는 것을 지켜보았다. 그는 어떤 사람에게도 도움을 청하려 하지 않았으니, 적어도 그것이 내내 그의 자랑이었다.\n\n둘이 죽었으나 그 아이들의 병은 길었다. 그러고 나서 빚이 그들에게 닥쳐왔다. 실은 빚은 지난 오 년 동안 느리지만 어김없는 걸음으로 그들에게 다가오고 있었다. 자기 아이들이 굶는 것을 보면서 내미는 빵을 받지 않을 사람이 어디 있는가? 자기 아내가 사무치는 궁핍 속에 누워 있는 것을 보면서 손 닿는 데에 방도가 있다면 그 방도를 찾지 않을 사람이 어디 있는가? 그리하여 빚이 그들에게 닥쳐왔고, 거친 사내들이 얼마 안 되는 돈을 내놓으라고 몰아댔다 — 세상에는 얼마 안 되나 그들에게는 도무지 갚을 수 없이 큰 액수였다. 그러면 그는 그 안쪽 방의 좁은 틈 같은 곳에 몸을 감추었다 — 깊은 부끄러움을 안고 세상으로부터 몸을 감추었으니, 부끄러움과 내려앉는 가슴과 부서진 정신을 안고서였다.\n\n그러나 그런 사람에게 벗이 하나도 없었단 말인가? 이런 말이 나올 것이다. 그런 사람들은 벗을 많이 사귀지 않는다고 나는 본다. 그러나 이 사람이 아주 벗 없는 사람은 아니었다. 콘월의 부목사 자리에 있는 그를 거의 해마다 한 번씩 같은 성직자인 옛 대학 벗이 찾아왔고, 그 벗은 자기 힘이 미치는 한 이 부목사와 그 아내를 도와주었다. 이 신사는 이웃의 어느 농부 집에 한 주일씩 묵었는데, 크롤리 씨가 절망에 빠져 있는 것을 보고서도 떠날 때에는 그의 넋에 위로 몇 방울을 남겨 놓았다. 이 점에서 이로움이 한쪽으로만 흐른 것도 아니었다. 크롤리 씨는 어떤 때에는 자기 자신을 위해서는 그토록 약했으나 남을 위해서는 강할 수 있었고, 자기가 사랑하는 이 사람에게 크게 도움이 되도록 한 번 이상 강했다. 그리고 돈으로 하는 도움도 있었다 — 그 이른 시절에는 큰 액수는 아니었으니 이 벗도 그때는 세상의 부유한 사람들 축에 들지 못했기 때문이다 — 그러나 그 소박한 살림살이에는 넉넉한 액수였다. 그것을 받아들이게 하는 일만 어찌어찌 되었다면 말이다. 그런데 그 일에는 끝없는 어려움이 있었다. 노골적으로 내미는 돈이라면 크롤리 씨는 하나도 받지 않았다. 그래도 아내가 거들어 여기저기 셈은 치러졌다. 그리고 케이트에게 신이 왔다 — 케이트가 신이 필요 없는 곳으로 옮겨 가기까지는. 해리와 프랭크에게 줄 옷감은 그 아내의 하나뿐인 옷가방 뚜껑 아래로 슬며시 들어왔다 — 그 여윈 손가락이 그 옷감으로 두 사내아이의 옷을 지었으나, 하느님의 뜻이 그러하여 그 옷을 입은 것은 하나뿐이었다.\n\n콘월의 부목사 자리에 있던 동안, 가장 혹독한 싸움을 치르던 동안 크롤리 씨 부부는 그러했다. 하루의 정당한 일이 하루의 정당한 품값만큼 값이 나간다고 여기는 사람에게는, 한 사람이 그토록 힘들게 일하고 그토록 적게 받았다는 것이 여간 모진 일로 보이지 않을 것이다. 그렇게 젊어서 혼인한 것이 온전히 그의 잘못이라고 여기는 사람도 있을 것이다. 그러나 그래도 그 물음은 남는다. 하루의 정당한 일은 하루의 정당한 품값만큼 값이 나가지 않는가? 이 사람은 정말로 힘들게 일했다 — 사람이 할 수 있는 일 가운데 어쩌면 가장 힘든 일에 매달려서. 그리고 십 년 동안 한 해에 칠십 파운드쯤을 벌었다. 혼인을 했든 홀몸이든, 그가 자기의 정당한 일에 대해 정당한 품값을 받았다고 말할 사람이 있겠는가? 그런데도 자기 돈을 어디에 쓸지만 알면 자기네 성직자에게 기꺼이 값을 치를 사람이 그토록 많다! 그러나 그것은 긴 이야기다. 로바츠 씨가 던스터블 양에게 말했듯이.\n\n콘월의 부목사 자리에 있던 동안 크롤리 씨는 그러했다.\n\n# 제15장. 레이디 러프턴의 사절\n\n그리고 그 뒤로 이어진 날들에, 크롤리 씨의 그 벗은—그런데 그 이름은 아직 밝히지 않았다—빠르고 큰 승진을 얻었다. 그때 그의 이름은 애러빈 씨였고, 그 빠르고 큰 승진이 절정에 이른 뒤로는 애러빈 박사가 되었다. 그 지난 세월에 그는 다만 라자러스 칼리지의 펠로였을 뿐이다. 그러다 동 바셋주 세인트 이월드의 교구 사제가 되었고, 아직 거기에 자리를 잡기도 전에 미망인 볼드 부인과 결혼하였으니, 토지와 공채에 든 재산을 지녔고 걸림돌이라고는 어린 아기 하나뿐인 미망인이었다. 아니, 그때는 아직 그녀와 결혼한 것도 아니었고—다만 그러기로 약조만 해 둔 참이었는데, 사람들이 그를 바체스터 수석 사제로 올려세웠다. 이 모든 것은 교구와 주의 연대기에서 읽을 수 있는 일이다.\n\n그리고 이제 재산이 넉넉해진 새 수석 사제는 캐멀퍼드의 어느 변호사의 도움을 받아 가난한 벗의 빚을 갚아 주는 일을 꾀하였다. 결국 그것은 얼마 되지도 않는 목록이어서, 통틀어 백 파운드를 그다지 넘지 않는 액수였다. 그러고 나서 열여덟 달이 지나는 동안 이 초라한 성직 자리가 수석 사제의 손에 굴러들었으니, 한 해 백삼십 파운드에 이르는 급여가 딸린 호글스톡의 성직이었다. 그것만 해도 콘월의 부목사직에 비하면 두 배 값어치였고, 게다가 딸린 집도 한 채 있었다. 가엾은 크롤리 부인은 그 소식을 듣고서, 이제 가난과의 싸움도 거의 끝난 것이라고 생각하였다. 십 년을 칠십 파운드로 살아온 사람들이 백삼십 파운드로 못 할 일이 무엇이 있겠는가?\n\n그리하여 그들은 그 춥고 황량한 고장을 떠나, 소박한 살림살이의 수호신들을 이끌고서 또 다른 고장에 자리를 잡았으니, 그곳도 춥고 황량하기는 하였으나 앞의 고장보다는 그토록 심하지 않았다. 그들은 자리를 잡고서 다시 사람의 매정함과 악마의 열성에 맞서는 싸움을 시작하였다. 나는 크롤리 씨가 딱딱하고 불쾌한 사람이었다고 말한 바 있는데, 과연 그러하였다. 끊이지 않는, 또 마땅히 받을 까닭도 없는 불운을 겪으면서도 불쾌해지지 않는 사람이라면 참으로 단단한 재목으로 지어진 사람이어야 한다. 이 사람은 슬픔에 그만큼 굴복하여, 슬픔이 그에게 뚜렷하고 지워지지 않는 흔적을 남겨 놓았다. 그는 사교를 대수롭게 여기지 않았고, 그것을 대수롭게 여기는 사람들은 악을 행하는 것이라고 판단하였다. 그는 이 슬픔들이 하느님의 손에서 온 것이며 결국에는 제 복을 위하여 작용할 것임을 사실로써 알았고 온 마음으로 믿었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그 슬픔들은 그를 음침하고 말없고 완고한 사람으로 만들었다. 그는 늘 마음속에 자기와 제 식구가 부당한 대접을 받아 왔다는 느낌을 품고 있었고, 너무나 자주 악마의 부추김을 받아, 이승의 삶이 그토록 고르지 못하게 만들어 놓은 것을 영원이 고르게 만들어 줄 것이라는 확신으로 스스로를 달랬으니—그것은 악마가 제 낚싯대와 낚싯줄을 벗어나려 몸부림치는 자들을 낚으려고 드리우는 마지막 미끼다.\n\n프램리의 소유지는 호글스톡 교구까지 뻗어 있지 않았다. 그러나 그럼에도 레이디 러프턴은 이 새로 온 사람들에게 친절을 베푸는 쪽으로 할 수 있는 일을 다 하였다. 섭리는 호글스톡에 레이디 러프턴을 내려 주지 않았고, 경이든 부인이든 지주든 지주 부인이든 그런 이를 대신할 사람도 내려 주지 않았다. 호글스톡의 농부들은 남자든 여자든 거칠고 무례한 무리여서, 그 사회적 지위가 신사 축에 드는 농부에 닿지 못하였다. 레이디 러프턴은 이것을 알고 있었고, 또 수석 사제의 부인인 애러빈 부인에게서 이 크롤리네 이야기를 얼마간 들었으므로, 제 등잔들의 심지를 돋우어 그 빛이 더 넓게 퍼지게 하고 그 힘의 얼마쯤을 저 쓸쓸한 집안에 쏟아 붓게 하였다.\n\n그리고 크롤리 부인에 관하여 말하자면, 레이디 러프턴은 제 수고와 호의가 헛되이 버려졌다고는 결코 여기지 않았다. 크롤리 부인은 그 친절을 고맙게 받아들였고, 그 손길 아래에서 삶의 부드러움을 얼마간 되찾았다. 프램리 코트에서 만찬을 함께하는 일이야 어림도 없었다. 다른 사정들이 알맞고 갖출 것이 다 갖추어져 있다 하여도 크롤리 씨가 그런 말은 들으려 하지 않을 것임을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 실상 크롤리 부인 자신도 곧, 그런 격식을 조금이라도 편안한 마음으로 치러 낼 만한 사람이 못 된다고 느낀다고 말하였다. 수석 사제는 두 사람이 수석 사제 관저에 와서 머물자는 말을 하곤 한다고 그녀는 말하였다. 그러나 두 사람 가운데 어느 쪽도 그것조차 견뎌 내지는 못할 것이라고 여겼다. 그러나 그래도 레이디 러프턴은 그녀에게 위로가 되었고, 그 가난한 여인은 어려울 때를 위하여 가까이에 부인 한 분이 있다는 것이 좋은 일이라고 느꼈다.\n\n크롤리 씨에게는 그 일이 훨씬 더 어려웠으나, 그에게도 아주 성과가 없지는 않았다. 레이디 러프턴은 그와 더불어 그의 교구와 제 교구의 이야기를 나누었고, 마크 로바츠를 그에게 보냈으며, 차츰차츰 그를 문명화하는 쪽으로 무언가를 이루어 냈다. 그와 로바츠 사이에도 우정이라기보다는 친밀함이 자라났다. 로바츠는 교회법이며 심지어 신학의 법에 관한 문제에서라면 그의 견해에 몸을 굽혔고, 참을성 있게 그의 말을 들었고, 동의할 수 있는 데서는 동의하였고, 동의할 수 없는 데서는 부드럽게 의견을 달리하였다. 로바츠는 누구에게나 자기를 상냥하게 보이는 사람이었으니 말이다. 그리하여 레이디 러프턴의 날개 아래에서 프램리와 호글스톡 사이에 하나의 연이 자라났고, 여기에는 로바츠 부인도 힘을 보탰다.\n\n그리고 이제 레이디 러프턴이 주위를 둘러보며, 여우 사냥이나 하는 제 교구의 변절한 목사에게 어떻게 하면 온당한 성직자의 힘이 가장 잘 미치게 할 수 있을까를 살피고 있을 때, 이 일에 크롤리 씨를 쓰면 되겠다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 크롤리 씨는 견해로 말하자면 틀림없이 제 편일 것이고, 그 견해를 동료 성직자에게 밝히기를 두려워하지도 않을 것이다. 그리하여 그녀는 크롤리 씨를 부르러 사람을 보냈다.\n\n겉모습으로 보자면 그는 마크 로바츠와 정반대였다. 그는 마르고 가늘고 여윈 사람으로, 어깨가 조금 굽었고, 창백하고 축 처진 긴 머리채는 텁수룩하였다. 이마는 높았으나 얼굴은 좁았고, 작은 회색 눈은 머릿속으로 깊이 꺼져 있었으며, 코는 잘생겼고, 입술은 얇았고, 입에는 뜻이 어려 있었다. 그의 얼굴을 보고서 그 표정에 어떤 목적과 뜻이 담겨 있음을 알아채지 못할 사람은 없었다. 그는 여름이나 겨울이나 늘 길고 흐릿한 회색 외투를 입었는데, 목까지 단추를 촘촘히 채워 올리고 자락은 발꿈치까지 내려왔다. 키는 꼬박 여섯 피트였으나, 몸이 하도 가늘어서 그보다 더 커 보였다.\n\n그는 레이디 러프턴의 부름을 받고 곧 왔는데, 하인 옆에 나란히 이륜마차에 몸을 올려놓고서 오는 동안 그에게 한마디도 말을 걸지 않았다. 그리고 그 하인은 그의 얼굴을 들여다보고서 그 말없음에 놀랐다. 마크 로바츠라면 호글스톡에서 프램리 코트까지 오는 길 내내 그와 이야기를 나누었을 것이다. 한편으로는 말과 땅에 관하여, 그러나 또 한편으로는 더 높은 것들에 관하여 논하면서.\n\n그러고 나서 레이디 러프턴은 크롤리 씨에게 속마음을 열고 제 근심을 털어놓았는데, 그러면서도 이야기 내내 로바츠 씨가 교구 성직자로서는 훌륭하다는 것을 힘주어 말하였다. “교회 안에서의 그 사람은 바로 내가 바라는 그대로의 성직자예요.” 그녀는 이렇게 설명하였으니, 교회의 가르침에 관한 크롤리 씨의 남다른 생각이 튀어나오는 것을 막고 그를 지금 다루려는 한 가지 사안에만 붙들어 두기 위함이었다. “하지만 그 사람이 이 성직록을 너무 젊어서 얻었어요, 크롤리 씨. 그래서 내가 바라는 만큼 무겁게 처신하지는 못하고 있어요. 그이를 그렇게 이른 나이에 그런 자리에 앉힌 것은 그이의 잘못이나 다름없이 내 잘못이기도 해요.”\n\n“그렇다고 생각합니다.” 크롤리 씨가 말하였는데, 그런 주제에는 아마 조금 속이 쓰렸을 것이다.\n\n“그렇지요, 그렇지요.” 마님은 어떤 노여움을 꿀꺽 삼키며 말을 이었다. “하지만 그건 이미 지나간 일이고 이제 어쩔 수 없는 일이에요. 로바츠 씨가 자기 직분에 자랑거리가 될 사람이 되리라는 것은 나도 의심하지 않아요. 그이의 마음은 제자리에 있고 그이의 심정은 착하니까요. 그렇지만 지금 그이가 유혹에 굴복하고 있는 것이 아닌가 나는 걱정이에요.”\n\n“한 주에 두세 번씩 사냥을 나간다고 저는 들었습니다. 우리 주위에서는 누구나 그 이야기를 하고 있습니다.”\n\n“아니에요, 크롤리 씨. 한 주에 두세 번은 아니에요. 한 번을 넘는 일은 아주 드물다고 나는 생각해요. 그리고 나는 그이가 무엇보다도 러프턴 경과 함께 있으려는 뜻으로 그러는 것이라고 정말로 믿어요.”\n\n“그것이 이 일을 더 낫게 만들어 준다고는 보이지 않습니다.” 크롤리 씨가 말하였다.\n\n“그것은 그이가, 내가 성직자에게는 악덕이라고 여기지 않을 수 없는 그 취미에 깊이 물들어 있지는 않다는 것을 보여 주는 셈이지요.”\n\n“그것은 모든 사람에게 악덕일 것입니다.” 크롤리 씨가 말하였다. “그것은 그 자체로 잔인하며, 게으름과 방탕으로 이끕니다.”\n\n레이디 러프턴은 다시 한 번 꿀꺽 삼켰다. 그녀는 크롤리 씨를 제 도움으로 삼으려고 그곳에 불렀으니, 그와 다투는 것은 이롭지 못하리라고 느꼈다. 그러나 제 아들의 소일거리가 게으르고 방탕하다는 말을 듣는 것은 좋지 않았다. 그녀는 사냥이란 늘 시골 신사에게 알맞은 소일거리라고 여겨 왔다. 실로 그녀의 눈에 그것은 영국 시골 생활의 남다른 제도 가운데 하나였으며, 그녀가 바셋주 사냥회를 무언가 신성한 것으로 보았다고 해도 거의 틀린 말이 아니었다. 그녀는 여우가 덫에 걸렸다는 이야기는 견디지 못하였고, 제 칠면조를 슬쩍 물어 가도 신음 소리 한 번 내지 않고 내주었다. 사정이 그러하니, 그것이 악덕이라는 말을 듣는 것이 좋지 않았고, 크롤리 씨에게 그 문제를 두고 의논할 마음은 조금도 없었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그녀는 노여움을 꿀꺽 삼켰다.\n\n“그것은 어쨌든 성직자에게 어울리지 않는 일이에요.” 그녀가 말하였다. “그리고 로바츠 씨가 선생의 견해를 높이 여기는 것을 내가 알고 있으니, 그이에게 그 일을 그만두라고 권해 주시는 것을 마다하지는 않으시겠지요. 그런 문제에 내가 몸소 끼어들면 그이가 서운하게 여길지도 모르겠어요.”\n\n“서운하게 여길 것이 틀림없습니다.” 크롤리 씨가 말하였다. “그런 주제로 성직자에게 충고를 하는 것은 여인의 소관이 아닙니다. 그 여인이 그에게 아주 가깝고 아주 소중한 사람이 아니라면 말입니다—아내나 어머니나 누이라면 모르되.”\n\n“같은 교구에 살고 있고, 또 어쩌면—” 그 교구에서 가장 앞자리에 있는 사람이며 자연히 다른 이들을 다스리는 사람이라고. 마님의 생각을 나타내기에 알맞은 말은 그런 것이었으리라. 그러나 그녀는 스스로를 돌아보고 그 말을 쓰지 않았다. 제 힘이 아무리 크게 미쳐야 마땅하다 하여도, 로바츠 씨에게 그의 해롭고 성직자답지 못한 습관을 두고 말할 알맞은 사람은 자기가 아니라고 그녀는 마음을 정해 둔 터였다. 그러니 이제 자기가 알맞은 사람임을 증명하려 들어 그 결심에서 벗어나려 하지는 않았다.\n\n“그렇습니다.” 크롤리 씨가 말하였다. “바로 그러합니다. 그만한 사정이라면, 그가 부인의 생활 방식이 충고를 요할 만하다고 여길 때 부인께 충고를 드릴 자격은 그에게 주겠습니다만, 부인께서 그에게 말을 붙이시는 일을 정당하게 만들어 주지는 결코 못합니다.”\n\n이것은 레이디 러프턴에게 몹시 가혹한 일이었다. 그녀는 여인의 힘을 다하여 최선을 다하려 애쓰고 있었고, 또 그리하면서도 죄지은 이의 마음이 상하지 않도록 애쓰고 있었다. 그런데도 도움을 청하여 불러낸 영적 위로자가 그녀를 마치 오만하고 남을 눌러 대는 사람인 양 대하는 것이었다. 그녀는 크롤리 씨의 도움을 청함으로써 제 교구 성직자를 대하는 제 처지의 약함을 스스로 인정한 셈이었다. 그런 사정이라면 그도 적어도 그 약함을 대놓고 면박하는 짓만은 삼갈 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n“글쎄요, 선생. 내 생활 방식이 그런 것을 요하지 않기를 바라요. 그렇지만 그것은 지금 이야기의 요점이 아니에요. 내가 알고 싶은 것은, 선생이 로바츠 씨에게 말해 주실 것인가 하는 것이에요.”\n\n“물론 말하겠습니다.” 그가 말하였다.\n\n“그러면 참으로 고맙겠어요. 하지만, 크롤리 씨, 부디—부디 이것만은 잊지 말아 주세요. 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 선생이 그이에게 가혹하게 대하시기를 바라지 않아요. 그이는 훌륭한 젊은이고, 또—”\n\n“레이디 러프턴, 제가 이 일을 한다면 제 방식대로, 제가 할 수 있는 만큼, 그때 하느님께서 제게 주시는 말로써 할 수밖에 없습니다. 저는 어느 누구에게도 가혹하지 않기를 바랍니다. 그러나 어떤 경우에든 진실 아닌 것을 말하는 것은 쓸데없기보다 더 나쁜 일입니다.”\n\n“물론이지요—물론이지요.”\n\n“귀가 너무 여려서 진실을 듣지 못한다면, 그 마음은 너무 뒤틀려서 진실로 이로움을 얻지 못할 것입니다.” 그러고 나서 크롤리 씨는 물러가려고 일어섰다.\n\n그러나 레이디 러프턴은 그가 자기와 함께 점심을 먹어야 한다고 우겼다. 그는 우물쭈물 어물어물하며 사양하고 싶어 하였으나, 이 문제에 있어서는 그녀가 단호하였다. 성직자에게 그 직분을 두고 충고할 만한 사람은 못 될지 몰라도, 손을 대접하는 문제라면 그녀는 자기가 무엇을 하는지 알고 있었다. 크롤리 씨가 요기도 하지 않고 이 집을 떠나서는 안 되었다. 이 일에서는 그녀가 뜻을 이루었다. 그리고 크롤리 씨는—앞에 놓인 것이 교구 사제와 그 교구민의 상대적 지위가 아니라 차게 식힌 구운 소고기와 뜨거운 감자가 되고 나니—겸손하고 순순하며 거의 수줍은 사람이 되었다. 레이디 러프턴이 셰리 대신 마데이라를 권하니 크롤리 씨는 이내 그대로 따랐고, 실은 그 차이를 조금도 알아채지 못하였다. 그러고 나서 이륜마차에는 크롤리 부인에게 보내는 갯양배추 한 바구니가 놓였다. 그것은 그가 감히 그럴 수 있었다면 두고 갔을 것이나, 감히 그러지 못하였다. 갯양배추 아래에 숨겨 놓은 아이들 몫의 마멀레이드에 대해서는 그에게 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 그것이야 그가 거들지 않아도 제 갈 곳으로 찾아갈 것임을 레이디 러프턴이 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그리하여 크롤리 씨는 프램리 코트의 이륜마차를 타고 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n이 일이 있은 지 사나흘 뒤에 그는 프램리 목사관까지 걸어갔다. 그날을 토요일로 잡은 것은 사냥개들이 그날은 결코 사냥을 나가지 않는다는 것을 알아 두었기 때문이다. 그리고 로바츠 씨가 교구 일을 보러 나가기 전에 꼭 붙잡을 수 있도록 일찍 나섰다. 이 목적을 이루기에는 넉넉히 이른 시각이었으니, 아홉 시 반쯤에 목사관 문에 이르렀을 때 교구 사제는 아내와 누이와 더불어 막 아침 식탁에 앉는 참이었다.\n\n“아니 크롤리,” 상대가 미처 입을 열기도 전에 로바츠가 말하였다. “참 훌륭한 분이에요.” 그러고서 그는 그를 의자에 앉혔고, 로바츠 부인이 그에게 차를 따라 주었고, 루시가 나이프와 접시를 그에게 내주었으니, 그가 무슨 명목으로 이 자리에 온 것을 변명해야 할지 미처 알아내기도 전이었다.\n\n“이렇게 불쑥 찾아온 것을 용서해 주시기 바랍니다.” 마침내 그가 나직이 중얼거렸다. “그러나 이따가 잠시 귀를 기울여 주셨으면 하는 일 이야기가 몇 마디 있습니다.”\n\n“물론이지요.” 로바츠가 크롤리 씨 앞의 접시에 구운 콩팥을 옮겨 놓으며 말하였다. “하지만 일 이야기를 하는 데에 좋은 아침 식사만 한 준비가 없어요. 루시, 크롤리 씨에게 버터 바른 토스트를 드려라. 달걀, 패니. 달걀은 어디 있지?” 그러자 제복을 입은 존이 새로 삶은 달걀을 들여왔다. “이제 되었군. 나는 달걀을 늘 뜨거울 때 먹어요, 크롤리. 당신도 그렇게 하기를 권해요.”\n\n이 모든 말에 크롤리 씨는 거의 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 이런 자리에서 조금도 편안하지 못하였다. 어쩌면 자기 식탁에 두고 온 식사와 지금 눈앞에 보이는 식사의 차이, 그리고 그런 차이가 생겨난 까닭이 무엇일까 하는 한 가닥 생각이 그의 머릿속을 지나갔을지도 모른다. 그러나 그렇다 하여도 그것은 아주 잠깐 스쳐 가는 생각이었으니, 이제 그의 마음을 온통 차지하고 있는 것은 다른 문제였다. 그러고 나서 아침 식사가 끝났고, 몇 분 뒤에 두 성직자는 목사관 서재에 마주 앉게 되었다.\n\n“로바츠 씨.” 나이가 위인 쪽이 책이 잘 채워진 서재 탁자 저쪽 편의 보통 의자 하나에 불편하게 자리를 잡고서 말을 시작하였다. 한편 마크는 난롯가 제 안락의자에 편하게 앉아 있었다. “저는 불쾌한 일로 당신을 찾아왔습니다.”\n\n마크의 마음은 곧 사워비 씨의 어음으로 날아갔으나, 크롤리 씨가 그 일과 무슨 관계가 있을 수 있다고는 생각할 수 없었다.\n\n“그러나 동료 성직자로서, 또 당신을 크게 존중하고 당신이 잘되기를 바라는 사람으로서, 저는 이 일을 손에 쥐어야 한다고 생각하였습니다.”\n\n“무슨 일이에요, 크롤리?”\n\n“로바츠 씨, 사람들이 당신의 지금 생활 방식은 그리스도의 군대의 병사에게 어울리지 않는다고 말합니다.”\n\n“사람들이 그렇게 말한다고요! 어떤 사람들이 말입니까?”\n\n“당신 주위 사람들, 당신과 한 이웃에 사는 사람들입니다. 당신의 삶을 지켜보며 당신이 하는 일을 다 아는 사람들, 당신이 제 발을 밝혀 줄 등불처럼 걸어가는 것을 보려고 우러러보다가, 정작 말 거간꾼과 사냥꾼들과 어울리고 사냥개를 뒤쫓아 말을 달리며 세상 낙을 좇는 가장 헛된 자들 사이에 자리를 잡고 있는 것을 보게 되는 사람들입니다. 착한 삶의 본을 기대할 권리가 있는데도 그것을 보지 못한다고 여기는 사람들입니다.”\n\n크롤리 씨는 곧바로 일의 뿌리로 들어갔고, 그리함으로써 자기가 할 일을 그만큼 더 쉽게 만든 것이 분명하였다. 불쾌한 일거리를 손에 쥐었을 때에는 곧바로 일의 뿌리로 들어가는 것만 한 것이 없다.\n\n“그런 사람들이 당신을 대리로 여기 보낸 것입니까?”\n\n“아무도 저를 대리로 보내지 않았고, 또 보낼 수도 없습니다. 저는 다른 누구의 마음이 아니라 제 마음을 말하러 왔습니다. 그러나 제가 당신 주위 사람들이 생각하고 말하는 것을 끌어대는 것은, 당신의 직분이 바로 그들에게 돌아가야 할 것이기 때문입니다. 당신은 주위 사람들에게 경건하고 깨끗한 삶을 살아야 할 빚을 지고 있습니다. 또한 훨씬 더 높은 뜻으로는 하늘에 계신 당신의 아버지께도 그 빚을 지고 있습니다. 이제 저는 감히 묻겠습니다. 당신은 그러한 삶을 살려고 최선을 다하고 있습니까?” 그러고 나서 그는 대답을 기다리며 말없이 있었다.\n\n그는 남다른 사람이었다. 그토록 겸손하고 온순하며, 세상의 흔한 사귐에서는 말할 수 없이 무능하고 서툴렀으나, 그의 마음이 매달려 있는 그 한 가지 주제에서는 그토록 담대하고 진취적이며 거의 웅변에 이르렀으니! 그는 그 자리에 앉아, 꺼져 든 회색 눈으로 상대의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 그 눈길에 그 희생자는 움츠러들었다. 그러고 나서 그는 제 말을 되풀이하였다. “이제 저는 감히 묻겠습니다, 로바츠 씨. 당신은 교구민들 가운데 있는 교구 성직자에게 어울릴 만한 삶을 살려고 최선을 다하고 있습니까?” 그리고 그는 다시 대답을 기다리며 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“그 물음에 그렇다고 마음 놓고 대답할 수 있는 사람은 우리 가운데 몇 없을 것입니다.” 마크가 나직한 목소리로 말하였다.\n\n“그러나 우리 가운데 그 물음에 당신만큼 대답을 못 하는 사람이 많다고 생각하십니까? 또 많다 하여도, 당신처럼 젊고 진취적이며 재능 있는 사람이 그런 무리에 함께 헤아려지는 것으로 만족하겠습니까? 그리스도의 갑주를 몸에 걸치고 나서 버림받은 자가 되는 것으로 만족하십니까? 그렇다고 말씀하신다면 제가 당신을 잘못 본 것이니, 저는 제 길을 가겠습니다.” 다시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그러고 나서 그는 말을 이었다. “제게 말해 주십시오, 형제여. 그리고 될 수 있다면 마음을 열어 주십시오.” 그리고 의자에서 일어나 방을 건너와 마크의 어깨에 다정하게 손을 얹었다.\n\n마크는 의자에 기대어 앉아 있었고, 처음에는 잠깐 동안만 뻔뻔하게 밀고 나가 볼까 생각하였다. 그러나 뻔뻔하게 버틸 생각은 이제 온통 그를 떠났다. 그는 편안한 자리에서 몸을 일으켜 탁자에 팔꿈치를 짚고 앞으로 기울여 앉아 있었으나, 이 말을 듣고 나서는 두 팔 위로 고개를 떨어뜨리고 두 손 사이에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n“이것은 무서운 타락입니다.” 크롤리가 말을 이었다. “떨어지는 것이 무섭고, 돌아오기가 그토록 어려우니 곱절로 무섭습니다. 그러나 저 생각 없는 죄인들 사이에 스스로를 하나로 놓아두는 것이 당신에게 만족스러울 수는 없습니다. 당신은 바로 그들의 죄를 짓밟기 위하여 그들 가운데 세워진 사람입니다. 당신은 사냥하는 목사가 되어, 하느님을 모욕하는 자들과 비웃는 마귀들 사이에서 기쁜 마음으로 말을 달립니다—그토록 뜻이 높았던 당신이, 그리스도의 종의 직분을 그토록 자주 그토록 훌륭히 말하던 당신이, 제 교회의 자잘한 세목을 두고 자랑스럽게 논할 수 있는 당신이, 마치 그 교회의 크고 단순한 가르침의 넓음만으로는 당신의 정력에 모자라기라도 하다는 듯이 말입니다! 그 뜨거운 논쟁들 내내 제 옆에 위선자가 있었을 리 없습니다!”\n\n“위선자는 아닙니다—위선자는 아닙니다.” 마크가 거의 흐느끼는 데까지 내려앉은 목소리로 말하였다.\n\n“그러나 버림받은 자입니다! 제가 당신을 그렇게 불러야 하겠습니까? 아닙니다, 로바츠 씨, 버림받은 자가 아닙니다. 위선자도 버림받은 자도 아니고, 걸어가다가 어둠 속에서 발을 헛디뎌 돌에 발이 상한 사람입니다. 이제부터 그가 손에 등불을 들고 제 길을 조심스럽게 살피며 가시덤불과 바위 사이를 삼가 걸어가게 하십시오—삼가면서도 담대하게, 사내다운 용기로, 그러나 그리스도인의 온순함으로, 모든 사람이 이 눈물의 골짜기를 지나는 순례길에서 마땅히 걸어가야 하는 대로 말입니다.” 그러고 나서 상대가 그를 붙들 틈도 주지 않고 그는 방에서, 그리고 그 집에서 서둘러 나갔고, 그 집안의 다른 사람들을 다시 보지도 않은 채 호글스톡으로 돌아가는 길을 성큼성큼 걸어갔으니, 자기가 받아 온 소임을 다하느라 이렇게 깊은 진흙을 십사 마일이나 터벅터벅 걸은 것이었다.\n\n로바츠 씨가 제 방을 나온 것은 몇 시간이 지나서였다. 크롤리가 정말로 가 버려서 다시 그를 볼 일이 없다는 것을 알자마자, 그는 방문을 잠그고 앉아 제 지금의 삶을 곰곰이 생각하였다. 열한 시쯤에 아내가 문을 두드렸는데, 그가 떠나는 것을 아무도 보지 못하였으므로 그 낯설고 이상한 성직자가 아직 거기 있는지 없는지 몰랐던 것이다. 그러나 마크는 명랑하게 대답하며, 공부하도록 자기를 혼자 두어 달라고 하였다.\n\n그때 그가 한 생각과 마음속의 결심이 그에게 도움이 되었기를 바라자.\n\n# 제16장. 포진스 부인의 아기\n\n사냥철은 이제 거의 다 지나갔고, 바셋주 세계의 높은 분들은 런던의 영광을 생각하고 있었다. 그 영광을 두고 레이디 러프턴은 언제나 마음이 여간 편치 않았다. 그분으로서는 한 해를 통째로 프램리 코트에 머물러 있고 싶었다. 다만 어떤 중대한 고려들이, 그런 처사는 스스로 보기에 온당치 못하다고 일러 주었을 뿐이다. 그분이 들어 온 역대 레이디 러프턴들은, 미망인 마님이든 그 이전 대의 마님이든, 늙어서 그런 일을 감당할 수 없게 될 때까지는 언제나 런던에서 사교철을 보냈다. 아니, 더러는 그 시기가 이미 한참 지난 뒤까지도 그러했다. 게다가 그분에게는, 어쩌면 아주 틀린 것도 아닌 생각이 하나 있었다. 자기가 해마다 그 시대의 흘러가는 문명을 조금씩 시골로 실어 들여 온다는 생각이었다. 아니, 확실히 틀리지 않은 생각이라고 말해도 되지 않을까? 그렇지 않다면 어떻게 새로 나온 모자의 모양이며 여인들의 허리를 새로 고친 선이 농사 짓는 지방까지 내려오고, 시골 사람의 눈이 우아함과 아름다움을 알아보는 법을 배우겠는가? 새로 고친 허리선이며 새 모자 같은 것은 도시에나 두는 편이 낫다고 생각하는 이들도 있다. 그러나 그런 사람들이 자기 논리를 끝까지 따라간다면, 쟁기 몰이 소년들에게는 붉은 흙물을 칠하고 우유 짜는 처녀들에게는 가죽을 걸치게 하고 싶어질 것이다.\n\n이런저런 이유로 레이디 러프턴은 늘 사월에 런던으로 올라가 유월 초까지 거기 머물렀다. 그러나 그분에게 이 기간은 대개 고행의 시간이었다. 런던에서 그분은 그리 대단한 인물이 아니었다. 그분은 그런 종류의 위세를 얻으려고 애쓴 적이 한 번도 없었고, 후원자 마님으로도, 사교계 유행 내각의 여성 국무 대신으로도 빛나지 않았다. 런던에서 그분은 답답하고 맥이 없었으며 마음에 맞는 소일거리도 없었다. 그래서 프램리에서 무슨 일이 되어 가고 있다는 소식을 읽을 때, 또 같은 종류의 시골 소식을 더 보내라고 분부를 적어 보낼 때가 가장 행복한 순간이었다.\n\n그러나 이번만은 그분의 런던행에 그 자체의 흥미를 얹어 줄, 사활이 걸린 일이 하나 있었다. 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 손님으로 맞아, 될 수 있는 데까지 아들이 그리젤다의 곁에 머무르게 하는 일이었다. 작전 계획은 이러했다. 우선 그랜틀리 부인과 부주교가 그리젤다를 데리고 한 달 동안 런던으로 올라간다. 그러고 나서 그들이 플럼스테드로 돌아갈 때 그리젤다는 레이디 러프턴에게로 온다. 이 배치가 레이디 러프턴의 마음에 모든 점에서 흡족한 것은 아니었다. 그랜틀리 부인이 러프턴-그랜틀리 집안 조약의 조건을 생각한다면 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 하틀탑 쪽 사람들에게 딱 잘라 등을 돌리지는 않고 있다는 것을 그분은 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 그랜틀리 부인 쪽에서는, 러프턴 경이 사랑을 만들고 밝히는 일에 어찌 그렇게 더디냐고, 그리고 활에 걸린 시위 하나가 어딘가 의심스러울 때에는 시위를 둘 걸어 두는 것이 절대로 필요하다고 변명할 수도 있었을 것이다. 그랜틀리 부인이 루시 로바츠와의 그 딱한 플라토닉한 우정에 대해 무슨 소리를 들은 것은 아닐까?\n\n삼월 말이 다 될 무렵 그랜틀리 부인에게서 편지 한 통이 왔다. 그 편지는 레이디 러프턴의 불안을 크게 더해 주었고, 그분이 직접 그 무대 위에 서서 그리젤다를 자기 손안에 두고 싶어 하는 마음을 어느 때보다 간절하게 만들었다. 런던 세계 전반과 특히 러프턴-그랜틀리 세계에 관한, 그저 예사로운 정도의 소식 몇 가지를 전한 뒤에, 그랜틀리 부인은 딸에 관해 은밀히 이렇게 적었다.\n\n“아니라고 해도 소용없는 일이겠지요.” 어머니의 자랑과 어머니의 겸손을 함께 담아 그분은 이렇게 썼다. “그 아이는 아주 많이들 칭찬해요. 제가 데리고 다닐 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 데서 청함을 받고, 저로서는 결코 가고 싶지 않은 집에서까지 청함을 받아요. 레이디 하틀탑의 첫 무도회만은 그 아이한테 거절할 수가 없었어요. 올해에 그만한 것은 달리 없을 테니까요. 그리고 물론 친애하는 레이디 러프턴, 그 아이가 당신과 함께 있을 때에는 그 집은 아예 이야기 밖의 일이겠지요. 저 역시 저 혼자만 걸린 문제라면 그럴 거예요. 물론 공작도 거기 있었어요. 온 세상이 다 모인 자리에서 레이디 하틀탑이 자기 응접실에서 좀 더 삼가지 않는 것이 저는 참으로 이상해요. 덤벨로 경이 그리젤다를 제 바람보다 훨씬 더 흠모하고 있다는 것은 제게 분명해요. 그 아이는, 착한 것이, 분별이 어찌나 좋은지 그런 일로 머리가 돌아가지는 않으리라고 저는 생각해요. 하지만 그런 남자의 흠모라면 마음이 흔들리지 않을 처녀가 몇이나 될까요? 아시다시피 후작은 몸이 몹시 약해졌고, 요즘 이 건축 열풍이 시작된 뒤로 랭커셔 재산이 연 이십만 파운드가 넘는다고들 해요!! 덤벨로 경이 그 아이에게 말을 많이 한 것 같지는 않아요. 사실 그이는 누구에게든 말을 많이 하는 사람이 아닌 듯해요. 하지만 그이는 늘 그 아이와 짝을 지어 춤을 추려고 일어서고, 그 아이가 그이가 마음에 걸릴 만한 다른 상대와 짝을 지어 일어서면 불안해하며 안절부절못하는 것이 저는 보여요. 요전 밤 던스터블 양의 집에서, 그리젤다가 우리가 아는 어떤 친구와 춤을 추고 있을 때 그이를 보는 것은 정말이지 민망한 일이었어요. 하지만 그 아이는 그날 저녁 참 좋아 보였고, 저는 그 아이가 그토록 생기 있는 것을 좀처럼 본 적이 없어요!”\n\n이 모든 것, 그리고 같은 편지에 담긴 같은 종류의 이야기가 잔뜩, 레이디 러프턴으로 하여금 런던에 있고 싶게 만들었다. 아주 확실한 것은 — 아무튼 그것만은 의심할 데가 없었다 — 그리젤다가 레이디 러프턴의 보호 아래로 옮겨 오고 나면 레이디 하틀탑의 겉만 번드레한 호사는 다시 보지 못하게 된다는 것이었다. 그리고 그분, 레이디 러프턴은, 그랜틀리 부인이 딸을 그런 집에 데리고 갔다는 것이 참으로 이상했다. 레이디 하틀탑에 관한 일은 온 세상이 다 알고 있었다. 옴니엄 공작을 언제나 만날 수 있는 런던의 집이 거기 하나뿐이라는 것도 알려져 있었다. 레이디 러프턴 자신으로서는 어린 처녀를 개서럼 성에 데리고 가는 편이 그보다 나을 것 같지도 않았다. 그런 까닭들로 그분은 벗인 그랜틀리 부인에게 적잖이 노여운 마음이 들었다. 그러나 그랜틀리 부인의 편지가 바로 그런 감정을 일으키려고, 곧 마님을 깨워 움직이게 하려는 뚜렷한 뜻으로 쓰였다는 데까지는 그분이 충분히 셈을 하지 못했던 것 같다. 사실 이런 일에서는 그랜틀리 부인이 레이디 러프턴보다 한결 유능한 여자였다. 자기 갈 길을 보고 그 길을 끝까지 따라가는 데 더 유능했다. 그분의 마음에는 러프턴-그랜틀리 결합이 최상이었다. 돈이 전부라고 여기지는 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 그것이 안 될 경우, 하틀탑-그랜틀리 결합도 나쁘지 않았다. 활에 걸어 두는 두 번째 시위로 여기자면, 조금도 나쁘지 않다고 그분은 생각했다.\n\n레이디 러프턴의 답장은 매우 다정했다. 그리젤다가 즐겁게 지낸다는 것을 알아 얼마나 기쁜지 모르겠다고 썼다. 덤벨로 경은 세상에 바보로 알려진 사람이고 그 어머니는 — 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼보다 조금도 나을 것이 없는 사람이라고 슬쩍 끼워 넣었다. 그러고는 사정이 생겨 생각보다 나흘 일찍 런던에 올라가게 되었으니, 친애하는 그리젤다가 당장 자기에게로 와 주기를 바란다고 덧붙였다. 러프턴 경은, 그분의 말로는, 브루턴 스트리트에서 잠을 자지는 않겠지만 — 레이디 러프턴은 브루턴 스트리트에 살았다 — 의회의 직무가 허락하는 한 되도록 많은 시간을 거기서 보내겠다고 약속했다는 것이었다.\n\n아, 레이디 러프턴! 레이디 러프턴! 그 마지막 말을 쓰면서, 그것이 받는 이의 마음에 그토록 센 효험을 내기를 바라면서, 당신은 지금 한바탕 — 거짓부렁을 늘어놓고 있다는 생각이 들지 않았던가? 당신이 아들에게 당신다운 살갑고 다정한 어머니의 말투로 이렇게 말한 것이 사실이 아니었던가. “루도빅, 올해도 브루턴 스트리트에서 네 얼굴을 좀 볼 수 있겠지? 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 나와 함께 있을 텐데, 그 아이를 심심하게 두어서는 안 되잖니?” 그러고서 아들은 “아, 그러죠, 어머니.” 하고 대답한 뒤, 그리 곱지 않은 태도로 방을 어슬렁어슬렁 나가지 않았던가? 아들이든 당신이든 의회의 직무에 대해 한마디라도 한 적이 있었던가? 한마디도 없었다! 아, 레이디 러프턴! 당신은 지금 벗에게 거짓부렁을 적어 보낸 것이 아닌가?\n\n요즘 우리는 아이들에게 진실에 대해 매우 엄해지고 있다. 열 살, 열두 살, 열네 살 나이에 도덕적 용기가 본래 얼마나 약한지를 생각하면 때로는 무섭게 엄하다. 그러나 우리 어른들이 우리 자신의 참과 거짓을 다스리는 엄격함의 도량이 조금이라도 늘고 있는지는 나는 모르겠다. 내가 아이들의 거짓을 옹호한다고 여겨지는 일은 결코 없기를 바란다. 그러나 거짓말은 부모에게보다 아이들에게 더 용서할 만한 것이다. 레이디 러프턴의 거짓부렁은 흔히 봐줄 만한 것으로 여겨지는 종류였다 — 적어도 어른들 사이에서는 그렇다. 그러나 그럼에도, 진실에만 머물 수 있었더라면 그분은 완전함에 더 가까웠을 것이다. 어떤 아이가 학교에서 집으로 편지를 써서, 다른 아이가 와서 함께 지내겠다고 약속했다고 말했는데 그 다른 아이는 그런 약속을 한 적이 없다고 해 보자. 그 첫째 아이는 목자와 스승들의 눈에 얼마나 못된 아이가 되겠는가!\n\n러프턴 경과 그 어머니 사이의 그 짧은 대화 — 나리의 의회 직무에 대해서는 한마디도 나오지 않았던 그 대화 — 는 그가 런던으로 떠나기 전날 저녁에 있었다. 그날 그는 확실히 기분이 좋은 편이 아니었고, 어머니에게 살가운 태도로 대하지도 않았다. 어머니가 그랜틀리 양 이야기를 꺼내자 그는 방을 나가 버렸다. 그리고 그날 저녁 다시 한 번, 어머니가 그리 현명하지 못하게 그리젤다의 아름다움에 대해 한두 마디를 하자, 그는 그 아가씨가 요술쟁이도 아닌데 템스강에 불을 지를 일이 어디 있겠느냐고 쏘아붙였다.\n\n“그 아이가 요술쟁이라면!” 적잖이 부아가 난 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “내가 지금 그 아이를 런던에서 데리고 다니려 하지도 않을 게다. 네가 요술쟁이라고 부르는 그런 아가씨들을 나도 많이 안다. 그이들은 한없이 떠들 수 있고, 늘 크게 떠들거나 아니면 귓속말로 떠들지. 나는 그이들이 싫고, 너도 마음속으로는 싫어할 것이 분명하다.”\n\n“아, 마음속으로 좋아하느냐 하는 것까지 따진다면 — 그건 너무 까다롭게 캐는 거예요.”\n\n“그리젤다 그랜틀리는 숙녀다. 숙녀이니 나는 그 아이를 런던에 데리고 있는 것이 기쁠 것이다. 저스티니아가 곁에 두고 싶어 할 만한 아가씨가 바로 그 아이지.”\n\n“그렇고말고요.” 러프턴 경이 말했다. “저스티니아한테는 아주 잘 맞을 겁니다.”\n\n이것은 러프턴 경으로서 마음씨 고운 말이 아니었다. 그리고 어머니는 그 말이 그가 러프턴-그랜틀리 결합에 등을 세우고 있다는 뜻으로 들렸으므로 그만큼 더 강하게 그것을 느꼈다. 자기를 잡으려고 무슨 계략이 깔리고 있다고 의심하게 되면 그가 꼭 그럴 것이라고 그분은 진작에 짐작하고 있었다. 그런데 이제 그가 정말로 그런 계략을 의심하는 것처럼 보였다. 그렇지 않다면 그리젤다가 누이에게는 아주 잘 맞겠다는 그 비꼬는 말이 어디서 나왔겠는가?\n\n이제 우리는 뒤로 돌아가, 나리의 언짢은 기분과 의심을 설명해 주고 그가 어머니에게 그토록 쌀쌀맞게 굴게 된 사연을 밝혀 줄 프램리의 작은 장면 하나를 그려야 한다. 이 장면은 로바츠 부인과 루시가 목사관 정원을 함께 걷던 그 저녁으로부터 열흘쯤 뒤에 있었다. 그 열흘 동안 루시는 그 젊은 귀족과 어떤 특별한 이야기에도 한 번도 끌려들지 않았다. 그 사이에 그녀는 프램리 코트에서 저녁을 먹었고 또 한 번 저녁을 거기서 보냈다. 러프턴 경도 세 번인가 네 번 목사관에 올라왔고, 그녀가 늘 다니는 산책길에서 그녀를 찾았다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴이 로바츠 부인에게 자기 두려움을 슬쩍 내비친 그날 이후로, 두 사람은 옛날의 그 허물없는 사이로 마주 앉은 적이 한 번도 없었다.\n\n러프턴 경은 그녀가 몹시 그리웠다. 처음에는 이 변화를 누군가의 계획된 처사로 돌리지 않았다. 사실 그는 짜증이 난다고 느끼기는 했으나 그 일을 그리 깊이 생각하지도 않았다. 그러나 떠날 날로 정해진 날이 가까워지자, 루시가 자기 어머니나 올케에게 몇 마디 하는 때가 아니면 그녀의 목소리를 도무지 듣지 못한다는 것이 몹시 이상하게 여겨졌다. 그래서 그는 떠나기 전에 그녀에게 말을 붙여, 그 알 수 없는 일의 사연을 자기에게 설명받아야겠다고 마음을 정했다.\n\n그리고 그는 그 뜻을 실행하여, 어느 날 오후 특별히 목사관을 찾아갔다. 그의 어머니가 그토록 때를 잘못 골라 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 칭송한 것은 바로 같은 날 저녁이었다. 로바츠는 그때 집을 떠나 있다는 것을 그는 알고 있었고, 로바츠 부인은 저택에 내려가 그의 어머니와 함께, 레이디 러프턴이 곧 집을 비우는 동안 특별히 돌보아야 할 가난한 이들의 명부를 만들고 있었다. 이를 이용해 그는 목사관 정원을 지나 거침없이 들어섰다. 정원사에게 아무렇지도 않은 목소리로 두 부인 중 누구라도 집에 있느냐고 물었고, 그러고서 바로 그 집 문간에서 딱하게도 루시를 붙잡았다.\n\n“들어가시는 길인가요, 나가시는 길인가요, 로바츠 양?”\n\n“글쎄요, 나가려던 길이었습니다.” 루시가 말했다. 그러고는 이 만남이 길어지는 것을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 면할 수 있을까 헤아리기 시작했다.\n\n“아, 나가시는 길이었군요. 제가 함께 가자고 해도 될지 어떨지는 모르겠지만 —”\n\n“그건 좀 아닐 듯합니다, 러프턴 경. 저는 지척에 사는 이웃 포진스 부인을 찾아가려는 길이니까요. 나리께서는 지금 당장 포진스 부인네나 그 갓난아기에게 특별히 볼일이 없으시겠지요?”\n\n“그럼 로바츠 양께서는 그쪽에 아주 특별한 볼일이 있으신가요?”\n\n“네, 특히 포진스 아기에게요. 포진스 아기는 정말 귀염둥이랍니다 — 태어난 지 이제 겨우 이틀이지요.” 그러면서 루시는 문간에 서서 이야기를 계속하지는 않겠다고 마음을 굳힌 듯이 한두 걸음 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n그것을 보자 그의 이마에 옅은 그늘이 스쳤고, 그녀가 뜻을 이루게 하지 않겠다는 마음이 굳어졌다. 루시 로바츠 같은 아가씨에게 그런 식으로 밀려날 그가 아니었다. 그는 그녀와 이야기하러 여기까지 왔고, 이야기를 하고야 말 것이었다. 적어도 그만한 것을 요구할 만큼의 친밀함은 두 사람 사이에 충분히 있었다.\n\n“로바츠 양,” 그가 말했다. “저는 내일 런던으로 떠납니다. 지금 작별 인사를 하지 않으면 아예 할 수가 없게 돼요.”\n\n“안녕히 가십시오, 러프턴 경.” 그녀는 손을 내주며, 옛날 그대로의 그 살갑고 너그럽고 거침없는 웃음을 그에게 지어 보이며 말했다. “그리고 제 어린 병아리들을 지켜 주겠다고 저에게 약속하신 그 법, 잊지 말고 의회에 내십시오.”\n\n그는 그녀의 손을 잡았으나, 그가 원한 것이 그것뿐은 아니었다. “포진스 부인과 그 아기가 십 분쯤 기다려 줄 수야 있겠지요. 저는 앞으로 몇 달 동안 로바츠 양을 다시 못 볼 텐데, 두 마디 나누는 것조차 아까워하시는 것 같군요.”\n\n“나리께 조금이라도 도움이 된다면 이백 마디라도 아깝지 않습니다.” 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 명랑하게 응접실로 되돌아 들어갔다. “다만 패니가 없으니 나리의 시간을 허비하게 할 것까지는 없다고 생각했을 뿐이지요.”\n\n그녀는 그보다 한없이 더 침착했고, 자기를 훨씬 더 잘 다스리고 있었다. 속으로는 다가오는 일을 생각하며 떨었으나, 겉으로는 아무런 동요도 내보이지 않았다 — 아직까지는 그랬다. 그가 자기에게 할 말이 무엇인지를 듣게 되었을 때에도 그러지 않을 만큼 자기를 붙들 수만 있다면 좋을 텐데.\n\n그렇게 굳게 마음먹고 여기까지 왔으나, 무슨 말을 하러 온 것인지는 그 자신도 잘 몰랐다. 그는 자기가 루시 로바츠를 사랑한다고 결심한 것도 결코 아니었고, 사랑하기에 그녀를 아내로 삼겠다고, 또는 사랑하지만 아내로 삼지는 않겠다고 결심한 것도 아니었다. 그는 이 문제에 대해 좋은 쪽으로든 나쁜 쪽으로든 머리를 써 본 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그는 그녀를 좋아하게 되었고 그녀가 아주 예쁘다고 여기게 되었다. 그녀와 이야기하는 것이 매우 즐겁다는 것을 알게 되었다. 반면에 그리젤다 그랜틀리와, 아니 그가 아는 다른 몇몇 젊은 아가씨들과 이야기하는 것은 흔히 고역이었다. 루시와 함께 보낸 반 시간들은 언제나 그를 만족하게 했다. 그는 다른 사람들과 있을 때보다 그녀와 있을 때 자기가 더 환해지고, 이야기할 만한 값이 있는 주제를 이야기하는 데 더 알맞아진다는 것을 알았다. 그리하여 그는 루시 로바츠를 마음 깊이 좋아하게 된 것이다. 자기 애정이 플라토닉한 것인지 반플라토닉한 것인지는 스스로 물어본 적이 없었다. 그러나 두 사람의 친밀함이 그렇게 갑자기 끊기기 얼마 전에 그는 그녀에게, 그럴 마음으로 받아들이는 처녀라면 누구라도 반플라토닉한 것으로 여길 수 있는 말들을 한 적이 있었다. 그는 그녀의 발밑에 몸을 던지며 애타는 열정에 삼켜지고 있다고 선언하지는 않았다. 그러나 사랑하는 여인의 손을 연인이 만지듯 그녀의 손을 만졌다. 자기 어머니와 누이와 벗들에 대해 이야기하며 그녀에게 마음속을 털어놓았다. 그리고 그녀를 자기의 소중한 벗 루시라고 불렀다.\n\n이 모든 것이 그녀에게는 매우 달았으나 또한 매우 독했다. 그녀는 이 젊은 귀족에 대한 자기 호감이 오라비의 호감과 똑같이 순전한 우정의 감정일 뿐이라고 스스로에게 여러 번 못을 박아 왔다. 그리고 그런 일에 대한 세상의 차가운 비아냥이 거짓임을 자기가 밝혀 보이겠다고 스스로에게 다짐했다. 그러나 이제 그녀는 그 문제에 대한 세상의 비아냥이, 차가울지언정 그만큼 참이 아닌 것은 아니라는 것을 인정했다. 그렇게 인정한 뒤에, 자기와 러프턴 경 사이의 가까운 결속은 모두 끝나야 한다고 마음을 정했다. 그녀는 결론에 이르렀으나 그는 아무 결론에도 이르지 못했다. 그리고 그런 마음가짐으로 그는 지금, 그녀가 분별을 내어 닫아 놓은 그 위태로운 우정을 다시 열려는 뜻을 품고 여기 와 있는 것이었다.\n\n“그래서 내일 떠나시는 것이지요?” 두 사람이 응접실 안에 들어서자마자 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“네, 내일 아침 이른 기차로 떠납니다. 그리고 우리가 언제 다시 만날지는 하늘만 알 일이지요.”\n\n“다음 겨울에 만나지 않겠습니까?”\n\n“네, 하루나 이틀쯤은 그렇겠지요. 제가 여기서 또 한 번 겨울을 보낼지는 모르겠습니다. 사실 사람이 어디 있게 될지는 누구도 말할 수 없는 것이니까요.”\n\n“그럼요, 말할 수 없지요. 적어도 나리 같은 분들은 말할 수 없겠지요. 저야 철새 족속이 아니니까요.”\n\n“그랬으면 좋겠는데요.”\n\n“고맙게 여기지는 않겠습니다. 나리의 그 떠돌이 살림은 젊은 아가씨들에게 맞지 않아요.”\n\n“그렇다면 요즘 아가씨들은 그 살림을 꽤 거침없이 받아들이고 있는 셈이지요. 온 세상에 보호자 없이 다니는 젊은 여인들이 있잖습니까.”\n\n“그리고 그이들이 몹시 지겹다고 여기시겠지요?”\n\n“아니요, 저는 좋습니다. 낡은 틀에서 벗어날 수 있는 것이 많을수록 저는 더 기쁩니다. 저는 내일이라도 급진파가 되겠어요 — 영락없는 민중의 사람이 되겠어요. 다만 그러면 제 어머니의 가슴이 무너질 테지요.”\n\n“무슨 일을 하시든, 러프턴 경, 그것만은 하지 마십시오.”\n\n“제가 로바츠 양을 그토록 좋아해 온 것도 그 때문입니다.” 그가 이어 말했다. “그 틀에서 벗어나 계시니까요.”\n\n“제가 그런가요?”\n\n“그렇습니다. 그리고 혼자서 자기 발걸음을 이끌며 나아가시지요. 할머니 대의 낡은 궤도가 어디로 데려가든 그대로 이리저리 실려 가지 않고요.”\n\n“그런데 저는 할머니 대의 낡은 궤도가 결국 가장 안전하고 가장 좋다는 생각이 아주 강하다는 것을 아십니까? 저는 그 궤도에서 멀리 벗어나지 않았고, 반드시 그리로 돌아갈 작정입니다.”\n\n“그건 있을 수 없는 일이에요! 오래 묵은 편견을 꼬아 만든 밧줄 사리를 든 노파들의 군대가 몰려온다 해도 로바츠 양을 그리로 끌어다 놓을 수는 없을 겁니다.”\n\n“그럴 수 없을 테지요, 러프턴 경. 그 말씀은 맞습니다. 하지만 한 사람이 —” 그러고서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 외아들을 걱정하는 사랑 많은 어머니 한 사람으로 그 일은 충분했다고 그에게 말할 수는 없었다. 정해진 궤도를 벗어난 이 일이 이미 자기의 안식을 깨뜨려, 평화롭고 행복했던 삶을 괴로운 싸움으로 바꾸어 놓았다고 그에게 설명할 수는 없었다.\n\n“로바츠 양이 되돌아가려 애쓰고 있다는 것을 저는 압니다.” 그가 말했다. “제게 눈이 있는데도 보지 못한다고 생각하시나요? 자, 루시, 그대와 나는 벗이었어요. 이런 식으로 헤어져서는 안 됩니다. 제 어머니는 여인 중의 귀감이에요. 진심으로 하는 말입니다. 여인 중의 귀감이지요. 그리고 저에 대한 그분의 사랑은 어머니의 사랑이 이를 수 있는 완전함 그 자체예요.”\n\n“그렇습니다, 그렇습니다. 그것을 인정하시니 저는 참 기쁩니다.”\n\n“그러지 않는다면 저는 짐승보다 못한 자일 테지요. 그러나 그렇다 해도 저는 그분이 저를 모든 일에서 이끌게 할 수는 없습니다. 그렇게 한다면 저는 사내이기를 그치게 될 거예요.”\n\n“나리께 그토록 참되게 조언해 줄 사람을 어디서 찾으시겠습니까?”\n\n“하지만 그렇다 해도 저는 저를 스스로 다스려야 합니다. 제 의심이 온전히 옳은지는 모르겠으나, 그분이 그대와 저 사이에 이 소원함을 만들어 놓았다는 생각이 듭니다. 그렇지 않았나요?”\n\n“저에게 말씀하셔서 그러신 것은 분명히 아닙니다.” 루시는 짙은 빛의 얼굴에 핏줄마다 홍옥처럼 붉은 물이 오르며 이렇게 말했다. 그러나 자기 피는 다스릴 수 없었어도 목소리는 여전히 자기 뜻 아래 있었다. 목소리와 몸가짐은 그러했다.\n\n“그러나 그분이 그렇게 하지 않았느냐는 말입니다. 그대가 저에게 진실 아닌 것을 말하지 않을 사람이라는 것은 제가 압니다.”\n\n“이 문제에 대해서는 참이든 거짓이든 저는 아무 말씀도 드리지 않겠습니다, 러프턴 경. 제가 입을 열 일이 아닌 주제입니다.”\n\n“아! 알겠습니다.” 그는 이렇게 말하고 의자에서 일어나, 불을 등지고 난로 선반에 기대어 섰다. “그분은 저를 그냥 놓아두어 제 벗을, 그리고 저의 —를 스스로 고르게 하지 못하시는 거지요.” 그러나 그는 그 빈자리를 채우지 않았다.\n\n“그런데 왜 그런 말씀을 저에게 하십니까, 러프턴 경?”\n\n“아니지요! 저는 제 벗을 고를 수 없습니다. 그 벗이 하느님이 지으신 것들 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 순결한 이라 해도요. 루시, 저는 그대가 저에 대한 마음을 거두었다고는 생각할 수 없어요. 그대가 저에 대한 마음을 가지고 있었다는 것은 제가 압니다.”\n\n이렇게 자기를 찾아내어 몰아세우고, 그러고서 자기가 여기 온 탓에 반드시 필요해진 그 설명의 무게를 온통 그녀에게 던져 놓는 것은 사내답지 못한 짓에 가깝다고 그녀는 느꼈다. 그러나 그렇다 해도 진실은 말해져야 했고, 하느님의 도우심으로 그녀는 그것을 말할 힘을 찾아낼 것이었다.\n\n“네, 러프턴 경, 저는 나리께 마음을 두고 있었습니다 — 그리고 지금도 두고 있습니다. 그 말로 나리께서 뜻하시는 것은, 우리처럼 서로 알고 지낸 지 얼마 되지 않은, 다른 집안의 신사와 숙녀 사이에 흔히 있을 수 있는 예사로운 안면의 감정보다 무언가 더한 것이겠지요.”\n\n“그렇습니다, 훨씬 더한 것이지요.” 그가 힘주어 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 그 ‘더한’ 것이 무엇인지는 제가 정하지 않겠습니다 — 그보다 더 가까운 무엇이겠지요.”\n\n“그렇습니다. 그리고 더 따뜻하고, 더 정답고, 서로의 마음과 가슴을 귀하게 여기는 두 사람에게 더 값있는 무엇이지요.”\n\n“그런 더 가까운 마음을 저는 나리께 느껴 왔습니다 — 아주 어리석게도요. 잠깐만요! 나리께서 저를 말하게 만드셨으니 이제 제 말을 끊지 마십시오. 그렇게 하면서 제가 아까 나리께서 말씀하신 그 현명한 할머니 대의 낡은 궤도를 슬기롭지 못하게 버렸다는 것을, 나리의 양심이 나리께 일러 주지 않습니까? 그렇게 하는 것이 저에게는 즐거웠습니다. 나리 같은 분과 거리낌 없는 우정을 누려도 된다고 생각할 때의 그 홀로 선 듯한 느낌이 저는 좋았습니다. 그리고 저와는 그토록 다른 나리의 신분이 그것을 더 끌리는 일로 만들었을 것이 분명합니다.”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리예요!”\n\n“아! 하지만 그랬습니다. 이제는 압니다. 그런데 그런 결합을 두고 세상이 저를 어떻게 말하겠습니까?”\n\n“세상이라니요!”\n\n“그렇습니다, 세상이요! 저는 나리처럼 그것을 무시할 여유가 있는 사람도 아니고, 그것을 무시할 만한 철인도 아닙니다. 세상은 목사의 누이인 제가 젊은 나리께 눈독을 들였고, 그 젊은 나리가 저를 웃음거리로 만들었다고 말할 것입니다.”\n\n“세상은 그런 말을 하지 못할 겁니다!” 러프턴 경이 아주 위세 있게 말했다.\n\n“아! 하지만 할 것입니다. 카누트 왕이 물결을 막지 못한 것처럼 나리도 그것을 막지 못하십니다. 나리의 어머님께서는 저를 이 일에서 지켜 주시려고 슬기롭게 끼어드신 것입니다. 그리고 제가 나리께 청할 수 있는 오직 하나의 은혜는, 나리께서도 저를 지켜 주시는 것입니다.” 그러고서 그녀는 당장 포진스 부인의 아기를 찾아 나서려는 듯이 일어섰다.\n\n“멈추세요, 루시!” 그가 그녀와 문 사이에 자기 몸을 세우며 말했다.\n\n“이제 더는 루시라고 부르시면 안 됩니다, 러프턴 경. 처음에 그것을 허락한 것은 제가 미친 듯이 어리석었던 탓입니다.”\n\n“하늘에 맹세하고, 루시라고 부를 겁니다 — 온 세상 앞에서 루시라고요. 내 루시, 나의 루시 — 내 마음의 가장 좋은 벗이며 내가 고른 사랑. 루시, 내 손이 여기 있어요. 그대가 내 마음을 얼마나 오래 가지고 있었는지는 이제 말할 것도 없지요.”\n\n승부는 이제 그녀의 발밑에 있었고, 그녀가 자기 승리를 느낀 것은 의심할 데가 없다. 그를 그녀 옆으로 끌어온 것은 미모가 아니라 그녀의 재빠른 재기와 말하는 입술이었다. 이제 그는 그녀가 자기에게 휘두른 힘이 그 무엇보다 컸음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀를 두고 떠나기보다는 모든 것을 걸겠다는 것이었다. 그녀는 정말로 자기 승리를 느꼈다. 그러나 그녀가 그렇게 느꼈다는 것을 그에게 알려 줄 만한 것은 그녀 얼굴에 하나도 없었다.\n\n이제 자기가 어떻게 할 것인지에 대해서는 그녀는 한순간도 망설이지 않았다. 그가 방금 한 그 고백에 떠밀려 들어간 것은 사랑 때문이 아니라 궁지에 몰린 탓이었다. 그녀가 그가 자기에게 입힌 상처를 그의 면전에 들이댔고, 그러자 그는 자기가 할 수 있는 가장 고귀한 희생으로 그 상처를 메우려는 후한 마음에 움직인 것이었다. 그러나 루시 로바츠는 희생을 받아들일 아가씨가 아니었다.\n\n그가 그녀의 허리를 감싸안으려는 듯 앞으로 한 걸음 나섰으나, 그녀는 물러나 그의 손이 닿지 않는 데로 비켰다. “러프턴 경!” 그녀가 말했다. “더 차분해지시면 이것이 잘못된 일임을 아시게 될 것입니다. 지금 우리 두 사람에게 가장 좋은 일은 헤어지는 것입니다.”\n\n“가장 좋은 일이 아니라 가장 나쁜 일이지요. 우리가 서로를 온전히 알게 되기 전에는요.”\n\n“그러면 저를 온전히 아셔야겠습니다. 저는 나리의 아내가 될 수 없습니다.”\n\n“루시! 저를 사랑하는 법을 배울 수 없다는 뜻인가요?”\n\n“배우려 하지 않겠다는 뜻입니다. 이 일을 끝까지 밀고 가지 마십시오. 그러지 않으면 나리께서 나리 자신의 어리석음 때문에 나리 자신을 미워하셔야 할 것입니다.”\n\n“하지만 저는 그대가 제 사랑을 받아들이거나, 아니면 가슴에 손을 얹고 저를 사랑할 수 없고 사랑하지 않겠다고 말할 때까지 끝까지 밀고 갈 겁니다.”\n\n“그렇다면 저를 놓아주시기를 청해야겠습니다.” 이렇게 말한 뒤, 그가 서둘러 방 안을 한두 번 오가는 동안 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. “그리고 러프턴 경,” 그녀가 이어 말했다. “지금 저를 놓고 가 주신다면, 나리께서 하신 그 말씀은 아예 입에 오른 적이 없는 것으로 하겠습니다.”\n\n“그 말이 입에 올랐다는 것을 누가 알든 저는 개의치 않습니다. 그것이 온 세상에 빨리 알려지면 알려질수록 저는 더 기쁠 겁니다. 다만 정말로 —”\n\n“어머님을 생각하십시오, 러프턴 경.”\n\n“제가 여태 만난 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 어여쁜 아가씨를 딸로 드리는 것보다 더 나은 일을 제가 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까? 제 어머니가 그대를 참으로 알게 되면, 저와 똑같이 그대를 사랑하실 겁니다. 루시, 저에게 위로가 될 한마디를 해 주세요.”\n\n“나리의 앞날의 평안을 해칠 말은 한마디도 드리지 않겠습니다. 제가 나리의 아내가 되는 것은 있을 수 없는 일입니다.”\n\n“저를 사랑할 수 없다는 뜻인가요?”\n\n“나리께는 저를 더 밀어붙일 권리가 없습니다.” 그녀가 이렇게 말하고, 이마에 노여운 주름을 지은 채 소파에 앉았다.\n\n“하늘에 맹세하고,” 그가 말했다. “그대가 가슴에 손을 얹고 저를 사랑할 수 없다고 말하기 전에는 그런 대답을 그대에게서 받지 않겠습니다.”\n\n“아, 어찌하여 저를 이토록 밀어붙이십니까, 러프턴 경?”\n\n“어찌하여라니요! 제 행복이 그것에 걸려 있으니까요. 제가 그 참된 진실을 알아야 마땅하니까요. 일이 이렇게 되었습니다. 저는 온 마음으로 그대를 사랑합니다. 그러니 그대의 마음이 저에게 어떻게 놓여 있는지를 저는 알아야 합니다.”\n\n그녀는 이제 다시 소파에서 일어나, 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보고 있었다.\n\n“러프턴 경,” 그녀가 말했다. “저는 나리를 사랑할 수 없습니다.” 그리고 말하면서 그녀는 정말로, 그가 바란 대로 자기 가슴에 손을 얹었다.\n\n“그렇다면 하느님께서 저를 도우시기를! 저는 참으로 비참합니다. 안녕히 계세요, 루시.” 그러고서 그는 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n“안녕히 가십시오, 나리. 저를 노여워하지 마십시오.”\n\n“아니, 아니, 아니요!” 그러고는 더 말이 없이 그는 그 방을, 그리고 그 집을 떠나 서둘러 집으로 돌아갔다. 그가 그날 저녁 어머니에게 그리젤다 그랜틀리라면 누이의 벗으로는 충분히 좋겠다고 말한 것은 조금도 놀라운 일이 아니었다. 그에게는 그런 벗이 필요하지 않았다.\n\n그리고 그가 완전히 가 버렸을 때 — 창에서 아주 보이지 않게 되었을 때 — 루시는 흔들림 없는 걸음으로 자기 방으로 올라가 문을 잠그고, 그러고서 침대에 몸을 던졌다. 왜, 아! 왜 자기는 그런 거짓말을 했을까? 무엇으로 그 거짓말이 옳다고 할 수 있을까? 사랑하는 온 마음으로 그를 사랑한다는 것을 스스로 알면서 한 말이니, 그것이 거짓말이 아니고 무엇인가?\n\n그러나 그의 어머니! 그리고 자기가 덫을 놓아 어리석은 젊은 나리를 잡았다고 말할 세상의 비웃음! 그녀의 자존심은 그것에 굽히려 하지 않았을 것이다. 그녀의 사랑이 강했으나, 그녀의 자존심은 어쩌면 그보다 더 강했다 — 적어도 그 만남의 시간 동안에는 더 강했다.\n\n그러나 자기가 한 그 거짓말을 그녀는 어떻게 스스로 용서한단 말인가?\n\n# 제17장. 프라우디 부인의 콘베르사치오네\n\n레이디 러프턴이 런던에 올라오기 전 그 몇 주 동안, 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 어머니의 세속스러움 탓에 얼마나 못된 일과 위험 속으로 끌려 들어갔는가를 생각하면 참으로 통탄할 일이었다 — 적어도 마님에게는 몹시 통탄할 일이었으니, 그분은 런던에서 벌어지는 일을 때때로 전해 듣고 있었다. 그리젤다가 사교계의 새 월계관을 거두어들이도록 허락된 못마땅한 집이 레이디 하틀탑의 집 하나뿐은 아니었다. 《모닝 포스트》는 그 젊은 아가씨가 던스터블 양의 이름난 야회 가운데 한 자리에서 미인들 중에서도 가장 많은 찬탄을 받았다고 버젓이 실었고, 그런 다음에는 그녀가 프라우디 부인의 콘베르사치오네에서 응접실을 빛냈다는 소문이 들려왔다.\n\n던스터블 양 자신에 대해서라면 레이디 러프턴은 어떤 악담도 드러내어 늘어놓을 수가 없었다. 레이디 러프턴도 알거니와 그녀는 옳은 축에 드는 사람들과 아주 많이 알고 지냈고, 레이디 러프턴의 지극히 보수적이고 그리 멀지도 않은 이웃인 그레셤네와는 다정한 벗이었다. 그러나 그러면서 그녀는 나쁜 축에 드는 사람들과도 그만큼 많이 알고 지냈다. 실로 그녀는 옴니엄 공작에서부터 지난 사반세기 동안 온갖 기본 덕목을 대표해 온 늙은 구디개퍼 노부인에 이르기까지 누구와도 가까웠다. 그녀는 당밀에게나 유황에게나 똑같이 달게 웃었고, 엑서터 홀에서도 제 집처럼 편안하여 — 세상 사람들 말이 그러하니 아마 딱 들어맞는 말은 아니겠지만 — 못마땅한 저교회파 주교를 한 사람 이상 고르는 데에 의견을 물어 온 적도 있었다 하며, 영대와 저녁 기도를 좋아하고 고해와 금요일의 생선을 개신교도답게 미워하지 않는다는 중부 여러 주의 어떤 무서운 고위 성직자의 교회 행사에도 그만 못하지 않게 자주 나타났다. 신념이 아주 굳은 레이디 러프턴은 이것이 마음에 들지 않아, 던스터블 양을 두고 하나님과 마몬을 아울러 섬기기란 불가능한 일이라고 말하곤 했다.\n\n그러나 그녀에게 훨씬 더 못마땅한 사람은 프라우디 부인이었다. 저 아래 바셋주에서 프라우디네와 그랜틀리네 사이의 반목이 얼마나 날카로운지, 교회 일에서 그들이 서로에게 점잖은 낯빛 한번 지어 보이지 못한 것이 언제나 어떠했는지, 그 둘이 교구 안에서 두 파를 이끌어 한자리에 놓이면 기름과 초 같았고 그 싸움마다 러프턴가의 영향력은 언제나 그랜틀리 편에 실려 왔다는 것을 — 내가 말하거니와 이 모든 것을 보아 온 레이디 러프턴은 그리젤다가 프라우디 부인의 저녁 전시회에 이끌려 갔다는 말을 듣고 놀랐다. “부주교가 그 일을 두고 의논을 받았더라면” 하고 그녀는 혼자 말했다. “이런 일은 결코 일어나지 않았을 텐데.” 그러나 그 점에서 그녀는 틀렸다. 딸을 세상에 내보이는 일에 관해서라면 부주교는 결코 끼어들지 않았으니까.\n\n대체로 나는 그랜틀리 부인이 레이디 러프턴보다 세상을 더 잘 알았다고 생각하는 편이다. 그랜틀리 부인은 마음속 깊은 곳에서 프라우디 부인을 미워했다 — 그러니까 한 그리스도인 부인이 다른 부인을 향해 품는 것을 스스로 허락하는 그런 종류의 미움으로. 물론 그랜틀리 부인은 프라우디 부인의 모든 잘못을 용서했고, 그녀가 잘되기를 바랐고, 그리스도인의 뜻으로는 다른 모든 여인과 마찬가지로 그녀와도 화평했다. 그러나 이 참음과 온유함 밑에는 — 어쩌면 그것과는 아무 상관도 없다고 말해야 하겠지만 — 날마다 쓰는 여느 무심한 말로 남자든 여자든 미움이라 부르는 적대의 물길이 분명히 흐르고 있었다. 그것은 바셋주에서 온 세상 사람들의 눈앞에서 한 해 내내 세차게 타올랐다. 그럼에도 그랜틀리 부인은 런던에서는 그리젤다를 프라우디 부인의 저녁 모임에 데려갔다.\n\n이 무렵 프라우디 부인은 자신이 주교 부인들 가운데 결코 가장 못한 사람은 아니라고 여기고 있었다. 그녀는 올해 글로스터 플레이스의 새집에서 사교 계절의 막을 열었는데, 적어도 손님 맞는 방들만은 여자 주교라면 바랄 만한 모든 것을 갖추고 있었다. 여기에 그녀는 아주 훌륭한 크기의 앞응접실 하나와, 이웃집과 밀치락달치락한 끝인 듯 뒤쪽 구석 하나를 어지간히 어색하게 잃어버렸으되 이 또한 어지간히 훌륭한 둘째 응접실 하나를 두었고, 그리고 세 번째 — 응접실이라 할까, 아니면 골방이라 할까? — 가 있었으니, 프라우디 부인은 세상에 세 번째 방이 있다는 것을 알리기 위해 바로 그 방에 앉아 있는 모습을 보이기를 즐겼다. 그리하여 통틀어 훌륭한 방 한 벌이었다고, 프라우디 부인 자신이 바셋주에서 온 여러 성직자 부인에게 은근히 말한 바와 같다. “과연 훌륭한 방 한 벌이에요, 프라우디 부인!” 바셋주에서 온 성직자 부인들은 으레 그렇게 대답했다.\n\n얼마 동안 프라우디 부인은 어떤 종류의 모임이나 여흥으로 이름을 떨쳐야 할지 몰라 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 무도회와 만찬은 물론 말이 되지 않았다. 딸들이 남의 집에서 밤새 춤추는 것에는 반대하지 않았다 — 적어도 요즈음에는 반대하지 않았으니, 유행하는 세상이 그것을 요구했고 젊은 아가씨들에게도 어쩌면 저들의 뜻이 있었으므로 — 그러나 자기 집에서, 그것도 주교의 앞치마 그늘 바로 아래서 춤을 춘다는 것은 죄악이요 망측한 일이 될 터였다. 그리고 만찬으로 말하자면 — 널리 사귄 사람들에게 후한 대접을 뻗칠 온갖 방식 가운데 그것이 가장 돈이 많이 든다.\n\n“먹고 마시는 그것만을 위해 우리가 벗들 사이로 나선다고 생각하면 끔찍한 일이오,” 프라우디 부인은 바셋주에서 온 성직자 부인들에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. “그건 참으로 감각적 성벽을 드러내는 것이오.”\n\n“정말 그래요, 프라우디 부인. 게다가 어찌나 상스러운지요!” 그 부인들은 이렇게 대답하곤 했다.\n\n그러나 그들 가운데 나이 든 이들은 그랜틀리 주교 — 그 영혼이 평안하시기를! — 의 좋았던 옛날 바체스터 주교관에서 베풀던 아낌없고 후한 대접을 아쉬움과 함께 떠올렸다. 그리 정중하지 못한 대답을 한 늙은 교구 사제의 아내가 한 사람 있었으니 —\n\n“배가 고플 때는요, 프라우디 부인,” 그녀는 이렇게 말했다. “우리 모두가 감각적 성벽을 갖게 되지요.”\n\n“세상이 그런 일은 다 집에서 치르고 온다면 훨씬 나을 것이오, 애실 부인,” 프라우디 부인은 재빨리 되받았다. 그 의견에는 나로서도 도무지 한 편이 되어 줄 수 없음을 여기서 밝혀 두어야 하겠다.\n\n그러나 콘베르사치오네라면 어떤 감각적 성벽에도 놀 자리를 주지 않을 터이고, 감각적 성벽을 흡족하게 하느라 너무나도 자주 생겨나는 그 견딜 수 없는 비용을 일으키지도 않을 터였다. 프라우디 부인은 그 낱말이 자기가 바랄 만한 모든 것을 갖추지는 못했다고 느꼈다. 오래 쓰이고 지금은 잊힌 탓에 빛이 좀 바랬고, 유행을 좇는 쪽이 아니라 먹물 든 축으로 여겨지는 런던 세상의 그 부분에게 말을 거는 듯했다. 그러나 그럼에도 거기에는 그녀에게 어울리는 영적인 기운이 있었고, 또한 절약이 있었다고도 말할 수 있겠다. 그리고 유행으로 말하자면, 그 낱말에 새로 닦은 금박을 다시 입히는 일이 어쩌면 프라우디 부인 정도의 사람에게 못할 짓은 아닐 터였다. 누군가 앞장서는 사람이 유행을 맨 먼저 만들어 내야 하는 법인데, 그것이 프라우디 부인이면 왜 안 되는가?\n\n그녀의 계획은 이러했다. 사람들을 마주 붙여 놓아 말이 통하면 말을 하게 하고, 그 이상을 얻어 낼 수 없다면 적어도 거기 멍하니 몸이라도 내보이게 만드는 것. 훌륭한 방 한 벌의 가구가 허락하는 한 되도록 많은 사람을 의자와 소파에 앉히되, 특히 뒤쪽 골방 — 바셋주에서 온 성직자 부인들에게는 작은 안응접실이라 부르곤 하던 그 방 — 벽에 붙인 의자 두 개와 속을 넣은 긴 의자를 쓰고, 나머지는 선 채로 있게 하거나 그녀의 표현대로 “무리를 짓게” 하는 것이었다. 그러고는 콘베르사치오네가 이어지는 두 시간 동안 네 차례에 걸쳐 은쟁반에 차와 케이크를 받쳐 돌리게 할 것이었다. 아주 조금의 케이크가 이런 식으로 얼마나 멀리까지 가는지는 놀라울 지경이다. 특히 저녁을 먹고 어지간히 이른 때에 내놓으면 더욱 그렇다. 남자들은 그것을 먹을 수 없고, 여자들은 접시도 없고 상도 없으니 삼가지 않을 수 없다. 존스 부인은 부스러지는 케이크 한 조각을 다 먹을 때까지 손에 들고 있다가는 자기 나들이옷을 크게 상하게 하고 만다는 것을 안다. 프라우디 부인이 주간 가계부를 앞에 놓고 자기 콘베르사치오네의 셈속을 들여다보았을 때, 양심은 그녀에게 옳은 일을 했다고 말해 주었다.\n\n차 마시러 나가는 것은 나쁜 일이 아니다. 저녁을 이르게 먹을 수 있고, 그다음에 가운데 차 항아리를 놓은 큰 상에 둘러앉기를 허락받는다면 말이다. 다만 신사들에게는 언제나 아침 식사용 큰 잔을 내놓기를 권하고 싶다. 그리고 유쾌한 이웃들과 함께라면 — 더 나아가 특히 유쾌한 이웃 한 사람과 함께라면 — 그 일은 내 취향으로는 사교의 가장 못한 모습과는 도무지 거리가 멀다. 그러나 나는 그 받쳐 돌리기를 싫어한다. 사교의 본론이 만찬이었을 때 덧붙여 나오는 골무만큼의 것이라면 모르되.\n\n그리고 사실 이 받쳐 돌리기는 연 팔백 파운드, 그쯤 되는 우리 이류 지주 축 사이에서 상스럽고 견딜 수 없는 골칫거리가 되었다. 우리의 타고난 편안함을 망가뜨리고 큰 소득을 가진 이들을 비참하도록 상스럽게 본뜨는 것이니 두 곱으로 견딜 수 없다. 옴니엄 공작과 레이디 하틀탑이 모든 것을 받쳐 돌리게 하는 것은 의심할 나위 없이 현명하다. 그런 집에서 이따금 저녁을 먹는 내 벗들이 말해 주는데, 거기서는 술이 마실 수 있는 만큼 빠르게 나오고, 양고기가 지체 없이 앞에 오며, 감자 나르는 이가 고기 나르는 이의 뒤꿈치를 바짝 따라온다는 것이다. 그보다 더 편안할 수는 없으니, 이 일류 귀인들이 제 물질적 편안함을 아는 것만은 우리도 인정해야 하리라. 그러나 우리 팔백 파운드 축은 이 일에서 그들을 따라갈 수 없으니, 그들의 오페라 특별석과 마차 행렬을 따라갈 수 없는 것과 마찬가지다. 고기 나르는 이며 잔 나르는 이며 그 밖의 것들로 말하자면, 이 계층에게 흔히 매인 줄은 손 놀림 재빠른 필리스와 청과물 장수를 넘어서지 못한다고 말해도 되지 않겠는가? 그리고 필리스가 아마도 그렇듯이 손 놀림이 재빠르고 청과물 장수가 아무리 부지런하다 해도, 제 손으로 무엇 하나 덜어 먹는 일을 메디아·페르시아의 법으로 금지당한 열두 사람에게 만찬을 치러 낼 수는 없다고 말해도 되지 않겠는가? 그리고 서로의 집을 오가며 밥을 먹는 우리 팔백짜리들에게 딱한 결말이 이러하다고 더 말해도 되지 않겠는가 — 우리는 너무나 자주 저녁을 아예 못 먹는다는 것. 감자를 든 필리스는 우리 양고기가 다 없어지거나 우리 힘으로는 어찌할 수 없게 식어 버린 뒤에야 우리에게 닿을 수 있고, 청과물 장수 가니메데스는 우리가 그 넥타이 매는 솜씨와 나무랄 데 없는 장갑의 흰빛에 감탄하기는 하지만 우리 셰리 잔을 채워 주는 데에는 실패한다.\n\n얼마 전에 어떤 부인이 이런 궁지에 빠져, 소화에 아무래도 필요할 얼마 안 되는 자극을 얻지 못한 채 남겨진 것을 보고, 나는 감히 나와 함께 술을 드시자고 청해 보았다. 그러나 내가 그녀에게 머리를 숙였을 때 그녀는 두 눈을 다 뜨고 나를 바라보며 놀라움에 사로잡혔다. 내가 몸에 물감만 칠하고 사나운 인디언 전쟁 춤에 함께 나서자고 했더라도 그 얼굴이 그보다 더 큰 놀라움을 드러낼 수는 없었을 것이다. 그런데도 나는 그녀가 그리스도인 남녀가 서로 술을 나누어 마시던 그 시절을 기억해 주었으면 했다.\n\n상에 앉아 잔을 입술로 들어 올리고 싶은 마음이 들 때마다 벗과 잔을 부딪칠 수 있었고, 내 접시의 급한 사정이 요구할 때면 뜨거운 감자를 향해 팔을 길게 뻗을 수 있었던 그 좋았던 옛날에 하나님이 함께하시기를.\n\n대접하는 일에서는 이렇게 법으로 정해 두어도 될 것 같다. 손님이 우리와 함께 있을 때 우리 상에 들여오는 남다른 호사나 웅장함이 무엇이든, 그것은 손님의 이익을 위해 들여와야 하고 우리 자신의 이익을 위해 들여와서는 안 된다는 것. 예컨대 우리 저녁이 우리에게 여느 때와 다른 방식으로 차려진다면, 그것은 우리 벗들이 우리의 평소 방식이 그들에게 줄 것보다 더 흡족하게 우리 음식을 먹을 수 있도록 그렇게 차려져야 한다. 그러나 그 변화는 우리 유행이 인정받게 하려고 그들의 실질적인 손해를 무릅쓰고 이루어져서는 결코 안 된다. 또, 내가 찬장과 상을 꾸미는 것이 손님들의 눈이 우아하고 보기에 즐거운 것에 머물기를 바라서라면, 나는 그 일에서 대접하는 마음에 걸맞게 처신하는 것이다. 그러나 내 목적이 온갖 은붙이를 보여 존스 부인을 부러움으로 죽이는 것이라면, 나는 아주 마음이 천한 놈이다. 이만한 넓이로 말하면 누구나 인정할 것이다. 그러나 우리가 늘 같은 생각을 마음에 두고 있다면 — 어쩌면 길이 그만큼 넓지 않아서, 무엇이 참된 대접인지 알아내려면 더 많이 생각해야 하는 자리에서도 — 우리 팔백 파운드 축은 우리 앞에 내놓는 고기와 접시들을 이리저리 다시 늘어놓아 보는 것보다 우리 벗들을 참으로 즐겁게 해 주는 쪽으로 더 크게 나아갈 수 있으리라 생각한다.\n\n러프턴–그랜틀리 동맹의 조건이 두 어머니 사이에서 그토록 엄숙하게 비준되었음을 우리가 아는 터에, 그랜틀리 부인이 딸을 프라우디 부인의 집으로 데려간 것은 덤벨로 경이 주교의 모임을 빛내 주겠다고 약속했다는 소식을 그녀가 얻어 들었기 때문이라고 짐작하는 것은 아마 우리에게 허락된 일이 아니겠다. 고귀한 계약 당사자들이 이따금 더 낮은 축의 계약자들이라면 부정직하다고 여길 만한 여유를 스스로에게 허락하는 것은 분명한 사실이다. 그러니 부주교의 부인이 자기 활에 걸어 둔 두 번째 시위를 생각했을 수도 있는 일이다. 어찌 되었든 덤벨로 경은 프라우디 부인의 집에 있었고, 그리하여 그리젤다는 소파의 한쪽 끝에 앉게 되었으며 그 가까이에는 덤벨로 경이 “무리를 지을” 수 있는 빈자리가 있게 되었다.\n\n그들이 거기 있게 된 지 얼마 되지 않아 덤벨로 경은 정말로 무리를 지었다. “좋은 날이오,” 그는 그렇게 말하며 다가와 그랜틀리 양의 팔꿈치 옆 빈자리를 차지했다.\n\n“저희는 오늘 마차를 타고 나갔는데, 꽤 춥다고 생각했어요,” 그리젤다가 말했다.\n\n“지독하게 춥소,” 덤벨로 경은 그렇게 말하고 나서 흰 넥타이를 고쳐 매고 구레나룻을 손질했다. 거기까지 이르자 그는 곧이어 어떤 다른 대화의 노력으로도 나아가지 않았다. 그리젤다도 그러지 않았다. 그러나 그는 후작에게 걸맞게 다시 무리를 지었고, 프라우디 부인에게 아주 짙은 만족을 주었다.\n\n“이렇게 오시니 참 고맙소, 덤벨로 경,” 그 부인은 그에게 다가와 손을 따뜻하게 잡으며 말했다. “제 초라한 조그만 차 모임에 오시니 참으로 고마운 일이오.”\n\n“썩 유쾌하다고 하겠소,” 덤벨로 경이 말했다. “나는 이런 게 좋소 — 번거로울 게 없으니.”\n\n“그렇지요. 그게 바로 이것의 묘미가 아니겠소? 번거로움도, 법석도, 겉치레도 없다는 것. 그게 내가 늘 하는 말이오. 내 생각으로는 사교란 사람들에게 생각을 주고받을 편의를 주는 것에 있소 — 우리가 대화라 부르는 그것 말이오.”\n\n“아, 예, 바로 그렇소.”\n\n“함께 먹고 마시는 데에 있는 것이 아니지요 — 그렇지 않소, 덤벨로 경? 그런데도 우리 삶의 관행은 그 짐승 같은 성벽을 흡족하게 하는 것만이 사람들을 한자리에 모을 수 있음을 보여 주는 듯하오. 세상은 이 점에서 참으로 큰 잘못을 저질렀소.”\n\n“나는 그래도 좋은 저녁이 좋소,” 덤벨로 경이 말했다.\n\n“아, 그렇지요, 물론 — 물론이오. 우리 입맛이 우리 즐거움을 위해 주어진 것이 아니라고 설교하려 드는 사람들 축에는 나도 결코 들지 않소. 좋아하지 않을 것이라면 무엇이 왜 좋겠소?”\n\n“정말로 좋은 저녁을 낼 수 있는 사람은 많은 것을 배운 사람이오,” 덤벨로 경이 여느 때와 달리 활기를 띠며 말했다.\n\n“엄청나게 많은 것이지요. 그것 자체가 하나의 예술이오. 그리고 적어도 나는 그것을 결코 업신여기지 않소. 그러나 우리가 늘 먹고만 있을 수는 없지 않겠소?”\n\n“없지요,” 덤벨로 경이 말했다. “늘 그럴 수는 없소.” 그러고 그는 자기 힘이 그렇게 제한되어 있음을 애달파하는 듯한 얼굴을 했다.\n\n그러고 나서 프라우디 부인은 그랜틀리 부인에게로 옮겨 갔다. 두 부인은 런던에서는 아주 다정했다. 저들의 고향 동네에서는 그토록 서로 죽이려 드는 싸움을 벌였건만. 그러나 그럼에도 프라우디 부인의 태도는 아주 눈 밝은 사람에게라면 그녀가 주교와 부주교의 차이를 안다는 것을 드러내 보였을 것이다. “이렇게 뵈니 정말 기쁘오,” 그녀가 말했다. “아니, 자리를 옮기려 마시오. 지금 당장 앉지는 않을 테니. 그런데 부주교는 왜 오지 않았소?”\n\n“도무지 될 수 없는 일이었어요. 정말 그랬어요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “부주교는 런던에서 자기 것이라 부를 만한 시간이 한 순간도 없어요.”\n\n“오래 머무르지는 않으신다지요.”\n\n“우리 둘 다 좋아하는 것보다는 훨씬 오래 머물러요, 정말이에요. 런던 생활은 저에게는 아주 골칫거리예요.”\n\n“그러나 어떤 자리에 있는 사람은 그것을 겪어 내야 하잖소,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “주교만 해도 상원에 나가야 하고.”\n\n“그래야 하나요?” 그랜틀리 부인이 주교 직무의 이 갈래에 대해서는 도무지 아는 바가 없는 사람처럼 물었다. “부주교에게는 그런 의무가 없다니 아주 다행이에요.”\n\n“아, 없지요. 그런 것은 하나도 없소,” 프라우디 부인이 아주 진지하게 말했다. “그런데 그랜틀리 양은 어쩌면 저리 좋아 보이오! 그 아이가 꽤 찬탄을 받았다는 말을 나도 듣기는 들었소.”\n\n이 말은 확실히 어머니로서 참기에 좀 힘든 것이었다. 온 세상이 인정한 바 — 그랜틀리 부인은 스스로 그렇게 믿게 되었다 — 그리젤다는 의심할 나위 없이 이번 계절의 미인이었다. 후작들과 귀족들이 벌써 그 미소를 놓고 다투었고, 그 옆얼굴에 대해 신문에 여러 단이 쓰였다. 그런 뒤에 자기 딸이 “꽤 찬탄을 받았다”는 말을 듣는 것은 너무 심했다. 그런 말투는 뺨이 붉은 예쁘장한 우유 짜는 계집아이에게나 어울릴 것이었다.\n\n“물론 그 점에서는 그 애가 부인 딸들에게는 미치지 못하지요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 아주 조용하게 말했다. 그런데 프라우디 자매는 유행하는 세상에서 저들의 미모에 대해 어떤 큰 찬사도 끌어내지 못한 터였다. 그들의 어머니는 그 비꼼을 온 힘으로 느꼈으나, 지금 이 자리에서 싸움을 벌이려 하지는 않았다. 그녀는 그 항목을 마음속에 적어 두고, 바체스터와 성당 참사회를 위해 남겨 두었다. 그런 빚은 갚을 방도가 조금이라도 제 힘 안에 있으면 그녀가 으레 언젠가는 갚는 것이었다.\n\n“그런데 저기 던스터블 양이 왔구려, 정말이지,” 그 부인이 던스터블 양이 방에 들어서는 것을 보고 말했다. 그리고 프라우디 부인은 자기의 이름 높은 손님을 맞이하러 가 버렸다.\n\n“그래, 이게 콘베르사치오네라는 거군요?” 그 부인이 여느 때처럼 목소리를 낮추지 않고 말했다. “아니, 참으로 좋네요. 대화한다는 뜻이지요, 프라우디 부인?”\n\n“하하하! 던스터블 양, 정말이지 당신 같은 사람은 또 없소.”\n\n“그렇지만 그런 뜻 아니에요? 그리고 차와 케이크? 그러고 나서 이야기하는 데 지치면 우리는 가 버리는 것 — 그렇지 않아요?”\n\n“그러나 앞으로 세 시간은 지치시면 안 되오.”\n\n“아, 저는 이야기하는 데 지치는 일이 없어요. 온 세상이 아는 일이지요. 안녕하세요, 주교님? 이 콘베르사치오네라는 게 참 좋은 것이네요, 그렇지 않아요?”\n\n주교는 두 손을 맞비비며 웃고, 자기도 꽤 좋다고 생각한다고 말했다.\n\n“프라우디 부인은 그 자잘한 준비마다 참 복이 많으시지요,” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n“그렇지요, 그렇지요,” 주교가 말했다. “나도 아내가 이런 일에는 재주가 있다고 생각해요. 그렇다고 스스로 흐뭇해하기도 하지요. 물론 던스터블 양은 훨씬 더 웅장한 것들에 익숙하겠지만요.”\n\n“저요! 세상에, 천만에요! 저만큼 웅장함을 싫어하는 사람은 없어요. 물론 저는 시키는 대로 해야 하지요. 큰 집에 살아야 하고, 키가 여섯 피트나 되는 하인을 셋 두어야 하고요. 가발이 머리에 무겁게 얹힌 마부와 저를 겁먹게 할 만큼 큰 말들을 두어야 해요. 그러지 않으면 저는 미친 사람으로 몰려서 제 일도 제가 못 다스린다는 판정을 받을 테니까요. 그렇지만 웅장함이라면, 저는 그걸 싫어해요. 저도 이 콘베르사치오네라는 것을 좀 해 봐야겠다고 정말로 생각해요. 프라우디 부인이 와서 요령을 한두 가지 알려 주실지 모르겠네요.”\n\n주교는 다시 두 손을 맞비비며 아내가 틀림없이 그러할 것이라고 말했다. 그는 던스터블 양과 함께 있으면 도무지 마음이 편치 않았으니, 그녀가 하는 말이 진심인지 아닌지를 좀처럼 가려낼 수 없었기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 무슨 핑계를 중얼거리며 총총 가 버렸고, 던스터블 양은 그가 너무나 뚜렷하게 드러낸 어리둥절함에 속으로 킥킥 웃었다. 던스터블 양은 본디 친절하고 너그럽고 마음이 열린 사람이었다. 그러나 그녀는 지금 친절과 너그러움과 열린 마음이 헛되이 버려지는 사람들 사이에서 아주 많이 살고 있었다. 그녀는 또한 영리했고 비꼴 줄도 알았다. 그리고 그런 재주가 그녀를 둘러싼 세상에서는 너그러움과 열린 마음보다 더 잘 통한다는 것을 알아냈다. 그래서 그녀는 달이 가고 해가 가도, 할 수도 있었을 만큼 좋은 기운으로 나아가지는 못한 채, 그래도 참으로 사랑할 수 있는 이들을 향한 따뜻한 애정만은 가슴에 품고 지내 왔다. 그리고 그녀는 자기가 마땅히 살아야 할 대로 살고 있지 못하다는 것을, 자기가 업신여기는 척하는 그 부에 제 성품의 튼튼함이 먹혀 들어가고 있다는 것을 알았다. 그 부의 화려함 때문이 아니라, 그것이 마치 필연인 듯 만들어 내는 삶의 방식 때문에. 그녀는 자기가 차츰 경건함을 잃고 비웃기 좋아하고 우스개로 몰아가기 쉬운 사람이 되어 가고 있음을 알았다. 그러나 이것을 알면서 또 이것을 미워하면서도, 그녀는 거기서 벗어날 길을 좀처럼 알지 못했다.\n\n그녀는 인간 본성의 더 검은 쪽을 너무나 많이 보아 온 터라, 검음이 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 그녀를 놀라게 하지 않았다. 그녀는 그토록 많은 망해 버린 낭비꾼들이 겨눈 상금이었고, 삶의 열린 물길을 지나가는 그녀를 그토록 많은 해적들이 들이받아 침몰시키려 했으므로, 그녀는 자기 돈주머니를 겨눈 그런 시도를 사내답지 못하다거나 지나치게 탐욕스럽다고 여기기를 그만두었다. 그녀는 자기 뜻의 힘과 슬기의 힘을 믿고 든든해하며, 제 싸움을 제 무기로 싸우는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n그녀에게는 참으로 사랑하는 벗이 몇 사람 있었으니 — 그들 사이에서는 그녀의 속 자아가 나와서 제 참된 목소리로 할 말을 담대하게 할 수 있었다. 그리고 그렇게 말하는 그 여인은 프라우디 부인이 떠받들고 옴니엄 공작이 잔치로 대접하고 해럴드 스미스 부인이 가슴에 품은 벗이라 주장하는 저 던스터블 양과는 아주 달랐다. 그런 이들 가운데서 그녀의 마음이 쉴 수 있는, 제 세상의 무거운 짐을 지는 것을 도와줄 특별한 동무 하나를 찾을 수만 있다면! 그러나 그런 벗을 어디서 찾을 것인가? — 날카로운 슬기와 헤아릴 수 없는 돈과 크게 웃는 목소리를 지닌 그녀가. 그녀에 관한 모든 것은 그녀가 값있게 여길 수 없는 이들을 끌어당기고, 제 운명을 기꺼이 이어 매고 싶은 그런 벗은 그녀에게서 겁을 주어 쫓아 버리도록 마련되어 있었다.\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 해럴드 스미스 부인을 만났다. 그 부인은 저녁 순회 길에 프라우디 부인의 훌륭한 방 한 벌을 일정에 넣고 거기에 이십 분을 바치고 있었다. “그러니 축하드려도 되겠지요,” 던스터블 양이 벗에게 서둘러 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 제발 그러지 마세요. 그러지 않으면 아무래도 축하를 도로 거두어들여야 할 판인데, 그건 너무 언짢은 일일 테니까요.”\n\n“그렇지만 어제 브록 경이 그이를 불렀다고 하던데요.” 이 무렵 브록 경은 총리였다.\n\n“불렀지요. 그래서 해럴드는 하루 종일 그이와 이리 갔다 저리 갔다 했어요. 그렇지만 그이는 슬기롭고 신중한 사람이 마땅히 하는 대로, 눈을 감고 입을 벌리고 하나님이 무엇을 주실지 지켜볼 줄을 몰라요. 언제나 흥정을 하려 드는데, 흥정을 좋아하는 총리는 없지요.”\n\n“그이가 결국 집에 돌아와서 흥정이 깨졌다고 말해야 한다면, 저는 그 처지에 서고 싶지 않네요.”\n\n“하하하! 글쎄요, 저도 아주 가만히 받아들이지는 않을 거예요. 그렇지만 우리 딱한 여자들이 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요? 정해지면, 이봐요, 곧바로 한 줄 적어 보낼게요.” 그러고 나서 해럴드 스미스 부인은 방들을 돌아보는 일정을 마치고 이십 분 안에 자기 마차로 돌아갔다.\n\n“옆얼굴이 아름답지요, 그렇지 않아요?” 저녁이 좀 더 깊었을 때 던스터블 양이 프라우디 부인에게 말했다. 물론 여기서 말한 옆얼굴은 그랜틀리 양의 것이었다.\n\n“그래요, 확실히 아름답소,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “딱한 것은 그것이 아무 뜻도 없다는 점이오.”\n\n“신사들은 그것이 꽤 많은 뜻을 지녔다고 생각하는 것 같은데요.”\n\n“나는 그 점을 잘 모르겠소. 보다시피 그 애에게는 대화가 없소. 한 마디도 없지. 지난 한 시간 동안 덤벨로 경을 팔꿈치 옆에 두고 저기 앉아 있었으면서 입을 세 번도 채 열지 않았소.”\n\n“하지만 프라우디 부인, 대체 누가 덤벨로 경에게 말을 걸 수 있겠어요?”\n\n프라우디 부인은 자기 딸 올리비아라면 기회만 얻는다면 틀림없이 그럴 수 있으리라고 생각했다. 하기야 올리비아에게는 대화가 그토록 많았으니.\n\n그리고 두 부인이 아직 그 젊은 한 쌍을 바라보고 있는 동안, 덤벨로 경이 다시 입을 열었다. “이만하면 이제 충분한 것 같소,” 그는 그리젤다에게 말을 걸며 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“다른 약속이 있으신가 봐요,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“아, 그렇소. 레이디 클랜텔브록스 댁으로 갈 생각이오.” 그러고 나서 그는 자리를 떴다. 그날 저녁 그와 그랜틀리 양 사이에 이 기록에 적힌 것 말고는 다른 어떤 말도 오가지 않았다. 그런데도 세상은 그와 그 젊은 아가씨가 여느 것보다 유별나다고 할 만큼 바짝 붙은 연애질로 저녁을 보냈다고 떠들었다. 그리고 그랜틀리 부인은 마차를 타고 숙소로 돌아가면서, 이제 그 대단한 하틀탑 집안의 수장이 그토록 맺고 싶어 하는 듯한 그만한 동맹을 못마땅해하는 것이 슬기로운 일일지 마음속에 의심이 들기 시작했다. 신중한 어머니는 이런 문제를 두고 딸에게 아직 한 마디도 하지 않았으나, 머지않아 그렇게 해야 할지도 몰랐다. 레이디 러프턴이 서둘러 런던에 올라오는 것도 다 좋은 일이지만, 러프턴 경이 브루턴 스트리트에 없다면 그것이 무슨 도움이 되겠는가?\n\n# 제18장. 새 각료의 후원\n\n그때, 마침 레이디 러프턴이 프램리를 떠나 런던으로 향하려 하던 무렵, 마크 로바츠는 그 역시 하루나 이틀쯤 수도로 올라오라고 다급히 청하는 편지 한 통을 받았다 — 놀러 오라는 것이 아니라 볼일이 있어서였다. 편지는 지칠 줄 모르는 그의 벗 사워비에게서 온 것이었다.\n\n“친애하는 로바츠,” 편지는 이렇게 시작했다:—\n\n바셋주 참사회원인 딱하고 작달막한 버슬렘이 죽었다는 소식을 방금 들었어요. 사람은 누구나 언젠가 죽는 법이지요 — 당신도 프램리 설교단에서 교구민들에게 한두 번은 그렇게 일러 주었을 테고요. 그 자리는 채워져야 하는데, 다른 사람이 아니라 당신이 그것을 차지하면 안 될 이유가 무엇입니까? 연 육백 파운드에 집 한 채예요. 버슬렘 영감 때는 구백이었지만 좋았던 옛 시절은 다 갔지요. 지금의 교회 체제 아래에서 그 집을 세놓을 수 있는지는 나도 모르겠어요. 전에는 그랬으니까요. 양초 장수의 미망인 위긴스 부인이 스탠호프 영감의 집에 살던 것을 나는 기억하고 있으니.\n\n해럴드 스미스가 방금 프티백 경으로 정부에 들어갔으니, 지금 이 순간이라면 청하기만 해도 이것을 얻어 낼 수 있으리라고 나는 생각해요. 그는 내 청을 딱 잘라 거절하지 못할 사람이고, 당신이 한마디만 해 주면 내가 그에게 말을 넣겠어요. 당신이 직접 올라오는 편이 좋겠지만, 우선 전신으로 “예” 아니면 “아니오” 한마디를 보내 주세요.\n\n물론 그러시겠지만 “예”라고 하실 참이면 꼭 올라오세요. 나는 “트래벌러스”나 하원에 있을 겁니다. 그 자리는 당신에게 딱 맞아요 — 수고로울 일은 하나도 없고, 당신의 지위를 높여 주고, 침대와 식탁에, 여물 시렁과 여물통에까지 얼마간 보태 줄 테니.\n\n언제나 그대의 벗,\n\n참으로 묘하게도, 당신 동생이 새 프티백 경의 개인 비서라는 이야기를 들었어요. 그의 주된 임무란 하인들더러 내 누이의 마차를 부르라고 이르는 일이 될 거라더군요. 해럴드가 취임한 뒤로 나는 그를 한 번밖에 보지 못했지만, 레이디 프티백은 그 일이 있은 뒤로 그의 키가 한 치는 자랐다고 하더군요.\n\n이는 분명 사워비 씨로서는 매우 선량한 짓이었고, 자기가 그 목사 벗에게 끼친 해악에 대해 무언가 갚아야 할 것이 있다는 느낌을 가슴에 품고 있음을 보여 주었다. 그리고 실상이 정말 그러했다. 서바셋주 의원보다 더 앞뒤 없는 인간은 있을 수 없었다. 그는 자신에 대해서도 앞뒤가 없었고, 자기와 얽히게 되는 다른 모든 사람에 대해서도 앞뒤가 없었다. 그는 제 자신을 망친 것과 마찬가지로 거의 아무 뉘우침 없이 벗들을 망칠 수 있었다. 그의 그물 앞에 들어온 것은 무엇이든 정당한 사냥감이었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 선량했고, 자기 길에 그럴 기회가 닿기만 하면 벗에게 좋은 일을 해 주기 위해 하늘과 땅을 움직일 마음이 있었다.\n\n그는 정말로 마크 로바츠를, 자기가 아는 누군가를 사랑할 수 있는 만큼은 사랑했다. 그는 자기가 이미 그에게 거의 돌이킬 수 없는 해를 입혔음을 알고 있었고, 그와 볼일이 끝나기 전에 아마도 더 깊이 해를 입힐 가망이 매우 높다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그럴 기회가 닿는다면 틀림없이 그렇게 할 것임은 아주 확실했다. 그러나 또한 곁가지로나마 벗에게 갚아 줄 기회가 닿는다면, 그것 또한 틀림없이 할 것이었다. 그런 기회가 이제 온 것이고, 그래서 그는 누이에게, 새 프티백 경이 빈 참사회원 자리를 마크 로바츠에게 얻어 주는 데 자기 영향력을 다 쓰겠다고 약속할 때까지 그를 가만두지 말라고 일러 두었던 것이다.\n\n사워비의 이 편지를 마크는 즉시 아내에게 보여 주었다. 저 저주받은 돈거래에 대해서는 한마디도 적히지 않았으니 얼마나 다행인가, 하고 그는 속으로 생각했다. 사워비를 더 잘 알았더라면, 그 신사는 절대적으로 필요해지기 전까지는 돈거래에 대해 결코 아무 말도 하지 않는다는 것을 알았을 것이다. “당신이 사워비 씨를 좋아하지 않는 건 알아,” 그가 말했다. “하지만 이건 참 선량한 짓이라는 것은 인정해야지.”\n\n“제가 싫은 건 그 사람에 대해 제가 듣는 평판이에요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 이제 어쩌면 좋겠어, 패니? 그의 말대로, 다른 사람이 아니라 내가 그 자리를 차지하면 안 될 이유가 무엇이겠어?”\n\n“교구 일에 지장은 없겠지요?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“우리가 있는 거리라면 조금도 지장이 없지. 존스 노인을 내보낼까 생각하기도 했지만, 이걸 맡게 되면 물론 부목사를 두어야 하겠지.”\n\n아내는 승진이 제 앞에 왔을 때 그것을 받아들이지 말라고 남편을 만류할 마음을 낼 수 없었다 — 어느 교구 사제의 아내가 남편을 그렇게 설득하겠는가? 그러나 그래도 그녀는 그것이 온전히 마음에 들지 않았다. 그녀는 콜디코츠에서 온 저 그리스인이, 손에 참사회원 자리라는 선물을 들고 올 때에도 두려웠다. 그리고 그렇다면 레이디 러프턴은 무엇이라 할 것인가?\n\n“그러면 당신은 런던으로 올라가야 한다고 생각하시는 거예요, 마크?”\n\n“물론이지. 그러니까, 이 일에서 해럴드 스미스의 호의를 받아들일 작정이라면 말이야.”\n\n“받아들이는 편이 낫겠지요,” 패니가 말했다. 아마도 받아들이지 않기를 바라는 것은 자기로서 부질없는 일이라고 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n“참사회원 자리란 것이, 패니, 교구 성직자들 사이에서 임자를 못 찾고 오래 남아 있는 물건이 아니야. 내 수입에 이만한 보탬을 거절하면서 어떻게 자식들에게 진 의무와 앞뒤를 맞출 수 있겠어?” 그리하여 그가 곧 실버브리지로 마차를 몰고 가 전신으로 소식을 보내고, 이튿날 몸소 런던으로 떠나는 것으로 정해졌다. “하지만 물론 먼저 레이디 러프턴을 뵈어야 해요,” 이 모든 것이 정해지자마자 패니가 말했다.\n\n마크는 점잖게 그럴 수 있었다면 이것을 피했을 것이나, 그것이 무례할 뿐 아니라 정략에도 어긋나는 일이라고 느꼈다. 그리고 지금의 정부로부터 이런 승진을 받게 되기를 바란다고 레이디 러프턴에게 말하기를 왜 두려워해야 하는가? 성직자가 바체스터의 참사회원이 되는 데 부끄러운 일은 하나도 없었다. 레이디 러프턴 자신도 참사회원들에게는 늘 매우 정중했고, 특히 얼마 전에 자연에 진 빚을 갚은 그 앙상하고 작달막한 사내, 작달막한 버슬렘 박사에게 그러했다. 그녀는 늘 성당 참사회를 매우 좋아했고, 프라우디 주교에 대한 그녀의 본래 반감도 주로 그가 대성당 성직자들에게 간섭한 데에 — 그의 간섭에, 또는 그의 아내나 전속 사제의 간섭에 — 뿌리를 두고 있었다. 이런 것들을 헤아리며 마크 로바츠는 레이디 러프턴이 자기의 좋은 운을 기뻐할 것이라고 스스로 믿어 보려 했다. 그러나 그는 그것을 믿지 못했다. 적어도 그녀는 콜디코츠의 그리스인이 주는 선물에는 진저리를 낼 것이었다.\n\n“아, 그렇군요,” 교구 사제가 자기 사정의 온 자락을 힘겹게 설명해 내자 그녀가 말했다. “그러면 축하해요, 로바츠 씨. 힘 있는 새 후원자를 두셨으니.”\n\n“레이디 러프턴, 이 성직록은 제가 이곳 프램리에서 맡은 자리에 아무 지장 없이 함께 지닐 수 있는 것이라는 데는 아마 저와 같이 생각하실 것입니다,” 그가 말했다. 벗들에게 던져진 그 비아냥은 못 들은 척 흘려보내기로 조심스레 작정한 것이었다.\n\n“그러기를 바라요. 물론 로바츠 씨는 아주 젊은 분이고, 이런 것들은 대개 나이가 더 지긋한 성직자들에게 주어져 왔지요.”\n\n“하지만 제가 그것을 거절해야 한다고 말씀하시려는 것은 아니시겠지요?”\n\n“정말로 제 조언을 구하러 오신 것이라면 제 조언이 무엇일지, 이렇게 갑작스러운 통고를 받고서는 저도 뭐라 말할 준비가 되어 있지 않아요. 로바츠 씨는 이미 마음을 정한 듯하니, 그러면 저는 그것을 헤아릴 필요가 없겠지요. 사정이 그러하니 기쁨을 함께 하겠어요. 모든 면에서 로바츠 씨에게 이롭게 되기를 바라요.”\n\n“레이디 러프턴, 저는 아직 그것을 결코 얻은 것이 아니라는 걸 아셔야 합니다.”\n\n“아, 저는 이미 제안을 받으신 줄 알았어요. 그 새 각료가 그 모든 것을 제 손에 쥐고 있는 것처럼 말씀하신 줄 알았으니.”\n\n“아, 아닙니다. 그 점에서 그의 영향력이 얼마만 한지는 저도 전혀 모릅니다. 다만 제게 편지를 보낸 사람이 확언하기로는—”\n\n“사워비 씨 말이지요. 왜 그 사람 이름을 대지 않으시는 거예요?”\n\n“사워비 씨가 확언하기로는 스미스 씨가 그것을 청할 것이며, 그 청이 이루어질 가망이 매우 높다고 합니다.”\n\n“아, 물론 그렇겠지요. 사워비 씨와 해럴드 스미스 씨가 함께라면 무슨 일에서든 틀림없이 이루어 내겠지요. 요즘 세상에서 이루어 내는 사람들이 바로 그런 부류니까요. 그러면 로바츠 씨, 기쁨을 함께 하겠어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 자기 말이 진심이라는 표시로 그에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n마크는 그녀의 손을 잡았고, 그 자리에서는 더 아무 말도 하지 않기로 작정했다. 레이디 러프턴이 예전처럼 자기에게 정을 담아 대하지 않는다는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었다. 그리고 이르든 늦든 그 문제를 그녀와 끝까지 따져 볼 작정이었다. 왜 이제는 자기를 만나면 거의 늘 비아냥으로 맞이하고, 자기가 그토록 잘 알고 그토록 고맙게 여기던 그 다정하고 오래된 미소로는 좀처럼 반겨 주지 않는지 물어보려 했다. 그녀가 정직하고 참되다는 것은 아주 확신하고 있었다. 대놓고 그 질문을 던지면 그녀는 숨김없이 대답해 줄 것이었다. 그리고 예전의 방식으로 돌아가겠다는 말을 그녀에게서 끌어낼 수 있다면, 그녀는 진심을 다해 그리로 돌아올 것이었다. 그러나 그는 바로 지금은 그렇게 할 수 없었다. 크롤리 씨가 그를 찾아온 것이 하루나 이틀 전이었으니, 크롤리 씨를 그곳으로 보낸 것이 레이디 러프턴이었을 법하지 않은가? 지금 이 순간 그의 손은 항변을 하기에 그리 깨끗하지 못했다. 그는 손을 씻을 것이고, 그런 다음에 항변할 것이었다.\n\n“해마다 얼마간은 바체스터에서 사는 게 어떻겠어?” 그날 저녁 그가 아내와 누이에게 말했다.\n\n“집이 둘이면 번거로울 뿐이지요,” 아내가 말했다. “게다가 우리는 여기서 아주 행복했잖아요.”\n\n“저는 대성당이 있는 도시를 늘 좋아했어요,” 루시가 말했다. “그중에도 성당 구내가 특별히 마음에 들고요.”\n\n“게다가 바체스터 구내야말로 온 구내 가운데 가장 꽉 닫힌 구내지,” 마크가 말했다. “그 문 안에 성당 참사회에 딸리지 않은 집은 한 채도 없어.”\n\n“하지만 집을 둘 유지해야 한다면 늘어난 수입은 금세 다 사라질 거예요,” 패니가 조심스레 말했다.\n\n“여름마다 가구까지 얹어서 세를 놓으면 되잖아요,” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 나는 정해진 기간이 오는 대로 거주 의무를 채워야 해,” 교구 사제가 말했다. “그리고 겨울 내내 프램리를 떠나 있는 건 정말이지 마음에 안 들어. 그러면 러프턴을 도무지 볼 수 없게 되지.” 그리고 그는 아마도 자기 사냥을 생각했고, 그다음 다시 저 손을 씻는 일을 생각했다.\n\n“저는 겨울에 떠나 있는 것쯤은 조금도 상관없어요,” 루시가 말했다. 지난 겨울이 자기에게 해 놓은 일을 생각하면서였다.\n\n“하지만 그런 크고 예스러운 집 하나에 가구를 채울 돈을 도대체 어디서 구하겠어요? 부디, 마크, 경솔한 일은 하지 마세요.” 그러면서 아내는 남편의 팔에 애정을 담아 손을 얹었다. 이런 식으로 참사회원 자리 문제가, 그가 런던으로 떠나기 전날 저녁 두 사람 사이에서 오갔다.\n\n해럴드 스미스가 지난 십 년 동안 제 인생의 정치 싸움을 이어 오며 쏟아 온 진지한 노력에 마침내 성공이 왕관을 씌워 주었다. 전임 프티백 경은 인도 개혁에 관한 총리의 생각을 삭일 수 없어 넌더리를 내며 사임했고, 해럴드 스미스 씨가 이런저런 우여곡절을 거친 끝에 그 자리에 앉혀졌다. 해럴드 스미스가 그 높은 관직에 총리 자신이라면 골랐을 만한 사람은 딱히 아니라는 말이 돌았다. 그러나 총리의 손은 사정에 적잖이 묶여 있었다. 그가 마지막으로 했던 큰 임명은 끔찍하게 인심을 잃은 것이어서, 그 자신은 의심할 바 없이 인기가 있었음에도 온 나라의 비명을 그에게 뒤집어씌울 만한 것이었다. 《주피터》는 살을 태우는 조소로, 빅토리아 여왕의 이 시대에 온갖 종류의 악덕이 내각으로 들어가는 통행증으로 여겨져야 하느냐고 물었다. 양원의 반대편 의원들은 순결한 도덕의 갑주를 두르고, 정치판 유베날리스들의 분개한 덕성과 날카로운 불만을 담아 저희 야유를 우레처럼 쏟아 냈다. 그의 벗들조차 낙담해 두 손을 들었다. 이런 사정 아래에서 그는 이번에는 어느 편에게도 특별히 거슬리지 않을 사람을 골라야 한다고 스스로 여겼다. 그런데 해럴드 스미스는 자기 아내와 함께 살고 있었고, 그의 처지는 여느 사람보다 더 궁하지도 않았다. 그는 경마 말을 두지 않았고, 그리고 브록 경이 이제야 처음으로 들은 바로는, 지방 도시들에서 대중적인 주제로 강연을 했다. 그는 어지간히 안전한 의석을 가지고 있었고, 필요하다면 하원을 향해 몇 야드씩 늘어놓을 수도 있었다. 게다가 브록 경은 자기 내각의 기계 전체가 아주 이르게 산산조각 날 것이라는 생각을 크게 품고 있었다. 그 자신의 평판은 나쁘지 않았으나, 그것으로는 자기와 저 최근에 고른 벗의 몫까지 대기에 부족했다. 이 모든 사정이 겹친 아래에서 그는 비어 있는 프티백 경의 자리를 채울 사람으로 해럴드 스미스를 골랐다.\n\n그리고 프티백 경은 몹시 자랑스러웠다. 지난 서너 달 동안 그와 서플하우스 씨는 이 내각을 속히 파멸로 보내 버리자고 뜻을 맞추어 왔다. “이런 식의 독재는 결코 통하지 않을 걸세,” 해럴드 스미스는 여왕 정부에 대한 불신임에 관해 자기가 앞으로 던질 표를 그렇게 정당화하며 스스로 말한 바 있었다. 그리고 서플하우스 씨도 이 문제에서 그와 온전히 뜻이 같았다. 그는 저 못된 늙은 파리스가 그 모습을 아예 업신여겨 버린 유노였으니, 그 역시 복수의 날이 오면 자기가 어느 표결 복도에 서 있을지 마음을 굳게 정해 두었던 것이다. 그러나 이제 해럴드 스미스의 눈에는 사정이 크게 달라져 있었다. 총리는 힘을 구해야 할 곳에서 새 힘을 구하고 제 내각의 몸에 새 피를 들임으로써 자기 지혜를 보여 주었다. 이제 백성이 새로 신뢰를 느낄 것이고, 아마 하원도 그러할 것이다. 서플하우스 씨는 — 그에게는 자기 영향력을 다 써 보겠다. 그러나 결국 서플하우스 씨가 전부는 아니었다.\n\n우리 교구 사제가 런던에 닿은 이튿날 아침 그는 프티백 청에 나갔다. 그것은 다우닝 가와 더 높은 정부의 신들 바로 이웃에 자리하고 있었고, 건물 자체는 별것 아니어서 한쪽은 버팀목으로 받쳐 놓았고 앞면은 불룩 튀어나왔으며 연기에 더럽고 먼지에 꾀죄죄하고 건축의 우아함이라든가 근대 과학의 개량이라 할 것이 하나도 없었으나, 그럼에도 그 위치가 그것에 지위를 주어 프티백 경 청의 서기들을 세상 살아가는 처지에서 아주 어엿한 사람으로 만들어 주었다. 마크는 전날 저녁 벗 사워비를 만났고, 그때 이튿날 아침 새 각료의 청에서 만나기로 약속해 두었다. 그리고 이제 그는 동생과 몇 분 담소를 나눌 수 있도록 약속한 시간보다 조금 이르게 그곳에 와 있었다.\n\n개인 비서의 방에 들어선 마크는, 관등이 바뀌면서 동생의 겉모습에 생겨난 변화를 보고 꽤나 놀랐다. 잭 로바츠는 몸이 잘 잡히고 다리가 곧고 몸놀림이 나긋한 젊은이여서 타고난 이점 덕에 보기에 즐거웠으나, 걸음새가 좀 덜렁대는 쪽이었고 옷차림에도 이따금 무심했다, 아니 지저분하다고 해도 좋았다. 그러나 이제 그는 완벽의 표본 그 자체였다. 멋들어진 프록코트는 그에게 흠 하나 없이 맞았고, 머리카락 한 올도 제자리를 벗어나지 않았고, 조끼와 바지는 윤이 나는 새것이었고, 구석의 우산걸이에 세워 둔 우산은 꼭 여미어져 있고 말끔하고 작고 앙증맞았다.\n\n“아, 존, 너 아주 큰사람이 됐구나,” 형이 말했다.\n\n“그건 잘 모르겠어요,” 존이 말했다. “다만 제가 엄청나게 뼈 빠지는 일을 치러야 한다는 것은 알겠어요.”\n\n“일 말이냐? 나는 네가 온 관청 자리 가운데 가장 편한 자리에 있는 줄 알았는데.”\n\n“아! 바로 그게 사람들이 하는 착각이에요. 우리가 한 면에 열다섯 줄, 한 줄에 다섯 낱말씩으로 대판 서류지를 몇 연씩 메우지 않으니까, 사람들은 우리 개인 비서들이 하는 일이 없다고 생각하지요. 이걸 보세요,” 그러면서 그는 작은 쪽지 열두 통쯤을 업신여기듯 툭 던져 놓았다. “형, 내가 말하는데, 각료의 후원 인사를 다루는 것은 쉬운 일이 아니에요. 지금 나는 이 사람들 하나하나에게 그를 흡족하게 할 편지를 써야 하는데, 그러면서도 그들이 청하는 부탁은 하나하나 다 거절해야 하고요.”\n\n“그건 어렵겠구나.”\n\n“어렵다는 말로는 어림도 없어요. 하지만 결국은 대개 그 요령에 달린 일이지요. ‘아니오라는 저 날카롭고 쏘아 대는 말에서 침을 뽑아내는’ 재간이 있어야 해요. 나는 그것을 매일 하고 있고, 사람들이 정말로 그걸 좋아한다는 생각까지 들어요.”\n\n“네 거절이 다른 사람들의 승낙보다 나은지도 모르겠구나.”\n\n“그런 뜻은 전혀 아니에요. 우리 개인 비서들은 다 똑같은 일을 해야 해요. 자, 이걸 믿을 수 있겠어요? 프티백 청에 로비 사환 자리가 비지 않았다고 사람들에게 알리는 데에만 나는 벌써 편지지 세 뭉치를 다 썼어요. 귀족 부인 일곱이 저마다 아끼는 시종을 그 자리에 넣어 달라고 청해 왔지요. 그런데 저기 — 저기 프티백 경이!”\n\n종이 울리자 개인 비서는 편지지에서 벌떡 일어나 그 큰사람의 방으로 잰걸음에 가 버렸다.\n\n“곧 만나 주시겠다는군요,” 그가 돌아와 말했다. “버긴스, 로바츠 목사님을 프티백 경께 안내해.”\n\n버긴스는 온 귀족 부인들이 그토록 기운차게 다투던, 비어 있지 않은 그 자리의 사환이었다. 그리고 마크는 버긴스를 두 걸음 따라가서 다음 방으로 안내되었다.\n\n개인 비서가 되는 것으로 사람이 달라진다면, 내각의 각료가 되는 것으로는 훨씬 더 달라진다. 로바츠는 그 방에 들어서면서, 이 사람이 바체스터의 강연장에서 프라우디 부인에게 그토록 모질게 시달렸던 그 해럴드 스미스와 같은 사람이라고는 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다. 그때 그는 짜증스럽고, 신경이 곤두서고, 안절부절못하고, 대수롭지 않았다. 이제 관사 벽난로 앞 깔개에 서서 웃음을 지으니, 그의 얼굴을 밝히는 그 친절하고 생색내는 웃음을 보는 것이 꽤나 즐거웠다. 그는 바지 주머니에 두 손을 넣고 그곳의 큰사람으로서, 자기 경 작위를 의식하며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 각료임을 느끼며 거기 서 있기를 흡족해했다. 사워비가 그와 함께 와서 조금 뒤쪽에 서 있었는데, 그 자리에서 이따금 각료의 어깨 너머로 목사에게 눈짓을 보냈다.\n\n“아, 로바츠, 만나서 반갑소. 그런데 참, 당신 동생이 내 개인 비서라니 얼마나 묘한 일이오!” 마크는 별난 우연이라고 말했다.\n\n“아주 영민한 젊은이오. 제 몸가짐만 잘 지키면 잘될 것이오.”\n\n“그 아이는 틀림없이 잘할 겁니다,” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“아! 그렇지, 그렇소. 나도 그럴 것이라 생각하오. 그런데 로바츠, 내가 당신에게 무엇을 해 주면 되겠소?”\n\n여기서 사워비 씨가 끼어들어, 로바츠 씨 자신은 무엇을 청할 뜻이 결코 없다는 것, 다만 그의 벗들이 바체스터의 이 참사회원 자리가 요즘 어느 성직자의 손에 놓이는 것보다 그의 손에 놓이는 것이 더 걸맞다고 생각했으므로, 그가 새 프티백 경만큼 깊이 존경하는 사람으로부터 그 영전을 받을 마음이 있다는 것을 설명으로 분명히 밝혔다.\n\n각료는 이것이 그리 마음에 들지 않았다. 그것이 그가 은혜를 베풀어 몸을 낮출 여지를 많이 좁혀 놓고, 마크 로바츠가 자기에게 올릴 것으로 기대했던 청원이라는 향을 빼앗아 갔기 때문이다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 매우 은근했다.\n\n“바체스터에 비어 있는 그 성직 영전에 관해 브록 경의 뜻이 무엇일지는 자기가 나서서 밝힐 수 없는 일이다,” 그가 말했다. “그 일에 대해서는 이미 그 경에게 분명히 말을 넣어 두었고, 자기 뜻이 참작될 것이라 믿을 만한 근거도 얼마간 있는 듯하다. 확실한 약속이 이루어진 것은 아니나, 그런 결과를 기대한다고까지는 아마 말해도 좋을 것이다. 그리 된다면 로바츠 씨가 그 자리를 차지한 것을 축하하는 일이 자기에게는 세상에서 가장 큰 기쁨이 될 것이다 — 로바츠 씨가 위엄과 경건과 형제 사랑으로 채울 것이라 확신하는 자리이니.” 그러고서 그가 말을 마치자 사워비 씨는 마지막으로 눈짓을 한 번 하고서, 이 일은 정해진 것으로 여기겠다고 말했다.\n\n“아니, 정해진 게 아니야, 나다니엘,” 신중한 각료가 말했다.\n\n“그게 그거지,” 사워비가 되받았다. “그 온갖 겉치레가 무슨 뜻인지는 우리가 다 알아. 마크, 관직에 있는 사람들은 결코 확실한 약속을 하지 않아요 — 저희 부엌 불 앞에서 구워지고 있는 양 다리에 대해서도 저희 자신에게 약속을 하지 않지요. 요즘은 안전해야 하는 것이 그토록 긴한 일이니까. 그렇지 않은가, 해럴드?”\n\n“가장 요긴하지,” 해럴드 스미스가 지혜롭게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. “그래, 로바츠, 이번엔 누구요?” 이 말은 어느 거물이 왔음을 알리려 들어온 개인 비서에게 한 것이었다. “좋아, 그러지. 미안하지만 이만 작별 인사를 하겠소. 좀 급한 일이 있어서요. 그리고 기억해 두시오, 로바츠 씨, 내가 당신을 위해 할 수 있는 일은 하겠소. 다만 약속이란 것은 없다는 걸 분명히 이해하셔야 하오.”\n\n“아, 약속 같은 건 전혀 없지 — 물론 아니고,” 사워비가 말했다. 그리고 그는 로바츠를 팔에 끼고 화이트홀을 거슬러 채링 크로스 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸으면서, 콜디코츠 마구간에서 제 머리를 먹어 치우고 있는 그 값을 헤아릴 수 없는 사냥말을 사라고 그에게 다시 졸라 댔다.\n\n# 제19장. 돈거래\n\n사워비 씨는 프램리의 교구 사제에게 이 좋은 선물을 안겨 주기로 마음을 굳히면서, 프티백 경과 가까운 인연이 있다는 그 하나의 영향력에만 온전히 기대지는 않았다. 그는 이번 일이 그보다 더 높은 권세까지 움직여 볼 만한 자리라고 느꼈고, 그래서 공작에게 그 문제를 꺼내 두었다 — 직접 청원한 것이 아니라 포더길 씨를 거쳐서였다. 사정을 아는 사람이라면 그런 일로 공작에게 곧바로 가겠다는 생각은 아무도 하지 않았다. 여자에 대해서나 말에 대해서나 그림에 대해서 이야기하고 싶다면 공작은 때로 넉넉히 상냥할 수도 있었다.\n\n그러나 포더길 씨를 통해 공작에게 말이 닿았다. 프램리의 성직자를 러프턴 편에서 이렇게 사들이는 것이 아말렉 사람들의 재물을 노략하는 갸륵한 일이 될 것이라고, 얼마간 꾀를 부려 아뢰어졌다. 그렇게 하면 옴니엄의 세력이 대성당 구내에까지 발판을 얻게 될 것이었다. 게다가 로바츠 씨가 러프턴 경 자신에게 적잖은 영향력을 지녔다는 것은 모두가 아는 바였다. 그렇게 이끌리자 옴니엄 공작은 총리에게 두 마디를 하기는 했고, 공작에게서 나온 두 마디는 브록 경에게조차 큰 힘을 미쳤다. 이 모든 일의 결말은, 마크 로바츠가 그 참사회원 자리를 얻었다는 것이었다. 그러나 그는 프램리로 돌아간 뒤 며칠이 지나서야 자기가 성공했다는 소식을 들었다.\n\n사워비 씨는 공작이 이 일로 기울인 그 큰 노력 — 콜디코츠의 그가 부른 대로는 유례없는 노력 — 을 그에게 알려 주는 것을 잊지 않았다. “각하께서 전에 언제 그런 일을 하셨는지 나는 알지 못해요,” 사워비가 말했다. “그리고 이것만은 아주 확실히 여겨도 좋아요. 각하께서 청하셨을 때 당신이 개서럼 성에 가지 않았더라면 지금 그 일을 해 주시지 않았을 겁니다. 아니, 포더길도 그것을 시도해 보아야 헛일이라는 것을 알았겠지요. 그리고 마크, 내 말을 들어 봐요 — 내가 내 둥지를 깎아내리는 것은 온당치 않지만, 나는 참으로 각하의 한마디가 프티백 경의 엄숙한 탄원보다 더 효험이 있을 것이라고 믿어요.”\n\n마크는 물론 마땅한 대로 고마움을 표했고, 그러고서 그 말을 백삼십 파운드에 사들였다. “그놈은 그만한 값이 넉넉히 있어요,” 사워비가 말했다. “네 발로 서 본 어느 짐승에게도 뒤지지 않지요. 그리고 내가 그놈을 당신에게 굳이 권하는 유일한 이유는, 토저의 날이 돌아올 때 당신이 우리를 위해 그쯤 되는 액수를 대야 할 것을 내가 알기 때문이에요.” 어째서 그 말을 다른 누군가에게 팔아서 정해진 방식으로 돈을 마련하지 않느냐고 물어볼 생각은 마크에게 떠오르지 않았다. 그러나 그것은 사워비 씨에게 맞는 일이 아니었을 것이다.\n\n마크는 그 짐승이 좋은 것임을 알았고, 숙소로 걸어가면서 새로 얻은 소유물에 반쯤 뿌듯했다. 그러나 그렇다면 그것을 아내에게 어떻게 변명할 것인가? 아니면 그 일에 아무 변명도 하지 않은 채로 그 짐승을 어떻게 자기 마구간에 들여놓을 것인가? 게다가 자기 수입의 절대 액수만 놓고 본다면, 마음에 들 때 말 한 마리 새로 사는 것쯤은 제 권리라고 여겨도 좋지 않은가? 그는 이 새 매입 소식을 들으면 크롤리 씨가 무엇이라 할지 궁금했다. 그는 요사이 벗들과 이웃들이 자기를 두고 무엇이라 말할지 몹시 궁금해하는 상태에 빠져 있었다.\n\n그는 이제 이틀 동안 도시에 있었고, 이튿날 아침 식사 후에 내려가서 금요일 오후에는 집에 닿을 참이었다. 그러나 그날 저녁, 막 자리에 들려 할 때 러프턴 경이 그가 머무는 호텔의 커피실로 들어오는 바람에 그는 놀랐다. 그는 다급한 걸음으로 걸어 들어왔고, 얼굴이 붉었으며, 몹시 노여워하고 있는 것이 분명했다.\n\n“로바츠,” 그가 벗에게 다가와 내미는 손을 잡으며 말했다. “자네, 이 토저라는 사람에 대해 무엇 아는 것이 있나?”\n\n“토저 — 무슨 토저? 사워비가 그런 사람을 말하는 것을 들은 적이 있네.”\n\n“물론 들었겠지. 내가 잘못 안 것이 아니라면 자네 자신이 그 사람에 대해 나에게 편지를 쓴 적이 있어.”\n\n“그럴 법하네. 사워비가 자네 일과 관련해서 그 사람을 언급한 것을 기억하네. 그런데 왜 나에게 묻는 건가?”\n\n“이 사람이 나에게 편지를 쓴 것으로도 모자라서, 내가 만찬 옷을 입고 있는데 아주 억지로 내 방까지 밀고 들어왔어. 그리고 뻔뻔스럽게도, 자기가 쥐고 있는 팔백 파운드 어음을 내가 결제하지 않으면 나를 걸어 법으로 나오겠다고 하더군.”\n\n“하지만 자네는 그 문제를 사워비와 다 정리했잖은가?”\n\n“정리했지, 나에게 아주 큰 비용을 물려 가면서. 시끄러운 일을 겪기보다는, 바보 천치처럼, 그가 청구한 모든 것을 터무니없이 비싸게 물어 주었어. 이건 순전한 사기야. 그리고 계속 이런다면 나는 이것을 사기로 폭로하겠어.”\n\n로바츠는 방을 둘러보았으나, 다행히 그 안에는 그들 둘 말고는 한 사람도 없었다. “사워비가 자네를 속여 먹고 있다는 말은 아니겠지?” 성직자가 말했다.\n\n“그렇게 보이네,” 러프턴 경이 말했다. “그리고 자네에게 솔직히 말하는데, 나는 이런 일을 더 견딜 기분이 아니야. 몇 해 전에 나는 저 사람의 잘못 때문에 얼간이 짓을 했어. 그러나 사천 파운드면 내가 실제로 잃은 것을 다 덮었을 거야. 나는 이제 그 액수의 세 배가 넘게 물어 주었어. 그러니, 하늘에 맹세하고! 이 일을 온통 드러내지 않고서는 더는 물지 않겠어.”\n\n“그런데 러프턴, 나는 이해가 안 되네. 그 어음이란 것이 무엇인가? — 자네 이름이 적혀 있나?”\n\n“그래, 적혀 있네. 내 이름을 부인하지는 않을 것이고, 정말로 필요하다면 물어 주겠어. 그러나 내가 물게 된다면 내 변호사가 그것을 낱낱이 캐게 할 것이고, 배심 앞에까지 가게 하겠어.”\n\n“하지만 그 어음들은 다 결제된 줄 알았는데?”\n\n“갱신할 때 구 어음들을 회수하는 일은 사워비에게 맡겨 두었어. 그런데 이제 실제로는 이미 결제된 것 하나가 나에게 청구되고 있어.”\n\n마크는 자기가 손수 서명한 두 문서를 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그 둘은 이제 틀림없이 토저의, 아니면 같은 업을 하는 다른 어느 신사의 손에 있었다. 그 둘 다 자기에게 청구될 수 있었으니, 첫째 것을 물어 주기만 하면 곧 둘째 것이 나올 것이었다. 그리고 그는 사워비가 미결 어음 한 장에 대해, 그것을 처리하려면 얼마 안 되는 돈을 물어야 한다고 자기에게 말했던 것을 떠올렸고, 이것을 러프턴 경에게 일러 주었다.\n\n“그런데 자네는 팔백 파운드를 얼마 안 되는 돈이라고 부르나? 나는 그렇지 않네.”\n\n“아마 그런 요구까지는 하지 않을 걸세.”\n\n“하지만 내 말은 그들이 그런 요구를 한다는 거야. 이미 했어. 내가 만난 그 사람은, 자기가 토저의 벗이라고 했지만 아마 토저 자신이었을 텐데, 이레나 열흘 안에 돈이 나오지 않으면 법으로 절차를 밟지 않을 수 없을 것이라고 나에게 딱 잘라 맹세하더군. 그것이 갱신된 구 어음이라고 내가 설명하니까, 자기 벗이 그것에 값을 온전히 다 치렀다고 우기더군.”\n\n“사워비는 자네가 그것을 되찾는 데 십 파운드쯤 물어야 할 것이라고 했네. 나라면 그 사람에게 그쯤 되는 액수를 내밀겠네.”\n\n“내 뜻은 그 사람에게 아무것도 내밀지 않고, 이 일을 내 변호사의 손에 맡겨 아무도 봐주지 말라고 지시하는 것이네. 나 자신도, 다른 누구도. 나는 사워비 같은 사람이 나를 오렌지처럼 짜내도록 두지 않겠어.”\n\n“하지만 러프턴, 자네는 나에게 노여워하는 것처럼 보이네.”\n\n“아니, 그렇지 않아. 그러나 이 사람에 대해서는 자네에게 조심하라고 일러 두는 것이 좋겠다고 생각하네. 요사이 그와의 거래는 주로 자네를 거쳐 왔으니, 그래서 —”\n\n“하지만 그것은 그와 자네의 뜻을 따라 그렇게 된 것일 뿐이네. 내가 자네들 둘 다에게 편의를 봐주고 싶었으니까. 자네가 나더러 이 어음들에 관계가 있다는 말을 하려는 것은 아니겠지.”\n\n“나는 자네가 그 사람과 어음으로 얽혀 있다는 것을 알고 있네.”\n\n“아니, 러프턴, 그러면 자네가 사기라고 부른 이 거래에서 내가 무슨 이해를 챙겼다고 나를 나무라는 것으로 알아들어야 하나?”\n\n“나에 관한 한 사기가 있었고, 지금도 사기가 벌어지고 있어.”\n\n“그러나 자네는 내 물음에 대답하지 않고 있네. 나에게 무슨 혐의를 씌우는 것인가? 그렇다면 나도 자네 생각에 찬성하네. 자네는 변호사에게 가는 편이 낫겠어.”\n\n“나도 그럴 생각이네.”\n\n“아주 좋아. 그러나 아무리 헤아려 보아도 나는 이보다 더 사리에 어긋난 사람, 이보다 더 사람 생각이 온당치 못한 사람을 들어 본 적이 없네. 오로지 자네를 돕겠다는 마음으로, 오로지 자네가 청하기에 나는 자네의 그 돈거래에 대해 사워비에게 말했네. 그러고 나서 그가 청하기에 — 그것도 자네가 청한 데서 비롯한 것이고, 자네가 나를 자네 사절로 그에게 보냈던 것처럼 그도 나를 자기 사절로 자네에게 보낸 것인데 — 나는 자네에게 편지를 쓰고 말을 했네. 그런데 이제 결말이 이렇군.”\n\n“나는 자네에게 아무 혐의도 씌우지 않네, 로바츠. 그러나 자네가 이 사람과 거래하고 있다는 것은 알고 있어. 자네가 나에게 스스로 그렇게 말했어.”\n\n“그렇네. 그가 청하기에 편의를 봐주려고 어음에 내 이름을 적었네.”\n\n“한 장에만?”\n\n“한 장에만. 그리고 그 뒤에 그것이 갱신된 것에, 아니 정확히는 같은 것이 아니라 그것을 대신하는 것에 적었네. 처음 것은 사백 파운드였고, 나중 것은 오백이었어.”\n\n“그것을 자네가 다 메워야 할 테고, 그러면 세상은 물론 자네가 바체스터의 그 참사회원 자리에 그 값을 치렀다고 말할 걸세.”\n\n이것은 견디기에 끔찍한 말이었다. 그는 요사이 자기를 놀래고 겁먹게 한 말을 많이 들었으나, 이처럼 끔찍한 것은 없었다. 이처럼 그를 멍하게 만드는 것, 참담과 파멸이 그토록 무섭게 실제라는 것을 그의 마음에 전해 주는 것은 없었다. 그는 곧바로 대답하지 않고, 불을 등지고 벽난로 앞 깔개에 서서 방 저편 끝까지 눈길을 던졌다. 여태 그의 눈은 러프턴 경의 얼굴에 붙어 있었으나, 이제는 러프턴 경과 자기 사이에 남은 일이 별로 없는 듯이 여겨졌다. 러프턴 경도, 러프턴 경의 어머니도 이제는 자기가 잘되기를 바라는 사람으로 헤아릴 수 없었다. 그렇다면 이제 누구를 헤아릴 수 있단 말인가? 자기가 이 모든 비참을 안겨 주고 있는, 품 안의 그 아내밖에.\n\n그 괴로움의 순간에 여러 생각이 그의 머릿속을 빠르게 지나갔다. 그는 바체스터의 이 영전을, 자기가 그것을 사들였다고 그토록 그럴싸하게 말할 수 있는 그 영전을 당장 버릴 것이었다. 해럴드 스미스에게 가서 딱 잘라 사양하겠다고 말할 것이었다. 그러고서 집에 돌아가 아내에게 일어난 일을 다 이야기할 것이었다 — 아직 무슨 도움이 될 수 있다면 레이디 러프턴에게도 다 이야기할 것이었다. 그 두 어음이 제시되는 대로 갚을 방도를 마련할 것이었고, 청구가 정당한지 아닌지 묻지도 않고, 누구에게도, 심지어 사워비에게도 불평하지 않을 것이었다. 필요하다면 수입의 절반을, 절반이 필요하다면 그만큼을 은행가 포레스트의 손에 맡겨 모두 갚아질 때까지 두겠다. 가진 말은 모두 팔아 버릴 것이었다. 하인과 마부도 내보낼 것이었고, 아무튼 사내답게 애써서 다시 든든한 땅에 발을 굳게 딛겠다. 그때 그 순간 그는 자기가 놓인 처지를, 그리고 자기를 거기 놓은 자기 자신의 어리석음을 온 영혼으로 진저리 냈다. 사워비와 해럴드 스미스와 함께 런던에 와서, 그런 일에는 도무지 힘이 없어야 마땅한 사람에게 성직의 영전을 청원하고, 말을 사고, 기한이 지난 어음을 처리하고 있다는 것을 어떻게 자기 양심과 앞뒤를 맞출 수 있겠는가? 그는 그것을 자기 양심과 맞추지 못했다. 크롤리 씨가 그를 버림받은 자라고 말한 것은 옳았다.\n\n러프턴 경은 이 만남 내내 노여움이 극에 달해 있었고 말을 할수록 더 노여워졌는데, 이제 방 안을 한두 번 오르내리며 걸었다. 그리고 그렇게 걷다가 자기가 온당하지 못했다는 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다. 그는 사워비를 규탄하고, 이 어음 문제로 자기 곧 러프턴 경이 조금이라도 더 시달리게 된다면 이 일 전체를 변호사의 손에 넘기겠다는 것을 로바츠가 그 신사에게 전하도록 만들 뜻으로 여기에 온 것이었다. 그런데 그렇게 하는 대신 그는 로바츠에게 혐의를 씌워 놓았다. 로바츠가 근래 이 모든 지긋지긋한 돈거래에서 자기 편보다 사워비 편이 되어 버렸다는 것이 그의 속을 긁었고, 그래서 이제 그는 뜻했던 것보다 훨씬 강한 말로 제 마음을 털어놓아 버렸던 것이다.\n\n“자네 개인에 대해서는, 마크,” 로바츠가 서 있는 자리로 돌아오며 그가 말했다. “자네 속을 긁을 말은 하나도 하고 싶지 않네.”\n\n“이미 넉넉히 말했네, 러프턴 경.”\n\n“내가 받은 대접에 노엽고 분한 것을 자네가 놀랍게 여길 수야 없겠지.”\n\n“나는 자네가 마음속에서, 자네에게 잘못을 저지른 사람들을 — 그런 잘못이 있었다면 — 다만 자네의 뜻과 기쁨을 이루어 주려 애쓴 사람들과 갈라 놓을 수도 있었으리라 생각하네. 내가 성직자로서 이런 일에 조금이라도 손을 댄 것이 매우 잘못이라는 것은 나도 잘 알고 있네. 사내로서 사워비 씨에게 내 이름을 빌려준 것이 터무니없이 어리석었다는 것도 넉넉히 알고 있어. 이것을 얼마간 무례하게 일러 받는 것도 어쩌면 잘된 일이겠지. 그러나 그 가르침이 자네에게서 올 줄은 정말 몰랐네.”\n\n“그래, 해악은 넉넉히 저질러졌네. 문제는 이제 우리 둘이 무엇을 하는 것이 좋겠느냐는 것이지.”\n\n“자네는 하려는 일을 말했네. 이 일을 변호사의 손에 맡길 것이라고.”\n\n“자네를 드러내려는 뜻은 조금도 없어.”\n\n“나를 드러낸다고, 러프턴 경! 아니, 남이 들으면 내가 자네 돈을 만졌다고 여기겠네.”\n\n“자네가 내 말을 잘못 알아듣는 걸세. 나는 그런 생각은 하지 않네. 그러나 이 참담한 일에 법의 절차가 밟히면 자네와 사워비 사이의 처리가 밝은 데로 드러날 것을 자네 스스로 모르지 않을 텐데?”\n\n“나와 사워비 사이의 처리라는 것은, 내가 대가로 받은 것도 앞으로 받을 것도 전혀 없는 큰돈을 그의 셈으로 갚거나 갚아야 하는 것으로 그칠 걸세.”\n\n“그러면 바체스터의 그 참사회원 자리에 대해서는 무슨 말이 나겠나?”\n\n“자네가 방금 나에게 씌운 혐의를 듣고서는, 나는 그것을 받는 것을 사양하겠네.”\n\n이 순간 다른 신사 서너 사람이 방으로 들어왔고, 우리 두 벗의 이야기는 끊어졌다. 두 사람은 여전히 불 가까이 서 있었으나, 몇 분 동안 어느 쪽도 아무 말을 하지 않았다. 로바츠는 러프턴 경이 가 주기를 기다리고 있었고, 러프턴 경은 자기가 하려고 온 말을 아직 하지 못했다. 마침내 그가 거의 속삭이듯이 다시 입을 열었다. “사워비에게 내일 내 방으로 오라고 청하는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 생각하네. 그리고 자네도 거기서 그를 만나는 것이 좋겠다고 생각해.”\n\n“내가 그 자리에 있어야 할 까닭을 나는 모르겠네,” 로바츠가 말했다. “자네 일에 끼어든 값으로 나는 넉넉히 고생할 것 같으니, 더는 끼어들지 않겠네.”\n\n“물론 자네를 오게 만들 수야 없지. 그러나 그것이 사워비에게 다만 공평한 일이겠고, 나에게는 은혜가 될 걸세.”\n\n로바츠는 다시 대여섯 번 방 안을 오르내리며, 지금의 이 다급한 처지에서 어떻게 하는 것이 자기에게 가장 어울릴지 마음을 정하려 애썼다. 자기 이름이 법정 앞에 끌려 나온다면 — 융통 어음에 관계했다고 신문에 드러나게 된다면, 그것은 틀림없이 자기에게 파멸이 될 것이었다. 이 거래에서 자기가 한 몫을 세상이 어떻게 말할지는 러프턴 경의 빗댄 말에서 이미 배운 바였다! 그리고 아내는 — 그런 폭로를 어떻게 견딜 것인가?\n\n“한 가지 조건이 있으면 내일 자네 방에서 사워비 씨를 만나겠네,” 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n“그 조건이 무엇인가?”\n\n“내가 사워비 씨와의 어떤 돈 문제에서도, 자네 일에서든 다른 누구의 일에서든 무슨 금전상의 이해를 챙겼다고 자네가 나를 의심하지 않는다는 확실한 다짐을 받는 것이네.”\n\n“나는 자네를 그런 일로 의심한 적이 없네. 다만 자네가 그와 얽혀 있다고 생각했을 뿐이야.”\n\n“그리고 나는 얽혀 있네 — 나는 그 어음들에 책임이 있어. 그러나 자네는 알았어야 하고, 또 알고 있듯이, 나는 그 책임을 지고서 한 실링도 받은 적이 없네. 나는 처음에는 자네의 벗으로, 그다음에는 나 자신의 벗으로 여긴 사람에게 편의를 봐주려 애썼을 뿐이고, 그 결과가 이것이네.”\n\n커피실 탁자 하나에 머리를 맞대고 앉아 있는 동안 러프턴 경은 마침내 그가 바라던 다짐을 주었다. 그리고 그러고 나서 로바츠는 프램리로 돌아가는 것을 토요일로 미루어, 이튿날 오후에 앨버니에 있는 러프턴 경의 사택에서 사워비를 만나겠다고 약속했다. 이렇게 정해지자마자 러프턴 경은 작별을 하고 제 길로 갔다.\n\n그 뒤로 딱한 마크는 매우 편치 못한 밤을 지냈다. 러프턴 경이, 바체스터의 그 참사회원 자리가 이 추천권을 쥔 사람에게 그 지명을 받은 자가 대 주기로 한 어떤 돈 융통에 대한 금전상의 보답으로 주어진 것이라고 생각했다는 것은 — 지금도 여전히 그렇게 생각하지 않는다 해도 — 넉넉히 분명했다. 세상에 이보다 더 나쁜 일은 없었다. 첫째로 그것은 성직 매매일 것이었고, 그다음으로는 말로 다 할 수 없이 비열하고 성직 매매다운 성직 매매일 것이었다. 그것을 생각만 해도 마크의 영혼은 두려움과 당혹으로 가득 찼다. 러프턴 경의 의심은 이제 가라앉았을지 모른다. 그러나 다른 사람들도 똑같이 생각할 것이고, 그들의 의심은 달랠 도리가 없을 것이었다. 그 다른 사람들이란 바깥 세상 사람들일 터인데, 그들은 성직자의 악덕이 들통난 것을 흐뭇하게 곱씹기를 언제나 그토록 갈망한다.\n\n그리고 그가 사들인 그 지긋지긋한 말은, 그것을 사들였다는 것 하나만으로도 사워비 씨와의 이 거래에서 값나가는 것이 자기에게 하나도 돌아오지 않았다는 말을 할 수 없게 만드는데 — 그것은 또 어떻게 할 것인가? 게다가 요사이 그는 넉넉히 감당할 수 있는 것보다 많은 돈을 써 왔고, 계속 쓰고 있었다. 참사회원직을 쥐겠다는 희망을 다 버려야 한다면, 이번 런던 걸음은 가장 무분별한 일이 될 것이었다. 그것은 마음을 정한 일이었다. 그러나 그러고 나서 그는, 사람이 그런 괴로움 속에서 늘 그러는 것처럼 정한 마음을 다시 풀었다. 러프턴 경에 대한 분노가 처음 치솟던 순간에 스스로 그려 놓았던 그 행실의 길, 그것을 따르자면 가난과 조롱과 불편을, 높은 희망의 소멸과 야심의 파멸을 맞아야 하는 그 길이 — 그는 몇 번이고 되풀이해 스스로에게 말했다 — 이제 자기에게 가장 좋은 길일 것이었다. 그러나 높은 희망을 버리고 가난과 조롱과 불편을 기꺼이 맞기가 우리에게 얼마나 어려운가!\n\n그러나 이튿날 아침 그는 바체스터의 참사회원 자리를 더는 바라지 않는다는 것을 해럴드 스미스에게 알리기로 마음먹고 담대하게 프티백 청으로 걸어 내려갔다. 그는 거기서 동생이 여전히 버긴스의 비어 있지 않은 자리에 대해 애타는 귀족 부인들에게 재주 있는 쪽지를 쓰고 있는 것을 보았다. 그러나 그곳의 큰사람, 프티백 경 자신은 거기 없었다. 하원이 열릴 무렵, 어쩌면 네 시나 조금 지나서 잠깐 들여다볼 법도 했으나, 아침에 청에 나와 있을 일은 확실히 없었다. 프티백 경의 직무는 틀림없이 다른 곳에서 수행되고 있었다. 어쩌면 그는 일을 집으로 들고 갔을지도 모른다 — 세상이 알아 두어야 할 일이지만, 열의가 넘치는 공무원에게는 드물지 않은 관행이다.\n\n마크는 동생에게 속을 털어놓고 그에게 전갈을 남길 생각도 해 보았다. 그러나 용기가 나지 않았다. 아니, 어쩌면 신중함이 그를 막았다고 하는 것이 더 정확할지도 모른다. 다른 누구에게 말하기 전에 아내에게 먼저 말하는 것이 좋겠다고 그는 생각했다. 그래서 그는 반 시간쯤 동생과 이야기만 나누고 그를 떠났다.\n\n그날은 러프턴 경의 방으로 갈 시간이 오기까지 몹시 지루했다. 그러나 마침내 그 시간이 왔고, 시계가 딱 치는 참에 그는 피카딜리를 벗어나 앨버니로 들어섰다. 건물에 들어서기 전에 앞마당을 지나가는데 바로 등 뒤에서 어떤 목소리가 그를 맞았다.\n\n“바체스터 탑의 큰 시계만큼이나 정확하시군요,” 사워비 씨가 말했다. “큰사람의 부름을 받는다는 것이 어떤 것인지 보시라고요, 참사회원님.”\n\n그는 돌아서서 기계처럼 사워비 씨에게 손을 내밀었다. 그리고 그를 바라보면서, 그가 이토록 겉모습이 즐겁고 이토록 근심에서 자유롭고 이토록 몸가짐이 흥겨운 것을 전에 본 적이 없다고 생각했다.\n\n“러프턴 경에게서 소식을 들었지요,” 마크가 확실히 매우 침울한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“그에게서 들었느냐고요! 아, 그야 물론 들었지요. 내 말을 들어 봐요, 마크,” 그리고 이제 그는 두 사람이 함께 앨버니의 복도를 걸어가는 동안 거의 속삭이듯이 말했다. “러프턴은 돈 문제에서는 어린애예요 — 완전한 어린애지요. 세상에서 가장 아끼고 가장 훌륭한 벗인 것은 알지만, 돈 문제에서는 아주 젖먹이예요.” 그러고서 그들은 나리의 방으로 들어섰다.\n\n러프턴 경의 얼굴도 넉넉히 침울했으나, 이것은 사워비를 조금도 주춤하게 하지 못했다. 그는 조금도 흐트러지지 않은 걸음걸이로, 흡족함이 빛나는 얼굴로 젊은 경에게 성큼성큼 걸어갔다.\n\n“그래, 러프턴, 어떻게 지냅니까?” 그가 말했다. “보아하니 내 갸륵한 벗 토저가 당신에게 얼마간 골칫거리를 안겼다지요?”\n\n그러자 러프턴 경은 흡족함이라고는 조금도 빛나지 않는 얼굴로, 토저가 자기에게 들이댄 그 부정한 요구의 이야기를 다시 시작했다. 사워비는 그의 말을 끊지 않고 끝까지 참고 들었다 — 아주 참고 들었다. 러프턴 경이 자기가 당한 억울함의 내력을 이야기하면서 갈수록 더 노여워지자 사워비 씨에게 어떤 위협을 서슴없이 내뱉었는데도, 앞서 마크 로바츠에게 그것을 내뱉었던 것과 같았는데도 그러했다. 그는 변호사를 통하지 않고서는 한 실링도 물지 않겠다고 말했다. 그리고 변호사에게, 무엇을 물기 전에 이 일 전체를 법정에서 드러내 놓게 하라고 지시할 것이라고 했다. 그것이 자기에게든 다른 누구에게든 어떤 결과가 되든 상관하지 않겠다고 그는 말했다. 이 사건 전체를 배심에게 넘기기로 마음을 굳혔다고 했다.\n\n“대배심이든 특별 배심이든 보통 배심이든, 원한다면 올드 주리든 어디로든 넘기시지요,” 사워비가 말했다. “진실은 이래요, 러프턴. 당신은 얼마간 돈을 잃었고, 그것을 갚는 데 얼마간 지체가 있었기에 시달림을 받은 거예요.”\n\n“나는 잃은 것의 세 배가 넘게 물었어요,” 러프턴 경이 발을 구르며 말했다.\n\n“그 문제는 지금 따지지 않겠어요. 그것은 얼마 전에, 당신 자신이 그 일을 부친 사람들의 손에서 결말이 났다고 나는 여겨요. 그런데 이것 하나는 말해 줄 수 있겠지요. 어째서 이 일로 로바츠가 시달려야 합니까? 그가 무엇을 했다고요?”\n\n“글쎄, 나도 모르겠어요. 그가 당신과 이 일을 처리했지요.”\n\n“그런 일 없어요. 그는 친절하게도 당신의 전갈을 내게 옮겨 주고, 나의 답을 당신에게 다시 옮겨 주었어요. 그가 이 일에서 한 몫은 그것뿐이에요.”\n\n“내가 그를 끌어들이고 싶어 한다고 여기는 것은 아니겠지요?”\n\n“당신이 누군가를 끌어들이고 싶어 한다고는 생각하지 않아요. 그러나 당신은 머리가 뜨겁고 상대하기가 어렵고, 게다가 매우 사리에 어긋나요. 그리고 더 나쁜 것은, 당신이 조금 의심이 많다는 말을 하지 않을 수 없군요. 이 모든 일에서 나는 당신에게 편의를 봐주려고 나 자신을 크게 시달리게 했는데, 돌아온 것은 반 페니보다 발길질이 더 많았어요.”\n\n“당신이 이 어음을 토저에게 주지 않았어요? 그가 지금 쥐고 있는 그 어음을?”\n\n“첫째로 그는 그것을 쥐고 있지 않아요. 그리고 둘째로 나는 그것을 그에게 주지 않았어요. 이런 것들은 지급을 청구하는 사람에게 닿기까지 수십 사람의 손을 지나갑니다.”\n\n“그러면 며칠 전에 나에게 온 사람은 누구요?”\n\n“그것은 내가 보기에 톰 토저, 우리 토저의 형제였을 거예요.”\n\n“그러면 그가 그 어음을 쥐고 있는 거지요. 내가 그것을 그와 함께 보았으니까.”\n\n“잠깐만요, 그것은 아주 그럴 법해요. 그것을 떠맡는 데 값을 물어야 할 것이라고 내가 당신에게 전갈을 보냈지요. 물론 그런 것들은 얼마간의 대가 없이 놓아주지 않으니까요.”\n\n“십 파운드라고 했지요,” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“십이든 이십이든, 그쯤 되는 액수였지요. 그러나 그 사람이 그런 액수를 청구할 것이라고 여길 만큼 당신이 어리숙하지는 않았을 텐데요. 물론 그는 전액을 요구했겠지요. 그 어음이 여기 있습니다, 러프턴 경.” 그러면서 사워비는 문서 한 장을 꺼내 탁자 너머로 나리에게 건넸다. “오늘 아침에 이십오 파운드를 주고 사 왔어요.”\n\n러프턴 경은 그 종이를 받아 들고 살펴보았다. “그렇군요,” 그가 말했다. “이것이 그 어음이에요. 이제 이것을 어떻게 하지요?”\n\n“집안 문서고에 넣어 두시든지,” 사워비가 말했다. “아니면 불 뒤로 던지시든지, 마음에 드는 대로 하세요.”\n\n“그러면 이것이 마지막인가요? 다른 것이 또 나올 수는 없어요?”\n\n“당신이 어떤 종이에 손을 댔는지는 나보다 당신이 더 잘 알지요. 나는 다른 것은 모릅니다. 마지막으로 갱신할 때 내가 아는 미결 어음은 그것뿐이었어요.”\n\n“그리고 당신이 그것에 이십오 파운드를 물었다고요?”\n\n“그랬어요. 당신이 그것을 두고 그토록 발끈해서, 내가 그것을 가져오지 않았더라면 오늘 오후에 그렇게 시끄럽게 굴었을 것이 아니었다면, 십오나 이십에 얻을 수도 있었을 거예요. 사나흘만 있으면 그들은 십오에 넘겼을 겁니다.”\n\n“십 파운드쯤 더 되는 것은 대수롭지 않아요. 물론 이십오는 내가 갚겠어요,” 이제 조금 부끄러워지기 시작한 러프턴 경이 말했다.\n\n“그것은 마음대로 하세요.”\n\n“아! 물론 내 일이지요. 그만한 액수라면 나는 아깝지 않아요.” 그러고서 그는 앉아서 그 돈만큼 수표를 써 넣었다.\n\n“그럼, 이제 러프턴, 내가 당신에게 몇 마디 하겠어요,” 사워비가 벽난로에 등을 대고 서서 손에 든 작은 지팡이를 놀리며 말했다. “제발 부탁이니 주위 사람들에게 좀 더 너그럽게 대하도록 애써 봐요. 당신은 무슨 일로 안절부절못하게 되면 세상이 참아 주지 않을 말을 마구 쓰지요. 하기야 로바츠나 나처럼 당신을 잘 아는 사람은 그것을 참아 줄 마음을 낼지 몰라도요. 당신은 내가 여기 온 뒤로 온갖 악행을 다 나에게 씌웠어요 —”\n\n“자, 사워비 —”\n\n“여보게, 내 말은 끝까지 하게 해 주세요. 당신은 나에게 혐의를 씌웠고, 또 그에게도 씌웠다고 나는 생각해요. 그런데 자기 자신에게 혐의를 씌워 보는 일은, 아마 당신에게 한 번도 떠오르지 않았겠지요.”\n\n“정말로 떠올랐어요.”\n\n“당신이 토저 같은 사람들과 상관한 것은 물론 잘못이었어요. 나도 크게 잘못했지요. 그것을 일러 주는 데 큰 도덕의 권위가 필요하지도 않아요. 모범이 될 신사들은 토저와 거래하지 않고, 거래하지 않는 편이 그들에게 훨씬 낫지요. 그러나 사람은 제가 제 등에 얹은 무게를 견딜 만한 등을 가져야 하는 법이에요. 앞으로는 될 수 있으면 토저를 멀리해요. 그러나 그와 거래해야 한다면, 제발 부탁이니 성을 참아요.”\n\n“그것은 다 그럴싸한 말이에요, 사워비. 그러나 당신도 나만큼 잘 알듯이 —”\n\n“나는 이것을 압니다,” 성경을 인용하며 악마가 말했다. 그러고서 이십오 파운드 수표를 접어 주머니에 넣었다. “사람이 가라지를 뿌리면 밀을 거두지 못하는 법이고, 그것을 기대해도 헛일이라는 것이지요. 나는 이런 일에는 질기고 많은 것을 견딜 수 있어요 — 너무 심하게 밀리지만 않는다면 말이지만,” 그러면서 그는 말하는 동안 러프턴 경의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다. “그러나 나는 당신이 로바츠에게 너무 모질었다고 생각해요.”\n\n“나는 신경 쓰지 마세요, 사워비. 러프턴 경과 나는 아주 오랜 벗이에요.”\n\n“그러니까 서로 허물없이 굴 수도 있다는 말이지요. 아주 좋아요. 그리고 이제 내 설교는 끝났어요. 친애하는 고위 성직자님, 축하를 드리게 해 주세요. 포더길에게서 들었는데, 당신의 그 작은 일이 확실히 결말이 났다더군요.”\n\n마크의 얼굴이 다시 흐려졌다. “나는 오히려,” 그가 말했다. “그 임명을 사양할 생각이에요.”\n\n“사양한다고요!” 사워비가 말했다. 그것을 얻으려고 온 힘을 다 쓴 그로서는, 교구 사제가 이렇게 마음이 흔들리는 것이 자기든 러프턴 경이든 자기에게 쏟아부을 수 있는 어떤 인격 모욕보다도 더 완전히 불쾌한 일이었다.\n\n“그럴 생각이에요,” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“왜요?”\n\n마크는 러프턴 경을 올려다보았고, 그러고서 한동안 잠자코 있었다.\n\n“지금 사정에서는 그런 희생을 할 까닭이 있을 수 없네,” 나리가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 어떤 사정에서라면 그럴 까닭이 있을 수 있겠어요?” 사워비가 물었다. “옴니엄 공작께서 당신이 자기 주에 속한 교구 성직자이기에 그 자리를 얻어 주시려고 얼마간 힘을 써 주셨는데, 이제 당신이 그것을 물리친다면 나는 그것을 괴이한 일로 여기겠어요.”\n\n그러자 로바츠는 자기 이유를 다 드러내어 말했다. 러프턴 경이 그 어음 거래에 대해, 그리고 그 참사회원 자리가 그 융통의 값으로 주어졌다고 사람들이 말하게 될 것이라는 데 대해 정확히 무엇이라고 했는지를 설명한 것이었다.\n\n“정말이지 그것은 너무하군요,” 사워비가 말했다.\n\n“자, 사워비, 나는 훈계를 듣지 않겠어요,” 러프턴 경이 말했다.\n\n“내 훈계는 끝났어요,” 그가 말했다. 벗을 너무 심하게 몰아붙이는 것은 자기에게 좋지 않다는 것을 어쩌면 알고 있었던 것이다. “두 번은 하지 않겠어요. 그러나 로바츠, 이것만은 말해 두지요. 내가 아는 한 해럴드 스미스는 이 임명에 관계한 것이 거의, 아니 전혀 없어요. 공작께서 총리에게, 이 주의 교구 성직자 하나가 성당 참사회에 들어가기를 몹시 바란다고 말씀하셨고, 그러고 나서 브록 경의 청에 따라 당신을 지명하셨어요. 그런 사정인데도 당신이 그것을 버리겠다는 말을 한다면, 나는 당신을 실성한 사람으로 믿겠어요. 당신이 나를 위해 인수한 그 어음에 대해서는 마음 놓아도 좋아요. 돈은 마련될 겁니다. 그러나 물론 그때가 오면 그 백삼십은 나에게 넘겨줘야 —”\n\n그러고서 사워비 씨는 작별을 했으니, 이 자리의 주인이 되었음은 확실했다. 쉰 살 먹은 사람이 정신을 차리고 있고 너무 딱딱하게 늘어놓지만 않는다면, 함께 있는 이들이 서른이 못 되었을 때는 대개 그 자리의 주인이 될 수 있는 법이다.\n\n로바츠는 그가 떠난 뒤로 앨버니에 오래 머물지 않고, 지난 일에 대한 러프턴 경의 후회의 말과 앞으로의 우정에 대한 많은 약속을 받고서 작별을 했다. 실제로 러프턴 경은 조금 부끄러워하고 있었다. “그리고 참사회원직 말인데, 이렇게 된 뒤이니 자네는 물론 그것을 받아야 해.” 그러면서도 나리는 말과 백삼십 파운드에 대한 사워비 씨의 그 암시를 놓치지 않고 알아들었다.\n\n로바츠는 호텔로 걸어 돌아가면서, 자기는 바체스터의 승진을 확실히 받아들일 것이라고 생각했고, 그 일에 대해 동생에게 한마디도 하지 않은 것이 아주 잘된 일이라고 여겼다. 대체로 그의 마음은 크게 들려 있었다. 어음에 대한 사워비의 그 다짐은 그에게 매우 위안이 되었다. 그리고 이상하게도 그는 그것을 온전히 믿었다. 사실 사워비는 이번 만남에서 그토록 완전히 이긴 말이었으므로, 러프턴 경도 로바츠도 그가 하는 말은 거의 무엇이든 믿을 마음이 들었다 — 두 사람 다 늘 그러던 것은 아니었지만.\n\n# 제20장. 내각에 든 해럴드 스미스\n\n며칠 동안 해럴드 스미스 패 전체가 고개를 아주 높이 쳐들고 다녔다. 그들의 사람이 각료가 되었다는 것만이 아니었다. 브록 경이 그를 뽑음으로써 놀랍도록 자기 당을 강하게 만들었고, 자신의 오만과 판단력 부족이 제 정부라는 정치체에 입힌 상처를 낫게 하는 데 크게 이바지했다는 소문이 나돌았기 때문이다. 해럴드 스미스주의자들은 몹시 우쭐해져 그렇게 말했다. 그리고 해럴드 자신이 이룩한 것을 생각한다면, 그 자신도 다소 우쭐해졌다 해서 놀랄 일은 아니다.\n\n누구에게든 처음으로 내각에 걸어 들어가는 날은 자랑스러운 날일 것이다. 그러나 마음이 겸손한 사람이 인생의 그런 국면을 생각하면, 그 마음은 내각이란 대체 무엇인가를 헤아리다 길을 잃고 만다. 거기 참석하는 이들은 신인가 사람인가? 그들은 의자에 앉는가, 구름 위에 걸터앉는가? 그들이 말할 때, 천구의 음악이 그 올림포스의 저택에 들려 그 화음으로 하늘을 졸음에 잠기게 하는가? 그들은 어떤 식으로 모이는가? 어떤 순서로 서로에게 말을 거는가? 모든 신들의 목소리가 자유롭고 동등한가? 내무부에서 온 터덕터덕 일하는 테미스나 식민부에서 온 케레스도, 창과 투구 없이는 결코 보이지 않는 여신인 외무부의 힘센 팔라스와 똑같이 넋을 빼앗긴 주목을 받으며 들리는가? 우리 화이트홀의 마르스는 거기서 옥새 상서의 빛나는 젊은 베누스에게 눈짓을 보내어, 우리 재무부에서 풀무질을 하되 아주 헛되지만은 않은 저 별난 땜장이 불카누스를 역겹게 만드는가? 대법관석의 늙은 사투르누스는 이 회의석에 앉은 채로 다른 시절의 유물이 되어 말없이 저기 앉아 있다고 해 두자. 그가 다스리는 전당은 이제 다른 곳에 있다. 우리 우정청의 메르쿠리우스는 위대한 유피테르가 명하는 대로 천체에서 천체로 날래게 날아갈 준비가 언제나 되어 있는가? 그러는 동안 파도에 익숙하지 않은 넵투누스는 인도부의 아폴로에게 필요한 도움을 건네는가? 이름은 대단하나 신들 사이에서는 업신여겨지는 추밀원 의장인 몸으로, 유노가 어떻게 홀로 떨어져 침울하고 뿌루퉁하게, 아무도 아끼지 않는 가운데 앉아 있는가—그것은 우리가 짐작할 수 있다. 박쿠스와 쿠피도가 통상부와 공공사업청을 서로 나누어 가진다면, 사물의 알맞음은 여느 때만큼이나 충분히 헤아려진 것이 되겠다. 그리고 암브로시아의 이 잔치에 가장 늦게 불려 온 프티백 청의 얌전한 디아나는—아직 제 낮은 목소리를 형제 신들 사이에서 들리게 하지도 못한 채 문간 가까이에 물러서 붙어 있지 않은가? 그러나 유피테르, 위대한 유피테르—늙은 유피테르, 올림포스의 왕이며 신들과 사람들 가운데 영웅인 그는, 제 목소리로 불러 모은 이 회의에서 어떻게 처신하는가? 그는 어깨에 창궁을 잘라 만든 자줏빛 외투를 걸치고 편안히 거기 누워 있는가? 배반한 신을 질서로 되돌릴 벼락이 늘 그 손에 있는가? 그는 그 불멸의 전당에 침묵을 명할 수 있는가? 다른 곳에서, 온갖 자리에서, 온갖 나라에서 그러하듯 거기에서도, 신들의 왕이자 사람들의 왕은 자기보다 작은 자들 위에서 왕이며 앞으로도 왕일 것이요, 다스리며 앞으로도 다스릴 것이 아닌가?\n\n해럴드 스미스는 신들의 회의가 열리는 그 존엄한 전당으로 불려 갔을 때 자신이 자랑스러운 사람이라고 느꼈다. 그러나 우리는 아마 처음 한두 번의 회의에서 그가 아주 앞장서는 몫을 맡으려 들지는 않았으리라고 결론지어도 좋을 것이다. 내 독자들 가운데 교구 위원회에 앉아 본 이도 있을 터인데, 그들의 회의석에 새로 온 사람이 얼마나 온순하고 대체로 얼마나 말이 없는지 기억하시리라. 그는 열의를 죽인 채 대체로 동의한다. 그러나 뜻이 다를 때에는 그런 자유를 누린 데 대해 사과한다. 그러나 이윽고 동료들의 목소리가 귀에 익숙해지고—방의 낯섦이 사라지고 앞에 놓인 탁자가 알 만하고 믿을 만해지면—그는 두려움과 당황을 벗어던지고, 격렬한 연설과 사나운 탁자 두드림으로 제 형제들을 감전시킨다. 해럴드 스미스도 그리 될 것이라고, 아마 그의 내각 수련 두 번째나 세 번째 철에는 그리 될 것이라고 해 두자. 아아! 아아! 그런 즐거움이 그토록 덧없어야 한다니!\n\n그리고 또, 그의 승리를 다소 무디게 만드는 타격 하나가 그에게 떨어졌다—잔인하고 비겁한 타격이, 그에게 다정해야 했을 손에서, 앞에 놓인 큰 길에서 자기를 떠받쳐 주기를 그가 애틋하게 기대하던 사람에게서 날아왔다. 해럴드 스미스의 봉사를 얻음으로써 총리가 제 몸에 새롭고 젊고 건강한 피를 넣었다고 그의 벗들은 말해 왔다. 해럴드 자신도 그 어구가 마음에 들었고, 다정한 서플하우스 같은 이가 그것을 어떻게 써서 효험을 낼 수 있을지 한눈에 알아보았다. 그러나 엘리시움 밖에 있는 서플하우스가 어째서 엘리시움 안에 있는 해럴드 스미스에게 다정하겠는가? 엘리시움에 싸여 목까지 복락에 잠긴 사람은 벗들이 자기에게서 떨어져 나가는 것을 볼 각오를 해야 한다. 사람의 본성이 그것을 견디지 못한다. 내가 옛 벗 존스에게서 무언가를 얻어 내고 싶다면, 나는 그가 높은 자리로 밀려 올라가는 것을 보고 싶다. 그러나 존스가 그 높은 자리에서도 나를 위해 아무것도 해 줄 수 없다면, 내 머리 위로 올라간 그의 승진은 모욕이요 상해다. 자기와 아주 가까운 벗이 가장 높은 자리에 오를 만하다고 누가 믿겠는가? 서플하우스 씨는 스미스 씨를 너무 가까이서 알았기에 그의 젊은 피를 대단하게 여기지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 《주피터》에 기사 하나가 실렸는데, 내각 전반에 대해 결코 칭찬이 아니었다. 그것은 이 문제의 젊은 피라는 관점을 꽤 늘어지게 물고 늘어졌고, 해럴드 스미스가 물을 탄 물보다 나을 것이 별로 없다는 듯이 슬쩍 비추었다. “총리는”이라고 그 기사는 말했다. “가장 높은 도덕적 격조를 지닌 귀족의 영향력을 새로 주입함으로써 최근 쇠약해진 기력을 보충한 데 이어, 다시 백성 가운데서 뽑은 또 하나의 힘의 탑을 제게 더했다. 브리틀백 경과 해럴드 스미스 씨의 봉사를 이제 가졌으니 그가 바라지 못할 것이 무엇이겠는가! 그토록 강력한 메데이아의 가마솥에서 되살아나면, 그의 온갖 쇠잔한 팔다리는—그중 어떤 것들은 아주 쇠잔해졌음을 인정하지 않을 수 없다—젊고 둥글고 튼튼하게 다시 나올 것이다. 새 기운이 모든 부서에 두루 퍼질 것이다. 인도는 구원받고 잠잠해질 것이다. 프랑스의 야심은 길들여질 것이다. 공평한 개혁이 우리 법정과 의회 선거를 다시 만들 것이다. 그리고 유토피아가 실현될 것이다. 해럴드 스미스 씨의 젊은 피에서 내각이 기대하는 결과가 바로 그런 것인 듯하다!”\n\n이것도 충분히 잔인했으나, 이조차도 그 기사가 끝맺는 말만큼 잔인하지는 않았다. 그때쯤에는 반어가 버려지고, 글쓴이는 이 문제에 대한 제 의견을 진지하게 털어놓았다. “우리는 브록 경에게 이렇게 확언해 드리고자 한다.” 기사는 말했다. “이런 동맹들은 그의 오만과 판단력 부족이 그것을 뒤덮어 버리겠다고 위협하는 저 빠른 몰락에서 그를 구해 주지 못할 것이다. 그 자신에 관해서라면 우리는 그의 사임을 듣게 되는 것을 애석해할 것이다. 그는 여러 면에서 지금 이 시기의 위급한 사태들에 대해 우리가 가진 최고의 정치인이다. 그러나 앞에 놓인 일에서 조력자로 해럴드 스미스 씨와 브리틀백 경 같은 사람들에게 기대는 그토록 잘못된 판단을 한다면, 그는 나라가 자기를 지지할 것이라고 기대해서는 안 된다. 해럴드 스미스 씨는 각료가 만들어져야 할 그런 재목으로 되어 있지 않다.”\n\n해럴드 스미스 씨는 아침 식탁에 앉아 이것을 읽으면서 붓질마다 서플하우스 씨의 손을 알아보았다, 아니 알아보았다고 말했다. 쇠잔한 팔다리에 관한 그 어구는 처음부터 끝까지 서플하우스였고, 유토피아의 실현도 그러했다. “재치를 부리고 싶을 때면 저이는 늘 유토피아 이야기를 하지.” 해럴드 스미스 씨는 그렇게 말했다—혼자서. 스미스 부인은 이 아침 식사 자리에 몸소 나와 있는 일이 흔치 않았으니까.\n\n그러고서 그는 청으로 내려갔고, 마주치는 사람마다 그 눈빛에서 《주피터》의 그 기사가 읽혔다는 통고를 보았다. 그의 개인 비서는 그 기사를 두고 하는 것이 분명한 암시로 킬킬거렸고, 버긴스가 그의 외투를 받아 드는 태도로 보아 그것이 사환실에도 잘 알려져 있음이 분명했다. “내가 나갈 때 저이가 내 빈자리를 채울 일은 없겠군.” 버긴스는 혼자 그렇게 말하고 있었다. 그러고서 오전이 흐르는 동안 내각 회의가 왔으니, 그가 참석한 두 번째 회의였다. 그리고 그는 거기 모인 신과 여신 하나하나의 낯빛에서, 저희 우두머리가 또 한 번 실수를 저질렀다고 여겨지고 있음을 읽었다. 서플하우스 씨가 다른 가락으로 쓰도록 이끌릴 수 있었다면, 그렇다면 정말로 그 새 피가 효험이 있다고 느껴졌을 것이다.\n\n이 모든 것이 그의 행복에 큰 걸림돌이었으나, 그래도 그것이 그의 지위라는 사실을 그에게서 앗아 갈 수는 없었다. 《주피터》가 그를 반대해 썼다는 이유로 브록 경이 그에게 사임을 청할 수는 없었다. 브록 경은 그런 이유로 새 동료를 버릴 사람도 아니었다. 그리하여 해럴드 스미스는 허리를 동여매고 새 열의로 프티백 청의 직무에 나섰다. “정말이지 《주피터》가 옳다.” 프티백 청 안팎의 모든 것을 설명하는 사적인 쪽지를 마흔여덟 장째 끝마치며 젊은 로바츠는 혼자 그렇게 말했다. 해럴드 스미스는 자기 개인 비서의 쪽지가 무섭도록 정확할 것을 요구했다.\n\n그러나 그럼에도, 걸림돌들이 있었음에도 해럴드 스미스는 새 명예를 누리며 행복했고, 해럴드 스미스 부인도 그것을 즐겼다. 그녀는 확실히 아는 사람들 사이에서 새 각료를 적잖이 놀려 먹었고, 그녀가 《주피터》의 글쓴이만큼이나 그에게 매섭지 않았는가 하는 것은 하나의 물음거리가 될 수 있다. 그녀는 던스터블 양에게 새 피에 관해 아주 많이 귓속말을 했고, 웨스트민스터 다리로 내려가 템스강에 정말로 불이 붙었는지 보고 오겠다는 말을 했다. 그러나 웃으면서도 그녀는 개선했고, 아무런 겉으로 드러나는 표시 없이 제 명예를 지고 있다고 스스로 여겼으나, 세상은 그녀가 개선하고 있음을 알았고 그 우쭐거림을 비웃었다.\n\n이 무렵 그녀는 또 야회 하나를 열었다—프라우디 부인네 같은 순결한 마음의 콘베르사치오네가 아니라, 노골적으로 사악하고 세속스러운 무도회였다. 바이올린과 아이스가 있었고, 샴페인은 프티백 청에서 해럴드에게 들어올 첫 분기 봉급을 날려 버릴 만큼 있었다. 우리에게 이 무도회가 주로 기억할 만한 것은 레이디 러프턴이 손님들 가운데 있었다는 사실 때문이다. 런던에 도착한 즉시 그분은 해럴드 스미스 부인에게서 자기와 그리젤다 몫의 초대 카드를 받고, 당장 그 영광을 사양하는 답장을 보내려 했다. 사워비 씨의 누이동생 집에 그분이 무슨 볼일이 있단 말인가? 그러나 마침 그때 아들이 함께 있었고, 아들이 어머니가 가면 좋겠다는 뜻을 밝히자 그분은 뜻을 굽혔다. 그가 설득하는 어조에 여느 때보다 더한 것이 없었다면—그것이 그분 자신에게만 관계된 것이었다면—그분은 그의 살가운 염려에 미소를 지어 보이고, 고맙다고 하면서 그의 이마에 입 맞출 구실을 어떻게든 만들어 냈을 것이며, 그러고도 여전히 사양했을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그분에게 자기와 그리젤다 둘 다를 일깨웠다. “어머니, 저를 만난다는 이유만으로도 가시는 게 좋겠어요.” 그가 말했다. “요전에 스미스 부인이 저를 붙잡고서, 제가 약속을 하기 전에는 놓아주지 않았거든요.”\n\n“그건 확실히 끌리는 일이지.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “나는 네가 거기 있을 것을 아는 집에 가는 것이 참 좋다.”\n\n“그리고 이제 그랜틀리 양이 어머니와 함께 있으니, 어머니는 그 아가씨를 위해 할 수 있는 데까지 해 주실 의무가 있잖아요.”\n\n“그야 물론이지, 루도빅. 그리고 그렇게 씩씩하게 내 의무를 일깨워 준 것은 네게 고맙다고 해야겠구나.” 그리하여 그분은 해럴드 스미스 부인네에 가겠다고 말했다. 딱한 부인! 그분은 그랜틀리 양에 관한 그 몇 마디에 그것이 받을 만한 것보다 훨씬 더 무게를 얹었다. 아들이 그리젤다를 만나고 싶어 안달한다는 것—제 소원을 이루려고 이런 자잘한 술수를 부린다는 것—을 생각하니 그분의 마음이 기뻤다. 그러나 그는 그저 머릿속이 텅 빈 데서, 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지 생각도 없이 말한 것이었다. 어머니를 기쁘게 해 드리고 싶다는 것 말고는 아무 뜻이 없었다.\n\n그러나 어쨌든 그는 해럴드 스미스 부인네에 갔고, 거기서 그리젤다 그랜틀리와 한 번 이상 춤을 추었다—덤벨로 경이 눈에 띄게 마음이 불편해지도록. 그는 늦게 왔는데, 그가 온 순간 덤벨로 경은 그리젤다를 팔에 걸고 방을 천천히 거슬러 오르고 있었고, 레이디 러프턴은 가까이 앉아 언짢은 눈으로 그 모습을 바라보고 있었다. 그러고서 그리젤다가 자리에 앉자, 덤벨로 경은 그 옆에 말없이 서 있었다.\n\n“루도빅.” 어머니가 귓속말을 했다. “그리젤다가 저 사람 때문에 진력이 다 났어. 유령처럼 따라다니잖니. 가서 좀 구해 주렴.”\n\n그는 가서 그녀를 구해 냈고, 그 뒤로 거의 한 시간을 잇달아 그녀와 춤을 추었다. 세상이 덤벨로 경에게 그 젊은 아가씨를 흠모한다는 평판을 주고 있음을 그는 알았고, 형제 귀족의 마음을 질투와 노여움으로 채우는 즐거움에 아주 살아 있었다. 게다가 그의 눈에 그리젤다는 아주 아름다웠으니, 그녀가 조금만 더 생기가 있었더라면, 또는 어머니의 술책이 한 생각만큼이라도 더 잘 감춰져 있었더라면, 프램리 목사관 응접실에서 말하고 맹세한 그 모든 것에도 불구하고 그리젤다는 그날 밤 러프턴의 빈 왕좌를 함께 나누자는 청을 받았을지도 모른다.\n\n우리의 늠름하고 쾌활한 로타리오가 어머니의 집에서 그랜틀리 양과 함께 적잖이 많은 날을 보냈다는 것을 기억해야 하고, 그런 가까움이 지닌 위험도 기억해야 한다. 러프턴 경은 아름다움을 보고도 마음이 움직이지 않을 사람이 결코 아니었고, 젊은 아가씨와 여러 시간을 보내면서 다정함에 다가서는 일이 조금도 없을 사람도 결코 아니었다. 그렇게 다가서는 일이 전혀 없었다면 레이디 러프턴도 아마 이 일을 밀고 나가지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그런 문제에 관한 그분의 생각으로는, 아들 루도빅이 몇 차례 그랜틀리 양에 대한 편애를 그분의 바람을 정당하게 만들 만큼, 그리고 기회 말고는 아무것도 모자란 것이 없다고 여기게 만들 만큼 충분히 드러냈다. 그런데 이제 스미스 부인의 이 무도회에서 그가 한동안 그런 기회를 이용하는 것처럼 보였고, 어머니의 마음은 기뻤다. 이 저녁에 일이 잘 되어 간다면 그분은 해럴드 스미스 부인의 모든 죄를 용서할 것이었다.\n\n그리고 한동안은 일이 잘 되어 갈 것처럼 보였다. 그렇다고 러프턴 경이 그리젤다에게 사랑을 고백할 뜻을 품고 거기 왔다거나, 자기가 그러고 있다는 어떤 굳은 생각을 가졌다고 짐작해서는 안 된다. 젊은 사내들은 그런 일에서 굳은 생각이라는 것이 없기가 얼마나 흔한지! 그들은 그토록 완전한 나방이다. 아름다운 촛불의 빛을 가지고 즐기며 눈이 부신 채로 불꽃 안팎을 들락날락 펄럭이다가, 성급한 한순간에 심지에 너무 가까이 달려들고, 그러면 날개가 그슬리고 다리가 부러진 채 떨어져, 혼인이라는 삼켜 버리는 불에 타서 부싯깃이 되고 만다. 행복한 혼인은 하늘에서 맺어진다고 사람들은 말하고, 나는 그것을 믿는다. 크레스웰 크레스웰 경이 있음에도 대부분의 혼인은 꽤 행복하다. 그런데도 그런 결과를 얻기 위해 땅에서 들이는 정성은 얼마나 적은지!\n\n“저희 어머니가 잘 대해 드리고 있나요?” 무도곡 사이에 함께 문간에 서 있을 때 러프턴 경이 그리젤다에게 말했다.\n\n“네, 아주 친절하세요.”\n\n“그토록 점잖고 얌전한 분의 손에 스스로를 맡기신 것은 경솔한 일이었어요. 그리고 실은 해럴드 스미스 부인의 이 첫 각료 무도회에 그랜틀리 양이 와 있는 것도 온전히 제 덕입니다. 그것을 아시는지 모르겠군요.”\n\n“네, 레이디 러프턴이 말씀해 주셨어요.”\n\n“그래서 고마운가요, 아닌가요? 제가 해를 끼친 겁니까, 은혜를 베푼 겁니까? 브루턴 스트리트의 소파 구석에 앉아 소설을 읽는 것과, 여기서 덤벨로 경과 폴카를 추는 척하는 것 중에 어느 것이 더 좋으신가요?”\n\n“무슨 말씀인지 모르겠어요. 저는 저녁 내내 덤벨로 경과 짝을 지어 일어선 적이 없어요. 카드리유를 추려고는 했지만 추지 않았어요.”\n\n“그렇지요. 바로 제 말이 그겁니다—추는 척한 것이지요. 덤벨로 경으로서는 그것만도 대단한 일 아닌가요?” 그러고서 러프턴 경은, 그 자신은 척하는 사람이 아니었으므로, 팔을 그녀의 허리에 두르고 두 사람은 방을 오르내리고 가로지르고 이리저리 돌며 나아갔으니, 그리젤다가 혀로 모자란 것을 발로 메운다는 것을 보여 주는 기운이었다. 그러는 동안 덤벨로 경은 옆에 서서 지켜보며, 러프턴 경은 혀나 매끄럽고 머릿속은 텅 빈 얼간이라고 속으로 생각했고, 만약 제 맞수가 그 재빠른 회전 가운데 하나에서 다리의 힘줄을 끊거나, 아니면 갑자기 다른 어떤 끔찍한 불행을—재산을 다 잃는 것이든, 완전한 눈멂이든, 만성 요통이든—당한다면 그것은 그에게 마땅한 일일 뿐이라고 헤아렸다. 그리고 그런 마음가짐으로 그는 잠자리에 들었으니, 남의 잘못을 용서한다는 기도를 그가 틀림없이 올렸을 터인데도 그러했다.\n\n그러고서 두 사람이 다시 서 있게 되었을 때, 러프턴 경은 새 숨을 얻으려 사납게 헐떡이는 사이사이의 자잘한 틈에서, 런던이 좋으냐고 그리젤다에게 물었다. “꽤 좋아요.” 그리젤다도 조금 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n“아무래도—저 아래 프램리에서는—아주 심심하셨겠지요.”\n\n“아니에요—좋았어요—아주.”\n\n“그랜틀리 양이 떠나—버렸을 때는 참 지루했어요. 그 집에는 말을 붙일 만한—사람이 하나도 없었지요.” 그러고서 두 사람은 폐가 잠잠해질 때까지 잠시 말이 없었다.\n\n“하나도 없었어요.” 그가 이어 말했다—작정한 거짓말은 아니었으니, 실은 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지 생각하고 있지 않았기 때문이다. 그리젤다가 떠난 것을 자기가 참으로 크게 홀가분해했다는 것, 그리고 같은 집에서 한 달을 함께 오간 그랜틀리 양과보다 한 시간에 루시 로바츠와 더 많은 이야기를 할 수 있었다는 것이 그 순간에는 그에게 떠오르지 않았다. 그러나 그렇다 해도 우리가 그에게 매섭게 굴 일은 아니다. 사랑과 전쟁에서는 무엇이든 정당하고, 이것이 사랑이 아니었다면 그것은 사랑의 짝으로 대신 서는 여느 물건이었다.\n\n“하나도 없었어요.” 러프턴 경이 말했다. “이튿날 아침에는 공원에서 목을 매달 뻔했지요—비가 온 덕에 안 했지만요.”\n\n“터무니없는 소리! 이야기 나눌 어머니가 계셨잖아요.”\n\n“아, 어머니요—그렇지요. 그리고 컬페퍼 대위도 거기 있었다고 덧붙여 주셔도 좋습니다. 저는 어머니를 참으로 깊이 사랑해요. 하지만 어머니가 그랜틀리 양이 없는 자리를 메울 수 있다고 생각하시나요?” 그러고 그의 목소리는 아주 다정했고, 눈도 그러했다.\n\n“그리고 로바츠 양도요. 저는 경께서 그 아가씨를 아주 많이 흠모하시는 줄 알았어요.”\n\n“뭐라고요, 루시 로바츠?” 러프턴 경은 루시의 이름이 지금 자기가 다룰 줄 아는 것보다 더 큰 것임을 느끼며 말했다. 실로 그 이름은 그 자잘한 희롱에 있던 생기를 다 죽여 놓았다. “루시 로바츠를 좋아하기는 하지요, 물론. 아주 영리한 사람입니다. 하지만 그랜틀리 양이 떠난 뒤로는 어쩌다 보니 그 사람을 거의, 아니 전혀 보지 못했어요.”\n\n이 말에 그리젤다는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 몸을 곧추세운 채, 동굴에서 오리온을 얼려 버릴 때의 디아나처럼 차갑게 보였다. 그리고 러프턴 경이 뒤이어 세 번 네 번 대화를 해 보려 애썼으나 그녀에게서 한 마디짜리 대답 이상은 얻어 낼 수 없었다. 그러고서 두 사람은 다시 춤을 추었으나, 그리젤다의 발걸음은 아까만큼 결코 활발하지 않았다.\n\n그날 두 사람 사이에 일어난 일은 여기 적은 것보다 아주 조금밖에 더 되지 않는다. 아이스나 레모네이드 한 잔이 덤으로 있었을 수도 있고, 어쩌면 손을 지그시 누르려는 되도록 희미한 시도가 있었을 수도 있다. 그러나 그랬다 해도 그것은 온전히 한쪽만의 일이었다. 그런 다가섬에 대해서라면 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 어느 디아나만큼이나 차가웠다.\n\n이 모두가 이토록 적은 것이었으나, 레이디 러프턴의 마음과 가슴을 채우기에는 넉넉했다. 딸 여섯을 둔 어머니라도 그 딸들을 제 손에서 떼어 놓고 싶어 안달한 것이, 레이디 러프턴이 아들이 결혼하는 것을—그러니까 알맞은 종류의 아가씨와 결혼하는 것을—보고 싶어 안달한 것보다 더하지는 않았다. 그런데 이제 정말로 그가 그분의 바람에 따르려 하는 것처럼 보였다. 그분은 저녁 내내 그를 지켜보면서, 그러고 있는 것이 눈에 띄지 않도록 애를 쓰며 마음을 졸였다. 그분은 덤벨로 경의 패배와 분노를 보았고, 아들의 승리와 자랑을 보았다. 그가 이미 무언가 말을 해 놓았고, 그것이 그리젤다의 차가움 때문에 아직 결판나지 않은 채로만 남아 있다는 것이 사실일 수 있을까? 그분 쪽에서 슬기롭게 도와주면 그 미결이 확실로 바뀌고 그 차가움이 따뜻함으로 바뀔 수 있지 않을까? 그러나 그런 끼어듦은 어느 것이나 아주 섬세한 손길을 요구하는 법이니—레이디 러프턴도 그것을 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n“저녁을 즐겁게 지냈어요?” 마님의 내실에서 그리젤다와 함께 난로 울에 발을 얹고 앉았을 때 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. 레이디 러프턴은 특별히 이 손님을 자기의 가장 사적인 성소인 이곳으로 불러들였으니, 여기는 원래 딸 말고는, 그리고 때때로 패니 로바츠 말고는 아무도 들일 수 없는 곳이었다. 그러나 그리젤다 같은 며느리라면 어느 성소에라도 들어오지 못할 데가 있겠는가?\n\n“아, 네—아주요.” 그리젤다가 말했다.\n\n“보아하니 미소를 대부분 루도빅에게 나누어 준 것 같군요.” 그러면서 레이디 러프턴은 그랬다면 좋겠다는 흡족한 표정을 지었다.\n\n“아! 모르겠어요.” 그리젤다가 말했다. “두세 번 그분과 짝을 지어 춤을 추기는 했어요.”\n\n“나를 기쁘게 하기에 지나치게 여러 번은 아니에요, 얘야. 나는 루도빅이 내 벗들과 춤추는 것을 보는 게 좋아요.”\n\n“저야 참으로 고마운 일이지요, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“그럴 것 없어요, 얘야. 그이가 그렇게 좋은 짝을 어디서 얻을 수 있을지 나는 모르겠어요.” 그러고서 그분은 얼마나 더 나아가도 좋을지 감이 잡히지 않아 잠시 말을 멈추었다. 그러는 동안 그리젤다는 가만히 앉아 이글거리는 숯불을 바라보고 있었다. “정말이지 나는 그이가 그리젤다를 아주 많이 흠모한다는 것을 알고 있어요.” 레이디 러프턴이 이어 말했다.\n\n“아! 아니에요, 분명 그렇지 않아요.” 그리젤다가 말했다. 그러고서 또 한 번 말이 없었다.\n\n“나는 이것만은 말할 수 있어요.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그이가 정말로 그러하다면—나는 그렇다고 믿어요—그것은 내게 아주 큰 기쁨이 되겠다는 것이에요. 얘야, 알다시피 나 자신이 그리젤다를 아주 좋아하니까요.”\n\n“아! 고마워요.” 그리젤다는 그렇게 말하고 아까보다 더 끈덕지게 숯불을 바라보았다.\n\n“그이는 성품이 아주 훌륭한 젊은 사람이에요—내 자식이지만 그것만은 말하겠어요—그리고 그리젤다와 그이 사이에 무슨 일이 있다면—”\n\n“정말로 아무것도 없어요, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“하지만 언제라도 그런 일이 있게 된다면, 루도빅이 그토록 훌륭한 선택을 했다고 생각하게 되어 나는 기쁠 거예요.”\n\n“하지만 그런 일은 결코 없을 거예요, 레이디 러프턴. 그분은 그런 것을 조금도 생각하고 있지 않아요.”\n\n“글쎄, 어느 날엔가는 그럴지도 모르지요. 그럼 잘 자요, 얘야.”\n\n“안녕히 자세요, 레이디 러프턴.” 그리고 그리젤다는 지극히 침착하게 그분에게 입을 맞추고 자기 침실로 갔다. 잠자리에 들기 전 그녀는 제 여러 옷가지들을 꼼꼼히 살펴, 저녁 내내 입고 쓰는 동안 어느 정도의 손상이 났는지 알아보았다.\n\n# 제21장. 왜 조랑말 퍽이 매를 맞았는가\n\n마크 로바츠는 올버니에서의 그 일이 있은 이튿날 집으로 돌아왔다. 마음이 적잖이 놓인 채였다. 이제 그는 참사회원 석을 받아들여도 그렇게 하는 것이 성직자로서 스스로에게 부끄러울 일은 아니라고 느꼈다. 아니, 사워비 씨가 한 말이 있고 거기에 러프턴 경이 동의한 뒤였으니, 그것을 마다하는 것이야말로 미친 짓이라고 그는 여겼다. 게다가 어음에 관한 사워비 씨의 약속도 그에게는 무척 마음 편한 것이었다. 어쩌면 그 값을 넉넉히 하는 말 한 필에 130파운드를 치러야 한다는 것 말고는 다른 손해 없이 이 모든 근심에서 벗어날 수도 있지 않겠는가?\n\n돌아온 이튿날 그는 참사회원직에 지명되었다는 정식 통지를 받았다. 사실 그는 이미 참사회원이었고, 수석 사제와 성당 참사회가 그를 참사회원 석에 앉히는 절차만 밟으면 그것으로 끝이었다. 수입은 벌써 그의 것이었다. 집도 일주일 뒤면 그에게 넘어올 것이었는데, 그 대목만은 어떻게든 할 수 있었더라면 기꺼이 없는 것으로 하고 싶은 부분이었다. 아내는 애정을 숨기지 않고, 그 처분에 흡족한 낯빛으로 그를 곱게 축하해 주었다. 이런 횡재에 제 행복을 즐기는 일은 남들이 거리낌 없이, 또 거리낌 없이 말로 드러내며 함께 즐거워해 주는가에 참으로 크게 달려 있다! 레이디 러프턴의 축하는 그로 하여금 이 일을 통째로 내던지게 만들 지경이었으나, 아내의 웃음이 그를 다시 북돋웠고, 루시의 따뜻하고 열띤 기쁨은 사워비 씨와 옴니엄 공작이 아주 고맙게 여겨지도록 만들었다. 그러고 나서 그 훌륭한 짐승 댄디가 목사관 마구간으로 들어왔으니, 마부와 정원사가 크게 기뻐하였고, “주인”이 사냥에 그토록 열을 올리게 된 뒤로 어느새 슬그머니 살림에 끼어들도록 허락된 마구간 조수 아이도 기뻐하였다. 그러나 이 흡족함은 응접실에서는 나누어지지 않았다. 말이 마구간 문으로 돌아 들어가는 첫걸음에 눈에 띄었고, 곧바로 물음이 터져 나왔다. 마크의 말로는, 그것은 “얼마 전 사워비 씨의 편의를 봐줄 셈으로 그에게서 사들인 말”이었다. “나, 마크는 사려 깊게 처리할 수 있게 되는 대로 다시 팔 작정”이었다. 앞서 말하였듯 이것은 만족스러운 답이 아니었다. 프램리 목사관의 두 부인은 말에 대해서도, 한 신사가 다른 신사의 마구간에 남는 것을 사들여 편의를 봐주는 것이 옳다고 여길 만한 방식에 대해서도 아는 바가 많지 않았다. 그러나 두 사람 다 댄디가 없어도 목사관 마구간에는 말이 넉넉하다는 것, 그리고 사냥말을 사서 곧바로 되팔 셈이라는 것은 아무리 좋게 말해도 성직자의 여느 취향과 하는 일에는 좀처럼 어울리지 않는 짓이라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n“그 말에 돈을 너무 많이 주지는 않았기를 바라요, 마크,” 패니가 말했다.\n\n“다시 받아 낼 것보다 많이 주지는 않았어,” 마크가 말했다. 그리고 패니는 그의 얼굴 모양새로 보아 그 이야기를 그 자리에서 더 캐지 않는 편이 좋겠다는 것을 알았다.\n\n“곧바로 거주 근무를 시작해야 할 것 같군,” 마크가 참사회원 석이라는 좀 더 기분 좋은 이야기로 되돌아가며 말했다.\n\n“그러면 우리 모두 곧장 바체스터로 가서 살아야 하나요?” 루시가 물었다.\n\n“그 집엔 가구도 없을 텐데요, 마크?” 아내가 말했다. “우리가 어떻게 지내야 할지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“겁내지 마. 바체스터에 하숙을 얻을 테니까.”\n\n“그럼 그동안 내내 당신을 못 보겠네요,” 로바츠 부인이 낙담하여 말했다. 그러나 참사회원께서는 매주 프램리를 오락가락할 것이며, 바체스터에서 잠을 자는 것은 아무래도 토요일과 일요일뿐일 것이고, 어쩌면 그때조차 늘 그렇지는 않을 것이라고 설명하였다.\n\n“참사회원 일이란 게 그리 힘든 일은 아닌 것 같네요,” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 아주 위엄 있는 자리예요,” 패니가 말했다. “참사회원은 교회의 위엄을 지닌 분들이잖아요 — 그렇지 않아요, 마크?”\n\n“단연 그렇지,” 그가 말했다. “특별 교회법에 따라 그 아내들도 그렇고. 고약한 것은 양쪽 다 가발을 써야 한다는 점이야.”\n\n“모자도 쓰게 되나요, 마크? 양옆에 도르르 말린 것이 달리고, 그것을 붙들어 매도록 끈이 꿰인 모자요?” 루시가 물었다.\n\n“그건 내게 딸려 오는 몫에는 들지 않는 모양이구나.”\n\n“장식 매듭도 없어요? 그럼 마크가 위엄 있는 분이라는 건 저는 절대로 믿지 않을 거예요. 여느 목사처럼 — 이를테면 크롤리 씨처럼 — 그런 모자를 쓰겠다는 말인가요?”\n\n“글쎄 — 모자 테를 한 번 비틀어 접는 것쯤은 해도 될 것 같다만, 참사회에서 수석 사제께 물어보기 전에는 결코 확실치 않구나.”\n\n이렇게 목사관에서는 그들에게 다가오는 좋은 것들을 두고 이야기를 나누었고, 새 말과, 지난겨울 동안 그토록 자주 신었던 사냥 장화와, 달라진 레이디 러프턴의 낯빛은 잊으려 애썼다. 어쩌면 나쁜 것들은 스러져 사라지고 좋은 것들만 그들에게 남을지도 몰랐다.\n\n이제 사월이었다. 들판은 푸르러지기 시작하였고, 바람은 동쪽에서 벗어나 부드럽고 온화해졌으며, 이른 봄꽃들이 목사관 정원에서 밝은 빛깔을 내보이고 있었고, 온갖 것이 달고 즐거웠다. 이맘때는 여느 해에도 로바츠 부인에게 소중한 철이었다. 그녀의 남편은 따뜻한 달이 오면 겨울 동안보다 늘 더 나은 목사였다. 그녀가 알지도 못하고 좋게 여기지도 않는 먼 고을의 벗들은 봄이 오면 떠나가, 그 집들을 죄 없이 비워 두었다. 교구 일이 더 잘 챙겨졌고, 어쩌면 집안 일도 그러하였다. 그런 철에 그는 본이 되는 목사요 본이 되는 남편이었으니, 지난날의 모자람을 오늘의 열심으로 제 양심에 갚는 것이었다. 그리고 또, 스스로 한 번도 인정한 적은 없었으나, 소중한 벗 레이디 러프턴이 없다는 것도 어쩌면 그 자체로 싫지 않은 일이었다. 로바츠 부인은 레이디 러프턴을 참으로 마음 깊이 사랑하였다. 그러나 마님에 대해서는 이것만은 인정해야 하겠는데, 그 온갖 좋은 자질에도 그분은 남을 휘두르는 쪽으로 기울어 있었다. 그분은 다스리기를 좋아하였고, 좋아한다는 것을 사람들이 느끼도록 하였다. 로바츠 부인이라면 제가 종노릇하는 심정에 짓눌려 있다고는 결코 고백하지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 어쩌면 그녀도 마음씨 고운 고양이가 잠시 자리를 비운 것을 즐길 만한 쥐였다. 레이디 러프턴이 없을 때에는 로바츠 부인 자신이 교구에서 더 마음껏 움직일 수 있었다.\n\n그리고 마크 역시 불행하지 않았다. 댄디를 곧바로 돈으로 바꾸는 일이 될 만하지 않았기는 하였으나 그러하였다. 실은 바로 이 무렵, 성직자가 성당 참사회의 한 사람이 되기 전에 반드시 거쳐야 하는 그 깊은 신비와 엄정한 교회 심사를 치르느라 바체스터에 자주 건너가 있던 그때에, 댄디는 오히려 옆구리에 박힌 가시였다. 그 딱한 어음들은 오월 초에 만기가 돌아올 것이었는데, 사월이 끝나기 전에 사워비가 그에게 편지를 보내어, 그 흉한 날에 대비하려고 있는 힘을 다하고 있으나 댄디의 값을 지금 당장 자기에게 보내 준다면 그 일이 크게 수월해지겠다고 하였다. 돈에 관한 사워비 씨의 어조는 때에 따라 이보다 더 다를 수 없었다. 그가 돈을 긁어모아야 할 때에는 무엇이든 그렇게 중대하였다. 서둘러야 하고, 사람의 힘을 넘어서는 애를 써야 하며, 백지 인수서를 손에 든 사람들이 이리저리 뛰어다녀야만 세상 끝나는 소리를 겨우 막을 수 있었다. 그러나 다른 때, 이쪽에서 되받아 청구를 넣으면, 그는 세상에서 가장 편안한 목소리와 가장 경쾌한 태도로 온갖 것이 아주 태평하다는 것을 증명해 낼 수 있었다. 이제 이 무렵 그는 사람의 힘을 넘어서는 애를 쓰는 그 기분에 들어 있었고, 댄디 값 백삼십 파운드를 큰 소리로 청하였다. 지난 일이 있고 나서 마크는 어음이 안전해지기까지는 한 푼도 내지 않겠다고 말할 수가 없었다. 그리하여 은행의 포레스트 씨의 도움을 받아, 그는 댄디의 값을 런던에 있는 벗 사워비에게 실제로 송금하였다.\n\n그리고 루시 로바츠 — 이제 그녀에 관해 한마디 하지 않을 수 없다. 세상이 그녀의 발 앞에 놓여 있던 그날, 그녀가 그 고귀한 구혼자를 어떻게 돌려보냈는지 우리는 보았다. 그저 물린 것이 아니라, 다시는 그녀에게 맹세의 그 달콤한 향을 바치려 들지 않도록 가르침을 받을 만한 방식으로 물린 것이었다. 그녀는 그를 사랑하지 않으며 사랑할 수도 없다고 그에게 딱 잘라 말하였고, 그리하여 부와 명예와 높은 지위만 내던진 것이 아니라, 그보다 더한 것을 — 그보다 훨씬 나쁜 것을 — 곧 그 사랑에 제 뜨거운 마음이 매달려 있는 그 연인을 제게서 떨쳐 버린 것이었다. 자기의 사랑이 그에게 매달려 있다는 것을 그녀는 그때에도 알고 있었고, 그가 떠나가자마자 더욱 온전히 인정하였다. 그녀의 자존심이 그만큼을 해낸 것이었고, 레이디 러프턴이 자기를 노려보며 아들을 덫에 걸었다고 말하게 하지는 않겠다는 그 굳은 결심이 그만큼을 해낸 것이었다.\n\n러프턴 경 그 사람을 두고, 귀족 지위와 넓은 땅과 잘생기고 복스러운 얼굴을 젖혀 놓으면 그는 처녀의 마음과 사랑을 받을 만한 값이 없었다고들 할 것을 나는 안다. 그런 말이 나오는 것은, 책 속의 주인공은 세상의 흔한 살림살이에 쓰이도록 마련된 주인공들보다 훨씬 나아야 한다고 사람들이 여기기 때문이다. 절대적이고 참된 영웅됨이 러프턴 경의 됨됨이에는 어지간한 만큼만 섞여 있었다는 것을 나도 고백해 두는 편이 좋겠다. 그러나 절대적이고 참된 영웅만이 여인의 사랑을 받을 만하다고 여겨진다면 세상이 어찌 되겠는가? 남자들은 무엇을 하겠는가? 그리고 — 아! 여자들은 어찌 되겠는가? 루시 로바츠도 마음속으로 그 물린 연인에게 그에게 참으로 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 영웅됨을 인정해 주지는 않았다. 어쩌면 그에게 실제로 있는 어느 만큼의 영웅됨조차 온전히 인정해 주지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그럼에도 그녀는, 자존심을 다치지 않고 그럴 수 있었더라면 그를 매우 기꺼이 받아들였을 것이다.\n\n처녀가 돈을 보고 시집가서는 안 된다는 데에 우리 모두는 뜻을 같이한다. 작위나 땅을 얻으려고, 수입이나 집안의 다이아몬드 한 벌을 얻으려고 제 몸을 팔 수 있는 숙녀는 농부가 제 양과 소를 다루듯 스스로를 다루는 것이니, 마음과 넋이 들어 있는 저의 안쪽 자아를, 제 동성 가운데 가장 낮은 타락의 자리에서 밥을 버는 저 딱한 여자보다 별로 더 값나가게 여기지 않는 것이다. 그러나 작위와 땅과 수입은 하와의 모든 딸들의 저울에서 무게를 지니는 것들이다 — 아담의 모든 아들들에게 그러한 것과 마찬가지로. 자리의 자랑과, 세상의 눈앞에서 넉넉히 살아가는 힘은 우리 모두에게 소중하다. 소중하도록 마련된 것임에 틀림없다. 다만 그만큼을 인정하면서 이것도 기억해 두자. 그 좋은 것들이 너무 값비싸질 수 있는 값이 있다는 것을. 그러므로 이 일에 관해서도 참을 말하고자 하는 터이니, 나는 고백하지 않을 수 없다. 루시가 레이디 러프턴이 된다는 것은 어떤 것이었을까를 얼마간 아쉬움을 품고 헤아려 보았다는 것을. 그런 사내의 아내가 되고, 그런 마음의 주인이 되고, 그런 운명의 안주인이 된다는 것 — 세상이 그녀에게 그보다 더한 것을, 그보다 나은 것을 무엇을 해 줄 수 있었겠는가? 그런데 이제 그녀는 그 모든 것을 내던져 버렸다. 레이디 러프턴이 자기를 꾀바르고 잔꾀 많은 처녀라 부르는 것을 견딜 수 없었기 때문이다! 그 두려움에 밀려 그녀는 거짓말로 그를 밀어냈다. 참을 말하는 것이 그토록 무섭게 마땅한 일이었던 그 문제에서 말이다.\n\n그러면서도 그녀는 오라비와 올케 앞에서 밝았다. 아주 혼자일 때, 밤중에 제 방에 있을 때나 홀로 걷는 길에서만, 소리 없는 눈물 한 방울이 눈초리에 맺혀 서서히 눈시울을 적셨다. “그녀는 제 사랑을 결코 말하지 않았다.” 그렇다고 숨김이 “그 다마스크빛 뺨을 먹어 들어가도록” 내버려 두지도 않았다. 하는 일마다에서, 집안을 다니는 걸음걸이에서, 여느 때의 조용한 웃음기에서 그녀는 예전과 다름없었다. 이 점에서 그녀는 하느님이 그녀에게 주신 남다른 힘을 보였다. 그러나 그렇다고 그녀가 잃어버린 사랑과 망가진 바람을 참으로 슬퍼하지 않은 것은 아니었다.\n\n“오늘 아침엔 호글스톡까지 마차로 다녀올 거예요,” 어느 날 아침 식사 자리에서 패니가 말했다. “마크, 당신은 우리와 같이 가지 않으시겠지요?”\n\n“음, 안 가겠어. 조랑말 마차는 셋이 타기엔 딱하지.”\n\n“아, 그거야 새 말이 당신을 그만큼은 태워다 줄 수 있으리라 여겼는데요. 크롤리 씨를 만나고 싶다고 말하시는 걸 들었어요.”\n\n“그럼, 만나고 싶어. 그리고 당신이 새 말이라 부르는 그 녀석이 내일 나를 거기까지 태워다 줄 거야. 열두 시쯤 건너가겠다고 말해 주겠어?”\n\n“더 이르게 말하는 편이 좋겠어요. 그분은 늘 교구를 돌아 나가 계시니까요.”\n\n“그럼 좋아, 열한 시라고 해. 내가 가는 것은 교구 일 때문이니, 나 때문에 집에 붙어 있는다 해서 그의 양심에 거슬릴 일도 없겠지.”\n\n“그러면 루시, 우리끼리 몰아야겠네요. 갈 때는 루시가 마차꾼을 하고, 돌아올 때는 바꾸기로 해요.” 루시는 그 모두에 동의하였고, 학교에서의 일이 끝나자마자 두 사람은 나섰다.\n\n정원을 함께 걸었던 그 저녁, 이제 한 달도 더 지난 그 저녁 이래로 두 사람 사이에서 러프턴 경에 관한 말은 한마디도 나오지 않았다. 그때 루시가 처신한 모양이, 그때까지 사랑의 오감이 없었다고 올케가 아주 확신하도록 만들어 놓았다. 그 뒤로 로바츠 부인의 마음에 무슨 의심을 지을 만한 일도 일어나지 않았다. 두 사람 사이의 그 가까운 친함이 죄 끝났다는 것은 그녀도 곧 알아보았고, 온갖 것이 마땅히 그러해야 할 대로 되었다고 생각하였다.\n\n“그런데 말이에요, 나는 이런 생각이 들어요,” 그날 조랑말 마차에서 그녀가 말했다. “러프턴 경이 그리젤다 그랜틀리와 혼인하겠구나 하는 생각이요.” 루시는 쥐고 있던 고삐를 저도 모르게 살짝 당기지 않을 수 없었고, 피가 심장으로 재게 몰리는 것을 느꼈다. 그러나 속을 드러내지는 않았다. “그럴지도 모르죠,” 그녀가 말하고는, 채찍으로 조랑말을 살짝 건드렸다.\n\n“아, 루시, 퍽이 매 맞는 건 두고 못 봐요. 아주 잘 가고 있었잖아요.”\n\n“퍽에게 용서를 빌어요. 하지만 보세요, 채찍을 맡고 있으면 그걸 써 보고 싶은 마음이 그렇게 간절해지는 거예요.”\n\n“아, 그래도 가만히 들고 있어야 해요. 나는 레이디 러프턴께서 그런 혼사를 반기실 거라고 거의 확신해요.”\n\n“그러실 만하지요. 그랜틀리 양은 재산을 크게 물려받겠지요, 아마.”\n\n“꼭 그것만은 아니에요. 그 아가씨는 레이디 러프턴께서 좋아하시는 부류의 젊은 숙녀지요. 숙녀답고 아주 아름답고—”\n\n“어머, 패니!”\n\n“정말 그렇다고 생각해요. 내가 사랑스럽다고 부를 만한 쪽은 아니지만, 아주 아름다워요. 그리고 조용하고 몸가짐이 조촐하지요. 들뜰 일을 찾지 않고, 제 할 일을 하는 데에는 양심을 다하는 사람일 거예요.”\n\n“양심을 다하기야 하겠지요, 틀림없이,” 루시가 비웃음 같은 것을 어조에 담아 말했다. “하지만 문제는 러프턴 경이 그 아가씨를 마음에 두는지가 아닐까요.”\n\n“마음에 두는 것 같아요, — 어떤 식으로는요. 루시에게 말을 걸던 만큼 그 아가씨에게 걸지는 않았지만—”\n\n“아! 그건 죄 레이디 러프턴 탓이지요. 그분이 러프턴 경에게 제대로 딱지를 붙여 두지 않으셨으니까요.”\n\n“그래도 큰 해가 난 것 같지는 않은데요?”\n\n“아! 하느님의 자비로 아주 조금이지요. 나로서는 서너 해 안에 털어 낼 테고요, 그건 의심 없어요 — 나귀 젖을 얻어 마시고 바람을 바꿀 수 있다면 말이지만.”\n\n“그걸 위해서라면 바체스터로 데려가 주겠어요. 그런데 말했듯이, 나는 러프턴 경이 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 정말 마음에 둔다고 생각해요.”\n\n“그렇다면 나는 그분의 취향이 유별나게 나쁘다고 정말로 생각해요,” 루시가 그때까지 쓰던 농담의 어조와는 크게 다른, 참된 것이 담긴 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“어머, 루시!” 올케가 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. “그럼 나귀 젖이 정말로 필요해질까 봐 걱정이네요.”\n\n“내 처지를 생각하면 나는 러프턴 경에 대해 아무것도 몰라야 마땅하겠지요. 젊은 숙녀가 젊은 신사를 아는 것은 아주 위험하다고 패니가 말하니까요. 하지만 나는 그분이 그리젤다 그랜틀리 같은 처녀를 마음에 두어서는 안 된다는 것을 알아볼 만큼은 그분을 알아요. 그 아가씨가 한낱 태엽 인형이라는 것, 차갑고 생기 없고 넋이 없고 심지어 맹물 같다는 것을 그분은 알아야 해요. 도덕으로야 훌륭한 점이 무엇이든 있겠지만, 머릿속에는 아무것도 없다고 나는 믿어요. 내가 여태 본 어떤 사람보다도 그 아가씨는 아주 온전히 조각상 같아요. 가만히 앉아서 우러러 받는 것이 그 아가씨가 바라는 전부지요. 그것을 못 얻는다면, 가만히 앉아서 우러러 받지 않는 것으로도 거의 넉넉할 사람이에요. 나는 패니처럼 레이디 러프턴을 우러러 받들지는 않아요. 하지만 그분을 그만큼은 좋게 여기니까, 아들의 아내로 그런 처녀를 고르시는 것이 이상해요. 그분이 그것을 바라신다는 것은 의심하지 않아요. 그러나 러프턴 경도 그것을 바란다면 나는 정말로 놀라겠어요.” 그러고서 말을 마치며 루시는 다시 조랑말에 채찍을 휘둘렀다. 이는 속이 상해서 한 짓이었으니, 속을 다 알려 주는 피가 얼굴에 퍼져 오른 것을 느꼈기 때문이었다.\n\n“어머, 루시, 러프턴 경이 루시의 오라비라 해도 그보다 더 열을 낼 수는 없겠어요.”\n\n“그래요, 그럴 수 없지요. 그분은 내가 여태 가까이 지낸 유일한 남자 벗이에요. 그분이 스스로를 내던지는 것을 생각하면 견딜 수가 없어요. 그런 일에 마음을 쓰는 것이 끔찍하게 온당치 못하다는 건 의심하지 않아요.”\n\n“그분과 그 어머니가 둘 다 흡족하다면 우리도 흡족해도 된다는 것쯤은 인정해도 좋을 것 같은데요.”\n\n“나는 흡족하지 않을 거예요. 나를 그렇게 바라봐도 소용없어요, 패니. 패니가 나더러 이 이야기를 하게 만드니, 나는 이 일에 대해서 거짓말은 하지 않겠어요. 나는 러프턴 경을 정말 아주 좋아해요. 그리고 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 거의 그만큼 정말 싫어해요. 그러니 그 둘이 부부가 된다면 나는 흡족하지 않을 거예요. 하기야 둘 중 누구도 내 승낙을 구하지는 않겠지요. 레이디 러프턴께서 그러실 법도 없고요.” 그러고서 두 사람은 아마 사분의 일 마일쯤을 말없이 갔다.\n\n“가엾은 퍽!” 마침내 루시가 말했다. “그랜틀리 양이 조각상처럼 보인다고 해서 더는 매를 맞지 않을 거야, 그렇지? 그리고 패니, 마크에게 나를 정신 병원에 넣으라고는 하지 말아 주세요. 나도 매와 왜가리는 가릴 줄 알아요. 그래서 그렇게 몹시 어울리지 않는 혼사를 보고 싶지 않은 거예요.” 그 뒤로 그 이야기는 더 나오지 않았고, 두 사람은 이 분 뒤에 호글스톡 성직자의 집에 이르렀다.\n\n크롤리 부인은 콘월의 부목사직에서 호글스톡으로 올 때 아이 둘을 데리고 왔고, 그 뒤로 두 아이가 더 그녀의 근심에 보태어졌다. 그중 하나가 지금 후두염으로 앓고 있었으니, 그 어머니에게 얼마간의 위로와 도움을 건네고자 이번 방문이 이루어진 것이었다. 두 부인은 퍽을 붙들고 있어 줄 아이 하나를 얻어 놓고 마차에서 내렸고, 이내 크롤리 부인의 단 하나뿐인 거실에 들어섰다. 그녀는 아이 요람의 발판에 발을 올려 흔들면서 거기 앉아 있었고, 그 무릎에는 태어난 지 석 달쯤 된 젖먹이가 누워 있었다. 앓고 있는 위의 아이가 병중이라 젖먹이의 자리를 차지해 버린 것이었다. 훨씬 위인 다른 두 아이도 그 방에 있었다. 맏이는 계집아이로 아홉 살쯤이었고, 다른 하나는 그보다 세 살 아래인 사내아이였다. 이 둘은 아버지의 팔꿈치 옆에 서 있었는데, 아버지는 문법의 초기 신비를 그 아이들에게 착실히 들여 넣어 주려 애쓰고 있었다. 사실을 말하자면 로바츠 부인은 크롤리 씨가 그 자리에 없기를 훨씬 더 바랐을 것이다. 그녀는 몰래 들여올 물건들을 몸에 지니고 또 곁에 두고 왔는데, 아이들에게 주는 선물이라 부르기로 한 것이었으나 실은 그 가엾고 너무나 부려진 어머니를 덜어 주려는 것이었고, 크롤리 씨가 있는 데서는 그것을 내놓기가 도저히 불가능하다는 것을 그들도 알고 있었다.\n\n앞서 말하였듯 그녀는 저 무서웠던 콘월 시절의 뒤끝만큼 그렇게까지 앙상하지도, 그렇게까지 초췌하지도 않았다. 레이디 러프턴과 애러빈 부인이 서로 힘을 합한 것과, 나아지기는 하였으나 여전히 딱한 수입이 주는 빈약한 편안함이, 그녀를 어린 시절의 부드러운 날들에 살았던 그 세상으로 얼마간 되돌려 놓는 데에 무언가를 해 놓았다. 그러나 연 백삼십 파운드라는 그 넉넉한 급여 — 우리 새 교구들 가운데 어떤 곳에서 성직자들의 수입이 지금 매겨지는 잣대로 보면 넉넉하다 할 급여 — 조차, 아내와 네 아이를 둔 신사가 장인의 집안이 누리는 여느 편안함만큼 사는 것을 허락하지 않았다. 그저 먹고 마시는 것만 보아도, 고기며 차며 버터의 양은 어떤 장인이라도 반쯤 굶는 것과나 어울린다고 여길 만한 만큼만 쓰였다. 아이들에게는 더 나은 옷이 있어야 하였고, 그에게도 더 나은 옷이 있어야 하였다. 그녀 자신이 걸치는 것으로 말하면, 크롤리 부인의 가장 좋은 겉옷을 참아 낼 장인의 아내는 거의 없었을 것이다. 그 옷을 지은 천값은, 그녀의 어머니가 온갖 어려움을 무릅쓰고 딸에게 조촐한 혼례 옷가지를 마련해 주었을 때에 치른 것이었다.\n\n루시는 크롤리 부인을 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 호글스톡을 찾는 일은 잦지 않았고, 대개 레이디 러프턴과 로바츠 부인이 함께 다녀왔다. 그것이 크롤리 씨에게 달갑지 않다는 것은 알려져 있었으니, 그는 혼자 내버려지는 데에서 거친 흡족함을 느끼는 사람이었다. 그는 자기를 덜어 주는 이들에게 노여움을 느낀다고 해도 거의 옳을 것이며, 빚을 갚아 준 일로 바체스터의 수석 사제를 아직까지 결코 용서하지 않은 것도 분명하였다. 지금의 성직록도 수석 사제가 그에게 준 것이었다. 그러기에 그 오랜 벗은, 옛날에 부목사인 그 자신만큼이나 무일푼으로 그 농가로 내려오던 때만큼 지금은 그에게 소중하지 않았다. 그때에는 둘이 몇 시간이고 바위에 둘린 바닷가를 함께 걸으며 물결 소리를 듣고 깊은 논쟁의 신비를 따졌으니, 어떤 때는 뜨거운 노여움으로, 또 어떤 때는 부드럽고 사랑스러운 너그러움으로 그러하였으되, 서로 상대의 참됨을 인정하는 마음만은 언제나 있었다. 이제 두 사람은 견주어 보면 가까이 살았으나, 그런 따짐을 할 기회는 생기지 않았다. 아무려나 크롤리 씨는 석 달에 한 번은 그 오랜 벗에게서 수석 사제관으로 찾아오라는 재촉을 받았고, 애러빈 박사는 크롤리 씨가 사람들과 어울리는 것을 마다한다면 집에 다른 누구도 두지 않겠노라 약조하였다. 그러나 그것은 그가 바라는 바가 아니었다. 수석 사제관의 화려함과 웅장함, 그리고 그 따뜻하고 안온한 서재의 편안함은 그를 그 자리에서 입 다물게 만들 것이었다. 애러빈 박사가 어찌하여 이 호글스톡으로 나와서, 예전에 함께 터벅터벅 걷던 대로 진창 골목을 함께 걸어 주지 않는가? 그렇게만 하였다면 그도 즐거울 수 있었을 것이다. 그렇게만 하였다면 말도 할 수 있었을 것이다. 그렇게만 하였다면 옛날이 그들에게 되돌아왔을 것이다. 그러나 이제는! — “애러빈은 요즘 언제나 매끈하고 좋은 말을 타고 다니오,” 언젠가 그가 아내에게 비웃음을 담아 말한 적이 있었다. 그의 가난은 그 자신에게 너무도 무서운 것이었기에, 넉넉한 벗을 사랑할 마음은 그에게 있지 않았다.\n\n# 제22장. 호글스톡 목사관\n\n앞 장 끝에서 우리는 루시 로바츠를 크롤리 부인에게 소개받기를 기다리는 자리에 두고 나왔다. 크롤리 부인은 젖먹이 하나를 무릎에 앉힌 채, 발밑 요람에 누운 또 하나를 흔들어 재우며 앉아 있었다. 그러는 동안 크롤리 씨는 낡은 문법책 낱장 사이에 손가락을 끼운 채 자리에서 일어났는데, 그 책으로 위의 두 아이를 가르치던 중이었다. 로바츠 부인과 루시가 거실에 들어섰을 때 크롤리 집안 사람들이 이렇게 모두 그들 앞에 있었다.\n\n“이쪽이 제 시누이 루시예요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. “크롤리 부인, 지금은 부디 움직이지 마세요. 아니면 움직이시려거든 아기는 제가 안을게요.” 그러고서 그녀는 팔을 내밀어 젖먹이를 받아 안았고, 아기는 그 품에서 아주 편안해했다. 그녀에게도 집에 이런 일거리가 제 몫으로 있었고 결코 소홀히 하지도 않았으니 말이다. 다만 유모의 손길이야 호글스톡보다 넉넉하기는 하였다.\n\n크롤리 부인은 그래도 일어나서 루시에게 만나서 반갑다고 말했고, 크롤리 씨는 문법책을 손에 든 채 겸손하고 온순한 낯으로 앞으로 나왔다. 우리가 그와 그의 반평생 동반자의 가장 깊은 속내를 들여다볼 수 있었다면, 그에게는 가난의 자랑에 가난하다는 수치의 감정이 섞여 있으나 그녀에게는 이 문제에 관하여 자랑도 부끄러움도 없다는 것을 보았을 것이다. 삶의 실상이 그녀에게 하도 준엄해진 까닭에 그 겉모양 따위는 아무것도 아니었다. 새 가운이 있으면 쓸모가 있으니 좋기야 하겠지만, 교회에 입고 가는 그 옷을 세 번이나 뒤집어 고쳐 지었다는 것을 온 주가 다 안다 해도 그녀에게는 아무렇지도 않을 일이었다. 그러나 그에게는 자기와 제 식구가 그토록 헐한 옷을 걸치고 있다는 생각이 쓰라렸다.\n\n“로바츠 양, 앉을 의자조차 찾기 어려우실 듯합니다,” 크롤리 씨가 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 찾을 수 있어요. 여기 있는 것이라야 이 도련님의 서고밖에 없으니까요,” 루시가 표지도 없는 해진 책 한 무더기를 탁자 위로 옮기며 말했다. “옮긴 것을 이 도련님이 용서해 주면 좋겠네요.”\n\n“그건 밥 것이 아니에요. 적어도 대부분은요. 제 거예요,” 여자아이가 말했다.\n\n“그래도 몇 권은 내 거야,” 남자아이가 말했다. “그렇지, 그레이스?”\n\n“그럼 너는 대단한 학자로구나?” 루시가 아이를 제 쪽으로 끌어당기며 물었다.\n\n“모르겠어요,” 그레이스가 쑥스러운 낯으로 말했다. “저는 그리스어 독본하고 불규칙 동사를 해요.”\n\n“그리스어 독본에 불규칙 동사라니!” 그러고서 루시는 놀라 두 손을 들어 보였다.\n\n“그리고 그레이스는 호라티우스 송가 하나를 통째로 외워요,” 밥이 말했다.\n\n“호라티우스 송가를!” 루시는 부끄럼 타는 그 어린 여자 신동을 여전히 제 무릎께에 꼭 붙여 안은 채 말했다.\n\n“그것이 제가 아이들에게 줄 수 있는 전부입니다,” 크롤리 씨가 변명하듯 말했다. “약간의 학식이 제 몫으로 돌아온 유일한 재산이니, 그것이나마 자식들과 나누어 가지려 애쓰는 것입니다.”\n\n“사람들이 그것이야말로 우리가 가질 수 있는 가장 좋은 재산이라고 하지요,” 루시는 그렇게 말했으나, 속으로는 호라티우스니 그리스어 불규칙 동사니 하는 것이 아홉 살 난 아가씨에게는 지나치게 이른 억지 공부 냄새가 난다고 생각했다. 그러나 그럼에도 그레이스는 예쁘고 순박해 보이는 아이였고, 제 편이 되어 준 사람에게 꼭 매달렸으며, 어루만져 주는 것을 좋아하는 듯했다. 그래서 루시는 크롤리 씨만 어서 치워지고 선물들을 꺼낼 수 있게 되기를 안절부절 바랐다.\n\n“로바츠 씨는 별고 없으시겠지요,” 크롤리 씨가 딱딱하고 격식 차린 목소리로 말했다. 프램리의 서재에서 동료 성직자와 단둘이 있을 때 그토록 힘차게 몰아붙이던 그 목소리와는 아주 달랐다.\n\n“네, 아주 잘 있어요, 고맙습니다. 그 좋은 일은 들으셨겠지요?”\n\n“예, 들었습니다,” 크롤리 씨가 침중하게 말했다. “그 승진이 이승에서나 저승에서나 모든 면으로 그에게 유익이 되기를 바랍니다.”\n\n그러나 그가 그 호의로운 축원을 내놓는 태도로 보아, 그의 바람과 그의 예상이 서로 손을 맞잡고 가지는 않는다는 것이 뻔히 드러나는 듯했다.\n\n“그런데 마크가 내일 여기 들르겠다고 전해 달라 했어요. 열한 시쯤이라고 했지요, 패니?”\n\n“네. 교구 일로 뵙고 싶어 하는 것 같아요,” 로바츠 부인이, 벌써 크롤리 부인과 아이 기르는 이야기로 열심히 주고받던 이야기에서 잠깐 눈을 들며 말했다.\n\n“부디 이렇게 전해 주십시오,” 크롤리 씨가 말했다. “기꺼이 뵙겠다고요. 다만 이제 새 직무가 그에게 얹혔으니, 아마 제가 프램리로 찾아가는 편이 나을 것입니다.”\n\n“새 직무가 아직은 그이를 그리 어지럽히지 않아요,” 루시가 말했다. “그리고 여기까지 말을 타고 오는 것쯤은 그이에게 수고랄 것도 없어요.”\n\n“예. 그 점에서는 그가 저보다 낫습니다. 저는 불행히도 말이 없으니까요.”\n\n그러고서 루시는 어린 사내아이를 어루만지기 시작했고, 슬금슬금 머프 속에서 생강 과자알 한 봉지를 꺼내어 아이의 손에 밀어 넣었다. 그녀에게는 올케만큼 기다리는 데 필요한 참을성이 없었다.\n\n사내아이는 봉지를 받아 안을 살짝 들여다보고는, 고개를 들어 루시의 얼굴을 보았다.\n\n“그게 무엇이냐, 밥?” 크롤리 씨가 말했다.\n\n“생강 과자예요,” 보비가 더듬거리며 말했다. 무슨 죄가 저질러졌다는 느낌이 들었으나, 아마도 자기 자신이 아직 그 죄를 깊이 진 것으로 셈해질 수는 없다는 느낌도 들었던 것이다.\n\n“로바츠 양,” 아버지가 말했다. “대단히 고맙습니다. 그러나 저희 아이들은 그런 것에 익숙하지 못합니다.”\n\n“저는 마음이 약한 여자예요, 크롤리 씨. 아이들을 보러 갈 때면 늘 이런 것을 지니고 다니지요. 그러니 용서해 주시고, 이 어린 아드님이 받게 해 주셔야 해요.”\n\n“아, 물론입니다. 밥, 아가, 그 봉지를 어머니께 갖다 드리렴. 어머니가 너와 그레이스에게 한 번에 하나씩 주실 것이다.” 그리하여 봉지는 엄숙한 태도로 아이들의 어머니에게 옮겨졌고, 어머니는 그것을 아들의 손에서 받아 책꽂이 높은 곳에 올려놓았다.\n\n“지금 하나도 안 되나요?” 루시 로바츠가 몹시 애처롭게 말했다. “그렇게 야박하게 굴지 마세요, 크롤리 씨. 아이들에게가 아니라 저에게요. 그것이 그 나름으로 맛이 좋은지 알아볼 수도 없나요?”\n\n“아주 좋은 것일 줄 압니다. 그러나 아이들 어머니는 지금은 두어 두는 편을 원할 것 같습니다.”\n\n이것은 루시에게 몹시 낙담스러운 일이었다. 생강 과자알 작은 봉지 하나가 이토록 큰 난관을 만들어 낸다면, 아직 머프 속에 들어 있는 구아바 젤리 단지와 봉봉 한 상자는 어떻게 처리할 것인가. 조랑말 마차에 실려 있는 오렌지 꾸러미는 또 어떻게 나눌 것인가. 게다가 앓는 아이에게 줄 젤리가 있었고, 닭 국물도 있었는데 이것 또한 실상은 또 하나의 젤리였다. 그리고 숨김없이 털어놓자면, 프램리 목사관 농막에서 가져온 생돼지고기 한 덩이와 달걀 한 바구니도 있었다. 로바츠 부인이 스스로 그럴 만한 힘이 있다고 여겨지면 내놓기로 한 것이었으나, 크롤리 씨의 눈앞에서 곧바로 내밀었다가는 여지없이 멸시받아 내던져질 것이 뻔했다. 포트 와인 두세 병을 보태자는 말도 나왔지만, 그 대목에서는 두 부인의 용기가 모자랐던 까닭에 술까지 그들의 난관에 보태지지는 않았다.\n\n루시는 크롤리 씨와 이야기를 이어 가기가 몹시 어렵다고 느꼈다. 더구나 로바츠 부인과 크롤리 부인이 곧 아래의 두 아이를 데리고 침실로 물러가 버렸으니 더욱 그러했다. “어쩌면 이렇게 운이 없을까,” 루시는 생각했다. “올케가 내 머프를 가지고 가지 않았으니!” 그러나 머프는 그 풍성한 내용물로 무겁게 그녀의 무릎 위에 놓여 있었다.\n\n“이제 한 해의 얼마쯤은 바체스터에서 지내시게 되겠지요,” 크롤리 씨가 말했다.\n\n“아직은 정말 잘 모르겠어요. 마크는 첫 한 달 거주 근무 동안은 하숙을 얻겠다고 말하고 있어요.”\n\n“그러나 사택이 나오지 않습니까?”\n\n“아, 네. 그렇겠지요.”\n\n“저로서는 그것이 그의 교구 일과, 그가 거기서 두루 쓰이는 일과 어긋나지 않을까 염려됩니다. 예컨대 학교 같은 것 말입니다.”\n\n“마크는 거리가 워낙 가까우니 거주 근무 동안에도 프램리를 오래 비울 필요는 없다고 생각해요. 그리고 학교 일은 레이디 러프턴이 아주 잘 챙겨 주시고요.”\n\n“아! 그렇지요. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 성직자가 아닙니다, 로바츠 양.”\n\n마님도 성직자나 거의 매한가지로 지독하다는 말이 루시의 혀끝까지 올라왔으나, 그녀는 스스로 참았다.\n\n이때 섭리가 로바츠 양에게 큰 구원을 보내 주었으니, 그것은 크롤리 부인의 팔이 붉은 잡일 하녀의 모습으로 왔다. 하녀는 주인에게 걸어와 그를 찾는 사람이 있다고 귀에 속삭였다. 그가 늘 교구 학교에 나가 있어야 하는 시각이었다. 그리고 그 출석이 어찌나 어김없었던지, 그를 찾는 사람들은 이 시각이면 거기서 그를 찾았고, 그가 없으면 거리낌 없이 사람을 보내어 불러냈다.\n\n“로바츠 양, 미안하지만 양해해 주셔야겠습니다,” 그가 일어나 모자와 지팡이를 집어 들며 말했다. 루시는 제가 조금이라도 방해가 되지 않기를 바란다고 말하고는, 벌써 어떻게 하면 제 보물들을 가장 잘 부려 놓을 수 있을지 궁리하기 시작했다. “로바츠 부인께 안부를 전해 주시고, 작별 인사를 드리는 기쁨을 놓쳐 미안하다고 말해 주시겠습니까? 그러나 부인이 학교 앞을 지날 때에는 아마 뵙게 될 것입니다.” 그러고서 그는 지팡이를 손에 들고 걸어 나갔고, 루시는 보비의 눈이 곧바로 생강 과자알 봉지에 머무는 것을 본 듯했다.\n\n“밥,” 루시가 거의 속삭이듯 말했다. “알사탕 좋아하니?”\n\n“아주 많이요,” 밥이 지극히 진중한 태도로, 아버지가 지나갔는지 보려고 창에 눈을 두고서 말했다.\n\n“그럼 이리 오렴,” 루시가 말했다. 그러나 그녀가 말하는 사이에 문이 다시 열리며 크롤리 씨가 나타났다. “책을 두고 갔습니다,” 그가 말하고는 방을 지나 되돌아와, 교구를 두루 돌아다니는 길에 늘 그와 함께하던 낡을 대로 낡은 기도서를 집어 들었다. 보비는 아버지를 보자 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러났고, 그레이스도 그러했다. 사실을 털어놓자면 그레이스도 불규칙 동사에 아랑곳없이 알사탕이라는 소리에 끌려 있었던 것이다. 그리고 루시는 머프에서 손을 빼며 죄지은 낯이 되었다. 그녀는 이 선량한 사람을 속이고 있는 것이 아닌가. 아니, 그의 자식들에게 아버지를 속이는 법을 가르치고 있는 것이 아닌가? 그러나 천사라 해도 속임수를 조금 쓰지 않고는 함께 살기 어려운 재목으로 지어진 사람들이 있는 것이다.\n\n“아빠 이제 가셨어요,” 보비가 속삭였다. “모퉁이 도는 걸 봤어요.” 적어도 이 아이는 배울 것을 배운 것이었다 — 그러는 것이 자연스러운 일이기도 했다.\n\n아빠가 갔다는 것을 알아차린 사람이 또 있었다. 밥과 그레이스가 아직도 큰 사탕 덩이를 세고 있는 동안, 저마다 그동안의 속을 달래느라 보리사탕 한 치씩을 입에 물고 있는 동안, 현관문이 열리며 큰 바구니 하나와 부엌 보에 싸맨 꾸러미 하나가 슬그머니 집 안으로 들어와, 크롤리 부인의 침실 탁자 위에서 로바츠 부인의 손으로 재빨리 풀렸다.\n\n“그래도 가져오는 것을 감행했어요,” 패니가 부끄러운 낯빛으로 말했다. “앓는 아이가 집을 온통 붙들고 있다는 것을 저도 아니까요.”\n\n“아! 내 벗이여,” 크롤리 부인이 로바츠 부인의 팔을 붙잡고 그 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다. “그런 부끄러움은 저에게는 이미 지나간 일이에요. 하느님이 궁핍으로 저희를 시험하셨으니, 아이들을 위해서라면 이런 도움이 반갑습니다.”\n\n“하지만 그분이 노하지는 않으실까요?”\n\n“제가 잘 해 보겠어요. 로바츠 부인, 그이를 보고 놀라시면 안 돼요. 그이의 처지는 이따금 견디기가 몹시 어려워요. 그런 일은 여자보다 남자에게 훨씬 더 못 견딜 것이지요.”\n\n패니는 마음속으로 이것을 선선히 인정할 준비가 되어 있지는 않았으나, 그 대목에 대해서는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. “저희가 부인께 쓸모가 있게 되기를 정말로 바라요,” 그녀가 말했다. “저를 오래된 벗으로만 여겨 주시고, 제가 필요하시면 편지를 주세요. 그분의 마음을 거스르지나 않을까 겁이 나서 자주 오기를 망설이는 거예요.”\n\n그러고서 차츰 두 사람 사이에 속을 터놓는 마음이 생겼고, 종신 부목사의 가난에 찌든 아내는 바체스터 참사회원의 넉넉한 젊은 아내에게 제 짐의 무게를 말할 수 있게 되었다. “괴로운 일이에요,” 앞의 부인은 그렇게 말했다. 제 주위 다른 성직자들의 아내들과 자기가 그토록 다르다고 느끼는 것이, 또 그들은 편안하게 사는데 자기는 두 손으로 온갖 일을 다 하고 기운을 쉬지 않고 짜내면서도 남편과 자식들 앞에 성한 음식을 놓기가 겨우겨우라는 것을 아는 것이 괴로웠다. 제 머리가 하는 일 전부가 이런 따위의 문제에 바쳐진다는 것은 무서운 일이요, 생각하기에 애통한 일이었다. “그러나 그럼에도 견딜 수 있어요,” 그녀가 말했다. 그이가 사내답게 처신하고 세상 앞에서 제 처지를 담대히 마주하기만 한다면. 그러고서 그녀는 그이가 예전에 저 아래 콘월에 살던 곳에서보다 이곳 호글스톡에서 얼마나 나았는지를 이야기하고, 따뜻한 말로 저희에게 그토록 많은 것을 해 준 그 벗에게 감사를 표했다.\n\n“애러빈 부인이 부인네가 자기들에게 와 주기를 몹시 바란다고 저에게 말했어요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아, 그래요. 하지만 그것은 안 될 일인 듯해요. 아이들이 있으니까요, 로바츠 부인.”\n\n“제가 둘은 맡아 돌봐 드릴게요.”\n\n“아, 아니에요. 그런 식으로 부인의 선의에 벌을 드릴 수는 없어요. 그렇지만 그이가 가지 않을 거예요. 그이만 가고 저는 집에 남을 수도 있지요. 이따금 그렇게 되면 어떨까 생각해 보았고, 그이를 설득하려고 제 힘으로 할 수 있는 것은 다 해 보았어요. 한 번만이라도 다시 세상과, 그러니까 성직자들의 세상과 섞일 수 있다면 자기 직무를 해내기에도 더 알맞아질 것이라고 말해 보았지요. 그러나 그이는 노여운 낯으로, 그것은 안 될 일이라고, 제 상의가 수석 사제의 식탁에 앉기에 걸맞지 않다고 대답해요.” 그런 이유를 말하면서 크롤리 부인은 얼굴이 붉어질 지경이었다.\n\n“네! 애러빈 박사 같은 오래된 벗한테요? 그것은 정말 터무니없는 소리예요.”\n\n“저도 그런 줄 알아요. 수석 사제님은 어떤 상의를 입고 가도 그이를 반갑게 맞아 주실 거예요. 그렇지만 실상은, 그이가 직무가 부르지 않는 한 부자의 집에 들어서는 것을 견디지 못한다는 거예요.”\n\n“하지만 그것은 분명 잘못된 생각이지요?”\n\n“잘못된 생각이에요. 그렇지만 제가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요? 그이는 부자를 제 원수로 여기는 듯해요. 그이는 마음을 놓고 이야기할 벗의 위로를 몹시 그리워하고 있어요. 자기와 같이 닦인 머리를 지닌 대등한 사람, 그 사람의 생각을 듣고 제 생각을 말할 수 있는 사람을요. 그런데 그런 벗은 머리만이 아니라 지갑까지도 대등해야 하니, 그런 사람을 그이가 어디서 찾겠어요?”\n\n“하지만 더 나은 성직 자리를 받게 되실 수도 있잖아요.”\n\n“아, 아니에요. 그리고 그이가 받는다 해도 저희는 이제 그런 자리에 걸맞지도 않아요. 제 아이들을 가르칠 수 있겠다고 생각할 수만 있다면, 딱한 우리 그레이스를 위해 무엇이라도 해 줄 수만 있다면—”\n\n이 말에 로바츠 부인은 한두 마디를 했을 뿐 많이 말하지 않았다. 그러나 남편의 허락을 얻을 수 있다면 그레이스를 위해 무엇이든 해야겠다고 마음을 굳혔다. 그것이 좋은 일이 아니겠는가? 섭리가 자신에게 베풀어 준 온갖 좋은 것들을 어질게 쓰는 것이야말로 자기가 마땅히 해야 할 일이 아니겠는가?\n\n그러고서 두 사람은 각기 다시 어린아이를 하나씩 안고 거실로 돌아갔고, 크롤리 부인은 닭 국물과 돼지 다리 고기와 달걀 꾸러미를 부엌에 감추어 두었다. 그동안 루시는 아이들과 어울려 있었는데, 결혼한 두 부인이 들어서니 가게가 하나 열려 있어 온갖 사치품이 놀랍도록 헐한 값에 척척 팔리고 사들여지는 중이었다. 구아바 젤리가 거기 있었고, 오렌지도, 붉고 노랗고 줄무늬 든 알사탕도 있었다. 게다가 그 장사 투기의 대담함 속에서 생강 과자까지 내려져 있었고, 과자알들은 널판 위에 펼쳐져 있었으며, 루시는 그 뒤에 가게 처녀로 서서 입맞춤을 받고 그것들을 넘겨주고 있었다.\n\n“엄마, 엄마,” 보비가 어머니에게 달려가며 말했다. “엄마도 저 사람한테서 뭘 사야 해요.” 그러고서 그는 손가락으로 그 가게 처녀를 가리켰다. “저 보리사탕 무더기는 입맞춤 두 번을 줘야 해요.” 그때 보비의 입을 보았다면, 그 아이의 입맞춤은 없어도 좋겠다고 말했을 것이다.\n\n두 사람이 다시 조랑말 마차에 올라 성마른 퍽의 뒤에 앉고, 문에서 넉넉히 멀어졌을 때, 먼저 입을 뗀 것은 패니였다.\n\n“그 두 사람은 어쩌면 그렇게 다를까요,” 그녀가 말했다. “머리도 다르고 마음결도 다르고요!”\n\n“그런데 부인의 머리가 그이의 머리보다 얼마나 더 높은 결인지요! 그이는 많은 일에 그토록 약하고, 부인은 모든 일에 그토록 강해요! 그이의 자랑은 얼마나 거짓되고, 그이의 부끄러움은 또 얼마나 거짓된지요!”\n\n“하지만 그이가 무엇을 견뎌야 하는지도 생각해야 해요. 그런 삶을 거짓된 자랑도 거짓된 부끄러움도 없이 견뎌 낼 수 있는 사람은 흔하지 않아요.”\n\n“그렇지만 부인은 둘 다 없잖아요,” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“한 집안에 영웅이 하나 있다고 해서 또 하나를 기대할 권리가 생기나요?” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. “내가 아는 사람들 가운데 영웅다움에 가장 가까운 이는 크롤리 부인일 거예요.”\n\n그러고서 두 사람은 호글스톡 학교 앞을 지났고, 크롤리 씨는 바퀴 소리를 듣고 밖으로 나왔다.\n\n“퍽 친절하셨습니다,” 그가 말했다. “제 딱한 아내와 그토록 오래 있어 주셨으니.”\n\n“가신 뒤에 이야기할 것이 아주 많았어요.”\n\n“참으로 고마운 일입니다. 요즘 아내는 벗을 자주 보지 못하니까요. 로바츠 씨에게 제가 내일 열한 시에 이 학교에 있겠다고 전해 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠습니까?”\n\n그러고서 그는 모자를 벗어 두 사람에게 절하였고, 두 사람은 마차를 몰아 나아갔다.\n\n“그이가 정말로 부인의 편안함을 마음에 두고 있다면, 그이를 그토록 나쁘게 생각하지는 않겠어요,” 루시가 말했다.\n\n# 제23장. 거인들의 승리\n\n그리고 이제 사월이 저물 무렵, 사람이 사는 온 지구의 구석구석에 거의 동시에 소식 하나가 도착했는데, 그것이 담고 있는 뜻은 우리 이야기의 주요 인물 가운데 한 사람에게—어떤 이는 이 이야기의 으뜸 인물이라 여길지도 모를 사람에게—끔찍한 것이었다. 의회의 높은 자리에 있는 사람들은 모두 틀림없이 그렇게 여길 것이며, 그런 이들의 아내와 딸들도 그럴 것이다. 신들을 상대로 싸움을 벌인 티탄들이 한동안 승세를 잡았다. 티포에우스와 미마스, 포르피리온과 로에쿠스, 무지에 절고 부패와 짝을 맺은 저 옛 거인 족속이 올림포스의 높은 곳까지 기어올랐다. 치명적이고 묵직한 투척물을 겁 없이 던져 대는 자, 그 무서운 무릿매를 늘 손에 들고 있는 자—언론계의 엔켈라도스인 서플하우스가 도운 것이었다. 그리고 별들의 회의가 온통 무너져 내리는 이 판국에, 가련한 디아나, 프티백의 디아나가 무엄한 오리온에게 그 자랑스러운 자리를 내주는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 다시 말해, 내각은 사임을 강요당했고, 그들과 함께 해럴드 스미스 씨도 물러났다.\n\n“그래서 딱한 해럴드는 관직의 단맛을 제대로 보기도 전에 밀려났습니다,” 사워비가 벗인 목사에게 편지를 써 보냈다. “그리고 제가 아는 한, 그가 내각에 들어간 뒤로 그 내각의 손에 떨어진 성직 추천권이라고는 단 하나뿐이었는데, 그것이 프램리까지 내려갔지요—제게는 큰 기쁨이자 만족입니다.” 그러나 자기에게 베풀어진 은혜를 그토록 자주 일깨워 받는 것이, 같은 사안을 두고 마크의 기쁨과 만족에 보탬이 되었다고는 도무지 하기 어려웠다.\n\n이 내각의 붕괴는 끔찍한 일이었고, 마지막까지 저 새 피 이론에 믿음을 두고 있던 해럴드 스미스에게는 더욱 그러했다. 자기가 이제 겨우 합류한 정부에 하원의 큰 다수가 반대표를 던졌다는 것을 그는 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다. “이런 식으로 계속 나간다면,” 그가 젊은 벗 그린 워커에게 말했다. “여왕의 정부를 운영해 나갈 수가 없소.” 여왕의 정부를 운영해 나가는 일이 어렵다는 저 주장은, 어떤 위대한 인물이 처음 그 생각을 내놓은 뒤로 근년에 자주 거론되어 왔다. 그럼에도 여왕의 정부는 운영되고 있으며, 이 일에 대한 사람들의 성향과 적성은 조금도 줄어드는 것 같지 않다. 젊은 정치가가 몇 되지 않는다면, 그것은 고참들이 제 마구가 덜컹거리는 소리를 그토록 좋아하기 때문이다.\n\n“여왕의 정부를 어떻게 운영해 나갈지 나는 정말 모르겠소,” 해럴드 스미스가 그린 워커에게 말했다. 여왕이 일급 정치가를 차례차례 불러들이던, 저 무서울 만큼 흥미로운 날들의 첫날, 두 사람은 하원 로비 한구석에 서 있었다. 그리고 몇몇 근심 많은 이들은 우리가 정말로 또 하나의 내각이라는 축복을 얻을 수 있을지 의심하기 시작하고 있었다. 신들은 모두 제자리에서 사라졌다. 거인들이 우리를 위해 무엇이든 해 줄 만큼 친절할 것인가, 아닌가? 거인들이 우리를 위해 아무것도 해 주지 않겠다고 거절할 것이라 여기는 듯한 사람들도 있었다. “이제 하원은 월요일까지 정회될 것이고, 나로서는 어지간한 것을 준다 해도 여왕 폐하의 처지에 놓이고 싶지 않소,” 해럴드 스미스 씨가 말했다.\n\n“맹세코, 나도 싫지요!” 그린 워커가 말했다. 그는 요즈음 확고한 해럴드 스미스파였는데, 한 내각 각료에게 실질적인 지지대로 붙어 있다는 데 자부심을 느끼고 있었다. 그저 브록파에 머무는 데 만족했다면 그는 아무것도 아닌 사람으로 셈되었을 것이다. “맹세코, 나도 싫지요!” 그러면서 그린 워커는 눈을 크게 뜨고 고개를 저었다. 여왕 폐하가 놓여 있을 위태로운 처지를 생각한 것이다. “—— 경이 외무부를 얻지 못하면 그들에게 합류하지 않으리라는 것을 나는 우연히 알고 있습니다.” 그러고서 그는 티탄들의 회의에 지극히 중요하다고들 하는, 팔이 백 개나 되는 어떤 기아스의 이름을 댔다.\n\n“그리고 그건 물론 불가능하지. 대체 그들이 어떻게 할 셈인지 나는 모르겠소. 시도니아도 있지. 그가 지금 무슨 어려움을 일으키고 있다고들 하는군.” 그런데 시도니아는 또 다른 거인이었고, 매우 강력하다고들 여겨졌다.\n\n“여왕께서 그를 만나지 않으실 거라는 건 우리 모두 알지요,” 그린 워커가 말했다. 그는 크루 정션 선거구의 하원 의원이자 레이디 하틀탑의 조카였으니, 여왕이 무엇을 할 것이고 무엇을 하지 않을 것인지 알아낼 완벽하게 정확한 수단을 물론 가지고 있었다.\n\n“사실은 말이오,” 해럴드 스미스가 축출당한 신의 처지로 다시 돌아와 말했다. “하원은 제가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 조금도 이해하지 못하고 있소. 제가 무엇을 원하는지도 모르지. 내가 그들에게 묻고 싶은 것은 이것이오. 여왕께 정부를 두어 드릴 뜻이 있는가, 없는가? 시도니아나 드 테리어 경 같은 사람들을 지지할 각오가 되어 있는가? 그렇다면 나는 그들의 순종하는 종이오. 다만 나는 몹시 놀라게 될 것이오, 그뿐이지.” 드 테리어 경은 이 무렵 모든 사람에게 거인들의 우두머리로 인정받고 있었다.\n\n“저도 그럴 겁니다—지독히 놀랄 테지요. 그들은 그럴 수 없어요, 아시겠지만. 맨체스터 사람들이 있잖습니까. 내 고향 쪽 일이니 그들에 대해서는 내가 좀 알아야 하지요. 그래서 말하는데, 그들은 드 테리어 경을 지지할 수 없습니다. 그건 자연스럽지 않을 테니까요.”\n\n“자연스럽다니! 인간의 본성은 이제 끝장이 났소,” 해럴드 스미스가 말했다. 세상이 자기가 합류한 정부를 뒤엎으려고 공모한다는 것을, 게다가 그가 그 정부를 위해 얼마나 해낼지 지켜보기를 기다리지도 않은 채 그런다는 것을 그는 좀처럼 이해할 수 없었다. “사실은 이렇소, 워커. 우리 사이에는 이제 당파에 대한 강한 정서가 남아 있지 않소.”\n\n“그렇지요, 쥐뿔도 없—” 그린 워커가 말했다. 그는 지금의 정치적 야심에 매우 열심이었다.\n\n“그것을 되찾기 전까지는 우리는 자리가 굳고 손이 확실한 정부를 결코 가질 수 없을 것이오. 아무도 사람을 한 주에서 다음 주까지 믿을 수 없소. 어느 달에 한 각료를 권좌에 앉힌 바로 그 의원들이, 다음 달에는 맨 먼저 그에게 반대표를 던지는 것이오.”\n\n“그런 짓은 막아야 합니다. 그러지 않으면 우리는 결코 아무 좋은 일도 못 할 거예요.”\n\n“브리틀백 경에 관해서 브록이 틀렸다는 것을 부인할 생각은 없소. 나는 그가 틀렸다고 생각하고, 처음부터 끝까지 그렇게 말해 왔지. 그러나 세상에 이럴 수가—!” 그러고서 해럴드 스미스는 말을 마치지 않은 채 고개를 돌리고 두 손을 마주쳤다. 시대의 어리석음에 놀랐다는 표시였다. 그가 아마 나타내려 한 뜻은 이러했다. 얼마 전 프티백 청에서 이루어진 그 임명과 같은 훌륭한 선행이, 그가 넌지시 가리킨 저 다른 악행을 속죄하기에 충분하다고 여겨지지 않는다면, 이 달 아래 세상의 모든 정의는 끝장이라는 것이다. 그토록 큰 덕이 드러났는데도 어떤 잘못도 용서받을 수 없다는 말인가?\n\n“저는 이걸 다 서플하우스 탓으로 돌립니다,” 그린 워커가 벗을 위로하려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n“그렇소,” 해럴드 스미스가 말했다. 이제 그는 의회 연설의 경계에 다다르고 있었으나, 여전히 숨을 죽인 채 단 한 사람의 청중에게 말하고 있었다. “그렇소. 우리는 돈에 팔리고 아무 책임도 지지 않는 언론의, 그것도 단 하나의 신문의 노예가 되어 가고 있소. 대단한 재주를 타고나지도 못했고, 대중의 신망을 누리지도 못했으며, 정치가로서 믿음을 받지도 못하고, 세상 일반에는 글쟁이로서조차 이름이 알려지지 않은 사람이 하나 있는데, 그런데도 《주피터》의 사원이라는 이유로 정부를 뒤엎고 온 나라를 당혹에 빠뜨릴 수 있는 것이오. 브록 경 같은 사람이 스스로 그토록 겁을 내도록 내버려 둔다는 것이 나에게는 놀랍기만 하오.” 그러면서도 해럴드 스미스가 서플하우스와, 《주피터》에 강경한 기사를 잇달아 싣고 여기에 맨체스터 사람들의 지지가 예상대로 더해지면 아마 총리를 그 자리에서 내던지는 데 효험이 있으리라고 의논한 지는 아직 한 달도 되지 않았다. 다만 그때 그 총리는 아직 젊은 피로 제 몸을 다시 북돋우지 않았었다. “여왕의 정부를 어떻게 운영해 나갈 것인가, 지금은 그것이 문제요,” 해럴드 스미스가 되풀이했다. 우리가 넌지시 가리킨 그 시기, 곧 한 달쯤 전에는 그에게 별로 당혹을 안기지 않았던 어려움이었다.\n\n바로 그때 사워비와 서플하우스가 함께 그들에게 합류했다. 총리가 정회를 통고한 뒤 대단찮은 안건 몇 가지가 처리된 하원에서 나온 것이었다.\n\n“그런데 해럴드,” 사워비가 말했다. “자네 상관의 성명은 어떻게 보나?”\n\n“나는 그것에 대해 할 말이 없네,” 해럴드 스미스가 모자의 처마 아래에서 매우 엄숙하게, 그리고 어쩌면 다소 험악하게 올려다보며 말했다. 사워비는 지난 위기 때 정부를 지지했다. 그런데 어째서 그가 지금 서플하우스 같은 자와 어울려 다니는 것이 눈에 띄는가?\n\n“꽤 잘했다고 나는 보네,” 사워비가 말했다.\n\n“참으로 아주 잘했지,” 서플하우스가 말했다. “그런 종류의 일은 늘 그렇게 해내니까. 사정 설명을 그만큼 잘하는 사람도 없고, 그만큼 먹히는 개인 성명을 내놓는 사람도 없네. 그는 그런 종류의 일에나 자신을 아껴 두어야 해.”\n\n“그러면 그동안 여왕의 정부는 누가 운영해 나가지?” 해럴드 스미스가 매우 험한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n“그건 이름이 덜한 사람들에게 맡겨 두어야지,” 《주피터》의 그 사람이 말했다. “사람들이 각료의 말을 정말로 귀 기울여 듣는 대목, 사람들이 정말로 마음을 쓰는 주제는 언제나 사사로운 것이네. 인도를 다스리는 최선의 방식에 우리 가운데 몇이 참으로 관심을 두는가? 그러나 총리의 인품에 닿는 문제라면 우리는 모두 울리는 꽹과리 주위로 벌떼처럼 모여들지.”\n\n“그것은 시기와 악의와 온갖 무자비함에서 나오는 것이지,” 해럴드 스미스가 말했다.\n\n“그렇지. 그리고 남의 것을 집어 들고 훔치는 짓, 험담과 거짓말과 비방에서도 나오지,” 사워비 씨가 말했다.\n\n“우리는 남의 자리를 바라고 탐내는 데 어찌나 쉬 기울어지는지,” 서플하우스가 말했다.\n\n“그런 사람도 있지,” 사워비가 말했다. “하지만 해악을 끼치는 건 험담과 거짓말과 비방이야. 그렇지 않나, 해럴드?”\n\n“그런데 그동안 여왕의 정부는 어떻게 운영해 나가지요?” 그린 워커 씨가 말했다.\n\n이튿날 아침 드 테리어 경이 버킹엄 궁에서 여왕을 뵈었다는 것이 알려졌고, 열두 시쯤에는 새 내각의 명단이 발표되었는데, 그것은 거인 족속 전부에게 지극히 만족스러운 것이었음이 틀림없다. 텔루스의 아들이라면 하나도 빠짐없이 그 안에 들었고, 딸들도 아주 많이 들었다. 그런데 그날 오후 늦게 브록 경이 다시 궁으로 불려 들어갔고, 웨스트 엔드의 클럽들 사이에서는 신들에게 다시 기회가 왔다는 말이 돌았다. “다만,” 해럴드 스미스 씨의 이익을 크게 대변한다고들 하는 석간신문 《퓨리스트》가 말했다. “다만 브록 경이 알맞은 사람을 알맞은 자리에 앉힐 지혜를 가질 수만 있다면. 그가 스미스 씨를 자기 정부에 끌어들인 것은 바로 요전의 일이었다. 이것이 옳은 방향으로 내디딘 걸음이었다는 데는 누구나 동의했으나, 불행히도 그 뒤에 일어난 소란을 막기에는 너무 늦었다. 이제 브록 경에게 여왕의 정부를 운영해 나갈 목적으로 정치가 명단을 고를 기회가 다시 올 듯하다. 그리고 스미스 씨 같은 사람들이 그 재능과 근면과 널리 인정받은 관직 적성으로 나라에 영구히 봉사할 수 있는 자리에 앉혀지기를 바라 마땅하다.”\n\n서플하우스는 사워비 씨를 팔꿈치 옆에 두고 클럽에서 이 글을 읽으면서, 문체가 너무나 뚜렷해서 지은이에 대해서는 의심의 여지가 없다고 단언했다. 그러나 우리 자신은 해럴드 스미스 씨가 그 기사를 직접 썼다고 생각하는 편이 아니다. 활자로 짜인 것을 그가 보았을 개연성은 있겠지만.\n\n그러나 이튿날 아침 《주피터》가 이 모든 문제를 결말지어, 그토록 사람을 부르고 다시 부른 끝에도 브록 경과 신들은 영구히 밀려났고 드 테리어 경과 거인들은 영구히 들어앉았음을 세상에 알렸다. 외무부가 아니면 가지 않겠다던 그 까탈스러운 거인은 사실 훨씬 덜 중요한 직무의 영역으로 갔고, 시도니아는 어느 고귀한 분이 수군거리며 싫어했음에도 최고 반열 거인의 온갖 부속물을 다 갖추고 출정의 막을 올렸다. “우리는 바란다,” 《주피터》가 말했다. “브록 경이 아직 한 수 배우기에 너무 늙지는 않았기를. 그렇다면 하원의, 그리고 나라의 것이라 해도 좋을 이번 결정이 브리틀백 경 같은 귀인들이나 해럴드 스미스 씨 같은 부러진 갈대들에게 믿음을 두지 말라고 그를 가르쳐 줄지도 모른다.” 그런데 서플하우스 씨가 던진 이 작별의 일격은 지나치게 매정하고 전혀 불필요했다고 우리는 늘 생각해 왔다.\n\n“이봐요,” 그 참사가 알려진 뒤 던스터블 양을 처음 만났을 때 스미스 부인이 말했다. “이 몰락을 내가 대체 어떻게 견디란 말이에요?” 그러고서 그녀는 레이스가 넉넉히 달린 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다.\n\n“그리스도인의 체념이지요,” 던스터블 양이 권했다.\n\n“쓸데없는 소리!” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. “당신 같은 백만장자들은 늘 그리스도인의 체념을 입에 올리지요. 무엇 하나 체념하고 내놓으라는 요구를 받아 본 적이 없으니까요. 내게 그리스도인의 체념이 조금이라도 있었다면 이런 겉치레와 허영을 마음에 두지도 않았을 거예요. 생각해 보세요, 이봐요. 내각 각료의 아내가 겨우 삼 주라니!”\n\n“딱한 스미스 씨는 그것을 어떻게 견디고 있어요?”\n\n“뭐요? 해럴드요? 그이는 복수의 희망만으로 살고 있어요. 서플하우스 씨를 끝장내고 나면 흐뭇하게 죽을 거예요.”\n\n그러고 나서 의회 양원에서 더 자세한 설명이 이어졌는데, 그것은 아주 흡족한 것이었다. 잘 자란 정중한 거인들은 신들에게, 자기들이 오사 산 위에 펠리온 산을 쌓아 올려 그렇게 권좌로 기어올라 온 것은 자기들 자신의 선의에 매우 어긋나는 일이었다고 다짐해 말했다. 거인들 자신은 위엄 있는 은퇴의 단맛을 더 좋아했으니 말이다. 그러나 백성의 목소리가 그들에게 너무 강했다. 그 애씀은 자기들이 아니라 다른 이들이 벌인 것이었고, 그들은 거인들이 국사의 머리에 서야 한다고 굳게 마음먹고 있었다. 실로 시대의 정신이 거인들에게 너무나 분명히 기울어 있어서 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 상원에서는 브리아레오스가, 하원에서는 오리온이 그렇게 말했다. 그러자 신들은 자기 자리를 내주는 것이 절대로 기쁘다고 했다. 그리고 신답지 않을 수 있는 어떤 하늘답지 못한 시기나 악의로부터도 어찌나 멀었던지, 정부의 일을 운영해 나가는 데 힘닿는 대로 모든 도움을 거인들에게 주겠다고 약속했다. 이에 거인들은 그런 값진 조언과 우호적인 도움에 얼마나 깊이 은혜를 입게 될지 모른다고 선언했다. 이 모든 것이 극도로 흐뭇했다. 그러나 그럼에도 보통 사람들은 여느 싸움이 옛날 그대로의 방식으로 계속되리라 여기는 듯했다. 훌륭한 연설을 하고 있을 때 원수를 사랑하기는 쉽다. 그러나 삶의 실제 하루하루의 일 속에서 그러기는 그토록 어렵다.\n\n그러나 거인들에게는 이런 남다른 장점이 있었고 언제나 있어 왔다. 곧 그들은 신들의 발자취를 따라가기를 도도하게 마다하는 일이 결코 없다는 것이다. 신들이 괴롭게 함께 숙의하여 무슨 솜씨 좋은 계획을 다듬어 놓았으면, 거인들은 언제라도 그것을 제 것으로 받아들이려 하는데, 그 어린것을 수양자식처럼 다루지 않고 오히려 칭찬하고 밀어붙여서 사람들이 그것을 의심할 여지 없이 자기네 머리에서 나온 자식으로 여기게 만든다. 그런데 바로 이 무렵에 주교의 수를 늘리는 계획이 크게 검토되고 있었다. 훌륭하고 부지런한 주교는 매우 바람직했고, 어떤 뛰어난 교회 인사들 사이에서는 그런 주교는 아무리 많아도 지나칠 수 없다는 강한 정서가 있었다. 브록 경은 법안을 다 마련해 두고 있었다. 수도 대주교의 헤라클레스 같은 수고를 나눠 질 웨스트민스터 주교를 하나 두고, 북쪽에는 광산업을 기독교화하고 뉴캐슬의 검댕 묻은 무리를 씻어 하얗게 할 주교를 또 하나 두어야 한다는 것이었다. 그 이름은 비벌리 주교라 하기로 했다. 그러나 이에 맞서 거인들은 저들의 야만스러운 힘을 온통 다 쏟아 낼 작정이었다고 알려져 있었다. 그들의 말로는, 더 필요한 것은 부목사와 지구 담당 성직자이지, 마차를 굴리고 다니는 주교가 더 필요한 것은 아니었다. 주교가 마차를 굴리고 다니는 것은 매우 좋은 일이다. 그러나 그런 복이라면 지금의 영국 세계는 넉넉히 가지고 있었다. 그러므로 브록 경과 신들은 자기들의 그 작은 계획을 크게 걱정하고 있었다.\n\n그러나 이제 거인들이 자리에 오르자마자, 주교 법안이 곧바로 추진될 것이라는 사실이 알려졌다. 그 법안이 신답기보다 거인답게 되도록 자잘한 손질이 몇 가지 가해지겠으나, 결과는 아주 똑같을 것이었다. 그러나 우리 손으로 임명한 주교는 매우 좋은 것일 수 있지만 우리 원수의 손으로 임명된 주교는 결코 좋을 것이 없다는 점은 인정해야 한다. 그리고 이 정서가 거인들에게 크게 작용했음은 의심의 여지가 없다. 어쨌든 새 주교 법안은 그들이 정부로서 하는 첫 일이 될 것이었고, 그것을 발의해 통과시키고 새 고위 성직자들을 골라 그 의자에 앉히는 일을 한꺼번에 해치울 것이었다—뇌조가 울기 시작해 거인들뿐 아니라 신들의 소행까지 끝장내 버리기 전에.\n\n이 결정에서 생겨난 여러 사소한 결과 가운데는 다음과 같은 것도 있었다. 그랜틀리 부주교와 부인이 런던으로 돌아와, 전에 머물던 하숙을 다시 잡았다는 것이다. 또한 이 두 번째 체류의 첫 주 동안 여러 차례, 그랜틀리 박사가 재무부 제1위원의 집무실로 들어가는 것이 눈에 띄었다. 이런 일에 무엇 하나 확고한 결정을 슬기롭게 내리려면 그 전에 이름 높은 고교회파 인사들 사이에 많은 의논이 필요했다. 그리고 바체스터 부주교보다 더 이름 높이 선 교회 인사는 몇 되지 않았다. 그러고 나서, 총리가 적어도 웨스트민스터 주교좌만은 처분했다는 소문이 세상에 돌기 시작했다.\n\n지금 이때는 그랜틀리 부인에게 매우 조바심 나는 시절이었다. 부주교 자신의 열망이 어떠했을지는 멈춰 서서 캐묻지 않겠다. 세월과 경험이 그에게 이 땅의 명예가 부질없음을 가르쳐, 바셋주 주임 사제관의 안락한 넉넉함으로 만족하게 만들었을지도 모른다. 그러나 성직자의 아내가 주교직에 반대해야 한다고 못 박는 교회 규율의 이론 따위는 없다. 부주교는 아마 총리에게 사심 없는 도움을 주려고 애쓴 것뿐이었겠지만, 그랜틀리 부인은 높은 자리에 앉아 적어도 프라우디 부인과 대등해지기를 정말로 갈망했다. 자기가 이렇게 애가 타는 것은 아이들을 위한 것이라고, 그녀는 스스로에게 말했다. 아이들이 세상 앞에 좋은 자리를 얻고 저마다 제 몫을 가장 잘 살릴 방편을 갖게 하려는 것이라고. “저 아래 플럼스테드에 갇혀 있으면 아무것도 할 수 없잖아요, 아시겠지만,” 그녀는 런던을 처음 찾았을 때 레이디 러프턴에게 그렇게 말했었다. 그러면서도 플럼스테드의 그 주임 사제관을 결코 부족하거나 하찮은 집이 아니라고 여겼던 때가 그리 오래 지난 것도 아니었다.\n\n그러고 나서 그리젤다가 어머니에게 돌아가야 하는가 하는 문제가 나왔다. 그러나 이 생각에는 레이디 러프턴이 매우 강하게 반대했고, 결국 그 반대가 이겼다. “그 착한 아이가 나와 함께 있어서 아주 행복하다고 나는 정말로 생각해요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그리고 언젠가 그 아이가 나에게 더 가까이 속하게 될 것이라면, 우리가 서로 알고 서로 사랑해 두는 것이 무척 좋은 일이지요.”\n\n사실을 말하면, 레이디 러프턴은 그리젤다를 알고 사랑하려고 애써 노력해 왔으나, 지금까지 자기가 바라는 만큼 다 이루지는 좀처럼 못했다. 그녀가 그리젤다를 사랑한다는 것은 분명했다—판단에서가 아니라 사람의 의지에서 솟아나는 그런 종류의 사랑으로. 그녀는 자기 자신에게도 남들에게도 처음부터 줄곧, 자기는 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 사랑한다고 말해 왔다. 그 젊은 아가씨의 얼굴을 흠모했고, 몸가짐을 마음에 들어 했고, 재산과 집안을 좋게 여겼으며, 다소 성급한 방식으로 그녀를 며느리로 골라 두었다. 그러므로 그녀는 그리젤다를 사랑했다. 그러나 자기가 이 젊은 벗을 아직 알고 있는지는 레이디 러프턴에게 전혀 분명하지 않았다. 그 혼사는 자기가 세운 계획이었으므로 그녀는 여전히 따뜻하게 그것을 붙들고 있었으나, 그 착한 아이가 자기 자신에게 며느리로서 꿈꿔 온 모든 것이 되어 줄지에 대해 얼마간 의심이 들기 시작했다.\n\n“하지만 레이디 러프턴,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “우리가 그 아이의 마음을 너무 심한 시험에 걸어 두는 것일 수도 있지 않을까요? 만약 그 아이가 그분을 마음에 두게 되었는데, 그러고서—”\n\n“아! 그렇게 되었다면 나는 그 결과를 조금도 두려워하지 않아요. 그 아이가 루도빅에게 사랑 같은 것을 조금이라도 내보인다면, 루도빅은 당장 그 발밑에 엎드릴 거예요. 그애는 충동적이지만 그 아이는 그렇지 않지요.”\n\n“바로 그래요, 레이디 러프턴. 충동적으로 그 아이의 애정을 구하는 것이 그분의 특권이고, 아무런 감정도 드러내지 않은 채 제 사랑을 구해 받는 것이 그 아이의 특권이지요. 요즘 젊은 아가씨들의 잘못은 아마 너무 충동적이라는 데 있을 거예요. 자기 것이 아닌 특권을 제 것으로 삼으려 들고, 그래서 정작 제 것인 특권을 잃지요.”\n\n“참 옳은 말이에요! 나도 아주 같은 생각이에요. 아마 바로 그 느낌 때문에 내가 그리젤다를 그토록 높이 보게 되었겠지요. 하지만 그렇더라도—” 하지만 그렇더라도, 젊은 아가씨가 신사의 목구멍으로 뛰어들거나 그 얼굴에 자기를 내던질 필요는 없다 해도, 자기가 살과 피로 되어 있다는 표시쯤은 좀 내보일 수 있는 것이다. 더구나 아버지와 어머니와 그에게 딸린 모든 사람이 그 사랑의 길을 매끄럽게 놓아 주려고 그토록 애쓰고 있을 때는 말이다. 그것이 레이디 러프턴의 마음속을 지나간 생각이었다. 그러나 그녀는 그것을 다 말하지는 않았다. 그저 눈빛으로 그렇게 말했을 뿐이다.\n\n“그 아이가 허락받지 않은 열정에 자기를 내주는 일은 결코 없을 거예요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다.\n\n“물론 그러지 않겠지요,” 레이디 러프턴이 선선히 동의하며 말했다. 어쩌면 마음속으로는, 그리젤다가 허락받았든 허락받지 않았든 어떤 열정에도 결코 자기를 내주지 않을까 봐 두려워하면서.\n\n“러프턴 경이 요즘 그 아이를 자주 보는지 나는 모르겠어요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 아마 나리의 한가한 시간에 관해 레이디 러프턴이 한 그 약속을 생각한 것이었다.\n\n“요 근래에는, 이런 변동들 사이에서, 아시겠지만, 다들 몹시 바빴어요. 루도빅은 줄곧 상원에 붙어 있었고, 또 요즘은 사내들이 클럽에 나가 있는 것이 그렇게 필요하다고들 하니까요.”\n\n“네, 네, 물론이지요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 지금의 위기가 지닌 중대함을 조금이라도 가볍게 볼 마음이 전혀 없었고, 지금 여왕의 고문들 가슴을 채우고 있는 것과 같은 일들이 이루어져야 할 때 사내들이 한데 모이는 것을 이상하게 여길 마음도 없었다. 그러나 마침내 두 어머니는 서로를 완전히 이해했다. 그리젤다는 여전히 레이디 러프턴과 함께 지내기로 했다. 그리고 마님의 아들이 청혼이라는 제 특권을 행사하도록 어떻게든 끌어낼 수만 있다면 그를 받아들이기로 했다. 그러나 그동안, 이것이 의심스러워 보였으므로, 그리젤다가 제 활에 걸어 두었을지도 모르는 다른 시위를 되도록 써먹을 특권만은 막지 않기로 했다.\n\n“그런데 어머니,” 어머니와 딸이 아무도 지켜보지 않는 사이에 이야기를 나누던 한때에 그리젤다가 말했다. “정말로 아버지를 주교로 만든다는 게 사실이에요?”\n\n“아직은 아무것도 알 수 없단다, 얘. 세상 사람들이 그 이야기를 하고 있지. 네 아버지가 드 테리어 경과 자주 만나고 있어.”\n\n“그분이 총리 아니에요?”\n\n“그렇지. 다행히도 그렇단다.”\n\n“총리는 마음에 드는 사람이면 누구나 주교로 만들 수 있는 줄 알았어요. 성직자라면 누구나요.”\n\n“하지만 빈 주교좌가 없어,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그럼 아무 가망이 없네요,” 그리젤다가 몹시 시무룩한 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n“주교를 둘 더 만들려고 의회 법률을 두려고 한단다. 적어도 사람들이 그렇게 이야기하고 있어. 그리고 만약 그렇게 되면—”\n\n“아버지가 웨스트민스터 주교가 되겠지요? 그러면 우리는 런던에서 살게 되나요?”\n\n“하지만 그 이야기는 하고 다니면 안 된다, 얘.”\n\n“네, 안 할게요. 그런데 어머니, 웨스트민스터 주교는 바체스터 주교보다 높겠지요? 저 프라우디 자매를 무시해 줄 수 있으면 정말 좋겠어요.” 그러니 그리젤다 그랜틀리조차 활기를 띨 수 있는 일이 있었음을 알 수 있을 것이다. 집안의 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로 그녀도 교회에 헌신적이었다.\n\n그날 오후 늦게 부주교는 마운트 스트리트로 저녁을 먹으러 돌아왔다. 하루를 온통 재무부 집무실과 성직자 회의와 자기 클럽 사이에서 보낸 뒤였다. 그리고 집에 들어서자 곧, 그가 좋은 소식을 안고 온 것이 아니라는 것이 아내에게 뚜렷해졌다.\n\n“거의 믿을 수가 없소,” 그가 응접실 난롯불에 등을 대고 서서 말했다.\n\n“무엇이 믿을 수 없어요?” 남편의 조바심을 온전히 함께 나누며 아내가 말했다.\n\n“사실로 알아낸 것이 아니라면, 브록 경이 한 일이라 해도 나는 믿지 않았을 것이오,” 부주교가 말했다.\n\n“무엇을 알아냈다는 거예요?” 애가 타는 아내가 말했다.\n\n“결국 그들이 그 법안에 반대할 작정이오.”\n\n“그럴 수가!” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 그렇게 하려 하오.”\n\n“새 주교 둘을 두는 그 법안 말이에요, 부주교님? 자기들 법안에 반대한다니!”\n\n“그렇소—자기들 법안에 반대한다는 것이오. 거의 믿을 수가 없지만, 사실이 그렇소. 우리에게 몇 가지 변경이 강요되었소. 그들이 잊고 있던 자잘한 것들, 아주 사소한 사항들이오. 그런데 이제 그들이 말하기를, 이 서푼짜리 머리카락 쪼개기 같은 점들 때문에 우리에 맞서 표결에 들어갈 수밖에 없다는 것이오. 그것도 브록 경 자신이 하는 짓이오. 정부에 대한 당파적 반대를 삼가겠다고 그가 그토록 말해 놓고서.”\n\n“그 사람에게는 너무 나쁘거나 너무 거짓된 일이란 없다고 나는 믿어요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다.\n\n“게다가 자기들이 권력을 쥐고 있을 때, 지금의 정부가 종교의 대의에 반대한다고 그토록 말해 놓고서! 이제 그들은, 드 테리어 경이 몇 주 전에는 그 법안에 반대할 훌륭한 이유를 그렇게 많이 갖고 있었으니 그 일에 그다지 애를 태울 리 없다고 떠들고 있소. 그런 자리에 있는 사람들에게 그런 이중 거래가 있다는 것이 끔찍하지 않소?”\n\n“속이 메스꺼워요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다.\n\n그러고 나서 두 사람 사이에 침묵이 흘렀다. 저마다 자기들에게 가해진 해코지를 생각한 것이다.\n\n“하지만 부주교님—”\n\n“뭐요?”\n\n“그 작은 점들을 양보해서 그들이 부끄러워 따라오게 만들 수는 없어요?”\n\n“그들을 부끄럽게 할 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없소.”\n\n“하지만 해 보는 게 좋지 않을까요?”\n\n판이 그토록 좋고 걸린 것이 그토록 중대했으므로, 그랜틀리 부인은 끝까지 걸어 볼 만한 값이 있다고 느꼈다.\n\n“소용없소.”\n\n“하지만 저라면 반드시 드 테리어 경에게 그것을 제안하겠어요. 나라가 그를 따라갈 거라고 확신해요. 적어도 교회는 그럴 거예요.”\n\n“불가능하오,” 부주교가 말했다. “사실을 말하면, 그 생각이 내게도 떠올랐소. 그러나 거기 있는 몇 사람은 그게 통하지 않을 거라고 여기는 듯했소.”\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 소파에 얼마 동안 앉아, 그토록 끔찍한 추락에서 아직 빠져나갈 길이 없지는 않을까 마음속으로 여전히 곰곰 생각했다.\n\n“하지만 부주교님—”\n\n“위층에 올라가 옷을 갈아입겠소,” 그가 낙심해서 말했다.\n\n“하지만 부주교님, 지금의 내각이 그런 주제라면 분명 다수를 얻을 수 있잖아요. 이제는 다수를 확실히 쥐고 있는 줄 알았어요.”\n\n“아니오. 확실하지 않소.”\n\n“하지만 적어도 가망은 그들에게 유리하지요? 그들이 제 할 일을 하고, 자기네 의원들을 한데 붙들어 두려고 힘써 주기를 정말 바라요.”\n\n그러고 나서 부주교는 진실을 온통 털어놓았다.\n\n“드 테리어 경은 지금 형편으로는 이번 회기에 그 문제를 아예 내놓지 않겠다고 하오. 그러니 우리는 플럼스테드로 돌아가는 게 좋겠소.”\n\n그러자 그랜틀리 부인은 더 할 말이 없다고 느꼈고, 사가로서는 그들의 고통 위에 베일을 드리우는 것이 마땅할 것이다.\n\n# 제24장. 「마그나 에스트 베리타스」\n\n독자는 이미 알고 있다. 겨울이 시작될 무렵 사워비 씨는 잃어버린 재산을 되찾고 세상에서 제자리를 바로 세울 계략을 품고 있었으니, 저 부유한 상속녀 던스터블 양과 혼인하는 것이었다. 이 이야기를 여기까지 나와 함께 따라온 분들의 평판 속에서 내 벗 사워비가 지금 그리 높이 서 있지는 못하지 않을까 나는 염려한다. 그는 낭비꾼이자 도박꾼으로, 그 낭비와 도박에 있어 정직하다고는 도저히 할 수 없는 사람으로 그려져 왔다. 그러나 그럼에도 사워비 씨보다 못한 사내들은 있으며, 설령 그가 던스터블 양에게 뜻을 이룬다 해도 그 숙녀가 끊임없이 제 발밑에 몸을 내던지는 구혼자들 가운데 어느 모로 보나 가장 못한 자를 골랐다고는 나는 말할 준비가 되어 있지 않다. 이 사내가 늘 무모해 보였고 또 실제로 철저히 무모했다 해도, 그 가슴 안에는 여전히 더 나은 것을 바라는 마음이 있었고, 그 머릿속에는 자기가 여태 정직한 영국 신사의 길을 놓쳐 왔다는 깨달음이 있었다. 그는 제 주(州)의 의원이라는 자기 지위를 자랑스러워했으니, 여태 그 자리를 빛낼 만한 일은 거의 하지 않았음에도 그러했다. 그는 콜디코츠의 제 영지를 자랑스러워했으니, 그 소유가 이제 거의 제 손을 떠나 버렸음에도 그러했다. 그는 제 핏줄에 흐르는 오래된 피를 자랑스러워했다. 그리고 그는 저 편하고 느긋하고 흥겨운 태도 또한 자랑스러워했으니, 세상의 판단 속에서 그 태도가 그의 낭비와 못된 행실을 씻어 주는 데 그토록 크게 이바지해 왔던 것이다. 한 번만 더 기회를 얻을 수 있다면, 이제 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 말했다, 만사가 아주 다르게 돌아갈 텐데. 그는 토저네 무리 전부와 딱 끊겠다고 맹세할 것이다. 어음을 굴리는 일도, 돈을 얻으려고 하늘만이 아는 몇백 퍼센트를 물어 주는 일도 그만둘 것이다. 더는 벗들을 뜯어먹지 않을 것이며, 옴니엄 공작의 손아귀에서 제 권리증서를 되찾아 올 것이다. 한 번만 더 기회를 얻을 수 있다면!\n\n던스터블 양의 재산이면 이 모든 일을, 아니 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 일을 해낼 수 있었다. 게다가 던스터블 양은 그가 정말로 마음에 들어 하는 여인이었다. 그녀는 부드럽지도, 여자답지도, 예쁘지도 않았고, 그리 젊지도 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 영리하고 침착했으며 어느 계층에 놓아도 밀리지 않을 사람이었다. 그리고 나이로 말하자면 사워비 씨 자신도 그리 젊지 않았다. 그런 혼사를 이룬다면 부끄러워할 까닭이 없었다. 벗들 앞에서 그 이야기를 하면서도 그들이 얼굴을 찌푸릴까 두려워할 일이 없고, 제 집으로 그들을 부르면서도 식탁의 상석에 앉은 사람이 자기를 망신시키지는 않으리라 온전히 믿을 수 있었다. 그리고 계략이 점점 또렷해질수록 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 다짐했다, 만일 뜻을 이루게 되면 그녀를 잘 대하고 그 돈을 빼앗지 않겠다고 — 도무지 어쩔 수 없는 만큼을 넘어서는.\n\n그는 콜디코츠에서 제 운수를 그녀의 발밑에 내려놓을 작정이었다. 그런데 그 숙녀가 몸을 사렸다. 그러면 개서럼 성에서 일을 치르기로 했는데, 그가 날을 잡아 둔 바로 그때 그 숙녀는 개서럼 성에서 달아나 버렸다. 그 뒤로는 런던에서 이런 일 저런 일이 잇달아 이 일을 미루게 만들었고, 이제 마침내 그는 어떤 결과가 나오든 제 운명을 알아내고 말겠다고 마음을 굳혔다. 일이 빨리 자리 잡히도록 손을 쓰지 못한다면, 아예 콜디코츠의 사워비 씨로서 그 숙녀 앞에 나설 수 없게 되는 날이 올 수도 있었다. 공작이 일이 정리되기를 반긴다는 소식이 포더길 씨를 통해 그에게 들어와 있었다. 그리고 사워비 씨는 그 전갈의 뜻을 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n사워비 씨가 이 싸움을 아무 동맹자의 도움도 없이 홀로 치르고 있었던 것은 아니다. 아니, 어떤 싸움에서도 그가 이번에 둔 것보다 더 믿음직한 동맹자를 둔 사내는 없었다. 그리고 온 생애에 걸쳐 좋을 때나 나쁠 때나 그에게 붙어 있던 단 하나의 충실한 동무인 이 동맹자가, 던스터블 양도 여자이니 혼인을 시킬 수 있겠다는 생각을 그의 머리에 처음 넣어 준 사람이었다.\n\n“궁한 모험가 백 명이 그 일을 해 보고 벌써 다 실패했어.” 그 계획이 처음 제 앞에 놓였을 때 사워비 씨는 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“하지만 그이도 언젠가는 누군가와 혼인하지 않겠어요? 그게 다른 남자면 되고 오라버니면 안 될 까닭이 무엇이죠?” 누이동생이 그렇게 대답했다. 내가 말한 그 동맹자가 바로 해럴드 스미스 부인이었기 때문이다.\n\n해럴드 스미스 부인에게 어떤 허물이 있었든, 그녀는 이 한 가지 미덕만은 자랑할 수 있었다 — 그녀는 오라비를 사랑했다. 아마도 그녀가 사랑한 사람은 그 하나뿐이었을 것이다. 자식은 없었다. 그리고 남편으로 말하자면, 그를 사랑한다는 생각은 애초에 그녀에게 떠오른 적이 없었다. 그녀는 지위를 얻으려고 그와 혼인했고, 영리한 여자였으므로, 소화가 잘 되고 제 성미를 다스릴 줄 아는 덕에, 어울리지 않는 혼인 뒤에 흔히 따라오는 그런 불행을 크게 겪지 않고 세상을 헤쳐 왔다. 집 안에서는 윗자리를 지켜 냈으나, 편하고 너그러운 태도로 그렇게 했으므로 그녀의 다스림은 견딜 만한 것이었다. 그리고 집 밖에서는 남편의 정치적 지위를 도와 주었으니, 그 흠들을 두고 누구보다도 날카롭게 웃는 사람이 그녀였음에도 그러했다. 그러나 그녀의 마음의 주인은 오라비였다. 그리고 그가 겪은 모든 곤경, 모든 낭비, 모든 무모함 속에서 그녀는 언제나 그를 도울 마음이 있었다. 그럴 셈으로 그녀는 던스터블 양과 가까워지기를 구했고, 지난 한 해 동안 그 숙녀의 변덕이라면 무엇이든 다 맞춰 주었다. 아니, 그보다도 그녀는 던스터블 양의 마음을 얻는 길이 변덕을 맞춰 주는 데 있지 않고, 재미가 한 줌 섞인 거리낌 없고 편한 왕래에, 그리고 적어도 정직해 보이는 겉모양에 있다는 것을 알아챌 만한 재치가 있었다. 해럴드 스미스 부인은 아마도 타고난 성품이 그리 정직하지는 않았다. 그러나 요 근래에는 던스터블 양을 위하여 정직이라는 신조를 세워 두고 있었으니 — 그것이 아주 헛되지는 않았던 것이, 던스터블 양과 해럴드 스미스 부인은 참으로 아주 가까운 사이였다.\n\n“내가 이 일을 하려면 더는 기다릴 수 없네.” 신들이 마지막으로 무너져 내린 지 하루나 이틀 뒤에 사워비 씨가 누이동생에게 말했다. 그런 때에 그녀가 그런 문제에 마음을 쏟을 수 있었다는 사실만 보아도 오라비를 향한 누이의 애정이 어떠했는지는 짐작할 만하다. 그러나 사실 내각 각료인 남편의 지위는, 주(州)의 지주인 오라비의 지위에 비하면 그녀에게 아무것도 아니었다.\n\n“때야 어느 때나 마찬가지지요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“당장 청혼하라고 나에게 권하는 말이로군.”\n\n“그럼요. 하지만 냇, 이건 쉬운 일이 아니라는 걸 잊지 마세요. 무릎을 꿇고 사랑한다고 맹세하는 식으로는 안 될 거예요.”\n\n“내가 그 일을 한다면 무릎은 확실히 꿇지 않고 할 걸세 — 그건 자네가 믿어도 좋아, 해리엇.”\n\n“그렇지요. 사랑한다고 맹세하지도 말고요. 던스터블 양에게 뜻을 이룰 길은 하나뿐이에요 — 그이한테 진실을 말해야 해요.”\n\n“뭐라고! 내가 기병이며 보병이며 용기병까지 통째로 망했다고 털어놓고, 그러니 나를 이 진창에서 건져 달라고 청하란 말인가?”\n\n“바로 그거예요. 이상하게 들리겠지만 그게 오라버니의 유일한 기회예요.”\n\n“콜디코츠에서 자네가 하던 말과는 아주 다르지 않은가.”\n\n“그렇지요. 하지만 그때 콜디코츠에 있을 때보다 지금은 그이를 훨씬 잘 알아요. 그 뒤로 나는 그 성격의 별난 점들을 살피는 일 말고는 별로 한 일이 없거든요. 그이가 정말로 오라버니를 좋아한다면 — 나는 좋아한다고 봐요 — 다른 죄는 다 용서해 줄 사람이지만 사랑한다고 맹세하는 죄만은 용서하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“사랑 이야기를 한마디도 안 하고 어떻게 청혼을 하는지 나는 도무지 모르겠네.”\n\n“하지만 아무 말도 하면 안 돼요. 단 한마디도요. 오라버니는 좋은 혈통과 높은 지위를 지닌 신사이지만 딱하게도 팔꿈치가 다 해졌다고 말해야 해요.”\n\n“그건 그이가 벌써 알고 있어.”\n\n“물론 알고 있지요. 하지만 그이는 그 말을 오라버니 입에서 곧바로 나오는 것으로 들어야 해요. 그러고 나서 그이와 혼인하여 제 형편을 바로 세우려 한다고 말하세요 — 그 돈을 위해서 그이와 혼인하려 한다고요.”\n\n“그래서야 그이 마음을 얻기는 어렵겠는걸.”\n\n“그래서 안 되면, 내가 아는 어떤 길로도 안 될 거예요. 앞서도 말했지만 이건 쉬운 일이 아니에요. 물론 그이의 행복도 살펴 주겠다는 것을 알아듣게 해야 하지요. 하지만 그것을 오라버니의 목적으로 앞세워서는 안 돼요. 오라버니의 첫째 목적은 그이의 돈이고, 뜻을 이룰 유일한 기회는 진실을 말하는 데 있어요.”\n\n“사내가 이런 처지에 놓이는 일은 좀처럼 없지.” 사워비 씨가 누이동생의 방을 오락가락하며 말했다. “그리고 참말이지, 나는 이 일을 감당할 만한 사람이 아닌 것 같아. 틀림없이 무너져 버릴 걸세. 런던에 그런 이야기를 들고 여자에게 찾아가서 나와 혼인해 달라고 청할 수 있는 사내가 있으리라고는 믿지 않아.”\n\n“못 하겠으면 아예 그만두는 게 좋겠지요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. “하지만 할 수 있다면 — 그런 식으로 끝까지 밀고 나갈 수 있다면 — 내 생각으로는 뜻을 이룰 가망이 나쁘지 않아요. 실은 말이죠,” 누이는 잠시 뒤에 이렇게 덧붙였는데, 그 사이에도 오라비는 계속 방을 오락가락하며 제 처지의 어려움을 곱씹고 있었다. “실은 말이죠, 오라버니 같은 남자들은 여자를 도무지 몰라요. 여자의 강한 데도, 약한 데도 제대로 셈해 주지 않지요. 너무 담대하면서 또 너무 겁이 많아요. 여자를 바보라고 여기고 그렇다고 대놓고 말하면서도, 여자가 착한 일을 할 거라고는 도무지 믿어 주지 못하지요. 그이가 오라버니에게 좋은 일을 해 줄 마음으로 혼인한다고 해서 안 될 것이 무엇이죠? 결국 그이가 잃는 것은 아주 적어요. 영지가 있으니, 그이가 그것을 되찾아 준다면 그것은 오라버니 것인 만큼 그이 것도 되는 거예요.”\n\n“정말이지 그거야 큰 은덕이겠지. 나는 낯이 얇아서 그이에게 그렇게까지는 못 내놓을 것 같은데.”\n\n“그이의 처지는 지금보다 오라버니의 아내가 되는 편이 훨씬 나아요. 오라버니는 너그럽고 성미도 좋으니 그이를 잘 대할 마음일 테고, 무엇보다 그이는 지금 처지에 있을 때보다 콜디코츠의 사워비 부인으로 있을 때 훨씬 더 행복할 거예요.”\n\n“혼인하는 데 마음이 있었다면 내일이라도 귀족의 아내가 될 수 있었겠지.”\n\n“하지만 나는 그이가 귀족의 아내가 되는 데 마음이 있다고 보지 않아요. 궁한 귀족이라면 내가 오라버니에게 권하는 그 방식으로 어쩌면 그이 마음을 얻을 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 궁한 귀족은 그 일을 어떻게 시작해야 하는지를 몰라요. 궁한 귀족들이 해 보았지요 — 여섯 명은 되리라고 나는 의심하지 않아요 — 그리고 다 실패했어요. 그이를 사랑한다고 시늉을 했으니까요. 어려운 일일지 몰라도 오라버니의 유일한 기회는 그이한테 진실을 말하는 거예요.”\n\n“그러면 어디서 하면 되겠나?”\n\n“원한다면 여기서 하세요. 하지만 그이 집이 더 나을 거예요.”\n\n“하지만 나는 거기서 그이를 만날 수가 없어 — 적어도 단둘이는 못 만나지. 그이는 절대 혼자 있는 일이 없다고 나는 알고 있네. 사랑을 걸어오는 자들을 막으려고 늘 여러 사람을 둘러 두고 있단 말이야. 참말이지 해리엇, 나는 이 일을 그만둘까 하네. 자네가 권하는 그런 선언을 내가 그이에게 한다는 것은 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이야.”\n\n“마음이 약해서야, 냇 — 그다음 구절은 알잖아요.”\n\n“하지만 그 시인은 자네가 권하는 그런 구애를 두고 말한 적이 없지. 내 빚 목록부터 꺼내 놓고, 그이가 나를 의심하면 포더길과 집행리들과 토저네 사람들을 증인으로 대라는 말이겠군.”\n\n“그 점을 두고는 오라버니를 의심하지 않을 거예요. 조금도 놀라지도 않을 거고요.”\n\n그러고는 다시 말이 끊어졌고, 그 사이에도 사워비 씨는 여전히 방을 오락가락하며 그토록 위태로운 일에 자기가 조금이라도 뜻을 이룰 가망이 있을까 없을까를 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n“이러면 어떻겠나, 해리엇.” 마침내 그가 말했다. “자네가 나를 위해 그 일을 해 주었으면 하네.”\n\n“그러죠.” 그녀가 말했다. “정말 그러기를 바라신다면 내가 한번 해 보겠어요.”\n\n“이것만은 분명해. 나는 결코 그 일을 내 입으로 못 하겠어. 그이의 돈을 위해 혼인하고 싶다고 그렇게 낱말을 다 갖춰 말할 용기가 나에게는 정말로 없네.”\n\n“좋아요, 냇, 내가 해 보지요. 어쨌든 나는 그이가 두렵지 않아요. 그이와 나는 아주 좋은 벗이고, 사실을 말하면 내가 아는 어느 여자보다 그이가 좋아요. 하지만 오라버니를 위해서가 아니었다면 나는 그이와 가까워지지도 않았을 거예요.”\n\n“그런데 이제는 역시 나 때문에 그이와 다투게 되겠군?”\n\n“전혀 그렇지 않아요. 그이가 내 제안을 받아들이든 안 받아들이든 우리는 계속 벗으로 지낼 거예요. 그이가 나를 위해 죽어 줄 거라고는 생각하지 않아요 — 나도 그이를 위해 죽지 않을 거고요. 하지만 세상 돌아가는 이치로 보면 우리는 서로 잘 맞아요. 이만한 자잘한 일로 우리 우애가 깨지지는 않아요.”\n\n그렇게 일이 정해졌다. 이튿날 해럴드 스미스 부인은 이 일 전부를 던스터블 양에게 설명할 틈을 찾아내어, 그 숙녀에게 재산을 — 몇천 파운드인지 헤아릴 수도 없는 그 재산을 — 서바셋주의 파산한 의원과 나누어 가지라고 청할 것이었으며, 그 의원은 그 보답으로 그녀에게 제 몸과 제 빚을 내어 줄 것이었다.\n\n해럴드 스미스 부인이 자기와 던스터블 양이 서로 잘 맞는다고 한 말은 진실 그대로였다. 그리고 그녀가 두 사람의 우정을 그려 낸 것도 어긋난 데가 없었다. 두 사람은 한쪽을 위해 다른 쪽이 죽을 준비가 되어 있지 않았다. 서로에게 사랑이니 애정이니 하는 말을 한 적도 없었다. 만날 때나 헤어질 때나 입을 맞추거나 울거나 긴말을 늘어놓는 일이 없었다. 한쪽이 다른 쪽에게 고마워해야 할 큰 은덕도 없었고, 한쪽이 용서해 주어야 할 끔찍한 해코지도 없었다. 그러나 두 사람은 서로 잘 맞았다. 그리고 내가 보기에는 이것이 이 세상에서 우리가 누리는 가장 즐거운 왕래 대부분의 비밀이다.\n\n그런데 두 사람이 서로 잘 맞는다는 것은 거의 애석한 일이었으니, 던스터블 양이 스스로만 알았다면 둘 중 훨씬 나은 사람이었기 때문이다. 그녀가 해럴드 스미스 부인과 더불어 자기로서는 아주 만족스러운 사이로 지낼 수 있게 되었다는 것은 거의 한탄할 만한 일이었다. 해럴드 스미스 부인은 세속적이고, 오라비를 뺀 온 세상에 대하여 마음이 없었으며, 앞에서 비친 대로 거의 정직하지 못한 사람이었다. 던스터블 양은 세속적이지 않았다 — 다만 지금 같은 살림살이가 그녀를 그렇게 만들 수도 있기는 했다. 그녀는 정이 두터웠고, 진실을 아꼈으며, 주위 사람들이 그렇게 하도록 놓아만 준다면 정직 쪽으로 기울 사람이었다. 그러나 그녀는 편안함과 익살을 좋아했고, 더러는 걸쭉하다고 해도 좋을 만한 재담을 좋아했으며, 세상의 수작을 비웃는 일을 몹시 사랑했다. 이 모든 성벽을 해럴드 스미스 부인은 다 맞춰 주었다.\n\n이런 사정이었으므로 두 사람은 이제 거의 날마다 함께 있었다. 해럴드 스미스 부인이 아침나절 이른 시각에 마차를 몰게 하여 던스터블 양의 집으로 가는 것은 아주 굳은 습관이 되어 있었다. 그리고 그 숙녀는 사워비 씨에게는 결코 혼자 있는 모습을 보이지 않았으나, 그 누이동생에게는 늘 혼자 있는 모습으로 발견되었다. 그러고 나면 두 사람은 함께 나가기도 했고, 그날의 기분이나 볼일이 이끄는 대로 따로따로 나가기도 했다. 이 동맹 안에서 둘은 서로에게 편했고, 서로의 발가락을 밟는 일이 없도록 잘 처신했다.\n\n사워비 씨와 해럴드 스미스 부인 사이에 약조가 이루어진 이튿날, 그 부인은 여느 때처럼 던스터블 양을 찾아가서, 그 상속녀가 오로지 자기만의 용도로 두고 있는 작은 방에서 이내 벗과 단둘이 있게 되었다. 특별한 때에는 여러 부류의 사람들이 그 방에 들어오기도 했다. 이따금 교회를 지어야 하는 목사 하나, 장안의 최신 험담 한 조각을 잔뜩 싣고 온 노부인 하나, 머리를 쓴 대가를 제대로 받지 못한 가난한 저술가 하나, 아니면 그 여린 몸에 세상의 무게가 너무 무겁게 얹힌 가난한 가정 교사 하나가 들어왔다. 그러나 어쩌다가라도 연인이 될 수 있는 남자들은 거기까지 길을 뚫지 못했고, 진력나게 하는 여자들도 그러했다. 요 근래에는, 곧 이번 런던 사교철 동안에는, 그 방의 문이 다른 누구보다 해럴드 스미스 부인에게 자주 열렸다.\n\n그리하여 이제 이 모든 친분을 맺어 온 목적을 이루기 위한 시도가 행해질 참이었다. 마차를 타고 그리로 오는 동안 해럴드 스미스 부인 자신도, 어렵고 위태로운 일에 앞서 흔히 찾아오는 그 가슴 내려앉는 느낌이 아주 없지는 않았다. 그녀는 그 자잘한 제안을 하는 데 아무 두려움도 느끼지 않으리라고 선언해 두었었다. 그러나 그녀는 그와 아주 비슷한 무엇을 느꼈다. 그리고 그 작은 방으로 들어설 때에는 이 일이 다 끝나 버렸으면 하고 진심으로 바랐다.\n\n“가엾은 스미스 씨는 오늘 어떠세요?” 던스터블 양이 조문하는 시늉을 하는 낯빛으로 물었고, 벗은 늘 앉는 안락의자에 자리를 잡았다. 신들의 몰락은 아직 사흘도 채 되지 않은 옛일이었으니, 지난번까지 프티백 경이던 이가 그 재난에서 아직 몸을 일으키지 못했다고 보아도 무리가 없었다.\n\n“글쎄요, 오늘 아침에는 좀 나아진 것 같아요. 적어도 달걀을 마주하는 태도로 보아서는 그렇게 판단하겠어요. 하지만 그이가 고기 썰는 칼을 다루는 손놀림은 여전히 마음에 들지 않아요. 그럴 때면 틀림없이 서플하우스 씨를 떠올리고 있는 거예요.”\n\n“가엾은 사람! 서플하우스 씨 말이에요. 결국 그이도 남들처럼 제 밥벌이를 따라가면 안 될 까닭이 무엇이죠? 살고 또 살게 하라, 나는 그렇게 말해요.”\n\n“아, 그런데 그 사람은 죽이고 또 죽게 하라지요. 호라티우스가 하는 말이 그거잖아요. 하지만 나는 이제 그런 이야기가 다 지겨워요. 오늘은 다른 이야기를 하려고 왔어요.”\n\n“나는 서플하우스 씨가 오히려 마음에 들어요.” 던스터블 양이 소리쳤다. “그이는 그 일을 두고 아무 거리낌이 없어요. 해야 할 일이 정해져 있고 섬겨야 할 명분이 정해져 있는데 — 그 명분이란 곧 자기 자신이지요 — 그 일을 하고 그 명분을 섬기려고 하느님이 제 손에 놓아 주신 무기를 쓰는 거예요.”\n\n“그건 들짐승이 하는 짓이지요.”\n\n“그러면 들짐승보다 정직한 사내를 어디서 찾겠어요? 호랑이는 배가 고파서, 당신을 먹고 싶어서 당신을 찢어 놓아요. 서플하우스 씨가 하는 짓이 그거예요. 그런데 우리 가운데는 배고픔이라는 핑계조차 없이 서로를 찢어 놓는 사람이 그토록 많아요. 부수는 재미 그것만으로 넉넉한 이유가 되지요.”\n\n“그런데 이봐요, 오늘 내가 당신을 찾아온 일은 결코 부수는 일이 아니에요. 들어 보면 당신도 인정할 거예요. 오히려 아주 온전히 구원하는 일이지요. 나는 당신에게 구애를 하러 왔어요.”\n\n“그러면 그 구원은 내 몫이 아닌 것 같군요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n던스터블 양이 이 방문의 뜻 전부를 곧바로 알아차렸다는 것, 그리고 그녀가 그리 크게 놀라지도 않았다는 것이 해럴드 스미스 부인에게는 아주 분명해졌다. 그 상속녀의 목소리로 보나, 그 얼굴에 이내 내려앉은 굳은 표정으로 보나, 아주 선선히 응낙해 줄 낌새는 아니었다. 하지만 큰 목적이란 큰 힘을 들여야만 얻어지는 것이다.\n\n“그건 그렇게 될 수도 있지요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. “당신에게도, 그리고 또 한 사람에게도, 나는 그러기를 바라요. 그러나 어쨌든 당신의 노여움을 사지는 않았으면 하는데요?”\n\n“아이고, 천만에요. 요즘은 그런 일로 노여워지는 법이 없어요.”\n\n“하기야 익숙해지셨겠지요.”\n\n“뱀장어처럼요, 이봐요. 세상에 조금도 마음에 걸리지 않아요 — 다만 이따금, 아시겠지만, 좀 지루하기는 하지요.”\n\n“그건 안 되게 애써 보겠어요. 그러니 대뜸 얼음을 깨는 편이 낫겠네요. 나다니엘의 형편은 당신도 어지간히 알고 있으니 그이가 그리 넉넉한 사람이 아니라는 것쯤은 아시겠지요.”\n\n“당신이 그렇게 물으시니, 나는 그이가 몹시 가난한 사람이라고 믿는다고 말해도 해로울 것이 없겠군요.”\n\n“세상에 조금도 해로울 것이 없어요. 오히려 그 반대지요. 이 일이 어떻게 되든, 내 소원은 모든 쪽에서 진실이 한 치도 어김없이 말해지는 거예요. 진실을, 진실 전부를, 진실 말고는 아무것도.”\n\n“「마그나 에스트 베리타스」.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “바체스터 주교가 콜디코츠에서 그만큼의 라틴어를 나에게 가르쳐 주었지요. 몇 마디 더 붙이기는 했는데, 긴 낱말이 하나 있어서 그건 잊어버렸어요.”\n\n“주교님 말씀이 지당하셨지요, 이봐요. 하지만 당신이 라틴어로 가시면 나는 길을 잃어요. 방금 말한 대로 내 오라비의 금전 형편은 아주 나쁜 상태예요. 그이에게는 아름다운 제 소유의 영지가 있는데, 몇 세기 동안 집안에 있었는지 나는 말할 수도 없어요 — 노르만 정복보다 훨씬 전이라는 것은 알지요.”\n\n“그러면 그때 우리 조상은 무엇을 하고 있었을까요?”\n\n“우리 조상이 무엇이었느냐는 우리 누구에게도 그리 대수롭지 않아요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 훈계하듯 고개를 저으며 말했다. “하지만 오래된 재산이 허물어지는 것을 보는 것은 슬픈 일이지요.”\n\n“그렇지요, 참으로. 오래된 것이든 새것이든 우리 가운데 제 재산이 허물어지는 것을 보고 싶은 사람은 없어요. 나도 벌써 그런 느낌이 조금 있어요. 내 것은 바로 얼마 전에 약방에서 만들어진 것이지만요.”\n\n“내가 당신 재산을 허무는 데 손을 보태는 일은 하느님이 막아 주시기를.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. “당신이 십 파운드 지폐 한 장을 잃는 빌미가 된다면 나는 미안할 거예요.”\n\n“「마그나 에스트 베리타스」, 그 다정한 주교님 말씀대로요.” 던스터블 양이 소리쳤다. “방금 우리가 약조한 대로, 진실을, 진실 전부를, 진실 말고는 아무것도, 그렇게 갑시다.”\n\n해럴드 스미스 부인은 제 앞에 놓인 일이 어렵다는 것을 정말로 깨닫기 시작했다. 사무적인 일이 걸릴 때면 던스터블 양에게는 도무지 무슨 자국을 낼 수 없을 것 같은 단단한 데가 있었다. 그녀가 사워비 씨의 손길을 거절하겠다는 뜻을 내보인 것은 아니었다. 그러나 눈에 먼지가 뿌려지는 것만은 안 되겠다고 야멸치게 다잡고 있었다! 해럴드 스미스 부인은 스스로 수작이라 부르는 것을 피하겠다고 마음을 다지고 시작했다. 그런데 이런 종류의 수작이 그녀의 여느 말솜씨에서 그토록 큰 몫을 차지하게 되어 버린 터라, 그것을 내버리기가 몹시 힘들다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n“나도 그러기를 바라요.” 그녀가 말했다. “물론 내 으뜸 목적은 오라비의 행복을 굳혀 주는 거예요.”\n\n“가엾은 해럴드 스미스 씨한테는 참 매정한 말이군요.”\n\n“자, 자, 자 — 내 말뜻을 알잖아요.”\n\n“그래요, 당신 말뜻을 나도 알 것 같아요. 당신 오라비는 좋은 집안의 신사이지만 재산이 없지요.”\n\n“그렇게까지 나쁘지는 않아요.”\n\n“그럼 형편이 궁색해진 것이라 하든, 당신이 원하는 어떤 말로 하든 좋아요. 그에 비하여 나는 집안은 없으나 재물은 넉넉한 숙녀지요. 당신 생각으로는 우리 둘을 붙여 놓고 혼사를 이루면 아주 좋은 일이 될 거라는 거고요 — 누구에게 좋은 일일까요?” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n“그래요, 바로 그거예요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“우리 둘 중 누구에게 말이죠? 이제 주교님과 그 아담한 라틴어 한 조각을 떠올려 보세요.”\n\n“그러면 나다니엘에게요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 담대하게 말했다. “그이에게 아주 좋은 일이 될 거예요.” 그렇게 말하면서 그녀의 얼굴에 옅은 웃음이 스쳤다. “자, 이건 정직하지요. 그렇지 않다면 무슨 흉계가 든 것일 테고요.”\n\n“그래요, 그만하면 넉넉히 정직해요. 그러면 그이가 이 말을 나에게 하라고 당신을 여기 보낸 거예요?”\n\n“그 일도 시켰고, 다른 것도 하나 있어요.”\n\n“그러면 그 다른 것을 들어 봅시다. 정말로 중요한 대목은 이미 다 말해졌다고 나는 의심하지 않아요.”\n\n“아니에요, 천만에요, 전부는 결코 아니에요. 하지만 이봐요, 당신이 정직을 그렇게 쫓아다니며 사람을 몰아세우니, 있는 사실을 있는 그대로 말할 수가 없잖아요. 당신은 사람을 그토록 앙상하게, 알몸으로 말하게 만들어요.”\n\n“아, 알몸인 것은 무엇이든 상스럽다고 여기시는군요. 진실까지도요.”\n\n“무슨 옷가지든 걸치고 돌아다닐 때가 더 점잖아 보이고 세상일에도 더 잘 맞는다고 나는 생각해요. 요즘 우리는 듣고 말하는 모든 것에 거짓말이라는 누룩이 섞인 데 하도 익숙해져서, 있는 그대로의 진실만큼 우리를 속이기 쉬운 것이 없어요. 가게 주인이 제 물건이 그저 그렇다고 말한다면 나는 물론 그 물건이 파딩 한 닢 값도 안 된다고 여길 거예요. 하지만 그런 이야기는 다 내 가엾은 오라비와는 아무 상관이 없지요. 그런데, 내가 무슨 말을 하고 있었죠?”\n\n“그이가 나를 얼마나 잘 대해 줄지 말하려는 참이었지요, 틀림없이.”\n\n“그런 쪽이었어요.”\n\n“그이가 나를 때리지는 않을 거라고, 내가 돈을 그이 손이 닿지 않게 묶어 두는 데 성공한다 해도 내 돈을 다 써 버리지는 않을 거라고, 내 아버지가 약제사였다는 이유로 나를 업신여기며 깔보지는 않을 거라고 말이지요! 그게 하려던 말이 아니었나요?”\n\n“나는 당신이 던스터블 양으로 있는 것보다 콜디코츠의 사워비 부인으로 있는 편이 더 행복할지 모른다는 말을 하려던 참이었어요 —”\n\n“레바논 산의 던스터블 양이지요. 그런데 사워비 씨가 보낸 전갈이 그것뿐인가요? 사랑이라든가 그런 종류의 무엇에 대한 말은 없고요? 알다시피 나는 그렇게 큰 뜀박질을 하기 전에 그이의 심정이 어떤지를 알아 두고 싶은데요.”\n\n“그이가 그 나이의 남자가 여태 가져 본 만큼 참된 마음을 당신에게 두고 있다고 나는 정말로 믿어요 —”\n\n“내 나이쯤 되는 여자에게요. 그야 참으로 애틋한 말투는 아니지요. 하지만 당신이 주교님의 금언을 잊지 않은 것은 반가워요.”\n\n“그럼 나더러 무슨 말을 하라는 거예요? 그이가 사랑에 죽어 간다고 말했다면 당신은 내가 당신을 속이려 든다고 했겠지요. 그런데 이제 내가 그렇게 말하지 않으니 그이가 애틋함이 모자란다고 하시고요. 당신은 참 만족시키기 어려운 분이라고 말해야겠네요.”\n\n“어쩌면 그렇겠지요. 게다가 덧붙여 아주 이치에 닿지도 않겠지요. 당신 오라비가 나에게 그렇게 큰 영광을 베풀겠다고 하는데 그런 물음은 아예 하지 말아야 했어요. 나의 남편이 되기를 황송하게도 바라 주시는 남자에게서 사랑까지 기대하다니, 그야 물론 괴이한 일이지요. 어떤 남자가 나를 사랑해야 한다고 여길 무슨 권리가 내게 있겠어요? 나는 부자니까 남편을 하나 얻을 수 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로 넉넉해야 하지요. 나에게 그런 영광을 베풀어 주시겠다는 신사분이 정말로 내 곁에 있기를 좋아할지, 아니면 자기 집안에 내가 있는 것을 마지못해 참아 주기만 할지를 캐물을 무슨 자격이 나 같은 사람에게 있겠어요?”\n\n“이봐요, 던스터블 양 —”\n\n“물론 나는 어떤 신사분이 나를 사랑해야 한다고 기대할 만큼 얼뜨기는 아니에요. 그리고 그런 자리에서 으레 줄줄이 늘어놓는 찬사를 나에게 면해 주신 것을 당신 오라비에게 고마워해야 한다는 느낌이 들어요. 어쨌든 그이는 지루하지가 않아요 — 아니, 그이를 대신한 당신이 그렇다고 해야겠지요. 그이 자신의 시간은 의회의 직무로 어찌나 가득 찼는지 이런 자잘한 일에는 직접 손을 댈 수도 없을 테니까요. 나는 정말로 그이에게 고마움을 느껴요. 그리고 어쩌면 재산 목록을 그이에게 건네주고 그이를 소유주로 앉힐 날을 이른 날짜로 잡아 주는 것 말고는 더 필요한 일이 없을지도 모르지요.”\n\n스미스 부인은 자기가 어지간히 심한 대접을 받고 있다고 느꼈다. 이 던스터블 양은 서로 속을 털어놓는 자리에서, 돈을 노린 구혼자들이 사랑을 꾸며 대며 짓는 낯짝을 그토록 자주 비웃었고 — 자기를 괴롭혀 온 자들을 두고 그토록 매섭게 말했는데, 그들이 자기 돈을 바랐기 때문이 아니라 자기를 바보로 여기는 그 어리석은 판단 때문이라고 했다 — 그러니 스미스 부인이 교섭을 여는 데 택한 방식이 더 나은 마음가짐으로 받아들여지기를 기대할 권리는 있었다. 결국 던스터블 양도 다른 여자들과 마찬가지여서, 사내들이 제 발밑에 무릎 꿇는 것을 좋아하는 것일까, 스미스 부인은 속으로 생각했다. 자기가 오라비에게 잘못 권한 것이고, 그이가 그 일을 옛날 방식대로 해 나가는 편이 더 나았을 것인가? “여자들은 참 다루기 어렵지.” 해럴드 스미스 부인은 제 성(性)을 떠올리며 혼자 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“그이가 직접 여기 오려고 했어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “하지만 그러지 말라고 내가 일렀지요.”\n\n“참 고마운 일이었네요.”\n\n“그이의 뜻이 정말 어떤 것인지를 내가 더 터놓고 더 거침없이 설명할 수 있으리라고 생각했거든요.”\n\n“아! 그 뜻이 떳떳하다는 것은 나도 의심하지 않아요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “그이가 그런 쪽으로 나를 속이려 하지는 않는다고 아주 확신해요.”\n\n웃지 않고 있기란 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이어서 해럴드 스미스 부인은 웃어 버렸다. “참말이지, 당신은 성인도 노엽게 만들 사람이군요.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“당신이 지금 나에게 권하는 그 인연으로 내가 그런 부류와 어울리게 될 것 같지는 않아요. 콜디코츠에는 여느 때 성인이 그리 많지 않다고 나는 알고 있는데요. 늘 그러하듯 내 다정한 주교님과 그 부인은 빼고 말이에요.”\n\n“하지만 이봐요, 나다니엘에게는 무슨 말을 해야 하죠?”\n\n“물론 내가 그이에게 얼마나 고마워하는지를 말해 주세요.”\n\n“한순간만 내 말을 들어 보세요. 이렇게 담대하고 낭만이라고는 없는 방식으로 당신에게 말한 것이 잘못이었나 보군요.”\n\n“전혀 아니에요. 진실을, 진실 전부를, 진실 말고는 아무것도. 그게 우리가 약조한 것이지요. 하지만 어떤 길에서든 첫 시도는 늘 조금 어설프기 쉬운 법이에요.”\n\n“나다니엘을 직접 당신에게 보내겠어요.”\n\n“아니에요, 그러지 마세요. 그이든 나든 왜 괴롭히나요? 나는 당신 오라비가 좋아요. 어떤 면으로는 아주 좋아요. 하지만 이 땅의 어떤 사정으로도 나는 그이와 혼인하도록 이끌리지 않을 거예요. 그이가 내 돈 때문에만 나와 혼인하려 한다는 것이 너무나 뻔해서, 당신조차 다른 이유는 감히 내놓아 보지도 못한 것이 아닌가요?”\n\n“그이가 당신 돈에 아무 마음도 두지 않았다고 말한다면 물론 터무니없는 소리였겠지요.”\n\n“물론 그랬겠지요 — 아주 터무니없는 소리였겠지요. 그이는 좋은 지위를 지닌 가난한 사람이고, 자기가 원하는 것을 내가 가졌으니 나와 혼인하고 싶은 거예요. 하지만 이봐요, 나는 그이가 가진 것을 원하지 않아요. 그러니 그 거래는 공평한 거래가 못 되지요.”\n\n“하지만 그이는 당신을 행복하게 해 주려고 있는 힘을 다할 거예요.”\n\n“나는 그이에게 참 고마워요. 하지만 보시다시피 나는 지금 이대로 아주 행복해요. 내가 얻을 것이 무엇이죠?”\n\n“당신이 좋아한다고 스스로 밝힌 반려 하나요.”\n\n“아! 하지만 당신 오라비 같은 반려라도 너무 많이 있으면 내가 좋아할지는 모르겠어요. 아니에요, 이봐요 — 그건 안 돼요. 내가 딱 한 번으로 다 말하는 거니까 믿어 주세요, 그건 안 돼요.”\n\n“그러면 던스터블 양, 당신은 결코 혼인하지 않겠다는 말인가요?”\n\n“내일이라도 하지요 — 마음에 드는 사람을 만나고 그 사람이 나를 받아 준다면요. 하지만 내 마음에 들 만한 사람은 나를 받아 주지 않을 것 같아요. 우선 첫째로, 내가 누군가와 혼인한다면 그 사람은 돈에 아주 무심한 이여야 해요.”\n\n“그러면 이 세상에서는 그런 사람을 못 찾을 거예요, 이봐요.”\n\n“아주 그럴 법하지요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n이 일을 두고 그 뒤에 더 나온 말들을 여기 다 옮길 필요는 없다. 던스터블 양이 그토록 분명하게 말했음에도 해럴드 스미스 부인은 제 뜻을 그렇게 단번에 접지는 않았다. 그녀는 콜디코츠가 어느 누구에게도 한 푼 빚지지 않게 되었을 때 그 벗이 콜디코츠의 여주인으로 앉게 될 처지가 얼마나 바람직한 것인지를 설명해 보려 했다. 그리고 콜디코츠의 주인이 그 궁색한 형편에서 벗어나 부유한 사람으로 알려지면, 신들이 올림포스로 돌아올 때에 귀족 작위를 받을 만하다는 인정을 받을 가망이 아주 크리라는 것까지 슬쩍 비쳤다. 물론 내각의 각료인 해럴드 스미스 씨가 있는 힘을 다할 것이었다. 그러나 다 아무 쓸모가 없었다. “그건 내 팔자가 아니에요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “그러니 더는 밀지 마세요.”\n\n“하지만 우리가 다투지는 않겠지요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 거의 애틋하게 말했다.\n\n“아, 아니지요. 우리가 왜 다투겠어요?”\n\n“그리고 내 오라비를 시무룩한 얼굴로 대하지도 않겠지요?”\n\n“내가 왜 그이를 시무룩한 얼굴로 대하겠어요? 하지만 스미스 부인, 나는 그이를 시무룩한 얼굴로 대하지 않는 것보다 더한 일도 하겠어요. 나는 당신이 좋고 당신 오라비도 좋아요. 그러니 내가 어떤 알맞은 방도로든 그이의 어려움을 도울 수 있다면 그렇게 말해 달라고 하세요.”\n\n그 뒤 얼마 지나지 않아 해럴드 스미스 부인은 자리를 떴다. 물론 그녀는 오라비가 던스터블 양에게서 그런 금전상의 도움을 받는다는 생각은 할 수도 없다고 아주 강한 태도로 선언했다. 그리고 그녀에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려 말하자면, 그 순간 그녀의 마음이 정말로 그러했다. 그러나 오라비를 만나러 가서 이 만남의 이야기를 그에게 들려주는 동안, 콜디코츠 재산을 두고 어쩌면 던스터블 양이 옴니엄 공작보다 더 나은 채권자일 수도 있겠다는 생각이 그녀에게 떠오르기는 했다.\n\n# 제25장. 충동이 없는 사람\n\n두 주교좌 문제를 두고 거인들이 마지막에 내린 그 결정이 그랜틀리 부주교의 속을 뒤집어 놓았다는 것은 놀랄 일로 여길 수 없다. 그는 정치가였으나 그들과 같은 정치가는 아니었다. 모든 문외한이 그렇듯이 그의 정치적인 눈은 짧은 데까지밖에 보지 못했고, 그의 정치적인 소망도 그만큼 좁았다. 자기 벗들이 관직에 들어앉자, 애초에 원수들이 내놓은 산물이었을 적에는 그토록 해롭다고 여겨졌던 그 주교 법안이—또 다른 프라우디네 같은 자들을 높은 자리와 큰 수입으로 끌어올려 교회에 끔찍한 손해를 입히려고 법으로 만들려던 것이 아니었던가?—그 주교 법안이, 내 말하건대, 벗들의 손에 들어가자 그에게는 거의 나라를 건지는 방편처럼 보이게 되었다. 게다가 이 일에서 거인들의 운수는 얼마나 좋았던가! 그들이 그런 법안을 처음 내놓은 자들이었다면 성공할 낌새조차 없었을 것이다. 그러나 이제—이제 두 주교가 신들의 힘없는 손에서 떨어져 그들의 입안으로 굴러 들어오고 있으니, 그들의 성공은 이미 확보된 것이 아니었던가? 그리하여 그랜틀리 박사는 허리띠를 동여매고 싸움터로 올라갔고, 승리가 너무 쉬울 것이라는 점을 거의 아쉬워할 지경이었다. 그 뒤에 온 실패는 당파 사람으로서의 그의 성미를 몹시 시험했다.\n\n내게는 늘 티탄들을 받드는 자들이야말로 이 점에서 크게 가엾이 여길 만하다는 생각이 든다. 거인들 자신, 곧 실제로 펠리온을 들어 옮기며 오사의 아래쪽 바위에 정강이를 까는 자들은 어떤 식으로든 언제나 올림포스의 회의를 향해 나아가고 있다. 그들의 가장 높은 방책은 하늘에서 빛줄기 하나를 낚아채는 것이다. 그렇지 않다면 무엇 때문에 오사 위에 펠리온을 얹겠는가? 몰래 뻗은 손이 유피테르의 창을 뚫고 들어가 벼락 한둘을, 아니면 그보다 덜 파괴적이나 만든 솜씨는 똑같이 신다운 물건 하나를 뽑아 낼 수도 있으리라는 것 말고 무슨 이유가 있겠는가? 그리고 높은 자리에 있는 거인들의 지혜란 바로 여기에 있다—세속에서 온 내력과 이론과 기호가 있음에도, 신의 솜씨로 만든 물건이 반드시 필요하다는 것을 그들은 볼 줄 안다는 것. 그러나 그들은 자기를 받드는 자들을 결코 함께 데려가지 않는다. 그들의 군대는 통째로 순교자의 군대다. “스무 해를 그들에게 붙어 있었는데, 그들이 나를 어떻게 대했는지 보시오!” 늙은 거인의 종이 늘 늘어놓는 하소연이 그것 아닌가? “나는 평생 우리 당에 참되게 굴었소. 그런데 지금 내가 어디에 있소?” 그는 그렇게 말한다. 어디에 있는가, 정말이지, 나의 벗이여? 둘레를 살펴보다가 그대는 제가 어디에 있는지조차 말할 수 없다는 것을 알기 시작한다. 나는 그것을 이상하게 여기지 않는다. 마른 땅 위에 영원히 가만히 서 있고자 하는 사람만큼, 끝에 가서 그토록 끔찍하게 더러운 늪에 처박히는 이는 없다.\n\n그랜틀리 박사는 속이 뒤집혔다. 그리고 그 자신은 무슨 느낌에서든 너무 참되고 철저한 사람이어서 어느 거인이 틀렸다고 소리 내어 말할 수는 없었으나, 그래도 마음속에는 세상이 제 발밑에서 내려앉고 있다는 슬픈 느낌이 있었다. 그는 여전히 넉넉히 문외한이었으므로, 좋은 명분을 걸고 정면으로 맞붙는 좋은 싸움 한판은 좋은 것이라고 생각했다. 물론 그는 웨스트민스터 주교가 되고 싶었고, 자기 눈에 온당해 보이는 어떤 방편으로든 그 성직 영전을 손에 넣기를 바라고 있었다. 그러면 안 되는가? 그러나 그의 소망은 거기서 끝나지 않았다. 그는 거인들이 주교좌에서든 다른 모든 일에서든 모든 것에서 이기기를 바랐다. 그리고 접전의 낌새가 처음 보이자마자 그들이 물러난다는 것을 그는 이해할 수가 없었다. 드러내 놓고 하는 말에서는 여러 신을 향해 목소리를 높였으나, 마음속 가장 깊은 데서는 포르피리온과 오리온 둘 다에게 넉넉히 쓰디쓴 마음을 품고 있었다.\n\n“박사님, 그건 될 일이 아니었습니다.—이번 회기에는 아니지요. 정말로 될 일이 아니었어요.” 재무부에서 털도 채 나지 않았으나 내막에는 유별나게 통한 젊은 괴물 새끼가 그에게 그렇게 말했다. 그 새끼는 제가 제 당의 온갖 수를 다 안다고 여겼고, 당을 받쳐 주는 순교자의 군대를 꽤 둔중하나 아주 쓸모 있는 늙다리 무리로 보고 있었다. 그랜틀리 박사는 털도 채 나지 않은 그 괴물 새끼와 이 문제를 따져 볼 마음이 없었다. 모든 괴물 가운데 가장 잘 다듬어진 자, 그들 가운데 신을 가장 닮은 그 거인이 그에게 한두 마디를 했고, 그도 그 거인에게 한두 마디를 했다. 포르피리온은 주교 법안이 될 일이 아니라고 그에게 말했고, 그는 그에 답하여 얼굴이 달아오르고 뺨에 피가 몰린 채로, 그 법안이 되지 못할 이유를 하나도 보지 못하겠다고 포르피리온에게 말했다. 정중한 거인은 그 무거운 머리를 흔들며 미소를 지었고, 그러고서 부주교는 그를 떠나, 재무부 청사의 복도를 마지막으로 걸어 나오면서 저도 모르게 신발에서 먼지를 조금 떨어 내었다. 마운트 스트리트의 하숙으로 걸어 돌아가는 동안 그의 머릿속으로 여러 가지 생각이, 그 성질이 아주 나쁘지만은 않은 생각들이 지나갔다. 무엇 때문에 주교좌를 놓고 제 속을 썩여야 하는가? 저 아래 플럼스테드의 주임 사제관에서 지금 이대로도 넉넉히 좋지 않은가? 이 나이에 저를 새 흙에 옮겨 심고, 새 직무를 맡고, 새 사람들 사이에서 사는 것이 오히려 해롭지 않겠는가? 바체스터에서 그는 쓸모가 있고 또 존경도 받고 있지 않은가. 그런데 저 위 웨스트민스터에서는, 다른 사람들이 일을 부리는 데 쓰는 연장쯤으로만 여겨지게 될 수도 있지 않겠는가? 그는 그 유별나게 내막에 통한 젊은 괴물 새끼의 말투가 아무래도 마음에 들지 않았다. 그 새끼는 그를 순교자의 군대에서 나온 이름난 늙다리로 뻔히 보고 있었다. 그는 아내를 데리고 바셋주로 돌아가, 섭리가 그에게 내려 준 좋은 것들로 만족하며 거기서 살겠다고 마음먹었다.\n\n저 높은 데 달린 정치의 포도가 시어졌다고, 비웃기 좋아하는 벗들은 말할 것이다. 그래서? 손이 닿지 않는 데 매달린 포도가 시어지는 것은 좋은 일이 아닌가? 제 손보다 훤히 높은 데 있는 포도를 모두 시다고 볼 줄 아는 이는 지혜롭지 않은가? 신들과 거인들이 다투어 얻으려는 저 여당 석의 포도, 못 먹고 참아야 할 때 그토록 괴롭고 먹고 나면 더욱 괴로운 저 포도는, 내게는 아주 시다. 나는 그것이 소화가 되지 않는 것이며, 그것을 먹은 이들이 레발렌타 아라비카가 고쳐 주겠다고 나서는 온갖 병을 다 겪는다고 확신한다. 지금 부주교의 처지도 그러했다. 그는 저 위 런던에 올라와 웨스트민스터 주교로 앉았을 때 제 양심에 얹혔을 짐을 생각했다. 그런 마음가짐으로 그는 아내에게 걸어 돌아갔다.\n\n아내와 마주 앉은 처음 몇 순간 동안은 아쉬움이 모두 되살아났다. 아니, 그때 그가 이 새로운 시골살이 만족의 교리를 늘어놓았다면 도무지 어울리지 않았을 것이다. 그가 그토록 온전히 믿었고—그토록 온전히 사랑했던 품속의 아내는 담장 높이 매달린 포도를 원하고 있었고, 그 순간에 그녀에게 야심을 내려놓으라고 가르치는 것은 제 힘에 넘치는 일임을 그는 알고 있었다. 그가 그런 쪽으로 무엇을 가르칠 수 있다 해도 그것은 차츰차츰 되어야 했다. 그러나 몇 분이 지나기도 전에 그는 아내의 운명과 자기의 결정을 그녀에게 말했다. “그러니 우리는 플럼스테드로 돌아가는 게 좋겠소,” 그가 말했다. 그리고 그녀는 반대하지 않았다.\n\n“가여운 그리젤다를 생각하면 안타까워요,” 그날 저녁 늦게 두 사람이 다시 마주 앉았을 때 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 그 아이는 레이디 러프턴 곁에 남는 것으로 알았소만?”\n\n“그야 그럴 거예요, 얼마 동안은요. 제 손을 떠나 그 아이를 맡길 사람으로 레이디 러프턴만 한 이는 없지요. 그분은 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 갖춘 분이에요.”\n\n“그렇지. 그러니 그리젤다에 관해서라면, 나는 그 아이를 가엾게 여길 이유가 있다고는 생각지 않소.”\n\n“가엾게 여길 일은 아니겠지요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “하지만 부주교님, 레이디 러프턴께는 물론 그분만의 뜻이 있잖아요.”\n\n“그분만의 뜻이라니?”\n\n“레이디 러프턴이 러프턴 경과 그리젤다를 맺어 주려고 몹시 애쓰고 계시다는 건 이제 비밀이라고 할 것도 없어요. 그리고 그것이 정해진 일이라면 아주 온당한 짝짓기이겠지만—”\n\n“러프턴 경이 그리젤다와 혼인을 한다고!” 부주교가 빠르게 말하며 눈썹을 치켜올렸다. 그때까지 딸이 앞으로 자리를 잡는 문제를 두고 그의 머리를 어지럽힌 생각은 몇 가지 되지 않았다. “그런 일은 꿈에도 생각해 본 적이 없소.”\n\n“하지만 다른 사람들은 꿈꾸는 것보다 더한 일을 해 놓았어요, 부주교님. 혼사 자체로 보자면 저는 흠잡을 데가 없다고 생각해요. 러프턴 경이 아주 부유한 사람이 되지는 않겠지만 재산은 남 보기에 번듯하고, 제가 알아본 바로는 사람됨도 대체로 좋아요. 두 사람이 서로를 좋아한다면 저는 그런 혼인으로 만족하겠어요. 하지만 그 아이를 레이디 러프턴 곁에 저 혼자 남겨 두고 간다는 생각에는 마음이 다 놓이지 않는다고 털어놓아야겠네요. 사람들은 정해지지도 않은 일을, 아주 그럴듯하게 끝내 정해지지 않을 수도 있는 일을 이미 정해진 일로 볼 거예요. 그러면 그 가여운 아이가 해를 입지요. 그 아이가 찬탄을 많이 받고 있다는 건 의심할 여지가 없어요. 그리고 덤벨로 경도—”\n\n부주교는 눈을 더욱 크게 떴다. 그런 사윗감을 골라잡을 여지가 저를 위해 마련되고 있다는 것을 그는 짐작조차 못 했고, 사실을 말하자면 아내의 야심이 다다른 높이에 거의 얼떨떨해졌다. 남작 작위와 연 이만 파운드를 지닌 러프턴 경이라면 겨우 넉넉히 좋다고 받아들일 만하겠지만, 그가 아니라면 재산이 그 열 배도 넘을 알 속의 후작이 제 딸을 받아 갈 준비를 하고 서 있다니! 그러고서 그는, 남편들이 때때로 그러듯이, 바체스터의 워든 관사 정원, 그 집 앞 느릅나무 아래로 구애하러 다니던 시절의 수전 하딩을 떠올렸고, 아직도 그 도시의 조촐한 하숙에 살고 있는 아내의 아버지, 다정한 노인 하딩 씨를 떠올렸다. 그리고 그렇게 떠올리면서 그 부인의 마음이 지닌 웅대함에 놀라며 감탄했다.\n\n“저는 드 테리어 경을 결코 용서할 수 없어요,” 속으로 여러 가지를 이어 붙이며 부인이 말했다.\n\n“터무니없는 소리요,” 부주교가 말했다. “용서해야 하오.”\n\n“그리고 지금 런던을 떠나야 한다는 게 저로서는 성가시다고 털어놓아야겠어요.”\n\n“어쩔 수 없는 일이오,” 부주교가 다소 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. 그는 어떤 대목에서는 제 뜻대로 하기를 택하는 사람이었고—또 그렇게 했다.\n\n“아, 예. 어쩔 수 없는 일인 줄 알아요,” 깊이 상처를 입었음을 내비치는 어조로 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “어쩔 수 없는 일인 줄 알아요. 가여운 그리젤다!” 그러고서 두 사람은 자리에 들었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그리젤다가 어머니에게 왔고, 철저히 남의 눈을 피한 만남에서 어머니는 딸이 앞으로 살아갈 일의 전망에 대하여 여태껏 해 본 적 없는 만큼 많은 말을 했다. 이 주제를 놓고 그랜틀리 부인은 그때까지 거의 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 딸이 러프턴 경이 바치는 언약의 향을 자기가 끼어드는 일 없이 받게 되었다면 그녀는 아주 흐뭇했을 것이다—아니, 그것이 덤벨로 경이 바치는 언약의 향이었더라도 어쩌면 그만큼 흐뭇했을 것이다. 그런 경우라면 제 딸이 넉넉히 애타는 마음으로 자기에게 알려 왔으리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었고, 어느 쪽이든 그 일은 아주 예쁜 연애 혼사로 정리되었을 것이다. 그리젤다 쪽에서 어떤 온당하지 못한 짓이나 어떤 무모한 짓을 할까 걱정하는 마음은 조금도 없었다. 그리젤다는 허락받지 않은 연정에 결코 몸을 맡기지 않을 아이라고 자랑했을 때 그녀는 제 딸을 철저히 알고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 지금 형편으로는, 그 활에 걸린 시위가 둘이고, 게다가 러프턴-그랜틀리 동맹 조약이—그리젤다 자신은 아무것도 모르는 그 조약이—버젓이 있는 터에, 마땅한 지침이 없어 그 가여운 아이가 발을 헛디딜 수도 있지 않겠는가? 이런 생각에 이끌려 그랜틀리 부인은 런던을 떠나기 전에 몇 마디 해 두기로 마음을 굳혔다. 그리하여 딸에게 짧은 글을 써 보냈고, 그리젤다는 두 시에 레이디 러프턴의 마차를 타고 마운트 스트리트에 이르렀다. 마차는 만남이 이어지는 동안 길 모퉁이 맥주 가게에서 그녀를 기다렸다.\n\n“그럼 아버지는 웨스트민스터 주교가 되지 못하는 거예요?” 거인들이 한 일이 그 모든 희망이 끝났음을 알아들을 만큼 넉넉히 설명된 뒤에 젊은 아가씨가 말했다.\n\n“그렇단다, 얘야. 적어도 지금은 아니야.”\n\n“정말 창피한 일이에요! 저는 다 정해진 줄 알았어요. 어머니, 드 테리어 경이 총리가 된 게 무슨 소용이에요, 마음에 드는 사람을 주교로 만들지도 못한다면?”\n\n“드 테리어 경이 네 아버지에게 조금이라도 잘 처신했다고는 생각하지 않는다. 하지만 그건 긴 이야기라서 지금 파고들 수는 없구나.”\n\n“그 프라우디네 사람들이 얼마나 기뻐할까요!”\n\n어머니가 그냥 두었더라면 그리젤다는 이 주제를 놓고 몇 시간이고 이야기했을 것이나, 그랜틀리 부인은 다른 문제로 넘어가야 했다. 그녀는 레이디 러프턴 이야기로 시작하여 그 마님이 얼마나 다정한 분인지를 말했고, 이어서 벗이자 안주인인 그분이 함께 런던에 머물러 있기에 알맞은 동안은 그리젤다도 런던에 남아 있으라고 말했다. 다만 그것이 아마 그리 오래지는 않을 것이라고 덧붙였다. 레이디 러프턴이 런던에 있을 때면 늘 프램리로 돌아가려고 서두른다는 것은 널리 알려진 일이었으니 말이다.\n\n“하지만 올해는 그렇게 서두르지 않으시는 것 같아요, 어머니,” 오월에는 플럼스테드보다 브루턴 스트리트가 좋고 레이디 러프턴 마차 문짝에 박힌 귀족 관에도 아무런 불만이 없던 그리젤다가 말했다.\n\n그러고서 그랜틀리 부인이 설명을 시작했다—아주 조심스럽게. “그래, 얘야, 올해는 그리 서두르지 않으실 게다.—그러니까, 네가 그분과 함께 있는 동안은 말이지.”\n\n“그분은 참 친절하세요.”\n\n“아주 친절하시지. 너도 그분을 많이 사랑해야 한다. 나는 그러고 있단다. 이 세상에 레이디 러프턴보다 더 큰 마음을 두고 있는 벗은 없어. 그래서 너를 그분께 맡기고 가는 것이 이토록 마음 편한 거야.”\n\n“그래도 어머니와 아버지가 그냥 올라와 계셨으면 좋았을 텐데요. 그러니까, 아버지를 주교로 만들어 주었더라면요.”\n\n“이제 그걸 생각해 봐야 아무 소용이 없다, 얘야. 내가 너에게 특별히 하고 싶었던 말은 이거야. 레이디 러프턴이 어떤 생각을 품고 계신지 너도 알아 두어야 한다고 본다.”\n\n“그분의 생각이라니!” 남이 무슨 생각을 하는지 헤아리는 일로 애를 써 본 적이 별로 없는 그리젤다가 말했다.\n\n“그렇다, 그리젤다. 프램리 코트에 내려가 지내는 동안에도, 또 여기 브루턴 스트리트로 올라온 뒤에도 그랬으리라 짐작하지만, 너는—러프턴 경을 꽤 자주 보았을 게다.”\n\n“브루턴 스트리트에는 아주 자주 오지는 않으세요.—그러니까, 아주 자주는 아니라는 말이에요.”\n\n“흐음,” 그랜틀리 부인이 아주 나직이 내뱉었다. 그녀는 그 소리를 아예 눌러 두었으면 했으나 그것은 그녀에게 너무 벅찬 일이었다. 레이디 러프턴이 저를 속이고 있다고 여길 만한 근거를 찾아낸다면 당장에 딸을 데려가고, 조약을 깨고, 하틀탑 쪽과의 결합을 준비할 것이었다. 그녀의 머릿속으로 지나간 생각이 그런 것이었다. 그러나 그 내내 그녀는 레이디 러프턴이 속이는 사람이 아님을 알고 있었다. 잘못은 레이디 러프턴에게 있지 않았고, 어쩌면 러프턴 경에게도 온전히 있지는 않았다. 레이디 러프턴이 제 딸을 두고 늘어놓았던 불평의 무게를 그랜틀리 부인은 다 알아들었다. 그리고 물론 제 아이를 감싸 주었고 대체로는 감싸는 데 성공했으나, 그리젤다가 조금 더 충동적이라면 일등으로 자리를 잡을 기회가 더 나아지리라는 것은 스스로에게 시인했다. 사내는 조각상과 혼인하고 싶어 하지 않는다. 그 조각상이 아무리 조각상답게 훌륭하다 해도 그렇다. 딸에게 충동적이 되라고 가르칠 수 없는 것은 키가 여섯 피트가 되라고 가르칠 수 없는 것과 마찬가지였다. 그러나 그렇게 보이도록 가르치는 것은 될 수 있는 일이 아닐까? 그 일은 어머니의 손으로도 아주 까다로운 일이었다.\n\n“물론 그분이 지금은 같은 집에서 살던 시골에서만큼 집에 붙어 있을 수는 없지,” 지금 이 순간 러프턴 경을 편들어야 하는 것이 제 소임인 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “클럽에도 가야 하고, 상원에도 가야 하고, 스무 군데를 다녀야 할 테니까.”\n\n“그분은 모임에 나가기를 아주 좋아하시고, 춤을 아름답게 추세요.”\n\n“그럴 테지. 나도 그만큼은 직접 보았고, 그분이 누구와 춤추기를 좋아하는지도 알 것 같구나.” 그러고서 어머니는 딸을 사랑스럽게 살짝 껴안았다.\n\n“저를 두고 하시는 말씀이에요, 어머니?”\n\n“그래, 너를 두고 하는 말이다, 얘야. 그리고 그게 사실이 아니냐? 레이디 러프턴 말씀이, 그분이 런던의 다른 누구보다 너와 춤추기를 더 좋아한다고 하시더구나.”\n\n“모르겠어요,” 땅바닥을 내려다보며 그리젤다가 말했다.\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 대체로 이것이 꽤 좋은 시작이라고 생각했다. 더 좋을 수도 있었다. 딸의 마음을 장래 남편의 마음에 이어 붙일 자리로, 왈츠 따위보다 성질이 더 무게 있는 마음 붙일 자리를 찾아낼 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 어떤 마음 붙일 자리든 하나도 없는 것보다는 나았다. 그리고 천성이 충동적이지 않은 사람에게서 마음 붙일 자리를 찾아내기란 참으로 어려운 일이다.\n\n“어쨌든 레이디 러프턴께서는 그렇게 말씀하신다,” 그랜틀리 부인이 한없이 조심스럽게 이어 갔다. “러프턴 경이 너보다 더 좋아하는 짝은 없다고 보시는 거야. 너 자신은 어떻게 생각하느냐, 그리젤다?”\n\n“모르겠어요, 어머니.”\n\n“하지만 젊은 아가씨라면 그런 것을 생각해 두어야 하지 않겠니?”\n\n“그래야 하나요, 어머니?”\n\n“그러는 게 아니냐? 사실은 말이다, 그리젤다, 레이디 러프턴께서 생각하시기를, 만일— 그분이 무엇을 생각하시는지 짐작이 가느냐?”\n\n“아니에요, 어머니.” 그러나 그것은 그리젤다 쪽에서 눙친 것이었다.\n\n“우리 그리젤다가 이 세상에서 자기 아들에게 가장 좋은 아내가 되어 줄 거라고 그분은 생각하신다. 나도 그렇게 생각해. 그분 아들이 그런 아내를 얻을 수 있다면 아주 운이 좋은 사람이라고 생각한다. 그래서 너는 어떻게 생각하느냐, 그리젤다?”\n\n“아무 생각도 없어요, 어머니.”\n\n그러나 그것으로는 될 일이 아니었다. 그녀가 생각을 하는 것이 반드시 필요했고, 어머니가 그렇게 말해 주는 것도 반드시 필요했다. 이만한 정도의 충동 없음이 무슨 결과로 이어질지는—하늘만 알 일이었다! 제 웃음을 얻으려 한숨짓는 귀족 구혼자를 놓고 아무 생각도 하지 않으려는 아가씨에게라면 러프턴-그랜틀리 조약이든 하틀탑 쪽 이해든 다 헛되이 버려질 것이었다. 게다가 그것은 자연스럽지도 않았다. 어머니가 알기로 그리젤다는 앞뒤 없이 몰아치는 감정을 지닌 아이였던 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그러나 그래도 좋아하는 것과 싫어하는 것은 있었다. 주교좌 문제에서는 그 아이도 넉넉히 예리했고, 새로 잘 지은 드레스라는 주제에 그리젤다 그랜틀리보다 더 깊은 관심을 드러낼 사람은 없었다. 제 앞날의 전망에 무심할 수 있을 리 없었고, 그 전망이 주로 혼인에 달려 있다는 것을 그 아이도 알고 있을 것이었다. 어머니는 딸에게 거의 화가 났으나, 그래도 아주 부드럽게 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n“아무 생각도 없다니! 하지만 내 귀여운 것, 너는 생각을 해야 한다. 러프턴 경이 네게 청혼을 한다면 무엇이라 답할지 마음을 정해 두어야 해. 레이디 러프턴께서 그분이 그러기를 바라고 계시니까.”\n\n“하지만 그분은 절대 안 그러실 거예요, 어머니.”\n\n“그런데 만일 그러신다면?”\n\n“하지만 절대 안 그러실 게 뻔해요. 그분은 그런 건 아예 생각하지 않으세요—그리고—그리고—”\n\n“그리고 뭐냐, 얘야?”\n\n“모르겠어요, 어머니.”\n\n“나에게는 터놓고 말할 수 있잖니, 얘야! 내가 마음 쓰는 것은 오직 네 행복뿐이다. 레이디 러프턴이나 나나 너희 둘이 서로를 넉넉히 마음에 둔다면 행복한 혼인이 될 거라고 생각한다. 그분은 아들이 너를 좋아한다고 보시지. 하지만 그분이 러프턴 경의 열 배가 되는 사람이라 해도, 네가 그분에게 마음을 두는 법을 익힐 수 없겠다고 여겨진다면 나는 이 일로 너를 괴롭히지 않을 게다. 하려던 말이 무엇이었느냐, 얘야?”\n\n“러프턴 경은 루시 로바츠를—를—를 다른 누구보다도 훨씬 더 마음에 두고 있어요, 제가 보기에는요,” 이번에는 태도에 조금 생기를 내며 그리젤다가 말했다. “땅딸막하고 조그맣고 까무잡잡한 것이 말이에요.”\n\n“루시 로바츠라니!” 딸이 시샘 같은 감정에 움직였다는 것을 알고 놀라면서도, 그런 쪽으로는 시샘할 근거가 있을 수 없다고 아주 확실히 여기는 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “루시 로바츠라니, 얘야! 러프턴 경이 예의로 말을 거는 것 말고 달리 그 아이에게 말을 붙일 생각을 해 본 적이 있다고는 여기지 않는다.”\n\n“아니, 있었어요, 어머니! 프램리에서 있었던 일을 기억 못 하세요?”\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 마음속으로 지난 일을 되짚어 보기 시작했고, 러프턴 경이 목사의 누이와 꽤 속내를 터놓는 투로 이야기하는 것을 한 번 본 기억이 있는 듯했다. 그러나 거기에는 아무것도 없다고 확신했다. 그리젤다가 제 앞에 내놓인 연인에게 그토록 차가운 이유가 그것이라면, 그것을 걷어 내지 못한다면 천 번 애석한 일일 터였다.\n\n“네가 말을 하니 그 아가씨가 생각나는구나,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “살빛이 검고, 키가 아주 작고, 몸매랄 것이 없는 아이였지. 내가 보기에는 아주 뒤로만 물러나 있는 것 같더라.”\n\n“그건 잘 모르겠어요, 어머니.”\n\n“내가 본 만큼은 그랬다. 하지만 그리젤다야, 그런 일을 마음에 두어서는 안 된다. 러프턴 경이야 어머니 집에 있는 아가씨라면 누구에게든 예의를 지켜야 하는 법이고, 로바츠 양에 관해서 그분이 그 밖의 다른 생각을 조금도 품고 있지 않다는 것은 아주 확실하다. 그 아이의 머리에 대해서는 내가 무엇이라 말할 수가 없구나. 내 앞에서는 입을 뗀 적이 없는 것 같으니 말이다. 그러나—”\n\n“아! 그 아이는 마음만 먹으면 제 할 말은 얼마든지 해요. 앙큼한 것이에요.”\n\n“하지만 어쨌든 얘야, 그 아이에게는 타고난 매력이라고 할 것이 전혀 없다. 그리고 러프턴 경이 로바츠 양이 하는 짓이나 하는 말에—에—에 넘어갈 사람이라고는 조금도 생각하지 않는다.”\n\n“타고난 매력”이라는 그 말이 나오는 순간, 그리젤다는 벽에 걸린 거울 가운데 하나에 제 옆모습이 비치도록 목을 돌려 냈고, 그러고서 고개를 뻣뻣이 세우고 눈을 살짝 놀려 보이니, 어머니가 보기에 아주 잘생겨 보였다. “저에게는 물론 아무것도 아닌 일이에요, 어머니,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 얘야, 아마 아닐 테지. 나도 그렇다고는 하지 않는다. 나는 네 마음에 조금이라도 억지를 씌우고 싶지 않아. 네 분별과 높은 지조를 온전히 믿고 있지 않다면 이런 식으로 네게 말하지 않을 게다. 그러나 믿고 있으니, 너와 러프턴 경이 서로를 좋아한다고 여겨진다면 레이디 러프턴이나 나나 아주 흐뭇할 것이라는 말을 해 두는 것이 가장 좋다고 생각했다.”\n\n“그분은 그런 건 절대 생각하지 않으실 거예요, 어머니.”\n\n“그리고 루시 로바츠에 관해서는, 부디 그 생각을 머리에서 지워 버리렴. 너를 위해서가 아니라면 그분을 위해서라도 말이다. 그분에게 그보다 나은 취향이 있다고 믿어 드려야 해.”\n\n그러나 그리젤다가 한번 머리에 들여놓은 것을 다시 끄집어 내기란 그리 쉬운 일이 아니었다. “취향이란 것은요, 어머니, 따져 볼 수가 없는 것이잖아요,” 그녀가 말했다. 그러고서 그 주제를 놓고 벌이던 이야기는 끝이 났다. 그것이 그랜틀리 부인의 마음에 남긴 결과는 덤벨로 쪽 이해에 유리한, 거의 확신에 이르는 느낌이었다.\n\n# 제26장. 충동적인 사람\n\n독자들께서는 모두, 호글스톡으로 마차를 몰고 가던 그 길에서 조랑말 퍽이 어떻게 매를 맞았는지 기억하시리라 믿는다. 그때 퍽 자신은 그리 크게 앓지 않았으리라 짐작해도 좋다. 그 살가죽은 로바츠 부인의 마음만큼 무르지 않았다. 그 작은 짐승은 귀리와 이 세상 온갖 좋은 것으로 배가 부른 터라, 채찍이 몸에 닿으면 껑충거리며 작은 두 귀를 흔들고 스무 걸음쯤을 무서운 속도로 내달아, 제 여주인으로 하여금 제가 끔찍한 일을 겪었다고 여기게 만들었다. 그러나 참으로 말하자면, 그 채찍질이 오가는 동안 가장 크게 앓은 것은 퍽이 아니었다.\n\n루시는 밝히지 않을 수 없었다 — 제 감정의 힘에 밀려, 그리고 러프턴 경과 그랜틀리 양 사이의 혼사가 온당하다고는 도무지 수긍할 수 없었기에 밀려 — 러프턴 경이 자기 오라비라 하여도 그만큼은 그분에게 마음을 쓴다고 밝히지 않을 수 없었다. 이 모든 것을, 아니 이보다 훨씬 더한 것을 그녀는 스스로에게 아주 자주 말해 왔다. 그러나 이제 그녀는 그것을 올케에게 소리 내어 말해 버렸고, 자기가 한 말이 기억되고 헤아려졌으며 어느 정도까지는 처신이 달라지는 까닭이 되었음을 알고 있었다. 패니는 예사 이야기 중에 러프턴 집안 사람들을 아주 드물게만 들먹였고, 남편이 그를 입에 올리지 않을 수 없게 만들 때가 아니면 러프턴 경에 관하여는 결코 말하지 않았다. 루시는 한 번 이상, 그 젊은 나리에 관하여 웃음 섞인, 그리고 어쩌면 반쯤 비웃는 투로 이야기함으로써 이것을 바로잡아 보려 하였다. 그의 사냥과 총질을 두고 비꼬았고, 그리젤다에 대한 그의 사랑을 두고 농으로 한마디 해 보려고 대담하게 나서기까지 하였다. 그러나 그녀는 자기가 실패했음을 느꼈다. 패니에 관하여는 온전히 실패했고, 오라비에 관하여는 그 눈을 감겨 두는 데에 무슨 효험이 있기보다 오히려 그 눈을 뜨게 하는 구실이 되기가 더 쉬웠다. 그리하여 그녀는 애쓰기를 그만두고 러프턴 경에 관하여 더는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그녀의 비밀은 말해져 버렸고, 그녀는 그것이 말해졌음을 알고 있었다.\n\n이 무렵 두 부인은 목사관 거실에 단둘이 남겨져 있는 때가 아주 많았다. 어쩌면 루시가 그곳에 온 뒤로 여태 그런 적이 없었을 만큼 많았다. 레이디 러프턴은 떠나 있었으므로 프램리 코트로 거의 매일 올라가던 걸음도 없었고, 마크는 이 며칠 바체스터에서 지내는 때가 많았으니, 그 성당 참사회의 한 사람으로 받아들여지기 전에 치러야 할 아주 무거운 임무가 틀림없이 있었던 것이다. 그는 스스로 즐겨 거주 근무라 부르는 것에 거의 곧바로 들어갔다. 곧, 한 달치 설교를 맡았고, 주일 아침 예배 전반에도 조금씩, 아주 위엄 있는 방식으로 거들었다. 집이 채 준비되지 않았던 까닭에 그가 바체스터에 딱히 살지는 않았다. 적어도 내세운 이유는 그것이었다. 앞서의 참사회원인 스탠호프 박사의 가재도구가 아직 치워지지 않았고, 채권자들이 그것에 대한 권리를 주장하고 있었으므로 얼마간 지체가 있을 듯하였다. 지난 시대의 후한 마음이 제가 쓰도록 마련해 준 그 훌륭한 집을 애타게 기다리는 신사에게라면 이것이 몹시 불편한 일이었을 터이나, 로바츠 씨는 그렇게 느끼지 않았다. 스탠호프 박사의 식구든 채권자든 앞으로 열두 달 동안 그 집을 붙들고 있어 준다면 그로서는 아주 흡족하였다. 그리고 이 처분 덕분에 그는 프램리 교회를 비운 첫 달을 레이디 러프턴의 눈길 한 번 받지 않고 넘길 수 있었으니, 레이디 러프턴이 그동안 줄곧 런던에 있었기 때문이다. 이 또한 편리한 일이었고, 우리 젊은 참사회원으로 하여금 자기의 새 성직 영전을 여태보다 좋게 보도록 가르쳐 주었다.\n\n그리하여 패니와 루시는 단둘이 있는 때가 많았다. 그리고 머리에 가득한 것에서 입이 말하듯, 마음에 가득한 것은 이런 흉허물 없는 시간에 더욱 말로 나오기 쉬운 법이다. 루시는 처음 제 처지를 헤아렸을 때, 입을 다무는 힘센 재주를 스스로에게 갖추어 주기로 마음먹었다. 제 사랑을 결코 말하지 않을 것은 물론이려니와, 감춤이 제 다마스크빛 뺨을 파먹게 두지도 않을 것이고, 비석 위에 앉은 인내의 상처럼 한순간이라도 앉아 있는 모습을 보이지도 않을 것이었다. 제 가슴속에서 제 싸움을 용감하게 싸워 원수를 온전히 이겨 낼 것이었다. 설교로든 굶김으로든 지치게 함으로든 제 사랑을 굴복시킬 것이며, 아무도 그것을 티끌만큼도 눈치채지 못할 것이었다. 러프턴 경과 손을 잡을 때에도 떨림 하나 없도록 스스로를 가르칠 것이고, 그의 아내를 놀랍도록 좋아할 준비까지 되어 있을 것이었다 — 다만 그 아내가 그리젤다 그랜틀리인 경우가 아니라면. 그녀의 결심은 그러하였다. 그러나 첫 주가 다 가기도 전에 그 결심은 산산조각으로 부서져 바람에 흩날려 갔다.\n\n두 사람은 비 오는 어느 날 하루를 온통 집 안에 함께 앉아 지냈다. 그리고 마크가 바체스터에서 수석 사제와 저녁을 먹기로 되어 있었으므로 두 사람은 이르게 저녁을 먹었으니, 아이들을 거의 무릎에 앉힌 채로 함께 먹었다. 남편들이 아내를 혼자 두고 나가면 부인들은 으레 그렇게 한다. 저녁이 되어 어스름이 내리는데, 아이들은 이제 물러갔고 두 사람은 여전히 거실에 앉아 있었다. 그때 로바츠 부인은 호글스톡을 다녀온 뒤로 다섯 번째로, 크롤리네에 무슨 좋은 일을 해 줄 수 있었으면 좋겠다는 소망을 말하기 시작했다. 특히 그레이스 크롤리에게 그러했으니, 아버지의 팔꿈치 옆에 서서 그리스어 불규칙 동사를 배우고 있던 그 아이는 로바츠 부인의 눈에 유별나게 딱하게 여길 만한 아이로 보였던 것이다.\n\n“어떻게 손을 대야 할지 모르겠어요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n그런데 호글스톡 방문을 들먹이는 말은 무엇이든 언제나 루시의 마음을, 그때 그 마음을 가장 크게 채우고 있던 일거리로 되돌려 놓았다. 그런 순간이면 그녀는 자기가 어떻게 퍽을 때렸는지, 그리고 반쯤은 농지거리 같으면서도 역시 너무 참된 그 태도로 어떻게 그 일을 사과하고 그 까닭을 밝혔는지를 떠올렸다. 그러므로 그녀는 그레이스 크롤리에 관하여 마땅히 그랬어야 할 만큼 생생하게 관심을 낼 수 없었다.\n\n“그렇지요. 아무도 모르는 일이에요,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“집으로 돌아오는 그 길에서 내내 그 생각을 했어요,” 패니가 말했다. “어려운 것은 이거예요. 그 아이를 데려다 무엇을 해 주어야 하나?”\n\n“그렇군요,” 루시는 말했으나, 마음속으로는 자기가 러프턴 경을 아주 많이 좋아한다고 밝혀 버린 그 길의 바로 그 지점을 떠올리고 있었다.\n\n“그 아이를 한 달쯤 여기 데려다 두고 그다음에 학교에 보낼 수 있다면 좋겠어요 — 하지만 크롤리 씨가 우리더러 그 아이 학비를 대게 하지는 않을 거예요.”\n\n“대지 않으실 것 같네요,” 루시는 크롤리 씨와 그 딸 그레이스로부터 아득히 멀어진 생각을 안고 말했다.\n\n“그러면 그 아이를 데려다 무엇을 해 주어야 할지 모르게 되겠지요, 그렇지 않아요?”\n\n“그렇지요. 모르실 거예요.”\n\n“아무것도 가르쳐 줄 사람 하나 없이 그 딱한 아이를 여기 이 집에 두는 것은 도무지 안 될 일이에요. 마크가 그 아이에게 그리스어 동사를 가르치지는 않을 테니까요.”\n\n“그러지 않을 것 같아요.”\n\n“루시, 내가 하는 말을 한마디도 듣고 있지 않네요. 지난 한 시간 동안 그랬던 것 같아요. 내가 무슨 이야기를 하고 있는지도 모르고 있는 게 아니에요?”\n\n“아니에요, 알아요 — 그레이스 크롤리요. 원하신다면 내가 가르쳐 보겠어요. 다만 나 자신이 아는 게 아무것도 없어서요.”\n\n“내가 하려는 말은 전혀 그게 아니고, 그런 일을 루시에게 떠맡기자고 청하지는 않을 것도 알잖아요. 하지만 나와 함께 그 일을 두고 이야기는 해 볼 수 있을 것 같은데요.”\n\n“그럴 수 있을까요? 좋아요, 하겠어요. 무엇인가요? 아, 그레이스 크롤리요 — 누가 그 아이에게 그리스어 불규칙 동사를 가르쳐 줄지 알고 싶으신 거지요. 아이고, 패니, 머리가 어찌나 아픈지 — 제발 나에게 노여워하지 마세요.” 그러고서 루시는 소파에 몸을 던지고 한 손을 아프게 이마로 올리며 싸움을 아주 놓아 버렸다.\n\n로바츠 부인은 한순간에 그녀 곁에 와 있었다. “우리 루시, 요새는 왜 그렇게 자주 머리가 아픈 거예요? 전에는 그런 두통이 없었잖아요.”\n\n“내가 멍청해지고 있으니까요. 마음 쓰지 마세요. 딱한 그레이스 이야기를 이어서 해요. 가정 교사를 두는 것은 안 될 일이겠지요?”\n\n“루시가 성치 않다는 게 보여요,” 로바츠 부인이 깊이 근심하는 낯으로 말했다. “무슨 일이에요, 우리 루시? 무슨 일이 있다는 게 보여요.”\n\n“무슨 일이라니요! 아니에요, 없어요. 말할 만한 일은 아무것도 없어요. 이따금은 데번셔로 돌아가 거기서 살까 하는 생각이 들어요. 얼마간은 블랜치와 지낼 수 있을 테고, 그다음에는 엑서터에 하숙을 하나 얻으면 되지요.”\n\n“데번셔로 돌아간다니요!” 그러고서 로바츠 부인은 시누이가 미쳐 가고 있다고 여기는 듯한 낯을 하였다. “왜 우리를 떠나려고 해요? 여기가 이제부터 언제까지나 루시 자신의, 자신의 집인데요.”\n\n“그런가요? 그렇다면 나는 딱한 처지에 있는 거예요. 아이고, 아이고, 나는 어쩌면 이렇게 바보일까! 나는 어쩌면 이렇게 천치였을까! 패니, 나는 여기 있을 수 없을 것 같아요. 애초에 오지 않았더라면 좋았겠다는 생각이 그렇게 간절해요. 정말이에요 — 정말이에요 — 정말이에요, 패니가 그렇게 무섭게 나를 바라보아도요.” 그러고서 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 올케의 품에 몸을 던지고 사납게 입을 맞추기 시작했다. “상처받은 척은 하지 마세요. 내가 패니를 사랑하는 것을 알잖아요. 내가 한평생 패니와 함께 살면서 패니가 완전한 사람이라고 여길 수 있다는 것도 알잖아요 — 실제로 그러니까요. 그렇지만 —”\n\n“마크가 무슨 말을 했어요?”\n\n“한마디도요, — 한 음절의 그림자조차 없어요. 마크가 아니에요. 아, 패니!”\n\n“무슨 말인지 알 것 같아 두려워요,” 로바츠 부인이 낮고 떨리는 목소리로, 얼굴에 깊은 슬픔을 드리운 채 말했다.\n\n“물론 알겠지요. 물론 알고 있지요. 처음부터 줄곧 알고 있었잖아요. 그 조랑말 마차에서의 그날부터요. 패니가 안다는 것을 나는 알고 있었어요. 그분의 이름을 감히 입에 올리지도 못하잖아요. 그것만으로도 패니가 안다는 것을 나에게 알려 주는 게 아닌가요? 그리고 나는, 나는 마크 앞에서라면 넉넉히 위선을 부릴 수 있어요. 하지만 내 위선은 패니 앞에서는 검사를 통과하지 못해요. 그러니 이제 데번셔로 가는 편이 낫지 않겠어요?”\n\n“우리 루시, 우리 귀한 루시.”\n\n“그 딱지를 붙이는 일에 대해서 내 말이 맞지 않았나요? 아, 하느님! 우리 처녀들은 어쩌면 이렇게 천치인가요! 부드러운 말 열두 마디가 나를 나인핀처럼 나동그라지게 만들고, 내 것이라 부를 땅을 한 치도 남겨 주지 않았으니! 그런데도 나는 내 힘을 그토록 자랑했어요. 결코 새침을 떨거나 얼이 빠지거나 감상에 젖지 않으리라고 그토록 확신했어요! 마크가, 아니면 패니가 그분을 좋아하는 식으로 나도 좋아하겠다고 그토록 다져 두었는데 —”\n\n“그분이 루시에게 해서는 안 될 말을 했다면 나는 그분을 조금도 좋아하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“하지만 그분은 그러지 않았어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 잠시 멈추어 헤아려 보았다. “그래요, 그러지 않았어요. 패니가 알게 된다 해도 그분에게 노여워할 만한 말은 한마디도 나에게 한 적이 없어요. 다만, 어쩌면 나를 루시라고 부른 것은 그렇겠지요. 그리고 그것은 내 잘못이지 그분의 잘못이 아니에요.”\n\n“루시가 부드러운 말을 두고 이야기했으니까 묻는 거예요.”\n\n“패니, 내가 얼마나 온전한 바보인지, 얼마나 말로 못 할 얼간이인지 패니는 모르고 있어요. 내가 말하는 그 부드러운 말이란, 아일랜드에서 보내 준 그 암소가 잘 자라느냐고 패니에게 물을 때 쓰는 것과 같은 말, 아니면 폰토의 어깻죽지를 두고 마크에게 하는 말과 같은 것이었어요. 그분은 나에게, 아버지를 알고 있었다고, 마크와 함께 학교를 다녔다고, 그리고 여기 목사관에서 패니와 그토록 좋은 벗이니 나와도 좋은 벗이 되어야 한다고 말했어요. 아니에요, 그분의 잘못이 아니었어요. 해를 지은 그 부드러운 말은 그런 것들이었어요. 그렇지만 그분의 어머니는 세상을 어찌 그렇게 잘 알고 계셨을까요! 무사하려면 나는 감히 그분을 바라보지도 말았어야 했어요.”\n\n“하지만 우리 루시 —”\n\n“패니가 무슨 말을 하려는지 알아요. 그리고 그것을 다 인정해요. 그분은 영웅이 아니에요. 그분에게는 이 세상에서 놀라울 만한 것이 하나도 없어요. 나는 그분이 지혜로운 말 한마디를 하는 것도, 시에 가까운 생각 하나를 내놓는 것도 들어 본 적이 없어요. 그분은 여우를 뒤쫓아 달리거나 딱한 새들을 죽이는 데에 온 기운을 바치고, 나는 그분이 크나큰 일 한 가지를 했다는 이야기를 여태 살면서 들은 적이 없어요. 그런데도 —”\n\n패니는 시누이가 말을 이어 가는 모양에 어찌나 놀랐던지 어떻게 말해야 할지 거의 알 수 없었다. “그분은 훌륭한 아들이지요,” 마침내 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“개서럼 성에 갈 때만 빼면요. 그분이 가진 것을 내가 말해 줄게요. 곧고 잘생긴 다리, 매끄러운 이마, 사람 좋은 눈, 그리고 흰 이빨이에요. 그런 완전함의 목록을 보고서 뼛속까지 얻어맞아 쓰러지지 않을 수 있었을까요? 하지만 그것이 다 한 일은 아니었어요, 패니. 그것쯤은 버텨 낼 수 있었어요. 적어도 버틸 수 있었을 것 같아요. 나를 죽인 것은 그분의 작위였어요. 나는 그때까지 귀족과 말을 해 본 적이 없었어요. 아, 나란 사람! 나는 어쩌면 이런 바보, 이런 짐승이었을까!” 그러고서 그녀는 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n사실을 말하자면 로바츠 부인은 딱한 루시의 병을 거의 알아볼 수 없었다. 그 괴로움이 참된 것임은 넉넉히 뚜렷하였다. 그러나 그녀는 자기 자신과 자기 고통을 두고 어찌나 반어를 섞어, 어찌나 농에 가까이 다가가며 말하는지, 어디까지가 진심인지 가려내기가 몹시 어려웠다. 게다가 루시는 로바츠 부인이 늘 온전히 알아듣지는 못하는 일종의 농지거리에 어찌나 잘 빠지는지, 그 탓에 로바츠 부인은 혀끝에 먼저 올라오는 말을 입 밖에 내기가 이따금 두려웠다. 그러나 이제 루시가 아주 눈물에 잠기고 격정으로 숨이 넘어갈 듯하니, 로바츠 부인도 더는 잠자코 있을 수 없었다. “우리 루시, 부디 그런 식으로 말하지 마세요. 다 잘될 거예요. 누구도 잘못을 저지르지 않았을 때에는 일이 언제나 잘되는 법이에요.”\n\n“그래요, 아무도 잘못을 저지르지 않았을 때지요. 그건 아버지가 논점 선취라고 부르시던 거예요. 하지만 내 말을 들어 보세요, 패니. 나는 지지 않을 거예요. 나 자신을 죽이든지, 아니면 이 일을 뚫고 나갈 거예요. 나 자신이 진심으로 부끄러워서, 이 싸움을 끝까지 싸워 내는 것이 나 자신에게 갚아야 할 빚이에요.”\n\n“어떤 싸움을 싸운다는 거예요, 우리 루시?”\n\n“이 싸움이요. 여기서, 지금, 이 순간에 나는 러프턴 경을 만날 수가 없어요. 그분이 문 안에 모습을 보이기라도 하면 놀란 닭처럼 달아나야 할 거예요. 그리고 그분이 이 교구에 있다는 것을 안다면 감히 집 밖으로 나가지도 못할 거예요.”\n\n“그럴 것 같지 않은데요. 루시가 속을 드러낸 적이 없다고 나는 확신하니까요.”\n\n“그래요, 없지요. 나 자신에 관하여는 거짓말과 위선을 꽤 잘해 냈다고 생각해요. 하지만 우리 패니, 패니는 절반도 몰라요. 그리고 알 수도 없고 알아서도 안 돼요.”\n\n“하지만 두 사람 사이에는 아무 일도 없었다고 말한 줄 알았는데요.”\n\n“내가 그랬나요? 그래요, 패니에게는 참되지 않은 말을 한마디도 한 적이 없어요. 나는 그분이 해서 잘못될 말은 아무것도 하지 않았다고 말했지요. 그것이 잘못일 수는 없었어요 —. 하지만 마음 쓰지 마세요. 내가 무엇을 하려는지 말해 줄게요. 지난 한 주 동안 그것을 생각해 왔어요 — 다만 마크에게는 말해야 하겠지요.”\n\n“내가 루시라면 마크에게 다 말하겠어요.”\n\n“뭐라고요, 마크에게요! 그렇게 하면, 패니, 나는 다시는, 다시는, 다시는 패니에게 말을 걸지 않을 거예요. 그러겠어요? — 참된 자매의 사랑으로 내 마음을 죄 패니에게 내주었는데도요?”\n\n로바츠 부인은 자기가 마크에게 무엇을 말하겠다고 한 것이 아니라고 설명해야 했고, 더구나 따로 허락을 받지 않는 한 그에게 아무것도 말하지 않겠다는 엄숙한 약속까지 하도록 설득되었다.\n\n“나는 수도원에 들어갈까 해요,” 루시가 말을 이었다. “그런 수도원이 어떤 곳인지 알지요?” 로바츠 부인은 아주 잘 알고 있다고 다짐해 주었고, 그러자 루시가 말을 이었다. “한 해 전이라면 나는 그런 삶을 생각할 처녀로는 영국에서 가장 마지막일 거라고 말했겠지요. 하지만 이제는 그것이 나에게 가장 좋은 일이 되겠다고 정말로 믿어요. 그러고서 나 자신을 굶기고, 나 자신을 채찍질하겠어요. 그런 식으로 내 마음과 내 영혼을 되찾아 오겠어요.”\n\n“루시의 영혼이라니요!” 로바츠 부인이 몸서리치는 어조로 말했다.\n\n“그러면 내 마음이라고 하죠, 패니가 그 말이 더 좋다면요. 그렇지만 나 자신이 마음을 두고 떠드는 것을 듣기가 싫어요. 나는 내 마음을 아끼지 않아요. 그냥 보내 버리겠어요 — 이 멋만 낸 젊은 귀족과 함께든 다른 누구와 함께든요. 그래서 여기가 — 여기가 — 여기가 잘못되었다는 느낌 없이 읽고, 말하고, 걷고, 자고, 먹을 수만 있다면요.” 그러고서 그녀는 손을 옆구리에 사납게 눌러 댔다. “내가 느끼는 이것이 무엇인가요, 패니? 왜 나는 몸이 이렇게 약해서 운동도 못 하나요? 왜 나는 책에 한순간도 마음을 붙들어 둘 수 없나요? 왜 나는 두 문장을 이어 쓸 수 없나요? 왜 내가 먹는 한 입 한 입이 목에 걸리나요? 아, 패니, 그것은 그분의 다리 때문일까요, 아니면 그분의 작위 때문일까요?”\n\n슬픔에 온통 잠겨 있으면서도 — 그녀는 참으로 슬펐다 — 로바츠 부인은 웃음을 참을 수 없었다. 그리고 실상 루시의 얼굴에는 이따금 거의 우스꽝스럽다 할 만한 무엇이 있었다. 자기를 조롱거리로 삼으려 애쓰며 짓는 그 반어를 어찌나 잘 연기하는지! “나를 좀 비웃어 주세요,” 그녀가 말했다. “이 세상에서 그만큼 나에게 좋은 것은 없어요. 굶기와 채찍이 아니라면 아무것도요. 사내가 잘생겼고 귀족이라는 까닭으로 마음을 쓰는 여자는 비굴한 바보 천치일 수밖에 없다고 나에게 말해 주기만 한다면요!”\n\n“하지만 그게 까닭이었던 것은 아니에요. 러프턴 경에게는 그보다 훨씬 더한 것이 있어요. 그리고 말을 해야 한다면, 루시, 루시가 그분과 사랑에 빠진 것이 이상할 것은 없다고 나는 말하지 않을 수 없어요. 다만 — 다만 그것이 —”\n\n“다만 무엇이요? 어서, 다 말해 보세요. 말을 가리지도 말고, 나를 꾸짖는다고 해서 내가 노여워할 거라고 생각하지도 마세요.”\n\n“다만 나는 루시가 너무 단단히 막아 두는 사람이어서, 어떤 신사도 — 그가 루시를 마음에 둔다는 것을 보이기까지는 — 마음에 두지 않으리라고 생각했다는 거예요.”\n\n“막아 둔다! 그래요, 그거예요. 바로 그 말이에요. 하지만 막아 두어야 했던 쪽은 그분이에요. 그분 앞에는 불막이라도 걸어 두어야 했어요 — 아니면 사랑막이라도, 그렇게 부르고 싶다면요. 막아 둔다니! 패니네가 죄 나를 끌어내기까지 나는 막아 두고 있지 않았나요? 내가 거기 가고 싶어 했나요? 그리고 거기 갔을 때, 나는 구석에 앉아서 저 아래 하인 방에 있었더라면 훨씬 제자리였겠다고 생각하며 스스로를 바보로 만들지 않았나요? 레이디 러프턴 — 그분이 나를 끌어내고, 그러고는 조심하라고 일러 주고, 그러고는, 그러고는 — 왜 레이디 러프턴은 무엇이든 제 뜻대로 해야 하나요? 왜 나는 그분을 위해 희생되어야 하나요? 나는 레이디 러프턴도, 그분에게 딸린 누구도 알고 싶어 하지 않았어요.”\n\n“레이디 러프턴을 탓할 까닭이 루시에게 있다고는 생각할 수 없어요. 어쩌면 누구를 그리 크게 탓할 까닭도 없을 것 같아요.”\n\n“그래요, 없지요. 죄 내 잘못이었어요. 그렇지만 패니, 아무리 애를 써서 뒤로 뒤로 되짚어 가도 내가 어디서 첫걸음을 잘못 디뎠는지는 알아볼 수가 없어요. 어디서 어긋났는지 모르겠어요. 잘못한 일이 하나 있는데, 그것이 내가 뉘우치지 않는 단 하나예요.”\n\n“그게 무엇이에요, 루시?”\n\n“나는 그분에게 거짓말을 했어요.”\n\n로바츠 부인은 온통 캄캄한 속에 있었고, 자기가 그렇다는 것을 느끼고 있었으므로, 벗으로서도 자매로서도 조언을 줄 수 없다는 것을 알았다. 루시는 — 로바츠 부인이 여긴 바로는 — 자기와 러프턴 경 사이에 지극히 하찮은 말들밖에는 오간 것이 없다고 밝히면서 시작했는데, 이제는 스스로 거짓말을 했다고 자기를 몰아세우고, 그 거짓말이야말로 자기가 뉘우치지 않는 단 하나라고 밝히는 것이었다!\n\n“그러지 않았기를 바라요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. “그랬다면 루시답지 않은 짓이었어요.”\n\n“하지만 나는 했어요. 그리고 그분이 다시 여기 와서 같은 식으로 나에게 말한다면 나는 그것을 되풀이할 거예요. 그러리라는 것을 알아요. 그러지 않는다면 온 세상이 나에게 달려들 거예요. 패니도 나에게 눈살을 찌푸리고 쌀쌀해지겠지요. 내 사랑 패니, 내가 정말로 패니를 언짢게 한다면 어떤 낯을 하겠어요?”\n\n“루시가 그럴 것 같지 않은데요.”\n\n“하지만 내가 그분에게 진실을 말했다면 그렇게 되었겠지요, 그렇지 않아요? 이제 말해 보세요. 아니, 패니, 말하지 않아도 돼요. 그것은 패니가 무서워서가 아니었어요. 아니, 그분이 무서워서도 아니었어요. 하기야 그분의 그 무서운 뿌루퉁함이 도무지 견딜 수 없을 것임은 하느님도 아시지요.”\n\n“루시를 알아들을 수가 없어요. 루시 말대로 두 사람 사이에 예사 이야기밖에 없었다면, 그분에게 무슨 진실을, 또 무슨 거짓을 말할 수 있었다는 거예요?”\n\n그러자 루시는 소파에서 일어나, 입을 열기까지 방의 길이를 두 번 오갔다. 로바츠 부인에게는 여자의 — 라고 말하려 했으나, 사람이라면 누구나 지니는 것이라고 말해 두겠는데 — 그 예사 호기심이 다 있었다. 게다가 자매의 사랑도 다 있었다. 그녀는 알고 싶기도 하고 걱정스럽기도 하여, 있던 자리에 그대로 앉은 채 말없이 눈을 제 벗에게 붙박아 두었다.\n\n“내가 그렇게 말했나요?” 마침내 루시가 말했다. “아니에요, 패니. 패니가 나를 잘못 알아들었어요. 나는 그렇게 말하지 않았어요. 아, 그래요, 암소와 개에 관한 것 말이지요. 그것은 다 참이었어요. 나는 내가 그런 바보가 되어 가는 동안 그분의 부드러운 말이 어떤 것이었는지를 이야기하고 있었어요. 그 뒤로 그분은 더 말했어요.”\n\n“그분이 무엇을 더 말했어요, 루시?”\n\n“말하고 싶어 못 견디겠어요, 패니를 믿을 수만 있다면요.” 그러고서 루시는 로바츠 부인의 발밑에 무릎을 꿇고 그 얼굴을 올려다보며, 남아 있는 눈물방울 사이로 웃었다. “말하고 싶은 마음이야 간절해요. 하지만 나는 아직 패니를 몰라요 — 패니가 아주 참된 사람인지를요. 나라면 참될 수 있어요 — 벗이 나에게 털어놓았다면 온 세상을 거스르고서도 참될 수 있어요. 패니가 참될 수 있다고 말한다면 나는 말하겠어요. 하지만 패니가 스스로를 의심한다면, 마크에게 죄 귀엣말로 옮겨야 한다면 — 그러면 우리 잠자코 있어요.”\n\n이것은 로바츠 부인에게 거의 무섭다 할 만한 것이었다. 혼인한 뒤로 여태 그녀의 마음을 지나간 생각 가운데 남편과 나누지 않은 것은 거의 없었다. 그런데 이제 이 모든 것이 어찌나 갑작스럽게 그녀에게 닥쳤던지, 그런 비밀을 — 루시의 오라비에게도 입에 올려서는 안 되고 자기 남편에게도 입에 올려서는 안 되는 비밀을 — 맡아 두는 사람이 되는 것이 좋은 일일지 어떨지 헤아릴 수조차 없었다. 그러나 비밀을 내주겠다는데 그것을 마다한 사람이 여태 있었던가? 하다못해 사랑의 비밀을 마다한 사람이 있었던가? 어느 자매가 그럴 수 있겠는가? 그리하여 로바츠 부인은 약속을 주었다. 그러면서 루시의 머리를 매만지고, 그 이마에 입을 맞추고, 무지개처럼 눈물 덕에 더 밝아진 그 눈을 들여다보았다. “그래서 그분이 루시에게 무슨 말을 했어요?”\n\n“무슨 말이요? 그저 이것이에요. 그분이 나에게 아내가 되어 달라고 청했다는 것이요.”\n\n“러프턴 경이 루시에게 청혼을 했어요?”\n\n“그래요, 나에게 청혼했어요. 믿을 만한 일이 아니지요, 그렇지 않아요? 그런 일이 일어났다고는 도무지 믿어지지 않지요, 그렇지 않아요?” 그러고서 루시는 다시 발을 딛고 일어섰으니, 남들이 자기를 어떤 업신여김으로 대할 것이라는 생각이 — 자기 자신이 스스로를 대하고 있는 그 업신여김의 생각이 — 피를 뺨으로 올려 보냈던 것이다. “그런데도 그것은 꿈이 아니에요. 꿈이 아니라고 나는 생각해요. 그분이 정말로 그랬다고 생각해요.”\n\n“생각한다니요, 루시!”\n\n“그래요, 확신한다고 말해도 좋겠지요.”\n\n“신사라면 정식으로 청혼을 하고서 그 뜻을 두고 루시를 의심 속에 남겨 두지는 않을 거예요.”\n\n“아이고, 그럼요. 그런 종류의 의심은 조금도 없었어요. 티끌만큼도요. 스미스 씨가 존스 양에게 스미스 부인이 되어 주는 영광을 달라고 청할 때에도 그보다 더 분명하게 말하지는 못했을 거예요. 나는 그것을 죄 꿈으로 꾸었을 수도 있다는 이야기를 하고 있었던 거예요.”\n\n“루시!”\n\n“그래요, 꿈이 아니었어요. 여기, 여기 서서, 바로 이 자리에서 — 깔개의 저 꽃무늬 위에서 — 그분은 나에게 아내가 되어 달라고 열두 번이나 애원했어요. 저것을 잘라 내어 내가 갖도록 패니와 마크가 허락해 줄지 모르겠네요.”\n\n“그래서 그분에게 어떤 답을 주었어요?”\n\n“나는 그분에게 거짓말을 했어요. 그분을 사랑하지 않는다고 말했지요.”\n\n“그분을 거절했어요?”\n\n“그래요, 나는 살아 있는 귀족을 거절했어요. 그것을 두고 생각할 거리가 있다는 것은 얼마간 흡족한 일이지요, 그렇지 않아요? 패니, 그런 거짓말을 한 것이 못된 짓이었을까요?”\n\n“그런데 왜 그분을 거절했어요?”\n\n“왜냐고요? 그것을 물을 수 있어요? 프램리 코트로 내려가서 이야기하는 중에 마님께 내가 아드님과 약혼했다고 아뢰는 일이 어떠했을지를 생각해 보세요. 레이디 러프턴을 생각해 보세요. 그렇지만 그것 때문도 아니었어요, 패니. 그것이 그분에게 좋은 일이라고, 그분이 뉘우치지 않을 것이라고 생각했다면 나는 무엇에도 맞섰을 거예요 — 그분을 위해서요. 패니의 찌푸린 눈살에도요. 패니도 눈살을 찌푸렸을 테니까요. 내가 러프턴 경과 혼인하는 것을 패니는 신성 모독이라고 여겼을 거예요! 그랬을 것임을 스스로 알잖아요.”\n\n로바츠 부인은 자기가 생각하는 것을 어떻게 말해야 할지, 아니 실은 무엇을 생각해야 마땅한지조차 거의 알 수 없었다. 이는 조언을 주려면 그전에 오래도록 골똘히 생각해야 할 일이었는데, 루시는 바로 그 순간에 그녀에게서 조언을 기다리고 있었다. 러프턴 경이 정말로 루시 로바츠를 사랑하고 루시 로바츠에게 사랑받고 있다면, 그 둘이 부부가 되어서 안 될 까닭이 무엇인가? 그러면서도 그녀는 그것이 — 루시가 말한 대로 신성 모독까지는 아니겠으나 그에 거의 못지않게 말썽스러운 것이 되겠다고 느꼈다. 레이디 러프턴이 무엇이라 말하고, 무엇이라 생각하고, 무엇이라 느끼겠는가? 그토록 치명적인 타격이 그분에게로 떨어져 나오게 될 그 목사관을 두고 무엇이라 말하고 생각하고 느끼겠는가? 교구 사제와 그 아내를 가장 검은 배은망덕으로 몰아세우지 않겠는가? 그런 형편 아래에서 프램리에서의 삶이 견딜 만한 것이겠는가?\n\n“루시가 하는 말이 나를 어찌나 놀라게 하는지, 아직은 그것을 두고 어떻게 말해야 할지도 거의 모르겠어요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“놀라운 일이었지요, 그렇지 않아요? 그분은 그때 제정신이 아니었을 거예요. 그분을 두고 달리 변명해 줄 것이 있을 수 없어요. 그 집안에 그런 것이 좀 있는지 어떤지 모르겠네요.”\n\n“무엇이요, 미친 것 말이에요?” 로바츠 부인이 아주 진지하게 말했다.\n\n“그래요, 그런 생각이 머릿속에 들어왔을 때 그분이 미쳐 있었던 것이 틀림없다고 생각하지 않아요? 그렇지만 패니는 그것을 믿지 않는군요. 그게 보여요. 그런데도 그것은 하늘만큼 참된 일이에요. 바로 여기, 이 자리에 서서 그분은 내가 그 사랑을 받아들일 때까지 끈질기게 매달리겠다고 말했어요. 무엇 때문에 내가 그분의 두 발이 다 저 구획 선 안에 들어 있다는 것을 유별나게 눈여겨보게 되었는지 모르겠어요.”\n\n“그런데 그 사랑을 받아들이지 않았어요?”\n\n“네, 나는 그것에 대해서 할 말이 없었어요. 보세요, 나는 여기 서서, 손을 가슴에 얹고 — 그분이 그렇게 하라고 시켰으니까요 — 그분을 사랑할 수 없다고 말했어요.”\n\n“그러고는요?”\n\n“그분은 가 버렸어요 — 마음이 부서진 사람 같은 낯을 하고요. 살아 있는 이 가운데 가장 딱한 넋이라고 말하면서 느릿느릿 슬며시 빠져나갔어요. 한동안은 나도 그 말을 믿었고, 거의 그분을 다시 불러 세울 수도 있었어요. 하지만 아니에요, 패니. 내가 지나치게 자랑스러워한다고, 아니면 내가 이겨 놓은 일을 두고 우쭐거린다고 생각하지 마세요. 그분은 문에 이르기도 전에 벗어난 것을 하느님께 감사드리고 있었어요.”\n\n“그것은 나는 믿지 않아요.”\n\n“하지만 나는 믿어요. 그리고 레이디 러프턴도 생각했어요. 그분이 나를 업신여기며 아들의 마음을 훔쳤다고 나를 몰아세우는 것을 내가 어떻게 견디겠어요? 이대로가 더 낫다는 것을 나는 알아요. 그렇지만 말해 주세요 — 거짓말은 언제나 잘못인가요, 아니면 목적이 수단을 옳게 만드는 일이 있을 수도 있나요? 나는 그분에게 진실을 말하고, 그분이 서 있던 땅에 입을 맞출 수도 있었다는 것을 알게 해 주어야 했나요?”\n\n이것은 신학자들에게 물을 물음이었으니 로바츠 부인이 스스로 나서서 답할 것이 아니었다. 그녀는 그 거짓말을 몰아세울 거리로 삼지도 않겠으나, 그것에 사죄를 선언해 주지도 않으려 하였다. 그 일에 관하여는 루시가 스스로 제 양심을 다스려야 했다. “그러면 나는 다음에 무엇을 해야 하나요?” 루시가 여전히 반은 비극이고 반은 비웃음인 어조로 말했다.\n\n“해야 한다니요?” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그래요, 무엇이든 해야 해요. 내가 사내라면 물론 스위스로 가겠지요. 아니면 사정이 나쁜 편이니 어쩌면 헝가리까지 갈지도 모르겠어요. 처녀들은 어떻게 하나요? 요즘은 죽지는 않는 모양이지요.”\n\n“루시, 나는 루시가 그분에게 티끌만큼도 마음을 쓴다고 믿지 않아요. 사랑에 빠졌다면 그것을 그런 식으로 말하지는 않을 거예요.”\n\n“그래요, 그래요. 그것이 내 단 하나뿐인 바람이에요. 패니에게 그것이 믿기지 않게 될 만큼 나 자신을 비웃을 수 있다면, 나도 차차 내가 그분에게 마음을 썼다는 것을 믿지 않게 되겠지요. 하지만 패니, 이건 아주 힘들어요. 내가 굶고, 날이 새기 전에 일어나고, 나 자신을 꼬집고, 아니면 무슨 지저분한 일을 한다면 — 냄비와 팬과 촛대를 닦는 일 같은 것이요. 그것이 가장 큰 효험이 있을 것 같아요. 굵은 베 한 조각을 얻어 두었는데, 그것을 지어 놓으면 입을 생각이에요.”\n\n“지금은 농을 하고 있네요, 루시. 나도 알아요.”\n\n“아니에요, 맹세코요. 내가 하는 말의 속뜻으로는 농이 아니에요. 피와 살을 거치지 않고서야 어떻게 내 마음에 손을 쓰겠어요?”\n\n“이런 괴로움을 견딜 힘을 하느님께서 주시기를 기도하지 않아요?”\n\n“하지만 기도를 어떻게 말로 지어야 하나요? 아니, 바라는 것조차 어떻게 말로 지어야 하나요? 나는 내가 저지른 잘못이 무엇인지를 몰라요. 대담하게 말하겠어요. 이 일에서 나는 내 잘못을 알아볼 수가 없어요. 나는 그저 내가 바보였다는 것을 알아냈을 뿐이에요.”\n\n이제 방 안은 아주 어두웠다. 적어도 새로 들어서는 사람에게라면 그러하였을 것이다. 두 사람은 눈이 그 어스름에 익숙해질 때까지 거기 남아 이야기하고 있었고, 갑자기 말발굽 소리에 방해받지 않았더라면 그대로 더 남아 있었을 것이다.\n\n“마크가 왔어요,” 패니가 벌떡 일어나 하인을 부르는 종 줄로 달려가며 말했다. 그가 들어설 때에 등불이 준비되어 있게 하려는 것이었다.\n\n“오늘 밤은 바체스터에 머무는 줄 알았는데요.”\n\n“나도 그렇게 알았어요. 하지만 확실하지 않을지도 모른다고 했어요. 저녁을 먹지 않았으면 어쩌지요?”\n\n남편이 집으로 돌아올 때 어진 아내의 마음에 언제나 가장 먼저 떠오르는 생각이 바로 그것이라고 나는 믿는다. 저녁은 먹었을까? 저녁으로 무엇을 내줄 수 있을까? 저녁이 마음에 들까? 아이고, 아이고! 집에는 찬 양고기밖에 없는데. 그러나 이번 경우에 이 댁의 주인은 저녁을 먹었고, 기분 좋음으로 환하게 빛나며 집에 돌아왔으니, 그 빛의 얼마쯤은 어쩌면 수석 사제의 클라레 덕이었다. “내가 그 사람들한테 말해 두었어,” 그가 말했다. “앞으로 두 달 동안 그 집을 그대로 차지하고 있어도 좋다고. 그쪽에서도 그렇게 하기로 했고.”\n\n“그건 아주 반가운 일이네요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그리고 훼손 배상을 두고서도 결국 그리 큰 말썽은 없을 것 같아.”\n\n“그건 참 기쁜 일이에요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. 그러나 그러면서도 그녀는 바체스터 구내의 그 집보다 루시를 훨씬 더 많이 생각하고 있었다.\n\n“나를 팔아넘기지는 않을 거지요,” 그날 밤 올케에게 헤어지는 입맞춤을 하면서 루시가 말했다.\n\n“그럼요. 루시가 허락을 주지 않는 한은요.”\n\n“아, 나는 결코 허락하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n# 제27장. 사우스 오들리 스트리트\n\n옴니엄 공작은 콜디코츠 저당 건을 어떻게든 정리하고 싶다는 뜻을 포더길 씨에게 통고해 두었고, 포더길 씨는 변호사의 온갖 군말을 다 붙여 지시서로 적어 놓은 것이나 다름없이 공작의 뜻을 알아들었다. 공작의 뜻은 이러했다. 콜디코츠를 쓸어 담아 곳간에 들이고, 개서럼 영지의 일부이자 한 몸으로 만들라는 것. 공작이 보기에 자기 벗과 던스터블 양 사이의 일은 지지부진한 듯했으니, 그렇다면 콜디코츠를 쓸어 담아 곳간에 들이는 것이 좋을 터였다. 게다가 주의 서부 지구에는 박솔 힐의 젊은 프랭크 그레셤이 콜디코츠 수렵림이라 불리는 왕실 소유지 전부를 사들이려고 정부와 교섭을 벌이고 있다는 소식이 들어와 있었다. 그 땅은 공작에게 먼저 제안되었으나 공작은 확답을 주지 않았다. 사워비 씨에게서 돈을 돌려받았더라면 그레셤 씨를 앞지를 수도 있었을 것이나 이제는 그럴 것 같지 않았고, 그래서 각하는 이쪽 땅이든 저쪽 땅이든 하나는 반드시 곳간에 들이겠다고 마음을 굳혔다. 그리하여 포더길 씨가 런던으로 올라왔고, 그리하여 사워비 씨는 몹시도 마음에 없는 걸음으로 포더길 씨와 사무적인 면담을 하지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 그러는 사이, 우리가 그를 마지막으로 본 뒤로, 사워비 씨는 자기 청혼에 던스터블 양이 준 대답을 누이동생에게서 들어 알고 있었고, 그쪽으로는 더 바랄 것이 없다는 것도 알고 있었다.\n\n그쪽에서 온전한 구원을 바랄 여지는 더 없었으나, 돈으로 도움을 주겠다는 제의는 있었다. 사워비 씨에게 공평하도록 말해 두자면, 그는 이제 던스터블 양에게서 그런 종류의 도움을 받는다는 것은 어림없는 일이라고 그 자리에서 딱 잘라 말했다. 그러나 누이동생은 그것이 한낱 사무상의 거래일 뿐이라고, 던스터블 양도 이자를 받을 것이라고, 게다가 공작은 백에 다섯을 받고 다른 채권자들은 백에 여섯, 일곱, 여덟, 열에 하늘만이 알 만큼 더 높은 것까지 받는데 그이가 백에 넷으로 만족해 준다면 모두에게 좋은 일이 아니겠느냐고 그에게 설명해 주었다. 그 자신도 포더길이 그러했듯이 공작의 전갈이 무슨 뜻인지 알고 있었다. 콜디코츠는 그 지방에 놓인 다른 좋은 땅이 수없이 그리되었듯이 쓸어 담겨 곳간에 들어갈 것이었다. 통째로 삼켜질 것이었고, 주인은 대대로 내려온 제 집 큰방에서 걸어 나가고, 사랑하던 오래된 숲을 떠나고, 갓난 시절부터 알아 온, 그리고 어른이 된 첫날부터 제 것이던 정원과 방목장과 즐거운 자리들을 남에게 온전히 내주게 될 것이었다.\n\n사내에게 이런 항복보다 더 쓴 것은 있을 수 없다. 이에 비하면, 제 손으로 벌어 모았을 뿐 실은 제 육신의 눈으로 본 적조차 없는 재물을 잃는 일이 무엇이겠는가? 그런 재물은 어떤 운으로 왔다가 다른 운으로 간다. 그것을 잃는 일은 그 사람이 벌이고 있는 승부의 한 부분이며, 이길 줄만 알고 질 줄을 모른다면 그는 딱하고 무르고 겁 많은 물건일 뿐이다. 그런 사내들은 대개 역경에도 그럭저럭 맞먹을 만한 마음가짐을 견딜 줄 안다. 그러나 대에서 대로 내려온 땅뙈기를 흥청망청 날려 버렸다는 것! 제 집안을 망친 사람이 바로 제 집안 사람이라는 것! 제 자식들과 손자들을 빛내 주었어야 할 것 전부를 제 아귀 속에 삼켜 버렸다는 것! 내가 보기에 이 세상의 불운이 그보다 더 멀리 갈 수는 도무지 없을 것 같다.\n\n사워비 씨는 그 무모함에도, 그리고 걸치고 쓰는 법을 그토록 잘 아는 그 겁 없는 유쾌함에도 아랑곳없이, 이 모든 것을 누구라도 느낄 수 있는 만큼 사무치게 느꼈다. 전적으로 제 잘못이었다. 그 땅뙈기는 온전히 제 것으로 그에게 왔는데, 이제 그가 죽기도 전에 그 한 조각 한 조각이 통째로 저 탐욕스러운 아귀 속으로 들어가게 될 것이었다. 공작은 그 땅을 담보로 잡힌 빚을 거의 다 사들여 두었으니, 이제는 그것을 깨끗이 쓸어 낼 수 있었다. 사워비는 포더길 씨에게서 그 전갈을 받았을 때 이것이 노려진 일임을 잘 알았다. 그리고 일단 자기가 콜디코츠의 사워비 씨이기를 그치는 날에는 서바셋주 의원으로 다시 뽑히기를 바랄 수 없다는 것도 잘 알았다. 그에게 이 세상은 다 끝난 것이 될 터였다. 그리고 이 세상이 자기에게는 다 끝났다고 헤아릴 때 그런 사내는 무엇을 느껴야 하겠는가?\n\n그날 아침 그는 여전히 밝은 얼굴을 지은 채 약속 자리로 갔다. 포더길 씨는 이런 일로 런던에 올라와 있을 때면 공작의 런던 법률 대리인인 검션 게이지비 상회의 집에 늘 방 하나를 쓸 수 있었고, 사워비 씨가 불려 간 곳도 바로 거기였다. 검션 게이지비 상회의 사무소는 사우스 오들리 스트리트에 있었다. 그런데 온 세상에 사워비 씨가 그 집 위층의 침침하고 꾀죄죄한 뒤쪽 응접실만큼 미워한 자리는 없었다고 해도 좋을 것이다. 그는 그곳에 아주 여러 번 가 보았으나, 갈 때마다 곤욕을 치르지 않은 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그것은 바로 이런 무서운 용도로 마련된 끔찍한 고문실이었고, 어쩌다 빚에 얽매인 딱한 시골 신사들의 기운을 마지막으로 꺾어 놓겠다는 뚜렷한 목적으로 가구를 들이고 벽지를 바르고 커튼을 달았음이 틀림없었다. 무엇이나 다 갈색빛 다홍이었다. 갈색이 되어 버린 다홍이었다. 햇빛, 진짜 따스한 해의 빛은 그 안까지 들어오는 법이 없었고, 촛불을 아무리 많이 켜도 그 갈색의 음침함을 밝힐 수는 없었다. 창은 한 번도 닦인 적이 없었다. 천장은 짙은 갈색이었다. 낡은 튀르크 융단은 먼지가 두껍게 앉은, 그것도 갈색인 융단이었다. 방 가운데 놓인 투박한 사무 탁자는 검은 가죽을 씌워 놓았던 것이나 이제는 그것도 갈색이었다. 난로 한쪽 벽감에는 꾀죄죄한 갈색 법률서가 가득한 책장이 있었으나 몇 해째 아무도 손대지 않았고, 난로 선반 위에는 그을음으로 검어진 낡은 법률 가계도 액자가 걸려 있었다. 파크 레인 가까운 사우스 오들리 스트리트의 검션 게이지비 상회 사무소에서 포더길 씨가 늘 쓰는 방이 이런 방이었다.\n\n나는 언젠가 이 방에 대한 이야기를 내 오랜 벗 하나에게서 들은 적이 있다. 그레셤스베리의 그레셤 씨, 곧 이제 콜디코츠 수렵림 가운데 왕실에 속한 부분을 사들이려는 프랭크 그레셤의 아버지 되는 이다. 그에게도 궂은 날이 있었다. 이제는 다행히 지나가 사라졌지만, 그 역시 저 방에 앉아 자기 재산을 마음대로 할 힘을 쥐고 그 힘을 쓸 작정인 사람들의 목소리를 들은 적이 있었다. 그가 내 마음에 남겨 놓은 그림은, 내가 아이였을 때 우돌포 성의 어느 방을 두고 품었던 그림과 거의 같았다. 그 우돌포의 방에는 의자가 하나 있어, 거기 앉은 사람은 팔다리가 하나씩 뽑혀 나가고, 머리는 이쪽으로 다리는 저쪽으로 끌려가고, 손에서는 손가락이 뜯겨 나가고, 턱에서는 이가 빠져 나가고, 머리에서는 머리털이, 뼈에서는 살이, 이음자리에서는 관절이 빠져 나가, 마침내 의자에 앉은 숨 없는 몸통 하나밖에 남지 않았다. 그레셤 씨는 나에게 말하기를 언제나 같은 자리에 앉았다고 했는데, 그렇게 앉아서 겪은 고문, 억지로 의논해야 했던 제 재산의 관절 빠짐, 억지로 지켜보아야 했던 제 몸 자체에 대한 수술은 내가 그 방을 우돌포의 방보다 더 나쁜 곳으로 여기게 만들었다. 그는 운 좋게도, 드문 행운의 예로서, 자기 뼈와 관절이 모두 다시 맞추어져 튼튼하게 살아나는 것을 볼 만큼 오래 살았다. 그러나 그 방을 이야기할 때면 언제나 소름이 돋지 않고서는 말하지 못했다.\n\n“이 세상 무엇을 놓고 따져 봐도,” 언젠가 그는 나에게 아주 엄숙하게 말했다. “하나도 없소, 내가 그 방에 다시 들어가게 만들 수 있는 것은.” 실로 이 감정이 그에게 어찌나 강했던지, 자기 형편이 돌아서던 날부터 그는 사우스 오들리 스트리트를 걸어 내려가는 일조차 하지 않았다. 그날 아침 사워비 씨는 이 고문실로 들어갔고, 거기서 이삼 분 뒤에 포더길 씨가 그와 자리를 같이했다.\n\n포더길 씨는 한 가지 점에서 그의 벗 사워비와 닮았다. 그는 전혀 다른 자리에서 전혀 다른 두 사람을 연기했다. 대체로 세상 사람들 앞에서는 먹고 마시기를 좋아하는, 흥이 많고 떠들썩하고 사람들이 좋아하는 사내였으며, 공작의 이익에 헌신한다고 알려져 있었고, 그 이익이 걸린 일에서는 다소 원칙이 없다거나 적어도 매섭다고들 여겨졌으나, 그 밖의 점에서는 사람 좋은 사내였다. 게다가 그가 언젠가 누구에게 이자도 물리지 않고 담보도 잡지 않고 돈을 빌려주었다는 소문까지 돌았다. 이번 자리에서 사워비는 그가 제 일에 딸린 온갖 재간과 부속물을 갖추고 왔음을 한눈에 알아보았다. 그는 짧고 빠른 걸음으로 방에 들어왔다. 오랜 벗과 악수를 하면서도 얼굴에 웃음이 없었다. 서류와 양피 문서를 잔뜩 담은 상자를 들고 왔고, 방에 들어선 지 채 일 분도 되지 않아 벌써 그 낡고 꾀죄죄한 의자 하나에 앉아 있었다.\n\n“런던에 온 지 얼마나 됐나, 포더길?” 사워비가 여전히 난로에 등을 대고 선 채로 말했다. 그가 마음을 굳혀 둔 것은 딱 하나였다. 무슨 일이 있어도 저 서류에 손을 대거나 눈을 주거나 귀를 기울이지는 않겠다는 것. 그런 짓에서 좋은 것이 나오지 않는다는 것쯤은 잘 알고 있었다. 그에게도 제 변호사가 있어서, 규칙에 맞게 털리고 있는지 지켜보게 해 두었다.\n\n“얼마나 됐냐고요? 그저께부터요. 내 평생 이렇게 바빴던 적이 없어요. 공작께서는 언제나처럼 무엇이든 한꺼번에 다 되기를 바라시니까요.”\n\n“내가 각하께 빚진 것을 한꺼번에 다 갚기를 바라신다면, 셈이 어긋나실 걸세.”\n\n“아, 그렇군요. 당신이 곧바로 본론으로 들어올 마음이라 반갑습니다. 그게 언제나 제일 좋으니까요. 이리 와서 앉지 않겠어요?”\n\n“고맙지만 사양하겠네. 서 있겠어.”\n\n“하지만 이 숫자들은 함께 훑어야 할 텐데요.”\n\n“숫자는 하나도 안 볼 걸세, 포더길. 그게 무슨 소용인가? 내게도 소용없고, 내가 보기에 자네에게도 소용없어. 뭐 잘못된 것이 있으면 포터네 사람들이 찾아낼 테지. 공작이 원하는 게 뭔가?”\n\n“음, 사실대로 말하자면, 자기 돈을 원하십니다.”\n\n“한 가지 뜻으로는, 그것도 가장 중요한 뜻으로는, 이미 갖고 계시지. 이자는 어김없이 받고 계시지 않은가?”\n\n“요즘 시절이 어떤지를 보면 그 점은 그럭저럭 괜찮지요. 하지만 사워비, 그건 말이 안 되는 얘기예요. 당신도 나만큼 공작을 잘 알고, 각하가 무엇을 원하시는지도 아주 잘 알잖아요. 각하는 당신에게 시간을 주셨어요. 당신이 돈을 마련하려고 무슨 걸음이라도 내디뎠더라면 그 땅을 지킬 수도 있었을 겁니다.”\n\n“십팔만 파운드라니! 그걸 마련하려고 내가 무슨 걸음을 내디딜 수 있었겠나? 어음이나 한 장 띄워서 토저더러 시티에 가져가 현금으로 바꿔 오게 하면 됐겠나!”\n\n“우리는 당신이 혼인하시려는 줄 알았지요.”\n\n“그건 다 틀어졌네.”\n\n“그렇다면 각하가 제 것을 찾으려 하시는 것을 두고 당신이 각하를 책망할 수는 없다고 봅니다. 그만큼 큰 돈이 더 오래 묶여 있는 것은 각하 형편에 맞지 않아요. 보시다시피 각하는 땅을 원하시고, 또 손에 넣으실 겁니다. 당신이 빚진 것을 갚아 놓았더라면 각하가 그 왕실 땅을 사셨을 텐데, 이제는 젊은 그레셤이 맞서 값을 불러 그 땅을 차지하게 되었다지요. 이 일로 각하의 심사가 뒤틀리셨습니다. 그러니 이쯤은 솔직히 말해 두는 게 좋겠는데, 각하는 돈이든 땅이든 어느 한쪽은 반드시 받아 내겠다고 작정하셨어요.”\n\n“그러니까 내가 내 땅에서 쫓겨난다는 말이군.”\n\n“그렇지요, 뭐. 당신이 그렇게 부르고 싶다면요. 내가 받은 지시는 당장 저당권을 실행하라는 것입니다.”\n\n“그렇다면 공작이 나를 유별나게 못되게 다루고 있다고 말할 수밖에 없네.”\n\n“글쎄요, 사워비, 나는 그렇게 보이지 않는데요.”\n\n“나는 그렇게 보이네. 각하는 제 돈을 시계처럼 어김없이 받아 갔어. 그런데도 이자만 들어가면 나를 조금도 건드리지 않았을 사람들한테서 그 빚들을 사들였지.”\n\n“의석은 차지하셨잖아요?”\n\n“의석! 그러니 내가 그 값을 치러야 한다는 말인가?”\n\n“누가 당신더러 그 값을 치르라고 하는 것 같지는 않은데요. 당신은 내가 아는 다른 많은 사람들과 똑같아요. 케이크를 먹어 놓고도 그대로 갖고 있기를 바라지요. 지난 이십 년 동안 그것을 먹어 왔으면서, 이제 각하가 자기 차례를 찾겠다고 하니 스스로 몹시 부당한 대우를 받는다고 생각하는군요.”\n\n“각하가 나를 팔아 넘긴다면 나는 정말 몹시 부당한 대우를 받았다고 생각할 걸세. 부당한 대우보다 더한 것이지. 험한 말을 쓰고 싶지는 않으나, 그건 부당한 대우 이상일 거야. 각하가 정말로 나를 그런 식으로 다룰 뜻이라는 것을 나는 도무지 믿을 수가 없네.”\n\n“자기 돈을 찾겠다고 하는 것이 그렇게 가혹한 일이랍니까!”\n\n“각하가 원하는 것은 자기 돈이 아니야. 내 땅이지.”\n\n“그러면 각하가 그 값을 치르지 않으셨습니까? 당신은 당신 땅의 값을 이미 받지 않았습니까? 이보세요, 사워비, 당신이 화를 내 봐야 아무 소용없어요. 지난 삼 년 동안 무엇이 당신에게 닥쳐오고 있는지 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있었잖아요. 각하가 아무 속셈도 없이 당신에게 돈을 빌려주셨을 리가 있습니까? 물론 각하께는 각하의 뜻이 있지요. 하지만 이 말은 하겠어요. 각하는 당신을 재촉하지 않으셨습니다. 그리고 당신이 그 자리를 지키기 위해 무엇이든 할 수 있었다면 할 수도 있었을 겁니다. 주위를 둘러볼 시간은 넉넉히 있었어요.”\n\n사워비는 처음에 자리를 잡은 그 자리에 여전히 서 있었고, 이제 한동안 말이 없었다. 얼굴은 몹시 굳어 있었으며, 그 낯빛에는 젊은 벗들에게 그토록 강하게 먹히던 그 사람 끄는 표정, 러프턴 경을 낚았고 마크 로바츠를 홀렸던 그 표정이 하나도 없었다. 세상이 그에게 등을 돌리고 있었고, 그를 둘러싼 것들이 끝을 향해 가고 있었다. 그는 자기가 참으로 케이크를 먹어 버렸으며, 이제는 제 머리에 총알을 박기로 마음먹지 않는 한 남은 일이 별로 없다는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다. 그는 러프턴 경에게, 사내의 등은 제가 스스로 얹는 어떤 짐에도 넉넉할 만큼 넓어야 한다고 말한 적이 있었다. 이제 자기 등이 이 짐에 넉넉할 만큼 넓고 튼튼하다고 뻐길 수 있을까? 그러나 그 쓰라린 순간에도 그에게는 여전히 사내다워야 마땅하다는 굳은 기억이 있었다. 마지막 파멸이 그에게 닥쳐오고 있었고, 그는 이내 함께 살아온 이들의 앎과 기억에서 쓸려 나갈 것이었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 끝까지 몸가짐을 훌륭히 지킬 것이었다. 제 자리를 제가 깔아 놓은 것이 사실이었으니, 그 위에 누우라고 하는 정의를 그도 알아들었다.\n\n그러는 동안 줄곧 포더길은 서류를 만지며 시간을 보냈다. 돈에 관한 고려와 계산에 깊이 잠긴 사람처럼 한 장 한 장 넘겨 갔다. 그러나 실은 그 내내 한 마디도 읽지 않았다. 거기에 그가 읽을 것은 아무것도 없었다. 이런 일에서 읽고 쓰고 셈하는 것은 아랫사람들이 한다. 포더길 씨 같은 큰 인물이 하는 것이 아니다. 그의 일은 사워비에게 이제 나가야 한다고 말해 주는 것이었다. 거기 있는 기록이란 것들은 아주 쓸데가 없었다. 공작에게 힘이 있었고, 공작에게 힘이 있다는 것을 사워비도 알았으며, 포더길의 일은 공작이 그 힘을 쓸 뜻이라는 것을 설명해 주는 것이었다. 그는 이 일에 익숙했으므로, 제가 그러고 있는 것이 무엇보다 중대한 일인 양 서류를 계속 넘기며 읽는 척했다.\n\n“내가 직접 공작을 만나겠네.” 사워비 씨가 마침내 말했다. 그 목소리에는 어딘가 거의 무서운 것이 있었다.\n\n“공작께서는 이런 종류의 일로는 당신을 만나지 않으실 거라는 걸 아시잖아요. 각하는 돈 이야기를 누구와도 하지 않으십니다. 그건 당신도 나만큼 잘 알잖아요.”\n\n“빌어먹을 ——, 그래도 그는 나와 말을 하게 될 걸세. 돈 이야기를 누구와도 하지 않는다니! 그토록 돈을 아끼면서 왜 그 이야기를 하는 것은 부끄러워하나? 그는 나를 만나게 될 걸세.”\n\n“나는 더 할 말이 없습니다, 사워비. 물론 각하께 당신을 만나 달라고 청하지는 않겠어요. 그리고 당신이 억지로 밀고 들어간다면 무슨 일이 일어날지는 아시겠지요. 각하가 당신에게 등을 돌리시게 되더라도 그건 내가 한 일이 아닙니다. 당신이 그런 식으로 내게 하는 말은 무엇이든, 누가 무슨 말을 하든, 내 밖으로 나가지 않아요.”\n\n“이 일은 내 변호사를 통해 처리하겠네.” 사워비는 그렇게 말하고 모자를 집어 들었고, 한 마디도 더 하지 않고 방을 나갔다.\n\n마지막에 악하였다고 판정받을 이들이 떨어지게 될 그 영원한 벌이 어떤 것인지 우리는 알지 못한다. 그러나 내 생각으로는 제 손으로 불러들인 파멸의 기억보다 더 무서운 형벌은 지어낼 수 없을 것 같다. 달릴 길은 다 달렸고 그것을 온전히 잃어버렸다는 것을, 마지막 기회가 가 버렸고 그것도 헛되이 가 버렸다는 것을, 끝이 왔으며 그와 함께 창피와 멸시와 자기 조롱이 왔다는 것을, 결코 씻을 수 없는 창피와 결코 벗을 수 없는 멸시와 영원히 제 창자를 파먹을 자기 조롱이 왔다는 것을 날마다 날마다 기억하는 것보다 더한 비참이 무엇이겠는가?\n\n사워비 씨는 이제 쉰이었다. 그는 살면서 기회를 누렸다. 그리고 사우스 오들리 스트리트를 되짚어 올라가며 그는 그 기회들을 어떻게 써 왔는지 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 어른이 되던 무렵에 훌륭한 땅을 손에 넣었다. 남들보다 나은 머리의 재간을 타고났다. 한 번도 어긋난 적 없는 건강을 받았고, 선과 악을 가리는 데 꽤나 밝은 눈도 받았다. 그런데 이제 그는 자신을 어떤 처지에 몰아넣었는가!\n\n그런데 저 포더길이라는 사람이 이 모든 것을 그토록 무섭도록 밝은 빛 아래 그의 앞에 늘어놓았던 것이다! 그가 마지막으로 헐려 나갈 날이 닥친 이제, 그가 헐려야 한다는 것, 눈에서도 마음에서도 사라지도록 단번에 처치되어야 한다는 것은 어떤 애매한 말로도 부드러워지지 않았고, 이를테면 반쯤 가려지지도 않았다. “당신은 케이크를 가졌고, 그것을 먹었어요. 그것도 걸신들려 먹었지요. 그만하면 넉넉하지 않습니까? 케이크를 두 번 먹으려고요? 케이크가 줄줄이 이어져 나오기를 바라나요? 아니지요, 이보세요. 걸신들려 먹는 자에게는 이 케이크가 줄줄이 이어져 나오는 일이 없습니다. 당신의 제안은 온당하지 않고, 채찍을 쥔 쪽인 우리는 그것을 들어 줄 생각이 없어요. 부디 사라져 주시지요. 조용히 거름 더미로 쓸려 들어가도록 몸을 맡기세요. 당신에게 값이 나가는 것이라고는 죄다 당신을 떠나 버렸습니다. 그러니 이런 말씀을 드리게 해 주세요. 당신은 이제, 쓰레기입니다.” 그러고 나면 무자비한 빗자루가 뿌리칠 수 없는 기세로 다가와, 쓰레기는 구덩이 속으로 쓸려 들어가 거기서 영원히 눈앞에서 감추어지는 것이다.\n\n그리고 애석한 것은 이것이다. 사람이 제 탐욕만 눌러 둔다면 케이크를 먹고도 그것을 그대로 가질 수 있다는 것. 그렇다, 그리고 그렇게 하면 걸신들린 아귀로 제 별미를 한꺼번에 삼켜 버리는 자보다 그 케이크의 맛을 두 배로 더 누린다는 것. 이 세상의 케이크는 먹으면 먹을수록 자란다. 먹는 자가 지나치게 탐욕스럽지만 않다면. 이 모든 지혜를 두고 사워비 씨는 검션 게이지비 상회의 건물을 떠나 걸으면서 슬픈 가슴과 몹시 우울한 마음으로 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n그의 뜻은 포더길 씨와 헤어진 뒤에 하원으로 내려가는 것이었으나, 당장 닥친 파멸의 앞날이 그에게 너무 무거웠고, 자기가 지금 곧바로 사람들 모이는 자리에 얼굴을 내밀 만한 상태가 아니라는 것을 그는 알았다. 그리고 그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 바체스터로 내려갈 뜻도 있었다. 몇 시간만 다녀올 생각이었는데, 로바츠가 자기를 위해 인수해 준 그 어음에 관해 손을 더 써 두기 위해서였다. 그 어음, 두 번째 것이 이제 만기가 되었고, 토저 씨가 그를 찾아왔던 것이다.\n\n“이래 봐야 세상에 아무 쓸데도 없습니다요, 사워비 씨.” 토저는 그렇게 말했다. “그 종이는 나한테 없고, 두 시간도 손에 쥐고 있지 않았습니다요. 그건 톰 토저를 거쳐 나가 버렸어요. 그건 사워비 씨도 나만큼이나 잘 아시잖습니까요.”\n\n그런데 토저, 곧 사워비 씨의 토저가 톰 토저를 입에 올릴 때면, 사워비 씨는 일곱 귀신이 불려 나오고 있다는 것을, 그 하나하나가 첫 귀신보다 더 악하다는 것을 알았다. 사워비 씨는 자기가 꾀어 화 속으로 끌어들인 그 딱한 목사에게 참된 마음 같은 것, 아니 오히려 애정 같은 것을 정말로 느끼고 있었고, 될 수 있다면 그를 토저의 독니에서 건져 주고 싶었다. 바체스터 은행의 포레스트 씨가 로바츠 씨를 위해 그 마지막 오백 파운드 어음을 떠맡아 줄 것 같았다. 다만 자기, 곧 사워비가 내려가서 그 일이 제대로 되는지 보아야 할 필요는 있었다. 다른 어음, 곧 앞의 것이자 액수가 더 적은 것에 대해서는, 그것은 토저 씨가 한동안 잠자코 있어 줄 듯했다.\n\n이 이틀에 대한 사워비의 계획은 그러했다. 그러나 이제, 이제 그가 로바츠를, 또는 다른 어떤 사람을 마음에 둘 여지가 무엇이 더 남아 있단 말인가. 당장 거름 더미로 쓸려 들어갈 처지인 그가?\n\n이런 마음가짐으로 그는 사우스 오들리 스트리트를 걸어 올라가, 그로스베너 광장의 한쪽을 건너, 거의 기계처럼 그린 스트리트로 들어섰다. 그린 스트리트의 저쪽 끝, 파크 레인 가까이에 해럴드 스미스 씨 부부가 살고 있었다.\n\n# 제28장. 손 선생\n\n던스터블 양이 개서럼 성에서 벗들인 그레셤네 — 젊은 프랭크 그레셤과 그 아내 — 를 만났을 때, 그녀는 곧바로 어떤 손 선생의 안부를 물었다. 그레셤 부인의 삼촌이었다. 손 선생은 늙은 독신 남자였는데, 던스터블 양은 사람으로서나 의사로서나 그를 크게 믿는 편이었다. 그렇다고 제 몸의 병을 손 선생에게 맡겨 고친 적이 있는 것은 아니었다 — 그 일에는 따로 제 의사인 이지먼 선생을 두고 있었으니 — 게다가 어떤 의사의 손길이든 필요할 만한 몸의 병을 그녀가 앓는 일은 좀처럼 없었다고 해도 좋다. 그러나 그녀는 벗들 사이에서 손 선생을 늘 놀라운 학식과 판단력을 지닌 사람이라고 말했고, 한두 번은 아주 중대한 일에서 그의 조언을 구해 그대로 따르기도 했다. 손 선생은 런던 세상에 익숙한 사람이 아니었다. 거기에 집을 두지도 않았고, 수도에 오는 일조차 드물었다. 그러나 던스터블 양은 그가 사는 그레셤스베리에서 그를 알게 되었고, 지난 몇 달 사이에 두 사람 사이에는 상당한 친밀함이 자라 있었다. 그는 지금 조카딸인 그레셤 부인의 집에 머무는 중이었다. 그러나 그가 올라온 가장 큰 이유는 그러기를 바란다고 던스터블 양이 말한 데 있었다. 그녀는 그의 조언을 원했고, 조카딸의 부추김에 그는 런던에 와서 그 조언을 주었다.\n\n이렇게 시골 환자들의 병상에서, 그중에도 조카딸이 그 아들과 혼인한 레이디 애러벨라 그레셤의 병상에서 손 선생을 불러올린 그 특별한 용무는, 어떤 큰 금전상의 이해에 관한 것이었다. 그런 일이라면 손 선생의 조언이 유별나게 값나가지는 않으리라고 여길 만도 했다. 그는 제 일로도 그런 문제에 밝았던 적이 없었고, 주식 시장의 사정도 땅값도 아는 바가 없었다. 그러나 던스터블 양은 제 뜻대로 하는 데 익숙한 여인이었고, 그 뜻에 마땅한 이유를 대라는 요구도 받지 않은 채 하고 싶은 대로 받아들여지는 데 익숙한 여인이었다.\n\n“이봐요,” 그녀는 젊은 그레셤 부인에게 말했다. “내가 이렇게까지 청하는데도 그 삼촌이 지금 런던에 올라오지 않는다면, 나는 그이를 곰이고 야만인이라고 여길 거예요. 그리고 두고 봐요, 다시는 그이한테 말을 붙이지 않을 테니 — 프랭크한테도 — 당신한테도. 그러니 알아서 챙기시는 게 좋아요.” 그레셤 부인이 벗의 그 위협을 위협하는 그대로 다 받아들이지는 않았을 것이다. 던스터블 양은 늘 말을 세게 했고, 그녀를 잘 아는 사람들은 그녀가 언제 비유를 빌려 제 생각을 말하는지를 대개 알아들었다. 이번에도 그 말을 죄다 진심으로 한 것은 아니었다. 그러나 그러면서도 그레셤 부인은 딱한 선생을 런던으로 데려오는 데 거센 영향력을 썼다.\n\n“게다가,” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “나는 내 콘베르사치오네에 그 선생을 꼭 모시기로 마음을 굳혔어요. 그이가 제 발로 오지 않겠다면 내가 내려가서 데려올 거예요. 내 다정한 벗 프라우디 부인의 으뜸패를 눌러 버리는 데에 마음을 다 걸어 놓았거든요. 그러니 나는 모두를 다 불러 모을 작정이에요!”\n\n이 모든 일의 결말은, 선생이 정말로 도성에 올라와 포트먼 광장에 있는 조카딸의 집에서 한 주의 거의 전부를 머물렀다는 것이다 — 사흘만 소홀히 대접받으면 죽을 줄로 아는 레이디 애러벨라에게는 크나큰 역겨움이었다. 그 용무에 관해서라면, 그가 큰 도움이 되었으리라고 나는 의심하지 않는다. 그는 상식과 정직한 뜻을 지니고 있었다. 그리고 나는 그 둘이 흔히 상당한 양의 세상 경험에 넉넉히 맞설 저울추가 된다고 생각하는 편이다. 세상 경험까지 아울러 가질 수 있다면—! 옳은 말이다! 그러나 모든 것을 다 얻기란 그렇게 어려운 일이다. 그러나 그 특별한 용무에 우리가 더 마음 쓸 필요는 없다. 그것은 이야기되고 마무리되었다고 여기기로 하고, 이제 던스터블 양의 콘베르사치오네에 갈 옷을 차려입기로 하자.\n\n그러나 그녀가 프라우디 부인에게서 이 자리에 쓰려고 급히 빌려 온 이름으로 제 모임을 드러내어 부를 만큼 재간이 없었다고 여기지는 말아야 한다. 이 작은 농담을 즐긴 것은 해럴드 스미스 부인과 그 밖의 몇십 명뿐인, 특별히 가까운 벗들 사이에서였을 뿐이다. 이번에 벗들을 제 집으로 부르는 초대장에는 조금도 뻐기는 데가 없었다. 그녀는 그저 이달 ——일 목요일 저녁 아홉 시가 지난 뒤 편한 대로 되도록 일찍 와 주면 기쁘겠다고, 여느 투로 알렸을 뿐이었다. 그러나 온 세상이 알고 있었다 — 그날 밤 온 세상이 던스터블 양의 집에 다 모이게 되어 있다는 것을, 그리고 온갖 계층의 사람들을, 신들과 거인들을, 성인과 죄인들을, 도덕이 너무 굳세어 광기에 이른 이들 — 우리의 다정한 벗 레이디 러프턴 같은 이들 — 과 반대쪽으로 광기에 이른 이들 — 레이디 하틀탑, 옴니엄 공작, 사워비 씨 같은 이들 — 을 한자리에 불러 모으려는 시도가 있을 것을. 동방에서는 정교회 순교자 하나를 붙잡아 왔고, 물 건너 저편에서는 기름 반드르르한 오늘날의 성 바울로를 데려왔다 — 이 자리에 있으려고 플럼스테드에서 그 먼 길을 올라온 그랜틀리 부주교에게는 소름 끼치고 어이없는 일이었다. 그랜틀리 부인도 거기 가고 싶어 좀이 쑤셨다. 그러나 오늘날의 성 바울로가 온다는 말을 듣고서는, 저를 데려가겠다는 말을 한마디도 하지 않은 남편에게 소리 높여 승리를 뽐냈다. 브록 경과 드 테리어 경이 그 모임에 온다는 것은 아무것도 아니었다. 신들의 유쾌한 왕과 거인들의 정중한 우두머리야 어느 집에서든 서로 더없이 기쁘게 악수를 나눌 수 있다. 그러나 서로를 두고는 고개나 주먹밖에는 흔들 것이 없는 사내들이 만나게 되어 있었다. 서플하우스가 올 것이고, 여자의 미움을 — 아니 정치인의 미움까지도 — 넘어서는 미움으로 이제 제 원수를 미워하는 해럴드 스미스도 올 것이었다. 하급 신들은 지금의 유배 처지에 몹시 씁쓸해하며 한 방에 모여들 것이고, 하급 거인들은 또 다른 방에서 승리에 무섭게 떠들어 댈 것이라고들 생각했다. 그것이 거인들의 흠이다. 그것만 빼면 나쁜 자들도 아닌데, 그들은 잠시의 성공이 주는 무게를 도무지 견딜 줄 모른다. 올림포스를 노리고 오를 때에는 — 그렇게 노리는 일이야말로 의심할 것 없이 그들의 타고난 처지인데 — 발가락과 손가락을 다 부지런히 놀려 긁고 기어오르는데, 그 모양에는 사람 좋은 악의와 자족스러운 근면이 섞여 있어 어느 편에게나 흐뭇하다. 그러나 그 애씀이 뜻밖에도, 그들 자신에게는 불행하게도 이루어져 버리면, 그들은 어찌할 바를 몰라 거인다운 체통조차 지킬 힘을 잃어버린다.\n\n던스터블 양의 집에 있을 그 모임이란 그토록 크고 그토록 갖가지였다. 그녀 자신은 웃어 넘기며 스스로를 놀렸다 — 해럴드 스미스 부인에게는 이 일을 대단한 우스개인 양 말하고, 프라우디 부인에게는 글로스터 플레이스의 그 세상에 이름난 모임들을 그저 흉내 내어 겨루어 보려는 것인 양 말했다. 그러나 도성 사람들은 대체로 무언가 큰 애를 쓰고 있다는 것을 알았고, 던스터블 양조차 다소 조바심을 치고 있으리라고들 짐작했다. 그렇게 훌륭하게 농담을 하면서도 실패하면 마음 아파할 것이라고 여겨진 것이다.\n\n프랭크 그레셤 부인에게는 그녀도 얼마쯤 진지하게 말했다. 서플하우스 씨의 그 대단한 동료 가운데 한 사람이 올지 오지 않을지 마음을 놓을 수 없다고, 그리고 그 놓이지 않는 마음이 괴롭다고 던스터블 양이 실토했을 때, 그 부인은 이렇게 말했다. “그렇지만 대체 무엇 때문에 그런 수고를 다 하시는 거예요? 수백 명이 오는 데다 온갖 빛깔의 큰 가발이며 작은 가발이 다 온다는데, 타워스 씨가 오든 오지 않든 그게 무슨 상관이에요?”\n\n그러나 던스터블 양은 거의 비명을 지르듯 대답했다. “이봐요, 그이가 없으면 아무것도 아니에요. 당신은 모르시는 거예요. 하지만 사실이 그러답니다 — 지금은 톰 타워스가 모든 사람이고 모든 것이에요.”\n\n그러자 그레셤 부인은, 이번이 처음도 아니건만, 벗의 허영을 두고 훈계를 시작했다. 그 훈계에 던스터블 양은 알쏭달쏭하게 이런 뜻을 비쳤다 — 이번 한 번만 마음껏 하도록 놓아준다면, 온 세상이 이번만 저를 받아 준다면, 자기는 — 무엇을 하겠다는 것인지는 끝까지 말하지 않았으나, 그레셤 부인이 끌어낸 뜻은 이러했다. 지금 유행의 제단에 올리는 이 향이 받아들여진다면, 던스터블 양은 이 사악한 세상의 허식과 허영을, 육신의 온갖 죄된 욕정을 당장 버리겠다는 것이었다.\n\n“이봐요, 그래도 그 선생은 남아 주시는 거죠? 그것만은 정해진 일로 여겨도 좋겠지요.”\n\n선생의 시간을 이렇게 청하면서 던스터블 양이 보인 열심은, 톰 타워스가 빠지지 않게 해 달라고 신들에게 빌 때의 열심에 조금도 못하지 않았다. 사실을 말하자면 손 선생은 처음에는 저녁 모임에 나오라고 런던에 붙들려 있으라는 것이 아주 터무니없는 청이라고 여겨 한동안 완강히 거절했다. 그러나 총리가 서너 명 온다는 것을, 그리고 톰 타워스까지도 몸소 그 자리에 있을 수 있다는 것을 알게 되자 그의 초연함도 힘을 잃었고, 그는 레이디 애러벨라에게 편지를 써서 이틀 더 자리를 비우는 것을 참아 주셔야 하겠다고, 아침저녁으로 순한 강장제는 그대로 이어도 좋다고 알렸다.\n\n그런데 던스터블 양은 왜 그 대단한 자리에 손 선생이 꼭 있어야 한다고 그렇게 애를 태웠을까? 정말이지 왜 그리 자주 그를 시골 진료에서, 조제대에서, 시골 병자들을 돌보는 요긴한 손길에서 불러내려 했을까? 선생은 그녀와 아무런 혈연의 끈으로도 이어져 있지 않았다. 그토록 가까웠어도 두 사람의 벗함은 아직 날수가 짧았다. 그녀는 온갖 조언과 좋은 훈수를 다 사들일 수 있는 아주 부유한 여자였고, 그는 부유하기는커녕 진료가 오래 흐트러지면 형편이 곤란해질 사람이었다. 그런데도 던스터블 양은 그의 시간을 불러 쓰는 데에, 그가 제 오라비라도 되었을 때보다 조금도 더 거리끼는 바가 없어 보였다. 이런 생각은 선생 자신에게는 하나도 떠오르지 않았다. 그는 마음이 단순한 사람이어서 일을 오는 대로 받아들였고, 즐겁게 오는 일은 더욱 그렇게 받아들였다. 그는 던스터블 양이 마음에 들었고 그 벗함이 고마웠으며, 그녀에게 저를 수고롭게 하고 불편하게 할 권리가 있는지를 스스로 물어볼 생각도 하지 않았다. 그러나 선생의 조카딸인 그레셤 부인에게는 그런 생각이 떠올랐다. 던스터블 양에게 무슨 뜻이 있는 것일까, 있다면 무슨 뜻일까? 그저 선생을 우러르는 마음일까, 아니면 변덕일까? 괴벽일까 — 아니면 혹시 사랑일 수도 있을까?\n\n이 두 벗의 나이를 말하자면, 어림잡아 여자는 마흔을 훌쩍 넘었고 남자는 쉰을 훌쩍 넘었다고 할 수 있다. 그런 형편에 사랑일 수가 있을까? 게다가 그 여자는 청혼을 거의 다발로 받아 온 사람이었다 — 문벌 있는 남자에게서, 유행을 아는 남자에게서, 권세 있는 남자에게서, 몸가짐이 보기 좋고 태도가 사근사근하며 취미가 다듬어졌고 말솜씨가 좋은 남자에게서. 그녀는 그런 이를 하나도 사랑하지 않았을 뿐 아니라, 그런 이들 가운데 누구에게도 저가 그를 사랑할 수 있으리라는 생각으로 구슬려지지 않았다. 손 선생의 취미가 다듬어졌고 그 태도가 사근사근하다는 것은 그를 귀하게 여기는 시골의 오랜 벗 서너 사람이야 인정할 법했다. 그러나 던스터블 양이 익숙한 세상, 날이 갈수록 그녀에게 더 소중해지는 듯한 그 런던의 세상은, 선생을 여자의 연정의 대상이 될 만한 남자로 보아 주지 않았을 것이다.\n\n그러면서도 그레셤 부인에게는 그 생각이 떠올랐다. 그녀는 이 시골 의사의 팔꿈치 옆에서 자랐다. 그의 딸이나 되는 것처럼 그와 함께 살았다. 여러 해 동안 그 집안을 돌보는 천사였고, 여인으로서 자연스러운 사랑에 마음이 열리기까지 그녀의 가장 깊은 마음은 다 그에게 있었다. 그녀의 눈에 선생은 흠이라 할 것이 거의 없는 사람이었다. 그러니 던스터블 양이 삼촌에게 마음을 두었다는 것이 그녀에게는 있을 수 없는 일로 보이지 않았다.\n\n던스터블 양은 언젠가 해럴드 스미스 부인에게, 자기가 혼인을 할 수도 있다고 말한 적이 있었다. 그때 내놓은 조건이란 오직 하나, 뽑히는 사람은 돈에 아주 무심한 이여야 한다는 것이었다. 벗들 사이에서 세상을 그만하면 정확히 안다고 여겨지던 해럴드 스미스 부인은, 그런 남자를 던스터블 양은 이 세상에서 결코 찾지 못할 것이라고 대꾸했다. 이 모든 말은 해럴드 스미스 부인 같은 벗과 이야기할 때 던스터블 양이 으레 쓰는 그 반쯤 우스개 섞인 익살 투로 오간 것이었다. 그러나 그녀는 같은 뜻의 말을 그레셤 부인에게도 한 번 이상 했다. 그리고 그레셤 부인은 여자들이 흔히 하듯 둘에 둘을 놓아 그 작은 셈의 답을 넷으로 냈고, 그 셈의 마지막 결론으로, 손 선생이 청하기만 하면 던스터블 양은 손 선생과 혼인하겠다고 마음을 정했다.\n\n그러자 그레셤 부인은 또 다른 두 가지 물음을 스스로 헤아려 보기 시작했다. 삼촌이 던스터블 양과 혼인하는 것이 좋은 일일까? 그렇다면 그가 그런 청혼을 하도록 이끄는 일이 될 법한 일일까? 찬반의 여러 이유를 살피고 갖가지 논거의 무게를 견주어 본 뒤, 그레셤 부인은 대체로 이 일이 나쁜 짜임은 아닐 듯하다고 생각했다. 던스터블 양에게 그녀 자신도 참된 정을 품고 있었고, 그 정은 남편도 함께 나누는 것이었다. 그녀는 던스터블 양이 세상에 바치는 희생을 여러 번 슬퍼하며, 벗이 허영과 무심함과 좋지 못한 생활 방식에 빠져 든다고 여겼다. 그러나 이런 혼인이라면 그 모든 것을 낫게 해 줄 것이었다. 그리고 손 선생 자신을 두고 말하면 — 이 일에서 그레셤 부인의 가장 절실한 생각이 향한 곳은 물론 그의 좋음이었는데 — 그가 홀몸으로 있기보다 혼인하는 편이 더 행복하겠다고 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 성미로 말하면 던스터블 양보다 높이 설 여자가 없었다. 그녀가 언짢은 기색을 보였다는 말을 들은 사람이 아무도 없었다. 또 그레셤 부인은 돈에 팔릴 마음과는 아주 거리가 먼 마음을 타고났으나, 신부의 재산에서 얼마간 이로움이 생기지 않을 수 없다고 느끼지 않을 수는 없었다. 지금은 프랭크 그레셤 부인인 메리 손도 스스로 큰 재산을 물려받은 사람이었다. 형편이 그녀의 손에 막대한 재산을 얹어 주었고, 행복과 부는 함께 갈 수 없다고 믿게 하려는 그 교훈이 참임을 그녀는 여태 겪어 알지 못했다. 그래서 그녀는 선생과 던스터블 양을 맺어 주는 것이 잘하는 일이겠다고 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n그러나 선생이 그런 청혼을 하도록 이끌 수 있을까? 그 편에서 이 일을 바라보면 무서운 어려움이 있다는 것을 그레셤 부인은 인정했다. 삼촌은 던스터블 양을 좋아했다. 그러나 그런 혼인이라는 생각이 그의 머리에 든 적은 한 번도 없다고 그녀는 확신했다. 그런 생각을 지어내 넣기가 매우 어려울 것이며 — 거의 있을 수 없는 일일 것이며 — 그 생각이 들어앉았다 하더라도 선생이 그 말을 꺼내도록 부추기기는 힘들 것이었다. 일을 통틀어 바라보면서 그녀는 이 혼사가 될 일이 아닐까 걱정했다.\n\n던스터블 양의 모임이 있는 날, 그레셤 부인과 삼촌은 포트먼 광장에서 둘이서만 저녁을 먹었다. 그레셤 씨는 아직 의회에 들어가지 않았으나 그 주의 자기 지구에 곧 결원이 생길 것으로 보였고, 그를 상대로 나서서 이길 가망이 있는 사람은 아무도 없다는 것이 알려져 있었다. 이 때문에 그는 자기 당의 정치인들 — 곧 그가 밀어 주어야 할 그 거인들 — 사이에 자주 끼게 되었고, 그래서 요즘은 제 집을 많이 비웠다.\n\n“정치란 사람의 시간을 무섭게 잡아먹는군.” 그는 아내에게 이렇게 말하고는, 폴 몰의 클럽으로 내려가 다른 여러 젊은 거인 애호가들과 저녁을 먹었다. 그런 축의 사람들에게 정치가 시간을 크게 잡아먹기는 한다 — 저녁 식사 때와 그 무렵에.\n\n“던스터블 양을 어떻게 생각하세요?” 커피를 앞에 놓고 함께 앉았을 때 그레셤 부인이 삼촌에게 말했다. 그녀는 그 물음에 아무것도 덧붙이지 않고, 있는 그대로 앙상하게 물었다.\n\n“던스터블 양을 어떻게 생각하느냐고!” 선생이 말했다. “글쎄, 메리. 너는 그분을 어떻게 생각하느냐? 아마 우리 생각이 같을 게다.”\n\n“그건 제가 물은 게 아니에요. 삼촌은 그분을 어떻게 생각하시느냐고요. 그분이 정직하다고 보세요?”\n\n“정직? 오, 그럼, 물론이지 — 아주 정직하다고 하겠다.”\n\n“성미도 좋고요?”\n\n“성미야 유별나게 좋지.”\n\n“정도 깊고요?”\n\n“글쎄, 그렇지 — 정도 깊지. 정이 깊은 사람이라고 나는 분명히 말하겠다.”\n\n“그분이 영리한 건 틀림없어요.”\n\n“그래, 영리하다고 본다.”\n\n“그리고, 그리고 — 마음결이 여자답고요.” 그레셤 부인은 차마 ‘숙녀답다’고까지는 말할 수 없다고 느꼈다. 감히 그럴 수 있었다면 기꺼이 그렇게 말했을 것이다.\n\n“오, 물론이지.” 선생이 말했다. “그런데 메리, 어쩌자고 그렇게 재간을 부려 던스터블 양의 성품을 해부하고 있는 거냐?”\n\n“그럼 삼촌, 왜 그러는지 말씀드리지요. 그건 —” 그러면서 그레셤 부인은 말하는 중에 의자에서 일어나 식탁을 돌아 삼촌 옆으로 가서, 얼굴이 그의 얼굴에 닿을 만큼 가까이 대고 팔을 그의 목에 둘렀다. 그러고는 그의 눈에 보이지 않는 등 뒤에 선 채로 말을 이었다. “그건 — 던스터블 양이 삼촌을 아주 많이 좋아한다고 제가 생각하기 때문이에요. 그리고 삼촌이 그분에게 아내가 되어 달라고 청하시면 그분이 행복해할 거라고 생각하기 때문이에요.”\n\n“메리!” 선생이 조카딸의 얼굴을 들여다보려 애쓰며 돌아앉아 말했다.\n\n“저는 아주 진심이에요, 삼촌 — 아주 진심이에요. 그분이 흘린 자잘한 말들과 제가 본 자잘한 일들로 보아, 지금 삼촌께 말씀드리는 것을 저는 정말 믿어요.”\n\n“그래서 너는 내가 —”\n\n“다정한 삼촌, 하나뿐인 제 사랑하는 삼촌, 저는 삼촌이 삼촌을 — 삼촌을 행복하게 할 일만 해 주시기를 바랄 뿐이에요. 삼촌에 비하면 던스터블 양이 저에게 무엇이겠어요?” 그러고는 몸을 굽혀 그에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n선생은 저에게 건네진 그 말에 너무 크게 놀라, 그 자리에서 더 무슨 대답도 하지 못하는 듯했다. 조카딸은 그것을 보고, 옷을 갈아입으러 가려고 그를 혼자 두었다. 그리고 두 사람이 응접실에서 다시 만났을 때에는 프랭크 그레셤이 함께 있었다.\n\n# 제29장. 던스터블 양의 초대\n\n던스터블 양은 응접실 계단 위의 작은 대기실에 서서 손님을 맞고 있었으나, 사랑에 애태우는 처녀처럼 보이지는 않았다. 그녀의 집은 런던에서 여기저기 눈에 띄는 별난 대저택 가운데 하나로, 도시의 가로와 연립 주택을 세울 때 흔히 따르는 규칙보다는 시골 건축의 규칙에 맞추어 지은 집이었다. 형제 되는 집들에서 뒤로 물러나 홀로 서 있었으므로 주인이 집을 한 바퀴 돌아 걸을 수도 있었다. 짧은 마차 길로 들어가게 되어 있었고, 큰 문은 건물 뒤쪽에 있었으며, 집의 앞면은 공원 가운데 하나를 바라보고 있었다. 던스터블 양은 이 집을 손에 넣는 데에도 여느 때와 같은 운을 누렸다. 어느 별난 백만장자가 엄청난 돈을 들여 지은 집이었는데, 그 별난 백만장자는 열두 달을 살아 본 뒤에 이 집에는 편안한 구석이 하나도 없으며, 주거의 관점에서 사람이 살아가는 데 꼭 있어야 할 세목이 대부분 빠져 있다고 선언했던 것이다. 그리하여 저택은 팔리게 되었고, 던스터블 양이 그것을 샀다. 크랜본 저택이라는 이름이 붙어 있었고 지금의 주인도 그 점은 고치지 않았으나, 세상은 대체로 그 집을 연고 저택이라 불렀고 던스터블 양 자신도 다른 어떤 이름만큼이나 그 이름을 자주 썼다. 던스터블 양을 놀려서 재미를 보기는 도무지 불가능했다. 그녀 자신이 언제나 그 농담에 한몫 끼어들었기 때문이다.\n\n그레셤 부인과 손 선생 사이에는 지난번 대화의 화제를 두고 그 뒤로 한마디도 더 오가지 않았다. 그러나 선생은 하인들의 무리 사이로 눈부신 불빛 속에서 그 부인의 문간에 들어서서, 앞에도 사람 떼요 뒤에도 사람 떼인 것을 보고는, 자기가 여기서 제집처럼 편안할 수는 도무지 없겠다고 느꼈다. 던스터블 양이라는 사람이 이렇게 사는 것은 다 괜찮을지 몰라도, 손 선생의 아내가 이렇게 사는 것은 괜찮을 수가 없었다. 그러나 이 모든 것은 부질없는 공상에 지나지 않았다. 스스로에게 열두 번도 더 말했듯이, 조카딸이 던스터블 양의 성품을 읽는 데 이상하게도 잘못을 저질렀다는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그레셤 일행이 계단이 열려 드는 대기실에 들어섰을 때, 던스터블 양은 가장 가까운 벗 몇 사람에게 둘러싸여 거기 서 있었다. 해럴드 스미스 부인은 그녀의 바로 옆에 앉아 있었고, 이지먼 선생은 벽에 붙여 놓은 소파에 기대 누워 있었으며, 던스터블 양과 늘 함께 지내는 부인이 그 곁에 있었다. 한두 사람이 더 있어서 이야기가 끊이지 않고 오갔는데, 그러지 않으면 지금 맡은 일 때문에 던스터블 양이 느낄 만한 무료함을 덜어 주려는 것이었다. 그레셤 부인이 남편의 팔에 기대어 방에 들어섰을 때, 그녀는 프라우디 부인의 등을 보았다. 그 부인은 주교의 팔에 기대어 맞은편 문으로 빠져나가는 중이었다.\n\n해럴드 스미스 부인은 오라비를 대신해 넣은 청을 던스터블 양이 그렇게 딱 잘라 물리쳤을 때 느꼈을 것이 분명한 언짢음에서 이미 벗어난 듯했다. 두 부인 사이의 가까움을 끊어 놓을 만한 감정이 단 하루라도 있었다면, 그 감정은 이제 씨도 없이 사그라졌다. 해럴드 스미스 부인이 예전과 조금도 다름없이 벗과 이야기를 나누고 있었으니 말이다. 그녀는 지나가는 손님마다 한마디씩 평을 붙였고, 집주인의 마음에 드는 투로 그리한 것이 분명했다. 던스터블 양이 가장 다정한 웃음을 띠고, 그 사람 좋은 기색에 남다른 빛깔을 입혀 주는 그 살가우면서도 즐거운 목소리로 대답했기 때문이다.\n\n“그 사람은 당신이 하는 일이 그저 남의 것을 베낀 것이라고 굳게 믿고 있어요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 프라우디 부인을 두고 말했다.\n\n“그렇고말고요. 요즘 저녁 모임에 무슨 대단히 새로운 것이 있을 수 있겠어요.”\n\n“하지만 그 사람은 당신이 자기를 베낀다고 생각해요.”\n\n“그러면 어때요? 나는 눈에 보이는 사람은 누구든 얼마간 베끼는걸요. 당신도 처음에 제 머리에서 나온 생각으로 큰 페티코트를 입기 시작한 건 아니잖아요? 프라우디 부인에게 그만한 자랑거리라도 있다면 부디 그것을 빼앗지 말아요. 저기 선생님과 그레셤 댁 사람들이 왔군요. 메리, 이 사랑스러운 사람, 어떻게 지냈어요?” 그러고는 그 거창한 차림새에도 아랑곳없이 던스터블 양은 그레셤 부인을 붙잡아 입을 맞추었다. 뒤에서 계단을 올라오던 열댓 명의 이름난 사교계 인사들이 볼썽사납다고 여길 만한 짓이었다.\n\n선생은 얼마 전에 자기에게 전해진 말 때문에 인사하는 태도가 어딘가 움츠러들어 있었다. 던스터블 양은 이제 부의 첨탑 맨 위에 서 있었고, 그가 손을 뻗어 닿을 수 없을 만큼 높은 데 있는 것은 물론이려니와 그의 삶의 궤도에서도 너무 멀리 떨어져 있어서, 도무지 자기를 그녀와 같은 자리에 놓을 수가 없어 보였다. 그렇게 높이 바랄 수도 없고 그렇게 낮게 내려올 수도 없었다. 이런 생각을 하면서 그는 두 사람 사이에 큰 거리가 있는 것처럼—그레셤스베리에서 다정한 벗으로 함께 지낸 시간이 아예 없었던 것처럼—던스터블 양에게 말을 걸었다. 그런 시간이 있었다. 그동안 던스터블 양과 손 선생은 같은 세상에 속한 사람들처럼 지냈다. 그리고 던스터블 양에 대해서는 적어도 이 말은 할 수 있다. 그녀는 그 시간을 잊을 생각이 조금도 없었다는 것이다.\n\n손 선생은 그저 손을 내밀었을 뿐, 곧 지나쳐 가려 했다.\n\n“가지 마세요, 선생님.” 그녀가 말했다. “제발, 아직 가지 마세요. 저 안으로 들어가 버리면 언제 붙잡을 수 있을지 모르겠어요. 앞으로 두 시간은 따라갈 수도 없을 테고요. 레이디 메러디스, 와 주셔서 정말 고마워요. 어머님도 오시겠지요? 아, 정말 기쁘네요! 그분으로서는 아시다시피 대단한 은혜예요. 조지 경, 경께서는 그 자체로 반쯤 죄인이시니 그렇게까지 대단하게 여기지는 않겠어요.”\n\n“아, 그렇소.” 조지 경이 말했다. “아마 큰 쪽 반이겠지.”\n\n“남자들은 세상을 신들과 거인들로 갈라 놓지요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “우리 여자들에게도 우리 나름의 편 가름이 있어요. 우리는 제 편에 따라 성인이 되기도 하고 죄인이 되기도 하지요. 그중 제일 못한 것은, 우리도 여러분만큼이나 자주 변절한다는 거예요.” 그 말에 조지 경은 웃으며 지나갔다.\n\n“선생님이 이런 걸 좋아하지 않으시는 줄은 알아요.” 그녀가 말을 이었다. “하지만 남들보다 유별나게 선생님 뜻대로만 지내실 까닭도 없지요. 그렇지 않아요, 프랭크?”\n\n“좋아하지 않으신다고 딱 잘라 말하기도 어렵겠는데요.” 그레셤 씨가 말했다. “여기 오신다는 벗들 가운데 꼭 뵙고 싶다고 선생님께서 스스로 인정하신 분들이 있으니까요.”\n\n“그런가요? 그러면 선생님도 변절할 희망이 좀 있군요. 하지만 굳건한 죄인 노릇은 결코 못 하실 거예요. 그렇지요, 메리? 새 재주를 배우기에는 너무 늙으셨어요. 그렇지 않아요, 선생님?”\n\n“그런 것 같군요.” 선생이 힘없이 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“손 선생님은 스스로를 성인의 군대에 넣으시나요?” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 물었다.\n\n“그야 물론이지요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “하지만 성인에도 여러 수도회가 있다는 걸 늘 잊지 말아야 해요. 그렇지요, 메리? 그리고 프란체스코회와 도미니코회가 서로 아주 잘 맞는다고 여기는 사람은 아무도 없지요. 손 선생님은 바체스터의 성 프라우디 학파에 속한 분이 아니에요. 이름난 젊은 수련 수녀를 팔꿈치 옆에 세우고 저 계단 모퉁이를 돌아오시는 저 여사제 쪽을 더 좋아하실 분이지요.”\n\n“내가 들은 바로 미루면 그랜틀리 양은 죄인 쪽으로 세어야 할 거예요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 레이디 러프턴이 젊은 벗을 데리고 다가오는 것을 보면서 말했다. “레이디 하틀탑을 성인으로 만들어 낼 수 있다면 또 모르지만요.”\n\n그러고 나서 레이디 러프턴이 방에 들어섰고, 던스터블 양은 그날 여러 손님에게 보인 것보다 훨씬 조용한 공대를 태도에 담아 그녀를 맞으러 나아갔다. “와 주셔서 정말 고마워요, 레이디 러프턴.” 그녀가 말했다. “그랜틀리 양을 함께 데려오셨으니 더욱 고맙지요.”\n\n레이디 러프턴이 짧고 곱게 인사말을 하는 동안 손 선생이 다가와 그녀와 악수를 했고, 프랭크 그레셤과 그의 아내도 그리했다. 프램리 사람들과 그레셤스베리 사람들은 같은 주에 사는 사이로 서로 아는 처지였으므로, 레이디 러프턴이 그 작은 방을 나와 프라우디 부인이라면 훌륭한 방 한 벌이라 불렀을 곳으로 들어가기까지 잠시 이런저런 이야기가 오갔다. “아버지도 오실 거예요.” 그랜틀리 양이 말했다. “적어도 그렇게 알고 있어요. 저는 아직 뵙지 못했지만요.”\n\n“그럼요, 저와 약속하셨어요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “그리고 부주교님은 약속을 지키실 분이지요. 그분이 안 계시면 교회 쪽 균형이 도무지 맞지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“아버지는 언제나 약속을 지키세요.” 그랜틀리 양이 거의 딱딱하다 할 만한 어조로 말했다. 그녀는 딱한 던스터블 양의 작은 농담을 전혀 알아듣지 못했거나, 적어도 그것에 응해 주기에는 지나치게 위엄이 있었다.\n\n“늙은 존 경이 당장 칠턴 헌드레즈를 받기로 했다더군요.” 레이디 러프턴이 프랭크 그레셤에게 반쯤 속삭이는 소리로 말했다. 레이디 러프턴은 늘 동바셋주의 정치에 예민한 관심을 두어 왔고, 이제 그레셤 집안 사람이 다시 이 주의 의원 자리에 앉게 된 것을 흡족해한다는 뜻을 나타내고 싶었다. 그레셤 집안은 아득한 옛날부터 바셋주의 오래된 주 대표 의원 집안이었다.\n\n“아, 예. 그렇게 될 줄 압니다.” 프랭크가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 그는 그만한 높은 명예에 자기를 내세우는 것이 거의 부끄럽게 느껴질 만큼 아직 젊었다.\n\n“물론 경합은 없겠지요.” 레이디 러프턴이 은근한 목소리로 말했다. “다행히도 동바셋주에서는 경합이 나는 일이 드물어요. 하지만 만일 있다 해도 프램리의 소작인은 하나같이 옳은 쪽에 표를 넣을 거예요. 그것만은 내가 보증할 수 있어요. 러프턴 경이 바로 오늘 아침에 나에게 그렇게 말했지요.”\n\n프랭크 그레셤은 젖내 나는 정치 초년생이라면 으레 할 만한 짧고 고운 답사를 했고, 이런저런 정중한 웅얼거림까지 더해져 러프턴 일행은 대기실에 일이 분쯤 붙들려 있게 되었다. 그러는 사이에도 세상은 밀려들어 네댓 개의 큰 응접실로 지나가고 있었다. 딱한 프라우디 부인의 가슴을 이미 시샘으로 속속까지 찌르고 있던 그 훌륭한 방 한 벌이었다. “주교들이 쓰도록 나라가 마련해 주어야 할 방이 바로 이런 방이지.” 그녀는 저도 모르게 혼자 이렇게 말했다.\n\n“그런데 사람들을 충분히 한데 모아 놓지는 못했소.” 그녀가 자기 주인에게 말했다.\n\n“그렇군요, 그렇군요. 그런 것 같지 않아요.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 그것이야말로 콘베르사치오네에 없어서는 안 될 것이오.” 프라우디 부인이 말을 이었다. “우리 글로스터 플레이스에서는—.” 그러나 그녀의 험한 평을 다 적지는 않겠다. 레이디 러프턴이 대기실에서 우리를 기다리고 있으니 말이다.\n\n그런데 이제 또 한 사람의 중대한 도착이 있었다. 참으로 대단히 중대한 도착이었다. 사실을 말하자면 던스터블 양은 특별한 두 사람을 맞아들이는 데에 마음을 걸어 두고 있었다. 그리고 뒤집어 볼 수 있는 돌은—분별을 지키면서 뒤집을 수 있는 돌은—하나도 남기지 않고 다 뒤집어 보았음에도, 이 놀라운 두 권세가에 대해서는 여전히 알 수가 없었다. 지금 우리가 이야기하고 있는 바로 그 순간에도, 그녀가 겉으로는 그렇게 가볍고 활달해 보였지만—가볍고 활달한 것이 본래 그녀의 성품이었으므로—속마음은 의심에 찢기고 있었다. 바라던 두 사람이 와 준다면 그녀의 저녁은 온전히 성공일 터였다. 그러나 오지 않는다면 애쓴 것이 다 헛되고 이 일은 실패가 될 터였다. 그리고 이번 모임에는 던스터블 양이 실패하지 않기를 몹시 애타게 바라게 만드는 사정이 얽혀 있었다. 이 땅의 두 위인이 《주피터》의 톰 타워스와 옴니엄 공작이었다는 것은 말로 밝힐 필요조차 없겠다.\n\n그리고 이제 바로 이 순간, 레이디 러프턴이 젊은 그레셤에게 정중한 인사말을 늘어놓으며 좀처럼 자리를 옮길 낌새를 보이지 않고, 던스터블 양은 이 새로운 세상에서 선생이 제집처럼 편안히 여기게 해 줄 말을 그의 귀에 속삭여 넣으려 애쓰고 있을 때, 한 소리가 들려왔다. 그 소리로 이 부인은 자기 소원의 절반은 어쨌든 이루어졌음을 알았다. 소리가 들려왔다—그러나 그것을 들은 것은 그녀 자신의 귀와 또 하나의 귀 밝은 귀뿐이었다. 해럴드 스미스 부인도 그 이름을 알아들었고, 공작이 다가오고 있음을 알았다.\n\n여기에는 큰 영광과 승리가 있었다. 그러나 각하는 어찌하여 이토록 고약한 순간에 오셨을까? 던스터블 양은 레이디 러프턴과 옴니엄 공작을 같은 집에 같은 시각에 불러들이는 것이 얼마나 온당하지 못한 일인지 잘 알고 있었다. 다만 레이디 러프턴을 청할 때에는 공작을 모실 희망이 없다고 믿게 되어 있었고, 그러다 그 희망이 밝아 왔을 때에는 이렇게 생각하며 스스로를 달랬던 것이다. 두 해가 몇 분쯤 같은 반구에 떠 있을 수는 있어도 서로 부딪치거나 서로의 궤도를 가로지르리라고는 보기 어렵다고. 그녀의 방들은 넓고 사람으로 붐빌 터였다. 공작은 한 번 훑고 지나가는 정도 이상은 하지 않을 것이고, 레이디 러프턴은 틀림없이 자기와 같은 부류의 사람들에게 둘러싸여 있을 것이었다. 이렇게 던스터블 양은 스스로를 달래 두었다. 그런데 이제 모든 것이 어그러져, 레이디 러프턴은 그녀의 생각대로라면 이 아래 영국 세상을 걸어 다니도록 허락받은 사탄의 대리자 가운데 가장 가까운 자와 옷깃이 닿을 만한 거리에 놓이게 된 것이다. 그녀가 비명을 지를까? 아니면 분개하여 집 밖으로 물러나 버릴까? 그것도 아니면 당당히 고개를 들고 손을 뻗은 채 들리는 목소리로 악마와 그의 모든 짓을 대담하게 물리칠까? 공작이 다가오는 동안 이런 것들을 생각하다가 던스터블 양은 마음의 중심을 거의 잃을 지경이었다.\n\n그러나 해럴드 스미스 부인은 잃지 않았다. “드디어 공작께서 오셨군요.” 그녀는 레이디 러프턴의 귀를 똑바로 붙잡으려는 어조로 말했다.\n\n스미스 부인은 마님이 앞으로 나아가 이 대면을 피할 짬이 아직 있으리라고 셈했던 것이다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 그 말을 들었더라도 온전히 알아듣지는 못했다. 어쨌든 그 순간 그 말은 담고자 한 뜻을 그녀의 머리에 전해 주지 못했다. 그녀는 프랭크 그레셤에게 마지막으로 한마디 속삭이려 걸음을 멈추었고, 그러고 나서 둘러보다가 자기 옷자락에 밀려 닿아 있는 신사가 바로—옴니엄 공작임을 알았다!\n\n이 큰 판에서, 불운을 이제 더는 피할 수 없게 되었을 때, 던스터블 양은 결코 자기 자신이나 자기 성품에 못 미치게 굴지 않았다. 그녀는 이 재난을 한탄했으나, 이제 남은 길은 그것을 되도록 잘 넘기는 것뿐임을 알았다. 공작은 그녀의 집에 와 주어 그녀를 높여 주었으니 그를 맞아들이는 것이 마땅했다. 그렇게 하는 것이 레이디 러프턴을 마지막 숨까지 몰아붙이는 일이 될지라도 그랬다.\n\n“공작.” 그녀가 말했다. “각하께서 이렇게 은혜를 베풀어 주시니 크게 영광입니다. 이토록 저에게 잘해 주실 줄은 미처 바라지도 못했어요.”\n\n“은혜는 온통 그쪽에서 온 것이오.” 공작이 그녀의 손 위로 몸을 굽히며 말했다.\n\n그리고 일이 여느 때의 순서대로 흘렀다면 그것으로 끝이었을 것이다. 공작은 걸음을 옮겨 얼굴을 보이고, 레이디 하틀탑에게, 주교에게, 그레셤 씨에게, 그런 이들에게 한두 마디를 건넨 다음, 다른 길로 방들을 나서서 조용히 빠져나갔을 것이다. 그에게 기대되는 의무가 그것이었고 그도 그렇게 했을 터이며, 그렇게 함으로써 이 모임의 값은 삼십 퍼센트가 올랐을 것이다. 그러나 일이 이렇게 되고 보니, 웨스트 엔드의 소문 장수들은 그에게서 훨씬 더 많은 것을 얻어 낼 판이었다.\n\n일이 그렇게 돌아가 그는 영락없이 레이디 러프턴에게 몸이 밀려 붙어 있었고, 그녀는 그 목소리를 듣고 던스터블 양의 말로 이 위인이 와 있다는 사실을 똑똑히 알게 되자, 재빨리, 그러면서도 여자다운 위엄을 잔뜩 지킨 채 몸을 돌려 옷자락을 그 닿음에서 거두어들였다. 그렇게 하는 통에 그녀는 영락없이 공작과 얼굴을 맞대게 되었고, 두 사람은 서로를 정면으로 바라보지 않을 수 없었다. “실례했소.” 공작이 말했다. 두 사람 사이에 오간 말은 그것뿐이었고 그 뒤로도 서로 말을 나눈 적이 없다. 그러나 그토록 단순한 말이었음에도, 말하는 이들의 자잘한 곁연기가 곁들여지자 그 말은 사교계에 상당한 발효를 일으켰다. 레이디 러프턴은 이지먼 선생 쪽으로 물러나면서 몸을 깊이 굽혀 인사했다. 깊이, 그리고 천천히, 오직 그녀만의 것인 도도한 옷자락 놀림과 함께 굽혀 인사했다. 그러나 그 인사가 아무리 말이 많았다 해도, 그녀의 눈이 서서히 내려가고 입술이 서서히 다물리는 데 담긴 목소리에 견주면 절반도 말하지 못했고, 공작의 몸에 붙은 죄악을 그만큼 세게 꾸짖지도 못했다. 인사를 시작할 때 그녀는 적의 얼굴을 정면으로 바라보고 있었다. 인사를 끝냈을 때 그녀의 눈은 땅을 향해 있었으나, 입가의 주름에는 말로 담을 수 없을 만큼 많은 멸시가 담겨 있었다. 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않고 물러났다. 얌전한 미덕과 여자의 약함이 낯 두꺼운 악덕과 사내의 힘 앞에서 언제나 그리해야 하는 대로 물러났다. 그럼에도 세상은 하나같이 그녀가 이 맞부딪침에서 이겼다고 여겼다. 공작은 용서를 청하면서, 부인에게 불편을 끼쳤다고 스스로 여기는 신사라면 누구나 짓는 그 다소 누그러진 미안함의 표정을 얼굴에 띠고 있었다. 그러나 그 위에—아니, 오히려 그 아래에—희미한 비웃음의 웃음기가 있었다. 레이디 러프턴의 몸가짐을 얼마간 우스워하지 않고서는 바라볼 수 없었던 것처럼. 던스터블 양이나 해럴드 스미스 부인처럼 눈이 날카로운 사람들에게는 이 모든 것이 읽혔고, 공작은 이런 소리 없는 속 빈정거림의 명수로 알려져 있었다. 그러나 그들조차—던스터블 양과 해럴드 스미스 부인조차—레이디 러프턴이 이겼다고 인정했다. 마님이 다시 눈을 들었을 때 공작은 이미 지나가 있었다. 그러자 그녀는 다시 그랜틀리 양의 손을 챙겨 들고 사람들 사이로 따라 들어갔다.\n\n“저런 걸 두고 불운하다고 하는 거예요.” 교전한 두 사람이 다 싸움터를 떠나자 던스터블 양이 말했다. “운수라는 게 때로는 사람을 거스르지요.”\n\n“하지만 여기서는 조금도 당신을 거스르지 않았어요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. “내일 아침에 마님의 속마음에까지 다다를 수 있다면, 그분이 공작을 만난 일로 아주 즐거워하고 있는 걸 알게 될 거예요. 그분이 자기 승리를 자랑하기를 그치기까지는 여러 해가 걸릴 테고, 프램리의 젊은 아가씨들은 앞으로 세 세대 동안 그 이야기를 할 거예요.”\n\n손 선생을 포함한 그레셤 일행은 싸움이 벌어지는 동안 대기실에 남아 있었다. 접전은 통틀어 이 분이 채 걸리지 않았고, 레이디 러프턴이 이지먼 선생의 무릎 위로 물러나는 통에 세 사람은 빠져나갈 길이 막혀 있었다. 그러나 이제 그들도 앞으로 지나가 보려 했다.\n\n“어머, 나를 버리고 가시겠다고요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “좋아요. 하지만 곧 찾아낼 테니까요. 프랭크, 방 하나에서는 춤을 추게 되어 있어요. 이 모임을 프라우디 부인의 콘베르사치오네와 구별하려는 것뿐이지요. 콘베르사치오네가 다 똑같으면 어리석은 일일 테니까요. 그렇지 않아요? 그러니 가서 춤을 추어 주면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“먹이 시간에도 또 다른 변주가 있을 테지요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아, 그럼요. 물론이지요. 그 점에서는 내가 세상에서 가장 상스러운 인간이에요. 나는 사람들에게 먹이고 마시게 하는 게 참 좋아요.—서플하우스 씨, 뵙게 되어 반가워요. 그런데 좀 말씀해 주세요—” 그러고서 그녀는 서플하우스 씨의 귀에 힘을 실어 속삭였고, 서플하우스 씨도 그녀의 귀에 속삭여 주었다. “그러면 그가 올 거라고 보시는군요?” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n서플하우스 씨는 그렇다고 했다. 그는 그렇게 보았다. 그러나 그 일을 사실로 못 박아 말할 근거는 없었다. 그러고 나서 그는 앞으로 지나갔는데, 지나면서 해럴드 스미스 부인은 거의 쳐다보지도 않았다.\n\n“낯짝이 어쩌면 저렇게 음흉할까요.” 그 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아! 이봐요, 당신은 편견이 있어요. 그럴 만도 하지요. 나로서는 늘 서플하우스가 마음에 들었어요. 그는 해코지를 하려고 들지요. 하지만 해코지가 그의 장사이고, 그는 그걸 감추지 않아요. 내가 정치가라면 서플하우스 씨가 나에게 등을 돌렸다고 화를 내는 일은, 지금 내가 나를 찌른 핀에게 화를 내는 것만큼이나 하지 않을 거예요. 그건 내 손이 서툰 탓이고, 핀을 좀 더 꾀 있게 쓰는 법을 알았어야 하는 거지요.”\n\n“하지만 제 편에 서겠다고 내세우고서는 그 편을 망치려고 온 힘을 다하는 사람은 미워하지 않을 수 없잖아요.”\n\n“이봐요, 그런 짓을 한 사람이 어디 한둘인가요. 그것도 서플하우스 씨보다 훨씬 크게 성공하면서요! 사랑과 전쟁에서는 무슨 짓을 해도 정당하다지요. 거기에 정치를 더해 넣으면 왜 안 되나요? 그렇게 하기로 서로 뜻만 맞출 수 있다면 그 많은 속앓이에서 벗어날 수 있을 테고, 그러면서 누구도 조금도 더 나빠지지 않을 거예요.”\n\n던스터블 양의 방들은 넓기는 넓었으나—“훌륭한 방 한 벌이기는 하지요. 다만 조금 너무—너무—너무 흩어져 있달까요. 그렇게 말해 두겠소. 그렇지 않소, 주교?”—이제는 거의 꽉 차 있어서, 온 사람 가운데 많은 이가 삼십 분쯤만 머물다 가지 않았다면 불편할 만큼 붐볐을 것이었다. 그래도 춤추는 이들을 위한 자리는 남겨 두었는데, 프라우디 부인에게는 크게 낭패였다. 그녀가 런던에서 춤추는 것을 통틀어 못마땅해했다는 말은 아니다. 다만 그녀가 몸소 사교계에 다시 세워 놓은 콘베르사치오네의 법이 이토록 난폭하게 짓밟히는 것에 분개했던 것이다.\n\n“콘베르사치오네는 아무 뜻도 없는 것이 되고 말 것이오.” 그녀가 뒤쪽 말에 힘을 잔뜩 실어 주교에게 말했다. “이런 식으로 다루어진다면 조금의 뜻도 없는 것이 되고 말 것이오.”\n\n“그렇지요, 없어질 거예요. 조금의 뜻도 없지요.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“춤은 제자리에서라면 아주 괜찮을 수도 있소.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“나 자신은 한 번도 그것을 반대한 적이 없어요. 그러니까, 평신도에게라면요.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 사람들이 더 높은 뜻을 위해 모인다고 내세운다면.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그 내세운 바에 맞게 행동해야 하는 것이오.”\n\n“그러지 않으면 위선자보다 나을 것이 없지요.” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“삽은 삽이라고 불러야 하는 법이오.” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그야 물론이지요.” 주교가 맞장구를 치며 말했다.\n\n“그리고 내가 수고와 비용을 무릅쓰고 콘베르사치오네를 들여왔을 때는.” 프라우디 부인이 자기가 부당한 대우를 받았다는 느낌을 뚜렷이 드러내며 말을 이었다. “그 말이 이토록—이토록—이토록 잘못 새겨지는 것을 보게 될 줄은 생각도 못 했소.” 그러고서 방 저쪽에 사귀어 둘 만한 사람들이 있는 것을 알아보고는, 주교더러 혼자 알아서 버티라고 두고 건너가 버렸다.\n\n레이디 러프턴은 승리를 거둔 뒤 춤추는 방으로 옮겨 갔다. 적이 거기까지 따라올 것 같지는 않았다. 그리고 거기 오래 있지 않아 아들이 그녀에게 합류했다. 지금 그녀의 마음은 그리젤다에 관한 일이 놓인 형편이 그리 흡족하지 않았다. 그녀는 자기 소원이 무엇인지를 젊은 벗에게 말해 주는 데까지 나아가 있었다. 그리젤다가 자기 며느리가 되기를 바란다고 밝혔던 것이다. 그런데 이에 대해 그리젤다 자신은 아무것도 밝히지 않았다. 그랜틀리 양처럼 잘 자란 아가씨라면 신사 쪽의 처신으로 그럴 자격을 얻기까지는 마음의 열기를 내보이지 않는 것이 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다. 그러나 그런 삼가는 태도가 온당하다는 것을 충분히 알고 있으면서도, 레이디 러프턴은 자기에게만은 그리젤다가 이 혼사가 자기 마음에 든다는 것을 드러내는 말 한마디쯤은 해 줄 수도 있었으리라 생각했다. 그러나 그리젤다는 그런 말을 한마디도 하지 않았고, 러프턴 경이 청하면 받아들이겠다는 것을 보이는 소리도 한 음절 내지 않았다. 그러면서 또 받아들이지 않겠다는 것을 보이는 소리도 한 음절 내지 않았다. 그럼에도 세상이 자기와 덤벨로 경을 두고 말이 많다는 것을 알면서도 그녀는 기회가 날 때마다 그 미래의 후작과 춤을 추러 일어섰다. 이 모든 것이 레이디 러프턴을 언짢게 했고, 그녀는 자기의 작은 계획을 어서 좋은 결말로 이끌지 못한다면 아예 손을 떼는 것이 나을지도 모른다고 생각하기 시작했다. 그녀는 여전히 아들을 위해 이 혼사를 바라고 있었다. 그리젤다가 좋은 아내가 될 것은 의심하지 않았다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 여태 바라 왔던 만큼 며느리에 대한 그토록 강한 정을 자기가 계속 지닐 수 있을지, 한때만큼 확신하지 못하게 되어 있었다.\n\n“루도빅, 여기 온 지 오래됐니?” 그녀는 아들의 얼굴에 눈이 닿을 때면 늘 짓던 그 웃음을 지으며 말했다.\n\n“방금 왔어요. 던스터블 양이 어머니가 여기 계시다고 해서 서둘러 뒤따라왔지요. 사람이 어쩌면 이렇게 많을까요! 브록 경을 보셨어요?”\n\n“눈에 띄지 않았다.”\n\n“그럼 드 테리어 경은요? 저는 가운데 방에서 두 분을 다 보았는데요.”\n\n“드 테리어 경은 내가 지나갈 때 악수를 해 주는 영광을 베풀었지.”\n\n“이렇게 뒤섞인 사람들은 본 적이 없어요. 저기 프라우디 부인은 여기서들 춤을 추려 한다고 제정신이 아니고요.”\n\n“프라우디 자매도 춤을 추잖아요.” 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 콘베르사치오네에서는 아니지요. 그 차이를 못 보시는군요. 그리고 저기 스퍼모일이 인형극의 펀치처럼 좋아서 어쩔 줄 모르고 있는 걸 보았어요. 제 나름의 동아리를 제 주위에 꽤 두르고서, 세상의 악덕이란 것에 아주 익숙한 사람처럼 재잘거리고 있었지요.”\n\n“여기에는 미리 생각했더라면 만나고 싶지 않았을 사람들이 분명히 있구나.” 방금 치른 접전을 마음에 둔 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“하지만 다 괜찮은 게 틀림없어요. 제가 부주교님과 함께 계단을 올라왔으니까요. 그건 확실한 증거지요, 그랜틀리 양?”\n\n“저는 두렵지 않아요. 어머님과 함께 있으면 안전할 수밖에 없다는 걸 아니까요.”\n\n“그건 그렇게 확실하지 않은데요.” 러프턴 경이 웃으며 말했다. “어머니, 아직 제일 나쁜 것은 모르시죠. 여기 누가 와 있는지 아세요?”\n\n“네가 누구를 말하는지 안다. 나는 이미 보았어.” 레이디 러프턴이 아주 조용히 말했다.\n\n“계단 맨 위에서 딱 마주쳤어요.” 그리젤다가 러프턴 경이 이전에 그 얼굴에서 본 적이 없는 생기를 띠고 말했다.\n\n“뭐라고요, 공작 말인가요?”\n\n“그래, 공작이다.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그 사람과 마주 닿게 될 줄 알았다면 나는 결코 오지 않았을 거야. 하지만 뜻하지 않은 일이었고, 이런 자리에서는 어쩔 수 없는 일이었다.”\n\n러프턴 경은 어머니의 목소리 결과 얼굴빛의 미묘한 그늘로, 그녀가 공작과 몸소 마주치는 일을 정말로 겪어 냈으며, 또 그 일에 대해 기대할 만한 만큼 분개하고 있지도 않다는 것을 곧 알아차렸다. 그녀는 여전히 던스터블 양의 집에 그대로 있었고, 던스터블 양의 처사에 대해 아무런 노여움도 드러내지 않았다. 공작이 어머니의 손을 잡고 만찬 자리로 데려가는 것을 보았더라도 러프턴 경이 이보다 더 놀랄 수는 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 이 일에 대해 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n“춤을 출 거니, 루도빅?” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 춤이 콘베르사치오네를 더럽힌다는 프라우디 부인의 생각에 저도 찬성하지 않는다고는 못 하겠는데요. 그랜틀리 양은 어떻게 생각해요?”\n\n그리젤다는 농담에 밝은 적이 한 번도 없었으므로, 러프턴 경이 자기와 춤추는 수고를 면하려 한다고 여겼다. 이것이 그녀를 화나게 했다. 젊은 아가씨인 자기와 젊은 신사인 다른 누군가 사이에서 벌어지는 사랑놀이나 희롱이나 사귐 가운데 그녀의 취향에 드는 것은 오직 춤이라는 즐거움 안에 있었기 때문이다. 이 문제에서 그녀는 프라우디 부인과 완전히 어긋나 있었고, 던스터블 양의 새 시도를 크게 칭찬했다. 사교의 자리에서 그리젤다에게는 혀보다 발끝이 더 요긴했고, 부드러운 말보다는 빠른 한 바퀴 돌기로 그녀를 얻을 가능성이 훨씬 컸다. 그녀가 받아들일 만한 청혼이란 왈츠가 잠깐 끊기는 사이에 숨이 넘어갈 듯한 두 마디로 전해지는 것이었고, 그러면 그녀는 등에 늘 받던 받침을 받으려 팔을 들어 올리면서 “아버지에게—여쭤—보세요.”라고 말할 힘 정도는 겨우 낼 수 있을 터였다. 그 뒤로는 모든 일이 제대로 정리되기까지 그 이야기가 입에 오르는 것을 그녀는 바라지 않을 것이었다.\n\n“그건 생각해 보지 않았어요.” 그리젤다가 러프턴 경에게서 얼굴을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n그러나 그랜틀리 양이 러프턴 경을 두고 아무 생각도 하지 않았다거나, 러프턴 경의 아내가 되고 싶다고 마음을 굳힌다면 레이디 러프턴을 제 편으로 두는 것이 얼마나 큰 이익일지 헤아려 보지 않았다고 여겨서는 안 된다. 그녀는 지금이 자기가 승리를 거둘 때임을, 미인으로 인정받은 이 첫 사교철이야말로 그때임을 잘 알고 있었다. 그리고 젊고 인물 좋은 총각 귀족이 산울타리의 검은딸기처럼 열리는 것이 아니라는 것도 알고 있었다. 러프턴 경이 그녀에게 청혼했다면 그녀는 미래의 하틀탑 후작부인에게 딸릴 더 큰 영광을 두고 조금도 아까워하지 않고 그를 곧바로 받아들였을 것이다. 그 방면으로 그녀에게 지혜가 모자라지는 않았다. 그러나 러프턴 경은 청혼하지 않았고, 그럴 뜻이 있다는 표시도 내보이지 않았다. 그리고 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 공평하게 말하자면, 그녀는 먼저 말을 붙이는 처녀가 아니었다. 덤벨로 경도 청혼하지는 않았다. 그러나 그는 표시를 내보였다. 새들이 서로 주고받는 것과 같은 말 없는 표시였는데, 혀보다 발끝 쓰기를 더 좋아하는 처녀에게는 말로 하는 표시만큼이나 뜻이 잘 통하는 것이었다.\n\n“그건 생각해 보지 않았어요.” 그리젤다가 아주 차갑게 말했다. 그리고 그 순간 한 신사가 그녀 앞에 서서 다음 춤에 손을 내어 달라고 청했다. 덤벨로 경이었다. 그리젤다는 가볍게 머리를 숙이는 것 말고는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 일어나 짝의 팔에 손을 걸었다.\n\n“다 끝나면 여기서 뵐 수 있을까요, 레이디 러프턴?” 그녀는 이렇게 말하고 춤추는 사람들 사이로 나아갔다. 앞에 놓인 일이 춤이라면 신사가 해야 할 마땅한 일은 어쨌든 부인에게 청하는 것이다. 그 마땅한 일을 러프턴 경은 빼먹었고, 이제 상은 바로 그의 코앞에서 채여 가 버렸다.\n\n덤벨로 경이 그 미인을 데리고 걸어갈 때 그의 몸에는 뚜렷이 승리의 기색이 감돌았다. 세상은 러프턴 경이 그녀와 혼인할 것이라고 말해 왔고, 또 덤벨로 경이 그녀를 마음에 두고 있다고도 말해 왔다. 이것이 덤벨로 경을 화나게 하여, 마치 자기가 뜻을 이루지 못한 구혼자로서 비웃음거리가 되어 돌아다니는 것 같은 느낌을 주었다. 러프턴 경이 없었다면 그는 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 그렇게까지 마음에 두지 않았을지도 모른다. 그러나 일이 그렇게 돌아가 그는 그녀를 마음에 두게 되었고, 같은 물건을 탐내는 자가 누구든 간에 후작 자리를 이을 자로서 원하는 것을 손에 넣는 것이 자기가 마땅히 할 일이라고 여겼다. 그림이 경매에서 그렇게 잘 팔리는 것도 이런 식이다. 덤벨로 경은 그랜틀리 양이 이제 경매인의 망치 아래 놓인 것으로 보았고, 러프턴 경이 자기와 값을 다투어 부르고 있다고 여겼다. 그래서 그가 그리젤다의 허리에 팔을 두르고 음악에 맞추어 방을 오가며 그녀를 빙빙 돌릴 때 그의 몸에는 승리의 기색이 감돌았다.\n\n레이디 러프턴과 아들은 서로를 바라보며 남게 되었다. 물론 그는 그리젤다에게 춤을 청할 생각이었으나, 그렇게 되지 못한 것을 그가 몹시 서운해했다고는 할 수 없었다. 물론 레이디 러프턴도 아들과 그리젤다가 함께 일어설 것이라고 기대했었고, 자기가 거두어 주는 아이에게 조금 화가 나는 쪽으로 기울었다.\n\n“잠깐쯤 기다려 줄 수도 있었을 텐데.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“왜요, 어머니? 아무도 기다려 주지 않는 일이 있잖아요. 이를테면 사냥터에서 문을 먼저 지나가라고 벗에게 양보하는 것 같은 일 말이에요. 그랜틀리 양은 먼저 청한 사람을 잡은 것이 아주 옳았어요.”\n\n레이디 러프턴은 이 계획의 끝이 어떻게 될지를 알아 두기로 마음을 굳혔다. 그리젤다를 언제까지나 데리고 있을 수는 없었고, 무엇이든 정해질 것이라면 두 사람이 다 런던에 있는 지금 정해져야 했다. 사교철이 끝나면 그리젤다는 플럼스테드로 돌아갈 것이고, 러프턴 경은 어디로 갈지 아직 아무도 몰랐다. 더 뒤의 기회를 바라보는 것은 소용없는 일이었다. 지금 서로 사랑하게 되지 못한다면 앞으로도 결코 그리되지 못할 것이었다. 레이디 러프턴은 자기 계획이 되지 않을까 봐 두려워지기 시작했으나, 적어도 아들에 관해서만은 바로 그 자리에서 진실을 알아내겠다고 마음을 정했다.\n\n“아, 그래. 그렇지. 누구와 춤을 추든 그 아이에게 다 같다면 말이다.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“다 같겠지요. 덤벨로가 저보다 숨이 길다는 것 말고는요.”\n\n“그 아이를 그런 식으로 말하는 것을 들으니 서운하구나, 루도빅.”\n\n“왜 서운하세요, 어머니?”\n\n“나는 바랐기 때문이지—너와 그 아이가 서로 좋아하게 되기를.” 그녀는 다정하고도 슬픈, 진지한 목소리로 이렇게 말하면서, 자기가 그에게 큰 청을 넣고 있음을 아는 사람처럼 애원하는 눈길로 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다.\n\n“예, 어머니. 어머니가 그것을 바라신다는 건 알고 있었어요.”\n\n“알고 있었다고, 루도빅!”\n\n“아이고, 그럼요. 어머니는 저에게 비밀을 감추는 데 조금도 날래지 못하시거든요. 그리고 어머니, 한때는, 하루쯤은, 어머니 뜻을 들어 드릴 수 있겠다고 생각했어요. 어머니가 저에게 그토록 잘해 주셨으니, 저는 어머니를 위해서라면 거의 무엇이든 하겠어요.”\n\n“아니, 아니, 아니야.” 그녀는 아들의 칭찬을, 그리고 그가 자기의 희망과 바람을 바쳐 내놓는 듯한 그 희생을 물리치며 말했다. “세상을 다 준다 해도 나 때문에 네가 그러기를 바라지는 않는다. 나보다 더 좋은 아들을 둔 어머니는 없었어. 내 유일한 욕심은 네 행복이야.”\n\n“하지만 어머니, 그 사람은 저를 행복하게 해 주지 못할 거예요. 한순간 그럴 수 있으리라고 생각할 만큼 제가 미쳐 있었지요. 한순간은 정말 그렇게 생각했어요. 저를 받아 달라고 청하려 했던 때가 한 번 있었어요. 하지만—”\n\n“하지만 뭐냐, 루도빅?”\n\n“아무것도 아니에요. 지나가 버렸어요. 이제는 결코 청하지 않을 겁니다. 사실 그 사람도 저를 받아 주지 않을 것 같아요. 그 사람은 뜻이 크고, 저보다 높은 사냥감을 노리고 있어요. 그리고 그 사람을 위해 이 말은 해 두어야겠는데, 그 사람은 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 잘 알고 있고, 손에 패를 쥐고 태어난 사람처럼 패를 놀립니다.”\n\n“결코 청하지 않겠다는 말이니?”\n\n“예, 어머니. 청했다 해도 그것은 어머니를 사랑해서였을 거예요. 오직 어머니를 사랑해서요.”\n\n“세상을 다 준다 해도 네가 그러는 것은 바라지 않는다.”\n\n“그 사람은 덤벨로에게 가게 두세요. 그 사람은 덤벨로에게 아주 훌륭한 아내가 될 거예요. 바로 덤벨로가 바랄 그런 아내지요. 그리고 어머니는 그 사람이 그런 혼사를 이루도록 도와주신 셈이 되어 아주 잘해 주신 게 되고요.”\n\n“하지만 루도빅, 나는 네가 자리를 잡는 걸 보고 싶어 못 견디겠구나.”\n\n“다 때가 되면 되지요, 어머니!”\n\n“아, 하지만 그 좋은 때가 지나가고 있어. 세월이 어찌나 빨리 달아나는지. 혼인할 생각은 하고 있겠지, 루도빅.”\n\n“하지만 어머니, 제가 어머니 마음에 들지 않는 아내를 데려온다면요?”\n\n“네가 사랑하는 사람이라면 누구든 마음에 들어 하겠다. 그러니까—”\n\n“그러니까, 어머니도 그 사람을 사랑한다면 말이지요? 그렇죠, 어머니?”\n\n“하지만 나는 네 취향을 그토록 믿고 있단다. 너는 숙녀답고 착하지 않은 사람은 좋아할 수 없다는 걸 안다.”\n\n“숙녀답고 착하다! 그것으로 넉넉할까요?” 그는 루시 로바츠를 생각하며 말했다.\n\n“그래, 네가 그 사람을 사랑한다면 넉넉하지. 나는 네가 돈을 마음에 두는 것을 바라지 않는다. 그리젤다에게는 요긴했을 재산이 있겠지만, 나는 네가 그것을 마음에 두기를 바라지 않아.” 이리하여 던스터블 양의 붐비는 방에 함께 서 있는 동안, 어머니와 아들은 러프턴–그랜틀리 동맹 조약을 비준하지 않기로 둘 사이에 정해 버렸다. “그랜틀리 부인에게 알려야겠구나.” 그리젤다가 자기 옆으로 돌아올 때 레이디 러프턴은 혼자 이렇게 말했다. 덤벨로 경과 그 짝 사이에 오간 말은 열두 마디가 채 되지 않았으나, 그 젊은 아가씨 쪽에서도 이제 위에 말한 그 조약이 결코 발효되지 않게 하겠다고 마음을 완전히 굳혀 두고 있었다.\n\n우리는 이 집 주인에게로 돌아가야 한다. 이 장은 그녀가 큰 위급한 상황에서 얼마나 잘 처신할 수 있는지를 보이려고 쓴 것이니, 그렇게 오래 그녀를 두고 떠나 있어서는 안 되었다. 그녀는 얼마쯤 지나면 현관문 가까운 자리를 떠나 사람들 가운데서 자기만의 벗들을 찾아낼 수 있을 것이라고 밝혀 두었었다. 그러나 그럴 기회는 밤이 아주 깊어서야 왔다. 손님은 끊이지 않고 이어졌다. 그녀는 짧은 인사말을 짓느라 죽을 만큼 지쳤고, 자기 자리를 해럴드 스미스 부인에게 대신 맡겨야겠다고 한 번 이상 말했다.\n\n그 부인은 이 모든 수고 내내 감탄할 만한 한결같음으로 그녀 곁에 붙어 있어서 그 일을 견딜 만하게 만들어 주었다. 벗 쪽에서 그만한 한결같음이 없었다면 견딜 수 없는 일이었을 것이다. 그리고 이것이 해럴드 스미스 부인의 큰 공로였음은 인정해야 한다. 이 큰 상속녀에 관한 그녀 자신의 희망은 모두 부서졌고, 그 대답은 아주 뚜렷한 말로 그녀에게 주어졌다. 그럼에도 그녀는 우정에 성실했고, 이 집에 시누이로서의 권리를 가진 사람이나 되는 것처럼 이날의 고단함을 견디려 들었다.\n\n한 시쯤에 그녀의 오라비가 왔다. 청혼이 있은 뒤로 그는 아직 던스터블 양을 만나지 못한 터였고, 누이동생이 어렵사리 달래어 이제 얼굴을 내밀게 한 것이었다.\n\n“무슨 소용이 있겠나?” 그가 말했다. “나는 이제 판이 끝났어.” 이 가난하고 망해 버린 못난이가 뜻한 것은, 던스터블 양을 상대로 한 그 판이 끝났다는 것만이 아니라, 그의 온 생애라는 큰 판이 편치 못한 끝을 향해 몰려가고 있다는 것이었다.\n\n“터무니없는 소리 하지 마세요.” 누이동생이 말했다. “옴니엄 공작 같은 사람이 자기 돈을 돌려 달라고 한다고 절망할 셈이에요? 그분에게 좋은 담보였던 것이라면 다른 사람에게도 좋은 담보가 될 거예요.” 그러고 나서 해럴드 스미스 부인은 던스터블 양에게 여느 때보다 더 살갑게 굴었다.\n\n던스터블 양이 거의 지쳐 쓰러질 지경이었으나 아직 기다려지는 그 큰 도착에 대한 희망으로 스스로를 떠받치려 애쓰고 있을 때—그 주인공이 얼굴을 내밀어 주는 복이 자기에게 온다면 그것은 아주 늦은 시각에야 있을 일임을 그녀는 알고 있었다—사워비 씨가 계단을 올라왔다. 그는 자기가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 태연한 뻔뻔함을 다 갖추고 이 시련을 지나가도록 스스로를 다잡아 두었다. 그러나 그 모든 뻔뻔함이 그에게 넉넉한 힘이 되어 주지 못했음이, 또 이 부인의 참으로 사람 좋은 기색이 없었다면 이 대면이 난처했을 것임이 뚜렷이 보였다.\n\n“우리 오라버니가 왔어요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. 속삭이는 목소리가 떨리는 것으로 그녀가 이 만남을 얼마간 걱정스럽게 바라보고 있음이 드러났다.\n\n“어떻게 지내세요, 사워비 씨?” 던스터블 양이 그를 맞으려고 거의 문간까지 걸어 나가며 말했다. “늦어도 아예 안 오는 것보다는 낫지요.”\n\n“이제 겨우 하원에서 빠져나왔습니다.” 그가 그녀에게 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n“아, 잘 알아요. 의원들 가운데서 당신은 「상 르프로슈」시지요. 해럴드 스미스 씨가 「상 푀르」이신 것처럼요. 그렇지 않아요, 이봐요?”\n\n“두 사람 모두에게 여간 매섭지 않게 해내셨다는 건 인정해야겠어요.” 스미스 부인이 웃으며 말했다. “그리고 딱한 해럴드에 관해서는 아주 억울한 일이지요. 나다니엘은 여기 있으니 스스로 변명할 수 있겠고요.”\n\n“그리고 언제 어느 때든 스스로를 변명하는 데 이분보다 나은 사람은 없지요. 그런데 사워비 씨, 저는 절망해서 죽을 지경이에요. 그가 올 거라고 보세요?”\n\n“그요? 누구 말입니까?”\n\n“이 답답한 분—‘그’가 둘이나 있는 것처럼 말씀하시네요! 둘이었지요. 하지만 다른 한 사람은 벌써 왔다 갔어요.”\n\n“맹세하지만 저는 모르겠군요.” 사워비 씨가 이제 다시 마음이 편해져 말했다. “하지만 제가 할 수 있는 일이 있습니까? 가서 누구를 데려올까요? 아, 톰 타워스! 그 일은 제가 도울 수 없을 것 같습니다. 하지만 저기 계단 아래에 그가 와 있네요!” 그러고서 사워비 씨는 누이동생과 함께 뒤로 물러서서 이 시대를 대표하는 위인에게 길을 내주었다.\n\n“은총의 천사들과 사자들이여, 저를 도우소서!” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “대체 어떻게 처신해야 하나요? 사워비 씨, 제가 무릎을 꿇어야 한다고 보세요? 이봐요, 그가 왕실 제복을 입은 기자를 등 뒤에 데리고 올까요?” 그러고서 던스터블 양은 두세 걸음 나아갔는데—사워비 씨에게 그랬던 것처럼 문간까지 나가지는 않았다—손을 내밀고 《주피터》의 타워스 씨에게 가장 달콤한 웃음을 지어 보였다.\n\n“타워스 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “제 집에서 뵐 기회를 얻게 되어 기쁩니다.”\n\n“던스터블 양, 이 자리에 있게 된 특권으로 저는 대단한 영광을 입었습니다.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“영광은 온통 제 쪽에 내려진 것이지요.” 그러면서 그녀는 아주 위엄 있는 우아함으로 머리를 숙이고 몸을 굽혀 인사했다. 둘은 서로의 농지거리를 속속들이 알아들었다. 그러고 나서 잠깐 사이에 두 사람은 아주 편안한 이야기에 들어갔다.\n\n“그런데 사워비, 이번에 겁주고 있는 해산 이야기는 어떻게 보나?” 톰 타워스가 말했다.\n\n“우리 모두 섭리의 손안에 있지.” 사워비 씨가 겉으로는 아무런 감정도 드러내지 않으려 애쓰며 말했다. 그러나 이 문제는 그에게 무섭게 중대한 것이었고, 이때까지 그는 그런 위협에 대해 들은 바가 없었다. 해럴드 스미스 부인도, 던스터블 양도, 그리고 이제 타워스 씨의 예언을 직접 듣거나 곧바로 전해 듣게 된 백 명의 다른 사람들도 들은 바가 없었다. 그러나 어떤 사람들에게는 그런 소식을 처음 지어내는 일이 주어져 있고, 예언의 성취는 흔히 예언자의 권위가 이루어 내는 것이다. 이튿날 아침에는 의회가 해산될 것이라는 소문이 온 상류 사회에 돌았다. “그자들은 그런 일에 양심이 없어. 양심이라고는 도무지 없지.” 어느 작은 신이 거인들을 두고 이렇게 말했다. 선거구에 돈이 많이 드는 작은 신이었다.\n\n타워스 씨는 거기 서서 이십 분쯤 이야기를 나누다가, 방 안으로는 들어가 보지도 않고 떠났다. 그는 자기가 초대된 목적에 맞게 응해 주었고, 던스터블 양을 즐거운 마음으로 남겨 두었다.\n\n“그가 와 주어서 정말 기뻐요.” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 승리한 기색으로 말했다.\n\n“그래요, 나도 기뻐요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “그러면서도 내가 그렇다는 것이 아주 부끄럽고요. 결국 그가 나에게, 아니 누구에게 무슨 좋은 일을 해 주었나요?” 이런 도덕적인 생각을 입에 담고 나서 그녀는 방 안으로 들어갔고, 이내 손 선생이 벽에 붙어 혼자 서 있는 것을 찾아냈다.\n\n“그런데 선생님.” 그녀가 말했다. “메리와 프랭크는 어디 있어요? 여기 혼자 서 계시니 도무지 편안해 보이지 않네요.”\n\n“고맙지만 예상했던 만큼은 편안해요.” 그가 말했다. “두 사람은 방 어딘가에 있고, 내가 보기에는 나만큼 즐거워하고 있지요.”\n\n“그런 식으로 말하시는 건 심술이에요, 선생님. 오늘 저녁에 내가 겪어 낸 것을 다 견뎌 내라는 소리를 들으셨다면 뭐라고 하셨을까요?”\n\n“취향이란 따질 수 없는 것이지요. 그래도 당신은 이런 걸 좋아하나 보군요.”\n\n“그건 나도 그렇게 확실하지 않아요. 팔을 좀 주세요. 저녁이라도 먹어야겠어요. 사람은 힘든 일을 해냈다는 생각을 늘 좋아하고, 성공했다는 것도 늘 좋아하지요.”\n\n“덕은 그 자체가 보상이라는 걸 우리 모두 알고 있지요.” 선생이 말했다.\n\n“그건 나에게 좀 야박한 말씀이네요.” 던스터블 양이 식탁에 앉으며 말했다. “그러면 내가 이런 모임을 여는 데서는 어떤 종류의 좋은 일도 나올 수 없다고 정말로 생각하시는 거예요?”\n\n“아, 그렇지는 않아요. 몇몇 사람은 틀림없이 즐거웠겠지요.”\n\n“선생님이 보시기에는 다 헛됨이지요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “헛됨이요 마음의 괴로움이라. 그야 뒤쪽은 확실히 잔뜩 있지요. 셰리를 좀 주세요. 맥주 한 잔이라면 무엇이든 내놓겠지만 그건 말도 안 되는 일이고요. 헛됨이요 마음의 괴로움이라! 그런데도 나는 좋은 일을 하려고 했던 거예요.”\n\n“내가 당신을 탓하고 있다고는 부디 여기지 말아요, 던스터블 양.”\n\n“아, 하지만 나는 그렇게 여겨요. 선생님만이 아니라, 아마 선생님의 판단보다 더 마음에 두는 또 한 사람도요. 그리고 그건, 말해 두지만, 대단한 말이랍니다. 선생님은 나를 탓하고 있어요, 손 선생님. 그리고 나도 나를 탓해요. 내가 잘못을 했다는 게 아니라, 이 놀음이 촛값만큼도 값이 나가지 않는다는 거지요.”\n\n“아, 바로 그게 문제지요.”\n\n“이 놀음은 촛값만큼도 값이 나가지 않아요. 그러면서도 공작과 톰 타워스를 다 모신 것은 승리였지요. 내가 그리 서투르게 해내지는 않았다고 인정하셔야 해요.”\n\n그 뒤 얼마 안 되어 그레셤 부부가 돌아갔고, 한 시간쯤 지나서야 던스터블 양은 제 침상까지 몸을 끌고 갈 수 있게 되었다.\n\n그런 모든 자리에서 물어야 할 큰 물음이 바로 이것이다. “이 놀음이 촛값만큼은 값이 나가는가?”\n\n# 제30장. 그랜틀리 집안의 승리\n\n지나가는 말로 언급한 적이 있는데—독자께서는 틀림없이 잊으셨을 터이나—그랜틀리 부인은 던스터블 양의 연회에 참석할 생각으로 런던에 올라오라는 남편의 특별한 초청을 받지 못했다. 그랜틀리 부인은 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았으나 적잖이 속이 상했다. 그 이름난 모임에 가지 못해 입은 손실 때문이 아니라, 딸의 일이야말로 어머니의 눈길로 살펴 주어야 한다고 느꼈기 때문이었다. 게다가 그녀는 저 러프턴-그랜틀리 조약이 끝내 인준될지를 의심하고 있었고, 의심하는 터라 딸을 레이디 러프턴의 손에 맡겨 두는 것이 그리 마음 편하지 않았다. 남편이 올라가기 전에 한두 마디 해 두기는 했으나 정말 한두 마디뿐이었으니, 그처럼 미묘한 일을 남편에게 맡기기가 망설여졌던 것이다. 그러므로 남편이 떠난 지 이틀째 되는 아침에 당장 런던으로 오라는 그의 편지를 받고 적잖이 놀랐다. 놀라기는 했으나 그녀의 마음은 낙담보다 오히려 희망으로 가득 찼으니, 딸의 분별을 온전히 믿고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n연회가 있은 다음 날 아침, 레이디 러프턴과 그리젤다는 여느 때처럼 함께 아침을 먹었으나 서로 상대의 태도가 달라졌음을 느꼈다. 레이디 러프턴은 이 젊은 벗이 여느 때보다 조금 덜 살펴 주고, 어쩌면 몸가짐도 덜 순순하다고 생각했다. 그리젤다는 레이디 러프턴이 덜 다정하다고 느꼈다. 그러나 둘 사이에 말은 거의 없었고, 마차가 문 앞에 왔을 때 그리젤다가 마님을 모시고 나가는 대신 집에 혼자 남아 있게 해 달라고 청했을 때에도 레이디 러프턴은 놀라움을 나타내지 않았다.\n\n그날 오후 브루턴 스트리트를 찾아온 사람은 부주교 말고는 아무도 없었다—적어도 들여보낸 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그는 저물 무렵에 와서 레이디 러프턴이 돌아올 때까지 딸과 함께 있었다. 그러고는 여느 때의 그답지 않게 훨씬 불쑥 작별을 하고, 방문이 그처럼 길어진 까닭에 대해서는 이렇다 할 말을 하지 않았다. 그리젤다도 이렇다 할 말을 하지 않았다. 그리하여 저녁은 흘러갔고, 두 사람은 저마다 저도 모르는 사이에 전보다 상대와 덜 친밀한 사이가 되었음을 느꼈다.\n\n이튿날도 그리젤다는 나가려 하지 않았는데, 네 시에 하인이 마운트 스트리트에서 온 편지를 그녀에게 가져왔다. 어머니가 런던에 도착했고 당장 딸을 보고 싶어 한다는 것이었다. 그랜틀리 부인은 레이디 러프턴에게 안부를 전하며, 다섯 시 반에 혹은 레이디 러프턴이 만나기에 편한 그 뒤의 어느 시각에라도 찾아뵙겠다고 했다. 그리젤다는 마운트 스트리트에 남아 저녁을 먹으라고, 편지는 그렇게 말했다. 레이디 러프턴은 말한 시각에 그랜틀리 부인을 만나면 아주 기쁠 것이라고 했다. 그리하여 그리젤다는 이 전갈을 받아 들고 어머니의 셋집으로 떠났다.\n\n“마차를 보내 드릴게요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “열 시쯤이면 되겠지요.”\n\n“고마워요,” 그리젤다가 말했다. “그거면 아주 좋겠어요.” 그러고는 갔다.\n\n정확히 다섯 시 반에 그랜틀리 부인은 레이디 러프턴의 응접실로 안내되어 들어왔다. 딸은 함께 오지 않았고, 레이디 러프턴은 벗의 얼굴빛으로 볼일을 의논하러 왔다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 사실 레이디 러프턴 자신도 볼일을 의논해야 할 처지였으니, 그랜틀리 부인에게 이제 집안 조약이 인준될 수 없다는 것을 알려야 했기 때문이다. 당사자인 신사가 그 결합을 마다한 것이었다. 그리하여 가엾은 레이디 러프턴은 앞에 놓인 일의 성격 때문에 마음이 편치 않았다.\n\n“올라오신 것이 좀 뜻밖이네요,” 벗이 소파에 앉자마자 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“예, 정말 그래요. 바로 오늘 아침에 부주교님한테서 편지를 받았는데, 그것 때문에 꼭 와야만 하게 되었답니다.”\n\n“나쁜 소식은 아니겠지요?” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“예, 나쁜 소식이라고는 할 수 없어요. 하지만 레이디 러프턴, 세상일이 언제나 바라는 그대로 되지는 않는 법이지요.”\n\n“정말 그렇지요,” 마님이 말했다. 지금 이 자리에서 제 마음을 가득 채우고 있는 소식을 그랜틀리 부인에게 설명해야 하는 것이 자신의 의무라는 것을 떠올리면서였다. 그러나 그랜틀리 부인이 먼저 제 이야기를 하도록 두기로 했으니, 어쩌면 한쪽이 다른 쪽과 걸려 있을지도 모른다고 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n“가엾은 우리 그리젤다!” 그랜틀리 부인이 거의 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. “그 아이를 두고 제가 어떤 희망을 품고 있었는지는 레이디 러프턴께 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요.”\n\n“그 아이가 무슨 말이라도 했나요—무슨, 그런—”\n\n“그 아이도 당장 말씀드렸을 거예요—그러는 것이 마땅히 댁에 대한 도리이기도 했지요—하지만 겁이 났던 거예요. 그럴 만도 했고요. 게다가 마음을 아주 굳히기 전에 아비와 저를 만나 보는 것이 옳았지요. 아무튼 이제 정해졌다고는 말씀드릴 수 있어요.”\n\n“무엇이 정해졌다는 거지요?” 레이디 러프턴이 물었다.\n\n“이런 일이 어떻게 될지야 물론 누구도 미리 알 수 없는 것이지요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 필요 이상으로 에두르며 변두리만 두드렸다. “제 마음의 가장 간절한 소원은 그 아이가 러프턴 경과 결혼하는 것을 보는 것이었어요. 그 아이를 저와 같은 주 안에 두고 싶은 마음이 정말 컸고, 그런 혼사라면 제 야심을 온전히 채워 주었을 텐데요.”\n\n“아무렴, 그야 채워 주었겠지요!” 레이디 러프턴은 이 말을 소리 내어 하지는 않았으나 마음속으로 그렇게 생각했다. 그랜틀리 부인은 제 딸과 러프턴 경의 혼사를 두고, 마치 그것을 참고 받아들이는 데에 그리스도인다운 절제를 얼마간 보여 주기라도 했을 것처럼 말하고 있는 것이 아닌가! 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 아주 좋은 처녀일지는 모른다. 그러나 그 아이라 해도—레이디 러프턴은 그 순간 그렇게 생각했다—값이 너무 높게 붙었을 수 있었다.\n\n“그랜틀리 부인,” 그녀가 말했다. “저는 요 며칠 사이에 이 일에 대한 우리 서로의 희망이 이루어지지 않으리라는 것을 미리 알고 있었어요. 러프턴 경 말인데요, 제 생각에는—하지만 설명할 필요는 없겠지요— 부인께서 런던에 올라오지 않으셨다면 제가 편지를 썼을 거예요. 아마 오늘쯤에요. 그 아이의 인생이 어떤 운명이 되든, 저는 그 아이가 행복하기를 진심으로 바라요.”\n\n“그렇게 될 거라고 생각해요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 크게 만족한 기색이 드러나는 어조로 말했다.\n\n“혹시—혹시 무슨 일이—”\n\n“덤벨로 경이 요전 밤 던스터블 양의 연회에서 그 아이에게 청혼했어요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 눈을 바닥에 붙인 채, 갑자기 몸가짐에 자못 순순한 태를 지으며 말했다. “그리고 그분은 어제 부주교님과 함께 있었고, 오늘 아침에도 또 그랬지요. 지금 이 순간에도 마운트 스트리트에 있을 것 같아요.”\n\n“아, 그렇군요!” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. 그 순간 그 소식에 아무 조건 없는 만족을 어조와 태도로 나타낼 만한 자제력을 지녔더라면, 그녀는 세상을 다 주고라도 그것을 얻었을 것이다. 그러나 그런 자제력은 없었고, 자신에게 그것이 모자란다는 것을 그녀는 괴롭게 알고 있었다.\n\n“예,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “일이 이만큼 정해졌고, 또 댁에서 그 아이를 그처럼 고맙게 염려해 주시는 것을 아는 터라, 곧바로 알려 드리는 것이 옳다고 생각했어요. 덤벨로 경의 처신은 더할 수 없이 반듯하고 명예롭고 후하답니다. 그러니 통틀어 보면 이 혼사는 저와 부주교님이 만족하지 않을 수 없는 것이지요.”\n\n“확실히 대단한 혼사네요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “레이디 하틀탑은 벌써 만나 보셨나요?”\n\n그런데 레이디 하틀탑이란 사람은 반가운 인연이라고 볼 수 없었으니, 이것이야말로 레이디 러프턴의 입에서 새어 나온 말 가운데 어떤 식으로든 깎아내리는 것으로 볼 수 있는 유일한 말이었다. 그러니 통틀어 보면 나는 그녀가 잘 처신했다고 생각한다.\n\n“덤벨로 경은 워낙 온전히 제 몸의 주인이라 그럴 필요가 없었어요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “후작께는 말씀이 들어갔고, 부주교님이 내일이나 모레 후작을 뵐 거예요.”\n\n레이디 러프턴에게 남은 일은 벗에게 축하를 하는 것뿐이었고, 그녀는 어쩌면 그다지 진심이 아닐 말로 그것을 했으나, 통틀어 보면 말을 고르기를 나쁘게 하지는 않았다.\n\n“그 아이가 아주 행복하기를 정말로 바라요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그리고 그 결합이”—이 낱말은 그랜틀리 부인의 귀에 아주 흐뭇하게 들렸다—“부인과 그 아이 아버지께 순수한 기쁨만을 드리기를 믿어요. 그 아이가 채우도록 불려 간 자리는 참으로 찬란한 자리인데, 저는 그것이 그 아이의 값보다 높다고는 생각하지 않아요.”\n\n이것은 매우 너그러운 말이었고, 그랜틀리 부인도 그렇게 느꼈다. 그녀는 제 소식이 가장 차가운 예의로 받아들여질 줄로 예상하고 있었고, 기회가 온다면 한바탕 싸울 각오도 충분히 되어 있었다. 그러나 전쟁을 바라지는 않았으므로, 레이디 러프턴의 따뜻함에 거의 고마움을 느꼈다.\n\n“레이디 러프턴,” 그녀가 말했다. “그렇게 말씀해 주시니 참 고마워요. 다른 누구에게도 말하지 않았고, 물론 댁이 아시기 전에는 아무에게도 말하지 않을 참이었어요. 그 아이를 댁만큼 잘 알고 잘 헤아려 준 사람은 아무도 없어요. 그리고 이것만은 자신 있게 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 그 아이가 새로운 삶의 자리에서 기다리는 우정 가운데, 댁의 우정을 기다리는 만큼의 절반이라도 기쁘게 기다리는 것은 하나도 없다는 것을요.”\n\n레이디 러프턴은 그 이상 말을 많이 하지 않았다. 앞으로 하틀탑 후작부인이 될 사람과 친밀하게 지내는 데서 큰 기쁨을 기대한다고는 차마 말할 수 없었다. 하틀탑 집안과 러프턴 집안은 적어도 그녀의 세대 동안은 서로 딴 세상에서 살아야 했고, 그녀는 그랜틀리 부인과의 오랜 벗 사이가 요구하는 말은 이제 다 한 것이었다. 그랜틀리 부인도 이 모든 것을 레이디 러프턴만큼이나 잘 알고 있었다. 그러나 그랜틀리 부인이 세상 물정에는 훨씬 나은 여인이었다.\n\n그리젤다는 그날 밤만 브루턴 스트리트로 돌아오고, 그로써 머무름을 끝내기로 정해졌다.\n\n“부주교님은 당장은 제가 런던에 남아 있는 편이 낫다고 생각해요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “그리고 사정이 워낙 특별하니 그 아이도—어쩌면 저와 함께 있는 것이 더 편할 거예요.”\n\n이 말에 레이디 러프턴은 전적으로 동의했다. 그리하여 두 사람은 더없이 좋은 벗으로 헤어졌으니, 서로를 지극히 다정한 몸짓으로 껴안았다.\n\n그날 저녁 그리젤다는 과연 브루턴 스트리트로 돌아왔고, 레이디 러프턴은 그 아이를 축하해야 하는 또 하나의 일을 치러야 했다. 둘 가운데 이쪽이 더 언짢은 일이었으니, 미리 궁리해 두어야 했던 만큼 더욱 그러했다. 그러나 이 젊은 아가씨의 뛰어난 분별과 야무진 성품이 그 일을 비교적 쉬운 것으로 만들어 주었다. 그녀는 울지도, 격해지지도, 히스테리를 일으키지도, 어떤 감정을 드러내지도 않았다. 고결한 자기 덤벨로니, 후한 자기 덤벨로니 하는 말조차 하지 않았다. 레이디 러프턴의 입맞춤을 거의 잠자코 받고, 그 친절에 나직이 고맙다고 하고, 앞으로 제게 올 영화에 대해서는 한마디도 비추지 않았다.\n\n“저는 일찍 자리에 들었으면 좋겠어요,” 그녀가 말했다. “짐을 챙겨야 하니까요.”\n\n“그건 리처즈가 다 해 줄 거예요, 얘야.”\n\n“아, 예, 고마워요. 리처즈만큼 친절한 사람은 없지요. 하지만 제 옷은 제가 챙기겠어요.” 그리하여 그녀는 일찍 자리에 들었다.\n\n레이디 러프턴은 그 뒤 이틀 동안 아들을 보지 못했으나, 보게 되었을 때에는 물론 그리젤다에 대해 한두 마디를 했다.\n\n“소식은 들었니, 루도빅?” 그녀가 물었다.\n\n“아, 예. 클럽마다 그 얘기예요. 버들가지 선물이 쏟아져 저는 파묻힐 지경이었어요.”\n\n“적어도 너는 아쉬워할 것이 없겠지,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“어머니도 마찬가지죠. 어머니도 아쉬워하실 것이 없다고 저는 믿어요. 아쉽지 않다고 말해 주세요. 어머니, 저를 위해서 그렇게 말해 주세요. 그 사람이 제 아내로 행복하게 지낼 만한 사람도, 저를 행복하게 해 줄 만한 사람도 아니었다는 것을, 어머니도 마음 깊은 속으로는 알고 계시지 않나요?”\n\n“그럴지도 모르지,” 레이디 러프턴이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. 그러고는 아들에게 입을 맞추고, 잉글랜드의 어떤 처녀도 이 아이에게 걸맞을 만큼 훌륭하지는 못하다고 마음속으로 단정했다.\n\n# 제31장. 노르웨이의 연어낚시\n\n덤벨로 경과 그리젤다 그랜틀리의 약혼은 그 뒤 열흘 동안 장안의 화제였다. 적어도 그것은 사람들의 이목을 독차지한 두 가지 이야기 가운데 하나였고, 다른 하나는 던스터블 양의 모임에서 톰 타워스가 처음 굴려 놓은 저 끔찍한 소문, 곧 의회가 해산될 위험이 있다는 소문이었다.\n\n“어쩌면 결국은 그게 우리에게 가장 좋은 일이 될지도 모르지요,” 크루 정션에서라면 자기 자리가 웬만큼 안전하다고 여기고 있던 그린 워커 씨가 말했다.\n\n“나는 그것을 아주 못된 술책으로 보오,” 자기 선거구에서는 그만큼 안전하지 못했고 선거에 드는 비용이 달갑지 않았던 해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “가을을 넘길 시간을 벌자고 하는 짓이오. 해산을 해 본다고 저들이 표 열 개를 더 얻지도 못할 것이고, 마흔 개가 못 되면 다수를 잡기도 어려울 것이오. 그런데도 저들에게는 공무를 맡은 자의 의무감이라는 것이 없소 — 조금도 없소. 하기야 그런 것을 가진 자가 대체 누가 있는지 나도 모르겠소만.”\n\n“그렇지요, 맹세코. 바로 그것입니다. 우리 아주머니 레이디 하틀탑도 그렇게 말씀하세요. 세상에 의무감이라는 것이 남아 있지 않다고요. 그런데 말이지요, 덤벨로가 스스로를 얼마나 별난 바보로 만들고 있는지!” 그러고 나서 이야기는 저 다른 화제로 넘어갔다.\n\n러프턴 경이 버들가지를 두고 스스로를 놀린 농담은 아주 훌륭했고, 그 일로 그의 마음이 쓰리다고는 아무도 꿈에도 생각하지 않았다. 세상은 스스로 어리석은 혼사라 부르기로 한 그 일을 두고 덤벨로 경을 비웃고 있었고, 러프턴 경의 벗들은 그 자신도 같은 방향으로 얼간이 짓을 했을 수 있다고는 한 번도 의심해 본 적이 없다는 듯이 그에게 그 이야기를 했다. 그러나 그런데도 그는 아주 흐뭇하지는 않았다. 그는 그리젤다와 혼인하기를 결코 바라지 않았다. 어머니의 계략을 처음 의심한 뒤로 그는 세상의 어떤 사정도 자기를 그렇게 하도록 이끌 수 없다고 열두 번도 더 스스로에게 선언했었다. 그녀가 그 아름다움에도 불구하고 차갑고 싱겁고 마음을 끄는 데가 없다고 단언했었다. 그런데도 덤벨로 경이 뜻을 이루었다는 것에 그는 화가 날 지경이었다. 그리고 이것은 더욱 변명할 수 없는 일이었으니, 그가 루시 로바츠를 한 번도 잊은 적이 없고, 그녀를 사랑하기를 그친 적이 없으며, 자기 가슴 안에서 그 여러 가지 이야기를 주고받을 때에는 그리젤다를 흠잡는 만큼이나 큰 목소리로 루시를 편들었기 때문이다.\n\n“그렇다면 당신의 주인공은,” 균형이 잘 잡힌 어느 비평가가 이렇게 말하는 것이 내 귀에 들린다, “그리 값이 나가는 사람이 아니군요.”\n\n우선 러프턴 경은 내 주인공이 아니다. 그리고 다음으로, 사람은 몹시 흠이 많으면서도 값이 크게 나갈 수 있다. 사람은 러프턴 경만큼 흠이 많으면서도 좋은 어머니와 좋은 아내를 가질 자격이 있을 수 있다. 그렇지 않다면 우리 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 지금 가진 어머니와 아내를 가질 자격이 없겠는가! 내 믿음으로는, 세상의 일에, 자식을 낳는 일에, 그리고 그 자식들을 위하여 고기를 썰고 셈을 치르고 몸부림치고 안달하는 일에 자리를 잡고 앉는 젊은이들 가운데, 그 자식들의 어머니가 될 수 있었을 네댓 사람과 먼저 사랑에 빠져 보지 않은 이, 그것도 아마 그 가운데 둘이나 셋과 한꺼번에 사랑에 빠져 보지 않은 이는 거의 없다. 그런데도 이 사내들은 대체로 마침내 제 몫으로 떨어지는 훌륭한 아내를 가질 자격이 있다. 이런 식으로 러프턴 경도 어느 정도까지는 그리젤다와 사랑에 빠져 있었던 것이다. 그의 삶에는 그녀의 분별이 그토록 훌륭하지 않았더라면 그가 그녀에게 손을 내밀었을 순간이 한 번 있었다. 그리고 그 순간은 다시 돌아오지 않았지만, 그리젤다가 남의 것으로 얻어져 남에게 걸치일 처지라는 것을 알게 되었을 때 그는 실망과 비슷한 어떤 감정으로 괴로워했다. 그러면 그는 구유 속의 개였구나, 독자는 이렇게 말할 것이다. 좋다. 그런데 우리 모두가 더하든 덜하든 부지런히 구유 속의 개가 아닌가? 그 구유 속 개짓이야말로 사람 마음의 가장 흔한 모습 가운데 하나가 아닌가?\n\n그러나 그렇다고 러프턴 경이 루시 로바츠를 참으로 사랑하고 있다는 사실이 조금도 덜해지지는 않았다. 어떤 덤벨로가 그 성채 앞에서 공성을 벌이고 있다고 여겨졌더라면, 그의 속상함은 아주 다른 방식으로 드러났을 것이다. 그는 거리낌 없는 얼굴과 흐뭇한 목소리로 그리젤다 그랜틀리에 대해 농담을 할 수 있었다. 그러나 루시에 관해 그와 같은 뜻의 소식을 들었더라면 그는 농담을 할 형편이 아니었을 것이고, 그것이 그의 밥맛에까지 영향을 미치지 않았을까 나는 크게 의심한다.\n\n“어머니,” 그리젤다의 약혼이 알려진 지 하루나 이틀 뒤에 그가 레이디 러프턴에게 말했다. “저는 노르웨이로 낚시를 가려고요.”\n\n“노르웨이로 — 낚시를!”\n\n“예. 꽤 좋은 일행이 모였어요. 클론타프가 가고, 컬페퍼도 —”\n\n“뭐, 그 끔찍한 사람이!”\n\n“그 사람은 낚시에는 솜씨가 아주 좋아요. — 그리고 해딩턴 피블스도, 또 — 또 — 통틀어 여섯이 될 거예요. 그리고 오늘로부터 이레 뒤에 떠나요.”\n\n“좀 갑작스럽구나, 루도빅.”\n\n“예, 갑작스럽지요. 그렇지만 저희는 런던에 진력이 났어요. 저 혼자라면 그렇게 일찍 가고 싶지도 않겠지만, 클론타프와 컬페퍼가 올해는 철이 이르다고 해요. 떠나기 전에 프램리에 내려가 봐야 해요 — 제 말들 일로요. 그래서 내일 거기 가 있겠다고 어머니께 말씀드리러 온 거예요.”\n\n“내일 프램리에! 사흘만 미룰 수 있다면 나도 함께 갈 텐데.”\n\n그러나 러프턴 경은 사흘을 미룰 수 없었다. 아마도 이번에는 자기가 프램리에 있는 동안 어머니가 거기 있는 것을 바라지 않았을 것이다. 마구간 일을 지시하는 데에도 그 일에 매여 있는 동안 혼자라면 마음이 더 편하겠다고 여겼을 것이다. 아무튼 그는 어머니와 함께 가는 것을 마다했고, 이튿날 아침에 정말로 혼자서 프램리로 내려갔다.\n\n“마크,” 그날 한낮 무렵 남편의 서재로 서둘러 들어서며 로바츠 부인이 말했다. “러프턴 경이 집에 와 계셔요. 들었어요?”\n\n“뭐! 여기 프램리에?”\n\n“프램리 코트에 와 계신다고 하인들이 그래요. 카슨이 방목장에서 말 몇 마리와 함께 있는 그분을 보았대요. 가서 만나 보지 않을 거예요?”\n\n“물론 갈 거야,” 서류를 덮으며 마크가 말했다. “레이디 러프턴은 안 계실 테고, 그 사람이 혼자라면 아마 와서 저녁을 먹겠지.”\n\n“그건 모르겠어요,” 딱한 루시를 생각하며 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그 사람은 조금도 까다롭지 않아. 우리한테 되는 것이면 그 사람에게도 될 거야. 아무튼 나는 청해 볼 참이야.” 그리고 더 말을 주고받지 않고 목사는 모자를 집어 들고 벗을 찾아 나섰다.\n\n정원사가 러프턴 경이 프램리에 왔다는 소식을 들고 들어왔을 때 루시 로바츠도 그 자리에 있었고, 패니가 남편에게 알리러 간 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n“그분이 여기 오지는 않겠지요?” 로바츠 부인이 돌아오자마자 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“모르겠어요,” 패니가 말했다. “안 오시기를 바라요. 그러셔서는 안 되고, 그러지 않으실 것 같아요. 그런데 마크는 저녁을 먹으러 오라고 청하겠다고 해요.”\n\n“그러면요, 패니, 저는 병이 나야겠어요. 달리 방도가 없어요.”\n\n“오시지 않을 것 같아요. 그렇게 매정하실 수는 없을 거예요. 정말이지 오시지 않으리라고 확신해요. 그래도 루시에게 말해 두는 것이 옳다고 생각했어요.”\n\n루시 역시 지금의 형편에서 러프턴 경이 목사관으로 올 리는 없다고 여겼다. 그리고 만일 그가 그렇게 한다면 자기가 식탁에 나와 앉아 있을 수는 없으리라고 스스로에게 선언했다. 그러나 그런데도 그가 프램리에 와 있다는 생각이 그녀에게 아주 괴롭기만 한 것은 아니었다. 그녀는 그의 도착에서 어떤 기쁨이 자기에게 오는 것을 인정하지 않았으나, 그래도 그가 곁에 있다는 데에는 그녀 자신도 모르는 사이에 그녀의 마음을 달래 주는 무엇이 있었다. 그러나 저 끔찍한 물음이 남아 있었다. — 그가 저녁을 먹으러 온다는 것이 사실로 드러나면 그녀는 어떻게 처신해야 하는가?\n\n“그분이 정말 오신다면요, 패니,” 잠시 말을 멈춘 뒤에 그녀가 엄숙하게 말했다. “저는 제 방에만 있어야 해요. 그러고서 마크는 무엇이든 마음대로 생각하도록 두는 거예요. 응접실에서 그분 앞에서 바보짓을 하는 것보다는 거기서 바보짓을 하는 편이 저에게 낫겠어요.”\n\n마크 로바츠는 모자와 지팡이를 들고, 러프턴 경이 말들과 마부들과 함께 있는 줄로 알고 있는 집 앞 방목장으로 곧장 건너갔다. 그 역시 마음이 그다지 편안하지는 못했으니, 토저 씨와의 편지 왕래가 늘어나고 있었기 때문이다. 그는 그 지칠 줄 모르는 신사로부터, “만기가 지난 어음들”이 지금 바체스터의 은행에 놓여 있으며 로바츠 씨 본인이 그것에 주의를 기울여 주기를 몹시 바란다는 통고를 받았다. 유별나게 딱한 사정 몇 가지가 겹쳐 일어난 까닭에, 토저 씨가 로바츠 씨의 이름을 믿고 내어 준 여러 금액을 더 이상 시간을 잃지 않고 토저 씨에게 되돌려 주는 것이 반드시 필요하게 되었다는 것이었다. 등등, 등등, 등등. 뚜렷한 위협이 밖으로 드러난 것은 아니었고, 이상하게도 실제 금액이 적혀 있지도 않았다. 그러나 로바츠 씨는 만기가 지난 어음 하나가 아니라 어음들이라고 적혀 있음을 아프도록 꼼꼼한 주의로 알아채지 않을 수 없었다. 만일 토저 씨가 그에게 구백 파운드를 당장 갚으라고 요구한다면 어찌 될 것인가? 지금까지 그는 사워비 씨에게 편지를 써 두었을 뿐이었고, 그 신사로부터 오늘 아침에 답장을 받았을 수도 있었으나 그런 답장은 아직 그에게 닿지 않았다. 그러니 그는 이때 마음이 그다지 편안한 형편이 아니었다.\n\n그는 이내 러프턴 경과 말들 곁에 이르렀다. 말 네댓 마리가 그만큼 되는 사내들과 아이들의 손에 매여 방목장 안을 천천히 걸리고 있었고, 그 등에 덮인 천이 하나씩 차례로 벗겨지고 있었다. 주인이 더 정확하게, 더 흐뭇하게 살펴볼 수 있도록 하려는 것이었다. 그러나 러프턴 경은 이렇게 제 할 일을 하고 제 몫을 치러 내면서도 온 마음으로 그것을 하고 있지는 않았으니, 마부장은 그것을 아주 잘 알아보았다. 그는 그 말들을 두고 안달을 냈고, 살펴보는 척을 웬만큼 갖추고 나면 되도록 빨리 그것들을 눈앞에서 치워 버리고 싶어 하는 듯했다.\n\n“잘 있었나, 러프턴?” 앞으로 나서며 로바츠가 말했다. “자네가 내려왔다고들 해서 곧바로 건너왔네.”\n\n“그래. 오늘 아침에야 닿았고, 곧 자네에게 건너가려던 참이었네. 나는 여섯 주쯤 노르웨이에 가 있을 참인데, 올해는 고기가 어찌나 이르다고들 하는지 당장 떠나야 한다는군. 떠나기 전에 자네와 이야기해 두고 싶은 일이 하나 있네. 사실 무엇보다도 나를 여기까지 내려오게 한 것이 그 일이야.”\n\n말을 하는 그의 태도에는 서두르는 듯하고 아주 편안하지만은 않은 데가 있어서 로바츠의 눈에 걸렸고, 이야기하겠다고 미리 알린 그 일이 나누기에 유쾌한 일은 아니겠구나 하는 생각이 들게 했다. 러프턴 경이 또 토저와 어음에 얽혀 든 것이 아닌가 하는 생각도 들었다.\n\n“오늘 우리와 함께 저녁을 먹지,” 그가 말했다. “내 짐작대로 자네가 아주 혼자라면 말이야.”\n\n“그래, 나 혼자야.”\n\n“그러면 오는 거지?”\n\n“음, 잘 모르겠네. 아니, 저녁을 먹으러 건너가지는 못할 것 같아. 그렇게 언짢은 얼굴은 하지 말게. 지금 곧 다 설명해 줄 테니.”\n\n무슨 바람이 불고 있는 것일까? 그리고 토저의 어음 때문에 러프턴 경이 목사관에서 저녁을 먹는 것이 마땅찮은 일이 될 수 있다니 어찌 그럴 수가 있는가? 그러나 로바츠는 그때는 그 일에 대해 더 아무 말도 하지 않고, 말들을 보는 쪽으로 이야기를 돌렸다.\n\n“유별나게 좋은 짐승들이군,” 그가 말했다.\n\n“음, 그렇지. 나도 모르겠네. 사람이 볼 말을 네댓 마리 가지고 있으면, 어찌 된 일인지 나갈 만한 것은 한 마리도 없거든. 저 밤색 암말은 아무도 찾지 않게 되고 나니 그림 같지. 그런데 지난겨울에는 사냥개를 따라가도록 나를 제대로 실어 준 날이 하루도 없었네. 안으로 들여라, 파운스. 그만하면 됐다.”\n\n“나리, 저 늙은 검정 말놈은 한번 눈여겨보시지 않겠습니까요?” 마부장 파운스가 처량한 어조로 말했다. “그놈은 훌륭합니다요, 나리 — 수사슴만큼이나 훌륭합니다요.”\n\n“사실을 말하면 나는 저것들이 너무 훌륭한 것 같네. 그래도 그만하면 됐다. 들여라. 그러면 마크, 자네가 한가하다면 이 근방을 한 바퀴 돌아보세.”\n\n마크는 물론 한가했고, 그래서 두 사람은 걸음을 떼었다.\n\n“자네는 마구간을 두고 만족시키기가 너무 어려운 사람이군,” 로바츠가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“마구간은 이제 접어 두게,” 러프턴 경이 말했다. “사실은 나는 그 일을 생각하고 있지 않네. 마크,” 이어 그는 아주 느닷없이 말했다. “자네가 나에게 솔직해 주기를 바라네. 자네 누이가 나에 대해 자네에게 무슨 말을 한 적이 있나?”\n\n“내 누이? 루시가?”\n\n“그래. 자네 누이 루시.”\n\n“아니, 없네. 적어도 별다른 것은 없었어. 지금 이 순간 내가 기억할 만한 것은 아무것도 없네.”\n\n“자네 아내도?”\n\n“자네에 대해 말한 적이 있느냐고? — 패니가? 물론 여느 투로는 있었지. 없을 수가 없지 않은가. 그런데 무슨 말인가?”\n\n“내가 자네 누이에게 청혼을 했다고 그 두 사람 가운데 누구든 자네에게 말한 적이 있나?”\n\n“자네가 루시에게 청혼을 했다고?”\n\n“그래. 내가 루시에게 청혼을 했다고.”\n\n“아니, 아무도 나에게 그런 말을 하지 않았네. 나는 그런 일을 꿈에도 생각해 본 적이 없고, 내가 믿는 한 그들도 그렇네. 누가 그런 소문을 퍼뜨렸거나, 그 두 사람 가운데 누구든 그런 일을 비추었다고 말했다면 그것은 비열한 거짓말이야. 맙소사! 러프턴, 자네는 그들을 무엇으로 보는가?”\n\n“하지만 나는 했네,” 나리가 말했다.\n\n“무엇을 했다고?” 목사가 말했다.\n\n“나는 정말 자네 누이에게 청혼을 했네.”\n\n“자네가 루시에게 혼인을 청했다니!”\n\n“그래, 했네. — 신사가 숙녀에게 쓸 수 있는 가장 분명한 말로.”\n\n“그래서 그 아이는 어떤 답을 했나?”\n\n“나를 거절했네. 그리고 마크, 나는 이제 그 청혼을 다시 하겠다는 뚜렷한 뜻으로 여기 내려왔네. 자네 누이의 답보다 더 단호한 것은 있을 수 없었지. 거의 예의에 어긋날 만큼 단호하다고 내게 여겨졌네. 그러나 그래도 그녀에게 무게를 지워서는 안 될 사정들이 그녀에게 무게를 지웠을 수도 있어. 그녀의 사랑이 다른 누구에게도 주어져 있지 않다면 나에게는 아직 기회가 있을지도 모르네. 마음이 약해서는 못 얻는다는 저 옛말 그대로지, 자네도 알 테고. 아무튼 나는 다시 한 번 내 운을 걸어 볼 참이야. 그리고 곰곰이 뜻을 세워 생각해 본 끝에, 그녀를 만나기 전에 자네에게 말해 두어야 한다는 결론에 이르렀네.”\n\n러프턴 경이 루시와 사랑에 빠졌다니! 이 말이 마크 로바츠의 마음속에서 몇 번이고 되풀이되는 동안, 그의 마음은 그 말에 끝없는 놀라움의 표를 덧붙였다. 어떻게 이런 일이 있을 수 있단 말인가 — 그리고 왜? 그가 헤아리기로 누이 루시는 아주 단순한 아이였다. 못생기지야 않았으나 결코 아름답지 않았고, 분명히 아둔하지는 않았으나 결코 빛나지도 않았다. 그리고 자기가 아는 모든 남자 가운데 러프턴 경이야말로 자기 누이 같은 아이와 사랑에 빠질 사람으로는 가장 마지막일 것이라고 그는 말했을 것이었다. 그런데 이제 그는 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 해야 하는가? 어떤 견해를 붙들어야 하는가? 어느 방향으로 움직여야 하는가? 한편에는 레이디 러프턴이 있었으니, 그는 그분에게 모든 것을 입은 몸이었다. 러프턴 경을 누이의 공인된 구혼자로 맞아들이는 데 동의한다면, 그분의 팔꿈치에서 몇 걸음 안 되는 그 목사관에서 그에게 삶이 어떻게 가능할 것인가? 루시에게는 의심할 것 없이 대단한 혼사일 것이다. 그러나 — 정말로 그는 루시가 프램리 코트에 절대로 군림하는 여왕이 될 수 있으리라고는 스스로 믿을 수가 없었다.\n\n“패니가 이 모든 일에 대해 무엇이든 알고 있다고 생각하나?” 잠시 뒤에 그가 말했다.\n\n“나로서는 도무지 알 수 없네. 그녀가 안다면 내가 알려서 안 것은 아니야. 그것은 자네가 가장 잘 답할 수 있으리라고 나는 생각했네.”\n\n“나는 전혀 답할 수가 없네,” 마크가 말했다. “적어도 나는 그런 일을 아주 멀찍이서도 짐작해 본 적이 없어.”\n\n“이제는 자네 짐작이 조금도 멀찍이 있을 필요가 없네,” 러프턴 경이 희미하게 웃으며 말했다. “그리고 자네는 그것을 사실로 알아도 좋아. 나는 그녀에게 혼인을 청했고, 거절당했고, 그것을 되풀이할 참이네. 그리고 나는 지금 자네를 내 속내 안으로 들이고 있는데, 그것은 자네가 그녀의 오라비로서, 또 내 벗으로서 할 수 있는 만큼 나를 도와 주기를 바라서네.” 그러고 나서 두 사람은 몇 발짝을 잠자코 걸었고, 그 뒤에 러프턴 경이 덧붙였다. “그리고 자네가 바란다면 나는 오늘 자네와 저녁을 먹겠네.”\n\n로바츠 씨는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐다. 의무가 자기에게 어떤 답을 요구하는지 생각해 낼 수 없었다. 누이가 스스로 그것을 바란다면 그로서는 누이와 그런 혼사 사이에 끼어들 권리가 없었다. 그러나 그래도 그 생각에는 무언가 끔찍한 데가 있었다! 그것이 나쁜 끝으로 갈 수밖에 없다는, 그 계획이 위태로운 것이라는, 그리고 마침내 그들 가운데 누구에게도 행복하게 마무리될 수 없다는 어렴풋한 생각이 그에게 있었다. 레이디 러프턴은 무슨 말씀을 하실 것인가? 의심할 것 없이 그것이 그의 낭패감의 가장 큰 근원이었다.\n\n“이 일에 대해 어머님께 말씀드렸나?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“내 어머니? 아니. 내 운명을 알기 전에 왜 그분께 말하겠나? 거절당할 가능성이 있다면 사람은 그런 일을 두고 말을 많이 하고 싶어 하지 않네. 내가 자네에게 말하는 것은 거짓 구실을 앞세워 자네 집에 발을 들이고 싶지 않기 때문이야.”\n\n“그렇지만 레이디 러프턴은 무슨 말씀을 하시겠나?”\n\n“처음 들으시면 언짢아하실 것이고, 스물네 시간 안에 마음을 푸실 것이고, 한 주쯤 지나면 루시가 그분이 가장 아끼는 사람이자 그분의 온갖 계략의 총리가 될 것이 있음직하다고 나는 생각하네. 자네는 내 어머니를 나만큼 알지 못하네. 그분은 나를 기쁘게 하려면 어깨에서 머리를 떼어 내어 주실 사람이야.”\n\n“그러니 바로 그런 이유로,” 마크 로바츠가 말했다. “자네는 될 수 있으면 그분을 기쁘게 해 드려야 하네.”\n\n“그분이 골라 주는 아내와 무조건 혼인할 수야 없지, 자네 말이 그런 뜻이라면,” 러프턴 경이 말했다.\n\n두 사람은 한 시간 동안 정원을 거닐었으나, 우리가 지금 두 사람을 데려다 놓은 지점에서 거의 더 나아가지 못했다. 마크 로바츠는 그 자리에서 대뜸 마음을 정할 수 없었다. 또한 그가 러프턴 경에게 한 번 이상 말한 대로, 루시가 무슨 일에서든 자기 뜻에 이끌리리라고는 조금도 확신할 수 없었다. 그리하여 마침내 두 사람 사이에는 러프턴 경이 이튿날 아침 식사 직후에 목사관으로 오는 것으로 정해졌다. 저녁 식사는 하지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 것도 함께 정해졌고, 로바츠는 될 수 있으면 아침까지 누이에게 어떤 조언을 할지 마음을 정해 두겠다고 약속했다.\n\n그는 프램리 코트에서 곧장 목사관으로 돌아갔다. 아내와 의논을 하기까지는 자기가 온통 어둠 속에 있다고 느꼈던 것이다. 루시가 레이디 러프턴이 된다면 자기는 어떤 기분일까? 그리고 그것이 누이의 팔자라고 레이디 러프턴에게 말하면서 그분의 얼굴을 어떻게 마주 볼까? 집에 돌아와 그는 곧바로 아내를 찾아냈고, 아내와 단둘이 방에 든 지 오 분도 되지 않아 적어도 아내가 아는 모든 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n“그러면 그 아이가 정말 그 사람을 사랑한다는 말이야?” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“정말 그래요. 그리고 그러는 것이 당연하지 않아요? 두 사람이 그렇게 자주 함께 있는 것을 볼 때 저는 그 아이가 그렇게 되지 않을까 걱정했어요. 그렇지만 그분이 그 아이를 마음에 두시리라고는 한 번도 생각하지 못했어요.”\n\n패니조차도 아직 루시의 매력을 절반도 인정해 주지 않고 있었다. 한 시간의 이야기 끝에 부부의 만남은 루시에게 보내는 전갈로 끝났으니, 서재로 와서 두 사람과 함께 있어 달라는 것이었다.\n\n“루시 고모,” 통통한 어린 것이 말을 하면서 고모의 팔에 안겨 올라갔다. “아빠하고 엄마가 떠재에서 고모를 부으신대요. 그런데 나는 고모하고 가치 가면 안 대요.”\n\n루시는 사내아이에게 입을 맞추고 그 얼굴을 자기 얼굴에 대고 누르면서, 제 피가 빠르게 심장으로 달려가는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n“고모하고 가치 가면 안 대요, 우리 아기?” 놀이 동무를 내려놓으며 그녀가 말했다. 그러나 그녀가 아이와 놀아 준 것은 오직, 제가 스스로를 거의 다스리지 못하고 있다는 것을 이 아이에게조차 드러내고 싶지 않아서였다. 그녀는 러프턴 경이 프램리에 와 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 오라비가 그를 만나러 갔다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그가 그날 저녁을 먹으러 이리로 오라는 청을 받았다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러니 서재로 모이라는 이 부름이 러프턴 경이 프램리에 온 데서 비롯한 것일 수밖에 없지 않겠는가? 그런데도 어떻게 그럴 수가 있단 말인가? 패니가 저녁 초대를 막으려고 저를 팔아넘긴 것일까? 러프턴 경 자신이 그 일을 입에 올렸을 리는 없지 않은가! 그러고 나서 그녀는 다시 몸을 굽혀 아이에게 입을 맞추고, 손으로 이마를 쓸어 머리를 매만지고, 될 수 있는 일이라면 제가 걸치고 있는 근심스러운 표정을 지워 내려 하고서, 천천히 오라비의 거실로 내려갔다.\n\n문을 열기 전에 그녀의 손이 문 위에서 한순간 멈추었다. 그러나 그녀는 무슨 일이 오든 용감하겠다고 작정해 두었다. 그녀는 문을 밀어 열고, 당당한 낯으로 눈을 크게 뜨고 느린 걸음으로 들어섰다.\n\n“프랭크가 저를 부르신다고 하던데요,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n로바츠 씨와 패니는 둘 다 난롯가에 서 있었고, 루시가 방에 들어섰을 때 서로 상대가 말을 꺼내기를 잠깐씩 기다렸다. 그러고서 패니가 입을 열었다.\n\n“러프턴 경이 오셨어요, 루시.”\n\n“오셨다니! 어디에요? 목사관에요?”\n\n“아니, 목사관은 아니고 프램리 코트에 와 있다,” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 내일 아침 식사 뒤에 이리로 찾아오겠다고 약속하셨어요,” 패니가 말했다. 그러고서 또 한 번 말이 멎었다. 로바츠 부인은 루시의 얼굴을 차마 바라보지 못했다. 그 비밀을 마크에게 말한 것은 그녀가 아니라 러프턴 경이었으니 그녀가 맡은 믿음을 팔아넘긴 것은 아니었다. 그러나 루시가 저를 팔아넘겼다고 여길 것이라는 느낌을 그녀는 지울 수 없었다.\n\n“좋아요,” 웃어 보려 애쓰며 루시가 말했다. “저야 조금도 마다할 것이 없어요.”\n\n“그런데 루시,” — 이때 로바츠 부인은 시누이의 허리에 팔을 둘렀다 — “그분은 특히 루시를 보러 오시는 거예요.”\n\n“아, 그러면 이야기가 다르지요. 그때 저는 아마 — 볼일이 있을 것 같아 걱정이에요.”\n\n“그분이 마크에게 다 말하셨어요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n루시는 이제 용기가 저를 거의 버리고 가는 것을 느꼈다. 어느 쪽으로 눈을 두어야 할지, 어떻게 서 있어야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 패니도 모든 것을 말해 버렸을까? 패니가 아는 것 가운데 러프턴 경이 알 수 없었던 것이 그토록 많았다. 그러나 사실 패니는 다 말했다 — 루시의 사랑의 온 이야기를 말했고, 그녀가 구혼자를 거절하게 만든 까닭들을 그려 주었다. 그리고 그 일을, 러프턴 경이 들었더라면 그의 사랑을 두 배로 격하게 만들었을 그런 말로 해 놓았다.\n\n그러고 나자 러프턴 경이 왜 프램리로 와서 이 온 이야기를 제 오라비에게 했을까 하는 생각이 루시에게 분명히 떠올랐다. 그녀는 잠깐 그가 그렇게 한 것에 자기가 화가 났다고 스스로 믿어 보려 했다. 그러나 그녀는 화가 나지 않았다. 그 일을 두고 이러니저러니 따질 겨를이 없었으나, 자기가 기억되고 생각되고 — 사랑받았다는 흐뭇한 느낌이 그녀에게 밀려왔다. 그렇지 않을 수가 있는가? 그가 아직도 자기를 사랑하지 않는다면 이 이야기를 제 오라비에게 스스로 털어놓았을 리가 있는가? 쉰 번이나 그녀는 그의 청혼이 순간의 일이었다고 스스로에게 말했고, 쉰 번이나 그렇게 말하면서 마음이 언짢았다. 그러나 이번에 그가 온 것은 순간의 일일 수가 없었다. 그녀는 제 쪽에서 터무니없는 열정에 속아 넘어간 것이라고 여겨 왔었다. 그러나 이제는 — 이제는 어떠한가? 그녀는 자기가 언젠가 레이디 러프턴이 될 것이라는 생각에까지는 이르지 않았다. 그녀는 여전히, 어딘가 뒤틀리게 고집스러운 방식으로 그런 결말에 반대하는 마음을 굳혀 두고 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 러프턴 경이 몸소 프램리로 내려와 몸소 이 이야기를 했다는 것을 느끼는 것은 어쩐지 알 수 없는 방식으로 그녀를 흐뭇하게 했다.\n\n“그분이 마크에게 다 말하셨어요,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. 그러고 나서 또 한순간 말이 멎었고, 그동안 이런 생각들이 루시의 마음을 지나갔다.\n\n“그래,” 마크가 말했다. “그 사람이 나에게 다 말했다. 그리고 네 입에서 직접 답을 받으려고 내일 아침 이리로 온다.”\n\n“무슨 답을요?” 루시가 떨면서 말했다.\n\n“아니에요, 루시. 그것을 루시 아니면 누가 말할 수 있겠어요?” 그리고 올케는 말을 하면서 그녀에게 바짝 몸을 붙였다. “그것은 루시가 스스로 말해야 해요.”\n\n로바츠 부인은 남편과의 그 긴 이야기 동안 루시를 위해 힘껏 변론했으니, 말하자면 레이디 러프턴에게 맞서는 편을 들었다. 그녀는 러프턴 경이 구혼을 굽히지 않는다면, 레이디 러프턴을 기쁘게 해 드리자고 목사관의 자기들이 루시가 스스로 얻어 낸 모든 것을 그녀에게서 빼앗는 일은 정당할 수 없다고 말했다.\n\n“그렇지만 그분은 우리가 이 일을 두고 짜고 꾸몄다고 생각하실 거야,” 마크가 말했다. “우리를 은혜를 모르는 자들이라 부르실 것이고, 루시의 삶을 참담하게 만드실 거야.” 이에 아내는 그 모든 것은 하느님의 손에 맡겨 두어야 한다고 대답했다. 그들은 짜지도 꾸미지도 않았다. 루시는 마음 깊은 속으로 그 사람을 사랑하면서도 이미 한 번 그를 거절했으니, 그토록 큰 상을 낚아챈 것으로 여겨지고 싶지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 러프턴 경이 그녀를 그토록 뜨겁게 사랑하여, 스스로 말한 대로 제 운명을 알아보겠다는 뜻으로 일부러 이런 식으로 여기까지 내려왔다면, 그렇다면 — 로바츠 부인은 이렇게 따졌다 — 두 사람의 레이디 러프턴에 대한 충심이 아무리 굳세다 해도, 루시와 그녀의 연인 사이에 가로막고 서는 것을 자기들의 양심에 정당하다고 할 수는 없다는 것이었다. 마크는 이에 대해 여전히 다소 주저했으니, 만일 이제 러프턴 경을 부추겨 놓고, 그 부추김을 받은 뒤 자기들이 레이디 러프턴과 다투게 되었을 때에 그가 어머니에게 이끌려 제 약속에서 벗어나 버린다면 두 사람의 처지가 얼마나 끔찍할 것인가를 들어 보였던 것이다. 이에 패니는 정의는 정의고 옳은 것은 옳은 것이라고 답했다. 모든 것을 루시에게 말해야 하고, 루시가 스스로 판단해야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n“그렇지만 저는 러프턴 경이 무엇을 바라시는지 모르겠어요,” 눈을 땅에 붙인 채, 그리고 이제 어느 때보다 더 떨면서 루시가 말했다. “그분은 저에게 오셨고, 저는 답을 드렸어요.”\n\n“그러면 그 답이 마지막인 거냐?” 마크가 말했다 — 다소 매정하게 그랬으니, 루시는 그 연인이 청혼을 되풀이했다는 말을 아직 듣지 못했던 것이다. 그러나 패니는 부당한 일이 있어서는 안 된다고 작정하고, 그래서 마침내 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n“루시가 답을 드린 것은 우리도 알아요. 그렇지만 신사들은 그런 문제를 두고 답 하나로 물러서지 않는 때가 있어요. 러프턴 경은 다시 청하실 작정이라고 마크에게 선언하셨어요. 그러려고 일부러 여기까지 내려오신 거예요.”\n\n“그러면 레이디 러프턴은 —” 루시가 속삭임을 겨우 넘는 소리로 말했다. 올케의 어깨에 기대어 여전히 얼굴을 감추고 있었다.\n\n“러프턴 경은 그 일을 어머님께 말씀드리지 않았다,” 마크가 말했다. 그러자 오라비의 목소리에서 루시는 곧, 적어도 오라비는 자기가 그 연인의 맹세를 받아들인다면 흐뭇해하지 않으리라는 것을 분명히 알아차렸다.\n\n“루시는 제 마음에서 나오는 대로 정해야 해요,” 패니가 너그럽게 말했다. “마크와 저는 루시가 얼마나 훌륭하게 처신했는지 알아요. 제가 다 말했으니까요.” 이 말을 듣자 루시는 몸을 떨며 올케에게 더 바짝 기댔다. “저로서는 마크에게 말하는 것밖에 달리 길이 없었어요. 그러는 것이 가장 좋았어요. 그렇지 않아요? 그렇지만 러프턴 경께는 아무것도 말하지 않았어요. 마크가 그분이 오늘 이리로 오시는 것을 막았어요. 그러면 루시가 허둥거릴 테니, 생각할 겨를을 주고 싶었던 거예요. 그렇지만 내일 아침에는 그분을 만날 수 있지요? 그러고서 답을 드리면 되고요.”\n\n루시는 이제 온전히 잠자코 서 있었다. 올케의 그 자매다운 친절에 — 자매의 사랑을 밀어 주려는 그 자매다운 마음에 — 올케를 몹시 사랑한다고 느꼈다. 그러나 그런데도 그녀의 마음에는, 러프턴 경이 총애받는 연인으로 맞아들여질 것이라는 생각을 품고 이리로 오게 두지 않겠다는 굳센 결심이 있었다. 그녀의 사랑은 강했으나 그녀의 자랑도 그만큼 강했다. 그리고 그녀는 레이디 러프턴의 눈에 담길 그 멸시를 차마 견딜 수가 없었다. “그분의 어머니가 나를 업신여기실 것이고, 그러면 그이도 나를 업신여기게 될 거야,” 그녀는 스스로에게 말했다. 그리고 어그러진 사랑과 야심을 굳세게 한 번 꿀꺽 삼키고서 그녀는 버티기로 마음을 정했다.\n\n“이제 우리가 나가 있을까요? 그리고 내일 아침 그분이 오시기 전에 다시 이야기하는 게 좋겠어요?” 패니가 말했다.\n\n“그게 가장 좋겠다,” 마크가 말했다. “오늘 밤 그 일을 마음속에서 이리저리 다 뒤집어 보아라. 기도를 마친 뒤에 그것을 생각해 보고 — 그리고 루시, 이리 오렴.” 그러고서 그는 그녀를 팔에 안고, 그녀에게 여느 때 보이지 않던 다정함으로 입을 맞추었다. “이 말은 해 두는 것이 마땅하겠다,” 그가 말했다. “나는 네 판단과 네 마음을 온전히 믿는다. 그리고 네가 어떤 결정에 이르든 나는 오라비로서 네 편에 서겠다. 패니와 나는 둘 다 네가 훌륭하게 처신했다고 생각하고, 둘 다 네가 가장 좋은 일을 하리라고 확신한다. 네가 무엇을 하든 나는 네게 붙어 있겠다 — 패니도 그럴 것이다.”\n\n“마크, 사랑하는 마크!”\n\n“그러면 이제 내일 아침까지 이 일에 대해서는 더 아무 말도 하지 않기로 해요,” 패니가 말했다.\n\n그러나 루시는 내일 아침까지 이 일에 대해 더 아무 말도 하지 않는 것이 자기 쪽에서 러프턴 경의 청혼을 받아들이는 것과 다름없는 일이 되리라고 느꼈다. 로바츠 부인이 알고 있었고, 이제 로바츠 씨도 그녀 마음의 비밀을 알고 있었다. 사정이 그러한데도 러프턴 경이 제 구혼을 스스로 변론하겠다는 뜻을 드러내고 이리로 오는 것을 그녀가 허락한다면, 그녀로서는 굽히지 않을 수가 없을 것이었다. 굽히지 않겠다고 마음을 굳혔다면, 지금이야말로 그녀가 제 자리를 지키고 제 싸움을 벌일 때였다.\n\n“가지 마세요, 패니. 적어도 아직은요,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“왜 그래요, 루시?”\n\n“제가 마크에게 말하는 동안 패니가 있어 주었으면 해요. 마크는 러프턴 경이 내일 여기 오시게 두어서는 안 돼요.”\n\n“오시게 두어서는 안 된다니!” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n로바츠 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 누이가 자기 마음속에서 시시각각 올라가고 있다고 느꼈다.\n\n“안 돼요. 마크가 그분께 오지 말라고 일러야 해요. 아무 좋을 것도 없는데 저를 아프게 하고 싶어 하지는 않으실 거예요. 여기 보세요, 마크.” 그리고 그녀는 오라비에게 걸어가 두 손을 그의 팔에 얹었다. “저는 러프턴 경을 사랑해요. 그분을 처음 알았을 때에는 그런 뜻도 그런 생각도 없었어요. 그렇지만 저는 그분을 사랑해요 — 몹시 사랑해요. 아마 패니가 마크를 사랑하는 것만큼이나 그럴 거예요. 마땅하다고 생각하시면 그분께 그렇게 말씀하셔도 돼요 — 아니, 그렇게 말씀하셔야 해요. 그러지 않으면 그분이 저를 알아듣지 못하실 거예요. 그렇지만 이것은 제가 하는 말로 전해 주세요. 그분의 어머니가 저에게 청하지 않는 한, 저는 결코 그분과 혼인하지 않겠다고요.”\n\n“그분은 그러지 않으실 것 같아 걱정이다,” 마크가 슬프게 말했다.\n\n“예. 그러지 않으시겠지요,” 이제 온 용기를 되찾은 루시가 말했다. “그분이 저를 며느리로 삼고 싶어 하실 것 같다고 여겨진다면, 제가 그런 조건을 붙일 필요도 없을 거예요. 그분이 그것을 바라지 않으실 것이기 때문이에요. 그분이 저를, 그 아들과 — 그 아들과 — 그 아들과 짝을 이루기에 걸맞지 않다고 여기실 것이기 때문이에요. 그분은 저를 미워하고 업신여기실 거예요. 그러면 그분도 저를 업신여기기 시작하고, 어쩌면 저를 사랑하기를 그치겠지요. 제가 그 아들을 해쳤다고 그분이 여기신다면 저는 저에게 얹히는 그분의 눈길을 견딜 수 없어요. 마크, 지금 그분께 가시겠지요? 그리고 이것을 설명해 주세요 — 필요한 만큼만요. 그분의 어머니가 저에게 청하신다면 저는 — 응하겠다고 말씀해 주세요. 그렇지만 그분이 결코 그러지 않으실 것을 제가 아니, 그분은 자기가 한 말을 다 잊힌 것으로 여기셔야 한다고요. 저에게도 그것은 잊힌 것과 같을 거라고요.”\n\n그것이 그녀의 판결이었다. 그리고 두 사람은 그녀의 굳셈을 — 다른 어느 때라면 마크가 고집이라고 불렀을 그것을 — 어찌나 확신했는지, 둘 중 누구도 그녀에게 그것을 고치게 하려 들지 않았다.\n\n“지금 가시겠지요 — 오늘 오후에요. 그러시겠지요?” 그녀가 말했고, 마크는 그러겠다고 약속했다. 그는 자기 자신이 크게 놓였다고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 레이디 러프턴은 아마도 아들이 목사의 누이와 사랑에 빠질 만큼 바보였다는 말을 들으실 것이었다. 그러나 지금의 형편에서라면 목사에게도 그 누이에게도 자기가 억울한 일을 당했다고 여기실 수는 없었다. 루시가 잘 처신하고 있었고, 마크는 누이가 자랑스러웠다. 루시가 매서운 기개로 처신하고 있었고, 패니는 그녀를 두고 마음 아파했다.\n\n“저녁때까지는 혼자 있고 싶어요,” 로바츠 부인이 그녀와 함께 방에서 나가려 하자 루시가 말했다. “패니, 언짢은 얼굴은 하지 마세요. 우리를 언짢게 할 일은 아무것도 없어요. 제가 염소 젖이 필요하겠다고 말했잖아요. 그게 다일 거예요.”\n\n로바츠는 한 시간을 아내와 함께 앉아 있은 뒤에 다시 프램리 코트로 돌아갔다. 그리고 한참을 찾아다닌 끝에 늦은 저녁을 먹으려고 집으로 돌아오는 러프턴 경을 만났다.\n\n“어머니가 그녀에게 청하지 않는 한이라니,” 이야기를 다 듣고 나서 그가 말했다. “그것은 터무니없는 소리네. 세상이 그런 식으로 돌아가지 않는다고 자네가 그녀에게 말해 주었겠지.”\n\n로바츠는, 남편의 어머니가 자기를 못마땅하게 바라보리라는 생각을 루시가 견딜 수 없어 한다는 것을 그에게 설명해 보려 했다.\n\n“내 어머니가 그녀를 — 그녀를 특히 싫어하신다고 그녀가 생각하는가?” 러프턴 경이 물었다.\n\n아니다. 로바츠도 그런 것이라고는 생각할 수 없었다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 아마 목사의 누이와의 혼인이 격에 맞지 않는 혼사라고 여기실 것이었다.\n\n“그것은 말이 되지 않네,” 러프턴 경이 말했다. “그분은 얼마 전부터 특히 내가 목사의 딸과 혼인하기를 바라 오셨으니까. 그런데 마크, 내 어머니 이야기를 하는 것은 부질없는 일이야. 요즘 세상에 사람이 어머니가 시키는 대로 혼인하는 것은 아니지.”\n\n이 모든 말에 대해 마크가 그에게 확언할 수 있었던 것은, 루시가 지금 하고 있는 일에 매우 굳세다는 것, 마음을 완전히 정해 두었다는 것, 그리고 러프턴 경이 그렇게 하는 것을 좋게 여기지 않는다면 어머니에게 말해야 할 어떤 의무에서도 그를 온전히 놓아 준다는 것뿐이었다. 그러나 이 모든 것은 별 소용이 없었다.\n\n“그러면 그녀가 나를 사랑하는 것이군?” 러프턴 경이 말했다.\n\n“글쎄,” 마크가 말했다. “그런지 그렇지 않은지는 내가 말하지 않겠네. 나는 그 아이가 스스로 전한 말을 되풀이할 수 있을 뿐이야. 자네 어머니의 청을 받고서가 아니라면 그 아이는 자네를 받아들일 수 없다는 것이지.” 그리고 그 말을 다시 하고서 그는 작별하고 목사관으로 돌아갔다.\n\n딱한 루시는 그토록 큰 위엄으로 그 대면을 끝내고, 오라비를 온전히 만족시키고, 올케가 곧바로 내미는 위로를 마다한 뒤에 제 침실로 몸을 옮겼다. 자기가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 일을 했는지 생각해 보아야 했고, 그러기 위해서는 혼자여야 했다. 그 일을 다시 헤아려 볼 때에는 어쩌면 오라비만큼 흐뭇하지는 않을지도 몰랐다. 그 대단한 몸가짐과 느긋한 걸음의 예절바름은 제 방에 잘 들어서기까지 그녀에게 남아 있었다. 어딘가 몸이 성치 않을 때면 제 몸을 숨겨 내는 짐승들이 있으니, 제가 앓는 그 약한 꼴을 남이 보는 것을 부끄럽게 여기는 듯하다. 사실 말 못 하는 짐승이라면 다 얼마간 그러지 않는지, 나로서는 확실히 알 수 없다. 그리고 이 점에서 루시는 말 못 하는 짐승과 같았다. 패니에게 속을 털어놓을 때에도 그녀는 제 불운을 농담으로 만들었고 제 마음의 병을 스스로 웃음거리로 삼아 말했다. 그러나 이제, 서두르지 않는 걸음으로 계단을 올라가 일부러 문을 잠근 뒤에, 그녀는 짐승과 새들이 하듯 잠잠함과 홀로 있음 속에서 앓기 위해 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n그녀는 침대 발치에 놓인 낮은 의자에 앉아, 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 손수건을 두 손으로 꽉 붙든 채 눈과 이마 위에 가로 얹었다. 그러고 나서 생각하기 시작했다. 생각하기 시작했고 또한 울기 시작했으니, 손수건 아래로 눈물이 흘러내렸다. 그리고 낮은 흐느낌이 들릴 만했다 — 다만 그 짐승이 홀로 앓으려고 몸을 감추어 버렸으니 들을 사람이 없었다.\n\n그녀는 제 행복의 모든 기회를 제게서 던져 버린 것이 아닌가? 그가 또 한 번 — 세 번째로 — 그녀에게 올 수 있을까? 아니다. 그럴 수 없다. 이 두 번째 거절의 방식과 그 자랑이 바로 그것을 있을 수 없는 일로 만들었다. 결심에 이르고 그것을 밝히면서 그녀를 움직인 것은, 레이디 러프턴이 그런 혼사를 진저리 나게 여기리라는 것을 안다는 사실이었다. 레이디 러프턴은 제 아들의 신부가 되어 달라고 몸을 낮추어 그녀에게 청하지도 않을 것이고 청할 수도 없을 것이었다. 행복의, 영화의, 야심의, 사랑의 기회가 다 사라져 버렸다. 그녀는 모든 것을 바쳐 버렸으니, 미덕에 바친 것이 아니라 자랑에 바쳤다. 그리고 그녀는 자기 자신만이 아니라 그까지도 바쳐 버렸다. 그가 처음 여기 왔을 때 — 그의 첫 방문을 두고 곰곰이 생각했을 때 — 그녀는 그에게 깊은 사랑이 있다고 좀처럼 믿어 주지 않았다. 그러나 이제는 — 그가 이제 그녀를 사랑한다는 것에는 의심이 있을 수 없었다. 런던의 사교철을 지내고, 아름다운 모든 것과 함께 낮과 밤을 보낸 뒤에, 그는 다시 그녀의 발 앞에 자기를 던지려고 저 작은 시골 목사관으로 돌아왔던 것이다. 그런데 그녀는 — 그녀는 온 마음으로 그를 사랑하면서도 그를 만나지 않겠다고 했다. 노부인의 시큰한 눈길을 견디지 못할 만큼 비루한 겁쟁이였기 때문에 그를 만나지 않겠다고 했다.\n\n“곧 내려갈게요,” 마침내 패니가 문을 두드리며 들여 달라고 청했을 때 그녀가 말했다. “문은 열지 않겠어요. 그렇지만 십 분 안에 내려가 있을게요. 정말이에요.” 그리고 그녀는 정말로 그렇게 했다. 로바츠 부인의 익숙한 눈에 알아볼 만한 눈물의 자취가 아주 없지는 않았을지 모르나, 그래도 이마는 매끄럽고 목소리는 제 뜻 아래 있었다.\n\n“그 아이가 정말로 그 사람을 사랑하는 걸까,” 그날 밤 마크가 아내에게 말했다.\n\n“사랑하느냐고요!” 아내가 대답했다. “정말로 사랑해요. 그리고 마크, 그 아이 몸가짐의 그 매서운 조용함에 속지 마세요. 제 생각으로 그 아이는 사랑 때문에 죽을 수도 있을 아이예요.”\n\n이튿날 러프턴 경은 프램리를 떠났다. 그리고 미리 정해 둔 대로 노르웨이의 연어낚시를 향해 출발했다.\n\n# 제32장. 「염소와 나침반」\n\n해럴드 스미스는 의회 해산이라는 그 소문 때문에 마음이 편치 않았다. 그러나 그에게 닥칠 불운은 사워비 씨에게 떨어질 매서움에 견주면 아무것도 아니었다. 해럴드 스미스는 자기 선거구를 잃을 수도 있고 잃지 않을 수도 있었으나, 사워비 씨는 틀림없이 자기 주를 잃을 것이었고, 그것을 잃으면 모든 것을 잃는 것이었다. 콜디코츠의 주인이 누가 되든 공작이 다시는 자기를 밀어 주지 않으리라는 것을 그는 이제 아주 확실히 느꼈다. 그리고 이런 일들을 곱씹으면서 그는 마음을 추스르는 일이 몹시 어렵다는 것을 알았다.\n\n톰 타워스는, 늘 그러하듯이, 그 일을 다 알고 있었던 듯하다. 던스터블 양의 집에서 그가 흘린 그 조그만 말은, 의심할 것도 없이 무르익은 숙고를 거쳐 깊은 정치적 동기와 더불어 나온 것이었는데, 거인들이 나라에 물으러 갈 것이라는 아주 널리 퍼진 소문의 겨우 열두 시간 앞선 선구였다. 신들이 사심 없이 그들에게 해 준 지지 약속이 아무리 후했다 해도, 거인들에게 의회의 다수가 없다는 것은 뚜렷했다. 이것은 정말로 뚜렷했고, 그래서 그들은 나라에 물으러 가려는 것이었다. 만일 그렇게 한다면 그 사심 없는 지지를 거두는 수밖에 없다고 올림포스의 아주 이름 높은 후예 한 사람이 짐짓 경고해 두었는데도 그러했다. 이 위협은 별로 무게가 있어 보이지 않았고, 던스터블 양의 모임이 있은 다음 날 두 시에는 그 명이 이미 내려진 것으로 여겨졌다. 소문은 톰 타워스에게서 비롯되었으나, 그때쯤에는 프티백 청의 버긴스에게까지 이르러 있었다.\n\n“우리한테야 아무 차이도 없는 게 아니겠습니까요, 나리. 그렇지 않습니까, 로바츠 씨?” 그 관청의 개인 비서 방에서 문 가까운 벽에 공손히 몸을 기대고 서서 버긴스가 그렇게 말했다.\n\n잡다하고 특별하고 정치적인 이야기가 그날 하루 동안 젊은 로바츠와 버긴스 사이에 꽤 많이 오갔다. 이 궂은 시절에 두 사람이 서로에게 크게 기대어 던져져 있었으니 그것은 자연스러운 일이었다. 지금 내각의 프티백 경은 해럴드 스미스 같은 사람이 아니었다. 그는 자기에게 오는 사사로운 편지에도 무심하고, 추천권에 딸린 직무조차 아무렇게나 여기는 거인이었다. 그는 청에 좀처럼 들르지 않았고, 해럴드 스미스의 짧은 치세에 뿌리와 가지를 다 뽑는 개혁이 이루어진 탓에 이 관청에는 다른 서기가 하나도 없었으니, 버긴스가 아니라면 젊은 로바츠가 누구와 이야기를 할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n“그렇겠지, 없을 테지.” 로바츠는 압지 위에 긴 의자에 앉은 터키 사람을 공들여 그린 그림을 마무리하면서 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“왜냐면 말입니다요, 나리, 우리는 이제 상원에 있으니까요. 마땅히 그래야 한다고 나는 늘 그렇게 생각하고들 있습니다요. 프티백 청이 하원에 있는 것은 국헌에 맞지 않는 게 아닌가 싶습니다요, 로바츠 씨. 아무튼 예전에는 그런 적이 도무지 없었습니다요.”\n\n“요즘은 그런 것들을 죄다 바꾸고 있네, 버긴스.” 로바츠는 터키 사람의 담배 연기에 마지막 손질을 하며 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“그럼 어떤 것인지 말씀드리겠습니다요, 로바츠 씨. 나는 나갈 생각입니다요. 이런 변화들을 다 견딜 수가 없습니다요. 이제 예순도 넘었으니 무슨 증명선지 자격증인지 하는 것도 받고 싶지 않습니다요. 연금이나 받고 걸어 나갈 생각입니다요. 이 청은 하원으로 내려온 뒤로는 도무지 옛날 그 청이 아니지 않습니까요.” 그러고 나서 버긴스는 한숨을 쉬며 물러나, 개인 비서 방 밖의 조그만 대기실에서 작은 탁자 위에 세워 놓은 크고 활짝 펼쳐진 관청 원부 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 포터 맥주 한 단지로 마음을 달래려 했다. 펼쳐진 책에 드러나 있는 날짜가 자기가 임명된 해와 거의 같을 만큼 오래된 것을 보고 버긴스는 다시 한숨을 쉬었다. 이런 책은 프티백 청에서 오래 쓰였기 때문이다. 그 시절에는 지위 높은 귀족이 프티백 경이었다. 사환의 마음이 끝없는 경외로 우러를 수 있는 그런 사람이었으니, 그는 사교철 동안 네 번쯤, 매우 엄숙하게 이 청을 드물게 찾아왔던 것이다. 그때의 프티백 경은 아래 사람들에게 몹시 높이 받들어졌고, 그가 그들 가운데로 온다는 일은 몇 시간 전부터, 그리고 그 뒤로 며칠 동안 이야깃거리가 되었다. 그런데 해럴드 스미스는 맨체스터 상회의 지배인처럼 부산스레 드나들었다. “관청이 갈수록 개판이 되어 가는군.” 버긴스는 포터 단지를 내려놓고, 문에 나타난 한 신사를 책 위로 올려다보면서 혼자 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“로바츠 씨가 방에 있느냐고요?” 신사의 말을 되받으며 버긴스가 말했다. “예, 사워비 씨. 거기 계실 겁니다요. 왼쪽 첫 번째 문입니다요.” 그러고 나서 찾아온 이가 주 선출 의원이라는 것을, 버긴스가 귀족 다음 자리로 여기는 그 자리라는 것을 떠올리고, 그는 일어나서 개인 비서의 문을 열고 손님을 안으로 안내했다.\n\n젊은 로바츠와 사워비 씨는 물론 해럴드 스미스의 치세 시절에 서로 알게 되었다. 그 짧은 동안 동바셋주 의원은 거의 날마다 프티백 청에 잠깐씩 들러, 그 기운찬 각료가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 알아보고, 반쯤 공적인 일들을 놓고 이야기를 나누고, 개인 비서에게 제 상관을 비웃는 법을 가르쳤다. 그러므로 이번 방문에도 남달라 보일 만한 것, 곧바로 무슨 특별한 설명을 요할 만한 것은 하나도 없었다. 그는 여느 때처럼 자리에 앉아 그날의 화제를 입에 올리기 시작했다.\n\n“우리 다 나가야 하게 됐네.” 사워비가 말했다.\n\n“그렇다고 들었어요.” 개인 비서가 말했다. “저야 아무 걱정이 없지요. 저 점잖은 버긴스가 말하듯이 우리는 이제 상원에 있으니까요.”\n\n“그 복 많은 귀족 양반들은 참 즐거운 세월을 보내지!” 사워비가 말했다. “선거구민도 없고, 쫓겨나는 일도 없고, 싸움도 없고, 정치적 견해를 가져야 할 까닭도 없고, 게다가 으레 그런 견해라는 것이 아예 없지!”\n\n“동바셋주에서는 어지간히 안전하시겠지요?” 로바츠가 말했다. “거기서는 공작이 거의 제 마음대로 하니까요.”\n\n“그렇지, 공작이 거의 제 마음대로 하지. 그런데 말이야, 자네 형은 어디 있나?”\n\n“집에 있어요.” 로바츠가 말했다. “적어도 그럴 거라고 짐작해요.”\n\n“프램리인가, 바체스터인가? 얼마 전에는 바체스터에서 거주 근무를 하고 있었던 것으로 아는데.”\n\n“지금은 프램리에 있어요, 그건 알아요. 어제 형수님한테서 심부름을 하나 맡기는 편지를 받았어요. 형은 거기 있었고, 러프턴 경은 막 떠난 뒤였어요.”\n\n“그렇지, 러프턴이 내려갔었지. 오늘 아침에 노르웨이로 떠났네. 나는 자네 형을 만나고 싶은데. 자네는 형한테서 따로 소식을 듣지 못했나?”\n\n“예, 요새는 못 들었어요. 마크는 편지를 잘 쓰지 않아요. 개인 비서로는 도무지 맞지 않을 사람이지요.”\n\n“적어도 해럴드 스미스의 비서로는 안 되겠지. 그런데 바체스터에서는 그를 붙잡을 수 없다는 것이 확실한가?”\n\n“전보를 내려보내시면 만나러 나올 거예요.”\n\n“그건 하고 싶지 않네. 전보 한 통이 시골에서는 얼마나 큰 법석을 일으키는지, 사람들 아내를 겁주고 그 근방의 말을 다 달리게 만들어 놓지.”\n\n“무슨 일인데요?”\n\n“대단한 일은 아무것도 아니네. 그가 자네에게 말했을지 몰라서 그랬지. 오늘 밤 우편으로 편지를 내려보내면 내일 바체스터에서 나를 만날 수 있겠지. 아니면 자네가 쓰게. 나는 편지 쓰는 일만큼 싫은 것이 없으니. 내가 찾아왔었다고, 그리고 원틀리의 용에서 나를 만나 주면 대단히 고맙겠다고만 전해 주게. 내일 두 시라고 하지. 나는 급행으로 내려가겠네.”\n\n마크 로바츠는 다가오는 이 돈 걱정을 사워비와 놓고 이야기하면서, 짧은 동안이라도 그 어음을 떠맡아야 할 필요가 있다면 아우에게서 그 돈을 빌릴 수 있을지도 모른다고 한 번 말한 적이 있었다. 아버지의 유산 가운데 나중 어음의 액수를 낼 수 있을 만큼은 아직 그 개인 비서의 손에 남아 있었고, 부탁을 받으면 그가 빌려 주리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 사워비 씨가 프티백 청을 찾은 것은 그런 부탁이 있었는지를 알고 싶은 마음에서였고, 또 그 성직자가 그렇게 하지 않았다는 것을 알게 되면 그 부탁을 자기가 직접 하겠다는, 절반쯤 굳은 결심에서였다. 그만한 돈이 손 닿는 데에, 말하자면 아무렇게나 놓여 있는데 자기가 몸을 굽혀 그것에 손을 얹지 않는다는 것은 그에게 아까운 일로 보였다. 그렇게 참는다는 것은 그가 평생 해 온 모든 버릇에 어긋나는 일이어서, 사냥꾼이 수꿩 한 마리를 그냥 지나가게 두는 것만큼이나 그에게 어려웠다. 그러나 그가 개인 비서의 방에서 의자에 앉아 몸을 흔들며 그 젊은이의 열린 얼굴을 바라보고 있는 동안, 후회 같은 무엇이 그의 마음에 닿았다.\n\n“예, 제가 편지를 쓰겠어요.” 존 로바츠가 말했다. “하지만 형은 저에게 무슨 특별한 이야기를 한 적이 없어요.”\n\n“안 했나? 그건 별로 대수롭지 않네. 그가 그러겠다고 말한 것으로 내가 알아들었다고 생각해서 그것만 말해 본 것이야.” 그러고 나서 사워비 씨는 계속 몸을 흔들었다. 존 로바츠 같은 젊은이에게, 아내도 자식도 어떤 종류의 짐도 없는 사내에게, 넉넉한 봉급으로 살아가고 있으니 그 돈을 잃는다 해도 상하는 데조차 없을 사람에게, 500파운드라는 그 조그만 돈을 입에 올리기가 어찌하여 그렇게 싫었을까? 그는 자기 자신의 무름을 이상하게 여겼다. 그 돈이 없어 급하기가 그에게는 극에 달해 있었다. 마크가 그 어음들을 갱신하기가 몹시 어려울 것이라고 짐작할 만한 까닭이 그에게는 있었으나, 그, 곧 사워비는 이 돈을 당장 자기 손에 넣을 수만 있다면 그 어음이 제시되는 것을 막을 수 있었다.\n\n“제가 뭐 해 드릴 일이 있나요?” 순진한 어린 양이 백정에게 제 목을 내밀며 그렇게 말했다.\n\n그러나 어떤 낯선 감정이 그 백정의 손가락을 저리게 하고 그 칼을 무디게 했다. 그는 그 물음이 있고 나서 반 분쯤 가만히 앉아 있었고, 그러고는 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 그 제안을 물렸다. “아니, 아니. 아무것도 없네, 고맙네. 마크에게 편지만 써서 내가 내일 거기 가겠다고 전해 주게.” 그러고 나서 그는 모자를 집어 들고 서둘러 청을 나섰다. “내가 참 얼간이로군.” 그는 가면서 혼자 이렇게 말했다. “이제 와서 까다롭게 구는 것이 무슨 쓸데가 있다고!”\n\n그러고 나서 그는 삯마차를 타고 뉴 로드 쪽으로 포트먼 스트리트를 절반쯤 올라간 곳까지 실려 갔고, 거기서 몇백 야드를 걸어 가로지르는 골목을 내려가 어느 술집에 이르렀다. 그 집은 「염소와 나침반」이라고 불렸는데, 누구라도 아주 뜻 없는 이름이라고 할 것이다. 그러나 이 집은 크롬웰 시절에 시골의 여관이었을 만큼 그 자리에 아주 오래전부터 자리 잡은 공중 유흥소라는 것을 자랑으로 삼았다. 그 무렵에 경건한 주인은 경건한 손님들을 위하여 경건한 문구를 걸어, “하느님이 우리를 에워싸고 계시니라”라고 밝혀 놓았던 것이다. 요즘에는 「염소와 나침반」도 그만큼 잘 들어맞는다. 그리고 이 집의 지금 됨됨이를 헤아리면, 그것이 옛 문구보다 어쩌면 덜 어울리지 않는 것이었다.\n\n“오스틴 씨 여기 있소?” 사워비 씨가 술청의 사내에게 물었다.\n\n“둘 중에 어느 분 말입니까요? 존 씨는 아니겠지요. 그분은 여기 없습니다요. 톰 씨는 안에 있습니다요. 왼편의 조그만 방입니다요.” 사워비 씨가 만나고 싶었던 사람은 형인 존이었으나, 그를 찾을 수 없었으므로 그는 그 조그만 방으로 들어갔다. 그 방에서 그가 만난 사람은, 이름 붙이는 한 가지 방식에 따르면 작은 오스틴 씨였고, 또 다른 방식에 따르면 톰 토저 씨였다. 법률 쪽 일을 하는 신사들에게는 그가 대개 점잖은 오스틴 집안의 사람이라고 자기를 소개하기를 골랐으나, 가까운 사람들 사이에서 그는 늘 토저였다.\n\n사워비 씨는 그 집안과 가까웠으나 토저네를 좋아하지는 않았다. 그중에서도 톰 토저를 특히 미워했다. 톰 토저는 목이 황소처럼 굵고 눈썹이 툭 튀어나온 사내였는데, 그 얼굴의 표정은 스스로 인정한 악당 기질을 웅변으로 말하고 있었다. “나는 악당이오,” 그 얼굴은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. “나도 그것을 알고 온 세상이 다 아오. 그렇지만 당신도 한 패요. 그건 온 세상은 모르지만 나는 아오. 사람이란 거의 다 악당이오. 어떤 놈은 무른 악당이고 어떤 놈은 약삭빠른 악당이오. 나는 약삭빠른 쪽이니, 그러니 눈 똑바로 뜨시오.” 톰 토저의 얼굴은 그런 말로 소리 내어 말하고 있었다. 그리고 마음속으로는 철저한 거짓말쟁이였으나 얼굴로는 거짓말쟁이가 아니었다.\n\n“여, 토저.” 사워비 씨가 그 더러운 악한과 정말로 악수까지 하며 말했다. “자네 형을 만나고 싶었네.”\n\n“존은 없고, 올 것 같지도 않소. 하지만 그거야 다 한가지요.”\n\n“그렇지, 그렇지. 그럴 테지. 자네들 둘이 짝을 지어 사냥한다는 것은 알고 있네.”\n\n“사냥이라니 무슨 말씀인지 나는 모르겠소, 사워비 씨. 사냥이란 것은 양반님들이 다 차지하고, 우리 같은 가난뱅이들에게는 일만 다 있는 거요. 우리가 이렇게 오래 밑지고 있는 이 푼돈이나 좀 채워 주시려는 것이면 좋겠소만.”\n\n“그 일로 찾아온 것이네. 자네가 무엇을 오래라고 부르는지는 모르겠네만, 토저, 마지막 어음은 겨우 이월 날짜로 된 것일세.”\n\n“만기가 지났잖소, 안 그렇소?”\n\n“아, 그렇지. 만기는 지났네. 그건 의심할 것이 없지.”\n\n“그럼요, 종이쪽 한 장이 돌아왔으면 그다음에 할 일은 그걸 떠맡는 거요. 그게 내 생각들이오. 그리고 사실을 말하자면, 사워비 씨, 우리는 요새 당신이 우리를 그리 떳떳하게 대해 주었다고 생각하지 않소. 그 러프턴 경 일에서는 당신이 우리를 유별나게 몰아붙였소.”\n\n“나도 어쩔 수가 없었다는 것을 자네도 알잖나.”\n\n“그럼요, 그리고 우리도 지금 어쩔 수가 없소. 바로 그게 문제란 말이오, 사워비 씨. 허, 딱하시기도 하오. 우리도 뭐가 뭔지는 알아요, 우리도 안다고요. 그래서 사실은 지금 당장 쥘 돈이 유별나게 말라 있는 형편이고, 우리는 그 몇백 파운드를 받아야 하오. 당장 받아야 하고, 그러지 못하면 그 성직자 양반의 살림을 다 팔아 넘겨야 하오. 목사에게서 돈을 받아 내는 일이 개에게서 뼈다귀를 빼앗는 것만큼 어렵지 않다면 내가 죽어도 좋소. 그 양반도 제 몫은 다 챙겼을 텐데, 왜 안 갚는 거요?”\n\n사워비 씨는 이 어음에 관하여 “손을 써 두겠다”는 뚜렷한 뜻으로 이튿날 바체스터로 내려가려 한다는 것을 밝힐 작정으로 찾아왔던 것이다. 그리고 그가 존 토저를 만났더라면 존은 그에게 얼마쯤 기한을 늘려 주지 않을 수 없었을 것이다. 톰도 존도 이것을 알고 있었고, 그래서 마음이 무른 쪽인 존이 자리를 피해 있었던 것이다. 톰이 물러질 위험은 없었다. 그리고 반 시간쯤 담판을 벌인 뒤에 사워비 씨는 그를 다시 남겨 두고 떠났으니, 그는 무른 기색이라고는 아무 조짐도 내보이지 않은 채였다.\n\n“우리가 바라는 건 현찰이오, 사워비 씨. 그게 다요.” 그 하원 의원이 방을 나설 때 그가 한 마지막 말은 그러했다.\n\n그러고 나서 사워비 씨는 또 다른 삯마차를 타고 누이동생의 집으로 실려 갔다. 돈에 쪼들리는 사람들, 곧 지금 사워비 씨가 그러한 것처럼 쪼들리는 사람들에 관하여 놀라운 것이 하나 있으니, 그들은 푼돈에 궁해 보이는 일이 도무지 없고 푼돈으로 살 수 있는 그 사치를 스스로 마다하는 일도 없다는 것이다. 삯마차, 만찬, 포도주, 연극, 새 장갑은 돈 문제에 빠져 허우적거리는 사람들이 언제라도 부릴 수 있는 것인데, 한 실링도 빚지지 않은 사람들은 그것들 없이 지내야 할 때가 어찌 그리 잦은가! 빚을 지지 않고 사는 것만큼 값비싼 즐거움은 없는 듯하다. 그러나 사람이 취미를 하나 가졌다면 그 값은 치러야 옳으니, 그것 또한 마땅한 일이다.\n\n해럴드 스미스 부인의 집은 옥스퍼드 스트리트만 건너면 되는 하노버 스퀘어 근처였으니 다른 사람이라면 그 한 실링을 아꼈을 것이다. 그러나 사워비 씨는 이런 것을 도무지 생각하지 않았다. 그는 평생 한 실링도 아껴 본 적이 없었고, 이제 와서 그러기 시작할 생각도 떠오르지 않았다. 그는 자기를 위해 집에 있어 달라고 미리 사람을 보내 두었고, 이제 와 보니 그녀가 기다리고 있었다.\n\n“해리엇,” 그는 안락의자에 몸을 뒤로 던지며 말했다. “판이 이제 거의 끝났네.”\n\n“터무니없는 소리.” 그녀가 말했다. “끝까지 밀고 나갈 기운만 있으면 판은 조금도 끝나지 않았어요.”\n\n“내가 말할 수 있는 것은, 오늘 아침에 공작의 변호사로부터 곧바로 저당권을 실행하겠다는 정식 통지를 받았다는 것뿐이네. 포더길이 아니라 사우스 오들리 스트리트의 그 사람들한테서 온 것이야.”\n\n“예상하고 있었잖아요.” 누이동생이 말했다.\n\n“그렇다고 그것이 어떻게 나아지는지는 모르겠네. 게다가 내가 정말로 예상했는지도 그리 확실하지 않아. 적어도 확실하다고 느끼지는 않았지. 이제는 의심할 것이 없네.”\n\n“의심할 것이 없는 편이 낫지요. 오라버니가 어떤 땅에 서야 하는지를 아는 편이 훨씬 나아요.”\n\n“내가 서 있을 땅이 곧 없어질 걸세. 적어도 내 것으로는 하나도, 한 에이커도 없을 거야.” 그 불행한 사내가 목소리에 큰 쓰라림을 담아 말했다.\n\n“실제로 오라버니가 지난해보다 지금 더 가난해질 수는 없어요. 이야기할 만큼 쓴 것도 없잖아요. 콜디코츠면 공작에게 진 빚을 다 갚기에 넉넉하다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없어요.”\n\n“겨우 그만큼일 걸세. 그러고 나면 나는 어찌하란 말인가? 나는 콜디코츠보다 그 의석이 더 아깝다는 생각까지 드네.”\n\n“내가 무엇을 권하는지 알잖아요.” 스미스 부인이 말했다. “던스터블 양에게, 공작이 쥐고 있는 것과 같은 담보로 그 돈을 대어 달라고 부탁하세요. 그러면 그이도 지금 공작이 그러한 만큼 안전할 거예요. 그리고 그 일을 이룰 수 있으면 공작에 맞서 주 선거에 나서세요. 어쩌면 지겠지요.”\n\n“나에게는 가망이 없을 걸세.”\n\n“하지만 오라버니가 공작의 손아귀에 든 물건이 아니라는 것은 보여 줄 수 있어요. 그게 내가 권하는 것이에요.” 스미스 부인이 몹시 기운차게 말했다. “그리고 원하신다면 내가 던스터블 양에게 말을 꺼내서, 그이의 변호사에게 그것을 살펴보게 해 달라고 부탁하겠어요.”\n\n“그 다른 어리석은 짓에 머리를 들이밀기 전에 이것을 해 두었더라면!”\n\n“그 일로 애태우지 말아요. 그이는 그런 투자로 잃을 것이 없으니, 오라버니가 그이에게 무슨 은혜를 청하는 것이 아니에요. 게다가 그이가 먼저 그러겠다고 하지 않았어요? 그리고 그이는 어제 그 다른 일을 오라버니에게 해 주기를 마다했으니, 지금 이 일은 오라버니에게 해 줄 바로 그런 여자예요. 나다니엘, 오라버니는 대개의 일은 다 알아요. 하지만 여자를 아는지는 나도 잘 모르겠어요. 적어도 그이 같은 여자라면 더욱 그렇지요.”\n\n보름쯤 전에 그 손을 얻으려 했던 바로 그 여인에게 돈의 도움을 구한다는 것은 사워비 씨에게 결을 거스르는 일이었다. 그러나 그는 누이동생이 이기게 두었다. 그만한 궁지에 몰린 사람이 결을 거스르지 않고 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가? 지금 이 순간 그는 마음속으로 공작과 포더길 씨와 검션 게이지비 상회와 개서럼 성과 사우스 오들리 스트리트의 온 족속에게 끝없는 미움을 느꼈다. 그들은 옴니엄이라는 이름이 이 주에서, 아니 영국에서 들려 본 적도 없었을 때부터 사워비 집안의 것이었던 것을 그에게서 빼앗으려 하고 있었다! 깊은 바다의 큰 레비아탄이 그를 먹이로 삼켜 버리려고 애가 타 있었다! 그는 삼켜지고, 없어지고, 눈앞에서 치워질 것이었다. 후회의 아픔 한 점 없이! 그토록 나쁜 날을 미룰 방편으로 지금 나서 줄 수 있는 수단이라면 무엇이든 반가울 것이었다. 그래서 그는 던스터블 양에게 이 두 번째 제안을 하는 소임을 누이동생에게 맡겼다. 공작을 저주하면서 — 그는 정말로 공작을 실컷 저주했으니 — 결국 공작은 제 것을 달라고 한 것뿐이라는 생각은 그에게 좀처럼 떠오르지 않았다.\n\n해럴드 스미스 부인을 두고 말한다면, 아내로서 그리고 사회의 한 사람으로서 그 됨됨이 전체를 어떻게 보든, 누이로서는 그녀에게 미덕이 있었다는 것만은 인정해야 한다.\n\n# 제33장. 위로\n\n이튿날 두 시 정각에 마크 로바츠는 원틀리의 용에 와 있었다. 해럴드 스미스의 강연이 끝난 뒤 일행이 아침을 먹었던 바로 그 방을 오르내리며 사워비 씨가 오기를 기다리고 있었다. 벗이 무슨 일로 자기를 보려 하는지는 그도 넉넉히 짚어 낼 수 있었고, 그 부름을 받은 것이 싫기보다는 오히려 반가웠다. 여태 보아 온 것으로 벗의 사람됨을 헤아리자면, 사워비 씨가 그 무서운 어음들에 대해 무언가 마련을 해 놓을 힘을 손에 쥐지 못한 한에는 몸을 피해 있었으리라 여겨졌기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 그 침침한 방을 오르내리며 기다리는 사람이 오기를 조바심 냈고, 시계가 세 시 십오 분 전을 치는데도 사워비 씨가 거기 없다는 것만으로 자기가 지독하게 홀대받고 있다고 생각했다. 그러나 시계가 세 시를 칠 때 사워비 씨는 거기 있었고, 마크 로바츠의 희망은 거의 끝나 있었다.\n\n“그 사람들이 구백 파운드를 요구하겠다는 말입니까?” 로바츠가 일어서서 그 하원 의원을 노엽게 노려보며 말했다.\n\n“그럴 것 같아 걱정입니다,” 사워비가 말했다. “무엇을 할 수 있을지 우리가 함께 살펴보려면 가장 나쁜 것을 당신에게 말해 두는 편이 좋겠다고 생각해요.”\n\n“나는 아무것도 할 수 없고, 아무것도 하지 않겠습니다,” 로바츠가 말했다. “그들이야 하고 싶은 대로 하라지요 — 법이 그들에게 허락하는 것을요.”\n\n그러고서 그는 패니와 아이 방을, 그리고 자존심 때문에 러프턴 경의 청혼을 거절한 루시를 생각했고, 눈에 고이는 눈물을 앞에 앉은 저 세상 물정에 밝은 매정한 사나이가 보지 못하도록 얼굴을 돌렸다.\n\n“하지만, 마크, 여보게 —” 사워비는 제 구슬리는 목소리의 힘에 다시 기대 보려 하면서 말했다.\n\n그러나 로바츠는 들으려 하지 않았다.\n\n“사워비 씨,” 그는 한 음절 한 음절마다 스스로 들통이 나는 침착함을 꾸며 내며 말했다. “당신이 나를 털어 갔다는 생각이 듭니다. 내가 바보였다는 것, 바보보다 더한 것이었다는 것은 나도 잘 알아요. 그러나 — 그러나 — 그러나 세상에서 당신이 차지한 자리가 이런 대접만은 나에게 하지 않도록 보장해 줄 것이라고 나는 생각했습니다.”\n\n사워비 씨는 결코 감정이 없는 사람이 아니었고, 지금 들은 그 말은 그를 몹시 깊이 베었다 — 분개하는 척하며 그 말에 맞설 수 있는 길이 도무지 없었기에 더욱 그러했다. 그는 제 벗을 털었고, 그 모든 재치를 가지고도 자기가 그러지 않았던 것처럼 보이게 만들 만큼 재치 있는 말을 이 순간에는 하나도 알지 못했다.\n\n“로바츠,” 그가 말했다. “지금은 나에게 무슨 말을 해도 좋아요. 나는 그것을 원망하지 않겠습니다.”\n\n“누가 당신의 원망을 마음에 두겠습니까?” 성직자가 그에게 사납게 돌아서며 말했다. “신사의 원망은 신사에게 무서운 것이고, 의로운 사람 하나의 원망은 또 다른 의로운 사람에게 무서운 것입니다. 당신의 원망이라니!” — 그러고서 그는 방의 길이를 두 번 오르내렸고, 사워비는 제 자리에서 말문이 막힌 채 남았다. “나를 이렇게 망쳐 놓을 궁리를 하던 때에 당신이 내 아내와 아이들을 한 번이라도 생각해 본 적이 있는지 궁금하군요!” 그러고서 그는 다시 방을 오르내렸다.\n\n“이 일을 어떻게든 타결해 볼 생각으로 이야기할 만큼 얼마 뒤에는 마음이 가라앉겠지요?”\n\n“아니요, 나는 그런 시도를 하지 않겠습니다. 당신 말로는 당신의 그 벗들이 나에게 구백 파운드의 청구권을 가지고 있고 그것을 즉시 갚으라고 요구한다지요. 그 돈 가운데 얼마를 내가 손에 쥐어 보았는지 당신은 법정에서 물음을 받게 될 겁니다. 나는 한 실링도 손대 본 적이 없다는 것을, 손대고 싶어 한 적조차 없다는 것을 당신은 알고 있어요. 나는 어떤 타결도 시도하지 않겠습니다. 내 몸은 여기 있고, 저기 내 집이 있습니다. 그들이 할 수 있는 최악을 하라지요.”\n\n“하지만, 마크 —”\n\n“내 성으로 부르시고, 그 다정한 척은 그만두시지요, 선생. 야바위꾼에게 그렇게 속아 넘어갔으니 내가 얼마나 얼간이였던가!”\n\n사워비는 이런 것을 조금도 예상하지 못했다. 로바츠에게 자기, 곧 사워비가 신사의 기개라 불렀을 만한 것이 있다는 것은 그도 늘 알고 있었다. 그는 로바츠를 담대하고 툭 터놓고 넉넉한 사람으로, 그렇게 해야 할 자리에서는 제 몫을 감당할 수 있고 제 마음을 말하기를 결코 꺼리지 않는 사람으로 여겨 왔다. 그러나 그에게서 이런 분개의 급류가 터져 나올 줄은 예상하지 못했고, 그가 이만한 깊이의 노여움을 낼 수 있는 사람이라고는 생각하지 못했다.\n\n“그런 말을 쓰신다면, 로바츠, 나는 당신을 두고 떠날 수밖에 없어요.”\n\n“그러셔도 좋습니다. 가시지요. 당신은 나에게서 구백 파운드를 뽑아내려는 그 사람들의 심부름꾼이라고 스스로 말하고 있어요. 당신은 그 궁리에서 제 몫을 다했고, 이제 그들의 전갈을 가져왔지요. 내가 보기에는 당신이 그들에게 돌아가는 편이 좋을 것 같습니다. 나로 말하면, 내 아내에게 앞으로 닥칠 운명을 알려 줄 시간이 필요합니다.”\n\n“로바츠, 당신은 언젠가 그 매정한 말을 후회하게 될 거예요.”\n\n“당신은 언젠가 당신이 한 매정한 짓을 후회하게 될지, 아니면 당신에게는 이런 일들이 정말로 우스개일 뿐인지 궁금하군요.”\n\n“나는 이 순간 파멸한 사람입니다,” 사워비가 말했다. “모든 것이 나에게서 떠나가고 있어요 — 세상에서 내가 선 자리, 집안의 땅, 아버지의 집, 의회의 내 의석, 내 나라 사람들 가운데서 살아갈 힘, 아니 실은 어디서든 살아갈 힘까지요. 그러나 이 모든 것이 지금 나를 짓누르는 것은, 내가 당신에게 가져다준 그 참담함이 나를 짓누르는 것만 못합니다.” 그러고서 사워비도 얼굴을 돌리고, 꾸며 낸 것이 아닌 눈물을 눈에서 닦아 냈다.\n\n로바츠는 여전히 방을 오르내리고 있었으나, 이 말을 듣고서 꾸짖음을 이어 갈 수는 없었다. 언제나 그러하다. 사람이 제 머리에 모욕을 쌓아 올리기를 견뎌 내면 남들의 모욕을 잠잠하게 만든다 — 그 순간만은. 사워비는 이것을 곰곰이 따져 본 것은 아니었으나 어렴풋이 느끼는 바가 있었고, 이제 이야기를 할 틈이 마침내 생겼다는 것을 곧 알아보았다.\n\n“당신은 나에게 온당하지 못합니다,” 그가 말했다. “나에게 이제 당신을 구할 마음이 없다고 여기는 것 말이에요. 오로지 그러기를 바라는 마음으로 나는 여기에 온 겁니다.”\n\n“그런데 당신이 바라는 것이 무엇입니까? 내가 또 어음 두 장을 인수하는 것이겠지요.”\n\n“두 장이 아니라, 갱신한 어음 한 장으로 —”\n\n“들어 보시지요, 사워비 씨. 이 세상에서 나에게 내놓을 수 있는 어떤 이유를 들어도 나는 인수라는 이름으로 어떤 어음에도 다시는 내 이름을 적지 않겠습니다. 나는 몹시 약했고 내 약함이 부끄럽습니다. 그러나 그만한 힘은 아직 남아 있기를 바랍니다. 나는 몹시 악했고 내 악함이 부끄럽습니다. 그러나 그만한 옳은 원칙은 아직 남아 있기를 바랍니다. 나는 다른 어떤 어음에도 내 이름을 적지 않겠습니다. 당신을 위해서도, 나 자신을 위해서조차도요.”\n\n“하지만 로바츠, 지금 당신 형편에서 그것은 미친 짓일 겁니다.”\n\n“그러면 나는 미치겠습니다.”\n\n“포레스트를 만나 보았어요? 당신이 그와 이야기를 해 보면 모든 것이 조정될 수 있다는 것을 알게 될 겁니다.”\n\n“나는 이미 포레스트 씨에게 백오십 파운드를 빚지고 있습니다. 당신이 그 말값을 내라고 나를 재촉했을 때 그에게서 얻은 것이지요. 그리고 나는 그 빚을 늘리지 않겠습니다. 그때도 내가 또 얼마나 얼간이였던지요. 어쩌면 당신은 기억하지 못할 테지만, 내가 그 말을 사기로 했을 때 그 값은 이 어음들을 갚는 데 내가 보태는 몫이 되기로 되어 있었습니다.”\n\n“나도 기억합니다. 하지만 그것이 어떻게 된 일인지 당신에게 말해 주겠어요.”\n\n“그런 것은 아무래도 좋습니다. 처음부터 끝까지 죄다 한통속이었지요.”\n\n“그러나 내 말을 들어 봐요. 내가 겪어 온 것을 다 알게 되면 당신도 나를 딱하게 여길 것이라고 생각해요. 당신이 그 말을 받았을 때 나에게는 당신에게 그 돈을 청할 뜻이 없었다는 것을 내 엄숙한 말로 맹세합니다 — 정말로 없었어요. 그러나 러프턴의 그 일을 당신도 기억하지요. 그가 런던의 당신 호텔로 찾아와서 미결 어음 한 장 때문에 그토록 노여워했던 일 말이에요.”\n\n“내가 관련된 한에서는 그가 아주 사리에 어긋났다는 것을 나는 알고 있습니다.”\n\n“그랬지요. 그러나 그것은 아무 차이가 없어요. 그는 노여움에 사로잡혀 그 일을 전부 드러내 놓기로 마음을 굳혔고, 그가 그렇게 하면 당신에게 가장 크게 해가 될 것이 나에게 보였습니다. 당신이 바체스터의 참사회원 자리를 막 받아들인 참이었으니까요.” 여기서 그 딱한 참사회원은 몸서리치게 움찔했다. “나는 그 어음을 회수하려고 하늘과 땅을 움직였어요. 그 독수리들은 내가 그것에 매기는 값을 알아채자 제 먹이에 딱 붙어 버렸고, 그것을 손에 넣으려고 나는 그 자리에서 백 파운드가 넘는 돈을 마련해야 했습니다. 그 어음에 정말로 물어야 할 돈은 한 실링까지 벌써 오래전에 다 갚아진 것이었는데도요. 그 백이십 파운드를 마련하고 싶었던 것만큼 돈을 얻고 싶었던 적은 내 평생 없었어요. 그리고 마지막 순간에 자비를 바라는 이 마음을 걸고 말하거니와, 나는 그것을 당신을 위해 했습니다. 러프턴은 그 일로 나를 해칠 수 없었어요.”\n\n“하지만 당신은 그것을 이십오 파운드에 사 왔다고 그에게 말했지요.”\n\n“그래요, 그에게 그렇게 말했습니다. 나는 그에게 그렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었어요. 그러지 않았다면 내가 그것을 얼마나 애타게 얻고 싶어 했는지 드러내어 스스로를 죄인으로 만들어 보이는 셈이 되었을 테니까요. 그리고 그와 당신이 함께 있는 자리에서 이 모든 것을 설명할 수 없었다는 것을 당신도 알잖아요. 당신은 진저리를 내며 그 참사회원 자리를 내던져 버렸을 겁니다.”\n\n그랬더라면 좋았을 것을! 그것이 이제 마크의 소원이었다 — 부질없는 소원이었다. 개서럼 성에서의 그날 밤 저지른 어리석음 때문에 그는 얼마나 깊은 낙담의 늪에 빠져 허우적거리게 되었는가! 그때 그는 어리석은 짓 하나를 했는데, 이제 거의 온전한 파멸로 그것을 후회해야 하는가? 그는 이 모든 거짓말에도 진저리가 났다. 어쩔 수 없이 헤치고 걸어야 하는 그 더러움에 그의 영혼 자체가 기가 꺾였다. 그는 자기도 모르는 사이에 사람 가운데 가장 낮은 밑바닥과 얽혀 버렸고, 날마다 나오는 신문에서 제 이름이 그들의 이름과 뒤섞인 것을 보아야 할 것이었다. 그런데 무엇을 위해 이런 짓을 했던가? 무엇을 위해 이렇게 제 마음을 더럽히고 제가 입은 성직자의 옷에 부끄러운 사람이 되었던가? 사워비 씨 같은 이의 벗이 되어 주려고!\n\n“그런데,” 사워비가 말을 이었다. “나는 그 돈을 얻어 냈어요. 그러나 갚는 것에 대해 나에게 요구된 그 다짐이 얼마나 엄격한 것이었는지 당신은 좀처럼 믿지 못할 겁니다. 나는 그것을 해럴드 스미스에게서 얻었는데, 앞으로 아무리 궁한 처지에 몰려도 다시는 그에게 도움을 바라지 않을 거예요. 나는 그것을 딱 보름 동안만 빌렸습니다. 그리고 그것을 갚으려고 당신에게 그 말값을 청하지 않을 수 없었어요. 마크, 나는 이 모든 것을 당신을 위해서 한 겁니다 — 정말로 그랬어요.”\n\n“그런데 이제 나는 당신의 그 친절에, 내가 세상에 가진 모든 것을 잃는 것으로 갚아야 하는군요.”\n\n“이 일을 포레스트 씨의 손에 맡기기만 하면 무엇 하나 손댈 것이 없습니다 — 말 등의 털 한 올까지도요. 아니, 당신이 그 전액을 당신 수입에서 조금씩 스스로 갚아야 하게 된다 해도 그렇습니다. 분기마다 만기가 돌아오는 어음을 여러 장 작성해 두면, 그러고 나면 —”\n\n“나는 어떤 어음도 작성하지 않겠습니다. 이 일로는 어떤 종이에도 내 이름을 적지 않겠어요. 그 점에 대해서는 내 마음이 온전히 정해졌습니다. 그들이야 와서 최악을 하라지요.”\n\n사워비 씨는 오랫동안 버티고 애썼으나 그 목사를 이 자리에서 조금도 움직일 수 없었다. 그는 사워비 씨가 조정이라 부르는 것을 위해서는 아무것도 하지 않겠다고 하면서, 자기는 프램리의 집에 그대로 있을 것이고 자기에게 청구할 것이 있는 사람은 누구든 법의 절차를 밟으면 된다고 우겼다.\n\n“나 자신은 아무것도 하지 않겠습니다,” 그는 말했다. “그러나 나를 상대로 절차가 밟힌다면, 나는 그 돈을 한 실링도 만져 본 적이 없다는 것을 밝히겠습니다.” 그리고 이런 결심으로 그는 원틀리의 용을 떠났다.\n\n사워비 씨는 한 번, 존 로바츠에게서 그만한 액수를 빌리는 것이 어떻겠느냐고 한마디 했다. 그러나 이에 대해 마크는 아무 말도 하려 하지 않았다. 사워비 씨는 이제 그가 그런 문제로 의논을 나누려는 벗이 아니었다. “나는 지금 내가 무엇을 할지 밝힐 준비가 되어 있지 않습니다,” 그는 말했다. “먼저 다른 사람들이 어떤 걸음을 밟는지 보아야 합니다.” 그러고서 그는 모자를 들고 나갔다. 그리고 원틀리의 용 마당에서, 이제 그토록 싫어할 이유가 많아진 그 말에 올라 천천히 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n그 말길에 여러 생각이 그의 머릿속을 지나갔으나, 오직 한 가지 결심만이 거기 자리를 붙였다. 그는 이제 아내에게 모든 것을 말해야 했다. 집행관이 문 앞에 와서 그를 주 감옥으로 끌고 갈 채비를 할 때까지, 또는 두 사람이 누워 자는 침대가 몸 아래에서 팔려 나갈 때까지 그것을 말하지 않고 두는 그런 매정한 짓은 하지 않을 것이었다. 그렇다, 그는 아내에게 모든 것을 말할 것이었다 — 결심이 다시 시들어 버리기 전에 당장. 그는 마당에서 말에서 내려, 부엌문에 있는 아내의 하녀를 보고서 안주인에게 서재로 와 주시라고 청해 달라고 일렀다. 뜻이 기울어 가도록 반 시간도 흘려보내지 않을 것이었다. 사람이 물에 빠지도록 정해져 있다면, 빠져서 끝을 내 버리는 편이 낫지 않겠는가?\n\n로바츠 부인이 그의 방으로 왔는데, 그가 방에 들어서는 순간에 그의 팔을 만질 만큼 때맞추어 닿았다.\n\n“메리가 당신이 나를 찾는다고 하던데요. 나는 정원 일을 하고 있었는데, 막 들어오는 것을 그 애가 붙잡았어요.”\n\n“그래, 패니, 당신을 찾았어. 잠깐 앉아.” 그러고서 그는 방을 건너가 채찍을 제자리에 놓았다.\n\n“아, 마크, 무슨 일이 있는 거예요?”\n\n“그래, 여보, 그래. 앉아, 패니. 당신이 앉아 주면 이야기하기가 더 나아.”\n\n그러나 그 딱한 부인은 앉고 싶지 않았다. 그가 무슨 불행을 비추어 말했으니, 그의 곁에 서서 그에게 매달리고 싶은 마음이 간절했다.\n\n“좋아요, 그러면. 꼭 그래야 한다면 앉을게요. 하지만 마크, 나를 겁나게 하지 말아요. 왜 얼굴이 그렇게 참담해요?”\n\n“패니, 내가 아주 잘못을 했어,” 그가 말했다. “아주 어리석었어. 당신에게 큰 슬픔과 괴로움을 가져온 것 같아.” 그러고서 그는 머리를 손에 기대고 얼굴을 아내에게서 돌렸다.\n\n“아, 마크, 사랑하는 마크, 내 마크! 그게 뭐예요?” 그러고서 그녀는 얼른 의자에서 일어나 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다. “나에게서 돌아서지 말아요. 말해 줘요, 마크! 말해 줘요, 우리가 함께 나누게요.”\n\n“그래, 패니, 이제 당신에게 말해야지. 그런데 당신이 그것을 듣고 나면 나를 어떻게 생각할지 나는 도무지 모르겠어.”\n\n“당신이 내 남편이라고 생각하겠지요, 마크. 그것을 생각할 거예요 — 그것을 가장 먼저요, 그것이 무엇이든지요.” 그러고서 그녀는 그의 무릎을 쓰다듬으며 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았고, 그의 한 손을 붙잡아 제 두 손 사이에 꼭 눌렀다. “당신이 어리석었다 해도, 내가 용서하지 못한다면 누가 당신을 용서하겠어요?”\n\n그러고서 그는 아내에게 모든 것을 말했다. 사워비 씨가 그를 자기 침실로 데리고 들어갔던 그 저녁부터 시작하여, 이제는 어음에 대해 이제는 말에 대해 차근차근 이야기해 나가니, 딱한 아내는 그 셈의 뒤엉킴 속에서 아주 길을 잃어버렸다. 그녀는 그의 이야기의 자세한 것들을 도무지 따라갈 수 없었고, 사워비 씨에 대한 그의 분개에 온전히 마음을 같이할 수도 없었다. 어음을 갱신한다는 것이 어떤 것인지 조금도 알아듣지 못했기 때문이다. 이 일에서 그녀에게 중요한 것은 오직 남편이 갚아 내라는 요구를 받을 돈의 액수뿐이었다 — 그것과, 그가 다시는 그런 빚을 지지 않을 것이라는 그녀의 강한 희망, 이미 확신이 되어 있는 그 희망뿐이었다.\n\n“그러면 그게 다 얼마예요, 여보?”\n\n“그 사람들이 나에게 구백 파운드를 청구하고 있어.”\n\n“아, 어쩌나! 그건 무서운 액수예요.”\n\n“그리고 은행에서 빌린 백오십 파운드가 있어 — 그 말값이지, 당신도 알잖아. 그리고 다른 빚들도 좀 있어 — 큰 액수는 아닌 것 같지만, 이제 사람들은 자기에게 받을 것이 있으면 한 실링까지 찾아 나설 거야. 내가 그것을 다 갚아야 한다면 천이백이나 천삼백 파운드가 될 거야.”\n\n“그건 한 해 수입만큼이나 되겠네요, 마크. 참사회원 자리를 넣어도요.”\n\n그것이 그녀가 한 유일한 꾸짖음의 말이었다 — 그것을 꾸짖음이라 부를 수 있다면.\n\n“그래,” 그가 말했다. “그리고 그것을 청구하는 사람들은 힘이 있는 한 어떤 희생을 치르게 하고서라도 인정 없이 받아 낼 사람들이야. 그리고 그 대가로 받은 것이 하나도 없이 이 모든 빚을 지게 되었다는 것을 생각하면! 아, 패니, 당신은 나를 어떻게 생각할까!”\n\n그러나 그녀는 그런 것은 조금도 생각하지 않겠다고 그에게 맹세했다 — 그것을 남편에게 거슬리는 것으로 마음에 두지 않겠다고, 그것이 남편에 대한 자기 신뢰를 조금도 덜어 놓을 수 없을 것이라고. 그가 자기 남편이 아닌가? 그것을 알게 되어서, 그를 위로해 줄 수 있게 되어서 그녀는 그토록 기뻤다. 그리고 그녀는 정말로 그를 위로했으니, 그가 그것을 이야기해 나가는 사이에 짐은 그의 어깨에서 점점 더 가벼워졌다. 그런 짐은 정말로 그렇게 가벼워진다. 한 쌍의 어깨를 짓눌러 부술 짐도 똑같이 나뉘면 — 둘이서 나누어 지고, 그 둘이 각기 더 무거운 쪽을 지려 한다면 — 깃털처럼 가벼워진다. 마음의 짐을 그렇게 나누는 것이야말로 사람이 아내를 원하는 으뜸된 목적 가운데 하나가 아니겠는가? 제 슬픔을 감추어 두는 것만큼 큰 어리석음은 없으니 말이다.\n\n그리고 이 아내는 기꺼이, 즐거이, 고맙게 제 몫을 받아들였다. 지아비와 함께 지아비의 온갖 괴로움을 견디는 것은 그녀에게 쉬운 일이었다. 그것은 그녀가 스스로 맹세한 일이었다. 그러나 지아비에게 자기가 모르는 괴로움이 있다고 생각하는 것 — 그것이야말로 그녀에게 견딜 수 없는 오직 하나의 일이었을 것이다.\n\n그러고서 두 사람은 계획을 의논했다 — 이 무서운 돈 곤경에서 벗어날 어떤 길이 있을지를. 참된 여인답게 로바츠 부인은 남는 것은 다 버리자고 그 자리에서 제의했다. 말은 다 팔 것이다. 암소는 팔지 않되 거기서 나오는 버터를 팔 것이다. 조랑말 마차를 팔고 마부는 내보낼 것이다. 하인이 나가야 하는 것은 너무나 당연한 일이라 말조차 거의 나오지 않았다. 그러나 바체스터의 그 집, 성당 구내에 있는 그 위엄 있는 참사회원 저택은 어떻게 할 것인가 — 한 해 더 비워 두도록 허락받을 수는 없을까, 어쩌면 세를 놓을 수는 없을까? 세상은 물론 그들의 불행을 알게 될 것이다. 그러나 그 불행에 용감하게 맞선다면 세상도 그 나무람에 덜 매서울 것이다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 레이디 러프턴에게는 모든 것을 말해야 했다.\n\n“패니, 아무튼 이것만은 믿어 줘,” 그가 말했다. “나에게 무엇을 내놓는다 해도 나는 다시는 어음에 내 이름을 적지 않을 거야.”\n\n그녀가 이 말에 고마워하며 준 입맞춤은, 그가 그날 그녀에게 가장 밝은 소식을 가져왔더라도 그러했을 만큼 따뜻하고 넉넉했다. 그리고 그날 저녁 그가 앉아서 아내뿐 아니라 루시와도 함께 이 모든 것을 이야기했을 때, 그는 어떻게 해서 제 괴로움이 이제 이토록 가벼워졌는지 이상하게 여겼다.\n\n사람이 저만의 즐거움을 가져야 하는지 어떤지는 나는 여기서 말하지 않겠다. 그러나 제 슬픔을 저만의 것으로 감추어 두는 것은 결코 그에게 값이 될 수 없다.\n\n# 제34장. 레이디 러프턴이 뜻밖의 일을 당하다\n\n러프턴 경은 런던으로 돌아오는 길에 다음으로 어떤 걸음을 내디뎌야 할지 정하기가 적잖이 어려웠다. 이따금 잠깐씩은, 루시가 제 앞길에 던져 놓은 이 번거로움만큼의 값이 없는 여인일지도 모른다고 생각하는—아니, 차라리 스스로에게 그렇게 말해 보는—쪽으로 반쯤 기울기도 했다. 루시를 몹시 사랑하고 있고 기꺼이 아내로 삼겠다고, 그런 순간에 그는 생각했거나 그렇게 말했다. 그러나— 하지만 그런 순간은 순간에 지나지 않았다. 사랑에 빠진 사내가 제 사랑이 어려워졌다는 이유로 덜 사랑하게 되는 일은 좀처럼 없다. 그리하여 그런 순간이 지나가면 그는 당장 어머니에게 말해 버리기로, 그리고 로바츠 양에게 승낙의 뜻을 밝혀 달라고 어머니를 밀어붙이기로 마음을 굳혔다. 어머니가 그리 기뻐하지 않을 것은 알고 있었다. 그러나 이 일에 제 뜻이 따로 있음을 보일 만큼 자기가 단단히 버티면 어머니도 아마 거스르지는 못할 터였다. 그는 이것을 은혜로 여겨 몸을 낮추어 청하지는 않을 작정이었다. 그저 좋은 어머니라면 아들을 위하여 망설임 없이 해 주어야 할 어머니의 소임 가운데 하나인 양, 그 절차를 치러 달라고 마님께 요구하려 했다. 앨버니에 있는 자기 거처에 이르렀을 때 그가 마지막으로 굳힌 결심은 그러했다.\n\n이튿날 그는 어머니를 만나지 않았다. 노르웨이로 떠나기 바로 전에 어머니와 마주 앉는 것이 좋으리라고, 그러면 같은 이야기를 두 번 되풀이할 일이 없으리라고 그는 생각했다. 그래서 그가 그 말을 꺼낸 것은 정말로 떠나기 하루 전날이었고, 그날 그는 브룩 스트리트로 아침을 먹으러 가겠다고 스스로 청해 두었다.\n\n“어머니.” 그가 식당의 안락의자 하나에 몸을 던지며 아주 불쑥 말했다. “드릴 말씀이 있어요.”\n\n어머니는 그 말이 중대한 것임을 곧 알아차렸고, 저만이 지닌 남다른 어머니의 본능으로 의논하려는 문제가 혼사에 관한 것이라고 짐작했다. 아들이 돈 이야기를 하려는 것이었다면 목소리도 눈빛도 달랐을 것이다. 페킨으로 순례를 떠나겠다거나 허드슨만 준주로 오래 낚시 여행을 가겠다는 생각을 품고 있는 경우라도—또 다른 식으로—역시 달랐을 것이다.\n\n“말씀이라니, 루도빅! 그래, 나는 아주 한가하다.”\n\n“루시 로바츠를 어떻게 보시는지 알고 싶어요.”\n\n레이디 러프턴은 얼굴이 창백해지며 겁에 질렸고, 피가 심장으로 차갑게 몰렸다. 아들이 사랑 이야기를 꺼내려 한다고 짐작했을 때에도 그녀는 기뻐하기보다 두려워했으나, 이만큼 나쁜 것은 두려워한 적조차 없었다. “내가 루시 로바츠를 어떻게 보느냐고?” 그녀는 아들의 말을 그대로 되받아 옮기며, 당황한 기색이 뚜렷한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“그래요, 어머니. 요사이 어머니는 제가 혼인을 해야 한다고 생각한다는 말씀을 한두 번 하셨고, 저도 그렇게 여기기 시작했어요. 어머니는 저를 위해 어느 성직자의 딸을 골라 두셨지만, 그 아가씨는 스스로 훨씬 더 나은 자리를 얻으려—”\n\n“결코 더 나은 자리가 아니다.” 레이디 러프턴이 날카롭게 말했다.\n\n“그러니 저는 제 손으로 다른 성직자의 누이를 고를까 해요. 어머니가 로바츠 양을 싫어하시지는 않겠지요?”\n\n“아아, 루도빅!”\n\n그 자리에서 레이디 러프턴이 할 수 있는 말은 그것뿐이었다.\n\n“그 사람에게 나쁜 데가 있나요? 어머니는 그 사람에게 무엇을 반대하시나요? 그 사람에게 제 아내가 되기에 마땅치 않은 무엇이 있나요?”\n\n한두 순간 레이디 러프턴은 말없이 앉아 생각을 가다듬었다. 루시 로바츠를 앞날의 레이디 러프턴이 될 수도 있는 사람으로 놓고 보자면 반대할 까닭이 아주 크다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그 까닭을 다 늘어놓기는 어려웠겠지만 하나하나가 여간 단단한 것이 아니었다. 루시 로바츠에게는 그녀가 보기에 아름다움도, 자태도, 몸가짐도, 갖추었으면 하는 교양조차 없었다. 레이디 러프턴 자신은 세속에 물든 여인이 아니었다. 그만한 자리에 있는 여인으로서 세속과 그렇게까지 멀 수 있을까 싶을 만큼 멀었다. 그럼에도, 자기가 그토록 오래 채워 온 자리를 이어받기에 마땅하다고 여겨질 만한 젊은 여인의 됨됨이에는 반드시 있어야 한다고 그녀가 여기는 세속의 자질이 몇 가지 있었다. 아들의 아내를 구하면서 그녀가 바란 것은 그 자질을, 똑같이 반드시 있어야 한다고 여기는 도덕상의 덕과 아울러 갖춘 사람이었다. 루시 로바츠에게 그 도덕상의 덕이 있는지 없는지는 알 수 없는 일이었으나, 나머지 자질로 말하면 레이디 러프턴은 루시를 도무지 모자란 사람으로 보았다. 루시는 결코 레이디 러프턴처럼 보일 수 없고, 레이디 러프턴이 마땅히 그래야 하듯 주 안에서 몸가짐을 지닐 수도 없을 터였다. 레이디 러프턴이 지체 있는 집 젊은 새댁에게서 보기를 좋아하는 그 조용한 몸가짐, 그 고요한 위엄이 루시에게는 없었다. 루시는 혀를 놀려서가 아니면 방 안에서 아무도 아닐 사람이라고, 그런 데 반해 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 저녁 내내 입을 다물고 있어도 그 자리에 있다는 사실의 장엄함만으로 누구에게나 깊은 자취를 남겼으리라고 그녀는 말했을 것이다. 게다가 루시에게는 돈이 없었고, 게다가 루시는 그녀 자신의 교구 성직자의 누이일 뿐이었다. 사람은 제 고향에서 좀처럼 선지자가 되지 못하는 법이니, 루시도 프램리에서는 선지자가 아니었다. 적어도 레이디 러프턴의 눈에는 아니었다. 독자도 기억하시겠지만 전에도 한 번 그녀는 이 일로 두려워한 적이 있었다. 아들을 두고 두려워한 것이 아니라—그런 어리석음을 아들이 저지르리라고는 차마 의심할 수도 없었으니까—어리석게도 나리가 자기를 사랑한다고 헛생각을 품을지 모를 루시를 두고 두려워한 것이었다. 아아, 아아! 이 순간 아들의 물음은 이 딱한 여인에게 무서운 일격의 무게로 떨어져 내렸다.\n\n“그 사람에게 제 아내가 되기에 마땅치 않은 무엇이 있나요?”\n\n그것이 아들이 마지막으로 한 말이었다.\n\n“루도빅, 내 사랑하는 루도빅!” 그녀는 일어나 아들 쪽으로 건너오며 말했다. “나는 그렇게 생각한다. 정말로 그렇게 생각한다.”\n\n“무엇을 그렇게 생각하세요?” 그가 거의 노한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“나는 그 아이가 네 아내가 되기에 마땅치 않다고 생각한다. 그 아이는 네가 골랐으면 하고 내가 바라는 계층의 사람이 아니야.”\n\n“그리젤다 그랜틀리와 같은 계층이에요.”\n\n“아니다, 얘야. 그 점에서 너는 잘못 알고 있는 것 같다. 그랜틀리네는 다른 삶의 자리에서 지내 왔다. 그들이 그렇다는 것쯤은 너도 느낄 텐데—”\n\n“맹세하고 말씀드리지만, 어머니, 저는 느끼지 못해요. 한 사람은 플럼스테드의 주임 사제고 다른 한 사람은 프램리의 교구 사제지요. 하지만 그걸 따져 봐야 소용없어요. 저는 어머니가 루시 로바츠를 마음에 들여 주시기를 바라요. 그것을 은혜로 청하려고 일부러 어머니께 온 거예요.”\n\n“네 아내로 말이냐, 루도빅?”\n\n“예, 제 아내로요.”\n\n“그러면 네가—그 아이와 약혼했다고 알아들어야 하는 것이냐?”\n\n“글쎄요, 그렇다고는 말씀드릴 수 없어요. 실제로 약혼한 것은 아니에요. 그러나 이것만은 정해진 일로 여기셔도 좋아요. 제 힘이 미치는 데까지 저는 그리 될 작정이에요. 마음은 굳혔고, 결코 바꾸지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“그 아가씨도 이 일을 다 알고 있느냐?”\n\n“물론이지요.”\n\n‘끔찍하고, 교활하고, 밉살스럽고, 뒤가 구린 계집애.’ 레이디 러프턴은 속으로 그렇게 말했다. 그런 말을 아들 앞에서 소리 내어 할 만큼 담대하지는 도무지 못했던 것이다. 러프턴 경이 이미 분명한 청혼으로 제 몸을 얽어 놓았다면 무엇을 바랄 수 있겠는가? “그 아이의 오라비와 로바츠 부인은? 그이들도 알고 있느냐?”\n\n“예, 둘 다 알고 있어요.”\n\n“그리고 둘 다 찬성하느냐?”\n\n“그렇다고는 말씀드릴 수 없어요. 로바츠 부인은 만나지 못했으니 그분 생각이 어떤지 저는 몰라요. 마크에 대해서는 솔직히 제 뜻을 말씀드리자면, 그가 마음으로부터 찬성한다고는 보지 않아요. 그는 어머니를 두려워하고, 어머니 생각이 어떤지 알고 싶어 할 거예요.”\n\n“어쨌든 그 말을 들으니 다행이다.” 레이디 러프턴이 엄숙하게 말했다. “그가 이 일을 부추기는 짓을 했다면 그건 아주 비루한 일이었을 게다.” 그러고서 또 한동안 짧은 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n러프턴 경은 이 일의 형편을 어머니에게 죄다 밝히지는 않기로 작정해 두었다. 모든 것이 어머니의 한마디에 걸려 있다는 것—곧 루시가 그와 혼인하겠다고 나설 조건은 오직 어머니 레이디 러프턴이 그러기를 바라 주는 것뿐이라는 것을 그는 말하지 않을 셈이었다. 지금 루시가 내린 판결에 따르자면 모든 것이 어머니에게 달려 있다는 것을 알게 하지 않을 셈이었다. 혼인을 하겠으니 허락해 달라고 어머니에게 청하는 일이 그는 몹시 싫었다. 그런데 사실을 다 털어놓는다면 그 허락을 청하지 않을 수 없게 될 것이었다. 그의 목적은 어머니가 루시를 좋게 생각하도록 만드는 것, 그리고 프램리에 내려가서 다정하고 너그럽고 애정 있게 대하도록 이끄는 것이었다. 그러고 나면 노르웨이에서 돌아와 다시 그곳을 찾을 때—그는 그럴 작정이었다—모든 일이 편안하게 풀릴 터였다. 그만큼은 이루어 낼 수 있으리라고 그는 생각했으니, 권위로 막을 힘도 없는 일에 맞서 봐야 소용없다고 어머니가 셈해 볼 것이라 믿었기 때문이다. 그러나 어머니가 최종 심판자라고, 모든 것이 어머니의 뜻에 걸려 있다고 말해 버린다면 그 허락은 아마 거절되고 말리라고 러프턴 경은 생각했다.\n\n“그래서, 어머니, 저에게 어떤 대답을 주실 작정이세요?” 그가 말했다. “제 마음은 아주 굳어졌어요. 그렇지 않았다면 어머니께 오지도 않았을 거예요. 이제 어머니는 시골집으로 내려가시겠지요. 저는 어머니가 루시를, 제가 약혼한 줄 아시는 처녀라면 누구에게든 몸소 그리 대하고 싶으실 그대로 대해 주시기를 바라요.”\n\n“하지만 너는 약혼한 것이 아니라고 하지 않았느냐.”\n\n“예, 아니에요. 그러나 저는 그 사람에게 청혼을 했고 거절당하지 않았어요. 그 사람은 저를—사랑한다고 털어놓았어요. 저에게가 아니라 오라비에게 말이지요. 이러한 형편에서 어머니가 제 뜻을 들어주실 것으로 믿어도 되겠어요?”\n\n그의 태도에는 어머니를 거의 겁나게 하는 무엇이, 그리고 들려주는 것보다 더 많은 것이 뒤에 있다고 여기게 하는 무엇이 있었다. 대체로 그의 태도는 트여 있고 부드럽고 꾸밈이 없었다. 그런데 지금 그는 말을 미리 준비해 두고 온 사람처럼, 고집스러울 뿐 아니라 매섭기로 마음을 굳힌 사람처럼 말했다.\n\n“너무나 뜻밖의 일을 당해서, 루도빅, 나는 도무지 대답을 해 줄 수가 없다. 내가 그런 혼인을 찬성하느냐고 묻는다면 찬성하지 않는다고 말해야겠다. 로바츠 양과 혼인하는 것은 너 자신을 내버리는 일이라고 나는 생각한다.”\n\n“그건 어머니가 그 사람을 모르시기 때문이에요.”\n\n“내가 너보다 그 아이를 더 잘 알고 있을 수도 있지 않겠느냐, 루도빅? 너는 그 아이와 희롱을 해 왔고—”\n\n“저는 그 말이 싫어요. 저에게는 늘 상스럽게만 들려요.”\n\n“네가 그편이 더 좋다면 그 아이에게 구애했다고 하겠다. 그런 형편에 놓인 신사들은 더러 홀리는 일이 있는 법이야.”\n\n“구애도 없이 사내가 처녀와 혼인하는 것을 어머니가 바라시지는 않겠지요. 사실은요, 어머니, 어머니의 취향과 제 취향이 꼭 같지가 않은 거예요. 어머니는 말 없는 미인을 좋아하시고 저는 말하는 미인을 좋아하지요. 그리고—”\n\n“너는 로바츠 양을 아름답다고 하느냐?”\n\n“예, 그렇게 말해요. 아주 아름다워요. 제가 우러러보는 아름다움을 그 사람이 지녔어요. 그럼 이만 가겠어요, 어머니. 떠나기 전에 다시 뵙지는 못할 거예요. 편지를 써도 소용없을 테지요. 떠나 있는 동안이 아주 짧고, 저희가 어디에 있을지도 잘 모르니까요. 돌아오는 대로 프램리로 내려가서 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 어머니께 듣겠어요. 제 바람은 다 말씀드렸으니, 어디까지 그 바람에 맞춰 주는 것이 옳겠는지는 어머니가 헤아려 보세요.” 그러고서 그는 어머니에게 입을 맞추고, 대답을 기다리지도 않은 채 자리를 떠났다.\n\n딱한 레이디 러프턴은 혼자 남겨지자 머리가 뱅뱅 도는 것 같았다. 이것이 제 온갖 뜻의, 아들을 향한 온갖 사랑의 끝이란 말인가? 이것이 로바츠네에게 베푼 온갖 친절의 결말이란 말인가? 그와 그의 누이를 프램리로 데려온 것이 바로 자기였음을 되짚으면서 그녀는 마크 로바츠가 거의 미웠다. 그가 지은 죄를 하나하나 떠올렸다. 교구를 비운 일, 개서럼 성에 다녀온 일, 팔리게 되어 있던 그 농장을 두고 벌인 거래, 사냥, 그리고 들은 바로는 옴니엄 쪽의 힘을 빌려 얻었다는 그 참사회원 자리를 받은 일. 이런 순간에 어떻게 그를 사랑할 수 있겠는가? 이어서 그녀는 그의 아내를 생각했다. 패니 로바츠가, 제 벗 패니가, 이런 혼인에 손을 보태다니, 그것을 막는 데 온 힘을 쓰지 않다니, 그렇게까지 저를 배신할 수가 있단 말인가? 그녀는 바로 이 문제를 두고 패니에게 말한 적이 있었다. 아들을 두고 두려워서가 아니라, 루시 같은 처녀와 러프턴 경 같은 사내 사이의 친밀함이 온당치 못하다는 두루뭉술한 생각에서였는데, 그때 패니도 그 말에 맞장구를 쳤다. 그 패니마저 원수로 여겨야 한다는 말인가?\n\n그러고서 차차 레이디 러프턴은 어떤 걸음을 밟는 것이 좋을지 헤아리기 시작했다. 첫째로, 당장 물러서서 이 혼인을 승낙해야 할까? 그녀에게 아주 분명한 것은 오직 하나, 아들과 영영 다투는 처지로 몰린다면 사는 것이 값없어진다는 것뿐이었다. 그런 일이 생기면 아마 그것이 그녀의 목숨을 앗아 갈 터였다. 다른 귀족 집안의 다툼에 관한 글을 읽을 때면—그런 다툼에 관한 이야기는 딱하게도 읽고 싶지 않은 독자의 눈에까지 더러 억지로 밀고 들어오는 법이다—그녀는 제 팔자가 남들 것과 같지 않음을 되새기며, 바리새인 같다 할 만한 마음으로 스스로를 껴안았다. 아비와 딸 사이, 어미와 아들 사이의 그런 다툼과 미움은 그녀 눈에는 관계된 사람 모두에게 부끄러운 일이었다. 그녀는 남편과 행복하게 살았고, 이웃과 편안하게 살았고, 세상 앞에 떳떳하게 살았고, 무엇보다도 자식들과 다정하게 살았다. 그녀는 어디에서나 러프턴 경을 거의 흠 없는 사람인 양 이야기했으며, 그렇게 이야기하면서 제 믿음을 어긴 적이 없었다. 이런 형편이라면 어떤 혼인이라도 다툼보다는 낫지 않겠는가?\n\n그러나 다시 생각하면, 그런 혼사로 하여 나날의 삶에서 그녀가 지녀 온 자랑이 얼마나 무너지겠는가! 그리고 다투지 않고도 그것을 막는 일이 제 힘 안에 있지 않겠는가? 루시 같은 계집애에게라면 혼인한 지 여섯 달도 못 되어 아들이 진력이 나고 말 것을 레이디 러프턴은 조금도 의심하지 않았으므로, 그토록 해로운 짓이 이루어지려는 것을 흩뜨리는 데에 그녀의 양심이 흔들릴 일은 없었다. 아들 자신도 이 일을 정해진 것으로 여기지 않는 것이 뚜렷했고, 어떤 식으로든 어머니의 승낙에 걸린 일로 여기는 것도 뚜렷했다. 이러니저러니 해도, 제 소임만을 생각하고 그 소임이 불러올지도 모르는 언짢은 결과는 생각하지 않는 편이 그녀에게—그들 모두에게—낫지 않겠는가? 그런 결합에 응하는 것이 그녀의 소임일 수는 없었다. 그러므로 그녀는 그것을 막으려 온 힘을 쓰기로 했다. 적어도 처음에는 그렇게 해 보아야 했다.\n\n그렇게 정하고 나서 그녀는 다음으로 어떻게 움직일지를 작정했다. 프램리에 이르는 대로 곧 루시 로바츠를 부르러 보내고, 러프턴 집안 같은 데에 억지로 끼어들려 하는 것이 그 처녀 쪽에서 얼마나 사악한 짓인지를 밝혀 주는 데에 온 말솜씨를—그리고 어쩌면 그녀를 그토록 남달라 보이게 하는 그 엄한 위엄도 얼마쯤—쓸 작정이었다. 그로부터 아무 행복도 나올 수 없다고, 불운으로 제 삶의 자리보다 높은 데 놓인 사람은 언제나 비참하다고 루시에게 설명하고, 요컨대 그런 자리에서 흔히 쓰이는 그 훌륭한 도덕상의 훈계를 다 끌어다 쓸 것이었다. 도덕은 어쩌면 헛일이 될지도 몰랐다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 제 위엄 있는 엄함에 많이 기대었다. 그리하여 그렇게 마음을 굳힌 뒤 그녀는 집으로 갈 길을 차렸다.\n\n프램리 목사관에서는 그 신사가 떠난 뒤 러프턴 경의 청혼에 관해 별로 말이 없었다. 적어도 루시 앞에서는 별로 없었다. 목사와 그 아내가 둘이서 그 이야기를 하는 것은 당연한 일이었으나, 루시가 그 일에 대해 말하는 것도, 루시에게 그 일을 말하는 것도 몇 마디에 지나지 않았다. 루시는 제 생각에, 어쩌면 제 바람에 홀로 맡겨져 있었다.\n\n그러고 나서 프램리에는 관심의 물길을 다른 데로 돌려놓는 다른 일들이 생겼다. 우선 사워비 씨가 원틀리의 용에 찾아온 일이 있었고, 그로 말미암아 마크 로바츠가 아내에게 사실을 털어놓은 일이 있었다. 그리고 그 뒷일이 아직 새로울 때, 주인의 안락을 크게 해치지 않고 살림에서 아낄 수 있는 자잘한 절약들을 두고 패니와 루시가 아직 마음을 정하지도 못했을 때, 호글스톡의 크롤리 부인이 열병에 걸렸다는 소식이 그들에게 닿았다. 이런 종류의 일로 이보다 더 끔찍한 것은 좀처럼 없을 터였다. 그 집안을 아는 사람들에게는, 그 담대한 머리가 단 하루라도 자리에 눕는다면 가장 흔한 살림의 필요조차 채워질 길이 없어 보였다. 게다가 딱한 크롤리 씨의 가난이 어떠했던가 하면, 병상에 마땅히 있어야 할 슬픈 물건들조차 남의 도움 없이는 좀처럼 갖출 수 없는 형편이었다.\n\n“제가 당장 건너가겠어요.” 패니가 말했다.\n\n“여보!” 남편이 말했다. “발진티푸스야. 당신은 먼저 아이들을 생각해야 해. 내가 가겠어.”\n\n“당신이 대체 무얼 할 수 있어요, 마크?” 아내가 말했다. “그런 자리에서 사내는 쓸모없는 것보다 오히려 더 나쁘지요. 게다가 병에도 훨씬 쉽게 걸려요.”\n\n“저는 아이도 없고 사내도 아니에요.” 루시가 웃으며 말했다. “두 가지가 다 면제되어서 고마운 일이지요. 제가 가겠어요. 그리고 돌아오면 아이들 곁에는 가지 않을게요.”\n\n그렇게 정해져서 루시는 조랑말 마차를 몰고 떠났다. 호글스톡의 병든 부인에게 필요하리라 여겨지는 것들을 목사관 곳간에서 챙겨 실었다. 그곳에 이르러 보니 문이 열려 있었고, 하녀가 나와 안으로 안내해 주기를 바랄 수도 없어서 그녀는 혼자 집 안으로 들어갔다. 거실에는 맏이인 그레이스 크롤리가 어머니의 의자에 얌전히 앉아 젖먹이를 안고 있었다. 그레이스 자신도 아직 어린아이였으나, 무슨 슬픔인지 잘 알고 있는 이런 자리에서 그 아이는 제 일을 열심히, 거의 엄숙하다 할 만큼 해내고 있었다. 여섯 살 난 사내아이인 남동생이 그 옆에 있었고, 그 아이는 또 다른 젖먹이를 맡고 있었다. 아이들은 거기 한데 모여 앉아, 조용하고 무겁고 말없이 저희끼리 저희를 돌보고 있었다. 아무도 저희를 돌보지 않도록 운명이 정해 놓았기 때문이었다.\n\n“우리 그레이스, 어머니는 어떠시니?” 루시가 그 아이에게 다가가 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n“불쌍한 엄마는 아주 많이 아파요.” 그레이스가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 아빠는 아주 슬퍼요.” 사내아이 보비가 말했다.\n\n“저는 아기 때문에 일어날 수가 없어요.” 그레이스가 말했다. “하지만 보비가 가서 아빠를 불러낼 수 있어요.”\n\n“내가 문을 두드리마.” 루시가 그렇게 말하고 침실 문 앞으로 걸어가 가만히 두드렸다. 세 번째로 그것을 되풀이하고서야 낮고 쉰 목소리가 그녀를 안으로 불렀고, 들어가 보니 크롤리 씨가 손에 책을 들고 침상 옆에 서 있었다. 그는 이렇게 들이닥친 것이 언짢다는 듯한 낯빛으로 거북하게 그녀를 바라보았고, 루시는 자기가 아내의 침상 옆에서 기도하던 그를 방해했음을 알아차렸다. 그러나 그는 방을 건너와 그녀와 악수를 하고, 여느 때와 같은 무겁고 엄숙한 목소리로 그녀의 물음에 답했다.\n\n“제 아내가 몹시 아픕니다.” 그가 말했다. “몹시 아픕니다. 하느님께서 우리를 무겁게 치셨으나 그분의 뜻이 이루어질 것입니다. 그러나 아내에게는 가시지 않는 것이 좋겠습니다, 로바츠 양. 발진티푸스입니다.”\n\n그러나 그 주의는 너무 늦었다. 루시는 이미 침상 옆에 가 있었고, 자기를 맞으려고 이불 위로 내밀어진 병든 여인의 손을 잡고 있었다. “로바츠 양.” 약한 목소리가 말했다. “참 고마운 일이에요. 하지만 여기서 아가씨를 보니 마음이 편치 않아요.”\n\n루시는 지체 없이 여러 가지 일을 제 손에 거두어들이고, 그 참혹한 살림에서 가장 절실한 것이 무엇인지 알아냈다. 정말로 참혹하기 그지없었던 것이다. 하나뿐인 하녀, 열여섯 살 난 처녀는 크롤리 부인이 열병으로 앓아눕는다는 것이 알려지자마자 어미가 데려가 버렸다. 그 딱한 어미도 그만한 몫은 해서, 아침저녁으로 몸소 내려와 한 시간쯤에 해낼 만한 일은 해 주기로 약속했다. 그러나 제 아이를 열병에 걸리도록 놓아둘 수는 없다고 했다. 그리하여 루시가 찾아온 이 무렵까지 간병인을 구하는 걸음은 아무것도 밟지 않은 채였다. 크롤리 씨가 그 자리의 소임을 제가 짊어지기로 작정해 버린 것이었다. 위생상의 조처라는 것을 도무지 알지 못하는 그는 무릎을 꿇고 기도에 몸을 던졌다. 기도가—참된 기도가—그 딱한 아내를 도울 수 있다면 그 도움만은 아내가 마음 놓고 믿어도 좋았다. 그러나 루시는 아내에게 다른 도움도 있어야 한다고 생각했다.\n\n“저희를 위해 무언가 해 주실 수 있다면.” 크롤리 부인이 말했다. “그 딱한 아이들을 위해 해 주세요.”\n\n“부인이 나으실 때까지 아이들을 모두 여기서 옮겨 놓겠어요.” 루시가 담대하게 말했다.\n\n“옮긴다니요!” 크롤리 씨가 말했다. 지금 이 순간에도—이런 궁지에 몰린 지금도—누가 제 짐의 한 자락이라도 덜어 준다는 생각에 그는 역겨움을 느꼈던 것이다.\n\n“네.” 루시가 말했다. “크롤리 부인이 방에서 나오실 수 있게 될 때까지 한두 주 아이들을 떼어 놓으시는 것이 나으리라고 저는 믿어요.”\n\n“하지만 어디로 간단 말입니까?” 그가 아주 침울하게 말했다.\n\n이 일에 대해서는 루시도 아직 아무 말을 할 수 없었다. 실은 프램리 목사관을 떠날 때 의논할 겨를이 없었던 것이다. 돌아가서 패니와 다 이야기해 보고, 아이들을 위험에서 빼내는 데 어떤 길이 가장 좋을지 알아볼 작정이었다. 아이들에게 열병의 독이 묻지 않았다는 확신이 서는 대로 모두 목사관에 거두는 것이 어째서 안 될 일이겠는가? 제대로 된 영국 부인이라면 병든 이웃을 위해 한 가지만 빼고 무엇이든 다 할 것이다. 그러나 어떤 이웃을 위해서도, 옮는 병을 제 집 아이 방의 울 안에 알고서 들이지는 않는다.\n\n루시가 젤리와 해열제를 내려놓는 동안 크롤리 씨는 씁쓸하게 이맛살을 찌푸리며 그것을 바라보았다. 그에게는 일이 이렇게까지 되고 말았다. 자선이라는 명목으로, 바로 제 눈앞에서, 먹을 것이 제 집으로 들어온 것이다. 그리고 마음속 깊은 데서 그는 그것을 가져왔다는 그 점 때문에 루시 로바츠가 싫었다. 아내의 형편이 달랐다면 그는 단지와 항아리를 조랑말 마차에 다시 실어 놓게 했겠지만 그럴 수 없었다. 아내가 그런 처지에 있으니 그것들을 거절하는 것도 야만스러운 짓이고, 거절하여 소동을 일으키는 것 또한 야만스러운 짓이었다. 그러나 들어오는 꾸러미마다 그의 자존심이 헐어 버린 등마루에 얹히는 짐이 되었고, 마침내 그 짐 전체가 거의 견딜 수 없는 것이 되었다. 아내는 앓는 중에도 이 모든 것을 보고 알아차려, 그를 편하게 해 주려고 힘없는 애를 조금 써 보기도 했다. 그러나 새로 얻은 권한을 쥔 루시는 매정했고, 닭 국물을 끓일 닭이 바로 그의 코앞에서 바구니 밖으로 꺼내어졌다.\n\n그러나 루시는 오래 머물지 않았다. 자기가 몸소 무엇을 해야 마땅한지 마음을 굳혔으므로 곧 프램리로 돌아갈 채비를 했다. “다시 오겠어요, 크롤리 씨.” 그녀가 말했다. “아마 오늘 저녁에요. 그리고 부인이 나으실 때까지 곁에 머물겠어요.” “간병인에게는 방이 필요하지 않아요.” 크롤리 씨가 내줄 침실이 없다는 말을 웅얼거리자 그녀는 말을 이었다. “부인 가까이에 무슨 자리라도 하나 마련하겠어요. 제가 얼마나 편안히 지내는지 보시게 될 거예요.” 그러고서 그녀는 조랑말 이륜마차에 올라 스스로 몰고 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n# 제35장. 코페투아 왕 이야기\n\n루시는 손수 말을 몰아 집으로 돌아오는 동안 마음을 일으켜 헤아려야 할 일이 많았다. 다시 가서 열병에 걸린 크롤리 부인을 간호하겠다는 것은 이미 굳게 정한 바였다. 그만한 일을 제 몸에 지우고 또 끝까지 해낼 수 있다고 믿을 만큼은 제 뜻대로 움직일 수 있는 처지였다. 그러나 아이들에 관한 약속은 어떻게 지켜야 하나? 아이들을 맡길 만한 농가라든가 아이들을 위해 빌릴 만한 오두막을 두고 스무 가지 계획이 머릿속을 지나갔다. 그러나 그 모두가 돈이 없다는 벽에 걸렸다. 게다가 지금으로서는 목사관에 사는 사람이라면 하나같이 지독한 절약을 맹세한 처지가 아닌가? 조랑말 마차를 이렇게 쓰는 것만 해도 지금보다 덜 급한 사정이었다면 어떤 경우에도 용납될 수 없는 일이었다. 마차는, 딱한 퍽까지도 팔기로 이미 정해져 있었으니까. 그러나 아이들에 관한 약속은 제 입으로 한 것이었고, 제 주머니의 돈이 몹시 얄팍하기는 하나 그 약속은 지켜야 했다.\n\n목사관에 이르렀을 때 루시의 머리는 물론 제 계획으로 가득했으나, 제가 없는 동안에 또 다른 관심거리가 생겨 있어서 올케의 온 마음을 지금의 계획에 붙들어 둘 수가 없었다. 레이디 러프턴이 그날 돌아왔고, 돌아오는 길로 곧 루시 로바츠 양에게 부치는 쪽지를 목사관으로 올려 보냈던 것이다. 루시가 조랑말 마차에서 내릴 때 그 쪽지는 패니의 손에 들려 있었다. 쪽지를 가져온 하인이 답을 청했으므로, 로바츠 양은 집에 없으니 돌아오면 몸소 답을 보내리라는 말이 구두로 전해졌다. 응접실에서 패니에게서 그 쪽지를 받아 들 때 루시의 얼굴에 핏기가 몰리고 손이 떨렸다는 것은 부인할 수 없다. 그 쪽지에 무엇이 적혀 있느냐에 이 세상 모든 것이 걸려 있을 수도 있었다. 그런데도 루시는 그것을 곧 뜯지 않고 손에 든 채 서 있었고, 패니가 그 이야기를 재촉해도 여전히 크롤리 부인 이야기로 말머리를 되돌리려 애썼다.\n\n그러면서도 루시의 마음은 편지에 붙어 있었고, 필체에서, 나아가 겉봉에 적힌 낱말에서까지 이미 흉한 조짐을 읽어 냈다. 레이디 러프턴이 호의를 품을 작정이었다면 세례명은 빼고 그냥 로바츠 양이라고만 적었을 것이다 — 적어도 루시는 그렇게 따졌다. 이런 일에서 누구나 그러하듯 아주 무의식중에. 사람은 제 한 생에서 짓는 결론의 절반을, 그 전제가 제 머릿속을 지나갔다는 것조차 또렷이 알지 못한 채 짓는다.\n\n마크가 나가 있어서 두 사람은 이제 단둘이었다.\n\n“그분 편지를 열어 보지 않겠어요?” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“네, 곧 열어 볼게요. 그런데 패니, 크롤리 부인 이야기를 먼저 해야 해요. 나는 오늘 저녁에 그리로 다시 가서 거기 머물러야 해요. 그렇게 하겠다고 약속했으니 반드시 약속을 지킬 거예요. 아이들을 그 집에서 빼내 오겠다는 약속도 했으니 그 일도 정해 두어야 해요. 그 댁 부인 형편이 참혹해요. 부인을 살펴 줄 사람이 크롤리 씨밖에 없고, 아이들은 아주 저희끼리 내버려져 있어요.”\n\n“거기 머무르러 다시 간다는 말이에요?”\n\n“네, 그래요. 그렇게 하겠다고 또렷이 약속했어요. 그리고 아이들 말인데, 패니가 아이들 일을 좀 어떻게 해 줄 수 없을까요 — 집 안은 아니라도, 적어도 처음에는 집 안이 아니어도 좋으니?” 그러면서도 이렇게 말하며 크롤리네를 위해 애원하는 내내, 루시는 손가락 사이에 든 그 편지에 무엇이 적혀 있을까를 그려 보고 있었다.\n\n“그렇게나 몹시 앓아요?” 로바츠 부인이 물었다.\n\n“얼마나 앓는지는 나도 뭐라 말할 수 없어요. 다만 이것만은 확실해요, 발진티푸스에 걸렸어요. 실버브리지에서 의사인지 의사 조수인지가 와 보긴 했지만, 내가 보기에는 더 나은 진찰을 몹시 바라는 형편이에요.”\n\n“그런데 루시, 편지를 읽지 않을 거예요? 그렇게 아무렇지도 않은 듯이 있으니 나는 놀랍기만 해요.”\n\n루시는 아무렇지도 않은 것과는 거리가 멀었고, 이제 봉투를 뜯기 시작했다. 쪽지는 아주 짧았고 이러한 말로 되어 있었다 —\n\n친애하는 로바츠 양,—꼭 만나 보고 싶은 일이 있으니 이곳 프램리 코트로 나에게 건너와 준다면 참으로 고맙겠어요. 이렇게 무례를 무릅쓰는 것은 용서해 주세요. 다만 목사관 쪽에서 만나기보다 이곳에서 만나는 편이 우리 둘 다에게 나으리라는 것은 로바츠 양도 아마 느낄 거예요. 그럼 이만 줄입니다.\n\n“자, 이제 나는 걸려들었어요.” 루시가 쪽지를 로바츠 부인에게 건네며 말했다. “딱한 처녀가 여태 들어 본 적 없는 말을 실컷 들어야 하겠지요. 그런데 내가 한 일을 생각하면 억울해요.”\n\n“그래요, 그리고 루시가 하지 않은 일도요.”\n\n“바로 그거예요. 내가 하지 않은 일도. 하지만 가야 하겠지요.” 그러고는 아까 풀어 놓았던 보닛 끈을 다시 매기 시작했다.\n\n“지금 곧 건너간다는 말이에요?”\n\n“네, 지금 곧. 왜 안 되나요? 치러 버리는 게 나아요. 그러고 나서 크롤리네로 갈 수 있고요. 그런데 패니, 딱한 것은 아직 한마디도 나오지 않았는데 나는 그것을 벌써 다 알고 있다는 거예요. 그러니 그걸 굳이 견뎌 낸다고 무슨 좋은 일이 있겠어요? 코페투아 왕이 거지의 딸과 혼인하려 했을 때 생겨난 세속의 불편들을 그분이 나에게 어떤 어조로 풀어 놓으실지 그려지지 않아요? 그리젤다가 무슨 일을 겪었는지를 — 부주교의 딸이 아니라 그 다른 그리젤다 말이에요 — 어떻게 풀어 놓으실지도요?”\n\n“하지만 그 그리젤다는 다 잘되지 않았나요.”\n\n“그랬지요. 하지만 나는 그리젤다가 아니고, 그분은 나라면 틀림없이 다 잘못될 것이라고 풀어 놓으실 거예요. 그런데 그걸 내가 미리 다 알고 있으니 무슨 소용이 있겠어요? 나는 이미 코페투아 왕더러 제 몸과 왕홀을 딴 데로 가져가시라고 청하지 않았나요?”\n\n그러고 나서 루시는 크롤리네 아이들 이야기를 한두 마디 더 하고 오후에 퍽과 조랑말 마차를 쓰게 해 준다는 약속을 받아 낸 뒤에 길을 나섰다. 퍽이 프램리로 돌아오는 길에 네 아이를 모두 데리고 오는 것으로 거의 이야기가 되었으나, 이 일은 마크와 의논해야 했다. 지금 세운 계획은 집 바깥에 아이들 방을 마련하는 것이었다. 한때 우유 방이던 곳으로, 지금은 마부와 그 아내가 쓰고 있었다. 그리고 전염될 위험이 없다는 것이 분명해지는 대로 아이들을 집 안으로 들이는 것이었다. 그러나 이 모두가 더 헤아려 볼 일이었다.\n\n패니는 루시가 온다는 것을 미리 알리는 뜻으로 레이디 러프턴에게 답장을 보내기를 바랐으나, 루시는 그 제안에 거의 답하지도 않고 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n“그런 격식을 그렇게 많이 차려서 무슨 소용이 있어요?” 루시가 말했다. “그분이 집에 계신 걸 나는 알아요. 안 계시더라도 다녀오는 데 십 분 잃을 뿐이지요.” 그러고는 갔다. 프램리 코트 저택 문 앞에 이르니 마님은 집에 있었다. 그렇다는 말을 듣자 심장이 목구멍까지 치밀어 올랐고, 그러고 나서 이 분이 채 지나기 전에 루시는 위층 그 작은 방에 있는 제 몸을 발견했다. 그 작은 방에서 우리는 전에 한 번 함께 있어 보았다 — 그대와 나, 아 독자여. 그러나 루시는 그 신성한 구역에 한 번도 들어와 본 적이 없었다. 레이디 러프턴이 장부와 서류를 들여다볼 때면 늘 앉는, 밑을 등나무로 짠 그 안락의자에 꼿꼿이 앉아 있는 모습을 처음 보는 사람의 마음에 외경을 불러일으키도록 꾸며진 무엇인가가 그 방의 공기에는 있었다. 그리고 루시를 이 방에서 맞기로 정할 때 마님은 그것을 알고 있었다. 그러나 거기에는 또 하나의 안락의자, 난롯가에 놓인 편안하고 푹신한 의자도 있었으니, 어느 오후에 레이디 러프턴이 그 의자에서 졸고 있는 것을 본 적이 있는 사람에게는 이 외경도 얼마쯤 흩어졌을지 모른다.\n\n“로바츠 양.” 마님은 의자에서 일어서지는 않고 찾아온 손에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. “여기까지 나에게 건너와 주어 참 고마워요. 내가 무슨 이야기를 하려는지는 로바츠 양도 틀림없이 알고 있겠지요. 그리고 목사관 쪽에서 만나기보다 여기서 만나는 편이 낫다는 데에는 나와 뜻이 같을 거예요.”\n\n이에 대해 루시는 그저 머리를 숙였고, 자기를 위해 놓여 있던 의자에 앉았다.\n\n“내 아들이,” 마님이 말을 이었다. “그 일을 두고 나에게 이야기를 했어요 — 내가 알기로는, 로바츠 양, 로바츠 양과 아들 사이에 약조 같은 것은 없었지요?”\n\n“전혀 없었습니다.” 루시가 말했다. “그분이 저에게 청혼을 하셨고 저는 거절했습니다.” 이 말을 루시는 아주 날카롭게 내뱉었다. 사정이 요구하는 것보다 훨씬 날카로웠음은 틀림없다. 그리고 무례하기도 하고 슬기롭지도 못한 무뚝뚝함이 실려 있었다. 그러나 그 순간 루시가 생각하고 있던 것은 레이디 러프턴에 대한 제 처지였고 — 러프턴 경에 대한 처지가 아니었다. 또 그 부인에 대한 제 감정이었고 — 그 신사에 대한 감정이 아니었다.\n\n“아.” 레이디 러프턴은 말을 전하는 태도에 조금 놀라 말했다. “그러면 로바츠 양과 내 아들 사이에 지금은 아무것도 없다고 — 이 일이 온통 끝났다고 알아 두어도 되겠군요?”\n\n“그것은 전적으로 마님께 달려 있습니다.”\n\n“나에게? 그런가요?”\n\n“아드님이 마님께 무슨 말씀을 드렸는지는 저도 모릅니다, 레이디 러프턴. 저로서는 이 일에서 마님께 무엇을 숨길 마음이 조금도 없습니다. 그리고 그분이 마님께 이 일을 말씀드렸다면 그분의 뜻도 그러하리라고 생각합니다. 그분이 이 일을 마님께 말씀드렸다고 짚어도 틀리지 않겠습니까?”\n\n“그래요, 말했어요. 바로 그래서 내가 무례를 무릅쓰고 로바츠 양을 불렀지요.”\n\n“그러면 그분이 무슨 말씀을 드렸는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까? 물론 저에 관한 것을 여쭙는 것입니다.” 루시가 말했다.\n\n레이디 러프턴은 이 물음에 답하기 전에, 이 젊은 아가씨가 이 대화에서 주도권을 너무 많이 쥐고 있다는 것, 실은 판을 제 방식대로 놀리고 있다는 것을 곰곰 생각하기 시작했다. 그것은 레이디 러프턴이 루시를 부르게 된 속뜻과는 조금도 맞지 않는 일이었다.\n\n“아들은 로바츠 양에게 혼인하자는 청을 했다고 나에게 말했어요.” 레이디 러프턴이 답했다. “그 어미로서 나에게는 물론 아주 중대한 일이지요. 그래서 로바츠 양을 만나 보고, 로바츠 양 자신의 분별과 판단과 높은 마음에 호소하는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했어요. 물론 로바츠 양도 알고 있겠지만—”\n\n루시가 로바츠 부인에게 미리 짐작해 말한 그대로, 코페투아 왕과 그리젤다를 예로 들어 가며 펼칠 훈계가 이제 오고 있었다. 그러나 루시는 잠시 그것을 막아 내는 데 성공했다.\n\n“그러면 러프턴 경이 제 대답이 무엇이었는지도 마님께 말씀드렸습니까?”\n\n“말로 하지는 않았어요. 하지만 로바츠 양 스스로 지금 거절했다고 말하고 있지요. 그러니 나는 로바츠 양의 그 훌륭한—”\n\n“잠깐만 기다려 주십시오, 레이디 러프턴. 아드님은 저에게 청혼을 하셨습니다. 목사관에 올라와 몸소 저에게 하셨고 저는 그때 그분을 거절했습니다 — 이제 와 생각하면 어리석은 짓이었습니다. 저는 그분을 몹시 사랑하니까요. 그러나 굳이 설명하지 않아도 될 여러 감정이 섞여서 그리했습니다. 그 가운데 가장 두드러진 것은 마님의 노여움에 대한 두려움이었음이 틀림없습니다. 그러고 나서 그분은 다시 오셨습니다. 저에게가 아니라 제 오라비에게 오셔서 그에게 청을 밀어붙이셨습니다. 그분의 처신보다 저에게 더 다정하고, 더 고귀하고, 더 사랑에 넘치고, 더 후한 것은 있을 수 없습니다. 처음, 그분이 저에게 직접 말씀하실 때에는 하시는 말씀을 모두 생각 없이 이끌려 하시는 것이라고 여겼습니다. 그분 자신은 그 사랑을 믿는 것이 보였으나 저는 그 사랑을 믿지 않았습니다. 그러나 그분이 다시 — 제 오라비에게 — 오셔서 그에게 청혼을 하셨을 때에는 믿지 않을 수 없었습니다. 마님께서 제 말을 알아들으실지 모르겠습니다. 그러나 저와 같은 자리에 놓인 처녀는, 어쩌면 그 자리의 충동으로 저에게 직접 건네지는 사랑의 말보다 그렇게 건네지는 사랑의 표시에서 열 배나 더한 확신을 느낍니다. 그리고 마님께서 기억해 주셔야 할 것이 있습니다. 저는 — 저 자신은 — 처음부터 그분을 사랑했습니다. 그분을 알면서도 사랑하지 않을 수 있으리라고 생각할 만큼 저는 어리석었습니다.”\n\n“나는 그 모든 일이 벌어지는 것을 다 보고 있었어요.” 레이디 러프턴이 어딘가 슬기로운 척하는 태로 말했다. “그리고 때를 놓치기 전에 그것을 그치게 하리라 바라며 손을 썼지요.”\n\n“모두가 보고 있었습니다. 당연한 일이었지요.” 루시가 마님의 슬기를 한 방에 무너뜨리며 말했다. “그래요, 저는 그럴 뜻이 없었으면서도 그분을 사랑하게 되었습니다. 그리고 온 마음으로 그분을 사랑합니다. 사랑하지 않는다고 애써 생각해 보아도 소용이 없습니다. 저는 내일이라도 그분과 제단 앞에 서서 손을 내어 드릴 수 있고, 여자가 마땅히 해야 할 대로 그분에게 제 도리를 다하고 있다고 느낄 수 있습니다. 그리고 이제 그분은 마님께 그 사랑을 말씀드렸고, 저는 제 사랑을 믿는 것처럼 그 사랑을 믿습니다—” 그러고는 잠시 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“하지만 이봐요, 로바츠 양—” 레이디 러프턴이 입을 열었다.\n\n그러나 루시는 이제 스스로 힘이 오른 상태에 이르러 있었고, 마님이 자기 말을 가로막게 두지 않았다.\n\n“죄송합니다, 레이디 러프턴. 곧 마치고 나서 마님 말씀을 듣겠습니다. 그리하여 제 오라비가 저에게 왔습니다. 이 청을 밀어붙이지도 않고, 그런 혼인을 바란다는 말도 하지 않고, 다만 제가 스스로 판단하도록 두면서 이튿날 아침에 아드님을 다시 만나 보라고 권했습니다. 만일 만났다면 저는 그분을 받아들이지 않을 수 없었을 것입니다. 헤아려 보십시오, 레이디 러프턴. 제 마음속에서 이미 그분의 사랑을 받아들여 놓고서 제가 어떻게 그분을 받아들이지 않을 수 있었겠습니까?”\n\n“그래서요?” 레이디 러프턴은 이제 자기 말을 끼워 넣고 싶지 않아 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“저는 그분을 만나지 않았습니다 — 만나기를 마다했습니다 — 제가 겁쟁이였기 때문입니다. 아드님의 아내로 이 집에 들어와 아드님 어머니의 차가운 눈길을 받는 것을 저는 견딜 수 없었습니다. 그분을 그토록 사랑했고, 지금도 그토록 사랑하고, 그분이 여기까지 내려와 저에게 되풀이해 주신 그 후한 청을 그토록 값지게 여기지만, 마님의 업신여김의 표적이 되면서 그분과 함께 살 수는 없었습니다. 그래서 저는 마님께서 저를 청해 주실 때 그분을 맞겠고 그 전에는 아니라고 전갈을 보냈습니다.”\n\n그리하여 제 뜻을 다 변호하고 — 스스로 믿기로는 사랑하는 이의 뜻까지 함께 변호하고 — 루시는 말을 마쳤고, 코페투아 왕 이야기를 들을 준비를 했다.\n\n그러나 레이디 러프턴은 말을 꺼내기가 여간 어렵지 않았다. 첫째로 마님은 결코 매정하거나 저만 아는 여인이 아니었다. 그리고 제 아들이 걸린 일이 아니었다면, 또 아들에게서 제게로 비쳐 오는 그 온갖 영화가 걸린 일이 아니었다면 마님의 마음은 루시 로바츠 쪽에 갔을 것이다. 그런 처지에서도 마님은 루시를 딱하게 여기고 찬탄했고, 어느 정도는 좋아하기까지 했다. 무엇이 제 아들의 사랑을 불러왔는지도 알아지기 시작했고, 몇 가지 불행한 딸린 사정만 없다면 눈앞의 이 처녀가 걸맞은 레이디 러프턴이 될 수도 있었으리라는 느낌이 들었다. 거기 앉아 이야기하는 동안 루시는 마님의 눈에 더 커졌고, 아가씨 티 나는 그 대수롭지 않음 — 마님이 제 나름으로 늘 루시에게 돌려 온 그 사회적 무게의 결여 — 을 많이 잃었다. 이렇게 나서서 제 처지를 밝힐 수 있는 처녀라면 앞으로 어느 때에도 나서서 제 처지를, 또 어쩌면 다른 몇 사람의 처지까지 밝힐 수 있을 것이었다.\n\n그러나 이런 이유 가운데 어느 것으로도, 또 그 모두로도 레이디 러프턴은 물러설 생각을 하지 않았다. 이 혼인을 이루거나 깨뜨릴 힘이 지극히 마땅하게도 마님의 손에 놓여 있었고, 그 힘은 쓸 수 있는 한 가장 잘 써서 아들에게 이롭게 해야 할 것이었다. 루시를 아무리 찬탄한다 해도 그 찬탄에 아들을 바칠 수는 없었다. 불행한 딸린 사정들은 여전히 그대로 남아 있었고, 마님의 생각으로는 그런 혼인을 온당치 못한 것으로 만들 만큼 힘이 셌다. 루시는 어느 신사의 누이였는데, 그 신사는 프램리 교구 성직자라는 남다른 자리 때문에 프램리 주인의 처남이 되기에는 걸맞지 않았다. 레이디 러프턴만큼 성직자를 좋아하는 사람도 없고 그만큼 성직자들과 다정하게 어울려 살기를 기꺼워하는 사람도 없었으나, 마님은 제 교구의 — 또는 아들의 교구의 — 성직자란 제 살림의 한 부분, 제게 딸린 재산의 한 부분 — 또는 아들의 것의 한 부분 — 이라는 느낌을 떨쳐 낼 수 없었고, 러프턴 경이 제 집에 — 딸려 사는 사람들 가운데서 혼인하는 것이 좋을 수는 없다고 느꼈다. 레이디 러프턴이라면 그 낱말을 입에 담지는 않았겠지만 마음속으로는 분명 그렇게 생각했다. 그리고 또 루시의 교육이 그토록 모자랐다. 어릴 적에 루시 곁에는 — 무어라 말해야 좋을까, 레이디 러프턴을 실제보다 더 세속적인 사람으로 보이게 하지 않으면서? — 그런 것의 방식에 익은 사람이 아무도 없었다. 이 점에서 루시에게 모자란 것은 말로 딱히 집어낼 수 없는 것이나, 바로 방금 루시가 제 사정을 늘어놓은 그 방식에서 그대로 드러났다. 루시는 재주와 좋은 성미와 건전한 판단을 보였다. 그러나 조용함이 없었고 가라앉음이 없었다. 레이디 러프턴이 젊은 여인에게서 가장 찬탄하는 힘의 종류는 아름답고 위엄 있는 말없음에 딸린 「비스 이네르티아이」였다. 딱한 루시에게는 그것이 조금도 없었다. 그리고 또 루시에게는 재산이 없었으니 그것은 작은 흠이기는 하나 흠이었다. 또 상류의 뜻으로 말하는 태생이 없었으니 그것은 더 큰 흠이었다. 그리고 사랑을 고백할 때 눈이 반짝이기는 했으나, 레이디 러프턴은 루시가 뚜렷한 미모를 지녔다고 인정할 마음은 없었다. 레이디 러프턴이 이 혼사는 깨뜨려야 한다고 여전히 마음먹게 한 불행한 딸린 사정이란 이러한 것들이었다.\n\n그러나 이 연극에서 제가 맡은 몫을 해내기는 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 어려웠고, 마님은 일이 분 동안 잠자코 앉아 있어야 했다. 그동안에도 로바츠 양은 더 말을 하려 들지 않았다.\n\n“이 일 전체에서 로바츠 양이 보여 준 훌륭한 분별에,” 레이디 러프턴이 마침내 말했다. “나는 크게 감동했어요. 그리고 이 말은 하게 해 주어야 해요, 로바츠 양, 나는 이제 런던을 떠날 때 품고 있던 것과는 아주 다른 마음으로 로바츠 양을 보고 있어요.” 이 말에 루시는 머리를 조금, 그러나 아주 딱딱하게 숙였다. 지금 드러난 칭송보다 앞서 은근히 담겨 있던 책잡음을 오히려 알아들었다는 뜻이었다.\n\n“그러나 내 마음은,” 레이디 러프턴이 말을 이었다. “이 일에서 내 가장 강한 마음은 어미의 것이어야 해요. 그런 혼인이 정말로 이루어졌을 때 내가 어떻게 처신할지는 지금 헤아릴 일이 아니지요. 하지만 그런 혼인은 아주 — 아주 잘못 짚은 것이라고 여긴다는 것은 털어놓아야겠어요. 러프턴 경보다 마음이 나은 젊은이는 없고, 그보다 지킬 바가 나은 이도, 제가 한 말을 그보다 더 깊이 여기는 이도 없어요. 하지만 그 아이는 제 앞날을 급하게 내다보는 일이라면 바로 잘못 짚기 마련인 사람이에요. 로바츠 양과 그 아이가 부부가 된다면, 그런 혼인은 그 아이의 행복에도 로바츠 양의 행복에도 이어지지 않을 거예요.”\n\n이제 그 훈계 전체가 오고 있는 것이 뻔했다. 그리고 루시는 제 약함을 이미 드러내 놓고 결정의 힘을 온통 레이디 러프턴의 손에 넘겨 놓았으니, 이것을 참고 있을 까닭이 없다고 보았다.\n\n“그 일을 두고는 다툴 필요가 없습니다, 레이디 러프턴.” 루시가 말했다. “제가 어떤 사정에서라야 아드님과 혼인하겠는지는 이미 말씀드렸습니다. 그리고 적어도 마님께서는 안전하십니다.”\n\n“아니에요, 나는 다툴 생각은 없었어요.” 레이디 러프턴이 거의 겸손하게 답했다. “다만 로바츠 양에게 나를 변명하고 싶었어요. 내가 승낙을 하지 않는 것을 매정하다고 여기지 않도록 말이지요. 나는 아들을 위해 가장 좋은 일을 하고 있다는 것을 로바츠 양이 믿게 하고 싶었어요.”\n\n“그리 여기고 계신 것은 저도 잘 알고 있으니 변명은 필요하지 않습니다.”\n\n“그래요, 바로 그렇지요. 물론 이는 견해의 문제이고, 나는 그리 여기고 있어요. 이 혼인이 두 사람 가운데 어느 쪽도 행복하게 해 주리라고는 믿을 수 없으니, 승낙한다고 말하는 것은 아주 잘못된 일이 될 거예요.”\n\n“그러면, 레이디 러프턴,” 루시가 의자에서 일어서며 말했다. “이제 두 사람 다 해야 할 말은 한 것 같으니 저는 이만 물러가겠습니다.”\n\n“안녕히 가요, 로바츠 양. 이 일에서 로바츠 양의 처신을 내가 얼마나 높이 보고 있는지 알아듣게 할 수 있었으면 좋겠어요. 어떤 칭찬으로도 모자라니, 로바츠 양의 집안 사람들에게 이 일을 이야기할 때에도 그렇게 말하기를 망설이지 않겠어요.” 이 말은 루시에게 여간 거북하지 않았다. 레이디 러프턴이 이 일을 두고 제 집안 사람들에게 어떤 칭찬을 하든 루시는 조금도 마음에 두지 않았으니까. “그리고 부디,” 레이디 러프턴이 말을 이었다. “로바츠 부인에게 내 사랑을 잘 전해 주고, 아주 곧 여기서 만나기를 바란다고 말해 주어요. 로바츠 씨도요. 다 함께 저녁을 먹을 날을 정해 두고 싶지만, 아마 내가 먼저 패니와 조금 이야기를 나누는 편이 나을 거예요.”\n\n루시는 무언가 중얼거렸는데, 그런 만찬 자리라면 자기를 넣을 작정으로는 차리지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 뜻이었다. 그러고는 물러 나왔다. 이 만남에서 루시가 단연 이겼고, 레이디 러프턴이 손을 잡게 내어 주는 동안 그 마음에는 그것을 아는 느낌이 있었다. 미리 준비해 둔 설교를 꺼내려는 시도가 있을 때마다 루시는 그때마다 적수의 말을 잘라 세웠고, 만나 있는 동안 마님이 겨우 한 마디 할 때 세 마디쯤은 했다. 그러나 그러면서도 집으로 걸어 돌아가는 동안 그 가슴에는 쓰디쓴 낙심이 있었고, 제 불행을 제가 지었다는 느낌도 있었다. 무엇 때문에 그렇게 낭만적이고 의협스럽고 제 몸을 바치려 들었던가. 제 낭만과 의협이 제게도 해가 되고 그분에게도 해가 되었으니 — 제 몸만이 아니라 그분까지 바친 것이었으니? 무엇 때문에 그렇게 레이디 러프턴의 손에 판을 놀려 주려고 애를 썼던가? 신사의 어머니가 그 혼인에 승낙하는 편이 아니라면 처녀는 그 신사의 손을 마다해야 한다는 것을, 세상의 일반 규범으로서 옳다고 여겼기 때문은 아니었다. 그런 교리라면 루시는 터무니없다고 여겼을 것이다. 처녀는 제 집안만 살피면 되고 그 이상은 아니라고 말했을 것이다. 루시를 움직인 것은 덕이 아니라 비겁이었고, 제 처신에 나무랄 데가 없었다는 자각에서 불행 속에 있는 우리에게 찾아오는 그 위로도 루시에게는 없었다. 레이디 러프턴은 루시의 마음에 외경을 불어넣었고, 제 안의 그런 느낌이란 제가 지녔다고 여기고 싶은 그 기개에는 천하고 비루하고 어울리지 않는 것이었다. 그것이 루시가 스스로에게 들이댄 죄목이었고, 그 죄목이 이 만남의 결말을 두고 어떠한 승리감도 느끼지 못하게 막았다.\n\n목사관에 이르니 마크가 있었고, 두 사람은 물론 루시를 기다리고 있었다. “그래,” 루시가 짧고 급한 투로 말했다. “퍽은 다시 준비됐어요? 나는 허비할 시간이 없어요. 가서 몇 가지 챙겨야 해요. 아이들 일은 정했어요, 패니?”\n\n“네, 곧 말해 줄게요. 그런데 레이디 러프턴은 만났어요?”\n\n“만났느냐고요? 아, 그럼요, 물론 만났지요. 그분이 나를 부르지 않았나요? 그러니 내가 그분 말을 어긴다는 것은 있을 수 있는 일이 아니었지요.”\n\n“그래서 뭐라고 하시더냐?”\n\n“마크는 어쩌면 그렇게 풋내기예요. 풋내기인 것만이 아니라 무례하기까지 해요, 내 망신 이야기를 나더러 되풀이하게 하니. 물론 그분은 내가 그분의 아들이신 나리와 혼인하는 것을 바라지 않는다고 말씀하셨고, 물론 나는 그러시다면 그런 일은 생각도 하지 않겠다고 말씀드렸지요.”\n\n“루시, 나는 루시를 이해할 수가 없어요.” 패니가 아주 무겁게 말했다. “루시가 이 일에 깊은 마음을 두고 있는지 아닌지 이따금 의심스러워요. 마음을 두고 있다면 어떻게 이 일을 두고 농을 할 수가 있어요?”\n\n“그래요, 이상한 일이에요. 나도 이따금 내가 마음을 두고 있는지 의심스러워요. 나는 창백해져야 하겠지요? 그리고 아주 야위어서 차츰 미쳐 가야 하겠지요? 나는 그런 짓을 할 생각이 조금도 없으니, 이 일은 더 눈여겨볼 값도 없어요.”\n\n“그런데 그분이 너에게 예의는 지키셨니, 루시?” 마크가 물었다. “태도가 예의 있었느냐는 말이다.”\n\n“아, 남다르게 그러셨지요. 믿기 어려우실 테지만 실제로 나에게 저녁을 먹으러 오라고 하셨어요. 그분은 좋은 기분을 보이고 싶을 때면 늘 그러시잖아요. 마크가 다리가 부러지고 그분이 그것을 딱하게 여기고 싶으면, 그분은 마크에게 저녁을 먹으러 오라고 하실 거예요.”\n\n“그분은 다정하게 하려던 것이겠지요.” 패니가 말했다. 패니는 오랜 벗을 놓을 마음이 없었으나, 싸울 일이 생긴다면 루시를 위해 싸울 준비는 아주 되어 있었다.\n\n“루시가 하도 비뚤어져서,” 마크가 말했다. “정말 무슨 일이 있었는지 알아낼 도리가 없군.”\n\n“참말이지, 그러면 마크도 내가 말해 줄 수 있는 만큼은 다 알고 있는 거예요. 그분은 러프턴 경이 나에게 청혼을 했느냐고 물으셨어요. 나는 그렇다고 했어요. 그분은 다음으로 그것을 받아들일 생각이냐고 물으셨어요. 그분의 승낙 없이는 아니라고 나는 말했어요. 그러고 나서 그분은 우리 모두를 저녁 먹으러 오라고 하셨어요. 있었던 일은 바로 그것뿐이고, 나는 내가 비뚤어진 데가 조금도 없다고 봐요.” 그러고 나서 루시는 의자에 몸을 던졌고, 마크와 패니는 서서 서로 마주 보았다.\n\n“마크,” 잠시 뒤에 루시가 말했다. “나에게 모질게 하지 마요. 나는 우리 모두를 위해서 이 일을 될 수 있는 대로 작게 여기는 거예요. 이러는 게 나아요, 패니, 내가 병든 소처럼 신음하며 돌아다니는 것보다는.” 그러자 두 사람이 루시를 보았고, 벌써 눈에서 눈물이 넘쳐 나오고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n“우리 루시, 우리 귀한 루시.” 패니가 곧 루시 앞에 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. “다시는 루시에게 모질게 굴지 않을게요.” 그러고 나서 두 사람은 함께 한참을 울었다.\n\n# 제36장. 호글스톡에서 아이를 채어 오다\n\n그러나 그 한바탕 큰 울음은 오래 걸리지 않았고, 루시는 곧 다시 조랑말 마차에 올라 있었다. 이번에는 오라비가 스스로 나서서 마차를 몰아 주기로 했고, 돌아올 때에는 크롤리네 아이들을 모두 데려오기로 하는 것이 이제 두 사람 사이의 양해였다. 모든 것이 다 마련되어 있었다. 마부와 그 아내는 본채로 들이고, 마당 건너 그들이 늘 쓰던 큰 침실은 노란 깃발을 내려도 좋을 만큼 안전해질 때까지 격리 병실로 바꾸어 쓸 작정이었다. 두 사람이 호글스톡으로 가는 길을 절반쯤 왔을 때 말을 탄 사람 하나가 뒤에서 따라붙었는데, 그가 옆으로 나란히 올라섰을 때 로바츠 씨는 그가 애러빈 박사, 곧 바체스터의 수석 사제이며 자기가 속한 성당 참사회의 수장임을 알아보았다. 수석 사제 또한 호글스톡으로 가는 길임이 곧 드러났다. 거기 있는 벗들에게 닥친 불행을 전해 들은 것이었다. 그는 소식이 자기에게 이르자마자, 어떻게 하면 도움을 가장 잘 베풀 수 있을지 알아보려고 곧바로 길을 나섰다고 말했다. 그러기 위하여 사십 마일에 가까운 말길을 감행한 것이었고, 집에 다시 이르는 것은 자정 무렵이나 되어야 할 듯하다고 이야기했다.\n\n“프램리를 지나가시지요?” 로바츠가 말했다.\n\n“그래요, 지나갑니다.” 수석 사제가 말했다.\n\n“그러시면 돌아가시는 길에 저희 집에서 식사를 하셔야 하겠습니다. 사제님도, 사제님의 말도요. 말이 그만큼 중요할 테니까요.” 이것이 제대로 정해지고, 수석 사제와 루시 사이에 마땅한 소개의 절차가 이루어진 뒤에 그들은 크롤리 씨의 성품에 대하여 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n“나는 그를 평생 알고 지냈어요.” 수석 사제가 말했다. “학교와 대학을 그와 함께 다녔고, 그 뒤로도 여러 해 그와 가장 가까이 지냈지요. 그런데도 그가 궁할 때 어떻게 도와야 할지를 나는 모릅니다. 그보다 마음에 자랑이 높은 사람은 만나 본 적이 없고, 제 슬픔을 벗들과 나누기를 그보다 덜 원하는 사람도 만나 본 적이 없어요.”\n\n“그가 사제님 말씀을 하는 것을 저는 여러 번 들었습니다.” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“내가 품은 가장 쓰라린 마음 가운데 하나는, 나에게 그토록 소중한 사람이 나와 그토록 가까이 살면서 내가 그를 그토록 조금밖에 보지 못한다는 것이지요. 그러나 내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요? 그는 내 집에 오지 않으려 합니다. 그리고 내가 그의 집에 가면, 내가 삽 모양 모자를 쓰고 말을 타고 왔다는 것 때문에 나에게 노여워하지요.”\n\n“저라면 모자와 말은 지나온 마지막 교구의 경계에 두고 오겠습니다.” 루시가 조심스레 말했다.\n\n“그렇지요, 정말 그래요. 그런 일에서라도 남의 마음을 거스르지 않아야 하지요. 그러나 그러면 내 상의와 조끼가 똑같이 거슬릴 겁니다. 나는 변했고 — 겉으로 드러나는 일에서 말입니다 — 그는 변하지 않았어요. 그것이 그를 성나게 하는 것이고, 내가 옛날의 나로 돌아갈 수 없는 한 그는 나를 같은 눈으로 보지 않을 것입니다.” 그러고서 그는 앞서 말을 몰아 나아갔는데, 로바츠와 그 누이가 그 자리에 이르기 전에 만남의 첫 아픔이 지나가 있게 하려는 것이라고 그는 말했다.\n\n수석 사제가 말을 몰아 다가섰을 때 크롤리 씨는 제 집 문 앞에 서서 작은 나무 난간에 몸을 기대고 있었다. 여러 시간 곁을 지키고 앉아 있다가 달콤한 여름 바람을 몇 모금 마시려고 나온 것이었고, 그렇게 선 채로 자식들 가운데 막내를 팔에 안고 있었다. 그 딱한 어린 젖먹이는 정말로 조용히 안겨 있었으나 즐거운 낯은 아니었다. 이 아버지는 사람의 성정이 낼 수 있는 만큼 뜨거운 정으로 제 자식들을 사랑하였으나, 아이들이 자기를 좋아하게 만드는 재간은 타고나지 못하였다. 어린것들의 마음에 드는 어떤 사람들의 그 재주야 재간이라 부를 것 이상이 못 되기 때문이다. 그런 사람들이 늘 가장 좋은 아버지이거나 가장 든든한 보호자인 것은 아니다. 그러나 그들은 아이들이 알아채는 어떤 「두크 아드 메」를 몸에 지니고 다니고, 그것이 삼 분 안에 다섯 살과 마흔다섯 살 사이의 담을 죄다 허물어 놓는다. 그러나 크롤리 씨는 준엄한 사람이어서, 아버지라면 마땅히 그러해야 하듯이 언제나 제 아이들의 영혼과 마음을 생각했고, 또한 아버지로서든 가르치는 자로서든 아마 그러지 않았어야 할 일이나, 그 영혼과 마음을 다루기에 어느 때고 알맞다고 여겼다. 그리하여 그의 아이들은 고를 수 있게 되면 그를 피했고, 그것이 이미 찢긴 그의 마음에 새 상처를 보태었으나, 그가 아이들을 향하여 품은 그 큰 사랑을 조금도 꺼뜨리지는 못하였다.\n\n그렇게 그는 얌전한 어린 젖먹이를 팔에 안고 서 있었다 — 얌전하기는 넉넉히 얌전하나, 그가 바라는 대로 그에게 반갑게 입 맞추고 부드러운 작은 손으로 그의 얼굴을 어루만져 주려고는 하지 않는 아이였다 — 그러다가 수석 사제가 자기 쪽으로 오는 것을 보았다. 그는 이제 손때 묻은 책들을 코에 안경을 걸치고서야 들여다볼 수 있게 되었으나, 바깥 벌판에서는 살쾡이처럼 눈이 밝았다. 그리하여 멀리서부터 벗을 알아보았고, 어떤 식으로 맞이할지를 궁리할 겨를이 있었다. 이 사람 또한 젤리와 닭이 아니라면 돈과 조언을 가지고 온 것이 틀림없었다 — 잘되어 가는 수석 사제가 가난한 동료 성직자에게 내밀 만한 그런 돈과 그런 조언을. 그리하여 아내의 안전을 그보다 더 애타게 바라는 남편이 세상에 있을 수 없었음에도, 크롤리 씨는 곧바로 등을 곧추세우고 그 제안들을 어떻게 물리칠 수 있을지 궁리하기 시작했다.\n\n“부인은 어떤가?” 수석 사제가 작은 문 가까이에 말을 세우고 벗의 손을 잡으려 손을 내밀며 처음 한 말은 이것이었다.\n\n“잘 있었나, 애러빈?” 그가 말했다. “바체스터에 자네를 붙들어 두는 일이 그토록 많은데 이렇게 멀리까지 와 주니 참으로 고맙네. 아내가 조금이라도 나아졌다고는 말할 수 없으나, 더 나빠졌는지도 나는 모르겠네. 이따금은 헛소리를 하는 것 같기도 하네만, 그것도 잘 모르겠어. 아무튼 정신이 흐트러지고, 그러고 나면 잠이 드네.”\n\n“그러나 열은 좀 내렸나?”\n\n“이따금은 내리고 이따금은 더 오르는 듯하네.”\n\n“아이들은?”\n\n“딱한 것들. 아직은 성하네.”\n\n“아이들은 여기서 데려 내와야 하네, 크롤리. 그것이야 두말할 것 없는 일이지.”\n\n크롤리 씨는 수석 사제의 그 권고에 명령하는 투가 있다고 여겼고, 곧바로 반대편에 서 버렸다.\n\n“그것이 어떠할지는 나도 모르겠네. 나는 아직 마음을 정하지 않았어.”\n\n“그러나 이보게 크롤리—”\n\n“섭리가 모든 경우에 그런 옮김을 허락하는 것은 아니네.” 그가 말했다. “더 가난한 계층에서는 아이들이 그런 위험을 견뎌야 하는 것이야.”\n\n“많은 경우에 그렇지.” 수석 사제는 지금 이 자리에서 그것을 다툼거리로 만들 마음이 조금도 없어 그렇게 말했다. “그러나 이번에는 그럴 필요가 없네. 아이들을 데려가도록 내가 손을 쓰는 것을 자네가 허락해 주어야 하네. 자네 시간은 여기 붙들려 있을 것이 뻔하니 말이야.”\n\n로바츠 양은 크롤리 부인 곁에 머물겠다는 뜻은 말해 두었으나, 아이들에 관한 프램리 쪽 계획에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n“자네 말은 내 어깨에서 짐을 벗겨 주겠다는 것이겠지 — 실상은 아이들 비용을 대 주겠다는 것 아닌가. 그것은 허락할 수 없네, 애러빈. 아이들은 마땅히 그러해야 할 대로 제 아버지와 제 어머니의 몫을 같이 받아야 하네.”\n\n이번에도 수석 사제는 다투고 싶은 마음이 없었고, 아이들 문제는 잠시 내려놓아 두는 것이 좋을지도 모른다고 생각했다.\n\n“그러면 곁에 간병하는 사람이 없나?” 그가 말했다.\n\n“없네, 없어. 지금은 내가 몸소 보고 있네. 여자가 하나 곧 올 것이야.”\n\n“어떤 여자인가?”\n\n“그래, 이름은 스텁스 부인이라 하네. 이 교구에 사는 사람이야. 아래 아이들을 재워 줄 것이고, 그리고— 그리고— 아니, 그런 것을 다 늘어놓아 자네를 번거롭게 할 것이 없네. 오겠다는 말이 있던 젊은 아가씨가 하나 있었네만, 아마도 너무 불편하다는 것을 알게 되었을 테지. 이대로가 나을 것이네.”\n\n“로바츠 양 말인가. 그 아가씨는 곧 여기 올 것이네. 오는 길에 내가 앞질러 왔어.” 그리고 애러빈 박사가 아직 말하고 있는 동안에 길 위에서 마차 바퀴 소리가 들렸다.\n\n“나는 이제 들어가서 아직 자고 있는지 보아야겠네.” 크롤리 씨가 말했다. 그러고서 그는 수석 사제를 여전히 말 위에 앉은 채 문간에 남겨 두고 집으로 들어갔다. ‘저 사람은 전염을 겁낼 것이다. 들어오라고 청하지 않으련다.’ 크롤리 씨는 속으로 그렇게 말했다.\n\n‘청하지도 않았는데 들어가면 그의 가난을 엿보는 것처럼 보이겠지.’ 수석 사제는 속으로 그렇게 말했다. 그리하여 그는 이제 이 근방을 알게 된 퍽이 문 앞에 멈출 때까지 거기 그대로 있었다.\n\n“안에 들어가 보지 않으셨습니까?” 로바츠가 말했다.\n\n“아니오. 크롤리가 문간에 나와서 나와 이야기를 했지요. 곧 나올 것 같군요.” 그러고서 마크 로바츠 또한 집주인이 다시 나타날 때까지 기다릴 채비를 했다.\n\n그러나 루시에게는 그런 격식을 따지는 망설임이 없었다. 이제 그녀는 크롤리 씨의 마음을 거스르든 아니든 그리 개의하지 않았다. 그녀의 생각은 앓는 부인의 침상 곁에 제 자리를 잡는 것, 그리고 네 아이를 떼어 보내는 것이었다. 될 수 있으면 아버지의 승낙을 얻어서, 그러나 그 승낙을 내주지 않는다면 승낙 없이라도 반드시 그렇게 하려는 것이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 마차에서 내려 꾸러미 몇 개를 손에 들고 곧장 집 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n“마크, 자리 밑에 큰 보퉁이가 있어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그걸 꺼내 놓아 주면 내가 곧 와서 가져갈게요.”\n\n오 분쯤 교회의 두 고위 성직자는 문간에 머물러 있었다. 하나는 제 말 등에, 다른 하나는 낮은 마차에 앉아 서로 몇 마디를 나누며 누군가 다시 집에서 나오기를 기다렸다. “다 마련되어 있어요, 정말로 그래요.” 그들의 귀에 먼저 이른 것은 이 말이었고, 그것은 루시에게서 나왔다. “조금도 번거로울 것이 없고 비용도 들지 않아요. 그리고 크롤리 부인이 침상에서 일어나실 수 있게 되면 곧 다 돌아올 거예요.”\n\n“그러나 로바츠 양, 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 것은—” 로바츠 양의 뒤를 따라 문간까지 나오며 하는 크롤리 씨의 목소리였다. 그러나 그때 위의 아이들 가운데 하나가 그를 앓는 이의 방으로 불렀고, 루시는 마음껏 나쁜 짓을 할 수 있게 남겨졌다.\n\n“아이들을 데리고 돌아갈 생각인가요?” 수석 사제가 말했다.\n\n“네, 로바츠 부인이 아이들을 맞을 채비를 해 두었습니다.”\n\n“여기 있는 내 벗에게는 내가 하는 것보다 훨씬 마음대로 할 수 있는 모양이군요.”\n\n“다 제 누이가 한 일입니다.” 로바츠가 말했다. “이런 일에서는 여자들이 늘 남자보다 담대하지요.” 그러고서 루시가 보비와 아래 아이들 가운데 하나를 데리고 다시 나타났다.\n\n“그이가 하는 말은 마음에 두지 마요.” 그녀가 말했다. “다 태우면 그냥 몰고 가요. 패니에게는 찾을 수 있는 것들을 바구니에 담아 넣었는데 얼마 되지 않는다고 전해 줘요. 그레이스에게 입힐 것은 그레인저 부인네 딸아이한테서 빌려야 할 거예요” — (그레인저 부인은 프램리의 어느 농부의 아내였다) — “그리고 마크, 퍽의 머리를 돌려놓아서 곧바로 떠날 수 있게 해 둬요. 그레이스와 나머지 하나는 내가 곧 데리고 나올게요.” 그러고서 그녀는 보비와 그 어린 여동생을 마차 뒤쪽에 실어 앉히는 일을 오라비에게 맡겨 두고 집 안의 제 일로 돌아갔다. 그녀는 방금 크롤리 부인의 침상을 들여다보고, 부인이 깨어 있는 것을 보고는 웃어 보이고, 여기 머물 뜻의 표로 제 보퉁이를 내려놓았고, 그러고는 한마디도 하지 않고 아이들에 관한 제 심부름을 하러 나섰던 것이다. 그녀는 그레이스를 불러 프램리로 가져갈 것들이 어디 있는지 가르쳐 달라 하고, 아이들에게는 이제 곧 저희에게 닥칠 운명을 되도록 잘 설명해 주고서, 그 일에 대해서는 크롤리 씨에게 한마디도 하지 않은 채 떠날 채비를 시켰다. 보비와 두 젖먹이 가운데 위의 아이는 마차 뒤쪽에 안전하게 실렸고, 아이들은 한마디도 없이 그렇게 앉혀지는 것을 그대로 받아들였다. 두 아이는 눈을 크게 뜨고 옆에서 말 위에 앉아 있는 수석 사제를 쳐다보았고, 로바츠 씨가 내리는 지시에 고분고분 따랐다 — 틀림없이 몹시 놀랐겠으나, 그럼에도 아주 잠잠한 채로.\n\n“그레이스, 어서, 착하지.” 젖먹이를 팔에 안고 돌아오며 루시가 말했다. “그리고 그레이스, 아기를 아주 조심해서 안아야 한다. 바구니도 가져오렴. 네가 올라앉으면 내가 건네줄 테니.” 그리하여 그레이스와 나머지 아이가 다른 자리에 실렸고, 아이들 옷을 담은 바구니가 그 위에 얹혔다. “그만하면 되겠어요, 마크. 잘 가요. 패니에게 모레는 꼭 사람을 보내라고, 그리고 잊지 말라고 전해 줘요—” 그러고서 그녀는 오라비의 귀에, 크롤리 씨가 듣는 데서는 입에 올릴 수 없는 낙농막의 어떤 먹을거리에 관한 당부를 속삭였다. “잘 가라, 얘들아. 착한 아이들이 되어야 한다. 모레는 어머니 소식을 듣게 될 거야.” 루시가 그렇게 말했고, 퍽은 주인의 목소리가 내는 소리에 이끌려 막 움직이기 시작했는데, 바로 그때 크롤리 씨가 집 문간에 다시 나타났다.\n\n“아, 아, 잠깐!” 그가 말했다. “로바츠 양, 정말로 그러지 않으시는 편이—”\n\n“그냥 가요, 마크.” 루시가 속삭이는 소리로 말했는데, 그 소리가 크롤리 씨에게 들렸는지는 알 수 없으나 수석 사제에게는 아주 또렷이 들렸다. 크롤리 씨가 나타나자 고삐를 당겨 퍽을 잠깐 세웠던 마크는 이제 채찍으로 그 성마른 작은 짐승을 건드렸다. 그리하여 짐을 실은 마차는 쏜살같이 내달았고, 퍽은 머리를 흔들며 무섭게 빠르고 짧은 걸음으로 달려 나가 이내 크롤리 씨를 제 식구에게서 갈라 놓았다.\n\n“로바츠 양.” 그가 말을 뗐다. “이 조처는 도무지 아무런—”\n\n“네.” 그녀가 그의 말을 끊으며 말했다. “오라비가 곧 돌아가야 했어요. 아시겠지만 아이들은 목사관에서 다 함께 지낼 거예요. 그것이 크롤리 부인께서 가장 좋아하실 일이라고 저는 생각해요. 하루 이틀 지나면 아이들은 로바츠 부인이 몸소 맡아 돌보게 될 거예요.”\n\n“그러나 로바츠 양, 제 식구의 짐을 다른 사람의 어깨에 얹을 뜻은 제게 조금도 없었습니다. 아이들은 곧 제 집으로 돌아와야 합니다 — 그러니까, 데려올 수 있게 되는 대로 곧요.”\n\n“내 생각에는 로바츠 양이 참으로 잘 처리해 주었네.” 수석 사제가 말했다. “아이들이 위험에서 벗어났다고 생각하면 크롤리 부인의 마음이 훨씬 더 편할 것이야.”\n\n“그리고 아이들은 목사관에서 아주 편안할 거예요.” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“그것은 조금도 의심하지 않습니다.” 크롤리 씨가 말했다. “그러나 그런 편안함이 지나치면 아이들이 제 집에 맞지 않게 되고 말 것입니다. 그리고 — 그리고 이 일이 치러지기 전에 좀 더 여유 있게 저와 의논해 주었으면 좋았겠다고 저는 생각합니다.”\n\n“크롤리 씨, 제가 앞서 여기 왔을 때 아이들을 떼어 놓는 편이 낫겠다고 정해졌었잖아요.” 루시가 사정하듯 말했다.\n\n“그런 조처에 제가 동의한 기억은 없습니다, 로바츠 양. 그러나— 오늘 밤에는 데려올 수 없겠지요?”\n\n“네, 오늘 밤은 안 되지요.” 루시가 말했다. “그러면 저는 이제 부인께 들어가 보겠어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 두 신사를 문간에 남겨 두고 집으로 돌아갔다. 그때 막벌이꾼의 아이 하나가 어슬렁어슬렁 지나갔고, 수석 사제는 그 아이를 붙들어 제 말을 맡기고서 말에서 내릴 수 있었으며, 그리하여 벗과 이야기하기에 좀 더 대등한 자리에 몸을 두게 되었다.\n\n“크롤리.” 두 사람이 문 앞의 작은 난간에 함께 기대어 선 채로, 그가 벗의 어깨에 정답게 손을 얹으며 말했다. “좋은 아가씨야 — 아주 좋은 아가씨네.”\n\n“그래.” 그가 느릿하게 말했다. “뜻은 좋지.”\n\n“아니, 뜻만이 아니라 잘 하고 있네. 훌륭하게 하고 있어. 지금 저 아가씨가 하는 처사보다 무엇이 더 나을 수 있겠나? 이 궁한 처지에서 자네 아내를 어떻게 도울 수 있을까 내가 궁리하고 있는 동안에—”\n\n“나는 도움을 바라지 않네. 적어도 사람에게서 받을 도움은 바라지 않아.” 크롤리가 쓰라리게 말했다.\n\n“아, 이 사람아, 자네가 하는 말을 생각해 보게! 그런 마음가짐에 반드시 따라붙는 사악함을 생각해 보게! 형제 되는 사람들의 도움 없이 혼자 걸을 수 있는 사람을 자네가 여태 하나라도 본 적이 있나?”\n\n크롤리 씨는 곧바로 대답을 하지 않고, 제 삶의 몫이 두루 쓰라리다는 것을 생각하며 혼자 걸을 때 늘 그러던 대로 두 팔을 등 뒤로 돌리고 두 손을 맞잡은 채, 집 앞 길을 따라 천천히 걸음을 옮기기 시작했다. 그는 상대에게 같이 걷자고 청하지는 않았으나, 혼자 있게 해 달라는 뜻으로 보이는 것 또한 그의 태도에 조금도 없었다. 아름다운 여름 오후였다. 봄의 자라남에서 여름이 막 터져 나온, 한 해 가운데 그 달콤한 철이었다. 여름이 아직 사흘밖에 되지 않아, 자연이 내어 놓을 수 있는 온갖 결의 초록이 아직도 물들지 않은 순전한 싱그러움 속에 있는 때였다. 나무들에는 사과꽃이 피어 있었고, 울타리는 오월의 산사꽃으로 향기로웠다. 다섯 시에도 뻐꾸기는 여전히 조금도 기운을 잃지 않고 부드러운 여름 노래를 울리고 있었으며, 울타리 밑 흔한 풀들까지도 새로 돋아난 향기로 향기로웠다. 참나무의 잎은 다 갖추어져 큰 가지며 잔가지가 모두 옷을 입었으나, 잎이 아직 무더기로 무겁게 늘어지지는 않아 가지마다의 굽이와 잔가지마다의 가늘어지는 곡선이 그 연한 초록빛 덮개를 통하여 다 보였다. 한 해 가운데 여름의 첫 주만큼 아름다운 때는 없다. 그리고 자연이 내어 주는 어떤 빛깔도, 심지어 가을의 화려한 색조조차도 오월의 첫 따뜻한 햇볕이 빚어낸 그 푸르름과 견줄 수 없다.\n\n이미 설명한 대로 호글스톡에는 풍경의 아름다움이라 할 것이 별로 없고, 호글스톡의 성직자 집은 길에서 물러 앉은 푸르고 비스듬한 언덕 위에, 창이 잔디밭으로 열리고 관목에 둘러싸이고 그 사이로 작은 교회 탑이 보이는 그런 자리에 놓여 있지 않았다. 영국의 농사 짓는 고장에서 우리 영혼의 목자들이 사는 아늑한 집들에 그토록 흔한 그 아름다움이 그 집에는 하나도 없었다. 호글스톡 목사관은 길가에 을씨년스럽게 서 있었고, 안쪽으로 호랑가시나무와 금사슬나무, 포르투갈 월계수와 장미나무를 둘러 심은 예쁜 울짱 따위도 없었다. 그러나 그럼에도 호글스톡조차 지금은 예뻤다. 꽃으로 덮인 사과나무들이 거기 있었고 울타리는 온통 꽃이었다. 개똥지빠귀들이 노래하고 있었고, 길가에는 여기저기 참나무 한 그루씩이 홀로 선 아름다움 속에 오롯이 서 있었다.\n\n“조금 더 걸어 보세.” 수석 사제가 말했다. “지금은 로바츠 양이 부인 곁에 있으니, 자네도 몇 분 방을 떠나 있는 것이 나을 것이네.”\n\n“아니.” 그가 말했다. “돌아가야 하네. 저 젊은 아가씨에게 내 일을 하도록 맡겨 둘 수는 없어.”\n\n“멈추게, 크롤리!” 그러고서 수석 사제는 그에게 손을 얹어 길에서 그를 세웠다. “그 아가씨는 자기 일을 하고 있는 것이네. 자네 집이 아닌 다른 집을 두고 그 아가씨를 말한다면 자네도 그렇게 말할 것이야. 이런 때에 자네 아내 곁에 여자가 있다는 것을, 게다가 부인이 부인에게 말하듯 아내에게 말해 줄 수 있는 여자가 있다는 것을 아는 것이 자네에게 위로가 되지 않는가?”\n\n“이런 편안함은 우리가 바랄 권리가 없는 것이네. 딱한 메리를 위해 내가 많은 것을 해 줄 수는 없었겠지만, 사람으로서 할 수 있는 일이라면 부족함이 없었을 것이야.”\n\n“그럴 것이라고 나는 확신하네. 잘 알고 있어. 사람이 혼자서 할 수 있는 일이라면 자네는 무엇이든 할 것이야 — 다만 한 가지만 빼고.” 그렇게 말하며 수석 사제는 상대의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다.\n\n“내가 하지 않을 일이 무엇인가?” 크롤리가 말했다.\n\n“자네 자신의 자랑을 희생하는 일이지.”\n\n“내 자랑?”\n\n“그래. 자네 자신의 자랑이야.”\n\n“나에게는 오랫동안 자랑이랄 것이 거의 없었네. 애러빈, 자네는 내 삶이 어떠했는지 모르네. 어떻게 사람이 자랑을 품을 수 있겠나, 이렇게—” 그러고서 그는 스스로 말을 멈추었다. 제 생각으로는 자기 안의 자랑의 싹까지 죽여 버린 그 온갖 억울한 일들의 목록을 늘어놓고 싶지 않았고, 제 가난과 제 궁핍과 제 처지의 부당함을 소리 내어 내세우고 싶지도 않았던 것이다. “아니. 자랑을 품을 수 있었으면 좋겠네만, 세상이 나에게 너무 무거웠고 나는 그런 것을 다 잊어버렸네.”\n\n“내가 자네를 알고 지낸 지 얼마나 되었나, 크롤리?”\n\n“얼마나라니? 아, 딱한 일이지! 이제 거의 한 평생이네.”\n\n“그리고 우리는 한때 형제나 다름없었지.”\n\n“그래. 그때는 형제처럼 서로 대등했네 — 우리의 살림에서나, 우리의 취미에서나, 우리가 사는 방식에서나.”\n\n“그런데도 자네는, 내가 주머니에 손을 넣어 삶의 팔자가 자네와 또 자네가 자네 자신보다 더 사랑하는 이들에게 던져 놓은 그 불편들을 덜어 주는 기쁨을, 나에게서 아까워하려는 것이네.”\n\n“나는 어느 누구의 자선에 기대어 살지 않겠네.” 크롤리가 거의 노여움을 드러내는 것에 이를 만큼 불쑥 말했다.\n\n“그것이 자랑이 아닌가?”\n\n“아니 — 그렇지. 그것은 자랑의 한 가지이나 자네가 말한 그 자랑은 아니네. 사람이 얼마간의 자랑이 없으면 정직할 수 없어. 자네 자신은 어떤가. 거지가 되기보다는 차라리 굶겠다고 하지 않겠나?”\n\n“나는 내 아내가 굶는 것을 보기보다는 차라리 빌어먹겠네.” 애러빈이 말했다.\n\n크롤리는 이 말을 듣고 홱 돌아서서, 두 손은 여전히 등 뒤에 두고 눈은 땅에 박은 채 수석 사제에게 등을 지고 섰다.\n\n“그러나 이번 일에는 빌어먹는다는 것이 문제가 아니네.” 수석 사제가 말을 이었다. “하느님이 기꺼이 내 손에 맡겨 주신 그 남아 넘치는 것들 가운데서, 나는 내가 사랑하는 이들의 궁핍을 돕고 싶은 것이야.”\n\n“아내는 굶고 있지 않네.” 크롤리가 몹시 쓰라린 목소리로, 그러면서도 제 자신을 변호하려는 뜻으로 말했다.\n\n“그래, 이 사람아. 굶고 있지 않은 줄 나도 아네. 그러니 내가 쓸 수 있는 가장 센 말로 이 일을 자네에게 내놓아 보려 했다고 해서 나에게 노여워하지 말게.”\n\n“애러빈, 자네는 그것을 한쪽에서만 보네. 나는 다른 쪽에서밖에 볼 수가 없어. 주는 것은 몹시 달지. 그것은 나도 의심하지 않네. 그러나 주어진 것을 받는 일은 몹시 쓰네. 얻어 먹는 빵은 사람의 목에 걸리고 그 피를 독하게 만들고 가슴 위에 납덩이처럼 얹히네. 자네는 그것을 겪어 본 적이 없어.”\n\n“그러나 바로 그것이 내가 자네를 탓하는 허물이네. 그것이 바로 자네가 희생해야 한다고 내가 말하는 그 자랑이야.”\n\n“그러면 어찌하여 내가 그리하라고 부름을 받아야 하나? 일꾼이 그 품값을 받아 마땅하지 않은가? 나는 일할 수 있고 또 일할 뜻이 있지 않은가? 나는 언제나 멍에에 어깨를 대어 오지 않았는가? 그런데 이제 부자의 부엌에서 나온 찌꺼기로 만족하는 것이 옳은 일인가? 애러빈, 자네와 나는 한때 대등했고 그때는 벗이었네. 서로의 생각을 알아듣고 서로의 슬픔을 함께 아파했지. 그러나 이제는 그럴 수 없네.”\n\n“그렇게 할 수 없다면, 그것은 온전히 자네 쪽에 있는 것이네.”\n\n“온전히 내 쪽에 있네. 우리가 어울리는 자리에서 아픔은 다 내 쪽에만 있을 것이기 때문이야. 닳아 버린 신과 해어진 셔츠를 걸친 나를 자네 식탁에서 본다고 자네가 아플 것은 없지. 나는 자네를 그렇게까지 하잘것없이 보지 않네. 내가 자네 의자 뒤에 선 하인의 십분의 일만큼도 차려입지 못하였어도 자네는 나에게 자네의 잔치 음식을 먹으라고 내놓을 것이야. 그러나 나를 바라보며 자네 방에 앉을 만한 자가 아니라고 여기는 사람들이 있다는 것을 아는 일은 나를 아프게 하네.”\n\n“그것이 내가 말하는 자랑이네 — 거짓된 자랑이야.”\n\n“그렇게 부르고 싶으면 그렇게 부르게. 그러나 애러빈, 자네의 어떤 설교로도 그것을 바꿀 수는 없네. 그것이 내 사내다움에서 나에게 남은 전부야. 저기 앓아누워 있는 그 딱한 부러진 갈대 — 나를 향한 사랑을 위하여 온 세상을 버린 사람, 내 아이들의 어머니이며 내 슬픔의 동반자이며 내 품의 아내인 그 사람 — 그 사람조차 이 일에서는 나를 바꾸지 못하네. 제 온갖 궁핍의 웅변으로 사정하여도 그렇네. 그 사람을 위해서라도 나는 구휼을 받으려 손을 내밀 수는 없어.”\n\n이제 두 사람은 집 문 앞으로 되돌아와 있었고, 크롤리 씨는 제가 무엇을 하고 있는지 거의 알지 못한 채 들어가려 하고 있었다.\n\n“내가 들어가면 크롤리 부인이 나를 볼 수 있겠나?” 수석 사제가 말했다.\n\n“아, 잠깐. 아니, 그러지 않는 것이 좋겠네.” 크롤리 씨가 말했다. “자네도 틀림없이 병이 옮을 수 있고, 그러면 애러빈 부인이 겁을 낼 것이네.”\n\n“나는 그런 것을 조금도 개의하지 않네.” 수석 사제가 말했다.\n\n“그러나 쓸데없는 일이야. 그러지 않는 것이 좋겠네. 아내의 방은 자네가 볼 만한 방이 도무지 아닐 것이 걱정이고, 알다시피 집 전체에 병이 배어 있을 수도 있네.”\n\n애러빈 박사는 이때 이미 거실에 들어와 있었다. 그러나 벗이 자기가 더 들어가지 않기를 정말로 애타게 바라는 것을 보고 그는 고집하지 않았다.\n\n“아무튼 로바츠 양이 부인 곁에 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로도 우리에게는 위로가 되겠네.”\n\n“그 젊은 아가씨는 참으로 선량하네 — 정말로 선량해.” 크롤리가 말했다. “그러나 내일은 제 집으로 돌아가리라고 나는 믿네. 내 집처럼 가난한 집에 머문다는 것은 될 수 없는 일이야. 그 아가씨가 필요로 할 것들 가운데 여기에 있을 것은 하나도 없네.”\n\n수석 사제는 지금 병구완이라는 일에 매인 루시 로바츠에게 필요한 것이 크롤리 부인의 병실에 계속 머무는 일을 불가능하게 만들 만큼 많지는 않으리라고 생각했다. 그리하여 그 딱한 부인이 남편의 다소 서투른 병구완에만 오롯이 맡겨지지는 않겠다는, 마음 놓이는 확신을 품고서 작별했다.\n\n# 제37장. 벗 없는 사워비 씨\n\n이제 서바셋주에서 놀라운 일들이 벌어지려 하고 있었고, 사람들의 마음은 몹시 어수선했다. 높은 자리에서 명이 내려졌고, 여왕께서는 충성스러운 하원을 해산하셨다. 거인들은 옛 하원으로는 이룰 수 있는 것이 거의 없거나 아예 없다는 것을 알고, 새로 한번 걸어 보면 그것이 저들에게 무엇을 가져다줄지 시험해 보기로 마음을 굳혔으니, 총선의 소란이 온 나라에 퍼지게 되어 있었다. 이것은 적잖은 짜증과 언짢음을 낳았다. 그 하원은 아직 삼 년도 채 되지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 하원 의원들은 자기 벗들을 만나고 선거구민들의 손을 맞잡는 일에서 헌정에 걸맞은 즐거움을 절로 느끼기는 하지만, 그럼에도 인간이라는 종의 낮은 층과 다를 바 없을 만큼은 되어서 자기 의석을 잃을 위험이 무엇인지 제대로 알아본다. 게다가 그 의석을 지키려고 애쓰는 데 상당한 지출이 반드시 따른다는 사실은 입법가의 마음에 유쾌한 일이 아니다.\n\n신들과 거인들 사이의 그 오랜 집안싸움이 지금 이 순간보다 더 높이 타오른 적은 없었다. 거인들은 나라를 섬기려고 자기들이 내디디려 한 걸음마다 당파 싸움에 막혔다고, 그것도 불과 몇 주 전에 상대편이 도와주겠다고 그토록 인심 좋게 약속을 해 놓고서 그랬다고 언명했다. 그러자 신들은 거인들의 보이오티아식 우둔함 때문에 자기들이 이 반대에 내몰린 것이라고 주장하며 대답했다. 물론 자기들은 도움을 약속했고, 어지간히 사려 깊은 조치라면 도울 준비가 되어 있었다. 그러나 정부가 마음대로 늙은 주교에게 연금을 주어 물릴 수 있게 하는 법안에까지 그럴 수는 없었다! 아니, 자기들의 아량에도 어딘가 한계가 있어야 하는데, 이런 시도가 나오고 보니 그 한계는 분명히 넘어선 것이었다.\n\n이 모든 일이 드러내어 벌어진 것은 톰 타워스가 던스터블 양의 모임에서 무심히 흘린 그 속삭임이 있은 뒤 겨우 하루나 이틀 만이었다—톰 타워스, 저 유쾌한 사람들 가운데 가장 유쾌한 사람이 흘린 속삭임이었다. 그런데 그가 어떻게 그것을 알 수 있었겠는가—정원의 가장 향기로운 꽃에서 또 다른 꽃으로 날아다니는 그 사람이,\n\n“패랭이꽃에는 설탕을 더하고 장미에는 꿀을 더하며, 주는 것으로 그토록 사랑받으면서도 가면서 아무것도 가져가지 않는 이”라 하지 않던가?\n\n그러나 속삭임은 소문으로 자라났고 소문은 사실이 되었으니, 정치의 세계는 들끓었다. 거인들은 저들의 주교 연금 법안 때문에 격분해서 하원을 위협했다—지극히 지혜롭지 못한 짓이었다. 그러자 분개한 의원들이 정직함으로 환하게 달아올라 일어서서, 저 하원의 어느 신사인들 자기 의석에 관한 기대나 두려움 때문에 표결에서 움직여질 수 있다고 여기는 것은 비열한 일이라고 언명하는 광경은 볼만했다. 그리하여 사정은 나쁜 데서 더 나쁜 데로 흘러갔고, 서로 다투는 이 두 편이 지금처럼 독기 오른 노여움으로 서로를 치고받은 적은 없었다—그토록 얼마 전에 호의와 존중과 참을성을 여러 갈래로 약속하면서 함께 판에 들어섰던 사이인데도!\n\n그러나 일반에서 개별로 옮겨 말한다면, 서바셋주 선거 지구보다 더 깊은 경악이 퍼진 곳은 어디에도 없었다고 해도 좋다. 해산 소식이 이 주에 닿기가 무섭게 공작이 자기가 낙점한 사람을 바꿀 뜻을 품고 있다는 것이 알려졌다. 사워비 씨는 개혁 법안이 나온 뒤로 줄곧 이 지구를 대표해 앉아 있었던 것이다! 그는 이 주의 제도 가운데 하나가 되어 있었고, 오래된 관례와 긴 세월 자리 잡아 온 힘으로, 세상이 다 아는 그 금전상의 난잡함에도 불구하고 주의 신사들에게 참아 넘겨져 왔고 심지어 좋게 여겨지기까지 했다. 이제 이 모든 것이 바뀌게 되어 있었다. 아직 공개된 이유는 하나도 없었으나, 덤벨로 경이 당선되기로 되어 있다는 것이 사람들의 이해였다. 덤벨로 경은 이 주에 땅 한 에이커도 가지고 있지 않았지만 그랬다. 소문은 이어서 덤벨로 경이 바셋주와 가까운 인연을 맺으려 하고 있다고 말했는데, 그것은 사실이었다. 그는 어느 젊은 아가씨와 혼인할 날을 앞두고 있었으니, 그 아가씨는 과연 다른 지구 출신이었다. 그리고 그는 지금 콜디코츠 수렵림이라 흔히 불리는 그 훌륭한 왕실 소유지를 사들이는 일로 정부와 교섭을 마무리하는 중이라고 했다. 또 이런 말도 있었다—다만 이 말은 여태까지 비밀스러운 속삭임으로만 전해져 왔다—콜디코츠 저택 자체가 머지않아 후작의 거처가 될 것이라는 말이었다. 공작이 그 집을 자기 것이라 주장하고 있었으니, 아주 머지않아 그 주장을 완결하고 소유를 넘겨받을 것이었다. 그러고 나면 두 사람 사이의 어떤 마련을 거쳐 그 집이 덤벨로 경에게 넘겨지게 되어 있었다.\n\n그러나 이와 아주 어긋나는 소문도 함께 퍼졌다. 공작에게 감히 맞서는 이들이, 그리고 싸움의 날이 오면 감히 맞서지도 못할 몇몇 사람들까지도 말하기를, 덤벨로 경을 바셋주의 유력자로 만드는 것은 각하의 힘으로도 안 되는 일이라고 했다. 그 왕실 소유지는—그런 사람들의 말로는—다른 지구에 있는 박솔 힐의 젊은 그레셤 씨의 손에 들어가게 되어 있고, 매입 조건도 이미 정해졌다고 했다. 그리고 사워비 씨의 재산과 콜디코츠의 저택에 대해서는—옴니엄 쪽 이해에 맞선 이들이 이어서 풀어 말하기로는—공작이 거기 들어가서 소유를 넘겨받을 수 있으리라는 것이 아직 결코 그렇게 확실하지 않았다. 그곳이 조용히 그에게 넘겨질 것은 아니었다. 큰 싸움이 벌어질 것이고, 그 막대한 저당이 어마어마한 재산을 가진 어느 부인의 손으로 갚아져 없어질 것이라고 믿는 사람들이 생겨나기 시작했다. 그러고 나서는 이런 이야기들을 입에 맞게 만들어 줄 연애담 한 자락도 빠지지 않았다. 어마어마한 재산을 가진 이 부인은 사워비 씨의 구애를 받고 자기 사랑을 시인했으나, 그의 사람됨을 이유로 그와 혼인하기를 마다했다는 것이다. 그러면서도 자기 사랑의 증표로 그의 빚을 다 갚아 주려 한다는 것이었다.\n\n사워비 씨가 공작의 분부에 순종하여 이 주에서 물러날 뜻이 없다는 것이 곧 소문의 자리를 넘어 넉넉히 드러났다. 벽보 하나가 지구 전체에 붙었는데, 거기에는 공작의 이름을 들어 빗댄 말이 하나도 없었으나, 사워비 씨가 서바셋주의 대표 자리에서 물러날 뜻을 품고 있다는 어떤 보도에도 벗들이 끌려가지 말기를 당부하는 내용이었다. “그는,” 벽보는 이렇게 말했다, “사반세기라는 온 기간에 걸쳐 같은 주를 대표해 앉아 있었으며, 그토록 여러 번 자기에게 내밀어진 영예이자 그토록 소중하게 여겨 온 영예를 가볍게 내놓지는 않을 것이다. 오늘날 하원에서 같은 선거구민 집단과의 인연이 서바셋주와 그를 묶어 온 인연만큼 오래도록 끊이지 않고 이어진 사람은 몇 되지 않는다. 그리고 그는 그 인연이 앞으로 올 또 한 시절을 지나 계속되어, 마침내 자기가 하원에서 주를 대표하는 의원들의 아버지가 되는 영광스러운 자리에 있게 되기를 자신 있게 바란다.” 벽보는 이보다 훨씬 많은 말을 하며 이 주에 크게 이해가 걸린 여러 가지 문제를 이러저러하게 비추었으나, 옴니엄 공작에 대해서는 한 마디도 하지 않았다. 이 일에서 공작이 무엇을 하고 있다고 여겨지는지 모두가 알고 있었는데도 그랬다. 그는 이를테면 지성소에 갇혀 있는 대라마였다. 헤아릴 수 없고, 보이지 않고, 굽힐 줄 모르는 이. 사람의 눈에 보이지도 사람의 귀에 들리지도 않고, 이런 시절에는 보통 사람이 속으로 떨지 않고서는 입에 올리기조차 어려운 이. 그러나 그럼에도 그들을 다스린다고 여겨지는 이는 그였다. 완곡한 말법은 다가오는 선거와 관련해서 어떤 공개 집회에서도 그의 이름이 입에 올려지지 말기를 요구했다. 그러나 그럼에도 이 주의 사람들은 대부분, 그가 그러기를 좋아하기만 하면 자기 개를 서바셋주의 의원으로 하원에 올려보낼 수도 있다고 믿었다.\n\n그러므로 우리 벗 사워비에게는 승산이 없다고 여겨졌다. 그러나 그는 자기가 도움을 받을 자격이 결코 없는 쪽에서 도움을 만나는 운을 얻었다. 그는 정치 인생 전체에 걸쳐 신들의 든든한 벗이었다—공작이 낙점한 사람이었으니 사실 그럴 수밖에 없기도 했다. 그럼에도 이번 경우에는 이 주와 연이 닿은 거인들이 모두 그를 구하러 나섰다. 그들이 이렇게 한 것은 공작에게 맞서겠다는 뜻을 내놓고 밝힌 것은 아니었다. 그들은 오래된 주 대표 의원이 자기 의석에서 밀려나는 것을 보고 싶지 않은 넉넉한 마음에서 움직인다고 언명했다. 그러나 세상은 이 싸움이 대라마를 겨누어 벌어지는 것임을 알았다. 그것은 귀족의 힘과 과두의 힘 사이의 겨룸이 될 터였는데, 서바셋주에 그 두 힘이 있는 대로의 겨룸이었다. 그리고 덧붙여 말한다면, 민주의 힘은 이쪽에서든 저쪽에서든 여기에 할 말이 거의 없을 것이었다. 낮은 층의 유권자들, 곧 작은 농부들과 장사꾼들은 틀림없이 공작 편에 늘어설 것이고, 그렇게 함으로써 자기들이 자유주의 편의 뜻을 밀어 준다고 스스로 흐뭇하게 여기려 애쓸 것이었다. 그러나 실제로 그들을 투표소로 이끄는 것은 자기네 대라마의 뜻에 대한 낡고 오래된 순종이요, 만일 그 라마가 몸을 일으켜 노여움으로 몸을 떨면 어쩌나 하는 재앙에 대한 두려움일 것이었다. 그 라마가 자기 위성들과 군대와 조신들을 데리고 걸어서 떠나 버린다면 이 주에 무슨 일이 닥치지 않을 것인가? 그가 거기 있었다, 대라마가. 그리고 그는 그들 가운데로 좀처럼 오지 않았고 오더라도 좀처럼 눈에 보이지 않았으나, 그럼에도—덜하기는커녕 오히려 더하게—그에게 순종하는 것은 몸에 좋은 일로 여겨졌고 그에게 맞서는 것은 위태로운 일로 여겨졌다. 시골의 대라마는 시골 영국에서 아직도 충분히 힘이 세다.\n\n그러나 그 신전의 사제인 포더길 씨는 사람들의 눈에 넉넉히 자주 띄었고, 자기 주변의 일들과 다가오는 변화에 대해 그가 얼마나 여러 가지 목소리로 말하는지 듣는 것은 볼만한 일이었다. 개서럼 영지뿐 아니라 다른 영지의 작은 농부들에게도 그는 공작을 두루 복을 베푸는 힘으로 이야기했다. 주위 모두에게 번영을 흘려보내고, 그 자리에 있음으로써 값을 떠받치고, 자기가 널리 일으키는 고용으로 구빈세가 파운드당 일 실링 사 펜스 위로 올라가지 못하게 막는다는 것이었다. 공작의 얼굴에 기꺼이 달려들려 하는 사람이라면 미친 사람일 것이라고 그는 여겼다. 멀리서 온 지주들에게는, 적어도 자기가 공작의 마음을 아는 한, 아무도 공작에게 어떤 간섭의 죄를 씌울 권리가 없다고 언명했다. 사람들은 자기가 아무것도 모르는 일에 대해 말을 하려 든다. 표 한 장을 부탁한 일 하나라도 그 자취를 공작에게까지 되짚어 갔다고 말할 수 있는 사람이 있느냐는 것이었다. 이 모든 것이 사람들 마음에 자리 잡은 확신을 바꾸어 놓지는 못했다. 그러나 그것은 그것대로 효험이 있어서, 공작이 하는 일을 둘러싼 신비를 더 두껍게 하는 데 이바지했다. 그러나 자기와 친한 사람들, 곧 자기 바로 곁에 있는 신사 계층에게는 포더길 씨가 그저 눈을 찡긋해 보였다. 그들도 사정이 어떤지 알았고 그도 알았다. 공작은 그런 일에서 여태 한 번도 물려 본 적이 없었고, 이제 와서 그런 수술을 스스로 받으려 하지는 않을 것이라고 포더길 씨는 여겼다.\n\n바셋주에 있는 공작의 재산과 관련해서 포더길 씨가 해낸 여러 가지 일에 대해 그가 어떤 방식으로 얼마만큼 보수를 받았는지 나는 들어 본 적이 없다. 그러나 그 액수가 얼마였든 그가 그것을 온전히 벌었다는 것만은 아주 확실하다. 그보다 더 충실한 당파의 사람도, 그 당파심 안에서 그보다 더 조심스러운 사람도 없었다. 다가오는 선거라는 이 일에서 그는, 자기 자신은—사사로이, 제 몫으로—덤벨로 경을 위해 활발히 움직일 뜻이라고 언명했다. 사워비 씨는 자기의 오랜 벗이며 아주 좋은 사람이었다. 그것은 사실이었다. 그러나 사워비가 주 대표 의원이 마땅히 차지해야 할 처지에 있지 않다는 것은 온 세상이 인정해야 할 것이었다. 그는 파산한 사람이었고, 재산을 가진 사람에게만 맞는 자리에 그를 붙들어 두는 것은 그 자신에게도 좋을 일이 아니었다. 그는—그, 포더길은—사워비 씨가 콜디코츠에 대한 모든 권리와 청구를 내놓아야 한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그렇다면 그가 하원 의석에 대해서는 권리와 청구를 가졌다고 인정해 주는 것보다 더 터무니없는 일이 무엇이겠는가? 덤벨로 경에 대해서라면, 그가 머지않아 이 주에서 가장 큰 땅 임자 가운데 하나가 될 것이 짐작되었으니, 그렇다면 대표 자리에 그보다 더 알맞은 사람이 누구겠는가? 그 위에 더해 포더길 씨는—그의 말로는—덤벨로 경의 대리인 자리를 맡기를 바란다고 털어놓는 것을 부끄러워하지 않았다. 그것은 자기의 다른 직무와 서로 어긋나지 않을 것이니, 그러므로 당연한 일로서 자기는 덤벨로 경을 밀 뜻이었다—그 자신이, 그러니까. 이 일에서 공작의 마음이야—! 그러나 포더길 씨가 그 문제를 어떻게 처리했는지는 내가 이미 풀어 놓았다.\n\n이 무렵에 사워비 씨는 자기 집으로 내려왔다—겉으로는 아직 자기 집이었으니까. 그러나 그는 아주 조용히 왔고, 그가 도착한 것이 자기 마을에서도 거의 알려지지 않았다. 벽보는 그렇게 널리 붙여 놓았으나 그 자신은 선거 운동의 걸음이라고는 하나도 떼지 않았다. 적어도 아직은 하나도 없었다. 의회에서 나오는 체포 면책은 곧 끝날 것이었고, 공작에게 가장 앙심을 품은 이들은 그가 법의 미르미돈들에게 넉넉한 기회를 주기만 하면 그를 체포하려는 걸음이 떼어질 것이라고 단언했다. 그런 경우에 그가 체포될 것은 몹시 있을 법한 일이었다. 그러나 그 일이 어떤 식으로든 공작이 부추겨 이루어질 것 같지는 않았다. 포더길 씨는 이렇게 빗대는 말이 자기를 매우 노엽게 한다고 분개하여 언명했다. 그러나 그는 무슨 일에든 매우 노여워하기에는 너무 신중한 사람이었고, 저렇게 제 표적을 지나쳐 넘어가 버린 비난을 자기 편의 밑천으로 만드는 법을 알고 있었다.\n\n사워비 씨는 아주 조용히 콜디코츠로 내려와 거기서 이틀을 완전히 혼자 머물렀다. 이곳은 이 작은 이야기의 앞쪽에서 마크 로바츠가 마차를 몰아 올라왔을 때 우리가 눈여겨보았던 모습과는 이제 아주 다른 모습을 띠고 있었다. 이제 창문에는 불빛이 없고 마구간에서는 어떤 목소리도 들려오지 않았다. 개도 짖지 않았고, 모든 것이 무덤처럼 죽어 있고 잠잠했다. 그 이틀의 대부분 동안 그는 집 안에 혼자 앉아 거의 아무것도 하지 않았다. 편지조차 뜯지 않았고, 그 편지들은 그가 앉아 있는 작은 조찬실의 그 붐비는 탁자 위에 무더기로 놓여 있었다. 그런 사람들의 편지는 무더기로 오는 것이고, 그 가운데 읽어서 즐거운 것은 몇 되지 않는다. 그는 거기 앉아 슬프기 그지없는 생각들에 시달렸고, 이따금 집 안을 이리저리 오갔으나, 대부분은 자기가 스스로를 끌어다 놓은 처지를 곱씹는 일에 매여 있었다. 만일 콜디코츠의 임자이기를 그치고 자기 주의 의원이기를 또한 그친다면, 그는 세상의 눈에 무엇이 될 것인가? 그는 여태껏 늘 세상의 앞에서 살아왔고, 언제나 빚에 시달리기는 했으나 두드러진 자리가 주는 설렘으로 버티고 위로를 얻어 왔다. 그의 빚과 곤경은 여태까지는 견딜 만한 것이었고, 너무 오래 그것을 쉽게 견뎌 온 나머지 그것들이 결코 견딜 수 없게 되지는 않으리라고 스스로 생각하도록 거의 길들여 놓았다. 그러나 이제는—\n\n저당권을 실행하라는 명이 내려졌고, 법의 하르피이아들은 그의 재산이라는 시체에 발톱을 박아 넣는 지금의 빠름으로, 여태 먹이에 다가가는 것을 미루도록 강요당해 온 그 지체를 스스로 벌충하고 있었다. 그리고 그의 의석에 관한 명도 함께 내려져 있었다. 그 벽보는 그의 누이동생과 던스터블 양, 그리고 거인들 쪽 이해에 서 있다고 짐작되는 클로저스틸이라는 이름의 잘 알려진 어느 선거 대리인이 힘을 합쳐 작성한 것이었다. 그러나 가련한 사워비는 그 벽보를 거의 믿지 않았다. 공작의 힘이 얼마나 큰지를 그보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없었다.\n\n그리하여 그는 그 빈 방들을 오가며 자기 지나간 삶과 앞으로 올 그 삶을 생각하는 동안 아무 희망이 없었다. 세상을 즐겁게 만들어 주었던 모든 것에 대해 이제 죽어 가고 있으니, 차라리 죽어 버리는 것이 그에게 좋지 않겠는가! 우리는 사워비 씨 같은 사람들을 보고 들으면서, 그들이 세상이 줄 수 있는 것을 다 누리며, 근심으로든 수고로든 값을 치르지 않고 그 모두를 누린다고 여기기 쉽다. 그러나 나는 그들 가운데 가장 무디어진 자에게조차 비참한 시기가 잦을 것이고 그 비참함도 매우 사무칠 것이라고 짐작한다. 이월의 연어와 새끼 양고기, 삼월의 완두와 햇감자가, 아무도 그 값을 치르지 않는다 해도 사람을 행복하게 해 주기는 어렵다. 그리고 자기가 「안테케덴템 스켈레스툼」이며 그 뒤를 절름거리기는 하나 반드시 오고 마는 네메시스가 뒤뚱뒤뚱 따라오고 있다는 느낌은 때때로 잠을 어지럽히지 않을 수 없다. 이번에는 스켈레스투스가 자기 네메시스에게 붙잡혔다고 느꼈다. 그 여신은 절름거렸고 그는 빠르게 달아났으나, 마침내 여신이 그를 입에 물었으니, 그에게 남은 것은 제 죽음의 몸부림을 보고 터지는 “와아” 하는 함성을 듣는 일뿐이었다.\n\n이제 그 큰 콜디코츠의 저택은 구슬프고 쓸쓸한 곳이었다. 숲은 온통 이른 새잎으로 푸르고 정원은 꽃으로 빽빽했으나, 그것들도 구슬프고 쓸쓸했다. 잔디는 다듬어지지 않았고 자갈 사이로 잡풀이 자라 나왔으며, 여기저기 금이 간 드리아스 상이 받침대에서 굴러떨어져 풀밭에 나동그라져 있어서 온 자리에 어수선한 꼴을 얹어 놓았다. 나무 격자 울은 여기서 부서지고 저기서 휘어 있었고, 대목 장미들은 땅으로 허리를 굽히고 있었고, 겨울에 진 잎들이 아직도 화단을 어지럽히고 있었다. 이틀째 되는 날 저녁 늦게 사워비 씨는 어슬렁 나가 정원을 지나 숲으로 들어갔다. 세상의 목숨 없는 것들 가운데 이 콜디코츠의 숲이 그에게는 가장 소중했다. 그는 벗들에게서 시에 가까운 감정이나 생각을 지녔다고 인정받는 사람이 아니었으나, 여기 체이스에 나오면 그의 마음은 거의 시적이 되었다. 숲의 나무들 사이를 헤매는 동안 그는 사람의 마음이 지닌 부드러움을 느낄 수 있게 되었다. 새들이 노래하는 것을 듣고, 길 위에서 들꽃을 여기저기 한 송이씩 꺾었다. 늙은 나무가 삭아 가는 것과 어린 나무가 자라 가는 것을 지켜보고, 숲의 굽이마다 눈에 그림을 그렸다. 길이 우묵한 골로 내려앉을 때 그 흙빛을 눈여겨보았고, 그러고 나서 그 길이 물길을 지나 건너편 언덕을 등지고 갈색으로, 거칠게, 고르지 않게, 아름답게 솟아오르는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그러고 나서는 앉아서 자기 오래된 집안을 생각했다. 그들이 저 콜디코츠 숲에서 아득한 옛날부터 어떻게 거닐었는지, 아버지와 아들과 손자가 차례를 어기지 않고 이어 가며 저마다 흠도 줄어듦도 없이 그것을 다음 사람에게 넘겨주었는지를. 그렇게 그는 앉아 있곤 했다. 그리고 지금도 그렇게 앉아, 이런 것들을 생각하면서 자기가 아예 태어나지 않았더라면 하고 바랐다.\n\n그가 집으로 돌아왔을 때는 캄캄한 밤이었다. 그리고 돌아오면서 그는 이곳을 아예 떠나고 이 싸움도 진 것으로 여겨 내놓기로 마음을 굳혔다. 공작이 그것을 가져가서 마음대로 하라지. 그리고 하원 의석은 덤벨로 경이든, 그만큼 재주를 타고난 다른 어느 젊은 귀족 자제든 자기 대신 차지하라지. 그는 이 무대에서 사라져 자기가 들리지도 보이지도 않을 어느 땅으로 몸을 옮겨, 거기서—굶주릴 것이었다. 이제 세상을 향한 그의 앞날은 그런 것이었다. 그러면서도 건강과 온갖 몸의 힘으로 말한다면 자기가 아직 한창때에 있다는 것을 그는 알았다. 그렇다, 한창때에! 그러나 그런 한창때에서 자기에게 남아 있는 것으로 그가 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가? 이제 도움이 될 만한 쪽으로 그가 어떻게 자기 머리나 자기 힘을 돌려 쓸 수 있겠는가? 이 뼈아픈 궁핍 속에서 그가 어떻게 제 손으로 가장 헐한 빵 한 조각이라도 벌 수 있겠는가? 그가 죽어 버리는 것이 그에게 더 낫지 않겠는가? 아무도 낭비꾼의 팔자를 부러워하지 말 것이다. 이른 완두와 샴페인의 날이 셀 수 없이 이어질 듯이 보인다 해도 그렇다. 저 절름거리는 네메시스가 놀음이 다 끝나기 전에 반드시 따라붙을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n사워비 씨가 집에 이르러 보니 그가 없는 동안 전보로 온 소식 하나가 그를 기다리고 있었다. 누이동생에게서 온 것으로, 그날 밤 그에게 오겠다는 소식이었다. 그녀는 우편 열차로 내려오는 중이고, 바체스터로 전보를 쳐서 역마를 잡아 두었으며, 자정을 두 시간쯤 지나 콜디코츠에 닿을 것이었다. 그러니 그녀의 볼일이 중대하다는 것은 넉넉히 드러나 있었다.\n\n두 시 정각에 바체스터 역마차가 과연 도착했고, 해럴드 스미스 부인은 자리에 들기 전에 한 시간쯤 오라비와 단둘이 문을 닫고 앉아 있었다.\n\n“그래요,” 이튿날 아침 두 사람이 조찬 식탁에 함께 앉았을 때 그녀가 말했다. “이제는 그 일을 어떻게 보세요? 그분의 제안을 받아들일 거라면 오늘 오후에는 그분의 변호사와 함께 있어야 해요.”\n\n“받아들여야 하겠지.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“물론이지요, 나도 그렇게 봐요. 그렇게 하면 공작이 가져갔을 때와 똑같이 온전히 그 재산이 오라버니 손에서 떠나가기는 하겠지만, 어쨌든 집은 오라버니가 사시는 동안 오라버니에게 남게 돼요.”\n\n“유지할 수도 없는 집이 무슨 소용인가?”\n\n“그런데 나는 그렇지 않다고 봐요. 그분은 정당한 이자보다 더 바라지 않을 거예요. 그리고 아주 잘 관리될 테니까 얼마쯤 남는 것이 있을 거라고 나는 생각해요—집을 유지할 만큼은 남을 거예요. 그리고 오라버니도 아시다시피, 우리도 시골에 어딘가 자리가 있어야 하잖아요.”\n\n“솔직히 말해 두겠네, 해리엇. 돈에 관한 일로는 해럴드와 다시는 아무 상관도 맺지 않겠어.”\n\n“아! 그건 오라버니가 굳이 그이에게 가려고 했기 때문이지요. 왜 나에게 오지 않으셨어요? 그리고요, 나다니엘, 이것 말고는 오라버니가 의석을 지킬 가망을 가질 길이 없어요. 그분은 내가 여태 만나 본 가운데 가장 별난 여인이지만, 공작을 이기기로 마음을 굳힌 것 같아요.”\n\n“나는 잘 이해가 되지 않지만, 조금도 반대할 것은 없네.”\n\n“그분은 공작이 왕실 소유지 일로 젊은 그레셤에게 간섭하고 있다고 여겨요. 나는 그분이 사무에 그렇게까지 밝은 줄은 몰랐어요. 내가 이 일을 처음 내놓았을 때 그분은 변호사가 그러듯이 그것을 받아 들었고, 그 다른 일에 있었던 것은 아예 다 잊어버린 듯이 보였어요.”\n\n“나도 그것을 잊을 수 있었으면 좋겠네.” 사워비 씨가 말했다.\n\n“나는 그분이 정말로 잊었다고 봐요. 내가 그 일을 얼마쯤 비쳐 말하지 않을 수 없었을 때—적어도 나로서는 그러지 않을 수 없다고 느꼈고, 그러고 나서는 그런 것을 뉘우쳤지요—그분은 그저 웃었어요. 늘 그러듯이 크게 소리 내어 웃고는, 그러고 나서 볼일 이야기를 이어 갔어요. 그렇지만 이것만은 분명히 해 두셨어요. 선거에 드는 비용은 전부 그분이 앞당겨 내주실 금액에 더해야 하고, 집은 아무런 임차료도 없이 오라버니에게 남겨 두어야 한다는 것이었어요. 집 둘레의 땅을 가지겠다면 소작인들이 하듯이 에이커 단위로 값을 치러야 해요. 그분은 평생을 변호사 사무실에서 지낸 사람인 것처럼 그 모든 것을 분명히 하셨어요.”\n\n이제 내 독자들은 그 훌륭한 누이동생 해럴드 스미스 부인이 오라비를 위해 마지막으로 어떤 걸음을 떼어 놓았는지 어지간히 다 이해할 것이며, 그날 낮 동안에 사워비 씨가 서둘러 런던으로 돌아가 던스터블 양의 변호사와 연락을 맺었다는 것을 알게 되어도 놀라지 않을 것이다.\n\n# 제38장. 혼인을 막을 까닭이나 마땅한 장애가 있는가?\n\n나는 이제 바셋주의 또 다른 시골 저택을 찾아가 볼 작정인데, 이번에 우리가 머물 곳은 주의 동부 지구다. 잉글랜드의 다른 어느 주와 마찬가지로 이곳에서도 지금은 선거 문제가 무엇보다 중대하다. 아들 그레셤 씨, 곧 늘 젊은 프랭크 그레셤이라 불리는 이가 박솔 힐이라는 곳에 산다는 이야기는 이미 나온 바 있다. 이 재산은 유언으로 그의 아내에게 넘어온 것이고, 그의 아버지가 아직 그레셤스베리에 있는 그레셤 집안의 본가를 지키고 있는 터라 그는 이제 그곳에 눌러살고 있었다.\n\n지금으로 말하면 던스터블 양은 박솔 힐에서 프랭크 그레셤 부인과 함께 지내고 있었다. 두 사람은 런던을 떠나 있었으니—사실 온 세상이 다 떠나 버려서 런던 상인들은 몹시 낙담하고 있었다. 이번 의회 해산은 시골 술집 주인들만 빼고 모두를 망치고 있었고, 다른 여러 가지와 더불어 런던 사교철도 물론 부수어 놓았다.\n\n해럴드 스미스 부인은 던스터블 양이 런던을 떠나기 전에 겨우 붙잡았다. 그러나 어쨌든 붙잡기는 했고, 그 큰 상속인은 곧바로 제 변호사들을 만나 콜디코츠 영지에 걸린 저당을 어떻게 다룰지 지시해 두었다. 던스터블 양은 저 자신과 저 자신의 금전 문제를 두고, 마치 그 관리에 자기가 좀처럼 끼어들지 못하는 사람인 양 말하는 습관이 있었다. 그러나 이것은 그녀가 늘 저지르는 자그마한 농의 하나였다. 실상 저 자신의 일을 던스터블 양만큼 속속들이 알고 저 자신의 일에 던스터블 양만큼 큰 목소리를 내는 부인은 거의 없었고, 신사라 해도 그리 많지 않았을 것이다. 근래의 사정이 그녀를 자주 바셋주로 데려왔고, 그녀는 그곳에서 아주 친밀한 우정을 맺었다. 그녀는 이제 될 수 있으면 바셋주의 토지 소유주가 되고 싶은 마음이 있어서, 그런 뜻으로 얼마 전 젊은 그레셤 씨와 왕실 소유 재산을 자기가 사들이기로 합의해 두었다. 다만 매입이 그의 이름으로 시작되었으므로 그대로 그의 이름으로 이어 가기로 했다. 그런데 이제, 우리가 아는 대로, 결국 공작이, 공작이 아니라면 덤벨로 후작이 체이스의 다음 주인이 될 것이라는 소문이 돌고 있었다. 그러나 던스터블 양은 이룰 수 있는 일이라면 뜻을 접는 사람이 아니었고, 게다가 이런 사정에서 사워비 몫의 콜디코츠 영지를 공작의 손아귀에서 건져 낼 힘이 제게 있음을 알게 된 것이 조금도 싫지 않았다. 공작이 무슨 까닭으로 그 다른 재산을 두고 자기를, 또는 자기 벗을 건드렸단 말인가? 그리하여 저당으로 공작에게 갚아야 할 돈은 전액 곧바로 치를 수 있게 마련해 두기로 했다. 그러나 던스터블 양이 쥐는 담보도 아주 확실해야 한다는 것 역시 함께 마련되었다.\n\n박솔 힐이나 그레셤스베리에 있는 던스터블 양은 런던에 있는 던스터블 양과 아주 다른 사람이었다. 그리고 그레셤 부인을 그토록 속상하게 한 것이 바로 이 다름이었다. 그 벗이 런던의 재치와 놀이에 능한 솜씨를 시골까지 가지고 오지 않는다는 것이 아니라, 시골에서 그녀를 사랑스럽게 만들어 주는 참된 선함과 정직을 사랑하는 마음을 런던으로 가지고 올라가지 않는다는 것이었다. 그녀는 말하자면 두 사람이었고, 그레셤 부인은 어떻게 한 부인이 한 해의 어느 철에는 다른 철보다, 또 어느 곳에서는 다른 어느 곳보다 더 세속적이 되도록 스스로를 놓아둘 수 있는지 이해할 수가 없었다.\n\n“아이참, 이봐요, 그걸 끝냈으니 나는 진심으로 기뻐요.” 박솔 힐에 닿은 다음 첫 아침, 응접실 책상 앞에 앉으며 던스터블 양이 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n“‘그걸’이 뭘 말하는 거예요?” 그레셤 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아, 런던하고 연기하고 늦은 밤들, 그리고 제집 계단 맨 위에 내처 네 시간을 서서 오고 싶어 하는 사람 아무에게나 절을 받는 일 말이에요. 그게 다 끝났어요—적어도 한 해 동안은요.”\n\n“좋아하시는 걸 아시잖아요.”\n\n“아니에요, 메리. 그게 바로 내가 모르는 거예요. 내가 그걸 좋아하는지 안 좋아하는지 나도 몰라요. 이따금 그 누구보다 다정한 여인, 해럴드 스미스 부인의 영이 내게 임하면 좋아하는 것 같기도 해요. 그러다가 또 다른 영들이 임하면 안 좋아하는 것 같고요.”\n\n“그러면 그 다른 영들의 임자는 누구예요?”\n\n“아! 당신도 물론 하나죠. 하지만 당신은 약하고 조그만 사람이라, 스미스 부인 같은 삼손과는 도무지 겨룰 수가 없어요. 게다가 당신도 알다시피 스스로 사악한 데로 조금 기운 사람이고요. 디베스의 식탁에 앉게 된 뒤로 당신은 런던을 어지간히 좋아하는 법을 배웠지요. 당신 삼촌—그이야말로 참으로 어찌해 볼 수 없고 가까이 갈 수도 없는 나사로여서, 큰 구렁이 있으니 내려올 수 없다고 말하는 사람이에요. 누가 그이에게 연 만 파운드를 물려주면 그이가 어떻게 처신할까요?”\n\n“보나 마나 아주 훌륭하게 처신하시겠죠.”\n\n“아, 그렇죠. 지금은 나사로이니 우리야 물론 좋게 말해야 하고요. 하지만 나는 그이가 시험받는 것을 보고 싶어요. 시험의 첫해가 지나기도 전에 벨그레이브 광장에 집을 얻고 조촐한 만찬으로 이름이 나 있을 게 틀림없어요.”\n\n“그럼 어때요? 그이가 은수자가 되기를 바라시는 건 아니잖아요?”\n\n“들리는 말로는 그이가 운을 시험해 보려 한다더군요—연 만 파운드로가 아니라 천이나 이천 파운드로요.”\n\n“무슨 말씀이세요?”\n\n“제인이 그러는데, 그레셤스베리에서는 다들 그이가 레이디 스캐처드와 결혼할 거라고 한다는군요.” 레이디 스캐처드는 그 근처에 사는 미망인이었다. 훌륭한 여인이었으나 가장 높은 축의 사교계를 빛낼 만하게 타고나지는 못한 사람이었다.\n\n“뭐라고요!” 그레셤 부인은 그런 소문에 노여움으로 눈을 번득이며 의자에서 일어서면서 소리쳤다.\n\n“참, 이봐요, 나를 잡아먹지는 말아요. 그렇다는 게 아니라, 제인이 그랬다는 말만 하는 거예요.”\n\n“그러면 제인을 집에서 내보내셔야죠.”\n\n“이건 믿어도 좋아요, 이봐요. 누가 제인에게 말해 주지 않았다면 제인이 내게 말했을 리 없어요.”\n\n“그래서 그 말을 믿으셨어요?”\n\n“그 얘기는 하지 않았어요.”\n\n“하지만 믿으신 얼굴인데요.”\n\n“내가요? 그럼 그게 어떤 얼굴인지 봅시다, 이 믿는 얼굴이라는 게.” 그러고서 던스터블 양은 일어나 난로 위 거울로 갔다. “그런데 메리, 사람의 얼굴을 믿어서는 안 된다는 것쯤은 알 만한 나이가 됐잖아요? 요즘 세상에는 아무것도 믿을 게 없어요. 그리고 나는 딱한 레이디 스캐처드에 관한 그 이야기를 믿지 않았어요. 선생님이 결혼하는 남자가 아니라는 걸 확실히 알 만큼은 내가 선생님을 잘 알아요.”\n\n“그건 참 고약하고 케케묵고 거짓된 말이에요—결혼하는 남자라니! 어떤 남자들은 한 달에 세 번 네 번 결혼하는 습관이라도 있는 것처럼 들리잖아요.”\n\n“그래도 그 말에는 담긴 뜻이 많아요. 한 남자가 결혼할 만한 사람인지 아닌지는 아주 금방 알 수 있어요.”\n\n“여자에 대해서도 같은 것을 알 수 있나요?”\n\n“그건 사정이 아주 달라요. 시집 안 간 여자라면 누구나 어쩔 수 없이 장에 나온 셈이지요. 하지만 처신을 바르게 하면 아무 기색도 내지 않아요. 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 보세요. 물론 그 아이는 남편을 얻을 작정이었고, 아주 대단한 남편을 얻었지요. 그러면서도 늘 입에 물린 버터도 녹지 않을 듯한 낯빛이었어요. 그 아이를 결혼하는 처녀라고 부르는 건 아주 잘못된 일이었을 거예요.”\n\n“아, 그야 물론 그랬죠.” 그레셤 부인은, 한 예쁜 젊은 부인이 다른 예쁜 젊은 부인을 두고 곧잘 드러내는 그런 앙칼진 투로 말한다. “하지만 당신이 남자에 대해서 늘 알 수 있다고 하듯이 여자에 대해서도 늘 알 수 있다면, 나는 당신에 대해서도 알 수 있어야 할 텐데요. 그런데 당신이 결혼하는 여자인지 어떤지는 나도 아직 마음을 정하지 못했어요.”\n\n던스터블 양은 잠깐 말이 없었다. 처음에는 그 물음을 어느 모로 진심에서 나온 것으로 받아들일 마음이었던 듯했다. 그러나 곧 웃음으로 넘겨 버리려 했다. “참, 그건 뜻밖이네요.” 그녀가 말했다. “바로 며칠 전에 내가 청혼을 몇 번이나 거절했는지 말해 줬는데 말이에요.”\n\n“네, 하지만 받아들일 마음이 든 청혼이 있었는지는 말해 주지 않으셨죠.”\n\n“그런 청혼은 한 번도 받아 본 적이 없어요. 그 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 나는 당신 사촌인 아너러블 조지를 결코 잊지 못할 거예요.”\n\n“그이는 내 사촌이 아니에요.”\n\n“그럼 남편 쪽 사촌이죠. 남자의 편지를 보여 주는 건 온당하지 않겠지만, 그이 편지는 당신에게 보여 주고 싶네요.”\n\n“그러면 혼자 지내기로 마음을 굳히신 거예요?”\n\n“그렇다고는 안 했어요. 그런데 왜 나를 이렇게 따져 물으세요?”\n\n“당신 생각을 그만큼 많이 하니까요. 나는 당신이 남자들의 속셈을 너무 겁내다가 아무도 정직할 수 있다는 것을 의심하게 될까 봐 걱정이에요. 그리고 또 이따금 나는 당신이 결혼하면 더 행복한 여인, 더 나은 여인이 될 거라는 생각도 해요.”\n\n“예컨대 아너러블 조지 같은 사람에게 가서요?”\n\n“아니에요, 그런 사람은 아니고요. 당신은 아마 가장 나쁜 예를 골라내셨네요.”\n\n“아니면 사워비 씨에게요?”\n\n“아니, 그것도 아니에요. 사워비 씨도 아니에요. 나는 당신이 주로 당신 돈을 바라보는 남자와 결혼하는 건 원하지 않아요.”\n\n“그러면 내가 어떻게 남더러 나를 두고 주로 다른 무엇을 바라보라고 바랄 수 있겠어요? 당신은 내 딱한 처지를 못 보시는군요, 이봐요? 내게 연 오백 파운드밖에 없다면, 나 같은 점잖은 중년 인물을 어디서 만날 수도 있겠지요. 그이는 나라는 사람 자체를 어지간히 좋아하고, 내 자그마한 수입도—역시 어지간히 좋아할 거예요. 심한 거짓말은 하지 않을 테고, 어쩌면 거짓말이라고는 하나도 안 할지도 몰라요. 나도 같은 식으로 그이에게 마음이 갈 테고, 우리는 아주 잘 지낼 수 있을 거예요. 그런데 지금 형편으로는, 사심 없는 사람이 나를 좋아하게 되는 일이 어떻게 있을 수 있겠어요? 그런 남자가 어떻게 그 일을 시작하겠어요? 양에게 머리가 둘 달렸다면, 머리가 둘이라는 사실이야말로 세상이 그 양에게서 맨 처음, 아니 실상 오직 하나 보는 것이 아니겠어요? 그것이 당연한 일이 아니겠어요? 나는 머리 둘 달린 양이에요. 우리 아버지가 모아 놓았고 그 뒤로 오월 소나기 맞은 풀처럼 자라 온 이 돈이 나를 기형으로 만들어 놓았어요. 나는 키가 여덟 피트나 되는 여자 거인도 아니고, 사람 손바닥 위에 올라서는 난쟁이도 아니지만—”\n\n“아니면 머리 둘 달린 양도—”\n\n“하지만 나는 돈이—어떤 사람들이 믿는 듯한 대로 육백만 파운드는 되는—시집 안 간 여자예요. 그런 형편에서, 머리 하나 달린 여느 짐승처럼 뜯어먹을 내 몫의 달콤한 풀을 얻을 가망이 나에게 공평하게 있겠어요? 나는 그리 아름다운 적이 없었고, 지금은 열다섯 해 전보다 더 아름다울 것도 없어요.”\n\n“그걸 막고 있는 게 그것이 아니라는 건 나도 잘 알아요. 스스로를 못생겼다고는 하지 않으시겠죠. 그리고 못생긴 여자들도 날마다 결혼하고, 예쁜 여자들만큼이나 사랑도 받아요.”\n\n“그래요? 그럼 그 얘기는 그만해요. 하지만 나는 내 아름다움 덕분에 사랑하는 이들이 많이 몰려올 거라고는 기대하지 않아요. 혹시 그런 이가 있다는 얘기를 들으면 꼭 알려 줘요.”\n\n그레셤 부인의 혀끝에는, 자기가 그런 이 하나를 알고 있다는 말이 거의 올라와 있었다—삼촌을 두고 하는 말이었다. 그러나 사실 그녀는 그런 것을 알고 있는 게 아니었다. 또 그것이 그렇다고 느낄 만한 좋은 근거가 자기에게 있다고 스스로 자랑할 수도 없었다. 그것을 입에 올려도 될 만큼 넉넉한 근거는 확실히 없었다. 삼촌은 그 일에 관해 그녀에게 한마디도 하지 않았고, 그런 혼인 이야기가 그에게 비쳐졌을 때에는 어리둥절해하고 난처해했다. 그러나 그럼에도 그레셤 부인은 이 두 사람이 각기 서로를 사랑할 마음이 넉넉히 있으며, 둘이 함께 있으면 혼자 있을 때보다 더 행복할 것이라고 생각했다. 그러나 어려움은 몹시 컸다. 선생은 던스터블 양의 돈을 탐낸다는 소리를 들을까 몹시 두려워할 것이고, 그렇다고 그녀가 선생에게 먼저 말을 꺼내도록 이끌 수 있으리라고는 좀처럼 기대할 수 없었다.\n\n“내가 생각해 낼 수 있는 사람으로 당신에게 조금이라도 맞을 남자는 우리 삼촌뿐이에요.” 그레셤 부인이 대담하게 말했다.\n\n“뭐라고요, 딱한 레이디 스캐처드의 것을 빼앗으라고요!” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n“아, 좋아요. 그런 식으로 삼촌 이름을 두고 농을 하시겠다면 나는 그만하겠어요.”\n\n“아이고, 이 아가씨 좀 보게! 그럼 나더러 무슨 말을 하라는 거예요? 그리고 농이라고 해도, 이만하면 해롭지 않잖아요. 당신은 선생님이 열일곱 살 처녀라도 되는 것처럼 그이 얘기에 조심스러워하네요.”\n\n“삼촌 때문이 아니에요. 딱하고 다정한 레이디 스캐처드를 두고 웃는 게 너무 부끄러운 일이라서요. 그이가 그 말을 들으면 삼촌이 가까이 있는 것에서 얻는 위안을 다 잃어버릴 거예요.”\n\n“그래서 내가 그이와 결혼해야 한다고요, 그이가 벗과 함께 마음 놓고 있게 하려고요!”\n\n“좋아요. 나는 그만하겠어요.” 그러고서 이미 자리에서 일어나 있던 그레셤 부인은 응접실 탁자에 놓으려고 들여온 꽃을 매만지는 일에 아주 부지런히 매달렸다. 그렇게 두 사람은 일이 분 동안 말이 없었고, 그동안 그녀는 결국 자기 역시 삼촌을 위해 그 큰 상속인을 붙잡으려 애쓰는 것으로 여겨질지도 모른다는 생각을 하기 시작했다.\n\n“이제 나한테 노여워하는군요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n“아니에요.”\n\n“아니라니, 노여워하고 있어요. 사람이 속상해하는 걸 내가 못 알아볼 만큼 어리석다고 생각해요? 마음이 제대로 가라앉아 있었다면 그 제라늄 꽃대가리를 그렇게 홱 뜯어내지 않았을 거예요.”\n\n“레이디 스캐처드에 관한 그 농이 싫어요.”\n\n“그게 다예요, 메리? 이제 될 수 있으면 참되게 굴어 봐요. 주교님 기억하죠? 「마그나 에스트 베리타스」.”\n\n“사실은 당신이 런던의 벗들 사이에서 날카롭게 굴고 날카로운 말을 하는 데 하도 익숙해져서, 그러지 않고는 남에게 대답 한마디 못 하게 된 거예요.”\n\n“내가 그래요? 아이고, 아이고, 메리, 당신은 어쩌면 그런 멘토르인지! 옥스퍼드에서 런던으로 놀러 올라온 딱한 젊은이도 그 방탕과 악행 때문에 나만큼 훈계를 들은 적은 없을 거예요. 자, 손 선생님께 용서를 빌고 레이디 스캐처드께도 빌어요. 앞으로는 날카롭게 굴지 않을 테고, 그리고 나는—어디 보자, 내가 무엇을 해야 했지요? 내가 그이와 결혼하는 것, 그거였죠?”\n\n“아니에요. 당신은 삼촌한테 반만큼도 어울리지 않아요.”\n\n“그건 나도 알아요. 아주 잘 알고 있어요. 이렇게 날카롭게 굴어도 나는 아주 겸손해요. 내가 스스로에게 아주 큰 값을 매긴다고는 나무라지 못할 거예요.”\n\n“아마 매겨야 할 만큼도 못 매기시는 거죠—당신 자신에게요.”\n\n“그건 무슨 뜻이에요, 메리? 나는 몰아세우는 것도, 놀리는 것도, 빈정대는 암시를 던지는 것도 참지 않겠어요. 당신 마음속에 무언가 있는데 그것을 똑바로 말할 용기가 나지 않아서 그러는 거잖아요. 할 말이 있으면 말해요.”\n\n그러나 그레셤 부인은 그 순간에 그것을 말하기로 마음먹지 않았다. 그녀는 입을 다물고 계속 꽃을 매만졌다—이제는 좀 더 흡족한 낯빛으로, 제라늄을 못살게 굴지도 않고. 그리고 꽃 다발을 알맞게 무리 지어 놓고는 화병을 방 이쪽에서 저쪽으로 앞뒤로 옮겨 가며 빛깔의 어울림을 살펴보았다. 마치 마음이 온통 꽃에만 쏠려 있어서 그 순간에는 다른 어떤 일에도 조금도 매여 있지 않은 듯이.\n\n그러나 던스터블 양은 이런 것을 참고 넘길 부인이 아니었다. 그녀는 제 자리에 잠자코 앉아 벗이 방을 한두 바퀴 도는 것을 지켜보다가, 자기도 자리에서 일어섰다. “메리,” 그녀가 말했다. “그 지긋지긋한 푸른 가지 조각들은 그만두고 화병은 그냥 그 자리에 두세요. 나를 안달나게 해서 화를 내게 만들려는 거잖아요.”\n\n“내가요?” 그레셤 부인은 큰 사발 앞에 마주 서서, 그 자리에서 자기 솜씨를 더 잘 볼 수 있다는 듯 고개를 조금 옆으로 기울이며 말했다.\n\n“그러고 있는 걸 알잖아요. 그러는 건 다 당신이 똑바로 말할 용기가 없어서예요. 괜히 이런 식으로 나를 걸고 나선 게 아니잖아요.”\n\n“나는 용기가 없어요. 바로 그거예요.” 그레셤 부인은 여전히 꽃다발의 배경이 되는 작은 잔가지들을 여기서 한 번 비틀고 저기서 한 번 바로잡으며 말했다. “나는 용기가 없어요—나쁜 속셈을 뒤집어쓸까 봐서요. 무언가 말할 생각이었는데, 두려우니까 말하지 않겠어요. 그리고 이제, 원하신다면 십 분 뒤에 모시고 나갈 준비가 돼요.”\n\n그러나 던스터블 양은 이런 식으로 물러설 사람이 아니었다. 그리고 사실을 말하자면, 그녀의 벗 그레셤 부인이 그녀를 도무지 온당하게 대하고 있지 않았음을 나는 시인해야 한다. 그레셤 부인은 이 일에 관해 아예 입을 다물고 있어야 했거나—그것이 아마 더 슬기로운 길이었을 것이다—아니면 제 속셈에 관해 제 양심이 든든하다고 믿고서 제 생각을 대담하게 밝혀야 했다. “나는 이 방에서 한 발도 움직이지 않겠어요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “당신과 이 일을 끝까지 따져 보기 전에는요. 그리고 뒤집어쓴다는 것 말인데—내가 당신에게 나쁜 속셈을 씌운다는 것 말이에요—당신이 어디까지 농을 하고 있고 어디까지 이른바 날카로운 말을 하고 있는지는 나도 모르겠어요. 하지만 내가 당신을 나쁘게 여길 거라고 당신이 생각할 권리는 없어요. 정말로 그렇게 생각한다면, 그것은 내가 당신에게 품은 사랑에 대한 반역이에요. 당신이 그렇게 생각한다고 내가 생각한다면, 나는 당신과 한집에 머물러 있을 수 없어요. 어쩌면! 참된 벗과 겉치레 벗 사이에 사람이 두는 차이를 당신이 알지 못한다니! 나는 당신에게 그런 것이 있다고 믿지 않아요. 그리고 당신이 나를 몰아세우려고만 하고 있다는 것도 알아요.” 그러고서 이번에는 던스터블 양이 방을 오르내리며 걸었다.\n\n“그래요, 이 사람은 몰아세우지 않기로 해요.” 그레셤 부인은 꽃을 놓고 벗의 허리에 팔을 두르며 말했다. “적어도 여기 이 집 안에서는요. 정작 저도 때로는 어지간히 몰아세우는 사람이면서 말이에요.”\n\n“메리, 당신은 이 일에 관해 되돌리기에는 너무 멀리 갔어요. 마음에 있던 것이 무엇인지 말해요. 그것이 나에 관한 것인 만큼 나도 정직하게 대답하겠어요.”\n\n그레셤 부인은 이제 그 자그마한 시도를 한 것을 후회하기 시작했다. 반쯤 농으로 암시를 던지는 것은 아주 좋았고, 어쩌면 제 쪽에서 격식을 갖춘 이야기를 꺼낼 필요 없이도 바라던 결과를 가져올 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 이제는 이렇게 따져 묻는 자리에 서게 된 터라 격식을 갖춘 무언가를 말해야 했다. 제가 바라는 바를 밝혀 스스로 매여야 하고, 아울러 제 벗이 무엇을 바랐던가에 관한 의견까지 밝혀야 했다. 게다가 그 제삼자가 무엇을 바라는지에 관해서는 한마디도 할 수 없는 채로 그것을 해야 했다.\n\n“그럼,” 그녀가 말했다. “내가 무슨 말을 하려 했는지 아시겠죠.”\n\n“알 것 같아요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “하지만 그렇다고 당신이 그것을 입 밖에 내야 할 필요가 조금이라도 줄어드는 건 아니에요. 당신은 제 생각을 암시만 해 놓고 아주 안전하게 있으면서, 나만 당신 생각을 풀이하다가 매이는 건 안 돼요. 나는 암시라는 게 싫어요, 꼭―고약한 것만큼이나. 나는 주교님의 교리를 따르겠어요. 「마그나 에스트 베리타스」.”\n\n“글쎄, 나는 모르겠어요.” 그레셤 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아! 하지만 나는 알아요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “그러니 말을 잇든지, 아니면 영원히 입을 다물든지 하세요.”\n\n“바로 그거예요.” 그레셤 부인이 말했다.\n\n“무엇이 바로 그거예요?” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n“방금 당신이 끝맺은 그 기도서의 구절이요. ‘여러분 가운데 이 두 사람이 성혼으로 맺어짐을 막을 까닭이나 마땅한 장애를 아는 이가 있으면, 그것을 알려야 합니다. 이것이 첫 번째 공시입니다.’ 던스터블 양, 무슨 까닭을 알고 있나요?”\n\n“그레셤 부인, 당신은 무슨 까닭을 알고 있나요?”\n\n“제 명예를 걸고 하나도 없어요!” 젊은 쪽 부인이 손을 가슴에 얹으며 말했다.\n\n“아! 그런데 정말 없어요?” 그러면서 던스터블 양은 그녀의 팔을 붙잡고, 힘이 들어간 나머지 거의 불쑥 말했다.\n\n“네, 정말 없어요. 무슨 장애가 있겠어요? 있다면 이 이야기를 꺼내지도 않았을 거예요. 나는 두 분이 함께 아주 행복할 거라고 정말 생각해요. 물론 장애가 하나 있지요. 그건 우리 모두 알아요. 그건 당신이 알아서 살펴야 할 일이에요.”\n\n“무슨 말이에요? 무슨 장애요?”\n\n“당신 자신의 돈이요.”\n\n“쳇! 당신은 프랭크 그레셤과 결혼할 때 그것이 장애가 되었나요?”\n\n“아! 거기서는 사정이 아주 달랐어요. 다 헤아려 보면 내가 내놓을 것보다 그이가 내놓을 것이 훨씬 많았어요. 그리고 우리가—우리가 처음 약혼했을 때 나에게는 돈이 없었어요.” 그러면서 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 젊은 날 사랑의 사정을 떠올린 것이었다. 그 사정은 모두 바셋주 연대기에 적혀 있으니, 이제 그것에 마음이 끌리는 남자와 여자라면 누구든 읽어 볼 수 있을 것이다.\n\n“그래요, 당신 것은 사랑으로 맺은 혼사였죠. 참말이지 메리, 나는 당신이 내가 들어 본 가운데 가장 행복한 여인이라는 생각을 자주 해요. 아직 아무것도 없었을 때 사랑받고 있다는 것을 그토록 확실히 알았고, 그러고서 온 것을 다 내줄 수 있었으니 말이에요.”\n\n“네, 나는 확실히 알았어요.” 그러고서 그녀는 눈에서 그 달콤한 눈물을 닦았다. 어떤 젊은이가 아주 단단히 마음먹은 태도로 온갖 권리를 주장하며 자기에게 찾아온 어느 날을 떠올린 것이었다. 그때 그녀는 상속인이 아니었다. “네, 나는 확실히 알았어요. 하지만 이제 당신은, 이봐요, 스스로를 다시 가난하게 만들 수는 없어요. 아무도 믿을 수 없다면—”\n\n“믿을 수 있어요. 그이는 믿을 수 있어요. 그것에 관해서라면 나는 그이를 온전히 믿어요. 하지만 그이가 나를 마음에 두어 줄지 내가 어떻게 알겠어요?”\n\n“그이가 당신을 좋아하는 걸 모르세요?”\n\n“아, 알아요. 그리고 레이디 스캐처드도 좋아하지요.”\n\n“던스터블 양!”\n\n“왜 레이디 스캐처드는 안 되고 나는 되나요? 우리는 같은 종류예요—같은 계층에서 나왔고요.”\n\n“그건 좀 아니라고 생각해요.”\n\n“아니에요, 같은 계층이에요. 다만 나는 공작과 공작부인들 사이에 억지로 끼어들었고, 그이는 하느님이 두신 자리에 그대로 있는 것으로 만족했을 뿐이지요. 내가 기교에서 그이를 앞선다면, 그이는 천성에서 나를 앞서요.”\n\n“터무니없는 소리를 하고 있는 걸 아시잖아요.”\n\n“우리 둘이 다 그러고 있다고 생각해요—순전히 터무니없는 소리를요. 열여덟 살 여학생들이 서로 주고받는 것 같은 소리 말이에요. 그런데 그게 마음이 놓이기도 하잖아요? 늘 이치에 닿는 소리만 해야 한다면 그건 무서운 저주일 거예요. 자, 그건 다 됐어요. 이제 나가요.”\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤 그레셤 부인은 자기가 마음에 두고 있는 그 자그마한 계획에 던스터블 양이 동의할 당사자가 될 것이라고 확신했다. 그러나 그 점은 이미 꽤 오래전부터 거의 의심하지 않고 있었다. 어려움은 다른 쪽에 있었고, 그녀가 지금까지 해낸 것이라고는, 삼촌이 그 일을 맡아 나서기만 한다면 성공을 틀림없이 약속해 줄 수 있다고 스스로 확인한 것뿐이었다. 삼촌은 그날 저녁 박솔 힐로 와서 하루 이틀 머물기로 되어 있었다. 이 일에 무언가 할 수 있는 것이 있다면 지금이 바로 그것을 할 때였다. 적어도 그레셤 부인은 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n선생은 정말로 왔고, 정해진 만큼 박솔 힐에 머물렀다. 그러나 그가 떠날 때 그레셤 부인은 성공하지 못했다. 사실 그는 여느 때와 달리 이번 방문을 즐기는 것 같지도 않았다. 그리고 얼마 전부터 그와 던스터블 양 사이에 늘 있던 그 즐겁고 다정한 오고 감이 아주 적었다. 둘 사이에 티격태격하는 일도 없었다. 그 부인의 런던 흥청거림을 두고 선생이 야단치는 일도, 선생의 시골 습관을 두고 그 부인이 놀리는 일도 없었다. 두 사람은 서로 아주 예의를 차렸고, 그레셤 부인의 생각으로는 지나칠 만큼 예의를 차렸다. 또 그녀가 볼 수 있었던 한, 선생이 머문 그 기간 내내 둘이 단둘이서 오 분씩이라도 함께 있는 일이 없었다. 어쩌면, 하고 그레셤 부인은 속으로 생각했다—두 벗을 가장 가깝고 가장 오래갈 우정으로 묶어 주기는커녕 내가 이 둘을 서로 틀어지게 해 놓은 것이라면 어쩌나!\n\n그러나 그녀는 여전히, 이 놀음을 시작한 이상 끝까지 놀아야 한다는 생각을 품고 있었다. 자기가 한 일이 좋은 결과로 이어지도록 끌고 가지 못한다면 반드시 나쁜 일이 되고 말리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 아니, 그렇게 해내지 못한다면 던스터블 양에게 제 생각과 감정을 밝히게 만들어 그녀에게 뚜렷한 해를 입힌 셈이 될 것이었다. 그녀는 이미 런던에서 삼촌에게 말을 해 두었고, 삼촌은 제 계획을 찬성한다는 말도 하지 않았지만 반대한다는 말도 하지 않았다. 그러므로 그녀는 그 사흘 내내 삼촌이 무슨 기색이라도—적어도 자기에게라도—보여 주기를 바라고 있었다. 이 일에 관해 삼촌 자신의 생각이 무엇인지 어떻게든 밝혀 주기를 바라고 있었다. 그러나 떠나는 날 아침이 왔는데도 삼촌은 아무것도 밝히지 않았다.\n\n“삼촌,” 삼촌이 이미 던스터블 양에게 작별을 하고 그레셤 부인과 악수까지 한 뒤, 그가 그곳에 머무는 마지막 오 분 동안에 그녀가 말했다. “런던에서 제가 드린 말씀을 생각해 보신 적이 있으세요?”\n\n“그래, 메리. 물론 생각해 봤다. 그런 생각이란 게 사람 머릿속에 한번 들어오면 저절로 생각하게 되는 거니까.”\n\n“그럼요? 그래서 다음은요? 저한테 말씀 좀 해 보세요. 그렇게 딱딱하게, 삼촌답지 않게 굴지 마세요.”\n\n“그 일에 관해 할 말이 별로 없다.”\n\n“이건 확실히 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 삼촌이 마음만 있으시면 그렇게 되실 수 있어요.”\n\n“메리! 메리!”\n\n“삼촌을 곤란에 빠뜨리는 게 아니라고 확신하지 않는다면 이런 말씀 안 드려요.”\n\n“이런 것을 바라는 건 어리석다, 메리. 늙은 사람을 꾀어 어리석은 짓을 하게 하려는 것도 어리석지.”\n\n“두 분이 다 더 행복해지실 거라는 걸 제가 아는데도 어리석은 건가요?”\n\n그는 그녀에게 더 대답하지 않았다. 늘 하던 대로 그녀가 입 맞출 수 있도록 몸을 굽히고서 길을 떠났고, 아무 소용도 없이 이 물을 다 흐려 놓았다는 생각에 그녀를 거의 참담한 마음으로 남겨 두었다. 던스터블 양은 자기를 어떻게 생각할까? 그러나 그날 오후 던스터블 양은 여느 때처럼 행복하고 마음이 고른 듯이 보였다.\n\n# 제39장. 연애 편지를 어떻게 쓰는가\n\n손 선생은 박솔 힐을 떠나기 전에 조카딸에게 던진 몇 마디 말에서 스스로를 늙은이라 불렀다. 그러나 그는 아직 예순의 이쪽 편으로 넉넉히 다섯 해는 남아 있었고, 몸에 지닌 나이의 흔적은 쉰다섯 먹은 사내들 대부분이 지니는 것보다도 적었다. 그를 바라본 사람이라면 누구나, 그 걸음이 그에게 좋게 여겨진다면 혼인을 하지 못할 까닭이 없다고 말했을 것이다. 그리고 신부로 점쳐진 이의 나이를 보더라도, 그 점에서 걸맞지 않은 것은 아무것도 없었다.\n\n그러면서도 그는 조카딸이 내놓은 그 제안을 생각이라도 하도록 스스로에게 허락한 일이 거의 부끄러웠다. 그날 그는 박솔 힐에서 말에 올라—주 안을 돌아다니는 길은 모두 말을 타고 다녔으므로—천천히 그레셤스베리의 집으로 향했는데, 그러면서 생각한 것은 권해진 혼사보다도 그것을 생각하고 있는 제 어리석음이었다. 이 나이에 이르러 제 길의 고른 걸음이 그런 생각 하나에 흐트러지도록 내버려 두다니, 그렇게 얼빠진 짓이 있을 수 있는가? 물론 그는 던스터블 양 같은 사람을 아내로 삼겠다고 스스로에게 내놓으면서 그 재산에 대한 생각을 조금도 하지 않을 수는 없었다. 그런데 그가 돈에 무심하다는 것을 세상이 알아주도록 살아온 것이야말로 그 평생의 자랑이었다. 직업이 그에게는 전부였다. 그가 먹는 빵이자 그가 숨 쉬는 공기였다. 그런데 이런 혼인을 하고서 어떻게 직업을 이어 나가겠는가? 그녀는 그가 자기와 함께 런던으로 가기를 바랄 터였다. 그러면 거기서 그녀의 뒤꽁무니에 매달린 채 세상에는 그 도시에서 가장 부유한 여인의 남편으로만 알려진 그는 무엇이 되겠는가? 그런 종류의 삶은 그에게 걸맞지 않은 것이었다. 그런데도 집으로 말을 몰아 가는 동안 그는 그 생각을 떨쳐 버리기로 마음을 정할 수가 없었다. 그는 그것을 계속 생각했고, 그러면서도 그것을 생각 속에 두고 있다고 스스로를 계속 나무랐다. 그 밤에 집에서 마음을 정하리라, 그렇게 그는 스스로에게 못을 박았다. 그리고 그때 조카딸에게 편지를 써서 그 이야기는 그만 접어 달라고 부탁하리라 하였다. 그렇게까지 결심에 이르고 나서, 그는 자기와 던스터블 양이 끝내 부부가 된다면 어떤 삶의 길을 걷는 것이 좋을지 골똘히 생각해 나갔다.\n\n돌아온 날에 그가 꼭 보아야 할 부인이 둘 있었다. 사실 그레셤스베리를 떠나 있을 때가 아니면 그는 대개 날마다 그 둘을 보았다. 그 가운데 첫째는—그 고장 사람들이 대체로 헤아리는 순서로 첫째는—지주의 아내인 레이디 애러벨라 그레셤으로, 선생의 아주 오래된 환자였다. 그녀는 오후 이른 때에 찾는 것이 그의 버릇이었다. 그러고 나서 지주가 날마다 하는 저녁 초대를 벗어날 수 있으면, 자기 집에서 급히 밥을 먹고 나서 나머지 한 사람, 곧 레이디 스캐처드에게 가는 것이 예사였다. 적어도 여름철 그의 관례는 그러하였다.\n\n“그래, 선생, 박솔 힐 사람들은 어떠하오?” 지주가 문 앞 자갈길에서 그를 붙잡아 세우고 말했다. 이 여름 몇 달 동안 지주는 할 일을 찾기가 몹시 어려웠다.\n\n“다들 잘 있는 것 같아요.”\n\n“프랭크에게 무슨 일이 있는지 모르겠소. 이제는 이곳을 싫어하는 게 아닌가 싶소. 선거 생각으로 머리가 가득한 게지.”\n\n“아, 예. 곧 이리 오겠다고 전해 달라더군요. 물론 겨룰 상대가 없을 테니 애쓸 일도 없지요.”\n\n“복 많은 녀석 아니오, 선생? 그 모든 것이 등 뒤가 아니라 앞에 놓여 있으니. 그래, 그래. 그만큼 착한 아이는 세상에 없었소,—없었지. 그리고 어디 보자, 메리의 산달이…” 그러고 나서 그 문제를 두고 아주 중요한 몇 마디가 오갔다.\n\n“이제 레이디 애러벨라께 잠깐 올라가 보겠어요.” 선생이 말했다.\n\n“더할 수 없이 짜증이 나 있소.” 지주가 말했다. “방금 내가 나왔소.”\n\n“특별히 잘못된 데는 없겠지요?”\n\n“없소, 없는 것 같소. 그러니까 선생 쪽으로 올 일은 없다는 말이오. 다만 유별나게 뾰로통해 있는데, 그건 늘 내 쪽으로 오지. 오늘은 물론 남아서 저녁을 하겠지요?”\n\n“오늘은 안 되겠어요, 지주님.”\n\n“터무니없는 소리. 하실 거요. 나는 꽤나 선생을 믿고 있었소. 오늘 선생을 붙들고 싶은 각별한 까닭이 있소,—아주 각별한 까닭이오.” 그러나 지주에게는 늘 각별한 까닭이 있었다.\n\n“참 미안하지만 오늘은 안 될 일이에요. 편지 한 통을 써야 하는데, 자리를 잡고 앉아 진지하게 써야 하는 것이라서요. 마님 방에서 내려오면 뵐 수 있을까요?”\n\n지주는 대답도 거의 하지 않고 뿌루퉁하게 돌아섰다. 이제 그 저녁의 지루함을 덜어 줄 가망이 없어졌기 때문이다. 그리고 선생은 이 층으로 환자에게 올라갔다.\n\n레이디 애러벨라는 앓고 있다고 말할 수는 없어도 늘 환자였다. 그녀가 자리에 누워 날마다 약을 복용하고 있었다거나, 그 심심한 삶의 길에 이따금 찾아드는 심심한 즐거움에 한몫 끼는 것을 막혔다고 여겨서는 안 된다. 다만 병자로 있는 것과 의사를 두는 것이 그녀의 마음결에 맞았고, 좋은 운수가 그녀의 팔꿈치 옆에 놓아 준 그 의사가 그녀의 경우를 속속들이 이해하고 있었으므로 크게 해로울 것은 없었다.\n\n“메리를 보러 갈 수 없다는 게 참 몹시도 애가 타요.” 그녀의 병세를 두고 으레 묻는 첫 물음이 오가고 나자 곧 레이디 애러벨라가 말했다.\n\n“아주 잘 있고, 오래지 않아 뵈러 올 거예요.”\n\n“그러지 말라고 내가 부탁하겠어요. 아무 지장이 없을 때에는 올 생각을 도무지 하지 않다가, 지금 같은 때에 길을 나서는 건—” 그러고서 레이디 애러벨라는 아주 심각하게 고개를 저었다. “그게 얼마나 중대한 일인지 생각해 보세요, 선생.” 그녀가 말했다. “걸려 있는 것이 얼마나 큰지 잊지 마세요.”\n\n“걸려 있는 것이 두 배로 크다 해도 그 아이에게는 반 페니어치도 해롭지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“터무니없는 소리, 선생, 내게 그런 말은 하지 마세요. 내가 나를 모를 리가 없잖아요. 나는 그 아이가 이번 봄에 런던으로 가는 걸 몹시 반대했지만, 물론 내가 한 말은 뒤로 밀렸어요. 늘 그렇지요. 그레셤 씨는 그 아이더러 가라고 부추기려고 일부러 박솔 힐까지 건너갔다고 나는 믿어요. 하지만 그이가 무엇을 마음에 두겠어요? 프랭크는 좋아하지요. 그렇지만 오늘 하루 너머를 내다볼 생각은 도무지 하지 않아요. 그이는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었지요, 선생도 잘 알잖아요.”\n\n“그 나들이가 그 아이에게는 세상 무엇보다 좋았어요.” 손 선생은 지주의 죄를 두고 이야기하기보다는 무엇이든 나은 편이라 여기며 말했다.\n\n“내가 그런 몸이었을 때에는 그런 나들이가 나한테 무슨 좋은 일이 될 거라고들 여기지 않았던 게 아주 잘 기억나요. 하지만 그때 뒤로 세상이 달라졌을지도 모르지요.”\n\n“예, 달라졌어요.” 선생이 말했다. “요즘은 우리가 그렇게까지 간섭하지 않아요.”\n\n“그렇게 많은 것이 조용히 있는 데 달려 있을 때에 내가 그런 재미를 청한 적이 없다는 건 나도 알아요. 프랭크가 태어나기 전 일이 기억나요—사실 그 아이들이 하나하나 태어날 때마다 그러했지요— 하지만 선생 말대로 그때는 세상이 달랐지요. 그리고 메리가 제 뜻을 꼭 이루려고 마음먹는 사람이라는 건 나도 어렵지 않게 믿어요.”\n\n“아니, 레이디 애러벨라, 프랭크가 새끼손가락을 하나 들어 보이기만 했어도 그 아이는 꼼짝할 생각 없이 집에 있었을 거예요.”\n\n“나도 늘 그러했어요. 그레셤 씨가 아주 작은 눈치라도 보이면 나는 물러섰지요. 하지만 그런 고분고분한 순종에 대해 무엇을 되받는지 나는 정말 모르겠어요. 그런데 올해에는요, 선생, 물론 나도 한두 주쯤 런던에 올라가 있고 싶었어요. 선생도 내가 오미크론 경을 한번 보는 것이 좋겠다고 여기는 듯했잖아요.”\n\n“아무 지장이 없을 거라고 말했지요.”\n\n“그래요, 아니, 바로 그거예요. 그리고 그레셤 씨는 내가 그걸 바라는 줄 알았으니 먼저 말을 내주었어도 좋았을 거라고 나는 생각해요. 이제 돈 때문일 까닭은 없을 테니까요.”\n\n“하지만 메리가 특별히 부인과 오거스타를 청했다고 알고 있었는데요?”\n\n“네, 메리는 참 착했어요. 나를 청하기는 했지요. 하지만 메리가 런던에서 가진 방을 하나도 남기지 않고 다 쓰고 싶어 하는 걸 나는 아주 잘 알아요. 그 집은 자기 하나에게도 조금도 넓지 않아요. 그리고 그 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 자매인 백작부인은 내가 자기 곁에 있어 주기를 몹시 바랐어요. 하지만 사람은 할 수 있으면 남에게 매이지 않고 지내고 싶은 것이고, 보름쯤이라면 그레셤 씨가 어떻게든 해 볼 수 있었을 거라고 나는 정말 생각해요. 그이가 그렇게 무섭게 궁하던 때에는 그런 일로 그이를 괴롭힌 적이 없어요,—하기야 그게 하나도 내 잘못이 아니었다는 건 하늘이 알지요.”\n\n“지주님은 런던을 싫어해요. 더운 날씨에 거기서 보름을 지내면 목숨이 거의 위태로울 거예요.”\n\n“그래도 나에게 청해 주는 예절은 갖출 수 있었을 거예요. 열에 아홉은 나도 가지 않았을 테지요. 나를 그렇게 베는 것은 바로 그 무심함이에요. 그이가 방금 여기 있었는데, 그런데, 이런 게 믿어지겠어요?—”\n\n그러나 선생은 오늘 하루는 더 이상의 불평을 피하기로 마음을 굳혔다. “레이디 애러벨라, 지주님이 부인을 집에 두고 어디로 나가서 제 재미나 보겠다고 마음먹는다면 부인 기분이 어떠할까요. 내 말을 믿어 주신다면, 그레셤 씨보다 못한 사내들도 있어요.” 이 모두가 마님의 오라비인 코시 백작을 가리킨 말임을 레이디 애러벨라는 아주 잘 알아들었다. 그리고 그 논거는 그녀의 입을 막는 데 아주 자주 쓰이는 것이었다.\n\n“참말로, 그렇다면 나는 그이가 여기 어정거리면서 그 지저분한 개들이나 돌보는 것보다 그편이 더 좋겠어요. 나는 이따금 참으로 그이에게 기운이 하나도 남지 않았다고 생각해요.”\n\n“그건 부인이 잘못 아신 거예요, 레이디 애러벨라.” 선생은 모자를 손에 들고 일어서며 그렇게 말하고는 더 이야기를 나누지 않고 빠져나왔다.\n\n집으로 가는 길에 그는 혼인한 삶의 그 국면이 그리 즐거운 것은 아니라고 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그레셤 씨와 그 아내는 세상 사람들에게 가장 좋은 사이로 지내는 줄로 여겨졌다. 두 사람은 늘 같은 집에 살았고, 나갈 일이 있으면 함께 나갔고, 집안 신자석에서 언제나 각자의 구석 자리에 앉았고, 새로움이 주는 행복을 좇는 가장 거친 꿈속에서도 크레스웰 크레스웰 경을 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 어떤 점에서는—예컨대 그레셤스베리의 집안 저택에서 함께 살림을 이어 온 세월을 놓고 본다면—본보기가 될 부부로 여겨질 수도 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 선생이 보는 한 두 사람은 서로의 행복을 크게 보태 주는 것 같지 않았다. 두 사람은 틀림없이 서로를 사랑했고, 어느 한쪽이 참으로 위태로워졌다면 그 위태로움은 다른 한쪽을 참담하게 만들었을 것이다. 그러면서도 어느 쪽이든 상대가 없는 편이 더 편안하지 않을까 하는 것은 넉넉히 물음이 될 만한 일이었다.\n\n선생은 버릇대로 다섯 시에 저녁을 먹고, 일곱 시에 오랜 벗 레이디 스캐처드의 오두막으로 올라갔다. 레이디 스캐처드는 세련된 여인이 아니었다. 젊은 날에는 막벌이꾼의 딸이었고 그러고 나서 막벌이꾼에게 시집을 갔다. 그러나 그 남편이 세상에서 올라갔고—앞서 말한 그 연대기에 적혀 있는 대로다—그 미망인은 이제 예쁜 오두막과 넉넉한 과부 몫 재산을 지닌 레이디 스캐처드였다. 그녀는 모든 점에서 레이디 애러벨라 그레셤과 정반대였다. 그럼에도 선생의 주선으로 두 부인은 얼마쯤 서로 아는 사이가 되어 있었다. 그녀의 혼인 생활도 손 선생이 얼마간 보아 온 바인데, 그에 대한 기억이 지금 그레셤스베리에 있는 현실보다 더 마음을 끄는 것이었는지는 물음이 될 만하다.\n\n두 여인 가운데 손 선생은 더 낮은 쪽 벗을 훨씬 좋아하여, 그녀에게는 의사의 모습으로가 아니라 이웃으로 찾아갔다. “그래, 마님.” 그는 넓은 정원 의자에 그녀 옆으로 앉으며 말했다—온 세상이 레이디 스캐처드를 「마님」이라 불렀다—“이 긴 여름날들이 마님께는 잘 맞으시오? 마님네 장미는 내가 저 큰 집에서 보는 어느 것보다 두 배는 잘 피었소.”\n\n“길다고 하실 만해요, 선생님. 참말 길기도 하지요.”\n\n“그러나 너무 길지는 않지. 자, 이제 마님이 불평하는 건 못 봐 주겠소. 마님을 비참하게 만드는 무엇이 있다고 말할 셈은 아니겠지요? 그러지 않는 게 좋겠소, 나는 믿지 않을 테니.”\n\n“에, 글쎄, 비참하다니요! 내가 비참한지 어떤지 나는 모르겠어요. 그런 말을 하면 못된 짓이지요, 이렇게 편한 것들을 두르고 있으면서.”\n\n“거의 그렇겠지요.” 선생은 이 말을 매섭게 하지 않았다. 부드럽고 다정한 어조로, 말하면서 그녀의 손을 가만히 눌러 주었다.\n\n“나도 못되게 굴 생각은 아니었어요. 나는 무엇에나 참 고맙게 여겨요—적어도 늘 그러려고 애써요. 그런데 선생님, 그게 어쩐지 참 외로워요.”\n\n“외롭다니! 나보다 더 외로울 리는 없소.”\n\n“아, 예. 선생님은 다르지요. 선생님은 어디든지들 다 갈 수 있잖아요. 하지만 혼자된 여자가 무얼 할 수 있어요? 내가 말씀드릴게요, 선생님. 나는 로저가 앞치마를 두르고 곡괭이를 손에 쥔 채로 돌아와 준다면 이 모두를 내놓겠어요. 밤마다 집에 들어올 적에 그 얼굴이 어찌나 잘 마음에 새겨져 있는지.”\n\n“그런데도 그때 마님이 지낸 삶은 고달팠지요, 그렇지 않소, 할멈? 마님은 지금 가진 것에 고맙게 여기는 편이 나을 거요.”\n\n“고맙게 여겨요. 전에도 그렇게 말씀드리지 않았나요?” 그녀가 좀 뾰로통하게 말했다. “하지만 이렇게 혼자 사는 건 서러운 삶이에요. 내 말하지만 나는 해나가 부러워요. 그 애는 제마이마가 부엌에 같이 앉아 있어 주니까요. 나도 이따금 그 애더러 나랑 같이 앉아 있으라고 하고 싶은데, 그 애가 싫다고 해요.”\n\n“아! 그러나 그 애에게 그런 말은 하지 말아야 하오. 그건 스스로를 낮추는 일이오.”\n\n“낮추고 높이는 게 나한테 무슨 상관이에요? 그이가 갔으니 아무 다를 게 없어요. 그이가 살아 있었다면 선생님 말대로 높이 있는 걸 마음에 두었을지도 모르지요. 에휴, 딱하기도. 나도 오래지 않아 그이를 따라갈 텐데, 그때는 아무 상관없을 거예요.”\n\n“우리 모두 이르든 늦든 그를 따라가게 되지요. 그건 넉넉히 확실한 일이오.”\n\n“에휴, 딱해라, 그건 참말 그래요. 오리얼 목사님이 설교에서 감상에 젖으면 우리에게 말해 주듯이, 그게 겨우 한 뼘 길이지요. 하지만 선생님, 둘이 혼인을 했는데 목사님 말대로 그 한 뼘을 함께 다 살지 못하는 건 고된 일이에요. 그래도 남들이 그러는 대로 나도 참고 견디는 수밖에 없겠지요. 그런데, 가시는 건 아니지요, 선생님? 남아서 나하고 차 한 잔 하세요. 해나가 올더니 젖소에서 받아 오는 그런 크림은 어디서도 못 보실 거예요. 자, 그러세요, 선생님.”\n\n그러나 선생에게는 써야 할 편지가 있었고, 해나의 크림을 약속받고서도 마음이 흔들리도록 스스로를 내버려 두지 않았다. 그래서 그는 앞서 지주를 노엽게 했듯이 떠나는 걸음으로 레이디 스캐처드를 노엽게 하고서 자기 길을 갔다. 그러면서 가는 동안 그 비참함에서 어느 쪽이 더 이치에 닿지 않는가를 생각했다. 벗인 레이디 애러벨라인가, 벗인 레이디 스캐처드인가. 앞의 사람은 어지간한 청이라면 한 번도 마다한 적이 없는, 살아 있는 남편을 두고 늘 불평했다. 뒤의 사람은 살아생전 늘 그녀에게 거만하고 매섭게 굴었으며 이따금 잔인하고 부당하기까지 했던, 죽은 남편을 잃은 것을 두고 웅얼거리며 날을 보냈다.\n\n선생에게는 써야 할 편지가 있었으나, 아직도 그 안에 무엇을 담을지 마음을 다 정하지 못했다. 사실 그때까지 그것을 누구에게 쓸 것인지도 정하지 못하고 있었다. 애써 보아 온 대로 이 일을 본다면 편지를 받을 사람은 조카딸 그레셤 부인일 터였다. 그러나 어둠 속으로 뛰어드는 이 걸음을 스스로 감행하게 된다면, 그 경우에는 던스터블 양에게 곧바로 말을 건네게 될 것이었다.\n\n그는 집으로 걸어갔는데, 가장 곧은 길로가 아니라 꽤 크게 돌아서 좁은 골목길을 따라 꽃이 무겁게 달린 두꺼운 산울타리 사이를 지나갔다. 생각이 아주 깊었다. 그녀가 자기와 혼인하고 싶어 한다는 말을 들었다. 그런데 그가 자기 생각만 해야 하는가? 그리고 돈을 두고 지녀 온 그 자랑은 참으로 마음에서 나온 사내다운 감정인가, 아니면 그와 비슷한 여러 감정처럼 부끄러워해야 마땅한 거짓된 자랑인가? 이 일에서 그가 옳게 행한다면 무엇 때문에 남들의 생각을 두려워하겠는가? 가엾은 레이디 스캐처드가 불평한 대로 홀로 지내는 삶은 넉넉히 쓰라린 것이었다. 그러나 또 레이디 스캐처드를 보고, 가까운 또 한 이웃인 벗 지주를 보면, 그를 혼인으로 이끌 만한 것은 그 근처에 거의 없었다. 그래서 그는 뒷짐을 지고 골목길을 지나 골똘한 생각에 잠긴 채 천천히 집으로 걸어갔다.\n\n집에 이르고 나서도 그는 딱 잘라 무엇을 하기로 마음이 더 기울지 않았다. 제 응접실에 앉아 혼자 차를 마시나 레이디 스캐처드와 함께 차를 마시나 마찬가지였을 것이다. 그는 펜도 종이도 꺼내지 않고, 마치 그 궂은 날을 미루듯이 더할 수 없이 늑장을 부리며 찻잔을 앞에 두고 꾸물거렸다. 못 박아 둔 것은 오직 하나였다. 곧 그 편지를 자리에 들기 전에 써야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n열한 시가 가까워서야 차를 다 마치고 나서 그는 아래층으로, 약 곳간 뒤에 있는 어수선한 작은 방으로 갔다. 글 쓰는 일은 으레 거기서 하던 것이었다. 그리고 여기서 그는 마침내 그 일에 자리를 잡고 앉았다. 그 순간에도 그는 망설이고 있었다. 그러나 던스터블 양에게 편지를 써서 그것이 어떻게 보이는지 살펴보기로 했다. 그것을 보내지 않기로 거의 마음을 굳혔다, 적어도 스스로에게는 그렇게 말했다. 하지만 써 본다고 해로울 것은 없었다. 그래서 그는 다음과 같이 그것을 썼다.\n\n그레셤스베리, 185—년 유월 —일.\n\n그만큼 써 놓고 나서 그는 의자에 등을 기대고 그 종이를 바라보았다. 이제 말해 두고 싶은 그것을 말할 낱말을 대체 어디서 찾아낸단 말인가? 그는 평생 그런 편지를 써 본 적이 없었고 그에 가까운 것조차 써 본 적이 없었으니, 이제 미리 생각해 보지 못한 어려움에 짓눌린 자신을 발견했다. 그는 또 반 시간을 그 종이를 바라보며 보냈고, 마침내 이 새로운 어려움에 거의 주춤하고 말았다. 가장 단순하고 가장 꾸밈없는 말을 쓰리라고 그는 스스로에게 거듭거듭 말했다. 그러나 단순하고 꾸밈없는 말을 쓰는 것이 늘 쉬운 일은 아니다. 죽마에 올라 한 자 반이나 되는 긴 낱말들을 늘어놓고 애상과 발작과 감탄 부호를 곁들여 행진하는 것만큼 쉬운 일은 결코 아니다. 그래도 편지는 마침내 저절로 써졌고, 그 안에 감탄 부호는 하나도 없었다.\n\n친애하는 던스터블 양께,—이제 드리려는 제안을 그대가 좋게 여기실 것이라고 제 것이 아닌 다른 판단에 이끌려 믿게 되지 않았더라면, 저는 지금 이 편지를 그대에게 쓰고 있지 않을 것임을 털어놓는 것이 옳다고 여깁니다. 그런 다른 판단이 없었다면, 돈을 두고 그대와 저 사이에 놓인 큰 차이가 그런 제안을 거짓되고 돈을 바라는 것으로 보이게 하지 않을까 두려워했을 것임을 저는 인정합니다. 지금 제가 마음 놓고 그대에게 청하는 것은 오직, 그런 허물에서 저를 벗겨 주시는 일뿐입니다.\n\n여기까지 읽으시면 제 뜻을 아실 것입니다. 우리는 참으로 아주 오래는 아니라 하여도 이제 얼마쯤 가깝게 서로를 알게 되었고, 저는 제가 그대와 함께 있어 기뻤던 만큼 그대도 저와 함께 있어 거의 그만큼 기뻐하지 않았나 이따금 짐작해 보았습니다. 이 일에서 제가 틀렸다면 그저 그렇다고 말해 주십시오. 저는 이 편지가 쓰이지 않았던 것처럼 우리 우정이 이어지도록 애쓰겠습니다. 그러나 제가 옳았다면, 그리고 우리 둘이 하나가 되는 것이 홀로 있을 때보다 우리 둘을 더 행복하게 만들 것이라고 그대가 생각할 수 있다면, 저는 그대에게 제 말과 언약을 참된 믿음으로 바치겠습니다. 그리고 세상의 짐이 그대 어깨 위에 가볍게 놓이도록 늙은 사내가 할 수 있는 일을 하겠습니다. 제 나이를 보면 제가 늙은 바보라는 생각을 저 스스로 좀처럼 누를 수가 없습니다. 그러나 그대 자신도 이제 소녀가 아님을 떠올림으로써 그 생각과 화해해 보려 합니다. 보시는 대로 저는 그대에게 아무 치사도 하지 않으며, 그대도 저에게서 그런 것을 바라실 필요가 없습니다.\n\n이보다 세 배를 더 쓴다 해도 이 글의 진실에 무엇을 보탤 수 있을지 저는 알지 못합니다. 필요한 것은 오직 그대가 제 뜻을 아시는 것뿐입니다. 그대가 저를 이미 참되고 정직한 사람으로 믿지 않으신다면, 제가 쓸 수 있는 어떤 것도 그대를 믿게 만들지 못할 것입니다.\n\n하느님의 축복이 그대에게 있기를. 그대가 저를 오래 답 없이 매달아 두지는 않으실 줄 압니다.\n\n정을 담아, 그대의 벗,\n\n다 쓰고 나서 그는 그녀의 돈에 대해 무엇을 덧붙이는 것이 옳지 않을까 하여 또 반 시간을 골똘히 생각했다. 그녀의 재산 전부에 관하여 그녀가 하고 싶은 대로 할 수 있다고—추신으로 적어도 될 일이었다—말해 주는 것이 좋지 않을까? 어쨌거나 그녀가 갚아야 할 빚은 그에게 하나도 없었고, 그에게는 여전히 제 앞가림에 넉넉한 제 벌이가 있었다. 그러나 한 시쯤에 그는 그 문제는 건드리지 않고 두는 편이 낫겠다는 결론에 이르렀다. 그녀가 그를 마음에 두고 그를 믿을 수 있으며, 또 그가 그녀를 믿어도 좋을 만한 사람이라면, 그런 말이 빠져 있다 해도 그녀가 그에게 오는 것을 막지는 못할 것이다. 그리고 그런 믿음이 없다면, 그쪽에서 그런 다짐을 해 준다고 해서 믿음이 생겨나지도 않을 것이다. 그래서 그는 편지를 두 번 읽고, 봉을 하고, 잠자리 촛대와 함께 그것을 들고 침실로 올라갔다. 편지가 써진 지금은 그것이 가야 한다는 것이 운명으로 못 박힌 일처럼 보였다. 그는 써 놓으면 어떻게 보이는지 알아보려고 그것을 썼다. 그러나 이제 써 놓고 보니 그것을 보내야 한다는 데에는 의심이 남지 않았다. 그래서 그는 편지를 옆 화장대 위에 두고 자리에 들었다. 그리고 아침 일찍—그 편지의 중대함이 그의 잠을 흩어 놓았던 것처럼 보일 만큼 일찍—그는 그것을 특별 심부름꾼에게 맡겨 박솔 힐로 보냈다.\n\n“답장을 기다립니까요?” 심부름 아이가 말했다.\n\n“아니다.” 선생이 말했다. “편지를 두고 그냥 오거라.”\n\n이 여름 몇 달 동안 박솔 힐의 아침 밥 때는 그리 이르지 않았다. 프랭크 그레셤은 틀림없이 기도 시간에 들어오기 전에 제 농장을 한 바퀴 돌았을 것이고, 그 아내는 아마 낙농실의 버터를 살피고 있었을 것이다. 어쨌든 두 사람은 열 시가 가까워서야 얼굴을 마주했고, 그러므로 그레셤스베리에서 박솔 힐까지 말을 달리는 데 두 시간 가까이 걸리는 일이었음에도 던스터블 양은 아래로 내려오기 전에 제 방에서 편지를 받았다.\n\n그녀는 하녀가 방에 함께 있는 동안 옷을 차려입으며 잠자코 그것을 읽었다. 그러나 그 서한이 여느 것보다 중대하다고 몸종이 여기게 만들 만한 기색은 조금도 보이지 않았다. 그녀는 그것을 읽고 나서 조용히 다시 접어 봉투에 넣고, 앉아 있던 탁자 위에 내려놓았다. 그러고서 꼬박 십오 분이 지나서야 그녀는 하인에게 그레셤 부인이 아직 제 방에 있는지 보아 달라고 청했다. “아침 밥 전에 오 분만 혼자서 그이를 만나고 싶으니까요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다.\n\n“배신자! 이 거짓되고 검은 배신자!” 던스터블 양이 벗과 단둘이 있게 되었을 때 처음으로 한 말이 이것이었다.\n\n“아니, 무슨 일이에요?”\n\n“이렇게 장난기가 많은 줄은, 또 혼사를 맺어 주려는 그렇게 날카롭고 흔해빠진 욕심이 있는 줄은 생각하지 못했어요. 자, 보세요. 첫 네 줄만 읽어요. 더 읽으면 안 돼요, 부디. 나머지는 사사로운 것이니까요. 댁의 삼촌이 편지에서 말하는 그 다른 판단은 누구의 것이지요?”\n\n“아, 던스터블 양! 나는 그걸 다 읽어야 해요.”\n\n“참말 그런 짓은 못 해요. 이게 연애 편지라고 여기는 게지요. 하지만 참으로 그 안에는 사랑이라는 말이 한 마디도 없어요.”\n\n“청혼하셨다는 걸 나는 알아요. 나는 참 기쁠 거예요. 던스터블 양이 그분을 좋아하는 걸 아니까요.”\n\n“나더러 늙은 여자라고 하시고, 어쩌면 내가 늙은 바보일지도 모른다고 넌지시 비추셨어요.”\n\n“틀림없이 그런 말씀은 하지 않으셨을 거예요.”\n\n“아! 하지만 나는 틀림없이 하셨다고 봐요. 앞의 것은 넉넉히 참말이고, 나는 진실을 두고 불평한 적이 없어요. 하지만 뒤의 것은 그것이 참인지 나로서는 조금도 그리 확실하지 않아요—그분이 뜻하는 그런 뜻으로는 말이에요.”\n\n“귀한 분, 우리 귀한 분, 이제 그런 식으로 하지 마세요. 나에게 속을 털어놓고, 농담 없이 말해 주세요.”\n\n“그분이 그렇게 고이 믿는 그 다른 판단은 누구의 것이었어요? 그것을 말해 주세요.”\n\n“내 것이에요, 내 것. 물론이지요. 그 일을 두고 그분에게 말할 수 있었던 사람은 나 말고 없어요. 물론 내가 그분에게 이야기했어요.”\n\n“그래서 무엇이라고 말했어요?”\n\n“나는 그분에게…”\n\n“자, 말해 봐요. 참말 사실을 들려주세요. 잘 들어요, 댁에게는 아무것도 그분에게 말할 권리가 없었다고 나는 똑바로 말해 두겠어요. 우리 사이에 오간 것은 믿고 오간 것이니까요. 하지만 무엇이라고 말했는지 들어 봅시다.”\n\n“그분이 청혼하시면 던스터블 양이 받아들이겠다고 말했어요.” 그렇게 말하면서 그레셤 부인은 벗의 얼굴을 의심스럽게 들여다보았다. 던스터블 양이 참으로 자기를 기뻐하는지 언짢아하는지 알 수 없었던 것이다. 언짢아한다면, 그러면 삼촌은 얼마나 속은 것인가!\n\n“그것을 사실로 말했어요?”\n\n“나는 그렇게 생각한다고 말했어요.”\n\n“그러면 나는 그분을 받아들일 수밖에 없겠네요.” 던스터블 양은 거짓으로 절망한 척 편지를 바닥에 떨어뜨리며 말했다.\n\n“내 귀한 분, 귀한 분, 우리 귀한 분!” 그레셤 부인은 울음을 터뜨리며 벗의 목에 몸을 던지며 말했다.\n\n“착실한 조카딸이 되도록 하세요.” 던스터블 양이 말했다. “그리고 이제 가서 옷차림을 마치게 해 주세요.”\n\n그날 오후에 그레셤스베리로 이런 말로 된 답장이 되돌아갔다.\n\n친애하는 손 선생님께,—저는 무슨 일에서나 선생님을 믿고 있고 또 믿겠습니다. 그리고 선생님이 바라시는 대로 될 것입니다. 메리가 선생님께 편지를 쓴답니다. 하지만 메리가 하는 말은 한 마디도 믿지 마십시오. 저는 다시는 믿지 않겠습니다. 이 일에서 메리가 아주 못되게 굴었으니까요.\n\n다정하고 아주 참된 마음으로,\n\n“그래서 나는 영국에서 가장 부유한 여인과 혼인하게 되었구나.” 그날 양갈비를 앞에 놓고 앉으며 손 선생이 혼자 중얼거렸다.\n\n# 제40장. 서로 죽이는 싸움\n\n플럼스테드 에피스코피의 주임 사제의 아내가 덤벨로 경의 약혼한 신부인 딸을 데리고 집으로 돌아왔을 때, 그곳에 얼마간 승리의 기분이 감돌았으리라는 것은 미루어 짐작해야 한다. 하틀탑 후작의 후계자는 재산으로 보아 당시 미혼의 젊은 귀족 가운데 가장 대단한 사람이었다. 게다가 그는 마음에 맞추기 어려운 사내로, 몹시 까다롭게 굴며 거드름을 피우는 사람으로 이름이 나 있었으니—그런 사내의 아내로 뽑혔다는 것은 한낱 교구 성직자의 딸에게는 대단한 일이었다. 그 행복한 처녀의 어머니가 어떤 식으로 이 사실을 레이디 러프턴에게 알렸는지, 이를테면 제 자랑을 너울 밑에 감추어 가며 알렸는지는 우리가 이미 보았다. 그리고 그 행복한 처녀가 제게 온 큰 복을 얼마나 얌전하게 견뎌 냈는지, 제 영화를 모르는 척하며 겸손하게 제 옷을 챙기는 일에나 매달렸는지도 우리는 보았다.\n\n그러나 그럼에도 플럼스테드 에피스코피에는 승리의 기분이 있었다. 어머니는 집으로 돌아오자 제 평생의 큰 목적에서 자기가 완전히 성공했음을 느끼기 시작했다. 아직 런던에 있는 동안에는 그 만족을 실감하지도 못했고, 잔이 입술에 닿기 전에 그것이 입에서 떨어져 나가지나 않을까 하는 의심이 그때는 있었다. 하틀탑 후작의 아들이라 해도 어버이의 권위 아래 있을지 모르는 일이고, 그리젤다와 귀족 관 사이에 담이 솟아오를지도 모르는 일이었다. 그러나 그런 종류의 일은 아무것도 없었다. 부주교는 후작과 마주 앉아 이야기를 나누었고, 그랜틀리 부인은 후작부인과 마주 앉아 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 그 고귀한 두 사람 가운데 어느 쪽도 아들의 혼사를 두고 흐뭇하다는 뜻을 내보이지는 않았으나, 또한 어느 쪽도 그것을 막아 보려는 시도를 하지 않았다. 덤벨로 경은 제 뜻을 지닌 사내였다—그랜틀리 집안 사람들이 저희끼리 자랑하는 말이 그러했다. 가엾은 그리젤다! 남편의 그 억센 뜻이라는 사실이 그녀에게 그다지 자랑거리가 되지 않는 날이 어쩌면 올지도 모른다. 그러나 앞에서 말했듯이 런던에서는 집안의 이 기쁨을 음미할 겨를이 없었다. 해야 할 일이 성질상 마음을 졸이게 하는 것이었고, 제 자랑을 늘어놓다가는 치명적인 일이 될 수도 있었다. 그러나 이제 플럼스테드에 무사히 이르고 나니 그 큰 진실이 온갖 찬란함을 다 갖추고 그들 위로 터져 나왔다.\n\n그랜틀리 부인에게는 딸이 하나뿐이었고, 그 아이의 성품을 만들어 주는 일과 그 아이가 세상에 자리를 잡게 하는 일이 어머니 평생의 유일한 큰 목적이었다. 그리젤다의 뛰어난 아름다움은 플럼스테드 집안 사람들이 오래전부터 알고 있었다. 그 아이의 신중함도, 몸가짐도, 태도도 의심할 것이 없었다. 그러나 아버지는 이따금 어머니에게 그리지가 그 아이의 형제들만큼 똑똑하지는 못한 것 같다고 비춘 적이 있었다. “저는 그 말에는 전혀 동의하지 않아요,” 그랜틀리 부인은 그렇게 대답했다. “게다가 부주교님이 똑똑하다고 부르는 것은 처녀에게는 조금도 필요하지 않은 것이에요. 그 아이는 나무랄 데 없이 숙녀답지요. 그건 부주교님도 부인하지 못하실 거예요.” 부주교는 그것을 부인하고 싶었던 적이 한 번도 없었고, 이제는 자기가 똑똑함이라고 부른 것이 젊은 아가씨에게 필요하지 않다는 것을 기꺼이 인정할 마음이 되었다.\n\n집안의 이 영화가 한창인 시기에 부주교 자신은 조금 뒤로 밀려나 있었고, 제 훌륭한 자식과 마음대로 어울리는 것도 좀처럼 허락받지 못했다. 사실 그를 위해 공평하게 말한다면, 그는 바체스터 일대를 위엄 있는 걸음으로 오락가락 누비는 그 개선 행렬에 함께 걸으려 하지 않았다고 해야 한다. 그는 딸에게 입 맞추고 축복하며, 남편을 사랑하고 좋은 아내가 되라고 일러 주었다. 그러나 그 아이가 후작을 제 것으로 붙잡음으로써 제 소임을 얼마나 훌륭하게 해냈는지를 생각하면, 이런 따위의 훈계는 자리에 맞지 않고 거의 상스러워 보였다. 부목사나 젖내 나는 법정 변호사와 혼인하려는 처녀들에게는 하느님이 그들을 부르시는 그 삶의 자리에서 제 소임을 다하라고 일러 줄 만하다. 그러나 앞으로 후작부인이 될 사람에게 아버지가 그런 훈계를 하는 것은 거의 당돌한 짓으로 보였다.\n\n“그 아이를 두고 두려워하실 까닭은 아무것도 없다고 생각해요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “여태 그 아이가 처신해 온 모양을 보면요.”\n\n“착한 아이였소,” 부주교가 말했다. “하지만 이제 큰 유혹이 있는 자리에 놓이게 되오.”\n\n“그 아이는 어떤 자리에라도 어울릴 만한 굳센 마음을 지녔어요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 제 자랑을 담아 대답했다.\n\n그러나 그럼에도 부주교조차 이 결합의 소식이 그곳에 알려진 뒤로는 바체스터의 성당 구내를 조금 더 자랑스러운 걸음으로 오갔다. 아버지가 살아 계신 마지막 몇 해 동안에는 그가 성당 구내에서 가장 큰 사람이던 시절이 있었다. 수석 사제는 늙고 병약했고, 그랜틀리 박사가 주교의 권위를 휘둘렀다. 그러나 그 뒤로 사정이 달라졌다. 새 주교가 그곳에 왔는데 그에게 철저히 적대적인 사람이었다. 새 수석 사제도 왔는데, 그는 부주교의 벗이기만 한 것이 아니라 그 아내 쪽으로 동서가 되는 사람이었다. 그러나 이 사람이 온 것조차 부주교의 권위를 줄여 놓았다. 성가대 사제들은 여느 때처럼 그의 말에 매달리지 않았고, 하급 참사회원들은 바체스터의 성직자 모임에서 그를 만나도 그의 웃음에 덜 굽실거리며 웃음으로 답했다. 그러나 이제는 그 옛날의 으뜸 자리가 그에게 되돌아온 듯했다. 많은 사람의 마음속에서, 후작의 장인이 된 부주교라면 그 자신이 어느 주교와도 맞먹는 사람이었다. 그는 수석 사제 말고 다른 사람들에게는 새 인연에 대해 별로 말하지 않았으나, 그 사실을 마음속으로 의식하고 있었고 제 머리 둘레에 비쳐 오는 영광의 빛도 의식하고 있었다.\n\n그러나 그랜틀리 부인에 관해서는, 그녀가 끝없이 이어지는 위엄찬 환호의 행렬 속을 걸어갔다고 말할 수 있다. 그녀가 벗들과 이웃들에게 덤벨로 경과 후작부인 이야기를 줄곧 늘어놓았다고 여겨서는 안 된다. 그녀는 그런 어리석은 짓을 하기에는 너무나 슬기로웠다. 다가오는 결합이 한번 알려진 뒤로는 하틀탑이라는 이름이 제 집안 울 밖에서 그녀의 입에 오르는 일이 거의 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 저 자신도 놀랄 만큼 손쉽게 대단한 여인의 몸가짐과 태를 갖추었다. 그녀는 아침 방문이라는 일과를, 마치 시골 지주들에게 너그럽게 대해 주는 것이 제 소임이기라도 한 듯이 치렀다. 수석 사제의 아내인 동생은 언니가 그 위세를 하도 담박하게 부리는 데 놀랐다. 그리고 프라우디 부인에게는 그 부인의 가슴이 거의 미어질 만한 태로 아랫사람 대하듯 굽어 살펴 주었다. “저 여자와는 아직 셈이 남았지,” 프라우디 부인이 혼자 말했다. 그녀는 덤벨로 경과 그리젤다에 관한 소식을 알게 된 뒤로 하틀탑 집안에 관하여 아주 해로운 사정 여러 가지를 알아낼 방도를 찾아 놓았던 것이다.\n\n그리젤다 자신은 그 행렬 속에 실려 다녔으나 제 몫으로 하는 일은 거의 없었으니, 마치 동방의 신 같았다. 그녀는 어머니의 어루만짐을 잠자코 받았고, 제 칭송을 들으며 어머니의 얼굴을 보고 웃었으나, 그 승리는 아무래도 안에 들어 있었다. 이 일을 두고 누구에게도 많은 말을 하지 않았고, 앞으로의 덤벨로 살림에 대해 이야기하기를 아주 마다하여 집안의 늙은 가정부를 크게 언짢게 했다. 그녀의 이모인 애러빈 부인은 앞으로의 바람에 대해 마음을 열어 말하도록 그녀를 이끌어 보려고 애를 썼으나, 조카는 조금도 꿈쩍하지 않았다. “아, 예, 그야 그렇지요, 이모.” 그리고 “생각해 볼게요, 엘리너 이모.” 아니면 “덤벨로 경이 그러기를 바라면 물론 그렇게 하겠어요.” 그녀에게서 얻어 낼 수 있는 것은 이 이상 아무것도 없었다. 그리하여 애러빈 부인은 대여섯 번 헛되게 시도해 본 뒤에 그 일을 그만두었다.\n\n그러나 그때 옷 문제가—혼례 옷가지 문제가 일어났다! 비꼬기 좋아하는 이들은 재봉사가 사람을 만든다고 흔히 말한다. 내가 그런 사람이라면, 모자 장수가 신부를 만든다고 단연 주장할 수도 있으리라. 처녀 시절이나 아내 시절과 갈라지는 신부 시절이라는 것에 관해서는—여인의 삶에서 그 두 시기 사이에 뚜렷한 금을 긋는 것이라는 점에서는—모자 장수가 신부를 만드는 데 실로 많은 일을 한다. 혼례 옷가지가 거기 없다면 그녀는 좀처럼 신부가 되지 못할 것이다. 그런 딸림 물건 없이 혼인하는 처녀는 그런 갈라짐의 금도 없이 아내라는 신분으로 넘어가 버리는 듯 보일 것이다. 제 혼례 치장을 처음으로 누리는 그 순간에 그녀는 신부가 된다. 그리고 그 좋은 물건들 가운데 가장 좋은 것을 싸서 챙길 옷으로 다루기 시작하는 그 다른 순간에 그녀는 아내가 된다.\n\n이 문제가 의논될 때 그리젤다는 마땅한 만큼의 관심이 모자라는 기색을 조금도 보이지 않았다. 그녀는 꾸준하게, 천천하게, 거의 엄숙하게 일에 착수했으니, 손에 든 볼일이 성급하게 다루면 죄가 될 만한 것이기라도 한 듯했다. 그 뜻이 웅장하고 그 이론이 깊은 데에 어머니마저 두려움을 느낄 정도였다. 그러나 그녀가 제 신분의 마지막 표시요 표적이며 제 신부됨의 알짬이요 이를테면 성막의 겉 너울인 그 큰 옷감, 곧 혼례복을 대뜸 궁리하러 달려들었다고 여기지는 마시라. 위대한 시인이 제 서사시의 큰 전환점에 꼭 필요한 영감에 이르기까지 차츰차츰 자신을 끌어올리듯이, 그녀도 그 성스러운 땅에 천천히 다가갔다. 그 땅에 이르면 그녀는 받드는 이들을 둘레에 앉히고 앉아 그 중대한 옷의 성질과 크기와 도안과 빛깔과 짜임과 꾸밈을 의논할 참이었다. 아니, 여기에 이르기까지 실로 해야 할 일이 많았다. 그리고 내가 이미 이야기한 그 시인이 먼저 무사 여신을 부르고 그다음에 작은 사건들을 차츰차츰 무대 위로 내보내듯이, 그랜틀리 양도 거룩한 열심으로 어머니의 도움을 청하고, 그다음에 혼례 옷가지의 눈에 보이는 웅장함을 받쳐 줄 바탕이 되어야 하는 온갖 속옷 물목을 마련했다.\n\n돈은 문제가 아니었다. 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 우리는 모두 안다. 그리고 그 말이 쓰일 때 그것이 흔히 되도록 싼값으로 찬란한 빛을 얻어 내겠다는 뜻임을 우리는 안다. 그러나 이번 경우에는 정말로 돈이 문제가 아니었다—적어도 보석을 빼고 부인의 옷에 쓸 수 있는 만큼의 돈은 그러했다. 다이아몬드 같은 것에 관해서는 부주교가 그 일을 제 손에 넣겠다고 곧바로 선언했다—덤벨로 경이나 하틀탑 쪽에서 고르는 일에 함께하고자 하는 데까지는 빼놓고 말이다. 그랜틀리 부인도 그런 결정을 아쉬워하지 않았다. 그녀는 조심성 없는 여인이 아니었으니, 그런 자리에서 보석 가게의 위험한 유혹들 가운데에 저 자신을 맡기는 책임을 두려워했을 것이다. 그러나 비단과 새틴에 관해서라면—프랑스제 보닛, 모슬린, 벨벳, 모자, 승마복, 조화, 머리 금장식, 별난 망사 세공, 에나멜 버클, 금 물린 실패 장식, 기계 장치를 넣은 페티코트에 관해서라면—또 구두와 장갑과 코르셋과 스타킹과 아마포와 플란넬과 옥양목에 관해서라면—돈은, 내가 양심을 걸고 말할 수 있는데, 문제가 아니었다. 그리고 이런 사정 아래에서 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 무엇으로도 칭찬을 다할 수 없을 만큼 엄숙한 부지런함과 꾸준한 끈기로 일에 착수했다.\n\n“그 아이가 행복하기를 바라요,” 애러빈 부인이 언니에게 말했다. 두 사람은 수석 사제 관저의 응접실에 함께 앉아 있었다.\n\n“아, 그럴 거예요. 그렇게 될 거라고 생각해요. 그러지 못할 까닭이 무어겠어요?” 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“아, 예, 까닭을 안다는 건 아니에요. 하지만 그 아이는 세상의 눈으로 보아 제 자리보다 훨씬 높은 자리로 올라가는 것이니, 그 아이 걱정을 안 할 수가 없어요.”\n\n“가난한 사내와 혼인하러 간다면 훨씬 더 걱정이 될 거예요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “저는 그리젤다가 높은 자리에 맞게 태어난 아이라고, 자연이 그 아이를 신분과 위세를 누리도록 내놓았다고 늘 생각해 왔어요. 보다시피 그 아이는 조금도 들떠 있지 않아요. 그 모든 것을 마치 처음부터 제 것인 양 받아들이지요. 만약 그런 뜻으로 말한 거라면, 그 아이가 마음이 뒤집힐 위험은 조금도 없다고 생각해요.”\n\n“저는 오히려 그 아이 마음을 생각한 거였어요,” 애러빈 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그 아이는 사랑하지 않고서 덤벨로 경을 받아들이지는 않았을 거예요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 조금 빠르게 말했다.\n\n“제가 하려는 말도 꼭 그건 아니에요, 수전 언니. 사랑하지 않았다면 그분을 받아들이지 않았으리라는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 높은 신분의 온갖 영화 속에서 마음을 싱싱하게 지키기란 참 어려운 일이에요. 그리고 그것을 타고난 권리로 누려 온 처녀보다, 그렇게 태어나지 않은 처녀에게 그 일이 더 어렵지요.”\n\n“싱싱한 마음이라는 게 무슨 말인지 저는 잘 모르겠어요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 좀 뾰로통하게 말했다. “그 아이가 제 소임을 다하고 남편을 사랑하고 하느님이 놓아 주신 자리를 어울리게 채운다면, 그 이상 무엇을 더 바라야 하는지 저는 모르겠어요. 어린 처녀가 처음으로 세상에 나서려는 때에 그 아이를 겁주는 식은 저는 조금도 좋게 보지 않아요.”\n\n“예, 저도 그 아이를 겁주지는 않겠어요. 그리젤다를 겁주기란 아무래도 어려운 일일 것 같군요.”\n\n“그러기를 바라요. 처녀에게 가장 중요한 일은 여인의 소임에 대해 옳은 생각을 지니도록 길러졌느냐 하는 것이지요. 물론 이 일을 두고 제가 자랑할 처지는 아니에요. 그 아이가 그러한 아이인 것에 대해서는 물론 제가 책임을 져야 하지요. 하지만 무엇을 바꾸었으면 좋겠다는 마음이 들 만한 데를 저는 도무지 못 찾겠다고, 이것만은 털어놓아야 하겠어요.” 그러고는 이 이야기를 그만 접게 두었다.\n\n이 처녀의 복을 크게 놀라워하면서도 스스로 말은 조금밖에 하지 않은 친척들 가운데에 그녀의 외할아버지 하딩 씨가 있었다. 그는 늙은 성직자로, 몸가짐이 꾸밈없고 소박하며, 성당 참사회의 선창 사제에 지나지 않았으므로 그리 두드러진 자리를 차지하고 있지 않았다. 딸인 그랜틀리 부인은 그를 사랑했고, 부주교도 늘 한결같이 지극한 예로써는 아니었을지언정 적어도 언제나 배려와 존중으로 그를 대했다. 그러나 나이 들고 꾸밈없는 사람이었던 만큼 플럼스테드의 젊은 사람들은 그를 그다지 크게 우러르지 않았다. 그는 다른 친척들보다 가난했고, 바셋주 사교계에서 고개를 높이 들려는 시도를 하지 않았다. 게다가 요 몇 해 동안 그 마음의 집은 수석 사제 관저였다. 그가 이 도시에 제 셋집을 지니고 있기는 했으나 차츰 그곳에서 마음이 떼어지도록 스스로 내버려 두고 있었다. 그는 수석 사제의 집에 제 침실을 두었고, 수석 사제의 서재에 제 안락의자를 두었고, 수석 사제 부인의 응접실 소파에 제 자리 한 귀퉁이를 두었다. 그러므로 다가오는 이 혼사에 그가 크게 끼어들 필요는 없었다. 그러나 그래도 손녀에게 축하의 말을 한마디 하는 것—그리고 어쩌면 충고의 말을 한마디 하는 것은 그의 소임이 되었다.\n\n“그리지야,” 그가 그녀에게 말했다. 그는 늘 그녀를 그리지라고 불렀으나 이 젊은 아가씨는 그 애칭의 다정함을 한 번도 고맙게 여긴 적이 없었다. “이리 와서 나에게 입 맞추고, 이 큰 출세를 축하하게 해 주렴. 참으로 진심으로 그러고 싶구나.”\n\n“고마워요, 할아버지,” 그녀가 그의 이마에 입술을 대며 말했으니, 이를테면 제 입맞춤을 아주 아껴 쓴 것이었다. 그러나 이제 그 입술은 지엄한 것이 되어 대성당의 늙은 삯일꾼의 이마보다는 더 고귀한 이마를 위해 남겨져 있었다. 하딩 씨는 여전히 일요일마다 쉬는 일 없이 대성당 성가대석의 그 낯익은 독서대 앞에 서서 탄원 기도를 선창하고 있었으니, 하틀탑 집안 사람들이 이 관행을 듣게 되면 기뻐하지 않을 것이라는 생각이 그리젤다의 마음속에 들어 있었다. 수석 사제나 부주교라면 아주 괜찮을 것이고, 외할아버지가 참사회원이라도 되었더라면 그녀는 그를 참아 주었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그 나이에 대성당의 막일하는 하급 성직자 가운데 하나로 있음으로써 제 집안을 거의 망신시키고 있다고 그녀는 생각했다. 그러므로 그녀는 그에게 아껴서 입 맞추었고, 그와 나누는 말도 몇 마디로 그치리라고 마음먹었다.\n\n“너는 대단한 귀부인이 될 게다, 그리지야,” 그가 말했다.\n\n“음!” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그렇게 말을 걸어오는데 그녀가 무슨 말을 하겠는가?\n\n“그리고 네가 행복하기를—그리고 남들을 행복하게 해 주기를 바란다.”\n\n“그러기를 바라요,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 언제나 뒤쪽을 가장 많이 생각하렴. 네 둘레 사람들의 행복을 생각하면 네 행복은 생각하지 않아도 저절로 올 게다. 그 말을 알아듣겠지, 그렇지 않니?”\n\n“아, 예, 알아들어요,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n두 사람이 이야기하는 동안 하딩 씨는 여전히 그녀의 손을 잡고 있었는데, 그리젤다는 마음에 없이, 그러므로 곱살스럽지 못하게 손을 그에게 맡겨 두었고, 그 손을 그에게서 잡아 빼는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n“그리고 그리지야—부유한 백작부인이 행복해지는 것도 젖 짜는 처녀가 행복해지는 것과 똑같이 쉬운 일이라고 나는 믿는다—”\n\n그리젤다가 고개를 조금 쳐들었는데, 그것은 그녀의 마음속에서 서로 다른 두 가지 작용이 일어난 데서 나온 것이었다. 첫째는 외할아버지가 제 신분을 앗아 가고 있다는 생각이었다. 그녀는 부유한 후작부인이 될 사람이었다. 그리고 둘째는 제 처지를 젖 짜는 처녀의 처지에 견주었다고 이 늙은이에게 느낀 노여움이었다.\n\n“똑같이 쉬운 일이라고 나는 믿는다,” 그가 말을 이었다. “남들은 그렇지 않다고 너에게 말하겠지만 말이다. 하지만 젖 짜는 처녀에게나 백작부인에게나 그것은 여인 자신에게 달린 일일 수밖에 없다. 백작부인이 된다는—그 사실 하나만으로 너를 행복하게 해 주지는 못할 게다.”\n\n“덤벨로 경은 지금은 자작일 뿐이에요,” 그리젤다가 말했다. “그 집안에는 백작 작위가 없어요.”\n\n“아! 그건 몰랐구나,” 하딩 씨는 이렇게 말하고 손녀의 손을 놓았다. 그리고 그 뒤로는 더 이상의 충고로 그녀를 괴롭히지 않았다.\n\n프라우디 부인과 주교는 그랜틀리 부인이 런던에서 돌아온 뒤에 둘 다 플럼스테드를 찾아왔고, 플럼스테드의 부인들도 물론 그 방문에 답례를 했다. 그랜틀리 집안과 프라우디 집안이 서로 미워하는 것은 마땅한 일이었다. 그들은 본질에서 교회 사람들이었고, 교회의 모든 일에 대한 그들의 뜻은 서로 맞서는 것이었다. 그들은 이 교구의 으뜸 자리를 놓고 싸울 수밖에 없었고, 어느 집안도 상대를 눌러 놓기를 너그러움과 좋은 기분을 낼 수 있을 만큼 해내지는 못했다. 그들은 정말로 서로 미워했고, 이 미움은 한때 주교와 그 성직자들 사이에 그토록 필요한 예의마저 아주 끊어 놓을 지경에까지 이르렀다. 그러나 이 원한의 쓰라림은 이겨 냈고, 두 집안의 부인들은 서로 오가는 사이를 이어 왔다.\n\n그러나 이제 이 혼사는 프라우디 부인이 견딜 수 있는 것을 거의 넘어서 있었다. 새 주교좌를 세우려던 그 일에서 그랜틀리 집안이 겪은 큰 낙심은, 그녀가 잘 알고 있었던 대로, 한때 그녀의 마음을 누그러뜨려 주었다. 그녀는 가엾고 딱한 그랜틀리 부인 이야기를 할 수 있었던 것이다! “그 부인은 이 일로 가슴이 미어져 있소. 그런 불운이 되풀이되는 것은 견디기 힘든 일이지요.” 그녀가 아주 흐뭇한 낯으로 이렇게 말하는 것을 사람들은 들은 바 있었고, 그 흐뭇함은 그녀에게 자못 잘 어울리는 것이었다. 그러나 이제 그 흐뭇함은 끝이 났다. 올리비아 프라우디는 베스널 그린의 지구 교회에 있는 홀아비 설교자를—아이가 셋이고 신자석 임대료에 매여 살아가는 사내를 받아들인 참이었다. 그런데 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 하틀탑 후작의 맏아들과 약혼을 한 것이다! 여인들에게 원수를 용서하라고 명하실 때, 이런 종류의 억울함까지 거기에 들어가라는 뜻이었을 수는 없다.\n\n그러나 프라우디 부인의 담력은 조금도 꺾이지 않았다. 그녀를 두고 자랑할 만한 것이 있다면, 무엇으로도 그 담력을 꺾을 수 없었다는 것이다. 바체스터로 돌아온 지 얼마 안 되어 그녀는 올리비아를 데리고—올리비아는 아주 마음이 없었다—플럼스테드까지 마차를 몰고 갔고, 그랜틀리 집안 사람들이 집에 없어 명함만 두고 왔다. 그리고 이제 알맞은 시일이 지난 뒤에 그랜틀리 부인과 그리젤다가 그 방문에 답례를 한 것이었다. 약혼한 사실이 알려진 뒤로 프라우디 집안의 부인들이 그랜틀리 양을 보는 것은 이번이 처음이었다.\n\n처음에 오간 칭찬의 무리는 산울타리 장미 덤불에 잔뜩 핀 꽃에 견줄 만한 것이었다. 그 꽃들은 눈에는 아름다웠으나 가시에 하도 촘촘히 둘러싸여 있어서 큰 위험 없이는 꺾을 수 없었다. 칭찬이 산울타리 위에 그대로 놓여 있도록 두는 동안에는—그것을 거두어 그 열매를 즐기려는 시도가 없는 동안에는—아무 해도 없었다. 그러나 그럴 뜻으로 처음 내민 손가락은 이내 핏방울이 맺힌 채 뒤로 물러났다.\n\n“물론 그리젤다에게는 대단한 혼사지요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 속삭이는 소리로 말했는데, 그 얌전함은 프라우디 부인의 것만큼 단단히 무기를 붙잡고 있지 않은 적이라면 무장을 풀게 할 만한 것이었다. “하지만 그것과는 따로, 이 인연은 여러 가지로 흐뭇한 것이에요.”\n\n“아, 그렇겠지요,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“덤벨로 경은 워낙 온전히 제 몸의 주인이니까요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말을 이었는데, 뜻하지 않은 승리의 반음이 그 얌전한 속삭임에 조금 섞여 들었다.\n\n“그리고 내가 듣는 말로는 앞으로도 그럴 것 같소,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. 그러자 긁힌 손이 곧바로 뒤로 물러났다.\n\n“물론 그 살림사—” 하고 프라우디 부인은 축하의 물목을 부드럽게 이어 가다가 문장의 나머지를 그랜틀리 부인의 귀에 바싹 대고 속삭였으니, 자기가 하는 말이 젊은 사람들에게는 한마디도 들리지 않게 하려는 것이었다.\n\n“그런 말은 한마디도 들은 적이 없어요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. “그리고 저는 그 말을 믿지 않아요.”\n\n“아, 내가 틀렸을지도 모르지요. 그러기를 진심으로 바라고요. 하지만 젊은 사내는 젊은 사내인 법이오. 그건 댁도 알 테지요—그리고 자식은 어버이를 닮는 법이지요. 이제 옴니엄 공작을 자주 뵙게 되겠구려.”\n\n그러나 그랜틀리 부인은 아무 저항 없이 나가떨어져 짓밟힐 여인이 아니었다. 그리고 장미 덤불에 긁히기는 했으나 아직 아주 싸움에서 물러난 처지는 아니었다. 그녀는 옴니엄 공작에 대해 아주 태연하게 몇 마디를 했으니, 그를 한낱 바셋주의 땅 임자로만 이야기한 것이었다. 그러고는 제 가장 다사로운 웃음으로 웃으며 곧 티클러 씨와 알게 되는 기쁨을 누렸으면 좋겠다는 바람을 말했다. 그러면서 올리비아 프라우디 쪽으로 예쁘게 조그맣게 고개를 숙였다. 그런데 티클러 씨란 베스널 그린의 지구 교회에 딸린 그 훌륭한 성직자였다.\n\n“팔월에 여기 내려올 거예요,” 올리비아가 씩씩하게 말했으니, 제 연애 일을 두고 부끄러워하지 않기로 마음먹은 것이었다.\n\n“그때쯤이면 아가씨는 대륙 곳곳을 돌며 뽐내고 있겠구려,” 프라우디 부인이 그리젤다에게 말했다. “덤벨로 경은 홈부르크며 엠스며 그런 곳에서 이름이 잘 알려져 있으니, 아가씨도 아주 제집처럼 편안하게 지내겠지요.”\n\n“저희는 로마로 갈 거예요,” 그리젤다가 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n“티클러 씨도 곧 이 교구로 들어오겠지요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “그분의 벗이던 슬로프 씨가 그분을 아주 좋게 말하는 것을 들은 기억이 나요.”\n\n이제는 방패를 던져 버리고 칼 뒤에 서서, 칼끝까지 가는 전쟁을 선포하고 자비를 주지도 받지도 않아야 할 때가 왔다고 그랜틀리 부인이 마음을 굳게 정한 것이 아니라면, 이런 말은 무엇으로도 정당하다 할 수 없었다. 슬로프 씨를 언급하는 것은 붉은 천이 황소에게 하는 것으로 여겨지는 그 작용을 프라우디 부인에게 했다. 그런데 그 언급이 슬로프 씨의 이름을 프라우디 부인의 앞날의 사위 이름과 다정한 괄호 안에 함께 묶어 놓았으니, 그 결과가 무서울 것은 틀림없었다. 게다가 이보다 더한 것이 있었다. 바로 그 슬로프 씨가 한때 올리비아 프라우디 양을 두고 대담한 희망을—그 젊은 아가씨 자신은 대담하다고 여기지 않았던 희망을 품은 적이 있었기 때문이다. 이 모든 것을 그랜틀리 부인은 알고 있었고, 알면서도 감히 그의 이름을 입에 올린 것이었다.\n\n프라우디 부인의 얼굴빛은 검은 노여움으로 어두워졌고, 사람들 앞에서 갖추는 몸가짐의 그 다듬어진 웃음은 그 본성의 짓밟힌 감정 앞에 자리를 내주었다.\n\n“댁이 말하는 그 사람은, 그랜틀리 부인,” 그녀가 말했다. “티클러 씨가 벗으로 알았던 사람이 아니오.”\n\n“아, 그런가요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “제가 잘못 알았을지도 모르겠네요. 슬로프 씨가 그분을 이야기하는 것을 들은 것은 분명한데요.”\n\n“슬로프 씨가 댁의 동생 뒤를 쫓아다니고 그 동생이 그를 그렇게 부추기던 때에는, 댁이 나보다 그를 더 많이 보았을 테지요, 그랜틀리 부인.”\n\n“프라우디 부인, 그런 일은 결코 없었어요.”\n\n“부주교께서 그렇게 여기셨고 그 일로 몹시 괴로워하셨다는 것을 나는 알 만한 근거가 있소.” 그런데 딱하게도 이것은 그랜틀리 부인이 부인할 수 없는 사실이었다.\n\n“부주교님이 슬로프 씨를 잘못 보셨을 수도 있지요,” 그녀가 말했다. “바체스터의 다른 몇 사람도 그랬듯이요. 하지만 그를 이곳에 데려온 책임은, 제 생각에는, 프라우디 부인 댁에 있지요.”\n\n이 교전이 이 대목에 이르렀을 때 그랜틀리 부인은 가엾은 올리비아의 지난 연애 일을 들추어 치명적인 상처를 입힐 수도 있었다. 그러나 그녀에게 너그러움이 없지는 않았다. 싸움이 가장 뜨거운 때에도 그녀는 젊고 연한 것을 살려 두는 법을 알고 있었다.\n\n“내가 이곳에 왔을 때에는, 그랜틀리 부인, 대성당 도시의 그 구내에서 얼마나 깊은 악함이 나올 수 있는지를 꿈에도 몰랐소,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그러면 그 귀한 올리비아를 위해서라도, 가엾은 티클러 씨를 바체스터로 데려오지는 마세요.”\n\n“티클러 씨는, 그랜틀리 부인, 도덕이 확실한 사람이고 생각의 결이 매우 신앙다운 사람이오. 딸의 앞날에 관해서 나만큼 안심할 수 있는 사람이 누구나 다 그렇기를 바라오.”\n\n“예, 그분이 이미 식구를 거느린 사람이라는 이점을 지녔다는 것은 저도 알아요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 일어서며 말했다. “안녕히 계세요, 프라우디 부인. 잘 있어요, 올리비아.”\n\n“그런 편이 이보다는 훨씬 나으—” 그러나 그 매질은 빈 허공에 떨어졌다. 그랜틀리 부인은 이미 계단으로 빠져나갔고, 올리비아는 앞문을 보아 줄 하인을 부르는 종을 울리고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 마차에 오르면서 그 싸움을 떠올리며 살짝 웃었고, 자리에 앉으면서 딸의 손을 가만히 눌렀다. 그러나 프라우디 부인의 얼굴은 적이 물러난 뒤에도 여전히 아케론처럼 어두웠고, 그녀는 노여운 어조로 딸을 일하러 보냈다. “네 처지에 그런 게으른 버릇에 빠져 있으면 티클러 씨가 불평할 큰 근거가 생길 게다,” 그녀가 말했다. 그러므로 나는 그 맞붙음에서 승리한 사람은 그랜틀리 부인이었다고 말해도 정당하다고 생각한다.\n\n# 제41장. 돈키호테\n\n루시가 레이디 러프턴과 마주 앉았던 그날, 수석 사제는 프램리 목사관에서 저녁을 먹었다. 그와 로바츠는 로바츠가 이 주교구에 들어온 뒤로 서로 알고 지내던 사이였고, 이제는 마크가 성당 참사회에서 성직 영전을 얻은 덕에 거의 가까운 벗이 되어 있었다. 수석 사제는 딱한 크롤리 씨의 아이들이 호글스톡에서 실려 나온 그 방식이 몹시 마음에 들어, 프램리 집안 사람들 모두에게 속을 털어놓고 싶은 마음이 되어 있었다. 그는 아직 말을 타고 집까지 돌아가야 했으므로 저녁을 먹은 뒤로 반 시간밖에는 머물기를 스스로에게 허락할 수 없었으나, 그 반 시간 동안 크롤리에 관하여 많은 말을 하였고, 착한 사마리아인의 몫을 해내는 그 방식을 두고 로바츠를 칭찬하였고, 그러고 나서는 차츰차츰, 바체스터를 떠나기 전에 자기 곧 수석 사제의 귀에 이런 말이 들어왔다고 알려 주었다. 시티의 어떤 사람들 손에 영장이 들어 있어서 그들이 프램리 교구 사제를 — 몸을 붙잡는 것인지 재산을 붙잡는 것인지는 자기도 알지 못하겠으나 — 붙잡을 수 있게 되었다는 것이었다.\n\n사실 이 소식은 수석 사제가 로바츠에게 조심하라고 일러 줄 수 있게 하려는 뜻에서 일부러 그에게 전해진 것이었다. 그러나 동료 성직자에게 그런 문제를 두고 말을 꺼내는 일이 그에게는 어찌나 언짢았던지, 그는 떠나기 앞서 마지막 오 분이 되기까지 그 말을 시작하지 못하였다.\n\n“이것을 무람없는 간섭으로 여기지는 않으시겠지요.” 수석 사제가 미안해하며 말했다.\n\n“아닙니다,” 마크가 말했다. “아니에요, 그렇게 여기지 않습니다.” 그는 마음이 어찌나 무거웠던지 그 일을 어떻게 말해야 할지 좀처럼 알 수 없었다.\n\n“나는 그런 일을 잘 알지 못해요.” 수석 사제가 말했다. “그러나 내가 그 처지에 있다면 변호사를 찾아가 보겠어요. 체포처럼 그렇게 끔찍하게 언짢은 일이야 피할 수 있으리라고 나는 여겨요.”\n\n“가혹한 일입니다.” 마크가 제 사정을 변호하며 말했다. “그 사람들이 저를 상대로 이런 청구를 쥐고 있다지만, 저는 돈으로든 돈어치로든 단 한 실링도 받은 적이 없습니다.”\n\n“그런데도 어음에 이름이 올라 있군요!” 수석 사제가 말했다.\n\n“예, 제 이름이 어음에 올라 있는 것은 틀림없습니다. 그러나 벗의 청을 들어주려고 한 일이었습니다.”\n\n그러고서 수석 사제는 제 조언을 다 하고 나서 말을 타고 떠났다. 로바츠 씨와 같은 처지에 놓인 성직자가, 만기에 갚을 힘도 없는 융통 어음에 제 이름을 붙이라고 우정에게서 청함을 받는 일이 어떻게 있을 수 있는지 그는 도무지 알 수 없었다!\n\n그날 저녁 목사관에서는 두 사람 다 넉넉히 참담하였다. 이때까지 마크는 어쩌면 이 어음들을 두고 자기에게 정말로 적대의 걸음을 내딛는 일은 없으리라고 바라 왔었다. 미리 헤아리지 못한 어떤 기회가 제게 이롭게 생길지도 모르고, 어음을 쥔 사람들이 이따금씩 조금씩 나누어 갚는 것을 받아들여 줄지도 모른다고. 그러나 이제는 재앙의 날이 정말로 한꺼번에 그에게 닥쳐오는 듯하였다. 그는 이제 아내에게 아무 비밀도 없었다. 변호사를 찾아갈 것인가? 그렇다면 어느 변호사에게? 그리고 변호사를 찾아냈을 때 그에게 무엇을 말할 것인가? 로바츠 부인은 한때 모든 것을 레이디 러프턴께 말씀드려야 한다고 권해 보았다. 그러나 마크는 도저히 그럴 마음이 되지 않았다. “그러면,” 그가 말했다. “내가 그분더러 돈을 빌려 달라고 하는 것처럼 보일 거야.”\n\n이튿날 아침 마크는 정말로 말을 타고 바체스터로 들어갔다. 그러나 가는 길에 붙잡히지나 않을까 겁을 내면서였다. 그리고 그는 정말로 변호사를 만났다. 그가 집을 비운 사이에 목사관에는 두 번 찾아온 사람이 있었다. 하나는 몹시 거칠어 보이는 사내였는데, 이 나라 판사 가운데 한 사람이 보낸 초대장 — 저녁 초대는 아닌 — 이라 할 만한 수상한 문서 한 장을 하인의 손에 남기고 갔다. 그리고 또 한 번의 방문은 레이디 러프턴이 몸소 온 것이었다.\n\n로바츠 부인은 그날 프램리 코트에 내려가기로 마음을 정해 두었었다. 늘 하던 대로라면 레이디 러프턴이 런던에서 돌아온 지 한두 시간 안에 벌써 거기 가 있었을 터인데, 이제 두 사람 사이는 여느 때와 같지 않았다. 루시의 이 일은 두 사람이 아무리 그러지 않으려 마음을 다잡아도 어쩔 수 없이 무엇인가를 달라지게 만들 것이었다. 그리고 정말로 로바츠 부인은, 루시가 러프턴 경과 처음 다정한 사이가 되기 시작한 뒤로 프램리 코트와의 그 가까움이 날마다 줄어들어 왔다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그 뒤로 그녀는 여느 때보다 프램리 코트에 덜 가 있었고, 마님이 집을 비운 동안 지난 해들보다 편지를 덜 받았으며, 교구의 모든 일을 예전처럼 다 믿고 맡겨 주지 않는다는 것도 알고 있었다. 이것이 그녀를 노엽게 하지는 않았으니, 그럴 수밖에 없는 일이라고 어느 만큼은 스스로 느끼고 있었기 때문이다. 그것은 그녀를 서럽게 하였으나, 어찌할 수 있었겠는가? 루시를 탓할 수도 없었고 레이디 러프턴을 탓할 수도 없었다. 러프턴 경은 정말로 탓하였으나, 남편 말고는 아무도 듣지 않는 데에서만 그렇게 하였다.\n\n그러나 그녀의 마음은 건너가서 마님의 따짐을 첫 매로 받아 내기로 굳어져 있었는데, 마님이 몸소 찾아오는 바람에 걸음이 멈추었다. 루시의 그 끔찍한 사랑 문제 — 이제 만나면 서로 입을 다물고 있을 수 없는 그 문제 — 가 아니라면 즐거운, 아니 적어도 언짢지는 않은 이야깃거리가 스무 가지는 있을 터였다. 그러나 그렇더라도 그 끔찍한 어음들이 그녀의 양심 위에 걸려 있어 그 무게로 그녀를 거의 짓눌러 놓았을 것이다. 레이디 러프턴이 응접실 창 쪽으로 걸어 올라온 그 순간, 로바츠 부인은 판사가 보낸 그 불길한 초대장을 손에 쥐고 있었다. 남편이 한 말은 접어 두고 죄다 털어놓아 버리는 것이 낫지 않을까? 나을지도 몰랐다. 다만 이것이 있었다 — 그녀는 여태까지 남편의 뜻을 거스르는 일을 한 번도 해 본 적이 없었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그 종이쪽을 문갑 안에 숨기고, 그 문제는 더 생각해 볼 여지로 남겨 두었다.\n\n만남은 당연한 일로서 다정한 포옹으로 시작되었다. “패니, 얘야” 하는 말과 “레이디 러프턴” 하는 말이 여느 때와 같은 따뜻함을 다하여 서로 오갔다. 그러고서 첫 물음은 아이들에 관한 것이었고 둘째는 학교에 관한 것이었다. 일 분이나 이 분 동안 로바츠 부인은 어쩌면 루시에 관해서는 아무 말도 없을지 모른다고 생각했다. 레이디 러프턴이 입을 다물고 있는 편이 좋다면 그녀 쪽에서는 적어도 그 이야기를 먼저 꺼내지 않을 참이었다.\n\n그러고 나서 포진스 부인의 아기에 관하여 한두 마디가 오갔고, 그 뒤에 레이디 러프턴은 패니가 혼자 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n“예,” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. “마크는 바체스터에 건너갔습니다.”\n\n“너무 오래 있다가 얼굴을 보여 주는 것이 아니었으면 좋겠어요. 어쩌면 내일 들를 수 있겠지요. 내일 두 사람이 와서 저녁을 먹지 않겠어요?”\n\n“내일은 어려울 것 같습니다, 레이디 러프턴. 그렇지만 마크는 틀림없이 건너가서 뵙고 올 것입니다.”\n\n“그런데 왜 저녁을 먹으러 오지 못하지요? 우리 사이에 달라지는 것이 없기를 바라요, 그렇지 않아요, 패니?” 그리고 레이디 러프턴은 그렇게 말하면서 상대의 얼굴을 들여다보았는데, 그 눈길이 어찌나 그러하였는지 로바츠 부인은 하마터면 일어나 옛 벗의 목에 몸을 던질 뻔하였다. 레이디 러프턴에게서 받아 온 것만큼 한결같은 사랑을 주는 벗을 그녀가 어디서 찾겠는가? 그리고 그분보다 더 다정하고 더 좋고 더 정직한 사람이 누구겠는가?\n\n“달라진다니요! 아니에요, 그러지 않기를 바랍니다, 레이디 러프턴.” 그리고 그렇게 말하면서 그녀의 눈에는 눈물이 고였다.\n\n“아, 그렇지만 하던 대로 나를 찾아오지 않는다면 나는 달라진 것이 있다고 여기겠어요. 늘 내가 집에 돌아오는 날이면 으레 와서 나와 함께 저녁을 먹었잖아요.”\n\n딱한 여인이 이 말에 무엇이라 할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n“어제는 딱한 크롤리 부인 일로 온 집안이 뒤숭숭했고, 수석 사제께서 여기서 저녁을 드셨습니다. 그 벗을 보시려고 호글스톡에 다녀오셨거든요.”\n\n“그 부인이 앓는다는 이야기는 들었어요. 내가 건너가서 무엇을 해야 할지 보아야겠어요. 패니는 가지 말아요, 알겠지요? 어린 아이들을 둔 몸으로! 그랬다가는 내가 결코 용서하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n그러고서 로바츠 부인은 루시가 어떻게 거기 갔고, 네 아이를 프램리로 보내고, 지금은 크롤리 부인을 간호할 뜻으로 몸소 호글스톡에 머물고 있는지를 설명했다. 그 이야기를 하면서 그녀는, 루시의 이름과 사람됨이 지금 이 순간 레이디 러프턴에게 남다른 뜻을 지니지 않았다면 썼을 그 힘찬 말들로 루시를 칭송하는 일은 삼갔다. 그러나 그렇더라도 루시의 다정함을 여러 번 짚지 않고서는 그 이야기를 할 수 없었다. 지금 이 순간에 루시의 미덕을 크게 내세우는 것은 레이디 러프턴에게 너그럽지 못한 일이었을 터이나, 그것을 아무것도 아닌 것으로 만드는 것은 루시에게 옳지 못한 일이었을 터였다.\n\n“그러면 그 아가씨가 정말로 지금 크롤리 부인 곁에 있다는 말이지요?” 레이디 러프턴이 물었다.\n\n“예, 그렇습니다. 마크가 어제 오후에 거기 두고 왔습니다.”\n\n“그러면 아이 넷이 다 이 집에 있어요?”\n\n“꼭 집 안이라고는 할 수 없습니다 — 그러니까 아직은요. 마차간 위에 격리 병실 같은 것을 마련해 두었습니다.”\n\n“무엇이라고요, 스텁스가 사는 데에요?”\n\n“예. 스텁스와 그 아내는 집으로 들어왔고, 아이들은 의사가 이제 옮을 염려가 없다고 할 때까지 그 위에 있게 되어 있습니다. 저는 아직 제 손님들을 제 눈으로 보지도 못했습니다.” 로바츠 부인이 살짝 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“이런!” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “참으로 손이 빨랐군요. 그래서 로바츠 양이 그쪽에 가 있다는 말이지요! 나는 크롤리 씨가 아이들 일로 어렵게 굴었을 줄 알았어요.”\n\n“그러셨습니다. 그렇지만 두 사람이 아이들을 채어 왔습니다 — 그러니까 루시와 마크가요. 수석 사제께서 그 이야기를 어찌나 하시던지요. 루시가 둘씩 데리고 나와 조랑말 마차에 실어 넣고, 그러고서 크롤리 씨가 길에 서서 부르는 동안 마크는 말을 몰아 내달렸습니다. 그때 수석 사제께서 거기 계셔서 그 모든 것을 보셨습니다.”\n\n“패니네 그 루시 아가씨는 한번 마음에 들여놓은 일이 있으면 몹시 단단히 마음을 정하는 젊은 아가씨인 듯하군요.” 레이디 러프턴이 이제 처음으로 자리에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n“예, 그렇습니다.” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. 그 즐거운 활기를 죄다 내려놓고서였으니, 겁내던 그 이야기가 이제 바로 앞에 닥쳐 있었기 때문이다.\n\n“몹시 단단히 마음을 정하는 젊은 아가씨지요.” 레이디 러프턴이 이어 말했다. “물론 패니, 얘야, 루도빅과 시누이 되는 아가씨에 관한 이 모든 일을 알고 있겠지요?”\n\n“예, 루시가 저에게 이야기해 주었습니다.”\n\n“참으로 불행한 일이에요 — 참으로.”\n\n“저는 루시에게 탓이 있다고 생각하지 않습니다.” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하는 동안 벌써 피가 그녀의 뺨으로 올라오고 있었다.\n\n“얘야, 아무도 그 아가씨를 몰아세우지 않았는데 너무 애타게 변호하려 들지 말아요. 사람이 그러면 그 편이 약한 것처럼 보이는 법이에요.”\n\n“그렇지만 루시에 관해서라면 제 편은 약하지 않습니다. 저는 그 아이에게 탓이 없다고 아주 굳게 느낍니다.”\n\n“패니, 누군가의 투사로 스스로를 서임해야겠다고 여길 때에 얼마나 고집스러워질 수 있는지 나는 알아요. 돈키호테도 패니보다 나은 편력 기사는 아니었지요. 그렇지만 적이 보이는 데에도 들리는 데에도 오기 전에 창과 방패를 집어 드는 것은 딱한 일이 아닌가요? 하기야 그 돈키호테라는 이들은 늘 그런 식이었지요.”\n\n‘어쩌면 적이 숨어서 기다리고 있을지도 모르지요.’ 그것이 로바츠 부인이 속으로 한 생각이었으나, 감히 입 밖에 내지 못하고 잠잠히 있었다.\n\n“내 바람은 오직 하나예요.” 레이디 러프턴이 이어 말했다. “내가 등을 돌리고 없을 때에도 패니가 나를 위하여 그만큼 씩씩하게 싸워 준다는 것이지요.”\n\n“아, 마님께서는 딱한 루시처럼 구름 아래에 계시는 일이 없으니까요.”\n\n“그렇지 않아요? 그렇지만 패니, 패니는 그 구름을 다 보지 못하는 거예요. 우리 가운데 누구에게도 해가 늘 비추는 것은 아니고, 쏟아지는 비와 거센 바람은 내 가장 고운 꽃들도 흩어 놓아요 — 딱한 그 아가씨의 꽃들을 흩어 놓은 것과 같이요. 패니, 얘야, 패니의 하늘의 그 밝음 위로 어떤 구름이 지나가기까지 오래오래 걸리기를 나는 바라요. 내가 아는 모든 사람 가운데 패니야말로 조용히 이어지는 햇살에 가장 어울리는 사람이에요.”\n\n그러자 로바츠 부인은 정말로 일어나 벗을 껴안았고, 그리하여 얼굴을 흘러내리는 눈물을 감추었다. 이어지는 햇살이라니! 그녀의 지평 위에는 이미 검은 점 하나가 뭉쳐 있었고, 그것은 물기둥 같은 비가 되어 쏟아질 것 같았다. 지금 레이디 러프턴의 바로 그 팔 밑에 놓인 문갑 속에 들어 있는 그 끔찍한 통지는 어찌 될 것인가?\n\n“그렇지만 나는 늙은 까마귀처럼 흉한 소리나 내려고 여기 온 것이 아니에요.” 그 포옹을 끝낸 뒤에 레이디 러프턴이 이어 말했다. “우리 모두에게 저마다의 서러움이 있을 법하지요. 그러나 나는 이것만은 아주 굳게 알고 있어요 — 우리가 제 소임을 정직하게 하려고 힘쓴다면 우리 모두 위로를 찾아낼 것이고 또 저마다 기쁨도 갖게 될 것이라는 것을요. 그러니 이제, 얘야, 패니와 내가 이 불행한 일을 두고 몇 마디 해 보아요. 서로 입을 다물고 있다면 그것은 자연스럽지 못한 일이겠지요, 그렇지 않아요?”\n\n“그렇겠습니다.” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“우리는 늘 사실보다 나쁘게 그려 보게 될 거예요 — 서로 상대의 생각을 두고요. 그런데 얼마 전에 내가 시누이 되는 아가씨와 루도빅에 관하여 패니에게 말했을 때 — 아마 기억하겠지요 —”\n\n“예, 기억합니다.”\n\n“그때 우리 둘은 정말로 아무 위험이 없을 것이라고 생각했지요. 사실을 그대로 말하자면 나는 그 아이의 정이 다른 데에 걸려 있다고 여겼고, 정말이지 그러기를 바랐어요. 그러나 그때 나는 아주 틀렸지요. 그렇게 생각한 것도 틀렸고 그렇게 바란 것도 틀렸어요.”\n\n로바츠 부인은 레이디 러프턴이 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 두고 하는 말임을 잘 알았으나, 지금은 그 이야기를 두고 자기 쪽에서 아무 말도 하지 않는 것이 조심스러운 처사라고 여겼다. 그러나 조랑말 마차에서 러프턴 경이 그런 혼인을 치를 수도 있다는 말이 나왔을 때 루시의 눈이 번쩍하던 것을 그녀는 떠올렸고, 레이디 러프턴이 바라던 것을 이루지 못한 것을 기뻐하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“그 뒤에 일어난 일을 두고 나는 로바츠 양에게 조금도 탓을 돌리지 않아요.” 마님이 이어 말했다. “그것만은 패니가 똑똑히 알아주었으면 해요.”\n\n“어떻게 누가 그 아이를 탓할 수 있을지 저는 모르겠습니다. 그 아이는 참으로 고귀하게 처신했습니다.”\n\n“누가 그럴 수 있는지를 따져 보아야 아무 쓸데도 없어요. 내가 그러지 않는다는 것으로 넉넉해요.”\n\n“그렇지만 저는 그것으로는 좀처럼 넉넉하지 않다고 생각합니다.” 로바츠 부인이 물러서지 않고 말했다.\n\n“넉넉하지 않아요?” 마님이 눈썹을 치켜올리며 물었다.\n\n“아닙니다. 루시가 무엇을 했고 무엇을 하고 있는지 생각만 해 보십시오. 그 아이가 마님의 아드님을 받아들이겠다고 말하기로 마음먹었더라면, 마님께서 어떻게 그 아이를 옳게 탓하실 수 있었을지 저는 정말 모르겠습니다. 제가 그런 일을 권하였을 것이라고 말하는 것은 결코 아닙니다.”\n\n“그것은 반가운 말이에요, 패니.”\n\n“저는 아무 권고도 하지 않았고, 그럴 필요도 없습니다. 제 판단으로 어떤 길을 가야 할지를 루시보다 더 밝게 볼 수 있는 사람을 저는 알지 못합니다. 마음이 그토록 굳세고, 타고난 성정이 그토록 자기를 눌러 이기는 사람에게 권고를 하기는 저로서는 겁이 납니다. 그 아이는 지금 자기를 희생하고 있습니다. 마님과 아드님 사이에 번거로움과 다툼을 가져오는 구실이 되지 않으려는 까닭입니다. 저에게 물으신다면, 레이디 러프턴, 저는 마님께서 그 아이에게 깊이 고마워해야 할 빚을 지고 계시다고 생각합니다. 정말로 그렇게 생각합니다. 그리고 그 아이를 탓하신다니 — 마님께서 탓하실 만한 무슨 일을 그 아이가 했습니까?”\n\n“말에 오른 돈키호테로군요!” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “패니, 나는 앞으로 늘 패니를 돈키호테라고 부르겠어요. 그리고 언젠가는 누구를 시켜 패니의 모험담을 쓰게 하겠어요. 그렇지만 얘야, 사실은 이러해요. 조심스럽지 못한 일이 있었어요. 패니가 굳이 그러겠다면 그것을 내 탓이라 불러도 좋아요 — 하기야 내가 어떻게 그 탓을 져야 하는지는 정말 좀처럼 알 수 없지만요. 나는 로바츠 양을 내 집에 청하지 않을 수 없었고, 내 아들을 집에서 내쫓을 수도 없었지요. 사실 그대로 말하면 그것은 예부터 있는 그 이야기였어요.”\n\n“그렇습니다. 세상만큼이나 오래된 이야기이고, 사람들이 세상에 태어나는 동안에는 이어질 이야기지요. 하느님께서 몸소 하시는 이야기입니다!”\n\n“그렇지만 얘야, 젊은 신사와 젊은 아가씨라면 만나기가 바쁘게 서로 사랑에 빠져야 한다는 말은 아니겠지요! 그런 교리는 아주 거북한 것이 될 거예요.”\n\n“아닙니다, 그런 말은 아닙니다. 러프턴 경과 그랜틀리 양은 서로 사랑에 빠지지 않았습니다. 마님께서는 그러기를 뜻하셨지만요. 그렇지만 그 대신에 러프턴 경과 루시가 그렇게 되는 것도 그만큼 자연스러운 일이 아니었습니까?”\n\n“패니, 젊은 아가씨라면 벗들의 승낙을 확인받기까지는 제 정에 고삐를 놓아 주어서는 안 된다고 여기는 것이 보통이에요.”\n\n“그리고 재산 있는 젊은 신사들은 마음대로 즐겨도 좋다고요! 세상이 그렇게 가르치는 것은 저도 압니다. 그러나 저는 그것이 옳다고는 여길 수 없습니다. 루시가 견뎌야 하는 그 끔찍한 괴로움이 저로 하여금 그것에 맞서 소리치게 만듭니다. 그 아이는 마님의 아드님을 찾아다니지 않았습니다. 위험이 있을지도 모른다고 짐작하기 시작한 그 순간부터 그 아이는 그분을 애써 피했습니다. 프램리 코트에 내려가려 하지 않았고, 그러지 않는 것을 마님께서도 몸소 말씀하셨지요. 그분과 마주칠까 하여 이 근방으로 나가려고도 좀처럼 하지 않았습니다. 그분이 이곳을 떠나 주실 때까지 아주 뒤로 물러나 있는 것으로 그 아이는 흡족했습니다. 그러나 그분이 — 그분이 여기 이 아이를 찾아와 만나겠다고 우기셨습니다. 제가 나가 있을 때에 그 아이를 찾아내고, 이야기하기로 마음을 굳혔노라고 선언하셨습니다. 그 아이가 어찌해야 했겠습니까? 그 아이는 정말로 빠져나가려 했으나 그분이 문간에서 그 아이를 막아 세웠습니다. 그분이 그 아이에게 청혼한 것이 그 아이의 잘못입니까?”\n\n“얘야, 아무도 그렇게 말하지 않았어요.”\n\n“아닙니다, 마님께서 그렇게 말씀하시는 것입니다. 젊은 아가씨는 허락 없이 제 정을 마음대로 놀려서는 안 된다고 저에게 말씀하실 때에요. 그 아이가 제발 그만해 달라고 빌었는데도 그분은 여기서 하고 싶은 말을 다 하겠다고 버티셨습니다. 그 아이가 어떤 말을 썼는지 저는 옮길 수 없으나 그 아이는 정말로 빌었습니다.”\n\n“그 아가씨가 잘 처신했다는 것은 나도 의심하지 않아요.”\n\n“그러나 그분은 — 그분은 버티시며 제 손을 받아 달라고 애원하셨습니다. 그때 그 아이는 그분을 거절했습니다, 레이디 러프턴 — 어떤 처녀들이 하듯 거짓 사양으로, 그 말대로 받아들여지기를 바라지도 않으면서 그런 것이 아니라, 한결같이, 그리고 하느님께서 용서해 주시기를, 거짓으로 그리했습니다. 마님의 마음이 어떠할지를 알고, 세상이 무엇이라 할지를 알았기에, 그 아이는 그분이 자기에게는 아무렇지도 않은 사람이라고 그분께 밝혔습니다. 마님을 위하여 그 아이가 그보다 더 무엇을 할 수 있었겠습니까?” 그러고서 로바츠 부인은 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“패니가 말을 다 마치기까지 기다리겠어요.”\n\n“처녀들이 제 정에 고삐를 놓아 준다고 마님께서 말씀하셨지요. 그 아이는 그렇게 하지 않았습니다. 그 아이는 전에 하던 그대로 제 일을 해 나갔습니다. 지나간 일을 저에게 말하지도 않았습니다 — 적어도 그때에는요. 그 아이는 그 모든 것을 아예 없었던 일처럼 하기로 마음을 굳혔습니다. 그 아이는 마님의 아드님을 사랑하게 되었으나, 그것은 그 아이의 불행이었고 어떻게든 스스로 이겨 내려는 것이었습니다. 그분이 그랜틀리 양과 약혼했다거나 곧 약혼하려 한다는 소식이 이곳 우리에게 이르렀습니다.”\n\n“그 소식은 거짓이었어요.”\n\n“예, 이제는 우리도 압니다. 그러나 그때 그 아이는 그것을 알지 못했습니다. 물론 괴로워하지 않을 수 없었지요. 그러나 그 아이는 속으로만 괴로워했습니다.” 이 말을 하면서 로바츠 부인은 조랑말 마차와 퍽이 어떻게 매를 맞았는지를 떠올렸다. “그분이 자기를 나쁘게 대했다고 그 아이는 조금도 투정하지 않았습니다 — 스스로에게조차 그러지 않았습니다. 그 아이는 그 청혼을 물리치는 것이 옳다고 생각했고, 그분에 관한 한 그것으로 끝이 되어야 했습니다.”\n\n“그것이야 당연한 일이겠지요.”\n\n“그러나 그것은 당연한 일이 되지 않았습니다, 레이디 러프턴. 그분은 그 청혼을 되풀이하시려고 일부러 런던에서 프램리로 돌아오셨습니다. 그 아이의 오라비를 부르셨고 — 젊은 아가씨가 벗들의 승낙을 기다려야 한다고 마님께서 말씀하시지요. 이 일에서 루시의 벗들은 누구겠습니까?”\n\n“물론 패니와 로바츠 씨지요.”\n\n“그렇습니다. 그 아이의 유일한 벗들이지요. 그런데 러프턴 경은 마크를 부르셔서 그에게 그 청혼을 되풀이하셨습니다. 아셔야 할 것은, 마크는 그때까지 이 일을 한마디도 들은 적이 없었으니 놀랐는지 어떤지는 짐작이 되실 것입니다. 러프턴 경은 더없이 격식을 갖추어 그 청혼을 되풀이하시고 루시를 만나게 해 달라고 요구하셨습니다. 그 아이는 만나기를 마다했습니다. 온 힘을 다해 막았는데도 그분이 이 방으로 들어오셨던 그날 뒤로 그 아이는 그분을 만난 적이 없습니다. 마크는 — 제 생각에는 아주 옳게도 — 러프턴 경이 여기 올라오시게 하려 했습니다. 두 분의 나이와 처지를 보아 그가 그것을 막을 권리는 있을 수 없었지요. 그러나 루시는 마님의 아드님을 만나기를 딱 잘라 마다하고, 그 대신 전갈을 보냈습니다. 그 뜻은 이제 마님께서도 아시는 그것입니다 — 마님께서 청해 주시지 않는 한 결코 그분을 받아들이지 않겠다는 것이었습니다.”\n\n“아주 옳은 전갈이었어요.”\n\n“그것에 관해서는 저는 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다. 그 아이가 그분을 받아들였더라도 저는 그 아이를 탓하지 않았을 것입니다 — 그리고 그렇게 그 아이에게 말했습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“패니가 그런 말을 했다는 것은 나로서는 알아들을 수 없어요.”\n\n“그렇지만 저는 그렇게 말했습니다. 지금 저 자신에 관하여 — 제가 옳았는지 그르렀는지를 두고 다투고 싶지는 않습니다. 그러나 저는 정말로 그렇게 말했습니다. 제가 줄 수 있는 허락이라면 무엇이든 그 아이는 받았을 것입니다. 그러나 그 아이는 다시 제 몸을 희생하기를 골랐습니다. 처녀가 사내에게 느낀 어떤 사랑에도 못하지 않은 참된 사랑으로 그분에게 마음을 두고 있다고 저는 믿습니다만요. 참말이지 저는 그 아이가 옳은지 모르겠습니다. 세상을 헤아리는 그런 마음도 어쩌면 너무 멀리까지 끌고 가는 수가 있습니다.”\n\n“나는 그 아가씨가 아주 옳았다고 생각해요.”\n\n“좋습니다, 레이디 러프턴. 그것은 저도 알아듣습니다. 그러나 그 아이 쪽에서 그런 희생을 치른 뒤에 — 오로지 마님을 위하여 치른 희생인데 — 어떻게 ‘그 아이를 탓하지는 않는다’는 말씀을 하실 수 있습니까? 처음부터 끝까지 그 처신이 그 위없이 훌륭했던 사람을 두고 하는 말씨가 그런 것입니까? 그 아이에게 탓할 데가 조금이라도 있다면, 그것은 — 그것은 —”\n\n그러나 여기서 로바츠 부인은 스스로 말을 멈추었다. 시누이를 변호하다가 그녀는 저를 몰아 거의 격정에 이르렀던 것이다. 그러나 그런 심사는 그녀에게 익숙한 것이 아니었고, 이제 마음속을 다 말해 버리고 나자 그녀는 갑자기 잠잠함 속으로 가라앉았다.\n\n“패니는 로바츠 양의 결정을 거의 아쉬워하는 듯이 내게 보이는군요.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“이 일에서 제 바람은 그 아이의 행복입니다. 그리고 그것을 망칠 만한 것이라면 무엇이든 저는 아쉬워합니다.”\n\n“그러면 우리의 안녕은 아무렇지도 않다는 말이지요. 그런데도 패니가 아니라면 마음에서 나오는 우정과 어려울 때의 헤아림을 내가 누구에게 바랄 수 있었을지 나는 모르겠어요.”\n\n딱한 로바츠 부인은 이 말에 거의 무너질 뻔했다. 몇 달 전, 루시가 오기 전이라면 그녀는 레이디 러프턴 집안의 이해가 제 남편의 것 다음으로, 그 바로 뒤에 가장 으뜸이 된다고 잘라 말했을 것이다. 그리고 지금도 이 말은 — 자기가 그들에게 무심하다는 이 몰아세움은 — 제 쪽의 그토록 검은 배은망덕을 따지는 것으로 여겨졌다. 어린 시절부터 그녀는 레이디 러프턴을 우러르고 사랑해 왔고, 여러 해 동안 그분을 여인에게 있는 좋고 우아한 모든 것의 축도로 여기도록 스스로를 가르쳐 왔다. 레이디 러프턴의 삶에 관한 이론들은 그녀에게 옳은 이론으로 받아들여져 왔고, 레이디 러프턴이 마음에 들인 사람이면 그녀도 마음에 들였다. 그러나 이제는 한 생애가 걸려 세워 온 이 모든 생각이 땅에 던져지게 될 듯하였다. 지난 여덟 달 동안만 알아 온 시누이를 변호하지 않을 수 없기 때문이었다. 루시를 위하여 한 말을 한마디라도 아쉬워한다는 것은 아니었다. 우연이 그녀와 루시를 함께 던져 놓았고, 루시는 그녀의 자매였으니 그녀에게서 자매로서의 대접을 받아야 했다. 그러나 그렇더라도 레이디 러프턴과 갈라서는 일이 있다면 그 결과가 얼마나 끔찍할지를 그녀는 못지않게 느꼈다.\n\n“아, 레이디 러프턴,” 그녀가 말했다. “그런 말씀은 하지 마십시오.”\n\n“그렇지만 패니, 얘야, 나는 내가 겪는 대로 말해야 해요. 방금 구름 이야기를 했지요. 그러면 이 모든 것이 내 하늘의 구름이 아니라고 생각해요? 루도빅은 로바츠 양에게 마음이 붙어 있다고 나에게 말하고, 패니는 그 아가씨가 루도빅에게 마음이 붙어 있다고 나에게 말하지요. 그리고 나는 그 둘 사이를 판결하라는 요구를 받고 있어요. 그 아가씨의 그 행동이 바로 나로 하여금 그러지 않을 수 없게 만들어요.”\n\n“레이디 러프턴,” 로바츠 부인이 자리에서 벌떡 일어나며 말했다. 그 순간 그녀에게는 이 모든 어려움이 옛 벗의 너그러운 처분 하나로 풀리려는 것처럼 여겨졌다.\n\n“그런데도 나는 그런 혼인을 승낙할 수 없어요.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n로바츠 부인은 더 아무 말도 하지 않고 제 자리로 돌아갔다.\n\n“그것이 사람의 지평 위에 뜬 구름이 아닌가요?” 마님이 이어 말했다. “그런 무게를 마음에 얹고 있으면서 내가 햇살을 쬐며 편안히 있을 수 있다고 생각해요? 루도빅이 곧 집에 올 텐데, 나는 그 돌아옴을 기쁘게 기다리기는커녕 겁을 내고 있어요. 나는 그 아이가 노르웨이에 그대로 있어 주었으면 해요. 몇 달이고 떠나 있어 주었으면 해요. 그리고 패니, 패니가 나를 헤아려 주지 않는다고 느끼는 것은 내 불행에 크게 보태지는 일이에요.”\n\n느리고 서럽고 매서운 어조로 이 말을 하고 나서 레이디 러프턴은 일어나 떠났다. 물론 로바츠 부인은 자기가 정말로 마님을 헤아리고 있다고, 여태 사랑해 온 그대로 정말로 사랑하고 있다고 다짐해 두지 않고서는 그분을 보내지 않았다. 그러나 상처는 입히기만큼 쉽게 나을 수 있는 것이 아니어서, 레이디 러프턴은 마음에 참으로 큰 서러움을 안고 제 길을 갔다. 그녀는 거만하고 남을 휘두르려 했고, 제 뜻대로 하기를 좋아했고, 제 처지가 불러 준 세상의 위엄에 지나칠 만큼 마음을 썼다. 그러나 그녀는 사랑하는 이들에게 서러움을 주면서 스스로 깊은 서러움을 겪지 않을 수 없는 여인이었다.\n\n# 제42장. 역청을 만지다\n\n한여름의 이 더운 날들, 곧 유월 끝과 칠월 초에 사워비 씨는 마음 편치 못한 시절을 보냈다. 누이동생의 권함에 따라 그는 서둘러 런던으로 올라갔고, 거기서 여러 날을 변호사들에게 매여 있었다. 그는 새 변호사들, 곧 던스터블 양의 법률 대리인들을 만나야 했다. 은행 뒤편 어두운 골목에 사무소를 둔, 조용하고 늙고 조심스러운 신사들이었고 이름은 슬로 바이더와일 상회였는데, 그들이나 그들의 서기가 무엇이든 이야기를 하거나 아무것도 아닌 것을 이야기하는 동안 그를 몇 시간이나 붙잡아 두는 데 조금도 거리낌이 없었다. 사워비 씨에게는 이 일이 미루어지지 않고 마무리되는 것이 목숨이 걸린 만큼 중대한 일이었으나, 이 마무리를 이제 맡아 보게 된 이 사람들은 법의 절차란 사람들이 느긋하게 햇볕을 쬐며 즐거워하는 햇살 좋은 언덕이라도 되는 듯이 굴었다. 그러고서 그는 또 한 번 이상 사우스 오들리 스트리트에 가야 했는데, 그것은 더한 곤욕이었다. 사우스 오들리 스트리트의 사람들이 이제는 전에 하던 버릇보다 덜 공손했기 때문이다. 사워비 씨가 이제는 공작의 의뢰인이 아니라 그의 맞수요, 이제는 그의 추천을 받은 자요 딸린 사람이 아니라 이 주에서 그의 원수라는 것이 거기서는 잘 알려져 있었다. “콜디코츠는,” 늙은 검션 씨가 젊은 게이지비 씨에게 이렇게 말했다. “콜디코츠는, 게이지비, 사워비에 관한 한 이미 구워 놓은 거위야. 그리고 그것을 공작이 갖게 되든 던스터블 양이 갖게 되든 그에게 무슨 차이가 있단 말인가? 나로서는 사워비 같은 신사가 어떻게 제 땅이, 그 돈에서 아직도 못된 약 냄새가 나는 약단지 계집의 손에 넘어가는 것을 보고 싶어 할 수 있는지 도무지 알 수가 없어. 그리고 사워비가 하는 짓만큼 은혜를 모르는 것도 없지.” 그는 말했다. “그는 스물다섯 해 동안 비용 한 푼 들이지 않고 이 주를 지녀 왔는데, 이제 값을 치를 때가 오니 그 값을 아까워하고 있어.” 그는 그것을 속임질보다 나은 무엇으로도 부르지 않았다, 그러지 않았다 — 그, 검션 씨는. 그의 생각에 따르면 사워비는 공작을 속이려 하고 있는 것이었다. 그러니 사워비 씨가 사우스 오들리 스트리트에 드나드는 일에서 그리 큰 즐거움을 느끼지 못했으리라는 것은 짐작할 만하다.\n\n그러고 나서 어음을 할인하는 온갖 말거머리들 사이에, 사워비의 시체에서 다시 한번 피를 빨아낼 수 있게 되었다는 소문이 퍼졌다. 그 부유한 던스터블 양이 그의 일을 떠맡았다는 것, 그만큼은 「염소와 나침반」 근방에 알려졌다. 톰 토저의 형은 그 여인과 사워비가 혼사를 이루려 하고 있으니 사워비의 이름이 적힌 종이쪽이라면 무엇이든 그 무게만큼의 은행권 값이 나가게 될 것이라고 떠벌렸다. 그러나 그 집안의 진짜 영웅인 톰 토저 자신은 코를 찡그리며 이것을 콧방귀로 물리쳤고, 제 형의 무름을 두고 몹시 업신여기는 말을 늘어놓았다. 그는 더 잘 알고 있었으니, 실로 그것이 사실이었다. 던스터블 양은 그 지주를 사들이고 있었고, 맹세코 그 여자는 그들까지 사들여야 했다 — 그들, 곧 다른 사람들 못지않게 이 토저들까지도! 그들은 제 값을 알고 있었다, 이 토저들은 알고 있었다. 그리하여 그들은 여느 때보다 더 부지런해졌다.\n\n이때 사워비 씨는 그들과 그 모든 동류에게서 거리를 두려고 애썼으나, 그 애씀이 온전히 들어맞지는 않았다. 하루나 이틀 변호사들에게서 빠져나올 수 있을 때마다 그는 콜디코츠로 달려 내려갔다. 그러나 톰 토저는 그 끈질김으로 거기까지 그를 따라 내려와, 앞문의 하인을 통해 뻔뻔스럽게 제 이름을 들여보냈다.\n\n“사워비 씨는 지금 이 순간 집에 계시지 않습니다요.” 잘 길들여진 하인이 그렇게 말했다.\n\n“그럼 나는 여기서 기다리고 있겠소.” 톰은 그렇게 말하고, 집 앞의 큰 돌계단 양옆을 지키고 선 문장 무늬의 돌 그리핀에 올라앉았다. 그리하여 이런 식으로 토저 씨는 제 뜻을 이루었다. 사워비는 아직 이 주의 의석을 다투고 있었으므로, 자기가 숨어 있다는 말을 원수들이 하게 두어서는 안 되었다. 이 주의 의석을 다툰다는 것은 던스터블 양과 맺은 그의 약조의 한 부분이었다. 공작이 못되게 굴었다는 생각이 그녀의 머리에 박혀 있었고, 그 값을 그가 치르게 하겠다고 그녀는 마음을 굳혔던 것이다. “공작이,” 그녀는 말했다. “참견을 충분히 오래 했지요.” 이제 그녀는 공작에 맞서서라도 콜디코츠 쪽 이해만으로 이 주의 의원 한 사람을 당선시키기에 넉넉하지 않은지 지켜볼 참이었다. 사워비 씨 자신은 그때 어찌나 시달리고 있었던지, 힘이 있었다면 이 점에서는 물러섰을 것이다. 그러나 던스터블 양의 뜻이 굳었으므로 그는 그녀에게 굽히지 않을 수 없었다. 이런 식으로 톰 토저 씨는 뜻을 이루어 사워비 씨의 앞까지 밀고 들어갔으니, 그 밀고 들어옴의 한 가지 결과가 사워비 씨가 제 벗 마크 로바츠에게 보낸 다음 편지였다.\n\n콜디코츠, 185─년 7월.\n\n친애하는 로바츠, — 나는 지금 이 순간 나 자신의 끝없는 곤경에 어찌나 시달리고 있는지 남의 곤경에는 거의 무디어져 있습니다. 사람이 형편이 좋으면 이기적으로 된다고들 하지요. 나는 그것을 겪어 본 적이 없으나, 형편이 나빠지면 그렇게 된다는 것은 아주 확실히 알고 있습니다. 그렇더라도 나는 당신의 그 어음들이 걱정입니다 —\n\n“내 어음이라니!” 목사관의 관목 산책길을 오르내리며 이 편지를 읽던 로바츠가 혼자 그렇게 말했다. 이 일은 그가 바체스터의 변호사를 찾아간 지 하루 이틀 뒤에 있었다.\n\n— 그리고 그 일로 당신이 더 이상 괴롭힘을 겪지 않도록 내가 막아 줄 수 있다고 여겨진다면 나는 크게 기뻐할 것입니다. 그 솔개 같은 톰 토저가 방금 나에게 와서 두 장 다 갚아야 한다고 우기고 있어요. 나중 것에는 아무 대가도 주어지지 않았다는 것을 그는 누구보다도 잘 알고 있습니다. 그러나 그 거래가 자기와 된 것이 아니고 자기 형과 된 것조차 아니라는 것 역시 알고 있으니, 그는 두 장 모두에 값을 치렀다고 맹세할 준비가 되어 있을 겁니다. 오백 파운드를 준다면 그는 무엇이든 맹세할 사람이고, 그 절반이라도 그 점은 마찬가지예요. 온갖 악의 아비가 톰 토저보다 더한 악당을 세상에 풀어놓은 적은 없다고 나는 생각합니다.\n\n그는 구백 파운드 전액에서 단 한 실링이라도 덜 받게 만들 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없다고 못 박고 있습니다. 내 빚이 곧 갚아진다는 말을 들은 탓에 이렇게 나온 것이지요. 하느님이 나를 도우시기를! 그 말의 뜻은, 지금 어느 백만장자에게 저당 잡혀 있는 이 딱한 땅뙈기가 임자를 바꾸어 그 대신 다른 백만장자에게 저당 잡히게 된다는 것뿐입니다. 이 바꿈으로 내가 얻을 이익이라면 앞으로 열두 달 동안 살 집이 있다는 것뿐이고, 그 밖에는 아무것도 없어요. 그러나 토저는 냄새를 아주 잘못 짚었습니다. 그리고 가장 나쁜 것은 그 앙심이 나에게보다 당신에게 떨어질 것이라는 점입니다.\n\n내가 당신에게 바라는 것은 이것입니다. 우리 둘이 나누어 그에게 백 파운드를 갚읍시다. 나에게 남은 마지막 비루먹은 말을 팔더라도 나는 오십을 채우겠습니다. 그리고 당신도 적어도 그만큼은 할 수 있다는 것을 나는 알아요. 그러고 나서 당신이 나와 함께 팔백 파운드 어음 한 장을 인수해 주세요. 그것은 포레스트가 보는 자리에서 하여 그의 손에 넘길 것이고, 당신은 그와 동시에 낡은 어음 두 장을 당신 손에 되돌려 받을 것입니다. 이 새 어음은 구십 일 기한으로 잡아야 해요. 그리고 그 동안에 나는 하늘과 땅을 움직여, 콜디코츠 땅에 담보로 잡히게 될 내 빚의 전체 목록에 그것을 넣도록 하겠습니다.\n\n그 말의 뜻은, 그 돈이 지금 있는 저당에 들어 있는 액수의 일부라고 여기게 하여 던스터블 양을 속여 그것을 내게 하겠다는 것이었다.\n\n요전 날 바체스터에서 다시는 어음을 작성하지 않겠다고 당신이 한 말은, 앞으로의 거래에 관해서라면 아주 좋습니다. 그런 결심보다 더 지혜로운 것은 없지요. 그러나 그것을 막을 방편이 당신 손에 그토록 가까이 있는데도 당신 가구가 압류되도록 두는 것은 어리석은 일 — 어리석음보다 못한 일일 겁니다. 새 어음을 포레스트의 손에 남겨 두면 이 토저네 같은 맹금들의 발톱에서 당신이 안전하다는 것을 확신할 수 있어요. 석 달이 지날 때까지 내가 그것을 마무리하지 못한다 해도, 포레스트는 당신에게 가장 편한 어떤 조정이라도 할 수 있게 해 줄 것입니다.\n\n부디, 이 사람아, 이것을 거절하지 마세요. 집행관들이 당신 아내의 응접실까지 들어갈지 모른다는 이 두려움이 나를 어떻게 짓누르는지 당신은 좀처럼 헤아리지 못할 겁니다. 당신이 나를 나쁘게 여기는 것을 나도 알고, 그것을 이상하게 여기지도 않아요. 그러나 내가 얼마나 무섭게 벌을 받고 있는지 안다면 그런 마음이 덜해질 것입니다. 내가 권하는 대로 하겠다는 말을 부디 들려주세요.\n\n언제나 충실한 당신의 벗,\n\n이에 대한 답으로 그 목사는 아주 짧은 답장을 썼다.\n\n프램리, 185─년 7월.\n\n나는 어떤 이유를 들어도 더는 어음에 내 이름을 적지 않겠습니다.\n\n그럼 이만,\n\n그러고 나서 이것을 써서 아내에게 보인 뒤에, 그는 다시 관목 산책길로 돌아가 그 길을 오르내리며, 그 신사와 벗하여 온 지난 사정들을 두루 되짚어 보면서 이따금 사워비의 편지를 들여다보았다.\n\n이 편지를 쓴 사람이 제 벗이라니 — 바로 그 사실이 그에게는 부끄러움이었다. 사워비는 제 자신과 제 소문을 어찌나 잘 알고 있었던지, 제 말이 무엇으로든 받아들여질 것이라고는 감히 짐작조차 하지 못했다. 약속한 것이 가장 흔해빠진 정직에 속하는 일일 때조차 그러했다. “낡은 어음들은 당신 손에 되돌려 줄 것입니다.” 그는 힘을 주어 그렇게 못 박아 두었으니, 그만큼 단단한 보증이 아니면 제 벗이요 편지 상대인 사람이 더 이상의 속임수에서 스스로 안전하다고 느끼지 못할 것을 알았기 때문이다. 마크 로바츠가 그토록 가까이 사귀고 싶어 했던 이 신사, 이 주 선출 의원, 콜디코츠의 주인은, 이제 자기 자신을 정직한 사람이라고 말하는 일을 아예 그만두어 버린 그런 삶의 국면에까지 이르러 있었다. 그는 의심받는 일에 어찌나 익숙해졌던지 그것을 으레 있는 일처럼 놓고 이야기했다. 입으로 한 말이든 글로 쓴 말이든 아무도 자기를 믿을 수 없다는 것을 그는 알았고, 이 확신을 감추려는 시도조차 없이 말하고 쓰는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n그런데 이 사람이 바로 그가 제 벗이라고 부르기를 그토록 기뻐했던 사람이었다. 이 사람을 위해서 그는 레이디 러프턴과 다툴 마음까지 있었고, 이 사람의 권함에 따라 제 평생의 가장 좋은 결심들을 그토록 여러 개나 알지 못하는 사이에 버렸다. 이제 그는 편지를 여전히 손에 든 채 그곳을 천천히 걸으면서, 학교 집에 들러 콜디코츠의 모임에 가겠다고 약속하는 편지를 사워비 씨에게 써 보냈던 그날을 되돌아보았다. 그때 그는 제 뜻대로 하고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 급했던지, 집에 돌아가 아내와 그 일을 놓고 의논하는 것조차 스스로에게 허락하지 않았다. 그는 또 자기가 옴니엄 공작의 집으로 꾀여 들어간 그 방식을, 그리고 그 꾐에 넘어가면 반드시 화를 입게 된다고 그때 마음에 품었던 확신을 생각했다. 그러고 나서 그는 사워비의 침실에서의 그 저녁을 떠올렸다. 어음이 꺼내어졌고, 그 위에 이름을 적으라는 설득에 넘어가도록 스스로를 내버려 둔 그 저녁을. 이런 식으로 벗을 돕고 싶은 마음이 있어서가 아니라, 거절할 수가 없어서였다. 그는 “아니오”라고 말할 용기가 없었다. 그때 자기가 저지르고 있는 잘못이 얼마나 큰지 알고 있었는데도. 그는 “아니오”라고 말할 용기가 없었고, 그리하여 이 모든 참담함과 쓰라린 뉘우침의 까닭이 그와 그의 집안에 닥친 것이었다.\n\n나는 성직자들에 관하여 많이 써 왔으나, 그렇게 하면서 그들의 직업상 행로가 어떤 방식으로 어떻게 굴러가는지를 그리기보다는 그들이 우리 사회 생활에 어떻게 걸려 있는지를 그려 보려고 애썼다. 뒤의 것을 했더라면 나는 의견을 밝힐 뜻이 없었던 주제들을 좀처럼 피해 갈 수 없었을 것이고, 내 소설에 설교를 잔뜩 실었거나 아니면 내 설교를 소설로 떨어뜨려 놓았을 것이다. 그러므로 나는 이 사람이 성직자로서 느끼고 행한 것에 관하여 내 이야기 속에서 거의 말하지 않았다.\n\n그러나 그 때문에 로바츠 씨가 제 성직의 자리에 딸린 의무에 무심했다고 여겨지는 것에는 나도 항변하지 않을 수 없다. 그는 즐거움을 좋아했고 꾐에 넘어갔으니, 단속하는 손이 미치지 않는 자리에 놓이고 마음대로 쓸 재물을 가진 스물여섯의 젊은이들이 흔히 그러하는 것과 같았다. 그 나이까지 부목사로 남아 움직임마다 윗사람의 눈 아래에 있었더라면, 우리는 이렇게 말할 수 있으리라, 그는 어떤 어음에도 이름을 적지 않았을 것이고, 사냥개를 따라 말을 달리지도 않았을 것이고, 개서럼 성의 죄악을 하나도 보지 않았을 것이다. 스물여섯에 이미 앞으로도 더는 나아지지 않을 만큼 홀로 설 만한 사람들이 있다 — 총리도, 학교의 교장도, 법관석의 판사도 될 만한, 거의 주교가 될 만한 사람들이. 그러나 마크 로바츠는 그런 사람들 가운데 하나가 아니었다. 그의 안에는 좋은 것을 향한 소질이 많이 있었으나, 그것에 걸맞게 행할 사내의 다져진 용기는 없었다. 그의 사내다움을 이룰 재목은 자라는 것이 느렸으니, 많은 사람이 그러한 대로였다. 그리하여 꾐이 그에게 내밀어졌을 때 그는 넘어졌다.\n\n그러나 그는 제 넘어짐을 깊이 슬퍼했고, 뉘우침의 시기가 이따금 그에게 찾아올 때마다, 갑주를 입고 나선 그 싸움을 땅에서 싸우는 사람에게 걸맞게 다시 한번 수레바퀴에 어깨를 대겠다고 마음을 굳혔다. 그는 크롤리 씨의 그 말을 몇 번이고 되풀이해 생각했고, 이제 사워비 씨의 편지를 손안에서 구기며 그 길을 오르내리는 동안 다시 그것을 생각했다 — “이것은 무서운 타락입니다. 떨어지는 것이 무섭고, 돌아오기가 그토록 어려우니 곱절로 무섭습니다.” 그렇다. 사람이 그 길을 즐거이 달려 내려가는 동안 — 어디를 향하여? — 무섭게 불어나는 비율로 스스로를 곱해 가는 어려움이 바로 그것이다. 그는 이제 돌아갈 수 없는 데까지 와 버린 것인가 — 제 교구의 목자로서 편안한 양심으로 다시는 머리를 들 수 없는 데까지! 그를 이 참담함으로 이끈 것은 사워비였고, 그에게 이 파멸을 가져온 것도 사워비였던가? 그러나 그렇다면 사워비가 그에게 값을 치르지 않았던가? 그가 지금 바체스터에서 지니고 있는 저 참사회원 자리가 사워비의 선물이 아니었던가? 그는 이제 가난한 사람이었다 — 곤궁하고 가난에 짓눌린 사람이었다. 그러면서도 그는 바체스터 성당 참사회의 좋은 것들을 나누어 받는 사람이 애초에 되지 않았더라면 하고 온 마음으로 바랐다.\n\n“그 참사회원 자리를 내놓겠어.” 그날 밤 그가 아내에게 말했다. “그것만은 마음을 정했다고 말할 수 있을 것 같아.”\n\n“하지만, 마크, 사람들이 그것을 이상하다고 하지 않겠어요?”\n\n“어쩔 수 없어 — 사람들이야 그렇게 말하겠지. 패니, 우리는 그보다 더한 말도 들어야 하게 될까 걱정이야.”\n\n“당신이 옳지 않거나 떳떳하지 못한 일을 했다고는 아무도 말할 수 없어요. 사워비 씨 같은 사람들이 있는 것이라면 —”\n\n“그의 잘못이 검다고 해서 내 잘못이 용서되지는 않아.” 그러고 나서 그는 다시 잠자코 앉아 눈을 가렸고, 그의 옆에 앉은 아내는 그의 손을 잡고 있었다.\n\n“당신을 참담하게 만들지 말아요, 마크. 일은 아직 다 잘될 거예요. 몇백 파운드를 잃는다고 당신이 망할 수는 없어요.”\n\n“돈이 문제가 아니야 — 돈이 문제가 아니야!”\n\n“하지만 당신은 잘못한 것이 없어요, 마크.”\n\n“집에 집행관이 들어와 있다는 것을 사람마다 다 알고 있는데, 내가 어떻게 교회에 들어가 그들 모두 앞에서 내 자리에 설 수 있겠어?” 그러고 나서 그는 탁자에 머리를 떨구고 소리 내어 흐느꼈다.\n\n마크 로바츠의 잘못은 주로 이것이었다. 그는 역청을 만지고도 더러워지지 않으리라고 생각했던 것이다. 그는 제 즐거운 목사관에서 제 둘레의 즐거운 상류 계층을 내다보며, 그쪽의 사람과 사물이 아주 마음을 끄는 것을 보았다. 다정한 아내가 있는 제 목사관이 그에게 지극히 소중했고, 레이디 러프턴의 애정 어린 벗함에도 그 나름의 값이 있었다. 그러나 해로와 옥스퍼드에서 가장 좋은 패에 끼어 살아 본 사람에게 이런 것들은 좀 심심하지 않았겠는가 — 더 활기찬 삶을 이따금 한바탕씩 곁들여 보탤 수 있는 것이 아니라면? 케이크와 맥주는 전에 대학에서 함께 살던 사람들의 입에 즐거웠던 만큼 그의 입에도 즐거웠다. 말을 보는 눈도 그들과 같았고, 그 말을 몰아 들판을 가로지르게 하는 담도 그들과 같았다. 게다가 그는 사람들이 자기를 좋아한다는 것을 알게 되었다 — 남자들도, 여자들도, 그것도 세상의 지위가 높은 남자와 여자들이. 그의 나귀 귀는 간지럼을 탔고, 그는 자기가 본디 높은 사람들의 자리에 어울리도록 태어났다고 여기는 버릇을 익혔다. 유행을 아는 사람들과 부자들의 집에서 세상의 남자와 여자들을 만나는 것이 마치 자기에게 정해진 길을 따라가는 것처럼 보였다. 그렇게 살아서 그렇게 잘된 성직자가 그가 처음은 아니었다. 그렇다, 성직자들이 그렇게 살아 왔고, 제 삶의 자리에서 제 나라 사람들의 — 그리고 제 임금들의 — 만족에 온전히 들어맞도록 제 의무를 다해 왔다. 그리하여 마크 로바츠는 역청을 만지되 될 수만 있으면 더러워지는 것은 면하겠다고 마음을 굳혔던 것이다. 그 결과가 어떠했는지는 여기까지 읽어 온 이들이 알아보았을 것이다.\n\n이튿날 늦은 오후에 목사관 문 앞으로 마차를 몰고 온 사람이 다른 누구도 아니고 바체스터의 은행 지배인 포레스트 씨였다 — 사워비가 늘 「데우스 엑스 마키나」로 가리켜 온 그 포레스트 씨, 마땅한 절차로 불러 대기만 하면 그들 모두를 지금의 곤경에서 건져 내고 토저 집안 전부를 물려 보낼 수 있는 사람이었다. 그 토저 족속을 두고서라면 누구나 그러고 싶었을 대로 울부짖으며 광야로 내쫓는 것이 아니라, 먹이로 배를 그토록 잔뜩 불려 그들에게서 더 이상의 괴롭힘을 두려워할 것이 없게 만드는 식으로 말이다. 이 모든 것을 포레스트 씨는 할 수 있었다. 아니, 그보다 더한 것도 아주 기꺼이 하려 했다! 다만 마크 로바츠가 그 은행가의 손에 스스로를 맡기고, 그 은행가가 바라는 서류에 순순히 이름을 적기만 하면 되었다.\n\n“이것은 아주 불쾌한 일입니다.” 마크의 서재에 둘만 들어앉자마자 포레스트 씨가 말했다. 이 말에 대한 답으로 그 목사는 그것이 아주 불쾌한 일임을 시인했다.\n\n“사워비 씨가 손을 써 놓은 덕에 당신은 지금 런던에서 그 장사를 하는 자들 가운데 거의 가장 악질인 악당 패의 손에 들어가 있습니다.”\n\n“그런 줄 짐작했습니다. 컬링도 같은 말을 했습니다.” 컬링은 그가 요즈음 도움을 청한 바체스터의 변호사였다.\n\n“컬링이 그들의 장사를 전부 폭로하겠다고 위협했습니다. 그런데 이 아래까지 내려와 있던 그들 가운데 한 사람, 토저라는 사람이, 폭로로 잃을 것은 자기보다 당신이 훨씬 많다고 되받았어요. 그는 더 나아가서, 영국의 어떤 배심이라도 자기 돈을 주지 않을 수 있으면 어디 한번 해 보라고까지 큰소리쳤습니다. 두 어음을 다 여느 장사 방식대로 할인했다고 그는 맹세했어요. 물론 이것은 거짓이지만, 그것이 거짓임을 밝히는 일은 불가능하지 않을까 걱정입니다. 당신이 성직자라는 것을, 그러니까 다른 사람들보다 당신을 더 세게 붙들 수 있다는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있습니다.”\n\n“그 부끄러움은 사워비에게 떨어질 것입니다.” 그 순간 그리스도인의 용서라는 강한 감정에 움직여진 것이라고는 좀처럼 할 수 없는 태도로 로바츠가 말했다.\n\n“걱정스럽지만, 로바츠 씨, 그는 얼마쯤 토저네와 같은 처지에 있습니다. 그는 당신처럼 그것을 느끼지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“나는 그것을 견뎌야 하겠지요, 포레스트 씨, 되는 대로 견뎌 내야지요.”\n\n“로바츠 씨, 내 의견을 하나 말씀드려도 되겠습니까? 이런 것을 들이대는 것을 사과해야 할지도 모르겠어요. 그러나 그 어음들이 내 창구에서 제시되고 부도가 났으니, 나는 어쩔 수 없이 그 사정을 알게 되었습니다.”\n\n“참으로 대단히 고맙습니다.” 마크가 말했다.\n\n“당신은 이 돈을 갚아야 합니다. 아니면 적어도 그 가운데 가장 큰 몫을요. 실은 전액을 갚아야 하지요. 다만 변호사가 당장 쥘 돈을 눈앞에 내보여 이 매들에게서 얼마쯤 깎아 낼 수 있다면 그만큼은 덜고요. 750파운드나 800파운드쯤이면 이 일 전부에서 당신을 벗어나게 해 줄지도 모릅니다.”\n\n“그러나 나에게는 그 액수의 사분의 일도 두고 있는 것이 없습니다.”\n\n“그렇겠지요, 없으실 겁니다. 그러나 내가 권하고 싶은 것은 이것입니다. 당신 자신의 책임으로 은행에서 그 돈을 빌리는 것이지요 — 자기 이름으로 당신을 도와줄 마음이 있는 어떤 벗의 연대 담보를 붙여서요. 러프턴 경이라면 아마 해 주실 겁니다.”\n\n“아니요, 포레스트 씨 —”\n\n“마음을 정하기 전에 우선 내 말을 들어 보세요. 이 걸음을 밟으신다면 당신은 물론 그 돈을 당신 스스로 갚겠다는 굳은 뜻으로 그러시는 것이겠지요 — 사워비에게든 다른 누구에게든 더는 기대지 않고요.”\n\n“나는 다시는 사워비 씨에게 기대지 않겠습니다. 그것은 믿으셔도 됩니다.”\n\n“내 말은 그 빚을 당신 자신의 빚으로 여기도록 스스로를 가르쳐야 한다는 것입니다. 그렇게 할 수 있다면 당신 수입으로 이자까지 얹어 이 년 안에 틀림없이 갚을 수 있어요. 러프턴 경이 자기 이름으로 도와주신다면, 나는 갚을 돈이 그 기간에 걸쳐 고르게 떨어지도록 어음들을 짜 놓겠습니다. 그러면 세상은 이 일을 아무것도 모를 것이고, 이 년이 지나면 당신은 다시 자유로운 사람이 됩니다. 로바츠 씨, 그보다 훨씬 비싼 값으로 경험을 사들인 사람이 많다는 것은 내가 보증할 수 있어요.”\n\n“포레스트 씨, 그것은 도무지 말이 되지 않습니다.”\n\n“러프턴 경이 자기 이름을 내주지 않을 것이라는 말씀이군요.”\n\n“내가 그에게 청하지 않을 것은 분명합니다. 그러나 그것만이 아닙니다. 우선 내 수입이 당신이 생각하는 그것이 되지 못할 겁니다. 나는 아마 바체스터의 참사회원직을 내놓을 테니까요.”\n\n“참사회원직을 내놓다니요! 연 육백 파운드를 내놓다니요!”\n\n“그리고 그것 말고도, 무엇으로 꾀어도 나는 다른 어음에 내 이름을 적지 않겠다고 말할 수 있을 것 같습니다. 나는 결코 잊지 않기를 바라는 교훈을 하나 배웠습니다.”\n\n“그러면 어떻게 하실 생각입니까?”\n\n“아무것도 하지 않겠습니다!”\n\n“그러면 그 사람들이 이 집의 가구를 하나도 남기지 않고 팔아 버릴 겁니다. 여기 있는 당신 재산이 그들이 청구하는 것 전부를 담보하기에 넉넉하다는 것을 그들은 알고 있어요.”\n\n“그럴 힘이 있다면 팔아야 하겠지요.”\n\n“그리고 온 세상이 그 사실을 알게 될 겁니다.”\n\n“그렇게 되어야 하겠지요. 사람이 저지른 잘못은 그 벌을 제가 져야 합니다. 나 하나뿐이라면 좋을 것을!”\n\n“바로 그것이 문제입니다, 로바츠 씨. 그런 참담함을 겪는 동안 당신 아내가 무엇을 견뎌야 할지 생각해 보세요! 내 의견을 따르시는 게 좋습니다. 러프턴 경이라면 틀림없이 —”\n\n그러나 러프턴 경이라는, 제 누이의 연인이라는 바로 그 이름이 다시 그에게 용기를 주었다. 그는 또 호텔 커피실로 자기를 찾아왔던 그날 밤에 러프턴 경이 자기에게 들이댔던 비난들을 생각했고, 그런 도움을 그에게 청한다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이라고 느꼈다. 차라리 레이디 러프턴에게 모든 것을 말하는 편이 나을 것이었다! 그녀 자신이 치를 값이 무엇이 되든 그녀가 자기를 건져 주리라는 것은 그가 아주 확실히 알고 있었다. 다만 이것뿐이었다. 도움을 얻으려고 그녀에게 눈을 돌린다면 그는 참으로 흙을 씹지 않을 수 없다는 것.\n\n“고맙습니다, 포레스트 씨. 그러나 나는 마음을 정했습니다. 사심 없는 친절을 베풀어 주신 것에 내가 덜 고마워한다고는 생각하지 마세요 — 그것이 사심 없는 것임을 나는 알고 있으니까요. 그러나 이것만은 자신 있게 말할 수 있을 것 같습니다. 그토록 무서운 재앙을 막기 위해서라 해도 나는 다시 어떤 어음에도 내 이름을 적지 않겠습니다. 두 번째 이름을 덧붙이지 않고 갚겠다는 내 약속만으로 받아 주실 수 있다 해도 나는 그러지 않겠습니다.”\n\n이런 형편에서 포레스트 씨가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 그저 마차를 몰아 바체스터로 돌아가는 것밖에 없었다. 그는 제 아는 바에 따라 그 젊은 성직자를 위해 할 수 있는 최선을 했고, 어쩌면 세상의 눈으로 보면 그의 의견이 나쁘지 않았다. 그러나 마크는 어음이라는 이름 자체를 두려워했다. 그는 무섭게 그슬린 개와 같았으니, 무엇으로도 그를 다시 불 가까이 가게 할 수는 없었다.\n\n“방금 온 이가 그 은행에서 온 사람 아니었어요?” 바퀴 소리가 멀어져 사라졌을 때 방으로 들어오며 패니가 말했다.\n\n“그래, 포레스트 씨야.”\n\n“그래서요, 여보?”\n\n“우리는 최악을 맞을 준비를 해야 해.”\n\n“당신은 이제 어떤 서류에도 이름을 적지 않을 거지요, 그렇죠, 마크?”\n\n“그래, 방금 딱 잘라 그러지 않겠다고 했어.”\n\n“그러면 나는 무엇이든 견딜 수 있어요. 그렇지만 여보, 사랑하는 마크, 레이디 러프턴에게 내가 말하게 해 주지 않겠어요?”\n\n그 일을 어느 쪽에서 들여다보아도 그 벌은 몹시 무거웠다.\n\n# 제43장. 그 아이는 대수롭지 않은 아이가 아니냐?\n\n그리고 프램리에서 한 달이 지나갔으나, 그곳의 우리 벗들에게 편안함이 조금이라도 더해진 것은 없었고, 목사관에서 날마다 기다려 온 그 파멸이 실제로 터져 나온 일도 없었다. 토저 쪽 이해를 위하여 움직이는 여러 인물에게서 로바츠 씨에게 잡다한 편지가 닿았고, 그는 그것을 모두 바체스터의 컬링 씨에게 넘겼다. 그 편지 가운데 몇 통은 돈을 달라는 애원을 담고 있었으니, 죄 없는 어느 미망인이 로바츠 씨의 이름을 믿고 제 가진 것을 다 넣도록 꾀임을 받았는데 로바츠 씨가 자기가 한 약조를 지키지 않는 탓에 이제 세 아이와 함께 다락방에서 굶고 있다고 늘어놓았다. 그러나 그 대다수는 위협으로 가득했다. 이틀만 더 참아 주겠고 그러고 나면 집행리들에게 저희 일을 하라고 이를 것이라고, 그다음에는 하루의 유예를 더 얹어 주겠고 그것이 다하는 날에는 전쟁의 개들을 풀어 놓을 것이라고 했다. 이런 것들은 오는 대로 곧 컬링 씨에게 보내졌고, 그는 그것을 하나하나 상대하지는 않으면서도 그 흉한 날을 막으려는 애를 이어 갔다. 둘째 어음은 로바츠 씨가 떠맡기로 하겠다는 것이 컬링 씨의 제안이었다. 그리고 250파운드씩 두 번에 나누어, 첫 번은 두 달 뒤에 두 번째는 넉 달 뒤에 지불하겠다는 것이었다. 이것이 토저 쪽 이해에 받아들여진다면 좋고, 받아들여지지 않는다면 집행리들이 할 수 있는 가장 험한 짓을 해야 할 것이며 토저 쪽 이해는 건질 수 있는 것이나 찾아보아야 할 것이었다. 토저 쪽 이해는 이 조건에 만족한다고 밝히려 하지 않았고, 그리하여 일은 그대로 이어져 갔다. 그동안 로바츠 부인의 두 뺨에서는 장미가 날마다 시들어 갔으니, 그런 사정 아래에서라면 쉽게 그려 볼 수 있는 일이다.\n\n그러는 동안 루시는 여전히 호글스톡에 남아 그곳에서 그 집의 절대적인 안주인이 되어 있었다. 가엾은 크롤리 부인은 죽음의 문턱에 있었다. 며칠은 헛소리를 했고 그 뒤로도 거의 정신을 놓은 것처럼 기운이 없었다. 그러나 이제 가장 험한 때는 지나갔고, 사람의 판단으로 말할 수 있는 한 그의 아이들이 어미 없는 아이가 되지도 않을 것이고 그가 홀아비가 되지도 않을 것이라는 말이 크롤리 씨에게 전해졌다. 이 몇 주 동안 루시는 한 번도 집에 가지 않았고 프램리 사람들을 누구도 만나지 않았다. “그렇게 조그만 목적을 위하여 전염을 옮길 위험을 무릅쓸 까닭이 무엇인가?” 루시 자신이 그렇게 내세우며 편지로만 소식을 주고받았고, 그 편지는 목사관에서 뜯기 전에 절차대로 훈증을 받았다. 그리하여 루시는 호글스톡에 남아 있었고, 이제 아기방의 온갖 대접을 다 받게 된 크롤리네 아이들은 프램리에 머물러 있었다. 그 아이들이 누워 자는 침대가 사워비 씨의 빚을 갚기 위하여 압류될 것이라고 날마다 기다려지는 형편이었음에도 아이들은 프램리에 머물러 있었다.\n\n루시는, 내가 말한 대로, 호글스톡 그 집의 안주인이 되었고 크롤리 씨 위에 완전히 올라섰다. 젤리와 고기 국물과 과일과 심지어 버터까지 러프턴 코트에서 왔고, 루시는 그것을 식탁 위에, 그것도 그의 앞 식탁보 위에 여봐란듯이 펼쳐 놓았는데도 그는 그것을 견뎌 냈다. 그가 이 맛난 것들을 스스로 거침없이 집어 먹었다고는 말할 수 없다. 그러나 차는 내어 주는 대로 마셨으니, 그 안에는 프램리의 크림이 들어 있었고 — 그가 알았더라면 — 차 자체도 프램리의 차 상자에서 나온 보히차였다. 사실 이 무렵 그는 이 낯선 사람의 지배에 제 몸을 내주고 있었다. 그리고 루시가 그의 셔츠에 단추를 달고 있는 것을 — 단추를 달면서 어쩌면 이따금 바늘을 다른 데에도 대고 있는 것을, 그것도 쓸모 없지 않게 그러고 있는 것을 — 마주쳤을 때에도 두 손을 들어 올리며 “원, 원” 하는 말밖에 하지 않았다.\n\n이 무렵 그가 루시에게 고맙다는 말을 하는 일은 아주 드물었다. 긴 저녁 동안 이따금 길게 이어지는 이야기를 나눈 적은 있었다. 그러나 그런 자리에서도 지금 두 사람이 놓인 살림의 형편에 대해서는 많은 말을 하지 않았다. 그가 말한 것은 주로 종교였으니, 루시를 따로 붙들고 훈계한 것이 아니라 그리스도인의 삶이란 어떠해야 하는지, 특히 성직자의 삶이란 어떠해야 하는지에 대한 제 생각을 늘어놓았다. “그러나 이것을 볼 수 있으면서도, 로바츠 양,” 그가 말했다. “저만큼 자주 떨어져 나간 사람이 없다는 것을 저는 말하지 않을 수 없습니다. 저는 악마와 그 온갖 소행을 끊었습니다. 그러나 그것은 입으로만 한 것입니다 — 입으로만 한 것입니다. 사람이 티끌 속에 엎드려 제 힘이라는 것이 다 물보다 약하다고 시인하지 않고서 어떻게 제 안에 있는 옛 아담을 십자가에 못 박을 수 있겠습니까?” 이 말이 얼마나 되풀이되든 루시는 참을성 있게 들었고, 제 신학이 내어 줄 수 있는 말들로 그를 위로했다. 그러나 그것이 끝나면 다시 제 지휘를 되찾아 집안일에 관한 제 분부에 그가 빈틈없이 따르도록 몰아붙였다.\n\n한 달이 다 갈 무렵 러프턴 경이 프램리 코트로 돌아왔다. 그가 온 것은 아주 뜻밖의 일이었다. 다만 어머니가 놀라움을 내보였을 때 그가 짚어 말한 대로, 그는 떠나기 전에 자기가 대어 둔 바로 그때에 돌아온 것이었다.\n\n“루도빅, 네가 와 주어서 얼마나 기쁜지는 말할 것도 없구나,” 마님이 아들의 얼굴을 들여다보며 그 팔을 지그시 누르고 말했다. “더구나 이렇게 될 줄은 좀처럼 생각하지 못했으니 더욱 그렇지.”\n\n첫날 저녁에 그는 어머니에게 루시에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 로바츠 집안에 관한 이야기는 얼마간 있었지만 그랬다.\n\n“로바츠 씨가 곤경에 빠진 것 같아 걱정이구나,” 레이디 러프턴이 아주 심각한 얼굴로 말했다. “나에게까지 들려오는 소문이 여간 괴로운 것이 아니야. 아직 아무에게도 아무 말 하지 않았다 — 패니에게도 하지 않았어. 그렇지만 그 얼굴을 보면, 그 목소리의 결을 들으면, 그이가 무슨 큰 슬픔을 겪고 있다는 것을 알 수 있구나.”\n\n“저는 다 알고 있어요,” 러프턴 경이 말했다.\n\n“네가 다 알고 있다고, 루도빅?”\n\n“예. 콜디코츠의 사워비 씨, 그 귀하신 제 벗 때문이지요. 그가 사워비의 어음을 인수해 주었어요. 실은 저에게 그렇게 말했습니다.”\n\n“그가 콜디코츠에 무슨 볼일이 있었단 말이냐? 그런 벗들과 무슨 상관이 있었단 말이냐? 내가 그를 어떻게 용서해야 할지 모르겠구나.”\n\n“그가 사워비를 알게 된 것은 저를 통해서였어요. 어머니, 그것을 잊지 마셔야 해요.”\n\n“그것이 무슨 변명이 되는지 나는 모르겠다. 네가 아는 사람이면 다 제 벗도 되어야 한다고 그가 여겨야 하느냐? 네 처지에서라면 교구 성직자인 그에게는 도무지 걸맞지 않은 동무들과도 이따금 어울려 지내야 하는 것이 마땅한 일이라고 헤아려 볼 만하지 않으냐. 그가 이것을 기억하려 하지 않으니 그것을 가르쳐 주어야 한다. 그가 개서럼 성에 갈 무슨 볼일이 있었단 말이냐?”\n\n“그가 바체스터의 참사회원 자리를 얻은 것이 거기 갔기 때문이지요.”\n\n“그 자리가 없는 편이 그에게 훨씬 나았을 것이고, 패니는 그것을 알아볼 만한 분별이 있다. 집이 둘씩이나 있어서 무엇을 하겠느냐? 참사회원 자리란 그보다 나이 든 사람들의 것이야 — 그것을 얻을 만한 일을 해 온 사람들, 그리고 삶의 끝머리에 이르러 좀 편안하기를 바라는 사람들의 것이지. 그가 그것을 아예 받지 않았더라면 좋았겠다고 나는 온 마음으로 바란다.”\n\n“그래도 연 육백 파운드에는 그 나름의 매력이 있지요,” 러프턴 경이 일어나 방에서 어슬렁 나가며 말했다.\n\n“마크가 정말로 어떤 어려움에 빠져 있다면,” 그날 저녁 늦게 그가 말했다. “우리가 그를 다시 제 발로 서게 해 주어야 해요.”\n\n“그 빚을 갚아 준다는 말이냐?”\n\n“예. 사워비의 어음을 인수해 준 것 말고는 그에게 빚이 없어요.”\n\n“얼마나 되겠느냐, 루도빅?”\n\n“천 파운드쯤, 조금 더 되거나 덜 되거나 할 거예요. 돈은 제가 마련하겠어요, 어머니. 다만 어머니께 갚는 일이 제가 뜻했던 만큼 이르지는 못하겠지요.” 그러자 어머니가 일어나 두 팔로 아들의 목을 껴안고, 자기가 아들에게 준 그 조그만 선물에 대해 앞으로 한마디라도 더 입에 올린다면 결코 용서하지 않겠다고 밝혔다. 어머니가 누릴 수 있는 즐거움 가운데 외아들에게 제 돈을 내주는 것보다 더 마음을 끄는 것은 없으리라고 나는 생각한다.\n\n루시의 이름이 처음 입에 오른 것은 이튿날 아침 식사 자리였다. 러프턴 경은 아침 일찍 — 목사관으로 올라가기 전에 — 이 일로 어머니를 몰아붙이기로 마음을 굳혀 두었다. 그러나 일이 되어 가는 형편이, 루시를 두고 러프턴 경이 따로 품은 소망과는 아무 상관 없이 로바츠 양이 한 일을 저절로 이야깃거리로 끌어올렸다. 크롤리 부인이 앓는다는 사실이 이야기에 올랐고, 크롤리네 아이들이 모두 목사관에 있게 된 사정을 레이디 러프턴이 밝혔던 것이다.\n\n“패니가 참 훌륭하게 처신했다고 말해야겠구나,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그이에게서 기대할 만한 그대로였지. 그리고 실은,” 마님이 거북한 어조로 덧붙였다. “로바츠 양도 그랬다. 로바츠 양은 호글스톡에 남아서 그 내내 크롤리 부인을 간호했어.”\n\n“호글스톡에 남았다니 — 그 열병 속에서요!” 나리가 소리쳤다.\n\n“그래, 정말 그랬다,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“그런데 지금도 거기 있나요?”\n\n“아, 그래. 아직 떠날 생각을 하고 있다는 말은 나도 못 들었다.”\n\n“그렇다면 저는 그것이 부끄러운 일이라고 말하겠어요 — 망신스럽도록 부끄러운 일이라고요!”\n\n“그렇지만 루도빅, 그 아이가 스스로 한 일이야.”\n\n“아, 예. 알겠어요. 그렇지만 왜 그 사람이 제 몸을 바쳐야 합니까? 이 고을에 삯을 주고 부를 간호인이 하나도 없어서, 그 사람이 가서 한 달을 옮는 열병의 병상 옆에 붙어 있어야 했다는 말인가요? 거기에는 의로움이 없어요.”\n\n“의로움이라니, 루도빅? 의로움이야 나는 잘 모르겠다만, 거기에는 큰 그리스도인의 사랑이 있었다. 크롤리 부인은 아마 제 목숨을 로바츠 양에게 빚졌을 것이다.”\n\n“그 사람이 앓았습니까? 지금 앓고 있습니까? 앓고 있는지 아닌지 저는 꼭 알아야 하겠어요. 아침을 먹고 나면 곧 제가 몸소 호글스톡으로 건너가겠습니다.”\n\n이 말에 레이디 러프턴은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 러프턴 경이 호글스톡에 가기로 정한다면 마님이 그것을 막을 수는 없었다. 그러나 아들이 가지 않는 편이 훨씬 나으리라고 마님은 생각했다. 아들도 루시 로바츠와 똑같이 전염에 그대로 열려 있을 것이었다. 게다가 크롤리 부인의 병상 옆이란 두 연인이 만나 이야기할 자리로 고를 수 있는 곳 가운데 가장 거북한 곳일 터였다. 로바츠 양에 관해서라면 지금 이 순간 사정이 자기를 매정하게 다루고 있다고 레이디 러프턴은 느꼈다. 물론 의롭지 못한 짓이 되지 않고 그렇게 할 수 있다면, 그 젊은 아가씨의 아름다움과 값에 대해 아들이 품고 있는 저 높은 생각을 깎아내리는 것이 마님의 소임이었을 것이다. 그런데 불행히도 마님은 그 아가씨를 칭찬하고 그 이름에 온갖 종류의 찬사를 얹지 않을 수 없었다. 레이디 러프턴은 그 바탕이 참된 여인이었으므로, 이토록 중대한 일에서 제 뜻을 이루려는 목적으로도 그저 입을 다물고만 있어서 저지를 수 있는 그런 속임수의 죄를 짓지는 않으려 했다. 그러나 그러면서도 루시를 칭송해야 하는 그 어쩔 수 없음과는 좀처럼 화해할 수가 없었다.\n\n아침을 먹고 나서 레이디 러프턴은 의자에서 일어났으나, 나갈 낌새는 조금도 보이지 않고 방 안을 서성거렸다. 늘 하던 대로라면 오늘은 무엇을 할 작정이냐고 아들에게 물었을 것이다. 그러나 지금은 그렇게 물을 엄두가 나지 않았다. 어디로 갈 것인지를 아들이 겨우 몇 분 전에 밝히지 않았던가? “점심때에는 너를 보게 되겠지?” 마침내 마님이 말했다.\n\n“점심때요? 글쎄요, 모르겠어요. 여기 보세요, 어머니. 제가 로바츠 양을 만나면 그 사람에게 무슨 말을 해야 합니까?” 그러면서 그는 어머니를 다잡아 물으며 등을 난로 선반에 기댔다.\n\n“그 아이에게 무슨 말을 해야 하느냐고, 루도빅?”\n\n“예. 제가 무슨 말을 해야 합니까 — 어머니께서 하시는 말로요? 어머니가 그 사람을 며느리로 맞으시겠다고 제가 말해야 합니까?”\n\n“루도빅, 나는 그것을 다 로바츠 양 본인에게 설명해 두었다.”\n\n“무엇을 설명하셨다는 말입니까?”\n\n“그런 혼인이 너를 행복하게 하지도, 그 아이를 행복하게 하지도 않으리라고 내가 생각한다고 말해 주었다.”\n\n“그런데 왜 그런 말씀을 하셨습니까? 왜 제가 어린아이라도 되는 것처럼 이런 일에서 저를 대신해 판단하는 일을 스스로 떠맡으셨습니까? 어머니, 하신 말씀을 거두어 주셔야 합니다.”\n\n러프턴 경은 이렇게 말하면서 어머니의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다. 그것도 어머니에게 은혜를 몸 낮추어 구하는 사람의 눈길이 아니라 명을 내리는 사람의 눈길이었다. 마님은 한 손을 아침 식탁에 얹은 채 아들 가까이 서서, 그 눈을 정면으로 마주할 엄두는 채 내지 못하고 거의 몰래 훔쳐보듯 아들을 바라보았다. 이 세상에 레이디 러프턴이 두려워하는 것은 한 가지뿐이었으니, 그것은 아들의 언짢은 낯빛이었다. 마님의 이 땅에 있는 하늘의 해는 아들이라는 존재를 통하여 마님을 비추었다. 아는 부인네들 가운데 몇이 저희 아들과 다투는 데까지 밀려갔듯이 마님도 아들과 다투는 데까지 밀려간다면, 마님에게 세상은 끝나 버릴 것이었다. 그렇다고 사실이 그렇게 하는 것을 절대로 어쩔 수 없게 만들 만큼 세지 않으리라는 뜻은 아니다. 어떤 사람들이 어떤 사정 아래에서라면 스스로 목숨을 끊겠다고 마음을 정해 두는 것과 마찬가지로, 마님도 어떤 사정 아래에서라면 아들에게서 떨어지는 것에까지 응해야 한다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 마님은 그릇된 일을 — 스스로 그릇되다고 아는 일을 — 아들을 위해서라도 하지 않을 것이었다. 제 행복이 다 허물어져 제 주위에 무너져 내려 짓눌리는 것이 어쩔 수 없는 일이라면 마님은 그것을 견뎌 내고, 그토록 어두운 세상에서 자기를 건져 주실 하느님의 때를 기다려야 했다. 해의 빛은 마님에게 여간 값진 것이 아니었으나, 그것조차 너무 비싼 값으로는 살 수 없는 것이었다.\n\n“어머니, 저는 전에도 제 선택이 정해졌다고 말씀드렸고 그때 승낙해 주시기를 청했습니다. 이제 어머니께서 그것을 생각해 보실 틈이 있었으니, 그래서 다시 청하러 왔습니다. 어머니가 루시에게 서슴없이 손을 내밀어 주신다면 제 혼인에 걸림돌이 없을 것이라고 알 만한 까닭이 저에게는 있습니다.”\n\n이 일은 온전히 레이디 러프턴의 손에 놓여 있었으나, 힘을 좋아하는 사람이면서도 마님은 참으로 그렇지 않기를 바랐다. 아들이 어머니에게 묻지 않고 혼인해 버린 뒤에 루시를 아내로 삼아 집에 데려왔다면 마님은 틀림없이 아들을 용서했을 것이고, 그 혼사를 아무리 싫어했다 해도 끝내는 그 신부를 품에 안았을 것이다. 그런데 이제는 제 판단을 행사하지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 아들이 지각없이 혼인한다면 그것은 마님이 한 일이 될 것이었다. 스스로 그릇되다고 믿는 일에 어떻게 입으로 밝혀 승낙해 줄 수 있겠는가?\n\n“그 사람에게 흠될 것을 무엇이라도 아십니까? 그 사람이 제 아내가 되어서는 안 될 무슨 까닭이라도요?” 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n“도덕상의 처신을 두고 하는 말이라면 물론 없다,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그렇지만 그런 혼인에 여간 걸맞지 않다고 내가 여길 젊은 아가씨들도 아주 많은데, 나로서는 그이들을 두고도 그만한 말은 해 줄 수 있겠다.”\n\n“예. 어떤 이는 상스러울 수도 있고, 어떤 이는 성미가 나쁠 수도 있고, 어떤 이는 못생겼을 수도 있겠지요. 또 어떤 이는 언짢은 인연을 짐으로 지고 있을 수도 있겠지요. 이런 사정 가운데 어느 하나라도 걸린다면 어머니께서 며느리를 마다하실 만하다는 것은 저도 알 수 있어요. 그렇지만 로바츠 양에게는 이런 것 가운데 어느 하나도 해당되지 않습니다. 그 사람이 어느 모로 보나 숙녀가 마땅히 그러해야 할 그대로가 아니라고 어머니께서 말씀하실 수 있다면 해 보십시오.”\n\n그렇지만 그 아이의 아버지는 의사였고, 그 아이는 교구 성직자의 누이이며, 키는 겨우 다섯 피트 두 치밖에 되지 않고, 살빛이 어쩌면 그렇게 검단 말인가! 레이디 러프턴이 제 반대 이유의 목록을 감히 늘어놓았다면 그 폭과 결이 그러했을 것이다. 그러나 마님은 그것을 감히 하지 못했다.\n\n“루도빅, 네가 아내에게서 구해야 할 것을 그 아이가 다 갖추었다고는 나는 말할 수 없다.” 마님의 대답은 그러했다.\n\n“그 사람에게 돈이 없다는 말씀입니까?”\n\n“아니, 그런 것이 아니다. 네가 돈을 으뜸 목적으로, 아니 반드시 있어야 할 목적으로 삼는 것을 보게 된다면 나는 여간 슬프지 않을 것이다. 네 아내에게 어쩌다 돈이 있다면 그것이 편리하다는 것을 너도 물론 알게 되겠지. 그렇지만 루도빅, 제발 나를 알아들어라. 네 행복을 그런 어쩔 수 없음에 매어 두라고 나는 한순간도 권하지 않겠다. 그 아이에게 재산이 없기 때문이 아니야 —”\n\n“그렇다면 무엇 때문입니까? 아침 식탁에서는 그 사람을 칭송하시며 얼마나 훌륭한 사람인지를 말씀하셨는데요.”\n\n“내 반대를 한마디로 담아야 한다면 나는 이렇게 말하겠다 —” 그러고서 마님은 말을 멈추었으니, 아들의 이마에 이미 모여들고 있는 그 찌푸림을 마주할 엄두가 좀처럼 나지 않았다.\n\n“무엇이라고 말씀하시겠다는 겁니까?” 러프턴 경이 거의 거칠게 말했다.\n\n“나에게 노여워하지 마라, 루도빅. 이 일에 대해 내가 생각하는 것도, 내가 말하는 것도 다 오직 한 가지 목적으로 생각하고 말하는 것이야 — 네 행복이라는 목적으로. 이 세상 무슨 일에서든 내가 달리 무슨 동기를 가질 수 있겠느냐?” 그러고서 마님은 아들에게 가까이 다가가 입을 맞추었다.\n\n“그렇지만 말씀해 주세요, 어머니. 그 반대가 무엇입니까? 딱한 루시의 죄목을 다 한자리에 몰아 담고 그 사람이 혼인해 사는 삶에 걸맞지 않다는 것을 증명한다는, 그 무서운 한마디가 무엇입니까?”\n\n“루도빅, 나는 그렇게 말하지 않았다. 내가 그러지 않았다는 것을 너도 알지 않느냐.”\n\n“그 한마디가 무엇입니까, 어머니?”\n\n그리하여 마침내 레이디 러프턴이 그 말을 입 밖에 냈다. “그 아이는 — 대수롭지 않아. 나는 그 아이가 아주 착한 아이라고 믿는다. 그렇지만 네가 그 아이를 올려 앉히려는 그 높은 자리를 채울 자격이 그 아이에게는 없어.”\n\n“대수롭지 않다니요!”\n\n“그래, 루도빅. 나는 그렇게 생각한다.”\n\n“그렇다면 어머니, 어머니는 그 사람을 모르십니다. 어머니가 모르는 처녀를 두고 말씀하고 계신다고 제가 말하는 것을 허락하셔야 합니다. 영어라는 말이 어머니께 내어 줄 수 있는 온갖 욕된 딱지 가운데에서, 그것이야말로 그 사람이 들을 만한 것으로는 거의 맨 마지막에 놓일 것입니다.”\n\n“나는 욕되게 할 뜻이 없었다.”\n\n“대수롭지 않다니요!”\n\n“네가 나를 온전히 알아듣지 못한 것인지도 모르겠구나, 루도빅.”\n\n“대수롭지 않다는 것이 무슨 뜻인지는 저도 압니다, 어머니.”\n\n“네 아내가 세상에서 차지해야 할 그 자리를 그 아이가 값있게 채우지는 못하리라고 나는 생각한다.”\n\n“무슨 말씀인지 알겠습니다.”\n\n“네 식탁의 상석에 앉아 네 낯을 세워 주지는 못할 것이다.”\n\n“아, 알겠습니다. 어머니는 제가 껑충하게 큰 아마존 같은 여자, 분홍과 흰빛으로 차린 유행의 여자 거인, 작은 사람들을 겁주어 예절을 지키게 만들 그런 여자와 혼인하기를 바라시는군요.”\n\n“아, 루도빅! 이제 나를 놀리려고 하는구나.”\n\n“제 평생 웃고 싶은 마음이 이보다 덜했던 적은 없습니다 — 결코 없어요, 정말입니다. 그리고 이제 저는 로바츠 양에 대한 어머니의 반대가 그 사람을 모르시는 데에서 나온 것이라고 어느 때보다 더 확신합니다. 어머니가 그 사람을 알게 되시면, 어머니가 아시는 어느 부인에게도 못지않게 그 사람이 제 자리를 지켜 낼 수 있다는 것을 — 그렇습니다, 제 남편의 자리까지 지켜 낼 수 있다는 것을 알게 되실 겁니다. 그 점에서라면 저는 그 사람을 두고 아무 두려움도 없을 것이라고 어머니께 말씀드릴 수 있습니다.”\n\n“얘야, 어쩌면 네가 좀처럼 —”\n\n“어머니, 저는 이렇게 생각합니다. 이런 일에서는 제가 저를 위하여 골라야 한다고요. 저는 골랐습니다. 그리고 이제 제 어머니로서 그 사람에게 가시어 어서 오라고 맞아 주시기를 청합니다. 어머니, 이것만은 털어놓겠습니다. 어머니가 제 아내를 사랑하지 않으신다는 생각이 들면 저는 행복하지 못할 것입니다.” 이 마지막 말은 어머니의 가슴에 사무치는 애정의 어조로 하고서 그는 방을 나섰다.\n\n가엾은 레이디 러프턴은 혼자 남게 되자 아들의 발걸음이 현관을 지나 멀어지는 소리가 들릴 때까지 기다렸고, 그러고 나서 늘 하는 아침 일거리를 보러 위층으로 올라갔다. 마님은 그 일에 몸을 담글 듯이 마침내 자리에 앉았다. 그러나 마음이 너무 가득 차 있어 펜을 들 수가 없었다. 마님은 여러 번, 자기 셈으로는 아직 오래 지나지 않은 날들에, 아들의 신부를 자기가 고를 것이고 그러고 나면 고른 그 사람을 온 마음으로 사랑할 것이라고 스스로에게 말한 적이 있었다. 그 새 여왕을 위하여 자기는 왕좌에서 물러날 것이었고, 아들의 아내가 더 큰 영화로 빛나도록 기꺼이 미망인 마님의 자리로 내려앉을 것이었다. 마님의 삶에서 가장 애틋한 백일몽은 다 아들이 새 레이디 러프턴을 — 영국의 뛰어난 여인들 가운데에서 마님 자신이 골라낸 사람을 — 집으로 데려올 그때에 걸려 있었고, 그 새 우상을 맨 처음 받들어 모시는 사람이 마님 자신이 되는 것에 걸려 있었다. 그러나 루시 로바츠를 위하여 왕좌에서 물러날 수 있겠는가? 목사관에서 온 그 조그만 처녀를 그 위에 앉히려고 제 옥좌를 내줄 수 있겠는가? 몇 달 전에 마님의 응접실 한구석에 누구에게 말을 걸기도 겁내며 어색하게 앉아 있던 그 조그만 계집애를, 우상을 받들다시피 하는 어머니의 그 믿음으로, 온전히 사랑스러운 믿음을 다 주어 품에 안을 수 있겠는가? 그러면서도 일이 여기까지 — 여기까지 — 와야 하는 듯싶었고, 그러지 않으면 마님의 그 백일몽은 아무래도 이루어지지 않을 것이었다.\n\n마님은 자리에 앉아, 루시가 그 왕좌를 채우는 것이 될 수 있는 일인지 생각해 보려고 애썼다. 아들의 뜻이 저로서는 너무 세다는 것이 있음직한 일임을 알아보기 시작했기 때문이다. 그러나 마님의 생각은 자꾸 그리젤다 그랜틀리에게로 날아갔다. 제 백일몽을 이루려고 처음으로, 또 지금까지 유일하게 무르익도록 해 본 그 시도에서 마님은 그리젤다를 제 여왕으로 골랐다. 마님은 거기서 실패했으니, 운명이 그랜틀리 양을 다른 왕좌로 정해 놓은 것을 보게 되었기 때문이다 — 세상의 셈으로는 다르고도 더 높은 왕좌로. 마님이라면 그리젤다를 남작의 아내로 만들었겠지만, 운명은 그 젊은 아가씨를 후작의 아내로 만들려 하고 있었다. 여기에 슬퍼할 까닭이 있었을까? 온갖 미덕을 갖춘 그랜틀리 양이 하틀탑 집안으로 넘겨지는 것을 마님은 정말로 애석하게 여겼을까? 레이디 러프턴은 낙심을 가볍게 견디는 여인이 아니었다. 그러나 그러면서도 러프턴-그랜틀리 혼인 조약이 끝나 버린 것을 생각할 때면 마님은 짐 하나를 벗은 것 같은 느낌을 거의 받았다. 만일 성공했는데 그 상이 결국 마님이 기대했던 것과 다른 것이었다면 어떻게 되었겠는가? 그 상이 한때 바랐던 것 그대로는 아니라고 마님은 이따금 생각하는 쪽으로 기울었다. 그리젤다는 레이디 러프턴이 여왕감으로 바라는 바로 그것처럼 보였다. 그러나 제 겉모습에만 기대는 여왕이 어떻게 군림하겠는가? 그 점에서라면 운명이 끼어든 것이 마님에게 잘된 일이었는지도 몰랐다. 그리젤다는 제 아들보다 덤벨로 경에게 더 잘 맞았다고 마님은 시인하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그렇더라도 — 루시 같은 여왕이라니! 그토록 조그만 임금 앞에서 이 왕국의 백성들이 마땅한 공경으로 무릎을 굽히는 일이 언제라도 있을 수 있겠는가? 게다가 훨씬 더 높은 자리에서 왕자들이 제 백성 가운데 가장 지체 높은 이와 혼인하는 것을 막는 그 느낌도 있었다. 왕가의 피를 이은 이는 태생으로 왕가가 아닌 신민을 왕가의 영예로 끌어올리지 못한다는 것이 이 나라들에서 인정된 규범이 아닌가! 루시는 목사의 누이이자 목사관에 사는 거주자라는 점에서 러프턴 집안의 신민이었다. 루시 자신이 여왕으로 쓸 만하다고 가정하더라도 — 왕관이 그 이마에 절차대로 놓인 뒤에 다스릴 재간이 얼마간 있을 수 있다고 인정하더라도 — 그러면 왕좌 가까이에 있는 그 성직자 오라비는 어떻게 하는가? 결국 프램리에 여왕이라는 것이 더 이상 없어지는 데에 이르지 않겠는가?\n\n그러면서도 마님은 자기가 물러서야 한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 스스로에게 그렇게 말하지는 않았다. 루시에게 손을 내밀며 딸이라고 이름 불러야 한다고 아직은 시인하지 않았다. 제 마음에게조차 그만큼 딱 잘라 말하지는 않았다 — 아직은 아니었다. 그러나 마님은 루시의 높은 자질을 헤아려 보기 시작했고, 그 처녀가 여왕이 되기에 걸맞지 않다 하더라도 아무튼 여인이 되기에는 걸맞다고 스스로에게 밝히기 시작했다. 그 몸이 아무리 대수롭지 않다 하더라도 그 몸 안에 기개가 있다는 것은 레이디 러프턴도 시인할 준비가 되어 있었다. 남을 위하여 제 몸을 바치는 힘 — 이 세상 모든 힘 가운데 으뜸인 그 힘 — 을 그 처녀가 얻었다는 것도 넉넉히 뚜렷했다. 착하다는 말의 흔한 뜻으로 그 처녀가 착한 아이라는 것은 레이디 러프턴도 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었다. 또 재치가 재빨랐고 움직임이 날랬으며 어떤 불기운을 타고났다. 불행하게도 러프턴 경의 사랑을 그 처녀에게 얻어다 준 것이 바로 그 불기운의 타고남이었다. 마님 자신도 루시 로바츠를 사랑하는 것이 넉넉히 될 수 있는 일이었다. 레이디 러프턴은 그것을 스스로에게 시인했다. 그러나 그러면 누가 그 앞에 무릎을 굽히고 여왕으로 받들 것인가? 그 아이가 그토록 대수롭지 않은 것이 딱한 일이 아닌가?\n\n그러나 그럼에도 그날 아침 레이디 러프턴이 두 시간을 아무 일도 하지 않고 제 방에 앉아 있는 동안 루시 로바츠의 별이 차츰 하늘에 떠오르고 있었다고 우리는 말할 수 있다. 무엇보다도 레이디 러프턴을 기르는 데 주로 필요한 양식은 사랑이었다 — 절대로 없어서는 안 될 유일한 양식이었다. 마님 자신은 이것을 알지 못했고, 마님을 가장 잘 아는 이들도 아마 마님을 두고 그렇게 말하지는 않았을 것이다. 그이들은 집안의 자랑이 마님의 나날의 먹이라고 밝혔을 것이고, 마님 자신도 그렇게 말했을 것이다. 다만 그것을 좀 덜 거슬리는 이름으로 불렀을 것이다. 아들의 명예, 그리고 제 집안의 명예! 이 세상에서 제게 가장 값진 것으로 마님은 그것을 들어 말했을 것이다. 그리고 이것은 얼마쯤 참이었으니, 아들이 명예를 잃었다면 마님은 슬픔에 잠겨 무덤으로 내려앉았을 것이다. 그러나 마님의 나날의 삶에 반드시 있어야 할 한 가지는 제 가까이에 있는 이들을 사랑하는 힘이었다.\n\n러프턴 경은 식당을 나서면서 곧바로 목사관으로 올라가려 했으나, 거기서 무슨 말을 할지 마음을 정하려고 우선 정원을 한 바퀴 어슬렁거렸다. 그는 어머니에게 노여워하고 있었으니, 어머니가 물러서서 저에게 굽히려 하고 있다는 것을 알아볼 눈치가 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 이 일에서는 제 뜻대로 하겠다는 것을 알아듣게 만들기로 마음을 굳혔다. 루시의 마음에 관하여 알아야 할 것을 그는 알아 두었고, 그러니 어머니의 반대에 막힐 수 있다는 것을 그는 생각조차 하려 하지 않았다. ‘영국에 나만큼 제 어머니를 더 사랑하는 아들은 없다,’ 그가 속으로 말했다. ‘그렇지만 사람이 참아 낼 수 없는 것들이 있다. 내가 그러라고 두었더라면 어머니는 나를 저 돌덩이와 혼인시켰을 것이다. 그런데 이제 그쪽에서 낙심하고 나니 — 대수롭지 않다니! 그토록 어처구니없고, 그토록 사실과 다르고, 그토록 사랑이 없고, 그토록 — 그런 말은 내 평생 들어 본 적이 없다. 어머니는 내가 용을 집에 데려오기를 바라시는 모양이다. 내가 정말 그렇게 한다면 그것이 어머니에게 마땅한 갚음일 것이다 — 어머니에게 이 집을 견딜 수 없는 곳으로 만들어 놓을 무슨 짐승 같은 것을 데려온다면 말이지.’ ‘그래도 어머니는 그것을 하셔야 한다,’ 그가 또 말했다. ‘그러지 않으면 어머니와 나는 다투게 될 것이다.’ 그러고서 그는 목사관으로 갈 채비를 하며 문을 향해 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n“나리, 무슨 일이 있었는지 들으셨습니까요?” 정원사가 문간으로 그에게 다가오며 말했다. 그 사람은 숨이 차 있었고 제가 가져온 소식의 엄청남에 거의 짓눌려 있었다.\n\n“아니, 아무것도 못 들었네. 무슨 일인가?”\n\n“집행관들이 목사관의 모든 것을 차지해 버렸습니다요.”\n\n# 제44장. 목사관에 들이친 블레셋 사람들\n\n토저네와 컬링 씨와 마크 로바츠 사이에 그 한 달 동안 일이 어떻게 되어 갔는지는 이미 이야기했다. 포레스트 씨는 그 일에서 아주 떠내려가 버렸고, 사워비 씨 또한 적어도 그 일에 손을 걷고 나서는 한에서는 마찬가지였다. 컬링 씨에게서 목사관으로 편지가 자주 왔고, 마침내는 사람을 따로 보내어 흉한 날이 코앞에 왔다는 전갈이 이르렀다. 톰 토저 같은 사내가 어떻게 나올지를 컬링 씨의 직업상 경험으로 미리 짚고 미리 말할 수 있는 한에서, 그는 이튿날 아침에 집행리들이 프램리 목사관에 들이닥칠 것이라고 보았다. 이 점에서 컬링 씨의 경험은 그를 속이지 않았다.\n\n“그러면 당신은 어떻게 하시겠어요, 마크?” 남편이 건네준 편지를 다 읽고 나서, 패니가 눈물 사이로 말했다.\n\n“아무것도 안 해. 내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어? 저들은 올 수밖에 없어.”\n\n“러프턴 경이 오늘 오셨어요. 그분에게 가 보지 않으시겠어요?”\n\n“아니. 그렇게 하면 돈을 달라고 청하는 것과 같아.”\n\n“그분에게서 빌리면 왜 안 되나요, 여보? 그분이 빌려주기에 그렇게 큰 액수도 아닐 텐데요.”\n\n“나는 그럴 수 없어. 루시를 생각해 봐. 루시가 그와 어떤 처지에 놓여 있는지를. 게다가 사워비와 그 돈 문제를 두고 러프턴과는 이미 말이 오간 일이 있어. 그는 내 탓이라고 여기고 있고, 나에게 그렇게 말할 거야. 그러면 우리 사이에 날 선 말이 오가게 되지. 내가 다그치면 그는 돈을 대 주기는 할 거야. 그러나 내가 그것을 받을 수 없게 만드는 식으로 대 주겠지.”\n\n그러고는 더 할 말이 없었다. 제 뜻대로 할 수 있었다면 로바츠 부인은 당장 레이디 러프턴에게 갔을 것이나, 그렇게 하도록 남편을 움직일 수는 없었다. 마님에게 도움을 청하는 데 대한 반대는 그 아들에게 청하는 데 대한 반대만큼이나 굳셌다. 이미 언짢은 감정이 조금 싹터 있었고, 그런 형편에서 돈의 도움을 청하기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 그러나 패니에게는, 이 곤경에서 벗어나게 해 줄 도움이 끝내는 그쪽에서 오거나 아니면 아예 오지 않을 것이라는 예언 같은 확신이 있었다. 그리고 허락만 받았다면 그녀는 기꺼이 큰 집에 모든 것을 알렸을 것이다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그들은 여느 때와 같은 시각에 아침을 먹었으나 크게 침울했다. 로바츠 부인이 혼인할 때 데려온 하녀가, 무슨 일이 벌어지려 하는지에 대한 소문이 부엌에까지 이르렀다고 알렸다. 마부 스텁스가 전날 바체스터에 가 있었는데, 그의 말로는—메리가 그렇게 전했다—온 도시가 그 이야기를 하고 있다는 것이었다. “마음 쓰지 말아라, 메리.” 로바츠 부인이 말했고, 메리는 “아이고, 예, 그럼요, 마님.” 하고 대답했다.\n\n그즈음 로바츠 부인은 여느 때라면 몹시 바빴다. 집에 아이가 여섯이나 있었고, 그 가운데 넷은 아기에게 필요한 것들을 변변히 갖추지 못한 채로 그녀에게 온 아이들이었으니 그럴 수밖에 없었다. 그래서 지금도 여느 때처럼 아침을 먹고 나자 곧 일을 하러 나섰다. 그러나 그녀는 집 안을 아주 느리게 돌아다녔고, 하인들에게 지시를 내리는 것조차 거의 하지 못했으며, 무슨 일인가 하고 제 곁에 매달리는 아이들에게도 슬픈 목소리로 말했다. 남편은 그와 같은 시각에 서재로 들어갔으나, 들어가서는 아무 일에도 손을 대려 하지 않았다. 그는 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 난로에 기대어 서서, 눈길을 앞의 탁자에 붙박아 두었으나 그 위에 놓인 무엇도 보고 있지 않았다. 제 일에 몸을 붙인다는 것은 그에게 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 성직자가 서재에서 하는 일이 보통 어떤 것인지를 떠올려 보시라. 그리고 그가 그런 일에 얼마나 알맞은 상태였을지 생각해 보시라! 그런 순간에 지어진 설교가 어떤 것이 되었겠으며, 그가 논거를 찾느라 그 거룩한 책을 뒤적이면서 어떤 흡족함을 느낄 수 있었겠는가? 이 점에서 그는 아내보다 형편이 나빴다. 그녀는 그래도 몸을 놀렸으나, 그는 꼼짝도 않고 아무것도 하지 않은 채 눈길을 붙박고 서서, 사람들이 자기를 두고 무슨 말을 할까만 생각하고 있었다.\n\n그로서는 다행히도 이 애타는 기다림은 길지 않았다. 아침 식탁에서 물러난 지 반 시간도 되지 않아 하인이 그의 방 문을 두드렸으니 말이다. 곤경이 시작될 무렵 그가 내보내기로 마음을 굳혔던 바로 그 하인이었으나, 바체스터 참사회원 자리 때문에 그대로 두고 있던 사람이었다.\n\n“목사님, 아룁니다만, 바깥에 사내 둘이 와 있습니다요.” 하인이 말했다.\n\n사내 둘! 마크는 그들이 어떤 사내들인지 넉넉히 알고 있었으나, 그런 사내 둘이 제 조용한 시골 목사관에 찾아온 것을 아주 당연한 일로 받아들일 수는 없었다.\n\n“누구인가, 존?” 그가 말했다. 대답을 바라서가 아니라 물음이 저절로 밀려 나왔기 때문이다.\n\n“아무래도—집행관인 것 같습니다요, 나리.”\n\n“되었다, 존. 그만하면 되었다. 물론 저들은 이 집에서 저들 하고 싶은 대로 해야 하겠지.”\n\n그러고서 하인이 물러난 뒤에도 그는 여전히 꼼짝도 않고, 앞서 서 있던 그대로 서 있었다. 그는 십 분을 그대로 있었으나 시간은 아주 느리게 갔다. 정오쯤에 무슨 낌새로 지금이 몇 시인지를 알게 되었을 때, 그는 그날이 아직 지나가기에는 멀었다는 것에 놀랐다.\n\n그러고서 또 한 번 문에 두드리는 소리가 났다. 그가 익히 아는 소리였다. 그리고 아내가 소리 없이 방 안으로 들어왔다. 그녀는 말을 하기 전에 그의 바로 앞까지 다가와 그의 팔에 제 팔을 끼웠다.\n\n“마크,” 그녀가 말했다. “그 사람들이 왔어요. 마당에 있어요.”\n\n“알고 있어.” 그가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다.\n\n“당신이 만나 보는 편이 나을까요, 여보?”\n\n“만나? 아니. 만나서 내가 무슨 도움이 되겠어? 하지만 곧 넉넉히 보게 될 거야. 몇 분 안에 여기로 오겠지.”\n\n“요리사 말로는 목록을 뜨고 있대요. 지금은 마구간에 있어요.”\n\n“되었어. 저들은 저들 하고 싶은 대로 할 수밖에. 나로서는 어쩔 수 없는 일이야.”\n\n“요리사 말로는, 저 사람들에게 끼니와 맥주를 주고 아무도 무엇을 들고 나가지만 않으면 아주 점잖게 굴 거라고 해요.”\n\n“점잖게라니! 하지만 그게 무슨 상관이야? 먹을 것이 남아 있는 동안은 저들 먹고 마시고 싶은 대로 하게 두어. 정육점에서 더 보내 주지는 않을 테니.”\n\n“하지만 마크, 정육점에 밀린 것은 없어요. 달마다 치르는 셈뿐이에요.”\n\n“되었어. 보면 알게 될 거야.”\n\n“아, 마크, 저를 그런 눈으로 보지 마세요. 저에게서 돌아서지 마세요. 이제 서로 붙들지 않는다면 무엇이 우리를 위로해 주겠어요?”\n\n“우리를 위로한다니! 하느님이 당신을 도우시기를! 패니, 당신이 나와 한 방에 있는 것을 견딜 수 있다는 게 나는 놀라워.”\n\n“마크, 우리 마크, 내 귀한, 내 가장 귀한 남편! 제가 아니라면 누가 당신에게 참되겠어요? 당신은 저에게서 돌아서면 안 돼요. 이런 일이 어떻게 당신과 저 사이를 갈라놓을 수 있겠어요?” 그러고서 그녀는 두 팔을 그의 목에 둘러 그를 껴안았다.\n\n그에게는 끔찍한 아침이었고, 그 아침의 모든 일이 목숨이 다하는 날까지 그의 기억에 붙어 있게 될 그런 아침이었다. 그는 제 자리를 그토록 자랑으로 삼아 왔고, 스스로 그토록 두드러진 위치를 차지해 왔으며, 어디서 익힌 재간으로 이웃 목사들의 머리보다 제 머리를 훨씬 높이 쳐들고 다니는 데 성공해 왔다. 그를 큰 사람들 사이로 데려간 것도, 그를 옴니엄 공작에게 소개한 것도, 그에게 바체스터의 참사회원 자리를 얻어 준 것도 바로 그것이었다. 그러나 이제 무슨 낯으로 머리를 쳐들 것인가? 애러빈네와 그랜틀리네는 무슨 말을 할 것인가? 주교는 어떻게 그를 비웃을 것이며, 프라우디 부인과 그 딸들은 저들이 드는 자리마다 그를 어떻게 입에 올릴 것인가? 크롤리는 그를 어떤 눈으로 볼 것인가—이미 한 번 그의 덜미를 잡았던 크롤리가? 크롤리의 그 냉엄한 얼굴이 이제 그의 앞에 어른거렸다. 아이들은 반쯤 헐벗고 아내는 종처럼 일하고 자신은 반쯤 굶주리면서도, 크롤리는 호글스톡의 제 집에 집행관을 한 번도 들인 적이 없었다! 그리고 그가 돌보아 주고 거의 딸린 사람처럼 대해 온 제 부목사 에번스는—다음 주일의 거룩한 직무를 두고 그 부목사의 얼굴을 어떻게 마주 보며 의논할 것인가?\n\n아내는 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 여전히 그의 곁에 서 있었다. 그리고 그녀를 보고 그녀의 괴로움을 생각하니, 사워비가 자기 위에 쌓아 올린 그 부당한 짓들에 대해서는 마음을 다스릴 수가 없었다. 그와 그의 아내에게 이 끔찍한 고통을 안긴 것은 사워비의 거짓말이요 사워비의 사기였다. “이 땅에 의로움이 있다면 그는 그 값을 치르게 될 거야.” 그가 마침내 말했다. 아내에게 들려주려고 한 말은 아니었으나 감정을 누를 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n“그에게 화가 내리기를 바라지 마세요, 마크. 그에게도 그만의 슬픔이 있을 거예요.”\n\n“그만의 슬픔이라니! 아니야. 그는 이만한 괴로움 따위에는 아무 느낌도 없는 사람이야. 부정직에 그토록 무디어져서 이 모든 일이 그에게는 웃음거리일 뿐이야. 거짓말에 하늘의 벌이 있다면—”\n\n“아, 마크, 그를 저주하지 마세요!”\n\n“그가 당신에게 무엇을 가져다주었는지를 보면서 어떻게 저주하지 않을 수 있겠어?”\n\n“‘원수 갚는 것은 내게 있으니, 주께서 이르시느니라.’” 젊은 아내가 대답했다. 꾸짖으려 마음먹은 사람처럼 엄숙한 설교조로가 아니라, 그의 귀에 가장 부드러운 속삭임으로였다. “그 일은 그분께 맡겨요, 마크. 그리고 우리로서는, 그분이 우리 모두의 마음을 부드럽게 해 주시기를 빌기로 해요. 우리를 괴로움에 빠뜨린 그 사람의 마음도, 우리 자신의 마음도요.”\n\n마크는 이 말에 대답할 겨를이 없었다. 다시 문에서 하인이 그를 어지럽혔기 때문이다. 이번에는 요리사가 몸소 와서 법 쪽 사람들의 전갈을 가져왔다. 그리고 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 그녀가 요리사로서 이런 일을 해야 할 필요가 있어서 온 것이 아니라는 점이다. 하인이든 로바츠 부인의 몸종이든 그녀만큼 얼마든지 올 수 있었으니 말이다. 그러나 일이 제 궤도에서 벗어나면 하인들도 늘 제 궤도에서 벗어난다. 여느 때라면 집사를 마구간으로 들여보낼 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없고, 하녀에게 프라이팬을 잡게 할 수 있는 것도 없다. 그러나 이제 이 새로운 흥분이 집안에 닥쳐 놓으니—집행관들이 집을 차지하고 앉았고 가재가 하나하나 명부에 올라가고 있으니—누구나 무슨 일이든 하려 들었다. 제 일만 빼놓고 무슨 일이든. 정원사는 귀한 아이들을 보고 있었고, 유모는 집행관들이 이르기 전에 방들을 치우고 있었고, 마부는 그들의 점심을 준비하러 부엌에 들어가 있었고, 요리사는 잉크병을 들고 돌아다니며 이 대단하신 권세가들의 분부를 죄다 받들고 있었다. 하인들만 두고 말한다면, 집행관들이 온 일이 여태까지는 오히려 즐거운 구경으로 여겨지지 않았을까 물어볼 만하다.\n\n“마님, 아룁니다만,” 제마이마 요리사가 말했다. “저 사람들이 어느 방에서 먼저 목로록을 뜨면 좋으시겠는지 알고 싶다고 합니다요. 저이들 말이, 마님이나 나리를 어쩔 수 없는 만큼보다 더 어지럽히지는 않겠다고요. 저이들 하는 일치고는요, 마님, 참 점잖습니다요—참말 점잖습니다요.”\n\n“응접실에 들어가도 되겠지.” 로바츠 부인이 슬프고 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 점잖은 여인이라면 누구나 제 응접실을 자랑으로 삼는 법이고, 그녀도 제 응접실을 크게 자랑으로 삼고 있었다. 그 방은 그들에게 돈이 넉넉했던 때, 혼인한 바로 뒤에 꾸민 것이어서 그 안의 모든 것이 곱고 좋고 그녀에게 소중했다. 곱고 좋고 소중한 것들이 놓인 응접실을 가진 부인들이여, 집행관 둘이 펜과 잉크통을 들고 그 사이를 헤집으며 집행 경매에 앞서 명부를 만든다는 것이 어떤 일일지 생각해 보시라. 게다가 그것이 제 잘못도 제 사치도 아닌 탓으로 그리 된다면! 거기에는 레이디 러프턴이 준 것도, 레이디 메러디스가 준 것도, 다른 벗들이 준 것도 있었으니, 그것들만이라도 이 더럽힘에서 건져 낼 수 있지 않을까 하는 생각이 그녀에게 떠오르기는 했다. 그러나 그런 말을 하면 그 말로 마크의 괴로움을 더하게 될까 하여 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n“그다음에는 식당이고요.” 제마이마 요리사가 거의 신이 난 어조로 말했다.\n\n“그래. 저들이 그러고 싶다면.”\n\n“그다음에는 여기 나리 서재이고요. 아니면 마님과 나리께서 그때도 여기 계시면 침실들이고요.”\n\n“저들 하고 싶은 대로 하라지, 요리사. 그것이야 대단할 것도 없으니.” 로바츠 부인이 말했다. 그러나 그 뒤 며칠 동안 제마이마는 그녀에게 도무지 마음에 드는 사람이 아니었다.\n\n요리사가 방에서 나가기가 무섭게 창 앞 자갈길에서 빠른 발소리가 들렸고, 곧 현관문이 열렸다.\n\n“주인은 어디 있나?” 익히 아는 러프턴 경의 목소리였다. 그러고서 반 분도 되지 않아 그도 서재에 들어와 있었다.\n\n“마크, 이게 다 무슨 일인가?” 그가 활기찬 목소리로, 밝은 얼굴로 말했다. “내가 와 있는 것을 몰랐나? 어제 내려왔네. 함부르크에서 배를 댄 것이 어제 아침이야. 그동안 잘 지내셨어요, 로바츠 부인? 참 딱한 일이 되었지요?”\n\n로바츠는 첫 순간에는 오랜 벗에게 어떻게 말을 붙여야 할지조차 알지 못했다. 제 처지의 부끄러움에 그는 말문이 막혔다. 더구나 제 불운은 러프턴 경이 얼마쯤은 풀어 줄 수 있는 것이었기에 더욱 그러했다. 그는 한 사람의 몫을 하게 된 뒤로 돈을 빌려 본 적이 없었으나, 그 젊은 귀족과 돈을 두고 말이 오간 일이 있었고 그때 벗이 자기에게 부당했다는 것을 알고 있었다. 이 두 겹의 이유로 그는 이제 한마디도 하지 못했다.\n\n“사워비 씨가 그이를 배신했습니다.” 로바츠 부인이 눈물을 닦으며 말했다. 여태까지 그녀는 사워비를 두고 한마디도 하지 않았으나, 이제는 남편을 지켜야 했다.\n\n“두말할 것도 없지. 그는 자기를 믿은 사람이면 누구든 언제나 배신해 왔다고 나는 보네. 얼마 전에 그가 어떤 사람인지 자네에게 말했지, 그러지 않았나? 그런데 마크, 자네는 어쩌자고 일을 이렇게까지 오게 두었나? 포레스트가 도와주지 않았나?”\n\n“포레스트 씨는 그이가 어음에 더 서명하기를 바랐지만 그이는 그러려 하지 않았습니다.” 로바츠 부인이 흐느끼며 말했다.\n\n“어음이란 독한 술을 홀짝이는 것과 같지.” 사려 깊은 젊은 나리가 말했다. “한번 시작하면 그만두기가 아주 어려운 것이야. 마크, 그 사람들이 지금 여기 와 있다는 게 참말인가?”\n\n“그렇네. 옆방에 있네.”\n\n“뭐, 응접실에?”\n\n“물건 목록을 적고 있습니다.” 로바츠 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그것만은 어떻게든 그만두게 해야지.” 나리는 이렇게 말하며 일이 벌어지고 있는 자리로 걸어 나갔다. 그가 방을 나가자 로바츠 부인도 그를 따라 나가, 남편을 혼자 남겨 두었다.\n\n“어째서 우리 어머니께 사람을 내려보내지 않았습니까?” 현관에 함께 서서 그가 속삭임보다 겨우 조금 큰 소리로 말했다.\n\n“그이가 그러지 못하게 했습니다.”\n\n“하지만 부인이 몸소 가시지 않은 것은 왜입니까? 아니면 우리가 이만큼 가까운 사이인데 나에게 편지를 쓰지 않은 것은 왜입니까?”\n\n러프턴 경과 루시 사이의 그 특별한 가까움 때문에, 설령 달리 그럴 수 있었다 하더라도 그렇게 할 수는 없었다는 것을 로바츠 부인은 그에게 설명할 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 그것이 사실임을 느꼈다.\n\n“자, 이 사람들, 여기서 참 못된 일을 하고 있군.” 그가 응접실로 걸어 들어가며 말했다. 그러자 요리사는 무릎을 깊이 굽혀 인사했고, 집행관들은 나리를 알아보고 일손을 멈추고 이마에 손을 올렸다. “미안하지만 이 일은 그만두어야 하네—당장. 자, 부엌으로 나가지. 아니면 바깥 어디로든. 그 큰 장화를 신고 가구들 사이에서 펜이며 잉크를 들고 있는 걸 보고 싶지 않네.”\n\n“저희는 아무 해도 끼치지 않았습니다요, 나리, 황송하오나.” 제마이마 요리사가 말했다.\n\n“저희야 마땅히 할 소임을 하고 있을 뿐입니다요.” 집행관 하나가 말했다.\n\n“저희가 그리하기로 맹세를 한 대로요, 나리, 황송하오나.” 다른 하나가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 신사분이나 숙녀분한테, 참말 신사고 숙녀인 분들한테 불펀을 끼쳐서 참 안됐습니다요, 나리. 하지만 일이란 게 생기고 마는 법인데, 저희 같은 것들이 어쩌겠습니까요?” 앞의 하나가 말했다.\n\n“저희는 맹세를 한 몸이니까요, 나리.” 뒤의 하나가 말했다. 그러나 그럼에도, 저들의 맹세에도, 또 저들이 내세운 그 엄한 필요에도 아랑곳없이, 그들은 그 귀족의 말 한마디에 일손을 놓았다. 귀족의 이름은 아직도 영국에서 크기 때문이다.\n\n“그러면 이제 이 방을 비우고, 로바츠 부인이 제 응접실에 들어가게 하게.”\n\n“나리, 황송하오나, 그러면 저희는 어떻게 되는 겁니까요? 저희는 누구를 바라보아야 합니까요?”\n\n이 점에서 그들을 온전히 만족시키려면 러프턴 경은 귀족으로서의 위세만으로는 모자랐다. 펜과 종이가 있어야 했다. 그러나 펜과 종이로 그는 그들을 만족시켰다. 그들이 스텁스의 방, 곧 전에 병든 말을 두던 칸으로 물러가기로 하는 데까지 만족시킨 것이다. 끼니와 맥주에 관한 약조도 마땅히 붙였고, 거기서 집을 비우라는 명령을 기다리기로 했으니, 그 명령은 나리의 위세로 이튿날 틀림없이 그들에게 이를 것이었다. 이 모든 것의 뜻은, 러프턴 경이 로바츠 씨가 진 빚 전부를 제 어깨에 짊어지기로 했다는 것이었다.\n\n그러고서 그는 서재로 돌아왔다. 마크는 아침을 먹은 바로 뒤에 제 몸을 세워 놓았던 그 자리에 거의 그대로 서 있었다. 로바츠 부인은 돌아오지 않고 아이들에게로 올라갔다. 블레셋 사람들을 맞으려고 아이 방을 치우라며 내렸던 지시를 거두려는 것이었다. “마크,” 그가 말했다. “이 일로 어쩔 수 없는 것보다 더 마음을 쓰지 말게. 저 사람들은 손을 놓았고, 내일 아침에는 이 집을 떠날 걸세.”\n\n“그러면 그 돈은—어떻게 치러지는 건가?” 딱한 성직자가 말했다.\n\n“지금은 그런 것으로 속을 끓이지 말게. 그 짐이 끝내는 자네 자신에게 떨어지도록—다른 누구에게도 아니라—그렇게 되도록 해 놓을 걸세. 하지만 자네 부인이 제 응접실에서 쫓겨나지 않아도 된다는 것은 자네에게도 위로가 될 거라고 나는 믿네.”\n\n“하지만 러프턴, 나는 자네에게 그렇게 하게 할 수 없네—그런 일이 있었는데—게다가 이런 때에—”\n\n“여보게, 나도 다 아네. 그 얘기를 이제 하려던 것이야. 자네는 컬링을 세웠으니 컬링이 마무리할 것이고, 맹세하지만 마크, 그 어음은 자네가 갚는 걸세. 다만 이번 급한 일에는 내 은행에 돈이 있네.”\n\n“하지만 러프턴—”\n\n“그리고 정직하게 말하자면, 컬링의 셈 말인데, 그것은 자네 일인 만큼 내 일이기도 해야 하네. 자네를 사워비와 이 난장판으로 끌어들인 것은 나였고, 런던에서 그 일로 내가 자네에게 얼마나 부당했는지를 이제는 아네. 하지만 사실은 사워비의 배신에 내가 거의 미칠 지경이었던 것이야. 그 뒤로 자네도 같은 일을 당했겠지. 그건 의심할 것도 없네.”\n\n“그는 나를 망쳐 놓았네.” 로바츠가 말했다.\n\n“아니야, 그가 그렇게까지 하지는 못했네. 그가 봐준 덕은 아니지만. 그럴 기회가 왔다면 그는 거리낌 없이 그렇게 했을 사람이야. 사실은 말이지 마크, 그런 사람의 사기가 얼마나 깊은지는 자네도 나도 짐작할 수 없어. 그는 언제나 돈을 찾고 있네. 벗으로서 가장 다정하게 어울리는 그 모든 시간에도—자네와 함께 술잔을 앞에 놓고 앉아 있을 때에도, 사냥터에서 자네 옆에 나란히 말을 달릴 때에도—그는 여전히 자네를 어떻게 써서 제 어떤 곤경을 넘길까를 생각하고 있다고 나는 믿네. 그는 그런 식으로 살아온 끝에 남을 속이는 데서 즐거움을 느끼게 되었고, 그 길에 하도 능해져서 자네나 내가 내일 다시 그와 마주 앉는다면 그는 또 우리를 이겨 놓을 걸세. 반드시 아주 피해야 할 사람이야. 나는 적어도 그만큼은 알게 되었네.”\n\n이런 견해를 말하는 데서 러프턴 경은 딱한 사워비에게 너무 매정했다. 사실 우리는 누구나 세상의 악당들에 대한 견해를 세울 때 너무 매정해지기 쉽다. 사워비 씨가 악당이었다는 것은 나도 부인할 수 없다. 거짓말을 하는 것은 악당의 짓이고, 그는 거짓말을 크게 하는 사람이었다. 하는 사람 자신이 지킬 수 없음을 아는 약속을 하는 것은 악당의 짓이고, 사워비 씨는 날마다 그것을 해 왔다. 남의 돈으로 사는 것은 악당의 짓이고, 사워비 씨는 오래도록 그렇게 살아왔다. 악당들과 기꺼이 거래하는 것도 악당의 짓이라고—적어도 나는 그렇게 보겠다—사워비 씨는 그런 거래를 한결같이 해 왔다. 여기 늘어놓은 그런 짓들로 드러나는 것보다 더 뚜렷한 악당 짓에 그가 때때로 빠지지 않았는지 어떤지는 나도 모른다. 그의 마음자리에는 아직 신사다운 몸가짐의 기미가 남아 있었고 더 나은 것들에 대한 취향이 그 안에 머물러 있었음을 알기에 나는 그에게 얼마쯤 다정한 마음을 품고 있으나, 그 큰 죄목에서 그를 놓아줄 수는 없다. 그러나 그 모든 것에도, 인정된 그의 악당 짓에도, 러프턴 경은 그를 판단하는 데서 너무 매정했다. 그의 안에는 아직 회개의 밑거리가 있었으니, 그에게 「로쿠스 페니텐티아이」가 주어질 수만 있었다면 그러했을 것이다. 그는 제가 저지른 나쁜 짓들을 두고 쓰라리게 슬퍼했고, 신사다움이 제게 무엇을 요구했을지도 잘 알고 있었다. 어떤 변화로도 돌이킬 수 없을 만큼 그가 너무 멀리 갔는지 어떤지—그에게 「로쿠스 페니텐티아이」가 아직 있을 수 있는 일인지 어떤지—그것은 그와 더 높은 힘 사이의 일이었다.\n\n“나를 탓할 사람은 나밖에 없네.” 마크가 여전히 같은 가슴 무너진 목소리로, 얼굴은 벗에게서 돌린 채로 말했다.\n\n빚은 이제 치러질 것이고 집행관들은 쫓겨날 것이었다. 그러나 그것으로 세상 앞에서 그가 바로 세워지지는 않았다. 집행리들이 프램리 목사관을 차고 앉아 있었다는 것을 모든 사람이—교구의 모든 성직자가—알게 될 것이고, 그는 바체스터 성당 구내에서 다시는 머리를 쳐들 수 없을 것이었다.\n\n“여보게, 우리가 다 이만한 자잘한 일로 스스로를 비참하게 만든다면—” 러프턴 경은 벗의 어깨에 다정하게 팔을 얹으며 말했다.\n\n“하지만 우리가 다 성직자는 아니지.” 마크가 말했다. 그러면서 그는 창가로 돌아섰고, 러프턴 경은 그의 뺨에 눈물이 있음을 알았다.\n\n그러고서 얼마 동안 두 사람 사이에는 아무 말도 없었다. 그러다 러프턴 경이 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n“마크, 여보게!”\n\n“왜 그러나.” 마크가 여전히 얼굴을 창 쪽으로 돌린 채로 말했다.\n\n“한 가지는 기억해 두어야 하네. 이 울짱을 넘게 자네를 도와주는 일은 나로서는 참말 아무 불편도 아니고, 게다가 나에게는 오랜 벗이라는 것보다도 더 나은 자격이 있네. 나는 이제 자네를 처남으로 여기고 있어.”\n\n마크가 천천히 돌아섰다. 얼굴 위의 눈물이 그대로 드러났다.\n\n“그러면” 하고 그가 말했다. “무슨 일이 더 있었다는 말인가?”\n\n“나는 자네 누이를 아내로 삼을 작정이네. 그녀가 나를 사랑한다는 말을 자네를 통해 보내왔고, 그 뒤에까지 터무니없는 소리에 매여 있을 생각은 없어. 그녀와 내가 둘 다 원한다면 살아 있는 누구도 우리 사이에 끼어들 권리가 없네. 맹세코 아무도 그러지 못할 걸세. 나는 무엇도 숨어서 하지 않겠네. 그러니 마님께 말씀드린 그대로 자네에게도 이렇게 말해 두는 것이야.”\n\n“그런데 마님은 무엇이라 하시는가?”\n\n“아무 말씀도 없으시네. 하지만 그렇게 갈 수는 없지. 어머니가 이런 식으로 나를 막으신다면 어머니와 나는 여기서 함께 살 수 없어. 호글스톡으로 자네 누이를 찾아가서 그녀를 놀라게 하고 싶지는 않네. 하지만 내가 지금 자네에게 말한 만큼은 내가 한 말로 그녀에게 전해 주기를 바라네. 그러지 않으면 그녀는 내가 자기를 잊었다고 여길 테니.”\n\n“그렇게 여기지는 않을 걸세.”\n\n“그렇게 여길 까닭이야 없지. 잘 있게, 이 사람. 이 사워비 건으로 자네와 마님 사이는 내가 다 잘 풀어 놓겠네.”\n\n그러고서 그는 작별하고 나가, 돈을 치르는 일을 마무르러 걸어갔다.\n\n“어머니,” 그가 그날 저녁 레이디 러프턴에게 말했다. “이 집행관 일을 두고 로바츠를 탓하지 마세요. 그의 잘못보다 제 잘못이 더 큽니다.”\n\n여태까지 레이디 러프턴과 아들 사이에 이 일을 두고 오간 말은 한마디도 없었다. 그녀는 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지를 끔찍한 낙담으로 들었고, 러프턴 경이 곧바로 목사관으로 갔다는 것도 들었다. 그러니 이제 그녀가 끼어들 수는 없었다. 필요한 돈이야 나오게 될 것을 그녀는 알고 있었으나, 그것으로 성직자의 집에 집행이 들어왔다는 그 끔찍한 수치가 씻기지는 않았다. 게다가 그는 그녀의 성직자였다—그녀 자신의 성직자, 그녀가 골라 세워 프램리로 데려왔고, 그녀가 고른 아내를 붙여 주었고, 그녀 자신의 손으로 좋은 것들을 채워 준 사람이었다! 그것은 끔찍한 사고였고, 그녀는 로바츠라는 이름을 애초에 들었다는 것 자체를 뉘우치기 시작했다. 그러나 제 돈을 내어 이 화를 줄이는 일이 필요하거나 될 수 있는 일이었다면 그녀는 손을 내미는 데 결코 더디지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 로바츠와 제 아들 사이에 그녀가 어떻게 끼어들 수 있겠는가. 더구나 루시와 러프턴 경 사이에 이야기되던 그 인연을 떠올린다면?\n\n“네 잘못이라니, 루도빅?”\n\n“예, 어머니. 그를 사워비 씨에게 소개한 것은 저였습니다. 그리고 사실을 말하자면, 그때 사워비 씨와 저 사이에 걸려 있던 돈 문제를 두고 제가 그에게 무슨 심부름 같은 것을 맡기지 않았다면 그가 사워비와 가까워지는 일도 결코 없었을 겁니다. 그 일들은 이제 다 끝났지요—참말 어머니 덕으로요.”\n\n“달리 붙드는 마음이 없었다 해도, 로바츠 씨는 성직자라는 신분만으로라도 그런 곤경에 빠지지 않았어야 했어.”\n\n“어떻든 어머니, 이 일은 그냥 넘겨 주시면 제 청을 들어주시는 것이 되겠어요.”\n\n“아, 그에게는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다.”\n\n“부인에게는 무슨 말이든 해 주시는 편이 좋아요. 그러지 않으면 오히려 이상할 테니까요. 그리고 그에게도 저라면 한두 마디는 하겠어요—어머니가 그토록 잘 아시는 그 다정한 한마디를요. 어머니가 아주 잠잠히 계시는 것보다 그편이 그에게 더 편할 겁니다.”\n\n그때 두 사람 사이에 더 이야기가 이어지지는 않았으나, 저녁이 깊어서 그녀는 손을 아들의 이마에 스쳐 길고 부드러운 머리카락을 제자리로 쓸어 넘겼다. 사랑의 어떤 각별한 마음이 움직일 때 늘 하던 대로였다. “루도빅,” 그녀가 말했다. “내 생각에는 너만큼 마음이 착한 사람이 없다. 로바츠 씨와 그 돈 문제는 네가 바라는 그대로 하겠다.” 그러고서 그 일을 두고 더 이야기된 것은 아무것도 없었다.\n\n# 제45장. 주교관의 축복\n\n그런데 이 무렵 무서운 소문들이 바체스터로 흘러들어, 대성당의 탑들 사이를 날아다니고 대성당 문 앞을 감돌았다. 그렇다, 참사회원들의 집 안에까지, 그리고 성가대 사제들의 더 조촐한 거실에까지 날아들었다. 그것들이 거기에서 주교관으로 올라갔는지, 아니면 주교관에서 구내로 내려왔는지는 내가 감히 말하지 않겠다. 그러나 그 소문들은 사람을 놀라게 하고 사람의 도리에 어긋나는 것이었으며, 그 구역에 그토록 빽빽하게 모여 있는 저 훌륭한 교회의 심정들에게는 의심할 여지 없이 애통한 것이었다.\n\n그 가운데 첫째 것은 새 참사회원과 그가 성당 참사회에 안긴 수치에 관한 것이었다. 그들 가운데 몇몇이 자랑삼아 말한 대로, 바체스터가 전에 한 번도 겪어 본 적 없는 수치라는 것이었다. 그러나 이 자랑도 다른 대개의 자랑처럼 참이라 하기는 어려웠다. 불과 몇 해 전에 작고한 참사회원 스탠호프 노박사의 집에 강제 집행이 들어온 일이 있었고, 그때 그 박사 자신도 의자며 탁자와 함께 자기까지 블레셋 사람들의 손에 떨어지지 않도록 밤중에 길을 떠나 이탈리아로 달아나야 했던 것이다.\n\n“참으로 망측한 창피요,” 프라우디 부인이 그 노박사를 두고서가 아니라 새로 죄를 지은 사람을 두고 말했다. “참으로 망측한 창피지요. 그 사람 등에서 법의를 벗겨 낸다 해도 마땅한 일일 뿐이오.”\n\n“그 사람 성직록은 압류되겠지요,” 이 교구를 다스리는 부인이 교회 일에 관하여 내리는 지시에 매우 열심히 귀 기울이는 젊은 하급 참사회원 하나가 말했으니, 그는 그만한 대접을 받을 만하게 큰 총애를 누리고 있었다. 프램리가 압류된다면, 남이 아니라 어째서 그가 그 직무를 맡아서는 안 되겠는가—주교가 정해 줄 만한 급여를 받고서?\n\n“그 사람은 빚에 목까지 잠겼다고 들었어요,” 앞으로 티클러 부인이 될 사람이 말했다. “그리고 대개는 사들이고서 값을 치르지 않은 말 때문이라던데요.”\n\n“그가 대성당 일로 건너올 때 아주 훌륭한 짐승을 타고 오는 것을 저는 봅니다,” 하급 참사회원이 말했다.\n\n“지금 그 집에는 집행리들이 들어와 있다고 들었소,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“그러면 그이는 감옥에 있지 않은가요?” 티클러 부인이 말했다.\n\n“아직 아니라면, 마땅히 그리 되어야지요,” 티클러 부인의 어머니가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 의심할 것 없이 곧 그리 될 겁니다,” 하급 참사회원이 말했다. “아주 평판이 좋지 못한 무리와 얽혀 있다고 저는 들었으니까요.”\n\n그 제목을 두고 주교관에서 나온 말이 이러했다. 그리고 의심할 것 없이 그곳에서는 재주가 덜한 성직자들의 집보다 더 많은 기개와 시적 재주가 펼쳐졌겠으나, 이것으로 로바츠 씨의 불운이 일반으로 어떻게 이야기되었는지는 드러난다. 사실 그가 그들의 손에서 이보다 나은 대접을 받을 자격도 없었다. 그러나 그의 이름은 흔히 그러하듯 아흐레 동안 매질의 열 사이를 지나가지는 않았다. 아니, 그의 명성이 그 절정에 머문 것은 이틀을 넘지 못했다. 이 갑작스러운 추락은 훨씬 더 사람을 괴롭게 하는 다른 소식 때문에 일어났다. 프라우디 부인이 하도 마음을 쓴 나머지, 그녀의 말로는 제 피가 스멀거리게 만든 소문 하나 때문이었다. 그리고 그녀는 남들의 피도—남들의 피가 그만큼 예민하다면—스멀거리게 하려고 몹시 마음을 썼다. 덤벨로 경이 그랜틀리 양을 버렸다는 것이었다.\n\n세상의 어느 험한 구석에서 이 잔혹한 소식이 바체스터에 떨어졌는지 나는 끝내 알아낼 수 없었다. 소문이라는 것이 얼마나 빠르게 날아가 온 도시에 저를 흔한 것으로 만들어 놓는지는 우리도 알고 있다. 프라우디 부인이 하틀탑 집안에 얽힌 사실들을 바체스터의 그 누구보다 더 많이 알고 있었다는 것은 놀랄 일이 아니었으니, 그런 사람들이 사는 큰 세상 물정에 그녀가 훨씬 더 통해 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 덤벨로 경이 이미 다른 젊은 아가씨 하나를 버린 적이 있다는 것을—세 사교철 전에 그와 약혼했던 레이디 줄리아 맥 멀을—알고 있었고, 그러므로 그런 일에서 그의 됨됨이는 믿을 것이 아니라고 선언한 것도 충분히 옳았다. 레이디 줄리아가 무서운 희롱꾼이었고 어떤 독일인 백작과 왈츠 추기에 몹시 빠져 있었으며 그 뒤 그 백작과 함께 달아나 버렸다는 것은—큰 세상 물정에 그토록 통해 있었다지만 프라우디 부인이 몰랐던 것이라고 나는 짐작한다. 이번 자리에서 교회에 속한 그 청중 가운데 누구에게도 그 이야기는 한마디도 하지 않았으니 말이다.\n\n“퀴버풀 부인, 이것은 우리 모두에게 무서운 경고가 될 것이오. 우리에게 아주 유익한 경고지요—이 세상의 것들을 믿지 말라는. 그 사람들이 딸의 이름을 그 젊은 귀족의 이름과 함께 묶기로 합의하기 전에 그 젊은 귀족에 대해 아무런 알아봄도 하지 않았을 것이 나는 걱정이오.” 이 말은 히램 요양원의 현 워든의 아내에게 한 것이었으니, 그 부인은 그녀에게서 은덕을 입은 바 있어 그 목소리에 주의 깊게 귀 기울여야 할 처지였다.\n\n“그러나 사실이 아니었으면 좋겠어요,” 퀴버풀 부인이 말했으니, 프라우디 부인에게 마땅히 바쳐야 할 충성이 있음에도 그랜틀리 집안이 잘되기를 바랄 만한 그녀 자신의 이유가 있었던 것이다.\n\n“나도 진심으로 그러기를 바라오,” 프라우디 부인이 목소리에 노여움을 살짝 실으며 말했다. “그러나 의심할 여지가 없는 것이 걱정이오. 그리고 이것은 우리가 마땅히 예상해도 좋았던 것에 지나지 않는다고 나는 고백해야 하겠소. 이것이 우리 모두에게 하나의 가르침으로, 하나의 본보기로, 주님의 자비를 알려 주는 가르침으로 받아들여지기를 나는 바라오. 그리고 부인의 남편께 청해 주었으면 하오—나에게서 나온 말이라고, 퀴버풀 부인—다음 안식일에 요양원의 아침저녁 강론에서 이 주제를 붙들고, 이 세상의 좋은 것들에 두는 믿음이 얼마나 거짓된 것인지를 보여 주시라고.” 이 분부를 퀴버풀 씨는 어느 정도 따랐으니, 바체스터에서 조용히 사는 것이 자기에게 큰 값이 있음을 느꼈던 것이다. 그러나 그 요양원의 늙은 구호 노인들로 이루어진 청중에게 야심 있는 혼인 계획을 조심하라고 경계하는 데까지는 나아가지 않았다.\n\n이런 경우에 늘 그러하듯이 이 일에서도, 그 소문은 부주교나 그 아내가 듣기 전에 성당 참사회 사람들 모두에게 알려져 있었다. 수석 사제도 그것을 들었으나 귀에 담지 않았다. 수석 사제의 아내도—처음에는—그러했다. 그리고 교구의 싸움에서 대개 그랜틀리 편에 서는 사람들은 그 소식에 코웃음을 치며, 부주교나 그랜틀리 부인이나 다 제 일은 넉넉히 알아서 처리할 수 있는 사람들이라고 서로 말했다. 그러나 방울지는 물이 돌을 파낸다. 그리하여 마침내 두려워할 근거가 있다는 것이 온 사방에서 인정되었다—플럼스테드만 빼고 온 사방에서.\n\n“저는 그 말에 아무것도 없다고 확신해요. 정말로 확신해요,” 애러빈 부인이 언니에게 속삭이며 말했다. “하지만 마음속으로 이리저리 굴려 본 뒤에 언니에게 말해 두는 것이 옳다고 생각했어요. 그런데 지금은 또 제가 잘못한 것이 아닌가 싶어요.”\n\n“잘한 거예요, 우리 엘리너,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “고맙게 생각해요. 하지만 우리는 그게 어떤 것인지 알지요. 다른 온갖 기독교의 축복과 마찬가지로, 그건 물론 주교관에서 오는 것이에요.” 그러고서 그랜틀리 부인과 그 동생 사이에 그 일을 두고 더 이야기된 것은 없었다.\n\n그러나 이튿날 아침 우편으로 편지 한 통이 왔으니, 그랜틀리 부인에게 온 것이었고 리틀배스의 소인이 찍혀 있었다. 편지는 이러했다—\n\n덤벨로 경이 지금의 약혼에서 빠져나갈 방도를 두고 어떤 벗들과 짜 놓았다는 것을 이 글을 쓰는 이는 알고 있습니다. 그러므로 이 일을 부인께 알려 드리는 것이 기독교인으로서 저의 의무라고 생각합니다.\n\n진심을 담아,\n\n그런데 마침 알 속의 티클러 부인의 가장 가깝고 속을 털어놓는 벗이 리틀배스에 산다는 것이 플럼스테드에 알려져 있었고, 또 마침—참으로 불행하게도—그 알 속의 티클러 부인이 이웃으로서 품은 따뜻한 마음에서 제 벗 그리젤다 그랜틀리에게 다정한 몇 줄을 적어 보내어, 덤벨로 경과 맺는 그 훌륭한 혼례를 여자다운 온갖 진심으로 축하한 일이 있었던 것이다.\n\n“이건 그 사람의 본래 글씨가 아니에요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 남편과 그 일을 이야기하며 말했다. “하지만 그 사람에게서 나온 것임은 틀림없다고 보셔도 돼요. 이건 우리가 날마다 주교관의 가르침에서 배우는 그 새로운 기독교의 한 부분이지요.”\n\n그러나 이런 일들은 부주교의 마음에 얼마간 작용을 했다. 그는 근래에 레이디 줄리아 맥 멀의 이야기를 알게 되었고, 자기 사위가—앞으로 곧 그리 되어야 할 사람이—그 일에서 전혀 나무랄 데 없었다고는 확신하지 못했다. 그리고 요즈음 덤벨로 경은 이렇다 할 표시를 크게 보이지 않았다. 그리젤다가 플럼스테드로 돌아오자마자 그는 그녀에게 훌륭한 에메랄드 선물을 보내 왔으나, 그것은 보석상에서 그녀에게 곧바로 온 것이었고 그 집 일 보는 사람이 주문한 것일 수도—아마도 그러했을 것이—있었다. 그 뒤로는 그가 오지도, 무엇을 보내지도, 편지를 쓰지도 않았다. 그리젤다는 사랑의 그 흔한 표시가 없는 것을 조금도 마음에 걸려 하는 것 같지 않았고, 제 큰 소임을 꾸준히 해 나갔다. 그녀가 어머니에게 말한 대로 “편지에 관해서는 아무 말도 없었으니 저는 기다리지 않아요”라는 것이었다. 그러나 부주교는 그다지 마음이 편하지 않았다. “덤벨로가 몸가짐을 흐트러뜨리지 않도록 단단히 붙들어 두시게, 아시겠소,” 그의 벗 하나가 클럽에서 그에게 귓속말로 그렇게 말했던 것이다. 하늘에 맹세코, 그렇다. 부주교는 그런 자리에서 받은 억울함을 아무렇지 않게 견딜 사람이 아니었다. 성직자라는 직분에도 불구하고, 제 몸에 닿은 억울함에 맞서 싸우려는 마음이 그보다 더한 사람은 드물었다—그리고 그보다 더 잘 싸울 수 있는 사람도 드물었다.\n\n“무엇인가 잘못된 것이 있을 수 있겠소?” 그가 아내에게 말했다. “내가 런던으로 올라가 볼 만한 일이오?” 그러나 그랜틀리 부인은 그 모든 것을 주교관의 교리 탓으로 돌렸다. 티클러 약혼의 온갖 사정을 보건대 이보다 더 자연스러운 일이 무엇이겠는가? 그러므로 그녀는 부주교가 어떤 조치를 취하는 데 반대표를 던졌다.\n\n그로부터 하루 이틀 뒤에 프라우디 부인이 구내에서 애러빈 부인을 만나 혼사 조약이 끝나 버린 일을 두고 드러내어 위로했다. 참으로 드러내어 그리했으니, 티클러 부인이—앞으로 그리 될 사람이—어머니와 함께 있었고, 애러빈 부인은 그 시누이 메리 볼드와 함께 있었기 때문이다.\n\n“그랜틀리 부인에게는 참 애통한 일일 테지요, 참으로 애통한 일이오,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “나도 진심으로 그 부인을 딱하게 여기오. 그러나 애러빈 부인, 이 모든 가르침은 우리의 영원한 복을 위하여 우리에게 보내지는 것이오.”\n\n“그렇지요,” 애러빈 부인이 말했다. “하지만 이 특별한 가르침에 관해서는, 저는 그것이—”\n\n“아—! 너무나 참인 것이 걱정이오. 의심할 자리가 없는 것이 걱정이오. 물론 부인도 덤벨로 경이 대륙으로 떠났다는 것은 알고 있겠지요.”\n\n애러빈 부인은 그것을 알지 못했고, 알지 못한다고 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“나흘 전에 불로뉴 쪽으로 길을 떠났어요,” 티클러 부인이 말했으니, 이 일 전체에 아주 밝은 듯했다. “가엾고 귀한 그리젤다가 참 안타까워요. 옷가지는 다 갖추어 놓았다고 들었는데. 참 애석한 일이지요.”\n\n“하지만 덤벨로 경이 대륙에서 돌아오지 않을 이유가 어디 있나요?” 볼드 양이 아주 조용히 말했다.\n\n“그야 없지요. 그이가 돌아오기를 나도 진심으로 바라오,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그리고 의심할 것 없이 언젠가는 돌아올 테지요. 그러나 그이가 사람들 말대로 그런 사내라면, 그런 혼인에서 그리젤다가 벗어나게 된 것은 참으로 잘된 일이오. 어차피 애러빈 부인, 이 세상의 것들이 무엇이오?—우리 발밑의 티끌, 우리 이 사이의 재, 가마에 넣으려고 베어 놓은 풀, 헛됨과 괴로움, 그 밖에는 아무것도 아니지요!” 이렇게 여러 가지 기독교다운 비유를 늘어놓은 것에 아주 흐뭇해하며 프라우디 부인은 걸어갔다. 그러면서도 지렁이며 구더기에 관하여 무엇인가를 여전히 웅얼거렸으니, 그것으로 그녀는 제가 속한 종족을, 그리고 그 가운데 특히 덤벨로 파와 그랜틀리 파를 가리키려 한 것이었다.\n\n이제 일이 이만큼까지 이르렀으므로 애러빈 부인은 언니를 만나 보는 것이 마땅한 소임이라고 생각했고, 그리하여 플럼스테드에서 의논한 끝에 부주교가 공식으로 주교관을 찾아가 그 소문을 부인해 달라고 청하기로 정해졌다. 그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 그 일을 했고 주교의 서재로 안내되어 들어갔는데, 그 방에는 주교와 프라우디 부인이 다 있었다. 주교는 일어나 그를 맞이하며 각별한 예를 갖추었고, 제 성직자 가운데 부주교가 가장 마음에 드는 사람이기라도 한 듯이 그에게 가장 다사로운 웃음을 지어 보였다. 그러나 프라우디 부인은 어딘가 어두운 낯빛이었으니, 그런 시각에 그런 방문이 있다면 무엇인가 특별한 용건이 걸려 있을 것을 아는 듯했다. 부주교가 예의를 차리는 뜻에서 주교관에 아침 방문을 하는 일은 많지 않았다.\n\n이번 자리에서 그는 곧바로 제 용건으로 뛰어들었다. “프라우디 부인, 오늘 아침 제가 찾아온 것은,” 그가 말했다. “부인께 한 가지 청을 드리고 싶어서입니다.” 그러자 프라우디 부인이 고개를 숙였다.\n\n“프라우디 부인이 아주 기꺼워할 거예요, 나는 그렇게 믿어요,” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“바체스터의 어리석은 몇 사람이 제 딸을 두고 이야기하고 있다는 것을 알았습니다,” 부주교가 말했다. “그래서 프라우디 부인께 청하고자—”\n\n그런 처지에 놓이면 대개의 여인은 제 자리가 난처하다는 것을 느끼고, 얼굴을 찡그리며 제가 한 말을 되씹어 삼킬 준비를 했을 것이다. 그러나 프라우디 부인은 그렇지 않았다. 그랜틀리 부인이 슬로프 씨를 그녀의 얼굴에 던지는 무람없는 짓을 했던 것이다—그것도 그녀 자신의 응접실에서. 그래서 그녀는 앙갚음을 하기로 마음을 굳혀 놓고 있었다. 게다가 그랜틀리 부인은 티클러 혼사를 비웃기까지 했으니, 이제 지나친 조심 따위가 프라우디 부인이 덤벨로 혼사를 두고 제 속을 말하는 것을 막아설 수는 없었다.\n\n“애석하지만 아주 많은 사람이 그 아이를 두고 이야기하고 있소,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그러나 가엾고 귀한 것, 그건 그 아이 탓이 아니오. 어느 처녀에게든 일어날 수 있는 일이지요. 다만 조금 더 조심을 했더라면—; 실례하겠소, 그랜틀리 박사.”\n\n“저는 바체스터에 퍼진 소문, 곧 덤벨로 경과 제 딸 사이의 혼사가 깨졌다는 소문을 두고 말씀드리려고 여기 왔습니다. 그리고—”\n\n“바체스터의 누구나 다 알고 있는 줄로 나는 아오,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n—“그리고,” 부주교가 이어서 말했다. “그 소문을 부인해 주시기를 청하러 왔습니다.”\n\n“부인이라니요! 아니, 그이는 아주 떠나 버렸소—이 나라 밖으로 나갔소!”\n\n“그 사람이 어디로 갔는지는 상관없습니다, 프라우디 부인. 저는 그 소문을 부인해 주시기를 청합니다.”\n\n“그러면 바체스터의 집집마다 다 돌아다녀야 할 거요,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“결코 그렇지 않습니다,” 부주교가 대답했다. “그리고 제가 어째서 여기 왔는지를 주교님께 설명해 드리는 것이 옳겠습니다. 그것은—”\n\n“주교는 이 일에 관하여 아무것도 모르오,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다.\n\n“세상에 아무것도 몰라요,” 주교가 말했다. “그리고 그 젊은 아가씨가 실망하지 않기를 나도 진심으로 바라요.”\n\n—“어제 부인께서 애러빈 부인에게 그 일을 그토록 분명하게 말씀하셨기 때문입니다.”\n\n“분명하게 말했다니! 물론 분명하게 말했지요. 말 밑에 감추어 둘 수 없는 일도 있는 법이오, 그랜틀리 박사. 그리고 이것이 그 가운데 하나인 듯하오. 이런 식으로 돌아다녀 봐야 덤벨로 경이 그 아가씨와 혼인하게 되지는 않을 거요.”\n\n그것은 참말이었다. 그러나 그리한다고 프라우디 부인이 입을 다물게 되지도 않을 것이었다. 어쩌면 부주교가 이번 걸음에서 잘못하고 있는 것인지도 몰랐고, 그도 이제 그런 생각이 들기 시작했다. “적어도,” 그가 말했다. “그런 소문에는 아무런 근거도 없다고 제가 말씀드리는 이상, 부인께서 관계하시는 한에서는 그것이 더 퍼져 나가지 않게 하겠다고 말씀해 주시는 친절은 베풀어 주시겠지요. 주교님, 그만한 것을 청하는 것이 지나친 청은 아니라고 저는 생각합니다.”\n\n“주교는 이 일에 관하여 아무것도 모르오,” 프라우디 부인이 다시 말했다.\n\n“전혀 아무것도 몰라요,” 주교가 말했다.\n\n“그리고 이 일에 관하여 나에게 이르러 온 소식을 나는 믿는다고 밝혀야 하는 터이니,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “내가 어떻게 그것을 부인할 수 있겠소. 그랜틀리 박사, 댁의 심정은 나도 쉽게 알 만하오. 댁의 딸의 처지를 생각하면 그 혼사는, 이 땅의 재물로 보아 아주 대단한 것이었지요. 그것이 깨진 일로 댁이 슬퍼하는 것을 나는 이상하게 여기지 않소. 그러나 이 슬픔이 댁과 그리젤다 양에게 축복으로 열매 맺기를 바라오. 이 세상의 낙심은 값진 향유이니, 그것을 그렇게 받아들이는 법을 댁이 알고 있으리라고 믿소.”\n\n사실 그랜틀리 박사가 주교관에 온 것은 아주 잘못한 일이었다. 그의 아내라면 프라우디 부인을 상대로 얼마간 승산이 있었겠지만 그에게는 전혀 없었다. 그녀가 바체스터에 온 뒤로 그가 그녀와 맞붙은 것은 두세 번뿐이었으나 그 모든 자리에서 그는 벽으로 밀렸다. 주교관을 찾아가는 일은 언제나 그가 그 집 사람들 앞에서 물러나며 도무지 바랄 것 없는 심정에 잠기는 것으로 끝났고, 이번에도 다시 그렇게 되었음을 그는 알았다. 그는 프라우디 부인에게 그 소문이 사실이 아니라고 말하도록 강제할 수 없었다. 또한 아내라면 했을 것처럼 그녀 자신의 딸을 두고 맞받아 찌르는 짓까지 하려고 몸을 낮출 수도 없었다. 그리하여 아주 실패하고서 그는 일어나 작별했다.\n\n그러나 이 일에서 가장 나쁜 것은, 집으로 돌아가면서 그가 그 소문에 얼마간 참이 있을지도 모른다는 생각을 마음에서 떨쳐 낼 수 없었다는 점이다. 만약 덤벨로 경이 대륙으로 가서 거기에서, 그랜틀리 양을 아내로 삼는 것이 불가능한 이유를 무엇인가 적어 보내리라 작정하고 떠난 것이라면? 그런 일은 전에도 그 신분의 사내들이 해 온 바 있었다. 티클러 부인이 리틀배스에서 편지를 쓴 그 ‘잘 바라는 이’였든, 아니면 제 벗을 그렇게 하도록 부추긴 것이었든, 그랜틀리 박사인 그에게 분명하게 보인 것은 프라우디 부인이 그토록 부지런히 퍼뜨리는 그 소문을 정말로 믿고 있다는 사실이었다. 바람이 그 생각의 아비였을지도 모른다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그러나 그 생각이 참으로 거기 있다는 것은, 그랜틀리 박사가 스스로 믿지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그의 아내는 덜 잘 믿는 편이어서 그를 얼마간 위로해 주었다. 그러나 그날 저녁 그는 편지 한 통을 받았으니, 프라우디 부인이 일으켜 놓은 의심을 크게 굳혀 주고 덤벨로 경에 대한 그 아내의 믿음까지 흔들어 놓는 것이었다. 그것은 한낱 아는 사람에게서 온 것이었고, 일이 예사롭게 돌아갔다면 그에게 편지를 쓸 만한 사람도 아니었다. 그리고 편지의 대부분은 예사로운 일들에 관한 것이어서, 그런 일로 편지를 쓰는 수고를 스스로 짊어질 생각을 그 신사가 했을 법하지도 않았다. 그러나 그 짧은 글 끝에 그는 이렇게 말했다—\n\n“물론 덤벨로가 파리로 떠난 것은 알고 계시겠지요. 돌아올 날이 언제로 정해졌다는 이야기는 저도 듣지 못했습니다.”\n\n“그러면 사실이군,” 부주교가 서재 탁자를 손으로 치며, 입가와 턱이 아주 하얘지면서 말했다.\n\n“그럴 수 없어요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 그러나 이제 그녀조차 떨고 있었다.\n\n“만약 그렇다면 그 사내의 상의 목덜미를 잡아 영국으로 끌고 와서 그 아비의 저택 계단 앞에서 망신을 주고 말겠소.”\n\n그리고 부주교가 그 위협을 입에 올릴 때 그는 영국 국교회의 성직자라는 다른 됨됨이보다 노한 영국인 아버지라는 됨됨이에 훨씬 더 잘 어울려 보였다. 부주교는 프라우디 부인에게 크게 밀렸으나, 사내들 사이에서 제 싸움을 어떻게 싸워야 하는지는 아는 사람이었다—더러는 제 성직의 옷을 너무 세세히 헤아리지 않고서.\n\n“덤벨로 경이 그런 일을 마음에 두었다면 지금쯤은 편지를 썼거나 벗 가운데 누구를 시켜 쓰게 했을 거예요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “빠져나가고 싶은 마음이야 있을 수 있겠지요. 하지만 그이는 제 이름을 너무 아끼는 사람이라 예를 갖추어 그리하려고 애쓰지 않을 리가 없어요.”\n\n이렇게 그 일이 의논되었고, 두 사람에게 다 그것이 하도 중대해 보였으므로 부주교는 곧바로 런던으로 가기로 마음을 정했다. 덤벨로 경이 프랑스로 갔다는 것은 그도 의심하지 않았다. 그러나 그 젊은 사내의 뜻을 아는 누군가를 런던에서 찾아낼 것이고, 그리하여 그가 언제 돌아오리라고 예상되는지도 틀림없이 들을 수 있을 것이었다. 참으로 걱정할 근거가 있다면 그 도망꾼을 대륙까지 뒤따라가겠지만, 확실히 알지 못하고서는 그리하지 않을 것이었다. 덤벨로 경이 지금 걸어 놓은 약속대로라면 그는 오는 팔월에 플럼스테드 에피스코피에 몸소 나타나, 그때 거기에서 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 아내로 맞아야 할 처지였다. 그러나 그가 이 점에서 제 말을 지킨다면 그 사이에 파리로 간다고 해서 그를 두고 시비할 권리는 누구에게도 없었다. 그런 처지에 놓인 대개의 신랑감이라면 의심할 것 없이 앞으로 제 아내가 될 사람에게 제 뜻을 알려 두었을 것이다. 그러나 덤벨로 경이 남들과 다르다면, 그 때문에 그에게 분개할 권리가 누구에게 있는가? 그는 다른 일에서도 남들과 달랐다. 그리고 무엇보다 하틀탑 후작의 맏아들이라는 점에서 남들과 달랐다. 티클러 같은 사람이야 주마다 제가 있는 곳을 알려도 다 좋겠지만, 후작의 맏아들에게는 그렇게 꼬박꼬박 하는 것이 거북한 일일 수도 있는 것이다! 그럼에도 부주교는 런던으로 올라가는 것이 그저 조심하는 길이라고 생각했다.\n\n“수전,” 부주교가 막 떠나려 하면서 아내에게 말했다—이때 두 사람 가운데 어느 쪽도 가장 좋은 심정에 있지는 않았다—“나는 그리젤다에게 조심할 말을 한마디 해 두는 것이 좋겠소.”\n\n“그만큼이나 그 일을 의심하시나요?” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 그러나 그녀조차 이 제안에 곧바로 아니라고 맞설 엄두를 내지 못했으니, 그만큼 그녀도 자기가 들은 것에 마음이 흔들려 있었던 것이다!\n\n“나는 그렇게 하겠소. 될 수 있는 대로 그 아이를 겁먹게 하지 않으면서. 만약 그 매가 떨어질 것이라면, 그렇게 해 두는 것이 매를 덜 아프게 해 줄 것이오.”\n\n“저는 죽을 것 같아요,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “하지만 그 아이는 견딜 수 있을 거예요.”\n\n이튿날 아침 그랜틀리 부인은 교묘하게 준비를 갖추어 남편이 저에게 남겨 놓고 간 그 일에 나섰다. 그 일에는 오래 걸렸으니, 그것을 하는 데 그녀가 매우 교묘했기 때문이다. 그러나 마침내 그녀의 입에서, 어쩌면—아주 희미하게나마—그들에게 아직도 어떤 낙심이 닥칠 수도 있다는 말이 새어 나왔다.\n\n“어머니, 혼례가 미루어진다는 말씀이세요?”\n\n“그리 될 것 같다는 말은 아니야. 하느님이 막아 주시겠지! 하지만 그럴 수도 있다는 거야. 이 이야기를 너에게 하는 것이 아주 잘못인지도 모르겠구나. 그러나 나는 네가 그것을 견딜 만한 분별이 있다는 것을 안다. 아버지는 런던으로 가셨으니 곧 소식을 들을 게다.”\n\n“그럼 어머니, 그 사람들에게 이름 수놓는 일은 더 하지 말라고 일러 두는 게 좋겠어요.”\n\n# 제46장. 레이디 러프턴의 청\n\n그날 집행관들은 끼니를 어김없이 받아먹었다 — 맥주도 받아 마셨다. 이런 형편에다 재산 목록을 만드는 따위의 일거리가 도무지 없었다는 것까지 겹치니, 나는 그것이 집행관에게는 이 땅의 낙원이라고 여긴다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 그들은 점잖은 말씨와, 남의 집에 들어와 있었던 것을 두고 늘어놓는 숱한 사과와 함께 걸어 나갔다. “참 미안하게 됐수,” 그들은 말했다. “신사다운 신사분을 괴롭혀 놓아서 말이우. 그렇지만 우리 장사가 이런 장사인데 어쩌겠수?” 그 말에 그들 가운데 하나가 “장사는 장사니까요” 하는 소리를 덧붙였다. 이 말을 나는 되받아 반박할 준비가 되어 있지 않다. 그러나 나는 모든 사람에게, 직업을 고를 때에는 걸음마다 사과를 해야 하는 직업은 피하라고 권하고 싶다 — 사과가 아니면 제 권리를 얼마간 거칠게 내세우기라도 해야 하는 직업 말이다. 젊은 남자 독자라면 저마다, 저는 집행리가 될 생각이 없다고 대답할지 모른다. 그렇지만 그와 한 갈래인 다른 생업들, 어쩌면 그런 이들 가운데 몇몇의 마음이 끌릴 만한 생업들이 있지 않은가?\n\n그들이 떠난 날 저녁에 마크는 레이디 러프턴에게서 짧은 편지를 받았는데, 이튿날 아침 일찍 들러 달라고 청하는 것이었다. 그래서 그는 아침을 먹고 나자 곧 프램리 코트로 건너갔다. 그의 마음이 그리 편치 않았으리라는 것은 짐작할 수 있는 일이다. 그러나 찬물에 처음 뛰어드는 것이 언제나 가장 견디기 어렵다던 아내의 말이 옳다는 것을 그는 느꼈다. 그 일을 두고 처음 꺼내는 말이 아무리 무섭게 차갑다 하더라도, 레이디 러프턴은 그의 수치를 두고두고 그의 면전에 들이댈 여인이 아니었다. 그는 그녀의 방에 들어서면서 여느 때의 낯빛과 몸가짐을 지키려고, 그리고 늘 하던 대로 거침없이 손을 내밀어 인사하려고 몹시 애를 썼다. 그러나 자기가 그러지 못했다는 것을 그는 알았다. 그리고 이렇게 말할 수도 있으리라. 착한 사람으로서 그 착함이 무너져 버린 사람은, 제 넘어짐의 수치를 부끄러운 낯빛 하나 없이 지고 갈 수는 없다고. 사람이 그럴 수 있게 된다면 그는 어느 모로도 착한 사람이 아니게 된다.\n\n“참 마음 아픈 일이었어요.” 첫인사를 끝내고 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n“예, 참으로 그렇습니다.” 그가 말했다. “딱한 패니에게는 몹시 슬픈 일이었습니다.”\n\n“글쎄요, 우리 모두에게는 저마다 슬픔의 시절이 얼마간 있어야 하는 법이지요. 그리고 우리 가운데 누구도 이보다 더한 것을 겪지 않는다면 어쩌면 그것이 복일지도 몰라요. 그 사람 자신은 하소연하지 않을 거예요, 틀림없이.”\n\n“그 사람이 하소연을 하겠습니까!”\n\n“그럼요, 하지 않을 거예요. 그러니 이제 내가 할 말은 이것뿐이에요, 로바츠 씨. 나는 로바츠 씨와 러프턴이 검은 양을 상대하는 일이라면 평생 쓸 만큼 넉넉히 겪었기를 바라요. 로바츠 씨의 지난 벗 사워비 씨는 검은 양이라고 나는 못 박아 두어야 하겠으니까요.”\n\n이렇게 마크의 이름을 제 아들의 이름과 나란히 놓는 것보다 더 다정하게 그 일을 건드릴 방법은 레이디 러프턴에게 있을 수 없었다. 그것은 꾸중의 쓴맛을 죄다 걷어내 버렸고, 그로써 그조차도 어렵지 않게 입을 열 수 있는 이야기가 되었다. 그러나 이제 그녀가 저에게 그토록 부드럽게 대하는 것을 보고 나니, 그는 그만큼 더 매섭게 저 자신을 몰아붙이지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n“저는 몹시 어리석었습니다.” 그가 말했다. “몹시 어리석고 몹시 잘못하고 몹시 사악했습니다.”\n\n“몹시 어리석었지요, 로바츠 씨 — 솔직하게, 그리고 이번 한 번으로 끝내려고 하는 말이에요. 그렇지만 내가 또한 믿는 바로는 그보다 더한 것은 아무것도 없어요. 이 일을 두고 우리가 딱 한마디만 나누어 두는 것이 서로에게 가장 좋겠다고 여겼어요. 그리고 이제는 이 일이 우리 사이에서 다시는 입에 오르지 않기를 권해요.”\n\n“하느님께서 마님께 복을 내리시기를 빕니다, 레이디 러프턴.” 그가 말했다. “마님 같은 벗을 가진 사람은 여태 아무도 없었으리라고 저는 생각합니다.”\n\n그녀는 이 만남 동안 몹시 조용했고 거의 가라앉아 있었으며, 여느 때 같은 활기로 말하지 않았다. 로바츠 씨와의 이 일이 그날 그녀가 끝내야 하는 유일한 일이 아니었고, 어쩌면 끝내기가 가장 어려운 일도 아니었기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 저에게 얹힌 칭찬을 듣고 그녀는 조금 기운을 냈다. 그것이 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 종류의 칭찬이었기 때문이다. 그녀는 자기가 좋은 벗이기를 정말로 바랐고, 어쩌면 그렇다고 스스로 흐뭇해하고 있었다.\n\n“그러면 오늘 저녁에 저녁을 먹으러 올라와서 내 우정을 흐뭇하게 해 줄 만큼은 마음을 써 주어야 해요. 물론 패니도 함께요. 무슨 핑계도 받아 주지 않겠어요. 이 일은 벌써 다 정해 놓았으니까요. 나는 그러기를 바라는 데에 남다른 까닭이 있어요.” 이 마지막의 거센 분부가 덧붙은 것은, 목사의 얼굴에 마다하는 낯빛이 떠오르는 것을 레이디 러프턴이 보았기 때문이었다. 딱한 레이디 러프턴! 그녀의 원수들은 — 그녀에게도 원수가 있었으니까 — 그녀를 두고 늘 이렇게 단언하곤 했다. 그녀의 좋은 마음이 스스로를 드러낼 줄 아는 방법이라고는 저녁 초대뿐이라고. 그렇지만 그 원수들에게 내가 물어보게 해 다오. 그것이 지금 이 세상에 있는 줄로 알려진 다른 어떤 방법에도 못하지 않게 좋은 방법이 아니겠느냐고. 이런 분부 아래에서 순종은 물론 어쩔 수 없는 것이었으므로, 그는 아내와 함께 저녁을 먹으러 건너오겠다고 약속했다. 그러고 나서 그가 물러가자 레이디 러프턴은 마차를 내오라고 일렀다.\n\n프램리에서 이런 일들이 벌어지는 동안에도 루시 로바츠는 여전히 호글스톡에 남아 크롤리 부인을 간병하고 있었다. 그녀를 프램리로 불러들일 일은 아무것도 생기지 않았다. 블레셋 사람들이 들이닥쳤다는 첫 소식을 전해 준 패니의 짧은 편지가 그들이 물러갔다는 것도 함께 알려 주었기 때문이고 — 구원이 어디에서 그들에게 이르렀는지도 함께 알려 주었기 때문이다. “그러니 그 일 때문에는 오지 말아요.” 그 편지는 이렇게 말했다. “그렇지만 그래도 될 수 있는 대로 빨리 와요. 루시가 없으니 온 집이 쓸쓸해요.”\n\n이 편지를 받은 이튿날 아침, 루시는 이제 여느 때처럼 낡은 안락의자 옆에 앉아 있었다. 얼마 전부터 그녀의 병자가 그 의자로 승진해 옮겨 앉게 되었던 것이다. 열병은 물러갔고 크롤리 부인은 천천히 기운을 되찾고 있었다 — 몹시 천천히였고, 너무 빨리 나으려고 애쓰다가는 병과 집안일을 못 하는 무력의 깊은 구렁으로 다시 곤두박질칠 수도 있다는 실버브리지 의사의 주의를 자주 들으면서였다.\n\n“내일이면 정말로 움직일 수 있겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다. “그러면, 루시, 더 이상 루시를 집에서 떼어 놓고 붙들어 둘 일이 없겠지요.”\n\n“나를 떼어 내려고 꽤 서두르시는 것 같아요. 크롤리 씨가 차에 든 크림을 두고 또 뭐라 하셨나 보군요.” 크롤리 씨는 한번, 날마다 몰래 먹을 것이 이 집으로 들어오고 있다고 굳게 믿는 바를 밝힌 적이 있었다. 제 잔에 우유가 아니라 크림이 들어 있는 것을 알아냈기 때문이었다. 그러나 크림은 그 전에도 여러 날 들어오고 있었으므로, 그것을 알아낸 그의 밝은 눈을 로바츠 양은 그리 대단하게 여기지 않았다.\n\n“아, 루시가 등을 돌리고 있을 때 그이가 루시를 어떻게 말하는지 루시는 모르지요.”\n\n“그러면 나를 어떻게 말하시나요? 다 말해 줄 용기는 없으실 텐데요.”\n\n“없어요. 그이처럼 보이는 사람의 입에서 나온 말이라면 루시는 어처구니없다고 여길 테니까요. 여자다움을 두고 시를 쓴다면 루시를 그 여주인공으로 삼겠다고 그이가 그래요.”\n\n“크림 단지를 손에 들고 있거나, 아니면 셔츠 깃에 단추를 달고 있는 여주인공이겠지요. 그렇지만 그이는 양고기 국물 일을 두고 나를 끝내 용서하지 않으셨어요. 대놓고 그 말 그대로, 내가 — 꾸며 대는 사람이라고 하셨어요. 그리고 그 일로 보자면, 크롤리 부인, 정말 내가 그랬지요.”\n\n“그이는 나에게 루시가 천사라고 했어요.”\n\n“원 세상에!”\n\n“돌보는 천사라고요. 그리고 정말 그랬어요. 루시를 벗으로 얻었으니, 내가 앓았던 것이 기쁘다고 할 마음까지도 거의 들어요.”\n\n“그렇지만 열병 없이도 그 복은 얻을 수 있었을 텐데요.”\n\n“아니에요, 얻지 못했을 거예요. 혼인해 살아온 동안 나는 벗을 하나도 사귀지 못했어요. 내 병이 루시를 내게 데려다주기까지는요. 그 일이 없었다면 루시를 참으로 알게 되는 일도 결코 없었을 거예요. 내가 어떻게 누구를 알게 되겠어요?”\n\n“이제는 그러시겠지요, 크롤리 부인. 그렇지 않아요? 그러겠다고 약속해 주세요. 몸이 나으면 프램리로 우리를 찾아오시겠지요? 벌써 약속하셨잖아요.”\n\n“내가 너무 힘이 없어서 마다할 수도 없을 때 루시가 그러게 만들었지요.”\n\n“그 약속을 지키게도 내가 만들 거예요. 그이도 오고 싶으면 오시라고 하세요. 그렇지만 부인은 그이가 좋다 하든 싫다 하든 오셔야 해요. 그리고 낡은 옷 이야기는 한마디도 듣지 않겠어요. 낡은 옷은 호글스톡에서만큼 프램리에서도 잘 입을 수 있어요.”\n\n이 모든 것으로 보아 크롤리 부인과 루시 로바츠가 이 간병의 기간 동안 몹시 가까워졌음이 드러날 것이다. 같은 병실에 몇 주씩 함께 갇혀 있게 되면 두 여인은 언제나 그렇게 되는 법이고, 적어도 그렇게 되어야 하는 법이다.\n\n두 사람 사이에 이야기가 아직 이어지고 있을 때 길 위에서 바퀴 소리가 들려왔다. 집 앞으로 지나가는 것은 큰길이 아니었고, 어떤 종류의 마차든 그곳에는 자주 다니지 않았다.\n\n“패니예요, 틀림없이.” 루시가 의자에서 일어서며 말했다.\n\n“말이 두 마리예요.” 병에는 언제나 딸려 오는 그 정확한 귀로 소리를 가려내며 크롤리 부인이 말했다. “그리고 조랑말 마차 소리도 아니에요.”\n\n“제대로 된 마차예요.” 창가에서 루시가 말했다. “그리고 여기에 서고 있어요. 프램리 코트에서 온 누군가예요. 하인을 내가 알아요.”\n\n그렇게 말하면서 그녀의 이마에 홍조가 올랐다. 러프턴 경이 아닐까, 그녀는 속으로 생각했다 — 러프턴 경이 살진 하인을 딸린 닫힌 마차를 타고 시골을 돌아다니지는 않는다는 것을 그 순간에는 잊고 있었다. 크롤리 부인과 그토록 가까워졌으면서도 그녀는 제 연애 일을 두고서는 그 새 벗에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n마차가 서고 하인이 내려왔으나 안에서 그에게 말을 거는 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n“프램리에서 무엇을 가져왔겠지요.” 크림 따위를 머리에 두고 루시가 말했다. 그녀가 거기 머무는 동안 크림 따위가 프램리 코트에서 한두 번 온 적이 있었기 때문이다. “그리고 마차가 아마 마침 이 길로 오게 되었겠지요.”\n\n그러나 이 알 수 없는 일은 곧 얼마쯤 밝혀졌다. 아니, 어쩌면 다른 쪽으로 더 알 수 없게 되었다. 열병이 처음 터졌을 때 겁먹은 어미가 데려가 버렸던 팔이 붉은 그 어린 하녀가 이제 제자리로 돌아와 있었는데, 그 아이가 바로 이 순간 외경에 사로잡힌 얼굴로 방에 들어와, 로바츠 양이 마차에 있는 큰 마님께 당장 가야 한다고 알렸다.\n\n“레이디 러프턴이신가 보군요.” 크롤리 부인이 말했다.\n\n루시의 심장은 어찌나 완전히 목구멍까지 치밀어 올랐던지 그 순간 그녀에게는 어떤 말도 할 수 없는 일이었다. 무엇 때문에 레이디 러프턴이 이 호글스톡까지 왔을까, 그리고 무엇 때문에 마차 안에서 저를, 이 루시 로바츠를 보고자 할까? 두 사람 사이의 일은 다 정해지지 않았던가? 그런데도 — ! 루시는 저에게 허락된 그 한순간의 생각으로는 그런 만남의 끝이 어찌 될 법한지 가려낼 수 없었다. 그녀의 가장 큰 마음은 그것을 지금 이 순간만이라도 미루고 싶다는 것이었다. 그러나 팔이 붉은 그 어린 하녀는 그것을 허락하지 않았다.\n\n“당장 오셔야 한답니다요.” 하녀가 말했다.\n\n그러자 루시는 한마디도 하지 않은 채 일어나 방을 나갔다. 그녀는 아래층으로, 좁은 통로를 따라, 그리고 작은 뜰을 지나 밖으로 걸어 나갔다. 걸음은 단단했으나 어디로 가는지, 무엇 하러 가는지는 거의 알지 못했다. 마음의 침착도 자기를 다스리는 힘도 다 그녀를 버리고 떠나 버렸다. 마땅히 해야 할 대로 말할 수 없다는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 지금 하고 있는 이 몸가짐을 뒤에 뉘우치게 될 것을 그녀는 느꼈으나, 그러면서도 저를 어쩔 수가 없었다. 무엇 때문에 레이디 러프턴이 여기까지 저를 찾아왔을까? 그녀는 걸어 나갔고, 큰 하인이 마차 문을 열어 잡고 서 있었다. 그녀는 거의 저도 모르게 올라섰다. 그리고 어떻게 거기 이르렀는지도 모르는 채, 레이디 러프턴 옆에 앉아 있는 제 몸을 발견했다.\n\n사실을 말하자면 마님 또한 이번 작전을 어떻게 끝까지 밀고 나가야 할지 몰라 조금 막막해하고 있었다. 그러나 먼저 말을 꺼내는 소임은 분명히 그녀에게 있었으므로, 그녀는 루시의 손을 잡고 입을 열었다.\n\n“로바츠 양,” 그녀가 말했다. “내 아들이 집에 왔어요. 그것을 알고 있는지 모르겠군요.”\n\n그녀는 낮고 부드러운 목소리로, 여느 자기 같지 않게 말했으나, 루시는 너무 어지러워서 그것을 알아차리지 못했다.\n\n“알지 못했습니다.” 루시가 말했다.\n\n그러나 그녀는 패니의 편지로 그렇게 전해 들어 알고 있었다. 다만 그 모든 것이 머리에서 빠져나가 버렸던 것이다.\n\n“그래요, 돌아왔어요. 노르웨이에 가 있었지요 — 낚시하러요.”\n\n“예.” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“얼마 전에 프램리 코트 위층 그 작은 방으로 나를 찾아왔을 때 있었던 일을 다 기억하고 있을 거예요.”\n\n그 말에 루시는, 온몸의 신경이 떨리고 팔다리가 하나하나 눈에 보이게 떨리고 있다고 잘못 생각하면서, 기억한다고 조그맣게 대답했다. 그때는 그토록 담대했는데 지금은 어찌하여 이토록 못난 겁쟁이인가?\n\n“그런데 얘야, 그때 내가 한 말은 다 그것이 가장 좋은 길이라고 여기고 한 말이었어요. 내가 다른 어느 누구보다 내 아들을 더 사랑한다고 해서, 적어도 얘야, 그것 때문에 나를 노여워하지는 않겠지요.”\n\n“아, 아닙니다.” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“그 아이는 아들 가운데 가장 좋은 아들이고 사내 가운데 가장 좋은 사내예요. 그리고 남편 가운데서도 가장 좋은 남편이 될 것이라고 나는 믿어요.”\n\n그렇게 말할 때 레이디 러프턴의 눈에 눈물이 가득했다는 것을 루시는 알아차렸다. 보아서가 아니라 본능으로였다. 루시 자신은 눈이 아주 멀어 버려서 얼굴을 들거나 고개를 돌릴 엄두를 내지 못했다. 무슨 소리를 낸다는 것은 아예 있을 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n“그리고 이제 나는, 루시, 그 아이의 아내가 되어 달라고 청하러 여기에 왔어요.”\n\n그 말을 들었다는 것만은 그녀도 아주 분명했다. 그 말은 뚜렷하게 그녀의 귀에 들어와 그 마땅한 뜻을 그녀의 머리에 남겨 놓았다. 그런데도 그녀는 움직일 수도, 그 말을 알아들었다는 어떤 표시를 낼 수도 없었다. 그런 처사를 이때다 하고 이용하여, 그토록 큰 자기희생으로 내놓아진 청을 받아들이는 것은 저에게 후하지 못한 일이 될 것처럼 보였다. 첫 순간에 그녀에게는 제 행복은 말할 것도 없고 그의 행복까지도 생각할 겨를이 없었다. 그녀는 오직 저에게 베풀어진 양보가 얼마나 큰 것인가만을 생각했다. 레이디 러프턴을 제 운명의 심판자로 세울 때 그녀는 제 사랑의 문제가 저에게 불리하게 판결된 것으로 여겼다. 레이디 러프턴이 저를 업신여기고 있는 동안에 그 며느리라는 자리에 있는 것을 견딜 수 없다는 것을 그녀는 알아 버렸고, 그래서 판을 접었다. 그녀는 판을 접으면서 자기를 희생했고, 희생이라 할 수 있는 한에서는 그까지도 희생했다. 이 일에서 제 말을 지키겠다고 그녀는 굳게 마음먹고 있었다. 그러나 그녀 루시가 마님에게 걸어 놓은 조건에 레이디 러프턴이 응하는 일이 있을 수 있다고는 스스로 한 번도 생각해 보지 않았다. 그런데도 사실이 그러하였으니, 그녀가 그토록 뚜렷하게 들은 대로였다. “그리고 이제 나는, 루시, 그 아이의 아내가 되어 달라고 청하러 여기에 왔어요.”\n\n두 사람이 얼마나 오래 말없이 함께 앉아 있었는지 나는 말할 수 없다. 분으로 세자면 아마 여러 분에 이르지도 않았을 것이나, 두 사람에게는 저마다 그 길이가 꽤 되는 것처럼 느껴졌다. 레이디 러프턴은 말하는 동안에 어느새 루시의 손을 잡아 두었고, 그것을 여전히 잡은 채 앉아 루시의 얼굴을 들여다보려고 애썼다 — 그러나 얼굴이 어찌나 많이 돌아가 있었던지 그녀는 그것을 거의 볼 수 없었다. 사실 레이디 러프턴의 눈도 온전히 말라 있지는 않았다. 그녀의 물음에는 아무 대답도 오지 않았고, 그래서 얼마쯤 지나자 그녀가 다시 말을 해야 했다.\n\n“내가 그 아이에게 돌아가서, 무슨 다른 반대가 있다고 — 엄한 노모 말고 또 다른 무엇이, 어쩌면 그렇게 쉽게 넘을 수 없는 어떤 걸림돌이 있다고 말해야 하나요, 루시?”\n\n“아닙니다.” 루시가 말했다. 그것이 그 순간 그녀가 할 수 있는 말의 전부였다.\n\n“그러면 그 아이에게 무엇이라고 전할까요? 예라고 — 그저 예라고 말할까요?”\n\n“그저 예라고요.” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“그러면 하나뿐인 아들이 너무 귀해서 첫마디에 내어 줄 수 없다고 여겼던 그 엄한 노모에게는 — 아무 말도 해 줄 것이 없나요?”\n\n“아, 레이디 러프턴!”\n\n“용서한다는 말 한마디도, 사랑한다는 표시 하나도 없나요? 그 노모는 언제까지나 엄하고 뒤틀리고 성가시고 마음에 들지 않는 사람으로 여겨져야 하나요?”\n\n루시는 천천히 고개를 돌려 옆에 앉은 이의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 사랑을 말할 목소리는 아직 없었으나 눈만은 사랑으로 가득 채울 수 있었고, 그렇게 하여 앞으로 어머니가 될 이에게 필요한 모든 약속을 다 할 수 있었다.\n\n“루시, 내 귀한 루시, 이제 루시는 나에게 몹시 귀한 사람이어야 해요.” 그러고 나서 두 사람은 서로의 품에 안겨 서로에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n레이디 러프턴은 이제 마부에게 길을 따라 얼마쯤 오르내리며 마차를 몰라고 일렀다. 그동안에 루시와 해 두어야 할 이야기를 끝내려는 것이었다. 그녀는 처음에는 그날 저녁에 루시를 프램리로 데려가고 싶어 하며, 이튿날 아침에 다시 크롤리 부인에게 보내 주겠다고 약속했다 — “무언가 두고 쓸 조처가 마련될 때까지”라고 했는데, 그 말로 레이디 러프턴이 뜻한 것은 앞날의 며느리 자리에 제대로 된 간병인을 대신 들여놓겠다는 것이었다. 이제 그녀의 눈에 루시 로바츠는, 크롤리 부인의 병상 옆에 앉아 시중드는 것이 걸맞지 않게 되는 그런 자격을 입고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 루시는 그날 저녁에 프램리로 돌아가려 하지 않았다. 아니, 이튿날 아침에도 그러려 하지 않았다. 패니가 여기로 저를 찾아와 준다면 몹시 기쁘겠고, 그러고 나서 집에 돌아가는 일을 정하겠다고 했다.\n\n“그렇지만 루시, 루도빅에게는 내가 무엇이라고 해야 하나요? 그 아이가 여기로 보러 온다면 어쩌면 거북하겠지요.”\n\n“아, 예, 레이디 러프턴. 부디 그러지 마시라고 그분께 일러 주십시오.”\n\n“그리고 내가 그 아이에게 전할 말이 그것뿐인가요?”\n\n“그분께 — 그분께 — 그분은 마님께서 아무것도 전해 주시기를 바라지 않으실 겁니다. 다만 저는 하루만 조용히 있고 싶습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”\n\n“그래요, 얘야, 조용히 있게 해 주지요. 그러면 모레예요. — 그보다 더 오래는 내어 줄 수 없다는 것을 알아 두어요. 이제는 집에 있는 것이 옳은 일이 될 테니까요. 그토록 가까이 있는데 눈으로 볼 수도 없게 된다면 그 아이는 몹시 못 견딜 일로 여길 거예요. 그리고 루시를 보고 싶어 할 사람이 또 하나 있을 거예요. 나는 루시를 아주 가까이 두고 싶어요. 나를 사랑하도록 가르칠 수 없다면 나는 비참해질 테니까요, 루시.” 그 말에 루시는 여러 가지 약속을 할 만큼의 목소리는 찾아냈다.\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 작은 쪽문 앞에서 마차에서 내려졌고, 레이디 러프턴은 프램리로 실려 돌아갔다. 그 하인이 로바츠 양을 위해 문을 잡아 줄 때 자기가 앞날의 안주인을 모시고 있다는 것을 알고 있었을까? 나는 알고 있었으리라고 짐작한다. 이런 부류의 사람들은 언제나 모든 것을 알고 있는 법이고, 마차 발판을 내려놓을 때의 그 유별난 공손함은 눈에 아주 잘 띄었기 때문이다.\n\n루시는 위층으로 돌아가면서 거의 제정신이 아닌 것처럼 느꼈다. 무엇을 해야 할지, 어떤 낯빛을 지어야 할지, 어떤 말로 말을 해야 할지 알지 못했다. 곧바로 크롤리 부인의 방으로 가야 하는 것이 마땅했으나, 그러면서도 혼자 있기를 그녀는 몹시 바랐다. 제 생각을 감추는 것도 드러내는 것도 도무지 할 수 없다는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그리고 지금 이 순간에는 제 행복을 두고 누구와 이야기하기도 바라지 않았다 — 지금 이 순간에 패니 로바츠와 이야기할 수는 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그녀는 지체하지 않고 크롤리 부인의 방으로 들어가서, 자기가 어지러워하고 있다는 것을 아는 사람들에게 흔히 있는 그 조금 다급하게 빨리 말하는 투로, 너무 오래 나가 있었던 것 같아 걱정이라고 말했다.\n\n“그래, 레이디 러프턴이었나요?”\n\n“예, 레이디 러프턴이었어요.”\n\n“어머, 루시. 루시하고 마님이 그렇게 가까운 사이인 줄은 몰랐어요.”\n\n“남달리 하고 싶으신 말씀이 있었어요.” 루시가 그 물음을 피하고 크롤리 부인의 눈길도 피하면서 말했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 여느 때 앉는 의자에 앉았다.\n\n“언짢은 일은 아니었기를 바라요.”\n\n“예, 언짢은 일은 조금도 아니었어요. 그런 종류의 일이 아니었어요. — 아, 크롤리 부인, 다음에 언제 말씀드릴게요. 그렇지만 지금은 부디 묻지 말아 주세요.” 그러고 나서 그녀는 일어나 달아났다. 혼자 있어야 하는 것이 도무지 어쩔 수 없는 일이었기 때문이다.\n\n제 방 — 아이들이 여느 때 자는 방 — 에 이르자 그녀는 마음을 가라앉히려고 크게 애를 썼으나 온전히 되지는 않았다. 그녀는 종이와 압지첩을 꺼냈다. 스스로에게 말한 대로 패니에게 편지를 쓸 셈이었는데, 다 쓰고 나면 그 편지를 찢어 버리게 될 것을 알면서였다. 그러나 그녀는 한 낱말도 이루어 낼 수 없었다. 손이 떨리고 눈이 흐리고 생각을 한곳에 붙들어 둘 수가 없었다. 그녀는 다만 앉아서 생각하고 궁리하고 바랄 수밖에 없었다. 이따금 눈에서 눈물을 닦아 내며, 지금의 이 마음자리가 어찌하여 저에게 이토록 아픈지를 스스로에게 물으면서였다. 지난 두세 달 동안 그녀는 러프턴 경에 대한 두려움을 조금도 느끼지 않았고, 늘 그와 대등한 자리에서 저를 지켜 왔으며, 그가 목사관에서 저에게 제 마음을 밝혔을 때에도 눈에 띄게 그럴 수 있었다. 그러나 이제 그녀는 그를 보게 될 첫 순간을 두고 무엇이라 이름 붙일 수 없는 두려움으로 앞을 내다보고 있었다.\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 프램리 코트에서 지낸 어느 저녁을 떠올리고, 그것을 돌아보는 데에 얼마간의 즐거움이 있다는 것을 스스로 인정했다. 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 거기 있었고, 그 아가씨와 러프턴 경 사이에 구애가 순순히 이루어지도록 두 집안의 온갖 헌법적 권능이 나서서 일하고 있었다. 루시는 그것을 다 보고 다 알아들었으나 제가 알아듣고 있다는 것은 알지 못했으며, 그것을 바라보느라 얼마간 괴로움을 겪었다. 그녀는 저를 한쪽으로 떼어 놓고 하소연하지 않았다 — 무언가 못한 데가 있다는 것을 아프게 의식하면서도, 그러면서 동시에 저 나름으로는 자기가 나은 쪽이라고 스스로에게 자랑하다시피 하면서였다. 그러고 나서 그가 그녀의 의자 뒤로 와서 그녀에게 속삭이며, 다정하고 마음씨 좋은 첫마디를 그녀에게 건넸고, 그녀는 그의 벗이 되겠다고 마음을 정했다 — 그의 벗이 되겠다고, 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 그의 아내가 될지도 모르는데도. 그 마음가짐이 얼마만 한 값이 나가는 것이었는지는 오래지 않아 그녀에게 드러났다. 그녀는 오래지 않아 그 우정의 결말을 스스로에게 고백했고, 제 벌을 용기로 견디겠다고 작정했다. 그런데 이제 —\n\n그녀는 그렇게 한 시간쯤 앉아 있었고, 그대로 하루를 다 앉아 지내고 싶었다. 그러나 그럴 수는 없는 일이었으므로 그녀는 일어나 얼굴과 눈을 씻고 크롤리 부인의 방으로 돌아갔다. 거기서 그녀는 크롤리 씨까지 있는 것을 보고 크게 기뻐했다. 그가 있는 동안에는 저에게 아무 물음도 던져지지 않을 것을 알았기 때문이다. 그는 언제나 그녀에게 몹시 정중했고, 옛 투의 다듬어진 예의로 그녀를 대했다 — 먹을 것을 들여오는 일을 두고 그녀가 거짓을 말했다고 소임의 의식에 밀려 나무라지 않을 수 없었던 그 한 번을 빼고는 그러했다. 그러나 그는 제 아내가 그리한 것처럼 그녀와 아주 터놓고 지내는 사이가 된 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그리고 그가 그러지 않았다는 것이 지금 그녀에게는 다행이었으니, 레이디 러프턴을 두고 이야기할 수는 없었을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n저녁에 세 사람이 함께 있게 되었을 때, 그녀는 이튿날 로바츠 부인이 건너올 것 같다는 말은 겨우 해낼 수 있었다.\n\n“우리는 가장 깊은 애석함으로 로바츠 양을 떠나보내야 하겠습니다.” 크롤리 씨가 말했다. “그러나 무슨 일이 있어도 더 오래 붙들어 둘 수는 없습니다. 이제 내 아내는 로바츠 양 없이도 지낼 수 있습니다. 로바츠 양이 우리에게 와 주지 않았더라면 아내가 어찌 되었을지는 나로서는 헤아릴 길이 없습니다.”\n\n“제가 간다고는 말하지 않았어요.” 루시가 말했다.\n\n“그렇지만 갈 거예요.” 크롤리 부인이 말했다. “그래요, 루시, 갈 거예요. 이제는 돌아가는 것이 옳다는 것을 나도 알아요. 아니, 우리는 더 오래 붙들어 두지 않겠어요. 그리고 딱하고 귀한 아이들도 — 그 아이들도 돌아와도 되겠지요. 로바츠 부인이 우리에게 해 주신 일을 내가 어떻게 고마워해야 할까요?”\n\n이튿날 로바츠 부인이 오면 루시가 함께 돌아가기로 정해졌다. 그러고 나서 그 긴 밤을 지켜 앉아 있는 동안에 — 이 마지막 밤에는 루시가 새벽이 밝고 나서도 한참까지 새 벗의 침상 옆을 떠나려 하지 않았다 — 그녀는 제 앞날의 운명이 어찌 되기로 되어 있는지를 크롤리 부인에게 이야기했다. 저 자신에게는 제 새 처지에 이상할 것이 아무것도 없어 보였다. 그러나 크롤리 부인에게는, 저처럼 — 저처럼 가난한 저에게 알 속의 귀족 부인이 병상 옆에 앉아 마실 잔을 건네주고 편히 누우라고 베개를 골라 주고 있다는 것이 놀라운 일이었다. 이상한 일이었고, 그녀는 여태 지녀 온 그 다정한 터놓음을 지켜 내기가 어려웠다. 루시는 그 순간에 그것을 느꼈다.\n\n“아무것도 달라져서는 안 돼요.” 그녀가 다급하게 말했다. “부인과 나 사이에서는 조금도 달라져서는 안 돼요. 아무것도 달라지지 않겠다고 나에게 약속해 주세요.”\n\n그 약속은 물론 받아 냈다. 그러나 그런 약속이 지켜지는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 아주 일찍 — 첫잠에 들어 있는 그녀를 깨울 만큼 일찍 — 목사관에서 그녀에게 편지가 왔다. 로바츠 부인이 레이디 러프턴의 저녁 자리에서 집으로 돌아온 뒤에 쓴 것이었다.\n\n편지는 이렇게 말했다. —\n\n어떻게 축하를 해야 하고, 어떻게 하면 기쁨을 함께 하겠다는 말을 넉넉할 만큼 열심히 할 수 있을까요? 나는 정말로 기쁨을 함께 하겠어요. 그리고 아주 행복해요. 지금 쓰는 것은 무엇보다 이 말을 하려는 것이에요. 내일 열두 시쯤 루시에게 가겠고, 그리고 나는 반드시 루시를 데리고 와야 해요. 내가 그러지 않으면, 도무지 그만큼 믿을 만하지 못한 다른 누군가가 꼭 그러겠다고 나설 테니까요.\n\n그러나 이것이 이렇게 편지의 으뜸된 부분이라고 밝혀져 있었고 내용으로는 그러했을지 모르나, 차지한 자리로 보아서는 결코 그렇지 않았다. 편지는 몹시 길었으니, 로바츠 부인이 자정이 지날 때까지 앉아 그것을 쓰고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그분에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠어요 [두 장이 그분의 이름으로 채워진 뒤에 그녀는 이렇게 이어 갔다]. 그렇지만 그분이 얼마나 아름답게 처신하셨는지는 말해야 해요. 그분이 사랑스러운 여인이라는 것을 루시도 인정하겠지요, 그렇지 않아요?\n\n루시는 어제의 그 방문 뒤로 이미 여러 번 그것을 인정했고, 그때부터 지금까지 줄곧 그래 온 대로, 저는 그것을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없다고 스스로에게 단언해 왔다.\n\n그분은 우리가 저녁 전에 응접실로 들어섰을 때 우리를 놀라게 하셨어요. 다른 무엇보다 먼저, 호글스톡으로 루시를 만나러 다녀오셨다고 우리에게 말씀하신 거예요. 러프턴 경은 물론 그 비밀을 지키고 있지 못하고 그것을 그대로 터뜨려 버리셨어요. 러프턴 경이 그것을 어떻게 다 이야기하셨는지는 지금 여기 적을 수 없지만, 있을 수 있는 가장 좋은 투로 하셨다는 것은 루시도 믿을 거예요. 러프턴 경은 내 손을 잡고 여섯 번은 힘주어 쥐셨고, 나는 또 무슨 다른 일까지 하려나 했지요. 그렇지만 하지는 않으셨으니 시샘할 것 없어요. 그리고 그분은 마크에게도 어찌나 다정하셨는지, 루시를 칭찬하는 말씀을 그렇게나 하시고, 루시 아버님께는 온갖 좋은 말씀을 다 바치셨어요. 그렇지만 러프턴 경은 루시를 데려오지 않았다고 그분을 대단히 꾸짖으셨어요. 그것은 청승맞고 터무니없는 짓이라고 하셨어요. 그러나 러프턴 경이 그분이 하신 일을 두고 그분을 얼마나 사랑하고 있는지 나는 볼 수 있었어요. 그리고 그분도 그것을 볼 수 있었어요. 나는 그분의 투를 아니까요. 그분이 러프턴 경을 몹시 흐뭇하게 여기셨다는 것을 나는 알아요. 그분은 저녁 내내 러프턴 경에게서 눈을 떼지 못하셨고, 러프턴 경이 그렇게 좋아 보이는 것은 나도 정말 한 번도 본 적이 없어요.\n\n그러고 나서 러프턴 경과 마크가 식당에 남아 있는 동안 — 무섭게 오래 남아 있었어요 — 그분은 나를 집 안으로 끌고 다니며 루시의 방들을 보여 주고, 루시가 어떻게 그곳의 안주인이 될 것인지를 풀어 놓으셨어요. 그분은 그 모든 것을 나무랄 데 없이 다 갖추어 놓으셨고, 여러 해 동안 그것을 생각해 오셨던 것이 틀림없어요. 지금 그분의 가장 큰 두려움은 루시와 러프턴 경이 러프턴으로 가서 거기서 살까 하는 것이에요. 루시에게 그분에게든 나에게든 고마운 마음이 조금이라도 있다면, 러프턴 경이 그러도록 두지 말아요. 러프턴에는 아직 돌 두 개가 서로 얹혀 있는 곳도 없다고 말해서 나는 그분을 달래 드렸어요. 그리고 정말 그런 형편인 줄로 나는 믿어요. 게다가 그곳이 세상에서 가장 보기 흉한 곳이라고 모두들 말하잖아요. 그분은 눈에 눈물을 담고 말을 이어, 루시가 프램리에 남아 있는 것으로 만족한다면 어떤 일에도 결코 끼어들지 않겠다고 단언하셨어요. 그분은 여태 살았던 어느 여인보다도 가장 좋은 여인이라고 나는 정말 그렇게 생각해요.\n\n내가 이 편지에서 옮긴 것은 그 가운데 작은 몫밖에 되지 않으나, 우리가 알아야 할 것은 다 여기에 들어 있다. 그날 열두 시 정각에 조랑말 퍽이 나타났으니, 로바츠 부인과 그레이스 크롤리를 뒤에 태우고서였다. 그레이스는 집안에서 얼마쯤 쓸모가 있으리라 하여 다시 데려온 것이었다. 그 순간에는 마음속 이야기라 할 것은 아무것도, 정다운 말이라 할 것도 아주 조금밖에 할 수 없었다. 크롤리 씨가 거기서 로바츠 양에게 작별을 하려고 기다리고 있었기 때문이고, 그는 제집에 온 손의 앞날이 어찌 되기로 되어 있는지를 아직 전해 듣지 못했기 때문이다. 그래서 두 사람은 서로 손을 힘주어 잡고 껴안을 수밖에 없었는데, 루시에게는 그것이 오히려 마음이 놓이는 일이었다. 올케에게조차도 이 일을 두고 어떻게 터놓고 말해야 할지 그녀는 아직 거의 알지 못했기 때문이다.\n\n“전능하신 하느님께서 로바츠 양께 복을 내리시기를 빕니다.” 크롤리 씨가 자기의 침침한 거실에 서서, 그녀를 조랑말 마차까지 이끌어 낼 준비를 하고서 말했다. “로바츠 양은 이 집에 햇빛을 들여 주셨습니다. 햇빛이라고는 없던 병의 시절에까지 그리하셨습니다. 그러니 하느님께서 로바츠 양께 복을 내리실 것입니다. 로바츠 양은 착한 사마리아 사람이 되어, 괴로움을 당한 이의 상처를 싸매고 기름과 향유를 부어 넣어 주셨습니다. 내 아이들의 어머니에게 로바츠 양은 목숨을 주셨고, 나에게는 빛과 위로와 좋은 말씀을 가져다주셨으니, 여태 흐뭇해진 적이 없던 내 마음속을 흐뭇하게 만들어 주셨습니다. 이 모든 것이 사랑에서 나온 것이니, 사랑은 자랑하지 아니하며 뽐내지 아니합니다. 믿음과 소망도 크고 아름답습니다. 그러나 사랑은 그 모두를 넘어섭니다.” 그리고 그렇게 말하고 나서 그는 그녀를 이끌어 내는 대신에 저리로 가서 몸을 숨겼다.\n\n패니가 퍽을 몰아 프램리로 돌아가는 동안 퍽이 어떻게 처신했는지, 그리고 마차에 앉은 그 두 여인이 어떻게 처신했는지 — 그것을 두고서는 아마 더 말할 것이 없을 것이다.\n\n# 제47장. 네메시스\n\n그러나 이 모든 기쁜 소식에도 불구하고, 아아, 이것만은 기억해 두어야 한다. 공정하되 라다만투스처럼 냉엄한 그 여신 포에나—우리 요즘 사람들은 보통 그를 「벌」이라 부르고, 그의 여신다움을 두고 말하려 할 때에는 「네메시스」라 부른다—는, 제 발이 때때로 절름거린다 해도, 또 악한 사람이 어쩌다 앞질러 달아난다 해도, 그 악한 사람을 붙잡는 데 좀처럼 실패하지 않는다는 것이다. 이번 경우에 그 악한 사람은 우리의 불운한 벗 마크 로바츠였다. 알면서도 역청을 만졌고, 개서럼 성에 갔고, 발 빠른 암말들을 몰아 이 고장을 가로질러 코볼즈 애시스까지 달렸고, 그러고서 아주 경솔하게 토저네 사람들 가운데로 떨어졌으니 악했다. 그리고 네메시스가 쓴 연장은 《주피터》의 톰 타워스 씨였으니, 요즘 우리 시절에 그 여신의 손에 들린 채찍으로 그보다 더 목숨을 앗을 만한 것은 없다.\n\n그러나 우선 먼저, 레이디 러프턴과 로바츠 씨 사이에 있었던 짧은 이야기 하나를 말해 두어야겠다. 다만 그 내용을 늘어놓지는 않겠다. 그 신사는 그 금전 거래에 관하여 마님께 몇 마디를 더 아뢰는 것이 옳다고 여겼다. 자기가 그 참사회원 자리를 사워비 씨의 손에서 받았다는 느낌을 지울 수 없다고, 그리고 그런 사정 아래에서 그동안 벌어진 모든 일을 생각한다면 그 자리를 쥐고 있는 동안에는 마음이 편할 수 없다고 그는 말했다. 자기가 이제 하려는 일이 러프턴 경과의 마지막 정산을 상당히 늦추게 될 것을 자기도 알고 있으나, 러프턴이 그것을 용서해 주고 자기가 하려는 일이 옳다는 데에 함께 뜻을 두어 주기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n이 이야기를 처음 들었을 때 레이디 러프턴은 그와 뜻이 온전히 같지 않았다. 이제 러프턴 경이 그 목사의 누이와 혼인하기로 되었으니, 그 목사가 교회의 고위 성직자인 것이 좋을 수도 있었다. 또 제 아들과 그토록 가까이 얽힌 사람이 돈 문제에서 편안한 것도 좋을 수 있었다. 게다가 앞날에는, 귀족의 처남에게 더 높은 성직의 영예가 올 어떤 먼 가능성도 어렴풋이 비쳤다. 그리고 사닥다리의 맨 윗단은 사람이 이미 한두 단을 올라서 있을 때에 언제나 더 쉽게 닿는 법이다. 그러나 그럼에도, 이 일이 그녀에게 온전히 설명되어 그 참사회원 자리가 주어진 사정을 똑똑히 보게 되었을 때, 그녀는 그 자리를 내놓는 편이 낫다는 데에 정말로 뜻을 같이했다.\n\n그리고 이 결론이 네메시스의 채찍이 떨어지기 전에 이르러 있었던 것은 두 사람 모두에게—프램리에 있는 그들 모두에게—다행한 일이었다. 물론 네메시스는 제 채찍이 그 사임을 이루어 냈다고 언명했다. 그러나 그것이 거짓 자랑이라는 것은 널리 이해되어 있었으니, 톰 타워스가 그 무서운 채찍끈을 제 머리 위로 휘둘러 돌리기 전에 이미 그 참사회원 자리가 성당 참사회에, 다시 말하면 정부의 손에 돌려져 있었다는 것을 바체스터의 성직자라면 모두 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 그 휘둘러 돌린 방식은 다음과 같았다.—\n\n잉글랜드 국교회가 지금 이 순간 이 나라의 여러 종파 가운데에서 자기가 그토록 큰 소리로 주장하는 그 우위를 지켜 나가는 것은 [《주피터》의 기사는 이렇게 말했다] 어지간히 어려운 일이다. 그리고 그 우위에 대한 어떤 일반의 인정이 아직도 통하도록 허락되어 있는 것은, 아마도 국교회 자신의 어떤 본디의 값어치 때문이라기보다는 오히려 그 지위에 대한 낡은 방식의, 오래된 애착 때문일 것이다. 그러나 만일 이 교회의 추천권을 쥔 이들과 성직에 있는 이들이 점잖은 행실의 일반 규범을 모두 무릅쓰고 무시할 만큼 대담하다면, 우리는 이 의협의 감정도 무너지고 말 것이라고 미리 말할 수 있으리라고 본다. 우리는 때때로 그런 경솔함의 사례들을 듣게 되고, 국교회를 가장 크게 공경한다고 여겨지는 이들의 어리석음에 놀라게 된다.\n\n운 좋은 성직자들이 올라갈 수 있는 위엄 있고 편안한 자리들 가운데에는 우리 여러 성당의 참사회원 자리가 있다. 널리 알려진 대로 그중 어떤 것은 딸린 보수가 거의 없거나 아예 없으나, 어떤 것은 이 세상의 좋은 것들로 넉넉하다. 훌륭한 살림집이 그 자리에 딸려 있고, 우리로서는 무엇인지 알기도 어려운 여러 집안의 특권이 함께 붙어 있으며, 게다가 성직에서 나오는 수입이 있으니 그 액수를 나누어 놓으면 힘겹게 일하는 성직의 종들 여럿의 마음을 기쁘게 해 줄 만하다. 개혁은 이 자리들 사이에서도 부지런히 움직여, 보수에 얼마만큼의 일을 붙여 놓고 너무 넘치는 자리에서는 남는 재물을 얼마쯤 깎아 냈다. 그러나 개혁은 이 자리들에 관대했으니, 제 직업의 힘겨운 일에 몸이 다 닳아 버린 이들을 위하여 그런 편안하고 위엄 있게 물러앉을 자리를 얼마쯤 두는 것이 좋다는 것을 인정한 것이다. 근래에는 젊은 주교를 임명하는 취향이 널리 퍼졌으니, 이는 주교란 정말로 힘겨운 일을 감당할 만한 사람이어야 한다는 느낌에서 나온 것임에 틀림이 없다. 그러나 젊은 참사회원이 바람직하게 여겨진다는 말은 우리가 여태 들어 본 적이 없다. 우리는 그런 자리에 뽑히는 성직자라면 긴 하루의 일로 편안한 저녁을 벌어 놓은 사람이어야 하고, 무엇보다도 그 삶이 여태까지 반듯했으며 따라서 사람의 짐작으로 앞으로도 반듯할 것이어서 자기가 몸을 붙이게 되는 성당에 영예가 될 만한 사람이어야 한다고 늘 생각해 왔다.\n\n그런데 우리는 요전 날, 바체스터 성당에 딸린 이 호사스러운 성직록 가운데 하나가 이웃 교구의 교구 사제인 마크 로바츠 목사에게, 성직록과 참사회원 자리를 함께 쥐는 것으로 하여 주어졌다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그리고 더 알아본 끝에 이 운 좋은 신사가 아직 서른에도 한참 못 미치는 나이라는 것을 알고 놀랐다. 그러나 우리는 그의 학식과 신앙심과 행실이 자기가 든 성당 참사회에 남다른 빛을 더해 줄 만한 것일지도 모른다고 믿고 싶었으므로, 마음에 걸리기는 했으나 잠잠히 있었다. 그런데 이제 우리 귀에, 아니 실은 온 세상의 귀에, 그 신앙심과 행실이 딱하게도 모자란다는 말이 들려왔다. 그리고 로바츠 씨를 그의 삶과 그가 어울리는 사람들로 미루어 판단한다면, 우리는 그 학식마저 의심하고 싶어진다. 그는 지금 이 순간, 아니 적어도 불과 며칠 전까지는, 런던의 아주 평판 나쁜 어음 할인업자 몇 사람의 소송으로 프램리의 제 목사관에 강제 집행을 들여 놓고 있었다. 그리고 아마 바체스터 성당 구내에 있는 그의 또 다른 집에도 강제 집행이 들어갔을 것이나, 그가 거기 거주 근무에 들어갈 필요가 있다고 여긴 적이 한 번도 없었던 까닭에 그것만은 면했다.\n\n그다음에는 저희 형제들의 행실에 주로 책임이 있다고 여겨지는 잉글랜드 국교회의 성직자들을 향한, 매우 엄한, 그리고 틀림없이 꼭 필요했을 충고가 이어졌다. 그리고 기사는 다음과 같이 끝을 맺었다.—\n\n이 참사회원 자리 가운데 많은 것은 각 성당의 수석 사제와 참사회가 주는 것이며, 그런 경우에는 마땅한 사람이 임명되도록 살피는 것이 수석 사제와 참사회의 소임이다. 그러나 다른 경우에는 고르는 권한이 왕실에 있으며, 그럴 때에는 그날의 정부에 똑같은 책임이 놓인다. 우리가 들은 바로 로바츠 씨는 전임 총리의 손으로 바체스터의 참사회원 자리에 임명되었으니, 우리는 그가 자기 추천권을 행사한 방식에 대해 무겁게 책잡혀야 마땅하다고 정말로 생각한다. 그러한 모든 경우에 그가 몸소 알아보아 스스로 만족하기란 될 수 없는 일일지도 모른다. 그러나 우리 정부는 처음부터 끝까지 대리 책임의 바탕 위에서 운영된다. 「쿠오드 파키트 페르 알리움, 파키트 페르 세」는 우리 각료들에게 특별한 방식으로 참말이며, 그들 가운데에서 높은 자리로 올라가는 사람이라면 누구든 그로써 짊어지는 위험을 감당해야 한다. 이 별난 경우에 우리는 그 추천이 아주 근래에 내각에 들어온 어느 각료의 손으로 이루어졌다고 전해 들었으니, 우리는 그가 임명될 때에 그것을 큰 잘못이라고 빗대어 말한 바 있다. 문제의 그 신사는 제 몫으로 높은 직무를 하나도 쥐고 있지 않았다. 그러나 이제 바체스터에서 저질러진 것과 같은 이런 해악은, 마땅하지 않은 사람들을 높은 자리로 들어 올릴 때 바로 그 뒤에 따라오는 종류의 재앙이니, 행정의 실패를 저지를 큰 여지가 그들 손안에 놓여 있지 않을 때조차 그러하다.\n\n로바츠 씨가 우리로 하여금 그에게 충고를 내밀도록 허락해 준다면, 그는 그 참사회원 자리를 다시 왕실의 처분에 맡기는 데 필요할 만한 절차를 지체 없이 밟을 것이다!\n\n여기서 한마디 해 두어도 좋겠다. 가련한 해럴드 스미스는 이 기사를 읽고 괴로움으로 몸을 뒤틀며, 이것이 자기가 미워하는 원수 서플하우스 씨의 솜씨라고 언명했다. 그 손을 자기는 안다는 것이었다. 적어도 그는 그렇게 말했다. 그러나 나로서는 그의 앙심이 그를 잘못 이끌었다고 믿고 싶다. 서플하우스 씨보다 더 큰 이가 우리 가련한 교구 사제를 벌하는 일을 스스로 떠맡았다고 나는 생각한다.\n\n이것은 프램리에 있는 그들 모두에게 매우 끔찍한 일이었고, 처음 읽었을 때에는 그들을 가루가 되도록 으깨어 놓는 듯했다. 가련한 로바츠 부인은 그것을 듣고 저희에게는 세상이 다 끝났다고 여기는 듯했다. 그것을 그녀에게 감추어 두려는 애가 있었으나, 그런 애는 늘 실패하는 법이고 이번에도 그러했다. 기사는 인정 많은 지방 신문마다 그대로 옮겨 실렸고, 그녀는 곧 무언가가 감추어지고 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 마침내 남편이 그것을 그녀에게 보여 주었고, 그러자 몇 시간 동안 그녀는 아주 무너져 버렸다. 며칠 동안은 얼굴을 내밀고 싶어 하지 않았고, 몇 주 동안은 몹시 슬펐다. 그러나 그 뒤로는 세상이 전에 하던 대로 거의 그대로 굴러가는 듯했다. 해는 그 기사가 씌어지지 않았을 때만큼이나 따습게 그들 위를 비추었다. 하늘의 해만 그런 것이 아니었으니, 하늘의 해야 대개 이교의 우레를 아무리 늘어놓아도 그 비침이 줄어들지 않는 법이다. 그들 자신의 세계를 비추는 그 다정한 해도 그러했으니, 그 따스함과 빛은 그들의 행복에 그토록 없어서는 안 될 것이었다. 이웃 주임 사제들은 뿌루퉁한 얼굴을 하지 않았고, 그 사제들의 부인들이 찾아오기를 마다하지도 않았다. 바체스터의 가게 사람들도 그녀를 수치를 입은 여인처럼 보지 않았다. 다만 프라우디 부인이 성당 구내에서 그녀를 지나칠 때 알아보았다는 표시로 가장 차가운 고갯짓을 했다는 것만은 인정해야 한다.\n\n오직 프라우디 부인의 마음에만 그 기사가 오래 남는 효험을 내는 듯했다. 한 가지 점에서는 그것이 어쩌면 이로웠다. 레이디 러프턴이 그 기사 때문에 곧바로 제 성직자와 한편이 되도록 이끌렸으니, 그리하여 로바츠 씨의 죄에 대한 기억이 프램리 코트 온 집안 사람들의 마음에서 더 빨리 사라져 갔던 것이다.\n\n그리고 사실 이 주 전체로 보아도, 여느 때만큼의 관심밖에 없는 시절이라면 이 일이 마땅히 받았으리라고 여겨졌을 그 온전한 주목을 이 일에 쏟을 형편이 아니었다. 지금 이 순간에는 총선 준비가 이루어지고 있었고, 동부 지구에서는 겨룸이 벌어지지 않게 되어 있었으나 서부에서는 매우 격렬한 싸움이 벌어지고 있었다. 그리고 그 싸움의 사정이 어찌나 사람을 들뜨게 하였던지, 로바츠 씨와 그의 기사는 제 시절이 다하기도 전에 잊혀졌다. 사워비 씨를 쫓아내라는 칙령이 개서럼 성에서 나왔고, 그에 맞서는 항명의 나팔 소리가 콜디코츠에서 울려, 사워비 씨를 위하여 공작의 분부에 따르지 않겠다고 항의했던 것이다.\n\n이 나라에는 헌법상 의원을 뽑아 의회로 보내는 일에 아무런 몫도 할 수 없는 두 부류의 사람이 있다. 곧 귀족과 여자다. 그런데도 이번 선거 싸움이 어느 귀족과 어느 여자 사이에서 벌어지고 있다는 것이 곧 이 주의 길이와 너비를 다 통하여 알려졌다. 던스터블 양이 이를테면 바로 그 마지막 고비에 콜디코츠 수렵림의 매수인으로 공표되었던 것이다. 그리고 이 매입은—바셋주 사람들이 사실은 아무것도 모르면서 언명하기로는—거인들이 신들을 누르고 잠시 우위를 얻지 않았더라면 아주 다른 쪽으로 갔을 것이라고 했다. 공작은 신들을 밀어 주는 사람이었고, 그래서 그의 돈은 거절당했다고 포더길 씨는 은근히 비쳐 말했다. 던스터블 양은 신들의 벗인 이 공작을 그의 제 고을에서 정면으로 맞설 각오가 되어 있었고, 그래서 그녀의 돈은 받아들여졌다는 것이었다. 그러나 나로서는 포더길 씨가 이 일에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다고 생각하고 싶고, 또 던스터블 양이 이기고 싶은 마음이 급한 나머지 공작이 여기는 그 재산의 값보다 더 많은 돈을 왕실에 내밀었으며 왕실이 그녀의 조바심을 이용했으니 온 국민에게는 그것이 뚜렷한 이익이 되었다고 짐작하고 싶다.\n\n그리고 던스터블 양이 사실은 콜디코츠 영지 전체의 임자이며, 사워비 씨를 주의 후보로 밀어 당선시키려 애쓰는 것은 곧 제 소작인의 편에 서 있는 것이라는 사실도 곧 알려졌다. 싸움이 이어지는 동안에 던스터블 양 자신이 마침내 항복했다는 것, 그리고 그녀가 그레셤스베리의 손 선생과, 아니 이제 맞은편 패가 즐겨 부르는 이름으로 하면 「그레셤스베리의 약제사」와 혼인할 참이라는 것도 알려졌다. “그 사람이야 평생 돌팔이나 조금 나은 정도였네.” 바체스터의 이름난 내과의 필그레이브 박사가 말했다. “그런데 이제는 돌팔이의 딸과 혼인하려 하네.” 이런 말과 이와 비슷한 말들에 손 선생은 스스로 그다지 시달리도록 내버려 두지 않았다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 일에서 포더길 씨와 선거 대리인 클로저스틸 씨 사이에 오간 아주 볼만한 야유 전단들이 잇달아 나왔다. 사워비 씨는 「그 여인의 애완물」이라 이름 붙었고, 이 애완물을 기르는 여인에 대한 묘사가 나왔는데, 그것은 던스터블 양의 겉모습이며 몸가짐이며 나이를 두고 결코 좋게 말해 주는 것이 아니었다. 그러고 나서 이 주의 서부 지구는 엄숙한 어조로—주와 선거구가 창 없는 담벼락과 헛간 문에 붙는 광고로 물음을 받게 되는 그런 방식으로—어느 여자에게 대표되는 것이 걸맞고 마땅한 일이겠느냐는 물음을 받았다. 그러자 이 주는 다시, 어느 공작에게 대표되는 것이 걸맞고 마땅한 일이겠느냐는 물음을 받았다. 그러고 나서 물음은 던스터블 양을 겨누어 더 사사로워졌고, 이 주가 어느 여자에게 넘겨지는 것으로도 모자라 레바논 연고를 팔던 여자에게 넘겨진다면 지워지지 않을 수치를 입는 것이 아니겠느냐는 따짐이 거세게 나왔다. 그러나 이 걸음으로 얻은 것은 별로 없었다. 맞대응 벽보가 이 불운한 주를 향하여, 이 주가 스스로 어느 귀족의 봉토가 되도록 내어 준다면, 더구나 상원의 자리를 더럽힌 이들 가운데 가장 부도덕한 귀족으로 알려진 어느 귀족의 봉토가 되도록 내어 준다면 그 수치가 얼마나 깊을 것인지를 풀어 설명했기 때문이다.\n\n그리하여 싸움은 아주 볼만하게 이어져 갔고, 돈이 마음대로 흐르도록 허락되었으므로 서바셋주 세상은 대체로 못마땅하지 않았다. 선거구나 주가 그런 종류의 수치를 얼마나 많이 눈 하나 깜박하지 않고 견뎌 낼 수 있는지는 놀라운 일이다. 또한 이 나라가 전체로는 헌법에 얼마나 지극한 값을 매기는지를 볼 때, 그 헌법의 원칙들이 사람들 하나하나에게는 얼마나 값이 나가지 않는지도 놀라운 일이다. 물론 공작은 몸을 내보이지 않았다. 그는 어떤 자리에서든 좀처럼 그러지 않았고, 이런 자리에서라면 결코 그러지 않았다. 그러나 포더길 씨는 어디에서나 눈에 띄었다. 던스터블 양도 제 등불을 말 아래 감추어 두지 않았다. 다만 그녀가 코시의 여관 문 위 현관 지붕 위에 올라 유권자들에게 연설을 했다고 널리 퍼진 소문은 사실에 바탕을 둔 것이 아니었음을, 나는 여기서 사가의 신의를 걸고 언명한다. 그녀가 코시에 있었고 그녀의 마차가 그 여관에 멈추었던 것은 틀림이 없다. 그러나 거기서든 다른 어디서든 그녀는 사람들 앞에 나서서 무엇을 보이는 일을 하지 않았다. “저를 프라우디 부인으로 잘못 본 것이겠지요.” 소문이 그녀의 귀에 닿았을 때 그녀는 그렇게 말했다.\n\n그러나 아아, 던스터블 양 쪽의 싸움에는 실패의 큰 요인이 하나 있었다. 사워비 씨 자신을 사내답게 싸우도록 이끌 수가 없었던 것이다. 그에게 내려진 뚜렷한 지시라면 그는 어지간히 따르기는 했다. 그가 이 겨룸에 나선다는 것은 그 흥정의 한 부분이었고, 그 흥정에서 그가 이제 물러날 수도 없었다. 그러나 제 몫으로 참되게 싸워 볼 기운이 그에게는 남아 있지 않았다. 그는 선거 유세장에 올라가 거기서 공작에게 맞설 수가 없었다. 이 일의 이른 시기에 포더길 씨가 그렇게 해 보라고 걸어 왔으나, 사워비 씨는 그 던져진 장갑을 끝내 집어 들지 않았다.\n\n“우리는 들었소.” 포더길 씨가 실버브리지의 「옴니엄 암스」 여관에서 한 그 대단한 연설에서 말했다. “우리는 이번 선거 동안에 옴니엄 공작에 대하여, 그리고 각하가 후보 가운데 한 사람에게 입혔다고들 하는 그 해코지에 대하여 많은 말을 들었소. 사워비 씨의 주장을 밀어 주는 신사들—그리고 그 여인—의 입에 공작의 이름이 아주 자주 오르오. 그러나 나는 사워비 씨 자신이 공작에 대하여 감히 무슨 말을 많이 했다고는 생각하지 않소. 나는 사워비 씨에게 선거 유세장에 올라 공작의 이름을 입에 올려 보라고 걸겠소.”\n\n그리고 실제로 사워비 씨는 공작의 이름을 끝내 입에 올리지 않았다.\n\n기운이 다한 뒤의 싸움은 서툰 싸움이 되는 법이고, 그런 일에 쏟을 사워비 씨의 기운은 이제 거의 다 꺾여 있었다. 공작이 포더길 씨의 도움을 얻어 그를 얽어 놓았던 그 그물에서 그가 빠져나온 것은 사실이다. 그러나 그는 한 사로잡힘에서 다른 사로잡힘으로 뚫고 나온 것일 뿐이었다. 돈이란 심상치 않은 것이다. 그리고 한번 나가 버린 돈은, 정치의 힘이나 명성이나 유행이 그럴 수 있는 것과 달리, 놀음판에서 패를 한번 섞는 것으로도 배틀도어 채로 한번 운 좋게 후려치는 것으로도 되돌려 받을 수 없다. 십만 파운드가 나갔으면 나간 것으로 남아 있어야 하니, 그것을 앞당겨 내어 드리는 영예를 누렸다고 주장하는 사람이 B 부인이든 C 경이든 마찬가지다. 어떤 운 좋은 술수도 그런 청구를 있는 것들 가운데에서 지워 내지 못한다—사워비 씨가 던스터블 양에게 시험해 본 것과 같은 술수가 될 수 있는 경우라면 모르되. 그에게는 공작보다 그 여인을 채권자로 두는 편이 틀림없이 나았으니, 그 여인이 다스리는 아래에서 제 옛집에 소작인으로 살아가는 것이 될 수 있는 일이었기 때문이다. 그러나 그동안 오고 간 모든 일을 겪고 나서 이것이 어지간히 슬픈 삶이라는 것을 그는 알게 되었다.\n\n던스터블 양 쪽에서 이 선거는 지고 말았다. 그녀는 이 겨룸을 높은 뜻으로 이어 가며 마지막 순간까지 싸웠고, 제 돈도 상대의 돈도 아끼지 않았다. 그러나 그녀가 이어 간 싸움은 이기지 못한 싸움이었다. 많은 신사들이 정말로 사워비 씨를 밀어 주었으니, 제 주를 공작의 종살이에서 풀어내고 싶은 마음이 넉넉했기 때문이다. 그러나 사워비 씨는 레이디 러프턴이 그를 두고 부른 대로 검은 양으로 여겨졌고, 선거가 끝났을 때 그는 서바셋주를 대표하는 자리에서 쫓겨난 자기를 보게 되었다.—스물다섯 해 동안 이 주를 쥐고 있은 끝에 영원히 쫓겨난 것이었다.\n\n불운한 사워비 씨! 나는 얼마간의 아쉬움을 느끼지 않고서는 여기서 그와 작별할 수가 없다. 더 나은 이끌림 아래에서라면 더 나은 것을 내었을 만한 무엇이 그의 안에 있었다는 것을 알기 때문이다. 태생이 높은 사람들 가운데에도 악당이 되려고 태어난 듯이 보이는 사람들이 있다. 그러나 사워비 씨는 내 생각으로는 신사가 되려고 태어난 사람이었다. 그가 신사가 아니었다는 것—그가 제게 정해진 길에서 벗어나 달아나 표주를 끔찍하게 그른 쪽으로 돌아 버렸다는 것—은 우리 모두 인정하기로 하자. 사람들과 어울리는 자리의 무심한 한때를 틈타 벗의 어음 인수를 얻어 내는 것은 신사다운 짓이 아니라 아주 불한당다운 짓이다. 그 일과 그와 비슷한 다른 일들이 그의 사람됨에 너무나 뚜렷하게 도장을 찍어 놓았다. 그러나 그럼에도 나는 사워비 씨를 위해 눈물 한 방울을 청하며, 그가 자키 클럽의 규칙에 맞추어 조심스럽게 제 경주를 달려 내지 못한 것을 애달파한다.\n\n그는 콜디코츠의 제 옛집에 소작인으로 살면서 제가 부치는 땅에서 살림을 얻어 내겠다는 그 계획을 시험해 보았다. 그러나 곧 그것을 버렸다. 그런 부지런한 일에 타고난 재간이 그에게는 없었고, 이 주에서 달라진 제 처지를 견딜 수도 없었다. 그는 곧 스스로 콜디코츠를 내놓았고, 그런 사람들이 사라지는 대로 사라져 버렸다.—꼭 있어야 할 수입이 아주 없는 채로 사라진 것은 아니었으니, 두 사람의 얽힌 일을 마지막으로 정리할 때에 이 점에 대해서는—이렇게까지 앞질러 말해도 된다면—손 부인의 사무를 맡아보는 사람이 각별히 마음을 썼기 때문이다.\n\n그리하여 공작이 낙점한 사람인 덤벨로 경이 당선되었으니, 아주 여러 해 동안 공작이 낙점한 사람이 늘 그러했던 대로였다. 여기에는 네메시스가 없었다—적어도 아직은 없었다. 그럼에도 그 절름거리는 발을 가진 여신은 반드시 그를, 그 공작을 붙잡을 것이다. 그가 붙잡히기에 마땅한 사람이라면 말이다. 우리와 함께 있는 자리에 각하가 얼굴을 내민 일이 어찌나 드물었던지, 나는 우리가 각하의 일에 대해서는 더 알아보는 일을 그만두어도 좋겠다고 생각한다.\n\n그러나 한 가지 점은 눈여겨볼 만하니, 여기 잉글랜드에서 우리가 우리 일을 얼마나 분별 있게 다스리는지를 보여 주기 때문이다. 이 이야기의 앞쪽에서 독자는 개서럼 성의 안쪽으로 안내를 받았고, 거기서 던스터블 양이 공작에게 가장 다정한 방식으로 대접받는 것을 보았다. 그날들 이후로 그 여인은 공작의 이웃이 되었고 그와 전쟁을 벌였으니, 공작으로서는 아마 그것이 몹시 괴로운 일이었을 것이다. 그러나 그럼에도, 개서럼 성에서 다음번 큰 자리가 열렸을 때 손 선생과 손 부인은 손님들 가운데 있었고, 공작이 몸소 나서서 그 부유한 이웃, 곧 한때 던스터블 양이던 이보다 더 정중하게 대한 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n# 제48장. 그들이 모두 결혼하여 아이 둘을 두고 그 뒤로 오래오래 행복하게 살았다는 이야기\n\n친애하는, 다정한, 마음을 함께 나누어 주는 독자여, 이 마지막 장에서 우리가 다루어야 할 사랑에 한숨짓는 짝은 네 쌍이다. 그리고 나는 합창대의 우두머리로서, 그 카드리유 가운데 어느 한 짝의 행복을 두고 여러분을 더 이상 의심으로 조이는 짓은 하지 않겠다. 그들은 모두 행복하게 되었다. 얼마 전 바체스터에서 있었던 그 조그만 소동에도 그러했다. 그리고 그들의 행복을 이야기하는 데에는—이제 그리해야 하는 대로 짧게—차례를 연월에 따라 잡아, 혼인의 제단에 먼저 나타난 이들에게 앞자리를 주겠다.\n\n그러면 칠월에, 대성당에서, 신부의 아버지가 제 심사 전속 사제의 보좌를 받아, 바체스터 주교의 맏딸 올리비아 프라우디를 베스널 그린의 트리니티 지구 교회 담임 성직자인 토바이어스 티클러 목사와 혼인으로 맺어 주었다. 이 경우 신랑에 대해서는 우리가 알게 된 지가 하도 짧으므로 많은 말을 할 필요는 아마 없을 것이다. 그는 혼례에 오면서 사랑하는 아이 셋을 데려오겠다고 하였으나, 앞으로 소중한 장모가 될 사람에게서 꽤 강한 어조로 나온 충고에 막혀 그 조치를 그만두었으니, 나는 그것이 지혜로운 일이었다고 생각한다. 티클러 씨는 넉넉한 사람이 아니었고 그 직업에서 여태 큰 이름을 얻은 바도 없었다. 그러나 마흔셋의 나이에 아직 앞에 놓인 기회가 넉넉했고, 이제 그의 공적이 교회의 높은 눈에 제대로 보이게 되었으니 마땅히 받아야 할 영전의 시원한 이슬이 그에게 내릴 것을 의심할 여지가 없다. 혼례는 아주 번듯했고, 올리비아는 그 힘든 시련을 훌륭한 몸가짐의 온당함으로 치러 냈다.\n\n그때까지, 아니 그로부터 며칠이 더 지날 때까지도, 덤벨로 경이 프랑스로 정말 떠났던 그 이상한 여행을 두고 바체스터에는 의심이 남아 있었다. 그런 처지에 놓인 사내가 앞으로 아내가 될 사람에게조차 알리지 않고 갑자기 파리로 가 버리면 사람들은 의심할 수밖에 없다. 그리하여 이 일을 두고 프라우디 부인은 제 자식의 혼례 조찬 자리에서까지 무거운 걱정을 입에 올렸다. “하느님이 두 사람에게 복을 내리시기를, 사랑하는 내 아이들이여,” 그녀가 제 식탁 상석에 일어서서 티클러 씨와 그 아내에게 말하였다. “두 사람의 온전한 행복을—곧 이 눈물의 골짜기에서 사람의 행복이 온전해질 수 있는 한에서 온전한 그 행복을—보고, 또 우리 불운한 이웃에게 떨어진 그 무서운 재앙을 생각하면, 나는 그분의 끝없는 자비와 선하심을 인정하지 않을 수 없소. 주께서 주시고, 주께서 가져가시느니라.” 이 말로 그녀가 뜻한 바는 의심할 것 없이, 티클러 씨는 자기 올리비아에게 주어졌으나 덤벨로 경은 부주교의 그리젤다에게서 거두어졌다는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 그 행복한 짝은 프라우디 부인의 마차를 타고 가장 가까운 역에서 한 역 더 간 곳까지 갔고, 거기에서 몰번으로 나아가 신혼여행을 보냈다.\n\n그리고 덤벨로 경이 파리에서 돌아왔고 하틀탑-그랜틀리 결합이 끝까지 이루어지게 되었다는 확실한 소식이 바체스터에 닿았을 때, 프라우디 부인에게 그것이 큰 위로가 되었으리라고 나는 믿는다. 그러나 그 젊은 나리가 달아날 작정이었다는 제 의견은 그녀가 여전히 버리지 않았으니—그것이 옳았는지 아닌지야 누가 말할 수 있겠는가? “부주교는 그 일을 해낸 방식에서 대단한 굳셈을 보였소,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그러나 마음에도 없는 남편과의 혼인으로 제 자식을 밀어 넣으면서 그 아이에게 가장 이로운 것을 헤아렸는지는, 나만은 의심하겠다는 뜻을 밝혀 두어야 하겠소. 하기야 불행하게도, 부주교가 얼마나 철저하게 세상일에 몸을 바친 사람인지는 우리가 다 알고 있으니.”\n\n이 경우에 부주교가 세상일에 바친 그 정성은 그가 의심할 것 없이 바랐던 그 성공으로 갚아졌다. 그는 실제로 런던으로 올라갔고, 덤벨로 경의 벗 한둘을 실제로 만났다. 그가 이 일을 한 것은, 그 자작 쪽에서 거짓말이나 흔들림이 있을까 두려워하는 사람처럼 나서서 한 것이 아니라, 그가 그토록 오래 이름을 얻어 온 그 조심스러움과 처신으로 한 것이었다. 프라우디 부인은 그가 바셋주를 떠나 있던 며칠 동안에 몸소 프랑스로 건너가 파리에서 덤벨로 경을 뒤쫓아 잡아냈다고 밝힌다. 이에 대해서 나는 아무것도 말할 준비가 없다. 그러나 부주교를 아는 사람이라면 다 그러하듯이, 그런 억울함을 피할 방도가 하나라도 남아 있는 한 그가 제 딸이 억울함을 당하는 것을 보고 있을 사람이 아니었다는 것만은 나도 아주 확실히 안다.\n\n그러나 그것이 어떠했든—부주교의 파리 여행이라는 그 논란거리 문제야 어떠했든—덤벨로 경은 팔월 오일에 플럼스테드에 나타났고, 사내답게 제 할 일을 치러 냈다. 하틀탑 집안은 이 결합을 피할 수 없다는 것이 드러나자, 바체스터 구내의 성직자다운 먼지와 그늘진 빛깔이 혼례 잔치의 화려함을 더럽히지 않도록 혼례를 하틀탑 프라이어리에서 치르자고 일을 꾸며 보려 했다. 사실을 말하자면 하틀탑 족속은 대체로 새로 얻은 성직자 인연을 자랑스러워하지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 이 문제에서 그랜틀리 부인은 아주 온당하게도 꺾이지 않았다. 마지막 순간에 어머니를 버리고 프라이어리에서 혼례를 올리겠다고 스스로 나서서 말하도록 신부를 꾀어 보려는 시도가 있었으나, 그것도 어떤 성과도 얻지 못했다. 하틀탑 족속은 그랜틀리 집안의 결과 그릇을 전혀 몰랐던 것이니, 알았더라면 그런 시도를 하지 않았을 것이다. 혼례는 플럼스테드에서 치러졌고, 그날 아침 덤벨로 경은 바체스터에서 주임 사제관까지 역마를 갈아 가며 건너왔다. 예식은 부주교가 보좌 없이 손수 집전했으나, 수석 사제와 선창 사제와 다른 성직자 둘도 그 자리에 있었다. 그리젤다의 몸가짐의 온당함은 올리비아 프라우디의 것과 조금도 다름이 없었다. 아니, 그날 그녀가 스스로를 지닌 그 조각상 같은 우아함과 훌륭한 귀족다운 태도를 넘어설 것은 아무것도 없었다. 예식이 그녀에게 요구한 서너 마디를 그녀는 편안하고 위엄 있게 말했다. 그 일을 어지럽히거나 벗들을 난처하게 할 흐느낌도 울음도 없었고, 그녀는 교회의 장부에 “그리젤다 그랜틀리”라고 제 이름을 서명했다—떨림도 없이, 그리고 후회도 없이.\n\n그랜틀리 부인은 딸이 아버지의 팔에 기대어 여행 마차로 걸어 나가려 할 때 현관에서 딸에게 입을 맞추고 복을 빌어 주었다. 그리고 그 아이는 마지막으로 귓속말을 하려고 어머니에게 얼굴을 들었다. “어머니,” 그녀가 말했다. “도버에 닿으면 제인이 무아르 앙티크를 바로 꺼낼 수 있겠지요?” 그랜틀리 부인은 웃으며 고개를 끄덕였고, 다시 제 아이에게 복을 빌어 주었다. 눈물 한 방울 흘린 사람이 없었고—적어도 그때는 없었고—그날의 흥겨운 화려함을 한순간이라도 흐려 놓을 슬픔의 낌새도 없었다. 그러나 그 어머니는 제 방에 홀로 있게 되었을 때 그 마지막 말을 되짚어 생각했고, 제 마음이 한숨지어 온 무엇인가가 없다는 것을 인정했다. 그녀는 제 동생에게 딸의 교육에 대해서는 후회할 것이 하나도 없다고 자랑한 바 있었다. 그러나 이제 제 성공을 얻고 혼자가 되었을 때, 그 자랑으로 여전히 스스로를 버텨 낼 수 있다고 느꼈던가? 알려 두어야 할 일이니, 그랜틀리 부인은 가슴에 심장을 지니고 있었고 그 심장 안에 믿음을 지니고 있었다. 세상은, 참으로, 쌓여 온 성직자의 재물이라는 그 온 무게로 그녀를 무겁게 눌러 왔다. 그러나 그것이 그녀를 아주 부서뜨려 놓은 것은 아니었다—그녀를 부서뜨린 것이 아니라 다만 그녀의 아이를 부서뜨렸을 뿐이었다. 아비의 죄는 삼사 대에까지 갚아지지 않는가?\n\n그러나 그러한 회한의 감정이 한동안 그랜틀리 부인의 기쁨의 그 가득함을 흠집 내었다 하더라도, 그것은 딸의 혼인 생활이 온전하게 성공한 덕에 이내 걷혔다. 가을이 끝날 무렵에 신부와 신랑은 여행에서 돌아왔고, 하틀탑 프라이어리의 온 모임에게는 덤벨로 경이 제가 한 거래를 조금도 못마땅해하지 않는다는 것이 뚜렷했다. 그의 아내는 그의 마음이 바랄 수 있는 마지막 한 치까지 어디에서나 찬탄을 받았다. 엠스에서도, 바덴에서도, 니스에서도 온 세상이 그 젊은 자작부인의 위엄 있는 아름다움에 마음을 맞았다. 그리고 또한 그녀의 몸가짐과 품과 태도의 높은 위엄이, 그녀의 우아함과 자태가 처음에 불러일으킨 그 우러르는 마음을 온통 받쳐 주었다. 그녀는 남의 소문을 옮기는 꾸며 낸 활기로 남편의 명예를 깎아내리는 일이 없었고, 누구든 그 여인 속의 귀족부인을 잊게 놓아두는 일도 없었다. 덤벨로 경은 조심스러움에 대한 제 명성이 그녀의 손에 있으면 아주 안전하다는 것을, 또 그가 몸가짐에 대해 무엇을 가르쳐 주어야 할 일이 하나도 없다는 것을 이내 알게 되었다.\n\n겨울이 다 가기 전에 그녀는 하틀탑 프라이어리의 온 모임의 마음도 똑같이 얻어 놓았다. 공작이 거기에 와 있었고, 후작부인에게 덤벨로가 이보다 더 잘할 수는 도저히 없었을 것이라고 밝혔다. “참으로, 저도 그럴 수는 없었으리라 생각해요,” 그 행복한 어머니가 말했다. “그 아이는 보아야 할 것은 다 보고, 보아서는 안 될 것은 하나도 보지 않지요.”\n\n그리고 사교철이 오자 런던에서 모든 사내가 그녀를 두고 온갖 칭찬을 노래했고, 덤벨로 경은 제가 그 나이의 사람들 가운데 가장 지혜로운 축에 든다고 헤아려지고 있음을 알게 되었다. 그는 모든 일을 제 대신 꾸려 주고, 결코 그를 괴롭히지 않고, 어느 여인도 싫어하지 않고, 모든 사내가 찬탄하는 아내를 얻었던 것이다. 이성의 잔치와 영혼의 흐름 같은 것이야, 사내와 그 아내 사이에 그런 흐름과 잔치가 꼭 있어야 하는가 하는 것부터가 물어볼 일이 아닌가? 부부간의 영혼의 흐름을 즐긴다고 참으로 말할 수 있는 사내가 몇이나 되며, 부부간의 이성의 잔치가 그 본성상 즐길 만한 것이라고 말할 수 있는 사내가 몇이나 되겠는가? 그러나 제 식탁 상석에 앉은 아름다운 여인이, 옷을 어떻게 입어야 하는지, 어떻게 앉아야 하는지, 마차에 어떻게 오르고 내려야 하는지를 아는 여인이—무지로 제 주인을 망신시키지 않고, 교태로 그를 애태우게 하지 않고, 제 재주로 그를 깎아내리지 않을 여인이—얼마나 아름다운 것인가! 나로서는 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 위대한 영국 귀족의 아내가 되도록 태어난 사람이라고 생각한다.\n\n“그러니까 결국,” 레이디 덤벨로를 이야기하며 던스터블 양이 말했다—이때 그녀는 손 부인이었다—“결국 우리의 그 별난 요즈음 철학자가 우리에게 일러 주는 말에도 얼마쯤 참이 있는 것이지요—‘오, 침묵이여, 그대의 힘은 위대하도다!’”\n\n우리의 오랜 벗 손 선생과 던스터블 양의 혼례는 목록에서 세 번째였으나, 그것은 구월이 다 갈 무렵에야 이루어졌다. 이런 경우에 변호사들이 해내야 할 일이 적지 않았고, 그리하여 그 부인이 내숭을 떤 것도 아니고 그 신사가 느린 것도 아니었으나 더 이른 혼례를 짜 맞추는 것은 될 일이 아니었다. 예식은 하노버 스퀘어의 세인트 조지 교회에서 치러졌고, 어느 특별한 점에서도 빛나지는 않았다. 그때 런던은 비어 있었고, 참석이 실제로 꼭 있어야 할 몇 사람만 그 일을 위해 시골에서 실려 올라왔다. 신부를 넘겨주는 소임은 이지먼 선생이 맡았고, 신부 들러리 둘은 던스터블 양의 말벗으로 함께 살아온 부인들이었다. 젊은 그레셤 씨와 그 아내가 그 자리에 있었고, 새로운 삶의 자리에 든 옛 벗을 놓아 버릴 마음이 조금도 없던 해럴드 스미스 부인도 그러했다.\n\n“우리는 그이를 던스터블 양이 아니라 손 부인이라고 부르게 될 텐데, 참으로 나는 달라지는 것이 그것뿐이리라 생각해요,” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다.\n\n해럴드 스미스 부인에게는 아마 그것이 달라지는 것의 전부였을 터이나, 가장 관계된 두 사람에게는 그렇지 않았다.\n\n손 선생과 그 아내 사이에 마련된 살림의 계획에 따르면, 그녀는 런던의 집을 그대로 지켜 사교철 가운데 제가 고르는 동안은 거기에 머물면서, 그가 그녀를 찾아오는 것이 좋게 여겨질 때에 그를 맞이하기로 되어 있었다. 그러나 시골에서는 그가 주인이 되기로 되어 있었다. 체이스에 저택을 한 채 지을 것이었고, 그것이 다 될 때까지는 그레셤스베리의 옛집을 그대로 두고 쓸 것이었다. 그 집이 작기는 했으나, 손 부인은—그 큰 재산에도—거기에 들어가는 것을 낮잡아 보지 않았다. 그러나 그 뒤의 사정이 두 사람의 계획을 바꾸어 놓았다. 사워비 씨가 콜디코츠에 살 수 없거나 살려 하지 않는다는 것이 드러났고, 그리하여 두 사람이 혼인한 둘째 해에 그곳이 두 사람을 위해 마련되었다. 두 사람은 이제 주 전체에 콜디코츠의 손 선생 부부로 널리 알려져 있다—콜디코츠의 손 선생 부부라 함은, 주의 동부 지구에 있는 그 잘 알려진 울라손의 손 씨네와 구별하기 위한 것이다. 여기에서 두 사람은 이웃들의 존중을 받으며, 옴니엄 공작과도 레이디 러프턴과도 동맹의 사이를 맺고 살아간다. “물론 그 정든 옛 가로수 길이야 저에게는 몹시 슬프겠지요,” 어느 런던 사교철이 끝날 무렵 콜디코츠로 내려오라는 청을 받았을 때 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하면서 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다.\n\n“글쎄, 이봐요, 내가 어쩌겠어요?” 손 부인이 말했다. “나로서는 그것을 베어 낼 수 없어요. 선생님이 그리하게 놓아두지 않을 테니까요.”\n\n“아, 그럴 수야 없지요,” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 한숨지으며 말했다. 그리고 그런 심정에도 그녀는 정말로 콜디코츠를 찾아갔다.\n\n그러나 러프턴 경이 행복한 사내가 된 것은 시월에 이르러서였다—다만 그 행복을 손에 쥐고 누리는 것이 그것을 미리 그려 보는 것보다 더 큰 기쁨이었다면 말이다. 혼인의 행복이 사해의 열매 같다고—입에 넣으면 입안에서 쓴 재로 변하는 사과 같다고 나는 말하지 않겠다. 그런 짐짓 꾸민 비꼼은 아주 거짓일 것이다. 그러나 그럼에도, 제단에서 예식이 치러지고 법이 인정하는 소유가 주어지는 그때에 사랑의 잔치에서 가장 달콤한 한 점은 이미 먹혀 버렸고 그 꽃의 가장 싱싱하고 가장 고운 붉은빛은 이미 낚아채여 지나가 버린 것이 사실이 아닌가? 사랑에는 어떤 향이, 뭐라 정할 수 없는 섬세한 맛이 있어서, 그것은 교회 문을 벗어나기도 전에 빠져나가 없어져 버린다. 처녀 시절의 이름과 함께 사라져 버리는 것이며, 아내라는 신분에 딸린 그 든든한 편안함과는 함께 있을 수 없는 것이다. 제 아내를 사랑하고 그 아내에게서 사랑받는 것은 사내에게 주어진 여느 몫이며, 벌을 걸고서 요구되는 의무다. 그러나 제 것이 아닌 젊음과 아름다움을 사랑하도록 허락받는 것—제 사랑이 거의 옳지 못한 것이기라도 한 듯 아직 세상의 눈길 앞에서 몸을 옹크리는 그 부드러운 사람에게 사랑받고 있음을 아는 것—이러한 기다림의 처지가 끝나는 때에 사내가 행복해진다고 할 수 있겠는가? 아니다. 남편이 제단에서 걸어 나올 때 그는 이미 제 잔치에서 가장 좋은 진미를 삼켜 버린 것이다. 그때부터 그를 위해 마련되어 있는 것은 혼인 생활의 소고기와 푸딩이다—아니, 어쩌면 빵과 치즈뿐일지도 모른다. 조심할 것이니, 빵 껍질 한 조각이 겨우 남아 있을 뿐일지도 모르고—아니, 어쩌면 껍질 한 조각조차 없을지도 모른다.\n\n그러나 끝을 맺기 전에, 잠깐만 그 진미로—소고기와 푸딩이 올라오기 전의 그때로 돌아가 보자. 루시가 아직 목사관에 있었고 러프턴 경이 아직 프램리 코트에 머물고 있던 때로 말이다. 그는 요즈음 자주 그러던 대로 어느 아침에 올라왔고, 몇 분 이야기를 나눈 뒤에 로바츠 부인은 방을 나갔다—그런 자리에서 그녀가 드물지 않게 하던 대로였다. 루시는 바늘일을 하고 있었고 그 일을 그대로 이어 갔다. 러프턴 경은 한두 순간 앉아서 그녀를 바라보다가, 문득 일어나 그녀 앞에 서서 이렇게 그녀에게 물었다—\n\n“루시,” 그가 말했다.\n\n“네, 루시가 어떻다는 말씀입니까? 오늘 아침에는 무슨 특별한 잘못이라도 있습니까?”\n\n“그래요, 아주 특별한 잘못이 있어요. 내가 여기, 이 방에서, 바로 이 자리에서 그대가 나를 사랑하는 것이 있을 수 있는 일이냐고 물었을 때—왜 있을 수 없는 일이라고 말했지요?”\n\n루시는 그 자리에서 대답하는 대신, 그의 기억이 제 기억만큼 좋은지 보려고 양탄자로 눈을 내리깔았다. 그렇다. 그는 전에 서 있던 바로 그 자리에 서 있었다. 온 세상의 어느 자리도 그녀의 눈앞에 그보다 더 자주 또렷이 떠오르는 곳은 없었다.\n\n“그날을 기억해요, 루시?” 그가 다시 말했다.\n\n“예, 기억합니다,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“왜 있을 수 없는 일이라고 말했지요?”\n\n“제가 있을 수 없다고 말했습니까?”\n\n그녀는 자기가 그렇게 말했다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그가 가 버릴 때까지 기다렸던 것을, 그러고 나서 제 방으로 올라가 그 거짓말의 비겁함을 스스로 꾸짖었던 것을 그녀는 기억했다. 그때 그녀는 그에게 거짓말을 했다. 그런데 이제—그 일로 그녀는 어떤 벌을 받고 있는가?\n\n“글쎄요, 있을 수 있는 일이었나 봅니다,” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그러나 그것이 나를 그토록 참담하게 만들 줄 알면서 왜 그렇게 말했지요?”\n\n“참담이라니요! 아니지요, 그때는 아주 흐뭇하게 가셨습니다! 그렇게 만족스러운 얼굴을 하신 것을 저는 여태 본 적이 없다고 생각했는걸요.”\n\n“루시!”\n\n“할 일을 다 하고 그렇게 운 좋게 빠져나오신 것이었지요! 저를 놀라게 하는 것은 어쨌든 다시 돌아오셨다는 점입니다. 하지만 물동이도 우물에 너무 자주 가면 깨지는 법이지요, 러프턴 경.”\n\n“그러나 이제는 사실을 말해 주겠지요?”\n\n“어떤 사실을요?”\n\n“그날, 내가 그대에게 왔을 때—그때 그대는 나를 조금이라도 사랑했나요?”\n\n“지나간 일은 지나간 일로 두시지요, 그래 주신다면요.”\n\n“그러나 맹세하지만 그대는 나에게 말해야 해요. 진심이 아니었다면 그렇게 나에게 대답한 것은 참 잔인한 짓이었지요. 그런데도 그대는 어머니가 크롤리 부인 집으로 그대를 데리러 건너간 뒤가 되도록 나를 다시 보지 않았어요.”\n\n“떨어져 있는 것이 저를—경에게 마음을 두게 만든 것입니다.”\n\n“루시, 맹세하지만 나는 그때 그대가 나를 사랑했다고 믿어요.”\n\n“루도빅, 어느 요술쟁이가 그것을 일러 주었나 봅니다.”\n\n그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 서 있었고, 그를 보고 웃으며 두 손을 들어 올리고 고개를 저었다. 그러나 이제 그녀는 그의 힘 안에 있었고, 그는 앙갚음을 했다—지난날의 그 거짓말과 지금의 그 농담에 대한 앙갚음을. 그녀를 온전히 제 것으로 두어 행복해질 때, 그가 지금보다 어떻게 더 행복할 수 있겠는가?\n\n그리고 이 무렵에 그녀가 말을 타는 일에 대한 그 청이 다시 나왔다—그러나 이번에는 그 지난날과는 아주 다른 결말이 되었다. 그때에는 반대가 참으로 많았다. 승마복이 없었고, 루시는 겁이 났다—아니면 겁이 난다고 말했다. 그리고 또 레이디 러프턴이 무슨 말을 하겠는가? 그러나 이제 레이디 러프턴은 그것이 아주 마땅한 일이라고 생각했다. 다만 그 말에 대해서는 아주 확실한가? 그 말을 여인이 타 본 적이 있다는 것을 루도빅은 확실히 아는가? 그리고 레이디 메러디스의 승마복들이 당연한 일처럼 끌려 나왔고, 그중 한 벌이 아무런 거리낌 없이 잘리고 가위질되고 고쳐졌다. 그리고 겁이라면, 루시 로바츠보다 더 담대하게 말을 타는 여인은 있을 수 없었다. 말을 타는 것이 그녀에게 딱 맞는 일이라는 것이 온 프램리에 아주 뚜렷했다. “하지만 루도빅, 네가 루시에게 알맞은 말을 제대로 구해 줄 때까지는 나는 마음이 놓이지 않을 게다,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다.\n\n그리고 또 그녀의 혼례 의복, 곧 그 혼례 옷가지의 일이 다가왔다. 그 일에서 그녀가 레이디 덤벨로만 한 역량이나 뚝심을 보였다고는 내가 자랑할 수 없다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 그것을 아주 중대한 일로 여겼다. 그리고 마님이 보기에 로바츠 부인이 넉넉한 기운으로 그 일에 나서지 않았으므로, 마님이 그 일을 대체로 제 손안에 넣어, 눈살과 고갯짓으로 루시의 입을 막아 버리고, 장화 끈의 끝쇠에 이르기까지 모든 것을 스스로 정했다.\n\n“얘야, 내가 무엇을 하고 있는지는 내가 안다고 좀 믿어 주어야 해요.” 그러면서 레이디 러프턴은 말하는 동안 그녀의 팔을 다독였다. “저스티니아 것도 다 내가 했고, 그 아이는 내가 사 준 것 가운데 어느 하나도 후회할 이유가 없었어요. 그 아이에게 물어보면 그렇다고 말해 줄 거예요.”\n\n루시는 앞으로 시누이가 될 그분에게 묻지 않았으니, 문제가 된 그 물건들에 대한 앞으로 시어머니가 될 그분의 판단을 조금도 의심하지 않았기 때문이다. 다만 그 돈이 문제였다! 그리고 여섯 다스나 되는 손수건을 한꺼번에 어디에 쓴다는 말인가? 러프턴 경이 인도 총독으로 나갈 일이 있는 것도 아니었다! 그러나 그리젤다의 상상으로는 열두 다스의 손수건도 너무 많은 것이 아니었다.\n\n그리고 루시는 프램리 코트의 응접실에 혼자 앉아, 처음으로 거기에 앉았던 그 저녁의 생각으로 제 마음을 가득 채우곤 했다. 그때 그녀는 괴로이, 마음속으로 눈물을 흘리며, 제 넋의 신음 소리를 내며, 그런 자리에 있는 것이 제 자리가 아니라고 마음을 정했던 것이다. 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 아주 편안하게 그 자리에 있어 레이디 러프턴에게 아낌을 받고 러프턴 경에게 찬탄을 받았다. 그러는 동안 그녀는 눈에 띄지 않는 데로 물러나 마음이 아팠으니, 자기가 둘레의 사람들에게 어울리는 벗이 아니라고 느꼈기 때문이었다. 그러다가 그가 그녀에게 왔고, 그녀에게 말을 걸어 사정을 거의 더 나쁘게 만들었고, 그 다정함으로 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 고이게 했으나, 그에게 편안히 말을 걸 수 없다는 느낌으로 여전히 그녀를 아프게 했다.\n\n그러나 이제 그녀의 사정은 다른 데에 이르러 있었다. 그가 그녀를 골랐다—온 세상 가운데에서 바로 그녀를 골라, 제 집과 제 영예와 제가 줄 수 있는 모든 것을 함께 나누도록 그녀를 거기로 데려온 것이다. 그녀는 그의 눈동자였고 그의 마음의 자랑이었다. 그리고 그녀가 그토록 어렵게 여겨 왔던 그 엄한 어머니, 처음에는 눈여겨볼 것도 못 되는 것으로 그녀를 지나쳐 버렸고 그러고 나서는 몸을 멀리 두라는 경고를 받으라고 그녀에게 사람을 보냈던 그 어머니가, 이제는 제 사랑과 아끼는 마음과 애틋한 살핌을 어떤 방식으로 보여야 넉넉할지 거의 알지 못했다.\n\n이런 순간에 루시가 자랑스럽지 않았다고—이런 생각에 그녀의 마음이 들뜨지 않았다고 나는 말하지 못하겠다. 실패가 부끄러움을 낳듯이 성공은 자랑을 낳는다. 그러나 그녀의 자랑은 남자에게든 여자에게든 조금도 부끄러울 것이 없는 종류의 것이었고, 순수하고 참된 사랑과, 또 하느님이 기꺼이 그녀를 부르신 그 삶의 자리에서 제 의무를 다하겠다는 온전한 결심을 함께 지니고 있었다. 그리젤다가 아니라 자기가 뽑혔다는 것을 생각하며 그녀는 참으로 크게 기뻐했다. 사랑을 한 사람이 그렇게 기뻐하지 않는 것이, 또 기뻐하면서 제 사랑을 자랑하지 않는 것이 있을 수 있는 일이겠는가?\n\n두 사람은 겨울을 온통 외국에서 보내며, 미망인 마님 레이디 러프턴에게는 프램리 코트에서 두 사람을 맞이할 계획과 준비를 맡겨 두었다. 그리고 이듬해 봄에 두 사람은 런던에 나타나 거기에 살림을 차렸다. 루시는 그렇게 큰 세상 앞에서 제 소임을 시작하는 데에 넋이 떨리고 가슴이 흔들리는 것을 속으로 느꼈으나, 그 일을 두고 남편에게는 거의 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 다른 여인들도 그녀보다 앞선 시절에 그만한 일을 해냈고, 용기로 그것을 이겨 냈다. 제 집에서 귀족 남녀가 자기에게 허리를 굽히고, 딱딱한 하원 의원들에게는 가벼운 이야기를 지어내 주어야 하는 그 자리는 어지간히 끔찍한 일일 것이었다. 그럼에도 그것은 견뎌야 할 일이었다. 그때가 왔고, 그녀는 견뎌 냈다. 그때가 왔고, 처음 여섯 주가 지나기도 전에 그녀는 그것이 어지간히 쉬운 일임을 알게 되었다. 귀족 남녀는 제 알맞은 자리를 찾아들어 애쓸 것도 없는 방식으로 그녀에게 여느 일들을 이야기했고, 하원 의원들은 그녀가 프램리 근방에서 알고 지낸 성직자들보다 딱딱할 것이 별로 없었다.\n\n그녀가 도시에 온 지 오래되지 않아 레이디 덤벨로를 만났다. 이 만남에서도 그녀는 속으로 얼마쯤 감정을 눌러 이겨 내야 했다. 프램리에서 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 만난 그 몇 번의 기회에 두 사람은 벗의 사이로 크게 나아간 것이 없었고, 루시는 그 부유한 미인에게 업신여김을 받았다고 느꼈다. 그녀 또한 그 맞수를 업신여기지는 않았다 해도 싫어하기는 했다. 그러나 이제는 어떻게 될 것인가? 레이디 덤벨로가 그녀를 업신여기기는 어려울 것이었으나, 그러면서도 두 사람이 벗으로 만나는 일도 있을 수 없어 보였다. 두 사람이 만났고, 루시는 레이디 러프턴이 한때 아끼던 그 사람에게 손을 내밀려고 사랑스럽게 서둘러 앞으로 나섰다. 레이디 덤벨로는 살짝 웃었다—프램리의 응접실에서 두 사람이 처음 인사를 나누었을 때 그 얼굴에 떠올랐던 바로 그 옛 웃음, 선 하나 달라진 것 없는 그 똑같은 웃음이었다. 그러고는 내민 손을 잡고, 한두 마디를 웅얼거리고, 뒤로 물러났다. 그것은 전에 했던 것과 조금도 다름이 없었다. 그녀는 루시 로바츠를 업신여긴 적이 없었다. 그녀는 목사의 누이에게 자기가 알고 지내는 사람들을 여느 때 맞이하는 만큼의 다정함을 베풀어 준 것이었고, 이제 그 귀족의 아내에게도 그보다 더 해 줄 수 있는 것이 없었다. 레이디 덤벨로와 레이디 러프턴은 그 뒤로 줄곧 서로 아는 사이로 지내며 이따금 서로의 집을 찾기도 했으나, 두 사람 사이의 친함은 결코 이보다 더 나아간 적이 없다.\n\n미망인 마님은 한 달쯤 도시로 올라왔고, 거기에 있는 동안에는 둘째 자리를 채우는 것으로 만족했다. 마님에게는 런던에서 큰 마님이 되려는 마음이 없었다. 그러나 그다음에 두 사람이 프램리 코트에서 함께 살림을 시작하는 그 힘든 시기가 왔다. 나이 든 그 부인은 식탁 상석의 제 자리를 격식을 갖추어 내놓았다—루시가 눈물로 다시 앉으라고 애원했음에도 격식을 갖추어 끝까지 내놓았다. 마님은 또한 똑같은 격식으로—로바츠 부인에게 큰 기운으로 제 결심을 몇 번이고 되풀이하여 말하며—이 집의 마땅한 안주인이 지닌 권위를 제 간섭으로 어떤 점에서도 깎아내리지 않겠다고 밝혔다. 그러나 그럼에도 늙은 레이디 러프턴이 여전히 그 교구에서 으뜸으로 군림하고 있다는 것은 프램리의 누구에게나 잘 알려진 일이다.\n\n“그래요, 얘야. 남쪽 조그만 정원이 보이는 그 큰 방이 언제나 아기방이었어요. 그리고 내 뜻을 묻는다면 앞으로도 그대로일 거예요. 물론, 마음에 드는 방이라면 어느 방이든—”\n\n그리고 남쪽 조그만 정원이 보이는 그 큰 방은 프램리 코트에서 여전히 아기방이다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-2860"]=[[["# CHAPTER I. “OMNES OMNIA BONA DICERE.”", "# 제1장. 「옴네스 옴니아 보나 디케레」"]], [["When young Mark Robarts was leaving college, his father might well declare that all men began to say all good things to him, and to extol his fortune in that he had a son blessed with so excellent a disposition.", "젊은 마크 로바츠가 대학을 떠날 무렵, 그의 아버지는 세상 모든 사람이 자기에게 온갖 좋은 말을 하기 시작했다고, 그토록 훌륭한 성품을 타고난 아들을 두었으니 복이 많다고 저마다 칭송한다고, 얼마든지 그렇게 말할 만했다."]], [["This father was a physician living at Exeter.", "이 아버지는 엑서터에 사는 의사였다."], ["He was a gentleman possessed of no private means, but enjoying a lucrative practice, which had enabled him to maintain and educate a family with all the advantages which money can give in this country.", "그는 사유 재산이라고는 없는 신사였으나 벌이가 좋은 개업을 누리고 있었고, 그 덕에 이 나라에서 돈이 줄 수 있는 온갖 이점을 갖추어 가족을 부양하고 교육시킬 수 있었다."], ["Mark was his eldest son and second child; and the first page or two of this narrative must be consumed in giving a catalogue of the good things which chance and conduct together had heaped upon this young man’s head.", "마크는 그의 맏아들이자 둘째 아이였다. 그리고 이 이야기의 첫 한두 쪽은, 우연과 처신이 함께 이 젊은이의 머리 위에 쌓아 올린 좋은 것들의 목록을 적는 데 써야 하겠다."]], [["His first step forward in life had arisen from his having been sent, while still very young, as a private pupil to the house of a clergyman, who was an old friend and intimate friend of his father’s.", "그가 인생에서 앞으로 내디딘 첫걸음은, 아직 아주 어릴 때 아버지의 오래고 가까운 벗이던 어느 성직자의 집으로 사숙 학생으로 보내진 데서 비롯되었다. 이 성직자에게는 다른 학생이 딱 한 명, 오직 한 명만 더 있었다."], ["This clergyman had one other, and only one other, pupil—the young Lord Lufton; and between the two boys, there had sprung up a close alliance.", "어린 러프턴 경이었다. 그리고 두 소년 사이에는 긴밀한 동맹이 싹텄다."]], [["While they were both so placed, Lady Lufton had visited her son, and then invited young Robarts to pass his next holidays at Framley Court.", "두 아이가 함께 그 집에 있는 동안 레이디 러프턴이 아들을 보러 왔고, 그러고는 어린 로바츠에게 다음 방학을 프램리 코트에서 보내라고 청했다."], ["This visit was made; and it ended in Mark going back to Exeter with a letter full of praise from the widowed peeress.", "이 방문은 실행되었다. 그리고 그 끝에 마크는 미망인이 된 그 귀족 부인의 칭찬으로 가득한 편지를 들고 엑서터로 돌아갔다."], ["She had been delighted, she said, in having such a companion for her son, and expressed a hope that the boys might remain together during the course of their education.", "아들에게 그런 동무가 생겨 무척 기뻤다고 그녀는 썼고, 두 소년이 학업을 마칠 때까지 줄곧 함께 지낼 수 있기를 바란다는 희망을 밝혔다."], ["Dr. Robarts was a man who thought much of the breath of peers and peeresses, and was by no means inclined to throw away any advantage which might arise to his child from such a friendship.", "로바츠 선생은 귀족 남녀의 입김을 대단히 중히 여기는 사람이었고, 그런 우정에서 제 자식에게 돌아올 수 있는 이점이라면 무엇 하나 내던질 생각이 결코 없었다."], ["When, therefore, the young lord was sent to Harrow, Mark Robarts went there also.", "그러므로 어린 경이 해로로 보내지자 마크 로바츠도 그곳으로 갔다."]], [["That the lord and his friend often quarrelled, and occasionally fought,—the fact even that for one period of three months they never spoke to each other—by no means interfered with the doctor’s hopes.", "그 경과 그의 벗이 자주 다투고 이따금 주먹질까지 했다는 것, 심지어 석 달 동안 서로 한마디도 하지 않은 시기가 있었다는 사실조차, 의사의 희망을 조금도 방해하지 않았다."], ["Mark again and again stayed a fortnight at Framley Court, and Lady Lufton always wrote about him in the highest terms.", "마크는 몇 번이고 다시 프램리 코트에서 보름씩 머물렀고, 레이디 러프턴은 언제나 그를 두고 가장 좋은 말로 편지를 썼다."]], [["And then the lads went together to Oxford, and here Mark’s good fortune followed him, consisting rather in the highly respectable manner in which he lived, than in any wonderful career of collegiate success. His family was proud of him, and the doctor was always ready to talk of him to his patients; not because he was a prizeman, and had gotten medals and scholarships, but on account of the excellence of his general conduct. He lived with the best set—he incurred no debts—he was fond of society, but able to avoid low society—liked his glass of wine, but was never known to be drunk; and, above all things, was one of the most popular men in the university.", "그러고 나서 두 소년은 함께 옥스퍼드로 갔고, 여기서도 마크의 좋은 운은 그를 따라다녔는데, 그 운이란 학업에서 놀라운 성취를 이룬 데 있다기보다 지극히 존경스러운 방식으로 생활했다는 데 있었다. 가족은 그를 자랑스러워했고, 의사는 늘 환자들에게 그의 이야기를 할 준비가 되어 있었다. 그가 수상자여서, 메달이며 장학금을 받아서가 아니라, 전반적인 처신이 훌륭해서였다. 그는 가장 좋은 무리와 어울렸다. 빚을 지지 않았다. 사교를 좋아하면서도 저열한 교제는 피할 줄 알았다. 포도주 한 잔을 즐겼으나 취한 모습을 보인 적이 없었다. 그리고 무엇보다도 그는 대학에서 가장 인기 있는 사람 가운데 하나였다."]], [["Then came the question of a profession for this young Hyperion, and on this subject, Dr.", "그다음에는 이 젊은 히페리온의 직업 문제가 나왔다."], ["Robarts was invited himself to go over to Framley Court to discuss the matter with Lady Lufton. Dr.", "그리고 이 문제를 두고 로바츠 선생은 몸소 프램리 코트로 건너와 레이디 러프턴과 상의하자는 초청을 받았다."], ["Robarts returned with a very strong conception that the Church was the profession best suited to his son.", "로바츠 선생은 성직이 아들에게 가장 알맞은 직업이라는 아주 강한 확신을 안고 돌아왔다."]], [["Lady Lufton had not sent for Dr. Robarts all the way from Exeter for nothing.", "레이디 러프턴이 로바츠 선생을 엑서터에서 그 먼 길을 불러들인 것은 아무 뜻 없이 한 일이 아니었다."], ["The living of Framley was in the gift of the Lufton family, and the next presentation would be in Lady Lufton’s hands, if it should fall vacant before the young lord was twenty-five years of age, and in the young lord’s hands if it should fall afterwards.", "프램리의 성직록은 러프턴 가문의 증여권에 속해 있었고, 다음 추천권은 어린 경이 스물다섯 살이 되기 전에 자리가 비면 레이디 러프턴의 손에 있고, 그 뒤에 비면 어린 경의 손에 있었다."], ["But the mother and the heir consented to give a joint promise to Dr. Robarts.", "그러나 어머니와 상속자는 로바츠 선생에게 공동으로 약속해 주기로 뜻을 모았다."], ["Now, as the present incumbent was over seventy, and as the living was worth £900 a year, there could be no doubt as to the eligibility of the clerical profession.", "그런데 현직자는 일흔이 넘었고 성직록은 연 900파운드의 값이 나갔으니, 성직이라는 직업이 얼마나 유리한지는 의심할 여지가 없었다."]], [["And I must further say, that the dowager and the doctor were justified in their choice by the life and principles of the young man—as far as any father can be justified in choosing such a profession for his son, and as far as any lay impropriator can be justified in making such a promise.", "그리고 나는 한마디 더 해 두어야 하겠다. 아버지가 아들의 직업을 그렇게 골라 줄 때 정당할 수 있는 한에서, 또 성직록을 쥔 속인이 그런 약속을 할 때 정당할 수 있는 한에서, 그 미망인과 의사는 그 젊은이의 생활과 원칙으로 자기들의 선택을 정당화할 수 있었다."], ["Had Lady Lufton had a second son, that second son would probably have had the living, and no one would have thought it wrong;—certainly not if that second son had been such a one as Mark Robarts.", "레이디 러프턴에게 둘째 아들이 있었다면 그 둘째 아들이 아마 그 성직록을 받았을 것이고, 아무도 그것을 잘못이라 여기지 않았을 것이다. 적어도 그 둘째 아들이 마크 로바츠 같은 사람이었다면 분명히 그랬을 것이다."]], [["Lady Lufton herself was a woman who thought much on religious matters, and would by no means have been disposed to place any one in a living, merely because such a one had been her son’s friend.", "레이디 러프턴 자신은 종교 문제를 많이 생각하는 여자였고, 단지 아들의 벗이라는 이유만으로 누구를 성직록에 앉힐 마음은 결코 없었을 것이다."], ["Her tendencies were High Church, and she was enabled to perceive that those of young Mark Robarts ran in the same direction.", "그녀의 성향은 고교회파였고, 젊은 마크 로바츠의 성향도 같은 방향으로 흐른다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["She was very desirous that her son should make an associate of his clergyman, and by this step she would insure, at any rate, that.", "그녀는 아들이 자기 교구의 성직자를 벗으로 삼기를 몹시 바랐고, 이렇게 하면 적어도 그것만은 확실히 해 둘 수 있었다."], ["She was anxious that the parish vicar should be one with whom she could herself fully co-operate, and was perhaps unconsciously wishful that he might in some measure be subject to her influence.", "교구 사제가 자기와 온전히 협력할 수 있는 사람이기를 원했고, 어쩌면 자기도 모르는 채, 그가 어느 정도는 자기 영향 아래 있어 주었으면 하고 바랐다."], ["Should she appoint an elder man, this might probably not be the case to the same extent; and should her son have the gift, it might probably not be the case at all.", "나이 든 사람을 앉히면 아마 같은 정도로는 그렇게 되지 않을 것이고, 아들이 추천권을 쥐게 되면 아마 아예 그렇게 되지 않을 것이었다."], ["And therefore it was resolved that the living should be given to young Robarts.", "그러므로 성직록은 젊은 로바츠에게 주기로 결정되었다."]], [["He took his degree—not with any brilliancy, but quite in the manner that his father desired; he then travelled for eight or ten months with Lord Lufton and a college don, and almost immediately after his return home was ordained.", "그는 학위를 받았다. 무슨 빛나는 성적은 아니었으나 아버지가 바라던 그대로였다. 그러고 나서 여덟 달인가 열 달 동안 러프턴 경과 대학 교원 한 사람과 함께 여행했고, 집에 돌아오자 거의 곧바로 성직에 서품되었다."]], [["The living of Framley is in the diocese of Barchester; and, seeing what were Mark’s hopes with reference to that diocese, it was by no means difficult to get him a curacy within it.", "프램리의 성직록은 바체스터 교구에 속한다. 그리고 마크가 그 교구를 두고 어떤 희망을 품고 있었는지를 보면, 그 안에서 부목사 자리를 얻는 일은 조금도 어렵지 않았다."], ["But this curacy he was not allowed long to fill.", "그러나 이 부목사 자리를 그는 오래 채우도록 허락받지 못했다."], ["He had not been in it above a twelvemonth, when poor old Dr. Stopford, the then vicar of Framley, was gathered to his fathers, and the full fruition of his rich hopes fell upon his shoulders.", "그 자리에 앉은 지 열두 달을 넘기지 않았을 때, 당시 프램리의 교구 사제였던 불쌍한 늙은 스톱퍼드 박사가 조상들에게로 돌아갔고, 그 풍성한 희망의 온전한 열매가 그의 어깨 위에 떨어졌다."]], [["But even yet more must be told of his good fortune before we can come to the actual incidents of our story.", "그러나 우리 이야기의 실제 사건에 이르기 전에 그의 좋은 운에 대해 아직 더 말해 두어야 할 것이 있다."], ["Lady Lufton, who, as I have said, thought much of clerical matters, did not carry her High Church principles so far as to advocate celibacy for the clergy.", "앞서 말했듯 성직에 관한 문제를 많이 생각한 레이디 러프턴은, 고교회파 원칙을 성직자의 독신을 주장할 만큼 멀리까지 밀고 나가지는 않았다."], ["On the contrary, she had an idea that a man could not be a good parish parson without a wife.", "오히려 그녀는 아내 없이는 좋은 교구 목사가 될 수 없다는 생각을 갖고 있었다."], ["So, having given to her favourite a position in the world, and an income sufficient for a gentleman’s wants, she set herself to work to find him a partner in those blessings.", "그래서 자기가 아끼는 사람에게 세상에서의 지위와 신사의 필요를 채울 만한 수입을 준 다음, 그녀는 그 축복을 함께 나눌 짝을 찾아 주는 일에 나섰다."]], [["And here also, as in other matters, he fell in with the views of his patroness—not, however, that they were declared to him in that marked manner in which the affair of the living had been broached.", "그리고 여기서도 다른 일에서와 마찬가지로 그는 후원자의 뜻과 맞아떨어졌다. 다만 성직록 일이 꺼내졌을 때처럼 그렇게 드러내 놓고 그에게 말해진 것은 아니었다."], ["Lady Lufton was much too highly gifted with woman’s craft for that.", "레이디 러프턴은 그런 식으로 하기에는 여자의 재간을 너무나 높이 타고난 사람이었다."], ["She never told the young vicar that Miss Monsell accompanied her ladyship’s married daughter to Framley Court expressly that he, Mark, might fall in love with her; but such was in truth the case.", "몬셀 양이 마님의 결혼한 딸을 따라 프램리 코트에 온 것은 다름 아니라 그, 마크가 그녀에게 반하게 하려는 것이라고, 그녀는 젊은 교구 사제에게 한 번도 말하지 않았다. 그러나 사실은 그러했다."]], [["Lady Lufton had but two children.", "레이디 러프턴에게는 아이가 둘뿐이었다."], ["The eldest, a daughter, had been married some four or five years to Sir George Meredith, and this Miss Monsell was a dear friend of hers.", "맏이인 딸은 사오 년 전에 조지 메러디스 경과 결혼했고, 이 몬셀 양은 그 딸의 소중한 벗이었다."], ["And now looms before me the novelist’s great difficulty.", "그리고 이제 소설가의 큰 난관이 내 앞에 어른거린다."], ["Miss Monsell,—or, rather, Mrs. Mark Robarts,—must be described.", "몬셀 양을, 아니 차라리 마크 로바츠 부인을 묘사해야 한다."], ["As Miss Monsell, our tale will have to take no prolonged note of her.", "몬셀 양으로서라면 우리 이야기가 그녀를 길게 다룰 일이 없을 것이다."], ["And yet we will call her Fanny Monsell, when we declare that she was one of the pleasantest companions that could be brought near to a man, as the future partner of his home, and owner of his heart.", "그러나 그래도 우리는 그녀를 패니 몬셀이라 부르겠다. 한 남자의 앞날의 가정을 함께 꾸리고 그 마음의 주인이 될 사람으로서 그에게 가까이 데려올 수 있는 가장 유쾌한 벗 가운데 하나였다고 말할 때 말이다."], ["And if high principles without asperity, female gentleness without weakness, a love of laughter without malice, and a true loving heart, can qualify a woman to be a parson’s wife, then was Fanny Monsell qualified to fill that station.", "그리고 모질지 않은 높은 원칙, 약하지 않은 여자다운 부드러움, 악의 없이 웃기를 좋아하는 마음, 그리고 참으로 사랑하는 심장이 한 여자에게 목사의 아내가 될 자격을 준다면, 패니 몬셀은 그 자리를 채울 자격을 갖추고 있었다."]], [["In person she was somewhat larger than common.", "몸은 보통보다 조금 컸다."], ["Her face would have been beautiful but that her mouth was large.", "입이 크지 않았다면 얼굴은 아름다웠을 것이다."], ["Her hair, which was copious, was of a bright brown; her eyes also were brown, and, being so, were the distinctive feature of her face, for brown eyes are not common.", "머리카락은 숱이 많고 밝은 갈색이었다. 눈도 갈색이었는데, 갈색 눈은 흔하지 않으므로 그 눈이 얼굴에서 두드러진 특징이었다."], ["They were liquid, large, and full either of tenderness or of mirth.", "물기 어리고 크고, 다정함이나 즐거움으로 가득한 눈이었다."], ["Mark Robarts still had his accustomed luck, when such a girl as this was brought to Framley for his wooing.", "이런 처녀가 구애를 받으라고 프램리로 데려와졌을 때에도 마크 로바츠에게는 여전히 늘 따르던 운이 있었다."]], [["And he did woo her—and won her.", "그리고 그는 정말로 구애했고, 그녀를 얻었다."], ["For Mark himself was a handsome fellow.", "마크 자신도 잘생긴 사내였으니까."], ["At this time the vicar was about twenty-five years of age, and the future Mrs. Robarts was two or three years younger.", "이때 교구 사제는 스물다섯 살쯤이었고, 앞날의 로바츠 부인은 두세 살 아래였다."], ["Nor did she come quite empty-handed to the vicarage.", "게다가 그녀는 목사관에 아주 빈손으로 오지도 않았다."], ["It cannot be said that Fanny Monsell was an heiress, but she had been left with a provision of some few thousand pounds.", "패니 몬셀이 상속녀였다고는 할 수 없지만, 몇천 파운드쯤의 재산이 그녀에게 남겨져 있었다."], ["This was so settled, that the interest of his wife’s money paid the heavy insurance on his life which young Robarts effected, and there was left to him, over and above, sufficient to furnish his parsonage in the very best style of clerical comfort,—and to start him on the road of life rejoicing.", "이것이 이렇게 정리되어, 아내 몫 돈의 이자로 젊은 로바츠가 든 무거운 생명 보험료를 냈고, 그러고도 남는 것이 있어 성직자의 안락에 가장 어울리는 최상의 방식으로 목사관을 꾸미고, 기쁨 속에 인생길로 나서게 하기에 충분했다."]], [["So much did Lady Lufton do for her protégé, and it may well be imagined that the Devonshire physician, sitting meditative over his parlour fire, looking back, as men will look back on the upshot of their life, was well contented with that upshot, as regarded his eldest offshoot, the Rev.", "레이디 러프턴은 자기 피후견인을 위해 그만큼을 해 주었다."], ["Mark Robarts, the vicar of Framley.", "그러니 데번셔의 그 의사가 응접실 난롯불 앞에 앉아 명상에 잠긴 채, 사람들이 흔히 제 인생의 결산을 돌아보듯 돌아보면서, 맏자식인 프램리의 교구 사제 마크 로바츠 목사에 관한 한 그 결산에 매우 흡족했으리라는 것은 넉넉히 상상할 수 있다."]], [["But little has as yet been said, personally, as to our hero himself, and perhaps it may not be necessary to say much.", "그러나 우리 주인공 자신에 대해서는 아직 개인적으로 이야기한 것이 거의 없다. 어쩌면 많이 말할 필요가 없을지도 모른다."], ["Let us hope that by degrees he may come forth upon the canvas, showing to the beholder the nature of the man inwardly and outwardly.", "차츰차츰 그가 화폭 위로 나와 보는 이에게 그 사람의 안팎을 다 보여 주기를 바라자. 여기서는 이렇게만 말해 두면 될 것이다."], ["Here it may suffice to say that he was no born heaven’s cherub, neither was he a born fallen devil’s spirit.", "그는 하늘의 천사로 태어난 것도 아니었고, 타락한 악마의 영으로 태어난 것도 아니었다."], ["Such as his training made him, such he was.", "그의 교육이 그를 만든 대로 그는 그러했다."], ["He had large capabilities for good—and aptitudes also for evil, quite enough: quite enough to make it needful that he should repel temptation as temptation only can be repelled.", "그에게는 선을 향한 큰 역량이 있었고, 악을 향한 소질도 넉넉히 있었다. 유혹은 오직 유혹을 물리치는 방식으로만 물리쳐야 한다는 것이 그에게 꼭 필요할 만큼은 넉넉히 있었다."], ["Much had been done to spoil him, but in the ordinary acceptation of the word he was not spoiled.", "그를 망칠 만한 일이 많이 있었지만, 흔히 쓰는 뜻으로 그는 망쳐지지 않았다."], ["He had too much tact, too much common sense, to believe himself to be the paragon which his mother thought him.", "어머니가 여기는 만큼 자기가 완벽한 사람이라고 믿기에는 그에게 재치와 상식이 너무 많았다."], ["Self-conceit was not, perhaps, his greatest danger.", "아마 자만심이 그의 가장 큰 위험은 아니었을 것이다."], ["Had he possessed more of it, he might have been a less agreeable man, but his course before him might on that account have been the safer.", "자만심이 좀 더 많았더라면 그는 덜 유쾌한 사람이 되었을지 모르나, 그 덕에 그의 앞에 놓인 길은 더 안전했을 수도 있다."]], [["In person he was manly, tall, and fair-haired, with a square forehead, denoting intelligence rather than thought, with clear white hands, filbert nails, and a power of dressing himself in such a manner that no one should ever observe of him that his clothes were either good or bad, shabby or smart.", "겉모습으로 보면 그는 남자다웠고, 키가 크고 머리는 금빛이었으며, 이마는 각이 져서 사색보다는 지성을 말해 주었고, 손은 희고 깨끗했으며 손톱은 개암 열매처럼 도톰했고, 옷을 입는 솜씨가 있어 그의 옷이 좋다거나 나쁘다거나, 낡았다거나 멋지다거나 하는 말을 누구도 그에 대해 한 적이 없었다."]], [["Such was Mark Robarts when at the age of twenty-five, or a little more, he married Fanny Monsell.", "스물다섯 살, 혹은 그보다 조금 더 되었을 때 패니 몬셀과 결혼한 마크 로바츠는 그러한 사람이었다."], ["The marriage was celebrated in his own church, for Miss Monsell had no home of her own, and had been staying for the last three months at Framley Court.", "결혼식은 그의 교회에서 치러졌다. 몬셀 양에게는 제 집이 없었고 지난 석 달을 프램리 코트에서 지냈기 때문이다."], ["She was given away by Sir George Meredith, and Lady Lufton herself saw that the wedding was what it should be, with almost as much care as she had bestowed on that of her own daughter.", "신부를 넘겨준 것은 조지 메러디스 경이었고, 레이디 러프턴은 자기 딸의 결혼에 쏟았던 것과 거의 다름없는 정성으로 이 결혼식이 마땅한 모습이 되도록 직접 살폈다."], ["The deed of marrying, the absolute tying of the knot, was performed by the Very Reverend the Dean of Barchester, an esteemed friend of Lady Lufton’s. And Mrs.", "결혼시키는 예식, 곧 매듭을 실제로 묶는 일은 레이디 러프턴의 존경하는 벗인 바체스터의 수석 사제께서 맡아 행했다."], ["Arabin, the dean’s wife, was of the party, though the distance from Barchester to Framley is long, and the roads deep, and no railway lends its assistance.", "그리고 바체스터에서 프램리까지 길이 멀고 도로가 험하고 철도의 도움도 없는데도 수석 사제의 부인 애러빈 부인이 일행에 있었다."], ["And Lord Lufton was there of course; and people protested that he would surely fall in love with one of the four beautiful bridesmaids, of whom Blanche Robarts, the vicar’s second sister, was by common acknowledgment by far the most beautiful.", "물론 러프턴 경도 왔다. 그리고 사람들은 그가 네 명의 아름다운 신부 들러리 가운데 누군가에게 반하고 말 것이라고 입을 모았는데, 그 넷 중에서 교구 사제의 둘째 누이인 블랜치 로바츠가 누구나 인정하듯 단연 가장 아름다웠다."]], [["And there was there another and a younger sister of Mark’s—who did not officiate at the ceremony, though she was present—and of whom no prediction was made, seeing that she was then only sixteen, but of whom mention is made here, as it will come to pass that my readers will know her hereafter.", "그리고 거기에는 마크의 또 다른, 더 어린 누이도 있었다. 예식에서 소임을 맡지는 않았으나 자리에는 있었고, 그때 겨우 열여섯 살이었으므로 그녀를 두고는 아무런 예측도 나오지 않았지만, 훗날 내 독자들이 그녀를 알게 될 터이므로 여기에 언급해 둔다."], ["Her name was Lucy Robarts.", "그녀의 이름은 루시 로바츠였다."]], [["And then the vicar and his wife went off on their wedding tour, the old curate taking care of the Framley souls the while.", "그러고 나서 교구 사제와 그의 아내는 신혼여행을 떠났고, 그동안 늙은 부목사가 프램리의 영혼들을 돌보았다."]], [["And in due time they returned; and after a further interval, in due course, a child was born to them; and then another; and after that came the period at which we will begin our story.", "그리고 때가 되어 그들은 돌아왔다. 얼마간의 세월이 더 지나 때가 차자 그들에게 아이가 태어났고, 그다음에 또 하나 태어났다."], ["But before doing so, may I not assert that all men were right in saying all manner of good things to the Devonshire physician, and in praising his luck in having such a son?", "그러고 나서 우리가 이야기를 시작하려는 시기가 왔다. 그러나 시작하기 전에, 사람들이 모두 데번셔의 그 의사에게 온갖 좋은 말을 하고 그런 아들을 둔 그의 운을 칭송한 것이 옳았다고 내가 주장해도 되지 않겠는가?"]], [["“You were up at the house to-day, I suppose?”", "“오늘 그 집에 올라갔었지?”"], ["said Mark to his wife, as he sat stretching himself in an easy chair in the drawing-room, before the fire, previously to his dressing for dinner.", "마크가 아내에게 말했다. 그는 저녁 식사 옷을 갈아입기 전에 응접실 난롯불 앞 안락의자에 몸을 길게 뻗고 앉아 있었다."], ["It was a November evening, and he had been out all day, and on such occasions the aptitude for delay in dressing is very powerful.", "십일월 저녁이었고 그는 하루 종일 밖에 있었으니, 그런 때에 옷 갈아입기를 미루려는 마음은 아주 강하다."], ["A strong-minded man goes direct from the hall-door to his chamber without encountering the temptation of the drawing-room fire.", "마음이 굳센 사람은 현관문에서 곧장 제 방으로 올라가 응접실 난롯불의 유혹을 만나지 않는다."]], [["“No; but Lady Lufton was down here.”", "“아니요, 그런데 레이디 러프턴이 여기 내려오셨어요.”"]], [["“Full of arguments in favour of Sarah Thompson?”", "“세라 톰프슨을 편드는 논거로 잔뜩 무장하고?”"]], [["“Exactly so, Mark.”", "“그래요, 딱 그랬어요, 마크.”"]], [["“And what did you say about Sarah Thompson?”", "“그래서 세라 톰프슨에 대해 당신은 뭐라고 했어?”"]], [["“Very little as coming from myself; but I did hint that you thought, or that I thought that you thought, that one of the regular trained schoolmistresses would be better.”", "“제 뜻으로는 별말 안 했어요. 하지만 당신이 그렇게 생각한다고, 아니 당신이 그렇게 생각하는 것 같다고, 정식으로 훈련받은 여교사가 더 나을 거라고 슬쩍 비추기는 했지요.”"]], [["“But her ladyship did not agree?”", "“그런데 마님은 동의하지 않았지?”"]], [["“Well, I won’t exactly say that;—though I think that perhaps she did not.”", "“글쎄, 꼭 그렇다고는 하지 않겠어요. 하기야 아마 동의하지 않으신 것 같기는 해요.”"]], [["“I am sure she did not.", "“동의하지 않은 게 분명해."], ["When she has a point to carry, she is very fond of carrying it.”", "그분은 관철할 뜻이 있으면 관철하기를 아주 좋아하니까.”"]], [["“But then, Mark, her points are generally so good.”", "“하지만 마크, 그분의 뜻은 대체로 훌륭하잖아요.”"]], [["“But, you see, in this affair of the school she is thinking more of her protégée than she does of the children.”", "“그래도 알겠지, 이 학교 문제에서 그분은 아이들보다 자기 피후견인을 더 생각하고 있어.”"]], [["“Tell her that, and I am sure she will give way.”", "“그 말씀을 그분께 하세요. 그러면 분명히 물러서실 거예요.”"]], [["And then again they were both silent.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 다시 말이 없었다."], ["And the vicar having thoroughly warmed himself, as far as this might be done by facing the fire, turned round and began the operation à tergo.", "그리고 교구 사제는 난롯불을 마주하는 것으로 할 수 있는 만큼 몸을 충분히 데우고 나서, 몸을 돌려 이번에는 뒤쪽으로 같은 작업을 시작했다."]], [["“Come, Mark, it is twenty minutes past six.", "“여보, 마크, 여섯 시 이십 분이에요."], ["Will you go and dress?”", "가서 옷 갈아입으시겠어요?”"]], [["“I’ll tell you what, Fanny: she must have her way about Sarah Thompson.", "“내 생각을 말해 줄게, 패니. 세라 톰프슨 일은 그분 뜻대로 하시게 해야 해."], ["You can see her to-morrow and tell her so.”", "내일 그분을 만나서 그렇게 말씀드려.”"]], [["“I am sure, Mark, I would not give way, if I thought it wrong.", "“정말이지 마크, 저라면 잘못된 일이라고 생각되면 물러서지 않겠어요."], ["Nor would she expect it.”", "그분도 그러기를 바라지는 않으실 거예요.”"]], [["“If I persist this time, I shall certainly have to yield the next; and then the next may probably be more important.”", "“이번에 내가 고집을 부리면 다음번에는 틀림없이 양보해야 할 텐데, 그다음 일은 아마 더 중요할지도 몰라.”"]], [["“But if it’s wrong, Mark?”", "“그렇지만 그게 잘못된 일이라면요, 마크?”"]], [["“I didn’t say it was wrong.", "“잘못됐다고는 하지 않았어."], ["Besides, if it is wrong, wrong in some infinitesimal degree, one must put up with it.", "게다가 잘못됐다 해도, 아주 미미한 정도로 잘못됐다면 참아야지."], ["Sarah Thompson is very respectable; the only question is whether she can teach.”", "세라 톰프슨은 아주 점잖은 사람이야. 문제는 오직 그녀가 가르칠 수 있느냐 하는 것뿐이지.”"]], [["The young wife, though she did not say so, had some idea that her husband was in error.", "젊은 아내는 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 남편이 틀렸다는 생각이 좀 들었다."], ["It is true that one must put up with wrong, with a great deal of wrong.", "잘못은 참아야 하는 것이 사실이고, 그것도 아주 많은 잘못을 참아야 하는 것이 사실이다."], ["But no one need put up with wrong that he can remedy.", "그러나 자기가 고칠 수 있는 잘못을 참아야 할 사람은 아무도 없다."], ["Why should he, the vicar, consent to receive an incompetent teacher for the parish children, when he was able to procure one that was competent?", "유능한 교사를 구할 수 있는데 왜 그가, 교구 사제가, 교구 아이들에게 무능한 교사를 받아들이는 데 동의해야 하는가?"], ["In such a case,—so thought Mrs. Robarts to herself,—she would have fought the matter out with Lady Lufton.", "그런 경우라면, 로바츠 부인은 혼자 그렇게 생각했다, 자기는 레이디 러프턴과 끝까지 싸웠을 것이다."]], [["On the next morning, however, she did as she was bid, and signified to the dowager that all objection to Sarah Thompson would be withdrawn.", "그러나 이튿날 아침 그녀는 시킨 대로 했고, 세라 톰프슨에 대한 반대는 모두 철회하겠다고 그 미망인에게 알렸다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["I was sure he would agree with me,”", "그이가 그녀가 어떤 사람인지 알게 되면 내 뜻에 동의할 줄 알았어요.”"], ["said her ladyship, “when he learned what sort of person she is.", "마님이 말했다. “설명만 하면 된다고 생각했지요.”"], ["I know I had only to explain;”—and then she plumed her feathers, and was very gracious; for, to tell the truth, Lady Lufton did not like to be opposed in things which concerned the parish nearly.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 깃을 다듬으며 아주 너그러워졌다. 사실을 말하자면 레이디 러프턴은 교구에 깊이 관계되는 일에서 반대당하는 것을 좋아하지 않았다."]], [["“And, Fanny,”", "“그리고 패니,”"], ["said Lady Lufton, in her kindest manner, “you are not going anywhere on Saturday, are you?”", "레이디 러프턴이 가장 다정한 어조로 말했다. “토요일에 어디 갈 일은 없지요?”"]], [["“No, I think not.”", "“네, 없을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Then you must come to us.", "“그러면 우리에게 와야 해요."], ["Justinia is to be here, you know”—Lady Meredith was named Justinia—“and you and Mr.", "저스티니아가 여기 올 거예요, 알지요” — 레이디 메러디스의 이름이 저스티니아였다 — “그러니 당신과 로바츠 씨는 월요일까지 우리와 함께 지내는 게 좋겠어요."], ["Robarts had better stay with us till Monday.", "그이는 일요일에 작은 서재를 혼자 다 쓰면 돼요."], ["He can have the little book-room all to himself on Sunday.", "메러디스 부부는 월요일에 떠나요."], ["The Merediths go on Monday; and Justinia won’t be happy if you are not with her.”", "그리고 저스티니아는 당신이 함께 있지 않으면 즐겁지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["It would be unjust to say that Lady Lufton had determined not to invite the Robartses if she were not allowed to have her own way about Sarah Thompson.", "세라 톰프슨 일에서 자기 뜻대로 하도록 허락받지 못했다면 레이디 러프턴이 로바츠네를 초대하지 않기로 마음먹었을 것이라고 말하는 것은 부당하겠다."], ["But such would have been the result.", "그러나 결과는 그리 되었을 것이다."], ["As it was, however, she was all kindness; and when Mrs.", "어쨌든 지금은 그녀가 온통 친절했다."], ["Robarts made some little excuse, saying that she was afraid she must return home in the evening, because of the children, Lady Lufton declared that there was room enough at Framley Court for baby and nurse, and so settled the matter in her own way, with a couple of nods and three taps of her umbrella.", "그래서 로바츠 부인이 아이들 때문에 저녁에는 집에 돌아가야 할 것 같다며 조금 사양의 말을 하자, 레이디 러프턴은 프램리 코트에는 아기와 유모가 있을 방도 넉넉하다고 단언하고, 고개를 두 번 끄덕이고 우산으로 세 번 톡톡 두드려 그 일을 자기 방식대로 정리해 버렸다."]], [["This was on a Tuesday morning, and on the same evening, before dinner, the vicar again seated himself in the same chair before the drawing-room fire, as soon as he had seen his horse led into the stable.", "이것은 화요일 아침의 일이었고, 같은 날 저녁 식사 전에 교구 사제는 자기 말이 마구간으로 끌려 들어가는 것을 보자마자 다시 응접실 난롯불 앞 그 의자에 앉았다."]], [["“Mark,”", "“마크,” 아내가 말했다."], ["said his wife, “the Merediths are to be at Framley on Saturday and Sunday; and I have promised that we will go up and stay over till Monday.”", "“메러디스 부부가 토요일과 일요일에 프램리에 와 있을 거예요. 그래서 우리가 올라가서 월요일까지 지내겠다고 약속했어요.”"]], [["“You don’t mean it!", "“그럴 리가!"], ["Goodness gracious, how provoking!”", "아이고, 이런 난처한 일이!”"]], [["“Why?", "“왜요?"], ["I thought you wouldn’t mind it.", "당신도 싫어하지 않을 줄 알았어요."], ["And Justinia would think it unkind if I were not there.”", "그리고 제가 거기 없으면 저스티니아가 서운하게 여길 거예요.”"]], [["“You can go, my dear, and of course will go.", "“당신은 가면 돼, 물론 가야지."], ["But as for me, it is impossible.”", "하지만 나는 안 돼, 그건 불가능해.”"]], [["“But why, love?”", "“그렇지만 왜요, 여보?”"]], [["“Why?", "“왜냐고?"], ["Just now, at the school-house, I answered a letter that was brought to me from Chaldicotes.", "방금 학교에서, 콜디코츠에서 온 편지에 답장을 썼어."], ["Sowerby insists on my going over there for a week or so; and I have said that I would.”", "사워비가 한 주쯤 거기 와 있으라고 우기더군. 그래서 그러겠다고 했어.”"]], [["“Go to Chaldicotes for a week, Mark?”", "“콜디코츠에 한 주씩이나 가요, 마크?”"]], [["“I believe I have even consented to ten days.”", "“열흘까지도 승낙한 것 같아.”"]], [["“And be away two Sundays?”", "“그러면 일요일을 두 번 비우게요?”"]], [["“No, Fanny, only one.", "“아니야, 패니, 한 번뿐이야."], ["Don’t be so censorious.”", "그렇게 책잡지 마.”"]], [["“Don’t call me censorious, Mark; you know I am not so.", "“저를 책잡는 사람이라고 하지 마세요, 마크. 제가 그렇지 않다는 걸 아시잖아요."], ["But I am so sorry.", "하지만 정말 안타까워요."], ["It is just what Lady Lufton won’t like.", "하필 레이디 러프턴이 싫어하실 일이잖아요."], ["Besides, you were away in Scotland two Sundays last month.”", "게다가 지난달에도 스코틀랜드에 가 계셔서 일요일을 두 번 비우셨고요.”"]], [["“In September, Fanny.", "“구월이었어, 패니."], ["And that is being censorious.”", "그리고 그게 바로 책잡는 거야.”"]], [["“Oh, but, Mark, dear Mark; don’t say so.", "“아, 하지만 마크, 마크, 그렇게 말씀하지 마세요."], ["You know I don’t mean it.", "제가 그런 뜻이 아니라는 걸 아시잖아요."], ["But Lady Lufton does not like those Chaldicotes people.", "하지만 레이디 러프턴은 그 콜디코츠 사람들을 좋아하지 않으세요."], ["You know Lord Lufton was with you the last time you were there; and how annoyed she was!”", "지난번에 거기 가셨을 때는 러프턴 경도 함께 계셨잖아요. 그때 그분이 얼마나 언짢아하셨는지 아시죠!”"]], [["“Lord Lufton won’t be with me now, for he is still in Scotland.", "“이번에는 러프턴 경이 나와 함께 있지 않아. 아직 스코틀랜드에 있으니까."], ["And the reason why I am going is this: Harold Smith and his wife will be there, and I am very anxious to know more of them.", "그리고 내가 가는 이유는 이거야. 해럴드 스미스와 그 부인이 거기 올 텐데, 나는 그들을 더 알아 두고 싶어."], ["I have no doubt that Harold Smith will be in the government some day, and I cannot afford to neglect such a man’s acquaintance.”", "해럴드 스미스는 틀림없이 언젠가 정부에 들어갈 텐데, 그런 사람과의 교분을 소홀히 할 수는 없지.”"]], [["“But, Mark, what do you want of any government?”", "“하지만 마크, 정부가 당신에게 무슨 소용이에요?”"]], [["“Well, Fanny, of course I am bound to say that I want nothing; neither in one sense do I; but nevertheless, I shall go and meet the Harold Smiths.”", "“글쎄, 패니, 물론 나는 아무것도 바라지 않는다고 말해야 하겠지. 어떤 뜻에서는 정말 바라지도 않아. 그렇지만 그래도 나는 가서 해럴드 스미스 부부를 만날 거야.”"]], [["“Could you not be back before Sunday?”", "“일요일 전에 돌아오실 수는 없어요?”"]], [["“I have promised to preach at Chaldicotes.", "“콜디코츠에서 설교하겠다고 약속했어."], ["Harold Smith is going to lecture at Barchester, about the Australasian archipelago, and I am to preach a charity sermon on the same subject.", "해럴드 스미스가 바체스터에서 오스트랄라시아 군도에 대해 강연을 할 거고, 나는 같은 주제로 자선 설교를 하는 거야."], ["They want to send out more missionaries.”", "선교사를 더 내보내고 싶어 하거든.”"]], [["“A charity sermon at Chaldicotes!”", "“콜디코츠에서 자선 설교라니!”"]], [["“And why not?", "“왜 안 되지?"], ["The house will be quite full, you know; and I dare say the Arabins will be there.”", "집이 손님으로 꽉 찰 텐데, 알지, 애러빈 부부도 아마 올 거야.”"]], [["“I think not; Mrs.", "“아닐 것 같아요."], ["Arabin may get on with Mrs. Harold Smith, though I doubt that; but I’m sure she’s not fond of Mrs.", "애러빈 부인이 해럴드 스미스 부인과는 잘 지낼지 몰라도, 그것도 의심스럽지만, 스미스 부인의 오라비를 좋아하지 않는 건 분명해요."], ["Smith’s brother. I don’t think she would stay at Chaldicotes.”", "콜디코츠에 머물지는 않으실 거예요.”"]], [["“And the bishop will probably be there for a day or two.”", "“그리고 주교도 아마 하루 이틀은 거기 계실 거야.”"]], [["“That is much more likely, Mark.", "“그건 훨씬 더 그럴듯하네요, 마크."], ["If the pleasure of meeting Mrs. Proudie is taking you to Chaldicotes, I have not a word more to say.”", "프라우디 부인을 만나는 즐거움 때문에 콜디코츠에 가시는 거라면, 저는 더 말할 것이 없어요.”"]], [["“I am not a bit more fond of Mrs. Proudie than you are, Fanny,”", "“나도 프라우디 부인을 좋아하는 정도가 당신보다 조금도 더하지 않아, 패니.”"], ["said the vicar, with something like vexation in the tone of his voice, for he thought that his wife was hard upon him.", "교구 사제가 목소리에 짜증 같은 것을 담아 말했다. 아내가 자기를 심하게 대한다고 생각한 것이다."], ["“But it is generally thought that a parish clergyman does well to meet his bishop now and then.", "“하지만 교구 성직자가 주교를 이따금 만나 두는 것이 좋다는 게 일반적인 생각이야."], ["And as I was invited there, especially to preach while all these people are staying at the place, I could not well refuse.”", "그리고 그 사람들이 다 그곳에 머무는 동안 특히 설교를 하라고 초청을 받았으니, 딱 거절할 수도 없었어.”"], ["And then he got up, and taking his candlestick, escaped to his dressing-room.", "그러고 나서 그는 일어나 촛대를 들고 옷 갈아입는 방으로 달아났다."]], [["“But what am I to say to Lady Lufton?”", "“하지만 레이디 러프턴께는 뭐라고 말씀드려야 해요?”"], ["his wife said to him, in the course of the evening.", "저녁나절에 아내가 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Just write her a note, and tell her that you find I had promised to preach at Chaldicotes next Sunday.", "“그냥 짧게 편지를 써서, 내가 다음 일요일에 콜디코츠에서 설교하겠다고 약속한 것을 당신이 알게 되었다고 말해."], ["You’ll go, of course?”", "당신은 물론 가는 거지?”"]], [["“Yes: but I know she’ll be annoyed.", "“네, 하지만 그분이 언짢아하실 걸 알아요."], ["You were away the last time she had people there.”", "지난번에 그분이 손님을 모았을 때도 당신은 나가 계셨잖아요.”"]], [["“It can’t be helped.", "“어쩔 수 없어."], ["She must put it down against Sarah Thompson.", "그분은 그걸 세라 톰프슨 건과 상쇄해야 해."], ["She ought not to expect to win always.”", "언제나 이기기를 바라서는 안 되지.”"]], [["“I should not have minded it, if she had lost, as you call it, about Sarah Thompson.", "“세라 톰프슨 일에서, 당신 말대로 그분이 지셨다면 저는 마음에 두지 않았을 거예요."], ["That was a case in which you ought to have had your own way.”", "그건 당신이 뜻을 관철해야 했던 일이에요.”"]], [["“And this other is a case in which I shall have it.", "“그리고 이 다른 일은 내가 뜻을 관철할 일이야."], ["It’s a pity that there should be such a difference; isn’t it?”", "이렇게 차이가 나는 게 딱하지, 그렇지 않아?”"]], [["Then the wife perceived that, vexed as she was, it would be better that she should say nothing further; and before she went to bed, she wrote the note to Lady Lufton, as her husband recommended.", "그러자 아내는, 속이 상하기는 했으나 더 말하지 않는 게 낫겠다고 깨달았다. 그리고 잠자리에 들기 전에 남편이 권한 대로 레이디 러프턴에게 편지를 썼다."]], [["# CHAPTER II. THE FRAMLEY SET, AND THE CHALDICOTES SET.", "# 제2장. 프램리 패와 콜디코츠 패"]], [["It will be necessary that I should say a word or two of some of the people named in the few preceding pages, and also of the localities in which they lived.", "앞의 몇 페이지에서 이름을 든 사람들 가운데 몇몇에 대해, 그리고 그들이 살던 고장에 대해서도, 내가 한두 마디 해 두어야 하겠다."]], [["Of Lady Lufton herself enough, perhaps, has been written to introduce her to my readers.", "레이디 러프턴 자신에 대해서는 독자에게 그녀를 소개할 만큼은 이미 적었을 것이다."], ["The Framley property belonged to her son; but as Lufton Park—an ancient ramshackle place in another county—had heretofore been the family residence of the Lufton family, Framley Court had been apportioned to her for her residence for life.", "프램리의 재산은 그녀의 아들 것이었다. 그러나 러프턴 파크—다른 주에 있는, 오래되어 다 낡아 빠진 집—가 여태까지 러프턴 집안의 본가였으므로, 프램리 코트는 그녀가 살아 있는 동안 머물 거처로 그녀에게 배정되어 있었다."], ["Lord Lufton himself was still unmarried; and as he had no establishment at Lufton Park—which indeed had not been inhabited since his grandfather died—he lived with his mother when it suited him to live anywhere in that neighbourhood.", "러프턴 경 자신은 아직 미혼이었다. 그리고 러프턴 파크에는—실은 조부가 죽은 뒤로 사람이 든 적이 없었다—살림을 차려 놓지 않았으므로, 그 근처에 머무는 것이 마음에 맞을 때면 그는 어머니와 함께 지냈다."], ["The widow would fain have seen more of him than he allowed her to do.", "미망인은 아들이 허락해 주는 것보다 훨씬 더 자주 그를 보고 싶어 했다."], ["He had a shooting-lodge in Scotland, and apartments in London, and a string of horses in Leicestershire—much to the disgust of the county gentry around him, who held that their own hunting was as good as any that England could afford.", "그는 스코틀랜드에 사냥용 별장을 두었고, 런던에 아파트를 두었고, 레스터셔에 말 여러 필을 두었다—자기들 사냥이 영국의 어느 사냥에도 뒤지지 않는다고 여기던 주변 시골 지주들의 비위를 몹시 거스르면서."], ["His lordship, however, paid his subscription to the East Barsetshire pack, and then thought himself at liberty to follow his own pleasure as to his own amusement.", "그러나 러프턴 경은 동 바셋주 사냥회에 회비를 냈고, 그러고 나면 자기가 무엇을 즐길지는 자기 뜻대로 해도 좋다고 여겼다."]], [["Framley itself was a pleasant country place, having about it nothing of seignorial dignity or grandeur, but possessing everything necessary for the comfort of country life.", "프램리 자체는 기분 좋은 시골이었다. 영주다운 위엄이나 웅장함이라 할 것은 아무것도 없었으나, 시골 생활의 안락에 필요한 것은 다 갖추고 있었다."], ["The house was a low building of two stories, built at different periods, and devoid of all pretensions to any style of architecture; but the rooms, though not lofty, were warm and comfortable, and the gardens were trim and neat beyond all others in the county.", "집은 이층으로 된 낮은 건물로, 시기를 달리하여 지어졌고, 무슨 건축 양식이라 내세울 만한 것은 전혀 없었다. 그래도 방들은 천장이 높지는 않아도 따뜻하고 편안했으며, 정원은 주 안의 다른 어느 정원보다도 손질이 잘 되어 단정했다."], ["Indeed, it was for its gardens only that Framley Court was celebrated.", "실상 프램리 코트가 이름난 것은 오직 그 정원 때문이었다."]], [["Village there was none, properly speaking.", "마을이라 할 것은, 엄밀히 말하면 없었다."], ["The high road went winding about through the Framley paddocks, shrubberies, and wood-skirted home fields, for a mile and a half, not two hundred yards of which ran in a straight line; and there was a cross-road which passed down through the domain, whereby there came to be a locality called Framley Cross.", "큰길이 프램리의 방목장과 관목숲과 숲이 둘러싼 안쪽 들판 사이를 한 마일 반 동안 구불구불 지나갔는데, 그 가운데 이백 야드도 곧게 뻗은 데가 없었다. 그리고 영지를 가로질러 내려가는 갈림길이 하나 있어서, 그 덕에 프램리 크로스라 불리는 곳이 생겼다."], ["Here stood the “Lufton Arms,”", "여기에 “러프턴 암스” 여관이 서 있었고, 또 여기 프램리 크로스에서 이따금 사냥개들이 모였다."], ["and here, at Framley Cross, the hounds occasionally would meet; for the Framley woods were drawn in spite of the young lord’s truant disposition; and then, at the Cross also, lived the shoemaker, who kept the post-office.", "젊은 러프턴 경이 제 고장 사냥을 등한히 하는 성미였음에도 프램리 숲에서는 몰이가 이루어졌기 때문이다. 그리고 이 크로스에는 우편취급소를 맡고 있는 구두장이도 살았다."]], [["Framley church was distant from this just a quarter of a mile, and stood immediately opposite to the chief entrance to Framley Court.", "프램리 교회는 여기서 딱 사분의 일 마일 떨어져 있었고, 프램리 코트의 정문 바로 맞은편에 서 있었다."], ["It was but a mean, ugly building, having been erected about a hundred years since, when all churches then built were made to be mean and ugly; nor was it large enough for the congregation, some of whom were thus driven to the dissenting chapels, the Sions and Ebenezers, which had got themselves established on each side of the parish, in putting down which Lady Lufton thought that her pet parson was hardly as energetic as he might be.", "그것은 초라하고 못생긴 건물에 지나지 않았는데, 백 년쯤 전에 세워진 것이라 그때 지어진 교회는 다 초라하고 못생기게 만들어졌던 것이다. 게다가 신자를 다 들일 만큼 크지도 못해서, 더러는 그 때문에 교구 양쪽에 자리를 잡은 비국교회 예배당, 곧 시온이니 에벤에셀이니 하는 데로 밀려갔다."], ["It was, therefore, a matter near to Lady Lufton’s heart to see a new church built, and she was urgent in her eloquence, both with her son and with the vicar, to have this good work commenced.", "레이디 러프턴은 그것들을 눌러 없애는 일에 자기가 아끼는 목사가 낼 수 있는 만큼의 열심을 내지 않는다고 여겼다. 그러므로 새 교회가 세워지는 것을 보는 일은 레이디 러프턴의 마음에 가까이 놓인 문제였고, 그녀는 아들에게나 교구 사제에게나 이 좋은 일을 시작하라고 간곡히 말을 쏟았다."]], [["Beyond the church, but close to it, were the boys’ school and girls’ school, two distinct buildings, which owed their erection to Lady Lufton’s energy; then came a neat little grocer’s shop, the neat grocer being the clerk and sexton, and the neat grocer’s wife, the pew-opener in the church.", "교회를 지나 그 가까이에 남학교와 여학교가 있었는데, 따로 떨어진 두 채로, 레이디 러프턴이 힘을 써서 세워진 것이었다. 그다음에는 아담한 식료품 가게가 나왔다."], ["Podgens was their name, and they were great favourites with her ladyship, both having been servants up at the house.", "그 아담한 식료품 장수는 교회 서기 겸 교회지기였고, 아담한 식료품 장수의 아내는 교회에서 신자석 문을 열어 주는 사람이었다. 포진스가 그들의 성이었고, 둘 다 전에 큰 집에서 하인으로 있었던 터라 레이디 러프턴이 몹시 아끼는 사람들이었다."]], [["And here the road took a sudden turn to the left, turning, as it were, away from Framley Court; and just beyond the turn was the vicarage, so that there was a little garden path running from the back of the vicarage grounds into the churchyard, cutting the Podgenses off into an isolated corner of their own;—from whence, to tell the truth, the vicar would have been glad to banish them and their cabbages, could he have had the power to do so.", "그리고 여기서 길은 갑자기 왼쪽으로 꺾여, 말하자면 프램리 코트에서 등을 돌리며 돌아갔다. 그 꺾인 데를 막 지나면 목사관이 있어서, 목사관 뜰 뒤에서 교회 묘지로 이어지는 작은 정원 길이 하나 났고, 그 길이 포진스네를 그들만의 외딴 구석으로 떼어 놓았다."], ["For has not the small vineyard of Naboth been always an eyesore to neighbouring potentates?", "사실을 말하면, 교구 사제는 힘만 있었다면 그들과 그들의 양배추를 그 구석에서 기꺼이 몰아냈을 것이다. 나봇의 작은 포도밭은 예부터 이웃한 권세가의 눈에 늘 가시였지 않은가?"]], [["The potentate in this case had as little excuse as Ahab, for nothing in the parsonage way could be more perfect than his parsonage.", "이 경우의 권세가에게는 아합만큼이나 변명거리가 없었다. 목사관이라는 점에서 그의 목사관보다 더 완전할 수 있는 것은 없었기 때문이다."], ["It had all the details requisite for the house of a moderate gentleman with moderate means, and none of those expensive superfluities which immoderate gentlemen demand, or which themselves demand—immoderate means.", "그것은 적당한 재산을 가진 적당한 신사의 집에 필요한 세목을 다 갖추었고, 적당함을 넘어선 신사들이 요구하는—또는 그것들 자신이 적당함을 넘어선 재산을 요구하는—값비싼 사치는 하나도 없었다."], ["And then the gardens and paddocks were exactly suited to it; and everything was in good order;—not exactly new, so as to be raw and uncovered, and redolent of workmen; but just at that era of their existence in which newness gives way to comfortable homeliness.", "그리고 정원과 방목장은 집에 꼭 어울렸으며, 모든 것이 잘 정돈되어 있었다. 아주 새것이어서 설익고 헐벗고 인부 냄새가 나는 것은 아니었고, 새것다움이 편안한 소박함으로 넘어가는 바로 그 시기에 놓여 있었다."]], [["Other village at Framley there was none.", "프램리에 다른 마을은 없었다."], ["At the back of the Court, up one of those cross-roads, there was another small shop or two, and there was a very neat cottage residence, in which lived the widow of a former curate, another protégé of Lady Lufton’s; and there was a big, staring brick house, in which the present curate lived; but this was a full mile distant from the church, and farther from Framley Court, standing on that cross-road which runs from Framley Cross in a direction away from the mansion. This gentleman, the Rev.", "코트 뒤편, 그 갈림길 가운데 하나를 따라 올라가면 작은 가게가 한둘 더 있었고, 아주 아담한 시골집 한 채가 있어서 전임 부목사의 미망인이 살았는데, 그녀 또한 레이디 러프턴이 거둔 사람이었다."], ["Evan Jones, might, from his age, have been the vicar’s father; but he had been for many years curate of Framley; and though he was personally disliked by Lady Lufton, as being Low Church in his principles, and unsightly in his appearance, nevertheless, she would not urge his removal.", "그리고 크고 눈에 거슬리는 벽돌집이 하나 있어 지금의 부목사가 살았다. 그러나 이 집은 교회에서 꼬박 한 마일이나 떨어져 있었고 프램리 코트에서는 더 멀었으며, 프램리 크로스에서 저택과 반대 방향으로 뻗어 가는 그 갈림길에 서 있었다."], ["He had two or three pupils in that large brick house, and if turned out from these and from his curacy, might find it difficult to establish himself elsewhere.", "이 신사, 곧 에번 존스 목사는 나이로 보면 교구 사제의 아버지라 해도 될 만했다. 그러나 그는 여러 해 동안 프램리의 부목사였고, 신조가 저교회파이고 생김새가 보기 좋지 않아 레이디 러프턴이 개인적으로 그를 싫어하기는 했으나, 그래도 그녀는 그를 내보내라고 다그치지는 않았다."], ["On this account mercy was extended to the Rev. E.", "그는 그 큰 벽돌집에서 두세 명의 학생을 가르쳤는데, 그 일에서도 부목사 자리에서도 밀려난다면 다른 데서 자리를 잡기가 어려울 것이었다."], ["Jones, and, in spite of his red face and awkward big feet, he was invited to dine at Framley Court, with his plain daughter, once in every three months.", "그런 까닭에 에번 존스 목사에게는 자비가 베풀어졌고, 붉은 얼굴과 어색하게 큰 발에도 불구하고 그는 석 달에 한 번씩 그 수수한 딸과 함께 프램리 코트의 만찬에 초대되었다."]], [["Over and above these, there was hardly a house in the parish of Framley, outside the bounds of Framley Court, except those of farmers and farm labourers; and yet the parish was of large extent.", "이 밖에는, 프램리 코트의 경계 밖 프램리 교구에 농부와 농사꾼의 집 말고는 집이라 할 것이 거의 없었다. 그러면서도 교구는 넓이가 컸다."]], [["Framley is in the eastern division of the county of Barsetshire, which, as all the world knows, is, politically speaking, as true blue a county as any in England.", "프램리는 바셋주의 동부에 있는데, 이 주가 세상이 다 알듯이 정치로 말하면 영국의 어느 주에도 못지않은 골수 보수의 땅이다."], ["There have been backslidings even here, it is true; but then, in what county have there not been such backslidings?", "여기서도 등을 돌린 자가 있었던 것은 사실이다. 그러나 그렇다면 그렇게 등을 돌린 자가 없었던 주가 어디 있는가?"], ["Where, in these pinchbeck days, can we hope to find the old agricultural virtue in all its purity?", "이 싸구려 도금 같은 시대에 옛 농촌의 덕을 조금도 섞이지 않은 채로 찾아보기를 어디서 바랄 수 있겠는가?"], ["But, among those backsliders, I regret to say, that men now reckon Lord Lufton.", "그런데 유감이지만 그 배신자들 가운데 사람들이 이제 러프턴 경을 넣는다."], ["Not that he is a violent Whig, or perhaps that he is a Whig at all.", "그가 과격한 휘그라거나, 아니 휘그이기라도 하다는 말은 아니다."], ["But he jeers and sneers at the old county doings; declares, when solicited on the subject, that, as far as he is concerned, Mr.", "다만 그는 이 주의 오래된 일들을 두고 비웃고 코웃음을 친다. 이 문제로 청을 받으면, 자기로서는 브라이트 씨가 원한다면 이 주의 의원으로 앉아도 좋다고 선언한다."], ["Bright may sit for the county, if he pleases; and alleges, that being unfortunately a peer, he has no right even to interest himself in the question.", "그리고 불행히도 자기는 귀족이니 이 문제에 관심을 가질 권리조차 없다고 내세운다."], ["All this is deeply regretted, for, in the old days, there was no portion of the county more decidedly true blue than that Framley district; and, indeed, up to the present day, the dowager is able to give an occasional helping hand.", "이 모든 일이 깊이 유감스러운 것은, 옛날에는 그 프램리 지역보다 더 확실하게 골수 보수인 데가 이 주에 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 실은 오늘날까지도 노부인은 이따금 손을 보태 줄 수 있다."]], [["Chaldicotes is the seat of Nathaniel Sowerby, Esq., who, at the moment supposed to be now present, is one of the members for the Western Division of Barsetshire.", "콜디코츠는 나다니엘 사워비 씨의 본거지인데, 그는 지금이라고 가정된 이 시점에 바셋주 서부의 하원 의원 가운데 한 사람이다."], ["But this Western Division can boast none of the fine political attributes which grace its twin brother.", "그러나 이 서부는 그 쌍둥이 형제를 빛내 주는 훌륭한 정치적 미덕이라면 하나도 자랑할 것이 없다."], ["It is decidedly Whig, and is almost governed in its politics by one or two great Whig families.", "이곳은 확실히 휘그이며, 그 정치는 한둘의 대단한 휘그 집안에 거의 지배당하고 있다."]], [["It has been said that Mark Robarts was about to pay a visit to Chaldicotes, and it has been hinted that his wife would have been as well pleased had this not been the case.", "마크 로바츠가 콜디코츠를 찾아가려 한다는 말은 이미 했고, 그렇지 않았더라면 그의 아내가 더 기뻐했을 것이라는 암시도 했다. 그것은 분명히 사실이었다."], ["Such was certainly the fact; for she, dear, prudent, excellent wife as she was, knew that Mr. Sowerby was not the most eligible friend in the world for a young clergyman, and knew, also, that there was but one other house in the whole county the name of which was so distasteful to Lady Lufton.", "그녀는 사랑스럽고 사려 깊고 훌륭한 아내였던 만큼, 사워비 씨가 젊은 성직자에게 세상에서 가장 알맞은 친구는 아니라는 것을 알았고, 또 이 주 전체에서 레이디 러프턴에게 그만큼 이름이 거슬리는 집은 딱 한 채밖에 더 없다는 것도 알았다."], ["The reasons for this were, I may say, manifold.", "그 이유는, 말하자면 여러 겹이었다."], ["In the first place, Mr. Sowerby was a Whig, and was seated in Parliament mainly by the interest of that great Whig autocrat the Duke of Omnium, whose residence was more dangerous even than that of Mr.", "첫째로 사워비 씨는 휘그였고, 주로 저 대단한 휘그 독재자 옴니엄 공작의 후원으로 의석을 얻고 있었다."], ["Sowerby, and whom Lady Lufton regarded as an impersonation of Lucifer upon earth. Mr.", "공작의 집은 사워비 씨의 집보다도 더 위험했고, 레이디 러프턴은 그를 이 땅에 나타난 루시퍼의 화신으로 여겼다."], ["Sowerby, too, was unmarried—as indeed, also, was Lord Lufton, much to his mother’s grief.", "사워비 씨 또한 미혼이었다. 실은 러프턴 경도 그러했고, 그것이 어머니에게는 큰 걱정거리였다."], ["Mr. Sowerby, it is true, was fifty, whereas the young lord was as yet only twenty-six, but, nevertheless, her ladyship was becoming anxious on the subject.", "사워비 씨가 쉰 살인데 젊은 러프턴 경은 아직 스물여섯밖에 되지 않은 것은 사실이지만, 그래도 레이디 러프턴은 그 일로 마음이 조여 오고 있었다."], ["In her mind, every man was bound to marry as soon as he could maintain a wife; and she held an idea—a quite private tenet, of which she was herself but imperfectly conscious—that men in general were inclined to neglect this duty for their own selfish gratifications, that the wicked ones encouraged the more innocent in this neglect, and that many would not marry at all, were not an unseen coercion exercised against them by the other sex.", "그녀의 생각으로는 남자란 아내를 먹여 살릴 수 있게 되면 곧 결혼해야 마땅했다. 그리고 그녀는 한 가지 생각을 품고 있었다—아주 사사로운 신념이어서 그녀 자신도 온전히 의식하지는 못했다—남자들이 대체로 제 이기적인 즐거움 때문에 이 의무를 소홀히 하려 든다는 것, 못된 자들이 더 순진한 자들을 부추겨 그렇게 소홀히 하게 만든다는 것, 그리고 다른 쪽 성이 보이지 않는 강제를 그들에게 가하지 않는다면 많은 남자가 아예 결혼하지 않을 것이라는 생각이었다."], ["The Duke of Omnium was the very head of all such sinners, and Lady Lufton greatly feared that her son might be made subject to the baneful Omnium influence, by means of Mr. Sowerby and Chaldicotes.", "옴니엄 공작은 그런 죄인들 가운데 바로 우두머리였고, 레이디 러프턴은 사워비 씨와 콜디코츠를 통해 자기 아들이 그 해로운 옴니엄의 영향 아래 놓이게 될까 몹시 두려워했다."]], [["And then Mr. Sowerby was known to be a very poor man, with a very large estate.", "게다가 사워비 씨는 아주 큰 영지를 가지고도 아주 가난한 사람으로 알려져 있었다."], ["He had wasted, men said, much on electioneering, and more in gambling.", "사람들 말로는 그가 선거 운동에 많이 쏟아 버렸고, 도박에는 더 많이 쏟아 버렸다고 했다."], ["A considerable portion of his property had already gone into the hands of the duke, who, as a rule, bought up everything around him that was to be purchased.", "그의 재산 가운데 상당한 몫이 이미 공작의 손에 넘어가 있었는데, 공작은 으레 제 주변에서 팔 만한 것이면 무엇이든 사들이는 사람이었다."], ["Indeed it was said of him by his enemies, that so covetous was he of Barsetshire property, that he would lead a young neighbour on to his ruin, in order that he might get his land. What—oh!", "실상 그의 적들은 그를 두고, 바셋주의 땅을 어찌나 탐내는지 젊은 이웃의 땅을 얻으려고 그 이웃을 파멸로 끌고 갈 사람이라고 했다."], ["what if he should come to be possessed in this way of any of the fair acres of Framley Court?", "그런데—아! 만일 그가 그런 식으로 프램리 코트의 좋은 땅 얼마간을 차지하게 된다면?"], ["What if he should become possessed of them all?", "만일 그것을 다 차지하게 된다면?"], ["It can hardly be wondered at that Lady Lufton should not like Chaldicotes.", "레이디 러프턴이 콜디코츠를 좋아하지 않은 것을 이상하게 여길 수는 없는 일이다."]], [["The Chaldicotes set, as Lady Lufton called them, were in every way opposed to what a set should be according to her ideas.", "레이디 러프턴이 콜디코츠 패라고 부르던 사람들은, 패라면 마땅히 이러해야 한다는 그녀의 생각에 어느 모로나 어긋났다."], ["She liked cheerful, quiet, well-to-do people, who loved their Church, their country, and their Queen, and who were not too anxious to make a noise in the world.", "그녀는 명랑하고 조용하고 살림이 넉넉한 사람들, 자기 교회와 자기 나라와 자기 여왕을 사랑하고 세상에서 시끄러운 소리를 내는 데 너무 마음을 쓰지 않는 사람들을 좋아했다."], ["She desired that all the farmers round her should be able to pay their rents without trouble, that all the old women should have warm flannel petticoats, that the working men should be saved from rheumatism by healthy food and dry houses, that they should all be obedient to their pastors and masters—temporal as well as spiritual.", "그녀는 주변의 농부들이 모두 어려움 없이 소작료를 낼 수 있기를, 노파들이 모두 따뜻한 플란넬 속치마를 갖기를, 일하는 사람들이 몸에 좋은 음식과 마른 집으로 류머티즘을 면하기를, 그리고 그들이 모두 자기네 목자와 주인에게—영적인 쪽으로나 세속적인 쪽으로나—순종하기를 바랐다."], ["That was her idea of loving her country.", "그것이 나라를 사랑한다는 그녀의 생각이었다."], ["She desired also that the copses should be full of pheasants, the stubble-field of partridges, and the gorse covers of foxes;—in that way, also, she loved her country.", "그녀는 또한 잡목숲에 꿩이 그득하고, 그루터기 밭에 자고가 그득하고, 가시금작화 덤불에 여우가 그득하기를 바랐다. 그런 식으로도 그녀는 나라를 사랑했다."], ["She had ardently longed, during that Crimean war, that the Russians might be beaten—but not by the French, to the exclusion of the English, as had seemed to her to be too much the case; and hardly by the English under the dictatorship of Lord Palmerston.", "저 크림 전쟁 동안 그녀는 러시아가 지기를 간절히 바랐다—그러나 그녀가 보기에는 너무 그런 쪽으로 흐르는 듯했지만, 영국을 제쳐 놓고 프랑스가 이겨서는 안 되었고, 파머스턴 경의 독재 아래 영국이 이기는 것도 별로 마땅치 않았다."], ["Indeed, she had had but little faith in that war after Lord Aberdeen had been expelled.", "실상 애버딘 경이 쫓겨난 뒤로 그녀는 그 전쟁에 믿음이 거의 없었다."], ["If, indeed, Lord Derby could have come in!", "아, 정말이지 더비 경이 들어올 수만 있었다면!"]], [["But now as to this Chaldicotes set.", "그런데 이제 이 콜디코츠 패에 대해 말해 보자."], ["After all, there was nothing so very dangerous about them; for it was in London, not in the country, that Mr.", "결국 그들에게 그리 위험할 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["Sowerby indulged, if he did indulge, his bachelor mal-practices.", "사워비 씨가 총각의 그릇된 짓을 즐겼다면 그것은 시골이 아니라 런던에서였으니까."], ["Speaking of them as a set, the chief offender was Mr. Harold Smith, or perhaps his wife.", "그들을 하나의 패로 놓고 말하면, 가장 큰 죄인은 해럴드 스미스 씨, 아니면 어쩌면 그의 아내였다."], ["He also was a member of Parliament, and, as many thought, a rising man.", "그도 하원 의원이었고, 많은 사람이 보기에 떠오르는 인물이었다."], ["His father had been for many years a debater in the House, and had held high office.", "그의 아버지는 여러 해 동안 의회의 논객이었고 높은 관직을 지냈다."], ["Harold, in early life, had intended himself for the cabinet; and if working hard at his trade could ensure success, he ought to obtain it sooner or later.", "해럴드는 젊었을 때부터 자신을 내각에 앉힐 작정이었다. 그리고 제 일을 부지런히 하는 것으로 성공이 보장된다면 그는 이르든 늦든 그것을 얻어야 마땅했다."], ["He had already filled more than one subordinate station, had been at the Treasury, and for a month or two at the Admiralty, astonishing official mankind by his diligence.", "그는 이미 하급 자리를 한 자리 이상 채워 보았고, 재무부에 있었고, 한두 달은 해군부에 있어서 부지런함으로 관청 사람들을 놀라게 했다."], ["Those last-named few months had been under Lord Aberdeen, with whom he had been forced to retire.", "마지막에 말한 그 몇 달은 애버딘 경 아래였고, 그와 함께 물러날 수밖에 없었다."], ["He was a younger son, and not possessed of any large fortune.", "그는 차남이어서 큰 재산을 가지지 못했다."], ["Politics as a profession was therefore of importance to him. He had in early life married a sister of Mr.", "그러므로 직업으로서의 정치는 그에게 중요했다."], ["Sowerby; and as the lady was some six or seven years older than himself, and had brought with her but a scanty dowry, people thought that in this matter Mr. Harold Smith had not been perspicacious.", "그는 젊었을 때 사워비 씨의 누이와 결혼했는데, 그 부인이 그보다 여섯 일곱 살쯤 위였고 가져온 지참금도 빈약했으므로, 사람들은 이 일에서 해럴드 스미스 씨가 눈이 밝지 못했다고 생각했다."], ["Mr. Harold Smith was not personally a popular man with any party, though some judged him to be eminently useful.", "해럴드 스미스 씨는 어느 당에서도 개인적으로 인기 있는 사람이 아니었으나, 더러는 그가 대단히 유용하다고 판단했다."], ["He was laborious, well-informed, and, on the whole, honest; but he was conceited, long-winded, and pompous.", "그는 부지런하고 아는 것이 많고 대체로 정직했다. 그러나 자만하고 말이 길고 뻐겼다."]], [["Mrs. Harold Smith was the very opposite of her lord.", "해럴드 스미스 부인은 그 남편과 정반대였다."], ["She was a clever, bright woman, good-looking for her time of life—and she was now over forty—with a keen sense of the value of all worldly things, and a keen relish for all the world’s pleasures.", "그녀는 영리하고 재기 있는 여자로, 나이에 비하면 보기 좋았다—이제 마흔이 넘었다—세상 온갖 것의 값을 날카롭게 알아보았고, 세상 온갖 즐거움을 날카롭게 맛보았다."], ["She was neither laborious, nor well-informed, nor perhaps altogether honest—what woman ever understood the necessity or recognized the advantage of political honesty?—but then she was neither dull nor pompous, and if she was conceited, she did not show it.", "그녀는 부지런하지도, 아는 것이 많지도, 어쩌면 온전히 정직하지도 않았다—정치적 정직의 필요를 이해하거나 그 이로움을 알아본 여자가 여태 어디 있었던가?—그러나 그 대신 둔하지도 뻐기지도 않았고, 자만이 있었다 해도 그것을 드러내지는 않았다. 그녀는 남편에 관해서는 실망한 여자였다."], ["She was a disappointed woman, as regards her husband; seeing that she had married him on the speculation that he would at once become politically important; and as yet Mr. Smith had not quite fulfilled the prophecies of his early life.", "그가 당장 정치적으로 중요한 인물이 될 것이라는 셈으로 그와 결혼했는데, 스미스 씨는 아직 젊은 시절의 예언을 다 채우지 못했으니 말이다."]], [["And Lady Lufton, when she spoke of the Chaldicotes set, distinctly included, in her own mind, the Bishop of Barchester, and his wife and daughter.", "그리고 레이디 러프턴은 콜디코츠 패를 말할 때 마음속으로 바체스터의 주교와 그 아내와 딸을 분명히 거기에 넣었다."], ["Seeing that Bishop Proudie was, of course, a man much addicted to religion and to religious thinking, and that Mr. Sowerby himself had no peculiar religious sentiments whatever, there would not at first sight appear to be ground for much intercourse, and perhaps there was not much of such intercourse; but Mrs.", "프라우디 주교는 물론 종교와 종교적 사색에 크게 몸을 담근 사람이고 사워비 씨 자신은 종교적 감정이라 할 것이 아무것도 없었으니, 언뜻 보면 서로 많이 오갈 근거가 있을 것 같지 않고, 어쩌면 그런 왕래가 많지도 않았다."], ["Proudie and Mrs. Harold Smith were firm friends of four or five years’ standing—ever since the Proudies came into the diocese; and therefore the bishop was usually taken to Chaldicotes whenever Mrs.", "그러나 프라우디 부인과 해럴드 스미스 부인은 네다섯 해 된 굳은 벗이었으니—프라우디 부부가 이 교구로 온 뒤로 줄곧 그러했다—그래서 스미스 부인이 오라비를 찾아올 때면 으레 주교도 콜디코츠로 끌려왔다."], ["Smith paid her brother a visit. Now Bishop Proudie was by no means a High Church dignitary, and Lady Lufton had never forgiven him for coming into that diocese.", "그런데 프라우디 주교는 결코 고교회파 고위 성직자가 아니었고, 레이디 러프턴은 그가 이 교구로 들어온 것을 끝내 용서하지 않았다."], ["She had, instinctively, a high respect for the episcopal office; but of Bishop Proudie himself she hardly thought better than she did of Mr.", "그녀는 본능적으로 주교라는 직분에 높은 경의를 품고 있었다."], ["Sowerby, or of that fabricator of evil, the Duke of Omnium. Whenever Mr.", "그러나 프라우디 주교 그 사람에 대해서는 사워비 씨나 저 악의 조작자 옴니엄 공작에 대해서보다 나을 것 없이 생각했다."], ["Robarts would plead that in going anywhere he would have the benefit of meeting the bishop, Lady Lufton would slightly curl her upper lip.", "로바츠 씨가 어디를 가면 주교를 만나는 이로움이 있다고 변명할 때마다 레이디 러프턴은 윗입술을 살짝 비틀었다."], ["She could not say in words, that Bishop Proudie—bishop as he certainly must be called—was no better than he ought to be; but by that curl of her lip she did explain to those who knew her that such was the inner feeling of her heart.", "프라우디 주교가—분명히 주교라고 불러야 하는 사람이지만—마땅히 그러해야 할 만큼도 못 된다고 말로는 할 수 없었다. 그러나 그 입술의 비틂으로 그녀는 자기를 아는 이들에게 자기 마음속 느낌이 그렇다는 것을 똑똑히 알렸다."]], [["And then it was understood—Mark Robarts, at least, had so heard, and the information soon reached Framley Court—that Mr. Supplehouse was to make one of the Chaldicotes party.", "그리고 또—적어도 마크 로바츠는 그렇게 들었고, 그 소식은 곧 프램리 코트에까지 닿았는데—서플하우스 씨가 콜디코츠 손님 가운데 한 사람이 될 것이라고 알려졌다."], ["Now Mr. Supplehouse was a worse companion for a gentlemanlike, young, High Church, conservative county parson than even Harold Smith.", "그런데 서플하우스 씨는 신사답고 젊고 고교회파인 보수적 시골 목사에게 해럴드 스미스보다도 더 나쁜 벗이었다."], ["He also was in Parliament, and had been extolled during the early days of that Russian war by some portion of the metropolitan daily press, as the only man who could save the country.", "그도 하원에 있었고, 저 러시아 전쟁 초기에 수도의 일간 신문 일부로부터 나라를 구할 수 있는 유일한 사람이라고 떠받들렸다. 그를 내각에 앉히라고 《주피터》는 말했다."], ["Let him be in the ministry, the Jupiter had said, and there would be some hope of reform, some chance that England’s ancient glory would not be allowed in these perilous times to go headlong to oblivion.", "그러면 개혁에 다소 희망이 있고, 이 위태로운 때에 영국의 오랜 영광이 곤두박질쳐 잊힘 속으로 떨어지도록 방치되지 않을 가망도 얼마간 있으리라고 했다."], ["And upon this the ministry, not anticipating much salvation from Mr. Supplehouse, but willing, as they usually are, to have the Jupiter at their back, did send for that gentleman, and gave him some footing among them.", "그러자 내각은 서플하우스 씨에게서 큰 구원을 기대한 것은 아니지만, 으레 그러하듯 《주피터》를 등에 두고자 하여, 그 신사를 불러들여 자기들 사이에 자리를 얼마간 마련해 주었다."], ["But how can a man born to save a nation, and to lead a people, be content to fill the chair of an under-secretary?", "그러나 나라를 구하고 백성을 이끌도록 태어난 사람이 어떻게 차관 자리를 채우는 것으로 만족할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Supplehouse was not content, and soon gave it to be understood that his place was much higher than any yet tendered to him.", "서플하우스는 만족하지 않았고, 곧 자기 자리는 여태 자기에게 내밀어진 어떤 자리보다도 훨씬 높은 데 있다는 뜻을 알려 놓았다."], ["The seals of high office, or war to the knife, was the alternative which he offered to a much-belaboured Head of Affairs—nothing doubting that the Head of Affairs would recognize the claimant’s value, and would have before his eyes a wholesome fear of the Jupiter.", "높은 관직의 직인이냐 아니면 칼을 뽑는 싸움이냐, 이것이 그가 몹시 두들겨 맞고 있던 국정의 수반에게 내민 양자택일이었다—국정의 수반이 이 청구자의 값을 알아볼 것이고 《주피터》에 대한 건전한 두려움을 눈앞에 둘 것이라고 조금도 의심하지 않은 채였다."], ["But the Head of Affairs, much belaboured as he was, knew that he might pay too high even for Mr.", "그러나 국정의 수반은 그렇게 두들겨 맞고 있으면서도, 서플하우스 씨와 《주피터》를 얻는 데조차 값을 너무 많이 치를 수 있다는 것은 알았다."], ["Supplehouse and the Jupiter; and the saviour of the nation was told that he might swing his tomahawk.", "그리하여 나라의 구원자는 제 토마호크를 마음대로 휘둘러도 좋다는 말을 들었다."], ["Since that time he had been swinging his tomahawk, but not with so much effect as had been anticipated.", "그때부터 그는 토마호크를 휘둘러 왔으나, 기대되었던 만큼 효험을 내지는 못했다."], ["He also was very intimate with Mr. Sowerby, and was decidedly one of the Chaldicotes set.", "그도 사워비 씨와 아주 가까웠고, 확실히 콜디코츠 패의 한 사람이었다."]], [["And there were many others included in the stigma whose sins were political or religious rather than moral.", "그리고 그 낙인 안에 든 사람이 많았는데, 그들의 죄는 도덕보다는 정치나 종교의 죄였다."], ["But they were gall and wormwood to Lady Lufton, who regarded them as children of the Lost One, and who grieved with a mother’s grief when she knew that her son was among them, and felt all a patron’s anger when she heard that her clerical protégé was about to seek such society.", "그러나 그들은 레이디 러프턴에게 쓰디쓴 쓸개즙이요 쑥이었다. 그녀는 그들을 타락한 자의 자식들로 여겼고, 자기 아들이 그들 가운데 있음을 알았을 때는 어머니의 슬픔으로 슬퍼했으며, 자기가 거둔 성직자가 그런 자리를 찾아가려 한다는 말을 들었을 때는 후원자의 노여움을 다 느꼈다."], ["Mrs. Robarts might well say that Lady Lufton would be annoyed.", "레이디 러프턴이 언짢아할 것이라고 로바츠 부인이 말한 것도 무리가 아니었다."]], [["“You won’t call at the house before you go, will you?”", "“떠나기 전에 큰 집에 들르지는 않을 거죠?”"], ["the wife asked on the following morning.", "이튿날 아침 아내가 물었다."], ["He was to start after lunch on that day, driving himself in his own gig, so as to reach Chaldicotes, some twenty-four miles distant, before dinner.", "그는 그날 점심을 먹고 나서, 자기 이륜마차를 스스로 몰아 이십사 마일쯤 떨어진 콜디코츠에 만찬 전에 닿도록 떠날 예정이었다."]], [["“No, I think not.", "“아니, 안 갈 생각이야."], ["What good should I do?”", "가서 무슨 소용이 있겠어?”"]], [["“Well, I can’t explain; but I think I should call: partly, perhaps, to show her that as I had determined to go, I was not afraid of telling her so.”", "“글쎄요, 설명은 못 하겠어요. 그래도 저라면 들를 것 같아요. 어쩌면 얼마쯤은, 가기로 정했으니 그렇게 말씀드리기가 두렵지 않다는 것을 그분에게 보여 드리려고요.”"]], [["“Afraid!", "“두렵다니!"], ["That’s nonsense, Fanny.", "말도 안 되는 소리야, 패니."], ["I’m not afraid of her.", "나는 그분이 두렵지 않아."], ["But I don’t see why I should bring down upon myself the disagreeable things she will say.", "다만 그분이 할 언짢은 소리를 내가 왜 스스로 불러들여야 하는지 모르겠어."], ["Besides, I have not time.", "게다가 시간도 없어."], ["I must walk up and see Jones about the duties; and then, what with getting ready, I shall have enough to do to get off in time.”", "예배 일을 두고 존스한테 걸어가 봐야 하고, 그러고 나서 떠날 준비까지 하려면 시간에 맞춰 나서기도 빠듯할 거야.”"]], [["He paid his visit to Mr. Jones, the curate, feeling no qualms of conscience there, as he rather boasted of all the members of Parliament he was going to meet, and of the bishop who would be with them.", "그는 부목사 존스 씨를 찾아갔는데, 거기서는 양심에 거리끼는 것을 조금도 느끼지 않고 자기가 만나게 될 하원 의원들과 그들과 함께 있을 주교를 오히려 자랑삼아 말했다."], ["Mr. Evan Jones was only his curate, and in speaking to him on the matter he could talk as though it were quite the proper thing for a vicar to meet his bishop at the house of a county member.", "에번 존스 씨는 그의 부목사일 뿐이었으니, 그 일을 두고 그에게 말할 때는 교구 사제가 주의 하원 의원 집에서 자기 주교를 만나는 것이 지극히 마땅한 일인 양 이야기할 수 있었다."], ["And one would be inclined to say that it was proper: only why could he not talk of it in the same tone to Lady Lufton?", "그리고 마땅한 일이라고 말하고 싶어질 법도 하다. 다만, 그렇다면 왜 그는 레이디 러프턴에게는 같은 어조로 그 이야기를 할 수 없었을까?"], ["And then, having kissed his wife and children, he drove off, well pleased with his prospect for the coming ten days, but already anticipating some discomfort on his return.", "그러고 나서 아내와 아이들에게 입을 맞춘 뒤 그는 마차를 몰고 떠났다. 앞으로 열흘의 전망에 아주 흡족했으나, 돌아왔을 때의 불편함도 벌써 미리 헤아리고 있었다."]], [["On the three following days, Mrs. Robarts did not meet her ladyship.", "그 뒤 사흘 동안 로바츠 부인은 레이디 러프턴을 만나지 않았다."], ["She did not exactly take any steps to avoid such a meeting, but she did not purposely go up to the big house.", "그런 만남을 피하려고 딱히 무슨 수를 쓴 것은 아니었으나, 일부러 큰 집으로 올라가지도 않았다."], ["She went to her school as usual, and made one or two calls among the farmers’ wives, but put no foot within the Framley Court grounds.", "그녀는 여느 때처럼 학교에 갔고 농부의 아내들 집에 한두 번 들렀으나, 프램리 코트의 뜰 안에는 발을 들이지 않았다."], ["She was braver than her husband, but even she did not wish to anticipate the evil day.", "그녀는 남편보다 용감했으나, 그런 그녀도 궂은 날을 미리 앞당기고 싶지는 않았다."]], [["On the Saturday, just before it began to get dusk, when she was thinking of preparing for the fatal plunge, her friend, Lady Meredith, came to her.", "토요일, 어스름이 깔리기 바로 전, 그 운명적인 걸음을 내디딜 준비를 생각하고 있을 때 그녀의 벗 레이디 메러디스가 찾아왔다."]], [["“So, Fanny, we shall again be so unfortunate as to miss Mr. Robarts,”", "“그래서 패니, 우리는 또 로바츠 씨를 못 만나는 불운을 겪게 되었네요.”"], ["said her ladyship.", "레이디 메러디스가 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "“네."], ["Did you ever know anything so unlucky?", "이렇게 운이 안 맞는 일이 또 있을까요?"], ["But he had promised Mr. Sowerby before he heard that you were coming.", "하지만 그이는 당신이 온다는 말을 듣기 전에 사워비 씨에게 약속을 해 버렸어요."], ["Pray do not think that he would have gone away had he known it.”", "알고서도 떠났으리라고는 제발 생각하지 말아요.”"]], [["“We should have been sorry to keep him from so much more amusing a party.”", "“그렇게 훨씬 더 재미난 모임에서 그이를 붙들어 두었다면 우리가 미안했겠지요.”"]], [["“Now, Justinia, you are unfair.", "“이봐요, 저스티니아, 그건 공정하지 않아요."], ["You intend to imply that he has gone to Chaldicotes, because he likes it better than Framley Court; but that is not the case.", "그이가 프램리 코트보다 콜디코츠를 더 좋아해서 거기 갔다는 뜻으로 말하는 거지요. 하지만 그렇지 않아요."], ["I hope Lady Lufton does not think that it is.”", "레이디 러프턴께서 그렇게 생각하지 않으시면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["Lady Meredith laughed as she put her arm round her friend’s waist.", "레이디 메러디스는 벗의 허리에 팔을 두르며 웃었다."], ["“Don’t lose your eloquence in defending him to me,”", "“나한테 그이를 변호하느라 말솜씨를 다 써 버리지 말아요.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You’ll want all that for my mother.”", "“그건 우리 어머니한테 다 쓸 데가 있을 거예요.”"]], [["“But is your mother angry?”", "“그런데 어머님께서 노하셨나요?”"], ["asked Mrs. Robarts, showing by her countenance, how eager she was for true tidings on the subject.", "로바츠 부인이 물으며, 그 일에 대해 참된 소식을 얼마나 간절히 알고 싶어 하는지를 얼굴에 드러냈다."]], [["“Well, Fanny, you know her ladyship as well as I do.", "“글쎄요, 패니, 어머니를 나만큼 당신도 잘 알잖아요."], ["She thinks so very highly of the vicar of Framley, that she does begrudge him to those politicians at Chaldicotes.”", "어머니는 프램리의 교구 사제를 아주 높이 여기시니까, 그이를 콜디코츠의 그 정치가들에게 내주기를 아까워하시는 거지요.”"]], [["“But, Justinia, the bishop is to be there, you know.”", "“하지만 저스티니아, 주교님도 거기 오신다잖아요.”"]], [["“I don’t think that that consideration will at all reconcile my mother to the gentleman’s absence.", "“그 점을 든다고 해서 어머니가 그 신사의 부재를 조금이라도 봐주실 것 같지는 않아요."], ["He ought to be very proud, I know, to find that he is so much thought of.", "그이는 자기를 그렇게까지 중히 여겨 준다는 것을 알면 아주 자랑스러워해야 마땅하지요."], ["But come, Fanny, I want you to walk back with me, and you can dress at the house.", "하지만 자, 패니, 나와 함께 걸어서 돌아가요. 옷은 큰 집에서 갈아입으면 되고요."], ["And now we’ll go and look at the children.”", "그럼 이제 아이들을 보러 가요.”"]], [["After that, as they walked together to Framley Court, Mrs. Robarts made her friend promise that she would stand by her if any serious attack were made on the absent clergyman.", "그런 뒤에, 프램리 코트로 함께 걸어가면서 로바츠 부인은 자리에 없는 성직자에게 어떤 심각한 공격이 가해지면 자기 편에 서 주겠다는 약속을 벗에게서 받아 냈다."]], [["“Are you going up to your room at once?”", "“곧 방으로 올라가시나요?”"], ["said the vicar’s wife, as soon as they were inside the porch leading into the hall.", "현관으로 이어지는 문간에 들어서자마자 교구 사제의 아내가 말했다."], ["Lady Meredith immediately knew what her friend meant, and decided that the evil day should not be postponed.", "레이디 메러디스는 벗이 무슨 뜻으로 그러는지 곧 알아차렸고, 궂은 날을 미루지는 말아야겠다고 마음을 정했다."], ["“We had better go in and have it over,”", "“들어가서 끝내 버리는 게 좋겠어요.”"], ["she said, “and then we shall be comfortable for the evening.”", "그녀가 말했다. “그러면 저녁은 마음 편히 지낼 수 있을 테니까요.”"], ["So the drawing-room door was opened, and there was Lady Lufton alone upon the sofa.", "그래서 응접실 문이 열렸고, 거기 레이디 러프턴이 혼자 소파에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Now, mamma,”", "“어머니,” 딸이 말했다."], ["said the daughter, “you mustn’t scold Fanny much about Mr. Robarts.", "“로바츠 씨 일로 패니를 너무 꾸짖으시면 안 돼요."], ["He has gone to preach a charity sermon before the bishop, and under those circumstances, perhaps, he could not refuse.”", "그이는 주교님 앞에서 자선 설교를 하러 간 거예요. 그런 처지라면 어쩌면 거절할 수 없었겠지요.”"], ["This was a stretch on the part of Lady Meredith—put in with much good nature, no doubt; but still a stretch; for no one had supposed that the bishop would remain at Chaldicotes for the Sunday.", "이것은 레이디 메러디스가 좀 늘려 말한 것이었다—의심할 것 없이 아주 선한 마음으로 끼워 넣은 말이었지만, 그래도 늘려 말한 것이었다. 주교가 일요일까지 콜디코츠에 머물 것이라고는 아무도 생각하지 않았으니까."]], [["“How do you do, Fanny?”", "“잘 지냈어요, 패니?”"], ["said Lady Lufton, getting up.", "레이디 러프턴이 일어서며 말했다."], ["“I am not going to scold her; and I don’t know how you can talk such nonsense, Justinia.", "“나는 이 사람을 꾸짖을 생각이 없어요. 그리고 저스티니아, 너는 어쩌면 그런 말도 안 되는 소리를 하는지 모르겠구나."], ["Of course, we are very sorry not to have Mr.", "물론 로바츠 씨가 없는 것은 우리에게 몹시 서운한 일이지요."], ["Robarts; more especially as he was not here the last Sunday that Sir George was with us. I do like to see Mr.", "더구나 조지 경이 우리와 함께 있던 지난 일요일에도 그이가 여기 없었으니 더 그래요."], ["Robarts in his own church, certainly; and I don’t like any other clergyman there as well.", "나는 로바츠 씨가 자기 교회에 서 있는 것을 보는 게 참으로 좋아요. 그리고 거기 다른 성직자가 서 있는 것은 그만큼 좋지가 않아요."], ["If Fanny takes that for scolding, why—”", "패니가 그것을 꾸짖는 말로 여긴다면, 뭐—”"]], [["“Oh! no, Lady Lufton; and it’s so kind of you to say so.", "“아, 아닙니다, 레이디 러프턴."], ["But Mr.", "그렇게 말씀해 주시니 참 고맙습니다."], ["Robarts was so sorry that he had accepted this invitation to Chaldicotes, before he heard that Sir George was coming, and—”", "하지만 로바츠 씨는 조지 경이 오신다는 말을 듣기 전에 콜디코츠의 이 초대를 받아들인 것을 어찌나 미안해했는지, 그래서—”"]], [["“Oh, I know that Chaldicotes has great attractions which we cannot offer,”", "“아, 콜디코츠에는 우리가 내놓을 수 없는 큰 매력이 있다는 것을 나도 알아요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“Indeed, it was not that.", "“정말 그런 것이 아닙니다."], ["But he was asked to preach, you know; and Mr.", "그이는 설교를 부탁받았어요. 그리고 해럴드 스미스 씨가—”"], ["Harold Smith—” Poor Fanny was only making it worse.", "가엾은 패니는 일을 더 나쁘게만 만들고 있었다."], ["Had she been worldly wise, she would have accepted the little compliment implied in Lady Lufton’s first rebuke, and then have held her peace.", "세상 물정에 밝았다면 그녀는 레이디 러프턴의 첫 꾸중에 담긴 그 작은 칭찬을 받아들이고 입을 다물었을 것이다."]], [["“Oh, yes; the Harold Smiths!", "“아, 그렇지요."], ["They are irresistible, I know.", "해럴드 스미스 부부라!"], ["How could any man refuse to join a party, graced both by Mrs.", "그 사람들은 뿌리칠 수 없다는 것을 나도 알아요."], ["Harold Smith and Mrs. Proudie—even though his duty should require him to stay away?”", "해럴드 스미스 부인과 프라우디 부인이 함께 빛내 주는 모임에 끼는 것을 어느 남자가 마다할 수 있겠어요—의무가 그더러 가지 말라고 요구한다 해도 말이지요?”"]], [["“Now, mamma—”", "“어머니, 그러지 마세요—”"], ["said Justinia.", "저스티니아가 말했다."]], [["“Well, my dear, what am I to say?", "“그럼 얘야, 내가 무슨 말을 하랴?"], ["You would not wish me to tell a fib.", "너도 내가 거짓말을 하기를 바라지는 않겠지."], ["I don’t like Mrs. Harold Smith—at least, what I hear of her; for it has not been my fortune to meet her since her marriage.", "나는 해럴드 스미스 부인이 마음에 들지 않는다—적어도 그 사람에 대해 내가 듣는 말이 그렇다."], ["It may be conceited; but to own the truth, I think that Mr.", "결혼한 뒤로는 그 사람을 만나 볼 복이 내게 없었으니까."], ["Robarts would be better off with us at Framley than with the Harold Smiths at Chaldicotes,—even though Mrs. Proudie be thrown into the bargain.”", "자만하는 소리일지 모르지만, 사실을 털어놓자면 나는 로바츠 씨가 콜디코츠에서 해럴드 스미스 부부와 함께 있기보다 프램리에서 우리와 함께 있는 편이 더 나을 거라고 생각한다—프라우디 부인을 덤으로 얹어 준다 해도 말이야.”"]], [["It was nearly dark, and therefore the rising colour in the face of Mrs. Robarts could not be seen.", "거의 어두웠으므로 로바츠 부인의 얼굴에 올라오는 붉은빛은 보이지 않았다."], ["She, however, was too good a wife to hear these things said without some anger within her bosom.", "그러나 그녀는 이런 말을 들으면서 가슴속에 노여움이 일지 않을 만큼 무른 아내가 아니었다."], ["She could blame her husband in her own mind; but it was intolerable to her that others should blame him in her hearing.", "그녀는 마음속으로는 남편을 나무랄 수 있었다. 그러나 남들이 자기가 듣는 데서 그를 나무라는 것은 참을 수 없었다."]], [["“He would undoubtedly be better off,”", "“그이가 더 나을 것은 의심할 것도 없습니다.”"], ["she said; “but then, Lady Lufton, people can’t always go exactly where they will be best off.", "그녀가 말했다. “하지만 레이디 러프턴, 사람이 늘 자기가 가장 나을 곳으로만 갈 수는 없지 않습니까."], ["Gentlemen sometimes must—”", "신사라면 때로는 어쩔 수 없이—”"]], [["“Well—well, my dear, that will do.", "“됐어요, 됐어요, 그만하면 됐어요."], ["He has not taken you, at any rate; and so we will forgive him.”", "아무튼 그이가 당신을 데려가지는 않았으니, 그것으로 우리가 그를 용서해 주기로 하지요.”"], ["And Lady Lufton kissed her.", "그러고서 레이디 러프턴은 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["“As it is,”—and she affected a low whisper between the two young wives—“as it is, we must e’en put up with poor old Evan Jones.", "“이렇게 된 바에는”—그러면서 그녀는 두 젊은 아내 사이에서 낮은 속삭임을 짓는 척했다—“이렇게 된 바에는 가엾은 늙은 에번 존스로 그냥 견뎌야 해요."], ["He is to be here to-night, and we must go and dress to receive him.”", "그이가 오늘 밤 여기 올 테니, 우리는 가서 옷을 갈아입고 그를 맞아야지요.”"]], [["And so they went off.", "그리하여 그들은 갔다."], ["Lady Lufton was quite good enough at heart to like Mrs. Robarts all the better for standing up for her absent lord.", "레이디 러프턴은 마음이 넉넉히 좋은 사람이어서, 로바츠 부인이 자리에 없는 남편을 감싸고 나선 것 때문에 그녀를 오히려 더 좋아하게 되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER III. CHALDICOTES.", "# 제3장. 콜디코츠"]], [["Chaldicotes is a house of much more pretension than Framley Court.", "콜디코츠는 프램리 코트보다 훨씬 더 위세를 부리는 집이다."], ["Indeed, if one looks at the ancient marks about it, rather than at those of the present day, it is a place of very considerable pretension.", "실로, 오늘날의 흔적이 아니라 그 집에 남은 옛 흔적을 들여다본다면, 이곳은 대단히 위세 있는 곳이다."], ["There is an old forest, not altogether belonging to the property, but attached to it, called the Chace of Chaldicotes.", "오래된 숲이 하나 있는데, 전부가 그 영지에 속한 것은 아니나 그에 딸려 있어 콜디코츠 수렵림이라 불린다."], ["A portion of this forest comes up close behind the mansion, and of itself gives a character and celebrity to the place.", "이 숲의 한 부분은 저택 바로 뒤까지 바싹 올라와 있고, 그것만으로도 이곳에 하나의 성격과 명성을 부여한다."], ["The Chace of Chaldicotes—the greater part of it, at least—is, as all the world knows, Crown property, and now, in these utilitarian days, is to be disforested.", "콜디코츠 수렵림은—적어도 그 대부분은—세상이 다 아는 바와 같이 왕실 소유이며, 이제 이 실리 위주의 시대에 이르러 숲의 지정이 해제될 처지다."], ["In former times it was a great forest, stretching half across the country, almost as far as Silverbridge; and there are bits of it, here and there, still to be seen at intervals throughout the whole distance; but the larger remaining portion, consisting of aged hollow oaks, centuries old, and wide-spreading withered beeches, stands in the two parishes of Chaldicotes and Uffley.", "옛날에는 나라의 절반을 가로질러 거의 실버브리지에까지 뻗은 큰 숲이었고, 그 전 구간에 걸쳐 여기저기 띄엄띄엄 그 조각들이 아직도 눈에 띈다. 그러나 남아 있는 큰 부분, 곧 몇백 년 묵어 속이 텅 빈 늙은 참나무들과 넓게 가지를 벌린 시든 너도밤나무들로 이루어진 그 부분은 콜디코츠와 어플리 두 교구에 걸쳐 서 있다."], ["People still come from afar to see the oaks of Chaldicotes, and to hear their feet rustle among the thick autumn leaves.", "사람들은 아직도 먼 데서 찾아와 콜디코츠의 참나무들을 보고, 두껍게 깔린 가을 낙엽 사이에서 제 발이 내는 바스락 소리를 듣는다."], ["But they will soon come no longer.", "그러나 머지않아 더는 오지 않을 것이다."], ["The giants of past ages are to give way to wheat and turnips; a ruthless Chancellor of the Exchequer, disregarding old associations and rural beauty, requires money returns from the lands; and the Chace of Chaldicotes is to vanish from the earth’s surface.", "지나간 시대의 거인들이 밀과 순무에게 자리를 내주게 되어 있다. 무자비한 재무 대신이 오래된 인연과 시골의 아름다움을 아랑곳하지 않고 그 땅에서 돈이 나오기를 요구하는 것이니, 콜디코츠 수렵림은 지상에서 사라질 것이다."]], [["Some part of it, however, is the private property of Mr.", "그러나 그 일부는 사워비 씨의 사유지다."], ["Sowerby, who hitherto, through all his pecuniary distresses, has managed to save from the axe and the auction-mart that portion of his paternal heritage.", "그는 여태껏 온갖 금전상의 곤경을 겪으면서도 아버지에게서 물려받은 그 몫만은 도끼와 경매장에서 용케 지켜 냈다."], ["The house of Chaldicotes is a large stone building, probably of the time of Charles the Second.", "콜디코츠의 저택은 큰 석조 건물로, 아마 찰스 2세 시대의 것이다."], ["It is approached on both fronts by a heavy double flight of stone steps.", "앞뒤 양쪽 정면 모두 묵직한 두 갈래 돌계단으로 오르게 되어 있다."], ["In the front of the house a long, solemn, straight avenue through a double row of lime-trees, leads away to lodge-gates, which stand in the centre of the village of Chaldicotes; but to the rear the windows open upon four different vistas, which run down through the forest: four open green rides, which all converge together at a large iron gateway, the barrier which divides the private grounds from the Chace.", "집 앞쪽으로는 보리수 두 줄 사이로 길고 엄숙하고 곧은 가로수길이 뻗어 문지기 집 대문까지 이어지는데, 그 대문은 콜디코츠 마을 한가운데 서 있다. 그러나 뒤쪽으로는 창문들이 서로 다른 네 개의 전망을 향해 열리고, 그 전망들은 숲을 따라 내리 뻗는다."], ["The Sowerbys, for many generations, have been rangers of the Chace of Chaldicotes, thus having almost as wide an authority over the Crown forest as over their own.", "초록이 열린 네 개의 승마길인데, 모두 큰 철제 대문 하나에서 한데 모이며, 그 대문이 사유지와 수렵림을 가르는 경계다. 사워비 집안은 여러 대에 걸쳐 콜디코츠 수렵림의 산림관 노릇을 해 왔으니, 왕실 숲에 대해서도 제 숲에 대해서와 거의 같은 권한을 지녀 온 셈이다."], ["But now all this is to cease, for the forest will be disforested.", "그러나 이제 이 모든 것이 끝나게 되어 있다. 숲의 지정이 해제될 것이기 때문이다."]], [["It was nearly dark as Mark Robarts drove up through the avenue of lime-trees to the hall-door; but it was easy to see that the house, which was dead and silent as the grave through nine months of the year, was now alive in all its parts.", "마크 로바츠가 보리수 가로수길을 지나 현관 앞까지 마차를 몰고 올라갔을 때는 거의 어두워져 있었다. 그러나 한 해 열두 달 중 아홉 달은 무덤처럼 죽어 조용하던 그 집이 지금은 구석구석 살아 있음을 알아보기는 어렵지 않았다."], ["There were lights in many of the windows, and a noise of voices came from the stables, and servants were moving about, and dogs barked, and the dark gravel before the front steps was cut up with many a coach-wheel.", "여러 창문에 불빛이 있었고, 마구간에서는 사람들 목소리가 들려왔으며, 하인들이 오락가락하고, 개들이 짖고, 현관 앞 계단 앞의 어두운 자갈밭은 수많은 마차 바퀴에 파여 있었다."]], [["“Oh, be that you, sir, Mr. Robarts?”", "“아, 로바츠 목사님이십니까요?”"], ["said a groom, taking the parson’s horse by the head, and touching his own hat.", "마부 하나가 목사의 말 머리를 붙들고 제 모자에 손을 대며 말했다."], ["“I hope I see your reverence well?”", "“목사님, 별고 없으신지요?”"]], [["“Quite well, Bob, thank you.", "“아주 잘 있네, 밥, 고맙군."], ["All well at Chaldicotes?”", "콜디코츠는 다 잘 있나?”"]], [["“Pretty bobbish, Mr. Robarts.", "“다들 기운이 펄펄합니다요, 로바츠 목사님."], ["Deal of life going on here now, sir.", "지금 여기는 아주 북적북적합니다요."], ["The bishop and his lady came this morning.”", "주교님하고 사모님이 오늘 아침에 오셨고요.”"]], [["“Oh—ah—yes!", "“오—아—그렇지!"], ["I understood they were to be here.", "두 분이 여기 오신다고 들었네."], ["Any of the young ladies?”", "따님들도 왔나?”"]], [["“One young lady.", "“아가씨는 한 분요."], ["Miss Olivia, I think they call her, your reverence.”", "올리비아 양이라고 부르는 것 같습니다요, 목사님.”"]], [["“And how’s Mr. Sowerby?”", "“사워비 씨는 어떠신가?”"]], [["“Very well, your reverence. He, and Mr.", "“아주 잘 계십니다요, 목사님."], ["Harold Smith, and Mr. Fothergill—that’s the duke’s man of business, you know—is getting off their horses now in the stable-yard there.”", "그분하고, 해럴드 스미스 씨하고, 포더길 씨하고—그 공작님 일 봐주는 분 아시죠—지금 저 마구간 마당에서 말에서 내리고 있습니다요.”"]], [["“Home from hunting—eh, Bob?”", "“사냥에서 돌아온 거지, 밥?”"]], [["“Yes, sir, just home, this minute.”", "“예, 목사님, 방금 막 돌아왔습니다요.”"], ["And then Mr. Robarts walked into the house, his portmanteau following on a footboy’s shoulder.", "그러고 나서 로바츠 씨는 집 안으로 들어갔고, 그의 큰 가죽 가방은 하인 아이의 어깨에 실려 뒤따랐다."]], [["It will be seen that our young vicar was very intimate at Chaldicotes; so much so that the groom knew him, and talked to him about the people in the house.", "보다시피 우리의 젊은 교구 사제는 콜디코츠와 무척 가까웠다. 마부가 그를 알아보고 집 안의 사람들에 대해 그에게 이야기를 늘어놓을 정도로 가까웠다."], ["Yes; he was intimate there: much more than he had given the Framley people to understand.", "그렇다, 그는 그 집과 가까웠다. 프램리 사람들에게 짐작하게 해 둔 것보다 훨씬 더 가까웠다."], ["Not that he had wilfully and overtly deceived any one; not that he had ever spoken a false word about Chaldicotes.", "그가 누구를 고의로 드러내어 속인 것은 아니다. 콜디코츠에 대해 거짓말을 한 적도 결코 없다."], ["But he had never boasted at home that he and Sowerby were near allies.", "그러나 그는 집에서 자기와 사워비가 가까운 한편이라고 자랑한 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["Neither had he told them there how often Mr. Sowerby and Lord Lufton were together in London.", "사워비 씨와 러프턴 경이 런던에서 얼마나 자주 함께 지내는지도 그들에게 말한 적이 없었다."], ["Why trouble women with such matters?", "그런 일로 여자들을 어지럽힐 이유가 어디 있는가?"], ["Why annoy so excellent a woman as Lady Lufton?", "레이디 러프턴처럼 훌륭한 부인을 성가시게 할 이유가 어디 있는가?"]], [["And then Mr. Sowerby was one whose intimacy few young men would wish to reject.", "게다가 사워비 씨는 젊은 사내라면 그와 가까이 사귈 기회를 마다하고 싶지 않을 그런 사람이었다."], ["He was fifty, and had lived, perhaps, not the most salutary life; but he dressed young, and usually looked well.", "그는 쉰이었고, 아마도 가장 건전한 생활을 해 온 것은 아니었다. 그러나 젊게 차려입었고 대개 보기 좋았다."], ["He was bald, with a good forehead, and sparkling moist eyes.", "대머리였으나 이마가 훤했고 눈은 반짝반짝 젖어 있었다."], ["He was a clever man, and a pleasant companion, and always good-humoured when it so suited him.", "머리가 잘 돌아가는 사람이었고, 함께 있으면 즐거운 상대였으며, 마음만 그리 먹으면 언제나 기분이 좋았다."], ["He was a gentleman, too, of high breeding and good birth, whose ancestors had been known in that county—longer, the farmers around would boast, than those of any other landowner in it, unless it be the Thornes of Ullathorne, or perhaps the Greshams of Greshamsbury—much longer than the De Courcys at Courcy Castle.", "또한 가정 교육이 높고 태생이 좋은 신사였다. 그의 조상들은 그 주에서 알려져 온 지가—주변의 농부들이 자랑하기로는—울라손의 손 집안이나 어쩌면 그레셤스베리의 그레셤 집안을 빼면 그 주의 다른 어느 지주보다도 오래되었고, 코시 성의 드 코시 집안보다야 훨씬 오래되었다."], ["As for the Duke of Omnium, he, comparatively speaking, was a new man.", "옴니엄 공작으로 치면, 그는 상대적으로 말해서 새 사람이었다."]], [["And then he was a member of Parliament, a friend of some men in power, and of others who might be there; a man who could talk about the world as one knowing the matter of which he talked.", "또한 그는 하원 의원이었고, 권력을 쥔 사람들 몇과 앞으로 쥘지도 모르는 사람들과 벗이었으며, 자기가 말하는 사안을 아는 자로서 세상 이야기를 할 줄 아는 사람이었다."], ["And moreover, whatever might be his ways of life at other times, when in the presence of a clergyman he rarely made himself offensive to clerical tastes.", "더욱이 다른 때에 그의 사는 방식이 어떠하든, 성직자 앞에 있을 때에는 성직자의 취향에 거슬리는 짓을 좀처럼 하지 않았다."], ["He neither swore, nor brought his vices on the carpet, nor sneered at the faith of the Church.", "욕설을 하지도 않았고, 자기 악덕을 상 위에 올려놓지도 않았고, 교회의 신앙을 비웃지도 않았다."], ["If he was no churchman himself, he at least knew how to live with those who were.", "그 자신이 교회의 사람이 아니라면, 적어도 그런 사람들과 어울려 지내는 법은 알았다."]], [["How was it possible that such a one as our vicar should not relish the intimacy of Mr. Sowerby?", "우리의 교구 사제 같은 이가 어떻게 사워비 씨와의 교분을 즐기지 않을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["It might be very well, he would say to himself, for a woman like Lady Lufton to turn up her nose at him—for Lady Lufton, who spent ten months of the year at Framley Court, and who during those ten months, and for the matter of that, during the two months also which she spent in London, saw no one out of her own set.", "레이디 러프턴 같은 부인이야 그를 코웃음 치며 볼 수도 있으리라고 그는 스스로에게 말했다. 한 해에 열 달을 프램리 코트에서 지내고, 그 열 달 동안은 물론이거니와 런던에서 지내는 나머지 두 달 동안에도 제 무리 밖의 사람은 아무도 만나지 않는 레이디 러프턴이야 그럴 수 있으리라고."], ["Women did not understand such things, the vicar said to himself; even his own wife—good, and nice, and sensible, and intelligent as she was—even she did not understand that a man in the world must meet all sorts of men; and that in these days it did not do for a clergyman to be a hermit.", "여자들은 그런 것을 이해하지 못한다고 교구 사제는 스스로에게 말했다. 착하고 다정하고 분별 있고 총명한 자기 아내조차도, 세상에 나가 사는 남자는 온갖 종류의 남자들을 만나야 한다는 것, 그리고 이런 시대에 성직자가 은자로 사는 것은 통하지 않는다는 것을 이해하지 못한다고."]], [["’Twas thus that Mark Robarts argued when he found himself called upon to defend himself before the bar of his own conscience for going to Chaldicotes and increasing his intimacy with Mr.", "콜디코츠에 가서 사워비 씨와의 교분을 더 깊게 하는 일을 두고 제 양심의 법정 앞에서 스스로를 변호해야 하는 처지에 놓였을 때, 마크 로바츠가 논한 논리는 이러했다."], ["Sowerby. He did know that Mr.", "그는 사워비 씨가 위험한 사람임을 알고 있었다."], ["Sowerby was a dangerous man; he was aware that he was over head and ears in debt, and that he had already entangled young Lord Lufton in some pecuniary embarrassment; his conscience did tell him that it would be well for him, as one of Christ’s soldiers, to look out for companions of a different stamp.", "그가 빚에 목까지 잠겨 있음을, 그리고 이미 젊은 러프턴 경을 금전상의 곤경에 끌어들여 놓았음을 알고 있었다. 그리스도의 병사 가운데 하나로서, 다른 품새의 벗들을 찾아보는 편이 자기에게 좋을 것이라고 그의 양심이 일러 주기는 했다."], ["But nevertheless he went to Chaldicotes, not satisfied with himself indeed, but repeating to himself a great many arguments why he should be so satisfied.", "그러나 그럼에도 그는 콜디코츠로 갔다. 자기 자신이 정말로 만족스러운 것은 아니었으나, 왜 만족해도 되는지에 대한 논거를 아주 많이 스스로에게 되풀이하면서."]], [["He was shown into the drawing-room at once, and there he found Mrs. Harold Smith, with Mrs.", "그는 곧바로 응접실로 안내되었고, 거기서 해럴드 스미스 부인과, 프라우디 부인 및 프라우디 양과, 그리고 전에 한 번도 본 적 없는 한 부인을 만났다."], ["and Miss Proudie, and a lady whom he had never before seen, and whose name he did not at first hear mentioned.", "그 부인의 이름은 처음에는 거명되는 것을 듣지 못했다."]], [["“Is that Mr. Robarts?”", "“로바츠 씨 아니세요?”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith, getting up to greet him, and screening her pretended ignorance under the veil of the darkness.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 그를 맞으러 일어나며, 실은 알고 있으면서도 모르는 척하는 것을 어둠의 장막 아래 감추고 말했다."], ["“And have you really driven over four-and-twenty miles of Barsetshire roads on such a day as this to assist us in our little difficulties?", "“이런 날에 바셋주의 길을 이십사 마일이나 마차로 달려오셔서, 우리의 자그마한 곤경을 도와주시겠다는 건가요?"], ["Well, we can promise you gratitude at any rate.”", "어쨌든 감사만은 약속드릴 수 있어요.”"]], [["And then the vicar shook hands with Mrs. Proudie, in that deferential manner which is due from a vicar to his bishop’s wife; and Mrs.", "그러고서 교구 사제는 프라우디 부인과, 교구 사제가 자기 주교의 부인에게 응당 갖추어야 할 그 공손한 태도로 악수를 나누었다."], ["Proudie returned the greeting with all that smiling condescension which a bishop’s wife should show to a vicar.", "프라우디 부인은 주교의 부인이 교구 사제에게 마땅히 보여야 할 저 미소 띤 겸양을 다하여 인사를 되받았다."], ["Miss Proudie was not quite so civil. Had Mr.", "프라우디 양은 그만큼 정중하지 않았다."], ["Robarts been still unmarried, she also could have smiled sweetly; but she had been exercising smiles on clergymen too long to waste them now on a married parish parson.", "로바츠 씨가 아직 미혼이었다면 그녀도 달콤하게 웃어 줄 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 성직자들에게 미소를 지어 보이는 일을 너무 오래 해 온 터라, 아내 있는 교구 목사에게 그것을 낭비할 수는 없었다."]], [["“And what are the difficulties, Mrs. Smith, in which I am to assist you?”", "“그런데 제가 도와드려야 할 곤경이란 무엇입니까, 스미스 부인?”"]], [["“We have six or seven gentlemen here, Mr. Robarts, and they always go out hunting before breakfast, and they never come back—I was going to say—till after dinner.", "“여기 신사분이 여섯인가 일곱인가 계신데요, 로바츠 씨, 이분들은 늘 아침 식사 전에 사냥을 나가서, 저녁 식사 뒤가 되도록—이렇게 말하려고 했어요—돌아오지 않아요."], ["I wish it were so, for then we should not have to wait for them.”", "정말 그랬으면 좋겠어요. 그러면 우리가 기다릴 일이 없을 테니까요.”"]], [["“Excepting Mr. Supplehouse, you know,”", "“서플하우스 씨는 빼고요.”"], ["said the unknown lady, in a loud voice.", "낯모를 부인이 큰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“And he is generally shut up in the library, writing articles.”", "“그리고 그분은 대개 서고에 갇혀서 논설을 쓰고 계시죠.”"]], [["“He’d be better employed if he were trying to break his neck like the others,”", "“다른 분들처럼 목이라도 부러뜨려 보려고 애쓰는 편이 그분한테는 더 나은 일거리일 텐데요.”"], ["said the unknown lady.", "낯모를 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Only he would never succeed,” said Mrs.", "“그런데 그분은 절대로 성공하지 못하실 거예요.”"], ["Harold Smith. “But perhaps, Mr.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["Robarts, you are as bad as the rest; perhaps you, too, will be hunting to-morrow.”", "“하지만 로바츠 씨, 어쩌면 당신도 나머지 분들만큼 못됐을지 모르지요. 어쩌면 당신도 내일 사냥을 나가실지 모르고요.”"]], [["“My dear Mrs. Smith!”", "“아니, 스미스 부인!”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie, in a tone denoting slight reproach, and modified horror.", "프라우디 부인이 가벼운 질책과 절제된 경악이 담긴 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["I forgot.", "내가 잊었네요."], ["No, of course, you won’t be hunting, Mr.", "아니지요, 물론 당신은 사냥을 나가지 않으실 거예요, 로바츠 씨."], ["Robarts; you’ll only be wishing that you could.”", "다만 나갈 수 있으면 좋겠다고 바라기만 하시겠지요.”"]], [["“Why can’t he?”", "“왜 못 나가시는데요?”"], ["said the lady with the loud voice.", "목소리 큰 부인이 말했다."]], [["“My dear Miss Dunstable!", "“어머, 던스터블 양!"], ["a clergyman hunt, while he is staying in the same house with the bishop?", "주교님과 같은 집에 머무는 동안에 성직자가 사냥을 한다니요?"], ["Think of the proprieties!”", "체면을 생각하셔야지요!”"]], [["“Oh—ah!", "“오—아!"], ["The bishop wouldn’t like it—wouldn’t he?", "주교님이 좋아하지 않으실 거란 말이죠?"], ["Now, do tell me, sir, what would the bishop do to you if you did hunt?”", "어디 좀 말씀해 보세요, 선생님. 정말로 사냥을 나가시면 주교님이 당신을 어떻게 하실까요?”"]], [["“It would depend upon his mood at the time, madam,”", "“그때 주교님의 심기에 달렸겠지요, 부인.”"], ["said Mr. Robarts.", "로바츠 씨가 말했다."], ["“If that were very stern, he might perhaps have me beheaded before the palace gates.”", "“심기가 아주 험하시다면, 어쩌면 주교관 문 앞에서 제 목을 베게 하실지도 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["Mrs.", "프라우디 부인은 의자에 앉은 몸을 곧추세워, 이런 투의 대화가 마음에 들지 않는다는 것을 드러내었다."], ["Proudie drew herself up in her chair, showing that she did not like the tone of the conversation; and Miss Proudie fixed her eyes vehemently on her book, showing that Miss Dunstable and her conversation were both beneath her notice.", "그리고 프라우디 양은 제 책에 눈을 맹렬히 붙여, 던스터블 양이든 그 대화든 자기가 눈길을 줄 만한 것이 아님을 드러내었다."]], [["“If these gentlemen do not mean to break their necks to-night,”", "“이 신사분들이 오늘 밤에는 목을 부러뜨릴 뜻이 없으시다면,” 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith, “I wish they’d let us know it.", "“우리한테 그걸 알려 주셨으면 좋겠어요."], ["It’s half-past six already.”", "벌써 여섯 시 반이니까요.”"]], [["And then Mr. Robarts gave them to understand that no such catastrophe could be looked for that day, as Mr. Sowerby and the other sportsmen were within the stable-yard when he entered the door.", "그러자 로바츠 씨가, 자기가 문에 들어설 때 사워비 씨와 다른 사냥꾼들이 마구간 마당에 있었으므로 오늘은 그런 참변을 기대할 수 없다는 뜻을 그들에게 알려 주었다."]], [["“Then, ladies, we may as well dress,”", "“그럼 부인들, 우리도 옷을 갈아입는 게 좋겠어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["But as she moved towards the door, it opened, and a short gentleman, with a slow, quiet step, entered the room; but was not yet to be distinguished through the dusk by the eyes of Mr.", "그러나 그녀가 문을 향해 움직이는데 그 문이 열리며, 키가 작은 신사 한 분이 느리고 조용한 걸음으로 방에 들어섰다. 그러나 로바츠 씨의 눈으로는 어스름 속에서 아직 그가 누구인지 가려낼 수 없었다."], ["Robarts.", "“아!"], ["“Oh! bishop, is that you?”", "주교님, 오셨어요?”"], ["said Mrs. Smith.", "스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["“Here is one of the luminaries of your diocese.”", "“여기 주교님 교구의 빛나는 별 한 분이 계세요.”"], ["And then the bishop, feeling through the dark, made his way up to the vicar and shook him cordially by the hand.", "그러자 주교는 어둠 속을 더듬어 교구 사제에게 다가와 그의 손을 다정하게 잡고 흔들었다."], ["“He was delighted to meet Mr. Robarts at Chaldicotes,”", "“콜디코츠에서 로바츠 씨를 만나 아주 기쁘다”고 그는 말했다—“참으로 기쁘다."], ["he said—“quite delighted. Was he not going to preach on behalf of the Papuan Mission next Sunday?", "다음 일요일에 파푸아 선교를 위해 설교를 하기로 되어 있지 않은가?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["so he, the bishop, had heard.", "그러니까 자기, 곧 주교가 그렇게 들었다."], ["It was a good work, an excellent work.”", "좋은 사업이고, 훌륭한 사업이다.”"], ["And then Dr. Proudie expressed himself as much grieved that he could not remain at Chaldicotes, and hear the sermon.", "그러고서 프라우디 박사는 자기가 콜디코츠에 남아 그 설교를 듣지 못하는 것이 무척 애석하다는 뜻을 말했다."], ["It was plain that his bishop thought no ill of him on account of his intimacy with Mr.", "자기 주교가 사워비 씨와의 교분을 두고 자기를 나쁘게 여기지 않는 것이 분명했다."], ["Sowerby. But then he felt in his own heart that he did not much regard his bishop’s opinion.", "그러나 그러면서도 그는 제 마음속으로, 자기가 주교의 의견을 그리 대수롭게 여기지 않는다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Ah, Robarts, I’m delighted to see you,”", "“아, 로바츠, 만나서 정말 반가워요.”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby, when they met on the drawing-room rug before dinner.", "저녁 식사 전에 응접실 깔개 위에서 마주쳤을 때 사워비 씨가 말했다."], ["“You know Harold Smith?", "“해럴드 스미스는 알지요?"], ["Yes, of course you do.", "그럼, 물론 알겠지."], ["Well, who else is there?", "그러면 또 누가 있나?"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["Supplehouse.", "서플하우스."], ["Mr. Supplehouse, allow me to introduce to you my friend Mr.", "서플하우스 씨, 제 친구 로바츠 씨를 소개하겠습니다."], ["Robarts. It is he who will extract the five-pound note out of your pocket next Sunday for these poor Papuans whom we are going to Christianize.", "우리가 기독교로 이끌려는 저 가엾은 파푸아인들을 위해 다음 일요일에 당신 주머니에서 5파운드 지폐를 끄집어낼 사람이 바로 이 사람이오."], ["That is, if Harold Smith does not finish the work out of hand at his Saturday lecture.", "그야 해럴드 스미스가 토요일 강연에서 그 일을 대번에 끝장내 버리지만 않는다면 말이지만."], ["And, Robarts, you have seen the bishop, of course:”", "그리고 로바츠, 주교님은 물론 봤겠지요.”"], ["this he said in a whisper.", "이 말은 속삭이듯 했다."], ["“A fine thing to be a bishop, isn’t it?", "“주교가 된다는 건 좋은 일이지요?"], ["I wish I had half your chance.", "나한테 당신 몫의 절반만이라도 기회가 있었으면 좋겠어요."], ["But, my dear fellow, I’ve made such a mistake; I haven’t got a bachelor parson for Miss Proudie.", "그런데 여보게, 내가 이런 실수를 했군. 프라우디 양 몫으로 총각 목사를 하나도 구해 두지 못했어요."], ["You must help me out, and take her in to dinner.”", "나를 좀 도와서 그녀를 식탁으로 데려가 줘야겠어요.”"], ["And then the great gong sounded, and off they went in pairs.", "그러고서 큰 징이 울렸고, 그들은 둘씩 짝을 지어 나갔다."]], [["At dinner Mark found himself seated between Miss Proudie and the lady whom he had heard named as Miss Dunstable.", "식탁에서 마크는 프라우디 양과, 던스터블 양이라고 불리는 것을 들은 그 부인 사이에 앉게 되었다."], ["Of the former he was not very fond, and, in spite of his host’s petition, was not inclined to play bachelor parson for her benefit.", "앞의 사람은 그가 그리 좋아하지 않는 상대였고, 주인의 청이 있었음에도 그녀를 위해 총각 목사 노릇을 해 줄 마음은 들지 않았다."], ["With the other lady he would willingly have chatted during the dinner, only that everybody else at table seemed to be intent on doing the same thing.", "뒤의 부인과는 식사 동안 기꺼이 이야기를 나누고 싶었으나, 식탁의 다른 모든 사람이 하나같이 같은 일에 열심인 듯했다. 그녀는 젊지도, 아름답지도, 유별나게 귀부인답지도 않았다."], ["She was neither young, nor beautiful, nor peculiarly ladylike; yet she seemed to enjoy a popularity which must have excited the envy of Mr. Supplehouse, and which certainly was not altogether to the taste of Mrs.", "그런데도 서플하우스 씨의 시샘을 돋우었음이 틀림없을 만한 인기를 누리는 듯했고, 그 인기는 프라우디 부인의 취향에도 결코 온전히 맞지 않는 것이었다—하기야 프라우디 부인도 남들만큼이나 그녀를 떠받들었다."], ["Proudie—who, however, fêted her as much as did the others. So that our clergyman found himself unable to obtain more than an inconsiderable share of the lady’s attention.", "그리하여 우리의 성직자는 그 부인의 관심 가운데 하찮은 몫밖에 얻어 낼 수 없었다."]], [["“Bishop,”", "“주교님,”"], ["said she, speaking across the table, “we have missed you so all day!", "그녀가 식탁 건너로 말을 건넸다. “저희가 온종일 주교님이 안 계셔서 얼마나 서운했는지요!"], ["we have had no one on earth to say a word to us.”", "말 한마디 붙일 사람이 이 세상에 하나도 없었어요.”"]], [["“My dear Miss Dunstable, had I known that— But I really was engaged on business of some importance.”", "“아, 던스터블 양, 내가 그걸 알았더라면— 그러나 나는 참으로 좀 중요한 일에 붙들려 있었소.”"]], [["“I don’t believe in business of importance; do you, Mrs.", "“저는 중요한 일이라는 걸 믿지 않아요."], ["Smith?”", "스미스 부인, 부인은 믿으세요?”"]], [["“Do I not?” said Mrs.", "“내가 안 믿겠어요?”"], ["Smith. “If you were married to Mr.", "스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["Harold Smith for one week, you’d believe in it.”", "“해럴드 스미스 씨와 결혼해서 한 주만 살아 보시면, 그건 믿게 될 거예요.”"]], [["“Should I, now?", "“그럴까요?"], ["What a pity that I can’t have that chance of improving my faith!", "제 믿음을 키울 그런 기회를 가질 수 없다니 참 아쉬운 일이네요!"], ["But you are a man of business, also, Mr.", "하지만 서플하우스 씨도 일하는 분이시죠."], ["Supplehouse; so they tell me.”", "사람들이 그러던데요.”"], ["And she turned to her neighbour on her right hand.", "그러면서 그녀는 오른쪽 옆자리 사람을 향해 돌아앉았다."]], [["“I cannot compare myself to Harold Smith,”", "“나를 해럴드 스미스에 견줄 수야 없지요.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But perhaps I may equal the bishop.”", "“그러나 주교님과는 어쩌면 맞먹을지도 모르겠소.”"]], [["“What does a man do, now, when he sits himself down to business?", "“남자가 일을 하려고 자리에 앉으면 대체 무엇을 하나요?"], ["How does he set about it?", "어떻게 시작하죠?"], ["What are his tools?", "도구가 뭐예요?"], ["A quire of blotting paper, I suppose, to begin with?”", "우선은 압지 한 첩쯤 있어야 하나요?”"]], [["“That depends, I should say, on his trade.", "“그건 업종에 달렸다고 해야겠지요."], ["A shoemaker begins by waxing his thread.”", "구두장이는 실에 밀을 먹이는 것으로 시작하오.”"]], [["“And Mr. Harold Smith—?”", "“그럼 해럴드 스미스 씨는요?”"]], [["“By counting up his yesterday’s figures, generally, I should say; or else by unrolling a ball of red tape.", "“대개 어제 자 숫자를 합쳐 보는 것으로 시작하겠지요. 그게 아니면 붉은 끈 뭉치를 풀어 놓는 것으로."], ["Well-docketed papers and statistical facts are his forte.”", "꼬리표 잘 달린 서류와 통계상의 사실이 그이의 장기니까.”"]], [["“And what does a bishop do?", "“그럼 주교는 무엇을 하죠?"], ["Can you tell me that?”", "그건 말해 주실 수 있어요?”"]], [["“He sends forth to his clergy either blessings or blowings-up, according to the state of his digestive organs.", "“소화 기관의 상태에 따라 자기 성직자들에게 축복을 내려보내거나 호통을 내려보내오."], ["But Mrs. Proudie can explain all that to you with the greatest accuracy.”", "그러나 그 모든 것은 프라우디 부인이 아주 정확하게 설명해 줄 수 있을 거요.”"]], [["“Can she, now?", "“그럴까요?"], ["I understand what you mean, but I don’t believe a word of it.", "무슨 말씀인지는 알겠지만, 저는 한마디도 믿지 않아요. 주교님은 자기 일을 자기가 처리하세요."], ["The bishop manages his own affairs himself, quite as much as you do, or Mr. Harold Smith.”", "당신이나 해럴드 스미스 씨만큼은 충분히요.”"]], [["“I, Miss Dunstable?”", "“내가 말이오, 던스터블 양?”"]], [["“Yes, you.”", "“그래요, 당신이요.”"]], [["“But I, unluckily, have not a wife to manage them for me.”", "“그러나 나에게는 안타깝게도 그 일을 대신 처리해 줄 아내가 없소.”"]], [["“Then you should not laugh at those who have, for you don’t know what you may come to yourself, when you’re married.”", "“그러면 아내가 있는 분들을 비웃지 마셔야죠. 당신도 결혼하고 나면 어떤 처지가 될지 모르는 일이니까요.”"]], [["Mr. Supplehouse began to make a pretty speech, saying that he would be delighted to incur any danger in that respect to which he might be subjected by the companionship of Miss Dunstable.", "서플하우스 씨는 던스터블 양과 짝을 이룸으로써 자기에게 닥칠 그 방면의 어떤 위험이든 기꺼이 무릅쓰겠다며 그럴싸한 말을 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["But before he was half through it, she had turned her back upon him, and begun a conversation with Mark Robarts.", "그러나 그가 그 말을 절반쯤 하기도 전에 그녀는 그에게 등을 돌리고, 마크 로바츠와 이야기를 시작해 버렸다."]], [["“Have you much work in your parish, Mr.", "“교구에 일이 많으세요, 로바츠 씨?”"], ["Robarts?” she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."], ["Now, Mark was not aware that she knew his name, or the fact of his having a parish, and was rather surprised by the question.", "그런데 마크는 그녀가 자기 이름을 안다는 것도, 자기에게 교구가 있다는 사실을 안다는 것도 몰랐던 터라 그 질문에 다소 놀랐다."], ["And he had not quite liked the tone in which she had seemed to speak of the bishop and his work.", "게다가 그녀가 주교와 주교의 일을 두고 말하던 어조가 그의 마음에 그리 들지 않았다."], ["His desire for her further acquaintance was therefore somewhat moderated, and he was not prepared to answer her question with much zeal.", "그래서 그녀와 더 알고 지내고 싶은 마음이 조금 식었고, 그녀의 질문에 그리 열을 내어 답할 준비가 되어 있지 않았다."]], [["“All parish clergymen have plenty of work, if they choose to do it.”", "“교구 성직자라면 누구나 하려고만 하면 일이 넘칩니다.”"]], [["“Ah, that is it; is it not, Mr.", "“아, 그거예요. 그렇지 않아요, 로바츠 씨?"], ["Robarts?", "하려고만 하면."], ["If they choose to do it?", "하는 분이 많지요."], ["A great many do—many that I know, do; and see what a result they have.", "제가 아는 분들 가운데도 많고요. 그런데 그분들이 어떤 결과를 얻는지 보세요."], ["But many neglect it—and see what a result they have.", "그러나 소홀히 하는 분도 많아요. 그리고 그분들이 어떤 결과를 얻는지도 보세요."], ["I think it ought to be the happiest life that a man can lead, that of a parish clergyman, with a wife and family, and a sufficient income.”", "저는 아내와 자식이 있고 넉넉한 수입이 있는 교구 성직자의 삶이야말로 사람이 누릴 수 있는 가장 행복한 삶이어야 한다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“I think it is,”", "“저도 그렇게 생각합니다.”"], ["said Mark Robarts, asking himself whether the contentment accruing to him from such blessings had made him satisfied at all points.", "마크 로바츠는 그렇게 말하면서, 그러한 복에서 나오는 넉넉함이 자기를 모든 점에서 만족시켰던가를 스스로에게 물었다."], ["He had all these things of which Miss Dunstable spoke, and yet he had told his wife, the other day, that he could not afford to neglect the acquaintance of a rising politician like Harold Smith.", "던스터블 양이 말한 그 모든 것을 그는 가지고 있었다. 그런데도 그는 며칠 전에 아내에게, 해럴드 스미스처럼 떠오르는 정치가와의 교분을 소홀히 할 처지는 못 된다고 말했던 것이다."]], [["“What I find fault with is this,”", "“제가 못마땅해하는 건 이거예요.”"], ["continued Miss Dunstable, “that we expect clergymen to do their duty, and don’t give them a sufficient income—give them hardly any income at all.", "던스터블 양이 말을 이었다. “우리는 성직자에게 제 본분을 다하라고 하면서, 넉넉한 수입을 주지 않는다는 거요—수입이랄 것을 거의 하나도 주지 않지요."], ["Is it not a scandal, that an educated gentleman with a family should be made to work half his life, and perhaps the whole, for a pittance of seventy pounds a year?”", "교육받은 신사가 딸린 식구를 두고서 제 인생의 절반을, 어쩌면 평생을, 연 70파운드라는 쥐꼬리를 받고 일하도록 만들어진다는 게 창피한 일이 아닌가요?”"]], [["Mark said that it was a scandal, and thought of Mr. Evan Jones and his daughter;—and thought also of his own worth, and his own house, and his own nine hundred a year.", "마크는 그것이 창피한 일이라고 말하고서, 에번 존스 씨와 그의 딸을 생각했다.—그리고 자기 자신의 값과, 자기 자신의 집과, 자기 자신의 연 900파운드도 생각했다."]], [["“And yet you clergymen are so proud—aristocratic would be the genteel word, I know—that you won’t take the money of common, ordinary poor people.", "“게다가 성직자분들은 어찌나 오만하신지—고상하게 말하자면 귀족적이라고 해야 할 줄은 알아요—보통의, 평범한 가난한 사람들의 돈은 받으려고 하지 않으세요."], ["You must be paid from land and endowments, from tithe and church property.", "땅과 기부 재산에서, 십일조와 교회 재산에서 받으셔야 하죠."], ["You can’t bring yourself to work for what you earn, as lawyers and doctors do.", "변호사나 의사가 하듯이 벌이만큼 일한다는 데에는 스스로를 들여놓을 수가 없으신 거예요."], ["It is better that curates should starve than undergo such ignominy as that.”", "그런 수모를 당하기보다는 부목사들이 굶어 죽는 편이 낫다는 거죠.”"]], [["“It is a long subject, Miss Dunstable.”", "“그건 긴 이야기입니다, 던스터블 양.”"]], [["“A very long one; and that means that I am not to say any more about it.”", "“아주 긴 이야기죠. 그리고 그 말은 제가 더는 그 이야기를 하지 말라는 뜻이고요.”"]], [["“I did not mean that exactly.”", "“꼭 그런 뜻은 아니었습니다.”"]], [["“Oh! but you did though, Mr.", "“아니에요!"], ["Robarts. And I can take a hint of that kind when I get it.", "그런 뜻이셨어요, 로바츠 씨."], ["You clergymen like to keep those long subjects for your sermons, when no one can answer you.", "그런 눈치는 제가 받으면 알아채요. 성직자분들은 그런 긴 이야기는 아무도 대꾸할 수 없는 설교 시간을 위해 남겨 두기를 좋아하시죠."], ["Now if I have a longing heart’s desire for anything at all in this world, it is to be able to get up into a pulpit, and preach a sermon.”", "자, 제가 이 세상에서 무엇이든 하나 마음속으로 간절히 바라는 게 있다면, 그건 설교단에 올라가서 설교를 한번 해 보는 거예요.”"]], [["“You can’t conceive how soon that appetite would pall upon you, after its first indulgence.”", "“그 식욕이 한 번 채워진 뒤에 얼마나 빨리 물려 버릴지는 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다.”"]], [["“That would depend upon whether I could get people to listen to me.", "“그건 사람들이 제 말을 듣게 만들 수 있느냐에 달렸겠죠."], ["It does not pall upon Mr. Spurgeon, I suppose.”", "스퍼전 씨는 물리지 않으신 것 같은데요.”"], ["Then her attention was called away by some question from Mr. Sowerby, and Mark Robarts found himself bound to address his conversation to Miss Proudie.", "그러고서 그녀의 관심은 사워비 씨의 어떤 질문에 이끌려 딴 데로 갔고, 마크 로바츠는 프라우디 양에게 말을 붙여야 하는 처지가 되었다."], ["Miss Proudie, however, was not thankful, and gave him little but monosyllables for his pains.", "그러나 프라우디 양은 고마워하지 않았고, 애쓴 값으로 그에게 준 것은 외마디 대답 정도가 거의 다였다."]], [["“Of course you know Harold Smith is going to give us a lecture about these islanders,”", "“해럴드 스미스가 그 섬사람들에 관해 우리한테 강연을 하기로 한 건 물론 알고 있지요?”"], ["Mr. Sowerby said to him, as they sat round the fire over their wine after dinner.", "저녁 식사 뒤에 포도주를 놓고 난롯가에 둘러앉았을 때 사워비 씨가 그에게 말했다."], ["Mark said that he had been so informed, and should be delighted to be one of the listeners.", "마크는 그렇게 들었으며 청중의 하나가 되는 것이 기쁠 것이라고 말했다."]], [["“You are bound to do that, as he is going to listen to you the day afterwards—or, at any rate, to pretend to do so, which is as much as you will do for him.", "“그야 해야 할 일이지요. 그 사람은 그다음 날 당신 설교를 들으러 갈 테니까요—적어도 듣는 척은 할 테고, 당신이 그 사람한테 해 줄 것도 그만큼일 테니까."], ["It’ll be a terrible bore—the lecture, I mean, not the sermon.”", "그건 지독한 골칫거리일 겁니다—강연 말이오, 설교가 아니라.”"], ["And he spoke very low into his friend’s ear.", "그러고서 그는 벗의 귀에 아주 낮게 말했다."], ["“Fancy having to drive ten miles after dusk, and ten miles back, to hear Harold Smith talk for two hours about Borneo!", "“해럴드 스미스가 보르네오에 관해 두 시간 떠드는 것을 들으러, 해 저문 뒤에 십 마일을 마차로 가고 십 마일을 되돌아와야 한다고 생각해 보세요!"], ["One must do it, you know.”", "그래도 해야 하는 일이니까요.”"]], [["“I daresay it will be very interesting.”", "“꽤 흥미로울 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“My dear fellow, you haven’t undergone so many of these things as I have.", "“여보게, 당신은 이런 일을 나만큼 겪어 보지 않았지요. 그러나 그 사람은 그걸 하는 게 옳아요."], ["But he’s right to do it.", "그게 그 사람의 밥벌이 길이니까."], ["It’s his line of life; and when a man begins a thing he ought to go on with it.", "그리고 사람이 어떤 일을 시작했으면 계속해 나가야 하는 법이지요."], ["Where’s Lufton all this time?”", "그런데 러프턴은 이 시간에 어디 있소?”"]], [["“In Scotland, when I last heard from him; but he’s probably at Melton now.”", "“마지막으로 소식을 들었을 때는 스코틀랜드에 있었는데, 지금은 아마 멜턴에 있을 겁니다.”"]], [["“It’s deuced shabby of him, not hunting here in his own county.", "“제 고향 주에서 사냥을 하지 않다니 그 사람 참 지독하게 야박하군."], ["He escapes all the bore of going to lectures, and giving feeds to the neighbours; that’s why he treats us so.", "강연에 가는 골칫거리며 이웃들에게 대접을 차려 내는 골칫거리를 다 피해 버리니, 그래서 우리를 그렇게 대하는 거지요."], ["He has no idea of his duty, has he?”", "제 본분이라는 걸 도무지 모르지 않소?”"]], [["“Lady Lufton does all that, you know.”", "“그건 다 레이디 러프턴이 하시니까요.”"]], [["“I wish I’d a Mrs. Sowerby mère to do it for me.", "“나한테도 그 일을 대신 해 줄 어머니 사워비 부인이 하나 있었으면 좋겠어요."], ["But then Lufton has no constituents to look after—lucky dog!", "하지만 러프턴에게는 챙겨야 할 선거구민이 없으니—운 좋은 놈이지!"], ["By-the-by, has he spoken to you about selling that outlying bit of land of his in Oxfordshire?", "그런데 말이오, 그 사람이 옥스퍼드셔에 떨어져 있는 땅뙈기를 팔겠다는 이야기를 당신한테 했소?"], ["It belongs to the Lufton property, and yet it doesn’t.", "러프턴 소유에 딸린 것이면서 딸리지 않은 것 같은 땅인데."], ["In my mind it gives more trouble than it’s worth.”", "내 생각에는 값보다 골치를 더 안기는 땅이오.”"]], [["Lord Lufton had spoken to Mark about this sale, and had explained to him that such a sacrifice was absolutely necessary, in consequence of certain pecuniary transactions between him, Lord Lufton, and Mr. Sowerby.", "러프턴 경은 그 매각에 관해 마크에게 이야기한 바 있었고, 자기와 사워비 씨 사이의 어떤 금전 거래 때문에 그런 희생이 절대로 불가피하다고 설명한 바 있었다."], ["But it was found impracticable to complete the business without Lady Lufton’s knowledge, and her son had commissioned Mr. Robarts not only to inform her ladyship, but to talk her over, and to appease her wrath.", "그러나 레이디 러프턴이 모르는 채로 그 일을 마무리하기는 불가능하다는 것이 드러났고, 그 아들은 로바츠 씨에게 마님께 그 사실을 알리는 것뿐 아니라 그녀를 설득하고 그 노여움을 달래는 일까지 맡겨 두었다."], ["This commission he had not yet attempted to execute, and it was probable that this visit to Chaldicotes would not do much to facilitate the business.", "이 소임을 그는 아직 실행에 옮겨 보려는 시도조차 하지 않았고, 이번 콜디코츠 방문이 그 일을 수월하게 하는 데 별 도움이 되지 않을 것은 뻔한 일이었다."]], [["“They are the most magnificent islands under the sun,”", "“그것은 해 아래 가장 훌륭한 섬들이오.”"], ["said Harold Smith to the bishop.", "해럴드 스미스가 주교에게 말했다."]], [["“Are they, indeed!”", "“정말 그렇습니까!”"], ["said the bishop, opening his eyes wide, and assuming a look of intense interest.", "주교가 눈을 크게 뜨고 몹시 흥미롭다는 표정을 지으며 말했다."]], [["“And the most intelligent people.”", "“그리고 가장 총명한 사람들이오.”"]], [["“Dear me!”", "“이런!”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["“All they want is guidance, encouragement, instruction—”", "“그들에게 필요한 것은 지도와 격려와 가르침뿐이니—”"]], [["“And Christianity,”", "“그리고 기독교지요.”"], ["suggested the bishop.", "주교가 거들었다."]], [["“And Christianity, of course,”", "“그리고 물론 기독교요.”"], ["said Mr. Smith, remembering that he was speaking to a dignitary of the Church.", "스미스 씨가 자기가 교회의 고위 인사에게 말하고 있음을 떠올리고서 말했다."], ["It was well to humour such people, Mr. Smith thought.", "그런 사람들은 맞춰 주는 것이 좋다고 스미스 씨는 생각했다."], ["But the Christianity was to be done in the Sunday sermon, and was not part of his work.", "그러나 기독교는 일요일 설교에서 할 일이고, 자기 일의 몫은 아니었다."]], [["“And how do you intend to begin with them?” asked Mr.", "“그러면 그들에게 어떻게 착수하실 작정이오?”"], ["Supplehouse, the business of whose life it had been to suggest difficulties.", "서플하우스 씨가 물었다. 어려운 점을 들이대는 것이 평생의 일거리였던 사람이다."]], [["“Begin with them—oh—why—it’s very easy to begin with them.", "“그들에게 착수한다—오—왜—그들에게 착수하는 것은 아주 쉬운 일이오."], ["The difficulty is to go on with them, after the money is all spent.", "어려운 것은 돈이 다 떨어진 뒤에 그들과 함께 계속 나아가는 일이지."], ["We’ll begin by explaining to them the benefits of civilization.”", "우리는 그들에게 문명의 이로움을 설명하는 것으로 시작할 것이오.”"]], [["“Capital plan!”", "“기막힌 계획이오!”"], ["said Mr. Supplehouse.", "서플하우스 씨가 말했다."], ["“But how do you set about it, Smith?”", "“그런데 그 일에 어떻게 손을 대오, 스미스?”"]], [["“How do we set about it?", "“그 일에 어떻게 손을 대느냐고?"], ["How did we set about it with Australia and America?", "오스트레일리아와 아메리카에서는 그 일에 어떻게 손을 댔소?"], ["It is very easy to criticize; but in such matters the great thing is to put one’s shoulder to the wheel.”", "비판하기야 아주 쉽지. 그러나 그런 일에서 중요한 것은 수레바퀴에 어깨를 대고 미는 것이오.”"]], [["“We sent our felons to Australia,”", "“오스트레일리아에는 우리가 중죄인들을 보냈고,”"], ["said Supplehouse, “and they began the work for us.", "서플하우스가 말했다. “그자들이 우리 대신 그 일을 시작해 주었소."], ["And as to America, we exterminated the people instead of civilizing them.”", "그리고 아메리카로 말하면, 우리는 그 사람들을 문명화하는 대신 몰살했지.”"]], [["“We did not exterminate the inhabitants of India,”", "“우리가 인도의 주민들을 몰살하지는 않았소.”"], ["said Harold Smith, angrily.", "해럴드 스미스가 화를 내며 말했다."]], [["“Nor have we attempted to Christianize them, as the bishop so properly wishes to do with your islanders.”", "“그들을 기독교로 이끌려고 하지도 않았고. 주교님이 당신의 그 섬사람들을 두고 그토록 지당하게 바라시는 것처럼 말이오.”"]], [["“Supplehouse, you are not fair,” said Mr.", "“서플하우스, 당신은 공정하지 않소.”"], ["Sowerby, “neither to Harold Smith nor to us;—you are making him rehearse his lecture, which is bad for him; and making us hear the rehearsal, which is bad for us.”", "사워비 씨가 말했다. “해럴드 스미스에게도, 우리에게도 말이오.—당신은 저 사람에게 강연을 미리 연습하게 만들고 있는데 그건 저 사람에게 나쁜 일이고, 우리에게 그 연습을 듣게 만들고 있는데 그건 우리에게 나쁜 일이오.”"]], [["“Supplehouse belongs to a clique which monopolizes the wisdom of England,”", "“서플하우스는 영국의 지혜를 독차지하는 패거리에 속해 있소.”"], ["said Harold Smith; “or, at any rate, thinks that it does.", "해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “적어도 저들 생각으로는 그렇다는 거지."], ["But the worst of them is that they are given to talk leading articles.”", "그런데 그 무리의 가장 나쁜 점은 사설을 읊어 대는 버릇이 들었다는 것이오.”"]], [["“Better that, than talk articles which are not leading,”", "“남을 이끌지 못하는 글을 읊어 대는 것보다야 낫지요.”"], ["said Mr. Supplehouse.", "서플하우스 씨가 말했다."], ["“Some first-class official men do that.”", "“일류 관리라는 분들 가운데 더러 그런 이가 있소.”"]], [["“Shall I meet you at the duke’s next week, Mr. Robarts?”", "“다음 주에 공작님 댁에서 뵙게 되겠소, 로바츠 씨?”"], ["said the bishop to him, soon after they had gone into the drawing-room.", "그들이 응접실로 들어간 지 얼마 되지 않아 주교가 그에게 말했다."]], [["Meet him at the duke’s!—the established enemy of Barsetshire mankind, as Lady Lufton regarded his grace!", "공작의 댁에서 만난다니!—레이디 러프턴이 여기던 대로라면 각하는 바셋주 인류의 공인된 원수였다!"], ["No idea of going to the duke’s had ever entered our hero’s mind; nor had he been aware that the duke was about to entertain any one.", "공작의 댁에 간다는 생각은 우리 주인공의 머릿속에 한 번도 들어온 적이 없었다. 공작이 누구를 접대할 참이라는 것도 그는 알지 못했다."]], [["“No, my lord; I think not.", "“아닙니다, 주교님."], ["Indeed, I have no acquaintance with his grace.”", "그럴 일은 없을 것 같습니다. 실은 저는 각하와 아무 안면이 없습니다.”"]], [["“Oh—ah!", "“오—아!"], ["I did not know.", "나는 몰랐소."], ["Because Mr. Sowerby is going; and so are the Harold Smiths, and, I think, Mr.", "사워비 씨가 가고, 해럴드 스미스 부부도 가고, 내가 알기로는 서플하우스 씨도 가기에 말이오."], ["Supplehouse. An excellent man is the duke;—that is, as regards all the county interests,”", "공작은 훌륭한 사람이오.—그러니까, 주의 온갖 이해에 관련해서는 그렇다는 말이오.”"], ["added the bishop, remembering that the moral character of his bachelor grace was not the very best in the world.", "주교는 그 총각 각하의 도덕적 품성이 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 것은 아님을 떠올리고서 그렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["And then his lordship began to ask some questions about the church affairs of Framley, in which a little interest as to Framley Court was also mixed up, when he was interrupted by a rather sharp voice, to which he instantly attended.", "그러고서 주교님은 프램리의 교회 사정에 관해 몇 가지 질문을 하기 시작했는데, 그 안에는 프램리 코트에 대한 약간의 관심도 섞여 있었다. 그러다가 그는 다소 날카로운 목소리에 말이 끊겼고, 곧바로 그 목소리에 응했다."]], [["“Bishop,”", "“주교.”"], ["said the rather sharp voice; and the bishop trotted across the room to the back of the sofa, on which his wife was sitting.", "다소 날카로운 그 목소리가 말했다. 그러자 주교는 방을 가로질러 종종걸음으로 건너가, 자기 아내가 앉아 있는 소파의 등 뒤에 섰다."], ["“Miss Dunstable thinks that she will be able to come to us for a couple of days, after we leave the duke’s.”", "“던스터블 양이 공작님 댁을 떠난 뒤에 우리 집에 이틀쯤 와 주실 수 있을 것 같다고 하시오.”"]], [["“I shall be delighted above all things,”", "“무엇보다도 기쁜 일이오.”"], ["said the bishop, bowing low to the dominant lady of the day.", "주교가 그날의 지배자인 그 부인에게 허리를 깊이 숙이며 말했다."], ["For be it known to all men, that Miss Dunstable was the great heiress of that name.", "모든 사람이 알아 둘 일이거니와, 던스터블 양은 그 이름으로 알려진 굴지의 상속녀였다."]], [["“Mrs. Proudie is so very kind as to say that she will take me in, with my poodle, parrot, and pet old woman.”", "“프라우디 부인이 아주 친절하게도, 제 푸들이며 앵무새며 아끼는 노파까지 함께 받아 주시겠다고 하시네요.”"]], [["“I tell Miss Dunstable that we shall have quite room for any of her suite,”", "“던스터블 양의 수행하는 이들이라면 누구든 넉넉히 자리가 있다고 내가 말씀드렸소.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["“And that it will give us no trouble.”", "“그리고 우리에게 아무 수고도 되지 않는다고요.”"]], [["“‘The labour we delight in physics pain,’”", "“‘우리가 즐거이 하는 수고는 아픔을 고쳐 주지요.’”"], ["said the gallant bishop, bowing low, and putting his hand upon his heart.", "늠름한 주교가 허리를 깊이 숙이고 제 가슴에 손을 얹으며 말했다."]], [["In the meantime, Mr. Fothergill had got hold of Mark Robarts.", "그러는 동안 포더길 씨가 마크 로바츠를 붙잡았다."], ["Mr. Fothergill was a gentleman, and a magistrate of the county, but he occupied the position of managing man on the Duke of Omnium’s estates.", "포더길 씨는 신사였고 그 주의 치안 판사였으나, 옴니엄 공작 영지의 살림을 맡아 보는 사람의 자리를 차지하고 있었다."], ["He was not exactly his agent; that is to say, he did not receive his rents; but he “managed”", "정확히 공작의 대리인은 아니었다. 다시 말해 지대를 받아들이지는 않았다."], ["for him, saw people, went about the county, wrote letters, supported the electioneering interest, did popularity when it was too much trouble for the duke to do it himself, and was, in fact, invaluable.", "그러나 공작을 위해 “관리”를 했고, 사람들을 만났고, 주 안을 돌아다녔고, 편지를 썼고, 선거 운동의 이해를 뒷받침했고, 공작이 몸소 나서기에 너무 번거로운 인기 관리 노릇도 해 주었으니, 실로 값을 매길 수 없는 사람이었다."], ["People in West Barsetshire would often say that they did not know what on earth the duke would do, if it were not for Mr. Fothergill.", "서바셋주 사람들은 포더길 씨가 없다면 공작이 대체 무엇을 어떻게 할지 도무지 모르겠다고 흔히 말했다."], ["Indeed, Mr. Fothergill was useful to the duke.", "과연 포더길 씨는 공작에게 요긴한 사람이었다."]], [["“Mr. Robarts,”", "“로바츠 씨,”"], ["he said, “I am very happy to have the pleasure of meeting you—very happy, indeed.", "그가 말했다. “뵙게 되어 정말 반갑습니다—정말로 반갑습니다."], ["I have often heard of you from our friend Sowerby.”", "우리 친구 사워비에게서 선생 말씀을 자주 들었지요.”"]], [["Mark bowed, and said that he was delighted to have the honour of making Mr. Fothergill’s acquaintance.", "마크는 고개를 숙이고, 포더길 씨와 알게 되는 영광을 얻어 기쁘다고 말했다."]], [["“I am commissioned by the Duke of Omnium,” continued Mr.", "“옴니엄 공작 각하께서 저에게 소임을 맡기셨습니다.”"], ["Fothergill, “to say how glad he will be if you will join his grace’s party at Gatherum Castle, next week.", "포더길 씨가 말을 이었다. “다음 주에 개서럼 성에서 열리는 각하의 모임에 선생께서 함께해 주시면 각하께서 얼마나 기뻐하실지 전해 달라고요."], ["The bishop will be there, and indeed nearly the whole set who are here now.", "주교님도 오실 것이고, 실은 지금 여기 모인 이들 거의 전부가 옵니다."], ["The duke would have written when he heard that you were to be at Chaldicotes; but things were hardly quite arranged then, so his grace has left it for me to tell you how happy he will be to make your acquaintance in his own house.", "선생께서 콜디코츠에 오신다는 말을 들으셨을 때 각하께서 직접 편지를 쓰려 하셨지만, 그때는 일이 아직 다 정해지지 않았던 터라, 각하께서 저에게 남겨 두시고 선생을 자기 집에서 알게 되면 얼마나 기쁠지 대신 말씀드리라 하셨지요. 사워비에게도 말해 두었는데,”"], ["I have spoken to Sowerby,”", "포더길 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["continued Mr. Fothergill, “and he very much hopes that you will be able to join us.”", "“그도 선생이 우리와 함께해 주시기를 몹시 바라고 있습니다.”"]], [["Mark felt that his face became red when this proposition was made to him.", "이런 제안을 받고서 마크는 자기 얼굴이 붉어지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["The party in the county to which he properly belonged—he and his wife, and all that made him happy and respectable—looked upon the Duke of Omnium with horror and amazement; and now he had absolutely received an invitation to the duke’s house!", "그가 원래 속해 있는 그 주의 무리는—그 자신과 그의 아내, 그리고 그를 행복하고 떳떳하게 만들어 주는 그 모든 것은—옴니엄 공작을 경악과 공포로 바라보았다. 그런데 이제 그가 공작의 집으로 오라는 초대를 실제로 받은 것이다!"], ["A proposition was made to him that he should be numbered among the duke’s friends!", "그가 공작의 벗들 가운데 하나로 헤아려지도록 하겠다는 제안이 그에게 온 것이다!"]], [["And though in one sense he was sorry that the proposition was made to him, yet in another he was proud of it.", "그리고 그 제안이 자기에게 온 것이 한편으로는 안타까웠으나, 다른 한편으로는 그것이 자랑스러웠다."], ["It is not every young man, let his profession be what it may, who can receive overtures of friendship from dukes without some elation.", "직업이 무엇이든, 공작에게서 우정의 제안을 받고서 조금도 우쭐하지 않을 젊은 남자는 세상에 흔하지 않다."], ["Mark, too, had risen in the world, as far as he had yet risen, by knowing great people; and he certainly had an ambition to rise higher.", "게다가 마크는 여기까지 올라온 만큼은 훌륭한 사람들을 알아서 올라온 것이었고, 더 높이 올라가고 싶은 야심이 분명히 있었다."], ["I will not degrade him by calling him a tuft-hunter; but he undoubtedly had a feeling that the paths most pleasant for a clergyman’s feet were those which were trodden by the great ones of the earth.", "그를 깎아내려 귀인 쫓아다니는 자라고 부르지는 않겠다. 그러나 성직자의 발에 가장 유쾌한 길은 세상의 위대한 이들이 밟는 길이라는 느낌을 그가 지녔던 것은 틀림없다."]], [["Nevertheless, at the moment he declined the duke’s invitation.", "그렇지만 그 자리에서는 그는 공작의 초대를 사양했다."], ["He was very much flattered, he said, but the duties of his parish would require him to return direct from Chaldicotes to Framley.", "대단히 과분한 말씀이지만 교구의 본분 때문에 콜디코츠에서 곧장 프램리로 돌아가야 한다고 그는 말했다."]], [["“You need not give me an answer to-night, you know,”", "“오늘 밤에 저에게 답을 주실 필요는 없습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Fothergill.", "포더길 씨가 말했다."], ["“Before the week is past, we will talk it over with Sowerby and the bishop.", "“이번 주가 지나기 전에 사워비와 주교님과 함께 그 이야기를 해 보지요."], ["It will be a thousand pities, Mr. Robarts, if you will allow me to say so, that you should neglect such an opportunity of knowing his grace.”", "이런 말씀을 드려도 된다면, 로바츠 씨, 각하를 알게 될 이런 기회를 그냥 흘려보내신다면 그건 천 번 아까운 일일 겁니다.”"]], [["When Mark went to bed, his mind was still set against going to the duke’s; but, nevertheless, he did feel that it was a pity that he should not do so.", "마크가 잠자리에 들 때에도 그의 마음은 여전히 공작의 댁에 가지 않겠다고 굳어져 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 가지 않는 것이 아깝다는 느낌을 지녔다."], ["After all, was it necessary that he should obey Lady Lufton in all things?", "결국, 그가 레이디 러프턴에게 모든 일에서 복종해야 할 이유가 있는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER IV. A MATTER OF CONSCIENCE.", "# 제4장. 양심에 걸리는 일"]], [["It is no doubt very wrong to long after a naughty thing.", "못된 것을 갈망하는 일은 의심할 여지 없이 아주 나쁘다."], ["But nevertheless we all do so.", "그러나 그럼에도 우리는 모두 그렇게 한다."], ["One may say that hankering after naughty things is the very essence of the evil into which we have been precipitated by Adam’s fall.", "못된 것을 탐내는 마음이야말로 아담의 타락으로 우리가 곤두박질쳐 떨어진 그 악의 본질이라고 말할 수도 있다."], ["When we confess that we are all sinners, we confess that we all long after naughty things.", "우리가 모두 죄인이라고 고백할 때, 우리는 우리 모두가 못된 것을 갈망한다고 고백하는 것이다."]], [["And ambition is a great vice—as Mark Antony told us a long time ago—a great vice, no doubt, if the ambition of the man be with reference to his own advancement, and not to the advancement of others.", "그리고 야망은 큰 악덕이다 — 오래전에 안토니우스가 우리에게 일러 주었듯이 — 그 사람의 야망이 남의 영달이 아니라 제 자신의 영달을 향한 것이라면, 그것은 의심할 여지 없이 큰 악덕이다."], ["But then, how many of us are there who are not ambitious in this vicious manner?", "그러나 그렇다면 우리 가운데 이 악덕의 방식으로 야망을 품지 않은 자가 몇이나 되는가?"]], [["And there is nothing viler than the desire to know great people—people of great rank, I should say; nothing worse than the hunting of titles and worshipping of wealth.", "그리고 지체 높은 사람들과 알고 지내려는 욕망보다 더 천한 것은 없다 — 신분이 높은 사람들이라고 해야 하겠다. 작위를 사냥하고 재물을 숭배하는 짓보다 더 나쁜 것은 없다."], ["We all know this, and say it every day of our lives.", "우리는 모두 이것을 알고 있고, 살아가는 하루하루 그렇게 말한다."], ["But presuming that a way into the society of Park Lane was open to us, and a way also into that of Bedford Row, how many of us are there who would prefer Bedford Row because it is so vile to worship wealth and title?", "그러나 파크 레인의 사교계로 드는 길이 우리에게 열려 있고 베드퍼드 로의 사교계로 드는 길 또한 열려 있다고 가정한다면, 재물과 작위를 숭배하는 것이 하도 천한 일이라 하여 베드퍼드 로를 택할 사람이 우리 가운데 몇이나 되겠는가?"]], [["I am led into these rather trite remarks by the necessity of putting forward some sort of excuse for that frame of mind in which the Rev. Mark Robarts awoke on the morning after his arrival at Chaldicotes.", "내가 이런 다소 진부한 언설로 끌려 들어온 것은, 콜디코츠에 도착한 이튿날 아침 마크 로바츠 목사가 눈을 뜰 때 그가 놓여 있던 그 마음의 상태에 대하여 무언가 변명 같은 것을 앞세워 둘 필요가 있기 때문이다."], ["And I trust that the fact of his being a clergyman will not be allowed to press against him unfairly.", "그리고 그가 성직자라는 사실이 그에게 부당하게 불리하도록 작용하지는 않기를 바란다."], ["Clergymen are subject to the same passions as other men; and, as far as I can see, give way to them, in one line or in another, almost as frequently.", "성직자도 다른 사람들과 똑같은 정념에 지배된다. 그리고 내가 보는 한, 이런 방면으로든 저런 방면으로든 그 정념에 굴복하는 일도 거의 같은 빈도로 있다."], ["Every clergyman should, by canonical rule, feel a personal disinclination to a bishopric; but yet we do not believe that such personal disinclination is generally very strong.", "교회법의 규칙에 따르면 모든 성직자는 주교직에 대하여 개인적으로는 마음이 끌리지 않아야 마땅하지만, 그러나 그런 개인적인 무심함이 대체로 아주 강하다고는 우리는 믿지 않는다."]], [["Mark’s first thoughts when he woke on that morning flew back to Mr.", "그날 아침 잠에서 깬 마크의 첫 생각은 포더길 씨의 초대로 날아갔다."], ["Fothergill’s invitation. The duke had sent a special message to say how peculiarly glad he, the duke, would be to make acquaintance with him, the parson!", "공작은 특별히 전갈을 보내어, 그 자신, 곧 공작이 그와, 곧 목사와 알게 되는 것을 얼마나 각별히 기쁘게 여기는지를 전해 왔던 것이다!"], ["How much of this message had been of Mr. Fothergill’s own manufacture, that Mark Robarts did not consider.", "그 전갈 가운데 얼마만큼이 포더길 씨 자신의 손에서 만들어진 것인지는 마크 로바츠가 헤아려 보지 않았다."]], [["He had obtained a living at an age when other young clergymen are beginning to think of a curacy, and he had obtained such a living as middle-aged parsons in their dreams regard as a possible Paradise for their old years.", "다른 젊은 성직자들이 부목사 자리나 생각하기 시작할 나이에 그는 성직록을 얻었고, 그것도 중년의 목사들이 꿈속에서나 노년의 낙원으로 여길 만한 성직록을 얻었다."], ["Of course he thought that all these good things had been the results of his own peculiar merits.", "물론 그는 이 모든 좋은 것들이 자기 자신의 남다른 공덕의 결과라고 생각했다."], ["Of course he felt that he was different from other parsons,—more fitted by nature for intimacy with great persons, more urbane, more polished, and more richly endowed with modern clerical well-to-do aptitudes.", "물론 그는 자기가 다른 목사들과는 다르다고 느꼈다. 지체 높은 사람들과 가까이 지내기에 천성이 더 알맞고, 더 도회적이고, 더 세련되고, 요즘 성직자에게 어울리는 유복한 재간을 더 넉넉히 타고났다고."], ["He was grateful to Lady Lufton for what she had done for him; but perhaps not so grateful as he should have been.", "그는 레이디 러프턴이 자기에게 해 준 일에 감사하고 있었다. 그러나 아마 마땅히 감사해야 할 만큼은 아니었다."]], [["At any rate he was not Lady Lufton’s servant, nor even her dependant.", "어쨌든 그는 레이디 러프턴의 하인이 아니었고, 그녀에게 매여 사는 사람조차 아니었다."], ["So much he had repeated to himself on many occasions, and had gone so far as to hint the same idea to his wife.", "그만한 말은 그가 여러 번 스스로에게 되뇌었고, 아내에게까지 그런 뜻을 슬쩍 내비칠 정도로 나아간 바 있었다."], ["In his career as parish priest he must in most things be the judge of his own actions—and in many also it was his duty to be the judge of those of his patroness.", "교구 사제로서의 이력에서 그는 대부분의 일에 대하여 자기 행동의 심판자여야 했다. 그리고 많은 일에서는 후원자인 그 부인의 행동을 심판하는 것 또한 그의 의무였다."], ["The fact of Lady Lufton having placed him in the living, could by no means make her the proper judge of his actions.", "레이디 러프턴이 그를 그 성직록에 앉혔다는 사실이 그녀를 그의 행동에 대한 온당한 심판자로 만들어 줄 수는 결코 없었다."], ["This he often said to himself; and he said as often that Lady Lufton certainly had a hankering after such a judgment-seat.", "이 말을 그는 자주 스스로에게 했다. 그리고 레이디 러프턴이 분명히 그런 심판석을 탐내고 있다는 말도 그만큼 자주 했다."]], [["Of whom generally did prime ministers and official bigwigs think it expedient to make bishops and deans?", "대체로 총리와 관가의 높은 분들은 누구를 주교와 수석 사제로 만드는 것이 마땅하다고 여겼던가?"], ["Was it not, as a rule, of those clergymen who had shown themselves able to perform their clerical duties efficiently, and able also to take their place with ease in high society?", "대개 성직자의 직무를 유능하게 수행할 수 있음을 보여 준 데다 상류 사교계에서도 편안하게 제자리를 잡을 수 있는 성직자들이 아니었던가? 그는 프램리에서 분명히 아주 잘 지내고 있었다."], ["He was very well off certainly at Framley; but he could never hope for anything beyond Framley, if he allowed himself to regard Lady Lufton as a bugbear.", "그러나 레이디 러프턴을 무서운 도깨비로 여기도록 자신을 내버려 둔다면 프램리 너머의 무엇도 결코 바랄 수 없을 것이었다."], ["Putting Lady Lufton and her prejudices out of the question, was there any reason why he ought not to accept the duke’s invitation?", "레이디 러프턴과 그녀의 편견을 문제 밖으로 밀어 둔다면, 그가 공작의 초대를 받아들여서는 안 될 이유가 있는가?"], ["He could not see that there was any such reason.", "그런 이유가 있다고는 그로서는 볼 수 없었다."], ["If any one could be a better judge on such a subject than himself, it must be his bishop.", "이런 문제에 대하여 자기보다 더 나은 심판자가 있을 수 있다면 그것은 그의 주교여야 했다."], ["And it was clear that the bishop wished him to go to Gatherum Castle.", "그리고 주교가 그더러 개서럼 성에 가기를 바란다는 것은 분명했다."]], [["The matter was still left open to him. Mr.", "그 문제는 아직 그에게 열린 채로 남아 있었다."], ["Fothergill had especially explained that; and therefore his ultimate decision was as yet within his own power.", "포더길 씨가 특별히 그렇게 설명해 두었다. 그러므로 마지막 결정은 아직 그 자신의 손안에 있었다."], ["Such a visit would cost him some money, for he knew that a man does not stay at great houses without expense; and then, in spite of his good income, he was not very flush of money.", "그런 방문에는 돈이 좀 들 것이었다. 큰 집에 머무는 데 비용이 들지 않을 수 없음을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["He had been down this year with Lord Lufton in Scotland.", "그리고 수입이 넉넉한데도 그는 수중에 현금이 그리 넉넉하지 않았다. 그해에 그는 러프턴 경과 함께 스코틀랜드에 다녀왔다."], ["Perhaps it might be more prudent for him to return home.", "아마 집으로 돌아가는 편이 더 신중한 처사일지도 몰랐다."]], [["But then an idea came to him that it behoved him as a man and a priest to break through that Framley thraldom under which he felt that he did to a certain extent exist.", "그러나 그때 한 가지 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다. 어느 정도는 자기가 그 안에서 살고 있다고 느끼는 프램리의 예속을 한 사람의 남자로서 또 한 사람의 사제로서 깨뜨리는 것이 마땅하다는 생각이었다."], ["Was it not the fact that he was about to decline this invitation from fear of Lady Lufton?", "그가 이 초대를 사양하려 하는 것은 레이디 러프턴이 두려워서가 아닌가?"], ["and if so, was that a motive by which he ought to be actuated?", "그렇다면 그것이 그를 움직여야 할 동기인가?"], ["It was incumbent on him to rid himself of that feeling.", "그런 감정을 스스로 떨어내는 것이 그의 책무였다."], ["And in this spirit he got up and dressed.", "그리고 그런 마음가짐으로 그는 일어나 옷을 입었다."]], [["There was hunting again on that day; and as the hounds were to meet near Chaldicotes, and to draw some coverts lying on the verge of the Chace, the ladies were to go in carriages through the drives of the forest, and Mr.", "그날도 사냥이 있었다. 그리고 사냥개들이 콜디코츠 근처에서 모여 체이스 가장자리에 놓인 덤불숲을 뒤지기로 되어 있었으므로, 부인들은 마차를 타고 그 숲의 길들을 지나가고, 로바츠 씨가 말을 타고 그들을 호위하기로 되어 있었다."], ["Robarts was to escort them on horseback.", "사실 그날은 사냥 자체보다는 부인들을 위해 마련된 그런 사냥날 가운데 하나였다."], ["Indeed it was one of those hunting-days got up rather for the ladies than for the sport.", "그런 날은 착실한 중년의 사냥꾼들에게는 대단한 골칫거리다. 그러나 젊은 친구들은 그런 날을 좋아한다."], ["Great nuisances they are to steady, middle-aged hunting men; but the young fellows like them because they have thereby an opportunity of showing off their sporting finery, and of doing a little flirtation on horseback.", "사냥 나들이옷의 멋을 뽐내고 말 위에서 약간의 연애 놀음을 할 기회가 생기기 때문이다. 주교도 그 일행에 끼일 마음이 있었다."], ["The bishop, also, had been minded to be of the party; so, at least, he had said on the previous evening; and a place in one of the carriages had been set apart for him: but since that, he and Mrs.", "적어도 전날 저녁에는 그렇게 말했다. 그리고 마차 하나에 그의 자리가 따로 마련되어 있었다."], ["Proudie had discussed the matter in private, and at breakfast his lordship declared that he had changed his mind.", "그러나 그 뒤에 그와 프라우디 부인이 그 문제를 따로 의논했고, 아침 식사 자리에서 주교께서는 마음이 바뀌었다고 선언했다."]], [["Mr. Sowerby was one of those men who are known to be very poor—as poor as debt can make a man—but who, nevertheless, enjoy all the luxuries which money can give.", "사워비 씨는 몹시 가난한 사람으로 알려진 — 빚이 사람을 가난하게 만들 수 있는 그만큼 가난한 — 그러면서도 돈이 줄 수 있는 온갖 사치를 다 누리는 그런 사람들 가운데 하나였다."], ["It was believed that he could not live in England out of jail but for his protection as a member of Parliament; and yet it seemed that there was no end to his horses and carriages, his servants and retinue.", "국회의원이라는 신분의 보호가 없다면 그는 감옥 밖에서 영국에 살 수 없을 것이라고들 믿었다. 그런데도 그의 말과 마차, 하인과 시종들에는 끝이 없는 것 같았다."], ["He had been at this work for a great many years, and practice, they say, makes perfect.", "그는 아주 여러 해 이 일에 종사해 왔고, 익히면 완벽해진다고 사람들은 말한다."], ["Such companions are very dangerous.", "그런 동료는 몹시 위험하다."], ["There is no cholera, no yellow-fever, no small-pox more contagious than debt.", "빚보다 더 잘 옮는 콜레라도, 황열도, 천연두도 없다."], ["If one lives habitually among embarrassed men, one catches it to a certainty.", "궁색한 사람들 사이에서 늘 살다 보면 반드시 그것에 걸린다."], ["No one had injured the community in this way more fatally than Mr. Sowerby.", "이런 방식으로 사워비 씨보다 더 치명적으로 그 사회를 해친 사람은 없었다."], ["But still he carried on the game himself; and now on this morning carriages and horses thronged at his gate, as though he were as substantially rich as his friend the Duke of Omnium.", "그러나 여전히 그 자신은 그 판을 이어 나갔다. 그리고 이제 이날 아침, 마치 그가 그의 벗 옴니엄 공작만큼이나 실속 있게 부유하기라도 한 듯이 마차와 말들이 그의 문간에 몰려들었다."]], [["“Robarts, my dear fellow,”", "“로바츠, 이 친구,”"], ["said Mr.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."], ["Sowerby, when they were well under way down one of the glades of the forest,—for the place where the hounds met was some four or five miles from the house of Chaldicotes,—“ride on with me a moment.", "그들이 숲의 빈터 길 하나를 따라 한참 나아갔을 때였다 — 사냥개들이 모이는 곳은 콜디코츠의 집에서 사오 마일 떨어져 있었으므로. “나와 잠깐 앞서 갑시다."], ["I want to speak to you; and if I stay behind we shall never get to the hounds.”", "할 말이 있어요. 그리고 내가 뒤에 남으면 우리는 사냥개들한테 영영 못 갈 겁니다.”"], ["So Mark, who had come expressly to escort the ladies, rode on alongside of Mr. Sowerby in his pink coat.", "그리하여 부인들을 호위하려고 일부러 나온 마크는 붉은 사냥 상의를 입은 사워비 씨와 나란히 말을 몰았다."]], [["“My dear fellow, Fothergill tells me that you have some hesitation about going to Gatherum Castle.”", "“이 친구, 포더길이 그러던데, 개서럼 성에 가는 일을 좀 망설인다고요.”"]], [["“Well, I did decline, certainly.", "“글쎄, 사양하기는 했지요, 분명히."], ["You know I am not a man of pleasure, as you are.", "나는 당신처럼 향락을 좇는 사람이 아니잖아요."], ["I have some duties to attend to.”", "나에게는 돌봐야 할 직무가 좀 있습니다.”"]], [["“Gammon!” said Mr.", "“허튼소리!”"], ["Sowerby; and as he said it he looked with a kind of derisive smile into the clergyman’s face.", "사워비 씨가 말했다. 그리고 그 말을 하면서 그는 일종의 비웃는 미소를 띠고 성직자의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."]], [["“It is easy enough to say that, Sowerby; and perhaps I have no right to expect that you should understand me.”", "“그런 말은 하기야 쉽지요, 사워비. 그리고 당신이 나를 이해해 주기를 기대할 권리도 아마 나에게는 없겠고요.”"]], [["“Ah, but I do understand you; and I say it is gammon.", "“아니, 나는 당신을 이해합니다. 그러니까 허튼소리라고 하는 거예요."], ["I would be the last man in the world to ridicule your scruples about duty, if this hesitation on your part arose from any such scruple.", "당신이 이렇게 망설이는 것이 정말로 그런 양심의 거리낌에서 나온 것이라면, 직무에 대한 당신의 거리낌을 비웃는 일에 나는 세상에서 가장 마지막에 나설 사람입니다."], ["But answer me honestly, do you not know that such is not the case?”", "하지만 정직하게 대답해 봐요. 사정이 그렇지 않다는 것을 당신도 알고 있지 않나요?”"]], [["“I know nothing of the kind.”", "“나는 그런 건 전혀 모릅니다.”"]], [["“Ah, but I think you do.", "“아니, 내 생각에는 알고 있어요."], ["If you persist in refusing this invitation will it not be because you are afraid of making Lady Lufton angry?", "이 초대를 끝까지 거절한다면, 그건 레이디 러프턴을 노엽게 만드는 것이 두려워서가 아닐까요?"], ["I do not know what there can be in that woman that she is able to hold both you and Lufton in leading-strings.”", "그 부인에게 무엇이 있어서 당신과 러프턴을 둘 다 걸음마 끈에 매어 둘 수 있는지 나는 모르겠군요.”"]], [["Robarts, of course, denied the charge and protested that he was not to be taken back to his own parsonage by any fear of Lady Lufton.", "로바츠는 물론 그 혐의를 부인하며, 레이디 러프턴이 두려워서 자기 목사관으로 되돌아갈 사람이 아니라고 항변했다."], ["But though he made such protest with warmth, he knew that he did so ineffectually.", "그러나 그렇게 열을 내어 항변하면서도 그 항변에 힘이 없다는 것을 그 자신이 알고 있었다."], ["Sowerby only smiled and said that the proof of the pudding was in the eating.", "사워비는 그저 미소를 지으며, 푸딩의 맛은 먹어 봐야 안다고 말했다."]], [["“What is the good of a man keeping a curate if it be not to save him from that sort of drudgery?”", "“그런 고된 잡일에서 벗어나려는 것이 아니라면, 사람이 부목사를 두는 것이 무슨 소용이 있습니까?”"], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Drudgery!", "“잡일이라니!"], ["If I were a drudge how could I be here to-day?”", "내가 잡일에 매인 사람이라면 오늘 여기 있을 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["“Well, Robarts, look here.", "“자, 로바츠, 들어 보세요."], ["I am speaking now, perhaps, with more of the energy of an old friend than circumstances fully warrant; but I am an older man than you, and as I have a regard for you I do not like to see you throw up a good game when it is in your hands.”", "지금 내가 하는 말은 아마 사정이 온전히 허락하는 것보다 더 오랜 벗의 기세로 하는 말일 겁니다. 하지만 나는 당신보다 나이가 많은 사람이고, 당신을 마음에 두고 있으니 손안에 든 좋은 판을 당신이 내던지는 것을 보고 싶지 않아요.”"]], [["“Oh, as far as that goes, Sowerby, I need hardly tell you that I appreciate your kindness.”", "“아, 그 점이라면, 사워비, 당신의 호의를 고맙게 여긴다고는 새삼 말할 필요도 없겠지요.”"]], [["“If you are content,”", "“당신이 평생 프램리에 살면서 그곳 노부인의 햇볕이나 쬐고 있는 것으로 만족한다면,”"], ["continued the man of the world, “to live at Framley all your life, and to warm yourself in the sunshine of the dowager there, why, in such case, it may perhaps be useless for you to extend the circle of your friends; but if you have higher ideas than these, I think you will be very wrong to omit the present opportunity of going to the duke’s.", "세상 물정에 밝은 그 사나이가 말을 이었다. “왜, 그런 경우라면 벗의 범위를 넓히는 일이 당신에게 아마 소용없을지도 모르지요."], ["I never knew the duke go so much out of his way to be civil to a clergyman as he has done in this instance.”", "하지만 그보다 더 높은 생각을 품고 있다면, 공작의 집에 가는 이번 기회를 빠뜨리는 것은 크게 잘못하는 일이라고 나는 생각합니다. 공작이 이번처럼 성직자에게 예를 갖추려고 그렇게까지 일부러 나서는 것은 나도 여태 본 적이 없어요.”"]], [["“I am sure I am very much obliged to him.”", "“정말이지 그분께 대단히 감사하고 있습니다.”"]], [["“The fact is, that you may, if you please, make yourself popular in the county; but you cannot do it by obeying all Lady Lufton’s behests.", "“사실은, 당신이 마음만 먹으면 이 주에서 인기를 얻을 수 있어요. 하지만 레이디 러프턴의 분부를 다 따르면서 그럴 수는 없습니다."], ["She is a dear old woman, I am sure.”", "참 좋은 노부인이지요, 그건 나도 압니다.”"]], [["“She is, Sowerby; and you would say so, if you knew her.”", "“그렇습니다, 사워비. 당신도 그분을 알게 되면 그렇게 말할 겁니다.”"]], [["“I don’t doubt it; but it would not do for you or me to live exactly according to her ideas.", "“의심하지 않아요. 하지만 당신이나 내가 딱 그분의 생각대로 사는 것은 될 일이 아니지요."], ["Now, here, in this case, the bishop of the diocese is to be one of the party, and he has, I believe, already expressed a wish that you should be another.”", "그런데 이번 경우에는 교구의 주교가 그 일행 가운데 한 사람이고, 그분은 내가 알기로 당신도 또 한 사람이 되기를 이미 바란다고 말했어요.”"]], [["“He asked me if I were going.”", "“가는지 나에게 물으셨습니다.”"]], [["“Exactly; and Archdeacon Grantly will be there.”", "“바로 그겁니다. 그리고 그랜틀리 부주교도 갈 거예요.”"]], [["“Will he?”", "“그래요?”"], ["asked Mark.", "마크가 물었다."], ["Now, that would be a great point gained, for Archdeacon Grantly was a close friend of Lady Lufton.", "그건 크게 얻는 점이 될 것이었다. 그랜틀리 부주교는 레이디 러프턴의 가까운 벗이었으니까."]], [["“So I understand from Fothergill.", "“포더길에게 그렇게 들었어요. 정말이지 당신이 가지 않는다면 그건 아주 잘못하는 일입니다."], ["Indeed, it will be very wrong of you not to go, and I tell you so plainly; and what is more, when you talk about your duty—you having a curate as you have—why, it is gammon.”", "나는 이 말을 툭 터놓고 하는 거예요. 게다가 당신처럼 부목사를 두고 있으면서 직무 이야기를 한다면 — 그거야, 허튼소리지요.”"], ["These last words he spoke looking back over his shoulder as he stood up in his stirrups, for he had caught the eye of the huntsman, who was surrounded by his hounds, and was now trotting on to join him.", "이 마지막 말은 그가 등자를 밟고 일어선 채로 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 한 말이었다. 사냥개들에 둘러싸여 있던 사냥개 몰이꾼이 눈에 들어왔고, 그가 이제 그에게 합류하려고 속보로 달려오고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["During a great portion of the day, Mark found himself riding by the side of Mrs.", "낮의 상당 부분 동안 마크는 프라우디 부인의 마차 옆에서 말을 타고 가는 자신을 발견했다."], ["Proudie, as that lady leaned back in her carriage. And Mrs.", "그 부인은 마차에 등을 기대고 앉아 있었다."], ["Proudie smiled on him graciously, though her daughter would not do so. Mrs.", "그리고 프라우디 부인은 그에게 정중하게 미소를 보냈다."], ["Proudie was fond of having an attendant clergyman; and as it was evident that Mr.", "그 딸은 그렇게 하지 않았지만. 프라우디 부인은 성직자를 하나 곁에 딸리고 다니기를 좋아했다."], ["Robarts lived among nice people—titled dowagers, members of Parliament, and people of that sort—she was quite willing to instal him as a sort of honorary chaplain pro tem.", "그리고 로바츠 씨가 좋은 사람들 사이에서 — 작위를 가진 노부인들, 국회의원들, 그런 부류의 사람들 사이에서 — 살고 있음이 분명했으므로, 그녀는 그를 일종의 임시 명예 전속 사제로 앉히는 데 아주 기꺼웠다."]], [["“I’ll tell you what we have settled, Mrs. Harold Smith and I,”", "“우리가 어떻게 정했는지 말씀드리지요, 해럴드 스미스 부인과 내가,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie to him.", "프라우디 부인이 그에게 말했다."], ["“This lecture at Barchester will be so late on Saturday evening, that you had all better come and dine with us.”", "“바체스터에서 하는 그 강연이 토요일 저녁 아주 늦게까지 갈 테니, 여러분 모두 우리 집에 와서 저녁을 드시는 것이 좋겠소.”"]], [["Mark bowed and thanked her, and declared that he should be very happy to make one of such a party.", "마크는 고개를 숙여 감사를 표하고, 그런 자리에 한몫 끼는 것이 아주 기쁘겠다고 말했다. 이 일에는 레이디 러프턴도 반대할 수 없을 것이었다."], ["Even Lady Lufton could not object to this, although she was not especially fond of Mrs. Proudie.", "그녀가 프라우디 부인을 특별히 좋아하지는 않았지만."]], [["“And then they are to sleep at the hotel.", "“그리고 그다음에 그분들은 호텔에서 자기로 되어 있소."], ["It will really be too late for ladies to think of going back so far at this time of the year.", "해가 이맘때인데 부인들이 그렇게 먼 길을 되돌아갈 생각을 하기에는 정말 너무 늦은 시각일 테니까."], ["I told Mrs. Harold Smith, and Miss Dunstable, too, that we could manage to make room at any rate for them.", "나는 해럴드 스미스 부인에게, 던스터블 양에게도, 그분들만은 어떻게든 방을 마련할 수 있다고 말했소."], ["But they will not leave the other ladies; so they go to the hotel for that night.", "그러나 다른 부인들을 두고 가려 하지 않으니, 그 밤은 호텔로 가는 거요."], ["But, Mr. Robarts, the bishop will never allow you to stay at the inn, so of course you will take a bed at the palace.”", "하지만 로바츠 씨, 주교는 결코 당신을 여관에 묵게 두지 않을 테니, 물론 당신은 주교관에서 침대를 하나 쓰게 될 거요.”"]], [["It immediately occurred to Mark that as the lecture was to be given on Saturday evening, the next morning would be Sunday; and, on that Sunday, he would have to preach at Chaldicotes.", "강연이 토요일 저녁에 열린다면 그 이튿날 아침은 일요일이고, 그 일요일에 자기는 콜디코츠에서 설교를 해야 한다는 생각이 곧바로 마크에게 떠올랐다."], ["“I thought they were all going to return the same night,”", "“모두 그날 밤에 돌아오시는 줄 알았습니다,”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Well, they did intend it; but you see Mrs.", "“그야 그럴 생각이었지요."], ["Smith is afraid.”", "하지만 보시오, 스미스 부인이 겁을 내니.”"]], [["“I should have to get back here on the Sunday morning, Mrs. Proudie.”", "“저는 일요일 아침에 여기로 돌아와 있어야 합니다, 프라우디 부인.”"]], [["“Ah, yes, that is bad—very bad, indeed.", "“아, 그렇군, 그건 곤란하오 — 정말이지 아주 곤란하오."], ["No one dislikes any interference with the Sabbath more than I do.", "안식일을 침범하는 일이라면 나만큼 싫어하는 사람도 없소."], ["Indeed, if I am particular about anything it is about that.", "정말이지, 내가 무엇 하나에 까다롭다면 바로 그것에 까다롭지요."], ["But some works are works of necessity, Mr. Robarts; are they not?", "그러나 어떤 일은 부득이한 일이 아니겠소, 로바츠 씨?"], ["Now you must necessarily be back at Chaldicotes on Sunday morning!”", "그런데 지금 당신은 일요일 아침에 부득이 콜디코츠로 돌아와 있어야 하오!”"], ["And so the matter was settled. Mrs.", "그리하여 그 문제는 그렇게 정해졌다."], ["Proudie was very firm in general in the matter of Sabbath-day observances; but when she had to deal with such persons as Mrs.", "프라우디 부인은 대체로 안식일을 지키는 문제에서 아주 단호했다."], ["Harold Smith, it was expedient that she should give way a little.", "그러나 해럴드 스미스 부인 같은 사람들을 상대해야 할 때에는 조금 물러 주는 것이 마땅했다."], ["“You can start as soon as it’s daylight, you know, if you like it, Mr. Robarts,”", "“날이 밝는 대로 곧 떠나셔도 되오, 그러고 싶으시면 말이오, 로바츠 씨,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["There was not much to boast of as to the hunting, but it was a very pleasant day for the ladies.", "사냥으로 자랑할 만한 것은 별로 없었으나, 부인들에게는 아주 즐거운 하루였다. 남자들은 체이스를 가로지르는 풀길을 오르내리며 말을 달렸다."], ["The men rode up and down the grass roads through the Chace, sometimes in the greatest possible hurry as though they never could go quick enough; and then the coachmen would drive very fast also, though they did not know why, for a fast pace of movement is another of those contagious diseases.", "때로는 아무리 빨리 가도 성에 차지 않는다는 듯이 있을 수 있는 가장 큰 서두름으로 달렸고, 그러면 마부들도 왜 그러는지는 모르면서 몹시 빠르게 마차를 몰았다. 움직임의 빠른 속도라는 것도 옮는 병 가운데 하나이기 때문이다."], ["And then again the sportsmen would move at an undertaker’s pace, when the fox had traversed and the hounds would be at a loss to know which was the hunt and which was the heel; and then the carriage also would go slowly, and the ladies would stand up and talk.", "그러다가 또 여우가 자취를 되짚어 사냥개들이 어느 쪽이 나아간 자취이고 어느 쪽이 되짚어 온 자취인지 알지 못해 헤맬 때에는, 사냥꾼들이 장의사의 걸음으로 움직였다. 그러면 마차도 천천히 갔고, 부인들은 일어서서 이야기를 했다."], ["And then the time for lunch came; and altogether the day went by pleasantly enough.", "그러고 나서 점심때가 왔다. 그리고 다 해서 그날은 흡족하게 지나갔다."]], [["“And so that’s hunting, is it?”", "“그래서 저것이 사냥이라는 거군요?”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, that’s hunting,”", "“그렇습니다, 저것이 사냥이지요,”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I did not see any gentleman do anything that I could not do myself, except there was one young man slipped off into the mud; and I shouldn’t like that.”", "“내가 직접 못 할 일을 하는 신사는 하나도 못 봤어요. 진흙탕으로 미끄러져 떨어진 젊은 사람 하나만 빼면요. 그건 나도 하고 싶지 않네요.”"]], [["“But there was no breaking of bones, was there, my dear?”", "“그래도 뼈가 부러진 사람은 없었잖아요, 그렇지요?”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“And nobody caught any foxes,”", "“그리고 여우를 잡은 사람도 없어요,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“The fact is, Mrs. Smith, that I don’t think much more of their sport than I do of their business.", "“실은 말이지요, 스미스 부인, 나는 저 사람들의 놀이도 저 사람들의 일만큼밖에는 대단하게 여기지 않아요."], ["I shall take to hunting a pack of hounds myself after this.”", "이걸 보고 나니 나도 사냥개를 한 무리 데리고 사냥을 해 볼까 싶네요.”"]], [["“Do, my dear, and I’ll be your whipper-in.", "“그러세요, 그러면 내가 부몰이꾼이 되어 드리지요."], ["I wonder whether Mrs. Proudie would join us.”", "프라우디 부인도 우리와 함께해 주실지 모르겠네요.”"]], [["“I shall be writing to the duke to-night,”", "“오늘 밤에 공작께 편지를 쓸 겁니다,”"], ["said Mr. Fothergill to Mark, as they were all riding up to the stable-yard together.", "그들이 다 함께 마구간 마당으로 올라가고 있을 때 포더길 씨가 마크에게 말했다."], ["“You will let me tell his grace that you will accept his invitation—will you not?”", "“초대를 받아들이시겠다고 각하께 전해도 되겠지요 — 그렇지 않습니까?”"]], [["“Upon my word, the duke is very kind,”", "“정말이지 공작께서 아주 친절하시군요,”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“He is very anxious to know you, I can assure you,”", "“각하께서 당신을 몹시 알고 싶어 하십니다, 내가 보증하지요,”"], ["said Fothergill.", "포더길이 말했다."]], [["What could a young flattered fool of a parson do, but say that he would go?", "추어올림을 받아 얼떨떨해진 젊고 어리석은 목사가 가겠다고 말하는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Mark did say that he would go; and in the course of the evening his friend Mr.", "마크는 가겠다고 말했다."], ["Sowerby congratulated him, and the bishop joked with him and said that he knew that he would not give up good company so soon; and Miss Dunstable said she would make him her chaplain as soon as Parliament would allow quack doctors to have such articles—an allusion which Mark did not understand, till he learned that Miss Dunstable was herself the proprietress of the celebrated Oil of Lebanon, invented by her late respected father, and patented by him with such wonderful results in the way of accumulated fortune; and Mrs.", "그리고 그날 저녁이 흘러가는 동안 그의 벗 사워비 씨는 그에게 축하를 했고, 주교는 그를 놀리며 그가 좋은 벗들을 그렇게 쉽게 포기하지 않을 줄 알고 있었다고 말했고, 던스터블 양은 국회가 돌팔이 의사에게도 그런 물건을 두는 것을 허락하는 대로 그를 자기 전속 사제로 삼겠다고 말했다 — 마크는 이 빗댐을 알아듣지 못했고, 던스터블 양 자신이 그 이름난 레바논 연고의 소유주라는 것을 알게 된 뒤에야 알아들었다. 그것은 그녀의 작고한 존경받는 아버지가 발명하여 특허를 낸 것이었고, 그리하여 재산이 쌓이는 방면으로 놀라운 결과를 낳은 것이었다."], ["Proudie made him quite one of their party, talking to him about all manner of church subjects; and then at last, even Miss Proudie smiled on him, when she learned that he had been thought worthy of a bed at a duke’s castle.", "그리고 프라우디 부인은 온갖 교회 문제를 그에게 이야기하며 그를 아주 그들 편의 한 사람으로 만들었다. 그리고 마침내 프라우디 양까지도, 그가 공작의 성에서 침대 하나를 받을 만하다고 여겨졌음을 알게 되자 그에게 미소를 보냈다."], ["And all the world seemed to be open to him.", "그리고 온 세상이 그에게 열려 있는 것 같았다."]], [["But he could not make himself happy that evening.", "그러나 그날 저녁 그는 스스로 행복해질 수 없었다."], ["On the next morning he must write to his wife; and he could already see the look of painful sorrow which would fall upon his Fanny’s brow when she learned that her husband was going to be a guest at the Duke of Omnium’s.", "이튿날 아침에는 아내에게 편지를 써야 했다. 그리고 자기 남편이 옴니엄 공작의 손님이 되려 한다는 것을 알게 될 때 그의 패니의 이마에 내려앉을 그 괴로운 슬픔의 표정을 그는 벌써 볼 수 있었다."], ["And he must tell her to send him money, and money was scarce.", "그리고 돈을 보내 달라고 그녀에게 말해야 했고, 돈은 넉넉하지 않았다."], ["And then, as to Lady Lufton, should he send her some message, or should he not?", "그리고 또 레이디 러프턴에게는 무슨 말을 전해야 하는가, 아니면 전하지 말아야 하는가?"], ["In either case he must declare war against her.", "어느 쪽이든 그는 그녀에게 선전을 포고해야 했다."], ["And then did he not owe everything to Lady Lufton?", "그리고 또 자기가 모든 것을 레이디 러프턴에게 힘입지 않았는가?"], ["And thus in spite of all his triumphs he could not get himself to bed in a happy frame of mind.", "그리하여 온갖 승리에도 불구하고 그는 행복한 마음으로 잠자리에 들 수 없었다."]], [["On the next day, which was Friday, he postponed the disagreeable task of writing.", "이튿날, 그러니까 금요일에 그는 그 언짢은 일을 뒤로 미루었다."], ["Saturday would do as well; and on Saturday morning, before they all started for Barchester, he did write.", "토요일도 마찬가지로 괜찮을 것이었다. 그리고 토요일 아침, 다들 바체스터로 떠나기 전에 그는 정말로 썼다."], ["And his letter ran as follows:—", "그리고 그의 편지는 이러했다 : —"]], [["Chaldicotes, — November, 185—.", "콜디코츠, 185─년 11월 ─일."]], [["DEAREST LOVE,—You will be astonished when I tell you how gay we all are here, and what further dissipations are in store for us.", "가장 사랑하는 이에게, — 우리가 여기서 얼마나 흥청거리고 있는지, 또 어떤 방탕이 앞으로 우리를 기다리고 있는지 말하면 당신은 놀랄 거야."], ["The Arabins, as you supposed, are not of our party; but the Proudies are,—as you supposed also.", "애러빈 부부는 당신이 짐작한 대로 우리 일행이 아니야. 그러나 프라우디 부부는 우리 일행이지 — 이것도 당신이 짐작한 대로야."], ["Your suppositions are always right.", "당신의 짐작은 언제나 옳아."], ["And what will you think when I tell you that I am to sleep at the palace on Saturday?", "그리고 내가 토요일에 주교관에서 잔다고 말하면 당신은 어떻게 생각할까?"], ["You know that there is to be a lecture in Barchester on that day.", "그날 바체스터에서 강연이 있다는 건 당신도 알지. 그런데, 우리는 물론 다 가야 해."], ["Well; we must all go, of course, as Harold Smith, one of our set here, is to give it.", "여기 우리 패의 한 사람인 해럴드 스미스가 그 강연을 하기로 되어 있으니까."], ["And now it turns out that we cannot get back the same night because there is no moon; and Mrs.", "그리고 이제 보니 달이 없어서 우리는 그날 밤에 돌아올 수 없다는 거야."], ["Bishop would not allow that my cloth should be contaminated by an hotel;—very kind and considerate, is it not?", "그리고 주교 부인께서는 내 성직자의 옷이 호텔로 더럽혀지는 것을 허락하지 않으시겠다는 거지 — 아주 친절하고 사려 깊지 않아?"]], [["But I have a more astounding piece of news for you than this.", "그러나 이보다 더 놀라운 소식이 당신에게 있어."], ["There is to be a great party at Gatherum Castle next week, and they have talked me over into accepting an invitation which the duke sent expressly to me.", "다음 주에 개서럼 성에서 큰 모임이 있는데, 공작이 나에게 특별히 보낸 초대를 받아들이도록 다들 나를 설득해 버렸어. 처음에는 거절했지."], ["I refused at first; but everybody here said that my doing so would be so strange; and then they all wanted to know my reason.", "그런데 여기 있는 사람들이 다 내가 그러는 것은 아주 이상한 일이라고 했고, 그러고는 다들 내 이유를 알고 싶어 했어."], ["When I came to render it, I did not know what reason I had to give.", "그 이유를 대려고 하니 내가 댈 이유가 무엇인지 알 수 없었어."], ["The bishop is going, and he thought it very odd that I should not go also, seeing that I was asked.", "주교가 가는데, 내가 청함을 받았는데도 가지 않는 것을 그분은 아주 괴상하게 여겼어."]], [["I know what my own darling will think, and I know that she will not be pleased, and I must put off my defence till I return to her from this ogre-land,—if ever I do get back alive.", "내 사랑하는 사람이 무엇을 생각할지 나는 알아. 그리고 그이가 기뻐하지 않을 것도 알아."], ["But joking apart, Fanny, I think that I should have been wrong to stand out, when so much was said about it.", "그래서 내 변명은 이 도깨비 나라에서 그이에게 돌아갈 때까지 미뤄 두어야겠어 — 살아서 돌아가기라도 한다면 말이지."], ["I should have been seeming to take upon myself to sit in judgment upon the duke.", "하지만 농담은 접어 두고, 패니, 그렇게 말이 많이 나온 판에 내가 끝까지 버티는 것은 잘못된 일이었으리라고 나는 생각해. 내가 공작을 심판하는 자리에 앉겠다고 나서는 꼴로 보였을 거야."], ["I doubt if there be a single clergyman in the diocese, under fifty years of age, who would have refused the invitation under such circumstances,—unless it be Crawley, who is so mad on the subject that he thinks it almost wrong to take a walk out of his own parish.", "이 교구에서 쉰 살이 안 된 성직자 가운데 그런 사정에서 그 초대를 거절했을 사람이 한 사람이라도 있을지 의심스러워 — 크롤리라면 몰라도. 그 사람은 이 문제에 하도 미쳐 있어서 자기 교구 밖으로 산책을 나가는 것조차 거의 잘못이라고 여기니까."]], [["I must stay at Gatherum Castle over Sunday week—indeed, we only go there on Friday.", "나는 다음 주 일요일이 지날 때까지 개서럼 성에 머물러야 해 — 실은 우리가 그리로 가는 것이 금요일이니까."], ["I have written to Jones about the duties.", "직무에 대해서는 존스에게 편지를 써 두었어."], ["I can make it up to him, as I know he wishes to go into Wales at Christmas.", "그에게 갚아 줄 수 있어. 그가 성탄절에 웨일스로 가고 싶어 하는 것을 내가 아니까."], ["My wanderings will all be over then, and he may go for a couple of months if he pleases.", "그때면 내 떠돌이 노릇도 다 끝날 테고, 그가 원하면 두어 달 다녀와도 좋아."], ["I suppose you will take my classes in the school on Sunday, as well as your own; but pray make them have a good fire.", "일요일에 학교에서 당신 반과 함께 내 반도 맡아 주겠지. 그런데 아이들에게 불은 잘 지펴 주도록 해."], ["If this is too much for you, make Mrs. Podgens take the boys.", "이것이 당신에게 너무 무거우면 포진스 부인에게 사내아이들을 맡겨."], ["Indeed I think that will be better.", "실은 그게 더 나을 것 같아."]], [["Of course you will tell her ladyship of my whereabouts.", "물론 당신은 마님께 내가 어디 있는지 알려 드릴 테지."], ["Tell her from me, that as regards the bishop, as well as regarding another great personage, the colour has been laid on perhaps a little too thickly.", "내 말로 이렇게 전해 줘. 주교에 관해서도, 또 다른 한 분 지체 높은 인물에 관해서도, 색을 아마 좀 너무 두껍게 칠해 놓은 것 같다고."], ["Not that Lady Lufton would ever like him.", "레이디 러프턴이 그 사람을 좋아하게 되지야 않겠지만."], ["Make her understand that my going to the duke’s has almost become a matter of conscience with me.", "공작 집에 가는 일이 나에게는 거의 양심에 걸리는 일이 되어 버렸다는 것을 그분이 알아주시게 해 줘."], ["I have not known how to make it appear that it would be right for me to refuse, without absolutely making a party matter of it.", "이 일을 아주 당파의 문제로 만들어 버리지 않고서는, 내가 거절하는 것이 옳다고 보이게 할 방도를 나는 알 수 없었어."], ["I saw that it would be said, that I, coming from Lady Lufton’s parish, could not go to the Duke of Omnium’s.", "내가 레이디 러프턴의 교구에서 온 사람이니 옴니엄 공작의 집에는 갈 수 없다는 말이 나올 것이 눈에 보였어."], ["This I did not choose.", "이것은 내가 택하지 않았어."]], [["I find that I shall want a little more money before I leave here, five or ten pounds—say ten pounds.", "여기를 떠나기 전에 돈이 좀 더 필요할 것 같아. 5파운드나 10파운드 — 10파운드라고 하자."], ["If you cannot spare it, get it from Davis.", "당신이 내놓을 수 없으면 데이비스에게서 받아."], ["He owes me more than that, a good deal.", "그가 나에게 갚을 것이 그보다 훨씬 많아."]], [["And now, God bless and preserve you, my own love.", "그러면 이제, 하느님께서 당신을 축복하시고 지켜 주시기를, 내 사랑."], ["Kiss my darling bairns for papa, and give them my blessing.", "아빠를 대신해 내 귀여운 아이들에게 입 맞춰 주고, 내 축복을 전해 줘."]], [["Always and ever your own,", "언제나 영원히 당신의 사람,"]], [["And then there was written, on an outside scrap which was folded round the full-written sheet of paper, “Make it as smooth at Framley Court as possible.”", "그리고 그다음에, 빽빽하게 쓴 종이를 감싸는 바깥쪽 쪽지에 이렇게 적혀 있었다. “프램리 코트에서 될 수 있는 대로 매끄럽게 해 줘.”"]], [["However strong, and reasonable, and unanswerable the body of Mark’s letter may have been, all his hesitation, weakness, doubt, and fear, were expressed in this short postscript.", "마크의 편지 본문이 아무리 강하고 조리 있고 반박할 수 없는 것이었다 해도, 그의 온갖 망설임과 약함과 의심과 두려움은 이 짧은 추신에 다 드러나 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER V. AMANTIUM IRÆ AMORIS INTEGRATIO.", "# 제5장. 「아만티움 이래 아모리스 인테그라티오」"]], [["And now, with my reader’s consent, I will follow the postman with that letter to Framley; not by its own circuitous route indeed, or by the same mode of conveyance; for that letter went into Barchester by the Courcy night mail-cart, which, on its road, passes through the villages of Uffley and Chaldicotes, reaching Barchester in time for the up mail-train to London.", "이제 독자의 양해를 얻어, 나는 그 편지를 든 우체부를 따라 프램리로 가 보려 한다. 물론 편지가 지나온 그 구불구불한 길을 그대로 따라가거나 같은 운송 수단을 타고 가겠다는 말은 아니다."], ["By that train, the letter was sent towards the metropolis as far as the junction of the Barset branch line, but there it was turned in its course, and came down again by the main line as far as Silverbridge; at which place, between six and seven in the morning, it was shouldered by the Framley footpost messenger, and in due course delivered at the Framley Parsonage exactly as Mrs.", "그 편지는 코시 야간 우편 마차에 실려 바체스터로 들어갔는데, 이 마차는 도중에 어플리와 콜디코츠 두 마을을 지나 런던행 상행 우편 열차 시간에 맞추어 바체스터에 닿는다. 편지는 그 열차에 실려 수도를 향해 바셋 지선의 분기점까지 갔다가, 거기서 방향을 돌려 다시 본선을 타고 실버브리지까지 내려왔다."], ["Robarts had finished reading prayers to the four servants. Or, I should say rather, that such would in its usual course have been that letter’s destiny.", "그리고 그곳에서 아침 여섯 시와 일곱 시 사이에 프램리 도보 우편 배달부의 어깨에 얹혔고, 로바츠 부인이 하인 넷에게 기도문을 읽어 주기를 막 끝냈을 무렵 어김없이 프램리 목사관에 배달되었다."], ["As it was, however, it reached Silverbridge on Sunday, and lay there till the Monday, as the Framley people have declined their Sunday post.", "아니, 이렇게 말하는 편이 옳겠다. 일이 여느 때처럼 흘러갔다면 그 편지의 운명은 그러했으리라는 것이다."], ["And then again, when the letter was delivered at the parsonage, on that wet Monday morning, Mrs.", "그러나 실제로는 편지가 일요일에 실버브리지에 닿았고, 프램리 사람들이 일요일 우편을 사양했던 터라 월요일까지 거기 놓여 있었다. 게다가 비 내리는 그 월요일 아침에 편지가 목사관에 배달되었을 때, 로바츠 부인은 집에 없었다."], ["Robarts was not at home. As we are all aware, she was staying with her ladyship at Framley Court.", "우리 모두가 아는 대로 그녀는 프램리 코트에서 마님과 함께 머물고 있었다."]], [["“Oh, but it’s mortial wet,”", "“허, 지독히도 젖었수,”"], ["said the shivering postman as he handed in that and the vicar’s newspaper.", "그 편지와 교구 사제의 신문을 건네주며 몸을 떨던 우체부가 말했다."], ["The vicar was a man of the world, and took the Jupiter.", "교구 사제는 세상 물정을 아는 사람이라 《주피터》를 받아 보았다."]], [["“Come in, Robin postman, and warm theeself awhile,”", "“들어오시우, 로빈 우체부. 잠깐 몸이나 녹이시구려,”"], ["said Jemima the cook, pushing a stool a little to one side, but still well in front of the big kitchen fire.", "요리사 제마이마가 등받이 없는 의자를 한쪽으로 조금 밀어 주면서 말했다. 그래도 의자는 여전히 큰 부엌 불 바로 앞에 있었다."]], [["“Well, I dudna jist know how it’ll be.", "“글쎄, 어찌 될지 나도 잘 모르겠수."], ["The wery ’edges ’as eyes and tells on me in Silverbridge, if I so much as stops to pick a blackberry.”", "실버브리지에선 울타리란 놈들까지 눈이 달려서, 내가 블랙베리 하나 따려고 멈추기만 해도 죄다 고해바친다니까.”"]], [["“There bain’t no hedges here, mon, nor yet no blackberries; so sit thee down and warm theeself.", "“여기는 울타리도 없고 블랙베리도 없수, 이 사람아. 그러니 앉아서 몸이나 녹이시우."], ["That’s better nor blackberries I’m thinking,”", "그게 블랙베리보다야 낫지 싶은데,”"], ["and she handed him a bowl of tea with a slice of buttered toast.", "그러면서 그녀는 버터 바른 토스트 한 조각과 함께 차 한 그릇을 그에게 건넸다."]], [["Robin postman took the proffered tea, put his dripping hat on the ground, and thanked Jemima cook.", "로빈 우체부는 내미는 차를 받아 들고, 물이 뚝뚝 떨어지는 모자를 바닥에 내려놓고, 요리사 제마이마에게 고맙다고 했다."], ["“But I dudna jist know how it’ll be,”", "“그래도 어찌 될지는 나도 잘 모르겠수,”"], ["said he; “only it do pour so tarnation heavy.”", "그가 말했다. “다만 비가 억수로 쏟아지니 말이우.”"], ["Which among us, O my readers, could have withstood that temptation?", "아, 독자들이여, 우리 가운데 누가 그런 유혹을 물리칠 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["Such was the circuitous course of Mark’s letter; but as it left Chaldicotes on Saturday evening, and reached Mrs.", "마크의 편지가 지나온 길은 그처럼 구불구불했다."], ["Robarts on the following morning, or would have done, but for that intervening Sunday, doing all its peregrinations during the night, it may be held that its course of transport was not inconveniently arranged.", "그러나 편지는 토요일 저녁에 콜디코츠를 떠나 이튿날 아침에 로바츠 부인에게 닿았고—중간에 그 일요일이 끼어들지 않았다면 닿았을 것이며—그 모든 여정을 밤 사이에 마쳤으니, 운송 경로가 그리 불편하게 짜여 있었다고는 할 수 없겠다."], ["We, however, will travel by a much shorter route.", "그러나 우리는 훨씬 짧은 길로 가겠다."]], [["Robin, in the course of his daily travels, passed, first the post-office at Framley, then the Framley Court back entrance, and then the vicar’s house, so that on this wet morning Jemima cook was not able to make use of his services in transporting this letter back to her mistress; for Robin had got another village before him, expectant of its letters.", "로빈은 날마다 도는 길에서 먼저 프램리 우체국을 지나고, 다음으로 프램리 코트의 뒷문을 지나고, 그다음에 교구 사제의 집을 지났다. 그래서 이 비 오는 아침에 요리사 제마이마는 이 편지를 안주인에게 되돌려 보내는 일에 그의 발을 빌릴 수가 없었다. 로빈에게는 편지를 기다리는 또 다른 마을이 앞에 남아 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Why didn’t thee leave it, mon, with Mr. Applejohn at the Court?”", "“왜 그 편지를 코트의 애플존 씨한테 맡기지 않았수, 이 사람아?”"], ["Mr. Applejohn was the butler who took the letter-bag.", "애플존 씨는 우편 행낭을 받는 집사였다."], ["“Thee know’st as how missus was there.”", "“안주인이 거기 계신 걸 알고 있었잖수.”"]], [["And then Robin, mindful of the tea and toast, explained to her courteously how the law made it imperative on him to bring the letter to the very house that was indicated, let the owner of the letter be where she might; and he laid down the law very satisfactorily with sundry long-worded quotations.", "그러자 로빈은 차와 토스트를 잊지 않고, 편지 주인이 어디에 있든 편지에 적힌 바로 그 집까지 편지를 가져가는 것이 법이 자기에게 지운 의무라고 그녀에게 정중히 설명했다. 그러고는 여러 가지 긴 낱말을 인용해 가며 법을 아주 만족스럽게 늘어놓았다."], ["Not to much effect, however, for the housemaid called him an oaf; and Robin would decidedly have had the worst of it had not the gardener come in and taken his part.", "그러나 큰 효험은 없었다. 하녀가 그를 얼간이라 불렀으니 말이다."], ["“They women knows nothin’, and understands nothin’,”", "정원사가 들어와 그의 편을 들어 주지 않았다면 로빈은 영락없이 지고 말았을 것이다. “저 여편네들은 아무것도 모르고 아무것도 못 알아듣는다니까,”"], ["said the gardener.", "정원사가 말했다."], ["“Give us hold of the letter.", "“편지 이리 주우."], ["I’ll take it up to the house.", "내가 저택으로 가져가겠수."], ["It’s the master’s fist.”", "주인 나리 글씨로군.”"], ["And then Robin postman went on one way, and the gardener, he went the other.", "그러고 로빈 우체부는 한쪽으로 갔고, 정원사는 다른 쪽으로 갔다."], ["The gardener never disliked an excuse for going up to the Court gardens, even on so wet a day as this.", "정원사는 이렇게 비 오는 날에도 코트의 정원으로 올라갈 구실이라면 싫어한 적이 없었다."]], [["Mrs. Robarts was sitting over the drawing-room fire with Lady Meredith, when her husband’s letter was brought to her.", "남편의 편지가 로바츠 부인에게 전해졌을 때, 그녀는 응접실 난롯가에 레이디 메러디스와 함께 앉아 있었다."], ["The Framley Court letter-bag had been discussed at breakfast; but that was now nearly an hour since, and Lady Lufton, as was her wont, was away in her own room writing her own letters, and looking after her own matters: for Lady Lufton was a person who dealt in figures herself, and understood business almost as well as Harold Smith.", "프램리 코트의 우편 행낭은 아침 식사 자리에서 다 뒤진 터였지만 그것은 이미 한 시간쯤 전 일이었고, 레이디 러프턴은 늘 하는 대로 자기 방에 들어가 자기 편지를 쓰고 자기 일을 살피고 있었다. 레이디 러프턴은 숫자를 스스로 다루는 사람이었고, 해럴드 스미스에 못지않게 사업을 잘 알았기 때문이다."], ["And on that morning she also had received a letter which had displeased her not a little. Whence arose this displeasure neither Mrs.", "그리고 그날 아침 그녀 역시 적잖이 마음을 상하게 하는 편지 한 통을 받았다."], ["Robarts nor Lady Meredith knew; but her ladyship’s brow had grown black at breakfast time; she had bundled up an ominous-looking epistle into her bag without speaking of it, and had left the room immediately that breakfast was over.", "그 불쾌함이 어디서 비롯되었는지는 로바츠 부인도 레이디 메러디스도 알지 못했다. 다만 마님의 이마는 아침 식사 때부터 어두워졌고, 그녀는 불길해 보이는 서한을 아무 말 없이 손가방에 쑤셔 넣고는 식사가 끝나자마자 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["“There’s something wrong,”", "“무슨 일이 있으신가 보군,”"], ["said Sir George.", "조지 경이 말했다."]], [["“Mamma does fret herself so much about Ludovic’s money matters,”", "“어머니는 루도빅의 돈 문제로 어찌나 마음을 태우시는지 몰라요,”"], ["said Lady Meredith.", "레이디 메러디스가 말했다."], ["Ludovic was Lord Lufton,—Ludovic Lufton, Baron Lufton of Lufton, in the county of Oxfordshire.", "루도빅은 러프턴 경이었다. 옥스퍼드셔주 러프턴의 러프턴 남작, 루도빅 러프턴."]], [["“And yet I don’t think Lufton gets much astray,”", "“그래도 러프턴이 그리 크게 어긋나는 것 같지는 않은데,”"], ["said Sir George, as he sauntered out of the room.", "방에서 어슬렁어슬렁 나가면서 조지 경이 말했다."], ["“Well, Justy; we’ll put off going then till to-morrow; but remember, it must be the first train.”", "“그럼 저스티, 떠나는 건 내일로 미루기로 하지. 하지만 첫차라야 하는 걸 잊지 말아.”"], ["Lady Meredith said she would remember, and then they went into the drawing-room, and there Mrs. Robarts received her letter.", "레이디 메러디스는 잊지 않겠다고 했고, 그러고 나서 두 여인은 응접실로 들어갔으며, 거기서 로바츠 부인이 편지를 받았다."]], [["Fanny, when she read it, hardly at first realized to herself the idea that her husband, the clergyman of Framley, the family clerical friend of Lady Lufton’s establishment, was going to stay with the Duke of Omnium.", "패니는 편지를 읽고도 처음에는 자기 남편이, 프램리의 성직자이자 레이디 러프턴 집안의 가까운 성직자 친구인 그이가 옴니엄 공작의 집에 머물려 한다는 사실을 좀처럼 실감하지 못했다. 프램리 코트에서는 공작과 그에게 딸린 모든 것이 해롭고 저주스럽다는 것이 철저하게 합의된 바였다."], ["It was so thoroughly understood at Framley Court that the duke and all belonging to him was noxious and damnable.", "그는 휘그였고, 독신이었고, 도박꾼이었고, 무슨 일에서나 부도덕했고, 교회의 원칙이라고는 없는 사람이었고, 젊은이를 타락시키는 자였고, 젊은 아내들의 맹세한 원수였고, 자잘한 사람들의 세습 재산을 삼켜 버리는 자였다."], ["He was a Whig, he was a bachelor, he was a gambler, he was immoral in every way, he was a man of no church principle, a corrupter of youth, a sworn foe of young wives, a swallower up of small men’s patrimonies; a man whom mothers feared for their sons, and sisters for their brothers; and worse again, whom fathers had cause to fear for their daughters, and brothers for their sisters;—a man who, with his belongings, dwelt, and must dwell, poles asunder from Lady Lufton and her belongings!", "어머니들이 아들을 위해 두려워하고 누이들이 오라비를 위해 두려워하는 사람, 그리고 더 나쁘게는 아버지들이 딸을 위해, 오라비들이 누이를 위해 두려워할 까닭이 있는 사람이었다. 그에게 딸린 것들과 더불어 레이디 러프턴과 그녀에게 딸린 것들로부터 양극처럼 멀리 떨어져 살았고, 또 그래야만 하는 사람이었다!"]], [["And it must be remembered that all these evil things were fully believed by Mrs. Robarts.", "그리고 이 모든 나쁜 소문을 로바츠 부인이 고스란히 믿고 있었다는 것을 잊지 말아야 한다."], ["Could it really be that her husband was going to dwell in the halls of Apollyon, to shelter himself beneath the wings of this very Lucifer?", "정말로 자기 남편이 아폴리온의 궁에 들어가 살고, 바로 그 루시퍼의 날개 밑에 몸을 숨기려 한다는 말인가? 슬픔의 구름이 그녀의 얼굴에 내려앉았다."], ["A cloud of sorrow settled upon her face, and then she read the letter again very slowly, not omitting the tell-tale postscript.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 그 편지를 아주 천천히 다시 읽었는데, 속내를 다 드러내는 추신도 빼놓지 않았다."]], [["“Oh, Justinia!”", "“오, 저스티니아!”"], ["at last she said.", "마침내 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“What, have you got bad news, too?”", "“왜요, 패니도 나쁜 소식을 받았어요?”"]], [["“I hardly know how to tell you what has occurred.", "“무슨 일이 생겼는지 어떻게 말해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["There; I suppose you had better read it;”", "자, 그냥 읽어 보는 게 나을 것 같아요.”"], ["and she handed her husband’s epistle to Lady Meredith,—keeping back, however, the postscript.", "그러고 그녀는 남편의 서한을 레이디 메러디스에게 건네주었다. 다만 추신은 접어서 감추었다."]], [["“What on earth will her ladyship say now?”", "“마님이 이번엔 대체 무슨 말씀을 하실까요?”"], ["said Lady Meredith, as she folded the paper, and replaced it in the envelope.", "종이를 접어 봉투에 다시 넣으면서 레이디 메러디스가 말했다."]], [["“What had I better do, Justinia?", "“어쩌면 좋아요, 저스티니아?"], ["how had I better tell her?”", "어떻게 말씀드리는 게 좋을까요?”"], ["And then the two ladies put their heads together, bethinking themselves how they might best deprecate the wrath of Lady Lufton.", "그러고 나서 두 여인은 머리를 맞대고, 레이디 러프턴의 노여움을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 달랠 수 있을지 궁리했다."], ["It had been arranged that Mrs. Robarts should go back to the parsonage after lunch, and she had persisted in her intention after it had been settled that the Merediths were to stay over that evening.", "로바츠 부인은 점심을 먹고 목사관으로 돌아가기로 되어 있었고, 메러디스 부부가 그날 저녁까지 머물기로 정해진 뒤에도 그 뜻을 굽히지 않았다."], ["Lady Meredith now advised her friend to carry out this determination without saying anything about her husband’s terrible iniquities, and then to send the letter up to Lady Lufton as soon as she reached the parsonage.", "이제 레이디 메러디스는 남편의 끔찍한 죄악에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않은 채 그 결심대로 떠나고, 목사관에 닿는 대로 편지를 레이디 러프턴에게 올려 보내라고 친구에게 권했다."], ["“Mamma will never know that you received it here,”", "“패니가 그 편지를 여기서 받았다는 건 어머니가 결코 모르실 거예요,”"], ["said Lady Meredith.", "레이디 메러디스가 말했다."]], [["But Mrs. Robarts would not consent to this.", "그러나 로바츠 부인은 그렇게 하는 데 동의하지 않았다."], ["Such a course seemed to her to be cowardly.", "그런 처사는 그녀에게 비겁해 보였다."], ["She knew that her husband was doing wrong; she felt that he knew it himself; but still it was necessary that she should defend him.", "남편이 잘못하고 있다는 것을 그녀는 알았고, 그이 자신도 그것을 안다고 느꼈다. 그럼에도 그녀가 그를 변호해야 한다는 것 또한 사실이었다."], ["However terrible might be the storm, it must break upon her own head.", "폭풍이 아무리 무섭게 몰아친다 해도 그것은 자기 머리 위에서 터져야 했다."], ["So she at once went up and tapped at Lady Lufton’s private door; and as she did so Lady Meredith followed her.", "그래서 그녀는 곧 위층으로 올라가 레이디 러프턴의 사실(私室) 문을 두드렸고, 그렇게 하는 그녀를 레이디 메러디스가 뒤따랐다."]], [["“Come in,”", "“들어오세요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton, and the voice did not sound soft and pleasant.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했는데, 그 목소리는 부드럽지도 상냥하지도 않았다."], ["When they entered, they found her sitting at her little writing table, with her head resting on her arm, and that letter which she had received that morning was lying open on the table before her.", "두 사람이 들어가 보니 그녀는 작은 필기 탁자 앞에 앉아 팔에 머리를 기대고 있었고, 그날 아침에 받은 그 편지가 탁자 위에 펼쳐진 채 놓여 있었다. 실은 지금 거기에 편지가 두 통 있었다."], ["Indeed there were two letters now there, one from a London lawyer to herself, and the other from her son to that London lawyer.", "하나는 런던의 어느 변호사가 그녀에게 보낸 것이고, 다른 하나는 그녀의 아들이 그 런던 변호사에게 보낸 것이었다."], ["It needs only be explained that the subject of those letters was the immediate sale of that outlying portion of the Lufton property in Oxfordshire, as to which Mr. Sowerby once spoke.", "그 편지들의 주제가 언젠가 사워비 씨가 말했던, 옥스퍼드셔에 있는 러프턴 가문 재산의 외딴 일부를 곧 팔아 치우는 일이었다는 것만 밝혀 두면 되겠다."], ["Lord Lufton had told the lawyer that the thing must be done at once, adding that his friend Robarts would have explained the whole affair to his mother.", "러프턴 경은 변호사에게 그 일을 당장 처리해야 한다고 말했고, 친구 로바츠가 그 일의 전말을 어머니에게 설명해 두었을 것이라고 덧붙였다."], ["And then the lawyer had written to Lady Lufton, as indeed was necessary; but unfortunately Lady Lufton had not hitherto heard a word of the matter.", "그리고 변호사는, 실로 그래야 마땅한 대로 레이디 러프턴에게 편지를 썼다. 그러나 불행히도 레이디 러프턴은 그때까지 그 일에 대해 한마디도 듣지 못했던 것이다."]], [["In her eyes the sale of family property was horrible; the fact that a young man with some fifteen or twenty thousand a year should require subsidiary money was horrible; that her own son should have not written to her himself was horrible; and it was also horrible that her own pet, the clergyman whom she had brought there to be her son’s friend, should be mixed up in the matter,—should be cognizant of it while she was not cognizant,—should be employed in it as a go-between and agent in her son’s bad courses.", "그녀의 눈에는 집안 재산을 파는 일이 끔찍했다. 연 수입이 1만 5천이나 2만 파운드나 되는 젊은이가 곁돈을 필요로 한다는 사실이 끔찍했다."], ["It was all horrible, and Lady Lufton was sitting there with a black brow and an uneasy heart.", "자기 아들이 자기에게 직접 편지를 쓰지 않았다는 것이 끔찍했다. 그리고 자기가 아끼는 사람, 아들의 친구가 되라고 자기가 이곳으로 데려온 그 성직자가 이 일에 얽혀 있다는 것—자기는 모르는 일을 그가 알고 있었다는 것, 아들의 나쁜 행로에서 중개인이자 대리인으로 부려졌다는 것—역시 끔찍했다."], ["As regarded our poor parson, we may say that in this matter he was blameless, except that he had hitherto lacked the courage to execute his friend’s commission.", "모든 것이 끔찍했고, 레이디 러프턴은 이마를 어둡게 하고 마음을 불편해하며 거기 앉아 있었다. 우리의 가난한 목사에 대해 말한다면, 이 일에서 그는 친구의 심부름을 실행할 용기를 그때까지 내지 못했다는 것 말고는 잘못이 없다고 해야 할 것이다."]], [["“What is it, Fanny?”", "“무슨 일이에요, 패니?”"], ["said Lady Lufton as soon as the door was opened; “I should have been down in half-an-hour, if you wanted me, Justinia.”", "문이 열리자마자 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “나를 찾았으면 반 시간 안에 내려갔을 텐데, 저스티니아.”"]], [["“Fanny has received a letter which makes her wish to speak to you at once,”", "“패니가 편지를 받았는데, 어머니께 당장 말씀드리고 싶어 해서요,”"], ["said Lady Meredith.", "레이디 메러디스가 말했다."], ["“What letter, Fanny?”", "“무슨 편지예요, 패니?”"]], [["Poor Fanny’s heart was in her mouth; she held it in her hand, but had not yet quite made up her mind whether she would show it bodily to Lady Lufton.", "가엾은 패니는 심장이 목까지 올라와 있었다. 편지를 손에 들고 있었지만, 그것을 레이디 러프턴에게 실물로 보여 줄지 아직 마음을 정하지 못했다."]], [["“From Mr. Robarts,”", "“로바츠 씨한테서 왔습니다,”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Well; I suppose he is going to stay another week at Chaldicotes.", "“그래요. 콜디코츠에 한 주 더 머물겠다는 거겠지요."], ["For my part I should be as well pleased;”", "나로서야 그러는 게 더 좋겠지만요.”"], ["and Lady Lufton’s voice was not friendly, for she was thinking of that farm in Oxfordshire.", "그러면서 레이디 러프턴의 목소리는 다정하지 않았다. 그녀는 옥스퍼드셔의 그 농장을 생각하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["The imprudence of the young is very sore to the prudence of their elders.", "젊은이들의 무분별은 나이 든 이들의 분별에 몹시 쓰라리다."], ["No woman could be less covetous, less grasping than Lady Lufton; but the sale of a portion of the old family property was to her as the loss of her own heart’s blood.", "레이디 러프턴만큼 욕심 없고 움키려 들지 않는 여인은 없었다. 그러나 오래된 집안 재산의 한 조각을 파는 일은 그녀에게 제 심장의 피를 잃는 것과 같았다."]], [["“Here is the letter, Lady Lufton; perhaps you had better read it;”", "“편지가 여기 있습니다, 레이디 러프턴. 읽어 보시는 게 좋겠습니다.”"], ["and Fanny handed it to her, again keeping back the postscript.", "그러고 패니는 이번에도 추신은 감춘 채 그것을 그녀에게 건넸다."], ["She had read and re-read the letter downstairs, but could not make out whether her husband had intended her to show it.", "그녀는 아래층에서 편지를 읽고 또 읽었지만, 남편이 그것을 보여 주라는 뜻이었는지는 알 수 없었다."], ["From the line of the argument she thought that he must have done so.", "글의 흐름으로 보아 그런 뜻이었으리라 생각했다."], ["At any rate he said for himself more than she could say for him, and so, probably, it was best that her ladyship should see it.", "어쨌든 그가 자기를 위해 한 말이 그녀가 그를 위해 할 수 있는 말보다 많았으니, 마님이 그것을 보는 편이 아마 가장 나을 것이었다."]], [["Lady Lufton took it, and read it, and her face grew blacker and blacker.", "레이디 러프턴은 그것을 받아 읽었고, 그녀의 얼굴은 갈수록 어두워졌다."], ["Her mind was set against the writer before she began it, and every word in it tended to make her feel more estranged from him.", "읽기 시작하기도 전에 그녀의 마음은 이미 그 글을 쓴 사람에게 등을 돌리고 있었고, 편지 속의 모든 낱말이 그를 더욱 멀게 느끼도록 만들었다."], ["“Oh, he is going to the palace, is he?—well; he must choose his own friends.", "“아, 주교관에 간다는 거군요. 그래요, 친구는 자기가 골라야지요."], ["Harold Smith one of his party!", "해럴드 스미스가 한패라니!"], ["It’s a pity, my dear, he did not see Miss Proudie before he met you, he might have lived to be the bishop’s chaplain.", "얘야, 그이가 패니를 만나기 전에 프라우디 양을 보지 못한 것이 안타깝군요. 그랬으면 주교의 전속 사제가 되고도 남았을 텐데."], ["Gatherum Castle!", "개서럼 성이라니!"], ["You don’t mean to tell me that he is going there?", "그이가 거기까지 간다는 말은 아니겠지요?"], ["Then I tell you fairly, Fanny, that I have done with him.”", "그렇다면 나도 솔직히 말하겠어요, 패니. 나는 그이와는 이제 끝이에요.”"]], [["“Oh, Lady Lufton, don’t say that,” said Mrs.", "“오, 레이디 러프턴, 그런 말씀 하지 마십시오,”"], ["Robarts, with tears in her eyes.", "눈에 눈물을 담고 로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Mamma, mamma, don’t speak in that way,”", "“어머니, 어머니, 그렇게 말씀하지 마세요,”"], ["said Lady Meredith.", "레이디 메러디스가 말했다."]], [["“But my dear, what am I to say?", "“하지만 얘야, 내가 무슨 말을 해야 하니?"], ["I must speak in that way.", "나는 그렇게 말해야 해."], ["You would not wish me to speak falsehoods, would you?", "네가 나더러 거짓을 말하기를 바라지는 않겠지?"], ["A man must choose for himself, but he can’t live with two different sets of people; at least, not if I belong to one and the Duke of Omnium to the other.", "사람은 스스로 골라야 하지만, 서로 다른 두 무리와 함께 살 수는 없어. 적어도 한쪽에 내가 있고 다른 쪽에 옴니엄 공작이 있다면 그럴 수 없지."], ["The bishop going indeed!", "주교까지 간다니 참!"], ["If there be anything that I hate it is hypocrisy.”", "내가 무엇을 싫어한다면 그건 위선이야.”"]], [["“There is no hypocrisy in that, Lady Lufton.”", "“그건 위선이 아닙니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“But I say there is, Fanny.", "“나는 위선이라고 말해요, 패니."], ["Very strange, indeed!", "정말이지 아주 괴상하군요!"], ["‘Put off his defence!’ Why should a man need any defence to his wife if he acts in a straightforward way?", "‘변명은 나중으로 미루겠소’라니! 사람이 곧게 행동한다면 아내에게 무슨 변명이 필요하겠어요?"], ["His own language condemns him: ‘Wrong to stand out!’ Now, will either of you tell me that Mr.", "그이 자신의 말이 그이를 벌하고 있어요. ‘버티는 것은 잘못이었을 것이오’라니!"], ["Robarts would really have thought it wrong to refuse that invitation?", "자, 두 사람 가운데 누구라도 로바츠 씨가 그 초대를 거절하는 것을 정말로 잘못이라 여겼으리라고 말해 주겠어요?"], ["I say that that is hypocrisy.", "나는 그것이 위선이라고 말하겠어요."], ["There is no other word for it.”", "달리 부를 말이 없어요.”"]], [["By this time the poor wife, who had been in tears, was wiping them away and preparing for action.", "그때쯤 눈물을 흘리던 가엾은 아내는 눈물을 닦고 싸울 준비를 하고 있었다."], ["Lady Lufton’s extreme severity gave her courage.", "레이디 러프턴의 지나친 혹독함이 그녀에게 용기를 주었다."], ["She knew that it behoved her to fight for her husband when he was thus attacked.", "남편이 이렇게 공격당할 때는 그를 위해 싸우는 것이 마땅하다는 것을 그녀는 알았다."], ["Had Lady Lufton been moderate in her remarks Mrs. Robarts would not have had a word to say.", "레이디 러프턴이 온건하게만 말했더라면 로바츠 부인은 한마디도 하지 못했을 것이다."]], [["“My husband may have been ill-judged,”", "“제 남편이 분별없이 판단했을지는 모릅니다,”"], ["she said, “but he is no hypocrite.”", "그녀가 말했다. “그러나 그이는 위선자가 아닙니다.”"]], [["“Very well, my dear, I dare say you know better than I; but to me it looks extremely like hypocrisy: eh, Justinia?”", "“좋아요, 패니. 내가 아는 것보다야 패니가 더 잘 알겠지요. 그러나 내 눈에는 몹시도 위선처럼 보여요. 그렇지 않니, 저스티니아?”"]], [["“Oh, mamma, do be moderate.”", "“어머니, 좀 누그러지세요.”"]], [["“Moderate!", "“누그러지라고!"], ["That’s all very well.", "그거야 말은 좋지."], ["How is one to moderate one’s feelings when one has been betrayed?”", "배신을 당한 사람이 어떻게 제 감정을 누그러뜨리겠니?”"]], [["“You do not mean that Mr. Robarts has betrayed you?”", "“로바츠 씨가 마님을 배신했다는 말씀은 아니시겠지요?”"], ["said the wife.", "아내가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no; of course not.”", "“아, 아니에요. 물론 아니지요.”"], ["And then she went on reading the letter: “‘Seem to have been standing in judgment upon the duke.’ Might he not use the same argument as to going into any house in the kingdom, however infamous?", "그러고 나서 그녀는 편지를 계속 읽어 갔다. “‘공작을 심판하는 자리에 앉는 것처럼 보였을 것이오.’ 이 나라의 어떤 집에 들어가든, 그 집이 아무리 악명 높든 같은 논리를 쓸 수 있지 않겠어요?"], ["We must all stand in judgment one upon another in that sense.", "그런 뜻이라면 우리는 모두 서로를 심판하는 자리에 서야 해요."], ["‘Crawley!’ Yes; if he were a little more like Mr.", "‘크롤리!’ 그래요."], ["Crawley it would be a good thing for me, and for the parish, and for you too, my dear.", "그이가 크롤리 씨를 조금이라도 닮았다면 나에게도, 이 교구에도, 그리고 패니에게도 좋은 일일 거예요."], ["God forgive me for bringing him here; that’s all.”", "그이를 여기 데려온 것을 하느님이 용서하시기를. 그뿐이에요.”"]], [["“Lady Lufton, I must say that you are very hard upon him—very hard.", "“레이디 러프턴, 마님이 그이에게 너무 모지시다고 말씀드리지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["I did not expect it from such a friend.”", "너무 모지십니다. 그런 친구분께서 그러실 줄은 몰랐습니다.”"]], [["“My dear, you ought to know me well enough to be sure that I shall speak my mind.", "“얘야, 내가 마음에 있는 말을 하는 사람이라는 건 알 만큼 알 텐데요."], ["‘Written to Jones’—yes; it is easy enough to write to poor Jones.", "‘존스에게 편지를 썼소’—그래요, 가엾은 존스에게 편지 쓰는 건 쉬운 일이지요."], ["He had better write to Jones, and bid him do the whole duty.", "존스에게 편지를 써서 의무를 다 맡기는 게 낫겠어요."], ["Then he can go and be the duke’s domestic chaplain.”", "그러고 나서 공작의 집안 전속 사제나 되면 되겠군요.”"]], [["“I believe my husband does as much of his own duty as any clergyman in the whole diocese,”", "“제 남편은 온 주교구의 어느 성직자에게도 뒤지지 않게 자기 의무를 다한다고 저는 믿습니다,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts, now again in tears.", "다시 눈물을 흘리며 로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“And you are to take his work in the school; you and Mrs. Podgens.", "“그리고 학교에서 그이가 할 일을 패니가 맡으라고요."], ["What with his curate and his wife and Mrs.", "패니와 포진스 부인이 말이지요."], ["Podgens, I don’t see why he should come back at all.”", "부목사와 아내와 포진스 부인이 있으니, 그이가 대체 왜 돌아와야 하는지 나로서는 알 수가 없군요.”"]], [["“Oh, mamma,”", "“어머니,”"], ["said Justinia, “pray, pray don’t be so harsh to her.”", "저스티니아가 말했다. “제발, 제발 패니에게 그렇게 매정하게 굴지 마세요.”"]], [["“Let me finish it, my dear;—oh, here I come.", "“끝까지 읽게 해 주렴, 얘야. 아, 여기 내가 나오는군."], ["‘Tell her ladyship my whereabouts.’ He little thought you’d show me this letter.”", "‘내가 어디 있는지 마님께 알려 주시오.’ 그이는 패니가 이 편지를 내게 보일 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐겠지요.”"]], [["“Didn’t he?”", "“몰랐을까요?”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts, putting out her hand to get it back, but in vain.", "편지를 되찾으려고 손을 내밀었으나 헛수고였던 로바츠 부인이 말했다."], ["“I thought it was for the best; I did indeed.”", "“저는 그게 가장 좋다고 생각했습니다. 정말 그랬습니다.”"]], [["“I had better finish it now, if you please.", "“이제 내가 끝까지 읽는 게 좋겠어요."], ["What is this?", "이건 뭔가요?"], ["How does he dare send his ribald jokes to me in such a matter?", "이런 일에 어떻게 감히 나에게 상스러운 농담을 보낼 수가 있지요?"], ["No, I do not suppose I ever shall like Dr.", "아니에요, 나는 프라우디 박사를 좋아하게 되리라고는 생각하지 않아요."], ["Proudie; I have never expected it.", "그런 걸 바란 적도 없어요."], ["A matter of conscience with him!", "그이에게는 양심의 문제라니!"], ["Well—well, well.", "원, 원, 원."], ["Had I not read it myself, I could not have believed it of him.", "내 눈으로 읽지 않았다면 그이가 이랬으리라고 믿지 못했을 거예요."], ["I would not positively have believed it.", "정말로 믿지 못했을 거예요."], ["‘Coming from my parish he could not go to the Duke of Omnium!’ And it is what I would wish to have said.", "‘내 교구에서 온 사람이 옴니엄 공작에게 갈 수는 없다!’ 그리고 그건 내가 하고 싶었던 말이기도 해요."], ["People fit for this parish should not be fit for the Duke of Omnium’s house.", "이 교구에 어울리는 사람이라면 옴니엄 공작의 집에는 어울리지 않아야 하지요."], ["And I had trusted that he would have this feeling more strongly than any one else in it.", "그리고 나는 그이가 이 교구의 누구보다도 그 감정을 더 강하게 품고 있으리라 믿었어요."], ["I have been deceived—that’s all.”", "나는 속았어요. 그뿐이에요.”"]], [["“He has done nothing to deceive you, Lady Lufton.”", "“그이는 마님을 속인 일이 없습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“I hope he will not have deceived you, my dear.", "“그이가 패니를 속이지 않기를 바라요, 얘야."], ["‘More money;’ yes, it is probable that he will want more money.", "‘돈이 더 필요하오.’ 그래요, 그이가 돈을 더 원하게 될 것도 그럴 법한 일이지요."], ["There is your letter, Fanny.", "편지 여기 있어요, 패니."], ["I am very sorry for it.", "참 안타까운 일이에요."], ["I can say nothing more.”", "더는 할 말이 없어요.”"], ["And she folded up the letter and gave it back to Mrs. Robarts.", "그러고 그녀는 편지를 접어 로바츠 부인에게 돌려주었다."]], [["“I thought it right to show it you,”", "“보여 드리는 것이 옳다고 생각했습니다,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“It did not much matter whether you did or no; of course I must have been told.”", "“보이든 안 보이든 큰 상관은 없었어요. 어차피 나는 들어야 했을 테니까요.”"]], [["“He especially begs me to tell you.”", "“그이가 마님께 꼭 알려 드리라고 했습니다.”"]], [["“Why, yes; he could not very well have kept me in the dark in such a matter.", "“그렇겠지요. 이런 일에 나를 어둠 속에 놓아둘 수야 없었겠지요."], ["He could not neglect his own work, and go and live with gamblers and adulterers at the Duke of Omnium’s without my knowing it.”", "제 일을 팽개친 채 옴니엄 공작의 집에서 도박꾼과 간음하는 자들과 어울려 지내면서 내가 모르게 할 수는 없었을 테니까요.”"]], [["And now Fanny Robarts’s cup was full, full to the overflowing. When she heard these words she forgot all about Lady Lufton, all about Lady Meredith, and remembered only her husband,—that he was her husband, and, in spite of his faults, a good and loving husband;—and that other fact also she remembered, that she was his wife.", "그리고 이제 패니 로바츠의 잔은 가득 찼다. 넘칠 만큼 가득 찼다. 이 말을 들었을 때 그녀는 레이디 러프턴도 레이디 메러디스도 다 잊고 오직 남편만을 떠올렸다. 그이가 자기 남편이라는 것, 결점이 있어도 착하고 사랑이 깊은 남편이라는 것을. 그리고 또 하나의 사실도 떠올렸다. 자기가 그이의 아내라는 것을."]], [["“Lady Lufton,”", "“레이디 러프턴,”"], ["she said, “you forget yourself in speaking in that way of my husband.”", "그녀가 말했다. “제 남편을 그런 식으로 말씀하시는 것은 도를 넘으시는 일입니다.”"]], [["“What!”", "“무엇이라고요!”"], ["said her ladyship; “you are to show me such a letter as that, and I am not to tell you what I think?”", "마님이 말했다. “나에게 그런 편지를 보여 주고서, 내가 생각하는 바는 말하지 말라는 거예요?”"]], [["“Not if you think such hard things as that.", "“그렇게 모진 것을 생각하신다면 안 됩니다."], ["Even you are not justified in speaking to me in that way, and I will not hear it.”", "마님이시라도 저에게 그런 식으로 말씀하실 권리는 없습니다. 저는 그것을 듣지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["“Heighty-tighty!”", "“어허, 이거 참 대단하시군!”"], ["said her ladyship.", "마님이 말했다."]], [["“Whether or no he is right in going to the Duke of Omnium’s, I will not pretend to judge.", "“그이가 옴니엄 공작의 집에 가는 것이 옳은지 그른지는 제가 감히 판단하지 않겠습니다."], ["He is the judge of his own actions, and neither you nor I.”", "그이의 행동을 판단하는 이는 그이 자신이고, 마님도 저도 아닙니다.”"]], [["“And when he leaves you with the butcher’s bill unpaid and no money to buy shoes for the children, who will be the judge then?”", "“그러다 그이가 정육점 외상값도 갚지 않고 아이들 신발 살 돈도 남기지 않은 채 패니를 두고 떠나면, 그때는 누가 판단하지요?”"]], [["“Not you, Lady Lufton.", "“마님은 아니십니다, 레이디 러프턴."], ["If such bad days should ever come—and neither you nor I have a right to expect them—I will not come to you in my troubles; not after this.”", "그런 나쁜 날이 온다 해도—마님도 저도 그런 날을 예상할 권리는 없습니다만—저는 어려울 때 마님께 오지 않겠습니다. 이런 일이 있은 뒤에는요.”"]], [["“Very well, my dear.", "“좋아요, 얘야."], ["You may go to the Duke of Omnium if that suits you better.”", "그게 더 좋다면 옴니엄 공작에게 가도 되겠지요.”"]], [["“Fanny, come away,”", "“패니, 그만 가요,”"], ["said Lady Meredith.", "레이디 메러디스가 말했다."], ["“Why should you try to anger my mother?”", "“왜 우리 어머니를 화나게 하려 해요?”"]], [["“I don’t want to anger her; but I won’t hear him abused in that way without speaking up for him.", "“화나게 하려는 게 아니에요. 하지만 그이가 그런 식으로 욕을 듣는 것을 두고 보면서 그이를 위해 말하지 않을 수는 없어요."], ["If I don’t defend him, who will?", "내가 변호하지 않으면 누가 하겠어요?"], ["Lady Lufton has said terrible things about him; and they are not true.”", "레이디 러프턴이 그이에 대해 끔찍한 말을 하셨어요. 그리고 그건 사실이 아니에요.”"]], [["“Oh, Fanny!”", "“오, 패니!”"], ["said Justinia.", "저스티니아가 말했다."]], [["“Very well, very well!”", "“좋아요, 좋아요!”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“This is the sort of return that one gets.”", "“이런 게 사람이 받는 보답이군요.”"]], [["“I don’t know what you mean by return, Lady Lufton: but would you wish me to stand by quietly and hear such things said of my husband?", "“보답이라는 말씀이 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠습니다, 레이디 러프턴. 하지만 제 남편이 그런 말을 듣는데도 저더러 잠자코 서 있으라는 말씀입니까?"], ["He does not live with such people as you have named.", "그이는 마님이 말씀하신 그런 사람들과 함께 살지 않습니다."], ["He does not neglect his duties.", "자기 의무를 소홀히 하지도 않습니다."], ["If every clergyman were as much in his parish, it would be well for some of them.", "모든 성직자가 그이만큼 자기 교구에 머문다면 그들 가운데 몇 사람에게는 잘된 일일 것입니다."], ["And in going to such a house as the Duke of Omnium’s it does make a difference that he goes there in company with the bishop.", "그리고 옴니엄 공작의 집 같은 곳에 갈 때, 그이가 주교와 함께 간다는 것은 분명 차이가 있습니다."], ["I can’t explain why, but I know that it does.”", "왜 그런지 설명할 수는 없지만, 차이가 있다는 것은 압니다.”"]], [["“Especially when the bishop is coupled up with the devil, as Mr. Robarts has done,”", "“로바츠 씨가 한 것처럼 주교를 악마와 한데 묶어 놓았을 때는 특히 그렇겠지요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton; “he can join the duke with them and then they’ll stand for the three Graces, won’t they, Justinia?”", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “거기에 공작까지 붙여 놓으면 셋이 삼미신 노릇을 하겠구나, 그렇지 않니, 저스티니아?”"], ["And Lady Lufton laughed a bitter little laugh at her own wit.", "그러고 나서 레이디 러프턴은 자기 재담에 씁쓸한 웃음을 조금 웃었다."]], [["“I suppose I may go now, Lady Lufton.”", "“이제 가도 되겠습니까, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“Oh, yes, certainly, my dear.”", "“아, 그래요, 물론이지요, 얘야.”"]], [["“I am sorry if I have made you angry with me; but I will not allow any one to speak against Mr.", "“제가 마님을 노엽게 했다면 죄송합니다. 하지만 누가 로바츠 씨를 두고 나쁜 말을 하는데 대꾸하지 않고 있을 수는 없습니다."], ["Robarts without answering them.", "마님은 그이에게 매우 부당하셨습니다."], ["You have been very unjust to him; and even though I do anger you, I must say so.”", "마님을 노엽게 하더라도 저는 그렇게 말해야 합니다.”"]], [["“Come, Fanny; this is too bad,”", "“이봐요, 패니. 이건 너무하군요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“You have been scolding me for the last half-hour because I would not congratulate you on this new friend that your husband has made, and now you are going to begin it all over again.", "“패니는 반 시간 내내 나를 꾸짖었어요. 남편이 새로 사귄 이 친구를 두고 내가 축하해 주지 않았다고 말이지요."], ["That is more than I can stand.", "그런데 이제 또 그 모든 것을 처음부터 다시 시작하려 하는군요."], ["If you have nothing else particular to say, you might as well leave me.”", "그건 내가 견딜 수 있는 정도를 넘어요. 달리 특별히 할 말이 없다면 이만 나가 주는 게 좋겠어요.”"], ["And Lady Lufton’s face as she spoke was unbending, severe, and harsh.", "그렇게 말하는 레이디 러프턴의 얼굴은 굽힘이 없고 엄하고 매서웠다."]], [["Mrs.", "로바츠 부인은 전에는 오랜 친구에게서 그런 말을 들어 본 적이 없었다."], ["Robarts had never before been so spoken to by her old friend; indeed she had never been so spoken to by any one, and she hardly knew how to bear herself.", "실은 누구에게서도 그런 말을 들어 본 적이 없었기에, 어떻게 처신해야 할지 알 수 없었다."]], [["“Very well, Lady Lufton,”", "“좋습니다, 레이디 러프턴,”"], ["she said; “then I will go.", "그녀가 말했다. “그러면 저는 가겠습니다."], ["Good-bye.”", "안녕히 계십시오.”"]], [["“Good-bye,”", "“잘 가요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton, and turning herself to her table she began to arrange her papers.", "레이디 러프턴이 말하고, 탁자를 향해 몸을 돌려 서류를 정리하기 시작했다."], ["Fanny had never before left Framley Court to go back to her own parsonage without a warm embrace.", "패니는 전에는 프램리 코트를 떠나 자기 목사관으로 돌아갈 때 따뜻한 포옹 없이 나선 적이 없었다."], ["Now she was to do so without even having her hand taken.", "이제 그녀는 손조차 잡히지 못한 채 그렇게 하게 되었다."], ["Had it come to this, that there was absolutely to be a quarrel between them,—a quarrel for ever?", "일이 여기까지 와서, 정말로 두 사람 사이에 다툼이—영원한 다툼이—생기게 되었단 말인가?"]], [["“Fanny is going, you know, mamma,”", "“패니가 가요, 어머니,”"], ["said Lady Meredith.", "레이디 메러디스가 말했다."], ["“She will be home before you are down again.”", "“어머니가 다시 내려오시기 전에 집에 가 있을 거예요.”"]], [["“I cannot help it, my dear.", "“어쩔 수 없구나, 얘야."], ["Fanny must do as she pleases.", "패니는 하고 싶은 대로 해야지."], ["I am not to be the judge of her actions.", "나는 그 사람의 행동을 판단하는 사람이 아니야."], ["She has just told me so.”", "방금 그 사람이 나에게 그렇게 말했으니.”"]], [["Mrs. Robarts had said nothing of the kind, but she was far too proud to point this out.", "로바츠 부인은 그런 말을 하지 않았지만, 그것을 짚어 말하기에는 자존심이 너무 높았다."], ["So with a gentle step she retreated through the door, and then Lady Meredith, having tried what a conciliatory whisper with her mother would do, followed her.", "그래서 그녀는 조용한 걸음으로 문을 나섰고, 그러고 나서 레이디 메러디스는 어머니에게 화해의 귀엣말을 시도해 본 뒤 그녀를 따라 나왔다."], ["Alas, the conciliatory whisper was altogether ineffectual!", "아, 그 화해의 귀엣말은 아무런 소용이 없었다!"]], [["The two ladies said nothing as they descended the stairs, but when they had regained the drawing-room they looked with blank horror into each other’s faces.", "두 여인은 계단을 내려오는 동안 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 응접실에 다시 들어서자 새하얗게 질린 얼굴로 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["What were they to do now?", "이제 어떻게 해야 하는가?"], ["Of such a tragedy as this they had had no remotest preconception.", "이런 비극은 두 사람이 조금도 예상하지 못한 일이었다."], ["Was it absolutely the case that Fanny Robarts was to walk out of Lady Lufton’s house as a declared enemy,—she who, before her marriage as well as since, had been almost treated as an adopted daughter of the family?", "정말로 패니 로바츠가, 결혼 전이나 후로나 이 집안의 양녀처럼 대접받아 온 그녀가, 선포된 원수가 되어 레이디 러프턴의 집에서 걸어 나가야 하는 것인가?"]], [["“Oh, Fanny, why did you answer my mother in that way?”", "“오, 패니, 왜 우리 어머니에게 그런 식으로 대답했어요?”"], ["said Lady Meredith.", "레이디 메러디스가 말했다."], ["“You saw that she was vexed.", "“어머니가 속상해하시는 게 보였잖아요."], ["She had other things to vex her besides this about Mr. Robarts.”", "로바츠 씨 일 말고도 어머니를 속상하게 하는 일이 또 있었어요.”"]], [["“And would not you answer any one who attacked Sir George?”", "“그럼 저스티니아는 누가 조지 경을 공격하는데도 대답하지 않겠어요?”"]], [["“No, not my own mother.", "“그럼요, 우리 어머니라면 안 하지요."], ["I would let her say what she pleased, and leave Sir George to fight his own battles.”", "어머니가 무슨 말씀을 하시든 하시게 두고, 조지 경은 스스로 싸우게 놔둘 거예요.”"]], [["“Ah, but it is different with you.", "“아, 하지만 저스티니아는 사정이 달라요."], ["You are her daughter, and Sir George—she would not dare to speak in that way as to Sir George’s doings.”", "저스티니아는 어머니의 딸이고, 조지 경은—어머니는 조지 경이 하는 일을 두고 그런 식으로 말할 엄두를 내지 않으실 거예요.”"]], [["“Indeed she would, if it pleased her.", "“아니에요, 마음만 있으시면 하실 거예요."], ["I am sorry I let you go up to her.”", "패니를 어머니께 올려 보낸 게 후회돼요.”"]], [["“It is as well that it should be over, Justinia.", "“이렇게 끝난 게 오히려 잘된 일이에요, 저스티니아."], ["As those are her thoughts about Mr. Robarts, it is quite as well that we should know them.", "그게 로바츠 씨에 대한 어머니의 생각이라면, 우리가 그것을 아는 게 차라리 나아요."], ["Even for all that I owe to her, and all the love I bear to you, I will not come to this house if I am to hear my husband abused;—not into any house.”", "제가 어머니께 입은 은혜가 아무리 크고 저스티니아를 사랑하는 마음이 아무리 깊어도, 남편이 욕을 듣는다면 저는 이 집에 오지 않겠어요. 어느 집에도요.”"]], [["“My dearest Fanny, we all know what happens when two angry people get together.”", "“내 사랑하는 패니, 화난 두 사람이 마주 앉으면 어떻게 되는지는 우리 모두 알아요.”"]], [["“I was not angry when I went up to her; not in the least.”", "“어머니께 올라갈 때 저는 화나지 않았어요. 조금도요.”"]], [["“It is no good looking back.", "“돌아보는 건 소용없어요."], ["What are we to do now, Fanny?”", "이제 우리가 어떻게 해야 할까요, 패니?”"]], [["“I suppose I had better go home,”", "“집으로 돌아가는 게 좋겠어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."], ["“I will go and put my things up, and then I will send James for them.”", "“가서 물건을 챙겨 두고, 제임스를 보내 가져오게 하겠어요.”"]], [["“Wait till after lunch, and then you will be able to kiss my mother before you leave us.”", "“점심때까지 기다려요. 그러면 떠나기 전에 우리 어머니에게 입을 맞출 수 있을 거예요.”"]], [["“No, Justinia; I cannot wait.", "“아니에요, 저스티니아."], ["I must answer Mr.", "기다릴 수 없어요."], ["Robarts by this post, and I must think what I have to say to him.", "이번 우편으로 로바츠 씨에게 답장을 써야 하는데, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 생각해 봐야 해요."], ["I could not write that letter here, and the post goes at four.” And Mrs.", "여기서는 그 편지를 쓸 수 없고, 우편은 네 시에 나가요.”"], ["Robarts got up from her chair, preparatory to her final departure.", "그러고 로바츠 부인은 마지막으로 떠날 준비를 하며 의자에서 일어섰다."]], [["“I shall come to you before dinner,”", "“저녁 식사 전에 찾아갈게요,”"], ["said Lady Meredith; “and if I can bring you good tidings, I shall expect you to come back here with me.", "레이디 메러디스가 말했다. “그리고 좋은 소식을 가져갈 수 있으면, 패니가 나와 함께 이리로 돌아오기를 바라겠어요."], ["It is out of the question that I should go away from Framley leaving you and my mother at enmity with each other.”", "패니와 우리 어머니가 서로 원수가 된 채로 내가 프램리를 떠난다는 건 있을 수 없는 일이에요.”"]], [["To this Mrs. Robarts made no answer; and in a very few minutes afterwards she was in her own nursery, kissing her children, and teaching the elder one to say something about papa.", "이에 로바츠 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["But, even as she taught him, the tears stood in her eyes, and the little fellow knew that everything was not right.", "그리고 아주 얼마 지나지 않아 그녀는 자기 아이 방에서 아이들에게 입을 맞추며, 큰아이에게 아빠에 대한 말을 가르치고 있었다. 그러나 가르치는 동안에도 눈에는 눈물이 고였고, 어린 녀석은 모든 것이 온전하지 않다는 것을 알았다."]], [["And there she sat till about two, doing little odds and ends of things for the children, and allowing that occupation to stand as an excuse to her for not commencing her letter.", "그리고 그녀는 두 시쯤까지 거기 앉아 아이들을 위해 자잘한 일들을 하며, 그 일을 편지를 시작하지 않는 핑계로 삼았다. 그러나 그러고 나니 남은 시간은 두 시간뿐이었고, 편지는 쓰기 어려울지도 몰랐다."], ["But then there remained only two hours to her, and it might be that the letter would be difficult in the writing—would require thought and changes, and must needs be copied, perhaps more than once.", "생각이 필요하고 고쳐 쓸 곳도 있어서 어쩌면 한 번 이상 옮겨 적어야 할지도 몰랐다. 돈이라면 집에 있었다."], ["As to the money, that she had in the house—as much, at least, as Mark now wanted, though the sending of it would leave her nearly penniless.", "적어도 마크가 지금 원하는 만큼은 있었다. 다만 그것을 보내면 그녀에게는 거의 한 푼도 남지 않을 것이었다."], ["She could, however, in case of personal need, resort to Davis as desired by him.", "그러나 정말 필요할 때는 그이가 말한 대로 데이비스에게 부탁할 수 있었다."]], [["So she got out her desk in the drawing-room and sat down and wrote her letter.", "그래서 그녀는 응접실에서 필기함을 꺼내 앉아 편지를 썼다. 쓰기 어려웠지만, 생각했던 만큼 오래 걸리지는 않았다."], ["It was difficult, though she found that it hardly took so long as she expected.", "어려웠던 것은 그이에게 사실을 말해야 한다고 느꼈으면서도, 친구들 사이에서 누리는 그이의 즐거움을 다 망쳐 놓고 싶지는 않았기 때문이다."], ["It was difficult, for she felt bound to tell him the truth; and yet she was anxious not to spoil all his pleasure among his friends.", "그러면서도 그녀는 레이디 러프턴이 매우 화가 나 있다고 그에게 알렸다. “정말이지 터무니없이 화가 나셨어요,”"], ["She told him, however, that Lady Lufton was very angry, “unreasonably angry, I must say,”", "그녀는 자기가 그의 편에 서지 않은 것이 아님을 보이려고 그렇게 끼워 넣었다. “그래서 우리는 아주 다투고 말았어요."], ["she put in, in order to show that she had not sided against him.", "이 일이 저를 불행하게 만들어요. 당신도 그러실 줄 알아요, 여보."], ["“And indeed we have quite quarrelled, and this has made me unhappy, as it will you, dearest; I know that.", "하지만 우리는 그분이 마음속으로는 얼마나 좋은 분인지 알고, 저스티니아는 그분에게 다른 걱정거리가 있었다고 해요."], ["But we both know how good she is at heart, and Justinia thinks that she had other things to trouble her; and I hope it will all be made up before you come home; only, dearest Mark, pray do not be longer than you said in your last letter.”", "당신이 집에 돌아오기 전에 다 풀어지기를 바라요. 다만 사랑하는 마크, 지난 편지에서 말한 것보다 더 오래 있지는 말아 주세요.”"], ["And then there were three or four paragraphs about the babies and two about the schools, which I may as well omit.", "그러고는 아기들에 대한 서너 문단과 학교에 대한 두 문단이 있었는데, 그건 내가 빼도 좋을 것이다."]], [["She had just finished her letter, and was carefully folding it for its envelope, with the two whole five-pound notes imprudently placed within it, when she heard a footstep on the gravel path which led up from a small wicket to the front-door.", "편지를 막 끝내고, 5파운드짜리 지폐 두 장을 고스란히, 무모하게도 그 안에 넣은 채 봉투에 넣으려고 조심스레 접고 있을 때, 그녀는 작은 쪽문에서 현관으로 이어지는 자갈길에 나는 발소리를 들었다."], ["The path ran near the drawing-room window, and she was just in time to catch a glimpse of the last fold of a passing cloak.", "그 길은 응접실 창 가까이로 지나갔고, 그녀는 스쳐 가는 외투의 마지막 자락을 가까스로 볼 수 있었다."], ["“It is Justinia,”", "“저스티니아구나,”"], ["she said to herself; and her heart became disturbed at the idea of again discussing the morning’s adventure.", "그녀는 마음속으로 말했다. 그러고 아침의 그 사건을 또 이야기하게 될 생각에 마음이 어지러워졌다."], ["“What am I to do,”", "“어떻게 해야 하지,”"], ["she had said to herself before, “if she wants me to beg her pardon?", "그녀는 앞서도 마음속으로 이렇게 말했었다. “저스티니아가 나더러 어머니께 용서를 빌라고 하면?"], ["I will not own before her that he is in the wrong.”", "그이가 잘못했다고 그 앞에서 인정하지는 않겠어.”"]], [["And then the door opened—for the visitor made her entrance without the aid of any servant—and Lady Lufton herself stood before her.", "그러고 문이 열렸다—손님은 하인의 도움 없이 몸소 들어섰던 것이다—그리고 레이디 러프턴이 그녀 앞에 서 있었다."], ["“Fanny,”", "“패니,”"], ["she said at once, “I have come to beg your pardon.”", "그녀가 곧바로 말했다. “내가 용서를 빌러 왔어요.”"]], [["“Oh, Lady Lufton!”", "“오, 레이디 러프턴!”"]], [["“I was very much harassed when you came to me just now;—by more things than one, my dear.", "“조금 전 패니가 나에게 왔을 때 나는 몹시 시달리고 있었어요. 한 가지 일만이 아니었지요, 얘야."], ["But, nevertheless, I should not have spoken to you of your husband as I did, and so I have come to beg your pardon.”", "그렇더라도 남편에 대해 그런 식으로 말하지는 않아야 했어요. 그래서 용서를 빌러 왔어요.”"]], [["Mrs. Robarts was past answering by the time that this was said,—past answering at least in words; so she jumped up and, with her eyes full of tears, threw herself into her old friend’s arms.", "이 말이 끝났을 때 로바츠 부인은 이미 대답할 수 있는 상태를 넘어섰다."], ["“Oh, Lady Lufton!”", "적어도 말로는 대답할 수 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 눈에 눈물을 가득 담고 오랜 친구의 품에 몸을 던졌다."], ["she sobbed forth again.", "“오, 레이디 러프턴!” 그녀가 다시 흐느끼며 말했다."]], [["“You will forgive me, won’t you?”", "“나를 용서해 주겠지요?”"], ["said her ladyship, as she returned her young friend’s caress.", "젊은 친구의 포옹에 답하며 마님이 말했다."], ["“Well, that’s right.", "“자, 됐어요."], ["I have not been at all happy since you left my den this morning, and I don’t suppose you have.", "오늘 아침 패니가 내 골방을 나간 뒤로 나도 조금도 행복하지 않았어요. 패니도 그랬으리라 생각해요."], ["But, Fanny, dearest, we love each other too well and know each other too thoroughly, to have a long quarrel, don’t we?”", "하지만 패니, 우리는 서로를 너무 사랑하고 서로를 너무 잘 알아서 오래 다툴 수는 없지요. 그렇지 않아요?”"]], [["“Oh, yes, Lady Lufton.”", "“오, 그렇습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“Of course we do.", "“물론 그렇지요."], ["Friends are not to be picked up on the road-side every day; nor are they to be thrown away lightly.", "친구란 날마다 길가에서 주울 수 있는 것이 아니고, 가볍게 버릴 수 있는 것도 아니에요."], ["And now sit down, my love, and let us have a little talk.", "이제 앉아요, 얘야. 우리 조금 이야기를 해요."], ["There, I must take my bonnet off.", "아, 모자를 벗어야겠어요."], ["You have pulled the strings so that you have almost choked me.”", "패니가 끈을 어찌나 당겼는지 숨이 막힐 지경이었어요.”"], ["And Lady Lufton deposited her bonnet on the table and seated herself comfortably in the corner of the sofa.", "그러고 레이디 러프턴은 모자를 탁자에 내려놓고 소파 구석에 편안히 앉았다."]], [["“My dear,”", "“얘야,”"], ["she said, “there is no duty which any woman owes to any other human being at all equal to that which she owes to her husband, and, therefore, you were quite right to stand up for Mr.", "그녀가 말했다. “어떤 여자가 다른 사람에게 지는 의무 가운데 남편에게 지는 의무만 한 것은 없어요."], ["Robarts this morning.”", "그러니 오늘 아침 로바츠 씨를 위해 나선 것은 아주 옳았어요.”"]], [["Upon this Mrs. Robarts said nothing, but she got her hand within that of her ladyship and gave it a slight squeeze.", "이 말에 로바츠 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 마님의 손 안에 자기 손을 넣고 가볍게 쥐었다."]], [["“And I loved you for what you were doing all the time.", "“그리고 나는 패니가 그렇게 하는 동안 줄곧 패니를 사랑했어요."], ["I did, my dear; though you were a little fierce, you know.", "정말이에요, 얘야. 조금 사납기는 했지만요."], ["Even Justinia admits that, and she has been at me ever since you went away.", "저스티니아도 그건 인정해요. 그 애는 패니가 떠난 뒤로 줄곧 나를 몰아세웠어요."], ["And indeed, I did not know that it was in you to look in that way out of those pretty eyes of yours.”", "그리고 정말이지, 그 예쁜 눈으로 그런 눈빛을 낼 줄은 몰랐어요.”"]], [["“Oh, Lady Lufton!”", "“오, 레이디 러프턴!”"]], [["“But I looked fierce enough too myself, I dare say; so we’ll say nothing more about that; will we?", "“하지만 나도 어지간히 사나운 얼굴을 했겠지요. 그러니 그 이야기는 더 하지 않기로 해요."], ["But now, about this good man of yours?”", "그럴까요? 그런데 이제 이 착한 남편 이야기를 해 봐요.”"]], [["“Dear Lady Lufton, you must forgive him.”", "“레이디 러프턴, 그이를 용서해 주십시오.”"]], [["“Well: as you ask me, I will.", "“그래요, 패니가 그렇게 청하니 용서하지요."], ["We’ll have nothing more said about the duke, either now or when he comes back; not a word.", "공작에 대해서는 더 아무 말도 하지 않겠어요. 지금도, 그이가 돌아온 뒤에도요."], ["Let me see—he’s to be back;—when is it?”", "한마디도요. 어디 보자, 그이가 돌아오는 건—언제였지요?”"]], [["“Wednesday week, I think.”", "“다음 주 수요일인 것 같습니다.”"]], [["“Ah, Wednesday.", "“아, 수요일."], ["Well, tell him to come and dine up at the house on Wednesday.", "그럼 수요일에 저택으로 저녁을 먹으러 오라고 전해요."], ["He’ll be in time, I suppose, and there shan’t be a word said about this horrid duke.”", "그때면 시간이 맞겠지요. 그리고 이 끔찍한 공작에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않겠어요.”"]], [["“I am so much obliged to you, Lady Lufton.”", "“정말 감사합니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“But look here, my dear; believe me, he’s better off without such friends.”", "“하지만 이걸 알아 둬요, 얘야. 내 말을 믿어요. 그이는 그런 친구들이 없는 편이 나아요.”"]], [["“Oh, I know he is; much better off.”", "“오, 저도 압니다. 훨씬 낫습니다.”"]], [["“Well, I’m glad you admit that, for I thought you seemed to be in favour of the duke.”", "“그렇게 인정해 주니 반갑군요. 나는 패니가 공작 편을 드는 것 같다고 생각했어요.”"]], [["“Oh, no, Lady Lufton.”", "“오, 아닙니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“That’s right, then.", "“그러면 됐어요."], ["And now, if you’ll take my advice, you’ll use your influence, as a good, dear sweet wife as you are, to prevent his going there any more.", "그리고 이제 내 말을 들어 준다면, 지금처럼 착하고 사랑스럽고 다정한 아내로서 패니의 영향력을 써서 그이가 다시는 거기 가지 않게 해요."], ["I’m an old woman and he is a young man, and it’s very natural that he should think me behind the times.", "나는 늙은 여자고 그이는 젊은 남자니, 그이가 나를 시대에 뒤진 사람이라 여기는 것도 아주 자연스러운 일이에요."], ["I’m not angry at that.", "그 일로 화를 내는 게 아니에요."], ["But he’ll find that it’s better for him, better for him in every way, to stick to his old friends.", "그러나 그이는 오랜 친구들을 지키는 것이 자기에게 더 낫다는 것을, 모든 면에서 더 낫다는 것을 알게 될 거예요."], ["It will be better for his peace of mind, better for his character as a clergyman, better for his pocket, better for his children and for you,—and better for his eternal welfare.", "마음의 평안에도 더 낫고, 성직자로서 인격에도 더 낫고, 주머니에도 더 낫고, 아이들과 패니에게도 더 낫고—그리고 영원한 복락에도 더 나아요."], ["The duke is not such a companion as he should seek;—nor if he is sought, should he allow himself to be led away.”", "공작은 그이가 찾아 나설 벗이 아니에요. 또 저쪽에서 찾아온다 해도 이끌려 가서는 안 되지요.”"]], [["And then Lady Lufton ceased, and Fanny Robarts kneeling at her feet sobbed, with her face hidden on her friend’s knees.", "그러고 나서 레이디 러프턴이 말을 그쳤고, 패니 로바츠는 그녀의 발 앞에 무릎을 꿇은 채 친구의 무릎에 얼굴을 감추고 흐느꼈다."], ["She had not a word now to say as to her husband’s capability of judging for himself.", "남편이 스스로 판단할 능력이 있다는 말은 이제 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["“And now I must be going again; but Justinia has made me promise,—promise, mind you, most solemnly, that I would have you back to dinner to-night,—by force if necessary.", "“그리고 이제 나는 또 가 봐야 해요. 그런데 저스티니아가 나에게서 약속을 받아 냈어요."], ["It was the only way I could make my peace with her; so you must not leave me in the lurch.”", "그것도 아주 엄숙하게 받아 냈지요. 오늘 저녁 식사에 패니를 데려오겠다고, 필요하다면 억지로라도 데려오겠다고요."], ["Of course, Fanny said that she would go and dine at Framley Court.", "그 애와 화해할 길이 그것뿐이었으니, 나를 곤경에 빠뜨리지 말아요.” 물론 패니는 프램리 코트에 가서 저녁을 먹겠다고 했다."]], [["“And you must not send that letter, by any means,”", "“그리고 그 편지는 절대로 보내지 말아요,”"], ["said her ladyship as she was leaving the room, poking with her umbrella at the epistle, which lay directed on Mrs.", "마님이 방을 나서면서, 로바츠 부인의 책상 위에 주소가 적힌 채 놓인 그 서한을 우산으로 찌르며 말했다."], ["Robarts’s desk. “I can understand very well what it contains.", "“거기 무엇이 들었는지 나는 아주 잘 알겠어요."], ["You must alter it altogether, my dear.”", "처음부터 다 고쳐 써야 해요, 얘야.”"], ["And then Lady Lufton went.", "그러고 나서 레이디 러프턴은 갔다."]], [["Mrs. Robarts instantly rushed to her desk and tore open her letter.", "로바츠 부인은 곧 책상으로 달려가 자기 편지를 찢어 열었다."], ["She looked at her watch and it was past four.", "시계를 보니 네 시가 지나 있었다."], ["She had hardly begun another when the postman came.", "다른 편지를 겨우 시작했을 때 우체부가 왔다. “오, 메리,”"], ["“Oh, Mary,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “do make him wait.", "“그 사람을 좀 기다리게 해 다오."], ["If he’ll wait a quarter of an hour I’ll give him a shilling.”", "십오 분만 기다려 주면 1실링을 주겠다고 해.”"]], [["“There’s no need of that, ma’am.", "“그러실 것 없습니다, 마님."], ["Let him have a glass of beer.”", "맥주 한 잔 주시면 됩니다.”"]], [["“Very well, Mary; but don’t give him too much, for fear he should drop the letters about.", "“그래, 메리. 하지만 너무 많이는 주지 마라."], ["I’ll be ready in ten minutes.”", "편지들을 흘리고 다니면 안 되니까. 십 분이면 준비될 거야.”"]], [["And in five minutes she had scrawled a very different sort of a letter.", "그리고 오 분 만에 그녀는 아주 다른 종류의 편지를 급히 갈겨썼다."], ["But he might want the money immediately, so she would not delay it for a day.", "그러나 그이가 돈을 곧 원할지도 몰랐으므로, 그녀는 그것을 하루라도 늦추려 하지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI. MR. HAROLD SMITH’S LECTURE.", "# 제6장. 해럴드 스미스 씨의 강연"]], [["On the whole the party at Chaldicotes was very pleasant, and the time passed away quickly enough. Mr.", "전체로 보아 콜디코츠의 모임은 매우 유쾌했고, 시간도 충분히 빠르게 흘러갔다."], ["Robarts’s chief friend there, independently of Mr. Sowerby, was Miss Dunstable, who seemed to take a great fancy to him, whereas she was not very accessible to the blandishments of Mr.", "로바츠 씨가 그곳에서 사귄 으뜸 벗은, 사워비 씨를 빼고 말한다면 던스터블 양이었다."], ["Supplehouse, nor more specially courteous even to her host than good manners required of her. But then Mr.", "그녀는 그에게 대단한 호감을 품은 듯했으나, 서플하우스 씨의 아첨에는 그다지 마음을 열지 않았고, 주인에게조차 좋은 예의가 요구하는 정도 이상으로 각별히 정중하지는 않았다."], ["Supplehouse and Mr. Sowerby were both bachelors, while Mark Robarts was a married man.", "하지만 서플하우스 씨와 사워비 씨는 둘 다 독신이었고, 마크 로바츠는 아내가 있는 사람이었다."]], [["With Mr. Sowerby Robarts had more than one communication respecting Lord Lufton and his affairs, which he would willingly have avoided had it been possible.", "로바츠는 사워비 씨와 러프턴 경과 그의 재정에 관하여 한 번 이상 이야기를 나누었는데, 할 수만 있었다면 기꺼이 피하고 싶은 이야기였다."], ["Sowerby was one of those men who are always mixing up business with pleasure, and who have usually some scheme in their mind which requires forwarding.", "사워비는 일과 즐거움을 늘 뒤섞어 놓는 부류의 사람, 그리고 머릿속에 밀고 나가야 할 무슨 계획을 언제나 하나쯤 품고 있는 부류의 사람이었다."], ["Men of this class have, as a rule, no daily work, no regular routine of labour; but it may be doubted whether they do not toil much more incessantly than those who have.", "이런 부류의 사람들은 대체로 날마다 할 일이 없고 정해진 노동의 일과도 없다. 그러나 그런 것을 가진 사람들보다 오히려 훨씬 더 끊임없이 애를 쓰고 있는 것이 아닌지는 의심해 볼 만하다."]], [["“Lufton is so dilatory,”", "“러프턴은 어찌나 굼뜬지,”"], ["Mr. Sowerby said.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."], ["“Why did he not arrange this at once, when he promised it?", "“약속했을 때 왜 그 일을 단번에 처리해 두지 않았을까요?"], ["And then he is so afraid of that old woman at Framley Court.", "게다가 프램리 코트의 그 노부인을 어찌나 무서워하는지."], ["Well, my dear fellow, say what you will; she is an old woman and she’ll never be younger.", "뭐, 이보세요, 무슨 말을 하셔도 좋습니다. 그분은 노부인이고, 앞으로 더 젊어지실 일도 없지요."], ["But do write to Lufton and tell him that this delay is inconvenient to me; he’ll do anything for you, I know.”", "아무튼 러프턴에게 편지를 써서 이렇게 늦어지면 내가 곤란하다고 전해 주세요. 그는 당신 말이라면 무엇이든 들을 사람이니까요.”"]], [["Mark said that he would write, and, indeed, did do so; but he did not at first like the tone of the conversation into which he was dragged.", "마크는 쓰겠다고 말했고, 실제로 썼다. 그러나 처음에는 자신이 끌려 들어간 대화의 어조가 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["It was very painful to him to hear Lady Lufton called an old woman, and hardly less so to discuss the propriety of Lord Lufton’s parting with his property.", "레이디 러프턴이 노부인이라 불리는 것을 듣기가 몹시 괴로웠고, 러프턴 경이 자기 재산을 떼어 내는 일이 옳은지 아닌지를 따지는 것도 그에 못지않게 괴로웠다."], ["This was irksome to him, till habit made it easy.", "습관이 그것을 쉽게 만들어 주기까지 이것은 그에게 거북한 일이었다."], ["But by degrees his feelings became less acute, and he accustomed himself to his friend Sowerby’s mode of talking.", "그러나 차츰 그의 감각은 무디어졌고, 그는 벗 사워비의 말투에 스스로를 익숙하게 만들어 갔다."]], [["And then on the Saturday afternoon they all went over to Barchester.", "그러고 나서 토요일 오후에 그들은 모두 바체스터로 건너갔다."], ["Harold Smith during the last forty-eight hours had become crammed to overflowing with Sarawak, Labuan, New Guinea, and the Salomon Islands.", "해럴드 스미스는 지난 사십팔 시간 동안 사라왁과 라부안, 뉴기니와 솔로몬 제도로 넘칠 만큼 꽉 채워진 사람이 되어 있었다."], ["As is the case with all men labouring under temporary specialities, he for the time had faith in nothing else, and was not content that any one near him should have any other faith.", "일시적인 전문 분야에 짓눌린 모든 사람이 그렇듯, 그는 당장은 그 밖의 어떤 것도 믿지 않았고, 자기 주변의 누구라도 다른 무엇을 믿는 것을 참지 못했다."], ["They called him Viscount Papua and Baron Borneo; and his wife, who headed the joke against him, insisted on having her title.", "사람들은 그를 파푸아 자작이라, 보르네오 남작이라 불렀다. 그를 놀리는 농담에 앞장선 아내는 자기 칭호를 달라고 우겼다."], ["Miss Dunstable swore that she would wed none but a South Sea islander; and to Mark was offered the income and duties of Bishop of Spices.", "던스터블 양은 남태평양 섬사람이 아니면 누구와도 혼인하지 않겠노라 맹세했고, 마크에게는 향료 주교의 수입과 직무가 제안되었다."], ["Nor did the Proudie family set themselves against these little sarcastic quips with any overwhelming severity.", "프라우디 집안 사람들도 이런 조그만 비꼬는 농담에 압도적인 엄격함으로 맞서지는 않았다."], ["It is sweet to unbend oneself at the proper opportunity, and this was the proper opportunity for Mrs. Proudie’s unbending.", "알맞은 기회에 스스로를 풀어놓는 것은 달콤한 일이고, 이때가 프라우디 부인이 스스로를 풀어놓기에 알맞은 기회였다."], ["No mortal can be seriously wise at all hours; and in these happy hours did that usually wise mortal, the bishop, lay aside for awhile his serious wisdom.", "어떤 인간도 하루의 온 시간을 진지하게 지혜로울 수는 없다. 그리하여 이 즐거운 시간에는 평소 지혜로운 그 인간, 곧 주교도 자신의 진지한 지혜를 얼마간 내려놓았다."]], [["“We think of dining at five to-morrow, my Lady Papua,”", "“내일은 다섯 시에 만찬을 할까 하는데, 레이디 파푸아,”"], ["said the facetious bishop; “will that suit his lordship and the affairs of State?", "익살맞은 주교가 말했다. “그것이 각하와 국사(國事)에 맞겠소?"], ["he!", "허!"], ["he!", "허!"], ["he!”", "허!”"], ["And the good prelate laughed at the fun.", "그러고서 이 선량한 고위 성직자는 그 농담에 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["How pleasantly young men and women of fifty or thereabouts can joke and flirt and poke their fun about, laughing and holding their sides, dealing in little innuendoes and rejoicing in nicknames when they have no Mentors of twenty-five or thirty near them to keep them in order.", "쉰 살쯤 되는 젊은 신사와 숙녀들은, 자기들을 단속할 스물다섯이나 서른 살의 멘토가 곁에 없을 때면 얼마나 유쾌하게 농담을 하고 희롱을 하고 서로를 찔러 대며, 옆구리를 붙잡고 웃고, 조그만 빈정거림을 주고받고, 별명을 즐거워하는가."], ["The vicar of Framley might perhaps have been regarded as such a Mentor, were it not for that capability of adapting himself to the company immediately around him on which he so much piqued himself.", "프램리의 교구 사제도 아마 그런 멘토로 여겨졌을지 모른다. 자기 바로 주변의 사람들에게 스스로를 맞추는 그 능력을 그가 그토록 자랑스러워하지 않았다면 말이다."], ["He therefore also talked to my Lady Papua, and was jocose about the Baron,—not altogether to the satisfaction of Mr. Harold Smith himself.", "그리하여 그도 레이디 파푸아에게 말을 걸고 남작을 두고 익살을 부렸는데, 해럴드 스미스 씨 본인에게는 그것이 온전히 만족스럽지는 않았다."]], [["For Mr. Harold Smith was in earnest and did not quite relish these jocundities.", "해럴드 스미스 씨는 진지했으므로 이런 희롱들이 그리 달갑지 않았다."], ["He had an idea that he could in about three months talk the British world into civilizing New Guinea, and that the world of Barsetshire would be made to go with him by one night’s efforts.", "그는 석 달쯤이면 영국 세상을 설득해서 뉴기니를 문명화하게 만들 수 있고, 바셋주의 세상은 하룻밤의 노력으로 자기 편에 서게 할 수 있다고 여겼다."], ["He did not understand why others should be less serious, and was inclined to resent somewhat stiffly the amenities of our friend Mark.", "그는 왜 남들이 자기보다 덜 진지해야 하는지를 이해하지 못했고, 우리 벗 마크의 상냥한 농담을 다소 딱딱하게 못마땅해하는 쪽으로 기울었다."]], [["“We must not keep the Baron waiting,”", "“남작을 기다리게 해서는 안 되겠지요,”"], ["said Mark, as they were preparing to start for Barchester.", "바체스터로 떠날 준비를 하면서 마크가 말했다."]], [["“I don’t know what you mean by the Baron, sir,”", "“남작이라는 말이 무슨 뜻인지 나는 모르겠소, 선생,”"], ["said Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스가 말했다."], ["“But perhaps the joke will be against you, when you are getting up into your pulpit to-morrow and sending the hat round among the clodhoppers of Chaldicotes.”", "“하지만 내일 당신이 설교단에 올라가 콜디코츠의 촌뜨기들 사이로 모금 모자를 돌릴 때에는, 아마 그 농담이 당신에게로 돌아갈 거요.”"]], [["“Those who live in glass houses shouldn’t throw stones; eh, Baron?”", "“유리 집에 사는 사람은 돌을 던지면 안 되지요. 그렇지 않아요, 남작?”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“Mr. Robarts’s sermon will be too near akin to your lecture to allow of his laughing.”", "“로바츠 씨의 설교는 당신 강연과 너무 가까운 친척이라, 그분도 웃을 처지가 못 될 텐데요.”"]], [["“If we can do nothing towards instructing the outer world till it’s done by the parsons,”", "“바깥 세상을 가르치는 일이 목사들 손으로 이루어질 때까지 우리가 아무것도 할 수 없다면,”"], ["said Harold Smith, “the outer world will have to wait a long time, I fear.”", "해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “바깥 세상은 아무래도 아주 오래 기다려야 할 거요.”"]], [["“Nobody can do anything of that kind short of a member of Parliament and a would-be minister,”", "“그런 일은 하원 의원이자 장관이 되려는 사람이 아니면 아무도 할 수 없지요,”"], ["whispered Mrs. Harold.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 속삭였다."]], [["And so they were all very pleasant together, in spite of a little fencing with edge-tools; and at three o’clock the cortége of carriages started for Barchester, that of the bishop, of course, leading the way.", "그리하여 날 선 도구로 조금씩 찌르고 막는 일이 있었음에도 그들은 다 함께 아주 유쾌했다. 그리고 세 시에 마차 행렬이 바체스터를 향해 출발했는데, 물론 주교의 마차가 앞장을 섰다."], ["His lordship, however, was not in it.", "그러나 주교 각하는 그 안에 없었다."]], [["“Mrs. Proudie, I’m sure you’ll let me go with you,”", "“프라우디 부인, 저를 함께 태워 주시겠지요,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, at the last moment, as she came down the big stone steps.", "커다란 돌계단을 내려오면서 마지막 순간에 던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“I want to hear the rest of that story about Mr. Slope.”", "“슬로프 씨에 관한 그 이야기의 남은 대목을 듣고 싶어서요.”"]], [["Now this upset everything.", "이 말이 모든 것을 뒤집어 놓았다."], ["The bishop was to have gone with his wife, Mrs. Smith, and Mark Robarts; and Mr.", "주교는 아내와 스미스 부인, 마크 로바츠와 함께 가기로 되어 있었고, 사워비 씨는 자기 사륜 경마차에 던스터블 양을 태울 수 있도록 일을 짜 놓았던 터였다."], ["Sowerby had so arranged matters that he could have accompanied Miss Dunstable in his phaeton.", "그러나 던스터블 양에게 무엇이든 안 된다고 하는 것은 아무도 꿈에조차 생각하지 못하는 일이었다."], ["But no one ever dreamed of denying Miss Dunstable anything.", "물론 마크가 자리를 내주었다."], ["Of course Mark gave way; but it ended in the bishop declaring that he had no special predilection for his own carriage, which he did in compliance with a glance from his wife’s eye.", "그러나 결국에는 주교가 자기 마차에 특별한 애착은 없노라고 선언하는 데까지 이르렀는데, 그가 그렇게 한 것은 아내의 눈에서 날아온 눈짓에 따른 것이었다."], ["Then other changes of course followed, and, at last, Mr. Sowerby and Harold Smith were the joint occupants of the phaeton.", "그러자 물론 다른 변경들이 잇따랐고, 마침내 사워비 씨와 해럴드 스미스가 그 경마차의 공동 탑승자가 되었다."]], [["The poor lecturer, as he seated himself, made some remark such as those he had been making for the last two days—for out of a full heart the mouth speaketh.", "가엾은 강연자는 자리에 앉으면서 지난 이틀 동안 해 오던 것과 같은 말을 몇 마디 했다. 마음이 가득하면 입이 말하는 법이니까."], ["But he spoke to an impatient listener.", "그러나 그가 말을 건 상대는 참을성이 없는 청자였다."], ["“D—— the South Sea islanders,”", "“남태평양 섬사람들 따위 빌어먹을,”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."], ["“You’ll have it all your own way in a few minutes, like a bull in a china-shop; but for Heaven’s sake let us have a little peace till that time comes.” It appeared that Mr.", "“몇 분 뒤면 도자기 가게에 들어간 황소처럼 다 자네 마음대로 하게 될 텐데, 제발 그때까지는 좀 조용히 있게 해 주게.”"], ["Sowerby’s little plan of having Miss Dunstable for his companion was not quite insignificant; and, indeed, it may be said that but few of his little plans were so.", "던스터블 양을 동행으로 삼으려던 사워비 씨의 조그만 계획이 그리 대수롭지 않은 것은 아니었던 모양이다. 아니, 그의 조그만 계획들 가운데 대수롭지 않은 것은 거의 없었다고 해도 좋다."], ["At the present moment he flung himself back in the carriage and prepared for sleep.", "지금 이 순간 그는 마차에 몸을 던져 기대고 잠들 준비를 했다."], ["He could further no plan of his by a tête-à-tête conversation with his brother-in-law.", "매부와 단둘이 마주 앉아 이야기해서는 자기 계획을 하나도 밀고 나갈 수 없었다."]], [["And then Mrs. Proudie began her story about Mr.", "그러고서 프라우디 부인이 슬로프 씨에 관한 이야기를 시작했다."], ["Slope, or rather recommenced it.", "아니, 다시 이어 갔다. 그녀는 이 신사에 관하여 말하기를 아주 좋아했다."], ["She was very fond of talking about this gentleman, who had once been her pet chaplain but was now her bitterest foe; and in telling the story, she had sometimes to whisper to Miss Dunstable, for there were one or two fie-fie little anecdotes about a married lady, not altogether fit for young Mr.", "한때는 그녀가 아끼던 전속 사제였으나 이제는 가장 지독한 원수가 된 사람이었다. 이야기를 하는 동안 그녀는 이따금 던스터블 양에게 귓속말을 해야 했다."], ["Robarts’s ears. But Mrs.", "어느 유부녀에 관한, 어머 어머 하게 되는 조그만 일화가 한둘 있었는데, 젊은 로바츠 씨의 귀에 온전히 어울리는 것은 아니었기 때문이다."], ["Harold Smith insisted on having them out loud, and Miss Dunstable would gratify that lady in spite of Mrs. Proudie’s winks.", "그러나 해럴드 스미스 부인은 그것을 큰 소리로 하라고 우겼고, 던스터블 양은 프라우디 부인이 눈짓을 하는데도 그 부인의 뜻을 채워 주었다."]], [["“What, kissing her hand, and he a clergyman!”", "“뭐라고요, 그 부인의 손에 입을 맞추었다고요, 성직자가?”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“I did not think they ever did such things, Mr. Robarts.”", "“성직자들이 그런 짓을 하는 줄은 몰랐어요, 로바츠 씨.”"]], [["“Still waters run deepest,”", "“고요한 물이 가장 깊지요,”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Hush-h-h,”", "“쉬이이,”"], ["looked, rather than spoke, Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인은 말했다기보다 그런 얼굴을 지었다."], ["“The grief of spirit which that bad man caused me nearly broke my heart, and all the while, you know, he was courting—”", "“그 못된 사람이 내 마음에 안긴 괴로움은 하마터면 내 가슴을 무너뜨릴 지경이었소. 그런데 그 내내, 알겠소, 그이가 구애하고 있던 상대는—”"], ["and then Mrs. Proudie whispered a name.", "그러고서 프라우디 부인은 어떤 이름을 속삭였다."]], [["“What, the dean’s wife!”", "“뭐라고요, 수석 사제 부인이라니!”"], ["shouted Miss Dunstable, in a voice which made the coachman of the next carriage give a chuck to his horses as he overheard her.", "던스터블 양이 어찌나 큰 소리로 외쳤던지, 그 말을 얻어들은 뒷마차의 마부가 말들에게 혀를 차 댔다."]], [["“The archdeacon’s sister-in-law!”", "“부주교의 처제라니!”"], ["screamed Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 소리를 질렀다."]], [["“What might he not have attempted next?”", "“그다음에는 무엇을 노렸을지 모를 일이네요,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["“She wasn’t the dean’s wife then, you know,”", "“그때는 그 사람이 수석 사제 부인이 아니었소, 알겠소,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie, explaining.", "프라우디 부인이 설명하듯 말했다."]], [["“Well, you’ve a gay set in the chapter, I must say,”", "“글쎄, 그 성당 참사회에는 참 흥겨운 패가 모였다고 해야겠네요,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“You ought to make one of them in Barchester, Mr. Robarts.”", "“로바츠 씨도 바체스터에서 그 한 사람이 되셔야겠어요.”"]], [["“Only perhaps Mrs. Robarts might not like it,”", "“다만 로바츠 부인이 좋아하지 않을지도 모르지요,”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“And then the schemes which he tried on with the bishop!”", "“게다가 그이가 주교에게 걸었던 그 계략들이란!”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["“It’s all fair in love and war, you know,”", "“사랑과 전쟁에서는 무엇이든 정당하다잖아요,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["“But he little knew whom he had to deal with when he began that,”", "“하지만 그 일을 시작할 때 자기가 누구를 상대해야 하는지는 조금도 몰랐소,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["“The bishop was too many for him,”", "“주교님이 그이에게는 너무 벅찬 상대였겠지요,”"], ["suggested Mrs. Harold Smith, very maliciously.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 아주 짓궂게 말을 보탰다."]], [["“If the bishop was not, somebody else was; and he was obliged to leave Barchester in utter disgrace.", "“주교가 아니었다면 다른 누군가가 그러했소. 그이는 온전한 망신을 지고 바체스터를 떠나야 했지."], ["He has since married the wife of some tallow-chandler.”", "그 뒤로는 어느 양초 장수의 아내와 혼인했다오.”"]], [["“The wife!”", "“아내라고요!”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“What a man!”", "“참 대단한 사람이네요!”"]], [["“Widow, I mean; but it’s all one to him.”", "“과부 말이오. 하지만 그이에게는 그게 그거지.”"]], [["“The gentleman was clearly born when Venus was in the ascendant,” said Mrs.", "“그 신사는 분명 금성이 하늘에 오르는 때에 태어났군요,”"], ["Smith. “You clergymen usually are, I believe, Mr.", "스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["Robarts.” So that Mrs.", "“성직자들은 대개 그렇다고 하더군요, 로바츠 씨.”"], ["Proudie’s carriage was by no means the dullest as they drove into Barchester that day; and by degrees our friend Mark became accustomed to his companions, and before they reached the palace he acknowledged to himself that Miss Dunstable was very good fun.", "그리하여 그날 바체스터로 들어가는 길에 프라우디 부인의 마차는 결코 가장 심심한 마차가 아니었다. 그리고 우리 벗 마크는 차츰 동행들에게 익숙해져서, 주교관에 이르기 전에는 던스터블 양이 아주 재미있는 사람이라고 스스로 인정하고 있었다."]], [["We cannot linger over the bishop’s dinner, though it was very good of its kind; and as Mr.", "주교의 만찬에 오래 머물러 있을 수는 없다. 물론 그 나름으로는 아주 훌륭한 만찬이었다."], ["Sowerby contrived to sit next to Miss Dunstable, thereby overturning a little scheme made by Mr. Supplehouse, he again shone forth in unclouded good humour.", "사워비 씨는 던스터블 양 옆자리에 앉는 데 성공하여 서플하우스 씨가 세워 둔 조그만 계획을 뒤집어 놓았고, 그 덕에 다시금 구름 없이 좋은 기분으로 빛났다."], ["But Mr. Harold Smith became impatient immediately on the withdrawal of the cloth.", "그러나 해럴드 스미스 씨는 식탁보가 걷히자마자 곧바로 안절부절못하기 시작했다."], ["The lecture was to begin at seven, and according to his watch that hour had already come.", "강연은 일곱 시에 시작해야 했고, 그의 시계로는 그 시각이 이미 와 있었다."], ["He declared that Sowerby and Supplehouse were endeavouring to delay matters in order that the Barchesterians might become vexed and impatient; and so the bishop was not allowed to exercise his hospitality in true episcopal fashion.", "그는 사워비와 서플하우스가 바체스터 사람들을 애가 타고 조바심 나게 만들려고 일을 일부러 늦추고 있다고 단언했다. 그래서 주교는 참으로 주교다운 방식으로 자기 환대를 베풀 기회를 얻지 못했다."]], [["“You forget, Sowerby,”", "“사워비, 자네는 잊고 있군,”"], ["said Supplehouse, “that the world here for the last fortnight has been looking forward to nothing else.”", "서플하우스가 말했다. “여기 세상이 지난 두 주 동안 그것 말고는 아무것도 기다리지 않았다는 것을.”"]], [["“The world shall be gratified at once,” said Mrs.", "“그럼 세상을 당장 만족시켜 드려야지요,”"], ["Harold, obeying a little nod from Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인의 조그만 고갯짓에 따르며 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["“Come, my dear,”", "“가요, 그대,”"], ["and she took hold of Miss Dunstable’s arm, “don’t let us keep Barchester waiting.", "그러면서 그녀는 던스터블 양의 팔을 붙잡았다. “바체스터를 기다리게 하지 맙시다."], ["We shall be ready in a quarter-of-an-hour, shall we not, Mrs.", "십오 분이면 준비가 되겠지요, 프라우디 부인?”"], ["Proudie?” and so they sailed off.", "그러고서 그들은 미끄러져 나갔다."]], [["“And we shall have time for one glass of claret,”", "“그러면 우리는 클라레 한 잔 할 시간이 있겠구려,”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["“There; that’s seven by the cathedral,”", "“저기, 대성당 종이 일곱 시를 치는군,”"], ["said Harold Smith, jumping up from his chair as he heard the clock.", "시계 소리를 듣고 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 해럴드 스미스가 말했다."], ["“If the people have come it would not be right in me to keep them waiting, and I shall go.”", "“사람들이 왔다면 내가 그들을 기다리게 하는 것은 옳지 않소. 나는 가겠소.”"]], [["“Just one glass of claret, Mr. Smith, and we’ll be off,”", "“클라레 딱 한 잔만 하고 곧 떠납시다, 스미스 씨,”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["“Those women will keep me an hour,”", "“저 여자들이 나를 한 시간은 붙잡아 둘 거요,”"], ["said Harold, filling his glass, and drinking it standing.", "해럴드가 잔을 채우고 선 채로 마시면서 말했다."], ["“They do it on purpose.”", "“일부러 그러는 거지.”"], ["He was thinking of his wife, but it seemed to the bishop as though his guest were actually speaking of Mrs. Proudie.", "그는 자기 아내를 생각하고 있었으나, 주교에게는 손님이 실은 프라우디 부인을 두고 말하는 것처럼 들렸다."]], [["It was rather late when they all found themselves in the big room of the Mechanics’ Institute; but I do not know whether this on the whole did them any harm.", "그들이 모두 직공 학원의 큰 방에 자리를 잡은 것은 꽤 늦은 시각이었다. 그러나 전체로 보아 이것이 그들에게 무슨 해가 되었는지는 나도 모르겠다."], ["Most of Mr. Smith’s hearers, excepting the party from the palace, were Barchester tradesmen with their wives and families; and they waited, not impatiently, for the big people.", "주교관에서 온 일행을 빼면 스미스 씨의 청중은 대부분 바체스터의 상인들과 그 아내와 아이들이었고, 그들은 조바심 내지 않고 높은 분들을 기다렸다."], ["And then the lecture was gratis, a fact which is always borne in mind by an Englishman when he comes to reckon up and calculate the way in which he is treated.", "게다가 강연은 무료였다. 영국 사람은 자기가 어떤 대접을 받고 있는지 셈하고 따질 때에 언제나 이 사실을 마음에 새겨 둔다."], ["When he pays his money, then he takes his choice; he may be impatient or not as he likes.", "돈을 낼 때에는 자기 몫을 고르게 된다. 그때는 마음대로 조바심을 내도 좋고 내지 않아도 좋다."], ["His sense of justice teaches him so much, and in accordance with that sense he usually acts.", "그의 정의감이 그에게 그만큼을 가르쳐 주고, 그는 대개 그 감각에 따라 행동한다."]], [["So the people on the benches rose graciously when the palace party entered the room.", "그리하여 주교관 일행이 방에 들어서자 긴 의자에 앉아 있던 사람들이 정중하게 일어섰다."], ["Seats for them had been kept in the front.", "그들의 자리는 앞줄에 마련되어 있었다."], ["There were three arm-chairs, which were filled, after some little hesitation, by the bishop, Mrs.", "팔걸이의자가 셋 있었는데, 얼마간 망설임이 있은 뒤 주교와 프라우디 부인과 던스터블 양이 그 자리를 채웠다."], ["Proudie, and Miss Dunstable—Mrs.", "스미스 부인은 그중 하나에 앉기를 딱 잘라 사양했다."], ["Smith positively declining to take one of them; though, as she admitted, her rank as Lady Papua of the islands did give her some claim. And this remark, as it was made quite out loud, reached Mr.", "물론 그녀 자신이 인정한 대로, 그 섬들의 레이디 파푸아라는 지위는 그녀에게 얼마간 권리를 주기는 했다."], ["Smith’s ears as he stood behind a little table on a small raised dais, holding his white kid gloves; and it annoyed him and rather put him out.", "그리고 이 말이 아주 큰 소리로 나왔던 까닭에, 조그맣게 높인 단 위의 조그만 탁자 뒤에 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 들고 서 있던 스미스 씨의 귀에까지 닿았다. 그것이 그를 짜증나게 하고 적잖이 흐트러뜨렸다."], ["He did not like that joke about Lady Papua.", "그는 레이디 파푸아에 관한 그 농담이 마음에 들지 않았다."]], [["And then the others of the party sat upon a front bench covered with red cloth.", "그러고서 일행의 나머지 사람들은 붉은 천을 덮은 앞줄 긴 의자에 앉았다."], ["“We shall find this very hard and very narrow about the second hour,” said Mr.", "“두 시간째쯤 되면 이게 아주 딱딱하고 아주 좁게 느껴질 걸세,”"], ["Sowerby, and Mr. Smith on his dais again overheard the words, and dashed his gloves down to the table.", "사워비 씨가 말했고, 단 위의 스미스 씨는 또 그 말을 얻어듣고 장갑을 탁자에 내던졌다."], ["He felt that all the room would hear it.", "방 안 사람들이 다 그 말을 들었을 것 같았다."]], [["And there were one or two gentlemen on the second seat who shook hands with some of our party.", "그리고 둘째 줄 자리에는 우리 일행의 몇몇과 악수를 나눈 신사가 한둘 있었다."], ["There was Mr. Thorne of Ullathorne, a good-natured old bachelor, whose residence was near enough to Barchester to allow of his coming in without much personal inconvenience; and next to him was Mr.", "울라손의 손 씨가 있었는데, 성품 좋은 늙은 독신 신사로, 그의 저택은 바체스터에서 가까워 몸소 큰 불편을 겪지 않고도 들어올 수 있었다."], ["Harding, an old clergyman of the chapter, with whom Mrs.", "그 옆에는 성당 참사회의 노(老) 성직자 하딩 씨가 있었다."], ["Proudie shook hands very graciously, making way for him to seat himself close behind her if he would so please. But Mr.", "프라우디 부인은 그와 아주 정중하게 악수를 하고, 그가 원한다면 자기 바로 뒤에 앉을 수 있도록 자리를 비켜 주었다."], ["Harding did not so please. Having paid his respects to the bishop he returned quietly to the side of his old friend Mr.", "그러나 하딩 씨는 그러기를 원하지 않았다."], ["Thorne, thereby angering Mrs. Proudie, as might easily be seen by her face.", "주교에게 인사를 드린 뒤 그는 조용히 오랜 벗 손 씨의 옆으로 돌아갔고, 그로써 프라우디 부인을 노엽게 했는데, 그것은 그녀의 얼굴만 보아도 쉽게 알 수 있었다."], ["And Mr. Chadwick also was there, the episcopal man of business for the diocese; but he also adhered to the two gentlemen above named.", "그리고 채드윅 씨도 그 자리에 있었으니 교구의 주교청 사무를 맡아보는 사람이었으나, 그 역시 앞에 말한 두 신사에게 붙어 있었다."]], [["And now that the bishop and the ladies had taken their places, Mr. Harold Smith relifted his gloves and again laid them down, hummed three times distinctly, and then began.", "이제 주교와 숙녀들이 자리를 잡았으므로, 해럴드 스미스 씨는 장갑을 다시 들었다가 또 내려놓고, 또렷하게 세 번 흠, 하고 목청을 가다듬은 뒤 시작했다."]], [["“It was,” he said, “the most peculiar characteristic of the present era in the British islands that those who were high placed before the world in rank, wealth, and education were willing to come forward and give their time and knowledge without fee or reward, for the advantage and amelioration of those who did not stand so high in the social scale.”", "“오늘날 영국 제도(諸島)에서 이 시대가 지닌 가장 특이한 성격은,”"], ["And then he paused for a moment, during which Mrs.", "그가 말했다."], ["Smith remarked to Miss Dunstable that that was pretty well for a beginning; and Miss Dunstable replied, “that as for herself she felt very grateful to rank, wealth, and education.”", "“지위와 부와 교육에서 세상 앞에 높이 놓인 이들이, 사회의 계단에서 그만큼 높이 서지 못한 이들의 이익과 향상을 위하여, 보수도 대가도 없이 스스로 나서서 자기 시간과 지식을 내어 주려 한다는 점이었소.” 그러고서 그는 잠시 말을 멈추었는데, 그 사이에 스미스 부인은 시작치고는 꽤 괜찮다고 던스터블 양에게 말했고, 던스터블 양은 “저로서는 지위와 부와 교육에 아주 감사하는 마음이 든답니다”"], ["Mr. Sowerby winked to Mr.", "하고 대답했다."], ["Supplehouse, who opened his eyes very wide and shrugged his shoulders.", "사워비 씨가 서플하우스 씨에게 눈을 찡긋했고, 서플하우스 씨는 눈을 아주 크게 뜨고는 어깨를 으쓱했다."], ["But the Barchesterians took it all in good part and gave the lecturer the applause of their hands and feet.", "그러나 바체스터 사람들은 그것을 모두 좋게 받아들이고 강연자에게 손과 발의 박수를 보냈다."]], [["And then, well pleased, he recommenced—“I do not make these remarks with reference to myself—”", "그러고서 그는 흡족해하며 다시 말을 이었다. “나는 이런 말을 나 자신을 두고 하는 것이 아니오—”"]], [["“I hope he’s not going to be modest,”", "“이제 와서 겸손을 부리려는 건 아니었으면 좋겠네요,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["“It will be quite new if he is,”", "“그렇다면 아주 새로운 일이지요,”"], ["replied Mrs. Smith.", "스미스 부인이 대답했다."]], [["“—so much as to many noble and talented lords and members of the Lower House who have lately from time to time devoted themselves to this good work.”", "“—그보다는 근래에 이따금 이 선한 일에 몸을 바쳐 온 고귀하고 재능 있는 여러 귀족과 하원 의원들을 두고 하는 말이오.”"], ["And then he went through a long list of peers and members of Parliament, beginning, of course, with Lord Boanerges, and ending with Mr.", "그러고서 그는 귀족과 국회의원들의 긴 명단을 훑어 나갔는데, 물론 보아네르게스 경으로 시작해서 그린 워커 씨로 끝을 맺었다."], ["Green Walker, a young gentleman who had lately been returned by his uncle’s interest for the borough of Crewe Junction, and had immediately made his entrance into public life by giving a lecture on the grammarians of the Latin language as exemplified at Eton school.", "그린 워커 씨는 근래에 숙부의 힘으로 크루 정션 선거구에서 당선된 젊은 신사로, 이튼 학교에서 본을 보이는 대로 라틴어 문법학자들에 관한 강연을 한 것으로 곧바로 공적 생활에 발을 들인 사람이었다."]], [["“On the present occasion,” Mr.", "“오늘 이 자리에서,”"], ["Smith continued, “our object is to learn something as to those grand and magnificent islands which lie far away, beyond the Indies, in the Southern Ocean; the lands of which produce rich spices and glorious fruits, and whose seas are imbedded with pearls and corals,—Papua and the Philippines, Borneo and the Moluccas.", "스미스 씨가 말을 이었다. “우리의 목적은 저 멀리, 인도 제도 너머 남쪽 대양에 놓인 그 크고 웅장한 섬들에 관하여 무엇인가를 배우는 것이오."], ["My friends, you are familiar with your maps, and you know the track which the equator makes for itself through those distant oceans.”", "그 땅은 풍성한 향료와 영광스러운 열매를 내고, 그 바다에는 진주와 산호가 박혀 있소. 파푸아와 필리핀, 보르네오와 몰루카 제도가 그것이오."], ["And then many heads were turned down, and there was a rustle of leaves; for not a few of those “who stood not so high in the social scale”", "벗들이여, 여러분은 지도에 익숙하고, 적도가 그 먼 바다들을 지나며 스스로 그려 내는 길을 알고 있소.” 그러자 많은 머리가 아래로 숙여지고 종이 넘기는 소리가 부스럭거렸다."], ["had brought their maps with them, and refreshed their memories as to the whereabouts of these wondrous islands.", "“사회의 계단에서 그만큼 높이 서지 못한” 이들 가운데 적잖은 수가 지도를 가지고 와서, 이 놀라운 섬들이 어디쯤 있는지 기억을 되살렸기 때문이다."]], [["And then Mr. Smith also, with a map in his hand, and pointing occasionally to another large map which hung against the wall, went into the geography of the matter.", "그러고서 스미스 씨도 손에 지도를 들고, 이따금 벽에 걸린 다른 큰 지도를 가리키며 이 문제의 지리로 들어갔다."], ["“We might have found that out from our atlases, I think, without coming all the way to Barchester,”", "“그런 건 바체스터까지 오지 않고도 우리 지도책에서 찾아낼 수 있었을 텐데요,” 저 매정한 조력자 해럴드 스미스 부인이 아주 잔인하게 말했다."], ["said that unsympathizing helpmate, Mrs.", "게다가 몹시 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 말이었다."], ["Harold, very cruelly—most illogically too, for there be so many things which we could find out ourselves by search, but which we never do find out unless they be specially told us; and why should not the latitude and longitude of Labuan be one—or rather two of these things?", "우리가 찾아보면 스스로 알아낼 수 있는 것이 그토록 많으나, 누가 따로 말해 주지 않으면 결코 알아내지 않는 것도 그만큼 많으니 말이다. 그러니 라부안의 위도와 경도가 그런 것들 가운데 하나, 아니 오히려 둘이 되지 못할 이유가 무엇인가?"]], [["And then, when he had duly marked the path of the line through Borneo, Celebes, and Gilolo, through the Macassar strait and the Molucca passage, Mr. Harold Smith rose to a higher flight.", "그러고서 보르네오와 셀레베스와 길롤로를 지나, 마카사르 해협과 몰루카 항로를 지나는 그 선의 길을 빠짐없이 짚어 보인 뒤, 해럴드 스미스 씨는 더 높은 비상으로 올라갔다."], ["“But what,”", "“그러나,”"], ["said he, “avails all that God can give to man, unless man will open his hand to receive the gift?", "그가 말했다. “사람이 손을 펴서 그 선물을 받으려 하지 않는다면, 하느님이 사람에게 주실 수 있는 그 모든 것이 무슨 소용이오?"], ["And what is this opening of the hand but the process of civilization—yes, my friends, the process of civilization?", "그리고 이 손을 펴는 일이 문명화의 과정이 아니면 무엇이겠소. 그렇소, 벗들이여, 문명화의 과정이오."], ["These South Sea islanders have all that a kind Providence can bestow on them; but that all is as nothing without education.", "저 남태평양 섬사람들은 자비로운 섭리가 그들에게 내려 줄 수 있는 모든 것을 가지고 있소. 그러나 그 모든 것은 교육이 없으면 아무것도 아니오."], ["That education and that civilization it is for you to bestow upon them—yes, my friends, for you; for you, citizens of Barchester as you are.”", "그 교육과 그 문명을 그들에게 내려 줄 사람은 바로 여러분이오. 그렇소, 벗들이여, 여러분이오."], ["And then he paused again, in order that the feet and hands might go to work.", "바로 여러분, 바체스터의 시민인 여러분 말이오.” 그러고서 그는 발과 손이 일하러 나설 수 있도록 다시 말을 멈추었다."], ["The feet and hands did go to work, during which Mr. Smith took a slight drink of water.", "발과 손은 과연 일하러 나섰고, 그동안 스미스 씨는 물을 조금 마셨다."]], [["He was now quite in his element and had got into the proper way of punching the table with his fists. A few words dropping from Mr.", "이제 그는 완전히 제 물을 만났고, 주먹으로 탁자를 두드리는 알맞은 방식도 찾아냈다."], ["Sowerby did now and again find their way to his ears, but the sound of his own voice had brought with it the accustomed charm, and he ran on from platitude to truism, and from truism back to platitude, with an eloquence that was charming to himself.", "사워비 씨에게서 흘러나온 몇 마디가 이따금 그의 귀에까지 길을 찾아들기는 했다. 그러나 자기 목소리의 울림이 익숙한 매력을 함께 데려왔으므로, 그는 진부한 소리에서 뻔한 소리로, 뻔한 소리에서 다시 진부한 소리로, 스스로에게는 매혹적인 웅변을 타고 달려 나갔다."]], [["“Civilization,”", "“문명이여,”"], ["he exclaimed, lifting up his eyes and hands to the ceiling.", "그가 눈과 손을 천장으로 들어 올리며 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, civilization—”", "“오, 문명이여—”"]], [["“There will not be a chance for us now for the next hour and a half,”", "“이제 앞으로 한 시간 반은 우리에게 기회가 없겠군,”"], ["said Mr. Supplehouse, groaning.", "서플하우스 씨가 신음하며 말했다."]], [["Harold Smith cast one eye down at him, but it immediately flew back to the ceiling.", "해럴드 스미스가 한쪽 눈을 그에게로 내리떴으나, 그 눈은 곧바로 천장으로 되돌아갔다."]], [["“Oh, civilization!", "“오, 문명이여!"], ["thou that ennoblest mankind and makest him equal to the gods, what is like unto thee?”", "인류를 고귀하게 하고 사람을 신들과 같게 만드는 너여, 무엇이 너와 같으랴?”"], ["Here Mrs. Proudie showed evident signs of disapprobation, which no doubt would have been shared by the bishop, had not that worthy prelate been asleep.", "이 대목에서 프라우디 부인은 뚜렷한 못마땅함의 표시를 드러냈고, 그 훌륭한 고위 성직자가 잠들어 있지 않았다면 주교도 틀림없이 그 마음을 같이했을 것이다."], ["But Mr. Smith continued unobservant; or, at any rate, regardless.", "그러나 스미스 씨는 알아차리지 못한 채, 아니 적어도 아랑곳하지 않은 채 계속했다."]], [["“What is like unto thee?", "“무엇이 너와 같으랴?"], ["Thou art the irrigating stream which makest fertile the barren plain.", "너는 메마른 들판을 기름지게 하는 물길이다."], ["Till thou comest all is dark and dreary; but at thy advent the noontide sun shines out, the earth gives forth her increase; the deep bowels of the rocks render up their tribute.", "네가 오기까지는 모든 것이 어둡고 쓸쓸하다. 그러나 네가 이르면 한낮의 해가 비쳐 나오고 땅은 그 소출을 내어 주며, 바위의 깊은 창자가 그 조공을 바친다."], ["Forms which were dull and hideous become endowed with grace and beauty, and vegetable existence rises to the scale of celestial life.", "무디고 흉하던 형상들이 우아함과 아름다움을 입고, 식물의 존재가 천상의 생명의 반열로 올라선다."], ["Then, too, Genius appears clad in a panoply of translucent armour, grasping in his hand the whole terrestrial surface, and making every rood of earth subservient to his purposes;—Genius, the child of civilization, the mother of the Arts!”", "그때 또한 천재가 반투명한 갑주를 온몸에 두르고 나타나, 그 손에 온 땅의 표면을 잡아쥐고 땅 한 뙈기 한 뙈기를 자기 목적에 복종시킨다. 천재, 문명의 아이요 예술의 어머니여!”"]], [["The last little bit, taken from the Pedigree of Progress, had a great success, and all Barchester went to work with its hands and feet;—all Barchester, except that ill-natured aristocratic front-row together with the three arm-chairs at the corner of it.", "「진보의 계보」에서 따온 마지막 조그만 대목은 큰 성공을 거두어, 바체스터 전체가 손과 발로 일하러 나섰다. 심사 고약한 저 귀족적 앞줄과 그 끝에 놓인 팔걸이의자 셋만은 빼고 말이다."], ["The aristocratic front-row felt itself to be too intimate with civilization to care much about it; and the three arm-chairs, or rather that special one which contained Mrs.", "그 귀족적 앞줄은 스스로가 문명과 너무 가까운 사이라서 문명 따위에 크게 마음을 쓸 것이 없다고 느꼈다."], ["Proudie, considered that there was a certain heathenness, a pagan sentimentality almost amounting to infidelity, contained in the lecturer’s remarks, with which she, a pillar of the Church, could not put up, seated as she was now in public conclave.", "그리고 팔걸이의자 셋은, 아니 그중에서도 프라우디 부인을 담고 있던 그 특별한 하나는, 강연자의 말 속에 어떤 이교스러움, 거의 불신앙에 이르는 이교도적 감상이 들어 있다고 여겼으니, 교회의 기둥인 그녀로서는 더구나 이렇게 공개된 회중 가운데 앉은 처지로서는 참아 넘길 수 없는 것이었다."]], [["“It is to civilization that we must look,”", "“우리가 바라보아야 할 것은 문명이오,” 강연자라면 잘 아는 방식으로 시에서 산문으로 내려오면서, 그럼으로써 둘 모두의 값을 보여 주면서 해럴드 스미스 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["continued Mr.", "“이 섬들의 어떠한 물질적 진보를 위해서든 말이오."], ["Harold Smith, descending from poetry to prose as a lecturer well knows how, and thereby showing the value of both—“for any material progress in these islands; and—”", "그리고—”"]], [["“And to Christianity,”", "“그리고 기독교요,”"], ["shouted Mrs.", "프라우디 부인이 외쳤고, 모인 사람들은 크게 놀랐으며 주교는 완전히 깨어났다."], ["Proudie, to the great amazement of the assembled people and to the thorough wakening of the bishop, who, jumping up in his chair at the sound of the well-known voice, exclaimed, “Certainly, certainly.”", "주교는 익숙한 그 목소리에 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 “물론이오, 물론이오” 하고 소리쳤다."]], [["“Hear, hear, hear,”", "“옳소, 옳소, 옳소,”"], ["said those on the benches who particularly belonged to Mrs. Proudie’s school of divinity in the city, and among the voices was distinctly heard that of a new verger in whose behalf she had greatly interested herself.", "이 도시에서 특히 프라우디 부인의 신학 학파에 속한 사람들이 긴 의자에서 말했고, 그 목소리들 가운데는 그녀가 몸소 크게 애를 써 준 새 성당 관리인의 목소리가 또렷이 들렸다."]], [["“Oh, yes, Christianity of course,”", "“아, 그렇소, 물론 기독교요,”"], ["said Harold Smith, upon whom the interruption did not seem to operate favourably.", "해럴드 스미스가 말했으나, 그 끼어듦이 그에게 좋게 작용한 것 같지는 않았다."]], [["“Christianity and Sabbath-day observance,” exclaimed Mrs.", "“기독교와 안식일 준수요,”"], ["Proudie, who, now that she had obtained the ear of the public, seemed well inclined to keep it.", "프라우디 부인이 외쳤다. 이제 대중의 귀를 얻은 그녀는 그것을 놓지 않으려는 기색이 뚜렷했다."], ["“Let us never forget that these islanders can never prosper unless they keep the Sabbath holy.”", "“저 섬사람들은 안식일을 거룩하게 지키지 않고서는 결코 번영할 수 없다는 것을 결코 잊지 맙시다.”"]], [["Poor Mr. Smith, having been so rudely dragged from his high horse, was never able to mount it again, and completed the lecture in a manner not at all comfortable to himself.", "가엾은 스미스 씨는 자기 높은 말에서 그렇게 무례하게 끌어내려진 뒤 다시는 그 위에 올라앉을 수 없었고, 자신에게는 조금도 편안하지 않은 방식으로 강연을 마쳤다."], ["He had there, on the table before him, a huge bundle of statistics with which he had meant to convince the reason of his hearers after he had taken full possession of their feelings.", "그의 앞 탁자에는 통계 뭉치가 커다랗게 놓여 있었는데, 청중의 감정을 온전히 사로잡은 다음에 그것으로 그들의 이성을 설득할 작정이었다."], ["But they fell very dull and flat.", "그러나 그 통계들은 아주 지루하고 맥없이 떨어졌다."], ["And at the moment when he was interrupted he was about to explain that that material progress to which he had alluded could not be attained without money; and that it behoved them, the people of Barchester before him, to come forward with their purses like men and brothers.", "그리고 말이 끊긴 그 순간에 그는, 자기가 언급한 그 물질적 진보는 돈 없이는 이룰 수 없으며, 그러므로 자기 앞에 있는 바체스터 사람들이 남자답게 형제답게 지갑을 들고 나서야 마땅하다고 설명하려는 참이었다. 그것도 시도해 보기는 했다."], ["He did also attempt this; but from the moment of that fatal onslaught from the arm-chair, it was clear to him and to every one else, that Mrs. Proudie was now the hero of the hour.", "그러나 팔걸이의자에서 날아온 그 치명적인 습격의 순간부터, 이제 이 시간의 주인공은 프라우디 부인이라는 것이 그에게도 다른 모든 사람에게도 분명했다."], ["His time had gone by, and the people of Barchester did not care a straw for his appeal.", "그의 때는 지나갔고, 바체스터 사람들은 그의 호소에 지푸라기만큼도 마음을 쓰지 않았다."]], [["From these causes the lecture was over full twenty minutes earlier than any one had expected, to the great delight of Messrs.", "이런 까닭으로 강연은 누구도 예상하지 못한 만큼, 꼬박 이십 분이나 일찍 끝났다."], ["Sowerby and Supplehouse, who, on that evening, moved and carried a vote of thanks to Mrs.", "사워비 씨와 서플하우스 씨는 크게 기뻐하여 그날 저녁 프라우디 부인에게 감사를 표하는 결의를 발의하고 통과시켰다."], ["Proudie. For they had gay doings yet before they went to their beds.", "그들에게는 잠자리에 들기 전에 아직 흥겨운 일이 남아 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Robarts, here one moment,” Mr.", "“로바츠, 잠깐 이리 오세요,”"], ["Sowerby said, as they were standing at the door of the Mechanics’ Institute.", "직공 학원 문간에 서 있을 때 사워비 씨가 말했다."], ["“Don’t you go off with Mr. and Mrs.", "“주교 씨와 주교 부인을 따라가 버리지 마세요."], ["Bishop. We are going to have a little supper at the Dragon of Wantly, and after what we have gone through upon my word we want it.", "우리는 원틀리의 용에서 조그맣게 밤참을 하려는데, 우리가 겪은 일을 생각하면 정말이지 그게 꼭 필요하지 않겠어요."], ["You can tell one of the palace servants to let you in.”", "주교관 하인 아무에게나 문을 열어 달라고 일러 두면 되지요.”"]], [["Mark considered the proposal wistfully.", "마크는 그 제안을 아쉬운 마음으로 곱씹었다. 용기가 있었다면 그 밤참 자리에 기꺼이 끼었을 것이다."], ["He would fain have joined the supper-party had he dared; but he, like many others of his cloth, had the fear of Mrs. Proudie before his eyes.", "그러나 그도 같은 옷을 입은 다른 많은 이들처럼 눈앞에 프라우디 부인에 대한 두려움을 두고 있었다."]], [["And a very merry supper they had; but poor Mr.", "그리고 그들은 아주 흥겨운 밤참을 했다."], ["Harold Smith was not the merriest of the party.", "그러나 가엾은 해럴드 스미스 씨는 그 자리에서 가장 흥겨운 사람이 아니었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII. SUNDAY MORNING.", "# 제7장. 일요일 아침"]], [["It was, perhaps, quite as well on the whole for Mark Robarts, that he did not go to that supper party.", "대체로 보아 마크 로바츠가 그 밤참 자리에 가지 않은 것은 아마도 잘된 일이었다."], ["It was eleven o’clock before they sat down, and nearly two before the gentlemen were in bed.", "그들이 자리에 앉은 것이 열한 시였고, 신사들이 잠자리에 든 것은 두 시가 가까워서였다."], ["It must be remembered that he had to preach, on the coming Sunday morning, a charity sermon on behalf of a mission to Mr.", "기억해 두어야 할 것은, 그가 다가오는 일요일 아침에 해럴드 스미스 씨의 섬사람들에게 보내는 선교 사업을 위하여 자선 설교를 해야 했다는 사실이다."], ["Harold Smith’s islanders; and, to tell the truth, it was a task for which he had now very little inclination.", "그리고 사실을 말하자면, 그것은 이제 그에게 마음이 거의 가지 않는 일거리였다."]], [["When first invited to do this, he had regarded the task seriously enough, as he always did regard such work, and he completed his sermon for the occasion before he left Framley; but, since that, an air of ridicule had been thrown over the whole affair, in which he had joined without much thinking of his own sermon, and this made him now heartily wish that he could choose a discourse upon any other subject.", "처음 이 일을 청탁받았을 때, 그는 그런 일을 늘 진지하게 여겨 왔듯이 이 일도 충분히 진지하게 여겼고, 프램리를 떠나기 전에 그 자리를 위한 설교문을 완성해 두었다. 그러나 그 뒤로 이 일 전체에 조롱의 기운이 덮였고, 그는 자기 설교문 따위는 그다지 생각하지 않은 채 그 조롱에 함께 끼었으니, 이것이 이제 그로 하여금 다른 어떤 주제로든 강론을 골랐으면 좋겠다고 진심으로 바라게 만들었다."]], [["He knew well that the very points on which he had most insisted, were those which had drawn most mirth from Miss Dunstable and Mrs.", "그는 잘 알고 있었다. 자기가 가장 힘주어 강조한 바로 그 대목들이 던스터블 양과 스미스 부인에게서 가장 큰 웃음을 끌어냈고, 자기 자신의 웃음도 가장 자주 터뜨리게 했던 것들이라는 사실을."], ["Smith, and had oftenest provoked his own laughter; and how was he now to preach on those matters in a fitting mood, knowing, as he would know, that those two ladies would be looking at him, would endeavour to catch his eye, and would turn him into ridicule as they had already turned the lecturer?", "그러니 저 두 부인이 자기를 바라보고 있을 것이며, 자기 눈길을 붙잡으려 애쓸 것이며, 이미 강연자를 조롱거리로 만들었듯이 자기도 조롱거리로 만들 것이라는 사실을 알면서, 아니 알 수밖에 없으면서, 그가 어떻게 그 문제들을 걸맞은 마음가짐으로 설교할 수 있겠는가?"]], [["In this he did injustice to one of the ladies, unconsciously.", "이 점에서 그는 자기도 모르게 두 부인 가운데 한 사람에게 부당했다."], ["Miss Dunstable, with all her aptitude for mirth, and we may almost fairly say for frolic, was in no way inclined to ridicule religion or anything which she thought to appertain to it.", "던스터블 양은 웃음에, 아니 거의 공정하게 말하자면 장난에까지 소질이 있었으나, 종교나 종교에 속한다고 자기가 여기는 것을 조롱할 마음은 조금도 없었다."], ["It may be presumed that among such things she did not include Mrs.", "그런 것들 가운데 프라우디 부인을 넣지는 않았으리라고 짐작해도 좋을 것이다."], ["Proudie, as she was willing enough to laugh at that lady; but Mark, had he known her better, might have been sure that she would have sat out his sermon with perfect propriety.", "그 부인을 두고는 얼마든지 웃을 뜻이 있었으니 말이다. 그러나 마크가 그녀를 더 잘 알았더라면, 그녀가 완벽하게 예의를 지키며 자기 설교를 끝까지 앉아 들었으리라는 것을 확실히 알 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["As it was, however, he did feel considerable uneasiness; and in the morning he got up early with the view of seeing what might be done in the way of emendation.", "그러나 실상 그는 상당한 불안을 느꼈다. 그래서 아침에 일찍 일어나 손질할 여지가 없는지 보기로 했다."], ["He cut out those parts which referred most specially to the islands,—he rejected altogether those names over which they had all laughed together so heartily,—and he inserted a string of general remarks, very useful, no doubt, which he flattered himself would rob his sermon of all similarity to Harold Smith’s lecture.", "그는 그 섬들을 특별히 언급한 대목들을 잘라 냈고, 그들이 다 함께 그토록 신나게 웃어 댔던 그 이름들은 아예 빼 버렸으며, 의심할 바 없이 매우 유익한 일반론을 줄줄이 끼워 넣었다. 이렇게 하면 자기 설교가 해럴드 스미스의 강연과 조금도 닮지 않게 되리라고 그는 스스로 만족스레 여겼다."], ["He had, perhaps, hoped, when writing it, to create some little sensation; but now he would be quite satisfied if it passed without remark.", "설교문을 쓸 때는 아마도 얼마간 반향을 일으키기를 바랐을 것이다. 그러나 이제는 아무 말 없이 지나가 준다면 그것만으로도 아주 만족할 터였다."]], [["But his troubles for that Sunday were destined to be many.", "그러나 그 일요일에 그가 겪을 곤란은 여러 가지가 되도록 정해져 있었다."], ["It had been arranged that the party at the hotel should breakfast at eight, and start at half-past eight punctually, so as to enable them to reach Chaldicotes in ample time to arrange their dresses before they went to church.", "여관에 묵은 일행은 여덟 시에 아침을 먹고 여덟 시 반에 어김없이 출발하여, 콜디코츠에 넉넉히 도착해 교회에 가기 전에 옷을 갖추어 입을 수 있게 하기로 이야기가 되어 있었다."], ["The church stood in the grounds, close to that long formal avenue of lime-trees, but within the front gates.", "교회는 저 길고 격식 있는 보리수 가로수길 가까이, 그러나 앞쪽 대문 안쪽의 영지 안에 서 있었다."], ["Their walk, therefore, after reaching Mr. Sowerby’s house, would not be long.", "그러니 사워비 씨의 집에 닿은 뒤의 걸음은 길지 않을 것이었다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie, who was herself an early body, would not hear of her guest—and he a clergyman—going out to the inn for his breakfast on a Sunday morning.", "프라우디 부인은 자신이 아침에 일찍 일어나는 사람이었으므로, 자기 손님이 — 게다가 성직자가 — 일요일 아침에 아침 식사를 하러 여관으로 나가는 것은 들으려 하지 않았다."], ["As regarded that Sabbath-day journey to Chaldicotes, to that she had given her assent, no doubt with much uneasiness of mind; but let them have as little desecration as possible.", "콜디코츠로 가는 그 안식일 여행에 관해서는, 의심할 바 없이 마음이 몹시 불편하기는 했으나 승낙을 해 주었다. 그러나 신성을 더럽히는 일은 되도록 적게 하자는 것이었다."], ["It was, therefore, an understood thing that he was to return with his friends; but he should not go without the advantage of family prayers and family breakfast.", "그러므로 그가 벗들과 함께 돌아가는 것은 양해된 일이었으되, 가족 기도와 가족 아침 식사의 은덕을 받지 않고서 가서는 안 되었다."], ["And so Mrs. Proudie on retiring to rest gave the necessary orders, to the great annoyance of her household.", "그리하여 프라우디 부인은 잠자리에 들면서 필요한 지시를 내렸고, 그것이 집안 사람들에게는 크게 짜증스러운 일이었다."]], [["To the great annoyance, at least, of her servants!", "적어도 그녀의 하인들에게는 크게 짜증스러운 일이었다!"], ["The bishop himself did not make his appearance till a much later hour.", "주교 자신은 훨씬 늦은 시각까지 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그는 이제 모든 일에서 아내의 통치를 뒷받침했다."], ["He in all things now supported his wife’s rule; in all things, now, I say; for there had been a moment, when in the first flush and pride of his episcopacy other ideas had filled his mind.", "이제는 모든 일에서 그렇다고 나는 말한다. 주교직의 첫 흥분과 자랑 속에서 다른 생각들이 그의 마음을 채웠던 한때가 있었기 때문이다."], ["Now, however, he gave no opposition to that good woman with whom Providence had blessed him; and in return for such conduct that good woman administered in all things to his little personal comforts.", "그러나 이제 그는 섭리가 자기에게 축복으로 내려 준 그 착한 여인에게 아무런 반대도 하지 않았다. 그리고 그런 처신의 보답으로 그 착한 여인은 모든 일에서 그의 자그마한 개인적 안락을 돌보아 주었다."], ["With what surprise did the bishop now look back upon that unholy war which he had once been tempted to wage against the wife of his bosom?", "한때 품속의 아내를 상대로 벌이려는 유혹에 빠졌던 그 불경한 전쟁을, 주교는 이제 얼마나 놀란 마음으로 돌아보았던가?"]], [["Nor did any of the Miss Proudies show themselves at that early hour.", "프라우디 자매 가운데 누구도 그 이른 시각에 모습을 보이지 않았다."], ["They, perhaps, were absent on a different ground.", "그들은 아마 다른 이유로 자리에 없었을 것이다."], ["With them Mrs. Proudie had not been so successful as with the bishop.", "프라우디 부인은 그들에게는 주교만큼 성공을 거두지 못했다."], ["They had wills of their own which became stronger and stronger every day.", "그들에게는 저마다 제 뜻이 있었고, 그 뜻은 날마다 더욱더 강해졌다."], ["Of the three with whom Mrs. Proudie was blessed one was already in a position to exercise that will in a legitimate way over a very excellent young clergyman in the diocese, the Rev.", "프라우디 부인이 축복으로 받은 세 딸 가운데 하나는 이미 교구의 아주 훌륭한 젊은 성직자 옵티머스 그레이 목사를 상대로 그 뜻을 정당한 방식으로 행사할 자리에 있었다."], ["Optimus Grey; but the other two, having as yet no such opening for their powers of command, were perhaps a little too much inclined to keep themselves in practice at home.", "그러나 나머지 둘은 아직 자기들의 명령권을 펼칠 그런 통로가 없었으므로, 아마도 집에서 연습을 계속하려는 쪽으로 조금 지나치게 기울어 있었다."]], [["But at half-past seven punctually Mrs. Proudie was there, and so was the domestic chaplain; so was Mr.", "그러나 일곱 시 반에는 프라우디 부인이 어김없이 그 자리에 있었고, 전속 사제도 그러했으며, 로바츠 씨도 그러했고, 집안 하인들도 다 그러했다 — 게으른 배교자 하나만 빼고."], ["Robarts, and so were the household servants,—all excepting one lazy recreant. “Where is Thomas?”", "“토머스는 어디 있느냐?”"], ["said she of the Argus eyes, standing up with her book of family prayers in her hand.", "아르고스의 눈을 가진 그 여인이 가족 기도서를 손에 들고 일어서서 말했다."], ["“So please you, ma’am, Tummas be bad with the tooth-ache.”", "“황송하오나 마님, 터머스는 이가 아파 앓고 있습니다요.” “이가 아프다니!”"], ["“Tooth-ache!” exclaimed Mrs.", "프라우디 부인이 소리쳤다."], ["Proudie; but her eyes said more terrible things than that.", "그러나 그녀의 눈은 그보다 훨씬 무서운 것들을 말하고 있었다."], ["“Let Thomas come to me before church.”", "“교회에 가기 전에 토머스를 내게 보내라.”"], ["And then they proceeded to prayers.", "그러고서 그들은 기도로 넘어갔다."], ["These were read by the chaplain, as it was proper and decent that they should be; but I cannot but think that Mrs.", "기도는 전속 사제가 읽었으니, 그것이 마땅하고 온당한 일이었다."], ["Proudie a little exceeded her office in taking upon herself to pronounce the blessing when the prayers were over.", "그러나 기도가 끝났을 때 프라우디 부인이 축도를 스스로 맡아 읊은 것은 자기 직분을 조금 넘어선 일이었다고 나는 생각하지 않을 수 없다."], ["She did it, however, in a clear, sonorous voice, and perhaps with more personal dignity than was within the chaplain’s compass.", "하기야 그녀는 맑고 낭랑한 목소리로 그것을 해냈으며, 어쩌면 전속 사제가 미칠 수 없는 위엄을 몸소 드러내었다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie was rather stern at breakfast, and the vicar of Framley felt an unaccountable desire to get out of the house.", "프라우디 부인은 아침 식사 자리에서 다소 엄했고, 프램리의 교구 사제는 이 집에서 나가고 싶다는 까닭 모를 마음에 사로잡혔다."], ["In the first place she was not dressed with her usual punctilious attention to the proprieties of her high situation.", "우선 그녀는 자기 높은 지위의 격식에 늘 기울이던 그 꼼꼼한 주의로 차려입고 있지 않았다."], ["It was evident that there was to be a further toilet before she sailed up the middle of the cathedral choir.", "대성당 성가대석 가운데를 미끄러져 올라가기 전에 다시 한 번 몸단속이 있을 것이 분명했다."], ["She had on a large loose cap with no other strings than those which were wanted for tying it beneath her chin, a cap with which the household and the chaplain were well acquainted, but which seemed ungracious in the eyes of Mr. Robarts after all the well-dressed holiday doings of the last week.", "그녀는 턱 아래로 매는 데 필요한 끈 말고는 다른 끈이 없는 크고 헐렁한 모자를 쓰고 있었는데, 집안 사람들과 전속 사제에게는 익숙한 모자였으나 지난 한 주간의 잘 차려입은 놀이 뒤인 로바츠 씨의 눈에는 볼품없어 보였다."], ["She wore also a large, loose, dark-coloured wrapper, which came well up round her neck, and which was not buoyed out, as were her dresses in general, with an under mechanism of petticoats.", "또 그녀는 목까지 잘 올라오는 크고 헐렁한 짙은 색 겉옷을 입고 있었는데, 그것은 그녀의 옷들이 대개 그러하듯 속치마의 장치로 부풀려져 있지 않았다."], ["It clung to her closely, and added to the inflexibility of her general appearance.", "그것은 몸에 착 달라붙어 그녀의 전체적인 모습에 딱딱함을 한층 더했다."], ["And then she had encased her feet in large carpet slippers, which no doubt were comfortable, but which struck her visitor as being strange and unsightly.", "그리고 그녀는 두 발을 커다란 카펫 슬리퍼에 넣고 있었으니, 그것은 의심할 바 없이 편안한 물건이겠으나 손님에게는 이상하고 보기 흉하게 느껴졌다."]], [["“Do you find a difficulty in getting your people together for early morning prayers?”", "“이른 아침 기도에 사람들을 모으기가 어렵소?”"], ["she said, as she commenced her operations with the teapot.", "그녀가 찻주전자를 들고 일을 시작하며 말했다."]], [["“I can’t say that I do,”", "“그렇다고는 못 하겠습니다.”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."], ["“But then we are seldom so early as this.”", "“하기야 저희는 이렇게 이른 적이 좀처럼 없지요.”"]], [["“Parish clergymen should be early, I think,”", "“교구 성직자는 일찍 일어나야 한다고 나는 생각하오.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“It sets a good example in the village.”", "“그것이 마을에 좋은 본이 되지요.”"]], [["“I am thinking of having morning prayers in the church,”", "“교회에서 아침 기도를 드릴까 생각 중입니다.”"], ["said Mr. Robarts.", "로바츠 씨가 말했다."]], [["“That’s nonsense,” said Mrs.", "“그건 부질없는 일이오.”"], ["Proudie, “and usually means worse than nonsense.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그리고 대개는 부질없는 것보다 더 나쁜 일을 뜻하지요."], ["I know what that comes to.", "그게 어떻게 되는지 나는 알고 있소."], ["If you have three services on Sunday and domestic prayers at home, you do very well.”", "일요일에 예배를 세 번 드리고 집에서 가족 기도를 드린다면 아주 잘하는 것이오.”"], ["And so saying she handed him his cup.", "그러고서 그녀는 그에게 찻잔을 건넸다."]], [["“But I have not three services on Sunday, Mrs. Proudie.”", "“그런데 저는 일요일에 예배를 세 번 드리지 않습니다, 프라우디 부인.”"]], [["“Then I think you should have.", "“그렇다면 그렇게 해야 한다고 생각하오."], ["Where can the poor people be so well off on Sundays as in church?", "가난한 사람들이 일요일에 교회만큼 편히 있을 곳이 어디 있겠소?"], ["The bishop intends to express a very strong opinion on this subject in his next charge; and then I am sure you will attend to his wishes.”", "주교께서는 다음 훈시에서 이 문제에 대해 매우 강한 의견을 밝히실 참이오. 그러면 로바츠 씨도 그분의 뜻을 분명 따르겠지요.”"]], [["To this Mark made no answer, but devoted himself to his egg.", "이 말에 마크는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 자기 달걀에 전념했다."]], [["“I suppose you have not a very large establishment at Framley?”", "“프램리에서는 살림을 그리 크게 하지는 않겠지요?”"], ["asked Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 물었다."]], [["“What, at the parsonage?”", "“목사관 말씀입니까?”"]], [["“Yes; you live at the parsonage, don’t you?”", "“그렇소. 목사관에 살지 않소?”"]], [["“Certainly—well; not very large, Mrs.", "“물론입니다 — 글쎄요, 그리 크지는 않습니다, 프라우디 부인."], ["Proudie; just enough to do the work, make things comfortable, and look after the children.”", "일을 해내고, 살림을 편하게 하고, 아이들을 돌볼 만큼만 두고 있지요.”"]], [["“It is a very fine living,”", "“아주 좋은 성직록이오.”"], ["said she; “very fine.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["I don’t remember that we have anything so good ourselves,—except it is Plumstead, the archdeacon’s place.", "“아주 좋지요. 우리 쪽에도 그만큼 좋은 것이 있는지는 기억이 나지 않소 — 부주교의 자리인 플럼스테드만 빼고는."], ["He has managed to butter his bread pretty well.”", "그 사람은 제 빵에 버터를 꽤 잘 발라 놓았지요.”"]], [["“His father was Bishop of Barchester.”", "“그분의 아버님이 바체스터 주교였습니다.”"]], [["“Oh, yes, I know all about him.", "“아, 그렇지요, 그 사람 일은 다 알고 있소."], ["Only for that he would barely have risen to be an archdeacon, I suspect.", "그것이 아니었다면 부주교 자리까지도 겨우 올라갔을까 싶소."], ["Let me see; yours is £800, is it not, Mr. Robarts?", "어디 보자, 로바츠 씨 것은 800파운드지요, 그렇지 않소?"], ["And you such a young man!", "그런데 그렇게 젊은 나이에!"], ["I suppose you have insured your life highly.”", "생명 보험은 넉넉히 들어 두었겠지요.”"]], [["“Pretty well, Mrs. Proudie.”", "“그럭저럭 들었습니다, 프라우디 부인.”"]], [["“And then, too, your wife had some little fortune, had she not?", "“그리고 부인도 얼마간 재산이 있었지요, 그렇지 않소?"], ["We cannot all fall on our feet like that; can we, Mr.", "우리가 다 그렇게 두 발로 떨어질 수는 없지요."], ["White?” and Mrs.", "그렇지 않소, 화이트 씨?”"], ["Proudie in her playful way appealed to the chaplain.", "그러고서 프라우디 부인은 장난스러운 투로 전속 사제에게 말을 붙였다."]], [["Mrs. Proudie was an imperious woman; but then so also was Lady Lufton; and it may therefore be said that Mr.", "프라우디 부인은 위세를 부리는 여인이었다."], ["Robarts ought to have been accustomed to feminine domination; but as he sat there munching his toast he could not but make a comparison between the two.", "그러나 레이디 러프턴 또한 그러했다. 그러니 로바츠 씨는 여인의 지배에 익숙해져 있어야 했다고 말할 만도 하다."], ["Lady Lufton in her little attempts sometimes angered him; but he certainly thought, comparing the lay lady and the clerical together, that the rule of the former was the lighter and the pleasanter.", "그렇지만 그는 거기 앉아 토스트를 씹으면서 두 사람을 견주어 보지 않을 수 없었다. 레이디 러프턴은 그 자그마한 시도들로 이따금 그를 노엽게 했다."], ["But then Lady Lufton had given him a living and a wife, and Mrs.", "그러나 세속의 부인과 교회의 부인을 나란히 놓고 견주자면, 앞쪽의 통치가 더 가볍고 더 즐겁다고 그는 분명히 생각했다."], ["Proudie had given him nothing.", "하기야 레이디 러프턴은 그에게 성직록과 아내를 주었고, 프라우디 부인은 그에게 아무것도 주지 않았다."]], [["Immediately after breakfast Mr. Robarts escaped to the Dragon of Wantly, partly because he had had enough of the matutinal Mrs.", "아침 식사가 끝나자마자 로바츠 씨는 원틀리의 용으로 빠져나갔다."], ["Proudie, and partly also in order that he might hurry his friends there.", "한편으로는 아침나절의 프라우디 부인을 충분히 겪었기 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 거기 있는 벗들을 재촉하려는 뜻도 있었다."], ["He was already becoming fidgety about the time, as Harold Smith had been on the preceding evening, and he did not give Mrs. Smith credit for much punctuality.", "그는 전날 저녁 해럴드 스미스가 그랬듯 벌써 시간에 조바심을 내고 있었고, 스미스 부인이 시간을 잘 지킬 것이라고는 별로 믿지 않았다."], ["When he arrived at the inn he asked if they had done breakfast, and was immediately told that not one of them was yet down.", "여관에 닿아 그들이 아침을 먹었느냐고 물으니, 아직 아무도 내려오지 않았다는 대답이 곧 돌아왔다."], ["It was already half-past eight, and they ought to be now under way on the road.", "벌써 여덟 시 반이었으니, 그들은 이제 길에 나서 있어야 했다."]], [["He immediately went to Mr. Sowerby’s room, and found that gentleman shaving himself.", "그는 곧 사워비 씨의 방으로 갔고, 그 신사가 수염을 깎고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["“Don’t be a bit uneasy,”", "“조금도 걱정하지 마세요.”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."], ["“You and Smith shall have my phaeton, and those horses will take you there in an hour.", "“당신과 스미스가 내 페이턴을 타면 되고, 그 말들이 한 시간 안에 거기까지 데려다 줄 겁니다."], ["Not, however, but what we shall all be in time.", "하기야 우리 모두 시간에 맞추게 될 테지만요."], ["We’ll send round to the whole party and ferret them out.”", "온 일행에게 사람을 돌려서 하나씩 다 몰아내지요.”"], ["And then Mr. Sowerby, having evoked manifold aid with various peals of the bell, sent messengers, male and female, flying to all the different rooms.", "그러고서 사워비 씨는 종을 여러 차례 울려 온갖 도움을 불러내어, 남녀 심부름꾼들을 여러 방으로 날려 보냈다."]], [["“I think I’ll hire a gig and go over at once,”", "“저는 이륜마차를 하나 빌려서 지금 곧 건너갈까 합니다.”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."], ["“It would not do for me to be late, you know.”", "“제가 늦으면 안 되는 것은 아시지요.”"]], [["“It won’t do for any of us to be late; and it’s all nonsense about hiring a gig.", "“우리 가운데 누구도 늦으면 안 되지요. 그리고 이륜마차를 빌린다는 건 다 부질없는 소리예요."], ["It would be just throwing a sovereign away, and we should pass you on the road.", "소버린 한 닢을 그냥 버리는 셈이고, 우리가 길에서 당신을 지나쳐 갈 텐데요."], ["Go down and see that the tea is made, and all that; and make them have the bill ready; and, Robarts, you may pay it too, if you like it.", "내려가서 차가 다 되었는지 보고, 그런 것들도 챙기고, 계산서를 준비해 두게 하세요. 그리고 로바츠, 원한다면 그 계산도 당신이 치러도 좋아요."], ["But I believe we may as well leave that to Baron Borneo—eh?”", "하지만 그건 보르네오 남작에게 맡겨 두어도 되겠지요, 안 그래요?”"]], [["And then Mark did go down and make the tea, and he did order the bill; and then he walked about the room, looking at his watch, and nervously waiting for the footsteps of his friends. And as he was so employed, he bethought himself whether it was fit that he should be so doing on a Sunday morning; whether it was good that he should be waiting there, in painful anxiety, to gallop over a dozen miles in order that he might not be too late with his sermon; whether his own snug room at home, with Fanny opposite to him, and his bairns crawling on the floor, with his own preparations for his own quiet service, and the warm pressure of Lady Lufton’s hand when that service should be over, was not better than all this.", "그리하여 마크는 정말로 내려가 차를 준비시켰고, 계산서도 시켜 두었다. 그러고서 그는 방 안을 서성이며 시계를 보고, 벗들의 발소리를 초조하게 기다렸다. 그렇게 하고 있는 동안 그는 문득 생각했다. 일요일 아침에 자기가 이런 일을 하고 있는 것이 마땅한가? 설교에 너무 늦지 않으려고 열두 마일을 달려가려고 여기서 괴로운 불안에 싸여 기다리고 있는 것이 좋은 일인가? 패니가 마주 앉아 있고 아이들이 바닥을 기어 다니는 집의 아늑한 자기 방, 자기 조용한 예배를 위한 자기 준비, 그리고 그 예배가 끝났을 때 레이디 러프턴이 따뜻하게 잡아 주는 손, 그것이 이 모든 것보다 낫지 않았던가?"]], [["He could not afford not to know Harold Smith, and Mr. Sowerby, and the Duke of Omnium, he had said to himself.", "자기는 해럴드 스미스와 사워비 씨와 옴니엄 공작을 모르고 지낼 수는 없다고 그는 스스로에게 말해 왔다."], ["He had to look to rise in the world, as other men did.", "다른 사람들이 하듯 세상에서 올라갈 길을 찾아야 했다."], ["But what pleasure had come to him as yet from these intimacies?", "그러나 이 친교에서 그에게 지금까지 어떤 즐거움이 왔던가?"], ["How much had he hitherto done towards his rising? To speak the truth he was not over well pleased with himself, as he made Mrs.", "올라가는 일을 위해 그가 이제까지 얼마나 해냈던가?"], ["Harold Smith’s tea and ordered Mr. Sowerby’s mutton-chops on that Sunday morning.", "사실을 말하자면, 그 일요일 아침에 해럴드 스미스 부인의 차를 끓이고 사워비 씨의 양갈비를 주문하면서 그는 자기 자신이 그리 마땅치 않았다."]], [["At a little after nine they all assembled; but even then he could not make the ladies understand that there was any cause for hurry; at least Mrs.", "아홉 시가 조금 지나 그들은 모두 모였다. 그러나 그때에도 그는 서둘러야 할 까닭이 있다는 것을 부인들에게 납득시킬 수 없었다."], ["Smith, who was the leader of the party, would not understand it.", "적어도 일행의 우두머리인 스미스 부인은 그것을 납득하려 하지 않았다."], ["When Mark again talked of hiring a gig, Miss Dunstable indeed said that she would join him; and seemed to be so far earnest in the matter that Mr. Sowerby hurried through his second egg in order to prevent such a catastrophe.", "마크가 다시 이륜마차를 빌리는 이야기를 하자 던스터블 양이 자기도 함께 타겠다고 말했고, 그 일에 어찌나 진심인 듯 보였던지 사워비 씨는 그런 참사를 막으려고 두 번째 달걀을 서둘러 먹어 치웠다."], ["And then Mark absolutely did order the gig; whereupon Mrs.", "그러고서 마크는 정말로 이륜마차를 시켰다."], ["Smith remarked that in such case she need not hurry herself; but the waiter brought up word that all the horses of the hotel were out, excepting one pair, neither of which could go in single harness.", "그러자 스미스 부인은 그렇다면 자기는 서두를 필요가 없겠다고 말했다. 그러나 급사가 올라와 전한 말은, 여관의 말이 한 쌍만 빼고 다 나가 있으며 그 한 쌍은 둘 다 외마차에 맬 수 없다는 것이었다."], ["Indeed, half of their stable establishment was already secured by Mr. Sowerby’s own party.", "사실 여관 마구간의 절반은 이미 사워비 씨 일행이 잡아 두고 있었다."]], [["“Then let me have the pair,”", "“그럼 그 한 쌍을 내가 쓰겠네.”"], ["said Mark, almost frantic with delay.", "지체 때문에 거의 미칠 지경이 된 마크가 말했다."]], [["“Nonsense, Robarts; we are ready now.", "“부질없는 소리예요, 로바츠. 우리 이제 준비되었어요."], ["He won’t want them, James.", "이분은 그것들이 필요 없겠네, 제임스."], ["Come, Supplehouse, have you done?”", "자, 서플하우스, 다 됐나?”"]], [["“Then I am to hurry myself, am I?”", "“그러면 이제 내가 서둘러야 하는 건가요?”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["“What changeable creatures you men are!", "“남자들이란 어찌나 변덕스러운 것들인지!"], ["May I be allowed half a cup more tea, Mr. Robarts?”", "차를 반 잔만 더 주셔도 될까요, 로바츠 씨?”"]], [["Mark, who was now really angry, turned away to the window.", "이제 정말로 화가 난 마크는 창가로 돌아섰다."], ["There was no charity in these people, he said to himself.", "이 사람들에게는 자애라는 것이 없다고 그는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["They knew the nature of his distress, and yet they only laughed at him.", "그들은 그의 곤란이 어떤 것인지 알면서도 그를 비웃기만 했다."], ["He did not, perhaps, reflect that he had assisted in the joke against Harold Smith on the previous evening.", "아마도 그는 전날 저녁에 자기가 해럴드 스미스를 놀리는 농담에 한몫 거들었다는 것은 떠올리지 않았다."]], [["“James,”", "“제임스,”"], ["said he, turning to the waiter, “let me have that pair of horses immediately, if you please.”", "그가 급사를 돌아보며 말했다. “미안하지만 그 말 한 쌍을 지금 곧 내주게.”"]], [["“Yes, sir; round in fifteen minutes, sir: only Ned, sir, the post-boy, sir; I fear he’s at his breakfast, sir; but we’ll have him here in less than no time, sir!”", "“예, 나리. 십오 분 안에 돌려 대겠습니다, 나리. 다만 마부 아이 네드가 말이지요, 나리, 아침을 먹고 있는 게 아닌가 싶습니다, 나리. 하지만 눈 깜짝할 사이도 안 되어 데려오겠습니다요, 나리!”"]], [["But before Ned and the pair were there, Mrs. Smith had absolutely got her bonnet on, and at ten they started.", "그러나 네드와 그 한 쌍이 오기 전에 스미스 부인이 어김없이 모자를 다 쓰고 나섰고, 열 시에 그들은 출발했다."], ["Mark did share the phaeton with Harold Smith, but the phaeton did not go any faster than the other carriages.", "마크는 해럴드 스미스와 페이턴을 함께 탔으나, 페이턴은 다른 마차들보다 조금도 빠르지 않았다. 과연 앞장은 섰으되 그뿐이었다."], ["They led the way, indeed, but that was all; and when the vicar’s watch told him that it was eleven, they were still a mile from Chaldicotes’ gate, although the horses were in a lather of steam; and they had only just entered the village when the church bells ceased to be heard.", "그리고 교구 사제의 시계가 열한 시라고 알려 주었을 때 그들은 아직 콜디코츠 대문에서 한 마일 떨어져 있었으니, 말들은 땀에 흠뻑 젖어 김을 내뿜고 있었다. 그리고 교회 종소리가 그치는 것이 들릴 때에야 그들은 겨우 마을에 들어섰다."]], [["“Come, you are in time, after all,”", "“보시오, 결국 시간에 맞췄구려.”"], ["said Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스가 말했다."], ["“Better time than I was last night.”", "“어젯밤의 내 시간보다는 나은 편이오.”"], ["Robarts could not explain to him that the entry of a clergyman into church, of a clergyman who is going to assist in the service, should not be made at the last minute, that it should be staid and decorous, and not done in scrambling haste, with running feet and scant breath.", "성직자가, 그것도 예배를 도우러 가는 성직자가 교회에 들어서는 일은 마지막 순간에 이루어져서는 안 되며, 차분하고 점잖아야 하고, 뛰는 걸음과 가쁜 숨으로 허둥지둥 해서는 안 된다는 것을 로바츠는 그에게 설명해 줄 수 없었다."]], [["“I suppose we’ll stop here, sir,”", "“여기서 세우면 되겠지요, 나리.”"], ["said the postilion, as he pulled up his horses short at the church-door, in the midst of the people who were congregated together ready for the service.", "마부 아이가 예배를 위해 모여 선 사람들 가운데서 교회 문 앞에 말을 딱 세우며 말했다."], ["But Mark had not anticipated being so late, and said at first that it was necessary that he should go on to the house; then, when the horses had again begun to move, he remembered that he could send for his gown, and as he got out of the carriage he gave his orders accordingly.", "그러나 마크는 자기가 이토록 늦을 줄은 미리 헤아리지 못했던 터라, 처음에는 집까지 가야 한다고 말했다. 그러다 말들이 다시 움직이기 시작했을 때, 사람을 보내 법의를 가져오게 할 수 있다는 것을 떠올렸고, 마차에서 내리면서 그대로 지시를 내렸다."], ["And now the other two carriages were there, and so there was a noise and confusion at the door—very unseemly, as Mark felt it; and the gentlemen spoke in loud voices, and Mrs.", "그리고 이제 다른 두 마차도 와 있었으므로 문간에는 소란과 혼잡이 일었으니 — 마크가 느끼기에 매우 볼품없는 일이었다."], ["Harold Smith declared that she had no prayer-book, and was much too tired to go in at present;—she would go home and rest herself, she said.", "신사들은 큰 목소리로 이야기했고, 해럴드 스미스 부인은 자기에게 기도서가 없으며 지금은 너무 지쳐서 들어갈 수 없다고 밝혔다. 집으로 가서 쉬겠다고 그녀는 말했다."], ["And two other ladies of the party did so also, leaving Miss Dunstable to go alone;—for which, however, she did not care one button.", "일행 가운데 다른 두 부인도 그렇게 하여, 던스터블 양만 혼자 들어가게 되었다. 하기야 그녀는 그런 일 따위는 조금도 개의치 않았다."], ["And then one of the party, who had a nasty habit of swearing, cursed at something as he walked in close to Mark’s elbow; and so they made their way up the church as the absolution was being read, and Mark Robarts felt thoroughly ashamed of himself.", "그리고 일행 가운데 욕하는 못된 버릇이 있는 한 사람이 마크의 팔꿈치에 바싹 붙어 걸어 들어오면서 무엇엔가 욕설을 퍼부었다. 그리하여 그들은 사죄 기도가 낭독되는 중에 교회 안을 걸어 올라갔고, 마크 로바츠는 자기 자신이 몹시 부끄러웠다."], ["If his rising in the world brought him in contact with such things as these, would it not be better for him that he should do without rising?", "세상에서 올라가는 일이 그를 이런 것들과 맞닿게 한다면, 올라가지 않고 지내는 편이 그에게 더 낫지 않겠는가?"]], [["His sermon went off without any special notice.", "그의 설교는 별다른 주목 없이 지나갔다."], ["Mrs. Harold Smith was not there, much to his satisfaction; and the others who were did not seem to pay any special attention to it.", "해럴드 스미스 부인은 그 자리에 없었으니 그로서는 크게 만족스러운 일이었고, 자리에 있던 다른 이들도 설교에 별다른 주의를 기울이는 것 같지 않았다."], ["The subject had lost its novelty, except with the ordinary church congregation, the farmers and labourers of the parish; and the “quality”", "그 주제는 새로움을 잃어버렸다. 다만 여느 교회 신자들, 곧 교구의 농부들과 일꾼들에게는 그렇지 않았다."], ["in the squire’s great pew were content to show their sympathy by a moderate subscription.", "그리고 지주의 큰 신도석에 앉은 “높은 분들”은 어지간한 헌금으로 공감을 보이는 데 만족했다."], ["Miss Dunstable, however, gave a ten-pound note, which swelled up the sum total to a respectable amount—for such a place as Chaldicotes.", "그러나 던스터블 양이 10파운드 지폐를 내놓아, 총액이 콜디코츠 같은 곳으로서는 남 앞에 내놓을 만한 액수까지 불어났다."]], [["“And now I hope I may never hear another word about New Guinea,”", "“그래, 이제는 뉴기니라는 말을 다시 듣지 않게 되기를 바라네.”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby, as they all clustered round the drawing-room fire after church.", "예배가 끝난 뒤 모두 응접실 난롯가에 둘러 모였을 때 사워비 씨가 말했다."], ["“That subject may be regarded as having been killed and buried; eh, Harold?”", "“그 주제는 죽어서 묻힌 것으로 여겨도 되겠지. 그렇지 않은가, 해럴드?”"]], [["“Certainly murdered last night,”", "“어젯밤에 확실히 살해되었지요.”"], ["said Mrs.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["Harold, “by that awful woman, Mrs. Proudie.”", "“저 무서운 여자, 프라우디 부인의 손에.”"]], [["“I wonder you did not make a dash at her and pull her out of the arm-chair,”", "“부인이 그 여자에게 달려들어 안락의자에서 끌어내지 않은 게 이상하네요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“I was expecting it, and thought that I should come to grief in the scrimmage.”", "“저는 그러기를 기다리고 있었고, 그 난투 속에서 제가 봉변을 당하겠구나 생각했지요.”"]], [["“I never knew a lady do such a brazen-faced thing before,”", "“숙녀가 그처럼 낯가죽 두꺼운 짓을 하는 건 여태 본 적이 없어요.”"], ["said Miss Kerrigy, a travelling friend of Miss Dunstable’s.", "던스터블 양의 여행 동무인 케리지 양이 말했다."]], [["“Nor I—never; in a public place, too,” said Dr.", "“나도 그렇습니다 — 여태 한 번도."], ["Easyman, a medical gentleman, who also often accompanied her.", "게다가 사람들이 모인 자리에서 말이지요.” 역시 그녀와 자주 동행하는 의사 이지먼 선생이 말했다."]], [["“As for brass,” said Mr.", "“낯가죽으로 말하자면,”"], ["Supplehouse, “she would never stop at anything for want of that.", "서플하우스 씨가 말했다. “그 부인은 그것이 모자라서 못 하는 일이란 없을 거요."], ["It is well that she has enough, for the poor bishop is but badly provided.”", "넉넉히 가지고 있으니 다행이지요. 불쌍한 주교는 그 점에서 아주 빈약하게 타고났으니 말이오.”"]], [["“I hardly heard what it was she did say,”", "“그 부인이 무슨 말을 했는지 나는 거의 듣지도 못했소.”"], ["said Harold Smith; “so I could not answer her, you know.", "해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “그러니 대답을 할 수도 없었지요, 아시겠소."], ["Something about Sundays, I believe.”", "일요일에 관한 무슨 이야기였던 것 같소.”"]], [["“She hoped you would not put the South Sea islanders up to Sabbath travelling,”", "“남태평양 섬사람들에게 안식일 여행을 가르치지 말아 달라고 바라더군.”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“And specially begged that you would establish Lord’s-day schools,”", "“그리고 주일 학교를 꼭 세워 달라고 특별히 청했지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Smith; and then they all went to work and picked Mrs.", "스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["Proudie to pieces from the top ribbon of her cap down to the sole of her slipper.", "그러고서 그들은 모두 일에 착수하여 프라우디 부인을 모자의 맨 위 리본에서 슬리퍼 밑바닥까지 낱낱이 헐어 놓았다."]], [["“And then she expects the poor parsons to fall in love with her daughters.", "“그러고 나서는 가난한 목사들이 자기 딸들과 사랑에 빠져 주기를 바라지요."], ["That’s the hardest thing of all,”", "그게 무엇보다 딱한 일이에요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["But, on the whole, when our vicar went to bed he did not feel that he had spent a profitable Sunday.", "그러나 대체로 보아, 우리 교구 사제가 잠자리에 들 때 그는 자기가 유익한 일요일을 보냈다고 느끼지 못했다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII. GATHERUM CASTLE.", "# 제8장. 개서럼 성"]], [["On the Tuesday morning Mark did receive his wife’s letter and the ten-pound note, whereby a strong proof was given of the honesty of the post-office people in Barsetshire.", "화요일 아침에 마크는 과연 아내의 편지와 10파운드짜리 지폐를 받았고, 이로써 바셋주 우체국 사람들의 정직함이 강력하게 증명된 셈이었다."], ["That letter, written as it had been in a hurry, while Robin post-boy was drinking a single mug of beer,—well, what of it if it was half filled a second time?—was nevertheless eloquent of his wife’s love and of her great triumph.", "우편배달 소년 로빈이 맥주를 딱 한 잔 들이켜는 동안 서둘러 쓴 그 편지는—뭐, 두 번째 잔을 반쯤 더 채웠다 한들 그게 무슨 상관이랴?—그럼에도 아내의 사랑과 그녀의 큰 승리를 웅변하고 있었다."]], [["I have only half a moment to send you the money [she said], for the postman is here waiting.", "돈만 보낼 시간밖에 없어요[그녀는 이렇게 적었다]. 우편배달부가 여기서 기다리고 있으니까요."], ["When I see you I’ll explain why I am so hurried.", "만나면 왜 이렇게 서둘렀는지 설명해 드릴게요."], ["Let me know that you get it safe.", "무사히 받으셨다고 알려 주세요. 이제 다 잘됐어요."], ["It is all right now, and Lady Lufton was here not a minute ago.", "레이디 러프턴이 방금 전까지 여기 계셨어요. 그분은 영 마음에 들지 않으셨어요."], ["She did not quite like it; about Gatherum Castle I mean; but you’ll hear nothing about it.", "개서럼 성 이야기 말이에요. 하지만 당신은 그 일로 아무 말도 듣지 않으실 거예요."], ["Only remember that you must dine at Framley Court on Wednesday week.", "다만 다음 주 수요일에는 반드시 프램리 코트에서 저녁을 드셔야 한다는 것만 기억하세요."], ["I have promised for you.", "제가 당신 대신 약속했어요."], ["You will: won’t you, dearest?", "그렇게 해 주실 거죠, 여보?"], ["I shall come and fetch you away if you attempt to stay longer than you have said.", "말씀하신 날보다 더 머무르려 하시면 제가 가서 끌고 올 거예요. 하지만 그러지 않으실 거라고 믿어요."], ["But I’m sure you won’t. God bless you, my own one!", "하느님의 축복이 있기를, 내 하나뿐인 사람!"], ["Mr. Jones gave us the same sermon he preached the second Sunday after Easter.", "존스 씨는 부활절 후 둘째 주일에 하셨던 설교를 또 하셨어요."], ["Twice in the same year is too often.", "한 해에 두 번은 너무 잦지요."], ["God bless you!", "하느님의 축복이 있기를!"], ["The children are quite well.", "아이들은 아주 잘 있어요."], ["Mark sends a big kiss.—Your own F.", "마크가 큰 입맞춤을 보냅니다.—당신의 F."]], [["Robarts, as he read this letter and crumpled the note up into his pocket, felt that it was much more satisfactory than he deserved.", "로바츠는 이 편지를 읽고 지폐를 구겨 주머니에 넣으면서, 자기가 받아 마땅한 것보다 훨씬 만족스러운 결과라고 느꼈다."], ["He knew that there must have been a fight, and that his wife, fighting loyally on his behalf, had got the best of it; and he knew also that her victory had not been owing to the goodness of her cause.", "한바탕 싸움이 있었을 것이고, 자기를 위해 충실히 싸운 아내가 그 싸움에서 이겼다는 것을 그는 알았다. 그리고 그녀의 승리가 그 명분이 훌륭했던 덕은 아니라는 것도 알았다."], ["He frequently declared to himself that he would not be afraid of Lady Lufton; but nevertheless these tidings that no reproaches were to be made to him afforded him great relief.", "그는 레이디 러프턴을 두려워하지 않겠다고 스스로에게 자주 선언했지만, 그럼에도 자기에게 아무 책망도 돌아오지 않는다는 이 소식은 그에게 큰 안도를 주었다."]], [["On the following Friday they all went to the duke’s, and found that the bishop and Mrs.", "다음 금요일에 그들은 모두 공작의 집으로 갔고, 주교와 프라우디 부인이 이미 와 있는 것을 보았다. 그 밖에도 여러 사람들이 있었는데, 대개 세상 일반의 평가로든 서부 바셋주의 평가로든 어느 정도 이름 있는 이들이었다."], ["Proudie were there before them; as were also sundry other people, mostly of some note, either in the estimation of the world at large or of that of West Barsetshire.", "보아네르게스 경이 와 있었다. 무슨 일에서든 제 뜻을 관철하는 노인으로, 모든 사람에게—보아하니 공작 자신에게까지도—지성의 왕으로 여겨지는 사람이었다."], ["Lord Boanerges was there, an old man who would have his own way in everything, and who was regarded by all men—apparently even by the duke himself—as an intellectual king, by no means of the constitutional kind,—as an intellectual emperor, rather, who took upon himself to rule all questions of mind without the assistance of any ministers whatever.", "그것도 결코 입헌군주 같은 왕이 아니라, 어떤 대신의 도움도 없이 정신에 관한 모든 문제를 홀로 다스리기를 자임하는 지성의 황제로 여겨졌다. 그리고 브롤 남작도 일행에 있었다."], ["And Baron Brawl was of the party, one of her Majesty’s puisne judges, as jovial a guest as ever entered a country house; but given to be rather sharp withal in his jovialities.", "여왕 폐하의 배석 판사 가운데 한 사람으로, 시골 저택에 들어선 손님 중에 그만큼 유쾌한 이가 없었다. 다만 그 유쾌함 속에 다소 날카로운 데가 있는 것이 흠이었다."], ["And there was Mr.", "또 그린 워커 씨가 있었다."], ["Green Walker, a young but rising man, the same who lectured not long since on a popular subject to his constituents at the Crewe Junction. Mr.", "젊지만 떠오르는 인물로, 얼마 전 크루 정션의 자기 선거구민들에게 대중적인 주제로 강연을 한 바로 그 사람이었다."], ["Green Walker was a nephew of the Marchioness of Hartletop, and the Marchioness of Hartletop was a friend of the Duke of Omnium’s. Mr.", "그린 워커 씨는 하틀탑 후작부인의 조카였고, 하틀탑 후작부인은 옴니엄 공작의 벗이었다."], ["Mark Robarts was certainly elated when he ascertained who composed the company of which he had been so earnestly pressed to make a portion.", "마크 로바츠 씨는 자기가 그토록 간곡히 한몫 끼라고 권유받은 그 일행이 누구로 이루어졌는지 알게 되자 분명 우쭐해졌다."], ["Would it have been wise in him to forego this on account of the prejudices of Lady Lufton?", "레이디 러프턴의 편견 때문에 이것을 포기하는 것이 현명한 일이었겠는가?"]], [["As the guests were so many and so great, the huge front portals of Gatherum Castle were thrown open, and the vast hall, adorned with trophies—with marble busts from Italy and armour from Wardour Street,—was thronged with gentlemen and ladies, and gave forth unwonted echoes to many a footstep.", "손님이 그렇게 많고 그렇게 대단했으므로 개서럼 성의 거대한 정면 문짝이 활짝 열렸고, 이탈리아에서 온 대리석 흉상과 워도어 스트리트에서 온 갑주 같은 전리품으로 장식된 광대한 홀은 신사 숙녀로 붐볐으며, 수많은 발소리에 낯선 메아리를 돌려주었다."], ["His grace himself, when Mark arrived there with Sowerby and Miss Dunstable—for in this instance Miss Dunstable did travel in the phaeton while Mark occupied a seat in the dicky—his grace himself was at this moment in the drawing-room, and nothing could exceed his urbanity.", "마크가 사워비와 던스터블 양과 함께 그곳에 도착했을 때—이번 경우에는 던스터블 양이 페이턴에 앉아 오고 마크가 뒷자리를 차지했다—각하 자신은 바로 그 순간 응접실에 있었고, 그의 정중함은 더할 나위가 없었다."]], [["“Oh, Miss Dunstable,”", "“오, 던스터블 양,”"], ["he said, taking that lady by the hand, and leading her up to the fire, “now I feel for the first time that Gatherum Castle has not been built for nothing.”", "그가 그 부인의 손을 잡고 난롯가로 이끌면서 말했다. “개서럼 성이 헛되이 지어진 것이 아니라는 것을 이제야 처음으로 느끼오.”"]], [["“Nobody ever supposed it was, your grace,”", "“헛되이 지어졌다고 생각한 사람은 아무도 없었을 텐데요, 각하,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“I am sure the architect did not think so when his bill was paid.”", "“건축가가 청구서를 받았을 때 분명 그렇게는 생각하지 않았을 거예요.”"], ["And Miss Dunstable put her toes up on the fender to warm them with as much self-possession as though her father had been a duke also, instead of a quack doctor.", "그러고는 던스터블 양은 발을 난로 울에 올려 데웠는데, 그 태연자약함이 마치 자기 아버지도 돌팔이 의사가 아니라 공작이었던 것 같았다."]], [["“We have given the strictest orders about the parrot,”", "“앵무새에 대해서는 아주 엄중하게 일러두었소,”"], ["said the duke—", "공작이 말했다—"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but I have not brought him after all,”", "하지만 결국 데려오지 못했어요,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["—“and I have had an aviary built on purpose,—just such as parrots are used to in their own country.", "—“게다가 새장도 일부러 지어 두었소. 앵무새들이 자기 나라에서 익숙하게 지내는 바로 그런 것으로 말이오."], ["Well, Miss Dunstable, I do call that unkind.", "그런데, 던스터블 양, 그건 정말 매정하다고 하겠소."], ["Is it too late to send for him?”", "지금 사람을 보내 데려오게 하기에는 너무 늦었소?”"]], [["“He and Dr. Easyman are travelling together.", "“그 애는 이지먼 선생과 함께 여행하고 있어요."], ["The truth was, I could not rob the doctor of his companion.”", "실은 선생한테서 그 벗을 빼앗을 수가 없었지요.”"]], [["“Why?", "“어째서?"], ["I have had another aviary built for him.", "그 사람을 위해서도 새장을 하나 더 지어 두었는데."], ["I declare, Miss Dunstable, the honour you are doing me is shorn of half its glory.", "던스터블 양, 단언하지만 당신이 내게 베푸는 영광이 반쯤은 빛을 잃었소."], ["But the poodle—I still trust in the poodle.”", "하지만 푸들은—푸들만은 아직 믿고 있소.”"]], [["“And your grace’s trust shall not in that respect be in vain.", "“그 점에서는 각하의 믿음이 헛되지 않을 거예요."], ["Where is he, I wonder?”", "그런데 그 애가 어디 있을까요?”"], ["And Miss Dunstable looked round as though she expected that somebody would certainly have brought her dog in after her.", "그러면서 던스터블 양은 누군가가 자기 뒤를 따라 개를 안고 들어왔으리라 기대하는 듯 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["“I declare I must go and look for him,—only think if they were to put him among your grace’s dogs,—how his morals would be destroyed!”", "“정말이지 가서 찾아봐야겠어요. 생각만 해도 그래요, 사람들이 그 애를 각하의 개들 사이에 넣어 놓았다면—그 애 품행이 어떻게 망가질까요!”"]], [["“Miss Dunstable, is that intended to be personal?”", "“던스터블 양, 그건 나를 두고 하는 말이오?”"], ["But the lady had turned away from the fire, and the duke was able to welcome his other guests.", "그러나 그 부인은 이미 난롯가에서 몸을 돌렸고, 공작은 다른 손님들을 맞이할 수 있었다."]], [["This he did with much courtesy.", "이 일을 그는 매우 정중하게 해냈다."], ["“Sowerby,”", "“사워비,”"], ["he said, “I am glad to find that you have survived the lecture.", "그가 말했다. “강연에서 살아 돌아온 것을 보니 반갑소."], ["I can assure you I had fears for you.”", "정말이지 나는 자네가 걱정스러웠다오.”"]], [["“I was brought back to life after considerable delay by the administration of tonics at the Dragon of Wantly.", "“원틀리의 용에서 한참 지체된 뒤에 강장제를 투여받고서야 겨우 되살아났습니다."], ["Will your grace allow me to present to you Mr.", "각하께 이 사람 로바츠 씨를 소개해 드려도 될까요."], ["Robarts, who on that occasion was not so fortunate.", "그날 이 사람은 저만큼 운이 좋지 못했습니다."], ["It was found necessary to carry him off to the palace, where he was obliged to undergo very vigorous treatment.”", "결국 주교관으로 실려 가서 아주 격렬한 치료를 받아야 했지요.”"]], [["And then the duke shook hands with Mr. Robarts, assuring him that he was most happy to make his acquaintance.", "그러고서 공작은 로바츠 씨와 악수를 하며, 알게 되어 무척 기쁘다고 말했다."], ["He had often heard of him since he came into the county; and then he asked after Lord Lufton, regretting that he had been unable to induce his lordship to come to Gatherum Castle.", "그가 이 주에 들어온 뒤로 그에 대한 이야기를 여러 번 들었노라고 했다. 그리고 러프턴 경의 안부를 물으면서, 그를 개서럼 성으로 오게 하지 못한 것이 아쉽다고 했다."]], [["“But you had a diversion at the lecture, I am told,”", "“그런데 강연에서 여흥이 하나 있었다고 들었소,”"], ["continued the duke.", "공작이 말을 이었다."], ["“There was a second performer, was there not, who almost eclipsed poor Harold Smith?”", "“두 번째 연사가 있어서 딱한 해럴드 스미스를 거의 무색하게 만들었다지요?”"], ["And then Mr. Sowerby gave an amusing sketch of the little Proudie episode.", "그러자 사워비 씨가 그 작은 프라우디 삽화를 재미있게 그려 보였다."]], [["“It has, of course, ruined your brother-in-law for ever as a lecturer,”", "“그렇다면 물론 자네 매부는 강연자로서는 영영 망한 것이오,”"], ["said the duke, laughing.", "공작이 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“If so, we shall feel ourselves under the deepest obligations to Mrs. Proudie,”", "“그렇다면 저희는 프라우디 부인께 이루 말할 수 없이 깊은 은혜를 입은 셈이지요,”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."], ["And then Harold Smith himself came up and received the duke’s sincere and hearty congratulations on the success of his enterprise at Barchester.", "그러고서 해럴드 스미스 자신이 다가왔고, 바체스터에서 벌인 자기 사업의 성공에 대해 공작의 진심 어리고 따뜻한 축하를 받았다."]], [["Mark Robarts had now turned away, and his attention was suddenly arrested by the loud voice of Miss Dunstable, who had stumbled across some very dear friends in her passage through the rooms, and who by no means hid from the public her delight upon the occasion.", "마크 로바츠는 이제 몸을 돌렸는데, 그의 주의가 갑자기 던스터블 양의 큰 목소리에 붙들렸다. 그녀는 방들을 지나가다가 아주 각별한 벗들과 우연히 마주친 참이었고, 그 기쁨을 조금도 세상에 감추지 않았다."]], [["“Well—well—well!”", "“어머—어머—어머!”"], ["she exclaimed, and then she seized upon a very quiet-looking, well-dressed, attractive young woman who was walking towards her, in company with a gentleman.", "그녀가 소리쳤다. 그러고는 한 신사와 함께 자기 쪽으로 걸어오던, 아주 조용해 보이고 잘 차려입은 매력적인 젊은 부인을 붙잡았다."], ["The gentleman and lady, as it turned out, were husband and wife.", "알고 보니 그 신사와 부인은 부부였다. “어머—어머—어머!"], ["“Well—well—well! I hardly hoped for this.”", "이건 바라지도 못했는데.”"], ["And then she took hold of the lady and kissed her enthusiastically, and after that grasped both the gentleman’s hands, shaking them stoutly.", "그러고 그녀는 그 부인을 붙들고 열렬히 입을 맞추었고, 이어 신사의 두 손을 잡아 힘차게 흔들었다."]], [["“And what a deal I shall have to say to you!”", "“그리고 내가 너한테 할 얘기가 얼마나 많은지!”"], ["she went on.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“You’ll upset all my other plans.", "“네가 내 다른 계획들을 다 뒤엎겠구나."], ["But, Mary, my dear, how long are you going to stay here?", "그런데 메리, 얘, 여기 얼마나 있을 거니?"], ["I go—let me see—I forget when, but it’s all put down in a book upstairs.", "나는—어디 보자—언제 떠나는지 잊어버렸지만, 다 위층 수첩에 적어 두었어."], ["But the next stage is at Mrs. Proudie’s.", "아무튼 다음 행선지는 프라우디 부인 댁이야."], ["I shan’t meet you there, I suppose.", "거기서는 너를 못 만나겠지."], ["And now, Frank, how’s the governor?”", "그래, 프랭크, 아버님은 어떠셔?”"]], [["The gentleman called Frank declared that the governor was all right—“mad about the hounds, of course, you know.”", "프랭크라고 불린 신사는 아버지가 아주 잘 계시다고 말했다.—“물론 사냥개들에 미쳐 계시지요, 아시겠지만.”"]], [["“Well, my dear, that’s better than the hounds being mad about him, like the poor gentleman they’ve put into a statue.", "“그래, 얘, 그게 동상으로 만들어져 버린 그 딱한 신사처럼 사냥개들이 그분에게 미쳐 있는 것보다는 낫지."], ["But talking of hounds, Frank, how badly they manage their foxes at Chaldicotes!", "하지만 사냥개 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 프랭크, 콜디코츠에서는 여우를 얼마나 형편없이 다루는지 몰라!"], ["I was out hunting all one day—”", "나도 하루는 사냥에 나가 봤는데—”"]], [["“You out hunting!”", "“사냥에 나가셨다고요!”"], ["said the lady called Mary.", "메리라고 불린 부인이 말했다."]], [["“And why shouldn’t I go out hunting?", "“내가 사냥에 나가면 안 될 이유가 어디 있니?"], ["I’ll tell you what, Mrs. Proudie was out hunting, too.", "얘기해 주지, 프라우디 부인도 사냥에 나왔더라."], ["But they didn’t catch a single fox; and, if you must have the truth, it seemed to me to be rather slow.”", "그런데 여우는 한 마리도 못 잡았어. 그리고 사실을 말하자면 나한테는 좀 시시해 보였다.”"]], [["“You were in the wrong division of the county,”", "“그 주에서 잘못된 쪽으로 가셨네요,”"], ["said the gentleman called Frank.", "프랭크라고 불린 신사가 말했다."]], [["“Of course I was.", "“당연히 그랬지."], ["When I really want to practise hunting I’ll go to Greshamsbury; not a doubt about that.”", "사냥을 정말로 해 보고 싶어지면 그레셤스베리로 갈 거야. 그건 의심할 나위가 없지.”"]], [["“Or to Boxall Hill,”", "“아니면 박솔 힐로요,”"], ["said the lady; “you’ll find quite as much zeal there as at Greshamsbury.”", "부인이 말했다. “거기서도 그레셤스베리만큼 열성을 보실 거예요.”"]], [["“And more discretion, you should add,”", "“게다가 분별도 더 있다고 덧붙여야지,”"], ["said the gentleman.", "신사가 말했다."]], [["“Ha!", "“하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["ha!”", "하!”"], ["laughed Miss Dunstable; “your discretion indeed!", "던스터블 양이 웃었다. “너희 분별력이라니, 참!"], ["But you have not told me a word about Lady Arabella.”", "그런데 레이디 애러벨라 얘기는 한마디도 안 해 주는구나.”"]], [["“My mother is quite well,”", "“어머니는 아주 잘 계세요,”"], ["said the gentleman.", "신사가 말했다."]], [["“And the doctor?", "“그러면 선생님은?"], ["By-the-by, my dear, I’ve had such a letter from the doctor; only two days ago.", "그러고 보니, 얘, 내가 선생님한테서 그런 편지를 받았단다. 겨우 이틀 전에."], ["I’ll show it you upstairs to-morrow.", "내일 위층에서 보여 주지."], ["But mind, it must be a positive secret.", "하지만 명심해, 절대 비밀이어야 해."], ["If he goes on in this way he’ll get himself into the Tower, or Coventry, or a blue-book, or some dreadful place.”", "그 사람이 이런 식으로 계속하다간 런던 탑이나 코번트리나 청서(靑書)나 무슨 끔찍한 데로 들어가고 말 거야.”"]], [["“Why; what has he said?”", "“어머, 무슨 말씀을 하셨는데요?”"]], [["“Never you mind, Master Frank: I don’t mean to show you the letter, you may be sure of that.", "“넌 알 것 없다, 프랭크 도련님. 너한테 그 편지를 보여 줄 마음은 없으니 그건 확실히 알아 두렴."], ["But if your wife will swear three times on a poker and tongs that she won’t reveal, I’ll show it to her.", "하지만 네 아내가 부지깽이와 부집게에 세 번 맹세해서 발설하지 않겠다고 하면 그 사람에게는 보여 주지."], ["And so you are quite settled at Boxall Hill, are you?”", "그래, 그럼 박솔 힐에 완전히 자리를 잡았니?”"]], [["“Frank’s horses are settled; and the dogs nearly so,”", "“프랭크의 말들은 자리를 잡았고 개들도 거의 잡았어요,”"], ["said Frank’s wife; “but I can’t boast much of anything else yet.”", "프랭크의 아내가 말했다. “하지만 그 밖의 것은 아직 뭐라고 자랑할 게 없네요.”"]], [["“Well, there’s a good time coming.", "“그래, 좋은 시절이 오고 있지."], ["I must go and change my things now.", "나는 이제 가서 옷을 갈아입어야겠다."], ["But, Mary, mind you get near me this evening; I have such a deal to say to you.”", "그런데 메리, 오늘 저녁에는 꼭 내 옆에 붙어 있어. 너한테 할 얘기가 아주 많으니까.”"], ["And then Miss Dunstable marched out of the room.", "그러고서 던스터블 양은 성큼성큼 방을 나섰다."]], [["All this had been said in so loud a voice that it was, as a matter of course, overheard by Mark Robarts—that part of the conversation of course I mean which had come from Miss Dunstable.", "이 모든 말이 어찌나 큰 목소리로 이루어졌던지, 당연한 일로 마크 로바츠에게도 다 들렸다—물론 대화 가운데 던스터블 양의 입에서 나온 부분을 말하는 것이다."], ["And then Mark learned that this was young Frank Gresham of Boxall Hill, son of old Mr. Gresham of Greshamsbury.", "그리고 마크는 이 사람이 박솔 힐의 젊은 프랭크 그레셤이며, 그레셤스베리의 늙은 그레셤 씨의 아들이라는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Frank had lately married a great heiress; a greater heiress, men said, even than Miss Dunstable; and as the marriage was hardly as yet more than six months old the Barsetshire world was still full of it.", "프랭크는 얼마 전 큰 상속녀와 결혼했는데, 사람들 말로는 던스터블 양보다도 더 큰 상속녀였다. 그리고 결혼한 지 겨우 여섯 달이 될까 말까 했으므로 바셋주 세상은 아직도 그 이야기로 가득했다."]], [["“The two heiresses seem to be very loving, don’t they?”", "“두 상속녀가 아주 다정해 보이지 않소?”"], ["said Mr. Supplehouse.", "서플하우스 씨가 말했다."], ["“Birds of a feather flock together, you know.", "“깃이 같은 새는 함께 모인다지요."], ["But they did say some little time ago that young Gresham was to have married Miss Dunstable himself.”", "하지만 얼마 전만 해도 저 젊은 그레셤이 던스터블 양과 결혼하기로 되어 있었다는 말이 있었소.”"]], [["“Miss Dunstable!", "“던스터블 양이라니요!"], ["why she might almost be his mother,”", "그분은 저 사람의 어머니라 해도 될 나이인데요,”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“That makes but little difference.", "“그런 건 별 차이가 없소."], ["He was obliged to marry money, and I believe there is no doubt that he did at one time propose to Miss Dunstable.”", "그는 돈과 결혼해야 할 처지였고, 한때 던스터블 양에게 청혼한 것은 의심할 여지가 없다고 믿소.”"]], [["“I have had a letter from Lufton,”", "“러프턴한테서 편지를 받았어요,”"], ["Mr. Sowerby said to him the next morning.", "이튿날 아침 사워비 씨가 그에게 말했다."], ["“He declares that the delay was all your fault.", "“지체된 것이 다 당신 탓이라고 하더군요."], ["You were to have told Lady Lufton before he did anything, and he was waiting to write about it till he heard from you.", "그가 무슨 일을 하기 전에 당신이 레이디 러프턴에게 말해 두어야 했는데, 그는 당신에게서 소식이 올 때까지 편지 쓰는 것을 기다리고 있었다는 거예요."], ["It seems that you never said a word to her ladyship on the subject.”", "당신이 그 일에 대해 마님께 한마디도 하지 않은 모양이던데요.”"]], [["“I never did, certainly.", "“분명히 한마디도 하지 않았지요."], ["My commission from Lufton was to break the matter to her when I found her in a proper humour for receiving it.", "러프턴이 내게 맡긴 일은 그분이 그 얘기를 받아들일 만한 기분일 때 꺼내라는 것이었어요."], ["If you knew Lady Lufton as well as I do, you would know that it is not every day that she would be in a humour for such tidings.”", "당신이 나만큼 레이디 러프턴을 잘 안다면, 그분이 그런 소식을 받아들일 기분이 되는 날이 아무 날이나 오는 것이 아니라는 걸 알 텐데요.”"]], [["“And so I was to be kept waiting indefinitely because you two between you were afraid of an old woman!", "“그래서 당신들 둘이 늙은 부인 하나를 무서워하는 바람에 나는 언제까지고 기다려야 했군요!"], ["However I have not a word to say against her, and the matter is settled now.”", "하지만 그분께 한마디도 나쁘게 말할 생각은 없고, 그 일은 이제 끝났어요.”"]], [["“Has the farm been sold?”", "“농장이 팔렸나요?”"]], [["“Not a bit of it.", "“전혀 아니에요."], ["The dowager could not bring her mind to suffer such profanation for the Lufton acres, and so she sold five thousand pounds out of the funds and sent the money to Lufton as a present;—sent it to him without saying a word, only hoping that it would suffice for his wants.", "노부인은 러프턴 땅에 그런 신성모독이 벌어지는 것을 차마 견디지 못하고, 공채에서 오천 파운드를 팔아 그 돈을 선물로 러프턴에게 보냈어요. 한마디 말도 없이 보냈지요."], ["I wish I had a mother, I know.”", "그저 그것으로 그가 쓸 데에 넉넉하기를 바란다면서요. 나도 어머니가 있었으면 좋겠어요, 정말이지.”"]], [["Mark found it impossible at the moment to make any remark upon what had been told him, but he felt a sudden qualm of conscience and a wish that he was at Framley instead of at Gatherum Castle at the present moment.", "마크는 지금 들은 이야기에 대해 그 순간 아무 말도 할 수 없었지만, 갑작스러운 양심의 통증을 느꼈고, 지금 개서럼 성이 아니라 프램리에 있었으면 좋겠다고 생각했다. 그는 레이디 러프턴의 수입과 그것이 쓰이는 방식을 꽤 잘 알고 있었다."], ["He knew a good deal respecting Lady Lufton’s income and the manner in which it was spent.", "홀로 사는 부인에게는 아주 넉넉한 액수였지만, 그녀는 거리낌 없이 손이 큰 방식으로 살았다."], ["It was very handsome for a single lady, but then she lived in a free and open-handed style; her charities were noble; there was no reason why she should save money, and her annual income was usually spent within the year.", "그녀의 자선은 훌륭했다. 그녀가 돈을 모아 둘 이유는 없었고, 해마다의 수입은 보통 그 해 안에 다 쓰였다."], ["Mark knew this, and he knew also that nothing short of an impossibility to maintain them would induce her to lessen her charities.", "마크는 이것을 알고 있었고, 또한 그것을 도저히 계속할 수 없게 되는 처지가 아니라면 그녀가 자선을 줄이는 일은 결코 없으리라는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["She had now given away a portion of her principal to save the property of her son—her son, who was so much more opulent than herself,—upon whose means, too, the world made fewer effectual claims.", "그런데 그녀는 이제 아들의 재산을 지키기 위해 자기 원금의 일부를 내놓은 것이다—자기보다 훨씬 부유한 아들, 게다가 세상이 그 재산에 대해 실제로 요구하는 것이 더 적은 아들을 위해서."]], [["And Mark knew, too, something of the purpose for which this money had gone.", "그리고 마크는 이 돈이 어떤 목적에 쓰였는지도 얼마간 알고 있었다."], ["There had been unsettled gambling claims between Sowerby and Lord Lufton, originating in affairs of the turf.", "사워비와 러프턴 경 사이에는 결말이 나지 않은 도박 채권이 있었는데, 경마에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["It had now been going on for four years, almost from the period when Lord Lufton had become of age.", "그것은 이제 사 년째 이어져 왔으니, 거의 러프턴 경이 성년이 된 무렵부터였다."], ["He had before now spoken to Robarts on the matter with much bitter anger, alleging that Mr. Sowerby was treating him unfairly, nay, dishonestly—that he was claiming money that was not due to him; and then he declared more than once that he would bring the matter before the Jockey Club.", "그는 전에도 이 문제로 로바츠에게 몹시 씁쓸한 노여움을 터뜨리며, 사워비 씨가 자기를 부당하게, 아니 부정직하게 대하고 있다고—받을 권리가 없는 돈을 요구하고 있다고 주장한 적이 있었다."], ["But Mark, knowing that Lord Lufton was not clear-sighted in these matters, and believing it to be impossible that Mr.", "그리고 그때 그는 이 일을 자키 클럽에 부치겠다고 여러 번 선언했다."], ["Sowerby should actually endeavour to defraud his friend, had smoothed down the young lord’s anger, and recommended him to get the case referred to some private arbiter.", "그러나 마크는 러프턴 경이 이런 문제에 밝지 못하다는 것을 알았고, 사워비 씨가 실제로 자기 벗을 속이려 할 리는 없다고 믿었으므로, 젊은 경의 노여움을 달래고 사건을 어떤 사사로운 중재자에게 맡기라고 권했다. 이 모든 일이 나중에 로바츠와 사워비 씨 자신 사이에서 논의되었고, 거기서 두 사람의 친분이 시작되었다."], ["All this had afterwards been discussed between Robarts and Mr.", "사건은 그렇게 부쳐졌고, 중재자는 사워비 씨가 지명했다."], ["Sowerby himself, and hence had originated their intimacy. The matter was so referred, Mr.", "그리고 판정이 자기에게 불리하게 나왔을 때 러프턴 경은 그것을 담담히 받아들였다."], ["Sowerby naming the referee; and Lord Lufton, when the matter was given against him, took it easily.", "그때는 이미 노여움이 가셔 있었다. “저 사람들 사이에서 나는 깨끗이 털렸어요,”"], ["His anger was over by that time.", "그는 마크에게 웃으며 말했다."], ["“I’ve been clean done among them,”", "“하지만 대수롭지 않아요."], ["he said to Mark, laughing; “but it does not signify; a man must pay for his experience.", "사람은 자기 경험에 값을 치러야 하는 법이지요."], ["Of course, Sowerby thinks it all right; I am bound to suppose so.”", "물론 사워비는 다 옳다고 생각할 테고, 나도 그렇다고 여길 수밖에요.”"], ["And then there had been some further delay as to the amount, and part of the money had been paid to a third person, and a bill had been given, and heaven and the Jews only know how much money Lord Lufton had paid in all; and now it was ended by his handing over to some wretched villain of a money-dealer, on behalf of Mr.", "그러고 나서 금액을 두고 또 지체가 있었고, 돈의 일부는 제삼자에게 지불되었고, 어음이 하나 발행되었으며, 러프턴 경이 도대체 모두 얼마를 지불했는지는 하늘과 유대인들만이 알 것이다. 그리고 이제 그것은, 사워비 씨를 위해 어떤 형편없는 고리대금업자 악당에게 오천 파운드라는 엄청난 금액을 넘겨주는 것으로 끝이 났다."], ["Sowerby, the enormous sum of five thousand pounds, which had been deducted from the means of his mother, Lady Lufton!", "그 돈은 그의 어머니 레이디 러프턴의 재산에서 떼어 낸 것이었다!"]], [["Mark, as he thought of all this, could not but feel a certain animosity against Mr. Sowerby—could not but suspect that he was a bad man.", "마크는 이 모든 것을 생각하면서 사워비 씨에 대해 어떤 반감을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다—그가 나쁜 사람이라고 의심하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Nay, must he not have known that he was very bad?", "아니, 그가 매우 나쁘다는 것을 그는 알고 있었던 것이 아닐까?"], ["And yet he continued walking with him through the duke’s grounds, still talking about Lord Lufton’s affairs, and still listening with interest to what Sowerby told him of his own.", "그런데도 그는 공작의 정원을 그와 함께 계속 거닐며, 여전히 러프턴 경의 일에 대해 이야기하고, 사워비가 자기 일에 대해 들려주는 것에 여전히 관심을 갖고 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“No man was ever robbed as I have been,”", "“나처럼 강탈당한 사람은 없어요,”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But I shall win through yet, in spite of them all.", "“하지만 나는 그자들 모두를 제치고 끝내 이겨 낼 겁니다."], ["But those Jews, Mark”—he had become very intimate with him in these latter days—“whatever you do, keep clear of them.", "그렇지만 그 유대인들 말이오, 마크”—요 며칠 사이 그는 그와 아주 친해져 있었다—“당신은 무슨 일이 있어도 그자들과는 얽히지 마세요."], ["Why, I could paper a room with their signatures; and yet I never had a claim upon one of them, though they always have claims on me!”", "아니, 나는 그자들 서명으로 방 하나를 도배할 수도 있어요. 그런데 나는 그자들 가운데 누구에게도 청구할 것이 하나도 없었고, 그자들은 늘 내게 청구할 것이 있지요!”"]], [["I have said above that this affair of Lord Lufton’s was ended; but it now appeared to Mark that it was not quite ended.", "위에서 나는 러프턴 경의 이 일이 끝났다고 말했다. 그러나 이제 마크에게는 그것이 아주 끝난 것이 아닌 듯이 보였다."], ["“Tell Lufton, you know,”", "“러프턴에게 전해 주세요,”"], ["said Sowerby, “that every bit of paper with his name has been taken up, except what that ruffian Tozer has.", "사워비가 말했다. “그의 이름이 적힌 종이는 저 토저라는 악당이 가진 것만 빼고 다 회수되었다고요."], ["Tozer may have one bill, I believe,—something that was not given up when it was renewed.", "토저가 어음 한 장은 갖고 있을 겁니다. 갱신할 때 넘겨받지 못한 것이지요."], ["But I’ll make my lawyer Gumption get that up.", "하지만 내 변호사 검션에게 그것도 회수하게 하겠어요."], ["It may cost ten pounds or twenty pounds, not more.", "십 파운드나 이십 파운드쯤 들 테고, 그 이상은 아닐 거예요."], ["You’ll remember that when you see Lufton, will you?”", "러프턴을 만나면 그것을 기억해 주겠지요?”"]], [["“You’ll see Lufton in all probability before I shall.”", "“러프턴은 아마 나보다 당신이 먼저 만날 텐데요.”"]], [["“Oh, did I not tell you?", "“아, 말하지 않았던가요?"], ["He’s going to Framley Court at once; you’ll find him there when you return.”", "그는 곧 프램리 코트로 갑니다. 당신이 돌아가면 거기 있을 거예요.”"]], [["“Find him at Framley!”", "“프램리에 와 있다니요!”"]], [["“Yes; this little cadeau from his mother has touched his filial heart.", "“그렇다니까요. 어머니가 보낸 이 작은 선물이 그의 효심을 건드린 거지요."], ["He is rushing home to Framley to pay back the dowager’s hard moidores in soft caresses.", "노부인의 딱딱한 금화를 부드러운 애교로 갚으러 프램리로 달려가는 겁니다."], ["I wish I had a mother; I know that.”", "나도 어머니가 있었으면 좋겠어요. 정말 그래요.”"]], [["And Mark still felt that he feared Mr. Sowerby, but he could not make up his mind to break away from him.", "그리고 마크는 여전히 사워비 씨가 무섭다고 느끼면서도, 그와 관계를 끊기로 마음을 정할 수는 없었다."]], [["And there was much talk of politics just then at the castle.", "그리고 그때 성에서는 정치 이야기가 많았다."], ["Not that the duke joined in it with any enthusiasm.", "공작이 무슨 열의를 갖고 거기 끼어든 것은 아니었다."], ["He was a Whig—a huge mountain of a colossal Whig—all the world knew that.", "그는 휘그였다—거대한 산더미 같은 어마어마한 휘그였다. 그것은 온 세상이 알고 있었다."], ["No opponent would have dreamed of tampering with his whiggery, nor would any brother Whig have dreamed of doubting it.", "어떤 반대파도 그의 휘그주의를 건드릴 꿈을 꾸지 않았을 것이고, 같은 휘그 형제라도 그것을 의심할 꿈을 꾸지 않았을 것이다."], ["But he was a Whig who gave very little practical support to any set of men, and very little practical opposition to any other set.", "그러나 그는 어떤 무리에게도 실제로는 거의 지원을 하지 않고, 다른 어떤 무리에게도 실제로는 거의 반대를 하지 않는 휘그였다."], ["He was above troubling himself with such sublunar matters.", "그는 그런 지상의 일들로 자신을 번거롭게 하는 것 위에 있었다."], ["At election time he supported, and always carried, Whig candidates; and in return he had been appointed lord lieutenant of the county by one Whig minister, and had received the Garter from another.", "선거 때면 그는 휘그 후보를 지원했고 언제나 당선시켰다. 그 대가로 그는 어느 휘그 대신에게서 주 지사로 임명되었고, 또 다른 대신에게서 가터 훈장을 받았다."], ["But these things were matters of course to a Duke of Omnium.", "그러나 이런 것들은 옴니엄 공작에게는 당연한 일이었다."], ["He was born to be a lord lieutenant and a knight of the Garter.", "그는 주 지사와 가터 훈장 기사가 되도록 태어난 사람이었다."]], [["But not the less on account of his apathy, or rather quiescence, was it thought that Gatherum Castle was a fitting place in which politicians might express to each other their present hopes and future aims, and concoct together little plots in a half-serious and half-mocking way.", "그러나 그의 무심함, 아니 오히려 조용함 때문에 오히려 더욱, 개서럼 성은 정치가들이 서로 지금의 희망과 앞날의 목표를 털어놓고 반은 진지하고 반은 조롱하는 식으로 함께 작은 음모를 꾸미기에 알맞은 장소로 여겨졌다."], ["Indeed it was hinted that Mr. Supplehouse and Harold Smith, with one or two others, were at Gatherum for this express purpose.", "실제로 서플하우스 씨와 해럴드 스미스, 그리고 한둘 다른 이들이 바로 이 목적으로 개서럼에 와 있다는 말이 나돌았다."], ["Mr. Fothergill, too, was a noted politician, and was supposed to know the duke’s mind well; and Mr.", "포더길 씨도 이름난 정치가였고 공작의 마음을 잘 안다고 알려져 있었다."], ["Green Walker, the nephew of the marchioness, was a young man whom the duke desired to have brought forward.", "그리고 후작부인의 조카인 그린 워커 씨는 공작이 앞으로 내세우고 싶어 하는 젊은이였다."], ["Mr. Sowerby also was the duke’s own member, and so the occasion suited well for the interchange of a few ideas.", "사워비 씨도 공작이 앉힌 하원 의원이었으니, 몇 가지 생각을 주고받기에 이 기회는 잘 맞았다."]], [["The then prime minister, angry as many men were with him, had not been altogether unsuccessful.", "당시의 총리는 많은 사람이 그에게 화를 내고 있었지만 아주 실패한 것은 아니었다."], ["He had brought the Russian war to a close, which, if not glorious, was at any rate much more so than Englishmen at one time had ventured to hope.", "그는 러시아 전쟁을 끝맺었는데, 영광스럽지는 않았다 해도 적어도 한때 영국인들이 감히 바랐던 것보다는 훨씬 나았다."], ["And he had had wonderful luck in that Indian mutiny.", "그리고 그는 인도의 그 반란에서 놀랄 만한 운을 누렸다."], ["It is true that many of those even who voted with him would declare that this was in no way attributable to him.", "물론 그와 함께 표를 던진 이들조차 그것이 결코 그의 공이 아니라고 말했다."], ["Great men had risen in India and done all that.", "인도에서 위인들이 일어나 그 모든 일을 해냈다는 것이다."], ["Even his minister there, the governor whom he had sent out, was not allowed in those days any credit for the success which was achieved under his orders.", "그가 파견한 총독, 곧 그곳에 있는 그의 대신조차도 그의 명령 아래 이루어진 성공에 대해 당시에는 아무 공로도 인정받지 못했다."], ["There was great reason to doubt the man at the helm.", "배의 키를 잡은 사람을 의심할 큰 이유가 있었다."], ["But nevertheless he had been lucky.", "그러나 그럼에도 그는 운이 좋았다."], ["There is no merit in a public man like success!", "공적 인물에게 성공만 한 공적은 없다!"]], [["But now, when the evil days were well nigh over, came the question whether he had not been too successful.", "그러나 이제 험한 날들이 거의 지나갔을 때, 그가 너무 성공한 것이 아니냐는 문제가 나왔다."], ["When a man has nailed fortune to his chariot-wheels he is apt to travel about in rather a proud fashion.", "사람이 행운을 자기 전차 바퀴에 못 박아 두면 자못 오만한 모습으로 돌아다니기 쉬운 법이다."], ["There are servants who think that their masters cannot do without them; and the public also may occasionally have some such servant.", "주인이 자기 없이는 아무것도 못 한다고 여기는 하인들이 있다. 그리고 대중에게도 이따금 그런 하인이 있을 수 있다."], ["What if this too successful minister were one of them!", "만일 이 너무 성공한 대신이 그런 하인 가운데 하나라면?"]], [["And then a discreet, commonplace, zealous member of the Lower House does not like to be jeered at, when he does his duty by his constituents and asks a few questions.", "그리고 신중하고 평범하고 열심인 하원 의원은 자기 선거구민에 대한 의무를 다하며 몇 가지 질문을 할 때 비웃음을 받는 것을 좋아하지 않는다."], ["An all-successful minister who cannot keep his triumph to himself, but must needs drive about in a proud fashion, laughing at commonplace zealous members—laughing even occasionally at members who are by no means commonplace, which is outrageous!—may it not be as well to ostracize him for awhile?", "모든 일에 성공한 대신이 그 승리를 혼자 삭이지 못하고 굳이 오만한 모습으로 돌아다니면서 평범하고 열심인 의원들을 비웃는다면—때로는 결코 평범하지 않은 의원들까지 비웃는다면, 이건 괘씸한 일이다!—잠시 그를 추방해 두는 것이 좋지 않을까?"]], [["“Had we not better throw in our shells against him?”", "“그에게 우리 포탄을 던져 넣는 게 좋지 않겠나?”"], ["says Mr. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 씨가 말한다."]], [["“Let us throw in our shells, by all means,”", "“무슨 일이 있어도 우리 포탄을 던져 넣자고,”"], ["says Mr. Supplehouse, mindful as Juno of his despised charms.", "유노처럼 자기가 무시당한 매력을 잊지 않고 있는 서플하우스 씨가 말한다."], ["And when Mr. Supplehouse declares himself an enemy, men know how much it means.", "그리고 서플하우스 씨가 스스로 적이라고 선언하면 사람들은 그것이 얼마나 큰 뜻인지 안다."], ["They know that that much-belaboured head of affairs must succumb to the terrible blows which are now in store for him.", "그 숱하게 두들겨 맞은 국정의 수장이 이제 준비되어 있는 무서운 타격에 굴복하지 않을 수 없다는 것을 안다. “그렇지, 우리 포탄을 던져 넣는 거야.”"], ["“Yes, we will throw in our shells.” And Mr.", "그러면서 서플하우스 씨는 눈을 번득이며 의자에서 일어난다."], ["Supplehouse rises from his chair with gleaming eyes.", "“그리스에 그만한 아들이 없단 말인가?"], ["“Has not Greece as noble sons as him?", "아니, 훨씬 더 훌륭한 아들이 있지. 저 사람은 배신자야."], ["ay, and much nobler, traitor that he is.", "사람은 그 벗을 보고 판단해야 하는 법이지,”"], ["We must judge a man by his friends,” says Mr.", "서플하우스 씨가 말한다."], ["Supplehouse; and he points away to the East, where our dear allies the French are supposed to live, and where our head of affairs is supposed to have too close an intimacy.", "그리고 그는 동쪽을 가리킨다. 우리의 친애하는 동맹인 프랑스인들이 산다고 하는 곳이자, 우리 국정의 수장이 너무 가까운 친교를 맺고 있다고 하는 곳이다."]], [["They all understand this, even Mr. Green Walker.", "이것은 모두가, 그린 워커 씨까지도 알아듣는다."], ["“I don’t know that he is any good to any of us at all, now,”", "“그가 이제 우리 누구에게도 조금이라도 좋은 구실을 한다고는 생각하지 않네,”"], ["says the talented member for the Crewe Junction.", "크루 정션의 그 재능 있는 의원이 말한다."], ["“He’s a great deal too uppish to suit my book; and I know a great many people that think so too.", "“내 취향에는 너무 거만해. 그렇게 생각하는 사람을 나는 아주 많이 알고 있지."], ["There’s my uncle—”", "우리 삼촌만 해도—”"]], [["“He’s the best fellow in the world,” said Mr.", "“그분은 세상에서 가장 좋은 분이지요,”"], ["Fothergill, who felt, perhaps, that that coming revelation about Mr.", "포더길 씨가 말했다. 그린 워커 씨의 삼촌에 관해 나오려는 그 폭로가 어쩌면 자기들에게 쓸모없을지도 모른다고 느꼈던 것이다."], ["Green Walker’s uncle might not be of use to them; “but the fact is one gets tired of the same man always.", "“하지만 사실은 같은 사람만 늘 있으면 싫증이 나는 법이에요."], ["One does not like partridge every day.", "자고새를 매일 먹고 싶은 사람은 없거든요."], ["As for me, I have nothing to do with it myself; but I would certainly like to change the dish.”", "나로서야 이 일에 아무 관계도 없지만, 요리를 바꾸는 것은 확실히 마음에 들 것 같군요.”"]], [["“If we’re merely to do as we are bid, and have no voice of our own, I don’t see what’s the good of going to the shop at all,”", "“우리가 그저 시키는 대로만 하고 우리 목소리를 낼 수 없다면, 아예 그 가게에 갈 이유가 뭔지 모르겠네,”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Not the least use,”", "“조금도 쓸모없지,”"], ["said Mr. Supplehouse.", "서플하우스 씨가 말했다."], ["“We are false to our constituents in submitting to such a dominion.”", "“그런 지배에 굴복하는 것은 우리 선거구민에 대한 배신이야.”"]], [["“Let’s have a change, then,”", "“그럼 한번 바꿔 보자고,”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."], ["“The matter’s pretty much in our own hands.”", "“일은 꽤 우리 손안에 있으니.”"]], [["“Altogether,”", "“전적으로 그렇지,”"], ["said Mr. Green Walker.", "그린 워커 씨가 말했다."], ["“That’s what my uncle always says.”", "“우리 삼촌도 늘 그렇게 말한다네.”"]], [["“The Manchester men will only be too happy for the chance,”", "“맨체스터 파는 그 기회를 너무나 기뻐할 걸세,”"], ["said Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스가 말했다."]], [["“And as for the high and dry gentlemen,” said Mr.", "“그리고 고답파 신사들이야,”"], ["Sowerby, “it’s not very likely that they will object to pick up the fruit when we shake the tree.”", "사워비 씨가 말했다. “우리가 나무를 흔들면 떨어진 열매를 주워 가는 것을 마다할 것 같지 않네.”"]], [["“As to picking up the fruit, that’s as may be,”", "“열매를 주워 가는 문제라면, 그건 어떻게 될지 모르지,”"], ["said Mr. Supplehouse.", "서플하우스 씨가 말했다."], ["Was he not the man to save the nation; and if so, why should he not pick up the fruit himself?", "그가 나라를 구할 사람이 아니던가? 그렇다면 왜 그가 스스로 열매를 주워서는 안 되는가?"], ["Had not the greatest power in the country pointed him out as such a saviour?", "이 나라의 가장 큰 권력이 그를 바로 그런 구원자로 지목하지 않았던가?"], ["What though the country at the present moment needed no more saving, might there not, nevertheless, be a good time coming?", "지금 이 순간 나라가 더 구원받을 것이 없다 해도, 그럼에도 좋은 시절이 오고 있는 것은 아닐까?"], ["Were there not rumours of other wars still prevalent—if indeed the actual war then going on was being brought to a close without his assistance by some other species of salvation?", "다른 전쟁들의 소문이 아직 돌고 있지 않은가—지금 진행 중인 저 전쟁이 그의 도움 없이 다른 종류의 구원으로 끝맺어지고 있다면 말이지만."], ["He thought of that country to which he had pointed, and of that friend of his enemies, and remembered that there might be still work for a mighty saviour.", "그는 자기가 가리켰던 그 나라를, 그리고 자기 적들의 그 벗을 생각하고, 위대한 구원자가 할 일이 아직 남아 있을지 모른다고 떠올렸다."], ["The public mind was now awake, and understood what it was about.", "대중의 마음은 이제 깨어 있었고 무슨 일인지 알고 있었다."], ["When a man gets into his head an idea that the public voice calls for him, it is astonishing how great becomes his trust in the wisdom of the public.", "사람이 대중의 목소리가 자기를 부른다는 생각을 머릿속에 넣게 되면, 대중의 지혜에 대한 그의 신뢰가 얼마나 커지는지 놀라울 정도다."], ["Vox populi vox Dei.", "「복스 포풀리 복스 데이」."], ["“Has it not been so always?”", "“언제나 그러지 않았던가?”"], ["he says to himself, as he gets up and as he goes to bed. And then Mr.", "그는 일어나면서도, 잠자리에 들면서도 스스로에게 그렇게 말한다."], ["Supplehouse felt that he was the master mind there at Gatherum Castle, and that those there were all puppets in his hand.", "그러고서 서플하우스 씨는 자기가 여기 개서럼 성의 주인 되는 두뇌이며 여기 있는 자들은 모두 자기 손안의 인형이라고 느꼈다."], ["It is such a pleasant thing to feel that one’s friends are puppets, and that the strings are in one’s own possession.", "자기 벗들이 인형이고 그 줄이 자기 손에 있다고 느끼는 것은 참 즐거운 일이다."], ["But what if Mr. Supplehouse himself were a puppet?", "그러나 만일 서플하우스 씨 자신이 인형이라면?"]], [["Some months afterwards, when the much-belaboured head of affairs was in very truth made to retire, when unkind shells were thrown in against him in great numbers, when he exclaimed, “Et tu, Brute!” till the words were stereotyped upon his lips, all men in all places talked much about the great Gatherum Castle confederation.", "몇 달 뒤에 그 숱하게 두들겨 맞은 국정의 수장이 정말로 물러나게 되었을 때, 매정한 포탄이 그에게 무수히 던져졌을 때, 그가 「에트 투, 브루테!」를 입술에 새겨질 만큼 되풀이해 외쳤을 때, 어디서나 모든 사람이 저 위대한 개서럼 성 연합에 대해 많은 이야기를 했다."], ["The Duke of Omnium, the world said, had taken into his high consideration the state of affairs, and seeing with his eagle’s eye that the welfare of his countrymen at large required that some great step should be initiated, he had at once summoned to his mansion many members of the Lower House, and some also of the House of Lords,—mention was here especially made of the all-venerable and all-wise Lord Boanerges; and men went on to say that there, in deep conclave, he had made known to them his views.", "세상 말로는, 옴니엄 공작이 나라의 사정을 높이 헤아려 보고 나라의 동포 전체의 복리를 위해 어떤 큰 조치를 시작할 필요가 있음을 그 독수리 같은 눈으로 알아보고서, 곧 자기 저택으로 하원 의원 여러 명과 상원 의원 몇 명을 불러 모았다는 것이었다—이 대목에서 특히 온 세상이 존경하고 온 세상이 지혜롭다 하는 보아네르게스 경이 언급되었다. 그리고 사람들은 이어서 말하기를, 거기서 그가 깊은 밀회 가운데 자기 견해를 그들에게 알렸다고 했다."], ["It was thus agreed that the head of affairs, Whig as he was, must fall.", "그리하여 국정의 수장이 휘그이기는 하나 무너져야 한다는 데 합의가 이루어졌다."], ["The country required it, and the duke did his duty.", "나라가 그것을 요구했고, 공작은 자기 의무를 다한 것이다."], ["This was the beginning, the world said, of that celebrated confederation, by which the ministry was overturned, and—as the Goody Twoshoes added,—the country saved.", "세상 말로는 이것이 저 유명한 연합의 시작이었으며, 그 연합에 의해 내각이 뒤집히고—《구디 투슈즈》가 덧붙인 대로—나라가 구원되었다."], ["But the Jupiter took all the credit to itself; and the Jupiter was not far wrong.", "그러나 《주피터》는 그 공을 다 자기 것으로 돌렸다. 그리고 《주피터》가 아주 틀린 것도 아니었다."], ["All the credit was due to the Jupiter—in that, as in everything else.", "그 공은 모두 《주피터》의 것이었다—이 일에서나 다른 모든 일에서나."]], [["In the meantime the Duke of Omnium entertained his guests in the quiet princely style, but did not condescend to have much conversation on politics either with Mr. Supplehouse or with Mr.", "그동안 옴니엄 공작은 조용하고 제후다운 방식으로 손님들을 대접했으나, 서플하우스 씨나 해럴드 스미스 씨와 정치 이야기를 많이 나누는 데까지 몸을 낮추지는 않았다."], ["Harold Smith. And as for Lord Boanerges, he spent the morning on which the above-described conversation took place in teaching Miss Dunstable to blow soap-bubbles on scientific principles.", "그리고 보아네르게스 경은, 위에서 그려 놓은 대화가 있던 그날 아침을 던스터블 양에게 비눗방울을 과학적 원리에 따라 부는 법을 가르치며 보냈다."]], [["“Dear, dear!”", "“어머나, 어머나!”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, as sparks of knowledge came flying in upon her mind.", "지식의 불꽃이 마음속으로 날아 들어오는 것을 느끼며 던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“I always thought that a soap-bubble was a soap-bubble, and I never asked the reason why.", "“저는 늘 비눗방울은 그저 비눗방울이라고 생각했고, 그 이유를 물어본 적이 없어요."], ["One doesn’t, you know, my lord.”", "사람들은 안 묻잖아요, 경.”"]], [["“Pardon me, Miss Dunstable,”", "“미안하지만, 던스터블 양,” 늙은 경이 말했다."], ["said the old lord, “one does; but nine hundred and ninety-nine do not.”", "“묻는 사람은 있소. 다만 구백구십구 명은 묻지 않지요.”"]], [["“And the nine hundred and ninety-nine have the best of it,”", "“그리고 그 구백구십구 명이 더 낫게 사는 거예요,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“What pleasure can one have in a ghost after one has seen the phosphorus rubbed on?”", "“인(燐)을 문질러 바르는 것을 본 뒤에 유령이 무슨 재미가 있겠어요?”"]], [["“Quite true, my dear lady.", "“지당한 말이오, 부인."], ["‘If ignorance be bliss, ’tis folly to be wise.’ It all lies in the ‘if.’”", "‘무지가 축복이라면 현명해지는 것은 어리석은 짓이다.’ 모든 것은 그 ‘라면’에 달려 있소.”"]], [["Then Miss Dunstable began to sing:—", "그러자 던스터블 양이 노래를 부르기 시작했다.—"]], [["“‘What tho’ I trace each herb and flower That sips the morning dew—’", "“‘아침 이슬을 마시는 / 풀과 꽃을 낱낱이 더듬는다 해도—’"]], [["—you know the rest, my lord.”", "—나머지는 아시죠, 경."]], [["Lord Boanerges did know almost everything, but he did not know that; and so Miss Dunstable went on:—", "보아네르게스 경은 거의 모든 것을 알고 있었으나 그것은 몰랐다. 그래서 던스터블 양이 이어 불렀다.—"]], [["“‘Did I not own Jehovah’s power How vain were all I knew.’”", "“‘여호와의 권능을 내 것으로 알지 못한다면 / 내가 아는 모든 것이 얼마나 헛되랴.’”"]], [["“Exactly, exactly, Miss Dunstable,”", "“정확하오, 정확하오, 던스터블 양,”"], ["said his lordship; “but why not own the power and trace the flower as well?", "경이 말했다. “그런데 권능을 내 것으로 알면서 꽃도 더듬으면 안 될 이유가 무엇이오?"], ["perhaps one might help the other.”", "어쩌면 하나가 다른 하나를 도울지도 모르지요.”"]], [["Upon the whole I am afraid that Lord Boanerges got the best of it.", "전체적으로 보아 나는 보아네르게스 경이 이겼다고 생각한다."], ["But then that is his line.", "하지만 그것이야 그의 본업이다."], ["He has been getting the best of it all his life.", "그는 평생 이기기만 해 왔다."]], [["It was observed by all that the duke was especially attentive to young Mr.", "공작이 젊은 프랭크 그레셤 씨에게 특별히 친절하다는 것을 모두가 알아보았다."], ["Frank Gresham, the gentleman on whom and on whose wife Miss Dunstable had seized so vehemently.", "던스터블 양이 그토록 격렬하게 붙들었던 그 신사와 그 아내의 그 신사다."], ["This Mr. Gresham was the richest commoner in the county, and it was rumoured that at the next election he would be one of the members for the East Riding.", "이 그레셤 씨는 이 주에서 가장 부유한 평민이었고, 다음 선거에서는 이스트 라이딩의 의원 중 한 사람이 될 것이라는 소문이 있었다."], ["Now the duke had little or nothing to do with the East Riding, and it was well known that young Gresham would be brought forward as a strong conservative.", "그런데 공작은 이스트 라이딩과는 관계가 거의 또는 전혀 없었고, 젊은 그레셤이 강경한 보수파로 나설 것이라는 것도 잘 알려져 있었다."], ["But, nevertheless, his acres were so extensive and his money so plentiful that he was worth a duke’s notice. Mr.", "그러나 그럼에도 그의 땅이 그토록 넓고 그의 돈이 그토록 넉넉했으므로 그는 공작이 눈여겨볼 만한 값이 있었다."], ["Sowerby also was almost more than civil to him, as was natural, seeing that this very young man by a mere scratch of his pen could turn a scrap of paper into a bank-note of almost fabulous value.", "사워비 씨도 그에게 거의 지나칠 만큼 예의를 다했는데, 그것도 당연한 일이었다. 이 아주 젊은 사람이 펜을 한 번 긁는 것만으로 종이 한 조각을 거의 신화적인 값의 은행권으로 바꿀 수 있었으니 말이다."]], [["“So you have the East Barsetshire hounds at Boxall Hill; have you not?”", "“그래서 동부 바셋주 사냥개들을 박솔 힐에 두고 있다지요?”"], ["said the duke.", "공작이 말했다."]], [["“The hounds are there,”", "“사냥개들은 거기 있습니다,”"], ["said Frank.", "프랭크가 말했다."], ["“But I am not the master.”", "“하지만 제가 주인은 아닙니다.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“오!"], ["I understood—”", "나는 알기로는—”"]], [["“My father has them.", "“아버지가 가지고 계십니다."], ["But he finds Boxall Hill more centrical than Greshamsbury.", "다만 아버지는 그레셤스베리보다 박솔 힐이 더 중심에 있다고 여기시지요."], ["The dogs and horses have to go shorter distances.”", "개와 말이 더 짧은 거리를 가면 되니까요.”"]], [["“Boxall Hill is very centrical.”", "“박솔 힐은 아주 중심에 있소.”"]], [["“Oh, exactly!”", "“오, 그렇습니다!”"]], [["“And your young gorse coverts are doing well?”", "“그리고 새로 심은 가시금작화 은신처는 잘되고 있소?”"]], [["“Pretty well—gorse won’t thrive everywhere, I find.", "“그저 그렇습니다—가시금작화가 어디서나 잘 자라는 것은 아니더군요."], ["I wish it would.”", "그랬으면 좋겠는데요.”"]], [["“That’s just what I say to Fothergill; and then where there’s much woodland you can’t get the vermin to leave it.”", "“내가 포더길에게 늘 하는 말이 바로 그것이오. 그리고 삼림이 많은 곳에서는 해로운 짐승들을 거기서 나오게 할 수가 없소.”"]], [["“But we haven’t a tree at Boxall Hill,”", "“하지만 박솔 힐에는 나무가 한 그루도 없어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Gresham.", "그레셤 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Ah, yes; you’re new there, certainly; you’ve enough of it at Greshamsbury in all conscience. There’s a larger extent of wood there than we have; isn’t there, Fothergill?”", "“아, 그렇지. 거기는 아직 새로 자리 잡은 곳이니. 그레셤스베리에는 아무리 봐도 넉넉하지요. 거기 숲이 우리 것보다 넓고. 그렇지 않소, 포더길?”"]], [["Mr. Fothergill said that the Greshamsbury woods were very extensive, but that, perhaps, he thought—", "포더길 씨는 그레셤스베리 숲이 매우 넓다고 하면서도, 다만 아마 자기 생각으로는—"]], [["“Oh, ah! I know,”", "“오, 아!"], ["said the duke.", "알고 있소,”"], ["“The Black Forest in its old days was nothing to Gatherum woods, according to Fothergill.", "공작이 말했다. “포더길에 따르면 옛날의 검은 숲도 개서럼 숲에 대면 아무것도 아니었지."], ["And then again, nothing in East Barsetshire could be equal to anything in West Barsetshire.", "그리고 또, 동부 바셋주에 있는 어떤 것도 서부 바셋주에 있는 것과 같을 수 없고."], ["Isn’t that it; eh, Fothergill?”", "그렇지 않소, 응, 포더길?”"]], [["Mr. Fothergill professed that he had been brought up in that faith and intended to die in it.", "포더길 씨는 자기가 그 믿음 속에서 자랐고 그 믿음 속에서 죽을 작정이라고 밝혔다."]], [["“Your exotics at Boxall Hill are very fine, magnificent!”", "“박솔 힐의 외래 식물들은 아주 훌륭합니다. 장관이지요!”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I’d sooner have one full-grown oak standing in its pride alone,”", "“저는 세상의 모든 외래 식물보다 다 자란 참나무 한 그루가 홀로 당당히 서 있는 것을 택하겠습니다,”"], ["said young Gresham, rather grandiloquently, “than all the exotics in the world.”", "젊은 그레셤이 다소 호언장담하듯 말했다."]], [["“They’ll come in due time,”", "“때가 되면 그것들도 자랄 거요,”"], ["said the duke.", "공작이 말했다."]], [["“But the due time won’t be in my days.", "“하지만 그 때는 제 생전에 오지 않을 겁니다."], ["And so they’re going to cut down Chaldicotes forest, are they, Mr. Sowerby?”", "그런데 콜디코츠 숲을 베어 낸다지요, 사워비 씨?”"]], [["“Well, I can’t tell you that.", "“글쎄, 그건 나도 모르겠어요."], ["They are going to disforest it.", "삼림 지정을 해제한다고 하더군요."], ["I have been ranger since I was twenty-two, and I don’t yet know whether that means cutting down.”", "나는 스물두 살 때부터 산림 감독관이었는데도 그것이 베어 낸다는 뜻인지 아직 모르겠어요.”"]], [["“Not only cutting down, but rooting up,”", "“베어 내는 것만이 아니라 뿌리째 뽑아냅니다,”"], ["said Mr. Fothergill.", "포더길 씨가 말했다."]], [["“It’s a murderous shame,”", "“살인 같은 짓입니다,”"], ["said Frank Gresham; “and I will say one thing, I don’t think any but a Whig government would do it.”", "프랭크 그레셤이 말했다. “그리고 한마디 하자면, 휘그 정부가 아니면 어떤 정부도 그런 일을 하지 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["“Ha, ha, ha!”", "“하, 하, 하!”"], ["laughed his grace.", "각하가 웃었다."], ["“At any rate I’m sure of this,”", "“적어도 이것만은 분명하오,”"], ["he said, “that if a conservative government did do so, the Whigs would be just as indignant as you are now.”", "그가 말했다. “보수 정부가 그렇게 했다면 휘그도 지금 당신만큼 분개했을 거라는 것 말이오.”"]], [["“I’ll tell you what you ought to do, Mr.", "“당신이 어떻게 하면 좋을지 말해 드리지요, 그레셤 씨,”"], ["Gresham,”", "사워비가 말했다."], ["said Sowerby: “put in an offer for the whole of the West Barsetshire crown property; they will be very glad to sell it.”", "“서부 바셋주 왕실 소유지 전체에 값을 매겨 사겠다고 내놓으세요. 그들은 아주 기뻐하며 팔 겁니다.”"]], [["“And we should be delighted to welcome you on this side of the border,”", "“그리고 우리는 국경 이쪽으로 당신을 맞아들이는 것을 기쁘게 여기겠소,”"], ["said the duke.", "공작이 말했다."]], [["Young Gresham did feel rather flattered.", "젊은 그레셤은 자못 우쭐한 기분이 들었다."], ["There were not many men in the county to whom such an offer could be made without an absurdity.", "이 주에서 그런 제안을 터무니없는 소리로 들리지 않게 할 수 있는 사람은 많지 않았다."], ["It might be doubted whether the duke himself could purchase the Chace of Chaldicotes with ready money; but that he, Gresham, could do so—he and his wife between them—no man did doubt.", "공작 자신이 현금으로 콜디코츠의 체이스를 살 수 있는지도 의심스러울 수 있었지만, 그, 그레셤이 그렇게 할 수 있다는 것은—그와 그의 아내가 함께라면—아무도 의심하지 않았다."], ["And then Mr. Gresham thought of a former day when he had once been at Gatherum Castle.", "그리고 그러다가 그레셤 씨는 언젠가 전에 개서럼 성에 왔던 때를 떠올렸다."], ["He had been poor enough then, and the duke had not treated him in the most courteous manner in the world.", "그때 그는 무척 가난했고, 공작은 그를 세상에서 가장 정중한 태도로 대해 주지 않았다."], ["How hard it is for a rich man not to lean upon his riches!", "부자가 자기 부에 기대지 않는 것이 얼마나 어려운가!"], ["harder, indeed, than for a camel to go through the eye of a needle.", "낙타가 바늘귀를 지나가는 것보다 정말로 더 어렵다."]], [["All Barsetshire knew—at any rate all West Barsetshire—that Miss Dunstable had been brought down in those parts in order that Mr. Sowerby might marry her.", "바셋주 전체가—적어도 서부 바셋주 전체가—던스터블 양이 사워비 씨와 결혼하도록 하기 위해 이 지방으로 데려와졌다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["It was not surmised that Miss Dunstable herself had had any previous notice of this arrangement, but it was supposed that the thing would turn out as a matter of course.", "던스터블 양 자신이 이 계획을 미리 알고 있었으리라고는 아무도 짐작하지 않았지만, 일이 저절로 그렇게 될 것이라고 여겨졌다."], ["Mr. Sowerby had no money, but then he was witty, clever, good-looking, and a member of Parliament.", "사워비 씨에게는 돈이 없었으나, 그는 재치 있고 영리하고 잘생겼으며 하원 의원이었다."], ["He lived before the world, represented an old family, and had an old place.", "그는 세상 앞에서 살았고, 오래된 가문을 대표했으며, 오래된 저택을 가지고 있었다."], ["How could Miss Dunstable possibly do better?", "던스터블 양이 이보다 더 나은 짝을 어떻게 얻겠는가?"], ["She was not so young now, and it was time that she should look about her.", "그녀는 이제 그렇게 젊지 않았고, 주위를 살펴볼 때가 되었다."]], [["The suggestion as regarded Mr. Sowerby was certainly true, and was not the less so as regarded some of Mr.", "사워비 씨에 관한 이 추측은 분명히 사실이었고, 사워비 씨의 몇몇 벗들에 관해서도 그에 못지않게 사실이었다."], ["Sowerby’s friends. His sister, Mrs.", "그의 누이 해럴드 스미스 부인은 이 일에 헌신했고, 그 목적으로 던스터블 양과 각별한 우정을 급히 쌓아 올렸다."], ["Harold Smith, had devoted herself to the work, and with this view had run up a dear friendship with Miss Dunstable. The bishop had intimated, nodding his head knowingly, that it would be a very good thing.", "주교는 아는 척 고개를 끄덕이며 그것이 아주 좋은 일이 될 것이라고 시사했다."], ["Mrs. Proudie had given in her adherence.", "프라우디 부인도 찬성을 표했다."], ["Mr. Supplehouse had been made to understand that it must be a case of “Paws off”", "서플하우스 씨는 자기가 이 지방에 머무는 동안은 「손 떼시오」의 경우여야 한다는 것을 이해하게 되었다."], ["with him, as long as he remained in that part of the world; and even the duke himself had desired Fothergill to manage it.", "그리고 공작 자신조차 포더길에게 그 일을 잘 처리하라고 지시했다."]], [["“He owes me an enormous sum of money,” said the duke, who held all Mr.", "“그가 내게 엄청난 금액을 빚지고 있소,”"], ["Sowerby’s title-deeds, “and I doubt whether the security will be sufficient.”", "사워비 씨의 모든 권리증서를 쥐고 있는 공작이 말했다. “그리고 그 담보가 넉넉할지 의심스럽소.”"]], [["“Your grace will find the security quite sufficient,” said Mr.", "“각하께서는 담보가 아주 넉넉하다는 것을 아시게 될 겁니다,”"], ["Fothergill; “but nevertheless it would be a good match.”", "포더길 씨가 말했다. “하지만 그렇더라도 좋은 혼사가 되겠지요.”"]], [["“Very good,” said the duke.", "“아주 좋지,”"], ["And then it became Mr.", "공작이 말했다."], ["Fothergill’s duty to see that Mr. Sowerby and Miss Dunstable became man and wife as speedily as possible.", "그리하여 사워비 씨와 던스터블 양이 되도록 빨리 부부가 되게 하는 것이 포더길 씨의 임무가 되었다."]], [["Some of the party, who were more wide awake than others, declared that he had made the offer; others, that he was just going to do so; and one very knowing lady went so far at one time as to say that he was making it at that moment.", "일행 가운데 남들보다 눈치가 밝은 이들은 그가 이미 청혼을 했다고 했고, 다른 이들은 그가 이제 막 하려는 참이라고 했다. 그리고 아주 잘 안다는 어떤 부인은 한때 그가 바로 그 순간에 청혼하고 있다고까지 말했다."], ["Bets also were laid as to the lady’s answer, as to the terms of the settlement, and as to the period of the marriage,—of all which poor Miss Dunstable of course knew nothing.", "그 부인의 대답에 대해서도, 재산 약정의 조건에 대해서도, 결혼 시기에 대해서도 돈이 걸렸다. 물론 딱한 던스터블 양은 그런 것을 아무것도 몰랐다."]], [["Mr. Sowerby, in spite of the publicity of his proceedings, proceeded in the matter very well.", "사워비 씨는 자기 일이 그렇게 공개되어 있었음에도 그 일을 아주 잘 처리해 나갔다."], ["He said little about it to those who joked with him, but carried on the fight with what best knowledge he had in such matters.", "그를 두고 농담하는 이들에게는 그 일에 대해 거의 말하지 않으면서, 이런 일에 자기가 가진 최선의 지식으로 싸움을 이어 갔다."], ["But so much it is given to us to declare with certainty, that he had not proposed on the evening previous to the morning fixed for the departure of Mark Robarts.", "그러나 이것만은 확실히 밝혀 둘 수 있다. 마크 로바츠가 떠나기로 정해진 날 아침의 전날 저녁까지, 그는 청혼을 하지 않았다."]], [["During the last two days Mr. Sowerby’s intimacy with Mark had grown warmer and warmer.", "지난 이틀 동안 사워비 씨의 마크에 대한 친밀함은 갈수록 더 따뜻해졌다."], ["He had talked to the vicar confidentially about the doings of these bigwigs now present at the castle, as though there were no other guest there with whom he could speak in so free a manner.", "그는 지금 성에 와 있는 이 거물들의 움직임에 대해 교구 사제에게, 마치 그렇게 거리낌 없이 말할 수 있는 다른 손님이 하나도 없는 것처럼 속을 털어놓았다."], ["He confided, it seemed, much more in Mark than in his brother-in-law, Harold Smith, or in any of his brother members of Parliament, and had altogether opened his heart to him in this affair of his anticipated marriage.", "보아하니 그는 자기 매부 해럴드 스미스나 다른 어떤 하원 동료보다도 마크를 더 믿었고, 이 예상되는 결혼 문제에서도 그에게 온 마음을 열어 놓았다."], ["Now Mr. Sowerby was a man of mark in the world, and all this flattered our young clergyman not a little.", "그런데 사워비 씨는 세상에서 이름 있는 사람이었으니, 이 모든 것이 우리의 젊은 성직자를 적잖이 우쭐하게 했다."]], [["On that evening before Robarts went away Sowerby asked him to come up into his bedroom when the whole party was breaking up, and there got him into an easy-chair while he, Sowerby, walked up and down the room.", "로바츠가 떠나기 전날 저녁, 일행이 모두 흩어질 때 사워비는 그에게 자기 침실로 올라오라고 청했고, 거기서 그를 안락의자에 앉혀 놓고 자기는, 곧 사워비는 방 안을 오르내리며 걸었다."]], [["“You can hardly tell, my dear fellow,”", "“당신은 짐작도 못 할 거예요, 마크,”"], ["said he, “the state of nervous anxiety in which this puts me.”", "그가 말했다. “이 일이 나를 어떤 신경 곤두선 불안 속에 몰아넣는지.”"]], [["“Why don’t you ask her and have done with it?", "“그냥 물어보고 끝내지 그러세요?"], ["She seems to me to be fond of your society.”", "그분은 당신과 함께 있는 걸 좋아하는 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“Ah, it is not that only; there are wheels within wheels;”", "“아, 그것만이 아니에요. 바퀴 속에 또 바퀴가 있지요.”"], ["and then he walked once or twice up and down the room, during which Mark thought that he might as well go to bed.", "그러고서 그는 한두 번 방 안을 오르내리며 걸었고, 그동안 마크는 이제 자리에 들어도 되겠다고 생각했다."]], [["“Not that I mind telling you everything,”", "“당신에게 다 털어놓는 것이 싫다는 건 아니에요,”"], ["said Sowerby.", "사워비가 말했다."], ["“I am infernally hard up for a little ready money just at the present moment.", "“바로 지금 이 순간 나는 얼마 안 되는 현금이 없어서 지독하게 곤궁해요."], ["It may be, and indeed I think it will be, the case that I shall be ruined in this matter for the want of it.”", "그리고 그것이 없어서 이 일에서 파멸할 수도 있어요. 아니, 그렇게 될 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Could not Harold Smith give it you?”", "“해럴드 스미스가 주지 못하나요?”"]], [["“Ha, ha, ha!", "“하, 하, 하!"], ["you don’t know Harold Smith.", "당신은 해럴드 스미스를 몰라요."], ["Did you ever hear of his lending a man a shilling in his life?”", "그가 평생 누구에게 한 실링이라도 빌려 주었다는 얘기를 들어 본 적 있어요?”"]], [["“Or Supplehouse?”", "“그럼 서플하우스는요?”"]], [["“Lord love you!", "“어이구, 참!"], ["You see me and Supplehouse together here, and he comes and stays at my house, and all that; but Supplehouse and I are no friends.", "당신은 나와 서플하우스가 여기 함께 있는 것을 보고, 그가 우리 집에 와서 지내기도 하고 그러니 그렇게 생각하겠지요. 하지만 서플하우스와 나는 벗이 아니에요."], ["Look you here, Mark—I would do more for your little finger than for his whole hand, including the pen which he holds in it.", "여기 보세요, 마크—나는 그의 손 전체를 위해서보다, 거기 들린 펜까지 다 합쳐서보다, 당신의 새끼손가락 하나를 위해서 더 많은 일을 할 거예요."], ["Fothergill indeed might—but then I know Fothergill is pressed himself at the present moment.", "포더길이라면 혹시 해 줄지도 모르지만, 나는 포더길이 지금 이 순간 자기도 쪼들리고 있다는 걸 알아요."], ["It is deuced hard, isn’t it?", "정말 죽겠지요, 그렇지 않아요?"], ["I must give up the whole game if I can’t put my hand upon £400 within the next two days.”", "앞으로 이틀 안에 400파운드를 손에 넣지 못하면 나는 이 판을 다 포기해야 해요.”"]], [["“Ask her for it, herself.”", "“그분에게 직접 청해 보세요.”"]], [["“What, the woman I wish to marry!", "“뭐라고요, 내가 아내로 맞고 싶은 여자에게?"], ["No, Mark, I’m not quite come to that.", "아니에요, 마크, 나는 아직 그 지경까지 가지는 않았어요."], ["I would sooner lose her than that.”", "그러기보다는 그녀를 잃는 것이 낫지요.”"]], [["Mark sat silent, gazing at the fire and wishing that he was in his own bedroom. He had an idea that Mr.", "마크는 잠자코 앉아 불을 바라보며, 자기 침실에 있었으면 좋겠다고 생각했다."], ["Sowerby wished him to produce this £400; and he knew also that he had not £400 in the world, and that if he had he would be acting very foolishly to give it to Mr.", "그는 사워비 씨가 이 400파운드를 자기가 내놓기를 바란다는 짐작이 들었다. 그리고 자기에게 400파운드가 세상에 없다는 것도, 있다 해도 그것을 사워비 씨에게 주는 것은 지극히 어리석은 짓이라는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["Sowerby. But nevertheless he felt half fascinated by the man, and half afraid of him.", "그러나 그럼에도 그는 이 사람에게 반쯤 매혹되고 반쯤 겁을 먹고 있었다."]], [["“Lufton owes it to me to do more than this,” continued Mr.", "“러프턴은 이보다 더한 일을 내게 해 줄 의무가 있어요,”"], ["Sowerby; “but then Lufton is not here.”", "사워비 씨가 말을 이었다. “하지만 러프턴은 여기 없지요.”"]], [["“Why, he has just paid five thousand pounds for you.”", "“아니, 그는 방금 당신을 위해 오천 파운드를 지불했잖아요.”"]], [["“Paid five thousand pounds for me!", "“나를 위해 오천 파운드를 지불했다고요!"], ["Indeed he has done no such thing: not a sixpence of it came into my hands.", "정말이지 그는 그런 일을 하지 않았어요. 그중 육 펜스도 내 손에 들어오지 않았어요."], ["Believe me, Mark, you don’t know the whole of that yet.", "믿어 주세요, 마크, 당신은 그 일의 전모를 아직 몰라요."], ["Not that I mean to say a word against Lufton.", "그렇다고 러프턴을 두고 한마디라도 나쁘게 말하려는 것은 아니에요."], ["He is the soul of honour; though so deucedly dilatory in money matters.", "그는 명예 그 자체지요. 다만 돈 문제에서는 지독하게 느리고요."], ["He thought he was right all through that affair, but no man was ever so confoundedly wrong.", "그는 그 일 전체에서 자기가 옳다고 생각했지만, 그렇게까지 어처구니없이 틀린 사람은 없었어요."], ["Why, don’t you remember that that was the very view you took of it yourself?”", "아니, 당신 자신도 그 일을 바로 그렇게 보았던 것을 기억하지 못해요?”"]], [["“I remember saying that I thought he was mistaken.”", "“그가 잘못 생각하는 것 같다고 말한 건 기억해요.”"]], [["“Of course he was mistaken.", "“물론 그가 잘못 생각한 거지요."], ["And dearly the mistake cost me.", "그리고 그 잘못이 내게 비싸게 값을 물렸어요."], ["I had to make good the money for two or three years.", "나는 이삼 년 동안 그 돈을 메워야 했어요."], ["And my property is not like his—I wish it were.”", "그리고 내 재산은 그의 것 같지가 않아요—그랬으면 좋겠지만.”"]], [["“Marry Miss Dunstable, and that will set it all right for you.”", "“던스터블 양과 결혼하세요. 그러면 그것이 다 해결될 텐데요.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["so I would if I had this money.", "이 돈이 있으면 그러겠어요."], ["At any rate I would bring it to the point.", "적어도 일을 결말까지 끌고 가겠지요."], ["Now, I tell you what, Mark; if you’ll assist me at this strait I’ll never forget it.", "그러니 이렇게 하면 어때요, 마크. 당신이 이 곤경에서 나를 도와준다면 나는 그것을 결코 잊지 않겠어요."], ["And the time will come round when I may be able to do something for you.”", "그리고 언젠가는 내가 당신을 위해 무언가 할 수 있는 때가 돌아올 거예요.”"]], [["“I have not got a hundred, no, not fifty pounds by me in the world.”", "“나는 세상에 백 파운드도, 아니 오십 파운드도 가진 것이 없어요.”"]], [["“Of course you’ve not.", "“물론 없겠지요."], ["Men don’t walk about the streets with £400 in their pockets.", "사람들이 주머니에 400파운드를 넣고 거리를 돌아다니지는 않지요."], ["I don’t suppose there’s a single man here in the house with such a sum at his bankers’, unless it be the duke.”", "이 집에 있는 사람 가운데 은행에 그런 금액을 둔 사람이 하나라도 있을 것 같지 않아요. 공작이라면 몰라도.”"]], [["“What is it you want then?”", "“그럼 원하는 게 뭐예요?”"]], [["“Why, your name, to be sure.", "“그야 당신의 이름이지요, 당연히."], ["Believe me, my dear fellow, I would not ask you really to put your hand into your pocket to such a tune as that.", "믿어 주세요, 내 좋은 벗, 정말로 당신에게 그만한 액수를 주머니에서 꺼내라고 청하지는 않을 거예요."], ["Allow me to draw on you for that amount at three months.", "석 달 기한으로 그 금액을 당신 앞으로 발행하게 해 주세요."], ["Long before that time I shall be flush enough.”", "그때가 오기 훨씬 전에 나는 넉넉해질 거예요.”"], ["And then, before Mark could answer, he had a bill stamp and pen and ink out on the table before him, and was filling in the bill as though his friend had already given his consent.", "그러고서 마크가 대답도 하기 전에 그는 어음 용지와 펜과 잉크를 탁자에 꺼내 놓고, 마치 벗이 이미 승낙한 것처럼 어음을 채워 넣고 있었다."]], [["“Upon my word, Sowerby, I had rather not do that.”", "“정말이지, 사워비, 나는 그렇게 하고 싶지 않아요.”"]], [["“Why!", "“왜요!"], ["what are you afraid of?”—Mr. Sowerby asked this very sharply.", "무엇이 무서워요?”—사워비 씨는 이것을 아주 날카롭게 물었다."], ["“Did you ever hear of my having neglected to take up a bill when it fell due?”", "“내가 만기가 된 어음을 인수하지 않고 내버려 두었다는 얘기를 들어 본 적 있어요?”"], ["Robarts thought that he had heard of such a thing; but in his confusion he was not exactly sure, and so he said nothing.", "로바츠는 그런 얘기를 들은 적이 있다고 생각했지만, 당황한 나머지 확실하게 짚어 낼 수가 없었고, 그래서 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“No, my boy; I have not come to that.", "“아니에요, 마크. 나는 그 지경까지 가지 않았어요."], ["Look here: just you write, ‘Accepted, Mark Robarts,’ across that, and then you shall never hear of the transaction again;—and you will have obliged me for ever.”", "여기 보세요. 그저 여기에 ‘인수함, 마크 로바츠’라고 써 주기만 하면, 당신은 이 거래에 대해 다시는 듣지 않을 거예요.—그리고 나에게 영원히 은혜를 베푸는 거지요.”"]], [["“As a clergyman it would be wrong of me,”", "“성직자로서 그렇게 하는 것은 옳지 않아요,”"], ["said Robarts.", "로바츠가 말했다."]], [["“As a clergyman!", "“성직자로서라니!"], ["Come, Mark!", "이봐요, 마크!"], ["If you don’t like to do as much as that for a friend, say so; but don’t let us have that sort of humbug.", "벗을 위해 그만한 일도 하고 싶지 않다면 그렇다고 말하세요. 하지만 그런 식의 수작은 그만둡시다."], ["If there be one class of men whose names would be found more frequent on the backs of bills in the provincial banks than another, clergymen are that class.", "지방 은행의 어음 뒷면에서 다른 어떤 계층보다 더 자주 이름이 발견되는 계층이 하나 있다면, 성직자가 바로 그 계층이에요."], ["Come, old fellow, you won’t throw me over when I am so hard pushed.”", "어서요, 마크, 내가 이렇게 몰려 있는데 나를 내던지지는 않을 거지요.”"]], [["Mark Robarts took the pen and signed the bill.", "마크 로바츠는 펜을 들어 어음에 서명했다."], ["It was the first time in his life that he had ever done such an act.", "그가 평생 그런 일을 한 것은 그때가 처음이었다."], ["Sowerby then shook him cordially by the hand, and he walked off to his own bedroom a wretched man.", "사워비는 그러고서 그의 손을 다정하게 흔들었고, 그는 참담한 사람이 되어 자기 침실로 걸어갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX. THE VICAR’S RETURN.", "# 제9장. 교구 사제의 귀환"]], [["The next morning Mr. Robarts took leave of all his grand friends with a heavy heart.", "이튿날 아침 로바츠 씨는 무거운 마음으로 그 지체 높은 벗들에게 작별을 알렸다."], ["He had lain awake half the night thinking of what he had done and trying to reconcile himself to his position.", "그는 밤의 절반을 뜬눈으로 누워 자기가 저지른 일을 생각하며 제 처지와 화해해 보려 애썼다. 사워비 씨의 방을 나서기가 무섭게 그는, 석 달이 지나면 그 400파운드 때문에 다시 시달리게 되리라는 것을 확신했다."], ["He had not well left Mr. Sowerby’s room before he felt certain that at the end of three months he would again be troubled about that £400.", "복도를 걸어가는 동안, 그 사내에 관해 알려진 온갖 전력이 어찌나 빠르게 밀려들었던지, 어음 인지를 앞에 놓고 펜과 잉크를 손 닿는 데 갖춘 채 그 안락의자에 앉아 있을 때에는 도무지 떠오르지 않던 것들이었다."], ["As he went along the passage all the man’s known antecedents crowded upon him much quicker than he could remember them when seated in that arm-chair with the bill stamp before him, and the pen and ink ready to his hand.", "러프턴 경이 그에게 했던 말이 떠올랐다 — 곤경에 버려졌다고 하소연하던 그 말이. 콜디코츠에서 돈을 받아 내는 것은 불가능하다는, 주 전체에 퍼져 있는 온갖 이야기들이 생각났다."], ["He remembered what Lord Lufton had told him—how he had complained of having been left in the lurch; he thought of all the stories current through the entire county as to the impossibility of getting money from Chaldicotes; he brought to mind the known character of the man, and then he knew that he must prepare himself to make good a portion at least of that heavy payment.", "그 사내의 익히 알려진 됨됨이가 되살아났다. 그러자 그는, 그 무거운 지불의 적어도 일부는 자기가 갚아야 할 각오를 해야 한다는 것을 알았다."]], [["Why had he come to this horrid place?", "무엇 하러 그는 이 끔찍한 곳에 왔던가? 프램리의 제 집에, 사람의 마음이 바랄 만한 것이 다 있지 않았던가?"], ["Had he not everything at home at Framley which the heart of man could desire?", "아니다. 사람의 마음은 수석 사제 자리를 바랄 수 있다 — 즉 교구 사제인 사람의 마음이 그렇다."], ["No; the heart of man can desire deaneries—the heart, that is, of the man vicar; and the heart of the man dean can desire bishoprics; and before the eyes of the man bishop does there not loom the transcendental glory of Lambeth?", "그리고 수석 사제인 사람의 마음은 주교 자리를 바랄 수 있다. 또 주교인 사람의 눈앞에는 램버스의 초월적인 영광이 어른거리지 않겠는가?"], ["He had owned to himself that he was ambitious; but he had to own to himself now also that he had hitherto taken but a sorry path towards the object of his ambition.", "그는 자기가 야심가임을 스스로 인정해 왔다. 그러나 이제는 또 하나, 여태 그 야심의 목적을 향해 걸어온 길이 참으로 딱한 길이었음도 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["On the next morning at breakfast-time, before his horse and gig arrived for him, no one was so bright as his friend Sowerby.", "이튿날 아침 식사 시간, 그를 태울 말과 이륜마차가 도착하기 전에, 벗 사워비만큼 환한 사람은 없었다."], ["“So you are off, are you?”", "“그래, 떠나시는군요?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I shall go this morning.”", "“네, 오늘 아침에 떠나려고요.”"]], [["“Say everything that’s kind from me to Lufton.", "“러프턴에게 제 다정한 인사를 다 전해 주세요."], ["I may possibly see him out hunting; otherwise we shan’t meet till the spring.", "사냥터에서라면 마주칠 수도 있겠지만, 그러지 않으면 봄까지는 못 만날 겁니다."], ["As to my going to Framley, that’s out of the question.", "제가 프램리에 가는 건 어림도 없는 일이고요."], ["Her ladyship would look for my tail, and swear that she smelt brimstone.", "마님은 제 꼬리를 찾으실 테고, 유황 냄새가 난다고 단언하실 테니까요."], ["By-bye, old fellow!”", "그럼 잘 가요, 이 친구!”"]], [["The German student when he first made his bargain with the devil felt an indescribable attraction to his new friend; and such was the case now with Robarts.", "그 독일인 학생도 악마와 처음 거래를 맺었을 때 새 친구에게 말로 다 할 수 없는 매력을 느꼈다. 지금 로바츠의 경우도 그러했다."], ["He shook Sowerby’s hand very warmly, said that he hoped he should meet him soon somewhere, and professed himself specially anxious to hear how that affair with the lady came off.", "그는 사워비의 손을 아주 따뜻하게 잡고, 어디서든 곧 다시 만나기를 바란다고 말했으며, 그 숙녀와의 일이 어떻게 되었는지 꼭 알려 달라며 유별난 관심을 내보였다."], ["As he had made his bargain—as he had undertaken to pay nearly half-a-year’s income for his dear friend—ought he not to have as much value as possible for his money?", "거래를 맺은 이상 — 이 소중한 벗을 위해 반년치 수입에 가까운 돈을 물어 주기로 한 이상 — 그 돈만큼의 값어치는 되도록 많이 챙겨야 하지 않겠는가?"], ["If the dear friendship of this flash member of Parliament did not represent that value, what else did do so? But then he felt, or fancied that he felt, that Mr.", "이 화려한 하원 의원의 소중한 우정이 그 값어치가 아니라면, 대체 무엇이 그 값어치겠는가?"], ["Sowerby did not care for him so much this morning as he had done on the previous evening.", "그러나 그때 그는, 사워비 씨가 지난 저녁만큼 오늘 아침에는 자기를 아껴 주지 않는다고 느꼈다. 아니면 느꼈다고 여겼다."], ["“By-bye,” said Mr.", "“그럼 잘 가요.”"], ["Sowerby, but he spoke no word as to such future meetings, nor did he even promise to write. Mr.", "사워비 씨는 그렇게 말했으나, 앞으로 만나자는 말은 한마디도 하지 않았고, 편지를 쓰겠다는 약속조차 하지 않았다."], ["Sowerby probably had many things on his mind; and it might be that it behoved him, having finished one piece of business, immediately to look to another.", "사워비 씨는 아마 마음에 걸린 일이 많았을 것이다. 한 건의 볼일을 끝냈으니 곧바로 다음 건을 챙기는 것이 그로서는 마땅한 일이었을지도 모른다."]], [["The sum for which Robarts had made himself responsible—which he so much feared that he would be called upon to pay—was very nearly half-a-year’s income; and as yet he had not put by one shilling since he had been married.", "로바츠가 스스로 책임을 지고 만 그 금액은 — 자기가 갚으라는 소리를 듣게 되지 않을까 그토록 두려워한 그 금액은 — 반년치 수입에 거의 맞먹었다. 그런데 그는 결혼한 뒤로 아직 한 실링도 모아 두지 못했다."], ["When he found himself settled in his parsonage, he found also that all the world regarded him as a rich man.", "목사관에 자리를 잡고 보니, 세상 사람 모두가 자기를 부유한 사람으로 여긴다는 것도 알게 되었다."], ["He had taken the dictum of all the world as true, and had set himself to work to live comfortably.", "그는 세상 모두의 그 판정을 참으로 받아들여, 편안하게 사는 일에 스스로 힘을 쏟았다. 부목사가 꼭 있어야 할 형편은 아니었다."], ["He had no absolute need of a curate; but he could afford the £70—as Lady Lufton had said rather injudiciously; and by keeping Jones in the parish he would be acting charitably to a brother clergyman, and would also place himself in a more independent position.", "그러나 70파운드쯤은 감당할 수 있었다 — 레이디 러프턴이 다소 분별없이 그렇게 말한 대로였다. 게다가 존스 씨를 교구에 붙들어 두면 같은 성직자 형제에게 자선을 베푸는 일이 되고, 자기 처지도 더 홀로 설 수 있는 자리에 놓이게 되었다."], ["Lady Lufton had wished to see her pet clergyman well-to-do and comfortable; but now, as matters had turned out, she much regretted this affair of the curate.", "레이디 러프턴은 자기가 아끼는 성직자가 넉넉하고 편안하게 지내는 것을 보고 싶어 했다. 그러나 이제 일이 이렇게 되고 보니, 그녀는 이 부목사 일을 몹시 후회했다."], ["Mr. Jones, she said to herself, more than once, must be made to depart from Framley.", "존스 씨는 프램리에서 떠나게 만들어야 한다고, 그녀는 혼자서 한 번 이상 되뇌었다."]], [["He had given his wife a pony-carriage, and for himself he had a saddle-horse, and a second horse for his gig.", "그는 아내에게 조랑말 마차를 사 주었고, 자기 몫으로는 승용마 한 마리와 이륜마차용 말 한 마리를 더 두었다."], ["A man in his position, well-to-do as he was, required as much as that.", "그만한 처지에, 그만큼 넉넉한 사람이라면 그 정도는 있어야 했다."], ["He had a footman also, and a gardener, and a groom.", "하인도 한 명 두었고, 정원사와 마부도 두었다."], ["The two latter were absolutely necessary, but about the former there had been a question.", "뒤의 둘은 없어서는 안 될 사람이었으나, 앞의 하나를 두고는 말이 있었다."], ["His wife had been decidedly hostile to the footman; but, in all such matters as that, to doubt is to be lost.", "아내는 그 하인을 두는 데 단연 반대였다. 그러나 그런 일에서는 무릇 망설이는 쪽이 지는 법이다."], ["When the footman had been discussed for a week it became quite clear to the master that he also was a necessary.", "하인 문제를 한 주 동안 의논하고 나니, 그 하인 역시 없어서는 안 될 사람이라는 것이 주인에게는 아주 분명해졌다."]], [["As he drove home that morning he pronounced to himself the doom of that footman, and the doom also of that saddle-horse.", "그날 아침 마차를 몰아 집으로 가면서 그는 그 하인에게 파면을 선고했고, 그 승용마에게도 함께 선고했다."], ["They at any rate should go.", "적어도 그 둘은 내보내야 했다."], ["And then he would spend no more money in trips to Scotland; and above all, he would keep out of the bedrooms of impoverished members of Parliament at the witching hour of midnight.", "그리고 앞으로는 스코틀랜드 여행에 돈을 쓰지 않을 것이며, 무엇보다도 한밤중 그 마법 같은 시각에 빈털터리 하원 의원의 침실에는 얼씬거리지 않을 것이었다."], ["Such resolves did he make to himself as he drove home; and bethought himself wearily how that £400 might be made to be forthcoming.", "집으로 마차를 몰며 그는 그런 결심들을 스스로 세웠다. 그러고는 그 400파운드를 어디서 마련해야 하나 지친 마음으로 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["As to any assistance in the matter from Sowerby,—of that he gave himself no promise.", "그 일에 사워비가 도움을 주는 것에 대해서는 — 그는 스스로 아무 기대도 걸지 않았다."]], [["But he almost felt himself happy again as his wife came out into the porch to meet him, with a silk shawl over her head, and pretending to shiver as she watched him descending from his gig.", "그러나 아내가 머리에 명주 숄을 쓰고 현관 밖으로 나와 그를 맞으며, 그가 이륜마차에서 내리는 것을 지켜보면서 짐짓 몸을 떠는 시늉을 할 때, 그는 다시 거의 행복해진 기분이었다."]], [["“My dear old man,”", "“여보, 우리 영감님,”"], ["she said, as she led him into the warm drawing-room with all his wrappings still about him, “you must be starved.”", "아내는 그가 걸친 것을 그대로 두른 채인 그를 따뜻한 응접실로 이끌면서 말했다. “꽁꽁 얼었겠어요.”"], ["But Mark during the whole drive had been thinking too much of that transaction in Mr. Sowerby’s bedroom to remember that the air was cold.", "그러나 마크는 오는 길 내내 사워비 씨 침실에서의 그 거래를 너무 골똘히 생각한 나머지 바깥 공기가 찬 줄도 몰랐다."], ["Now he had his arm round his own dear Fanny’s waist; but was he to tell her of that transaction?", "이제 그는 사랑하는 패니의 허리에 팔을 감고 있었다. 그런데 그 거래를 아내에게 말해야 할까?"], ["At any rate he would not do it now, while his two boys were in his arms, rubbing the moisture from his whiskers with their kisses.", "적어도 지금은 아니었다. 두 아들이 그의 품에 안겨, 입맞춤으로 그의 구레나룻에 서린 물기를 문질러 닦아 내는 이 순간에는."], ["After all, what is there equal to that coming home?", "아무리 생각해도, 집에 돌아오는 그 순간에 견줄 만한 것이 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["“And so Lufton is here.", "“그래서 러프턴이 와 있단 말이지."], ["I say, Frank, gently, old boy,”—Frank was his eldest son—“you’ll have baby into the fender.”", "얘, 프랭크, 살살 하렴,” — 프랭크는 그의 맏아들이었다 — “그러다 아기를 벽난로 울에 처박겠구나.”"]], [["“Let me take baby; it’s impossible to hold the two of them, they are so strong,”", "“아기는 제가 안을게요. 둘을 한꺼번에 안기는 무리예요, 어찌나 힘이 센지,”"], ["said the proud mother.", "자랑스러운 어머니가 말했다."], ["“Oh, yes, he came home early yesterday.”", "“아, 네, 어제 이른 시각에 돌아오셨어요.”"]], [["“Have you seen him?”", "“당신은 만나 봤어?”"]], [["“He was here yesterday, with her ladyship; and I lunched there to-day.", "“어제 마님과 함께 여기 오셨어요. 그리고 저는 오늘 그 댁에서 점심을 먹었고요."], ["The letter came, you know, in time to stop the Merediths.", "편지가 때맞춰 닿아서 메러디스 부부를 붙들어 두었잖아요."], ["They don’t go till to-morrow, so you will meet them after all.", "그분들은 내일이나 떠나실 테니 당신도 결국 만나게 될 거예요."], ["Sir George is wild about it, but Lady Lufton would have her way.", "조지 경은 그 일로 몹시 성이 났지만, 레이디 러프턴이 뜻을 굽히지 않으셨죠."], ["You never saw her in such a state as she is.”", "마님이 저러시는 건 당신도 여태 본 적 없을 거예요.”"]], [["“Good spirits, eh?”", "“기분이 아주 좋으시다는 거지?”"]], [["“I should think so.", "“그럼요."], ["All Lord Lufton’s horses are coming, and he’s to be here till March.”", "러프턴 경의 말들이 다 오는 중이고, 경께서 3월까지 여기 계실 거래요.”"]], [["“Till March!”", "“3월까지!”"]], [["“So her ladyship whispered to me.", "“마님이 저한테 그렇게 속삭이셨어요."], ["She could not conceal her triumph at his coming.", "아들이 온다는 데 대한 승리감을 감추지 못하시더군요."], ["He’s going to give up Leicestershire this year altogether.", "경께서는 올해 레스터셔를 아예 접으실 거래요."], ["I wonder what has brought it all about?”", "대체 무슨 일로 이렇게 됐을까요?”"], ["Mark knew very well what had brought it about; he had been made acquainted, as the reader has also, with the price at which Lady Lufton had purchased her son’s visit.", "마크는 무슨 일로 그렇게 되었는지 아주 잘 알고 있었다. 독자도 이미 알게 되었듯이, 레이디 러프턴이 아들의 방문을 사들인 값이 얼마였는지 그는 들어 알고 있었다."], ["But no one had told Mrs. Robarts that the mother had made her son a present of five thousand pounds.", "그러나 어머니가 아들에게 5,000파운드를 선물했다는 것은 아무도 로바츠 부인에게 말해 주지 않았다."]], [["“She’s in a good humour about everything now,”", "“이제는 무슨 일에든 기분이 좋으세요,”"], ["continued Fanny; “so you need say nothing at all about Gatherum Castle.”", "패니가 말을 이었다. “그러니 개서럼 성 이야기는 아무것도 하실 필요 없어요.”"]], [["“But she was very angry when she first heard it; was she not?”", "“하지만 처음 그 얘기를 들었을 때는 몹시 화가 났었지. 그렇지 않아?”"]], [["“Well, Mark, to tell the truth, she was; and we had quite a scene there up in her own room up-stairs,—Justinia and I.", "“글쎄요, 마크, 사실대로 말하면 그러셨어요. 위층 그분 방에서 한바탕 난리가 났죠 — 저스티니아와 저요."], ["She had heard something else that she did not like at the same time; and then—but you know her way.", "마음에 안 드는 다른 소식도 같은 때에 들으셨거든요. 그래서 — 하지만 그분 성미는 당신도 아시잖아요."], ["She blazed up quite hot.”", "아주 뜨겁게 확 타오르셨어요.”"]], [["“And said all manner of horrid things about me.”", "“그리고 나에 대해 온갖 험한 말을 하셨겠지.”"]], [["“About the duke she did.", "“공작에 대해서라면 그러셨죠."], ["You know she never did like the duke; and for the matter of that, neither do I.", "그분이 공작을 좋아한 적이 없다는 건 당신도 아시고요. 그리고 그 문제라면 저도 마찬가지예요."], ["I tell you that fairly, Master Mark!”", "미리 분명히 말해 두는데요, 마크 도련님!”"]], [["“The duke is not so bad as he’s painted.”", "“공작은 소문만큼 나쁜 사람은 아니야.”"]], [["“Ah, that’s what you say about another great person.", "“아, 그건 또 다른 어떤 위대한 분에 대해서도 당신이 하는 말이잖아요."], ["However, he won’t come here to trouble us, I suppose.", "어쨌든 그이가 우리를 괴롭히러 여기 오지는 않겠죠. 아무튼 저는 그분 기분이 세상에서 가장 좋다고는 할 수 없는 상태로 그 댁을 나왔어요."], ["And then I left her, not in the best temper in the world; for I blazed up too, you must know.”", "저도 확 타올랐거든요. 그것도 아셔야 해요.”"]], [["“I am sure you did,”", "“그랬을 테지,”"], ["said Mark, pressing his arm round her waist.", "마크는 아내의 허리를 팔로 감아 안으며 말했다."]], [["“And then we were going to have a dreadful war, I thought; and I came home and wrote such a doleful letter to you. But what should happen when I had just closed it, but in came her ladyship—all alone, and—. But I can’t tell you what she did or said, only she behaved beautifully; just like herself too; so full of love and truth and honesty. There’s nobody like her, Mark; and she’s better than all the dukes that ever wore—whatever dukes do wear.”", "“그래서 이제 끔찍한 전쟁이 벌어지겠구나 싶었어요. 집에 돌아와서 당신에게 그렇게 처량한 편지를 썼고요. 그런데 봉투를 막 봉하자마자 무슨 일이 있었겠어요, 마님이 들어오셨어요 — 혼자서, 그리고 —. 하지만 그분이 무엇을 하고 무슨 말을 하셨는지는 당신에게 말할 수 없어요. 다만 참으로 아름답게 행동하셨어요. 딱 그분다우셨죠. 사랑과 진실과 정직으로 가득해서요. 그분 같은 사람은 없어요, 마크. 그리고 그분은 여태 무엇을 걸쳤든 그 모든 공작들보다 훌륭한 분이에요 — 공작들이 무엇을 걸치는지야 모르겠지만요.”"]], [["“Horns and hoofs; that’s their usual apparel, according to you and Lady Lufton,”", "“뿔과 굽이지. 당신과 레이디 러프턴에 따르면 그게 그들이 늘 걸치는 것이고,”"], ["said he, remembering what Mr. Sowerby had said of himself.", "그는 사워비 씨가 자기 자신에 대해 했던 말을 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["“You may say what you like about me, Mark, but you shan’t abuse Lady Lufton.", "“저에 대해서는 무슨 말이든 하셔도 돼요, 마크. 하지만 레이디 러프턴을 험담하시면 안 돼요."], ["And if horns and hoofs mean wickedness and dissipation, I believe it’s not far wrong.", "그리고 뿔과 굽이 사악함과 방탕을 뜻하는 거라면, 그리 틀린 말도 아닐 거예요."], ["But get off your big coat and make yourself comfortable.”", "자, 그 두꺼운 외투는 벗고 편히 앉으세요.”"], ["And that was all the scolding that Mark Robarts got from his wife on the occasion of his great iniquity.", "마크 로바츠가 그 큰 죄악을 지은 일로 아내에게서 들은 꾸중은 그것이 전부였다."]], [["“I will certainly tell her about this bill transaction,”", "“이 어음 거래는 아내에게 반드시 말하겠다,” 그는 혼자 되뇌었다."], ["he said to himself; “but not to-day; not till after I have seen Lufton.”", "“그러나 오늘은 아니다. 러프턴을 만나 본 뒤가 아니면 안 된다.”"]], [["That evening they dined at Framley Court, and there they met the young lord; they found also Lady Lufton still in high good-humour.", "그날 저녁 그들은 프램리 코트에서 만찬을 했고, 거기서 그 젊은 경을 만났다. 레이디 러프턴 역시 여전히 기분이 아주 좋은 것을 보았다."], ["Lord Lufton himself was a fine, bright-looking young man; not so tall as Mark Robarts, and with perhaps less intelligence marked on his face; but his features were finer, and there was in his countenance a thorough appearance of good-humour and sweet temper.", "러프턴 경은 준수하고 환한 인상의 젊은이였다. 마크 로바츠만큼 키가 크지는 않았고, 얼굴에 새겨진 지력은 아마 덜했다."], ["It was, indeed, a pleasant face to look upon, and dearly Lady Lufton loved to gaze at it.", "그러나 이목구비가 더 고왔고, 그 얼굴에는 좋은 기분과 부드러운 성미가 그대로 드러나 있었다. 참으로 보기에 즐거운 얼굴이었으니, 레이디 러프턴은 그 얼굴을 바라보는 것을 몹시 사랑했다."]], [["“Well, Mark, so you have been among the Philistines?”", "“그래, 마크, 자네 블레셋 사람들 틈에 있었다지?”"], ["that was his lordship’s first remark.", "그것이 경의 첫마디였다."], ["Robarts laughed as he took his friend’s hands, and bethought himself how truly that was the case; that he was, in very truth, already “himself in bonds under Philistian yoke.”", "로바츠는 벗의 두 손을 잡으며 웃었고, 그것이 얼마나 참말인지를 속으로 생각했다. 그는 정말로 이미 “블레셋의 멍에 아래 스스로 사슬에 묶인”"], ["Alas, alas, it is very hard to break asunder the bonds of the latter-day Philistines.", "몸이었다. 아, 슬프도다, 오늘날의 블레셋 사람들이 채운 사슬은 끊어 내기가 참으로 어렵다."], ["When a Samson does now and then pull a temple down about their ears, is he not sure to be engulfed in the ruin with them?", "삼손이 이따금 그들의 머리 위로 신전을 무너뜨린다 해도, 그 자신도 그들과 함께 그 폐허에 묻히고 마는 것이 아닌가?"], ["There is no horse-leech that sticks so fast as your latter-day Philistine.", "오늘날의 블레셋 사람만큼 끈덕지게 달라붙는 말거머리는 없다."]], [["“So you have caught Sir George, after all,”", "“그래서 결국 조지 경을 붙잡았군요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton; and that was nearly all she did say in allusion to his absence.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다. 그의 부재에 대해 그녀가 한 말은 그것이 거의 전부였다."], ["There was afterwards some conversation about the lecture, and from her ladyship’s remarks, it certainly was apparent that she did not like the people among whom the vicar had been lately staying; but she said no word that was personal to him himself, or that could be taken as a reproach.", "그 뒤에 강연에 관한 이야기가 조금 오갔는데, 마님의 말에서 그 교구 사제가 근래 머물렀던 사람들을 그녀가 좋아하지 않는다는 것은 분명히 드러났다. 그러나 그 자신을 겨냥한 말이나 책망으로 받아들일 만한 말은 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["The little episode of Mrs. Proudie’s address in the lecture-room had already reached Framley, and it was only to be expected that Lady Lufton should enjoy the joke.", "강연장에서 프라우디 부인이 연설을 했다는 그 작은 일화는 이미 프램리까지 닿아 있었으니, 레이디 러프턴이 그 우스개를 즐거워하는 것은 당연한 일이었다."], ["She would affect to believe that the body of the lecture had been given by the bishop’s wife; and afterwards, when Mark described her costume at that Sunday morning breakfast-table, Lady Lufton would assume that such had been the dress in which she had exercised her faculties in public.", "그녀는 강연의 본문 자체가 주교 부인의 입에서 나온 것이라고 짐짓 믿는 척했다. 그리고 나중에 마크가 그 일요일 아침 식탁에서 보았던 부인의 옷차림을 이야기하자, 레이디 러프턴은 부인이 바로 그 옷을 입은 채로 대중 앞에서 제 재능을 뽐낸 것으로 여기려 했다."]], [["“I would have given a five-pound note to have heard it,”", "“그 이야기를 직접 들었다면 5파운드 지폐라도 냈을 거요,”"], ["said Sir George.", "조지 경이 말했다."]], [["“So would not I,”", "“나는 아니에요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“When one hears of such things described so graphically as Mr. Robarts now tells it, one can hardly help laughing.", "“로바츠 씨가 지금처럼 생생하게 그려 주는 이야기로 들으면 웃지 않기가 어렵죠."], ["But it would give me great pain to see the wife of one of our bishops place herself in such a situation.", "하지만 우리 주교 가운데 한 분의 부인이 스스로 그런 처지에 서는 것을 직접 보는 일은 나로서는 몹시 마음 아플 거예요."], ["For he is a bishop after all.”", "그래도 그는 어쨌든 주교니까요.”"]], [["“Well, upon my word, my lady, I agree with Meredith,”", "“정말이지, 마님, 저는 메러디스 생각에 찬성이에요,”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."], ["“It must have been good fun.", "“꽤나 볼만했겠죠."], ["As it did happen, you know,—as the Church was doomed to the disgrace, I should like to have heard it.”", "어차피 일이 벌어진 이상 — 교회가 그런 망신을 당할 운명이었던 이상, 저도 그 이야기를 직접 들었으면 좋았을 겁니다.”"]], [["“I know you would have been shocked, Ludovic.”", "“너는 충격을 받았을 게다, 루도빅.”"]], [["“I should have got over that in time, mother.", "“시간이 지나면 그건 넘겼을 거예요, 어머니."], ["It would have been like a bull-fight I suppose—horrible to see no doubt, but extremely interesting.", "아마 투우 같았겠죠 — 보기에는 틀림없이 끔찍하지만 몹시 흥미로운."], ["And Harold Smith, Mark; what did he do all the while?”", "그런데 해럴드 스미스는, 마크, 그 내내 무얼 하고 있었나?”"]], [["“It didn’t take so very long, you know,”", "“그리 오래 걸리지는 않았네,”"], ["said Robarts.", "로바츠가 말했다."]], [["“And the poor bishop,”", "“그 딱한 주교님은,”"], ["said Lady Meredith; “how did he look?", "레이디 메러디스가 말했다. “어떤 모습이었나요?"], ["I really do pity him.”", "저는 정말로 그분이 안됐어요.”"]], [["“Well, he was asleep, I think.”", "“글쎄요, 주교님은 잠들어 계셨던 것 같습니다.”"]], [["“What, slept through it all?”", "“뭐요, 그 내내 자고 있었단 말이오?”"], ["said Sir George.", "조지 경이 말했다."]], [["“It awakened him; and then he jumped up and said something.”", "“그 소리에 깨셨습니다. 그러고는 벌떡 일어나 뭐라고 말씀하셨죠.”"]], [["“What, out loud too?”", "“뭐요, 소리 내어서 말이오?”"]], [["“Only one word or so.”", "“한마디쯤이었습니다.”"]], [["“What a disgraceful scene!”", "“참 볼썽사나운 광경이군요!”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“To those who remember the good old man who was in the diocese before him it is perfectly shocking.", "“그 앞에 그 교구에 계셨던 선량한 노인을 기억하는 사람에게는 정말이지 충격적인 일이에요."], ["He confirmed you, Ludovic, and you ought to remember him.", "그분이 네게 견진을 주셨지, 루도빅. 너도 그분을 기억해야 한다."], ["It was over at Barchester, and you went and lunched with him afterwards.”", "바체스터에서였고, 그 뒤에 너는 그분과 점심을 먹었어.”"]], [["“I do remember; and especially this, that I never ate such tarts in my life, before or since.", "“기억해요. 특히 이건 기억나죠."], ["The old man particularly called my attention to them, and seemed remarkably pleased that I concurred in his sentiments.", "그런 타르트는 그전에도 그 뒤로도 평생 먹어 본 적이 없다는 것. 그 노인께서 특별히 제게 그것을 권하셨고, 제가 그 뜻에 동의하니 유난히 기뻐하시는 것 같았어요."], ["There are no such tarts as those going in the palace now, I’ll be bound.”", "지금 주교관에서는 그만한 타르트가 나오지 않을 겁니다. 틀림없어요.”"]], [["“Mrs. Proudie will be very happy to do her best for you if you will go and try,”", "“가서 한번 청해 보겠다면 프라우디 부인이 기꺼이 최선을 다해 줄 거요,”"], ["said Sir George.", "조지 경이 말했다."]], [["“I beg that he will do no such thing,”", "“그 애가 그런 짓은 하지 않기를 바라요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton, and that was the only severe word she said about any of Mark’s visitings.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다. 마크의 그 방문들에 관해 그녀가 한 매서운 말은 그것이 전부였다."]], [["As Sir George Meredith was there, Robarts could say nothing then to Lord Lufton about Mr. Sowerby and Mr.", "조지 메러디스 경이 그 자리에 있었으므로, 로바츠는 사워비 씨와 사워비 씨의 돈 문제에 대해서 러프턴 경에게 그때는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다."], ["Sowerby’s money affairs; but he did make an appointment for a tête-à-tête on the next morning.", "그러나 이튿날 아침에 단둘이 만나기로 약속은 해 두었다."]], [["“You must come down and see my nags, Mark; they came to-day.", "“내려와서 내 말들을 좀 보아야 하네, 마크. 오늘 도착했어."], ["The Merediths will be off at twelve, and then we can have an hour together.”", "메러디스 부부는 열두 시에 떠날 테니, 그다음에 우리 한 시간쯤 함께 있을 수 있겠지.”"], ["Mark said he would, and then went home with his wife under his arm.", "마크는 그러겠다고 했고, 그러고는 아내를 팔에 끼고 집으로 갔다."]], [["“Well, now, is not she kind?”", "“자, 이제, 그분이 다정하지 않으세요?”"], ["said Fanny, as soon as they were out on the gravel together.", "자갈길에 함께 나서기가 무섭게 패니가 말했다."]], [["“She is kind; kinder than I can tell you just at present.", "“다정하지. 지금 당장은 내가 당신에게 말해 줄 수 있는 것보다 더 다정하고."], ["But did you ever know anything so bitter as she is to the poor bishop?", "그런데 그분이 그 딱한 주교에게 그토록 매서운 걸 당신은 여태 알고 있었어?"], ["And really the bishop is not so bad.”", "정말이지 그 주교는 그리 나쁜 사람이 아니야.”"]], [["“Yes; I know something much more bitter; and that is what she thinks of the bishop’s wife.", "“네, 저는 훨씬 더 매서운 것도 알아요. 그분이 주교 부인을 어떻게 여기시는지 말이에요."], ["And you know, Mark, it was so unladylike, her getting up in that way.", "그리고 마크, 그렇게 일어나서 나서는 건 정말 숙녀답지 못한 일이었어요."], ["What must the people of Barchester think of her?”", "바체스터 사람들이 그 부인을 어떻게 생각하겠어요?”"]], [["“As far as I could see the people of Barchester liked it.”", "“내가 본 바로는 바체스터 사람들은 그걸 좋아했어.”"]], [["“Nonsense, Mark; they could not.", "“말도 안 돼요, 마크. 그럴 수가 없죠."], ["But never mind that now.", "하지만 그건 지금 접어 두고요."], ["I want you to own that she is good.” And then Mrs.", "저는 당신이 그분이 훌륭한 분이라고 인정하시길 바라요.”"], ["Robarts went on with another long eulogy on the dowager.", "그러고 나서 로바츠 부인은 그 노마님에 대한 또 한 차례 긴 찬사를 이어 갔다."], ["Since that affair of the pardon-begging at the parsonage Mrs.", "목사관에서 용서를 청하러 온 그 일이 있은 뒤로, 로바츠 부인은 자기 벗을 어떻게 더 좋게 생각해야 할지 알 수 없을 만큼 좋게 생각하고 있었다."], ["Robarts hardly knew how to think well enough of her friend.", "게다가 그 끔찍한 폭풍과 태풍의 위협이 지나간 뒤의 저녁이 참으로 즐거웠다."], ["And the evening had been so pleasant after the dreadful storm and threatenings of hurricanes; her husband had been so well received after his lapse of judgment; the wounds that had looked so sore had been so thoroughly healed, and everything was so pleasant.", "남편이 판단을 그르친 뒤에도 그토록 잘 맞아들여졌다. 그토록 쓰라려 보였던 상처가 그토록 깨끗이 아물었고, 모든 것이 그토록 즐거웠다."], ["How all of this would have been changed had she known of that little bill!", "그 작은 어음 이야기를 알았더라면 이 모든 것이 얼마나 달라졌겠는가!"]], [["At twelve the next morning the lord and the vicar were walking through the Framley stables together.", "이튿날 아침 열두 시에 경과 교구 사제는 프램리의 마구간을 함께 거닐고 있었다."], ["Quite a commotion had been made there, for the larger portion of these buildings had of late years seldom been used.", "그곳은 한바탕 소동이 벌어져 있었으니, 이 건물의 큰 부분은 근래 여러 해 동안 좀처럼 쓰이지 않았기 때문이다."], ["But now all was crowding and activity.", "그런데 이제는 온통 붐비고 부산했다."], ["Seven or eight very precious animals had followed Lord Lufton from Leicestershire, and all of them required dimensions that were thought to be rather excessive by the Framley old-fashioned groom.", "아주 값진 말 일곱인가 여덟 마리가 레스터셔에서 러프턴 경을 따라왔고, 그것들은 모두 프램리의 구식 마부가 보기에는 다소 과하다 싶은 넓이를 요구했다."], ["My lord, however, had a head man of his own who took the matter quite into his own hands.", "그러나 경에게는 이 일을 제 손에 온전히 거머쥔 제 사람이 따로 있었다."]], [["Mark, priest as he was, was quite worldly enough to be fond of a good horse; and for some little time allowed Lord Lufton to descant on the merit of this four-year-old filly, and that magnificent Rattlebones colt, out of a Mousetrap mare; but he had other things that lay heavy on his mind, and after bestowing half an hour on the stud, he contrived to get his friend away to the shrubbery walks.", "마크는 성직자이면서도 좋은 말을 좋아할 만큼은 넉넉히 세속적이었다. 그래서 한동안은 러프턴 경이 이 네 살배기 암말의 장점을, 그리고 마우스트랩 어미에서 난 저 훌륭한 래틀본스 수망아지의 장점을 늘어놓게 두었다. 그러나 그에게는 마음을 무겁게 누르는 다른 일이 있었으므로, 말들에게 반 시간을 바친 뒤에 그는 벗을 관목 산책길로 데려가는 데 성공했다."]], [["“So you have settled with Sowerby,”", "“그래, 사워비와는 정리했다고,”"], ["Robarts began by saying.", "로바츠가 먼저 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Settled with him; yes, but do you know the price?”", "“정리했지. 그래, 그런데 그 값이 얼마인지 아나?”"]], [["“I believe that you have paid five thousand pounds.”", "“5,000파운드를 냈다고 들었네.”"]], [["“Yes, and about three before; and that in a matter in which I did not really owe one shilling.", "“그렇지. 그리고 그전에도 3,000쯤."], ["Whatever I do in future, I’ll keep out of Sowerby’s grip.”", "그것도 내가 실제로는 한 실링도 빚지지 않은 일에서 말이야. 앞으로 무슨 일을 하든, 사워비의 손아귀에는 들어가지 않겠네.”"]], [["“But you don’t think he has been unfair to you.”", "“그렇다고 그가 자네에게 부당하게 했다고 생각하는 건 아니지.”"]], [["“Mark, to tell you the truth I have banished the affair from my mind, and don’t wish to take it up again.", "“마크, 솔직히 말하면 나는 그 일을 머리에서 쫓아냈고 다시 꺼내고 싶지 않네."], ["My mother has paid the money to save the property, and of course I must pay her back.", "어머니가 재산을 지키려고 그 돈을 내주셨으니, 물론 내가 되갚아야 하지."], ["But I think I may promise that I will not have any more money dealings with Sowerby.", "하지만 앞으로 사워비와는 돈 거래를 더 하지 않겠다고는 약속할 수 있을 것 같네."], ["I will not say that he is dishonest, but at any rate he is sharp.”", "그가 부정직하다고까지는 말하지 않겠지만, 어쨌든 약삭빠른 사람이야.”"]], [["“Well, Lufton; what will you say when I tell you that I have put my name to a bill for him, for four hundred pounds?”", "“그런데 러프턴, 내가 그를 위해 400파운드 어음에 내 이름을 적었다고 말하면 자네는 뭐라고 하겠나?”"]], [["“Say; why I should say—; but you’re joking; a man in your position would never do such a thing.”", "“뭐라고 하겠느냐고. 그야 나는 이렇게 말하겠지 —. 아니, 자네 농담하는 거지. 자네 같은 처지의 사람이 그런 일을 할 리가 없어.”"]], [["“But I have done it.”", "“하지만 나는 했네.”"]], [["Lord Lufton gave a long low whistle.", "러프턴 경이 길고 낮게 휘파람을 불었다."]], [["“He asked me the last night that I was there, making a great favour of it, and declaring that no bill of his had ever yet been dishonoured.”", "“내가 거기 있던 마지막 밤에 그가 청했네. 대단한 청이라도 되는 듯이 굴면서, 자기 어음이 부도난 적은 여태 한 번도 없다고 못 박더군.”"]], [["Lord Lufton whistled again.", "러프턴 경이 또 휘파람을 불었다."], ["“No bill of his dishonoured!", "“자기 어음이 부도난 적이 없다니!"], ["Why the pocket-books of the Jews are stuffed full of his dishonoured papers!", "유대인들 지갑에는 그가 부도낸 종이가 잔뜩 들어차 있는데!"], ["And you have really given him your name for four hundred pounds?”", "그런데 자네가 정말로 400파운드에 이름을 내주었단 말인가?”"]], [["“I have certainly.”", "“분명히 그랬네.”"]], [["“At what date?”", "“기한은 언제인가?”"]], [["“Three months.”", "“석 달.”"]], [["“And have you thought where you are to get the money?”", "“그러면 그 돈을 어디서 구할지는 생각해 보았나?”"]], [["“I know very well that I can’t get it; not at least by that time.", "“구할 수 없다는 건 아주 잘 알고 있네. 적어도 그때까지는 안 되지."], ["The bankers must renew it for me, and I must pay it by degrees.", "은행에서 갱신해 주어야 하고, 나는 조금씩 나누어 갚아야 할 걸세."], ["That is, if Sowerby really does not take it up.”", "사워비가 정말로 그것을 떠맡지 않는다면 말이지.”"]], [["“It is just as likely that he will take up the national debt.”", "“그가 국채를 떠맡을 가능성이나 마찬가지일 걸세.”"]], [["Robarts then told him about the projected marriage with Miss Dunstable, giving it as his opinion that the lady would probably accept the gentleman.", "그러고 나서 로바츠는 던스터블 양과 하려는 그 혼인 이야기를 그에게 전하며, 그 숙녀가 아마 그 신사를 받아들일 것이라는 제 생각을 덧붙였다."]], [["“Not at all improbable,”", "“전혀 있을 수 없는 일은 아니지,” 경이 말했다."], ["said his lordship, “for Sowerby is an agreeable fellow; and if it be so, he will have all that he wants for life.", "“사워비는 사람 마음에 드는 자이니까. 그렇게만 되면 그는 평생 원하는 것을 다 갖게 될 걸세."], ["But his creditors will gain nothing.", "하지만 그의 채권자들은 아무것도 얻지 못하지."], ["The duke, who has his title-deeds, will doubtless get his money, and the estate will in fact belong to the wife.", "그의 권리 증서를 쥐고 있는 공작은 틀림없이 자기 돈을 받아 낼 테고, 영지는 사실상 아내의 것이 될 테지."], ["But the small fry, such as you, will not get a shilling.”", "그러나 자네 같은 잔챙이들은 한 실링도 못 받네.”"]], [["Poor Mark!", "딱한 마크!"], ["He had had an inkling of this before; but it had hardly presented itself to him in such certain terms.", "그는 전에도 이런 짐작은 하고 있었다. 그러나 이토록 확실한 말로 눈앞에 들이닥친 적은 없었다."], ["It was, then, a positive fact, that in punishment for his weakness in having signed that bill he would have to pay, not only four hundred pounds, but four hundred pounds with interest, and expenses of renewal, and commission, and bill stamps.", "그러니 그 어음에 서명한 자신의 약함에 대한 벌로, 그는 400파운드만이 아니라 이자와 갱신 비용과 수수료와 어음 인지값까지 얹은 400파운드를 물어야 한다는 것이 명백한 사실이었다. 그렇다."], ["Yes; he had certainly got among the Philistines during that visit of his to the duke.", "공작을 찾아간 그 방문 동안 그는 분명히 블레셋 사람들 틈에 들어가 버렸다."], ["It began to appear to him pretty clearly that it would have been better for him to have relinquished altogether the glories of Chaldicotes and Gatherum Castle.", "콜디코츠와 개서럼 성의 영광을 아예 포기하는 편이 자기에게 나았으리라는 것이 이제 그에게 꽤 분명하게 보이기 시작했다."]], [["And now, how was he to tell his wife?", "그런데 이제, 아내에게는 어떻게 말해야 하는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER X. LUCY ROBARTS.", "# 제10장. 루시 로바츠"]], [["And now how was he to tell his wife?", "그런데 이제 이 일을 아내에게 어떻게 말한다?"], ["That was the consideration heavy on Mark Robarts’ mind when last we left him; and he turned the matter often in his thoughts before he could bring himself to a resolution.", "우리가 마크 로바츠를 마지막으로 두고 떠나올 때 그의 마음을 무겁게 짓누르던 고민이 바로 그것이었다. 그는 결심에 이르기까지 그 문제를 여러 번 마음속에서 뒤집어 보았다."], ["At last he did do so, and one may say that it was not altogether a bad one, if only he could carry it out.", "마침내 결심을 세웠는데, 그것을 실행에 옮길 수만 있다면 아주 나쁜 결심은 아니었다고 말할 만하다."]], [["He would ascertain in what bank that bill of his had been discounted.", "자기 어음이 어느 은행에서 할인되었는지를 알아내기로 했다."], ["He would ask Sowerby, and if he could not learn from him, he would go to the three banks in Barchester.", "사워비 씨에게 물어보고, 그에게서 알아낼 수 없으면 바체스터의 은행 세 곳을 찾아가 볼 참이었다."], ["That it had been taken to one of them he felt tolerably certain.", "그 어음이 그중 한 곳으로 넘어갔으리라는 것은 꽤 확실하게 느끼고 있었다."], ["He would explain to the manager his conviction that he would have to make good the amount, his inability to do so at the end of the three months, and the whole state of his income; and then the banker would explain to him how the matter might be arranged.", "지배인에게 자기가 결국 그 금액을 물어내야 할 것이라는 확신과, 석 달이 끝나는 날에는 그럴 능력이 없다는 사정과, 자기 수입의 형편을 통틀어 설명하면, 은행가가 이 일을 어떻게 처리할 수 있는지 알려 줄 것이었다."], ["He thought that he could pay £50 every three months with interest.", "이자를 붙여 석 달마다 50파운드씩은 낼 수 있으리라 생각했다."], ["As soon as this should have been concerted with the banker, he would let his wife know all about it.", "은행가와 이 일을 맞추어 놓는 즉시 아내에게 모든 것을 알릴 것이었다."], ["Were he to tell her at the present moment, while the matter was all unsettled, the intelligence would frighten her into illness.", "만사가 이렇게 미정인 지금 이 순간에 아내에게 말한다면, 그 소식은 아내를 겁에 질려 병이 나게 만들 터였다."]], [["But on the next morning there came to him tidings by the hands of Robin postman, which for a long while upset all his plans.", "그러나 이튿날 아침 우체부 로빈의 손을 거쳐 소식이 하나 닿았고, 그 소식은 오랫동안 그의 모든 계획을 헝클어 놓았다."], ["The letter was from Exeter.", "편지는 엑서터에서 온 것이었다."], ["His father had been taken ill, and had very quickly been pronounced to be in danger.", "아버지가 병으로 누웠고, 이내 위태롭다는 진단이 내려졌다는 것이었다."], ["That evening—the evening on which his sister wrote—the old man was much worse, and it was desirable that Mark should go off to Exeter as quickly as possible.", "그 저녁—누이가 편지를 쓴 그 저녁—에 노인은 훨씬 더 나빠졌으며, 마크가 되도록 빨리 엑서터로 떠나오는 것이 좋겠다고 했다."], ["Of course he went to Exeter—again leaving the Framley souls at the mercy of the Welsh Low Churchman.", "물론 그는 엑서터로 갔다. 프램리의 영혼들은 또 한 번 웨일스 출신 저교회파 목사의 처분에 맡겨졌다."], ["Framley is only four miles from Silverbridge, and at Silverbridge he was on the direct road to the west.", "프램리는 실버브리지에서 4마일밖에 떨어져 있지 않고, 실버브리지에서는 서쪽으로 가는 직행로에 오르게 된다."], ["He was therefore at Exeter before nightfall on that day.", "그래서 그는 그날 해가 지기 전에 엑서터에 닿았다."]], [["But nevertheless he arrived there too late to see his father again alive.", "그러나 그것으로도 아버지의 살아 있는 얼굴을 다시 보기에는 너무 늦었다."], ["The old man’s illness had been sudden and rapid, and he expired without again seeing his eldest son.", "노인의 병은 갑작스럽고 빨랐고, 그는 맏아들을 다시 보지 못한 채 숨을 거두었다."], ["Mark arrived at the house of mourning just as they were learning to realize the full change in their position.", "마크가 상가에 이르렀을 때, 식구들은 마침 자기들의 처지가 온통 달라졌음을 실감하기 시작하던 참이었다."]], [["The doctor’s career had been on the whole successful, but nevertheless he did not leave behind him as much money as the world had given him credit for possessing.", "로바츠 선생의 이력은 대체로 성공적이었지만, 그럼에도 세상이 그가 가졌으리라 여겨 준 만큼의 돈을 남기지는 못했다."], ["Who ever does? Dr.", "누가 그러기나 하던가?"], ["Robarts had educated a large family, had always lived with every comfort, and had never possessed a shilling but what he had earned himself.", "로바츠 선생은 대가족을 교육시켰고, 언제나 온갖 안락을 누리며 살았으며, 스스로 벌지 않은 1실링은 가져 본 적이 없었다."], ["A physician’s fees come in, no doubt, with comfortable rapidity as soon as rich old gentlemen and middle-aged ladies begin to put their faith in him; but fees run out almost with equal rapidity when a wife and seven children are treated to everything that the world considers most desirable.", "부유한 노신사들과 중년 부인들이 그를 신뢰하기 시작하면 의사의 진료비란 과연 흐뭇할 만큼 빠르게 들어온다. 그러나 아내 하나와 아이 일곱에게 세상이 가장 바람직하다고 여기는 것을 다 해 주다 보면, 그 진료비는 들어오던 것과 거의 같은 속도로 빠져나간다."], ["Mark, we have seen, had been educated at Harrow and Oxford, and it may be said, therefore, that he had received his patrimony early in life.", "우리가 보았듯이 마크는 해로와 옥스퍼드에서 교육을 받았으니, 그는 제 몫의 유산을 인생의 이른 시기에 미리 받았다고 해도 되겠다. 둘째 형제인 제럴드 로바츠에게는 정예 연대의 장교직이 사들여졌다."], ["For Gerald Robarts, the second brother, a commission had been bought in a crack regiment.", "그 역시 운이 좋아 크림반도에서 살아남아 대위가 되었고, 소령 계급을 사들일 대금까지 예치되어 있었다."], ["He also had been lucky, having lived and become a captain in the Crimea; and the purchase-money was lodged for his majority.", "그리고 아들 가운데 막내인 존 로바츠는 프티백 청의 서기로, 벌써 프티백 경 본인의 개인 비서 보좌관 자리에까지 올라 있었다. 지금까지 봉급이 많은 자리는 아니었을지언정 신임이 상당히 두터운 자리였다."], ["And John Robarts, the youngest, was a clerk in the Petty Bag Office, and was already assistant private secretary to the Lord Petty Bag himself—a place of considerable trust, if not hitherto of large emolument; and on his education money had been spent freely, for in these days a young man cannot get into the Petty Bag Office without knowing at least three modern languages; and he must be well up in trigonometry too, in bible theology, or in one dead language—at his option.", "그의 교육에도 돈이 아낌없이 들어갔는데, 요즘은 젊은 사람이 현대 어학을 적어도 세 가지는 알지 못하면 프티백 청에 들어갈 수 없기 때문이다. 게다가 삼각법이나 성서 신학, 또는 사어 하나에도 정통해야 한다—그 셋 중 무엇을 택하느냐는 본인의 재량이다."]], [["And the doctor had four daughters.", "그리고 선생에게는 딸이 넷 있었다."], ["The two elder were married, including that Blanche with whom Lord Lufton was to have fallen in love at the vicar’s wedding.", "위의 두 딸은 이미 시집을 갔는데, 그중에는 교구 사제의 결혼식에서 러프턴 경이 반해 버렸어야 마땅했던 그 블랜치도 있었다."], ["A Devonshire squire had done this in the lord’s place; but on marrying her it was necessary that he should have a few thousand pounds, two or three perhaps, and the old doctor had managed that they should be forthcoming.", "경이 할 일을 데번셔의 어느 지주가 대신 해 주었다. 다만 그가 블랜치와 결혼하려면 몇천 파운드, 아마 2, 3천 파운드는 있어야 했고, 늙은 선생이 그 돈이 나오도록 마련해 주었다."], ["The elder also had not been sent away from the paternal mansion quite empty-handed.", "위의 딸도 아버지의 집을 아주 빈손으로 떠난 것은 아니었다."], ["There were, therefore, at the time of the doctor’s death two children left at home, of whom one only, Lucy, the younger, will come much across us in the course of our story.", "그러므로 선생이 세상을 떠날 무렵 집에 남아 있는 자식은 둘이었고, 그중 아래인 루시 하나만이 우리 이야기가 흘러가는 동안 우리 앞에 자주 나서게 될 것이다."]], [["Mark stayed for ten days at Exeter, he and the Devonshire squire having been named as executors in the will.", "마크는 엑서터에 열흘을 머물렀다. 그와 데번셔의 지주가 유언장에서 집행자로 지명되어 있었기 때문이다."], ["In this document it was explained that the doctor trusted that provision had been made for most of his children.", "그 문서에는 선생이 자기 자식들 대부분에 대해서는 이미 대비가 되어 있다고 믿는다는 뜻이 적혀 있었다."], ["As for his dear son Mark, he said, he was aware that he need be under no uneasiness.", "사랑하는 아들 마크에 대해서는, 그가 조금도 불안해할 필요가 없음을 자기는 알고 있다고 했다."], ["On hearing this read Mark smiled sweetly, and looked very gracious; but, nevertheless, his heart did sink somewhat within him, for there had been a hope that a small windfall, coming now so opportunely, might enable him to rid himself at once of that dreadful Sowerby incubus.", "그 대목을 읽어 주는 것을 듣고 마크는 곱게 웃으며 아주 너그러운 표정을 지었다. 그러면서도 가슴은 속으로 다소 내려앉았다."], ["And then the will went on to declare that Mary, and Gerald, and Blanche, had also, by God’s providence, been placed beyond want.", "마침맞은 때에 떨어지는 자그마한 횡재가 저 끔찍한 사워비 씨의 몽마를 단번에 떨어낼 수 있게 해 주지 않을까 하는 바람이 있었던 것이다."], ["And here, looking into the squire’s face, one might have thought that his heart fell a little also; for he had not so full a command of his feelings as his brother-in-law, who had been so much more before the world.", "이어 유언장은 메리와 제럴드와 블랜치도 하느님의 섭리로 궁핍을 면할 자리에 놓였음을 선언했다. 그리고 이 대목에서 지주의 얼굴을 들여다본 사람이라면 그의 가슴도 조금 내려앉았다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["To John, the assistant private secretary, was left a legacy of a thousand pounds; and to Jane and Lucy certain sums in certain four per cents., which were quite sufficient to add an efficient value to the hands of those young ladies in the eyes of most prudent young would-be Benedicts.", "세상에 훨씬 더 많이 나서 본 처남만큼 자기 감정을 잘 다스리지는 못했으니까. 개인 비서 보좌관인 존에게는 1,000파운드의 유산이 남겨졌고, 제인과 루시에게는 4퍼센트 공채로 얼마씩이 남겨졌는데, 이는 장가들 마음을 품은 신중한 젊은 베네딕들의 눈에 그 아가씨들의 손 값을 실속 있게 올려 주기에 충분한 액수였다."], ["Over and beyond this there was nothing but the furniture, which he desired might be sold, and the proceeds divided among them all.", "그 밖에는 가구뿐이었고, 선생은 그것을 팔아 그 돈을 모두에게 나누어 주기를 바랐다."], ["It might come to sixty or seventy pounds a piece, and pay the expenses incidental on his death.", "한 사람 앞에 60이나 70파운드쯤은 될 터였으니, 장례에 딸린 비용을 치를 만했다."]], [["And then all men and women there and thereabouts said that old Dr. Robarts had done well.", "그러고 나서 그곳과 그 근방의 남녀노소가 다들 늙은 로바츠 선생이 잘했다고 말했다."], ["His life had been good and prosperous, and his will was just.", "그의 삶은 훌륭하고 넉넉했으며, 그의 유언은 공정했다."], ["And Mark, among others, so declared,—and was so convinced in spite of his own little disappointment.", "마크도 여러 사람과 함께 그렇게 말했고, 제 나름의 작은 실망에도 불구하고 정말로 그렇게 믿었다."], ["And on the third morning after the reading of the will Squire Crowdy, of Creamclotted Hall, altogether got over his grief, and said that it was all right.", "그리고 유언장을 읽은 지 사흘째 아침에 크림클로티드 홀의 크라우디 지주는 슬픔을 완전히 털어 버리고, 다 잘된 일이라고 말했다."], ["And then it was decided that Jane should go home with him,—for there was a brother squire who, it was thought, might have an eye to Jane;—and Lucy, the younger, should be taken to Framley Parsonage.", "이어 제인은 그와 함께 그의 집으로 가는 것으로 정해졌다. 제인에게 마음을 두었을지도 모른다고들 여기는 이웃 지주가 하나 있었기 때문이다."], ["In a fortnight from the receipt of that letter Mark arrived at his own house with his sister Lucy under his wing.", "그리고 아래인 루시는 프램리 목사관으로 데려가기로 했다. 그 편지를 받은 지 보름 만에 마크는 누이 루시를 날개 밑에 품고 자기 집에 이르렀다."]], [["All this interfered greatly with Mark’s wise resolution as to the Sowerby-bill incubus.", "이 모든 일이 사워비 씨의 어음이라는 몽마에 관한 마크의 현명한 결심을 크게 방해했다."], ["In the first place he could not get to Barchester as soon as he had intended, and then an idea came across him that possibly it might be well that he should borrow the money of his brother John, explaining the circumstances, of course, and paying him due interest.", "우선 그는 작정했던 만큼 빨리 바체스터에 갈 수 없었다. 그러다 어쩌면 동생 존에게서 그 돈을 빌리는 것이 좋을지도 모른다는 생각이 스쳤다."], ["But he had not liked to broach the subject when they were there in Exeter, standing, as it were, over their father’s grave, and so the matter was postponed.", "물론 사정을 설명하고 응당한 이자를 물면서 말이다. 그러나 엑서터에서, 말하자면 아버지의 무덤 앞에 서 있는 동안에는 그 이야기를 꺼내고 싶지 않았고, 그래서 그 일은 뒤로 밀렸다."], ["There was still ample time for arrangement before the bill would come due, and he would not tell Fanny till he had made up his mind what that arrangement would be.", "어음의 기일이 오기까지는 처리할 시간이 아직 넉넉했고, 그는 그 처리를 어떻게 할지 마음을 정하기 전까지는 패니에게 말하지 않을 작정이었다."], ["It would kill her, he said to himself over and over again, were he to tell her of it without being able to tell her also that the means of liquidating the debt were to be forthcoming.", "빚을 청산할 방도도 함께 말해 줄 수 없는 채로 그 이야기를 꺼낸다면 아내는 죽고 말 것이라고, 그는 몇 번이고 스스로에게 되뇌었다."]], [["And now I must say a word about Lucy Robarts.", "그러니 이제 나는 루시 로바츠에 대해 한마디 해야 한다."], ["If one might only go on without those descriptions, how pleasant it would all be!", "이런 묘사 없이 그냥 넘어갈 수만 있다면 얼마나 즐거운 일이겠는가!"], ["But Lucy Robarts has to play a forward part in this little drama, and those who care for such matters must be made to understand something of her form and likeness.", "그러나 루시 로바츠는 이 작은 극에서 앞자리를 맡아야 하고, 그런 것에 마음을 쓰는 분들에게는 그녀의 모양과 됨됨이를 어느 만큼 알려 드리지 않을 수 없다."], ["When last we mentioned her as appearing, though not in any prominent position, at her brother’s wedding, she was only sixteen; but now, at the time of her father’s death, somewhat over two years having since elapsed, she was nearly nineteen.", "지난번에 우리가 그녀를 언급했을 때, 그러니까 두드러진 자리는 아니었으나 오라비의 결혼식에 나타났을 때 그녀는 겨우 열여섯이었다. 그러나 이제 아버지가 세상을 떠난 시점에는 그 뒤로 두 해가 좀 넘게 지나 열아홉에 가까워져 있었다."], ["Laying aside for the sake of clearness that indefinite term of girl—for girls are girls from the age of three up to forty-three, if not previously married—dropping that generic word, we may say that then, at that wedding of her brother, she was a child; and now, at the death of her father, she was a woman.", "분명하게 하기 위해 ‘소녀’라는 저 애매한 말은 치워 두자—미리 시집을 가지 않는다면 소녀란 세 살부터 마흔세 살까지 소녀이니 말이다. 그 두루뭉술한 낱말을 버리고 말한다면, 오라비의 그 결혼식에서 그녀는 아이였고, 아버지가 세상을 떠난 지금 그녀는 여자였다."]], [["Nothing, perhaps, adds so much to womanhood, turns the child so quickly into a woman, as such death-bed scenes as these.", "이런 임종의 자리만큼 여자다움을 보태 주는 것, 아이를 그토록 빨리 여자로 바꾸어 놓는 것은 아마 없을 것이다."], ["Hitherto but little had fallen to Lucy to do in the way of woman’s duties.", "그때까지 루시가 여자의 소임이라 할 만한 일로 해 온 것은 별로 없었다."], ["Of money transactions she had known nothing, beyond a jocose attempt to make her annual allowance of twenty-five pounds cover all her personal wants—an attempt which was made jocose by the loving bounty of her father.", "돈거래에 관해서는 해마다 받는 25파운드의 용돈으로 자기 씀씀이를 다 감당해 보겠다는 장난스러운 시도밖에는 아는 것이 없었다. 그 시도를 장난이 되게 만든 것은 아버지의 사랑 넘치는 후함이었다."], ["Her sister, who was three years her elder—for John came in between them—had managed the house; that is, she had made the tea and talked to the housekeeper about the dinners.", "살림은 그녀보다 세 살 위인 언니가 맡았는데—두 사람 사이에 존이 끼어 있었다—말하자면 차를 끓이고 가정부와 저녁 식탁에 대해 이야기하는 일이었다."], ["But Lucy had sat at her father’s elbow, had read to him of evenings when he went to sleep, had brought him his slippers and looked after the comforts of his easy-chair.", "그러나 루시는 아버지의 팔꿈치 곁에 앉아 있었고, 저녁에 아버지가 잠들 때까지 책을 읽어 드렸고, 실내화를 가져다 드렸고, 안락의자가 편안한지 살폈다."], ["All this she had done as a child; but when she stood at the coffin head, and knelt at the coffin side, then she was a woman.", "이 모든 것을 그녀는 아이로서 했다. 그러나 관 머리에 서고 관 옆에 무릎을 꿇었을 때, 그녀는 여자였다."]], [["She was smaller in stature than either of her three sisters, to all of whom had been acceded the praise of being fine women—a eulogy which the people of Exeter, looking back at the elder sisters, and the general remembrance of them which pervaded the city, were not willing to extend to Lucy.", "그녀는 세 언니 누구보다도 키가 작았다. 언니들에게는 모두 훌륭한 여인이라는 칭찬이 돌아갔지만, 엑서터 사람들은 언니들을 돌아보고 도시에 두루 남아 있는 그들의 기억을 떠올리면서, 그 칭찬만은 루시에게까지 넓혀 줄 마음이 없었다."], ["“Dear—dear!”", "“아유, 아유!”"], ["had been said of her; “poor Lucy is not like a Robarts at all; is she, now, Mrs.", "하는 말이 그녀를 두고 나왔다. “가엾은 루시는 도무지 로바츠 집안 사람 같지가 않아요."], ["Pole?”—for as the daughters had become fine women, so had the sons grown into stalwart men.", "그렇지 않아요, 폴 부인?” 딸들이 훌륭한 여인이 되었듯이 아들들도 늠름한 사내로 자랐기 때문이다."], ["And then Mrs.", "그러면 폴 부인은 이렇게 대답했다."], ["Pole had answered: “Not a bit; is she, now?", "“털끝만큼도 안 그렇지요, 안 그래요?"], ["Only think what Blanche was at her age.", "블랜치가 저 아이 나이에 어땠는지 한번 생각해 보세요."], ["But she has fine eyes, for all that; and they do say she is the cleverest of them all.”", "그래도 눈만은 고운 눈을 가졌어요. 그리고 그 집에서 제일 똑똑한 아이라는 말이 있기는 하지요.”"]], [["And that, too, is so true a description of her, that I do not know that I can add much to it.", "그리고 그것은 그녀에 대한 참으로 정확한 묘사여서, 내가 여기에 무엇을 더 보탤 수 있을지 모르겠다."], ["She was not like Blanche; for Blanche had a bright complexion, and a fine neck, and a noble bust, et vera incessu patuit Dea—a true goddess, that is, as far as the eye went.", "그녀는 블랜치와 같지 않았다. 블랜치는 환한 얼굴빛과 고운 목덜미와 당당한 가슴을 가졌으니, 「에트 베라 인케수 파투이트 데아」—즉, 눈으로 보는 한에서는 진짜 여신이었다."], ["She had a grand idea, moreover, of an apple-pie, and had not reigned eighteen months at Creamclotted Hall before she knew all the mysteries of pigs and milk, and most of those appertaining to cider and green geese.", "게다가 그녀는 애플파이에 대해 훌륭한 식견을 지녔고, 크림클로티드 홀에서 군림한 지 열여덟 달이 되기도 전에 돼지와 우유에 관한 온갖 비밀과 사과술과 어린 거위에 관한 비밀 대부분을 알아 버렸다."]], [["Lucy had no neck at all worth speaking of,—no neck, I mean, that ever produced eloquence; she was brown, too, and had addicted herself in nowise, as she undoubtedly should have done, to larder utility.", "루시에게는 말할 만한 목덜미라는 것이 도무지 없었다—내 말은, 누구의 입에서 찬사를 끌어낸 적이 있는 목덜미가 없었다는 뜻이다. 게다가 살빛이 검었고, 마땅히 그러했어야 함이 분명한데도 식품 저장실에서 쓸모 있는 일에는 조금도 몸을 담그지 않았다."], ["In regard to the neck and colour, poor girl, she could not help herself; but in that other respect she must be held as having wasted her opportunities.", "목덜미와 살빛에 관해서라면, 가엾은 아이, 저로서는 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 그러나 그 다른 한 가지에 관해서라면 기회를 헛되이 썼다고 할 수밖에 없다."]], [["But then what eyes she had!", "그러나 그 눈이라니! 폴 부인의 말이 옳았다."], ["Mrs. Pole was right there.", "그 눈은 사람을 향해 번쩍였다—언제나 부드럽게는 아니었다."], ["They flashed upon you—not always softly; indeed not often softly, if you were a stranger to her; but whether softly or savagely, with a brilliancy that dazzled you as you looked at them.", "그녀와 낯선 사이라면 부드럽게 번쩍이는 일은 실로 드물었다. 그러나 부드럽든 거칠든, 바라보는 사람의 눈이 부실 만큼 빛났다."], ["And who shall say of what colour they were?", "그리고 그 눈이 무슨 색이었다고 누가 말할 수 있으랴? 아마 초록이었을 것이다."], ["Green probably, for most eyes are green—green or grey, if green be thought uncomely for an eye-colour.", "대개의 눈은 초록이니까. 초록이 눈 색으로 어울리지 않는다고 여겨진다면 초록이나 회색이라고 해 두자."], ["But it was not their colour, but their fire, which struck one with such surprise.", "그러나 그렇게 사람을 놀라게 한 것은 그 색이 아니라 그 안의 불이었다."]], [["Lucy Robarts was thoroughly a brunette.", "루시 로바츠는 철저하게 갈색 머리 여인이었다."], ["Sometimes the dark tint of her cheek was exquisitely rich and lovely, and the fringes of her eyes were long and soft, and her small teeth, which one so seldom saw, were white as pearls, and her hair, though short, was beautifully soft—by no means black, but yet of so dark a shade of brown.", "어떤 때는 뺨의 짙은 빛깔이 말할 수 없이 그윽하고 사랑스러웠고, 눈시울의 속눈썹은 길고 부드러웠고, 좀처럼 보이지 않는 작은 이는 진주처럼 희었고, 머리카락은 짧았어도 아름답게 부드러웠다—결코 검지는 않고, 그러면서도 아주 짙은 갈색이었다."], ["Blanche, too, was noted for fine teeth.", "블랜치도 고운 이로 이름이 났었다."], ["They were white and regular and lofty as a new row of houses in a French city.", "프랑스 어느 도시에 새로 들어선 집들처럼 희고 고르고 높직한 이였다."], ["But then when she laughed she was all teeth; as she was all neck when she sat at the piano.", "그러나 그녀가 웃으면 온몸이 이뿐이었다. 피아노 앞에 앉으면 온몸이 목덜미뿐이었던 것처럼."], ["But Lucy’s teeth!—it was only now and again, when in some sudden burst of wonder she would sit for a moment with her lips apart, that the fine finished lines and dainty pearl-white colour of that perfect set of ivory could be seen. Mrs.", "그런데 루시의 이라니!—어쩌다 한 번, 문득 놀라움이 터져 나와 입술을 벌린 채 잠깐 앉아 있을 때에만, 그 완벽한 상아 한 벌의 곱게 다듬어진 선과 우아한 진주빛 흰색을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Pole would have said a word of her teeth also, but that to her they had never been made visible.", "폴 부인도 그녀의 이에 대해서 한마디 했을 것이다. 다만 폴 부인 앞에서는 그 이가 한 번도 드러난 적이 없었다."]], [["“But they do say that she is the cleverest of them all,”", "“그래도 그 집에서 제일 똑똑한 아이라는 말이 있기는 하지요.”"], ["Mrs. Pole had added, very properly.", "폴 부인은 아주 온당하게 그렇게 덧붙였다."], ["The people of Exeter had expressed such an opinion, and had been quite just in doing so.", "엑서터 사람들이 그런 의견을 내놓았고, 그렇게 한 것은 지극히 옳았다."], ["I do not know how it happens, but it always does happen, that everybody in every small town knows which is the brightest-witted in every family.", "어떻게 그렇게 되는지는 모르겠으나, 작은 고을에서는 어느 집에서 누가 가장 머리가 밝은지를 누구나 다 아는 일이 언제나 벌어진다."], ["In this respect Mrs. Pole had only expressed public opinion, and public opinion was right.", "이 점에서 폴 부인은 여론을 그대로 옮긴 것일 뿐이었고, 그 여론은 옳았다."], ["Lucy Robarts was blessed with an intelligence keener than that of her brothers or sisters.", "루시 로바츠는 오라비들이나 언니들보다 예리한 지성을 타고났다."]], [["“To tell the truth, Mark, I admire Lucy more than I do Blanche.”", "“사실을 말하면요, 마크, 저는 블랜치보다 루시가 더 마음에 들어요.”"], ["This had been said by Mrs. Robarts within a few hours of her having assumed that name.", "로바츠 부인이 그 이름을 얻은 지 몇 시간도 되지 않아 한 말이었다."], ["“She’s not a beauty, I know, but yet I do.”", "“미인이 아닌 건 저도 알아요. 그래도 그런걸요.”"]], [["“My dearest Fanny!”", "“내 사랑 패니!”"], ["Mark had answered in a tone of surprise.", "마크가 놀란 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“I do then; of course people won’t think so; but I never seem to care about regular beauties.", "“정말 그래요. 물론 남들은 그렇게 생각하지 않겠지요."], ["Perhaps I envy them too much.”", "그런데 저는 반듯한 미인들에게는 도무지 마음이 가지 않는 것 같아요. 어쩌면 그들을 너무 부러워하기 때문인지도 모르지요.”"]], [["What Mark said next need not be repeated, but everybody may be sure that it contained some gross flattery for his young bride.", "마크가 그다음에 한 말은 되풀이할 필요가 없겠으나, 그 안에 젊은 신부를 향한 낯 두꺼운 아첨이 얼마간 들어 있었으리라는 것은 누구든 확실히 짐작해도 좋다."], ["He remembered this, however, and had always called Lucy his wife’s pet.", "다만 그는 이 일을 기억해 두었고, 그 뒤로 늘 루시를 아내가 아끼는 아이라고 불렀다."], ["Neither of the sisters had since that been at Framley; and though Fanny had spent a week at Exeter on the occasion of Blanche’s marriage, it could hardly be said that she was very intimate with them.", "그때 이후로 누이들 가운데 프램리에 와 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그리고 패니가 블랜치의 결혼식 때문에 엑서터에서 한 주를 보낸 적은 있으나, 그녀가 시누이들과 아주 가까운 사이였다고는 하기 어려웠다."], ["Nevertheless, when it became expedient that one of them should go to Framley, the remembrance of what his wife had said immediately induced Mark to make the offer to Lucy; and Jane, who was of a kindred soul with Blanche, was delighted to go to Creamclotted Hall.", "그럼에도 둘 중 하나가 프램리로 가는 것이 마땅해졌을 때, 아내가 했던 말의 기억이 마크로 하여금 곧바로 루시에게 그 제안을 하게 만들었다. 그리고 블랜치와 마음이 닮은 제인은 크림클로티드 홀로 가는 것을 기뻐했다."], ["The acres of Heavybed House, down in that fat Totnes country, adjoined those of Creamclotted Hall, and Heavybed House still wanted a mistress.", "저 기름진 토트니스 지방 아래쪽에 있는 헤비베드 하우스의 땅이 크림클로티드 홀의 땅과 맞닿아 있었고, 헤비베드 하우스에는 아직 안주인이 없었던 것이다."]], [["Fanny was delighted when the news reached her.", "소식이 닿았을 때 패니는 몹시 기뻐했다."], ["It would of course be proper that one of his sisters should live with Mark under their present circumstances, and she was happy to think that that quiet little bright-eyed creature was to come and nestle with her under the same roof.", "지금의 사정으로 보면 시누이들 가운데 하나가 마크와 함께 사는 것이 물론 온당한 일이었고, 그 조용하고 자그마한 눈 밝은 아이가 와서 같은 지붕 아래 저와 함께 깃들게 된다는 생각에 그녀는 마음이 흐뭇했다. 아이들도 그 고모를 몹시 사랑할 것이다—다만 엄마를 사랑하는 만큼까지는 아니고."], ["The children should so love her—only not quite so much as they loved mamma; and the snug little room that looks out over the porch, in which the chimney never smokes, should be made ready for her; and she should be allowed her share of driving the pony—which was a great sacrifice of self on the part of Mrs.", "그리고 현관 위로 내다보이는, 굴뚝이 도무지 연기를 내지 않는 그 아늑한 작은 방을 그녀를 위해 마련해 주어야지. 그리고 조랑말을 몰 차례도 나누어 주어야지—이것은 로바츠 부인 쪽에서는 큰 자기희생이었다."], ["Robarts—and Lady Lufton’s best good-will should be bespoken.", "그리고 레이디 러프턴의 가장 좋은 호의도 미리 청해 두어야지."], ["In fact, Lucy was not unfortunate in the destination that was laid out for her.", "사실 루시는 자기에게 정해진 갈 곳에 관해서라면 불운하지 않았다."]], [["Lady Lufton had of course heard of the doctor’s death, and had sent all manner of kind messages to Mark, advising him not to hurry home by any means until everything was settled at Exeter.", "레이디 러프턴도 물론 선생의 죽음을 들어 알고 있었고, 마크에게 온갖 자애로운 말을 전하며 엑서터의 일이 다 정리되기 전에는 무슨 일이 있어도 서둘러 돌아오지 말라고 일러 주었다."], ["And then she was told of the new-comer that was expected in the parish.", "그러고 나서 교구에 새 사람이 오기로 되어 있다는 이야기를 들었다."], ["When she heard that it was Lucy, the younger, she also was satisfied; for Blanche’s charms, though indisputable, had not been altogether to her taste.", "그 사람이 아래인 루시라는 말을 듣고 그분도 마음이 놓였다. 블랜치의 매력은 나무랄 데가 없었지만 아무래도 그분의 취향에는 맞지 않았기 때문이다."], ["If a second Blanche were to arrive there what danger might there not be for young Lord Lufton!", "두 번째 블랜치가 그곳에 온다면 젊은 러프턴 경에게 어떤 위험이 닥치지 않겠는가!"]], [["“Quite right,”", "“아주 잘했어요.” 마님이 말했다."], ["said her ladyship, “just what he ought to do.", "“마땅히 그렇게 해야 하지요. 그 아가씨를 기억하는 것 같아요."], ["I think I remember the young lady; rather small, is she not, and very retiring?”", "몸집이 좀 작지 않았나요? 그리고 아주 내성적이었지요?”"]], [["“Rather small and very retiring.", "“몸집이 좀 작고 아주 내성적이라니."], ["What a description!”", "그게 무슨 묘사예요!”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."]], [["“Never mind, Ludovic; some young ladies must be small, and some at least ought to be retiring.", "“신경 쓰지 마라, 루도빅. 아가씨들 중에도 몸집이 작은 사람은 있어야 하고, 적어도 몇은 내성적이어야 하는 거야."], ["We shall be delighted to make her acquaintance.”", "우리는 그 아가씨와 알게 되어 기쁠 거예요.”"]], [["“I remember your other sister-in-law very well,”", "“저는 부인의 다른 시누이를 아주 잘 기억해요.”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."], ["“She was a beautiful woman.”", "“아름다운 여인이었지요.”"]], [["“I don’t think you will consider Lucy a beauty,”", "“루시를 미인으로 보시지는 않을 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Small, retiring, and—”", "“몸집이 작고, 내성적이고, 그리고—”"], ["so far Lord Lufton had gone, when Mrs. Robarts finished by the word, “plain.”", "러프턴 경이 여기까지 갔을 때 로바츠 부인이 “못생겼다”는 말로 끝을 맺어 주었다."], ["She had liked Lucy’s face, but she had thought that others probably did not do so.", "그녀는 루시의 얼굴이 마음에 들었으나, 다른 사람들은 아마 그렇지 않을 것이라고 생각했던 것이다."]], [["“Upon my word,”", "“어머나, 정말이지.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, “you don’t deserve to have a sister-in-law.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그런 말을 하니 시누이를 둘 자격이 없어요."], ["I remember her very well, and can say that she is not plain.", "나는 그 아가씨를 아주 잘 기억하는데, 못생기지 않았다고 말할 수 있어요."], ["I was very much taken with her manner at your wedding, my dear; and thought more of her than I did of the beauty, I can tell you.”", "나는 그대 결혼식에서 그 아가씨의 몸가짐이 무척 마음에 들었어요. 그리고 솔직히 말하면 그 미인보다 그 아가씨를 더 높이 보았지요.”"]], [["“I must confess I do not remember her at all,”", "“저는 그 아가씨가 전혀 기억나지 않는다고 고백해야겠군요.”"], ["said his lordship.", "경이 말했다."], ["And so the conversation ended.", "그리고 그렇게 대화는 끝났다."]], [["And then at the end of the fortnight Mark arrived with his sister.", "그리고 보름이 끝날 무렵 마크가 누이를 데리고 도착했다."], ["They did not reach Framley till long after dark—somewhere between six and seven—and by this time it was December.", "두 사람이 프램리에 이른 것은 어두워진 지 한참 뒤인 여섯 시와 일곱 시 사이 어름이었고, 이때는 벌써 12월이었다."], ["There was snow on the ground, and frost in the air, and no moon, and cautious men when they went on the roads had their horses’ shoes cocked.", "땅에는 눈이 있었고 공기에는 서릿발이 섰고 달은 없었으며, 조심성 있는 사람들은 길을 나설 때 말굽에 미끄럼막이 징을 박았다."], ["Such being the state of the weather Mark’s gig had been nearly filled with cloaks and shawls when it was sent over to Silverbridge.", "날씨가 그런 형편이었으므로 마크의 이륜마차는 실버브리지로 보내질 때 외투와 숄로 거의 가득 찼다."], ["And a cart was sent for Lucy’s luggage, and all manner of preparations had been made.", "그리고 루시의 짐을 실을 수레도 보냈고, 온갖 준비가 다 되어 있었다."], ["Three times had Fanny gone herself to see that the fire burned brightly in the little room over the porch, and at the moment that the sound of the wheels was heard she was engaged in opening her son’s mind as to the nature of an aunt.", "패니는 현관 위 작은 방의 불이 환하게 타고 있는지 몸소 세 번이나 가서 보았고, 바퀴 소리가 들려온 순간에는 고모라는 것이 어떤 존재인지를 아들의 머리에 열어 주는 일에 매달려 있었다."], ["Hitherto papa and mamma and Lady Lufton were all that he had known, excepting, of course, the satellites of the nursery.", "그때까지 아들이 알고 있던 사람은 아빠와 엄마와 레이디 러프턴뿐이었다. 물론 아기방의 위성들은 빼고서."]], [["And then in three minutes Lucy was standing by the fire.", "그러고서 삼 분 뒤에 루시는 난롯가에 서 있었다."], ["Those three minutes had been taken up in embraces between the husband and the wife.", "그 삼 분은 남편과 아내가 서로 껴안는 데 쓰였다."], ["Let who would be brought as a visitor to the house, after a fortnight’s absence, she would kiss him before she welcomed any one else.", "누구를 손님으로 집에 데려왔든, 보름을 떠나 있었다면 그녀는 다른 누구를 맞이하기 전에 먼저 남편에게 입을 맞출 사람이었다."], ["But then she turned to Lucy, and began to assist her with her cloaks.", "그러나 곧 루시에게로 돌아서서 외투를 벗는 것을 돕기 시작했다."]], [["“Oh, thank you,”", "“아, 고마워요.” 루시가 말했다."], ["said Lucy; “I’m not cold,—not very at least.", "“저는 춥지 않아요. 적어도 아주 춥지는 않아요."], ["Don’t trouble yourself: I can do it.”", "수고하지 마세요. 제가 할 수 있어요.”"], ["But here she had made a false boast, for her fingers had been so numbed that she could not do nor undo anything.", "그러나 여기서 그녀는 거짓 호언을 한 것이었다. 손가락이 너무 얼어서 무엇을 채우지도 풀지도 못했으니 말이다."]], [["They were all in black, of course; but the sombreness of Lucy’s clothes struck Fanny much more than her own.", "물론 모두 검은 옷을 입고 있었다. 그러나 루시의 옷이 지닌 침울함은 자기 옷의 침울함보다 훨씬 더 패니의 마음을 쳤다."], ["They seemed to have swallowed her up in their blackness, and to have made her almost an emblem of death.", "그 옷은 검은 빛으로 그 아이를 통째로 삼켜 버린 듯했고, 그 아이를 거의 죽음의 표상으로 만들어 놓은 듯했다."], ["She did not look up, but kept her face turned towards the fire, and seemed almost afraid of her position.", "루시는 눈을 들지 않고 얼굴을 난롯불 쪽으로 돌린 채, 자기가 놓인 자리를 거의 두려워하는 듯이 보였다."]], [["“She may say what she likes, Fanny,”", "“저 아이가 뭐라고 하든, 패니.”"], ["said Mark, “but she is very cold.", "마크가 말했다. “저 아이는 몹시 추워."], ["And so am I,—cold enough.", "나도 그렇고—꽤 춥다."], ["You had better go up with her to her room.", "저 아이를 데리고 방으로 올라가는 게 좋겠어."], ["We won’t do much in the dressing way to-night; eh, Lucy?”", "오늘 밤은 옷 갈아입는 일로 애쓰지 말자. 그렇지, 루시?”"]], [["In the bedroom Lucy thawed a little, and Fanny, as she kissed her, said to herself that she had been wrong as to that word “plain.”", "침실에서 루시는 조금 녹았고, 패니는 그녀에게 입을 맞추면서 “못생겼다”는 그 말에 관해서는 자기가 틀렸다고 속으로 말했다."], ["Lucy, at any rate, was not plain.", "적어도 루시는 못생기지 않았다."]], [["“You will be used to us soon,”", "“곧 우리에게 익숙해질 거예요.”"], ["said Fanny, “and then I hope we shall make you comfortable.”", "패니가 말했다. “그러고 나면 우리가 아가씨를 편안하게 해 줄 수 있으면 좋겠어요.”"], ["And she took her sister-in-law’s hand and pressed it.", "그러고서 시누이의 손을 잡아 꼭 눌렀다."]], [["Lucy looked up at her, and her eyes then were tender enough.", "루시가 그녀를 올려다보았고, 그때 그 눈은 넉넉히 부드러웠다."], ["“I am sure I shall be happy here,”", "“저는 여기서 틀림없이 행복할 거예요.”"], ["she said, “with you.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["But—but—dear papa!”", "“이렇게 함께라면요."], ["And then they got into each other’s arms, and had a great bout of kissing and crying.", "하지만—하지만—아버지가!” 그러고서 두 사람은 서로의 품에 안겨 한바탕 입을 맞추고 울었다."], ["“Plain,”", "“못생겼다니.”"], ["said Fanny to herself, as at last she got her guest’s hair smoothed and the tears washed from her eyes—“plain!", "마침내 손님의 머리를 매만져 주고 눈에서 눈물을 씻어 준 뒤에 패니는 속으로 말했다."], ["She has the loveliest countenance that I ever looked at in my life!”", "“못생겼다니! 내 평생 본 얼굴 가운데 가장 사랑스러운 얼굴인데!”"]], [["“Your sister is quite beautiful,”", "“당신 누이는 아주 아름다워요.”"], ["she said to Mark, as they talked her over alone before they went to sleep that night.", "그날 밤 잠자리에 들기 전에 둘이서만 그 아이에 대해 이야기하며 그녀가 마크에게 말했다."]], [["“No, she’s not beautiful; but she’s a very good girl, and clever enough too, in her sort of way.”", "“아니야, 아름답지는 않아. 하지만 아주 착한 아이지. 그리고 제 나름으로는 꽤 영리하기도 하고.”"]], [["“I think her perfectly lovely.", "“저는 그 아이가 완벽하게 사랑스러워요."], ["I never saw such eyes in my life before.”", "저는 평생 그런 눈은 본 적이 없어요.”"]], [["“I’ll leave her in your hands then; you shall get her a husband.”", "“그럼 그 아이는 당신 손에 맡기지. 당신이 남편을 하나 구해 줘.”"]], [["“That mayn’t be so easy.", "“그게 그리 쉽지는 않을 거예요."], ["I don’t think she’d marry anybody.”", "그 아이는 아무하고도 결혼하지 않을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Well, I hope not.", "“글쎄, 나도 그러기를 바라."], ["But she seems to me to be exactly cut out for an old maid;—to be aunt Lucy for ever and ever to your bairns.”", "하지만 내가 보기에 그 아이는 딱 노처녀로 마름질된 사람 같아. 영원토록 당신 꼬맹이들의 루시 고모로 지낼 사람이지.”"]], [["“And so she shall, with all my heart.", "“그렇게 되기를 저도 진심으로 바라요."], ["But I don’t think she will, very long.", "하지만 그리 오래 그러지는 않을 것 같아요."], ["I have no doubt she will be hard to please; but if I were a man I should fall in love with her at once.", "그 아이 마음에 들기가 어려우리라는 건 저도 의심하지 않아요. 그래도 제가 남자라면 그 아이에게 단번에 사랑에 빠졌을 거예요."], ["Did you ever observe her teeth, Mark?”", "그 아이 이를 눈여겨본 적 있어요, 마크?”"]], [["“I don’t think I ever did.”", "“본 적이 없는 것 같군.”"]], [["“You wouldn’t know whether any one had a tooth in their head, I believe.”", "“당신은 누가 머리에 이를 하나라도 달고 있는지 없는지도 모를 사람이에요, 정말이지.”"]], [["“No one except you, my dear; and I know all yours by heart.”", "“당신만 빼고 아무도 모르지. 그리고 당신 이는 하나하나 다 외고 있다고.”"]], [["“You are a goose.”", "“당신은 거위예요.”"]], [["“And a very sleepy one; so, if you please, I’ll go to roost.”", "“그것도 아주 졸린 거위지. 그러니 미안하지만 나는 홰에 올라가겠어.”"], ["And thus there was nothing more said about Lucy’s beauty on that occasion.", "그리하여 그날은 루시의 아름다움에 대해 더 이야기되는 것이 없었다."]], [["For the first two days Mrs. Robarts did not make much of her sister-in-law.", "처음 이틀 동안 로바츠 부인은 시누이와 그다지 가까워지지 못했다."], ["Lucy, indeed, was not demonstrative; and she was, moreover, one of those few persons—for they are very few—who are contented to go on with their existence without making themselves the centre of any special outward circle.", "루시는 과연 감정을 드러내는 사람이 아니었다. 게다가 그녀는—참으로 아주 드문 사람들이지만—어떤 특별한 바깥 동그라미의 중심에 자기를 앉히지 않고도 그저 살아가는 것으로 만족하는 그 몇 안 되는 사람 가운데 하나였다."], ["To the ordinary run of minds it is impossible not to do this.", "보통 사람들의 마음으로는 그러지 않기가 불가능하다."], ["A man’s own dinner is to himself so important that he cannot bring himself to believe that it is a matter utterly indifferent to every one else.", "사람은 자기 저녁 식사가 자기에게 너무나 중요해서, 그것이 다른 모든 사람에게는 조금도 상관없는 일이라는 것을 도무지 믿을 수 없다."], ["A lady’s collection of baby-clothes, in early years, and of house linen and curtain-fringes in later life, is so very interesting to her own eyes, that she cannot believe but what other people will rejoice to behold it.", "젊은 시절에는 아기 옷을, 나이가 든 뒤에는 집안 리넨과 커튼 술 장식을 모아 놓은 것이 부인네 자신의 눈에는 너무나 흥미로워서, 다른 사람들도 그것을 보고 기뻐하지 않으리라고는 믿을 수 없는 것이다."], ["I would not, however, be held as regarding this tendency as evil.", "그렇다고 내가 이 성향을 나쁜 것으로 여긴다고 받아들여지지는 않기를 바란다."], ["It leads to conversation of some sort among people, and perhaps to a kind of sympathy. Mrs.", "그것은 사람들 사이에 어떻게든 이야기를 만들어 내고, 아마 일종의 공감도 만들어 낸다."], ["Jones will look at Mrs. White’s linen-chest, hoping that Mrs.", "존스 부인은 화이트 부인의 리넨 장을 들여다볼 것이다."], ["White may be induced to look at hers.", "화이트 부인도 자기 것을 들여다보게 되기를 바라면서."], ["One can only pour out of a jug that which is in it.", "항아리에서는 그 안에 든 것만 쏟아 낼 수 있다."], ["For the most of us, if we do not talk of ourselves, or at any rate of the individual circles of which we are the centres, we can talk of nothing.", "우리 대다수는 자기 이야기를, 적어도 자기가 그 중심인 저마다의 동그라미 이야기를 하지 않으면 아무 이야기도 할 수 없다."], ["I cannot hold with those who wish to put down the insignificant chatter of the world.", "그러니 세상의 대수롭지 않은 잡담을 눌러 없애고 싶어 하는 사람들에게 나는 동의할 수 없다."], ["As for myself, I am always happy to look at Mrs. Jones’s linen, and never omit an opportunity of giving her the details of my own dinners.", "나로서는 존스 부인의 리넨을 들여다보는 일이 언제나 즐겁고, 그 부인에게 내 저녁 식사의 세목을 늘어놓을 기회는 한 번도 빼놓지 않는다."]], [["But Lucy Robarts had not this gift.", "그러나 루시 로바츠에게는 이 재주가 없었다."], ["She had come there as a stranger into her sister-in-law’s house, and at first seemed as though she would be contented in simply having her corner in the drawing-room and her place at the parlour-table.", "그녀는 낯선 사람으로 올케의 집에 들어왔고, 처음에는 응접실의 한 구석과 거실 식탁의 한 자리를 얻은 것만으로 만족할 사람처럼 보였다."], ["She did not seem to need the comforts of condolence and open-hearted talking.", "위로의 말과 속을 터놓는 이야기가 주는 편안함을 필요로 하지 않는 듯했다."], ["I do not mean to say that she was moody, that she did not answer when she was spoken to, or that she took no notice of the children; but she did not at once throw herself and all her hopes and sorrows into Fanny’s heart, as Fanny would have had her do.", "그녀가 침울했다거나, 말을 걸어도 대답하지 않았다거나, 아이들을 아랑곳하지 않았다는 뜻은 아니다. 다만 그녀는 패니가 바랐을 만큼 자기 자신과 자기의 모든 희망과 슬픔을 곧바로 패니의 가슴에 던져 넣지는 않았다."]], [["Mrs. Robarts herself was what we call demonstrative.", "로바츠 부인 자신은 우리가 이르는바 감정을 드러내는 사람이었다."], ["When she was angry with Lady Lufton she showed it.", "레이디 러프턴에게 화가 났을 때 그녀는 그것을 드러냈다."], ["And as since that time her love and admiration for Lady Lufton had increased, she showed that also.", "그리고 그때 이후로 레이디 러프턴에 대한 사랑과 흠모가 커졌으니, 그것 또한 드러냈다."], ["When she was in any way displeased with her husband, she could not hide it, even though she tried to do so, and fancied herself successful;—no more than she could hide her warm, constant, overflowing woman’s love.", "남편에게 어떤 식으로든 마음이 언짢을 때에는 감추려 애쓰고 스스로 잘 감추었다고 여기면서도 감출 수가 없었다. 따뜻하고 한결같고 넘쳐흐르는 여자의 사랑을 감출 수 없는 것과 마찬가지였다."], ["She could not walk through a room hanging on her husband’s arm without seeming to proclaim to every one there that she thought him the best man in it.", "남편의 팔에 매달려 방을 지나가면서, 자기는 이 방에서 이 사람이 가장 훌륭한 남자라고 여긴다는 것을 거기 있는 모든 사람에게 알리는 듯이 보이지 않을 수가 없었다."], ["She was demonstrative, and therefore she was the more disappointed in that Lucy did not rush at once with all her cares into her open heart.", "그녀는 감정을 드러내는 사람이었고, 그러므로 루시가 자기 근심을 다 안고 활짝 열린 자기 가슴으로 단번에 달려들지 않는 것이 그만큼 더 실망스러웠다."]], [["“She is so quiet,”", "“그 아이는 너무 조용해요.”"], ["Fanny said to her husband.", "패니가 남편에게 말했다."]], [["“That’s her nature,”", "“그게 저 아이 천성이야.”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."], ["“She always was quiet as a child.", "“어릴 때도 늘 조용했지."], ["While we were smashing everything, she would never crack a teacup.”", "우리가 온갖 것을 깨부수고 다닐 때 저 아이는 찻잔 하나도 금 가게 하는 일이 없었어.”"]], [["“I wish she would break something now,”", "“지금이라도 뭘 좀 깨뜨려 주면 좋겠어요.”"], ["said Fanny, “and then perhaps we should get to talk about it.”", "패니가 말했다. “그러면 어쩌면 그 이야기라도 하게 될 텐데요.”"], ["But she did not on this account give over loving her sister-in-law.", "그러나 그렇다고 해서 그녀가 시누이를 사랑하는 일을 그만둔 것은 아니었다."], ["She probably valued her the more, unconsciously, for not having those aptitudes with which she herself was endowed.", "아마 자기 자신에게 주어진 그 재주들을 그 아이가 갖고 있지 않다는 바로 그 점 때문에, 그녀는 자기도 모르게 그 아이를 더 소중히 여겼을 것이다."]], [["And then after two days Lady Lufton called; of course it may be supposed that Fanny had said a good deal to her new inmate about Lady Lufton.", "그리고 이틀이 지난 뒤 레이디 러프턴이 찾아왔다. 물론 패니가 새 식구에게 레이디 러프턴에 대해 어지간히 많이 이야기해 두었으리라고 짐작해도 좋다."], ["A neighbour of that kind in the country exercises so large an influence upon the whole tenor of one’s life, that to abstain from such talk is out of the question.", "시골에서 그런 이웃은 한 사람의 삶의 흐름 전체에 그토록 큰 영향을 미치므로, 그런 이야기를 삼가는 것은 애초에 있을 수 없는 일이다."], ["Mrs. Robarts had been brought up almost under the dowager’s wing, and of course she regarded her as being worthy of much talking.", "로바츠 부인은 거의 그 미망인 마님의 날개 밑에서 자랐으니, 물론 그분을 많이 이야기할 만한 가치가 있는 분으로 여겼다."], ["Do not let persons on this account suppose that Mrs. Robarts was a tuft-hunter, or a toad-eater.", "그렇다고 로바츠 부인을 귀족 뒤꽁무니나 쫓는 사람, 알랑쇠로 여기는 이는 없기를 바란다."], ["If they do not see the difference they have yet got to study the earliest principles of human nature.", "그 차이를 알아보지 못하는 사람이라면 인간 본성의 가장 기초적인 원리부터 아직 더 공부해야 한다."]], [["Lady Lufton called, and Lucy was struck dumb.", "레이디 러프턴이 찾아왔고, 루시는 벙어리가 되었다."], ["Fanny was particularly anxious that her ladyship’s first impression should be favourable, and to effect this, she especially endeavoured to throw the two together during that visit.", "패니는 마님의 첫인상이 호의적이기를 유난히 바랐고, 그렇게 되도록 하려고 그 방문 동안 두 사람을 한데 붙여 놓으려고 특히 애를 썼다. 그러나 이 점에서 그녀는 지혜롭지 못했다."], ["But in this she was unwise. Lady Lufton, however, had woman-craft enough not to be led into any egregious error by Lucy’s silence.", "그렇더라도 레이디 러프턴에게는 루시의 침묵 때문에 터무니없는 오판으로 끌려가지 않을 만한 여자의 안목이 넉넉히 있었다."]], [["“And what day will you come and dine with us?”", "“그래, 어느 날에 우리 집에 와서 저녁을 먹겠어요?”"], ["said Lady Lufton, turning expressly to her old friend Fanny.", "레이디 러프턴이 일부러 오랜 벗 패니 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, do you name the day.", "“아유, 날은 마님이 정해 주세요."], ["We never have many engagements, you know.”", "저희야 약속이 많은 적이 없다는 걸 아시잖아요.”"]], [["“Will Thursday do, Miss Robarts?", "“목요일이면 괜찮겠어요, 로바츠 양? 아는 사람은 아무도 만나지 않을 거예요."], ["You will meet nobody you know, only my son; so you need not regard it as going out.", "내 아들뿐이니까요. 그러니 나들이라고 여길 필요가 없어요."], ["Fanny here will tell you that stepping over to Framley Court is no more going out, than when you go from one room to another in the parsonage.", "여기 패니가 말해 줄 거예요. 프램리 코트에 올라가는 것은 목사관에서 이 방에서 저 방으로 건너가는 것보다 더 나들이일 것이 없다고요."], ["Is it, Fanny?”", "그렇지 않아요, 패니?”"]], [["Fanny laughed and said that that stepping over to Framley Court certainly was done so often that perhaps they did not think so much about it as they ought to do.", "패니는 웃으면서, 프램리 코트에 올라가는 일은 과연 너무 자주 하는 일이어서 어쩌면 자기들이 마땅히 여겨야 할 만큼 대단하게 여기지 않는지도 모르겠다고 말했다."]], [["“We consider ourselves a sort of happy family here, Miss Robarts, and are delighted to have the opportunity of including you in the ménage.”", "“우리는 이곳에서 우리끼리를 일종의 행복한 한 식구로 여기고 있어요, 로바츠 양. 그러니 그 살림 안에 아가씨를 끼워 넣을 기회를 얻게 되어 기뻐요.”"]], [["Lucy gave her ladyship one of her sweetest smiles, but what she said at that moment was inaudible.", "루시는 마님에게 자기의 가장 고운 미소 가운데 하나를 보냈으나, 그 순간 그녀가 한 말은 알아들을 수 없었다."], ["It was plain, however, that she could not bring herself even to go as far as Framley Court for her dinner just at present.", "그렇더라도 당장은 저녁을 먹기 위해 프램리 코트까지 올라가는 일조차 스스로 감당할 수 없다는 것은 분명했다."], ["“It was very kind of Lady Lufton,”", "“레이디 러프턴께서 참 자애로우세요.”"], ["she said to Fanny; “but it was so very soon, and—and—and if they would only go without her, she would be so happy.”", "그녀가 패니에게 말했다. “그런데 너무 이르고, 그리고—그리고—그리고 저를 빼놓고 두 분만 가 주신다면 저는 참 좋겠어요.”"], ["But as the object was to go with her—expressly to take her there—the dinner was adjourned for a short time—sine die.", "그러나 목적이 그녀와 함께 가는 것—바로 그녀를 거기에 데려가는 것—이었으므로, 만찬은 잠깐 동안 미루어졌다—「시네 디에」, 곧 기약 없이."]], [["# CHAPTER XI. GRISELDA GRANTLY.", "# 제11장. 그리젤다 그랜틀리"]], [["It was nearly a month after this that Lucy was first introduced to Lord Lufton, and then it was brought about only by accident.", "루시가 러프턴 경에게 처음으로 소개된 것은 그로부터 거의 한 달이 지난 뒤였고, 그것도 순전히 우연으로 이루어진 일이었다."], ["During that time Lady Lufton had been often at the parsonage, and had in a certain degree learned to know Lucy; but the stranger in the parish had never yet plucked up courage to accept one of the numerous invitations that had reached her.", "그동안 레이디 러프턴은 자주 목사관에 왔고, 어느 정도는 루시를 알게 되었다. 그러나 이 교구의 낯선 사람은 자기에게 온 수많은 초대 가운데 하나라도 받아들일 용기를 아직 내지 못했다."], ["Mr. Robarts and his wife had frequently been at Framley Court, but the dreaded day of Lucy’s initiation had not yet arrived.", "로바츠 씨와 그의 아내는 프램리 코트에 자주 갔지만, 루시가 첫걸음을 떼는 그 두려운 날은 아직 오지 않았다."]], [["She had seen Lord Lufton in church, but hardly so as to know him, and beyond that she had not seen him at all.", "그녀는 교회에서 러프턴 경을 본 적이 있었으나 얼굴을 안다고 하기도 어려웠고, 그 밖에는 그를 전혀 보지 못했다."], ["One day, however—or rather, one evening, for it was already dusk—he overtook her and Mrs. Robarts on the road walking towards the vicarage.", "그런데 어느 날—아니 차라리 어느 저녁, 벌써 어스름이 깔린 때였으니—그가 목사관 쪽으로 걸어가는 그녀와 로바츠 부인을 길에서 뒤따라 앞질렀다."], ["He had his gun on his shoulder, three pointers were at his heels, and a gamekeeper followed a little in the rear.", "그는 총을 어깨에 걸치고 있었고, 포인터 세 마리가 발뒤꿈치를 따랐으며, 사냥터 관리인이 조금 뒤에서 따라오고 있었다."]], [["“How are you, Mrs. Robarts?”", "“안녕하세요, 로바츠 부인?”"], ["he said, almost before he had overtaken them.", "그는 두 사람을 따라잡기도 전에 벌써 그렇게 말했다."], ["“I have been chasing you along the road for the last half mile.", "“지난 반 마일 동안 길에서 두 분을 쫓아왔어요."], ["I never knew ladies walk so fast.”", "부인들이 이렇게 빨리 걷는 줄은 몰랐네요.”"]], [["“We should be frozen if we were to dawdle about as you gentlemen do,”", "“우리가 신사분들처럼 어슬렁거리다가는 얼어 죽을걸요.”"], ["and then she stopped and shook hands with him.", "그러고서 그녀는 멈춰 서서 그와 악수했다."], ["She forgot at the moment that Lucy and he had not met, and therefore she did not introduce them.", "그 순간 그녀는 루시와 그가 아직 만난 적이 없다는 것을 잊고 있었고, 그래서 두 사람을 소개하지 않았다."]], [["“Won’t you make me known to your sister-in-law?”", "“시누이를 저에게 소개해 주지 않으시겠어요?”"], ["said he, taking off his hat, and bowing to Lucy.", "그가 모자를 벗고 루시에게 고개를 숙이며 말했다."], ["“I have never yet had the pleasure of meeting her, though we have been neighbours for a month and more.”", "“한 달 남짓 이웃으로 지냈는데도 아직 뵐 기쁨을 누리지 못했지요.”"]], [["Fanny made her excuses and introduced them, and then they went on till they came to Framley Gate, Lord Lufton talking to them both, and Fanny answering for the two, and there they stopped for a moment.", "패니는 미안하다고 하고서 두 사람을 소개했고, 그러고서 그들은 프램리 문에 이를 때까지 걸었다. 러프턴 경은 두 사람에게 다 말을 걸었고 패니가 둘을 대신해 대답했다. 그리고 거기서 그들은 잠시 멈추어 섰다."]], [["“I am surprised to see you alone,” Mrs.", "“혼자 계신 걸 보니 놀랍네요.”"], ["Robarts had just said; “I thought that Captain Culpepper was with you.”", "로바츠 부인이 바로 앞서 그렇게 말했었다. “컬페퍼 대위가 함께 있는 줄 알았어요.”"]], [["“The captain has left me for this one day.", "“대위는 오늘 하루 저를 두고 갔어요."], ["If you’ll whisper I’ll tell you where he has gone.", "귓속말로 하시겠다면 어디로 갔는지 알려 드리지요."], ["I dare not speak it out loud, even to the woods.”", "숲을 향해서도 큰 소리로는 말할 수가 없어서요.”"]], [["“To what terrible place can he have taken himself?", "“대체 어떤 무서운 곳으로 가 버렸다는 거예요?"], ["I’ll have no whisperings about such horrors.”", "그런 끔찍한 일로 귓속말은 하지 않겠어요.”"]], [["“He has gone to—to—but you’ll promise not to tell my mother?”", "“그가 간 곳은—간 곳은—하지만 어머니께 말하지 않겠다고 약속해 주시겠어요?”"]], [["“Not tell your mother!", "“어머님께 말하지 말라니!"], ["Well, now you have excited my curiosity!", "이제 정말 호기심이 이는군요!"], ["where can he be?”", "어디에 있을 수 있을까요?”"]], [["“Do you promise, then?”", "“그럼 약속하시는 거지요?”"]], [["“Oh, yes!", "“네, 약속하겠어요."], ["I will promise, because I am sure Lady Lufton won’t ask me as to Captain Culpepper’s whereabouts.", "레이디 러프턴께서 컬페퍼 대위가 어디 있는지 저에게 물으실 일은 없을 테니까요. 우리는 말하지 않을 거예요."], ["We won’t tell; will we, Lucy?”", "그렇죠, 루시?”"]], [["“He has gone to Gatherum Castle for a day’s pheasant-shooting.", "“하루 꿩 사냥을 하러 개서럼 성에 갔어요."], ["Now, mind, you must not betray us.", "자, 명심하세요, 저희를 배신하시면 안 돼요."], ["Her ladyship supposes that he is shut up in his room with a toothache.", "마님께서는 그가 치통으로 방에 갇혀 있는 줄로 알고 계세요."], ["We did not dare to mention the name to her.”", "저희는 감히 그 이름을 어머니께 입에 올리지 못했지요.”"]], [["And then it appeared that Mrs. Robarts had some engagement which made it necessary that she should go up and see Lady Lufton, whereas Lucy was intending to walk on to the parsonage alone.", "그러고 나서 로바츠 부인에게는 레이디 러프턴을 뵈러 올라가야 할 약속이 있는 것으로 드러났고, 루시는 혼자 목사관까지 걸어갈 참이었다."]], [["“And I have promised to go to your husband,”", "“그리고 저는 부인의 남편께 가겠다고 약속했지요.”"], ["said Lord Lufton; “or rather to your husband’s dog, Ponto.", "러프턴 경이 말했다. “아니, 남편의 개 폰토에게라고 하는 편이 맞겠네요."], ["And I will do two other good things—I will carry a brace of pheasants with me, and protect Miss Robarts from the evil spirits of the Framley roads.”", "그리고 좋은 일 두 가지를 더 하겠어요—꿩 한 쌍을 들고 가고, 프램리 길의 악령들로부터 로바츠 양을 지켜 드리지요.”"], ["And so Mrs. Robarts turned in at the gate, and Lucy and his lordship walked off together.", "그리하여 로바츠 부인은 문 안으로 들어섰고, 루시와 러프턴 경은 함께 걸어 나갔다."]], [["Lord Lufton, though he had never before spoken to Miss Robarts, had already found out that she was by no means plain.", "러프턴 경은 로바츠 양과 전에 말을 나눈 적이 한 번도 없었으나, 그녀가 결코 못생기지 않았다는 것은 이미 알아차리고 있었다."], ["Though he had hardly seen her except at church, he had already made himself certain that the owner of that face must be worth knowing, and was not sorry to have the present opportunity of speaking to her.", "교회에서 말고는 거의 본 적이 없었지만, 저 얼굴의 주인은 알아 둘 만한 사람이라고 그는 벌써 확신하고 있었고, 그래서 지금 그녀와 이야기할 기회가 생긴 것이 싫지 않았다."], ["“So you have an unknown damsel shut up in your castle,”", "“그러니까 성 안에 이름 모를 처녀를 가둬 두고 계신 거군요.”"], ["he had once said to Mrs. Robarts.", "언젠가 그는 로바츠 부인에게 그렇게 말했다."], ["“If she be kept a prisoner much longer, I shall find it my duty to come and release her by force of arms.”", "“그녀를 더 오래 죄수로 두신다면 저는 무력으로 쳐들어가 구해 내는 것이 제 의무라고 여기겠어요.”"], ["He had been there twice with the object of seeing her, but on both occasions Lucy had managed to escape.", "그는 그녀를 볼 셈으로 두 번 그곳에 갔으나, 두 번 다 루시가 용케 몸을 피했다."], ["Now we may say she was fairly caught, and Lord Lufton, taking a pair of pheasants from the gamekeeper, and swinging them over his shoulder, walked off with his prey.", "이제 우리는 그녀가 제대로 붙잡혔다고 말할 수 있고, 러프턴 경은 사냥터 관리인에게서 꿩 한 쌍을 받아 어깨에 걸쳐 메고서 제 먹이를 들고 걸어 나갔다."]], [["“You have been here a long time,”", "“여기 오신 지 오래되었는데도,”"], ["he said, “without our having had the pleasure of seeing you.”", "그가 말했다. “저희는 아직 뵐 기쁨을 누리지 못했지요.”"]], [["“Yes, my lord,”", "“예, 그렇습니다, 러프턴 경.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."], ["Lords had not been frequent among her acquaintance hitherto.", "여태껏 그녀가 알고 지낸 사람들 가운데 경이라 불리는 이는 흔하지 않았다."]], [["“I tell Mrs.", "“저는 로바츠 부인께 이렇게 말하지요."], ["Robarts that she has been confining you illegally, and that we shall release you by force or stratagem.”", "부인이 당신을 불법으로 가두어 두고 있으니 저희가 힘으로든 꾀로든 풀어 드리겠다고요.”"]], [["“I—I—I have had a great sorrow lately.”", "“저는—저는—저는 얼마 전에 큰 슬픔을 겪었습니다.”"]], [["“Yes, Miss Robarts; I know you have; and I am only joking, you know.", "“예, 로바츠 양. 알고 있어요."], ["But I do hope that now you will be able to come amongst us.", "그리고 저는 그저 농담을 하는 거예요, 아시겠지요. 하지만 이제는 저희와 어울리실 수 있게 되기를 정말 바라요."], ["My mother is so anxious that you should do so.”", "어머니께서 그러시기를 몹시 바라고 계세요.”"]], [["“I am sure she is very kind, and you also—my lord.”", "“마님께서 무척 친절하시다는 걸 잘 알고 있습니다. 그리고 당신께서도—러프턴 경.”"]], [["“I never knew my own father,”", "“저는 제 아버지를 알지 못했어요.”"], ["said Lord Lufton, speaking gravely.", "러프턴 경이 무겁게 말했다."], ["“But I can well understand what a loss you have had.”", "“하지만 당신이 무엇을 잃었는지는 잘 헤아릴 수 있지요.”"], ["And then, after pausing a moment, he continued, “I remember Dr.", "그러고서 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그는 말을 이었다."], ["Robarts well.”", "“로바츠 선생을 잘 기억해요.”"]], [["“Do you, indeed?”", "“정말이십니까?”"], ["said Lucy, turning sharply towards him, and speaking now with some animation in her voice.", "루시가 그를 향해 홱 돌아서며 말했고, 이제 그 목소리에는 어느 정도 생기가 실려 있었다."], ["Nobody had yet spoken to her about her father since she had been at Framley.", "프램리에 온 뒤로 아무도 아버지 이야기를 그녀에게 꺼내지 않았다."], ["It had been as though the subject were a forbidden one.", "그 화제가 금기이기라도 한 듯했다."], ["And how frequently is this the case!", "그런데 이런 일이 얼마나 흔한가!"], ["When those we love are dead, our friends dread to mention them, though to us who are bereaved no subject would be so pleasant as their names.", "우리가 사랑하는 이들이 죽으면 벗들은 그 이름을 입에 올리기를 두려워한다. 사별한 우리에게는 그 이름만큼 반가운 화제가 없는데도 그렇다."], ["But we rarely understand how to treat our own sorrow or those of others.", "그러나 우리는 제 슬픔도 남의 슬픔도 어떻게 다루어야 하는지 좀처럼 알지 못한다."]], [["There was once a people in some land—and they may be still there for what I know—who thought it sacrilegious to stay the course of a raging fire.", "어느 땅에 한 백성이 있었다—내가 아는 한 아직도 거기 있을지 모른다—그들은 타오르는 불의 진행을 막는 것이 신성을 모독하는 짓이라고 여겼다."], ["If a house were being burned, burn it must, even though there were facilities for saving it.", "집이 불타고 있다면, 구할 방도가 있더라도 타야 한다는 것이었다. 감히 누가 신의 진행에 끼어들겠는가?"], ["For who would dare to interfere with the course of the god?", "슬픔에 대한 우리의 생각도 그와 거의 같다."], ["Our idea of sorrow is much the same. We think it wicked, or at any rate heartless, to put it out.", "우리는 그것을 꺼 버리는 일이 못된 짓이거나, 적어도 몰인정한 짓이라고 여긴다."], ["If a man’s wife be dead, he should go about lugubrious, with long face, for at least two years, or perhaps with full length for eighteen months, decreasing gradually during the other six.", "아내가 죽었으면 남자는 적어도 두 해는 긴 얼굴로 침울하게 지내야 하고, 아니면 열여덟 달은 그 길이를 온전히 채우고서 남은 여섯 달 동안 차츰 줄여 가야 한다."], ["If he be a man who can quench his sorrow—put out his fire as it were—in less time than that, let him at any rate not show his power!", "그보다 짧은 시간에 제 슬픔을 삭일 수 있는—이를테면 제 불을 끌 수 있는—사람이라면, 적어도 그 능력을 남에게 보이지는 말아야 한다!"]], [["“Yes: I remember him,”", "“예, 그분을 기억해요.”"], ["continued Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말을 이었다."], ["“He came twice to Framley while I was a boy, consulting with my mother about Mark and myself,—whether the Eton floggings were not more efficacious than those at Harrow.", "“제가 소년일 때 두 번 프램리에 오셔서 마크와 제 일로 어머니와 의논하셨지요—이튼의 매질이 해로의 매질보다 더 효험이 있지 않겠느냐 하고요."], ["He was very kind to me, foreboding all manner of good things on my behalf.”", "저에게 무척 친절하셨고, 제 앞날에 온갖 좋은 일을 점쳐 주셨어요.”"]], [["“He was very kind to every one,”", "“아버지는 누구에게나 친절하셨습니다.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“I should think he would have been—a kind, good, genial man—just the man to be adored by his own family.”", "“그러셨을 것 같아요—친절하고 선하고 너그러운 분, 바로 제 식구들에게서 흠모받을 만한 분이었지요.”"]], [["“Exactly; and so he was.", "“바로 그렇습니다. 정말 그러셨어요."], ["I do not remember that I ever heard an unkind word from him.", "저는 아버지에게서 모진 말을 한 마디라도 들은 기억이 없습니다."], ["There was not a harsh tone in his voice.", "그 목소리에는 거친 가락이 없었습니다."], ["And he was generous as the day.”", "그리고 너그럽기가 한없었습니다.”"], ["Lucy, we have said, was not generally demonstrative, but now, on this subject, and with this absolute stranger, she became almost eloquent.", "루시는 앞서 말한 대로 좀처럼 감정을 드러내지 않는 사람이었으나, 지금 이 화제 앞에서, 그리고 이 온전한 낯선 사람 앞에서 거의 웅변가처럼 되었다."]], [["“I do not wonder that you should feel his loss, Miss Robarts.”", "“아버님을 잃은 것을 그렇게 느끼시는 게 이상하지 않아요, 로바츠 양.”"]], [["“Oh, I do feel it.", "“네, 정말 그렇게 느낍니다."], ["Mark is the best of brothers, and, as for Fanny, she is too kind and too good to me.", "마크는 세상에서 가장 좋은 오라비이고, 패니는 저에게 너무나 친절하고 너무나 좋은 사람입니다."], ["But I had always been specially my father’s friend.", "하지만 저는 늘 각별히 아버지의 벗이었습니다."], ["For the last year or two we had lived so much together!”", "지난 한두 해는 그렇게 함께 지냈으니까요!”"]], [["“He was an old man when he died, was he not?”", "“돌아가실 때 연로하셨지요, 그렇지 않아요?”"]], [["“Just seventy, my lord.”", "“막 일흔이었습니다, 러프턴 경.”"]], [["“Ah, then he was old.", "“아, 그러면 연로하셨네요."], ["My mother is only fifty, and we sometimes call her the old woman.", "저희 어머니는 겨우 쉰인데도 저희는 이따금 노부인이라고 불러요."], ["Do you think she looks older than that?", "그보다 더 늙어 보이시나요?"], ["We all say that she makes herself out to be so much more ancient than she need do.”", "저희는 다들 어머니가 굳이 그럴 필요도 없는데 훨씬 더 늙은 사람처럼 스스로를 꾸며 놓는다고 말해요.”"]], [["“Lady Lufton does not dress young.”", "“레이디 러프턴께서는 젊게 차려입지 않으십니다.”"]], [["“That is it.", "“그렇지요."], ["She never has, in my memory.", "제 기억으로는 한 번도 그러신 적이 없어요."], ["She always used to wear black when I first recollect her.", "제가 처음 기억하는 무렵에는 늘 검정을 입으셨어요."], ["She has given that up now; but she is still very sombre; is she not?”", "이제는 그건 그만두셨지만, 여전히 아주 침침하시지요, 그렇지 않아요?”"]], [["“I do not like ladies to dress very young, that is, ladies of—of—”", "“저는 부인들이 아주 젊게 차려입는 것을 좋아하지 않습니다. 그러니까, 그—그런 나이의 부인들이—”"]], [["“Ladies of fifty, we will say?”", "“쉰 살 부인들이라고 해 두지요?”"]], [["“Very well; ladies of fifty, if you like it.”", "“좋습니다. 그렇게 하시겠다면, 쉰 살 부인들이라고요.”"]], [["“Then I am sure you will like my mother.”", "“그러면 저희 어머니를 분명 마음에 들어 하시겠어요.”"]], [["They had now turned up through the parsonage wicket, a little gate that opened into the garden at a point on the road nearer than the chief entrance.", "이제 그들은 목사관 쪽문을 지나 올라섰다. 큰 정문보다 길에서 더 가까운 지점에서 정원으로 들어가는 작은 문이었다."]], [["“I suppose I shall find Mark up at the house?”", "“마크는 저 위 집에 있겠지요?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I daresay you will, my lord.”", "“그러실 겁니다, 러프턴 경.”"]], [["“Well, I’ll go round this way, for my business is partly in the stable.", "“그럼 저는 이쪽으로 돌아갈게요. 제 볼일이 한편으로는 마구간에 있으니까요."], ["You see I am quite at home here, though you never have seen me before.", "보시다시피 저는 여기서 아주 제집처럼 굴지요. 당신은 저를 전에 한 번도 본 적이 없지만요."], ["But, Miss Robarts, now that the ice is broken, I hope that we may be friends.”", "하지만 로바츠 양, 이제 얼음이 깨졌으니 우리가 벗이 될 수 있기를 바라요.”"], ["He then put out his hand, and when she gave him hers he pressed it almost as an old friend might have done.", "그러고서 그는 손을 내밀었고, 그녀가 손을 내주자 오래된 벗이라도 되는 것처럼 거의 그렇게 꽉 잡았다."]], [["And, indeed, Lucy had talked to him almost as though he were an old friend.", "그리고 실제로 루시도 그가 오래된 벗이기라도 한 듯이 그에게 이야기했다."], ["For a minute or two she had forgotten that he was a lord and a stranger—had forgotten also to be stiff and guarded as was her wont.", "일이 분 동안 그녀는 그가 경이며 낯선 사람이라는 것을 잊었고, 늘 그러던 대로 딱딱하게 경계하는 것도 잊었다."], ["Lord Lufton had spoken to her as though he had really cared to know her; and she, unconsciously, had been taken by the compliment.", "러프턴 경은 정말로 그녀를 알고 싶어 하는 것처럼 말을 걸었고, 그녀는 자기도 모르게 그 호의에 마음이 끌렸다."], ["Lord Lufton, indeed, had not thought much about it—excepting as thus, that he liked the glance of a pair of bright eyes, as most other young men do like it.", "러프턴 경 자신은 사실 그 일을 그리 깊이 생각하지 않았다—다만 이 정도, 다른 젊은 사내들이 대개 그렇듯 그도 반짝이는 두 눈의 눈길을 좋아했다는 것뿐이었다."], ["But, on this occasion, the evening had been so dark, that he had hardly seen Lucy’s eyes at all.", "그러나 이번에는 저녁이 너무 어두워서 그는 루시의 눈을 거의 보지도 못했다."]], [["“Well, Lucy, I hope you liked your companion,” Mrs.", "“그래, 루시, 함께 온 분이 마음에 들었어요?”"], ["Robarts said, as the three of them clustered round the drawing-room fire before dinner.", "저녁 식사 전에 세 사람이 응접실 난로 앞에 모여 앉았을 때 로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes; pretty well,”", "“네, 그럭저럭요.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“That is not at all complimentary to his lordship.”", "“그건 그분께 조금도 칭찬이 못 되네요.”"]], [["“I did not mean to be complimentary, Fanny.”", "“칭찬할 뜻은 없었어요, 패니.”"]], [["“Lucy is a great deal too matter-of-fact for compliments,”", "“루시는 칭찬을 하기에는 너무 사실만 따지지.”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“What I meant was, that I had no great opportunity for judging, seeing that I was only with Lord Lufton for about ten minutes.”", "“제 말은 판단할 기회가 별로 없었다는 뜻이에요. 러프턴 경과 십 분쯤밖에 같이 있지 않았으니까요.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but there are girls here who would give their eyes for ten minutes of Lord Lufton to themselves.", "하지만 여기에는 러프턴 경을 십 분 독차지하려고 두 눈이라도 내줄 아가씨들이 있어요."], ["You do not know how he’s valued.", "그분이 얼마나 값비싼 분인지 루시는 몰라요."], ["He has the character of being always able to make himself agreeable to ladies at half a minute’s warning.”", "그분은 삼십 초만 예고를 받으면 언제든 부인들의 마음에 들 수 있는 사람이라는 평판을 얻고 있지요.”"]], [["“Perhaps he had not the half-minute’s warning in this case,”", "“어쩌면 이번에는 삼십 초 예고도 못 받았겠지요.”"], ["said Lucy,—hypocrite that she was.", "루시가 말했다—그녀는 위선자였다."]], [["“Poor Lucy,”", "“가엾은 루시,”"], ["said her brother; “he was coming up to see Ponto’s shoulder, and I am afraid he was thinking more about the dog than you.”", "오라비가 말했다. “그는 폰토의 어깨를 보러 올라오던 길이었고, 안타깝지만 너보다 개를 더 생각하고 있었을 거야.”"]], [["“Very likely,”", "“그랬겠지요.”"], ["said Lucy; and then they went in to dinner.", "루시가 말했다. 그러고서 그들은 저녁 식사를 하러 들어갔다."]], [["Lucy had been a hypocrite, for she had confessed to herself, while dressing, that Lord Lufton had been very pleasant; but then it is allowed to young ladies to be hypocrites when the subject under discussion is the character of a young gentleman.", "루시는 위선자였다. 옷을 갈아입으면서 러프턴 경이 무척 유쾌한 사람이었다고 스스로 인정했으니 말이다. 그러나 논의되는 화제가 젊은 신사의 사람됨일 때는 젊은 아가씨들에게 위선자가 되는 것이 허락되어 있다."]], [["Soon after that, Lucy did dine at Framley Court.", "그로부터 얼마 지나지 않아 루시는 정말로 프램리 코트에서 저녁 식사를 했다."], ["Captain Culpepper, in spite of his enormity with reference to Gatherum Castle, was still staying there, as was also a clergyman from the neighbourhood of Barchester with his wife and daughter.", "컬페퍼 대위는 개서럼 성에 관한 그 대죄에도 불구하고 아직 그곳에 머물고 있었고, 바체스터 근교에서 온 한 성직자도 부인과 딸과 함께 머물고 있었다."], ["This was Archdeacon Grantly, a gentleman whom we have mentioned before, and who was as well known in the diocese as the bishop himself,—and more thought about by many clergymen than even that illustrious prelate.", "이 사람이 바로 그랜틀리 부주교로, 우리가 앞서 언급한 신사이며, 주교 자신만큼이나 교구에 널리 알려진 사람이었다—그리고 많은 성직자에게는 저 이름 높은 고위 성직자보다도 더 자주 마음에 오르는 사람이었다."]], [["Miss Grantly was a young lady not much older than Lucy Robarts, and she also was quiet, and not given to much talking in open company.", "그랜틀리 양은 루시 로바츠보다 그리 많이 나이 들지 않은 젊은 아가씨였고, 그녀 또한 조용하며 여러 사람 앞에서 말을 많이 하는 편이 아니었다."], ["She was decidedly a beauty, but somewhat statuesque in her loveliness.", "그녀는 분명히 미인이었으나, 그 아름다움에는 어딘가 조각상 같은 데가 있었다."], ["Her forehead was high and white, but perhaps too like marble to gratify the taste of those who are fond of flesh and blood.", "이마는 높고 희었으나, 살과 피를 좋아하는 이들의 취향을 만족시키기에는 아마 너무 대리석 같았다."], ["Her eyes were large and exquisitely formed, but they seldom showed much emotion.", "눈은 크고 그 모양이 나무랄 데 없었으나, 감정을 많이 드러내는 일이 드물었다."], ["She, indeed, was impassive herself, and betrayed but little of her feelings.", "사실 그녀 자신이 무표정했고, 제 마음을 좀처럼 내보이지 않았다."], ["Her nose was nearly Grecian, not coming absolutely in a straight line from her forehead, but doing so nearly enough to entitle it to be considered as classical.", "코는 거의 그리스식이었다. 이마에서 딱 곧은 선으로 내려오는 것은 아니었으나, 고전적이라 할 만하다고 여길 만큼은 거의 그러했다."], ["Her mouth, too, was very fine—artists, at least, said so, and connoisseurs in beauty; but to me she always seemed as though she wanted fulness of lip.", "입도 아주 훌륭했다—적어도 화가들과 아름다움의 감식가들은 그렇게 말했다. 그러나 내게는 늘 입술의 도톰함이 모자라 보였다."], ["But the exquisite symmetry of her cheek and chin and lower face no man could deny.", "그래도 뺨과 턱과 아래쪽 얼굴의 그 나무랄 데 없는 균형은 어떤 남자도 부정할 수 없었다."], ["Her hair was light, and being always dressed with considerable care, did not detract from her appearance; but it lacked that richness which gives such luxuriance to feminine loveliness.", "머리털은 밝은 빛이었고 늘 적잖은 정성으로 손질되어 있어서 그 모습을 깎아내리지 않았으나, 여성의 아름다움에 그토록 풍요로움을 주는 그 그윽함이 없었다."], ["She was tall and slight, and very graceful in her movements; but there were those who thought that she wanted the ease and abandon of youth.", "키가 크고 날씬했으며 움직임이 아주 우아했으나, 젊음의 편안함과 거침없음이 그녀에게 모자란다고 여기는 이들도 있었다."], ["They said that she was too composed and stiff for her age, and that she gave but little to society beyond the beauty of her form and face.", "그들은 그녀가 나이에 걸맞지 않게 너무 침착하고 딱딱하며, 제 몸매와 얼굴의 아름다움 말고는 사교에 내주는 것이 거의 없다고 말했다."]], [["There can be no doubt, however, that she was considered by most men and women to be the beauty of Barsetshire, and that gentlemen from neighbouring counties would come many miles through dirty roads on the mere hope of being able to dance with her.", "그렇지만 그녀가 대개의 남자와 여자에게 바셋주의 미인으로 여겨졌고, 이웃 여러 주의 신사들이 그녀와 춤을 출 수 있으리라는 기대만으로 진창길을 여러 마일씩 달려왔다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다."], ["Whatever attractions she may have lacked, she had at any rate created for herself a great reputation.", "어떤 매력이 모자랐든, 적어도 그녀는 스스로 큰 명성을 만들어 냈다."], ["She had spent two months of the last spring in London, and even there she had made a sensation; and people had said that Lord Dumbello, Lady Hartletop’s eldest son, had been peculiarly struck with her.", "그녀는 지난봄에 두 달을 런던에서 지냈고, 거기서도 화제를 일으켰다. 그리고 레이디 하틀탑의 맏아들 덤벨로 경이 그녀에게 유별나게 마음을 빼앗겼다는 말이 사람들 사이에 돌았다."]], [["It may be imagined that the archdeacon was proud of her, and so indeed was Mrs. Grantly—more proud, perhaps, of her daughter’s beauty, than so excellent a woman should have allowed herself to be of such an attribute.", "부주교가 딸을 자랑스러워한 것은 짐작할 만하고, 그랜틀리 부인도 그러했다—어쩌면 그토록 훌륭한 여인이 그런 속성을 두고 스스로에게 허락해도 좋을 정도보다 더 딸의 아름다움을 자랑스러워했다."], ["Griselda—that was her name—was now an only daughter.", "그리젤다—그것이 그녀의 이름이었다—는 이제 외딸이었다."], ["One sister she had had, but that sister had died.", "자매가 하나 있었으나 그 자매는 죽었다."], ["There were two brothers also left, one in the Church and the other in the army.", "남자 형제도 둘 남아 있었는데, 하나는 교회에, 다른 하나는 군에 있었다."], ["That was the extent of the archdeacon’s family, and as the archdeacon was a very rich man—he was the only child of his father, who had been Bishop of Barchester for a great many years; and in those years it had been worth a man’s while to be Bishop of Barchester—it was supposed that Miss Grantly would have a large fortune.", "그것이 부주교 집안의 전부였다. 그리고 부주교는 아주 부유한 사람이었으므로—그는 오래도록 바체스터 주교를 지낸 아버지의 외아들이었고, 그 시절에는 바체스터 주교를 지낼 값이 있었다—그랜틀리 양이 큰 재산을 갖게 될 것이라고 사람들은 짐작했다."], ["Mrs. Grantly, however, had been heard to say, that she was in no hurry to see her daughter established in the world;—ordinary young ladies are merely married, but those of real importance are established:—and this, if anything, added to the value of the prize.", "그러나 그랜틀리 부인은 딸을 세상에 자리 잡게 하는 일을 서두를 생각이 없다고 말한 적이 있다고 전해졌다—보통 아가씨들은 그저 결혼하지만, 정말로 중요한 아가씨들은 자리를 잡는다—그리고 이것은 오히려 그 상품의 값을 더해 주었다."], ["Mothers sometimes depreciate their wares by an undue solicitude to dispose of them.", "어머니들은 이따금 물건을 처분하려고 지나치게 애를 씀으로써 제 물건의 값을 떨어뜨린다."]], [["But to tell the truth openly and at once—a virtue for which a novelist does not receive very much commendation—Griselda Grantly was, to a certain extent, already given away.", "그러나 솔직히 그리고 단번에 진실을 말한다면—소설가가 그리 많은 칭찬을 받지 못하는 미덕이지만—그리젤다 그랜틀리는 어느 정도 이미 남에게 주어져 있었다."], ["Not that she, Griselda, knew anything about it, or that the thrice happy gentleman had been made aware of his good fortune; nor even had the archdeacon been told.", "그녀 자신, 그리젤다가 그것을 알고 있었다는 것도 아니고, 세 겹으로 복된 그 신사가 제 행운을 알게 되었다는 것도 아니다. 부주교조차 듣지 못했다."], ["But Mrs. Grantly and Lady Lufton had been closeted together more than once, and terms had been signed and sealed between them.", "그러나 그랜틀리 부인과 레이디 러프턴은 한 번 이상 단둘이 문을 닫고 마주 앉았고, 두 사람 사이에 조건들이 서명되고 봉인되었다."], ["Not signed on parchment, and sealed with wax, as is the case with treaties made by kings and diplomats,—to be broken by the same; but signed with little words, and sealed with certain pressings of the hand,—a treaty which between two such contracting parties would be binding enough.", "왕과 외교관들이 맺는 조약처럼 양피지에 서명하고 밀랍으로 봉인한 것은 아니었다—그런 조약은 같은 사람들 손에 깨지기도 한다—그러나 자잘한 말로 서명하고 손을 몇 번 지그시 잡는 것으로 봉인했으니, 이런 두 계약 당사자 사이에서라면 충분히 구속력이 있는 조약이었다."], ["And by the terms of this treaty Griselda Grantly was to become Lady Lufton.", "그리고 이 조약의 조건에 따라 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 레이디 러프턴이 될 것이었다."]], [["Lady Lufton had hitherto been fortunate in her matrimonial speculations.", "레이디 러프턴은 여태 혼사 투기에서 운이 좋았다."], ["She had selected Sir George for her daughter, and Sir George, with the utmost good nature, had fallen in with her views.", "그녀는 딸을 위해 조지 경을 골랐고, 조지 경은 너그러움을 다해 그 뜻에 따라 주었다."], ["She had selected Fanny Monsell for Mr. Robarts, and Fanny Monsell had not rebelled against her for a moment.", "그녀는 로바츠 씨를 위해 패니 몬셀을 골랐고, 패니 몬셀은 한순간도 그녀에게 반항하지 않았다."], ["There was a prestige of success about her doings, and she felt almost confident that her dear son Ludovic must fall in love with Griselda.", "그녀가 하는 일에는 성공의 위세가 감돌았고, 그래서 그녀는 사랑하는 아들 루도빅이 그리젤다에게 반하고 말 것이라고 거의 확신하고 있었다."]], [["As to the lady herself, nothing, Lady Lufton thought, could be much better than such a match for her son.", "그 아가씨 자신을 두고 말하면, 레이디 러프턴의 생각으로는 아들에게 그만한 혼처보다 나은 것이 그리 있을 수 없었다."], ["Lady Lufton, I have said, was a good churchwoman, and the archdeacon was the very type of that branch of the Church which she venerated.", "레이디 러프턴은 앞서 말했듯 훌륭한 교회의 여인이었고, 부주교는 그녀가 우러르는 그 교회 갈래의 바로 전형이었다."], ["The Grantlys, too, were of a good family,—not noble, indeed; but in such matters Lady Lufton did not want everything.", "그랜틀리 집안도 좋은 가문이었다—과연 귀족은 아니었지만, 그런 일에서 레이디 러프턴이 모든 것을 다 바란 것은 아니었다."], ["She was one of those persons who, in placing their hopes at a moderate pitch, may fairly trust to see them realized.", "그녀는 바람을 알맞은 높이에 두어 그것이 이루어지는 것을 볼 만하게 기대할 수 있는 사람들 가운데 하나였다."], ["She would fain that her son’s wife should be handsome; this she wished for his sake, that he might be proud of his wife, and because men love to look on beauty.", "아들의 아내가 아름답기를 그녀는 바라 마지않았다. 이것은 아들을 위해 바란 것이니, 아들이 아내를 자랑스러워할 수 있도록, 또 사내들이 아름다움을 바라보기를 좋아하니 그러했다."], ["But she was afraid of vivacious beauty, of those soft, sparkling feminine charms which are spread out as lures for all the world, soft dimples, laughing eyes, luscious lips, conscious smiles, and easy whispers.", "그러나 그녀는 발랄한 아름다움, 온 세상을 향해 미끼로 펼쳐 놓는 저 부드럽고 반짝이는 여성스러운 매력을 두려워했다. 부드러운 보조개, 웃는 눈, 물오른 입술, 뜻을 담은 미소, 거침없는 귓속말 같은 것을."], ["What if her son should bring her home a rattling, rapid-spoken, painted piece of Eve’s flesh such as this?", "만일 아들이 그런 종류를, 재잘거리고 빠르게 지껄이며 분칠한 이브의 살점을 집으로 데려온다면?"], ["Would not the glory and joy of her life be over, even though such child of their first mother should have come forth to the present day ennobled by the blood of two dozen successive British peers?", "우리 첫 어머니의 그런 자식이 스물네 명의 잇따른 영국 귀족의 피로 고귀해져서 오늘에 이르렀다 해도, 그녀 인생의 영광과 기쁨은 끝나 버리지 않겠는가?"]], [["And then, too, Griselda’s money would not be useless.", "그리고 또한 그리젤다의 돈도 소용없지 않을 것이었다."], ["Lady Lufton, with all her high-flown ideas, was not an imprudent woman.", "레이디 러프턴은 그 온갖 드높은 생각에도 불구하고 분별없는 여자가 아니었다. 아들이 헤프게 썼다는 것은 알았다."], ["She knew that her son had been extravagant, though she did not believe that he had been reckless; and she was well content to think that some balsam from the old bishop’s coffers should be made to cure the slight wounds which his early imprudence might have inflicted on the carcase of the family property.", "아무렇게나 마구 썼다고는 믿지 않았지만 그랬다. 그리고 노주교의 금고에서 나온 향유가 아들의 이른 무분별이 집안 재산의 몸통에 입혔을 그 작은 상처들을 낫게 하는 데 쓰이리라는 생각에 그녀는 흡족했다."], ["And thus, in this way, and for these reasons, Griselda Grantly had been chosen out from all the world to be the future Lady Lufton.", "그리하여 이런 식으로, 이런 이유들로, 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 온 세상에서 뽑혀 앞날의 레이디 러프턴이 되기로 정해진 것이었다."]], [["Lord Lufton had met Griselda more than once already; had met her before these high contracting parties had come to any terms whatsoever, and had evidently admired her.", "러프턴 경은 이미 한 번 이상 그리젤다를 만난 적이 있었다. 이 드높은 계약 당사자들이 아무런 조건에 이르기도 전에 그녀를 만났고, 분명히 그녀에게 감탄했다."], ["Lord Dumbello had remained silent one whole evening in London with ineffable disgust, because Lord Lufton had been rather particular in his attentions; but then Lord Dumbello’s muteness was his most eloquent mode of expression.", "런던에서 어느 저녁 내내 덤벨로 경은 말로 할 수 없는 역겨움에 잠겨 입을 다물고 있었는데, 러프턴 경이 그녀에게 좀 유별나게 마음을 쏟았기 때문이었다. 그러나 덤벨로 경의 침묵은 그가 뜻을 드러내는 가장 웅변적인 방식이었다."], ["Both Lady Hartletop and Mrs. Grantly, when they saw him, knew very well what he meant.", "레이디 하틀탑도 그랜틀리 부인도 그를 보면 그가 무슨 뜻인지 아주 잘 알았다."], ["But that match would not exactly have suited Mrs. Grantly’s views.", "그러나 그 혼사는 그랜틀리 부인의 뜻에 꼭 맞지 않았을 것이다."], ["The Hartletop people were not in her line.", "하틀탑 사람들은 그녀의 줄이 아니었다."], ["They belonged altogether to another set, being connected, as we have heard before, with the Omnium interest—“those horrid Gatherum people,” as Lady Lufton would say to her, raising her hands and eyebrows, and shaking her head.", "그들은 앞서 들은 대로 옴니엄 쪽 이해와 얽혀 아주 다른 패에 속해 있었다—“저 끔찍한 개서럼 사람들”이라고 레이디 러프턴은 그녀에게 손과 눈썹을 치켜올리고 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["Lady Lufton probably thought that they ate babies in pies during their midnight orgies at Gatherum Castle; and that widows were kept in cells, and occasionally put on racks for the amusement of the duke’s guests.", "레이디 러프턴은 아마도 그들이 개서럼 성에서 한밤중에 흥청거리며 아기를 파이에 넣어 먹고, 과부들을 감방에 가둬 두었다가 이따금 공작의 손님들을 즐겁게 하려고 형틀에 올린다고 생각했을 것이다."]], [["When the Robarts’s party entered the drawing-room the Grantlys were already there, and the archdeacon’s voice sounded loud and imposing in Lucy’s ears, as she heard him speaking while she was yet on the threshold of the door.", "로바츠 씨네 일행이 응접실에 들어섰을 때 그랜틀리 사람들은 이미 거기 있었고, 부주교의 목소리가 루시의 귀에 크고 위엄 있게 울렸다. 그녀가 아직 문지방에 서 있는데도 그가 말하는 소리가 들려왔던 것이다."]], [["“My dear Lady Lufton, I would believe anything on earth about her—anything.", "“친애하는 레이디 러프턴, 나는 그 여자에 대해서라면 세상 무엇이든 믿을 수 있소—무엇이든지."], ["There is nothing too outrageous for her.", "그 여자에게 너무 터무니없는 일이란 없소."], ["Had she insisted on going there with the bishop’s apron on, I should not have been surprised.”", "주교의 앞치마를 두르고 거기 가겠다고 우겼다 해도 나는 놀라지 않았을 것이오.”"], ["And then they all knew that the archdeacon was talking about Mrs.", "그러고서 그들은 모두 부주교가 프라우디 부인 이야기를 하고 있음을 알았다."], ["Proudie, for Mrs. Proudie was his bugbear.", "프라우디 부인은 그의 골칫거리였으니까."]], [["Lady Lufton after receiving her guests introduced Lucy to Griselda Grantly.", "레이디 러프턴은 손님들을 맞은 뒤 루시를 그리젤다 그랜틀리에게 소개했다."], ["Miss Grantly smiled graciously, bowed slightly, and then remarked in the lowest voice possible that it was exceedingly cold.", "그랜틀리 양은 우아하게 미소 짓고 살짝 고개를 숙인 뒤, 날이 몹시 춥다고 낼 수 있는 가장 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["A low voice, we know, is an excellent thing in woman.", "낮은 목소리는 우리가 알듯 여자에게 훌륭한 것이다."]], [["Lucy, who thought that she was bound to speak, said that it was cold, but that she did not mind it when she was walking.", "루시는 자기가 무슨 말을 해야 한다고 생각해서, 날이 춥지만 걸을 때는 개의치 않는다고 말했다."], ["And then Griselda smiled again, somewhat less graciously than before, and so the conversation ended.", "그러자 그리젤다는 앞서보다 다소 덜 우아하게 다시 미소 지었고, 그렇게 대화는 끝났다."], ["Miss Grantly was the elder of the two, and having seen most of the world, should have been the best able to talk, but perhaps she was not very anxious for a conversation with Miss Robarts.", "그랜틀리 양은 둘 중 나이가 위였고 세상을 가장 많이 보았으니 말을 가장 잘할 수 있어야 했겠지만, 어쩌면 그녀는 로바츠 양과 이야기를 나누고 싶은 마음이 별로 없었다."]], [["“So, Robarts, I hear that you have been preaching at Chaldicotes,”", "“그래, 로바츠, 콜디코츠에서 설교를 했다고 들었소.”"], ["said the archdeacon, still rather loudly.", "부주교가 여전히 좀 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["“I saw Sowerby the other day, and he told me that you gave them the fag end of Mrs. Proudie’s lecture.”", "“며칠 전에 사워비를 만났는데, 자네가 프라우디 부인 강연의 꼬리 토막을 들려주었다더군.”"]], [["“It was ill-natured of Sowerby to say the fag end,”", "“꼬리 토막이라니 사워비도 심술궂었습니다.”"], ["said Robarts.", "로바츠가 말했다."], ["“We divided the matter into thirds.", "“우리는 그 일을 셋으로 나누었지요."], ["Harold Smith took the first part, I the last—”", "해럴드 스미스가 첫 몫을, 제가 마지막 몫을—”"]], [["“And the lady the intervening portion.", "“그리고 그 부인이 가운데 토막을 맡았군."], ["You have electrified the county between you; but I am told that she had the best of it.”", "자네들이 함께 이 주를 감전시켰지. 하지만 내가 듣기로는 그 부인이 가장 잘했다더군.”"]], [["“I was so sorry that Mr. Robarts went there,”", "“로바츠 씨가 거기 간 것이 참 안타까웠어요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, as she walked into the dining-room leaning on the archdeacon’s arm.", "레이디 러프턴이 부주교의 팔에 기대어 식당으로 걸어 들어가며 말했다."]], [["“I am inclined to think he could not very well have helped himself,”", "“그로서는 어쩔 도리가 별로 없었으리라는 쪽으로 나는 기울고 있소.”"], ["said the archdeacon, who was never willing to lean heavily on a brother parson, unless on one who had utterly and irrevocably gone away from his side of the Church.", "부주교가 말했다. 그는 자기 쪽 교회에서 아주 그리고 돌이킬 수 없이 떠나 버린 사람이 아니라면 형제 목사를 무겁게 짓누를 마음이 결코 없는 사람이었다."]], [["“Do you think not, archdeacon?”", "“어쩔 도리가 없었다고 보세요, 부주교님?”"]], [["“Why, no: Sowerby is a friend of Lufton’s—”", "“그야, 그렇소. 사워비는 러프턴의 벗이니—”"]], [["“Not particularly,”", "“딱히 그렇지는 않아요.”"], ["said poor Lady Lufton, in a deprecating tone.", "가엾은 레이디 러프턴이 손사래를 치는 듯한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Well, they have been intimate; and Robarts, when he was asked to preach at Chaldicotes, could not well refuse.”", "“그래도 두 사람은 가깝게 지냈소. 그러니 로바츠가 콜디코츠에서 설교해 달라는 청을 받았을 때 마다할 수는 없었지.”"]], [["“But then he went afterwards to Gatherum Castle.", "“하지만 그러고서 개서럼 성에 갔지요."], ["Not that I am vexed with him at all now, you understand.", "이제는 제가 그이에게 조금도 화가 나 있지 않다는 걸 아셔야 해요."], ["But it is such a dangerous house, you know.”", "그렇지만 그건 아시다시피 그렇게 위험한 집이니까요.”"]], [["“So it is.—But the very fact of the duke’s wishing to have a clergyman there, should always be taken as a sign of grace, Lady Lufton.", "“그렇소.—하지만 공작이 성직자를 거기 두고 싶어 했다는 바로 그 사실은 언제나 은총의 표시로 받아들여야 하오, 레이디 러프턴."], ["The air was impure, no doubt; but it was less impure with Robarts there than it would have been without him.", "공기가 더러웠던 것은 의심할 것 없소. 그러나 로바츠가 거기 있었으니 그가 없을 때보다는 덜 더러웠지."], ["But, gracious heavens!", "그런데, 맙소사!"], ["what blasphemy have I been saying about impure air?", "더러운 공기라니 내가 무슨 망언을 하고 있었소?"], ["Why, the bishop was there!”", "아니, 주교가 거기 있었는데!”"]], [["“Yes, the bishop was there,”", "“네, 주교님이 거기 있었지요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, and they both understood each other thoroughly.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했고, 두 사람은 서로를 아주 잘 알아들었다."]], [["Lord Lufton took out Mrs. Grantly to dinner, and matters were so managed that Miss Grantly sat on his other side.", "러프턴 경은 그랜틀리 부인을 이끌고 식당으로 갔고, 그랜틀리 양이 그의 다른 쪽에 앉도록 일이 짜여 있었다."], ["There was no management apparent in this to anybody; but there she was, while Lucy was placed between her brother and Captain Culpepper.", "이것이 누구에게도 짜인 일로 보이지는 않았으나, 어쨌든 그녀는 거기 있었고, 루시는 오라비와 컬페퍼 대위 사이에 놓였다."], ["Captain Culpepper was a man with an enormous moustache, and a great aptitude for slaughtering game; but as he had no other strong characteristics, it was not probable that he would make himself very agreeable to poor Lucy.", "컬페퍼 대위는 엄청난 콧수염을 기르고 사냥감을 잡는 데 큰 재간이 있는 사내였다. 그러나 그 밖에 두드러진 성질이 없었으니, 그가 가엾은 루시에게 아주 유쾌한 사람이 되어 줄 것 같지는 않았다."]], [["She had seen Lord Lufton once, for two minutes, since the day of that walk, and then he had addressed her quite like an old friend.", "그 산책의 날 뒤로 그녀는 러프턴 경을 딱 한 번, 이 분쯤 보았고, 그때 그는 오래된 벗에게 하듯 그녀에게 말을 걸었다."], ["It had been in the parsonage drawing-room, and Fanny had been there.", "목사관 응접실에서였고 패니도 거기 있었다."], ["Fanny now was so well accustomed to his lordship, that she thought but little of this, but to Lucy it had been very pleasant.", "이제 패니는 그분에게 아주 익숙해져서 그것을 대수롭게 여기지 않았으나, 루시에게는 아주 반가운 일이었다."], ["He was not forward or familiar, but kind, and gentle, and pleasant; and Lucy did feel that she liked him.", "그는 앞으로 나서지도 않고 지나치게 허물없지도 않았으며, 친절하고 부드럽고 유쾌했다. 그리고 루시는 정말로 그가 마음에 들었다."]], [["Now, on this evening, he had hitherto hardly spoken to her; but then she knew that there were other people in the company to whom he was bound to speak.", "그런데 이 저녁에 그는 여태 그녀에게 거의 말을 걸지 않았다. 그러나 그가 말을 걸어야 할 다른 사람들이 자리에 있다는 것은 그녀도 알았다."], ["She was not exactly humble-minded in the usual sense of the word; but she did recognize the fact that her position was less important than that of other people there, and that therefore it was probable that to a certain extent she would be overlooked.", "그녀는 흔히 말하는 뜻으로 마음이 겸허한 사람은 딱히 아니었다. 그러나 자기 위치가 거기 있는 다른 사람들의 위치보다 덜 중요하고, 그러니 어느 정도는 자기가 지나쳐질 법하다는 사실은 알아보고 있었다."], ["But not the less would she have liked to occupy the seat to which Miss Grantly had found her way.", "그렇더라도 그랜틀리 양이 찾아 들어간 그 자리를 차지하고 싶은 마음이 덜하지는 않았다. 그녀는 러프턴 경과 시시덕거리고 싶은 것이 아니었다."], ["She did not want to flirt with Lord Lufton; she was not such a fool as that; but she would have liked to have heard the sound of his voice close to her ear, instead of that of Captain Culpepper’s knife and fork.", "그렇게 바보는 아니었다. 그러나 컬페퍼 대위의 나이프와 포크 소리 대신 그의 목소리가 귀 가까이서 들리기를 바랐다."]], [["This was the first occasion on which she had endeavoured to dress herself with care since her father had died; and now, sombre though she was in her deep mourning, she did look very well.", "아버지가 죽은 뒤로 정성을 들여 옷을 차려입어 보려 한 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 그리고 이제 깊은 상복 차림으로 침침했지만 그녀는 정말 아주 보기 좋았다."]], [["“There is an expression about her forehead that is full of poetry,”", "“저 아이 이마에는 시로 가득 찬 표정이 있어요.”"], ["Fanny had said to her husband.", "패니가 남편에게 말했다."]], [["“Don’t you turn her head, Fanny, and make her believe that she is a beauty,”", "“패니, 저 애 머리를 돌려 놓아서 자기가 미인이라고 믿게 하지는 마.”"], ["Mark had answered.", "마크가 대답했다."]], [["“I doubt it is not so easy to turn her head, Mark.", "“저 아이 머리는 그렇게 쉽게 돌려지지 않을 거예요, 마크."], ["There is more in Lucy than you imagine, and so you will find out before long.” It was thus that Mrs.", "루시에게는 당신이 생각하는 것보다 더 많은 것이 있고, 당신도 오래지 않아 알게 될 거예요.”"], ["Robarts prophesied about her sister-in-law.", "로바츠 부인은 이렇게 시누이를 두고 예언했다."], ["Had she been asked she might perhaps have said that Lucy’s presence would be dangerous to the Grantly interest at Framley Court.", "물어보았다면 그녀는 어쩌면 루시가 있는 것이 프램리 코트에서 그랜틀리 쪽 이해에 위험하리라고 말했을지도 모른다."]], [["Lord Lufton’s voice was audible enough as he went on talking to Miss Grantly—his voice, but not his words.", "러프턴 경이 그랜틀리 양에게 계속 말하는 동안 그의 목소리는 넉넉히 들렸다—목소리는 들렸으나 말은 들리지 않았다."], ["He talked in such a way that there was no appearance of whispering, and yet the person to whom he spoke, and she only, could hear what he said.", "그는 귓속말을 하는 티가 조금도 없이 말했으면서도, 그가 말을 거는 사람만이, 오직 그 사람만이 그가 하는 말을 들을 수 있었다."], ["Mrs. Grantly the while conversed constantly with Lucy’s brother, who sat at Lucy’s left hand.", "그러는 동안 그랜틀리 부인은 루시의 왼편에 앉은 루시의 오라비와 끊임없이 이야기했다."], ["She never lacked for subjects on which to speak to a country clergyman of the right sort, and thus Griselda was left quite uninterrupted.", "그녀는 알맞은 종류의 시골 성직자에게 말할 화제가 모자란 적이 없었고, 그리하여 그리젤다는 아무런 방해도 받지 않고 남겨졌다."]], [["But Lucy could not but observe that Griselda herself seemed to have very little to say,—or at any rate to say very little.", "그러나 루시는 그리젤다 자신이 할 말이 아주 적은—아니 적어도 하는 말이 아주 적은—듯 보이는 것을 눈치채지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Every now and then she did open her mouth, and some word or brace of words would fall from it.", "이따금 그녀가 입을 열면 한 마디, 혹은 두어 마디가 떨어지기는 했다."], ["But for the most part she seemed to be content in the fact that Lord Lufton was paying her attention.", "그러나 대체로 그녀는 러프턴 경이 자기에게 마음을 쏟고 있다는 사실만으로 흡족한 듯했다."], ["She showed no animation, but sat there still and graceful, composed and classical, as she always was.", "그녀는 생기를 보이지 않았고, 늘 그렇듯 고요하고 우아하게, 침착하고 고전적으로 그저 앉아 있었다."], ["Lucy, who could not keep her ears from listening or her eyes from looking, thought that had she been there she would have endeavoured to take a more prominent part in the conversation.", "귀를 기울이지 않을 수도 눈길을 두지 않을 수도 없었던 루시는, 자기가 거기 있었다면 그 대화에서 더 앞장선 몫을 맡으려 애썼을 것이라고 생각했다."], ["But then Griselda Grantly probably knew much better than Lucy did how to comport herself in such a situation.", "그러나 그런 자리에서 어떻게 처신해야 하는지는 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 아마 루시보다 훨씬 잘 알았다."], ["Perhaps it might be that young men, such as Lord Lufton, liked to hear the sound of their own voices.", "어쩌면 러프턴 경 같은 젊은 사내들은 제 목소리 듣기를 좋아하는지도 몰랐다."]], [["“Immense deal of game about here,”", "“여기 사냥감이 엄청나게 많소.”"], ["Captain Culpepper said to her towards the end of the dinner.", "저녁 식사가 끝날 무렵 컬페퍼 대위가 그녀에게 말했다."], ["It was the second attempt he had made; on the former he had asked her whether she knew any of the fellows of the 9th.", "그가 시도한 두 번째였다. 앞서 그는 제9연대 사람들 가운데 아는 이가 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Is there?”", "“그런가요?”"], ["said Lucy. “Oh!", "루시가 말했다."], ["I saw Lord Lufton the other day with a great armful of pheasants.”", "“아! 며칠 전에 러프턴 경이 꿩을 한 아름 안고 있는 걸 보았어요.”"]], [["“An armful!", "“한 아름이라!"], ["Why we had seven cartloads the other day at Gatherum.”", "우리는 며칠 전 개서럼에서 일곱 수레를 채웠소.”"]], [["“Seven carts full of pheasants!”", "“꿩 일곱 수레라니요!”"], ["said Lucy, amazed.", "루시가 놀라 말했다."]], [["“That’s not so much.", "“그건 그리 많은 것도 아니오."], ["We had eight guns, you know.", "우리는 총이 여덟 자루였으니까."], ["Eight guns will do a deal of work when the game has been well got together.", "사냥감을 잘 모아 놓으면 총 여덟 자루가 일을 아주 많이 해내지요."], ["They manage all that capitally at Gatherum.", "개서럼에서는 그런 일을 다 훌륭하게 해내오."], ["Been at the duke’s, eh?”", "공작 댁에 가 보았소?”"]], [["Lucy had heard the Framley report as to Gatherum Castle, and said with a sort of shudder that she had never been at that place.", "루시는 개서럼 성에 관한 프램리 쪽 이야기를 들은 바 있어서, 몸을 떨듯이 그런 곳에는 한 번도 가 본 적이 없다고 말했다."], ["After this, Captain Culpepper troubled her no further.", "이 뒤로 컬페퍼 대위는 그녀를 더 이상 괴롭히지 않았다."]], [["When the ladies had taken themselves to the drawing-room Lucy found herself hardly better off than she had been at the dinner-table.", "부인들이 응접실로 물러난 뒤에도 루시의 처지는 식탁에 있을 때보다 나을 것이 거의 없었다."], ["Lady Lufton and Mrs. Grantly got themselves on to a sofa together, and there chatted confidentially into each other’s ears.", "레이디 러프턴과 그랜틀리 부인은 함께 소파에 자리를 잡고 거기서 서로 귀에 대고 다정하게 이야기했다."], ["Her ladyship had introduced Lucy and Miss Grantly, and then she naturally thought that the young people might do very well together.", "마님은 루시와 그랜틀리 양을 소개해 놓았으니, 젊은 사람들끼리 잘 어울릴 만하다고 자연히 생각한 것이었다."], ["Mrs. Robarts did attempt to bring about a joint conversation, which should include the three, and for ten minutes or so she worked hard at it.", "로바츠 부인은 셋이 함께하는 대화를 이끌어 보려 했고, 십 분쯤은 애를 썼다."], ["But it did not thrive.", "그러나 잘되지 않았다."], ["Miss Grantly was monosyllabic, smiling, however, at every monosyllable; and Lucy found that nothing would occur to her at that moment worthy of being spoken.", "그랜틀리 양은 한 마디씩만 했고, 그러면서도 그 한 마디마다 미소를 지었다. 그리고 루시는 그 순간 입에 올릴 만한 이야기가 아무것도 떠오르지 않았다."], ["There she sat, still and motionless, afraid to take up a book, and thinking in her heart how much happier she would have been at home at the parsonage.", "그녀는 거기 고요히 꼼짝없이 앉아, 책을 들 용기도 없이, 목사관 집에 있었더라면 얼마나 더 행복했을까 마음속으로 생각했다. 자기는 사교에 맞게 태어나지 않았다."], ["She was not made for society; she felt sure of that; and another time she would let Mark and Fanny come to Framley Court by themselves.", "그것만은 분명하다고 느꼈다. 그리고 다음에는 마크와 패니만 프램리 코트에 오게 하리라고 생각했다."]], [["And then the gentlemen came in, and there was another stir in the room.", "그러고서 신사들이 들어왔고, 방에는 또 한 번 움직임이 일었다."], ["Lady Lufton got up and bustled about; she poked the fire and shifted the candles, spoke a few words to Dr.", "레이디 러프턴은 일어나 부산히 돌아다녔다."], ["Grantly, whispered something to her son, patted Lucy on the cheek, told Fanny, who was a musician, that they would have a little music, and ended by putting her two hands on Griselda’s shoulders and telling her that the fit of her frock was perfect.", "난롯불을 쑤시고 촛대를 옮기고, 그랜틀리 박사에게 몇 마디 하고, 아들에게 무언가 귓속말을 하고, 루시의 뺨을 두드리고, 음악을 아는 패니에게 음악을 조금 하자고 말했다. 그리고 끝으로 그리젤다의 두 어깨에 손을 얹고 옷이 몸에 꼭 맞는다고 말해 주었다."], ["For Lady Lufton, though she did dress old herself, as Lucy had said, delighted to see those around her neat and pretty, jaunty and graceful.", "레이디 러프턴은 루시가 말했듯 자기는 늙게 차려입었지만, 주위 사람들이 말끔하고 예쁘고 멋들어지고 우아한 것을 보기를 좋아했으니까."]], [["“Dear Lady Lufton!”", "“친애하는 레이디 러프턴!”"], ["said Griselda, putting up her hand so as to press the end of her ladyship’s fingers.", "그리젤다가 손을 들어 마님의 손끝을 지그시 누르며 말했다."], ["It was the first piece of animation she had shown, and Lucy Robarts watched it all.", "그것은 그녀가 보인 첫 생기였고, 루시 로바츠는 그것을 다 지켜보았다."]], [["And then there was music.", "그러고서 음악이 있었다."], ["Lucy neither played nor sang; Fanny did both, and for an amateur did both well.", "루시는 연주도 노래도 하지 않았다. 패니는 둘 다 했고, 아마추어로서는 둘 다 잘했다."], ["Griselda did not sing, but she played; and did so in a manner that showed that neither her own labour nor her father’s money had been spared in her instruction.", "그리젤다는 노래는 하지 않았으나 연주는 했다. 그리고 그 연주는 제 수고도 아버지의 돈도 배움에 아끼지 않았음을 보여 주는 것이었다."], ["Lord Lufton sang also, a little, and Captain Culpepper a very little; so that they got up a concert among them.", "러프턴 경도 조금 노래했고, 컬페퍼 대위는 아주 조금 했다. 그래서 그들끼리 연주회가 차려졌다."], ["In the meantime the doctor and Mark stood talking together on the rug before the fire; the two mothers sat contented, watching the billings and the cooings of their offspring—and Lucy sat alone, turning over the leaves of a book of pictures.", "그동안 박사와 마크는 난로 앞 깔개에 서서 함께 이야기했고, 두 어머니는 흡족하게 앉아 제 자식들이 서로 다정하게 속살거리며 어르는 모습을 지켜보았다—그리고 루시는 혼자 앉아 그림책의 낱장을 넘겼다."], ["She made up her mind fully, then and there, that she was quite unfitted by disposition for such work as this.", "그녀는 그때 거기서, 자기가 이런 일에 성품으로 도무지 맞지 않는다고 완전히 마음을 정했다."], ["She cared for no one, and no one cared for her.", "그녀는 아무도 아끼지 않았고, 아무도 그녀를 아끼지 않았다."], ["Well, she must go through with it now; but another time she would know better.", "그래, 이제는 이것을 끝까지 견뎌야 한다. 그러나 다음에는 더 잘 알 것이다."], ["With her own book and a fireside she never felt herself to be miserable as she was now.", "제 책과 난롯가만 있으면 그녀는 지금처럼 스스로 비참하다고 느낀 적이 없었다."]], [["She had turned her back to the music, for she was sick of seeing Lord Lufton watch the artistic motion of Miss Grantly’s fingers, and was sitting at a small table as far away from the piano as a long room would permit, when she was suddenly roused from a reverie of self-reproach by a voice close behind her: “Miss Robarts,”", "그녀는 음악에 등을 돌렸다. 러프턴 경이 그랜틀리 양의 손가락이 예술적으로 움직이는 것을 지켜보는 모습이 지겨웠기 때문이다."], ["said the voice, “why have you cut us all?”", "그리고 긴 방이 허락하는 한 피아노에서 멀리 떨어진 작은 탁자에 앉아 있었는데, 바로 등 뒤에서 들린 목소리에 자책의 생각에서 갑자기 깨어났다. “로바츠 양,”"], ["and Lucy felt that though she heard the words plainly, nobody else did.", "그 목소리가 말했다. “왜 저희를 다 저버리셨어요?”"], ["Lord Lufton was now speaking to her as he had before spoken to Miss Grantly.", "그리고 루시는 자기에게는 그 말이 똑똑히 들렸으나 다른 누구에게도 들리지 않았음을 느꼈다. 러프턴 경은 앞서 그랜틀리 양에게 말할 때처럼 이제 그녀에게 말하고 있었다."]], [["“I don’t play, my lord,”", "“저는 연주를 하지 않습니다, 러프턴 경.”"], ["said Lucy, “nor yet sing.”", "루시가 말했다. “노래도 하지 않습니다.”"]], [["“That would have made your company so much more valuable to us, for we are terribly badly off for listeners.", "“그렇다면 함께해 주시는 것이 저희에게 훨씬 더 값진 일이 되었겠네요. 저희는 들어 줄 사람이 몹시 모자라니까요."], ["Perhaps you don’t like music?”", "어쩌면 음악을 좋아하지 않으시나요?”"]], [["“I do like it,—sometimes very much.”", "“좋아합니다—어떤 때는 아주 많이요.”"]], [["“And when are the sometimes?", "“그 어떤 때는 언제인가요?"], ["But we shall find it all out in time.", "하지만 그건 차차 다 알아내겠지요."], ["We shall have unravelled all your mysteries, and read all your riddles, by—when shall I say?—by the end of the winter.", "당신의 신비를 다 풀고 수수께끼를 다 읽어 낼 거예요. 언제라고 할까요?—겨울이 끝날 무렵쯤."], ["Shall we not?”", "그렇지 않겠어요?”"]], [["“I do not know that I have got any mysteries.”", "“저에게 무슨 신비가 있는지 모르겠습니다.”"]], [["“Oh, but you have!", "“아, 있어요!"], ["It is very mysterious in you to come and sit here, with your back to us all—”", "우리 모두에게 등을 돌리고 와서 여기 앉아 있는 것부터가 아주 신비로운데요—”"]], [["“Oh, Lord Lufton; if I have done wrong—!”", "“아, 러프턴 경. 제가 잘못했다면—!”"], ["and poor Lucy almost started from her chair, and a deep flush came across her dark cheek.", "그러면서 가엾은 루시는 의자에서 거의 벌떡 일어날 듯했고, 짙은 뺨에 깊은 홍조가 번졌다."]], [["“No—no; you have done no wrong.", "“아니에요—아니에요."], ["I was only joking.", "잘못하신 게 없어요."], ["It is we who have done wrong in leaving you to yourself—you who are the greatest stranger among us.”", "저는 그저 농담을 한 거예요. 잘못한 것은 저희지요, 당신을 혼자 두었으니까요—저희 가운데 가장 낯선 손님인 당신을요.”"]], [["“I have been very well, thank you.", "“저는 아주 잘 있었습니다, 고맙습니다."], ["I don’t care about being left alone.", "혼자 남겨지는 것은 개의치 않습니다."], ["I have always been used to it.”", "늘 그렇게 지내는 데 익숙했습니다.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but we must break you of the habit.", "하지만 저희가 그 버릇을 고쳐 드려야겠어요."], ["We won’t allow you to make a hermit of yourself.", "스스로 은자가 되게 놓아두지 않을 거예요."], ["But the truth is, Miss Robarts, you don’t know us yet, and therefore you are not quite happy among us.”", "그렇지만 사실은요, 로바츠 양, 아직 저희를 모르시니까 저희 사이에서 그리 행복하지 않으신 거예요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["yes, I am; you are all very good to me.”", "저는 행복합니다. 다들 저에게 아주 좋게 대해 주십니다.”"]], [["“You must let us be good to you.", "“저희가 좋게 대하도록 놓아두셔야 해요."], ["At any rate, you must let me be so.", "어쨌든 저만은 그러도록 놓아두셔야 해요."], ["You know, don’t you, that Mark and I have been dear friends since we were seven years old.", "아시지요, 마크와 저는 일곱 살 때부터 소중한 벗이었어요."], ["His wife has been my sister’s dearest friend almost as long; and now that you are with them, you must be a dear friend too.", "그의 아내는 거의 그만큼 오래 제 누이의 가장 소중한 벗이었지요. 그리고 이제 당신이 그들과 함께 있으니 당신도 소중한 벗이 되어야 해요."], ["You won’t refuse the offer; will you?”", "이 제안을 마다하지는 않으시겠지요?”"]], [["“Oh, no,”", "“아, 그러지 않겠습니다.” 그녀는 아주 귓속말로 말했다."], ["she said, quite in a whisper; and, indeed, she could hardly raise her voice above a whisper, fearing that tears would fall from her tell-tale eyes.", "그리고 실제로 귓속말 이상으로 목소리를 높이기가 어려웠다. 속을 다 드러내는 눈에서 눈물이 떨어질까 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["“Dr. and Mrs.", "“그랜틀리 박사 부부는 이틀쯤 뒤에 떠나실 거고, 그러면 저희가 당신을 이리로 불러들여야겠어요."], ["Grantly will have gone in a couple of days, and then we must get you down here. Miss Grantly is to remain for Christmas, and you two must become bosom friends.”", "그랜틀리 양은 성탄절까지 머물 예정이니, 두 분이 마음을 나누는 벗이 되어야 해요.”"]], [["Lucy smiled, and tried to look pleased, but she felt that she and Griselda Grantly could never be bosom friends—could never have anything in common between them.", "루시는 미소 지으며 기뻐하는 표정을 지어 보이려 했으나, 자기와 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 결코 마음을 나누는 벗이 될 수 없다고—둘 사이에 통하는 것이 결코 있을 수 없다고 느꼈다."], ["She felt sure that Griselda despised her, little, brown, plain, and unimportant as she was.", "그녀는 자기가 작고 가무잡잡하고 못생기고 대수롭지 않은 사람이니 그리젤다가 자기를 업신여긴다고 확신했다. 자기는 거꾸로 그리젤다를 업신여길 수 없었다."], ["She herself could not despise Griselda in turn; indeed she could not but admire Miss Grantly’s great beauty and dignity of demeanour; but she knew that she could never love her.", "실은 그랜틀리 양의 대단한 아름다움과 몸가짐의 품위에 감탄하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 자기가 그녀를 사랑할 수는 결코 없다는 것을 알았다."], ["It is hardly possible that the proud-hearted should love those who despise them; and Lucy Robarts was very proud-hearted.", "마음이 도도한 사람이 자기를 업신여기는 이들을 사랑하는 일은 좀처럼 있을 수 없다. 그리고 루시 로바츠는 마음이 몹시 도도했다."]], [["“Don’t you think she is very handsome?”", "“저분이 아주 아름답다고 생각하지 않으세요?”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, very,”", "“아, 아주 그렇습니다.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."], ["“Nobody can doubt that.”", "“누구도 의심할 수 없습니다.”"]], [["“Ludovic,”", "“루도빅,”"], ["said Lady Lufton—not quite approving of her son’s remaining so long at the back of Lucy’s chair—“won’t you give us another song?", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다—아들이 루시의 의자 뒤에 그렇게 오래 머물러 있는 것이 그리 마땅치 않았던 것이다—“노래 한 곡 더 들려주지 않으련?"], ["Mrs. Robarts and Miss Grantly are still at the piano.”", "로바츠 부인과 그랜틀리 양이 아직 피아노 앞에 있구나.”"]], [["“I have sung away all that I knew, mother.", "“제가 아는 건 다 불러 버렸어요, 어머니."], ["There’s Culpepper has not had a chance yet.", "컬페퍼는 아직 기회가 없었네요."], ["He has got to give us his dream—how he ‘dreamt that he dwelt in marble halls!’”", "그가 자기 꿈을 들려주어야 해요—어떻게 ‘대리석 궁전에 사는 꿈을 꾸었네’ 하는 그것을요.”"]], [["“I sang that an hour ago,”", "“그건 한 시간 전에 불렀소.”"], ["said the captain, not over pleased.", "대위가 그리 반갑지 않은 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["“But you certainly have not told us how ‘your little lovers came!’”", "“하지만 어떻게 ‘그대의 어린 연인들이 찾아왔네’ 하는 건 분명 아직 안 들려주셨지요.”"]], [["The captain, however, would not sing any more.", "그러나 대위는 더는 노래하려 하지 않았다."], ["And then the party was broken up, and the Robartses went home to their parsonage.", "그러고서 모임은 흩어졌고, 로바츠 씨네 사람들은 목사관으로 돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII. THE LITTLE BILL.", "# 제12장. 자그마한 어음"]], [["Lucy, during those last fifteen minutes of her sojourn in the Framley Court drawing-room, somewhat modified the very strong opinion she had before formed as to her unfitness for such society.", "루시는 프램리 코트 응접실에 머문 그 마지막 십오 분 동안, 자기가 그런 사교계에 맞지 않는다고 앞서 품었던 그 아주 굳은 생각을 다소 고쳐 잡았다."], ["It was very pleasant sitting there in that easy chair, while Lord Lufton stood at the back of it saying nice, soft, good-natured words to her.", "러프턴 경이 안락의자 등 뒤에 서서 그녀에게 다정하고 부드럽고 마음씨 좋은 말을 건네는 동안, 그 의자에 그렇게 앉아 있는 것은 아주 즐거웠다."], ["She was sure that in a little time she could feel a true friendship for him, and that she could do so without any risk of falling in love with him.", "조금만 지나면 그에게 참된 우정을 느낄 수 있을 것이고, 게다가 그를 사랑하게 될 위험 없이 그렇게 할 수 있을 것이라고 그녀는 확신했다."], ["But then she had a glimmering of an idea that such a friendship would be open to all manner of remarks, and would hardly be compatible with the world’s ordinary ways.", "그러나 그러면서도 그런 우정이란 온갖 종류의 뒷말에 열려 있을 것이고 세상의 여느 방식과는 좀처럼 어울리지 않으리라는 생각이 어렴풋이 그녀에게 비쳤다."], ["At any rate it would be pleasant to be at Framley Court, if he would come and occasionally notice her.", "어쨌든 그가 와서 이따금 자기를 알아봐 준다면 프램리 코트에 있는 것은 즐거울 것이었다."], ["But she did not admit to herself that such a visit would be intolerable if his whole time were devoted to Griselda Grantly.", "그러나 그의 시간이 온통 그리젤다 그랜틀리에게 바쳐진다면 그런 방문은 견딜 수 없으리라는 것은 스스로 인정하지 않았다."], ["She neither admitted it, nor thought it; but nevertheless, in a strange unconscious way, such a feeling did find entrance in her bosom.", "인정하지도 않았고 생각하지도 않았다. 그러나 그럼에도 이상하게, 저도 모르는 방식으로 그런 감정이 그녀의 가슴에 자리를 얻은 것은 사실이었다."]], [["And then the Christmas holidays passed away.", "그리고 성탄 휴일이 지나갔다."], ["How much of this enjoyment fell to her share, and how much of this suffering she endured, we will not attempt accurately to describe.", "이 즐거움 가운데 얼마만큼이 그녀의 몫으로 떨어졌고 이 고통 가운데 얼마만큼을 그녀가 견뎌 냈는지는 정확하게 그려 보려 하지 않겠다."], ["Miss Grantly remained at Framley Court up to Twelfth Night, and the Robartses also spent most of the season at the house.", "그랜틀리 양은 십이야까지 프램리 코트에 머물렀고, 로바츠 집안 사람들도 그 시절의 대부분을 그 집에서 보냈다."], ["Lady Lufton, no doubt, had hoped that everything might have been arranged on this occasion in accordance with her wishes, but such had not been the case.", "레이디 러프턴은 의심할 여지 없이 이번 기회에 모든 것이 자기 소망대로 정돈되기를 바랐으나, 그렇게 되지는 않았다. 러프턴 경은 분명히 그랜틀리 양을 매우 찬탄했다."], ["Lord Lufton had evidently admired Miss Grantly very much; indeed, he had said so to his mother half-a-dozen times; but it may almost be questioned whether the pleasure Lady Lufton derived from this was not more than neutralized by an opinion he once put forward that Griselda Grantly wanted some of the fire of Lucy Robarts.", "실은 어머니에게 그렇게 여섯 번은 말했다. 그러나 그리젤다 그랜틀리에게는 루시 로바츠의 불기운이 좀 모자란다고 그가 한번 내놓은 의견 때문에, 레이디 러프턴이 그 찬탄에서 얻은 기쁨이 도리어 상쇄되고도 남지 않았는지 의심스러울 지경이었다."]], [["“Surely, Ludovic, you would never compare the two girls,”", "“루도빅, 설마 그 두 아가씨를 견주려는 것은 아니겠지,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“Of course not.", "“물론 아니에요."], ["They are the very antipodes to each other.", "그 두 사람은 서로 정반대에 놓여 있는걸요."], ["Miss Grantly would probably be more to my taste; but then I am wise enough to know that it is so because my taste is a bad taste.”", "아마 그랜틀리 양이 제 취향에는 더 맞겠지요. 하지만 그것이 제 취향이 나쁜 취향이기 때문이라는 것쯤은 저도 알 만큼은 아는 사람입니다.”"]], [["“I know no man with a more accurate or refined taste in such matters,”", "“그런 일에 너보다 더 정확하고 세련된 취향을 가진 남자는 나도 알지 못한다,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["Beyond this she did not dare to go.", "이 이상으로 그녀는 감히 나아가지 못했다."], ["She knew very well that her strategy would be vain should her son once learn that she had a strategy.", "자기에게 계략이 있다는 것을 아들이 한번 알아차리기만 하면 그 계략이 헛일이 되어 버릴 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["To tell the truth, Lady Lufton was becoming somewhat indifferent to Lucy Robarts.", "사실을 말하자면 레이디 러프턴은 루시 로바츠에게 어느 정도 무심해지고 있었다. 그녀는 그 어린 아가씨에게 아주 친절했다."], ["She had been very kind to the little girl; but the little girl seemed hardly to appreciate the kindness as she should do—and then Lord Lufton would talk to Lucy, “which was so unnecessary, you know;”", "그런데 그 어린 아가씨는 그 친절을 마땅히 여겨야 할 만큼 여기는 것 같지 않았고 — 게다가 러프턴 경이 루시에게 말을 걸려 하는 것이었으니, “그건 그렇게까지 할 필요가 없는 일이잖아요” 하는 마음이었다."], ["and Lucy had got into a way of talking quite freely with Lord Lufton, having completely dropped that short, spasmodic, ugly exclamation of “my lord.”", "그리고 루시는 그 짧고 발작적이고 보기 흉한 “러프턴 경” 소리를 완전히 떨어 버린 채 러프턴 경과 아주 거침없이 이야기하는 버릇이 들었다."]], [["And so the Christmas festivities were at an end, and January wore itself away.", "그리하여 성탄의 잔치는 끝이 났고, 일월도 저물어 갔다."], ["During the greater part of this month Lord Lufton did not remain at Framley, but was nevertheless in the county, hunting with the hounds of both divisions, and staying at various houses.", "이 달의 대부분 동안 러프턴 경은 프램리에 머물지 않았으나, 그럼에도 주 안에 있으면서 두 지구의 사냥개들과 함께 사냥을 하고 여러 집에 머물렀다. 두세 밤은 콜디코츠에서 보냈다."], ["Two or three nights he spent at Chaldicotes; and one—let it only be told in an under voice—at Gatherum Castle!", "그리고 한 밤은 — 이것은 목소리를 낮추어서만 말하기로 하자 — 개서럼 성에서 보냈다! 이 일에 대해 그는 레이디 러프턴에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Of this he said nothing to Lady Lufton.", "마크에게 말한 대로, “어머니를 왜 불행하게 만들겠나?”"], ["“Why make her unhappy?” as he said to Mark.", "그러나 레이디 러프턴은 그것을 알고 있었다."], ["But Lady Lufton knew it, though she said not a word to him—knew it, and was unhappy.", "그에게 한마디도 하지 않았지만 — 알고 있었고, 불행했다."], ["“If he would only marry Griselda, there would be an end of that danger,”", "“그이가 그리젤다와 결혼만 해 준다면 저 위험도 끝이 날 텐데,”"], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 혼자 그렇게 말했다."]], [["But now we must go back for a while to the vicar and his little bill.", "그러나 이제 우리는 잠시 그 교구 사제와 그의 자그마한 어음으로 돌아가야 한다."], ["It will be remembered, that his first idea with reference to that trouble, after the reading of his father’s will, was to borrow the money from his brother John.", "기억하시겠지만, 아버지의 유언장을 읽은 뒤 그 곤경에 관하여 그가 처음 떠올린 생각은 아우 존에게서 그 돈을 빌리는 것이었다."], ["John was down at Exeter at the time, and was to stay one night at the parsonage on his way to London.", "존은 그때 엑서터에 내려와 있었고, 런던으로 가는 길에 하룻밤을 목사관에서 지내기로 되어 있었다."], ["Mark would broach the matter to him on the journey, painful though it would be to him to tell the story of his own folly to a brother so much younger than himself, and who had always looked up to him, clergyman and full-blown vicar as he was, with a deference greater than that which such difference in age required.", "마크는 그 길에서 아우에게 이 문제를 꺼내 볼 작정이었다. 자기보다 그렇게 어리고, 게다가 성직자요 버젓한 교구 사제인 자기를 나이 차이가 요구하는 것보다 더 큰 공경으로 언제나 우러러보아 온 아우에게 자기 자신의 어리석음을 이야기하는 것이 그에게는 괴로운 일이었지만 말이다."]], [["The story was told, however; but was told all in vain, as Mark found out before he reached Framley.", "그러나 이야기는 하고야 말았다. 다만 마크가 프램리에 이르기 전에 알아낸 대로, 온전히 헛되게 한 이야기였다."], ["His brother John immediately declared that he would lend him the money, of course—eight hundred, if his brother wanted it.", "아우 존은 당장에 돈을 빌려주겠다고 선언했다. 물론 빌려주겠다고 — 형이 원한다면 800파운드를."], ["He, John, confessed that, as regarded the remaining two, he should like to feel the pleasure of immediate possession.", "자기, 곧 존은 남은 200파운드에 관해서는 곧바로 손에 넣는 기쁨을 맛보고 싶다고 털어놓았다."], ["As for interest, he would not take any—take interest from a brother!", "이자에 관해서라면 받지 않겠다고 — 형에게서 이자를 받는다니!"], ["of course not.", "그럴 수 없었다."], ["Well, if Mark made such a fuss about it, he supposed he must take it; but would rather not.", "좋다, 마크가 그 일로 그렇게 야단을 떤다면 받아야 하겠지만, 그래도 받지 않는 편이 좋겠다고 했다."], ["Mark should have his own way, and do just what he liked.", "마크가 하고 싶은 대로 해야 하고, 그저 마음에 드는 대로 하라고 했다."]], [["This was all very well, and Mark had fully made up his mind that his brother should not be kept long out of his money.", "이것은 다 아주 좋았고, 마크는 아우를 오래 자기 돈에서 떼어 놓지는 않겠다고 마음을 굳게 정했다."], ["But then arose the question, how was that money to be reached?", "그러나 그다음에 문제가 생겼다. 그 돈에 어떻게 손이 닿는가?"], ["He, Mark, was executor, or one of the executors under his father’s will, and, therefore, no doubt, could put his hand upon it; but his brother wanted five months of being of age, and could not therefore as yet be put legally in possession of the legacy.", "그, 마크는 아버지의 유언장에 따른 유언 집행자였고, 아니면 집행자 가운데 하나였으니, 의심할 것 없이 그 돈에 손을 얹을 수는 있었다. 그러나 아우는 성년이 되기까지 다섯 달이 남아 있었고, 그러므로 아직은 법적으로 그 유산의 소유자가 될 수 없었다."]], [["“That’s a bore,”", "“그건 난처하군요,”"], ["said the assistant private secretary to the Lord Petty Bag, thinking, perhaps, as much of his own immediate wish for ready cash as he did of his brother’s necessities.", "프티백 경의 개인 비서보가 말했다. 아마 아우의 궁핍만큼이나 현금이 지금 당장 있으면 좋겠다는 자기 자신의 바람을 생각하면서 한 말이었을 것이다."], ["Mark felt that it was a bore, but there was nothing more to be done in that direction.", "마크도 그것이 난처한 일이라고 느꼈으나, 그 방면으로는 더 할 일이 없었다."], ["He must now find out how far the bankers could assist him.", "이제 그는 은행이 어디까지 자기를 도울 수 있는지 알아봐야 했다."]], [["Some week or two after his return to Framley he went over to Barchester, and called there on a certain Mr.", "프램리로 돌아온 뒤 한두 주가 지나 그는 바체스터로 건너가, 그곳 은행 가운데 하나의 지배인이며 자기와 안면이 있는 포레스트 씨라는 사람을 찾아갔다."], ["Forrest, the manager of one of the banks, with whom he was acquainted; and with many injunctions as to secrecy told this manager the whole of his story.", "그리고 비밀을 지켜 달라고 여러 번 당부하면서 이 지배인에게 자기 이야기를 다 털어놓았다."], ["At first he concealed the name of his friend Sowerby, but it soon appeared that no such concealment was of any avail.", "처음에는 벗 사워비의 이름을 감추었으나, 그런 감추기가 아무 소용이 없다는 것이 곧 드러났다."], ["“That’s Sowerby, of course,”", "“그거야 사워비지요, 뻔한 일입니다,”"], ["said Mr. Forrest.", "포레스트 씨가 말했다."], ["“I know you are intimate with him; and all his friends go through that, sooner or later.”", "“선생이 그 사람과 가깝게 지내는 건 나도 압니다. 그리고 그의 벗은 누구나 이르든 늦든 그 일을 겪지요.”"]], [["It seemed to Mark as though Mr. Forrest made very light of the whole transaction.", "포레스트 씨는 그 거래 전체를 아주 가볍게 여기는 것처럼 마크에게는 보였다."]], [["“I cannot possibly pay the bill when it falls due,”", "“만기가 되어도 저로서는 그 어음을 도저히 갚을 수 없습니다,”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no, of course not,”", "“아, 아니지요, 그야 그렇지요,”"], ["said Mr. Forrest.", "포레스트 씨가 말했다."], ["“It’s never very convenient to hand out four hundred pounds at a blow.", "“400파운드를 한꺼번에 내놓는 일이야 언제라도 그리 만만치 않습니다."], ["Nobody will expect you to pay it!”", "그걸 갚으라고 기대할 사람은 아무도 없어요!”"]], [["“But I suppose I shall have to do it sooner or later?”", "“하지만 이르든 늦든 제가 갚아야 하는 것이겠지요?”"]], [["“Well, that’s as may be.", "“글쎄요, 그건 그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있지요."], ["It will depend partly on how you manage with Sowerby, and partly on the hands it gets into.", "얼마쯤은 선생이 사워비와 어떻게 처리하는가에 달렸고, 얼마쯤은 그 어음이 누구 손에 들어가는가에 달렸습니다."], ["As the bill has your name on it, they’ll have patience as long as the interest is paid, and the commissions on renewal.", "어음에 선생 이름이 적혀 있으니, 이자가 지불되고 갱신 수수료가 지불되는 동안에는 그쪽에서 참고 기다릴 겁니다."], ["But no doubt it will have to be met some day by somebody.”", "하지만 언젠가는 누군가가 그것을 결제해야 하는 것이 틀림없지요.”"]], [["Mr. Forrest said that he was sure that the bill was not in Barchester; Mr.", "포레스트 씨는 그 어음이 바체스터에 있지 않을 것이 분명하다고 말했다."], ["Sowerby would not, he thought, have brought it to a Barchester bank.", "사워비 씨가 그것을 바체스터의 은행으로 들고 오지는 않았을 것이라고 그는 생각했다."], ["The bill was probably in London, but doubtless would be sent to Barchester for collection.", "어음은 아마 런던에 있을 것이나, 추심을 위해 바체스터로 보내질 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["“If it comes in my way,” said Mr.", "“그것이 내 손에 들어오면,”"], ["Forrest, “I will give you plenty of time, so that you may manage about the renewal with Sowerby.", "포레스트 씨가 말했다. “시간을 넉넉히 드리지요."], ["I suppose he’ll pay the expense of doing that.”", "그래서 사워비와 갱신 일을 처리하실 수 있게요. 그 비용은 그가 물겠지요.”"]], [["Mark’s heart was somewhat lighter as he left the bank.", "은행을 나설 때 마크의 마음은 다소 가벼워졌다."], ["Mr. Forrest had made so little of the whole transaction that he felt himself justified in making little of it also.", "포레스트 씨가 그 거래 전체를 하도 가볍게 여겼으므로 자기도 그것을 가볍게 여겨도 된다는 생각이 들었다."], ["“It may be as well,”", "“아마 이렇게 하는 게 좋겠지,”"], ["said he to himself, as he drove home, “not to tell Fanny anything about it till the three months have run round.", "마차를 몰아 집으로 돌아가면서 그는 혼자 생각했다. “석 달이 다 돌아가기까지는 패니에게 아무 말도 하지 않는 것이."], ["I must make some arrangement then.”", "그때는 무슨 방도를 마련해야겠지.”"], ["And in this way his mind was easier during the last of those three months than it had been during the two former.", "그리하여 그 석 달 가운데 마지막 달 동안 그의 마음은 앞의 두 달 동안보다 편했다."], ["That feeling of over-due bills, of bills coming due, of accounts overdrawn, of tradesmen unpaid, of general money cares, is very dreadful at first; but it is astonishing how soon men get used to it.", "기한이 지난 어음, 다가오는 만기, 초과 인출된 계좌, 값을 치르지 못한 상인들, 돈에 관한 온갖 걱정 — 그런 느낌은 처음에는 몹시 끔찍하다. 그러나 사람이 그것에 익숙해지는 속도는 놀랍기만 하다."], ["A load which would crush a man at first becomes, by habit, not only endurable, but easy and comfortable to the bearer.", "처음에는 사람을 짓눌러 부수었을 짐이 습관이 되고 나면 견딜 만한 것이 되고, 나아가 짊어진 사람에게 편하고 안락한 것까지 된다."], ["The habitual debtor goes along jaunty and with elastic step, almost enjoying the excitement of his embarrassments.", "상습적인 채무자는 경쾌하게, 탄력 있는 걸음으로 걸어 다니며 제 곤경이 주는 흥분을 거의 즐긴다. 사워비 씨가 바로 그러했다."], ["There was Mr. Sowerby himself; who ever saw a cloud on his brow?", "그의 이마에 구름이 낀 것을 누가 본 적이 있는가?"], ["It made one almost in love with ruin to be in his company.", "그와 함께 있으면 파산과 사랑에 빠질 지경이었다."], ["And even now, already, Mark Robarts was thinking to himself quite comfortably about this bill;—how very pleasantly those bankers managed these things.", "그리고 지금 벌써 마크 로바츠도 이 어음에 관하여 아주 편안하게 생각하고 있었다. 은행가들이 이런 일을 얼마나 유쾌하게 다루는가!"], ["Pay it!", "갚아라?"], ["No; no one will be so unreasonable as to expect you to do that! And then Mr.", "아니, 그런 것을 하라고 기대할 만큼 사리에 어긋난 사람은 아무도 없다!"], ["Sowerby certainly was a pleasant fellow, and gave a man something in return for his money.", "게다가 사워비 씨는 분명히 유쾌한 친구였고, 사람이 낸 돈에 대해 무엇인가는 돌려주었다."], ["It was still a question with Mark whether Lord Lufton had not been too hard on Sowerby.", "러프턴 경이 사워비에게 너무 모지게 굴지 않았느냐 하는 것이 마크에게는 여전히 물음거리였다."], ["Had that gentleman fallen across his clerical friend at the present moment, he might no doubt have gotten from him an acceptance for another four hundred pounds.", "그 신사가 마침 이 무렵에 그 성직자 벗과 마주쳤더라면, 그로부터 또 400파운드의 인수를 얻어 낼 수도 있었을 것이다."]], [["One is almost inclined to believe that there is something pleasurable in the excitement of such embarrassments, as there is also in the excitement of drink.", "그런 곤경이 주는 흥분에는 술이 주는 흥분과 마찬가지로 무엇인가 즐거운 것이 있다고 거의 믿고 싶어진다."], ["But then, at last, the time does come when the excitement is over, and when nothing but the misery is left.", "그러나 그러다가 마침내 흥분이 다 지나가고 오직 비참만 남는 때가 온다."], ["If there be an existence of wretchedness on earth it must be that of the elderly, worn-out roué, who has run this race of debt and bills of accommodation and acceptances,—of what, if we were not in these days somewhat afraid of good broad English, we might call lying and swindling, falsehood and fraud—and who, having ruined all whom he should have loved, having burnt up every one who would trust him much, and scorched all who would trust him a little, is at last left to finish his life with such bread and water as these men get, without one honest thought to strengthen his sinking heart, or one honest friend to hold his shivering hand!", "이 땅에 참담함의 삶이라는 것이 있다면 그것은 빚과 융통 어음과 인수의 경주를 달려온 늙고 지쳐 빠진 난봉꾼의 삶일 것이다 — 요즘 우리가 넓고 시원한 영어를 다소 두려워하지 않는다면 거짓말이요 사기요 허위요 협잡이라 불렀을 그것의 경주를 달려온 사람 말이다. 그리고 사랑해야 마땅했던 모든 이를 망쳐 놓고, 자기를 크게 믿어 준 사람은 누구나 태워 버리고 조금이라도 믿어 준 사람은 누구나 그슬려 놓은 끝에, 마침내 그런 자들이 얻는 빵과 물로 남은 목숨을 마치도록 버려진 사람 — 가라앉는 가슴을 붙들어 줄 정직한 생각 하나 없이, 떨리는 손을 잡아 줄 정직한 벗 하나 없이!"], ["If a man could only think of that, as he puts his name to the first little bill, as to which he is so good-naturedly assured that it can easily be renewed!", "사람이 첫 자그마한 어음에 자기 이름을 적을 때, 갱신이야 얼마든지 쉽게 할 수 있다고 그렇게 마음씨 좋게 다짐받으면서 그 이름을 적을 때, 그것만이라도 생각할 수 있다면!"]], [["When the three months had nearly run out, it so happened that Robarts met his friend Sowerby.", "석 달이 거의 다 되었을 무렵, 마침 로바츠가 벗 사워비를 만나게 되었다."], ["Mark had once or twice ridden with Lord Lufton as far as the meet of the hounds, and may, perhaps, have gone a field or two farther on some occasions.", "마크는 한두 번 러프턴 경과 함께 사냥개들이 모이는 곳까지 말을 타고 갔고, 어떤 경우에는 아마 들판 한두 개쯤 더 나아갔을 것이다. 어떤 목사들이 그러듯이 그가 사냥에 손을 댔다고 독자는 생각하지 마시라."], ["The reader must not think that he had taken to hunting, as some parsons do; and it is singular enough that whenever they do so they always show a special aptitude for the pursuit, as though hunting were an employment peculiarly congenial with a cure of souls in the country.", "그리고 참으로 이상한 일이지만, 목사들이 그럴 때면 언제나 그 놀이에 유별난 재간을 보인다. 마치 사냥이 시골에서 영혼을 돌보는 일과 각별히 어울리는 소일거리라도 되는 듯이."], ["Such a thought would do our vicar injustice.", "그런 생각은 우리 교구 사제에게 부당한 것이겠다."], ["But when Lord Lufton would ask him what on earth could be the harm of riding along the roads to look at the hounds, he hardly knew what sensible answer to give his lordship.", "그러나 러프턴 경이 길을 따라 말을 타고 가서 사냥개들을 구경하는 것이 대체 무슨 해가 되느냐고 물을 때, 그는 그 경에게 어떤 분별 있는 대답을 해야 할지 좀처럼 알지 못했다."], ["It would be absurd to say that his time would be better employed at home in clerical matters, for it was notorious that he had not clerical pursuits for the employment of half his time.", "집에서 성직의 일에 시간을 쓰는 편이 더 낫겠다고 말하는 것은 우스운 일이었을 터이다. 그는 자기 시간의 절반을 쓸 만큼의 성직 업무조차 가지고 있지 않다는 것이 널리 알려진 일이었으니 말이다."], ["In this way, therefore, he had got into a habit of looking at the hounds, and keeping up his acquaintance in the county, meeting Lord Dumbello, Mr.", "그리하여 이런 식으로 그는 사냥개들을 구경하고 주 안의 교분을 이어 가는 버릇이 들었고, 덤벨로 경, 그린 워커 씨, 해럴드 스미스, 그리고 그런 부류의 다른 죄인들과 만나게 되었다."], ["Green Walker, Harold Smith, and other such like sinners; and on one such occasion, as the three months were nearly closing, he did meet Mr. Sowerby.", "그리고 석 달이 거의 다 되어 가던 그런 자리 가운데 하나에서 그는 사워비 씨를 만난 것이다."]], [["“Look here, Sowerby; I want to speak to you for half a moment.", "“이봐요, 사워비. 잠깐만 이야기 좀 합시다."], ["What are you doing about that bill?”", "그 어음은 어떻게 하고 있어요?”"]], [["“Bill—bill!", "“어음 — 어음!"], ["what bill?—which bill?", "무슨 어음? 어느 어음?"], ["The whole bill, and nothing but the bill.", "어음의 전부, 오직 어음만을."], ["That seems to be the conversation now-a-days of all men, morning, noon, and night.”", "요즘은 아침이고 낮이고 밤이고 모든 사람의 이야기가 그것뿐인 것 같군요.”"]], [["“Don’t you know the bill I signed for you for four hundred pounds?”", "“내가 당신을 위해 400파운드로 서명해 준 그 어음을 모른다는 겁니까?”"]], [["“Did you, though?", "“정말 그랬어요?"], ["Was not that rather green of you?”", "그건 좀 풋내 나는 짓이 아니었나요?”"]], [["This did seem strange to Mark. Could it really be the fact that Mr.", "이것은 정말이지 마크에게 이상하게 보였다."], ["Sowerby had so many bills flying about that he had absolutely forgotten that occurrence in the Gatherum Castle bedroom?", "사워비 씨에게 날아다니는 어음이 하도 많아서 개서럼 성 침실에서 있었던 그 일을 아주 잊어버렸다는 것이 사실일 수 있는가?"], ["And then to be called green by the very man whom he had obliged!", "게다가 자기가 은혜를 베풀어 준 바로 그 사람에게서 풋내기라는 소리를 듣다니!"]], [["“Perhaps I was,”", "“아마 그랬겠지요,”"], ["said Mark, in a tone that showed that he was somewhat piqued.", "마크가 말했다. 좀 부아가 났음을 드러내는 목소리였다."], ["“But all the same I should be glad to know how it will be taken up.”", "“하지만 어쨌든 그것이 어떻게 결제될지는 알면 좋겠군요.”"]], [["“Oh, Mark, what a ruffian you are to spoil my day’s sport in this way.", "“아, 마크, 이런 식으로 내 하루 사냥을 망쳐 놓다니 당신은 정말 무뢰한이군요."], ["Any man but a parson would be too good a Christian for such intense cruelty.", "목사가 아닌 사람이라면 누구라도 그렇게 지독하게 잔인할 만큼 훌륭한 기독교인은 못 될 겁니다."], ["But let me see—four hundred pounds?", "하지만 어디 봅시다 — 400파운드?"], ["Oh, yes—Tozer has it.”", "아, 그래요 — 토저가 가지고 있어요.”"]], [["“And what will Tozer do with it?”", "“그러면 토저는 그것을 어떻게 할 겁니까?”"]], [["“Make money of it; whatever way he may go to work he will do that.”", "“그걸로 돈을 만들지요. 어떤 식으로 일을 벌이든 그것만은 할 겁니다.”"]], [["“But will Tozer bring it to me on the 20th?”", "“하지만 토저가 20일에 그것을 내게 들고 오겠지요?”"]], [["“Oh, Lord, no!", "“아이고, 천만에요!"], ["Upon my word, Mark, you are deliciously green.", "정말이지 마크, 당신은 어여쁘게 풋내기군요."], ["A cat would as soon think of killing a mouse directly she got it into her claws.", "고양이가 쥐를 발톱에 넣자마자 곧바로 죽여 버릴 생각을 할 리가 없지요."], ["But, joking apart, you need not trouble yourself.", "하지만 농담은 접어 두고, 당신은 애를 태울 필요가 없어요."], ["Maybe you will hear no more about it; or, perhaps, which no doubt is more probable, I may have to send it to you to be renewed.", "어쩌면 그 일에 대해 더는 아무 말도 못 듣게 될지 모르고, 아니면 아마도, 그쪽이 훨씬 더 그럴 법하기는 하지만, 갱신하시라고 내가 그것을 당신에게 보내야 할지도 모르지요."], ["But you need do nothing till you hear from me or somebody else.”", "하지만 나에게서든 다른 누구에게서든 소식을 듣기까지는 당신은 아무것도 할 필요가 없습니다.”"]], [["“Only do not let any one come down upon me for the money.”", "“다만 아무도 그 돈 때문에 나를 덮치게 하지는 마세요.”"]], [["“There is not the slightest fear of that.", "“그럴 염려는 조금도 없어요."], ["Tally-ho, old fellow!", "탤리호, 이 사람아!"], ["He’s away.", "놈이 달아났군."], ["Tally-ho!", "탤리호!"], ["right over by Gossetts’ barn.", "바로 저기 고셋네 헛간 쪽으로."], ["Come along, and never mind Tozer—‘Sufficient for the day is the evil thereof.’”", "갑시다, 토저는 잊어버리고 — ‘그날의 괴로움은 그날에 족하니라.’”"], ["And away they both went together, parson and member of Parliament.", "그리하여 두 사람은 함께 달려 나갔다. 목사와 국회의원이."]], [["And then again on that occasion Mark went home with a sort of feeling that the bill did not matter.", "그리하여 이번에도 마크는 그 어음이 별일 아니라는 듯한 느낌을 품고 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["Tozer would manage it somehow; and it was quite clear that it would not do to tell his wife of it just at present.", "토저가 어떻게든 처리해 줄 것이다. 그리고 지금 당장 아내에게 그것을 이야기해서는 안 될 것이 아주 분명했다."]], [["On the 21st of that month of February, however, he did receive a reminder that the bill and all concerning it had not merely been a farce.", "그러나 그 이월 21일에 그는 그 어음과 그에 관한 모든 일이 한갓 익살극이 아니었음을 일러 주는 것을 받았다."], ["This was a letter from Mr.", "그것은 사워비 씨에게서 온 편지였다."], ["Sowerby, dated from Chaldicotes, though not bearing the Barchester post-mark, in which that gentleman suggested a renewal—not exactly of the old bill, but of a new one.", "콜디코츠에서 날짜가 적혀 있었으나 바체스터의 소인은 찍혀 있지 않았고, 그 안에서 그 신사는 갱신을 제안하고 있었다 — 꼭 낡은 어음의 갱신이 아니라 새 어음의 갱신을."], ["It seemed to Mark that the letter had been posted in London.", "그 편지는 런던에서 부쳐진 것 같다고 마크에게는 보였다."], ["If I give it entire, I shall, perhaps, most quickly explain its purport:", "그것을 온전히 옮겨 놓으면 아마 그 뜻이 가장 빨리 설명되겠다 : —"]], [["Chaldicotes,—20th February, 185—.", "콜디코츠, 185─년 2월 20일."]], [["MY DEAR MARK,—“Lend not thy name to the money-dealers, for the same is a destruction and a snare.”", "내 사랑하는 마크에게, — “네 이름을 돈 장사꾼에게 빌려주지 말라, 그것은 멸망이요 올무니라.”"], ["If that be not in the Proverbs, it ought to be.", "이 말이 잠언에 없다면 마땅히 있어야 할 겁니다."], ["Tozer has given me certain signs of his being alive and strong this cold weather.", "토저가 이 추운 날씨에도 자기가 살아서 기운차다는 표시를 몇 가지 내게 보내 왔어요."], ["As we can neither of us take up that bill for £400 at the moment, we must renew it, and pay him his commission and interest, with all the rest of his perquisites, and pickings, and stealings—from all which, I can assure you, Tozer does not keep his hands as he should do.", "우리 둘 다 지금 당장은 그 400파운드 어음을 결제할 수 없으니, 그것을 갱신하고 그에게 수수료와 이자를 물어야 합니다. 그 밖에 그의 온갖 부수입과 곁수입과 도둑질까지 다 얹어서요 — 그 모든 일에서 토저가 마땅히 지켜야 할 만큼 제 손을 깨끗이 지키지 않는다는 것은 내가 보증할 수 있어요."]], [["To cover this and some other little outstanding trifles, I have filled in the new bill for £500, making it due 23rd of May next.", "이것과 그 밖의 자잘하게 밀린 것들을 덮으려고 새 어음은 500파운드로 채워 넣었고, 만기는 오는 5월 23일로 했습니다."], ["Before that time, a certain accident will, I trust, have occurred to your impoverished friend.", "그때가 오기 전에 당신의 궁색한 벗에게 어떤 사고가 하나 일어나 있을 것이라고 나는 믿어요."], ["By-the-by, I never told you how she went off from Gatherum Castle, the morning after you left us, with the Greshams.", "그러고 보니, 당신이 우리를 떠난 이튿날 아침에 그 여인이 그레셤네와 함께 개서럼 성을 떠나 버린 이야기를 아직 안 했군요. 짐수레 밧줄로도 그이를 붙들지 못했을 겁니다."], ["Cart-ropes would not hold her, even though the duke held them; which he did, with all the strength of his ducal hands.", "공작이 그 밧줄을 붙들었는데도요. 그리고 공작은 정말로 붙들었지요, 그 공작다운 손의 온 힘을 다해서."], ["She would go to meet some doctor of theirs, and so I was put off for that time; but I think that the matter stands in a good train.", "그이가 그 집안의 어떤 의사를 만나러 가겠다고 하니 나는 그때는 물러날 수밖에 없었어요. 하지만 그 일은 좋은 궤도에 올라 있다고 생각합니다."]], [["Do not lose a post in sending back the bill accepted, as Tozer may annoy you—nay, undoubtedly will, if the matter be not in his hand, duly signed by both of us, the day after to-morrow.", "인수 서명을 한 어음을 되돌려 보내는 데 우편 한 편도 놓치지 마세요. 토저가 당신을 괴롭힐 수 있으니까요 — 아니, 모레까지 그 서류가 우리 둘의 서명을 제대로 받아 그의 손에 들어가 있지 않으면 틀림없이 괴롭힐 겁니다."], ["He is an ungrateful brute; he has lived on me for these eight years, and would not let me off a single squeeze now to save my life.", "그는 은혜를 모르는 짐승이에요. 여덟 해 동안 나를 뜯어먹고 살았는데도 지금 내 목숨을 살리려고 한 번의 짜내기조차 면해 주려 하지 않을 사람입니다."], ["But I am specially anxious to save you from the annoyance and cost of lawyers’ letters; and if delayed, it might get into the papers.", "하지만 나는 변호사 편지의 번거로움과 비용에서 당신을 지켜 주고 싶은 마음이 각별해요. 그리고 늦어지면 그 일이 신문에까지 오를 수도 있습니다."]], [["Put it under cover to me, at No. 7, Duke Street, St.", "세인트 제임스 듀크 스트리트 7번지의 나에게로 봉해 보내세요."], ["James’s. I shall be in town by that time.", "그때면 나는 런던에 있을 겁니다."]], [["Good-bye, old fellow.", "그럼 잘 있어요, 이 사람아."], ["That was a decent brush we had the other day from Cobbold’s Ashes.", "요전 날 코볼즈 애시스에서 우리가 한 그 몰이는 제법이었지요."], ["I wish I could get that brown horse from you.", "그 갈색 말을 당신에게서 얻을 수 있으면 좋겠군요."], ["I would not mind going to a hundred and thirty.", "130까지는 갈 마음이 있습니다."]], [["Yours ever,", "언제나 당신의 벗,"]], [["When Mark had read it through he looked down on his table to see whether the old bill had fallen from the letter; but no, there was no enclosure, and had been no enclosure but the new bill.", "마크가 그것을 다 읽고 나서, 낡은 어음이 편지에서 떨어져 나왔는지 보려고 책상을 내려다보았다. 그러나 아니었다."], ["And then he read the letter through again, and found that there was no word about the old bill,—not a syllable, at least, as to its whereabouts.", "동봉된 것은 없었고, 새 어음 말고는 애초에 동봉된 것이 없었다. 그러고 나서 그는 편지를 다시 처음부터 읽어 보았고, 낡은 어음에 관해서는 한마디도 없다는 것을 알아냈다 — 적어도 그것이 어디 있는지에 관해서는 한 음절도 없었다."], ["Sowerby did not even say that it would remain in his own hands.", "사워비는 그것이 자기 손에 남아 있으리라는 말조차 하지 않았다."]], [["Mark did not in truth know much about such things.", "마크는 사실 그런 일에 관해 아는 것이 많지 않았다."], ["It might be that the very fact of his signing this second document would render that first document null and void; and from Sowerby’s silence on the subject, it might be argued that this was so well known to be the case, that he had not thought of explaining it.", "이 두 번째 서류에 그가 서명한다는 바로 그 사실이 저 첫 번째 서류를 무효로 만들어 버리는 것일 수도 있었다. 그리고 사워비가 그 문제에 대해 잠자코 있는 것을 보면, 그것이 워낙 뻔한 일로 알려져 있어서 그가 설명할 생각조차 하지 않았다고 볼 수도 있었다."], ["But yet Mark could not see how this should be so.", "그러나 그러면서도 마크는 어째서 그렇게 되는지 알 수가 없었다."]], [["But what was he to do?", "그러나 그가 어떻게 해야 하는가?"], ["That threat of cost and lawyers, and specially of the newspapers, did have its effect upon him—as no doubt it was intended to do.", "비용과 변호사에 대한, 특히 신문에 대한 그 위협은 정말로 그에게 효험을 발휘했다 — 의심할 것 없이 그렇게 하려고 한 것이었다."], ["And then he was utterly dumfounded by Sowerby’s impudence in drawing on him for £500 instead of £400, “covering,”", "그리고 400파운드가 아니라 500파운드로 자기에게 어음을 발행한 사워비의 뻔뻔함에 그는 아주 어안이 벙벙해졌다."], ["as Sowerby so good-humouredly said, “sundry little outstanding trifles.”", "사워비가 그렇게 마음씨 좋게 말한 대로 “자잘하게 밀린 여러 가지”를 “덮기” 위해서라니."]], [["But at last he did sign the bill, and sent it off, as Sowerby had directed.", "그러나 마침내 그는 그 어음에 서명을 하고, 사워비가 지시한 대로 그것을 부쳐 보냈다."], ["What else was he to do?", "그 밖에 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["Fool that he was.", "어리석은 사람."], ["A man always can do right, even though he has done wrong before.", "사람은 앞서 그릇된 일을 했더라도 언제나 옳은 일을 할 수 있다."], ["But that previous wrong adds so much difficulty to the path—a difficulty which increases in tremendous ratio, till a man at last is choked in his struggling, and is drowned beneath the waters.", "그러나 그 앞서의 그릇된 일이 그 길에 그토록 많은 어려움을 더한다 — 무서운 비율로 늘어나는 어려움을. 그리하여 마침내 사람은 몸부림 속에서 숨이 막히고 물 아래로 가라앉아 죽는다."]], [["And then he put away Sowerby’s letter carefully, locking it up from his wife’s sight.", "그러고 나서 그는 사워비의 편지를 조심스럽게 치워 두었다. 아내의 눈에 닿지 않도록 자물쇠를 채워서."], ["It was a letter that no parish clergyman should have received.", "그것은 어떤 교구 성직자도 받아서는 안 될 편지였다."], ["So much he acknowledged to himself.", "그만큼은 그도 스스로 인정했다."], ["But nevertheless it was necessary that he should keep it.", "그러나 그럼에도 그것을 보관해 두는 것은 필요한 일이었다."], ["And now again for a few hours this affair made him very miserable.", "그리하여 이번에도 몇 시간 동안 이 일은 그를 몹시 비참하게 만들었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIII. DELICATE HINTS.", "# 제13장. 조심스러운 암시"]], [["Lady Lufton had been greatly rejoiced at that good deed which her son did in giving up his Leicestershire hunting, and coming to reside for the winter at Framley.", "레이디 러프턴은 아들이 레스터셔의 사냥을 접고 겨울을 지내러 프램리로 온 그 착한 처사에 크게 기뻐하고 있었다."], ["It was proper, and becoming, and comfortable in the extreme.", "그것은 온당하고, 어울리고, 더할 나위 없이 마음 편한 일이었다."], ["An English nobleman ought to hunt in the county where he himself owns the fields over which he rides; he ought to receive the respect and honour due to him from his own tenants; he ought to sleep under a roof of his own, and he ought also—so Lady Lufton thought—to fall in love with a young embryo bride of his own mother’s choosing.", "영국의 귀인이라면 자기가 말을 달리는 그 들판을 스스로 소유한 주에서 사냥을 해야 하고, 자기 소작인들로부터 마땅히 자기에게 돌아올 존경과 예우를 받아야 하고, 자기 것인 지붕 아래에서 잠을 자야 하며, 또한 — 레이디 러프턴의 생각으로는 — 자기 어머니가 골라 놓은 어린 신붓감과 사랑에 빠져야 하는 것이었다."]], [["And then it was so pleasant to have him there in the house.", "그리고 그가 집에 있다는 것은 참으로 즐거운 일이었다."], ["Lady Lufton was not a woman who allowed her life to be what people in common parlance call dull.", "레이디 러프턴은 세상에서 흔히 무료하다고 부르는 그런 삶을 스스로에게 허락하는 여자가 아니었다."], ["She had too many duties, and thought too much of them, to allow of her suffering from tedium and ennui.", "그녀에게는 해야 할 일이 너무 많았고 그 일들을 너무 무겁게 여겼으므로, 지루함과 권태에 시달릴 틈이 없었다."], ["But nevertheless the house was more joyous to her when he was there.", "그럼에도 그가 있을 때면 집은 그녀에게 더 흥겨웠다."], ["There was a reason for some little gaiety, which would never have been attracted thither by herself, but which, nevertheless, she did enjoy when it was brought about by his presence.", "그녀 혼자서는 결코 이 집으로 끌어들이지 못했을, 그러나 그가 있어서 생겨난 것이라면 그녀도 정말로 즐기게 되는 약간의 흥겨움 — 그런 것에 이유가 생기는 것이었다."], ["She was younger and brighter when he was there, thinking more of the future and less of the past.", "그가 있을 때 그녀는 더 젊고 더 밝았으며, 지난 일보다 앞일을 더 많이 생각했다. 그녀는 그를 바라볼 수 있었고, 그것만으로도 그녀에게는 행복이었다."], ["She could look at him, and that alone was happiness to her.", "게다가 그는 그녀에게 다정하게 굴었다."], ["And then he was pleasant-mannered with her; joking with her on her little old-world prejudices in a tone that was musical to her ear as coming from him; smiling on her, reminding her of those smiles which she had loved so dearly when as yet he was all her own, lying there in his little bed beside her chair.", "그녀의 조그만 구시대적 편견을 두고 농을 걸어도, 그의 입에서 나오면 그 어조가 그녀의 귀에는 음악이었다. 그가 그녀에게 미소를 지어 보일 때면, 그가 아직 온전히 그녀만의 것이던 시절, 그녀의 의자 옆 작은 침대에 누워 있던 시절에 그토록 애틋하게 사랑했던 그 미소들이 떠올랐다."], ["He was kind and gracious to her, behaving like a good son, at any rate while he was there in her presence.", "그는 그녀에게 친절하고 너그러웠으며, 적어도 그녀 앞에 있는 동안에는 착한 아들답게 행동했다."], ["When we add, to this, her fears that he might not be so perfect in his conduct when absent, we may well imagine that Lady Lufton was pleased to have him there at Framley Court.", "여기에, 눈앞에 없을 때에는 그의 행실이 그만큼 온전하지 못할지도 모른다는 그녀의 두려움까지 보태면, 레이디 러프턴이 그를 프램리 코트에 두고 있어서 기뻐한 것을 우리도 넉넉히 짐작할 수 있다."]], [["She had hardly said a word to him as to that five thousand pounds.", "그 5천 파운드에 관하여 그녀는 그에게 거의 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["Many a night, as she lay thinking on her pillow, she said to herself that no money had ever been better expended, since it had brought him back to his own house.", "여러 밤 베개에 누워 생각에 잠기면서 그녀는, 그 돈이 그를 그의 집으로 돌아오게 했으니 그보다 더 잘 쓰인 돈은 여태 없었다고 혼자 되뇌었다."], ["He had thanked her for it in his own open way, declaring that he would pay it back to her during the coming year, and comforting her heart by his rejoicing that the property had not been sold.", "그는 자기다운 시원한 태도로 그 일을 고마워했고, 다음 해 안에 갚겠다고 언명했으며, 재산이 팔리지 않은 것을 기뻐함으로써 그녀의 마음을 위로해 주었다."]], [["“I don’t like the idea of parting with an acre of it,”", "“그 땅에서 한 에이커라도 떼어 낸다는 건 생각만 해도 싫어요,”"], ["he had said.", "그가 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“Of course not, Ludovic.", "“그럼 그렇지, 루도빅."], ["Never let the estate decrease in your hands.", "네 손에서 영지가 줄어들게 하는 일은 절대로 없어야 한다."], ["It is only by such resolutions as that that English noblemen and English gentlemen can preserve their country.", "영국의 귀인들과 영국의 신사들이 자기 나라를 지켜 낼 수 있는 것은 오직 그런 결심으로써다."], ["I cannot bear to see property changing hands.”", "나는 땅이 남의 손으로 넘어가는 것을 차마 보지 못하겠구나.”"]], [["“Well, I suppose it’s a good thing to have land in the market sometimes, so that the millionnaires may know what to do with their money.”", "“글쎄요, 백만장자들이 자기 돈을 어떻게 해야 할지 알 수 있도록 이따금 땅이 시장에 나오는 것도 좋은 일이 아닐까요.”"]], [["“God forbid that yours should be there!”", "“하느님이 막아 주시기를, 네 땅이 거기 나가는 일만은!”"], ["And the widow made a little mental prayer that her son’s acres might be protected from the millionnaires and other Philistines.", "그러고서 이 미망인은 아들의 땅이 백만장자들과 그 밖의 블레셋 사람들로부터 지켜지기를 마음속으로 짧게 기도했다."]], [["“Why, yes: I don’t exactly want to see a Jew tailor investing his earnings at Lufton,”", "“하기야 그렇지요. 유대인 재봉사가 벌어들인 돈을 러프턴에 투자하는 것을 보고 싶지는 않으니까요,”"], ["said the lord.", "나리가 말했다."]], [["“Heaven forbid!”", "“하늘이 막아 주시기를!”"], ["said the widow.", "이 미망인이 말했다."]], [["All this, as I have said, was very nice.", "앞에서도 말했듯이, 이 모든 것이 참 좋았다."], ["It was manifest to her ladyship, from his lordship’s way of talking, that no vital injury had as yet been done: he had no cares on his mind, and spoke freely about the property: but nevertheless there were clouds even now, at this period of bliss, which somewhat obscured the brilliancy of Lady Lufton’s sky.", "나리의 이야기하는 태도로 보아 아직 치명적인 손상은 없었다는 것이 마님에게는 분명했다. 그의 마음에는 걱정이 없었고 재산에 관해서도 거침없이 말했다."], ["Why was Ludovic so slow in that affair of Griselda Grantly?", "그러나 그럼에도 이 복된 시절에조차 레이디 러프턴의 하늘의 빛을 다소 흐리게 하는 구름이 있었다."], ["why so often in these latter winter days did he saunter over to the parsonage?", "루도빅은 왜 그리젤다 그랜틀리의 일에 그렇게 더딘가? 겨울이 저물어 가는 이 며칠 동안 그는 왜 그렇게 자주 목사관으로 어슬렁어슬렁 내려가는가?"], ["And then that terrible visit to Gatherum Castle!", "그리고 개서럼 성의 그 끔찍한 방문!"]], [["What actually did happen at Gatherum Castle, she never knew.", "개서럼 성에서 실제로 무슨 일이 있었는지 그녀는 끝내 알지 못했다."], ["We, however, are more intrusive, less delicate in our inquiries, and we can say.", "그러나 우리는 더 뻔뻔하고 캐묻는 데에 덜 조심스러우니, 우리는 말할 수 있다."], ["He had a very bad day’s sport with the West Barsetshire.", "그는 서바셋주 사냥회와 함께 대단히 형편없는 하루를 보냈다."], ["The county is altogether short of foxes, and some one who understands the matter must take that point up before they can do any good.", "이 주에는 도무지 여우가 모자라니, 사정을 아는 누군가가 그 점을 손보지 않고서는 아무 성과도 볼 수 없을 것이다."], ["And after that he had had rather a dull dinner with the duke.", "그런 다음 그는 공작과 함께 다소 심심한 만찬을 했다."], ["Sowerby had been there, and in the evening he and Sowerby had played billiards.", "사워비가 거기 있었고, 저녁에 그와 사워비는 당구를 쳤다."], ["Sowerby had won a pound or two, and that had been the extent of the damage done.", "사워비가 한두 파운드를 땄고, 그것이 입은 손해의 전부였다."]], [["But those saunterings over to the parsonage might be more dangerous.", "그러나 목사관으로 어슬렁거려 내려가는 그 걸음은 더 위험할 수 있었다."], ["Not that it ever occurred to Lady Lufton as possible that her son should fall in love with Lucy Robarts.", "아들이 루시 로바츠와 사랑에 빠질 수도 있다는 생각이 레이디 러프턴의 머리에 떠오른 적은 결코 없었다."], ["Lucy’s personal attractions were not of a nature to give ground for such a fear as that.", "루시의 됨됨이가 지닌 매력은 그런 두려움에 근거를 줄 만한 것이 아니었다."], ["But he might turn the girl’s head with his chatter; she might be fool enough to fancy any folly; and, moreover, people would talk.", "그러나 그가 재잘거리는 말로 그 아이의 머리를 돌려놓을 수는 있었다. 그 아이가 어떤 어리석은 일이라도 상상할 만큼 어리석을 수도 있었다."], ["Why should he go to the parsonage now more frequently than he had ever done before Lucy came there?", "게다가 사람들이 입을 놀릴 것이었다. 루시가 그곳에 오기 전에 여태 그랬던 것보다 지금 더 자주 목사관에 갈 까닭이 그에게 무엇인가?"]], [["And then her ladyship, in reference to the same trouble, hardly knew how to manage her invitations to the parsonage.", "그리고 마님은 바로 그 걱정 때문에, 목사관 사람들을 초대하는 일을 어떻게 다뤄야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["These hitherto had been very frequent, and she had been in the habit of thinking that they could hardly be too much so; but now she was almost afraid to continue the custom.", "그때까지 그 초대는 대단히 빈번했고, 아무리 잦아도 지나칠 것이 없다고 그녀는 늘 생각해 왔다. 그러나 이제는 그 관례를 이어 가기가 거의 두려웠다."], ["She could not ask the parson and his wife without Lucy; and when Lucy was there, her son would pass the greater part of the evening in talking to her, or playing chess with her.", "목사와 그 아내를 부르면서 루시를 빼놓을 수는 없었다. 그리고 루시가 오면 아들은 저녁의 대부분을 그 아이와 이야기하거나 그 아이와 체스를 두며 보내는 것이었다."], ["Now this did disturb Lady Lufton not a little.", "이것이 레이디 러프턴의 마음을 적지 않게 어지럽혔다."]], [["And then Lucy took it all so quietly.", "게다가 루시는 그 모든 것을 너무나 태연하게 받아들였다."], ["On her first arrival at Framley she had been so shy, so silent, and so much awestruck by the grandeur of Framley Court, that Lady Lufton had sympathized with her and encouraged her.", "프램리에 처음 왔을 때 그 아이는 어찌나 수줍고, 어찌나 말이 없고, 프램리 코트의 위세에 어찌나 압도되어 있었던지, 레이디 러프턴은 그 아이를 딱하게 여겨 기운을 돋우어 주었다."], ["She had endeavoured to moderate the blaze of her own splendour, in order that Lucy’s unaccustomed eyes might not be dazzled.", "익숙하지 못한 루시의 눈이 부시지 않도록 그녀는 자기 화려함의 불빛을 누그러뜨리려 애쓰기까지 했다."], ["But all this was changed now.", "그러나 이제 그 모든 것이 달라졌다."], ["Lucy could listen to the young lord’s voice by the hour together—without being dazzled in the least.", "루시는 젊은 나리의 목소리를 몇 시간이고 내리 들을 수 있었다 — 조금도 눈이 부시지 않은 채로."]], [["Under these circumstances two things occurred to her.", "이런 사정 아래에서 그녀에게 두 가지 방도가 떠올랐다."], ["She would speak either to her son or to Fanny Robarts, and by a little diplomacy have this evil remedied.", "아들에게 말하든지 패니 로바츠에게 말하든지 하여, 약간의 수완으로 이 폐단을 고쳐 놓겠다는 것이었다."], ["And then she had to determine on which step she would take.", "그러고서 그녀는 그 둘 가운데 어느 쪽을 택할지 정해야 했다."]], [["“Nothing could be more reasonable than Ludovic.”", "“루도빅만큼 말이 통하는 아이는 없지.”"], ["So at least she said to herself over and over again.", "적어도 그녀는 몇 번이고 스스로에게 그렇게 말했다."], ["But then Ludovic understood nothing about such matters; and he had, moreover, a habit, inherited from his father, of taking the bit between his teeth whenever he suspected interference.", "그러나 루도빅은 그런 일에 대해서는 아무것도 몰랐다. 게다가 그에게는 아버지에게서 물려받은 버릇이 하나 있었으니, 남이 간섭한다는 낌새만 채면 재갈을 이로 물어 버리는 버릇이었다."], ["Drive him gently without pulling his mouth about, and you might take him anywhere, almost at any pace; but a smart touch, let it be ever so slight, would bring him on his haunches, and then it might be a question whether you could get him another mile that day.", "입을 마구 당기지 않고 부드럽게 몰아가면 어디로든 데려갈 수 있고 거의 어떤 걸음으로든 갈 수 있다. 그러나 아무리 가벼운 것이라도 한 번 날카롭게 채기만 하면 그는 뒷다리로 주저앉아 버릴 것이고, 그러면 그날 그를 한 마일이라도 더 나아가게 할 수 있을지가 문제가 될 것이었다."], ["So that on the whole Lady Lufton thought that the other plan would be the best.", "그러니 이러저러 따져 보아 레이디 러프턴은 다른 쪽 방도가 제일 낫겠다고 생각했다."], ["I have no doubt that Lady Lufton was right.", "레이디 러프턴이 옳았다는 데에 나는 의심을 두지 않는다."]], [["She got Fanny up into her own den one afternoon, and seated her discreetly in an easy arm-chair, making her guest take off her bonnet, and showing by various signs that the visit was regarded as one of great moment.", "어느 날 오후 그녀는 패니를 자기만의 방으로 올라오게 하여 안락의자에 조심스레 앉히고, 손님에게 보닛을 벗게 하고, 이 방문이 대단히 중대한 것으로 여겨진다는 것을 여러 가지 표시로 드러냈다."]], [["“Fanny,”", "“패니,”"], ["she said, “I want to speak to you about something that is important and necessary to mention, and yet it is a very delicate affair to speak of.”", "그녀가 말했다. “중요하고 꼭 말해 두어야 할 일이 있어서 이야기를 하려는데, 그런데 그게 말을 꺼내기에는 아주 조심스러운 일이에요.”"], ["Fanny opened her eyes, and said that she hoped that nothing was wrong.", "패니는 눈을 크게 뜨고, 아무 일도 잘못된 게 없기를 바란다고 말했다."]], [["“No, my dear, I think nothing is wrong: I hope so, and I think I may say I’m sure of it; but then it’s always well to be on one’s guard.”", "“아니에요, 얘야, 잘못된 건 없다고 생각해요. 그러기를 바라고, 확실히 그렇다고 말해도 된다고 생각해요. 그렇지만 조심해 두는 것은 언제나 좋은 일이지요.”"]], [["“Yes, it is,”", "“예, 그렇습니다,” 패니가 말했다."], ["said Fanny, who knew that something unpleasant was coming—something as to which she might probably be called upon to differ from her ladyship. Mrs.", "그녀는 무언가 달갑지 않은 것이 오고 있다는 것을, 아마도 마님과 뜻을 달리해야 할 무엇인가가 오고 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["Robarts’ own fears, however, were running entirely in the direction of her husband;—and, indeed, Lady Lufton had a word or two to say on that subject also, only not exactly now.", "그러나 로바츠 부인 자신의 두려움은 온통 남편 쪽으로 달려가고 있었다. 그리고 사실 레이디 러프턴도 그 문제에 관하여 한두 마디 할 말이 있었으나, 바로 지금은 아니었다."], ["A hunting parson was not at all to her taste; but that matter might be allowed to remain in abeyance for a few days.", "사냥을 다니는 목사는 그녀의 취향에 도무지 맞지 않았다. 그러나 그 문제는 며칠쯤 미뤄 두어도 될 일이었다."]], [["“Now, Fanny, you know that we have all liked your sister-in-law, Lucy, very much.”", "“자, 패니, 우리가 다들 패니의 시누이 루시를 아주 좋아했다는 건 알고 있겠지요.”"], ["And then Mrs. Robarts’ mind was immediately opened, and she knew the rest as well as though it had all been spoken.", "그러자 로바츠 부인의 마음은 즉시 열렸고, 나머지 말이 다 나온 것이나 다름없이 그녀는 그것을 알아차렸다."], ["“I need hardly tell you that, for I am sure we have shown it.”", "“그 말은 새삼 할 필요도 없겠지요. 우리가 그것을 보여 주었으니까요.”"]], [["“You have, indeed, as you always do.”", "“정말 그러셨습니다. 언제나 그러시듯이요.”"]], [["“And you must not think that I am going to complain,”", "“그리고 내가 무슨 불평을 하려는 거라고 생각하면 안 돼요,”"], ["continued Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 이어 말했다."]], [["“I hope there is nothing to complain of,”", "“불평하실 일이 없기를 바랍니다,”"], ["said Fanny, speaking by no means in a defiant tone, but humbly as it were, and deprecating her ladyship’s wrath.", "패니가 말했다. 대드는 어조와는 조금도 거리가 멀었고, 오히려 겸허하게, 마님의 노여움을 미리 달래는 어조였다."], ["Fanny had gained one signal victory over Lady Lufton, and on that account, with a prudence equal to her generosity, felt that she could afford to be submissive.", "패니는 레이디 러프턴을 상대로 한 번 뚜렷한 승리를 거둔 바 있었으므로, 그 너그러움에 못지않은 신중함으로, 자기가 고분고분해도 될 만하다고 느꼈다."], ["It might, perhaps, not be long before she would be equally anxious to conquer again.", "어쩌면 다시 한번 이겨야 하겠다고 애태울 날이 머지않을지도 몰랐다."]], [["“Well, no; I don’t think there is,”", "“아니, 그럼요. 없다고 생각해요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“Nothing to complain of; but a little chat between you and me may, perhaps, set matters right, which, otherwise, might become troublesome.”", "“불평할 일은 없어요. 그렇지만 우리 둘이 조금 이야기를 나눠 두면, 그러지 않았을 때 골칫거리가 될 수도 있는 일들을 바로잡을 수 있을지도 모르지요.”"]], [["“Is it about Lucy?”", "“루시 이야기입니까?”"]], [["“Yes, my dear—about Lucy.", "“그래요, 얘야 — 루시 이야기예요."], ["She is a very nice, good girl, and a credit to her father—”", "참 착하고 좋은 아이이고, 아버지에게 자랑이 될 아이지요 —”"]], [["“And a great comfort to us,”", "“그리고 저희에게는 큰 위안입니다,”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."]], [["“I am sure she is: she must be a very pleasant companion to you, and so useful about the children; but—” And then Lady Lufton paused for a moment; for she, eloquent and discreet as she always was, felt herself rather at a loss for words to express her exact meaning.", "“그렇겠지요. 아주 즐거운 벗일 테고, 아이들 일에도 그렇게 도움이 되겠지요. 그런데 —” 그러고서 레이디 러프턴은 잠시 말을 멈추었다. 늘 그렇듯 능변이고 조심스러운 그녀였으나, 자기가 뜻하는 바를 정확히 나타낼 말이 얼른 떠오르지 않았던 것이다."]], [["“I don’t know what I should do without her,”", "“그 아이가 없으면 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠습니다,”"], ["said Fanny, speaking with the object of assisting her ladyship in her embarrassment.", "패니가 말했다. 난처해하는 마님을 도와줄 셈으로 한 말이었다."]], [["“But the truth is this: she and Lord Lufton are getting into the way of being too much together—of talking to each other too exclusively.", "“그런데 사실은 이렇답니다. 그 아이와 러프턴 경이 너무 자주 함께 있는 버릇이 들고 있어요 — 서로만 붙들고 이야기하는 버릇이요."], ["I am sure you must have noticed it, Fanny.", "패니도 분명 눈치를 챘을 거예요."], ["It is not that I suspect any evil.", "내가 무슨 나쁜 일을 의심한다는 게 아니에요."], ["I don’t think that I am suspicious by nature.”", "나는 천성이 의심 많은 사람은 아니라고 생각해요.”"]], [["“Oh! no,”", "“아!"], ["said Fanny.", "그러실 리가요,” 패니가 말했다."]], [["“But they will each of them get wrong ideas about the other, and about themselves.", "“그렇지만 두 사람은 각기 상대에 대해서도, 자기 자신에 대해서도 잘못된 생각을 품게 될 거예요. 루시는 어쩌면 루도빅이 실제보다 더 깊은 뜻을 두고 있다고 여기게 되겠지요."], ["Lucy will, perhaps, think that Ludovic means more than he does, and Ludovic will—”", "그리고 루도빅은 —” 그러나 루도빅이 무엇을 할지, 무엇을 생각할지를 말하기는 그렇게 쉽지 않았다."], ["But it was not quite so easy to say what Ludovic might do or think; but Lady Lufton went on: “I am sure that you understand me, Fanny, with your excellent sense and tact.", "그래도 레이디 러프턴은 말을 이었다. “패니는 그 훌륭한 분별과 재치로 내 말을 알아들을 거예요."], ["Lucy is clever, and amusing, and all that; and Ludovic, like all young men, is perhaps ignorant that his attentions may be taken to mean more than he intends—”", "루시는 똑똑하고, 재미있고, 그런 아이지요. 그리고 루도빅은 젊은 남자들이 다 그렇듯이, 자기가 보이는 관심이 자기가 뜻한 것보다 더 깊은 것으로 받아들여질 수 있다는 걸 모르는지도 몰라요 —”"]], [["“You don’t think that Lucy is in love with him?”", "“루시가 러프턴 경을 사랑하고 있다고 여기시는 것은 아니시겠지요?”"]], [["“Oh dear, no—nothing of the kind.", "“아이고, 아니에요 — 그런 게 아니에요."], ["If I thought it had come to that, I should recommend that she should be sent away altogether.", "일이 그렇게까지 되었다고 생각한다면 나는 그 아이를 아주 내보내라고 권했을 거예요."], ["I am sure she is not so foolish as that.”", "그 아이가 그만큼 어리석지는 않다고 믿어요.”"]], [["“I don’t think there is anything in it at all, Lady Lufton.”", "“저는 거기에 아무것도 없다고 생각합니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“I don’t think there is, my dear, and therefore I would not for worlds make any suggestion about it to Lord Lufton.", "“없다고 나도 생각해요, 얘야. 그러니 이 일에 관해서 러프턴 경에게 무슨 말을 꺼내는 짓은 무슨 일이 있어도 하지 않을 거예요."], ["I would not let him suppose that I suspected Lucy of being so imprudent.", "내가 루시를 그렇게 분별없는 아이로 의심한다고 그가 여기게 할 수는 없어요."], ["But still, it may be well that you should just say a word to her.", "그렇지만 그래도, 패니가 그 아이에게 한마디쯤 해 두는 것은 좋은 일일 거예요."], ["A little management now and then, in such matters, is so useful.”", "그런 일에는 이따금 조금씩 손을 써 두는 것이 아주 쓸모가 있지요.”"]], [["“But what shall I say to her?”", "“그런데 그 아이에게 무슨 말을 해야 하겠습니까?”"]], [["“Just explain to her that any young lady who talks so much to the same young gentleman will certainly be observed—that people will accuse her of setting her cap at Lord Lufton.", "“같은 젊은 신사와 그렇게 많이 이야기하는 아가씨는 반드시 남의 눈에 띈다는 것 — 사람들이 그 아이가 러프턴 경을 노린다고 흉을 잡을 거라는 것만 일러 주면 돼요. 내가 그 아이를 의심한다는 게 아니에요."], ["Not that I suspect her—I give her credit for too much proper feeling: I know her education has been good, and her principles are upright.", "나는 그 아이에게 온당한 마음가짐이 넉넉하다고 믿어요. 좋은 교육을 받았고 품성이 곧다는 것도 알아요."], ["But people will talk of her.", "그러나 사람들은 그 아이 이야기를 할 거예요."], ["You must understand that, Fanny, as well as I do.”", "패니도 나만큼 그것을 알고 있을 거예요.”"]], [["Fanny could not help meditating whether proper feeling, education, and upright principles did forbid Lucy Robarts to fall in love with Lord Lufton; but her doubts on this subject, if she held any, were not communicated to her ladyship.", "온당한 마음가짐과 교육과 곧은 품성이 정말로 루시 로바츠에게 러프턴 경을 사랑하는 일을 금하는 것인지, 패니는 생각해 보지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 이 문제에 관한 의심이 그녀에게 있었다 해도 그것이 마님에게 전해지지는 않았다."], ["It had never entered into her mind that a match was possible between Lord Lufton and Lucy Robarts, nor had she the slightest wish to encourage it now that the idea was suggested to her.", "러프턴 경과 루시 로바츠 사이에 혼사가 가능하다는 생각은 그녀의 머리에 한 번도 들어온 적이 없었고, 이제 그 생각이 들이밀어졌다 해서 그것을 거들어 주고 싶은 마음도 조금도 없었다."], ["On such a matter she could sympathize with Lady Lufton, though she did not completely agree with her as to the expediency of any interference.", "그런 문제에 관해서라면 그녀도 레이디 러프턴과 마음을 같이할 수 있었다. 다만 무슨 간섭을 하는 것이 온당하냐 하는 데에는 완전히 뜻을 같이하지 않았다."], ["Nevertheless, she at once offered to speak to Lucy.", "그럼에도 그녀는 곧 루시에게 말해 보겠다고 나섰다."]], [["“I don’t think that Lucy has any idea in her head upon the subject,”", "“루시는 그 일에 관해서 머릿속에 아무 생각도 없을 것입니다,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I dare say not—I don’t suppose she has.", "“그렇겠지요 — 없을 거라고 나도 짐작해요."], ["But young ladies sometimes allow themselves to fall in love, and then to think themselves very ill-used, just because they have had no idea in their head.”", "그런데 아가씨들은 머릿속에 아무 생각도 없었다는 바로 그 까닭으로, 이따금 저도 모르게 사랑에 빠져 버리고, 그러고는 자기가 몹시 부당한 대접을 받았다고 여기게 되지요.”"]], [["“I will put her on her guard if you wish it, Lady Lufton.”", "“원하신다면 그 아이에게 조심하라고 일러 두겠습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“Exactly, my dear; that is just it.", "“그래요, 얘야. 바로 그거예요."], ["Put her on her guard—that is all that is necessary.", "조심하라고 일러 두는 것 — 그것으로 다 됐어요."], ["She is a dear, good, clever girl, and it would be very sad if anything were to interrupt our comfortable way of getting on with her.”", "사랑스럽고 착하고 똑똑한 아이인데, 무슨 일로 그 아이와 편안히 지내 온 이 모양새가 끊긴다면 참 서운한 일이겠지요.”"]], [["Mrs. Robarts knew to a nicety the exact meaning of this threat.", "로바츠 부인은 이 위협의 정확한 뜻을 털끝만큼도 틀리지 않게 알아들었다."], ["If Lucy would persist in securing to herself so much of Lord Lufton’s time and attention, her visits to Framley Court must become less frequent.", "루시가 러프턴 경의 시간과 관심을 그렇게 많이 자기 몫으로 붙들어 두기를 그치지 않는다면, 그 아이가 프램리 코트에 올라오는 일이 드물어져야 한다는 것이었다."], ["Lady Lufton would do much, very much, indeed, for her friends at the parsonage; but not even for them could she permit her son’s prospects in life to be endangered.", "레이디 러프턴은 목사관의 벗들을 위하여 많은 일을, 참으로 많은 일을 해 줄 사람이었다. 그러나 그들을 위해서라도 아들의 앞날이 위태로워지는 것을 허락할 수는 없었다."]], [["There was nothing more said between them, and Mrs. Robarts got up to take her leave, having promised to speak to Lucy.", "두 사람 사이에 더 오간 말은 없었고, 로바츠 부인은 루시에게 말하겠다고 약속한 뒤 물러가려고 일어섰다."]], [["“You manage everything so perfectly,”", "“패니는 무슨 일이든 그렇게 완벽하게 처리하니,”"], ["said Lady Lufton, as she pressed Mrs.", "레이디 러프턴이 로바츠 부인의 손을 꼭 잡으며 말했다."], ["Robarts’ hand, “that I am quite at ease now that I find you will agree with me.” Mrs.", "“나와 뜻이 같다는 것을 알고 나니 이제 아주 마음이 놓여요.”"], ["Robarts did not exactly agree with her ladyship, but she hardly thought it worth her while to say so.", "로바츠 부인은 마님과 뜻이 딱 같지는 않았으나, 그렇다고 말할 만한 값이 있다고는 좀처럼 여기지 않았다."]], [["Mrs. Robarts immediately started off on her walk to her own home, and when she had got out of the grounds into the road, where it makes a turn towards the parsonage, nearly opposite to Podgens’ shop, she saw Lord Lufton on horseback, and Lucy standing beside him.", "로바츠 부인은 곧바로 걸어서 제 집으로 향했다."], ["It was already nearly five o’clock, and it was getting dusk; but as she approached, or rather as she came suddenly within sight of them, she could see that they were in close conversation.", "그리고 저택의 정원을 벗어나 길로 나와, 포진스네 가게 거의 맞은편에서 길이 목사관 쪽으로 굽어지는 그곳에 이르렀을 때, 말을 탄 러프턴 경과 그 옆에 서 있는 루시를 보았다. 벌써 다섯 시가 가까웠고 어스름이 내리고 있었다."], ["Lord Lufton’s face was towards her, and his horse was standing still; he was leaning over towards his companion, and the whip, which he held in his right hand, hung almost over her arm and down her back, as though his hand had touched and perhaps rested on her shoulder.", "그러나 다가가면서, 아니 그보다는 불현듯 두 사람이 보이는 데에 이르면서, 그녀는 두 사람이 바싹 붙어 이야기하고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 러프턴 경의 얼굴은 그녀 쪽을 향해 있었고 말은 가만히 서 있었다."], ["She was standing by his side, looking up into his face, with one gloved hand resting on the horse’s neck.", "그는 곁에 선 이쪽으로 몸을 굽히고 있었고, 오른손에 든 채찍은 마치 그의 손이 그 아이의 어깨에 닿았다가 어쩌면 그대로 얹혀 있기라도 한 듯이 그 아이의 팔 위로, 등 아래로 거의 드리워져 있었다. 그 아이는 그의 옆에 서서 그의 얼굴을 올려다보고 있었고, 장갑 낀 한 손은 말의 목에 얹혀 있었다."], ["Mrs. Robarts, as she saw them, could not but own that there might be cause for Lady Lufton’s fears.", "두 사람을 본 로바츠 부인은 레이디 러프턴의 두려움에 까닭이 있을 수도 있다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["But then Lucy’s manner, as Mrs. Robarts approached, was calculated to dissipate any such fears, and to prove that there was no ground for them.", "그러나 로바츠 부인이 다가올 때 루시가 보인 태도는 그런 두려움을 흩어 버리고 그것에 근거가 없다는 것을 증명하기에 알맞은 것이었다."], ["She did not move from her position, or allow her hand to drop, or show that she was in any way either confused or conscious.", "그녀는 서 있던 자리에서 움직이지도, 손을 내려놓지도, 무슨 당황한 낌새나 남의 눈을 의식하는 낌새를 보이지도 않았다."], ["She stood her ground, and when her sister-in-law came up was smiling and at her ease.", "그녀는 그 자리에 그대로 서 있었고, 올케가 다가왔을 때에는 웃고 있었으며 태연했다."]], [["“Lord Lufton wants me to learn to ride,”", "“러프턴 경께서 저더러 말 타는 법을 배우라고 하시네요,”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“To learn to ride!”", "“말 타는 법을 배우라고!”"], ["said Fanny, not knowing what answer to make to such a proposition.", "패니가 말했다. 그런 제안에 무슨 대답을 해야 할지 알 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇습니다,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he.", "“이 말이라면 아주 훌륭하게 태워 줄 거예요. 양처럼 순한 놈이고요."], ["“This horse would carry her beautifully: he is as quiet as a lamb, and I made Gregory go out with him yesterday with a sheet hanging over him like a lady’s habit, and the man got up into a lady’s saddle.”", "어제는 그레고리에게 부인용 승마복처럼 이 놈에게 천을 늘어뜨려 두르게 하고 끌고 나가라고 시켰지요. 그래서 그 사람이 부인용 안장에 올라앉았습니다.”"]], [["“I think Gregory would make a better hand of it than Lucy.”", "“그레고리가 루시보다 더 잘 해내겠군요.”"]], [["“The horse cantered with him as though he had carried a lady all his life, and his mouth is like velvet; indeed, that is his fault—he is too soft-mouthed.”", "“그 말은 평생 부인을 태우고 다닌 놈처럼 그를 태우고 캔터로 달렸어요. 그리고 입이 벨벳 같지요. 실은 그게 그 놈의 흠이에요 — 입이 너무 무릅니다.”"]], [["“I suppose that’s the same sort of thing as a man being soft-hearted,”", "“사람이 마음이 무른 것과 같은 종류의 일인 듯합니다,”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“Exactly: you ought to ride them both with a very light hand.", "“바로 그렇습니다. 둘 다 아주 가벼운 손으로 다루어야 해요."], ["They are difficult cattle to manage, but very pleasant when you know how to do it.”", "다루기 까다로운 짐승들이지만, 다루는 법을 알고 나면 아주 흡족하지요.”"]], [["“But you see I don’t know how to do it,”", "“그런데 보시다시피 저는 다루는 법을 모릅니다,”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“As regards the horse, you will learn in two days, and I do hope you will try.", "“말에 관해서라면 이틀이면 배울 겁니다. 그리고 정말 한번 해 보시기를 바랍니다."], ["Don’t you think it will be an excellent thing for her, Mrs. Robarts?”", "로바츠 양에게 아주 좋은 일이 되지 않겠어요, 로바츠 부인?”"]], [["“Lucy has got no habit,” said Mrs.", "“루시에게는 승마복이 없어요,”"], ["Robarts, making use of the excuse common on all such occasions.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다. 그런 자리에서 흔히 쓰는 핑계를 끌어댄 것이었다."]], [["“There is one of Justinia’s in the house, I know.", "“저희 집에 저스티니아의 승마복이 하나 있는 걸 압니다."], ["She always leaves one here, in order that she may be able to ride when she comes.”", "올 때 말을 탈 수 있도록 늘 여기에 하나 두고 가지요.”"]], [["“She would not think of taking such a liberty with Lady Meredith’s things,”", "“레이디 메러디스의 물건을 그렇게 마음대로 쓰는 무례는 이 아이가 꿈에도 생각하지 못할 거예요,”"], ["said Fanny, almost frightened at the proposal.", "패니가 말했다. 그 제안에 거의 겁을 먹은 듯했다."]], [["“Of course it is out of the question, Fanny,” said Lucy, now speaking rather seriously. “In the first place, I would not take Lord Lufton’s horse; in the second place, I would not take Lady Meredith’s habit; in the third place, I should be a great deal too much frightened; and, lastly, it is quite out of the question for a great many other very good reasons.”", "“물론 있을 수 없는 일이에요, 패니,” 루시가 말했다. 이제는 다소 진지한 어조였다. “첫째로, 저는 러프턴 경의 말을 받지 않겠어요. 둘째로, 레이디 메러디스의 승마복을 받지 않겠어요. 셋째로, 저는 너무나 많이 무서울 거예요. 그리고 끝으로, 그 밖의 아주 훌륭한 여러 까닭으로 그것은 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이에요.”"]], [["“Nonsense,”", "“터무니없는 소리,”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."]], [["“A great deal of nonsense,”", "“터무니없는 소리가 아주 많습니다,”"], ["said Lucy, laughing, “but all of it of Lord Lufton’s talking.", "루시가 웃으며 말했다. “그런데 그게 다 러프턴 경께서 하신 소리지요."], ["But we are getting cold—are we not, Fanny?—so we will wish you good-night.”", "그런데 우리 춥잖아요 — 그렇지요, 패니? — 그러니 안녕히 계시라고 인사드리겠습니다.”"], ["And then the two ladies shook hands with him, and walked on towards the parsonage.", "그러고서 두 사람은 그와 악수를 하고 목사관을 향해 걸어갔다."]], [["That which astonished Mrs. Robarts the most in all this was the perfectly collected manner in which Lucy spoke and conducted herself.", "이 모든 일에서 로바츠 부인을 가장 놀라게 한 것은 루시가 말하고 처신하는 태도가 완전히 침착했다는 점이었다."], ["This connected, as she could not but connect it, with the air of chagrin with which Lord Lufton received Lucy’s decision, made it manifest to Mrs.", "이것을, 루시의 결정을 받아들이는 러프턴 경의 낙담한 낯빛과 이어 놓고 보지 않을 수 없었으니, 로바츠 부인에게는 러프턴 경이 루시가 말 타는 법을 배우겠다고 승낙하지 않아서 속이 상해 있다는 것이 분명해졌다."], ["Robarts that Lord Lufton was annoyed because Lucy would not consent to learn to ride; whereas she, Lucy herself, had given her refusal in a firm and decided tone, as though resolved that nothing more should be said about it.", "그런데 루시 자신은, 그 일에 관해서 더는 말이 나오지 않게 하겠다고 작심한 듯이 단단하고 딱 잘린 어조로 거절을 내놓은 것이었다."]], [["They walked on in silence for a minute or two, till they reached the parsonage gate, and then Lucy said, laughing, “Can’t you fancy me sitting on that great big horse?", "두 사람은 목사관 문에 이르기까지 한두 순간 말없이 걸었다. 그러다가 루시가 웃으며 말했다."], ["I wonder what Lady Lufton would say if she saw me there, and his lordship giving me my first lesson?”", "“제가 그 커다란 말 위에 올라앉은 걸 상상할 수 있어요? 레이디 러프턴께서, 제가 그 위에 앉아 있고 그 나리께서 첫 수업을 해 주시는 것을 보셨다면 무슨 말씀을 하셨을까요?”"]], [["“I don’t think she would like it,”", "“좋아하지 않으실 것 같아요,”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."]], [["“I’m sure she would not.", "“좋아하지 않으실 게 분명해요."], ["But I will not try her temper in that respect.", "그렇지만 그 점으로 그분의 심기를 건드릴 생각은 없어요."], ["Sometimes I fancy that she does not even like seeing Lord Lufton talking to me.”", "러프턴 경이 저와 이야기하는 것을 보시는 것조차 그분은 좋아하지 않으신다는 생각이 이따금 들어요.”"]], [["“She does not like it, Lucy, when she sees him flirting with you.”", "“좋아하지 않으시지요, 루시. 그분이 루시하고 희롱하는 걸 보시면요.”"]], [["This Mrs. Robarts said rather gravely, whereas Lucy had been speaking in a half-bantering tone.", "로바츠 부인은 이 말을 다소 무겁게 했다."], ["As soon as even the word flirting was out of Fanny’s mouth, she was conscious that she had been guilty of an injustice in using it.", "루시는 반쯤 놀리는 어조로 말하고 있었는데 말이다. 희롱이라는 말이 입에서 나오자마자 패니는 그 말을 쓴 것이 부당한 짓이었음을 깨달았다."], ["She had wished to say something which would convey to her sister-in-law an idea of what Lady Lufton would dislike; but in doing so, she had unintentionally brought against her an accusation.", "그녀는 레이디 러프턴이 무엇을 싫어할지를 시누이에게 전해 줄 무슨 말을 하고 싶었을 뿐이었다. 그런데 그렇게 하는 가운데 뜻하지 않게 그 아이에게 죄를 씌운 것이었다."]], [["“Flirting, Fanny!”", "“희롱이라니요, 패니!”"], ["said Lucy, standing still in the path, and looking up into her companion’s face with all her eyes.", "루시가 길에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 온 눈으로 옆 사람의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다."], ["“Do you mean to say that I have been flirting with Lord Lufton?”", "“제가 러프턴 경과 희롱을 했다는 말인가요?”"]], [["“I did not say that.”", "“그런 말이 아니에요.”"]], [["“Or that I have allowed him to flirt with me?”", "“아니면 제가 그분이 저를 희롱하도록 내버려 두었다는 말인가요?”"]], [["“I did not mean to shock you, Lucy.”", "“놀라게 하려던 건 아니었어요, 루시.”"]], [["“What did you mean, Fanny?”", "“그러면 무슨 뜻이었어요, 패니?”"]], [["“Why, just this: that Lady Lufton would not be pleased if he paid you marked attentions, and if you received them;—just like that affair of the riding; it was better to decline it.”", "“그저 이런 뜻이에요. 그분이 루시에게 눈에 띄게 관심을 쏟고 루시가 그것을 받아들이면 레이디 러프턴께서 좋아하지 않으실 거라는 뜻이지요. 저 승마 이야기와 똑같아요. 거절하는 편이 나았던 거예요.”"]], [["“Of course I declined it; of course I never dreamt of accepting such an offer.", "“물론 저는 거절했어요. 물론 그런 제안을 받아들이는 건 꿈에도 생각하지 않았어요."], ["Go riding about the country on his horses!", "그분의 말을 타고 시골을 돌아다니다니요!"], ["What have I done, Fanny, that you should suppose such a thing?”", "제가 무슨 짓을 했다고 패니가 그런 생각을 해요?”"]], [["“You have done nothing, dearest.”", "“아무 짓도 하지 않았어요, 우리 루시.”"]], [["“Then why did you speak as you did just now?”", "“그러면 왜 아까 그렇게 말했어요?”"]], [["“Because I wished to put you on your guard.", "“조심하라고 일러 주고 싶었기 때문이에요."], ["You know, Lucy, that I do not intend to find fault with you; but you may be sure, as a rule, that intimate friendships between young gentlemen and young ladies are dangerous things.”", "루시도 알잖아요, 루시를 흠잡을 뜻은 없어요. 그렇지만 대체로, 젊은 신사와 젊은 아가씨 사이의 가까운 우정은 위험한 것이라고 믿어도 좋아요.”"]], [["They then walked up to the hall-door in silence.", "그러고서 두 사람은 말없이 현관문까지 걸어 올라갔다."], ["When they had reached it, Lucy stood in the doorway instead of entering it, and said, “Fanny, let us take another turn together, if you are not tired.”", "문에 이르자 루시는 안으로 들어서는 대신 문간에 선 채로 말했다. “패니, 피곤하지 않으면 우리 한 바퀴 더 걸어요.”"]], [["“No, I’m not tired.”", "“아니, 피곤하지 않아요.”"]], [["“It will be better that I should understand you at once,”—and then they again moved away from the house.", "“패니 뜻을 지금 당장 알아 두는 게 좋겠어요.” 그러고서 두 사람은 다시 집에서 멀어져 갔다."], ["“Tell me truly now, do you think that Lord Lufton and I have been flirting?”", "“이제 사실대로 말해 주세요. 러프턴 경과 제가 희롱을 하고 있었다고 생각해요?”"]], [["“I do think that he is a little inclined to flirt with you.”", "“그분이 루시를 조금 희롱하려는 기색이 있다고는 생각해요.”"]], [["“And Lady Lufton has been asking you to lecture me about it?”", "“그래서 레이디 러프턴께서 그 일로 저를 훈계하라고 부탁하신 거예요?”"]], [["Poor Mrs. Robarts hardly knew what to say.", "가엾은 로바츠 부인은 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["She thought well of all the persons concerned, and was very anxious to behave well by all of them;—was particularly anxious to create no ill feeling, and wished that everybody should be comfortable, and on good terms with everybody else.", "그녀는 관련된 모든 사람을 좋게 여겼고 그들 모두에게 잘 처신하고 싶어 애가 탔다. 특히 나쁜 감정이 생기지 않기를 애타게 바랐고, 누구나 마음 편하고 서로 사이가 좋기를 바랐다."], ["But yet the truth was forced out of her when this question was asked so suddenly.", "그러나 이 물음이 이렇게 불현듯 날아들었을 때 진실은 그녀의 입에서 밀려 나오고 말았다."]], [["“Not to lecture you, Lucy,”", "“훈계하라고는 하지 않으셨어요, 루시,”"], ["she said at last.", "마침내 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Well, to preach to me, or to talk to me, or to give me a lesson; to say something that shall drive me to put my back up against Lord Lufton?”", "“그럼 저에게 설교를 하라거나, 이야기를 하라거나, 한 수 가르치라거나요. 러프턴 경에게 등을 돌리게 만들 무슨 말을 하라고요?”"]], [["“To caution you, dearest.", "“조심하라고 일러 주라고요, 우리 루시."], ["Had you heard what she said, you would hardly have felt angry with Lady Lufton.”", "그분이 하신 말씀을 직접 들었다면 레이디 러프턴께 좀처럼 화가 나지 않았을 거예요.”"]], [["“Well, to caution me.", "“그럼, 조심하라고요."], ["It is such a pleasant thing for a girl to be cautioned against falling in love with a gentleman, especially when the gentleman is very rich, and a lord, and all that sort of thing!”", "아가씨가 어떤 신사와 사랑에 빠지지 말라고 주의를 받는 건 참 유쾌한 일이지요. 더구나 그 신사가 아주 부자이고, 경이고, 그런 온갖 것을 다 갖추었을 때에는요!”"]], [["“Nobody for a moment attributes anything wrong to you, Lucy.”", "“아무도 한순간이라도 루시에게 잘못이 있다고 하지 않아요, 루시.”"]], [["“Anything wrong—no.", "“잘못이라 — 그래요, 없지요."], ["I don’t know whether it would be anything wrong, even if I were to fall in love with him.", "제가 그분과 사랑에 빠진다 해도 그것이 잘못일지 어떨지는 저도 모르겠어요."], ["I wonder whether they cautioned Griselda Grantly when she was here?", "그리젤다 그랜틀리가 여기 있을 때에도 사람들이 그 아가씨에게 조심하라고 했을까요?"], ["I suppose when young lords go about, all the girls are cautioned as a matter of course.", "젊은 경들이 돌아다닐 때에는 아가씨들이 다 으레 주의를 받는 모양이지요."], ["Why do they not label him ‘dangerous’?”", "왜 그분에게 ‘위험’이라고 딱지를 붙여 두지 않는 걸까요?”"], ["And then again they were silent for a moment, as Mrs.", "그러고서 두 사람은 또 잠시 말이 없었다."], ["Robarts did not feel that she had anything further to say on the matter.", "로바츠 부인은 이 일에 관하여 더 할 말이 있다고 느끼지 못했던 것이다."]], [["“‘Poison’ should be the word with any one so fatal as Lord Lufton; and he ought to be made up of some particular colour, for fear he should be swallowed in mistake.”", "“러프턴 경처럼 그렇게 치명적인 사람이라면 ‘독약’이라고 적어야 마땅하지요. 그리고 잘못 삼키는 일이 없도록 눈에 확 띄는 색으로 조제해 두어야 하고요.”"]], [["“You will be safe, you see,”", "“보다시피 루시는 안전하겠네요,”"], ["said Fanny, laughing, “as you have been specially cautioned as to this individual bottle.”", "패니가 웃으며 말했다. “바로 이 병 하나에 관해서는 특별히 주의를 받았으니까요.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but what’s the use of that after I have had so many doses?", "그렇지만 이미 그렇게 여러 번 복용한 뒤에 그게 무슨 쓸모가 있어요?"], ["It is no good telling me about it now, when the mischief is done,—after I have been taking it for I don’t know how long.", "이미 화를 다 입은 지금에 와서, 얼마나 오래 그것을 먹어 왔는지도 모를 지금에 와서 말해 주는 건 아무 도움이 안 되지요."], ["Dear!", "아이고!"], ["dear! dear!", "아이고!"], ["and I regarded it as a mere commonplace powder, good for the complexion.", "아이고! 그런데 저는 그것을 안색에 좋은 흔해빠진 가루약 정도로 여겼답니다."], ["I wonder whether it’s too late, or whether there’s any antidote?”", "너무 늦었는지, 아니면 해독제라도 있는지 궁금해요.”"]], [["Mrs. Robarts did not always quite understand her sister-in-law, and now she was a little at a loss.", "로바츠 부인은 시누이의 말을 늘 다 알아듣지는 못했고, 지금도 조금 막막했다."], ["“I don’t think there’s much harm done yet on either side,”", "“아직 어느 쪽에도 큰 화는 없다고 생각해요,”"], ["she said, cheerily.", "그녀가 밝게 말했다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["you don’t know, Fanny.", "패니는 모르잖아요."], ["But I do think that if I die—as I shall—I feel I shall;—and if so, I do think it ought to go very hard with Lady Lufton.", "그렇지만 제가 죽는다면 — 저는 죽을 거예요, 죽을 것 같은 느낌이 들어요 — 그렇게 되면 레이디 러프턴께서 아주 호되게 당하셔야 마땅하다고 생각해요."], ["Why didn’t she label him ‘dangerous’ in time?”", "왜 제때에 그분에게 ‘위험’이라고 딱지를 붙여 두지 않으셨을까요?”"], ["And then they went into the house and up to their own rooms.", "그러고서 두 사람은 집으로 들어가 각자의 방으로 올라갔다."]], [["It was difficult for any one to understand Lucy’s state of mind at present, and it can hardly be said that she understood it herself.", "지금 루시의 마음 상태를 이해하기는 누구에게든 어려운 일이었고, 그녀 자신이 그것을 이해했다고도 좀처럼 말할 수 없었다."], ["She felt that she had received a severe blow in having been thus made the subject of remark with reference to Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경과 관련해서 이렇게 남의 입에 오르는 처지가 된 것이 그녀에게는 호된 타격으로 느껴졌다."], ["She knew that her pleasant evenings at Framley Court were now over, and that she could not again talk to him in an unrestrained tone and without embarrassment.", "프램리 코트에서 보내던 즐거운 저녁들이 이제 끝났다는 것을, 그리고 다시는 거리낌 없는 어조로, 어색함 없이 그와 이야기할 수 없다는 것을 그녀는 알았다."], ["She had felt the air of the whole place to be very cold before her intimacy with him, and now it must be cold again.", "그와 친해지기 전에는 그곳의 공기가 온통 아주 차갑게 느껴졌는데, 이제 다시 차가워질 수밖에 없었다. 그녀에게는 두 집이 열려 있었다."], ["Two homes had been open to her, Framley Court and the parsonage; and now, as far as comfort was concerned, she must confine herself to the latter.", "프램리 코트와 목사관이었다. 그런데 이제 마음 편함을 두고 말하자면 그녀는 뒤의 것에만 머물러야 했다."], ["She could not again be comfortable in Lady Lufton’s drawing-room.", "레이디 러프턴의 응접실에서 그녀는 다시 마음이 편할 수 없었다."]], [["But then she could not help asking herself whether Lady Lufton was not right.", "그러나 그러면서도 그녀는 레이디 러프턴이 옳지 않은가 하고 스스로에게 묻지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She had had courage enough, and presence of mind, to joke about the matter when her sister-in-law spoke to her, and yet she was quite aware that it was no joking matter.", "올케가 말을 걸어왔을 때 그 일을 두고 농을 할 만한 용기와 침착함은 있었으나, 그것이 결코 농담거리가 아니라는 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Lord Lufton had not absolutely made love to her, but he had latterly spoken to her in a manner which she knew was not compatible with that ordinary comfortable masculine friendship with the idea of which she had once satisfied herself.", "러프턴 경이 딱 잘라 그녀에게 구애를 한 것은 아니었다. 그러나 요즈음 그가 그녀에게 말하는 태도는, 언젠가 그녀가 스스로를 달래며 만족해했던 그 예사롭고 마음 편한 사내 벗의 우정이라는 생각과는 어울리지 않는다는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다."], ["Was not Fanny right when she said that intimate friendships of that nature were dangerous things?", "그런 성질의 가까운 우정은 위험한 것이라고 패니가 말했을 때, 패니는 옳지 않았던가?"]], [["Yes, Lucy, very dangerous.", "그렇다, 루시야, 아주 위험한 것이다."], ["Lucy, before she went to bed that night, had owned to herself that they were so; and lying there with sleepless eyes and a moist pillow, she was driven to confess that the label would in truth be now too late, that the caution had come to her after the poison had been swallowed.", "루시는 그날 밤 잠자리에 들기 전에 그것이 그렇다는 것을 스스로에게 인정했다. 그리고 잠들지 못하는 눈과 젖은 베개를 안고 누워, 이제 그 딱지가 사실 너무 늦었다는 것을, 독약을 이미 삼킨 뒤에야 주의가 그녀에게 왔다는 것을 고백하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Was there any antidote?", "해독제가 있는가?"], ["That was all that was left for her to consider.", "그것만이 그녀가 헤아려 볼 거리로 남아 있었다."], ["But, nevertheless, on the following morning she could appear quite at her ease.", "그러나 그럼에도 이튿날 아침 그녀는 아주 태연한 낯으로 나타날 수 있었다."], ["And when Mark had left the house after breakfast, she could still joke with Fanny as to Lady Lufton’s poison cupboard.", "그리고 아침 식사 뒤에 마크가 집을 나선 다음, 그녀는 여전히 레이디 러프턴의 독약 장을 두고 패니와 농을 할 수 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIV. MR. CRAWLEY OF HOGGLESTOCK.", "# 제14장. 호글스톡의 크롤리 씨"]], [["And then there was that other trouble in Lady Lufton’s mind, the sins, namely, of her selected parson.", "그리고 레이디 러프턴의 마음에는 또 다른 근심이 있었다. 다름 아니라 그녀가 골라 앉힌 목사의 죄과였다."], ["She had selected him, and she was by no means inclined to give him up, even though his sins against parsondom were grievous.", "그를 고른 것은 그녀였고, 목사의 도리를 거스른 그의 죄가 아무리 무겁다 해도 그녀는 결코 그를 놓을 마음이 없었다."], ["Indeed she was a woman not prone to give up anything, and of all things not prone to give up a protégé.", "실로 그녀는 무엇이든 놓아 버리는 데 익숙한 여자가 아니었고, 그 무엇보다도 자기가 후원하는 사람을 놓아 버리는 데는 더욱 익숙하지 않았다."], ["The very fact that she herself had selected him was the strongest argument in his favour.", "그를 고른 사람이 바로 자기 자신이라는 사실이야말로 그에게 유리한 가장 강력한 논거였다."]], [["But his sins against parsondom were becoming very grievous in her eyes, and she was at a loss to know what steps to take.", "그러나 목사의 도리를 거스른 그의 죄는 그녀의 눈에 점점 심각해져 갔고, 그녀는 어떤 조치를 취해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못했다."], ["She hardly dared to take him to task, him himself.", "그를, 바로 그 사람 본인을 붙잡고 따진다는 것은 엄두가 나지 않았다."], ["Were she to do so, and should he then tell her to mind her own business—as he probably might do, though not in those words—there would be a schism in the parish; and almost anything would be better than that.", "만약 그렇게 했다가 그가 남의 일에 참견하지 말라고 — 그런 말 그대로는 아니겠지만 아마 그런 뜻으로 — 대답한다면 교구에 분열이 생길 것이었다. 그리고 그것보다는 무슨 일이라도 나았다."], ["The whole work of her life would be upset, all the outlets of her energy would be impeded if not absolutely closed, if a state of things were to come to pass in which she and the parson of her parish should not be on good terms.", "그녀와 자기 교구의 목사가 서로 좋게 지내지 못하는 사태가 벌어진다면, 그녀 일생의 사업 전체가 뒤집히고 그녀의 활력이 흘러 나가는 모든 출구가 아주 막히지는 않더라도 막힘을 당할 터였다."]], [["But what was to be done?", "그러나 어찌해야 하는가?"], ["Early in the winter he had gone to Chaldicotes and to Gatherum Castle, consorting with gamblers, Whigs, atheists, men of loose pleasure, and Proudieites.", "겨울 초에 그는 콜디코츠와 개서럼 성에 가서 도박꾼, 휘그, 무신론자, 방탕한 향락꾼, 그리고 프라우디 파 사람들과 어울렸다. 그것은 그녀가 눈감아 주었다."], ["That she had condoned; and now he was turning out a hunting parson on her hands.", "그런데 이제 그는 그녀가 보는 앞에서 사냥하는 목사가 되어 가고 있었다."], ["It was all very well for Fanny to say that he merely looked at the hounds as he rode about his parish.", "그가 교구를 돌아다니다가 사냥개들을 그저 구경한 것뿐이라고 패니가 말하는 것은 그럴 수 있는 일이었다."], ["Fanny might be deceived.", "패니는 속을 수도 있었다."], ["Being his wife, it might be her duty not to see her husband’s iniquities.", "그의 아내이니, 남편의 죄악을 보지 않는 것이 그녀의 의무일 수도 있었다."], ["But Lady Lufton could not be deceived.", "그러나 레이디 러프턴은 속을 수 없었다."], ["She knew very well in what part of the county Cobbold’s Ashes lay.", "코볼즈 애시스가 주의 어느 편에 있는지 그녀는 아주 잘 알고 있었다."], ["It was not in Framley parish, nor in the next parish to it.", "그곳은 프램리 교구도 아니고 그 옆 교구도 아니었다."], ["It was half-way across to Chaldicotes—in the western division; and she had heard of that run in which two horses had been killed, and in which Parson Robarts had won such immortal glory among West Barsetshire sportsmen.", "콜디코츠까지 가는 길의 절반쯤 — 주의 서부 지구에 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 말 두 마리가 죽어 나간 그 몰이 이야기를, 그 몰이에서 로바츠 목사가 서바셋주 수렵꾼들 사이에 불멸의 영광을 얻었다는 이야기를 들어 알고 있었다."], ["It was not easy to keep Lady Lufton in the dark as to matters occurring in her own county.", "자기 주 안에서 일어나는 일을 레이디 러프턴에게 감춰 두기란 쉽지 않았다."]], [["All these things she knew, but as yet had not noticed, grieving over them in her own heart the more on that account.", "이 모든 일을 그녀는 알고 있었으나 아직 아무 말도 꺼내지 않았고, 그런 까닭에 마음속으로 더욱 애를 태웠다."], ["Spoken grief relieves itself; and when one can give counsel, one always hopes at least that that counsel will be effective.", "입으로 나온 슬픔은 스스로 덜어지는 법이요, 충고를 할 수 있을 때에는 적어도 그 충고가 효험이 있으리라는 희망이라도 품게 되는 법이다."], ["To her son she had said, more than once, that it was a pity that Mr.", "아들에게는 로바츠 씨가 사냥개를 따라다니는 것이 안타까운 일이라고 여러 번 말한 바 있었다."], ["Robarts should follow the hounds.—“The world has agreed that it is unbecoming in a clergyman,”", "— “성직자에게 어울리지 않는 일이라고 세상이 다 그러잖니.” 그녀는 못마땅하다는 어조로 이렇게 다그쳤다."], ["she would urge, in her deprecatory tone.", "그러나 아들은 그녀에게 아무런 위로도 주지 않았다."], ["But her son would by no means give her any comfort.", "“그는 사냥을 하는 게 아니에요, 어머니."], ["“He doesn’t hunt, you know—not as I do,”", "저처럼 하는 건 아니지요.” 그는 이렇게 말했다."], ["he would say. “And if he did, I really don’t see the harm of it.", "“설령 한다 해도 무슨 해가 되는지 저는 정말 모르겠어요."], ["A man must have some amusement, even if he be an archbishop.”", "사람이란 대주교라 해도 소일거리 하나쯤은 있어야 하는 법이지요.”"], ["“He has amusement at home,”", "“소일거리는 집에 있잖니.”"], ["Lady Lufton would answer.", "레이디 러프턴은 이렇게 대답했다."], ["“What does his wife do—and his sister?”", "“그 아내는 무얼 하고, 그 누이는 무얼 한단 말이냐?”"], ["This allusion to Lucy, however, was very soon dropped.", "그러나 루시를 두고 하는 이런 말은 곧 자취를 감췄다."]], [["Lord Lufton would in no wise help her.", "러프턴 경은 어머니를 도울 마음이 조금도 없었다."], ["He would not even passively discourage the vicar, or refrain from offering to give him a seat in going to the meets.", "그 교구 사제를 소극적으로라도 말리지 않았고, 사냥 집합 장소로 갈 때 마차에 자리를 내주겠다는 말을 삼가지도 않았다."], ["Mark and Lord Lufton had been boys together, and his lordship knew that Mark in his heart would enjoy a brush across the country quite as well as he himself; and then what was the harm of it?", "마크와 러프턴 경은 어릴 때부터 함께 자란 사이였고, 마크도 속으로는 자기와 똑같이 들판을 가로지르는 한바탕 질주를 즐긴다는 것을 경은 알고 있었다. 그렇다면 그게 무슨 해가 되겠는가?"]], [["Lady Lufton’s best aid had been in Mark’s own conscience.", "레이디 러프턴의 가장 든든한 조력자는 마크 자신의 양심이었다."], ["He had taken himself to task more than once, and had promised himself that he would not become a sporting parson.", "그는 한 번 이상 스스로를 나무랐고, 사냥이나 다니는 목사가 되지는 않겠다고 스스로에게 약속했다. 실로 그만큼까지 타락하도록 자신을 내버려 둔다면 앞날의 승진에 무슨 희망을 걸 수 있겠는가?"], ["Indeed, where would be his hopes of ulterior promotion, if he allowed himself to degenerate so far as that?", "성직자의 생활에 필요하다고 여겨지는 예절을 되짚어 보고 앞으로 자신이 살아갈 방식을 그려 볼 때, 그가 품은 뜻은 사제 특유의 유난한 엄격함은 취하지 않겠다는 것이었다. 춤이나 카드판을, 극장이나 소설 읽기를 규탄하는 사람으로 이름나지는 않겠다는 것이었다."], ["It had been his intention, in reviewing what he considered to be the necessary proprieties of clerical life, in laying out his own future mode of living, to assume no peculiar sacerdotal strictness; he would not be known as a denouncer of dancing or of card-tables, of theatres or of novel-reading; he would take the world around him as he found it, endeavouring by precept and practice to lend a hand to the gradual amelioration which Christianity is producing; but he would attempt no sudden or majestic reforms.", "그는 주위의 세상을 있는 그대로 받아들이면서, 가르침과 몸가짐으로 기독교가 이루어 가는 점진적인 개선에 손을 보태려 애쓸 것이었다. 그러나 갑작스럽거나 거창한 개혁은 시도하지 않을 것이었다."], ["Cake and ale would still be popular, and ginger be hot in the mouth, let him preach ever so—let him be never so solemn a hermit; but a bright face, a true trusting heart, a strong arm, and an humble mind, might do much in teaching those around him that men may be gay and yet not profligate, that women may be devout and yet not dead to the world.", "그가 아무리 설교를 한들, 아무리 엄숙한 은자가 된들, 과자와 맥주는 여전히 인기가 있을 것이고 생강은 여전히 입안을 얼얼하게 할 것이었다. 그러나 밝은 얼굴, 참되고 믿음직한 마음, 튼튼한 팔, 겸손한 정신이라면, 사람이 유쾌하면서도 방탕하지 않을 수 있고 여자가 신앙이 깊으면서도 세상에 죽어 있지 않을 수 있다는 것을 주위 사람들에게 가르치는 데 큰 몫을 할지도 몰랐다."]], [["Such had been his ideas as to his own future life; and though many would think that as a clergyman he should have gone about his work with more serious devotion of thought, nevertheless there was some wisdom in them;—some folly also, undoubtedly, as appeared by the troubles into which they led him.", "자기 앞날의 삶에 대한 그의 생각은 그러했다. 성직자라면 좀 더 진지하게 마음을 바쳐 자기 일에 임했어야 한다고 여길 사람이 많겠지만, 그래도 그 생각에는 얼마간의 지혜가 있었다. 물론 얼마간의 어리석음도 있었으니, 그 생각이 그를 끌고 들어간 곤경이 그것을 드러냈다."]], [["“I will not affect to think that to be bad,” said he to himself, “which in my heart of hearts does not seem to be bad.”", "“내 마음 깊은 곳에서 나쁘게 보이지 않는 것을 나쁘다고 여기는 척하지는 않겠다.”"], ["And thus he resolved that he might live without contamination among hunting squires.", "그는 스스로에게 이렇게 말했다. 그리하여 그는 사냥하는 지주들 사이에서도 물들지 않고 살 수 있으리라고 마음을 정했다."], ["And then, being a man only too prone by nature to do as others did around him, he found by degrees that that could hardly be wrong for him which he admitted to be right for others.", "그런데 그는 본래 주위 사람들이 하는 대로 따라 하기가 너무 쉬운 사람이었으므로, 남에게 옳다고 인정한 일이 자기에게 그르기는 어렵겠다는 생각에 차츰 이르렀다."]], [["But still his conscience upbraided him, and he declared to himself more than once that after this year he would hunt no more.", "그래도 양심은 그를 꾸짖었고, 그는 올해가 지나면 다시는 사냥을 하지 않겠다고 한 번 이상 스스로에게 선언했다."], ["And then his own Fanny would look at him on his return home on those days in a manner that cut him to the heart.", "그리고 그런 날 집에 돌아오면 그의 패니가 그를 바라보는 눈길이 그의 가슴을 찔렀다. 그녀는 그에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She would say nothing to him.", "비웃는 어조로, 노한 눈으로 오늘 사냥이 즐거웠느냐고 물은 적도 없었다."], ["She never inquired in a sneering tone, and with angry eyes, whether he had enjoyed his day’s sport; but when he spoke of it, she could not answer him with enthusiasm; and in other matters which concerned him she was always enthusiastic.", "그러나 그가 사냥 이야기를 꺼내면 그녀는 신이 나서 대꾸해 주지 못했다. 그와 관련된 다른 일이라면 그녀는 언제나 신이 나서 대꾸하는 사람이었다."]], [["After a while, too, he made matters worse, for about the end of March he did another very foolish thing.", "게다가 얼마 뒤에는 사태를 더 나쁘게 만들었으니, 삼월 말께에 또 하나 아주 어리석은 일을 저지른 것이다."], ["He almost consented to buy an expensive horse from Sowerby—an animal which he by no means wanted, and which, if once possessed, would certainly lead him into further trouble.", "그는 사워비에게서 값비싼 말을 사겠다고 거의 승낙해 버렸다 — 조금도 필요하지 않은 짐승이요, 한번 손에 넣으면 반드시 그를 더 깊은 곤경으로 끌고 갈 짐승이었다."], ["A gentleman, when he has a good horse in his stable, does not like to leave him there eating his head off.", "신사란 마구간에 좋은 말이 있으면 그 말을 거기 세워 두고 밥값이나 먹어 치우게 하고 싶지 않은 법이다."], ["If he be a gig-horse, the owner of him will be keen to drive a gig; if a hunter, the happy possessor will wish to be with a pack of hounds.", "이륜마차 말이라면 주인은 이륜마차를 몰고 싶어 안달할 것이고, 사냥말이라면 그 흐뭇한 임자는 사냥개 무리와 함께 나서고 싶어질 것이다."]], [["“Mark,”", "“마크,”"], ["said Sowerby to him one day, when they were out together, “this brute of mine is so fresh, I can hardly ride him; you are young and strong; change with me for an hour or so.”", "어느 날 함께 나가 있을 때 사워비가 그에게 말했다. “내 이놈이 어찌나 기운이 넘치는지 도무지 탈 수가 없어요."], ["And then they did change, and the horse on which Robarts found himself mounted went away with him beautifully.", "당신은 젊고 튼튼하니 한 시간쯤 나와 바꿔 타 봐요.” 그래서 두 사람은 말을 바꿔 탔고, 로바츠가 올라앉은 그 말은 그를 태우고 훌륭하게 달려 나갔다."]], [["“He’s a splendid animal,”", "“훌륭한 짐승이군요.”"], ["said Mark, when they again met.", "다시 만났을 때 마크가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, for a man of your weight.", "“그래요, 당신만큼 몸무게가 나가는 사람에게는 그렇지요."], ["He’s thrown away upon me;—too much of a horse for my purposes.", "나에게는 아까운 말이에요. 내 용도에는 너무 과한 말이지요."], ["I don’t get along now quite as well as I used to do.", "나는 이제 예전만큼 잘 따라가지 못하니까요."], ["He is a nice sort of hunter; just rising six, you know.”", "사냥말로는 좋은 축이에요. 이제 여섯 살로 접어들고 있고요.”"]], [["How it came to pass that the price of the splendid animal was mentioned between them, I need not describe with exactness.", "그 훌륭한 짐승의 값이 두 사람 사이에서 거론되기에 이른 경위를 내가 정확히 그려 보일 필요는 없다."], ["But it did come to pass that Mr. Sowerby told the parson that the horse should be his for £130.", "다만 사워비 씨가 그 목사에게 그 말을 130파운드에 넘기겠다고 말한 일은 실제로 벌어졌다."]], [["“And I really wish you’d take him,” said Sowerby.", "“그리고 정말이지 당신이 그 말을 가져갔으면 좋겠어요.”"], ["“It would be the means of partially relieving my mind of a great weight.”", "사워비가 말했다. “그러면 내 마음을 짓누르는 큰 짐이 얼마쯤은 덜릴 테니까요.”"]], [["Mark looked up into his friend’s face with an air of surprise, for he did not at the moment understand how this should be the case.", "마크는 놀란 얼굴로 벗의 얼굴을 올려다보았다. 어째서 그렇게 되는지 그 순간에는 알 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["“I am afraid, you know, that you will have to put your hand into your pocket sooner or later about that accursed bill—”", "“당신도 알다시피 나는 그 저주받을 어음 때문에 당신이 조만간 주머니에 손을 넣어야 하지 않을까 걱정이거든요 —”"], ["Mark shrank as the profane word struck his ears—“and I should be glad to think that you had got something in hand in the way of value.”", "그 불경한 말이 귀에 들어오자 마크는 몸을 움츠렸다 — “그러니 당신이 값이 될 만한 것을 손에 쥐고 있다고 생각할 수 있으면 나로서는 기쁘겠어요.”"]], [["“Do you mean that I shall have to pay the whole sum of £500?”", "“내가 500파운드를 통째로 물어야 한다는 말인가요?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아니, 천만에요."], ["dear, no; nothing of the kind.", "그런 게 아니에요."], ["But something I dare say you will have to pay: if you like to take Dandy for a hundred and thirty, you can be prepared for that amount when Tozer comes to you.", "하지만 얼마쯤은 물어야 할 겁니다. 댄디를 백삼십에 가져가겠다면, 토저가 당신에게 찾아올 때 그만한 액수는 준비된 셈이 되지요."], ["The horse is dog cheap, and you will have a long day for your money.”", "그 말은 헐값이나 다름없어요. 낸 돈으로 두고두고 재미를 볼 겁니다.”"]], [["Mark at first declared, in a quiet, determined tone, that he did not want the horse; but it afterwards appeared to him that if it were so fated that he must pay a portion of Mr.", "마크는 처음에는 조용하고 단호한 어조로 그 말이 필요하지 않다고 잘라 말했다."], ["Sowerby’s debts, he might as well repay himself to any extent within his power. It would be as well perhaps that he should take the horse and sell him.", "그러나 나중에는, 사워비 씨의 빚 가운데 얼마를 자기가 물어야 할 운명이라면 힘이 미치는 한 얼마라도 되받아 두는 편이 낫지 않겠느냐는 생각이 들었다."], ["It did not occur to him that by so doing he would put it in Mr.", "그 말을 받아 두었다가 팔아 버리는 것도 어쩌면 괜찮은 일이었다."], ["Sowerby’s power to say that some valuable consideration had passed between them with reference to this bill, and that he would be aiding that gentleman in preparing an inextricable confusion of money-matters between them.", "그렇게 하면 이 어음과 관련하여 두 사람 사이에 값나가는 대가가 오갔다고 사워비 씨가 말할 수 있게 되고, 두 사람 사이의 금전 관계를 풀 수 없는 뒤엉킴으로 만들어 놓으려는 그 신사의 일을 자기가 돕는 셈이 된다는 것은 그의 머리에 떠오르지 않았다."], ["Mr. Sowerby well knew the value of this.", "사워비 씨는 이것의 값을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["It would enable him to make a plausible story, as he had done in that other case of Lord Lufton.", "그것이 있으면 러프턴 경의 그 다른 경우에 그랬듯이 그럴싸한 이야기를 꾸며 낼 수 있었다."]], [["“Are you going to have Dandy?”", "“댄디를 가져가겠어요?”"], ["Sowerby said to him again.", "사워비가 그에게 다시 말했다."]], [["“I can’t say that I will just at present,”", "“당장은 그러겠다고 말할 수 없군요.”"], ["said the parson.", "목사가 말했다."], ["“What should I want of him now the season’s over?”", "“철도 다 끝났는데 이제 그 말을 어디에 쓰겠어요?”"]], [["“Exactly, my dear fellow; and what do I want of him now the season’s over?", "“바로 그거예요, 마크. 철이 끝난 지금 나 역시 그 말을 어디에 쓰겠어요?"], ["If it were the beginning of October instead of the end of March, Dandy would be up at two hundred and thirty instead of one: in six months’ time that horse will be worth anything you like to ask for him.", "지금이 삼월 말이 아니라 시월 초라면 댄디는 백이 아니라 이백삼십까지 올라 있을 겁니다. 여섯 달만 지나면 그 말은 당신이 부르고 싶은 값이 무엇이든 그만한 값이 나갈 거예요."], ["Look at his bone.”", "그 골격을 좀 봐요.”"]], [["The vicar did look at his bones, examining the brute in a very knowing and unclerical manner.", "그 교구 사제는 정말로 골격을 들여다보았으니, 아주 능숙하고 도무지 성직자답지 않은 태도로 그 짐승을 살폈다."], ["He lifted the animal’s four feet, one after another, handling the frogs, and measuring with his eye the proportion of the parts; he passed his hand up and down the legs, spanning the bones of the lower joint; he peered into his eyes, took into consideration the width of his chest, the dip of his back, the form of his ribs, the curve of his haunches, and his capabilities for breathing when pressed by work.", "그는 말의 네 발을 하나씩 차례로 들어 굽바닥의 삼각 살을 만져 보고 각 부분의 균형을 눈으로 재었다. 다리를 위아래로 손으로 훑으며 아래 관절의 뼈를 손으로 감아 보았다."], ["And then he stood away a little, eyeing him from the side, and taking in a general idea of the form and make of the whole.", "눈을 들여다보았고, 가슴통의 너비, 등의 처짐, 갈비의 모양, 엉덩이의 곡선, 그리고 일을 심하게 시켰을 때의 호흡 능력을 헤아렸다. 그리고 조금 물러서서 옆에서 바라보며 전체의 모양과 됨됨이를 대강 가늠했다."], ["“He seems to stand over a little, I think,”", "“무릎이 조금 앞으로 넘어간 것 같은데요.”"], ["said the parson.", "목사가 말했다."]], [["“It’s the lie of the ground.", "“땅이 기울어서 그래요."], ["Move him about, Bob.", "밥, 말을 좀 걸려 봐."], ["There now, let him stand there.”", "그래, 이제 거기 세워 둬.”"]], [["“He’s not perfect,”", "“완전하지는 않군요.”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."], ["“I don’t quite like his heels; but no doubt he’s a nicish cut of a horse.”", "“뒷굽이 그다지 마음에 들지 않아요. 하지만 제법 괜찮은 축의 말인 건 틀림없네요.”"]], [["“I rather think he is.", "“나는 꽤 괜찮다고 보는데요."], ["If he were perfect, as you say, he would not be going into your stables for a hundred and thirty.", "당신 말대로 완전하기까지 하다면 백삼십에 당신 마구간으로 들어가지는 않겠지요."], ["Do you ever remember to have seen a perfect horse?”", "완전한 말을 본 기억이 있어요?”"]], [["“Your mare Mrs. Gamp was as nearly perfect as possible.”", "“당신의 암말 갬프 부인은 거의 완전하다 할 만했지요.”"]], [["“Even Mrs. Gamp had her faults.", "“갬프 부인에게도 흠은 있었어요."], ["In the first place she was a bad feeder.", "우선 먹성이 좋지 않았지요."], ["But one certainly doesn’t often come across anything much better than Mrs. Gamp.”", "하지만 갬프 부인보다 훨씬 나은 말을 만나는 일이 흔하지 않은 건 분명해요.”"], ["And thus the matter was talked over between them with much stable conversation, all of which tended to make Sowerby more and more oblivious of his friend’s sacred profession, and perhaps to make the vicar himself too frequently oblivious of it also.", "이런 식으로 두 사람 사이에서 그 일이 이야기되었고 마구간 이야기가 길게 오갔으니, 그 모든 것이 사워비로 하여금 벗의 성스러운 직분을 점점 더 잊게 만들었고, 어쩌면 그 교구 사제 자신도 그것을 너무 자주 잊게 만들었을 것이다."], ["But no: he was not oblivious of it.", "그러나 아니었다. 그는 그것을 잊지 않았다."], ["He was even mindful of it; but mindful of it in such a manner that his thoughts on the subject were nowadays always painful.", "오히려 마음에 두고 있었다. 다만 마음에 두는 방식이 그러하여, 요즘 그 문제에 대한 그의 생각은 언제나 괴로웠다."]], [["There is a parish called Hogglestock lying away quite in the northern extremity of the eastern division of the county—lying also on the borders of the western division.", "주의 동부 지구 북쪽 맨 끝에, 그러면서 서부 지구와 맞닿은 경계에도 걸쳐 있는 호글스톡이라는 교구가 있다."], ["I almost fear that it will become necessary, before this history be completed, to provide a map of Barsetshire for the due explanation of all these localities.", "이 이야기가 끝나기 전에 이 모든 지명을 제대로 설명하려면 바셋주의 지도를 하나 붙여야 하지 않을까 나는 은근히 걱정이 된다."], ["Framley is also in the northern portion of the county, but just to the south of the grand trunk line of railway from which the branch to Barchester strikes off at a point some thirty miles nearer to London.", "프램리도 주의 북부에 있으나, 런던 쪽으로 삼십 마일쯤 더 가까운 지점에서 바체스터로 갈라져 나가는 지선이 뻗어 나오는 그 간선 철도의 바로 남쪽이다."], ["The station for Framley Court is Silverbridge, which is, however, in the western division of the county.", "프램리 코트로 가는 역은 실버브리지인데, 그 역은 주의 서부 지구에 있다."], ["Hogglestock is to the north of the railway, the line of which, however, runs through a portion of the parish, and it adjoins Framley, though the churches are as much as seven miles apart.", "호글스톡은 철도의 북쪽이지만 그 철도 노선은 교구의 일부를 지나가며, 교회끼리는 칠 마일이나 떨어져 있어도 교구는 프램리와 맞붙어 있다."], ["Barsetshire, taken altogether, is a pleasant green tree-becrowded county, with large bosky hedges, pretty damp deep lanes, and roads with broad grass margins running along them.", "바셋주는 전체로 보아 나무가 빽빽한 정겨운 초록 고을이니, 우거진 큰 산울타리와 촉촉하고 깊숙한 예쁜 시골길, 그리고 넓은 풀밭 가장자리를 끼고 뻗은 도로가 있다. 이 주의 일반적인 성격은 그러하다."], ["Such is the general nature of the county; but just up in its northern extremity this nature alters.", "그러나 북쪽 맨 끝에 이르면 그 성격이 달라진다. 거기는 황량하고 보기 흉하며, 산울타리는 낮게 인공으로 다듬어져 있고 숲이 없다."], ["There it is bleak and ugly, with low artificial hedges and without wood; not uncultivated, as it is all portioned out into new-looking large fields, bearing turnips and wheat and mangel, all in due course of agricultural rotation; but it has none of the special beauties of English cultivation.", "그렇다고 농사를 짓지 않는 것은 아니어서, 온 땅이 새로 낸 듯한 큰 밭으로 갈라져 순무와 밀과 사료 무를 윤작의 순서에 따라 내고 있다. 그러나 영국식 농지 특유의 아름다움은 조금도 없다."], ["There is not a gentleman’s house in the parish of Hogglestock besides that of the clergyman; and this, though it is certainly the house of a gentleman, can hardly be said to be fit to be so.", "호글스톡 교구에는 성직자의 집 말고는 신사의 집이 한 채도 없다. 그리고 그 집도 신사의 집이기는 하나 신사의 집으로 걸맞다고는 도무지 말할 수 없다."], ["It is ugly, and straight, and small.", "보기 흉하고, 멋없이 각지고, 작다."], ["There is a garden attached to the house, half in front of it and half behind; but this garden, like the rest of the parish, is by no means ornamental, though sufficiently useful.", "집에는 정원이 딸려 있어 절반은 앞에, 절반은 뒤에 있다. 그러나 이 정원도 교구의 다른 데와 마찬가지로 쓸모는 충분하나 볼거리는 전혀 없다."], ["It produces cabbages, but no trees: potatoes of, I believe, an excellent description, but hardly any flowers, and nothing worthy of the name of a shrub.", "양배추는 나지만 나무는 없고, 감자는 아주 훌륭한 종류라고 나는 알고 있으나 꽃은 거의 없으며, 관목이라는 이름에 걸맞은 것은 하나도 없다."], ["Indeed the whole parish of Hogglestock should have been in the adjoining county, which is by no means so attractive as Barsetshire;—a fact well known to those few of my readers who are well acquainted with their own country.", "실로 호글스톡 교구 전체는 바셋주만큼 매력이 있다고는 결코 할 수 없는 이웃 주에 들어가 있어야 했다. 이는 자기 나라를 잘 아는 몇 안 되는 내 독자들이라면 익히 아는 사실이다."]], [["Mr. Crawley, whose name has been mentioned in these pages, was the incumbent of Hogglestock.", "이 지면에서 이름이 언급된 바 있는 크롤리 씨가 호글스톡의 담임 성직자였다."], ["On what principle the remuneration of our parish clergymen was settled when the original settlement was made, no deepest, keenest lover of middle-aged ecclesiastical black-letter learning can, I take it, now say.", "애초에 제도가 정해질 때 우리 교구 성직자의 보수가 어떤 원리로 정해졌는지는, 중세 교회의 검은 고딕 활자 학문을 가장 깊고 가장 열렬히 사랑하는 사람이라도 이제는 말할 수 없으리라고 나는 본다."], ["That the priests were to be paid from tithes of the parish produce, out of which tithes certain other good things were to be bought and paid for, such as church repairs and education, of so much the most of us have an inkling.", "사제들이 교구에서 나는 것의 십일조로 보수를 받고, 그 십일조에서 교회 수리며 교육 같은 다른 좋은 일들의 비용도 대게 되어 있었다는 것 정도는 우리 대부분이 어렴풋이 안다."], ["That a rector, being a big sort of parson, owned the tithes of his parish in full,—or at any rate that part of them intended for the clergyman,—and that a vicar was somebody’s deputy, and therefore entitled only to little tithes, as being a little body: of so much we that are simple in such matters have a general idea.", "주임 사제는 큰 축의 목사이므로 자기 교구의 십일조를 전부 — 적어도 성직자에게 돌아갈 몫은 전부 — 차지했고, 교구 사제는 남의 대리인이니 작은 몸인 만큼 소십일조에만 권리가 있었다는 것 정도는, 이런 일에 어두운 우리도 대강 알고 있다."], ["But one cannot conceive that even in this way any approximation could have been made, even in those old mediæval days, towards a fair proportioning of the pay to the work.", "그러나 그 옛 중세에도 그런 식으로나마 일에 대한 보수를 공정하게 배분하는 데 조금이라도 가까이 갔다고는 생각할 수 없다."], ["At any rate, it is clear enough that there is no such approximation now.", "아무튼 지금은 그런 근사치조차 없다는 것만은 충분히 분명하다."]], [["And what a screech would there not be among the clergy of the Church, even in these reforming days, if any over-bold reformer were to suggest that such an approximation should be attempted?", "그리고 개혁의 시대라는 이 시절에조차, 어떤 지나치게 대담한 개혁가가 그런 근사치를 한번 시도해 보자고 제안한다면 교회의 성직자들 사이에서 얼마나 큰 비명이 터져 나올 것인가?"], ["Let those who know clergymen, and like them, and have lived with them, only fancy it!", "성직자를 알고, 성직자를 좋아하고, 성직자와 더불어 살아 본 사람들은 한번 상상이나 해 보시라!"], ["Clergymen to be paid, not according to the temporalities of any living which they may have acquired either by merit or favour, but in accordance with the work to be done!", "성직자가 공로로든 은고로든 얻어 낸 성직록의 세속 수입에 따라 보수를 받는 것이 아니라, 해야 할 일에 따라 보수를 받는다니!"], ["O Doddington!", "오 도딩턴이여!"], ["and O Stanhope, think of this, if an idea so sacrilegious can find entrance into your warm ecclesiastical bosoms!", "오 스탠호프여, 이토록 신성을 모독하는 생각이 그대들의 따뜻한 성직자의 품에 들어갈 수 있다면, 이것을 한번 생각해 보시라!"], ["Ecclesiastical work to be bought and paid for according to its quantity and quality!", "교회의 일을 그 양과 질에 따라 사고팔고 값을 치른다니!"]], [["But, nevertheless, one may prophesy that we Englishmen must come to this, disagreeable as the idea undoubtedly is.", "그러나 그럼에도 우리 영국인은 결국 여기에 이르지 않을 수 없으리라고 예언해 둘 수 있다. 그 생각이 아무리 언짢은 것임에 틀림없더라도 그렇다."], ["Most pleasant-minded churchmen feel, I think, on this subject pretty much in the same way.", "마음이 너그러운 교인들도 이 문제에 대해서는 대개 나와 비슷하게 느낀다고 나는 생각한다."], ["Our present arrangement of parochial incomes is beloved as being time-honoured, gentlemanlike, English, and picturesque.", "교구 수입에 관한 우리의 지금 제도는 오래되었다는 이유로, 신사답고 영국답고 그림 같다는 이유로 사랑을 받는다."], ["We would fain adhere to it closely as long as we can, but we know that we do so by the force of our prejudices, and not by that of our judgment.", "우리는 될 수 있는 한 그것에 바싹 매달리고 싶어 하나, 우리가 그러는 것이 판단의 힘이 아니라 편견의 힘 때문임을 우리도 안다."], ["A time-honoured, gentlemanlike, English, picturesque arrangement is so far very delightful.", "오래되고, 신사답고, 영국답고, 그림 같은 제도라는 것은 거기까지는 아주 흐뭇하다."], ["But are there not other attributes very desirable—nay, absolutely necessary—in respect to which this time-honoured, picturesque arrangement is so very deficient?", "그러나 그 밖에도 몹시 바람직한 — 아니, 반드시 있어야 할 — 성질들이 있는데, 이 오래되고 그림 같은 제도는 바로 그 점에서 지독히 모자라지 않은가?"]], [["How pleasant it was, too, that one bishop should be getting fifteen thousand a year and another with an equal cure of parsons only four!", "게다가 어떤 주교는 한 해에 만 오천을 받고, 목사를 거느린 정도가 똑같은 다른 주교는 겨우 사천을 받는다는 것이 또 얼마나 흐뭇한 일이었던가!"], ["That a certain prelate could get twenty thousand one year and his successor in the same diocese only five the next!", "어느 고위 성직자는 한 해에 이만을 받는데 같은 교구의 후임자는 이듬해에 겨우 오천을 받을 수 있었다는 것이!"], ["There was something in it pleasant, and picturesque; it was an arrangement endowed with feudal charms, and the change which they have made was distasteful to many of us.", "거기에는 흐뭇하고 그림 같은 무엇이 있었다. 봉건의 매력이 깃든 제도였고, 그것을 바꿔 놓은 일은 우리 가운데 많은 사람의 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["A bishop with a regular salary, and no appanage of land and land-bailiffs, is only half a bishop.", "정해진 봉급을 받고 영지도 토지 관리인도 딸리지 않은 주교는 반쪽 주교일 뿐이다."], ["Let any man prove to me the contrary ever so thoroughly—let me prove it to my own self ever so often—my heart in this matter is not thereby a whit altered.", "누가 나에게 그 반대를 아무리 철저히 증명해 보인다 한들 — 내가 나 자신에게 아무리 자주 증명해 본다 한들 — 이 문제에 관한 내 마음은 그로써 눈곱만큼도 달라지지 않는다."], ["One liked to know that there was a dean or two who got his three thousand a year, and that old Dr. Purple held four stalls, one of which was golden, and the other three silver-gilt!", "한 해에 삼천을 받는 수석 사제가 하나둘 있고, 늙은 퍼플 박사는 참사회원 자리를 네 개나 쥐고 있었으며 그중 하나는 황금 자리요 나머지 셋은 은에 금박을 씌운 자리였다는 것을 아는 게 좋았다!"], ["Such knowledge was always pleasant to me!", "그런 것을 아는 일은 나에게 언제나 흐뭇했다!"], ["A golden stall!", "황금 자리라니!"], ["How sweet is the sound thereof to church-loving ears!", "교회를 사랑하는 귀에 그 울림이 얼마나 달가운가!"]], [["But bishops have been shorn of their beauty, and deans are in their decadence.", "그러나 주교들은 그 아름다움을 깎였고 수석 사제들은 쇠락의 길에 있다."], ["A utilitarian age requires the fatness of the ecclesiastical land, in order that it may be divided out into small portions of provender, on which necessary working clergymen may live,—into portions so infinitesimally small that working clergymen can hardly live.", "공리주의의 시대는 교회 땅의 기름진 몫을 요구한다. 그것을 잘게 나누어, 꼭 필요한 일하는 성직자들이 먹고살 만한 작은 여물로 만들자는 것이다 — 일하는 성직자가 도무지 먹고살 수 없을 만큼 터무니없이 잘게 나눈 몫으로."], ["And the full-blown rectors and vicars, with full-blown tithes—with tithes when too full-blown for strict utilitarian principles—will necessarily follow.", "그리고 십일조가 활짝 만개한 주임 사제와 교구 사제들도 — 엄격한 공리주의 원칙에 비추어 너무 만개한 십일조를 쥔 이들도 — 반드시 그 뒤를 따를 것이다."], ["Stanhope and Doddington must bow their heads, with such compensation for temporal rights as may be extracted,—but probably without such compensation as may be desired.", "스탠호프와 도딩턴은 머리를 숙여야 하리라. 세속의 권리에 대해서는 받아 낼 수 있는 만큼의 보상을 받겠으나, 바라는 만큼의 보상은 아마 받지 못할 것이다."], ["In other trades, professions, and lines of life, men are paid according to their work.", "다른 장사와 직업과 생업에서는 사람이 일한 만큼 보수를 받는다."], ["Let it be so in the Church.", "교회에서도 그렇게 되게 하라."], ["Such will sooner or later be the edict of a utilitarian, reforming, matter-of-fact House of Parliament.", "공리주의적이고 개혁적이고 실무적인 의회의 명령이 조만간 그렇게 나올 것이다."]], [["I have a scheme of my own on the subject, which I will not introduce here, seeing that neither men nor women would read it.", "이 문제에 대해서는 나에게도 나름의 방안이 있으나, 남자도 여자도 읽지 않을 터이니 여기 내놓지는 않겠다."], ["And with reference to this matter, I will only here further explain that all these words have been brought about by the fact, necessary to be here stated, that Mr. Crawley only received one hundred and thirty pounds a year for performing the whole parochial duty of the parish of Hogglestock.", "그리고 이 일과 관련하여 여기서 더 밝혀 둘 것은 다만, 이 모든 말이 나오게 된 계기가 여기 반드시 적어 두어야 할 사실, 곧 크롤리 씨가 호글스톡 교구의 교구 업무 전부를 도맡아 하면서 한 해에 백삼십 파운드밖에 받지 못했다는 사실이라는 점이다."], ["And Hogglestock is a large parish.", "게다가 호글스톡은 큰 교구다."], ["It includes two populous villages, abounding in brickmakers, a race of men very troublesome to a zealous parson who won’t let men go rollicking to the devil without interference.", "벽돌 굽는 이들이 잔뜩 사는 인구 많은 마을이 둘 들어 있는데, 사람이 흥청거리며 마귀에게로 걸어가는 것을 그냥 두고 보지 못하는 열심한 목사에게 이 무리는 몹시 골칫거리다."], ["Hogglestock has full work for two men; and yet all the funds therein applicable to parson’s work is this miserable stipend of one hundred and thirty pounds a year.", "호글스톡에는 두 사람이 꼬박 매달릴 만한 일이 있다. 그런데도 그 안에서 목사의 일에 쓸 수 있는 재원이라고는 이 한 해 백삼십 파운드라는 초라한 봉급이 전부다."], ["It is a stipend neither picturesque, nor time-honoured, nor feudal, for Hogglestock takes rank only as a perpetual curacy.", "그림 같지도, 오래되지도, 봉건적이지도 않은 봉급이다. 호글스톡은 종신 부목사직으로만 등급이 매겨져 있기 때문이다."]], [["Mr. Crawley has been mentioned before as a clergyman of whom Mr.", "크롤리 씨는 앞서, 자기 교구 밖으로 산책 나가는 것조차 옳지 않게 여기는 사람이라고 로바츠 씨가 말한 그 성직자로 언급된 바 있다."], ["Robarts said, that he almost thought it wrong to take a walk out of his own parish.", "그렇게 말할 때 마크 로바츠는 물론 같은 목사인 그를 우스갯거리로 삼은 것이다."], ["In so saying Mark Robarts of course burlesqued his brother parson; but there can be no doubt that Mr. Crawley was a strict man,—a strict, stern, unpleasant man, and one who feared God and his own conscience.", "그러나 크롤리 씨가 엄격한 사람이었다는 것에는 의심의 여지가 없다 — 엄격하고 준엄하고 사람을 편하게 해 주지 않는 사람, 하느님과 자기 양심을 두려워하는 사람이었다."], ["We must say a word or two of Mr. Crawley and his concerns.", "우리는 크롤리 씨와 그의 처지에 대해 한두 마디 해 두어야 한다."]], [["He was now some forty years of age, but of these he had not been in possession even of his present benefice for more than four or five.", "그는 이제 마흔 살쯤 되었으나, 그 가운데 지금의 성직록을 차지한 것은 사오 년밖에 되지 않았다."], ["The first ten years of his life as a clergyman had been passed in performing the duties and struggling through the life of a curate in a bleak, ugly, cold parish on the northern coast of Cornwall.", "성직자로서 그의 삶의 첫 십 년은 콘월 북쪽 해안의 황량하고 보기 흉하고 추운 교구에서 부목사의 직무를 치르며 그 삶을 헤쳐 나가는 데 다 들어갔다."], ["It had been a weary life and a fearful struggle, made up of duties ill requited and not always satisfactorily performed, of love and poverty, of increasing cares, of sickness, debt, and death.", "그것은 지치는 삶이요 무서운 싸움이었으니, 제대로 보답받지 못하고 늘 만족스럽게 해내지도 못한 직무로, 사랑과 가난으로, 늘어 가는 근심으로, 병과 빚과 죽음으로 이루어진 삶이었다."], ["For Mr. Crawley had married almost as soon as he was ordained, and children had been born to him in that chill, comfortless Cornish cottage.", "크롤리 씨는 서품을 받자마자 거의 곧바로 혼인했고, 그 차고 아늑함이라곤 없는 콘월의 오두막에서 그에게 아이들이 태어났던 것이다."], ["He had married a lady well educated and softly nurtured, but not dowered with worldly wealth.", "그가 혼인한 여자는 교육을 잘 받고 곱게 자랐으나 세상의 재물은 지니고 오지 못한 사람이었다."], ["They two had gone forth determined to fight bravely together; to disregard the world and the world’s ways, looking only to God and to each other for their comfort.", "두 사람은 함께 용감히 싸우기로 마음을 굳히고 나섰다. 세상과 세상의 방식은 아예 거들떠보지 않고, 위로를 얻을 곳으로는 오직 하느님과 서로만을 바라보기로 했다."], ["They would give up ideas of gentle living, of soft raiment, and delicate feeding.", "점잖은 살림이며 부드러운 옷가지며 고운 음식 따위의 생각은 버릴 것이었다."], ["Others,—those that work with their hands, even the bettermost of such workers—could live in decency and health upon even such provision as he could earn as a clergyman.", "다른 사람들, 곧 손으로 일하는 사람들, 그중에서도 형편이 나은 축이라 해도, 그가 성직자로 벌 수 있는 만큼의 살림만으로도 체면을 지키며 건강하게 살아가고 있었다."], ["In such manner would they live, so poorly and so decently, working out their work, not with their hands but with their hearts.", "그렇게 그들도 살 것이었다. 그토록 가난하게, 그토록 체면을 지키며, 손이 아니라 마음으로 자기 일을 해내며 살 것이었다."]], [["And so they had established themselves, beginning the world with one bare-footed little girl of fourteen to aid them in their small household matters; and for a while they had both kept heart, loving each other dearly, and prospering somewhat in their work.", "그리하여 그들은 자리를 잡았으니, 조그만 살림을 거들어 줄 사람으로 맨발의 열네 살 난 계집아이 하나를 두고 세상을 시작했다. 그리고 한동안은 둘 다 마음을 잃지 않고 서로를 깊이 사랑하며 자기 일에서도 어느 만큼 잘되어 갔다."], ["But a man who has once walked the world as a gentleman knows not what it is to change his position, and place himself lower down in the social rank.", "그러나 한번 신사로서 세상을 걸어 본 사람은 자기 처지를 바꾸어 사회의 서열에서 더 아래로 내려앉는다는 것이 어떤 일인지 알지 못한다."], ["Much less can he know what it is so to put down the woman whom he loves.", "자기가 사랑하는 여자를 그렇게 끌어내린다는 것이 어떤 일인지는 더욱 알지 못한다."], ["There are a thousand things, mean and trifling in themselves, which a man despises when he thinks of them in his philosophy, but to dispense with which puts his philosophy to so stern a proof.", "그 자체로는 하잘것없고 사소한 일이 천 가지나 있어서, 사람이 자기 철학으로 그것들을 생각할 때에는 업신여기지만, 정작 그것들 없이 지내야 할 때에는 그 철학이 준엄한 시험을 받는다."], ["Let any plainest man who reads this think of his usual mode of getting himself into his matutinal garments, and confess how much such a struggle would cost him.", "이 글을 읽는 아무리 소박한 사람이라도, 아침에 옷을 갖춰 입는 자기 평소의 방식을 한번 떠올려 보고 그런 싸움이 자기에게 얼마나 비싼 값을 물릴지 고백해 보시라."]], [["And then children had come.", "그러고서 아이들이 태어났다."], ["The wife of the labouring man does rear her children, and often rears them in health, without even so many appliances of comfort as found their way into Mrs.", "막일하는 사람의 아내도 자기 아이들을 길러 내며, 크롤리 부인의 오두막에 들어와 있던 것만큼의 편의조차 없이도 흔히 건강하게 길러 낸다."], ["Crawley’s cottage; but the task to her was almost more than she could accomplish.", "그러나 그녀에게 그 일은 거의 감당할 수 없을 만큼의 짐이었다."], ["Not that she ever fainted or gave way: she was made of the sterner metal of the two, and could last on while he was prostrate.", "그렇다고 그녀가 기절하거나 주저앉았다는 말은 아니다. 그녀는 두 사람 가운데 더 단단한 쇠로 만들어진 사람이어서, 그가 엎어져 있는 동안에도 버텨 낼 수 있었다."]], [["And sometimes he was prostrate—prostrate in soul and spirit.", "그리고 이따금 그는 정말로 엎어졌다 — 넋과 얼이 엎어졌다."], ["Then would he complain with bitter voice, crying out that the world was too hard for him, that his back was broken with his burden, that his God had deserted him.", "그럴 때면 그는 쓰라린 목소리로 하소연하며, 세상이 자기에게 너무 모질다고, 짐에 짓눌려 등이 부러졌다고, 자기의 하느님이 자기를 버렸다고 외쳤다."], ["For days and days, in such moods, he would stay within his cottage, never darkening the door or seeing other face than those of his own inmates.", "그런 심사에 빠지면 그는 며칠이고 며칠이고 오두막 안에 들어박혀, 문간에 그림자도 비치지 않고 집안 식구 아닌 다른 얼굴은 보지도 않았다."], ["Those days were terrible both to him and her.", "그런 날들은 그에게나 그녀에게나 끔찍했다."], ["He would sit there unwashed, with his unshorn face resting on his hand, with an old dressing-gown hanging loose about him, hardly tasting food, seldom speaking, striving to pray, but striving so frequently in vain.", "그는 세수도 하지 않은 채, 깎지 않은 얼굴을 손에 기대고, 낡은 실내 가운을 헐렁하게 걸치고 거기 앉아, 음식은 거의 입에 대지 않고, 말은 좀처럼 하지 않고, 기도하려 애쓰다가 그 애씀이 하도 자주 헛되이 끝나곤 했다."], ["And then he would rise from his chair, and, with a burst of frenzy, call upon his Creator to remove him from this misery.", "그러다가 그는 의자에서 일어나 미친 듯이 터져 나오는 소리로 자기 창조주를 불러, 이 참담함에서 자기를 데려가 달라고 부르짖었다."]], [["In these moments she never deserted him.", "그런 순간에도 그녀는 결코 그를 버리지 않았다."], ["At one period they had had four children, and though the whole weight of this young brood rested on her arms, on her muscles, on her strength of mind and body, she never ceased in her efforts to comfort him.", "한때 그들에게는 아이가 넷 있었고, 이 어린 무리의 무게가 온전히 그녀의 팔에, 그녀의 근육에, 그녀의 마음과 몸의 힘에 얹혀 있었으나, 그녀는 그를 위로하려는 애씀을 한 번도 그치지 않았다."], ["Then at length, falling utterly upon the ground, he would pour forth piteous prayers for mercy, and, after a night of sleep, would once more go forth to his work.", "그러면 마침내 그는 땅바닥에 아주 엎드려 자비를 구하는 애처로운 기도를 쏟아 놓았고, 하룻밤 잠을 자고 나면 다시 한번 일하러 나섰다."]], [["But she never yielded to despair: the struggle was never beyond her powers of endurance.", "그러나 그녀는 절망에 굽히지 않았다. 그 싸움이 그녀가 견딜 수 있는 힘을 넘어선 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["She had possessed her share of woman’s loveliness, but that was now all gone.", "여자로서의 고운 모습도 제 몫만큼은 지녔던 사람이었으나 그것은 이제 다 사라졌다."], ["Her colour quickly faded, and the fresh, soft tints soon deserted her face and forehead.", "혈색은 빠르게 시들었고, 싱싱하고 부드러운 빛깔은 이내 그 얼굴과 이마에서 물러갔다."], ["She became thin, and rough, and almost haggard: thin, till her cheek-bones were nearly pressing through her skin, till her elbows were sharp, and her finger-bones as those of a skeleton.", "그녀는 마르고 거칠어져 거의 파리해졌다. 광대뼈가 살갗을 뚫고 나올 지경으로, 팔꿈치가 뾰족해질 지경으로, 손가락뼈가 해골의 것처럼 될 지경으로 말랐다."], ["Her eye did not lose its lustre, but it became unnaturally bright, prominent, and too large for her wan face.", "눈은 빛을 잃지 않았으나 부자연스럽게 반짝이고 튀어나와, 핏기 없는 얼굴에 어울리지 않게 너무 커 보였다."], ["The soft brown locks which she had once loved to brush back, scorning, as she would boast to herself, to care that they should be seen, were now sparse enough and all untidy and unclean.", "한때는 뒤로 빗어 넘기기를 좋아하던, 남이 보든 말든 마음 쓰지 않는다고 스스로 자랑하곤 했던 그 부드러운 갈색 머리채는 이제 성글기도 하고 온통 헝클어지고 깨끗하지도 않았다."], ["It was matter of little thought now whether they were seen or no.", "그것이 남의 눈에 보이는지 아닌지는 이제 별로 생각할 거리가 아니었다."], ["Whether he could be made fit to go into his pulpit—whether they might be fed—those four innocents—and their backs kept from the cold wind—that was now the matter of her thought.", "그를 설교단에 올려보낼 만한 사람으로 만들 수 있을지 — 그 네 어린것들이 먹을 것은 있을지 — 그 등을 찬 바람에서 지켜 줄 수 있을지, 그것이 이제 그녀가 생각하는 거리였다."]], [["And then two of them died, and she went forth herself to see them laid under the frost-bound sod, lest he should faint in his work over their graves.", "그러고서 그 가운데 둘이 죽었고, 그가 아이들의 무덤 앞에서 자기 직무를 치르다 쓰러지지 않게 하려고 그녀가 스스로 나가 아이들을 얼어붙은 땅 아래 누이는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["For he would ask aid from no man—such at least was his boast through all.", "그는 어떤 사람에게도 도움을 청하려 하지 않았으니, 적어도 그것이 내내 그의 자랑이었다."]], [["Two of them died, but their illness had been long; and then debts came upon them.", "둘이 죽었으나 그 아이들의 병은 길었다. 그러고 나서 빚이 그들에게 닥쳐왔다."], ["Debt, indeed, had been creeping on them with slow but sure feet during the last five years.", "실은 빚은 지난 오 년 동안 느리지만 어김없는 걸음으로 그들에게 다가오고 있었다."], ["Who can see his children hungry, and not take bread if it be offered?", "자기 아이들이 굶는 것을 보면서 내미는 빵을 받지 않을 사람이 어디 있는가?"], ["Who can see his wife lying in sharpest want, and not seek a remedy if there be a remedy within reach?", "자기 아내가 사무치는 궁핍 속에 누워 있는 것을 보면서 손 닿는 데에 방도가 있다면 그 방도를 찾지 않을 사람이 어디 있는가?"], ["So debt had come upon them, and rude men pressed for small sums of money—for sums small to the world, but impossibly large to them.", "그리하여 빚이 그들에게 닥쳐왔고, 거친 사내들이 얼마 안 되는 돈을 내놓으라고 몰아댔다 — 세상에는 얼마 안 되나 그들에게는 도무지 갚을 수 없이 큰 액수였다."], ["And he would hide himself within there, in that cranny of an inner chamber—hide himself with deep shame from the world, with shame, and a sinking heart, and a broken spirit.", "그러면 그는 그 안쪽 방의 좁은 틈 같은 곳에 몸을 감추었다 — 깊은 부끄러움을 안고 세상으로부터 몸을 감추었으니, 부끄러움과 내려앉는 가슴과 부서진 정신을 안고서였다."]], [["But had such a man no friend? it will be said.", "그러나 그런 사람에게 벗이 하나도 없었단 말인가?"], ["Such men, I take it, do not make many friends.", "이런 말이 나올 것이다."], ["But this man was not utterly friendless.", "그런 사람들은 벗을 많이 사귀지 않는다고 나는 본다."], ["Almost every year one visit was paid to him in his Cornish curacy by a brother clergyman, an old college friend, who, as far as might in him lie, did give aid to the curate and his wife.", "그러나 이 사람이 아주 벗 없는 사람은 아니었다. 콘월의 부목사 자리에 있는 그를 거의 해마다 한 번씩 같은 성직자인 옛 대학 벗이 찾아왔고, 그 벗은 자기 힘이 미치는 한 이 부목사와 그 아내를 도와주었다."], ["This gentleman would take up his abode for a week at a farmer’s in the neighbourhood, and though he found Mr. Crawley in despair, he would leave him with some drops of comfort in his soul.", "이 신사는 이웃의 어느 농부 집에 한 주일씩 묵었는데, 크롤리 씨가 절망에 빠져 있는 것을 보고서도 떠날 때에는 그의 넋에 위로 몇 방울을 남겨 놓았다."], ["Nor were the benefits in this respect all on one side.", "이 점에서 이로움이 한쪽으로만 흐른 것도 아니었다."], ["Mr. Crawley, though at some periods weak enough for himself, could be strong for others; and, more than once, was strong to the great advantage of this man whom he loved.", "크롤리 씨는 어떤 때에는 자기 자신을 위해서는 그토록 약했으나 남을 위해서는 강할 수 있었고, 자기가 사랑하는 이 사람에게 크게 도움이 되도록 한 번 이상 강했다."], ["And then, too, pecuniary assistance was forthcoming—in those earlier years not in great amount, for this friend was not then among the rich ones of the earth—but in amount sufficient for that moderate hearth, if only its acceptance could have been managed.", "그리고 돈으로 하는 도움도 있었다 — 그 이른 시절에는 큰 액수는 아니었으니 이 벗도 그때는 세상의 부유한 사람들 축에 들지 못했기 때문이다 — 그러나 그 소박한 살림살이에는 넉넉한 액수였다. 그것을 받아들이게 하는 일만 어찌어찌 되었다면 말이다."], ["But in that matter there were difficulties without end.", "그런데 그 일에는 끝없는 어려움이 있었다. 노골적으로 내미는 돈이라면 크롤리 씨는 하나도 받지 않았다."], ["Of absolute money tenders Mr. Crawley would accept none.", "그래도 아내가 거들어 여기저기 셈은 치러졌다."], ["But a bill here and there was paid, the wife assisting; and shoes came for Kate—till Kate was placed beyond the need of shoes; and cloth for Harry and Frank found its way surreptitiously in beneath the cover of that wife’s solitary trunk—cloth with which those lean fingers worked garments for the two boys, to be worn—such was God’s will—only by the one.", "그리고 케이트에게 신이 왔다 — 케이트가 신이 필요 없는 곳으로 옮겨 가기까지는. 해리와 프랭크에게 줄 옷감은 그 아내의 하나뿐인 옷가방 뚜껑 아래로 슬며시 들어왔다 — 그 여윈 손가락이 그 옷감으로 두 사내아이의 옷을 지었으나, 하느님의 뜻이 그러하여 그 옷을 입은 것은 하나뿐이었다."]], [["Such were Mr. and Mrs.", "콘월의 부목사 자리에 있던 동안, 가장 혹독한 싸움을 치르던 동안 크롤리 씨 부부는 그러했다."], ["Crawley in their Cornish curacy, and during their severest struggles. To one who thinks that a fair day’s work is worth a fair day’s wages, it seems hard enough that a man should work so hard and receive so little.", "하루의 정당한 일이 하루의 정당한 품값만큼 값이 나간다고 여기는 사람에게는, 한 사람이 그토록 힘들게 일하고 그토록 적게 받았다는 것이 여간 모진 일로 보이지 않을 것이다."], ["There will be those who think that the fault was all his own in marrying so young.", "그렇게 젊어서 혼인한 것이 온전히 그의 잘못이라고 여기는 사람도 있을 것이다."], ["But still there remains that question, Is not a fair day’s work worth a fair day’s wages?", "그러나 그래도 그 물음은 남는다. 하루의 정당한 일은 하루의 정당한 품값만큼 값이 나가지 않는가?"], ["This man did work hard—at a task perhaps the hardest of any that a man may do; and for ten years he earned some seventy pounds a year.", "이 사람은 정말로 힘들게 일했다 — 사람이 할 수 있는 일 가운데 어쩌면 가장 힘든 일에 매달려서. 그리고 십 년 동안 한 해에 칠십 파운드쯤을 벌었다."], ["Will any one say that he received fair wages for his fair work, let him be married or single?", "혼인을 했든 홀몸이든, 그가 자기의 정당한 일에 대해 정당한 품값을 받았다고 말할 사람이 있겠는가?"], ["And yet there are so many who would fain pay their clergy, if they only knew how to apply their money!", "그런데도 자기 돈을 어디에 쓸지만 알면 자기네 성직자에게 기꺼이 값을 치를 사람이 그토록 많다!"], ["But that is a long subject, as Mr.", "그러나 그것은 긴 이야기다."], ["Robarts had told Miss Dunstable.", "로바츠 씨가 던스터블 양에게 말했듯이."]], [["Such was Mr. Crawley in his Cornish curacy.", "콘월의 부목사 자리에 있던 동안 크롤리 씨는 그러했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XV. LADY LUFTON’S AMBASSADOR.", "# 제15장. 레이디 러프턴의 사절"]], [["And then, in the days which followed, that friend of Mr. Crawley’s, whose name, by-the-by, is yet to be mentioned, received quick and great promotion.", "그리고 그 뒤로 이어진 날들에, 크롤리 씨의 그 벗은—그런데 그 이름은 아직 밝히지 않았다—빠르고 큰 승진을 얻었다."], ["Mr. Arabin by name he was then;—Dr.", "그때 그의 이름은 애러빈 씨였고, 그 빠르고 큰 승진이 절정에 이른 뒤로는 애러빈 박사가 되었다."], ["Arabin afterwards, when that quick and great promotion reached its climax. He had been simply a Fellow of Lazarus in those former years.", "그 지난 세월에 그는 다만 라자러스 칼리지의 펠로였을 뿐이다."], ["Then he became Vicar of St. Ewold’s, in East Barsetshire, and had not yet got himself settled there when he married the Widow Bold, a widow with belongings in land and funded money, and with but one small baby as an encumbrance.", "그러다 동 바셋주 세인트 이월드의 교구 사제가 되었고, 아직 거기에 자리를 잡기도 전에 미망인 볼드 부인과 결혼하였으니, 토지와 공채에 든 재산을 지녔고 걸림돌이라고는 어린 아기 하나뿐인 미망인이었다."], ["Nor had he even yet married her,—had only engaged himself so to do, when they made him Dean of Barchester—all which may be read in the diocesan and county chronicles.", "아니, 그때는 아직 그녀와 결혼한 것도 아니었고—다만 그러기로 약조만 해 둔 참이었는데, 사람들이 그를 바체스터 수석 사제로 올려세웠다. 이 모든 것은 교구와 주의 연대기에서 읽을 수 있는 일이다."]], [["And now that he was wealthy, the new dean did contrive to pay the debts of his poor friend, some lawyer of Camelford assisting him.", "그리고 이제 재산이 넉넉해진 새 수석 사제는 캐멀퍼드의 어느 변호사의 도움을 받아 가난한 벗의 빚을 갚아 주는 일을 꾀하였다."], ["It was but a paltry schedule after all, amounting in the total to something not much above a hundred pounds.", "결국 그것은 얼마 되지도 않는 목록이어서, 통틀어 백 파운드를 그다지 넘지 않는 액수였다."], ["And then, in the course of eighteen months, this poor piece of preferment fell in the dean’s way, this incumbency of Hogglestock with its stipend reaching one hundred and thirty pounds a year.", "그러고 나서 열여덟 달이 지나는 동안 이 초라한 성직 자리가 수석 사제의 손에 굴러들었으니, 한 해 백삼십 파운드에 이르는 급여가 딸린 호글스톡의 성직이었다."], ["Even that was worth double the Cornish curacy, and there was, moreover, a house attached to it.", "그것만 해도 콘월의 부목사직에 비하면 두 배 값어치였고, 게다가 딸린 집도 한 채 있었다."], ["Poor Mrs. Crawley, when she heard of it, thought that their struggles of poverty were now well nigh over.", "가엾은 크롤리 부인은 그 소식을 듣고서, 이제 가난과의 싸움도 거의 끝난 것이라고 생각하였다."], ["What might not be done with a hundred and thirty pounds by people who had lived for ten years on seventy?", "십 년을 칠십 파운드로 살아온 사람들이 백삼십 파운드로 못 할 일이 무엇이 있겠는가?"]], [["And so they moved away out of that cold, bleak country, carrying with them their humble household gods, and settled themselves in another country, cold and bleak also, but less terribly so than the former.", "그리하여 그들은 그 춥고 황량한 고장을 떠나, 소박한 살림살이의 수호신들을 이끌고서 또 다른 고장에 자리를 잡았으니, 그곳도 춥고 황량하기는 하였으나 앞의 고장보다는 그토록 심하지 않았다."], ["They settled themselves, and again began their struggles against man’s hardness and the devil’s zeal.", "그들은 자리를 잡고서 다시 사람의 매정함과 악마의 열성에 맞서는 싸움을 시작하였다."], ["I have said that Mr. Crawley was a stern, unpleasant man; and it certainly was so.", "나는 크롤리 씨가 딱딱하고 불쾌한 사람이었다고 말한 바 있는데, 과연 그러하였다."], ["The man must be made of very sterling stuff, whom continued and undeserved misfortune does not make unpleasant.", "끊이지 않는, 또 마땅히 받을 까닭도 없는 불운을 겪으면서도 불쾌해지지 않는 사람이라면 참으로 단단한 재목으로 지어진 사람이어야 한다."], ["This man had so far succumbed to grief, that it had left upon him its marks, palpable and not to be effaced.", "이 사람은 슬픔에 그만큼 굴복하여, 슬픔이 그에게 뚜렷하고 지워지지 않는 흔적을 남겨 놓았다."], ["He cared little for society, judging men to be doing evil who did care for it.", "그는 사교를 대수롭게 여기지 않았고, 그것을 대수롭게 여기는 사람들은 악을 행하는 것이라고 판단하였다."], ["He knew as a fact, and believed with all his heart, that these sorrows had come to him from the hand of God, and that they would work for his weal in the long run; but not the less did they make him morose, silent, and dogged.", "그는 이 슬픔들이 하느님의 손에서 온 것이며 결국에는 제 복을 위하여 작용할 것임을 사실로써 알았고 온 마음으로 믿었다. 그러나 그럼에도 그 슬픔들은 그를 음침하고 말없고 완고한 사람으로 만들었다."], ["He had always at his heart a feeling that he and his had been ill-used, and too often solaced himself, at the devil’s bidding, with the conviction that eternity would make equal that which life in this world had made so unequal;—the last bait that with which the devil angles after those who are struggling to elude his rod and line.", "그는 늘 마음속에 자기와 제 식구가 부당한 대접을 받아 왔다는 느낌을 품고 있었고, 너무나 자주 악마의 부추김을 받아, 이승의 삶이 그토록 고르지 못하게 만들어 놓은 것을 영원이 고르게 만들어 줄 것이라는 확신으로 스스로를 달랬으니—그것은 악마가 제 낚싯대와 낚싯줄을 벗어나려 몸부림치는 자들을 낚으려고 드리우는 마지막 미끼다."]], [["The Framley property did not run into the parish of Hogglestock; but nevertheless Lady Lufton did what she could in the way of kindness to these new-comers.", "프램리의 소유지는 호글스톡 교구까지 뻗어 있지 않았다. 그러나 그럼에도 레이디 러프턴은 이 새로 온 사람들에게 친절을 베푸는 쪽으로 할 수 있는 일을 다 하였다."], ["Providence had not supplied Hogglestock with a Lady Lufton, or with any substitute in the shape of lord or lady, squire or squiress.", "섭리는 호글스톡에 레이디 러프턴을 내려 주지 않았고, 경이든 부인이든 지주든 지주 부인이든 그런 이를 대신할 사람도 내려 주지 않았다."], ["The Hogglestock farmers, male and female, were a rude, rough set, not bordering in their social rank on the farmer gentle; and Lady Lufton, knowing this, and hearing something of these Crawleys from Mrs.", "호글스톡의 농부들은 남자든 여자든 거칠고 무례한 무리여서, 그 사회적 지위가 신사 축에 드는 농부에 닿지 못하였다."], ["Arabin, the dean’s wife, trimmed her lamps, so that they should shed a wider light, and pour forth some of their influence on that forlorn household.", "레이디 러프턴은 이것을 알고 있었고, 또 수석 사제의 부인인 애러빈 부인에게서 이 크롤리네 이야기를 얼마간 들었으므로, 제 등잔들의 심지를 돋우어 그 빛이 더 넓게 퍼지게 하고 그 힘의 얼마쯤을 저 쓸쓸한 집안에 쏟아 붓게 하였다."]], [["And as regards Mrs. Crawley, Lady Lufton by no means found that her work and good-will were thrown away.", "그리고 크롤리 부인에 관하여 말하자면, 레이디 러프턴은 제 수고와 호의가 헛되이 버려졌다고는 결코 여기지 않았다."], ["Mrs. Crawley accepted her kindness with thankfulness, and returned to some of the softnesses of life under her hand.", "크롤리 부인은 그 친절을 고맙게 받아들였고, 그 손길 아래에서 삶의 부드러움을 얼마간 되찾았다."], ["As for dining at Framley Court, that was out of the question.", "프램리 코트에서 만찬을 함께하는 일이야 어림도 없었다."], ["Mr. Crawley, she knew, would not hear of it, even if other things were fitting and appliances were at command.", "다른 사정들이 알맞고 갖출 것이 다 갖추어져 있다 하여도 크롤리 씨가 그런 말은 들으려 하지 않을 것임을 그녀는 알고 있었다."], ["Indeed Mrs. Crawley at once said that she felt herself unfit to go through such a ceremony with anything like comfort.", "실상 크롤리 부인 자신도 곧, 그런 격식을 조금이라도 편안한 마음으로 치러 낼 만한 사람이 못 된다고 느낀다고 말하였다."], ["The dean, she said, would talk of their going to stay at the deanery; but she thought it quite impossible that either of them should endure even that.", "수석 사제는 두 사람이 수석 사제 관저에 와서 머물자는 말을 하곤 한다고 그녀는 말하였다. 그러나 두 사람 가운데 어느 쪽도 그것조차 견뎌 내지는 못할 것이라고 여겼다."], ["But, all the same, Lady Lufton was a comfort to her; and the poor woman felt that it was well to have a lady near her in case of need.", "그러나 그래도 레이디 러프턴은 그녀에게 위로가 되었고, 그 가난한 여인은 어려울 때를 위하여 가까이에 부인 한 분이 있다는 것이 좋은 일이라고 느꼈다."]], [["The task was much harder with Mr. Crawley, but even with him it was not altogether unsuccessful.", "크롤리 씨에게는 그 일이 훨씬 더 어려웠으나, 그에게도 아주 성과가 없지는 않았다."], ["Lady Lufton talked to him of his parish and of her own; made Mark Robarts go to him, and by degrees did something towards civilizing him.", "레이디 러프턴은 그와 더불어 그의 교구와 제 교구의 이야기를 나누었고, 마크 로바츠를 그에게 보냈으며, 차츰차츰 그를 문명화하는 쪽으로 무언가를 이루어 냈다."], ["Between him and Robarts too there grew up an intimacy rather than a friendship.", "그와 로바츠 사이에도 우정이라기보다는 친밀함이 자라났다."], ["Robarts would submit to his opinion on matters of ecclesiastical and even theological law, would listen to him with patience, would agree with him where he could, and differ from him mildly when he could not.", "로바츠는 교회법이며 심지어 신학의 법에 관한 문제에서라면 그의 견해에 몸을 굽혔고, 참을성 있게 그의 말을 들었고, 동의할 수 있는 데서는 동의하였고, 동의할 수 없는 데서는 부드럽게 의견을 달리하였다."], ["For Robarts was a man who made himself pleasant to all men.", "로바츠는 누구에게나 자기를 상냥하게 보이는 사람이었으니 말이다."], ["And thus, under Lady Lufton’s wing, there grew up a connection between Framley and Hogglestock, in which Mrs. Robarts also assisted.", "그리하여 레이디 러프턴의 날개 아래에서 프램리와 호글스톡 사이에 하나의 연이 자라났고, 여기에는 로바츠 부인도 힘을 보탰다."]], [["And now that Lady Lufton was looking about her, to see how she might best bring proper clerical influence to bear upon her own recreant fox-hunting parson, it occurred to her that she might use Mr. Crawley in the matter.", "그리고 이제 레이디 러프턴이 주위를 둘러보며, 여우 사냥이나 하는 제 교구의 변절한 목사에게 어떻게 하면 온당한 성직자의 힘이 가장 잘 미치게 할 수 있을까를 살피고 있을 때, 이 일에 크롤리 씨를 쓰면 되겠다는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["Mr. Crawley would certainly be on her side as far as opinion went, and would have no fear as to expressing his opinion to his brother clergyman.", "크롤리 씨는 견해로 말하자면 틀림없이 제 편일 것이고, 그 견해를 동료 성직자에게 밝히기를 두려워하지도 않을 것이다."], ["So she sent for Mr. Crawley.", "그리하여 그녀는 크롤리 씨를 부르러 사람을 보냈다."]], [["In appearance he was the very opposite to Mark Robarts.", "겉모습으로 보자면 그는 마크 로바츠와 정반대였다."], ["He was a lean, slim, meagre man, with shoulders slightly curved, and pale, lank, long locks of ragged hair; his forehead was high, but his face was narrow; his small grey eyes were deeply sunken in his head, his nose was well-formed, his lips thin, and his mouth expressive.", "그는 마르고 가늘고 여윈 사람으로, 어깨가 조금 굽었고, 창백하고 축 처진 긴 머리채는 텁수룩하였다. 이마는 높았으나 얼굴은 좁았고, 작은 회색 눈은 머릿속으로 깊이 꺼져 있었으며, 코는 잘생겼고, 입술은 얇았고, 입에는 뜻이 어려 있었다."], ["Nobody could look at him without seeing that there was a purpose and a meaning in his countenance.", "그의 얼굴을 보고서 그 표정에 어떤 목적과 뜻이 담겨 있음을 알아채지 못할 사람은 없었다."], ["He always wore, in summer and winter, a long dusky gray coat, which buttoned close up to his neck and descended almost to his heels.", "그는 여름이나 겨울이나 늘 길고 흐릿한 회색 외투를 입었는데, 목까지 단추를 촘촘히 채워 올리고 자락은 발꿈치까지 내려왔다."], ["He was full six feet high, but being so slight in build, he looked as though he were taller.", "키는 꼬박 여섯 피트였으나, 몸이 하도 가늘어서 그보다 더 커 보였다."]], [["He came at once at Lady Lufton’s bidding, putting himself into the gig beside the servant, to whom he spoke no single word during the journey.", "그는 레이디 러프턴의 부름을 받고 곧 왔는데, 하인 옆에 나란히 이륜마차에 몸을 올려놓고서 오는 동안 그에게 한마디도 말을 걸지 않았다."], ["And the man, looking into his face, was struck with taciturnity.", "그리고 그 하인은 그의 얼굴을 들여다보고서 그 말없음에 놀랐다."], ["Now Mark Robarts would have talked with him the whole way from Hogglestock to Framley Court; discoursing partly as to horses and land, but partly also as to higher things.", "마크 로바츠라면 호글스톡에서 프램리 코트까지 오는 길 내내 그와 이야기를 나누었을 것이다. 한편으로는 말과 땅에 관하여, 그러나 또 한편으로는 더 높은 것들에 관하여 논하면서."]], [["And then Lady Lufton opened her mind and told her griefs to Mr. Crawley, urging, however, through the whole length of her narrative, that Mr.", "그러고 나서 레이디 러프턴은 크롤리 씨에게 속마음을 열고 제 근심을 털어놓았는데, 그러면서도 이야기 내내 로바츠 씨가 교구 성직자로서는 훌륭하다는 것을 힘주어 말하였다."], ["Robarts was an excellent parish clergyman,—“just such a clergyman in his church as I would wish him to be,”", "“교회 안에서의 그 사람은 바로 내가 바라는 그대로의 성직자예요.”"], ["she explained, with the view of saving herself from an expression of any of Mr. Crawley’s special ideas as to church teaching, and of confining him to the one subject-matter in hand; “but he got this living so young, Mr.", "그녀는 이렇게 설명하였으니, 교회의 가르침에 관한 크롤리 씨의 남다른 생각이 튀어나오는 것을 막고 그를 지금 다루려는 한 가지 사안에만 붙들어 두기 위함이었다."], ["Crawley, that he is hardly quite as steady as I could wish him to be.", "“하지만 그 사람이 이 성직록을 너무 젊어서 얻었어요, 크롤리 씨."], ["It has been as much my fault as his own in placing him in such a position so early in life.”", "그래서 내가 바라는 만큼 무겁게 처신하지는 못하고 있어요. 그이를 그렇게 이른 나이에 그런 자리에 앉힌 것은 그이의 잘못이나 다름없이 내 잘못이기도 해요.”"]], [["“I think it has,” said Mr.", "“그렇다고 생각합니다.”"], ["Crawley, who might perhaps be a little sore on such a subject.", "크롤리 씨가 말하였는데, 그런 주제에는 아마 조금 속이 쓰렸을 것이다."]], [["“Quite so, quite so,”", "“그렇지요, 그렇지요.”"], ["continued her ladyship, swallowing down with a gulp a certain sense of anger.", "마님은 어떤 노여움을 꿀꺽 삼키며 말을 이었다. “하지만 그건 이미 지나간 일이고 이제 어쩔 수 없는 일이에요."], ["“But that is done now, and is past cure. That Mr.", "로바츠 씨가 자기 직분에 자랑거리가 될 사람이 되리라는 것은 나도 의심하지 않아요."], ["Robarts will become a credit to his profession, I do not doubt, for his heart is in the right place and his sentiments are good; but I fear that at present he is succumbing to temptation.”", "그이의 마음은 제자리에 있고 그이의 심정은 착하니까요. 그렇지만 지금 그이가 유혹에 굴복하고 있는 것이 아닌가 나는 걱정이에요.”"]], [["“I am told that he hunts two or three times a week.", "“한 주에 두세 번씩 사냥을 나간다고 저는 들었습니다."], ["Everybody round us is talking about it.”", "우리 주위에서는 누구나 그 이야기를 하고 있습니다.”"]], [["“No, Mr.", "“아니에요, 크롤리 씨."], ["Crawley; not two or three times a week; very seldom above once, I think.", "한 주에 두세 번은 아니에요. 한 번을 넘는 일은 아주 드물다고 나는 생각해요."], ["And then I do believe he does it more with the view of being with Lord Lufton than anything else.”", "그리고 나는 그이가 무엇보다도 러프턴 경과 함께 있으려는 뜻으로 그러는 것이라고 정말로 믿어요.”"]], [["“I cannot see that that would make the matter better,”", "“그것이 이 일을 더 낫게 만들어 준다고는 보이지 않습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Crawley.", "크롤리 씨가 말하였다."]], [["“It would show that he was not strongly imbued with a taste which I cannot but regard as vicious in a clergyman.”", "“그것은 그이가, 내가 성직자에게는 악덕이라고 여기지 않을 수 없는 그 취미에 깊이 물들어 있지는 않다는 것을 보여 주는 셈이지요.”"]], [["“It must be vicious in all men,”", "“그것은 모든 사람에게 악덕일 것입니다.”"], ["said Mr. Crawley.", "크롤리 씨가 말하였다."], ["“It is in itself cruel, and leads to idleness and profligacy.”", "“그것은 그 자체로 잔인하며, 게으름과 방탕으로 이끕니다.”"]], [["Again Lady Lufton made a gulp. She had called Mr.", "레이디 러프턴은 다시 한 번 꿀꺽 삼켰다."], ["Crawley thither to her aid, and felt that it would be inexpedient to quarrel with him.", "그녀는 크롤리 씨를 제 도움으로 삼으려고 그곳에 불렀으니, 그와 다투는 것은 이롭지 못하리라고 느꼈다."], ["But she did not like to be told that her son’s amusement was idle and profligate.", "그러나 제 아들의 소일거리가 게으르고 방탕하다는 말을 듣는 것은 좋지 않았다."], ["She had always regarded hunting as a proper pursuit for a country gentleman.", "그녀는 사냥이란 늘 시골 신사에게 알맞은 소일거리라고 여겨 왔다."], ["It was, indeed, in her eyes one of the peculiar institutions of country life in England, and it may be almost said that she looked upon the Barsetshire hunt as something sacred.", "실로 그녀의 눈에 그것은 영국 시골 생활의 남다른 제도 가운데 하나였으며, 그녀가 바셋주 사냥회를 무언가 신성한 것으로 보았다고 해도 거의 틀린 말이 아니었다."], ["She could not endure to hear that a fox was trapped, and allowed her turkeys to be purloined without a groan.", "그녀는 여우가 덫에 걸렸다는 이야기는 견디지 못하였고, 제 칠면조를 슬쩍 물어 가도 신음 소리 한 번 내지 않고 내주었다."], ["Such being the case, she did not like being told that it was vicious, and had by no means wished to consult Mr. Crawley on that matter.", "사정이 그러하니, 그것이 악덕이라는 말을 듣는 것이 좋지 않았고, 크롤리 씨에게 그 문제를 두고 의논할 마음은 조금도 없었다."], ["But nevertheless, she swallowed down her wrath.", "그러나 그럼에도 그녀는 노여움을 꿀꺽 삼켰다."]], [["“It is at any rate unbecoming in a clergyman,”", "“그것은 어쨌든 성직자에게 어울리지 않는 일이에요.”"], ["she said; “and as I know that Mr.", "그녀가 말하였다."], ["Robarts places a high value on your opinion, perhaps you will not object to advise him to discontinue it.", "“그리고 로바츠 씨가 선생의 견해를 높이 여기는 것을 내가 알고 있으니, 그이에게 그 일을 그만두라고 권해 주시는 것을 마다하지는 않으시겠지요."], ["He might possibly feel aggrieved were I to interfere personally on such a question.”", "그런 문제에 내가 몸소 끼어들면 그이가 서운하게 여길지도 모르겠어요.”"]], [["“I have no doubt he would,”", "“서운하게 여길 것이 틀림없습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Crawley.", "크롤리 씨가 말하였다."], ["“It is not within a woman’s province to give counsel to a clergyman on such a subject, unless she be very near and very dear to him—his wife, or mother, or sister.”", "“그런 주제로 성직자에게 충고를 하는 것은 여인의 소관이 아닙니다. 그 여인이 그에게 아주 가깝고 아주 소중한 사람이 아니라면 말입니다—아내나 어머니나 누이라면 모르되.”"]], [["“As living in the same parish, you know, and being, perhaps—”", "“같은 교구에 살고 있고, 또 어쩌면—”"], ["the leading person in it, and the one who naturally rules the others.", "그 교구에서 가장 앞자리에 있는 사람이며 자연히 다른 이들을 다스리는 사람이라고."], ["Those would have been the fitting words for the expression of her ladyship’s ideas; but she remembered herself, and did not use them.", "마님의 생각을 나타내기에 알맞은 말은 그런 것이었으리라. 그러나 그녀는 스스로를 돌아보고 그 말을 쓰지 않았다."], ["She had made up her mind that, great as her influence ought to be, she was not the proper person to speak to Mr.", "제 힘이 아무리 크게 미쳐야 마땅하다 하여도, 로바츠 씨에게 그의 해롭고 성직자답지 못한 습관을 두고 말할 알맞은 사람은 자기가 아니라고 그녀는 마음을 정해 둔 터였다."], ["Robarts as to his pernicious, unclerical habits, and she would not now depart from her resolve by attempting to prove that she was the proper person.", "그러니 이제 자기가 알맞은 사람임을 증명하려 들어 그 결심에서 벗어나려 하지는 않았다."]], [["“Yes,” said Mr.", "“그렇습니다.”"], ["Crawley, “just so.", "크롤리 씨가 말하였다."], ["All that would entitle him to offer you his counsel if he thought that your mode of life was such as to require it, but could by no means justify you in addressing yourself to him.”", "“바로 그러합니다. 그만한 사정이라면, 그가 부인의 생활 방식이 충고를 요할 만하다고 여길 때 부인께 충고를 드릴 자격은 그에게 주겠습니다만, 부인께서 그에게 말을 붙이시는 일을 정당하게 만들어 주지는 결코 못합니다.”"]], [["This was very hard upon Lady Lufton.", "이것은 레이디 러프턴에게 몹시 가혹한 일이었다."], ["She was endeavouring with all her woman’s strength to do her best, and endeavouring so to do it that the feelings of the sinner might be spared; and yet the ghostly comforter whom she had evoked to her aid, treated her as though she were arrogant and overbearing.", "그녀는 여인의 힘을 다하여 최선을 다하려 애쓰고 있었고, 또 그리하면서도 죄지은 이의 마음이 상하지 않도록 애쓰고 있었다. 그런데도 도움을 청하여 불러낸 영적 위로자가 그녀를 마치 오만하고 남을 눌러 대는 사람인 양 대하는 것이었다."], ["She acknowledged the weakness of her own position with reference to her parish clergyman by calling in the aid of Mr.", "그녀는 크롤리 씨의 도움을 청함으로써 제 교구 성직자를 대하는 제 처지의 약함을 스스로 인정한 셈이었다."], ["Crawley; and under such circumstances, he might, at any rate, have abstained from throwing that weakness in her teeth.", "그런 사정이라면 그도 적어도 그 약함을 대놓고 면박하는 짓만은 삼갈 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["“Well, sir; I hope my mode of life may not require it; but that is not exactly to the point: what I wish to know is, whether you will speak to Mr.", "“글쎄요, 선생. 내 생활 방식이 그런 것을 요하지 않기를 바라요."], ["Robarts?”", "그렇지만 그것은 지금 이야기의 요점이 아니에요. 내가 알고 싶은 것은, 선생이 로바츠 씨에게 말해 주실 것인가 하는 것이에요.”"]], [["“Certainly I will,”", "“물론 말하겠습니다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말하였다."]], [["“Then I shall be much obliged to you. But, Mr.", "“그러면 참으로 고맙겠어요."], ["Crawley, pray—pray, remember this: I would not on any account wish that you should be harsh with him.", "하지만, 크롤리 씨, 부디—부디 이것만은 잊지 말아 주세요. 나는 무슨 일이 있어도 선생이 그이에게 가혹하게 대하시기를 바라지 않아요."], ["He is an excellent young man, and—”", "그이는 훌륭한 젊은이고, 또—”"]], [["“Lady Lufton, if I do this, I can only do it in my own way, as best I may, using such words as God may give me at the time.", "“레이디 러프턴, 제가 이 일을 한다면 제 방식대로, 제가 할 수 있는 만큼, 그때 하느님께서 제게 주시는 말로써 할 수밖에 없습니다."], ["I hope that I am harsh to no man; but it is worse than useless, in all cases, to speak anything but the truth.”", "저는 어느 누구에게도 가혹하지 않기를 바랍니다. 그러나 어떤 경우에든 진실 아닌 것을 말하는 것은 쓸데없기보다 더 나쁜 일입니다.”"]], [["“Of course—of course.”", "“물론이지요—물론이지요.”"]], [["“If the ears be too delicate to hear the truth, the mind will be too perverse to profit by it.”", "“귀가 너무 여려서 진실을 듣지 못한다면, 그 마음은 너무 뒤틀려서 진실로 이로움을 얻지 못할 것입니다.”"], ["And then Mr. Crawley got up to take his leave.", "그러고 나서 크롤리 씨는 물러가려고 일어섰다."]], [["But Lady Lufton insisted that he should go with her to luncheon.", "그러나 레이디 러프턴은 그가 자기와 함께 점심을 먹어야 한다고 우겼다."], ["He hummed and ha’d and would fain have refused, but on this subject she was peremptory.", "그는 우물쭈물 어물어물하며 사양하고 싶어 하였으나, 이 문제에 있어서는 그녀가 단호하였다."], ["It might be that she was unfit to advise a clergyman as to his duties, but in a matter of hospitality she did know what she was about.", "성직자에게 그 직분을 두고 충고할 만한 사람은 못 될지 몰라도, 손을 대접하는 문제라면 그녀는 자기가 무엇을 하는지 알고 있었다."], ["Mr. Crawley should not leave the house without refreshment.", "크롤리 씨가 요기도 하지 않고 이 집을 떠나서는 안 되었다."], ["As to this, she carried her point; and Mr.", "이 일에서는 그녀가 뜻을 이루었다."], ["Crawley—when the matter before him was cold roast-beef and hot potatoes, instead of the relative position of a parish priest and his parishioner—became humble, submissive, and almost timid.", "그리고 크롤리 씨는—앞에 놓인 것이 교구 사제와 그 교구민의 상대적 지위가 아니라 차게 식힌 구운 소고기와 뜨거운 감자가 되고 나니—겸손하고 순순하며 거의 수줍은 사람이 되었다."], ["Lady Lufton recommended Madeira instead of Sherry, and Mr. Crawley obeyed at once, and was, indeed, perfectly unconscious of the difference.", "레이디 러프턴이 셰리 대신 마데이라를 권하니 크롤리 씨는 이내 그대로 따랐고, 실은 그 차이를 조금도 알아채지 못하였다."], ["Then there was a basket of seakale in the gig for Mrs.", "그러고 나서 이륜마차에는 크롤리 부인에게 보내는 갯양배추 한 바구니가 놓였다."], ["Crawley; that he would have left behind had he dared, but he did not dare.", "그것은 그가 감히 그럴 수 있었다면 두고 갔을 것이나, 감히 그러지 못하였다."], ["Not a word was said to him as to the marmalade for the children which was hidden under the seakale, Lady Lufton feeling well aware that that would find its way to its proper destination without any necessity for his co-operation.", "갯양배추 아래에 숨겨 놓은 아이들 몫의 마멀레이드에 대해서는 그에게 한마디도 하지 않았으니, 그것이야 그가 거들지 않아도 제 갈 곳으로 찾아갈 것임을 레이디 러프턴이 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["And then Mr. Crawley returned home in the Framley Court gig.", "그리하여 크롤리 씨는 프램리 코트의 이륜마차를 타고 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Three or four days after this he walked over to Framley Parsonage.", "이 일이 있은 지 사나흘 뒤에 그는 프램리 목사관까지 걸어갔다."], ["This he did on a Saturday, having learned that the hounds never hunted on that day; and he started early, so that he might be sure to catch Mr.", "그날을 토요일로 잡은 것은 사냥개들이 그날은 결코 사냥을 나가지 않는다는 것을 알아 두었기 때문이다."], ["Robarts before he went out on his parish business.", "그리고 로바츠 씨가 교구 일을 보러 나가기 전에 꼭 붙잡을 수 있도록 일찍 나섰다."], ["He was quite early enough to attain this object, for when he reached the parsonage door at about half-past nine, the vicar, with his wife and sister, were just sitting down to breakfast.", "이 목적을 이루기에는 넉넉히 이른 시각이었으니, 아홉 시 반쯤에 목사관 문에 이르렀을 때 교구 사제는 아내와 누이와 더불어 막 아침 식탁에 앉는 참이었다."]], [["“Oh, Crawley,”", "“아니 크롤리,”"], ["said Robarts, before the other had well spoken, “you are a capital fellow;”", "상대가 미처 입을 열기도 전에 로바츠가 말하였다. “참 훌륭한 분이에요.”"], ["and then he got him into a chair, and Mrs. Robarts had poured him out tea, and Lucy had surrendered to him a knife and plate, before he knew under what guise to excuse his coming among them.", "그러고서 그는 그를 의자에 앉혔고, 로바츠 부인이 그에게 차를 따라 주었고, 루시가 나이프와 접시를 그에게 내주었으니, 그가 무슨 명목으로 이 자리에 온 것을 변명해야 할지 미처 알아내기도 전이었다."]], [["“I hope you will excuse this intrusion,”", "“이렇게 불쑥 찾아온 것을 용서해 주시기 바랍니다.”"], ["at last he muttered; “but I have a few words of business to which I will request your attention presently.”", "마침내 그가 나직이 중얼거렸다. “그러나 이따가 잠시 귀를 기울여 주셨으면 하는 일 이야기가 몇 마디 있습니다.”"]], [["“Certainly,”", "“물론이지요.”"], ["said Robarts, conveying a broiled kidney on to the plate before Mr.", "로바츠가 크롤리 씨 앞의 접시에 구운 콩팥을 옮겨 놓으며 말하였다."], ["Crawley; “but there is no preparation for business like a good breakfast.", "“하지만 일 이야기를 하는 데에 좋은 아침 식사만 한 준비가 없어요."], ["Lucy, hand Mr. Crawley the buttered toast.", "루시, 크롤리 씨에게 버터 바른 토스트를 드려라."], ["Eggs, Fanny; where are the eggs?”", "달걀, 패니. 달걀은 어디 있지?”"], ["And then John, in livery, brought in the fresh eggs.", "그러자 제복을 입은 존이 새로 삶은 달걀을 들여왔다."], ["“Now we shall do.", "“이제 되었군."], ["I always eat my eggs while they’re hot, Crawley, and I advise you to do the same.”", "나는 달걀을 늘 뜨거울 때 먹어요, 크롤리. 당신도 그렇게 하기를 권해요.”"]], [["To all this Mr. Crawley said very little, and he was not at all at home under the circumstances.", "이 모든 말에 크롤리 씨는 거의 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 이런 자리에서 조금도 편안하지 못하였다."], ["Perhaps a thought did pass across his brain, as to the difference between the meal which he had left on his own table, and that which he now saw before him; and as to any cause which might exist for such difference.", "어쩌면 자기 식탁에 두고 온 식사와 지금 눈앞에 보이는 식사의 차이, 그리고 그런 차이가 생겨난 까닭이 무엇일까 하는 한 가닥 생각이 그의 머릿속을 지나갔을지도 모른다."], ["But, if so, it was a very fleeting thought, for he had far other matter now fully occupying his mind.", "그러나 그렇다 하여도 그것은 아주 잠깐 스쳐 가는 생각이었으니, 이제 그의 마음을 온통 차지하고 있는 것은 다른 문제였다."], ["And then the breakfast was over, and in a few minutes the two clergymen found themselves together in the parsonage study.", "그러고 나서 아침 식사가 끝났고, 몇 분 뒤에 두 성직자는 목사관 서재에 마주 앉게 되었다."]], [["“Mr.", "“로바츠 씨.”"], ["Robarts,”", "나이가 위인 쪽이 책이 잘 채워진 서재 탁자 저쪽 편의 보통 의자 하나에 불편하게 자리를 잡고서 말을 시작하였다."], ["began the senior, when he had seated himself uncomfortably on one of the ordinary chairs at the farther side of the well-stored library table, while Mark was sitting at his ease in his own arm-chair by the fire, “I have called upon you on an unpleasant business.”", "한편 마크는 난롯가 제 안락의자에 편하게 앉아 있었다. “저는 불쾌한 일로 당신을 찾아왔습니다.”"]], [["Mark’s mind immediately flew off to Mr. Sowerby’s bill, but he could not think it possible that Mr. Crawley could have had anything to do with that.", "마크의 마음은 곧 사워비 씨의 어음으로 날아갔으나, 크롤리 씨가 그 일과 무슨 관계가 있을 수 있다고는 생각할 수 없었다."]], [["“But as a brother clergyman, and as one who esteems you much and wishes you well, I have thought myself bound to take this matter in hand.”", "“그러나 동료 성직자로서, 또 당신을 크게 존중하고 당신이 잘되기를 바라는 사람으로서, 저는 이 일을 손에 쥐어야 한다고 생각하였습니다.”"]], [["“What matter is it, Crawley?”", "“무슨 일이에요, 크롤리?”"]], [["“Mr. Robarts, men say that your present mode of life is one that is not befitting a soldier in Christ’s army.”", "“로바츠 씨, 사람들이 당신의 지금 생활 방식은 그리스도의 군대의 병사에게 어울리지 않는다고 말합니다.”"]], [["“Men say so!", "“사람들이 그렇게 말한다고요!"], ["what men?”", "어떤 사람들이 말입니까?”"]], [["“The men around you, of your own neighbourhood; those who watch your life, and know all your doings; those who look to see you walking as a lamp to guide their feet, but find you consorting with horse-jockeys and hunters, galloping after hounds, and taking your place among the vainest of worldly pleasure-seekers.", "“당신 주위 사람들, 당신과 한 이웃에 사는 사람들입니다. 당신의 삶을 지켜보며 당신이 하는 일을 다 아는 사람들, 당신이 제 발을 밝혀 줄 등불처럼 걸어가는 것을 보려고 우러러보다가, 정작 말 거간꾼과 사냥꾼들과 어울리고 사냥개를 뒤쫓아 말을 달리며 세상 낙을 좇는 가장 헛된 자들 사이에 자리를 잡고 있는 것을 보게 되는 사람들입니다."], ["Those who have a right to expect an example of good living, and who think that they do not see it.”", "착한 삶의 본을 기대할 권리가 있는데도 그것을 보지 못한다고 여기는 사람들입니다.”"]], [["Mr. Crawley had gone at once to the root of the matter, and in doing so had certainly made his own task so much the easier.", "크롤리 씨는 곧바로 일의 뿌리로 들어갔고, 그리함으로써 자기가 할 일을 그만큼 더 쉽게 만든 것이 분명하였다."], ["There is nothing like going to the root of the matter at once when one has on hand an unpleasant piece of business.", "불쾌한 일거리를 손에 쥐었을 때에는 곧바로 일의 뿌리로 들어가는 것만 한 것이 없다."]], [["“And have such men deputed you to come here?”", "“그런 사람들이 당신을 대리로 여기 보낸 것입니까?”"]], [["“No one has or could depute me.", "“아무도 저를 대리로 보내지 않았고, 또 보낼 수도 없습니다."], ["I have come to speak my own mind, not that of any other.", "저는 다른 누구의 마음이 아니라 제 마음을 말하러 왔습니다."], ["But I refer to what those around you think and say, because it is to them that your duties are due.", "그러나 제가 당신 주위 사람들이 생각하고 말하는 것을 끌어대는 것은, 당신의 직분이 바로 그들에게 돌아가야 할 것이기 때문입니다."], ["You owe it to those around you to live a godly, cleanly life;—as you owe it also, in a much higher way, to your Father who is in heaven.", "당신은 주위 사람들에게 경건하고 깨끗한 삶을 살아야 할 빚을 지고 있습니다. 또한 훨씬 더 높은 뜻으로는 하늘에 계신 당신의 아버지께도 그 빚을 지고 있습니다."], ["I now make bold to ask you whether you are doing your best to lead such a life as that?”", "이제 저는 감히 묻겠습니다. 당신은 그러한 삶을 살려고 최선을 다하고 있습니까?”"], ["And then he remained silent, waiting for an answer.", "그러고 나서 그는 대답을 기다리며 말없이 있었다."]], [["He was a singular man; so humble and meek, so unutterably inefficient and awkward in the ordinary intercourse of life, but so bold and enterprising, almost eloquent, on the one subject which was the work of his mind!", "그는 남다른 사람이었다. 그토록 겸손하고 온순하며, 세상의 흔한 사귐에서는 말할 수 없이 무능하고 서툴렀으나, 그의 마음이 매달려 있는 그 한 가지 주제에서는 그토록 담대하고 진취적이며 거의 웅변에 이르렀으니!"], ["As he sat there, he looked into his companion’s face from out his sunken grey eyes with a gaze which made his victim quail.", "그는 그 자리에 앉아, 꺼져 든 회색 눈으로 상대의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 그 눈길에 그 희생자는 움츠러들었다."], ["And then repeated his words: “I now make bold to ask you, Mr.", "그러고 나서 그는 제 말을 되풀이하였다."], ["Robarts, whether you are doing your best to lead such a life as may become a parish clergyman among his parishioners?”", "“이제 저는 감히 묻겠습니다, 로바츠 씨. 당신은 교구민들 가운데 있는 교구 성직자에게 어울릴 만한 삶을 살려고 최선을 다하고 있습니까?”"], ["And again he paused for an answer.", "그리고 그는 다시 대답을 기다리며 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“There are but few of us,”", "“그 물음에 그렇다고 마음 놓고 대답할 수 있는 사람은 우리 가운데 몇 없을 것입니다.”"], ["said Mark in a low tone, “who could safely answer that question in the affirmative.”", "마크가 나직한 목소리로 말하였다."]], [["“But are there many, think you, among us who would find the question so unanswerable as yourself?", "“그러나 우리 가운데 그 물음에 당신만큼 대답을 못 하는 사람이 많다고 생각하십니까?"], ["And even were there many, would you, young, enterprising, and talented as you are, be content to be numbered among them?", "또 많다 하여도, 당신처럼 젊고 진취적이며 재능 있는 사람이 그런 무리에 함께 헤아려지는 것으로 만족하겠습니까?"], ["Are you satisfied to be a castaway after you have taken upon yourself Christ’s armour?", "그리스도의 갑주를 몸에 걸치고 나서 버림받은 자가 되는 것으로 만족하십니까?"], ["If you will say so, I am mistaken in you, and will go my way.”", "그렇다고 말씀하신다면 제가 당신을 잘못 본 것이니, 저는 제 길을 가겠습니다.”"], ["There was again a pause, and then he went on.", "다시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그러고 나서 그는 말을 이었다."], ["“Speak to me, my brother, and open your heart if it be possible.”", "“제게 말해 주십시오, 형제여. 그리고 될 수 있다면 마음을 열어 주십시오.”"], ["And rising from his chair, he walked across the room, and laid his hand tenderly on Mark’s shoulder.", "그리고 의자에서 일어나 방을 건너와 마크의 어깨에 다정하게 손을 얹었다."]], [["Mark had been sitting lounging in his chair, and had at first, for a moment only, thought to brazen it out.", "마크는 의자에 기대어 앉아 있었고, 처음에는 잠깐 동안만 뻔뻔하게 밀고 나가 볼까 생각하였다."], ["But all idea of brazening had now left him.", "그러나 뻔뻔하게 버틸 생각은 이제 온통 그를 떠났다."], ["He had raised himself from his comfortable ease, and was leaning forward with his elbow on the table; but now, when he heard these words, he allowed his head to sink upon his arms, and he buried his face between his hands.", "그는 편안한 자리에서 몸을 일으켜 탁자에 팔꿈치를 짚고 앞으로 기울여 앉아 있었으나, 이 말을 듣고 나서는 두 팔 위로 고개를 떨어뜨리고 두 손 사이에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["“It is a terrible falling off,”", "“이것은 무서운 타락입니다.”"], ["continued Crawley: “terrible in the fall, but doubly terrible through that difficulty of returning.", "크롤리가 말을 이었다. “떨어지는 것이 무섭고, 돌아오기가 그토록 어려우니 곱절로 무섭습니다."], ["But it cannot be that it should content you to place yourself as one among those thoughtless sinners, for the crushing of whose sin you have been placed here among them.", "그러나 저 생각 없는 죄인들 사이에 스스로를 하나로 놓아두는 것이 당신에게 만족스러울 수는 없습니다. 당신은 바로 그들의 죄를 짓밟기 위하여 그들 가운데 세워진 사람입니다."], ["You become a hunting parson, and ride with a happy mind among blasphemers and mocking devils—you, whose aspirations were so high, who have spoken so often and so well of the duties of a minister of Christ; you, who can argue in your pride as to the petty details of your Church, as though the broad teachings of its great and simple lessons were not enough for your energies!", "당신은 사냥하는 목사가 되어, 하느님을 모욕하는 자들과 비웃는 마귀들 사이에서 기쁜 마음으로 말을 달립니다—그토록 뜻이 높았던 당신이, 그리스도의 종의 직분을 그토록 자주 그토록 훌륭히 말하던 당신이, 제 교회의 자잘한 세목을 두고 자랑스럽게 논할 수 있는 당신이, 마치 그 교회의 크고 단순한 가르침의 넓음만으로는 당신의 정력에 모자라기라도 하다는 듯이 말입니다!"], ["It cannot be that I have had a hypocrite beside me in all those eager controversies!”", "그 뜨거운 논쟁들 내내 제 옆에 위선자가 있었을 리 없습니다!”"]], [["“Not a hypocrite—not a hypocrite,”", "“위선자는 아닙니다—위선자는 아닙니다.”"], ["said Mark, in a tone which was almost reduced to sobbing.", "마크가 거의 흐느끼는 데까지 내려앉은 목소리로 말하였다."]], [["“But a castaway!", "“그러나 버림받은 자입니다!"], ["Is it so that I must call you? No, Mr.", "제가 당신을 그렇게 불러야 하겠습니까?"], ["Robarts, not a castaway; neither a hypocrite, nor a castaway; but one who in walking has stumbled in the dark and bruised his feet among the stones.", "아닙니다, 로바츠 씨, 버림받은 자가 아닙니다. 위선자도 버림받은 자도 아니고, 걸어가다가 어둠 속에서 발을 헛디뎌 돌에 발이 상한 사람입니다."], ["Henceforth let him take a lantern in his hand, and look warily to his path, and walk cautiously among the thorns and rocks,—cautiously, but yet boldly, with manly courage, but Christian meekness, as all men should walk on their pilgrimage through this vale of tears.”", "이제부터 그가 손에 등불을 들고 제 길을 조심스럽게 살피며 가시덤불과 바위 사이를 삼가 걸어가게 하십시오—삼가면서도 담대하게, 사내다운 용기로, 그러나 그리스도인의 온순함으로, 모든 사람이 이 눈물의 골짜기를 지나는 순례길에서 마땅히 걸어가야 하는 대로 말입니다.”"], ["And then without giving his companion time to stop him he hurried out of the room, and from the house, and without again seeing any others of the family, stalked back on his road to Hogglestock, thus tramping fourteen miles through the deep mud in performance of the mission on which he had been sent.", "그러고 나서 상대가 그를 붙들 틈도 주지 않고 그는 방에서, 그리고 그 집에서 서둘러 나갔고, 그 집안의 다른 사람들을 다시 보지도 않은 채 호글스톡으로 돌아가는 길을 성큼성큼 걸어갔으니, 자기가 받아 온 소임을 다하느라 이렇게 깊은 진흙을 십사 마일이나 터벅터벅 걸은 것이었다."]], [["It was some hours before Mr. Robarts left his room.", "로바츠 씨가 제 방을 나온 것은 몇 시간이 지나서였다."], ["As soon as he found that Crawley was really gone, and that he should see him no more, he turned the lock of his door, and sat himself down to think over his present life.", "크롤리가 정말로 가 버려서 다시 그를 볼 일이 없다는 것을 알자마자, 그는 방문을 잠그고 앉아 제 지금의 삶을 곰곰이 생각하였다."], ["At about eleven his wife knocked, not knowing whether that other strange clergyman were there or no, for none had seen his departure.", "열한 시쯤에 아내가 문을 두드렸는데, 그가 떠나는 것을 아무도 보지 못하였으므로 그 낯설고 이상한 성직자가 아직 거기 있는지 없는지 몰랐던 것이다."], ["But Mark, answering cheerily, desired that he might be left to his studies.", "그러나 마크는 명랑하게 대답하며, 공부하도록 자기를 혼자 두어 달라고 하였다."]], [["Let us hope that his thoughts and mental resolves were then of service to him.", "그때 그가 한 생각과 마음속의 결심이 그에게 도움이 되었기를 바라자."]], [["# CHAPTER XVI. MRS. PODGENS’ BABY.", "# 제16장. 포진스 부인의 아기"]], [["The hunting season had now nearly passed away, and the great ones of the Barsetshire world were thinking of the glories of London.", "사냥철은 이제 거의 다 지나갔고, 바셋주 세계의 높은 분들은 런던의 영광을 생각하고 있었다."], ["Of these glories Lady Lufton always thought with much inquietude of mind.", "그 영광을 두고 레이디 러프턴은 언제나 마음이 여간 편치 않았다."], ["She would fain have remained throughout the whole year at Framley Court, did not certain grave considerations render such a course on her part improper in her own estimation.", "그분으로서는 한 해를 통째로 프램리 코트에 머물러 있고 싶었다. 다만 어떤 중대한 고려들이, 그런 처사는 스스로 보기에 온당치 못하다고 일러 주었을 뿐이다."], ["All the Lady Luftons of whom she had heard, dowager and ante-dowager, had always had their seasons in London, till old age had incapacitated them for such doings—sometimes for clearly long after the arrival of such period.", "그분이 들어 온 역대 레이디 러프턴들은, 미망인 마님이든 그 이전 대의 마님이든, 늙어서 그런 일을 감당할 수 없게 될 때까지는 언제나 런던에서 사교철을 보냈다. 아니, 더러는 그 시기가 이미 한참 지난 뒤까지도 그러했다."], ["And then she had an idea, perhaps not altogether erroneous, that she annually imported back with her into the country somewhat of the passing civilization of the times:—may we not say an idea that certainly was not erroneous?", "게다가 그분에게는, 어쩌면 아주 틀린 것도 아닌 생각이 하나 있었다. 자기가 해마다 그 시대의 흘러가는 문명을 조금씩 시골로 실어 들여 온다는 생각이었다."], ["for how otherwise is it that the forms of new caps and remodelled shapes for women’s waists find their way down into agricultural parts, and that the rural eye learns to appreciate grace and beauty?", "아니, 확실히 틀리지 않은 생각이라고 말해도 되지 않을까? 그렇지 않다면 어떻게 새로 나온 모자의 모양이며 여인들의 허리를 새로 고친 선이 농사 짓는 지방까지 내려오고, 시골 사람의 눈이 우아함과 아름다움을 알아보는 법을 배우겠는가?"], ["There are those who think that remodelled waists and new caps had better be kept to the towns; but such people, if they would follow out their own argument, would wish to see ploughboys painted with ruddle and milkmaids covered with skins.", "새로 고친 허리선이며 새 모자 같은 것은 도시에나 두는 편이 낫다고 생각하는 이들도 있다. 그러나 그런 사람들이 자기 논리를 끝까지 따라간다면, 쟁기 몰이 소년들에게는 붉은 흙물을 칠하고 우유 짜는 처녀들에게는 가죽을 걸치게 하고 싶어질 것이다."]], [["For these and other reasons Lady Lufton always went to London in April, and stayed there till the beginning of June.", "이런저런 이유로 레이디 러프턴은 늘 사월에 런던으로 올라가 유월 초까지 거기 머물렀다."], ["But for her this was usually a period of penance.", "그러나 그분에게 이 기간은 대개 고행의 시간이었다."], ["In London she was no very great personage.", "런던에서 그분은 그리 대단한 인물이 아니었다."], ["She had never laid herself out for greatness of that sort, and did not shine as a lady-patroness or state secretary in the female cabinet of fashion.", "그분은 그런 종류의 위세를 얻으려고 애쓴 적이 한 번도 없었고, 후원자 마님으로도, 사교계 유행 내각의 여성 국무 대신으로도 빛나지 않았다."], ["She was dull and listless, and without congenial pursuits in London, and spent her happiest moments in reading accounts of what was being done at Framley, and in writing orders for further local information of the same kind.", "런던에서 그분은 답답하고 맥이 없었으며 마음에 맞는 소일거리도 없었다. 그래서 프램리에서 무슨 일이 되어 가고 있다는 소식을 읽을 때, 또 같은 종류의 시골 소식을 더 보내라고 분부를 적어 보낼 때가 가장 행복한 순간이었다."]], [["But on this occasion there was a matter of vital import to give an interest of its own to her visit to town.", "그러나 이번만은 그분의 런던행에 그 자체의 흥미를 얹어 줄, 사활이 걸린 일이 하나 있었다."], ["She was to entertain Griselda Grantly, and as far as might be possible to induce her son to remain in Griselda’s society.", "그리젤다 그랜틀리를 손님으로 맞아, 될 수 있는 데까지 아들이 그리젤다의 곁에 머무르게 하는 일이었다."], ["The plan of the campaign was to be as follows:—Mrs.", "작전 계획은 이러했다."], ["Grantly and the archdeacon were in the first place to go up to London for a month, taking Griselda with them; and then, when they returned to Plumstead, Griselda was to go to Lady Lufton.", "우선 그랜틀리 부인과 부주교가 그리젤다를 데리고 한 달 동안 런던으로 올라간다. 그러고 나서 그들이 플럼스테드로 돌아갈 때 그리젤다는 레이디 러프턴에게로 온다."], ["This arrangement was not at all points agreeable to Lady Lufton, for she knew that Mrs.", "이 배치가 레이디 러프턴의 마음에 모든 점에서 흡족한 것은 아니었다."], ["Grantly did not turn her back on the Hartletop people quite as cordially as she should do, considering the terms of the Lufton-Grantly family treaty. But then Mrs.", "그랜틀리 부인이 러프턴-그랜틀리 집안 조약의 조건을 생각한다면 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 하틀탑 쪽 사람들에게 딱 잘라 등을 돌리지는 않고 있다는 것을 그분은 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Grantly might have alleged in excuse the slow manner in which Lord Lufton proceeded in the making and declaring of his love, and the absolute necessity which there is for two strings to one’s bow, when one string may be in any way doubtful.", "그러나 그랜틀리 부인 쪽에서는, 러프턴 경이 사랑을 만들고 밝히는 일에 어찌 그렇게 더디냐고, 그리고 활에 걸린 시위 하나가 어딘가 의심스러울 때에는 시위를 둘 걸어 두는 것이 절대로 필요하다고 변명할 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["Could it be possible that Mrs. Grantly had heard anything of that unfortunate Platonic friendship with Lucy Robarts?", "그랜틀리 부인이 루시 로바츠와의 그 딱한 플라토닉한 우정에 대해 무슨 소리를 들은 것은 아닐까?"]], [["There came a letter from Mrs.", "삼월 말이 다 될 무렵 그랜틀리 부인에게서 편지 한 통이 왔다."], ["Grantly just about the end of March, which added much to Lady Lufton’s uneasiness, and made her more than ever anxious to be herself on the scene of action and to have Griselda in her own hands.", "그 편지는 레이디 러프턴의 불안을 크게 더해 주었고, 그분이 직접 그 무대 위에 서서 그리젤다를 자기 손안에 두고 싶어 하는 마음을 어느 때보다 간절하게 만들었다."], ["After some communications of mere ordinary importance with reference to the London world in general and the Lufton-Grantly world in particular, Mrs. Grantly wrote confidentially about her daughter:", "런던 세계 전반과 특히 러프턴-그랜틀리 세계에 관한, 그저 예사로운 정도의 소식 몇 가지를 전한 뒤에, 그랜틀리 부인은 딸에 관해 은밀히 이렇게 적었다."]], [["“It would be useless to deny,”", "“아니라고 해도 소용없는 일이겠지요.”"], ["she said, with a mother’s pride and a mother’s humility, “that she is very much admired.", "어머니의 자랑과 어머니의 겸손을 함께 담아 그분은 이렇게 썼다. “그 아이는 아주 많이들 칭찬해요."], ["She is asked out a great deal more than I can take her, and to houses to which I myself by no means wish to go.", "제가 데리고 다닐 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 데서 청함을 받고, 저로서는 결코 가고 싶지 않은 집에서까지 청함을 받아요. 레이디 하틀탑의 첫 무도회만은 그 아이한테 거절할 수가 없었어요."], ["I could not refuse her as to Lady Hartletop’s first ball, for there will be nothing else this year like them; and of course when with you, dear Lady Lufton, that house will be out of the question.", "올해에 그만한 것은 달리 없을 테니까요. 그리고 물론 친애하는 레이디 러프턴, 그 아이가 당신과 함께 있을 때에는 그 집은 아예 이야기 밖의 일이겠지요."], ["So indeed would it be with me, were I myself only concerned.", "저 역시 저 혼자만 걸린 문제라면 그럴 거예요."], ["The duke was there, of course, and I really wonder Lady Hartletop should not be more discreet in her own drawing-room when all the world is there.", "물론 공작도 거기 있었어요. 온 세상이 다 모인 자리에서 레이디 하틀탑이 자기 응접실에서 좀 더 삼가지 않는 것이 저는 참으로 이상해요."], ["It is clear to me that Lord Dumbello admires Griselda much more than I could wish.", "덤벨로 경이 그리젤다를 제 바람보다 훨씬 더 흠모하고 있다는 것은 제게 분명해요."], ["She, dear girl, has such excellent sense that I do not think it likely that her head should be turned by it; but with how many girls would not the admiration of such a man be irresistible?", "그 아이는, 착한 것이, 분별이 어찌나 좋은지 그런 일로 머리가 돌아가지는 않으리라고 저는 생각해요. 하지만 그런 남자의 흠모라면 마음이 흔들리지 않을 처녀가 몇이나 될까요?"], ["The marquis, you know, is very feeble, and I am told that since this rage for building has come on, the Lancashire property is over two hundred thousand a year!!", "아시다시피 후작은 몸이 몹시 약해졌고, 요즘 이 건축 열풍이 시작된 뒤로 랭커셔 재산이 연 이십만 파운드가 넘는다고들 해요!!"], ["I do not think that Lord Dumbello has said much to her.", "덤벨로 경이 그 아이에게 말을 많이 한 것 같지는 않아요."], ["Indeed it seems to me that he never does say much to any one.", "사실 그이는 누구에게든 말을 많이 하는 사람이 아닌 듯해요."], ["But he always stands up to dance with her, and I see that he is uneasy and fidgety when she stands up with any other partner whom he could care about.", "하지만 그이는 늘 그 아이와 짝을 지어 춤을 추려고 일어서고, 그 아이가 그이가 마음에 걸릴 만한 다른 상대와 짝을 지어 일어서면 불안해하며 안절부절못하는 것이 저는 보여요."], ["It was really embarrassing to see him the other night at Miss Dunstable’s, when Griselda was dancing with a certain friend of ours.", "요전 밤 던스터블 양의 집에서, 그리젤다가 우리가 아는 어떤 친구와 춤을 추고 있을 때 그이를 보는 것은 정말이지 민망한 일이었어요."], ["But she did look very well that evening, and I have seldom seen her more animated!”", "하지만 그 아이는 그날 저녁 참 좋아 보였고, 저는 그 아이가 그토록 생기 있는 것을 좀처럼 본 적이 없어요!”"]], [["All this, and a great deal more of the same sort in the same letter, tended to make Lady Lufton anxious to be in London.", "이 모든 것, 그리고 같은 편지에 담긴 같은 종류의 이야기가 잔뜩, 레이디 러프턴으로 하여금 런던에 있고 싶게 만들었다."], ["It was quite certain—there was no doubt of that, at any rate—that Griselda would see no more of Lady Hartletop’s meretricious grandeur when she had been transferred to Lady Lufton’s guardianship.", "아주 확실한 것은 — 아무튼 그것만은 의심할 데가 없었다 — 그리젤다가 레이디 러프턴의 보호 아래로 옮겨 오고 나면 레이디 하틀탑의 겉만 번드레한 호사는 다시 보지 못하게 된다는 것이었다."], ["And she, Lady Lufton, did wonder that Mrs. Grantly should have taken her daughter to such a house.", "그리고 그분, 레이디 러프턴은, 그랜틀리 부인이 딸을 그런 집에 데리고 갔다는 것이 참으로 이상했다."], ["All about Lady Hartletop was known to all the world.", "레이디 하틀탑에 관한 일은 온 세상이 다 알고 있었다."], ["It was known that it was almost the only house in London at which the Duke of Omnium was constantly to be met.", "옴니엄 공작을 언제나 만날 수 있는 런던의 집이 거기 하나뿐이라는 것도 알려져 있었다."], ["Lady Lufton herself would almost as soon think of taking a young girl to Gatherum Castle; and on these accounts she did feel rather angry with her friend Mrs.", "레이디 러프턴 자신으로서는 어린 처녀를 개서럼 성에 데리고 가는 편이 그보다 나을 것 같지도 않았다."], ["Grantly. But then perhaps she did not sufficiently calculate that Mrs.", "그런 까닭들로 그분은 벗인 그랜틀리 부인에게 적잖이 노여운 마음이 들었다."], ["Grantly’s letter had been written purposely to produce such feelings—with the express view of awakening her ladyship to the necessity of action. Indeed in such a matter as this Mrs.", "그러나 그랜틀리 부인의 편지가 바로 그런 감정을 일으키려고, 곧 마님을 깨워 움직이게 하려는 뚜렷한 뜻으로 쓰였다는 데까지는 그분이 충분히 셈을 하지 못했던 것 같다."], ["Grantly was a more able woman than Lady Lufton—more able to see her way and to follow it out.", "사실 이런 일에서는 그랜틀리 부인이 레이디 러프턴보다 한결 유능한 여자였다. 자기 갈 길을 보고 그 길을 끝까지 따라가는 데 더 유능했다."], ["The Lufton-Grantly alliance was in her mind the best, seeing that she did not regard money as everything.", "그분의 마음에는 러프턴-그랜틀리 결합이 최상이었다. 돈이 전부라고 여기지는 않았기 때문이다."], ["But failing that, the Hartletop-Grantly alliance was not bad.", "그러나 그것이 안 될 경우, 하틀탑-그랜틀리 결합도 나쁘지 않았다."], ["Regarding it as a second string to her bow, she thought that it was not at all bad.", "활에 걸어 두는 두 번째 시위로 여기자면, 조금도 나쁘지 않다고 그분은 생각했다."]], [["Lady Lufton’s reply was very affectionate.", "레이디 러프턴의 답장은 매우 다정했다."], ["She declared how happy she was to know that Griselda was enjoying herself; she insinuated that Lord Dumbello was known to the world as a fool, and his mother as—being not a bit better than she ought to be; and then she added that circumstances would bring herself up to town four days sooner than she had expected, and that she hoped her dear Griselda would come to her at once.", "그리젤다가 즐겁게 지낸다는 것을 알아 얼마나 기쁜지 모르겠다고 썼다. 덤벨로 경은 세상에 바보로 알려진 사람이고 그 어머니는 — 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼보다 조금도 나을 것이 없는 사람이라고 슬쩍 끼워 넣었다."], ["Lord Lufton, she said, though he would not sleep in Bruton Street—Lady Lufton lived in Bruton Street—had promised to pass there as much of his time as his parliamentary duties would permit.", "그러고는 사정이 생겨 생각보다 나흘 일찍 런던에 올라가게 되었으니, 친애하는 그리젤다가 당장 자기에게로 와 주기를 바란다고 덧붙였다. 러프턴 경은, 그분의 말로는, 브루턴 스트리트에서 잠을 자지는 않겠지만 — 레이디 러프턴은 브루턴 스트리트에 살았다 — 의회의 직무가 허락하는 한 되도록 많은 시간을 거기서 보내겠다고 약속했다는 것이었다."]], [["O Lady Lufton!", "아, 레이디 러프턴!"], ["Lady Lufton!", "레이디 러프턴!"], ["did it not occur to you, when you wrote those last words, intending that they should have so strong an effect on the mind of your correspondent, that you were telling a—tarradiddle?", "그 마지막 말을 쓰면서, 그것이 받는 이의 마음에 그토록 센 효험을 내기를 바라면서, 당신은 지금 한바탕 — 거짓부렁을 늘어놓고 있다는 생각이 들지 않았던가?"], ["Was it not the case that you had said to your son, in your own dear, kind, motherly way: “Ludovic, we shall see something of you in Bruton Street this year, shall we not?", "당신이 아들에게 당신다운 살갑고 다정한 어머니의 말투로 이렇게 말한 것이 사실이 아니었던가. “루도빅, 올해도 브루턴 스트리트에서 네 얼굴을 좀 볼 수 있겠지?"], ["Griselda Grantly will be with me, and we must not let her be dull—must we?”", "그리젤다 그랜틀리가 나와 함께 있을 텐데, 그 아이를 심심하게 두어서는 안 되잖니?”"], ["And then had he not answered, “Oh, of course, mother,”", "그러고서 아들은 “아, 그러죠, 어머니.”"], ["and sauntered out of the room, not altogether graciously?", "하고 대답한 뒤, 그리 곱지 않은 태도로 방을 어슬렁어슬렁 나가지 않았던가?"], ["Had he, or you, said a word about his parliamentary duties?", "아들이든 당신이든 의회의 직무에 대해 한마디라도 한 적이 있었던가?"], ["Not a word!", "한마디도 없었다!"], ["O Lady Lufton!", "아, 레이디 러프턴!"], ["have you not now written a tarradiddle to your friend?", "당신은 지금 벗에게 거짓부렁을 적어 보낸 것이 아닌가?"]], [["In these days we are becoming very strict about truth with our children; terribly strict occasionally, when we consider the natural weakness of the moral courage at the ages of ten, twelve, and fourteen.", "요즘 우리는 아이들에게 진실에 대해 매우 엄해지고 있다. 열 살, 열두 살, 열네 살 나이에 도덕적 용기가 본래 얼마나 약한지를 생각하면 때로는 무섭게 엄하다."], ["But I do not know that we are at all increasing the measure of strictness with which we, grown-up people, regulate our own truth and falsehood.", "그러나 우리 어른들이 우리 자신의 참과 거짓을 다스리는 엄격함의 도량이 조금이라도 늘고 있는지는 나는 모르겠다."], ["Heaven forbid that I should be thought to advocate falsehood in children; but an untruth is more pardonable in them than in their parents.", "내가 아이들의 거짓을 옹호한다고 여겨지는 일은 결코 없기를 바란다. 그러나 거짓말은 부모에게보다 아이들에게 더 용서할 만한 것이다."], ["Lady Lufton’s tarradiddle was of a nature that is usually considered excusable—at least with grown people; but, nevertheless, she would have been nearer to perfection could she have confined herself to the truth.", "레이디 러프턴의 거짓부렁은 흔히 봐줄 만한 것으로 여겨지는 종류였다 — 적어도 어른들 사이에서는 그렇다. 그러나 그럼에도, 진실에만 머물 수 있었더라면 그분은 완전함에 더 가까웠을 것이다."], ["Let us suppose that a boy were to write home from school, saying that another boy had promised to come and stay with him, that other having given no such promise—what a very naughty boy would that first boy be in the eyes of his pastors and masters!", "어떤 아이가 학교에서 집으로 편지를 써서, 다른 아이가 와서 함께 지내겠다고 약속했다고 말했는데 그 다른 아이는 그런 약속을 한 적이 없다고 해 보자. 그 첫째 아이는 목자와 스승들의 눈에 얼마나 못된 아이가 되겠는가!"]], [["That little conversation between Lord Lufton and his mother—in which nothing was said about his lordship’s parliamentary duties—took place on the evening before he started for London.", "러프턴 경과 그 어머니 사이의 그 짧은 대화 — 나리의 의회 직무에 대해서는 한마디도 나오지 않았던 그 대화 — 는 그가 런던으로 떠나기 전날 저녁에 있었다."], ["On that occasion he certainly was not in his best humour, nor did he behave to his mother in his kindest manner.", "그날 그는 확실히 기분이 좋은 편이 아니었고, 어머니에게 살가운 태도로 대하지도 않았다."], ["He had then left the room when she began to talk about Miss Grantly; and once again in the course of the evening, when his mother, not very judiciously, said a word or two about Griselda’s beauty, he had remarked that she was no conjuror, and would hardly set the Thames on fire.", "어머니가 그랜틀리 양 이야기를 꺼내자 그는 방을 나가 버렸다. 그리고 그날 저녁 다시 한 번, 어머니가 그리 현명하지 못하게 그리젤다의 아름다움에 대해 한두 마디를 하자, 그는 그 아가씨가 요술쟁이도 아닌데 템스강에 불을 지를 일이 어디 있겠느냐고 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“If she were a conjuror!”", "“그 아이가 요술쟁이라면!”"], ["said Lady Lufton, rather piqued, “I should not now be going to take her out in London.", "적잖이 부아가 난 레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “내가 지금 그 아이를 런던에서 데리고 다니려 하지도 않을 게다."], ["I know many of those sort of girls whom you call conjurors; they can talk for ever, and always talk either loudly or in a whisper.", "네가 요술쟁이라고 부르는 그런 아가씨들을 나도 많이 안다. 그이들은 한없이 떠들 수 있고, 늘 크게 떠들거나 아니면 귓속말로 떠들지."], ["I don’t like them, and I am sure that you do not in your heart.”", "나는 그이들이 싫고, 너도 마음속으로는 싫어할 것이 분명하다.”"]], [["“Oh, as to liking them in my heart—that is being very particular.”", "“아, 마음속으로 좋아하느냐 하는 것까지 따진다면 — 그건 너무 까다롭게 캐는 거예요.”"]], [["“Griselda Grantly is a lady, and as such I shall be happy to have her with me in town.", "“그리젤다 그랜틀리는 숙녀다. 숙녀이니 나는 그 아이를 런던에 데리고 있는 것이 기쁠 것이다."], ["She is just the girl that Justinia will like to have with her.”", "저스티니아가 곁에 두고 싶어 할 만한 아가씨가 바로 그 아이지.”"]], [["“Exactly,”", "“그렇고말고요.”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."], ["“She will do exceedingly well for Justinia.”", "“저스티니아한테는 아주 잘 맞을 겁니다.”"]], [["Now this was not good-natured on the part of Lord Lufton; and his mother felt it the more strongly, inasmuch as it seemed to signify that he was setting his back up against the Lufton-Grantly alliance.", "이것은 러프턴 경으로서 마음씨 고운 말이 아니었다. 그리고 어머니는 그 말이 그가 러프턴-그랜틀리 결합에 등을 세우고 있다는 뜻으로 들렸으므로 그만큼 더 강하게 그것을 느꼈다."], ["She had been pretty sure that he would do so in the event of his suspecting that a plot was being laid to catch him; and now it almost appeared that he did suspect such a plot.", "자기를 잡으려고 무슨 계략이 깔리고 있다고 의심하게 되면 그가 꼭 그럴 것이라고 그분은 진작에 짐작하고 있었다. 그런데 이제 그가 정말로 그런 계략을 의심하는 것처럼 보였다."], ["Why else that sarcasm as to Griselda doing very well for his sister?", "그렇지 않다면 그리젤다가 누이에게는 아주 잘 맞겠다는 그 비꼬는 말이 어디서 나왔겠는가?"]], [["And now we must go back and describe a little scene at Framley which will account for his lordship’s ill-humour and suspicions, and explain how it came to pass that he so snubbed his mother.", "이제 우리는 뒤로 돌아가, 나리의 언짢은 기분과 의심을 설명해 주고 그가 어머니에게 그토록 쌀쌀맞게 굴게 된 사연을 밝혀 줄 프램리의 작은 장면 하나를 그려야 한다. 이 장면은 로바츠 부인과 루시가 목사관 정원을 함께 걷던 그 저녁으로부터 열흘쯤 뒤에 있었다."], ["This scene took place about ten days after the evening on which Mrs.", "그 열흘 동안 루시는 그 젊은 귀족과 어떤 특별한 이야기에도 한 번도 끌려들지 않았다."], ["Robarts and Lucy were walking together in the parsonage garden, and during those ten days Lucy had not once allowed herself to be entrapped into any special conversation with the young peer.", "그 사이에 그녀는 프램리 코트에서 저녁을 먹었고 또 한 번 저녁을 거기서 보냈다. 러프턴 경도 세 번인가 네 번 목사관에 올라왔고, 그녀가 늘 다니는 산책길에서 그녀를 찾았다."], ["She had dined at Framley Court during that interval, and had spent a second evening there; Lord Lufton had also been up at the parsonage on three or four occasions, and had looked for her in her usual walks; but, nevertheless, they had never come together in their old familiar way, since the day on which Lady Lufton had hinted her fears to Mrs. Robarts.", "그러나 레이디 러프턴이 로바츠 부인에게 자기 두려움을 슬쩍 내비친 그날 이후로, 두 사람은 옛날의 그 허물없는 사이로 마주 앉은 적이 한 번도 없었다."]], [["Lord Lufton had very much missed her.", "러프턴 경은 그녀가 몹시 그리웠다."], ["At first he had not attributed this change to a purposed scheme of action on the part of any one; nor, indeed, had he much thought about it, although he had felt himself to be annoyed.", "처음에는 이 변화를 누군가의 계획된 처사로 돌리지 않았다. 사실 그는 짜증이 난다고 느끼기는 했으나 그 일을 그리 깊이 생각하지도 않았다."], ["But as the period fixed for his departure grew near, it did occur to him as very odd that he should never hear Lucy’s voice unless when she said a few words to his mother, or to her sister-in-law.", "그러나 떠날 날로 정해진 날이 가까워지자, 루시가 자기 어머니나 올케에게 몇 마디 하는 때가 아니면 그녀의 목소리를 도무지 듣지 못한다는 것이 몹시 이상하게 여겨졌다."], ["And then he made up his mind that he would speak to her before he went, and that the mystery should be explained to him.", "그래서 그는 떠나기 전에 그녀에게 말을 붙여, 그 알 수 없는 일의 사연을 자기에게 설명받아야겠다고 마음을 정했다."]], [["And he carried out his purpose, calling at the parsonage on one special afternoon; and it was on the evening of the same day that his mother sang the praises of Griselda Grantly so inopportunely.", "그리고 그는 그 뜻을 실행하여, 어느 날 오후 특별히 목사관을 찾아갔다. 그의 어머니가 그토록 때를 잘못 골라 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 칭송한 것은 바로 같은 날 저녁이었다."], ["Robarts, he knew, was then absent from home, and Mrs. Robarts was with his mother down at the house, preparing lists of the poor people to be specially attended to in Lady Lufton’s approaching absence.", "로바츠는 그때 집을 떠나 있다는 것을 그는 알고 있었고, 로바츠 부인은 저택에 내려가 그의 어머니와 함께, 레이디 러프턴이 곧 집을 비우는 동안 특별히 돌보아야 할 가난한 이들의 명부를 만들고 있었다."], ["Taking advantage of this, he walked boldly in through the parsonage garden; asked the gardener, with an indifferent voice, whether either of the ladies were at home, and then caught poor Lucy exactly on the doorstep of the house.", "이를 이용해 그는 목사관 정원을 지나 거침없이 들어섰다. 정원사에게 아무렇지도 않은 목소리로 두 부인 중 누구라도 집에 있느냐고 물었고, 그러고서 바로 그 집 문간에서 딱하게도 루시를 붙잡았다."]], [["“Were you going in or out, Miss Robarts?”", "“들어가시는 길인가요, 나가시는 길인가요, 로바츠 양?”"]], [["“Well, I was going out,”", "“글쎄요, 나가려던 길이었습니다.”"], ["said Lucy; and she began to consider how best she might get quit of any prolonged encounter.", "루시가 말했다. 그러고는 이 만남이 길어지는 것을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 면할 수 있을까 헤아리기 시작했다."]], [["“Oh, going out, were you?", "“아, 나가시는 길이었군요."], ["I don’t know whether I may offer to—”", "제가 함께 가자고 해도 될지 어떨지는 모르겠지만 —”"]], [["“Well, Lord Lufton, not exactly, seeing that I am about to pay a visit to our near neighbour, Mrs.", "“그건 좀 아닐 듯합니다, 러프턴 경."], ["Podgens. Perhaps you have no particular call towards Mrs.", "저는 지척에 사는 이웃 포진스 부인을 찾아가려는 길이니까요."], ["Podgens’ just at present, or to her new baby?”", "나리께서는 지금 당장 포진스 부인네나 그 갓난아기에게 특별히 볼일이 없으시겠지요?”"]], [["“And have you any very particular call that way?”", "“그럼 로바츠 양께서는 그쪽에 아주 특별한 볼일이 있으신가요?”"]], [["“Yes, and especially to Baby Podgens.", "“네, 특히 포진스 아기에게요."], ["Baby Podgens is a real little duck—only just two days old.”", "포진스 아기는 정말 귀염둥이랍니다 — 태어난 지 이제 겨우 이틀이지요.”"], ["And Lucy, as she spoke, progressed a step or two, as though she were determined not to remain there talking on the doorstep.", "그러면서 루시는 문간에 서서 이야기를 계속하지는 않겠다고 마음을 굳힌 듯이 한두 걸음 앞으로 나아갔다."]], [["A slight cloud came across his brow as he saw this, and made him resolve that she should not gain her purpose.", "그것을 보자 그의 이마에 옅은 그늘이 스쳤고, 그녀가 뜻을 이루게 하지 않겠다는 마음이 굳어졌다."], ["He was not going to be foiled in that way by such a girl as Lucy Robarts.", "루시 로바츠 같은 아가씨에게 그런 식으로 밀려날 그가 아니었다."], ["He had come there to speak to her, and speak to her he would.", "그는 그녀와 이야기하러 여기까지 왔고, 이야기를 하고야 말 것이었다."], ["There had been enough of intimacy between them to justify him in demanding, at any rate, as much as that.", "적어도 그만한 것을 요구할 만큼의 친밀함은 두 사람 사이에 충분히 있었다."]], [["“Miss Robarts,”", "“로바츠 양,” 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “I am starting for London to-morrow, and if I do not say good-bye to you now, I shall not be able to do so at all.”", "“저는 내일 런던으로 떠납니다. 지금 작별 인사를 하지 않으면 아예 할 수가 없게 돼요.”"]], [["“Good-bye, Lord Lufton,”", "“안녕히 가십시오, 러프턴 경.”"], ["she said, giving him her hand, and smiling on him with her old genial, good-humoured, racy smile.", "그녀는 손을 내주며, 옛날 그대로의 그 살갑고 너그럽고 거침없는 웃음을 그에게 지어 보이며 말했다."], ["“And mind you bring into Parliament that law which you promised me for defending my young chickens.”", "“그리고 제 어린 병아리들을 지켜 주겠다고 저에게 약속하신 그 법, 잊지 말고 의회에 내십시오.”"]], [["He took her hand, but that was not all that he wanted.", "그는 그녀의 손을 잡았으나, 그가 원한 것이 그것뿐은 아니었다."], ["“Surely Mrs. Podgens and her baby can wait ten minutes.", "“포진스 부인과 그 아기가 십 분쯤 기다려 줄 수야 있겠지요."], ["I shall not see you again for months to come, and yet you seem to begrudge me two words.”", "저는 앞으로 몇 달 동안 로바츠 양을 다시 못 볼 텐데, 두 마디 나누는 것조차 아까워하시는 것 같군요.”"]], [["“Not two hundred if they can be of any service to you,”", "“나리께 조금이라도 도움이 된다면 이백 마디라도 아깝지 않습니다.”"], ["said she, walking cheerily back into the drawing-room; “only I did not think it worth while to waste your time, as Fanny is not here.”", "그녀는 이렇게 말하며 명랑하게 응접실로 되돌아 들어갔다. “다만 패니가 없으니 나리의 시간을 허비하게 할 것까지는 없다고 생각했을 뿐이지요.”"]], [["She was infinitely more collected, more master of herself than he was.", "그녀는 그보다 한없이 더 침착했고, 자기를 훨씬 더 잘 다스리고 있었다."], ["Inwardly, she did tremble at the idea of what was coming, but outwardly she showed no agitation—none as yet; if only she could so possess herself as to refrain from doing so, when she heard what he might have to say to her.", "속으로는 다가오는 일을 생각하며 떨었으나, 겉으로는 아무런 동요도 내보이지 않았다 — 아직까지는 그랬다. 그가 자기에게 할 말이 무엇인지를 듣게 되었을 때에도 그러지 않을 만큼 자기를 붙들 수만 있다면 좋을 텐데."]], [["He hardly knew what it was for the saying of which he had so resolutely come thither.", "그렇게 굳게 마음먹고 여기까지 왔으나, 무슨 말을 하러 온 것인지는 그 자신도 잘 몰랐다."], ["He had by no means made up his mind that he loved Lucy Robarts; nor had he made up his mind that, loving her, he would, or that, loving her, he would not, make her his wife.", "그는 자기가 루시 로바츠를 사랑한다고 결심한 것도 결코 아니었고, 사랑하기에 그녀를 아내로 삼겠다고, 또는 사랑하지만 아내로 삼지는 않겠다고 결심한 것도 아니었다. 그는 이 문제에 대해 좋은 쪽으로든 나쁜 쪽으로든 머리를 써 본 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["He had never used his mind in the matter in any way, either for good or evil.", "그는 그녀를 좋아하게 되었고 그녀가 아주 예쁘다고 여기게 되었다. 그녀와 이야기하는 것이 매우 즐겁다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["He had learned to like her and to think that she was very pretty.", "반면에 그리젤다 그랜틀리와, 아니 그가 아는 다른 몇몇 젊은 아가씨들과 이야기하는 것은 흔히 고역이었다."], ["He had found out that it was very pleasant to talk to her; whereas, talking to Griselda Grantly, and, indeed, to some other young ladies of his acquaintance, was often hard work.", "루시와 함께 보낸 반 시간들은 언제나 그를 만족하게 했다. 그는 다른 사람들과 있을 때보다 그녀와 있을 때 자기가 더 환해지고, 이야기할 만한 값이 있는 주제를 이야기하는 데 더 알맞아진다는 것을 알았다."], ["The half-hours which he had spent with Lucy had always been satisfactory to him.", "그리하여 그는 루시 로바츠를 마음 깊이 좋아하게 된 것이다."], ["He had found himself to be more bright with her than with other people, and more apt to discuss subjects worth discussing; and thus it had come about that he thoroughly liked Lucy Robarts.", "자기 애정이 플라토닉한 것인지 반플라토닉한 것인지는 스스로 물어본 적이 없었다. 그러나 두 사람의 친밀함이 그렇게 갑자기 끊기기 얼마 전에 그는 그녀에게, 그럴 마음으로 받아들이는 처녀라면 누구라도 반플라토닉한 것으로 여길 수 있는 말들을 한 적이 있었다."], ["As to whether his affection was Platonic or anti-Platonic he had never asked himself; but he had spoken words to her, shortly before that sudden cessation of their intimacy, which might have been taken as anti-Platonic by any girl so disposed to regard them.", "그는 그녀의 발밑에 몸을 던지며 애타는 열정에 삼켜지고 있다고 선언하지는 않았다. 그러나 사랑하는 여인의 손을 연인이 만지듯 그녀의 손을 만졌다."], ["He had not thrown himself at her feet, and declared himself to be devoured by a consuming passion; but he had touched her hand as lovers touch those of women whom they love; he had had his confidences with her, talking to her of his own mother, of his sister, and of his friends; and he had called her his own dear friend Lucy.", "자기 어머니와 누이와 벗들에 대해 이야기하며 그녀에게 마음속을 털어놓았다. 그리고 그녀를 자기의 소중한 벗 루시라고 불렀다."]], [["All this had been very sweet to her, but very poisonous also.", "이 모든 것이 그녀에게는 매우 달았으나 또한 매우 독했다."], ["She had declared to herself very frequently that her liking for this young nobleman was as purely a feeling of mere friendship as was that of her brother; and she had professed to herself that she would give the lie to the world’s cold sarcasms on such subjects.", "그녀는 이 젊은 귀족에 대한 자기 호감이 오라비의 호감과 똑같이 순전한 우정의 감정일 뿐이라고 스스로에게 여러 번 못을 박아 왔다. 그리고 그런 일에 대한 세상의 차가운 비아냥이 거짓임을 자기가 밝혀 보이겠다고 스스로에게 다짐했다."], ["But she had now acknowledged that the sarcasms of the world on that matter, cold though they may be, are not the less true; and having so acknowledged, she had resolved that all close alliance between herself and Lord Lufton must be at an end.", "그러나 이제 그녀는 그 문제에 대한 세상의 비아냥이, 차가울지언정 그만큼 참이 아닌 것은 아니라는 것을 인정했다. 그렇게 인정한 뒤에, 자기와 러프턴 경 사이의 가까운 결속은 모두 끝나야 한다고 마음을 정했다."], ["She had come to a conclusion, but he had come to none; and in this frame of mind he was now there with the object of reopening that dangerous friendship which she had had the sense to close.", "그녀는 결론에 이르렀으나 그는 아무 결론에도 이르지 못했다. 그리고 그런 마음가짐으로 그는 지금, 그녀가 분별을 내어 닫아 놓은 그 위태로운 우정을 다시 열려는 뜻을 품고 여기 와 있는 것이었다."]], [["“And so you are going to-morrow?”", "“그래서 내일 떠나시는 것이지요?”"], ["she said, as soon as they were both within the drawing-room.", "두 사람이 응접실 안에 들어서자마자 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes: I’m off by the early train to-morrow morning, and Heaven knows when we may meet again.”", "“네, 내일 아침 이른 기차로 떠납니다. 그리고 우리가 언제 다시 만날지는 하늘만 알 일이지요.”"]], [["“Next winter, shall we not?”", "“다음 겨울에 만나지 않겠습니까?”"]], [["“Yes, for a day or two, I suppose.", "“네, 하루나 이틀쯤은 그렇겠지요."], ["I do not know whether I shall pass another winter here.", "제가 여기서 또 한 번 겨울을 보낼지는 모르겠습니다."], ["Indeed, one can never say where one will be.”", "사실 사람이 어디 있게 될지는 누구도 말할 수 없는 것이니까요.”"]], [["“No, one can’t; such as you, at least, cannot.", "“그럼요, 말할 수 없지요. 적어도 나리 같은 분들은 말할 수 없겠지요."], ["I am not of a migratory tribe myself.”", "저야 철새 족속이 아니니까요.”"]], [["“I wish you were.”", "“그랬으면 좋겠는데요.”"]], [["“I’m not a bit obliged to you.", "“고맙게 여기지는 않겠습니다."], ["Your nomade life does not agree with young ladies.”", "나리의 그 떠돌이 살림은 젊은 아가씨들에게 맞지 않아요.”"]], [["“I think they are taking to it pretty freely, then.", "“그렇다면 요즘 아가씨들은 그 살림을 꽤 거침없이 받아들이고 있는 셈이지요."], ["We have unprotected young women all about the world.”", "온 세상에 보호자 없이 다니는 젊은 여인들이 있잖습니까.”"]], [["“And great bores you find them, I suppose?”", "“그리고 그이들이 몹시 지겹다고 여기시겠지요?”"]], [["“No; I like it.", "“아니요, 저는 좋습니다."], ["The more we can get out of old-fashioned grooves the better I am pleased.", "낡은 틀에서 벗어날 수 있는 것이 많을수록 저는 더 기쁩니다."], ["I should be a radical to-morrow—a regular man of the people,—only I should break my mother’s heart.”", "저는 내일이라도 급진파가 되겠어요 — 영락없는 민중의 사람이 되겠어요. 다만 그러면 제 어머니의 가슴이 무너질 테지요.”"]], [["“Whatever you do, Lord Lufton, do not do that.”", "“무슨 일을 하시든, 러프턴 경, 그것만은 하지 마십시오.”"]], [["“That is why I have liked you so much,”", "“제가 로바츠 양을 그토록 좋아해 온 것도 그 때문입니다.”"], ["he continued, “because you get out of the grooves.”", "그가 이어 말했다. “그 틀에서 벗어나 계시니까요.”"]], [["“Do I?”", "“제가 그런가요?”"]], [["“Yes; and go along by yourself, guiding your own footsteps; not carried hither and thither, just as your grandmother’s old tramway may chance to take you.”", "“그렇습니다. 그리고 혼자서 자기 발걸음을 이끌며 나아가시지요. 할머니 대의 낡은 궤도가 어디로 데려가든 그대로 이리저리 실려 가지 않고요.”"]], [["“Do you know I have a strong idea that my grandmother’s old tramway will be the safest and the best after all?", "“그런데 저는 할머니 대의 낡은 궤도가 결국 가장 안전하고 가장 좋다는 생각이 아주 강하다는 것을 아십니까?"], ["I have not left it very far, and I certainly mean to go back to it.”", "저는 그 궤도에서 멀리 벗어나지 않았고, 반드시 그리로 돌아갈 작정입니다.”"]], [["“That’s impossible!", "“그건 있을 수 없는 일이에요!"], ["An army of old women, with coils of ropes made out of time-honoured prejudices, could not drag you back.”", "오래 묵은 편견을 꼬아 만든 밧줄 사리를 든 노파들의 군대가 몰려온다 해도 로바츠 양을 그리로 끌어다 놓을 수는 없을 겁니다.”"]], [["“No, Lord Lufton, that is true. But one—”", "“그럴 수 없을 테지요, 러프턴 경."], ["and then she stopped herself.", "그 말씀은 맞습니다. 하지만 한 사람이 —”"], ["She could not tell him that one loving mother, anxious for her only son, had sufficed to do it.", "그러고서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 외아들을 걱정하는 사랑 많은 어머니 한 사람으로 그 일은 충분했다고 그에게 말할 수는 없었다."], ["She could not explain to him that this departure from the established tramway had already broken her own rest, and turned her peaceful happy life into a grievous battle.", "정해진 궤도를 벗어난 이 일이 이미 자기의 안식을 깨뜨려, 평화롭고 행복했던 삶을 괴로운 싸움으로 바꾸어 놓았다고 그에게 설명할 수는 없었다."]], [["“I know that you are trying to go back,”", "“로바츠 양이 되돌아가려 애쓰고 있다는 것을 저는 압니다.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Do you think that I have eyes and cannot see?", "“제게 눈이 있는데도 보지 못한다고 생각하시나요?"], ["Come, Lucy, you and I have been friends, and we must not part in this way.", "자, 루시, 그대와 나는 벗이었어요. 이런 식으로 헤어져서는 안 됩니다."], ["My mother is a paragon among women.", "제 어머니는 여인 중의 귀감이에요. 진심으로 하는 말입니다."], ["I say it in earnest;—a paragon among women: and her love for me is the perfection of motherly love.”", "여인 중의 귀감이지요. 그리고 저에 대한 그분의 사랑은 어머니의 사랑이 이를 수 있는 완전함 그 자체예요.”"]], [["“It is, it is; and I am so glad that you acknowledge it.”", "“그렇습니다, 그렇습니다. 그것을 인정하시니 저는 참 기쁩니다.”"]], [["“I should be worse than a brute did I not do so; but, nevertheless, I cannot allow her to lead me in all things.", "“그러지 않는다면 저는 짐승보다 못한 자일 테지요. 그러나 그렇다 해도 저는 그분이 저를 모든 일에서 이끌게 할 수는 없습니다."], ["Were I to do so, I should cease to be a man.”", "그렇게 한다면 저는 사내이기를 그치게 될 거예요.”"]], [["“Where can you find any one who will counsel you so truly?”", "“나리께 그토록 참되게 조언해 줄 사람을 어디서 찾으시겠습니까?”"]], [["“But, nevertheless, I must rule myself.", "“하지만 그렇다 해도 저는 저를 스스로 다스려야 합니다."], ["I do not know whether my suspicions may be perfectly just, but I fancy that she has created this estrangement between you and me.", "제 의심이 온전히 옳은지는 모르겠으나, 그분이 그대와 저 사이에 이 소원함을 만들어 놓았다는 생각이 듭니다."], ["Has it not been so?”", "그렇지 않았나요?”"]], [["“Certainly not by speaking to me,”", "“저에게 말씀하셔서 그러신 것은 분명히 아닙니다.”"], ["said Lucy, blushing ruby-red through every vein of her deep-tinted face.", "루시는 짙은 빛의 얼굴에 핏줄마다 홍옥처럼 붉은 물이 오르며 이렇게 말했다."], ["But though she could not command her blood, her voice was still under her control—her voice and her manner.", "그러나 자기 피는 다스릴 수 없었어도 목소리는 여전히 자기 뜻 아래 있었다. 목소리와 몸가짐은 그러했다."]], [["“But has she not done so?", "“그러나 그분이 그렇게 하지 않았느냐는 말입니다."], ["You, I know, will tell me nothing but the truth.”", "그대가 저에게 진실 아닌 것을 말하지 않을 사람이라는 것은 제가 압니다.”"]], [["“I will tell you nothing on this matter, Lord Lufton, whether true or false.", "“이 문제에 대해서는 참이든 거짓이든 저는 아무 말씀도 드리지 않겠습니다, 러프턴 경."], ["It is a subject on which it does not concern me to speak.”", "제가 입을 열 일이 아닌 주제입니다.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["I understand,”", "알겠습니다.”"], ["he said; and rising from his chair, he stood against the chimney-piece with his back to the fire.", "그는 이렇게 말하고 의자에서 일어나, 불을 등지고 난로 선반에 기대어 섰다."], ["“She cannot leave me alone to choose for myself my own friends, and my own—;”", "“그분은 저를 그냥 놓아두어 제 벗을, 그리고 저의 —를 스스로 고르게 하지 못하시는 거지요.”"], ["but he did not fill up the void.", "그러나 그는 그 빈자리를 채우지 않았다."]], [["“But why tell me this, Lord Lufton?”", "“그런데 왜 그런 말씀을 저에게 하십니까, 러프턴 경?”"]], [["“No!", "“아니지요!"], ["I am not to choose my own friends, though they be among the best and purest of God’s creatures.", "저는 제 벗을 고를 수 없습니다. 그 벗이 하느님이 지으신 것들 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 순결한 이라 해도요."], ["Lucy, I cannot think that you have ceased to have a regard for me.", "루시, 저는 그대가 저에 대한 마음을 거두었다고는 생각할 수 없어요."], ["That you had a regard for me, I am sure.”", "그대가 저에 대한 마음을 가지고 있었다는 것은 제가 압니다.”"]], [["She felt that it was almost unmanly of him thus to seek her out, and hunt her down, and then throw upon her the whole weight of the explanation that his coming thither made necessary.", "이렇게 자기를 찾아내어 몰아세우고, 그러고서 자기가 여기 온 탓에 반드시 필요해진 그 설명의 무게를 온통 그녀에게 던져 놓는 것은 사내답지 못한 짓에 가깝다고 그녀는 느꼈다."], ["But, nevertheless, the truth must be told, and with God’s help she would find strength for the telling of it.", "그러나 그렇다 해도 진실은 말해져야 했고, 하느님의 도우심으로 그녀는 그것을 말할 힘을 찾아낼 것이었다."]], [["“Yes, Lord Lufton, I had a regard for you—and have.", "“네, 러프턴 경, 저는 나리께 마음을 두고 있었습니다 — 그리고 지금도 두고 있습니다."], ["By that word you mean something more than the customary feeling of acquaintance which may ordinarily prevail between a gentleman and lady of different families, who have known each other so short a time as we have done.”", "그 말로 나리께서 뜻하시는 것은, 우리처럼 서로 알고 지낸 지 얼마 되지 않은, 다른 집안의 신사와 숙녀 사이에 흔히 있을 수 있는 예사로운 안면의 감정보다 무언가 더한 것이겠지요.”"]], [["“Yes, something much more,”", "“그렇습니다, 훨씬 더한 것이지요.”"], ["said he, with energy.", "그가 힘주어 말했다."]], [["“Well, I will not define the much—something closer than that.”", "“그렇다면 그 ‘더한’ 것이 무엇인지는 제가 정하지 않겠습니다 — 그보다 더 가까운 무엇이겠지요.”"]], [["“Yes, and warmer, and dearer, and more worthy of two human creatures who value each other’s minds and hearts.”", "“그렇습니다. 그리고 더 따뜻하고, 더 정답고, 서로의 마음과 가슴을 귀하게 여기는 두 사람에게 더 값있는 무엇이지요.”"]], [["“Some such closer regard I have felt for you—very foolishly.", "“그런 더 가까운 마음을 저는 나리께 느껴 왔습니다 — 아주 어리석게도요. 잠깐만요!"], ["Stop! You have made me speak, and do not interrupt me now.", "나리께서 저를 말하게 만드셨으니 이제 제 말을 끊지 마십시오."], ["Does not your conscience tell you that in doing so I have unwisely deserted those wise old grandmother’s tramways of which you spoke just now?", "그렇게 하면서 제가 아까 나리께서 말씀하신 그 현명한 할머니 대의 낡은 궤도를 슬기롭지 못하게 버렸다는 것을, 나리의 양심이 나리께 일러 주지 않습니까?"], ["It has been pleasant to me to do so.", "그렇게 하는 것이 저에게는 즐거웠습니다."], ["I have liked the feeling of independence with which I have thought that I might indulge in an open friendship with such as you are.", "나리 같은 분과 거리낌 없는 우정을 누려도 된다고 생각할 때의 그 홀로 선 듯한 느낌이 저는 좋았습니다."], ["And your rank, so different from my own, has doubtless made this more attractive.”", "그리고 저와는 그토록 다른 나리의 신분이 그것을 더 끌리는 일로 만들었을 것이 분명합니다.”"]], [["“Nonsense!”", "“말도 안 되는 소리예요!”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but it has.", "하지만 그랬습니다."], ["I know it now.", "이제는 압니다."], ["But what will the world say of me as to such an alliance?”", "그런데 그런 결합을 두고 세상이 저를 어떻게 말하겠습니까?”"]], [["“The world!”", "“세상이라니요!”"]], [["“Yes, the world!", "“그렇습니다, 세상이요!"], ["I am not such a philosopher as to disregard it, though you may afford to do so.", "저는 나리처럼 그것을 무시할 여유가 있는 사람도 아니고, 그것을 무시할 만한 철인도 아닙니다."], ["The world will say that I, the parson’s sister, set my cap at the young lord, and that the young lord had made a fool of me.”", "세상은 목사의 누이인 제가 젊은 나리께 눈독을 들였고, 그 젊은 나리가 저를 웃음거리로 만들었다고 말할 것입니다.”"]], [["“The world shall say no such thing!”", "“세상은 그런 말을 하지 못할 겁니다!”"], ["said Lord Lufton, very imperiously.", "러프턴 경이 아주 위세 있게 말했다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but it will.", "하지만 할 것입니다."], ["You can no more stop it, than King Canute could the waters.", "카누트 왕이 물결을 막지 못한 것처럼 나리도 그것을 막지 못하십니다."], ["Your mother has interfered wisely to spare me from this; and the only favour that I can ask you is, that you will spare me also.”", "나리의 어머님께서는 저를 이 일에서 지켜 주시려고 슬기롭게 끼어드신 것입니다. 그리고 제가 나리께 청할 수 있는 오직 하나의 은혜는, 나리께서도 저를 지켜 주시는 것입니다.”"], ["And then she got up as though she intended at once to walk forth to her visit to Mrs. Podgens’ baby.", "그러고서 그녀는 당장 포진스 부인의 아기를 찾아 나서려는 듯이 일어섰다."]], [["“Stop, Lucy!”", "“멈추세요, 루시!”"], ["he said, putting himself between her and the door.", "그가 그녀와 문 사이에 자기 몸을 세우며 말했다."]], [["“It must not be Lucy any longer, Lord Lufton; I was madly foolish when I first allowed it.”", "“이제 더는 루시라고 부르시면 안 됩니다, 러프턴 경. 처음에 그것을 허락한 것은 제가 미친 듯이 어리석었던 탓입니다.”"]], [["“By heavens! but it shall be Lucy—Lucy before all the world.", "“하늘에 맹세하고, 루시라고 부를 겁니다 — 온 세상 앞에서 루시라고요."], ["My Lucy, my own Lucy—my heart’s best friend, and chosen love.", "내 루시, 나의 루시 — 내 마음의 가장 좋은 벗이며 내가 고른 사랑."], ["Lucy, there is my hand.", "루시, 내 손이 여기 있어요."], ["How long you may have had my heart, it matters not to say now.”", "그대가 내 마음을 얼마나 오래 가지고 있었는지는 이제 말할 것도 없지요.”"]], [["The game was at her feet now, and no doubt she felt her triumph.", "승부는 이제 그녀의 발밑에 있었고, 그녀가 자기 승리를 느낀 것은 의심할 데가 없다."], ["Her ready wit and speaking lip, not her beauty, had brought him to her side; and now he was forced to acknowledge that her power over him had been supreme.", "그를 그녀 옆으로 끌어온 것은 미모가 아니라 그녀의 재빠른 재기와 말하는 입술이었다. 이제 그는 그녀가 자기에게 휘두른 힘이 그 무엇보다 컸음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Sooner than leave her he would risk all.", "그녀를 두고 떠나기보다는 모든 것을 걸겠다는 것이었다."], ["She did feel her triumph; but there was nothing in her face to tell him that she did so.", "그녀는 정말로 자기 승리를 느꼈다. 그러나 그녀가 그렇게 느꼈다는 것을 그에게 알려 줄 만한 것은 그녀 얼굴에 하나도 없었다."]], [["As to what she would now do she did not for a moment doubt.", "이제 자기가 어떻게 할 것인지에 대해서는 그녀는 한순간도 망설이지 않았다."], ["He had been precipitated into the declaration he had made, not by his love, but by his embarrassment.", "그가 방금 한 그 고백에 떠밀려 들어간 것은 사랑 때문이 아니라 궁지에 몰린 탓이었다."], ["She had thrown in his teeth the injury which he had done her, and he had then been moved by his generosity to repair that injury by the noblest sacrifice which he could make.", "그녀가 그가 자기에게 입힌 상처를 그의 면전에 들이댔고, 그러자 그는 자기가 할 수 있는 가장 고귀한 희생으로 그 상처를 메우려는 후한 마음에 움직인 것이었다."], ["But Lucy Robarts was not the girl to accept a sacrifice.", "그러나 루시 로바츠는 희생을 받아들일 아가씨가 아니었다."]], [["He had stepped forward as though he were going to clasp her round the waist, but she receded, and got beyond the reach of his hand.", "그가 그녀의 허리를 감싸안으려는 듯 앞으로 한 걸음 나섰으나, 그녀는 물러나 그의 손이 닿지 않는 데로 비켰다. “러프턴 경!”"], ["“Lord Lufton!”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “when you are more cool you will know that this is wrong.", "“더 차분해지시면 이것이 잘못된 일임을 아시게 될 것입니다."], ["The best thing for both of us now is to part.”", "지금 우리 두 사람에게 가장 좋은 일은 헤어지는 것입니다.”"]], [["“Not the best thing, but the very worst, till we perfectly understand each other.”", "“가장 좋은 일이 아니라 가장 나쁜 일이지요. 우리가 서로를 온전히 알게 되기 전에는요.”"]], [["“Then perfectly understand me, that I cannot be your wife.”", "“그러면 저를 온전히 아셔야겠습니다. 저는 나리의 아내가 될 수 없습니다.”"]], [["“Lucy!", "“루시!"], ["do you mean that you cannot learn to love me?”", "저를 사랑하는 법을 배울 수 없다는 뜻인가요?”"]], [["“I mean that I shall not try.", "“배우려 하지 않겠다는 뜻입니다."], ["Do not persevere in this, or you will have to hate yourself for your own folly.”", "이 일을 끝까지 밀고 가지 마십시오. 그러지 않으면 나리께서 나리 자신의 어리석음 때문에 나리 자신을 미워하셔야 할 것입니다.”"]], [["“But I will persevere till you accept my love, or say with your hand on your heart that you cannot and will not love me.”", "“하지만 저는 그대가 제 사랑을 받아들이거나, 아니면 가슴에 손을 얹고 저를 사랑할 수 없고 사랑하지 않겠다고 말할 때까지 끝까지 밀고 갈 겁니다.”"]], [["“Then I must beg you to let me go,”", "“그렇다면 저를 놓아주시기를 청해야겠습니다.”"], ["and having so said, she paused while he walked once or twice hurriedly up and down the room.", "이렇게 말한 뒤, 그가 서둘러 방 안을 한두 번 오가는 동안 그녀는 말을 멈추었다."], ["“And, Lord Lufton,”", "“그리고 러프턴 경,”"], ["she continued, “if you will leave me now, the words that you have spoken shall be as though they had never been uttered.”", "그녀가 이어 말했다. “지금 저를 놓고 가 주신다면, 나리께서 하신 그 말씀은 아예 입에 오른 적이 없는 것으로 하겠습니다.”"]], [["“I care not who knows that they have been uttered.", "“그 말이 입에 올랐다는 것을 누가 알든 저는 개의치 않습니다."], ["The sooner that they are known to all the world, the better I shall be pleased, unless indeed—”", "그것이 온 세상에 빨리 알려지면 알려질수록 저는 더 기쁠 겁니다. 다만 정말로 —”"]], [["“Think of your mother, Lord Lufton.”", "“어머님을 생각하십시오, 러프턴 경.”"]], [["“What can I do better than give her as a daughter the best and sweetest girl I have ever met?", "“제가 여태 만난 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 어여쁜 아가씨를 딸로 드리는 것보다 더 나은 일을 제가 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["When my mother really knows you, she will love you as I do.", "제 어머니가 그대를 참으로 알게 되면, 저와 똑같이 그대를 사랑하실 겁니다."], ["Lucy, say one word to me of comfort.”", "루시, 저에게 위로가 될 한마디를 해 주세요.”"]], [["“I will say no word to you that shall injure your future comfort.", "“나리의 앞날의 평안을 해칠 말은 한마디도 드리지 않겠습니다."], ["It is impossible that I should be your wife.”", "제가 나리의 아내가 되는 것은 있을 수 없는 일입니다.”"]], [["“Do you mean that you cannot love me?”", "“저를 사랑할 수 없다는 뜻인가요?”"]], [["“You have no right to press me any further,”", "“나리께는 저를 더 밀어붙일 권리가 없습니다.”"], ["she said; and sat down upon the sofa, with an angry frown upon her forehead.", "그녀가 이렇게 말하고, 이마에 노여운 주름을 지은 채 소파에 앉았다."]], [["“By heavens,”", "“하늘에 맹세하고,”"], ["he said, “I will take no such answer from you till you put your hand upon your heart, and say that you cannot love me.”", "그가 말했다. “그대가 가슴에 손을 얹고 저를 사랑할 수 없다고 말하기 전에는 그런 대답을 그대에게서 받지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["“Oh, why should you press me so, Lord Lufton?”", "“아, 어찌하여 저를 이토록 밀어붙이십니까, 러프턴 경?”"]], [["“Why!", "“어찌하여라니요! 제 행복이 그것에 걸려 있으니까요."], ["because my happiness depends upon it; because it behoves me to know the very truth.", "제가 그 참된 진실을 알아야 마땅하니까요. 일이 이렇게 되었습니다."], ["It has come to this, that I love you with my whole heart, and I must know how your heart stands towards me.”", "저는 온 마음으로 그대를 사랑합니다. 그러니 그대의 마음이 저에게 어떻게 놓여 있는지를 저는 알아야 합니다.”"]], [["She had now again risen from the sofa, and was looking steadily in his face.", "그녀는 이제 다시 소파에서 일어나, 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Lord Lufton,”", "“러프턴 경,”"], ["she said, “I cannot love you,”", "그녀가 말했다. “저는 나리를 사랑할 수 없습니다.”"], ["and as she spoke she did put her hand, as he had desired, upon her heart.", "그리고 말하면서 그녀는 정말로, 그가 바란 대로 자기 가슴에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“Then God help me!", "“그렇다면 하느님께서 저를 도우시기를!"], ["for I am very wretched.", "저는 참으로 비참합니다."], ["Good-bye, Lucy,”", "안녕히 계세요, 루시.”"], ["and he stretched out his hand to her.", "그러고서 그는 그녀에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Good-bye, my lord.", "“안녕히 가십시오, 나리."], ["Do not be angry with me.”", "저를 노여워하지 마십시오.”"]], [["“No, no, no!”", "“아니, 아니, 아니요!”"], ["and without further speech he left the room and the house, and hurried home.", "그러고는 더 말이 없이 그는 그 방을, 그리고 그 집을 떠나 서둘러 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["It was hardly surprising that he should that evening tell his mother that Griselda Grantly would be a companion sufficiently good for his sister.", "그가 그날 저녁 어머니에게 그리젤다 그랜틀리라면 누이의 벗으로는 충분히 좋겠다고 말한 것은 조금도 놀라운 일이 아니었다."], ["He wanted no such companion.", "그에게는 그런 벗이 필요하지 않았다."]], [["And when he was well gone—absolutely out of sight from the window—Lucy walked steadily up to her room, locked the door, and then threw herself on the bed.", "그리고 그가 완전히 가 버렸을 때 — 창에서 아주 보이지 않게 되었을 때 — 루시는 흔들림 없는 걸음으로 자기 방으로 올라가 문을 잠그고, 그러고서 침대에 몸을 던졌다."], ["Why—oh!", "왜, 아!"], ["why had she told such a falsehood?", "왜 자기는 그런 거짓말을 했을까?"], ["Could anything justify her in a lie?", "무엇으로 그 거짓말이 옳다고 할 수 있을까?"], ["Was it not a lie—knowing as she did that she loved him with all her loving heart?", "사랑하는 온 마음으로 그를 사랑한다는 것을 스스로 알면서 한 말이니, 그것이 거짓말이 아니고 무엇인가?"]], [["But, then, his mother!", "그러나 그의 어머니!"], ["and the sneers of the world, which would have declared that she had set her trap, and caught the foolish young lord!", "그리고 자기가 덫을 놓아 어리석은 젊은 나리를 잡았다고 말할 세상의 비웃음!"], ["Her pride would not have submitted to that.", "그녀의 자존심은 그것에 굽히려 하지 않았을 것이다."], ["Strong as her love was, yet her pride was, perhaps, stronger—stronger at any rate during that interview.", "그녀의 사랑이 강했으나, 그녀의 자존심은 어쩌면 그보다 더 강했다 — 적어도 그 만남의 시간 동안에는 더 강했다."]], [["But how was she to forgive herself the falsehood she had told?", "그러나 자기가 한 그 거짓말을 그녀는 어떻게 스스로 용서한단 말인가?"]], [["# CHAPTER XVII. MRS. PROUDIE’S CONVERSAZIONE.", "# 제17장. 프라우디 부인의 콘베르사치오네"]], [["It was grievous to think of the mischief and danger into which Griselda Grantly was brought by the worldliness of her mother in those few weeks previous to Lady Lufton’s arrival in town—very grievous, at least, to her ladyship, as from time to time she heard of what was done in London.", "레이디 러프턴이 런던에 올라오기 전 그 몇 주 동안, 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 어머니의 세속스러움 탓에 얼마나 못된 일과 위험 속으로 끌려 들어갔는가를 생각하면 참으로 통탄할 일이었다 — 적어도 마님에게는 몹시 통탄할 일이었으니, 그분은 런던에서 벌어지는 일을 때때로 전해 듣고 있었다."], ["Lady Hartletop’s was not the only objectionable house at which Griselda was allowed to reap fresh fashionable laurels.", "그리젤다가 사교계의 새 월계관을 거두어들이도록 허락된 못마땅한 집이 레이디 하틀탑의 집 하나뿐은 아니었다."], ["It had been stated openly in the Morning Post that that young lady had been the most admired among the beautiful at one of Miss Dunstable’s celebrated soirées, and then she was heard of as gracing the drawing-room at Mrs. Proudie’s conversazione.", "《모닝 포스트》는 그 젊은 아가씨가 던스터블 양의 이름난 야회 가운데 한 자리에서 미인들 중에서도 가장 많은 찬탄을 받았다고 버젓이 실었고, 그런 다음에는 그녀가 프라우디 부인의 콘베르사치오네에서 응접실을 빛냈다는 소문이 들려왔다."]], [["Of Miss Dunstable herself Lady Lufton was not able openly to allege any evil.", "던스터블 양 자신에 대해서라면 레이디 러프턴은 어떤 악담도 드러내어 늘어놓을 수가 없었다."], ["She was acquainted, Lady Lufton knew, with very many people of the right sort, and was the dear friend of Lady Lufton’s highly conservative and not very distant neighbours, the Greshams.", "레이디 러프턴도 알거니와 그녀는 옳은 축에 드는 사람들과 아주 많이 알고 지냈고, 레이디 러프턴의 지극히 보수적이고 그리 멀지도 않은 이웃인 그레셤네와는 다정한 벗이었다."], ["But then she was also acquainted with so many people of the bad sort.", "그러나 그러면서 그녀는 나쁜 축에 드는 사람들과도 그만큼 많이 알고 지냈다."], ["Indeed, she was intimate with everybody, from the Duke of Omnium to old Dowager Lady Goodygaffer, who had represented all the cardinal virtues for the last quarter of a century.", "실로 그녀는 옴니엄 공작에서부터 지난 사반세기 동안 온갖 기본 덕목을 대표해 온 늙은 구디개퍼 노부인에 이르기까지 누구와도 가까웠다."], ["She smiled with equal sweetness on treacle and on brimstone; was quite at home at Exeter Hall, having been consulted—so the world said, probably not with exact truth—as to the selection of more than one disagreeably Low Church bishop; and was not less frequent in her attendance at the ecclesiastical doings of a certain terrible prelate in the Midland counties, who was supposed to favour stoles and vespers, and to have no proper Protestant hatred for auricular confession and fish on Fridays.", "그녀는 당밀에게나 유황에게나 똑같이 달게 웃었고, 엑서터 홀에서도 제 집처럼 편안하여 — 세상 사람들 말이 그러하니 아마 딱 들어맞는 말은 아니겠지만 — 못마땅한 저교회파 주교를 한 사람 이상 고르는 데에 의견을 물어 온 적도 있었다 하며, 영대와 저녁 기도를 좋아하고 고해와 금요일의 생선을 개신교도답게 미워하지 않는다는 중부 여러 주의 어떤 무서운 고위 성직자의 교회 행사에도 그만 못하지 않게 자주 나타났다."], ["Lady Lufton, who was very staunch, did not like this, and would say of Miss Dunstable that it was impossible to serve both God and Mammon.", "신념이 아주 굳은 레이디 러프턴은 이것이 마음에 들지 않아, 던스터블 양을 두고 하나님과 마몬을 아울러 섬기기란 불가능한 일이라고 말하곤 했다."]], [["But Mrs. Proudie was much more objectionable to her.", "그러나 그녀에게 훨씬 더 못마땅한 사람은 프라우디 부인이었다."], ["Seeing how sharp was the feud between the Proudies and the Grantlys down in Barsetshire, how absolutely unable they had always been to carry a decent face towards each other in church matters, how they headed two parties in the diocese, which were, when brought together, as oil and vinegar, in which battles the whole Lufton influence had always been brought to bear on the Grantly side;—seeing all this, I say, Lady Lufton was surprised to hear that Griselda had been taken to Mrs.", "저 아래 바셋주에서 프라우디네와 그랜틀리네 사이의 반목이 얼마나 날카로운지, 교회 일에서 그들이 서로에게 점잖은 낯빛 한번 지어 보이지 못한 것이 언제나 어떠했는지, 그 둘이 교구 안에서 두 파를 이끌어 한자리에 놓이면 기름과 초 같았고 그 싸움마다 러프턴가의 영향력은 언제나 그랜틀리 편에 실려 왔다는 것을 — 내가 말하거니와 이 모든 것을 보아 온 레이디 러프턴은 그리젤다가 프라우디 부인의 저녁 전시회에 이끌려 갔다는 말을 듣고 놀랐다. “부주교가 그 일을 두고 의논을 받았더라면”"], ["Proudie’s evening exhibition.", "하고 그녀는 혼자 말했다."], ["“Had the archdeacon been consulted about it,”", "“이런 일은 결코 일어나지 않았을 텐데.”"], ["she said to herself, “this would never have happened.”", "그러나 그 점에서 그녀는 틀렸다."], ["But there she was wrong, for in matters concerning his daughter’s introduction to the world the archdeacon never interfered.", "딸을 세상에 내보이는 일에 관해서라면 부주교는 결코 끼어들지 않았으니까."]], [["On the whole, I am inclined to think that Mrs. Grantly understood the world better than did Lady Lufton. In her heart of hearts Mrs. Grantly hated Mrs. Proudie—that is, with that sort of hatred one Christian lady allows herself to feel towards another. Of course Mrs. Grantly forgave Mrs. Proudie all her offences, and wished her well, and was at peace with her, in the Christian sense of the word, as with all other women. But under this forbearance and meekness, and perhaps, we may say, wholly unconnected with it, there was certainly a current of antagonistic feeling which, in the ordinary unconsidered language of every day, men and women do call hatred. This raged and was strong throughout the whole year in Barsetshire, before the eyes of all mankind. But, nevertheless, Mrs. Grantly took Griselda to Mrs. Proudie’s evening parties in London.", "대체로 나는 그랜틀리 부인이 레이디 러프턴보다 세상을 더 잘 알았다고 생각하는 편이다. 그랜틀리 부인은 마음속 깊은 곳에서 프라우디 부인을 미워했다 — 그러니까 한 그리스도인 부인이 다른 부인을 향해 품는 것을 스스로 허락하는 그런 종류의 미움으로. 물론 그랜틀리 부인은 프라우디 부인의 모든 잘못을 용서했고, 그녀가 잘되기를 바랐고, 그리스도인의 뜻으로는 다른 모든 여인과 마찬가지로 그녀와도 화평했다. 그러나 이 참음과 온유함 밑에는 — 어쩌면 그것과는 아무 상관도 없다고 말해야 하겠지만 — 날마다 쓰는 여느 무심한 말로 남자든 여자든 미움이라 부르는 적대의 물길이 분명히 흐르고 있었다. 그것은 바셋주에서 온 세상 사람들의 눈앞에서 한 해 내내 세차게 타올랐다. 그럼에도 그랜틀리 부인은 런던에서는 그리젤다를 프라우디 부인의 저녁 모임에 데려갔다."]], [["In these days Mrs. Proudie considered herself to be by no means the least among bishops’ wives.", "이 무렵 프라우디 부인은 자신이 주교 부인들 가운데 결코 가장 못한 사람은 아니라고 여기고 있었다."], ["She had opened the season this year in a new house in Gloucester Place, at which the reception rooms, at any rate, were all that a lady bishop could desire.", "그녀는 올해 글로스터 플레이스의 새집에서 사교 계절의 막을 열었는데, 적어도 손님 맞는 방들만은 여자 주교라면 바랄 만한 모든 것을 갖추고 있었다."], ["Here she had a front drawing-room of very noble dimensions, a second drawing-room rather noble also, though it had lost one of its back corners awkwardly enough, apparently in a jostle with the neighbouring house; and then there was a third—shall we say drawing-room, or closet?—in which Mrs.", "여기에 그녀는 아주 훌륭한 크기의 앞응접실 하나와, 이웃집과 밀치락달치락한 끝인 듯 뒤쪽 구석 하나를 어지간히 어색하게 잃어버렸으되 이 또한 어지간히 훌륭한 둘째 응접실 하나를 두었고, 그리고 세 번째 — 응접실이라 할까, 아니면 골방이라 할까?"], ["Proudie delighted to be seen sitting, in order that the world might know that there was a third room; altogether a noble suite, as Mrs.", "— 가 있었으니, 프라우디 부인은 세상에 세 번째 방이 있다는 것을 알리기 위해 바로 그 방에 앉아 있는 모습을 보이기를 즐겼다."], ["Proudie herself said in confidence to more than one clergyman’s wife from Barsetshire.", "그리하여 통틀어 훌륭한 방 한 벌이었다고, 프라우디 부인 자신이 바셋주에서 온 여러 성직자 부인에게 은근히 말한 바와 같다."], ["“A noble suite, indeed, Mrs. Proudie!”", "“과연 훌륭한 방 한 벌이에요, 프라우디 부인!”"], ["the clergymen’s wives from Barsetshire would usually answer.", "바셋주에서 온 성직자 부인들은 으레 그렇게 대답했다."]], [["For some time Mrs. Proudie was much at a loss to know by what sort of party or entertainment she would make herself famous.", "얼마 동안 프라우디 부인은 어떤 종류의 모임이나 여흥으로 이름을 떨쳐야 할지 몰라 몹시 갈피를 잡지 못했다."], ["Balls and suppers were of course out of the question.", "무도회와 만찬은 물론 말이 되지 않았다."], ["She did not object to her daughters dancing all night at other houses—at least, of late she had not objected, for the fashionable world required it, and the young ladies had perhaps a will of their own—but dancing at her house—absolutely under the shade of the bishop’s apron—would be a sin and a scandal.", "딸들이 남의 집에서 밤새 춤추는 것에는 반대하지 않았다 — 적어도 요즈음에는 반대하지 않았으니, 유행하는 세상이 그것을 요구했고 젊은 아가씨들에게도 어쩌면 저들의 뜻이 있었으므로 — 그러나 자기 집에서, 그것도 주교의 앞치마 그늘 바로 아래서 춤을 춘다는 것은 죄악이요 망측한 일이 될 터였다."], ["And then as to suppers—of all modes in which one may extend one’s hospitality to a large acquaintance, they are the most costly.", "그리고 만찬으로 말하자면 — 널리 사귄 사람들에게 후한 대접을 뻗칠 온갖 방식 가운데 그것이 가장 돈이 많이 든다."]], [["“It is horrid to think that we should go out among our friends for the mere sake of eating and drinking,”", "“먹고 마시는 그것만을 위해 우리가 벗들 사이로 나선다고 생각하면 끔찍한 일이오,”"], ["Mrs. Proudie would say to the clergymen’s wives from Barsetshire.", "프라우디 부인은 바셋주에서 온 성직자 부인들에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["“It shows such a sensual propensity.”", "“그건 참으로 감각적 성벽을 드러내는 것이오.”"]], [["“Indeed it does, Mrs.", "“정말 그래요, 프라우디 부인."], ["Proudie; and is so vulgar too!”", "게다가 어찌나 상스러운지요!”"], ["those ladies would reply.", "그 부인들은 이렇게 대답하곤 했다."]], [["But the elder among them would remember with regret the unsparing, open-handed hospitality of Barchester palace in the good old days of Bishop Grantly—God rest his soul!", "그러나 그들 가운데 나이 든 이들은 그랜틀리 주교 — 그 영혼이 평안하시기를! — 의 좋았던 옛날 바체스터 주교관에서 베풀던 아낌없고 후한 대접을 아쉬움과 함께 떠올렸다."], ["One old vicar’s wife there was whose answer had not been so courteous—", "그리 정중하지 못한 대답을 한 늙은 교구 사제의 아내가 한 사람 있었으니 —"]], [["“When we are hungry, Mrs. Proudie,”", "“배가 고플 때는요, 프라우디 부인,”"], ["she had said, “we do all have sensual propensities.”", "그녀는 이렇게 말했다. “우리 모두가 감각적 성벽을 갖게 되지요.”"]], [["“It would be much better, Mrs.", "“세상이 그런 일은 다 집에서 치르고 온다면 훨씬 나을 것이오, 애실 부인,”"], ["Athill, if the world would provide for all that at home,”", "프라우디 부인은 재빨리 되받았다."], ["Mrs. Proudie had rapidly replied; with which opinion I must here profess that I cannot by any means bring myself to coincide.", "그 의견에는 나로서도 도무지 한 편이 되어 줄 수 없음을 여기서 밝혀 두어야 하겠다."]], [["But a conversazione would give play to no sensual propensity, nor occasion that intolerable expense which the gratification of sensual propensities too often produces.", "그러나 콘베르사치오네라면 어떤 감각적 성벽에도 놀 자리를 주지 않을 터이고, 감각적 성벽을 흡족하게 하느라 너무나도 자주 생겨나는 그 견딜 수 없는 비용을 일으키지도 않을 터였다."], ["Mrs. Proudie felt that the word was not all that she could have desired.", "프라우디 부인은 그 낱말이 자기가 바랄 만한 모든 것을 갖추지는 못했다고 느꼈다."], ["It was a little faded by old use and present oblivion, and seemed to address itself to that portion of the London world that is considered blue, rather than fashionable.", "오래 쓰이고 지금은 잊힌 탓에 빛이 좀 바랬고, 유행을 좇는 쪽이 아니라 먹물 든 축으로 여겨지는 런던 세상의 그 부분에게 말을 거는 듯했다."], ["But, nevertheless, there was a spirituality about it which suited her, and one may also say an economy.", "그러나 그럼에도 거기에는 그녀에게 어울리는 영적인 기운이 있었고, 또한 절약이 있었다고도 말할 수 있겠다."], ["And then as regarded fashion, it might perhaps not be beyond the power of a Mrs. Proudie to regild the word with a newly burnished gilding.", "그리고 유행으로 말하자면, 그 낱말에 새로 닦은 금박을 다시 입히는 일이 어쩌면 프라우디 부인 정도의 사람에게 못할 짓은 아닐 터였다."], ["Some leading person must produce fashion at first hand, and why not Mrs. Proudie?", "누군가 앞장서는 사람이 유행을 맨 먼저 만들어 내야 하는 법인데, 그것이 프라우디 부인이면 왜 안 되는가?"]], [["Her plan was to set the people by the ears talking, if talk they would, or to induce them to show themselves there inert if no more could be got from them.", "그녀의 계획은 이러했다. 사람들을 마주 붙여 놓아 말이 통하면 말을 하게 하고, 그 이상을 얻어 낼 수 없다면 적어도 거기 멍하니 몸이라도 내보이게 만드는 것."], ["To accommodate with chairs and sofas as many as the furniture of her noble suite of rooms would allow, especially with the two chairs and padded bench against the wall in the back closet—the small inner drawing-room, as she would call it to the clergymen’s wives from Barsetshire—and to let the others stand about upright, or “group themselves,”", "훌륭한 방 한 벌의 가구가 허락하는 한 되도록 많은 사람을 의자와 소파에 앉히되, 특히 뒤쪽 골방 — 바셋주에서 온 성직자 부인들에게는 작은 안응접실이라 부르곤 하던 그 방 — 벽에 붙인 의자 두 개와 속을 넣은 긴 의자를 쓰고, 나머지는 선 채로 있게 하거나 그녀의 표현대로 “무리를 짓게”"], ["as she described it.", "하는 것이었다."], ["Then four times during the two hours’ period of her conversazione tea and cake were to be handed round on salvers.", "그러고는 콘베르사치오네가 이어지는 두 시간 동안 네 차례에 걸쳐 은쟁반에 차와 케이크를 받쳐 돌리게 할 것이었다."], ["It is astonishing how far a very little cake will go in this way, particularly if administered tolerably early after dinner.", "아주 조금의 케이크가 이런 식으로 얼마나 멀리까지 가는지는 놀라울 지경이다. 특히 저녁을 먹고 어지간히 이른 때에 내놓으면 더욱 그렇다."], ["The men can’t eat it, and the women, having no plates and no table, are obliged to abstain. Mrs.", "남자들은 그것을 먹을 수 없고, 여자들은 접시도 없고 상도 없으니 삼가지 않을 수 없다."], ["Jones knows that she cannot hold a piece of crumbly cake in her hand till it be consumed without doing serious injury to her best dress. When Mrs.", "존스 부인은 부스러지는 케이크 한 조각을 다 먹을 때까지 손에 들고 있다가는 자기 나들이옷을 크게 상하게 하고 만다는 것을 안다."], ["Proudie, with her weekly books before her, looked into the financial upshot of her conversazione, her conscience told her that she had done the right thing.", "프라우디 부인이 주간 가계부를 앞에 놓고 자기 콘베르사치오네의 셈속을 들여다보았을 때, 양심은 그녀에게 옳은 일을 했다고 말해 주었다."]], [["Going out to tea is not a bad thing, if one can contrive to dine early, and then be allowed to sit round a big table with a tea urn in the middle.", "차 마시러 나가는 것은 나쁜 일이 아니다. 저녁을 이르게 먹을 수 있고, 그다음에 가운데 차 항아리를 놓은 큰 상에 둘러앉기를 허락받는다면 말이다."], ["I would, however, suggest that breakfast cups should always be provided for the gentlemen.", "다만 신사들에게는 언제나 아침 식사용 큰 잔을 내놓기를 권하고 싶다."], ["And then with pleasant neighbours,—or more especially with a pleasant neighbour,—the affair is not, according to my taste, by any means the worst phase of society.", "그리고 유쾌한 이웃들과 함께라면 — 더 나아가 특히 유쾌한 이웃 한 사람과 함께라면 — 그 일은 내 취향으로는 사교의 가장 못한 모습과는 도무지 거리가 멀다."], ["But I do dislike that handing round, unless it be of a subsidiary thimbleful when the business of the social intercourse has been dinner.", "그러나 나는 그 받쳐 돌리기를 싫어한다. 사교의 본론이 만찬이었을 때 덧붙여 나오는 골무만큼의 것이라면 모르되."]], [["And indeed this handing round has become a vulgar and an intolerable nuisance among us second-class gentry with our eight hundred a year—there or thereabouts;—doubly intolerable as being destructive of our natural comforts, and a wretchedly vulgar aping of men with large incomes.", "그리고 사실 이 받쳐 돌리기는 연 팔백 파운드, 그쯤 되는 우리 이류 지주 축 사이에서 상스럽고 견딜 수 없는 골칫거리가 되었다. 우리의 타고난 편안함을 망가뜨리고 큰 소득을 가진 이들을 비참하도록 상스럽게 본뜨는 것이니 두 곱으로 견딜 수 없다."], ["The Duke of Omnium and Lady Hartletop are undoubtedly wise to have everything handed round.", "옴니엄 공작과 레이디 하틀탑이 모든 것을 받쳐 돌리게 하는 것은 의심할 나위 없이 현명하다."], ["Friends of mine who occasionally dine at such houses tell me that they get their wine quite as quickly as they can drink it, that their mutton is brought to them without delay, and that the potato-bearer follows quick upon the heels of carnifer.", "그런 집에서 이따금 저녁을 먹는 내 벗들이 말해 주는데, 거기서는 술이 마실 수 있는 만큼 빠르게 나오고, 양고기가 지체 없이 앞에 오며, 감자 나르는 이가 고기 나르는 이의 뒤꿈치를 바짝 따라온다는 것이다."], ["Nothing can be more comfortable, and we may no doubt acknowledge that these first-class grandees do understand their material comforts.", "그보다 더 편안할 수는 없으니, 이 일류 귀인들이 제 물질적 편안함을 아는 것만은 우리도 인정해야 하리라."], ["But we of the eight hundred can no more come up to them in this than we can in their opera-boxes and equipages.", "그러나 우리 팔백 파운드 축은 이 일에서 그들을 따라갈 수 없으니, 그들의 오페라 특별석과 마차 행렬을 따라갈 수 없는 것과 마찬가지다."], ["May I not say that the usual tether of this class, in the way of carnifers, cup-bearers, and the rest, does not reach beyond neat-handed Phyllis and the greengrocer?", "고기 나르는 이며 잔 나르는 이며 그 밖의 것들로 말하자면, 이 계층에게 흔히 매인 줄은 손 놀림 재빠른 필리스와 청과물 장수를 넘어서지 못한다고 말해도 되지 않겠는가?"], ["and that Phyllis, neat-handed as she probably is, and the greengrocer, though he be ever so active, cannot administer a dinner to twelve people who are prohibited by a Medo-Persian law from all self-administration whatever?", "그리고 필리스가 아마도 그렇듯이 손 놀림이 재빠르고 청과물 장수가 아무리 부지런하다 해도, 제 손으로 무엇 하나 덜어 먹는 일을 메디아·페르시아의 법으로 금지당한 열두 사람에게 만찬을 치러 낼 수는 없다고 말해도 되지 않겠는가?"], ["And may I not further say that the lamentable consequence to us eight hundreders dining out among each other is this, that we too often get no dinner at all.", "그리고 서로의 집을 오가며 밥을 먹는 우리 팔백짜리들에게 딱한 결말이 이러하다고 더 말해도 되지 않겠는가 — 우리는 너무나 자주 저녁을 아예 못 먹는다는 것."], ["Phyllis, with the potatoes, cannot reach us till our mutton is devoured, or in a lukewarm state past our power of managing; and Ganymede, the greengrocer, though we admire the skill of his necktie and the whiteness of his unexceptionable gloves, fails to keep us going in Sherry.", "감자를 든 필리스는 우리 양고기가 다 없어지거나 우리 힘으로는 어찌할 수 없게 식어 버린 뒤에야 우리에게 닿을 수 있고, 청과물 장수 가니메데스는 우리가 그 넥타이 매는 솜씨와 나무랄 데 없는 장갑의 흰빛에 감탄하기는 하지만 우리 셰리 잔을 채워 주는 데에는 실패한다."]], [["Seeing a lady the other day in this strait, left without a small modicum of stimulus which was no doubt necessary for her good digestion, I ventured to ask her to drink wine with me.", "얼마 전에 어떤 부인이 이런 궁지에 빠져, 소화에 아무래도 필요할 얼마 안 되는 자극을 얻지 못한 채 남겨진 것을 보고, 나는 감히 나와 함께 술을 드시자고 청해 보았다."], ["But when I bowed my head at her, she looked at me with all her eyes, struck with amazement.", "그러나 내가 그녀에게 머리를 숙였을 때 그녀는 두 눈을 다 뜨고 나를 바라보며 놀라움에 사로잡혔다."], ["Had I suggested that she should join me in a wild Indian war-dance, with nothing on but my paint, her face could not have shown greater astonishment.", "내가 몸에 물감만 칠하고 사나운 인디언 전쟁 춤에 함께 나서자고 했더라도 그 얼굴이 그보다 더 큰 놀라움을 드러낼 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["And yet I should have thought she might have remembered the days when Christian men and women used to drink wine with each other.", "그런데도 나는 그녀가 그리스도인 남녀가 서로 술을 나누어 마시던 그 시절을 기억해 주었으면 했다."]], [["God be with the good old days when I could hobnob with my friend over the table as often as I was inclined to lift my glass to my lips, and make a long arm for a hot potato whenever the exigencies of my plate required it.", "상에 앉아 잔을 입술로 들어 올리고 싶은 마음이 들 때마다 벗과 잔을 부딪칠 수 있었고, 내 접시의 급한 사정이 요구할 때면 뜨거운 감자를 향해 팔을 길게 뻗을 수 있었던 그 좋았던 옛날에 하나님이 함께하시기를."]], [["I think it may be laid down as a rule in affairs of hospitality, that whatever extra luxury or grandeur we introduce at our tables when guests are with us, should be introduced for the advantage of the guest and not for our own.", "대접하는 일에서는 이렇게 법으로 정해 두어도 될 것 같다. 손님이 우리와 함께 있을 때 우리 상에 들여오는 남다른 호사나 웅장함이 무엇이든, 그것은 손님의 이익을 위해 들여와야 하고 우리 자신의 이익을 위해 들여와서는 안 된다는 것."], ["If, for instance, our dinner be served in a manner different from that usual to us, it should be so served in order that our friends may with more satisfaction eat our repast than our everyday practice would produce on them.", "예컨대 우리 저녁이 우리에게 여느 때와 다른 방식으로 차려진다면, 그것은 우리 벗들이 우리의 평소 방식이 그들에게 줄 것보다 더 흡족하게 우리 음식을 먹을 수 있도록 그렇게 차려져야 한다."], ["But the change should by no means be made to their material detriment in order that our fashion may be acknowledged.", "그러나 그 변화는 우리 유행이 인정받게 하려고 그들의 실질적인 손해를 무릅쓰고 이루어져서는 결코 안 된다."], ["Again, if I decorate my sideboard and table, wishing that the eyes of my visitors may rest on that which is elegant and pleasant to the sight, I act in that matter with a becoming sense of hospitality; but if my object be to kill Mrs.", "또, 내가 찬장과 상을 꾸미는 것이 손님들의 눈이 우아하고 보기에 즐거운 것에 머물기를 바라서라면, 나는 그 일에서 대접하는 마음에 걸맞게 처신하는 것이다."], ["Jones with envy at the sight of all my silver trinkets, I am a very mean-spirited fellow.", "그러나 내 목적이 온갖 은붙이를 보여 존스 부인을 부러움으로 죽이는 것이라면, 나는 아주 마음이 천한 놈이다."], ["This, in a broad way, will be acknowledged; but if we would bear in mind the same idea at all times,—on occasions when the way perhaps may not be so broad, when more thinking may be required to ascertain what is true hospitality,—I think we of the eight hundred would make a greater advance towards really entertaining our own friends than by any rearrangement of the actual meats and dishes which we set before them.", "이만한 넓이로 말하면 누구나 인정할 것이다. 그러나 우리가 늘 같은 생각을 마음에 두고 있다면 — 어쩌면 길이 그만큼 넓지 않아서, 무엇이 참된 대접인지 알아내려면 더 많이 생각해야 하는 자리에서도 — 우리 팔백 파운드 축은 우리 앞에 내놓는 고기와 접시들을 이리저리 다시 늘어놓아 보는 것보다 우리 벗들을 참으로 즐겁게 해 주는 쪽으로 더 크게 나아갈 수 있으리라 생각한다."]], [["Knowing as we do, that the terms of the Lufton-Grantly alliance had been so solemnly ratified between the two mothers, it is perhaps hardly open to us to suppose that Mrs. Grantly was induced to take her daughter to Mrs.", "러프턴–그랜틀리 동맹의 조건이 두 어머니 사이에서 그토록 엄숙하게 비준되었음을 우리가 아는 터에, 그랜틀리 부인이 딸을 프라우디 부인의 집으로 데려간 것은 덤벨로 경이 주교의 모임을 빛내 주겠다고 약속했다는 소식을 그녀가 얻어 들었기 때문이라고 짐작하는 것은 아마 우리에게 허락된 일이 아니겠다."], ["Proudie’s by any knowledge which she may have acquired that Lord Dumbello had promised to grace the bishop’s assembly.", "고귀한 계약 당사자들이 이따금 더 낮은 축의 계약자들이라면 부정직하다고 여길 만한 여유를 스스로에게 허락하는 것은 분명한 사실이다."], ["It is certainly the fact that high contracting parties do sometimes allow themselves a latitude which would be considered dishonest by contractors of a lower sort; and it may be possible that the archdeacon’s wife did think of that second string with which her bow was furnished.", "그러니 부주교의 부인이 자기 활에 걸어 둔 두 번째 시위를 생각했을 수도 있는 일이다. 어찌 되었든 덤벨로 경은 프라우디 부인의 집에 있었고, 그리하여 그리젤다는 소파의 한쪽 끝에 앉게 되었으며 그 가까이에는 덤벨로 경이 “무리를 지을”"], ["Be that as it may, Lord Dumbello was at Mrs. Proudie’s, and it did so come to pass that Griselda was seated at the corner of a sofa close to which was a vacant space in which his lordship could—“group himself.”", "수 있는 빈자리가 있게 되었다."]], [["They had not been long there before Lord Dumbello did group himself.", "그들이 거기 있게 된 지 얼마 되지 않아 덤벨로 경은 정말로 무리를 지었다."], ["“Fine day,”", "“좋은 날이오,”"], ["he said, coming up and occupying the vacant position by Miss Grantly’s elbow.", "그는 그렇게 말하며 다가와 그랜틀리 양의 팔꿈치 옆 빈자리를 차지했다."]], [["“We were driving to-day, and we thought it rather cold,”", "“저희는 오늘 마차를 타고 나갔는데, 꽤 춥다고 생각했어요,”"], ["said Griselda.", "그리젤다가 말했다."]], [["“Deuced cold,”", "“지독하게 춥소,”"], ["said Lord Dumbello, and then he adjusted his white cravat and touched up his whiskers.", "덤벨로 경은 그렇게 말하고 나서 흰 넥타이를 고쳐 매고 구레나룻을 손질했다."], ["Having got so far, he did not proceed to any other immediate conversational efforts; nor did Griselda.", "거기까지 이르자 그는 곧이어 어떤 다른 대화의 노력으로도 나아가지 않았다. 그리젤다도 그러지 않았다."], ["But he grouped himself again as became a marquis, and gave very intense satisfaction to Mrs. Proudie.", "그러나 그는 후작에게 걸맞게 다시 무리를 지었고, 프라우디 부인에게 아주 짙은 만족을 주었다."]], [["“This is so kind of you, Lord Dumbello,”", "“이렇게 오시니 참 고맙소, 덤벨로 경,”"], ["said that lady, coming up to him and shaking his hand warmly; “so very kind of you to come to my poor little tea-party.”", "그 부인은 그에게 다가와 손을 따뜻하게 잡으며 말했다. “제 초라한 조그만 차 모임에 오시니 참으로 고마운 일이오.”"]], [["“Uncommonly pleasant, I call it,”", "“썩 유쾌하다고 하겠소,”"], ["said his lordship.", "덤벨로 경이 말했다."], ["“I like this sort of thing—no trouble, you know.”", "“나는 이런 게 좋소 — 번거로울 게 없으니.”"]], [["“No; that is the charm of it: isn’t it?", "“그렇지요. 그게 바로 이것의 묘미가 아니겠소?"], ["no trouble, or fuss, or parade.", "번거로움도, 법석도, 겉치레도 없다는 것."], ["That’s what I always say.", "그게 내가 늘 하는 말이오."], ["According to my ideas, society consists in giving people facility for an interchange of thoughts—what we call conversation.”", "내 생각으로는 사교란 사람들에게 생각을 주고받을 편의를 주는 것에 있소 — 우리가 대화라 부르는 그것 말이오.”"]], [["“Aw, yes, exactly.”", "“아, 예, 바로 그렇소.”"]], [["“Not in eating and drinking together—eh, Lord Dumbello?", "“함께 먹고 마시는 데에 있는 것이 아니지요 — 그렇지 않소, 덤벨로 경?"], ["And yet the practice of our lives would seem to show that the indulgence of those animal propensities can alone suffice to bring people together.", "그런데도 우리 삶의 관행은 그 짐승 같은 성벽을 흡족하게 하는 것만이 사람들을 한자리에 모을 수 있음을 보여 주는 듯하오."], ["The world in this has surely made a great mistake.”", "세상은 이 점에서 참으로 큰 잘못을 저질렀소.”"]], [["“I like a good dinner all the same,”", "“나는 그래도 좋은 저녁이 좋소,”"], ["said Lord Dumbello.", "덤벨로 경이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes, of course—of course.", "“아, 그렇지요, 물론 — 물론이오."], ["I am by no means one of those who would pretend to preach that our tastes have not been given to us for our enjoyment.", "우리 입맛이 우리 즐거움을 위해 주어진 것이 아니라고 설교하려 드는 사람들 축에는 나도 결코 들지 않소."], ["Why should things be nice if we are not to like them?”", "좋아하지 않을 것이라면 무엇이 왜 좋겠소?”"]], [["“A man who can really give a good dinner has learned a great deal,”", "“정말로 좋은 저녁을 낼 수 있는 사람은 많은 것을 배운 사람이오,”"], ["said Lord Dumbello, with unusual animation.", "덤벨로 경이 여느 때와 달리 활기를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“An immense deal.", "“엄청나게 많은 것이지요."], ["It is quite an art in itself; and one which I, at any rate, by no means despise.", "그것 자체가 하나의 예술이오. 그리고 적어도 나는 그것을 결코 업신여기지 않소."], ["But we cannot always be eating—can we?”", "그러나 우리가 늘 먹고만 있을 수는 없지 않겠소?”"]], [["“No,”", "“없지요,”"], ["said Lord Dumbello, “not always.”", "덤벨로 경이 말했다. “늘 그럴 수는 없소.”"], ["And he looked as though he lamented that his powers should be so circumscribed.", "그러고 그는 자기 힘이 그렇게 제한되어 있음을 애달파하는 듯한 얼굴을 했다."]], [["And then Mrs. Proudie passed on to Mrs.", "그러고 나서 프라우디 부인은 그랜틀리 부인에게로 옮겨 갔다."], ["Grantly. The two ladies were quite friendly in London; though down in their own neighbourhood they waged a war so internecine in its nature.", "두 부인은 런던에서는 아주 다정했다."], ["But nevertheless Mrs.", "저들의 고향 동네에서는 그토록 서로 죽이려 드는 싸움을 벌였건만."], ["Proudie’s manner might have showed to a very close observer that she knew the difference between a bishop and an archdeacon.", "그러나 그럼에도 프라우디 부인의 태도는 아주 눈 밝은 사람에게라면 그녀가 주교와 부주교의 차이를 안다는 것을 드러내 보였을 것이다."], ["“I am so delighted to see you,”", "“이렇게 뵈니 정말 기쁘오,”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“No, don’t mind moving; I won’t sit down just at present.", "“아니, 자리를 옮기려 마시오. 지금 당장 앉지는 않을 테니."], ["But why didn’t the archdeacon come?”", "그런데 부주교는 왜 오지 않았소?”"]], [["“It was quite impossible; it was indeed,”", "“도무지 될 수 없는 일이었어요. 정말 그랬어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“The archdeacon never has a moment in London that he can call his own.”", "“부주교는 런던에서 자기 것이라 부를 만한 시간이 한 순간도 없어요.”"]], [["“You don’t stay up very long, I believe.”", "“오래 머무르지는 않으신다지요.”"]], [["“A good deal longer than we either of us like, I can assure you.", "“우리 둘 다 좋아하는 것보다는 훨씬 오래 머물러요, 정말이에요."], ["London life is a perfect nuisance to me.”", "런던 생활은 저에게는 아주 골칫거리예요.”"]], [["“But people in a certain position must go through with it, you know,”", "“그러나 어떤 자리에 있는 사람은 그것을 겪어 내야 하잖소,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["“The bishop, for instance, must attend the House.”", "“주교만 해도 상원에 나가야 하고.”"]], [["“Must he?” asked Mrs.", "“그래야 하나요?”"], ["Grantly, as though she were not at all well informed with reference to this branch of a bishop’s business.", "그랜틀리 부인이 주교 직무의 이 갈래에 대해서는 도무지 아는 바가 없는 사람처럼 물었다."], ["“I am very glad that archdeacons are under no such liability.”", "“부주교에게는 그런 의무가 없다니 아주 다행이에요.”"]], [["“Oh, no; there’s nothing of that sort,”", "“아, 없지요. 그런 것은 하나도 없소,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie, very seriously.", "프라우디 부인이 아주 진지하게 말했다."], ["“But how uncommonly well Miss Grantly is looking!", "“그런데 그랜틀리 양은 어쩌면 저리 좋아 보이오!"], ["I do hear that she has quite been admired.”", "그 아이가 꽤 찬탄을 받았다는 말을 나도 듣기는 들었소.”"]], [["This phrase certainly was a little hard for the mother to bear.", "이 말은 확실히 어머니로서 참기에 좀 힘든 것이었다."], ["All the world had acknowledged, so Mrs. Grantly had taught herself to believe, that Griselda was undoubtedly the beauty of the season.", "온 세상이 인정한 바 — 그랜틀리 부인은 스스로 그렇게 믿게 되었다 — 그리젤다는 의심할 나위 없이 이번 계절의 미인이었다."], ["Marquises and lords were already contending for her smiles, and paragraphs had been written in newspapers as to her profile.", "후작들과 귀족들이 벌써 그 미소를 놓고 다투었고, 그 옆얼굴에 대해 신문에 여러 단이 쓰였다."], ["It was too hard to be told, after that, that her daughter had been “quite admired.”", "그런 뒤에 자기 딸이 “꽤 찬탄을 받았다”는 말을 듣는 것은 너무 심했다."], ["Such a phrase might suit a pretty little red-cheeked milkmaid of a girl.", "그런 말투는 뺨이 붉은 예쁘장한 우유 짜는 계집아이에게나 어울릴 것이었다."]], [["“She cannot, of course, come near your girls in that respect,”", "“물론 그 점에서는 그 애가 부인 딸들에게는 미치지 못하지요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly, very quietly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 아주 조용하게 말했다."], ["Now the Miss Proudies had not elicited from the fashionable world any very loud encomiums on their beauty.", "그런데 프라우디 자매는 유행하는 세상에서 저들의 미모에 대해 어떤 큰 찬사도 끌어내지 못한 터였다."], ["Their mother felt the taunt in its fullest force, but she would not essay to do battle on the present arena.", "그들의 어머니는 그 비꼼을 온 힘으로 느꼈으나, 지금 이 자리에서 싸움을 벌이려 하지는 않았다."], ["She jotted down the item in her mind, and kept it over for Barchester and the chapter.", "그녀는 그 항목을 마음속에 적어 두고, 바체스터와 성당 참사회를 위해 남겨 두었다."], ["Such debts as those she usually paid on some day, if the means of doing so were at all within her power.", "그런 빚은 갚을 방도가 조금이라도 제 힘 안에 있으면 그녀가 으레 언젠가는 갚는 것이었다."]], [["“But there is Miss Dunstable, I declare,”", "“그런데 저기 던스터블 양이 왔구려, 정말이지,”"], ["she said, seeing that that lady had entered the room; and away went Mrs.", "그 부인이 던스터블 양이 방에 들어서는 것을 보고 말했다."], ["Proudie to welcome her distinguished guest.", "그리고 프라우디 부인은 자기의 이름 높은 손님을 맞이하러 가 버렸다."]], [["“And so this is a conversazione, is it?”", "“그래, 이게 콘베르사치오네라는 거군요?”"], ["said that lady, speaking, as usual, not in a suppressed voice.", "그 부인이 여느 때처럼 목소리를 낮추지 않고 말했다."], ["“Well, I declare, it’s very nice.", "“아니, 참으로 좋네요."], ["It means conversation, don’t it, Mrs. Proudie?”", "대화한다는 뜻이지요, 프라우디 부인?”"]], [["“Ha, ha, ha!", "“하하하!"], ["Miss Dunstable, there is nobody like you, I declare.”", "던스터블 양, 정말이지 당신 같은 사람은 또 없소.”"]], [["“Well, but don’t it?", "“그렇지만 그런 뜻 아니에요?"], ["and tea and cake?", "그리고 차와 케이크?"], ["and then, when we’re tired of talking, we go away,—isn’t that it?”", "그러고 나서 이야기하는 데 지치면 우리는 가 버리는 것 — 그렇지 않아요?”"]], [["“But you must not be tired for these three hours yet.”", "“그러나 앞으로 세 시간은 지치시면 안 되오.”"]], [["“Oh, I’m never tired of talking; all the world knows that.", "“아, 저는 이야기하는 데 지치는 일이 없어요. 온 세상이 아는 일이지요."], ["How do, bishop?", "안녕하세요, 주교님?"], ["A very nice sort of thing this conversazione, isn’t it now?”", "이 콘베르사치오네라는 게 참 좋은 것이네요, 그렇지 않아요?”"]], [["The bishop rubbed his hands together and smiled, and said that he thought it was rather nice.", "주교는 두 손을 맞비비며 웃고, 자기도 꽤 좋다고 생각한다고 말했다."]], [["“Mrs. Proudie is so fortunate in all her little arrangements,”", "“프라우디 부인은 그 자잘한 준비마다 참 복이 많으시지요,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes,” said the bishop.", "“그렇지요, 그렇지요,”"], ["“I think she is happy in these matters.", "주교가 말했다. “나도 아내가 이런 일에는 재주가 있다고 생각해요."], ["I do flatter myself that she is so.", "그렇다고 스스로 흐뭇해하기도 하지요."], ["Of course, Miss Dunstable, you are accustomed to things on a much grander scale.”", "물론 던스터블 양은 훨씬 더 웅장한 것들에 익숙하겠지만요.”"]], [["“I!", "“저요!"], ["Lord bless you, no!", "세상에, 천만에요!"], ["Nobody hates grandeur so much as I do.", "저만큼 웅장함을 싫어하는 사람은 없어요."], ["Of course I must do as I am told.", "물론 저는 시키는 대로 해야 하지요."], ["I must live in a big House, and have three footmen six feet high.", "큰 집에 살아야 하고, 키가 여섯 피트나 되는 하인을 셋 두어야 하고요."], ["I must have a coachman with a top-heavy wig, and horses so big that they frighten me.", "가발이 머리에 무겁게 얹힌 마부와 저를 겁먹게 할 만큼 큰 말들을 두어야 해요."], ["If I did not, I should be made out a lunatic and declared unable to manage my own affairs.", "그러지 않으면 저는 미친 사람으로 몰려서 제 일도 제가 못 다스린다는 판정을 받을 테니까요."], ["But as for grandeur, I hate it.", "그렇지만 웅장함이라면, 저는 그걸 싫어해요."], ["I certainly think that I shall have some of these conversaziones.", "저도 이 콘베르사치오네라는 것을 좀 해 봐야겠다고 정말로 생각해요."], ["I wonder whether Mrs. Proudie will come and put me up to a wrinkle or two.”", "프라우디 부인이 와서 요령을 한두 가지 알려 주실지 모르겠네요.”"]], [["The bishop again rubbed his hands, and said that he was sure she would.", "주교는 다시 두 손을 맞비비며 아내가 틀림없이 그러할 것이라고 말했다."], ["He never felt quite at his ease with Miss Dunstable, as he rarely could ascertain whether or no she was earnest in what she was saying.", "그는 던스터블 양과 함께 있으면 도무지 마음이 편치 않았으니, 그녀가 하는 말이 진심인지 아닌지를 좀처럼 가려낼 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["So he trotted off, muttering some excuse as he went, and Miss Dunstable chuckled with an inward chuckle at his too evident bewilderment.", "그래서 그는 무슨 핑계를 중얼거리며 총총 가 버렸고, 던스터블 양은 그가 너무나 뚜렷하게 드러낸 어리둥절함에 속으로 킥킥 웃었다."], ["Miss Dunstable was by nature kind, generous, and open-hearted; but she was living now very much with people on whom kindness, generosity, and open-heartedness were thrown away.", "던스터블 양은 본디 친절하고 너그럽고 마음이 열린 사람이었다. 그러나 그녀는 지금 친절과 너그러움과 열린 마음이 헛되이 버려지는 사람들 사이에서 아주 많이 살고 있었다."], ["She was clever also, and could be sarcastic; and she found that those qualities told better in the world around her than generosity and an open heart.", "그녀는 또한 영리했고 비꼴 줄도 알았다. 그리고 그런 재주가 그녀를 둘러싼 세상에서는 너그러움과 열린 마음보다 더 잘 통한다는 것을 알아냈다."], ["And so she went on from month to month, and year to year, not progressing in a good spirit as she might have done, but still carrying within her bosom a warm affection for those she could really love.", "그래서 그녀는 달이 가고 해가 가도, 할 수도 있었을 만큼 좋은 기운으로 나아가지는 못한 채, 그래도 참으로 사랑할 수 있는 이들을 향한 따뜻한 애정만은 가슴에 품고 지내 왔다."], ["And she knew that she was hardly living as she should live,—that the wealth which she affected to despise was eating into the soundness of her character, not by its splendour, but by the style of life which it had seemed to produce as a necessity.", "그리고 그녀는 자기가 마땅히 살아야 할 대로 살고 있지 못하다는 것을, 자기가 업신여기는 척하는 그 부에 제 성품의 튼튼함이 먹혀 들어가고 있다는 것을 알았다. 그 부의 화려함 때문이 아니라, 그것이 마치 필연인 듯 만들어 내는 삶의 방식 때문에."], ["She knew that she was gradually becoming irreverent, scornful, and prone to ridicule; but yet, knowing this and hating it, she hardly knew how to break from it.", "그녀는 자기가 차츰 경건함을 잃고 비웃기 좋아하고 우스개로 몰아가기 쉬운 사람이 되어 가고 있음을 알았다. 그러나 이것을 알면서 또 이것을 미워하면서도, 그녀는 거기서 벗어날 길을 좀처럼 알지 못했다."]], [["She had seen so much of the blacker side of human nature that blackness no longer startled her as it should do.", "그녀는 인간 본성의 더 검은 쪽을 너무나 많이 보아 온 터라, 검음이 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 그녀를 놀라게 하지 않았다."], ["She had been the prize at which so many ruined spendthrifts had aimed; so many pirates had endeavoured to run her down while sailing in the open waters of life, that she had ceased to regard such attempts on her money-bags as unmanly or over-covetous.", "그녀는 그토록 많은 망해 버린 낭비꾼들이 겨눈 상금이었고, 삶의 열린 물길을 지나가는 그녀를 그토록 많은 해적들이 들이받아 침몰시키려 했으므로, 그녀는 자기 돈주머니를 겨눈 그런 시도를 사내답지 못하다거나 지나치게 탐욕스럽다고 여기기를 그만두었다."], ["She was content to fight her own battle with her own weapons, feeling secure in her own strength of purpose and strength of wit.", "그녀는 자기 뜻의 힘과 슬기의 힘을 믿고 든든해하며, 제 싸움을 제 무기로 싸우는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["Some few friends she had whom she really loved,—among whom her inner self could come out and speak boldly what it had to say with its own true voice.", "그녀에게는 참으로 사랑하는 벗이 몇 사람 있었으니 — 그들 사이에서는 그녀의 속 자아가 나와서 제 참된 목소리로 할 말을 담대하게 할 수 있었다."], ["And the woman who thus so spoke was very different from that Miss Dunstable whom Mrs. Proudie courted, and the Duke of Omnium fêted, and Mrs.", "그리고 그렇게 말하는 그 여인은 프라우디 부인이 떠받들고 옴니엄 공작이 잔치로 대접하고 해럴드 스미스 부인이 가슴에 품은 벗이라 주장하는 저 던스터블 양과는 아주 달랐다."], ["Harold Smith claimed as her bosom friend. If only she could find among such one special companion on whom her heart might rest, who would help her to bear the heavy burdens of her world!", "그런 이들 가운데서 그녀의 마음이 쉴 수 있는, 제 세상의 무거운 짐을 지는 것을 도와줄 특별한 동무 하나를 찾을 수만 있다면!"], ["But where was she to find such a friend?—she with her keen wit, her untold money, and loud laughing voice.", "그러나 그런 벗을 어디서 찾을 것인가? — 날카로운 슬기와 헤아릴 수 없는 돈과 크게 웃는 목소리를 지닌 그녀가."], ["Everything about her was calculated to attract those whom she could not value, and to scare from her the sort of friend to whom she would fain have linked her lot.", "그녀에 관한 모든 것은 그녀가 값있게 여길 수 없는 이들을 끌어당기고, 제 운명을 기꺼이 이어 매고 싶은 그런 벗은 그녀에게서 겁을 주어 쫓아 버리도록 마련되어 있었다."]], [["And then she met Mrs. Harold Smith, who had taken Mrs.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 해럴드 스미스 부인을 만났다."], ["Proudie’s noble suite of rooms in her tour for the evening, and was devoting to them a period of twenty minutes.", "그 부인은 저녁 순회 길에 프라우디 부인의 훌륭한 방 한 벌을 일정에 넣고 거기에 이십 분을 바치고 있었다."], ["“And so I may congratulate you,”", "“그러니 축하드려도 되겠지요,”"], ["Miss Dunstable said eagerly to her friend.", "던스터블 양이 벗에게 서둘러 말했다."]], [["“No, in mercy’s name do no such thing, or you may too probably have to uncongratulate me again; and that will be so unpleasant.”", "“아니에요, 제발 그러지 마세요. 그러지 않으면 아무래도 축하를 도로 거두어들여야 할 판인데, 그건 너무 언짢은 일일 테니까요.”"]], [["“But they told me that Lord Brock had sent for him yesterday.”", "“그렇지만 어제 브록 경이 그이를 불렀다고 하던데요.”"], ["Now at this period Lord Brock was Prime Minister.", "이 무렵 브록 경은 총리였다."]], [["“So he did, and Harold was with him backwards and forwards all the day.", "“불렀지요. 그래서 해럴드는 하루 종일 그이와 이리 갔다 저리 갔다 했어요."], ["But he can’t shut his eyes and open his mouth, and see what God will send him, as a wise and prudent man should do.", "그렇지만 그이는 슬기롭고 신중한 사람이 마땅히 하는 대로, 눈을 감고 입을 벌리고 하나님이 무엇을 주실지 지켜볼 줄을 몰라요."], ["He is always for bargaining, and no Prime Minister likes that.”", "언제나 흥정을 하려 드는데, 흥정을 좋아하는 총리는 없지요.”"]], [["“I would not be in his shoes if, after all, he has to come home and say that the bargain is off.”", "“그이가 결국 집에 돌아와서 흥정이 깨졌다고 말해야 한다면, 저는 그 처지에 서고 싶지 않네요.”"]], [["“Ha, ha, ha!", "“하하하!"], ["Well, I should not take it very quietly.", "글쎄요, 저도 아주 가만히 받아들이지는 않을 거예요."], ["But what can we poor women do, you know?", "그렇지만 우리 딱한 여자들이 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["When it is settled, my dear, I’ll send you a line at once.”", "정해지면, 이봐요, 곧바로 한 줄 적어 보낼게요.”"], ["And then Mrs. Harold Smith finished her course round the rooms, and regained her carriage within the twenty minutes.", "그러고 나서 해럴드 스미스 부인은 방들을 돌아보는 일정을 마치고 이십 분 안에 자기 마차로 돌아갔다."]], [["“Beautiful profile, has she not?”", "“옆얼굴이 아름답지요, 그렇지 않아요?”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, somewhat later in the evening, to Mrs. Proudie.", "저녁이 좀 더 깊었을 때 던스터블 양이 프라우디 부인에게 말했다."], ["Of course, the profile spoken of belonged to Miss Grantly.", "물론 여기서 말한 옆얼굴은 그랜틀리 양의 것이었다."]], [["“Yes, it is beautiful, certainly,”", "“그래요, 확실히 아름답소,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["“The pity is that it means nothing.”", "“딱한 것은 그것이 아무 뜻도 없다는 점이오.”"]], [["“The gentlemen seem to think that it means a good deal.”", "“신사들은 그것이 꽤 많은 뜻을 지녔다고 생각하는 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“I am not sure of that.", "“나는 그 점을 잘 모르겠소."], ["She has no conversation, you see; not a word.", "보다시피 그 애에게는 대화가 없소."], ["She has been sitting there with Lord Dumbello at her elbow for the last hour, and yet she has hardly opened her mouth three times.”", "한 마디도 없지. 지난 한 시간 동안 덤벨로 경을 팔꿈치 옆에 두고 저기 앉아 있었으면서 입을 세 번도 채 열지 않았소.”"]], [["“But, my dear Mrs. Proudie, who on earth could talk to Lord Dumbello?”", "“하지만 프라우디 부인, 대체 누가 덤벨로 경에게 말을 걸 수 있겠어요?”"]], [["Mrs. Proudie thought that her own daughter Olivia would undoubtedly be able to do so, if only she could get the opportunity.", "프라우디 부인은 자기 딸 올리비아라면 기회만 얻는다면 틀림없이 그럴 수 있으리라고 생각했다."], ["But, then, Olivia had so much conversation.", "하기야 올리비아에게는 대화가 그토록 많았으니."]], [["And while the two ladies were yet looking at the youthful pair, Lord Dumbello did speak again.", "그리고 두 부인이 아직 그 젊은 한 쌍을 바라보고 있는 동안, 덤벨로 경이 다시 입을 열었다."], ["“I think I have had enough of this now,”", "“이만하면 이제 충분한 것 같소,”"], ["said he, addressing himself to Griselda.", "그는 그리젤다에게 말을 걸며 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“I suppose you have other engagements,”", "“다른 약속이 있으신가 봐요,”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes; and I believe I shall go to Lady Clantelbrocks.”", "“아, 그렇소. 레이디 클랜텔브록스 댁으로 갈 생각이오.”"], ["And then he took his departure.", "그러고 나서 그는 자리를 떴다."], ["No other word was spoken that evening between him and Miss Grantly beyond those given in this chronicle, and yet the world declared that he and that young lady had passed the evening in so close a flirtation as to make the matter more than ordinarily particular; and Mrs.", "그날 저녁 그와 그랜틀리 양 사이에 이 기록에 적힌 것 말고는 다른 어떤 말도 오가지 않았다. 그런데도 세상은 그와 그 젊은 아가씨가 여느 것보다 유별나다고 할 만큼 바짝 붙은 연애질로 저녁을 보냈다고 떠들었다."], ["Grantly, as she was driven home to her lodgings, began to have doubts in her mind whether it would be wise to discountenance so great an alliance as that which the head of the great Hartletop family now seemed so desirous to establish.", "그리고 그랜틀리 부인은 마차를 타고 숙소로 돌아가면서, 이제 그 대단한 하틀탑 집안의 수장이 그토록 맺고 싶어 하는 듯한 그만한 동맹을 못마땅해하는 것이 슬기로운 일일지 마음속에 의심이 들기 시작했다."], ["The prudent mother had not yet spoken a word to her daughter on these subjects, but it might soon become necessary to do so.", "신중한 어머니는 이런 문제를 두고 딸에게 아직 한 마디도 하지 않았으나, 머지않아 그렇게 해야 할지도 몰랐다."], ["It was all very well for Lady Lufton to hurry up to town, but of what service would that be, if Lord Lufton were not to be found in Bruton Street?", "레이디 러프턴이 서둘러 런던에 올라오는 것도 다 좋은 일이지만, 러프턴 경이 브루턴 스트리트에 없다면 그것이 무슨 도움이 되겠는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER XVIII. THE NEW MINISTER’S PATRONAGE.", "# 제18장. 새 각료의 후원"]], [["At that time, just as Lady Lufton was about to leave Framley for London, Mark Robarts received a pressing letter, inviting him also to go up to the metropolis for a day or two—not for pleasure, but on business.", "그때, 마침 레이디 러프턴이 프램리를 떠나 런던으로 향하려 하던 무렵, 마크 로바츠는 그 역시 하루나 이틀쯤 수도로 올라오라고 다급히 청하는 편지 한 통을 받았다 — 놀러 오라는 것이 아니라 볼일이 있어서였다."], ["The letter was from his indefatigable friend Sowerby.", "편지는 지칠 줄 모르는 그의 벗 사워비에게서 온 것이었다."]], [["“My dear Robarts,”", "“친애하는 로바츠,”"], ["the letter ran:—", "편지는 이렇게 시작했다:—"]], [["I have just heard that poor little Burslem, the Barsetshire prebendary, is dead.", "바셋주 참사회원인 딱하고 작달막한 버슬렘이 죽었다는 소식을 방금 들었어요."], ["We must all die some day, you know,—as you have told your parishioners from the Framley pulpit more than once, no doubt.", "사람은 누구나 언젠가 죽는 법이지요 — 당신도 프램리 설교단에서 교구민들에게 한두 번은 그렇게 일러 주었을 테고요."], ["The stall must be filled up, and why should not you have it as well as another?", "그 자리는 채워져야 하는데, 다른 사람이 아니라 당신이 그것을 차지하면 안 될 이유가 무엇입니까?"], ["It is six hundred a year and a house.", "연 육백 파운드에 집 한 채예요."], ["Little Burslem had nine, but the good old times are gone.", "버슬렘 영감 때는 구백이었지만 좋았던 옛 시절은 다 갔지요."], ["Whether the house is letable or not under the present ecclesiastical régime, I do not know.", "지금의 교회 체제 아래에서 그 집을 세놓을 수 있는지는 나도 모르겠어요. 전에는 그랬으니까요."], ["It used to be so, for I remember Mrs. Wiggins, the tallow-chandler’s widow, living in old Stanhope’s house.", "양초 장수의 미망인 위긴스 부인이 스탠호프 영감의 집에 살던 것을 나는 기억하고 있으니."]], [["Harold Smith has just joined the Government as Lord Petty Bag, and could, I think, at the present moment get this for asking.", "해럴드 스미스가 방금 프티백 경으로 정부에 들어갔으니, 지금 이 순간이라면 청하기만 해도 이것을 얻어 낼 수 있으리라고 나는 생각해요."], ["He cannot well refuse me, and, if you will say the word, I will speak to him.", "그는 내 청을 딱 잘라 거절하지 못할 사람이고, 당신이 한마디만 해 주면 내가 그에게 말을 넣겠어요."], ["You had better come up yourself; but say the word “Yes,”", "당신이 직접 올라오는 편이 좋겠지만, 우선 전신으로 “예” 아니면 “아니오”"], ["or “No,” by the wires.", "한마디를 보내 주세요."]], [["If you say “Yes,” as of course you will, do not fail to come up.", "물론 그러시겠지만 “예”라고 하실 참이면 꼭 올라오세요."], ["You will find me at the “Travellers,” or at the House.", "나는 “트래벌러스”나 하원에 있을 겁니다."], ["The stall will just suit you,—will give you no trouble, improve your position, and give some little assistance towards bed and board, and rack and manger.", "그 자리는 당신에게 딱 맞아요 — 수고로울 일은 하나도 없고, 당신의 지위를 높여 주고, 침대와 식탁에, 여물 시렁과 여물통에까지 얼마간 보태 줄 테니."]], [["Yours ever faithfully,", "언제나 그대의 벗,"]], [["Singularly enough, I hear that your brother is private secretary to the new Lord Petty Bag.", "참으로 묘하게도, 당신 동생이 새 프티백 경의 개인 비서라는 이야기를 들었어요."], ["I am told that his chief duty will consist in desiring the servants to call my sister’s carriage.", "그의 주된 임무란 하인들더러 내 누이의 마차를 부르라고 이르는 일이 될 거라더군요."], ["I have only seen Harold once since he accepted office; but my Lady Petty Bag says that he has certainly grown an inch since that occurrence.", "해럴드가 취임한 뒤로 나는 그를 한 번밖에 보지 못했지만, 레이디 프티백은 그 일이 있은 뒤로 그의 키가 한 치는 자랐다고 하더군요."]], [["This was certainly very good-natured on the part of Mr. Sowerby, and showed that he had a feeling within his bosom that he owed something to his friend the parson for the injury he had done him.", "이는 분명 사워비 씨로서는 매우 선량한 짓이었고, 자기가 그 목사 벗에게 끼친 해악에 대해 무언가 갚아야 할 것이 있다는 느낌을 가슴에 품고 있음을 보여 주었다."], ["And such was in truth the case.", "그리고 실상이 정말 그러했다."], ["A more reckless being than the member for West Barsetshire could not exist.", "서바셋주 의원보다 더 앞뒤 없는 인간은 있을 수 없었다."], ["He was reckless for himself, and reckless for all others with whom he might be concerned.", "그는 자신에 대해서도 앞뒤가 없었고, 자기와 얽히게 되는 다른 모든 사람에 대해서도 앞뒤가 없었다."], ["He could ruin his friends with as little remorse as he had ruined himself.", "그는 제 자신을 망친 것과 마찬가지로 거의 아무 뉘우침 없이 벗들을 망칠 수 있었다."], ["All was fair game that came in the way of his net.", "그의 그물 앞에 들어온 것은 무엇이든 정당한 사냥감이었다."], ["But, nevertheless, he was good-natured, and willing to move heaven and earth to do a friend a good turn, if it came in his way to do so.", "그러나 그럼에도 그는 선량했고, 자기 길에 그럴 기회가 닿기만 하면 벗에게 좋은 일을 해 주기 위해 하늘과 땅을 움직일 마음이 있었다."]], [["He did really love Mark Robarts as much as it was given him to love any among his acquaintance.", "그는 정말로 마크 로바츠를, 자기가 아는 누군가를 사랑할 수 있는 만큼은 사랑했다."], ["He knew that he had already done him an almost irreparable injury, and might very probably injure him still deeper before he had done with him.", "그는 자기가 이미 그에게 거의 돌이킬 수 없는 해를 입혔음을 알고 있었고, 그와 볼일이 끝나기 전에 아마도 더 깊이 해를 입힐 가망이 매우 높다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["That he would undoubtedly do so, if it came in his way, was very certain.", "그럴 기회가 닿는다면 틀림없이 그렇게 할 것임은 아주 확실했다."], ["But then, if it also came in his way to repay his friend by any side blow, he would also undoubtedly do that.", "그러나 또한 곁가지로나마 벗에게 갚아 줄 기회가 닿는다면, 그것 또한 틀림없이 할 것이었다."], ["Such an occasion had now come, and he had desired his sister to give the new Lord Petty Bag no rest till he should have promised to use all his influence in getting the vacant prebend for Mark Robarts.", "그런 기회가 이제 온 것이고, 그래서 그는 누이에게, 새 프티백 경이 빈 참사회원 자리를 마크 로바츠에게 얻어 주는 데 자기 영향력을 다 쓰겠다고 약속할 때까지 그를 가만두지 말라고 일러 두었던 것이다."]], [["This letter of Sowerby’s Mark immediately showed to his wife.", "사워비의 이 편지를 마크는 즉시 아내에게 보여 주었다."], ["How lucky, thought he to himself, that not a word was said in it about those accursed money transactions!", "저 저주받은 돈거래에 대해서는 한마디도 적히지 않았으니 얼마나 다행인가, 하고 그는 속으로 생각했다."], ["Had he understood Sowerby better he would have known that that gentleman never said anything about money transactions until it became absolutely necessary.", "사워비를 더 잘 알았더라면, 그 신사는 절대적으로 필요해지기 전까지는 돈거래에 대해 결코 아무 말도 하지 않는다는 것을 알았을 것이다."], ["“I know you don’t like Mr. Sowerby,”", "“당신이 사워비 씨를 좋아하지 않는 건 알아,”"], ["he said; “but you must own that this is very good-natured.”", "그가 말했다. “하지만 이건 참 선량한 짓이라는 것은 인정해야지.”"]], [["“It is the character I hear of him that I don’t like,”", "“제가 싫은 건 그 사람에 대해 제가 듣는 평판이에요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“But what shall I do now, Fanny?", "“하지만 이제 어쩌면 좋겠어, 패니?"], ["As he says, why should not I have the stall as well as another?”", "그의 말대로, 다른 사람이 아니라 내가 그 자리를 차지하면 안 될 이유가 무엇이겠어?”"]], [["“I suppose it would not interfere with your parish?”", "“교구 일에 지장은 없겠지요?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Not in the least, at the distance at which we are.", "“우리가 있는 거리라면 조금도 지장이 없지."], ["I did think of giving up old Jones; but if I take this, of course I must keep a curate.”", "존스 노인을 내보낼까 생각하기도 했지만, 이걸 맡게 되면 물론 부목사를 두어야 하겠지.”"]], [["His wife could not find it in her heart to dissuade him from accepting promotion when it came in his way—what vicar’s wife would have so persuaded her husband?", "아내는 승진이 제 앞에 왔을 때 그것을 받아들이지 말라고 남편을 만류할 마음을 낼 수 없었다 — 어느 교구 사제의 아내가 남편을 그렇게 설득하겠는가?"], ["But yet she did not altogether like it.", "그러나 그래도 그녀는 그것이 온전히 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["She feared that Greek from Chaldicotes, even when he came with the present of a prebendal stall in his hands.", "그녀는 콜디코츠에서 온 저 그리스인이, 손에 참사회원 자리라는 선물을 들고 올 때에도 두려웠다."], ["And then what would Lady Lufton say?", "그리고 그렇다면 레이디 러프턴은 무엇이라 할 것인가?"]], [["“And do you think that you must go up to London, Mark?”", "“그러면 당신은 런던으로 올라가야 한다고 생각하시는 거예요, 마크?”"]], [["“Oh, certainly; that is, if I intend to accept Harold Smith’s kind offices in the matter.”", "“물론이지. 그러니까, 이 일에서 해럴드 스미스의 호의를 받아들일 작정이라면 말이야.”"]], [["“I suppose it will be better to accept them,”", "“받아들이는 편이 낫겠지요,” 패니가 말했다."], ["said Fanny, feeling perhaps that it would be useless in her to hope that they should not be accepted.", "아마도 받아들이지 않기를 바라는 것은 자기로서 부질없는 일이라고 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["“Prebendal stalls, Fanny, don’t generally go begging long among parish clergymen.", "“참사회원 자리란 것이, 패니, 교구 성직자들 사이에서 임자를 못 찾고 오래 남아 있는 물건이 아니야."], ["How could I reconcile it to the duty I owe to my children to refuse such an increase to my income?”", "내 수입에 이만한 보탬을 거절하면서 어떻게 자식들에게 진 의무와 앞뒤를 맞출 수 있겠어?”"], ["And so it was settled that he should at once drive to Silverbridge and send off a message by telegraph, and that he should himself proceed to London on the following day.", "그리하여 그가 곧 실버브리지로 마차를 몰고 가 전신으로 소식을 보내고, 이튿날 몸소 런던으로 떠나는 것으로 정해졌다."], ["“But you must see Lady Lufton first, of course,”", "“하지만 물론 먼저 레이디 러프턴을 뵈어야 해요,”"], ["said Fanny, as soon as all this was settled.", "이 모든 것이 정해지자마자 패니가 말했다."]], [["Mark would have avoided this if he could have decently done so, but he felt that it would be impolitic, as well as indecent.", "마크는 점잖게 그럴 수 있었다면 이것을 피했을 것이나, 그것이 무례할 뿐 아니라 정략에도 어긋나는 일이라고 느꼈다."], ["And why should he be afraid to tell Lady Lufton that he hoped to receive this piece of promotion from the present government?", "그리고 지금의 정부로부터 이런 승진을 받게 되기를 바란다고 레이디 러프턴에게 말하기를 왜 두려워해야 하는가?"], ["There was nothing disgraceful in a clergyman becoming a prebendary of Barchester.", "성직자가 바체스터의 참사회원이 되는 데 부끄러운 일은 하나도 없었다."], ["Lady Lufton herself had always been very civil to the prebendaries, and especially to little Dr. Burslem, the meagre little man who had just now paid the debt of nature.", "레이디 러프턴 자신도 참사회원들에게는 늘 매우 정중했고, 특히 얼마 전에 자연에 진 빚을 갚은 그 앙상하고 작달막한 사내, 작달막한 버슬렘 박사에게 그러했다."], ["She had always been very fond of the chapter, and her original dislike to Bishop Proudie had been chiefly founded on his interference with the cathedral clergy,—on his interference, or on that of his wife or chaplain.", "그녀는 늘 성당 참사회를 매우 좋아했고, 프라우디 주교에 대한 그녀의 본래 반감도 주로 그가 대성당 성직자들에게 간섭한 데에 — 그의 간섭에, 또는 그의 아내나 전속 사제의 간섭에 — 뿌리를 두고 있었다."], ["Considering these things Mark Robarts tried to make himself believe that Lady Lufton would be delighted at his good fortune.", "이런 것들을 헤아리며 마크 로바츠는 레이디 러프턴이 자기의 좋은 운을 기뻐할 것이라고 스스로 믿어 보려 했다."], ["But yet he did not believe it.", "그러나 그는 그것을 믿지 못했다."], ["She at any rate would revolt from the gift of the Greek of Chaldicotes.", "적어도 그녀는 콜디코츠의 그리스인이 주는 선물에는 진저리를 낼 것이었다."]], [["“Oh, indeed,”", "“아, 그렇군요,”"], ["she said, when the vicar had with some difficulty explained to her all the circumstances of the case.", "교구 사제가 자기 사정의 온 자락을 힘겹게 설명해 내자 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Well, I congratulate you, Mr.", "“그러면 축하해요, 로바츠 씨."], ["Robarts, on your powerful new patron.”", "힘 있는 새 후원자를 두셨으니.”"]], [["“You will probably feel with me, Lady Lufton, that the benefice is one which I can hold without any detriment to me in my position here at Framley,”", "“레이디 러프턴, 이 성직록은 제가 이곳 프램리에서 맡은 자리에 아무 지장 없이 함께 지닐 수 있는 것이라는 데는 아마 저와 같이 생각하실 것입니다,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, prudently resolving to let the slur upon his friends pass by unheeded.", "벗들에게 던져진 그 비아냥은 못 들은 척 흘려보내기로 조심스레 작정한 것이었다."]], [["“Well, I hope so.", "“그러기를 바라요."], ["Of course, you are a very young man, Mr. Robarts, and these things have generally been given to clergymen more advanced in life.”", "물론 로바츠 씨는 아주 젊은 분이고, 이런 것들은 대개 나이가 더 지긋한 성직자들에게 주어져 왔지요.”"]], [["“But you do not mean to say that you think I ought to refuse it?”", "“하지만 제가 그것을 거절해야 한다고 말씀하시려는 것은 아니시겠지요?”"]], [["“What my advice to you might be if you really came to me for advice, I am hardly prepared to say at so very short a notice.", "“정말로 제 조언을 구하러 오신 것이라면 제 조언이 무엇일지, 이렇게 갑작스러운 통고를 받고서는 저도 뭐라 말할 준비가 되어 있지 않아요."], ["You seem to have made up your mind, and therefore I need not consider it.", "로바츠 씨는 이미 마음을 정한 듯하니, 그러면 저는 그것을 헤아릴 필요가 없겠지요."], ["As it is, I wish you joy, and hope that it may turn out to your advantage in every way.”", "사정이 그러하니 기쁨을 함께 하겠어요. 모든 면에서 로바츠 씨에게 이롭게 되기를 바라요.”"]], [["“You understand, Lady Lufton, that I have by no means got it as yet.”", "“레이디 러프턴, 저는 아직 그것을 결코 얻은 것이 아니라는 걸 아셔야 합니다.”"]], [["“Oh, I thought it had been offered to you: I thought you spoke of this new minister as having all that in his own hand.”", "“아, 저는 이미 제안을 받으신 줄 알았어요. 그 새 각료가 그 모든 것을 제 손에 쥐고 있는 것처럼 말씀하신 줄 알았으니.”"]], [["“Oh, dear, no.", "“아, 아닙니다."], ["What may be the amount of his influence in that respect I do not at all know.", "그 점에서 그의 영향력이 얼마만 한지는 저도 전혀 모릅니다."], ["But my correspondent assures me—”", "다만 제게 편지를 보낸 사람이 확언하기로는—”"]], [["“Mr. Sowerby, you mean.", "“사워비 씨 말이지요."], ["Why don’t you call him by his name?”", "왜 그 사람 이름을 대지 않으시는 거예요?”"]], [["“Mr. Sowerby assures me that Mr. Smith will ask for it; and thinks it most probable that his request will be successful.”", "“사워비 씨가 확언하기로는 스미스 씨가 그것을 청할 것이며, 그 청이 이루어질 가망이 매우 높다고 합니다.”"]], [["“Oh, of course. Mr.", "“아, 물론 그렇겠지요."], ["Sowerby and Mr. Harold Smith together would no doubt be successful in anything.", "사워비 씨와 해럴드 스미스 씨가 함께라면 무슨 일에서든 틀림없이 이루어 내겠지요."], ["They are the sort of men who are successful nowadays.", "요즘 세상에서 이루어 내는 사람들이 바로 그런 부류니까요."], ["Well, Mr. Robarts, I wish you joy.”", "그러면 로바츠 씨, 기쁨을 함께 하겠어요.”"], ["And she gave him her hand in token of her sincerity.", "그러고서 그녀는 자기 말이 진심이라는 표시로 그에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["Mark took her hand, resolving to say nothing further on that occasion.", "마크는 그녀의 손을 잡았고, 그 자리에서는 더 아무 말도 하지 않기로 작정했다."], ["That Lady Lufton was not now cordial with him, as she used to be, he was well aware; and sooner or later he was determined to have the matter out with her.", "레이디 러프턴이 예전처럼 자기에게 정을 담아 대하지 않는다는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었다. 그리고 이르든 늦든 그 문제를 그녀와 끝까지 따져 볼 작정이었다."], ["He would ask her why she now so constantly met him with a taunt, and so seldom greeted him with that kind old affectionate smile which he knew and appreciated so well.", "왜 이제는 자기를 만나면 거의 늘 비아냥으로 맞이하고, 자기가 그토록 잘 알고 그토록 고맙게 여기던 그 다정하고 오래된 미소로는 좀처럼 반겨 주지 않는지 물어보려 했다."], ["That she was honest and true, he was quite sure.", "그녀가 정직하고 참되다는 것은 아주 확신하고 있었다."], ["If he asked her the question plainly, she would answer him openly.", "대놓고 그 질문을 던지면 그녀는 숨김없이 대답해 줄 것이었다."], ["And if he could induce her to say that she would return to her old ways, return to them she would in a hearty manner.", "그리고 예전의 방식으로 돌아가겠다는 말을 그녀에게서 끌어낼 수 있다면, 그녀는 진심을 다해 그리로 돌아올 것이었다."], ["But he could not do this just at present. It was but a day or two since Mr.", "그러나 그는 바로 지금은 그렇게 할 수 없었다."], ["Crawley had been with him; and was it not probable that Mr. Crawley had been sent thither by Lady Lufton?", "크롤리 씨가 그를 찾아온 것이 하루나 이틀 전이었으니, 크롤리 씨를 그곳으로 보낸 것이 레이디 러프턴이었을 법하지 않은가?"], ["His own hands were not clean enough for a remonstrance at the present moment.", "지금 이 순간 그의 손은 항변을 하기에 그리 깨끗하지 못했다."], ["He would cleanse them, and then he would remonstrate.", "그는 손을 씻을 것이고, 그런 다음에 항변할 것이었다."]], [["“Would you like to live part of the year in Barchester?”", "“해마다 얼마간은 바체스터에서 사는 게 어떻겠어?”"], ["he said to his wife and sister that evening.", "그날 저녁 그가 아내와 누이에게 말했다."]], [["“I think that two houses are only a trouble,”", "“집이 둘이면 번거로울 뿐이지요,”"], ["said his wife.", "아내가 말했다."], ["“And we have been very happy here.”", "“게다가 우리는 여기서 아주 행복했잖아요.”"]], [["“I have always liked a cathedral town,”", "“저는 대성당이 있는 도시를 늘 좋아했어요,”"], ["said Lucy; “and I am particularly fond of the close.”", "루시가 말했다. “그중에도 성당 구내가 특별히 마음에 들고요.”"]], [["“And Barchester-close is the closest of all closes,” said Mark.", "“게다가 바체스터 구내야말로 온 구내 가운데 가장 꽉 닫힌 구내지,”"], ["“There is not a single house within the gateways that does not belong to the chapter.”", "마크가 말했다. “그 문 안에 성당 참사회에 딸리지 않은 집은 한 채도 없어.”"]], [["“But if we are to keep up two houses, the additional income will soon be wasted,”", "“하지만 집을 둘 유지해야 한다면 늘어난 수입은 금세 다 사라질 거예요,”"], ["said Fanny prudently.", "패니가 조심스레 말했다."]], [["“The thing would be, to let the house furnished every summer,”", "“여름마다 가구까지 얹어서 세를 놓으면 되잖아요,”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“But I must take my residence as the terms come,”", "“하지만 나는 정해진 기간이 오는 대로 거주 의무를 채워야 해,”"], ["said the vicar; “and I certainly should not like to be away from Framley all the winter; I should never see anything of Lufton.”", "교구 사제가 말했다. “그리고 겨울 내내 프램리를 떠나 있는 건 정말이지 마음에 안 들어."], ["And perhaps he thought of his hunting, and then thought again of that cleansing of his hands.", "그러면 러프턴을 도무지 볼 수 없게 되지.” 그리고 그는 아마도 자기 사냥을 생각했고, 그다음 다시 저 손을 씻는 일을 생각했다."]], [["“I should not a bit mind being away during the winter,”", "“저는 겨울에 떠나 있는 것쯤은 조금도 상관없어요,”"], ["said Lucy, thinking of what the last winter had done for her.", "루시가 말했다. 지난 겨울이 자기에게 해 놓은 일을 생각하면서였다."]], [["“But where on earth should we find money to furnish one of those large, old-fashioned houses?", "“하지만 그런 크고 예스러운 집 하나에 가구를 채울 돈을 도대체 어디서 구하겠어요?"], ["Pray, Mark, do not do anything rash.”", "부디, 마크, 경솔한 일은 하지 마세요.”"], ["And the wife laid her hand affectionately on her husband’s arm.", "그러면서 아내는 남편의 팔에 애정을 담아 손을 얹었다."], ["In this manner the question of the prebend was discussed between them on the evening before he started for London.", "이런 식으로 참사회원 자리 문제가, 그가 런던으로 떠나기 전날 저녁 두 사람 사이에서 오갔다."]], [["Success had at last crowned the earnest effort with which Harold Smith had carried on the political battle of his life for the last ten years.", "해럴드 스미스가 지난 십 년 동안 제 인생의 정치 싸움을 이어 오며 쏟아 온 진지한 노력에 마침내 성공이 왕관을 씌워 주었다."], ["The late Lord Petty Bag had resigned in disgust, having been unable to digest the Prime Minister’s ideas on Indian Reform, and Mr. Harold Smith, after sundry hitches in the business, was installed in his place.", "전임 프티백 경은 인도 개혁에 관한 총리의 생각을 삭일 수 없어 넌더리를 내며 사임했고, 해럴드 스미스 씨가 이런저런 우여곡절을 거친 끝에 그 자리에 앉혀졌다."], ["It was said that Harold Smith was not exactly the man whom the Premier would himself have chosen for that high office; but the Premier’s hands were a good deal tied by circumstances.", "해럴드 스미스가 그 높은 관직에 총리 자신이라면 골랐을 만한 사람은 딱히 아니라는 말이 돌았다. 그러나 총리의 손은 사정에 적잖이 묶여 있었다."], ["The last great appointment he had made had been terribly unpopular,—so much so as to subject him, popular as he undoubtedly was himself, to a screech from the whole nation.", "그가 마지막으로 했던 큰 임명은 끔찍하게 인심을 잃은 것이어서, 그 자신은 의심할 바 없이 인기가 있었음에도 온 나라의 비명을 그에게 뒤집어씌울 만한 것이었다."], ["The Jupiter, with withering scorn, had asked whether vice of every kind was to be considered, in these days of Queen Victoria, as a passport to the cabinet.", "《주피터》는 살을 태우는 조소로, 빅토리아 여왕의 이 시대에 온갖 종류의 악덕이 내각으로 들어가는 통행증으로 여겨져야 하느냐고 물었다."], ["Adverse members of both Houses had arrayed themselves in a pure panoply of morality, and thundered forth their sarcasms with the indignant virtue and keen discontent of political Juvenals; and even his own friends had held up their hands in dismay.", "양원의 반대편 의원들은 순결한 도덕의 갑주를 두르고, 정치판 유베날리스들의 분개한 덕성과 날카로운 불만을 담아 저희 야유를 우레처럼 쏟아 냈다. 그의 벗들조차 낙담해 두 손을 들었다."], ["Under these circumstances he had thought himself obliged in the present instance to select a man who would not be especially objectionable to any party.", "이런 사정 아래에서 그는 이번에는 어느 편에게도 특별히 거슬리지 않을 사람을 골라야 한다고 스스로 여겼다."], ["Now Harold Smith lived with his wife, and his circumstances were not more than ordinarily embarrassed.", "그런데 해럴드 스미스는 자기 아내와 함께 살고 있었고, 그의 처지는 여느 사람보다 더 궁하지도 않았다."], ["He kept no race-horses; and, as Lord Brock now heard for the first time, gave lectures in provincial towns on popular subjects.", "그는 경마 말을 두지 않았고, 그리고 브록 경이 이제야 처음으로 들은 바로는, 지방 도시들에서 대중적인 주제로 강연을 했다."], ["He had a seat which was tolerably secure, and could talk to the House by the yard if required to do so.", "그는 어지간히 안전한 의석을 가지고 있었고, 필요하다면 하원을 향해 몇 야드씩 늘어놓을 수도 있었다."], ["Moreover, Lord Brock had a great idea that the whole machinery of his own ministry would break to pieces very speedily.", "게다가 브록 경은 자기 내각의 기계 전체가 아주 이르게 산산조각 날 것이라는 생각을 크게 품고 있었다."], ["His own reputation was not bad, but it was insufficient for himself and that lately selected friend of his.", "그 자신의 평판은 나쁘지 않았으나, 그것으로는 자기와 저 최근에 고른 벗의 몫까지 대기에 부족했다."], ["Under all these circumstances combined, he chose Harold Smith to fill the vacant office of Lord Petty Bag.", "이 모든 사정이 겹친 아래에서 그는 비어 있는 프티백 경의 자리를 채울 사람으로 해럴드 스미스를 골랐다."]], [["And very proud the Lord Petty Bag was.", "그리고 프티백 경은 몹시 자랑스러웠다."], ["For the last three or four months, he and Mr. Supplehouse had been agreeing to consign the ministry to speedy perdition.", "지난 서너 달 동안 그와 서플하우스 씨는 이 내각을 속히 파멸로 보내 버리자고 뜻을 맞추어 왔다."], ["“This sort of dictatorship will never do,”", "“이런 식의 독재는 결코 통하지 않을 걸세,”"], ["Harold Smith had himself said, justifying that future vote of his as to want of confidence in the Queen’s government.", "해럴드 스미스는 여왕 정부에 대한 불신임에 관해 자기가 앞으로 던질 표를 그렇게 정당화하며 스스로 말한 바 있었다."], ["And Mr. Supplehouse in this matter had fully agreed with him.", "그리고 서플하우스 씨도 이 문제에서 그와 온전히 뜻이 같았다."], ["He was a Juno whose form that wicked old Paris had utterly despised, and he, too, had quite made up his mind as to the lobby in which he would be found when that day of vengeance should arrive.", "그는 저 못된 늙은 파리스가 그 모습을 아예 업신여겨 버린 유노였으니, 그 역시 복수의 날이 오면 자기가 어느 표결 복도에 서 있을지 마음을 굳게 정해 두었던 것이다."], ["But now things were much altered in Harold Smith’s views.", "그러나 이제 해럴드 스미스의 눈에는 사정이 크게 달라져 있었다."], ["The Premier had shown his wisdom in seeking for new strength where strength ought to be sought, and introducing new blood into the body of his ministry.", "총리는 힘을 구해야 할 곳에서 새 힘을 구하고 제 내각의 몸에 새 피를 들임으로써 자기 지혜를 보여 주었다."], ["The people would now feel fresh confidence, and probably the House also.", "이제 백성이 새로 신뢰를 느낄 것이고, 아마 하원도 그러할 것이다."], ["As to Mr. Supplehouse—he would use all his influence on Supplehouse.", "서플하우스 씨는 — 그에게는 자기 영향력을 다 써 보겠다."], ["But, after all, Mr. Supplehouse was not everything.", "그러나 결국 서플하우스 씨가 전부는 아니었다."]], [["On the morning after our vicar’s arrival in London he attended at the Petty Bag office.", "우리 교구 사제가 런던에 닿은 이튿날 아침 그는 프티백 청에 나갔다."], ["It was situated in the close neighbourhood of Downing Street and the higher governmental gods; and though the building itself was not much, seeing that it was shored up on one side, that it bulged out in the front, was foul with smoke, dingy with dirt, and was devoid of any single architectural grace or modern scientific improvement, nevertheless its position gave it a status in the world which made the clerks in the Lord Petty Bag’s office quite respectable in their walk in life.", "그것은 다우닝 가와 더 높은 정부의 신들 바로 이웃에 자리하고 있었고, 건물 자체는 별것 아니어서 한쪽은 버팀목으로 받쳐 놓았고 앞면은 불룩 튀어나왔으며 연기에 더럽고 먼지에 꾀죄죄하고 건축의 우아함이라든가 근대 과학의 개량이라 할 것이 하나도 없었으나, 그럼에도 그 위치가 그것에 지위를 주어 프티백 경 청의 서기들을 세상 살아가는 처지에서 아주 어엿한 사람으로 만들어 주었다."], ["Mark had seen his friend Sowerby on the previous evening, and had then made an appointment with him for the following morning at the new minister’s office.", "마크는 전날 저녁 벗 사워비를 만났고, 그때 이튿날 아침 새 각료의 청에서 만나기로 약속해 두었다."], ["And now he was there a little before his time, in order that he might have a few moments’ chat with his brother.", "그리고 이제 그는 동생과 몇 분 담소를 나눌 수 있도록 약속한 시간보다 조금 이르게 그곳에 와 있었다."]], [["When Mark found himself in the private secretary’s room he was quite astonished to see the change in his brother’s appearance which the change in his official rank had produced.", "개인 비서의 방에 들어선 마크는, 관등이 바뀌면서 동생의 겉모습에 생겨난 변화를 보고 꽤나 놀랐다."], ["Jack Robarts had been a well-built, straight-legged, lissome young fellow, pleasant to the eye because of his natural advantages, but rather given to a harum-skarum style of gait, and occasionally careless, not to say slovenly, in his dress.", "잭 로바츠는 몸이 잘 잡히고 다리가 곧고 몸놀림이 나긋한 젊은이여서 타고난 이점 덕에 보기에 즐거웠으나, 걸음새가 좀 덜렁대는 쪽이었고 옷차림에도 이따금 무심했다, 아니 지저분하다고 해도 좋았다."], ["But now he was the very pink of perfection.", "그러나 이제 그는 완벽의 표본 그 자체였다."], ["His jaunty frock-coat fitted him to perfection; not a hair of his head was out of place; his waistcoat and trousers were glossy and new, and his umbrella, which stood in the umbrella-stand in the corner, was tight, and neat, and small, and natty.", "멋들어진 프록코트는 그에게 흠 하나 없이 맞았고, 머리카락 한 올도 제자리를 벗어나지 않았고, 조끼와 바지는 윤이 나는 새것이었고, 구석의 우산걸이에 세워 둔 우산은 꼭 여미어져 있고 말끔하고 작고 앙증맞았다."]], [["“Well, John, you’ve become quite a great man,”", "“아, 존, 너 아주 큰사람이 됐구나,”"], ["said his brother.", "형이 말했다."]], [["“I don’t know much about that,”", "“그건 잘 모르겠어요,”"], ["said John; “but I find that I have an enormous deal of fagging to go through.”", "존이 말했다. “다만 제가 엄청나게 뼈 빠지는 일을 치러야 한다는 것은 알겠어요.”"]], [["“Do you mean work?", "“일 말이냐?"], ["I thought you had about the easiest berth in the whole Civil Service.”", "나는 네가 온 관청 자리 가운데 가장 편한 자리에 있는 줄 알았는데.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["that’s just the mistake that people make.", "바로 그게 사람들이 하는 착각이에요."], ["Because we don’t cover whole reams of foolscap paper at the rate of fifteen lines to a page, and five words to a line, people think that we private secretaries have got nothing to do.", "우리가 한 면에 열다섯 줄, 한 줄에 다섯 낱말씩으로 대판 서류지를 몇 연씩 메우지 않으니까, 사람들은 우리 개인 비서들이 하는 일이 없다고 생각하지요. 이걸 보세요,”"], ["Look here,” and he tossed over scornfully a dozen or so of little notes.", "그러면서 그는 작은 쪽지 열두 통쯤을 업신여기듯 툭 던져 놓았다."], ["“I tell you what, Mark; it is no easy matter to manage the patronage of a cabinet minister.", "“형, 내가 말하는데, 각료의 후원 인사를 다루는 것은 쉬운 일이 아니에요."], ["Now I am bound to write to every one of these fellows a letter that will please him; and yet I shall refuse to every one of them the request which he asks.”", "지금 나는 이 사람들 하나하나에게 그를 흡족하게 할 편지를 써야 하는데, 그러면서도 그들이 청하는 부탁은 하나하나 다 거절해야 하고요.”"]], [["“That must be difficult.”", "“그건 어렵겠구나.”"]], [["“Difficult is no word for it.", "“어렵다는 말로는 어림도 없어요."], ["But, after all, it consists chiefly in the knack of the thing.", "하지만 결국은 대개 그 요령에 달린 일이지요."], ["One must have the wit ‘from such a sharp and waspish word as No to pluck the sting.’ I do it every day, and I really think that the people like it.”", "‘아니오라는 저 날카롭고 쏘아 대는 말에서 침을 뽑아내는’ 재간이 있어야 해요. 나는 그것을 매일 하고 있고, 사람들이 정말로 그걸 좋아한다는 생각까지 들어요.”"]], [["“Perhaps your refusals are better than other people’s acquiescences.”", "“네 거절이 다른 사람들의 승낙보다 나은지도 모르겠구나.”"]], [["“I don’t mean that at all.", "“그런 뜻은 전혀 아니에요."], ["We private secretaries have all to do the same thing.", "우리 개인 비서들은 다 똑같은 일을 해야 해요."], ["Now, would you believe it?", "자, 이걸 믿을 수 있겠어요?"], ["I have used up three lifts of note-paper already in telling people that there is no vacancy for a lobby messenger in the Petty Bag office.", "프티백 청에 로비 사환 자리가 비지 않았다고 사람들에게 알리는 데에만 나는 벌써 편지지 세 뭉치를 다 썼어요."], ["Seven peeresses have asked for it for their favourite footmen.", "귀족 부인 일곱이 저마다 아끼는 시종을 그 자리에 넣어 달라고 청해 왔지요."], ["But there—there’s the Lord Petty Bag!”", "그런데 저기 — 저기 프티백 경이!”"]], [["A bell rang and the private secretary, jumping up from his note-paper, tripped away quickly to the great man’s room.", "종이 울리자 개인 비서는 편지지에서 벌떡 일어나 그 큰사람의 방으로 잰걸음에 가 버렸다."]], [["“He’ll see you at once,”", "“곧 만나 주시겠다는군요,”"], ["said he, returning.", "그가 돌아와 말했다."], ["“Buggins, show the Reverend Mr. Robarts to the Lord Petty Bag.”", "“버긴스, 로바츠 목사님을 프티백 경께 안내해.”"]], [["Buggins was the messenger for whose not vacant place all the peeresses were striving with so much animation.", "버긴스는 온 귀족 부인들이 그토록 기운차게 다투던, 비어 있지 않은 그 자리의 사환이었다."], ["And then Mark, following Buggins for two steps, was ushered into the next room.", "그리고 마크는 버긴스를 두 걸음 따라가서 다음 방으로 안내되었다."]], [["If a man be altered by becoming a private secretary, he is much more altered by being made a cabinet minister.", "개인 비서가 되는 것으로 사람이 달라진다면, 내각의 각료가 되는 것으로는 훨씬 더 달라진다."], ["Robarts, as he entered the room, could hardly believe that this was the same Harold Smith whom Mrs. Proudie bothered so cruelly in the lecture-room at Barchester.", "로바츠는 그 방에 들어서면서, 이 사람이 바체스터의 강연장에서 프라우디 부인에게 그토록 모질게 시달렸던 그 해럴드 스미스와 같은 사람이라고는 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다."], ["Then he was cross, and touchy, and uneasy, and insignificant.", "그때 그는 짜증스럽고, 신경이 곤두서고, 안절부절못하고, 대수롭지 않았다."], ["Now, as he stood smiling on the hearthrug of his official fireplace, it was quite pleasant to see the kind, patronizing smile which lighted up his features.", "이제 관사 벽난로 앞 깔개에 서서 웃음을 지으니, 그의 얼굴을 밝히는 그 친절하고 생색내는 웃음을 보는 것이 꽤나 즐거웠다."], ["He delighted to stand there, with his hands in his trousers’ pocket, the great man of the place, conscious of his lordship, and feeling himself every inch a minister.", "그는 바지 주머니에 두 손을 넣고 그곳의 큰사람으로서, 자기 경 작위를 의식하며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 각료임을 느끼며 거기 서 있기를 흡족해했다."], ["Sowerby had come with him, and was standing a little in the background, from which position he winked occasionally at the parson over the minister’s shoulder.", "사워비가 그와 함께 와서 조금 뒤쪽에 서 있었는데, 그 자리에서 이따금 각료의 어깨 너머로 목사에게 눈짓을 보냈다."]], [["“Ah, Robarts, delighted to see you.", "“아, 로바츠, 만나서 반갑소."], ["How odd, by-the-by, that your brother should be my private secretary!”", "그런데 참, 당신 동생이 내 개인 비서라니 얼마나 묘한 일이오!”"], ["Mark said that it was a singular coincidence.", "마크는 별난 우연이라고 말했다."]], [["“A very smart young fellow, and, if he minds himself, he’ll do well.”", "“아주 영민한 젊은이오. 제 몸가짐만 잘 지키면 잘될 것이오.”"]], [["“I’m quite sure he’ll do well,”", "“그 아이는 틀림없이 잘할 겁니다,”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["well, yes; I think he will.", "그렇지, 그렇소. 나도 그럴 것이라 생각하오."], ["And now, what can I do for you, Robarts?”", "그런데 로바츠, 내가 당신에게 무엇을 해 주면 되겠소?”"]], [["Hereupon Mr. Sowerby struck in, making it apparent by his explanation that Mr. Robarts himself by no means intended to ask for anything; but that, as his friends had thought that this stall at Barchester might be put into his hands with more fitness than in those of any other clergyman of the day, he was willing to accept the piece of preferment from a man whom he respected so much as he did the new Lord Petty Bag.", "여기서 사워비 씨가 끼어들어, 로바츠 씨 자신은 무엇을 청할 뜻이 결코 없다는 것, 다만 그의 벗들이 바체스터의 이 참사회원 자리가 요즘 어느 성직자의 손에 놓이는 것보다 그의 손에 놓이는 것이 더 걸맞다고 생각했으므로, 그가 새 프티백 경만큼 깊이 존경하는 사람으로부터 그 영전을 받을 마음이 있다는 것을 설명으로 분명히 밝혔다."]], [["The minister did not quite like this, as it restricted him from much of his condescension, and robbed him of the incense of a petition which he had expected Mark Robarts would make to him.", "각료는 이것이 그리 마음에 들지 않았다. 그것이 그가 은혜를 베풀어 몸을 낮출 여지를 많이 좁혀 놓고, 마크 로바츠가 자기에게 올릴 것으로 기대했던 청원이라는 향을 빼앗아 갔기 때문이다."], ["But, nevertheless, he was very gracious.", "그러나 그럼에도 그는 매우 은근했다."]], [["“He could not take upon himself to declare,” he said, “what might be Lord Brock’s pleasure with reference to the preferment at Barchester which was vacant.", "“바체스터에 비어 있는 그 성직 영전에 관해 브록 경의 뜻이 무엇일지는 자기가 나서서 밝힐 수 없는 일이다,”"], ["He had certainly already spoken to his lordship on the subject, and had perhaps some reason to believe that his own wishes would be consulted.", "그가 말했다. “그 일에 대해서는 이미 그 경에게 분명히 말을 넣어 두었고, 자기 뜻이 참작될 것이라 믿을 만한 근거도 얼마간 있는 듯하다."], ["No distinct promise had been made, but he might perhaps go so far as to say that he expected such result. If so, it would give him the greatest pleasure in the world to congratulate Mr.", "확실한 약속이 이루어진 것은 아니나, 그런 결과를 기대한다고까지는 아마 말해도 좋을 것이다."], ["Robarts on the possession of the stall—a stall which he was sure Mr. Robarts would fill with dignity, piety, and brotherly love.”", "그리 된다면 로바츠 씨가 그 자리를 차지한 것을 축하하는 일이 자기에게는 세상에서 가장 큰 기쁨이 될 것이다 — 로바츠 씨가 위엄과 경건과 형제 사랑으로 채울 것이라 확신하는 자리이니.”"], ["And then, when he had finished, Mr. Sowerby gave a final wink, and said that he regarded the matter as settled.", "그러고서 그가 말을 마치자 사워비 씨는 마지막으로 눈짓을 한 번 하고서, 이 일은 정해진 것으로 여기겠다고 말했다."]], [["“No, not settled, Nathaniel,”", "“아니, 정해진 게 아니야, 나다니엘,”"], ["said the cautious minister.", "신중한 각료가 말했다."]], [["“It’s the same thing,”", "“그게 그거지,”"], ["rejoined Sowerby.", "사워비가 되받았다."], ["“We all know what all that flummery means.", "“그 온갖 겉치레가 무슨 뜻인지는 우리가 다 알아."], ["Men in office, Mark, never do make a distinct promise,—not even to themselves of the leg of mutton which is roasting before their kitchen fires.", "마크, 관직에 있는 사람들은 결코 확실한 약속을 하지 않아요 — 저희 부엌 불 앞에서 구워지고 있는 양 다리에 대해서도 저희 자신에게 약속을 하지 않지요."], ["It is so necessary in these days to be safe; is it not, Harold?”", "요즘은 안전해야 하는 것이 그토록 긴한 일이니까. 그렇지 않은가, 해럴드?”"]], [["“Most expedient,”", "“가장 요긴하지,”"], ["said Harold Smith, shaking his head wisely.", "해럴드 스미스가 지혜롭게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["“Well, Robarts, who is it now?”", "“그래, 로바츠, 이번엔 누구요?”"], ["This he said to his private secretary, who came to notice the arrival of some bigwig.", "이 말은 어느 거물이 왔음을 알리려 들어온 개인 비서에게 한 것이었다."], ["“Well, yes.", "“좋아, 그러지."], ["I will say good morning, with your leave, for I am a little hurried.", "미안하지만 이만 작별 인사를 하겠소."], ["And remember, Mr.", "좀 급한 일이 있어서요."], ["Robarts, I will do what I can for you; but you must distinctly understand that there is no promise.”", "그리고 기억해 두시오, 로바츠 씨, 내가 당신을 위해 할 수 있는 일은 하겠소. 다만 약속이란 것은 없다는 걸 분명히 이해하셔야 하오.”"]], [["“Oh, no promise at all,” said Sowerby—“of course not.”", "“아, 약속 같은 건 전혀 없지 — 물론 아니고,”"], ["And then, as he sauntered up Whitehall towards Charing Cross, with Robarts on his arm, he again pressed upon him the sale of that invaluable hunter, who was eating his head off his shoulders in the stable at Chaldicotes.", "사워비가 말했다. 그리고 그는 로바츠를 팔에 끼고 화이트홀을 거슬러 채링 크로스 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸으면서, 콜디코츠 마구간에서 제 머리를 먹어 치우고 있는 그 값을 헤아릴 수 없는 사냥말을 사라고 그에게 다시 졸라 댔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIX. MONEY DEALINGS.", "# 제19장. 돈거래"]], [["Mr. Sowerby, in his resolution to obtain this good gift for the Vicar of Framley, did not depend quite alone on the influence of his near connection with the Lord Petty Bag.", "사워비 씨는 프램리의 교구 사제에게 이 좋은 선물을 안겨 주기로 마음을 굳히면서, 프티백 경과 가까운 인연이 있다는 그 하나의 영향력에만 온전히 기대지는 않았다."], ["He felt the occasion to be one on which he might endeavour to move even higher powers than that, and therefore he had opened the matter to the duke—not by direct application, but through Mr. Fothergill.", "그는 이번 일이 그보다 더 높은 권세까지 움직여 볼 만한 자리라고 느꼈고, 그래서 공작에게 그 문제를 꺼내 두었다 — 직접 청원한 것이 아니라 포더길 씨를 거쳐서였다."], ["No man who understood matters ever thought of going direct to the duke in such an affair as that.", "사정을 아는 사람이라면 그런 일로 공작에게 곧바로 가겠다는 생각은 아무도 하지 않았다."], ["If one wanted to speak about a woman or a horse or a picture the duke could, on occasions, be affable enough.", "여자에 대해서나 말에 대해서나 그림에 대해서 이야기하고 싶다면 공작은 때로 넉넉히 상냥할 수도 있었다."]], [["But through Mr. Fothergill the duke was approached.", "그러나 포더길 씨를 통해 공작에게 말이 닿았다."], ["It was represented, with some cunning, that this buying over of the Framley clergyman from the Lufton side would be a praiseworthy spoiling of the Amalekites.", "프램리의 성직자를 러프턴 편에서 이렇게 사들이는 것이 아말렉 사람들의 재물을 노략하는 갸륵한 일이 될 것이라고, 얼마간 꾀를 부려 아뢰어졌다."], ["The doing so would give the Omnium interest a hold even in the cathedral close.", "그렇게 하면 옴니엄의 세력이 대성당 구내에까지 발판을 얻게 될 것이었다."], ["And then it was known to all men that Mr. Robarts had considerable influence over Lord Lufton himself.", "게다가 로바츠 씨가 러프턴 경 자신에게 적잖은 영향력을 지녔다는 것은 모두가 아는 바였다."], ["So guided, the Duke of Omnium did say two words to the Prime Minister, and two words from the duke went a great way, even with Lord Brock.", "그렇게 이끌리자 옴니엄 공작은 총리에게 두 마디를 하기는 했고, 공작에게서 나온 두 마디는 브록 경에게조차 큰 힘을 미쳤다."], ["The upshot of all this was, that Mark Robarts did get the stall; but he did not hear the tidings of his success till some days after his return to Framley.", "이 모든 일의 결말은, 마크 로바츠가 그 참사회원 자리를 얻었다는 것이었다. 그러나 그는 프램리로 돌아간 뒤 며칠이 지나서야 자기가 성공했다는 소식을 들었다."]], [["Mr. Sowerby did not forget to tell him of the great effort—the unusual effort, as he of Chaldicotes called it—which the duke had made on the subject.", "사워비 씨는 공작이 이 일로 기울인 그 큰 노력 — 콜디코츠의 그가 부른 대로는 유례없는 노력 — 을 그에게 알려 주는 것을 잊지 않았다."], ["“I don’t know when he has done such a thing before,”", "“각하께서 전에 언제 그런 일을 하셨는지 나는 알지 못해요,” 사워비가 말했다."], ["said Sowerby; “and you may be quite sure of this, he would not have done it now, had you not gone to Gatherum Castle when he asked you: indeed, Fothergill would have known that it was vain to attempt it.", "“그리고 이것만은 아주 확실히 여겨도 좋아요. 각하께서 청하셨을 때 당신이 개서럼 성에 가지 않았더라면 지금 그 일을 해 주시지 않았을 겁니다."], ["And I’ll tell you what, Mark—it does not do for me to make little of my own nest, but I truly believe the duke’s word will be more efficacious than the Lord Petty Bag’s solemn adjuration.”", "아니, 포더길도 그것을 시도해 보아야 헛일이라는 것을 알았겠지요. 그리고 마크, 내 말을 들어 봐요 — 내가 내 둥지를 깎아내리는 것은 온당치 않지만, 나는 참으로 각하의 한마디가 프티백 경의 엄숙한 탄원보다 더 효험이 있을 것이라고 믿어요.”"]], [["Mark, of course, expressed his gratitude in proper terms, and did buy the horse for a hundred and thirty pounds.", "마크는 물론 마땅한 대로 고마움을 표했고, 그러고서 그 말을 백삼십 파운드에 사들였다. “그놈은 그만한 값이 넉넉히 있어요,”"], ["“He’s as well worth it,”", "사워비가 말했다."], ["said Sowerby, “as any animal that ever stood on four legs; and my only reason for pressing him on you is, that when Tozer’s day does come round, I know you will have to stand to us to something about that tune.”", "“네 발로 서 본 어느 짐승에게도 뒤지지 않지요. 그리고 내가 그놈을 당신에게 굳이 권하는 유일한 이유는, 토저의 날이 돌아올 때 당신이 우리를 위해 그쯤 되는 액수를 대야 할 것을 내가 알기 때문이에요.”"], ["It did not occur to Mark to ask him why the horse should not be sold to some one else, and the money forthcoming in the regular way.", "어째서 그 말을 다른 누군가에게 팔아서 정해진 방식으로 돈을 마련하지 않느냐고 물어볼 생각은 마크에게 떠오르지 않았다."], ["But this would not have suited Mr. Sowerby.", "그러나 그것은 사워비 씨에게 맞는 일이 아니었을 것이다."]], [["Mark knew that the beast was good, and as he walked to his lodgings was half proud of his new possession.", "마크는 그 짐승이 좋은 것임을 알았고, 숙소로 걸어가면서 새로 얻은 소유물에 반쯤 뿌듯했다."], ["But then, how would he justify it to his wife, or how introduce the animal into his stables without attempting any justification in the matter?", "그러나 그렇다면 그것을 아내에게 어떻게 변명할 것인가? 아니면 그 일에 아무 변명도 하지 않은 채로 그 짐승을 어떻게 자기 마구간에 들여놓을 것인가?"], ["And yet, looking to the absolute amount of his income, surely he might feel himself entitled to buy a new horse when it suited him.", "게다가 자기 수입의 절대 액수만 놓고 본다면, 마음에 들 때 말 한 마리 새로 사는 것쯤은 제 권리라고 여겨도 좋지 않은가?"], ["He wondered what Mr. Crawley would say when he heard of the new purchase.", "그는 이 새 매입 소식을 들으면 크롤리 씨가 무엇이라 할지 궁금했다."], ["He had lately fallen into a state of much wondering as to what his friends and neighbours would say about him.", "그는 요사이 벗들과 이웃들이 자기를 두고 무엇이라 말할지 몹시 궁금해하는 상태에 빠져 있었다."]], [["He had now been two days in town, and was to go down after breakfast on the following morning so that he might reach home by Friday afternoon.", "그는 이제 이틀 동안 도시에 있었고, 이튿날 아침 식사 후에 내려가서 금요일 오후에는 집에 닿을 참이었다."], ["But on that evening, just as he was going to bed, he was surprised by Lord Lufton coming into the coffee-room at his hotel.", "그러나 그날 저녁, 막 자리에 들려 할 때 러프턴 경이 그가 머무는 호텔의 커피실로 들어오는 바람에 그는 놀랐다."], ["He walked in with a hurried step, his face was red, and it was clear that he was very angry.", "그는 다급한 걸음으로 걸어 들어왔고, 얼굴이 붉었으며, 몹시 노여워하고 있는 것이 분명했다."]], [["“Robarts,”", "“로바츠,”"], ["said he, walking up to his friend and taking the hand that was extended to him, “do you know anything about this man, Tozer?”", "그가 벗에게 다가와 내미는 손을 잡으며 말했다. “자네, 이 토저라는 사람에 대해 무엇 아는 것이 있나?”"]], [["“Tozer—what Tozer?", "“토저 — 무슨 토저?"], ["I have heard Sowerby speak of such a man.”", "사워비가 그런 사람을 말하는 것을 들은 적이 있네.”"]], [["“Of course you have.", "“물론 들었겠지."], ["If I do not mistake you have written to me about him yourself.”", "내가 잘못 안 것이 아니라면 자네 자신이 그 사람에 대해 나에게 편지를 쓴 적이 있어.”"]], [["“Very probably.", "“그럴 법하네."], ["I remember Sowerby mentioning the man with reference to your affairs.", "사워비가 자네 일과 관련해서 그 사람을 언급한 것을 기억하네."], ["But why do you ask me?”", "그런데 왜 나에게 묻는 건가?”"]], [["“This man has not only written to me, but has absolutely forced his way into my rooms when I was dressing for dinner; and absolutely had the impudence to tell me that if I did not honour some bill which he holds for eight hundred pounds he would proceed against me.”", "“이 사람이 나에게 편지를 쓴 것으로도 모자라서, 내가 만찬 옷을 입고 있는데 아주 억지로 내 방까지 밀고 들어왔어. 그리고 뻔뻔스럽게도, 자기가 쥐고 있는 팔백 파운드 어음을 내가 결제하지 않으면 나를 걸어 법으로 나오겠다고 하더군.”"]], [["“But you settled all that matter with Sowerby?”", "“하지만 자네는 그 문제를 사워비와 다 정리했잖은가?”"]], [["“I did settle it at a very great cost to me.", "“정리했지, 나에게 아주 큰 비용을 물려 가면서."], ["Sooner than have a fuss I paid him through the nose—like a fool that I was—everything that he claimed.", "시끄러운 일을 겪기보다는, 바보 천치처럼, 그가 청구한 모든 것을 터무니없이 비싸게 물어 주었어."], ["This is an absolute swindle, and if it goes on I will expose it as such.”", "이건 순전한 사기야. 그리고 계속 이런다면 나는 이것을 사기로 폭로하겠어.”"]], [["Robarts looked round the room, but luckily there was not a soul in it but themselves.", "로바츠는 방을 둘러보았으나, 다행히 그 안에는 그들 둘 말고는 한 사람도 없었다."], ["“You do not mean to say that Sowerby is swindling you?”", "“사워비가 자네를 속여 먹고 있다는 말은 아니겠지?”"], ["said the clergyman.", "성직자가 말했다."]], [["“It looks very like it,”", "“그렇게 보이네,”"], ["said Lord Lufton; “and I tell you fairly that I am not in a humour to endure any more of this sort of thing.", "러프턴 경이 말했다. “그리고 자네에게 솔직히 말하는데, 나는 이런 일을 더 견딜 기분이 아니야."], ["Some years ago I made an ass of myself through that man’s fault.", "몇 해 전에 나는 저 사람의 잘못 때문에 얼간이 짓을 했어."], ["But four thousand pounds should have covered the whole of what I really lost.", "그러나 사천 파운드면 내가 실제로 잃은 것을 다 덮었을 거야."], ["I have now paid more than three times that sum; and, by heavens!", "나는 이제 그 액수의 세 배가 넘게 물어 주었어. 그러니, 하늘에 맹세하고!"], ["I will not pay more without exposing the whole affair.”", "이 일을 온통 드러내지 않고서는 더는 물지 않겠어.”"]], [["“But, Lufton, I do not understand.", "“그런데 러프턴, 나는 이해가 안 되네."], ["What is this bill?—has it your name to it?”", "그 어음이란 것이 무엇인가? — 자네 이름이 적혀 있나?”"]], [["“Yes, it has: I’ll not deny my name, and if there be absolute need I will pay it; but if I do so, my lawyer shall sift it, and it shall go before a jury.”", "“그래, 적혀 있네. 내 이름을 부인하지는 않을 것이고, 정말로 필요하다면 물어 주겠어. 그러나 내가 물게 된다면 내 변호사가 그것을 낱낱이 캐게 할 것이고, 배심 앞에까지 가게 하겠어.”"]], [["“But I thought all those bills were paid?”", "“하지만 그 어음들은 다 결제된 줄 알았는데?”"]], [["“I left it to Sowerby to get up the old bills when they were renewed, and now one of them that has in truth been already honoured is brought against me.”", "“갱신할 때 구 어음들을 회수하는 일은 사워비에게 맡겨 두었어. 그런데 이제 실제로는 이미 결제된 것 하나가 나에게 청구되고 있어.”"]], [["Mark could not but think of the two documents which he himself had signed, and both of which were now undoubtedly in the hands of Tozer, or of some other gentleman of the same profession;—which both might be brought against him, the second as soon as he should have satisfied the first.", "마크는 자기가 손수 서명한 두 문서를 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그 둘은 이제 틀림없이 토저의, 아니면 같은 업을 하는 다른 어느 신사의 손에 있었다."], ["And then he remembered that Sowerby had said something to him about an outstanding bill, for the filling up of which some trifle must be paid, and of this he reminded Lord Lufton.", "그 둘 다 자기에게 청구될 수 있었으니, 첫째 것을 물어 주기만 하면 곧 둘째 것이 나올 것이었다. 그리고 그는 사워비가 미결 어음 한 장에 대해, 그것을 처리하려면 얼마 안 되는 돈을 물어야 한다고 자기에게 말했던 것을 떠올렸고, 이것을 러프턴 경에게 일러 주었다."]], [["“And do you call eight hundred pounds a trifle?", "“그런데 자네는 팔백 파운드를 얼마 안 되는 돈이라고 부르나?"], ["If so, I do not.”", "나는 그렇지 않네.”"]], [["“They will probably make no such demand as that.”", "“아마 그런 요구까지는 하지 않을 걸세.”"]], [["“But I tell you they do make such a demand, and have made it.", "“하지만 내 말은 그들이 그런 요구를 한다는 거야."], ["The man whom I saw, and who told me that he was Tozer’s friend, but who was probably Tozer himself, positively swore to me that he would be obliged to take legal proceedings if the money were not forthcoming within a week or ten days.", "이미 했어. 내가 만난 그 사람은, 자기가 토저의 벗이라고 했지만 아마 토저 자신이었을 텐데, 이레나 열흘 안에 돈이 나오지 않으면 법으로 절차를 밟지 않을 수 없을 것이라고 나에게 딱 잘라 맹세하더군."], ["When I explained to him that it was an old bill that had been renewed, he declared that his friend had given full value for it.”", "그것이 갱신된 구 어음이라고 내가 설명하니까, 자기 벗이 그것에 값을 온전히 다 치렀다고 우기더군.”"]], [["“Sowerby said that you would probably have to pay ten pounds to redeem it.", "“사워비는 자네가 그것을 되찾는 데 십 파운드쯤 물어야 할 것이라고 했네."], ["I should offer the man some such sum as that.”", "나라면 그 사람에게 그쯤 되는 액수를 내밀겠네.”"]], [["“My intention is to offer the man nothing, but to leave the affair in the hands of my lawyer with instructions to him to spare none;—neither myself nor any one else.", "“내 뜻은 그 사람에게 아무것도 내밀지 않고, 이 일을 내 변호사의 손에 맡겨 아무도 봐주지 말라고 지시하는 것이네. 나 자신도, 다른 누구도."], ["I am not going to allow such a man as Sowerby to squeeze me like an orange.”", "나는 사워비 같은 사람이 나를 오렌지처럼 짜내도록 두지 않겠어.”"]], [["“But, Lufton, you seem as though you were angry with me.”", "“하지만 러프턴, 자네는 나에게 노여워하는 것처럼 보이네.”"]], [["“No, I am not.", "“아니, 그렇지 않아."], ["But I think it is as well to caution you about this man; my transactions with him lately have chiefly been through you, and therefore—”", "그러나 이 사람에 대해서는 자네에게 조심하라고 일러 두는 것이 좋겠다고 생각하네. 요사이 그와의 거래는 주로 자네를 거쳐 왔으니, 그래서 —”"]], [["“But they have only been so through his and your wish: because I have been anxious to oblige you both.", "“하지만 그것은 그와 자네의 뜻을 따라 그렇게 된 것일 뿐이네. 내가 자네들 둘 다에게 편의를 봐주고 싶었으니까."], ["I hope you don’t mean to say that I am concerned in these bills.”", "자네가 나더러 이 어음들에 관계가 있다는 말을 하려는 것은 아니겠지.”"]], [["“I know that you are concerned in bills with him.”", "“나는 자네가 그 사람과 어음으로 얽혀 있다는 것을 알고 있네.”"]], [["“Why, Lufton, am I to understand, then, that you are accusing me of having any interest in these transactions which you have called swindling?”", "“아니, 러프턴, 그러면 자네가 사기라고 부른 이 거래에서 내가 무슨 이해를 챙겼다고 나를 나무라는 것으로 알아들어야 하나?”"]], [["“As far as I am concerned there has been swindling, and there is swindling going on now.”", "“나에 관한 한 사기가 있었고, 지금도 사기가 벌어지고 있어.”"]], [["“But you do not answer my question.", "“그러나 자네는 내 물음에 대답하지 않고 있네."], ["Do you bring any accusation against me?", "나에게 무슨 혐의를 씌우는 것인가?"], ["If so, I agree with you that you had better go to your lawyer.”", "그렇다면 나도 자네 생각에 찬성하네. 자네는 변호사에게 가는 편이 낫겠어.”"]], [["“I think that is what I shall do.”", "“나도 그럴 생각이네.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“아주 좋아."], ["But upon the whole, I never heard of a more unreasonable man, or of one whose thoughts are more unjust than yours.", "그러나 아무리 헤아려 보아도 나는 이보다 더 사리에 어긋난 사람, 이보다 더 사람 생각이 온당치 못한 사람을 들어 본 적이 없네."], ["Solely with the view of assisting you, and solely at your request, I spoke to Sowerby about these money transactions of yours.", "오로지 자네를 돕겠다는 마음으로, 오로지 자네가 청하기에 나는 자네의 그 돈거래에 대해 사워비에게 말했네."], ["Then at his request, which originated out of your request, he using me as his ambassador to you, as you had used me as yours to him, I wrote and spoke to you.", "그러고 나서 그가 청하기에 — 그것도 자네가 청한 데서 비롯한 것이고, 자네가 나를 자네 사절로 그에게 보냈던 것처럼 그도 나를 자기 사절로 자네에게 보낸 것인데 — 나는 자네에게 편지를 쓰고 말을 했네."], ["And now this is the upshot.”", "그런데 이제 결말이 이렇군.”"]], [["“I bring no accusation against you, Robarts; but I know you have dealings with this man.", "“나는 자네에게 아무 혐의도 씌우지 않네, 로바츠. 그러나 자네가 이 사람과 거래하고 있다는 것은 알고 있어."], ["You have told me so yourself.”", "자네가 나에게 스스로 그렇게 말했어.”"]], [["“Yes, at his request to accommodate him, I have put my name to a bill.”", "“그렇네. 그가 청하기에 편의를 봐주려고 어음에 내 이름을 적었네.”"]], [["“Only to one?”", "“한 장에만?”"]], [["“Only to one; and then to that same renewed, or not exactly to that same, but to one which stands for it.", "“한 장에만. 그리고 그 뒤에 그것이 갱신된 것에, 아니 정확히는 같은 것이 아니라 그것을 대신하는 것에 적었네."], ["The first was for four hundred pounds; the last for five hundred.”", "처음 것은 사백 파운드였고, 나중 것은 오백이었어.”"]], [["“All which you will have to make good, and the world will of course tell you that you have paid that price for this stall at Barchester.”", "“그것을 자네가 다 메워야 할 테고, 그러면 세상은 물론 자네가 바체스터의 그 참사회원 자리에 그 값을 치렀다고 말할 걸세.”"]], [["This was terrible to be borne.", "이것은 견디기에 끔찍한 말이었다."], ["He had heard much lately which had frightened and scared him, but nothing so terrible as this; nothing which so stunned him, or conveyed to his mind so frightful a reality of misery and ruin.", "그는 요사이 자기를 놀래고 겁먹게 한 말을 많이 들었으나, 이처럼 끔찍한 것은 없었다. 이처럼 그를 멍하게 만드는 것, 참담과 파멸이 그토록 무섭게 실제라는 것을 그의 마음에 전해 주는 것은 없었다."], ["He made no immediate answer, but standing on the hearthrug with his back to the fire, looked up the whole length of the room.", "그는 곧바로 대답하지 않고, 불을 등지고 벽난로 앞 깔개에 서서 방 저편 끝까지 눈길을 던졌다."], ["Hitherto his eyes had been fixed upon Lord Lufton’s face, but now it seemed to him as though he had but little more to do with Lord Lufton.", "여태 그의 눈은 러프턴 경의 얼굴에 붙어 있었으나, 이제는 러프턴 경과 자기 사이에 남은 일이 별로 없는 듯이 여겨졌다."], ["Lord Lufton and Lord Lufton’s mother were neither now to be counted among those who wished him well.", "러프턴 경도, 러프턴 경의 어머니도 이제는 자기가 잘되기를 바라는 사람으로 헤아릴 수 없었다."], ["Upon whom indeed could he now count, except that wife of his bosom upon whom he was bringing all this wretchedness?", "그렇다면 이제 누구를 헤아릴 수 있단 말인가? 자기가 이 모든 비참을 안겨 주고 있는, 품 안의 그 아내밖에."]], [["In that moment of agony ideas ran quickly through his brain.", "그 괴로움의 순간에 여러 생각이 그의 머릿속을 빠르게 지나갔다."], ["He would immediately abandon this preferment at Barchester, of which it might be said with so much colour that he had bought it.", "그는 바체스터의 이 영전을, 자기가 그것을 사들였다고 그토록 그럴싸하게 말할 수 있는 그 영전을 당장 버릴 것이었다."], ["He would go to Harold Smith, and say positively that he declined it.", "해럴드 스미스에게 가서 딱 잘라 사양하겠다고 말할 것이었다."], ["Then he would return home and tell his wife all that had occurred;—tell the whole also to Lady Lufton, if that might still be of any service.", "그러고서 집에 돌아가 아내에게 일어난 일을 다 이야기할 것이었다 — 아직 무슨 도움이 될 수 있다면 레이디 러프턴에게도 다 이야기할 것이었다."], ["He would make arrangement for the payment of both those bills as they might be presented, asking no questions as to the justice of the claim, making no complaint to any one, not even to Sowerby.", "그 두 어음이 제시되는 대로 갚을 방도를 마련할 것이었고, 청구가 정당한지 아닌지 묻지도 않고, 누구에게도, 심지어 사워비에게도 불평하지 않을 것이었다."], ["He would put half his income, if half were necessary, into the hands of Forrest the banker, till all was paid.", "필요하다면 수입의 절반을, 절반이 필요하다면 그만큼을 은행가 포레스트의 손에 맡겨 모두 갚아질 때까지 두겠다."], ["He would sell every horse he had.", "가진 말은 모두 팔아 버릴 것이었다."], ["He would part with his footman and groom, and at any rate strive like a man to get again a firm footing on good ground.", "하인과 마부도 내보낼 것이었고, 아무튼 사내답게 애써서 다시 든든한 땅에 발을 굳게 딛겠다."], ["Then, at that moment, he loathed with his whole soul the position in which he found himself placed, and his own folly which had placed him there.", "그때 그 순간 그는 자기가 놓인 처지를, 그리고 자기를 거기 놓은 자기 자신의 어리석음을 온 영혼으로 진저리 냈다."], ["How could he reconcile it to his conscience that he was there in London with Sowerby and Harold Smith, petitioning for Church preferment to a man who should have been altogether powerless in such a matter, buying horses, and arranging about past due bills?", "사워비와 해럴드 스미스와 함께 런던에 와서, 그런 일에는 도무지 힘이 없어야 마땅한 사람에게 성직의 영전을 청원하고, 말을 사고, 기한이 지난 어음을 처리하고 있다는 것을 어떻게 자기 양심과 앞뒤를 맞출 수 있겠는가?"], ["He did not reconcile it to his conscience. Mr.", "그는 그것을 자기 양심과 맞추지 못했다."], ["Crawley had been right when he told him that he was a castaway.", "크롤리 씨가 그를 버림받은 자라고 말한 것은 옳았다."]], [["Lord Lufton, whose anger during the whole interview had been extreme, and who had become more angry the more he talked, had now walked once or twice up and down the room; and as he so walked the idea did occur to him that he had been unjust.", "러프턴 경은 이 만남 내내 노여움이 극에 달해 있었고 말을 할수록 더 노여워졌는데, 이제 방 안을 한두 번 오르내리며 걸었다. 그리고 그렇게 걷다가 자기가 온당하지 못했다는 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다."], ["He had come there with the intention of exclaiming against Sowerby, and of inducing Robarts to convey to that gentleman, that if he, Lord Lufton, were made to undergo any further annoyance about this bill, the whole affair should be thrown into the lawyer’s hands; but instead of doing this, he had brought an accusation against Robarts.", "그는 사워비를 규탄하고, 이 어음 문제로 자기 곧 러프턴 경이 조금이라도 더 시달리게 된다면 이 일 전체를 변호사의 손에 넘기겠다는 것을 로바츠가 그 신사에게 전하도록 만들 뜻으로 여기에 온 것이었다. 그런데 그렇게 하는 대신 그는 로바츠에게 혐의를 씌워 놓았다."], ["That Robarts had latterly become Sowerby’s friend rather than his own in all these horrid money dealings, had galled him; and now he had expressed himself in terms much stronger than he had intended to use.", "로바츠가 근래 이 모든 지긋지긋한 돈거래에서 자기 편보다 사워비 편이 되어 버렸다는 것이 그의 속을 긁었고, 그래서 이제 그는 뜻했던 것보다 훨씬 강한 말로 제 마음을 털어놓아 버렸던 것이다."]], [["“As to you personally, Mark,”", "“자네 개인에 대해서는, 마크,”"], ["he said, coming back to the spot on which Robarts was standing, “I do not wish to say anything that shall annoy you.”", "로바츠가 서 있는 자리로 돌아오며 그가 말했다. “자네 속을 긁을 말은 하나도 하고 싶지 않네.”"]], [["“You have said quite enough, Lord Lufton.”", "“이미 넉넉히 말했네, 러프턴 경.”"]], [["“You cannot be surprised that I should be angry and indignant at the treatment I have received.”", "“내가 받은 대접에 노엽고 분한 것을 자네가 놀랍게 여길 수야 없겠지.”"]], [["“You might, I think, have separated in your mind those who have wronged you, if there has been such wrong, from those who have only endeavoured to do your will and pleasure for you.", "“나는 자네가 마음속에서, 자네에게 잘못을 저지른 사람들을 — 그런 잘못이 있었다면 — 다만 자네의 뜻과 기쁨을 이루어 주려 애쓴 사람들과 갈라 놓을 수도 있었으리라 생각하네."], ["That I, as a clergyman, have been very wrong in taking any part whatsoever in these matters, I am well aware.", "내가 성직자로서 이런 일에 조금이라도 손을 댄 것이 매우 잘못이라는 것은 나도 잘 알고 있네."], ["That as a man I have been outrageously foolish in lending my name to Mr.", "사내로서 사워비 씨에게 내 이름을 빌려준 것이 터무니없이 어리석었다는 것도 넉넉히 알고 있어."], ["Sowerby, I also know well enough: it is perhaps as well that I should be told of this somewhat rudely; but I certainly did not expect the lesson to come from you.”", "이것을 얼마간 무례하게 일러 받는 것도 어쩌면 잘된 일이겠지. 그러나 그 가르침이 자네에게서 올 줄은 정말 몰랐네.”"]], [["“Well, there has been mischief enough.", "“그래, 해악은 넉넉히 저질러졌네."], ["The question is, what we had better now both do?”", "문제는 이제 우리 둘이 무엇을 하는 것이 좋겠느냐는 것이지.”"]], [["“You have said what you mean to do.", "“자네는 하려는 일을 말했네."], ["You will put the affair into the hands of your lawyer.”", "이 일을 변호사의 손에 맡길 것이라고.”"]], [["“Not with any object of exposing you.”", "“자네를 드러내려는 뜻은 조금도 없어.”"]], [["“Exposing me, Lord Lufton!", "“나를 드러낸다고, 러프턴 경!"], ["Why, one would think that I had had the handling of your money.”", "아니, 남이 들으면 내가 자네 돈을 만졌다고 여기겠네.”"]], [["“You will misunderstand me.", "“자네가 내 말을 잘못 알아듣는 걸세."], ["I think no such thing.", "나는 그런 생각은 하지 않네."], ["But do you not know yourself that if legal steps be taken in this wretched affair, your arrangements with Sowerby will be brought to light?”", "그러나 이 참담한 일에 법의 절차가 밟히면 자네와 사워비 사이의 처리가 밝은 데로 드러날 것을 자네 스스로 모르지 않을 텐데?”"]], [["“My arrangements with Sowerby will consist in paying or having to pay, on his account, a large sum of money, for which I have never had and shall never have any consideration whatever.”", "“나와 사워비 사이의 처리라는 것은, 내가 대가로 받은 것도 앞으로 받을 것도 전혀 없는 큰돈을 그의 셈으로 갚거나 갚아야 하는 것으로 그칠 걸세.”"]], [["“And what will be said about this stall at Barchester?”", "“그러면 바체스터의 그 참사회원 자리에 대해서는 무슨 말이 나겠나?”"]], [["“After the charge which you brought against me just now, I shall decline to accept it.”", "“자네가 방금 나에게 씌운 혐의를 듣고서는, 나는 그것을 받는 것을 사양하겠네.”"]], [["At this moment three or four other gentlemen entered the room, and the conversation between our two friends was stopped.", "이 순간 다른 신사 서너 사람이 방으로 들어왔고, 우리 두 벗의 이야기는 끊어졌다."], ["They still remained standing near the fire, but for a few minutes neither of them said anything.", "두 사람은 여전히 불 가까이 서 있었으나, 몇 분 동안 어느 쪽도 아무 말을 하지 않았다."], ["Robarts was waiting till Lord Lufton should go away, and Lord Lufton had not yet said that which he had come to say.", "로바츠는 러프턴 경이 가 주기를 기다리고 있었고, 러프턴 경은 자기가 하려고 온 말을 아직 하지 못했다. 마침내 그가 거의 속삭이듯이 다시 입을 열었다."], ["At last he spoke again, almost in a whisper: “I think it will be best to ask Sowerby to come to my rooms to-morrow, and I think also that you should meet him there.”", "“사워비에게 내일 내 방으로 오라고 청하는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 생각하네. 그리고 자네도 거기서 그를 만나는 것이 좋겠다고 생각해.”"]], [["“I do not see any necessity for my presence,” said Robarts.", "“내가 그 자리에 있어야 할 까닭을 나는 모르겠네,”"], ["“It seems probable that I shall suffer enough for meddling with your affairs, and I will do so no more.”", "로바츠가 말했다. “자네 일에 끼어든 값으로 나는 넉넉히 고생할 것 같으니, 더는 끼어들지 않겠네.”"]], [["“Of course I cannot make you come; but I think it will be only just to Sowerby, and it will be a favour to me.”", "“물론 자네를 오게 만들 수야 없지. 그러나 그것이 사워비에게 다만 공평한 일이겠고, 나에게는 은혜가 될 걸세.”"]], [["Robarts again walked up and down the room for half-a-dozen times, trying to resolve what it would most become him to do in the present emergency.", "로바츠는 다시 대여섯 번 방 안을 오르내리며, 지금의 이 다급한 처지에서 어떻게 하는 것이 자기에게 가장 어울릴지 마음을 정하려 애썼다."], ["If his name were dragged before the courts,—if he should be shown up in the public papers as having been engaged in accommodation bills, that would certainly be ruinous to him.", "자기 이름이 법정 앞에 끌려 나온다면 — 융통 어음에 관계했다고 신문에 드러나게 된다면, 그것은 틀림없이 자기에게 파멸이 될 것이었다."], ["He had already learned from Lord Lufton’s innuendoes what he might expect to hear as the public version of his share in these transactions!", "이 거래에서 자기가 한 몫을 세상이 어떻게 말할지는 러프턴 경의 빗댄 말에서 이미 배운 바였다!"], ["And then his wife,—how would she bear such exposure?", "그리고 아내는 — 그런 폭로를 어떻게 견딜 것인가?"]], [["“I will meet Mr. Sowerby at your rooms to-morrow, on one condition,”", "“한 가지 조건이 있으면 내일 자네 방에서 사워비 씨를 만나겠네,”"], ["he at last said.", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["“And what is that?”", "“그 조건이 무엇인가?”"]], [["“That I receive your positive assurance that I am not suspected by you of having had any pecuniary interest whatever in any money matters with Mr. Sowerby, either as concerns your affairs or those of anybody else.”", "“내가 사워비 씨와의 어떤 돈 문제에서도, 자네 일에서든 다른 누구의 일에서든 무슨 금전상의 이해를 챙겼다고 자네가 나를 의심하지 않는다는 확실한 다짐을 받는 것이네.”"]], [["“I have never suspected you of any such thing.", "“나는 자네를 그런 일로 의심한 적이 없네."], ["But I have thought that you were compromised with him.”", "다만 자네가 그와 얽혀 있다고 생각했을 뿐이야.”"]], [["“And so I am—I am liable for these bills.", "“그리고 나는 얽혀 있네 — 나는 그 어음들에 책임이 있어."], ["But you ought to have known, and do know, that I have never received a shilling on account of such liability.", "그러나 자네는 알았어야 하고, 또 알고 있듯이, 나는 그 책임을 지고서 한 실링도 받은 적이 없네."], ["I have endeavoured to oblige a man whom I regarded first as your friend, and then as my own; and this has been the result.”", "나는 처음에는 자네의 벗으로, 그다음에는 나 자신의 벗으로 여긴 사람에게 편의를 봐주려 애썼을 뿐이고, 그 결과가 이것이네.”"]], [["Lord Lufton did at last give him the assurance that he desired, as they sat with their heads together over one of the coffee-room tables; and then Robarts promised that he would postpone his return to Framley till the Saturday, so that he might meet Sowerby at Lord Lufton’s chambers in the Albany on the following afternoon.", "커피실 탁자 하나에 머리를 맞대고 앉아 있는 동안 러프턴 경은 마침내 그가 바라던 다짐을 주었다. 그리고 그러고 나서 로바츠는 프램리로 돌아가는 것을 토요일로 미루어, 이튿날 오후에 앨버니에 있는 러프턴 경의 사택에서 사워비를 만나겠다고 약속했다."], ["As soon as this was arranged, Lord Lufton took his leave and went his way.", "이렇게 정해지자마자 러프턴 경은 작별을 하고 제 길로 갔다."]], [["After that poor Mark had a very uneasy night of it.", "그 뒤로 딱한 마크는 매우 편치 못한 밤을 지냈다."], ["It was clear enough that Lord Lufton had thought, if he did not still think, that the stall at Barchester was to be given as pecuniary recompense in return for certain money accommodation to be afforded by the nominee to the dispenser of this patronage.", "러프턴 경이, 바체스터의 그 참사회원 자리가 이 추천권을 쥔 사람에게 그 지명을 받은 자가 대 주기로 한 어떤 돈 융통에 대한 금전상의 보답으로 주어진 것이라고 생각했다는 것은 — 지금도 여전히 그렇게 생각하지 않는다 해도 — 넉넉히 분명했다."], ["Nothing on earth could be worse than this.", "세상에 이보다 더 나쁜 일은 없었다."], ["In the first place it would be simony; and then it would be simony beyond all description mean and simoniacal.", "첫째로 그것은 성직 매매일 것이었고, 그다음으로는 말로 다 할 수 없이 비열하고 성직 매매다운 성직 매매일 것이었다."], ["The very thought of it filled Mark’s soul with horror and dismay.", "그것을 생각만 해도 마크의 영혼은 두려움과 당혹으로 가득 찼다. 러프턴 경의 의심은 이제 가라앉았을지 모른다."], ["It might be that Lord Lufton’s suspicions were now at rest; but others would think the same thing, and their suspicions it would be impossible to allay; those others would consist of the outer world, which is always so eager to gloat over the detected vice of a clergyman.", "그러나 다른 사람들도 똑같이 생각할 것이고, 그들의 의심은 달랠 도리가 없을 것이었다. 그 다른 사람들이란 바깥 세상 사람들일 터인데, 그들은 성직자의 악덕이 들통난 것을 흐뭇하게 곱씹기를 언제나 그토록 갈망한다."]], [["And then that wretched horse which he had purchased, and the purchase of which should have prohibited him from saying that nothing of value had accrued to him in these transactions with Mr. Sowerby!", "그리고 그가 사들인 그 지긋지긋한 말은, 그것을 사들였다는 것 하나만으로도 사워비 씨와의 이 거래에서 값나가는 것이 자기에게 하나도 돌아오지 않았다는 말을 할 수 없게 만드는데 — 그것은 또 어떻게 할 것인가?"], ["what was he to do about that? And then of late he had been spending, and had continued to spend, more money than he could well afford.", "게다가 요사이 그는 넉넉히 감당할 수 있는 것보다 많은 돈을 써 왔고, 계속 쓰고 있었다."], ["This very journey of his up to London would be most imprudent, if it should become necessary for him to give up all hope of holding the prebend.", "참사회원직을 쥐겠다는 희망을 다 버려야 한다면, 이번 런던 걸음은 가장 무분별한 일이 될 것이었다. 그것은 마음을 정한 일이었다."], ["As to that he had made up his mind; but then again he unmade it, as men always do in such troubles.", "그러나 그러고 나서 그는, 사람이 그런 괴로움 속에서 늘 그러는 것처럼 정한 마음을 다시 풀었다."], ["That line of conduct which he had laid down for himself in the first moments of his indignation against Lord Lufton, by adopting which he would have to encounter poverty, and ridicule, and discomfort, the annihilation of his high hopes, and the ruin of his ambition—that, he said to himself over and over again, would now be the best for him.", "러프턴 경에 대한 분노가 처음 치솟던 순간에 스스로 그려 놓았던 그 행실의 길, 그것을 따르자면 가난과 조롱과 불편을, 높은 희망의 소멸과 야심의 파멸을 맞아야 하는 그 길이 — 그는 몇 번이고 되풀이해 스스로에게 말했다 — 이제 자기에게 가장 좋은 길일 것이었다."], ["But it is so hard for us to give up our high hopes, and willingly encounter poverty, ridicule, and discomfort!", "그러나 높은 희망을 버리고 가난과 조롱과 불편을 기꺼이 맞기가 우리에게 얼마나 어려운가!"]], [["On the following morning, however, he boldly walked down to the Petty Bag office, determined to let Harold Smith know that he was no longer desirous of the Barchester stall.", "그러나 이튿날 아침 그는 바체스터의 참사회원 자리를 더는 바라지 않는다는 것을 해럴드 스미스에게 알리기로 마음먹고 담대하게 프티백 청으로 걸어 내려갔다."], ["He found his brother there, still writing artistic notes to anxious peeresses on the subject of Buggins’ non-vacant situation; but the great man of the place, the Lord Petty Bag himself, was not there.", "그는 거기서 동생이 여전히 버긴스의 비어 있지 않은 자리에 대해 애타는 귀족 부인들에게 재주 있는 쪽지를 쓰고 있는 것을 보았다. 그러나 그곳의 큰사람, 프티백 경 자신은 거기 없었다."], ["He might probably look in when the House was beginning to sit, perhaps at four or a little after; but he certainly would not be at the office in the morning.", "하원이 열릴 무렵, 어쩌면 네 시나 조금 지나서 잠깐 들여다볼 법도 했으나, 아침에 청에 나와 있을 일은 확실히 없었다."], ["The functions of the Lord Petty Bag he was no doubt performing elsewhere.", "프티백 경의 직무는 틀림없이 다른 곳에서 수행되고 있었다."], ["Perhaps he had carried his work home with him—a practice which the world should know is not uncommon with civil servants of exceeding zeal.", "어쩌면 그는 일을 집으로 들고 갔을지도 모른다 — 세상이 알아 두어야 할 일이지만, 열의가 넘치는 공무원에게는 드물지 않은 관행이다."]], [["Mark did think of opening his heart to his brother, and of leaving his message with him.", "마크는 동생에게 속을 털어놓고 그에게 전갈을 남길 생각도 해 보았다."], ["But his courage failed him, or perhaps it might be more correct to say that his prudence prevented him.", "그러나 용기가 나지 않았다. 아니, 어쩌면 신중함이 그를 막았다고 하는 것이 더 정확할지도 모른다."], ["It would be better for him, he thought, to tell his wife before he told any one else.", "다른 누구에게 말하기 전에 아내에게 먼저 말하는 것이 좋겠다고 그는 생각했다."], ["So he merely chatted with his brother for half an hour and then left him.", "그래서 그는 반 시간쯤 동생과 이야기만 나누고 그를 떠났다."]], [["The day was very tedious till the hour came at which he was to attend at Lord Lufton’s rooms; but at last it did come, and just as the clock struck, he turned out of Piccadilly into the Albany.", "그날은 러프턴 경의 방으로 갈 시간이 오기까지 몹시 지루했다. 그러나 마침내 그 시간이 왔고, 시계가 딱 치는 참에 그는 피카딜리를 벗어나 앨버니로 들어섰다."], ["As he was going across the court before he entered the building, he was greeted by a voice just behind him.", "건물에 들어서기 전에 앞마당을 지나가는데 바로 등 뒤에서 어떤 목소리가 그를 맞았다."]], [["“As punctual as the big clock on Barchester tower,”", "“바체스터 탑의 큰 시계만큼이나 정확하시군요,”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."], ["“See what it is to have a summons from a great man, Mr. Prebendary.”", "“큰사람의 부름을 받는다는 것이 어떤 것인지 보시라고요, 참사회원님.”"]], [["He turned round and extended his hand mechanically to Mr.", "그는 돌아서서 기계처럼 사워비 씨에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["Sowerby, and as he looked at him he thought he had never before seen him so pleasant in appearance, so free from care, and so joyous in demeanour.", "그리고 그를 바라보면서, 그가 이토록 겉모습이 즐겁고 이토록 근심에서 자유롭고 이토록 몸가짐이 흥겨운 것을 전에 본 적이 없다고 생각했다."]], [["“You have heard from Lord Lufton,”", "“러프턴 경에게서 소식을 들었지요,”"], ["said Mark in a voice that was certainly very lugubrious.", "마크가 확실히 매우 침울한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Heard from him!", "“그에게서 들었느냐고요!"], ["oh, yes, of course I have heard from him.", "아, 그야 물론 들었지요."], ["I’ll tell you what it is, Mark,”", "내 말을 들어 봐요, 마크,”"], ["and he now spoke almost in a whisper as they walked together along the Albany passage, “Lufton is a child in money matters—a perfect child.", "그리고 이제 그는 두 사람이 함께 앨버니의 복도를 걸어가는 동안 거의 속삭이듯이 말했다. “러프턴은 돈 문제에서는 어린애예요 — 완전한 어린애지요."], ["The dearest, finest fellow in the world, you know; but a very baby in money matters.”", "세상에서 가장 아끼고 가장 훌륭한 벗인 것은 알지만, 돈 문제에서는 아주 젖먹이예요.”"], ["And then they entered his lordship’s rooms.", "그러고서 그들은 나리의 방으로 들어섰다."]], [["Lord Lufton’s countenance also was lugubrious enough, but this did not in the least abash Sowerby, who walked quickly up to the young lord with his gait perfectly self-possessed and his face radiant with satisfaction.", "러프턴 경의 얼굴도 넉넉히 침울했으나, 이것은 사워비를 조금도 주춤하게 하지 못했다. 그는 조금도 흐트러지지 않은 걸음걸이로, 흡족함이 빛나는 얼굴로 젊은 경에게 성큼성큼 걸어갔다."]], [["“Well, Lufton, how are you?”", "“그래, 러프턴, 어떻게 지냅니까?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“It seems that my worthy friend Tozer has been giving you some trouble?”", "“보아하니 내 갸륵한 벗 토저가 당신에게 얼마간 골칫거리를 안겼다지요?”"]], [["Then Lord Lufton with a face by no means radiant with satisfaction again began the story of Tozer’s fraudulent demand upon him.", "그러자 러프턴 경은 흡족함이라고는 조금도 빛나지 않는 얼굴로, 토저가 자기에게 들이댄 그 부정한 요구의 이야기를 다시 시작했다."], ["Sowerby did not interrupt him, but listened patiently to the end;—quite patiently, although Lord Lufton, as he made himself more and more angry by the history of his own wrongs, did not hesitate to pronounce certain threats against Mr.", "사워비는 그의 말을 끊지 않고 끝까지 참고 들었다 — 아주 참고 들었다. 러프턴 경이 자기가 당한 억울함의 내력을 이야기하면서 갈수록 더 노여워지자 사워비 씨에게 어떤 위협을 서슴없이 내뱉었는데도, 앞서 마크 로바츠에게 그것을 내뱉었던 것과 같았는데도 그러했다."], ["Sowerby, as he had pronounced them before against Mark Robarts.", "그는 변호사를 통하지 않고서는 한 실링도 물지 않겠다고 말했다."], ["He would not, he said, pay a shilling, except through his lawyer; and he would instruct his lawyer, that before he paid anything, the whole matter should be exposed openly in court.", "그리고 변호사에게, 무엇을 물기 전에 이 일 전체를 법정에서 드러내 놓게 하라고 지시할 것이라고 했다."], ["He did not care, he said, what might be the effect on himself or any one else.", "그것이 자기에게든 다른 누구에게든 어떤 결과가 되든 상관하지 않겠다고 그는 말했다."], ["He was determined that the whole case should go to a jury.", "이 사건 전체를 배심에게 넘기기로 마음을 굳혔다고 했다."]], [["“To grand jury, and special jury, and common jury, and Old Jewry, if you like,”", "“대배심이든 특별 배심이든 보통 배심이든, 원한다면 올드 주리든 어디로든 넘기시지요,”"], ["said Sowerby.", "사워비가 말했다."], ["“The truth is, Lufton, you lost some money, and as there was some delay in paying it, you have been harassed.”", "“진실은 이래요, 러프턴. 당신은 얼마간 돈을 잃었고, 그것을 갚는 데 얼마간 지체가 있었기에 시달림을 받은 거예요.”"]], [["“I have paid more than I lost three times over,”", "“나는 잃은 것의 세 배가 넘게 물었어요,”"], ["said Lord Lufton, stamping his foot.", "러프턴 경이 발을 구르며 말했다."]], [["“I will not go into that question now.", "“그 문제는 지금 따지지 않겠어요."], ["It was settled, as I thought, some time ago by persons to whom you yourself referred it.", "그것은 얼마 전에, 당신 자신이 그 일을 부친 사람들의 손에서 결말이 났다고 나는 여겨요."], ["But will you tell me this: Why on earth should Robarts be troubled in this matter?", "그런데 이것 하나는 말해 줄 수 있겠지요. 어째서 이 일로 로바츠가 시달려야 합니까?"], ["What has he done?”", "그가 무엇을 했다고요?”"]], [["“Well, I don’t know.", "“글쎄, 나도 모르겠어요."], ["He arranged the matter with you.”", "그가 당신과 이 일을 처리했지요.”"]], [["“No such thing.", "“그런 일 없어요."], ["He was kind enough to carry a message from you to me, and to convey back a return message from me to you.", "그는 친절하게도 당신의 전갈을 내게 옮겨 주고, 나의 답을 당신에게 다시 옮겨 주었어요."], ["That has been his part in it.”", "그가 이 일에서 한 몫은 그것뿐이에요.”"]], [["“You don’t suppose that I want to implicate him: do you?”", "“내가 그를 끌어들이고 싶어 한다고 여기는 것은 아니겠지요?”"]], [["“I don’t think you want to implicate any one, but you are hot-headed and difficult to deal with, and very irrational into the bargain.", "“당신이 누군가를 끌어들이고 싶어 한다고는 생각하지 않아요. 그러나 당신은 머리가 뜨겁고 상대하기가 어렵고, 게다가 매우 사리에 어긋나요."], ["And, what is worse, I must say you are a little suspicious.", "그리고 더 나쁜 것은, 당신이 조금 의심이 많다는 말을 하지 않을 수 없군요."], ["In all this matter I have harassed myself greatly to oblige you, and in return I have got more kicks than halfpence.”", "이 모든 일에서 나는 당신에게 편의를 봐주려고 나 자신을 크게 시달리게 했는데, 돌아온 것은 반 페니보다 발길질이 더 많았어요.”"]], [["“Did not you give this bill to Tozer—the bill which he now holds?”", "“당신이 이 어음을 토저에게 주지 않았어요? 그가 지금 쥐고 있는 그 어음을?”"]], [["“In the first place he does not hold it; and in the next place I did not give it to him.", "“첫째로 그는 그것을 쥐고 있지 않아요. 그리고 둘째로 나는 그것을 그에게 주지 않았어요."], ["These things pass through scores of hands before they reach the man who makes the application for payment.”", "이런 것들은 지급을 청구하는 사람에게 닿기까지 수십 사람의 손을 지나갑니다.”"]], [["“And who came to me the other day?”", "“그러면 며칠 전에 나에게 온 사람은 누구요?”"]], [["“That, I take it, was Tom Tozer, a brother of our Tozer’s.”", "“그것은 내가 보기에 톰 토저, 우리 토저의 형제였을 거예요.”"]], [["“Then he holds the bill, for I saw it with him.”", "“그러면 그가 그 어음을 쥐고 있는 거지요. 내가 그것을 그와 함께 보았으니까.”"]], [["“Wait a moment; that is very likely.", "“잠깐만요, 그것은 아주 그럴 법해요."], ["I sent you word that you would have to pay for taking it up.", "그것을 떠맡는 데 값을 물어야 할 것이라고 내가 당신에게 전갈을 보냈지요."], ["Of course they don’t abandon those sort of things without some consideration.”", "물론 그런 것들은 얼마간의 대가 없이 놓아주지 않으니까요.”"]], [["“Ten pounds, you said,”", "“십 파운드라고 했지요,”"], ["observed Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“Ten or twenty; some such sum as that.", "“십이든 이십이든, 그쯤 되는 액수였지요."], ["But you were hardly so soft as to suppose that the man would ask for such a sum.", "그러나 그 사람이 그런 액수를 청구할 것이라고 여길 만큼 당신이 어리숙하지는 않았을 텐데요."], ["Of course he would demand the full payment.", "물론 그는 전액을 요구했겠지요."], ["There is the bill, Lord Lufton,”", "그 어음이 여기 있습니다, 러프턴 경.”"], ["and Sowerby, producing a document, handed it across the table to his lordship.", "그러면서 사워비는 문서 한 장을 꺼내 탁자 너머로 나리에게 건넸다."], ["“I gave five-and-twenty pounds for it this morning.”", "“오늘 아침에 이십오 파운드를 주고 사 왔어요.”"]], [["Lord Lufton took the paper and looked at it.", "러프턴 경은 그 종이를 받아 들고 살펴보았다."], ["“Yes,”", "“그렇군요,”"], ["said he, “that’s the bill.", "그가 말했다. “이것이 그 어음이에요."], ["What am I to do with it now?”", "이제 이것을 어떻게 하지요?”"]], [["“Put it with the family archives,”", "“집안 문서고에 넣어 두시든지,”"], ["said Sowerby,—“or behind the fire, just which you please.”", "사워비가 말했다. “아니면 불 뒤로 던지시든지, 마음에 드는 대로 하세요.”"]], [["“And is this the last of them?", "“그러면 이것이 마지막인가요?"], ["Can no other be brought up?”", "다른 것이 또 나올 수는 없어요?”"]], [["“You know better than I do what paper you may have put your hand to.", "“당신이 어떤 종이에 손을 댔는지는 나보다 당신이 더 잘 알지요."], ["I know of no other.", "나는 다른 것은 모릅니다."], ["At the last renewal that was the only outstanding bill of which I was aware.”", "마지막으로 갱신할 때 내가 아는 미결 어음은 그것뿐이었어요.”"]], [["“And you have paid five-and-twenty pounds for it?”", "“그리고 당신이 그것에 이십오 파운드를 물었다고요?”"]], [["“I have.", "“그랬어요."], ["Only that you have been in such a tantrum about it, and would have made such a noise this afternoon if I had not brought it, I might have had it for fifteen or twenty.", "당신이 그것을 두고 그토록 발끈해서, 내가 그것을 가져오지 않았더라면 오늘 오후에 그렇게 시끄럽게 굴었을 것이 아니었다면, 십오나 이십에 얻을 수도 있었을 거예요."], ["In three or four days they would have taken fifteen.”", "사나흘만 있으면 그들은 십오에 넘겼을 겁니다.”"]], [["“The odd ten pounds does not signify, and I’ll pay you the twenty-five, of course,”", "“십 파운드쯤 더 되는 것은 대수롭지 않아요. 물론 이십오는 내가 갚겠어요,”"], ["said Lord Lufton, who now began to feel a little ashamed of himself.", "이제 조금 부끄러워지기 시작한 러프턴 경이 말했다."]], [["“You may do as you please about that.”", "“그것은 마음대로 하세요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["it’s my affair, as a matter of course.", "물론 내 일이지요."], ["Any amount of that kind I don’t mind,”", "그만한 액수라면 나는 아깝지 않아요.”"], ["and he sat down to fill in a cheque for the money.", "그러고서 그는 앉아서 그 돈만큼 수표를 써 넣었다."]], [["“Well, now, Lufton, let me say a few words to you,”", "“그럼, 이제 러프턴, 내가 당신에게 몇 마디 하겠어요,”"], ["said Sowerby, standing with his back against the fireplace, and playing with a small cane which he held in his hand.", "사워비가 벽난로에 등을 대고 서서 손에 든 작은 지팡이를 놀리며 말했다."], ["“For heaven’s sake try and be a little more charitable to those around you.", "“제발 부탁이니 주위 사람들에게 좀 더 너그럽게 대하도록 애써 봐요."], ["When you become fidgety about anything, you indulge in language which the world won’t stand, though men who know you as well as Robarts and I may consent to put up with it.", "당신은 무슨 일로 안절부절못하게 되면 세상이 참아 주지 않을 말을 마구 쓰지요. 하기야 로바츠나 나처럼 당신을 잘 아는 사람은 그것을 참아 줄 마음을 낼지 몰라도요."], ["You have accused me, since I have been here, of all manner of iniquity—”", "당신은 내가 여기 온 뒤로 온갖 악행을 다 나에게 씌웠어요 —”"]], [["“Now, Sowerby—”", "“자, 사워비 —”"]], [["“My dear fellow, let me have my say out.", "“여보게, 내 말은 끝까지 하게 해 주세요."], ["You have accused me, I say, and I believe that you have accused him.", "당신은 나에게 혐의를 씌웠고, 또 그에게도 씌웠다고 나는 생각해요."], ["But it has never occurred to you, I daresay, to accuse yourself.”", "그런데 자기 자신에게 혐의를 씌워 보는 일은, 아마 당신에게 한 번도 떠오르지 않았겠지요.”"]], [["“Indeed it has.”", "“정말로 떠올랐어요.”"]], [["“Of course you have been wrong in having to do with such men as Tozer.", "“당신이 토저 같은 사람들과 상관한 것은 물론 잘못이었어요."], ["I have also been very wrong.", "나도 크게 잘못했지요."], ["It wants no great moral authority to tell us that.", "그것을 일러 주는 데 큰 도덕의 권위가 필요하지도 않아요."], ["Pattern gentlemen don’t have dealings with Tozer, and very much the better they are for not having them.", "모범이 될 신사들은 토저와 거래하지 않고, 거래하지 않는 편이 그들에게 훨씬 낫지요."], ["But a man should have back enough to bear the weight which he himself puts on it.", "그러나 사람은 제가 제 등에 얹은 무게를 견딜 만한 등을 가져야 하는 법이에요."], ["Keep away from Tozer, if you can, for the future; but if you do deal with him, for heaven’s sake keep your temper.”", "앞으로는 될 수 있으면 토저를 멀리해요. 그러나 그와 거래해야 한다면, 제발 부탁이니 성을 참아요.”"]], [["“That’s all very fine, Sowerby; but you know as well as I do—”", "“그것은 다 그럴싸한 말이에요, 사워비. 그러나 당신도 나만큼 잘 알듯이 —”"]], [["“I know this,”", "“나는 이것을 압니다,”"], ["said the devil, quoting Scripture, as he folded up the check for twenty-five pounds, and put it in his pocket, “that when a man sows tares, he won’t reap wheat, and it’s no use to expect it.", "성경을 인용하며 악마가 말했다. 그러고서 이십오 파운드 수표를 접어 주머니에 넣었다."], ["I am tough in these matters, and can bear a great deal—that is, if I be not pushed too far,”", "“사람이 가라지를 뿌리면 밀을 거두지 못하는 법이고, 그것을 기대해도 헛일이라는 것이지요. 나는 이런 일에는 질기고 많은 것을 견딜 수 있어요 — 너무 심하게 밀리지만 않는다면 말이지만,”"], ["and he looked full into Lord Lufton’s face as he spoke; “but I think you have been very hard upon Robarts.”", "그러면서 그는 말하는 동안 러프턴 경의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다. “그러나 나는 당신이 로바츠에게 너무 모질었다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“Never mind me, Sowerby; Lord Lufton and I are very old friends.”", "“나는 신경 쓰지 마세요, 사워비. 러프턴 경과 나는 아주 오랜 벗이에요.”"]], [["“And may therefore take a liberty with each other.", "“그러니까 서로 허물없이 굴 수도 있다는 말이지요."], ["Very well.", "아주 좋아요."], ["And now I’ve done my sermon.", "그리고 이제 내 설교는 끝났어요."], ["My dear dignitary, allow me to congratulate you.", "친애하는 고위 성직자님, 축하를 드리게 해 주세요."], ["I hear from Fothergill that that little affair of yours has been definitely settled.”", "포더길에게서 들었는데, 당신의 그 작은 일이 확실히 결말이 났다더군요.”"]], [["Mark’s face again became clouded.", "마크의 얼굴이 다시 흐려졌다."], ["“I rather think,”", "“나는 오히려,”"], ["said he, “that I shall decline the presentation.”", "그가 말했다. “그 임명을 사양할 생각이에요.”"]], [["“Decline it!”", "“사양한다고요!”"], ["said Sowerby, who, having used his utmost efforts to obtain it, would have been more absolutely offended by such vacillation on the vicar’s part than by any personal abuse which either he or Lord Lufton could heap upon him.", "사워비가 말했다. 그것을 얻으려고 온 힘을 다 쓴 그로서는, 교구 사제가 이렇게 마음이 흔들리는 것이 자기든 러프턴 경이든 자기에게 쏟아부을 수 있는 어떤 인격 모욕보다도 더 완전히 불쾌한 일이었다."]], [["“I think I shall,”", "“그럴 생각이에요,”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“And why?”", "“왜요?”"]], [["Mark looked up at Lord Lufton, and then remained silent for a moment.", "마크는 러프턴 경을 올려다보았고, 그러고서 한동안 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“There can be no occasion for such a sacrifice under the present circumstances,”", "“지금 사정에서는 그런 희생을 할 까닭이 있을 수 없네,”"], ["said his lordship.", "나리가 말했다."]], [["“And under what circumstances could there be occasion for it?”", "“그러면 어떤 사정에서라면 그럴 까닭이 있을 수 있겠어요?”"], ["asked Sowerby.", "사워비가 물었다."], ["“The Duke of Omnium has used some little influence to get the place for you as a parish clergyman belonging to his county, and I should think it monstrous if you were now to reject it.”", "“옴니엄 공작께서 당신이 자기 주에 속한 교구 성직자이기에 그 자리를 얻어 주시려고 얼마간 힘을 써 주셨는데, 이제 당신이 그것을 물리친다면 나는 그것을 괴이한 일로 여기겠어요.”"]], [["And then Robarts openly stated the whole of his reasons, explaining exactly what Lord Lufton had said with reference to the bill transactions, and to the allegation which would be made as to the stall having been given in payment for the accommodation.", "그러자 로바츠는 자기 이유를 다 드러내어 말했다. 러프턴 경이 그 어음 거래에 대해, 그리고 그 참사회원 자리가 그 융통의 값으로 주어졌다고 사람들이 말하게 될 것이라는 데 대해 정확히 무엇이라고 했는지를 설명한 것이었다."]], [["“Upon my word that’s too bad,”", "“정말이지 그것은 너무하군요,”"], ["said Sowerby.", "사워비가 말했다."]], [["“Now, Sowerby, I won’t be lectured,”", "“자, 사워비, 나는 훈계를 듣지 않겠어요,”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."]], [["“I have done my lecture,”", "“내 훈계는 끝났어요,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, aware, perhaps, that it would not do for him to push his friend too far, “and I shall not give a second.", "벗을 너무 심하게 몰아붙이는 것은 자기에게 좋지 않다는 것을 어쩌면 알고 있었던 것이다. “두 번은 하지 않겠어요."], ["But, Robarts, let me tell you this: as far as I know, Harold Smith has had little or nothing to do with the appointment.", "그러나 로바츠, 이것만은 말해 두지요. 내가 아는 한 해럴드 스미스는 이 임명에 관계한 것이 거의, 아니 전혀 없어요."], ["The duke has told the Prime Minister that he was very anxious that a parish clergyman from the county should go into the chapter, and then, at Lord Brock’s request, he named you.", "공작께서 총리에게, 이 주의 교구 성직자 하나가 성당 참사회에 들어가기를 몹시 바란다고 말씀하셨고, 그러고 나서 브록 경의 청에 따라 당신을 지명하셨어요."], ["If under those circumstances you talk of giving it up, I shall believe you to be insane.", "그런 사정인데도 당신이 그것을 버리겠다는 말을 한다면, 나는 당신을 실성한 사람으로 믿겠어요."], ["As for the bill which you accepted for me, you need have no uneasiness about it.", "당신이 나를 위해 인수한 그 어음에 대해서는 마음 놓아도 좋아요."], ["The money will be ready; but of course, when that time comes, you will let me have the hundred and thirty for—”", "돈은 마련될 겁니다. 그러나 물론 그때가 오면 그 백삼십은 나에게 넘겨줘야 —”"]], [["And then Mr. Sowerby took his leave, having certainly made himself master of the occasion.", "그러고서 사워비 씨는 작별을 했으니, 이 자리의 주인이 되었음은 확실했다."], ["If a man of fifty have his wits about him, and be not too prosy, he can generally make himself master of the occasion, when his companions are under thirty.", "쉰 살 먹은 사람이 정신을 차리고 있고 너무 딱딱하게 늘어놓지만 않는다면, 함께 있는 이들이 서른이 못 되었을 때는 대개 그 자리의 주인이 될 수 있는 법이다."]], [["Robarts did not stay at the Albany long after him, but took his leave, having received some assurances of Lord Lufton’s regret for what had passed and many promises of his friendship for the future.", "로바츠는 그가 떠난 뒤로 앨버니에 오래 머물지 않고, 지난 일에 대한 러프턴 경의 후회의 말과 앞으로의 우정에 대한 많은 약속을 받고서 작별을 했다."], ["Indeed Lord Lufton was a little ashamed of himself.", "실제로 러프턴 경은 조금 부끄러워하고 있었다."], ["“And as for the prebend, after what has passed, of course you must accept it.”", "“그리고 참사회원직 말인데, 이렇게 된 뒤이니 자네는 물론 그것을 받아야 해.”"], ["Nevertheless his lordship had not omitted to notice Mr. Sowerby’s hint about the horse and the hundred and thirty pounds.", "그러면서도 나리는 말과 백삼십 파운드에 대한 사워비 씨의 그 암시를 놓치지 않고 알아들었다."]], [["Robarts, as he walked back to his hotel, thought that he certainly would accept the Barchester promotion, and was very glad that he had said nothing on the subject to his brother.", "로바츠는 호텔로 걸어 돌아가면서, 자기는 바체스터의 승진을 확실히 받아들일 것이라고 생각했고, 그 일에 대해 동생에게 한마디도 하지 않은 것이 아주 잘된 일이라고 여겼다."], ["On the whole his spirits were much raised.", "대체로 그의 마음은 크게 들려 있었다."], ["That assurance of Sowerby’s about the bill was very comforting to him; and strange to say, he absolutely believed it.", "어음에 대한 사워비의 그 다짐은 그에게 매우 위안이 되었다. 그리고 이상하게도 그는 그것을 온전히 믿었다."], ["In truth Sowerby had been so completely the winning horse at the late meeting, that both Lord Lufton and Robarts were inclined to believe almost anything he said;—which was not always the case with either of them.", "사실 사워비는 이번 만남에서 그토록 완전히 이긴 말이었으므로, 러프턴 경도 로바츠도 그가 하는 말은 거의 무엇이든 믿을 마음이 들었다 — 두 사람 다 늘 그러던 것은 아니었지만."]], [["# CHAPTER XX. HAROLD SMITH IN THE CABINET.", "# 제20장. 내각에 든 해럴드 스미스"]], [["For a few days the whole Harold Smith party held their heads very high.", "며칠 동안 해럴드 스미스 패 전체가 고개를 아주 높이 쳐들고 다녔다."], ["It was not only that their man had been made a cabinet minister; but a rumour had got abroad that Lord Brock, in selecting him, had amazingly strengthened his party, and done much to cure the wounds which his own arrogance and lack of judgment had inflicted on the body politic of his government.", "그들의 사람이 각료가 되었다는 것만이 아니었다. 브록 경이 그를 뽑음으로써 놀랍도록 자기 당을 강하게 만들었고, 자신의 오만과 판단력 부족이 제 정부라는 정치체에 입힌 상처를 낫게 하는 데 크게 이바지했다는 소문이 나돌았기 때문이다."], ["So said the Harold-Smithians, much elated.", "해럴드 스미스주의자들은 몹시 우쭐해져 그렇게 말했다."], ["And when we consider what Harold had himself achieved, we need not be surprised that he himself was somewhat elated also.", "그리고 해럴드 자신이 이룩한 것을 생각한다면, 그 자신도 다소 우쭐해졌다 해서 놀랄 일은 아니다."]], [["It must be a proud day for any man when he first walks into a cabinet.", "누구에게든 처음으로 내각에 걸어 들어가는 날은 자랑스러운 날일 것이다."], ["But when a humble-minded man thinks of such a phase of life, his mind becomes lost in wondering what a cabinet is.", "그러나 마음이 겸손한 사람이 인생의 그런 국면을 생각하면, 그 마음은 내각이란 대체 무엇인가를 헤아리다 길을 잃고 만다."], ["Are they gods that attend there or men?", "거기 참석하는 이들은 신인가 사람인가?"], ["Do they sit on chairs, or hang about on clouds?", "그들은 의자에 앉는가, 구름 위에 걸터앉는가?"], ["When they speak, is the music of the spheres audible in their Olympian mansion, making heaven drowsy with its harmony?", "그들이 말할 때, 천구의 음악이 그 올림포스의 저택에 들려 그 화음으로 하늘을 졸음에 잠기게 하는가?"], ["In what way do they congregate?", "그들은 어떤 식으로 모이는가?"], ["In what order do they address each other?", "어떤 순서로 서로에게 말을 거는가?"], ["Are the voices of all the deities free and equal?", "모든 신들의 목소리가 자유롭고 동등한가?"], ["Is plodding Themis from the Home Department, or Ceres from the Colonies, heard with as rapt attention as powerful Pallas of the Foreign Office, the goddess that is never seen without her lance and helmet?", "내무부에서 온 터덕터덕 일하는 테미스나 식민부에서 온 케레스도, 창과 투구 없이는 결코 보이지 않는 여신인 외무부의 힘센 팔라스와 똑같이 넋을 빼앗긴 주목을 받으며 들리는가?"], ["Does our Whitehall Mars make eyes there at bright young Venus of the Privy Seal, disgusting that quaint tinkering Vulcan, who is blowing his bellows at our Exchequer, not altogether unsuccessfully?", "우리 화이트홀의 마르스는 거기서 옥새 상서의 빛나는 젊은 베누스에게 눈짓을 보내어, 우리 재무부에서 풀무질을 하되 아주 헛되지만은 않은 저 별난 땜장이 불카누스를 역겹게 만드는가?"], ["Old Saturn of the Woolsack sits there mute, we will say, a relic of other days, as seated in this divan.", "대법관석의 늙은 사투르누스는 이 회의석에 앉은 채로 다른 시절의 유물이 되어 말없이 저기 앉아 있다고 해 두자."], ["The hall in which he rules is now elsewhere.", "그가 다스리는 전당은 이제 다른 곳에 있다."], ["Is our Mercury of the Post Office ever ready to fly nimbly from globe to globe, as great Jove may order him, while Neptune, unaccustomed to the waves, offers needful assistance to the Apollo of the India Board?", "우리 우정청의 메르쿠리우스는 위대한 유피테르가 명하는 대로 천체에서 천체로 날래게 날아갈 준비가 언제나 되어 있는가? 그러는 동안 파도에 익숙하지 않은 넵투누스는 인도부의 아폴로에게 필요한 도움을 건네는가?"], ["How Juno sits apart, glum and huffy, uncared for, Council President though she be, great in name, but despised among gods—that we can guess.", "이름은 대단하나 신들 사이에서는 업신여겨지는 추밀원 의장인 몸으로, 유노가 어떻게 홀로 떨어져 침울하고 뿌루퉁하게, 아무도 아끼지 않는 가운데 앉아 있는가—그것은 우리가 짐작할 수 있다."], ["If Bacchus and Cupid share Trade and the Board of Works between them, the fitness of things will have been as fully consulted as is usual.", "박쿠스와 쿠피도가 통상부와 공공사업청을 서로 나누어 가진다면, 사물의 알맞음은 여느 때만큼이나 충분히 헤아려진 것이 되겠다."], ["And modest Diana of the Petty Bag, latest summoned to these banquets of ambrosia,—does she not cling retiring near the doors, hardly able as yet to make her low voice heard among her brother deities?", "그리고 암브로시아의 이 잔치에 가장 늦게 불려 온 프티백 청의 얌전한 디아나는—아직 제 낮은 목소리를 형제 신들 사이에서 들리게 하지도 못한 채 문간 가까이에 물러서 붙어 있지 않은가?"], ["But Jove, great Jove—old Jove, the King of Olympus, hero among gods and men, how does he carry himself in these councils summoned by his voice?", "그러나 유피테르, 위대한 유피테르—늙은 유피테르, 올림포스의 왕이며 신들과 사람들 가운데 영웅인 그는, 제 목소리로 불러 모은 이 회의에서 어떻게 처신하는가?"], ["Does he lie there at his ease, with his purple cloak cut from the firmament around his shoulders?", "그는 어깨에 창궁을 잘라 만든 자줏빛 외투를 걸치고 편안히 거기 누워 있는가?"], ["Is his thunderbolt ever at his hand to reduce a recreant god to order?", "배반한 신을 질서로 되돌릴 벼락이 늘 그 손에 있는가?"], ["Can he proclaim silence in that immortal hall?", "그는 그 불멸의 전당에 침묵을 명할 수 있는가?"], ["Is it not there, as elsewhere, in all places, and among all nations, that a king of gods and a king of men is and will be king, rules and will rule, over those who are smaller than himself?", "다른 곳에서, 온갖 자리에서, 온갖 나라에서 그러하듯 거기에서도, 신들의 왕이자 사람들의 왕은 자기보다 작은 자들 위에서 왕이며 앞으로도 왕일 것이요, 다스리며 앞으로도 다스릴 것이 아닌가?"]], [["Harold Smith, when he was summoned to the august hall of divine councils, did feel himself to be a proud man; but we may perhaps conclude that at the first meeting or two he did not attempt to take a very leading part.", "해럴드 스미스는 신들의 회의가 열리는 그 존엄한 전당으로 불려 갔을 때 자신이 자랑스러운 사람이라고 느꼈다. 그러나 우리는 아마 처음 한두 번의 회의에서 그가 아주 앞장서는 몫을 맡으려 들지는 않았으리라고 결론지어도 좋을 것이다."], ["Some of my readers may have sat at vestries, and will remember how mild, and for the most part, mute, is a new-comer at their board.", "내 독자들 가운데 교구 위원회에 앉아 본 이도 있을 터인데, 그들의 회의석에 새로 온 사람이 얼마나 온순하고 대체로 얼마나 말이 없는지 기억하시리라."], ["He agrees generally, with abated enthusiasm; but should he differ, he apologizes for the liberty.", "그는 열의를 죽인 채 대체로 동의한다. 그러나 뜻이 다를 때에는 그런 자유를 누린 데 대해 사과한다."], ["But anon, when the voices of his colleagues have become habitual in his ears—when the strangeness of the room is gone, and the table before him is known and trusted—he throws off his awe and dismay, and electrifies his brotherhood by the vehemence of his declamation and the violence of his thumping.", "그러나 이윽고 동료들의 목소리가 귀에 익숙해지고—방의 낯섦이 사라지고 앞에 놓인 탁자가 알 만하고 믿을 만해지면—그는 두려움과 당황을 벗어던지고, 격렬한 연설과 사나운 탁자 두드림으로 제 형제들을 감전시킨다."], ["So let us suppose it will be with Harold Smith, perhaps in the second or third season of his cabinet practice.", "해럴드 스미스도 그리 될 것이라고, 아마 그의 내각 수련 두 번째나 세 번째 철에는 그리 될 것이라고 해 두자."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["alas!", "아아!"], ["that such pleasures should be so fleeting!", "그런 즐거움이 그토록 덧없어야 한다니!"]], [["And then, too, there came upon him a blow which somewhat modified his triumph—a cruel, dastard blow, from a hand which should have been friendly to him, from one to whom he had fondly looked to buoy him up in the great course that was before him.", "그리고 또, 그의 승리를 다소 무디게 만드는 타격 하나가 그에게 떨어졌다—잔인하고 비겁한 타격이, 그에게 다정해야 했을 손에서, 앞에 놓인 큰 길에서 자기를 떠받쳐 주기를 그가 애틋하게 기대하던 사람에게서 날아왔다."], ["It had been said by his friends that in obtaining Harold Smith’s services the Prime Minister had infused new young healthy blood into his body.", "해럴드 스미스의 봉사를 얻음으로써 총리가 제 몸에 새롭고 젊고 건강한 피를 넣었다고 그의 벗들은 말해 왔다."], ["Harold himself had liked the phrase, and had seen at a glance how it might have been made to tell by some friendly Supplehouse or the like.", "해럴드 자신도 그 어구가 마음에 들었고, 다정한 서플하우스 같은 이가 그것을 어떻게 써서 효험을 낼 수 있을지 한눈에 알아보았다."], ["But why should a Supplehouse out of Elysium be friendly to a Harold Smith within it?", "그러나 엘리시움 밖에 있는 서플하우스가 어째서 엘리시움 안에 있는 해럴드 스미스에게 다정하겠는가?"], ["Men lapped in Elysium, steeped to the neck in bliss, must expect to see their friends fall off from them.", "엘리시움에 싸여 목까지 복락에 잠긴 사람은 벗들이 자기에게서 떨어져 나가는 것을 볼 각오를 해야 한다."], ["Human nature cannot stand it.", "사람의 본성이 그것을 견디지 못한다."], ["If I want to get anything from my old friend Jones, I like to see him shoved up into a high place.", "내가 옛 벗 존스에게서 무언가를 얻어 내고 싶다면, 나는 그가 높은 자리로 밀려 올라가는 것을 보고 싶다."], ["But if Jones, even in his high place, can do nothing for me, then his exaltation above my head is an insult and an injury.", "그러나 존스가 그 높은 자리에서도 나를 위해 아무것도 해 줄 수 없다면, 내 머리 위로 올라간 그의 승진은 모욕이요 상해다."], ["Who ever believes his own dear intimate companion to be fit for the highest promotion? Mr.", "자기와 아주 가까운 벗이 가장 높은 자리에 오를 만하다고 누가 믿겠는가?"], ["Supplehouse had known Mr. Smith too closely to think much of his young blood.", "서플하우스 씨는 스미스 씨를 너무 가까이서 알았기에 그의 젊은 피를 대단하게 여기지 않았다."]], [["Consequently, there appeared an article in the Jupiter, which was by no means complimentary to the ministry in general.", "그리하여 《주피터》에 기사 하나가 실렸는데, 내각 전반에 대해 결코 칭찬이 아니었다."], ["It harped a good deal on the young blood view of the question, and seemed to insinuate that Harold Smith was not much better than diluted water. “The Prime Minister,”", "그것은 이 문제의 젊은 피라는 관점을 꽤 늘어지게 물고 늘어졌고, 해럴드 스미스가 물을 탄 물보다 나을 것이 별로 없다는 듯이 슬쩍 비추었다."], ["the article said, “having lately recruited his impaired vigour by a new infusion of aristocratic influence of the highest moral tone, had again added to himself another tower of strength chosen from among the people.", "“총리는”이라고 그 기사는 말했다. “가장 높은 도덕적 격조를 지닌 귀족의 영향력을 새로 주입함으로써 최근 쇠약해진 기력을 보충한 데 이어, 다시 백성 가운데서 뽑은 또 하나의 힘의 탑을 제게 더했다."], ["What might he not hope, now that he possessed the services of Lord Brittleback and Mr. Harold Smith!", "브리틀백 경과 해럴드 스미스 씨의 봉사를 이제 가졌으니 그가 바라지 못할 것이 무엇이겠는가!"], ["Renovated in a Medea’s caldron of such potency, all his effete limbs—and it must be acknowledged that some of them had become very effete—would come forth young and round and robust.", "그토록 강력한 메데이아의 가마솥에서 되살아나면, 그의 온갖 쇠잔한 팔다리는—그중 어떤 것들은 아주 쇠잔해졌음을 인정하지 않을 수 없다—젊고 둥글고 튼튼하게 다시 나올 것이다. 새 기운이 모든 부서에 두루 퍼질 것이다."], ["A new energy would diffuse itself through every department; India would be saved and quieted; the ambition of France would be tamed; even-handed reform would remodel our courts of law and parliamentary elections; and Utopia would be realized.", "인도는 구원받고 잠잠해질 것이다. 프랑스의 야심은 길들여질 것이다."], ["Such, it seems, is the result expected in the ministry from Mr.", "공평한 개혁이 우리 법정과 의회 선거를 다시 만들 것이다. 그리고 유토피아가 실현될 것이다."], ["Harold Smith’s young blood!”", "해럴드 스미스 씨의 젊은 피에서 내각이 기대하는 결과가 바로 그런 것인 듯하다!”"]], [["This was cruel enough, but even this was hardly so cruel as the words with which the article ended.", "이것도 충분히 잔인했으나, 이조차도 그 기사가 끝맺는 말만큼 잔인하지는 않았다."], ["By that time irony had been dropped, and the writer spoke out earnestly his opinion upon the matter.", "그때쯤에는 반어가 버려지고, 글쓴이는 이 문제에 대한 제 의견을 진지하게 털어놓았다."], ["“We beg to assure Lord Brock,”", "“우리는 브록 경에게 이렇게 확언해 드리고자 한다.”"], ["said the article, “that such alliances as these will not save him from the speedy fall with which his arrogance and want of judgment threaten to overwhelm it.", "기사는 말했다. “이런 동맹들은 그의 오만과 판단력 부족이 그것을 뒤덮어 버리겠다고 위협하는 저 빠른 몰락에서 그를 구해 주지 못할 것이다."], ["As regards himself we shall be sorry to hear of his resignation.", "그 자신에 관해서라면 우리는 그의 사임을 듣게 되는 것을 애석해할 것이다."], ["He is in many respects the best statesman that we possess for the emergencies of the present period.", "그는 여러 면에서 지금 이 시기의 위급한 사태들에 대해 우리가 가진 최고의 정치인이다."], ["But if he be so ill-judged as to rest on such men as Mr. Harold Smith and Lord Brittleback for his assistants in the work which is before him, he must not expect that the country will support him.", "그러나 앞에 놓인 일에서 조력자로 해럴드 스미스 씨와 브리틀백 경 같은 사람들에게 기대는 그토록 잘못된 판단을 한다면, 그는 나라가 자기를 지지할 것이라고 기대해서는 안 된다."], ["Mr. Harold Smith is not made of the stuff from which cabinet ministers should be formed.”", "해럴드 스미스 씨는 각료가 만들어져야 할 그런 재목으로 되어 있지 않다.”"]], [["Mr. Harold Smith, as he read this, seated at his breakfast-table, recognized, or said that he recognized, the hand of Mr.", "해럴드 스미스 씨는 아침 식탁에 앉아 이것을 읽으면서 붓질마다 서플하우스 씨의 손을 알아보았다, 아니 알아보았다고 말했다."], ["Supplehouse in every touch. That phrase about the effete limbs was Supplehouse all over, as was also the realization of Utopia.", "쇠잔한 팔다리에 관한 그 어구는 처음부터 끝까지 서플하우스였고, 유토피아의 실현도 그러했다."], ["“When he wants to be witty, he always talks about Utopia,”", "“재치를 부리고 싶을 때면 저이는 늘 유토피아 이야기를 하지.”"], ["said Mr. Harold Smith—to himself: for Mrs.", "해럴드 스미스 씨는 그렇게 말했다—혼자서."], ["Harold was not usually present in the flesh at these matutinal meals.", "스미스 부인은 이 아침 식사 자리에 몸소 나와 있는 일이 흔치 않았으니까."]], [["And then he went down to his office, and saw in the glance of every man that he met an announcement that that article in the Jupiter had been read.", "그러고서 그는 청으로 내려갔고, 마주치는 사람마다 그 눈빛에서 《주피터》의 그 기사가 읽혔다는 통고를 보았다."], ["His private secretary tittered in evident allusion to the article, and the way in which Buggins took his coat made it clear that it was well known in the messengers’ lobby.", "그의 개인 비서는 그 기사를 두고 하는 것이 분명한 암시로 킬킬거렸고, 버긴스가 그의 외투를 받아 드는 태도로 보아 그것이 사환실에도 잘 알려져 있음이 분명했다."], ["“He won’t have to fill up my vacancy when I go,”", "“내가 나갈 때 저이가 내 빈자리를 채울 일은 없겠군.”"], ["Buggins was saying to himself.", "버긴스는 혼자 그렇게 말하고 있었다."], ["And then in the course of the morning came the cabinet council, the second that he had attended, and he read in the countenance of every god and goddess there assembled that their chief was thought to have made another mistake.", "그러고서 오전이 흐르는 동안 내각 회의가 왔으니, 그가 참석한 두 번째 회의였다. 그리고 그는 거기 모인 신과 여신 하나하나의 낯빛에서, 저희 우두머리가 또 한 번 실수를 저질렀다고 여겨지고 있음을 읽었다."], ["If Mr. Supplehouse could have been induced to write in another strain, then indeed that new blood might have been felt to have been efficacious.", "서플하우스 씨가 다른 가락으로 쓰도록 이끌릴 수 있었다면, 그렇다면 정말로 그 새 피가 효험이 있다고 느껴졌을 것이다."]], [["All this was a great drawback to his happiness, but still it could not rob him of the fact of his position.", "이 모든 것이 그의 행복에 큰 걸림돌이었으나, 그래도 그것이 그의 지위라는 사실을 그에게서 앗아 갈 수는 없었다."], ["Lord Brock could not ask him to resign because the Jupiter had written against him; nor was Lord Brock the man to desert a new colleague for such a reason.", "《주피터》가 그를 반대해 썼다는 이유로 브록 경이 그에게 사임을 청할 수는 없었다. 브록 경은 그런 이유로 새 동료를 버릴 사람도 아니었다."], ["So Harold Smith girded his loins, and went about the duties of the Petty Bag with new zeal.", "그리하여 해럴드 스미스는 허리를 동여매고 새 열의로 프티백 청의 직무에 나섰다."], ["“Upon my word, the Jupiter is right,”", "“정말이지 《주피터》가 옳다.”"], ["said young Robarts to himself, as he finished his fourth dozen of private notes explanatory of everything in and about the Petty Bag Office.", "프티백 청 안팎의 모든 것을 설명하는 사적인 쪽지를 마흔여덟 장째 끝마치며 젊은 로바츠는 혼자 그렇게 말했다."], ["Harold Smith required that his private secretary’s notes should be so terribly precise.", "해럴드 스미스는 자기 개인 비서의 쪽지가 무섭도록 정확할 것을 요구했다."]], [["But nevertheless, in spite of his drawbacks, Harold Smith was happy in his new honours, and Mrs. Harold Smith enjoyed them also.", "그러나 그럼에도, 걸림돌들이 있었음에도 해럴드 스미스는 새 명예를 누리며 행복했고, 해럴드 스미스 부인도 그것을 즐겼다."], ["She certainly, among her acquaintance, did quiz the new cabinet minister not a little, and it may be a question whether she was not as hard upon him as the writer in the Jupiter.", "그녀는 확실히 아는 사람들 사이에서 새 각료를 적잖이 놀려 먹었고, 그녀가 《주피터》의 글쓴이만큼이나 그에게 매섭지 않았는가 하는 것은 하나의 물음거리가 될 수 있다."], ["She whispered a great deal to Miss Dunstable about new blood, and talked of going down to Westminster Bridge to see whether the Thames were really on fire.", "그녀는 던스터블 양에게 새 피에 관해 아주 많이 귓속말을 했고, 웨스트민스터 다리로 내려가 템스강에 정말로 불이 붙었는지 보고 오겠다는 말을 했다."], ["But though she laughed, she triumphed, and though she flattered herself that she bore her honours without any outward sign, the world knew that she was triumphing, and ridiculed her elation.", "그러나 웃으면서도 그녀는 개선했고, 아무런 겉으로 드러나는 표시 없이 제 명예를 지고 있다고 스스로 여겼으나, 세상은 그녀가 개선하고 있음을 알았고 그 우쭐거림을 비웃었다."]], [["About this time she also gave a party—not a pure-minded conversazione like Mrs. Proudie, but a downright wicked worldly dance, at which there were fiddles, ices, and champagne sufficient to run away with the first quarter’s salary accruing to Harold from the Petty Bag Office.", "이 무렵 그녀는 또 야회 하나를 열었다—프라우디 부인네 같은 순결한 마음의 콘베르사치오네가 아니라, 노골적으로 사악하고 세속스러운 무도회였다."], ["To us this ball is chiefly memorable from the fact that Lady Lufton was among the guests.", "바이올린과 아이스가 있었고, 샴페인은 프티백 청에서 해럴드에게 들어올 첫 분기 봉급을 날려 버릴 만큼 있었다."], ["Immediately on her arrival in town she received cards from Mrs. H.", "우리에게 이 무도회가 주로 기억할 만한 것은 레이디 러프턴이 손님들 가운데 있었다는 사실 때문이다."], ["Smith for herself and Griselda, and was about to send back a reply at once declining the honour.", "런던에 도착한 즉시 그분은 해럴드 스미스 부인에게서 자기와 그리젤다 몫의 초대 카드를 받고, 당장 그 영광을 사양하는 답장을 보내려 했다."], ["What had she to do at the house of Mr. Sowerby’s sister?", "사워비 씨의 누이동생 집에 그분이 무슨 볼일이 있단 말인가?"], ["But it so happened that at that moment her son was with her, and as he expressed a wish that she should go, she yielded.", "그러나 마침 그때 아들이 함께 있었고, 아들이 어머니가 가면 좋겠다는 뜻을 밝히자 그분은 뜻을 굽혔다."], ["Had there been nothing in his tone of persuasion more than ordinary,—had it merely had reference to herself,—she would have smiled on him for his kind solicitude, have made out some occasion for kissing his forehead as she thanked him, and would still have declined.", "그가 설득하는 어조에 여느 때보다 더한 것이 없었다면—그것이 그분 자신에게만 관계된 것이었다면—그분은 그의 살가운 염려에 미소를 지어 보이고, 고맙다고 하면서 그의 이마에 입 맞출 구실을 어떻게든 만들어 냈을 것이며, 그러고도 여전히 사양했을 것이다."], ["But he had reminded her both of himself and Griselda.", "그러나 그는 그분에게 자기와 그리젤다 둘 다를 일깨웠다."], ["“You might as well go, mother, for the sake of meeting me,”", "“어머니, 저를 만난다는 이유만으로도 가시는 게 좋겠어요.”"], ["he said; “Mrs.", "그가 말했다."], ["Harold caught me the other day, and would not liberate me till I had given her a promise.”", "“요전에 스미스 부인이 저를 붙잡고서, 제가 약속을 하기 전에는 놓아주지 않았거든요.”"]], [["“That is an attraction certainly,”", "“그건 확실히 끌리는 일이지.”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“I do like going to a house when I know that you will be there.”", "“나는 네가 거기 있을 것을 아는 집에 가는 것이 참 좋다.”"]], [["“And now that Miss Grantly is with you—you owe it to her to do the best you can for her.”", "“그리고 이제 그랜틀리 양이 어머니와 함께 있으니, 어머니는 그 아가씨를 위해 할 수 있는 데까지 해 주실 의무가 있잖아요.”"]], [["“I certainly do, Ludovic; and I have to thank you for reminding me of my duty so gallantly.”", "“그야 물론이지, 루도빅. 그리고 그렇게 씩씩하게 내 의무를 일깨워 준 것은 네게 고맙다고 해야겠구나.”"], ["And so she said that she would go to Mrs. Harold Smith’s.", "그리하여 그분은 해럴드 스미스 부인네에 가겠다고 말했다."], ["Poor lady!", "딱한 부인!"], ["She gave much more weight to those few words about Miss Grantly than they deserved.", "그분은 그랜틀리 양에 관한 그 몇 마디에 그것이 받을 만한 것보다 훨씬 더 무게를 얹었다."], ["It rejoiced her heart to think that her son was anxious to meet Griselda—that he should perpetrate this little ruse in order to gain his wish.", "아들이 그리젤다를 만나고 싶어 안달한다는 것—제 소원을 이루려고 이런 자잘한 술수를 부린다는 것—을 생각하니 그분의 마음이 기뻤다."], ["But he had spoken out of the mere emptiness of his mind, without thought of what he was saying, excepting that he wished to please his mother.", "그러나 그는 그저 머릿속이 텅 빈 데서, 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지 생각도 없이 말한 것이었다. 어머니를 기쁘게 해 드리고 싶다는 것 말고는 아무 뜻이 없었다."]], [["But nevertheless he went to Mrs. Harold Smith’s, and when there he did dance more than once with Griselda Grantly—to the manifest discomfiture of Lord Dumbello.", "그러나 어쨌든 그는 해럴드 스미스 부인네에 갔고, 거기서 그리젤다 그랜틀리와 한 번 이상 춤을 추었다—덤벨로 경이 눈에 띄게 마음이 불편해지도록."], ["He came in late, and at the moment Lord Dumbello was moving slowly up the room, with Griselda on his arm, while Lady Lufton was sitting near looking on with unhappy eyes.", "그는 늦게 왔는데, 그가 온 순간 덤벨로 경은 그리젤다를 팔에 걸고 방을 천천히 거슬러 오르고 있었고, 레이디 러프턴은 가까이 앉아 언짢은 눈으로 그 모습을 바라보고 있었다."], ["And then Griselda sat down, and Lord Dumbello stood mute at her elbow.", "그러고서 그리젤다가 자리에 앉자, 덤벨로 경은 그 옆에 말없이 서 있었다."]], [["“Ludovic,”", "“루도빅.”"], ["whispered his mother, “Griselda is absolutely bored by that man, who follows her like a ghost.", "어머니가 귓속말을 했다. “그리젤다가 저 사람 때문에 진력이 다 났어."], ["Do go and rescue her.”", "유령처럼 따라다니잖니. 가서 좀 구해 주렴.”"]], [["He did go and rescue her, and afterwards danced with her for the best part of an hour consecutively.", "그는 가서 그녀를 구해 냈고, 그 뒤로 거의 한 시간을 잇달아 그녀와 춤을 추었다."], ["He knew that the world gave Lord Dumbello the credit of admiring the young lady, and was quite alive to the pleasure of filling his brother nobleman’s heart with jealousy and anger.", "세상이 덤벨로 경에게 그 젊은 아가씨를 흠모한다는 평판을 주고 있음을 그는 알았고, 형제 귀족의 마음을 질투와 노여움으로 채우는 즐거움에 아주 살아 있었다."], ["Moreover, Griselda was in his eyes very beautiful, and had she been one whit more animated, or had his mother’s tactics been but a thought better concealed, Griselda might have been asked that night to share the vacant throne at Lufton, in spite of all that had been said and sworn in the drawing-room of Framley Parsonage.", "게다가 그의 눈에 그리젤다는 아주 아름다웠으니, 그녀가 조금만 더 생기가 있었더라면, 또는 어머니의 술책이 한 생각만큼이라도 더 잘 감춰져 있었더라면, 프램리 목사관 응접실에서 말하고 맹세한 그 모든 것에도 불구하고 그리젤다는 그날 밤 러프턴의 빈 왕좌를 함께 나누자는 청을 받았을지도 모른다."]], [["It must be remembered that our gallant, gay Lothario had passed some considerable number of days with Miss Grantly in his mother’s house, and the danger of such contiguity must be remembered also.", "우리의 늠름하고 쾌활한 로타리오가 어머니의 집에서 그랜틀리 양과 함께 적잖이 많은 날을 보냈다는 것을 기억해야 하고, 그런 가까움이 지닌 위험도 기억해야 한다."], ["Lord Lufton was by no means a man capable of seeing beauty unmoved or of spending hours with a young lady without some approach to tenderness.", "러프턴 경은 아름다움을 보고도 마음이 움직이지 않을 사람이 결코 아니었고, 젊은 아가씨와 여러 시간을 보내면서 다정함에 다가서는 일이 조금도 없을 사람도 결코 아니었다."], ["Had there been no such approach, it is probable that Lady Lufton would not have pursued the matter.", "그렇게 다가서는 일이 전혀 없었다면 레이디 러프턴도 아마 이 일을 밀고 나가지 않았을 것이다."], ["But, according to her ideas on such subjects, her son Ludovic had on some occasions shown quite sufficient partiality for Miss Grantly to justify her in her hopes, and to lead her to think that nothing but opportunity was wanted.", "그러나 그런 문제에 관한 그분의 생각으로는, 아들 루도빅이 몇 차례 그랜틀리 양에 대한 편애를 그분의 바람을 정당하게 만들 만큼, 그리고 기회 말고는 아무것도 모자란 것이 없다고 여기게 만들 만큼 충분히 드러냈다."], ["Now, at this ball of Mrs. Smith’s, he did, for a while, seem to be taking advantage of such opportunity, and his mother’s heart was glad.", "그런데 이제 스미스 부인의 이 무도회에서 그가 한동안 그런 기회를 이용하는 것처럼 보였고, 어머니의 마음은 기뻤다."], ["If things should turn out well on this evening she would forgive Mrs. Harold Smith all her sins.", "이 저녁에 일이 잘 되어 간다면 그분은 해럴드 스미스 부인의 모든 죄를 용서할 것이었다."]], [["And for a while it looked as though things would turn out well.", "그리고 한동안은 일이 잘 되어 갈 것처럼 보였다."], ["Not that it must be supposed that Lord Lufton had come there with any intention of making love to Griselda, or that he ever had any fixed thought that he was doing so.", "그렇다고 러프턴 경이 그리젤다에게 사랑을 고백할 뜻을 품고 거기 왔다거나, 자기가 그러고 있다는 어떤 굳은 생각을 가졌다고 짐작해서는 안 된다."], ["Young men in such matters are so often without any fixed thoughts!", "젊은 사내들은 그런 일에서 굳은 생각이라는 것이 없기가 얼마나 흔한지!"], ["They are such absolute moths.", "그들은 그토록 완전한 나방이다."], ["They amuse themselves with the light of the beautiful candle, fluttering about, on and off, in and out of the flame with dazzled eyes, till in a rash moment they rush in too near the wick, and then fall with singed wings and crippled legs, burnt up and reduced to tinder by the consuming fire of matrimony.", "아름다운 촛불의 빛을 가지고 즐기며 눈이 부신 채로 불꽃 안팎을 들락날락 펄럭이다가, 성급한 한순간에 심지에 너무 가까이 달려들고, 그러면 날개가 그슬리고 다리가 부러진 채 떨어져, 혼인이라는 삼켜 버리는 불에 타서 부싯깃이 되고 만다."], ["Happy marriages, men say, are made in heaven, and I believe it.", "행복한 혼인은 하늘에서 맺어진다고 사람들은 말하고, 나는 그것을 믿는다."], ["Most marriages are fairly happy, in spite of Sir Cresswell Cresswell; and yet how little care is taken on earth towards such a result!", "크레스웰 크레스웰 경이 있음에도 대부분의 혼인은 꽤 행복하다. 그런데도 그런 결과를 얻기 위해 땅에서 들이는 정성은 얼마나 적은지!"]], [["“I hope my mother is using you well?”", "“저희 어머니가 잘 대해 드리고 있나요?”"], ["said Lord Lufton to Griselda, as they were standing together in a doorway between the dances.", "무도곡 사이에 함께 문간에 서 있을 때 러프턴 경이 그리젤다에게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes: she is very kind.”", "“네, 아주 친절하세요.”"]], [["“You have been rash to trust yourself in the hands of so very staid and demure a person.", "“그토록 점잖고 얌전한 분의 손에 스스로를 맡기신 것은 경솔한 일이었어요."], ["And, indeed, you owe your presence here at Mrs. Harold Smith’s first cabinet ball altogether to me.", "그리고 실은 해럴드 스미스 부인의 이 첫 각료 무도회에 그랜틀리 양이 와 있는 것도 온전히 제 덕입니다."], ["I don’t know whether you are aware of that.”", "그것을 아시는지 모르겠군요.”"]], [["“Oh, yes: Lady Lufton told me.”", "“네, 레이디 러프턴이 말씀해 주셨어요.”"]], [["“And are you grateful or otherwise?", "“그래서 고마운가요, 아닌가요?"], ["Have I done you an injury or a benefit?", "제가 해를 끼친 겁니까, 은혜를 베푼 겁니까?"], ["Which do you find best, sitting with a novel in the corner of a sofa in Bruton Street, or pretending to dance polkas here with Lord Dumbello?”", "브루턴 스트리트의 소파 구석에 앉아 소설을 읽는 것과, 여기서 덤벨로 경과 폴카를 추는 척하는 것 중에 어느 것이 더 좋으신가요?”"]], [["“I don’t know what you mean.", "“무슨 말씀인지 모르겠어요."], ["I haven’t stood up with Lord Dumbello all the evening.", "저는 저녁 내내 덤벨로 경과 짝을 지어 일어선 적이 없어요."], ["We were going to dance a quadrille, but we didn’t.”", "카드리유를 추려고는 했지만 추지 않았어요.”"]], [["“Exactly; just what I say;—pretending to do it.", "“그렇지요. 바로 제 말이 그겁니다—추는 척한 것이지요."], ["Even that’s a good deal for Lord Dumbello; isn’t it?”", "덤벨로 경으로서는 그것만도 대단한 일 아닌가요?”"], ["And then Lord Lufton, not being a pretender himself, put his arm round her waist, and away they went up and down the room, and across and about, with an energy which showed that what Griselda lacked in her tongue she made up with her feet.", "그러고서 러프턴 경은, 그 자신은 척하는 사람이 아니었으므로, 팔을 그녀의 허리에 두르고 두 사람은 방을 오르내리고 가로지르고 이리저리 돌며 나아갔으니, 그리젤다가 혀로 모자란 것을 발로 메운다는 것을 보여 주는 기운이었다."], ["Lord Dumbello, in the meantime, stood by, observant, thinking to himself that Lord Lufton was a glib-tongued, empty-headed ass, and reflecting that if his rival were to break the tendons of his leg in one of those rapid evolutions, or suddenly come by any other dreadful misfortune, such as the loss of all his property, absolute blindness, or chronic lumbago, it would only serve him right.", "그러는 동안 덤벨로 경은 옆에 서서 지켜보며, 러프턴 경은 혀나 매끄럽고 머릿속은 텅 빈 얼간이라고 속으로 생각했고, 만약 제 맞수가 그 재빠른 회전 가운데 하나에서 다리의 힘줄을 끊거나, 아니면 갑자기 다른 어떤 끔찍한 불행을—재산을 다 잃는 것이든, 완전한 눈멂이든, 만성 요통이든—당한다면 그것은 그에게 마땅한 일일 뿐이라고 헤아렸다."], ["And in that frame of mind he went to bed, in spite of the prayer which no doubt he said as to his forgiveness of other people’s trespasses.", "그리고 그런 마음가짐으로 그는 잠자리에 들었으니, 남의 잘못을 용서한다는 기도를 그가 틀림없이 올렸을 터인데도 그러했다."]], [["And then, when they were again standing, Lord Lufton, in the little intervals between his violent gasps for fresh breath, asked Griselda if she liked London.", "그러고서 두 사람이 다시 서 있게 되었을 때, 러프턴 경은 새 숨을 얻으려 사납게 헐떡이는 사이사이의 자잘한 틈에서, 런던이 좋으냐고 그리젤다에게 물었다."], ["“Pretty well,”", "“꽤 좋아요.”"], ["said Griselda, gasping also a little herself.", "그리젤다도 조금 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["“I am afraid—you were very dull—down at Framley.”", "“아무래도—저 아래 프램리에서는—아주 심심하셨겠지요.”"]], [["“Oh, no;—I liked it—particularly.”", "“아니에요—좋았어요—아주.”"]], [["“It was a great bore when you went—away, I know.", "“그랜틀리 양이 떠나—버렸을 때는 참 지루했어요."], ["There wasn’t a soul—about the house worth speaking to.”", "그 집에는 말을 붙일 만한—사람이 하나도 없었지요.”"], ["And they remained silent for a minute till their lungs had become quiescent.", "그러고서 두 사람은 폐가 잠잠해질 때까지 잠시 말이 없었다."]], [["“Not a soul,”", "“하나도 없었어요.”"], ["he continued—not of falsehood prepense, for he was not in fact thinking of what he was saying.", "그가 이어 말했다—작정한 거짓말은 아니었으니, 실은 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지 생각하고 있지 않았기 때문이다."], ["It did not occur to him at the moment that he had truly found Griselda’s going a great relief, and that he had been able to do more in the way of conversation with Lucy Robarts in one hour than with Miss Grantly during a month of intercourse in the same house.", "그리젤다가 떠난 것을 자기가 참으로 크게 홀가분해했다는 것, 그리고 같은 집에서 한 달을 함께 오간 그랜틀리 양과보다 한 시간에 루시 로바츠와 더 많은 이야기를 할 수 있었다는 것이 그 순간에는 그에게 떠오르지 않았다."], ["But, nevertheless, we should not be hard upon him.", "그러나 그렇다 해도 우리가 그에게 매섭게 굴 일은 아니다."], ["All is fair in love and war; and if this was not love, it was the usual thing that stands as a counterpart for it.", "사랑과 전쟁에서는 무엇이든 정당하고, 이것이 사랑이 아니었다면 그것은 사랑의 짝으로 대신 서는 여느 물건이었다."]], [["“Not a soul,”", "“하나도 없었어요.”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."], ["“I was very nearly hanging myself in the park next morning;—only it rained.”", "“이튿날 아침에는 공원에서 목을 매달 뻔했지요—비가 온 덕에 안 했지만요.”"]], [["“What nonsense!", "“터무니없는 소리!"], ["You had your mother to talk to.”", "이야기 나눌 어머니가 계셨잖아요.”"]], [["“Oh, my mother,—yes; and you may tell me too, if you please, that Captain Culpepper was there.", "“아, 어머니요—그렇지요. 그리고 컬페퍼 대위도 거기 있었다고 덧붙여 주셔도 좋습니다."], ["I do love my mother dearly; but do you think that she could make up for your absence?”", "저는 어머니를 참으로 깊이 사랑해요. 하지만 어머니가 그랜틀리 양이 없는 자리를 메울 수 있다고 생각하시나요?”"], ["And his voice was very tender, and so were his eyes.", "그러고 그의 목소리는 아주 다정했고, 눈도 그러했다."]], [["“And Miss Robarts; I thought you admired her very much?”", "“그리고 로바츠 양도요. 저는 경께서 그 아가씨를 아주 많이 흠모하시는 줄 알았어요.”"]], [["“What, Lucy Robarts?”", "“뭐라고요, 루시 로바츠?”"], ["said Lord Lufton, feeling that Lucy’s name was more than he at present knew how to manage.", "러프턴 경은 루시의 이름이 지금 자기가 다룰 줄 아는 것보다 더 큰 것임을 느끼며 말했다."], ["Indeed that name destroyed all the life there was in that little flirtation.", "실로 그 이름은 그 자잘한 희롱에 있던 생기를 다 죽여 놓았다."], ["“I do like Lucy Robarts, certainly.", "“루시 로바츠를 좋아하기는 하지요, 물론."], ["She is very clever; but it so happened that I saw little or nothing of her after you were gone.”", "아주 영리한 사람입니다. 하지만 그랜틀리 양이 떠난 뒤로는 어쩌다 보니 그 사람을 거의, 아니 전혀 보지 못했어요.”"]], [["To this Griselda made no answer, but drew herself up, and looked as cold as Diana when she froze Orion in the cave.", "이 말에 그리젤다는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 몸을 곧추세운 채, 동굴에서 오리온을 얼려 버릴 때의 디아나처럼 차갑게 보였다."], ["Nor could she be got to give more than monosyllabic answers to the three or four succeeding attempts at conversation which Lord Lufton made.", "그리고 러프턴 경이 뒤이어 세 번 네 번 대화를 해 보려 애썼으나 그녀에게서 한 마디짜리 대답 이상은 얻어 낼 수 없었다."], ["And then they danced again, but Griselda’s steps were by no means so lively as before.", "그러고서 두 사람은 다시 춤을 추었으나, 그리젤다의 발걸음은 아까만큼 결코 활발하지 않았다."]], [["What took place between them on that occasion was very little more than what has been here related.", "그날 두 사람 사이에 일어난 일은 여기 적은 것보다 아주 조금밖에 더 되지 않는다."], ["There may have been an ice or a glass of lemonade into the bargain, and perhaps the faintest possible attempt at hand-pressing.", "아이스나 레모네이드 한 잔이 덤으로 있었을 수도 있고, 어쩌면 손을 지그시 누르려는 되도록 희미한 시도가 있었을 수도 있다."], ["But if so, it was all on one side.", "그러나 그랬다 해도 그것은 온전히 한쪽만의 일이었다."], ["To such overtures as that Griselda Grantly was as cold as any Diana.", "그런 다가섬에 대해서라면 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 어느 디아나만큼이나 차가웠다."]], [["But little as all this was, it was sufficient to fill Lady Lufton’s mind and heart.", "이 모두가 이토록 적은 것이었으나, 레이디 러프턴의 마음과 가슴을 채우기에는 넉넉했다."], ["No mother with six daughters was ever more anxious to get them off her hands, than Lady Lufton was to see her son married,—married, that is, to some girl of the right sort.", "딸 여섯을 둔 어머니라도 그 딸들을 제 손에서 떼어 놓고 싶어 안달한 것이, 레이디 러프턴이 아들이 결혼하는 것을—그러니까 알맞은 종류의 아가씨와 결혼하는 것을—보고 싶어 안달한 것보다 더하지는 않았다."], ["And now it really did seem as though he were actually going to comply with her wishes.", "그런데 이제 정말로 그가 그분의 바람에 따르려 하는 것처럼 보였다."], ["She had watched him during the whole evening, painfully endeavouring not to be observed in doing so.", "그분은 저녁 내내 그를 지켜보면서, 그러고 있는 것이 눈에 띄지 않도록 애를 쓰며 마음을 졸였다."], ["She had seen Lord Dumbello’s failure and wrath, and she had seen her son’s victory and pride.", "그분은 덤벨로 경의 패배와 분노를 보았고, 아들의 승리와 자랑을 보았다."], ["Could it be the case that he had already said something, which was still allowed to be indecisive only through Griselda’s coldness?", "그가 이미 무언가 말을 해 놓았고, 그것이 그리젤다의 차가움 때문에 아직 결판나지 않은 채로만 남아 있다는 것이 사실일 수 있을까?"], ["Might it not be the case, that by some judicious aid on her part, that indecision might be turned into certainty, and that coldness into warmth?", "그분 쪽에서 슬기롭게 도와주면 그 미결이 확실로 바뀌고 그 차가움이 따뜻함으로 바뀔 수 있지 않을까?"], ["But then any such interference requires so delicate a touch,—as Lady Lufton was well aware.", "그러나 그런 끼어듦은 어느 것이나 아주 섬세한 손길을 요구하는 법이니—레이디 러프턴도 그것을 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["“Have you had a pleasant evening?”", "“저녁을 즐겁게 지냈어요?”"], ["Lady Lufton said, when she and Griselda were seated together with their feet on the fender of her ladyship’s dressing-room.", "마님의 내실에서 그리젤다와 함께 난로 울에 발을 얹고 앉았을 때 레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["Lady Lufton had especially invited her guest into this, her most private sanctum, to which as a rule none had admittance but her daughter, and sometimes Fanny Robarts.", "레이디 러프턴은 특별히 이 손님을 자기의 가장 사적인 성소인 이곳으로 불러들였으니, 여기는 원래 딸 말고는, 그리고 때때로 패니 로바츠 말고는 아무도 들일 수 없는 곳이었다."], ["But to what sanctum might not such a daughter-in-law as Griselda have admittance?", "그러나 그리젤다 같은 며느리라면 어느 성소에라도 들어오지 못할 데가 있겠는가?"]], [["“Oh, yes—very,”", "“아, 네—아주요.”"], ["said Griselda.", "그리젤다가 말했다."]], [["“It seemed to me that you bestowed most of your smiles upon Ludovic.”", "“보아하니 미소를 대부분 루도빅에게 나누어 준 것 같군요.”"], ["And Lady Lufton put on a look of good pleasure that such should have been the case.", "그러면서 레이디 러프턴은 그랬다면 좋겠다는 흡족한 표정을 지었다."]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["I don’t know,”", "모르겠어요.”"], ["said Griselda: “I did dance with him two or three times.”", "그리젤다가 말했다. “두세 번 그분과 짝을 지어 춤을 추기는 했어요.”"]], [["“Not once too often to please me, my dear.", "“나를 기쁘게 하기에 지나치게 여러 번은 아니에요, 얘야."], ["I like to see Ludovic dancing with my friends.”", "나는 루도빅이 내 벗들과 춤추는 것을 보는 게 좋아요.”"]], [["“I am sure I am very much obliged to you, Lady Lufton.”", "“저야 참으로 고마운 일이지요, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“Not at all, my dear.", "“그럴 것 없어요, 얘야."], ["I don’t know where he could get so nice a partner.”", "그이가 그렇게 좋은 짝을 어디서 얻을 수 있을지 나는 모르겠어요.”"], ["And then she paused a moment, not feeling how far she might go.", "그러고서 그분은 얼마나 더 나아가도 좋을지 감이 잡히지 않아 잠시 말을 멈추었다."], ["In the meantime Griselda sat still, staring at the hot coals.", "그러는 동안 그리젤다는 가만히 앉아 이글거리는 숯불을 바라보고 있었다."], ["“Indeed, I know that he admires you very much,”", "“정말이지 나는 그이가 그리젤다를 아주 많이 흠모한다는 것을 알고 있어요.”"], ["continued Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 이어 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["no, I am sure he doesn’t,”", "아니에요, 분명 그렇지 않아요.”"], ["said Griselda; and then there was another pause.", "그리젤다가 말했다. 그러고서 또 한 번 말이 없었다."]], [["“I can only say this,”", "“나는 이것만은 말할 수 있어요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, “that if he does do so—and I believe he does—it would give me very great pleasure.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그이가 정말로 그러하다면—나는 그렇다고 믿어요—그것은 내게 아주 큰 기쁨이 되겠다는 것이에요."], ["For you know, my dear, that I am very fond of you myself.”", "얘야, 알다시피 나 자신이 그리젤다를 아주 좋아하니까요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["thank you,”", "고마워요.”"], ["said Griselda, and stared at the coals more perseveringly than before.", "그리젤다는 그렇게 말하고 아까보다 더 끈덕지게 숯불을 바라보았다."]], [["“He is a young man of a most excellent disposition—though he is my own son, I will say that—and if there should be anything between you and him—”", "“그이는 성품이 아주 훌륭한 젊은 사람이에요—내 자식이지만 그것만은 말하겠어요—그리고 그리젤다와 그이 사이에 무슨 일이 있다면—”"]], [["“There isn’t, indeed, Lady Lufton.”", "“정말로 아무것도 없어요, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“But if there ever should be, I should be delighted to think that Ludovic had made so good a choice.”", "“하지만 언제라도 그런 일이 있게 된다면, 루도빅이 그토록 훌륭한 선택을 했다고 생각하게 되어 나는 기쁠 거예요.”"]], [["“But there will never be anything of the sort, I’m sure, Lady Lufton.", "“하지만 그런 일은 결코 없을 거예요, 레이디 러프턴."], ["He is not thinking of such a thing in the least.”", "그분은 그런 것을 조금도 생각하고 있지 않아요.”"]], [["“Well, perhaps he may, some day.", "“글쎄, 어느 날엔가는 그럴지도 모르지요."], ["And now, good-night, my dear.”", "그럼 잘 자요, 얘야.”"]], [["“Good-night, Lady Lufton.”", "“안녕히 자세요, 레이디 러프턴.”"], ["And Griselda kissed her with the utmost composure, and betook herself to her own bedroom.", "그리고 그리젤다는 지극히 침착하게 그분에게 입을 맞추고 자기 침실로 갔다."], ["Before she retired to sleep she looked carefully to her different articles of dress, discovering what amount of damage the evening’s wear and tear might have inflicted.", "잠자리에 들기 전 그녀는 제 여러 옷가지들을 꼼꼼히 살펴, 저녁 내내 입고 쓰는 동안 어느 정도의 손상이 났는지 알아보았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXI. WHY PUCK, THE PONY, WAS BEATEN.", "# 제21장. 왜 조랑말 퍽이 매를 맞았는가"]], [["Mark Robarts returned home the day after the scene at the Albany, considerably relieved in spirit.", "마크 로바츠는 올버니에서의 그 일이 있은 이튿날 집으로 돌아왔다. 마음이 적잖이 놓인 채였다."], ["He now felt that he might accept the stall without discredit to himself as a clergyman in doing so.", "이제 그는 참사회원 석을 받아들여도 그렇게 하는 것이 성직자로서 스스로에게 부끄러울 일은 아니라고 느꼈다."], ["Indeed, after what Mr. Sowerby had said, and after Lord Lufton’s assent to it, it would have been madness, he considered, to decline it.", "아니, 사워비 씨가 한 말이 있고 거기에 러프턴 경이 동의한 뒤였으니, 그것을 마다하는 것이야말로 미친 짓이라고 그는 여겼다."], ["And then, too, Mr. Sowerby’s promise about the bills was very comfortable to him.", "게다가 어음에 관한 사워비 씨의 약속도 그에게는 무척 마음 편한 것이었다."], ["After all, might it not be possible that he might get rid of all these troubles with no other drawback than that of having to pay £130 for a horse that was well worth the money?", "어쩌면 그 값을 넉넉히 하는 말 한 필에 130파운드를 치러야 한다는 것 말고는 다른 손해 없이 이 모든 근심에서 벗어날 수도 있지 않겠는가?"]], [["On the day after his return he received proper authentic tidings of his presentation to the prebend.", "돌아온 이튿날 그는 참사회원직에 지명되었다는 정식 통지를 받았다."], ["He was, in fact, already prebendary, or would be as soon as the dean and chapter had gone through the form of instituting him in his stall.", "사실 그는 이미 참사회원이었고, 수석 사제와 성당 참사회가 그를 참사회원 석에 앉히는 절차만 밟으면 그것으로 끝이었다."], ["The income was already his own; and the house also would be given up to him in a week’s time—a part of the arrangement with which he would most willingly have dispensed had it been at all possible to do so.", "수입은 벌써 그의 것이었다. 집도 일주일 뒤면 그에게 넘어올 것이었는데, 그 대목만은 어떻게든 할 수 있었더라면 기꺼이 없는 것으로 하고 싶은 부분이었다."], ["His wife congratulated him nicely, with open affection, and apparent satisfaction at the arrangement.", "아내는 애정을 숨기지 않고, 그 처분에 흡족한 낯빛으로 그를 곱게 축하해 주었다."], ["The enjoyment of one’s own happiness at such windfalls depends so much on the free and freely expressed enjoyment of others!", "이런 횡재에 제 행복을 즐기는 일은 남들이 거리낌 없이, 또 거리낌 없이 말로 드러내며 함께 즐거워해 주는가에 참으로 크게 달려 있다!"], ["Lady Lufton’s congratulations had nearly made him throw up the whole thing; but his wife’s smiles re-encouraged him; and Lucy’s warm and eager joy made him feel quite delighted with Mr. Sowerby and the Duke of Omnium.", "레이디 러프턴의 축하는 그로 하여금 이 일을 통째로 내던지게 만들 지경이었으나, 아내의 웃음이 그를 다시 북돋웠고, 루시의 따뜻하고 열띤 기쁨은 사워비 씨와 옴니엄 공작이 아주 고맙게 여겨지도록 만들었다."], ["And then that splendid animal, Dandy, came home to the parsonage stables, much to the delight of the groom and gardener, and of the assistant stable boy who had been allowed to creep into the establishment, unawares as it were, since “master” had taken so keenly to hunting.", "그러고 나서 그 훌륭한 짐승 댄디가 목사관 마구간으로 들어왔으니, 마부와 정원사가 크게 기뻐하였고, “주인”이 사냥에 그토록 열을 올리게 된 뒤로 어느새 슬그머니 살림에 끼어들도록 허락된 마구간 조수 아이도 기뻐하였다."], ["But this satisfaction was not shared in the drawing-room.", "그러나 이 흡족함은 응접실에서는 나누어지지 않았다."], ["The horse was seen on his first journey round to the stable gate, and questions were immediately asked.", "말이 마구간 문으로 돌아 들어가는 첫걸음에 눈에 띄었고, 곧바로 물음이 터져 나왔다."], ["It was a horse, Mark said, “which he had bought from Mr. Sowerby some little time since with the object of obliging him.", "마크의 말로는, 그것은 “얼마 전 사워비 씨의 편의를 봐줄 셈으로 그에게서 사들인 말”이었다."], ["He, Mark, intended to sell him again, as soon as he could do so judiciously.”", "“나, 마크는 사려 깊게 처리할 수 있게 되는 대로 다시 팔 작정”이었다."], ["This, as I have said above, was not satisfactory.", "앞서 말하였듯 이것은 만족스러운 답이 아니었다."], ["Neither of the two ladies at Framley Parsonage knew much about horses, or of the manner in which one gentleman might think it proper to oblige another by purchasing the superfluities of his stable; but they did both feel that there were horses enough in the parsonage stable without Dandy, and that the purchasing of a hunter with the view of immediately selling him again, was, to say the least of it, an operation hardly congenial with the usual tastes and pursuits of a clergyman.", "프램리 목사관의 두 부인은 말에 대해서도, 한 신사가 다른 신사의 마구간에 남는 것을 사들여 편의를 봐주는 것이 옳다고 여길 만한 방식에 대해서도 아는 바가 많지 않았다. 그러나 두 사람 다 댄디가 없어도 목사관 마구간에는 말이 넉넉하다는 것, 그리고 사냥말을 사서 곧바로 되팔 셈이라는 것은 아무리 좋게 말해도 성직자의 여느 취향과 하는 일에는 좀처럼 어울리지 않는 짓이라는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“I hope you did not give very much money for him, Mark,”", "“그 말에 돈을 너무 많이 주지는 않았기를 바라요, 마크,”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."]], [["“Not more than I shall get again,”", "“다시 받아 낼 것보다 많이 주지는 않았어,”"], ["said Mark; and Fanny saw from the form of his countenance that she had better not pursue the subject any further at that moment.", "마크가 말했다. 그리고 패니는 그의 얼굴 모양새로 보아 그 이야기를 그 자리에서 더 캐지 않는 편이 좋겠다는 것을 알았다."]], [["“I suppose I shall have to go into residence almost immediately,”", "“곧바로 거주 근무를 시작해야 할 것 같군,”"], ["said Mark, recurring to the more agreeable subject of the stall.", "마크가 참사회원 석이라는 좀 더 기분 좋은 이야기로 되돌아가며 말했다."]], [["“And shall we all have to go and live at Barchester at once?”", "“그러면 우리 모두 곧장 바체스터로 가서 살아야 하나요?”"], ["asked Lucy.", "루시가 물었다."]], [["“The house will not be furnished, will it, Mark?”", "“그 집엔 가구도 없을 텐데요, 마크?”"], ["said his wife.", "아내가 말했다."], ["“I don’t know how we shall get on.”", "“우리가 어떻게 지내야 할지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["“Don’t frighten yourselves.", "“겁내지 마."], ["I shall take lodgings in Barchester.”", "바체스터에 하숙을 얻을 테니까.”"]], [["“And we shall not see you all the time,”", "“그럼 그동안 내내 당신을 못 보겠네요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts with dismay.", "로바츠 부인이 낙담하여 말했다."], ["But the prebendary explained that he would be backwards and forwards at Framley every week, and that in all probability he would only sleep at Barchester on the Saturdays and Sundays—and, perhaps, not always then.", "그러나 참사회원께서는 매주 프램리를 오락가락할 것이며, 바체스터에서 잠을 자는 것은 아무래도 토요일과 일요일뿐일 것이고, 어쩌면 그때조차 늘 그렇지는 않을 것이라고 설명하였다."]], [["“It does not seem very hard work, that of a prebendary,”", "“참사회원 일이란 게 그리 힘든 일은 아닌 것 같네요,”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“But it is very dignified,”", "“하지만 아주 위엄 있는 자리예요,”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."], ["“Prebendaries are dignitaries of the Church—are they not, Mark?”", "“참사회원은 교회의 위엄을 지닌 분들이잖아요 — 그렇지 않아요, 마크?”"]], [["“Decidedly,”", "“단연 그렇지,”"], ["said he; “and their wives also, by special canon law.", "그가 말했다. “특별 교회법에 따라 그 아내들도 그렇고."], ["The worst of it is that both of them are obliged to wear wigs.”", "고약한 것은 양쪽 다 가발을 써야 한다는 점이야.”"]], [["“Shall you have a hat, Mark, with curly things at the side, and strings through to hold them up?”", "“모자도 쓰게 되나요, 마크? 양옆에 도르르 말린 것이 달리고, 그것을 붙들어 매도록 끈이 꿰인 모자요?”"], ["asked Lucy.", "루시가 물었다."]], [["“I fear that does not come within my perquisites.”", "“그건 내게 딸려 오는 몫에는 들지 않는 모양이구나.”"]], [["“Nor a rosette?", "“장식 매듭도 없어요?"], ["Then I shall never believe that you are a dignitary.", "그럼 마크가 위엄 있는 분이라는 건 저는 절대로 믿지 않을 거예요."], ["Do you mean to say that you will wear a hat like a common parson—like Mr. Crawley, for instance?”", "여느 목사처럼 — 이를테면 크롤리 씨처럼 — 그런 모자를 쓰겠다는 말인가요?”"]], [["“Well—I believe I may give a twist to the leaf; but I am by no means sure till I shall have consulted the dean in chapter.”", "“글쎄 — 모자 테를 한 번 비틀어 접는 것쯤은 해도 될 것 같다만, 참사회에서 수석 사제께 물어보기 전에는 결코 확실치 않구나.”"]], [["And thus at the parsonage they talked over the good things that were coming to them, and endeavoured to forget the new horse, and the hunting boots that had been used so often during the last winter, and Lady Lufton’s altered countenance.", "이렇게 목사관에서는 그들에게 다가오는 좋은 것들을 두고 이야기를 나누었고, 새 말과, 지난겨울 동안 그토록 자주 신었던 사냥 장화와, 달라진 레이디 러프턴의 낯빛은 잊으려 애썼다."], ["It might be that the evils would vanish away, and the good things alone remain to them.", "어쩌면 나쁜 것들은 스러져 사라지고 좋은 것들만 그들에게 남을지도 몰랐다."]], [["It was now the month of April, and the fields were beginning to look green, and the wind had got itself out of the east and was soft and genial, and the early spring flowers were showing their bright colours in the parsonage garden, and all things were sweet and pleasant.", "이제 사월이었다. 들판은 푸르러지기 시작하였고, 바람은 동쪽에서 벗어나 부드럽고 온화해졌으며, 이른 봄꽃들이 목사관 정원에서 밝은 빛깔을 내보이고 있었고, 온갖 것이 달고 즐거웠다."], ["This was a period of the year that was usually dear to Mrs. Robarts.", "이맘때는 여느 해에도 로바츠 부인에게 소중한 철이었다."], ["Her husband was always a better parson when the warm months came than he had been during the winter.", "그녀의 남편은 따뜻한 달이 오면 겨울 동안보다 늘 더 나은 목사였다."], ["The distant county friends whom she did not know and of whom she did not approve went away when the spring came, leaving their houses innocent and empty.", "그녀가 알지도 못하고 좋게 여기지도 않는 먼 고을의 벗들은 봄이 오면 떠나가, 그 집들을 죄 없이 비워 두었다."], ["The parish duty was better attended to, and perhaps domestic duties also.", "교구 일이 더 잘 챙겨졌고, 어쩌면 집안 일도 그러하였다."], ["At such period he was a pattern parson and a pattern husband, atoning to his own conscience for past shortcomings by present zeal.", "그런 철에 그는 본이 되는 목사요 본이 되는 남편이었으니, 지난날의 모자람을 오늘의 열심으로 제 양심에 갚는 것이었다."], ["And then, though she had never acknowledged it to herself, the absence of her dear friend Lady Lufton was perhaps in itself not disagreeable. Mrs.", "그리고 또, 스스로 한 번도 인정한 적은 없었으나, 소중한 벗 레이디 러프턴이 없다는 것도 어쩌면 그 자체로 싫지 않은 일이었다."], ["Robarts did love Lady Lufton heartily; but it must be acknowledged of her ladyship, that, with all her good qualities, she was inclined to be masterful.", "로바츠 부인은 레이디 러프턴을 참으로 마음 깊이 사랑하였다. 그러나 마님에 대해서는 이것만은 인정해야 하겠는데, 그 온갖 좋은 자질에도 그분은 남을 휘두르는 쪽으로 기울어 있었다."], ["She liked to rule, and she made people feel that she liked it. Mrs.", "그분은 다스리기를 좋아하였고, 좋아한다는 것을 사람들이 느끼도록 하였다."], ["Robarts would never have confessed that she laboured under a sense of thraldom; but perhaps she was mouse enough to enjoy the temporary absence of her kind-hearted cat.", "로바츠 부인이라면 제가 종노릇하는 심정에 짓눌려 있다고는 결코 고백하지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 어쩌면 그녀도 마음씨 고운 고양이가 잠시 자리를 비운 것을 즐길 만한 쥐였다."], ["When Lady Lufton was away Mrs. Robarts herself had more play in the parish.", "레이디 러프턴이 없을 때에는 로바츠 부인 자신이 교구에서 더 마음껏 움직일 수 있었다."]], [["And Mark also was not unhappy, though he did not find it practicable immediately to turn Dandy into money.", "그리고 마크 역시 불행하지 않았다. 댄디를 곧바로 돈으로 바꾸는 일이 될 만하지 않았기는 하였으나 그러하였다."], ["Indeed, just at this moment, when he was a good deal over at Barchester, going through those deep mysteries and rigid ecclesiastical examinations which are necessary before a clergyman can become one of a chapter, Dandy was rather a thorn in his side.", "실은 바로 이 무렵, 성직자가 성당 참사회의 한 사람이 되기 전에 반드시 거쳐야 하는 그 깊은 신비와 엄정한 교회 심사를 치르느라 바체스터에 자주 건너가 있던 그때에, 댄디는 오히려 옆구리에 박힌 가시였다."], ["Those wretched bills were to come due early in May, and before the end of April Sowerby wrote to him saying that he was doing his utmost to provide for the evil day; but that if the price of Dandy could be remitted to him at once, it would greatly facilitate his object.", "그 딱한 어음들은 오월 초에 만기가 돌아올 것이었는데, 사월이 끝나기 전에 사워비가 그에게 편지를 보내어, 그 흉한 날에 대비하려고 있는 힘을 다하고 있으나 댄디의 값을 지금 당장 자기에게 보내 준다면 그 일이 크게 수월해지겠다고 하였다. 돈에 관한 사워비 씨의 어조는 때에 따라 이보다 더 다를 수 없었다."], ["Nothing could be more different than Mr. Sowerby’s tone about money at different times.", "그가 돈을 긁어모아야 할 때에는 무엇이든 그렇게 중대하였다."], ["When he wanted to raise the wind, everything was so important; haste and superhuman efforts, and men running to and fro with blank acceptances in their hands, could alone stave off the crack of doom; but at other times, when retaliatory applications were made to him, he could prove with the easiest voice and most jaunty manner that everything was quite serene.", "서둘러야 하고, 사람의 힘을 넘어서는 애를 써야 하며, 백지 인수서를 손에 든 사람들이 이리저리 뛰어다녀야만 세상 끝나는 소리를 겨우 막을 수 있었다. 그러나 다른 때, 이쪽에서 되받아 청구를 넣으면, 그는 세상에서 가장 편안한 목소리와 가장 경쾌한 태도로 온갖 것이 아주 태평하다는 것을 증명해 낼 수 있었다."], ["Now, at this period, he was in that mood of superhuman efforts, and he called loudly for the hundred and thirty pounds for Dandy.", "이제 이 무렵 그는 사람의 힘을 넘어서는 애를 쓰는 그 기분에 들어 있었고, 댄디 값 백삼십 파운드를 큰 소리로 청하였다."], ["After what had passed, Mark could not bring himself to say that he would pay nothing till the bills were safe; and therefore with the assistance of Mr.", "지난 일이 있고 나서 마크는 어음이 안전해지기까지는 한 푼도 내지 않겠다고 말할 수가 없었다."], ["Forrest of the Bank, he did remit the price of Dandy to his friend Sowerby in London.", "그리하여 은행의 포레스트 씨의 도움을 받아, 그는 댄디의 값을 런던에 있는 벗 사워비에게 실제로 송금하였다."]], [["And Lucy Robarts—we must now say a word of her.", "그리고 루시 로바츠 — 이제 그녀에 관해 한마디 하지 않을 수 없다."], ["We have seen how, on that occasion, when the world was at her feet, she had sent her noble suitor away, not only dismissed, but so dismissed that he might be taught never again to offer to her the sweet incense of his vows.", "세상이 그녀의 발 앞에 놓여 있던 그날, 그녀가 그 고귀한 구혼자를 어떻게 돌려보냈는지 우리는 보았다. 그저 물린 것이 아니라, 다시는 그녀에게 맹세의 그 달콤한 향을 바치려 들지 않도록 가르침을 받을 만한 방식으로 물린 것이었다."], ["She had declared to him plainly that she did not love him and could not love him, and had thus thrown away not only riches and honour and high station, but more than that—much worse than that—she had flung away from her the lover to whose love her warm heart clung.", "그녀는 그를 사랑하지 않으며 사랑할 수도 없다고 그에게 딱 잘라 말하였고, 그리하여 부와 명예와 높은 지위만 내던진 것이 아니라, 그보다 더한 것을 — 그보다 훨씬 나쁜 것을 — 곧 그 사랑에 제 뜨거운 마음이 매달려 있는 그 연인을 제게서 떨쳐 버린 것이었다."], ["That her love did cling to him, she knew even then, and owned more thoroughly as soon as he was gone.", "자기의 사랑이 그에게 매달려 있다는 것을 그녀는 그때에도 알고 있었고, 그가 떠나가자마자 더욱 온전히 인정하였다."], ["So much her pride had done for her, and that strong resolve that Lady Lufton should not scowl on her and tell her that she had entrapped her son.", "그녀의 자존심이 그만큼을 해낸 것이었고, 레이디 러프턴이 자기를 노려보며 아들을 덫에 걸었다고 말하게 하지는 않겠다는 그 굳은 결심이 그만큼을 해낸 것이었다."]], [["I know it will be said of Lord Lufton himself that, putting aside his peerage and broad acres, and handsome, sonsy face, he was not worth a girl’s care and love.", "러프턴 경 그 사람을 두고, 귀족 지위와 넓은 땅과 잘생기고 복스러운 얼굴을 젖혀 놓으면 그는 처녀의 마음과 사랑을 받을 만한 값이 없었다고들 할 것을 나는 안다."], ["That will be said because people think that heroes in books should be so much better than heroes got up for the world’s common wear and tear.", "그런 말이 나오는 것은, 책 속의 주인공은 세상의 흔한 살림살이에 쓰이도록 마련된 주인공들보다 훨씬 나아야 한다고 사람들이 여기기 때문이다."], ["I may as well confess that of absolute, true heroism there was only a moderate admixture in Lord Lufton’s composition; but what would the world come to if none but absolute true heroes were to be thought worthy of women’s love?", "절대적이고 참된 영웅됨이 러프턴 경의 됨됨이에는 어지간한 만큼만 섞여 있었다는 것을 나도 고백해 두는 편이 좋겠다. 그러나 절대적이고 참된 영웅만이 여인의 사랑을 받을 만하다고 여겨진다면 세상이 어찌 되겠는가?"], ["What would the men do?", "남자들은 무엇을 하겠는가?"], ["and what—oh!", "그리고 — 아!"], ["what would become of the women?", "여자들은 어찌 되겠는가? 루시 로바츠도 마음속으로 그 물린 연인에게 그에게 참으로 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 영웅됨을 인정해 주지는 않았다."], ["Lucy Robarts in her heart did not give her dismissed lover credit for much more heroism than did truly appertain to him;—did not, perhaps, give him full credit for a certain amount of heroism which did really appertain to him; but, nevertheless, she would have been very glad to take him could she have done so without wounding her pride.", "어쩌면 그에게 실제로 있는 어느 만큼의 영웅됨조차 온전히 인정해 주지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그럼에도 그녀는, 자존심을 다치지 않고 그럴 수 있었더라면 그를 매우 기꺼이 받아들였을 것이다."]], [["That girls should not marry for money we are all agreed.", "처녀가 돈을 보고 시집가서는 안 된다는 데에 우리 모두는 뜻을 같이한다."], ["A lady who can sell herself for a title or an estate, for an income or a set of family diamonds, treats herself as a farmer treats his sheep and oxen—makes hardly more of herself, of her own inner self, in which are comprised a mind and soul, than the poor wretch of her own sex who earns her bread in the lowest stage of degradation.", "작위나 땅을 얻으려고, 수입이나 집안의 다이아몬드 한 벌을 얻으려고 제 몸을 팔 수 있는 숙녀는 농부가 제 양과 소를 다루듯 스스로를 다루는 것이니, 마음과 넋이 들어 있는 저의 안쪽 자아를, 제 동성 가운데 가장 낮은 타락의 자리에서 밥을 버는 저 딱한 여자보다 별로 더 값나가게 여기지 않는 것이다."], ["But a title, and an estate, and an income, are matters which will weigh in the balance with all Eve’s daughters—as they do with all Adam’s sons.", "그러나 작위와 땅과 수입은 하와의 모든 딸들의 저울에서 무게를 지니는 것들이다 — 아담의 모든 아들들에게 그러한 것과 마찬가지로."], ["Pride of place, and the power of living well in front of the world’s eye, are dear to us all;—are, doubtless, intended to be dear.", "자리의 자랑과, 세상의 눈앞에서 넉넉히 살아가는 힘은 우리 모두에게 소중하다. 소중하도록 마련된 것임에 틀림없다."], ["Only in acknowledging so much, let us remember that there are prices at which these good things may be too costly.", "다만 그만큼을 인정하면서 이것도 기억해 두자. 그 좋은 것들이 너무 값비싸질 수 있는 값이 있다는 것을."], ["Therefore, being desirous, too, of telling the truth in this matter, I must confess that Lucy did speculate with some regret on what it would have been to be Lady Lufton.", "그러므로 이 일에 관해서도 참을 말하고자 하는 터이니, 나는 고백하지 않을 수 없다. 루시가 레이디 러프턴이 된다는 것은 어떤 것이었을까를 얼마간 아쉬움을 품고 헤아려 보았다는 것을."], ["To have been the wife of such a man, the owner of such a heart, the mistress of such a destiny—what more or what better could the world have done for her?", "그런 사내의 아내가 되고, 그런 마음의 주인이 되고, 그런 운명의 안주인이 된다는 것 — 세상이 그녀에게 그보다 더한 것을, 그보다 나은 것을 무엇을 해 줄 수 있었겠는가?"], ["And now she had thrown all that aside because she would not endure that Lady Lufton should call her a scheming, artful girl!", "그런데 이제 그녀는 그 모든 것을 내던져 버렸다. 레이디 러프턴이 자기를 꾀바르고 잔꾀 많은 처녀라 부르는 것을 견딜 수 없었기 때문이다!"], ["Actuated by that fear she had repulsed him with a falsehood, though the matter was one on which it was so terribly expedient that she should tell the truth.", "그 두려움에 밀려 그녀는 거짓말로 그를 밀어냈다. 참을 말하는 것이 그토록 무섭게 마땅한 일이었던 그 문제에서 말이다."]], [["And yet she was cheerful with her brother and sister-in-law.", "그러면서도 그녀는 오라비와 올케 앞에서 밝았다."], ["It was when she was quite alone, at night in her own room, or in her solitary walks, that a single silent tear would gather in the corner of her eye and gradually moisten her eyelids.", "아주 혼자일 때, 밤중에 제 방에 있을 때나 홀로 걷는 길에서만, 소리 없는 눈물 한 방울이 눈초리에 맺혀 서서히 눈시울을 적셨다. “그녀는 제 사랑을 결코 말하지 않았다.”"], ["“She never told her love,” nor did she allow concealment to “feed on her damask cheek.”", "그렇다고 숨김이 “그 다마스크빛 뺨을 먹어 들어가도록”"], ["In all her employments, in her ways about the house, and her accustomed quiet mirth, she was the same as ever.", "내버려 두지도 않았다. 하는 일마다에서, 집안을 다니는 걸음걸이에서, 여느 때의 조용한 웃음기에서 그녀는 예전과 다름없었다."], ["In this she showed the peculiar strength which God had given her.", "이 점에서 그녀는 하느님이 그녀에게 주신 남다른 힘을 보였다."], ["But not the less did she in truth mourn for her lost love and spoiled ambition.", "그러나 그렇다고 그녀가 잃어버린 사랑과 망가진 바람을 참으로 슬퍼하지 않은 것은 아니었다."]], [["“We are going to drive over to Hogglestock this morning,”", "“오늘 아침엔 호글스톡까지 마차로 다녀올 거예요,”"], ["Fanny said one day at breakfast.", "어느 날 아침 식사 자리에서 패니가 말했다."], ["“I suppose, Mark, you won’t go with us?”", "“마크, 당신은 우리와 같이 가지 않으시겠지요?”"]], [["“Well, no; I think not.", "“음, 안 가겠어."], ["The pony-carriage is wretched for three.”", "조랑말 마차는 셋이 타기엔 딱하지.”"]], [["“Oh, as for that, I should have thought the new horse might have been able to carry you as far as that.", "“아, 그거야 새 말이 당신을 그만큼은 태워다 줄 수 있으리라 여겼는데요."], ["I heard you say you wanted to see Mr. Crawley.”", "크롤리 씨를 만나고 싶다고 말하시는 걸 들었어요.”"]], [["“So I do; and the new horse, as you call him, shall carry me there to-morrow.", "“그럼, 만나고 싶어. 그리고 당신이 새 말이라 부르는 그 녀석이 내일 나를 거기까지 태워다 줄 거야."], ["Will you say that I’ll be over about twelve o’clock?”", "열두 시쯤 건너가겠다고 말해 주겠어?”"]], [["“You had better say earlier, as he is always out about the parish.”", "“더 이르게 말하는 편이 좋겠어요. 그분은 늘 교구를 돌아 나가 계시니까요.”"]], [["“Very well, say eleven.", "“그럼 좋아, 열한 시라고 해."], ["It is parish business about which I am going, so it need not irk his conscience to stay in for me.”", "내가 가는 것은 교구 일 때문이니, 나 때문에 집에 붙어 있는다 해서 그의 양심에 거슬릴 일도 없겠지.”"]], [["“Well, Lucy, we must drive ourselves, that’s all.", "“그러면 루시, 우리끼리 몰아야겠네요."], ["You shall be charioteer going, and then we’ll change coming back.”", "갈 때는 루시가 마차꾼을 하고, 돌아올 때는 바꾸기로 해요.”"], ["To all which Lucy agreed, and as soon as their work in the school was over they started.", "루시는 그 모두에 동의하였고, 학교에서의 일이 끝나자마자 두 사람은 나섰다."]], [["Not a word had been spoken between them about Lord Lufton since that evening, now more than a month ago, on which they had been walking together in the garden.", "정원을 함께 걸었던 그 저녁, 이제 한 달도 더 지난 그 저녁 이래로 두 사람 사이에서 러프턴 경에 관한 말은 한마디도 나오지 않았다."], ["Lucy had so demeaned herself on that occasion as to make her sister-in-law quite sure that there had been no love passages up to that time; and nothing had since occurred which had created any suspicion in Mrs.", "그때 루시가 처신한 모양이, 그때까지 사랑의 오감이 없었다고 올케가 아주 확신하도록 만들어 놓았다. 그 뒤로 로바츠 부인의 마음에 무슨 의심을 지을 만한 일도 일어나지 않았다."], ["Robarts’ mind. She had seen at once that all the close intimacy between them was over, and thought that everything was as it should be.", "두 사람 사이의 그 가까운 친함이 죄 끝났다는 것은 그녀도 곧 알아보았고, 온갖 것이 마땅히 그러해야 할 대로 되었다고 생각하였다."]], [["“Do you know, I have an idea,”", "“그런데 말이에요, 나는 이런 생각이 들어요,”"], ["she said in the pony-carriage that day, “that Lord Lufton will marry Griselda Grantly.”", "그날 조랑말 마차에서 그녀가 말했다. “러프턴 경이 그리젤다 그랜틀리와 혼인하겠구나 하는 생각이요.”"], ["Lucy could not refrain from giving a little check at the reins which she was holding, and she felt that the blood rushed quickly to her heart.", "루시는 쥐고 있던 고삐를 저도 모르게 살짝 당기지 않을 수 없었고, 피가 심장으로 재게 몰리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["But she did not betray herself.", "그러나 속을 드러내지는 않았다."], ["“Perhaps he may,”", "“그럴지도 모르죠,”"], ["she said, and then gave the pony a little touch with her whip.", "그녀가 말하고는, 채찍으로 조랑말을 살짝 건드렸다."]], [["“Oh, Lucy, I won’t have Puck beaten.", "“아, 루시, 퍽이 매 맞는 건 두고 못 봐요."], ["He was going very nicely.”", "아주 잘 가고 있었잖아요.”"]], [["“I beg Puck’s pardon.", "“퍽에게 용서를 빌어요."], ["But you see when one is trusted with a whip one feels such a longing to use it.”", "하지만 보세요, 채찍을 맡고 있으면 그걸 써 보고 싶은 마음이 그렇게 간절해지는 거예요.”"]], [["“Oh, but you should keep it still.", "“아, 그래도 가만히 들고 있어야 해요."], ["I feel almost certain that Lady Lufton would like such a match.”", "나는 레이디 러프턴께서 그런 혼사를 반기실 거라고 거의 확신해요.”"]], [["“I daresay she might.", "“그러실 만하지요."], ["Miss Grantly will have a large fortune, I believe.”", "그랜틀리 양은 재산을 크게 물려받겠지요, 아마.”"]], [["“It is not that altogether: but she is the sort of young lady that Lady Lufton likes.", "“꼭 그것만은 아니에요. 그 아가씨는 레이디 러프턴께서 좋아하시는 부류의 젊은 숙녀지요."], ["She is ladylike and very beautiful—”", "숙녀답고 아주 아름답고—”"]], [["“Come, Fanny!”", "“어머, 패니!”"]], [["“I really think she is; not what I should call lovely, you know, but very beautiful.", "“정말 그렇다고 생각해요. 내가 사랑스럽다고 부를 만한 쪽은 아니지만, 아주 아름다워요."], ["And then she is quiet and reserved; she does not require excitement, and I am sure is conscientious in the performance of her duties.”", "그리고 조용하고 몸가짐이 조촐하지요. 들뜰 일을 찾지 않고, 제 할 일을 하는 데에는 양심을 다하는 사람일 거예요.”"]], [["“Very conscientious, I have no doubt,”", "“양심을 다하기야 하겠지요, 틀림없이,”"], ["said Lucy, with something like a sneer in her tone.", "루시가 비웃음 같은 것을 어조에 담아 말했다."], ["“But the question, I suppose, is, whether Lord Lufton likes her.”", "“하지만 문제는 러프턴 경이 그 아가씨를 마음에 두는지가 아닐까요.”"]], [["“I think he does,—in a sort of way.", "“마음에 두는 것 같아요, — 어떤 식으로는요."], ["He did not talk to her so much as he did to you—”", "루시에게 말을 걸던 만큼 그 아가씨에게 걸지는 않았지만—”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["that was all Lady Lufton’s fault, because she didn’t have him properly labelled.”", "그건 죄 레이디 러프턴 탓이지요. 그분이 러프턴 경에게 제대로 딱지를 붙여 두지 않으셨으니까요.”"]], [["“There does not seem to have been much harm done?”", "“그래도 큰 해가 난 것 같지는 않은데요?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["by God’s mercy, very little.", "하느님의 자비로 아주 조금이지요."], ["As for me, I shall get over it in three or four years I don’t doubt—that’s if I can get ass’s milk and change of air.”", "나로서는 서너 해 안에 털어 낼 테고요, 그건 의심 없어요 — 나귀 젖을 얻어 마시고 바람을 바꿀 수 있다면 말이지만.”"]], [["“We’ll take you to Barchester for that.", "“그걸 위해서라면 바체스터로 데려가 주겠어요."], ["But as I was saying, I really do think Lord Lufton likes Griselda Grantly.”", "그런데 말했듯이, 나는 러프턴 경이 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 정말 마음에 둔다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“Then I really do think that he has uncommon bad taste,”", "“그렇다면 나는 그분의 취향이 유별나게 나쁘다고 정말로 생각해요,”"], ["said Lucy, with a reality in her voice differing much from the tone of banter she had hitherto used.", "루시가 그때까지 쓰던 농담의 어조와는 크게 다른, 참된 것이 담긴 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“What, Lucy!”", "“어머, 루시!”"], ["said her sister-in-law, looking at her.", "올케가 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Then I fear we shall really want the ass’s milk.”", "“그럼 나귀 젖이 정말로 필요해질까 봐 걱정이네요.”"]], [["“Perhaps, considering my position, I ought to know nothing of Lord Lufton, for you say that it is very dangerous for young ladies to know young gentlemen.", "“내 처지를 생각하면 나는 러프턴 경에 대해 아무것도 몰라야 마땅하겠지요. 젊은 숙녀가 젊은 신사를 아는 것은 아주 위험하다고 패니가 말하니까요."], ["But I do know enough of him to understand that he ought not to like such a girl as Griselda Grantly.", "하지만 나는 그분이 그리젤다 그랜틀리 같은 처녀를 마음에 두어서는 안 된다는 것을 알아볼 만큼은 그분을 알아요."], ["He ought to know that she is a mere automaton, cold, lifeless, spiritless, and even vapid.", "그 아가씨가 한낱 태엽 인형이라는 것, 차갑고 생기 없고 넋이 없고 심지어 맹물 같다는 것을 그분은 알아야 해요."], ["There is, I believe, nothing in her mentally, whatever may be her moral excellences.", "도덕으로야 훌륭한 점이 무엇이든 있겠지만, 머릿속에는 아무것도 없다고 나는 믿어요."], ["To me she is more absolutely like a statue than any other human being I ever saw.", "내가 여태 본 어떤 사람보다도 그 아가씨는 아주 온전히 조각상 같아요."], ["To sit still and be admired is all that she desires; and if she cannot get that, to sit still and not be admired would almost suffice for her.", "가만히 앉아서 우러러 받는 것이 그 아가씨가 바라는 전부지요. 그것을 못 얻는다면, 가만히 앉아서 우러러 받지 않는 것으로도 거의 넉넉할 사람이에요."], ["I do not worship Lady Lufton as you do; but I think quite well enough of her to wonder that she should choose such a girl as that for her son’s wife.", "나는 패니처럼 레이디 러프턴을 우러러 받들지는 않아요. 하지만 그분을 그만큼은 좋게 여기니까, 아들의 아내로 그런 처녀를 고르시는 것이 이상해요."], ["That she does wish it, I do not doubt.", "그분이 그것을 바라신다는 것은 의심하지 않아요."], ["But I shall indeed be surprised if he wishes it also.”", "그러나 러프턴 경도 그것을 바란다면 나는 정말로 놀라겠어요.”"], ["And then as she finished her speech, Lucy again flogged the pony.", "그러고서 말을 마치며 루시는 다시 조랑말에 채찍을 휘둘렀다."], ["This she did in vexation, because she felt that the tell-tale blood had suffused her face.", "이는 속이 상해서 한 짓이었으니, 속을 다 알려 주는 피가 얼굴에 퍼져 오른 것을 느꼈기 때문이었다."]], [["“Why, Lucy, if he were your brother you could not be more eager about it.”", "“어머, 루시, 러프턴 경이 루시의 오라비라 해도 그보다 더 열을 낼 수는 없겠어요.”"]], [["“No, I could not.", "“그래요, 그럴 수 없지요."], ["He is the only man friend with whom I was ever intimate, and I cannot bear to think that he should throw himself away.", "그분은 내가 여태 가까이 지낸 유일한 남자 벗이에요. 그분이 스스로를 내던지는 것을 생각하면 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["It’s horridly improper to care about such a thing, I have no doubt.”", "그런 일에 마음을 쓰는 것이 끔찍하게 온당치 못하다는 건 의심하지 않아요.”"]], [["“I think we might acknowledge that if he and his mother are both satisfied, we may be satisfied also.”", "“그분과 그 어머니가 둘 다 흡족하다면 우리도 흡족해도 된다는 것쯤은 인정해도 좋을 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“I shall not be satisfied.", "“나는 흡족하지 않을 거예요."], ["It’s no use your looking at me, Fanny.", "나를 그렇게 바라봐도 소용없어요, 패니."], ["You will make me talk of it, and I won’t tell a lie on the subject.", "패니가 나더러 이 이야기를 하게 만드니, 나는 이 일에 대해서 거짓말은 하지 않겠어요."], ["I do like Lord Lufton very much; and I do dislike Griselda Grantly almost as much.", "나는 러프턴 경을 정말 아주 좋아해요. 그리고 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 거의 그만큼 정말 싫어해요."], ["Therefore I shall not be satisfied if they become man and wife.", "그러니 그 둘이 부부가 된다면 나는 흡족하지 않을 거예요."], ["However, I do not suppose that either of them will ask my consent; nor is it probable that Lady Lufton will do so.”", "하기야 둘 중 누구도 내 승낙을 구하지는 않겠지요. 레이디 러프턴께서 그러실 법도 없고요.”"], ["And then they went on for perhaps a quarter of a mile without speaking.", "그러고서 두 사람은 아마 사분의 일 마일쯤을 말없이 갔다."]], [["“Poor Puck!”", "“가엾은 퍽!”"], ["at last Lucy said.", "마침내 루시가 말했다."], ["“He shan’t be whipped any more, shall he, because Miss Grantly looks like a statue?", "“그랜틀리 양이 조각상처럼 보인다고 해서 더는 매를 맞지 않을 거야, 그렇지?"], ["And, Fanny, don’t tell Mark to put me into a lunatic asylum.", "그리고 패니, 마크에게 나를 정신 병원에 넣으라고는 하지 말아 주세요."], ["I also know a hawk from a heron, and that’s why I don’t like to see such a very unfitting marriage.”", "나도 매와 왜가리는 가릴 줄 알아요. 그래서 그렇게 몹시 어울리지 않는 혼사를 보고 싶지 않은 거예요.”"], ["There was then nothing more said on the subject, and in two minutes they arrived at the house of the Hogglestock clergyman.", "그 뒤로 그 이야기는 더 나오지 않았고, 두 사람은 이 분 뒤에 호글스톡 성직자의 집에 이르렀다."]], [["Mrs. Crawley had brought two children with her when she came from the Cornish curacy to Hogglestock, and two other babies had been added to her cares since then.", "크롤리 부인은 콘월의 부목사직에서 호글스톡으로 올 때 아이 둘을 데리고 왔고, 그 뒤로 두 아이가 더 그녀의 근심에 보태어졌다."], ["One of these was now ill with croup, and it was with the object of offering to the mother some comfort and solace, that the present visit was made.", "그중 하나가 지금 후두염으로 앓고 있었으니, 그 어머니에게 얼마간의 위로와 도움을 건네고자 이번 방문이 이루어진 것이었다."], ["The two ladies got down from their carriage, having obtained the services of a boy to hold Puck, and soon found themselves in Mrs. Crawley’s single sitting-room.", "두 부인은 퍽을 붙들고 있어 줄 아이 하나를 얻어 놓고 마차에서 내렸고, 이내 크롤리 부인의 단 하나뿐인 거실에 들어섰다."], ["She was sitting there with her foot on the board of a child’s cradle, rocking it, while an infant about three months old was lying in her lap.", "그녀는 아이 요람의 발판에 발을 올려 흔들면서 거기 앉아 있었고, 그 무릎에는 태어난 지 석 달쯤 된 젖먹이가 누워 있었다."], ["For the elder one, who was the sufferer, had in her illness usurped the baby’s place.", "앓고 있는 위의 아이가 병중이라 젖먹이의 자리를 차지해 버린 것이었다."], ["Two other children, considerably older, were also in the room.", "훨씬 위인 다른 두 아이도 그 방에 있었다."], ["The eldest was a girl, perhaps nine years of age, and the other a boy three years her junior.", "맏이는 계집아이로 아홉 살쯤이었고, 다른 하나는 그보다 세 살 아래인 사내아이였다."], ["These were standing at their father’s elbow, who was studiously endeavouring to initiate them in the early mysteries of grammar.", "이 둘은 아버지의 팔꿈치 옆에 서 있었는데, 아버지는 문법의 초기 신비를 그 아이들에게 착실히 들여 넣어 주려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["To tell the truth Mrs. Robarts would much have preferred that Mr.", "사실을 말하자면 로바츠 부인은 크롤리 씨가 그 자리에 없기를 훨씬 더 바랐을 것이다."], ["Crawley had not been there, for she had with her and about her certain contraband articles, presents for the children, as they were to be called, but in truth relief for that poor, much-tasked mother, which they knew it would be impossible to introduce in Mr. Crawley’s presence.", "그녀는 몰래 들여올 물건들을 몸에 지니고 또 곁에 두고 왔는데, 아이들에게 주는 선물이라 부르기로 한 것이었으나 실은 그 가엾고 너무나 부려진 어머니를 덜어 주려는 것이었고, 크롤리 씨가 있는 데서는 그것을 내놓기가 도저히 불가능하다는 것을 그들도 알고 있었다."]], [["She, as we have said, was not quite so gaunt, not altogether so haggard as in the latter of those dreadful Cornish days.", "앞서 말하였듯 그녀는 저 무서웠던 콘월 시절의 뒤끝만큼 그렇게까지 앙상하지도, 그렇게까지 초췌하지도 않았다."], ["Lady Lufton and Mrs. Arabin between them, and the scanty comfort of their improved, though still wretched income, had done something towards bringing her back to the world in which she had lived in the soft days of her childhood.", "레이디 러프턴과 애러빈 부인이 서로 힘을 합한 것과, 나아지기는 하였으나 여전히 딱한 수입이 주는 빈약한 편안함이, 그녀를 어린 시절의 부드러운 날들에 살았던 그 세상으로 얼마간 되돌려 놓는 데에 무언가를 해 놓았다."], ["But even the liberal stipend of a hundred and thirty pounds a-year—liberal according to the scale by which the incomes of clergymen in some of our new districts are now apportioned—would not admit of a gentleman with his wife and four children living with the ordinary comforts of an artisan’s family.", "그러나 연 백삼십 파운드라는 그 넉넉한 급여 — 우리 새 교구들 가운데 어떤 곳에서 성직자들의 수입이 지금 매겨지는 잣대로 보면 넉넉하다 할 급여 — 조차, 아내와 네 아이를 둔 신사가 장인의 집안이 누리는 여느 편안함만큼 사는 것을 허락하지 않았다."], ["As regards the mere eating and drinking, the amounts of butcher’s meat and tea and butter, they of course were used in quantities which any artisan would have regarded as compatible only with demi-starvation.", "그저 먹고 마시는 것만 보아도, 고기며 차며 버터의 양은 어떤 장인이라도 반쯤 굶는 것과나 어울린다고 여길 만한 만큼만 쓰였다."], ["Better clothing for her children was necessary, and better clothing for him.", "아이들에게는 더 나은 옷이 있어야 하였고, 그에게도 더 나은 옷이 있어야 하였다."], ["As for her own raiment, the wives of few artisans would have been content to put up with Mrs. Crawley’s best gown.", "그녀 자신이 걸치는 것으로 말하면, 크롤리 부인의 가장 좋은 겉옷을 참아 낼 장인의 아내는 거의 없었을 것이다."], ["The stuff of which it was made had been paid for by her mother when she with much difficulty bestowed upon her daughter her modest wedding trousseau.", "그 옷을 지은 천값은, 그녀의 어머니가 온갖 어려움을 무릅쓰고 딸에게 조촐한 혼례 옷가지를 마련해 주었을 때에 치른 것이었다."]], [["Lucy had never seen Mrs. Crawley.", "루시는 크롤리 부인을 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["These visits to Hogglestock were not frequent, and had generally been made by Lady Lufton and Mrs. Robarts together.", "호글스톡을 찾는 일은 잦지 않았고, 대개 레이디 러프턴과 로바츠 부인이 함께 다녀왔다."], ["It was known that they were distasteful to Mr. Crawley, who felt a savage satisfaction in being left to himself.", "그것이 크롤리 씨에게 달갑지 않다는 것은 알려져 있었으니, 그는 혼자 내버려지는 데에서 거친 흡족함을 느끼는 사람이었다."], ["It may almost be said of him that he felt angry with those who relieved him, and he had certainly never as yet forgiven the Dean of Barchester for paying his debts.", "그는 자기를 덜어 주는 이들에게 노여움을 느낀다고 해도 거의 옳을 것이며, 빚을 갚아 준 일로 바체스터의 수석 사제를 아직까지 결코 용서하지 않은 것도 분명하였다."], ["The dean had also given him his present living; and consequently his old friend was not now so dear to him as when in old days he would come down to that farm-house, almost as penniless as the curate himself.", "지금의 성직록도 수석 사제가 그에게 준 것이었다. 그러기에 그 오랜 벗은, 옛날에 부목사인 그 자신만큼이나 무일푼으로 그 농가로 내려오던 때만큼 지금은 그에게 소중하지 않았다."], ["Then they would walk together for hours along the rock-bound shore, listening to the waves, discussing deep polemical mysteries, sometimes with hot fury, then again with tender, loving charity, but always with a mutual acknowledgment of each other’s truth.", "그때에는 둘이 몇 시간이고 바위에 둘린 바닷가를 함께 걸으며 물결 소리를 듣고 깊은 논쟁의 신비를 따졌으니, 어떤 때는 뜨거운 노여움으로, 또 어떤 때는 부드럽고 사랑스러운 너그러움으로 그러하였으되, 서로 상대의 참됨을 인정하는 마음만은 언제나 있었다."], ["Now they lived comparatively near together, but no opportunities arose for such discussions. At any rate once a quarter Mr.", "이제 두 사람은 견주어 보면 가까이 살았으나, 그런 따짐을 할 기회는 생기지 않았다."], ["Crawley was pressed by his old friend to visit him at the deanery, and Dr. Arabin had promised that no one else should be in the house if Mr.", "아무려나 크롤리 씨는 석 달에 한 번은 그 오랜 벗에게서 수석 사제관으로 찾아오라는 재촉을 받았고, 애러빈 박사는 크롤리 씨가 사람들과 어울리는 것을 마다한다면 집에 다른 누구도 두지 않겠노라 약조하였다."], ["Crawley objected to society. But this was not what he wanted.", "그러나 그것은 그가 바라는 바가 아니었다."], ["The finery and grandeur of the deanery, and the comfort of that warm, snug library, would silence him at once. Why did not Dr.", "수석 사제관의 화려함과 웅장함, 그리고 그 따뜻하고 안온한 서재의 편안함은 그를 그 자리에서 입 다물게 만들 것이었다."], ["Arabin come out there to Hogglestock, and tramp with him through the dirty lanes as they used to tramp?", "애러빈 박사가 어찌하여 이 호글스톡으로 나와서, 예전에 함께 터벅터벅 걷던 대로 진창 골목을 함께 걸어 주지 않는가? 그렇게만 하였다면 그도 즐거울 수 있었을 것이다."], ["Then he could have enjoyed himself; then he could have talked; then old days would have come back to them.", "그렇게만 하였다면 말도 할 수 있었을 것이다. 그렇게만 하였다면 옛날이 그들에게 되돌아왔을 것이다."], ["But now!—“Arabin always rides on a sleek, fine horse, now-a-days,”", "그러나 이제는! — “애러빈은 요즘 언제나 매끈하고 좋은 말을 타고 다니오,”"], ["he once said to his wife with a sneer.", "언젠가 그가 아내에게 비웃음을 담아 말한 적이 있었다."], ["His poverty had been so terrible to himself that it was not in his heart to love a rich friend.", "그의 가난은 그 자신에게 너무도 무서운 것이었기에, 넉넉한 벗을 사랑할 마음은 그에게 있지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXII. HOGGLESTOCK PARSONAGE.", "# 제22장. 호글스톡 목사관"]], [["At the end of the last chapter, we left Lucy Robarts waiting for an introduction to Mrs.", "앞 장 끝에서 우리는 루시 로바츠를 크롤리 부인에게 소개받기를 기다리는 자리에 두고 나왔다."], ["Crawley, who was sitting with one baby in her lap while she was rocking another who lay in a cradle at her feet.", "크롤리 부인은 젖먹이 하나를 무릎에 앉힌 채, 발밑 요람에 누운 또 하나를 흔들어 재우며 앉아 있었다."], ["Mr. Crawley, in the meanwhile, had risen from his seat with his finger between the leaves of an old grammar out of which he had been teaching his two elder children.", "그러는 동안 크롤리 씨는 낡은 문법책 낱장 사이에 손가락을 끼운 채 자리에서 일어났는데, 그 책으로 위의 두 아이를 가르치던 중이었다."], ["The whole Crawley family was thus before them when Mrs. Robarts and Lucy entered the sitting-room.", "로바츠 부인과 루시가 거실에 들어섰을 때 크롤리 집안 사람들이 이렇게 모두 그들 앞에 있었다."]], [["“This is my sister-in-law, Lucy,”", "“이쪽이 제 시누이 루시예요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."], ["“Pray don’t move now, Mrs.", "“크롤리 부인, 지금은 부디 움직이지 마세요."], ["Crawley; or if you do, let me take baby.”", "아니면 움직이시려거든 아기는 제가 안을게요.” 그러고서 그녀는 팔을 내밀어 젖먹이를 받아 안았고, 아기는 그 품에서 아주 편안해했다."], ["And she put out her arms and took the infant into them, making him quite at home there; for she had work of this kind of her own, at home, which she by no means neglected, though the attendance of nurses was more plentiful with her than at Hogglestock.", "그녀에게도 집에 이런 일거리가 제 몫으로 있었고 결코 소홀히 하지도 않았으니 말이다. 다만 유모의 손길이야 호글스톡보다 넉넉하기는 하였다."]], [["Mrs. Crawley did get up, and told Lucy that she was glad to see her, and Mr.", "크롤리 부인은 그래도 일어나서 루시에게 만나서 반갑다고 말했고, 크롤리 씨는 문법책을 손에 든 채 겸손하고 온순한 낯으로 앞으로 나왔다."], ["Crawley came forward, grammar in hand, looking humble and meek. Could we have looked into the innermost spirit of him and his life’s partner, we should have seen that mixed with the pride of his poverty there was some feeling of disgrace that he was poor, but that with her, regarding this matter, there was neither pride nor shame.", "우리가 그와 그의 반평생 동반자의 가장 깊은 속내를 들여다볼 수 있었다면, 그에게는 가난의 자랑에 가난하다는 수치의 감정이 섞여 있으나 그녀에게는 이 문제에 관하여 자랑도 부끄러움도 없다는 것을 보았을 것이다."], ["The realities of life had become so stern to her that the outward aspects of them were as nothing.", "삶의 실상이 그녀에게 하도 준엄해진 까닭에 그 겉모양 따위는 아무것도 아니었다."], ["She would have liked a new gown because it would have been useful; but it would have been nothing to her if all the county knew that the one in which she went to church had been turned three times.", "새 가운이 있으면 쓸모가 있으니 좋기야 하겠지만, 교회에 입고 가는 그 옷을 세 번이나 뒤집어 고쳐 지었다는 것을 온 주가 다 안다 해도 그녀에게는 아무렇지도 않을 일이었다."], ["It galled him, however, to think that he and his were so poorly dressed.", "그러나 그에게는 자기와 제 식구가 그토록 헐한 옷을 걸치고 있다는 생각이 쓰라렸다."]], [["“I am afraid you can hardly find a chair, Miss Robarts,”", "“로바츠 양, 앉을 의자조차 찾기 어려우실 듯합니다,”"], ["said Mr. Crawley.", "크롤리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes; there is nothing here but this young gentleman’s library,”", "“아니에요, 찾을 수 있어요. 여기 있는 것이라야 이 도련님의 서고밖에 없으니까요,”"], ["said Lucy, moving a pile of ragged, coverless books on to the table.", "루시가 표지도 없는 해진 책 한 무더기를 탁자 위로 옮기며 말했다."], ["“I hope he’ll forgive me for moving them.”", "“옮긴 것을 이 도련님이 용서해 주면 좋겠네요.”"]], [["“They are not Bob’s,—at least, not the most of them,—but mine,”", "“그건 밥 것이 아니에요. 적어도 대부분은요."], ["said the girl.", "제 거예요,” 여자아이가 말했다."]], [["“But some of them are mine,”", "“그래도 몇 권은 내 거야,”"], ["said the boy; “ain’t they, Grace?”", "남자아이가 말했다. “그렇지, 그레이스?”"]], [["“And are you a great scholar?”", "“그럼 너는 대단한 학자로구나?”"], ["asked Lucy, drawing the child to her.", "루시가 아이를 제 쪽으로 끌어당기며 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know,”", "“모르겠어요,”"], ["said Grace, with a sheepish face.", "그레이스가 쑥스러운 낯으로 말했다."], ["“I am in Greek Delectus and the irregular verbs.”", "“저는 그리스어 독본하고 불규칙 동사를 해요.”"]], [["“Greek Delectus and the irregular verbs!”", "“그리스어 독본에 불규칙 동사라니!”"], ["And Lucy put up her hands with astonishment.", "그러고서 루시는 놀라 두 손을 들어 보였다."]], [["“And she knows an ode of Horace all by heart,”", "“그리고 그레이스는 호라티우스 송가 하나를 통째로 외워요,”"], ["said Bob.", "밥이 말했다."]], [["“An ode of Horace!”", "“호라티우스 송가를!”"], ["said Lucy, still holding the young shamefaced female prodigy close to her knees.", "루시는 부끄럼 타는 그 어린 여자 신동을 여전히 제 무릎께에 꼭 붙여 안은 채 말했다."]], [["“It is all that I can give them,” said Mr.", "“그것이 제가 아이들에게 줄 수 있는 전부입니다,”"], ["Crawley, apologetically.", "크롤리 씨가 변명하듯 말했다."], ["“A little scholarship is the only fortune that has come in my way, and I endeavour to share that with my children.”", "“약간의 학식이 제 몫으로 돌아온 유일한 재산이니, 그것이나마 자식들과 나누어 가지려 애쓰는 것입니다.”"]], [["“I believe men say that it is the best fortune any of us can have,”", "“사람들이 그것이야말로 우리가 가질 수 있는 가장 좋은 재산이라고 하지요,”"], ["said Lucy, thinking, however, in her own mind, that Horace and the irregular Greek verbs savoured too much of precocious forcing in a young lady of nine years old.", "루시는 그렇게 말했으나, 속으로는 호라티우스니 그리스어 불규칙 동사니 하는 것이 아홉 살 난 아가씨에게는 지나치게 이른 억지 공부 냄새가 난다고 생각했다."], ["But, nevertheless, Grace was a pretty, simple-looking girl, and clung to her ally closely, and seemed to like being fondled.", "그러나 그럼에도 그레이스는 예쁘고 순박해 보이는 아이였고, 제 편이 되어 준 사람에게 꼭 매달렸으며, 어루만져 주는 것을 좋아하는 듯했다."], ["So that Lucy anxiously wished that Mr. Crawley could be got rid of and the presents produced.", "그래서 루시는 크롤리 씨만 어서 치워지고 선물들을 꺼낼 수 있게 되기를 안절부절 바랐다."]], [["“I hope you have left Mr.", "“로바츠 씨는 별고 없으시겠지요,”"], ["Robarts quite well,” said Mr.", "크롤리 씨가 딱딱하고 격식 차린 목소리로 말했다."], ["Crawley, with a stiff, ceremonial voice, differing very much from that in which he had so energetically addressed his brother clergyman when they were alone together in the study at Framley.", "프램리의 서재에서 동료 성직자와 단둘이 있을 때 그토록 힘차게 몰아붙이던 그 목소리와는 아주 달랐다."]], [["“He is quite well, thank you.", "“네, 아주 잘 있어요, 고맙습니다."], ["I suppose you have heard of his good fortune?”", "그 좋은 일은 들으셨겠지요?”"]], [["“Yes; I have heard of it,”", "“예, 들었습니다,”"], ["said Mr. Crawley, gravely.", "크롤리 씨가 침중하게 말했다."], ["“I hope that his promotion may tend in every way to his advantage here and hereafter.”", "“그 승진이 이승에서나 저승에서나 모든 면으로 그에게 유익이 되기를 바랍니다.”"]], [["It seemed, however, to be manifest from the manner in which he expressed his kind wishes, that his hopes and expectations did not go hand-in-hand together.", "그러나 그가 그 호의로운 축원을 내놓는 태도로 보아, 그의 바람과 그의 예상이 서로 손을 맞잡고 가지는 않는다는 것이 뻔히 드러나는 듯했다."]], [["“By-the-by, he desired us to say that he will call here to-morrow; at about eleven, didn’t he say, Fanny?”", "“그런데 마크가 내일 여기 들르겠다고 전해 달라 했어요. 열한 시쯤이라고 했지요, 패니?”"]], [["“Yes; he wishes to see you about some parish business, I think,” said Mrs.", "“네."], ["Robarts, looking up for a moment from the anxious discussion in which she was already engaged with Mrs.", "교구 일로 뵙고 싶어 하는 것 같아요,”"], ["Crawley on nursery matters.", "로바츠 부인이, 벌써 크롤리 부인과 아이 기르는 이야기로 열심히 주고받던 이야기에서 잠깐 눈을 들며 말했다."]], [["“Pray tell him,”", "“부디 이렇게 전해 주십시오,”"], ["said Mr.", "크롤리 씨가 말했다."], ["Crawley, “that I shall be happy to see him; though, perhaps, now that new duties have been thrown upon him, it will be better that I should visit him at Framley.”", "“기꺼이 뵙겠다고요. 다만 이제 새 직무가 그에게 얹혔으니, 아마 제가 프램리로 찾아가는 편이 나을 것입니다.”"]], [["“His new duties do not disturb him much as yet,”", "“새 직무가 아직은 그이를 그리 어지럽히지 않아요,”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."], ["“And his riding over here will be no trouble to him.”", "“그리고 여기까지 말을 타고 오는 것쯤은 그이에게 수고랄 것도 없어요.”"]], [["“Yes; there he has the advantage over me.", "“예. 그 점에서는 그가 저보다 낫습니다."], ["I unfortunately have no horse.”", "저는 불행히도 말이 없으니까요.”"]], [["And then Lucy began petting the little boy, and by degrees slipped a small bag of gingerbread-nuts out of her muff into his hands.", "그러고서 루시는 어린 사내아이를 어루만지기 시작했고, 슬금슬금 머프 속에서 생강 과자알 한 봉지를 꺼내어 아이의 손에 밀어 넣었다."], ["She had not the patience necessary for waiting, as had her sister-in-law.", "그녀에게는 올케만큼 기다리는 데 필요한 참을성이 없었다."]], [["The boy took the bag, peeped into it, and then looked up into her face.", "사내아이는 봉지를 받아 안을 살짝 들여다보고는, 고개를 들어 루시의 얼굴을 보았다."]], [["“What is that, Bob?”", "“그게 무엇이냐, 밥?”"], ["said Mr. Crawley.", "크롤리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Gingerbread,”", "“생강 과자예요,”"], ["faltered Bobby, feeling that a sin had been committed, though, probably, feeling also that he himself could hardly as yet be accounted as deeply guilty.", "보비가 더듬거리며 말했다. 무슨 죄가 저질러졌다는 느낌이 들었으나, 아마도 자기 자신이 아직 그 죄를 깊이 진 것으로 셈해질 수는 없다는 느낌도 들었던 것이다."]], [["“Miss Robarts,”", "“로바츠 양,” 아버지가 말했다."], ["said the father, “we are very much obliged to you; but our children are hardly used to such things.”", "“대단히 고맙습니다. 그러나 저희 아이들은 그런 것에 익숙하지 못합니다.”"]], [["“I am a lady with a weak mind, Mr.", "“저는 마음이 약한 여자예요, 크롤리 씨."], ["Crawley, and always carry things of this sort about with me when I go to visit children; so you must forgive me, and allow your little boy to accept them.”", "아이들을 보러 갈 때면 늘 이런 것을 지니고 다니지요. 그러니 용서해 주시고, 이 어린 아드님이 받게 해 주셔야 해요.”"]], [["“Oh, certainly.", "“아, 물론입니다."], ["Bob, my child, give the bag to your mamma, and she will let you and Grace have them, one at a time.”", "밥, 아가, 그 봉지를 어머니께 갖다 드리렴. 어머니가 너와 그레이스에게 한 번에 하나씩 주실 것이다.”"], ["And then the bag in a solemn manner was carried over to their mother, who, taking it from her son’s hands, laid it high on a bookshelf.", "그리하여 봉지는 엄숙한 태도로 아이들의 어머니에게 옮겨졌고, 어머니는 그것을 아들의 손에서 받아 책꽂이 높은 곳에 올려놓았다."]], [["“And not one now?”", "“지금 하나도 안 되나요?”"], ["said Lucy Robarts, very piteously.", "루시 로바츠가 몹시 애처롭게 말했다."], ["“Don’t be so hard, Mr.", "“그렇게 야박하게 굴지 마세요, 크롤리 씨."], ["Crawley,—not upon them, but upon me.", "아이들에게가 아니라 저에게요."], ["May I not learn whether they are good of their kind?”", "그것이 그 나름으로 맛이 좋은지 알아볼 수도 없나요?”"]], [["“I am sure they are very good; but I think their mamma will prefer their being put by for the present.”", "“아주 좋은 것일 줄 압니다. 그러나 아이들 어머니는 지금은 두어 두는 편을 원할 것 같습니다.”"]], [["This was very discouraging to Lucy.", "이것은 루시에게 몹시 낙담스러운 일이었다."], ["If one small bag of gingerbread-nuts created so great a difficulty, how was she to dispose of the pot of guava jelly and box of bonbons, which were still in her muff; or how distribute the packet of oranges with which the pony-carriage was laden?", "생강 과자알 작은 봉지 하나가 이토록 큰 난관을 만들어 낸다면, 아직 머프 속에 들어 있는 구아바 젤리 단지와 봉봉 한 상자는 어떻게 처리할 것인가. 조랑말 마차에 실려 있는 오렌지 꾸러미는 또 어떻게 나눌 것인가."], ["And there was jelly for the sick child, and chicken broth, which was, indeed, another jelly; and, to tell the truth openly, there was also a joint of fresh pork and a basket of eggs from the Framley Parsonage farmyard, which Mrs.", "게다가 앓는 아이에게 줄 젤리가 있었고, 닭 국물도 있었는데 이것 또한 실상은 또 하나의 젤리였다. 그리고 숨김없이 털어놓자면, 프램리 목사관 농막에서 가져온 생돼지고기 한 덩이와 달걀 한 바구니도 있었다."], ["Robarts was to introduce, should she find herself capable of doing so; but which would certainly be cast out with utter scorn by Mr. Crawley, if tendered in his immediate presence.", "로바츠 부인이 스스로 그럴 만한 힘이 있다고 여겨지면 내놓기로 한 것이었으나, 크롤리 씨의 눈앞에서 곧바로 내밀었다가는 여지없이 멸시받아 내던져질 것이 뻔했다."], ["There had also been a suggestion as to adding two or three bottles of port; but the courage of the ladies had failed them on that head, and the wine was not now added to their difficulties.", "포트 와인 두세 병을 보태자는 말도 나왔지만, 그 대목에서는 두 부인의 용기가 모자랐던 까닭에 술까지 그들의 난관에 보태지지는 않았다."]], [["Lucy found it very difficult to keep up a conversation with Mr. Crawley—the more so, as Mrs.", "루시는 크롤리 씨와 이야기를 이어 가기가 몹시 어렵다고 느꼈다."], ["Robarts and Mrs. Crawley presently withdrew into a bedroom, taking the two younger children with them.", "더구나 로바츠 부인과 크롤리 부인이 곧 아래의 두 아이를 데리고 침실로 물러가 버렸으니 더욱 그러했다."], ["“How unlucky,”", "“어쩌면 이렇게 운이 없을까,”"], ["thought Lucy, “that she has not got my muff with her!”", "루시는 생각했다. “올케가 내 머프를 가지고 가지 않았으니!”"], ["But the muff lay in her lap, ponderous with its rich enclosures.", "그러나 머프는 그 풍성한 내용물로 무겁게 그녀의 무릎 위에 놓여 있었다."]], [["“I suppose you will live in Barchester for a portion of the year now,”", "“이제 한 해의 얼마쯤은 바체스터에서 지내시게 되겠지요,”"], ["said Mr. Crawley.", "크롤리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I really do not know as yet; Mark talks of taking lodgings for his first month’s residence.”", "“아직은 정말 잘 모르겠어요. 마크는 첫 한 달 거주 근무 동안은 하숙을 얻겠다고 말하고 있어요.”"]], [["“But he will have the house, will he not?”", "“그러나 사택이 나오지 않습니까?”"]], [["“Oh, yes; I suppose so.”", "“아, 네. 그렇겠지요.”"]], [["“I fear he will find it interfere with his own parish—with his general utility there: the schools, for instance.”", "“저로서는 그것이 그의 교구 일과, 그가 거기서 두루 쓰이는 일과 어긋나지 않을까 염려됩니다. 예컨대 학교 같은 것 말입니다.”"]], [["“Mark thinks that, as he is so near, he need not be much absent from Framley, even during his residence.", "“마크는 거리가 워낙 가까우니 거주 근무 동안에도 프램리를 오래 비울 필요는 없다고 생각해요."], ["And then Lady Lufton is so good about the schools.”", "그리고 학교 일은 레이디 러프턴이 아주 잘 챙겨 주시고요.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["yes; but Lady Lufton is not a clergyman, Miss Robarts.”", "그렇지요. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 성직자가 아닙니다, 로바츠 양.”"]], [["It was on Lucy’s tongue to say that her ladyship was pretty nearly as bad, but she stopped herself.", "마님도 성직자나 거의 매한가지로 지독하다는 말이 루시의 혀끝까지 올라왔으나, 그녀는 스스로 참았다."]], [["At this moment Providence sent great relief to Miss Robarts in the shape of Mrs.", "이때 섭리가 로바츠 양에게 큰 구원을 보내 주었으니, 그것은 크롤리 부인의 팔이 붉은 잡일 하녀의 모습으로 왔다."], ["Crawley’s red-armed maid-of-all-work, who, walking up to her master, whispered into his ear that he was wanted.", "하녀는 주인에게 걸어와 그를 찾는 사람이 있다고 귀에 속삭였다."], ["It was the time of day at which his attendance was always required in his parish school; and that attendance being so punctually given, those who wanted him looked for him there at this hour, and if he were absent, did not scruple to send for him.", "그가 늘 교구 학교에 나가 있어야 하는 시각이었다. 그리고 그 출석이 어찌나 어김없었던지, 그를 찾는 사람들은 이 시각이면 거기서 그를 찾았고, 그가 없으면 거리낌 없이 사람을 보내어 불러냈다."]], [["“Miss Robarts, I am afraid you must excuse me,”", "“로바츠 양, 미안하지만 양해해 주셔야겠습니다,”"], ["said he, getting up and taking his hat and stick.", "그가 일어나 모자와 지팡이를 집어 들며 말했다."], ["Lucy begged that she might not be at all in the way, and already began to speculate how she might best unload her treasures.", "루시는 제가 조금이라도 방해가 되지 않기를 바란다고 말하고는, 벌써 어떻게 하면 제 보물들을 가장 잘 부려 놓을 수 있을지 궁리하기 시작했다."], ["“Will you make my compliments to Mrs. Robarts, and say that I am sorry to miss the pleasure of wishing her good-bye?", "“로바츠 부인께 안부를 전해 주시고, 작별 인사를 드리는 기쁨을 놓쳐 미안하다고 말해 주시겠습니까?"], ["But I shall probably see her as she passes the school-house.”", "그러나 부인이 학교 앞을 지날 때에는 아마 뵙게 될 것입니다.”"], ["And then, stick in hand, he walked forth, and Lucy fancied that Bobby’s eyes immediately rested on the bag of gingerbread-nuts.", "그러고서 그는 지팡이를 손에 들고 걸어 나갔고, 루시는 보비의 눈이 곧바로 생강 과자알 봉지에 머무는 것을 본 듯했다."]], [["“Bob,”", "“밥,”"], ["said she, almost in a whisper, “do you like sugar-plums?”", "루시가 거의 속삭이듯 말했다. “알사탕 좋아하니?”"]], [["“Very much indeed,”", "“아주 많이요,”"], ["said Bob, with exceeding gravity, and with his eye upon the window to see whether his father had passed.", "밥이 지극히 진중한 태도로, 아버지가 지나갔는지 보려고 창에 눈을 두고서 말했다."]], [["“Then come here,”", "“그럼 이리 오렴,”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."], ["But as she spoke the door again opened, and Mr. Crawley reappeared.", "그러나 그녀가 말하는 사이에 문이 다시 열리며 크롤리 씨가 나타났다."], ["“I have left a book behind me,”", "“책을 두고 갔습니다,”"], ["he said; and, coming back through the room, he took up the well-worn prayer-book which accompanied him in all his wanderings through the parish.", "그가 말하고는 방을 지나 되돌아와, 교구를 두루 돌아다니는 길에 늘 그와 함께하던 낡을 대로 낡은 기도서를 집어 들었다. 보비는 아버지를 보자 몇 걸음 뒤로 물러났고, 그레이스도 그러했다."], ["Bobby, when he saw his father, had retreated a few steps back, as also did Grace, who, to confess the truth, had been attracted by the sound of sugar-plums, in spite of the irregular verbs.", "사실을 털어놓자면 그레이스도 불규칙 동사에 아랑곳없이 알사탕이라는 소리에 끌려 있었던 것이다. 그리고 루시는 머프에서 손을 빼며 죄지은 낯이 되었다."], ["And Lucy withdrew her hand from her muff, and looked guilty.", "그녀는 이 선량한 사람을 속이고 있는 것이 아닌가."], ["Was she not deceiving the good man—nay, teaching his own children to deceive him?", "아니, 그의 자식들에게 아버지를 속이는 법을 가르치고 있는 것이 아닌가?"], ["But there are men made of such stuff that an angel could hardly live with them without some deceit.", "그러나 천사라 해도 속임수를 조금 쓰지 않고는 함께 살기 어려운 재목으로 지어진 사람들이 있는 것이다."]], [["“Papa’s gone now,”", "“아빠 이제 가셨어요,”"], ["whispered Bobby; “I saw him turn round the corner.”", "보비가 속삭였다. “모퉁이 도는 걸 봤어요.”"], ["He, at any rate, had learned his lesson—as it was natural that he should do.", "적어도 이 아이는 배울 것을 배운 것이었다 — 그러는 것이 자연스러운 일이기도 했다."]], [["Some one else, also, had learned that papa was gone; for while Bob and Grace were still counting the big lumps of sugar-candy, each employed the while for inward solace with an inch of barley-sugar, the front-door opened, and a big basket, and a bundle done up in a kitchen-cloth, made surreptitious entrance into the house, and were quickly unpacked by Mrs.", "아빠가 갔다는 것을 알아차린 사람이 또 있었다."], ["Robarts herself on the table in Mrs. Crawley’s bedroom.", "밥과 그레이스가 아직도 큰 사탕 덩이를 세고 있는 동안, 저마다 그동안의 속을 달래느라 보리사탕 한 치씩을 입에 물고 있는 동안, 현관문이 열리며 큰 바구니 하나와 부엌 보에 싸맨 꾸러미 하나가 슬그머니 집 안으로 들어와, 크롤리 부인의 침실 탁자 위에서 로바츠 부인의 손으로 재빨리 풀렸다."]], [["“I did venture to bring them,”", "“그래도 가져오는 것을 감행했어요,”"], ["said Fanny, with a look of shame, “for I know how a sick child occupies the whole house.”", "패니가 부끄러운 낯빛으로 말했다. “앓는 아이가 집을 온통 붙들고 있다는 것을 저도 아니까요.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["my friend,”", "내 벗이여,”"], ["said Mrs. Crawley, taking hold of Mrs.", "크롤리 부인이 로바츠 부인의 팔을 붙잡고 그 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["Robarts’ arm and looking into her face, “that sort of shame is over with me.", "“그런 부끄러움은 저에게는 이미 지나간 일이에요."], ["God has tried us with want, and for my children’s sake I am glad of such relief.”", "하느님이 궁핍으로 저희를 시험하셨으니, 아이들을 위해서라면 이런 도움이 반갑습니다.”"]], [["“But will he be angry?”", "“하지만 그분이 노하지는 않으실까요?”"]], [["“I will manage it. Dear Mrs.", "“제가 잘 해 보겠어요."], ["Robarts, you must not be surprised at him.", "로바츠 부인, 그이를 보고 놀라시면 안 돼요."], ["His lot is sometimes very hard to bear: such things are so much worse for a man than for a woman.”", "그이의 처지는 이따금 견디기가 몹시 어려워요. 그런 일은 여자보다 남자에게 훨씬 더 못 견딜 것이지요.”"]], [["Fanny was not quite prepared to admit this in her own heart, but she made no reply on that head.", "패니는 마음속으로 이것을 선선히 인정할 준비가 되어 있지는 않았으나, 그 대목에 대해서는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["“I am sure I hope we may be able to be of use to you,”", "“저희가 부인께 쓸모가 있게 되기를 정말로 바라요,”"], ["she said, “if you will only look upon me as an old friend, and write to me if you want me.", "그녀가 말했다. “저를 오래된 벗으로만 여겨 주시고, 제가 필요하시면 편지를 주세요."], ["I hesitate to come frequently for fear that I should offend him.”", "그분의 마음을 거스르지나 않을까 겁이 나서 자주 오기를 망설이는 거예요.”"]], [["And then, by degrees, there was confidence between them, and the poverty-stricken helpmate of the perpetual curate was able to speak of the weight of her burden to the well-to-do young wife of the Barchester prebendary.", "그러고서 차츰 두 사람 사이에 속을 터놓는 마음이 생겼고, 종신 부목사의 가난에 찌든 아내는 바체스터 참사회원의 넉넉한 젊은 아내에게 제 짐의 무게를 말할 수 있게 되었다."], ["“It was hard,”", "“괴로운 일이에요,”"], ["the former said, “to feel herself so different from the wives of other clergymen around her—to know that they lived softly, while she, with all the work of her hands, and unceasing struggle of her energies, could hardly manage to place wholesome food before her husband and children.", "앞의 부인은 그렇게 말했다. 제 주위 다른 성직자들의 아내들과 자기가 그토록 다르다고 느끼는 것이, 또 그들은 편안하게 사는데 자기는 두 손으로 온갖 일을 다 하고 기운을 쉬지 않고 짜내면서도 남편과 자식들 앞에 성한 음식을 놓기가 겨우겨우라는 것을 아는 것이 괴로웠다."], ["It was a terrible thing—a grievous thing to think of, that all the work of her mind should be given up to such subjects as these.", "제 머리가 하는 일 전부가 이런 따위의 문제에 바쳐진다는 것은 무서운 일이요, 생각하기에 애통한 일이었다."], ["But, nevertheless, she could bear it,”", "“그러나 그럼에도 견딜 수 있어요,”"], ["she said, “as long as he would carry himself like a man, and face his lot boldly before the world.”", "그녀가 말했다. 그이가 사내답게 처신하고 세상 앞에서 제 처지를 담대히 마주하기만 한다면."], ["And then she told how he had been better there at Hogglestock than in their former residence down in Cornwall, and in warm language she expressed her thanks to the friend who had done so much for them.", "그러고서 그녀는 그이가 예전에 저 아래 콘월에 살던 곳에서보다 이곳 호글스톡에서 얼마나 나았는지를 이야기하고, 따뜻한 말로 저희에게 그토록 많은 것을 해 준 그 벗에게 감사를 표했다."]], [["“Mrs. Arabin told me that she was so anxious you should go to them,”", "“애러빈 부인이 부인네가 자기들에게 와 주기를 몹시 바란다고 저에게 말했어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Ah, yes; but that I fear is impossible.", "“아, 그래요. 하지만 그것은 안 될 일인 듯해요."], ["The children, you know, Mrs. Robarts.”", "아이들이 있으니까요, 로바츠 부인.”"]], [["“I would take care of two of them for you.”", "“제가 둘은 맡아 돌봐 드릴게요.”"]], [["“Oh, no; I could not punish you for your goodness in that way.", "“아, 아니에요. 그런 식으로 부인의 선의에 벌을 드릴 수는 없어요."], ["But he would not go.", "그렇지만 그이가 가지 않을 거예요."], ["He could go and leave me at home.", "그이만 가고 저는 집에 남을 수도 있지요."], ["Sometimes I have thought that it might be so, and I have done all in my power to persuade him.", "이따금 그렇게 되면 어떨까 생각해 보았고, 그이를 설득하려고 제 힘으로 할 수 있는 것은 다 해 보았어요."], ["I have told him that if he could mix once more with the world, with the clerical world, you know, that he would be better fitted for the performance of his own duties.", "한 번만이라도 다시 세상과, 그러니까 성직자들의 세상과 섞일 수 있다면 자기 직무를 해내기에도 더 알맞아질 것이라고 말해 보았지요."], ["But he answers me angrily, that it is impossible—that his coat is not fit for the dean’s table,” and Mrs.", "그러나 그이는 노여운 낯으로, 그것은 안 될 일이라고, 제 상의가 수석 사제의 식탁에 앉기에 걸맞지 않다고 대답해요.”"], ["Crawley almost blushed as she spoke of such a reason.", "그런 이유를 말하면서 크롤리 부인은 얼굴이 붉어질 지경이었다."]], [["“What!", "“네!"], ["with an old friend like Dr. Arabin?", "애러빈 박사 같은 오래된 벗한테요?"], ["Surely that must be nonsense.”", "그것은 정말 터무니없는 소리예요.”"]], [["“I know that it is.", "“저도 그런 줄 알아요."], ["The dean would be glad to see him with any coat.", "수석 사제님은 어떤 상의를 입고 가도 그이를 반갑게 맞아 주실 거예요."], ["But the fact is that he cannot bear to enter the house of a rich man unless his duty calls him there.”", "그렇지만 실상은, 그이가 직무가 부르지 않는 한 부자의 집에 들어서는 것을 견디지 못한다는 거예요.”"]], [["“But surely that is a mistake?”", "“하지만 그것은 분명 잘못된 생각이지요?”"]], [["“It is a mistake.", "“잘못된 생각이에요."], ["But what can I do?", "그렇지만 제가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["I fear that he regards the rich as his enemies.", "그이는 부자를 제 원수로 여기는 듯해요."], ["He is pining for the solace of some friend to whom he could talk—for some equal, with a mind educated like his own, to whose thoughts he could listen, and to whom he could speak his own thoughts.", "그이는 마음을 놓고 이야기할 벗의 위로를 몹시 그리워하고 있어요. 자기와 같이 닦인 머리를 지닌 대등한 사람, 그 사람의 생각을 듣고 제 생각을 말할 수 있는 사람을요."], ["But such a friend must be equal, not only in mind, but in purse; and where can he ever find such a man as that?”", "그런데 그런 벗은 머리만이 아니라 지갑까지도 대등해야 하니, 그런 사람을 그이가 어디서 찾겠어요?”"]], [["“But you may get better preferment.”", "“하지만 더 나은 성직 자리를 받게 되실 수도 있잖아요.”"]], [["“Ah, no; and if he did, we are hardly fit for it now.", "“아, 아니에요. 그리고 그이가 받는다 해도 저희는 이제 그런 자리에 걸맞지도 않아요."], ["If I could think that I could educate my children; if I could only do something for my poor Grace—”", "제 아이들을 가르칠 수 있겠다고 생각할 수만 있다면, 딱한 우리 그레이스를 위해 무엇이라도 해 줄 수만 있다면—”"]], [["In answer to this Mrs. Robarts said a word or two, but not much.", "이 말에 로바츠 부인은 한두 마디를 했을 뿐 많이 말하지 않았다."], ["She resolved, however, that if she could get her husband’s leave, something should be done for Grace.", "그러나 남편의 허락을 얻을 수 있다면 그레이스를 위해 무엇이든 해야겠다고 마음을 굳혔다."], ["Would it not be a good work?", "그것이 좋은 일이 아니겠는가?"], ["and was it not incumbent on her to make some kindly use of all the goods with which Providence had blessed herself?", "섭리가 자신에게 베풀어 준 온갖 좋은 것들을 어질게 쓰는 것이야말로 자기가 마땅히 해야 할 일이 아니겠는가?"]], [["And then they went back to the sitting-room, each again with a young child in her arms, Mrs.", "그러고서 두 사람은 각기 다시 어린아이를 하나씩 안고 거실로 돌아갔고, 크롤리 부인은 닭 국물과 돼지 다리 고기와 달걀 꾸러미를 부엌에 감추어 두었다."], ["Crawley having stowed away in the kitchen the chicken broth and the leg of pork and the supply of eggs.", "그동안 루시는 아이들과 어울려 있었는데, 결혼한 두 부인이 들어서니 가게가 하나 열려 있어 온갖 사치품이 놀랍도록 헐한 값에 척척 팔리고 사들여지는 중이었다."], ["Lucy had been engaged the while with the children, and when the two married ladies entered, they found that a shop had been opened at which all manner of luxuries were being readily sold and purchased at marvellously easy prices; the guava jelly was there, and the oranges, and the sugar-plums, red and yellow and striped; and, moreover, the gingerbread had been taken down in the audacity of their commercial speculations, and the nuts were spread out upon a board, behind which Lucy stood as shop-girl, disposing of them for kisses.", "구아바 젤리가 거기 있었고, 오렌지도, 붉고 노랗고 줄무늬 든 알사탕도 있었다. 게다가 그 장사 투기의 대담함 속에서 생강 과자까지 내려져 있었고, 과자알들은 널판 위에 펼쳐져 있었으며, 루시는 그 뒤에 가게 처녀로 서서 입맞춤을 받고 그것들을 넘겨주고 있었다."]], [["“Mamma, mamma,”", "“엄마, 엄마,”"], ["said Bobby, running up to his mother, “you must buy something of her,”", "보비가 어머니에게 달려가며 말했다. “엄마도 저 사람한테서 뭘 사야 해요.”"], ["and he pointed with his fingers at the shop-girl.", "그러고서 그는 손가락으로 그 가게 처녀를 가리켰다."], ["“You must give her two kisses for that heap of barley-sugar.”", "“저 보리사탕 무더기는 입맞춤 두 번을 줘야 해요.”"], ["Looking at Bobby’s mouth at the time, one would have said that his kisses might be dispensed with.", "그때 보비의 입을 보았다면, 그 아이의 입맞춤은 없어도 좋겠다고 말했을 것이다."]], [["When they were again in the pony-carriage, behind the impatient Puck, and were well away from the door, Fanny was the first to speak.", "두 사람이 다시 조랑말 마차에 올라 성마른 퍽의 뒤에 앉고, 문에서 넉넉히 멀어졌을 때, 먼저 입을 뗀 것은 패니였다."]], [["“How very different those two are,”", "“그 두 사람은 어쩌면 그렇게 다를까요,”"], ["she said; “different in their minds and in their spirit!”", "그녀가 말했다. “머리도 다르고 마음결도 다르고요!”"]], [["“But how much higher toned is her mind than his!", "“그런데 부인의 머리가 그이의 머리보다 얼마나 더 높은 결인지요!"], ["How weak he is in many things, and how strong she is in everything!", "그이는 많은 일에 그토록 약하고, 부인은 모든 일에 그토록 강해요!"], ["How false is his pride, and how false his shame!”", "그이의 자랑은 얼마나 거짓되고, 그이의 부끄러움은 또 얼마나 거짓된지요!”"]], [["“But we must remember what he has to bear.", "“하지만 그이가 무엇을 견뎌야 하는지도 생각해야 해요."], ["It is not every one that can endure such a life as his without false pride and false shame.”", "그런 삶을 거짓된 자랑도 거짓된 부끄러움도 없이 견뎌 낼 수 있는 사람은 흔하지 않아요.”"]], [["“But she has neither,”", "“그렇지만 부인은 둘 다 없잖아요,”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“Because you have one hero in a family, does that give you a right to expect another?”", "“한 집안에 영웅이 하나 있다고 해서 또 하나를 기대할 권리가 생기나요?”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."], ["“Of all my own acquaintance, Mrs. Crawley, I think, comes nearest to heroism.”", "“내가 아는 사람들 가운데 영웅다움에 가장 가까운 이는 크롤리 부인일 거예요.”"]], [["And then they passed by the Hogglestock school, and Mr. Crawley, when he heard the noise of the wheels, came out.", "그러고서 두 사람은 호글스톡 학교 앞을 지났고, 크롤리 씨는 바퀴 소리를 듣고 밖으로 나왔다."]], [["“You have been very kind,”", "“퍽 친절하셨습니다,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “to remain so long with my poor wife.”", "“제 딱한 아내와 그토록 오래 있어 주셨으니.”"]], [["“We had a great many things to talk about, after you went.”", "“가신 뒤에 이야기할 것이 아주 많았어요.”"]], [["“It is very kind of you, for she does not often see a friend, now-a-days.", "“참으로 고마운 일입니다. 요즘 아내는 벗을 자주 보지 못하니까요."], ["Will you have the goodness to tell Mr. Robarts that I shall be here at the school, at eleven o’clock to-morrow?”", "로바츠 씨에게 제가 내일 열한 시에 이 학교에 있겠다고 전해 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["And then he bowed, taking off his hat to them, and they drove on.", "그러고서 그는 모자를 벗어 두 사람에게 절하였고, 두 사람은 마차를 몰아 나아갔다."]], [["“If he really does care about her comfort, I shall not think so badly of him,”", "“그이가 정말로 부인의 편안함을 마음에 두고 있다면, 그이를 그토록 나쁘게 생각하지는 않겠어요,”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIII. THE TRIUMPH OF THE GIANTS.", "# 제23장. 거인들의 승리"]], [["And now about the end of April news arrived almost simultaneously in all quarters of the habitable globe that was terrible in its import to one of the chief persons of our history;—some may think to the chief person in it.", "그리고 이제 사월이 저물 무렵, 사람이 사는 온 지구의 구석구석에 거의 동시에 소식 하나가 도착했는데, 그것이 담고 있는 뜻은 우리 이야기의 주요 인물 가운데 한 사람에게—어떤 이는 이 이야기의 으뜸 인물이라 여길지도 모를 사람에게—끔찍한 것이었다."], ["All high parliamentary people will doubtless so think, and the wives and daughters of such.", "의회의 높은 자리에 있는 사람들은 모두 틀림없이 그렇게 여길 것이며, 그런 이들의 아내와 딸들도 그럴 것이다."], ["The Titans warring against the gods had been for awhile successful.", "신들을 상대로 싸움을 벌인 티탄들이 한동안 승세를 잡았다."], ["Typhœus and Mimas, Porphyrion and Rhœcus, the giant brood of old, steeped in ignorance and wedded to corruption, had scaled the heights of Olympus, assisted by that audacious flinger of deadly ponderous missiles, who stands ever ready armed with his terrific sling—Supplehouse, the Enceladus of the press.", "티포에우스와 미마스, 포르피리온과 로에쿠스, 무지에 절고 부패와 짝을 맺은 저 옛 거인 족속이 올림포스의 높은 곳까지 기어올랐다. 치명적이고 묵직한 투척물을 겁 없이 던져 대는 자, 그 무서운 무릿매를 늘 손에 들고 있는 자—언론계의 엔켈라도스인 서플하우스가 도운 것이었다."], ["And in this universal cataclasm of the starry councils, what could a poor Diana do, Diana of the Petty Bag, but abandon her pride of place to some rude Orion?", "그리고 별들의 회의가 온통 무너져 내리는 이 판국에, 가련한 디아나, 프티백의 디아나가 무엄한 오리온에게 그 자랑스러운 자리를 내주는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["In other words, the ministry had been compelled to resign, and with them Mr. Harold Smith.", "다시 말해, 내각은 사임을 강요당했고, 그들과 함께 해럴드 스미스 씨도 물러났다."]], [["“And so poor Harold is out, before he has well tasted the sweets of office,”", "“그래서 딱한 해럴드는 관직의 단맛을 제대로 보기도 전에 밀려났습니다,”"], ["said Sowerby, writing to his friend the parson; “and as far as I know, the only piece of Church patronage which has fallen in the way of the ministry since he joined it, has made its way down to Framley—to my great joy and contentment.”", "사워비가 벗인 목사에게 편지를 써 보냈다. “그리고 제가 아는 한, 그가 내각에 들어간 뒤로 그 내각의 손에 떨어진 성직 추천권이라고는 단 하나뿐이었는데, 그것이 프램리까지 내려갔지요—제게는 큰 기쁨이자 만족입니다.”"], ["But it hardly tended to Mark’s joy and contentment on the same subject that he should be so often reminded of the benefit conferred upon him.", "그러나 자기에게 베풀어진 은혜를 그토록 자주 일깨워 받는 것이, 같은 사안을 두고 마크의 기쁨과 만족에 보탬이 되었다고는 도무지 하기 어려웠다."]], [["Terrible was this break-down of the ministry, and especially to Harold Smith, who to the last had had confidence in that theory of new blood.", "이 내각의 붕괴는 끔찍한 일이었고, 마지막까지 저 새 피 이론에 믿음을 두고 있던 해럴드 스미스에게는 더욱 그러했다."], ["He could hardly believe that a large majority of the House should vote against a government which he had only just joined.", "자기가 이제 겨우 합류한 정부에 하원의 큰 다수가 반대표를 던졌다는 것을 그는 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다."], ["“If we are to go on in this way,”", "“이런 식으로 계속 나간다면,”"], ["he said to his young friend Green Walker, “the Queen’s government cannot be carried on.”", "그가 젊은 벗 그린 워커에게 말했다. “여왕의 정부를 운영해 나갈 수가 없소.”"], ["That alleged difficulty as to carrying on the Queen’s government has been frequently mooted in late years since a certain great man first introduced the idea.", "여왕의 정부를 운영해 나가는 일이 어렵다는 저 주장은, 어떤 위대한 인물이 처음 그 생각을 내놓은 뒤로 근년에 자주 거론되어 왔다."], ["Nevertheless, the Queen’s government is carried on, and the propensity and aptitude of men for this work seems to be not at all on the decrease.", "그럼에도 여왕의 정부는 운영되고 있으며, 이 일에 대한 사람들의 성향과 적성은 조금도 줄어드는 것 같지 않다."], ["If we have but few young statesmen, it is because the old stagers are so fond of the rattle of their harness.", "젊은 정치가가 몇 되지 않는다면, 그것은 고참들이 제 마구가 덜컹거리는 소리를 그토록 좋아하기 때문이다."]], [["“I really do not see how the Queen’s government is to be carried on,”", "“여왕의 정부를 어떻게 운영해 나갈지 나는 정말 모르겠소,” 해럴드 스미스가 그린 워커에게 말했다."], ["said Harold Smith to Green Walker, standing in a corner of one of the lobbies of the House of Commons on the first of those days of awful interest, in which the Queen was sending for one crack statesman after another; and some anxious men were beginning to doubt whether or no we should, in truth, be able to obtain the blessing of another cabinet.", "여왕이 일급 정치가를 차례차례 불러들이던, 저 무서울 만큼 흥미로운 날들의 첫날, 두 사람은 하원 로비 한구석에 서 있었다. 그리고 몇몇 근심 많은 이들은 우리가 정말로 또 하나의 내각이라는 축복을 얻을 수 있을지 의심하기 시작하고 있었다."], ["The gods had all vanished from their places.", "신들은 모두 제자리에서 사라졌다."], ["Would the giants be good enough to do anything for us or no?", "거인들이 우리를 위해 무엇이든 해 줄 만큼 친절할 것인가, 아닌가?"], ["There were men who seemed to think that the giants would refuse to do anything for us.", "거인들이 우리를 위해 아무것도 해 주지 않겠다고 거절할 것이라 여기는 듯한 사람들도 있었다."], ["“The House will now be adjourned over till Monday, and I would not be in her Majesty’s shoes for something,”", "“이제 하원은 월요일까지 정회될 것이고, 나로서는 어지간한 것을 준다 해도 여왕 폐하의 처지에 놓이고 싶지 않소,”"], ["said Mr. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“By Jove! no,”", "“맹세코, 나도 싫지요!”"], ["said Green Walker, who in these days was a stanch Harold Smithian, having felt a pride in joining himself on as a substantial support to a cabinet minister.", "그린 워커가 말했다. 그는 요즈음 확고한 해럴드 스미스파였는데, 한 내각 각료에게 실질적인 지지대로 붙어 있다는 데 자부심을 느끼고 있었다."], ["Had he contented himself with being merely a Brockite, he would have counted as nobody.", "그저 브록파에 머무는 데 만족했다면 그는 아무것도 아닌 사람으로 셈되었을 것이다."], ["“By Jove! no,”", "“맹세코, 나도 싫지요!”"], ["and Green Walker opened his eyes and shook his head, as he thought of the perilous condition in which her Majesty must be placed.", "그러면서 그린 워커는 눈을 크게 뜨고 고개를 저었다. 여왕 폐하가 놓여 있을 위태로운 처지를 생각한 것이다."], ["“I happen to know that Lord —— won’t join them unless he has the Foreign Office,”", "“—— 경이 외무부를 얻지 못하면 그들에게 합류하지 않으리라는 것을 나는 우연히 알고 있습니다.”"], ["and he mentioned some hundred-handed Gyas supposed to be of the utmost importance to the counsels of the Titans.", "그러고서 그는 티탄들의 회의에 지극히 중요하다고들 하는, 팔이 백 개나 되는 어떤 기아스의 이름을 댔다."]], [["“And that, of course, is impossible.", "“그리고 그건 물론 불가능하지."], ["I don’t see what on earth they are to do.", "대체 그들이 어떻게 할 셈인지 나는 모르겠소."], ["There’s Sidonia; they do say that he’s making some difficulty now.”", "시도니아도 있지. 그가 지금 무슨 어려움을 일으키고 있다고들 하는군.”"], ["Now Sidonia was another giant, supposed to be very powerful.", "그런데 시도니아는 또 다른 거인이었고, 매우 강력하다고들 여겨졌다."]], [["“We all know that the Queen won’t see him,”", "“여왕께서 그를 만나지 않으실 거라는 건 우리 모두 알지요,”"], ["said Green Walker, who, being a member of Parliament for the Crewe Junction, and nephew to Lady Hartletop, of course had perfectly correct means of ascertaining what the Queen would do, and what she would not.", "그린 워커가 말했다. 그는 크루 정션 선거구의 하원 의원이자 레이디 하틀탑의 조카였으니, 여왕이 무엇을 할 것이고 무엇을 하지 않을 것인지 알아낼 완벽하게 정확한 수단을 물론 가지고 있었다."]], [["“The fact is,”", "“사실은 말이오,”"], ["said Harold Smith, recurring again to his own situation as an ejected god, “that the House does not in the least understand what it is about;—doesn’t know what it wants.", "해럴드 스미스가 축출당한 신의 처지로 다시 돌아와 말했다. “하원은 제가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 조금도 이해하지 못하고 있소."], ["The question I should like to ask them is this: do they intend that the Queen shall have a government, or do they not?", "제가 무엇을 원하는지도 모르지. 내가 그들에게 묻고 싶은 것은 이것이오."], ["Are they prepared to support such men as Sidonia and Lord De Terrier?", "여왕께 정부를 두어 드릴 뜻이 있는가, 없는가? 시도니아나 드 테리어 경 같은 사람들을 지지할 각오가 되어 있는가?"], ["If so, I am their obedient humble servant; but I shall be very much surprised, that’s all.”", "그렇다면 나는 그들의 순종하는 종이오. 다만 나는 몹시 놀라게 될 것이오, 그뿐이지.”"], ["Lord De Terrier was at this time recognized by all men as the leader of the giants.", "드 테리어 경은 이 무렵 모든 사람에게 거인들의 우두머리로 인정받고 있었다."]], [["“And so shall I,—deucedly surprised.", "“저도 그럴 겁니다—지독히 놀랄 테지요."], ["They can’t do it, you know.", "그들은 그럴 수 없어요, 아시겠지만."], ["There are the Manchester men.", "맨체스터 사람들이 있잖습니까."], ["I ought to know something about them down in my country; and I say they can’t support Lord De Terrier.", "내 고향 쪽 일이니 그들에 대해서는 내가 좀 알아야 하지요. 그래서 말하는데, 그들은 드 테리어 경을 지지할 수 없습니다."], ["It wouldn’t be natural.”", "그건 자연스럽지 않을 테니까요.”"]], [["“Natural!", "“자연스럽다니! 인간의 본성은 이제 끝장이 났소,”"], ["Human nature has come to an end, I think,”", "해럴드 스미스가 말했다. 세상이 자기가 합류한 정부를 뒤엎으려고 공모한다는 것을, 게다가 그가 그 정부를 위해 얼마나 해낼지 지켜보기를 기다리지도 않은 채 그런다는 것을 그는 좀처럼 이해할 수 없었다."], ["said Harold Smith, who could hardly understand that the world should conspire to throw over a government which he had joined, and that, too, before the world had waited to see how much he would do for it; “the fact is this, Walker, we have no longer among us any strong feeling of party.”", "“사실은 이렇소, 워커. 우리 사이에는 이제 당파에 대한 강한 정서가 남아 있지 않소.”"]], [["“No, not a d——,”", "“그렇지요, 쥐뿔도 없—”"], ["said Green Walker, who was very energetic in his present political aspirations.", "그린 워커가 말했다. 그는 지금의 정치적 야심에 매우 열심이었다."]], [["“And till we can recover that, we shall never be able to have a government firm-seated and sure-handed.", "“그것을 되찾기 전까지는 우리는 자리가 굳고 손이 확실한 정부를 결코 가질 수 없을 것이오."], ["Nobody can count on men from one week to another.", "아무도 사람을 한 주에서 다음 주까지 믿을 수 없소."], ["The very members who in one month place a minister in power, are the very first to vote against him in the next.”", "어느 달에 한 각료를 권좌에 앉힌 바로 그 의원들이, 다음 달에는 맨 먼저 그에게 반대표를 던지는 것이오.”"]], [["“We must put a stop to that sort of thing, otherwise we shall never do any good.”", "“그런 짓은 막아야 합니다. 그러지 않으면 우리는 결코 아무 좋은 일도 못 할 거예요.”"]], [["“I don’t mean to deny that Brock was wrong with reference to Lord Brittleback.", "“브리틀백 경에 관해서 브록이 틀렸다는 것을 부인할 생각은 없소."], ["I think that he was wrong, and I said so all through.", "나는 그가 틀렸다고 생각하고, 처음부터 끝까지 그렇게 말해 왔지."], ["But, heavens on earth—!”", "그러나 세상에 이럴 수가—!”"], ["and instead of completing his speech Harold Smith turned away his head, and struck his hands together in token of his astonishment at the fatuity of the age.", "그러고서 해럴드 스미스는 말을 마치지 않은 채 고개를 돌리고 두 손을 마주쳤다. 시대의 어리석음에 놀랐다는 표시였다."], ["What he probably meant to express was this: that if such a good deed as that late appointment made at the Petty Bag Office were not held sufficient to atone for that other evil deed to which he had alluded, there would be an end of all justice in sublunary matters.", "그가 아마 나타내려 한 뜻은 이러했다. 얼마 전 프티백 청에서 이루어진 그 임명과 같은 훌륭한 선행이, 그가 넌지시 가리킨 저 다른 악행을 속죄하기에 충분하다고 여겨지지 않는다면, 이 달 아래 세상의 모든 정의는 끝장이라는 것이다."], ["Was no offence to be forgiven, even when so great virtue had been displayed?", "그토록 큰 덕이 드러났는데도 어떤 잘못도 용서받을 수 없다는 말인가?"]], [["“I attribute it all to Supplehouse,”", "“저는 이걸 다 서플하우스 탓으로 돌립니다,”"], ["said Green Walker, trying to console his friend.", "그린 워커가 벗을 위로하려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그렇소,”"], ["said Harold Smith, now verging on the bounds of parliamentary eloquence, although he still spoke with bated breath, and to one solitary hearer.", "해럴드 스미스가 말했다. 이제 그는 의회 연설의 경계에 다다르고 있었으나, 여전히 숨을 죽인 채 단 한 사람의 청중에게 말하고 있었다."], ["“Yes; we are becoming the slaves of a mercenary and irresponsible press—of one single newspaper.", "“그렇소. 우리는 돈에 팔리고 아무 책임도 지지 않는 언론의, 그것도 단 하나의 신문의 노예가 되어 가고 있소."], ["There is a man endowed with no great talent, enjoying no public confidence, untrusted as a politician, and unheard of even as a writer by the world at large, and yet, because he is on the staff of the Jupiter, he is able to overturn the government and throw the whole country into dismay.", "대단한 재주를 타고나지도 못했고, 대중의 신망을 누리지도 못했으며, 정치가로서 믿음을 받지도 못하고, 세상 일반에는 글쟁이로서조차 이름이 알려지지 않은 사람이 하나 있는데, 그런데도 《주피터》의 사원이라는 이유로 정부를 뒤엎고 온 나라를 당혹에 빠뜨릴 수 있는 것이오."], ["It is astonishing to me that a man like Lord Brock should allow himself to be so timid.”", "브록 경 같은 사람이 스스로 그토록 겁을 내도록 내버려 둔다는 것이 나에게는 놀랍기만 하오.”"], ["And nevertheless it was not yet a month since Harold Smith had been counselling with Supplehouse how a series of strong articles in the Jupiter, together with the expected support of the Manchester men, might probably be effective in hurling the minister from his seat.", "그러면서도 해럴드 스미스가 서플하우스와, 《주피터》에 강경한 기사를 잇달아 싣고 여기에 맨체스터 사람들의 지지가 예상대로 더해지면 아마 총리를 그 자리에서 내던지는 데 효험이 있으리라고 의논한 지는 아직 한 달도 되지 않았다."], ["But at that time the minister had not revigorated himself with young blood.", "다만 그때 그 총리는 아직 젊은 피로 제 몸을 다시 북돋우지 않았었다."], ["“How the Queen’s government is to be carried on, that is the question now,”", "“여왕의 정부를 어떻게 운영해 나갈 것인가, 지금은 그것이 문제요,”"], ["Harold Smith repeated.", "해럴드 스미스가 되풀이했다."], ["A difficulty which had not caused him much dismay at that period, about a month since, to which we have alluded.", "우리가 넌지시 가리킨 그 시기, 곧 한 달쯤 전에는 그에게 별로 당혹을 안기지 않았던 어려움이었다."]], [["At this moment Sowerby and Supplehouse together joined them, having come out of the House, in which some unimportant business had been completed after the minister’s notice of adjournment.", "바로 그때 사워비와 서플하우스가 함께 그들에게 합류했다. 총리가 정회를 통고한 뒤 대단찮은 안건 몇 가지가 처리된 하원에서 나온 것이었다."]], [["“Well, Harold,”", "“그런데 해럴드,”"], ["said Sowerby, “what do you say to your governor’s statement?”", "사워비가 말했다. “자네 상관의 성명은 어떻게 보나?”"]], [["“I have nothing to say to it,”", "“나는 그것에 대해 할 말이 없네,”"], ["said Harold Smith, looking up very solemnly from under the penthouse of his hat, and, perhaps, rather savagely.", "해럴드 스미스가 모자의 처마 아래에서 매우 엄숙하게, 그리고 어쩌면 다소 험악하게 올려다보며 말했다."], ["Sowerby had supported the government at the late crisis; but why was he now seen herding with such a one as Supplehouse?", "사워비는 지난 위기 때 정부를 지지했다. 그런데 어째서 그가 지금 서플하우스 같은 자와 어울려 다니는 것이 눈에 띄는가?"]], [["“He did it pretty well, I think,”", "“꽤 잘했다고 나는 보네,”"], ["said Sowerby.", "사워비가 말했다."]], [["“Very well, indeed,”", "“참으로 아주 잘했지,”"], ["said Supplehouse; “as he always does those sort of things.", "서플하우스가 말했다. “그런 종류의 일은 늘 그렇게 해내니까."], ["No man makes so good an explanation of circumstances, or comes out with so telling a personal statement.", "사정 설명을 그만큼 잘하는 사람도 없고, 그만큼 먹히는 개인 성명을 내놓는 사람도 없네."], ["He ought to keep himself in reserve for those sort of things.”", "그는 그런 종류의 일에나 자신을 아껴 두어야 해.”"]], [["“And who in the meantime is to carry on the Queen’s government?”", "“그러면 그동안 여왕의 정부는 누가 운영해 나가지?”"], ["said Harold Smith, looking very stern.", "해럴드 스미스가 매우 험한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["“That should be left to men of lesser mark,”", "“그건 이름이 덜한 사람들에게 맡겨 두어야지,”"], ["said he of the Jupiter.", "《주피터》의 그 사람이 말했다."], ["“The points as to which one really listens to a minister, the subjects about which men really care, are always personal.", "“사람들이 각료의 말을 정말로 귀 기울여 듣는 대목, 사람들이 정말로 마음을 쓰는 주제는 언제나 사사로운 것이네."], ["How many of us are truly interested as to the best mode of governing India?", "인도를 다스리는 최선의 방식에 우리 가운데 몇이 참으로 관심을 두는가?"], ["But in a question touching the character of a prime minister we all muster together like bees round a sounding cymbal.”", "그러나 총리의 인품에 닿는 문제라면 우리는 모두 울리는 꽹과리 주위로 벌떼처럼 모여들지.”"]], [["“That arises from envy, malice, and all uncharitableness,”", "“그것은 시기와 악의와 온갖 무자비함에서 나오는 것이지,”"], ["said Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스가 말했다."]], [["“Yes; and from picking and stealing, evil speaking, lying, and slandering,”", "“그렇지. 그리고 남의 것을 집어 들고 훔치는 짓, 험담과 거짓말과 비방에서도 나오지,”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“We are so prone to desire and covet other men’s places,”", "“우리는 남의 자리를 바라고 탐내는 데 어찌나 쉬 기울어지는지,”"], ["said Supplehouse.", "서플하우스가 말했다."]], [["“Some men are so,”", "“그런 사람도 있지,”"], ["said Sowerby; “but it is the evil speaking, lying, and slandering, which does the mischief.", "사워비가 말했다. “하지만 해악을 끼치는 건 험담과 거짓말과 비방이야."], ["Is it not, Harold?”", "그렇지 않나, 해럴드?”"]], [["“And in the meantime how is the Queen’s government to be carried on?”", "“그런데 그동안 여왕의 정부는 어떻게 운영해 나가지요?”"], ["said Mr. Green Walker.", "그린 워커 씨가 말했다."]], [["On the following morning it was known that Lord De Terrier was with the Queen at Buckingham Palace, and at about twelve a list of the new ministry was published, which must have been in the highest degree satisfactory to the whole brood of giants.", "이튿날 아침 드 테리어 경이 버킹엄 궁에서 여왕을 뵈었다는 것이 알려졌고, 열두 시쯤에는 새 내각의 명단이 발표되었는데, 그것은 거인 족속 전부에게 지극히 만족스러운 것이었음이 틀림없다."], ["Every son of Tellus was included in it, as were also very many of the daughters.", "텔루스의 아들이라면 하나도 빠짐없이 그 안에 들었고, 딸들도 아주 많이 들었다."], ["But then, late in the afternoon, Lord Brock was again summoned to the palace, and it was thought in the West End among the clubs that the gods had again a chance.", "그런데 그날 오후 늦게 브록 경이 다시 궁으로 불려 들어갔고, 웨스트 엔드의 클럽들 사이에서는 신들에게 다시 기회가 왔다는 말이 돌았다."], ["“If only,”", "“다만,”"], ["said the Purist, an evening paper which was supposed to be very much in the interest of Mr.", "해럴드 스미스 씨의 이익을 크게 대변한다고들 하는 석간신문 《퓨리스트》가 말했다."], ["Harold Smith, “if only Lord Brock can have the wisdom to place the right men in the right places.", "“다만 브록 경이 알맞은 사람을 알맞은 자리에 앉힐 지혜를 가질 수만 있다면."], ["It was only the other day that he introduced Mr. Smith into his government.", "그가 스미스 씨를 자기 정부에 끌어들인 것은 바로 요전의 일이었다."], ["That this was a step in the right direction every one has acknowledged, though unfortunately it was made too late to prevent the disturbance which has since occurred.", "이것이 옳은 방향으로 내디딘 걸음이었다는 데는 누구나 동의했으나, 불행히도 그 뒤에 일어난 소란을 막기에는 너무 늦었다."], ["It now appears probable that his lordship will again have an opportunity of selecting a list of statesmen with the view of carrying on the Queen’s government; and it is to be hoped that such men as Mr.", "이제 브록 경에게 여왕의 정부를 운영해 나갈 목적으로 정치가 명단을 고를 기회가 다시 올 듯하다."], ["Smith may be placed in situations in which their talents, industry, and acknowledged official aptitudes, may be of permanent service to the country.”", "그리고 스미스 씨 같은 사람들이 그 재능과 근면과 널리 인정받은 관직 적성으로 나라에 영구히 봉사할 수 있는 자리에 앉혀지기를 바라 마땅하다.”"]], [["Supplehouse, when he read this at the club with Mr. Sowerby at his elbow, declared that the style was too well marked to leave any doubt as to the author; but we ourselves are not inclined to think that Mr.", "서플하우스는 사워비 씨를 팔꿈치 옆에 두고 클럽에서 이 글을 읽으면서, 문체가 너무나 뚜렷해서 지은이에 대해서는 의심의 여지가 없다고 단언했다."], ["Harold Smith wrote the article himself, although it may be probable that he saw it in type.", "그러나 우리 자신은 해럴드 스미스 씨가 그 기사를 직접 썼다고 생각하는 편이 아니다. 활자로 짜인 것을 그가 보았을 개연성은 있겠지만."]], [["But the Jupiter the next morning settled the whole question, and made it known to the world that, in spite of all the sendings and re-sendings, Lord Brock and the gods were permanently out, and Lord De Terrier and the giants permanently in.", "그러나 이튿날 아침 《주피터》가 이 모든 문제를 결말지어, 그토록 사람을 부르고 다시 부른 끝에도 브록 경과 신들은 영구히 밀려났고 드 테리어 경과 거인들은 영구히 들어앉았음을 세상에 알렸다."], ["That fractious giant who would only go to the Foreign Office had, in fact, gone to some sphere of much less important duty, and Sidonia, in spite of the whispered dislike of an illustrious personage, opened the campaign with all the full appanages of a giant of the highest standing.", "외무부가 아니면 가지 않겠다던 그 까탈스러운 거인은 사실 훨씬 덜 중요한 직무의 영역으로 갔고, 시도니아는 어느 고귀한 분이 수군거리며 싫어했음에도 최고 반열 거인의 온갖 부속물을 다 갖추고 출정의 막을 올렸다."], ["“We hope,”", "“우리는 바란다,”"], ["said the Jupiter, “that Lord Brock may not yet be too old to take a lesson.", "《주피터》가 말했다. “브록 경이 아직 한 수 배우기에 너무 늙지는 않았기를."], ["If so, the present decision of the House of Commons, and we may say of the country also, may teach him not to put his trust in such princes as Lord Brittleback, or such broken reeds as Mr. Harold Smith.”", "그렇다면 하원의, 그리고 나라의 것이라 해도 좋을 이번 결정이 브리틀백 경 같은 귀인들이나 해럴드 스미스 씨 같은 부러진 갈대들에게 믿음을 두지 말라고 그를 가르쳐 줄지도 모른다.”"], ["Now this parting blow we always thought to be exceedingly unkind, and altogether unnecessary, on the part of Mr. Supplehouse.", "그런데 서플하우스 씨가 던진 이 작별의 일격은 지나치게 매정하고 전혀 불필요했다고 우리는 늘 생각해 왔다."]], [["“My dear,” said Mrs.", "“이봐요,”"], ["Harold, when she first met Miss Dunstable after the catastrophe was known, “how am I possibly to endure this degradation?”", "그 참사가 알려진 뒤 던스터블 양을 처음 만났을 때 스미스 부인이 말했다. “이 몰락을 내가 대체 어떻게 견디란 말이에요?”"], ["And she put her deeply-laced handkerchief up to her eyes.", "그러고서 그녀는 레이스가 넉넉히 달린 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“Christian resignation,”", "“그리스도인의 체념이지요,”"], ["suggested Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 권했다."]], [["“Fiddlestick!”", "“쓸데없는 소리!”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["“You millionnaires always talk of Christian resignation, because you never are called on to resign anything.", "“당신 같은 백만장자들은 늘 그리스도인의 체념을 입에 올리지요. 무엇 하나 체념하고 내놓으라는 요구를 받아 본 적이 없으니까요."], ["If I had any Christian resignation, I shouldn’t have cared for such pomps and vanities.", "내게 그리스도인의 체념이 조금이라도 있었다면 이런 겉치레와 허영을 마음에 두지도 않았을 거예요."], ["Think of it, my dear; a cabinet minister’s wife for only three weeks!”", "생각해 보세요, 이봐요. 내각 각료의 아내가 겨우 삼 주라니!”"]], [["“How does poor Mr. Smith endure it?”", "“딱한 스미스 씨는 그것을 어떻게 견디고 있어요?”"]], [["“What?", "“뭐요?"], ["Harold?", "해럴드요?"], ["He only lives on the hope of vengeance.", "그이는 복수의 희망만으로 살고 있어요."], ["When he has put an end to Mr. Supplehouse, he will be content to die.”", "서플하우스 씨를 끝장내고 나면 흐뭇하게 죽을 거예요.”"]], [["And then there were further explanations in both Houses of Parliament, which were altogether satisfactory.", "그러고 나서 의회 양원에서 더 자세한 설명이 이어졌는데, 그것은 아주 흡족한 것이었다."], ["The high-bred, courteous giants assured the gods that they had piled Pelion on Ossa and thus climbed up into power, very much in opposition to their own good-wills; for they, the giants themselves, preferred the sweets of dignified retirement.", "잘 자란 정중한 거인들은 신들에게, 자기들이 오사 산 위에 펠리온 산을 쌓아 올려 그렇게 권좌로 기어올라 온 것은 자기들 자신의 선의에 매우 어긋나는 일이었다고 다짐해 말했다. 거인들 자신은 위엄 있는 은퇴의 단맛을 더 좋아했으니 말이다."], ["But the voice of the people had been too strong for them; the effort had been made, not by themselves, but by others, who were determined that the giants should be at the head of affairs.", "그러나 백성의 목소리가 그들에게 너무 강했다. 그 애씀은 자기들이 아니라 다른 이들이 벌인 것이었고, 그들은 거인들이 국사의 머리에 서야 한다고 굳게 마음먹고 있었다."], ["Indeed, the spirit of the times was so clearly in favour of giants that there had been no alternative.", "실로 시대의 정신이 거인들에게 너무나 분명히 기울어 있어서 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 상원에서는 브리아레오스가, 하원에서는 오리온이 그렇게 말했다."], ["So said Briareus to the Lords, and Orion to the Commons.", "그러자 신들은 자기 자리를 내주는 것이 절대로 기쁘다고 했다."], ["And then the gods were absolutely happy in ceding their places; and so far were they from any uncelestial envy or malice which might not be divine, that they promised to give the giants all the assistance in their power in carrying on the work of government; upon which the giants declared how deeply indebted they would be for such valuable counsel and friendly assistance.", "그리고 신답지 않을 수 있는 어떤 하늘답지 못한 시기나 악의로부터도 어찌나 멀었던지, 정부의 일을 운영해 나가는 데 힘닿는 대로 모든 도움을 거인들에게 주겠다고 약속했다. 이에 거인들은 그런 값진 조언과 우호적인 도움에 얼마나 깊이 은혜를 입게 될지 모른다고 선언했다."], ["All this was delightful in the extreme; but not the less did ordinary men seem to expect that the usual battle would go on in the old customary way.", "이 모든 것이 극도로 흐뭇했다. 그러나 그럼에도 보통 사람들은 여느 싸움이 옛날 그대로의 방식으로 계속되리라 여기는 듯했다."], ["It is easy to love one’s enemy when one is making fine speeches; but so difficult to do so in the actual everyday work of life.", "훌륭한 연설을 하고 있을 때 원수를 사랑하기는 쉽다. 그러나 삶의 실제 하루하루의 일 속에서 그러기는 그토록 어렵다."]], [["But there was and always has been this peculiar good point about the giants, that they are never too proud to follow in the footsteps of the gods.", "그러나 거인들에게는 이런 남다른 장점이 있었고 언제나 있어 왔다. 곧 그들은 신들의 발자취를 따라가기를 도도하게 마다하는 일이 결코 없다는 것이다."], ["If the gods, deliberating painfully together, have elaborated any skilful project, the giants are always willing to adopt it as their own, not treating the bantling as a foster-child, but praising it and pushing it so that men should regard it as the undoubted offspring of their own brains.", "신들이 괴롭게 함께 숙의하여 무슨 솜씨 좋은 계획을 다듬어 놓았으면, 거인들은 언제라도 그것을 제 것으로 받아들이려 하는데, 그 어린것을 수양자식처럼 다루지 않고 오히려 칭찬하고 밀어붙여서 사람들이 그것을 의심할 여지 없이 자기네 머리에서 나온 자식으로 여기게 만든다."], ["Now just at this time there had been a plan much thought of for increasing the number of the bishops.", "그런데 바로 이 무렵에 주교의 수를 늘리는 계획이 크게 검토되고 있었다."], ["Good active bishops were very desirable, and there was a strong feeling among certain excellent churchmen that there could hardly be too many of them.", "훌륭하고 부지런한 주교는 매우 바람직했고, 어떤 뛰어난 교회 인사들 사이에서는 그런 주교는 아무리 많아도 지나칠 수 없다는 강한 정서가 있었다."], ["Lord Brock had his measure cut and dry.", "브록 경은 법안을 다 마련해 두고 있었다."], ["There should be a Bishop of Westminster to share the Herculean toils of the metropolitan prelate, and another up in the North to Christianize the mining interests and wash white the blackamoors of Newcastle: Bishop of Beverley he should be called.", "수도 대주교의 헤라클레스 같은 수고를 나눠 질 웨스트민스터 주교를 하나 두고, 북쪽에는 광산업을 기독교화하고 뉴캐슬의 검댕 묻은 무리를 씻어 하얗게 할 주교를 또 하나 두어야 한다는 것이었다. 그 이름은 비벌리 주교라 하기로 했다."], ["But, in opposition to this, the giants, it was known, had intended to put forth the whole measure of their brute force.", "그러나 이에 맞서 거인들은 저들의 야만스러운 힘을 온통 다 쏟아 낼 작정이었다고 알려져 있었다."], ["More curates, they said, were wanting, and district incumbents; not more bishops rolling in carriages.", "그들의 말로는, 더 필요한 것은 부목사와 지구 담당 성직자이지, 마차를 굴리고 다니는 주교가 더 필요한 것은 아니었다."], ["That bishops should roll in carriages was very good; but of such blessings the English world for the present had enough.", "주교가 마차를 굴리고 다니는 것은 매우 좋은 일이다. 그러나 그런 복이라면 지금의 영국 세계는 넉넉히 가지고 있었다."], ["And therefore Lord Brock and the gods had had much fear as to their little project.", "그러므로 브록 경과 신들은 자기들의 그 작은 계획을 크게 걱정하고 있었다."]], [["But now, immediately on the accession of the giants, it was known that the bishop bill was to be gone on with immediately.", "그러나 이제 거인들이 자리에 오르자마자, 주교 법안이 곧바로 추진될 것이라는 사실이 알려졌다."], ["Some small changes would be effected so that the bill should be gigantic rather than divine; but the result would be altogether the same.", "그 법안이 신답기보다 거인답게 되도록 자잘한 손질이 몇 가지 가해지겠으나, 결과는 아주 똑같을 것이었다."], ["It must, however, be admitted that bishops appointed by ourselves may be very good things, whereas those appointed by our adversaries will be anything but good.", "그러나 우리 손으로 임명한 주교는 매우 좋은 것일 수 있지만 우리 원수의 손으로 임명된 주교는 결코 좋을 것이 없다는 점은 인정해야 한다."], ["And, no doubt, this feeling went a long way with the giants.", "그리고 이 정서가 거인들에게 크게 작용했음은 의심의 여지가 없다."], ["Be that as it may, the new bishop bill was to be their first work of government, and it was to be brought forward and carried, and the new prelates selected and put into their chairs all at once,—before the grouse should begin to crow and put an end to the doings of gods as well as giants.", "어쨌든 새 주교 법안은 그들이 정부로서 하는 첫 일이 될 것이었고, 그것을 발의해 통과시키고 새 고위 성직자들을 골라 그 의자에 앉히는 일을 한꺼번에 해치울 것이었다—뇌조가 울기 시작해 거인들뿐 아니라 신들의 소행까지 끝장내 버리기 전에."]], [["Among other minor effects arising from this decision was the following, that Archdeacon and Mrs.", "이 결정에서 생겨난 여러 사소한 결과 가운데는 다음과 같은 것도 있었다."], ["Grantly returned to London, and again took the lodgings in which they had before been staying.", "그랜틀리 부주교와 부인이 런던으로 돌아와, 전에 머물던 하숙을 다시 잡았다는 것이다."], ["On various occasions also during the first week of this second sojourn, Dr. Grantly might be seen entering the official chambers of the First Lord of the Treasury.", "또한 이 두 번째 체류의 첫 주 동안 여러 차례, 그랜틀리 박사가 재무부 제1위원의 집무실로 들어가는 것이 눈에 띄었다."], ["Much counsel was necessary among high churchmen of great repute before any fixed resolution could wisely be made in such a matter as this; and few churchmen stood in higher repute than the Archdeacon of Barchester.", "이런 일에 무엇 하나 확고한 결정을 슬기롭게 내리려면 그 전에 이름 높은 고교회파 인사들 사이에 많은 의논이 필요했다. 그리고 바체스터 부주교보다 더 이름 높이 선 교회 인사는 몇 되지 않았다."], ["And then it began to be rumoured in the world that the minister had disposed at any rate of the see of Westminster.", "그러고 나서, 총리가 적어도 웨스트민스터 주교좌만은 처분했다는 소문이 세상에 돌기 시작했다."]], [["This present time was a very nervous one for Mrs. Grantly.", "지금 이때는 그랜틀리 부인에게 매우 조바심 나는 시절이었다."], ["What might be the aspirations of the archdeacon himself, we will not stop to inquire.", "부주교 자신의 열망이 어떠했을지는 멈춰 서서 캐묻지 않겠다."], ["It may be that time and experience had taught him the futility of earthly honours, and made him content with the comfortable opulence of his Barsetshire rectory.", "세월과 경험이 그에게 이 땅의 명예가 부질없음을 가르쳐, 바셋주 주임 사제관의 안락한 넉넉함으로 만족하게 만들었을지도 모른다."], ["But there is no theory of church discipline which makes it necessary that a clergyman’s wife should have an objection to a bishopric.", "그러나 성직자의 아내가 주교직에 반대해야 한다고 못 박는 교회 규율의 이론 따위는 없다."], ["The archdeacon probably was only anxious to give a disinterested aid to the minister, but Mrs.", "부주교는 아마 총리에게 사심 없는 도움을 주려고 애쓴 것뿐이었겠지만, 그랜틀리 부인은 높은 자리에 앉아 적어도 프라우디 부인과 대등해지기를 정말로 갈망했다."], ["Grantly did long to sit in high places, and be at any rate equal to Mrs. Proudie.", "자기가 이렇게 애가 타는 것은 아이들을 위한 것이라고, 그녀는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["It was for her children, she said to herself, that she was thus anxious,—that they should have a good position before the world, and the means of making the best of themselves.", "아이들이 세상 앞에 좋은 자리를 얻고 저마다 제 몫을 가장 잘 살릴 방편을 갖게 하려는 것이라고. “저 아래 플럼스테드에 갇혀 있으면 아무것도 할 수 없잖아요, 아시겠지만,”"], ["“One is able to do nothing, you know, shut up there, down at Plumstead,”", "그녀는 런던을 처음 찾았을 때 레이디 러프턴에게 그렇게 말했었다."], ["she had remarked to Lady Lufton on the occasion of her first visit to London, and yet the time was not long past when she had thought that rectory house at Plumstead to be by no means insufficient or contemptible.", "그러면서도 플럼스테드의 그 주임 사제관을 결코 부족하거나 하찮은 집이 아니라고 여겼던 때가 그리 오래 지난 것도 아니었다."]], [["And then there came a question whether or no Griselda should go back to her mother; but this idea was very strongly opposed by Lady Lufton, and ultimately with success.", "그러고 나서 그리젤다가 어머니에게 돌아가야 하는가 하는 문제가 나왔다. 그러나 이 생각에는 레이디 러프턴이 매우 강하게 반대했고, 결국 그 반대가 이겼다."], ["“I really think the dear girl is very happy with me,”", "“그 착한 아이가 나와 함께 있어서 아주 행복하다고 나는 정말로 생각해요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton; “and if ever she is to belong to me more closely, it will be so well that we should know and love one another.”", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다. “그리고 언젠가 그 아이가 나에게 더 가까이 속하게 될 것이라면, 우리가 서로 알고 서로 사랑해 두는 것이 무척 좋은 일이지요.”"]], [["To tell the truth, Lady Lufton had been trying hard to know and love Griselda, but hitherto she had scarcely succeeded to the full extent of her wishes.", "사실을 말하면, 레이디 러프턴은 그리젤다를 알고 사랑하려고 애써 노력해 왔으나, 지금까지 자기가 바라는 만큼 다 이루지는 좀처럼 못했다."], ["That she loved Griselda was certain,—with that sort of love which springs from a person’s volition and not from the judgment.", "그녀가 그리젤다를 사랑한다는 것은 분명했다—판단에서가 아니라 사람의 의지에서 솟아나는 그런 종류의 사랑으로."], ["She had said all along to herself and others that she did love Griselda Grantly.", "그녀는 자기 자신에게도 남들에게도 처음부터 줄곧, 자기는 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 사랑한다고 말해 왔다."], ["She had admired the young lady’s face, liked her manner, approved of her fortune and family, and had selected her for a daughter-in-law in a somewhat impetuous manner.", "그 젊은 아가씨의 얼굴을 흠모했고, 몸가짐을 마음에 들어 했고, 재산과 집안을 좋게 여겼으며, 다소 성급한 방식으로 그녀를 며느리로 골라 두었다."], ["Therefore she loved her.", "그러므로 그녀는 그리젤다를 사랑했다."], ["But it was by no means clear to Lady Lufton that she did as yet know her young friend.", "그러나 자기가 이 젊은 벗을 아직 알고 있는지는 레이디 러프턴에게 전혀 분명하지 않았다."], ["The match was a plan of her own, and therefore she stuck to it as warmly as ever, but she began to have some misgivings whether or no the dear girl would be to her herself all that she had dreamed of in a daughter-in-law.", "그 혼사는 자기가 세운 계획이었으므로 그녀는 여전히 따뜻하게 그것을 붙들고 있었으나, 그 착한 아이가 자기 자신에게 며느리로서 꿈꿔 온 모든 것이 되어 줄지에 대해 얼마간 의심이 들기 시작했다."]], [["“But, dear Lady Lufton,” said Mrs.", "“하지만 레이디 러프턴,”"], ["Grantly, “is it not possible that we may put her affections to too severe a test?", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “우리가 그 아이의 마음을 너무 심한 시험에 걸어 두는 것일 수도 있지 않을까요?"], ["What, if she should learn to regard him, and then—”", "만약 그 아이가 그분을 마음에 두게 되었는데, 그러고서—”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["if she did, I should have no fear of the result.", "그렇게 되었다면 나는 그 결과를 조금도 두려워하지 않아요."], ["If she showed anything like love for Ludovic, he would be at her feet in a moment.", "그 아이가 루도빅에게 사랑 같은 것을 조금이라도 내보인다면, 루도빅은 당장 그 발밑에 엎드릴 거예요."], ["He is impulsive, but she is not.”", "그애는 충동적이지만 그 아이는 그렇지 않지요.”"]], [["“Exactly, Lady Lufton.", "“바로 그래요, 레이디 러프턴."], ["It is his privilege to be impulsive and to sue for her affection, and hers to have her love sought for without making any demonstration.", "충동적으로 그 아이의 애정을 구하는 것이 그분의 특권이고, 아무런 감정도 드러내지 않은 채 제 사랑을 구해 받는 것이 그 아이의 특권이지요."], ["It is perhaps the fault of young ladies of the present day that they are too impulsive.", "요즘 젊은 아가씨들의 잘못은 아마 너무 충동적이라는 데 있을 거예요."], ["They assume privileges which are not their own, and thus lose those which are.”", "자기 것이 아닌 특권을 제 것으로 삼으려 들고, 그래서 정작 제 것인 특권을 잃지요.”"]], [["“Quite true!", "“참 옳은 말이에요! 나도 아주 같은 생각이에요."], ["I quite agree with you.", "아마 바로 그 느낌 때문에 내가 그리젤다를 그토록 높이 보게 되었겠지요. 하지만 그렇더라도—”"], ["It is probably that very feeling that has made me think so highly of Griselda. But then—”", "하지만 그렇더라도, 젊은 아가씨가 신사의 목구멍으로 뛰어들거나 그 얼굴에 자기를 내던질 필요는 없다 해도, 자기가 살과 피로 되어 있다는 표시쯤은 좀 내보일 수 있는 것이다."], ["But then a young lady, though she need not jump down a gentleman’s throat, or throw herself into his face, may give some signs that she is made of flesh and blood; especially when her papa and mamma and all belonging to her are so anxious to make the path of her love run smooth.", "더구나 아버지와 어머니와 그에게 딸린 모든 사람이 그 사랑의 길을 매끄럽게 놓아 주려고 그토록 애쓰고 있을 때는 말이다. 그것이 레이디 러프턴의 마음속을 지나간 생각이었다."], ["That was what was passing through Lady Lufton’s mind; but she did not say it all; she merely looked it.", "그러나 그녀는 그것을 다 말하지는 않았다. 그저 눈빛으로 그렇게 말했을 뿐이다."]], [["“I don’t think she will ever allow herself to indulge in an unauthorized passion,”", "“그 아이가 허락받지 않은 열정에 자기를 내주는 일은 결코 없을 거예요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I am sure she will not,”", "“물론 그러지 않겠지요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton, with ready agreement, fearing perhaps in her heart that Griselda would never indulge in any passion, authorized or unauthorized.", "레이디 러프턴이 선선히 동의하며 말했다. 어쩌면 마음속으로는, 그리젤다가 허락받았든 허락받지 않았든 어떤 열정에도 결코 자기를 내주지 않을까 봐 두려워하면서."]], [["“I don’t know whether Lord Lufton sees much of her now,” said Mrs.", "“러프턴 경이 요즘 그 아이를 자주 보는지 나는 모르겠어요,”"], ["Grantly, thinking perhaps of that promise of Lady Lufton’s with reference to his lordship’s spare time.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 아마 나리의 한가한 시간에 관해 레이디 러프턴이 한 그 약속을 생각한 것이었다."]], [["“Just lately, during these changes, you know, everybody has been so much engaged.", "“요 근래에는, 이런 변동들 사이에서, 아시겠지만, 다들 몹시 바빴어요."], ["Ludovic has been constantly at the House, and then men find it so necessary to be at their clubs just now.”", "루도빅은 줄곧 상원에 붙어 있었고, 또 요즘은 사내들이 클럽에 나가 있는 것이 그렇게 필요하다고들 하니까요.”"]], [["“Yes, yes, of course,” said Mrs.", "“네, 네, 물론이지요,”"], ["Grantly, who was not at all disposed to think little of the importance of the present crisis, or to wonder that men should congregate together when such deeds were to be done as those which now occupied the breasts of the Queen’s advisers.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 지금의 위기가 지닌 중대함을 조금이라도 가볍게 볼 마음이 전혀 없었고, 지금 여왕의 고문들 가슴을 채우고 있는 것과 같은 일들이 이루어져야 할 때 사내들이 한데 모이는 것을 이상하게 여길 마음도 없었다."], ["At last, however, the two mothers perfectly understood each other.", "그러나 마침내 두 어머니는 서로를 완전히 이해했다. 그리젤다는 여전히 레이디 러프턴과 함께 지내기로 했다."], ["Griselda was still to remain with Lady Lufton; and was to accept her ladyship’s son, if he could only be induced to exercise his privilege of asking her; but in the meantime, as this seemed to be doubtful, Griselda was not to be debarred from her privilege of making what use she could of any other string which she might have to her bow.", "그리고 마님의 아들이 청혼이라는 제 특권을 행사하도록 어떻게든 끌어낼 수만 있다면 그를 받아들이기로 했다. 그러나 그동안, 이것이 의심스러워 보였으므로, 그리젤다가 제 활에 걸어 두었을지도 모르는 다른 시위를 되도록 써먹을 특권만은 막지 않기로 했다."]], [["“But, mamma,”", "“그런데 어머니,”"], ["said Griselda, in a moment of unwatched intercourse between the mother and daughter, “is it really true that they are going to make papa a bishop?”", "어머니와 딸이 아무도 지켜보지 않는 사이에 이야기를 나누던 한때에 그리젤다가 말했다. “정말로 아버지를 주교로 만든다는 게 사실이에요?”"]], [["“We can tell nothing as yet, my dear.", "“아직은 아무것도 알 수 없단다, 얘."], ["People in the world are talking about it.", "세상 사람들이 그 이야기를 하고 있지."], ["Your papa has been a good deal with Lord De Terrier.”", "네 아버지가 드 테리어 경과 자주 만나고 있어.”"]], [["“And isn’t he prime minister?”", "“그분이 총리 아니에요?”"]], [["“Oh, yes; I am happy to say that he is.”", "“그렇지. 다행히도 그렇단다.”"]], [["“I thought the prime minister could make any one a bishop that he chooses,—any clergyman, that is.”", "“총리는 마음에 드는 사람이면 누구나 주교로 만들 수 있는 줄 알았어요. 성직자라면 누구나요.”"]], [["“But there is no see vacant,”", "“하지만 빈 주교좌가 없어,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Then there isn’t any chance,”", "“그럼 아무 가망이 없네요,”"], ["said Griselda, looking very glum.", "그리젤다가 몹시 시무룩한 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["“They are going to have an Act of Parliament for making two more bishops.", "“주교를 둘 더 만들려고 의회 법률을 두려고 한단다."], ["That’s what they are talking about at least.", "적어도 사람들이 그렇게 이야기하고 있어."], ["And if they do—”", "그리고 만약 그렇게 되면—”"]], [["“Papa will be Bishop of Westminster—won’t he?", "“아버지가 웨스트민스터 주교가 되겠지요?"], ["And we shall live in London?”", "그러면 우리는 런던에서 살게 되나요?”"]], [["“But you must not talk about it, my dear.”", "“하지만 그 이야기는 하고 다니면 안 된다, 얘.”"]], [["“No, I won’t.", "“네, 안 할게요."], ["But, mamma, a Bishop of Westminster will be higher than a Bishop of Barchester; won’t he?", "그런데 어머니, 웨스트민스터 주교는 바체스터 주교보다 높겠지요?"], ["I shall so like to be able to snub those Miss Proudies.”", "저 프라우디 자매를 무시해 줄 수 있으면 정말 좋겠어요.”"], ["It will therefore be seen that there were matters on which even Griselda Grantly could be animated.", "그러니 그리젤다 그랜틀리조차 활기를 띨 수 있는 일이 있었음을 알 수 있을 것이다."], ["Like the rest of her family she was devoted to the Church.", "집안의 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로 그녀도 교회에 헌신적이었다."]], [["Late on that afternoon the archdeacon returned home to dine in Mount Street, having spent the whole of the day between the Treasury chambers, a meeting of Convocation, and his club.", "그날 오후 늦게 부주교는 마운트 스트리트로 저녁을 먹으러 돌아왔다. 하루를 온통 재무부 집무실과 성직자 회의와 자기 클럽 사이에서 보낸 뒤였다."], ["And when he did get home it was soon manifest to his wife that he was not laden with good news.", "그리고 집에 들어서자 곧, 그가 좋은 소식을 안고 온 것이 아니라는 것이 아내에게 뚜렷해졌다."]], [["“It is almost incredible,”", "“거의 믿을 수가 없소,”"], ["he said, standing with his back to the drawing-room fire.", "그가 응접실 난롯불에 등을 대고 서서 말했다."]], [["“What is incredible?”", "“무엇이 믿을 수 없어요?”"], ["said his wife, sharing her husband’s anxiety to the full.", "남편의 조바심을 온전히 함께 나누며 아내가 말했다."]], [["“If I had not learned it as fact, I would not have believed it, even of Lord Brock,”", "“사실로 알아낸 것이 아니라면, 브록 경이 한 일이라 해도 나는 믿지 않았을 것이오,”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."]], [["“Learned what?”", "“무엇을 알아냈다는 거예요?”"], ["said the anxious wife.", "애가 타는 아내가 말했다."]], [["“After all, they are going to oppose the bill.”", "“결국 그들이 그 법안에 반대할 작정이오.”"]], [["“Impossible!”", "“그럴 수가!”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."]], [["“But they are.”", "“하지만 그렇게 하려 하오.”"]], [["“The bill for the two new bishops, archdeacon?", "“새 주교 둘을 두는 그 법안 말이에요, 부주교님?"], ["oppose their own bill!”", "자기들 법안에 반대한다니!”"]], [["“Yes—oppose their own bill.", "“그렇소—자기들 법안에 반대한다는 것이오."], ["It is almost incredible; but so it is.", "거의 믿을 수가 없지만, 사실이 그렇소. 우리에게 몇 가지 변경이 강요되었소."], ["Some changes have been forced upon us; little things which they had forgotten—quite minor matters; and they now say that they will be obliged to divide against us on these twopenny-halfpenny, hair-splitting points.", "그들이 잊고 있던 자잘한 것들, 아주 사소한 사항들이오. 그런데 이제 그들이 말하기를, 이 서푼짜리 머리카락 쪼개기 같은 점들 때문에 우리에 맞서 표결에 들어갈 수밖에 없다는 것이오."], ["It is Lord Brock’s own doing too, after all that he said about abstaining from factious opposition to the government.”", "그것도 브록 경 자신이 하는 짓이오. 정부에 대한 당파적 반대를 삼가겠다고 그가 그토록 말해 놓고서.”"]], [["“I believe there is nothing too bad or too false for that man,”", "“그 사람에게는 너무 나쁘거나 너무 거짓된 일이란 없다고 나는 믿어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."]], [["“After all they said, too, when they were in power themselves, as to the present government opposing the cause of religion!", "“게다가 자기들이 권력을 쥐고 있을 때, 지금의 정부가 종교의 대의에 반대한다고 그토록 말해 놓고서!"], ["They declare now that Lord De Terrier cannot be very anxious about it, as he had so many good reasons against it a few weeks ago.", "이제 그들은, 드 테리어 경이 몇 주 전에는 그 법안에 반대할 훌륭한 이유를 그렇게 많이 갖고 있었으니 그 일에 그다지 애를 태울 리 없다고 떠들고 있소."], ["Is it not dreadful that there should be such double-dealing in men in such positions?”", "그런 자리에 있는 사람들에게 그런 이중 거래가 있다는 것이 끔찍하지 않소?”"]], [["“It is sickening,”", "“속이 메스꺼워요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."]], [["And then there was a pause between them as each thought of the injury that was done to them.", "그러고 나서 두 사람 사이에 침묵이 흘렀다. 저마다 자기들에게 가해진 해코지를 생각한 것이다."]], [["“But, archdeacon—”", "“하지만 부주교님—”"]], [["“Well?”", "“뭐요?”"]], [["“Could you not give up those small points and shame them into compliance?”", "“그 작은 점들을 양보해서 그들이 부끄러워 따라오게 만들 수는 없어요?”"]], [["“Nothing would shame them.”", "“그들을 부끄럽게 할 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없소.”"]], [["“But would it not be well to try?”", "“하지만 해 보는 게 좋지 않을까요?”"]], [["The game was so good a one, and the stake so important, that Mrs. Grantly felt that it would be worth playing for to the last.", "판이 그토록 좋고 걸린 것이 그토록 중대했으므로, 그랜틀리 부인은 끝까지 걸어 볼 만한 값이 있다고 느꼈다."]], [["“It is no good.”", "“소용없소.”"]], [["“But I certainly would suggest it to Lord De Terrier.", "“하지만 저라면 반드시 드 테리어 경에게 그것을 제안하겠어요."], ["I am sure the country would go along with him; at any rate the Church would.”", "나라가 그를 따라갈 거라고 확신해요. 적어도 교회는 그럴 거예요.”"]], [["“It is impossible,”", "“불가능하오,”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."], ["“To tell the truth, it did occur to me.", "“사실을 말하면, 그 생각이 내게도 떠올랐소."], ["But some of them down there seemed to think that it would not do.”", "그러나 거기 있는 몇 사람은 그게 통하지 않을 거라고 여기는 듯했소.”"]], [["Mrs. Grantly sat awhile on the sofa, still meditating in her mind whether there might not yet be some escape from so terrible a downfall.", "그랜틀리 부인은 소파에 얼마 동안 앉아, 그토록 끔찍한 추락에서 아직 빠져나갈 길이 없지는 않을까 마음속으로 여전히 곰곰 생각했다."]], [["“But, archdeacon—”", "“하지만 부주교님—”"]], [["“I’ll go upstairs and dress,”", "“위층에 올라가 옷을 갈아입겠소,”"], ["said he, in despondency.", "그가 낙심해서 말했다."]], [["“But, archdeacon, surely the present ministry may have a majority on such a subject as that; I thought they were sure of a majority now.”", "“하지만 부주교님, 지금의 내각이 그런 주제라면 분명 다수를 얻을 수 있잖아요. 이제는 다수를 확실히 쥐고 있는 줄 알았어요.”"]], [["“No; not sure.”", "“아니오. 확실하지 않소.”"]], [["“But at any rate the chances are in their favour?", "“하지만 적어도 가망은 그들에게 유리하지요?"], ["I do hope they’ll do their duty, and exert themselves to keep their members together.”", "그들이 제 할 일을 하고, 자기네 의원들을 한데 붙들어 두려고 힘써 주기를 정말 바라요.”"]], [["And then the archdeacon told out the whole of the truth.", "그러고 나서 부주교는 진실을 온통 털어놓았다."]], [["“Lord De Terrier says that under the present circumstances he will not bring the matter forward this session at all.", "“드 테리어 경은 지금 형편으로는 이번 회기에 그 문제를 아예 내놓지 않겠다고 하오."], ["So we had better go back to Plumstead.”", "그러니 우리는 플럼스테드로 돌아가는 게 좋겠소.”"]], [["Mrs. Grantly then felt that there was nothing further to be said, and it will be proper that the historian should drop a veil over their sufferings.", "그러자 그랜틀리 부인은 더 할 말이 없다고 느꼈고, 사가로서는 그들의 고통 위에 베일을 드리우는 것이 마땅할 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIV. MAGNA EST VERITAS.", "# 제24장. 「마그나 에스트 베리타스」"]], [["It was made known to the reader that in the early part of the winter Mr.", "독자는 이미 알고 있다."], ["Sowerby had a scheme for retrieving his lost fortunes, and setting himself right in the world, by marrying that rich heiress, Miss Dunstable.", "겨울이 시작될 무렵 사워비 씨는 잃어버린 재산을 되찾고 세상에서 제자리를 바로 세울 계략을 품고 있었으니, 저 부유한 상속녀 던스터블 양과 혼인하는 것이었다."], ["I fear my friend Sowerby does not, at present, stand high in the estimation of those who have come on with me thus far in this narrative.", "이 이야기를 여기까지 나와 함께 따라온 분들의 평판 속에서 내 벗 사워비가 지금 그리 높이 서 있지는 못하지 않을까 나는 염려한다. 그는 낭비꾼이자 도박꾼으로, 그 낭비와 도박에 있어 정직하다고는 도저히 할 수 없는 사람으로 그려져 왔다."], ["He has been described as a spendthrift and gambler, and as one scarcely honest in his extravagance and gambling. But nevertheless there are worse men than Mr.", "그러나 그럼에도 사워비 씨보다 못한 사내들은 있으며, 설령 그가 던스터블 양에게 뜻을 이룬다 해도 그 숙녀가 끊임없이 제 발밑에 몸을 내던지는 구혼자들 가운데 어느 모로 보나 가장 못한 자를 골랐다고는 나는 말할 준비가 되어 있지 않다."], ["Sowerby, and I am not prepared to say that, should he be successful with Miss Dunstable, that lady would choose by any means the worst of the suitors who are continually throwing themselves at her feet.", "이 사내가 늘 무모해 보였고 또 실제로 철저히 무모했다 해도, 그 가슴 안에는 여전히 더 나은 것을 바라는 마음이 있었고, 그 머릿속에는 자기가 여태 정직한 영국 신사의 길을 놓쳐 왔다는 깨달음이 있었다."], ["Reckless as this man always appeared to be, reckless as he absolutely was, there was still within his heart a desire for better things, and in his mind an understanding that he had hitherto missed the career of an honest English gentleman.", "그는 제 주(州)의 의원이라는 자기 지위를 자랑스러워했으니, 여태 그 자리를 빛낼 만한 일은 거의 하지 않았음에도 그러했다. 그는 콜디코츠의 제 영지를 자랑스러워했으니, 그 소유가 이제 거의 제 손을 떠나 버렸음에도 그러했다."], ["He was proud of his position as member for his county, though hitherto he had done so little to grace it; he was proud of his domain at Chaldicotes, though the possession of it had so nearly passed out of his own hands; he was proud of the old blood that flowed in his veins; and he was proud also of that easy, comfortable, gay manner, which went so far in the world’s judgment to atone for his extravagance and evil practices.", "그는 제 핏줄에 흐르는 오래된 피를 자랑스러워했다. 그리고 그는 저 편하고 느긋하고 흥겨운 태도 또한 자랑스러워했으니, 세상의 판단 속에서 그 태도가 그의 낭비와 못된 행실을 씻어 주는 데 그토록 크게 이바지해 왔던 것이다."], ["If only he could get another chance, as he now said to himself, things should go very differently with him.", "한 번만 더 기회를 얻을 수 있다면, 이제 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 말했다, 만사가 아주 다르게 돌아갈 텐데."], ["He would utterly forswear the whole company of Tozers.", "그는 토저네 무리 전부와 딱 끊겠다고 맹세할 것이다."], ["He would cease to deal in bills, and to pay heaven only knows how many hundred per cent. for his moneys.", "어음을 굴리는 일도, 돈을 얻으려고 하늘만이 아는 몇백 퍼센트를 물어 주는 일도 그만둘 것이다."], ["He would no longer prey upon his friends, and would redeem his title-deeds from the clutches of the Duke of Omnium.", "더는 벗들을 뜯어먹지 않을 것이며, 옴니엄 공작의 손아귀에서 제 권리증서를 되찾아 올 것이다."], ["If only he could get another chance!", "한 번만 더 기회를 얻을 수 있다면!"]], [["Miss Dunstable’s fortune would do all this and ever so much more, and then, moreover, Miss Dunstable was a woman whom he really liked.", "던스터블 양의 재산이면 이 모든 일을, 아니 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 일을 해낼 수 있었다. 게다가 던스터블 양은 그가 정말로 마음에 들어 하는 여인이었다."], ["She was not soft, feminine, or pretty, nor was she very young; but she was clever, self-possessed, and quite able to hold her own in any class; and as to age, Mr.", "그녀는 부드럽지도, 여자답지도, 예쁘지도 않았고, 그리 젊지도 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 영리하고 침착했으며 어느 계층에 놓아도 밀리지 않을 사람이었다."], ["Sowerby was not very young himself.", "그리고 나이로 말하자면 사워비 씨 자신도 그리 젊지 않았다."], ["In making such a match he would have no cause of shame.", "그런 혼사를 이룬다면 부끄러워할 까닭이 없었다."], ["He could speak of it before his friends without fear of their grimaces, and ask them to his house, with the full assurance that the head of his table would not disgrace him.", "벗들 앞에서 그 이야기를 하면서도 그들이 얼굴을 찌푸릴까 두려워할 일이 없고, 제 집으로 그들을 부르면서도 식탁의 상석에 앉은 사람이 자기를 망신시키지는 않으리라 온전히 믿을 수 있었다."], ["And then as the scheme grew clearer and clearer to him, he declared to himself that if he should be successful, he would use her well, and not rob her of her money—beyond what was absolutely necessary.", "그리고 계략이 점점 또렷해질수록 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 다짐했다, 만일 뜻을 이루게 되면 그녀를 잘 대하고 그 돈을 빼앗지 않겠다고 — 도무지 어쩔 수 없는 만큼을 넘어서는."]], [["He had intended to have laid his fortunes at her feet at Chaldicotes; but the lady had been coy.", "그는 콜디코츠에서 제 운수를 그녀의 발밑에 내려놓을 작정이었다. 그런데 그 숙녀가 몸을 사렸다."], ["Then the deed was to have been done at Gatherum Castle, but the lady ran away from Gatherum Castle just at the time on which he had fixed.", "그러면 개서럼 성에서 일을 치르기로 했는데, 그가 날을 잡아 둔 바로 그때 그 숙녀는 개서럼 성에서 달아나 버렸다."], ["And since that, one circumstance after another had postponed the affair in London, till now at last he was resolved that he would know his fate, let it be what it might.", "그 뒤로는 런던에서 이런 일 저런 일이 잇달아 이 일을 미루게 만들었고, 이제 마침내 그는 어떤 결과가 나오든 제 운명을 알아내고 말겠다고 마음을 굳혔다."], ["If he could not contrive that things should speedily be arranged, it might come to pass that he would be altogether debarred from presenting himself to the lady as Mr. Sowerby of Chaldicotes.", "일이 빨리 자리 잡히도록 손을 쓰지 못한다면, 아예 콜디코츠의 사워비 씨로서 그 숙녀 앞에 나설 수 없게 되는 날이 올 수도 있었다."], ["Tidings had reached him, through Mr. Fothergill, that the duke would be glad to have matters arranged; and Mr.", "공작이 일이 정리되기를 반긴다는 소식이 포더길 씨를 통해 그에게 들어와 있었다."], ["Sowerby well knew the meaning of that message.", "그리고 사워비 씨는 그 전갈의 뜻을 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Sowerby was not fighting this campaign alone, without the aid of any ally.", "사워비 씨가 이 싸움을 아무 동맹자의 도움도 없이 홀로 치르고 있었던 것은 아니다."], ["Indeed, no man ever had a more trusty ally in any campaign than he had in this.", "아니, 어떤 싸움에서도 그가 이번에 둔 것보다 더 믿음직한 동맹자를 둔 사내는 없었다."], ["And it was this ally, the only faithful comrade that clung to him through good and ill during his whole life, who first put it into his head that Miss Dunstable was a woman and might be married.", "그리고 온 생애에 걸쳐 좋을 때나 나쁠 때나 그에게 붙어 있던 단 하나의 충실한 동무인 이 동맹자가, 던스터블 양도 여자이니 혼인을 시킬 수 있겠다는 생각을 그의 머리에 처음 넣어 준 사람이었다."]], [["“A hundred needy adventurers have attempted it, and failed already,”", "“궁한 모험가 백 명이 그 일을 해 보고 벌써 다 실패했어.”"], ["Mr. Sowerby had said, when the plan was first proposed to him.", "그 계획이 처음 제 앞에 놓였을 때 사워비 씨는 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“But, nevertheless, she will some day marry some one; and why not you as well as another?”", "“하지만 그이도 언젠가는 누군가와 혼인하지 않겠어요? 그게 다른 남자면 되고 오라버니면 안 될 까닭이 무엇이죠?”"], ["his sister had answered.", "누이동생이 그렇게 대답했다."], ["For Mrs. Harold Smith was the ally of whom I have spoken.", "내가 말한 그 동맹자가 바로 해럴드 스미스 부인이었기 때문이다."]], [["Mrs. Harold Smith, whatever may have been her faults, could boast of this virtue—that she loved her brother.", "해럴드 스미스 부인에게 어떤 허물이 있었든, 그녀는 이 한 가지 미덕만은 자랑할 수 있었다 — 그녀는 오라비를 사랑했다."], ["He was probably the only human being that she did love.", "아마도 그녀가 사랑한 사람은 그 하나뿐이었을 것이다."], ["Children she had none; and as for her husband, it had never occurred to her to love him.", "자식은 없었다. 그리고 남편으로 말하자면, 그를 사랑한다는 생각은 애초에 그녀에게 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["She had married him for a position; and being a clever woman, with a good digestion and command of her temper, had managed to get through the world without much of that unhappiness which usually follows ill-assorted marriages.", "그녀는 지위를 얻으려고 그와 혼인했고, 영리한 여자였으므로, 소화가 잘 되고 제 성미를 다스릴 줄 아는 덕에, 어울리지 않는 혼인 뒤에 흔히 따라오는 그런 불행을 크게 겪지 않고 세상을 헤쳐 왔다."], ["At home she managed to keep the upper hand, but she did so in an easy, good-humoured way that made her rule bearable; and away from home she assisted her lord’s political standing, though she laughed more keenly than any one else at his foibles.", "집 안에서는 윗자리를 지켜 냈으나, 편하고 너그러운 태도로 그렇게 했으므로 그녀의 다스림은 견딜 만한 것이었다. 그리고 집 밖에서는 남편의 정치적 지위를 도와 주었으니, 그 흠들을 두고 누구보다도 날카롭게 웃는 사람이 그녀였음에도 그러했다."], ["But the lord of her heart was her brother; and in all his scrapes, all his extravagances, and all his recklessness, she had ever been willing to assist him.", "그러나 그녀의 마음의 주인은 오라비였다. 그리고 그가 겪은 모든 곤경, 모든 낭비, 모든 무모함 속에서 그녀는 언제나 그를 도울 마음이 있었다."], ["With the view of doing this she had sought the intimacy of Miss Dunstable, and for the last year past had indulged every caprice of that lady.", "그럴 셈으로 그녀는 던스터블 양과 가까워지기를 구했고, 지난 한 해 동안 그 숙녀의 변덕이라면 무엇이든 다 맞춰 주었다. 아니, 그보다도 그녀는 던스터블 양의 마음을 얻는 길이 변덕을 맞춰 주는 데 있지 않고, 재미가 한 줌 섞인 거리낌 없고 편한 왕래에, 그리고 적어도 정직해 보이는 겉모양에 있다는 것을 알아챌 만한 재치가 있었다."], ["Or rather, she had had the wit to learn that Miss Dunstable was to be won, not by the indulgence of caprices, but by free and easy intercourse, with a dash of fun, and, at any rate, a semblance of honesty. Mrs.", "해럴드 스미스 부인은 아마도 타고난 성품이 그리 정직하지는 않았다."], ["Harold Smith was not, perhaps, herself very honest by disposition; but in these latter days she had taken up a theory of honesty for the sake of Miss Dunstable—not altogether in vain, for Miss Dunstable and Mrs. Harold Smith were certainly very intimate.", "그러나 요 근래에는 던스터블 양을 위하여 정직이라는 신조를 세워 두고 있었으니 — 그것이 아주 헛되지는 않았던 것이, 던스터블 양과 해럴드 스미스 부인은 참으로 아주 가까운 사이였다."]], [["“If I am to do it at all, I must not wait any longer,”", "“내가 이 일을 하려면 더는 기다릴 수 없네.”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby to his sister a day or two after the final break-down of the gods.", "신들이 마지막으로 무너져 내린 지 하루나 이틀 뒤에 사워비 씨가 누이동생에게 말했다."], ["The affection of the sister for the brother may be imagined from the fact that at such a time she could give up her mind to such a subject.", "그런 때에 그녀가 그런 문제에 마음을 쏟을 수 있었다는 사실만 보아도 오라비를 향한 누이의 애정이 어떠했는지는 짐작할 만하다."], ["But, in truth, her husband’s position as a cabinet minister was as nothing to her compared with her brother’s position as a county gentleman.", "그러나 사실 내각 각료인 남편의 지위는, 주(州)의 지주인 오라비의 지위에 비하면 그녀에게 아무것도 아니었다."]], [["“One time is as good as another,”", "“때야 어느 때나 마찬가지지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“You mean that you would advise me to ask her at once.”", "“당장 청혼하라고 나에게 권하는 말이로군.”"]], [["“Certainly.", "“그럼요."], ["But you must remember, Nat, that you will have no easy task.", "하지만 냇, 이건 쉬운 일이 아니라는 걸 잊지 마세요."], ["It will not do for you to kneel down and swear that you love her.”", "무릎을 꿇고 사랑한다고 맹세하는 식으로는 안 될 거예요.”"]], [["“If I do it at all, I shall certainly do it without kneeling—you may be sure of that, Harriet.”", "“내가 그 일을 한다면 무릎은 확실히 꿇지 않고 할 걸세 — 그건 자네가 믿어도 좋아, 해리엇.”"]], [["“Yes, and without swearing that you love her.", "“그렇지요. 사랑한다고 맹세하지도 말고요."], ["There is only one way in which you can be successful with Miss Dunstable—you must tell her the truth.”", "던스터블 양에게 뜻을 이룰 길은 하나뿐이에요 — 그이한테 진실을 말해야 해요.”"]], [["“What!—tell her that I am ruined, horse, foot, and dragoons, and then bid her help me out of the mire?”", "“뭐라고! 내가 기병이며 보병이며 용기병까지 통째로 망했다고 털어놓고, 그러니 나를 이 진창에서 건져 달라고 청하란 말인가?”"]], [["“Exactly: that will be your only chance, strange as it may appear.”", "“바로 그거예요. 이상하게 들리겠지만 그게 오라버니의 유일한 기회예요.”"]], [["“This is very different from what you used to say, down at Chaldicotes.”", "“콜디코츠에서 자네가 하던 말과는 아주 다르지 않은가.”"]], [["“So it is; but I know her much better than I did when we were there.", "“그렇지요. 하지만 그때 콜디코츠에 있을 때보다 지금은 그이를 훨씬 잘 알아요."], ["Since then I have done but little else than study the freaks of her character.", "그 뒤로 나는 그 성격의 별난 점들을 살피는 일 말고는 별로 한 일이 없거든요."], ["If she really likes you—and I think she does—she could forgive you any other crime but that of swearing that you loved her.”", "그이가 정말로 오라버니를 좋아한다면 — 나는 좋아한다고 봐요 — 다른 죄는 다 용서해 줄 사람이지만 사랑한다고 맹세하는 죄만은 용서하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“I should hardly know how to propose without saying something about it.”", "“사랑 이야기를 한마디도 안 하고 어떻게 청혼을 하는지 나는 도무지 모르겠네.”"]], [["“But you must say nothing—not a word; you must tell her that you are a gentleman of good blood and high station, but sadly out at elbows.”", "“하지만 아무 말도 하면 안 돼요. 단 한마디도요. 오라버니는 좋은 혈통과 높은 지위를 지닌 신사이지만 딱하게도 팔꿈치가 다 해졌다고 말해야 해요.”"]], [["“She knows that already.”", "“그건 그이가 벌써 알고 있어.”"]], [["“Of course she does; but she must know it as coming directly from your own mouth.", "“물론 알고 있지요. 하지만 그이는 그 말을 오라버니 입에서 곧바로 나오는 것으로 들어야 해요."], ["And then tell her that you propose to set yourself right by marrying her—by marrying her for the sake of her money.”", "그러고 나서 그이와 혼인하여 제 형편을 바로 세우려 한다고 말하세요 — 그 돈을 위해서 그이와 혼인하려 한다고요.”"]], [["“That will hardly win her, I should say.”", "“그래서야 그이 마음을 얻기는 어렵겠는걸.”"]], [["“If it does not, no other way, that I know of, will do so.", "“그래서 안 되면, 내가 아는 어떤 길로도 안 될 거예요."], ["As I told you before, it will be no easy task.", "앞서도 말했지만 이건 쉬운 일이 아니에요."], ["Of course you must make her understand that her happiness shall be cared for; but that must not be put prominently forward as your object.", "물론 그이의 행복도 살펴 주겠다는 것을 알아듣게 해야 하지요. 하지만 그것을 오라버니의 목적으로 앞세워서는 안 돼요."], ["Your first object is her money, and your only chance for success is in telling the truth.”", "오라버니의 첫째 목적은 그이의 돈이고, 뜻을 이룰 유일한 기회는 진실을 말하는 데 있어요.”"]], [["“It is very seldom that a man finds himself in such a position as that,”", "“사내가 이런 처지에 놓이는 일은 좀처럼 없지.”"], ["said Sowerby, walking up and down his sister’s room; “and, upon my word, I don’t think I am up to the task.", "사워비 씨가 누이동생의 방을 오락가락하며 말했다. “그리고 참말이지, 나는 이 일을 감당할 만한 사람이 아닌 것 같아."], ["I should certainly break down.", "틀림없이 무너져 버릴 걸세."], ["I don’t believe there’s a man in London could go to a woman with such a story as that, and then ask her to marry him.”", "런던에 그런 이야기를 들고 여자에게 찾아가서 나와 혼인해 달라고 청할 수 있는 사내가 있으리라고는 믿지 않아.”"]], [["“If you cannot, you may as well give it up,”", "“못 하겠으면 아예 그만두는 게 좋겠지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["“But if you can do it—if you can go through with it in that manner—my own opinion is that your chance of success would not be bad.", "“하지만 할 수 있다면 — 그런 식으로 끝까지 밀고 나갈 수 있다면 — 내 생각으로는 뜻을 이룰 가망이 나쁘지 않아요."], ["The fact is,”", "실은 말이죠,”"], ["added the sister after awhile, during which her brother was continuing his walk and meditating on the difficulties of his position—“the fact is, you men never understand a woman; you give her credit neither for her strength, nor for her weakness.", "누이는 잠시 뒤에 이렇게 덧붙였는데, 그 사이에도 오라비는 계속 방을 오락가락하며 제 처지의 어려움을 곱씹고 있었다. “실은 말이죠, 오라버니 같은 남자들은 여자를 도무지 몰라요."], ["You are too bold, and too timid: you think she is a fool and tell her so, and yet never can trust her to do a kind action.", "여자의 강한 데도, 약한 데도 제대로 셈해 주지 않지요. 너무 담대하면서 또 너무 겁이 많아요."], ["Why should she not marry you with the intention of doing you a good turn?", "여자를 바보라고 여기고 그렇다고 대놓고 말하면서도, 여자가 착한 일을 할 거라고는 도무지 믿어 주지 못하지요. 그이가 오라버니에게 좋은 일을 해 줄 마음으로 혼인한다고 해서 안 될 것이 무엇이죠?"], ["After all, she would lose very little: there is the estate, and if she redeemed it, it would belong to her as well as to you.”", "결국 그이가 잃는 것은 아주 적어요. 영지가 있으니, 그이가 그것을 되찾아 준다면 그것은 오라버니 것인 만큼 그이 것도 되는 거예요.”"]], [["“It would be a good turn, indeed.", "“정말이지 그거야 큰 은덕이겠지."], ["I fear I should be too modest to put it to her in that way.”", "나는 낯이 얇아서 그이에게 그렇게까지는 못 내놓을 것 같은데.”"]], [["“Her position would be much better as your wife than it is at present.", "“그이의 처지는 지금보다 오라버니의 아내가 되는 편이 훨씬 나아요."], ["You are good-humoured and good-tempered, you would intend to treat her well, and, on the whole, she would be much happier as Mrs. Sowerby, of Chaldicotes, than she can be in her present position.”", "오라버니는 너그럽고 성미도 좋으니 그이를 잘 대할 마음일 테고, 무엇보다 그이는 지금 처지에 있을 때보다 콜디코츠의 사워비 부인으로 있을 때 훨씬 더 행복할 거예요.”"]], [["“If she cared about being married, I suppose she could be a peer’s wife to-morrow.”", "“혼인하는 데 마음이 있었다면 내일이라도 귀족의 아내가 될 수 있었겠지.”"]], [["“But I don’t think she cares about being a peer’s wife.", "“하지만 나는 그이가 귀족의 아내가 되는 데 마음이 있다고 보지 않아요."], ["A needy peer might perhaps win her in the way that I propose to you; but then a needy peer would not know how to set about it.", "궁한 귀족이라면 내가 오라버니에게 권하는 그 방식으로 어쩌면 그이 마음을 얻을 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 궁한 귀족은 그 일을 어떻게 시작해야 하는지를 몰라요."], ["Needy peers have tried—half a dozen I have no doubt—and have failed, because they have pretended that they were in love with her.", "궁한 귀족들이 해 보았지요 — 여섯 명은 되리라고 나는 의심하지 않아요 — 그리고 다 실패했어요. 그이를 사랑한다고 시늉을 했으니까요."], ["It may be difficult, but your only chance is to tell her the truth.”", "어려운 일일지 몰라도 오라버니의 유일한 기회는 그이한테 진실을 말하는 거예요.”"]], [["“And where shall I do it?”", "“그러면 어디서 하면 되겠나?”"]], [["“Here if you choose; but her own house will be better.”", "“원한다면 여기서 하세요. 하지만 그이 집이 더 나을 거예요.”"]], [["“But I never can see her there—at least, not alone.", "“하지만 나는 거기서 그이를 만날 수가 없어 — 적어도 단둘이는 못 만나지."], ["I believe that she never is alone.", "그이는 절대 혼자 있는 일이 없다고 나는 알고 있네."], ["She always keeps a lot of people round her in order to stave off her lovers.", "사랑을 걸어오는 자들을 막으려고 늘 여러 사람을 둘러 두고 있단 말이야."], ["Upon my word, Harriet, I think I’ll give it up.", "참말이지 해리엇, 나는 이 일을 그만둘까 하네."], ["It is impossible that I should make such a declaration to her as that you propose.”", "자네가 권하는 그런 선언을 내가 그이에게 한다는 것은 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이야.”"]], [["“Faint heart, Nat—you know the rest.”", "“마음이 약해서야, 냇 — 그다음 구절은 알잖아요.”"]], [["“But the poet never alluded to such wooing as that you have suggested.", "“하지만 그 시인은 자네가 권하는 그런 구애를 두고 말한 적이 없지."], ["I suppose I had better begin with a schedule of my debts, and make reference, if she doubts me, to Fothergill, the sheriff’s officers, and the Tozer family.”", "내 빚 목록부터 꺼내 놓고, 그이가 나를 의심하면 포더길과 집행리들과 토저네 사람들을 증인으로 대라는 말이겠군.”"]], [["“She will not doubt you, on that head; nor will she be a bit surprised.”", "“그 점을 두고는 오라버니를 의심하지 않을 거예요. 조금도 놀라지도 않을 거고요.”"]], [["Then there was again a pause, during which Mr. Sowerby still walked up and down the room, thinking whether or no he might possibly have any chance of success in so hazardous an enterprise.", "그러고는 다시 말이 끊어졌고, 그 사이에도 사워비 씨는 여전히 방을 오락가락하며 그토록 위태로운 일에 자기가 조금이라도 뜻을 이룰 가망이 있을까 없을까를 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["“I tell you what, Harriet,”", "“이러면 어떻겠나, 해리엇.”"], ["at last he said; “I wish you’d do it for me.”", "마침내 그가 말했다. “자네가 나를 위해 그 일을 해 주었으면 하네.”"]], [["“Well,”", "“그러죠.”"], ["said she, “if you really mean it, I will make the attempt.”", "그녀가 말했다. “정말 그러기를 바라신다면 내가 한번 해 보겠어요.”"]], [["“I am sure of this, that I shall never make it myself.", "“이것만은 분명해. 나는 결코 그 일을 내 입으로 못 하겠어."], ["I positively should not have the courage to tell her in so many words, that I wanted to marry her for her money.”", "그이의 돈을 위해 혼인하고 싶다고 그렇게 낱말을 다 갖춰 말할 용기가 나에게는 정말로 없네.”"]], [["“Well, Nat, I will attempt it.", "“좋아요, 냇, 내가 해 보지요."], ["At any rate, I am not afraid of her.", "어쨌든 나는 그이가 두렵지 않아요."], ["She and I are excellent friends, and, to tell the truth, I think I like her better than any other woman that I know; but I never should have been intimate with her, had it not been for your sake.”", "그이와 나는 아주 좋은 벗이고, 사실을 말하면 내가 아는 어느 여자보다 그이가 좋아요. 하지만 오라버니를 위해서가 아니었다면 나는 그이와 가까워지지도 않았을 거예요.”"]], [["“And now you will have to quarrel with her, also for my sake?”", "“그런데 이제는 역시 나 때문에 그이와 다투게 되겠군?”"]], [["“Not at all.", "“전혀 그렇지 않아요."], ["You’ll find that whether she accedes to my proposition or not, we shall continue friends.", "그이가 내 제안을 받아들이든 안 받아들이든 우리는 계속 벗으로 지낼 거예요."], ["I do not think that she would die for me—nor I for her.", "그이가 나를 위해 죽어 줄 거라고는 생각하지 않아요 — 나도 그이를 위해 죽지 않을 거고요."], ["But as the world goes we suit each other.", "하지만 세상 돌아가는 이치로 보면 우리는 서로 잘 맞아요."], ["Such a little trifle as this will not break our loves.”", "이만한 자잘한 일로 우리 우애가 깨지지는 않아요.”"]], [["And so it was settled.", "그렇게 일이 정해졌다."], ["On the following day Mrs. Harold Smith was to find an opportunity of explaining the whole matter to Miss Dunstable, and was to ask that lady to share her fortune—some incredible number of thousands of pounds—with the bankrupt member for West Barsetshire, who in return was to bestow on her—himself and his debts.", "이튿날 해럴드 스미스 부인은 이 일 전부를 던스터블 양에게 설명할 틈을 찾아내어, 그 숙녀에게 재산을 — 몇천 파운드인지 헤아릴 수도 없는 그 재산을 — 서바셋주의 파산한 의원과 나누어 가지라고 청할 것이었으며, 그 의원은 그 보답으로 그녀에게 제 몸과 제 빚을 내어 줄 것이었다."]], [["Mrs. Harold Smith had spoken no more than the truth in saying that she and Miss Dunstable suited one another.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 자기와 던스터블 양이 서로 잘 맞는다고 한 말은 진실 그대로였다."], ["And she had not improperly described their friendship.", "그리고 그녀가 두 사람의 우정을 그려 낸 것도 어긋난 데가 없었다."], ["They were not prepared to die, one for the sake of the other.", "두 사람은 한쪽을 위해 다른 쪽이 죽을 준비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["They had said nothing to each other of mutual love and affection.", "서로에게 사랑이니 애정이니 하는 말을 한 적도 없었다."], ["They never kissed, or cried, or made speeches, when they met or when they parted.", "만날 때나 헤어질 때나 입을 맞추거나 울거나 긴말을 늘어놓는 일이 없었다."], ["There was no great benefit for which either had to be grateful to the other; no terrible injury which either had forgiven.", "한쪽이 다른 쪽에게 고마워해야 할 큰 은덕도 없었고, 한쪽이 용서해 주어야 할 끔찍한 해코지도 없었다."], ["But they suited each other; and this, I take it, is the secret of most of our pleasantest intercourse in the world.", "그러나 두 사람은 서로 잘 맞았다. 그리고 내가 보기에는 이것이 이 세상에서 우리가 누리는 가장 즐거운 왕래 대부분의 비밀이다."]], [["And it was almost grievous that they should suit each other, for Miss Dunstable was much the worthier of the two, had she but known it herself.", "그런데 두 사람이 서로 잘 맞는다는 것은 거의 애석한 일이었으니, 던스터블 양이 스스로만 알았다면 둘 중 훨씬 나은 사람이었기 때문이다."], ["It was almost to be lamented that she should have found herself able to live with Mrs. Harold Smith on terms that were perfectly satisfactory to herself.", "그녀가 해럴드 스미스 부인과 더불어 자기로서는 아주 만족스러운 사이로 지낼 수 있게 되었다는 것은 거의 한탄할 만한 일이었다."], ["Mrs. Harold Smith was worldly, heartless—to all the world but her brother—and, as has been above hinted, almost dishonest.", "해럴드 스미스 부인은 세속적이고, 오라비를 뺀 온 세상에 대하여 마음이 없었으며, 앞에서 비친 대로 거의 정직하지 못한 사람이었다."], ["Miss Dunstable was not worldly, though it was possible that her present style of life might make her so; she was affectionate, fond of truth, and prone to honesty, if those around would but allow her to exercise it.", "던스터블 양은 세속적이지 않았다 — 다만 지금 같은 살림살이가 그녀를 그렇게 만들 수도 있기는 했다. 그녀는 정이 두터웠고, 진실을 아꼈으며, 주위 사람들이 그렇게 하도록 놓아만 준다면 정직 쪽으로 기울 사람이었다."], ["But she was fond of ease and humour, sometimes of wit that might almost be called broad, and she had a thorough love of ridiculing the world’s humbugs.", "그러나 그녀는 편안함과 익살을 좋아했고, 더러는 걸쭉하다고 해도 좋을 만한 재담을 좋아했으며, 세상의 수작을 비웃는 일을 몹시 사랑했다."], ["In all these propensities Mrs. Harold Smith indulged her.", "이 모든 성벽을 해럴드 스미스 부인은 다 맞춰 주었다."]], [["Under these circumstances they were now together almost every day.", "이런 사정이었으므로 두 사람은 이제 거의 날마다 함께 있었다."], ["It had become quite a habit with Mrs. Harold Smith to have herself driven early in the forenoon to Miss Dunstable’s house; and that lady, though she could never be found alone by Mr.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 아침나절 이른 시각에 마차를 몰게 하여 던스터블 양의 집으로 가는 것은 아주 굳은 습관이 되어 있었다."], ["Sowerby, was habitually so found by his sister. And after that they would go out together, or each separately, as fancy or the business of the day might direct them.", "그리고 그 숙녀는 사워비 씨에게는 결코 혼자 있는 모습을 보이지 않았으나, 그 누이동생에게는 늘 혼자 있는 모습으로 발견되었다."], ["Each was easy to the other in this alliance, and they so managed that they never trod on each other’s corns.", "그러고 나면 두 사람은 함께 나가기도 했고, 그날의 기분이나 볼일이 이끄는 대로 따로따로 나가기도 했다. 이 동맹 안에서 둘은 서로에게 편했고, 서로의 발가락을 밟는 일이 없도록 잘 처신했다."]], [["On the day following the agreement made between Mr. Sowerby and Mrs.", "사워비 씨와 해럴드 스미스 부인 사이에 약조가 이루어진 이튿날, 그 부인은 여느 때처럼 던스터블 양을 찾아가서, 그 상속녀가 오로지 자기만의 용도로 두고 있는 작은 방에서 이내 벗과 단둘이 있게 되었다."], ["Harold Smith, that lady as usual called on Miss Dunstable, and soon found herself alone with her friend in a small room which the heiress kept solely for her own purposes.", "특별한 때에는 여러 부류의 사람들이 그 방에 들어오기도 했다."], ["On special occasions persons of various sorts were there admitted; occasionally a parson who had a church to build, or a dowager laden with the last morsel of town slander, or a poor author who could not get due payment for the efforts of his brain, or a poor governess on whose feeble stamina the weight of the world had borne too hardly.", "이따금 교회를 지어야 하는 목사 하나, 장안의 최신 험담 한 조각을 잔뜩 싣고 온 노부인 하나, 머리를 쓴 대가를 제대로 받지 못한 가난한 저술가 하나, 아니면 그 여린 몸에 세상의 무게가 너무 무겁게 얹힌 가난한 가정 교사 하나가 들어왔다."], ["But men who by possibility could be lovers did not make their way thither, nor women who could be bores.", "그러나 어쩌다가라도 연인이 될 수 있는 남자들은 거기까지 길을 뚫지 못했고, 진력나게 하는 여자들도 그러했다."], ["In these latter days, that is, during the present London season, the doors of it had been oftener opened to Mrs. Harold Smith than to any other person.", "요 근래에는, 곧 이번 런던 사교철 동안에는, 그 방의 문이 다른 누구보다 해럴드 스미스 부인에게 자주 열렸다."]], [["And now the effort was to be made with the object of which all this intimacy had been effected.", "그리하여 이제 이 모든 친분을 맺어 온 목적을 이루기 위한 시도가 행해질 참이었다."], ["As she came thither in her carriage, Mrs. Harold Smith herself was not altogether devoid of that sinking of the heart which is so frequently the forerunner of any difficult and hazardous undertaking.", "마차를 타고 그리로 오는 동안 해럴드 스미스 부인 자신도, 어렵고 위태로운 일에 앞서 흔히 찾아오는 그 가슴 내려앉는 느낌이 아주 없지는 않았다."], ["She had declared that she would feel no fear in making the little proposition.", "그녀는 그 자잘한 제안을 하는 데 아무 두려움도 느끼지 않으리라고 선언해 두었었다."], ["But she did feel something very like it; and when she made her entrance into the little room she certainly wished that the work was done and over.", "그러나 그녀는 그와 아주 비슷한 무엇을 느꼈다. 그리고 그 작은 방으로 들어설 때에는 이 일이 다 끝나 버렸으면 하고 진심으로 바랐다."]], [["“How is poor Mr. Smith to-day?”", "“가엾은 스미스 씨는 오늘 어떠세요?”"], ["asked Miss Dunstable, with an air of mock condolence, as her friend seated herself in her accustomed easy-chair.", "던스터블 양이 조문하는 시늉을 하는 낯빛으로 물었고, 벗은 늘 앉는 안락의자에 자리를 잡았다."], ["The downfall of the gods was as yet a history hardly three days old, and it might well be supposed that the late lord of the Petty Bag had hardly recovered from his misfortune.", "신들의 몰락은 아직 사흘도 채 되지 않은 옛일이었으니, 지난번까지 프티백 경이던 이가 그 재난에서 아직 몸을 일으키지 못했다고 보아도 무리가 없었다."]], [["“Well, he is better, I think, this morning; at least I should judge so from the manner in which he confronted his eggs.", "“글쎄요, 오늘 아침에는 좀 나아진 것 같아요. 적어도 달걀을 마주하는 태도로 보아서는 그렇게 판단하겠어요."], ["But still I don’t like the way he handles the carving-knife.", "하지만 그이가 고기 썰는 칼을 다루는 손놀림은 여전히 마음에 들지 않아요."], ["I am sure he is always thinking of Mr. Supplehouse at those moments.”", "그럴 때면 틀림없이 서플하우스 씨를 떠올리고 있는 거예요.”"]], [["“Poor man!", "“가엾은 사람!"], ["I mean Supplehouse.", "서플하우스 씨 말이에요."], ["After all, why shouldn’t he follow his trade as well as another?", "결국 그이도 남들처럼 제 밥벌이를 따라가면 안 될 까닭이 무엇이죠?"], ["Live and let live, that’s what I say.”", "살고 또 살게 하라, 나는 그렇게 말해요.”"]], [["“Ay, but it’s kill and let kill with him.", "“아, 그런데 그 사람은 죽이고 또 죽게 하라지요."], ["That is what Horace says.", "호라티우스가 하는 말이 그거잖아요."], ["However, I am tired of all that now, and I came here to-day to talk about something else.”", "하지만 나는 이제 그런 이야기가 다 지겨워요. 오늘은 다른 이야기를 하려고 왔어요.”"]], [["“I rather like Mr. Supplehouse myself,”", "“나는 서플하우스 씨가 오히려 마음에 들어요.”"], ["exclaimed Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 소리쳤다."], ["“He never makes any bones about the matter.", "“그이는 그 일을 두고 아무 거리낌이 없어요."], ["He has a certain work to do, and a certain cause to serve—namely, his own; and in order to do that work, and serve that cause, he uses such weapons as God has placed in his hands.”", "해야 할 일이 정해져 있고 섬겨야 할 명분이 정해져 있는데 — 그 명분이란 곧 자기 자신이지요 — 그 일을 하고 그 명분을 섬기려고 하느님이 제 손에 놓아 주신 무기를 쓰는 거예요.”"]], [["“That’s what the wild beasts do.”", "“그건 들짐승이 하는 짓이지요.”"]], [["“And where will you find men honester than they?", "“그러면 들짐승보다 정직한 사내를 어디서 찾겠어요?"], ["The tiger tears you up because he is hungry and wants to eat you.", "호랑이는 배가 고파서, 당신을 먹고 싶어서 당신을 찢어 놓아요."], ["That’s what Supplehouse does.", "서플하우스 씨가 하는 짓이 그거예요."], ["But there are so many among us tearing up one another without any excuse of hunger.", "그런데 우리 가운데는 배고픔이라는 핑계조차 없이 서로를 찢어 놓는 사람이 그토록 많아요."], ["The mere pleasure of destroying is reason enough.”", "부수는 재미 그것만으로 넉넉한 이유가 되지요.”"]], [["“Well, my dear, my mission to you to-day is certainly not one of destruction, as you will admit when you hear it.", "“그런데 이봐요, 오늘 내가 당신을 찾아온 일은 결코 부수는 일이 아니에요. 들어 보면 당신도 인정할 거예요."], ["It is one, rather, very absolutely of salvation.", "오히려 아주 온전히 구원하는 일이지요."], ["I have come to make love to you.”", "나는 당신에게 구애를 하러 왔어요.”"]], [["“Then the salvation, I suppose, is not for myself,”", "“그러면 그 구원은 내 몫이 아닌 것 같군요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["It was quite clear to Mrs. Harold Smith that Miss Dunstable had immediately understood the whole purport of this visit, and that she was not in any great measure surprised.", "던스터블 양이 이 방문의 뜻 전부를 곧바로 알아차렸다는 것, 그리고 그녀가 그리 크게 놀라지도 않았다는 것이 해럴드 스미스 부인에게는 아주 분명해졌다."], ["It did not seem from the tone of the heiress’s voice, or from the serious look which at once settled on her face, that she would be prepared to give a very ready compliance.", "그 상속녀의 목소리로 보나, 그 얼굴에 이내 내려앉은 굳은 표정으로 보나, 아주 선선히 응낙해 줄 낌새는 아니었다."], ["But then great objects can only be won with great efforts.", "하지만 큰 목적이란 큰 힘을 들여야만 얻어지는 것이다."]], [["“That’s as may be,”", "“그건 그렇게 될 수도 있지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["“For you and another also, I hope.", "“당신에게도, 그리고 또 한 사람에게도, 나는 그러기를 바라요."], ["But I trust, at any rate, that I may not offend you?”", "그러나 어쨌든 당신의 노여움을 사지는 않았으면 하는데요?”"]], [["“Oh, laws, no; nothing of that kind ever offends me now.”", "“아이고, 천만에요. 요즘은 그런 일로 노여워지는 법이 없어요.”"]], [["“Well, I suppose you’re used to it.”", "“하기야 익숙해지셨겠지요.”"]], [["“Like the eels, my dear.", "“뱀장어처럼요, 이봐요."], ["I don’t mind it the least in the world—only sometimes, you know, it is a little tedious.”", "세상에 조금도 마음에 걸리지 않아요 — 다만 이따금, 아시겠지만, 좀 지루하기는 하지요.”"]], [["“I’ll endeavour to avoid that, so I may as well break the ice at once.", "“그건 안 되게 애써 보겠어요. 그러니 대뜸 얼음을 깨는 편이 낫겠네요."], ["You know enough of Nathaniel’s affairs to be aware that he is not a very rich man.”", "나다니엘의 형편은 당신도 어지간히 알고 있으니 그이가 그리 넉넉한 사람이 아니라는 것쯤은 아시겠지요.”"]], [["“Since you do ask me about it, I suppose there’s no harm in saying that I believe him to be a very poor man.”", "“당신이 그렇게 물으시니, 나는 그이가 몹시 가난한 사람이라고 믿는다고 말해도 해로울 것이 없겠군요.”"]], [["“Not the least harm in the world, but just the reverse.", "“세상에 조금도 해로울 것이 없어요. 오히려 그 반대지요."], ["Whatever may come of this, my wish is that the truth should be told scrupulously on all sides; the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth.”", "이 일이 어떻게 되든, 내 소원은 모든 쪽에서 진실이 한 치도 어김없이 말해지는 거예요. 진실을, 진실 전부를, 진실 말고는 아무것도.”"]], [["“Magna est veritas,”", "“「마그나 에스트 베리타스」.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“The Bishop of Barchester taught me as much Latin as that at Chaldicotes; and he did add some more, but there was a long word, and I forgot it.”", "“바체스터 주교가 콜디코츠에서 그만큼의 라틴어를 나에게 가르쳐 주었지요. 몇 마디 더 붙이기는 했는데, 긴 낱말이 하나 있어서 그건 잊어버렸어요.”"]], [["“The bishop was quite right, my dear, I’m sure.", "“주교님 말씀이 지당하셨지요, 이봐요."], ["But if you go to your Latin, I’m lost.", "하지만 당신이 라틴어로 가시면 나는 길을 잃어요."], ["As we were just now saying, my brother’s pecuniary affairs are in a very bad state.", "방금 말한 대로 내 오라비의 금전 형편은 아주 나쁜 상태예요."], ["He has a beautiful property of his own, which has been in the family for I can’t say how many centuries—long before the Conquest, I know.”", "그이에게는 아름다운 제 소유의 영지가 있는데, 몇 세기 동안 집안에 있었는지 나는 말할 수도 없어요 — 노르만 정복보다 훨씬 전이라는 것은 알지요.”"]], [["“I wonder what my ancestors were then?”", "“그러면 그때 우리 조상은 무엇을 하고 있었을까요?”"]], [["“It does not much signify to any of us,” said Mrs.", "“우리 조상이 무엇이었느냐는 우리 누구에게도 그리 대수롭지 않아요.”"], ["Harold Smith, with a moral shake of her head, “what our ancestors were; but it’s a sad thing to see an old property go to ruin.”", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 훈계하듯 고개를 저으며 말했다. “하지만 오래된 재산이 허물어지는 것을 보는 것은 슬픈 일이지요.”"]], [["“Yes, indeed; we none of us like to see our property going to ruin, whether it be old or new.", "“그렇지요, 참으로. 오래된 것이든 새것이든 우리 가운데 제 재산이 허물어지는 것을 보고 싶은 사람은 없어요."], ["I have some of that sort of feeling already, although mine was only made the other day out of an apothecary’s shop.”", "나도 벌써 그런 느낌이 조금 있어요. 내 것은 바로 얼마 전에 약방에서 만들어진 것이지만요.”"]], [["“God forbid that I should ever help you to ruin it,”", "“내가 당신 재산을 허무는 데 손을 보태는 일은 하느님이 막아 주시기를.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["“I should be sorry to be the means of your losing a ten-pound note.”", "“당신이 십 파운드 지폐 한 장을 잃는 빌미가 된다면 나는 미안할 거예요.”"]], [["“Magna est veritas, as the dear bishop said,”", "“「마그나 에스트 베리타스」, 그 다정한 주교님 말씀대로요.”"], ["exclaimed Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 소리쳤다."], ["“Let us have the truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth, as we agreed just now.”", "“방금 우리가 약조한 대로, 진실을, 진실 전부를, 진실 말고는 아무것도, 그렇게 갑시다.”"]], [["Mrs. Harold Smith did begin to find that the task before her was difficult.", "해럴드 스미스 부인은 제 앞에 놓인 일이 어렵다는 것을 정말로 깨닫기 시작했다."], ["There was a hardness about Miss Dunstable when matters of business were concerned on which it seemed almost impossible to make any impression.", "사무적인 일이 걸릴 때면 던스터블 양에게는 도무지 무슨 자국을 낼 수 없을 것 같은 단단한 데가 있었다. 그녀가 사워비 씨의 손길을 거절하겠다는 뜻을 내보인 것은 아니었다."], ["It was not that she had evinced any determination to refuse the tender of Mr.", "그러나 눈에 먼지가 뿌려지는 것만은 안 되겠다고 야멸치게 다잡고 있었다!"], ["Sowerby’s hand; but she was so painfully resolute not to have dust thrown in her eyes! Mrs.", "해럴드 스미스 부인은 스스로 수작이라 부르는 것을 피하겠다고 마음을 다지고 시작했다."], ["Harold Smith had commenced with a mind fixed upon avoiding what she called humbug; but this sort of humbug had become so prominent a part of her usual rhetoric, that she found it very hard to abandon it.", "그런데 이런 종류의 수작이 그녀의 여느 말솜씨에서 그토록 큰 몫을 차지하게 되어 버린 터라, 그것을 내버리기가 몹시 힘들다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["“And that’s what I wish,”", "“나도 그러기를 바라요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Of course my chief object is to secure my brother’s happiness.”", "“물론 내 으뜸 목적은 오라비의 행복을 굳혀 주는 거예요.”"]], [["“That’s very unkind to poor Mr. Harold Smith.”", "“가엾은 해럴드 스미스 씨한테는 참 매정한 말이군요.”"]], [["“Well, well, well—you know what I mean.”", "“자, 자, 자 — 내 말뜻을 알잖아요.”"]], [["“Yes, I think I do know what you mean.", "“그래요, 당신 말뜻을 나도 알 것 같아요."], ["Your brother is a gentleman of good family, but of no means.”", "당신 오라비는 좋은 집안의 신사이지만 재산이 없지요.”"]], [["“Not quite so bad as that.”", "“그렇게까지 나쁘지는 않아요.”"]], [["“Of embarrassed means, then, or anything that you will; whereas I am a lady of no family, but of sufficient wealth.", "“그럼 형편이 궁색해진 것이라 하든, 당신이 원하는 어떤 말로 하든 좋아요. 그에 비하여 나는 집안은 없으나 재물은 넉넉한 숙녀지요."], ["You think that if you brought us together and made a match of it, it would be a very good thing for—for whom?”", "당신 생각으로는 우리 둘을 붙여 놓고 혼사를 이루면 아주 좋은 일이 될 거라는 거고요 — 누구에게 좋은 일일까요?”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, exactly,”", "“그래요, 바로 그거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“For which of us?", "“우리 둘 중 누구에게 말이죠?"], ["Remember the bishop now and his nice little bit of Latin.”", "이제 주교님과 그 아담한 라틴어 한 조각을 떠올려 보세요.”"]], [["“For Nathaniel then,”", "“그러면 나다니엘에게요.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith, boldly.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 담대하게 말했다."], ["“It would be a very good thing for him.”", "“그이에게 아주 좋은 일이 될 거예요.”"], ["And a slight smile came across her face as she said it.", "그렇게 말하면서 그녀의 얼굴에 옅은 웃음이 스쳤다. “자, 이건 정직하지요."], ["“Now that’s honest, or the mischief is in it.”", "그렇지 않다면 무슨 흉계가 든 것일 테고요.”"]], [["“Yes, that’s honest enough.", "“그래요, 그만하면 넉넉히 정직해요."], ["And did he send you here to tell me this?”", "그러면 그이가 이 말을 나에게 하라고 당신을 여기 보낸 거예요?”"]], [["“Well, he did that, and something else.”", "“그 일도 시켰고, 다른 것도 하나 있어요.”"]], [["“And now let’s have the something else.", "“그러면 그 다른 것을 들어 봅시다."], ["The really important part, I have no doubt, has been spoken.”", "정말로 중요한 대목은 이미 다 말해졌다고 나는 의심하지 않아요.”"]], [["“No, by no means, by no means all of it.", "“아니에요, 천만에요, 전부는 결코 아니에요."], ["But you are so hard on one, my dear, with your running after honesty, that one is not able to tell the real facts as they are.", "하지만 이봐요, 당신이 정직을 그렇게 쫓아다니며 사람을 몰아세우니, 있는 사실을 있는 그대로 말할 수가 없잖아요."], ["You make one speak in such a bald, naked way.”", "당신은 사람을 그토록 앙상하게, 알몸으로 말하게 만들어요.”"]], [["“Ah, you think that anything naked must be indecent; even truth.”", "“아, 알몸인 것은 무엇이든 상스럽다고 여기시는군요. 진실까지도요.”"]], [["“I think it is more proper-looking, and better suited, too, for the world’s work, when it goes about with some sort of a garment on it.", "“무슨 옷가지든 걸치고 돌아다닐 때가 더 점잖아 보이고 세상일에도 더 잘 맞는다고 나는 생각해요."], ["We are so used to a leaven of falsehood in all we hear and say, now-a-days, that nothing is more likely to deceive us than the absolute truth.", "요즘 우리는 듣고 말하는 모든 것에 거짓말이라는 누룩이 섞인 데 하도 익숙해져서, 있는 그대로의 진실만큼 우리를 속이기 쉬운 것이 없어요."], ["If a shopkeeper told me that his wares were simply middling, of course, I should think that they were not worth a farthing.", "가게 주인이 제 물건이 그저 그렇다고 말한다면 나는 물론 그 물건이 파딩 한 닢 값도 안 된다고 여길 거예요."], ["But all that has nothing to do with my poor brother.", "하지만 그런 이야기는 다 내 가엾은 오라비와는 아무 상관이 없지요."], ["Well, what was I saying?”", "그런데, 내가 무슨 말을 하고 있었죠?”"]], [["“You were going to tell me how well he would use me, no doubt.”", "“그이가 나를 얼마나 잘 대해 줄지 말하려는 참이었지요, 틀림없이.”"]], [["“Something of that kind.”", "“그런 쪽이었어요.”"]], [["“That he wouldn’t beat me; or spend all my money if I managed to have it tied up out of his power; or look down on me with contempt because my father was an apothecary!", "“그이가 나를 때리지는 않을 거라고, 내가 돈을 그이 손이 닿지 않게 묶어 두는 데 성공한다 해도 내 돈을 다 써 버리지는 않을 거라고, 내 아버지가 약제사였다는 이유로 나를 업신여기며 깔보지는 않을 거라고 말이지요!"], ["Was not that what you were going to say?”", "그게 하려던 말이 아니었나요?”"]], [["“I was going to tell you that you might be more happy as Mrs. Sowerby of Chaldicotes than you can be as Miss Dunstable—”", "“나는 당신이 던스터블 양으로 있는 것보다 콜디코츠의 사워비 부인으로 있는 편이 더 행복할지 모른다는 말을 하려던 참이었어요 —”"]], [["“Of Mount Lebanon. And had Mr.", "“레바논 산의 던스터블 양이지요."], ["Sowerby no other message to send?—nothing about love, or anything of that sort?", "그런데 사워비 씨가 보낸 전갈이 그것뿐인가요? 사랑이라든가 그런 종류의 무엇에 대한 말은 없고요?"], ["I should like, you know, to understand what his feelings are before I take such a leap.”", "알다시피 나는 그렇게 큰 뜀박질을 하기 전에 그이의 심정이 어떤지를 알아 두고 싶은데요.”"]], [["“I do believe he has as true a regard for you as any man of his age ever does have—”", "“그이가 그 나이의 남자가 여태 가져 본 만큼 참된 마음을 당신에게 두고 있다고 나는 정말로 믿어요 —”"]], [["“For any woman of mine.", "“내 나이쯤 되는 여자에게요."], ["That is not putting it in a very devoted way certainly; but I am glad to see that you remember the bishop’s maxim.”", "그야 참으로 애틋한 말투는 아니지요. 하지만 당신이 주교님의 금언을 잊지 않은 것은 반가워요.”"]], [["“What would you have me say?", "“그럼 나더러 무슨 말을 하라는 거예요?"], ["If I told you that he was dying for love, you would say, I was trying to cheat you; and now because I don’t tell you so, you say that he is wanting in devotion.", "그이가 사랑에 죽어 간다고 말했다면 당신은 내가 당신을 속이려 든다고 했겠지요. 그런데 이제 내가 그렇게 말하지 않으니 그이가 애틋함이 모자란다고 하시고요."], ["I must say you are hard to please.”", "당신은 참 만족시키기 어려운 분이라고 말해야겠네요.”"]], [["“Perhaps I am, and very unreasonable into the bargain.", "“어쩌면 그렇겠지요. 게다가 덧붙여 아주 이치에 닿지도 않겠지요."], ["I ought to ask no questions of the kind when your brother proposes to do me so much honour.", "당신 오라비가 나에게 그렇게 큰 영광을 베풀겠다고 하는데 그런 물음은 아예 하지 말아야 했어요."], ["As for my expecting the love of a man who condescends to wish to be my husband, that, of course, would be monstrous.", "나의 남편이 되기를 황송하게도 바라 주시는 남자에게서 사랑까지 기대하다니, 그야 물론 괴이한 일이지요."], ["What right can I have to think that any man should love me?", "어떤 남자가 나를 사랑해야 한다고 여길 무슨 권리가 내게 있겠어요?"], ["It ought to be enough for me to know that as I am rich, I can get a husband.", "나는 부자니까 남편을 하나 얻을 수 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로 넉넉해야 하지요."], ["What business can such as I have to inquire whether the gentleman who would so honour me really would like my company, or would only deign to put up with my presence in his household?”", "나에게 그런 영광을 베풀어 주시겠다는 신사분이 정말로 내 곁에 있기를 좋아할지, 아니면 자기 집안에 내가 있는 것을 마지못해 참아 주기만 할지를 캐물을 무슨 자격이 나 같은 사람에게 있겠어요?”"]], [["“Now, my dear Miss Dunstable—”", "“이봐요, 던스터블 양 —”"]], [["“Of course I am not such an ass as to expect that any gentleman should love me; and I feel that I ought to be obliged to your brother for sparing me the string of complimentary declarations which are usual on such occasions.", "“물론 나는 어떤 신사분이 나를 사랑해야 한다고 기대할 만큼 얼뜨기는 아니에요. 그리고 그런 자리에서 으레 줄줄이 늘어놓는 찬사를 나에게 면해 주신 것을 당신 오라비에게 고마워해야 한다는 느낌이 들어요."], ["He, at any rate, is not tedious—or rather you on his behalf; for no doubt his own time is so occupied with his parliamentary duties that he cannot attend to this little matter himself.", "어쨌든 그이는 지루하지가 않아요 — 아니, 그이를 대신한 당신이 그렇다고 해야겠지요. 그이 자신의 시간은 의회의 직무로 어찌나 가득 찼는지 이런 자잘한 일에는 직접 손을 댈 수도 없을 테니까요."], ["I do feel grateful to him; and perhaps nothing more will be necessary than to give him a schedule of the property, and name an early day for putting him in possession.”", "나는 정말로 그이에게 고마움을 느껴요. 그리고 어쩌면 재산 목록을 그이에게 건네주고 그이를 소유주로 앉힐 날을 이른 날짜로 잡아 주는 것 말고는 더 필요한 일이 없을지도 모르지요.”"]], [["Mrs. Smith did feel that she was rather badly used.", "스미스 부인은 자기가 어지간히 심한 대접을 받고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["This Miss Dunstable, in their mutual confidences, had so often ridiculed the love-making grimaces of her mercenary suitors—had spoken so fiercely against those who had persecuted her, not because they had desired her money, but on account of their ill-judgment in thinking her to be a fool—that Mrs. Smith had a right to expect that the method she had adopted for opening the negotiation would be taken in a better spirit.", "이 던스터블 양은 서로 속을 털어놓는 자리에서, 돈을 노린 구혼자들이 사랑을 꾸며 대며 짓는 낯짝을 그토록 자주 비웃었고 — 자기를 괴롭혀 온 자들을 두고 그토록 매섭게 말했는데, 그들이 자기 돈을 바랐기 때문이 아니라 자기를 바보로 여기는 그 어리석은 판단 때문이라고 했다 — 그러니 스미스 부인이 교섭을 여는 데 택한 방식이 더 나은 마음가짐으로 받아들여지기를 기대할 권리는 있었다."], ["Could it be possible, after all, thought Mrs. Smith to herself, that Miss Dunstable was like other women, and that she did like to have men kneeling at her feet?", "결국 던스터블 양도 다른 여자들과 마찬가지여서, 사내들이 제 발밑에 무릎 꿇는 것을 좋아하는 것일까, 스미스 부인은 속으로 생각했다."], ["Could it be the case that she had advised her brother badly, and that it would have been better for him to have gone about his work in the old-fashioned way?", "자기가 오라비에게 잘못 권한 것이고, 그이가 그 일을 옛날 방식대로 해 나가는 편이 더 나았을 것인가?"], ["“They are very hard to manage,”", "“여자들은 참 다루기 어렵지.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith to herself, thinking of her own sex.", "해럴드 스미스 부인은 제 성(性)을 떠올리며 혼자 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“He was coming here himself,”", "“그이가 직접 여기 오려고 했어요.”"], ["said she, “but I advised him not to do so.”", "그녀가 말했다. “하지만 그러지 말라고 내가 일렀지요.”"]], [["“That was so kind of you.”", "“참 고마운 일이었네요.”"]], [["“I thought that I could explain to you more openly and more freely, what his intentions really are.”", "“그이의 뜻이 정말 어떤 것인지를 내가 더 터놓고 더 거침없이 설명할 수 있으리라고 생각했거든요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["I have no doubt that they are honourable,”", "그 뜻이 떳떳하다는 것은 나도 의심하지 않아요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“He does not want to deceive me in that way, I am quite sure.”", "“그이가 그런 쪽으로 나를 속이려 하지는 않는다고 아주 확신해요.”"]], [["It was impossible to help laughing, and Mrs. Harold Smith did laugh.", "웃지 않고 있기란 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이어서 해럴드 스미스 부인은 웃어 버렸다."], ["“Upon my word, you would provoke a saint,”", "“참말이지, 당신은 성인도 노엽게 만들 사람이군요.”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I am not likely to get into any such company by the alliance that you are now suggesting to me.", "“당신이 지금 나에게 권하는 그 인연으로 내가 그런 부류와 어울리게 될 것 같지는 않아요."], ["There are not many saints usually at Chaldicotes, I believe;—always excepting my dear bishop and his wife.”", "콜디코츠에는 여느 때 성인이 그리 많지 않다고 나는 알고 있는데요. 늘 그러하듯 내 다정한 주교님과 그 부인은 빼고 말이에요.”"]], [["“But, my dear, what am I to say to Nathaniel?”", "“하지만 이봐요, 나다니엘에게는 무슨 말을 해야 하죠?”"]], [["“Tell him, of course, how much obliged to him I am.”", "“물론 내가 그이에게 얼마나 고마워하는지를 말해 주세요.”"]], [["“Do listen to me one moment.", "“한순간만 내 말을 들어 보세요."], ["I daresay that I have done wrong to speak to you in such a bold, unromantic way.”", "이렇게 담대하고 낭만이라고는 없는 방식으로 당신에게 말한 것이 잘못이었나 보군요.”"]], [["“Not at all.", "“전혀 아니에요."], ["The truth, the whole truth, and nothing but the truth.", "진실을, 진실 전부를, 진실 말고는 아무것도."], ["That’s what we agreed upon.", "그게 우리가 약조한 것이지요."], ["But one’s first efforts in any line are always apt to be a little uncouth.”", "하지만 어떤 길에서든 첫 시도는 늘 조금 어설프기 쉬운 법이에요.”"]], [["“I will send Nathaniel to you himself.”", "“나다니엘을 직접 당신에게 보내겠어요.”"]], [["“No, do not do so.", "“아니에요, 그러지 마세요."], ["Why torment either him or me?", "그이든 나든 왜 괴롭히나요?"], ["I do like your brother; in a certain way I like him much.", "나는 당신 오라비가 좋아요. 어떤 면으로는 아주 좋아요."], ["But no earthly consideration would induce me to marry him.", "하지만 이 땅의 어떤 사정으로도 나는 그이와 혼인하도록 이끌리지 않을 거예요."], ["Is it not so glaringly plain that he would marry me for my money only, that you have not even dared to suggest any other reason?”", "그이가 내 돈 때문에만 나와 혼인하려 한다는 것이 너무나 뻔해서, 당신조차 다른 이유는 감히 내놓아 보지도 못한 것이 아닌가요?”"]], [["“Of course it would have been nonsense to say that he had no regard whatever towards your money.”", "“그이가 당신 돈에 아무 마음도 두지 않았다고 말한다면 물론 터무니없는 소리였겠지요.”"]], [["“Of course it would—absolute nonsense.", "“물론 그랬겠지요 — 아주 터무니없는 소리였겠지요."], ["He is a poor man with a good position, and he wants to marry me because I have got that which he wants.", "그이는 좋은 지위를 지닌 가난한 사람이고, 자기가 원하는 것을 내가 가졌으니 나와 혼인하고 싶은 거예요."], ["But, my dear, I do not want that which he has got, and therefore the bargain would not be a fair one.”", "하지만 이봐요, 나는 그이가 가진 것을 원하지 않아요. 그러니 그 거래는 공평한 거래가 못 되지요.”"]], [["“But he would do his very best to make you happy.”", "“하지만 그이는 당신을 행복하게 해 주려고 있는 힘을 다할 거예요.”"]], [["“I am so much obliged to him; but you see, I am very happy as I am.", "“나는 그이에게 참 고마워요. 하지만 보시다시피 나는 지금 이대로 아주 행복해요."], ["What should I gain?”", "내가 얻을 것이 무엇이죠?”"]], [["“A companion whom you confess that you like.”", "“당신이 좋아한다고 스스로 밝힌 반려 하나요.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but I don’t know that I should like too much even of such a companion as your brother.", "하지만 당신 오라비 같은 반려라도 너무 많이 있으면 내가 좋아할지는 모르겠어요."], ["No, my dear—it won’t do.", "아니에요, 이봐요 — 그건 안 돼요."], ["Believe me when I tell you, once for all, that it won’t do.”", "내가 딱 한 번으로 다 말하는 거니까 믿어 주세요, 그건 안 돼요.”"]], [["“Do you mean, then, Miss Dunstable, that you’ll never marry?”", "“그러면 던스터블 양, 당신은 결코 혼인하지 않겠다는 말인가요?”"]], [["“To-morrow—if I met any one that I fancied, and he would have me.", "“내일이라도 하지요 — 마음에 드는 사람을 만나고 그 사람이 나를 받아 준다면요."], ["But I rather think that any that I may fancy won’t have me.", "하지만 내 마음에 들 만한 사람은 나를 받아 주지 않을 것 같아요."], ["In the first place, if I marry any one, the man must be quite indifferent to money.”", "우선 첫째로, 내가 누군가와 혼인한다면 그 사람은 돈에 아주 무심한 이여야 해요.”"]], [["“Then you’ll not find him in this world, my dear.”", "“그러면 이 세상에서는 그런 사람을 못 찾을 거예요, 이봐요.”"]], [["“Very possibly not,”", "“아주 그럴 법하지요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["All that was further said upon the subject need not be here repeated.", "이 일을 두고 그 뒤에 더 나온 말들을 여기 다 옮길 필요는 없다."], ["Mrs. Harold Smith did not give up her cause quite at once, although Miss Dunstable had spoken so plainly.", "던스터블 양이 그토록 분명하게 말했음에도 해럴드 스미스 부인은 제 뜻을 그렇게 단번에 접지는 않았다."], ["She tried to explain how eligible would be her friend’s situation as mistress of Chaldicotes, when Chaldicotes should owe no penny to any man: and went so far as to hint that the master of Chaldicotes, if relieved of his embarrassments and known as a rich man, might in all probability be found worthy of a peerage when the gods should return to Olympus.", "그녀는 콜디코츠가 어느 누구에게도 한 푼 빚지지 않게 되었을 때 그 벗이 콜디코츠의 여주인으로 앉게 될 처지가 얼마나 바람직한 것인지를 설명해 보려 했다. 그리고 콜디코츠의 주인이 그 궁색한 형편에서 벗어나 부유한 사람으로 알려지면, 신들이 올림포스로 돌아올 때에 귀족 작위를 받을 만하다는 인정을 받을 가망이 아주 크리라는 것까지 슬쩍 비쳤다."], ["Mr. Harold Smith, as a cabinet minister, would, of course, do his best.", "물론 내각의 각료인 해럴드 스미스 씨가 있는 힘을 다할 것이었다."], ["But it was all of no use.", "그러나 다 아무 쓸모가 없었다."], ["“It’s not my destiny,”", "“그건 내 팔자가 아니에요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, “and therefore do not press it any longer.”", "던스터블 양이 말했다. “그러니 더는 밀지 마세요.”"]], [["“But we shall not quarrel,”", "“하지만 우리가 다투지는 않겠지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith, almost tenderly.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 거의 애틋하게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no—why should we quarrel?”", "“아, 아니지요. 우리가 왜 다투겠어요?”"]], [["“And you won’t look glum at my brother?”", "“그리고 내 오라비를 시무룩한 얼굴로 대하지도 않겠지요?”"]], [["“Why should I look glum at him?", "“내가 왜 그이를 시무룩한 얼굴로 대하겠어요?"], ["But, Mrs. Smith, I’ll do more than not looking glum at him.", "하지만 스미스 부인, 나는 그이를 시무룩한 얼굴로 대하지 않는 것보다 더한 일도 하겠어요."], ["I do like you, and I do like your brother, and if I can in any moderate way assist him in his difficulties, let him tell me so.”", "나는 당신이 좋고 당신 오라비도 좋아요. 그러니 내가 어떤 알맞은 방도로든 그이의 어려움을 도울 수 있다면 그렇게 말해 달라고 하세요.”"]], [["Soon after this, Mrs.", "그 뒤 얼마 지나지 않아 해럴드 스미스 부인은 자리를 떴다."], ["Harold Smith went her way.", "물론 그녀는 오라비가 던스터블 양에게서 그런 금전상의 도움을 받는다는 생각은 할 수도 없다고 아주 강한 태도로 선언했다."], ["Of course, she declared in a very strong manner that her brother could not think of accepting from Miss Dunstable any such pecuniary assistance as that offered—and, to give her her due, such was the feeling of her mind at the moment; but as she went to meet her brother and gave him an account of this interview, it did occur to her that possibly Miss Dunstable might be a better creditor than the Duke of Omnium for the Chaldicotes property.", "그리고 그녀에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려 말하자면, 그 순간 그녀의 마음이 정말로 그러했다. 그러나 오라비를 만나러 가서 이 만남의 이야기를 그에게 들려주는 동안, 콜디코츠 재산을 두고 어쩌면 던스터블 양이 옴니엄 공작보다 더 나은 채권자일 수도 있겠다는 생각이 그녀에게 떠오르기는 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXV. NON-IMPULSIVE.", "# 제25장. 충동이 없는 사람"]], [["It cannot be held as astonishing, that that last decision on the part of the giants in the matter of the two bishoprics should have disgusted Archdeacon Grantly.", "두 주교좌 문제를 두고 거인들이 마지막에 내린 그 결정이 그랜틀리 부주교의 속을 뒤집어 놓았다는 것은 놀랄 일로 여길 수 없다."], ["He was a politician, but not a politician as they were.", "그는 정치가였으나 그들과 같은 정치가는 아니었다."], ["As is the case with all exoteric men, his political eyes saw a short way only, and his political aspirations were as limited.", "모든 문외한이 그렇듯이 그의 정치적인 눈은 짧은 데까지밖에 보지 못했고, 그의 정치적인 소망도 그만큼 좁았다."], ["When his friends came into office, that bishop bill, which as the original product of his enemies had been regarded by him as being so pernicious—for was it not about to be made law in order that other Proudies and such like might be hoisted up into high places and large incomes, to the terrible detriment of the Church?—that bishop bill, I say, in the hands of his friends, had appeared to him to be a means of almost national salvation.", "자기 벗들이 관직에 들어앉자, 애초에 원수들이 내놓은 산물이었을 적에는 그토록 해롭다고 여겨졌던 그 주교 법안이—또 다른 프라우디네 같은 자들을 높은 자리와 큰 수입으로 끌어올려 교회에 끔찍한 손해를 입히려고 법으로 만들려던 것이 아니었던가?—그 주교 법안이, 내 말하건대, 벗들의 손에 들어가자 그에게는 거의 나라를 건지는 방편처럼 보이게 되었다."], ["And then, how great had been the good fortune of the giants in this matter!", "게다가 이 일에서 거인들의 운수는 얼마나 좋았던가!"], ["Had they been the originators of such a measure they would not have had a chance of success; but now—now that the two bishops were falling into their mouths out of the weak hands of the gods, was not their success ensured?", "그들이 그런 법안을 처음 내놓은 자들이었다면 성공할 낌새조차 없었을 것이다. 그러나 이제—이제 두 주교가 신들의 힘없는 손에서 떨어져 그들의 입안으로 굴러 들어오고 있으니, 그들의 성공은 이미 확보된 것이 아니었던가?"], ["So Dr. Grantly had girded up his loins and marched up to the fight, almost regretting that the triumph would be so easy.", "그리하여 그랜틀리 박사는 허리띠를 동여매고 싸움터로 올라갔고, 승리가 너무 쉬울 것이라는 점을 거의 아쉬워할 지경이었다."], ["The subsequent failure was very trying to his temper as a party man.", "그 뒤에 온 실패는 당파 사람으로서의 그의 성미를 몹시 시험했다."]], [["It always strikes me that the supporters of the Titans are in this respect much to be pitied.", "내게는 늘 티탄들을 받드는 자들이야말로 이 점에서 크게 가엾이 여길 만하다는 생각이 든다."], ["The giants themselves, those who are actually handling Pelion and breaking their shins over the lower rocks of Ossa, are always advancing in some sort towards the councils of Olympus.", "거인들 자신, 곧 실제로 펠리온을 들어 옮기며 오사의 아래쪽 바위에 정강이를 까는 자들은 어떤 식으로든 언제나 올림포스의 회의를 향해 나아가고 있다. 그들의 가장 높은 방책은 하늘에서 빛줄기 하나를 낚아채는 것이다."], ["Their highest policy is to snatch some ray from heaven.", "그렇지 않다면 무엇 때문에 오사 위에 펠리온을 얹겠는가?"], ["Why else put Pelion on Ossa, unless it be that a furtive hand, making its way through Jove’s windows, may pluck forth a thunderbolt or two, or some article less destructive, but of manufacture equally divine?", "몰래 뻗은 손이 유피테르의 창을 뚫고 들어가 벼락 한둘을, 아니면 그보다 덜 파괴적이나 만든 솜씨는 똑같이 신다운 물건 하나를 뽑아 낼 수도 있으리라는 것 말고 무슨 이유가 있겠는가?"], ["And in this consists the wisdom of the higher giants—that, in spite of their mundane antecedents, theories, and predilections, they can see that articles of divine manufacture are necessary.", "그리고 높은 자리에 있는 거인들의 지혜란 바로 여기에 있다—세속에서 온 내력과 이론과 기호가 있음에도, 신의 솜씨로 만든 물건이 반드시 필요하다는 것을 그들은 볼 줄 안다는 것."], ["But then they never carry their supporters with them.", "그러나 그들은 자기를 받드는 자들을 결코 함께 데려가지 않는다."], ["Their whole army is an army of martyrs.", "그들의 군대는 통째로 순교자의 군대다."], ["“For twenty years I have stuck to them, and see how they have treated me!”", "“스무 해를 그들에게 붙어 있었는데, 그들이 나를 어떻게 대했는지 보시오!”"], ["Is not that always the plaint of an old giant-slave?", "늙은 거인의 종이 늘 늘어놓는 하소연이 그것 아닌가?"], ["“I have been true to my party all my life, and where am I now?”", "“나는 평생 우리 당에 참되게 굴었소. 그런데 지금 내가 어디에 있소?”"], ["he says. Where, indeed, my friend?", "그는 그렇게 말한다."], ["Looking about you, you begin to learn that you cannot describe your whereabouts.", "어디에 있는가, 정말이지, 나의 벗이여? 둘레를 살펴보다가 그대는 제가 어디에 있는지조차 말할 수 없다는 것을 알기 시작한다."], ["I do not marvel at that.", "나는 그것을 이상하게 여기지 않는다."], ["No one finds himself planted at last in so terribly foul a morass, as he would fain stand still for ever on dry ground.", "마른 땅 위에 영원히 가만히 서 있고자 하는 사람만큼, 끝에 가서 그토록 끔찍하게 더러운 늪에 처박히는 이는 없다."]], [["Dr. Grantly was disgusted; and although he was himself too true and thorough in all his feelings, to be able to say aloud that any Giant was wrong, still he had a sad feeling within his heart that the world was sinking from under him.", "그랜틀리 박사는 속이 뒤집혔다."], ["He was still sufficiently exoteric to think that a good stand-up fight in a good cause was a good thing.", "그리고 그 자신은 무슨 느낌에서든 너무 참되고 철저한 사람이어서 어느 거인이 틀렸다고 소리 내어 말할 수는 없었으나, 그래도 마음속에는 세상이 제 발밑에서 내려앉고 있다는 슬픈 느낌이 있었다."], ["No doubt he did wish to be Bishop of Westminster, and was anxious to compass that preferment by any means that might appear to him to be fair.", "그는 여전히 넉넉히 문외한이었으므로, 좋은 명분을 걸고 정면으로 맞붙는 좋은 싸움 한판은 좋은 것이라고 생각했다. 물론 그는 웨스트민스터 주교가 되고 싶었고, 자기 눈에 온당해 보이는 어떤 방편으로든 그 성직 영전을 손에 넣기를 바라고 있었다."], ["And why not?", "그러면 안 되는가?"], ["But this was not the end of his aspirations.", "그러나 그의 소망은 거기서 끝나지 않았다."], ["He wished that the giants might prevail in everything, in bishoprics as in all other matters; and he could not understand that they should give way on the very first appearance of a skirmish.", "그는 거인들이 주교좌에서든 다른 모든 일에서든 모든 것에서 이기기를 바랐다. 그리고 접전의 낌새가 처음 보이자마자 그들이 물러난다는 것을 그는 이해할 수가 없었다."], ["In his open talk he was loud against many a god; but in his heart of hearts he was bitter enough against both Porphyrion and Orion.", "드러내 놓고 하는 말에서는 여러 신을 향해 목소리를 높였으나, 마음속 가장 깊은 데서는 포르피리온과 오리온 둘 다에게 넉넉히 쓰디쓴 마음을 품고 있었다."]], [["“My dear doctor, it would not do;—not in this session; it would not indeed.”", "“박사님, 그건 될 일이 아니었습니다.—이번 회기에는 아니지요. 정말로 될 일이 아니었어요.”"], ["So had spoken to him a half-fledged but especially esoteric young monster-cub at the Treasury, who considered himself as up to all the dodges of his party, and regarded the army of martyrs who supported it as a rather heavy, but very useful collection of fogeys.", "재무부에서 털도 채 나지 않았으나 내막에는 유별나게 통한 젊은 괴물 새끼가 그에게 그렇게 말했다. 그 새끼는 제가 제 당의 온갖 수를 다 안다고 여겼고, 당을 받쳐 주는 순교자의 군대를 꽤 둔중하나 아주 쓸모 있는 늙다리 무리로 보고 있었다."], ["Dr. Grantly had not cared to discuss the matter with the half-fledged monster-cub.", "그랜틀리 박사는 털도 채 나지 않은 그 괴물 새끼와 이 문제를 따져 볼 마음이 없었다."], ["The best licked of all the monsters, the Giant most like a god of them all, had said a word or two to him; and he also had said a word or two to that Giant.", "모든 괴물 가운데 가장 잘 다듬어진 자, 그들 가운데 신을 가장 닮은 그 거인이 그에게 한두 마디를 했고, 그도 그 거인에게 한두 마디를 했다."], ["Porphyrion had told him that the bishop bill would not do; and he, in return, speaking with warm face, and blood in his cheeks, had told Porphyrion that he saw no reason why the bill should not do.", "포르피리온은 주교 법안이 될 일이 아니라고 그에게 말했고, 그는 그에 답하여 얼굴이 달아오르고 뺨에 피가 몰린 채로, 그 법안이 되지 못할 이유를 하나도 보지 못하겠다고 포르피리온에게 말했다."], ["The courteous Giant had smiled as he shook his ponderous head, and then the archdeacon had left him, unconsciously shaking some dust from his shoes, as he paced the passages of the Treasury chambers for the last time.", "정중한 거인은 그 무거운 머리를 흔들며 미소를 지었고, 그러고서 부주교는 그를 떠나, 재무부 청사의 복도를 마지막으로 걸어 나오면서 저도 모르게 신발에서 먼지를 조금 떨어 내었다."], ["As he walked back to his lodgings in Mount Street, many thoughts, not altogether bad in their nature, passed through his mind.", "마운트 스트리트의 하숙으로 걸어 돌아가는 동안 그의 머릿속으로 여러 가지 생각이, 그 성질이 아주 나쁘지만은 않은 생각들이 지나갔다."], ["Why should he trouble himself about a bishopric?", "무엇 때문에 주교좌를 놓고 제 속을 썩여야 하는가?"], ["Was he not well as he was, in his rectory down at Plumstead?", "저 아래 플럼스테드의 주임 사제관에서 지금 이대로도 넉넉히 좋지 않은가?"], ["Might it not be ill for him at his age to transplant himself into new soil, to engage in new duties, and live among new people?", "이 나이에 저를 새 흙에 옮겨 심고, 새 직무를 맡고, 새 사람들 사이에서 사는 것이 오히려 해롭지 않겠는가?"], ["Was he not useful at Barchester, and respected also; and might it not be possible, that up there at Westminster, he might be regarded merely as a tool with which other men could work?", "바체스터에서 그는 쓸모가 있고 또 존경도 받고 있지 않은가. 그런데 저 위 웨스트민스터에서는, 다른 사람들이 일을 부리는 데 쓰는 연장쯤으로만 여겨지게 될 수도 있지 않겠는가?"], ["He had not quite liked the tone of that specially esoteric young monster-cub, who had clearly regarded him as a distinguished fogey from the army of martyrs.", "그는 그 유별나게 내막에 통한 젊은 괴물 새끼의 말투가 아무래도 마음에 들지 않았다. 그 새끼는 그를 순교자의 군대에서 나온 이름난 늙다리로 뻔히 보고 있었다."], ["He would take his wife back to Barsetshire, and there live contented with the good things which Providence had given him.", "그는 아내를 데리고 바셋주로 돌아가, 섭리가 그에게 내려 준 좋은 것들로 만족하며 거기서 살겠다고 마음먹었다."]], [["Those high political grapes had become sour, my sneering friends will say. Well?", "저 높은 데 달린 정치의 포도가 시어졌다고, 비웃기 좋아하는 벗들은 말할 것이다."], ["Is it not a good thing that grapes should become sour which hang out of reach?", "그래서? 손이 닿지 않는 데 매달린 포도가 시어지는 것은 좋은 일이 아닌가?"], ["Is he not wise who can regard all grapes as sour which are manifestly too high for his hand?", "제 손보다 훤히 높은 데 있는 포도를 모두 시다고 볼 줄 아는 이는 지혜롭지 않은가?"], ["Those grapes of the Treasury bench, for which gods and giants fight, suffering so much when they are forced to abstain from eating, and so much more when they do eat,—those grapes are very sour to me.", "신들과 거인들이 다투어 얻으려는 저 여당 석의 포도, 못 먹고 참아야 할 때 그토록 괴롭고 먹고 나면 더욱 괴로운 저 포도는, 내게는 아주 시다."], ["I am sure that they are indigestible, and that those who eat them undergo all the ills which the Revalenta Arabica is prepared to cure.", "나는 그것이 소화가 되지 않는 것이며, 그것을 먹은 이들이 레발렌타 아라비카가 고쳐 주겠다고 나서는 온갖 병을 다 겪는다고 확신한다."], ["And so it was now with the archdeacon.", "지금 부주교의 처지도 그러했다."], ["He thought of the strain which would have been put on his conscience had he come up there to sit in London as Bishop of Westminster; and in this frame of mind he walked home to his wife.", "그는 저 위 런던에 올라와 웨스트민스터 주교로 앉았을 때 제 양심에 얹혔을 짐을 생각했다. 그런 마음가짐으로 그는 아내에게 걸어 돌아갔다."]], [["During the first few moments of his interview with her all his regrets had come back upon him.", "아내와 마주 앉은 처음 몇 순간 동안은 아쉬움이 모두 되살아났다."], ["Indeed, it would have hardly suited for him then to have preached this new doctrine of rural contentment.", "아니, 그때 그가 이 새로운 시골살이 만족의 교리를 늘어놓았다면 도무지 어울리지 않았을 것이다."], ["The wife of his bosom, whom he so fully trusted—had so fully loved—wished for grapes that hung high upon the wall, and he knew that it was past his power to teach her at the moment to drop her ambition.", "그가 그토록 온전히 믿었고—그토록 온전히 사랑했던 품속의 아내는 담장 높이 매달린 포도를 원하고 있었고, 그 순간에 그녀에게 야심을 내려놓으라고 가르치는 것은 제 힘에 넘치는 일임을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["Any teaching that he might effect in that way, must come by degrees.", "그가 그런 쪽으로 무엇을 가르칠 수 있다 해도 그것은 차츰차츰 되어야 했다."], ["But before many minutes were over he had told her of her fate and of his own decision.", "그러나 몇 분이 지나기도 전에 그는 아내의 운명과 자기의 결정을 그녀에게 말했다."], ["“So we had better go back to Plumstead,”", "“그러니 우리는 플럼스테드로 돌아가는 게 좋겠소,”"], ["he said; and she had not dissented.", "그가 말했다. 그리고 그녀는 반대하지 않았다."]], [["“I am sorry for poor Griselda’s sake,” Mrs.", "“가여운 그리젤다를 생각하면 안타까워요,”"], ["Grantly had remarked later in the evening, when they were again together.", "그날 저녁 늦게 두 사람이 다시 마주 앉았을 때 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."]], [["“But I thought she was to remain with Lady Lufton?”", "“하지만 그 아이는 레이디 러프턴 곁에 남는 것으로 알았소만?”"]], [["“Well; so she will, for a little time.", "“그야 그럴 거예요, 얼마 동안은요."], ["There is no one with whom I would so soon trust her out of my own care as with Lady Lufton.", "제 손을 떠나 그 아이를 맡길 사람으로 레이디 러프턴만 한 이는 없지요."], ["She is all that one can desire.”", "그분은 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 갖춘 분이에요.”"]], [["“Exactly; and as far as Griselda is concerned, I cannot say that I think she is to be pitied.”", "“그렇지. 그러니 그리젤다에 관해서라면, 나는 그 아이를 가엾게 여길 이유가 있다고는 생각지 않소.”"]], [["“Not to be pitied, perhaps,”", "“가엾게 여길 일은 아니겠지요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“But, you see, archdeacon, Lady Lufton, of course, has her own views.”", "“하지만 부주교님, 레이디 러프턴께는 물론 그분만의 뜻이 있잖아요.”"]], [["“Her own views?”", "“그분만의 뜻이라니?”"]], [["“It is hardly any secret that she is very anxious to make a match between Lord Lufton and Griselda.", "“레이디 러프턴이 러프턴 경과 그리젤다를 맺어 주려고 몹시 애쓰고 계시다는 건 이제 비밀이라고 할 것도 없어요."], ["And though that might be a very proper arrangement if it were fixed—”", "그리고 그것이 정해진 일이라면 아주 온당한 짝짓기이겠지만—”"]], [["“Lord Lufton marry Griselda!”", "“러프턴 경이 그리젤다와 혼인을 한다고!”"], ["said the archdeacon, speaking quick and raising his eyebrows.", "부주교가 빠르게 말하며 눈썹을 치켜올렸다."], ["His mind had as yet been troubled by but few thoughts respecting his child’s future establishment.", "그때까지 딸이 앞으로 자리를 잡는 문제를 두고 그의 머리를 어지럽힌 생각은 몇 가지 되지 않았다."], ["“I had never dreamt of such a thing.”", "“그런 일은 꿈에도 생각해 본 적이 없소.”"]], [["“But other people have done more than dream of it, archdeacon.", "“하지만 다른 사람들은 꿈꾸는 것보다 더한 일을 해 놓았어요, 부주교님."], ["As regards the match itself, it would, I think, be unobjectionable.", "혼사 자체로 보자면 저는 흠잡을 데가 없다고 생각해요."], ["Lord Lufton will not be a very rich man, but his property is respectable, and as far as I can learn his character is on the whole good.", "러프턴 경이 아주 부유한 사람이 되지는 않겠지만 재산은 남 보기에 번듯하고, 제가 알아본 바로는 사람됨도 대체로 좋아요."], ["If they like each other, I should be contented with such a marriage.", "두 사람이 서로를 좋아한다면 저는 그런 혼인으로 만족하겠어요."], ["But, I must own, I am not quite satisfied at the idea of leaving her all alone with Lady Lufton.", "하지만 그 아이를 레이디 러프턴 곁에 저 혼자 남겨 두고 간다는 생각에는 마음이 다 놓이지 않는다고 털어놓아야겠네요."], ["People will look on it as a settled thing, when it is not settled—and very probably may not be settled; and that will do the poor girl harm.", "사람들은 정해지지도 않은 일을, 아주 그럴듯하게 끝내 정해지지 않을 수도 있는 일을 이미 정해진 일로 볼 거예요. 그러면 그 가여운 아이가 해를 입지요."], ["She is very much admired; there can be no doubt of that; and Lord Dumbello—”", "그 아이가 찬탄을 많이 받고 있다는 건 의심할 여지가 없어요. 그리고 덤벨로 경도—”"]], [["The archdeacon opened his eyes still wider.", "부주교는 눈을 더욱 크게 떴다."], ["He had had no idea that such a choice of sons-in-law was being prepared for him; and, to tell the truth, was almost bewildered by the height of his wife’s ambition.", "그런 사윗감을 골라잡을 여지가 저를 위해 마련되고 있다는 것을 그는 짐작조차 못 했고, 사실을 말하자면 아내의 야심이 다다른 높이에 거의 얼떨떨해졌다."], ["Lord Lufton, with his barony and twenty thousand a year, might be accepted as just good enough; but failing him there was an embryo marquis, whose fortune would be more than ten times as great, all ready to accept his child!", "남작 작위와 연 이만 파운드를 지닌 러프턴 경이라면 겨우 넉넉히 좋다고 받아들일 만하겠지만, 그가 아니라면 재산이 그 열 배도 넘을 알 속의 후작이 제 딸을 받아 갈 준비를 하고 서 있다니!"], ["And then he thought, as husbands sometimes will think, of Susan Harding as she was when he had gone a-courting to her under the elms before the house in the warden’s garden at Barchester, and of dear old Mr.", "그러고서 그는, 남편들이 때때로 그러듯이, 바체스터의 워든 관사 정원, 그 집 앞 느릅나무 아래로 구애하러 다니던 시절의 수전 하딩을 떠올렸고, 아직도 그 도시의 조촐한 하숙에 살고 있는 아내의 아버지, 다정한 노인 하딩 씨를 떠올렸다."], ["Harding, his wife’s father, who still lived in humble lodgings in that city; and as he thought, he wondered at and admired the greatness of that lady’s mind.", "그리고 그렇게 떠올리면서 그 부인의 마음이 지닌 웅대함에 놀라며 감탄했다."]], [["“I never can forgive Lord De Terrier,”", "“저는 드 테리어 경을 결코 용서할 수 없어요,”"], ["said the lady, connecting various points together in her own mind.", "속으로 여러 가지를 이어 붙이며 부인이 말했다."]], [["“That’s nonsense,”", "“터무니없는 소리요,”"], ["said the archdeacon.", "부주교가 말했다."], ["“You must forgive him.”", "“용서해야 하오.”"]], [["“And I must confess that it annoys me to leave London at present.”", "“그리고 지금 런던을 떠나야 한다는 게 저로서는 성가시다고 털어놓아야겠어요.”"]], [["“It can’t be helped,”", "“어쩔 수 없는 일이오,”"], ["said the archdeacon, somewhat gruffly; for he was a man who, on certain points, chose to have his own way—and had it.", "부주교가 다소 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. 그는 어떤 대목에서는 제 뜻대로 하기를 택하는 사람이었고—또 그렇게 했다."]], [["“Oh, no: I know it can’t be helped,”", "“아, 예. 어쩔 수 없는 일인 줄 알아요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly, in a tone which implied a deep injury.", "깊이 상처를 입었음을 내비치는 어조로 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“I know it can’t be helped.", "“어쩔 수 없는 일인 줄 알아요."], ["Poor Griselda!”", "가여운 그리젤다!”"], ["And then they went to bed.", "그러고서 두 사람은 자리에 들었다."]], [["On the next morning Griselda came to her, and in an interview that was strictly private, her mother said more to her than she had ever yet spoken, as to the prospects of her future life.", "이튿날 아침 그리젤다가 어머니에게 왔고, 철저히 남의 눈을 피한 만남에서 어머니는 딸이 앞으로 살아갈 일의 전망에 대하여 여태껏 해 본 적 없는 만큼 많은 말을 했다."], ["Hitherto, on this subject, Mrs. Grantly had said little or nothing.", "이 주제를 놓고 그랜틀리 부인은 그때까지 거의 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She would have been well pleased that her daughter should have received the incense of Lord Lufton’s vows—or, perhaps, as well pleased had it been the incense of Lord Dumbello’s vows—without any interference on her part.", "딸이 러프턴 경이 바치는 언약의 향을 자기가 끼어드는 일 없이 받게 되었다면 그녀는 아주 흐뭇했을 것이다—아니, 그것이 덤벨로 경이 바치는 언약의 향이었더라도 어쩌면 그만큼 흐뭇했을 것이다."], ["In such case her child, she knew, would have told her with quite sufficient eagerness, and the matter in either case would have been arranged as a very pretty love match.", "그런 경우라면 제 딸이 넉넉히 애타는 마음으로 자기에게 알려 왔으리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었고, 어느 쪽이든 그 일은 아주 예쁜 연애 혼사로 정리되었을 것이다."], ["She had no fear of any impropriety or of any rashness on Griselda’s part.", "그리젤다 쪽에서 어떤 온당하지 못한 짓이나 어떤 무모한 짓을 할까 걱정하는 마음은 조금도 없었다."], ["She had thoroughly known her daughter when she boasted that Griselda would never indulge in an unauthorized passion.", "그리젤다는 허락받지 않은 연정에 결코 몸을 맡기지 않을 아이라고 자랑했을 때 그녀는 제 딸을 철저히 알고 있었던 것이다."], ["But as matters now stood, with those two strings to her bow, and with that Lufton-Grantly alliance treaty in existence—of which she, Griselda herself, knew nothing—might it not be possible that the poor child should stumble through want of adequate direction?", "그러나 지금 형편으로는, 그 활에 걸린 시위가 둘이고, 게다가 러프턴-그랜틀리 동맹 조약이—그리젤다 자신은 아무것도 모르는 그 조약이—버젓이 있는 터에, 마땅한 지침이 없어 그 가여운 아이가 발을 헛디딜 수도 있지 않겠는가?"], ["Guided by these thoughts, Mrs. Grantly had resolved to say a few words before she left London.", "이런 생각에 이끌려 그랜틀리 부인은 런던을 떠나기 전에 몇 마디 해 두기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["So she wrote a line to her daughter, and Griselda reached Mount Street at two o’clock in Lady Lufton’s carriage, which, during the interview, waited for her at the beer-shop round the corner.", "그리하여 딸에게 짧은 글을 써 보냈고, 그리젤다는 두 시에 레이디 러프턴의 마차를 타고 마운트 스트리트에 이르렀다. 마차는 만남이 이어지는 동안 길 모퉁이 맥주 가게에서 그녀를 기다렸다."]], [["“And papa won’t be Bishop of Westminster?”", "“그럼 아버지는 웨스트민스터 주교가 되지 못하는 거예요?”"], ["said the young lady, when the doings of the giants had been sufficiently explained to make her understand that all those hopes were over.", "거인들이 한 일이 그 모든 희망이 끝났음을 알아들을 만큼 넉넉히 설명된 뒤에 젊은 아가씨가 말했다."]], [["“No, my dear; at any rate not now.”", "“그렇단다, 얘야. 적어도 지금은 아니야.”"]], [["“What a shame!", "“정말 창피한 일이에요!"], ["I thought it was all settled.", "저는 다 정해진 줄 알았어요."], ["What’s the good, mamma, of Lord De Terrier being prime minister, if he can’t make whom he likes a bishop?”", "어머니, 드 테리어 경이 총리가 된 게 무슨 소용이에요, 마음에 드는 사람을 주교로 만들지도 못한다면?”"]], [["“I don’t think that Lord De Terrier has behaved at all well to your father.", "“드 테리어 경이 네 아버지에게 조금이라도 잘 처신했다고는 생각하지 않는다."], ["However, that’s a long question, and we can’t go into it now.”", "하지만 그건 긴 이야기라서 지금 파고들 수는 없구나.”"]], [["“How glad those Proudies will be!”", "“그 프라우디네 사람들이 얼마나 기뻐할까요!”"]], [["Griselda would have talked by the hour on this subject had her mother allowed her, but it was necessary that Mrs.", "어머니가 그냥 두었더라면 그리젤다는 이 주제를 놓고 몇 시간이고 이야기했을 것이나, 그랜틀리 부인은 다른 문제로 넘어가야 했다. 그녀는 레이디 러프턴 이야기로 시작하여 그 마님이 얼마나 다정한 분인지를 말했고, 이어서 벗이자 안주인인 그분이 함께 런던에 머물러 있기에 알맞은 동안은 그리젤다도 런던에 남아 있으라고 말했다."], ["Grantly should go to other matters.", "다만 그것이 아마 그리 오래지는 않을 것이라고 덧붙였다."], ["She began about Lady Lufton, saying what a dear woman her ladyship was; and then went on to say that Griselda was to remain in London as long as it suited her friend and hostess to stay there with her; but added, that this might probably not be very long, as it was notorious that Lady Lufton, when in London, was always in a hurry to get back to Framley.", "레이디 러프턴이 런던에 있을 때면 늘 프램리로 돌아가려고 서두른다는 것은 널리 알려진 일이었으니 말이다."]], [["“But I don’t think she is in such a hurry this year, mamma,”", "“하지만 올해는 그렇게 서두르지 않으시는 것 같아요, 어머니,”"], ["said Griselda, who in the month of May preferred Bruton Street to Plumstead, and had no objection whatever to the coronet on the panels of Lady Lufton’s coach.", "오월에는 플럼스테드보다 브루턴 스트리트가 좋고 레이디 러프턴 마차 문짝에 박힌 귀족 관에도 아무런 불만이 없던 그리젤다가 말했다."]], [["And then Mrs. Grantly commenced her explanation—very cautiously.", "그러고서 그랜틀리 부인이 설명을 시작했다—아주 조심스럽게."], ["“No, my dear, I daresay she is not in such a hurry this year,—that is, as long as you remain with her.”", "“그래, 얘야, 올해는 그리 서두르지 않으실 게다.—그러니까, 네가 그분과 함께 있는 동안은 말이지.”"]], [["“I am sure she is very kind.”", "“그분은 참 친절하세요.”"]], [["“She is very kind, and you ought to love her very much.", "“아주 친절하시지. 너도 그분을 많이 사랑해야 한다."], ["I know I do.", "나는 그러고 있단다."], ["I have no friend in the world for whom I have a greater regard than for Lady Lufton.", "이 세상에 레이디 러프턴보다 더 큰 마음을 두고 있는 벗은 없어."], ["It is that which makes me so happy to leave you with her.”", "그래서 너를 그분께 맡기고 가는 것이 이토록 마음 편한 거야.”"]], [["“All the same, I wish that you and papa had remained up; that is, if they had made papa a bishop.”", "“그래도 어머니와 아버지가 그냥 올라와 계셨으면 좋았을 텐데요. 그러니까, 아버지를 주교로 만들어 주었더라면요.”"]], [["“It’s no good thinking of that now, my dear.", "“이제 그걸 생각해 봐야 아무 소용이 없다, 얘야."], ["What I particularly wanted to say to you was this: I think you should know what are the ideas which Lady Lufton entertains.”", "내가 너에게 특별히 하고 싶었던 말은 이거야. 레이디 러프턴이 어떤 생각을 품고 계신지 너도 알아 두어야 한다고 본다.”"]], [["“Her ideas!”", "“그분의 생각이라니!”"], ["said Griselda, who had never troubled herself much in thinking about other people’s thoughts.", "남이 무슨 생각을 하는지 헤아리는 일로 애를 써 본 적이 별로 없는 그리젤다가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Griselda.", "“그렇다, 그리젤다."], ["While you were staying down at Framley Court, and also, I suppose, since you have been up here in Bruton Street, you must have seen a good deal of—Lord Lufton.”", "프램리 코트에 내려가 지내는 동안에도, 또 여기 브루턴 스트리트로 올라온 뒤에도 그랬으리라 짐작하지만, 너는—러프턴 경을 꽤 자주 보았을 게다.”"]], [["“He doesn’t come very often to Bruton Street,—that is to say, not very often.”", "“브루턴 스트리트에는 아주 자주 오지는 않으세요.—그러니까, 아주 자주는 아니라는 말이에요.”"]], [["“H-m,”", "“흐음,”"], ["ejaculated Mrs. Grantly, very gently.", "그랜틀리 부인이 아주 나직이 내뱉었다."], ["She would willingly have repressed the sound altogether, but it had been too much for her.", "그녀는 그 소리를 아예 눌러 두었으면 했으나 그것은 그녀에게 너무 벅찬 일이었다."], ["If she found reason to think that Lady Lufton was playing her false, she would immediately take her daughter away, break up the treaty, and prepare for the Hartletop alliance.", "레이디 러프턴이 저를 속이고 있다고 여길 만한 근거를 찾아낸다면 당장에 딸을 데려가고, 조약을 깨고, 하틀탑 쪽과의 결합을 준비할 것이었다."], ["Such were the thoughts that ran through her mind.", "그녀의 머릿속으로 지나간 생각이 그런 것이었다."], ["But she knew all the while that Lady Lufton was not false.", "그러나 그 내내 그녀는 레이디 러프턴이 속이는 사람이 아님을 알고 있었다."], ["The fault was not with Lady Lufton; nor, perhaps, altogether with Lord Lufton. Mrs.", "잘못은 레이디 러프턴에게 있지 않았고, 어쩌면 러프턴 경에게도 온전히 있지는 않았다."], ["Grantly had understood the full force of the complaint which Lady Lufton had made against her daughter; and though she had of course defended her child, and on the whole had defended her successfully, yet she confessed to herself that Griselda’s chance of a first-rate establishment would be better if she were a little more impulsive.", "레이디 러프턴이 제 딸을 두고 늘어놓았던 불평의 무게를 그랜틀리 부인은 다 알아들었다. 그리고 물론 제 아이를 감싸 주었고 대체로는 감싸는 데 성공했으나, 그리젤다가 조금 더 충동적이라면 일등으로 자리를 잡을 기회가 더 나아지리라는 것은 스스로에게 시인했다."], ["A man does not wish to marry a statue, let the statue be ever so statuesque.", "사내는 조각상과 혼인하고 싶어 하지 않는다. 그 조각상이 아무리 조각상답게 훌륭하다 해도 그렇다."], ["She could not teach her daughter to be impulsive, any more than she could teach her to be six feet high; but might it not be possible to teach her to seem so?", "딸에게 충동적이 되라고 가르칠 수 없는 것은 키가 여섯 피트가 되라고 가르칠 수 없는 것과 마찬가지였다. 그러나 그렇게 보이도록 가르치는 것은 될 수 있는 일이 아닐까?"], ["The task was a very delicate one, even for a mother’s hand.", "그 일은 어머니의 손으로도 아주 까다로운 일이었다."]], [["“Of course he cannot be at home now as much as he was down in the country, when he was living in the same house,”", "“물론 그분이 지금은 같은 집에서 살던 시골에서만큼 집에 붙어 있을 수는 없지,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly, whose business it was to take Lord Lufton’s part at the present moment.", "지금 이 순간 러프턴 경을 편들어야 하는 것이 제 소임인 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“He must be at his club, and at the House of Lords, and in twenty places.”", "“클럽에도 가야 하고, 상원에도 가야 하고, 스무 군데를 다녀야 할 테니까.”"]], [["“He is very fond of going to parties, and he dances beautifully.”", "“그분은 모임에 나가기를 아주 좋아하시고, 춤을 아름답게 추세요.”"]], [["“I am sure he does.", "“그럴 테지."], ["I have seen as much as that myself, and I think I know some one with whom he likes to dance.”", "나도 그만큼은 직접 보았고, 그분이 누구와 춤추기를 좋아하는지도 알 것 같구나.”"], ["And the mother gave her daughter a loving little squeeze.", "그러고서 어머니는 딸을 사랑스럽게 살짝 껴안았다."]], [["“Do you mean me, mamma?”", "“저를 두고 하시는 말씀이에요, 어머니?”"]], [["“Yes, I do mean you, my dear.", "“그래, 너를 두고 하는 말이다, 얘야."], ["And is it not true?", "그리고 그게 사실이 아니냐?"], ["Lady Lufton says that he likes dancing with you better than with any one else in London.”", "레이디 러프턴 말씀이, 그분이 런던의 다른 누구보다 너와 춤추기를 더 좋아한다고 하시더구나.”"]], [["“I don’t know,”", "“모르겠어요,”"], ["said Griselda, looking down upon the ground.", "땅바닥을 내려다보며 그리젤다가 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Grantly thought that this upon the whole was rather a good opening.", "그랜틀리 부인은 대체로 이것이 꽤 좋은 시작이라고 생각했다."], ["It might have been better.", "더 좋을 수도 있었다."], ["Some point of interest more serious in its nature than that of a waltz might have been found on which to connect her daughter’s sympathies with those of her future husband.", "딸의 마음을 장래 남편의 마음에 이어 붙일 자리로, 왈츠 따위보다 성질이 더 무게 있는 마음 붙일 자리를 찾아낼 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["But any point of interest was better than none; and it is so difficult to find points of interest in persons who by their nature are not impulsive.", "그러나 어떤 마음 붙일 자리든 하나도 없는 것보다는 나았다. 그리고 천성이 충동적이지 않은 사람에게서 마음 붙일 자리를 찾아내기란 참으로 어려운 일이다."]], [["“Lady Lufton says so, at any rate,”", "“어쨌든 레이디 러프턴께서는 그렇게 말씀하신다,”"], ["continued Mrs. Grantly, ever so cautiously.", "그랜틀리 부인이 한없이 조심스럽게 이어 갔다."], ["“She thinks that Lord Lufton likes no partner better.", "“러프턴 경이 너보다 더 좋아하는 짝은 없다고 보시는 거야."], ["What do you think yourself, Griselda?”", "너 자신은 어떻게 생각하느냐, 그리젤다?”"]], [["“I don’t know, mamma.”", "“모르겠어요, 어머니.”"]], [["“But young ladies must think of such things, must they not?”", "“하지만 젊은 아가씨라면 그런 것을 생각해 두어야 하지 않겠니?”"]], [["“Must they, mamma?”", "“그래야 하나요, 어머니?”"]], [["“I suppose they do, don’t they?", "“그러는 게 아니냐?"], ["The truth is, Griselda, that Lady Lufton thinks that if— Can you guess what it is she thinks?”", "사실은 말이다, 그리젤다, 레이디 러프턴께서 생각하시기를, 만일— 그분이 무엇을 생각하시는지 짐작이 가느냐?”"]], [["“No, mamma.”", "“아니에요, 어머니.”"], ["But that was a fib on Griselda’s part.", "그러나 그것은 그리젤다 쪽에서 눙친 것이었다."]], [["“She thinks that my Griselda would make the best possible wife in the world for her son; and I think so too.", "“우리 그리젤다가 이 세상에서 자기 아들에게 가장 좋은 아내가 되어 줄 거라고 그분은 생각하신다. 나도 그렇게 생각해."], ["I think that her son will be a very fortunate man if he can get such a wife.", "그분 아들이 그런 아내를 얻을 수 있다면 아주 운이 좋은 사람이라고 생각한다."], ["And now what do you think, Griselda?”", "그래서 너는 어떻게 생각하느냐, 그리젤다?”"]], [["“I don’t think anything, mamma.”", "“아무 생각도 없어요, 어머니.”"]], [["But that would not do.", "그러나 그것으로는 될 일이 아니었다."], ["It was absolutely necessary that she should think, and absolutely necessary that her mother should tell her so.", "그녀가 생각을 하는 것이 반드시 필요했고, 어머니가 그렇게 말해 주는 것도 반드시 필요했다."], ["Such a degree of unimpulsiveness as this would lead to—heaven knows what results!", "이만한 정도의 충동 없음이 무슨 결과로 이어질지는—하늘만 알 일이었다!"], ["Lufton-Grantly treaties and Hartletop interests would be all thrown away upon a young lady who would not think anything of a noble suitor sighing for her smiles.", "제 웃음을 얻으려 한숨짓는 귀족 구혼자를 놓고 아무 생각도 하지 않으려는 아가씨에게라면 러프턴-그랜틀리 조약이든 하틀탑 쪽 이해든 다 헛되이 버려질 것이었다."], ["Besides, it was not natural.", "게다가 그것은 자연스럽지도 않았다."], ["Griselda, as her mother knew, had never been a girl of headlong feeling; but still she had had her likes and her dislikes.", "어머니가 알기로 그리젤다는 앞뒤 없이 몰아치는 감정을 지닌 아이였던 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그러나 그래도 좋아하는 것과 싫어하는 것은 있었다."], ["In that matter of the bishopric she was keen enough; and no one could evince a deeper interest in the subject of a well-made new dress than Griselda Grantly.", "주교좌 문제에서는 그 아이도 넉넉히 예리했고, 새로 잘 지은 드레스라는 주제에 그리젤다 그랜틀리보다 더 깊은 관심을 드러낼 사람은 없었다."], ["It was not possible that she should be indifferent as to her future prospects, and she must know that those prospects depended mainly on her marriage.", "제 앞날의 전망에 무심할 수 있을 리 없었고, 그 전망이 주로 혼인에 달려 있다는 것을 그 아이도 알고 있을 것이었다."], ["Her mother was almost angry with her, but nevertheless she went on very gently:", "어머니는 딸에게 거의 화가 났으나, 그래도 아주 부드럽게 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“You don’t think anything!", "“아무 생각도 없다니!"], ["But, my darling, you must think.", "하지만 내 귀여운 것, 너는 생각을 해야 한다."], ["You must make up your mind what would be your answer if Lord Lufton were to propose to you.", "러프턴 경이 네게 청혼을 한다면 무엇이라 답할지 마음을 정해 두어야 해."], ["That is what Lady Lufton wishes him to do.”", "레이디 러프턴께서 그분이 그러기를 바라고 계시니까.”"]], [["“But he never will, mamma.”", "“하지만 그분은 절대 안 그러실 거예요, 어머니.”"]], [["“And if he did?”", "“그런데 만일 그러신다면?”"]], [["“But I’m sure he never will.", "“하지만 절대 안 그러실 게 뻔해요."], ["He doesn’t think of such a thing at all—and—and—”", "그분은 그런 건 아예 생각하지 않으세요—그리고—그리고—”"]], [["“And what, my dear?”", "“그리고 뭐냐, 얘야?”"]], [["“I don’t know, mamma.”", "“모르겠어요, 어머니.”"]], [["“Surely you can speak out to me, dearest!", "“나에게는 터놓고 말할 수 있잖니, 얘야!"], ["All I care about is your happiness.", "내가 마음 쓰는 것은 오직 네 행복뿐이다."], ["Both Lady Lufton and I think that it would be a happy marriage if you both cared for each other enough.", "레이디 러프턴이나 나나 너희 둘이 서로를 넉넉히 마음에 둔다면 행복한 혼인이 될 거라고 생각한다."], ["She thinks that he is fond of you.", "그분은 아들이 너를 좋아한다고 보시지."], ["But if he were ten times Lord Lufton I would not tease you about it if I thought that you could not learn to care about him.", "하지만 그분이 러프턴 경의 열 배가 되는 사람이라 해도, 네가 그분에게 마음을 두는 법을 익힐 수 없겠다고 여겨진다면 나는 이 일로 너를 괴롭히지 않을 게다."], ["What was it you were going to say, my dear?”", "하려던 말이 무엇이었느냐, 얘야?”"]], [["“Lord Lufton thinks a great deal more of Lucy Robarts than he does of—of—of any one else, I believe,”", "“러프턴 경은 루시 로바츠를—를—를 다른 누구보다도 훨씬 더 마음에 두고 있어요, 제가 보기에는요,”"], ["said Griselda, showing now some little animation by her manner, “dumpy little black thing that she is.”", "이번에는 태도에 조금 생기를 내며 그리젤다가 말했다. “땅딸막하고 조그맣고 까무잡잡한 것이 말이에요.”"]], [["“Lucy Robarts!” said Mrs.", "“루시 로바츠라니!”"], ["Grantly, taken by surprise at finding that her daughter was moved by such a passion as jealousy, and feeling also perfectly assured that there could not be any possible ground for jealousy in such a direction as that.", "딸이 시샘 같은 감정에 움직였다는 것을 알고 놀라면서도, 그런 쪽으로는 시샘할 근거가 있을 수 없다고 아주 확실히 여기는 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “루시 로바츠라니, 얘야!"], ["“Lucy Robarts, my dear! I don’t suppose Lord Lufton ever thought of speaking to her, except in the way of civility.”", "러프턴 경이 예의로 말을 거는 것 말고 달리 그 아이에게 말을 붙일 생각을 해 본 적이 있다고는 여기지 않는다.”"]], [["“Yes, he did, mamma!", "“아니, 있었어요, 어머니!"], ["Don’t you remember at Framley?”", "프램리에서 있었던 일을 기억 못 하세요?”"]], [["Mrs. Grantly began to look back in her mind, and she thought she did remember having once observed Lord Lufton talking in rather a confidential manner with the parson’s sister.", "그랜틀리 부인은 마음속으로 지난 일을 되짚어 보기 시작했고, 러프턴 경이 목사의 누이와 꽤 속내를 터놓는 투로 이야기하는 것을 한 번 본 기억이 있는 듯했다."], ["But she was sure that there was nothing in it.", "그러나 거기에는 아무것도 없다고 확신했다."], ["If that was the reason why Griselda was so cold to her proposed lover, it would be a thousand pities that it should not be removed.", "그리젤다가 제 앞에 내놓인 연인에게 그토록 차가운 이유가 그것이라면, 그것을 걷어 내지 못한다면 천 번 애석한 일일 터였다."]], [["“Now you mention her, I do remember the young lady,”", "“네가 말을 하니 그 아가씨가 생각나는구나,” 그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs. Grantly, “a dark girl, very low, and without much figure.", "“살빛이 검고, 키가 아주 작고, 몸매랄 것이 없는 아이였지."], ["She seemed to me to keep very much in the background.”", "내가 보기에는 아주 뒤로만 물러나 있는 것 같더라.”"]], [["“I don’t know much about that, mamma.”", "“그건 잘 모르겠어요, 어머니.”"]], [["“As far as I saw her, she did.", "“내가 본 만큼은 그랬다."], ["But, my dear Griselda, you should not allow yourself to think of such a thing.", "하지만 그리젤다야, 그런 일을 마음에 두어서는 안 된다."], ["Lord Lufton, of course, is bound to be civil to any young lady in his mother’s house, and I am quite sure that he has no other idea whatever with regard to Miss Robarts.", "러프턴 경이야 어머니 집에 있는 아가씨라면 누구에게든 예의를 지켜야 하는 법이고, 로바츠 양에 관해서 그분이 그 밖의 다른 생각을 조금도 품고 있지 않다는 것은 아주 확실하다. 그 아이의 머리에 대해서는 내가 무엇이라 말할 수가 없구나."], ["I certainly cannot speak as to her intellect, for I do not think she opened her mouth in my presence; but—”", "내 앞에서는 입을 뗀 적이 없는 것 같으니 말이다. 그러나—”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["she has plenty to say for herself, when she pleases.", "그 아이는 마음만 먹으면 제 할 말은 얼마든지 해요."], ["She’s a sly little thing.”", "앙큼한 것이에요.”"]], [["“But, at any rate, my dear, she has no personal attractions whatever, and I do not at all think that Lord Lufton is a man to be taken by—by—by anything that Miss Robarts might do or say.”", "“하지만 어쨌든 얘야, 그 아이에게는 타고난 매력이라고 할 것이 전혀 없다. 그리고 러프턴 경이 로바츠 양이 하는 짓이나 하는 말에—에—에 넘어갈 사람이라고는 조금도 생각하지 않는다.”"]], [["As those words “personal attractions” were uttered, Griselda managed so to turn her neck as to catch a side view of herself in one of the mirrors on the wall, and then she bridled herself up, and made a little play with her eyes, and looked, as her mother thought, very well.", "“타고난 매력”이라는 그 말이 나오는 순간, 그리젤다는 벽에 걸린 거울 가운데 하나에 제 옆모습이 비치도록 목을 돌려 냈고, 그러고서 고개를 뻣뻣이 세우고 눈을 살짝 놀려 보이니, 어머니가 보기에 아주 잘생겨 보였다."], ["“It is all nothing to me, mamma, of course,”", "“저에게는 물론 아무것도 아닌 일이에요, 어머니,”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Well, my dear, perhaps not.", "“그래, 얘야, 아마 아닐 테지."], ["I don’t say that it is.", "나도 그렇다고는 하지 않는다."], ["I do not wish to put the slightest constraint upon your feelings.", "나는 네 마음에 조금이라도 억지를 씌우고 싶지 않아."], ["If I did not have the most thorough dependence on your good sense and high principles, I should not speak to you in this way.", "네 분별과 높은 지조를 온전히 믿고 있지 않다면 이런 식으로 네게 말하지 않을 게다."], ["But as I have, I thought it best to tell you that both Lady Lufton and I should be well pleased if we thought that you and Lord Lufton were fond of each other.”", "그러나 믿고 있으니, 너와 러프턴 경이 서로를 좋아한다고 여겨진다면 레이디 러프턴이나 나나 아주 흐뭇할 것이라는 말을 해 두는 것이 가장 좋다고 생각했다.”"]], [["“I am sure he never thinks of such a thing, mamma.”", "“그분은 그런 건 절대 생각하지 않으실 거예요, 어머니.”"]], [["“And as for Lucy Robarts, pray get that idea out of your head; if not for your sake, then for his.", "“그리고 루시 로바츠에 관해서는, 부디 그 생각을 머리에서 지워 버리렴. 너를 위해서가 아니라면 그분을 위해서라도 말이다."], ["You should give him credit for better taste.”", "그분에게 그보다 나은 취향이 있다고 믿어 드려야 해.”"]], [["But it was not so easy to take anything out of Griselda’s head that she had once taken into it.", "그러나 그리젤다가 한번 머리에 들여놓은 것을 다시 끄집어 내기란 그리 쉬운 일이 아니었다."], ["“As for tastes, mamma, there is no accounting for them,”", "“취향이란 것은요, 어머니, 따져 볼 수가 없는 것이잖아요,”"], ["she said; and then the colloquy on that subject was over.", "그녀가 말했다. 그러고서 그 주제를 놓고 벌이던 이야기는 끝이 났다."], ["The result of it on Mrs. Grantly’s mind was a feeling amounting almost to a conviction in favour of the Dumbello interest.", "그것이 그랜틀리 부인의 마음에 남긴 결과는 덤벨로 쪽 이해에 유리한, 거의 확신에 이르는 느낌이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVI. IMPULSIVE.", "# 제26장. 충동적인 사람"]], [["I trust my readers will all remember how Puck the pony was beaten during that drive to Hogglestock.", "독자들께서는 모두, 호글스톡으로 마차를 몰고 가던 그 길에서 조랑말 퍽이 어떻게 매를 맞았는지 기억하시리라 믿는다."], ["It may be presumed that Puck himself on that occasion did not suffer much.", "그때 퍽 자신은 그리 크게 앓지 않았으리라 짐작해도 좋다."], ["His skin was not so soft as Mrs. Robarts’s heart.", "그 살가죽은 로바츠 부인의 마음만큼 무르지 않았다."], ["The little beast was full of oats and all the good things of this world, and therefore, when the whip touched him, he would dance about and shake his little ears, and run on at a tremendous pace for twenty yards, making his mistress think that he had endured terrible things.", "그 작은 짐승은 귀리와 이 세상 온갖 좋은 것으로 배가 부른 터라, 채찍이 몸에 닿으면 껑충거리며 작은 두 귀를 흔들고 스무 걸음쯤을 무서운 속도로 내달아, 제 여주인으로 하여금 제가 끔찍한 일을 겪었다고 여기게 만들었다."], ["But, in truth, during those whippings Puck was not the chief sufferer.", "그러나 참으로 말하자면, 그 채찍질이 오가는 동안 가장 크게 앓은 것은 퍽이 아니었다."]], [["Lucy had been forced to declare—forced by the strength of her own feelings, and by the impossibility of assenting to the propriety of a marriage between Lord Lufton and Miss Grantly—, she had been forced to declare that she did care about Lord Lufton as much as though he were her brother.", "루시는 밝히지 않을 수 없었다 — 제 감정의 힘에 밀려, 그리고 러프턴 경과 그랜틀리 양 사이의 혼사가 온당하다고는 도무지 수긍할 수 없었기에 밀려 — 러프턴 경이 자기 오라비라 하여도 그만큼은 그분에게 마음을 쓴다고 밝히지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She had said all this to herself,—nay, much more than this—very often.", "이 모든 것을, 아니 이보다 훨씬 더한 것을 그녀는 스스로에게 아주 자주 말해 왔다."], ["But now she had said it out loud to her sister-in-law; and she knew that what she had said was remembered, considered, and had, to a certain extent, become the cause of altered conduct.", "그러나 이제 그녀는 그것을 올케에게 소리 내어 말해 버렸고, 자기가 한 말이 기억되고 헤아려졌으며 어느 정도까지는 처신이 달라지는 까닭이 되었음을 알고 있었다."], ["Fanny alluded very seldom to the Luftons in casual conversation, and never spoke about Lord Lufton, unless when her husband made it impossible that she should not speak of him.", "패니는 예사 이야기 중에 러프턴 집안 사람들을 아주 드물게만 들먹였고, 남편이 그를 입에 올리지 않을 수 없게 만들 때가 아니면 러프턴 경에 관하여는 결코 말하지 않았다."], ["Lucy had attempted on more than one occasion to remedy this, by talking about the young lord in a laughing and, perhaps, half-jeering way; she had been sarcastic as to his hunting and shooting, and had boldly attempted to say a word in joke about his love for Griselda.", "루시는 한 번 이상, 그 젊은 나리에 관하여 웃음 섞인, 그리고 어쩌면 반쯤 비웃는 투로 이야기함으로써 이것을 바로잡아 보려 하였다. 그의 사냥과 총질을 두고 비꼬았고, 그리젤다에 대한 그의 사랑을 두고 농으로 한마디 해 보려고 대담하게 나서기까지 하였다."], ["But she felt that she had failed; that she had failed altogether as regarded Fanny; and that as to her brother, she would more probably be the means of opening his eyes, than have any effect in keeping them closed.", "그러나 그녀는 자기가 실패했음을 느꼈다. 패니에 관하여는 온전히 실패했고, 오라비에 관하여는 그 눈을 감겨 두는 데에 무슨 효험이 있기보다 오히려 그 눈을 뜨게 하는 구실이 되기가 더 쉬웠다."], ["So she gave up her efforts and spoke no further word about Lord Lufton.", "그리하여 그녀는 애쓰기를 그만두고 러프턴 경에 관하여 더는 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["Her secret had been told, and she knew that it had been told.", "그녀의 비밀은 말해져 버렸고, 그녀는 그것이 말해졌음을 알고 있었다."]], [["At this time the two ladies were left a great deal alone together in the drawing-room at the parsonage; more, perhaps, than had ever yet been the case since Lucy had been there.", "이 무렵 두 부인은 목사관 거실에 단둘이 남겨져 있는 때가 아주 많았다. 어쩌면 루시가 그곳에 온 뒤로 여태 그런 적이 없었을 만큼 많았다."], ["Lady Lufton was away, and therefore the almost daily visit to Framley Court was not made; and Mark in these days was a great deal at Barchester, having, no doubt, very onerous duties to perform before he could be admitted as one of that chapter.", "레이디 러프턴은 떠나 있었으므로 프램리 코트로 거의 매일 올라가던 걸음도 없었고, 마크는 이 며칠 바체스터에서 지내는 때가 많았으니, 그 성당 참사회의 한 사람으로 받아들여지기 전에 치러야 할 아주 무거운 임무가 틀림없이 있었던 것이다."], ["He went into, what he was pleased to call residence, almost at once.", "그는 스스로 즐겨 거주 근무라 부르는 것에 거의 곧바로 들어갔다."], ["That is, he took his month of preaching, aiding also in some slight and very dignified way, in the general Sunday morning services.", "곧, 한 달치 설교를 맡았고, 주일 아침 예배 전반에도 조금씩, 아주 위엄 있는 방식으로 거들었다."], ["He did not exactly live at Barchester, because the house was not ready.", "집이 채 준비되지 않았던 까닭에 그가 바체스터에 딱히 살지는 않았다."], ["That at least was the assumed reason.", "적어도 내세운 이유는 그것이었다."], ["The chattels of Dr. Stanhope, the late prebendary, had not been as yet removed, and there was likely to be some little delay, creditors asserting their right to them.", "앞서의 참사회원인 스탠호프 박사의 가재도구가 아직 치워지지 않았고, 채권자들이 그것에 대한 권리를 주장하고 있었으므로 얼마간 지체가 있을 듯하였다."], ["This might have been very inconvenient to a gentleman anxiously expecting the excellent house which the liberality of past ages had provided for his use; but it was not so felt by Mr. Robarts.", "지난 시대의 후한 마음이 제가 쓰도록 마련해 준 그 훌륭한 집을 애타게 기다리는 신사에게라면 이것이 몹시 불편한 일이었을 터이나, 로바츠 씨는 그렇게 느끼지 않았다."], ["If Dr. Stanhope’s family or creditors would keep the house for the next twelve months, he would be well pleased.", "스탠호프 박사의 식구든 채권자든 앞으로 열두 달 동안 그 집을 붙들고 있어 준다면 그로서는 아주 흡족하였다."], ["And by this arrangement he was enabled to get through his first month of absence from the church of Framley without any notice from Lady Lufton, seeing that Lady Lufton was in London all the time.", "그리고 이 처분 덕분에 그는 프램리 교회를 비운 첫 달을 레이디 러프턴의 눈길 한 번 받지 않고 넘길 수 있었으니, 레이디 러프턴이 그동안 줄곧 런던에 있었기 때문이다."], ["This also was convenient, and taught our young prebendary to look on his new preferment more favourably than he had hitherto done.", "이 또한 편리한 일이었고, 우리 젊은 참사회원으로 하여금 자기의 새 성직 영전을 여태보다 좋게 보도록 가르쳐 주었다."]], [["Fanny and Lucy were thus left much alone: and as out of the full head the mouth speaks, so is the full heart more prone to speak at such periods of confidence as these.", "그리하여 패니와 루시는 단둘이 있는 때가 많았다. 그리고 머리에 가득한 것에서 입이 말하듯, 마음에 가득한 것은 이런 흉허물 없는 시간에 더욱 말로 나오기 쉬운 법이다."], ["Lucy, when she first thought of her own state, determined to endow herself with a powerful gift of reticence.", "루시는 처음 제 처지를 헤아렸을 때, 입을 다무는 힘센 재주를 스스로에게 갖추어 주기로 마음먹었다."], ["She would never tell her love, certainly; but neither would she let concealment feed on her damask cheek, nor would she ever be found for a moment sitting like Patience on a monument.", "제 사랑을 결코 말하지 않을 것은 물론이려니와, 감춤이 제 다마스크빛 뺨을 파먹게 두지도 않을 것이고, 비석 위에 앉은 인내의 상처럼 한순간이라도 앉아 있는 모습을 보이지도 않을 것이었다."], ["She would fight her own fight bravely within her own bosom, and conquer her enemy altogether.", "제 가슴속에서 제 싸움을 용감하게 싸워 원수를 온전히 이겨 낼 것이었다."], ["She would either preach, or starve, or weary her love into subjection, and no one should be a bit the wiser.", "설교로든 굶김으로든 지치게 함으로든 제 사랑을 굴복시킬 것이며, 아무도 그것을 티끌만큼도 눈치채지 못할 것이었다."], ["She would teach herself to shake hands with Lord Lufton without a quiver, and would be prepared to like his wife amazingly—unless indeed that wife should be Griselda Grantly.", "러프턴 경과 손을 잡을 때에도 떨림 하나 없도록 스스로를 가르칠 것이고, 그의 아내를 놀랍도록 좋아할 준비까지 되어 있을 것이었다 — 다만 그 아내가 그리젤다 그랜틀리인 경우가 아니라면."], ["Such were her resolutions; but at the end of the first week they were broken into shivers and scattered to the winds.", "그녀의 결심은 그러하였다. 그러나 첫 주가 다 가기도 전에 그 결심은 산산조각으로 부서져 바람에 흩날려 갔다."]], [["They had been sitting in the house together the whole of one wet day; and as Mark was to dine in Barchester with the dean, they had had dinner early, eating with the children almost in their laps.", "두 사람은 비 오는 어느 날 하루를 온통 집 안에 함께 앉아 지냈다. 그리고 마크가 바체스터에서 수석 사제와 저녁을 먹기로 되어 있었으므로 두 사람은 이르게 저녁을 먹었으니, 아이들을 거의 무릎에 앉힌 채로 함께 먹었다."], ["It is so that ladies do, when their husbands leave them to themselves.", "남편들이 아내를 혼자 두고 나가면 부인들은 으레 그렇게 한다."], ["It was getting dusk towards evening, and they were still sitting in the drawing-room, the children now having retired, when Mrs.", "저녁이 되어 어스름이 내리는데, 아이들은 이제 물러갔고 두 사람은 여전히 거실에 앉아 있었다. 그때 로바츠 부인은 호글스톡을 다녀온 뒤로 다섯 번째로, 크롤리네에 무슨 좋은 일을 해 줄 수 있었으면 좋겠다는 소망을 말하기 시작했다."], ["Robarts for the fifth time since her visit to Hogglestock began to express her wish that she could do some good to the Crawleys,—to Grace Crawley in particular, who, standing up there at her father’s elbow, learning Greek irregular verbs, had appeared to Mrs. Robarts to be an especial object of pity.", "특히 그레이스 크롤리에게 그러했으니, 아버지의 팔꿈치 옆에 서서 그리스어 불규칙 동사를 배우고 있던 그 아이는 로바츠 부인의 눈에 유별나게 딱하게 여길 만한 아이로 보였던 것이다."]], [["“I don’t know how to set about it,”", "“어떻게 손을 대야 할지 모르겠어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["Now any allusion to that visit to Hogglestock always drove Lucy’s mind back to the consideration of the subject which had most occupied it at the time.", "그런데 호글스톡 방문을 들먹이는 말은 무엇이든 언제나 루시의 마음을, 그때 그 마음을 가장 크게 채우고 있던 일거리로 되돌려 놓았다."], ["She at such moments remembered how she had beaten Puck, and how in her half-bantering but still too serious manner she had apologized for doing so, and had explained the reason.", "그런 순간이면 그녀는 자기가 어떻게 퍽을 때렸는지, 그리고 반쯤은 농지거리 같으면서도 역시 너무 참된 그 태도로 어떻게 그 일을 사과하고 그 까닭을 밝혔는지를 떠올렸다."], ["And therefore she could not interest herself about Grace Crawley as vividly as she should have done.", "그러므로 그녀는 그레이스 크롤리에 관하여 마땅히 그랬어야 할 만큼 생생하게 관심을 낼 수 없었다."]], [["“No; one never does,”", "“그렇지요. 아무도 모르는 일이에요,”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I was thinking about it all that day as I drove home,”", "“집으로 돌아오는 그 길에서 내내 그 생각을 했어요,”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."], ["“The difficulty is this: What can we do with her?”", "“어려운 것은 이거예요. 그 아이를 데려다 무엇을 해 주어야 하나?”"]], [["“Exactly,”", "“그렇군요,”"], ["said Lucy, remembering the very point of the road at which she had declared that she did like Lord Lufton very much.", "루시는 말했으나, 마음속으로는 자기가 러프턴 경을 아주 많이 좋아한다고 밝혀 버린 그 길의 바로 그 지점을 떠올리고 있었다."]], [["“If we could have her here for a month or so and then send her to school;—but I know Mr. Crawley would not allow us to pay for her schooling.”", "“그 아이를 한 달쯤 여기 데려다 두고 그다음에 학교에 보낼 수 있다면 좋겠어요 — 하지만 크롤리 씨가 우리더러 그 아이 학비를 대게 하지는 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“I don’t think he would,”", "“대지 않으실 것 같네요,”"], ["said Lucy, with her thoughts far removed from Mr. Crawley and his daughter Grace.", "루시는 크롤리 씨와 그 딸 그레이스로부터 아득히 멀어진 생각을 안고 말했다."]], [["“And then we should not know what to do with her; should we?”", "“그러면 그 아이를 데려다 무엇을 해 주어야 할지 모르게 되겠지요, 그렇지 않아요?”"]], [["“No; you would not.”", "“그렇지요. 모르실 거예요.”"]], [["“It would never do to have the poor girl about the house here, with no one to teach her anything.", "“아무것도 가르쳐 줄 사람 하나 없이 그 딱한 아이를 여기 이 집에 두는 것은 도무지 안 될 일이에요."], ["Mark would not teach her Greek verbs, you know.”", "마크가 그 아이에게 그리스어 동사를 가르치지는 않을 테니까요.”"]], [["“I suppose not.”", "“그러지 않을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Lucy, you are not attending to a word I say to you, and I don’t think you have for the last hour.", "“루시, 내가 하는 말을 한마디도 듣고 있지 않네요. 지난 한 시간 동안 그랬던 것 같아요."], ["I don’t believe you know what I am talking about.”", "내가 무슨 이야기를 하고 있는지도 모르고 있는 게 아니에요?”"]], [["“Oh, yes, I do—Grace Crawley; I’ll try and teach her if you like, only I don’t know anything myself.”", "“아니에요, 알아요 — 그레이스 크롤리요. 원하신다면 내가 가르쳐 보겠어요. 다만 나 자신이 아는 게 아무것도 없어서요.”"]], [["“That’s not what I mean at all, and you know I would not ask you to take such a task as that on yourself.", "“내가 하려는 말은 전혀 그게 아니고, 그런 일을 루시에게 떠맡기자고 청하지는 않을 것도 알잖아요."], ["But I do think you might talk it over with me.”", "하지만 나와 함께 그 일을 두고 이야기는 해 볼 수 있을 것 같은데요.”"]], [["“Might I?", "“그럴 수 있을까요?"], ["very well; I will.", "좋아요, 하겠어요."], ["What is it?", "무엇인가요?"], ["Oh, Grace Crawley—you want to know who is to teach her the irregular Greek verbs.", "아, 그레이스 크롤리요 — 누가 그 아이에게 그리스어 불규칙 동사를 가르쳐 줄지 알고 싶으신 거지요."], ["Oh dear, Fanny, my head does ache so: pray don’t be angry with me.”", "아이고, 패니, 머리가 어찌나 아픈지 — 제발 나에게 노여워하지 마세요.”"], ["And then Lucy, throwing herself back on the sofa, put one hand up painfully to her forehead, and altogether gave up the battle.", "그러고서 루시는 소파에 몸을 던지고 한 손을 아프게 이마로 올리며 싸움을 아주 놓아 버렸다."]], [["Mrs. Robarts was by her side in a moment.", "로바츠 부인은 한순간에 그녀 곁에 와 있었다."], ["“Dearest Lucy, what is it makes your head ache so often now?", "“우리 루시, 요새는 왜 그렇게 자주 머리가 아픈 거예요?"], ["you used not to have those headaches.”", "전에는 그런 두통이 없었잖아요.”"]], [["“It’s because I’m growing stupid: never mind.", "“내가 멍청해지고 있으니까요. 마음 쓰지 마세요."], ["We will go on about poor Grace.", "딱한 그레이스 이야기를 이어서 해요."], ["It would not do to have a governess, would it?”", "가정 교사를 두는 것은 안 될 일이겠지요?”"]], [["“I can see that you are not well, Lucy,”", "“루시가 성치 않다는 게 보여요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts, with a look of deep concern.", "로바츠 부인이 깊이 근심하는 낯으로 말했다."], ["“What is it, dearest?", "“무슨 일이에요, 우리 루시?"], ["I can see that something is the matter.”", "무슨 일이 있다는 게 보여요.”"]], [["“Something the matter!", "“무슨 일이라니요!"], ["No, there’s not; nothing worth talking of.", "아니에요, 없어요. 말할 만한 일은 아무것도 없어요."], ["Sometimes I think I’ll go back to Devonshire and live there.", "이따금은 데번셔로 돌아가 거기서 살까 하는 생각이 들어요."], ["I could stay with Blanche for a time, and then get a lodging in Exeter.”", "얼마간은 블랜치와 지낼 수 있을 테고, 그다음에는 엑서터에 하숙을 하나 얻으면 되지요.”"]], [["“Go back to Devonshire!”", "“데번셔로 돌아간다니요!”"], ["and Mrs. Robarts looked as though she thought that her sister-in-law was going mad.", "그러고서 로바츠 부인은 시누이가 미쳐 가고 있다고 여기는 듯한 낯을 하였다."], ["“Why do you want to go away from us?", "“왜 우리를 떠나려고 해요?"], ["This is to be your own, own home, always now.”", "여기가 이제부터 언제까지나 루시 자신의, 자신의 집인데요.”"]], [["“Is it?", "“그런가요?"], ["Then I am in a bad way.", "그렇다면 나는 딱한 처지에 있는 거예요."], ["Oh dear, oh dear, what a fool I am!", "아이고, 아이고, 나는 어쩌면 이렇게 바보일까!"], ["What an idiot I’ve been!", "나는 어쩌면 이렇게 천치였을까!"], ["Fanny, I don’t think I can stay here; and I do so wish I’d never come.", "패니, 나는 여기 있을 수 없을 것 같아요. 애초에 오지 않았더라면 좋았겠다는 생각이 그렇게 간절해요."], ["I do—I do—I do, though you look at me so horribly,”", "정말이에요 — 정말이에요 — 정말이에요, 패니가 그렇게 무섭게 나를 바라보아도요.”"], ["and jumping up she threw herself into her sister-in-law’s arms and began kissing her violently.", "그러고서 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 올케의 품에 몸을 던지고 사납게 입을 맞추기 시작했다."], ["“Don’t pretend to be wounded, for you know that I love you.", "“상처받은 척은 하지 마세요. 내가 패니를 사랑하는 것을 알잖아요."], ["You know that I could live with you all my life, and think you were perfect—as you are; but—”", "내가 한평생 패니와 함께 살면서 패니가 완전한 사람이라고 여길 수 있다는 것도 알잖아요 — 실제로 그러니까요. 그렇지만 —”"]], [["“Has Mark said anything?”", "“마크가 무슨 말을 했어요?”"]], [["“Not a word,—not a ghost of a syllable.", "“한마디도요, — 한 음절의 그림자조차 없어요."], ["It is not Mark; oh, Fanny!”", "마크가 아니에요. 아, 패니!”"]], [["“I am afraid I know what you mean,” said Mrs.", "“무슨 말인지 알 것 같아 두려워요,”"], ["Robarts in a low tremulous voice, and with deep sorrow painted on her face.", "로바츠 부인이 낮고 떨리는 목소리로, 얼굴에 깊은 슬픔을 드리운 채 말했다."]], [["“Of course you do; of course you know; you have known it all along: since that day in the pony-carriage.", "“물론 알겠지요. 물론 알고 있지요."], ["I knew that you knew it.", "처음부터 줄곧 알고 있었잖아요. 그 조랑말 마차에서의 그날부터요."], ["You do not dare to mention his name: would not that tell me that you know it?", "패니가 안다는 것을 나는 알고 있었어요. 그분의 이름을 감히 입에 올리지도 못하잖아요."], ["And I, I am hypocrite enough for Mark; but my hypocrisy won’t pass muster before you.", "그것만으로도 패니가 안다는 것을 나에게 알려 주는 게 아닌가요? 그리고 나는, 나는 마크 앞에서라면 넉넉히 위선을 부릴 수 있어요."], ["And, now, had I not better go to Devonshire?”", "하지만 내 위선은 패니 앞에서는 검사를 통과하지 못해요. 그러니 이제 데번셔로 가는 편이 낫지 않겠어요?”"]], [["“Dearest, dearest Lucy.”", "“우리 루시, 우리 귀한 루시.”"]], [["“Was I not right about that labelling?", "“그 딱지를 붙이는 일에 대해서 내 말이 맞지 않았나요?"], ["O heavens!", "아, 하느님!"], ["what idiots we girls are!", "우리 처녀들은 어쩌면 이렇게 천치인가요!"], ["That a dozen soft words should have bowled me over like a ninepin, and left me without an inch of ground to call my own.", "부드러운 말 열두 마디가 나를 나인핀처럼 나동그라지게 만들고, 내 것이라 부를 땅을 한 치도 남겨 주지 않았으니!"], ["And I was so proud of my own strength; so sure that I should never be missish, and spoony, and sentimental!", "그런데도 나는 내 힘을 그토록 자랑했어요. 결코 새침을 떨거나 얼이 빠지거나 감상에 젖지 않으리라고 그토록 확신했어요!"], ["I was so determined to like him as Mark does, or you—”", "마크가, 아니면 패니가 그분을 좋아하는 식으로 나도 좋아하겠다고 그토록 다져 두었는데 —”"]], [["“I shall not like him at all if he has spoken words to you that he should not have spoken.”", "“그분이 루시에게 해서는 안 될 말을 했다면 나는 그분을 조금도 좋아하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“But he has not.”", "“하지만 그분은 그러지 않았어요.”"], ["And then she stopped a moment to consider.", "그러고서 그녀는 잠시 멈추어 헤아려 보았다."], ["“No, he has not.", "“그래요, 그러지 않았어요."], ["He never said a word to me that would make you angry with him if you knew of it.", "패니가 알게 된다 해도 그분에게 노여워할 만한 말은 한마디도 나에게 한 적이 없어요."], ["Except, perhaps, that he called me Lucy; and that was my fault, not his.”", "다만, 어쩌면 나를 루시라고 부른 것은 그렇겠지요. 그리고 그것은 내 잘못이지 그분의 잘못이 아니에요.”"]], [["“Because you talked of soft words.”", "“루시가 부드러운 말을 두고 이야기했으니까 묻는 거예요.”"]], [["“Fanny, you have no idea what an absolute fool I am, what an unutterable ass.", "“패니, 내가 얼마나 온전한 바보인지, 얼마나 말로 못 할 얼간이인지 패니는 모르고 있어요."], ["The soft words of which I tell you were of the kind which he speaks to you when he asks you how the cow gets on which he sent you from Ireland, or to Mark about Ponto’s shoulder.", "내가 말하는 그 부드러운 말이란, 아일랜드에서 보내 준 그 암소가 잘 자라느냐고 패니에게 물을 때 쓰는 것과 같은 말, 아니면 폰토의 어깻죽지를 두고 마크에게 하는 말과 같은 것이었어요."], ["He told me that he knew papa, and that he was at school with Mark, and that as he was such good friends with you here at the parsonage, he must be good friends with me too.", "그분은 나에게, 아버지를 알고 있었다고, 마크와 함께 학교를 다녔다고, 그리고 여기 목사관에서 패니와 그토록 좋은 벗이니 나와도 좋은 벗이 되어야 한다고 말했어요."], ["No; it has not been his fault.", "아니에요, 그분의 잘못이 아니었어요."], ["The soft words which did the mischief were such as those.", "해를 지은 그 부드러운 말은 그런 것들이었어요."], ["But how well his mother understood the world!", "그렇지만 그분의 어머니는 세상을 어찌 그렇게 잘 알고 계셨을까요!"], ["In order to have been safe, I should not have dared to look at him.”", "무사하려면 나는 감히 그분을 바라보지도 말았어야 했어요.”"]], [["“But, dearest Lucy—”", "“하지만 우리 루시 —”"]], [["“I know what you are going to say, and I admit it all.", "“패니가 무슨 말을 하려는지 알아요. 그리고 그것을 다 인정해요."], ["He is no hero.", "그분은 영웅이 아니에요."], ["There is nothing on earth wonderful about him.", "그분에게는 이 세상에서 놀라울 만한 것이 하나도 없어요."], ["I never heard him say a single word of wisdom, or utter a thought that was akin to poetry.", "나는 그분이 지혜로운 말 한마디를 하는 것도, 시에 가까운 생각 하나를 내놓는 것도 들어 본 적이 없어요."], ["He devotes all his energies to riding after a fox or killing poor birds, and I never heard of his doing a single great action in my life.", "그분은 여우를 뒤쫓아 달리거나 딱한 새들을 죽이는 데에 온 기운을 바치고, 나는 그분이 크나큰 일 한 가지를 했다는 이야기를 여태 살면서 들은 적이 없어요."], ["And yet—”", "그런데도 —”"]], [["Fanny was so astounded by the way her sister-in-law went on, that she hardly knew how to speak.", "패니는 시누이가 말을 이어 가는 모양에 어찌나 놀랐던지 어떻게 말해야 할지 거의 알 수 없었다."], ["“He is an excellent son, I believe,”", "“그분은 훌륭한 아들이지요,”"], ["at last she said.", "마침내 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Except when he goes to Gatherum Castle.", "“개서럼 성에 갈 때만 빼면요."], ["I’ll tell you what he has: he has fine straight legs, and a smooth forehead, and a good-humoured eye, and white teeth.", "그분이 가진 것을 내가 말해 줄게요. 곧고 잘생긴 다리, 매끄러운 이마, 사람 좋은 눈, 그리고 흰 이빨이에요."], ["Was it possible to see such a catalogue of perfections, and not fall down, stricken to the very bone?", "그런 완전함의 목록을 보고서 뼛속까지 얻어맞아 쓰러지지 않을 수 있었을까요?"], ["But it was not that that did it all, Fanny.", "하지만 그것이 다 한 일은 아니었어요, 패니."], ["I could have stood against that.", "그것쯤은 버텨 낼 수 있었어요."], ["I think I could at least.", "적어도 버틸 수 있었을 것 같아요."], ["It was his title that killed me.", "나를 죽인 것은 그분의 작위였어요."], ["I had never spoken to a lord before. Oh, me!", "나는 그때까지 귀족과 말을 해 본 적이 없었어요."], ["what a fool, what a beast I have been!”", "아, 나란 사람! 나는 어쩌면 이런 바보, 이런 짐승이었을까!”"], ["And then she burst out into tears.", "그러고서 그녀는 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["Mrs. Robarts, to tell the truth, could hardly understand poor Lucy’s ailment.", "사실을 말하자면 로바츠 부인은 딱한 루시의 병을 거의 알아볼 수 없었다."], ["It was evident enough that her misery was real; but yet she spoke of herself and her sufferings with so much irony, with so near an approach to joking, that it was very hard to tell how far she was in earnest.", "그 괴로움이 참된 것임은 넉넉히 뚜렷하였다. 그러나 그녀는 자기 자신과 자기 고통을 두고 어찌나 반어를 섞어, 어찌나 농에 가까이 다가가며 말하는지, 어디까지가 진심인지 가려내기가 몹시 어려웠다."], ["Lucy, too, was so much given to a species of badinage which Mrs. Robarts did not always quite understand, that the latter was afraid sometimes to speak out what came uppermost to her tongue.", "게다가 루시는 로바츠 부인이 늘 온전히 알아듣지는 못하는 일종의 농지거리에 어찌나 잘 빠지는지, 그 탓에 로바츠 부인은 혀끝에 먼저 올라오는 말을 입 밖에 내기가 이따금 두려웠다."], ["But now that Lucy was absolutely in tears, and was almost breathless with excitement, she could not remain silent any longer.", "그러나 이제 루시가 아주 눈물에 잠기고 격정으로 숨이 넘어갈 듯하니, 로바츠 부인도 더는 잠자코 있을 수 없었다."], ["“Dearest Lucy, pray do not speak in that way; it will all come right.", "“우리 루시, 부디 그런 식으로 말하지 마세요. 다 잘될 거예요."], ["Things always do come right when no one has acted wrongly.”", "누구도 잘못을 저지르지 않았을 때에는 일이 언제나 잘되는 법이에요.”"]], [["“Yes, when nobody has done wrongly.", "“그래요, 아무도 잘못을 저지르지 않았을 때지요."], ["That’s what papa used to call begging the question.", "그건 아버지가 논점 선취라고 부르시던 거예요."], ["But I’ll tell you what, Fanny; I will not be beaten.", "하지만 내 말을 들어 보세요, 패니. 나는 지지 않을 거예요."], ["I will either kill myself or get through it.", "나 자신을 죽이든지, 아니면 이 일을 뚫고 나갈 거예요."], ["I am so heartily self-ashamed that I owe it to myself to fight the battle out.”", "나 자신이 진심으로 부끄러워서, 이 싸움을 끝까지 싸워 내는 것이 나 자신에게 갚아야 할 빚이에요.”"]], [["“To fight what battle, dearest?”", "“어떤 싸움을 싸운다는 거예요, 우리 루시?”"]], [["“This battle.", "“이 싸움이요."], ["Here, now, at the present moment, I could not meet Lord Lufton.", "여기서, 지금, 이 순간에 나는 러프턴 경을 만날 수가 없어요."], ["I should have to run like a scared fowl if he were to show himself within the gate; and I should not dare to go out of the house, if I knew that he was in the parish.”", "그분이 문 안에 모습을 보이기라도 하면 놀란 닭처럼 달아나야 할 거예요. 그리고 그분이 이 교구에 있다는 것을 안다면 감히 집 밖으로 나가지도 못할 거예요.”"]], [["“I don’t see that, for I am sure you have not betrayed yourself.”", "“그럴 것 같지 않은데요. 루시가 속을 드러낸 적이 없다고 나는 확신하니까요.”"]], [["“Well, no; as for myself, I believe I have done the lying and the hypocrisy pretty well.", "“그래요, 없지요. 나 자신에 관하여는 거짓말과 위선을 꽤 잘해 냈다고 생각해요."], ["But, dearest Fanny, you don’t know half; and you cannot and must not know.”", "하지만 우리 패니, 패니는 절반도 몰라요. 그리고 알 수도 없고 알아서도 안 돼요.”"]], [["“But I thought you said there had been nothing whatever between you.”", "“하지만 두 사람 사이에는 아무 일도 없었다고 말한 줄 알았는데요.”"]], [["“Did I?", "“내가 그랬나요?"], ["Well, to you I have not said a word that was not true.", "그래요, 패니에게는 참되지 않은 말을 한마디도 한 적이 없어요."], ["I said that he had spoken nothing that it was wrong for him to say.", "나는 그분이 해서 잘못될 말은 아무것도 하지 않았다고 말했지요."], ["It could not be wrong—.", "그것이 잘못일 수는 없었어요 —."], ["But never mind.", "하지만 마음 쓰지 마세요."], ["I’ll tell you what I mean to do.", "내가 무엇을 하려는지 말해 줄게요."], ["I have been thinking of it for the last week—only I shall have to tell Mark.”", "지난 한 주 동안 그것을 생각해 왔어요 — 다만 마크에게는 말해야 하겠지요.”"]], [["“If I were you I would tell him all.”", "“내가 루시라면 마크에게 다 말하겠어요.”"]], [["“What, Mark!", "“뭐라고요, 마크에게요!"], ["If you do, Fanny, I’ll never, never, never speak to you again.", "그렇게 하면, 패니, 나는 다시는, 다시는, 다시는 패니에게 말을 걸지 않을 거예요."], ["Would you—when I have given you all my heart in true sisterly love?”", "그러겠어요? — 참된 자매의 사랑으로 내 마음을 죄 패니에게 내주었는데도요?”"]], [["Mrs. Robarts had to explain that she had not proposed to tell anything to Mark herself, and was persuaded, moreover, to give a solemn promise that she would not tell anything to him unless specially authorized to do so.", "로바츠 부인은 자기가 마크에게 무엇을 말하겠다고 한 것이 아니라고 설명해야 했고, 더구나 따로 허락을 받지 않는 한 그에게 아무것도 말하지 않겠다는 엄숙한 약속까지 하도록 설득되었다."]], [["“I’ll go into a home, I think,”", "“나는 수도원에 들어갈까 해요,” 루시가 말을 이었다."], ["continued Lucy.", "“그런 수도원이 어떤 곳인지 알지요?”"], ["“You know what those homes are?” Mrs.", "로바츠 부인은 아주 잘 알고 있다고 다짐해 주었고, 그러자 루시가 말을 이었다."], ["Robarts assured her that she knew very well, and then Lucy went on: “A year ago I should have said that I was the last girl in England to think of such a life, but I do believe now that it would be the best thing for me.", "“한 해 전이라면 나는 그런 삶을 생각할 처녀로는 영국에서 가장 마지막일 거라고 말했겠지요. 하지만 이제는 그것이 나에게 가장 좋은 일이 되겠다고 정말로 믿어요."], ["And then I’ll starve myself, and flog myself, and in that way I’ll get back my own mind and my own soul.”", "그러고서 나 자신을 굶기고, 나 자신을 채찍질하겠어요. 그런 식으로 내 마음과 내 영혼을 되찾아 오겠어요.”"]], [["“Your own soul, Lucy!”", "“루시의 영혼이라니요!”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts, in a tone of horror.", "로바츠 부인이 몸서리치는 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Well, my own heart, if you like it better; but I hate to hear myself talking about hearts.", "“그러면 내 마음이라고 하죠, 패니가 그 말이 더 좋다면요. 그렇지만 나 자신이 마음을 두고 떠드는 것을 듣기가 싫어요."], ["I don’t care for my heart.", "나는 내 마음을 아끼지 않아요."], ["I’d let it go—with this young popinjay lord or anyone else, so that I could read, and talk, and walk, and sleep, and eat, without always feeling that I was wrong here—here—here,”", "그냥 보내 버리겠어요 — 이 멋만 낸 젊은 귀족과 함께든 다른 누구와 함께든요. 그래서 여기가 — 여기가 — 여기가 잘못되었다는 느낌 없이 읽고, 말하고, 걷고, 자고, 먹을 수만 있다면요.”"], ["and she pressed her hand vehemently against her side.", "그러고서 그녀는 손을 옆구리에 사납게 눌러 댔다."], ["“What is it that I feel, Fanny?", "“내가 느끼는 이것이 무엇인가요, 패니?"], ["Why am I so weak in body that I cannot take exercise?", "왜 나는 몸이 이렇게 약해서 운동도 못 하나요?"], ["Why cannot I keep my mind on a book for one moment?", "왜 나는 책에 한순간도 마음을 붙들어 둘 수 없나요?"], ["Why can I not write two sentences together?", "왜 나는 두 문장을 이어 쓸 수 없나요?"], ["Why should every mouthful that I eat stick in my throat?", "왜 내가 먹는 한 입 한 입이 목에 걸리나요?"], ["Oh, Fanny, is it his legs, think you, or is it his title?”", "아, 패니, 그것은 그분의 다리 때문일까요, 아니면 그분의 작위 때문일까요?”"]], [["Through all her sorrow,—and she was very sorrowful,—Mrs. Robarts could not help smiling.", "슬픔에 온통 잠겨 있으면서도 — 그녀는 참으로 슬펐다 — 로바츠 부인은 웃음을 참을 수 없었다."], ["And, indeed, there was every now and then something even in Lucy’s look that was almost comic.", "그리고 실상 루시의 얼굴에는 이따금 거의 우스꽝스럽다 할 만한 무엇이 있었다."], ["She acted the irony so well with which she strove to throw ridicule on herself!", "자기를 조롱거리로 삼으려 애쓰며 짓는 그 반어를 어찌나 잘 연기하는지!"], ["“Do laugh at me,” she said.", "“나를 좀 비웃어 주세요,”"], ["“Nothing on earth will do me so much good as that; nothing, unless it be starvation and a whip.", "그녀가 말했다. “이 세상에서 그만큼 나에게 좋은 것은 없어요."], ["If you would only tell me that I must be a sneak and an idiot to care for a man because he is good-looking and a lord!”", "굶기와 채찍이 아니라면 아무것도요. 사내가 잘생겼고 귀족이라는 까닭으로 마음을 쓰는 여자는 비굴한 바보 천치일 수밖에 없다고 나에게 말해 주기만 한다면요!”"]], [["“But that has not been the reason.", "“하지만 그게 까닭이었던 것은 아니에요. 러프턴 경에게는 그보다 훨씬 더한 것이 있어요."], ["There is a great deal more in Lord Lufton than that; and since I must speak, dear Lucy, I cannot but say that I should not wonder at your being in love with him, only—only that—”", "그리고 말을 해야 한다면, 루시, 루시가 그분과 사랑에 빠진 것이 이상할 것은 없다고 나는 말하지 않을 수 없어요. 다만 — 다만 그것이 —”"]], [["“Only what?", "“다만 무엇이요?"], ["Come, out with it.", "어서, 다 말해 보세요."], ["Do not mince matters, or think that I shall be angry with you because you scold me.”", "말을 가리지도 말고, 나를 꾸짖는다고 해서 내가 노여워할 거라고 생각하지도 마세요.”"]], [["“Only that I should have thought that you would have been too guarded to have—have cared for any gentleman till—till he had shown that he cared for you.”", "“다만 나는 루시가 너무 단단히 막아 두는 사람이어서, 어떤 신사도 — 그가 루시를 마음에 둔다는 것을 보이기까지는 — 마음에 두지 않으리라고 생각했다는 거예요.”"]], [["“Guarded!", "“막아 둔다!"], ["Yes, that’s it; that’s just the word.", "그래요, 그거예요. 바로 그 말이에요."], ["But it’s he that should have been guarded.", "하지만 막아 두어야 했던 쪽은 그분이에요."], ["He should have had a fire-guard hung before him—or a love-guard, if you will.", "그분 앞에는 불막이라도 걸어 두어야 했어요 — 아니면 사랑막이라도, 그렇게 부르고 싶다면요."], ["Guarded!", "막아 둔다니!"], ["Was I not guarded, till you all would drag me out?", "패니네가 죄 나를 끌어내기까지 나는 막아 두고 있지 않았나요?"], ["Did I want to go there?", "내가 거기 가고 싶어 했나요?"], ["And when I was there, did I not make a fool of myself, sitting in a corner, and thinking how much better placed I should have been down in the servants’ hall.", "그리고 거기 갔을 때, 나는 구석에 앉아서 저 아래 하인 방에 있었더라면 훨씬 제자리였겠다고 생각하며 스스로를 바보로 만들지 않았나요?"], ["Lady Lufton—she dragged me out, and then cautioned me, and then, then— Why is Lady Lufton to have it all her own way?", "레이디 러프턴 — 그분이 나를 끌어내고, 그러고는 조심하라고 일러 주고, 그러고는, 그러고는 — 왜 레이디 러프턴은 무엇이든 제 뜻대로 해야 하나요?"], ["Why am I to be sacrificed for her?", "왜 나는 그분을 위해 희생되어야 하나요?"], ["I did not want to know Lady Lufton, or any one belonging to her.”", "나는 레이디 러프턴도, 그분에게 딸린 누구도 알고 싶어 하지 않았어요.”"]], [["“I cannot think that you have any cause to blame Lady Lufton, nor, perhaps, to blame anybody very much.”", "“레이디 러프턴을 탓할 까닭이 루시에게 있다고는 생각할 수 없어요. 어쩌면 누구를 그리 크게 탓할 까닭도 없을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Well, no, it has been all my own fault; though for the life of me, Fanny, going back and back, I cannot see where I took the first false step.", "“그래요, 없지요. 죄 내 잘못이었어요."], ["I do not know where I went wrong.", "그렇지만 패니, 아무리 애를 써서 뒤로 뒤로 되짚어 가도 내가 어디서 첫걸음을 잘못 디뎠는지는 알아볼 수가 없어요."], ["One wrong thing I did, and it is the only thing that I do not regret.”", "어디서 어긋났는지 모르겠어요. 잘못한 일이 하나 있는데, 그것이 내가 뉘우치지 않는 단 하나예요.”"]], [["“What was that, Lucy?”", "“그게 무엇이에요, 루시?”"]], [["“I told him a lie.”", "“나는 그분에게 거짓말을 했어요.”"]], [["Mrs. Robarts was altogether in the dark, and feeling that she was so, she knew that she could not give counsel as a friend or a sister.", "로바츠 부인은 온통 캄캄한 속에 있었고, 자기가 그렇다는 것을 느끼고 있었으므로, 벗으로서도 자매로서도 조언을 줄 수 없다는 것을 알았다."], ["Lucy had begun by declaring—so Mrs. Robarts thought—that nothing had passed between her and Lord Lufton but words of most trivial import, and yet she now accused herself of falsehood, and declared that that falsehood was the only thing which she did not regret!", "루시는 — 로바츠 부인이 여긴 바로는 — 자기와 러프턴 경 사이에 지극히 하찮은 말들밖에는 오간 것이 없다고 밝히면서 시작했는데, 이제는 스스로 거짓말을 했다고 자기를 몰아세우고, 그 거짓말이야말로 자기가 뉘우치지 않는 단 하나라고 밝히는 것이었다!"]], [["“I hope not,”", "“그러지 않았기를 바라요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."], ["“If you did, you were very unlike yourself.”", "“그랬다면 루시답지 않은 짓이었어요.”"]], [["“But I did, and were he here again, speaking to me in the same way, I should repeat it.", "“하지만 나는 했어요. 그리고 그분이 다시 여기 와서 같은 식으로 나에게 말한다면 나는 그것을 되풀이할 거예요."], ["I know I should.", "그러리라는 것을 알아요."], ["If I did not, I should have all the world on me.", "그러지 않는다면 온 세상이 나에게 달려들 거예요."], ["You would frown on me, and be cold.", "패니도 나에게 눈살을 찌푸리고 쌀쌀해지겠지요."], ["My darling Fanny, how would you look if I really displeasured you?”", "내 사랑 패니, 내가 정말로 패니를 언짢게 한다면 어떤 낯을 하겠어요?”"]], [["“I don’t think you will do that, Lucy.”", "“루시가 그럴 것 같지 않은데요.”"]], [["“But if I told him the truth I should, should I not?", "“하지만 내가 그분에게 진실을 말했다면 그렇게 되었겠지요, 그렇지 않아요?"], ["Speak now.", "이제 말해 보세요."], ["But no, Fanny, you need not speak.", "아니, 패니, 말하지 않아도 돼요. 그것은 패니가 무서워서가 아니었어요."], ["It was not the fear of you; no, nor even of her: though Heaven knows that her terrible glumness would be quite unendurable.”", "아니, 그분이 무서워서도 아니었어요. 하기야 그분의 그 무서운 뿌루퉁함이 도무지 견딜 수 없을 것임은 하느님도 아시지요.”"]], [["“I cannot understand you, Lucy.", "“루시를 알아들을 수가 없어요."], ["What truth or what untruth can you have told him if, as you say, there has been nothing between you but ordinary conversation?”", "루시 말대로 두 사람 사이에 예사 이야기밖에 없었다면, 그분에게 무슨 진실을, 또 무슨 거짓을 말할 수 있었다는 거예요?”"]], [["Lucy then got up from the sofa, and walked twice the length of the room before she spoke. Mrs.", "그러자 루시는 소파에서 일어나, 입을 열기까지 방의 길이를 두 번 오갔다."], ["Robarts had all the ordinary curiosity—I was going to say, of a woman, but I mean to say, of humanity; and she had, moreover, all the love of a sister.", "로바츠 부인에게는 여자의 — 라고 말하려 했으나, 사람이라면 누구나 지니는 것이라고 말해 두겠는데 — 그 예사 호기심이 다 있었다. 게다가 자매의 사랑도 다 있었다."], ["She was both curious and anxious, and remained sitting where she was, silent, and with her eyes fixed on her companion.", "그녀는 알고 싶기도 하고 걱정스럽기도 하여, 있던 자리에 그대로 앉은 채 말없이 눈을 제 벗에게 붙박아 두었다."]], [["“Did I say so?”", "“내가 그렇게 말했나요?”"], ["Lucy said at last.", "마침내 루시가 말했다. “아니에요, 패니."], ["“No, Fanny; you have mistaken me: I did not say that.", "패니가 나를 잘못 알아들었어요. 나는 그렇게 말하지 않았어요."], ["Ah, yes, about the cow and the dog.", "아, 그래요, 암소와 개에 관한 것 말이지요."], ["All that was true.", "그것은 다 참이었어요."], ["I was telling you of what his soft words had been while I was becoming such a fool.", "나는 내가 그런 바보가 되어 가는 동안 그분의 부드러운 말이 어떤 것이었는지를 이야기하고 있었어요."], ["Since that he has said more.”", "그 뒤로 그분은 더 말했어요.”"]], [["“What more has he said, Lucy?”", "“그분이 무엇을 더 말했어요, 루시?”"]], [["“I yearn to tell you, if only I can trust you;”", "“말하고 싶어 못 견디겠어요, 패니를 믿을 수만 있다면요.”"], ["and Lucy knelt down at the feet of Mrs. Robarts, looking up into her face and smiling through the remaining drops of her tears.", "그러고서 루시는 로바츠 부인의 발밑에 무릎을 꿇고 그 얼굴을 올려다보며, 남아 있는 눈물방울 사이로 웃었다."], ["“I would fain tell you, but I do not know you yet,—whether you are quite true.", "“말하고 싶은 마음이야 간절해요. 하지만 나는 아직 패니를 몰라요 — 패니가 아주 참된 사람인지를요."], ["I could be true,—true against all the world, if my friend told me.", "나라면 참될 수 있어요 — 벗이 나에게 털어놓았다면 온 세상을 거스르고서도 참될 수 있어요."], ["I will tell you, Fanny, if you say that you can be true.", "패니가 참될 수 있다고 말한다면 나는 말하겠어요."], ["But if you doubt yourself, if you must whisper all to Mark—then let us be silent.”", "하지만 패니가 스스로를 의심한다면, 마크에게 죄 귀엣말로 옮겨야 한다면 — 그러면 우리 잠자코 있어요.”"]], [["There was something almost awful in this to Mrs. Robarts.", "이것은 로바츠 부인에게 거의 무섭다 할 만한 것이었다."], ["Hitherto, since their marriage, hardly a thought had passed through her mind which she had not shared with her husband.", "혼인한 뒤로 여태 그녀의 마음을 지나간 생각 가운데 남편과 나누지 않은 것은 거의 없었다."], ["But now all this had come upon her so suddenly, that she was unable to think whether it would be well that she should become the depositary of such a secret,—not to be mentioned to Lucy’s brother, not to be mentioned to her own husband.", "그런데 이제 이 모든 것이 어찌나 갑작스럽게 그녀에게 닥쳤던지, 그런 비밀을 — 루시의 오라비에게도 입에 올려서는 안 되고 자기 남편에게도 입에 올려서는 안 되는 비밀을 — 맡아 두는 사람이 되는 것이 좋은 일일지 어떨지 헤아릴 수조차 없었다."], ["But who ever yet was offered a secret and declined it?", "그러나 비밀을 내주겠다는데 그것을 마다한 사람이 여태 있었던가?"], ["Who at least ever declined a love secret?", "하다못해 사랑의 비밀을 마다한 사람이 있었던가?"], ["What sister could do so? Mrs.", "어느 자매가 그럴 수 있겠는가?"], ["Robarts therefore gave the promise, smoothing Lucy’s hair as she did so, and kissing her forehead and looking into her eyes, which, like a rainbow, were the brighter for her tears.", "그리하여 로바츠 부인은 약속을 주었다. 그러면서 루시의 머리를 매만지고, 그 이마에 입을 맞추고, 무지개처럼 눈물 덕에 더 밝아진 그 눈을 들여다보았다."], ["“And what has he said to you, Lucy?”", "“그래서 그분이 루시에게 무슨 말을 했어요?”"]], [["“What?", "“무슨 말이요?"], ["Only this, that he asked me to be his wife.”", "그저 이것이에요. 그분이 나에게 아내가 되어 달라고 청했다는 것이요.”"]], [["“Lord Lufton proposed to you?”", "“러프턴 경이 루시에게 청혼을 했어요?”"]], [["“Yes; proposed to me.", "“그래요, 나에게 청혼했어요."], ["It is not credible, is it?", "믿을 만한 일이 아니지요, 그렇지 않아요?"], ["You cannot bring yourself to believe that such a thing happened, can you?”", "그런 일이 일어났다고는 도무지 믿어지지 않지요, 그렇지 않아요?”"], ["And Lucy rose again to her feet, as the idea of the scorn with which she felt that others would treat her—with which she herself treated herself—made the blood rise to her cheek.", "그러고서 루시는 다시 발을 딛고 일어섰으니, 남들이 자기를 어떤 업신여김으로 대할 것이라는 생각이 — 자기 자신이 스스로를 대하고 있는 그 업신여김의 생각이 — 피를 뺨으로 올려 보냈던 것이다."], ["“And yet it is not a dream.", "“그런데도 그것은 꿈이 아니에요."], ["I think that it is not a dream.", "꿈이 아니라고 나는 생각해요."], ["I think that he really did.”", "그분이 정말로 그랬다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“Think, Lucy!”", "“생각한다니요, 루시!”"]], [["“Well, I may say that I am sure.”", "“그래요, 확신한다고 말해도 좋겠지요.”"]], [["“A gentleman would not make you a formal proposal, and leave you in doubt as to what he meant.”", "“신사라면 정식으로 청혼을 하고서 그 뜻을 두고 루시를 의심 속에 남겨 두지는 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“Oh dear, no.", "“아이고, 그럼요."], ["There was no doubt at all of that kind; none in the least.", "그런 종류의 의심은 조금도 없었어요. 티끌만큼도요."], ["Mr. Smith in asking Miss Jones to do him the honour of becoming Mrs.", "스미스 씨가 존스 양에게 스미스 부인이 되어 주는 영광을 달라고 청할 때에도 그보다 더 분명하게 말하지는 못했을 거예요."], ["Smith never spoke more plainly. I was alluding to the possibility of having dreamt it all.”", "나는 그것을 죄 꿈으로 꾸었을 수도 있다는 이야기를 하고 있었던 거예요.”"]], [["“Lucy!”", "“루시!”"]], [["“Well, it was not a dream.", "“그래요, 꿈이 아니었어요."], ["Here, standing here, on this very spot—on that flower of the carpet—he begged me a dozen times to be his wife.", "여기, 여기 서서, 바로 이 자리에서 — 깔개의 저 꽃무늬 위에서 — 그분은 나에게 아내가 되어 달라고 열두 번이나 애원했어요."], ["I wonder whether you and Mark would let me cut it out and keep it.”", "저것을 잘라 내어 내가 갖도록 패니와 마크가 허락해 줄지 모르겠네요.”"]], [["“And what answer did you make to him?”", "“그래서 그분에게 어떤 답을 주었어요?”"]], [["“I lied to him and told him that I did not love him.”", "“나는 그분에게 거짓말을 했어요. 그분을 사랑하지 않는다고 말했지요.”"]], [["“You refused him?”", "“그분을 거절했어요?”"]], [["“Yes; I refused a live lord.", "“그래요, 나는 살아 있는 귀족을 거절했어요."], ["There is some satisfaction in having that to think of, is there not?", "그것을 두고 생각할 거리가 있다는 것은 얼마간 흡족한 일이지요, 그렇지 않아요?"], ["Fanny, was I wicked to tell that falsehood?”", "패니, 그런 거짓말을 한 것이 못된 짓이었을까요?”"]], [["“And why did you refuse him?”", "“그런데 왜 그분을 거절했어요?”"]], [["“Why? Can you ask?", "“왜냐고요?"], ["Think what it would have been to go down to Framley Court, and to tell her ladyship in the course of conversation that I was engaged to her son.", "그것을 물을 수 있어요? 프램리 코트로 내려가서 이야기하는 중에 마님께 내가 아드님과 약혼했다고 아뢰는 일이 어떠했을지를 생각해 보세요."], ["Think of Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴을 생각해 보세요."], ["But yet it was not that, Fanny.", "그렇지만 그것 때문도 아니었어요, 패니."], ["Had I thought that it was good for him, that he would not have repented, I would have braved anything—for his sake.", "그것이 그분에게 좋은 일이라고, 그분이 뉘우치지 않을 것이라고 생각했다면 나는 무엇에도 맞섰을 거예요 — 그분을 위해서요."], ["Even your frown, for you would have frowned.", "패니의 찌푸린 눈살에도요. 패니도 눈살을 찌푸렸을 테니까요."], ["You would have thought it sacrilege for me to marry Lord Lufton!", "내가 러프턴 경과 혼인하는 것을 패니는 신성 모독이라고 여겼을 거예요!"], ["You know you would.”", "그랬을 것임을 스스로 알잖아요.”"]], [["Mrs. Robarts hardly knew how to say what she thought, or indeed what she ought to think.", "로바츠 부인은 자기가 생각하는 것을 어떻게 말해야 할지, 아니 실은 무엇을 생각해야 마땅한지조차 거의 알 수 없었다."], ["It was a matter on which much meditation would be required before she could give advice, and there was Lucy expecting counsel from her at that very moment.", "이는 조언을 주려면 그전에 오래도록 골똘히 생각해야 할 일이었는데, 루시는 바로 그 순간에 그녀에게서 조언을 기다리고 있었다."], ["If Lord Lufton really loved Lucy Robarts, and was loved by Lucy Robarts, why should not they two become man and wife?", "러프턴 경이 정말로 루시 로바츠를 사랑하고 루시 로바츠에게 사랑받고 있다면, 그 둘이 부부가 되어서 안 될 까닭이 무엇인가?"], ["And yet she did feel that it would be—perhaps not sacrilege, as Lucy had said, but something almost as troublesome.", "그러면서도 그녀는 그것이 — 루시가 말한 대로 신성 모독까지는 아니겠으나 그에 거의 못지않게 말썽스러운 것이 되겠다고 느꼈다."], ["What would Lady Lufton say, or think, or feel?", "레이디 러프턴이 무엇이라 말하고, 무엇이라 생각하고, 무엇이라 느끼겠는가?"], ["What would she say, and think, and feel as to that parsonage from which so deadly a blow would fall upon her?", "그토록 치명적인 타격이 그분에게로 떨어져 나오게 될 그 목사관을 두고 무엇이라 말하고 생각하고 느끼겠는가?"], ["Would she not accuse the vicar and the vicar’s wife of the blackest ingratitude?", "교구 사제와 그 아내를 가장 검은 배은망덕으로 몰아세우지 않겠는가?"], ["Would life be endurable at Framley under such circumstances as those?", "그런 형편 아래에서 프램리에서의 삶이 견딜 만한 것이겠는가?"]], [["“What you tell me so surprises me, that I hardly as yet know how to speak about it,”", "“루시가 하는 말이 나를 어찌나 놀라게 하는지, 아직은 그것을 두고 어떻게 말해야 할지도 거의 모르겠어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“It was amazing, was it not?", "“놀라운 일이었지요, 그렇지 않아요?"], ["He must have been insane at the time; there can be no other excuse made for him.", "그분은 그때 제정신이 아니었을 거예요. 그분을 두고 달리 변명해 줄 것이 있을 수 없어요."], ["I wonder whether there is anything of that sort in the family?”", "그 집안에 그런 것이 좀 있는지 어떤지 모르겠네요.”"]], [["“What; madness?” said Mrs.", "“무엇이요, 미친 것 말이에요?”"], ["Robarts, quite in earnest.", "로바츠 부인이 아주 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“Well, don’t you think he must have been mad when such an idea as that came into his head?", "“그래요, 그런 생각이 머릿속에 들어왔을 때 그분이 미쳐 있었던 것이 틀림없다고 생각하지 않아요?"], ["But you don’t believe it; I can see that.", "그렇지만 패니는 그것을 믿지 않는군요. 그게 보여요."], ["And yet it is as true as heaven.", "그런데도 그것은 하늘만큼 참된 일이에요."], ["Standing exactly here, on this spot, he said that he would persevere till I accepted his love.", "바로 여기, 이 자리에 서서 그분은 내가 그 사랑을 받아들일 때까지 끈질기게 매달리겠다고 말했어요."], ["I wonder what made me specially observe that both his feet were within the lines of that division.”", "무엇 때문에 내가 그분의 두 발이 다 저 구획 선 안에 들어 있다는 것을 유별나게 눈여겨보게 되었는지 모르겠어요.”"]], [["“And you would not accept his love?”", "“그런데 그 사랑을 받아들이지 않았어요?”"]], [["“No; I would have nothing to say to it.", "“네, 나는 그것에 대해서 할 말이 없었어요."], ["Look you, I stood here, and putting my hand upon my heart,—for he bade me to do that,—I said that I could not love him.”", "보세요, 나는 여기 서서, 손을 가슴에 얹고 — 그분이 그렇게 하라고 시켰으니까요 — 그분을 사랑할 수 없다고 말했어요.”"]], [["“And what then?”", "“그러고는요?”"]], [["“He went away,—with a look as though he were heart-broken.", "“그분은 가 버렸어요 — 마음이 부서진 사람 같은 낯을 하고요."], ["He crept away slowly, saying that he was the most wretched soul alive.", "살아 있는 이 가운데 가장 딱한 넋이라고 말하면서 느릿느릿 슬며시 빠져나갔어요."], ["For a minute I believed him, and could almost have called him back.", "한동안은 나도 그 말을 믿었고, 거의 그분을 다시 불러 세울 수도 있었어요. 하지만 아니에요, 패니."], ["But, no, Fanny; do not think that I am over proud, or conceited about my conquest.", "내가 지나치게 자랑스러워한다고, 아니면 내가 이겨 놓은 일을 두고 우쭐거린다고 생각하지 마세요."], ["He had not reached the gate before he was thanking God for his escape.”", "그분은 문에 이르기도 전에 벗어난 것을 하느님께 감사드리고 있었어요.”"]], [["“That I do not believe.”", "“그것은 나는 믿지 않아요.”"]], [["“But I do; and I thought of Lady Lufton too.", "“하지만 나는 믿어요. 그리고 레이디 러프턴도 생각했어요."], ["How could I bear that she should scorn me, and accuse me of stealing her son’s heart?", "그분이 나를 업신여기며 아들의 마음을 훔쳤다고 나를 몰아세우는 것을 내가 어떻게 견디겠어요?"], ["I know that it is better as it is; but tell me—is a falsehood always wrong, or can it be possible that the end should justify the means?", "이대로가 더 낫다는 것을 나는 알아요. 그렇지만 말해 주세요 — 거짓말은 언제나 잘못인가요, 아니면 목적이 수단을 옳게 만드는 일이 있을 수도 있나요?"], ["Ought I to have told him the truth, and to have let him know that I could almost kiss the ground on which he stood?”", "나는 그분에게 진실을 말하고, 그분이 서 있던 땅에 입을 맞출 수도 있었다는 것을 알게 해 주어야 했나요?”"]], [["This was a question for the doctors which Mrs. Robarts would not take upon herself to answer.", "이것은 신학자들에게 물을 물음이었으니 로바츠 부인이 스스로 나서서 답할 것이 아니었다."], ["She would not make that falsehood matter of accusation, but neither would she pronounce for it any absolution.", "그녀는 그 거짓말을 몰아세울 거리로 삼지도 않겠으나, 그것에 사죄를 선언해 주지도 않으려 하였다."], ["In that matter Lucy must regulate her own conscience.", "그 일에 관하여는 루시가 스스로 제 양심을 다스려야 했다."], ["“And what shall I do next?”", "“그러면 나는 다음에 무엇을 해야 하나요?”"], ["said Lucy, still speaking in a tone that was half tragic and half jeering.", "루시가 여전히 반은 비극이고 반은 비웃음인 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Do?” said Mrs.", "“해야 한다니요?”"], ["Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, something must be done.", "“그래요, 무엇이든 해야 해요."], ["If I were a man I should go to Switzerland, of course; or, as the case is a bad one, perhaps as far as Hungary.", "내가 사내라면 물론 스위스로 가겠지요. 아니면 사정이 나쁜 편이니 어쩌면 헝가리까지 갈지도 모르겠어요."], ["What is it that girls do?", "처녀들은 어떻게 하나요?"], ["they don’t die now-a-days, I believe.”", "요즘은 죽지는 않는 모양이지요.”"]], [["“Lucy, I do not believe that you care for him one jot.", "“루시, 나는 루시가 그분에게 티끌만큼도 마음을 쓴다고 믿지 않아요."], ["If you were in love you would not speak of it like that.”", "사랑에 빠졌다면 그것을 그런 식으로 말하지는 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“There, there.", "“그래요, 그래요."], ["That’s my only hope.", "그것이 내 단 하나뿐인 바람이에요."], ["If I could laugh at myself till it had become incredible to you, I also, by degrees, should cease to believe that I had cared for him.", "패니에게 그것이 믿기지 않게 될 만큼 나 자신을 비웃을 수 있다면, 나도 차차 내가 그분에게 마음을 썼다는 것을 믿지 않게 되겠지요."], ["But, Fanny, it is very hard.", "하지만 패니, 이건 아주 힘들어요."], ["If I were to starve, and rise before daybreak, and pinch myself, or do some nasty work,—clean the pots and pans and the candlesticks; that I think would do the most good.", "내가 굶고, 날이 새기 전에 일어나고, 나 자신을 꼬집고, 아니면 무슨 지저분한 일을 한다면 — 냄비와 팬과 촛대를 닦는 일 같은 것이요. 그것이 가장 큰 효험이 있을 것 같아요."], ["I have got a piece of sack-cloth, and I mean to wear that, when I have made it up.”", "굵은 베 한 조각을 얻어 두었는데, 그것을 지어 놓으면 입을 생각이에요.”"]], [["“You are joking now, Lucy, I know.”", "“지금은 농을 하고 있네요, 루시. 나도 알아요.”"]], [["“No, by my word; not in the spirit of what I am saying.", "“아니에요, 맹세코요. 내가 하는 말의 속뜻으로는 농이 아니에요."], ["How shall I act upon my heart, if I do not do it through the blood and the flesh?”", "피와 살을 거치지 않고서야 어떻게 내 마음에 손을 쓰겠어요?”"]], [["“Do you not pray that God will give you strength to bear these troubles?”", "“이런 괴로움을 견딜 힘을 하느님께서 주시기를 기도하지 않아요?”"]], [["“But how is one to word one’s prayer, or how even to word one’s wishes?", "“하지만 기도를 어떻게 말로 지어야 하나요? 아니, 바라는 것조차 어떻게 말로 지어야 하나요?"], ["I do not know what is the wrong that I have done.", "나는 내가 저지른 잘못이 무엇인지를 몰라요."], ["I say it boldly; in this matter I cannot see my own fault.", "대담하게 말하겠어요. 이 일에서 나는 내 잘못을 알아볼 수가 없어요."], ["I have simply found that I have been a fool.”", "나는 그저 내가 바보였다는 것을 알아냈을 뿐이에요.”"]], [["It was now quite dark in the room, or would have been so to any one entering it afresh.", "이제 방 안은 아주 어두웠다. 적어도 새로 들어서는 사람에게라면 그러하였을 것이다."], ["They had remained there talking till their eyes had become accustomed to the gloom, and would still have remained, had they not suddenly been disturbed by the sound of a horse’s feet.", "두 사람은 눈이 그 어스름에 익숙해질 때까지 거기 남아 이야기하고 있었고, 갑자기 말발굽 소리에 방해받지 않았더라면 그대로 더 남아 있었을 것이다."]], [["“There is Mark,”", "“마크가 왔어요,”"], ["said Fanny, jumping up and running to the bell, that lights might be ready when he should enter.", "패니가 벌떡 일어나 하인을 부르는 종 줄로 달려가며 말했다. 그가 들어설 때에 등불이 준비되어 있게 하려는 것이었다."]], [["“I thought he remained in Barchester to-night.”", "“오늘 밤은 바체스터에 머무는 줄 알았는데요.”"]], [["“And so did I; but he said it might be doubtful.", "“나도 그렇게 알았어요. 하지만 확실하지 않을지도 모른다고 했어요."], ["What shall we do if he has not dined?”", "저녁을 먹지 않았으면 어쩌지요?”"]], [["That, I believe, is always the first thought in the mind of a good wife when her husband returns home.", "남편이 집으로 돌아올 때 어진 아내의 마음에 언제나 가장 먼저 떠오르는 생각이 바로 그것이라고 나는 믿는다."], ["Has he had his dinner?", "저녁은 먹었을까?"], ["What can I give him for dinner?", "저녁으로 무엇을 내줄 수 있을까?"], ["Will he like his dinner?", "저녁이 마음에 들까?"], ["Oh dear, oh dear!", "아이고, 아이고!"], ["there is nothing in the house but cold mutton.", "집에는 찬 양고기밖에 없는데."], ["But on this occasion the lord of the mansion had dined, and came home radiant with good-humour, and owing, perhaps, a little of his radiance to the dean’s claret.", "그러나 이번 경우에 이 댁의 주인은 저녁을 먹었고, 기분 좋음으로 환하게 빛나며 집에 돌아왔으니, 그 빛의 얼마쯤은 어쩌면 수석 사제의 클라레 덕이었다. “내가 그 사람들한테 말해 두었어,”"], ["“I have told them,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “that they may keep possession of the house for the next two months, and they have agreed to that arrangement.”", "“앞으로 두 달 동안 그 집을 그대로 차지하고 있어도 좋다고. 그쪽에서도 그렇게 하기로 했고.”"]], [["“That is very pleasant,”", "“그건 아주 반가운 일이네요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“And I don’t think we shall have so much trouble about the dilapidations after all.”", "“그리고 훼손 배상을 두고서도 결국 그리 큰 말썽은 없을 것 같아.”"]], [["“I am very glad of that,”", "“그건 참 기쁜 일이에요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."], ["But nevertheless she was thinking much more of Lucy than of the house in Barchester Close.", "그러나 그러면서도 그녀는 바체스터 구내의 그 집보다 루시를 훨씬 더 많이 생각하고 있었다."]], [["“You won’t betray me,”", "“나를 팔아넘기지는 않을 거지요,”"], ["said Lucy, as she gave her sister-in-law a parting kiss at night.", "그날 밤 올케에게 헤어지는 입맞춤을 하면서 루시가 말했다."]], [["“No; not unless you give me permission.”", "“그럼요. 루시가 허락을 주지 않는 한은요.”"]], [["“Ah; I shall never do that.”", "“아, 나는 결코 허락하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXVII. SOUTH AUDLEY STREET.", "# 제27장. 사우스 오들리 스트리트"]], [["The Duke of Omnium had notified to Mr. Fothergill his wish that some arrangement should be made about the Chaldicotes mortgages, and Mr.", "옴니엄 공작은 콜디코츠 저당 건을 어떻게든 정리하고 싶다는 뜻을 포더길 씨에게 통고해 두었고, 포더길 씨는 변호사의 온갖 군말을 다 붙여 지시서로 적어 놓은 것이나 다름없이 공작의 뜻을 알아들었다."], ["Fothergill had understood what the duke meant as well as though his instructions had been written down with all a lawyer’s verbosity. The duke’s meaning was this, that Chaldicotes was to be swept up and garnered, and made part and parcel of the Gatherum property.", "공작의 뜻은 이러했다."], ["It had seemed to the duke that that affair between his friend and Miss Dunstable was hanging fire, and, therefore, it would be well that Chaldicotes should be swept up and garnered.", "콜디코츠를 쓸어 담아 곳간에 들이고, 개서럼 영지의 일부이자 한 몸으로 만들라는 것."], ["And, moreover, tidings had come into the western division of the county that young Frank Gresham of Boxall Hill was in treaty with the Government for the purchase of all that Crown property called the Chace of Chaldicotes.", "공작이 보기에 자기 벗과 던스터블 양 사이의 일은 지지부진한 듯했으니, 그렇다면 콜디코츠를 쓸어 담아 곳간에 들이는 것이 좋을 터였다."], ["It had been offered to the duke, but the duke had given no definite answer. Had he got his money back from Mr.", "게다가 주의 서부 지구에는 박솔 힐의 젊은 프랭크 그레셤이 콜디코츠 수렵림이라 불리는 왕실 소유지 전부를 사들이려고 정부와 교섭을 벌이고 있다는 소식이 들어와 있었다."], ["Sowerby, he could have forestalled Mr. Gresham; but now that did not seem to be probable, and his grace was resolved that either the one property or the other should be duly garnered.", "그 땅은 공작에게 먼저 제안되었으나 공작은 확답을 주지 않았다."], ["Therefore Mr. Fothergill went up to town, and therefore Mr.", "사워비 씨에게서 돈을 돌려받았더라면 그레셤 씨를 앞지를 수도 있었을 것이나 이제는 그럴 것 같지 않았고, 그래서 각하는 이쪽 땅이든 저쪽 땅이든 하나는 반드시 곳간에 들이겠다고 마음을 굳혔다."], ["Sowerby was, most unwillingly, compelled to have a business interview with Mr. Fothergill.", "그리하여 포더길 씨가 런던으로 올라왔고, 그리하여 사워비 씨는 몹시도 마음에 없는 걸음으로 포더길 씨와 사무적인 면담을 하지 않을 수 없게 되었다."], ["In the meantime, since last we saw him, Mr. Sowerby had learned from his sister the answer which Miss Dunstable had given to his proposition, and knew that he had no further hope in that direction.", "그러는 사이, 우리가 그를 마지막으로 본 뒤로, 사워비 씨는 자기 청혼에 던스터블 양이 준 대답을 누이동생에게서 들어 알고 있었고, 그쪽으로는 더 바랄 것이 없다는 것도 알고 있었다."]], [["There was no further hope thence of absolute deliverance, but there had been a tender of money services. To give Mr.", "그쪽에서 온전한 구원을 바랄 여지는 더 없었으나, 돈으로 도움을 주겠다는 제의는 있었다."], ["Sowerby his due, he had at once declared that it would be quite out of the question that he should now receive any assistance of that sort from Miss Dunstable; but his sister had explained to him that it would be a mere business transaction; that Miss Dunstable would receive her interest; and that, if she would be content with four per cent., whereas the duke received five, and other creditors six, seven, eight, ten, and heaven only knows how much more, it might be well for all parties.", "사워비 씨에게 공평하도록 말해 두자면, 그는 이제 던스터블 양에게서 그런 종류의 도움을 받는다는 것은 어림없는 일이라고 그 자리에서 딱 잘라 말했다. 그러나 누이동생은 그것이 한낱 사무상의 거래일 뿐이라고, 던스터블 양도 이자를 받을 것이라고, 게다가 공작은 백에 다섯을 받고 다른 채권자들은 백에 여섯, 일곱, 여덟, 열에 하늘만이 알 만큼 더 높은 것까지 받는데 그이가 백에 넷으로 만족해 준다면 모두에게 좋은 일이 아니겠느냐고 그에게 설명해 주었다."], ["He, himself, understood, as well as Fothergill had done, what was the meaning of the duke’s message.", "그 자신도 포더길이 그러했듯이 공작의 전갈이 무슨 뜻인지 알고 있었다."], ["Chaldicotes was to be gathered up and garnered, as had been done with so many another fair property lying in those regions.", "콜디코츠는 그 지방에 놓인 다른 좋은 땅이 수없이 그리되었듯이 쓸어 담겨 곳간에 들어갈 것이었다."], ["It was to be swallowed whole, and the master was to walk out from his old family hall, to leave the old woods that he loved, to give up utterly to another the parks and paddocks and pleasant places which he had known from his earliest infancy, and owned from his earliest manhood.", "통째로 삼켜질 것이었고, 주인은 대대로 내려온 제 집 큰방에서 걸어 나가고, 사랑하던 오래된 숲을 떠나고, 갓난 시절부터 알아 온, 그리고 어른이 된 첫날부터 제 것이던 정원과 방목장과 즐거운 자리들을 남에게 온전히 내주게 될 것이었다."]], [["There can be nothing more bitter to a man than such a surrender.", "사내에게 이런 항복보다 더 쓴 것은 있을 수 없다."], ["What, compared to this, can be the loss of wealth to one who has himself made it, and brought it together, but has never actually seen it with his bodily eyes?", "이에 비하면, 제 손으로 벌어 모았을 뿐 실은 제 육신의 눈으로 본 적조차 없는 재물을 잃는 일이 무엇이겠는가? 그런 재물은 어떤 운으로 왔다가 다른 운으로 간다."], ["Such wealth has come by one chance, and goes by another: the loss of it is part of the game which the man is playing; and if he cannot lose as well as win, he is a poor, weak, cowardly creature.", "그것을 잃는 일은 그 사람이 벌이고 있는 승부의 한 부분이며, 이길 줄만 알고 질 줄을 모른다면 그는 딱하고 무르고 겁 많은 물건일 뿐이다. 그런 사내들은 대개 역경에도 그럭저럭 맞먹을 만한 마음가짐을 견딜 줄 안다."], ["Such men, as a rule, do know how to bear a mind fairly equal to adversity.", "그러나 대에서 대로 내려온 땅뙈기를 흥청망청 날려 버렸다는 것!"], ["But to have squandered the acres which have descended from generation to generation; to be the member of one’s family that has ruined that family; to have swallowed up in one’s own maw all that should have graced one’s children, and one’s grandchildren!", "제 집안을 망친 사람이 바로 제 집안 사람이라는 것! 제 자식들과 손자들을 빛내 주었어야 할 것 전부를 제 아귀 속에 삼켜 버렸다는 것!"], ["It seems to me that the misfortunes of this world can hardly go beyond that!", "내가 보기에 이 세상의 불운이 그보다 더 멀리 갈 수는 도무지 없을 것 같다."]], [["Mr. Sowerby, in spite of his recklessness and that dare-devil gaiety which he knew so well how to wear and use, felt all this as keenly as any man could feel it.", "사워비 씨는 그 무모함에도, 그리고 걸치고 쓰는 법을 그토록 잘 아는 그 겁 없는 유쾌함에도 아랑곳없이, 이 모든 것을 누구라도 느낄 수 있는 만큼 사무치게 느꼈다."], ["It had been absolutely his own fault.", "전적으로 제 잘못이었다."], ["The acres had come to him all his own, and now, before his death, every one of them would have gone bodily into that greedy maw.", "그 땅뙈기는 온전히 제 것으로 그에게 왔는데, 이제 그가 죽기도 전에 그 한 조각 한 조각이 통째로 저 탐욕스러운 아귀 속으로 들어가게 될 것이었다."], ["The duke had bought up nearly all the debts which had been secured upon the property, and now could make a clean sweep of it.", "공작은 그 땅을 담보로 잡힌 빚을 거의 다 사들여 두었으니, 이제는 그것을 깨끗이 쓸어 낼 수 있었다."], ["Sowerby, when he received that message from Mr. Fothergill, knew well that this was intended; and he knew well also, that when once he should cease to be Mr.", "사워비는 포더길 씨에게서 그 전갈을 받았을 때 이것이 노려진 일임을 잘 알았다."], ["Sowerby of Chaldicotes, he need never again hope to be returned as member for West Barsetshire.", "그리고 일단 자기가 콜디코츠의 사워비 씨이기를 그치는 날에는 서바셋주 의원으로 다시 뽑히기를 바랄 수 없다는 것도 잘 알았다."], ["This world would for him be all over.", "그에게 이 세상은 다 끝난 것이 될 터였다."], ["And what must such a man feel when he reflects that this world is for him all over?", "그리고 이 세상이 자기에게는 다 끝났다고 헤아릴 때 그런 사내는 무엇을 느껴야 하겠는가?"]], [["On the morning in question he went to his appointment, still bearing a cheerful countenance. Mr.", "그날 아침 그는 여전히 밝은 얼굴을 지은 채 약속 자리로 갔다."], ["Fothergill, when in town on such business as this, always had a room at his service in the house of Messrs. Gumption and Gagebee, the duke’s London law agents, and it was thither that Mr.", "포더길 씨는 이런 일로 런던에 올라와 있을 때면 공작의 런던 법률 대리인인 검션 게이지비 상회의 집에 늘 방 하나를 쓸 수 있었고, 사워비 씨가 불려 간 곳도 바로 거기였다."], ["Sowerby had been summoned. The house of business of Messrs.", "검션 게이지비 상회의 사무소는 사우스 오들리 스트리트에 있었다."], ["Gumption and Gagebee was in South Audley Street; and it may be said that there was no spot on the whole earth which Mr.", "그런데 온 세상에 사워비 씨가 그 집 위층의 침침하고 꾀죄죄한 뒤쪽 응접실만큼 미워한 자리는 없었다고 해도 좋을 것이다."], ["Sowerby so hated as he did the gloomy, dingy back sitting-room up-stairs in that house. He had been there very often, but had never been there without annoyance.", "그는 그곳에 아주 여러 번 가 보았으나, 갈 때마다 곤욕을 치르지 않은 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["It was a horrid torture-chamber, kept for such dread purposes as these, and no doubt had been furnished, and papered, and curtained with the express object of finally breaking down the spirits of such poor country gentlemen as chanced to be involved.", "그것은 바로 이런 무서운 용도로 마련된 끔찍한 고문실이었고, 어쩌다 빚에 얽매인 딱한 시골 신사들의 기운을 마지막으로 꺾어 놓겠다는 뚜렷한 목적으로 가구를 들이고 벽지를 바르고 커튼을 달았음이 틀림없었다."], ["Everything was of a brown crimson,—of a crimson that had become brown.", "무엇이나 다 갈색빛 다홍이었다. 갈색이 되어 버린 다홍이었다."], ["Sunlight, real genial light of the sun, never made its way there, and no amount of candles could illumine the gloom of that brownness.", "햇빛, 진짜 따스한 해의 빛은 그 안까지 들어오는 법이 없었고, 촛불을 아무리 많이 켜도 그 갈색의 음침함을 밝힐 수는 없었다. 창은 한 번도 닦인 적이 없었다."], ["The windows were never washed; the ceiling was of a dark brown; the old Turkey carpet was thick with dust, and brown withal.", "천장은 짙은 갈색이었다. 낡은 튀르크 융단은 먼지가 두껍게 앉은, 그것도 갈색인 융단이었다."], ["The ungainly office-table, in the middle of the room, had been covered with black leather, but that was now brown.", "방 가운데 놓인 투박한 사무 탁자는 검은 가죽을 씌워 놓았던 것이나 이제는 그것도 갈색이었다."], ["There was a bookcase full of dingy brown law books in a recess on one side of the fireplace, but no one had touched them for years, and over the chimney-piece hung some old legal pedigree table, black with soot. Such was the room which Mr.", "난로 한쪽 벽감에는 꾀죄죄한 갈색 법률서가 가득한 책장이 있었으나 몇 해째 아무도 손대지 않았고, 난로 선반 위에는 그을음으로 검어진 낡은 법률 가계도 액자가 걸려 있었다."], ["Fothergill always used in the business house of Messrs. Gumption and Gagebee, in South Audley Street, near to Park Lane.", "파크 레인 가까운 사우스 오들리 스트리트의 검션 게이지비 상회 사무소에서 포더길 씨가 늘 쓰는 방이 이런 방이었다."]], [["I once heard this room spoken of by an old friend of mine, one Mr.", "나는 언젠가 이 방에 대한 이야기를 내 오랜 벗 하나에게서 들은 적이 있다."], ["Gresham of Greshamsbury, the father of Frank Gresham, who was now about to purchase that part of the Chace of Chaldicotes which belonged to the Crown.", "그레셤스베리의 그레셤 씨, 곧 이제 콜디코츠 수렵림 가운데 왕실에 속한 부분을 사들이려는 프랭크 그레셤의 아버지 되는 이다."], ["He also had had evil days, though now happily they were past and gone; and he, too, had sat in that room, and listened to the voice of men who were powerful over his property, and intended to use that power.", "그에게도 궂은 날이 있었다. 이제는 다행히 지나가 사라졌지만, 그 역시 저 방에 앉아 자기 재산을 마음대로 할 힘을 쥐고 그 힘을 쓸 작정인 사람들의 목소리를 들은 적이 있었다."], ["The idea which he left on my mind was much the same as that which I had entertained, when a boy, of a certain room in the castle of Udolpho.", "그가 내 마음에 남겨 놓은 그림은, 내가 아이였을 때 우돌포 성의 어느 방을 두고 품었던 그림과 거의 같았다."], ["There was a chair in that Udolpho room in which those who sat were dragged out limb by limb, the head one way and the legs another; the fingers were dragged off from the hands, and the teeth out from the jaws, and the hair off the head, and the flesh from the bones, and the joints from their sockets, till there was nothing left but a lifeless trunk seated in the chair. Mr.", "그 우돌포의 방에는 의자가 하나 있어, 거기 앉은 사람은 팔다리가 하나씩 뽑혀 나가고, 머리는 이쪽으로 다리는 저쪽으로 끌려가고, 손에서는 손가락이 뜯겨 나가고, 턱에서는 이가 빠져 나가고, 머리에서는 머리털이, 뼈에서는 살이, 이음자리에서는 관절이 빠져 나가, 마침내 의자에 앉은 숨 없는 몸통 하나밖에 남지 않았다."], ["Gresham, as he told me, always sat in the same seat, and the tortures he suffered when so seated, the dislocations of his property which he was forced to discuss, the operations on his very self which he was forced to witness, made me regard that room as worse than the chamber of Udolpho.", "그레셤 씨는 나에게 말하기를 언제나 같은 자리에 앉았다고 했는데, 그렇게 앉아서 겪은 고문, 억지로 의논해야 했던 제 재산의 관절 빠짐, 억지로 지켜보아야 했던 제 몸 자체에 대한 수술은 내가 그 방을 우돌포의 방보다 더 나쁜 곳으로 여기게 만들었다."], ["He, luckily—a rare instance of good fortune—had lived to see all his bones and joints put together again, and flourishing soundly; but he never could speak of the room without horror.", "그는 운 좋게도, 드문 행운의 예로서, 자기 뼈와 관절이 모두 다시 맞추어져 튼튼하게 살아나는 것을 볼 만큼 오래 살았다. 그러나 그 방을 이야기할 때면 언제나 소름이 돋지 않고서는 말하지 못했다."]], [["“No consideration on earth,”", "“이 세상 무엇을 놓고 따져 봐도,”"], ["he once said to me, very solemnly,—“I say none, should make me again enter that room.”", "언젠가 그는 나에게 아주 엄숙하게 말했다. “하나도 없소, 내가 그 방에 다시 들어가게 만들 수 있는 것은.”"], ["And indeed this feeling was so strong with him, that from the day when his affairs took a turn he would never even walk down South Audley Street. On the morning in question into this torture-chamber Mr.", "실로 이 감정이 그에게 어찌나 강했던지, 자기 형편이 돌아서던 날부터 그는 사우스 오들리 스트리트를 걸어 내려가는 일조차 하지 않았다."], ["Sowerby went, and there, after some two or three minutes, he was joined by Mr. Fothergill.", "그날 아침 사워비 씨는 이 고문실로 들어갔고, 거기서 이삼 분 뒤에 포더길 씨가 그와 자리를 같이했다."]], [["Mr. Fothergill was, in one respect, like to his friend Sowerby.", "포더길 씨는 한 가지 점에서 그의 벗 사워비와 닮았다."], ["He enacted two altogether different persons on occasions which were altogether different.", "그는 전혀 다른 자리에서 전혀 다른 두 사람을 연기했다."], ["Generally speaking, with the world at large, he was a jolly, rollicking, popular man, fond of eating and drinking, known to be devoted to the duke’s interests, and supposed to be somewhat unscrupulous, or at any rate hard, when they were concerned; but in other respects a good-natured fellow; and there was a report about that he had once lent somebody money, without charging him interest or taking security.", "대체로 세상 사람들 앞에서는 먹고 마시기를 좋아하는, 흥이 많고 떠들썩하고 사람들이 좋아하는 사내였으며, 공작의 이익에 헌신한다고 알려져 있었고, 그 이익이 걸린 일에서는 다소 원칙이 없다거나 적어도 매섭다고들 여겨졌으나, 그 밖의 점에서는 사람 좋은 사내였다. 게다가 그가 언젠가 누구에게 이자도 물리지 않고 담보도 잡지 않고 돈을 빌려주었다는 소문까지 돌았다."], ["On the present occasion Sowerby saw at a glance that he had come thither with all the aptitudes and appurtenances of his business about him.", "이번 자리에서 사워비는 그가 제 일에 딸린 온갖 재간과 부속물을 갖추고 왔음을 한눈에 알아보았다. 그는 짧고 빠른 걸음으로 방에 들어왔다."], ["He walked into the room with a short, quick step; there was no smile on his face as he shook hands with his old friend; he brought with him a box laden with papers and parchments, and he had not been a minute in the room before he was seated in one of the old dingy chairs.", "오랜 벗과 악수를 하면서도 얼굴에 웃음이 없었다. 서류와 양피 문서를 잔뜩 담은 상자를 들고 왔고, 방에 들어선 지 채 일 분도 되지 않아 벌써 그 낡고 꾀죄죄한 의자 하나에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“How long have you been in town, Fothergill?”", "“런던에 온 지 얼마나 됐나, 포더길?”"], ["said Sowerby, still standing with his back against the chimney.", "사워비가 여전히 난로에 등을 대고 선 채로 말했다."], ["He had resolved on only one thing—that nothing should induce him to touch, look at, or listen to any of those papers.", "그가 마음을 굳혀 둔 것은 딱 하나였다. 무슨 일이 있어도 저 서류에 손을 대거나 눈을 주거나 귀를 기울이지는 않겠다는 것."], ["He knew well enough that no good would come of that.", "그런 짓에서 좋은 것이 나오지 않는다는 것쯤은 잘 알고 있었다."], ["He also had his own lawyer, to see that he was pilfered according to rule.", "그에게도 제 변호사가 있어서, 규칙에 맞게 털리고 있는지 지켜보게 해 두었다."]], [["“How long?", "“얼마나 됐냐고요?"], ["Since the day before yesterday.", "그저께부터요."], ["I never was so busy in my life.", "내 평생 이렇게 바빴던 적이 없어요."], ["The duke, as usual, wants to have everything done at once.”", "공작께서는 언제나처럼 무엇이든 한꺼번에 다 되기를 바라시니까요.”"]], [["“If he wants to have all that I owe him paid at once, he is like to be out in his reckoning.”", "“내가 각하께 빚진 것을 한꺼번에 다 갚기를 바라신다면, 셈이 어긋나실 걸세.”"]], [["“Ah, well; I’m glad you are ready to come quickly to business, because it’s always best.", "“아, 그렇군요. 당신이 곧바로 본론으로 들어올 마음이라 반갑습니다."], ["Won’t you come and sit down here?”", "그게 언제나 제일 좋으니까요. 이리 와서 앉지 않겠어요?”"]], [["“No, thank you; I’ll stand.”", "“고맙지만 사양하겠네. 서 있겠어.”"]], [["“But we shall have to go through these figures, you know.”", "“하지만 이 숫자들은 함께 훑어야 할 텐데요.”"]], [["“Not a figure, Fothergill.", "“숫자는 하나도 안 볼 걸세, 포더길."], ["What good would it do?", "그게 무슨 소용인가?"], ["None to me, and none to you either, as I take it; if there is anything wrong, Potter’s fellows will find it out.", "내게도 소용없고, 내가 보기에 자네에게도 소용없어. 뭐 잘못된 것이 있으면 포터네 사람들이 찾아낼 테지."], ["What is it the duke wants?”", "공작이 원하는 게 뭔가?”"]], [["“Well; to tell the truth, he wants his money.”", "“음, 사실대로 말하자면, 자기 돈을 원하십니다.”"]], [["“In one sense, and that the main sense, he has got it.", "“한 가지 뜻으로는, 그것도 가장 중요한 뜻으로는, 이미 갖고 계시지."], ["He gets his interest regularly, does not he?”", "이자는 어김없이 받고 계시지 않은가?”"]], [["“Pretty well for that, seeing how times are.", "“요즘 시절이 어떤지를 보면 그 점은 그럭저럭 괜찮지요."], ["But, Sowerby, that’s nonsense.", "하지만 사워비, 그건 말이 안 되는 얘기예요."], ["You understand the duke as well as I do, and you know very well what he wants.", "당신도 나만큼 공작을 잘 알고, 각하가 무엇을 원하시는지도 아주 잘 알잖아요."], ["He has given you time, and if you had taken any steps towards getting the money, you might have saved the property.”", "각하는 당신에게 시간을 주셨어요. 당신이 돈을 마련하려고 무슨 걸음이라도 내디뎠더라면 그 땅을 지킬 수도 있었을 겁니다.”"]], [["“A hundred and eighty thousand pounds!", "“십팔만 파운드라니!"], ["What steps could I take to get that?", "그걸 마련하려고 내가 무슨 걸음을 내디딜 수 있었겠나?"], ["Fly a bill, and let Tozer have it to get cash on it in the City!”", "어음이나 한 장 띄워서 토저더러 시티에 가져가 현금으로 바꿔 오게 하면 됐겠나!”"]], [["“We hoped you were going to marry.”", "“우리는 당신이 혼인하시려는 줄 알았지요.”"]], [["“That’s all off.”", "“그건 다 틀어졌네.”"]], [["“Then I don’t think you can blame the duke for looking for his own.", "“그렇다면 각하가 제 것을 찾으려 하시는 것을 두고 당신이 각하를 책망할 수는 없다고 봅니다."], ["It does not suit him to have so large a sum standing out any longer.", "그만큼 큰 돈이 더 오래 묶여 있는 것은 각하 형편에 맞지 않아요."], ["You see, he wants land, and will have it.", "보시다시피 각하는 땅을 원하시고, 또 손에 넣으실 겁니다."], ["Had you paid off what you owed him, he would have purchased the Crown property; and now, it seems, young Gresham has bid against him, and is to have it.", "당신이 빚진 것을 갚아 놓았더라면 각하가 그 왕실 땅을 사셨을 텐데, 이제는 젊은 그레셤이 맞서 값을 불러 그 땅을 차지하게 되었다지요. 이 일로 각하의 심사가 뒤틀리셨습니다."], ["This has riled him, and I may as well tell you fairly, that he is determined to have either money or marbles.”", "그러니 이쯤은 솔직히 말해 두는 게 좋겠는데, 각하는 돈이든 땅이든 어느 한쪽은 반드시 받아 내겠다고 작정하셨어요.”"]], [["“You mean that I am to be dispossessed.”", "“그러니까 내가 내 땅에서 쫓겨난다는 말이군.”"]], [["“Well, yes; if you choose to call it so.", "“그렇지요, 뭐. 당신이 그렇게 부르고 싶다면요."], ["My instructions are to foreclose at once.”", "내가 받은 지시는 당장 저당권을 실행하라는 것입니다.”"]], [["“Then I must say the duke is treating me most uncommonly ill.”", "“그렇다면 공작이 나를 유별나게 못되게 다루고 있다고 말할 수밖에 없네.”"]], [["“Well, Sowerby, I can’t see it.”", "“글쎄요, 사워비, 나는 그렇게 보이지 않는데요.”"]], [["“I can, though.", "“나는 그렇게 보이네."], ["He has his money like clock-work; and he has bought up these debts from persons who would have never disturbed me as long as they got their interest.”", "각하는 제 돈을 시계처럼 어김없이 받아 갔어. 그런데도 이자만 들어가면 나를 조금도 건드리지 않았을 사람들한테서 그 빚들을 사들였지.”"]], [["“Haven’t you had the seat?”", "“의석은 차지하셨잖아요?”"]], [["“The seat!", "“의석!"], ["and is it expected that I am to pay for that?”", "그러니 내가 그 값을 치러야 한다는 말인가?”"]], [["“I don’t see that any one is asking you to pay for it.", "“누가 당신더러 그 값을 치르라고 하는 것 같지는 않은데요."], ["You are like a great many other people that I know.", "당신은 내가 아는 다른 많은 사람들과 똑같아요."], ["You want to eat your cake and have it.", "케이크를 먹어 놓고도 그대로 갖고 있기를 바라지요."], ["You have been eating it for the last twenty years, and now you think yourself very ill-used because the duke wants to have his turn.”", "지난 이십 년 동안 그것을 먹어 왔으면서, 이제 각하가 자기 차례를 찾겠다고 하니 스스로 몹시 부당한 대우를 받는다고 생각하는군요.”"]], [["“I shall think myself very ill-used if he sells me out—worse than ill-used.", "“각하가 나를 팔아 넘긴다면 나는 정말 몹시 부당한 대우를 받았다고 생각할 걸세."], ["I do not want to use strong language, but it will be more than ill-usage.", "부당한 대우보다 더한 것이지. 험한 말을 쓰고 싶지는 않으나, 그건 부당한 대우 이상일 거야."], ["I can hardly believe that he really means to treat me in that way.”", "각하가 정말로 나를 그런 식으로 다룰 뜻이라는 것을 나는 도무지 믿을 수가 없네.”"]], [["“It is very hard that he should want his own money!”", "“자기 돈을 찾겠다고 하는 것이 그렇게 가혹한 일이랍니까!”"]], [["“It is not his money that he wants.", "“각하가 원하는 것은 자기 돈이 아니야."], ["It is my property.”", "내 땅이지.”"]], [["“And has he not paid for it?", "“그러면 각하가 그 값을 치르지 않으셨습니까?"], ["Have you not had the price of your property?", "당신은 당신 땅의 값을 이미 받지 않았습니까?"], ["Now, Sowerby, it is of no use for you to be angry; you have known for the last three years what was coming on you as well as I did.", "이보세요, 사워비, 당신이 화를 내 봐야 아무 소용없어요. 지난 삼 년 동안 무엇이 당신에게 닥쳐오고 있는지 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있었잖아요."], ["Why should the duke lend you money without an object?", "각하가 아무 속셈도 없이 당신에게 돈을 빌려주셨을 리가 있습니까?"], ["Of course he has his own views.", "물론 각하께는 각하의 뜻이 있지요. 하지만 이 말은 하겠어요."], ["But I do say this; he has not hurried you; and had you been able to do anything to save the place you might have done it.", "각하는 당신을 재촉하지 않으셨습니다. 그리고 당신이 그 자리를 지키기 위해 무엇이든 할 수 있었다면 할 수도 있었을 겁니다."], ["You have had time enough to look about you.”", "주위를 둘러볼 시간은 넉넉히 있었어요.”"]], [["Sowerby still stood in the place in which he had first fixed himself, and now for awhile he remained silent.", "사워비는 처음에 자리를 잡은 그 자리에 여전히 서 있었고, 이제 한동안 말이 없었다."], ["His face was very stern, and there was in his countenance none of those winning looks which often told so powerfully with his young friends,—which had caught Lord Lufton and had charmed Mark Robarts.", "얼굴은 몹시 굳어 있었으며, 그 낯빛에는 젊은 벗들에게 그토록 강하게 먹히던 그 사람 끄는 표정, 러프턴 경을 낚았고 마크 로바츠를 홀렸던 그 표정이 하나도 없었다."], ["The world was going against him, and things around him were coming to an end.", "세상이 그에게 등을 돌리고 있었고, 그를 둘러싼 것들이 끝을 향해 가고 있었다."], ["He was beginning to perceive that he had in truth eaten his cake, and that there was now little left for him to do,—unless he chose to blow out his brains.", "그는 자기가 참으로 케이크를 먹어 버렸으며, 이제는 제 머리에 총알을 박기로 마음먹지 않는 한 남은 일이 별로 없다는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다."], ["He had said to Lord Lufton that a man’s back should be broad enough for any burden with which he himself might load it.", "그는 러프턴 경에게, 사내의 등은 제가 스스로 얹는 어떤 짐에도 넉넉할 만큼 넓어야 한다고 말한 적이 있었다."], ["Could he now boast that his back was broad enough and strong enough for this burden?", "이제 자기 등이 이 짐에 넉넉할 만큼 넓고 튼튼하다고 뻐길 수 있을까?"], ["But he had even then, at that bitter moment, a strong remembrance that it behoved him still to be a man.", "그러나 그 쓰라린 순간에도 그에게는 여전히 사내다워야 마땅하다는 굳은 기억이 있었다."], ["His final ruin was coming on him, and he would soon be swept away out of the knowledge and memory of those with whom he had lived.", "마지막 파멸이 그에게 닥쳐오고 있었고, 그는 이내 함께 살아온 이들의 앎과 기억에서 쓸려 나갈 것이었다."], ["But, nevertheless, he would bear himself well to the last.", "그러나 그럼에도 그는 끝까지 몸가짐을 훌륭히 지킬 것이었다."], ["It was true that he had made his own bed, and he understood the justice which required him to lie upon it.", "제 자리를 제가 깔아 놓은 것이 사실이었으니, 그 위에 누우라고 하는 정의를 그도 알아들었다."]], [["During all this time Fothergill occupied himself with the papers.", "그러는 동안 줄곧 포더길은 서류를 만지며 시간을 보냈다."], ["He continued to turn over one sheet after another, as though he were deeply engaged in money considerations and calculations.", "돈에 관한 고려와 계산에 깊이 잠긴 사람처럼 한 장 한 장 넘겨 갔다."], ["But, in truth, during all that time he did not read a word.", "그러나 실은 그 내내 한 마디도 읽지 않았다."], ["There was nothing there for him to read.", "거기에 그가 읽을 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["The reading and the writing, and the arithmetic in such matters, are done by underlings—not by such big men as Mr.", "이런 일에서 읽고 쓰고 셈하는 것은 아랫사람들이 한다. 포더길 씨 같은 큰 인물이 하는 것이 아니다."], ["Fothergill. His business was to tell Sowerby that he was to go.", "그의 일은 사워비에게 이제 나가야 한다고 말해 주는 것이었다."], ["All those records there were of very little use.", "거기 있는 기록이란 것들은 아주 쓸데가 없었다."], ["The duke had the power; Sowerby knew that the duke had the power; and Fothergill’s business was to explain that the duke meant to exercise his power.", "공작에게 힘이 있었고, 공작에게 힘이 있다는 것을 사워비도 알았으며, 포더길의 일은 공작이 그 힘을 쓸 뜻이라는 것을 설명해 주는 것이었다."], ["He was used to the work, and went on turning over the papers and pretending to read them, as though his doing so were of the greatest moment.", "그는 이 일에 익숙했으므로, 제가 그러고 있는 것이 무엇보다 중대한 일인 양 서류를 계속 넘기며 읽는 척했다."]], [["“I shall see the duke myself,” Mr.", "“내가 직접 공작을 만나겠네.”"], ["Sowerby said at last, and there was something almost dreadful in the sound of his voice.", "사워비 씨가 마침내 말했다. 그 목소리에는 어딘가 거의 무서운 것이 있었다."]], [["“You know that the duke won’t see you on a matter of this kind.", "“공작께서는 이런 종류의 일로는 당신을 만나지 않으실 거라는 걸 아시잖아요."], ["He never speaks to anyone about money; you know that as well as I do.”", "각하는 돈 이야기를 누구와도 하지 않으십니다. 그건 당신도 나만큼 잘 알잖아요.”"]], [["“By ——, but he shall speak to me.", "“빌어먹을 ——, 그래도 그는 나와 말을 하게 될 걸세."], ["Never speak to anyone about money!", "돈 이야기를 누구와도 하지 않는다니!"], ["Why is he ashamed to speak of it when he loves it so dearly?", "그토록 돈을 아끼면서 왜 그 이야기를 하는 것은 부끄러워하나?"], ["He shall see me.”", "그는 나를 만나게 될 걸세.”"]], [["“I have nothing further to say, Sowerby.", "“나는 더 할 말이 없습니다, 사워비."], ["Of course I shan’t ask his grace to see you; and if you force your way in on him you know what will happen.", "물론 각하께 당신을 만나 달라고 청하지는 않겠어요. 그리고 당신이 억지로 밀고 들어간다면 무슨 일이 일어날지는 아시겠지요."], ["It won’t be my doing if he is set against you.", "각하가 당신에게 등을 돌리시게 되더라도 그건 내가 한 일이 아닙니다."], ["Nothing that you say to me in that way,—nothing that anybody ever says,—goes beyond myself.”", "당신이 그런 식으로 내게 하는 말은 무엇이든, 누가 무슨 말을 하든, 내 밖으로 나가지 않아요.”"]], [["“I shall manage the matter through my own lawyer,”", "“이 일은 내 변호사를 통해 처리하겠네.”"], ["said Sowerby; and then he took his hat, and, without uttering another word, left the room.", "사워비는 그렇게 말하고 모자를 집어 들었고, 한 마디도 더 하지 않고 방을 나갔다."]], [["We know not what may be the nature of that eternal punishment to which those will be doomed who shall be judged to have been evil at the last; but methinks that no more terrible torment can be devised than the memory of self-imposed ruin.", "마지막에 악하였다고 판정받을 이들이 떨어지게 될 그 영원한 벌이 어떤 것인지 우리는 알지 못한다. 그러나 내 생각으로는 제 손으로 불러들인 파멸의 기억보다 더 무서운 형벌은 지어낼 수 없을 것 같다."], ["What wretchedness can exceed that of remembering from day to day that the race has been all run, and has been altogether lost; that the last chance has gone, and has gone in vain; that the end has come, and with it disgrace, contempt, and self-scorn—disgrace that never can be redeemed, contempt that never can be removed, and self-scorn that will eat into one’s vitals for ever?", "달릴 길은 다 달렸고 그것을 온전히 잃어버렸다는 것을, 마지막 기회가 가 버렸고 그것도 헛되이 가 버렸다는 것을, 끝이 왔으며 그와 함께 창피와 멸시와 자기 조롱이 왔다는 것을, 결코 씻을 수 없는 창피와 결코 벗을 수 없는 멸시와 영원히 제 창자를 파먹을 자기 조롱이 왔다는 것을 날마다 날마다 기억하는 것보다 더한 비참이 무엇이겠는가?"]], [["Mr. Sowerby was now fifty; he had enjoyed his chances in life; and as he walked back, up South Audley Street, he could not but think of the uses he had made of them. He had fallen into the possession of a fine property on the attainment of his manhood; he had been endowed with more than average gifts of intellect; never-failing health had been given to him, and a vision fairly clear in discerning good from evil; and now to what a pass had he brought himself!", "사워비 씨는 이제 쉰이었다. 그는 살면서 기회를 누렸다. 그리고 사우스 오들리 스트리트를 되짚어 올라가며 그는 그 기회들을 어떻게 써 왔는지 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 어른이 되던 무렵에 훌륭한 땅을 손에 넣었다. 남들보다 나은 머리의 재간을 타고났다. 한 번도 어긋난 적 없는 건강을 받았고, 선과 악을 가리는 데 꽤나 밝은 눈도 받았다. 그런데 이제 그는 자신을 어떤 처지에 몰아넣었는가!"]], [["And that man Fothergill had put all this before him in so terribly clear a light!", "그런데 저 포더길이라는 사람이 이 모든 것을 그토록 무섭도록 밝은 빛 아래 그의 앞에 늘어놓았던 것이다!"], ["Now that the day for his final demolishment had arrived, the necessity that he should be demolished—finished away at once, out of sight and out of mind—had not been softened, or, as it were, half hidden, by any ambiguous phrase.", "그가 마지막으로 헐려 나갈 날이 닥친 이제, 그가 헐려야 한다는 것, 눈에서도 마음에서도 사라지도록 단번에 처치되어야 한다는 것은 어떤 애매한 말로도 부드러워지지 않았고, 이를테면 반쯤 가려지지도 않았다."], ["“You have had your cake, and eaten it—eaten it greedily.", "“당신은 케이크를 가졌고, 그것을 먹었어요. 그것도 걸신들려 먹었지요."], ["Is not that sufficient for you?", "그만하면 넉넉하지 않습니까?"], ["Would you eat your cake twice?", "케이크를 두 번 먹으려고요?"], ["Would you have a succession of cakes?", "케이크가 줄줄이 이어져 나오기를 바라나요?"], ["No, my friend; there is no succession of these cakes for those who eat them greedily.", "아니지요, 이보세요. 걸신들려 먹는 자에게는 이 케이크가 줄줄이 이어져 나오는 일이 없습니다."], ["Your proposition is not a fair one, and we who have the whip-hand of you will not listen to it.", "당신의 제안은 온당하지 않고, 채찍을 쥔 쪽인 우리는 그것을 들어 줄 생각이 없어요."], ["Be good enough to vanish.", "부디 사라져 주시지요."], ["Permit yourself to be swept quietly into the dunghill.", "조용히 거름 더미로 쓸려 들어가도록 몸을 맡기세요."], ["All that there was about you of value has departed from you; and allow me to say that you are now—rubbish.”", "당신에게 값이 나가는 것이라고는 죄다 당신을 떠나 버렸습니다. 그러니 이런 말씀을 드리게 해 주세요."], ["And then the ruthless besom comes with irresistible rush, and the rubbish is swept into the pit, there to be hidden for ever from the sight.", "당신은 이제, 쓰레기입니다.” 그러고 나면 무자비한 빗자루가 뿌리칠 수 없는 기세로 다가와, 쓰레기는 구덩이 속으로 쓸려 들어가 거기서 영원히 눈앞에서 감추어지는 것이다."]], [["And the pity of it is this—that a man, if he will only restrain his greed, may eat his cake and yet have it; ay, and in so doing will have twice more the flavour of the cake than he who with gourmandizing maw will devour his dainty all at once.", "그리고 애석한 것은 이것이다. 사람이 제 탐욕만 눌러 둔다면 케이크를 먹고도 그것을 그대로 가질 수 있다는 것."], ["Cakes in this world will grow by being fed on, if only the feeder be not too insatiate.", "그렇다, 그리고 그렇게 하면 걸신들린 아귀로 제 별미를 한꺼번에 삼켜 버리는 자보다 그 케이크의 맛을 두 배로 더 누린다는 것. 이 세상의 케이크는 먹으면 먹을수록 자란다."], ["On all which wisdom Mr.", "먹는 자가 지나치게 탐욕스럽지만 않다면."], ["Sowerby pondered with sad heart and very melancholy mind as he walked away from the premises of Messrs. Gumption and Gagebee.", "이 모든 지혜를 두고 사워비 씨는 검션 게이지비 상회의 건물을 떠나 걸으면서 슬픈 가슴과 몹시 우울한 마음으로 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["His intention had been to go down to the House after leaving Mr. Fothergill, but the prospect of immediate ruin had been too much for him, and he knew that he was not fit to be seen at once among the haunts of men.", "그의 뜻은 포더길 씨와 헤어진 뒤에 하원으로 내려가는 것이었으나, 당장 닥친 파멸의 앞날이 그에게 너무 무거웠고, 자기가 지금 곧바로 사람들 모이는 자리에 얼굴을 내밀 만한 상태가 아니라는 것을 그는 알았다."], ["And he had intended also to go down to Barchester early on the following morning—only for a few hours, that he might make further arrangements respecting that bill which Robarts had accepted for him.", "그리고 그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 바체스터로 내려갈 뜻도 있었다. 몇 시간만 다녀올 생각이었는데, 로바츠가 자기를 위해 인수해 준 그 어음에 관해 손을 더 써 두기 위해서였다."], ["That bill—the second one—had now become due, and Mr. Tozer had been with him.", "그 어음, 두 번째 것이 이제 만기가 되었고, 토저 씨가 그를 찾아왔던 것이다."]], [["“Now it ain’t no use in life, Mr. Sowerby,”", "“이래 봐야 세상에 아무 쓸데도 없습니다요, 사워비 씨.”"], ["Tozer had said.", "토저는 그렇게 말했다."], ["“I ain’t got the paper myself, nor didn’t ’old it, not two hours.", "“그 종이는 나한테 없고, 두 시간도 손에 쥐고 있지 않았습니다요."], ["It went away through Tom Tozer; you knows that, Mr.", "그건 톰 토저를 거쳐 나가 버렸어요."], ["Sowerby, as well as I do.”", "그건 사워비 씨도 나만큼이나 잘 아시잖습니까요.”"]], [["Now, whenever Tozer, Mr. Sowerby’s Tozer, spoke of Tom Tozer, Mr.", "그런데 토저, 곧 사워비 씨의 토저가 톰 토저를 입에 올릴 때면, 사워비 씨는 일곱 귀신이 불려 나오고 있다는 것을, 그 하나하나가 첫 귀신보다 더 악하다는 것을 알았다."], ["Sowerby knew that seven devils were being evoked, each worse than the first devil. Mr.", "사워비 씨는 자기가 꾀어 화 속으로 끌어들인 그 딱한 목사에게 참된 마음 같은 것, 아니 오히려 애정 같은 것을 정말로 느끼고 있었고, 될 수 있다면 그를 토저의 독니에서 건져 주고 싶었다."], ["Sowerby did feel something like sincere regard, or rather love, for that poor parson whom he had inveigled into mischief, and would fain save him, if it were possible, from the Tozer fang. Mr.", "바체스터 은행의 포레스트 씨가 로바츠 씨를 위해 그 마지막 오백 파운드 어음을 떠맡아 줄 것 같았다."], ["Forrest, of the Barchester bank, would probably take up that last five hundred pound bill, on behalf of Mr. Robarts,—only it would be needful that he, Sowerby, should run down and see that this was properly done.", "다만 자기, 곧 사워비가 내려가서 그 일이 제대로 되는지 보아야 할 필요는 있었다."], ["As to the other bill—the former and lesser one—as to that, Mr. Tozer would probably be quiet for a while.", "다른 어음, 곧 앞의 것이자 액수가 더 적은 것에 대해서는, 그것은 토저 씨가 한동안 잠자코 있어 줄 듯했다."]], [["Such had been Sowerby’s programme for these two days; but now—what further possibility was there now that he should care for Robarts, or any other human being; he that was to be swept at once into the dung-heap?", "이 이틀에 대한 사워비의 계획은 그러했다. 그러나 이제, 이제 그가 로바츠를, 또는 다른 어떤 사람을 마음에 둘 여지가 무엇이 더 남아 있단 말인가. 당장 거름 더미로 쓸려 들어갈 처지인 그가?"]], [["In this frame of mind he walked up South Audley Street, and crossed one side of Grosvenor Square, and went almost mechanically into Green Street. At the farther end of Green Street, near to Park Lane, lived Mr.", "이런 마음가짐으로 그는 사우스 오들리 스트리트를 걸어 올라가, 그로스베너 광장의 한쪽을 건너, 거의 기계처럼 그린 스트리트로 들어섰다."], ["and Mrs. Harold Smith.", "그린 스트리트의 저쪽 끝, 파크 레인 가까이에 해럴드 스미스 씨 부부가 살고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVIII. DR. THORNE.", "# 제28장. 손 선생"]], [["When Miss Dunstable met her friends, the Greshams—young Frank Gresham and his wife—at Gatherum Castle, she immediately asked after one Dr.", "던스터블 양이 개서럼 성에서 벗들인 그레셤네 — 젊은 프랭크 그레셤과 그 아내 — 를 만났을 때, 그녀는 곧바로 어떤 손 선생의 안부를 물었다."], ["Thorne, who was Mrs. Gresham’s uncle.", "그레셤 부인의 삼촌이었다."], ["Dr. Thorne was an old bachelor, in whom both as a man and a doctor Miss Dunstable was inclined to place much confidence.", "손 선생은 늙은 독신 남자였는데, 던스터블 양은 사람으로서나 의사로서나 그를 크게 믿는 편이었다."], ["Not that she had ever entrusted the cure of her bodily ailments to Dr. Thorne—for she kept a doctor of her own, Dr.", "그렇다고 제 몸의 병을 손 선생에게 맡겨 고친 적이 있는 것은 아니었다 — 그 일에는 따로 제 의사인 이지먼 선생을 두고 있었으니 — 게다가 어떤 의사의 손길이든 필요할 만한 몸의 병을 그녀가 앓는 일은 좀처럼 없었다고 해도 좋다."], ["Easyman, for this purpose—and it may moreover be said that she rarely had bodily ailments requiring the care of any doctor. But she always spoke of Dr.", "그러나 그녀는 벗들 사이에서 손 선생을 늘 놀라운 학식과 판단력을 지닌 사람이라고 말했고, 한두 번은 아주 중대한 일에서 그의 조언을 구해 그대로 따르기도 했다."], ["Thorne among her friends as a man of wonderful erudition and judgment; and had once or twice asked and acted on his advice in matters of much moment.", "손 선생은 런던 세상에 익숙한 사람이 아니었다. 거기에 집을 두지도 않았고, 수도에 오는 일조차 드물었다."], ["Dr. Thorne was not a man accustomed to the London world; he kept no house there, and seldom even visited the metropolis; but Miss Dunstable had known him at Greshamsbury, where he lived, and there had for some months past grown up a considerable intimacy between them.", "그러나 던스터블 양은 그가 사는 그레셤스베리에서 그를 알게 되었고, 지난 몇 달 사이에 두 사람 사이에는 상당한 친밀함이 자라 있었다."], ["He was now staying at the house of his niece, Mrs.", "그는 지금 조카딸인 그레셤 부인의 집에 머무는 중이었다."], ["Gresham; but the chief reason of his coming up had been a desire expressed by Miss Dunstable, that he should do so.", "그러나 그가 올라온 가장 큰 이유는 그러기를 바란다고 던스터블 양이 말한 데 있었다."], ["She had wished for his advice; and at the instigation of his niece he had visited London and given it.", "그녀는 그의 조언을 원했고, 조카딸의 부추김에 그는 런던에 와서 그 조언을 주었다."]], [["The special piece of business as to which Dr. Thorne had thus been summoned from the bedsides of his country patients, and especially from the bedside of Lady Arabella Gresham, to whose son his niece was married, related to certain large money interests, as to which one might have imagined that Dr.", "이렇게 시골 환자들의 병상에서, 그중에도 조카딸이 그 아들과 혼인한 레이디 애러벨라 그레셤의 병상에서 손 선생을 불러올린 그 특별한 용무는, 어떤 큰 금전상의 이해에 관한 것이었다."], ["Thorne’s advice would not be peculiarly valuable.", "그런 일이라면 손 선생의 조언이 유별나게 값나가지는 않으리라고 여길 만도 했다."], ["He had never been much versed in such matters on his own account, and was knowing neither in the ways of the share market, nor in the prices of land.", "그는 제 일로도 그런 문제에 밝았던 적이 없었고, 주식 시장의 사정도 땅값도 아는 바가 없었다."], ["But Miss Dunstable was a lady accustomed to have her own way, and to be indulged in her own wishes without being called on to give adequate reasons for them.", "그러나 던스터블 양은 제 뜻대로 하는 데 익숙한 여인이었고, 그 뜻에 마땅한 이유를 대라는 요구도 받지 않은 채 하고 싶은 대로 받아들여지는 데 익숙한 여인이었다."]], [["“My dear,”", "“이봐요,”"], ["she had said to young Mrs.", "그녀는 젊은 그레셤 부인에게 말했다."], ["Gresham, “if your uncle don’t come up to London now, when I make such a point of it, I shall think that he is a bear and a savage; and I certainly will never speak to him again,—or to Frank—or to you; so you had better see to it.”", "“내가 이렇게까지 청하는데도 그 삼촌이 지금 런던에 올라오지 않는다면, 나는 그이를 곰이고 야만인이라고 여길 거예요. 그리고 두고 봐요, 다시는 그이한테 말을 붙이지 않을 테니 — 프랭크한테도 — 당신한테도."], ["Mrs. Gresham had not probably taken her friend’s threat as meaning quite all that it threatened.", "그러니 알아서 챙기시는 게 좋아요.”"], ["Miss Dunstable habitually used strong language; and those who knew her well, generally understood when she was to be taken as expressing her thoughts by figures of speech.", "그레셤 부인이 벗의 그 위협을 위협하는 그대로 다 받아들이지는 않았을 것이다. 던스터블 양은 늘 말을 세게 했고, 그녀를 잘 아는 사람들은 그녀가 언제 비유를 빌려 제 생각을 말하는지를 대개 알아들었다."], ["In this instance she had not meant it all; but, nevertheless, Mrs.", "이번에도 그 말을 죄다 진심으로 한 것은 아니었다."], ["Gresham had used violent influence in bringing the poor doctor up to London.", "그러나 그러면서도 그레셤 부인은 딱한 선생을 런던으로 데려오는 데 거센 영향력을 썼다."]], [["“Besides,”", "“게다가,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, “I have resolved on having the doctor at my conversazione, and if he won’t come of himself, I shall go down and fetch him.", "던스터블 양이 말했다. “나는 내 콘베르사치오네에 그 선생을 꼭 모시기로 마음을 굳혔어요."], ["I have set my heart on trumping my dear friend Mrs.", "그이가 제 발로 오지 않겠다면 내가 내려가서 데려올 거예요."], ["Proudie’s best card; so I mean to get everybody!”", "내 다정한 벗 프라우디 부인의 으뜸패를 눌러 버리는 데에 마음을 다 걸어 놓았거든요. 그러니 나는 모두를 다 불러 모을 작정이에요!”"]], [["The upshot of all this was, that the doctor did come up to town, and remained the best part of a week at his niece’s house in Portman Square—to the great disgust of the Lady Arabella, who conceived that she must die if neglected for three days.", "이 모든 일의 결말은, 선생이 정말로 도성에 올라와 포트먼 광장에 있는 조카딸의 집에서 한 주의 거의 전부를 머물렀다는 것이다 — 사흘만 소홀히 대접받으면 죽을 줄로 아는 레이디 애러벨라에게는 크나큰 역겨움이었다."], ["As to the matter of business, I have no doubt but that he was of great use.", "그 용무에 관해서라면, 그가 큰 도움이 되었으리라고 나는 의심하지 않는다."], ["He was possessed of common sense and an honest purpose; and I am inclined to think that they are often a sufficient counterpoise to a considerable amount of worldly experience.", "그는 상식과 정직한 뜻을 지니고 있었다. 그리고 나는 그 둘이 흔히 상당한 양의 세상 경험에 넉넉히 맞설 저울추가 된다고 생각하는 편이다."], ["If one could have the worldly experience also—!", "세상 경험까지 아울러 가질 수 있다면—! 옳은 말이다!"], ["True! but then it is so difficult to get everything.", "그러나 모든 것을 다 얻기란 그렇게 어려운 일이다."], ["But with that special matter of business we need not have any further concern.", "그러나 그 특별한 용무에 우리가 더 마음 쓸 필요는 없다."], ["We will presume it to have been discussed and completed, and will now dress ourselves for Miss Dunstable’s conversazione.", "그것은 이야기되고 마무리되었다고 여기기로 하고, 이제 던스터블 양의 콘베르사치오네에 갈 옷을 차려입기로 하자."]], [["But it must not be supposed that she was so poor in genius as to call her party openly by a name borrowed for the nonce from Mrs. Proudie.", "그러나 그녀가 프라우디 부인에게서 이 자리에 쓰려고 급히 빌려 온 이름으로 제 모임을 드러내어 부를 만큼 재간이 없었다고 여기지는 말아야 한다."], ["It was only among her specially intimate friends, Mrs. Harold Smith and some few dozen others, that she indulged in this little joke.", "이 작은 농담을 즐긴 것은 해럴드 스미스 부인과 그 밖의 몇십 명뿐인, 특별히 가까운 벗들 사이에서였을 뿐이다."], ["There had been nothing in the least pretentious about the card with which she summoned her friends to her house on this occasion.", "이번에 벗들을 제 집으로 부르는 초대장에는 조금도 뻐기는 데가 없었다."], ["She had merely signified in some ordinary way, that she would be glad to see them as soon after nine o’clock on Thursday evening, the —— instant, as might be convenient.", "그녀는 그저 이달 ——일 목요일 저녁 아홉 시가 지난 뒤 편한 대로 되도록 일찍 와 주면 기쁘겠다고, 여느 투로 알렸을 뿐이었다."], ["But all the world understood that all the world was to be gathered together at Miss Dunstable’s house on the night in question,—that an effort was to be made to bring together people of all classes, gods and giants, saints and sinners, those rabid through the strength of their morality, such as our dear friend Lady Lufton, and those who were rabid in the opposite direction, such as Lady Hartletop, the Duke of Omnium, and Mr. Sowerby.", "그러나 온 세상이 알고 있었다 — 그날 밤 온 세상이 던스터블 양의 집에 다 모이게 되어 있다는 것을, 그리고 온갖 계층의 사람들을, 신들과 거인들을, 성인과 죄인들을, 도덕이 너무 굳세어 광기에 이른 이들 — 우리의 다정한 벗 레이디 러프턴 같은 이들 — 과 반대쪽으로 광기에 이른 이들 — 레이디 하틀탑, 옴니엄 공작, 사워비 씨 같은 이들 — 을 한자리에 불러 모으려는 시도가 있을 것을."], ["An orthodox martyr had been caught from the East, and an oily latter-day St. Paul from the other side of the water—to the horror and amazement of Archdeacon Grantly, who had come up all the way from Plumstead to be present on the occasion.", "동방에서는 정교회 순교자 하나를 붙잡아 왔고, 물 건너 저편에서는 기름 반드르르한 오늘날의 성 바울로를 데려왔다 — 이 자리에 있으려고 플럼스테드에서 그 먼 길을 올라온 그랜틀리 부주교에게는 소름 끼치고 어이없는 일이었다."], ["Mrs. Grantly also had hankered to be there; but when she heard of the presence of the latter-day St.", "그랜틀리 부인도 거기 가고 싶어 좀이 쑤셨다."], ["Paul, she triumphed loudly over her husband, who had made no offer to take her.", "그러나 오늘날의 성 바울로가 온다는 말을 듣고서는, 저를 데려가겠다는 말을 한마디도 하지 않은 남편에게 소리 높여 승리를 뽐냈다."], ["That Lords Brock and De Terrier were to be at the gathering was nothing.", "브록 경과 드 테리어 경이 그 모임에 온다는 것은 아무것도 아니었다."], ["The pleasant king of the gods and the courtly chief of the giants could shake hands with each other in any house with the greatest pleasure; but men were to meet who, in reference to each other, could shake nothing but their heads or their fists.", "신들의 유쾌한 왕과 거인들의 정중한 우두머리야 어느 집에서든 서로 더없이 기쁘게 악수를 나눌 수 있다. 그러나 서로를 두고는 고개나 주먹밖에는 흔들 것이 없는 사내들이 만나게 되어 있었다."], ["Supplehouse was to be there, and Harold Smith, who now hated his enemy with a hatred surpassing that of women—or even of politicians.", "서플하우스가 올 것이고, 여자의 미움을 — 아니 정치인의 미움까지도 — 넘어서는 미움으로 이제 제 원수를 미워하는 해럴드 스미스도 올 것이었다."], ["The minor gods, it was thought, would congregate together in one room, very bitter in their present state of banishment; and the minor giants in another, terribly loud in their triumph.", "하급 신들은 지금의 유배 처지에 몹시 씁쓸해하며 한 방에 모여들 것이고, 하급 거인들은 또 다른 방에서 승리에 무섭게 떠들어 댈 것이라고들 생각했다."], ["That is the fault of the giants, who, otherwise, are not bad fellows; they are unable to endure the weight of any temporary success.", "그것이 거인들의 흠이다. 그것만 빼면 나쁜 자들도 아닌데, 그들은 잠시의 성공이 주는 무게를 도무지 견딜 줄 모른다."], ["When attempting Olympus—and this work of attempting is doubtless their natural condition—they scratch and scramble, diligently using both toes and fingers, with a mixture of good-humoured virulence and self-satisfied industry that is gratifying to all parties.", "올림포스를 노리고 오를 때에는 — 그렇게 노리는 일이야말로 의심할 것 없이 그들의 타고난 처지인데 — 발가락과 손가락을 다 부지런히 놀려 긁고 기어오르는데, 그 모양에는 사람 좋은 악의와 자족스러운 근면이 섞여 있어 어느 편에게나 흐뭇하다."], ["But whenever their efforts are unexpectedly, and for themselves unfortunately successful, they are so taken aback that they lose the power of behaving themselves with even gigantesque propriety.", "그러나 그 애씀이 뜻밖에도, 그들 자신에게는 불행하게도 이루어져 버리면, 그들은 어찌할 바를 몰라 거인다운 체통조차 지킬 힘을 잃어버린다."]], [["Such, so great and so various, was to be the intended gathering at Miss Dunstable’s house.", "던스터블 양의 집에 있을 그 모임이란 그토록 크고 그토록 갖가지였다."], ["She herself laughed, and quizzed herself—speaking of the affair to Mrs. Harold Smith as though it were an excellent joke, and to Mrs.", "그녀 자신은 웃어 넘기며 스스로를 놀렸다 — 해럴드 스미스 부인에게는 이 일을 대단한 우스개인 양 말하고, 프라우디 부인에게는 글로스터 플레이스의 그 세상에 이름난 모임들을 그저 흉내 내어 겨루어 보려는 것인 양 말했다."], ["Proudie as though she were simply emulous of rivalling those world-famous assemblies in Gloucester Place; but the town at large knew that an effort was being made, and it was supposed that even Miss Dunstable was somewhat nervous.", "그러나 도성 사람들은 대체로 무언가 큰 애를 쓰고 있다는 것을 알았고, 던스터블 양조차 다소 조바심을 치고 있으리라고들 짐작했다."], ["In spite of her excellent joking it was presumed that she would be unhappy if she failed.", "그렇게 훌륭하게 농담을 하면서도 실패하면 마음 아파할 것이라고 여겨진 것이다."]], [["To Mrs. Frank Gresham she did speak with some little seriousness.", "프랭크 그레셤 부인에게는 그녀도 얼마쯤 진지하게 말했다."], ["“But why on earth should you give yourself all this trouble?”", "서플하우스 씨의 그 대단한 동료 가운데 한 사람이 올지 오지 않을지 마음을 놓을 수 없다고, 그리고 그 놓이지 않는 마음이 괴롭다고 던스터블 양이 실토했을 때, 그 부인은 이렇게 말했다."], ["that lady had said, when Miss Dunstable owned that she was doubtful, and unhappy in her doubts, as to the coming of one of the great colleagues of Mr. Supplehouse.", "“그렇지만 대체 무엇 때문에 그런 수고를 다 하시는 거예요?"], ["“When such hundreds are coming, big wigs and little wigs of all shades, what can it matter whether Mr. Towers be there or not?”", "수백 명이 오는 데다 온갖 빛깔의 큰 가발이며 작은 가발이 다 온다는데, 타워스 씨가 오든 오지 않든 그게 무슨 상관이에요?”"]], [["But Miss Dunstable had answered almost with a screech,—“My dear, it will be nothing without him.", "그러나 던스터블 양은 거의 비명을 지르듯 대답했다. “이봐요, 그이가 없으면 아무것도 아니에요."], ["You don’t understand; but the fact is, that Tom Towers is everybody and everything at present.”", "당신은 모르시는 거예요. 하지만 사실이 그러답니다 — 지금은 톰 타워스가 모든 사람이고 모든 것이에요.”"]], [["And then, by no means for the first time, Mrs.", "그러자 그레셤 부인은, 이번이 처음도 아니건만, 벗의 허영을 두고 훈계를 시작했다."], ["Gresham began to lecture her friend as to her vanity; in answer to which lecture Miss Dunstable mysteriously hinted, that if she were only allowed her full swing on this occasion,—if all the world would now indulge her, she would— She did not quite say what she would do, but the inference drawn by Mrs.", "그 훈계에 던스터블 양은 알쏭달쏭하게 이런 뜻을 비쳤다 — 이번 한 번만 마음껏 하도록 놓아준다면, 온 세상이 이번만 저를 받아 준다면, 자기는 — 무엇을 하겠다는 것인지는 끝까지 말하지 않았으나, 그레셤 부인이 끌어낸 뜻은 이러했다."], ["Gresham was this: that if the incense now offered on the altar of Fashion were accepted, Miss Dunstable would at once abandon the pomps and vanities of this wicked world, and all the sinful lusts of the flesh.", "지금 유행의 제단에 올리는 이 향이 받아들여진다면, 던스터블 양은 이 사악한 세상의 허식과 허영을, 육신의 온갖 죄된 욕정을 당장 버리겠다는 것이었다."]], [["“But the doctor will stay, my dear?", "“이봐요, 그래도 그 선생은 남아 주시는 거죠?"], ["I hope I may look on that as fixed.”", "그것만은 정해진 일로 여겨도 좋겠지요.”"]], [["Miss Dunstable, in making this demand on the doctor’s time, showed an energy quite equal to that with which she invoked the gods that Tom Towers might not be absent. Now, to tell the truth, Dr.", "선생의 시간을 이렇게 청하면서 던스터블 양이 보인 열심은, 톰 타워스가 빠지지 않게 해 달라고 신들에게 빌 때의 열심에 조금도 못하지 않았다."], ["Thorne had at first thought it very unreasonable that he should be asked to remain up in London in order that he might be present at an evening party, and had for a while pertinaciously refused; but when he learned that three or four prime ministers were expected, and that it was possible that even Tom Towers might be there in the flesh, his philosophy also had become weak, and he had written to Lady Arabella to say that his prolonged absence for two days further must be endured, and that the mild tonics, morning and evening, might be continued.", "사실을 말하자면 손 선생은 처음에는 저녁 모임에 나오라고 런던에 붙들려 있으라는 것이 아주 터무니없는 청이라고 여겨 한동안 완강히 거절했다. 그러나 총리가 서너 명 온다는 것을, 그리고 톰 타워스까지도 몸소 그 자리에 있을 수 있다는 것을 알게 되자 그의 초연함도 힘을 잃었고, 그는 레이디 애러벨라에게 편지를 써서 이틀 더 자리를 비우는 것을 참아 주셔야 하겠다고, 아침저녁으로 순한 강장제는 그대로 이어도 좋다고 알렸다."]], [["But why should Miss Dunstable be so anxious that Dr. Thorne should be present on this grand occasion?", "그런데 던스터블 양은 왜 그 대단한 자리에 손 선생이 꼭 있어야 한다고 그렇게 애를 태웠을까?"], ["Why, indeed, should she be so frequently inclined to summon him away from his country practice, his compounding board, and his useful ministrations to rural ailments?", "정말이지 왜 그리 자주 그를 시골 진료에서, 조제대에서, 시골 병자들을 돌보는 요긴한 손길에서 불러내려 했을까?"], ["The doctor was connected with her by no ties of blood.", "선생은 그녀와 아무런 혈연의 끈으로도 이어져 있지 않았다."], ["Their friendship, intimate as it was, had as yet been but of short date.", "그토록 가까웠어도 두 사람의 벗함은 아직 날수가 짧았다."], ["She was a very rich woman, capable of purchasing all manner of advice and good counsel, whereas he was so far from being rich, that any continued disturbance to his practice might be inconvenient to him.", "그녀는 온갖 조언과 좋은 훈수를 다 사들일 수 있는 아주 부유한 여자였고, 그는 부유하기는커녕 진료가 오래 흐트러지면 형편이 곤란해질 사람이었다."], ["Nevertheless, Miss Dunstable seemed to have no more compunction in making calls upon his time, than she might have felt had he been her brother.", "그런데도 던스터블 양은 그의 시간을 불러 쓰는 데에, 그가 제 오라비라도 되었을 때보다 조금도 더 거리끼는 바가 없어 보였다."], ["No ideas on this matter suggested themselves to the doctor himself.", "이런 생각은 선생 자신에게는 하나도 떠오르지 않았다."], ["He was a simple-minded man, taking things as they came, and especially so taking things that came pleasantly.", "그는 마음이 단순한 사람이어서 일을 오는 대로 받아들였고, 즐겁게 오는 일은 더욱 그렇게 받아들였다."], ["He liked Miss Dunstable, and was gratified by her friendship, and did not think of asking himself whether she had a right to put him to trouble and inconvenience.", "그는 던스터블 양이 마음에 들었고 그 벗함이 고마웠으며, 그녀에게 저를 수고롭게 하고 불편하게 할 권리가 있는지를 스스로 물어볼 생각도 하지 않았다."], ["But such ideas did occur to Mrs. Gresham, the doctor’s niece.", "그러나 선생의 조카딸인 그레셤 부인에게는 그런 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["Had Miss Dunstable any object, and if so, what object?", "던스터블 양에게 무슨 뜻이 있는 것일까, 있다면 무슨 뜻일까?"], ["Was it simply veneration for the doctor, or was it caprice?", "그저 선생을 우러르는 마음일까, 아니면 변덕일까?"], ["Was it eccentricity—or could it possibly be love?", "괴벽일까 — 아니면 혹시 사랑일 수도 있을까?"]], [["In speaking of the ages of these two friends it may be said in round terms that the lady was well past forty, and that the gentleman was well past fifty.", "이 두 벗의 나이를 말하자면, 어림잡아 여자는 마흔을 훌쩍 넘었고 남자는 쉰을 훌쩍 넘었다고 할 수 있다."], ["Under such circumstances could it be love?", "그런 형편에 사랑일 수가 있을까?"], ["The lady, too, was one who had had offers almost by the dozen,—offers from men of rank, from men of fashion, and from men of power; from men endowed with personal attractions, with pleasant manners, with cultivated tastes, and with eloquent tongues.", "게다가 그 여자는 청혼을 거의 다발로 받아 온 사람이었다 — 문벌 있는 남자에게서, 유행을 아는 남자에게서, 권세 있는 남자에게서, 몸가짐이 보기 좋고 태도가 사근사근하며 취미가 다듬어졌고 말솜씨가 좋은 남자에게서."], ["Not only had she loved none such, but by none such had she been cajoled into an idea that it was possible that she could love them. That Dr.", "그녀는 그런 이를 하나도 사랑하지 않았을 뿐 아니라, 그런 이들 가운데 누구에게도 저가 그를 사랑할 수 있으리라는 생각으로 구슬려지지 않았다."], ["Thorne’s tastes were cultivated, and his manners pleasant, might probably be admitted by three or four old friends in the country who valued him; but the world in London, that world to which Miss Dunstable was accustomed, and which was apparently becoming dearer to her day by day, would not have regarded the doctor as a man likely to become the object of a lady’s passion.", "손 선생의 취미가 다듬어졌고 그 태도가 사근사근하다는 것은 그를 귀하게 여기는 시골의 오랜 벗 서너 사람이야 인정할 법했다. 그러나 던스터블 양이 익숙한 세상, 날이 갈수록 그녀에게 더 소중해지는 듯한 그 런던의 세상은, 선생을 여자의 연정의 대상이 될 만한 남자로 보아 주지 않았을 것이다."]], [["But nevertheless the idea did occur to Mrs.", "그러면서도 그레셤 부인에게는 그 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["Gresham.", "그녀는 이 시골 의사의 팔꿈치 옆에서 자랐다."], ["She had been brought up at the elbow of this country practitioner; she had lived with him as though she had been his daughter; she had been for years the ministering angel of his household; and, till her heart had opened to the natural love of womanhood, all her closest sympathies had been with him.", "그의 딸이나 되는 것처럼 그와 함께 살았다. 여러 해 동안 그 집안을 돌보는 천사였고, 여인으로서 자연스러운 사랑에 마음이 열리기까지 그녀의 가장 깊은 마음은 다 그에게 있었다."], ["In her eyes the doctor was all but perfect; and it did not seem to her to be out of the question that Miss Dunstable should have fallen in love with her uncle.", "그녀의 눈에 선생은 흠이라 할 것이 거의 없는 사람이었다. 그러니 던스터블 양이 삼촌에게 마음을 두었다는 것이 그녀에게는 있을 수 없는 일로 보이지 않았다."]], [["Miss Dunstable once said to Mrs. Harold Smith that it was possible that she might marry, the only condition then expressed being this, that the man elected should be one who was quite indifferent as to money.", "던스터블 양은 언젠가 해럴드 스미스 부인에게, 자기가 혼인을 할 수도 있다고 말한 적이 있었다."], ["Mrs. Harold Smith, who, by her friends, was presumed to know the world with tolerable accuracy, had replied that such a man Miss Dunstable would never find in this world.", "그때 내놓은 조건이란 오직 하나, 뽑히는 사람은 돈에 아주 무심한 이여야 한다는 것이었다."], ["All this had passed in that half comic vein of banter which Miss Dunstable so commonly used when conversing with such friends as Mrs.", "벗들 사이에서 세상을 그만하면 정확히 안다고 여겨지던 해럴드 스미스 부인은, 그런 남자를 던스터블 양은 이 세상에서 결코 찾지 못할 것이라고 대꾸했다."], ["Harold Smith; but she had spoken words of the same import more than once to Mrs. Gresham; and Mrs.", "이 모든 말은 해럴드 스미스 부인 같은 벗과 이야기할 때 던스터블 양이 으레 쓰는 그 반쯤 우스개 섞인 익살 투로 오간 것이었다."], ["Gresham, putting two and two together as women do, had made four of the little sum; and, as the final result of the calculation, determined that Miss Dunstable would marry Dr.", "그러나 그녀는 같은 뜻의 말을 그레셤 부인에게도 한 번 이상 했다."], ["Thorne if Dr. Thorne would ask her.", "그리고 그레셤 부인은 여자들이 흔히 하듯 둘에 둘을 놓아 그 작은 셈의 답을 넷으로 냈고, 그 셈의 마지막 결론으로, 손 선생이 청하기만 하면 던스터블 양은 손 선생과 혼인하겠다고 마음을 정했다."]], [["And then Mrs. Gresham began to bethink herself of two other questions.", "그러자 그레셤 부인은 또 다른 두 가지 물음을 스스로 헤아려 보기 시작했다."], ["Would it be well that her uncle should marry Miss Dunstable?", "삼촌이 던스터블 양과 혼인하는 것이 좋은 일일까?"], ["and if so, would it be possible to induce him to make such a proposition?", "그렇다면 그가 그런 청혼을 하도록 이끄는 일이 될 법한 일일까?"], ["After the consideration of many pros and cons, and the balancing of very various arguments, Mrs. Gresham thought that the arrangement on the whole might not be a bad one.", "찬반의 여러 이유를 살피고 갖가지 논거의 무게를 견주어 본 뒤, 그레셤 부인은 대체로 이 일이 나쁜 짜임은 아닐 듯하다고 생각했다."], ["For Miss Dunstable she herself had a sincere affection, which was shared by her husband.", "던스터블 양에게 그녀 자신도 참된 정을 품고 있었고, 그 정은 남편도 함께 나누는 것이었다."], ["She had often grieved at the sacrifices Miss Dunstable made to the world, thinking that her friend was falling into vanity, indifference, and an ill mode of life; but such a marriage as this would probably cure all that.", "그녀는 던스터블 양이 세상에 바치는 희생을 여러 번 슬퍼하며, 벗이 허영과 무심함과 좋지 못한 생활 방식에 빠져 든다고 여겼다."], ["And then as to Dr. Thorne himself, to whose benefit were of course applied Mrs.", "그러나 이런 혼인이라면 그 모든 것을 낫게 해 줄 것이었다."], ["Gresham’s most earnest thoughts in this matter, she could not but think that he would be happier married than he was single.", "그리고 손 선생 자신을 두고 말하면 — 이 일에서 그레셤 부인의 가장 절실한 생각이 향한 곳은 물론 그의 좋음이었는데 — 그가 홀몸으로 있기보다 혼인하는 편이 더 행복하겠다고 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["In point of temper, no woman could stand higher than Miss Dunstable; no one had ever heard of her being in an ill humour; and then though Mrs.", "성미로 말하면 던스터블 양보다 높이 설 여자가 없었다. 그녀가 언짢은 기색을 보였다는 말을 들은 사람이 아무도 없었다."], ["Gresham was gifted with a mind which was far removed from being mercenary, it was impossible not to feel that some benefit must accrue from the bride’s wealth.", "또 그레셤 부인은 돈에 팔릴 마음과는 아주 거리가 먼 마음을 타고났으나, 신부의 재산에서 얼마간 이로움이 생기지 않을 수 없다고 느끼지 않을 수는 없었다."], ["Mary Thorne, the present Mrs. Frank Gresham, had herself been a great heiress.", "지금은 프랭크 그레셤 부인인 메리 손도 스스로 큰 재산을 물려받은 사람이었다."], ["Circumstances had weighted her hand with enormous possessions, and hitherto she had not realized the truth of that lesson which would teach us to believe that happiness and riches are incompatible.", "형편이 그녀의 손에 막대한 재산을 얹어 주었고, 행복과 부는 함께 갈 수 없다고 믿게 하려는 그 교훈이 참임을 그녀는 여태 겪어 알지 못했다."], ["Therefore she resolved that it might be well if the doctor and Miss Dunstable were brought together.", "그래서 그녀는 선생과 던스터블 양을 맺어 주는 것이 잘하는 일이겠다고 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["But could the doctor be induced to make such an offer? Mrs.", "그러나 선생이 그런 청혼을 하도록 이끌 수 있을까?"], ["Gresham acknowledged a terrible difficulty in looking at the matter from that point of view.", "그 편에서 이 일을 바라보면 무서운 어려움이 있다는 것을 그레셤 부인은 인정했다. 삼촌은 던스터블 양을 좋아했다."], ["Her uncle was fond of Miss Dunstable; but she was sure that an idea of such a marriage had never entered his head; that it would be very difficult—almost impossible—to create such an idea; and that if the idea were there, the doctor could hardly be instigated to make the proposition.", "그러나 그런 혼인이라는 생각이 그의 머리에 든 적은 한 번도 없다고 그녀는 확신했다. 그런 생각을 지어내 넣기가 매우 어려울 것이며 — 거의 있을 수 없는 일일 것이며 — 그 생각이 들어앉았다 하더라도 선생이 그 말을 꺼내도록 부추기기는 힘들 것이었다."], ["Looking at the matter as a whole, she feared that the match was not practicable.", "일을 통틀어 바라보면서 그녀는 이 혼사가 될 일이 아닐까 걱정했다."]], [["On the day of Miss Dunstable’s party, Mrs. Gresham and her uncle dined together alone in Portman Square.", "던스터블 양의 모임이 있는 날, 그레셤 부인과 삼촌은 포트먼 광장에서 둘이서만 저녁을 먹었다."], ["Mr. Gresham was not yet in Parliament, but an almost immediate vacancy was expected in his division of the county, and it was known that no one could stand against him with any chance of success.", "그레셤 씨는 아직 의회에 들어가지 않았으나 그 주의 자기 지구에 곧 결원이 생길 것으로 보였고, 그를 상대로 나서서 이길 가망이 있는 사람은 아무도 없다는 것이 알려져 있었다."], ["This threw him much among the politicians of his party—those giants, namely, whom it would be his business to support—and on this account he was a good deal away from his own house at the present moment.", "이 때문에 그는 자기 당의 정치인들 — 곧 그가 밀어 주어야 할 그 거인들 — 사이에 자주 끼게 되었고, 그래서 요즘은 제 집을 많이 비웠다."]], [["“Politics make a terrible demand on a man’s time,”", "“정치란 사람의 시간을 무섭게 잡아먹는군.”"], ["he said to his wife; and then went down to dine at his club in Pall Mall with sundry other young philogeants.", "그는 아내에게 이렇게 말하고는, 폴 몰의 클럽으로 내려가 다른 여러 젊은 거인 애호가들과 저녁을 먹었다."], ["On men of that class politics do make a great demand—at the hour of dinner and thereabouts.", "그런 축의 사람들에게 정치가 시간을 크게 잡아먹기는 한다 — 저녁 식사 때와 그 무렵에."]], [["“What do you think of Miss Dunstable?”", "“던스터블 양을 어떻게 생각하세요?”"], ["said Mrs. Gresham to her uncle, as they sat together over their coffee.", "커피를 앞에 놓고 함께 앉았을 때 그레셤 부인이 삼촌에게 말했다."], ["She added nothing to the question, but asked it in all its baldness.", "그녀는 그 물음에 아무것도 덧붙이지 않고, 있는 그대로 앙상하게 물었다."]], [["“Think about her!”", "“던스터블 양을 어떻게 생각하느냐고!”"], ["said the doctor.", "선생이 말했다."], ["“Well, Mary; what do you think about her?", "“글쎄, 메리. 너는 그분을 어떻게 생각하느냐?"], ["I dare say we think the same.”", "아마 우리 생각이 같을 게다.”"]], [["“But that’s not the question.", "“그건 제가 물은 게 아니에요."], ["What do you think about her?", "삼촌은 그분을 어떻게 생각하시느냐고요."], ["Do you think she’s honest?”", "그분이 정직하다고 보세요?”"]], [["“Honest?", "“정직?"], ["Oh, yes, certainly—very honest, I should say.”", "오, 그럼, 물론이지 — 아주 정직하다고 하겠다.”"]], [["“And good-tempered?”", "“성미도 좋고요?”"]], [["“Uncommonly good-tempered.”", "“성미야 유별나게 좋지.”"]], [["“And affectionate?”", "“정도 깊고요?”"]], [["“Well; yes,—and affectionate.", "“글쎄, 그렇지 — 정도 깊지."], ["I should certainly say that she is affectionate.”", "정이 깊은 사람이라고 나는 분명히 말하겠다.”"]], [["“I’m sure she’s clever.”", "“그분이 영리한 건 틀림없어요.”"]], [["“Yes, I think she’s clever.”", "“그래, 영리하다고 본다.”"]], [["“And, and—and womanly in her feelings.” Mrs.", "“그리고, 그리고 — 마음결이 여자답고요.”"], ["Gresham felt that she could not quite say lady-like, though she would fain have done so had she dared.", "그레셤 부인은 차마 ‘숙녀답다’고까지는 말할 수 없다고 느꼈다. 감히 그럴 수 있었다면 기꺼이 그렇게 말했을 것이다."]], [["“Oh, certainly,”", "“오, 물론이지.”"], ["said the doctor.", "선생이 말했다."], ["“But, Mary, why are you dissecting Miss Dunstable’s character with so much ingenuity?”", "“그런데 메리, 어쩌자고 그렇게 재간을 부려 던스터블 양의 성품을 해부하고 있는 거냐?”"]], [["“Well, uncle, I will tell you why; because—”", "“그럼 삼촌, 왜 그러는지 말씀드리지요. 그건 —”"], ["and Mrs.", "그러면서 그레셤 부인은 말하는 중에 의자에서 일어나 식탁을 돌아 삼촌 옆으로 가서, 얼굴이 그의 얼굴에 닿을 만큼 가까이 대고 팔을 그의 목에 둘렀다. 그러고는 그의 눈에 보이지 않는 등 뒤에 선 채로 말을 이었다."], ["Gresham, while she was speaking, got up from her chair, and going round the table to her uncle’s side, put her arm round his neck till her face was close to his, and then continued speaking as she stood behind him out of his sight—“because—I think that Miss Dunstable is—is very fond of you; and that it would make her happy if you would—ask her to be your wife.”", "“그건 — 던스터블 양이 삼촌을 아주 많이 좋아한다고 제가 생각하기 때문이에요. 그리고 삼촌이 그분에게 아내가 되어 달라고 청하시면 그분이 행복해할 거라고 생각하기 때문이에요.”"]], [["“Mary!”", "“메리!”"], ["said the doctor, turning round with an endeavour to look his niece in the face.", "선생이 조카딸의 얼굴을 들여다보려 애쓰며 돌아앉아 말했다."]], [["“I am quite in earnest, uncle—quite in earnest.", "“저는 아주 진심이에요, 삼촌 — 아주 진심이에요."], ["From little things that she has said, and little things that I have seen, I do believe what I now tell you.”", "그분이 흘린 자잘한 말들과 제가 본 자잘한 일들로 보아, 지금 삼촌께 말씀드리는 것을 저는 정말 믿어요.”"]], [["“And you want me to—”", "“그래서 너는 내가 —”"]], [["“Dear uncle; my own one darling uncle, I want you only to do that which will make you—make you happy.", "“다정한 삼촌, 하나뿐인 제 사랑하는 삼촌, 저는 삼촌이 삼촌을 — 삼촌을 행복하게 할 일만 해 주시기를 바랄 뿐이에요."], ["What is Miss Dunstable to me compared to you?”", "삼촌에 비하면 던스터블 양이 저에게 무엇이겠어요?”"], ["And then she stooped down and kissed him.", "그러고는 몸을 굽혀 그에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["The doctor was apparently too much astounded by the intimation given him to make any further immediate reply.", "선생은 저에게 건네진 그 말에 너무 크게 놀라, 그 자리에서 더 무슨 대답도 하지 못하는 듯했다."], ["His niece, seeing this, left him that she might go and dress; and when they met again in the drawing-room Frank Gresham was with them.", "조카딸은 그것을 보고, 옷을 갈아입으러 가려고 그를 혼자 두었다. 그리고 두 사람이 응접실에서 다시 만났을 때에는 프랭크 그레셤이 함께 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIX. MISS DUNSTABLE AT HOME.", "# 제29장. 던스터블 양의 초대"]], [["Miss Dunstable did not look like a love-lorn maiden, as she stood in a small ante-chamber at the top of her drawing-room stairs receiving her guests.", "던스터블 양은 응접실 계단 위의 작은 대기실에 서서 손님을 맞고 있었으나, 사랑에 애태우는 처녀처럼 보이지는 않았다."], ["Her house was one of those abnormal mansions, which are to be seen here and there in London, built in compliance rather with the rules of rural architecture, than with those which usually govern the erection of city streets and town terraces.", "그녀의 집은 런던에서 여기저기 눈에 띄는 별난 대저택 가운데 하나로, 도시의 가로와 연립 주택을 세울 때 흔히 따르는 규칙보다는 시골 건축의 규칙에 맞추어 지은 집이었다."], ["It stood back from its brethren, and alone, so that its owner could walk round it.", "형제 되는 집들에서 뒤로 물러나 홀로 서 있었으므로 주인이 집을 한 바퀴 돌아 걸을 수도 있었다."], ["It was approached by a short carriageway; the chief door was in the back of the building; and the front of the house looked on to one of the parks.", "짧은 마차 길로 들어가게 되어 있었고, 큰 문은 건물 뒤쪽에 있었으며, 집의 앞면은 공원 가운데 하나를 바라보고 있었다."], ["Miss Dunstable in procuring it had had her usual luck.", "던스터블 양은 이 집을 손에 넣는 데에도 여느 때와 같은 운을 누렸다."], ["It had been built by an eccentric millionnaire at an enormous cost; and the eccentric millionnaire, after living in it for twelve months, had declared that it did not possess a single comfort, and that it was deficient in most of those details which, in point of house accommodation, are necessary to the very existence of man.", "어느 별난 백만장자가 엄청난 돈을 들여 지은 집이었는데, 그 별난 백만장자는 열두 달을 살아 본 뒤에 이 집에는 편안한 구석이 하나도 없으며, 주거의 관점에서 사람이 살아가는 데 꼭 있어야 할 세목이 대부분 빠져 있다고 선언했던 것이다."], ["Consequently the mansion was sold, and Miss Dunstable was the purchaser.", "그리하여 저택은 팔리게 되었고, 던스터블 양이 그것을 샀다."], ["Cranbourn House it had been named, and its present owner had made no change in this respect; but the world at large very generally called it Ointment Hall, and Miss Dunstable herself as frequently used that name for it as any other.", "크랜본 저택이라는 이름이 붙어 있었고 지금의 주인도 그 점은 고치지 않았으나, 세상은 대체로 그 집을 연고 저택이라 불렀고 던스터블 양 자신도 다른 어떤 이름만큼이나 그 이름을 자주 썼다."], ["It was impossible to quiz Miss Dunstable with any success, because she always joined in the joke herself.", "던스터블 양을 놀려서 재미를 보기는 도무지 불가능했다. 그녀 자신이 언제나 그 농담에 한몫 끼어들었기 때문이다."]], [["Not a word further had passed between Mrs. Gresham and Dr.", "그레셤 부인과 손 선생 사이에는 지난번 대화의 화제를 두고 그 뒤로 한마디도 더 오가지 않았다."], ["Thorne on the subject of their last conversation; but the doctor as he entered the lady’s portals amongst a tribe of servants and in a glare of light, and saw the crowd before him and the crowd behind him, felt that it was quite impossible that he should ever be at home there.", "그러나 선생은 하인들의 무리 사이로 눈부신 불빛 속에서 그 부인의 문간에 들어서서, 앞에도 사람 떼요 뒤에도 사람 떼인 것을 보고는, 자기가 여기서 제집처럼 편안할 수는 도무지 없겠다고 느꼈다."], ["It might be all right that a Miss Dunstable should live in this way, but it could not be right that the wife of Dr. Thorne should so live.", "던스터블 양이라는 사람이 이렇게 사는 것은 다 괜찮을지 몰라도, 손 선생의 아내가 이렇게 사는 것은 괜찮을 수가 없었다."], ["But all this was a matter of the merest speculation, for he was well aware—as he said to himself a dozen times—that his niece had blundered strangely in her reading of Miss Dunstable’s character.", "그러나 이 모든 것은 부질없는 공상에 지나지 않았다. 스스로에게 열두 번도 더 말했듯이, 조카딸이 던스터블 양의 성품을 읽는 데 이상하게도 잘못을 저질렀다는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["When the Gresham party entered the ante-room into which the staircase opened, they found Miss Dunstable standing there surrounded by a few of her most intimate allies. Mrs.", "그레셤 일행이 계단이 열려 드는 대기실에 들어섰을 때, 던스터블 양은 가장 가까운 벗 몇 사람에게 둘러싸여 거기 서 있었다."], ["Harold Smith was sitting quite close to her; Dr. Easyman was reclining on a sofa against the wall, and the lady who habitually lived with Miss Dunstable was by his side.", "해럴드 스미스 부인은 그녀의 바로 옆에 앉아 있었고, 이지먼 선생은 벽에 붙여 놓은 소파에 기대 누워 있었으며, 던스터블 양과 늘 함께 지내는 부인이 그 곁에 있었다."], ["One or two others were there also, so that a little running conversation was kept up, in order to relieve Miss Dunstable of the tedium which might otherwise be engendered by the work she had in hand.", "한두 사람이 더 있어서 이야기가 끊이지 않고 오갔는데, 그러지 않으면 지금 맡은 일 때문에 던스터블 양이 느낄 만한 무료함을 덜어 주려는 것이었다."], ["As Mrs. Gresham, leaning on her husband’s arm, entered the room, she saw the back of Mrs.", "그레셤 부인이 남편의 팔에 기대어 방에 들어섰을 때, 그녀는 프라우디 부인의 등을 보았다."], ["Proudie, as that lady made her way through the opposite door, leaning on the arm of the bishop.", "그 부인은 주교의 팔에 기대어 맞은편 문으로 빠져나가는 중이었다."]], [["Mrs. Harold Smith had apparently recovered from the annoyance which she must no doubt have felt when Miss Dunstable so utterly rejected her suit on behalf of her brother.", "해럴드 스미스 부인은 오라비를 대신해 넣은 청을 던스터블 양이 그렇게 딱 잘라 물리쳤을 때 느꼈을 것이 분명한 언짢음에서 이미 벗어난 듯했다."], ["If any feeling had existed, even for a day, calculated to put a stop to the intimacy between the two ladies, that feeling had altogether died away, for Mrs.", "두 부인 사이의 가까움을 끊어 놓을 만한 감정이 단 하루라도 있었다면, 그 감정은 이제 씨도 없이 사그라졌다."], ["Harold Smith was conversing with her friend, quite in the old way.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 예전과 조금도 다름없이 벗과 이야기를 나누고 있었으니 말이다."], ["She made some remark on each of the guests as they passed by, and apparently did so in a manner satisfactory to the owner of the house, for Miss Dunstable answered with her kindest smiles, and in that genial, happy tone of voice which gave its peculiar character to her good humour:", "그녀는 지나가는 손님마다 한마디씩 평을 붙였고, 집주인의 마음에 드는 투로 그리한 것이 분명했다. 던스터블 양이 가장 다정한 웃음을 띠고, 그 사람 좋은 기색에 남다른 빛깔을 입혀 주는 그 살가우면서도 즐거운 목소리로 대답했기 때문이다."]], [["“She is quite convinced that you are a mere plagiarist in what you are doing,” said Mrs.", "“그 사람은 당신이 하는 일이 그저 남의 것을 베낀 것이라고 굳게 믿고 있어요.”"], ["Harold Smith, speaking of Mrs. Proudie.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 프라우디 부인을 두고 말했다."]], [["“And so I am.", "“그렇고말고요."], ["I don’t suppose there can be anything very original now-a-days about an evening party.”", "요즘 저녁 모임에 무슨 대단히 새로운 것이 있을 수 있겠어요.”"]], [["“But she thinks you are copying her.”", "“하지만 그 사람은 당신이 자기를 베낀다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“And why not?", "“그러면 어때요?"], ["I copy everybody that I see, more or less.", "나는 눈에 보이는 사람은 누구든 얼마간 베끼는걸요."], ["You did not at first begin to wear big petticoats out of your own head?", "당신도 처음에 제 머리에서 나온 생각으로 큰 페티코트를 입기 시작한 건 아니잖아요?"], ["If Mrs. Proudie has any such pride as that, pray don’t rob her of it.", "프라우디 부인에게 그만한 자랑거리라도 있다면 부디 그것을 빼앗지 말아요."], ["Here’s the doctor and the Greshams.", "저기 선생님과 그레셤 댁 사람들이 왔군요."], ["Mary, my darling, how are you?”", "메리, 이 사랑스러운 사람, 어떻게 지냈어요?”"], ["and in spite of all her grandeur of apparel, Miss Dunstable took hold of Mrs.", "그러고는 그 거창한 차림새에도 아랑곳없이 던스터블 양은 그레셤 부인을 붙잡아 입을 맞추었다."], ["Gresham and kissed her—to the disgust of the dozen-and-a-half of the distinguished fashionable world who were passing up the stairs behind.", "뒤에서 계단을 올라오던 열댓 명의 이름난 사교계 인사들이 볼썽사납다고 여길 만한 짓이었다."]], [["The doctor was somewhat repressed in his mode of address by the communication which had so lately been made to him.", "선생은 얼마 전에 자기에게 전해진 말 때문에 인사하는 태도가 어딘가 움츠러들어 있었다."], ["Miss Dunstable was now standing on the very top of the pinnacle of wealth, and seemed to him to be not only so much above his reach, but also so far removed from his track in life, that he could not in any way put himself on a level with her.", "던스터블 양은 이제 부의 첨탑 맨 위에 서 있었고, 그가 손을 뻗어 닿을 수 없을 만큼 높은 데 있는 것은 물론이려니와 그의 삶의 궤도에서도 너무 멀리 떨어져 있어서, 도무지 자기를 그녀와 같은 자리에 놓을 수가 없어 보였다."], ["He could neither aspire so high nor descend so low; and thinking of this he spoke to Miss Dunstable as though there were some great distance between them,—as though there had been no hours of intimate friendship down at Greshamsbury.", "그렇게 높이 바랄 수도 없고 그렇게 낮게 내려올 수도 없었다. 이런 생각을 하면서 그는 두 사람 사이에 큰 거리가 있는 것처럼—그레셤스베리에서 다정한 벗으로 함께 지낸 시간이 아예 없었던 것처럼—던스터블 양에게 말을 걸었다."], ["There had been such hours, during which Miss Dunstable and Dr.", "그런 시간이 있었다. 그동안 던스터블 양과 손 선생은 같은 세상에 속한 사람들처럼 지냈다."], ["Thorne had lived as though they belonged to the same world: and this at any rate may be said of Miss Dunstable, that she had no idea of forgetting them.", "그리고 던스터블 양에 대해서는 적어도 이 말은 할 수 있다. 그녀는 그 시간을 잊을 생각이 조금도 없었다는 것이다."]], [["Dr. Thorne merely gave her his hand, and then prepared to pass on.", "손 선생은 그저 손을 내밀었을 뿐, 곧 지나쳐 가려 했다."]], [["“Don’t go, doctor,”", "“가지 마세요, 선생님.”"], ["she said; “for heaven’s sake, don’t go yet.", "그녀가 말했다. “제발, 아직 가지 마세요."], ["I don’t know when I may catch you if you get in there.", "저 안으로 들어가 버리면 언제 붙잡을 수 있을지 모르겠어요."], ["I shan’t be able to follow you for the next two hours.", "앞으로 두 시간은 따라갈 수도 없을 테고요."], ["Lady Meredith, I am so much obliged to you for coming—your mother will be here, I hope.", "레이디 메러디스, 와 주셔서 정말 고마워요. 어머님도 오시겠지요?"], ["Oh, I am so glad!", "아, 정말 기쁘네요!"], ["From her you know that is quite a favour.", "그분으로서는 아시다시피 대단한 은혜예요."], ["You, Sir George, are half a sinner yourself, so I don’t think so much about it.”", "조지 경, 경께서는 그 자체로 반쯤 죄인이시니 그렇게까지 대단하게 여기지는 않겠어요.”"]], [["“Oh, quite so,”", "“아, 그렇소.”"], ["said Sir George; “perhaps rather the largest half.”", "조지 경이 말했다. “아마 큰 쪽 반이겠지.”"]], [["“The men divide the world into gods and giants,”", "“남자들은 세상을 신들과 거인들로 갈라 놓지요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“We women have our divisions also.", "“우리 여자들에게도 우리 나름의 편 가름이 있어요."], ["We are saints or sinners according to our party.", "우리는 제 편에 따라 성인이 되기도 하고 죄인이 되기도 하지요."], ["The worst of it is, that we rat almost as often as you do.”", "그중 제일 못한 것은, 우리도 여러분만큼이나 자주 변절한다는 거예요.”"], ["Whereupon Sir George laughed and passed on.", "그 말에 조지 경은 웃으며 지나갔다."]], [["“I know, doctor, you don’t like this kind of thing,”", "“선생님이 이런 걸 좋아하지 않으시는 줄은 알아요.” 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["she continued, “but there is no reason why you should indulge yourself altogether in your own way, more than another—is there, Frank?”", "“하지만 남들보다 유별나게 선생님 뜻대로만 지내실 까닭도 없지요. 그렇지 않아요, 프랭크?”"]], [["“I am not so sure but he does like it,”", "“좋아하지 않으신다고 딱 잘라 말하기도 어렵겠는데요.”"], ["said Mr. Gresham.", "그레셤 씨가 말했다."], ["“There are some of your reputed friends whom he owns that he is anxious to see.”", "“여기 오신다는 벗들 가운데 꼭 뵙고 싶다고 선생님께서 스스로 인정하신 분들이 있으니까요.”"]], [["“Are there?", "“그런가요?"], ["Then there is some hope of his ratting too.", "그러면 선생님도 변절할 희망이 좀 있군요."], ["But he’ll never make a good staunch sinner; will he, Mary?", "하지만 굳건한 죄인 노릇은 결코 못 하실 거예요. 그렇지요, 메리?"], ["You’re too old to learn new tricks; eh, doctor?”", "새 재주를 배우기에는 너무 늙으셨어요. 그렇지 않아요, 선생님?”"]], [["“I am afraid I am,”", "“그런 것 같군요.”"], ["said the doctor, with a faint laugh.", "선생이 힘없이 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“Does Dr. Thorne rank himself among the army of saints?”", "“손 선생님은 스스로를 성인의 군대에 넣으시나요?”"], ["asked Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Decidedly,”", "“그야 물론이지요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“But you must always remember that there are saints of different orders; are there not, Mary?", "“하지만 성인에도 여러 수도회가 있다는 걸 늘 잊지 말아야 해요. 그렇지요, 메리?"], ["and nobody supposes that the Franciscans and the Dominicans agree very well together.", "그리고 프란체스코회와 도미니코회가 서로 아주 잘 맞는다고 여기는 사람은 아무도 없지요."], ["Dr. Thorne does not belong to the school of St.", "손 선생님은 바체스터의 성 프라우디 학파에 속한 분이 아니에요."], ["Proudie, of Barchester; he would prefer the priestess whom I see coming round the corner of the staircase, with a very famous young novice at her elbow.”", "이름난 젊은 수련 수녀를 팔꿈치 옆에 세우고 저 계단 모퉁이를 돌아오시는 저 여사제 쪽을 더 좋아하실 분이지요.”"]], [["“From all that I can hear, you will have to reckon Miss Grantly among the sinners,” said Mrs.", "“내가 들은 바로 미루면 그랜틀리 양은 죄인 쪽으로 세어야 할 거예요.”"], ["Harold Smith—seeing that Lady Lufton with her young friend was approaching—“unless, indeed, you can make a saint of Lady Hartletop.”", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 레이디 러프턴이 젊은 벗을 데리고 다가오는 것을 보면서 말했다. “레이디 하틀탑을 성인으로 만들어 낼 수 있다면 또 모르지만요.”"]], [["And then Lady Lufton entered the room, and Miss Dunstable came forward to meet her with more quiet respect in her manner than she had as yet shown to many of her guests.", "그러고 나서 레이디 러프턴이 방에 들어섰고, 던스터블 양은 그날 여러 손님에게 보인 것보다 훨씬 조용한 공대를 태도에 담아 그녀를 맞으러 나아갔다."], ["“I am much obliged to you for coming, Lady Lufton,”", "“와 주셔서 정말 고마워요, 레이디 러프턴.”"], ["she said, “and the more so, for bringing Miss Grantly with you.”", "그녀가 말했다. “그랜틀리 양을 함께 데려오셨으니 더욱 고맙지요.”"]], [["Lady Lufton uttered some pretty little speech, during which Dr. Thorne came up and shook hands with her; as did also Frank Gresham and his wife.", "레이디 러프턴이 짧고 곱게 인사말을 하는 동안 손 선생이 다가와 그녀와 악수를 했고, 프랭크 그레셤과 그의 아내도 그리했다."], ["There was a county acquaintance between the Framley people and the Greshamsbury people, and therefore there was a little general conversation before Lady Lufton passed out of the small room into what Mrs.", "프램리 사람들과 그레셤스베리 사람들은 같은 주에 사는 사이로 서로 아는 처지였으므로, 레이디 러프턴이 그 작은 방을 나와 프라우디 부인이라면 훌륭한 방 한 벌이라 불렀을 곳으로 들어가기까지 잠시 이런저런 이야기가 오갔다."], ["Proudie would have called the noble suite of apartments.", "“아버지도 오실 거예요.”"], ["“Papa will be here,”", "그랜틀리 양이 말했다."], ["said Miss Grantly; “at least so I understand.", "“적어도 그렇게 알고 있어요."], ["I have not seen him yet myself.”", "저는 아직 뵙지 못했지만요.”"]], [["“Oh, yes, he has promised me,”", "“그럼요, 저와 약속하셨어요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable; “and the archdeacon, I know, will keep his word.", "던스터블 양이 말했다. “그리고 부주교님은 약속을 지키실 분이지요."], ["I should by no means have the proper ecclesiastical balance without him.”", "그분이 안 계시면 교회 쪽 균형이 도무지 맞지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“Papa always does keep his word,”", "“아버지는 언제나 약속을 지키세요.”"], ["said Miss Grantly, in a tone that was almost severe.", "그랜틀리 양이 거의 딱딱하다 할 만한 어조로 말했다."], ["She had not at all understood poor Miss Dunstable’s little joke, or at any rate she was too dignified to respond to it.", "그녀는 딱한 던스터블 양의 작은 농담을 전혀 알아듣지 못했거나, 적어도 그것에 응해 주기에는 지나치게 위엄이 있었다."]], [["“I understand that old Sir John is to accept the Chiltern Hundreds at once,”", "“늙은 존 경이 당장 칠턴 헌드레즈를 받기로 했다더군요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, in a half whisper to Frank Gresham.", "레이디 러프턴이 프랭크 그레셤에게 반쯤 속삭이는 소리로 말했다."], ["Lady Lufton had always taken a keen interest in the politics of East Barsetshire, and was now desirous of expressing her satisfaction that a Gresham should again sit for the county.", "레이디 러프턴은 늘 동바셋주의 정치에 예민한 관심을 두어 왔고, 이제 그레셤 집안 사람이 다시 이 주의 의원 자리에 앉게 된 것을 흡족해한다는 뜻을 나타내고 싶었다."], ["The Greshams had been old county members in Barsetshire, time out of mind.", "그레셤 집안은 아득한 옛날부터 바셋주의 오래된 주 대표 의원 집안이었다."]], [["“Oh, yes; I believe so,”", "“아, 예."], ["said Frank, blushing.", "그렇게 될 줄 압니다.”"], ["He was still young enough to feel almost ashamed of putting himself forward for such high honours.", "프랭크가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 그는 그만한 높은 명예에 자기를 내세우는 것이 거의 부끄럽게 느껴질 만큼 아직 젊었다."]], [["“There will be no contest, of course,”", "“물론 경합은 없겠지요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, confidentially.", "레이디 러프턴이 은근한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“There seldom is in East Barsetshire, I am happy to say.", "“다행히도 동바셋주에서는 경합이 나는 일이 드물어요."], ["But if there were, every tenant at Framley would vote on the right side; I can assure you of that.", "하지만 만일 있다 해도 프램리의 소작인은 하나같이 옳은 쪽에 표를 넣을 거예요. 그것만은 내가 보증할 수 있어요."], ["Lord Lufton was saying so to me only this morning.”", "러프턴 경이 바로 오늘 아침에 나에게 그렇게 말했지요.”"]], [["Frank Gresham made a pretty little speech in reply, such as young sucking politicians are expected to make; and this, with sundry other small courteous murmurings, detained the Lufton party for a minute or two in the ante-chamber.", "프랭크 그레셤은 젖내 나는 정치 초년생이라면 으레 할 만한 짧고 고운 답사를 했고, 이런저런 정중한 웅얼거림까지 더해져 러프턴 일행은 대기실에 일이 분쯤 붙들려 있게 되었다."], ["In the meantime the world was pressing on and passing through to the four or five large reception-rooms—the noble suite, which was already piercing poor Mrs.", "그러는 사이에도 세상은 밀려들어 네댓 개의 큰 응접실로 지나가고 있었다. 딱한 프라우디 부인의 가슴을 이미 시샘으로 속속까지 찌르고 있던 그 훌륭한 방 한 벌이었다."], ["Proudie’s heart with envy to the very core. “These are the sort of rooms,”", "“주교들이 쓰도록 나라가 마련해 주어야 할 방이 바로 이런 방이지.”"], ["she said to herself unconsciously, “which ought to be provided by the country for the use of its bishops.”", "그녀는 저도 모르게 혼자 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“But the people are not brought enough together,”", "“그런데 사람들을 충분히 한데 모아 놓지는 못했소.”"], ["she said to her lord.", "그녀가 자기 주인에게 말했다."]], [["“No, no; I don’t think they are,”", "“그렇군요, 그렇군요. 그런 것 같지 않아요.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["“And that is so essential for a conversazione,”", "“그리고 그것이야말로 콘베르사치오네에 없어서는 안 될 것이오.”"], ["continued Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["“Now in Gloucester Place—.”", "“우리 글로스터 플레이스에서는—.”"], ["But we will not record all her adverse criticisms, as Lady Lufton is waiting for us in the ante-room.", "그러나 그녀의 험한 평을 다 적지는 않겠다. 레이디 러프턴이 대기실에서 우리를 기다리고 있으니 말이다."]], [["And now another arrival of moment had taken place;—an arrival indeed of very great moment.", "그런데 이제 또 한 사람의 중대한 도착이 있었다. 참으로 대단히 중대한 도착이었다."], ["To tell the truth, Miss Dunstable’s heart had been set upon having two special persons; and though no stone had been left unturned,—no stone which could be turned with discretion,—she was still left in doubt as to both these two wondrous potentates.", "사실을 말하자면 던스터블 양은 특별한 두 사람을 맞아들이는 데에 마음을 걸어 두고 있었다. 그리고 뒤집어 볼 수 있는 돌은—분별을 지키면서 뒤집을 수 있는 돌은—하나도 남기지 않고 다 뒤집어 보았음에도, 이 놀라운 두 권세가에 대해서는 여전히 알 수가 없었다."], ["At the very moment of which we are now speaking, light and airy as she appeared to be—for it was her character to be light and airy—her mind was torn with doubts.", "지금 우리가 이야기하고 있는 바로 그 순간에도, 그녀가 겉으로는 그렇게 가볍고 활달해 보였지만—가볍고 활달한 것이 본래 그녀의 성품이었으므로—속마음은 의심에 찢기고 있었다. 바라던 두 사람이 와 준다면 그녀의 저녁은 온전히 성공일 터였다."], ["If the wished-for two would come, her evening would be thoroughly successful; but if not, all her trouble would have been thrown away, and the thing would have been a failure; and there were circumstances connected with the present assembly which made Miss Dunstable very anxious that she should not fail.", "그러나 오지 않는다면 애쓴 것이 다 헛되고 이 일은 실패가 될 터였다. 그리고 이번 모임에는 던스터블 양이 실패하지 않기를 몹시 애타게 바라게 만드는 사정이 얽혀 있었다."], ["That the two great ones of the earth were Tom Towers of the Jupiter, and the Duke of Omnium, need hardly be expressed in words.", "이 땅의 두 위인이 《주피터》의 톰 타워스와 옴니엄 공작이었다는 것은 말로 밝힐 필요조차 없겠다."]], [["And now, at this very moment, as Lady Lufton was making her civil speeches to young Gresham, apparently in no hurry to move on, and while Miss Dunstable was endeavouring to whisper something into the doctor’s ear, which would make him feel himself at home in this new world, a sound was heard which made that lady know that half her wish had at any rate been granted to her.", "그리고 이제 바로 이 순간, 레이디 러프턴이 젊은 그레셤에게 정중한 인사말을 늘어놓으며 좀처럼 자리를 옮길 낌새를 보이지 않고, 던스터블 양은 이 새로운 세상에서 선생이 제집처럼 편안히 여기게 해 줄 말을 그의 귀에 속삭여 넣으려 애쓰고 있을 때, 한 소리가 들려왔다. 그 소리로 이 부인은 자기 소원의 절반은 어쨌든 이루어졌음을 알았다."], ["A sound was heard—but only by her own and one other attentive pair of ears. Mrs.", "소리가 들려왔다—그러나 그것을 들은 것은 그녀 자신의 귀와 또 하나의 귀 밝은 귀뿐이었다."], ["Harold Smith had also caught the name, and knew that the duke was approaching.", "해럴드 스미스 부인도 그 이름을 알아들었고, 공작이 다가오고 있음을 알았다."]], [["There was great glory and triumph in this; but why had his grace come at so unchancy a moment?", "여기에는 큰 영광과 승리가 있었다. 그러나 각하는 어찌하여 이토록 고약한 순간에 오셨을까?"], ["Miss Dunstable had been fully aware of the impropriety of bringing Lady Lufton and the Duke of Omnium into the same house at the same time; but when she had asked Lady Lufton, she had been led to believe that there was no hope of obtaining the duke; and then, when that hope had dawned upon her, she had comforted herself with the reflection that the two suns, though they might for some few minutes be in the same hemisphere, could hardly be expected to clash, or come across each other’s orbits.", "던스터블 양은 레이디 러프턴과 옴니엄 공작을 같은 집에 같은 시각에 불러들이는 것이 얼마나 온당하지 못한 일인지 잘 알고 있었다. 다만 레이디 러프턴을 청할 때에는 공작을 모실 희망이 없다고 믿게 되어 있었고, 그러다 그 희망이 밝아 왔을 때에는 이렇게 생각하며 스스로를 달랬던 것이다."], ["Her rooms were large and would be crowded; the duke would probably do little more than walk through them once, and Lady Lufton would certainly be surrounded by persons of her own class.", "두 해가 몇 분쯤 같은 반구에 떠 있을 수는 있어도 서로 부딪치거나 서로의 궤도를 가로지르리라고는 보기 어렵다고. 그녀의 방들은 넓고 사람으로 붐빌 터였다."], ["Thus Miss Dunstable had comforted herself.", "공작은 한 번 훑고 지나가는 정도 이상은 하지 않을 것이고, 레이디 러프턴은 틀림없이 자기와 같은 부류의 사람들에게 둘러싸여 있을 것이었다."], ["But now all things were going wrong, and Lady Lufton would find herself in close contiguity to the nearest representative of Satanic agency, which, according to her ideas, was allowed to walk this nether English world of ours.", "이렇게 던스터블 양은 스스로를 달래 두었다. 그런데 이제 모든 것이 어그러져, 레이디 러프턴은 그녀의 생각대로라면 이 아래 영국 세상을 걸어 다니도록 허락받은 사탄의 대리자 가운데 가장 가까운 자와 옷깃이 닿을 만한 거리에 놓이게 된 것이다."], ["Would she scream?", "그녀가 비명을 지를까?"], ["or indignantly retreat out of the house?—or would she proudly raise her head, and with outstretched hand and audible voice, boldly defy the devil and all his works?", "아니면 분개하여 집 밖으로 물러나 버릴까? 그것도 아니면 당당히 고개를 들고 손을 뻗은 채 들리는 목소리로 악마와 그의 모든 짓을 대담하게 물리칠까?"], ["In thinking of these things as the duke approached Miss Dunstable almost lost her presence of mind.", "공작이 다가오는 동안 이런 것들을 생각하다가 던스터블 양은 마음의 중심을 거의 잃을 지경이었다."]], [["But Mrs. Harold Smith did not lose hers.", "그러나 해럴드 스미스 부인은 잃지 않았다."], ["“So here at last is the duke,”", "“드디어 공작께서 오셨군요.”"], ["she said, in a tone intended to catch the express attention of Lady Lufton.", "그녀는 레이디 러프턴의 귀를 똑바로 붙잡으려는 어조로 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Smith had calculated that there might still be time for her ladyship to pass on and avoid the interview.", "스미스 부인은 마님이 앞으로 나아가 이 대면을 피할 짬이 아직 있으리라고 셈했던 것이다."], ["But Lady Lufton, if she heard the words, did not completely understand them.", "그러나 레이디 러프턴은 그 말을 들었더라도 온전히 알아듣지는 못했다."], ["At any rate they did not convey to her mind at the moment the meaning they were intended to convey.", "어쨌든 그 순간 그 말은 담고자 한 뜻을 그녀의 머리에 전해 주지 못했다."], ["She paused to whisper a last little speech to Frank Gresham, and then looking round, found that the gentleman who was pressing against her dress was—the Duke of Omnium!", "그녀는 프랭크 그레셤에게 마지막으로 한마디 속삭이려 걸음을 멈추었고, 그러고 나서 둘러보다가 자기 옷자락에 밀려 닿아 있는 신사가 바로—옴니엄 공작임을 알았다!"]], [["On this great occasion, when the misfortune could no longer be avoided, Miss Dunstable was by no means beneath herself or her character.", "이 큰 판에서, 불운을 이제 더는 피할 수 없게 되었을 때, 던스터블 양은 결코 자기 자신이나 자기 성품에 못 미치게 굴지 않았다."], ["She deplored the calamity, but she now saw that it was only left to her to make the best of it.", "그녀는 이 재난을 한탄했으나, 이제 남은 길은 그것을 되도록 잘 넘기는 것뿐임을 알았다."], ["The duke had honoured her by coming to her house, and she was bound to welcome him, though in doing so she should bring Lady Lufton to her last gasp.", "공작은 그녀의 집에 와 주어 그녀를 높여 주었으니 그를 맞아들이는 것이 마땅했다. 그렇게 하는 것이 레이디 러프턴을 마지막 숨까지 몰아붙이는 일이 될지라도 그랬다."]], [["“Duke,”", "“공작.”"], ["she said, “I am greatly honoured by this kindness on the part of your grace.", "그녀가 말했다. “각하께서 이렇게 은혜를 베풀어 주시니 크게 영광입니다."], ["I hardly expected that you would be so good to me.”", "이토록 저에게 잘해 주실 줄은 미처 바라지도 못했어요.”"]], [["“The goodness is all on the other side,”", "“은혜는 온통 그쪽에서 온 것이오.”"], ["said the duke, bowing over her hand.", "공작이 그녀의 손 위로 몸을 굽히며 말했다."]], [["And then in the usual course of things this would have been all.", "그리고 일이 여느 때의 순서대로 흘렀다면 그것으로 끝이었을 것이다."], ["The duke would have walked on and shown himself, would have said a word or two to Lady Hartletop, to the bishop, to Mr. Gresham, and such like, and would then have left the rooms by another way, and quietly escaped.", "공작은 걸음을 옮겨 얼굴을 보이고, 레이디 하틀탑에게, 주교에게, 그레셤 씨에게, 그런 이들에게 한두 마디를 건넨 다음, 다른 길로 방들을 나서서 조용히 빠져나갔을 것이다."], ["This was the duty expected from him, and this he would have done, and the value of the party would have been increased thirty per cent.", "그에게 기대되는 의무가 그것이었고 그도 그렇게 했을 터이며, 그렇게 함으로써 이 모임의 값은 삼십 퍼센트가 올랐을 것이다."], ["by such doing; but now, as it was, the news-mongers of the West End were likely to get much more out of him.", "그러나 일이 이렇게 되고 보니, 웨스트 엔드의 소문 장수들은 그에게서 훨씬 더 많은 것을 얻어 낼 판이었다."]], [["Circumstances had so turned out that he had absolutely been pressed close against Lady Lufton, and she, when she heard the voice, and was made positively acquainted with the fact of the great man’s presence by Miss Dunstable’s words, turned round quickly, but still with much feminine dignity, removing her dress from the contact.", "일이 그렇게 돌아가 그는 영락없이 레이디 러프턴에게 몸이 밀려 붙어 있었고, 그녀는 그 목소리를 듣고 던스터블 양의 말로 이 위인이 와 있다는 사실을 똑똑히 알게 되자, 재빨리, 그러면서도 여자다운 위엄을 잔뜩 지킨 채 몸을 돌려 옷자락을 그 닿음에서 거두어들였다."], ["In doing this she was brought absolutely face to face with the duke, so that each could not but look full at the other.", "그렇게 하는 통에 그녀는 영락없이 공작과 얼굴을 맞대게 되었고, 두 사람은 서로를 정면으로 바라보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["“I beg your pardon,”", "“실례했소.”"], ["said the duke.", "공작이 말했다."], ["They were the only words that had ever passed between them, nor have they spoken to each other since; but simple as they were, accompanied by the little by-play of the speakers, they gave rise to a considerable amount of ferment in the fashionable world.", "두 사람 사이에 오간 말은 그것뿐이었고 그 뒤로도 서로 말을 나눈 적이 없다. 그러나 그토록 단순한 말이었음에도, 말하는 이들의 자잘한 곁연기가 곁들여지자 그 말은 사교계에 상당한 발효를 일으켰다."], ["Lady Lufton, as she retreated back on to Dr.", "레이디 러프턴은 이지먼 선생 쪽으로 물러나면서 몸을 깊이 굽혀 인사했다."], ["Easyman, curtseyed low; she curtseyed low and slowly, and with a haughty arrangement of her drapery that was all her own; but the curtsey, though it was eloquent, did not say half so much,—did not reprobate the habitual iniquities of the duke with a voice nearly as potent as that which was expressed in the gradual fall of her eye and the gradual pressure of her lips.", "깊이, 그리고 천천히, 오직 그녀만의 것인 도도한 옷자락 놀림과 함께 굽혀 인사했다. 그러나 그 인사가 아무리 말이 많았다 해도, 그녀의 눈이 서서히 내려가고 입술이 서서히 다물리는 데 담긴 목소리에 견주면 절반도 말하지 못했고, 공작의 몸에 붙은 죄악을 그만큼 세게 꾸짖지도 못했다."], ["When she commenced her curtsey she was looking full in her foe’s face.", "인사를 시작할 때 그녀는 적의 얼굴을 정면으로 바라보고 있었다."], ["By the time that she had completed it her eyes were turned upon the ground, but there was an ineffable amount of scorn expressed in the lines of her mouth.", "인사를 끝냈을 때 그녀의 눈은 땅을 향해 있었으나, 입가의 주름에는 말로 담을 수 없을 만큼 많은 멸시가 담겨 있었다. 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않고 물러났다."], ["She spoke no word, and retreated, as modest virtue and feminine weakness must ever retreat, before barefaced vice and virile power; but nevertheless she was held by all the world to have had the best of the encounter.", "얌전한 미덕과 여자의 약함이 낯 두꺼운 악덕과 사내의 힘 앞에서 언제나 그리해야 하는 대로 물러났다. 그럼에도 세상은 하나같이 그녀가 이 맞부딪침에서 이겼다고 여겼다."], ["The duke, as he begged her pardon, wore in his countenance that expression of modified sorrow which is common to any gentleman who is supposed by himself to have incommoded a lady.", "공작은 용서를 청하면서, 부인에게 불편을 끼쳤다고 스스로 여기는 신사라면 누구나 짓는 그 다소 누그러진 미안함의 표정을 얼굴에 띠고 있었다."], ["But over and above this,—or rather under it,—there was a slight smile of derision, as though it were impossible for him to look upon the bearing of Lady Lufton without some amount of ridicule.", "그러나 그 위에—아니, 오히려 그 아래에—희미한 비웃음의 웃음기가 있었다. 레이디 러프턴의 몸가짐을 얼마간 우스워하지 않고서는 바라볼 수 없었던 것처럼."], ["All this was legible to eyes so keen as those of Miss Dunstable and Mrs. Harold Smith, and the duke was known to be a master of this silent inward sarcasm; but even by them,—by Miss Dunstable and Mrs.", "던스터블 양이나 해럴드 스미스 부인처럼 눈이 날카로운 사람들에게는 이 모든 것이 읽혔고, 공작은 이런 소리 없는 속 빈정거림의 명수로 알려져 있었다."], ["Harold Smith,—it was admitted that Lady Lufton had conquered.", "그러나 그들조차—던스터블 양과 해럴드 스미스 부인조차—레이디 러프턴이 이겼다고 인정했다."], ["When her ladyship again looked up, the duke had passed on; she then resumed the care of Miss Grantly’s hand, and followed in among the company.", "마님이 다시 눈을 들었을 때 공작은 이미 지나가 있었다. 그러자 그녀는 다시 그랜틀리 양의 손을 챙겨 들고 사람들 사이로 따라 들어갔다."]], [["“That is what I call unfortunate,”", "“저런 걸 두고 불운하다고 하는 거예요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, as soon as both belligerents had departed from the field of battle.", "교전한 두 사람이 다 싸움터를 떠나자 던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“The fates sometimes will be against one.”", "“운수라는 게 때로는 사람을 거스르지요.”"]], [["“But they have not been at all against you here,”", "“하지만 여기서는 조금도 당신을 거스르지 않았어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["“If you could arrive at her ladyship’s private thoughts to-morrow morning, you would find her to be quite happy in having met the duke.", "“내일 아침에 마님의 속마음에까지 다다를 수 있다면, 그분이 공작을 만난 일로 아주 즐거워하고 있는 걸 알게 될 거예요."], ["It will be years before she has done boasting of her triumph, and it will be talked of by the young ladies of Framley for the next three generations.”", "그분이 자기 승리를 자랑하기를 그치기까지는 여러 해가 걸릴 테고, 프램리의 젊은 아가씨들은 앞으로 세 세대 동안 그 이야기를 할 거예요.”"]], [["The Gresham party, including Dr. Thorne, had remained in the ante-chamber during the battle.", "손 선생을 포함한 그레셤 일행은 싸움이 벌어지는 동안 대기실에 남아 있었다."], ["The whole combat did not occupy above two minutes, and the three of them were hemmed off from escape by Lady Lufton’s retreat into Dr.", "접전은 통틀어 이 분이 채 걸리지 않았고, 레이디 러프턴이 이지먼 선생의 무릎 위로 물러나는 통에 세 사람은 빠져나갈 길이 막혀 있었다."], ["Easyman’s lap; but now they, too, essayed to pass on.", "그러나 이제 그들도 앞으로 지나가 보려 했다."]], [["“What, you will desert me,”", "“어머, 나를 버리고 가시겠다고요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“Very well; but I shall find you out by-and-by.", "“좋아요. 하지만 곧 찾아낼 테니까요."], ["Frank, there is to be some dancing in one of the rooms,—just to distinguish the affair from Mrs.", "프랭크, 방 하나에서는 춤을 추게 되어 있어요. 이 모임을 프라우디 부인의 콘베르사치오네와 구별하려는 것뿐이지요."], ["Proudie’s conversazione. It would be stupid, you know, if all conversaziones were alike; wouldn’t it?", "콘베르사치오네가 다 똑같으면 어리석은 일일 테니까요."], ["So I hope you will go and dance.”", "그렇지 않아요? 그러니 가서 춤을 추어 주면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["“There will, I presume, be another variation at feeding time,”", "“먹이 시간에도 또 다른 변주가 있을 테지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes; certainly; I am the most vulgar of all wretches in that respect.", "“아, 그럼요. 물론이지요."], ["I do love to set people eating and drinking.—Mr.", "그 점에서는 내가 세상에서 가장 상스러운 인간이에요."], ["Supplehouse, I am delighted to see you; but do tell me—”", "나는 사람들에게 먹이고 마시게 하는 게 참 좋아요.—서플하우스 씨, 뵙게 되어 반가워요."], ["and then she whispered with great energy into the ear of Mr.", "그런데 좀 말씀해 주세요—”"], ["Supplehouse, and Mr. Supplehouse again whispered into her ear.", "그러고서 그녀는 서플하우스 씨의 귀에 힘을 실어 속삭였고, 서플하우스 씨도 그녀의 귀에 속삭여 주었다."], ["“You think he will, then?”", "“그러면 그가 올 거라고 보시는군요?”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Supplehouse assented; he did think so; but he had no warrant for stating the circumstance as a fact.", "서플하우스 씨는 그렇다고 했다."], ["And then he passed on, hardly looking at Mrs.", "그는 그렇게 보았다. 그러나 그 일을 사실로 못 박아 말할 근거는 없었다."], ["Harold Smith as he passed.", "그러고 나서 그는 앞으로 지나갔는데, 지나면서 해럴드 스미스 부인은 거의 쳐다보지도 않았다."]], [["“What a hang-dog countenance he has,”", "“낯짝이 어쩌면 저렇게 음흉할까요.”"], ["said that lady.", "그 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["you’re prejudiced, my dear, and no wonder; as for myself I always liked Supplehouse.", "이봐요, 당신은 편견이 있어요. 그럴 만도 하지요."], ["He means mischief; but then mischief is his trade, and he does not conceal it.", "나로서는 늘 서플하우스가 마음에 들었어요. 그는 해코지를 하려고 들지요."], ["If I were a politician I should as soon think of being angry with Mr.", "하지만 해코지가 그의 장사이고, 그는 그걸 감추지 않아요."], ["Supplehouse for turning against me as I am now with a pin for pricking me.", "내가 정치가라면 서플하우스 씨가 나에게 등을 돌렸다고 화를 내는 일은, 지금 내가 나를 찌른 핀에게 화를 내는 것만큼이나 하지 않을 거예요."], ["It’s my own awkwardness, and I ought to have known how to use the pin more craftily.”", "그건 내 손이 서툰 탓이고, 핀을 좀 더 꾀 있게 쓰는 법을 알았어야 하는 거지요.”"]], [["“But you must detest a man who professes to stand by his party, and then does his best to ruin it.”", "“하지만 제 편에 서겠다고 내세우고서는 그 편을 망치려고 온 힘을 다하는 사람은 미워하지 않을 수 없잖아요.”"]], [["“So many have done that, my dear; and with much more success than Mr.", "“이봐요, 그런 짓을 한 사람이 어디 한둘인가요. 그것도 서플하우스 씨보다 훨씬 크게 성공하면서요!"], ["Supplehouse! All is fair in love and war,—why not add politics to the list?", "사랑과 전쟁에서는 무슨 짓을 해도 정당하다지요."], ["If we could only agree to do that, it would save us from such a deal of heartburning, and would make none of us a bit the worse.”", "거기에 정치를 더해 넣으면 왜 안 되나요? 그렇게 하기로 서로 뜻만 맞출 수 있다면 그 많은 속앓이에서 벗어날 수 있을 테고, 그러면서 누구도 조금도 더 나빠지지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["Miss Dunstable’s rooms, large as they were—“a noble suite of rooms certainly, though perhaps a little too—too—too scattered, we will say, eh, bishop?”—were now nearly full, and would have been inconveniently crowded, were it not that many who came only remained for half-an-hour or so.", "던스터블 양의 방들은 넓기는 넓었으나—“훌륭한 방 한 벌이기는 하지요. 다만 조금 너무—너무—너무 흩어져 있달까요."], ["Space, however, had been kept for the dancers—much to Mrs.", "그렇게 말해 두겠소. 그렇지 않소, 주교?”—이제는 거의 꽉 차 있어서, 온 사람 가운데 많은 이가 삼십 분쯤만 머물다 가지 않았다면 불편할 만큼 붐볐을 것이었다."], ["Proudie’s consternation.", "그래도 춤추는 이들을 위한 자리는 남겨 두었는데, 프라우디 부인에게는 크게 낭패였다."], ["Not that she disapproved of dancing in London, as a rule; but she was indignant that the laws of a conversazione, as re-established by herself in the fashionable world, should be so violently infringed.", "그녀가 런던에서 춤추는 것을 통틀어 못마땅해했다는 말은 아니다. 다만 그녀가 몸소 사교계에 다시 세워 놓은 콘베르사치오네의 법이 이토록 난폭하게 짓밟히는 것에 분개했던 것이다."]], [["“Conversaziones will come to mean nothing,”", "“콘베르사치오네는 아무 뜻도 없는 것이 되고 말 것이오.”"], ["she said to the bishop, putting great stress on the latter word, “nothing at all, if they are to be treated in this way.”", "그녀가 뒤쪽 말에 힘을 잔뜩 실어 주교에게 말했다. “이런 식으로 다루어진다면 조금의 뜻도 없는 것이 되고 말 것이오.”"]], [["“No, they won’t; nothing in the least,”", "“그렇지요, 없어질 거예요. 조금의 뜻도 없지요.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["“Dancing may be very well in its place,”", "“춤은 제자리에서라면 아주 괜찮을 수도 있소.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I have never objected to it myself; that is, for the laity,”", "“나 자신은 한 번도 그것을 반대한 적이 없어요. 그러니까, 평신도에게라면요.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["“But when people profess to assemble for higher objects,” said Mrs.", "“하지만 사람들이 더 높은 뜻을 위해 모인다고 내세운다면.”"], ["Proudie, “they ought to act up to their professions.”", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “그 내세운 바에 맞게 행동해야 하는 것이오.”"]], [["“Otherwise they are no better than hypocrites,”", "“그러지 않으면 위선자보다 나을 것이 없지요.”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["“A spade should be called a spade,”", "“삽은 삽이라고 불러야 하는 법이오.”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Decidedly,”", "“그야 물론이지요.”"], ["said the bishop, assenting.", "주교가 맞장구를 치며 말했다."]], [["“And when I undertook the trouble and expense of introducing conversaziones,” continued Mrs.", "“그리고 내가 수고와 비용을 무릅쓰고 콘베르사치오네를 들여왔을 때는.”"], ["Proudie, with an evident feeling that she had been ill-used, “I had no idea of seeing the word so—so—so misinterpreted;”", "프라우디 부인이 자기가 부당한 대우를 받았다는 느낌을 뚜렷이 드러내며 말을 이었다. “그 말이 이토록—이토록—이토록 잘못 새겨지는 것을 보게 될 줄은 생각도 못 했소.”"], ["and then observing certain desirable acquaintances at the other side of the room, she went across, leaving the bishop to fend for himself.", "그러고서 방 저쪽에 사귀어 둘 만한 사람들이 있는 것을 알아보고는, 주교더러 혼자 알아서 버티라고 두고 건너가 버렸다."]], [["Lady Lufton, having achieved her success, passed on to the dancing, whither it was not probable that her enemy would follow her, and she had not been there very long before she was joined by her son.", "레이디 러프턴은 승리를 거둔 뒤 춤추는 방으로 옮겨 갔다. 적이 거기까지 따라올 것 같지는 않았다."], ["Her heart at the present moment was not quite satisfied at the state of affairs with reference to Griselda.", "그리고 거기 오래 있지 않아 아들이 그녀에게 합류했다. 지금 그녀의 마음은 그리젤다에 관한 일이 놓인 형편이 그리 흡족하지 않았다."], ["She had gone so far as to tell her young friend what were her own wishes; she had declared her desire that Griselda should become her daughter-in-law; but in answer to this Griselda herself had declared nothing.", "그녀는 자기 소원이 무엇인지를 젊은 벗에게 말해 주는 데까지 나아가 있었다. 그리젤다가 자기 며느리가 되기를 바란다고 밝혔던 것이다."], ["It was, to be sure, no more than natural that a young lady so well brought up as Miss Grantly should show no signs of a passion till she was warranted in showing them by the proceedings of the gentleman; but notwithstanding this—fully aware as she was of the propriety of such reticence—Lady Lufton did think that to her Griselda might have spoken some word evincing that the alliance would be satisfactory to her.", "그런데 이에 대해 그리젤다 자신은 아무것도 밝히지 않았다. 그랜틀리 양처럼 잘 자란 아가씨라면 신사 쪽의 처신으로 그럴 자격을 얻기까지는 마음의 열기를 내보이지 않는 것이 지극히 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["Griselda, however, had spoken no such word, nor had she uttered a syllable to show that she would accept Lord Lufton if he did offer.", "그러나 그런 삼가는 태도가 온당하다는 것을 충분히 알고 있으면서도, 레이디 러프턴은 자기에게만은 그리젤다가 이 혼사가 자기 마음에 든다는 것을 드러내는 말 한마디쯤은 해 줄 수도 있었으리라 생각했다."], ["Then again she had uttered no syllable to show that she would not accept him; but, nevertheless, although she knew that the world had been talking about her and Lord Dumbello, she stood up to dance with the future marquess on every possible occasion.", "그러나 그리젤다는 그런 말을 한마디도 하지 않았고, 러프턴 경이 청하면 받아들이겠다는 것을 보이는 소리도 한 음절 내지 않았다. 그러면서 또 받아들이지 않겠다는 것을 보이는 소리도 한 음절 내지 않았다."], ["All this did give annoyance to Lady Lufton, who began to bethink herself that if she could not quickly bring her little plan to a favourable issue, it might be well for her to wash her hands of it.", "그럼에도 세상이 자기와 덤벨로 경을 두고 말이 많다는 것을 알면서도 그녀는 기회가 날 때마다 그 미래의 후작과 춤을 추러 일어섰다. 이 모든 것이 레이디 러프턴을 언짢게 했고, 그녀는 자기의 작은 계획을 어서 좋은 결말로 이끌지 못한다면 아예 손을 떼는 것이 나을지도 모른다고 생각하기 시작했다."], ["She was still anxious for the match on her son’s account.", "그녀는 여전히 아들을 위해 이 혼사를 바라고 있었다."], ["Griselda would, she did not doubt, make a good wife; but Lady Lufton was not so sure as she once had been that she herself would be able to keep up so strong a feeling for her daughter-in-law as she had hitherto hoped to do.", "그리젤다가 좋은 아내가 될 것은 의심하지 않았다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 여태 바라 왔던 만큼 며느리에 대한 그토록 강한 정을 자기가 계속 지닐 수 있을지, 한때만큼 확신하지 못하게 되어 있었다."]], [["“Ludovic, have you been here long?”", "“루도빅, 여기 온 지 오래됐니?”"], ["she said, smiling as she always did smile when her eyes fell upon her son’s face.", "그녀는 아들의 얼굴에 눈이 닿을 때면 늘 짓던 그 웃음을 지으며 말했다."]], [["“This instant arrived; and I hurried on after you, as Miss Dunstable told me that you were here.", "“방금 왔어요. 던스터블 양이 어머니가 여기 계시다고 해서 서둘러 뒤따라왔지요."], ["What a crowd she has!", "사람이 어쩌면 이렇게 많을까요!"], ["Did you see Lord Brock?”", "브록 경을 보셨어요?”"]], [["“I did not observe him.”", "“눈에 띄지 않았다.”"]], [["“Or Lord De Terrier?", "“그럼 드 테리어 경은요?"], ["I saw them both in the centre room.”", "저는 가운데 방에서 두 분을 다 보았는데요.”"]], [["“Lord De Terrier did me the honour of shaking hands with me as I passed through.”", "“드 테리어 경은 내가 지나갈 때 악수를 해 주는 영광을 베풀었지.”"]], [["“I never saw such a mixture of people.", "“이렇게 뒤섞인 사람들은 본 적이 없어요."], ["There is Mrs. Proudie going out of her mind because you are all going to dance.”", "저기 프라우디 부인은 여기서들 춤을 추려 한다고 제정신이 아니고요.”"]], [["“The Miss Proudies dance,”", "“프라우디 자매도 춤을 추잖아요.”"], ["said Griselda Grantly.", "그리젤다 그랜틀리가 말했다."]], [["“But not at conversaziones.", "“하지만 콘베르사치오네에서는 아니지요."], ["You don’t see the difference.", "그 차이를 못 보시는군요."], ["And I saw Spermoil there, looking as pleased as Punch.", "그리고 저기 스퍼모일이 인형극의 펀치처럼 좋아서 어쩔 줄 모르고 있는 걸 보았어요."], ["He had quite a circle of his own round him, and was chattering away as though he were quite accustomed to the wickednesses of the world.”", "제 나름의 동아리를 제 주위에 꽤 두르고서, 세상의 악덕이란 것에 아주 익숙한 사람처럼 재잘거리고 있었지요.”"]], [["“There certainly are people here whom one would not have wished to meet, had one thought of it,”", "“여기에는 미리 생각했더라면 만나고 싶지 않았을 사람들이 분명히 있구나.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, mindful of her late engagement.", "방금 치른 접전을 마음에 둔 레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“But it must be all right, for I walked up the stairs with the archdeacon.", "“하지만 다 괜찮은 게 틀림없어요. 제가 부주교님과 함께 계단을 올라왔으니까요."], ["That is an absolute proof, is it not, Miss Grantly?”", "그건 확실한 증거지요, 그랜틀리 양?”"]], [["“I have no fears.", "“저는 두렵지 않아요."], ["When I am with your mother I know I must be safe.”", "어머님과 함께 있으면 안전할 수밖에 없다는 걸 아니까요.”"]], [["“I am not so sure of that,”", "“그건 그렇게 확실하지 않은데요.”"], ["said Lord Lufton, laughing.", "러프턴 경이 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Mother, you hardly know the worst of it yet.", "“어머니, 아직 제일 나쁜 것은 모르시죠."], ["Who is here, do you think?”", "여기 누가 와 있는지 아세요?”"]], [["“I know whom you mean; I have seen him,”", "“네가 누구를 말하는지 안다. 나는 이미 보았어.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, very quietly.", "레이디 러프턴이 아주 조용히 말했다."]], [["“We came across him just at the top of the stairs,”", "“계단 맨 위에서 딱 마주쳤어요.”"], ["said Griselda, with more animation in her face than ever Lord Lufton had seen there before.", "그리젤다가 러프턴 경이 이전에 그 얼굴에서 본 적이 없는 생기를 띠고 말했다."]], [["“What; the duke?”", "“뭐라고요, 공작 말인가요?”"]], [["“Yes, the duke,”", "“그래, 공작이다.”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“I certainly should not have come had I expected to be brought in contact with that man.", "“그 사람과 마주 닿게 될 줄 알았다면 나는 결코 오지 않았을 거야."], ["But it was an accident, and on such an occasion as this it could not be helped.”", "하지만 뜻하지 않은 일이었고, 이런 자리에서는 어쩔 수 없는 일이었다.”"]], [["Lord Lufton at once perceived, by the tone of his mother’s voice and by the shades of her countenance that she had absolutely endured some personal encounter with the duke, and also that she was by no means so indignant at the occurrence as might have been expected.", "러프턴 경은 어머니의 목소리 결과 얼굴빛의 미묘한 그늘로, 그녀가 공작과 몸소 마주치는 일을 정말로 겪어 냈으며, 또 그 일에 대해 기대할 만한 만큼 분개하고 있지도 않다는 것을 곧 알아차렸다."], ["There she was, still in Miss Dunstable’s house, and expressing no anger as to Miss Dunstable’s conduct.", "그녀는 여전히 던스터블 양의 집에 그대로 있었고, 던스터블 양의 처사에 대해 아무런 노여움도 드러내지 않았다."], ["Lord Lufton could hardly have been more surprised had he seen the duke handing his mother down to supper; he said, however, nothing further on the subject.", "공작이 어머니의 손을 잡고 만찬 자리로 데려가는 것을 보았더라도 러프턴 경이 이보다 더 놀랄 수는 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 이 일에 대해 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Are you going to dance, Ludovic?”", "“춤을 출 거니, 루도빅?”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“Well, I am not sure that I do not agree with Mrs. Proudie in thinking that dancing would contaminate a conversazione.", "“글쎄요, 춤이 콘베르사치오네를 더럽힌다는 프라우디 부인의 생각에 저도 찬성하지 않는다고는 못 하겠는데요."], ["What are your ideas, Miss Grantly?”", "그랜틀리 양은 어떻게 생각해요?”"]], [["Griselda was never very good at a joke, and imagined that Lord Lufton wanted to escape the trouble of dancing with her. This angered her.", "그리젤다는 농담에 밝은 적이 한 번도 없었으므로, 러프턴 경이 자기와 춤추는 수고를 면하려 한다고 여겼다."], ["For the only species of love-making, or flirtation, or sociability between herself as a young lady, and any other self as a young gentleman, which recommended itself to her taste, was to be found in the amusement of dancing.", "이것이 그녀를 화나게 했다. 젊은 아가씨인 자기와 젊은 신사인 다른 누군가 사이에서 벌어지는 사랑놀이나 희롱이나 사귐 가운데 그녀의 취향에 드는 것은 오직 춤이라는 즐거움 안에 있었기 때문이다."], ["She was altogether at variance with Mrs. Proudie on this matter, and gave Miss Dunstable great credit for her innovation.", "이 문제에서 그녀는 프라우디 부인과 완전히 어긋나 있었고, 던스터블 양의 새 시도를 크게 칭찬했다."], ["In society Griselda’s toes were more serviceable to her than her tongue, and she was to be won by a rapid twirl much more probably than by a soft word.", "사교의 자리에서 그리젤다에게는 혀보다 발끝이 더 요긴했고, 부드러운 말보다는 빠른 한 바퀴 돌기로 그녀를 얻을 가능성이 훨씬 컸다."], ["The offer of which she would approve would be conveyed by two all but breathless words during a spasmodic pause in a waltz; and then as she lifted up her arm to receive the accustomed support at her back, she might just find power enough to say, “You—must ask—papa.”", "그녀가 받아들일 만한 청혼이란 왈츠가 잠깐 끊기는 사이에 숨이 넘어갈 듯한 두 마디로 전해지는 것이었고, 그러면 그녀는 등에 늘 받던 받침을 받으려 팔을 들어 올리면서 “아버지에게—여쭤—보세요.”라고 말할 힘 정도는 겨우 낼 수 있을 터였다."], ["After that she would not care to have the affair mentioned till everything was properly settled.", "그 뒤로는 모든 일이 제대로 정리되기까지 그 이야기가 입에 오르는 것을 그녀는 바라지 않을 것이었다."]], [["“I have not thought about it,”", "“그건 생각해 보지 않았어요.”"], ["said Griselda, turning her face away from Lord Lufton.", "그리젤다가 러프턴 경에게서 얼굴을 돌리며 말했다."]], [["It must not, however, be supposed that Miss Grantly had not thought about Lord Lufton, or that she had not considered how great might be the advantage of having Lady Lufton on her side if she made up her mind that she did wish to become Lord Lufton’s wife.", "그러나 그랜틀리 양이 러프턴 경을 두고 아무 생각도 하지 않았다거나, 러프턴 경의 아내가 되고 싶다고 마음을 굳힌다면 레이디 러프턴을 제 편으로 두는 것이 얼마나 큰 이익일지 헤아려 보지 않았다고 여겨서는 안 된다."], ["She knew well that now was her time for a triumph, now in this very first season of her acknowledged beauty; and she knew also that young, good-looking bachelor lords do not grow on hedges like blackberries.", "그녀는 지금이 자기가 승리를 거둘 때임을, 미인으로 인정받은 이 첫 사교철이야말로 그때임을 잘 알고 있었다. 그리고 젊고 인물 좋은 총각 귀족이 산울타리의 검은딸기처럼 열리는 것이 아니라는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["Had Lord Lufton offered to her, she would have accepted him at once without any remorse as to the greater glories which might appertain to a future Marchioness of Hartletop.", "러프턴 경이 그녀에게 청혼했다면 그녀는 미래의 하틀탑 후작부인에게 딸릴 더 큰 영광을 두고 조금도 아까워하지 않고 그를 곧바로 받아들였을 것이다. 그 방면으로 그녀에게 지혜가 모자라지는 않았다."], ["In that direction she was not without sufficient wisdom.", "그러나 러프턴 경은 청혼하지 않았고, 그럴 뜻이 있다는 표시도 내보이지 않았다."], ["But then Lord Lufton had not offered to her, nor given any signs that he intended to do so; and to give Griselda Grantly her due, she was not a girl to make a first overture.", "그리고 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 공평하게 말하자면, 그녀는 먼저 말을 붙이는 처녀가 아니었다. 덤벨로 경도 청혼하지는 않았다."], ["Neither had Lord Dumbello offered; but he had given signs,—dumb signs, such as birds give to each other, quite as intelligible as verbal signs to a girl who preferred the use of her toes to that of her tongue.", "그러나 그는 표시를 내보였다. 새들이 서로 주고받는 것과 같은 말 없는 표시였는데, 혀보다 발끝 쓰기를 더 좋아하는 처녀에게는 말로 하는 표시만큼이나 뜻이 잘 통하는 것이었다."]], [["“I have not thought about it,”", "“그건 생각해 보지 않았어요.”"], ["said Griselda, very coldly, and at that moment a gentleman stood before her and asked her hand for the next dance.", "그리젤다가 아주 차갑게 말했다. 그리고 그 순간 한 신사가 그녀 앞에 서서 다음 춤에 손을 내어 달라고 청했다."], ["It was Lord Dumbello; and Griselda, making no reply except by a slight bow, got up and put her hand within her partner’s arm.", "덤벨로 경이었다. 그리젤다는 가볍게 머리를 숙이는 것 말고는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 일어나 짝의 팔에 손을 걸었다."]], [["“Shall I find you here, Lady Lufton, when we have done?”", "“다 끝나면 여기서 뵐 수 있을까요, 레이디 러프턴?”"], ["she said; and then started off among the dancers.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하고 춤추는 사람들 사이로 나아갔다."], ["When the work before one is dancing the proper thing for a gentleman to do is, at any rate, to ask a lady; this proper thing Lord Lufton had omitted, and now the prize was taken away from under his very nose.", "앞에 놓인 일이 춤이라면 신사가 해야 할 마땅한 일은 어쨌든 부인에게 청하는 것이다. 그 마땅한 일을 러프턴 경은 빼먹었고, 이제 상은 바로 그의 코앞에서 채여 가 버렸다."]], [["There was clearly an air of triumph about Lord Dumbello as he walked away with the beauty.", "덤벨로 경이 그 미인을 데리고 걸어갈 때 그의 몸에는 뚜렷이 승리의 기색이 감돌았다."], ["The world had been saying that Lord Lufton was to marry her, and the world had also been saying that Lord Dumbello admired her.", "세상은 러프턴 경이 그녀와 혼인할 것이라고 말해 왔고, 또 덤벨로 경이 그녀를 마음에 두고 있다고도 말해 왔다."], ["Now this had angered Lord Dumbello, and made him feel as though he walked about, a mark of scorn, as a disappointed suitor.", "이것이 덤벨로 경을 화나게 하여, 마치 자기가 뜻을 이루지 못한 구혼자로서 비웃음거리가 되어 돌아다니는 것 같은 느낌을 주었다."], ["Had it not been for Lord Lufton, perhaps he would not have cared so much for Griselda Grantly; but circumstances had so turned out that he did care for her, and felt it to be incumbent upon him as the heir to a marquisate to obtain what he wanted, let who would have a hankering after the same article.", "러프턴 경이 없었다면 그는 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 그렇게까지 마음에 두지 않았을지도 모른다. 그러나 일이 그렇게 돌아가 그는 그녀를 마음에 두게 되었고, 같은 물건을 탐내는 자가 누구든 간에 후작 자리를 이을 자로서 원하는 것을 손에 넣는 것이 자기가 마땅히 할 일이라고 여겼다."], ["It is in this way that pictures are so well sold at auctions; and Lord Dumbello regarded Miss Grantly as being now subject to the auctioneer’s hammer, and conceived that Lord Lufton was bidding against him.", "그림이 경매에서 그렇게 잘 팔리는 것도 이런 식이다. 덤벨로 경은 그랜틀리 양이 이제 경매인의 망치 아래 놓인 것으로 보았고, 러프턴 경이 자기와 값을 다투어 부르고 있다고 여겼다."], ["There was, therefore, an air of triumph about him as he put his arm round Griselda’s waist and whirled her up and down the room in obedience to the music.", "그래서 그가 그리젤다의 허리에 팔을 두르고 음악에 맞추어 방을 오가며 그녀를 빙빙 돌릴 때 그의 몸에는 승리의 기색이 감돌았다."]], [["Lady Lufton and her son were left together looking at each other.", "레이디 러프턴과 아들은 서로를 바라보며 남게 되었다."], ["Of course he had intended to ask Griselda to dance, but it cannot be said that he very much regretted his disappointment.", "물론 그는 그리젤다에게 춤을 청할 생각이었으나, 그렇게 되지 못한 것을 그가 몹시 서운해했다고는 할 수 없었다."], ["Of course also Lady Lufton had expected that her son and Griselda would stand up together, and she was a little inclined to be angry with her protégée.", "물론 레이디 러프턴도 아들과 그리젤다가 함께 일어설 것이라고 기대했었고, 자기가 거두어 주는 아이에게 조금 화가 나는 쪽으로 기울었다."]], [["“I think she might have waited a minute,”", "“잠깐쯤 기다려 줄 수도 있었을 텐데.”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“But why, mother?", "“왜요, 어머니?"], ["There are certain things for which no one ever waits: to give a friend, for instance, the first passage through a gate out hunting, and such like.", "아무도 기다려 주지 않는 일이 있잖아요. 이를테면 사냥터에서 문을 먼저 지나가라고 벗에게 양보하는 것 같은 일 말이에요."], ["Miss Grantly was quite right to take the first that offered.”", "그랜틀리 양은 먼저 청한 사람을 잡은 것이 아주 옳았어요.”"]], [["Lady Lufton had determined to learn what was to be the end of this scheme of hers.", "레이디 러프턴은 이 계획의 끝이 어떻게 될지를 알아 두기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["She could not have Griselda always with her, and if anything were to be arranged it must be arranged now, while both of them were in London.", "그리젤다를 언제까지나 데리고 있을 수는 없었고, 무엇이든 정해질 것이라면 두 사람이 다 런던에 있는 지금 정해져야 했다."], ["At the close of the season Griselda would return to Plumstead, and Lord Lufton would go—nobody as yet knew where.", "사교철이 끝나면 그리젤다는 플럼스테드로 돌아갈 것이고, 러프턴 경은 어디로 갈지 아직 아무도 몰랐다."], ["It would be useless to look forward to further opportunities.", "더 뒤의 기회를 바라보는 것은 소용없는 일이었다."], ["If they did not contrive to love each other now, they would never do so.", "지금 서로 사랑하게 되지 못한다면 앞으로도 결코 그리되지 못할 것이었다."], ["Lady Lufton was beginning to fear that her plan would not work, but she made up her mind that she would learn the truth then and there,—at least as far as her son was concerned.", "레이디 러프턴은 자기 계획이 되지 않을까 봐 두려워지기 시작했으나, 적어도 아들에 관해서만은 바로 그 자리에서 진실을 알아내겠다고 마음을 정했다."]], [["“Oh, yes; quite so;—if it is equal to her with which she dances,”", "“아, 그래. 그렇지."], ["said Lady Lufton.", "누구와 춤을 추든 그 아이에게 다 같다면 말이다.” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“Quite equal, I should think—unless it be that Dumbello is longer-winded than I am.”", "“다 같겠지요. 덤벨로가 저보다 숨이 길다는 것 말고는요.”"]], [["“I am sorry to hear you speak of her in that way, Ludovic.”", "“그 아이를 그런 식으로 말하는 것을 들으니 서운하구나, 루도빅.”"]], [["“Why sorry, mother?”", "“왜 서운하세요, 어머니?”"]], [["“Because I had hoped—that you and she would have liked each other.”", "“나는 바랐기 때문이지—너와 그 아이가 서로 좋아하게 되기를.”"], ["This she said in a serious tone of voice, tender and sad, looking up into his face with a plaintive gaze, as though she knew that she were asking of him some great favour.", "그녀는 다정하고도 슬픈, 진지한 목소리로 이렇게 말하면서, 자기가 그에게 큰 청을 넣고 있음을 아는 사람처럼 애원하는 눈길로 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."]], [["“Yes, mother, I have known that you have wished that.”", "“예, 어머니. 어머니가 그것을 바라신다는 건 알고 있었어요.”"]], [["“You have known it, Ludovic!”", "“알고 있었다고, 루도빅!”"]], [["“Oh, dear, yes; you are not at all sharp at keeping your secrets from me.", "“아이고, 그럼요. 어머니는 저에게 비밀을 감추는 데 조금도 날래지 못하시거든요."], ["And, mother, at one time, for a day or so, I thought that I could oblige you.", "그리고 어머니, 한때는, 하루쯤은, 어머니 뜻을 들어 드릴 수 있겠다고 생각했어요."], ["You have been so good to me, that I would almost do anything for you.”", "어머니가 저에게 그토록 잘해 주셨으니, 저는 어머니를 위해서라면 거의 무엇이든 하겠어요.”"]], [["“Oh, no, no, no,”", "“아니, 아니, 아니야.”"], ["she said, deprecating his praise, and the sacrifice which he seemed to offer of his own hopes and aspirations.", "그녀는 아들의 칭찬을, 그리고 그가 자기의 희망과 바람을 바쳐 내놓는 듯한 그 희생을 물리치며 말했다."], ["“I would not for worlds have you do so for my sake.", "“세상을 다 준다 해도 나 때문에 네가 그러기를 바라지는 않는다."], ["No mother ever had a better son, and my only ambition is for your happiness.”", "나보다 더 좋은 아들을 둔 어머니는 없었어. 내 유일한 욕심은 네 행복이야.”"]], [["“But, mother, she would not make me happy.", "“하지만 어머니, 그 사람은 저를 행복하게 해 주지 못할 거예요."], ["I was mad enough for a moment to think that she could do so—for a moment I did think so.", "한순간 그럴 수 있으리라고 생각할 만큼 제가 미쳐 있었지요. 한순간은 정말 그렇게 생각했어요."], ["There was one occasion on which I would have asked her to take me, but—”", "저를 받아 달라고 청하려 했던 때가 한 번 있었어요. 하지만—”"]], [["“But what, Ludovic?”", "“하지만 뭐냐, 루도빅?”"]], [["“Never mind; it passed away; and now I shall never ask her.", "“아무것도 아니에요. 지나가 버렸어요."], ["Indeed I do not think she would have me.", "이제는 결코 청하지 않을 겁니다."], ["She is ambitious, and flying at higher game than I am.", "사실 그 사람도 저를 받아 주지 않을 것 같아요."], ["And I must say this for her, that she knows well what she is doing, and plays her cards as though she had been born with them in her hand.”", "그 사람은 뜻이 크고, 저보다 높은 사냥감을 노리고 있어요. 그리고 그 사람을 위해 이 말은 해 두어야겠는데, 그 사람은 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 잘 알고 있고, 손에 패를 쥐고 태어난 사람처럼 패를 놀립니다.”"]], [["“You will never ask her?”", "“결코 청하지 않겠다는 말이니?”"]], [["“No, mother; had I done so, it would have been for love of you—only for love of you.”", "“예, 어머니. 청했다 해도 그것은 어머니를 사랑해서였을 거예요. 오직 어머니를 사랑해서요.”"]], [["“I would not for worlds that you should do that.”", "“세상을 다 준다 해도 네가 그러는 것은 바라지 않는다.”"]], [["“Let her have Dumbello; she will make an excellent wife for him, just the wife that he will want.", "“그 사람은 덤벨로에게 가게 두세요. 그 사람은 덤벨로에게 아주 훌륭한 아내가 될 거예요."], ["And you, you will have been so good to her in assisting her to such a matter.”", "바로 덤벨로가 바랄 그런 아내지요. 그리고 어머니는 그 사람이 그런 혼사를 이루도록 도와주신 셈이 되어 아주 잘해 주신 게 되고요.”"]], [["“But, Ludovic, I am so anxious to see you settled.”", "“하지만 루도빅, 나는 네가 자리를 잡는 걸 보고 싶어 못 견디겠구나.”"]], [["“All in good time, mother!”", "“다 때가 되면 되지요, 어머니!”"]], [["“Ah, but the good time is passing away.", "“아, 하지만 그 좋은 때가 지나가고 있어."], ["Years run so very quickly.", "세월이 어찌나 빨리 달아나는지."], ["I hope you think about marrying, Ludovic.”", "혼인할 생각은 하고 있겠지, 루도빅.”"]], [["“But, mother, what if I brought you a wife that you did not approve?”", "“하지만 어머니, 제가 어머니 마음에 들지 않는 아내를 데려온다면요?”"]], [["“I will approve of any one that you love; that is—”", "“네가 사랑하는 사람이라면 누구든 마음에 들어 하겠다. 그러니까—”"]], [["“That is, if you love her also; eh, mother?”", "“그러니까, 어머니도 그 사람을 사랑한다면 말이지요? 그렇죠, 어머니?”"]], [["“But I rely with such confidence on your taste.", "“하지만 나는 네 취향을 그토록 믿고 있단다."], ["I know that you can like no one that is not lady-like and good.”", "너는 숙녀답고 착하지 않은 사람은 좋아할 수 없다는 걸 안다.”"]], [["“Lady-like and good!", "“숙녀답고 착하다!"], ["Will that suffice?”", "그것으로 넉넉할까요?”"], ["said he, thinking of Lucy Robarts.", "그는 루시 로바츠를 생각하며 말했다."]], [["“Yes; it will suffice, if you love her.", "“그래, 네가 그 사람을 사랑한다면 넉넉하지."], ["I don’t want you to care for money.", "나는 네가 돈을 마음에 두는 것을 바라지 않는다."], ["Griselda will have a fortune that would have been convenient; but I do not wish you to care for that.”", "그리젤다에게는 요긴했을 재산이 있겠지만, 나는 네가 그것을 마음에 두기를 바라지 않아.”"], ["And thus, as they stood together in Miss Dunstable’s crowded room, the mother and son settled between themselves that the Lufton-Grantly alliance treaty was not to be ratified.", "이리하여 던스터블 양의 붐비는 방에 함께 서 있는 동안, 어머니와 아들은 러프턴–그랜틀리 동맹 조약을 비준하지 않기로 둘 사이에 정해 버렸다."], ["“I suppose I must let Mrs. Grantly know,”", "“그랜틀리 부인에게 알려야겠구나.”"], ["said Lady Lufton to herself, as Griselda returned to her side.", "그리젤다가 자기 옆으로 돌아올 때 레이디 러프턴은 혼자 이렇게 말했다."], ["There had not been above a dozen words spoken between Lord Dumbello and his partner, but that young lady also had now fully made up her mind that the treaty above mentioned should never be brought into operation.", "덤벨로 경과 그 짝 사이에 오간 말은 열두 마디가 채 되지 않았으나, 그 젊은 아가씨 쪽에서도 이제 위에 말한 그 조약이 결코 발효되지 않게 하겠다고 마음을 완전히 굳혀 두고 있었다."]], [["We must go back to our hostess, whom we should not have left for so long a time, seeing that this chapter is written to show how well she could conduct herself in great emergencies.", "우리는 이 집 주인에게로 돌아가야 한다. 이 장은 그녀가 큰 위급한 상황에서 얼마나 잘 처신할 수 있는지를 보이려고 쓴 것이니, 그렇게 오래 그녀를 두고 떠나 있어서는 안 되었다."], ["She had declared that after awhile she would be able to leave her position near the entrance door, and find out her own peculiar friends among the crowd; but the opportunity for doing so did not come till very late in the evening.", "그녀는 얼마쯤 지나면 현관문 가까운 자리를 떠나 사람들 가운데서 자기만의 벗들을 찾아낼 수 있을 것이라고 밝혀 두었었다."], ["There was a continuation of arrivals; she was wearied to death with making little speeches, and had more than once declared that she must depute Mrs.", "그러나 그럴 기회는 밤이 아주 깊어서야 왔다. 손님은 끊이지 않고 이어졌다."], ["Harold Smith to take her place.", "그녀는 짧은 인사말을 짓느라 죽을 만큼 지쳤고, 자기 자리를 해럴드 스미스 부인에게 대신 맡겨야겠다고 한 번 이상 말했다."]], [["That lady stuck to her through all her labours with admirable constancy, and made the work bearable.", "그 부인은 이 모든 수고 내내 감탄할 만한 한결같음으로 그녀 곁에 붙어 있어서 그 일을 견딜 만하게 만들어 주었다."], ["Without some such constancy on a friend’s part, it would have been unbearable.", "벗 쪽에서 그만한 한결같음이 없었다면 견딜 수 없는 일이었을 것이다."], ["And it must be acknowledged that this was much to the credit of Mrs. Harold Smith.", "그리고 이것이 해럴드 스미스 부인의 큰 공로였음은 인정해야 한다."], ["Her own hopes with reference to the great heiress had all been shattered, and her answer had been given to her in very plain language.", "이 큰 상속녀에 관한 그녀 자신의 희망은 모두 부서졌고, 그 대답은 아주 뚜렷한 말로 그녀에게 주어졌다."], ["But, nevertheless, she was true to her friendship, and was almost as willing to endure fatigue on the occasion as though she had a sister-in-law’s right in the house.", "그럼에도 그녀는 우정에 성실했고, 이 집에 시누이로서의 권리를 가진 사람이나 되는 것처럼 이날의 고단함을 견디려 들었다."]], [["At about one o’clock her brother came.", "한 시쯤에 그녀의 오라비가 왔다."], ["He had not yet seen Miss Dunstable since the offer had been made, and had now with difficulty been persuaded by his sister to show himself.", "청혼이 있은 뒤로 그는 아직 던스터블 양을 만나지 못한 터였고, 누이동생이 어렵사리 달래어 이제 얼굴을 내밀게 한 것이었다."]], [["“What can be the use?”", "“무슨 소용이 있겠나?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“The game is up with me now;”—meaning, poor, ruined ne’er-do-well, not only that that game with Miss Dunstable was up, but that the great game of his whole life was being brought to an uncomfortable termination.", "“나는 이제 판이 끝났어.” 이 가난하고 망해 버린 못난이가 뜻한 것은, 던스터블 양을 상대로 한 그 판이 끝났다는 것만이 아니라, 그의 온 생애라는 큰 판이 편치 못한 끝을 향해 몰려가고 있다는 것이었다."]], [["“Nonsense,” said his sister.", "“터무니없는 소리 하지 마세요.”"], ["“Do you mean to despair because a man like the Duke of Omnium wants his money?", "누이동생이 말했다. “옴니엄 공작 같은 사람이 자기 돈을 돌려 달라고 한다고 절망할 셈이에요?"], ["What has been good security for him will be good security for another;”", "그분에게 좋은 담보였던 것이라면 다른 사람에게도 좋은 담보가 될 거예요.”"], ["and then Mrs. Harold Smith made herself more agreeable than ever to Miss Dunstable.", "그러고 나서 해럴드 스미스 부인은 던스터블 양에게 여느 때보다 더 살갑게 굴었다."]], [["When Miss Dunstable was nearly worn out, but was still endeavouring to buoy herself up by a hope of the still-expected great arrival—for she knew that the hero would show himself only at a very late hour if it were to be her good fortune that he showed himself at all—Mr. Sowerby walked up the stairs.", "던스터블 양이 거의 지쳐 쓰러질 지경이었으나 아직 기다려지는 그 큰 도착에 대한 희망으로 스스로를 떠받치려 애쓰고 있을 때—그 주인공이 얼굴을 내밀어 주는 복이 자기에게 온다면 그것은 아주 늦은 시각에야 있을 일임을 그녀는 알고 있었다—사워비 씨가 계단을 올라왔다."], ["He had schooled himself to go through this ordeal with all the cool effrontery which was at his command; but it was clearly to be seen that all his effrontery did not stand him in sufficient stead, and that the interview would have been embarrassing had it not been for the genuine good-humour of the lady.", "그는 자기가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 태연한 뻔뻔함을 다 갖추고 이 시련을 지나가도록 스스로를 다잡아 두었다. 그러나 그 모든 뻔뻔함이 그에게 넉넉한 힘이 되어 주지 못했음이, 또 이 부인의 참으로 사람 좋은 기색이 없었다면 이 대면이 난처했을 것임이 뚜렷이 보였다."]], [["“Here is my brother,” said Mrs.", "“우리 오라버니가 왔어요.”"], ["Harold Smith, showing by the tremulousness of the whisper that she looked forward to the meeting with some amount of apprehension.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. 속삭이는 목소리가 떨리는 것으로 그녀가 이 만남을 얼마간 걱정스럽게 바라보고 있음이 드러났다."]], [["“How do you do, Mr. Sowerby?”", "“어떻게 지내세요, 사워비 씨?”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, walking almost into the doorway to welcome him.", "던스터블 양이 그를 맞으려고 거의 문간까지 걸어 나가며 말했다."], ["“Better late than never.”", "“늦어도 아예 안 오는 것보다는 낫지요.”"]], [["“I have only just got away from the House,”", "“이제 겨우 하원에서 빠져나왔습니다.”"], ["said he, as he gave her his hand.", "그가 그녀에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I know well that you are sans reproche among senators;—as Mr.", "“아, 잘 알아요. 의원들 가운데서 당신은 「상 르프로슈」시지요."], ["Harold Smith is sans peur;—eh, my dear?”", "해럴드 스미스 씨가 「상 푀르」이신 것처럼요. 그렇지 않아요, 이봐요?”"]], [["“I must confess that you have contrived to be uncommonly severe upon them both,”", "“두 사람 모두에게 여간 매섭지 않게 해내셨다는 건 인정해야겠어요.”"], ["said Mrs.", "스미스 부인이 웃으며 말했다."], ["Harold, laughing; “and as regards poor Harold, most undeservedly so: Nathaniel is here, and may defend himself.”", "“그리고 딱한 해럴드에 관해서는 아주 억울한 일이지요. 나다니엘은 여기 있으니 스스로 변명할 수 있겠고요.”"]], [["“And no one is better able to do so on all occasions.", "“그리고 언제 어느 때든 스스로를 변명하는 데 이분보다 나은 사람은 없지요."], ["But, my dear Mr. Sowerby, I am dying of despair.", "그런데 사워비 씨, 저는 절망해서 죽을 지경이에요."], ["Do you think he’ll come?”", "그가 올 거라고 보세요?”"]], [["“He?", "“그요?"], ["who?”", "누구 말입니까?”"]], [["“You stupid man—as if there were more than one he!", "“이 답답한 분—‘그’가 둘이나 있는 것처럼 말씀하시네요!"], ["There were two, but the other has been.”", "둘이었지요. 하지만 다른 한 사람은 벌써 왔다 갔어요.”"]], [["“Upon my word, I don’t understand,”", "“맹세하지만 저는 모르겠군요.”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby, now again at his ease.", "사워비 씨가 이제 다시 마음이 편해져 말했다."], ["“But can I do anything?", "“하지만 제가 할 수 있는 일이 있습니까?"], ["shall I go and fetch any one?", "가서 누구를 데려올까요?"], ["Oh, Tom Towers!", "아, 톰 타워스!"], ["I fear I can’t help you.", "그 일은 제가 도울 수 없을 것 같습니다."], ["But here he is at the foot of the stairs!”", "하지만 저기 계단 아래에 그가 와 있네요!”"], ["And then Mr. Sowerby stood back with his sister to make way for the great representative man of the age.", "그러고서 사워비 씨는 누이동생과 함께 뒤로 물러서서 이 시대를 대표하는 위인에게 길을 내주었다."]], [["“Angels and ministers of grace, assist me!”", "“은총의 천사들과 사자들이여, 저를 도우소서!”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“How on earth am I to behave myself?", "“대체 어떻게 처신해야 하나요?"], ["Mr. Sowerby, do you think that I ought to kneel down?", "사워비 씨, 제가 무릎을 꿇어야 한다고 보세요?"], ["My dear, will he have a reporter at his back in the royal livery?” And then Miss Dunstable advanced two or three steps—not into the doorway, as she had done for Mr.", "이봐요, 그가 왕실 제복을 입은 기자를 등 뒤에 데리고 올까요?”"], ["Sowerby—put out her hand, and smiled her sweetest on Mr. Towers, of the Jupiter.", "그러고서 던스터블 양은 두세 걸음 나아갔는데—사워비 씨에게 그랬던 것처럼 문간까지 나가지는 않았다—손을 내밀고 《주피터》의 타워스 씨에게 가장 달콤한 웃음을 지어 보였다."]], [["“Mr. Towers,”", "“타워스 씨.”"], ["she said, “I am delighted to have this opportunity of seeing you in my own house.”", "그녀가 말했다. “제 집에서 뵐 기회를 얻게 되어 기쁩니다.”"]], [["“Miss Dunstable, I am immensely honoured by the privilege of being here,”", "“던스터블 양, 이 자리에 있게 된 특권으로 저는 대단한 영광을 입었습니다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“The honour done is all conferred on me,”", "“영광은 온통 제 쪽에 내려진 것이지요.”"], ["and she bowed and curtseyed with very stately grace.", "그러면서 그녀는 아주 위엄 있는 우아함으로 머리를 숙이고 몸을 굽혀 인사했다."], ["Each thoroughly understood the badinage of the other; and then, in a few moments, they were engaged in very easy conversation.", "둘은 서로의 농지거리를 속속들이 알아들었다. 그러고 나서 잠깐 사이에 두 사람은 아주 편안한 이야기에 들어갔다."]], [["“By-the-by, Sowerby, what do you think of this threatened dissolution?”", "“그런데 사워비, 이번에 겁주고 있는 해산 이야기는 어떻게 보나?”"], ["said Tom Towers.", "톰 타워스가 말했다."]], [["“We are all in the hands of Providence,”", "“우리 모두 섭리의 손안에 있지.”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby, striving to take the matter without any outward show of emotion.", "사워비 씨가 겉으로는 아무런 감정도 드러내지 않으려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["But the question was one of terrible import to him, and up to this time he had heard of no such threat. Nor had Mrs.", "그러나 이 문제는 그에게 무섭게 중대한 것이었고, 이때까지 그는 그런 위협에 대해 들은 바가 없었다."], ["Harold Smith, nor Miss Dunstable, nor had a hundred others who now either listened to the vaticinations of Mr. Towers, or to the immediate report made of them.", "해럴드 스미스 부인도, 던스터블 양도, 그리고 이제 타워스 씨의 예언을 직접 듣거나 곧바로 전해 듣게 된 백 명의 다른 사람들도 들은 바가 없었다."], ["But it is given to some men to originate such tidings, and the performance of the prophecy is often brought about by the authority of the prophet.", "그러나 어떤 사람들에게는 그런 소식을 처음 지어내는 일이 주어져 있고, 예언의 성취는 흔히 예언자의 권위가 이루어 내는 것이다."], ["On the following morning the rumour that there would be a dissolution was current in all high circles.", "이튿날 아침에는 의회가 해산될 것이라는 소문이 온 상류 사회에 돌았다."], ["“They have no conscience in such matters; no conscience whatever,”", "“그자들은 그런 일에 양심이 없어. 양심이라고는 도무지 없지.”"], ["said a small god, speaking of the giants,—a small god, whose constituency was expensive.", "어느 작은 신이 거인들을 두고 이렇게 말했다. 선거구에 돈이 많이 드는 작은 신이었다."]], [["Mr. Towers stood there chatting for about twenty minutes, and then took his departure without making his way into the room.", "타워스 씨는 거기 서서 이십 분쯤 이야기를 나누다가, 방 안으로는 들어가 보지도 않고 떠났다."], ["He had answered the purpose for which he had been invited, and left Miss Dunstable in a happy frame of mind.", "그는 자기가 초대된 목적에 맞게 응해 주었고, 던스터블 양을 즐거운 마음으로 남겨 두었다."]], [["“I am very glad that he came,”", "“그가 와 주어서 정말 기뻐요.”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith, with an air of triumph.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 승리한 기색으로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I am glad,”", "“그래요, 나도 기뻐요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, “though I am thoroughly ashamed that I should be so.", "던스터블 양이 말했다. “그러면서도 내가 그렇다는 것이 아주 부끄럽고요."], ["After all, what good has he done to me or to any one?”", "결국 그가 나에게, 아니 누구에게 무슨 좋은 일을 해 주었나요?”"], ["And having uttered this moral reflection, she made her way into the rooms, and soon discovered Dr. Thorne standing by himself against the wall.", "이런 도덕적인 생각을 입에 담고 나서 그녀는 방 안으로 들어갔고, 이내 손 선생이 벽에 붙어 혼자 서 있는 것을 찾아냈다."]], [["“Well, doctor,”", "“그런데 선생님.”"], ["she said, “where are Mary and Frank?", "그녀가 말했다. “메리와 프랭크는 어디 있어요?"], ["You do not look at all comfortable, standing here by yourself.”", "여기 혼자 서 계시니 도무지 편안해 보이지 않네요.”"]], [["“I am quite as comfortable as I expected, thank you,”", "“고맙지만 예상했던 만큼은 편안해요.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he. “They are in the room somewhere, and, as I believe, equally happy.”", "“두 사람은 방 어딘가에 있고, 내가 보기에는 나만큼 즐거워하고 있지요.”"]], [["“That’s spiteful in you, doctor, to speak in that way.", "“그런 식으로 말하시는 건 심술이에요, 선생님."], ["What would you say if you were called on to endure all that I have gone through this evening?”", "오늘 저녁에 내가 겪어 낸 것을 다 견뎌 내라는 소리를 들으셨다면 뭐라고 하셨을까요?”"]], [["“There is no accounting for tastes, but I presume you like it.”", "“취향이란 따질 수 없는 것이지요. 그래도 당신은 이런 걸 좋아하나 보군요.”"]], [["“I am not so sure of that.", "“그건 나도 그렇게 확실하지 않아요."], ["Give me your arm, and let me get some supper.", "팔을 좀 주세요. 저녁이라도 먹어야겠어요."], ["One always likes the idea of having done hard work, and one always likes to have been successful.”", "사람은 힘든 일을 해냈다는 생각을 늘 좋아하고, 성공했다는 것도 늘 좋아하지요.”"]], [["“We all know that virtue is its own reward,”", "“덕은 그 자체가 보상이라는 걸 우리 모두 알고 있지요.”"], ["said the doctor.", "선생이 말했다."]], [["“Well, that is something hard upon me,”", "“그건 나에게 좀 야박한 말씀이네요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, as she sat down to table.", "던스터블 양이 식탁에 앉으며 말했다."], ["“And you really think that no good of any sort can come from my giving such a party as this?”", "“그러면 내가 이런 모임을 여는 데서는 어떤 종류의 좋은 일도 나올 수 없다고 정말로 생각하시는 거예요?”"]], [["“Oh, yes; some people, no doubt, have been amused.”", "“아, 그렇지는 않아요. 몇몇 사람은 틀림없이 즐거웠겠지요.”"]], [["“It is all vanity in your estimation,”", "“선생님이 보시기에는 다 헛됨이지요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable; “vanity and vexation of spirit.", "던스터블 양이 말했다. “헛됨이요 마음의 괴로움이라."], ["Well; there is a good deal of the latter, certainly.", "그야 뒤쪽은 확실히 잔뜩 있지요."], ["Sherry, if you please.", "셰리를 좀 주세요."], ["I would give anything for a glass of beer, but that is out of the question.", "맥주 한 잔이라면 무엇이든 내놓겠지만 그건 말도 안 되는 일이고요."], ["Vanity and vexation of spirit!", "헛됨이요 마음의 괴로움이라!"], ["And yet I meant to do good.”", "그런데도 나는 좋은 일을 하려고 했던 거예요.”"]], [["“Pray, do not suppose that I am condemning you, Miss Dunstable.”", "“내가 당신을 탓하고 있다고는 부디 여기지 말아요, 던스터블 양.”"]], [["“Ah, but I do suppose it.", "“아, 하지만 나는 그렇게 여겨요."], ["Not only you, but another also, whose judgment I care for perhaps more than yours; and that, let me tell you, is saying a great deal.", "선생님만이 아니라, 아마 선생님의 판단보다 더 마음에 두는 또 한 사람도요. 그리고 그건, 말해 두지만, 대단한 말이랍니다."], ["You do condemn me, Dr.", "선생님은 나를 탓하고 있어요, 손 선생님."], ["Thorne, and I also condemn myself.", "그리고 나도 나를 탓해요."], ["It is not that I have done wrong, but the game is not worth the candle.”", "내가 잘못을 했다는 게 아니라, 이 놀음이 촛값만큼도 값이 나가지 않는다는 거지요.”"]], [["“Ah; that’s the question.”", "“아, 바로 그게 문제지요.”"]], [["“The game is not worth the candle.", "“이 놀음은 촛값만큼도 값이 나가지 않아요."], ["And yet it was a triumph to have both the duke and Tom Towers.", "그러면서도 공작과 톰 타워스를 다 모신 것은 승리였지요."], ["You must confess that I have not managed badly.”", "내가 그리 서투르게 해내지는 않았다고 인정하셔야 해요.”"]], [["Soon after that the Greshams went away, and in an hour’s time or so, Miss Dunstable was allowed to drag herself to her own bed.", "그 뒤 얼마 안 되어 그레셤 부부가 돌아갔고, 한 시간쯤 지나서야 던스터블 양은 제 침상까지 몸을 끌고 갈 수 있게 되었다."]], [["That is the great question to be asked on all such occasions, “Is the game worth the candle?”", "그런 모든 자리에서 물어야 할 큰 물음이 바로 이것이다. “이 놀음이 촛값만큼은 값이 나가는가?”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXX. THE GRANTLY TRIUMPH.", "# 제30장. 그랜틀리 집안의 승리"]], [["It has been mentioned cursorily—the reader, no doubt, will have forgotten it—that Mrs. Grantly was not specially invited by her husband to go up to town with a view of being present at Miss Dunstable’s party.", "지나가는 말로 언급한 적이 있는데—독자께서는 틀림없이 잊으셨을 터이나—그랜틀리 부인은 던스터블 양의 연회에 참석할 생각으로 런던에 올라오라는 남편의 특별한 초청을 받지 못했다."], ["Mrs. Grantly said nothing on the subject, but she was somewhat chagrined; not on account of the loss she sustained with reference to that celebrated assembly, but because she felt that her daughter’s affairs required the supervision of a mother’s eye.", "그랜틀리 부인은 그 일에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았으나 적잖이 속이 상했다."], ["She also doubted the final ratification of that Lufton-Grantly treaty, and, doubting it, she did not feel quite satisfied that her daughter should be left in Lady Lufton’s hands.", "그 이름난 모임에 가지 못해 입은 손실 때문이 아니라, 딸의 일이야말로 어머니의 눈길로 살펴 주어야 한다고 느꼈기 때문이었다."], ["She had said a word or two to the archdeacon before he went up, but only a word or two, for she hesitated to trust him in so delicate a matter.", "게다가 그녀는 저 러프턴-그랜틀리 조약이 끝내 인준될지를 의심하고 있었고, 의심하는 터라 딸을 레이디 러프턴의 손에 맡겨 두는 것이 그리 마음 편하지 않았다."], ["She was, therefore, not a little surprised at receiving, on the second morning after her husband’s departure, a letter from him desiring her immediate presence in London.", "남편이 올라가기 전에 한두 마디 해 두기는 했으나 정말 한두 마디뿐이었으니, 그처럼 미묘한 일을 남편에게 맡기기가 망설여졌던 것이다. 그러므로 남편이 떠난 지 이틀째 되는 아침에 당장 런던으로 오라는 그의 편지를 받고 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["She was surprised; but her heart was filled rather with hope than dismay, for she had full confidence in her daughter’s discretion.", "놀라기는 했으나 그녀의 마음은 낙담보다 오히려 희망으로 가득 찼으니, 딸의 분별을 온전히 믿고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["On the morning after the party, Lady Lufton and Griselda had breakfasted together as usual, but each felt that the manner of the other was altered.", "연회가 있은 다음 날 아침, 레이디 러프턴과 그리젤다는 여느 때처럼 함께 아침을 먹었으나 서로 상대의 태도가 달라졌음을 느꼈다."], ["Lady Lufton thought that her young friend was somewhat less attentive, and perhaps less meek in her demeanour, than usual; and Griselda felt that Lady Lufton was less affectionate.", "레이디 러프턴은 이 젊은 벗이 여느 때보다 조금 덜 살펴 주고, 어쩌면 몸가짐도 덜 순순하다고 생각했다. 그리젤다는 레이디 러프턴이 덜 다정하다고 느꼈다."], ["Very little, however, was said between them, and Lady Lufton expressed no surprise when Griselda begged to be left alone at home, instead of accompanying her ladyship when the carriage came to the door.", "그러나 둘 사이에 말은 거의 없었고, 마차가 문 앞에 왔을 때 그리젤다가 마님을 모시고 나가는 대신 집에 혼자 남아 있게 해 달라고 청했을 때에도 레이디 러프턴은 놀라움을 나타내지 않았다."]], [["Nobody called in Bruton Street that afternoon—no one, at least, was let in—except the archdeacon.", "그날 오후 브루턴 스트리트를 찾아온 사람은 부주교 말고는 아무도 없었다—적어도 들여보낸 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["He came there late in the day, and remained with his daughter till Lady Lufton returned.", "그는 저물 무렵에 와서 레이디 러프턴이 돌아올 때까지 딸과 함께 있었다."], ["Then he took his leave, with more abruptness than was usual with him, and without saying anything special to account for the duration of his visit.", "그러고는 여느 때의 그답지 않게 훨씬 불쑥 작별을 하고, 방문이 그처럼 길어진 까닭에 대해서는 이렇다 할 말을 하지 않았다."], ["Neither did Griselda say anything special; and so the evening wore away, each feeling in some unconscious manner that she was on less intimate terms with the other than had previously been the case.", "그리젤다도 이렇다 할 말을 하지 않았다. 그리하여 저녁은 흘러갔고, 두 사람은 저마다 저도 모르는 사이에 전보다 상대와 덜 친밀한 사이가 되었음을 느꼈다."]], [["On the next day also Griselda would not go out, but at four o’clock a servant brought a letter to her from Mount Street.", "이튿날도 그리젤다는 나가려 하지 않았는데, 네 시에 하인이 마운트 스트리트에서 온 편지를 그녀에게 가져왔다."], ["Her mother had arrived in London and wished to see her at once. Mrs.", "어머니가 런던에 도착했고 당장 딸을 보고 싶어 한다는 것이었다."], ["Grantly sent her love to Lady Lufton, and would call at half-past five, or at any later hour at which it might be convenient for Lady Lufton to see her.", "그랜틀리 부인은 레이디 러프턴에게 안부를 전하며, 다섯 시 반에 혹은 레이디 러프턴이 만나기에 편한 그 뒤의 어느 시각에라도 찾아뵙겠다고 했다."], ["Griselda was to stay and dine in Mount Street; so said the letter.", "그리젤다는 마운트 스트리트에 남아 저녁을 먹으라고, 편지는 그렇게 말했다."], ["Lady Lufton declared that she would be very happy to see Mrs.", "레이디 러프턴은 말한 시각에 그랜틀리 부인을 만나면 아주 기쁠 것이라고 했다."], ["Grantly at the hour named; and then, armed with this message, Griselda started for her mother’s lodgings.", "그리하여 그리젤다는 이 전갈을 받아 들고 어머니의 셋집으로 떠났다."]], [["“I’ll send the carriage for you,”", "“마차를 보내 드릴게요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“I suppose about ten will do.”", "“열 시쯤이면 되겠지요.”"]], [["“Thank you,”", "“고마워요,”"], ["said Griselda, “that will do very nicely;”", "그리젤다가 말했다. “그거면 아주 좋겠어요.”"], ["and then she went.", "그러고는 갔다."]], [["Exactly at half-past five Mrs. Grantly was shown into Lady Lufton’s drawing-room.", "정확히 다섯 시 반에 그랜틀리 부인은 레이디 러프턴의 응접실로 안내되어 들어왔다."], ["Her daughter did not come with her, and Lady Lufton could see by the expression of her friend’s face that business was to be discussed.", "딸은 함께 오지 않았고, 레이디 러프턴은 벗의 얼굴빛으로 볼일을 의논하러 왔다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["Indeed, it was necessary that she herself should discuss business, for Mrs. Grantly must now be told that the family treaty could not be ratified.", "사실 레이디 러프턴 자신도 볼일을 의논해야 할 처지였으니, 그랜틀리 부인에게 이제 집안 조약이 인준될 수 없다는 것을 알려야 했기 때문이다."], ["The gentleman declined the alliance, and poor Lady Lufton was uneasy in her mind at the nature of the task before her.", "당사자인 신사가 그 결합을 마다한 것이었다. 그리하여 가엾은 레이디 러프턴은 앞에 놓인 일의 성격 때문에 마음이 편치 않았다."]], [["“Your coming up has been rather unexpected,”", "“올라오신 것이 좀 뜻밖이네요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton, as soon as her friend was seated on the sofa.", "벗이 소파에 앉자마자 레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, indeed; I got a letter from the archdeacon only this morning, which made it absolutely necessary that I should come.”", "“예, 정말 그래요. 바로 오늘 아침에 부주교님한테서 편지를 받았는데, 그것 때문에 꼭 와야만 하게 되었답니다.”"]], [["“No bad news, I hope?”", "“나쁜 소식은 아니겠지요?”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“No; I can’t call it bad news.", "“예, 나쁜 소식이라고는 할 수 없어요."], ["But, dear Lady Lufton, things won’t always turn out exactly as one would have them.”", "하지만 레이디 러프턴, 세상일이 언제나 바라는 그대로 되지는 않는 법이지요.”"]], [["“No, indeed,”", "“정말 그렇지요,”"], ["said her ladyship, remembering that it was incumbent on her to explain to Mrs.", "마님이 말했다."], ["Grantly now at this present interview the tidings with which her mind was fraught. She would, however, let Mrs.", "지금 이 자리에서 제 마음을 가득 채우고 있는 소식을 그랜틀리 부인에게 설명해야 하는 것이 자신의 의무라는 것을 떠올리면서였다."], ["Grantly first tell her own story, feeling, perhaps, that the one might possibly bear upon the other.", "그러나 그랜틀리 부인이 먼저 제 이야기를 하도록 두기로 했으니, 어쩌면 한쪽이 다른 쪽과 걸려 있을지도 모른다고 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["“Poor dear Griselda!”", "“가엾은 우리 그리젤다!”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly, almost with a sigh.", "그랜틀리 부인이 거의 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“I need not tell you, Lady Lufton, what my hopes were regarding her.”", "“그 아이를 두고 제가 어떤 희망을 품고 있었는지는 레이디 러프턴께 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지요.”"]], [["“Has she told you anything—anything that—”", "“그 아이가 무슨 말이라도 했나요—무슨, 그런—”"]], [["“She would have spoken to you at once—and it was due to you that she should have done so—but she was timid; and not unnaturally so.", "“그 아이도 당장 말씀드렸을 거예요—그러는 것이 마땅히 댁에 대한 도리이기도 했지요—하지만 겁이 났던 거예요."], ["And then it was right that she should see her father and me before she quite made up her own mind.", "그럴 만도 했고요. 게다가 마음을 아주 굳히기 전에 아비와 저를 만나 보는 것이 옳았지요."], ["But I may say that it is settled now.”", "아무튼 이제 정해졌다고는 말씀드릴 수 있어요.”"]], [["“What is settled?”", "“무엇이 정해졌다는 거지요?”"], ["asked Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 물었다."]], [["“Of course it is impossible for any one to tell beforehand how these things will turn out,” continued Mrs.", "“이런 일이 어떻게 될지야 물론 누구도 미리 알 수 없는 것이지요,”"], ["Grantly, beating about the bush rather more than was necessary.", "그랜틀리 부인이 필요 이상으로 에두르며 변두리만 두드렸다."], ["“The dearest wish of my heart was to see her married to Lord Lufton.", "“제 마음의 가장 간절한 소원은 그 아이가 러프턴 경과 결혼하는 것을 보는 것이었어요."], ["I should so much have wished to have her in the same county with me, and such a match as that would have fully satisfied my ambition.”", "그 아이를 저와 같은 주 안에 두고 싶은 마음이 정말 컸고, 그런 혼사라면 제 야심을 온전히 채워 주었을 텐데요.”"]], [["“Well, I should rather think it might!”", "“아무렴, 그야 채워 주었겠지요!”"], ["Lady Lufton did not say this out loud, but she thought it. Mrs.", "레이디 러프턴은 이 말을 소리 내어 하지는 않았으나 마음속으로 그렇게 생각했다."], ["Grantly was absolutely speaking of a match between her daughter and Lord Lufton as though she would have displayed some amount of Christian moderation in putting up with it!", "그랜틀리 부인은 제 딸과 러프턴 경의 혼사를 두고, 마치 그것을 참고 받아들이는 데에 그리스도인다운 절제를 얼마간 보여 주기라도 했을 것처럼 말하고 있는 것이 아닌가!"], ["Griselda Grantly might be a very nice girl; but even she—so thought Lady Lufton at the moment—might possibly be priced too highly.", "그리젤다 그랜틀리가 아주 좋은 처녀일지는 모른다. 그러나 그 아이라 해도—레이디 러프턴은 그 순간 그렇게 생각했다—값이 너무 높게 붙었을 수 있었다."]], [["“Dear Mrs. Grantly,”", "“그랜틀리 부인,”"], ["she said, “I have foreseen for the last few days that our mutual hopes in this respect would not be gratified.", "그녀가 말했다. “저는 요 며칠 사이에 이 일에 대한 우리 서로의 희망이 이루어지지 않으리라는 것을 미리 알고 있었어요."], ["Lord Lufton, I think;—but perhaps it is not necessary to explain— Had you not come up to town I should have written to you,—probably to-day.", "러프턴 경 말인데요, 제 생각에는—하지만 설명할 필요는 없겠지요— 부인께서 런던에 올라오지 않으셨다면 제가 편지를 썼을 거예요. 아마 오늘쯤에요."], ["Whatever may be dear Griselda’s fate in life, I sincerely hope that she may be happy.”", "그 아이의 인생이 어떤 운명이 되든, 저는 그 아이가 행복하기를 진심으로 바라요.”"]], [["“I think she will,” said Mrs.", "“그렇게 될 거라고 생각해요,”"], ["Grantly, in a tone that expressed much satisfaction.", "그랜틀리 부인이 크게 만족한 기색이 드러나는 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Has—has anything—”", "“혹시—혹시 무슨 일이—”"]], [["“Lord Dumbello proposed to Griselda the other night, at Miss Dunstable’s party,” said Mrs.", "“덤벨로 경이 요전 밤 던스터블 양의 연회에서 그 아이에게 청혼했어요,”"], ["Grantly, with her eyes fixed upon the floor, and assuming on the sudden much meekness in her manner; “and his lordship was with the archdeacon yesterday, and again this morning.", "그랜틀리 부인이 눈을 바닥에 붙인 채, 갑자기 몸가짐에 자못 순순한 태를 지으며 말했다. “그리고 그분은 어제 부주교님과 함께 있었고, 오늘 아침에도 또 그랬지요."], ["I fancy he is in Mount Street at the present moment.”", "지금 이 순간에도 마운트 스트리트에 있을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Oh, indeed!” said Lady Lufton.", "“아, 그렇군요!”"], ["She would have given worlds to have possessed at the moment sufficient self-command to have enabled her to express in her tone and manner unqualified satisfaction at the tidings.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다. 그 순간 그 소식에 아무 조건 없는 만족을 어조와 태도로 나타낼 만한 자제력을 지녔더라면, 그녀는 세상을 다 주고라도 그것을 얻었을 것이다."], ["But she had not such self-command, and was painfully aware of her own deficiency.", "그러나 그런 자제력은 없었고, 자신에게 그것이 모자란다는 것을 그녀는 괴롭게 알고 있었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“예,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“And as it is all so far settled, and as I know you are so kindly anxious about dear Griselda, I thought it right to let you know at once.", "“일이 이만큼 정해졌고, 또 댁에서 그 아이를 그처럼 고맙게 염려해 주시는 것을 아는 터라, 곧바로 알려 드리는 것이 옳다고 생각했어요."], ["Nothing can be more upright, honourable, and generous, than Lord Dumbello’s conduct; and, on the whole, the match is one with which I and the archdeacon cannot but be contented.”", "덤벨로 경의 처신은 더할 수 없이 반듯하고 명예롭고 후하답니다. 그러니 통틀어 보면 이 혼사는 저와 부주교님이 만족하지 않을 수 없는 것이지요.”"]], [["“It is certainly a great match,”", "“확실히 대단한 혼사네요,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“Have you seen Lady Hartletop yet?”", "“레이디 하틀탑은 벌써 만나 보셨나요?”"]], [["Now Lady Hartletop could not be regarded as an agreeable connection, but this was the only word which escaped from Lady Lufton that could be considered in any way disparaging, and, on the whole, I think that she behaved well.", "그런데 레이디 하틀탑이란 사람은 반가운 인연이라고 볼 수 없었으니, 이것이야말로 레이디 러프턴의 입에서 새어 나온 말 가운데 어떤 식으로든 깎아내리는 것으로 볼 수 있는 유일한 말이었다. 그러니 통틀어 보면 나는 그녀가 잘 처신했다고 생각한다."]], [["“Lord Dumbello is so completely his own master that that has not been necessary,”", "“덤벨로 경은 워낙 온전히 제 몸의 주인이라 그럴 필요가 없었어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“The marquis has been told, and the archdeacon will see him either to-morrow or the day after.”", "“후작께는 말씀이 들어갔고, 부주교님이 내일이나 모레 후작을 뵐 거예요.”"]], [["There was nothing left for Lady Lufton but to congratulate her friend, and this she did in words perhaps not very sincere, but which, on the whole, were not badly chosen.", "레이디 러프턴에게 남은 일은 벗에게 축하를 하는 것뿐이었고, 그녀는 어쩌면 그다지 진심이 아닐 말로 그것을 했으나, 통틀어 보면 말을 고르기를 나쁘게 하지는 않았다."]], [["“I am sure I hope she will be very happy,”", "“그 아이가 아주 행복하기를 정말로 바라요,” 레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["said Lady Lufton, “and I trust that the alliance”—the word was very agreeable to Mrs. Grantly’s ear—“will give unalloyed gratification to you and to her father.", "“그리고 그 결합이”—이 낱말은 그랜틀리 부인의 귀에 아주 흐뭇하게 들렸다—“부인과 그 아이 아버지께 순수한 기쁨만을 드리기를 믿어요."], ["The position which she is called to fill is a very splendid one, but I do not think that it is above her merits.”", "그 아이가 채우도록 불려 간 자리는 참으로 찬란한 자리인데, 저는 그것이 그 아이의 값보다 높다고는 생각하지 않아요.”"]], [["This was very generous, and so Mrs. Grantly felt it.", "이것은 매우 너그러운 말이었고, 그랜틀리 부인도 그렇게 느꼈다."], ["She had expected that her news would be received with the coldest shade of civility, and she was quite prepared to do battle if there were occasion.", "그녀는 제 소식이 가장 차가운 예의로 받아들여질 줄로 예상하고 있었고, 기회가 온다면 한바탕 싸울 각오도 충분히 되어 있었다."], ["But she had no wish for war, and was almost grateful to Lady Lufton for her cordiality.", "그러나 전쟁을 바라지는 않았으므로, 레이디 러프턴의 따뜻함에 거의 고마움을 느꼈다."]], [["“Dear Lady Lufton,”", "“레이디 러프턴,”"], ["she said, “it is so kind of you to say so.", "그녀가 말했다. “그렇게 말씀해 주시니 참 고마워요."], ["I have told no one else, and of course would tell no one till you knew it.", "다른 누구에게도 말하지 않았고, 물론 댁이 아시기 전에는 아무에게도 말하지 않을 참이었어요."], ["No one has known her and understood her so well as you have done.", "그 아이를 댁만큼 잘 알고 잘 헤아려 준 사람은 아무도 없어요."], ["And I can assure you of this: that there is no one to whose friendship she looks forward in her new sphere of life with half so much pleasure as she does to yours.”", "그리고 이것만은 자신 있게 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 그 아이가 새로운 삶의 자리에서 기다리는 우정 가운데, 댁의 우정을 기다리는 만큼의 절반이라도 기쁘게 기다리는 것은 하나도 없다는 것을요.”"]], [["Lady Lufton did not say much further.", "레이디 러프턴은 그 이상 말을 많이 하지 않았다."], ["She could not declare that she expected much gratification from an intimacy with the future Marchioness of Hartletop.", "앞으로 하틀탑 후작부인이 될 사람과 친밀하게 지내는 데서 큰 기쁨을 기대한다고는 차마 말할 수 없었다."], ["The Hartletops and Luftons must, at any rate for her generation, live in a world apart, and she had now said all that her old friendship with Mrs. Grantly required.", "하틀탑 집안과 러프턴 집안은 적어도 그녀의 세대 동안은 서로 딴 세상에서 살아야 했고, 그녀는 그랜틀리 부인과의 오랜 벗 사이가 요구하는 말은 이제 다 한 것이었다."], ["Mrs. Grantly understood all this quite as well as did Lady Lufton; but then Mrs.", "그랜틀리 부인도 이 모든 것을 레이디 러프턴만큼이나 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Grantly was much the better woman of the world.", "그러나 그랜틀리 부인이 세상 물정에는 훨씬 나은 여인이었다."]], [["It was arranged that Griselda should come back to Bruton Street for that night, and that her visit should then be brought to a close.", "그리젤다는 그날 밤만 브루턴 스트리트로 돌아오고, 그로써 머무름을 끝내기로 정해졌다."]], [["“The archdeacon thinks that for the present I had better remain up in town,” said Mrs.", "“부주교님은 당장은 제가 런던에 남아 있는 편이 낫다고 생각해요,”"], ["Grantly, “and under the very peculiar circumstances Griselda will be—perhaps more comfortable with me.”", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “그리고 사정이 워낙 특별하니 그 아이도—어쩌면 저와 함께 있는 것이 더 편할 거예요.”"]], [["To this Lady Lufton entirely agreed; and so they parted, excellent friends, embracing each other in a most affectionate manner.", "이 말에 레이디 러프턴은 전적으로 동의했다. 그리하여 두 사람은 더없이 좋은 벗으로 헤어졌으니, 서로를 지극히 다정한 몸짓으로 껴안았다."]], [["That evening Griselda did return to Bruton Street, and Lady Lufton had to go through the further task of congratulating her.", "그날 저녁 그리젤다는 과연 브루턴 스트리트로 돌아왔고, 레이디 러프턴은 그 아이를 축하해야 하는 또 하나의 일을 치러야 했다."], ["This was the more disagreeable of the two, especially so as it had to be thought over beforehand.", "둘 가운데 이쪽이 더 언짢은 일이었으니, 미리 궁리해 두어야 했던 만큼 더욱 그러했다."], ["But the young lady’s excellent good sense and sterling qualities made the task comparatively an easy one.", "그러나 이 젊은 아가씨의 뛰어난 분별과 야무진 성품이 그 일을 비교적 쉬운 것으로 만들어 주었다."], ["She neither cried, nor was impassioned, nor went into hysterics, nor showed any emotion.", "그녀는 울지도, 격해지지도, 히스테리를 일으키지도, 어떤 감정을 드러내지도 않았다."], ["She did not even talk of her noble Dumbello—her generous Dumbello.", "고결한 자기 덤벨로니, 후한 자기 덤벨로니 하는 말조차 하지 않았다."], ["She took Lady Lufton’s kisses almost in silence, thanked her gently for her kindness, and made no allusion to her own future grandeur.", "레이디 러프턴의 입맞춤을 거의 잠자코 받고, 그 친절에 나직이 고맙다고 하고, 앞으로 제게 올 영화에 대해서는 한마디도 비추지 않았다."]], [["“I think I should like to go to bed early,”", "“저는 일찍 자리에 들었으면 좋겠어요,”"], ["she said, “as I must see to my packing up.”", "그녀가 말했다. “짐을 챙겨야 하니까요.”"]], [["“Richards will do all that for you, my dear.”", "“그건 리처즈가 다 해 줄 거예요, 얘야.”"]], [["“Oh, yes, thank you, nothing can be kinder than Richards.", "“아, 예, 고마워요. 리처즈만큼 친절한 사람은 없지요."], ["But I’ll just see to my own dresses.”", "하지만 제 옷은 제가 챙기겠어요.”"], ["And so she went to bed early.", "그리하여 그녀는 일찍 자리에 들었다."]], [["Lady Lufton did not see her son for the next two days, but when she did, of course she said a word or two about Griselda.", "레이디 러프턴은 그 뒤 이틀 동안 아들을 보지 못했으나, 보게 되었을 때에는 물론 그리젤다에 대해 한두 마디를 했다."]], [["“You have heard the news, Ludovic?”", "“소식은 들었니, 루도빅?”"], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes: it’s at all the clubs.", "“아, 예. 클럽마다 그 얘기예요."], ["I have been overwhelmed with presents of willow branches.”", "버들가지 선물이 쏟아져 저는 파묻힐 지경이었어요.”"]], [["“You, at any rate, have got nothing to regret,”", "“적어도 너는 아쉬워할 것이 없겠지,”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Nor you either, mother.", "“어머니도 마찬가지죠."], ["I am sure that you do not think you have.", "어머니도 아쉬워하실 것이 없다고 저는 믿어요."], ["Say that you do not regret it.", "아쉽지 않다고 말해 주세요."], ["Dearest mother, say so for my sake.", "어머니, 저를 위해서 그렇게 말해 주세요."], ["Do you not know in your heart of hearts that she was not suited to be happy as my wife,—or to make me happy?”", "그 사람이 제 아내로 행복하게 지낼 만한 사람도, 저를 행복하게 해 줄 만한 사람도 아니었다는 것을, 어머니도 마음 깊은 속으로는 알고 계시지 않나요?”"]], [["“Perhaps not,”", "“그럴지도 모르지,”"], ["said Lady Lufton, sighing.", "레이디 러프턴이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["And then she kissed her son, and declared to herself that no girl in England could be good enough for him.", "그러고는 아들에게 입을 맞추고, 잉글랜드의 어떤 처녀도 이 아이에게 걸맞을 만큼 훌륭하지는 못하다고 마음속으로 단정했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXI. SALMON FISHING IN NORWAY.", "# 제31장. 노르웨이의 연어낚시"]], [["Lord Dumbello’s engagement with Griselda Grantly was the talk of the town for the next ten days.", "덤벨로 경과 그리젤다 그랜틀리의 약혼은 그 뒤 열흘 동안 장안의 화제였다."], ["It formed, at least, one of two subjects which monopolized attention, the other being that dreadful rumour, first put in motion by Tom Towers at Miss Dunstable’s party, as to a threatened dissolution of Parliament.", "적어도 그것은 사람들의 이목을 독차지한 두 가지 이야기 가운데 하나였고, 다른 하나는 던스터블 양의 모임에서 톰 타워스가 처음 굴려 놓은 저 끔찍한 소문, 곧 의회가 해산될 위험이 있다는 소문이었다."]], [["“Perhaps, after all, it will be the best thing for us,”", "“어쩌면 결국은 그게 우리에게 가장 좋은 일이 될지도 모르지요,”"], ["said Mr. Green Walker, who felt himself to be tolerably safe at Crewe Junction.", "크루 정션에서라면 자기 자리가 웬만큼 안전하다고 여기고 있던 그린 워커 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I regard it as a most wicked attempt,”", "“나는 그것을 아주 못된 술책으로 보오,”"], ["said Harold Smith, who was not equally secure in his own borough, and to whom the expense of an election was disagreeable.", "자기 선거구에서는 그만큼 안전하지 못했고 선거에 드는 비용이 달갑지 않았던 해럴드 스미스가 말했다. “가을을 넘길 시간을 벌자고 하는 짓이오."], ["“It is done in order that they may get time to tide over the autumn.", "해산을 해 본다고 저들이 표 열 개를 더 얻지도 못할 것이고, 마흔 개가 못 되면 다수를 잡기도 어려울 것이오."], ["They won’t gain ten votes by a dissolution, and less than forty would hardly give them a majority.", "그런데도 저들에게는 공무를 맡은 자의 의무감이라는 것이 없소 — 조금도 없소."], ["But they have no sense of public duty—none whatever. Indeed, I don’t know who has.”", "하기야 그런 것을 가진 자가 대체 누가 있는지 나도 모르겠소만.”"]], [["“No, by Jove; that’s just it.", "“그렇지요, 맹세코. 바로 그것입니다."], ["That’s what my aunt Lady Hartletop says; there is no sense of duty left in the world.", "우리 아주머니 레이디 하틀탑도 그렇게 말씀하세요. 세상에 의무감이라는 것이 남아 있지 않다고요."], ["By-the-by, what an uncommon fool Dumbello is making himself!”", "그런데 말이지요, 덤벨로가 스스로를 얼마나 별난 바보로 만들고 있는지!”"], ["And then the conversation went off to that other topic.", "그러고 나서 이야기는 저 다른 화제로 넘어갔다."]], [["Lord Lufton’s joke against himself about the willow branches was all very well, and nobody dreamed that his heart was sore in that matter.", "러프턴 경이 버들가지를 두고 스스로를 놀린 농담은 아주 훌륭했고, 그 일로 그의 마음이 쓰리다고는 아무도 꿈에도 생각하지 않았다. 세상은 스스로 어리석은 혼사라 부르기로 한 그 일을 두고 덤벨로 경을 비웃고 있었고, 러프턴 경의 벗들은 그 자신도 같은 방향으로 얼간이 짓을 했을 수 있다고는 한 번도 의심해 본 적이 없다는 듯이 그에게 그 이야기를 했다."], ["The world was laughing at Lord Dumbello for what it chose to call a foolish match, and Lord Lufton’s friends talked to him about it as though they had never suspected that he could have made an ass of himself in the same direction; but, nevertheless, he was not altogether contented.", "그러나 그런데도 그는 아주 흐뭇하지는 않았다. 그는 그리젤다와 혼인하기를 결코 바라지 않았다."], ["He by no means wished to marry Griselda; he had declared to himself a dozen times since he had first suspected his mother’s manœuvres, that no consideration on earth should induce him to do so; he had pronounced her to be cold, insipid, and unattractive in spite of her beauty; and yet he felt almost angry that Lord Dumbello should have been successful.", "어머니의 계략을 처음 의심한 뒤로 그는 세상의 어떤 사정도 자기를 그렇게 하도록 이끌 수 없다고 열두 번도 더 스스로에게 선언했었다. 그녀가 그 아름다움에도 불구하고 차갑고 싱겁고 마음을 끄는 데가 없다고 단언했었다."], ["And this, too, was the more inexcusable, seeing that he had never forgotten Lucy Robarts, had never ceased to love her, and that, in holding those various conversations within his own bosom, he was as loud in Lucy’s favour as he was in dispraise of Griselda.", "그런데도 덤벨로 경이 뜻을 이루었다는 것에 그는 화가 날 지경이었다. 그리고 이것은 더욱 변명할 수 없는 일이었으니, 그가 루시 로바츠를 한 번도 잊은 적이 없고, 그녀를 사랑하기를 그친 적이 없으며, 자기 가슴 안에서 그 여러 가지 이야기를 주고받을 때에는 그리젤다를 흠잡는 만큼이나 큰 목소리로 루시를 편들었기 때문이다."]], [["“Your hero, then,”", "“그렇다면 당신의 주인공은,”"], ["I hear some well-balanced critic say, “is not worth very much.”", "균형이 잘 잡힌 어느 비평가가 이렇게 말하는 것이 내 귀에 들린다, “그리 값이 나가는 사람이 아니군요.”"]], [["In the first place Lord Lufton is not my hero; and in the next place, a man may be very imperfect and yet worth a great deal.", "우선 러프턴 경은 내 주인공이 아니다. 그리고 다음으로, 사람은 몹시 흠이 많으면서도 값이 크게 나갈 수 있다."], ["A man may be as imperfect as Lord Lufton, and yet worthy of a good mother and a good wife.", "사람은 러프턴 경만큼 흠이 많으면서도 좋은 어머니와 좋은 아내를 가질 자격이 있을 수 있다."], ["If not, how many of us are unworthy of the mothers and wives we have!", "그렇지 않다면 우리 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 지금 가진 어머니와 아내를 가질 자격이 없겠는가!"], ["It is my belief that few young men settle themselves down to the work of the world, to the begetting of children, and carving and paying and struggling and fretting for the same, without having first been in love with four or five possible mothers for them, and probably with two or three at the same time.", "내 믿음으로는, 세상의 일에, 자식을 낳는 일에, 그리고 그 자식들을 위하여 고기를 썰고 셈을 치르고 몸부림치고 안달하는 일에 자리를 잡고 앉는 젊은이들 가운데, 그 자식들의 어머니가 될 수 있었을 네댓 사람과 먼저 사랑에 빠져 보지 않은 이, 그것도 아마 그 가운데 둘이나 셋과 한꺼번에 사랑에 빠져 보지 않은 이는 거의 없다."], ["And yet these men are, as a rule, worthy of the excellent wives that ultimately fall to their lot.", "그런데도 이 사내들은 대체로 마침내 제 몫으로 떨어지는 훌륭한 아내를 가질 자격이 있다."], ["In this way Lord Lufton had, to a certain extent, been in love with Griselda.", "이런 식으로 러프턴 경도 어느 정도까지는 그리젤다와 사랑에 빠져 있었던 것이다."], ["There had been one moment in his life in which he would have offered her his hand, had not her discretion been so excellent; and though that moment never returned, still he suffered from some feeling akin to disappointment when he learned that Griselda had been won and was to be worn.", "그의 삶에는 그녀의 분별이 그토록 훌륭하지 않았더라면 그가 그녀에게 손을 내밀었을 순간이 한 번 있었다. 그리고 그 순간은 다시 돌아오지 않았지만, 그리젤다가 남의 것으로 얻어져 남에게 걸치일 처지라는 것을 알게 되었을 때 그는 실망과 비슷한 어떤 감정으로 괴로워했다."], ["He was, then, a dog in the manger, you will say.", "그러면 그는 구유 속의 개였구나, 독자는 이렇게 말할 것이다."], ["Well; and are we not all dogs in the manger, more or less actively?", "좋다. 그런데 우리 모두가 더하든 덜하든 부지런히 구유 속의 개가 아닌가?"], ["Is not that manger-doggishness one of the most common phases of the human heart?", "그 구유 속 개짓이야말로 사람 마음의 가장 흔한 모습 가운데 하나가 아닌가?"]], [["But not the less was Lord Lufton truly in love with Lucy Robarts.", "그러나 그렇다고 러프턴 경이 루시 로바츠를 참으로 사랑하고 있다는 사실이 조금도 덜해지지는 않았다."], ["Had he fancied that any Dumbello was carrying on a siege before that fortress, his vexation would have manifested itself in a very different manner.", "어떤 덤벨로가 그 성채 앞에서 공성을 벌이고 있다고 여겨졌더라면, 그의 속상함은 아주 다른 방식으로 드러났을 것이다."], ["He could joke about Griselda Grantly with a frank face and a happy tone of voice; but had he heard of any tidings of a similar import with reference to Lucy, he would have been past all joking, and I much doubt whether it would not even have affected his appetite.", "그는 거리낌 없는 얼굴과 흐뭇한 목소리로 그리젤다 그랜틀리에 대해 농담을 할 수 있었다. 그러나 루시에 관해 그와 같은 뜻의 소식을 들었더라면 그는 농담을 할 형편이 아니었을 것이고, 그것이 그의 밥맛에까지 영향을 미치지 않았을까 나는 크게 의심한다."]], [["“Mother,”", "“어머니,”"], ["he said to Lady Lufton a day or two after the declaration of Griselda’s engagement, “I am going to Norway to fish.”", "그리젤다의 약혼이 알려진 지 하루나 이틀 뒤에 그가 레이디 러프턴에게 말했다. “저는 노르웨이로 낚시를 가려고요.”"]], [["“To Norway,—to fish!”", "“노르웨이로 — 낚시를!”"]], [["“Yes.", "“예."], ["We’ve got rather a nice party.", "꽤 좋은 일행이 모였어요."], ["Clontarf is going, and Culpepper—”", "클론타프가 가고, 컬페퍼도 —”"]], [["“What, that horrid man!”", "“뭐, 그 끔찍한 사람이!”"]], [["“He’s an excellent hand at fishing;—and Haddington Peebles, and—and—there’ll be six of us altogether; and we start this day week.”", "“그 사람은 낚시에는 솜씨가 아주 좋아요. — 그리고 해딩턴 피블스도, 또 — 또 — 통틀어 여섯이 될 거예요. 그리고 오늘로부터 이레 뒤에 떠나요.”"]], [["“That’s rather sudden, Ludovic.”", "“좀 갑작스럽구나, 루도빅.”"]], [["“Yes, it is sudden; but we’re sick of London.", "“예, 갑작스럽지요. 그렇지만 저희는 런던에 진력이 났어요."], ["I should not care to go so soon myself, but Clontarf and Culpepper say that the season is early this year.", "저 혼자라면 그렇게 일찍 가고 싶지도 않겠지만, 클론타프와 컬페퍼가 올해는 철이 이르다고 해요."], ["I must go down to Framley before I start—about my horses; and therefore I came to tell you that I shall be there to-morrow.”", "떠나기 전에 프램리에 내려가 봐야 해요 — 제 말들 일로요. 그래서 내일 거기 가 있겠다고 어머니께 말씀드리러 온 거예요.”"]], [["“At Framley to-morrow!", "“내일 프램리에!"], ["If you could put it off for three days I should be going myself.”", "사흘만 미룰 수 있다면 나도 함께 갈 텐데.”"]], [["But Lord Lufton could not put it off for three days.", "그러나 러프턴 경은 사흘을 미룰 수 없었다."], ["It may be that on this occasion he did not wish for his mother’s presence at Framley while he was there; that he conceived that he should be more at his ease in giving orders about his stable if he were alone while so employed.", "아마도 이번에는 자기가 프램리에 있는 동안 어머니가 거기 있는 것을 바라지 않았을 것이다. 마구간 일을 지시하는 데에도 그 일에 매여 있는 동안 혼자라면 마음이 더 편하겠다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["At any rate he declined her company, and on the following morning did go down to Framley by himself.", "아무튼 그는 어머니와 함께 가는 것을 마다했고, 이튿날 아침에 정말로 혼자서 프램리로 내려갔다."]], [["“Mark,” said Mrs.", "“마크,”"], ["Robarts, hurrying into her husband’s book-room about the middle of the day, “Lord Lufton is at home.", "그날 한낮 무렵 남편의 서재로 서둘러 들어서며 로바츠 부인이 말했다. “러프턴 경이 집에 와 계셔요."], ["Have you heard it?”", "들었어요?”"]], [["“What!", "“뭐!"], ["here at Framley?”", "여기 프램리에?”"]], [["“He is over at Framley Court; so the servants say.", "“프램리 코트에 와 계신다고 하인들이 그래요."], ["Carson saw him in the paddock with some of the horses.", "카슨이 방목장에서 말 몇 마리와 함께 있는 그분을 보았대요."], ["Won’t you go and see him?”", "가서 만나 보지 않을 거예요?”"]], [["“Of course I will,”", "“물론 갈 거야,”"], ["said Mark, shutting up his papers.", "서류를 덮으며 마크가 말했다."], ["“Lady Lufton can’t be here, and if he is alone he will probably come and dine.”", "“레이디 러프턴은 안 계실 테고, 그 사람이 혼자라면 아마 와서 저녁을 먹겠지.”"]], [["“I don’t know about that,”", "“그건 모르겠어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts, thinking of poor Lucy.", "딱한 루시를 생각하며 로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“He is not in the least particular.", "“그 사람은 조금도 까다롭지 않아."], ["What does for us will do for him.", "우리한테 되는 것이면 그 사람에게도 될 거야."], ["I shall ask him, at any rate.”", "아무튼 나는 청해 볼 참이야.”"], ["And without further parley the clergyman took up his hat and went off in search of his friend.", "그리고 더 말을 주고받지 않고 목사는 모자를 집어 들고 벗을 찾아 나섰다."]], [["Lucy Robarts had been present when the gardener brought in tidings of Lord Lufton’s arrival at Framley, and was aware that Fanny had gone to tell her husband.", "정원사가 러프턴 경이 프램리에 왔다는 소식을 들고 들어왔을 때 루시 로바츠도 그 자리에 있었고, 패니가 남편에게 알리러 간 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["“He won’t come here, will he?”", "“그분이 여기 오지는 않겠지요?”"], ["she said, as soon as Mrs. Robarts returned.", "로바츠 부인이 돌아오자마자 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I can’t say,”", "“모르겠어요,”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."], ["“I hope not.", "“안 오시기를 바라요."], ["He ought not to do so, and I don’t think he will.", "그러셔서는 안 되고, 그러지 않으실 것 같아요."], ["But Mark says that he will ask him to dinner.”", "그런데 마크는 저녁을 먹으러 오라고 청하겠다고 해요.”"]], [["“Then, Fanny, I must be taken ill.", "“그러면요, 패니, 저는 병이 나야겠어요."], ["There is nothing else for it.”", "달리 방도가 없어요.”"]], [["“I don’t think he will come.", "“오시지 않을 것 같아요."], ["I don’t think he can be so cruel.", "그렇게 매정하실 수는 없을 거예요."], ["Indeed, I feel sure that he won’t; but I thought it right to tell you.”", "정말이지 오시지 않으리라고 확신해요. 그래도 루시에게 말해 두는 것이 옳다고 생각했어요.”"]], [["Lucy also conceived that it was improbable that Lord Lufton should come to the parsonage under the present circumstances; and she declared to herself that it would not be possible that she should appear at table if he did do so; but, nevertheless, the idea of his being at Framley was, perhaps, not altogether painful to her.", "루시 역시 지금의 형편에서 러프턴 경이 목사관으로 올 리는 없다고 여겼다. 그리고 만일 그가 그렇게 한다면 자기가 식탁에 나와 앉아 있을 수는 없으리라고 스스로에게 선언했다."], ["She did not recognize any pleasure as coming to her from his arrival, but still there was something in his presence which was, unconsciously to herself, soothing to her feelings.", "그러나 그런데도 그가 프램리에 와 있다는 생각이 그녀에게 아주 괴롭기만 한 것은 아니었다. 그녀는 그의 도착에서 어떤 기쁨이 자기에게 오는 것을 인정하지 않았으나, 그래도 그가 곁에 있다는 데에는 그녀 자신도 모르는 사이에 그녀의 마음을 달래 주는 무엇이 있었다."], ["But that terrible question remained;—how was she to act if it should turn out that he was coming to dinner?", "그러나 저 끔찍한 물음이 남아 있었다. — 그가 저녁을 먹으러 온다는 것이 사실로 드러나면 그녀는 어떻게 처신해야 하는가?"]], [["“If he does come, Fanny,”", "“그분이 정말 오신다면요, 패니,”"], ["she said, solemnly, after a pause, “I must keep to my own room, and leave Mark to think what he pleases.", "잠시 말을 멈춘 뒤에 그녀가 엄숙하게 말했다. “저는 제 방에만 있어야 해요."], ["It will be better for me to make a fool of myself there, than in his presence in the drawing-room.”", "그러고서 마크는 무엇이든 마음대로 생각하도록 두는 거예요. 응접실에서 그분 앞에서 바보짓을 하는 것보다는 거기서 바보짓을 하는 편이 저에게 낫겠어요.”"]], [["Mark Robarts took his hat and stick and went over at once to the home paddock, in which he knew that Lord Lufton was engaged with the horses and grooms. He also was in no supremely happy frame of mind, for his correspondence with Mr.", "마크 로바츠는 모자와 지팡이를 들고, 러프턴 경이 말들과 마부들과 함께 있는 줄로 알고 있는 집 앞 방목장으로 곧장 건너갔다."], ["Tozer was on the increase. He had received notice from that indefatigable gentleman that certain “overdue bills”", "그 역시 마음이 그다지 편안하지는 못했으니, 토저 씨와의 편지 왕래가 늘어나고 있었기 때문이다."], ["were now lying at the bank in Barchester, and were very desirous of his, Mr. Robarts’s, notice.", "그는 그 지칠 줄 모르는 신사로부터, “만기가 지난 어음들”이 지금 바체스터의 은행에 놓여 있으며 로바츠 씨 본인이 그것에 주의를 기울여 주기를 몹시 바란다는 통고를 받았다."], ["A concatenation of certain peculiarly unfortunate circumstances made it indispensably necessary that Mr. Tozer should be repaid, without further loss of time, the various sums of money which he had advanced on the credit of Mr.", "유별나게 딱한 사정 몇 가지가 겹쳐 일어난 까닭에, 토저 씨가 로바츠 씨의 이름을 믿고 내어 준 여러 금액을 더 이상 시간을 잃지 않고 토저 씨에게 되돌려 주는 것이 반드시 필요하게 되었다는 것이었다."], ["Robarts’s name, &c. &c.", "등등, 등등, 등등."], ["&c. No absolute threat was put forth, and, singular to say, no actual amount was named.", "뚜렷한 위협이 밖으로 드러난 것은 아니었고, 이상하게도 실제 금액이 적혀 있지도 않았다."], ["Mr. Robarts, however, could not but observe, with a most painfully accurate attention, that mention was made, not of an overdue bill, but of overdue bills.", "그러나 로바츠 씨는 만기가 지난 어음 하나가 아니라 어음들이라고 적혀 있음을 아프도록 꼼꼼한 주의로 알아채지 않을 수 없었다."], ["What if Mr. Tozer were to demand from him the instant repayment of nine hundred pounds?", "만일 토저 씨가 그에게 구백 파운드를 당장 갚으라고 요구한다면 어찌 될 것인가?"], ["Hitherto he had merely written to Mr. Sowerby, and he might have had an answer from that gentleman this morning, but no such answer had as yet reached him.", "지금까지 그는 사워비 씨에게 편지를 써 두었을 뿐이었고, 그 신사로부터 오늘 아침에 답장을 받았을 수도 있었으나 그런 답장은 아직 그에게 닿지 않았다."], ["Consequently he was not, at the present moment, in a very happy frame of mind.", "그러니 그는 이때 마음이 그다지 편안한 형편이 아니었다."]], [["He soon found himself with Lord Lufton and the horses.", "그는 이내 러프턴 경과 말들 곁에 이르렀다."], ["Four or five of them were being walked slowly about the paddock in the care of as many men or boys, and the sheets were being taken off them—off one after another, so that their master might look at them with the more accuracy and satisfaction.", "말 네댓 마리가 그만큼 되는 사내들과 아이들의 손에 매여 방목장 안을 천천히 걸리고 있었고, 그 등에 덮인 천이 하나씩 차례로 벗겨지고 있었다. 주인이 더 정확하게, 더 흐뭇하게 살펴볼 수 있도록 하려는 것이었다."], ["But though Lord Lufton was thus doing his duty, and going through his work, he was not doing it with his whole heart,—as the head groom perceived very well.", "그러나 러프턴 경은 이렇게 제 할 일을 하고 제 몫을 치러 내면서도 온 마음으로 그것을 하고 있지는 않았으니, 마부장은 그것을 아주 잘 알아보았다."], ["He was fretful about the nags, and seemed anxious to get them out of his sight as soon as he had made a decent pretext of looking at them.", "그는 그 말들을 두고 안달을 냈고, 살펴보는 척을 웬만큼 갖추고 나면 되도록 빨리 그것들을 눈앞에서 치워 버리고 싶어 하는 듯했다."]], [["“How are you, Lufton?”", "“잘 있었나, 러프턴?”"], ["said Robarts, coming forward.", "앞으로 나서며 로바츠가 말했다."], ["“They told me that you were down, and so I came across at once.”", "“자네가 내려왔다고들 해서 곧바로 건너왔네.”"]], [["“Yes; I only got here this morning, and should have been over with you directly.", "“그래. 오늘 아침에야 닿았고, 곧 자네에게 건너가려던 참이었네."], ["I am going to Norway for six weeks or so, and it seems that the fish are so early this year, that we must start at once.", "나는 여섯 주쯤 노르웨이에 가 있을 참인데, 올해는 고기가 어찌나 이르다고들 하는지 당장 떠나야 한다는군."], ["I have a matter on which I want to speak to you before I leave; and, indeed, it was that which brought me down more than anything else.”", "떠나기 전에 자네와 이야기해 두고 싶은 일이 하나 있네. 사실 무엇보다도 나를 여기까지 내려오게 한 것이 그 일이야.”"]], [["There was something hurried and not altogether easy about his manner as he spoke, which struck Robarts, and made him think that this promised matter to be spoken of would not be agreeable in discussion.", "말을 하는 그의 태도에는 서두르는 듯하고 아주 편안하지만은 않은 데가 있어서 로바츠의 눈에 걸렸고, 이야기하겠다고 미리 알린 그 일이 나누기에 유쾌한 일은 아니겠구나 하는 생각이 들게 했다."], ["He did not know whether Lord Lufton might not again be mixed up with Tozer and the bills.", "러프턴 경이 또 토저와 어음에 얽혀 든 것이 아닌가 하는 생각도 들었다."]], [["“You will dine with us to-day,”", "“오늘 우리와 함께 저녁을 먹지,”"], ["he said, “if, as I suppose, you are all alone.”", "그가 말했다. “내 짐작대로 자네가 아주 혼자라면 말이야.”"]], [["“Yes, I am all alone.”", "“그래, 나 혼자야.”"]], [["“Then you’ll come?”", "“그러면 오는 거지?”"]], [["“Well, I don’t quite know.", "“음, 잘 모르겠네."], ["No, I don’t think I can go over to dinner.", "아니, 저녁을 먹으러 건너가지는 못할 것 같아."], ["Don’t look so disgusted.", "그렇게 언짢은 얼굴은 하지 말게."], ["I’ll explain it all to you just now.”", "지금 곧 다 설명해 줄 테니.”"]], [["What could there be in the wind; and how was it possible that Tozer’s bill should make it inexpedient for Lord Lufton to dine at the parsonage?", "무슨 바람이 불고 있는 것일까? 그리고 토저의 어음 때문에 러프턴 경이 목사관에서 저녁을 먹는 것이 마땅찮은 일이 될 수 있다니 어찌 그럴 수가 있는가?"], ["Robarts, however, said nothing further about it at the moment, but turned off to look at the horses.", "그러나 로바츠는 그때는 그 일에 대해 더 아무 말도 하지 않고, 말들을 보는 쪽으로 이야기를 돌렸다."]], [["“They are an uncommonly nice set of animals,”", "“유별나게 좋은 짐승들이군,”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Well, yes; I don’t know.", "“음, 그렇지. 나도 모르겠네."], ["When a man has four or five horses to look at, somehow or other he never has one fit to go.", "사람이 볼 말을 네댓 마리 가지고 있으면, 어찌 된 일인지 나갈 만한 것은 한 마리도 없거든."], ["That chestnut mare is a picture, now that nobody wants her; but she wasn’t able to carry me well to hounds a single day last winter.", "저 밤색 암말은 아무도 찾지 않게 되고 나니 그림 같지. 그런데 지난겨울에는 사냥개를 따라가도록 나를 제대로 실어 준 날이 하루도 없었네."], ["Take them in, Pounce; that’ll do.”", "안으로 들여라, 파운스. 그만하면 됐다.”"]], [["“Won’t your lordship run your eye over the old black ’oss?”", "“나리, 저 늙은 검정 말놈은 한번 눈여겨보시지 않겠습니까요?”"], ["said Pounce, the head groom, in a melancholy tone; “he’s as fine, sir—as fine as a stag.”", "마부장 파운스가 처량한 어조로 말했다. “그놈은 훌륭합니다요, 나리 — 수사슴만큼이나 훌륭합니다요.”"]], [["“To tell you the truth, I think they’re too fine; but that’ll do; take them in.", "“사실을 말하면 나는 저것들이 너무 훌륭한 것 같네. 그래도 그만하면 됐다."], ["And now, Mark, if you’re at leisure, we’ll take a turn round the place.”", "들여라. 그러면 마크, 자네가 한가하다면 이 근방을 한 바퀴 돌아보세.”"]], [["Mark, of course, was at leisure, and so they started on their walk.", "마크는 물론 한가했고, 그래서 두 사람은 걸음을 떼었다."]], [["“You’re too difficult to please about your stable,”", "“자네는 마구간을 두고 만족시키기가 너무 어려운 사람이군,”"], ["Robarts began.", "로바츠가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Never mind the stable now,”", "“마구간은 이제 접어 두게,”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."], ["“The truth is, I am not thinking about it.", "“사실은 나는 그 일을 생각하고 있지 않네."], ["Mark,”", "마크,”"], ["he then said, very abruptly, “I want you to be frank with me.", "이어 그는 아주 느닷없이 말했다. “자네가 나에게 솔직해 주기를 바라네."], ["Has your sister ever spoken to you about me?”", "자네 누이가 나에 대해 자네에게 무슨 말을 한 적이 있나?”"]], [["“My sister; Lucy?”", "“내 누이? 루시가?”"]], [["“Yes; your sister Lucy.”", "“그래. 자네 누이 루시.”"]], [["“No, never; at least nothing especial; nothing that I can remember at this moment.”", "“아니, 없네. 적어도 별다른 것은 없었어. 지금 이 순간 내가 기억할 만한 것은 아무것도 없네.”"]], [["“Nor your wife?”", "“자네 아내도?”"]], [["“Spoken about you!—Fanny?", "“자네에 대해 말한 적이 있느냐고?"], ["Of course she has, in an ordinary way.", "— 패니가? 물론 여느 투로는 있었지."], ["It would be impossible that she should not.", "없을 수가 없지 않은가."], ["But what do you mean?”", "그런데 무슨 말인가?”"]], [["“Have either of them told you that I made an offer to your sister?”", "“내가 자네 누이에게 청혼을 했다고 그 두 사람 가운데 누구든 자네에게 말한 적이 있나?”"]], [["“That you made an offer to Lucy?”", "“자네가 루시에게 청혼을 했다고?”"]], [["“Yes, that I made an offer to Lucy.”", "“그래. 내가 루시에게 청혼을 했다고.”"]], [["“No; nobody has told me so.", "“아니, 아무도 나에게 그런 말을 하지 않았네."], ["I have never dreamed of such a thing; nor, as far as I believe, have they.", "나는 그런 일을 꿈에도 생각해 본 적이 없고, 내가 믿는 한 그들도 그렇네."], ["If anybody has spread such a report, or said that either of them have hinted at such a thing, it is a base lie.", "누가 그런 소문을 퍼뜨렸거나, 그 두 사람 가운데 누구든 그런 일을 비추었다고 말했다면 그것은 비열한 거짓말이야."], ["Good heavens!", "맙소사!"], ["Lufton, for what do you take them?”", "러프턴, 자네는 그들을 무엇으로 보는가?”"]], [["“But I did,”", "“하지만 나는 했네,”"], ["said his lordship.", "나리가 말했다."]], [["“Did what?”", "“무엇을 했다고?”"], ["said the parson.", "목사가 말했다."]], [["“I did make your sister an offer.”", "“나는 정말 자네 누이에게 청혼을 했네.”"]], [["“You made Lucy an offer of marriage!”", "“자네가 루시에게 혼인을 청했다니!”"]], [["“Yes, I did;—in as plain language as a gentleman could use to a lady.”", "“그래, 했네. — 신사가 숙녀에게 쓸 수 있는 가장 분명한 말로.”"]], [["“And what answer did she make?”", "“그래서 그 아이는 어떤 답을 했나?”"]], [["“She refused me.", "“나를 거절했네."], ["And now, Mark, I have come down here with the express purpose of making that offer again.", "그리고 마크, 나는 이제 그 청혼을 다시 하겠다는 뚜렷한 뜻으로 여기 내려왔네."], ["Nothing could be more decided than your sister’s answer.", "자네 누이의 답보다 더 단호한 것은 있을 수 없었지."], ["It struck me as being almost uncourteously decided.", "거의 예의에 어긋날 만큼 단호하다고 내게 여겨졌네."], ["But still it is possible that circumstances may have weighed with her, which ought not to weigh with her.", "그러나 그래도 그녀에게 무게를 지워서는 안 될 사정들이 그녀에게 무게를 지웠을 수도 있어. 그녀의 사랑이 다른 누구에게도 주어져 있지 않다면 나에게는 아직 기회가 있을지도 모르네."], ["If her love be not given to any one else, I may still have a chance of it.", "마음이 약해서는 못 얻는다는 저 옛말 그대로지, 자네도 알 테고."], ["It’s the old story of faint heart, you know: at any rate, I mean to try my luck again; and thinking over it with deliberate purpose, I have come to the conclusion that I ought to tell you before I see her.”", "아무튼 나는 다시 한 번 내 운을 걸어 볼 참이야. 그리고 곰곰이 뜻을 세워 생각해 본 끝에, 그녀를 만나기 전에 자네에게 말해 두어야 한다는 결론에 이르렀네.”"]], [["Lord Lufton in love with Lucy!", "러프턴 경이 루시와 사랑에 빠졌다니!"], ["As these words repeated themselves over and over again within Mark Robarts’s mind, his mind added to them notes of surprise without end.", "이 말이 마크 로바츠의 마음속에서 몇 번이고 되풀이되는 동안, 그의 마음은 그 말에 끝없는 놀라움의 표를 덧붙였다."], ["How had it possibly come about,—and why?", "어떻게 이런 일이 있을 수 있단 말인가 — 그리고 왜?"], ["In his estimation his sister Lucy was a very simple girl—not plain indeed, but by no means beautiful; certainly not stupid, but by no means brilliant.", "그가 헤아리기로 누이 루시는 아주 단순한 아이였다. 못생기지야 않았으나 결코 아름답지 않았고, 분명히 아둔하지는 않았으나 결코 빛나지도 않았다."], ["And then, he would have said, that of all men whom he knew, Lord Lufton would have been the last to fall in love with such a girl as his sister.", "그리고 자기가 아는 모든 남자 가운데 러프턴 경이야말로 자기 누이 같은 아이와 사랑에 빠질 사람으로는 가장 마지막일 것이라고 그는 말했을 것이었다."], ["And now, what was he to say or do?", "그런데 이제 그는 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 해야 하는가?"], ["What views was he bound to hold?", "어떤 견해를 붙들어야 하는가?"], ["In what direction should he act?", "어느 방향으로 움직여야 하는가?"], ["There was Lady Lufton on the one side, to whom he owed everything.", "한편에는 레이디 러프턴이 있었으니, 그는 그분에게 모든 것을 입은 몸이었다."], ["How would life be possible to him in that parsonage—within a few yards of her elbow—if he consented to receive Lord Lufton as the acknowledged suitor of his sister?", "러프턴 경을 누이의 공인된 구혼자로 맞아들이는 데 동의한다면, 그분의 팔꿈치에서 몇 걸음 안 되는 그 목사관에서 그에게 삶이 어떻게 가능할 것인가?"], ["It would be a great match for Lucy, doubtless; but— Indeed, he could not bring himself to believe that Lucy could in truth become the absolute reigning queen of Framley Court.", "루시에게는 의심할 것 없이 대단한 혼사일 것이다. 그러나 — 정말로 그는 루시가 프램리 코트에 절대로 군림하는 여왕이 될 수 있으리라고는 스스로 믿을 수가 없었다."]], [["“Do you think that Fanny knows anything of all this?”", "“패니가 이 모든 일에 대해 무엇이든 알고 있다고 생각하나?”"], ["he said, after a moment or two.", "잠시 뒤에 그가 말했다."]], [["“I cannot possibly tell.", "“나로서는 도무지 알 수 없네."], ["If she does it is not with my knowledge.", "그녀가 안다면 내가 알려서 안 것은 아니야."], ["I should have thought that you could best answer that.”", "그것은 자네가 가장 잘 답할 수 있으리라고 나는 생각했네.”"]], [["“I cannot answer it at all,”", "“나는 전혀 답할 수가 없네,”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."], ["“I, at least, have had no remotest idea of such a thing.”", "“적어도 나는 그런 일을 아주 멀찍이서도 짐작해 본 적이 없어.”"]], [["“Your ideas of it now need not be at all remote,”", "“이제는 자네 짐작이 조금도 멀찍이 있을 필요가 없네,”"], ["said Lord Lufton, with a faint smile; “and you may know it as a fact.", "러프턴 경이 희미하게 웃으며 말했다. “그리고 자네는 그것을 사실로 알아도 좋아."], ["I did make her an offer of marriage; I was refused; I am going to repeat it; and I am now taking you into my confidence, in order that, as her brother, and as my friend, you may give me such assistance as you can.”", "나는 그녀에게 혼인을 청했고, 거절당했고, 그것을 되풀이할 참이네. 그리고 나는 지금 자네를 내 속내 안으로 들이고 있는데, 그것은 자네가 그녀의 오라비로서, 또 내 벗으로서 할 수 있는 만큼 나를 도와 주기를 바라서네.”"], ["They then walked on in silence for some yards, after which Lord Lufton added: “And now I’ll dine with you to-day if you wish it.”", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 몇 발짝을 잠자코 걸었고, 그 뒤에 러프턴 경이 덧붙였다. “그리고 자네가 바란다면 나는 오늘 자네와 저녁을 먹겠네.”"]], [["Mr. Robarts did not know what to say; he could not bethink himself what answer duty required of him.", "로바츠 씨는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐다."], ["He had no right to interfere between his sister and such a marriage, if she herself should wish it; but still there was something terrible in the thought of it!", "의무가 자기에게 어떤 답을 요구하는지 생각해 낼 수 없었다. 누이가 스스로 그것을 바란다면 그로서는 누이와 그런 혼사 사이에 끼어들 권리가 없었다."], ["He had a vague conception that it must come to evil; that the project was a dangerous one; and that it could not finally result happily for any of them.", "그러나 그래도 그 생각에는 무언가 끔찍한 데가 있었다! 그것이 나쁜 끝으로 갈 수밖에 없다는, 그 계획이 위태로운 것이라는, 그리고 마침내 그들 가운데 누구에게도 행복하게 마무리될 수 없다는 어렴풋한 생각이 그에게 있었다."], ["What would Lady Lufton say?", "레이디 러프턴은 무슨 말씀을 하실 것인가?"], ["That undoubtedly was the chief source of his dismay.", "의심할 것 없이 그것이 그의 낭패감의 가장 큰 근원이었다."]], [["“Have you spoken to your mother about this?”", "“이 일에 대해 어머님께 말씀드렸나?”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“My mother?", "“내 어머니?"], ["no; why speak to her till I know my fate?", "아니. 내 운명을 알기 전에 왜 그분께 말하겠나?"], ["A man does not like to speak much of such matters if there be a probability of his being rejected.", "거절당할 가능성이 있다면 사람은 그런 일을 두고 말을 많이 하고 싶어 하지 않네."], ["I tell you because I do not like to make my way into your house under a false pretence.”", "내가 자네에게 말하는 것은 거짓 구실을 앞세워 자네 집에 발을 들이고 싶지 않기 때문이야.”"]], [["“But what would Lady Lufton say?”", "“그렇지만 레이디 러프턴은 무슨 말씀을 하시겠나?”"]], [["“I think it probable that she would be displeased on the first hearing it; that in four-and-twenty hours she would be reconciled; and that after a week or so Lucy would be her dearest favourite and the prime minister of all her machinations.", "“처음 들으시면 언짢아하실 것이고, 스물네 시간 안에 마음을 푸실 것이고, 한 주쯤 지나면 루시가 그분이 가장 아끼는 사람이자 그분의 온갖 계략의 총리가 될 것이 있음직하다고 나는 생각하네."], ["You don’t know my mother as well as I do.", "자네는 내 어머니를 나만큼 알지 못하네."], ["She would give her head off her shoulders to do me a pleasure.”", "그분은 나를 기쁘게 하려면 어깨에서 머리를 떼어 내어 주실 사람이야.”"]], [["“And for that reason,”", "“그러니 바로 그런 이유로,”"], ["said Mark Robarts, “you ought, if possible, to do her pleasure.”", "마크 로바츠가 말했다. “자네는 될 수 있으면 그분을 기쁘게 해 드려야 하네.”"]], [["“I cannot absolutely marry a wife of her choosing, if you mean that,”", "“그분이 골라 주는 아내와 무조건 혼인할 수야 없지, 자네 말이 그런 뜻이라면,”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."]], [["They went on walking about the garden for an hour, but they hardly got any farther than the point to which we have now brought them.", "두 사람은 한 시간 동안 정원을 거닐었으나, 우리가 지금 두 사람을 데려다 놓은 지점에서 거의 더 나아가지 못했다."], ["Mark Robarts could not make up his mind on the spur of the moment; nor, as he said more than once to Lord Lufton, could he be at all sure that Lucy would in any way be guided by him.", "마크 로바츠는 그 자리에서 대뜸 마음을 정할 수 없었다. 또한 그가 러프턴 경에게 한 번 이상 말한 대로, 루시가 무슨 일에서든 자기 뜻에 이끌리리라고는 조금도 확신할 수 없었다."], ["It was, therefore, at last settled between them that Lord Lufton should come to the parsonage immediately after breakfast on the following morning.", "그리하여 마침내 두 사람 사이에는 러프턴 경이 이튿날 아침 식사 직후에 목사관으로 오는 것으로 정해졌다."], ["It was agreed also that the dinner had better not come off, and Robarts promised that he would, if possible, have determined by the morning as to what advice he would give his sister.", "저녁 식사는 하지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 것도 함께 정해졌고, 로바츠는 될 수 있으면 아침까지 누이에게 어떤 조언을 할지 마음을 정해 두겠다고 약속했다."]], [["He went direct home to the parsonage from Framley Court, feeling that he was altogether in the dark till he should have consulted his wife.", "그는 프램리 코트에서 곧장 목사관으로 돌아갔다. 아내와 의논을 하기까지는 자기가 온통 어둠 속에 있다고 느꼈던 것이다."], ["How would he feel if Lucy were to become Lady Lufton?", "루시가 레이디 러프턴이 된다면 자기는 어떤 기분일까?"], ["and how would he look Lady Lufton in the face in telling her that such was to be his sister’s destiny?", "그리고 그것이 누이의 팔자라고 레이디 러프턴에게 말하면서 그분의 얼굴을 어떻게 마주 볼까?"], ["On returning home he immediately found his wife, and had not been closeted with her five minutes before he knew, at any rate, all that she knew.", "집에 돌아와 그는 곧바로 아내를 찾아냈고, 아내와 단둘이 방에 든 지 오 분도 되지 않아 적어도 아내가 아는 모든 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["“And you mean to say that she does love him?”", "“그러면 그 아이가 정말 그 사람을 사랑한다는 말이야?”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“Indeed she does; and is it not natural that she should?", "“정말 그래요. 그리고 그러는 것이 당연하지 않아요?"], ["When I saw them so much together I feared that she would.", "두 사람이 그렇게 자주 함께 있는 것을 볼 때 저는 그 아이가 그렇게 되지 않을까 걱정했어요."], ["But I never thought that he would care for her.”", "그렇지만 그분이 그 아이를 마음에 두시리라고는 한 번도 생각하지 못했어요.”"]], [["Even Fanny did not as yet give Lucy credit for half her attractiveness.", "패니조차도 아직 루시의 매력을 절반도 인정해 주지 않고 있었다."], ["After an hour’s talking the interview between the husband and wife ended in a message to Lucy, begging her to join them both in the book-room.", "한 시간의 이야기 끝에 부부의 만남은 루시에게 보내는 전갈로 끝났으니, 서재로 와서 두 사람과 함께 있어 달라는 것이었다."]], [["“Aunt Lucy,”", "“루시 고모,” 통통한 어린 것이 말을 하면서 고모의 팔에 안겨 올라갔다."], ["said a chubby little darling, who was taken up into his aunt’s arms as he spoke, “papa and mamma ’ant ’oo in te tuddy, and I musn’t go wis ’oo.”", "“아빠하고 엄마가 떠재에서 고모를 부으신대요. 그런데 나는 고모하고 가치 가면 안 대요.”"]], [["Lucy, as she kissed the boy and pressed his face against her own, felt that her blood was running quick to her heart.", "루시는 사내아이에게 입을 맞추고 그 얼굴을 자기 얼굴에 대고 누르면서, 제 피가 빠르게 심장으로 달려가는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Musn’t ’oo go wis me, my own one?”", "“고모하고 가치 가면 안 대요, 우리 아기?”"], ["she said, as she put her playfellow down; but she played with the child only because she did not wish to betray even to him that she was hardly mistress of herself.", "놀이 동무를 내려놓으며 그녀가 말했다. 그러나 그녀가 아이와 놀아 준 것은 오직, 제가 스스로를 거의 다스리지 못하고 있다는 것을 이 아이에게조차 드러내고 싶지 않아서였다."], ["She knew that Lord Lufton was at Framley; she knew that her brother had been to him; she knew that a proposal had been made that he should come there that day to dinner.", "그녀는 러프턴 경이 프램리에 와 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 오라비가 그를 만나러 갔다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["Must it not therefore be the case that this call to a meeting in the study had arisen out of Lord Lufton’s arrival at Framley?", "그가 그날 저녁을 먹으러 이리로 오라는 청을 받았다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러니 서재로 모이라는 이 부름이 러프턴 경이 프램리에 온 데서 비롯한 것일 수밖에 없지 않겠는가?"], ["and yet, how could it have done so?", "그런데도 어떻게 그럴 수가 있단 말인가?"], ["Had Fanny betrayed her in order to prevent the dinner invitation?", "패니가 저녁 초대를 막으려고 저를 팔아넘긴 것일까?"], ["It could not be possible that Lord Lufton himself should have spoken on the subject!", "러프턴 경 자신이 그 일을 입에 올렸을 리는 없지 않은가!"], ["And then she again stooped to kiss the child, rubbed her hands across her forehead to smooth her hair, and erase, if that might be possible, the look of care which she wore, and then descended slowly to her brother’s sitting-room.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 다시 몸을 굽혀 아이에게 입을 맞추고, 손으로 이마를 쓸어 머리를 매만지고, 될 수 있는 일이라면 제가 걸치고 있는 근심스러운 표정을 지워 내려 하고서, 천천히 오라비의 거실로 내려갔다."]], [["Her hand paused for a second on the door ere she opened it, but she had resolved that, come what might, she would be brave.", "문을 열기 전에 그녀의 손이 문 위에서 한순간 멈추었다. 그러나 그녀는 무슨 일이 오든 용감하겠다고 작정해 두었다."], ["She pushed it open and walked in with a bold front, with eyes wide open, and a slow step.", "그녀는 문을 밀어 열고, 당당한 낯으로 눈을 크게 뜨고 느린 걸음으로 들어섰다."]], [["“Frank says that you want me,”", "“프랭크가 저를 부르신다고 하던데요,”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["Mr.", "로바츠 씨와 패니는 둘 다 난롯가에 서 있었고, 루시가 방에 들어섰을 때 서로 상대가 말을 꺼내기를 잠깐씩 기다렸다."], ["Robarts and Fanny were both standing up by the fireplace, and each waited a second for the other to speak when Lucy entered the room; and then Fanny began,—", "그러고서 패니가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Lord Lufton is here, Lucy.”", "“러프턴 경이 오셨어요, 루시.”"]], [["“Here! Where?", "“오셨다니!"], ["At the parsonage?”", "어디에요? 목사관에요?”"]], [["“No, not at the parsonage; but over at Framley Court,”", "“아니, 목사관은 아니고 프램리 코트에 와 있다,”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“And he promises to call here after breakfast to-morrow,”", "“그리고 내일 아침 식사 뒤에 이리로 찾아오겠다고 약속하셨어요,”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."], ["And then again there was a pause.", "그러고서 또 한 번 말이 멎었다."], ["Mrs. Robarts hardly dared to look Lucy in the face.", "로바츠 부인은 루시의 얼굴을 차마 바라보지 못했다."], ["She had not betrayed her trust, seeing that the secret had been told to Mark, not by her, but by Lord Lufton; but she could not but feel that Lucy would think that she had betrayed it.", "그 비밀을 마크에게 말한 것은 그녀가 아니라 러프턴 경이었으니 그녀가 맡은 믿음을 팔아넘긴 것은 아니었다. 그러나 루시가 저를 팔아넘겼다고 여길 것이라는 느낌을 그녀는 지울 수 없었다."]], [["“Very well,”", "“좋아요,”"], ["said Lucy, trying to smile; “I have no objection in life.”", "웃어 보려 애쓰며 루시가 말했다. “저야 조금도 마다할 것이 없어요.”"]], [["“But, Lucy, dear,”—and now Mrs.", "“그런데 루시,”"], ["Robarts put her arm round her sister-in-law’s waist,—“he is coming here especially to see you.”", "— 이때 로바츠 부인은 시누이의 허리에 팔을 둘렀다 — “그분은 특히 루시를 보러 오시는 거예요.”"]], [["“Oh; that makes a difference.", "“아, 그러면 이야기가 다르지요."], ["I am afraid that I shall be—engaged.”", "그때 저는 아마 — 볼일이 있을 것 같아 걱정이에요.”"]], [["“He has told everything to Mark,”", "“그분이 마크에게 다 말하셨어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["Lucy now felt that her bravery was almost deserting her.", "루시는 이제 용기가 저를 거의 버리고 가는 것을 느꼈다."], ["She hardly knew which way to look or how to stand.", "어느 쪽으로 눈을 두어야 할지, 어떻게 서 있어야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["Had Fanny told everything also?", "패니도 모든 것을 말해 버렸을까?"], ["There was so much that Fanny knew that Lord Lufton could not have known.", "패니가 아는 것 가운데 러프턴 경이 알 수 없었던 것이 그토록 많았다."], ["But, in truth, Fanny had told all—the whole story of Lucy’s love, and had described the reasons which had induced her to reject her suitor; and had done so in words which, had Lord Lufton heard them, would have made him twice as passionate in his love.", "그러나 사실 패니는 다 말했다 — 루시의 사랑의 온 이야기를 말했고, 그녀가 구혼자를 거절하게 만든 까닭들을 그려 주었다. 그리고 그 일을, 러프턴 경이 들었더라면 그의 사랑을 두 배로 격하게 만들었을 그런 말로 해 놓았다."]], [["And then it certainly did occur to Lucy to think why Lord Lufton should have come to Framley and told all this history to her brother.", "그러고 나자 러프턴 경이 왜 프램리로 와서 이 온 이야기를 제 오라비에게 했을까 하는 생각이 루시에게 분명히 떠올랐다."], ["She attempted for a moment to make herself believe that she was angry with him for doing so.", "그녀는 잠깐 그가 그렇게 한 것에 자기가 화가 났다고 스스로 믿어 보려 했다."], ["But she was not angry.", "그러나 그녀는 화가 나지 않았다."], ["She had not time to argue much about it, but there came upon her a gratified sensation of having been remembered, and thought of, and—loved.", "그 일을 두고 이러니저러니 따질 겨를이 없었으나, 자기가 기억되고 생각되고 — 사랑받았다는 흐뭇한 느낌이 그녀에게 밀려왔다."], ["Must it not be so?", "그렇지 않을 수가 있는가?"], ["Could it be possible that he himself would have told this tale to her brother, if he did not still love her?", "그가 아직도 자기를 사랑하지 않는다면 이 이야기를 제 오라비에게 스스로 털어놓았을 리가 있는가?"], ["Fifty times she had said to herself that his offer had been an affair of the moment, and fifty times she had been unhappy in so saying.", "쉰 번이나 그녀는 그의 청혼이 순간의 일이었다고 스스로에게 말했고, 쉰 번이나 그렇게 말하면서 마음이 언짢았다."], ["But this new coming of his could not be an affair of the moment.", "그러나 이번에 그가 온 것은 순간의 일일 수가 없었다."], ["She had been the dupe, she had thought, of an absurd passion on her own part; but now—how was it now?", "그녀는 제 쪽에서 터무니없는 열정에 속아 넘어간 것이라고 여겨 왔었다. 그러나 이제는 — 이제는 어떠한가?"], ["She did not bring herself to think that she should ever be Lady Lufton.", "그녀는 자기가 언젠가 레이디 러프턴이 될 것이라는 생각에까지는 이르지 않았다."], ["She had still, in some perversely obstinate manner, made up her mind against that result.", "그녀는 여전히, 어딘가 뒤틀리게 고집스러운 방식으로 그런 결말에 반대하는 마음을 굳혀 두고 있었다."], ["But yet, nevertheless, it did in some unaccountable manner satisfy her to feel that Lord Lufton had himself come down to Framley and himself told this story.", "그러나 그럼에도 러프턴 경이 몸소 프램리로 내려와 몸소 이 이야기를 했다는 것을 느끼는 것은 어쩐지 알 수 없는 방식으로 그녀를 흐뭇하게 했다."]], [["“He has told everything to Mark,” said Mrs.", "“그분이 마크에게 다 말하셨어요,”"], ["Robarts; and then again there was a pause for a moment, during which these thoughts passed through Lucy’s mind.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다. 그러고 나서 또 한순간 말이 멎었고, 그동안 이런 생각들이 루시의 마음을 지나갔다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래,” 마크가 말했다."], ["said Mark, “he has told me all, and he is coming here to-morrow morning that he may receive an answer from yourself.”", "“그 사람이 나에게 다 말했다. 그리고 네 입에서 직접 답을 받으려고 내일 아침 이리로 온다.”"]], [["“What answer?”", "“무슨 답을요?”"], ["said Lucy, trembling.", "루시가 떨면서 말했다."]], [["“Nay, dearest; who can say that but yourself?”", "“아니에요, 루시. 그것을 루시 아니면 누가 말할 수 있겠어요?”"], ["and her sister-in-law, as she spoke, pressed close against her.", "그리고 올케는 말을 하면서 그녀에게 바짝 몸을 붙였다."], ["“You must say that yourself.”", "“그것은 루시가 스스로 말해야 해요.”"]], [["Mrs. Robarts in her long conversation with her husband had pleaded strongly on Lucy’s behalf, taking, as it were, a part against Lady Lufton.", "로바츠 부인은 남편과의 그 긴 이야기 동안 루시를 위해 힘껏 변론했으니, 말하자면 레이디 러프턴에게 맞서는 편을 들었다."], ["She had said that if Lord Lufton persevered in his suit, they at the parsonage could not be justified in robbing Lucy of all that she had won for herself, in order to do Lady Lufton’s pleasure.", "그녀는 러프턴 경이 구혼을 굽히지 않는다면, 레이디 러프턴을 기쁘게 해 드리자고 목사관의 자기들이 루시가 스스로 얻어 낸 모든 것을 그녀에게서 빼앗는 일은 정당할 수 없다고 말했다."]], [["“But she will think,” said Mark, “that we have plotted and intrigued for this.", "“그렇지만 그분은 우리가 이 일을 두고 짜고 꾸몄다고 생각하실 거야,”"], ["She will call us ungrateful, and will make Lucy’s life wretched.”", "마크가 말했다. “우리를 은혜를 모르는 자들이라 부르실 것이고, 루시의 삶을 참담하게 만드실 거야.”"], ["To which the wife had answered, that all that must be left in God’s hands.", "이에 아내는 그 모든 것은 하느님의 손에 맡겨 두어야 한다고 대답했다."], ["They had not plotted or intrigued.", "그들은 짜지도 꾸미지도 않았다."], ["Lucy, though loving the man in her heart of hearts, had already once refused him, because she would not be thought to have snatched at so great a prize.", "루시는 마음 깊은 속으로 그 사람을 사랑하면서도 이미 한 번 그를 거절했으니, 그토록 큰 상을 낚아챈 것으로 여겨지고 싶지 않았기 때문이다."], ["But if Lord Lufton loved her so warmly that he had come down there in this manner, on purpose, as he himself had put it, that he might learn his fate, then—so argued Mrs. Robarts—they two, let their loyalty to Lady Lufton be ever so strong, could not justify it to their consciences to stand between Lucy and her lover.", "그러나 러프턴 경이 그녀를 그토록 뜨겁게 사랑하여, 스스로 말한 대로 제 운명을 알아보겠다는 뜻으로 일부러 이런 식으로 여기까지 내려왔다면, 그렇다면 — 로바츠 부인은 이렇게 따졌다 — 두 사람의 레이디 러프턴에 대한 충심이 아무리 굳세다 해도, 루시와 그녀의 연인 사이에 가로막고 서는 것을 자기들의 양심에 정당하다고 할 수는 없다는 것이었다."], ["Mark had still somewhat demurred to this, suggesting how terrible would be their plight if they should now encourage Lord Lufton, and if he, after such encouragement, when they should have quarrelled with Lady Lufton, should allow himself to be led away from his engagement by his mother.", "마크는 이에 대해 여전히 다소 주저했으니, 만일 이제 러프턴 경을 부추겨 놓고, 그 부추김을 받은 뒤 자기들이 레이디 러프턴과 다투게 되었을 때에 그가 어머니에게 이끌려 제 약속에서 벗어나 버린다면 두 사람의 처지가 얼마나 끔찍할 것인가를 들어 보였던 것이다."], ["To which Fanny had answered that justice was justice, and that right was right.", "이에 패니는 정의는 정의고 옳은 것은 옳은 것이라고 답했다."], ["Everything must be told to Lucy, and she must judge for herself.", "모든 것을 루시에게 말해야 하고, 루시가 스스로 판단해야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["“But I do not know what Lord Lufton wants,”", "“그렇지만 저는 러프턴 경이 무엇을 바라시는지 모르겠어요,”"], ["said Lucy, with her eyes fixed upon the ground, and now trembling more than ever.", "눈을 땅에 붙인 채, 그리고 이제 어느 때보다 더 떨면서 루시가 말했다."], ["“He did come to me, and I did give him an answer.”", "“그분은 저에게 오셨고, 저는 답을 드렸어요.”"]], [["“And is that answer to be final?”", "“그러면 그 답이 마지막인 거냐?”"], ["said Mark,—somewhat cruelly, for Lucy had not yet been told that her lover had made any repetition of his proposal.", "마크가 말했다 — 다소 매정하게 그랬으니, 루시는 그 연인이 청혼을 되풀이했다는 말을 아직 듣지 못했던 것이다."], ["Fanny, however, determined that no injustice should be done, and therefore she at last continued the story.", "그러나 패니는 부당한 일이 있어서는 안 된다고 작정하고, 그래서 마침내 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“We know that you did give him an answer, dearest; but gentlemen sometimes will not put up with one answer on such a subject.", "“루시가 답을 드린 것은 우리도 알아요. 그렇지만 신사들은 그런 문제를 두고 답 하나로 물러서지 않는 때가 있어요."], ["Lord Lufton has declared to Mark that he means to ask again.", "러프턴 경은 다시 청하실 작정이라고 마크에게 선언하셨어요."], ["He has come down here on purpose to do so.”", "그러려고 일부러 여기까지 내려오신 거예요.”"]], [["“And Lady Lufton—”", "“그러면 레이디 러프턴은 —”"], ["said Lucy, speaking hardly above a whisper, and still hiding her face as she leaned against her sister’s shoulder.", "루시가 속삭임을 겨우 넘는 소리로 말했다. 올케의 어깨에 기대어 여전히 얼굴을 감추고 있었다."]], [["“Lord Lufton has not spoken to his mother about it,”", "“러프턴 경은 그 일을 어머님께 말씀드리지 않았다,”"], ["said Mark; and it immediately became clear to Lucy, from the tone of her brother’s voice, that he, at least, would not be pleased, should she accept her lover’s vow.", "마크가 말했다. 그러자 오라비의 목소리에서 루시는 곧, 적어도 오라비는 자기가 그 연인의 맹세를 받아들인다면 흐뭇해하지 않으리라는 것을 분명히 알아차렸다."]], [["“You must decide out of your own heart, dear,”", "“루시는 제 마음에서 나오는 대로 정해야 해요,”"], ["said Fanny, generously.", "패니가 너그럽게 말했다."], ["“Mark and I know how well you have behaved, for I have told him everything.”", "“마크와 저는 루시가 얼마나 훌륭하게 처신했는지 알아요. 제가 다 말했으니까요.”"], ["Lucy shuddered and leaned closer against her sister as this was said to her.", "이 말을 듣자 루시는 몸을 떨며 올케에게 더 바짝 기댔다."], ["“I had no alternative, dearest, but to tell him.", "“저로서는 마크에게 말하는 것밖에 달리 길이 없었어요."], ["It was best so; was it not?", "그러는 것이 가장 좋았어요."], ["But nothing has been told to Lord Lufton.", "그렇지 않아요? 그렇지만 러프턴 경께는 아무것도 말하지 않았어요."], ["Mark would not let him come here to-day, because it would have flurried you, and he wished to give you time to think.", "마크가 그분이 오늘 이리로 오시는 것을 막았어요. 그러면 루시가 허둥거릴 테니, 생각할 겨를을 주고 싶었던 거예요."], ["But you can see him to-morrow morning,—can you not?", "그렇지만 내일 아침에는 그분을 만날 수 있지요?"], ["and then answer him.”", "그러고서 답을 드리면 되고요.”"]], [["Lucy now stood perfectly silent, feeling that she dearly loved her sister-in-law for her sisterly kindness—for that sisterly wish to promote a sister’s love; but still there was in her mind a strong resolve not to allow Lord Lufton to come there under the idea that he would be received as a favoured lover.", "루시는 이제 온전히 잠자코 서 있었다. 올케의 그 자매다운 친절에 — 자매의 사랑을 밀어 주려는 그 자매다운 마음에 — 올케를 몹시 사랑한다고 느꼈다."], ["Her love was powerful, but so also was her pride; and she could not bring herself to bear the scorn which would lay in Lady Lufton’s eyes.", "그러나 그런데도 그녀의 마음에는, 러프턴 경이 총애받는 연인으로 맞아들여질 것이라는 생각을 품고 이리로 오게 두지 않겠다는 굳센 결심이 있었다. 그녀의 사랑은 강했으나 그녀의 자랑도 그만큼 강했다."], ["“His mother will despise me, and then he will despise me too,”", "그리고 그녀는 레이디 러프턴의 눈에 담길 그 멸시를 차마 견딜 수가 없었다. “그분의 어머니가 나를 업신여기실 것이고, 그러면 그이도 나를 업신여기게 될 거야,”"], ["she said to herself; and with a strong gulp of disappointed love and ambition she determined to persist.", "그녀는 스스로에게 말했다. 그리고 어그러진 사랑과 야심을 굳세게 한 번 꿀꺽 삼키고서 그녀는 버티기로 마음을 정했다."]], [["“Shall we leave you now, dear; and speak of it again to-morrow morning, before he comes?”", "“이제 우리가 나가 있을까요? 그리고 내일 아침 그분이 오시기 전에 다시 이야기하는 게 좋겠어요?”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."]], [["“That will be the best,”", "“그게 가장 좋겠다,”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."], ["“Turn it in your mind every way to-night.", "“오늘 밤 그 일을 마음속에서 이리저리 다 뒤집어 보아라. 기도를 마친 뒤에 그것을 생각해 보고 — 그리고 루시, 이리 오렴.”"], ["Think of it when you have said your prayers—and, Lucy, come here to me;”—then, taking her in his arms, he kissed her with a tenderness that was not customary with him towards her.", "그러고서 그는 그녀를 팔에 안고, 그녀에게 여느 때 보이지 않던 다정함으로 입을 맞추었다. “이 말은 해 두는 것이 마땅하겠다,”"], ["“It is fair,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “that I should tell you this: that I have perfect confidence in your judgment and feeling; and that I will stand by you as your brother in whatever decision you may come to.", "“나는 네 판단과 네 마음을 온전히 믿는다. 그리고 네가 어떤 결정에 이르든 나는 오라비로서 네 편에 서겠다."], ["Fanny and I both think that you have behaved excellently, and are both of us sure that you will do what is best.", "패니와 나는 둘 다 네가 훌륭하게 처신했다고 생각하고, 둘 다 네가 가장 좋은 일을 하리라고 확신한다."], ["Whatever you do I will stick to you;—and so will Fanny.”", "네가 무엇을 하든 나는 네게 붙어 있겠다 — 패니도 그럴 것이다.”"]], [["“Dearest, dearest Mark!”", "“마크, 사랑하는 마크!”"]], [["“And now we will say nothing more about it till to-morrow morning,”", "“그러면 이제 내일 아침까지 이 일에 대해서는 더 아무 말도 하지 않기로 해요,”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."]], [["But Lucy felt that this saying nothing more about it till to-morrow morning would be tantamount to an acceptance on her part of Lord Lufton’s offer. Mrs.", "그러나 루시는 내일 아침까지 이 일에 대해 더 아무 말도 하지 않는 것이 자기 쪽에서 러프턴 경의 청혼을 받아들이는 것과 다름없는 일이 되리라고 느꼈다."], ["Robarts knew, and Mr. Robarts also now knew, the secret of her heart; and if, such being the case, she allowed Lord Lufton to come there with the acknowledged purpose of pleading his own suit, it would be impossible for her not to yield.", "로바츠 부인이 알고 있었고, 이제 로바츠 씨도 그녀 마음의 비밀을 알고 있었다."], ["If she were resolved that she would not yield, now was the time for her to stand her ground and make her fight.", "사정이 그러한데도 러프턴 경이 제 구혼을 스스로 변론하겠다는 뜻을 드러내고 이리로 오는 것을 그녀가 허락한다면, 그녀로서는 굽히지 않을 수가 없을 것이었다. 굽히지 않겠다고 마음을 굳혔다면, 지금이야말로 그녀가 제 자리를 지키고 제 싸움을 벌일 때였다."]], [["“Do not go, Fanny; at least not quite yet,”", "“가지 마세요, 패니. 적어도 아직은요,”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Well, dear?”", "“왜 그래요, 루시?”"]], [["“I want you to stay while I tell Mark.", "“제가 마크에게 말하는 동안 패니가 있어 주었으면 해요."], ["He must not let Lord Lufton come here to-morrow.”", "마크는 러프턴 경이 내일 여기 오시게 두어서는 안 돼요.”"]], [["“Not let him!”", "“오시게 두어서는 안 된다니!”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Robarts said nothing, but he felt that his sister was rising in his esteem from minute to minute.", "로바츠 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 누이가 자기 마음속에서 시시각각 올라가고 있다고 느꼈다."]], [["“No; Mark must bid him not come.", "“안 돼요. 마크가 그분께 오지 말라고 일러야 해요."], ["He will not wish to pain me when it can do no good.", "아무 좋을 것도 없는데 저를 아프게 하고 싶어 하지는 않으실 거예요."], ["Look here, Mark;”", "여기 보세요, 마크.”"], ["and she walked over to her brother, and put both her hands upon his arm.", "그리고 그녀는 오라비에게 걸어가 두 손을 그의 팔에 얹었다."], ["“I do love Lord Lufton.", "“저는 러프턴 경을 사랑해요."], ["I had no such meaning or thought when I first knew him.", "그분을 처음 알았을 때에는 그런 뜻도 그런 생각도 없었어요."], ["But I do love him—I love him dearly;—almost as well as Fanny loves you, I suppose.", "그렇지만 저는 그분을 사랑해요 — 몹시 사랑해요. 아마 패니가 마크를 사랑하는 것만큼이나 그럴 거예요."], ["You may tell him so if you think proper—nay, you must tell him so, or he will not understand me.", "마땅하다고 생각하시면 그분께 그렇게 말씀하셔도 돼요 — 아니, 그렇게 말씀하셔야 해요. 그러지 않으면 그분이 저를 알아듣지 못하실 거예요."], ["But tell him this, as coming from me: that I will never marry him, unless his mother asks me.”", "그렇지만 이것은 제가 하는 말로 전해 주세요. 그분의 어머니가 저에게 청하지 않는 한, 저는 결코 그분과 혼인하지 않겠다고요.”"]], [["“She will not do that, I fear,”", "“그분은 그러지 않으실 것 같아 걱정이다,”"], ["said Mark, sorrowfully.", "마크가 슬프게 말했다."]], [["“No; I suppose not,”", "“예. 그러지 않으시겠지요,”"], ["said Lucy, now regaining all her courage.", "이제 온 용기를 되찾은 루시가 말했다."], ["“If I thought it probable that she should wish me to be her daughter-in-law, it would not be necessary that I should make such a stipulation.", "“그분이 저를 며느리로 삼고 싶어 하실 것 같다고 여겨진다면, 제가 그런 조건을 붙일 필요도 없을 거예요. 그분이 그것을 바라지 않으실 것이기 때문이에요."], ["It is because she will not wish it; because she would regard me as unfit to—to—to mate with her son.", "그분이 저를, 그 아들과 — 그 아들과 — 그 아들과 짝을 이루기에 걸맞지 않다고 여기실 것이기 때문이에요."], ["She would hate me, and scorn me; and then he would begin to scorn me, and perhaps would cease to love me.", "그분은 저를 미워하고 업신여기실 거예요. 그러면 그분도 저를 업신여기기 시작하고, 어쩌면 저를 사랑하기를 그치겠지요."], ["I could not bear her eye upon me, if she thought that I had injured her son.", "제가 그 아들을 해쳤다고 그분이 여기신다면 저는 저에게 얹히는 그분의 눈길을 견딜 수 없어요."], ["Mark, you will go to him now; will you not?", "마크, 지금 그분께 가시겠지요?"], ["and explain this to him;—as much of it as is necessary.", "그리고 이것을 설명해 주세요 — 필요한 만큼만요."], ["Tell him, that if his mother asks me I will—consent.", "그분의 어머니가 저에게 청하신다면 저는 — 응하겠다고 말씀해 주세요."], ["But that as I know that she never will, he is to look upon all that he has said as forgotten.", "그렇지만 그분이 결코 그러지 않으실 것을 제가 아니, 그분은 자기가 한 말을 다 잊힌 것으로 여기셔야 한다고요."], ["With me it shall be the same as though it were forgotten.”", "저에게도 그것은 잊힌 것과 같을 거라고요.”"]], [["Such was her verdict, and so confident were they both of her firmness—of her obstinacy Mark would have called it on any other occasion,—that they, neither of them, sought to make her alter it.", "그것이 그녀의 판결이었다. 그리고 두 사람은 그녀의 굳셈을 — 다른 어느 때라면 마크가 고집이라고 불렀을 그것을 — 어찌나 확신했는지, 둘 중 누구도 그녀에게 그것을 고치게 하려 들지 않았다."]], [["“You will go to him now,—this afternoon; will you not?”", "“지금 가시겠지요 — 오늘 오후에요. 그러시겠지요?”"], ["she said; and Mark promised that he would.", "그녀가 말했고, 마크는 그러겠다고 약속했다."], ["He could not but feel that he himself was greatly relieved.", "그는 자기 자신이 크게 놓였다고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Lady Lufton might probably hear that her son had been fool enough to fall in love with the parson’s sister, but under existing circumstances she could not consider herself aggrieved either by the parson or by his sister.", "레이디 러프턴은 아마도 아들이 목사의 누이와 사랑에 빠질 만큼 바보였다는 말을 들으실 것이었다. 그러나 지금의 형편에서라면 목사에게도 그 누이에게도 자기가 억울한 일을 당했다고 여기실 수는 없었다."], ["Lucy was behaving well, and Mark was proud of her.", "루시가 잘 처신하고 있었고, 마크는 누이가 자랑스러웠다."], ["Lucy was behaving with fierce spirit, and Fanny was grieving for her.", "루시가 매서운 기개로 처신하고 있었고, 패니는 그녀를 두고 마음 아파했다."]], [["“I’d rather be by myself till dinner-time,”", "“저녁때까지는 혼자 있고 싶어요,”"], ["said Lucy, as Mrs. Robarts prepared to go with her out of the room.", "로바츠 부인이 그녀와 함께 방에서 나가려 하자 루시가 말했다."], ["“Dear Fanny, don’t look unhappy; there’s nothing to make us unhappy.", "“패니, 언짢은 얼굴은 하지 마세요. 우리를 언짢게 할 일은 아무것도 없어요."], ["I told you I should want goat’s milk, and that will be all.”", "제가 염소 젖이 필요하겠다고 말했잖아요. 그게 다일 거예요.”"]], [["Robarts, after sitting for an hour with his wife, did return again to Framley Court; and, after a considerable search, found Lord Lufton returning home to a late dinner.", "로바츠는 한 시간을 아내와 함께 앉아 있은 뒤에 다시 프램리 코트로 돌아갔다. 그리고 한참을 찾아다닌 끝에 늦은 저녁을 먹으려고 집으로 돌아오는 러프턴 경을 만났다."]], [["“Unless my mother asks her,”", "“어머니가 그녀에게 청하지 않는 한이라니,”"], ["said he, when the story had been told him.", "이야기를 다 듣고 나서 그가 말했다."], ["“That is nonsense.", "“그것은 터무니없는 소리네."], ["Surely you told her that such is not the way of the world.”", "세상이 그런 식으로 돌아가지 않는다고 자네가 그녀에게 말해 주었겠지.”"]], [["Robarts endeavoured to explain to him that Lucy could not endure to think that her husband’s mother should look on her with disfavour.", "로바츠는, 남편의 어머니가 자기를 못마땅하게 바라보리라는 생각을 루시가 견딜 수 없어 한다는 것을 그에게 설명해 보려 했다."]], [["“Does she think that my mother dislikes her—her specially?”", "“내 어머니가 그녀를 — 그녀를 특히 싫어하신다고 그녀가 생각하는가?”"], ["asked Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 물었다."]], [["No; Robarts could not suppose that that was the case; but Lady Lufton might probably think that a marriage with a clergyman’s sister would be a mésalliance.", "아니다. 로바츠도 그런 것이라고는 생각할 수 없었다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 아마 목사의 누이와의 혼인이 격에 맞지 않는 혼사라고 여기실 것이었다."]], [["“That is out of the question,”", "“그것은 말이 되지 않네,”"], ["said Lord Lufton; “as she has especially wanted me to marry a clergyman’s daughter for some time past.", "러프턴 경이 말했다. “그분은 얼마 전부터 특히 내가 목사의 딸과 혼인하기를 바라 오셨으니까."], ["But, Mark, it is absurd talking about my mother.", "그런데 마크, 내 어머니 이야기를 하는 것은 부질없는 일이야."], ["A man in these days is not to marry as his mother bids him.”", "요즘 세상에 사람이 어머니가 시키는 대로 혼인하는 것은 아니지.”"]], [["Mark could only assure him, in answer to all this, that Lucy was very firm in what she was doing, that she had quite made up her mind, and that she altogether absolved Lord Lufton from any necessity to speak to his mother, if he did not think well of doing so.", "이 모든 말에 대해 마크가 그에게 확언할 수 있었던 것은, 루시가 지금 하고 있는 일에 매우 굳세다는 것, 마음을 완전히 정해 두었다는 것, 그리고 러프턴 경이 그렇게 하는 것을 좋게 여기지 않는다면 어머니에게 말해야 할 어떤 의무에서도 그를 온전히 놓아 준다는 것뿐이었다."], ["But all this was to very little purpose.", "그러나 이 모든 것은 별 소용이 없었다."]], [["“She does love me then?”", "“그러면 그녀가 나를 사랑하는 것이군?”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."]], [["“Well,”", "“글쎄,”"], ["said Mark, “I will not say whether she does or does not.", "마크가 말했다. “그런지 그렇지 않은지는 내가 말하지 않겠네."], ["I can only repeat her own message.", "나는 그 아이가 스스로 전한 말을 되풀이할 수 있을 뿐이야."], ["She cannot accept you, unless she does so at your mother’s request.”", "자네 어머니의 청을 받고서가 아니라면 그 아이는 자네를 받아들일 수 없다는 것이지.”"], ["And having said that again, he took his leave, and went back to the parsonage.", "그리고 그 말을 다시 하고서 그는 작별하고 목사관으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Poor Lucy, having finished her interview with so much dignity, having fully satisfied her brother, and declined any immediate consolation from her sister-in-law, betook herself to her own bed-room.", "딱한 루시는 그토록 큰 위엄으로 그 대면을 끝내고, 오라비를 온전히 만족시키고, 올케가 곧바로 내미는 위로를 마다한 뒤에 제 침실로 몸을 옮겼다."], ["She had to think over what she had said and done, and it was necessary that she should be alone to do so.", "자기가 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 일을 했는지 생각해 보아야 했고, 그러기 위해서는 혼자여야 했다."], ["It might be that, when she came to reconsider the matter, she would not be quite so well satisfied as was her brother.", "그 일을 다시 헤아려 볼 때에는 어쩌면 오라비만큼 흐뭇하지는 않을지도 몰랐다."], ["Her grandeur of demeanour and slow propriety of carriage lasted her till she was well into her own room.", "그 대단한 몸가짐과 느긋한 걸음의 예절바름은 제 방에 잘 들어서기까지 그녀에게 남아 있었다."], ["There are animals who, when they are ailing in any way, contrive to hide themselves, ashamed, as it were, that the weakness of their suffering should be witnessed.", "어딘가 몸이 성치 않을 때면 제 몸을 숨겨 내는 짐승들이 있으니, 제가 앓는 그 약한 꼴을 남이 보는 것을 부끄럽게 여기는 듯하다."], ["Indeed, I am not sure whether all dumb animals do not do so more or less; and in this respect Lucy was like a dumb animal.", "사실 말 못 하는 짐승이라면 다 얼마간 그러지 않는지, 나로서는 확실히 알 수 없다. 그리고 이 점에서 루시는 말 못 하는 짐승과 같았다."], ["Even in her confidences with Fanny she made a joke of her own misfortunes, and spoke of her heart ailments with self-ridicule.", "패니에게 속을 털어놓을 때에도 그녀는 제 불운을 농담으로 만들었고 제 마음의 병을 스스로 웃음거리로 삼아 말했다."], ["But now, having walked up the staircase with no hurried step, and having deliberately locked the door, she turned herself round to suffer in silence and solitude—as do the beasts and birds.", "그러나 이제, 서두르지 않는 걸음으로 계단을 올라가 일부러 문을 잠근 뒤에, 그녀는 짐승과 새들이 하듯 잠잠함과 홀로 있음 속에서 앓기 위해 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["She sat herself down on a low chair, which stood at the foot of her bed, and, throwing back her head, held her handkerchief across her eyes and forehead, holding it tight in both her hands; and then she began to think.", "그녀는 침대 발치에 놓인 낮은 의자에 앉아, 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 손수건을 두 손으로 꽉 붙든 채 눈과 이마 위에 가로 얹었다. 그러고 나서 생각하기 시작했다."], ["She began to think and also to cry, for the tears came running down from beneath the handkerchief; and low sobs were to be heard,—only that the animal had taken itself off, to suffer in solitude.", "생각하기 시작했고 또한 울기 시작했으니, 손수건 아래로 눈물이 흘러내렸다. 그리고 낮은 흐느낌이 들릴 만했다 — 다만 그 짐승이 홀로 앓으려고 몸을 감추어 버렸으니 들을 사람이 없었다."]], [["Had she not thrown from her all her chances of happiness?", "그녀는 제 행복의 모든 기회를 제게서 던져 버린 것이 아닌가?"], ["Was it possible that he should come to her yet again,—a third time?", "그가 또 한 번 — 세 번째로 — 그녀에게 올 수 있을까?"], ["No; it was not possible.", "아니다. 그럴 수 없다."], ["The very mode and pride of this, her second rejection of him, made it impossible.", "이 두 번째 거절의 방식과 그 자랑이 바로 그것을 있을 수 없는 일로 만들었다."], ["In coming to her determination, and making her avowal, she had been actuated by the knowledge that Lady Lufton would regard such a marriage with abhorrence.", "결심에 이르고 그것을 밝히면서 그녀를 움직인 것은, 레이디 러프턴이 그런 혼사를 진저리 나게 여기리라는 것을 안다는 사실이었다."], ["Lady Lufton would not and could not ask her to condescend to be her son’s bride.", "레이디 러프턴은 제 아들의 신부가 되어 달라고 몸을 낮추어 그녀에게 청하지도 않을 것이고 청할 수도 없을 것이었다."], ["Her chance of happiness, of glory, of ambition, of love, was all gone.", "행복의, 영화의, 야심의, 사랑의 기회가 다 사라져 버렸다."], ["She had sacrificed everything, not to virtue, but to pride; and she had sacrificed not only herself, but him.", "그녀는 모든 것을 바쳐 버렸으니, 미덕에 바친 것이 아니라 자랑에 바쳤다. 그리고 그녀는 자기 자신만이 아니라 그까지도 바쳐 버렸다."], ["When first he came there—when she had meditated over his first visit—she had hardly given him credit for deep love; but now—there could be no doubt that he loved her now.", "그가 처음 여기 왔을 때 — 그의 첫 방문을 두고 곰곰이 생각했을 때 — 그녀는 그에게 깊은 사랑이 있다고 좀처럼 믿어 주지 않았다. 그러나 이제는 — 그가 이제 그녀를 사랑한다는 것에는 의심이 있을 수 없었다."], ["After his season in London, his days and nights passed with all that was beautiful, he had returned there, to that little country parsonage, that he might again throw himself at her feet.", "런던의 사교철을 지내고, 아름다운 모든 것과 함께 낮과 밤을 보낸 뒤에, 그는 다시 그녀의 발 앞에 자기를 던지려고 저 작은 시골 목사관으로 돌아왔던 것이다."], ["And she—she had refused to see him, though she loved him with all her heart; she had refused to see him, because she was so vile a coward that she could not bear the sour looks of an old woman!", "그런데 그녀는 — 그녀는 온 마음으로 그를 사랑하면서도 그를 만나지 않겠다고 했다. 노부인의 시큰한 눈길을 견디지 못할 만큼 비루한 겁쟁이였기 때문에 그를 만나지 않겠다고 했다."]], [["“I will come down directly,”", "“곧 내려갈게요,”"], ["she said, when Fanny at last knocked at the door, begging to be admitted.", "마침내 패니가 문을 두드리며 들여 달라고 청했을 때 그녀가 말했다."], ["“I won’t open it, love, but I will be with you in ten minutes; I will, indeed.”", "“문은 열지 않겠어요. 그렇지만 십 분 안에 내려가 있을게요."], ["And so she was; not, perhaps, without traces of tears, discernible by the experienced eye of Mrs.", "정말이에요.” 그리고 그녀는 정말로 그렇게 했다."], ["Robarts, but yet with a smooth brow, and voice under her own command.", "로바츠 부인의 익숙한 눈에 알아볼 만한 눈물의 자취가 아주 없지는 않았을지 모르나, 그래도 이마는 매끄럽고 목소리는 제 뜻 아래 있었다."]], [["“I wonder whether she really loves him,”", "“그 아이가 정말로 그 사람을 사랑하는 걸까,”"], ["Mark said to his wife that night.", "그날 밤 마크가 아내에게 말했다."]], [["“Love him!”", "“사랑하느냐고요!” 아내가 대답했다."], ["his wife had answered; “indeed she does; and, Mark, do not be led away by the stern quiet of her demeanour.", "“정말로 사랑해요. 그리고 마크, 그 아이 몸가짐의 그 매서운 조용함에 속지 마세요."], ["To my thinking she is a girl who might almost die for love.”", "제 생각으로 그 아이는 사랑 때문에 죽을 수도 있을 아이예요.”"]], [["On the next day Lord Lufton left Framley; and started, according to his arrangements, for the Norway salmon fishing.", "이튿날 러프턴 경은 프램리를 떠났다. 그리고 미리 정해 둔 대로 노르웨이의 연어낚시를 향해 출발했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXII. THE GOAT AND COMPASSES.", "# 제32장. 「염소와 나침반」"]], [["Harold Smith had been made unhappy by that rumour of a dissolution; but the misfortune to him would be as nothing compared to the severity with which it would fall on Mr.", "해럴드 스미스는 의회 해산이라는 그 소문 때문에 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["Sowerby. Harold Smith might or might not lose his borough, but Mr.", "그러나 그에게 닥칠 불운은 사워비 씨에게 떨어질 매서움에 견주면 아무것도 아니었다."], ["Sowerby would undoubtedly lose his county; and, in losing that, he would lose everything.", "해럴드 스미스는 자기 선거구를 잃을 수도 있고 잃지 않을 수도 있었으나, 사워비 씨는 틀림없이 자기 주를 잃을 것이었고, 그것을 잃으면 모든 것을 잃는 것이었다."], ["He felt very certain now that the duke would not support him again, let who would be master of Chaldicotes; and as he reflected on these things he found it very hard to keep up his spirits.", "콜디코츠의 주인이 누가 되든 공작이 다시는 자기를 밀어 주지 않으리라는 것을 그는 이제 아주 확실히 느꼈다. 그리고 이런 일들을 곱씹으면서 그는 마음을 추스르는 일이 몹시 어렵다는 것을 알았다."]], [["Tom Towers, it seems, had known all about it, as he always does.", "톰 타워스는, 늘 그러하듯이, 그 일을 다 알고 있었던 듯하다."], ["The little remark which had dropped from him at Miss Dunstable’s, made, no doubt, after mature deliberation, and with profound political motives, was the forerunner, only by twelve hours, of a very general report that the giants were going to the country.", "던스터블 양의 집에서 그가 흘린 그 조그만 말은, 의심할 것도 없이 무르익은 숙고를 거쳐 깊은 정치적 동기와 더불어 나온 것이었는데, 거인들이 나라에 물으러 갈 것이라는 아주 널리 퍼진 소문의 겨우 열두 시간 앞선 선구였다."], ["It was manifest that the giants had not a majority in Parliament, generous as had been the promises of support disinterestedly made to them by the gods.", "신들이 사심 없이 그들에게 해 준 지지 약속이 아무리 후했다 해도, 거인들에게 의회의 다수가 없다는 것은 뚜렷했다."], ["This indeed was manifest, and therefore they were going to the country, although they had been deliberately warned by a very prominent scion of Olympus that if they did do so that disinterested support must be withdrawn.", "이것은 정말로 뚜렷했고, 그래서 그들은 나라에 물으러 가려는 것이었다. 만일 그렇게 한다면 그 사심 없는 지지를 거두는 수밖에 없다고 올림포스의 아주 이름 높은 후예 한 사람이 짐짓 경고해 두었는데도 그러했다."], ["This threat did not seem to weigh much, and by two o’clock on the day following Miss Dunstable’s party, the fiat was presumed to have gone forth.", "이 위협은 별로 무게가 있어 보이지 않았고, 던스터블 양의 모임이 있은 다음 날 두 시에는 그 명이 이미 내려진 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["The rumour had begun with Tom Towers, but by that time it had reached Buggins at the Petty Bag Office.", "소문은 톰 타워스에게서 비롯되었으나, 그때쯤에는 프티백 청의 버긴스에게까지 이르러 있었다."]], [["“It won’t make no difference to hus, sir; will it, Mr. Robarts?”", "“우리한테야 아무 차이도 없는 게 아니겠습니까요, 나리."], ["said Buggins, as he leaned respectfully against the wall near the door, in the room of the private secretary at that establishment.", "그렇지 않습니까, 로바츠 씨?” 그 관청의 개인 비서 방에서 문 가까운 벽에 공손히 몸을 기대고 서서 버긴스가 그렇게 말했다."]], [["A good deal of conversation, miscellaneous, special, and political, went on between young Robarts and Buggins in the course of the day; as was natural, seeing that they were thrown in these evil times very much upon each other.", "잡다하고 특별하고 정치적인 이야기가 그날 하루 동안 젊은 로바츠와 버긴스 사이에 꽤 많이 오갔다. 이 궂은 시절에 두 사람이 서로에게 크게 기대어 던져져 있었으니 그것은 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["The Lord Petty Bag of the present ministry was not such a one as Harold Smith.", "지금 내각의 프티백 경은 해럴드 스미스 같은 사람이 아니었다."], ["He was a giant indifferent to his private notes, and careless as to the duties even of patronage; he rarely visited the office, and as there were no other clerks in the establishment—owing to a root and branch reform carried out in the short reign of Harold Smith—to whom could young Robarts talk, if not to Buggins?", "그는 자기에게 오는 사사로운 편지에도 무심하고, 추천권에 딸린 직무조차 아무렇게나 여기는 거인이었다. 그는 청에 좀처럼 들르지 않았고, 해럴드 스미스의 짧은 치세에 뿌리와 가지를 다 뽑는 개혁이 이루어진 탓에 이 관청에는 다른 서기가 하나도 없었으니, 버긴스가 아니라면 젊은 로바츠가 누구와 이야기를 할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["“No; I suppose not,”", "“그렇겠지, 없을 테지.”"], ["said Robarts, as he completed on his blotting-paper an elaborate picture of a Turk seated on his divan.", "로바츠는 압지 위에 긴 의자에 앉은 터키 사람을 공들여 그린 그림을 마무리하면서 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“’Cause, you see, sir, we’re in the Upper ’Ouse, now;—as I always thinks we hought to be.", "“왜냐면 말입니다요, 나리, 우리는 이제 상원에 있으니까요. 마땅히 그래야 한다고 나는 늘 그렇게 생각하고들 있습니다요."], ["I don’t think it ain’t constitutional for the Petty Bag to be in the Commons, Mr.", "프티백 청이 하원에 있는 것은 국헌에 맞지 않는 게 아닌가 싶습니다요, 로바츠 씨."], ["Robarts. Hany ways, it never usen’t.”", "아무튼 예전에는 그런 적이 도무지 없었습니다요.”"]], [["“They’re changing all those sort of things now-a-days, Buggins,”", "“요즘은 그런 것들을 죄다 바꾸고 있네, 버긴스.”"], ["said Robarts, giving the final touch to the Turk’s smoke.", "로바츠는 터키 사람의 담배 연기에 마지막 손질을 하며 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“Well; I’ll tell you what it is, Mr.", "“그럼 어떤 것인지 말씀드리겠습니다요, 로바츠 씨."], ["Robarts: I think I’ll go.", "나는 나갈 생각입니다요."], ["I can’t stand all these changes.", "이런 변화들을 다 견딜 수가 없습니다요."], ["I’m turned of sixty now, and don’t want any ’stifflicates.", "이제 예순도 넘었으니 무슨 증명선지 자격증인지 하는 것도 받고 싶지 않습니다요."], ["I think I’ll take my pension and walk.", "연금이나 받고 걸어 나갈 생각입니다요."], ["The hoffice ain’t the same place at all since it come down among the Commons.”", "이 청은 하원으로 내려온 뒤로는 도무지 옛날 그 청이 아니지 않습니까요.”"], ["And then Buggins retired sighing, to console himself with a pot of porter behind a large open office ledger, set up on end on a small table in the little lobby outside the private secretary’s room.", "그러고 나서 버긴스는 한숨을 쉬며 물러나, 개인 비서 방 밖의 조그만 대기실에서 작은 탁자 위에 세워 놓은 크고 활짝 펼쳐진 관청 원부 뒤에 몸을 숨기고 포터 맥주 한 단지로 마음을 달래려 했다."], ["Buggins sighed again as he saw that the date made visible in the open book was almost as old as his own appointment; for such a book as this lasted long in the Petty Bag Office.", "펼쳐진 책에 드러나 있는 날짜가 자기가 임명된 해와 거의 같을 만큼 오래된 것을 보고 버긴스는 다시 한숨을 쉬었다. 이런 책은 프티백 청에서 오래 쓰였기 때문이다."], ["A peer of high degree had been Lord Petty Bag in those days; one whom a messenger’s heart could respect with infinite veneration, as he made his unaccustomed visits to the office with much solemnity—perhaps four times during the season.", "그 시절에는 지위 높은 귀족이 프티백 경이었다. 사환의 마음이 끝없는 경외로 우러를 수 있는 그런 사람이었으니, 그는 사교철 동안 네 번쯤, 매우 엄숙하게 이 청을 드물게 찾아왔던 것이다."], ["The Lord Petty Bag then was highly regarded by his staff, and his coming among them was talked about for some hours previously and for some days afterwards; but Harold Smith had bustled in and out like the managing clerk in a Manchester house.", "그때의 프티백 경은 아래 사람들에게 몹시 높이 받들어졌고, 그가 그들 가운데로 온다는 일은 몇 시간 전부터, 그리고 그 뒤로 며칠 동안 이야깃거리가 되었다. 그런데 해럴드 스미스는 맨체스터 상회의 지배인처럼 부산스레 드나들었다."], ["“The service is going to the dogs,”", "“관청이 갈수록 개판이 되어 가는군.”"], ["said Buggins to himself, as he put down the porter pot and looked up over the book at a gentleman who presented himself at the door.", "버긴스는 포터 단지를 내려놓고, 문에 나타난 한 신사를 책 위로 올려다보면서 혼자 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Robarts in his room?”", "“로바츠 씨가 방에 있느냐고요?”"], ["said Buggins, repeating the gentleman’s words. “Yes, Mr.", "신사의 말을 되받으며 버긴스가 말했다."], ["Sowerby; you’ll find him there; first door to the left.”", "“예, 사워비 씨. 거기 계실 겁니다요."], ["And then, remembering that the visitor was a county member—a position which Buggins regarded as next to that of a peer—he got up, and, opening the private secretary’s door, ushered in the visitor.", "왼쪽 첫 번째 문입니다요.” 그러고 나서 찾아온 이가 주 선출 의원이라는 것을, 버긴스가 귀족 다음 자리로 여기는 그 자리라는 것을 떠올리고, 그는 일어나서 개인 비서의 문을 열고 손님을 안으로 안내했다."]], [["Young Robarts and Mr. Sowerby had, of course, become acquainted in the days of Harold Smith’s reign.", "젊은 로바츠와 사워비 씨는 물론 해럴드 스미스의 치세 시절에 서로 알게 되었다."], ["During that short time the member for East Barset had on most days dropped in at the Petty Bag Office for a minute or two, finding out what the energetic cabinet minister was doing, chatting on semi-official subjects, and teaching the private secretary to laugh at his master.", "그 짧은 동안 동바셋주 의원은 거의 날마다 프티백 청에 잠깐씩 들러, 그 기운찬 각료가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 알아보고, 반쯤 공적인 일들을 놓고 이야기를 나누고, 개인 비서에게 제 상관을 비웃는 법을 가르쳤다."], ["There was nothing, therefore, in his present visit which need appear to be singular, or which required any immediate special explanation.", "그러므로 이번 방문에도 남달라 보일 만한 것, 곧바로 무슨 특별한 설명을 요할 만한 것은 하나도 없었다."], ["He sat himself down in his ordinary way, and began to speak of the subject of the day.", "그는 여느 때처럼 자리에 앉아 그날의 화제를 입에 올리기 시작했다."]], [["“We’re all to go,”", "“우리 다 나가야 하게 됐네.”"], ["said Sowerby.", "사워비가 말했다."]], [["“So I hear,”", "“그렇다고 들었어요.”"], ["said the private secretary.", "개인 비서가 말했다."], ["“It will give me no trouble, for, as the respectable Buggins says, we’re in the Upper House now.”", "“저야 아무 걱정이 없지요. 저 점잖은 버긴스가 말하듯이 우리는 이제 상원에 있으니까요.”"]], [["“What a delightful time those lucky dogs of lords do have!”", "“그 복 많은 귀족 양반들은 참 즐거운 세월을 보내지!”"], ["said Sowerby.", "사워비가 말했다."], ["“No constituents, no turning out, no fighting, no necessity for political opinions,—and, as a rule, no such opinions at all!”", "“선거구민도 없고, 쫓겨나는 일도 없고, 싸움도 없고, 정치적 견해를 가져야 할 까닭도 없고, 게다가 으레 그런 견해라는 것이 아예 없지!”"]], [["“I suppose you’re tolerably safe in East Barsetshire?”", "“동바셋주에서는 어지간히 안전하시겠지요?”"], ["said Robarts.", "로바츠가 말했다."], ["“The duke has it pretty much his own way there.”", "“거기서는 공작이 거의 제 마음대로 하니까요.”"]], [["“Yes; the duke does have it pretty much his own way.", "“그렇지, 공작이 거의 제 마음대로 하지."], ["By-the-by, where is your brother?”", "그런데 말이야, 자네 형은 어디 있나?”"]], [["“At home,”", "“집에 있어요.”"], ["said Robarts; “at least I presume so.”", "로바츠가 말했다. “적어도 그럴 거라고 짐작해요.”"]], [["“At Framley or at Barchester?", "“프램리인가, 바체스터인가?"], ["I believe he was in residence at Barchester not long since.”", "얼마 전에는 바체스터에서 거주 근무를 하고 있었던 것으로 아는데.”"]], [["“He’s at Framley now, I know.", "“지금은 프램리에 있어요, 그건 알아요."], ["I got a letter only yesterday from his wife, with a commission.", "어제 형수님한테서 심부름을 하나 맡기는 편지를 받았어요."], ["He was there, and Lord Lufton had just left.”", "형은 거기 있었고, 러프턴 경은 막 떠난 뒤였어요.”"]], [["“Yes; Lufton was down.", "“그렇지, 러프턴이 내려갔었지."], ["He started for Norway this morning.", "오늘 아침에 노르웨이로 떠났네."], ["I want to see your brother.", "나는 자네 형을 만나고 싶은데."], ["You have not heard from him yourself, have you?”", "자네는 형한테서 따로 소식을 듣지 못했나?”"]], [["“No; not lately.", "“예, 요새는 못 들었어요."], ["Mark is a bad correspondent.", "마크는 편지를 잘 쓰지 않아요."], ["He would not do at all for a private secretary.”", "개인 비서로는 도무지 맞지 않을 사람이지요.”"]], [["“At any rate, not to Harold Smith.", "“적어도 해럴드 스미스의 비서로는 안 되겠지."], ["But you are sure I should not catch him at Barchester?”", "그런데 바체스터에서는 그를 붙잡을 수 없다는 것이 확실한가?”"]], [["“Send down by telegraph, and he would meet you.”", "“전보를 내려보내시면 만나러 나올 거예요.”"]], [["“I don’t want to do that.", "“그건 하고 싶지 않네."], ["A telegraph message makes such a fuss in the country, frightening people’s wives, and setting all the horses about the place galloping.”", "전보 한 통이 시골에서는 얼마나 큰 법석을 일으키는지, 사람들 아내를 겁주고 그 근방의 말을 다 달리게 만들어 놓지.”"]], [["“What is it about?”", "“무슨 일인데요?”"]], [["“Nothing of any great consequence.", "“대단한 일은 아무것도 아니네."], ["I didn’t know whether he might have told you.", "그가 자네에게 말했을지 몰라서 그랬지."], ["I’ll write down by to-night’s post, and then he can meet me at Barchester to-morrow.", "오늘 밤 우편으로 편지를 내려보내면 내일 바체스터에서 나를 만날 수 있겠지."], ["Or do you write.", "아니면 자네가 쓰게."], ["There’s nothing I hate so much as letter-writing;—just tell him that I called, and that I shall be much obliged if he can meet me at the Dragon of Wantly—say at two to-morrow.", "나는 편지 쓰는 일만큼 싫은 것이 없으니. 내가 찾아왔었다고, 그리고 원틀리의 용에서 나를 만나 주면 대단히 고맙겠다고만 전해 주게."], ["I will go down by the express.”", "내일 두 시라고 하지. 나는 급행으로 내려가겠네.”"]], [["Mark Robarts, in talking over this coming money trouble with Sowerby, had once mentioned that if it were necessary to take up the bill for a short time he might be able to borrow the money from his brother.", "마크 로바츠는 다가오는 이 돈 걱정을 사워비와 놓고 이야기하면서, 짧은 동안이라도 그 어음을 떠맡아야 할 필요가 있다면 아우에게서 그 돈을 빌릴 수 있을지도 모른다고 한 번 말한 적이 있었다."], ["So much of the father’s legacy still remained in the hands of the private secretary as would enable him to produce the amount of the latter bill, and there could be no doubt that he would lend it if asked.", "아버지의 유산 가운데 나중 어음의 액수를 낼 수 있을 만큼은 아직 그 개인 비서의 손에 남아 있었고, 부탁을 받으면 그가 빌려 주리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["Mr. Sowerby’s visit to the Petty Bag Office had been caused by a desire to learn whether any such request had been made,—and also by a half-formed resolution to make the request himself if he should find that the clergyman had not done so.", "사워비 씨가 프티백 청을 찾은 것은 그런 부탁이 있었는지를 알고 싶은 마음에서였고, 또 그 성직자가 그렇게 하지 않았다는 것을 알게 되면 그 부탁을 자기가 직접 하겠다는, 절반쯤 굳은 결심에서였다."], ["It seemed to him to be a pity that such a sum should be lying about, as it were, within reach, and that he should not stoop to put his hands upon it.", "그만한 돈이 손 닿는 데에, 말하자면 아무렇게나 놓여 있는데 자기가 몸을 굽혀 그것에 손을 얹지 않는다는 것은 그에게 아까운 일로 보였다."], ["Such abstinence would be so contrary to all the practice of his life that it was as difficult to him as it is for a sportsman to let pass a cock-pheasant.", "그렇게 참는다는 것은 그가 평생 해 온 모든 버릇에 어긋나는 일이어서, 사냥꾼이 수꿩 한 마리를 그냥 지나가게 두는 것만큼이나 그에게 어려웠다."], ["But yet something like remorse touched his heart as he sat there balancing himself on his chair in the private secretary’s room, and looking at the young man’s open face.", "그러나 그가 개인 비서의 방에서 의자에 앉아 몸을 흔들며 그 젊은이의 열린 얼굴을 바라보고 있는 동안, 후회 같은 무엇이 그의 마음에 닿았다."]], [["“Yes; I’ll write to him,”", "“예, 제가 편지를 쓰겠어요.”"], ["said John Robarts; “but he hasn’t said anything to me about anything particular.”", "존 로바츠가 말했다. “하지만 형은 저에게 무슨 특별한 이야기를 한 적이 없어요.”"]], [["“Hasn’t he?", "“안 했나?"], ["It does not much signify.", "그건 별로 대수롭지 않네."], ["I only mentioned it because I thought I understood him to say that he would.”", "그가 그러겠다고 말한 것으로 내가 알아들었다고 생각해서 그것만 말해 본 것이야.”"], ["And then Mr. Sowerby went on swinging himself.", "그러고 나서 사워비 씨는 계속 몸을 흔들었다."], ["How was it that he felt so averse to mention that little sum of £500 to a young man like John Robarts, a fellow without wife or children or calls on him of any sort, who would not even be injured by the loss of the money, seeing that he had an ample salary on which to live?", "존 로바츠 같은 젊은이에게, 아내도 자식도 어떤 종류의 짐도 없는 사내에게, 넉넉한 봉급으로 살아가고 있으니 그 돈을 잃는다 해도 상하는 데조차 없을 사람에게, 500파운드라는 그 조그만 돈을 입에 올리기가 어찌하여 그렇게 싫었을까?"], ["He wondered at his own weakness.", "그는 자기 자신의 무름을 이상하게 여겼다."], ["The want of the money was urgent on him in the extreme.", "그 돈이 없어 급하기가 그에게는 극에 달해 있었다."], ["He had reasons for supposing that Mark would find it very difficult to renew the bills, but he, Sowerby, could stop their presentation if he could get this money at once into his own hands.", "마크가 그 어음들을 갱신하기가 몹시 어려울 것이라고 짐작할 만한 까닭이 그에게는 있었으나, 그, 곧 사워비는 이 돈을 당장 자기 손에 넣을 수만 있다면 그 어음이 제시되는 것을 막을 수 있었다."]], [["“Can I do anything for you?”", "“제가 뭐 해 드릴 일이 있나요?”"], ["said the innocent lamb, offering his throat to the butcher.", "순진한 어린 양이 백정에게 제 목을 내밀며 그렇게 말했다."]], [["But some unwonted feeling numbed the butcher’s fingers, and blunted his knife.", "그러나 어떤 낯선 감정이 그 백정의 손가락을 저리게 하고 그 칼을 무디게 했다."], ["He sat still for half a minute after the question, and then jumping from his seat, declined the offer.", "그는 그 물음이 있고 나서 반 분쯤 가만히 앉아 있었고, 그러고는 자리에서 벌떡 일어나 그 제안을 물렸다."], ["“No, no; nothing, thank you.", "“아니, 아니. 아무것도 없네, 고맙네."], ["Only write to Mark, and say that I shall be there to-morrow,”", "마크에게 편지만 써서 내가 내일 거기 가겠다고 전해 주게.”"], ["and then, taking his hat, he hurried out of the office.", "그러고 나서 그는 모자를 집어 들고 서둘러 청을 나섰다."], ["“What an ass I am,”", "“내가 참 얼간이로군.”"], ["he said to himself as he went: “as if it were of any use now to be particular!”", "그는 가면서 혼자 이렇게 말했다. “이제 와서 까다롭게 구는 것이 무슨 쓸데가 있다고!”"]], [["He then got into a cab and had himself driven half way up Portman Street towards the New Road, and walking from thence a few hundred yards down a cross-street he came to a public-house.", "그러고 나서 그는 삯마차를 타고 뉴 로드 쪽으로 포트먼 스트리트를 절반쯤 올라간 곳까지 실려 갔고, 거기서 몇백 야드를 걸어 가로지르는 골목을 내려가 어느 술집에 이르렀다."], ["It was called the “Goat and Compasses,”—a very meaningless name, one would say; but the house boasted of being a place of public entertainment very long established on that site, having been a tavern out in the country in the days of Cromwell.", "그 집은 「염소와 나침반」이라고 불렸는데, 누구라도 아주 뜻 없는 이름이라고 할 것이다. 그러나 이 집은 크롬웰 시절에 시골의 여관이었을 만큼 그 자리에 아주 오래전부터 자리 잡은 공중 유흥소라는 것을 자랑으로 삼았다."], ["At that time the pious landlord, putting up a pious legend for the benefit of his pious customers, had declared that—“God encompasseth us.” The “Goat and Compasses”", "그 무렵에 경건한 주인은 경건한 손님들을 위하여 경건한 문구를 걸어, “하느님이 우리를 에워싸고 계시니라”라고 밝혀 놓았던 것이다."], ["in these days does quite as well; and, considering the present character of the house, was perhaps less unsuitable than the old legend.", "요즘에는 「염소와 나침반」도 그만큼 잘 들어맞는다. 그리고 이 집의 지금 됨됨이를 헤아리면, 그것이 옛 문구보다 어쩌면 덜 어울리지 않는 것이었다."]], [["“Is Mr. Austen here?”", "“오스틴 씨 여기 있소?”"], ["asked Mr. Sowerby of the man at the bar.", "사워비 씨가 술청의 사내에게 물었다."]], [["“Which on ’em? Not Mr.", "“둘 중에 어느 분 말입니까요?"], ["John; he ain’t here. Mr.", "존 씨는 아니겠지요."], ["Tom is in—the little room on the left-hand side.”", "그분은 여기 없습니다요. 톰 씨는 안에 있습니다요."], ["The man whom Mr.", "왼편의 조그만 방입니다요.”"], ["Sowerby would have preferred to see was the elder brother, John; but as he was not to be found, he did go into the little room. In that room he found—Mr.", "사워비 씨가 만나고 싶었던 사람은 형인 존이었으나, 그를 찾을 수 없었으므로 그는 그 조그만 방으로 들어갔다."], ["Austen, Junior, according to one arrangement of nomenclature, and Mr. Tom Tozer according to another.", "그 방에서 그가 만난 사람은, 이름 붙이는 한 가지 방식에 따르면 작은 오스틴 씨였고, 또 다른 방식에 따르면 톰 토저 씨였다."], ["To gentlemen of the legal profession he generally chose to introduce himself as belonging to the respectable family of the Austens; but among his intimates he had always been—Tozer.", "법률 쪽 일을 하는 신사들에게는 그가 대개 점잖은 오스틴 집안의 사람이라고 자기를 소개하기를 골랐으나, 가까운 사람들 사이에서 그는 늘 토저였다."]], [["Mr. Sowerby, though he was intimate with the family, did not love the Tozers; but he especially hated Tom Tozer.", "사워비 씨는 그 집안과 가까웠으나 토저네를 좋아하지는 않았다."], ["Tom Tozer was a bull-necked, beetle-browed fellow, the expression of whose face was eloquent with acknowledged roguery.", "그중에서도 톰 토저를 특히 미워했다. 톰 토저는 목이 황소처럼 굵고 눈썹이 툭 튀어나온 사내였는데, 그 얼굴의 표정은 스스로 인정한 악당 기질을 웅변으로 말하고 있었다."], ["“I am a rogue,”", "“나는 악당이오,”"], ["it seemed to say.", "그 얼굴은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다."], ["“I know it; all the world knows it: but you’re another.", "“나도 그것을 알고 온 세상이 다 아오. 그렇지만 당신도 한 패요."], ["All the world don’t know that, but I do.", "그건 온 세상은 모르지만 나는 아오."], ["Men are all rogues, pretty nigh.", "사람이란 거의 다 악당이오."], ["Some are soft rogues, and some are ’cute rogues.", "어떤 놈은 무른 악당이고 어떤 놈은 약삭빠른 악당이오."], ["I am a ’cute one; so mind your eye.”", "나는 약삭빠른 쪽이니, 그러니 눈 똑바로 뜨시오.”"], ["It was with such words that Tom Tozer’s face spoke out; and though a thorough liar in his heart, he was not a liar in his face.", "톰 토저의 얼굴은 그런 말로 소리 내어 말하고 있었다. 그리고 마음속으로는 철저한 거짓말쟁이였으나 얼굴로는 거짓말쟁이가 아니었다."]], [["“Well, Tozer,” said Mr.", "“여, 토저.”"], ["Sowerby, absolutely shaking hands with the dirty miscreant, “I wanted to see your brother.”", "사워비 씨가 그 더러운 악한과 정말로 악수까지 하며 말했다. “자네 형을 만나고 싶었네.”"]], [["“John ain’t here, and ain’t like; but it’s all as one.”", "“존은 없고, 올 것 같지도 않소. 하지만 그거야 다 한가지요.”"]], [["“Yes, yes; I suppose it is.", "“그렇지, 그렇지. 그럴 테지."], ["I know you two hunt in couples.”", "자네들 둘이 짝을 지어 사냥한다는 것은 알고 있네.”"]], [["“I don’t know what you mean about hunting, Mr.", "“사냥이라니 무슨 말씀인지 나는 모르겠소, 사워비 씨."], ["Sowerby. You gents ’as all the hunting, and we poor folk ’as all the work.", "사냥이란 것은 양반님들이 다 차지하고, 우리 같은 가난뱅이들에게는 일만 다 있는 거요."], ["I hope you’re going to make up this trifle of money we’re out of so long.”", "우리가 이렇게 오래 밑지고 있는 이 푼돈이나 좀 채워 주시려는 것이면 좋겠소만.”"]], [["“It’s about that I’ve called.", "“그 일로 찾아온 것이네."], ["I don’t know what you call long, Tozer; but the last bill was only dated in February.”", "자네가 무엇을 오래라고 부르는지는 모르겠네만, 토저, 마지막 어음은 겨우 이월 날짜로 된 것일세.”"]], [["“It’s overdue; ain’t it?”", "“만기가 지났잖소, 안 그렇소?”"]], [["“Oh, yes; it’s overdue.", "“아, 그렇지. 만기는 지났네."], ["There’s no doubt about that.”", "그건 의심할 것이 없지.”"]], [["“Well; when a bit of paper is come round, the next thing is to take it up.", "“그럼요, 종이쪽 한 장이 돌아왔으면 그다음에 할 일은 그걸 떠맡는 거요."], ["Them’s my ideas.", "그게 내 생각들이오."], ["And to tell you the truth, Mr. Sowerby, we don’t think as ’ow you’ve been treating us just on the square lately.", "그리고 사실을 말하자면, 사워비 씨, 우리는 요새 당신이 우리를 그리 떳떳하게 대해 주었다고 생각하지 않소."], ["In that matter of Lord Lufton’s you was down on us uncommon.”", "그 러프턴 경 일에서는 당신이 우리를 유별나게 몰아붙였소.”"]], [["“You know I couldn’t help myself.”", "“나도 어쩔 수가 없었다는 것을 자네도 알잖나.”"]], [["“Well; and we can’t help ourselves now.", "“그럼요, 그리고 우리도 지금 어쩔 수가 없소."], ["That’s where it is, Mr. Sowerby.", "바로 그게 문제란 말이오, 사워비 씨."], ["Lord love you; we know what’s what, we do.", "허, 딱하시기도 하오. 우리도 뭐가 뭔지는 알아요, 우리도 안다고요."], ["And so, the fact is we’re uncommon low as to the ready just at present, and we must have them few hundred pounds.", "그래서 사실은 지금 당장 쥘 돈이 유별나게 말라 있는 형편이고, 우리는 그 몇백 파운드를 받아야 하오."], ["We must have them at once, or we must sell up that clerical gent.", "당장 받아야 하고, 그러지 못하면 그 성직자 양반의 살림을 다 팔아 넘겨야 하오."], ["I’m dashed if it ain’t as hard to get money from a parson as it is to take a bone from a dog.", "목사에게서 돈을 받아 내는 일이 개에게서 뼈다귀를 빼앗는 것만큼 어렵지 않다면 내가 죽어도 좋소."], ["’E’s ’ad ’is account, no doubt, and why don’t ’e pay?”", "그 양반도 제 몫은 다 챙겼을 텐데, 왜 안 갚는 거요?”"]], [["Mr. Sowerby had called with the intention of explaining that he was about to proceed to Barchester on the following day with the express view of “making arrangements”", "사워비 씨는 이 어음에 관하여 “손을 써 두겠다”는 뚜렷한 뜻으로 이튿날 바체스터로 내려가려 한다는 것을 밝힐 작정으로 찾아왔던 것이다."], ["about this bill; and had he seen John Tozer, John would have been compelled to accord to him some little extension of time.", "그리고 그가 존 토저를 만났더라면 존은 그에게 얼마쯤 기한을 늘려 주지 않을 수 없었을 것이다."], ["Both Tom and John knew this; and, therefore, John—the soft-hearted one—kept out of the way.", "톰도 존도 이것을 알고 있었고, 그래서 마음이 무른 쪽인 존이 자리를 피해 있었던 것이다. 톰이 물러질 위험은 없었다."], ["There was no danger that Tom would be weak; and, after some half-hour of parley, he was again left by Mr. Sowerby, without having evinced any symptom of weakness.", "그리고 반 시간쯤 담판을 벌인 뒤에 사워비 씨는 그를 다시 남겨 두고 떠났으니, 그는 무른 기색이라고는 아무 조짐도 내보이지 않은 채였다."]], [["“It’s the dibs as we want, Mr. Sowerby; that’s all,”", "“우리가 바라는 건 현찰이오, 사워비 씨."], ["were the last words which he spoke as the member of Parliament left the room.", "그게 다요.” 그 하원 의원이 방을 나설 때 그가 한 마지막 말은 그러했다."]], [["Mr. Sowerby then got into another cab, and had himself driven to his sister’s house.", "그러고 나서 사워비 씨는 또 다른 삯마차를 타고 누이동생의 집으로 실려 갔다."], ["It is a remarkable thing with reference to men who are distressed for money—distressed as was now the case with Mr. Sowerby—that they never seem at a loss for small sums, or deny themselves those luxuries which small sums purchase.", "돈에 쪼들리는 사람들, 곧 지금 사워비 씨가 그러한 것처럼 쪼들리는 사람들에 관하여 놀라운 것이 하나 있으니, 그들은 푼돈에 궁해 보이는 일이 도무지 없고 푼돈으로 살 수 있는 그 사치를 스스로 마다하는 일도 없다는 것이다."], ["Cabs, dinners, wine, theatres, and new gloves are always at the command of men who are drowned in pecuniary embarrassments, whereas those who don’t owe a shilling are so frequently obliged to go without them!", "삯마차, 만찬, 포도주, 연극, 새 장갑은 돈 문제에 빠져 허우적거리는 사람들이 언제라도 부릴 수 있는 것인데, 한 실링도 빚지지 않은 사람들은 그것들 없이 지내야 할 때가 어찌 그리 잦은가!"], ["It would seem that there is no gratification so costly as that of keeping out of debt.", "빚을 지지 않고 사는 것만큼 값비싼 즐거움은 없는 듯하다."], ["But then it is only fair that, if a man has a hobby, he should pay for it.", "그러나 사람이 취미를 하나 가졌다면 그 값은 치러야 옳으니, 그것 또한 마땅한 일이다."]], [["Any one else would have saved his shilling, as Mrs. Harold Smith’s house was only just across Oxford Street, in the neighbourhood of Hanover Square; but Mr.", "해럴드 스미스 부인의 집은 옥스퍼드 스트리트만 건너면 되는 하노버 스퀘어 근처였으니 다른 사람이라면 그 한 실링을 아꼈을 것이다."], ["Sowerby never thought of this.", "그러나 사워비 씨는 이런 것을 도무지 생각하지 않았다."], ["He had never saved a shilling in his life, and it did not occur to him to begin now.", "그는 평생 한 실링도 아껴 본 적이 없었고, 이제 와서 그러기 시작할 생각도 떠오르지 않았다."], ["He had sent word to her to remain at home for him, and he now found her waiting.", "그는 자기를 위해 집에 있어 달라고 미리 사람을 보내 두었고, 이제 와 보니 그녀가 기다리고 있었다."]], [["“Harriet,”", "“해리엇,”"], ["said he, throwing himself back into an easy chair, “the game is pretty well up at last.”", "그는 안락의자에 몸을 뒤로 던지며 말했다. “판이 이제 거의 끝났네.”"]], [["“Nonsense,” said she.", "“터무니없는 소리.”"], ["“The game is not up at all if you have the spirit to carry it on.”", "그녀가 말했다. “끝까지 밀고 나갈 기운만 있으면 판은 조금도 끝나지 않았어요.”"]], [["“I can only say that I got a formal notice this morning from the duke’s lawyer, saying that he meant to foreclose at once;—not from Fothergill, but from those people in South Audley Street.”", "“내가 말할 수 있는 것은, 오늘 아침에 공작의 변호사로부터 곧바로 저당권을 실행하겠다는 정식 통지를 받았다는 것뿐이네. 포더길이 아니라 사우스 오들리 스트리트의 그 사람들한테서 온 것이야.”"]], [["“You expected that,”", "“예상하고 있었잖아요.”"], ["said his sister.", "누이동생이 말했다."]], [["“I don’t see how that makes it any better; besides, I am not quite sure that I did expect it; at any rate I did not feel certain.", "“그렇다고 그것이 어떻게 나아지는지는 모르겠네. 게다가 내가 정말로 예상했는지도 그리 확실하지 않아."], ["There is no doubt now.”", "적어도 확실하다고 느끼지는 않았지. 이제는 의심할 것이 없네.”"]], [["“It is better that there should be no doubt.", "“의심할 것이 없는 편이 낫지요."], ["It is much better that you should know on what ground you have to stand.”", "오라버니가 어떤 땅에 서야 하는지를 아는 편이 훨씬 나아요.”"]], [["“I shall soon have no ground to stand on, none at least of my own,—not an acre,”", "“내가 서 있을 땅이 곧 없어질 걸세. 적어도 내 것으로는 하나도, 한 에이커도 없을 거야.”"], ["said the unhappy man, with great bitterness in his tone.", "그 불행한 사내가 목소리에 큰 쓰라림을 담아 말했다."]], [["“You can’t in reality be poorer now than you were last year.", "“실제로 오라버니가 지난해보다 지금 더 가난해질 수는 없어요."], ["You have not spent anything to speak of.", "이야기할 만큼 쓴 것도 없잖아요."], ["There can be no doubt that Chaldicotes will be ample to pay all you owe the duke.”", "콜디코츠면 공작에게 진 빚을 다 갚기에 넉넉하다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없어요.”"]], [["“It’s as much as it will; and what am I to do then?", "“겨우 그만큼일 걸세. 그러고 나면 나는 어찌하란 말인가?"], ["I almost think more of the seat than I do of Chaldicotes.”", "나는 콜디코츠보다 그 의석이 더 아깝다는 생각까지 드네.”"]], [["“You know what I advise,”", "“내가 무엇을 권하는지 알잖아요.”"], ["said Mrs. Smith.", "스미스 부인이 말했다."], ["“Ask Miss Dunstable to advance the money on the same security which the duke holds.", "“던스터블 양에게, 공작이 쥐고 있는 것과 같은 담보로 그 돈을 대어 달라고 부탁하세요."], ["She will be as safe then as he is now.", "그러면 그이도 지금 공작이 그러한 만큼 안전할 거예요."], ["And if you can arrange that, stand for the county against him; perhaps you may be beaten.”", "그리고 그 일을 이룰 수 있으면 공작에 맞서 주 선거에 나서세요. 어쩌면 지겠지요.”"]], [["“I shouldn’t have a chance.”", "“나에게는 가망이 없을 걸세.”"]], [["“But it would show that you are not a creature in the duke’s hands.", "“하지만 오라버니가 공작의 손아귀에 든 물건이 아니라는 것은 보여 줄 수 있어요."], ["That’s my advice,” said Mrs.", "그게 내가 권하는 것이에요.”"], ["Smith, with much spirit; “and if you wish, I’ll broach it to Miss Dunstable, and ask her to get her lawyer to look into it.”", "스미스 부인이 몹시 기운차게 말했다. “그리고 원하신다면 내가 던스터블 양에게 말을 꺼내서, 그이의 변호사에게 그것을 살펴보게 해 달라고 부탁하겠어요.”"]], [["“If I had done this before I had run my head into that other absurdity!”", "“그 다른 어리석은 짓에 머리를 들이밀기 전에 이것을 해 두었더라면!”"]], [["“Don’t fret yourself about that; she will lose nothing by such an investment, and therefore you are not asking any favour of her.", "“그 일로 애태우지 말아요. 그이는 그런 투자로 잃을 것이 없으니, 오라버니가 그이에게 무슨 은혜를 청하는 것이 아니에요."], ["Besides, did she not make the offer?", "게다가 그이가 먼저 그러겠다고 하지 않았어요?"], ["and she is just the woman to do this for you now, because she refused to do that other thing for you yesterday.", "그리고 그이는 어제 그 다른 일을 오라버니에게 해 주기를 마다했으니, 지금 이 일은 오라버니에게 해 줄 바로 그런 여자예요. 나다니엘, 오라버니는 대개의 일은 다 알아요."], ["You understand most things, Nathaniel; but I am not sure that you understand women; not, at any rate, such a woman as her.”", "하지만 여자를 아는지는 나도 잘 모르겠어요. 적어도 그이 같은 여자라면 더욱 그렇지요.”"]], [["It went against the grain with Mr. Sowerby, this seeking of pecuniary assistance from the very woman whose hand he had attempted to gain about a fortnight since; but he allowed his sister to prevail.", "보름쯤 전에 그 손을 얻으려 했던 바로 그 여인에게 돈의 도움을 구한다는 것은 사워비 씨에게 결을 거스르는 일이었다."], ["What could any man do in such straits that would not go against the grain?", "그러나 그는 누이동생이 이기게 두었다."], ["At the present moment he felt in his mind an infinite hatred against the duke, Mr.", "그만한 궁지에 몰린 사람이 결을 거스르지 않고 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Fothergill, Gumption and Gagebee, and all the tribes of Gatherum Castle and South Audley Street; they wanted to rob him of that which had belonged to the Sowerbys before the name of Omnium had been heard of in the county, or in England!", "지금 이 순간 그는 마음속으로 공작과 포더길 씨와 검션 게이지비 상회와 개서럼 성과 사우스 오들리 스트리트의 온 족속에게 끝없는 미움을 느꼈다. 그들은 옴니엄이라는 이름이 이 주에서, 아니 영국에서 들려 본 적도 없었을 때부터 사워비 집안의 것이었던 것을 그에게서 빼앗으려 하고 있었다!"], ["The great leviathan of the deep was anxious to swallow him up as a prey!", "깊은 바다의 큰 레비아탄이 그를 먹이로 삼켜 버리려고 애가 타 있었다!"], ["He was to be swallowed up, and made away with, and put out of sight, without a pang of remorse!", "그는 삼켜지고, 없어지고, 눈앞에서 치워질 것이었다. 후회의 아픔 한 점 없이!"], ["Any measure which could now present itself as the means of staving off so evil a day would be acceptable; and therefore he gave his sister the commission of making this second proposal to Miss Dunstable.", "그토록 나쁜 날을 미룰 방편으로 지금 나서 줄 수 있는 수단이라면 무엇이든 반가울 것이었다. 그래서 그는 던스터블 양에게 이 두 번째 제안을 하는 소임을 누이동생에게 맡겼다."], ["In cursing the duke—for he did curse the duke lustily—it hardly occurred to him to think that, after all, the duke only asked for his own.", "공작을 저주하면서 — 그는 정말로 공작을 실컷 저주했으니 — 결국 공작은 제 것을 달라고 한 것뿐이라는 생각은 그에게 좀처럼 떠오르지 않았다."]], [["As for Mrs. Harold Smith, whatever may be the view taken of her general character as a wife and a member of society, it must be admitted that as a sister she had virtues.", "해럴드 스미스 부인을 두고 말한다면, 아내로서 그리고 사회의 한 사람으로서 그 됨됨이 전체를 어떻게 보든, 누이로서는 그녀에게 미덕이 있었다는 것만은 인정해야 한다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIII. CONSOLATION.", "# 제33장. 위로"]], [["On the next day, at two o’clock punctually, Mark Robarts was at the “Dragon of Wantly,”", "이튿날 두 시 정각에 마크 로바츠는 원틀리의 용에 와 있었다."], ["walking up and down the very room in which the party had breakfasted after Harold Smith’s lecture, and waiting for the arrival of Mr.", "해럴드 스미스의 강연이 끝난 뒤 일행이 아침을 먹었던 바로 그 방을 오르내리며 사워비 씨가 오기를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Sowerby. He had been very well able to divine what was the business on which his friend wished to see him, and he had been rather glad than otherwise to receive the summons.", "벗이 무슨 일로 자기를 보려 하는지는 그도 넉넉히 짚어 낼 수 있었고, 그 부름을 받은 것이 싫기보다는 오히려 반가웠다."], ["Judging of his friend’s character by what he had hitherto seen, he thought that Mr. Sowerby would have kept out of the way, unless he had it in his power to make some provision for these terrible bills.", "여태 보아 온 것으로 벗의 사람됨을 헤아리자면, 사워비 씨가 그 무서운 어음들에 대해 무언가 마련을 해 놓을 힘을 손에 쥐지 못한 한에는 몸을 피해 있었으리라 여겨졌기 때문이다."], ["So he walked up and down the dingy room, impatient for the expected arrival, and thought himself wickedly ill-used in that Mr. Sowerby was not there when the clock struck a quarter to three.", "그래서 그는 그 침침한 방을 오르내리며 기다리는 사람이 오기를 조바심 냈고, 시계가 세 시 십오 분 전을 치는데도 사워비 씨가 거기 없다는 것만으로 자기가 지독하게 홀대받고 있다고 생각했다."], ["But when the clock struck three, Mr. Sowerby was there, and Mark Robarts’s hopes were nearly at an end.", "그러나 시계가 세 시를 칠 때 사워비 씨는 거기 있었고, 마크 로바츠의 희망은 거의 끝나 있었다."]], [["“Do you mean that they will demand nine hundred pounds?”", "“그 사람들이 구백 파운드를 요구하겠다는 말입니까?”"], ["said Robarts, standing up and glaring angrily at the member of Parliament.", "로바츠가 일어서서 그 하원 의원을 노엽게 노려보며 말했다."]], [["“I fear that they will,”", "“그럴 것 같아 걱정입니다,”"], ["said Sowerby.", "사워비가 말했다."], ["“I think it is best to tell you the worst, in order that we may see what can be done.”", "“무엇을 할 수 있을지 우리가 함께 살펴보려면 가장 나쁜 것을 당신에게 말해 두는 편이 좋겠다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“I can do nothing, and will do nothing,”", "“나는 아무것도 할 수 없고, 아무것도 하지 않겠습니다,”"], ["said Robarts.", "로바츠가 말했다."], ["“They may do what they choose—what the law allows them.”", "“그들이야 하고 싶은 대로 하라지요 — 법이 그들에게 허락하는 것을요.”"]], [["And then he thought of Fanny and his nursery, and Lucy refusing in her pride Lord Lufton’s offer, and he turned away his face that the hard man of the world before him might not see the tear gathering in his eye.", "그러고서 그는 패니와 아이 방을, 그리고 자존심 때문에 러프턴 경의 청혼을 거절한 루시를 생각했고, 눈에 고이는 눈물을 앞에 앉은 저 세상 물정에 밝은 매정한 사나이가 보지 못하도록 얼굴을 돌렸다."]], [["“But, Mark, my dear fellow—”", "“하지만, 마크, 여보게 —”"], ["said Sowerby, trying to have recourse to the power of his cajoling voice.", "사워비는 제 구슬리는 목소리의 힘에 다시 기대 보려 하면서 말했다."]], [["Robarts, however, would not listen.", "그러나 로바츠는 들으려 하지 않았다."]], [["“Mr. Sowerby,”", "“사워비 씨,”"], ["said he, with an attempt at calmness which betrayed itself at every syllable, “it seems to me that you have robbed me.", "그는 한 음절 한 음절마다 스스로 들통이 나는 침착함을 꾸며 내며 말했다. “당신이 나를 털어 갔다는 생각이 듭니다."], ["That I have been a fool, and worse than a fool, I know well; but—but—but I thought that your position in the world would guarantee me from such treatment as this.”", "내가 바보였다는 것, 바보보다 더한 것이었다는 것은 나도 잘 알아요. 그러나 — 그러나 — 그러나 세상에서 당신이 차지한 자리가 이런 대접만은 나에게 하지 않도록 보장해 줄 것이라고 나는 생각했습니다.”"]], [["Mr. Sowerby was by no means without feeling, and the words which he now heard cut him very deeply—the more so because it was impossible that he should answer them with an attempt at indignation.", "사워비 씨는 결코 감정이 없는 사람이 아니었고, 지금 들은 그 말은 그를 몹시 깊이 베었다 — 분개하는 척하며 그 말에 맞설 수 있는 길이 도무지 없었기에 더욱 그러했다."], ["He had robbed his friend, and, with all his wit, knew no words at the present moment sufficiently witty to make it seem that he had not done so.", "그는 제 벗을 털었고, 그 모든 재치를 가지고도 자기가 그러지 않았던 것처럼 보이게 만들 만큼 재치 있는 말을 이 순간에는 하나도 알지 못했다."]], [["“Robarts,”", "“로바츠,” 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “you may say what you like to me now; I shall not resent it.”", "“지금은 나에게 무슨 말을 해도 좋아요. 나는 그것을 원망하지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["“Who would care for your resentment?”", "“누가 당신의 원망을 마음에 두겠습니까?”"], ["said the clergyman, turning on him with ferocity.", "성직자가 그에게 사납게 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“The resentment of a gentleman is terrible to a gentleman; and the resentment of one just man is terrible to another.", "“신사의 원망은 신사에게 무서운 것이고, 의로운 사람 하나의 원망은 또 다른 의로운 사람에게 무서운 것입니다. 당신의 원망이라니!”"], ["Your resentment!”—and then he walked twice the length of the room, leaving Sowerby dumb in his seat.", "— 그러고서 그는 방의 길이를 두 번 오르내렸고, 사워비는 제 자리에서 말문이 막힌 채 남았다."], ["“I wonder whether you ever thought of my wife and children when you were plotting this ruin for me!”", "“나를 이렇게 망쳐 놓을 궁리를 하던 때에 당신이 내 아내와 아이들을 한 번이라도 생각해 본 적이 있는지 궁금하군요!”"], ["And then again he walked the room.", "그러고서 그는 다시 방을 오르내렸다."]], [["“I suppose you will be calm enough presently to speak of this with some attempt to make a settlement?”", "“이 일을 어떻게든 타결해 볼 생각으로 이야기할 만큼 얼마 뒤에는 마음이 가라앉겠지요?”"]], [["“No; I will make no such attempt.", "“아니요, 나는 그런 시도를 하지 않겠습니다."], ["These friends of yours, you tell me, have a claim on me for nine hundred pounds, of which they demand immediate payment.", "당신 말로는 당신의 그 벗들이 나에게 구백 파운드의 청구권을 가지고 있고 그것을 즉시 갚으라고 요구한다지요."], ["You shall be asked in a court of law how much of that money I have handled.", "그 돈 가운데 얼마를 내가 손에 쥐어 보았는지 당신은 법정에서 물음을 받게 될 겁니다."], ["You know that I have never touched—have never wanted to touch—one shilling.", "나는 한 실링도 손대 본 적이 없다는 것을, 손대고 싶어 한 적조차 없다는 것을 당신은 알고 있어요."], ["I will make no attempt at any settlement.", "나는 어떤 타결도 시도하지 않겠습니다."], ["My person is here, and there is my house.", "내 몸은 여기 있고, 저기 내 집이 있습니다."], ["Let them do their worst.”", "그들이 할 수 있는 최악을 하라지요.”"]], [["“But, Mark—”", "“하지만, 마크 —”"]], [["“Call me by my name, sir, and drop that affectation of regard.", "“내 성으로 부르시고, 그 다정한 척은 그만두시지요, 선생."], ["What an ass I have been to be so cozened by a sharper!”", "야바위꾼에게 그렇게 속아 넘어갔으니 내가 얼마나 얼간이였던가!”"]], [["Sowerby had by no means expected this.", "사워비는 이런 것을 조금도 예상하지 못했다."], ["He had always known that Robarts possessed what he, Sowerby, would have called the spirit of a gentleman.", "로바츠에게 자기, 곧 사워비가 신사의 기개라 불렀을 만한 것이 있다는 것은 그도 늘 알고 있었다."], ["He had regarded him as a bold, open, generous fellow, able to take his own part when called on to do so, and by no means disinclined to speak his own mind; but he had not expected from him such a torrent of indignation, or thought that he was capable of such a depth of anger.", "그는 로바츠를 담대하고 툭 터놓고 넉넉한 사람으로, 그렇게 해야 할 자리에서는 제 몫을 감당할 수 있고 제 마음을 말하기를 결코 꺼리지 않는 사람으로 여겨 왔다. 그러나 그에게서 이런 분개의 급류가 터져 나올 줄은 예상하지 못했고, 그가 이만한 깊이의 노여움을 낼 수 있는 사람이라고는 생각하지 못했다."]], [["“If you use such language as that, Robarts, I can only leave you.”", "“그런 말을 쓰신다면, 로바츠, 나는 당신을 두고 떠날 수밖에 없어요.”"]], [["“You are welcome. Go.", "“그러셔도 좋습니다."], ["You tell me that you are the messenger of these men who intend to work nine hundred pounds out of me.", "가시지요. 당신은 나에게서 구백 파운드를 뽑아내려는 그 사람들의 심부름꾼이라고 스스로 말하고 있어요."], ["You have done your part in the plot, and have now brought their message.", "당신은 그 궁리에서 제 몫을 다했고, 이제 그들의 전갈을 가져왔지요."], ["It seems to me that you had better go back to them.", "내가 보기에는 당신이 그들에게 돌아가는 편이 좋을 것 같습니다."], ["As for me, I want my time to prepare my wife for the destiny before her.”", "나로 말하면, 내 아내에게 앞으로 닥칠 운명을 알려 줄 시간이 필요합니다.”"]], [["“Robarts, you will be sorry some day for the cruelty of your words.”", "“로바츠, 당신은 언젠가 그 매정한 말을 후회하게 될 거예요.”"]], [["“I wonder whether you will ever be sorry for the cruelty of your doings, or whether these things are really a joke to you.”", "“당신은 언젠가 당신이 한 매정한 짓을 후회하게 될지, 아니면 당신에게는 이런 일들이 정말로 우스개일 뿐인지 궁금하군요.”"]], [["“I am at this moment a ruined man,”", "“나는 이 순간 파멸한 사람입니다,”"], ["said Sowerby.", "사워비가 말했다."], ["“Everything is going from me,—my place in the world, the estate of my family, my father’s house, my seat in Parliament, the power of living among my countrymen, or, indeed, of living anywhere;—but all this does not oppress me now so much as the misery which I have brought upon you.”", "“모든 것이 나에게서 떠나가고 있어요 — 세상에서 내가 선 자리, 집안의 땅, 아버지의 집, 의회의 내 의석, 내 나라 사람들 가운데서 살아갈 힘, 아니 실은 어디서든 살아갈 힘까지요. 그러나 이 모든 것이 지금 나를 짓누르는 것은, 내가 당신에게 가져다준 그 참담함이 나를 짓누르는 것만 못합니다.”"], ["And then Sowerby also turned away his face, and wiped from his eyes tears which were not artificial.", "그러고서 사워비도 얼굴을 돌리고, 꾸며 낸 것이 아닌 눈물을 눈에서 닦아 냈다."]], [["Robarts was still walking up and down the room, but it was not possible for him to continue his reproaches after this.", "로바츠는 여전히 방을 오르내리고 있었으나, 이 말을 듣고서 꾸짖음을 이어 갈 수는 없었다."], ["This is always the case.", "언제나 그러하다."], ["Let a man endure to heap contumely on his own head, and he will silence the contumely of others—for the moment.", "사람이 제 머리에 모욕을 쌓아 올리기를 견뎌 내면 남들의 모욕을 잠잠하게 만든다 — 그 순간만은."], ["Sowerby, without meditating on the matter, had had some inkling of this, and immediately saw that there was at last an opening for conversation.", "사워비는 이것을 곰곰이 따져 본 것은 아니었으나 어렴풋이 느끼는 바가 있었고, 이제 이야기를 할 틈이 마침내 생겼다는 것을 곧 알아보았다."]], [["“You are unjust to me,”", "“당신은 나에게 온당하지 못합니다,”"], ["said he, “in supposing that I have now no wish to save you.", "그가 말했다. “나에게 이제 당신을 구할 마음이 없다고 여기는 것 말이에요."], ["It is solely in the hope of doing so that I have come here.”", "오로지 그러기를 바라는 마음으로 나는 여기에 온 겁니다.”"]], [["“And what is your hope?", "“그런데 당신이 바라는 것이 무엇입니까?"], ["That I should accept another brace of bills, I suppose.”", "내가 또 어음 두 장을 인수하는 것이겠지요.”"]], [["“Not a brace; but one renewed bill for—”", "“두 장이 아니라, 갱신한 어음 한 장으로 —”"]], [["“Look here, Mr. Sowerby.", "“들어 보시지요, 사워비 씨."], ["On no earthly consideration that can be put before me will I again sign my name to any bill in the guise of an acceptance.", "이 세상에서 나에게 내놓을 수 있는 어떤 이유를 들어도 나는 인수라는 이름으로 어떤 어음에도 다시는 내 이름을 적지 않겠습니다."], ["I have been very weak, and am ashamed of my weakness; but so much strength as that, I hope, is left to me.", "나는 몹시 약했고 내 약함이 부끄럽습니다. 그러나 그만한 힘은 아직 남아 있기를 바랍니다."], ["I have been very wicked, and am ashamed of my wickedness; but so much right principle as that, I hope, remains.", "나는 몹시 악했고 내 악함이 부끄럽습니다. 그러나 그만한 옳은 원칙은 아직 남아 있기를 바랍니다."], ["I will put my name to no other bill; not for you, not even for myself.”", "나는 다른 어떤 어음에도 내 이름을 적지 않겠습니다. 당신을 위해서도, 나 자신을 위해서조차도요.”"]], [["“But, Robarts, under your present circumstances that will be madness.”", "“하지만 로바츠, 지금 당신 형편에서 그것은 미친 짓일 겁니다.”"]], [["“Then I will be mad.”", "“그러면 나는 미치겠습니다.”"]], [["“Have you seen Forrest?", "“포레스트를 만나 보았어요?"], ["If you will speak to him I think you will find that everything can be accommodated.”", "당신이 그와 이야기를 해 보면 모든 것이 조정될 수 있다는 것을 알게 될 겁니다.”"]], [["“I already owe Mr.", "“나는 이미 포레스트 씨에게 백오십 파운드를 빚지고 있습니다."], ["Forrest a hundred and fifty pounds, which I obtained from him when you pressed me for the price of that horse, and I will not increase the debt.", "당신이 그 말값을 내라고 나를 재촉했을 때 그에게서 얻은 것이지요. 그리고 나는 그 빚을 늘리지 않겠습니다."], ["What a fool I was again there.", "그때도 내가 또 얼마나 얼간이였던지요."], ["Perhaps you do not remember that, when I agreed to buy the horse, the price was to be my contribution to the liquidation of these bills.”", "어쩌면 당신은 기억하지 못할 테지만, 내가 그 말을 사기로 했을 때 그 값은 이 어음들을 갚는 데 내가 보태는 몫이 되기로 되어 있었습니다.”"]], [["“I do remember it; but I will tell you how that was.”", "“나도 기억합니다. 하지만 그것이 어떻게 된 일인지 당신에게 말해 주겠어요.”"]], [["“It does not signify.", "“그런 것은 아무래도 좋습니다."], ["It has been all of a piece.”", "처음부터 끝까지 죄다 한통속이었지요.”"]], [["“But listen to me.", "“그러나 내 말을 들어 봐요."], ["I think you would feel for me if you knew all that I have gone through.", "내가 겪어 온 것을 다 알게 되면 당신도 나를 딱하게 여길 것이라고 생각해요."], ["I pledge you my solemn word that I had no intention of asking you for the money when you took the horse;—indeed I had not.", "당신이 그 말을 받았을 때 나에게는 당신에게 그 돈을 청할 뜻이 없었다는 것을 내 엄숙한 말로 맹세합니다 — 정말로 없었어요."], ["But you remember that affair of Lufton’s, when he came to you at your hotel in London and was so angry about an outstanding bill.”", "그러나 러프턴의 그 일을 당신도 기억하지요. 그가 런던의 당신 호텔로 찾아와서 미결 어음 한 장 때문에 그토록 노여워했던 일 말이에요.”"]], [["“I know that he was very unreasonable as far as I was concerned.”", "“내가 관련된 한에서는 그가 아주 사리에 어긋났다는 것을 나는 알고 있습니다.”"]], [["“He was so; but that makes no difference.", "“그랬지요. 그러나 그것은 아무 차이가 없어요."], ["He was resolved, in his rage, to expose the whole affair; and I saw that, if he did so, it would be most injurious to you, seeing that you had just accepted your stall at Barchester.”", "그는 노여움에 사로잡혀 그 일을 전부 드러내 놓기로 마음을 굳혔고, 그가 그렇게 하면 당신에게 가장 크게 해가 될 것이 나에게 보였습니다. 당신이 바체스터의 참사회원 자리를 막 받아들인 참이었으니까요.”"], ["Here the poor prebendary winced terribly.", "여기서 그 딱한 참사회원은 몸서리치게 움찔했다."], ["“I moved heaven and earth to get up that bill.", "“나는 그 어음을 회수하려고 하늘과 땅을 움직였어요."], ["Those vultures stuck to their prey when they found the value which I attached to it, and I was forced to raise above a hundred pounds at the moment to obtain possession of it, although every shilling absolutely due on it had long since been paid.", "그 독수리들은 내가 그것에 매기는 값을 알아채자 제 먹이에 딱 붙어 버렸고, 그것을 손에 넣으려고 나는 그 자리에서 백 파운드가 넘는 돈을 마련해야 했습니다. 그 어음에 정말로 물어야 할 돈은 한 실링까지 벌써 오래전에 다 갚아진 것이었는데도요."], ["Never in my life did I wish to get money as I did to raise that hundred and twenty pounds; and as I hope for mercy in my last moments, I did that for your sake.", "그 백이십 파운드를 마련하고 싶었던 것만큼 돈을 얻고 싶었던 적은 내 평생 없었어요. 그리고 마지막 순간에 자비를 바라는 이 마음을 걸고 말하거니와, 나는 그것을 당신을 위해 했습니다."], ["Lufton could not have injured me in that matter.”", "러프턴은 그 일로 나를 해칠 수 없었어요.”"]], [["“But you told him that you got it for twenty-five pounds.”", "“하지만 당신은 그것을 이십오 파운드에 사 왔다고 그에게 말했지요.”"]], [["“Yes, I told him so.", "“그래요, 그에게 그렇게 말했습니다."], ["I was obliged to tell him that, or I should have apparently condemned myself by showing how anxious I was to get it.", "나는 그에게 그렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었어요. 그러지 않았다면 내가 그것을 얼마나 애타게 얻고 싶어 했는지 드러내어 스스로를 죄인으로 만들어 보이는 셈이 되었을 테니까요."], ["And you know I could not have explained all this before him and you.", "그리고 그와 당신이 함께 있는 자리에서 이 모든 것을 설명할 수 없었다는 것을 당신도 알잖아요."], ["You would have thrown up the stall in disgust.”", "당신은 진저리를 내며 그 참사회원 자리를 내던져 버렸을 겁니다.”"]], [["Would that he had!", "그랬더라면 좋았을 것을!"], ["That was Mark’s wish now,—his futile wish.", "그것이 이제 마크의 소원이었다 — 부질없는 소원이었다."], ["In what a slough of despond had he come to wallow in consequence of his folly on that night at Gatherum Castle!", "개서럼 성에서의 그날 밤 저지른 어리석음 때문에 그는 얼마나 깊은 낙담의 늪에 빠져 허우적거리게 되었는가!"], ["He had then done a silly thing, and was he now to rue it by almost total ruin?", "그때 그는 어리석은 짓 하나를 했는데, 이제 거의 온전한 파멸로 그것을 후회해야 하는가?"], ["He was sickened also with all these lies.", "그는 이 모든 거짓말에도 진저리가 났다."], ["His very soul was dismayed by the dirt through which he was forced to wade.", "어쩔 수 없이 헤치고 걸어야 하는 그 더러움에 그의 영혼 자체가 기가 꺾였다."], ["He had become unconsciously connected with the lowest dregs of mankind, and would have to see his name mingled with theirs in the daily newspapers.", "그는 자기도 모르는 사이에 사람 가운데 가장 낮은 밑바닥과 얽혀 버렸고, 날마다 나오는 신문에서 제 이름이 그들의 이름과 뒤섞인 것을 보아야 할 것이었다."], ["And for what had he done this?", "그런데 무엇을 위해 이런 짓을 했던가?"], ["Why had he thus filed his mind and made himself a disgrace to his cloth?", "무엇을 위해 이렇게 제 마음을 더럽히고 제가 입은 성직자의 옷에 부끄러운 사람이 되었던가?"], ["In order that he might befriend such a one as Mr. Sowerby!", "사워비 씨 같은 이의 벗이 되어 주려고!"]], [["“Well,”", "“그런데,”"], ["continued Sowerby, “I did get the money, but you would hardly believe the rigour of the pledge which was exacted from me for repayment.", "사워비가 말을 이었다. “나는 그 돈을 얻어 냈어요."], ["I got it from Harold Smith, and never, in my worst straits, will I again look to him for assistance.", "그러나 갚는 것에 대해 나에게 요구된 그 다짐이 얼마나 엄격한 것이었는지 당신은 좀처럼 믿지 못할 겁니다."], ["I borrowed it only for a fortnight; and in order that I might repay it, I was obliged to ask you for the price of the horse.", "나는 그것을 해럴드 스미스에게서 얻었는데, 앞으로 아무리 궁한 처지에 몰려도 다시는 그에게 도움을 바라지 않을 거예요. 나는 그것을 딱 보름 동안만 빌렸습니다."], ["Mark, it was on your behalf that I did all this,—indeed it was.”", "그리고 그것을 갚으려고 당신에게 그 말값을 청하지 않을 수 없었어요. 마크, 나는 이 모든 것을 당신을 위해서 한 겁니다 — 정말로 그랬어요.”"]], [["“And now I am to repay you for your kindness by the loss of all that I have in the world.”", "“그런데 이제 나는 당신의 그 친절에, 내가 세상에 가진 모든 것을 잃는 것으로 갚아야 하는군요.”"]], [["“If you will put the affair into the hands of Mr.", "“이 일을 포레스트 씨의 손에 맡기기만 하면 무엇 하나 손댈 것이 없습니다 — 말 등의 털 한 올까지도요."], ["Forrest, nothing need be touched,—not a hair of a horse’s back; no, not though you should be obliged to pay the whole amount yourself, gradually out of your income.", "아니, 당신이 그 전액을 당신 수입에서 조금씩 스스로 갚아야 하게 된다 해도 그렇습니다."], ["You must execute a series of bills, falling due quarterly, and then—”", "분기마다 만기가 돌아오는 어음을 여러 장 작성해 두면, 그러고 나면 —”"]], [["“I will execute no bill, I will put my name to no paper in the matter; as to that my mind is fully made up.", "“나는 어떤 어음도 작성하지 않겠습니다. 이 일로는 어떤 종이에도 내 이름을 적지 않겠어요."], ["They may come and do their worst.”", "그 점에 대해서는 내 마음이 온전히 정해졌습니다. 그들이야 와서 최악을 하라지요.”"]], [["Mr. Sowerby persevered for a long time, but he was quite unable to move the parson from this position.", "사워비 씨는 오랫동안 버티고 애썼으나 그 목사를 이 자리에서 조금도 움직일 수 없었다."], ["He would do nothing towards making what Mr. Sowerby called an arrangement, but persisted that he would remain at home at Framley, and that any one who had a claim upon him might take legal steps.", "그는 사워비 씨가 조정이라 부르는 것을 위해서는 아무것도 하지 않겠다고 하면서, 자기는 프램리의 집에 그대로 있을 것이고 자기에게 청구할 것이 있는 사람은 누구든 법의 절차를 밟으면 된다고 우겼다."]], [["“I shall do nothing myself,”", "“나 자신은 아무것도 하지 않겠습니다,”"], ["he said; “but if proceedings against me be taken, I shall prove that I have never had a shilling of the money.”", "그는 말했다. “그러나 나를 상대로 절차가 밟힌다면, 나는 그 돈을 한 실링도 만져 본 적이 없다는 것을 밝히겠습니다.”"], ["And in this resolution he quitted the Dragon of Wantly.", "그리고 이런 결심으로 그는 원틀리의 용을 떠났다."]], [["Mr. Sowerby at one time said a word as to the expediency of borrowing that sum of money from John Robarts; but as to this Mark would say nothing.", "사워비 씨는 한 번, 존 로바츠에게서 그만한 액수를 빌리는 것이 어떻겠느냐고 한마디 했다."], ["Mr. Sowerby was not the friend with whom he now intended to hold consultation in such matters.", "그러나 이에 대해 마크는 아무 말도 하려 하지 않았다."], ["“I am not at present prepared,”", "사워비 씨는 이제 그가 그런 문제로 의논을 나누려는 벗이 아니었다. “나는 지금 내가 무엇을 할지 밝힐 준비가 되어 있지 않습니다,”"], ["he said, “to declare what I may do; I must first see what steps others take.”", "그는 말했다. “먼저 다른 사람들이 어떤 걸음을 밟는지 보아야 합니다.”"], ["And then he took his hat and went off; and mounting his horse in the yard of the Dragon of Wantly—that horse which he had now so many reasons to dislike—he slowly rode back home.", "그러고서 그는 모자를 들고 나갔다. 그리고 원틀리의 용 마당에서, 이제 그토록 싫어할 이유가 많아진 그 말에 올라 천천히 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Many thoughts passed through his mind during that ride, but only one resolution obtained for itself a fixture there.", "그 말길에 여러 생각이 그의 머릿속을 지나갔으나, 오직 한 가지 결심만이 거기 자리를 붙였다."], ["He must now tell his wife everything.", "그는 이제 아내에게 모든 것을 말해야 했다."], ["He would not be so cruel as to let it remain untold until a bailiff were at the door, ready to walk him off to the county gaol, or until the bed on which they slept was to be sold from under them.", "집행관이 문 앞에 와서 그를 주 감옥으로 끌고 갈 채비를 할 때까지, 또는 두 사람이 누워 자는 침대가 몸 아래에서 팔려 나갈 때까지 그것을 말하지 않고 두는 그런 매정한 짓은 하지 않을 것이었다."], ["Yes, he would tell her everything,—immediately, before his resolution could again have faded away.", "그렇다, 그는 아내에게 모든 것을 말할 것이었다 — 결심이 다시 시들어 버리기 전에 당장."], ["He got off his horse in the yard, and seeing his wife’s maid at the kitchen door, desired her to beg her mistress to come to him in the book-room.", "그는 마당에서 말에서 내려, 부엌문에 있는 아내의 하녀를 보고서 안주인에게 서재로 와 주시라고 청해 달라고 일렀다."], ["He would not allow one half-hour to pass towards the waning of his purpose.", "뜻이 기울어 가도록 반 시간도 흘려보내지 않을 것이었다."], ["If it be ordained that a man shall drown, had he not better drown and have done with it?", "사람이 물에 빠지도록 정해져 있다면, 빠져서 끝을 내 버리는 편이 낫지 않겠는가?"]], [["Mrs. Robarts came to him in his room, reaching him in time to touch his arm as he entered it.", "로바츠 부인이 그의 방으로 왔는데, 그가 방에 들어서는 순간에 그의 팔을 만질 만큼 때맞추어 닿았다."]], [["“Mary says you want me.", "“메리가 당신이 나를 찾는다고 하던데요."], ["I have been gardening, and she caught me just as I came in.”", "나는 정원 일을 하고 있었는데, 막 들어오는 것을 그 애가 붙잡았어요.”"]], [["“Yes, Fanny, I do want you.", "“그래, 패니, 당신을 찾았어."], ["Sit down for a moment.”", "잠깐 앉아.”"], ["And walking across the room, he placed his whip in its proper place.", "그러고서 그는 방을 건너가 채찍을 제자리에 놓았다."]], [["“Oh, Mark, is there anything the matter?”", "“아, 마크, 무슨 일이 있는 거예요?”"]], [["“Yes, dearest; yes.", "“그래, 여보, 그래."], ["Sit down, Fanny; I can talk to you better if you will sit.”", "앉아, 패니. 당신이 앉아 주면 이야기하기가 더 나아.”"]], [["But she, poor lady, did not wish to sit.", "그러나 그 딱한 부인은 앉고 싶지 않았다."], ["He had hinted at some misfortune, and therefore she felt a longing to stand by him and cling to him.", "그가 무슨 불행을 비추어 말했으니, 그의 곁에 서서 그에게 매달리고 싶은 마음이 간절했다."]], [["“Well, there; I will if I must; but, Mark, do not frighten me.", "“좋아요, 그러면. 꼭 그래야 한다면 앉을게요."], ["Why is your face so very wretched?”", "하지만 마크, 나를 겁나게 하지 말아요. 왜 얼굴이 그렇게 참담해요?”"]], [["“Fanny, I have done very wrong,” he said.", "“패니, 내가 아주 잘못을 했어,”"], ["“I have been very foolish.", "그가 말했다. “아주 어리석었어."], ["I fear that I have brought upon you great sorrow and trouble.”", "당신에게 큰 슬픔과 괴로움을 가져온 것 같아.”"], ["And then he leaned his head upon his hand and turned his face away from her.", "그러고서 그는 머리를 손에 기대고 얼굴을 아내에게서 돌렸다."]], [["“Oh, Mark, dearest Mark, my own Mark!", "“아, 마크, 사랑하는 마크, 내 마크!"], ["what is it?”", "그게 뭐예요?”"], ["and then she was quickly up from her chair, and went down on her knees before him.", "그러고서 그녀는 얼른 의자에서 일어나 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["“Do not turn from me.", "“나에게서 돌아서지 말아요."], ["Tell me, Mark!", "말해 줘요, 마크!"], ["tell me, that we may share it.”", "말해 줘요, 우리가 함께 나누게요.”"]], [["“Yes, Fanny, I must tell you now; but I hardly know what you will think of me when you have heard it.”", "“그래, 패니, 이제 당신에게 말해야지. 그런데 당신이 그것을 듣고 나면 나를 어떻게 생각할지 나는 도무지 모르겠어.”"]], [["“I will think that you are my own husband, Mark; I will think that—that chiefly, whatever it may be.”", "“당신이 내 남편이라고 생각하겠지요, 마크. 그것을 생각할 거예요 — 그것을 가장 먼저요, 그것이 무엇이든지요.”"], ["And then she caressed his knees, and looked up in his face, and, getting hold of one of his hands, pressed it between her own.", "그러고서 그녀는 그의 무릎을 쓰다듬으며 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았고, 그의 한 손을 붙잡아 제 두 손 사이에 꼭 눌렀다."], ["“Even if you have been foolish, who should forgive you if I cannot?”", "“당신이 어리석었다 해도, 내가 용서하지 못한다면 누가 당신을 용서하겠어요?”"]], [["And then he told it her all, beginning from that evening when Mr.", "그러고서 그는 아내에게 모든 것을 말했다."], ["Sowerby had got him into his bedroom, and going on gradually, now about the bills, and now about the horses, till his poor wife was utterly lost in the complexity of the accounts.", "사워비 씨가 그를 자기 침실로 데리고 들어갔던 그 저녁부터 시작하여, 이제는 어음에 대해 이제는 말에 대해 차근차근 이야기해 나가니, 딱한 아내는 그 셈의 뒤엉킴 속에서 아주 길을 잃어버렸다."], ["She could by no means follow him in the details of his story; nor could she quite sympathize with him in his indignation against Mr.", "그녀는 그의 이야기의 자세한 것들을 도무지 따라갈 수 없었고, 사워비 씨에 대한 그의 분개에 온전히 마음을 같이할 수도 없었다."], ["Sowerby, seeing that she did not comprehend at all the nature of the renewing of a bill.", "어음을 갱신한다는 것이 어떤 것인지 조금도 알아듣지 못했기 때문이다."], ["The only part to her of importance in the matter was the amount of money which her husband would be called upon to pay;—that and her strong hope, which was already a conviction, that he would never again incur such debts.", "이 일에서 그녀에게 중요한 것은 오직 남편이 갚아 내라는 요구를 받을 돈의 액수뿐이었다 — 그것과, 그가 다시는 그런 빚을 지지 않을 것이라는 그녀의 강한 희망, 이미 확신이 되어 있는 그 희망뿐이었다."]], [["“And how much is it, dearest, altogether?”", "“그러면 그게 다 얼마예요, 여보?”"]], [["“These men claim nine hundred pounds of me.”", "“그 사람들이 나에게 구백 파운드를 청구하고 있어.”"]], [["“Oh, dear!", "“아, 어쩌나!"], ["that is a terrible sum.”", "그건 무서운 액수예요.”"]], [["“And then there is the hundred and fifty which I have borrowed from the bank—the price of the horse, you know; and there are some other debts,—not a great deal, I think; but people will now look for every shilling that is due to them.", "“그리고 은행에서 빌린 백오십 파운드가 있어 — 그 말값이지, 당신도 알잖아. 그리고 다른 빚들도 좀 있어 — 큰 액수는 아닌 것 같지만, 이제 사람들은 자기에게 받을 것이 있으면 한 실링까지 찾아 나설 거야."], ["If I have to pay it all, it will be twelve or thirteen hundred pounds.”", "내가 그것을 다 갚아야 한다면 천이백이나 천삼백 파운드가 될 거야.”"]], [["“That will be as much as a year’s income, Mark; even with the stall.”", "“그건 한 해 수입만큼이나 되겠네요, 마크. 참사회원 자리를 넣어도요.”"]], [["That was the only word of reproach she said,—if that could be called a reproach.", "그것이 그녀가 한 유일한 꾸짖음의 말이었다 — 그것을 꾸짖음이라 부를 수 있다면."]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래,”"], ["he said; “and it is claimed by men who will have no pity in exacting it at any sacrifice, if they have the power.", "그가 말했다. “그리고 그것을 청구하는 사람들은 힘이 있는 한 어떤 희생을 치르게 하고서라도 인정 없이 받아 낼 사람들이야."], ["And to think that I should have incurred all this debt without having received anything for it.", "그리고 그 대가로 받은 것이 하나도 없이 이 모든 빚을 지게 되었다는 것을 생각하면!"], ["Oh, Fanny, what will you think of me!”", "아, 패니, 당신은 나를 어떻게 생각할까!”"]], [["But she swore to him that she would think nothing of it;—that she would never bear it in her mind against him,—that it could have no effect in lessening her trust in him.", "그러나 그녀는 그런 것은 조금도 생각하지 않겠다고 그에게 맹세했다 — 그것을 남편에게 거슬리는 것으로 마음에 두지 않겠다고, 그것이 남편에 대한 자기 신뢰를 조금도 덜어 놓을 수 없을 것이라고."], ["Was he not her husband?", "그가 자기 남편이 아닌가?"], ["She was so glad she knew it, that she might comfort him.", "그것을 알게 되어서, 그를 위로해 줄 수 있게 되어서 그녀는 그토록 기뻤다."], ["And she did comfort him, making the weight seem lighter and lighter on his shoulders as he talked of it.", "그리고 그녀는 정말로 그를 위로했으니, 그가 그것을 이야기해 나가는 사이에 짐은 그의 어깨에서 점점 더 가벼워졌다."], ["And such weights do thus become lighter.", "그런 짐은 정말로 그렇게 가벼워진다."], ["A burden that will crush a single pair of shoulders will, when equally divided—when shared by two, each of whom is willing to take the heavier part—become light as a feather.", "한 쌍의 어깨를 짓눌러 부술 짐도 똑같이 나뉘면 — 둘이서 나누어 지고, 그 둘이 각기 더 무거운 쪽을 지려 한다면 — 깃털처럼 가벼워진다."], ["Is not that sharing of the mind’s burdens one of the chief purposes for which a man wants a wife?", "마음의 짐을 그렇게 나누는 것이야말로 사람이 아내를 원하는 으뜸된 목적 가운데 하나가 아니겠는가?"], ["For there is no folly so great as keeping one’s sorrows hidden.", "제 슬픔을 감추어 두는 것만큼 큰 어리석음은 없으니 말이다."]], [["And this wife cheerfully, gladly, thankfully took her share.", "그리고 이 아내는 기꺼이, 즐거이, 고맙게 제 몫을 받아들였다."], ["To endure with her lord all her lord’s troubles was easy to her; it was the work to which she had pledged herself.", "지아비와 함께 지아비의 온갖 괴로움을 견디는 것은 그녀에게 쉬운 일이었다. 그것은 그녀가 스스로 맹세한 일이었다."], ["But to have thought that her lord had troubles not communicated to her—that would have been to her the one thing not to be borne.", "그러나 지아비에게 자기가 모르는 괴로움이 있다고 생각하는 것 — 그것이야말로 그녀에게 견딜 수 없는 오직 하나의 일이었을 것이다."]], [["And then they discussed their plans;—what mode of escape they might have out of this terrible money difficulty.", "그러고서 두 사람은 계획을 의논했다 — 이 무서운 돈 곤경에서 벗어날 어떤 길이 있을지를. 참된 여인답게 로바츠 부인은 남는 것은 다 버리자고 그 자리에서 제의했다."], ["Like a true woman, Mrs.", "말은 다 팔 것이다."], ["Robarts proposed at once to abandon all superfluities.", "암소는 팔지 않되 거기서 나오는 버터를 팔 것이다."], ["They would sell all their horses; they would not sell their cows, but would sell the butter that came from them; they would sell the pony-carriage, and get rid of the groom.", "조랑말 마차를 팔고 마부는 내보낼 것이다."], ["That the footman must go was so much a matter of course, that it was hardly mentioned.", "하인이 나가야 하는 것은 너무나 당연한 일이라 말조차 거의 나오지 않았다."], ["But then, as to that house at Barchester, the dignified prebendal mansion in the close—might they not be allowed to leave it unoccupied for one year longer,—perhaps to let it?", "그러나 바체스터의 그 집, 성당 구내에 있는 그 위엄 있는 참사회원 저택은 어떻게 할 것인가 — 한 해 더 비워 두도록 허락받을 수는 없을까, 어쩌면 세를 놓을 수는 없을까?"], ["The world of course must know of their misfortune; but if that misfortune was faced bravely, the world would be less bitter in its condemnation.", "세상은 물론 그들의 불행을 알게 될 것이다. 그러나 그 불행에 용감하게 맞선다면 세상도 그 나무람에 덜 매서울 것이다."], ["And then, above all things, everything must be told to Lady Lufton.", "그리고 무엇보다도, 레이디 러프턴에게는 모든 것을 말해야 했다."]], [["“You may, at any rate, believe this, Fanny,”", "“패니, 아무튼 이것만은 믿어 줘,”"], ["said he, “that for no consideration which can be offered to me will I ever put my name to another bill.”", "그가 말했다. “나에게 무엇을 내놓는다 해도 나는 다시는 어음에 내 이름을 적지 않을 거야.”"]], [["The kiss with which she thanked him for this was as warm and generous as though he had brought to her that day news of the brightest; and when he sat, as he did that evening, discussing it all, not only with his wife, but with Lucy, he wondered how it was that his troubles were now so light.", "그녀가 이 말에 고마워하며 준 입맞춤은, 그가 그날 그녀에게 가장 밝은 소식을 가져왔더라도 그러했을 만큼 따뜻하고 넉넉했다. 그리고 그날 저녁 그가 앉아서 아내뿐 아니라 루시와도 함께 이 모든 것을 이야기했을 때, 그는 어떻게 해서 제 괴로움이 이제 이토록 가벼워졌는지 이상하게 여겼다."]], [["Whether or no a man should have his own private pleasures, I will not now say; but it never can be worth his while to keep his sorrows private.", "사람이 저만의 즐거움을 가져야 하는지 어떤지는 나는 여기서 말하지 않겠다. 그러나 제 슬픔을 저만의 것으로 감추어 두는 것은 결코 그에게 값이 될 수 없다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIV. LADY LUFTON IS TAKEN BY SURPRISE.", "# 제34장. 레이디 러프턴이 뜻밖의 일을 당하다"]], [["Lord Lufton, as he returned to town, found some difficulty in resolving what step he would next take.", "러프턴 경은 런던으로 돌아오는 길에 다음으로 어떤 걸음을 내디뎌야 할지 정하기가 적잖이 어려웠다."], ["Sometimes, for a minute or two, he was half inclined to think—or rather to say to himself—that Lucy was perhaps not worth the trouble which she threw in his way.", "이따금 잠깐씩은, 루시가 제 앞길에 던져 놓은 이 번거로움만큼의 값이 없는 여인일지도 모른다고 생각하는—아니, 차라리 스스로에게 그렇게 말해 보는—쪽으로 반쯤 기울기도 했다."], ["He loved her very dearly, and would willingly make her his wife, he thought or said at such moments; but— Such moments, however, were only moments.", "루시를 몹시 사랑하고 있고 기꺼이 아내로 삼겠다고, 그런 순간에 그는 생각했거나 그렇게 말했다. 그러나— 하지만 그런 순간은 순간에 지나지 않았다."], ["A man in love seldom loves less because his love becomes difficult.", "사랑에 빠진 사내가 제 사랑이 어려워졌다는 이유로 덜 사랑하게 되는 일은 좀처럼 없다."], ["And thus, when those moments were over, he would determine to tell his mother at once, and urge her to signify her consent to Miss Robarts.", "그리하여 그런 순간이 지나가면 그는 당장 어머니에게 말해 버리기로, 그리고 로바츠 양에게 승낙의 뜻을 밝혀 달라고 어머니를 밀어붙이기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["That she would not be quite pleased he knew; but if he were firm enough to show that he had a will of his own in this matter, she would probably not gainsay him.", "어머니가 그리 기뻐하지 않을 것은 알고 있었다. 그러나 이 일에 제 뜻이 따로 있음을 보일 만큼 자기가 단단히 버티면 어머니도 아마 거스르지는 못할 터였다."], ["He would not ask this humbly, as a favour, but request her ladyship to go through the ceremony as though it were one of those motherly duties which she as a good mother could not hesitate to perform on behalf of her son.", "그는 이것을 은혜로 여겨 몸을 낮추어 청하지는 않을 작정이었다. 그저 좋은 어머니라면 아들을 위하여 망설임 없이 해 주어야 할 어머니의 소임 가운데 하나인 양, 그 절차를 치러 달라고 마님께 요구하려 했다."], ["Such was the final resolve with which he reached his chambers in the Albany.", "앨버니에 있는 자기 거처에 이르렀을 때 그가 마지막으로 굳힌 결심은 그러했다."]], [["On the next day he did not see his mother.", "이튿날 그는 어머니를 만나지 않았다."], ["It would be well, he thought, to have his interview with her immediately before he started for Norway, so that there might be no repetition of it; and it was on the day before he did start that he made his communication, having invited himself to breakfast in Brook Street on the occasion.", "노르웨이로 떠나기 바로 전에 어머니와 마주 앉는 것이 좋으리라고, 그러면 같은 이야기를 두 번 되풀이할 일이 없으리라고 그는 생각했다. 그래서 그가 그 말을 꺼낸 것은 정말로 떠나기 하루 전날이었고, 그날 그는 브룩 스트리트로 아침을 먹으러 가겠다고 스스로 청해 두었다."]], [["“Mother,”", "“어머니.”"], ["he said, quite abruptly, throwing himself into one of the dining-room arm-chairs, “I have a thing to tell you.”", "그가 식당의 안락의자 하나에 몸을 던지며 아주 불쑥 말했다. “드릴 말씀이 있어요.”"]], [["His mother at once knew that the thing was important, and with her own peculiar motherly instinct imagined that the question to be discussed had reference to matrimony.", "어머니는 그 말이 중대한 것임을 곧 알아차렸고, 저만이 지닌 남다른 어머니의 본능으로 의논하려는 문제가 혼사에 관한 것이라고 짐작했다."], ["Had her son desired to speak to her about money, his tone and look would have been different; as would also have been the case—in a different way—had he entertained any thought of a pilgrimage to Pekin, or a prolonged fishing excursion to the Hudson Bay territories.", "아들이 돈 이야기를 하려는 것이었다면 목소리도 눈빛도 달랐을 것이다. 페킨으로 순례를 떠나겠다거나 허드슨만 준주로 오래 낚시 여행을 가겠다는 생각을 품고 있는 경우라도—또 다른 식으로—역시 달랐을 것이다."]], [["“A thing, Ludovic!", "“말씀이라니, 루도빅!"], ["well; I am quite at liberty.”", "그래, 나는 아주 한가하다.”"]], [["“I want to know what you think of Lucy Robarts?”", "“루시 로바츠를 어떻게 보시는지 알고 싶어요.”"]], [["Lady Lufton became pale and frightened, and the blood ran cold to her heart.", "레이디 러프턴은 얼굴이 창백해지며 겁에 질렸고, 피가 심장으로 차갑게 몰렸다."], ["She had feared more than rejoiced in conceiving that her son was about to talk of love, but she had feared nothing so bad as this.", "아들이 사랑 이야기를 꺼내려 한다고 짐작했을 때에도 그녀는 기뻐하기보다 두려워했으나, 이만큼 나쁜 것은 두려워한 적조차 없었다."], ["“What do I think of Lucy Robarts?”", "“내가 루시 로바츠를 어떻게 보느냐고?”"], ["she said, repeating her son’s words in a tone of evident dismay.", "그녀는 아들의 말을 그대로 되받아 옮기며, 당황한 기색이 뚜렷한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, mother; you have said once or twice lately that you thought I ought to marry, and I am beginning to think so too.", "“그래요, 어머니. 요사이 어머니는 제가 혼인을 해야 한다고 생각한다는 말씀을 한두 번 하셨고, 저도 그렇게 여기기 시작했어요."], ["You selected one clergyman’s daughter for me, but that lady is going to do much better with herself—”", "어머니는 저를 위해 어느 성직자의 딸을 골라 두셨지만, 그 아가씨는 스스로 훨씬 더 나은 자리를 얻으려—”"]], [["“Indeed she is not,”", "“결코 더 나은 자리가 아니다.”"], ["said Lady Lufton sharply.", "레이디 러프턴이 날카롭게 말했다."]], [["“And therefore I rather think I shall select for myself another clergyman’s sister.", "“그러니 저는 제 손으로 다른 성직자의 누이를 고를까 해요."], ["You don’t dislike Miss Robarts, I hope?”", "어머니가 로바츠 양을 싫어하시지는 않겠지요?”"]], [["“Oh, Ludovic!”", "“아아, 루도빅!”"]], [["It was all that Lady Lufton could say at the spur of the moment.", "그 자리에서 레이디 러프턴이 할 수 있는 말은 그것뿐이었다."]], [["“Is there any harm in her?", "“그 사람에게 나쁜 데가 있나요?"], ["Have you any objection to her?", "어머니는 그 사람에게 무엇을 반대하시나요?"], ["Is there anything about her that makes her unfit to be my wife?”", "그 사람에게 제 아내가 되기에 마땅치 않은 무엇이 있나요?”"]], [["For a moment or two Lady Lufton sat silent, collecting her thoughts.", "한두 순간 레이디 러프턴은 말없이 앉아 생각을 가다듬었다."], ["She thought that there was very great objection to Lucy Robarts, regarding her as the possible future Lady Lufton.", "루시 로바츠를 앞날의 레이디 러프턴이 될 수도 있는 사람으로 놓고 보자면 반대할 까닭이 아주 크다고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["She could hardly have stated all her reasons, but they were very cogent.", "그 까닭을 다 늘어놓기는 어려웠겠지만 하나하나가 여간 단단한 것이 아니었다."], ["Lucy Robarts had, in her eyes, neither beauty, nor style, nor manner, nor even the education which was desirable.", "루시 로바츠에게는 그녀가 보기에 아름다움도, 자태도, 몸가짐도, 갖추었으면 하는 교양조차 없었다."], ["Lady Lufton was not herself a worldly woman.", "레이디 러프턴 자신은 세속에 물든 여인이 아니었다."], ["She was almost as far removed from being so as a woman could be in her position.", "그만한 자리에 있는 여인으로서 세속과 그렇게까지 멀 수 있을까 싶을 만큼 멀었다."], ["But, nevertheless, there were certain worldly attributes which she regarded as essential to the character of any young lady who might be considered fit to take the place which she herself had so long filled.", "그럼에도, 자기가 그토록 오래 채워 온 자리를 이어받기에 마땅하다고 여겨질 만한 젊은 여인의 됨됨이에는 반드시 있어야 한다고 그녀가 여기는 세속의 자질이 몇 가지 있었다."], ["It was her desire in looking for a wife for her son to combine these with certain moral excellences which she regarded as equally essential.", "아들의 아내를 구하면서 그녀가 바란 것은 그 자질을, 똑같이 반드시 있어야 한다고 여기는 도덕상의 덕과 아울러 갖춘 사람이었다."], ["Lucy Robarts might have the moral excellences, or she might not; but as to the other attributes Lady Lufton regarded her as altogether deficient.", "루시 로바츠에게 그 도덕상의 덕이 있는지 없는지는 알 수 없는 일이었으나, 나머지 자질로 말하면 레이디 러프턴은 루시를 도무지 모자란 사람으로 보았다."], ["She could never look like a Lady Lufton, or carry herself in the county as a Lady Lufton should do.", "루시는 결코 레이디 러프턴처럼 보일 수 없고, 레이디 러프턴이 마땅히 그래야 하듯 주 안에서 몸가짐을 지닐 수도 없을 터였다."], ["She had not that quiet personal demeanour—that dignity of repose—which Lady Lufton loved to look upon in a young married woman of rank.", "레이디 러프턴이 지체 있는 집 젊은 새댁에게서 보기를 좋아하는 그 조용한 몸가짐, 그 고요한 위엄이 루시에게는 없었다."], ["Lucy, she would have said, could be nobody in a room except by dint of her tongue, whereas Griselda Grantly would have held her peace for a whole evening, and yet would have impressed everybody by the majesty of her presence.", "루시는 혀를 놀려서가 아니면 방 안에서 아무도 아닐 사람이라고, 그런 데 반해 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 저녁 내내 입을 다물고 있어도 그 자리에 있다는 사실의 장엄함만으로 누구에게나 깊은 자취를 남겼으리라고 그녀는 말했을 것이다."], ["Then again Lucy had no money—and, again, Lucy was only the sister of her own parish clergyman.", "게다가 루시에게는 돈이 없었고, 게다가 루시는 그녀 자신의 교구 성직자의 누이일 뿐이었다."], ["People are rarely prophets in their own country, and Lucy was no prophet at Framley; she was none, at least, in the eyes of Lady Lufton.", "사람은 제 고향에서 좀처럼 선지자가 되지 못하는 법이니, 루시도 프램리에서는 선지자가 아니었다. 적어도 레이디 러프턴의 눈에는 아니었다."], ["Once before, as may be remembered, she had had fears on this subject—fears, not so much for her son, whom she could hardly bring herself to suspect of such a folly, but for Lucy, who might be foolish enough to fancy that the lord was in love with her.", "독자도 기억하시겠지만 전에도 한 번 그녀는 이 일로 두려워한 적이 있었다. 아들을 두고 두려워한 것이 아니라—그런 어리석음을 아들이 저지르리라고는 차마 의심할 수도 없었으니까—어리석게도 나리가 자기를 사랑한다고 헛생각을 품을지 모를 루시를 두고 두려워한 것이었다."], ["Alas! alas!", "아아, 아아!"], ["her son’s question fell upon the poor woman at the present moment with the weight of a terrible blow.", "이 순간 아들의 물음은 이 딱한 여인에게 무서운 일격의 무게로 떨어져 내렸다."]], [["“Is there anything about her which makes her unfit to be my wife?”", "“그 사람에게 제 아내가 되기에 마땅치 않은 무엇이 있나요?”"]], [["Those were her son’s last words.", "그것이 아들이 마지막으로 한 말이었다."]], [["“Dearest Ludovic, dearest Ludovic!”", "“루도빅, 내 사랑하는 루도빅!” 그녀는 일어나 아들 쪽으로 건너오며 말했다."], ["and she got up and came over to him, “I do think so; I do, indeed.”", "“나는 그렇게 생각한다. 정말로 그렇게 생각한다.”"]], [["“Think what?”", "“무엇을 그렇게 생각하세요?”"], ["said he, in a tone that was almost angry.", "그가 거의 노한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“I do think that she is unfit to be your wife.", "“나는 그 아이가 네 아내가 되기에 마땅치 않다고 생각한다."], ["She is not of that class from which I would wish to see you choose.”", "그 아이는 네가 골랐으면 하고 내가 바라는 계층의 사람이 아니야.”"]], [["“She is of the same class as Griselda Grantly.”", "“그리젤다 그랜틀리와 같은 계층이에요.”"]], [["“No, dearest.", "“아니다, 얘야."], ["I think you are in error there.", "그 점에서 너는 잘못 알고 있는 것 같다."], ["The Grantlys have moved in a different sphere of life.", "그랜틀리네는 다른 삶의 자리에서 지내 왔다."], ["I think you must feel that they are—”", "그들이 그렇다는 것쯤은 너도 느낄 텐데—”"]], [["“Upon my word, mother, I don’t.", "“맹세하고 말씀드리지만, 어머니, 저는 느끼지 못해요."], ["One man is Rector of Plumstead, and the other is Vicar of Framley.", "한 사람은 플럼스테드의 주임 사제고 다른 한 사람은 프램리의 교구 사제지요."], ["But it is no good arguing that.", "하지만 그걸 따져 봐야 소용없어요."], ["I want you to take to Lucy Robarts.", "저는 어머니가 루시 로바츠를 마음에 들여 주시기를 바라요."], ["I have come to you on purpose to ask it of you as a favour.”", "그것을 은혜로 청하려고 일부러 어머니께 온 거예요.”"]], [["“Do you mean as your wife, Ludovic?”", "“네 아내로 말이냐, 루도빅?”"]], [["“Yes; as my wife.”", "“예, 제 아내로요.”"]], [["“Am I to understand that you are—are engaged to her?”", "“그러면 네가—그 아이와 약혼했다고 알아들어야 하는 것이냐?”"]], [["“Well, I cannot say that I am—not actually engaged to her.", "“글쎄요, 그렇다고는 말씀드릴 수 없어요. 실제로 약혼한 것은 아니에요."], ["But you may take this for granted, that, as far as it lies in my power, I intend to become so.", "그러나 이것만은 정해진 일로 여기셔도 좋아요. 제 힘이 미치는 데까지 저는 그리 될 작정이에요."], ["My mind is made up, and I certainly shall not alter it.”", "마음은 굳혔고, 결코 바꾸지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“And the young lady knows all this?”", "“그 아가씨도 이 일을 다 알고 있느냐?”"]], [["“Certainly.”", "“물론이지요.”"]], [["“Horrid, sly, detestable, underhand girl,”", "‘끔찍하고, 교활하고, 밉살스럽고, 뒤가 구린 계집애.’ 레이디 러프턴은 속으로 그렇게 말했다."], ["Lady Lufton said to herself, not being by any means brave enough to speak out such language before her son.", "그런 말을 아들 앞에서 소리 내어 할 만큼 담대하지는 도무지 못했던 것이다."], ["What hope could there be if Lord Lufton had already committed himself by a positive offer?", "러프턴 경이 이미 분명한 청혼으로 제 몸을 얽어 놓았다면 무엇을 바랄 수 있겠는가?"], ["“And her brother, and Mrs.", "“그 아이의 오라비와 로바츠 부인은?"], ["Robarts; are they aware of it?”", "그이들도 알고 있느냐?”"]], [["“Yes; both of them.”", "“예, 둘 다 알고 있어요.”"]], [["“And both approve of it?”", "“그리고 둘 다 찬성하느냐?”"]], [["“Well, I cannot say that.", "“그렇다고는 말씀드릴 수 없어요."], ["I have not seen Mrs. Robarts, and do not know what may be her opinion.", "로바츠 부인은 만나지 못했으니 그분 생각이 어떤지 저는 몰라요."], ["To speak my mind honestly about Mark, I do not think he does cordially approve.", "마크에 대해서는 솔직히 제 뜻을 말씀드리자면, 그가 마음으로부터 찬성한다고는 보지 않아요."], ["He is afraid of you, and would be desirous of knowing what you think.”", "그는 어머니를 두려워하고, 어머니 생각이 어떤지 알고 싶어 할 거예요.”"]], [["“I am glad, at any rate, to hear that,”", "“어쨌든 그 말을 들으니 다행이다.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, gravely.", "레이디 러프턴이 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["“Had he done anything to encourage this, it would have been very base.”", "“그가 이 일을 부추기는 짓을 했다면 그건 아주 비루한 일이었을 게다.”"], ["And then there was another short period of silence.", "그러고서 또 한동안 짧은 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["Lord Lufton had determined not to explain to his mother the whole state of the case.", "러프턴 경은 이 일의 형편을 어머니에게 죄다 밝히지는 않기로 작정해 두었다."], ["He would not tell her that everything depended on her word—that Lucy was ready to marry him only on condition that she, Lady Lufton, would desire her to do so.", "모든 것이 어머니의 한마디에 걸려 있다는 것—곧 루시가 그와 혼인하겠다고 나설 조건은 오직 어머니 레이디 러프턴이 그러기를 바라 주는 것뿐이라는 것을 그는 말하지 않을 셈이었다."], ["He would not let her know that everything depended on her—according to Lucy’s present verdict.", "지금 루시가 내린 판결에 따르자면 모든 것이 어머니에게 달려 있다는 것을 알게 하지 않을 셈이었다."], ["He had a strong disinclination to ask his mother’s permission to get married; and he would have to ask it were he to tell her the whole truth.", "혼인을 하겠으니 허락해 달라고 어머니에게 청하는 일이 그는 몹시 싫었다. 그런데 사실을 다 털어놓는다면 그 허락을 청하지 않을 수 없게 될 것이었다."], ["His object was to make her think well of Lucy, and to induce her to be kind, and generous, and affectionate down at Framley.", "그의 목적은 어머니가 루시를 좋게 생각하도록 만드는 것, 그리고 프램리에 내려가서 다정하고 너그럽고 애정 있게 대하도록 이끄는 것이었다."], ["Then things would all turn out comfortably when he again visited that place, as he intended to do on his return from Norway.", "그러고 나면 노르웨이에서 돌아와 다시 그곳을 찾을 때—그는 그럴 작정이었다—모든 일이 편안하게 풀릴 터였다."], ["So much he thought it possible he might effect, relying on his mother’s probable calculation that it would be useless for her to oppose a measure which she had no power of stopping by authority.", "그만큼은 이루어 낼 수 있으리라고 그는 생각했으니, 권위로 막을 힘도 없는 일에 맞서 봐야 소용없다고 어머니가 셈해 볼 것이라 믿었기 때문이다."], ["But were he to tell her that she was to be the final judge, that everything was to depend on her will, then, so thought Lord Lufton, that permission would in all probability be refused.", "그러나 어머니가 최종 심판자라고, 모든 것이 어머니의 뜻에 걸려 있다고 말해 버린다면 그 허락은 아마 거절되고 말리라고 러프턴 경은 생각했다."]], [["“Well, mother, what answer do you intend to give me?”", "“그래서, 어머니, 저에게 어떤 대답을 주실 작정이세요?”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“My mind is positively made up.", "“제 마음은 아주 굳어졌어요."], ["I should not have come to you had not that been the case.", "그렇지 않았다면 어머니께 오지도 않았을 거예요."], ["You will now be going down home, and I would wish you to treat Lucy as you yourself would wish to treat any girl to whom you knew that I was engaged.”", "이제 어머니는 시골집으로 내려가시겠지요. 저는 어머니가 루시를, 제가 약혼한 줄 아시는 처녀라면 누구에게든 몸소 그리 대하고 싶으실 그대로 대해 주시기를 바라요.”"]], [["“But you say that you are not engaged.”", "“하지만 너는 약혼한 것이 아니라고 하지 않았느냐.”"]], [["“No, I am not; but I have made my offer to her, and I have not been rejected.", "“예, 아니에요. 그러나 저는 그 사람에게 청혼을 했고 거절당하지 않았어요."], ["She has confessed that she—loves me,—not to myself, but to her brother.", "그 사람은 저를—사랑한다고 털어놓았어요. 저에게가 아니라 오라비에게 말이지요."], ["Under these circumstances, may I count upon your obliging me?”", "이러한 형편에서 어머니가 제 뜻을 들어주실 것으로 믿어도 되겠어요?”"]], [["There was something in his manner which almost frightened his mother, and made her think that there was more behind than was told to her.", "그의 태도에는 어머니를 거의 겁나게 하는 무엇이, 그리고 들려주는 것보다 더 많은 것이 뒤에 있다고 여기게 하는 무엇이 있었다."], ["Generally speaking, his manner was open, gentle, and unguarded; but now he spoke as though he had prepared his words, and was resolved on being harsh as well as obstinate.", "대체로 그의 태도는 트여 있고 부드럽고 꾸밈이 없었다. 그런데 지금 그는 말을 미리 준비해 두고 온 사람처럼, 고집스러울 뿐 아니라 매섭기로 마음을 굳힌 사람처럼 말했다."]], [["“I am so much taken by surprise, Ludovic, that I can hardly give you an answer.", "“너무나 뜻밖의 일을 당해서, 루도빅, 나는 도무지 대답을 해 줄 수가 없다."], ["If you ask me whether I approve of such a marriage, I must say that I do not; I think that you would be throwing yourself away in marrying Miss Robarts.”", "내가 그런 혼인을 찬성하느냐고 묻는다면 찬성하지 않는다고 말해야겠다. 로바츠 양과 혼인하는 것은 너 자신을 내버리는 일이라고 나는 생각한다.”"]], [["“That is because you do not know her.”", "“그건 어머니가 그 사람을 모르시기 때문이에요.”"]], [["“May it not be possible that I know her better than you do, dear Ludovic?", "“내가 너보다 그 아이를 더 잘 알고 있을 수도 있지 않겠느냐, 루도빅?"], ["You have been flirting with her—”", "너는 그 아이와 희롱을 해 왔고—”"]], [["“I hate that word; it always sounds to me to be vulgar.”", "“저는 그 말이 싫어요. 저에게는 늘 상스럽게만 들려요.”"]], [["“I will say making love to her, if you like it better; and gentlemen under these circumstances will sometimes become infatuated.”", "“네가 그편이 더 좋다면 그 아이에게 구애했다고 하겠다. 그런 형편에 놓인 신사들은 더러 홀리는 일이 있는 법이야.”"]], [["“You would not have a man marry a girl without making love to her.", "“구애도 없이 사내가 처녀와 혼인하는 것을 어머니가 바라시지는 않겠지요. 사실은요, 어머니, 어머니의 취향과 제 취향이 꼭 같지가 않은 거예요."], ["The fact is, mother, that your tastes and mine are not exactly the same; you like silent beauty, whereas I like talking beauty, and then—”", "어머니는 말 없는 미인을 좋아하시고 저는 말하는 미인을 좋아하지요. 그리고—”"]], [["“Do you call Miss Robarts beautiful?”", "“너는 로바츠 양을 아름답다고 하느냐?”"]], [["“Yes, I do; very beautiful; she has the beauty that I admire.", "“예, 그렇게 말해요. 아주 아름다워요."], ["Good-bye now, mother; I shall not see you again before I start.", "제가 우러러보는 아름다움을 그 사람이 지녔어요. 그럼 이만 가겠어요, 어머니."], ["It will be no use writing, as I shall be away so short a time, and I don’t quite know where we shall be.", "떠나기 전에 다시 뵙지는 못할 거예요. 편지를 써도 소용없을 테지요."], ["I shall come down to Framley immediately I return, and shall learn from you how the land lies.", "떠나 있는 동안이 아주 짧고, 저희가 어디에 있을지도 잘 모르니까요. 돌아오는 대로 프램리로 내려가서 사정이 어떻게 돌아가는지 어머니께 듣겠어요."], ["I have told you my wishes, and you will consider how far you think it right to fall in with them.”", "제 바람은 다 말씀드렸으니, 어디까지 그 바람에 맞춰 주는 것이 옳겠는지는 어머니가 헤아려 보세요.”"], ["He then kissed her, and without waiting for her reply he took his leave.", "그러고서 그는 어머니에게 입을 맞추고, 대답을 기다리지도 않은 채 자리를 떠났다."]], [["Poor Lady Lufton, when she was left to herself, felt that her head was going round and round.", "딱한 레이디 러프턴은 혼자 남겨지자 머리가 뱅뱅 도는 것 같았다."], ["Was this to be the end of all her ambition,—of all her love for her son?", "이것이 제 온갖 뜻의, 아들을 향한 온갖 사랑의 끝이란 말인가?"], ["and was this to be the result of all her kindness to the Robartses?", "이것이 로바츠네에게 베푼 온갖 친절의 결말이란 말인가?"], ["She almost hated Mark Robarts as she reflected that she had been the means of bringing him and his sister to Framley.", "그와 그의 누이를 프램리로 데려온 것이 바로 자기였음을 되짚으면서 그녀는 마크 로바츠가 거의 미웠다."], ["She thought over all his sins, his absences from the parish, his visit to Gatherum Castle, his dealings with reference to that farm which was to have been sold, his hunting, and then his acceptance of that stall, given, as she had been told, through the Omnium interest.", "그가 지은 죄를 하나하나 떠올렸다. 교구를 비운 일, 개서럼 성에 다녀온 일, 팔리게 되어 있던 그 농장을 두고 벌인 거래, 사냥, 그리고 들은 바로는 옴니엄 쪽의 힘을 빌려 얻었다는 그 참사회원 자리를 받은 일."], ["How could she love him at such a moment as this?", "이런 순간에 어떻게 그를 사랑할 수 있겠는가?"], ["And then she thought of his wife.", "이어서 그녀는 그의 아내를 생각했다."], ["Could it be possible that Fanny Robarts, her own friend Fanny, would be so untrue to her as to lend any assistance to such a marriage as this; as not to use all her power in preventing it?", "패니 로바츠가, 제 벗 패니가, 이런 혼인에 손을 보태다니, 그것을 막는 데 온 힘을 쓰지 않다니, 그렇게까지 저를 배신할 수가 있단 말인가?"], ["She had spoken to Fanny on this very subject,—not fearing for her son, but with a general idea of the impropriety of intimacies between such girls as Lucy and such men as Lord Lufton, and then Fanny had agreed with her.", "그녀는 바로 이 문제를 두고 패니에게 말한 적이 있었다. 아들을 두고 두려워서가 아니라, 루시 같은 처녀와 러프턴 경 같은 사내 사이의 친밀함이 온당치 못하다는 두루뭉술한 생각에서였는데, 그때 패니도 그 말에 맞장구를 쳤다."], ["Could it be possible that even she must be regarded as an enemy?", "그 패니마저 원수로 여겨야 한다는 말인가?"]], [["And then by degrees Lady Lufton began to reflect what steps she had better take.", "그러고서 차차 레이디 러프턴은 어떤 걸음을 밟는 것이 좋을지 헤아리기 시작했다."], ["In the first place, should she give in at once, and consent to the marriage?", "첫째로, 당장 물러서서 이 혼인을 승낙해야 할까?"], ["The only thing quite certain to her was this, that life would be not worth having if she were forced into a permanent quarrel with her son.", "그녀에게 아주 분명한 것은 오직 하나, 아들과 영영 다투는 처지로 몰린다면 사는 것이 값없어진다는 것뿐이었다."], ["Such an event would probably kill her.", "그런 일이 생기면 아마 그것이 그녀의 목숨을 앗아 갈 터였다."], ["When she read of quarrels in other noble families—and the accounts of such quarrels will sometimes, unfortunately, force themselves upon the attention of unwilling readers—she would hug herself, with a spirit that was almost pharisaical, reflecting that her destiny was not like that of others.", "다른 귀족 집안의 다툼에 관한 글을 읽을 때면—그런 다툼에 관한 이야기는 딱하게도 읽고 싶지 않은 독자의 눈에까지 더러 억지로 밀고 들어오는 법이다—그녀는 제 팔자가 남들 것과 같지 않음을 되새기며, 바리새인 같다 할 만한 마음으로 스스로를 껴안았다."], ["Such quarrels and hatreds between fathers and daughters, and mothers and sons, were in her eyes disreputable to all the persons concerned.", "아비와 딸 사이, 어미와 아들 사이의 그런 다툼과 미움은 그녀 눈에는 관계된 사람 모두에게 부끄러운 일이었다."], ["She had lived happily with her husband, comfortably with her neighbours, respectably with the world, and, above all things, affectionately with her children.", "그녀는 남편과 행복하게 살았고, 이웃과 편안하게 살았고, 세상 앞에 떳떳하게 살았고, 무엇보다도 자식들과 다정하게 살았다."], ["She spoke everywhere of Lord Lufton as though he were nearly perfect,—and in so speaking, she had not belied her convictions.", "그녀는 어디에서나 러프턴 경을 거의 흠 없는 사람인 양 이야기했으며, 그렇게 이야기하면서 제 믿음을 어긴 적이 없었다."], ["Under these circumstances, would not any marriage be better than a quarrel?", "이런 형편이라면 어떤 혼인이라도 다툼보다는 낫지 않겠는가?"]], [["But then, again, how much of the pride of her daily life would be destroyed by such a match as that!", "그러나 다시 생각하면, 그런 혼사로 하여 나날의 삶에서 그녀가 지녀 온 자랑이 얼마나 무너지겠는가!"], ["And might it not be within her power to prevent it without any quarrel?", "그리고 다투지 않고도 그것을 막는 일이 제 힘 안에 있지 않겠는가?"], ["That her son would be sick of such a chit as Lucy before he had been married to her six months—of that Lady Lufton entertained no doubt, and therefore her conscience would not be disquieted in disturbing the consummation of an arrangement so pernicious.", "루시 같은 계집애에게라면 혼인한 지 여섯 달도 못 되어 아들이 진력이 나고 말 것을 레이디 러프턴은 조금도 의심하지 않았으므로, 그토록 해로운 짓이 이루어지려는 것을 흩뜨리는 데에 그녀의 양심이 흔들릴 일은 없었다."], ["It was evident that the matter was not considered as settled even by her son; and also evident that he regarded the matter as being in some way dependent on his mother’s consent.", "아들 자신도 이 일을 정해진 것으로 여기지 않는 것이 뚜렷했고, 어떤 식으로든 어머니의 승낙에 걸린 일로 여기는 것도 뚜렷했다."], ["On the whole, might it not be better for her—better for them all—that she should think wholly of her duty, and not of the disagreeable results to which that duty might possibly lead?", "이러니저러니 해도, 제 소임만을 생각하고 그 소임이 불러올지도 모르는 언짢은 결과는 생각하지 않는 편이 그녀에게—그들 모두에게—낫지 않겠는가?"], ["It could not be her duty to accede to such an alliance; and therefore she would do her best to prevent it.", "그런 결합에 응하는 것이 그녀의 소임일 수는 없었다. 그러므로 그녀는 그것을 막으려 온 힘을 쓰기로 했다."], ["Such, at least, should be her attempt in the first instance.", "적어도 처음에는 그렇게 해 보아야 했다."]], [["Having so decided, she next resolved on her course of action.", "그렇게 정하고 나서 그녀는 다음으로 어떻게 움직일지를 작정했다."], ["Immediately on her arrival at Framley, she would send for Lucy Robarts, and use all her eloquence—and perhaps also a little of that stern dignity for which she was so remarkable—in explaining to that young lady how very wicked it was on her part to think of forcing herself into such a family as that of the Luftons.", "프램리에 이르는 대로 곧 루시 로바츠를 부르러 보내고, 러프턴 집안 같은 데에 억지로 끼어들려 하는 것이 그 처녀 쪽에서 얼마나 사악한 짓인지를 밝혀 주는 데에 온 말솜씨를—그리고 어쩌면 그녀를 그토록 남달라 보이게 하는 그 엄한 위엄도 얼마쯤—쓸 작정이었다."], ["She would explain to Lucy that no happiness could come of it, that people placed by misfortune above their sphere are always miserable; and, in short, make use of all those excellent moral lessons which are so customary on such occasions.", "그로부터 아무 행복도 나올 수 없다고, 불운으로 제 삶의 자리보다 높은 데 놓인 사람은 언제나 비참하다고 루시에게 설명하고, 요컨대 그런 자리에서 흔히 쓰이는 그 훌륭한 도덕상의 훈계를 다 끌어다 쓸 것이었다."], ["The morality might, perhaps, be thrown away; but Lady Lufton depended much on her dignified sternness.", "도덕은 어쩌면 헛일이 될지도 몰랐다. 그러나 레이디 러프턴은 제 위엄 있는 엄함에 많이 기대었다."], ["And then, having so resolved, she prepared for her journey home.", "그리하여 그렇게 마음을 굳힌 뒤 그녀는 집으로 갈 길을 차렸다."]], [["Very little had been said at Framley Parsonage about Lord Lufton’s offer after the departure of that gentleman; very little, at least, in Lucy’s presence.", "프램리 목사관에서는 그 신사가 떠난 뒤 러프턴 경의 청혼에 관해 별로 말이 없었다. 적어도 루시 앞에서는 별로 없었다."], ["That the parson and his wife should talk about it between themselves was a matter of course; but very few words were spoken on the matter either by or to Lucy.", "목사와 그 아내가 둘이서 그 이야기를 하는 것은 당연한 일이었으나, 루시가 그 일에 대해 말하는 것도, 루시에게 그 일을 말하는 것도 몇 마디에 지나지 않았다."], ["She was left to her own thoughts, and possibly to her own hopes.", "루시는 제 생각에, 어쩌면 제 바람에 홀로 맡겨져 있었다."]], [["And then other matters came up at Framley which turned the current of interest into other tracks.", "그러고 나서 프램리에는 관심의 물길을 다른 데로 돌려놓는 다른 일들이 생겼다."], ["In the first place there was the visit made by Mr. Sowerby to the Dragon of Wantly, and the consequent revelation made by Mark Robarts to his wife.", "우선 사워비 씨가 원틀리의 용에 찾아온 일이 있었고, 그로 말미암아 마크 로바츠가 아내에게 사실을 털어놓은 일이 있었다."], ["And while that latter subject was yet new, before Fanny and Lucy had as yet made up their minds as to all the little economies which might be practised in the household without serious detriment to the master’s comfort, news reached them that Mrs. Crawley of Hogglestock had been stricken with fever.", "그리고 그 뒷일이 아직 새로울 때, 주인의 안락을 크게 해치지 않고 살림에서 아낄 수 있는 자잘한 절약들을 두고 패니와 루시가 아직 마음을 정하지도 못했을 때, 호글스톡의 크롤리 부인이 열병에 걸렸다는 소식이 그들에게 닿았다."], ["Nothing of the kind could well be more dreadful than this.", "이런 종류의 일로 이보다 더 끔찍한 것은 좀처럼 없을 터였다."], ["To those who knew the family it seemed impossible that their most ordinary wants could be supplied if that courageous head were even for a day laid low; and then the poverty of poor Mr.", "그 집안을 아는 사람들에게는, 그 담대한 머리가 단 하루라도 자리에 눕는다면 가장 흔한 살림의 필요조차 채워질 길이 없어 보였다."], ["Crawley was such that the sad necessities of a sick bed could hardly be supplied without assistance.", "게다가 딱한 크롤리 씨의 가난이 어떠했던가 하면, 병상에 마땅히 있어야 할 슬픈 물건들조차 남의 도움 없이는 좀처럼 갖출 수 없는 형편이었다."]], [["“I will go over at once,”", "“제가 당장 건너가겠어요.”"], ["said Fanny.", "패니가 말했다."]], [["“My dear!”", "“여보!” 남편이 말했다."], ["said her husband, “it is typhus, and you must first think of the children.", "“발진티푸스야. 당신은 먼저 아이들을 생각해야 해."], ["I will go.”", "내가 가겠어.”"]], [["“What on earth could you do, Mark?”", "“당신이 대체 무얼 할 수 있어요, 마크?”"], ["said his wife.", "아내가 말했다."], ["“Men on such occasions are almost worse than useless; and then they are so much more liable to infection.”", "“그런 자리에서 사내는 쓸모없는 것보다 오히려 더 나쁘지요. 게다가 병에도 훨씬 쉽게 걸려요.”"]], [["“I have no children, nor am I a man,”", "“저는 아이도 없고 사내도 아니에요.”"], ["said Lucy, smiling; “for both of which exemptions I am thankful.", "루시가 웃으며 말했다. “두 가지가 다 면제되어서 고마운 일이지요."], ["I will go, and when I come back I will keep clear of the bairns.”", "제가 가겠어요. 그리고 돌아오면 아이들 곁에는 가지 않을게요.”"]], [["So it was settled, and Lucy started in the pony-carriage, carrying with her such things from the parsonage storehouse as were thought to be suitable to the wants of the sick lady at Hogglestock.", "그렇게 정해져서 루시는 조랑말 마차를 몰고 떠났다. 호글스톡의 병든 부인에게 필요하리라 여겨지는 것들을 목사관 곳간에서 챙겨 실었다."], ["When she arrived there, she made her way into the house, finding the door open, and not being able to obtain the assistance of the servant girl in ushering her in.", "그곳에 이르러 보니 문이 열려 있었고, 하녀가 나와 안으로 안내해 주기를 바랄 수도 없어서 그녀는 혼자 집 안으로 들어갔다."], ["In the parlour she found Grace Crawley, the eldest child, sitting demurely in her mother’s chair nursing an infant.", "거실에는 맏이인 그레이스 크롤리가 어머니의 의자에 얌전히 앉아 젖먹이를 안고 있었다."], ["She, Grace herself, was still a young child, but not the less, on this occasion of well-understood sorrow, did she go through her task not only with zeal but almost with solemnity.", "그레이스 자신도 아직 어린아이였으나, 무슨 슬픔인지 잘 알고 있는 이런 자리에서 그 아이는 제 일을 열심히, 거의 엄숙하다 할 만큼 해내고 있었다."], ["Her brother, a boy of six years old, was with her, and he had the care of another baby.", "여섯 살 난 사내아이인 남동생이 그 옆에 있었고, 그 아이는 또 다른 젖먹이를 맡고 있었다."], ["There they sat in a cluster, quiet, grave, and silent, attending on themselves, because it had been willed by fate that no one else should attend on them.", "아이들은 거기 한데 모여 앉아, 조용하고 무겁고 말없이 저희끼리 저희를 돌보고 있었다. 아무도 저희를 돌보지 않도록 운명이 정해 놓았기 때문이었다."]], [["“How is your mamma, dear Grace?”", "“우리 그레이스, 어머니는 어떠시니?”"], ["said Lucy, walking up to her, and holding out her hand.", "루시가 그 아이에게 다가가 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Poor mamma is very ill, indeed,”", "“불쌍한 엄마는 아주 많이 아파요.”"], ["said Grace.", "그레이스가 말했다."]], [["“And papa is very unhappy,”", "“그리고 아빠는 아주 슬퍼요.”"], ["said Bobby, the boy.", "사내아이 보비가 말했다."]], [["“I can’t get up because of baby,”", "“저는 아기 때문에 일어날 수가 없어요.”"], ["said Grace; “but Bobby can go and call papa out.”", "그레이스가 말했다. “하지만 보비가 가서 아빠를 불러낼 수 있어요.”"]], [["“I will knock at the door,”", "“내가 문을 두드리마.”"], ["said Lucy, and so saying she walked up to the bedroom door, and tapped against it lightly.", "루시가 그렇게 말하고 침실 문 앞으로 걸어가 가만히 두드렸다."], ["She repeated this for the third time before she was summoned in by a low hoarse voice, and then on entering she saw Mr. Crawley standing by the bedside with a book in his hand.", "세 번째로 그것을 되풀이하고서야 낮고 쉰 목소리가 그녀를 안으로 불렀고, 들어가 보니 크롤리 씨가 손에 책을 들고 침상 옆에 서 있었다."], ["He looked at her uncomfortably, in a manner which seemed to show that he was annoyed by this intrusion, and Lucy was aware that she had disturbed him while at prayers by the bedside of his wife.", "그는 이렇게 들이닥친 것이 언짢다는 듯한 낯빛으로 거북하게 그녀를 바라보았고, 루시는 자기가 아내의 침상 옆에서 기도하던 그를 방해했음을 알아차렸다."], ["He came across the room, however, and shook hands with her, and answered her inquiries in his ordinary grave and solemn voice.", "그러나 그는 방을 건너와 그녀와 악수를 하고, 여느 때와 같은 무겁고 엄숙한 목소리로 그녀의 물음에 답했다."]], [["“Mrs. Crawley is very ill,”", "“제 아내가 몹시 아픕니다.”"], ["he said, “very ill.", "그가 말했다. “몹시 아픕니다."], ["God has stricken us heavily, but His will be done.", "하느님께서 우리를 무겁게 치셨으나 그분의 뜻이 이루어질 것입니다."], ["But you had better not go to her, Miss Robarts.", "그러나 아내에게는 가시지 않는 것이 좋겠습니다, 로바츠 양."], ["It is typhus.”", "발진티푸스입니다.”"]], [["The caution, however, was too late; for Lucy was already by the bedside, and had taken the hand of the sick woman, which had been extended on the coverlid to greet her.", "그러나 그 주의는 너무 늦었다. 루시는 이미 침상 옆에 가 있었고, 자기를 맞으려고 이불 위로 내밀어진 병든 여인의 손을 잡고 있었다."], ["“Dear Miss Robarts,”", "“로바츠 양.” 약한 목소리가 말했다."], ["said a weak voice; “this is very good of you; but it makes me unhappy to see you here.”", "“참 고마운 일이에요. 하지만 여기서 아가씨를 보니 마음이 편치 않아요.”"]], [["Lucy lost no time in taking sundry matters into her own hands, and ascertaining what was most wanted in that wretched household.", "루시는 지체 없이 여러 가지 일을 제 손에 거두어들이고, 그 참혹한 살림에서 가장 절실한 것이 무엇인지 알아냈다."], ["For it was wretched enough.", "정말로 참혹하기 그지없었던 것이다."], ["Their only servant, a girl of sixteen, had been taken away by her mother as soon as it became known that Mrs. Crawley was ill with fever.", "하나뿐인 하녀, 열여섯 살 난 처녀는 크롤리 부인이 열병으로 앓아눕는다는 것이 알려지자마자 어미가 데려가 버렸다."], ["The poor mother, to give her her due, had promised to come down morning and evening herself, to do such work as might be done in an hour or so; but she could not, she said, leave her child to catch the fever.", "그 딱한 어미도 그만한 몫은 해서, 아침저녁으로 몸소 내려와 한 시간쯤에 해낼 만한 일은 해 주기로 약속했다. 그러나 제 아이를 열병에 걸리도록 놓아둘 수는 없다고 했다."], ["And now, at the period of Lucy’s visit, no step had been taken to procure a nurse, Mr.", "그리하여 루시가 찾아온 이 무렵까지 간병인을 구하는 걸음은 아무것도 밟지 않은 채였다."], ["Crawley having resolved to take upon himself the duties of that position.", "크롤리 씨가 그 자리의 소임을 제가 짊어지기로 작정해 버린 것이었다."], ["In his absolute ignorance of all sanatory measures, he had thrown himself on his knees to pray; and if prayers—true prayers—might succour his poor wife, of such succour she might be confident.", "위생상의 조처라는 것을 도무지 알지 못하는 그는 무릎을 꿇고 기도에 몸을 던졌다. 기도가—참된 기도가—그 딱한 아내를 도울 수 있다면 그 도움만은 아내가 마음 놓고 믿어도 좋았다."], ["Lucy, however, thought that other aid also was wanting to her.", "그러나 루시는 아내에게 다른 도움도 있어야 한다고 생각했다."]], [["“If you can do anything for us,” said Mrs.", "“저희를 위해 무언가 해 주실 수 있다면.”"], ["Crawley, “let it be for the poor children.”", "크롤리 부인이 말했다. “그 딱한 아이들을 위해 해 주세요.”"]], [["“I will have them all moved from this till you are better,”", "“부인이 나으실 때까지 아이들을 모두 여기서 옮겨 놓겠어요.”"], ["said Lucy, boldly.", "루시가 담대하게 말했다."]], [["“Moved!” said Mr.", "“옮긴다니요!”"], ["Crawley, who even now—even in his present strait—felt a repugnance to the idea that any one should relieve him of any portion of his burden.", "크롤리 씨가 말했다. 지금 이 순간에도—이런 궁지에 몰린 지금도—누가 제 짐의 한 자락이라도 덜어 준다는 생각에 그는 역겨움을 느꼈던 것이다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["said Lucy; “I am sure it will be better that you should lose them for a week or two, till Mrs.", "루시가 말했다."], ["Crawley may be able to leave her room.”", "“크롤리 부인이 방에서 나오실 수 있게 될 때까지 한두 주 아이들을 떼어 놓으시는 것이 나으리라고 저는 믿어요.”"]], [["“But where are they to go?”", "“하지만 어디로 간단 말입니까?”"], ["said he, very gloomily.", "그가 아주 침울하게 말했다."]], [["As to this Lucy was not as yet able to say anything.", "이 일에 대해서는 루시도 아직 아무 말을 할 수 없었다."], ["Indeed when she left Framley Parsonage there had been no time for discussion.", "실은 프램리 목사관을 떠날 때 의논할 겨를이 없었던 것이다."], ["She would go back and talk it all over with Fanny, and find out in what way the children might be best put out of danger.", "돌아가서 패니와 다 이야기해 보고, 아이들을 위험에서 빼내는 데 어떤 길이 가장 좋을지 알아볼 작정이었다."], ["Why should they not all be harboured at the parsonage, as soon as assurance could be felt that they were not tainted with the poison of the fever?", "아이들에게 열병의 독이 묻지 않았다는 확신이 서는 대로 모두 목사관에 거두는 것이 어째서 안 될 일이겠는가?"], ["An English lady of the right sort will do all things but one for a sick neighbour; but for no neighbour will she wittingly admit contagious sickness within the precincts of her own nursery.", "제대로 된 영국 부인이라면 병든 이웃을 위해 한 가지만 빼고 무엇이든 다 할 것이다. 그러나 어떤 이웃을 위해서도, 옮는 병을 제 집 아이 방의 울 안에 알고서 들이지는 않는다."]], [["Lucy unloaded her jellies and her febrifuges, Mr. Crawley frowning at her bitterly the while.", "루시가 젤리와 해열제를 내려놓는 동안 크롤리 씨는 씁쓸하게 이맛살을 찌푸리며 그것을 바라보았다."], ["It had come to this with him, that food had been brought into his house, as an act of charity, in his very presence, and in his heart of hearts he disliked Lucy Robarts in that she had brought it.", "그에게는 일이 이렇게까지 되고 말았다. 자선이라는 명목으로, 바로 제 눈앞에서, 먹을 것이 제 집으로 들어온 것이다."], ["He could not cause the jars and the pots to be replaced in the pony-carriage, as he would have done had the position of his wife been different.", "그리고 마음속 깊은 데서 그는 그것을 가져왔다는 그 점 때문에 루시 로바츠가 싫었다. 아내의 형편이 달랐다면 그는 단지와 항아리를 조랑말 마차에 다시 실어 놓게 했겠지만 그럴 수 없었다."], ["In her state it would have been barbarous to refuse them, and barbarous also to have created the fracas of a refusal; but each parcel that was introduced was an additional weight laid on the sore withers of his pride, till the total burden became almost intolerable.", "아내가 그런 처지에 있으니 그것들을 거절하는 것도 야만스러운 짓이고, 거절하여 소동을 일으키는 것 또한 야만스러운 짓이었다. 그러나 들어오는 꾸러미마다 그의 자존심이 헐어 버린 등마루에 얹히는 짐이 되었고, 마침내 그 짐 전체가 거의 견딜 수 없는 것이 되었다."], ["All this his wife saw and recognized even in her illness, and did make some slight ineffectual efforts to give him ease; but Lucy in her new power was ruthless, and the chicken to make the chicken-broth was taken out of the basket under his very nose.", "아내는 앓는 중에도 이 모든 것을 보고 알아차려, 그를 편하게 해 주려고 힘없는 애를 조금 써 보기도 했다. 그러나 새로 얻은 권한을 쥔 루시는 매정했고, 닭 국물을 끓일 닭이 바로 그의 코앞에서 바구니 밖으로 꺼내어졌다."]], [["But Lucy did not remain long.", "그러나 루시는 오래 머물지 않았다."], ["She had made up her mind what it behoved her to do herself, and she was soon ready to return to Framley.", "자기가 몸소 무엇을 해야 마땅한지 마음을 굳혔으므로 곧 프램리로 돌아갈 채비를 했다."], ["“I shall be back again, Mr.", "“다시 오겠어요, 크롤리 씨.”"], ["Crawley,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “probably this evening, and I shall stay with her till she is better.”", "“아마 오늘 저녁에요. 그리고 부인이 나으실 때까지 곁에 머물겠어요.”"], ["“Nurses don’t want rooms,” she went on to say, when Mr.", "“간병인에게는 방이 필요하지 않아요.”"], ["Crawley muttered something as to there being no bed-chamber.", "크롤리 씨가 내줄 침실이 없다는 말을 웅얼거리자 그녀는 말을 이었다."], ["“I shall make up some sort of a litter near her; you’ll see that I shall be very snug.”", "“부인 가까이에 무슨 자리라도 하나 마련하겠어요. 제가 얼마나 편안히 지내는지 보시게 될 거예요.”"], ["And then she got into the pony-chaise, and drove herself home.", "그러고서 그녀는 조랑말 이륜마차에 올라 스스로 몰고 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXV. THE STORY OF KING COPHETUA.", "# 제35장. 코페투아 왕 이야기"]], [["Lucy as she drove herself home had much as to which it was necessary that she should arouse her thoughts.", "루시는 손수 말을 몰아 집으로 돌아오는 동안 마음을 일으켜 헤아려야 할 일이 많았다."], ["That she would go back and nurse Mrs. Crawley through her fever she was resolved.", "다시 가서 열병에 걸린 크롤리 부인을 간호하겠다는 것은 이미 굳게 정한 바였다."], ["She was free agent enough to take so much on herself, and to feel sure that she could carry it through.", "그만한 일을 제 몸에 지우고 또 끝까지 해낼 수 있다고 믿을 만큼은 제 뜻대로 움직일 수 있는 처지였다."], ["But how was she to redeem her promise about the children?", "그러나 아이들에 관한 약속은 어떻게 지켜야 하나?"], ["Twenty plans ran through her mind, as to farm-houses in which they might be placed, or cottages which might be hired for them; but all these entailed the want of money; and at the present moment, were not all the inhabitants of the parsonage pledged to a dire economy?", "아이들을 맡길 만한 농가라든가 아이들을 위해 빌릴 만한 오두막을 두고 스무 가지 계획이 머릿속을 지나갔다. 그러나 그 모두가 돈이 없다는 벽에 걸렸다."], ["This use of the pony-carriage would have been illicit under any circumstances less pressing than the present, for it had been decided that the carriage, and even poor Puck himself, should be sold.", "게다가 지금으로서는 목사관에 사는 사람이라면 하나같이 지독한 절약을 맹세한 처지가 아닌가? 조랑말 마차를 이렇게 쓰는 것만 해도 지금보다 덜 급한 사정이었다면 어떤 경우에도 용납될 수 없는 일이었다."], ["She had, however, given her promise about the children, and though her own stock of money was very low, that promise should be redeemed.", "마차는, 딱한 퍽까지도 팔기로 이미 정해져 있었으니까. 그러나 아이들에 관한 약속은 제 입으로 한 것이었고, 제 주머니의 돈이 몹시 얄팍하기는 하나 그 약속은 지켜야 했다."]], [["When she reached the parsonage she was of course full of her schemes, but she found that another subject of interest had come up in her absence, which prevented her from obtaining the undivided attention of her sister-in-law to her present plans.", "목사관에 이르렀을 때 루시의 머리는 물론 제 계획으로 가득했으나, 제가 없는 동안에 또 다른 관심거리가 생겨 있어서 올케의 온 마음을 지금의 계획에 붙들어 둘 수가 없었다."], ["Lady Lufton had returned that day, and immediately on her return had sent up a note addressed to Miss Lucy Robarts, which note was in Fanny’s hands when Lucy stepped out of the pony-carriage.", "레이디 러프턴이 그날 돌아왔고, 돌아오는 길로 곧 루시 로바츠 양에게 부치는 쪽지를 목사관으로 올려 보냈던 것이다. 루시가 조랑말 마차에서 내릴 때 그 쪽지는 패니의 손에 들려 있었다."], ["The servant who brought it had asked for an answer, and a verbal answer had been sent, saying that Miss Robarts was away from home, and would herself send a reply when she returned.", "쪽지를 가져온 하인이 답을 청했으므로, 로바츠 양은 집에 없으니 돌아오면 몸소 답을 보내리라는 말이 구두로 전해졌다."], ["It cannot be denied that the colour came to Lucy’s face, and that her hand trembled when she took the note from Fanny in the drawing-room.", "응접실에서 패니에게서 그 쪽지를 받아 들 때 루시의 얼굴에 핏기가 몰리고 손이 떨렸다는 것은 부인할 수 없다. 그 쪽지에 무엇이 적혀 있느냐에 이 세상 모든 것이 걸려 있을 수도 있었다."], ["Everything in the world to her might depend on what that note contained; and yet she did not open it at once, but stood with it in her hand, and when Fanny pressed her on the subject, still endeavoured to bring back the conversation to the subject of Mrs. Crawley.", "그런데도 루시는 그것을 곧 뜯지 않고 손에 든 채 서 있었고, 패니가 그 이야기를 재촉해도 여전히 크롤리 부인 이야기로 말머리를 되돌리려 애썼다."]], [["But yet her mind was intent on the letter, and she had already augured ill from the handwriting and even from the words of the address.", "그러면서도 루시의 마음은 편지에 붙어 있었고, 필체에서, 나아가 겉봉에 적힌 낱말에서까지 이미 흉한 조짐을 읽어 냈다."], ["Had Lady Lufton intended to be propitious, she would have directed her letter to Miss Robarts, without the Christian name; so at least argued Lucy,—quite unconsciously, as one does argue in such matters.", "레이디 러프턴이 호의를 품을 작정이었다면 세례명은 빼고 그냥 로바츠 양이라고만 적었을 것이다 — 적어도 루시는 그렇게 따졌다. 이런 일에서 누구나 그러하듯 아주 무의식중에."], ["One forms half the conclusions of one’s life without any distinct knowledge that the premises have even passed through one’s mind.", "사람은 제 한 생에서 짓는 결론의 절반을, 그 전제가 제 머릿속을 지나갔다는 것조차 또렷이 알지 못한 채 짓는다."]], [["They were now alone together, as Mark was out.", "마크가 나가 있어서 두 사람은 이제 단둘이었다."]], [["“Won’t you open her letter?”", "“그분 편지를 열어 보지 않겠어요?”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, immediately; but, Fanny, I must speak to you about Mrs. Crawley first.", "“네, 곧 열어 볼게요."], ["I must go back there this evening, and stay there; I have promised to do so, and shall certainly keep my promise.", "그런데 패니, 크롤리 부인 이야기를 먼저 해야 해요. 나는 오늘 저녁에 그리로 다시 가서 거기 머물러야 해요."], ["I have promised also that the children shall be taken away, and we must arrange about that.", "그렇게 하겠다고 약속했으니 반드시 약속을 지킬 거예요. 아이들을 그 집에서 빼내 오겠다는 약속도 했으니 그 일도 정해 두어야 해요."], ["It is dreadful, the state she is in.", "그 댁 부인 형편이 참혹해요."], ["There is no one to see to her but Mr. Crawley, and the children are altogether left to themselves.”", "부인을 살펴 줄 사람이 크롤리 씨밖에 없고, 아이들은 아주 저희끼리 내버려져 있어요.”"]], [["“Do you mean that you are going back to stay?”", "“거기 머무르러 다시 간다는 말이에요?”"]], [["“Yes, certainly; I have made a distinct promise that I would do so.", "“네, 그래요. 그렇게 하겠다고 또렷이 약속했어요."], ["And about the children; could not you manage for the children, Fanny,—not perhaps in the house; at least not at first perhaps?”", "그리고 아이들 말인데, 패니가 아이들 일을 좀 어떻게 해 줄 수 없을까요 — 집 안은 아니라도, 적어도 처음에는 집 안이 아니어도 좋으니?”"], ["And yet during all the time that she was thus speaking and pleading for the Crawleys, she was endeavouring to imagine what might be the contents of that letter which she held between her fingers.", "그러면서도 이렇게 말하며 크롤리네를 위해 애원하는 내내, 루시는 손가락 사이에 든 그 편지에 무엇이 적혀 있을까를 그려 보고 있었다."]], [["“And is she so very ill?”", "“그렇게나 몹시 앓아요?”"], ["asked Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 물었다."]], [["“I cannot say how ill she may be, except this, that she certainly has typhus fever.", "“얼마나 앓는지는 나도 뭐라 말할 수 없어요. 다만 이것만은 확실해요, 발진티푸스에 걸렸어요."], ["They have had some doctor or doctor’s assistant from Silverbridge; but it seems to me that they are greatly in want of better advice.”", "실버브리지에서 의사인지 의사 조수인지가 와 보긴 했지만, 내가 보기에는 더 나은 진찰을 몹시 바라는 형편이에요.”"]], [["“But, Lucy, will you not read your letter?", "“그런데 루시, 편지를 읽지 않을 거예요?"], ["It is astonishing to me that you should be so indifferent about it.”", "그렇게 아무렇지도 않은 듯이 있으니 나는 놀랍기만 해요.”"]], [["Lucy was anything but indifferent, and now did proceed to tear the envelope.", "루시는 아무렇지도 않은 것과는 거리가 멀었고, 이제 봉투를 뜯기 시작했다."], ["The note was very short, and ran in these words,—", "쪽지는 아주 짧았고 이러한 말로 되어 있었다 —"]], [["MY DEAR MISS ROBARTS,—I am particularly anxious to see you, and shall feel much obliged to you if you can step over to me here, at Framley Court.", "친애하는 로바츠 양,—꼭 만나 보고 싶은 일이 있으니 이곳 프램리 코트로 나에게 건너와 준다면 참으로 고맙겠어요."], ["I must apologize for taking this liberty with you, but you will probably feel that an interview here would suit us both better than one at the parsonage.", "이렇게 무례를 무릅쓰는 것은 용서해 주세요. 다만 목사관 쪽에서 만나기보다 이곳에서 만나는 편이 우리 둘 다에게 나으리라는 것은 로바츠 양도 아마 느낄 거예요."], ["Truly yours,", "그럼 이만 줄입니다."]], [["“There; I am in for it now,”", "“자, 이제 나는 걸려들었어요.”"], ["said Lucy, handing the note over to Mrs. Robarts.", "루시가 쪽지를 로바츠 부인에게 건네며 말했다."], ["“I shall have to be talked to as never poor girl was talked to before; and when one thinks of what I have done, it is hard.”", "“딱한 처녀가 여태 들어 본 적 없는 말을 실컷 들어야 하겠지요. 그런데 내가 한 일을 생각하면 억울해요.”"]], [["“Yes; and of what you have not done.”", "“그래요, 그리고 루시가 하지 않은 일도요.”"]], [["“Exactly; and of what I have not done.", "“바로 그거예요. 내가 하지 않은 일도."], ["But I suppose I must go,”", "하지만 가야 하겠지요.”"], ["and she proceeded to re-tie the strings of her bonnet, which she had loosened.", "그러고는 아까 풀어 놓았던 보닛 끈을 다시 매기 시작했다."]], [["“Do you mean that you are going over at once?”", "“지금 곧 건너간다는 말이에요?”"]], [["“Yes; immediately.", "“네, 지금 곧."], ["Why not?", "왜 안 되나요?"], ["it will be better to have it over, and then I can go to the Crawleys.", "치러 버리는 게 나아요. 그러고 나서 크롤리네로 갈 수 있고요."], ["But, Fanny, the pity of it is that I know it all as well as though it had been already spoken; and what good can there be in my having to endure it?", "그런데 패니, 딱한 것은 아직 한마디도 나오지 않았는데 나는 그것을 벌써 다 알고 있다는 거예요. 그러니 그걸 굳이 견뎌 낸다고 무슨 좋은 일이 있겠어요?"], ["Can’t you fancy the tone in which she will explain to me the conventional inconveniences which arose when King Cophetua would marry the beggar’s daughter?", "코페투아 왕이 거지의 딸과 혼인하려 했을 때 생겨난 세속의 불편들을 그분이 나에게 어떤 어조로 풀어 놓으실지 그려지지 않아요?"], ["how she will explain what Griselda went through;—not the archdeacon’s daughter, but the other Griselda?”", "그리젤다가 무슨 일을 겪었는지를 — 부주교의 딸이 아니라 그 다른 그리젤다 말이에요 — 어떻게 풀어 놓으실지도요?”"]], [["“But it all came right with her.”", "“하지만 그 그리젤다는 다 잘되지 않았나요.”"]], [["“Yes; but then I am not Griselda, and she will explain how it would certainly all go wrong with me.", "“그랬지요. 하지만 나는 그리젤다가 아니고, 그분은 나라면 틀림없이 다 잘못될 것이라고 풀어 놓으실 거예요."], ["But what’s the good when I know it all beforehand?", "그런데 그걸 내가 미리 다 알고 있으니 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?"], ["Have I not desired King Cophetua to take himself and sceptre elsewhere?”", "나는 이미 코페투아 왕더러 제 몸과 왕홀을 딴 데로 가져가시라고 청하지 않았나요?”"]], [["And then she started, having first said another word or two about the Crawley children, and obtained a promise of Puck and the pony-carriage for the afternoon.", "그러고 나서 루시는 크롤리네 아이들 이야기를 한두 마디 더 하고 오후에 퍽과 조랑말 마차를 쓰게 해 준다는 약속을 받아 낸 뒤에 길을 나섰다."], ["It was also almost agreed that Puck on his return to Framley should bring back the four children with him; but on this subject it was necessary that Mark should be consulted.", "퍽이 프램리로 돌아오는 길에 네 아이를 모두 데리고 오는 것으로 거의 이야기가 되었으나, 이 일은 마크와 의논해야 했다. 지금 세운 계획은 집 바깥에 아이들 방을 마련하는 것이었다."], ["The present scheme was to prepare for them a room outside the house, once the dairy, at present occupied by the groom and his wife; and to bring them into the house as soon as it was manifest that there was no danger from infection.", "한때 우유 방이던 곳으로, 지금은 마부와 그 아내가 쓰고 있었다. 그리고 전염될 위험이 없다는 것이 분명해지는 대로 아이들을 집 안으로 들이는 것이었다."], ["But all this was to be matter for deliberation.", "그러나 이 모두가 더 헤아려 볼 일이었다."]], [["Fanny wanted her to send over a note, in reply to Lady Lufton’s, as harbinger of her coming; but Lucy marched off, hardly answering this proposition.", "패니는 루시가 온다는 것을 미리 알리는 뜻으로 레이디 러프턴에게 답장을 보내기를 바랐으나, 루시는 그 제안에 거의 답하지도 않고 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["“What’s the use of such a deal of ceremony?”", "“그런 격식을 그렇게 많이 차려서 무슨 소용이 있어요?”"], ["she said.", "루시가 말했다."], ["“I know she’s at home; and if she is not, I shall only lose ten minutes in going.”", "“그분이 집에 계신 걸 나는 알아요. 안 계시더라도 다녀오는 데 십 분 잃을 뿐이지요.”"], ["And so she went, and on reaching the door of Framley Court house found that her ladyship was at home.", "그러고는 갔다. 프램리 코트 저택 문 앞에 이르니 마님은 집에 있었다."], ["Her heart almost came to her mouth as she was told so, and then, in two minutes’ time, she found herself in the little room upstairs.", "그렇다는 말을 듣자 심장이 목구멍까지 치밀어 올랐고, 그러고 나서 이 분이 채 지나기 전에 루시는 위층 그 작은 방에 있는 제 몸을 발견했다."], ["In that little room we found ourselves once before,—you, and I, O my reader;—but Lucy had never before visited that hallowed precinct.", "그 작은 방에서 우리는 전에 한 번 함께 있어 보았다 — 그대와 나, 아 독자여. 그러나 루시는 그 신성한 구역에 한 번도 들어와 본 적이 없었다."], ["There was something in its air calculated to inspire awe in those who first saw Lady Lufton sitting bolt upright in the cane-bottomed arm-chair, which she always occupied when at work at her books and papers; and this she knew when she determined to receive Lucy in that apartment.", "레이디 러프턴이 장부와 서류를 들여다볼 때면 늘 앉는, 밑을 등나무로 짠 그 안락의자에 꼿꼿이 앉아 있는 모습을 처음 보는 사람의 마음에 외경을 불러일으키도록 꾸며진 무엇인가가 그 방의 공기에는 있었다. 그리고 루시를 이 방에서 맞기로 정할 때 마님은 그것을 알고 있었다."], ["But there was there another arm-chair, an easy, cozy chair, which stood by the fireside; and for those who had caught Lady Lufton napping in that chair of an afternoon, some of this awe had perhaps been dissipated.", "그러나 거기에는 또 하나의 안락의자, 난롯가에 놓인 편안하고 푹신한 의자도 있었으니, 어느 오후에 레이디 러프턴이 그 의자에서 졸고 있는 것을 본 적이 있는 사람에게는 이 외경도 얼마쯤 흩어졌을지 모른다."]], [["“Miss Robarts,”", "“로바츠 양.”"], ["she said, not rising from her chair, but holding out her hand to her visitor; “I am much obliged to you for having come over to me here.", "마님은 의자에서 일어서지는 않고 찾아온 손에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. “여기까지 나에게 건너와 주어 참 고마워요."], ["You, no doubt, are aware of the subject on which I wish to speak to you, and will agree with me that it is better that we should meet here than over at the parsonage.”", "내가 무슨 이야기를 하려는지는 로바츠 양도 틀림없이 알고 있겠지요. 그리고 목사관 쪽에서 만나기보다 여기서 만나는 편이 낫다는 데에는 나와 뜻이 같을 거예요.”"]], [["In answer to which Lucy merely bowed her head, and took her seat on the chair which had been prepared for her.", "이에 대해 루시는 그저 머리를 숙였고, 자기를 위해 놓여 있던 의자에 앉았다."]], [["“My son,”", "“내 아들이,”"], ["continued her ladyship, “has spoken to me on the subject of—I think I understand, Miss Robarts, that there has been no engagement between you and him?”", "마님이 말을 이었다. “그 일을 두고 나에게 이야기를 했어요 — 내가 알기로는, 로바츠 양, 로바츠 양과 아들 사이에 약조 같은 것은 없었지요?”"]], [["“None whatever,”", "“전혀 없었습니다.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."], ["“He made me an offer and I refused him.”", "“그분이 저에게 청혼을 하셨고 저는 거절했습니다.” 이 말을 루시는 아주 날카롭게 내뱉었다."], ["This she said very sharply;—more so undoubtedly than the circumstances required; and with a brusqueness that was injudicious as well as uncourteous.", "사정이 요구하는 것보다 훨씬 날카로웠음은 틀림없다. 그리고 무례하기도 하고 슬기롭지도 못한 무뚝뚝함이 실려 있었다."], ["But at the moment, she was thinking of her own position with reference to Lady Lufton—not to Lord Lufton; and of her feelings with reference to the lady—not to the gentleman.", "그러나 그 순간 루시가 생각하고 있던 것은 레이디 러프턴에 대한 제 처지였고 — 러프턴 경에 대한 처지가 아니었다. 또 그 부인에 대한 제 감정이었고 — 그 신사에 대한 감정이 아니었다."]], [["“Oh,”", "“아.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, a little startled by the manner of the communication.", "레이디 러프턴은 말을 전하는 태도에 조금 놀라 말했다."], ["“Then I am to understand that there is nothing now going on between you and my son;—that the whole affair is over?”", "“그러면 로바츠 양과 내 아들 사이에 지금은 아무것도 없다고 — 이 일이 온통 끝났다고 알아 두어도 되겠군요?”"]], [["“That depends entirely upon you.”", "“그것은 전적으로 마님께 달려 있습니다.”"]], [["“On me!", "“나에게?"], ["does it?”", "그런가요?”"]], [["“I do not know what your son may have told you, Lady Lufton.", "“아드님이 마님께 무슨 말씀을 드렸는지는 저도 모릅니다, 레이디 러프턴."], ["For myself, I do not care to have any secrets from you in this matter; and as he has spoken to you about it, I suppose that such is his wish also.", "저로서는 이 일에서 마님께 무엇을 숨길 마음이 조금도 없습니다. 그리고 그분이 마님께 이 일을 말씀드렸다면 그분의 뜻도 그러하리라고 생각합니다."], ["Am I right in presuming that he has spoken to you on the subject?”", "그분이 이 일을 마님께 말씀드렸다고 짚어도 틀리지 않겠습니까?”"]], [["“Yes, he has; and it is for that reason that I have taken the liberty of sending for you.”", "“그래요, 말했어요. 바로 그래서 내가 무례를 무릅쓰고 로바츠 양을 불렀지요.”"]], [["“And may I ask what he has told you?", "“그러면 그분이 무슨 말씀을 드렸는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?"], ["I mean, of course, as regards myself,”", "물론 저에 관한 것을 여쭙는 것입니다.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["Lady Lufton, before she answered this question, began to reflect that the young lady was taking too much of the initiative in this conversation, and was, in fact, playing the game in her own fashion, which was not at all in accordance with those motives which had induced Lady Lufton to send for her.", "레이디 러프턴은 이 물음에 답하기 전에, 이 젊은 아가씨가 이 대화에서 주도권을 너무 많이 쥐고 있다는 것, 실은 판을 제 방식대로 놀리고 있다는 것을 곰곰 생각하기 시작했다. 그것은 레이디 러프턴이 루시를 부르게 된 속뜻과는 조금도 맞지 않는 일이었다."]], [["“He has told me that he made you an offer of marriage,”", "“아들은 로바츠 양에게 혼인하자는 청을 했다고 나에게 말했어요.” 레이디 러프턴이 답했다."], ["replied Lady Lufton; “a matter which, of course, is very serious to me, as his mother; and I have thought, therefore, that I had better see you, and appeal to your own good sense and judgment and high feeling.", "“그 어미로서 나에게는 물론 아주 중대한 일이지요. 그래서 로바츠 양을 만나 보고, 로바츠 양 자신의 분별과 판단과 높은 마음에 호소하는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했어요."], ["Of course you are aware—”", "물론 로바츠 양도 알고 있겠지만—”"]], [["Now was coming the lecture to be illustrated by King Cophetua and Griselda, as Lucy had suggested to Mrs.", "루시가 로바츠 부인에게 미리 짐작해 말한 그대로, 코페투아 왕과 그리젤다를 예로 들어 가며 펼칠 훈계가 이제 오고 있었다."], ["Robarts; but she succeeded in stopping it for awhile.", "그러나 루시는 잠시 그것을 막아 내는 데 성공했다."]], [["“And did Lord Lufton tell you what was my answer?”", "“그러면 러프턴 경이 제 대답이 무엇이었는지도 마님께 말씀드렸습니까?”"]], [["“Not in words.", "“말로 하지는 않았어요."], ["But you yourself now say that you refused him; and I must express my admiration for your good—”", "하지만 로바츠 양 스스로 지금 거절했다고 말하고 있지요. 그러니 나는 로바츠 양의 그 훌륭한—”"]], [["“Wait half a moment, Lady Lufton.", "“잠깐만 기다려 주십시오, 레이디 러프턴."], ["Your son did make me an offer.", "아드님은 저에게 청혼을 하셨습니다."], ["He made it to me in person, up at the parsonage, and I then refused him;—foolishly, as I now believe, for I dearly love him.", "목사관에 올라와 몸소 저에게 하셨고 저는 그때 그분을 거절했습니다 — 이제 와 생각하면 어리석은 짓이었습니다. 저는 그분을 몹시 사랑하니까요."], ["But I did so from a mixture of feelings which I need not, perhaps, explain; that most prominent, no doubt, was a fear of your displeasure.", "그러나 굳이 설명하지 않아도 될 여러 감정이 섞여서 그리했습니다. 그 가운데 가장 두드러진 것은 마님의 노여움에 대한 두려움이었음이 틀림없습니다."], ["And then he came again, not to me but to my brother, and urged his suit to him.", "그러고 나서 그분은 다시 오셨습니다. 저에게가 아니라 제 오라비에게 오셔서 그에게 청을 밀어붙이셨습니다."], ["Nothing can have been kinder to me, more noble, more loving, more generous, than his conduct.", "그분의 처신보다 저에게 더 다정하고, 더 고귀하고, 더 사랑에 넘치고, 더 후한 것은 있을 수 없습니다."], ["At first I thought, when he was speaking to myself, that he was led on thoughtlessly to say all that he did say.", "처음, 그분이 저에게 직접 말씀하실 때에는 하시는 말씀을 모두 생각 없이 이끌려 하시는 것이라고 여겼습니다."], ["I did not trust his love, though I saw that he did trust it himself.", "그분 자신은 그 사랑을 믿는 것이 보였으나 저는 그 사랑을 믿지 않았습니다."], ["But I could not but trust it when he came again—to my brother, and made his proposal to him.", "그러나 그분이 다시 — 제 오라비에게 — 오셔서 그에게 청혼을 하셨을 때에는 믿지 않을 수 없었습니다."], ["I don’t know whether you will understand me, Lady Lufton; but a girl placed as I am feels ten times more assurance in such a tender of affection as that, than in one made to herself, at the spur of the moment, perhaps.", "마님께서 제 말을 알아들으실지 모르겠습니다. 그러나 저와 같은 자리에 놓인 처녀는, 어쩌면 그 자리의 충동으로 저에게 직접 건네지는 사랑의 말보다 그렇게 건네지는 사랑의 표시에서 열 배나 더한 확신을 느낍니다."], ["And then you must remember that I—I myself—I loved him from the first.", "그리고 마님께서 기억해 주셔야 할 것이 있습니다. 저는 — 저 자신은 — 처음부터 그분을 사랑했습니다."], ["I was foolish enough to think that I could know him and not love him.”", "그분을 알면서도 사랑하지 않을 수 있으리라고 생각할 만큼 저는 어리석었습니다.”"]], [["“I saw all that going on,”", "“나는 그 모든 일이 벌어지는 것을 다 보고 있었어요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, with a certain assumption of wisdom about her; “and took steps which I hoped would have put a stop to it in time.”", "레이디 러프턴이 어딘가 슬기로운 척하는 태로 말했다. “그리고 때를 놓치기 전에 그것을 그치게 하리라 바라며 손을 썼지요.”"]], [["“Everybody saw it.", "“모두가 보고 있었습니다."], ["It was a matter of course,”", "당연한 일이었지요.”"], ["said Lucy, destroying her ladyship’s wisdom at a blow.", "루시가 마님의 슬기를 한 방에 무너뜨리며 말했다."], ["“Well; I did learn to love him, not meaning to do so; and I do love him with all my heart.", "“그래요, 저는 그럴 뜻이 없었으면서도 그분을 사랑하게 되었습니다. 그리고 온 마음으로 그분을 사랑합니다."], ["It is no use my striving to think that I do not; and I could stand with him at the altar to-morrow and give him my hand, feeling that I was doing my duty by him, as a woman should do.", "사랑하지 않는다고 애써 생각해 보아도 소용이 없습니다. 저는 내일이라도 그분과 제단 앞에 서서 손을 내어 드릴 수 있고, 여자가 마땅히 해야 할 대로 그분에게 제 도리를 다하고 있다고 느낄 수 있습니다."], ["And now he has told you of his love, and I believe in that as I do in my own—”", "그리고 이제 그분은 마님께 그 사랑을 말씀드렸고, 저는 제 사랑을 믿는 것처럼 그 사랑을 믿습니다—”"], ["And then for a moment she paused.", "그러고는 잠시 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“But, my dear Miss Robarts—”", "“하지만 이봐요, 로바츠 양—”"], ["began Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 입을 열었다."]], [["Lucy, however, had now worked herself up into a condition of power, and would not allow her ladyship to interrupt her in her speech.", "그러나 루시는 이제 스스로 힘이 오른 상태에 이르러 있었고, 마님이 자기 말을 가로막게 두지 않았다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Lady Lufton; I shall have done directly, and then I will hear you.", "“죄송합니다, 레이디 러프턴. 곧 마치고 나서 마님 말씀을 듣겠습니다."], ["And so my brother came to me, not urging this suit, expressing no wish for such a marriage, but allowing me to judge for myself, and proposing that I should see your son again on the following morning.", "그리하여 제 오라비가 저에게 왔습니다. 이 청을 밀어붙이지도 않고, 그런 혼인을 바란다는 말도 하지 않고, 다만 제가 스스로 판단하도록 두면서 이튿날 아침에 아드님을 다시 만나 보라고 권했습니다."], ["Had I done so, I could not but have accepted him.", "만일 만났다면 저는 그분을 받아들이지 않을 수 없었을 것입니다."], ["Think of it, Lady Lufton.", "헤아려 보십시오, 레이디 러프턴."], ["How could I have done other than accept him, seeing that in my heart I had accepted his love already?”", "제 마음속에서 이미 그분의 사랑을 받아들여 놓고서 제가 어떻게 그분을 받아들이지 않을 수 있었겠습니까?”"]], [["“Well?”", "“그래서요?”"], ["said Lady Lufton, not wishing now to put in any speech of her own.", "레이디 러프턴은 이제 자기 말을 끼워 넣고 싶지 않아 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“I did not see him—I refused to do so—because I was a coward.", "“저는 그분을 만나지 않았습니다 — 만나기를 마다했습니다 — 제가 겁쟁이였기 때문입니다."], ["I could not endure to come into this house as your son’s wife, and be coldly looked on by your son’s mother.", "아드님의 아내로 이 집에 들어와 아드님 어머니의 차가운 눈길을 받는 것을 저는 견딜 수 없었습니다."], ["Much as I loved him, much as I do love him, dearly as I prize the generous offer which he came down here to repeat to me, I could not live with him to be made the object of your scorn.", "그분을 그토록 사랑했고, 지금도 그토록 사랑하고, 그분이 여기까지 내려와 저에게 되풀이해 주신 그 후한 청을 그토록 값지게 여기지만, 마님의 업신여김의 표적이 되면서 그분과 함께 살 수는 없었습니다."], ["I sent him word, therefore, that I would have him when you would ask me, and not before.”", "그래서 저는 마님께서 저를 청해 주실 때 그분을 맞겠고 그 전에는 아니라고 전갈을 보냈습니다.”"]], [["And then, having thus pleaded her cause—and pleaded as she believed the cause of her lover also—she ceased from speaking, and prepared herself to listen to the story of King Cophetua.", "그리하여 제 뜻을 다 변호하고 — 스스로 믿기로는 사랑하는 이의 뜻까지 함께 변호하고 — 루시는 말을 마쳤고, 코페투아 왕 이야기를 들을 준비를 했다."]], [["But Lady Lufton felt considerable difficulty in commencing her speech.", "그러나 레이디 러프턴은 말을 꺼내기가 여간 어렵지 않았다."], ["In the first place she was by no means a hard-hearted or a selfish woman; and were it not that her own son was concerned, and all the glory which was reflected upon her from her son, her sympathies would have been given to Lucy Robarts.", "첫째로 마님은 결코 매정하거나 저만 아는 여인이 아니었다. 그리고 제 아들이 걸린 일이 아니었다면, 또 아들에게서 제게로 비쳐 오는 그 온갖 영화가 걸린 일이 아니었다면 마님의 마음은 루시 로바츠 쪽에 갔을 것이다."], ["As it was, she did sympathize with her, and admire her, and to a certain extent like her.", "그런 처지에서도 마님은 루시를 딱하게 여기고 찬탄했고, 어느 정도는 좋아하기까지 했다."], ["She began also to understand what it was that had brought about her son’s love, and to feel that but for certain unfortunate concomitant circumstances the girl before her might have made a fitting Lady Lufton.", "무엇이 제 아들의 사랑을 불러왔는지도 알아지기 시작했고, 몇 가지 불행한 딸린 사정만 없다면 눈앞의 이 처녀가 걸맞은 레이디 러프턴이 될 수도 있었으리라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["Lucy had grown bigger in her eyes while sitting there and talking, and had lost much of that missish want of importance—that lack of social weight which Lady Lufton in her own opinion had always imputed to her.", "거기 앉아 이야기하는 동안 루시는 마님의 눈에 더 커졌고, 아가씨 티 나는 그 대수롭지 않음 — 마님이 제 나름으로 늘 루시에게 돌려 온 그 사회적 무게의 결여 — 을 많이 잃었다."], ["A girl that could thus speak up and explain her own position now, would be able to speak up and explain her own, and perhaps some other positions at any future time.", "이렇게 나서서 제 처지를 밝힐 수 있는 처녀라면 앞으로 어느 때에도 나서서 제 처지를, 또 어쩌면 다른 몇 사람의 처지까지 밝힐 수 있을 것이었다."]], [["But not for all or any of these reasons did Lady Lufton think of giving way.", "그러나 이런 이유 가운데 어느 것으로도, 또 그 모두로도 레이디 러프턴은 물러설 생각을 하지 않았다."], ["The power of making or marring this marriage was placed in her hands, as was very fitting, and that power it behoved her to use, as best she might use it, to her son’s advantage.", "이 혼인을 이루거나 깨뜨릴 힘이 지극히 마땅하게도 마님의 손에 놓여 있었고, 그 힘은 쓸 수 있는 한 가장 잘 써서 아들에게 이롭게 해야 할 것이었다."], ["Much as she might admire Lucy, she could not sacrifice her son to that admiration.", "루시를 아무리 찬탄한다 해도 그 찬탄에 아들을 바칠 수는 없었다."], ["The unfortunate concomitant circumstances still remained, and were of sufficient force, as she thought, to make such a marriage inexpedient.", "불행한 딸린 사정들은 여전히 그대로 남아 있었고, 마님의 생각으로는 그런 혼인을 온당치 못한 것으로 만들 만큼 힘이 셌다."], ["Lucy was the sister of a gentleman, who by his peculiar position as parish clergyman of Framley was unfitted to be the brother-in-law of the owner of Framley.", "루시는 어느 신사의 누이였는데, 그 신사는 프램리 교구 성직자라는 남다른 자리 때문에 프램리 주인의 처남이 되기에는 걸맞지 않았다."], ["Nobody liked clergymen better than Lady Lufton, or was more willing to live with them on terms of affectionate intimacy, but she could not get over the feeling that the clergyman of her own parish,—or of her son’s,—was a part of her own establishment, of her own appanage,—or of his,—and that it could not be well that Lord Lufton should marry among his own—dependants.", "레이디 러프턴만큼 성직자를 좋아하는 사람도 없고 그만큼 성직자들과 다정하게 어울려 살기를 기꺼워하는 사람도 없었으나, 마님은 제 교구의 — 또는 아들의 교구의 — 성직자란 제 살림의 한 부분, 제게 딸린 재산의 한 부분 — 또는 아들의 것의 한 부분 — 이라는 느낌을 떨쳐 낼 수 없었고, 러프턴 경이 제 집에 — 딸려 사는 사람들 가운데서 혼인하는 것이 좋을 수는 없다고 느꼈다."], ["Lady Lufton would not have used the word, but she did think it.", "레이디 러프턴이라면 그 낱말을 입에 담지는 않았겠지만 마음속으로는 분명 그렇게 생각했다."], ["And then, too, Lucy’s education had been so deficient.", "그리고 또 루시의 교육이 그토록 모자랐다."], ["She had had no one about her in early life accustomed to the ways of,—of what shall I say, without making Lady Lufton appear more worldly than she was?", "어릴 적에 루시 곁에는 — 무어라 말해야 좋을까, 레이디 러프턴을 실제보다 더 세속적인 사람으로 보이게 하지 않으면서? — 그런 것의 방식에 익은 사람이 아무도 없었다."], ["Lucy’s wants in this respect, not to be defined in words, had been exemplified by the very way in which she had just now stated her case.", "이 점에서 루시에게 모자란 것은 말로 딱히 집어낼 수 없는 것이나, 바로 방금 루시가 제 사정을 늘어놓은 그 방식에서 그대로 드러났다."], ["She had shown talent, good temper, and sound judgment; but there had been no quiet, no repose about her.", "루시는 재주와 좋은 성미와 건전한 판단을 보였다. 그러나 조용함이 없었고 가라앉음이 없었다."], ["The species of power in young ladies which Lady Lufton most admired was the vis inertiæ belonging to beautiful and dignified reticence; of this poor Lucy had none.", "레이디 러프턴이 젊은 여인에게서 가장 찬탄하는 힘의 종류는 아름답고 위엄 있는 말없음에 딸린 「비스 이네르티아이」였다. 딱한 루시에게는 그것이 조금도 없었다."], ["Then, too, she had no fortune, which, though a minor evil, was an evil; and she had no birth, in the high-life sense of the word, which was a greater evil.", "그리고 또 루시에게는 재산이 없었으니 그것은 작은 흠이기는 하나 흠이었다. 또 상류의 뜻으로 말하는 태생이 없었으니 그것은 더 큰 흠이었다."], ["And then, though her eyes had sparkled when she confessed her love, Lady Lufton was not prepared to admit that she was possessed of positive beauty.", "그리고 사랑을 고백할 때 눈이 반짝이기는 했으나, 레이디 러프턴은 루시가 뚜렷한 미모를 지녔다고 인정할 마음은 없었다."], ["Such were the unfortunate concomitant circumstances which still induced Lady Lufton to resolve that the match must be marred.", "레이디 러프턴이 이 혼사는 깨뜨려야 한다고 여전히 마음먹게 한 불행한 딸린 사정이란 이러한 것들이었다."]], [["But the performance of her part in this play was much more difficult than she had imagined, and she found herself obliged to sit silent for a minute or two, during which, however, Miss Robarts made no attempt at further speech.", "그러나 이 연극에서 제가 맡은 몫을 해내기는 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 어려웠고, 마님은 일이 분 동안 잠자코 앉아 있어야 했다. 그동안에도 로바츠 양은 더 말을 하려 들지 않았다."]], [["“I am greatly struck,”", "“이 일 전체에서 로바츠 양이 보여 준 훌륭한 분별에,” 레이디 러프턴이 마침내 말했다."], ["Lady Lufton said at last, “by the excellent sense you have displayed in the whole of this affair; and you must allow me to say, Miss Robarts, that I now regard you with very different feelings from those which I entertained when I left London.”", "“나는 크게 감동했어요. 그리고 이 말은 하게 해 주어야 해요, 로바츠 양, 나는 이제 런던을 떠날 때 품고 있던 것과는 아주 다른 마음으로 로바츠 양을 보고 있어요.”"], ["Upon this Lucy bowed her head, slightly but very stiffly; acknowledging rather the former censure implied than the present eulogium expressed.", "이 말에 루시는 머리를 조금, 그러나 아주 딱딱하게 숙였다. 지금 드러난 칭송보다 앞서 은근히 담겨 있던 책잡음을 오히려 알아들었다는 뜻이었다."]], [["“But my feelings,”", "“그러나 내 마음은,”"], ["continued Lady Lufton, “my strongest feelings in this matter, must be those of a mother.", "레이디 러프턴이 말을 이었다. “이 일에서 내 가장 강한 마음은 어미의 것이어야 해요."], ["What might be my conduct if such a marriage did take place, I need not now consider.", "그런 혼인이 정말로 이루어졌을 때 내가 어떻게 처신할지는 지금 헤아릴 일이 아니지요."], ["But I must confess that I should think such a marriage very—very ill-judged.", "하지만 그런 혼인은 아주 — 아주 잘못 짚은 것이라고 여긴다는 것은 털어놓아야겠어요."], ["A better hearted young man than Lord Lufton does not exist, nor one with better principles, or a deeper regard for his word; but he is exactly the man to be mistaken in any hurried outlook as to his future life.", "러프턴 경보다 마음이 나은 젊은이는 없고, 그보다 지킬 바가 나은 이도, 제가 한 말을 그보다 더 깊이 여기는 이도 없어요. 하지만 그 아이는 제 앞날을 급하게 내다보는 일이라면 바로 잘못 짚기 마련인 사람이에요."], ["Were you and he to become man and wife, such a marriage would tend to the happiness neither of him nor of you.”", "로바츠 양과 그 아이가 부부가 된다면, 그런 혼인은 그 아이의 행복에도 로바츠 양의 행복에도 이어지지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["It was clear that the whole lecture was now coming; and as Lucy had openly declared her own weakness, and thrown all the power of decision into the hands of Lady Lufton, she did not see why she should endure this.", "이제 그 훈계 전체가 오고 있는 것이 뻔했다. 그리고 루시는 제 약함을 이미 드러내 놓고 결정의 힘을 온통 레이디 러프턴의 손에 넘겨 놓았으니, 이것을 참고 있을 까닭이 없다고 보았다."]], [["“We need not argue about that, Lady Lufton,”", "“그 일을 두고는 다툴 필요가 없습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"], ["she said.", "루시가 말했다."], ["“I have told you the only circumstances under which I would marry your son; and you, at any rate, are safe.”", "“제가 어떤 사정에서라야 아드님과 혼인하겠는지는 이미 말씀드렸습니다. 그리고 적어도 마님께서는 안전하십니다.”"]], [["“No; I was not wishing to argue,”", "“아니에요, 나는 다툴 생각은 없었어요.”"], ["answered Lady Lufton, almost humbly; “but I was desirous of excusing myself to you, so that you should not think me cruel in withholding my consent.", "레이디 러프턴이 거의 겸손하게 답했다. “다만 로바츠 양에게 나를 변명하고 싶었어요."], ["I wished to make you believe that I was doing the best for my son.”", "내가 승낙을 하지 않는 것을 매정하다고 여기지 않도록 말이지요. 나는 아들을 위해 가장 좋은 일을 하고 있다는 것을 로바츠 양이 믿게 하고 싶었어요.”"]], [["“I am sure that you think you are, and therefore no excuse is necessary.”", "“그리 여기고 계신 것은 저도 잘 알고 있으니 변명은 필요하지 않습니다.”"]], [["“No; exactly; of course it is a matter of opinion, and I do think so.", "“그래요, 바로 그렇지요. 물론 이는 견해의 문제이고, 나는 그리 여기고 있어요."], ["I cannot believe that this marriage would make either of you happy, and therefore I should be very wrong to express my consent.”", "이 혼인이 두 사람 가운데 어느 쪽도 행복하게 해 주리라고는 믿을 수 없으니, 승낙한다고 말하는 것은 아주 잘못된 일이 될 거예요.”"]], [["“Then, Lady Lufton,”", "“그러면, 레이디 러프턴,”"], ["said Lucy, rising from her chair, “I suppose we have both now said what is necessary, and I will therefore wish you good-bye.”", "루시가 의자에서 일어서며 말했다. “이제 두 사람 다 해야 할 말은 한 것 같으니 저는 이만 물러가겠습니다.”"]], [["“Good-bye, Miss Robarts.", "“안녕히 가요, 로바츠 양."], ["I wish I could make you understand how very highly I regard your conduct in this matter.", "이 일에서 로바츠 양의 처신을 내가 얼마나 높이 보고 있는지 알아듣게 할 수 있었으면 좋겠어요."], ["It has been above all praise, and so I shall not hesitate to say when speaking of it to your relatives.”", "어떤 칭찬으로도 모자라니, 로바츠 양의 집안 사람들에게 이 일을 이야기할 때에도 그렇게 말하기를 망설이지 않겠어요.”"], ["This was disagreeable enough to Lucy, who cared but little for any praise which Lady Lufton might express to her relatives in this matter.", "이 말은 루시에게 여간 거북하지 않았다. 레이디 러프턴이 이 일을 두고 제 집안 사람들에게 어떤 칭찬을 하든 루시는 조금도 마음에 두지 않았으니까."], ["“And pray,”", "“그리고 부디,”"], ["continued Lady Lufton, “give my best love to Mrs.", "레이디 러프턴이 말을 이었다."], ["Robarts, and tell her that I shall hope to see her over here very soon, and Mr.", "“로바츠 부인에게 내 사랑을 잘 전해 주고, 아주 곧 여기서 만나기를 바란다고 말해 주어요."], ["Robarts also.", "로바츠 씨도요."], ["I would name a day for you all to dine, but perhaps it will be better that I should have a little talk with Fanny first.”", "다 함께 저녁을 먹을 날을 정해 두고 싶지만, 아마 내가 먼저 패니와 조금 이야기를 나누는 편이 나을 거예요.”"]], [["Lucy muttered something, which was intended to signify that any such dinner-party had better not be made up with the intention of including her, and then took her leave.", "루시는 무언가 중얼거렸는데, 그런 만찬 자리라면 자기를 넣을 작정으로는 차리지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 뜻이었다. 그러고는 물러 나왔다."], ["She had decidedly had the best of the interview, and there was a consciousness of this in her heart as she allowed Lady Lufton to shake hands with her.", "이 만남에서 루시가 단연 이겼고, 레이디 러프턴이 손을 잡게 내어 주는 동안 그 마음에는 그것을 아는 느낌이 있었다."], ["She had stopped her antagonist short on each occasion on which an attempt had been made to produce the homily which had been prepared, and during the interview had spoken probably three words for every one which her ladyship had been able to utter.", "미리 준비해 둔 설교를 꺼내려는 시도가 있을 때마다 루시는 그때마다 적수의 말을 잘라 세웠고, 만나 있는 동안 마님이 겨우 한 마디 할 때 세 마디쯤은 했다."], ["But, nevertheless, there was a bitter feeling of disappointment about her heart as she walked back home; and a feeling, also, that she herself had caused her own unhappiness.", "그러나 그러면서도 집으로 걸어 돌아가는 동안 그 가슴에는 쓰디쓴 낙심이 있었고, 제 불행을 제가 지었다는 느낌도 있었다."], ["Why should she have been so romantic and chivalrous and self-sacrificing, seeing that her romance and chivalry had all been to his detriment as well as to hers,—seeing that she sacrificed him as well as herself?", "무엇 때문에 그렇게 낭만적이고 의협스럽고 제 몸을 바치려 들었던가. 제 낭만과 의협이 제게도 해가 되고 그분에게도 해가 되었으니 — 제 몸만이 아니라 그분까지 바친 것이었으니?"], ["Why should she have been so anxious to play into Lady Lufton’s hands?", "무엇 때문에 그렇게 레이디 러프턴의 손에 판을 놀려 주려고 애를 썼던가?"], ["It was not because she thought it right, as a general social rule, that a lady should refuse a gentleman’s hand, unless the gentleman’s mother were a consenting party to the marriage.", "신사의 어머니가 그 혼인에 승낙하는 편이 아니라면 처녀는 그 신사의 손을 마다해야 한다는 것을, 세상의 일반 규범으로서 옳다고 여겼기 때문은 아니었다."], ["She would have held any such doctrine as absurd.", "그런 교리라면 루시는 터무니없다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["The lady, she would have said, would have had to look to her own family and no further.", "처녀는 제 집안만 살피면 되고 그 이상은 아니라고 말했을 것이다."], ["It was not virtue but cowardice which had influenced her, and she had none of that solace which may come to us in misfortune from a consciousness that our own conduct has been blameless.", "루시를 움직인 것은 덕이 아니라 비겁이었고, 제 처신에 나무랄 데가 없었다는 자각에서 불행 속에 있는 우리에게 찾아오는 그 위로도 루시에게는 없었다."], ["Lady Lufton had inspired her with awe, and any such feeling on her part was mean, ignoble, and unbecoming the spirit with which she wished to think that she was endowed.", "레이디 러프턴은 루시의 마음에 외경을 불어넣었고, 제 안의 그런 느낌이란 제가 지녔다고 여기고 싶은 그 기개에는 천하고 비루하고 어울리지 않는 것이었다."], ["That was the accusation which she brought against herself, and it forbade her to feel any triumph as to the result of her interview.", "그것이 루시가 스스로에게 들이댄 죄목이었고, 그 죄목이 이 만남의 결말을 두고 어떠한 승리감도 느끼지 못하게 막았다."]], [["When she reached the parsonage, Mark was there, and they were of course expecting her.", "목사관에 이르니 마크가 있었고, 두 사람은 물론 루시를 기다리고 있었다."], ["“Well,”", "“그래,”"], ["said she, in her short, hurried manner, “is Puck ready again?", "루시가 짧고 급한 투로 말했다. “퍽은 다시 준비됐어요?"], ["I have no time to lose, and I must go and pack up a few things.", "나는 허비할 시간이 없어요. 가서 몇 가지 챙겨야 해요."], ["Have you settled about the children, Fanny?”", "아이들 일은 정했어요, 패니?”"]], [["“Yes; I will tell you directly; but you have seen Lady Lufton?”", "“네, 곧 말해 줄게요. 그런데 레이디 러프턴은 만났어요?”"]], [["“Seen her!", "“만났느냐고요?"], ["Oh, yes, of course I have seen her.", "아, 그럼요, 물론 만났지요."], ["Did she not send for me?", "그분이 나를 부르지 않았나요?"], ["and in that case it was not on the cards that I should disobey her.”", "그러니 내가 그분 말을 어긴다는 것은 있을 수 있는 일이 아니었지요.”"]], [["“And what did she say?”", "“그래서 뭐라고 하시더냐?”"]], [["“How green you are, Mark; and not only green, but impolite also, to make me repeat the story of my own disgrace.", "“마크는 어쩌면 그렇게 풋내기예요. 풋내기인 것만이 아니라 무례하기까지 해요, 내 망신 이야기를 나더러 되풀이하게 하니."], ["Of course she told me that she did not intend that I should marry my lord, her son; and of course I said that under those circumstances I should not think of doing such a thing.”", "물론 그분은 내가 그분의 아들이신 나리와 혼인하는 것을 바라지 않는다고 말씀하셨고, 물론 나는 그러시다면 그런 일은 생각도 하지 않겠다고 말씀드렸지요.”"]], [["“Lucy, I cannot understand you,”", "“루시, 나는 루시를 이해할 수가 없어요.”"], ["said Fanny, very gravely.", "패니가 아주 무겁게 말했다."], ["“I am sometimes inclined to doubt whether you have any deep feeling in the matter or not.", "“루시가 이 일에 깊은 마음을 두고 있는지 아닌지 이따금 의심스러워요."], ["If you have, how can you bring yourself to joke about it?”", "마음을 두고 있다면 어떻게 이 일을 두고 농을 할 수가 있어요?”"]], [["“Well, it is singular; and sometimes I doubt myself whether I have.", "“그래요, 이상한 일이에요. 나도 이따금 내가 마음을 두고 있는지 의심스러워요."], ["I ought to be pale, ought I not?", "나는 창백해져야 하겠지요?"], ["and very thin, and to go mad by degrees?", "그리고 아주 야위어서 차츰 미쳐 가야 하겠지요?"], ["I have not the least intention of doing anything of the kind, and, therefore, the matter is not worth any further notice.”", "나는 그런 짓을 할 생각이 조금도 없으니, 이 일은 더 눈여겨볼 값도 없어요.”"]], [["“But was she civil to you, Lucy?”", "“그런데 그분이 너에게 예의는 지키셨니, 루시?”"], ["asked Mark; “civil in her manner, you know?”", "마크가 물었다. “태도가 예의 있었느냐는 말이다.”"]], [["“Oh, uncommonly so.", "“아, 남다르게 그러셨지요."], ["You will hardly believe it, but she actually asked me to dine.", "믿기 어려우실 테지만 실제로 나에게 저녁을 먹으러 오라고 하셨어요."], ["She always does, you know, when she wants to show her good-humour.", "그분은 좋은 기분을 보이고 싶을 때면 늘 그러시잖아요."], ["If you’d broken your leg, and she wished to commiserate you, she’d ask you to dinner.”", "마크가 다리가 부러지고 그분이 그것을 딱하게 여기고 싶으면, 그분은 마크에게 저녁을 먹으러 오라고 하실 거예요.”"]], [["“I suppose she meant to be kind,”", "“그분은 다정하게 하려던 것이겠지요.”"], ["said Fanny, who was not disposed to give up her old friend, though she was quite ready to fight Lucy’s battle, if there were any occasion for a battle to be fought.", "패니가 말했다. 패니는 오랜 벗을 놓을 마음이 없었으나, 싸울 일이 생긴다면 루시를 위해 싸울 준비는 아주 되어 있었다."]], [["“Lucy is so perverse,”", "“루시가 하도 비뚤어져서,”"], ["said Mark, “that it is impossible to learn from her what really has taken place.”", "마크가 말했다. “정말 무슨 일이 있었는지 알아낼 도리가 없군.”"]], [["“Upon my word, then, you know it all as well as I can tell you.", "“참말이지, 그러면 마크도 내가 말해 줄 수 있는 만큼은 다 알고 있는 거예요."], ["She asked me if Lord Lufton had made me an offer.", "그분은 러프턴 경이 나에게 청혼을 했느냐고 물으셨어요."], ["I said, yes.", "나는 그렇다고 했어요."], ["She asked next, if I meant to accept it.", "그분은 다음으로 그것을 받아들일 생각이냐고 물으셨어요."], ["Not without her approval, I said.", "그분의 승낙 없이는 아니라고 나는 말했어요."], ["And then she asked us all to dinner.", "그러고 나서 그분은 우리 모두를 저녁 먹으러 오라고 하셨어요."], ["That is exactly what took place, and I cannot see that I have been perverse at all.”", "있었던 일은 바로 그것뿐이고, 나는 내가 비뚤어진 데가 조금도 없다고 봐요.”"], ["After that she threw herself into a chair, and Mark and Fanny stood looking at each other.", "그러고 나서 루시는 의자에 몸을 던졌고, 마크와 패니는 서서 서로 마주 보았다."]], [["“Mark,”", "“마크,”"], ["she said, after a while, “don’t be unkind to me.", "잠시 뒤에 루시가 말했다. “나에게 모질게 하지 마요."], ["I make as little of it as I can, for all our sakes.", "나는 우리 모두를 위해서 이 일을 될 수 있는 대로 작게 여기는 거예요."], ["It is better so, Fanny, than that I should go about moaning, like a sick cow;”", "이러는 게 나아요, 패니, 내가 병든 소처럼 신음하며 돌아다니는 것보다는.”"], ["and then they looked at her, and saw that the tears were already brimming over from her eyes.", "그러자 두 사람이 루시를 보았고, 벌써 눈에서 눈물이 넘쳐 나오고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Dearest, dearest Lucy,”", "“우리 루시, 우리 귀한 루시.”"], ["said Fanny, immediately going down on her knees before her, “I won’t be unkind to you again.”", "패니가 곧 루시 앞에 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다. “다시는 루시에게 모질게 굴지 않을게요.”"], ["And then they had a great cry together.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 함께 한참을 울었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVI. KIDNAPPING AT HOGGLESTOCK.", "# 제36장. 호글스톡에서 아이를 채어 오다"]], [["The great cry, however, did not take long, and Lucy was soon in the pony-carriage again.", "그러나 그 한바탕 큰 울음은 오래 걸리지 않았고, 루시는 곧 다시 조랑말 마차에 올라 있었다."], ["On this occasion her brother volunteered to drive her, and it was now understood that he was to bring back with him all the Crawley children.", "이번에는 오라비가 스스로 나서서 마차를 몰아 주기로 했고, 돌아올 때에는 크롤리네 아이들을 모두 데려오기로 하는 것이 이제 두 사람 사이의 양해였다."], ["The whole thing had been arranged; the groom and his wife were to be taken into the house, and the big bedroom across the yard, usually occupied by them, was to be converted into a quarantine hospital until such time as it might be safe to pull down the yellow flag.", "모든 것이 다 마련되어 있었다. 마부와 그 아내는 본채로 들이고, 마당 건너 그들이 늘 쓰던 큰 침실은 노란 깃발을 내려도 좋을 만큼 안전해질 때까지 격리 병실로 바꾸어 쓸 작정이었다."], ["They were about half way on their road to Hogglestock when they were overtaken by a man on horseback, whom, when he came up beside them, Mr. Robarts recognized as Dr.", "두 사람이 호글스톡으로 가는 길을 절반쯤 왔을 때 말을 탄 사람 하나가 뒤에서 따라붙었는데, 그가 옆으로 나란히 올라섰을 때 로바츠 씨는 그가 애러빈 박사, 곧 바체스터의 수석 사제이며 자기가 속한 성당 참사회의 수장임을 알아보았다."], ["Arabin, Dean of Barchester, and head of the chapter to which he himself belonged.", "수석 사제 또한 호글스톡으로 가는 길임이 곧 드러났다."], ["It immediately appeared that the dean also was going to Hogglestock, having heard of the misfortune that had befallen his friends there; he had, he said, started as soon as the news reached him, in order that he might ascertain how best he might render assistance.", "거기 있는 벗들에게 닥친 불행을 전해 들은 것이었다. 그는 소식이 자기에게 이르자마자, 어떻게 하면 도움을 가장 잘 베풀 수 있을지 알아보려고 곧바로 길을 나섰다고 말했다."], ["To effect this he had undertaken a ride of nearly forty miles, and explained that he did not expect to reach home again much before midnight.", "그러기 위하여 사십 마일에 가까운 말길을 감행한 것이었고, 집에 다시 이르는 것은 자정 무렵이나 되어야 할 듯하다고 이야기했다."]], [["“You pass by Framley?”", "“프램리를 지나가시지요?”"], ["said Robarts.", "로바츠가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I do,”", "“그래요, 지나갑니다.”"], ["said the dean.", "수석 사제가 말했다."]], [["“Then of course you will dine with us as you go home; you and your horse also, which will be quite as important.”", "“그러시면 돌아가시는 길에 저희 집에서 식사를 하셔야 하겠습니다. 사제님도, 사제님의 말도요."], ["This having been duly settled, and the proper ceremony of introduction having taken place between the dean and Lucy, they proceeded to discuss the character of Mr.", "말이 그만큼 중요할 테니까요.”"], ["Crawley.", "이것이 제대로 정해지고, 수석 사제와 루시 사이에 마땅한 소개의 절차가 이루어진 뒤에 그들은 크롤리 씨의 성품에 대하여 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“I have known him all my life,”", "“나는 그를 평생 알고 지냈어요.” 수석 사제가 말했다."], ["said the dean, “having been at school and college with him, and for years since that I was on terms of the closest intimacy with him; but in spite of that, I do not know how to help him in his need.", "“학교와 대학을 그와 함께 다녔고, 그 뒤로도 여러 해 그와 가장 가까이 지냈지요. 그런데도 그가 궁할 때 어떻게 도와야 할지를 나는 모릅니다."], ["A prouder-hearted man I never met, or one less willing to share his sorrows with his friends.”", "그보다 마음에 자랑이 높은 사람은 만나 본 적이 없고, 제 슬픔을 벗들과 나누기를 그보다 덜 원하는 사람도 만나 본 적이 없어요.”"]], [["“I have often heard him speak of you,”", "“그가 사제님 말씀을 하는 것을 저는 여러 번 들었습니다.”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“One of the bitterest feelings I have is that a man so dear to me should live so near to me, and that I should see so little of him.", "“내가 품은 가장 쓰라린 마음 가운데 하나는, 나에게 그토록 소중한 사람이 나와 그토록 가까이 살면서 내가 그를 그토록 조금밖에 보지 못한다는 것이지요."], ["But what can I do?", "그러나 내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["He will not come to my house; and when I go to his he is angry with me because I wear a shovel hat and ride on horseback.”", "그는 내 집에 오지 않으려 합니다. 그리고 내가 그의 집에 가면, 내가 삽 모양 모자를 쓰고 말을 타고 왔다는 것 때문에 나에게 노여워하지요.”"]], [["“I should leave my hat and my horse at the borders of the last parish,”", "“저라면 모자와 말은 지나온 마지막 교구의 경계에 두고 오겠습니다.”"], ["said Lucy, timidly.", "루시가 조심스레 말했다."]], [["“Well; yes, certainly; one ought not to give offence even in such matters as that; but my coat and waistcoat would then be equally objectionable.", "“그렇지요, 정말 그래요. 그런 일에서라도 남의 마음을 거스르지 않아야 하지요."], ["I have changed,—in outward matters I mean,—and he has not.", "그러나 그러면 내 상의와 조끼가 똑같이 거슬릴 겁니다."], ["That irritates him, and unless I could be what I was in the old days, he will not look at me with the same eyes;”", "나는 변했고 — 겉으로 드러나는 일에서 말입니다 — 그는 변하지 않았어요. 그것이 그를 성나게 하는 것이고, 내가 옛날의 나로 돌아갈 수 없는 한 그는 나를 같은 눈으로 보지 않을 것입니다.”"], ["and then he rode on, in order, as he said, that the first pang of the interview might be over before Robarts and his sister came upon the scene.", "그러고서 그는 앞서 말을 몰아 나아갔는데, 로바츠와 그 누이가 그 자리에 이르기 전에 만남의 첫 아픔이 지나가 있게 하려는 것이라고 그는 말했다."]], [["Mr. Crawley was standing before his door, leaning over the little wooden railing, when the dean trotted up on his horse.", "수석 사제가 말을 몰아 다가섰을 때 크롤리 씨는 제 집 문 앞에 서서 작은 나무 난간에 몸을 기대고 있었다."], ["He had come out after hours of close watching to get a few mouthfuls of the sweet summer air, and as he stood there he held the youngest of his children in his arms.", "여러 시간 곁을 지키고 앉아 있다가 달콤한 여름 바람을 몇 모금 마시려고 나온 것이었고, 그렇게 선 채로 자식들 가운데 막내를 팔에 안고 있었다."], ["The poor little baby sat there, quiet indeed, but hardly happy.", "그 딱한 어린 젖먹이는 정말로 조용히 안겨 있었으나 즐거운 낯은 아니었다."], ["This father, though he loved his offspring with an affection as intense as that which human nature can supply, was not gifted with the knack of making children fond of him; for it is hardly more than a knack, that aptitude which some men have of gaining the good graces of the young.", "이 아버지는 사람의 성정이 낼 수 있는 만큼 뜨거운 정으로 제 자식들을 사랑하였으나, 아이들이 자기를 좋아하게 만드는 재간은 타고나지 못하였다. 어린것들의 마음에 드는 어떤 사람들의 그 재주야 재간이라 부를 것 이상이 못 되기 때문이다."], ["Such men are not always the best fathers or the safest guardians; but they carry about with them a certain duc ad me which children recognize, and which in three minutes upsets all the barriers between five and five-and-forty.", "그런 사람들이 늘 가장 좋은 아버지이거나 가장 든든한 보호자인 것은 아니다. 그러나 그들은 아이들이 알아채는 어떤 「두크 아드 메」를 몸에 지니고 다니고, 그것이 삼 분 안에 다섯 살과 마흔다섯 살 사이의 담을 죄다 허물어 놓는다."], ["But Mr. Crawley was a stern man, thinking ever of the souls and minds of his bairns—as a father should do; and thinking also that every season was fitted for operating on these souls and minds—as, perhaps, he should not have done either as a father or as a teacher.", "그러나 크롤리 씨는 준엄한 사람이어서, 아버지라면 마땅히 그러해야 하듯이 언제나 제 아이들의 영혼과 마음을 생각했고, 또한 아버지로서든 가르치는 자로서든 아마 그러지 않았어야 할 일이나, 그 영혼과 마음을 다루기에 어느 때고 알맞다고 여겼다."], ["And consequently his children avoided him when the choice was given them, thereby adding fresh wounds to his torn heart, but by no means quenching any of the great love with which he regarded them.", "그리하여 그의 아이들은 고를 수 있게 되면 그를 피했고, 그것이 이미 찢긴 그의 마음에 새 상처를 보태었으나, 그가 아이들을 향하여 품은 그 큰 사랑을 조금도 꺼뜨리지는 못하였다."]], [["He was standing there thus with a placid little baby in his arms—a baby placid enough, but one that would not kiss him eagerly, and stroke his face with her soft little hands, as he would have had her do—when he saw the dean coming towards him.", "그렇게 그는 얌전한 어린 젖먹이를 팔에 안고 서 있었다 — 얌전하기는 넉넉히 얌전하나, 그가 바라는 대로 그에게 반갑게 입 맞추고 부드러운 작은 손으로 그의 얼굴을 어루만져 주려고는 하지 않는 아이였다 — 그러다가 수석 사제가 자기 쪽으로 오는 것을 보았다."], ["He was sharp-sighted as a lynx out in the open air, though now obliged to pore over his well-fingered books with spectacles on his nose; and thus he knew his friend from a long distance, and had time to meditate the mode of his greeting.", "그는 이제 손때 묻은 책들을 코에 안경을 걸치고서야 들여다볼 수 있게 되었으나, 바깥 벌판에서는 살쾡이처럼 눈이 밝았다. 그리하여 멀리서부터 벗을 알아보았고, 어떤 식으로 맞이할지를 궁리할 겨를이 있었다."], ["He too doubtless had come, if not with jelly and chicken, then with money and advice;—with money and advice such as a thriving dean might offer to a poor brother clergyman; and Mr.", "이 사람 또한 젤리와 닭이 아니라면 돈과 조언을 가지고 온 것이 틀림없었다 — 잘되어 가는 수석 사제가 가난한 동료 성직자에게 내밀 만한 그런 돈과 그런 조언을."], ["Crawley, though no husband could possibly be more anxious for a wife’s safety than he was, immediately put his back up and began to bethink himself how these tenders might be rejected.", "그리하여 아내의 안전을 그보다 더 애타게 바라는 남편이 세상에 있을 수 없었음에도, 크롤리 씨는 곧바로 등을 곧추세우고 그 제안들을 어떻게 물리칠 수 있을지 궁리하기 시작했다."]], [["“How is she?”", "“부인은 어떤가?”"], ["were the first words which the dean spoke as he pulled up his horse close to the little gate, and put out his hand to take that of his friend.", "수석 사제가 작은 문 가까이에 말을 세우고 벗의 손을 잡으려 손을 내밀며 처음 한 말은 이것이었다."]], [["“How are you, Arabin?”", "“잘 있었나, 애러빈?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“It is very kind of you to come so far, seeing how much there is to keep you at Barchester.", "“바체스터에 자네를 붙들어 두는 일이 그토록 많은데 이렇게 멀리까지 와 주니 참으로 고맙네."], ["I cannot say that she is any better, but I do not know that she is worse.", "아내가 조금이라도 나아졌다고는 말할 수 없으나, 더 나빠졌는지도 나는 모르겠네."], ["Sometimes I fancy that she is delirious, though I hardly know.", "이따금은 헛소리를 하는 것 같기도 하네만, 그것도 잘 모르겠어."], ["At any rate her mind wanders, and then after that she sleeps.”", "아무튼 정신이 흐트러지고, 그러고 나면 잠이 드네.”"]], [["“But is the fever less?”", "“그러나 열은 좀 내렸나?”"]], [["“Sometimes less and sometimes more, I imagine.”", "“이따금은 내리고 이따금은 더 오르는 듯하네.”"]], [["“And the children?”", "“아이들은?”"]], [["“Poor things; they are well as yet.”", "“딱한 것들. 아직은 성하네.”"]], [["“They must be taken from this, Crawley, as a matter of course.”", "“아이들은 여기서 데려 내와야 하네, 크롤리. 그것이야 두말할 것 없는 일이지.”"]], [["Mr. Crawley fancied that there was a tone of authority in the dean’s advice, and immediately put himself into opposition.", "크롤리 씨는 수석 사제의 그 권고에 명령하는 투가 있다고 여겼고, 곧바로 반대편에 서 버렸다."]], [["“I do not know how that may be; I have not yet made up my mind.”", "“그것이 어떠할지는 나도 모르겠네. 나는 아직 마음을 정하지 않았어.”"]], [["“But, my dear Crawley—”", "“그러나 이보게 크롤리—”"]], [["“Providence does not admit of such removals in all cases,”", "“섭리가 모든 경우에 그런 옮김을 허락하는 것은 아니네.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Among the poorer classes the children must endure such perils.”", "“더 가난한 계층에서는 아이들이 그런 위험을 견뎌야 하는 것이야.”"]], [["“In many cases it is so,”", "“많은 경우에 그렇지.”"], ["said the dean, by no means inclined to make an argument of it at the present moment; “but in this case they need not.", "수석 사제는 지금 이 자리에서 그것을 다툼거리로 만들 마음이 조금도 없어 그렇게 말했다. “그러나 이번에는 그럴 필요가 없네."], ["You must allow me to make arrangements for sending for them, as of course your time is occupied here.”", "아이들을 데려가도록 내가 손을 쓰는 것을 자네가 허락해 주어야 하네. 자네 시간은 여기 붙들려 있을 것이 뻔하니 말이야.”"]], [["Miss Robarts, though she had mentioned her intention of staying with Mrs. Crawley, had said nothing of the Framley plan with reference to the children.", "로바츠 양은 크롤리 부인 곁에 머물겠다는 뜻은 말해 두었으나, 아이들에 관한 프램리 쪽 계획에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["“What you mean is that you intend to take the burden off my shoulders—in fact, to pay for them.", "“자네 말은 내 어깨에서 짐을 벗겨 주겠다는 것이겠지 — 실상은 아이들 비용을 대 주겠다는 것 아닌가."], ["I cannot allow that, Arabin.", "그것은 허락할 수 없네, 애러빈."], ["They must take the lot of their father and their mother, as it is proper that they should do.”", "아이들은 마땅히 그러해야 할 대로 제 아버지와 제 어머니의 몫을 같이 받아야 하네.”"]], [["Again the dean had no inclination for arguing, and thought it might be well to let the question of the children drop for a little while.", "이번에도 수석 사제는 다투고 싶은 마음이 없었고, 아이들 문제는 잠시 내려놓아 두는 것이 좋을지도 모른다고 생각했다."]], [["“And is there no nurse with her?”", "“그러면 곁에 간병하는 사람이 없나?”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“No, no; I am seeing to her myself at the present moment.", "“없네, 없어. 지금은 내가 몸소 보고 있네."], ["A woman will be here just now.”", "여자가 하나 곧 올 것이야.”"]], [["“What woman?”", "“어떤 여자인가?”"]], [["“Well; her name is Mrs.", "“그래, 이름은 스텁스 부인이라 하네."], ["Stubbs; she lives in the parish.", "이 교구에 사는 사람이야."], ["She will put the younger children to bed, and—and—but it’s no use troubling you with all that.", "아래 아이들을 재워 줄 것이고, 그리고— 그리고— 아니, 그런 것을 다 늘어놓아 자네를 번거롭게 할 것이 없네."], ["There was a young lady talked of coming, but no doubt she has found it too inconvenient.", "오겠다는 말이 있던 젊은 아가씨가 하나 있었네만, 아마도 너무 불편하다는 것을 알게 되었을 테지."], ["It will be better as it is.”", "이대로가 나을 것이네.”"]], [["“You mean Miss Robarts; she will be here directly; I passed her as I came here;”", "“로바츠 양 말인가. 그 아가씨는 곧 여기 올 것이네."], ["and as Dr.", "오는 길에 내가 앞질러 왔어.”"], ["Arabin was yet speaking, the noise of the carriage wheels was heard upon the road.", "그리고 애러빈 박사가 아직 말하고 있는 동안에 길 위에서 마차 바퀴 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“I will go in now,” said Mr.", "“나는 이제 들어가서 아직 자고 있는지 보아야겠네.”"], ["Crawley, “and see if she still sleeps;”", "크롤리 씨가 말했다."], ["and then he entered the house, leaving the dean at the door still seated upon his horse.", "그러고서 그는 수석 사제를 여전히 말 위에 앉은 채 문간에 남겨 두고 집으로 들어갔다."], ["“He will be afraid of the infection, and I will not ask him to come in,”", "‘저 사람은 전염을 겁낼 것이다."], ["said Mr. Crawley to himself.", "들어오라고 청하지 않으련다.’ 크롤리 씨는 속으로 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“I shall seem to be prying into his poverty, if I enter unasked,” said the dean to himself.", "‘청하지도 않았는데 들어가면 그의 가난을 엿보는 것처럼 보이겠지.’ 수석 사제는 속으로 그렇게 말했다."], ["And so he remained there till Puck, now acquainted with the locality, stopped at the door.", "그리하여 그는 이제 이 근방을 알게 된 퍽이 문 앞에 멈출 때까지 거기 그대로 있었다."]], [["“Have you not been in?”", "“안에 들어가 보지 않으셨습니까?”"], ["said Robarts.", "로바츠가 말했다."]], [["“No; Crawley has been at the door talking to me; he will be here directly, I suppose;”", "“아니오. 크롤리가 문간에 나와서 나와 이야기를 했지요."], ["and then Mark Robarts also prepared himself to wait till the master of the house should reappear.", "곧 나올 것 같군요.” 그러고서 마크 로바츠 또한 집주인이 다시 나타날 때까지 기다릴 채비를 했다."]], [["But Lucy had no such punctilious misgivings; she did not much care now whether she offended Mr. Crawley or no.", "그러나 루시에게는 그런 격식을 따지는 망설임이 없었다."], ["Her idea was to place herself by the sick woman’s bedside, and to send the four children away;—with their father’s consent if it might be; but certainly without it if that consent were withheld.", "이제 그녀는 크롤리 씨의 마음을 거스르든 아니든 그리 개의하지 않았다. 그녀의 생각은 앓는 부인의 침상 곁에 제 자리를 잡는 것, 그리고 네 아이를 떼어 보내는 것이었다."], ["So she got down from the carriage, and taking certain packages in her hand made her way direct into the house.", "될 수 있으면 아버지의 승낙을 얻어서, 그러나 그 승낙을 내주지 않는다면 승낙 없이라도 반드시 그렇게 하려는 것이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 마차에서 내려 꾸러미 몇 개를 손에 들고 곧장 집 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["“There’s a big bundle under the seat, Mark,”", "“마크, 자리 밑에 큰 보퉁이가 있어요.”"], ["she said; “I’ll come and fetch it directly, if you’ll drag it out.”", "그녀가 말했다. “그걸 꺼내 놓아 주면 내가 곧 와서 가져갈게요.”"]], [["For some five minutes the two dignitaries of the Church remained at the door, one on his cob and the other in his low carriage, saying a few words to each other and waiting till some one should again appear from the house.", "오 분쯤 교회의 두 고위 성직자는 문간에 머물러 있었다. 하나는 제 말 등에, 다른 하나는 낮은 마차에 앉아 서로 몇 마디를 나누며 누군가 다시 집에서 나오기를 기다렸다."], ["“It is all arranged, indeed it is,”", "“다 마련되어 있어요, 정말로 그래요.”"], ["were the first words which reached their ears, and these came from Lucy.", "그들의 귀에 먼저 이른 것은 이 말이었고, 그것은 루시에게서 나왔다. “조금도 번거로울 것이 없고 비용도 들지 않아요."], ["“There will be no trouble at all, and no expense, and they shall all come back as soon as Mrs. Crawley is able to get out of bed.”", "그리고 크롤리 부인이 침상에서 일어나실 수 있게 되면 곧 다 돌아올 거예요.”"]], [["“But, Miss Robarts, I can assure—” That was Mr.", "“그러나 로바츠 양, 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 것은—”"], ["Crawley’s voice, heard from him as he followed Miss Robarts to the door; but one of the elder children had then called him into the sick room, and Lucy was left to do her worst.", "로바츠 양의 뒤를 따라 문간까지 나오며 하는 크롤리 씨의 목소리였다. 그러나 그때 위의 아이들 가운데 하나가 그를 앓는 이의 방으로 불렀고, 루시는 마음껏 나쁜 짓을 할 수 있게 남겨졌다."]], [["“Are you going to take the children back with you?”", "“아이들을 데리고 돌아갈 생각인가요?”"], ["said the dean.", "수석 사제가 말했다."]], [["“Yes; Mrs. Robarts has prepared for them.”", "“네, 로바츠 부인이 아이들을 맞을 채비를 해 두었습니다.”"]], [["“You can take greater liberties with my friend here than I can.”", "“여기 있는 내 벗에게는 내가 하는 것보다 훨씬 마음대로 할 수 있는 모양이군요.”"]], [["“It is all my sister’s doing,”", "“다 제 누이가 한 일입니다.”"], ["said Robarts.", "로바츠가 말했다."], ["“Women are always bolder in such matters than men.”", "“이런 일에서는 여자들이 늘 남자보다 담대하지요.”"], ["And then Lucy reappeared, bringing Bobby with her, and one of the younger children.", "그러고서 루시가 보비와 아래 아이들 가운데 하나를 데리고 다시 나타났다."]], [["“Do not mind what he says,”", "“그이가 하는 말은 마음에 두지 마요.”"], ["said she, “but drive away when you have got them all.", "그녀가 말했다. “다 태우면 그냥 몰고 가요."], ["Tell Fanny I have put into the basket what things I could find, but they are very few.", "패니에게는 찾을 수 있는 것들을 바구니에 담아 넣었는데 얼마 되지 않는다고 전해 줘요."], ["She must borrow things for Grace from Mrs. Granger’s little girl”—(Mrs.", "그레이스에게 입힐 것은 그레인저 부인네 딸아이한테서 빌려야 할 거예요”"], ["Granger was the wife of a Framley farmer);—“and, Mark, turn Puck’s head round, so that you may be off in a moment.", "— (그레인저 부인은 프램리의 어느 농부의 아내였다) — “그리고 마크, 퍽의 머리를 돌려놓아서 곧바로 떠날 수 있게 해 둬요."], ["I’ll have Grace and the other one here directly.”", "그레이스와 나머지 하나는 내가 곧 데리고 나올게요.”"], ["And then, leaving her brother to pack Bobby and his little sister on the back part of the vehicle, she returned to her business in the house.", "그러고서 그녀는 보비와 그 어린 여동생을 마차 뒤쪽에 실어 앉히는 일을 오라비에게 맡겨 두고 집 안의 제 일로 돌아갔다."], ["She had just looked in at Mrs. Crawley’s bed, and finding her awake, had smiled on her, and deposited her bundle in token of her intended stay, and then, without speaking a word, had gone on her errand about the children.", "그녀는 방금 크롤리 부인의 침상을 들여다보고, 부인이 깨어 있는 것을 보고는 웃어 보이고, 여기 머물 뜻의 표로 제 보퉁이를 내려놓았고, 그러고는 한마디도 하지 않고 아이들에 관한 제 심부름을 하러 나섰던 것이다."], ["She had called to Grace to show her where she might find such things as were to be taken to Framley, and having explained to the bairns, as well as she might, the destiny which immediately awaited them, prepared them for their departure without saying a word to Mr. Crawley on the subject.", "그녀는 그레이스를 불러 프램리로 가져갈 것들이 어디 있는지 가르쳐 달라 하고, 아이들에게는 이제 곧 저희에게 닥칠 운명을 되도록 잘 설명해 주고서, 그 일에 대해서는 크롤리 씨에게 한마디도 하지 않은 채 떠날 채비를 시켰다."], ["Bobby and the elder of the two infants were stowed away safely in the back part of the carriage, where they allowed themselves to be placed without saying a word.", "보비와 두 젖먹이 가운데 위의 아이는 마차 뒤쪽에 안전하게 실렸고, 아이들은 한마디도 없이 그렇게 앉혀지는 것을 그대로 받아들였다."], ["They opened their eyes and stared at the dean, who sat by on his horse, and assented to such orders as Mr. Robarts gave them,—no doubt with much surprise, but nevertheless in absolute silence.", "두 아이는 눈을 크게 뜨고 옆에서 말 위에 앉아 있는 수석 사제를 쳐다보았고, 로바츠 씨가 내리는 지시에 고분고분 따랐다 — 틀림없이 몹시 놀랐겠으나, 그럼에도 아주 잠잠한 채로."]], [["“Now, Grace, be quick, there’s a dear,”", "“그레이스, 어서, 착하지.”"], ["said Lucy, returning with the infant in her arms.", "젖먹이를 팔에 안고 돌아오며 루시가 말했다."], ["“And, Grace, mind you are very careful about baby; and bring the basket; I’ll give it you when you are in.”", "“그리고 그레이스, 아기를 아주 조심해서 안아야 한다. 바구니도 가져오렴."], ["Grace and the other child were then packed on to the other seat, and a basket with children’s clothes put in on the top of them.", "네가 올라앉으면 내가 건네줄 테니.” 그리하여 그레이스와 나머지 아이가 다른 자리에 실렸고, 아이들 옷을 담은 바구니가 그 위에 얹혔다."], ["“That’ll do, Mark; good-bye; tell Fanny to be sure and send the day after to-morrow, and not to forget—”", "“그만하면 되겠어요, 마크. 잘 가요."], ["and then she whispered into her brother’s ear an injunction about certain dairy comforts which might not be spoken of in the hearing of Mr.", "패니에게 모레는 꼭 사람을 보내라고, 그리고 잊지 말라고 전해 줘요—” 그러고서 그녀는 오라비의 귀에, 크롤리 씨가 듣는 데서는 입에 올릴 수 없는 낙농막의 어떤 먹을거리에 관한 당부를 속삭였다."], ["Crawley.", "“잘 가라, 얘들아."], ["“Good-bye, dears; mind you are good children; you shall hear about mamma the day after to-morrow,”", "착한 아이들이 되어야 한다. 모레는 어머니 소식을 듣게 될 거야.”"], ["said Lucy; and Puck, admonished by a sound from his master’s voice, began to move just as Mr. Crawley reappeared at the house door.", "루시가 그렇게 말했고, 퍽은 주인의 목소리가 내는 소리에 이끌려 막 움직이기 시작했는데, 바로 그때 크롤리 씨가 집 문간에 다시 나타났다."]], [["“Oh, oh, stop!”", "“아, 아, 잠깐!”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Miss Robarts, you really had better not—”", "“로바츠 양, 정말로 그러지 않으시는 편이—”"]], [["“Go on, Mark,”", "“그냥 가요, 마크.”"], ["said Lucy, in a whisper, which, whether audible or not by Mr. Crawley, was heard very plainly by the dean.", "루시가 속삭이는 소리로 말했는데, 그 소리가 크롤리 씨에게 들렸는지는 알 수 없으나 수석 사제에게는 아주 또렷이 들렸다."], ["And Mark, who had slightly arrested Puck by the reins on the appearance of Mr.", "크롤리 씨가 나타나자 고삐를 당겨 퍽을 잠깐 세웠던 마크는 이제 채찍으로 그 성마른 작은 짐승을 건드렸다."], ["Crawley, now touched the impatient little beast with his whip; and the vehicle with its freight darted off rapidly, Puck shaking his head and going away with a tremendously quick short trot which soon separated Mr. Crawley from his family.", "그리하여 짐을 실은 마차는 쏜살같이 내달았고, 퍽은 머리를 흔들며 무섭게 빠르고 짧은 걸음으로 달려 나가 이내 크롤리 씨를 제 식구에게서 갈라 놓았다."]], [["“Miss Robarts,”", "“로바츠 양.”"], ["he began, “this step has been taken altogether without—”", "그가 말을 뗐다. “이 조처는 도무지 아무런—”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“네.”"], ["said she, interrupting him.", "그녀가 그의 말을 끊으며 말했다."], ["“My brother was obliged to return at once.", "“오라비가 곧 돌아가야 했어요. 아시겠지만 아이들은 목사관에서 다 함께 지낼 거예요."], ["The children, you know, will remain all together at the parsonage; and that, I think, is what Mrs. Crawley will best like.", "그것이 크롤리 부인께서 가장 좋아하실 일이라고 저는 생각해요."], ["In a day or two they will be under Mrs. Robarts’s own charge.”", "하루 이틀 지나면 아이들은 로바츠 부인이 몸소 맡아 돌보게 될 거예요.”"]], [["“But, my dear Miss Robarts, I had no intention whatever of putting the burden of my family on the shoulders of another person.", "“그러나 로바츠 양, 제 식구의 짐을 다른 사람의 어깨에 얹을 뜻은 제게 조금도 없었습니다."], ["They must return to their own home immediately—that is, as soon as they can be brought back.”", "아이들은 곧 제 집으로 돌아와야 합니다 — 그러니까, 데려올 수 있게 되는 대로 곧요.”"]], [["“I really think Miss Robarts has managed very well,”", "“내 생각에는 로바츠 양이 참으로 잘 처리해 주었네.”"], ["said the dean. “Mrs.", "수석 사제가 말했다."], ["Crawley must be so much more comfortable to think that they are out of danger.”", "“아이들이 위험에서 벗어났다고 생각하면 크롤리 부인의 마음이 훨씬 더 편할 것이야.”"]], [["“And they will be quite comfortable at the parsonage,”", "“그리고 아이들은 목사관에서 아주 편안할 거예요.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“I do not at all doubt that,”", "“그것은 조금도 의심하지 않습니다.”"], ["said Mr.", "크롤리 씨가 말했다."], ["Crawley; “but too much of such comforts will unfit them for their home; and—and I could have wished that I had been consulted more at leisure before the proceeding had been taken.”", "“그러나 그런 편안함이 지나치면 아이들이 제 집에 맞지 않게 되고 말 것입니다. 그리고 — 그리고 이 일이 치러지기 전에 좀 더 여유 있게 저와 의논해 주었으면 좋았겠다고 저는 생각합니다.”"]], [["“It was arranged, Mr. Crawley, when I was here before, that the children had better go away,”", "“크롤리 씨, 제가 앞서 여기 왔을 때 아이들을 떼어 놓는 편이 낫겠다고 정해졌었잖아요.”"], ["pleaded Lucy.", "루시가 사정하듯 말했다."]], [["“I do not remember agreeing to such a measure, Miss Robarts; however— I suppose they cannot be had back to-night?”", "“그런 조처에 제가 동의한 기억은 없습니다, 로바츠 양. 그러나— 오늘 밤에는 데려올 수 없겠지요?”"]], [["“No, not to-night,” said Lucy.", "“네, 오늘 밤은 안 되지요.”"], ["“And now I will go in to your wife.”", "루시가 말했다. “그러면 저는 이제 부인께 들어가 보겠어요.”"], ["And then she returned to the house, leaving the two gentlemen at the door.", "그러고서 그녀는 두 신사를 문간에 남겨 두고 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["At this moment a labourer’s boy came sauntering by, and the dean, obtaining possession of his services for the custody of his horse, was able to dismount and put himself on a more equal footing for conversation with his friend.", "그때 막벌이꾼의 아이 하나가 어슬렁어슬렁 지나갔고, 수석 사제는 그 아이를 붙들어 제 말을 맡기고서 말에서 내릴 수 있었으며, 그리하여 벗과 이야기하기에 좀 더 대등한 자리에 몸을 두게 되었다."]], [["“Crawley,”", "“크롤리.”"], ["said he, putting his hand affectionately on his friend’s shoulder, as they both stood leaning on the little rail before the door; “that is a good girl—a very good girl.”", "두 사람이 문 앞의 작은 난간에 함께 기대어 선 채로, 그가 벗의 어깨에 정답게 손을 얹으며 말했다. “좋은 아가씨야 — 아주 좋은 아가씨네.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“그래.”"], ["said he slowly; “she means well.”", "그가 느릿하게 말했다. “뜻은 좋지.”"]], [["“Nay, but she does well; she does excellently.", "“아니, 뜻만이 아니라 잘 하고 있네. 훌륭하게 하고 있어."], ["What can be better than her conduct now?", "지금 저 아가씨가 하는 처사보다 무엇이 더 나을 수 있겠나?"], ["While I was meditating how I might possibly assist your wife in this strait—”", "이 궁한 처지에서 자네 아내를 어떻게 도울 수 있을까 내가 궁리하고 있는 동안에—”"]], [["“I want no assistance; none, at least, from man,”", "“나는 도움을 바라지 않네. 적어도 사람에게서 받을 도움은 바라지 않아.”"], ["said Crawley, bitterly.", "크롤리가 쓰라리게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, my friend, think of what you are saying!", "“아, 이 사람아, 자네가 하는 말을 생각해 보게!"], ["Think of the wickedness which must accompany such a state of mind!", "그런 마음가짐에 반드시 따라붙는 사악함을 생각해 보게!"], ["Have you ever known any man able to walk alone, without assistance from his brother men?”", "형제 되는 사람들의 도움 없이 혼자 걸을 수 있는 사람을 자네가 여태 하나라도 본 적이 있나?”"]], [["Mr. Crawley did not make any immediate answer, but putting his arms behind his back and closing his hands, as was his wont when he walked alone thinking of the general bitterness of his lot in life, began to move slowly along the road in front of his house.", "크롤리 씨는 곧바로 대답을 하지 않고, 제 삶의 몫이 두루 쓰라리다는 것을 생각하며 혼자 걸을 때 늘 그러던 대로 두 팔을 등 뒤로 돌리고 두 손을 맞잡은 채, 집 앞 길을 따라 천천히 걸음을 옮기기 시작했다."], ["He did not invite the other to walk with him, but neither was there anything in his manner which seemed to indicate that he had intended to be left to himself.", "그는 상대에게 같이 걷자고 청하지는 않았으나, 혼자 있게 해 달라는 뜻으로 보이는 것 또한 그의 태도에 조금도 없었다. 아름다운 여름 오후였다."], ["It was a beautiful summer afternoon, at that delicious period of the year when summer has just burst forth from the growth of spring; when the summer is yet but three days old, and all the various shades of green which nature can put forth are still in their unsoiled purity of freshness.", "봄의 자라남에서 여름이 막 터져 나온, 한 해 가운데 그 달콤한 철이었다. 여름이 아직 사흘밖에 되지 않아, 자연이 내어 놓을 수 있는 온갖 결의 초록이 아직도 물들지 않은 순전한 싱그러움 속에 있는 때였다."], ["The apple blossoms were on the trees, and the hedges were sweet with May.", "나무들에는 사과꽃이 피어 있었고, 울타리는 오월의 산사꽃으로 향기로웠다."], ["The cuckoo at five o’clock was still sounding his soft summer call with unabated energy, and even the common grasses of the hedgerows were sweet with the fragrance of their new growth.", "다섯 시에도 뻐꾸기는 여전히 조금도 기운을 잃지 않고 부드러운 여름 노래를 울리고 있었으며, 울타리 밑 흔한 풀들까지도 새로 돋아난 향기로 향기로웠다."], ["The foliage of the oaks was complete, so that every bough and twig was clothed; but the leaves did not yet hang heavy in masses, and the bend of every bough and the tapering curve of every twig were visible through their light green covering.", "참나무의 잎은 다 갖추어져 큰 가지며 잔가지가 모두 옷을 입었으나, 잎이 아직 무더기로 무겁게 늘어지지는 않아 가지마다의 굽이와 잔가지마다의 가늘어지는 곡선이 그 연한 초록빛 덮개를 통하여 다 보였다."], ["There is no time of the year equal in beauty to the first week in summer; and no colour which nature gives, not even the gorgeous hues of autumn, which can equal the verdure produced by the first warm suns of May.", "한 해 가운데 여름의 첫 주만큼 아름다운 때는 없다. 그리고 자연이 내어 주는 어떤 빛깔도, 심지어 가을의 화려한 색조조차도 오월의 첫 따뜻한 햇볕이 빚어낸 그 푸르름과 견줄 수 없다."]], [["Hogglestock, as has been explained, has little to offer in the way of landskip beauty, and the clergyman’s house at Hogglestock was not placed on a green slopy bank of land, retired from the road, with its windows opening on to a lawn, surrounded by shrubs, with a view of the small church tower seen through them; it had none of that beauty which is so common to the cozy houses of our spiritual pastors in the agricultural parts of England.", "이미 설명한 대로 호글스톡에는 풍경의 아름다움이라 할 것이 별로 없고, 호글스톡의 성직자 집은 길에서 물러 앉은 푸르고 비스듬한 언덕 위에, 창이 잔디밭으로 열리고 관목에 둘러싸이고 그 사이로 작은 교회 탑이 보이는 그런 자리에 놓여 있지 않았다. 영국의 농사 짓는 고장에서 우리 영혼의 목자들이 사는 아늑한 집들에 그토록 흔한 그 아름다움이 그 집에는 하나도 없었다."], ["Hogglestock Parsonage stood bleak beside the road, with no pretty paling lined inside by hollies and laburnum, Portugal laurels and rose-trees.", "호글스톡 목사관은 길가에 을씨년스럽게 서 있었고, 안쪽으로 호랑가시나무와 금사슬나무, 포르투갈 월계수와 장미나무를 둘러 심은 예쁜 울짱 따위도 없었다."], ["But, nevertheless, even Hogglestock was pretty now.", "그러나 그럼에도 호글스톡조차 지금은 예뻤다."], ["There were apple-trees there covered with blossom, and the hedgerows were in full flower.", "꽃으로 덮인 사과나무들이 거기 있었고 울타리는 온통 꽃이었다."], ["There were thrushes singing, and here and there an oak-tree stood in the roadside, perfect in its solitary beauty.", "개똥지빠귀들이 노래하고 있었고, 길가에는 여기저기 참나무 한 그루씩이 홀로 선 아름다움 속에 오롯이 서 있었다."]], [["“Let us walk on a little,”", "“조금 더 걸어 보세.”"], ["said the dean.", "수석 사제가 말했다."], ["“Miss Robarts is with her now, and you will be better for leaving the room for a few minutes.”", "“지금은 로바츠 양이 부인 곁에 있으니, 자네도 몇 분 방을 떠나 있는 것이 나을 것이네.”"]], [["“No,”", "“아니.” 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “I must go back; I cannot leave that young lady to do my work.”", "“돌아가야 하네. 저 젊은 아가씨에게 내 일을 하도록 맡겨 둘 수는 없어.”"]], [["“Stop, Crawley!”", "“멈추게, 크롤리!”"], ["And the dean, putting his hand upon him, stayed him in the road.", "그러고서 수석 사제는 그에게 손을 얹어 길에서 그를 세웠다."], ["“She is doing her own work, and if you were speaking of her with reference to any other household than your own, you would say so.", "“그 아가씨는 자기 일을 하고 있는 것이네. 자네 집이 아닌 다른 집을 두고 그 아가씨를 말한다면 자네도 그렇게 말할 것이야."], ["Is it not a comfort to you to know that your wife has a woman near her at such a time as this; and a woman, too, who can speak to her as one lady does to another?”", "이런 때에 자네 아내 곁에 여자가 있다는 것을, 게다가 부인이 부인에게 말하듯 아내에게 말해 줄 수 있는 여자가 있다는 것을 아는 것이 자네에게 위로가 되지 않는가?”"]], [["“These are comforts which we have no right to expect.", "“이런 편안함은 우리가 바랄 권리가 없는 것이네."], ["I could not have done much for poor Mary; but what a man could have done should not have been wanting.”", "딱한 메리를 위해 내가 많은 것을 해 줄 수는 없었겠지만, 사람으로서 할 수 있는 일이라면 부족함이 없었을 것이야.”"]], [["“I am sure of it; I know it well.", "“그럴 것이라고 나는 확신하네. 잘 알고 있어."], ["What any man could do by himself you would do—excepting one thing.”", "사람이 혼자서 할 수 있는 일이라면 자네는 무엇이든 할 것이야 — 다만 한 가지만 빼고.”"], ["And the dean as he spoke looked full into the other’s face.", "그렇게 말하며 수석 사제는 상대의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다."]], [["“And what is there I would not do?”", "“내가 하지 않을 일이 무엇인가?”"], ["said Crawley.", "크롤리가 말했다."]], [["“Sacrifice your own pride.”", "“자네 자신의 자랑을 희생하는 일이지.”"]], [["“My pride?”", "“내 자랑?”"]], [["“Yes; your own pride.”", "“그래. 자네 자신의 자랑이야.”"]], [["“I have had but little pride this many a day.", "“나에게는 오랫동안 자랑이랄 것이 거의 없었네."], ["Arabin, you do not know what my life has been.", "애러빈, 자네는 내 삶이 어떠했는지 모르네."], ["How is a man to be proud who—”", "어떻게 사람이 자랑을 품을 수 있겠나, 이렇게—”"], ["And then he stopped himself, not wishing to go through the catalogue of those grievances, which, as he thought, had killed the very germs of pride within him, or to insist by spoken words on his poverty, his wants, and the injustice of his position.", "그러고서 그는 스스로 말을 멈추었다. 제 생각으로는 자기 안의 자랑의 싹까지 죽여 버린 그 온갖 억울한 일들의 목록을 늘어놓고 싶지 않았고, 제 가난과 제 궁핍과 제 처지의 부당함을 소리 내어 내세우고 싶지도 않았던 것이다."], ["“No; I wish I could be proud; but the world has been too heavy to me, and I have forgotten all that.”", "“아니. 자랑을 품을 수 있었으면 좋겠네만, 세상이 나에게 너무 무거웠고 나는 그런 것을 다 잊어버렸네.”"]], [["“How long have I known you, Crawley?”", "“내가 자네를 알고 지낸 지 얼마나 되었나, 크롤리?”"]], [["“How long?", "“얼마나라니?"], ["Ah dear!", "아, 딱한 일이지!"], ["a life-time nearly, now.”", "이제 거의 한 평생이네.”"]], [["“And we were like brothers once.”", "“그리고 우리는 한때 형제나 다름없었지.”"]], [["“Yes; we were equal as brothers then—in our fortunes, our tastes, and our modes of life.”", "“그래. 그때는 형제처럼 서로 대등했네 — 우리의 살림에서나, 우리의 취미에서나, 우리가 사는 방식에서나.”"]], [["“And yet you would begrudge me the pleasure of putting my hand in my pocket, and relieving the inconveniences which have been thrown on you, and those you love better than yourself, by the chances of your fate in life.”", "“그런데도 자네는, 내가 주머니에 손을 넣어 삶의 팔자가 자네와 또 자네가 자네 자신보다 더 사랑하는 이들에게 던져 놓은 그 불편들을 덜어 주는 기쁨을, 나에게서 아까워하려는 것이네.”"]], [["“I will live on no man’s charity,”", "“나는 어느 누구의 자선에 기대어 살지 않겠네.”"], ["said Crawley, with an abruptness which amounted almost to an expression of anger.", "크롤리가 거의 노여움을 드러내는 것에 이를 만큼 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“And is not that pride?”", "“그것이 자랑이 아닌가?”"]], [["“No—yes;—it is a species of pride, but not that pride of which you spoke.", "“아니 — 그렇지. 그것은 자랑의 한 가지이나 자네가 말한 그 자랑은 아니네."], ["A man cannot be honest if he have not some pride.", "사람이 얼마간의 자랑이 없으면 정직할 수 없어."], ["You yourself;—would you not rather starve than become a beggar?”", "자네 자신은 어떤가. 거지가 되기보다는 차라리 굶겠다고 하지 않겠나?”"]], [["“I would rather beg than see my wife starve,”", "“나는 내 아내가 굶는 것을 보기보다는 차라리 빌어먹겠네.”"], ["said Arabin.", "애러빈이 말했다."]], [["Crawley when he heard these words turned sharply round, and stood with his back to the dean, with his hands still behind him, and with his eyes fixed upon the ground.", "크롤리는 이 말을 듣고 홱 돌아서서, 두 손은 여전히 등 뒤에 두고 눈은 땅에 박은 채 수석 사제에게 등을 지고 섰다."]], [["“But in this case there is no question of begging,”", "“그러나 이번 일에는 빌어먹는다는 것이 문제가 아니네.”"], ["continued the dean.", "수석 사제가 말을 이었다."], ["“I, out of those superfluities which it has pleased God to put at my disposal, am anxious to assist the needs of those whom I love.”", "“하느님이 기꺼이 내 손에 맡겨 주신 그 남아 넘치는 것들 가운데서, 나는 내가 사랑하는 이들의 궁핍을 돕고 싶은 것이야.”"]], [["“She is not starving,”", "“아내는 굶고 있지 않네.”"], ["said Crawley, in a voice very bitter, but still intended to be exculpatory of himself.", "크롤리가 몹시 쓰라린 목소리로, 그러면서도 제 자신을 변호하려는 뜻으로 말했다."]], [["“No, my dear friend; I know she is not, and do not you be angry with me because I have endeavoured to put the matter to you in the strongest language I could use.”", "“그래, 이 사람아. 굶고 있지 않은 줄 나도 아네. 그러니 내가 쓸 수 있는 가장 센 말로 이 일을 자네에게 내놓아 보려 했다고 해서 나에게 노여워하지 말게.”"]], [["“You look at it, Arabin, from one side only; I can only look at it from the other.", "“애러빈, 자네는 그것을 한쪽에서만 보네. 나는 다른 쪽에서밖에 볼 수가 없어."], ["It is very sweet to give; I do not doubt that.", "주는 것은 몹시 달지. 그것은 나도 의심하지 않네."], ["But the taking of what is given is very bitter.", "그러나 주어진 것을 받는 일은 몹시 쓰네."], ["Gift bread chokes in a man’s throat and poisons his blood, and sits like lead upon the heart.", "얻어 먹는 빵은 사람의 목에 걸리고 그 피를 독하게 만들고 가슴 위에 납덩이처럼 얹히네."], ["You have never tried it.”", "자네는 그것을 겪어 본 적이 없어.”"]], [["“But that is the very fault for which I blame you.", "“그러나 바로 그것이 내가 자네를 탓하는 허물이네."], ["That is the pride which I say you ought to sacrifice.”", "그것이 바로 자네가 희생해야 한다고 내가 말하는 그 자랑이야.”"]], [["“And why should I be called on to do so?", "“그러면 어찌하여 내가 그리하라고 부름을 받아야 하나?"], ["Is not the labourer worthy of his hire?", "일꾼이 그 품값을 받아 마땅하지 않은가?"], ["Am I not able to work, and willing?", "나는 일할 수 있고 또 일할 뜻이 있지 않은가?"], ["Have I not always had my shoulder to the collar, and is it right that I should now be contented with the scraps from a rich man’s kitchen?", "나는 언제나 멍에에 어깨를 대어 오지 않았는가? 그런데 이제 부자의 부엌에서 나온 찌꺼기로 만족하는 것이 옳은 일인가?"], ["Arabin, you and I were equal once and we were then friends, understanding each other’s thoughts and sympathizing with each other’s sorrows.", "애러빈, 자네와 나는 한때 대등했고 그때는 벗이었네. 서로의 생각을 알아듣고 서로의 슬픔을 함께 아파했지."], ["But it cannot be so now.”", "그러나 이제는 그럴 수 없네.”"]], [["“If there be such inability, it is all with you.”", "“그렇게 할 수 없다면, 그것은 온전히 자네 쪽에 있는 것이네.”"]], [["“It is all with me,—because in our connection the pain would all be on my side.", "“온전히 내 쪽에 있네. 우리가 어울리는 자리에서 아픔은 다 내 쪽에만 있을 것이기 때문이야."], ["It would not hurt you to see me at your table with worn shoes and a ragged shirt.", "닳아 버린 신과 해어진 셔츠를 걸친 나를 자네 식탁에서 본다고 자네가 아플 것은 없지."], ["I do not think so meanly of you as that.", "나는 자네를 그렇게까지 하잘것없이 보지 않네."], ["You would give me your feast to eat though I were not clad a tithe as well as the menial behind your chair.", "내가 자네 의자 뒤에 선 하인의 십분의 일만큼도 차려입지 못하였어도 자네는 나에게 자네의 잔치 음식을 먹으라고 내놓을 것이야."], ["But it would hurt me to know that there were those looking at me who thought me unfit to sit in your rooms.”", "그러나 나를 바라보며 자네 방에 앉을 만한 자가 아니라고 여기는 사람들이 있다는 것을 아는 일은 나를 아프게 하네.”"]], [["“That is the pride of which I speak;—false pride.”", "“그것이 내가 말하는 자랑이네 — 거짓된 자랑이야.”"]], [["“Call it so if you will; but, Arabin, no preaching of yours can alter it.", "“그렇게 부르고 싶으면 그렇게 부르게. 그러나 애러빈, 자네의 어떤 설교로도 그것을 바꿀 수는 없네."], ["It is all that is left to me of my manliness.", "그것이 내 사내다움에서 나에게 남은 전부야."], ["That poor broken reed who is lying there sick,—who has sacrificed all the world to her love for me,—who is the mother of my children, and the partner of my sorrows and the wife of my bosom,—even she cannot change me in this, though she pleads with the eloquence of all her wants.", "저기 앓아누워 있는 그 딱한 부러진 갈대 — 나를 향한 사랑을 위하여 온 세상을 버린 사람, 내 아이들의 어머니이며 내 슬픔의 동반자이며 내 품의 아내인 그 사람 — 그 사람조차 이 일에서는 나를 바꾸지 못하네. 제 온갖 궁핍의 웅변으로 사정하여도 그렇네."], ["Not even for her can I hold out my hand for a dole.”", "그 사람을 위해서라도 나는 구휼을 받으려 손을 내밀 수는 없어.”"]], [["They had now come back to the door of the house, and Mr. Crawley, hardly conscious of what he was doing, was preparing to enter.", "이제 두 사람은 집 문 앞으로 되돌아와 있었고, 크롤리 씨는 제가 무엇을 하고 있는지 거의 알지 못한 채 들어가려 하고 있었다."]], [["“Will Mrs. Crawley be able to see me if I come in?”", "“내가 들어가면 크롤리 부인이 나를 볼 수 있겠나?”"], ["said the dean.", "수석 사제가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, stop; no; you had better not do so,”", "“아, 잠깐. 아니, 그러지 않는 것이 좋겠네.”"], ["said Mr. Crawley.", "크롤리 씨가 말했다."], ["“You, no doubt, might be subject to infection, and then Mrs. Arabin would be frightened.”", "“자네도 틀림없이 병이 옮을 수 있고, 그러면 애러빈 부인이 겁을 낼 것이네.”"]], [["“I do not care about it in the least,”", "“나는 그런 것을 조금도 개의하지 않네.”"], ["said the dean.", "수석 사제가 말했다."]], [["“But it is of no use; you had better not.", "“그러나 쓸데없는 일이야. 그러지 않는 것이 좋겠네."], ["Her room, I fear, is quite unfit for you to see; and the whole house, you know, may be infected.”", "아내의 방은 자네가 볼 만한 방이 도무지 아닐 것이 걱정이고, 알다시피 집 전체에 병이 배어 있을 수도 있네.”"]], [["Dr.", "애러빈 박사는 이때 이미 거실에 들어와 있었다."], ["Arabin by this time was in the sitting-room; but seeing that his friend was really anxious that he should not go farther, he did not persist.", "그러나 벗이 자기가 더 들어가지 않기를 정말로 애타게 바라는 것을 보고 그는 고집하지 않았다."]], [["“It will be a comfort to us, at any rate, to know that Miss Robarts is with her.”", "“아무튼 로바츠 양이 부인 곁에 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로도 우리에게는 위로가 되겠네.”"]], [["“The young lady is very good—very good indeed,”", "“그 젊은 아가씨는 참으로 선량하네 — 정말로 선량해.”"], ["said Crawley; “but I trust she will return to her home to-morrow.", "크롤리가 말했다. “그러나 내일은 제 집으로 돌아가리라고 나는 믿네."], ["It is impossible that she should remain in so poor a house as mine.", "내 집처럼 가난한 집에 머문다는 것은 될 수 없는 일이야."], ["There will be nothing here of all the things that she will want.”", "그 아가씨가 필요로 할 것들 가운데 여기에 있을 것은 하나도 없네.”"]], [["The dean thought that Lucy Robarts’s wants during her present occupation of nursing would not be so numerous as to make her continued sojourn in Mrs.", "수석 사제는 지금 병구완이라는 일에 매인 루시 로바츠에게 필요한 것이 크롤리 부인의 병실에 계속 머무는 일을 불가능하게 만들 만큼 많지는 않으리라고 생각했다."], ["Crawley’s sick room impossible, and therefore took his leave with a satisfied conviction that the poor lady would not be left wholly to the somewhat unskilful nursing of her husband.", "그리하여 그 딱한 부인이 남편의 다소 서투른 병구완에만 오롯이 맡겨지지는 않겠다는, 마음 놓이는 확신을 품고서 작별했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVII. MR. SOWERBY WITHOUT COMPANY.", "# 제37장. 벗 없는 사워비 씨"]], [["And now there were going to be wondrous doings in West Barsetshire, and men’s minds were much disturbed.", "이제 서바셋주에서 놀라운 일들이 벌어지려 하고 있었고, 사람들의 마음은 몹시 어수선했다. 높은 자리에서 명이 내려졌고, 여왕께서는 충성스러운 하원을 해산하셨다."], ["The fiat had gone forth from the high places, and the Queen had dissolved her faithful Commons.", "거인들은 옛 하원으로는 이룰 수 있는 것이 거의 없거나 아예 없다는 것을 알고, 새로 한번 걸어 보면 그것이 저들에게 무엇을 가져다줄지 시험해 보기로 마음을 굳혔으니, 총선의 소란이 온 나라에 퍼지게 되어 있었다."], ["The giants, finding that they could effect little or nothing with the old House, had resolved to try what a new venture would do for them, and the hubbub of a general election was to pervade the country.", "이것은 적잖은 짜증과 언짢음을 낳았다. 그 하원은 아직 삼 년도 채 되지 않았기 때문이다."], ["This produced no inconsiderable irritation and annoyance, for the House was not as yet quite three years old; and members of Parliament, though they naturally feel a constitutional pleasure in meeting their friends and in pressing the hands of their constituents, are, nevertheless, so far akin to the lower order of humanity that they appreciate the danger of losing their seats; and the certainty of a considerable outlay in their endeavours to retain them is not agreeable to the legislative mind.", "그리고 하원 의원들은 자기 벗들을 만나고 선거구민들의 손을 맞잡는 일에서 헌정에 걸맞은 즐거움을 절로 느끼기는 하지만, 그럼에도 인간이라는 종의 낮은 층과 다를 바 없을 만큼은 되어서 자기 의석을 잃을 위험이 무엇인지 제대로 알아본다. 게다가 그 의석을 지키려고 애쓰는 데 상당한 지출이 반드시 따른다는 사실은 입법가의 마음에 유쾌한 일이 아니다."]], [["Never did the old family fury between the gods and giants rage higher than at the present moment.", "신들과 거인들 사이의 그 오랜 집안싸움이 지금 이 순간보다 더 높이 타오른 적은 없었다."], ["The giants declared that every turn which they attempted to take in their country’s service had been thwarted by faction, in spite of those benign promises of assistance made to them only a few weeks since by their opponents; and the gods answered by asserting that they were driven to this opposition by the Bœotian fatuity of the giants.", "거인들은 나라를 섬기려고 자기들이 내디디려 한 걸음마다 당파 싸움에 막혔다고, 그것도 불과 몇 주 전에 상대편이 도와주겠다고 그토록 인심 좋게 약속을 해 놓고서 그랬다고 언명했다. 그러자 신들은 거인들의 보이오티아식 우둔함 때문에 자기들이 이 반대에 내몰린 것이라고 주장하며 대답했다."], ["They had no doubt promised their aid, and were ready to give it to measures that were decently prudent; but not to a bill enabling government at its will to pension aged bishops!", "물론 자기들은 도움을 약속했고, 어지간히 사려 깊은 조치라면 도울 준비가 되어 있었다. 그러나 정부가 마음대로 늙은 주교에게 연금을 주어 물릴 수 있게 하는 법안에까지 그럴 수는 없었다!"], ["No; there must be some limit to their tolerance, and when such attempts as these were made that limit had been clearly passed.", "아니, 자기들의 아량에도 어딘가 한계가 있어야 하는데, 이런 시도가 나오고 보니 그 한계는 분명히 넘어선 것이었다."]], [["All this had taken place openly only a day or two after that casual whisper dropped by Tom Towers at Miss Dunstable’s party—by Tom Towers, that most pleasant of all pleasant fellows.", "이 모든 일이 드러내어 벌어진 것은 톰 타워스가 던스터블 양의 모임에서 무심히 흘린 그 속삭임이 있은 뒤 겨우 하루나 이틀 만이었다—톰 타워스, 저 유쾌한 사람들 가운데 가장 유쾌한 사람이 흘린 속삭임이었다."], ["And how should he have known it,—he who flutters from one sweetest flower of the garden to another,", "그런데 그가 어떻게 그것을 알 수 있었겠는가—정원의 가장 향기로운 꽃에서 또 다른 꽃으로 날아다니는 그 사람이,"]], [["“Adding sugar to the pink, and honey to the rose, So loved for what he gives, but taking nothing as he goes”?", "“패랭이꽃에는 설탕을 더하고 장미에는 꿀을 더하며, 주는 것으로 그토록 사랑받으면서도 가면서 아무것도 가져가지 않는 이”라 하지 않던가?"]], [["But the whisper had grown into a rumour, and the rumour into a fact, and the political world was in a ferment.", "그러나 속삭임은 소문으로 자라났고 소문은 사실이 되었으니, 정치의 세계는 들끓었다."], ["The giants, furious about their bishops’ pension bill, threatened the House—most injudiciously; and then it was beautiful to see how indignant members got up, glowing with honesty, and declared that it was base to conceive that any gentleman in that House could be actuated in his vote by any hopes or fears with reference to his seat.", "거인들은 저들의 주교 연금 법안 때문에 격분해서 하원을 위협했다—지극히 지혜롭지 못한 짓이었다. 그러자 분개한 의원들이 정직함으로 환하게 달아올라 일어서서, 저 하원의 어느 신사인들 자기 의석에 관한 기대나 두려움 때문에 표결에서 움직여질 수 있다고 여기는 것은 비열한 일이라고 언명하는 광경은 볼만했다."], ["And so matters grew from bad to worse, and these contending parties never hit at each other with such envenomed wrath as they did now;—having entered the ring together so lately with such manifold promises of good-will, respect, and forbearance!", "그리하여 사정은 나쁜 데서 더 나쁜 데로 흘러갔고, 서로 다투는 이 두 편이 지금처럼 독기 오른 노여움으로 서로를 치고받은 적은 없었다—그토록 얼마 전에 호의와 존중과 참을성을 여러 갈래로 약속하면서 함께 판에 들어섰던 사이인데도!"]], [["But going from the general to the particular, we may say that nowhere was a deeper consternation spread than in the electoral division of West Barsetshire.", "그러나 일반에서 개별로 옮겨 말한다면, 서바셋주 선거 지구보다 더 깊은 경악이 퍼진 곳은 어디에도 없었다고 해도 좋다."], ["No sooner had the tidings of the dissolution reached the county than it was known that the duke intended to change his nominee.", "해산 소식이 이 주에 닿기가 무섭게 공작이 자기가 낙점한 사람을 바꿀 뜻을 품고 있다는 것이 알려졌다."], ["Mr. Sowerby had now sat for the division since the Reform Bill!", "사워비 씨는 개혁 법안이 나온 뒤로 줄곧 이 지구를 대표해 앉아 있었던 것이다!"], ["He had become one of the county institutions, and by the dint of custom and long establishment had been borne with and even liked by the county gentlemen, in spite of his well-known pecuniary irregularities.", "그는 이 주의 제도 가운데 하나가 되어 있었고, 오래된 관례와 긴 세월 자리 잡아 온 힘으로, 세상이 다 아는 그 금전상의 난잡함에도 불구하고 주의 신사들에게 참아 넘겨져 왔고 심지어 좋게 여겨지기까지 했다."], ["Now all this was to be changed.", "이제 이 모든 것이 바뀌게 되어 있었다."], ["No reason had as yet been publicly given, but it was understood that Lord Dumbello was to be returned, although he did not own an acre of land in the county.", "아직 공개된 이유는 하나도 없었으나, 덤벨로 경이 당선되기로 되어 있다는 것이 사람들의 이해였다. 덤벨로 경은 이 주에 땅 한 에이커도 가지고 있지 않았지만 그랬다."], ["It is true that rumour went on to say that Lord Dumbello was about to form close connections with Barsetshire.", "소문은 이어서 덤벨로 경이 바셋주와 가까운 인연을 맺으려 하고 있다고 말했는데, 그것은 사실이었다."], ["He was on the eve of marrying a young lady, from the other division indeed, and was now engaged, so it was said, in completing arrangements with the government for the purchase of that noble crown property usually known as the Chace of Chaldicotes.", "그는 어느 젊은 아가씨와 혼인할 날을 앞두고 있었으니, 그 아가씨는 과연 다른 지구 출신이었다. 그리고 그는 지금 콜디코츠 수렵림이라 흔히 불리는 그 훌륭한 왕실 소유지를 사들이는 일로 정부와 교섭을 마무리하는 중이라고 했다."], ["It was also stated—this statement, however, had hitherto been only announced in confidential whispers—that Chaldicotes House itself would soon become the residence of the marquis.", "또 이런 말도 있었다—다만 이 말은 여태까지 비밀스러운 속삭임으로만 전해져 왔다—콜디코츠 저택 자체가 머지않아 후작의 거처가 될 것이라는 말이었다."], ["The duke was claiming it as his own—would very shortly have completed his claims and taken possession;—and then, by some arrangement between them, it was to be made over to Lord Dumbello.", "공작이 그 집을 자기 것이라 주장하고 있었으니, 아주 머지않아 그 주장을 완결하고 소유를 넘겨받을 것이었다. 그러고 나면 두 사람 사이의 어떤 마련을 거쳐 그 집이 덤벨로 경에게 넘겨지게 되어 있었다."]], [["But very contrary rumours to these got abroad also.", "그러나 이와 아주 어긋나는 소문도 함께 퍼졌다."], ["Men said—such as dared to oppose the duke, and some few also who did not dare to oppose him when the day of battle came—that it was beyond his grace’s power to turn Lord Dumbello into a Barsetshire magnate.", "공작에게 감히 맞서는 이들이, 그리고 싸움의 날이 오면 감히 맞서지도 못할 몇몇 사람들까지도 말하기를, 덤벨로 경을 바셋주의 유력자로 만드는 것은 각하의 힘으로도 안 되는 일이라고 했다."], ["The crown property—such men said—was to fall into the hands of young Mr. Gresham, of Boxall Hill, in the other division, and that the terms of purchase had been already settled.", "그 왕실 소유지는—그런 사람들의 말로는—다른 지구에 있는 박솔 힐의 젊은 그레셤 씨의 손에 들어가게 되어 있고, 매입 조건도 이미 정해졌다고 했다."], ["And as to Mr. Sowerby’s property and the house of Chaldicotes—these opponents of the Omnium interest went on to explain—it was by no means as yet so certain that the duke would be able to enter it and take possession.", "그리고 사워비 씨의 재산과 콜디코츠의 저택에 대해서는—옴니엄 쪽 이해에 맞선 이들이 이어서 풀어 말하기로는—공작이 거기 들어가서 소유를 넘겨받을 수 있으리라는 것이 아직 결코 그렇게 확실하지 않았다."], ["The place was not to be given up to him quietly.", "그곳이 조용히 그에게 넘겨질 것은 아니었다."], ["A great fight would be made, and it was beginning to be believed that the enormous mortgages would be paid off by a lady of immense wealth.", "큰 싸움이 벌어질 것이고, 그 막대한 저당이 어마어마한 재산을 가진 어느 부인의 손으로 갚아져 없어질 것이라고 믿는 사람들이 생겨나기 시작했다."], ["And then a dash of romance was not wanting to make these stories palatable.", "그러고 나서는 이런 이야기들을 입에 맞게 만들어 줄 연애담 한 자락도 빠지지 않았다."], ["This lady of immense wealth had been courted by Mr. Sowerby, had acknowledged her love,—but had refused to marry him on account of his character.", "어마어마한 재산을 가진 이 부인은 사워비 씨의 구애를 받고 자기 사랑을 시인했으나, 그의 사람됨을 이유로 그와 혼인하기를 마다했다는 것이다."], ["In testimony of her love, however, she was about to pay all his debts.", "그러면서도 자기 사랑의 증표로 그의 빚을 다 갚아 주려 한다는 것이었다."]], [["It was soon put beyond a rumour, and became manifest enough, that Mr. Sowerby did not intend to retire from the county in obedience to the duke’s behests.", "사워비 씨가 공작의 분부에 순종하여 이 주에서 물러날 뜻이 없다는 것이 곧 소문의 자리를 넘어 넉넉히 드러났다."], ["A placard was posted through the whole division in which no allusion was made by name to the duke, but in which Mr. Sowerby warned his friends not to be led away by any report that he intended to retire from the representation of West Barsetshire.", "벽보 하나가 지구 전체에 붙었는데, 거기에는 공작의 이름을 들어 빗댄 말이 하나도 없었으나, 사워비 씨가 서바셋주의 대표 자리에서 물러날 뜻을 품고 있다는 어떤 보도에도 벗들이 끌려가지 말기를 당부하는 내용이었다."], ["“He had sat,”", "“그는,”"], ["the placard said, “for the same county during the full period of a quarter of a century, and he would not lightly give up an honour that had been extended to him so often and which he prized so dearly.", "벽보는 이렇게 말했다, “사반세기라는 온 기간에 걸쳐 같은 주를 대표해 앉아 있었으며, 그토록 여러 번 자기에게 내밀어진 영예이자 그토록 소중하게 여겨 온 영예를 가볍게 내놓지는 않을 것이다. 오늘날 하원에서 같은 선거구민 집단과의 인연이 서바셋주와 그를 묶어 온 인연만큼 오래도록 끊이지 않고 이어진 사람은 몇 되지 않는다."], ["There were but few men now in the House whose connection with the same body of constituents had remained unbroken so long as had that which bound him to West Barsetshire; and he confidently hoped that that connection might be continued through another period of coming years till he might find himself in the glorious position of being the father of the county members of the House of Commons.”", "그리고 그는 그 인연이 앞으로 올 또 한 시절을 지나 계속되어, 마침내 자기가 하원에서 주를 대표하는 의원들의 아버지가 되는 영광스러운 자리에 있게 되기를 자신 있게 바란다.” 벽보는 이보다 훨씬 많은 말을 하며 이 주에 크게 이해가 걸린 여러 가지 문제를 이러저러하게 비추었으나, 옴니엄 공작에 대해서는 한 마디도 하지 않았다."], ["The placard said much more than this, and hinted at sundry and various questions, all of great interest to the county; but it did not say one word of the Duke of Omnium, though every one knew what the duke was supposed to be doing in the matter.", "이 일에서 공작이 무엇을 하고 있다고 여겨지는지 모두가 알고 있었는데도 그랬다. 그는 이를테면 지성소에 갇혀 있는 대라마였다."], ["He was, as it were, a great Llama, shut up in a holy of holies, inscrutable, invisible, inexorable,—not to be seen by men’s eyes or heard by their ears, hardly to be mentioned by ordinary men at such periods as these without an inward quaking.", "헤아릴 수 없고, 보이지 않고, 굽힐 줄 모르는 이. 사람의 눈에 보이지도 사람의 귀에 들리지도 않고, 이런 시절에는 보통 사람이 속으로 떨지 않고서는 입에 올리기조차 어려운 이."], ["But nevertheless, it was he who was supposed to rule them.", "그러나 그럼에도 그들을 다스린다고 여겨지는 이는 그였다."], ["Euphemism required that his name should be mentioned at no public meetings in connection with the coming election; but, nevertheless, most men in the county believed that he could send his dog up to the House of Commons as member for West Barsetshire if it so pleased him.", "완곡한 말법은 다가오는 선거와 관련해서 어떤 공개 집회에서도 그의 이름이 입에 올려지지 말기를 요구했다. 그러나 그럼에도 이 주의 사람들은 대부분, 그가 그러기를 좋아하기만 하면 자기 개를 서바셋주의 의원으로 하원에 올려보낼 수도 있다고 믿었다."]], [["It was supposed, therefore, that our friend Sowerby would have no chance; but he was lucky in finding assistance in a quarter from which he certainly had not deserved it.", "그러므로 우리 벗 사워비에게는 승산이 없다고 여겨졌다. 그러나 그는 자기가 도움을 받을 자격이 결코 없는 쪽에서 도움을 만나는 운을 얻었다."], ["He had been a staunch friend of the gods during the whole of his political life,—as, indeed, was to be expected, seeing that he had been the duke’s nominee; but, nevertheless, on the present occasion, all the giants connected with the county came forward to his rescue.", "그는 정치 인생 전체에 걸쳐 신들의 든든한 벗이었다—공작이 낙점한 사람이었으니 사실 그럴 수밖에 없기도 했다. 그럼에도 이번 경우에는 이 주와 연이 닿은 거인들이 모두 그를 구하러 나섰다."], ["They did not do this with the acknowledged purpose of opposing the duke; they declared that they were actuated by a generous disinclination to see an old county member put from his seat;—but the world knew that the battle was to be waged against the great Llama.", "그들이 이렇게 한 것은 공작에게 맞서겠다는 뜻을 내놓고 밝힌 것은 아니었다. 그들은 오래된 주 대표 의원이 자기 의석에서 밀려나는 것을 보고 싶지 않은 넉넉한 마음에서 움직인다고 언명했다."], ["It was to be a contest between the powers of aristocracy and the powers of oligarchy, as those powers existed in West Barsetshire,—and, it may be added, that democracy would have very little to say to it, on one side or on the other.", "그러나 세상은 이 싸움이 대라마를 겨누어 벌어지는 것임을 알았다. 그것은 귀족의 힘과 과두의 힘 사이의 겨룸이 될 터였는데, 서바셋주에 그 두 힘이 있는 대로의 겨룸이었다."], ["The lower order of voters, the small farmers and tradesmen, would no doubt range themselves on the side of the duke, and would endeavour to flatter themselves that they were thereby furthering the views of the liberal side; but they would in fact be led to the poll by an old-fashioned, time-honoured adherence to the will of their great Llama; and by an apprehension of evil if that Llama should arise and shake himself in his wrath.", "그리고 덧붙여 말한다면, 민주의 힘은 이쪽에서든 저쪽에서든 여기에 할 말이 거의 없을 것이었다. 낮은 층의 유권자들, 곧 작은 농부들과 장사꾼들은 틀림없이 공작 편에 늘어설 것이고, 그렇게 함으로써 자기들이 자유주의 편의 뜻을 밀어 준다고 스스로 흐뭇하게 여기려 애쓸 것이었다."], ["What might not come to the county if the Llama were to walk himself off, he with his satellites and armies and courtiers?", "그러나 실제로 그들을 투표소로 이끄는 것은 자기네 대라마의 뜻에 대한 낡고 오래된 순종이요, 만일 그 라마가 몸을 일으켜 노여움으로 몸을 떨면 어쩌나 하는 재앙에 대한 두려움일 것이었다. 그 라마가 자기 위성들과 군대와 조신들을 데리고 걸어서 떠나 버린다면 이 주에 무슨 일이 닥치지 않을 것인가?"], ["There he was, a great Llama; and though he came among them but seldom, and was scarcely seen when he did come, nevertheless—and not the less but rather the more—was obedience to him considered as salutary and opposition regarded as dangerous.", "그가 거기 있었다, 대라마가. 그리고 그는 그들 가운데로 좀처럼 오지 않았고 오더라도 좀처럼 눈에 보이지 않았으나, 그럼에도—덜하기는커녕 오히려 더하게—그에게 순종하는 것은 몸에 좋은 일로 여겨졌고 그에게 맞서는 것은 위태로운 일로 여겨졌다."], ["A great rural Llama is still sufficiently mighty in rural England.", "시골의 대라마는 시골 영국에서 아직도 충분히 힘이 세다."]], [["But the priest of the temple, Mr. Fothergill, was frequent enough in men’s eyes, and it was beautiful to hear with how varied a voice he alluded to the things around him and to the changes which were coming.", "그러나 그 신전의 사제인 포더길 씨는 사람들의 눈에 넉넉히 자주 띄었고, 자기 주변의 일들과 다가오는 변화에 대해 그가 얼마나 여러 가지 목소리로 말하는지 듣는 것은 볼만한 일이었다."], ["To the small farmers, not only on the Gatherum property but on others also, he spoke of the duke as a beneficent influence, shedding prosperity on all around him, keeping up prices by his presence, and forbidding the poor rates to rise above one and fourpence in the pound by the general employment which he occasioned.", "개서럼 영지뿐 아니라 다른 영지의 작은 농부들에게도 그는 공작을 두루 복을 베푸는 힘으로 이야기했다. 주위 모두에게 번영을 흘려보내고, 그 자리에 있음으로써 값을 떠받치고, 자기가 널리 일으키는 고용으로 구빈세가 파운드당 일 실링 사 펜스 위로 올라가지 못하게 막는다는 것이었다."], ["Men must be mad, he thought, who would willingly fly in the duke’s face.", "공작의 얼굴에 기꺼이 달려들려 하는 사람이라면 미친 사람일 것이라고 그는 여겼다."], ["To the squires from a distance he declared that no one had a right to charge the duke with any interference;—as far, at least, as he knew the duke’s mind.", "멀리서 온 지주들에게는, 적어도 자기가 공작의 마음을 아는 한, 아무도 공작에게 어떤 간섭의 죄를 씌울 권리가 없다고 언명했다."], ["People would talk of things of which they understood nothing.", "사람들은 자기가 아무것도 모르는 일에 대해 말을 하려 든다."], ["Could any one say that he had traced a single request for a vote home to the duke?", "표 한 장을 부탁한 일 하나라도 그 자취를 공작에게까지 되짚어 갔다고 말할 수 있는 사람이 있느냐는 것이었다."], ["All this did not alter the settled conviction on men’s minds; but it had its effect, and tended to increase the mystery in which the duke’s doings were enveloped.", "이 모든 것이 사람들 마음에 자리 잡은 확신을 바꾸어 놓지는 못했다. 그러나 그것은 그것대로 효험이 있어서, 공작이 하는 일을 둘러싼 신비를 더 두껍게 하는 데 이바지했다."], ["But to his own familiars, to the gentry immediately around him, Mr. Fothergill merely winked his eye.", "그러나 자기와 친한 사람들, 곧 자기 바로 곁에 있는 신사 계층에게는 포더길 씨가 그저 눈을 찡긋해 보였다."], ["They knew what was what, and so did he.", "그들도 사정이 어떤지 알았고 그도 알았다."], ["The duke had never been bit yet in such matters, and Mr. Fothergill did not think that he would now submit himself to any such operation.", "공작은 그런 일에서 여태 한 번도 물려 본 적이 없었고, 이제 와서 그런 수술을 스스로 받으려 하지는 않을 것이라고 포더길 씨는 여겼다."]], [["I never heard in what manner and at what rate Mr. Fothergill received remuneration for the various services performed by him with reference to the duke’s property in Barsetshire; but I am very sure that, whatever might be the amount, he earned it thoroughly.", "바셋주에 있는 공작의 재산과 관련해서 포더길 씨가 해낸 여러 가지 일에 대해 그가 어떤 방식으로 얼마만큼 보수를 받았는지 나는 들어 본 적이 없다."], ["Never was there a more faithful partisan, or one who, in his partisanship, was more discreet.", "그러나 그 액수가 얼마였든 그가 그것을 온전히 벌었다는 것만은 아주 확실하다."], ["In this matter of the coming election he declared that he himself,—personally, on his own hook,—did intend to bestir himself actively on behalf of Lord Dumbello.", "그보다 더 충실한 당파의 사람도, 그 당파심 안에서 그보다 더 조심스러운 사람도 없었다. 다가오는 선거라는 이 일에서 그는, 자기 자신은—사사로이, 제 몫으로—덤벨로 경을 위해 활발히 움직일 뜻이라고 언명했다."], ["Mr. Sowerby was an old friend of his, and a very good fellow.", "사워비 씨는 자기의 오랜 벗이며 아주 좋은 사람이었다."], ["That was true.", "그것은 사실이었다."], ["But all the world must admit that Sowerby was not in the position which a county member ought to occupy.", "그러나 사워비가 주 대표 의원이 마땅히 차지해야 할 처지에 있지 않다는 것은 온 세상이 인정해야 할 것이었다."], ["He was a ruined man, and it would not be for his own advantage that he should be maintained in a position which was fit only for a man of property. He knew—he, Fothergill—that Mr.", "그는 파산한 사람이었고, 재산을 가진 사람에게만 맞는 자리에 그를 붙들어 두는 것은 그 자신에게도 좋을 일이 아니었다."], ["Sowerby must abandon all right and claim to Chaldicotes; and if so, what would be more absurd than to acknowledge that he had a right and claim to the seat in Parliament?", "그는—그, 포더길은—사워비 씨가 콜디코츠에 대한 모든 권리와 청구를 내놓아야 한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그렇다면 그가 하원 의석에 대해서는 권리와 청구를 가졌다고 인정해 주는 것보다 더 터무니없는 일이 무엇이겠는가?"], ["As to Lord Dumbello, it was probable that he would soon become one of the largest landowners in the county; and, as such, who could be more fit for the representation?", "덤벨로 경에 대해서라면, 그가 머지않아 이 주에서 가장 큰 땅 임자 가운데 하나가 될 것이 짐작되었으니, 그렇다면 대표 자리에 그보다 더 알맞은 사람이 누구겠는가?"], ["Beyond this, Mr. Fothergill was not ashamed to confess—so he said—that he hoped to hold Lord Dumbello’s agency.", "그 위에 더해 포더길 씨는—그의 말로는—덤벨로 경의 대리인 자리를 맡기를 바란다고 털어놓는 것을 부끄러워하지 않았다."], ["It would be compatible with his other duties, and therefore, as a matter of course, he intended to support Lord Dumbello;—he himself, that is.", "그것은 자기의 다른 직무와 서로 어긋나지 않을 것이니, 그러므로 당연한 일로서 자기는 덤벨로 경을 밀 뜻이었다—그 자신이, 그러니까."], ["As to the duke’s mind in the matter—!", "이 일에서 공작의 마음이야—!"], ["But I have already explained how Mr. Fothergill disposed of that.", "그러나 포더길 씨가 그 문제를 어떻게 처리했는지는 내가 이미 풀어 놓았다."]], [["In these days, Mr.", "이 무렵에 사워비 씨는 자기 집으로 내려왔다—겉으로는 아직 자기 집이었으니까."], ["Sowerby came down to his own house—for ostensibly it was still his own house—but he came very quietly, and his arrival was hardly known in his own village.", "그러나 그는 아주 조용히 왔고, 그가 도착한 것이 자기 마을에서도 거의 알려지지 않았다."], ["Though his placard was stuck up so widely, he himself took no electioneering steps; none, at least, as yet.", "벽보는 그렇게 널리 붙여 놓았으나 그 자신은 선거 운동의 걸음이라고는 하나도 떼지 않았다. 적어도 아직은 하나도 없었다."], ["The protection against arrest which he derived from Parliament would soon be over, and those who were most bitter against the duke averred that steps would be taken to arrest him, should he give sufficient opportunity to the myrmidons of the law.", "의회에서 나오는 체포 면책은 곧 끝날 것이었고, 공작에게 가장 앙심을 품은 이들은 그가 법의 미르미돈들에게 넉넉한 기회를 주기만 하면 그를 체포하려는 걸음이 떼어질 것이라고 단언했다. 그런 경우에 그가 체포될 것은 몹시 있을 법한 일이었다."], ["That he would, in such case, be arrested was very likely; but it was not likely that this would be done in any way at the duke’s instance.", "그러나 그 일이 어떤 식으로든 공작이 부추겨 이루어질 것 같지는 않았다. 포더길 씨는 이렇게 빗대는 말이 자기를 매우 노엽게 한다고 분개하여 언명했다."], ["Mr. Fothergill declared indignantly that this insinuation made him very angry; but he was too prudent a man to be very angry at anything, and he knew how to make capital on his own side of charges such as these which overshot their own mark.", "그러나 그는 무슨 일에든 매우 노여워하기에는 너무 신중한 사람이었고, 저렇게 제 표적을 지나쳐 넘어가 버린 비난을 자기 편의 밑천으로 만드는 법을 알고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Sowerby came down very quietly to Chaldicotes, and there he remained for a couple of days, quite alone.", "사워비 씨는 아주 조용히 콜디코츠로 내려와 거기서 이틀을 완전히 혼자 머물렀다."], ["The place bore a very different aspect now to that which we noticed when Mark Robarts drove up to it, in the early pages of this little narrative.", "이곳은 이 작은 이야기의 앞쪽에서 마크 로바츠가 마차를 몰아 올라왔을 때 우리가 눈여겨보았던 모습과는 이제 아주 다른 모습을 띠고 있었다."], ["There were no lights in the windows now, and no voices came from the stables; no dogs barked, and all was dead and silent as the grave.", "이제 창문에는 불빛이 없고 마구간에서는 어떤 목소리도 들려오지 않았다. 개도 짖지 않았고, 모든 것이 무덤처럼 죽어 있고 잠잠했다."], ["During the greater portion of those two days he sat alone within the house, almost unoccupied.", "그 이틀의 대부분 동안 그는 집 안에 혼자 앉아 거의 아무것도 하지 않았다."], ["He did not even open his letters, which lay piled on a crowded table in the small breakfast parlour in which he sat; for the letters of such men come in piles, and there are few of them which are pleasant in the reading.", "편지조차 뜯지 않았고, 그 편지들은 그가 앉아 있는 작은 조찬실의 그 붐비는 탁자 위에 무더기로 놓여 있었다. 그런 사람들의 편지는 무더기로 오는 것이고, 그 가운데 읽어서 즐거운 것은 몇 되지 않는다."], ["There he sat, troubled with thoughts which were sad enough, now and then moving to and fro the house, but for the most part occupied in thinking over the position to which he had brought himself.", "그는 거기 앉아 슬프기 그지없는 생각들에 시달렸고, 이따금 집 안을 이리저리 오갔으나, 대부분은 자기가 스스로를 끌어다 놓은 처지를 곱씹는 일에 매여 있었다."], ["What would he be in the world’s eye, if he ceased to be the owner of Chaldicotes, and ceased also to be the member for his county?", "만일 콜디코츠의 임자이기를 그치고 자기 주의 의원이기를 또한 그친다면, 그는 세상의 눈에 무엇이 될 것인가?"], ["He had lived ever before the world, and, though always harassed by encumbrances, had been sustained and comforted by the excitement of a prominent position.", "그는 여태껏 늘 세상의 앞에서 살아왔고, 언제나 빚에 시달리기는 했으나 두드러진 자리가 주는 설렘으로 버티고 위로를 얻어 왔다."], ["His debts and difficulties had hitherto been bearable, and he had borne them with ease so long that he had almost taught himself to think that they would never be unendurable.", "그의 빚과 곤경은 여태까지는 견딜 만한 것이었고, 너무 오래 그것을 쉽게 견뎌 온 나머지 그것들이 결코 견딜 수 없게 되지는 않으리라고 스스로 생각하도록 거의 길들여 놓았다."], ["But now,—", "그러나 이제는—"]], [["The order for foreclosing had gone forth, and the harpies of the law, by their present speed in sticking their claws into the carcase of his property, were atoning to themselves for the delay with which they had hitherto been compelled to approach their prey.", "저당권을 실행하라는 명이 내려졌고, 법의 하르피이아들은 그의 재산이라는 시체에 발톱을 박아 넣는 지금의 빠름으로, 여태 먹이에 다가가는 것을 미루도록 강요당해 온 그 지체를 스스로 벌충하고 있었다."], ["And the order as to his seat had gone forth also.", "그리고 그의 의석에 관한 명도 함께 내려져 있었다."], ["That placard had been drawn up by the combined efforts of his sister, Miss Dunstable, and a certain well-known electioneering agent, named Closerstill, presumed to be in the interest of the giants.", "그 벽보는 그의 누이동생과 던스터블 양, 그리고 거인들 쪽 이해에 서 있다고 짐작되는 클로저스틸이라는 이름의 잘 알려진 어느 선거 대리인이 힘을 합쳐 작성한 것이었다."], ["But poor Sowerby had but little confidence in the placard.", "그러나 가련한 사워비는 그 벽보를 거의 믿지 않았다."], ["No one knew better than he how great was the duke’s power.", "공작의 힘이 얼마나 큰지를 그보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없었다."]], [["He was hopeless, therefore, as he walked about through those empty rooms, thinking of his past life and of that life which was to come.", "그리하여 그는 그 빈 방들을 오가며 자기 지나간 삶과 앞으로 올 그 삶을 생각하는 동안 아무 희망이 없었다."], ["Would it not be well for him that he were dead, now that he was dying to all that had made the world pleasant! We see and hear of such men as Mr.", "세상을 즐겁게 만들어 주었던 모든 것에 대해 이제 죽어 가고 있으니, 차라리 죽어 버리는 것이 그에게 좋지 않겠는가!"], ["Sowerby, and are apt to think that they enjoy all that the world can give, and that they enjoy that all without payment either in care or labour; but I doubt that, with even the most callous of them, their periods of wretchedness must be frequent, and that wretchedness very intense.", "우리는 사워비 씨 같은 사람들을 보고 들으면서, 그들이 세상이 줄 수 있는 것을 다 누리며, 근심으로든 수고로든 값을 치르지 않고 그 모두를 누린다고 여기기 쉽다. 그러나 나는 그들 가운데 가장 무디어진 자에게조차 비참한 시기가 잦을 것이고 그 비참함도 매우 사무칠 것이라고 짐작한다."], ["Salmon and lamb in February and green pease and new potatoes in March can hardly make a man happy, even though nobody pays for them; and the feeling that one is an antecedentem scelestum after whom a sure, though lame, Nemesis is hobbling, must sometimes disturb one’s slumbers.", "이월의 연어와 새끼 양고기, 삼월의 완두와 햇감자가, 아무도 그 값을 치르지 않는다 해도 사람을 행복하게 해 주기는 어렵다. 그리고 자기가 「안테케덴템 스켈레스툼」이며 그 뒤를 절름거리기는 하나 반드시 오고 마는 네메시스가 뒤뚱뒤뚱 따라오고 있다는 느낌은 때때로 잠을 어지럽히지 않을 수 없다."], ["On the present occasion Scelestus felt that his Nemesis had overtaken him.", "이번에는 스켈레스투스가 자기 네메시스에게 붙잡혔다고 느꼈다."], ["Lame as she had been, and swift as he had run, she had mouthed him at last, and there was nothing left for him but to listen to the “whoop”", "그 여신은 절름거렸고 그는 빠르게 달아났으나, 마침내 여신이 그를 입에 물었으니, 그에게 남은 것은 제 죽음의 몸부림을 보고 터지는 “와아”"], ["set up at the sight of his own death-throes.", "하는 함성을 듣는 일뿐이었다."]], [["It was a melancholy, dreary place now, that big house of Chaldicotes; and though the woods were all green with their early leaves, and the gardens thick with flowers, they also were melancholy and dreary.", "이제 그 큰 콜디코츠의 저택은 구슬프고 쓸쓸한 곳이었다. 숲은 온통 이른 새잎으로 푸르고 정원은 꽃으로 빽빽했으나, 그것들도 구슬프고 쓸쓸했다."], ["The lawns were untrimmed and weeds were growing through the gravel, and here and there a cracked Dryad, tumbled from her pedestal and sprawling in the grass, gave a look of disorder to the whole place.", "잔디는 다듬어지지 않았고 자갈 사이로 잡풀이 자라 나왔으며, 여기저기 금이 간 드리아스 상이 받침대에서 굴러떨어져 풀밭에 나동그라져 있어서 온 자리에 어수선한 꼴을 얹어 놓았다."], ["The wooden trellis-work was shattered here and bending there, the standard rose-trees were stooping to the ground, and the leaves of the winter still encumbered the borders.", "나무 격자 울은 여기서 부서지고 저기서 휘어 있었고, 대목 장미들은 땅으로 허리를 굽히고 있었고, 겨울에 진 잎들이 아직도 화단을 어지럽히고 있었다."], ["Late in the evening of the second day Mr. Sowerby strolled out, and went through the gardens into the wood.", "이틀째 되는 날 저녁 늦게 사워비 씨는 어슬렁 나가 정원을 지나 숲으로 들어갔다."], ["Of all the inanimate things of the world this wood of Chaldicotes was the dearest to him.", "세상의 목숨 없는 것들 가운데 이 콜디코츠의 숲이 그에게는 가장 소중했다."], ["He was not a man to whom his companions gave much credit for feelings or thoughts akin to poetry, but here, out in the Chace, his mind would be almost poetical.", "그는 벗들에게서 시에 가까운 감정이나 생각을 지녔다고 인정받는 사람이 아니었으나, 여기 체이스에 나오면 그의 마음은 거의 시적이 되었다."], ["While wandering among the forest trees, he became susceptible of the tenderness of human nature: he would listen to the birds singing, and pick here and there a wild flower on his path.", "숲의 나무들 사이를 헤매는 동안 그는 사람의 마음이 지닌 부드러움을 느낄 수 있게 되었다. 새들이 노래하는 것을 듣고, 길 위에서 들꽃을 여기저기 한 송이씩 꺾었다."], ["He would watch the decay of the old trees and the progress of the young, and make pictures in his eyes of every turn in the wood.", "늙은 나무가 삭아 가는 것과 어린 나무가 자라 가는 것을 지켜보고, 숲의 굽이마다 눈에 그림을 그렸다."], ["He would mark the colour of a bit of road as it dipped into a dell, and then, passing through a water-course, rose brown, rough, irregular, and beautiful against the bank on the other side.", "길이 우묵한 골로 내려앉을 때 그 흙빛을 눈여겨보았고, 그러고 나서 그 길이 물길을 지나 건너편 언덕을 등지고 갈색으로, 거칠게, 고르지 않게, 아름답게 솟아오르는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그러고 나서는 앉아서 자기 오래된 집안을 생각했다."], ["And then he would sit and think of his old family: how they had roamed there time out of mind in those Chaldicotes woods, father and son and grandson in regular succession, each giving them over, without blemish or decrease, to his successor.", "그들이 저 콜디코츠 숲에서 아득한 옛날부터 어떻게 거닐었는지, 아버지와 아들과 손자가 차례를 어기지 않고 이어 가며 저마다 흠도 줄어듦도 없이 그것을 다음 사람에게 넘겨주었는지를. 그렇게 그는 앉아 있곤 했다."], ["So he would sit; and so he did sit even now, and, thinking of these things, wished that he had never been born.", "그리고 지금도 그렇게 앉아, 이런 것들을 생각하면서 자기가 아예 태어나지 않았더라면 하고 바랐다."]], [["It was dark night when he returned to the house, and as he did so he resolved that he would quit the place altogether, and give up the battle as lost.", "그가 집으로 돌아왔을 때는 캄캄한 밤이었다. 그리고 돌아오면서 그는 이곳을 아예 떠나고 이 싸움도 진 것으로 여겨 내놓기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["The duke should take it and do as he pleased with it; and as for the seat in Parliament, Lord Dumbello, or any other equally gifted young patrician, might hold it for him.", "공작이 그것을 가져가서 마음대로 하라지. 그리고 하원 의석은 덤벨로 경이든, 그만큼 재주를 타고난 다른 어느 젊은 귀족 자제든 자기 대신 차지하라지."], ["He would vanish from the scene and betake himself to some land from whence he would be neither heard nor seen, and there—starve.", "그는 이 무대에서 사라져 자기가 들리지도 보이지도 않을 어느 땅으로 몸을 옮겨, 거기서—굶주릴 것이었다."], ["Such were now his future outlooks into the world; and yet, as regards health and all physical capacities, he knew that he was still in the prime of his life.", "이제 세상을 향한 그의 앞날은 그런 것이었다. 그러면서도 건강과 온갖 몸의 힘으로 말한다면 자기가 아직 한창때에 있다는 것을 그는 알았다."], ["Yes; in the prime of his life!", "그렇다, 한창때에!"], ["But what could he do with what remained to him of such prime?", "그러나 그런 한창때에서 자기에게 남아 있는 것으로 그가 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["How could he turn either his mind or his strength to such account as might now be serviceable?", "이제 도움이 될 만한 쪽으로 그가 어떻게 자기 머리나 자기 힘을 돌려 쓸 수 있겠는가?"], ["How could he, in his sore need, earn for himself even the barest bread?", "이 뼈아픈 궁핍 속에서 그가 어떻게 제 손으로 가장 헐한 빵 한 조각이라도 벌 수 있겠는가? 그가 죽어 버리는 것이 그에게 더 낫지 않겠는가?"], ["Would it not be better for him that he should die?", "아무도 낭비꾼의 팔자를 부러워하지 말 것이다."], ["Let not any one covet the lot of a spendthrift, even though the days of his early pease and champagne seem to be unnumbered; for that lame Nemesis will surely be up before the game has been all played out.", "이른 완두와 샴페인의 날이 셀 수 없이 이어질 듯이 보인다 해도 그렇다. 저 절름거리는 네메시스가 놀음이 다 끝나기 전에 반드시 따라붙을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["When Mr. Sowerby reached his house he found that a message by telegraph had arrived for him in his absence.", "사워비 씨가 집에 이르러 보니 그가 없는 동안 전보로 온 소식 하나가 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["It was from his sister, and it informed him that she would be with him that night.", "누이동생에게서 온 것으로, 그날 밤 그에게 오겠다는 소식이었다."], ["She was coming down by the mail train, had telegraphed to Barchester for post-horses, and would be at Chaldicotes about two hours after midnight.", "그녀는 우편 열차로 내려오는 중이고, 바체스터로 전보를 쳐서 역마를 잡아 두었으며, 자정을 두 시간쯤 지나 콜디코츠에 닿을 것이었다."], ["It was therefore manifest enough that her business was of importance.", "그러니 그녀의 볼일이 중대하다는 것은 넉넉히 드러나 있었다."]], [["Exactly at two the Barchester post-chaise did arrive, and Mrs. Harold Smith, before she retired to her bed, was closeted for about an hour with her brother.", "두 시 정각에 바체스터 역마차가 과연 도착했고, 해럴드 스미스 부인은 자리에 들기 전에 한 시간쯤 오라비와 단둘이 문을 닫고 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Well,”", "“그래요,”"], ["she said, the following morning, as they sat together at the breakfast-table, “what do you say to it now?", "이튿날 아침 두 사람이 조찬 식탁에 함께 앉았을 때 그녀가 말했다. “이제는 그 일을 어떻게 보세요?"], ["If you accept her offer you should be with her lawyer this afternoon.”", "그분의 제안을 받아들일 거라면 오늘 오후에는 그분의 변호사와 함께 있어야 해요.”"]], [["“I suppose I must accept it,”", "“받아들여야 하겠지.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Certainly, I think so.", "“물론이지요, 나도 그렇게 봐요."], ["No doubt it will take the property out of your own hands as completely as though the duke had it, but it will leave you the house, at any rate, for your life.”", "그렇게 하면 공작이 가져갔을 때와 똑같이 온전히 그 재산이 오라버니 손에서 떠나가기는 하겠지만, 어쨌든 집은 오라버니가 사시는 동안 오라버니에게 남게 돼요.”"]], [["“What good will the house be, when I can’t keep it up?”", "“유지할 수도 없는 집이 무슨 소용인가?”"]], [["“But I am not so sure of that.", "“그런데 나는 그렇지 않다고 봐요."], ["She will not want more than her fair interest; and as it will be thoroughly well managed, I should think that there would be something over—something enough to keep up the house.", "그분은 정당한 이자보다 더 바라지 않을 거예요. 그리고 아주 잘 관리될 테니까 얼마쯤 남는 것이 있을 거라고 나는 생각해요—집을 유지할 만큼은 남을 거예요."], ["And then, you know, we must have some place in the country.”", "그리고 오라버니도 아시다시피, 우리도 시골에 어딘가 자리가 있어야 하잖아요.”"]], [["“I tell you fairly, Harriet, that I will have nothing further to do with Harold in the way of money.”", "“솔직히 말해 두겠네, 해리엇. 돈에 관한 일로는 해럴드와 다시는 아무 상관도 맺지 않겠어.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["that was because you would go to him.", "그건 오라버니가 굳이 그이에게 가려고 했기 때문이지요."], ["Why did you not come to me?", "왜 나에게 오지 않으셨어요?"], ["And then, Nathaniel, it is the only way in which you can have a chance of keeping the seat.", "그리고요, 나다니엘, 이것 말고는 오라버니가 의석을 지킬 가망을 가질 길이 없어요."], ["She is the queerest woman I ever met, but she seems resolved on beating the duke.”", "그분은 내가 여태 만나 본 가운데 가장 별난 여인이지만, 공작을 이기기로 마음을 굳힌 것 같아요.”"]], [["“I do not quite understand it, but I have not the slightest objection.”", "“나는 잘 이해가 되지 않지만, 조금도 반대할 것은 없네.”"]], [["“She thinks that he is interfering with young Gresham about the crown property.", "“그분은 공작이 왕실 소유지 일로 젊은 그레셤에게 간섭하고 있다고 여겨요."], ["I had no idea that she had so much business at her fingers’ ends.", "나는 그분이 사무에 그렇게까지 밝은 줄은 몰랐어요."], ["When I first proposed the matter she took it up quite as a lawyer might, and seemed to have forgotten altogether what occurred about that other matter.”", "내가 이 일을 처음 내놓았을 때 그분은 변호사가 그러듯이 그것을 받아 들었고, 그 다른 일에 있었던 것은 아예 다 잊어버린 듯이 보였어요.”"]], [["“I wish I could forget it also,”", "“나도 그것을 잊을 수 있었으면 좋겠네.”"], ["said Mr. Sowerby.", "사워비 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I really think that she does.", "“나는 그분이 정말로 잊었다고 봐요."], ["When I was obliged to make some allusion to it—at least I felt myself obliged, and was sorry afterwards that I did—she merely laughed—a great loud laugh as she always does, and then went on about the business.", "내가 그 일을 얼마쯤 비쳐 말하지 않을 수 없었을 때—적어도 나로서는 그러지 않을 수 없다고 느꼈고, 그러고 나서는 그런 것을 뉘우쳤지요—그분은 그저 웃었어요. 늘 그러듯이 크게 소리 내어 웃고는, 그러고 나서 볼일 이야기를 이어 갔어요."], ["However, she was clear about this, that all the expenses of the election should be added to the sum to be advanced by her, and that the house should be left to you without any rent.", "그렇지만 이것만은 분명히 해 두셨어요. 선거에 드는 비용은 전부 그분이 앞당겨 내주실 금액에 더해야 하고, 집은 아무런 임차료도 없이 오라버니에게 남겨 두어야 한다는 것이었어요."], ["If you choose to take the land round the house you must pay for it, by the acre, as the tenants do.", "집 둘레의 땅을 가지겠다면 소작인들이 하듯이 에이커 단위로 값을 치러야 해요."], ["She was as clear about it all as though she had passed her life in a lawyer’s office.”", "그분은 평생을 변호사 사무실에서 지낸 사람인 것처럼 그 모든 것을 분명히 하셨어요.”"]], [["My readers will now pretty well understand what last step that excellent sister, Mrs. Harold Smith, had taken on her brother’s behalf, nor will they be surprised to learn that in the course of the day Mr. Sowerby hurried back to town and put himself into communication with Miss Dunstable’s lawyer.", "이제 내 독자들은 그 훌륭한 누이동생 해럴드 스미스 부인이 오라비를 위해 마지막으로 어떤 걸음을 떼어 놓았는지 어지간히 다 이해할 것이며, 그날 낮 동안에 사워비 씨가 서둘러 런던으로 돌아가 던스터블 양의 변호사와 연락을 맺었다는 것을 알게 되어도 놀라지 않을 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVIII. IS THERE CAUSE OR JUST IMPEDIMENT?", "# 제38장. 혼인을 막을 까닭이나 마땅한 장애가 있는가?"]], [["I now purpose to visit another country house in Barsetshire, but on this occasion our sojourn shall be in the eastern division, in which, as in every other county in England, electioneering matters are paramount at the present moment.", "나는 이제 바셋주의 또 다른 시골 저택을 찾아가 볼 작정인데, 이번에 우리가 머물 곳은 주의 동부 지구다. 잉글랜드의 다른 어느 주와 마찬가지로 이곳에서도 지금은 선거 문제가 무엇보다 중대하다."], ["It has been mentioned that Mr. Gresham, junior, young Frank Gresham as he was always called, lived at a place called Boxall Hill.", "아들 그레셤 씨, 곧 늘 젊은 프랭크 그레셤이라 불리는 이가 박솔 힐이라는 곳에 산다는 이야기는 이미 나온 바 있다."], ["This property had come to his wife by will, and he was now settled there,—seeing that his father still held the family seat of the Greshams at Greshamsbury.", "이 재산은 유언으로 그의 아내에게 넘어온 것이고, 그의 아버지가 아직 그레셤스베리에 있는 그레셤 집안의 본가를 지키고 있는 터라 그는 이제 그곳에 눌러살고 있었다."]], [["At the present moment Miss Dunstable was staying at Boxall Hill with Mrs. Frank Gresham.", "지금으로 말하면 던스터블 양은 박솔 힐에서 프랭크 그레셤 부인과 함께 지내고 있었다."], ["They had left London,—as, indeed, all the world had done, to the terrible dismay of the London tradesmen.", "두 사람은 런던을 떠나 있었으니—사실 온 세상이 다 떠나 버려서 런던 상인들은 몹시 낙담하고 있었다."], ["This dissolution of Parliament was ruining everybody except the country publicans, and had of course destroyed the London season among other things.", "이번 의회 해산은 시골 술집 주인들만 빼고 모두를 망치고 있었고, 다른 여러 가지와 더불어 런던 사교철도 물론 부수어 놓았다."]], [["Mrs. Harold Smith had only just managed to catch Miss Dunstable before she left London; but she did do so, and the great heiress had at once seen her lawyers, and instructed them how to act with reference to the mortgages on the Chaldicotes property.", "해럴드 스미스 부인은 던스터블 양이 런던을 떠나기 전에 겨우 붙잡았다."], ["Miss Dunstable was in the habit of speaking of herself and her own pecuniary concerns as though she herself were rarely allowed to meddle in their management; but this was one of those small jokes which she ordinarily perpetrated; for in truth few ladies, and perhaps not many gentlemen, have a more thorough knowledge of their own concerns or a more potent voice in their own affairs, than was possessed by Miss Dunstable.", "그러나 어쨌든 붙잡기는 했고, 그 큰 상속인은 곧바로 제 변호사들을 만나 콜디코츠 영지에 걸린 저당을 어떻게 다룰지 지시해 두었다. 던스터블 양은 저 자신과 저 자신의 금전 문제를 두고, 마치 그 관리에 자기가 좀처럼 끼어들지 못하는 사람인 양 말하는 습관이 있었다."], ["Circumstances had lately brought her much into Barsetshire and she had there contracted very intimate friendships.", "그러나 이것은 그녀가 늘 저지르는 자그마한 농의 하나였다."], ["She was now disposed to become, if possible, a Barsetshire proprietor, and with this view had lately agreed with young Mr.", "실상 저 자신의 일을 던스터블 양만큼 속속들이 알고 저 자신의 일에 던스터블 양만큼 큰 목소리를 내는 부인은 거의 없었고, 신사라 해도 그리 많지 않았을 것이다."], ["Gresham that she would become the purchaser of the Crown property.", "근래의 사정이 그녀를 자주 바셋주로 데려왔고, 그녀는 그곳에서 아주 친밀한 우정을 맺었다."], ["As, however, the purchase had been commenced in his name, it was so to be continued; but now, as we are aware, it was rumoured that, after all, the duke, or, if not the duke, then the Marquis of Dumbello, was to be the future owner of the Chace.", "그녀는 이제 될 수 있으면 바셋주의 토지 소유주가 되고 싶은 마음이 있어서, 그런 뜻으로 얼마 전 젊은 그레셤 씨와 왕실 소유 재산을 자기가 사들이기로 합의해 두었다. 다만 매입이 그의 이름으로 시작되었으므로 그대로 그의 이름으로 이어 가기로 했다."], ["Miss Dunstable, however, was not a person to give up her object if she could attain it, nor, under the circumstances, was she at all displeased at finding herself endowed with the power of rescuing the Sowerby portion of the Chaldicotes property from the duke’s clutches.", "그런데 이제, 우리가 아는 대로, 결국 공작이, 공작이 아니라면 덤벨로 후작이 체이스의 다음 주인이 될 것이라는 소문이 돌고 있었다. 그러나 던스터블 양은 이룰 수 있는 일이라면 뜻을 접는 사람이 아니었고, 게다가 이런 사정에서 사워비 몫의 콜디코츠 영지를 공작의 손아귀에서 건져 낼 힘이 제게 있음을 알게 된 것이 조금도 싫지 않았다."], ["Why had the duke meddled with her, or with her friend, as to the other property?", "공작이 무슨 까닭으로 그 다른 재산을 두고 자기를, 또는 자기 벗을 건드렸단 말인가?"], ["Therefore it was arranged that the full amount due to the duke on mortgage should be ready for immediate payment; but it was arranged also that the security as held by Miss Dunstable should be very valid.", "그리하여 저당으로 공작에게 갚아야 할 돈은 전액 곧바로 치를 수 있게 마련해 두기로 했다. 그러나 던스터블 양이 쥐는 담보도 아주 확실해야 한다는 것 역시 함께 마련되었다."]], [["Miss Dunstable, at Boxall Hill or at Greshamsbury, was a very different person from Miss Dunstable in London; and it was this difference which so much vexed Mrs.", "박솔 힐이나 그레셤스베리에 있는 던스터블 양은 런던에 있는 던스터블 양과 아주 다른 사람이었다. 그리고 그레셤 부인을 그토록 속상하게 한 것이 바로 이 다름이었다."], ["Gresham; not that her friend omitted to bring with her into the country her London wit and aptitude for fun, but that she did not take with her up to town the genuine goodness and love of honesty which made her loveable in the country.", "그 벗이 런던의 재치와 놀이에 능한 솜씨를 시골까지 가지고 오지 않는다는 것이 아니라, 시골에서 그녀를 사랑스럽게 만들어 주는 참된 선함과 정직을 사랑하는 마음을 런던으로 가지고 올라가지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["She was as it were two persons, and Mrs. Gresham could not understand that any lady should permit herself to be more worldly at one time of the year than at another—or in one place than in any other.", "그녀는 말하자면 두 사람이었고, 그레셤 부인은 어떻게 한 부인이 한 해의 어느 철에는 다른 철보다, 또 어느 곳에서는 다른 어느 곳보다 더 세속적이 되도록 스스로를 놓아둘 수 있는지 이해할 수가 없었다."]], [["“Well, my dear, I am heartily glad we’ve done with that,”", "“아이참, 이봐요, 그걸 끝냈으니 나는 진심으로 기뻐요.”"], ["Miss Dunstable said to her, as she sat herself down to her desk in the drawing-room on the first morning after her arrival at Boxall Hill.", "박솔 힐에 닿은 다음 첫 아침, 응접실 책상 앞에 앉으며 던스터블 양이 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“What does ‘that’ mean?”", "“‘그걸’이 뭘 말하는 거예요?”"], ["said Mrs. Gresham.", "그레셤 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Why, London and smoke and late hours, and standing on one’s legs for four hours at a stretch on the top of one’s own staircase, to be bowed at by any one who chooses to come.", "“아, 런던하고 연기하고 늦은 밤들, 그리고 제집 계단 맨 위에 내처 네 시간을 서서 오고 싶어 하는 사람 아무에게나 절을 받는 일 말이에요."], ["That’s all done—for one year, at any rate.”", "그게 다 끝났어요—적어도 한 해 동안은요.”"]], [["“You know you like it.”", "“좋아하시는 걸 아시잖아요.”"]], [["“No, Mary; that’s just what I don’t know.", "“아니에요, 메리."], ["I don’t know whether I like it or not.", "그게 바로 내가 모르는 거예요."], ["Sometimes, when the spirit of that dearest of all women, Mrs.", "내가 그걸 좋아하는지 안 좋아하는지 나도 몰라요."], ["Harold Smith, is upon me, I think that I do like it; but then again, when other spirits are on me, I think that I don’t.”", "이따금 그 누구보다 다정한 여인, 해럴드 스미스 부인의 영이 내게 임하면 좋아하는 것 같기도 해요. 그러다가 또 다른 영들이 임하면 안 좋아하는 것 같고요.”"]], [["“And who are the owners of the other spirits?”", "“그러면 그 다른 영들의 임자는 누구예요?”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["you are one, of course.", "당신도 물론 하나죠."], ["But you are a weak little thing, by no means able to contend with such a Samson as Mrs. Harold.", "하지만 당신은 약하고 조그만 사람이라, 스미스 부인 같은 삼손과는 도무지 겨룰 수가 없어요."], ["And then you are a little given to wickedness yourself, you know.", "게다가 당신도 알다시피 스스로 사악한 데로 조금 기운 사람이고요."], ["You’ve learned to like London well enough since you sat down to the table of Dives.", "디베스의 식탁에 앉게 된 뒤로 당신은 런던을 어지간히 좋아하는 법을 배웠지요."], ["Your uncle,—he’s the real impracticable, unapproachable Lazarus who declares that he can’t come down because of the big gulf.", "당신 삼촌—그이야말로 참으로 어찌해 볼 수 없고 가까이 갈 수도 없는 나사로여서, 큰 구렁이 있으니 내려올 수 없다고 말하는 사람이에요."], ["I wonder how he’d behave, if somebody left him ten thousand a year?”", "누가 그이에게 연 만 파운드를 물려주면 그이가 어떻게 처신할까요?”"]], [["“Uncommonly well, I am sure.”", "“보나 마나 아주 훌륭하게 처신하시겠죠.”"]], [["“Oh, yes; he is a Lazarus now, so of course we are bound to speak well of him; but I should like to see him tried.", "“아, 그렇죠. 지금은 나사로이니 우리야 물론 좋게 말해야 하고요."], ["I don’t doubt but what he’d have a house in Belgrave Square, and become noted for his little dinners before the first year of his trial was over.”", "하지만 나는 그이가 시험받는 것을 보고 싶어요. 시험의 첫해가 지나기도 전에 벨그레이브 광장에 집을 얻고 조촐한 만찬으로 이름이 나 있을 게 틀림없어요.”"]], [["“Well, and why not?", "“그럼 어때요?"], ["You would not wish him to be an anchorite?”", "그이가 은수자가 되기를 바라시는 건 아니잖아요?”"]], [["“I am told that he is going to try his luck,—not with ten thousand a year, but with one or two.”", "“들리는 말로는 그이가 운을 시험해 보려 한다더군요—연 만 파운드로가 아니라 천이나 이천 파운드로요.”"]], [["“What do you mean?”", "“무슨 말씀이세요?”"]], [["“Jane tells me that they all say at Greshamsbury that he is going to marry Lady Scatcherd.”", "“제인이 그러는데, 그레셤스베리에서는 다들 그이가 레이디 스캐처드와 결혼할 거라고 한다는군요.”"], ["Now Lady Scatcherd was a widow living in those parts; an excellent woman, but one not formed by nature to grace society of the highest order.", "레이디 스캐처드는 그 근처에 사는 미망인이었다. 훌륭한 여인이었으나 가장 높은 축의 사교계를 빛낼 만하게 타고나지는 못한 사람이었다."]], [["“What!” exclaimed Mrs.", "“뭐라고요!”"], ["Gresham, rising up from her chair while her eyes flashed with anger at such a rumour.", "그레셤 부인은 그런 소문에 노여움으로 눈을 번득이며 의자에서 일어서면서 소리쳤다."]], [["“Well, my dear, don’t eat me.", "“참, 이봐요, 나를 잡아먹지는 말아요."], ["I don’t say it is so; I only say that Jane said so.”", "그렇다는 게 아니라, 제인이 그랬다는 말만 하는 거예요.”"]], [["“Then you ought to send Jane out of the house.”", "“그러면 제인을 집에서 내보내셔야죠.”"]], [["“You may be sure of this, my dear: Jane would not have told me if somebody had not told her.”", "“이건 믿어도 좋아요, 이봐요. 누가 제인에게 말해 주지 않았다면 제인이 내게 말했을 리 없어요.”"]], [["“And you believed it?”", "“그래서 그 말을 믿으셨어요?”"]], [["“I have said nothing about that.”", "“그 얘기는 하지 않았어요.”"]], [["“But you look as if you had believed it.”", "“하지만 믿으신 얼굴인데요.”"]], [["“Do I?", "“내가요?"], ["Let us see what sort of a look it is, this look of faith.”", "그럼 그게 어떤 얼굴인지 봅시다, 이 믿는 얼굴이라는 게.”"], ["And Miss Dunstable got up and went to the glass over the fire-place.", "그러고서 던스터블 양은 일어나 난로 위 거울로 갔다."], ["“But, Mary, my dear, ain’t you old enough to know that you should not credit people’s looks?", "“그런데 메리, 사람의 얼굴을 믿어서는 안 된다는 것쯤은 알 만한 나이가 됐잖아요?"], ["You should believe nothing now-a-days; and I did not believe the story about poor Lady Scatcherd.", "요즘 세상에는 아무것도 믿을 게 없어요. 그리고 나는 딱한 레이디 스캐처드에 관한 그 이야기를 믿지 않았어요."], ["I know the doctor well enough to be sure that he is not a marrying man.”", "선생님이 결혼하는 남자가 아니라는 걸 확실히 알 만큼은 내가 선생님을 잘 알아요.”"]], [["“What a nasty, hackneyed, false phrase that is—that of a marrying man!", "“그건 참 고약하고 케케묵고 거짓된 말이에요—결혼하는 남자라니!"], ["It sounds as though some men were in the habit of getting married three or four times a month.”", "어떤 남자들은 한 달에 세 번 네 번 결혼하는 습관이라도 있는 것처럼 들리잖아요.”"]], [["“It means a great deal all the same.", "“그래도 그 말에는 담긴 뜻이 많아요."], ["One can tell very soon whether a man is likely to marry or no.”", "한 남자가 결혼할 만한 사람인지 아닌지는 아주 금방 알 수 있어요.”"]], [["“And can one tell the same of a woman?”", "“여자에 대해서도 같은 것을 알 수 있나요?”"]], [["“The thing is so different.", "“그건 사정이 아주 달라요."], ["All unmarried women are necessarily in the market; but if they behave themselves properly they make no signs.", "시집 안 간 여자라면 누구나 어쩔 수 없이 장에 나온 셈이지요. 하지만 처신을 바르게 하면 아무 기색도 내지 않아요."], ["Now there was Griselda Grantly; of course she intended to get herself a husband, and a very grand one she has got; but she always looked as though butter would not melt in her mouth.", "그리젤다 그랜틀리를 보세요. 물론 그 아이는 남편을 얻을 작정이었고, 아주 대단한 남편을 얻었지요."], ["It would have been very wrong to call her a marrying girl.”", "그러면서도 늘 입에 물린 버터도 녹지 않을 듯한 낯빛이었어요. 그 아이를 결혼하는 처녀라고 부르는 건 아주 잘못된 일이었을 거예요.”"]], [["“Oh, of course she was,”", "“아, 그야 물론 그랬죠.”"], ["says Mrs. Gresham, with that sort of acrimony which one pretty young woman so frequently expresses with reference to another.", "그레셤 부인은, 한 예쁜 젊은 부인이 다른 예쁜 젊은 부인을 두고 곧잘 드러내는 그런 앙칼진 투로 말한다."], ["“But if one could always tell of a woman, as you say you can of a man, I should be able to tell of you.", "“하지만 당신이 남자에 대해서 늘 알 수 있다고 하듯이 여자에 대해서도 늘 알 수 있다면, 나는 당신에 대해서도 알 수 있어야 할 텐데요."], ["Now, I wonder whether you are a marrying woman? I have never been able to make up my mind yet.”", "그런데 당신이 결혼하는 여자인지 어떤지는 나도 아직 마음을 정하지 못했어요.”"]], [["Miss Dunstable remained silent for a few moments, as though she were at first minded to take the question as being, in some sort, one made in earnest; but then she attempted to laugh it off.", "던스터블 양은 잠깐 말이 없었다. 처음에는 그 물음을 어느 모로 진심에서 나온 것으로 받아들일 마음이었던 듯했다."], ["“Well, I wonder at that,”", "그러나 곧 웃음으로 넘겨 버리려 했다. “참, 그건 뜻밖이네요.”"], ["said she, “as it was only the other day I told you how many offers I had refused.”", "그녀가 말했다. “바로 며칠 전에 내가 청혼을 몇 번이나 거절했는지 말해 줬는데 말이에요.”"]], [["“Yes; but you did not tell me whether any had been made that you meant to accept.”", "“네, 하지만 받아들일 마음이 든 청혼이 있었는지는 말해 주지 않으셨죠.”"]], [["“None such was ever made to me.", "“그런 청혼은 한 번도 받아 본 적이 없어요."], ["Talking of that, I shall never forget your cousin, the Honourable George.”", "그 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 나는 당신 사촌인 아너러블 조지를 결코 잊지 못할 거예요.”"]], [["“He is not my cousin.”", "“그이는 내 사촌이 아니에요.”"]], [["“Well, your husband’s.", "“그럼 남편 쪽 사촌이죠."], ["It would not be fair to show a man’s letters; but I should like to show you his.”", "남자의 편지를 보여 주는 건 온당하지 않겠지만, 그이 편지는 당신에게 보여 주고 싶네요.”"]], [["“You are determined, then, to remain single?”", "“그러면 혼자 지내기로 마음을 굳히신 거예요?”"]], [["“I didn’t say that.", "“그렇다고는 안 했어요."], ["But why do you cross-question me so?”", "그런데 왜 나를 이렇게 따져 물으세요?”"]], [["“Because I think so much about you.", "“당신 생각을 그만큼 많이 하니까요."], ["I am afraid that you will become so afraid of men’s motives as to doubt that any one can be honest.", "나는 당신이 남자들의 속셈을 너무 겁내다가 아무도 정직할 수 있다는 것을 의심하게 될까 봐 걱정이에요."], ["And yet sometimes I think you would be a happier woman and a better woman, if you were married.”", "그리고 또 이따금 나는 당신이 결혼하면 더 행복한 여인, 더 나은 여인이 될 거라는 생각도 해요.”"]], [["“To such an one as the Honourable George, for instance?”", "“예컨대 아너러블 조지 같은 사람에게 가서요?”"]], [["“No, not to such an one as him; you have probably picked out the worst.”", "“아니에요, 그런 사람은 아니고요. 당신은 아마 가장 나쁜 예를 골라내셨네요.”"]], [["“Or to Mr. Sowerby?”", "“아니면 사워비 씨에게요?”"]], [["“Well, no; not to Mr.", "“아니, 그것도 아니에요."], ["Sowerby, either.", "사워비 씨도 아니에요."], ["I would not have you marry any man that looked to you for your money principally.”", "나는 당신이 주로 당신 돈을 바라보는 남자와 결혼하는 건 원하지 않아요.”"]], [["“And how is it possible that I should expect any one to look to me principally for anything else?", "“그러면 내가 어떻게 남더러 나를 두고 주로 다른 무엇을 바라보라고 바랄 수 있겠어요?"], ["You don’t see my difficulty, my dear?", "당신은 내 딱한 처지를 못 보시는군요, 이봐요?"], ["If I had only five hundred a year, I might come across some decent middle-aged personage, like myself, who would like me, myself, pretty well, and would like my little income—pretty well also.", "내게 연 오백 파운드밖에 없다면, 나 같은 점잖은 중년 인물을 어디서 만날 수도 있겠지요. 그이는 나라는 사람 자체를 어지간히 좋아하고, 내 자그마한 수입도—역시 어지간히 좋아할 거예요."], ["He would not tell me any violent lie, and perhaps no lie at all.", "심한 거짓말은 하지 않을 테고, 어쩌면 거짓말이라고는 하나도 안 할지도 몰라요."], ["I should take to him in the same sort of way, and we might do very well.", "나도 같은 식으로 그이에게 마음이 갈 테고, 우리는 아주 잘 지낼 수 있을 거예요."], ["But, as it is, how is it possible that any disinterested person should learn to like me?", "그런데 지금 형편으로는, 사심 없는 사람이 나를 좋아하게 되는 일이 어떻게 있을 수 있겠어요?"], ["How could such a man set about it?", "그런 남자가 어떻게 그 일을 시작하겠어요?"], ["If a sheep have two heads, is not the fact of the two heads the first and, indeed, only thing which the world regards in that sheep?", "양에게 머리가 둘 달렸다면, 머리가 둘이라는 사실이야말로 세상이 그 양에게서 맨 처음, 아니 실상 오직 하나 보는 것이 아니겠어요?"], ["Must it not be so as a matter of course?", "그것이 당연한 일이 아니겠어요?"], ["I am a sheep with two heads.", "나는 머리 둘 달린 양이에요."], ["All this money which my father put together, and which has been growing since like grass under May showers, has turned me into an abortion.", "우리 아버지가 모아 놓았고 그 뒤로 오월 소나기 맞은 풀처럼 자라 온 이 돈이 나를 기형으로 만들어 놓았어요."], ["I am not the giantess eight feet high, or the dwarf that stands in the man’s hand,—”", "나는 키가 여덟 피트나 되는 여자 거인도 아니고, 사람 손바닥 위에 올라서는 난쟁이도 아니지만—”"]], [["“Or the two-headed sheep—”", "“아니면 머리 둘 달린 양도—”"]], [["“But I am the unmarried woman with—half a dozen millions of money—as I believe some people think.", "“하지만 나는 돈이—어떤 사람들이 믿는 듯한 대로 육백만 파운드는 되는—시집 안 간 여자예요."], ["Under such circumstances have I a fair chance of getting my own sweet bit of grass to nibble, like any ordinary animal with one head?", "그런 형편에서, 머리 하나 달린 여느 짐승처럼 뜯어먹을 내 몫의 달콤한 풀을 얻을 가망이 나에게 공평하게 있겠어요?"], ["I never was very beautiful, and I am not more so now than I was fifteen years ago.”", "나는 그리 아름다운 적이 없었고, 지금은 열다섯 해 전보다 더 아름다울 것도 없어요.”"]], [["“I am quite sure it is not that which hinders it.", "“그걸 막고 있는 게 그것이 아니라는 건 나도 잘 알아요."], ["You would not call yourself plain; and even plain women are married every day, and are loved, too, as well as pretty women.”", "스스로를 못생겼다고는 하지 않으시겠죠. 그리고 못생긴 여자들도 날마다 결혼하고, 예쁜 여자들만큼이나 사랑도 받아요.”"]], [["“Are they?", "“그래요?"], ["Well, we won’t say more about that; but I don’t expect a great many lovers on account of my beauty.", "그럼 그 얘기는 그만해요. 하지만 나는 내 아름다움 덕분에 사랑하는 이들이 많이 몰려올 거라고는 기대하지 않아요."], ["If ever you hear of such an one, mind you tell me.”", "혹시 그런 이가 있다는 얘기를 들으면 꼭 알려 줘요.”"]], [["It was almost on Mrs. Gresham’s tongue to say that she did know of one such—meaning her uncle.", "그레셤 부인의 혀끝에는, 자기가 그런 이 하나를 알고 있다는 말이 거의 올라와 있었다—삼촌을 두고 하는 말이었다."], ["But in truth, she did not know any such thing; nor could she boast to herself that she had good grounds for feeling that it was so—certainly none sufficient to justify her in speaking of it.", "그러나 사실 그녀는 그런 것을 알고 있는 게 아니었다. 또 그것이 그렇다고 느낄 만한 좋은 근거가 자기에게 있다고 스스로 자랑할 수도 없었다."], ["Her uncle had said no word to her on the matter, and had been confused and embarrassed when the idea of such a marriage was hinted to him.", "그것을 입에 올려도 될 만큼 넉넉한 근거는 확실히 없었다. 삼촌은 그 일에 관해 그녀에게 한마디도 하지 않았고, 그런 혼인 이야기가 그에게 비쳐졌을 때에는 어리둥절해하고 난처해했다."], ["But, nevertheless, Mrs. Gresham did think that each of these two was well inclined to love the other, and that they would be happier together than they would be single.", "그러나 그럼에도 그레셤 부인은 이 두 사람이 각기 서로를 사랑할 마음이 넉넉히 있으며, 둘이 함께 있으면 혼자 있을 때보다 더 행복할 것이라고 생각했다."], ["The difficulty, however, was very great, for the doctor would be terribly afraid of being thought covetous in regard to Miss Dunstable’s money; and it would hardly be expected that she should be induced to make the first overture to the doctor.", "그러나 어려움은 몹시 컸다. 선생은 던스터블 양의 돈을 탐낸다는 소리를 들을까 몹시 두려워할 것이고, 그렇다고 그녀가 선생에게 먼저 말을 꺼내도록 이끌 수 있으리라고는 좀처럼 기대할 수 없었다."]], [["“My uncle would be the only man that I can think of that would be at all fit for you,”", "“내가 생각해 낼 수 있는 사람으로 당신에게 조금이라도 맞을 남자는 우리 삼촌뿐이에요.”"], ["said Mrs. Gresham, boldly.", "그레셤 부인이 대담하게 말했다."]], [["“What, and rob poor Lady Scatcherd!”", "“뭐라고요, 딱한 레이디 스캐처드의 것을 빼앗으라고요!”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, very well.", "“아, 좋아요."], ["If you choose to make a joke of his name in that way, I have done.”", "그런 식으로 삼촌 이름을 두고 농을 하시겠다면 나는 그만하겠어요.”"]], [["“Why, God bless the girl!", "“아이고, 이 아가씨 좀 보게!"], ["what does she want me to say?", "그럼 나더러 무슨 말을 하라는 거예요?"], ["And as for joking, surely that is innocent enough.", "그리고 농이라고 해도, 이만하면 해롭지 않잖아요."], ["You’re as tender about the doctor as though he were a girl of seventeen.”", "당신은 선생님이 열일곱 살 처녀라도 되는 것처럼 그이 얘기에 조심스러워하네요.”"]], [["“It’s not about him; but it’s such a shame to laugh at poor dear Lady Scatcherd.", "“삼촌 때문이 아니에요. 딱하고 다정한 레이디 스캐처드를 두고 웃는 게 너무 부끄러운 일이라서요."], ["If she were to hear it she’d lose all comfort in having my uncle near her.”", "그이가 그 말을 들으면 삼촌이 가까이 있는 것에서 얻는 위안을 다 잃어버릴 거예요.”"]], [["“And I’m to marry him, so that she may be safe with her friend!”", "“그래서 내가 그이와 결혼해야 한다고요, 그이가 벗과 함께 마음 놓고 있게 하려고요!”"]], [["“Very well; I have done.”", "“좋아요. 나는 그만하겠어요.”"], ["And Mrs. Gresham, who had already got up from her seat, employed herself very sedulously in arranging flowers which had been brought in for the drawing-room tables.", "그러고서 이미 자리에서 일어나 있던 그레셤 부인은 응접실 탁자에 놓으려고 들여온 꽃을 매만지는 일에 아주 부지런히 매달렸다."], ["Thus they remained silent for a minute or two, during which she began to reflect that, after all, it might probably be thought that she also was endeavouring to catch the great heiress for her uncle.", "그렇게 두 사람은 일이 분 동안 말이 없었고, 그동안 그녀는 결국 자기 역시 삼촌을 위해 그 큰 상속인을 붙잡으려 애쓰는 것으로 여겨질지도 모른다는 생각을 하기 시작했다."]], [["“And now you are angry with me,”", "“이제 나한테 노여워하는군요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["“No, I am not.”", "“아니에요.”"]], [["“Oh, but you are.", "“아니라니, 노여워하고 있어요."], ["Do you think I’m such a fool as not to see when a person’s vexed?", "사람이 속상해하는 걸 내가 못 알아볼 만큼 어리석다고 생각해요?"], ["You wouldn’t have twitched that geranium’s head off if you’d been in a proper frame of mind.”", "마음이 제대로 가라앉아 있었다면 그 제라늄 꽃대가리를 그렇게 홱 뜯어내지 않았을 거예요.”"]], [["“I don’t like that joke about Lady Scatcherd.”", "“레이디 스캐처드에 관한 그 농이 싫어요.”"]], [["“And is that all, Mary?", "“그게 다예요, 메리?"], ["Now do try and be true, if you can.", "이제 될 수 있으면 참되게 굴어 봐요."], ["You remember the bishop?", "주교님 기억하죠?"], ["Magna est veritas.”", "「마그나 에스트 베리타스」.”"]], [["“The fact is you’ve got into such a way of being sharp, and saying sharp things among your friends up in London, that you can hardly answer a person without it.”", "“사실은 당신이 런던의 벗들 사이에서 날카롭게 굴고 날카로운 말을 하는 데 하도 익숙해져서, 그러지 않고는 남에게 대답 한마디 못 하게 된 거예요.”"]], [["“Can’t I?", "“내가 그래요?"], ["Dear, dear, what a Mentor you are, Mary!", "아이고, 아이고, 메리, 당신은 어쩌면 그런 멘토르인지!"], ["No poor lad that ever ran up from Oxford for a spree in town got so lectured for his dissipation and iniquities as I do. Well, I beg Dr.", "옥스퍼드에서 런던으로 놀러 올라온 딱한 젊은이도 그 방탕과 악행 때문에 나만큼 훈계를 들은 적은 없을 거예요."], ["Thorne’s pardon, and Lady Scatcherd’s, and I won’t be sharp any more; and I will—let me see, what was it I was to do?", "자, 손 선생님께 용서를 빌고 레이디 스캐처드께도 빌어요. 앞으로는 날카롭게 굴지 않을 테고, 그리고 나는—어디 보자, 내가 무엇을 해야 했지요?"], ["Marry him myself, I believe; was not that it?”", "내가 그이와 결혼하는 것, 그거였죠?”"]], [["“No; you’re not half good enough for him.”", "“아니에요. 당신은 삼촌한테 반만큼도 어울리지 않아요.”"]], [["“I know that.", "“그건 나도 알아요."], ["I’m quite sure of that.", "아주 잘 알고 있어요."], ["Though I am so sharp, I’m very humble.", "이렇게 날카롭게 굴어도 나는 아주 겸손해요."], ["You can’t accuse me of putting any very great value on myself.”", "내가 스스로에게 아주 큰 값을 매긴다고는 나무라지 못할 거예요.”"]], [["“Perhaps not as much as you ought to do—on yourself.”", "“아마 매겨야 할 만큼도 못 매기시는 거죠—당신 자신에게요.”"]], [["“Now, what do you mean, Mary?", "“그건 무슨 뜻이에요, 메리?"], ["I won’t be bullied and teased, and have innuendos thrown out at me, because you’ve got something on your mind, and don’t quite dare to speak it out.", "나는 몰아세우는 것도, 놀리는 것도, 빈정대는 암시를 던지는 것도 참지 않겠어요. 당신 마음속에 무언가 있는데 그것을 똑바로 말할 용기가 나지 않아서 그러는 거잖아요."], ["If you have got anything to say, say it.”", "할 말이 있으면 말해요.”"]], [["But Mrs. Gresham did not choose to say it at that moment.", "그러나 그레셤 부인은 그 순간에 그것을 말하기로 마음먹지 않았다."], ["She held her peace, and went on arranging her flowers—now with a more satisfied air, and without destruction to the geraniums.", "그녀는 입을 다물고 계속 꽃을 매만졌다—이제는 좀 더 흡족한 낯빛으로, 제라늄을 못살게 굴지도 않고."], ["And when she had grouped her bunches properly she carried the jar from one part of the room to another, backwards and forwards, trying the effect of the colours, as though her mind was quite intent upon her flowers, and was for the moment wholly unoccupied with any other subject.", "그리고 꽃 다발을 알맞게 무리 지어 놓고는 화병을 방 이쪽에서 저쪽으로 앞뒤로 옮겨 가며 빛깔의 어울림을 살펴보았다. 마치 마음이 온통 꽃에만 쏠려 있어서 그 순간에는 다른 어떤 일에도 조금도 매여 있지 않은 듯이."]], [["But Miss Dunstable was not the woman to put up with this.", "그러나 던스터블 양은 이런 것을 참고 넘길 부인이 아니었다."], ["She sat silent in her place, while her friend made one or two turns about the room; and then she got up from her seat also.", "그녀는 제 자리에 잠자코 앉아 벗이 방을 한두 바퀴 도는 것을 지켜보다가, 자기도 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["“Mary,”", "“메리,”"], ["she said, “give over about those wretched bits of green branches and leave the jars where they are.", "그녀가 말했다. “그 지긋지긋한 푸른 가지 조각들은 그만두고 화병은 그냥 그 자리에 두세요."], ["You’re trying to fidget me into a passion.”", "나를 안달나게 해서 화를 내게 만들려는 거잖아요.”"]], [["“Am I?” said Mrs.", "“내가요?”"], ["Gresham, standing opposite to a big bowl, and putting her head a little on one side, as though she could better look at her handiwork in that position.", "그레셤 부인은 큰 사발 앞에 마주 서서, 그 자리에서 자기 솜씨를 더 잘 볼 수 있다는 듯 고개를 조금 옆으로 기울이며 말했다."]], [["“You know you are; and it’s all because you lack courage to speak out.", "“그러고 있는 걸 알잖아요. 그러는 건 다 당신이 똑바로 말할 용기가 없어서예요."], ["You didn’t begin at me in this way for nothing.”", "괜히 이런 식으로 나를 걸고 나선 게 아니잖아요.”"]], [["“I do lack courage.", "“나는 용기가 없어요."], ["That’s just it,”", "바로 그거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Gresham, still giving a twist here and a set there to some of the small sprigs which constituted the background of her bouquet.", "그레셤 부인은 여전히 꽃다발의 배경이 되는 작은 잔가지들을 여기서 한 번 비틀고 저기서 한 번 바로잡으며 말했다."], ["“I do lack courage—to have ill motives imputed to me.", "“나는 용기가 없어요—나쁜 속셈을 뒤집어쓸까 봐서요."], ["I was thinking of saying something, and I am afraid, and therefore I will not say it.", "무언가 말할 생각이었는데, 두려우니까 말하지 않겠어요."], ["And now, if you like, I will be ready to take you out in ten minutes.”", "그리고 이제, 원하신다면 십 분 뒤에 모시고 나갈 준비가 돼요.”"]], [["But Miss Dunstable was not going to be put off in this way.", "그러나 던스터블 양은 이런 식으로 물러설 사람이 아니었다."], ["And to tell the truth, I must admit that her friend Mrs. Gresham was not using her altogether well.", "그리고 사실을 말하자면, 그녀의 벗 그레셤 부인이 그녀를 도무지 온당하게 대하고 있지 않았음을 나는 시인해야 한다."], ["She should either have held her peace on the matter altogether,—which would probably have been her wiser course,—or she should have declared her own ideas boldly, feeling secure in her own conscience as to her own motives.", "그레셤 부인은 이 일에 관해 아예 입을 다물고 있어야 했거나—그것이 아마 더 슬기로운 길이었을 것이다—아니면 제 속셈에 관해 제 양심이 든든하다고 믿고서 제 생각을 대담하게 밝혀야 했다."], ["“I shall not stir from this room,”", "“나는 이 방에서 한 발도 움직이지 않겠어요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, “till I have had this matter out with you.", "던스터블 양이 말했다. “당신과 이 일을 끝까지 따져 보기 전에는요."], ["And as for imputations,—my imputing bad motives to you,—I don’t know how far you may be joking, and saying what you call sharp things to me; but you have no right to think that I should think evil of you.", "그리고 뒤집어쓴다는 것 말인데—내가 당신에게 나쁜 속셈을 씌운다는 것 말이에요—당신이 어디까지 농을 하고 있고 어디까지 이른바 날카로운 말을 하고 있는지는 나도 모르겠어요. 하지만 내가 당신을 나쁘게 여길 거라고 당신이 생각할 권리는 없어요."], ["If you really do think so, it is treason to the love I have for you.", "정말로 그렇게 생각한다면, 그것은 내가 당신에게 품은 사랑에 대한 반역이에요."], ["If I thought that you thought so, I could not remain in the house with you.", "당신이 그렇게 생각한다고 내가 생각한다면, 나는 당신과 한집에 머물러 있을 수 없어요."], ["What!", "어쩌면!"], ["you are not able to know the difference which one makes between one’s real friends and one’s mock friends!", "참된 벗과 겉치레 벗 사이에 사람이 두는 차이를 당신이 알지 못한다니! 나는 당신에게 그런 것이 있다고 믿지 않아요."], ["I don’t believe it of you, and I know you are only striving to bully me.”", "그리고 당신이 나를 몰아세우려고만 하고 있다는 것도 알아요.”"], ["And Miss Dunstable now took her turn of walking up and down the room.", "그러고서 이번에는 던스터블 양이 방을 오르내리며 걸었다."]], [["“Well, she shan’t be bullied,”", "“그래요, 이 사람은 몰아세우지 않기로 해요.” 그레셤 부인은 꽃을 놓고 벗의 허리에 팔을 두르며 말했다."], ["said Mrs.", "“적어도 여기 이 집 안에서는요."], ["Gresham, leaving her flowers, and putting her arm round her friend’s waist;—“at least, not here, in this house, although she is sometimes such a bully herself.”", "정작 저도 때로는 어지간히 몰아세우는 사람이면서 말이에요.”"]], [["“Mary, you have gone too far about this to go back.", "“메리, 당신은 이 일에 관해 되돌리기에는 너무 멀리 갔어요."], ["Tell me what it was that was on your mind, and as far as it concerns me, I will answer you honestly.”", "마음에 있던 것이 무엇인지 말해요. 그것이 나에 관한 것인 만큼 나도 정직하게 대답하겠어요.”"]], [["Mrs. Gresham now began to repent that she had made her little attempt.", "그레셤 부인은 이제 그 자그마한 시도를 한 것을 후회하기 시작했다."], ["That uttering of hints in a half-joking way was all very well, and might possibly bring about the desired result, without the necessity of any formal suggestion on her part; but now she was so brought to book that she must say something formal.", "반쯤 농으로 암시를 던지는 것은 아주 좋았고, 어쩌면 제 쪽에서 격식을 갖춘 이야기를 꺼낼 필요 없이도 바라던 결과를 가져올 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 이제는 이렇게 따져 묻는 자리에 서게 된 터라 격식을 갖춘 무언가를 말해야 했다."], ["She must commit herself to the expression of her own wishes, and to an expression also of an opinion as to what had been the wishes of her friend; and this she must do without being able to say anything as to the wishes of that third person.", "제가 바라는 바를 밝혀 스스로 매여야 하고, 아울러 제 벗이 무엇을 바랐던가에 관한 의견까지 밝혀야 했다. 게다가 그 제삼자가 무엇을 바라는지에 관해서는 한마디도 할 수 없는 채로 그것을 해야 했다."]], [["“Well,”", "“그럼,”"], ["she said, “I suppose you know what I meant.”", "그녀가 말했다. “내가 무슨 말을 하려 했는지 아시겠죠.”"]], [["“I suppose I did,”", "“알 것 같아요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable; “but it is not at all the less necessary that you should say it out.", "던스터블 양이 말했다. “하지만 그렇다고 당신이 그것을 입 밖에 내야 할 필요가 조금이라도 줄어드는 건 아니에요."], ["I am not to commit myself by my interpretation of your thoughts, while you remain perfectly secure in having only hinted your own.", "당신은 제 생각을 암시만 해 놓고 아주 안전하게 있으면서, 나만 당신 생각을 풀이하다가 매이는 건 안 돼요."], ["I hate hints, as I do—the mischief.", "나는 암시라는 게 싫어요, 꼭―고약한 것만큼이나."], ["I go in for the bishop’s doctrine.", "나는 주교님의 교리를 따르겠어요."], ["Magna est veritas.”", "「마그나 에스트 베리타스」.”"]], [["“Well, I don’t know,”", "“글쎄, 나는 모르겠어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Gresham.", "그레셤 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but I do,”", "하지만 나는 알아요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“And therefore go on, or for ever hold your peace.”", "“그러니 말을 잇든지, 아니면 영원히 입을 다물든지 하세요.”"]], [["“That’s just it,”", "“바로 그거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Gresham.", "그레셤 부인이 말했다."]], [["“What’s just it?”", "“무엇이 바로 그거예요?”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["“The quotation out of the Prayer Book which you finished just now.", "“방금 당신이 끝맺은 그 기도서의 구절이요."], ["‘If any of you know cause or just impediment why these two persons should not be joined together in holy matrimony, ye are to declare it.", "‘여러분 가운데 이 두 사람이 성혼으로 맺어짐을 막을 까닭이나 마땅한 장애를 아는 이가 있으면, 그것을 알려야 합니다."], ["This is the first time of asking.’ Do you know any cause, Miss Dunstable?”", "이것이 첫 번째 공시입니다.’ 던스터블 양, 무슨 까닭을 알고 있나요?”"]], [["“Do you know any, Mrs. Gresham?”", "“그레셤 부인, 당신은 무슨 까닭을 알고 있나요?”"]], [["“None, on my honour!”", "“제 명예를 걸고 하나도 없어요!”"], ["said the younger lady, putting her hand upon her breast.", "젊은 쪽 부인이 손을 가슴에 얹으며 말했다."]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but do you not?”", "그런데 정말 없어요?”"], ["and Miss Dunstable caught hold of her arm, and spoke almost abruptly in her energy.", "그러면서 던스터블 양은 그녀의 팔을 붙잡고, 힘이 들어간 나머지 거의 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“No, certainly not.", "“네, 정말 없어요."], ["What impediment?", "무슨 장애가 있겠어요?"], ["If I did, I should not have broached the subject.", "있다면 이 이야기를 꺼내지도 않았을 거예요."], ["I declare I think you would both be very happy together.", "나는 두 분이 함께 아주 행복할 거라고 정말 생각해요."], ["Of course, there is one impediment; we all know that.", "물론 장애가 하나 있지요. 그건 우리 모두 알아요."], ["That must be your look out.”", "그건 당신이 알아서 살펴야 할 일이에요.”"]], [["“What do you mean?", "“무슨 말이에요?"], ["What impediment?”", "무슨 장애요?”"]], [["“Your own money.”", "“당신 자신의 돈이요.”"]], [["“Psha!", "“쳇!"], ["Did you find that an impediment in marrying Frank Gresham?”", "당신은 프랭크 그레셤과 결혼할 때 그것이 장애가 되었나요?”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["the matter was so different there.", "거기서는 사정이 아주 달랐어요."], ["He had much more to give than I had, when all was counted.", "다 헤아려 보면 내가 내놓을 것보다 그이가 내놓을 것이 훨씬 많았어요."], ["And I had no money when we—when we were first engaged.”", "그리고 우리가—우리가 처음 약혼했을 때 나에게는 돈이 없었어요.” 그러면서 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 고였다."], ["And the tears came into her eyes as she thought of the circumstances of her early love;—all of which have been narrated in the county chronicles of Barsetshire, and may now be read by men and women interested therein.", "젊은 날 사랑의 사정을 떠올린 것이었다. 그 사정은 모두 바셋주 연대기에 적혀 있으니, 이제 그것에 마음이 끌리는 남자와 여자라면 누구든 읽어 볼 수 있을 것이다."]], [["“Yes; yours was a love match.", "“그래요, 당신 것은 사랑으로 맺은 혼사였죠."], ["I declare, Mary, I often think that you are the happiest woman of whom I ever heard; to have it all to give, when you were so sure that you were loved while you yet had nothing.”", "참말이지 메리, 나는 당신이 내가 들어 본 가운데 가장 행복한 여인이라는 생각을 자주 해요. 아직 아무것도 없었을 때 사랑받고 있다는 것을 그토록 확실히 알았고, 그러고서 온 것을 다 내줄 수 있었으니 말이에요.”"]], [["“Yes; I was sure,”", "“네, 나는 확실히 알았어요.”"], ["and she wiped the sweet tears from her eyes, as she remembered a certain day when a certain youth had come to her, claiming all kinds of privileges in a very determined manner.", "그러고서 그녀는 눈에서 그 달콤한 눈물을 닦았다. 어떤 젊은이가 아주 단단히 마음먹은 태도로 온갖 권리를 주장하며 자기에게 찾아온 어느 날을 떠올린 것이었다."], ["She had been no heiress then.", "그때 그녀는 상속인이 아니었다."], ["“Yes; I was sure.", "“네, 나는 확실히 알았어요."], ["But now with you, dear, you can’t make yourself poor again.", "하지만 이제 당신은, 이봐요, 스스로를 다시 가난하게 만들 수는 없어요."], ["If you can trust no one—”", "아무도 믿을 수 없다면—”"]], [["“I can.", "“믿을 수 있어요."], ["I can trust him.", "그이는 믿을 수 있어요."], ["As regards that I do trust him altogether.", "그것에 관해서라면 나는 그이를 온전히 믿어요."], ["But how can I tell that he would care for me?”", "하지만 그이가 나를 마음에 두어 줄지 내가 어떻게 알겠어요?”"]], [["“Do you not know that he likes you?”", "“그이가 당신을 좋아하는 걸 모르세요?”"]], [["“Ah, yes; and so he does Lady Scatcherd.”", "“아, 알아요. 그리고 레이디 스캐처드도 좋아하지요.”"]], [["“Miss Dunstable!”", "“던스터블 양!”"]], [["“And why not Lady Scatcherd, as well as me?", "“왜 레이디 스캐처드는 안 되고 나는 되나요?"], ["We are of the same kind—come from the same class.”", "우리는 같은 종류예요—같은 계층에서 나왔고요.”"]], [["“Not quite that, I think.”", "“그건 좀 아니라고 생각해요.”"]], [["“Yes, from the same class; only I have managed to poke myself up among dukes and duchesses, whereas she has been content to remain where God placed her.", "“아니에요, 같은 계층이에요. 다만 나는 공작과 공작부인들 사이에 억지로 끼어들었고, 그이는 하느님이 두신 자리에 그대로 있는 것으로 만족했을 뿐이지요."], ["Where I beat her in art, she beats me in nature.”", "내가 기교에서 그이를 앞선다면, 그이는 천성에서 나를 앞서요.”"]], [["“You know you are talking nonsense.”", "“터무니없는 소리를 하고 있는 걸 아시잖아요.”"]], [["“I think that we are both doing that—absolute nonsense; such as schoolgirls of eighteen talk to each other.", "“우리 둘이 다 그러고 있다고 생각해요—순전히 터무니없는 소리를요. 열여덟 살 여학생들이 서로 주고받는 것 같은 소리 말이에요."], ["But there is a relief in it; is there not?", "그런데 그게 마음이 놓이기도 하잖아요?"], ["It would be a terrible curse to have to talk sense always.", "늘 이치에 닿는 소리만 해야 한다면 그건 무서운 저주일 거예요."], ["Well, that’s done; and now let us go out.”", "자, 그건 다 됐어요. 이제 나가요.”"]], [["Mrs. Gresham was sure after this that Miss Dunstable would be a consenting party to the little arrangement which she contemplated.", "이 일이 있은 뒤 그레셤 부인은 자기가 마음에 두고 있는 그 자그마한 계획에 던스터블 양이 동의할 당사자가 될 것이라고 확신했다."], ["But of that she had felt but little doubt for some considerable time past.", "그러나 그 점은 이미 꽤 오래전부터 거의 의심하지 않고 있었다."], ["The difficulty lay on the other side, and all that she had as yet done was to convince herself that she would be safe in assuring her uncle of success if he could be induced to take the enterprise in hand.", "어려움은 다른 쪽에 있었고, 그녀가 지금까지 해낸 것이라고는, 삼촌이 그 일을 맡아 나서기만 한다면 성공을 틀림없이 약속해 줄 수 있다고 스스로 확인한 것뿐이었다."], ["He was to come to Boxall Hill that evening, and to remain there for a day or two.", "삼촌은 그날 저녁 박솔 힐로 와서 하루 이틀 머물기로 되어 있었다."], ["If anything could be done in the matter, now would be the time for doing it.", "이 일에 무언가 할 수 있는 것이 있다면 지금이 바로 그것을 할 때였다."], ["So at least thought Mrs. Gresham.", "적어도 그레셤 부인은 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["The doctor did come, and did remain for the allotted time at Boxall Hill; but when he left, Mrs.", "선생은 정말로 왔고, 정해진 만큼 박솔 힐에 머물렀다."], ["Gresham had not been successful.", "그러나 그가 떠날 때 그레셤 부인은 성공하지 못했다."], ["Indeed, he did not seem to enjoy his visit as was usual with him; and there was very little of that pleasant friendly intercourse which for some time past had been customary between him and Miss Dunstable.", "사실 그는 여느 때와 달리 이번 방문을 즐기는 것 같지도 않았다. 그리고 얼마 전부터 그와 던스터블 양 사이에 늘 있던 그 즐겁고 다정한 오고 감이 아주 적었다."], ["There were no passages of arms between them; no abuse from the doctor against the lady’s London gaiety; no raillery from the lady as to the doctor’s country habits.", "둘 사이에 티격태격하는 일도 없었다. 그 부인의 런던 흥청거림을 두고 선생이 야단치는 일도, 선생의 시골 습관을 두고 그 부인이 놀리는 일도 없었다."], ["They were very courteous to each other, and, as Mrs.", "두 사람은 서로 아주 예의를 차렸고, 그레셤 부인의 생각으로는 지나칠 만큼 예의를 차렸다."], ["Gresham thought, too civil by half; nor, as far as she could see, did they ever remain alone in each other’s company for five minutes at a time during the whole period of the doctor’s visit.", "또 그녀가 볼 수 있었던 한, 선생이 머문 그 기간 내내 둘이 단둘이서 오 분씩이라도 함께 있는 일이 없었다."], ["What, thought Mrs. Gresham to herself,—what if she had set these two friends at variance with each other, instead of binding them together in the closest and most durable friendship!", "어쩌면, 하고 그레셤 부인은 속으로 생각했다—두 벗을 가장 가깝고 가장 오래갈 우정으로 묶어 주기는커녕 내가 이 둘을 서로 틀어지게 해 놓은 것이라면 어쩌나!"]], [["But still she had an idea that, as she had begun to play this game, she must play it out.", "그러나 그녀는 여전히, 이 놀음을 시작한 이상 끝까지 놀아야 한다는 생각을 품고 있었다."], ["She felt conscious that what she had done must do evil, unless she could so carry it on as to make it result in good.", "자기가 한 일이 좋은 결과로 이어지도록 끌고 가지 못한다면 반드시 나쁜 일이 되고 말리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다."], ["Indeed, unless she could so manage, she would have done a manifest injury to Miss Dunstable in forcing her to declare her thoughts and feelings.", "아니, 그렇게 해내지 못한다면 던스터블 양에게 제 생각과 감정을 밝히게 만들어 그녀에게 뚜렷한 해를 입힌 셈이 될 것이었다."], ["She had already spoken to her uncle in London, and though he had said nothing to show that he approved of her plan, neither had he said anything to show that he disapproved it.", "그녀는 이미 런던에서 삼촌에게 말을 해 두었고, 삼촌은 제 계획을 찬성한다는 말도 하지 않았지만 반대한다는 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Therefore she had hoped through the whole of those three days that he would make some sign,—at any rate to her; that he would in some way declare what were his own thoughts on this matter.", "그러므로 그녀는 그 사흘 내내 삼촌이 무슨 기색이라도—적어도 자기에게라도—보여 주기를 바라고 있었다. 이 일에 관해 삼촌 자신의 생각이 무엇인지 어떻게든 밝혀 주기를 바라고 있었다."], ["But the morning of his departure came, and he had declared nothing.", "그러나 떠나는 날 아침이 왔는데도 삼촌은 아무것도 밝히지 않았다."]], [["“Uncle,”", "“삼촌,”"], ["she said, in the last five minutes of his sojourn there, after he had already taken leave of Miss Dunstable and shaken hands with Mrs.", "삼촌이 이미 던스터블 양에게 작별을 하고 그레셤 부인과 악수까지 한 뒤, 그가 그곳에 머무는 마지막 오 분 동안에 그녀가 말했다."], ["Gresham, “have you ever thought of what I said to you up in London?”", "“런던에서 제가 드린 말씀을 생각해 보신 적이 있으세요?”"]], [["“Yes, Mary; of course I have thought about it.", "“그래, 메리. 물론 생각해 봤다."], ["Such an idea as that, when put into a man’s head, will make itself thought about.”", "그런 생각이란 게 사람 머릿속에 한번 들어오면 저절로 생각하게 되는 거니까.”"]], [["“Well; and what next?", "“그럼요? 그래서 다음은요?"], ["Do talk to me about it.", "저한테 말씀 좀 해 보세요."], ["Do not be so hard and unlike yourself.”", "그렇게 딱딱하게, 삼촌답지 않게 굴지 마세요.”"]], [["“I have very little to say about it.”", "“그 일에 관해 할 말이 별로 없다.”"]], [["“I can tell you this for certain, you may if you like.”", "“이건 확실히 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 삼촌이 마음만 있으시면 그렇게 되실 수 있어요.”"]], [["“Mary!", "“메리!"], ["Mary!”", "메리!”"]], [["“I would not say so if I were not sure that I should not lead you into trouble.”", "“삼촌을 곤란에 빠뜨리는 게 아니라고 확신하지 않는다면 이런 말씀 안 드려요.”"]], [["“You are foolish in wishing this, my dear; foolish in trying to tempt an old man into a folly.”", "“이런 것을 바라는 건 어리석다, 메리. 늙은 사람을 꾀어 어리석은 짓을 하게 하려는 것도 어리석지.”"]], [["“Not foolish if I know that it will make you both happier.”", "“두 분이 다 더 행복해지실 거라는 걸 제가 아는데도 어리석은 건가요?”"]], [["He made her no further reply, but stooping down that she might kiss him, as was his wont, went his way, leaving her almost miserable in the thought that she had troubled all these waters to no purpose.", "그는 그녀에게 더 대답하지 않았다. 늘 하던 대로 그녀가 입 맞출 수 있도록 몸을 굽히고서 길을 떠났고, 아무 소용도 없이 이 물을 다 흐려 놓았다는 생각에 그녀를 거의 참담한 마음으로 남겨 두었다."], ["What would Miss Dunstable think of her?", "던스터블 양은 자기를 어떻게 생각할까?"], ["But on that afternoon Miss Dunstable seemed to be as happy and even-tempered as ever.", "그러나 그날 오후 던스터블 양은 여느 때처럼 행복하고 마음이 고른 듯이 보였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIX. HOW TO WRITE A LOVE LETTER.", "# 제39장. 연애 편지를 어떻게 쓰는가"]], [["Dr.", "손 선생은 박솔 힐을 떠나기 전에 조카딸에게 던진 몇 마디 말에서 스스로를 늙은이라 불렀다."], ["Thorne, in the few words which he spoke to his niece before he left Boxall Hill, had called himself an old man; but he was as yet on the right side of sixty by five good years, and bore about with him less of the marks of age than most men of fifty-five do bear.", "그러나 그는 아직 예순의 이쪽 편으로 넉넉히 다섯 해는 남아 있었고, 몸에 지닌 나이의 흔적은 쉰다섯 먹은 사내들 대부분이 지니는 것보다도 적었다."], ["One would have said in looking at him that there was no reason why he should not marry if he found that such a step seemed good to him; and looking at the age of the proposed bride, there was nothing unsuitable in that respect.", "그를 바라본 사람이라면 누구나, 그 걸음이 그에게 좋게 여겨진다면 혼인을 하지 못할 까닭이 없다고 말했을 것이다. 그리고 신부로 점쳐진 이의 나이를 보더라도, 그 점에서 걸맞지 않은 것은 아무것도 없었다."]], [["But nevertheless he felt almost ashamed of himself, in that he allowed himself even to think of the proposition which his niece had made.", "그러면서도 그는 조카딸이 내놓은 그 제안을 생각이라도 하도록 스스로에게 허락한 일이 거의 부끄러웠다."], ["He mounted his horse that day at Boxall Hill—for he made all his journeys about the county on horseback—and rode slowly home to Greshamsbury, thinking not so much of the suggested marriage as of his own folly in thinking of it.", "그날 그는 박솔 힐에서 말에 올라—주 안을 돌아다니는 길은 모두 말을 타고 다녔으므로—천천히 그레셤스베리의 집으로 향했는데, 그러면서 생각한 것은 권해진 혼사보다도 그것을 생각하고 있는 제 어리석음이었다."], ["How could he be such an ass at his time of life as to allow the even course of his way to be disturbed by any such idea?", "이 나이에 이르러 제 길의 고른 걸음이 그런 생각 하나에 흐트러지도록 내버려 두다니, 그렇게 얼빠진 짓이 있을 수 있는가?"], ["Of course he could not propose to himself such a wife as Miss Dunstable without having some thoughts as to her wealth; and it had been the pride of his life so to live that the world might know that he was indifferent about money.", "물론 그는 던스터블 양 같은 사람을 아내로 삼겠다고 스스로에게 내놓으면서 그 재산에 대한 생각을 조금도 하지 않을 수는 없었다. 그런데 그가 돈에 무심하다는 것을 세상이 알아주도록 살아온 것이야말로 그 평생의 자랑이었다."], ["His profession was all in all to him,—the air which he breathed as well as the bread which he ate; and how could he follow his profession if he made such a marriage as this?", "직업이 그에게는 전부였다. 그가 먹는 빵이자 그가 숨 쉬는 공기였다."], ["She would expect him to go to London with her; and what would he become, dangling at her heels there, known only to the world as the husband of the richest woman in the town?", "그런데 이런 혼인을 하고서 어떻게 직업을 이어 나가겠는가? 그녀는 그가 자기와 함께 런던으로 가기를 바랄 터였다."], ["The kind of life was one which would be unsuitable to him;—and yet, as he rode home, he could not resolve to rid himself of the idea.", "그러면 거기서 그녀의 뒤꽁무니에 매달린 채 세상에는 그 도시에서 가장 부유한 여인의 남편으로만 알려진 그는 무엇이 되겠는가? 그런 종류의 삶은 그에게 걸맞지 않은 것이었다."], ["He went on thinking of it, though he still continued to condemn himself for keeping it in his thoughts.", "그런데도 집으로 말을 몰아 가는 동안 그는 그 생각을 떨쳐 버리기로 마음을 정할 수가 없었다."], ["That night at home he would make up his mind, so he declared to himself; and would then write to his niece begging her to drop the subject.", "그는 그것을 계속 생각했고, 그러면서도 그것을 생각 속에 두고 있다고 스스로를 계속 나무랐다. 그 밤에 집에서 마음을 정하리라, 그렇게 그는 스스로에게 못을 박았다."], ["Having so far come to a resolution he went on meditating what course of life it might be well for him to pursue if he and Miss Dunstable should, after all, become man and wife.", "그리고 그때 조카딸에게 편지를 써서 그 이야기는 그만 접어 달라고 부탁하리라 하였다. 그렇게까지 결심에 이르고 나서, 그는 자기와 던스터블 양이 끝내 부부가 된다면 어떤 삶의 길을 걷는 것이 좋을지 골똘히 생각해 나갔다."]], [["There were two ladies whom it behoved him to see on the day of his arrival—whom, indeed, he generally saw every day except when absent from Greshamsbury.", "돌아온 날에 그가 꼭 보아야 할 부인이 둘 있었다. 사실 그레셤스베리를 떠나 있을 때가 아니면 그는 대개 날마다 그 둘을 보았다."], ["The first of these—first in the general consideration of the people of the place—was the wife of the squire, Lady Arabella Gresham, a very old patient of the doctor’s.", "그 가운데 첫째는—그 고장 사람들이 대체로 헤아리는 순서로 첫째는—지주의 아내인 레이디 애러벨라 그레셤으로, 선생의 아주 오래된 환자였다."], ["Her it was his custom to visit early in the afternoon; and then, if he were able to escape the squire’s daily invitation to dinner, he customarily went to the other, Lady Scatcherd, when the rapid meal in his own house was over.", "그녀는 오후 이른 때에 찾는 것이 그의 버릇이었다. 그러고 나서 지주가 날마다 하는 저녁 초대를 벗어날 수 있으면, 자기 집에서 급히 밥을 먹고 나서 나머지 한 사람, 곧 레이디 스캐처드에게 가는 것이 예사였다."], ["Such, at least, was his summer practice.", "적어도 여름철 그의 관례는 그러하였다."]], [["“Well, doctor, how are they at Boxall Hill?”", "“그래, 선생, 박솔 힐 사람들은 어떠하오?”"], ["said the squire, waylaying him on the gravel sweep before the door.", "지주가 문 앞 자갈길에서 그를 붙잡아 세우고 말했다."], ["The squire was very hard set for occupation in these summer months.", "이 여름 몇 달 동안 지주는 할 일을 찾기가 몹시 어려웠다."]], [["“Quite well, I believe.”", "“다들 잘 있는 것 같아요.”"]], [["“I don’t know what’s come to Frank.", "“프랭크에게 무슨 일이 있는지 모르겠소."], ["I think he hates this place now.", "이제는 이곳을 싫어하는 게 아닌가 싶소."], ["He’s full of the election, I suppose.”", "선거 생각으로 머리가 가득한 게지.”"]], [["“Oh, yes; he told me to say he should be over here soon.", "“아, 예. 곧 이리 오겠다고 전해 달라더군요."], ["Of course there’ll be no contest, so he need not trouble himself.”", "물론 겨룰 상대가 없을 테니 애쓸 일도 없지요.”"]], [["“Happy dog, isn’t he, doctor?", "“복 많은 녀석 아니오, 선생?"], ["to have it all before him instead of behind him.", "그 모든 것이 등 뒤가 아니라 앞에 놓여 있으니."], ["Well, well; he’s as good a lad as ever lived,—as ever lived.", "그래, 그래. 그만큼 착한 아이는 세상에 없었소,—없었지."], ["And let me see; Mary’s time—”", "그리고 어디 보자, 메리의 산달이…”"], ["And then there were a few very important words spoken on that subject.", "그러고 나서 그 문제를 두고 아주 중요한 몇 마디가 오갔다."]], [["“I’ll just step up to Lady Arabella now,”", "“이제 레이디 애러벨라께 잠깐 올라가 보겠어요.”"], ["said the doctor.", "선생이 말했다."]], [["“She’s as fretful as possible,”", "“더할 수 없이 짜증이 나 있소.”"], ["said the squire.", "지주가 말했다."], ["“I’ve just left her.”", "“방금 내가 나왔소.”"]], [["“Nothing special the matter, I hope?”", "“특별히 잘못된 데는 없겠지요?”"]], [["“No, I think not; nothing in your way, that is; only specially cross, which always comes in my way.", "“없소, 없는 것 같소. 그러니까 선생 쪽으로 올 일은 없다는 말이오."], ["You’ll stop and dine to-day, of course?”", "다만 유별나게 뾰로통해 있는데, 그건 늘 내 쪽으로 오지. 오늘은 물론 남아서 저녁을 하겠지요?”"]], [["“Not to-day, squire.”", "“오늘은 안 되겠어요, 지주님.”"]], [["“Nonsense; you will.", "“터무니없는 소리."], ["I have been quite counting on you.", "하실 거요. 나는 꽤나 선생을 믿고 있었소."], ["I have a particular reason for wanting to have you to-day,—a most particular reason.”", "오늘 선생을 붙들고 싶은 각별한 까닭이 있소,—아주 각별한 까닭이오.”"], ["But the squire always had his particular reasons.", "그러나 지주에게는 늘 각별한 까닭이 있었다."]], [["“I’m very sorry, but it is impossible to-day.", "“참 미안하지만 오늘은 안 될 일이에요."], ["I shall have a letter to write that I must sit down to seriously.", "편지 한 통을 써야 하는데, 자리를 잡고 앉아 진지하게 써야 하는 것이라서요."], ["Shall I see you when I come down from her ladyship?”", "마님 방에서 내려오면 뵐 수 있을까요?”"]], [["The squire turned away sulkily, almost without answering him, for he now had no prospect of any alleviation to the tedium of the evening; and the doctor went up-stairs to his patient.", "지주는 대답도 거의 하지 않고 뿌루퉁하게 돌아섰다. 이제 그 저녁의 지루함을 덜어 줄 가망이 없어졌기 때문이다. 그리고 선생은 이 층으로 환자에게 올라갔다."]], [["For Lady Arabella, though it cannot be said that she was ill, was always a patient.", "레이디 애러벨라는 앓고 있다고 말할 수는 없어도 늘 환자였다."], ["It must not be supposed that she kept her bed and swallowed daily doses, or was prevented from taking her share in such prosy gaieties as came from time to time in the way of her prosy life; but it suited her turn of mind to be an invalid and to have a doctor; and as the doctor whom her good fates had placed at her elbow thoroughly understood her case, no great harm was done.", "그녀가 자리에 누워 날마다 약을 복용하고 있었다거나, 그 심심한 삶의 길에 이따금 찾아드는 심심한 즐거움에 한몫 끼는 것을 막혔다고 여겨서는 안 된다. 다만 병자로 있는 것과 의사를 두는 것이 그녀의 마음결에 맞았고, 좋은 운수가 그녀의 팔꿈치 옆에 놓아 준 그 의사가 그녀의 경우를 속속들이 이해하고 있었으므로 크게 해로울 것은 없었다."]], [["“It frets me dreadfully that I cannot get to see Mary,”", "“메리를 보러 갈 수 없다는 게 참 몹시도 애가 타요.”"], ["Lady Arabella said, as soon as the first ordinary question as to her ailments had been asked and answered.", "그녀의 병세를 두고 으레 묻는 첫 물음이 오가고 나자 곧 레이디 애러벨라가 말했다."]], [["“She’s quite well and will be over to see you before long.”", "“아주 잘 있고, 오래지 않아 뵈러 올 거예요.”"]], [["“Now I beg that she won’t.", "“그러지 말라고 내가 부탁하겠어요."], ["She never thinks of coming when there can be no possible objection, and travelling, at the present moment, would be—”", "아무 지장이 없을 때에는 올 생각을 도무지 하지 않다가, 지금 같은 때에 길을 나서는 건—”"], ["Whereupon the Lady Arabella shook her head very gravely.", "그러고서 레이디 애러벨라는 아주 심각하게 고개를 저었다."], ["“Only think of the importance of it, doctor,” she said.", "“그게 얼마나 중대한 일인지 생각해 보세요, 선생.”"], ["“Remember the enormous stake there is to be considered.”", "그녀가 말했다. “걸려 있는 것이 얼마나 큰지 잊지 마세요.”"]], [["“It would not do her a ha’porth of harm if the stake were twice as large.”", "“걸려 있는 것이 두 배로 크다 해도 그 아이에게는 반 페니어치도 해롭지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“Nonsense, doctor, don’t tell me; as if I didn’t know myself.", "“터무니없는 소리, 선생, 내게 그런 말은 하지 마세요."], ["I was very much against her going to London this spring, but of course what I said was overruled.", "내가 나를 모를 리가 없잖아요. 나는 그 아이가 이번 봄에 런던으로 가는 걸 몹시 반대했지만, 물론 내가 한 말은 뒤로 밀렸어요."], ["It always is.", "늘 그렇지요."], ["I do believe Mr. Gresham went over to Boxall Hill, on purpose to induce her to go.", "그레셤 씨는 그 아이더러 가라고 부추기려고 일부러 박솔 힐까지 건너갔다고 나는 믿어요."], ["But what does he care?", "하지만 그이가 무엇을 마음에 두겠어요?"], ["He’s fond of Frank; but he never thinks of looking beyond the present day.", "프랭크는 좋아하지요. 그렇지만 오늘 하루 너머를 내다볼 생각은 도무지 하지 않아요."], ["He never did, as you know well enough, doctor.”", "그이는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었지요, 선생도 잘 알잖아요.”"]], [["“The trip did her all the good in the world,” said Dr.", "“그 나들이가 그 아이에게는 세상 무엇보다 좋았어요.”"], ["Thorne, preferring anything to a conversation respecting the squire’s sins.", "손 선생은 지주의 죄를 두고 이야기하기보다는 무엇이든 나은 편이라 여기며 말했다."]], [["“I very well remember that when I was in that way it wasn’t thought that such trips would do me any good.", "“내가 그런 몸이었을 때에는 그런 나들이가 나한테 무슨 좋은 일이 될 거라고들 여기지 않았던 게 아주 잘 기억나요."], ["But, perhaps, things are altered since then.”", "하지만 그때 뒤로 세상이 달라졌을지도 모르지요.”"]], [["“Yes, they are,”", "“예, 달라졌어요.”"], ["said the doctor.", "선생이 말했다."], ["“We don’t interfere so much now-a-days.”", "“요즘은 우리가 그렇게까지 간섭하지 않아요.”"]], [["“I know I never asked for such amusements when so much depended on quietness.", "“그렇게 많은 것이 조용히 있는 데 달려 있을 때에 내가 그런 재미를 청한 적이 없다는 건 나도 알아요."], ["I remember before Frank was born—and, indeed, when all of them were born— But as you say, things were different then; and I can easily believe that Mary is a person quite determined to have her own way.”", "프랭크가 태어나기 전 일이 기억나요—사실 그 아이들이 하나하나 태어날 때마다 그러했지요— 하지만 선생 말대로 그때는 세상이 달랐지요. 그리고 메리가 제 뜻을 꼭 이루려고 마음먹는 사람이라는 건 나도 어렵지 않게 믿어요.”"]], [["“Why, Lady Arabella, she would have stayed at home without wishing to stir if Frank had done so much as hold up his little finger.”", "“아니, 레이디 애러벨라, 프랭크가 새끼손가락을 하나 들어 보이기만 했어도 그 아이는 꼼짝할 생각 없이 집에 있었을 거예요.”"]], [["“So did I always. If Mr.", "“나도 늘 그러했어요."], ["Gresham made the slightest hint I gave way.", "그레셤 씨가 아주 작은 눈치라도 보이면 나는 물러섰지요."], ["But I really don’t see what one gets in return for such implicit obedience.", "하지만 그런 고분고분한 순종에 대해 무엇을 되받는지 나는 정말 모르겠어요."], ["Now this year, doctor, of course I should have liked to have been up in London for a week or two.", "그런데 올해에는요, 선생, 물론 나도 한두 주쯤 런던에 올라가 있고 싶었어요."], ["You seemed to think yourself that I might as well see Sir Omicron.”", "선생도 내가 오미크론 경을 한번 보는 것이 좋겠다고 여기는 듯했잖아요.”"]], [["“There could be no possible objection, I said.”", "“아무 지장이 없을 거라고 말했지요.”"]], [["“Well; no; exactly; and as Mr.", "“그래요, 아니, 바로 그거예요."], ["Gresham knew I wished it, I think he might as well have offered it.", "그리고 그레셤 씨는 내가 그걸 바라는 줄 알았으니 먼저 말을 내주었어도 좋았을 거라고 나는 생각해요."], ["I suppose there can be no reason now about money.”", "이제 돈 때문일 까닭은 없을 테니까요.”"]], [["“But I understood that Mary specially asked you and Augusta?”", "“하지만 메리가 특별히 부인과 오거스타를 청했다고 알고 있었는데요?”"]], [["“Yes; Mary was very good.", "“네, 메리는 참 착했어요."], ["She did ask me.", "나를 청하기는 했지요."], ["But I know very well that Mary wants all the room she has got in London.", "하지만 메리가 런던에서 가진 방을 하나도 남기지 않고 다 쓰고 싶어 하는 걸 나는 아주 잘 알아요."], ["The house is not at all too large for herself.", "그 집은 자기 하나에게도 조금도 넓지 않아요."], ["And, for the matter of that, my sister, the countess, was very anxious that I should be with her.", "그리고 그 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 자매인 백작부인은 내가 자기 곁에 있어 주기를 몹시 바랐어요."], ["But one does like to be independent if one can, and for one fortnight I do think that Mr. Gresham might have managed it.", "하지만 사람은 할 수 있으면 남에게 매이지 않고 지내고 싶은 것이고, 보름쯤이라면 그레셤 씨가 어떻게든 해 볼 수 있었을 거라고 나는 정말 생각해요."], ["When I knew that he was so dreadfully out at elbows I never troubled him about it,—though, goodness knows, all that was never my fault.”", "그이가 그렇게 무섭게 궁하던 때에는 그런 일로 그이를 괴롭힌 적이 없어요,—하기야 그게 하나도 내 잘못이 아니었다는 건 하늘이 알지요.”"]], [["“The squire hates London.", "“지주님은 런던을 싫어해요."], ["A fortnight there in warm weather would nearly be the death of him.”", "더운 날씨에 거기서 보름을 지내면 목숨이 거의 위태로울 거예요.”"]], [["“He might at any rate have paid me the compliment of asking me.", "“그래도 나에게 청해 주는 예절은 갖출 수 있었을 거예요."], ["The chances are ten to one I should not have gone.", "열에 아홉은 나도 가지 않았을 테지요."], ["It is that indifference that cuts me so.", "나를 그렇게 베는 것은 바로 그 무심함이에요."], ["He was here just now, and, would you believe it?—”", "그이가 방금 여기 있었는데, 그런데, 이런 게 믿어지겠어요?—”"]], [["But the doctor was determined to avoid further complaint for the present day.", "그러나 선생은 오늘 하루는 더 이상의 불평을 피하기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["“I wonder what you would feel, Lady Arabella, if the squire were to take it into his head to go away and amuse himself, leaving you at home.", "“레이디 애러벨라, 지주님이 부인을 집에 두고 어디로 나가서 제 재미나 보겠다고 마음먹는다면 부인 기분이 어떠할까요."], ["There are worse men than Mr. Gresham, if you will believe me.”", "내 말을 믿어 주신다면, 그레셤 씨보다 못한 사내들도 있어요.”"], ["All this was an allusion to Earl de Courcy, her ladyship’s brother, as Lady Arabella very well understood; and the argument was one which was very often used to silence her.", "이 모두가 마님의 오라비인 코시 백작을 가리킨 말임을 레이디 애러벨라는 아주 잘 알아들었다. 그리고 그 논거는 그녀의 입을 막는 데 아주 자주 쓰이는 것이었다."]], [["“Upon my word, then, I should like it better than his hanging about here doing nothing but attend to those nasty dogs.", "“참말로, 그렇다면 나는 그이가 여기 어정거리면서 그 지저분한 개들이나 돌보는 것보다 그편이 더 좋겠어요."], ["I really sometimes think that he has no spirit left.”", "나는 이따금 참으로 그이에게 기운이 하나도 남지 않았다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“You are mistaken there, Lady Arabella,”", "“그건 부인이 잘못 아신 거예요, 레이디 애러벨라.”"], ["said the doctor, rising with his hat in his hand and making his escape without further parley.", "선생은 모자를 손에 들고 일어서며 그렇게 말하고는 더 이야기를 나누지 않고 빠져나왔다."]], [["As he went home he could not but think that that phase of married life was not a very pleasant one.", "집으로 가는 길에 그는 혼인한 삶의 그 국면이 그리 즐거운 것은 아니라고 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Mr. Gresham and his wife were supposed by the world to live on the best of terms.", "그레셤 씨와 그 아내는 세상 사람들에게 가장 좋은 사이로 지내는 줄로 여겨졌다."], ["They always inhabited the same house, went out together when they did go out, always sat in their respective corners in the family pew, and in their wildest dreams after the happiness of novelty never thought of Sir Cresswell Cresswell.", "두 사람은 늘 같은 집에 살았고, 나갈 일이 있으면 함께 나갔고, 집안 신자석에서 언제나 각자의 구석 자리에 앉았고, 새로움이 주는 행복을 좇는 가장 거친 꿈속에서도 크레스웰 크레스웰 경을 생각해 본 적이 없었다."], ["In some respects—with regard, for instance, to the continued duration of their joint domesticity at the family mansion of Greshamsbury—they might have been taken for a pattern couple.", "어떤 점에서는—예컨대 그레셤스베리의 집안 저택에서 함께 살림을 이어 온 세월을 놓고 본다면—본보기가 될 부부로 여겨질 수도 있었다."], ["But yet, as far as the doctor could see, they did not seem to add much to the happiness of each other.", "그러나 그럼에도 선생이 보는 한 두 사람은 서로의 행복을 크게 보태 주는 것 같지 않았다."], ["They loved each other, doubtless, and had either of them been in real danger, that danger would have made the other miserable; but yet it might well be a question whether either would not be more comfortable without the other.", "두 사람은 틀림없이 서로를 사랑했고, 어느 한쪽이 참으로 위태로워졌다면 그 위태로움은 다른 한쪽을 참담하게 만들었을 것이다. 그러면서도 어느 쪽이든 상대가 없는 편이 더 편안하지 않을까 하는 것은 넉넉히 물음이 될 만한 일이었다."]], [["The doctor, as was his custom, dined at five, and at seven he went up to the cottage of his old friend Lady Scatcherd.", "선생은 버릇대로 다섯 시에 저녁을 먹고, 일곱 시에 오랜 벗 레이디 스캐처드의 오두막으로 올라갔다."], ["Lady Scatcherd was not a refined woman, having in her early days been a labourer’s daughter and having then married a labourer.", "레이디 스캐처드는 세련된 여인이 아니었다. 젊은 날에는 막벌이꾼의 딸이었고 그러고 나서 막벌이꾼에게 시집을 갔다."], ["But her husband had risen in the world—as has been told in those chronicles before mentioned,—and his widow was now Lady Scatcherd with a pretty cottage and a good jointure.", "그러나 그 남편이 세상에서 올라갔고—앞서 말한 그 연대기에 적혀 있는 대로다—그 미망인은 이제 예쁜 오두막과 넉넉한 과부 몫 재산을 지닌 레이디 스캐처드였다."], ["She was in all things the very opposite to Lady Arabella Gresham; nevertheless, under the doctor’s auspices, the two ladies were in some measure acquainted with each other.", "그녀는 모든 점에서 레이디 애러벨라 그레셤과 정반대였다. 그럼에도 선생의 주선으로 두 부인은 얼마쯤 서로 아는 사이가 되어 있었다."], ["Of her married life, also, Dr. Thorne had seen something, and it may be questioned whether the memory of that was more alluring than the reality now existing at Greshamsbury.", "그녀의 혼인 생활도 손 선생이 얼마간 보아 온 바인데, 그에 대한 기억이 지금 그레셤스베리에 있는 현실보다 더 마음을 끄는 것이었는지는 물음이 될 만하다."]], [["Of the two women Dr. Thorne much preferred his humbler friend, and to her he made his visits not in the guise of a doctor, but as a neighbour.", "두 여인 가운데 손 선생은 더 낮은 쪽 벗을 훨씬 좋아하여, 그녀에게는 의사의 모습으로가 아니라 이웃으로 찾아갔다."], ["“Well, my lady,”", "“그래, 마님.”"], ["he said, as he sat down by her on a broad garden seat—all the world called Lady Scatcherd “my lady,”—“and how do these long summer days agree with you?", "그는 넓은 정원 의자에 그녀 옆으로 앉으며 말했다—온 세상이 레이디 스캐처드를 「마님」이라 불렀다—“이 긴 여름날들이 마님께는 잘 맞으시오?"], ["Your roses are twice better out than any I see up at the big house.”", "마님네 장미는 내가 저 큰 집에서 보는 어느 것보다 두 배는 잘 피었소.”"]], [["“You may well call them long, doctor.", "“길다고 하실 만해요, 선생님."], ["They’re long enough surely.”", "참말 길기도 하지요.”"]], [["“But not too long.", "“그러나 너무 길지는 않지."], ["Come, now, I won’t have you complaining.", "자, 이제 마님이 불평하는 건 못 봐 주겠소."], ["You don’t mean to tell me that you have anything to make you wretched?", "마님을 비참하게 만드는 무엇이 있다고 말할 셈은 아니겠지요?"], ["You had better not, for I won’t believe you.”", "그러지 않는 게 좋겠소, 나는 믿지 않을 테니.”"]], [["“Eh; well; wretched!", "“에, 글쎄, 비참하다니요!"], ["I don’t know as I’m wretched.", "내가 비참한지 어떤지 나는 모르겠어요."], ["It’d be wicked to say that, and I with such comforts about me.”", "그런 말을 하면 못된 짓이지요, 이렇게 편한 것들을 두르고 있으면서.”"]], [["“I think it would, almost.”", "“거의 그렇겠지요.”"], ["The doctor did not say this harshly, but in a soft, friendly tone, and pressing her hand gently as he spoke.", "선생은 이 말을 매섭게 하지 않았다. 부드럽고 다정한 어조로, 말하면서 그녀의 손을 가만히 눌러 주었다."]], [["“And I didn’t mean to be wicked.", "“나도 못되게 굴 생각은 아니었어요."], ["I’m very thankful for everything—leastways, I always try to be.", "나는 무엇에나 참 고맙게 여겨요—적어도 늘 그러려고 애써요."], ["But, doctor, it is so lonely like.”", "그런데 선생님, 그게 어쩐지 참 외로워요.”"]], [["“Lonely!", "“외롭다니!"], ["not more lonely than I am.”", "나보다 더 외로울 리는 없소.”"]], [["“Oh, yes; you’re different.", "“아, 예. 선생님은 다르지요."], ["You can go everywheres.", "선생님은 어디든지들 다 갈 수 있잖아요."], ["But what can a lone woman do?", "하지만 혼자된 여자가 무얼 할 수 있어요?"], ["I’ll tell you what, doctor; I’d give it all up to have Roger back with his apron on and his pick in his hand.", "내가 말씀드릴게요, 선생님. 나는 로저가 앞치마를 두르고 곡괭이를 손에 쥔 채로 돌아와 준다면 이 모두를 내놓겠어요."], ["How well I mind his look when he’d come home o’ nights.”", "밤마다 집에 들어올 적에 그 얼굴이 어찌나 잘 마음에 새겨져 있는지.”"]], [["“And yet it was a hard life you had then, eh, old woman?", "“그런데도 그때 마님이 지낸 삶은 고달팠지요, 그렇지 않소, 할멈?"], ["It would be better for you to be thankful for what you’ve got.”", "마님은 지금 가진 것에 고맙게 여기는 편이 나을 거요.”"]], [["“I am thankful.", "“고맙게 여겨요."], ["Didn’t I tell you so before?”", "전에도 그렇게 말씀드리지 않았나요?”"], ["said she, somewhat crossly.", "그녀가 좀 뾰로통하게 말했다."], ["“But it’s a sad life, this living alone.", "“하지만 이렇게 혼자 사는 건 서러운 삶이에요."], ["I declares I envy Hannah, ’cause she’s got Jemima to sit in the kitchen with her.", "내 말하지만 나는 해나가 부러워요. 그 애는 제마이마가 부엌에 같이 앉아 있어 주니까요."], ["I want her to sit with me sometimes, but she won’t.”", "나도 이따금 그 애더러 나랑 같이 앉아 있으라고 하고 싶은데, 그 애가 싫다고 해요.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but you shouldn’t ask her.", "그러나 그 애에게 그런 말은 하지 말아야 하오."], ["It’s letting yourself down.”", "그건 스스로를 낮추는 일이오.”"]], [["“What do I care about down or up?", "“낮추고 높이는 게 나한테 무슨 상관이에요?"], ["It makes no difference, as he’s gone.", "그이가 갔으니 아무 다를 게 없어요."], ["If he had lived one might have cared about being up, as you call it.", "그이가 살아 있었다면 선생님 말대로 높이 있는 걸 마음에 두었을지도 모르지요."], ["Eh, deary; I’ll be going after him before long, and it will be no matter then.”", "에휴, 딱하기도. 나도 오래지 않아 그이를 따라갈 텐데, 그때는 아무 상관없을 거예요.”"]], [["“We shall all be going after him, sooner or later; that’s sure enough.”", "“우리 모두 이르든 늦든 그를 따라가게 되지요. 그건 넉넉히 확실한 일이오.”"]], [["“Eh, dear, that’s true, surely.", "“에휴, 딱해라, 그건 참말 그래요."], ["It’s only a span long, as Parson Oriel tells us when he gets romantic in his sermons.", "오리얼 목사님이 설교에서 감상에 젖으면 우리에게 말해 주듯이, 그게 겨우 한 뼘 길이지요."], ["But it’s a hard thing, doctor, when two is married, as they can’t have their span, as he calls it, out together.", "하지만 선생님, 둘이 혼인을 했는데 목사님 말대로 그 한 뼘을 함께 다 살지 못하는 건 고된 일이에요."], ["Well, I must only put up with it, I suppose, as others does.", "그래도 남들이 그러는 대로 나도 참고 견디는 수밖에 없겠지요."], ["Now, you’re not going, doctor?", "그런데, 가시는 건 아니지요, 선생님?"], ["You’ll stop and have a dish of tea with me.", "남아서 나하고 차 한 잔 하세요."], ["You never see such cream as Hannah has from the Alderney cow.", "해나가 올더니 젖소에서 받아 오는 그런 크림은 어디서도 못 보실 거예요."], ["Do’ey now, doctor.”", "자, 그러세요, 선생님.”"]], [["But the doctor had his letter to write, and would not allow himself to be tempted even by the promise of Hannah’s cream.", "그러나 선생에게는 써야 할 편지가 있었고, 해나의 크림을 약속받고서도 마음이 흔들리도록 스스로를 내버려 두지 않았다. 그래서 그는 앞서 지주를 노엽게 했듯이 떠나는 걸음으로 레이디 스캐처드를 노엽게 하고서 자기 길을 갔다."], ["So he went his way, angering Lady Scatcherd by his departure as he had before angered the squire, and thinking as he went which was most unreasonable in her wretchedness, his friend Lady Arabella, or his friend Lady Scatcherd.", "그러면서 가는 동안 그 비참함에서 어느 쪽이 더 이치에 닿지 않는가를 생각했다. 벗인 레이디 애러벨라인가, 벗인 레이디 스캐처드인가."], ["The former was always complaining of an existing husband who never refused her any moderate request; and the other passed her days in murmuring at the loss of a dead husband, who in his life had ever been to her imperious and harsh, and had sometimes been cruel and unjust.", "앞의 사람은 어지간한 청이라면 한 번도 마다한 적이 없는, 살아 있는 남편을 두고 늘 불평했다. 뒤의 사람은 살아생전 늘 그녀에게 거만하고 매섭게 굴었으며 이따금 잔인하고 부당하기까지 했던, 죽은 남편을 잃은 것을 두고 웅얼거리며 날을 보냈다."]], [["The doctor had his letter to write, but even yet he had not quite made up his mind what he would put into it; indeed, he had not hitherto resolved to whom it should be written.", "선생에게는 써야 할 편지가 있었으나, 아직도 그 안에 무엇을 담을지 마음을 다 정하지 못했다. 사실 그때까지 그것을 누구에게 쓸 것인지도 정하지 못하고 있었다."], ["Looking at the matter as he had endeavoured to look at it, his niece, Mrs.", "애써 보아 온 대로 이 일을 본다면 편지를 받을 사람은 조카딸 그레셤 부인일 터였다."], ["Gresham, would be his correspondent; but if he brought himself to take this jump in the dark, in that case he would address himself direct to Miss Dunstable.", "그러나 어둠 속으로 뛰어드는 이 걸음을 스스로 감행하게 된다면, 그 경우에는 던스터블 양에게 곧바로 말을 건네게 될 것이었다."]], [["He walked home, not by the straightest road, but taking a considerable curve, round by narrow lanes, and through thick flower-laden hedges,—very thoughtful.", "그는 집으로 걸어갔는데, 가장 곧은 길로가 아니라 꽤 크게 돌아서 좁은 골목길을 따라 꽃이 무겁게 달린 두꺼운 산울타리 사이를 지나갔다."], ["He was told that she wished to marry him; and was he to think only of himself?", "생각이 아주 깊었다. 그녀가 자기와 혼인하고 싶어 한다는 말을 들었다."], ["And as to that pride of his about money, was it in truth a hearty, manly feeling; or was it a false pride, of which it behoved him to be ashamed as it did of many cognate feelings?", "그런데 그가 자기 생각만 해야 하는가? 그리고 돈을 두고 지녀 온 그 자랑은 참으로 마음에서 나온 사내다운 감정인가, 아니면 그와 비슷한 여러 감정처럼 부끄러워해야 마땅한 거짓된 자랑인가?"], ["If he acted rightly in this matter, why should he be afraid of the thoughts of any one?", "이 일에서 그가 옳게 행한다면 무엇 때문에 남들의 생각을 두려워하겠는가?"], ["A life of solitude was bitter enough, as poor Lady Scatcherd had complained.", "가엾은 레이디 스캐처드가 불평한 대로 홀로 지내는 삶은 넉넉히 쓰라린 것이었다."], ["But then, looking at Lady Scatcherd, and looking also at his other near neighbour, his friend the squire, there was little thereabouts to lead him on to matrimony.", "그러나 또 레이디 스캐처드를 보고, 가까운 또 한 이웃인 벗 지주를 보면, 그를 혼인으로 이끌 만한 것은 그 근처에 거의 없었다."], ["So he walked home slowly through the lanes, very meditative, with his hands behind his back.", "그래서 그는 뒷짐을 지고 골목길을 지나 골똘한 생각에 잠긴 채 천천히 집으로 걸어갔다."]], [["Nor when he got home was he much more inclined to any resolute line of action.", "집에 이르고 나서도 그는 딱 잘라 무엇을 하기로 마음이 더 기울지 않았다."], ["He might have drunk his tea with Lady Scatcherd, as well as have sat there in his own drawing-room, drinking it alone; for he got no pen and paper, and he dawdled over his teacup with the utmost dilatoriness, putting off, as it were, the evil day.", "제 응접실에 앉아 혼자 차를 마시나 레이디 스캐처드와 함께 차를 마시나 마찬가지였을 것이다. 그는 펜도 종이도 꺼내지 않고, 마치 그 궂은 날을 미루듯이 더할 수 없이 늑장을 부리며 찻잔을 앞에 두고 꾸물거렸다."], ["To only one thing was he fixed—to this, namely, that that letter should be written before he went to bed.", "못 박아 둔 것은 오직 하나였다. 곧 그 편지를 자리에 들기 전에 써야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["Having finished his tea, which did not take place till near eleven, he went downstairs to an untidy little room which lay behind his depôt of medicines, and in which he was wont to do his writing; and herein he did at last set himself down to his work.", "열한 시가 가까워서야 차를 다 마치고 나서 그는 아래층으로, 약 곳간 뒤에 있는 어수선한 작은 방으로 갔다. 글 쓰는 일은 으레 거기서 하던 것이었다."], ["Even at that moment he was in doubt.", "그리고 여기서 그는 마침내 그 일에 자리를 잡고 앉았다."], ["But he would write his letter to Miss Dunstable and see how it looked.", "그 순간에도 그는 망설이고 있었다. 그러나 던스터블 양에게 편지를 써서 그것이 어떻게 보이는지 살펴보기로 했다."], ["He was almost determined not to send it; so, at least, he said to himself: but he could do no harm by writing it.", "그것을 보내지 않기로 거의 마음을 굳혔다, 적어도 스스로에게는 그렇게 말했다. 하지만 써 본다고 해로울 것은 없었다."], ["So he did write it, as follows:—", "그래서 그는 다음과 같이 그것을 썼다."]], [["Greshamsbury, — June, 185—.", "그레셤스베리, 185—년 유월 —일."]], [["When he had got so far, he leaned back in his chair and looked at the paper.", "그만큼 써 놓고 나서 그는 의자에 등을 기대고 그 종이를 바라보았다."], ["How on earth was he to find words to say that which he now wished to have said?", "이제 말해 두고 싶은 그것을 말할 낱말을 대체 어디서 찾아낸단 말인가?"], ["He had never written such a letter in his life, or anything approaching to it, and now found himself overwhelmed with a difficulty of which he had not previously thought.", "그는 평생 그런 편지를 써 본 적이 없었고 그에 가까운 것조차 써 본 적이 없었으니, 이제 미리 생각해 보지 못한 어려움에 짓눌린 자신을 발견했다. 그는 또 반 시간을 그 종이를 바라보며 보냈고, 마침내 이 새로운 어려움에 거의 주춤하고 말았다."], ["He spent another half-hour in looking at the paper, and was at last nearly deterred by this new difficulty.", "가장 단순하고 가장 꾸밈없는 말을 쓰리라고 그는 스스로에게 거듭거듭 말했다."], ["He would use the simplest, plainest language, he said to himself over and over again; but it is not always easy to use simple, plain language,—by no means so easy as to mount on stilts, and to march along with sesquipedalian words, with pathos, spasms, and notes of interjection.", "그러나 단순하고 꾸밈없는 말을 쓰는 것이 늘 쉬운 일은 아니다. 죽마에 올라 한 자 반이나 되는 긴 낱말들을 늘어놓고 애상과 발작과 감탄 부호를 곁들여 행진하는 것만큼 쉬운 일은 결코 아니다."], ["But the letter did at last get itself written, and there was not a note of interjection in it.", "그래도 편지는 마침내 저절로 써졌고, 그 안에 감탄 부호는 하나도 없었다."]], [["MY DEAR MISS DUNSTABLE,—I think it right to confess that I should not be now writing this letter to you, had I not been led to believe by other judgment than my own that the proposition which I am going to make would be regarded by you with favour.", "친애하는 던스터블 양께,—이제 드리려는 제안을 그대가 좋게 여기실 것이라고 제 것이 아닌 다른 판단에 이끌려 믿게 되지 않았더라면, 저는 지금 이 편지를 그대에게 쓰고 있지 않을 것임을 털어놓는 것이 옳다고 여깁니다."], ["Without such other judgment I should, I own, have feared that the great disparity between you and me in regard to money would have given to such a proposition an appearance of being false and mercenary.", "그런 다른 판단이 없었다면, 돈을 두고 그대와 저 사이에 놓인 큰 차이가 그런 제안을 거짓되고 돈을 바라는 것으로 보이게 하지 않을까 두려워했을 것임을 저는 인정합니다."], ["All I ask of you now, with confidence, is to acquit me of such fault as that.", "지금 제가 마음 놓고 그대에게 청하는 것은 오직, 그런 허물에서 저를 벗겨 주시는 일뿐입니다."]], [["When you have read so far you will understand what I mean.", "여기까지 읽으시면 제 뜻을 아실 것입니다."], ["We have known each other now somewhat intimately, though indeed not very long, and I have sometimes fancied that you were almost as well pleased to be with me as I have been to be with you.", "우리는 참으로 아주 오래는 아니라 하여도 이제 얼마쯤 가깝게 서로를 알게 되었고, 저는 제가 그대와 함께 있어 기뻤던 만큼 그대도 저와 함께 있어 거의 그만큼 기뻐하지 않았나 이따금 짐작해 보았습니다."], ["If I have been wrong in this, tell me so simply, and I will endeavour to let our friendship run on as though this letter had not been written.", "이 일에서 제가 틀렸다면 그저 그렇다고 말해 주십시오. 저는 이 편지가 쓰이지 않았던 것처럼 우리 우정이 이어지도록 애쓰겠습니다."], ["But if I have been right, and if it be possible that you can think that a union between us will make us both happier than we are single, I will plight you my word and troth with good faith, and will do what an old man may do to make the burden of the world lie light upon your shoulders.", "그러나 제가 옳았다면, 그리고 우리 둘이 하나가 되는 것이 홀로 있을 때보다 우리 둘을 더 행복하게 만들 것이라고 그대가 생각할 수 있다면, 저는 그대에게 제 말과 언약을 참된 믿음으로 바치겠습니다. 그리고 세상의 짐이 그대 어깨 위에 가볍게 놓이도록 늙은 사내가 할 수 있는 일을 하겠습니다."], ["Looking at my age I can hardly keep myself from thinking that I am an old fool: but I try to reconcile myself to that by remembering that you yourself are no longer a girl.", "제 나이를 보면 제가 늙은 바보라는 생각을 저 스스로 좀처럼 누를 수가 없습니다. 그러나 그대 자신도 이제 소녀가 아님을 떠올림으로써 그 생각과 화해해 보려 합니다."], ["You see that I pay you no compliments, and that you need expect none from me.", "보시는 대로 저는 그대에게 아무 치사도 하지 않으며, 그대도 저에게서 그런 것을 바라실 필요가 없습니다."]], [["I do not know that I could add anything to the truth of this, if I were to write three times as much.", "이보다 세 배를 더 쓴다 해도 이 글의 진실에 무엇을 보탤 수 있을지 저는 알지 못합니다."], ["All that is necessary is, that you should know what I mean.", "필요한 것은 오직 그대가 제 뜻을 아시는 것뿐입니다."], ["If you do not believe me to be true and honest already, nothing that I can write will make you believe it.", "그대가 저를 이미 참되고 정직한 사람으로 믿지 않으신다면, 제가 쓸 수 있는 어떤 것도 그대를 믿게 만들지 못할 것입니다."]], [["God bless you.", "하느님의 축복이 그대에게 있기를."], ["I know you will not keep me long in suspense for an answer.", "그대가 저를 오래 답 없이 매달아 두지는 않으실 줄 압니다."]], [["Affectionately your friend,", "정을 담아, 그대의 벗,"]], [["When he had finished he meditated again for another half-hour whether it would not be right that he should add something about her money.", "다 쓰고 나서 그는 그녀의 돈에 대해 무엇을 덧붙이는 것이 옳지 않을까 하여 또 반 시간을 골똘히 생각했다."], ["Would it not be well for him to tell her—it might be said in a postscript—that with regard to all her wealth she would be free to do what she chose?", "그녀의 재산 전부에 관하여 그녀가 하고 싶은 대로 할 수 있다고—추신으로 적어도 될 일이었다—말해 주는 것이 좋지 않을까?"], ["At any rate he owed no debts for her to pay, and would still have his own income, sufficient for his own purposes.", "어쨌거나 그녀가 갚아야 할 빚은 그에게 하나도 없었고, 그에게는 여전히 제 앞가림에 넉넉한 제 벌이가 있었다."], ["But about one o’clock he came to the conclusion that it would be better to leave the matter alone.", "그러나 한 시쯤에 그는 그 문제는 건드리지 않고 두는 편이 낫겠다는 결론에 이르렀다."], ["If she cared for him, and could trust him, and was worthy also that he should trust her, no omission of such a statement would deter her from coming to him: and if there were no such trust, it would not be created by any such assurance on his part.", "그녀가 그를 마음에 두고 그를 믿을 수 있으며, 또 그가 그녀를 믿어도 좋을 만한 사람이라면, 그런 말이 빠져 있다 해도 그녀가 그에게 오는 것을 막지는 못할 것이다. 그리고 그런 믿음이 없다면, 그쪽에서 그런 다짐을 해 준다고 해서 믿음이 생겨나지도 않을 것이다."], ["So he read the letter over twice, sealed it, and took it up, together with his bed candle, into his bed-room.", "그래서 그는 편지를 두 번 읽고, 봉을 하고, 잠자리 촛대와 함께 그것을 들고 침실로 올라갔다."], ["Now that the letter was written it seemed to be a thing fixed by fate that it must go.", "편지가 써진 지금은 그것이 가야 한다는 것이 운명으로 못 박힌 일처럼 보였다."], ["He had written it that he might see how it looked when written; but now that it was written, there remained no doubt but that it must be sent.", "그는 써 놓으면 어떻게 보이는지 알아보려고 그것을 썼다. 그러나 이제 써 놓고 보니 그것을 보내야 한다는 데에는 의심이 남지 않았다."], ["So he went to bed, with the letter on the toilette-table beside him; and early in the morning—so early as to make it seem that the importance of the letter had disturbed his rest—he sent it off by a special messenger to Boxall Hill.", "그래서 그는 편지를 옆 화장대 위에 두고 자리에 들었다. 그리고 아침 일찍—그 편지의 중대함이 그의 잠을 흩어 놓았던 것처럼 보일 만큼 일찍—그는 그것을 특별 심부름꾼에게 맡겨 박솔 힐로 보냈다."]], [["“I’se wait for an answer?”", "“답장을 기다립니까요?”"], ["said the boy.", "심부름 아이가 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "“아니다.”"], ["said the doctor: “leave the letter, and come away.”", "선생이 말했다. “편지를 두고 그냥 오거라.”"]], [["The breakfast hour was not very early at Boxall Hill in these summer months.", "이 여름 몇 달 동안 박솔 힐의 아침 밥 때는 그리 이르지 않았다."], ["Frank Gresham, no doubt, went round his farm before he came in for prayers, and his wife was probably looking to the butter in the dairy.", "프랭크 그레셤은 틀림없이 기도 시간에 들어오기 전에 제 농장을 한 바퀴 돌았을 것이고, 그 아내는 아마 낙농실의 버터를 살피고 있었을 것이다."], ["At any rate, they did not meet till near ten, and therefore, though the ride from Greshamsbury to Boxall Hill was nearly two hours’ work, Miss Dunstable had her letter in her own room before she came down.", "어쨌든 두 사람은 열 시가 가까워서야 얼굴을 마주했고, 그러므로 그레셤스베리에서 박솔 힐까지 말을 달리는 데 두 시간 가까이 걸리는 일이었음에도 던스터블 양은 아래로 내려오기 전에 제 방에서 편지를 받았다."]], [["She read it in silence as she was dressing, while the maid was with her in the room; but she made no sign which could induce her Abigail to think that the epistle was more than ordinarily important.", "그녀는 하녀가 방에 함께 있는 동안 옷을 차려입으며 잠자코 그것을 읽었다. 그러나 그 서한이 여느 것보다 중대하다고 몸종이 여기게 만들 만한 기색은 조금도 보이지 않았다."], ["She read it, and then quietly refolding it and placing it in the envelope, she put it down on the table at which she was sitting.", "그녀는 그것을 읽고 나서 조용히 다시 접어 봉투에 넣고, 앉아 있던 탁자 위에 내려놓았다."], ["It was full fifteen minutes afterwards that she begged her servant to see if Mrs. Gresham were still in her own room.", "그러고서 꼬박 십오 분이 지나서야 그녀는 하인에게 그레셤 부인이 아직 제 방에 있는지 보아 달라고 청했다."], ["“Because I want to see her for five minutes, alone, before breakfast,”", "“아침 밥 전에 오 분만 혼자서 그이를 만나고 싶으니까요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."]], [["“You traitor; you false, black traitor!”", "“배신자! 이 거짓되고 검은 배신자!”"], ["were the first words which Miss Dunstable spoke when she found herself alone with her friend.", "던스터블 양이 벗과 단둘이 있게 되었을 때 처음으로 한 말이 이것이었다."]], [["“Why, what’s the matter?”", "“아니, 무슨 일이에요?”"]], [["“I did not think there was so much mischief in you, nor so keen and commonplace a desire for match-making.", "“이렇게 장난기가 많은 줄은, 또 혼사를 맺어 주려는 그렇게 날카롭고 흔해빠진 욕심이 있는 줄은 생각하지 못했어요."], ["Look here.", "자, 보세요."], ["Read the first four lines; not more, if you please; the rest is private.", "첫 네 줄만 읽어요. 더 읽으면 안 돼요, 부디."], ["Whose is the other judgment of whom your uncle speaks in his letter?”", "나머지는 사사로운 것이니까요. 댁의 삼촌이 편지에서 말하는 그 다른 판단은 누구의 것이지요?”"]], [["“Oh, Miss Dunstable!", "“아, 던스터블 양!"], ["I must read it all.”", "나는 그걸 다 읽어야 해요.”"]], [["“Indeed you’ll do no such thing.", "“참말 그런 짓은 못 해요."], ["You think it’s a love-letter, I dare say; but indeed there’s not a word about love in it.”", "이게 연애 편지라고 여기는 게지요. 하지만 참으로 그 안에는 사랑이라는 말이 한 마디도 없어요.”"]], [["“I know he has offered.", "“청혼하셨다는 걸 나는 알아요."], ["I shall be so glad, for I know you like him.”", "나는 참 기쁠 거예요. 던스터블 양이 그분을 좋아하는 걸 아니까요.”"]], [["“He tells me that I am an old woman, and insinuates that I may probably be an old fool.”", "“나더러 늙은 여자라고 하시고, 어쩌면 내가 늙은 바보일지도 모른다고 넌지시 비추셨어요.”"]], [["“I am sure he does not say that.”", "“틀림없이 그런 말씀은 하지 않으셨을 거예요.”"]], [["“Ah!", "“아!"], ["but I’m sure that he does.", "하지만 나는 틀림없이 하셨다고 봐요."], ["The former is true enough, and I never complain of the truth.", "앞의 것은 넉넉히 참말이고, 나는 진실을 두고 불평한 적이 없어요."], ["But as to the latter, I am by no means so certain that it is true—not in the sense that he means it.”", "하지만 뒤의 것은 그것이 참인지 나로서는 조금도 그리 확실하지 않아요—그분이 뜻하는 그런 뜻으로는 말이에요.”"]], [["“Dear, dearest woman, don’t go on in that way now.", "“귀한 분, 우리 귀한 분, 이제 그런 식으로 하지 마세요."], ["Do speak out to me, and speak without jesting.”", "나에게 속을 털어놓고, 농담 없이 말해 주세요.”"]], [["“Whose was the other judgment to whom he trusts so implicitly?", "“그분이 그렇게 고이 믿는 그 다른 판단은 누구의 것이었어요?"], ["Tell me that.”", "그것을 말해 주세요.”"]], [["“Mine, mine, of course.", "“내 것이에요, 내 것. 물론이지요."], ["No one else can have spoken to him about it.", "그 일을 두고 그분에게 말할 수 있었던 사람은 나 말고 없어요."], ["Of course I talked to him.”", "물론 내가 그분에게 이야기했어요.”"]], [["“And what did you tell him?”", "“그래서 무엇이라고 말했어요?”"]], [["“I told him—”", "“나는 그분에게…”"]], [["“Well, out with it.", "“자, 말해 봐요."], ["Let me have the real facts.", "참말 사실을 들려주세요."], ["Mind, I tell you fairly that you had no right to tell him anything.", "잘 들어요, 댁에게는 아무것도 그분에게 말할 권리가 없었다고 나는 똑바로 말해 두겠어요."], ["What passed between us, passed in confidence.", "우리 사이에 오간 것은 믿고 오간 것이니까요."], ["But let us hear what you did say.”", "하지만 무엇이라고 말했는지 들어 봅시다.”"]], [["“I told him that you would have him if he offered.” And Mrs.", "“그분이 청혼하시면 던스터블 양이 받아들이겠다고 말했어요.”"], ["Gresham, as she spoke, looked into her friend’s face doubtingly, not knowing whether in very truth Miss Dunstable were pleased with her or displeased.", "그렇게 말하면서 그레셤 부인은 벗의 얼굴을 의심스럽게 들여다보았다. 던스터블 양이 참으로 자기를 기뻐하는지 언짢아하는지 알 수 없었던 것이다."], ["If she were displeased, then how had her uncle been deceived!", "언짢아한다면, 그러면 삼촌은 얼마나 속은 것인가!"]], [["“You told him that as a fact?”", "“그것을 사실로 말했어요?”"]], [["“I told him that I thought so.”", "“나는 그렇게 생각한다고 말했어요.”"]], [["“Then I suppose I am bound to have him,”", "“그러면 나는 그분을 받아들일 수밖에 없겠네요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, dropping the letter on to the floor in mock despair.", "던스터블 양은 거짓으로 절망한 척 편지를 바닥에 떨어뜨리며 말했다."]], [["“My dear, dear, dearest woman!” said Mrs.", "“내 귀한 분, 귀한 분, 우리 귀한 분!”"], ["Gresham, bursting into tears, and throwing herself on to her friend’s neck.", "그레셤 부인은 울음을 터뜨리며 벗의 목에 몸을 던지며 말했다."]], [["“Mind you are a dutiful niece,”", "“착실한 조카딸이 되도록 하세요.”"], ["said Miss Dunstable.", "던스터블 양이 말했다."], ["“And now let me go and finish dressing.”", "“그리고 이제 가서 옷차림을 마치게 해 주세요.”"]], [["In the course of the afternoon, an answer was sent back to Greshamsbury, in these words:—", "그날 오후에 그레셤스베리로 이런 말로 된 답장이 되돌아갔다."]], [["DEAR DR.", "친애하는 손 선생님께,—저는 무슨 일에서나 선생님을 믿고 있고 또 믿겠습니다."], ["THORNE,—I do and will trust you in everything; and it shall be as you would have it.", "그리고 선생님이 바라시는 대로 될 것입니다. 메리가 선생님께 편지를 쓴답니다."], ["Mary writes to you; but do not believe a word she says.", "하지만 메리가 하는 말은 한 마디도 믿지 마십시오."], ["I never will again, for she has behaved so bad in this matter.", "저는 다시는 믿지 않겠습니다. 이 일에서 메리가 아주 못되게 굴었으니까요."]], [["Yours affectionately and very truly,", "다정하고 아주 참된 마음으로,"]], [["“And so I am going to marry the richest woman in England,” said Dr.", "“그래서 나는 영국에서 가장 부유한 여인과 혼인하게 되었구나.”"], ["Thorne to himself, as he sat down that day to his mutton-chop.", "그날 양갈비를 앞에 놓고 앉으며 손 선생이 혼자 중얼거렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER XL. INTERNECINE.", "# 제40장. 서로 죽이는 싸움"]], [["It must be conceived that there was some feeling of triumph at Plumstead Episcopi, when the wife of the rector returned home with her daughter, the bride elect of the Lord Dumbello.", "플럼스테드 에피스코피의 주임 사제의 아내가 덤벨로 경의 약혼한 신부인 딸을 데리고 집으로 돌아왔을 때, 그곳에 얼마간 승리의 기분이 감돌았으리라는 것은 미루어 짐작해야 한다."], ["The heir of the Marquis of Hartletop was, in wealth, the most considerable unmarried young nobleman of the day; he was noted, too, as a man difficult to be pleased, as one who was very fine and who gave himself airs,—and to have been selected as the wife of such a man as this was a great thing for the daughter of a parish clergyman.", "하틀탑 후작의 후계자는 재산으로 보아 당시 미혼의 젊은 귀족 가운데 가장 대단한 사람이었다. 게다가 그는 마음에 맞추기 어려운 사내로, 몹시 까다롭게 굴며 거드름을 피우는 사람으로 이름이 나 있었으니—그런 사내의 아내로 뽑혔다는 것은 한낱 교구 성직자의 딸에게는 대단한 일이었다."], ["We have seen in what manner the happy girl’s mother communicated the fact to Lady Lufton, hiding, as it were, her pride under a veil; and we have seen also how meekly the happy girl bore her own great fortune, applying herself humbly to the packing of her clothes, as though she ignored her own glory.", "그 행복한 처녀의 어머니가 어떤 식으로 이 사실을 레이디 러프턴에게 알렸는지, 이를테면 제 자랑을 너울 밑에 감추어 가며 알렸는지는 우리가 이미 보았다. 그리고 그 행복한 처녀가 제게 온 큰 복을 얼마나 얌전하게 견뎌 냈는지, 제 영화를 모르는 척하며 겸손하게 제 옷을 챙기는 일에나 매달렸는지도 우리는 보았다."]], [["But nevertheless there was triumph at Plumstead Episcopi.", "그러나 그럼에도 플럼스테드 에피스코피에는 승리의 기분이 있었다."], ["The mother, when she returned home, began to feel that she had been thoroughly successful in the great object of her life.", "어머니는 집으로 돌아오자 제 평생의 큰 목적에서 자기가 완전히 성공했음을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["While she was yet in London she had hardly realized her satisfaction, and there were doubts then whether the cup might not be dashed from her lips before it was tasted.", "아직 런던에 있는 동안에는 그 만족을 실감하지도 못했고, 잔이 입술에 닿기 전에 그것이 입에서 떨어져 나가지나 않을까 하는 의심이 그때는 있었다."], ["It might be that even the son of the Marquis of Hartletop was subject to parental authority, and that barriers should spring up between Griselda and her coronet; but there had been nothing of the kind.", "하틀탑 후작의 아들이라 해도 어버이의 권위 아래 있을지 모르는 일이고, 그리젤다와 귀족 관 사이에 담이 솟아오를지도 모르는 일이었다."], ["The archdeacon had been closeted with the marquis, and Mrs.", "그러나 그런 종류의 일은 아무것도 없었다."], ["Grantly had been closeted with the marchioness; and though neither of those noble persons had expressed themselves gratified by their son’s proposed marriage, so also neither of them had made any attempt to prevent it.", "부주교는 후작과 마주 앉아 이야기를 나누었고, 그랜틀리 부인은 후작부인과 마주 앉아 이야기를 나누었다. 그리고 그 고귀한 두 사람 가운데 어느 쪽도 아들의 혼사를 두고 흐뭇하다는 뜻을 내보이지는 않았으나, 또한 어느 쪽도 그것을 막아 보려는 시도를 하지 않았다."], ["Lord Dumbello was a man who had a will of his own,—as the Grantlys boasted amongst themselves.", "덤벨로 경은 제 뜻을 지닌 사내였다—그랜틀리 집안 사람들이 저희끼리 자랑하는 말이 그러했다."], ["Poor Griselda!", "가엾은 그리젤다!"], ["the day may perhaps come when this fact of her lord’s masterful will may not to her be matter of much boasting.", "남편의 그 억센 뜻이라는 사실이 그녀에게 그다지 자랑거리가 되지 않는 날이 어쩌면 올지도 모른다."], ["But in London, as I was saying, there had been no time for an appreciation of the family joy.", "그러나 앞에서 말했듯이 런던에서는 집안의 이 기쁨을 음미할 겨를이 없었다."], ["The work to be done was nervous in its nature, and self-glorification might have been fatal; but now, when they were safe at Plumstead, the great truth burst upon them in all its splendour.", "해야 할 일이 성질상 마음을 졸이게 하는 것이었고, 제 자랑을 늘어놓다가는 치명적인 일이 될 수도 있었다. 그러나 이제 플럼스테드에 무사히 이르고 나니 그 큰 진실이 온갖 찬란함을 다 갖추고 그들 위로 터져 나왔다."]], [["Mrs. Grantly had but one daughter, and the formation of that child’s character and her establishment in the world had been the one main object of the mother’s life.", "그랜틀리 부인에게는 딸이 하나뿐이었고, 그 아이의 성품을 만들어 주는 일과 그 아이가 세상에 자리를 잡게 하는 일이 어머니 평생의 유일한 큰 목적이었다."], ["Of Griselda’s great beauty the Plumstead household had long been conscious; of her discretion also, of her conduct, and of her demeanour there had been no doubt.", "그리젤다의 뛰어난 아름다움은 플럼스테드 집안 사람들이 오래전부터 알고 있었다. 그 아이의 신중함도, 몸가짐도, 태도도 의심할 것이 없었다."], ["But the father had sometimes hinted to the mother that he did not think that Grizzy was quite so clever as her brothers.", "그러나 아버지는 이따금 어머니에게 그리지가 그 아이의 형제들만큼 똑똑하지는 못한 것 같다고 비춘 적이 있었다."], ["“I don’t agree with you at all,”", "“저는 그 말에는 전혀 동의하지 않아요,”"], ["Mrs. Grantly had answered.", "그랜틀리 부인은 그렇게 대답했다."], ["“Besides, what you call cleverness is not at all necessary in a girl; she is perfectly ladylike; even you won’t deny that.”", "“게다가 부주교님이 똑똑하다고 부르는 것은 처녀에게는 조금도 필요하지 않은 것이에요. 그 아이는 나무랄 데 없이 숙녀답지요."], ["The archdeacon had never wished to deny it, and was now fain to admit that what he had called cleverness was not necessary in a young lady.", "그건 부주교님도 부인하지 못하실 거예요.” 부주교는 그것을 부인하고 싶었던 적이 한 번도 없었고, 이제는 자기가 똑똑함이라고 부른 것이 젊은 아가씨에게 필요하지 않다는 것을 기꺼이 인정할 마음이 되었다."]], [["At this period of the family glory the archdeacon himself was kept a little in abeyance, and was hardly allowed free intercourse with his own magnificent child.", "집안의 이 영화가 한창인 시기에 부주교 자신은 조금 뒤로 밀려나 있었고, 제 훌륭한 자식과 마음대로 어울리는 것도 좀처럼 허락받지 못했다."], ["Indeed, to give him his due, it must be said of him that he would not consent to walk in the triumphal procession which moved with stately step, to and fro, through the Barchester regions.", "사실 그를 위해 공평하게 말한다면, 그는 바체스터 일대를 위엄 있는 걸음으로 오락가락 누비는 그 개선 행렬에 함께 걸으려 하지 않았다고 해야 한다."], ["He kissed his daughter and blessed her, and bade her love her husband and be a good wife; but such injunctions as these, seeing how splendidly she had done her duty in securing to herself a marquis, seemed out of place and almost vulgar.", "그는 딸에게 입 맞추고 축복하며, 남편을 사랑하고 좋은 아내가 되라고 일러 주었다. 그러나 그 아이가 후작을 제 것으로 붙잡음으로써 제 소임을 얼마나 훌륭하게 해냈는지를 생각하면, 이런 따위의 훈계는 자리에 맞지 않고 거의 상스러워 보였다."], ["Girls about to marry curates or sucking barristers should be told to do their duty in that station of life to which God might be calling them; but it seemed to be almost an impertinence in a father to give such an injunction to a future marchioness.", "부목사나 젖내 나는 법정 변호사와 혼인하려는 처녀들에게는 하느님이 그들을 부르시는 그 삶의 자리에서 제 소임을 다하라고 일러 줄 만하다. 그러나 앞으로 후작부인이 될 사람에게 아버지가 그런 훈계를 하는 것은 거의 당돌한 짓으로 보였다."]], [["“I do not think that you have any ground for fear on her behalf,” said Mrs.", "“그 아이를 두고 두려워하실 까닭은 아무것도 없다고 생각해요,”"], ["Grantly, “seeing in what way she has hitherto conducted herself.”", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “여태 그 아이가 처신해 온 모양을 보면요.”"]], [["“She has been a good girl,”", "“착한 아이였소,”"], ["said the archdeacon, “but she is about to be placed in a position of great temptation.”", "부주교가 말했다. “하지만 이제 큰 유혹이 있는 자리에 놓이게 되오.”"]], [["“She has a strength of mind suited for any position,”", "“그 아이는 어떤 자리에라도 어울릴 만한 굳센 마음을 지녔어요,”"], ["replied Mrs. Grantly, vain-gloriously.", "그랜틀리 부인이 제 자랑을 담아 대답했다."]], [["But nevertheless even the archdeacon moved about through the close at Barchester with a somewhat prouder step since the tidings of this alliance had become known there.", "그러나 그럼에도 부주교조차 이 결합의 소식이 그곳에 알려진 뒤로는 바체스터의 성당 구내를 조금 더 자랑스러운 걸음으로 오갔다."], ["The time had been—in the latter days of his father’s lifetime—when he was the greatest man of the close.", "아버지가 살아 계신 마지막 몇 해 동안에는 그가 성당 구내에서 가장 큰 사람이던 시절이 있었다."], ["The dean had been old and infirm, and Dr. Grantly had wielded the bishop’s authority.", "수석 사제는 늙고 병약했고, 그랜틀리 박사가 주교의 권위를 휘둘렀다."], ["But since that things had altered.", "그러나 그 뒤로 사정이 달라졌다."], ["A new bishop had come there, absolutely hostile to him.", "새 주교가 그곳에 왔는데 그에게 철저히 적대적인 사람이었다."], ["A new dean had also come, who was not only his friend, but the brother-in-law of his wife; but even this advent had lessened the authority of the archdeacon.", "새 수석 사제도 왔는데, 그는 부주교의 벗이기만 한 것이 아니라 그 아내 쪽으로 동서가 되는 사람이었다. 그러나 이 사람이 온 것조차 부주교의 권위를 줄여 놓았다."], ["The vicars choral did not hang upon his words as they had been wont to do, and the minor canons smiled in return to his smile less obsequiously when they met him in the clerical circles of Barchester.", "성가대 사제들은 여느 때처럼 그의 말에 매달리지 않았고, 하급 참사회원들은 바체스터의 성직자 모임에서 그를 만나도 그의 웃음에 덜 굽실거리며 웃음으로 답했다."], ["But now it seemed that his old supremacy was restored to him.", "그러나 이제는 그 옛날의 으뜸 자리가 그에게 되돌아온 듯했다."], ["In the minds of many men an archdeacon, who was the father-in-law of a marquis, was himself as good as any bishop.", "많은 사람의 마음속에서, 후작의 장인이 된 부주교라면 그 자신이 어느 주교와도 맞먹는 사람이었다."], ["He did not say much of his new connection to others beside the dean, but he was conscious of the fact, and conscious also of the reflected glory which shone around his own head.", "그는 수석 사제 말고 다른 사람들에게는 새 인연에 대해 별로 말하지 않았으나, 그 사실을 마음속으로 의식하고 있었고 제 머리 둘레에 비쳐 오는 영광의 빛도 의식하고 있었다."]], [["But as regards Mrs. Grantly it may be said that she moved in an unending procession of stately ovation.", "그러나 그랜틀리 부인에 관해서는, 그녀가 끝없이 이어지는 위엄찬 환호의 행렬 속을 걸어갔다고 말할 수 있다."], ["It must not be supposed that she continually talked to her friends and neighbours of Lord Dumbello and the marchioness.", "그녀가 벗들과 이웃들에게 덤벨로 경과 후작부인 이야기를 줄곧 늘어놓았다고 여겨서는 안 된다."], ["She was by far too wise for such folly as that.", "그녀는 그런 어리석은 짓을 하기에는 너무나 슬기로웠다."], ["The coming alliance having been once announced, the name of Hartletop was hardly mentioned by her out of her own domestic circle.", "다가오는 결합이 한번 알려진 뒤로는 하틀탑이라는 이름이 제 집안 울 밖에서 그녀의 입에 오르는 일이 거의 없었다."], ["But she assumed, with an ease that was surprising even to herself, the airs and graces of a mighty woman.", "그러나 그녀는 저 자신도 놀랄 만큼 손쉽게 대단한 여인의 몸가짐과 태를 갖추었다."], ["She went through her work of morning calls as though it were her business to be affable to the country gentry.", "그녀는 아침 방문이라는 일과를, 마치 시골 지주들에게 너그럽게 대해 주는 것이 제 소임이기라도 한 듯이 치렀다."], ["She astonished her sister, the dean’s wife, by the simplicity of her grandeur; and condescended to Mrs.", "수석 사제의 아내인 동생은 언니가 그 위세를 하도 담박하게 부리는 데 놀랐다."], ["Proudie in a manner which nearly broke that lady’s heart.", "그리고 프라우디 부인에게는 그 부인의 가슴이 거의 미어질 만한 태로 아랫사람 대하듯 굽어 살펴 주었다."], ["“I shall be even with her yet,” said Mrs.", "“저 여자와는 아직 셈이 남았지,”"], ["Proudie to herself, who had contrived to learn various very deleterious circumstances respecting the Hartletop family since the news about Lord Dumbello and Griselda had become known to her.", "프라우디 부인이 혼자 말했다. 그녀는 덤벨로 경과 그리젤다에 관한 소식을 알게 된 뒤로 하틀탑 집안에 관하여 아주 해로운 사정 여러 가지를 알아낼 방도를 찾아 놓았던 것이다."]], [["Griselda herself was carried about in the procession, taking but little part in it of her own, like an Eastern god.", "그리젤다 자신은 그 행렬 속에 실려 다녔으나 제 몫으로 하는 일은 거의 없었으니, 마치 동방의 신 같았다."], ["She suffered her mother’s caresses and smiled in her mother’s face as she listened to her own praises, but her triumph was apparently within.", "그녀는 어머니의 어루만짐을 잠자코 받았고, 제 칭송을 들으며 어머니의 얼굴을 보고 웃었으나, 그 승리는 아무래도 안에 들어 있었다."], ["To no one did she say much on the subject, and greatly disgusted the old family housekeeper by declining altogether to discuss the future Dumbello ménage.", "이 일을 두고 누구에게도 많은 말을 하지 않았고, 앞으로의 덤벨로 살림에 대해 이야기하기를 아주 마다하여 집안의 늙은 가정부를 크게 언짢게 했다."], ["To her aunt, Mrs. Arabin, who strove hard to lead her into some open-hearted speech as to her future aspirations, she was perfectly impassive.", "그녀의 이모인 애러빈 부인은 앞으로의 바람에 대해 마음을 열어 말하도록 그녀를 이끌어 보려고 애를 썼으나, 조카는 조금도 꿈쩍하지 않았다."], ["“Oh, yes, aunt, of course,”", "“아, 예, 그야 그렇지요, 이모.”"], ["and “I’ll think about it, aunt Eleanor,”", "그리고 “생각해 볼게요, 엘리너 이모.”"], ["or “Of course I shall do that if Lord Dumbello wishes it.”", "아니면 “덤벨로 경이 그러기를 바라면 물론 그렇게 하겠어요.” 그녀에게서 얻어 낼 수 있는 것은 이 이상 아무것도 없었다."], ["Nothing beyond this could be got from her; and so, after half-a-dozen ineffectual attempts, Mrs. Arabin abandoned the matter.", "그리하여 애러빈 부인은 대여섯 번 헛되게 시도해 본 뒤에 그 일을 그만두었다."]], [["But then there arose the subject of clothes—of the wedding trousseau!", "그러나 그때 옷 문제가—혼례 옷가지 문제가 일어났다!"], ["Sarcastic people are wont to say that the tailor makes the man.", "비꼬기 좋아하는 이들은 재봉사가 사람을 만든다고 흔히 말한다."], ["Were I such a one, I might certainly assert that the milliner makes the bride.", "내가 그런 사람이라면, 모자 장수가 신부를 만든다고 단연 주장할 수도 있으리라."], ["As regarding her bridehood, in distinction either to her girlhood or her wifehood—as being a line of plain demarcation between those two periods of a woman’s life—the milliner does do much to make her.", "처녀 시절이나 아내 시절과 갈라지는 신부 시절이라는 것에 관해서는—여인의 삶에서 그 두 시기 사이에 뚜렷한 금을 긋는 것이라는 점에서는—모자 장수가 신부를 만드는 데 실로 많은 일을 한다."], ["She would be hardly a bride if the trousseau were not there.", "혼례 옷가지가 거기 없다면 그녀는 좀처럼 신부가 되지 못할 것이다."], ["A girl married without some such appendage would seem to pass into the condition of a wife without any such line of demarcation.", "그런 딸림 물건 없이 혼인하는 처녀는 그런 갈라짐의 금도 없이 아내라는 신분으로 넘어가 버리는 듯 보일 것이다."], ["In that moment in which she finds herself in the first fruition of her marriage finery she becomes a bride; and in that other moment, when she begins to act upon the finest of these things as clothes to be packed up, she becomes a wife.", "제 혼례 치장을 처음으로 누리는 그 순간에 그녀는 신부가 된다. 그리고 그 좋은 물건들 가운데 가장 좋은 것을 싸서 챙길 옷으로 다루기 시작하는 그 다른 순간에 그녀는 아내가 된다."]], [["When this subject was discussed Griselda displayed no lack of a becoming interest.", "이 문제가 의논될 때 그리젤다는 마땅한 만큼의 관심이 모자라는 기색을 조금도 보이지 않았다."], ["She went to work steadily, slowly, and almost with solemnity, as though the business in hand were one which it would be wicked to treat with impatience.", "그녀는 꾸준하게, 천천하게, 거의 엄숙하게 일에 착수했으니, 손에 든 볼일이 성급하게 다루면 죄가 될 만한 것이기라도 한 듯했다."], ["She even struck her mother with awe by the grandeur of her ideas and the depth of her theories.", "그 뜻이 웅장하고 그 이론이 깊은 데에 어머니마저 두려움을 느낄 정도였다."], ["Nor let it be supposed that she rushed away at once to the consideration of the great fabric which was to be the ultimate sign and mark of her status, the quintessence of her briding, the outer veil, as it were, of the tabernacle—namely, her wedding-dress.", "그러나 그녀가 제 신분의 마지막 표시요 표적이며 제 신부됨의 알짬이요 이를테면 성막의 겉 너울인 그 큰 옷감, 곧 혼례복을 대뜸 궁리하러 달려들었다고 여기지는 마시라."], ["As a great poet works himself up by degrees to that inspiration which is necessary for the grand turning point of his epic, so did she slowly approach the hallowed ground on which she would sit, with her ministers around her, when about to discuss the nature, the extent, the design, the colouring, the structure, and the ornamentation of that momentous piece of apparel.", "위대한 시인이 제 서사시의 큰 전환점에 꼭 필요한 영감에 이르기까지 차츰차츰 자신을 끌어올리듯이, 그녀도 그 성스러운 땅에 천천히 다가갔다. 그 땅에 이르면 그녀는 받드는 이들을 둘레에 앉히고 앉아 그 중대한 옷의 성질과 크기와 도안과 빛깔과 짜임과 꾸밈을 의논할 참이었다."], ["No; there was much indeed to be done before she came to this; and as the poet, to whom I have already alluded, first invokes his muse, and then brings his smaller events gradually out upon his stage, so did Miss Grantly with sacred fervour ask her mother’s aid, and then prepare her list of all those articles of under-clothing which must be the substratum for the visible magnificence of her trousseau.", "아니, 여기에 이르기까지 실로 해야 할 일이 많았다. 그리고 내가 이미 이야기한 그 시인이 먼저 무사 여신을 부르고 그다음에 작은 사건들을 차츰차츰 무대 위로 내보내듯이, 그랜틀리 양도 거룩한 열심으로 어머니의 도움을 청하고, 그다음에 혼례 옷가지의 눈에 보이는 웅장함을 받쳐 줄 바탕이 되어야 하는 온갖 속옷 물목을 마련했다."]], [["Money was no object.", "돈은 문제가 아니었다."], ["We all know what that means; and frequently understand, when the words are used, that a blaze of splendour is to be attained at the cheapest possible price.", "그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 우리는 모두 안다. 그리고 그 말이 쓰일 때 그것이 흔히 되도록 싼값으로 찬란한 빛을 얻어 내겠다는 뜻임을 우리는 안다."], ["But, in this instance, money was no object;—such an amount of money, at least, as could by any possibility be spent on a lady’s clothes, independently of her jewels.", "그러나 이번 경우에는 정말로 돈이 문제가 아니었다—적어도 보석을 빼고 부인의 옷에 쓸 수 있는 만큼의 돈은 그러했다."], ["With reference to diamonds and such like, the archdeacon at once declared his intention of taking the matter into his own hands—except in so far as Lord Dumbello, or the Hartletop interest, might be pleased to participate in the selection.", "다이아몬드 같은 것에 관해서는 부주교가 그 일을 제 손에 넣겠다고 곧바로 선언했다—덤벨로 경이나 하틀탑 쪽에서 고르는 일에 함께하고자 하는 데까지는 빼놓고 말이다."], ["Nor was Mrs. Grantly sorry for such a decision.", "그랜틀리 부인도 그런 결정을 아쉬워하지 않았다."], ["She was not an imprudent woman, and would have dreaded the responsibility of trusting herself on such an occasion among the dangerous temptations of a jeweller’s shop.", "그녀는 조심성 없는 여인이 아니었으니, 그런 자리에서 보석 가게의 위험한 유혹들 가운데에 저 자신을 맡기는 책임을 두려워했을 것이다."], ["But as far as silks and satins went—in the matter of French bonnets, muslins, velvets, hats, riding-habits, artificial flowers, head-gilding, curious nettings, enamelled buckles, golden tagged bobbins, and mechanical petticoats—as regarded shoes, and gloves, and corsets, and stockings, and linen, and flannel, and calico—money, I may conscientiously assert, was no object.", "그러나 비단과 새틴에 관해서라면—프랑스제 보닛, 모슬린, 벨벳, 모자, 승마복, 조화, 머리 금장식, 별난 망사 세공, 에나멜 버클, 금 물린 실패 장식, 기계 장치를 넣은 페티코트에 관해서라면—또 구두와 장갑과 코르셋과 스타킹과 아마포와 플란넬과 옥양목에 관해서라면—돈은, 내가 양심을 걸고 말할 수 있는데, 문제가 아니었다."], ["And, under these circumstances, Griselda Grantly went to work with a solemn industry and a steady perseverance that was beyond all praise.", "그리고 이런 사정 아래에서 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 무엇으로도 칭찬을 다할 수 없을 만큼 엄숙한 부지런함과 꾸준한 끈기로 일에 착수했다."]], [["“I hope she will be happy,” Mrs.", "“그 아이가 행복하기를 바라요,”"], ["Arabin said to her sister, as the two were sitting together in the dean’s drawing-room.", "애러빈 부인이 언니에게 말했다. 두 사람은 수석 사제 관저의 응접실에 함께 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Oh, yes; I think she will.", "“아, 그럴 거예요. 그렇게 될 거라고 생각해요."], ["Why should she not?”", "그러지 못할 까닭이 무어겠어요?”"], ["said the mother.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no; I know of no reason.", "“아, 예, 까닭을 안다는 건 아니에요."], ["But she is going up into a station so much above her own in the eyes of the world that one cannot but feel anxious for her.”", "하지만 그 아이는 세상의 눈으로 보아 제 자리보다 훨씬 높은 자리로 올라가는 것이니, 그 아이 걱정을 안 할 수가 없어요.”"]], [["“I should feel much more anxious if she were going to marry a poor man,”", "“가난한 사내와 혼인하러 간다면 훨씬 더 걱정이 될 거예요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“It has always seemed to me that Griselda was fitted for a high position; that nature intended her for rank and state.", "“저는 그리젤다가 높은 자리에 맞게 태어난 아이라고, 자연이 그 아이를 신분과 위세를 누리도록 내놓았다고 늘 생각해 왔어요."], ["You see that she is not a bit elated.", "보다시피 그 아이는 조금도 들떠 있지 않아요."], ["She takes it all as if it were her own by right.", "그 모든 것을 마치 처음부터 제 것인 양 받아들이지요."], ["I do not think that there is any danger that her head will be turned, if you mean that.”", "만약 그런 뜻으로 말한 거라면, 그 아이가 마음이 뒤집힐 위험은 조금도 없다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“I was thinking rather of her heart,”", "“저는 오히려 그 아이 마음을 생각한 거였어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Arabin.", "애러빈 부인이 말했다."]], [["“She never would have taken Lord Dumbello without loving him,” said Mrs.", "“그 아이는 사랑하지 않고서 덤벨로 경을 받아들이지는 않았을 거예요,”"], ["Grantly, speaking rather quickly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 조금 빠르게 말했다."]], [["“That is not quite what I mean either, Susan.", "“제가 하려는 말도 꼭 그건 아니에요, 수전 언니."], ["I am sure she would not have accepted him had she not loved him.", "사랑하지 않았다면 그분을 받아들이지 않았으리라는 건 저도 알아요."], ["But it is so hard to keep the heart fresh among all the grandeurs of high rank; and it is harder for a girl to do so who has not been born to it, than for one who has enjoyed it as her birthright.”", "하지만 높은 신분의 온갖 영화 속에서 마음을 싱싱하게 지키기란 참 어려운 일이에요. 그리고 그것을 타고난 권리로 누려 온 처녀보다, 그렇게 태어나지 않은 처녀에게 그 일이 더 어렵지요.”"]], [["“I don’t quite understand about fresh hearts,”", "“싱싱한 마음이라는 게 무슨 말인지 저는 잘 모르겠어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly, pettishly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 좀 뾰로통하게 말했다."], ["“If she does her duty, and loves her husband, and fills the position in which God has placed her with propriety, I don’t know that we need look for anything more.", "“그 아이가 제 소임을 다하고 남편을 사랑하고 하느님이 놓아 주신 자리를 어울리게 채운다면, 그 이상 무엇을 더 바라야 하는지 저는 모르겠어요."], ["I don’t at all approve of the plan of frightening a young girl when she is making her first outset into the world.”", "어린 처녀가 처음으로 세상에 나서려는 때에 그 아이를 겁주는 식은 저는 조금도 좋게 보지 않아요.”"]], [["“No; I would not frighten her.", "“예, 저도 그 아이를 겁주지는 않겠어요."], ["I think it would be almost difficult to frighten Griselda.”", "그리젤다를 겁주기란 아무래도 어려운 일일 것 같군요.”"]], [["“I hope it would.", "“그러기를 바라요."], ["The great matter with a girl is whether she has been brought up with proper notions as to a woman’s duty.", "처녀에게 가장 중요한 일은 여인의 소임에 대해 옳은 생각을 지니도록 길러졌느냐 하는 것이지요. 물론 이 일을 두고 제가 자랑할 처지는 아니에요."], ["Of course it is not for me to boast on this subject.", "그 아이가 그러한 아이인 것에 대해서는 물론 제가 책임을 져야 하지요."], ["Such as she is, I, of course, am responsible. But I must own that I do not see occasion to wish for any change.”", "하지만 무엇을 바꾸었으면 좋겠다는 마음이 들 만한 데를 저는 도무지 못 찾겠다고, 이것만은 털어놓아야 하겠어요.”"], ["And then the subject was allowed to drop.", "그러고는 이 이야기를 그만 접게 두었다."]], [["Among those of her relations who wondered much at the girl’s fortune, but allowed themselves to say but little, was her grandfather, Mr.", "이 처녀의 복을 크게 놀라워하면서도 스스로 말은 조금밖에 하지 않은 친척들 가운데에 그녀의 외할아버지 하딩 씨가 있었다."], ["Harding. He was an old clergyman, plain and simple in his manners, and not occupying a very prominent position, seeing that he was only precentor to the chapter.", "그는 늙은 성직자로, 몸가짐이 꾸밈없고 소박하며, 성당 참사회의 선창 사제에 지나지 않았으므로 그리 두드러진 자리를 차지하고 있지 않았다."], ["He was loved by his daughter, Mrs. Grantly, and was treated by the archdeacon, if not invariably with the highest respect, at least always with consideration and regard.", "딸인 그랜틀리 부인은 그를 사랑했고, 부주교도 늘 한결같이 지극한 예로써는 아니었을지언정 적어도 언제나 배려와 존중으로 그를 대했다."], ["But, old and plain as he was, the young people at Plumstead did not hold him in any great reverence.", "그러나 나이 들고 꾸밈없는 사람이었던 만큼 플럼스테드의 젊은 사람들은 그를 그다지 크게 우러르지 않았다."], ["He was poorer than their other relatives, and made no attempt to hold his head high in Barsetshire circles.", "그는 다른 친척들보다 가난했고, 바셋주 사교계에서 고개를 높이 들려는 시도를 하지 않았다."], ["Moreover, in these latter days, the home of his heart had been at the deanery.", "게다가 요 몇 해 동안 그 마음의 집은 수석 사제 관저였다."], ["He had, indeed, a lodging of his own in the city, but was gradually allowing himself to be weaned away from it.", "그가 이 도시에 제 셋집을 지니고 있기는 했으나 차츰 그곳에서 마음이 떼어지도록 스스로 내버려 두고 있었다."], ["He had his own bedroom in the dean’s house, his own arm-chair in the dean’s library, and his own corner on a sofa in Mrs. Dean’s drawing-room.", "그는 수석 사제의 집에 제 침실을 두었고, 수석 사제의 서재에 제 안락의자를 두었고, 수석 사제 부인의 응접실 소파에 제 자리 한 귀퉁이를 두었다."], ["It was not, therefore, necessary that he should interfere greatly in this coming marriage; but still it became his duty to say a word of congratulation to his granddaughter,—and perhaps to say a word of advice.", "그러므로 다가오는 이 혼사에 그가 크게 끼어들 필요는 없었다. 그러나 그래도 손녀에게 축하의 말을 한마디 하는 것—그리고 어쩌면 충고의 말을 한마디 하는 것은 그의 소임이 되었다."]], [["“Grizzy, my dear,”", "“그리지야,” 그가 그녀에게 말했다."], ["he said to her—he always called her Grizzy, but the endearment of the appellation had never been appreciated by the young lady—“come and kiss me, and let me congratulate you on your great promotion.", "그는 늘 그녀를 그리지라고 불렀으나 이 젊은 아가씨는 그 애칭의 다정함을 한 번도 고맙게 여긴 적이 없었다. “이리 와서 나에게 입 맞추고, 이 큰 출세를 축하하게 해 주렴."], ["I do so very heartily.”", "참으로 진심으로 그러고 싶구나.”"]], [["“Thank you, grandpapa,”", "“고마워요, 할아버지,”"], ["she said, touching his forehead with her lips, thus being, as it were, very sparing with her kiss.", "그녀가 그의 이마에 입술을 대며 말했으니, 이를테면 제 입맞춤을 아주 아껴 쓴 것이었다."], ["But those lips now were august and reserved for nobler foreheads than that of an old cathedral hack. For Mr.", "그러나 이제 그 입술은 지엄한 것이 되어 대성당의 늙은 삯일꾼의 이마보다는 더 고귀한 이마를 위해 남겨져 있었다."], ["Harding still chanted the Litany from Sunday to Sunday, unceasingly, standing at that well-known desk in the cathedral choir; and Griselda had a thought in her mind that when the Hartletop people should hear of the practice they would not be delighted.", "하딩 씨는 여전히 일요일마다 쉬는 일 없이 대성당 성가대석의 그 낯익은 독서대 앞에 서서 탄원 기도를 선창하고 있었으니, 하틀탑 집안 사람들이 이 관행을 듣게 되면 기뻐하지 않을 것이라는 생각이 그리젤다의 마음속에 들어 있었다."], ["Dean and archdeacon might be very well, and if her grandfather had even been a prebendary, she might have put up with him; but he had, she thought, almost disgraced his family in being, at his age, one of the working menial clergy of the cathedral.", "수석 사제나 부주교라면 아주 괜찮을 것이고, 외할아버지가 참사회원이라도 되었더라면 그녀는 그를 참아 주었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그 나이에 대성당의 막일하는 하급 성직자 가운데 하나로 있음으로써 제 집안을 거의 망신시키고 있다고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["She kissed him, therefore, sparingly, and resolved that her words with him should be few.", "그러므로 그녀는 그에게 아껴서 입 맞추었고, 그와 나누는 말도 몇 마디로 그치리라고 마음먹었다."]], [["“You are going to be a great lady, Grizzy,”", "“너는 대단한 귀부인이 될 게다, 그리지야,”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Umph!”", "“음!”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["What was she to say when so addressed?", "그렇게 말을 걸어오는데 그녀가 무슨 말을 하겠는가?"]], [["“And I hope you will be happy,—and make others happy.”", "“그리고 네가 행복하기를—그리고 남들을 행복하게 해 주기를 바란다.”"]], [["“I hope I shall,”", "“그러기를 바라요,”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“But always think most about the latter, my dear.", "“하지만 언제나 뒤쪽을 가장 많이 생각하렴."], ["Think about the happiness of those around you, and your own will come without thinking.", "네 둘레 사람들의 행복을 생각하면 네 행복은 생각하지 않아도 저절로 올 게다."], ["You understand that; do you not?”", "그 말을 알아듣겠지, 그렇지 않니?”"]], [["“Oh, yes, I understand,”", "“아, 예, 알아들어요,”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["As they were speaking Mr. Harding still held her hand, but Griselda left it with him unwillingly, and therefore ungraciously, looking as though she were dragging it from him.", "두 사람이 이야기하는 동안 하딩 씨는 여전히 그녀의 손을 잡고 있었는데, 그리젤다는 마음에 없이, 그러므로 곱살스럽지 못하게 손을 그에게 맡겨 두었고, 그 손을 그에게서 잡아 빼는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["“And Grizzy—I believe it is quite as easy for a rich countess to be happy, as for a dairymaid—”", "“그리고 그리지야—부유한 백작부인이 행복해지는 것도 젖 짜는 처녀가 행복해지는 것과 똑같이 쉬운 일이라고 나는 믿는다—”"]], [["Griselda gave her head a little chuck which was produced by two different operations of her mind.", "그리젤다가 고개를 조금 쳐들었는데, 그것은 그녀의 마음속에서 서로 다른 두 가지 작용이 일어난 데서 나온 것이었다."], ["The first was a reflection that her grandpapa was robbing her of her rank.", "첫째는 외할아버지가 제 신분을 앗아 가고 있다는 생각이었다."], ["She was to be a rich marchioness.", "그녀는 부유한 후작부인이 될 사람이었다."], ["And the second was a feeling of anger at the old man for comparing her lot to that of a dairymaid.", "그리고 둘째는 제 처지를 젖 짜는 처녀의 처지에 견주었다고 이 늙은이에게 느낀 노여움이었다."]], [["“Quite as easy, I believe,”", "“똑같이 쉬운 일이라고 나는 믿는다,”"], ["continued he; “though others will tell you that it is not so.", "그가 말을 이었다. “남들은 그렇지 않다고 너에게 말하겠지만 말이다."], ["But with the countess as with the dairymaid, it must depend on the woman herself.", "하지만 젖 짜는 처녀에게나 백작부인에게나 그것은 여인 자신에게 달린 일일 수밖에 없다."], ["Being a countess—that fact alone won’t make you happy.”", "백작부인이 된다는—그 사실 하나만으로 너를 행복하게 해 주지는 못할 게다.”"]], [["“Lord Dumbello at present is only a viscount,”", "“덤벨로 경은 지금은 자작일 뿐이에요,”"], ["said Griselda.", "그리젤다가 말했다."], ["“There is no earl’s title in the family.”", "“그 집안에는 백작 작위가 없어요.”"]], [["“Oh!", "“아!"], ["I did not know,” said Mr.", "그건 몰랐구나,”"], ["Harding, relinquishing his granddaughter’s hand; and, after that, he troubled her with no further advice.", "하딩 씨는 이렇게 말하고 손녀의 손을 놓았다. 그리고 그 뒤로는 더 이상의 충고로 그녀를 괴롭히지 않았다."]], [["Both Mrs. Proudie and the bishop had called at Plumstead since Mrs.", "프라우디 부인과 주교는 그랜틀리 부인이 런던에서 돌아온 뒤에 둘 다 플럼스테드를 찾아왔고, 플럼스테드의 부인들도 물론 그 방문에 답례를 했다."], ["Grantly had come back from London, and the ladies from Plumstead, of course, returned the visit.", "그랜틀리 집안과 프라우디 집안이 서로 미워하는 것은 마땅한 일이었다."], ["It was natural that the Grantlys and Proudies should hate each other. They were essentially church people, and their views on all church matters were antagonistic.", "그들은 본질에서 교회 사람들이었고, 교회의 모든 일에 대한 그들의 뜻은 서로 맞서는 것이었다."], ["They had been compelled to fight for supremacy in the diocese, and neither family had so conquered the other as to have become capable of magnanimity and good-humour.", "그들은 이 교구의 으뜸 자리를 놓고 싸울 수밖에 없었고, 어느 집안도 상대를 눌러 놓기를 너그러움과 좋은 기분을 낼 수 있을 만큼 해내지는 못했다."], ["They did hate each other, and this hatred had, at one time, almost produced an absolute disseverance of even the courtesies which are so necessary between a bishop and his clergy.", "그들은 정말로 서로 미워했고, 이 미움은 한때 주교와 그 성직자들 사이에 그토록 필요한 예의마저 아주 끊어 놓을 지경에까지 이르렀다."], ["But the bitterness of this rancour had been overcome, and the ladies of the families had continued on visiting terms.", "그러나 이 원한의 쓰라림은 이겨 냈고, 두 집안의 부인들은 서로 오가는 사이를 이어 왔다."]], [["But now this match was almost more than Mrs. Proudie could bear.", "그러나 이제 이 혼사는 프라우디 부인이 견딜 수 있는 것을 거의 넘어서 있었다."], ["The great disappointment which, as she well knew, the Grantlys had encountered in that matter of the proposed new bishopric had for the moment mollified her.", "새 주교좌를 세우려던 그 일에서 그랜틀리 집안이 겪은 큰 낙심은, 그녀가 잘 알고 있었던 대로, 한때 그녀의 마음을 누그러뜨려 주었다."], ["She had been able to talk of poor dear Mrs. Grantly!", "그녀는 가엾고 딱한 그랜틀리 부인 이야기를 할 수 있었던 것이다!"], ["“She is heartbroken, you know, in this matter, and the repetition of such misfortunes is hard to bear,”", "“그 부인은 이 일로 가슴이 미어져 있소. 그런 불운이 되풀이되는 것은 견디기 힘든 일이지요.”"], ["she had been heard to say, with a complacency which had been quite becoming to her.", "그녀가 아주 흐뭇한 낯으로 이렇게 말하는 것을 사람들은 들은 바 있었고, 그 흐뭇함은 그녀에게 자못 잘 어울리는 것이었다."], ["But now that complacency was at an end.", "그러나 이제 그 흐뭇함은 끝이 났다."], ["Olivia Proudie had just accepted a widowed preacher at a district church in Bethnal Green,—a man with three children, who was dependent on pew-rents; and Griselda Grantly was engaged to the eldest son of the Marquis of Hartletop!", "올리비아 프라우디는 베스널 그린의 지구 교회에 있는 홀아비 설교자를—아이가 셋이고 신자석 임대료에 매여 살아가는 사내를 받아들인 참이었다. 그런데 그리젤다 그랜틀리는 하틀탑 후작의 맏아들과 약혼을 한 것이다!"], ["When women are enjoined to forgive their enemies it cannot be intended that such wrongs as these should be included.", "여인들에게 원수를 용서하라고 명하실 때, 이런 종류의 억울함까지 거기에 들어가라는 뜻이었을 수는 없다."]], [["But Mrs. Proudie’s courage was nothing daunted.", "그러나 프라우디 부인의 담력은 조금도 꺾이지 않았다."], ["It may be boasted of her that nothing could daunt her courage.", "그녀를 두고 자랑할 만한 것이 있다면, 무엇으로도 그 담력을 꺾을 수 없었다는 것이다."], ["Soon after her return to Barchester, she and Olivia—Olivia being very unwilling—had driven over to Plumstead, and, not finding the Grantlys at home, had left their cards; and now, at a proper interval, Mrs.", "바체스터로 돌아온 지 얼마 안 되어 그녀는 올리비아를 데리고—올리비아는 아주 마음이 없었다—플럼스테드까지 마차를 몰고 갔고, 그랜틀리 집안 사람들이 집에 없어 명함만 두고 왔다. 그리고 이제 알맞은 시일이 지난 뒤에 그랜틀리 부인과 그리젤다가 그 방문에 답례를 한 것이었다."], ["Grantly and Griselda returned the visit. It was the first time that Miss Grantly had been seen by the Proudie ladies since the fact of her engagement had become known.", "약혼한 사실이 알려진 뒤로 프라우디 집안의 부인들이 그랜틀리 양을 보는 것은 이번이 처음이었다."]], [["The first bevy of compliments that passed might be likened to a crowd of flowers on a hedge rosebush.", "처음에 오간 칭찬의 무리는 산울타리 장미 덤불에 잔뜩 핀 꽃에 견줄 만한 것이었다."], ["They were beautiful to the eye but were so closely environed by thorns that they could not be plucked without great danger.", "그 꽃들은 눈에는 아름다웠으나 가시에 하도 촘촘히 둘러싸여 있어서 큰 위험 없이는 꺾을 수 없었다."], ["As long as the compliments were allowed to remain on the hedge—while no attempt was made to garner them and realize their fruits for enjoyment—they did no mischief; but the first finger that was put forth for such a purpose was soon drawn back, marked with spots of blood.", "칭찬이 산울타리 위에 그대로 놓여 있도록 두는 동안에는—그것을 거두어 그 열매를 즐기려는 시도가 없는 동안에는—아무 해도 없었다. 그러나 그럴 뜻으로 처음 내민 손가락은 이내 핏방울이 맺힌 채 뒤로 물러났다."]], [["“Of course it is a great match for Griselda,”", "“물론 그리젤다에게는 대단한 혼사지요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly, in a whisper the meekness of which would have disarmed an enemy whose weapons were less firmly clutched than those of Mrs.", "그랜틀리 부인이 속삭이는 소리로 말했는데, 그 얌전함은 프라우디 부인의 것만큼 단단히 무기를 붙잡고 있지 않은 적이라면 무장을 풀게 할 만한 것이었다."], ["Proudie; “but, independently of that, the connection is one which is gratifying in many ways.”", "“하지만 그것과는 따로, 이 인연은 여러 가지로 흐뭇한 것이에요.”"]], [["“Oh, no doubt,”", "“아, 그렇겠지요,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Lord Dumbello is so completely his own master,” continued Mrs.", "“덤벨로 경은 워낙 온전히 제 몸의 주인이니까요,”"], ["Grantly, and a slight, unintended semi-tone of triumph mingled itself with the meekness of that whisper.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말을 이었는데, 뜻하지 않은 승리의 반음이 그 얌전한 속삭임에 조금 섞여 들었다."]], [["“And is likely to remain so, from all I hear,” said Mrs.", "“그리고 내가 듣는 말로는 앞으로도 그럴 것 같소,”"], ["Proudie, and the scratched hand was at once drawn back.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. 그러자 긁힌 손이 곧바로 뒤로 물러났다."]], [["“Of course the estab—,” and then Mrs.", "“물론 그 살림사—”"], ["Proudie, who was blandly continuing her list of congratulations, whispered her sentence close into the ear of Mrs. Grantly, so that not a word of what she said might be audible by the young people.", "하고 프라우디 부인은 축하의 물목을 부드럽게 이어 가다가 문장의 나머지를 그랜틀리 부인의 귀에 바싹 대고 속삭였으니, 자기가 하는 말이 젊은 사람들에게는 한마디도 들리지 않게 하려는 것이었다."]], [["“I never heard a word of it,” said Mrs.", "“그런 말은 한마디도 들은 적이 없어요,”"], ["Grantly, gathering herself up, “and I don’t believe it.”", "그랜틀리 부인이 몸을 곧추세우며 말했다. “그리고 저는 그 말을 믿지 않아요.”"]], [["“Oh, I may be wrong; and I’m sure I hope so.", "“아, 내가 틀렸을지도 모르지요. 그러기를 진심으로 바라고요."], ["But young men will be young men, you know;—and children will take after their parents.", "하지만 젊은 사내는 젊은 사내인 법이오. 그건 댁도 알 테지요—그리고 자식은 어버이를 닮는 법이지요."], ["I suppose you will see a great deal of the Duke of Omnium now.”", "이제 옴니엄 공작을 자주 뵙게 되겠구려.”"]], [["But Mrs. Grantly was not a woman to be knocked down and trampled on without resistance; and though she had been lacerated by the rosebush she was not as yet placed altogether hors de combat.", "그러나 그랜틀리 부인은 아무 저항 없이 나가떨어져 짓밟힐 여인이 아니었다."], ["She said some word about the Duke of Omnium very tranquilly, speaking of him merely as a Barsetshire proprietor, and then, smiling with her sweetest smile, expressed a hope that she might soon have the pleasure of becoming acquainted with Mr.", "그리고 장미 덤불에 긁히기는 했으나 아직 아주 싸움에서 물러난 처지는 아니었다. 그녀는 옴니엄 공작에 대해 아주 태연하게 몇 마디를 했으니, 그를 한낱 바셋주의 땅 임자로만 이야기한 것이었다."], ["Tickler; and as she spoke she made a pretty little bow towards Olivia Proudie. Now Mr.", "그러고는 제 가장 다사로운 웃음으로 웃으며 곧 티클러 씨와 알게 되는 기쁨을 누렸으면 좋겠다는 바람을 말했다."], ["Tickler was the worthy clergyman attached to the district church at Bethnal Green.", "그러면서 올리비아 프라우디 쪽으로 예쁘게 조그맣게 고개를 숙였다. 그런데 티클러 씨란 베스널 그린의 지구 교회에 딸린 그 훌륭한 성직자였다."]], [["“He’ll be down here in August,”", "“팔월에 여기 내려올 거예요,”"], ["said Olivia, boldly, determined not to be shamefaced about her love affairs.", "올리비아가 씩씩하게 말했으니, 제 연애 일을 두고 부끄러워하지 않기로 마음먹은 것이었다."]], [["“You’ll be starring it about the Continent by that time, my dear,”", "“그때쯤이면 아가씨는 대륙 곳곳을 돌며 뽐내고 있겠구려,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie to Griselda.", "프라우디 부인이 그리젤다에게 말했다."], ["“Lord Dumbello is well known at Homburg and Ems, and places of that sort; so you will find yourself quite at home.”", "“덤벨로 경은 홈부르크며 엠스며 그런 곳에서 이름이 잘 알려져 있으니, 아가씨도 아주 제집처럼 편안하게 지내겠지요.”"]], [["“We are going to Rome,”", "“저희는 로마로 갈 거예요,”"], ["said Griselda, majestically.", "그리젤다가 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["“I suppose Mr. Tickler will come into the diocese soon,”", "“티클러 씨도 곧 이 교구로 들어오겠지요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“I remember hearing him very favourably spoken of by Mr. Slope, who was a friend of his.”", "“그분의 벗이던 슬로프 씨가 그분을 아주 좋게 말하는 것을 들은 기억이 나요.”"]], [["Nothing short of a fixed resolve on the part of Mrs. Grantly that the time had now come in which she must throw away her shield and stand behind her sword, declare war to the knife, and neither give nor take quarter, could have justified such a speech as this.", "이제는 방패를 던져 버리고 칼 뒤에 서서, 칼끝까지 가는 전쟁을 선포하고 자비를 주지도 받지도 않아야 할 때가 왔다고 그랜틀리 부인이 마음을 굳게 정한 것이 아니라면, 이런 말은 무엇으로도 정당하다 할 수 없었다."], ["Any allusion to Mr. Slope acted on Mrs.", "슬로프 씨를 언급하는 것은 붉은 천이 황소에게 하는 것으로 여겨지는 그 작용을 프라우디 부인에게 했다."], ["Proudie as a red cloth is supposed to act on a bull; but when that allusion connected the name of Mr. Slope in a friendly bracket with that of Mrs.", "그런데 그 언급이 슬로프 씨의 이름을 프라우디 부인의 앞날의 사위 이름과 다정한 괄호 안에 함께 묶어 놓았으니, 그 결과가 무서울 것은 틀림없었다."], ["Proudie’s future son-in-law it might be certain that the effect would be terrific.", "게다가 이보다 더한 것이 있었다."], ["And there was more than this: for that very Mr. Slope had once entertained audacious hopes—hopes not thought to be audacious by the young lady herself—with reference to Miss Olivia Proudie.", "바로 그 슬로프 씨가 한때 올리비아 프라우디 양을 두고 대담한 희망을—그 젊은 아가씨 자신은 대담하다고 여기지 않았던 희망을 품은 적이 있었기 때문이다."], ["All this Mrs. Grantly knew, and, knowing it, still dared to mention his name.", "이 모든 것을 그랜틀리 부인은 알고 있었고, 알면서도 감히 그의 이름을 입에 올린 것이었다."]], [["The countenance of Mrs. Proudie became darkened with black anger, and the polished smile of her company manners gave place before the outraged feelings of her nature.", "프라우디 부인의 얼굴빛은 검은 노여움으로 어두워졌고, 사람들 앞에서 갖추는 몸가짐의 그 다듬어진 웃음은 그 본성의 짓밟힌 감정 앞에 자리를 내주었다."]], [["“The man you speak of, Mrs.", "“댁이 말하는 그 사람은, 그랜틀리 부인,”"], ["Grantly,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “was never known as a friend by Mr. Tickler.”", "“티클러 씨가 벗으로 알았던 사람이 아니오.”"]], [["“Oh, indeed,”", "“아, 그런가요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“Perhaps I have made a mistake.", "“제가 잘못 알았을지도 모르겠네요."], ["I am sure I have heard Mr. Slope mention him.”", "슬로프 씨가 그분을 이야기하는 것을 들은 것은 분명한데요.”"]], [["“When Mr. Slope was running after your sister, Mrs. Grantly, and was encouraged by her as he was, you perhaps saw more of him than I did.”", "“슬로프 씨가 댁의 동생 뒤를 쫓아다니고 그 동생이 그를 그렇게 부추기던 때에는, 댁이 나보다 그를 더 많이 보았을 테지요, 그랜틀리 부인.”"]], [["“Mrs. Proudie, that was never the case.”", "“프라우디 부인, 그런 일은 결코 없었어요.”"]], [["“I have reason to know that the archdeacon conceived it to be so, and that he was very unhappy about it.”", "“부주교께서 그렇게 여기셨고 그 일로 몹시 괴로워하셨다는 것을 나는 알 만한 근거가 있소.”"], ["Now this, unfortunately, was a fact which Mrs. Grantly could not deny.", "그런데 딱하게도 이것은 그랜틀리 부인이 부인할 수 없는 사실이었다."]], [["“The archdeacon may have been mistaken about Mr. Slope,”", "“부주교님이 슬로프 씨를 잘못 보셨을 수도 있지요,”"], ["she said, “as were some other people at Barchester.", "그녀가 말했다. “바체스터의 다른 몇 사람도 그랬듯이요."], ["But it was you, I think, Mrs. Proudie, who were responsible for bringing him here.”", "하지만 그를 이곳에 데려온 책임은, 제 생각에는, 프라우디 부인 댁에 있지요.”"]], [["Mrs. Grantly, at this period of the engagement, might have inflicted a fatal wound by referring to poor Olivia’s former love affairs, but she was not destitute of generosity.", "이 교전이 이 대목에 이르렀을 때 그랜틀리 부인은 가엾은 올리비아의 지난 연애 일을 들추어 치명적인 상처를 입힐 수도 있었다."], ["Even in the extremest heat of the battle she knew how to spare the young and tender.", "그러나 그녀에게 너그러움이 없지는 않았다. 싸움이 가장 뜨거운 때에도 그녀는 젊고 연한 것을 살려 두는 법을 알고 있었다."]], [["“When I came here, Mrs. Grantly, I little dreamed what a depth of wickedness might be found in the very close of a cathedral city,”", "“내가 이곳에 왔을 때에는, 그랜틀리 부인, 대성당 도시의 그 구내에서 얼마나 깊은 악함이 나올 수 있는지를 꿈에도 몰랐소,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Then, for dear Olivia’s sake, pray do not bring poor Mr. Tickler to Barchester.”", "“그러면 그 귀한 올리비아를 위해서라도, 가엾은 티클러 씨를 바체스터로 데려오지는 마세요.”"]], [["“Mr. Tickler, Mrs.", "“티클러 씨는, 그랜틀리 부인, 도덕이 확실한 사람이고 생각의 결이 매우 신앙다운 사람이오."], ["Grantly, is a man of assured morals and of a highly religious tone of thinking. I wish every one could be so safe as regards their daughters’ future prospects as I am.”", "딸의 앞날에 관해서 나만큼 안심할 수 있는 사람이 누구나 다 그렇기를 바라오.”"]], [["“Yes, I know he has the advantage of being a family man,”", "“예, 그분이 이미 식구를 거느린 사람이라는 이점을 지녔다는 것은 저도 알아요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly, getting up.", "그랜틀리 부인이 일어서며 말했다."], ["“Good morning, Mrs.", "“안녕히 계세요, 프라우디 부인."], ["Proudie; good day, Olivia.”", "잘 있어요, 올리비아.”"]], [["“A great deal better that than—”", "“그런 편이 이보다는 훨씬 나으—”"], ["But the blow fell upon the empty air; for Mrs.", "그러나 그 매질은 빈 허공에 떨어졌다."], ["Grantly had already escaped on to the staircase while Olivia was ringing the bell for the servant to attend the front-door.", "그랜틀리 부인은 이미 계단으로 빠져나갔고, 올리비아는 앞문을 보아 줄 하인을 부르는 종을 울리고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Mrs. Grantly, as she got into her carriage, smiled slightly, thinking of the battle, and as she sat down she gently pressed her daughter’s hand.", "그랜틀리 부인은 마차에 오르면서 그 싸움을 떠올리며 살짝 웃었고, 자리에 앉으면서 딸의 손을 가만히 눌렀다."], ["But Mrs. Proudie’s face was still dark as Acheron when her enemy withdrew, and with angry tone she sent her daughter to her work.", "그러나 프라우디 부인의 얼굴은 적이 물러난 뒤에도 여전히 아케론처럼 어두웠고, 그녀는 노여운 어조로 딸을 일하러 보냈다."], ["“Mr. Tickler will have great reason to complain if, in your position, you indulge such habits of idleness,”", "“네 처지에 그런 게으른 버릇에 빠져 있으면 티클러 씨가 불평할 큰 근거가 생길 게다,”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["Therefore I conceive that I am justified in saying that in that encounter Mrs. Grantly was the conqueror.", "그러므로 나는 그 맞붙음에서 승리한 사람은 그랜틀리 부인이었다고 말해도 정당하다고 생각한다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLI. DON QUIXOTE.", "# 제41장. 돈키호테"]], [["On the day on which Lucy had her interview with Lady Lufton the dean dined at Framley Parsonage.", "루시가 레이디 러프턴과 마주 앉았던 그날, 수석 사제는 프램리 목사관에서 저녁을 먹었다."], ["He and Robarts had known each other since the latter had been in the diocese, and now, owing to Mark’s preferment in the chapter, had become almost intimate.", "그와 로바츠는 로바츠가 이 주교구에 들어온 뒤로 서로 알고 지내던 사이였고, 이제는 마크가 성당 참사회에서 성직 영전을 얻은 덕에 거의 가까운 벗이 되어 있었다."], ["The dean was greatly pleased with the manner in which poor Mr. Crawley’s children had been conveyed away from Hogglestock, and was inclined to open his heart to the whole Framley household.", "수석 사제는 딱한 크롤리 씨의 아이들이 호글스톡에서 실려 나온 그 방식이 몹시 마음에 들어, 프램리 집안 사람들 모두에게 속을 털어놓고 싶은 마음이 되어 있었다."], ["As he still had to ride home he could only allow himself to remain half an hour after dinner, but in that half-hour he said a great deal about Crawley, complimented Robarts on the manner in which he was playing the part of the Good Samaritan, and then by degrees informed him that it had come to his, the dean’s, ears, before he left Barchester, that a writ was in the hands of certain persons in the city, enabling them to seize—he did not know whether it was the person or the property of the vicar of Framley.", "그는 아직 말을 타고 집까지 돌아가야 했으므로 저녁을 먹은 뒤로 반 시간밖에는 머물기를 스스로에게 허락할 수 없었으나, 그 반 시간 동안 크롤리에 관하여 많은 말을 하였고, 착한 사마리아인의 몫을 해내는 그 방식을 두고 로바츠를 칭찬하였고, 그러고 나서는 차츰차츰, 바체스터를 떠나기 전에 자기 곧 수석 사제의 귀에 이런 말이 들어왔다고 알려 주었다. 시티의 어떤 사람들 손에 영장이 들어 있어서 그들이 프램리 교구 사제를 — 몸을 붙잡는 것인지 재산을 붙잡는 것인지는 자기도 알지 못하겠으나 — 붙잡을 수 있게 되었다는 것이었다."]], [["The fact was that these tidings had been conveyed to the dean with the express intent that he might put Robarts on his guard; but the task of speaking on such a subject to a brother clergyman had been so unpleasant to him that he had been unable to introduce it till the last five minutes before his departure.", "사실 이 소식은 수석 사제가 로바츠에게 조심하라고 일러 줄 수 있게 하려는 뜻에서 일부러 그에게 전해진 것이었다. 그러나 동료 성직자에게 그런 문제를 두고 말을 꺼내는 일이 그에게는 어찌나 언짢았던지, 그는 떠나기 앞서 마지막 오 분이 되기까지 그 말을 시작하지 못하였다."]], [["“I hope you will not put it down as an impertinent interference,”", "“이것을 무람없는 간섭으로 여기지는 않으시겠지요.”"], ["said the dean, apologizing.", "수석 사제가 미안해하며 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "“아닙니다,”"], ["said Mark; “no, I do not think that.”", "마크가 말했다. “아니에요, 그렇게 여기지 않습니다.”"], ["He was so sad at heart that he hardly knew how to speak of it.", "그는 마음이 어찌나 무거웠던지 그 일을 어떻게 말해야 할지 좀처럼 알 수 없었다."]], [["“I do not understand much about such matters,”", "“나는 그런 일을 잘 알지 못해요.”"], ["said the dean; “but I think, if I were you, I should go to a lawyer.", "수석 사제가 말했다. “그러나 내가 그 처지에 있다면 변호사를 찾아가 보겠어요."], ["I should imagine that anything so terribly disagreeable as an arrest might be avoided.”", "체포처럼 그렇게 끔찍하게 언짢은 일이야 피할 수 있으리라고 나는 여겨요.”"]], [["“It is a hard case,”", "“가혹한 일입니다.”"], ["said Mark, pleading his own cause.", "마크가 제 사정을 변호하며 말했다."], ["“Though these men have this claim against me I have never received a shilling either in money or money’s worth.”", "“그 사람들이 저를 상대로 이런 청구를 쥐고 있다지만, 저는 돈으로든 돈어치로든 단 한 실링도 받은 적이 없습니다.”"]], [["“And yet your name is to the bills!”", "“그런데도 어음에 이름이 올라 있군요!”"], ["said the dean.", "수석 사제가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, my name is to the bills, certainly, but it was to oblige a friend.”", "“예, 제 이름이 어음에 올라 있는 것은 틀림없습니다. 그러나 벗의 청을 들어주려고 한 일이었습니다.”"]], [["And then the dean, having given his advice, rode away.", "그러고서 수석 사제는 제 조언을 다 하고 나서 말을 타고 떠났다."], ["He could not understand how a clergyman, situated as was Mr. Robarts, could find himself called upon by friendship to attach his name to accommodation bills which he had not the power of liquidating when due!", "로바츠 씨와 같은 처지에 놓인 성직자가, 만기에 갚을 힘도 없는 융통 어음에 제 이름을 붙이라고 우정에게서 청함을 받는 일이 어떻게 있을 수 있는지 그는 도무지 알 수 없었다!"]], [["On that evening they were both wretched enough at the parsonage.", "그날 저녁 목사관에서는 두 사람 다 넉넉히 참담하였다."], ["Hitherto Mark had hoped that perhaps, after all, no absolutely hostile steps would be taken against him with reference to these bills.", "이때까지 마크는 어쩌면 이 어음들을 두고 자기에게 정말로 적대의 걸음을 내딛는 일은 없으리라고 바라 왔었다."], ["Some unforeseen chance might occur in his favour, or the persons holding them might consent to take small instalments of payment from time to time; but now it seemed that the evil day was actually coming upon him at a blow.", "미리 헤아리지 못한 어떤 기회가 제게 이롭게 생길지도 모르고, 어음을 쥔 사람들이 이따금씩 조금씩 나누어 갚는 것을 받아들여 줄지도 모른다고. 그러나 이제는 재앙의 날이 정말로 한꺼번에 그에게 닥쳐오는 듯하였다."], ["He had no longer any secrets from his wife.", "그는 이제 아내에게 아무 비밀도 없었다."], ["Should he go to a lawyer?", "변호사를 찾아갈 것인가?"], ["and if so, to what lawyer?", "그렇다면 어느 변호사에게?"], ["And when he had found his lawyer, what should he say to him?", "그리고 변호사를 찾아냈을 때 그에게 무엇을 말할 것인가?"], ["Mrs. Robarts at one time suggested that everything should be told to Lady Lufton.", "로바츠 부인은 한때 모든 것을 레이디 러프턴께 말씀드려야 한다고 권해 보았다."], ["Mark, however, could not bring himself to do that.", "그러나 마크는 도저히 그럴 마음이 되지 않았다. “그러면,”"], ["“It would seem,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “as though I wanted her to lend me the money.”", "“내가 그분더러 돈을 빌려 달라고 하는 것처럼 보일 거야.”"]], [["On the following morning Mark did ride into Barchester, dreading, however, lest he should be arrested on his journey, and he did see a lawyer. During his absence two calls were made at the parsonage—one by a very rough-looking individual, who left a suspicious document in the hands of the servant, purporting to be an invitation—not to dinner—from one of the judges of the land; and the other call was made by Lady Lufton in person.", "이튿날 아침 마크는 정말로 말을 타고 바체스터로 들어갔다. 그러나 가는 길에 붙잡히지나 않을까 겁을 내면서였다. 그리고 그는 정말로 변호사를 만났다. 그가 집을 비운 사이에 목사관에는 두 번 찾아온 사람이 있었다. 하나는 몹시 거칠어 보이는 사내였는데, 이 나라 판사 가운데 한 사람이 보낸 초대장 — 저녁 초대는 아닌 — 이라 할 만한 수상한 문서 한 장을 하인의 손에 남기고 갔다. 그리고 또 한 번의 방문은 레이디 러프턴이 몸소 온 것이었다."]], [["Mrs. Robarts had determined to go down to Framley Court on that day.", "로바츠 부인은 그날 프램리 코트에 내려가기로 마음을 정해 두었었다."], ["In accordance with her usual custom she would have been there within an hour or two of Lady Lufton’s return from London, but things between them were not now as they usually had been.", "늘 하던 대로라면 레이디 러프턴이 런던에서 돌아온 지 한두 시간 안에 벌써 거기 가 있었을 터인데, 이제 두 사람 사이는 여느 때와 같지 않았다."], ["This affair of Lucy’s must make a difference, let them both resolve to the contrary as they might. And, indeed, Mrs.", "루시의 이 일은 두 사람이 아무리 그러지 않으려 마음을 다잡아도 어쩔 수 없이 무엇인가를 달라지게 만들 것이었다."], ["Robarts had found that the closeness of her intimacy with Framley Court had been diminishing from day to day since Lucy had first begun to be on friendly terms with Lord Lufton.", "그리고 정말로 로바츠 부인은, 루시가 러프턴 경과 처음 다정한 사이가 되기 시작한 뒤로 프램리 코트와의 그 가까움이 날마다 줄어들어 왔다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Since that she had been less at Framley Court than usual; she had heard from Lady Lufton less frequently by letter during her absence than she had done in former years, and was aware that she was less implicitly trusted with all the affairs of the parish.", "그 뒤로 그녀는 여느 때보다 프램리 코트에 덜 가 있었고, 마님이 집을 비운 동안 지난 해들보다 편지를 덜 받았으며, 교구의 모든 일을 예전처럼 다 믿고 맡겨 주지 않는다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["This had not made her angry, for she was in a manner conscious that it must be so.", "이것이 그녀를 노엽게 하지는 않았으니, 그럴 수밖에 없는 일이라고 어느 만큼은 스스로 느끼고 있었기 때문이다."], ["It made her unhappy, but what could she do?", "그것은 그녀를 서럽게 하였으나, 어찌할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["She could not blame Lucy, nor could she blame Lady Lufton.", "루시를 탓할 수도 없었고 레이디 러프턴을 탓할 수도 없었다."], ["Lord Lufton she did blame, but she did so in the hearing of no one but her husband.", "러프턴 경은 정말로 탓하였으나, 남편 말고는 아무도 듣지 않는 데에서만 그렇게 하였다."]], [["Her mind, however, was made up to go over and bear the first brunt of her ladyship’s arguments, when she was stopped by her ladyship’s arrival.", "그러나 그녀의 마음은 건너가서 마님의 따짐을 첫 매로 받아 내기로 굳어져 있었는데, 마님이 몸소 찾아오는 바람에 걸음이 멈추었다."], ["If it were not for this terrible matter of Lucy’s love—a matter on which they could not now be silent when they met—there would be twenty subjects of pleasant, or, at any rate, not unpleasant conversation.", "루시의 그 끔찍한 사랑 문제 — 이제 만나면 서로 입을 다물고 있을 수 없는 그 문제 — 가 아니라면 즐거운, 아니 적어도 언짢지는 않은 이야깃거리가 스무 가지는 있을 터였다."], ["But even then there would be those terrible bills hanging over her conscience, and almost crushing her by their weight.", "그러나 그렇더라도 그 끔찍한 어음들이 그녀의 양심 위에 걸려 있어 그 무게로 그녀를 거의 짓눌러 놓았을 것이다."], ["At the moment in which Lady Lufton walked up to the drawing-room window, Mrs. Robarts held in her hand that ominous invitation from the judge.", "레이디 러프턴이 응접실 창 쪽으로 걸어 올라온 그 순간, 로바츠 부인은 판사가 보낸 그 불길한 초대장을 손에 쥐고 있었다."], ["Would it not be well that she should make a clean breast of it all, disregarding what her husband had said?", "남편이 한 말은 접어 두고 죄다 털어놓아 버리는 것이 낫지 않을까? 나을지도 몰랐다."], ["It might be well: only this—she had never yet done anything in opposition to her husband’s wishes.", "다만 이것이 있었다 — 그녀는 여태까지 남편의 뜻을 거스르는 일을 한 번도 해 본 적이 없었다."], ["So she hid the slip within her desk, and left the matter open to consideration.", "그리하여 그녀는 그 종이쪽을 문갑 안에 숨기고, 그 문제는 더 생각해 볼 여지로 남겨 두었다."]], [["The interview commenced with an affectionate embrace, as was a matter of course.", "만남은 당연한 일로서 다정한 포옹으로 시작되었다."], ["“Dear Fanny,”", "“패니, 얘야”"], ["and “Dear Lady Lufton,”", "하는 말과 “레이디 러프턴”"], ["was said between them with all the usual warmth.", "하는 말이 여느 때와 같은 따뜻함을 다하여 서로 오갔다."], ["And then the first inquiry was made about the children, and the second about the school.", "그러고서 첫 물음은 아이들에 관한 것이었고 둘째는 학교에 관한 것이었다."], ["For a minute or two Mrs. Robarts thought that, perhaps, nothing was to be said about Lucy.", "일 분이나 이 분 동안 로바츠 부인은 어쩌면 루시에 관해서는 아무 말도 없을지 모른다고 생각했다."], ["If it pleased Lady Lufton to be silent she, at least, would not commence the subject.", "레이디 러프턴이 입을 다물고 있는 편이 좋다면 그녀 쪽에서는 적어도 그 이야기를 먼저 꺼내지 않을 참이었다."]], [["Then there was a word or two spoken about Mrs. Podgens’ baby, after which Lady Lufton asked whether Fanny were alone.", "그러고 나서 포진스 부인의 아기에 관하여 한두 마디가 오갔고, 그 뒤에 레이디 러프턴은 패니가 혼자 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "“예,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."], ["“Mark has gone over to Barchester.”", "“마크는 바체스터에 건너갔습니다.”"]], [["“I hope he will not be long before he lets me see him.", "“너무 오래 있다가 얼굴을 보여 주는 것이 아니었으면 좋겠어요."], ["Perhaps he can call to-morrow.", "어쩌면 내일 들를 수 있겠지요."], ["Would you both come and dine to-morrow?”", "내일 두 사람이 와서 저녁을 먹지 않겠어요?”"]], [["“Not to-morrow, I think, Lady Lufton; but Mark, I am sure, will go over and call.”", "“내일은 어려울 것 같습니다, 레이디 러프턴. 그렇지만 마크는 틀림없이 건너가서 뵙고 올 것입니다.”"]], [["“And why not come to dinner?", "“그런데 왜 저녁을 먹으러 오지 못하지요?"], ["I hope there is to be no change among us, eh, Fanny?”", "우리 사이에 달라지는 것이 없기를 바라요, 그렇지 않아요, 패니?”"], ["and Lady Lufton as she spoke looked into the other’s face in a manner which almost made Mrs. Robarts get up and throw herself on her old friend’s neck.", "그리고 레이디 러프턴은 그렇게 말하면서 상대의 얼굴을 들여다보았는데, 그 눈길이 어찌나 그러하였는지 로바츠 부인은 하마터면 일어나 옛 벗의 목에 몸을 던질 뻔하였다."], ["Where was she to find a friend who would give her such constant love as she had received from Lady Lufton?", "레이디 러프턴에게서 받아 온 것만큼 한결같은 사랑을 주는 벗을 그녀가 어디서 찾겠는가?"], ["And who was kinder, better, more honest than she?", "그리고 그분보다 더 다정하고 더 좋고 더 정직한 사람이 누구겠는가?"]], [["“Change!", "“달라진다니요!"], ["no, I hope not, Lady Lufton;”", "아니에요, 그러지 않기를 바랍니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"], ["and as she spoke the tears stood in her eyes.", "그리고 그렇게 말하면서 그녀의 눈에는 눈물이 고였다."]], [["“Ah, but I shall think there is if you will not come to me as you used to do.", "“아, 그렇지만 하던 대로 나를 찾아오지 않는다면 나는 달라진 것이 있다고 여기겠어요."], ["You always used to come and dine with me the day I came home, as a matter of course.”", "늘 내가 집에 돌아오는 날이면 으레 와서 나와 함께 저녁을 먹었잖아요.”"]], [["What could she say, poor woman, to this?", "딱한 여인이 이 말에 무엇이라 할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["“We were all in confusion yesterday about poor Mrs.", "“어제는 딱한 크롤리 부인 일로 온 집안이 뒤숭숭했고, 수석 사제께서 여기서 저녁을 드셨습니다."], ["Crawley, and the dean dined here; he had been over at Hogglestock to see his friend.”", "그 벗을 보시려고 호글스톡에 다녀오셨거든요.”"]], [["“I have heard of her illness, and will go over and see what ought to be done.", "“그 부인이 앓는다는 이야기는 들었어요. 내가 건너가서 무엇을 해야 할지 보아야겠어요."], ["Don’t you go, do you hear, Fanny?", "패니는 가지 말아요, 알겠지요?"], ["You with your young children!", "어린 아이들을 둔 몸으로!"], ["I should never forgive you if you did.”", "그랬다가는 내가 결코 용서하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["And then Mrs. Robarts explained how Lucy had gone there, had sent the four children back to Framley, and was herself now staying at Hogglestock with the object of nursing Mrs.", "그러고서 로바츠 부인은 루시가 어떻게 거기 갔고, 네 아이를 프램리로 보내고, 지금은 크롤리 부인을 간호할 뜻으로 몸소 호글스톡에 머물고 있는지를 설명했다."], ["Crawley. In telling the story she abstained from praising Lucy with all the strong language which she would have used had not Lucy’s name and character been at the present moment of peculiar import to Lady Lufton; but nevertheless she could not tell it without dwelling much on Lucy’s kindness.", "그 이야기를 하면서 그녀는, 루시의 이름과 사람됨이 지금 이 순간 레이디 러프턴에게 남다른 뜻을 지니지 않았다면 썼을 그 힘찬 말들로 루시를 칭송하는 일은 삼갔다."], ["It would have been ungenerous to Lady Lufton to make much of Lucy’s virtue at this present moment, but unjust to Lucy to make nothing of it.", "그러나 그렇더라도 루시의 다정함을 여러 번 짚지 않고서는 그 이야기를 할 수 없었다. 지금 이 순간에 루시의 미덕을 크게 내세우는 것은 레이디 러프턴에게 너그럽지 못한 일이었을 터이나, 그것을 아무것도 아닌 것으로 만드는 것은 루시에게 옳지 못한 일이었을 터였다."]], [["“And she is actually with Mrs. Crawley now?”", "“그러면 그 아가씨가 정말로 지금 크롤리 부인 곁에 있다는 말이지요?”"], ["asked Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes; Mark left her there yesterday afternoon.”", "“예, 그렇습니다. 마크가 어제 오후에 거기 두고 왔습니다.”"]], [["“And the four children are all here in the house?”", "“그러면 아이 넷이 다 이 집에 있어요?”"]], [["“Not exactly in the house—that is, not as yet.", "“꼭 집 안이라고는 할 수 없습니다 — 그러니까 아직은요."], ["We have arranged a sort of quarantine hospital over the coach-house.”", "마차간 위에 격리 병실 같은 것을 마련해 두었습니다.”"]], [["“What, where Stubbs lives?”", "“무엇이라고요, 스텁스가 사는 데에요?”"]], [["“Yes; Stubbs and his wife have come into the house, and the children are to remain up there till the doctor says that there is no danger of infection.", "“예. 스텁스와 그 아내는 집으로 들어왔고, 아이들은 의사가 이제 옮을 염려가 없다고 할 때까지 그 위에 있게 되어 있습니다."], ["I have not even seen my visitors myself as yet,”", "저는 아직 제 손님들을 제 눈으로 보지도 못했습니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts with a slight laugh.", "로바츠 부인이 살짝 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“Dear me!”", "“이런!”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“I declare you have been very prompt.", "“참으로 손이 빨랐군요."], ["And so Miss Robarts is over there! I should have thought Mr.", "그래서 로바츠 양이 그쪽에 가 있다는 말이지요!"], ["Crawley would have made a difficulty about the children.”", "나는 크롤리 씨가 아이들 일로 어렵게 굴었을 줄 알았어요.”"]], [["“Well, he did; but they kidnapped them,—that is, Lucy and Mark did.", "“그러셨습니다. 그렇지만 두 사람이 아이들을 채어 왔습니다 — 그러니까 루시와 마크가요."], ["The dean gave me such an account of it.", "수석 사제께서 그 이야기를 어찌나 하시던지요."], ["Lucy brought them out by twos and packed them in the pony-carriage, and then Mark drove off at a gallop while Mr. Crawley stood calling to them in the road.", "루시가 둘씩 데리고 나와 조랑말 마차에 실어 넣고, 그러고서 크롤리 씨가 길에 서서 부르는 동안 마크는 말을 몰아 내달렸습니다."], ["The dean was there at the time and saw it all.”", "그때 수석 사제께서 거기 계셔서 그 모든 것을 보셨습니다.”"]], [["“That Miss Lucy of yours seems to be a very determined young lady when she takes a thing into her head,”", "“패니네 그 루시 아가씨는 한번 마음에 들여놓은 일이 있으면 몹시 단단히 마음을 정하는 젊은 아가씨인 듯하군요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton, now sitting down for the first time.", "레이디 러프턴이 이제 처음으로 자리에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, she is,” said Mrs.", "“예, 그렇습니다.”"], ["Robarts, having laid aside all her pleasant animation, for the discussion which she dreaded was now at hand.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다. 그 즐거운 활기를 죄다 내려놓고서였으니, 겁내던 그 이야기가 이제 바로 앞에 닥쳐 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“A very determined young lady,”", "“몹시 단단히 마음을 정하는 젊은 아가씨지요.”"], ["continued Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 이어 말했다."], ["“Of course, my dear Fanny, you know all this about Ludovic and your sister-in-law?”", "“물론 패니, 얘야, 루도빅과 시누이 되는 아가씨에 관한 이 모든 일을 알고 있겠지요?”"]], [["“Yes, she has told me about it.”", "“예, 루시가 저에게 이야기해 주었습니다.”"]], [["“It is very unfortunate—very.”", "“참으로 불행한 일이에요 — 참으로.”"]], [["“I do not think Lucy has been to blame,” said Mrs.", "“저는 루시에게 탓이 있다고 생각하지 않습니다.”"], ["Robarts; and as she spoke the blood was already mounting to her cheeks.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하는 동안 벌써 피가 그녀의 뺨으로 올라오고 있었다."]], [["“Do not be too anxious to defend her, my dear, before any one accuses her.", "“얘야, 아무도 그 아가씨를 몰아세우지 않았는데 너무 애타게 변호하려 들지 말아요."], ["Whenever a person does that it looks as though their cause were weak.”", "사람이 그러면 그 편이 약한 것처럼 보이는 법이에요.”"]], [["“But my cause is not weak as far as Lucy is concerned; I feel quite sure that she has not been to blame.”", "“그렇지만 루시에 관해서라면 제 편은 약하지 않습니다. 저는 그 아이에게 탓이 없다고 아주 굳게 느낍니다.”"]], [["“I know how obstinate you can be, Fanny, when you think it necessary to dub yourself any one’s champion.", "“패니, 누군가의 투사로 스스로를 서임해야겠다고 여길 때에 얼마나 고집스러워질 수 있는지 나는 알아요."], ["Don Quixote was not a better knight-errant than you are.", "돈키호테도 패니보다 나은 편력 기사는 아니었지요."], ["But is it not a pity to take up your lance and shield before an enemy is within sight or hearing?", "그렇지만 적이 보이는 데에도 들리는 데에도 오기 전에 창과 방패를 집어 드는 것은 딱한 일이 아닌가요?"], ["But that was ever the way with your Don Quixotes.”", "하기야 그 돈키호테라는 이들은 늘 그런 식이었지요.”"]], [["“Perhaps there may be an enemy in ambush.” That was Mrs. Robarts’ thought to herself, but she did not dare to express it, so she remained silent.", "‘어쩌면 적이 숨어서 기다리고 있을지도 모르지요.’ 그것이 로바츠 부인이 속으로 한 생각이었으나, 감히 입 밖에 내지 못하고 잠잠히 있었다."]], [["“My only hope is,”", "“내 바람은 오직 하나예요.”"], ["continued Lady Lufton, “that when my back is turned you fight as gallantly for me.”", "레이디 러프턴이 이어 말했다. “내가 등을 돌리고 없을 때에도 패니가 나를 위하여 그만큼 씩씩하게 싸워 준다는 것이지요.”"]], [["“Ah, you are never under a cloud, like poor Lucy.”", "“아, 마님께서는 딱한 루시처럼 구름 아래에 계시는 일이 없으니까요.”"]], [["“Am I not?", "“그렇지 않아요?"], ["But, Fanny, you do not see all the clouds.", "그렇지만 패니, 패니는 그 구름을 다 보지 못하는 거예요."], ["The sun does not always shine for any of us, and the down-pouring rain and the heavy wind scatter also my fairest flowers,—as they have done hers, poor girl.", "우리 가운데 누구에게도 해가 늘 비추는 것은 아니고, 쏟아지는 비와 거센 바람은 내 가장 고운 꽃들도 흩어 놓아요 — 딱한 그 아가씨의 꽃들을 흩어 놓은 것과 같이요."], ["Dear Fanny, I hope it may be long before any cloud comes across the brightness of your heaven.", "패니, 얘야, 패니의 하늘의 그 밝음 위로 어떤 구름이 지나가기까지 오래오래 걸리기를 나는 바라요."], ["Of all the creatures I know you are the one most fitted for quiet continued sunshine.”", "내가 아는 모든 사람 가운데 패니야말로 조용히 이어지는 햇살에 가장 어울리는 사람이에요.”"]], [["And then Mrs. Robarts did get up and embrace her friend, thus hiding the tears which were running down her face.", "그러자 로바츠 부인은 정말로 일어나 벗을 껴안았고, 그리하여 얼굴을 흘러내리는 눈물을 감추었다."], ["Continued sunshine indeed!", "이어지는 햇살이라니!"], ["A dark spot had already gathered on her horizon which was likely to fall in a very waterspout of rain.", "그녀의 지평 위에는 이미 검은 점 하나가 뭉쳐 있었고, 그것은 물기둥 같은 비가 되어 쏟아질 것 같았다."], ["What was to come of that terrible notice which was now lying in the desk under Lady Lufton’s very arm?", "지금 레이디 러프턴의 바로 그 팔 밑에 놓인 문갑 속에 들어 있는 그 끔찍한 통지는 어찌 될 것인가?"]], [["“But I am not come here to croak like an old raven,”", "“그렇지만 나는 늙은 까마귀처럼 흉한 소리나 내려고 여기 온 것이 아니에요.”"], ["continued Lady Lufton, when she had brought this embrace to an end.", "그 포옹을 끝낸 뒤에 레이디 러프턴이 이어 말했다."], ["“It is probable that we all may have our sorrows; but I am quite sure of this,—that if we endeavour to do our duties honestly, we shall all find our consolation and all have our joys also.", "“우리 모두에게 저마다의 서러움이 있을 법하지요. 그러나 나는 이것만은 아주 굳게 알고 있어요 — 우리가 제 소임을 정직하게 하려고 힘쓴다면 우리 모두 위로를 찾아낼 것이고 또 저마다 기쁨도 갖게 될 것이라는 것을요."], ["And now, my dear, let you and I say a few words about this unfortunate affair.", "그러니 이제, 얘야, 패니와 내가 이 불행한 일을 두고 몇 마디 해 보아요."], ["It would not be natural if we were to hold our tongues to each other; would it?”", "서로 입을 다물고 있다면 그것은 자연스럽지 못한 일이겠지요, 그렇지 않아요?”"]], [["“I suppose not,”", "“그렇겠습니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“We should always be conceiving worse than the truth,—each as to the other’s thoughts.", "“우리는 늘 사실보다 나쁘게 그려 보게 될 거예요 — 서로 상대의 생각을 두고요."], ["Now, some time ago, when I spoke to you about your sister-in-law and Ludovic—I daresay you remember—”", "그런데 얼마 전에 내가 시누이 되는 아가씨와 루도빅에 관하여 패니에게 말했을 때 — 아마 기억하겠지요 —”"]], [["“Oh, yes, I remember.”", "“예, 기억합니다.”"]], [["“We both thought then that there would really be no danger.", "“그때 우리 둘은 정말로 아무 위험이 없을 것이라고 생각했지요. 사실을 그대로 말하자면 나는 그 아이의 정이 다른 데에 걸려 있다고 여겼고, 정말이지 그러기를 바랐어요."], ["To tell you the plain truth I fancied, and indeed hoped, that his affections were engaged elsewhere; but I was altogether wrong then; wrong in thinking it, and wrong in hoping it.”", "그러나 그때 나는 아주 틀렸지요. 그렇게 생각한 것도 틀렸고 그렇게 바란 것도 틀렸어요.”"]], [["Mrs. Robarts knew well that Lady Lufton was alluding to Griselda Grantly, but she conceived that it would be discreet to say nothing herself on that subject at present.", "로바츠 부인은 레이디 러프턴이 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 두고 하는 말임을 잘 알았으나, 지금은 그 이야기를 두고 자기 쪽에서 아무 말도 하지 않는 것이 조심스러운 처사라고 여겼다."], ["She remembered, however, Lucy’s flashing eye when the possibility of Lord Lufton making such a marriage was spoken of in the pony-carriage, and could not but feel glad that Lady Lufton had been disappointed.", "그러나 조랑말 마차에서 러프턴 경이 그런 혼인을 치를 수도 있다는 말이 나왔을 때 루시의 눈이 번쩍하던 것을 그녀는 떠올렸고, 레이디 러프턴이 바라던 것을 이루지 못한 것을 기뻐하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“I do not at all impute any blame to Miss Robarts for what has occurred since,”", "“그 뒤에 일어난 일을 두고 나는 로바츠 양에게 조금도 탓을 돌리지 않아요.”"], ["continued her ladyship.", "마님이 이어 말했다."], ["“I wish you distinctly to understand that.”", "“그것만은 패니가 똑똑히 알아주었으면 해요.”"]], [["“I do not see how any one could blame her.", "“어떻게 누가 그 아이를 탓할 수 있을지 저는 모르겠습니다."], ["She has behaved so nobly.”", "그 아이는 참으로 고귀하게 처신했습니다.”"]], [["“It is of no use inquiring whether any one can.", "“누가 그럴 수 있는지를 따져 보아야 아무 쓸데도 없어요."], ["It is sufficient that I do not.”", "내가 그러지 않는다는 것으로 넉넉해요.”"]], [["“But I think that is hardly sufficient,” said Mrs.", "“그렇지만 저는 그것으로는 좀처럼 넉넉하지 않다고 생각합니다.”"], ["Robarts, pertinaciously.", "로바츠 부인이 물러서지 않고 말했다."]], [["“Is it not?”", "“넉넉하지 않아요?”"], ["asked her ladyship, raising her eyebrows.", "마님이 눈썹을 치켜올리며 물었다."]], [["“No.", "“아닙니다."], ["Only think what Lucy has done and is doing.", "루시가 무엇을 했고 무엇을 하고 있는지 생각만 해 보십시오."], ["If she had chosen to say that she would accept your son I really do not know how you could have justly blamed her.", "그 아이가 마님의 아드님을 받아들이겠다고 말하기로 마음먹었더라면, 마님께서 어떻게 그 아이를 옳게 탓하실 수 있었을지 저는 정말 모르겠습니다."], ["I do not by any means say that I would have advised such a thing.”", "제가 그런 일을 권하였을 것이라고 말하는 것은 결코 아닙니다.”"]], [["“I am glad of that, Fanny.”", "“그것은 반가운 말이에요, 패니.”"]], [["“I have not given any advice; nor is it needed.", "“저는 아무 권고도 하지 않았고, 그럴 필요도 없습니다."], ["I know no one more able than Lucy to see clearly, by her own judgment, what course she ought to pursue.", "제 판단으로 어떤 길을 가야 할지를 루시보다 더 밝게 볼 수 있는 사람을 저는 알지 못합니다."], ["I should be afraid to advise one whose mind is so strong, and who, of her own nature, is so self-denying as she is.", "마음이 그토록 굳세고, 타고난 성정이 그토록 자기를 눌러 이기는 사람에게 권고를 하기는 저로서는 겁이 납니다."], ["She is sacrificing herself now, because she will not be the means of bringing trouble and dissension between you and your son.", "그 아이는 지금 자기를 희생하고 있습니다. 마님과 아드님 사이에 번거로움과 다툼을 가져오는 구실이 되지 않으려는 까닭입니다."], ["If you ask me, Lady Lufton, I think you owe her a deep debt of gratitude. I do, indeed.", "저에게 물으신다면, 레이디 러프턴, 저는 마님께서 그 아이에게 깊이 고마워해야 할 빚을 지고 계시다고 생각합니다."], ["And as for blaming her—what has she done that you possibly could blame?”", "정말로 그렇게 생각합니다. 그리고 그 아이를 탓하신다니 — 마님께서 탓하실 만한 무슨 일을 그 아이가 했습니까?”"]], [["“Don Quixote on horseback!”", "“말에 오른 돈키호테로군요!”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“Fanny, I shall always call you Don Quixote, and some day or other I will get somebody to write your adventures.", "“패니, 나는 앞으로 늘 패니를 돈키호테라고 부르겠어요. 그리고 언젠가는 누구를 시켜 패니의 모험담을 쓰게 하겠어요."], ["But the truth is this, my dear: there has been imprudence.", "그렇지만 얘야, 사실은 이러해요. 조심스럽지 못한 일이 있었어요."], ["You may call it mine, if you will—though I really hardly see how I am to take the blame.", "패니가 굳이 그러겠다면 그것을 내 탓이라 불러도 좋아요 — 하기야 내가 어떻게 그 탓을 져야 하는지는 정말 좀처럼 알 수 없지만요."], ["I could not do other than ask Miss Robarts to my house, and I could not very well turn my son out of it.", "나는 로바츠 양을 내 집에 청하지 않을 수 없었고, 내 아들을 집에서 내쫓을 수도 없었지요."], ["In point of fact, it has been the old story.”", "사실 그대로 말하면 그것은 예부터 있는 그 이야기였어요.”"]], [["“Exactly; the story that is as old as the world, and which will continue as long as people are born into it.", "“그렇습니다. 세상만큼이나 오래된 이야기이고, 사람들이 세상에 태어나는 동안에는 이어질 이야기지요."], ["It is a story of God’s own telling!”", "하느님께서 몸소 하시는 이야기입니다!”"]], [["“But, my dear child, you do not mean that every young gentleman and every young lady should fall in love with each other directly they meet!", "“그렇지만 얘야, 젊은 신사와 젊은 아가씨라면 만나기가 바쁘게 서로 사랑에 빠져야 한다는 말은 아니겠지요!"], ["Such a doctrine would be very inconvenient.”", "그런 교리는 아주 거북한 것이 될 거예요.”"]], [["“No, I do not mean that.", "“아닙니다, 그런 말은 아닙니다."], ["Lord Lufton and Miss Grantly did not fall in love with each other, though you meant them to do so.", "러프턴 경과 그랜틀리 양은 서로 사랑에 빠지지 않았습니다. 마님께서는 그러기를 뜻하셨지만요."], ["But was it not quite as natural that Lord Lufton and Lucy should do so instead?”", "그렇지만 그 대신에 러프턴 경과 루시가 그렇게 되는 것도 그만큼 자연스러운 일이 아니었습니까?”"]], [["“It is generally thought, Fanny, that young ladies should not give loose to their affections until they have been certified of their friends’ approval.”", "“패니, 젊은 아가씨라면 벗들의 승낙을 확인받기까지는 제 정에 고삐를 놓아 주어서는 안 된다고 여기는 것이 보통이에요.”"]], [["“And that young gentlemen of fortune may amuse themselves as they please!", "“그리고 재산 있는 젊은 신사들은 마음대로 즐겨도 좋다고요!"], ["I know that is what the world teaches, but I cannot agree to the justice of it.", "세상이 그렇게 가르치는 것은 저도 압니다. 그러나 저는 그것이 옳다고는 여길 수 없습니다."], ["The terrible suffering which Lucy has to endure makes me cry out against it.", "루시가 견뎌야 하는 그 끔찍한 괴로움이 저로 하여금 그것에 맞서 소리치게 만듭니다."], ["She did not seek your son.", "그 아이는 마님의 아드님을 찾아다니지 않았습니다."], ["The moment she began to suspect that there might be danger she avoided him scrupulously.", "위험이 있을지도 모른다고 짐작하기 시작한 그 순간부터 그 아이는 그분을 애써 피했습니다."], ["She would not go down to Framley Court, though her not doing so was remarked by yourself.", "프램리 코트에 내려가려 하지 않았고, 그러지 않는 것을 마님께서도 몸소 말씀하셨지요."], ["She would hardly go out about the place lest she should meet him.", "그분과 마주칠까 하여 이 근방으로 나가려고도 좀처럼 하지 않았습니다."], ["She was contented to put herself altogether in the background till he should have pleased to leave the place.", "그분이 이곳을 떠나 주실 때까지 아주 뒤로 물러나 있는 것으로 그 아이는 흡족했습니다."], ["But he—he came to her here, and insisted on seeing her.", "그러나 그분이 — 그분이 여기 이 아이를 찾아와 만나겠다고 우기셨습니다."], ["He found her when I was out, and declared himself determined to speak to her.", "제가 나가 있을 때에 그 아이를 찾아내고, 이야기하기로 마음을 굳혔노라고 선언하셨습니다."], ["What was she to do?", "그 아이가 어찌해야 했겠습니까?"], ["She did try to escape, but he stopped her at the door.", "그 아이는 정말로 빠져나가려 했으나 그분이 문간에서 그 아이를 막아 세웠습니다."], ["Was it her fault that he made her an offer?”", "그분이 그 아이에게 청혼한 것이 그 아이의 잘못입니까?”"]], [["“My dear, no one has said so.”", "“얘야, 아무도 그렇게 말하지 않았어요.”"]], [["“Yes, but you do say so when you tell me that young ladies should not give play to their affections without permission.", "“아닙니다, 마님께서 그렇게 말씀하시는 것입니다. 젊은 아가씨는 허락 없이 제 정을 마음대로 놀려서는 안 된다고 저에게 말씀하실 때에요."], ["He persisted in saying to her, here, all that it pleased him, though she implored him to be silent.", "그 아이가 제발 그만해 달라고 빌었는데도 그분은 여기서 하고 싶은 말을 다 하겠다고 버티셨습니다."], ["I cannot tell the words she used, but she did implore him.”", "그 아이가 어떤 말을 썼는지 저는 옮길 수 없으나 그 아이는 정말로 빌었습니다.”"]], [["“I do not doubt that she behaved well.”", "“그 아가씨가 잘 처신했다는 것은 나도 의심하지 않아요.”"]], [["“But he—he persisted, and begged her to accept his hand.", "“그러나 그분은 — 그분은 버티시며 제 손을 받아 달라고 애원하셨습니다."], ["She refused him then, Lady Lufton—not as some girls do, with a mock reserve, not intending to be taken at their words—but steadily, and, God forgive her, untruly.", "그때 그 아이는 그분을 거절했습니다, 레이디 러프턴 — 어떤 처녀들이 하듯 거짓 사양으로, 그 말대로 받아들여지기를 바라지도 않으면서 그런 것이 아니라, 한결같이, 그리고 하느님께서 용서해 주시기를, 거짓으로 그리했습니다."], ["Knowing what your feelings would be, and knowing what the world would say, she declared to him that he was indifferent to her.", "마님의 마음이 어떠할지를 알고, 세상이 무엇이라 할지를 알았기에, 그 아이는 그분이 자기에게는 아무렇지도 않은 사람이라고 그분께 밝혔습니다."], ["What more could she do in your behalf?”", "마님을 위하여 그 아이가 그보다 더 무엇을 할 수 있었겠습니까?”"], ["And then Mrs. Robarts paused.", "그러고서 로바츠 부인은 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“I shall wait till you have done, Fanny.”", "“패니가 말을 다 마치기까지 기다리겠어요.”"]], [["“You spoke of girls giving loose to their affections.", "“처녀들이 제 정에 고삐를 놓아 준다고 마님께서 말씀하셨지요."], ["She did not do so.", "그 아이는 그렇게 하지 않았습니다."], ["She went about her work exactly as she had done before.", "그 아이는 전에 하던 그대로 제 일을 해 나갔습니다."], ["She did not even speak to me of what had passed—not then, at least.", "지나간 일을 저에게 말하지도 않았습니다 — 적어도 그때에는요."], ["She determined that it should all be as though it had never been.", "그 아이는 그 모든 것을 아예 없었던 일처럼 하기로 마음을 굳혔습니다."], ["She had learned to love your son; but that was her misfortune and she would get over it as she might.", "그 아이는 마님의 아드님을 사랑하게 되었으나, 그것은 그 아이의 불행이었고 어떻게든 스스로 이겨 내려는 것이었습니다."], ["Tidings came to us here that he was engaged, or about to engage himself, to Miss Grantly.”", "그분이 그랜틀리 양과 약혼했다거나 곧 약혼하려 한다는 소식이 이곳 우리에게 이르렀습니다.”"]], [["“Those tidings were untrue.”", "“그 소식은 거짓이었어요.”"]], [["“Yes, we know that now; but she did not know it then.", "“예, 이제는 우리도 압니다. 그러나 그때 그 아이는 그것을 알지 못했습니다."], ["Of course she could not but suffer; but she suffered within herself.”", "물론 괴로워하지 않을 수 없었지요. 그러나 그 아이는 속으로만 괴로워했습니다.”"], ["Mrs. Robarts, as she said this, remembered the pony-carriage and how Puck had been beaten.", "이 말을 하면서 로바츠 부인은 조랑말 마차와 퍽이 어떻게 매를 맞았는지를 떠올렸다."], ["“She made no complaint that he had ill-treated her—not even to herself.", "“그분이 자기를 나쁘게 대했다고 그 아이는 조금도 투정하지 않았습니다 — 스스로에게조차 그러지 않았습니다."], ["She had thought it right to reject his offer; and there, as far as he was concerned, was to be an end of it.”", "그 아이는 그 청혼을 물리치는 것이 옳다고 생각했고, 그분에 관한 한 그것으로 끝이 되어야 했습니다.”"]], [["“That would be a matter of course, I should suppose.”", "“그것이야 당연한 일이겠지요.”"]], [["“But it was not a matter of course, Lady Lufton.", "“그러나 그것은 당연한 일이 되지 않았습니다, 레이디 러프턴."], ["He returned from London to Framley on purpose to repeat his offer.", "그분은 그 청혼을 되풀이하시려고 일부러 런던에서 프램리로 돌아오셨습니다."], ["He sent for her brother— You talk of a young lady waiting for her friends’ approval.", "그 아이의 오라비를 부르셨고 — 젊은 아가씨가 벗들의 승낙을 기다려야 한다고 마님께서 말씀하시지요."], ["In this matter who would be Lucy’s friends?”", "이 일에서 루시의 벗들은 누구겠습니까?”"]], [["“You and Mr. Robarts, of course.”", "“물론 패니와 로바츠 씨지요.”"]], [["“Exactly; her only friends.", "“그렇습니다. 그 아이의 유일한 벗들이지요."], ["Well, Lord Lufton sent for Mark and repeated his offer to him.", "그런데 러프턴 경은 마크를 부르셔서 그에게 그 청혼을 되풀이하셨습니다."], ["Mind you, Mark had never heard a word of this before, and you may guess whether or no he was surprised.", "아셔야 할 것은, 마크는 그때까지 이 일을 한마디도 들은 적이 없었으니 놀랐는지 어떤지는 짐작이 되실 것입니다."], ["Lord Lufton repeated his offer in the most formal manner and claimed permission to see Lucy.", "러프턴 경은 더없이 격식을 갖추어 그 청혼을 되풀이하시고 루시를 만나게 해 달라고 요구하셨습니다."], ["She refused to see him.", "그 아이는 만나기를 마다했습니다."], ["She has never seen him since that day when, in opposition to all her efforts, he made his way into this room.", "온 힘을 다해 막았는데도 그분이 이 방으로 들어오셨던 그날 뒤로 그 아이는 그분을 만난 적이 없습니다."], ["Mark,—as I think very properly,—would have allowed Lord Lufton to come up here.", "마크는 — 제 생각에는 아주 옳게도 — 러프턴 경이 여기 올라오시게 하려 했습니다."], ["Looking at both their ages and position he could have had no right to forbid it.", "두 분의 나이와 처지를 보아 그가 그것을 막을 권리는 있을 수 없었지요."], ["But Lucy positively refused to see your son, and sent him a message instead, of the purport of which you are now aware—that she would never accept him unless she did so at your request.”", "그러나 루시는 마님의 아드님을 만나기를 딱 잘라 마다하고, 그 대신 전갈을 보냈습니다. 그 뜻은 이제 마님께서도 아시는 그것입니다 — 마님께서 청해 주시지 않는 한 결코 그분을 받아들이지 않겠다는 것이었습니다.”"]], [["“It was a very proper message.”", "“아주 옳은 전갈이었어요.”"]], [["“I say nothing about that.", "“그것에 관해서는 저는 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다."], ["Had she accepted him I would not have blamed her:—and so I told her, Lady Lufton.”", "그 아이가 그분을 받아들였더라도 저는 그 아이를 탓하지 않았을 것입니다 — 그리고 그렇게 그 아이에게 말했습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“I cannot understand your saying that, Fanny.”", "“패니가 그런 말을 했다는 것은 나로서는 알아들을 수 없어요.”"]], [["“Well; I did say so.", "“그렇지만 저는 그렇게 말했습니다."], ["I don’t want to argue now about myself,—whether I was right or wrong, but I did say so.", "지금 저 자신에 관하여 — 제가 옳았는지 그르렀는지를 두고 다투고 싶지는 않습니다. 그러나 저는 정말로 그렇게 말했습니다."], ["Whatever sanction I could give she would have had.", "제가 줄 수 있는 허락이라면 무엇이든 그 아이는 받았을 것입니다."], ["But she again chose to sacrifice herself, although I believe she regards him with as true a love as ever a girl felt for a man.", "그러나 그 아이는 다시 제 몸을 희생하기를 골랐습니다. 처녀가 사내에게 느낀 어떤 사랑에도 못하지 않은 참된 사랑으로 그분에게 마음을 두고 있다고 저는 믿습니다만요."], ["Upon my word I don’t know that she is right.", "참말이지 저는 그 아이가 옳은지 모르겠습니다."], ["Those considerations for the world may perhaps be carried too far.”", "세상을 헤아리는 그런 마음도 어쩌면 너무 멀리까지 끌고 가는 수가 있습니다.”"]], [["“I think that she was perfectly right.”", "“나는 그 아가씨가 아주 옳았다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“Very well, Lady Lufton; I can understand that.", "“좋습니다, 레이디 러프턴. 그것은 저도 알아듣습니다."], ["But after such sacrifice on her part—a sacrifice made entirely to you—how can you talk of ‘not blaming her’?", "그러나 그 아이 쪽에서 그런 희생을 치른 뒤에 — 오로지 마님을 위하여 치른 희생인데 — 어떻게 ‘그 아이를 탓하지는 않는다’는 말씀을 하실 수 있습니까?"], ["Is that the language in which you speak of those whose conduct from first to last has been superlatively excellent?", "처음부터 끝까지 그 처신이 그 위없이 훌륭했던 사람을 두고 하는 말씨가 그런 것입니까?"], ["If she is open to blame at all, it is—it is—”", "그 아이에게 탓할 데가 조금이라도 있다면, 그것은 — 그것은 —”"]], [["But here Mrs. Robarts stopped herself.", "그러나 여기서 로바츠 부인은 스스로 말을 멈추었다."], ["In defending her sister she had worked herself almost into a passion; but such a state of feeling was not customary to her, and now that she had spoken her mind she sank suddenly into silence.", "시누이를 변호하다가 그녀는 저를 몰아 거의 격정에 이르렀던 것이다. 그러나 그런 심사는 그녀에게 익숙한 것이 아니었고, 이제 마음속을 다 말해 버리고 나자 그녀는 갑자기 잠잠함 속으로 가라앉았다."]], [["“It seems to me, Fanny, that you almost regret Miss Robarts’ decision,”", "“패니는 로바츠 양의 결정을 거의 아쉬워하는 듯이 내게 보이는군요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“My wish in this matter is for her happiness, and I regret anything that may mar it.”", "“이 일에서 제 바람은 그 아이의 행복입니다. 그리고 그것을 망칠 만한 것이라면 무엇이든 저는 아쉬워합니다.”"]], [["“You think nothing then of our welfare, and yet I do not know to whom I might have looked for hearty friendship and for sympathy in difficulties, if not to you?”", "“그러면 우리의 안녕은 아무렇지도 않다는 말이지요. 그런데도 패니가 아니라면 마음에서 나오는 우정과 어려울 때의 헤아림을 내가 누구에게 바랄 수 있었을지 나는 모르겠어요.”"]], [["Poor Mrs. Robarts was almost upset by this.", "딱한 로바츠 부인은 이 말에 거의 무너질 뻔했다."], ["A few months ago, before Lucy’s arrival, she would have declared that the interests of Lady Lufton’s family would have been paramount with her, after and next to those of her own husband.", "몇 달 전, 루시가 오기 전이라면 그녀는 레이디 러프턴 집안의 이해가 제 남편의 것 다음으로, 그 바로 뒤에 가장 으뜸이 된다고 잘라 말했을 것이다."], ["And even now, it seemed to argue so black an ingratitude on her part—this accusation that she was indifferent to them!", "그리고 지금도 이 말은 — 자기가 그들에게 무심하다는 이 몰아세움은 — 제 쪽의 그토록 검은 배은망덕을 따지는 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["From her childhood upwards she had revered and loved Lady Lufton, and for years had taught herself to regard her as an epitome of all that was good and gracious in woman.", "어린 시절부터 그녀는 레이디 러프턴을 우러르고 사랑해 왔고, 여러 해 동안 그분을 여인에게 있는 좋고 우아한 모든 것의 축도로 여기도록 스스로를 가르쳐 왔다."], ["Lady Lufton’s theories of life had been accepted by her as the right theories, and those whom Lady Lufton had liked she had liked.", "레이디 러프턴의 삶에 관한 이론들은 그녀에게 옳은 이론으로 받아들여져 왔고, 레이디 러프턴이 마음에 들인 사람이면 그녀도 마음에 들였다."], ["But now it seemed that all these ideas which it had taken a life to build up were to be thrown to the ground, because she was bound to defend a sister-in-law whom she had only known for the last eight months.", "그러나 이제는 한 생애가 걸려 세워 온 이 모든 생각이 땅에 던져지게 될 듯하였다. 지난 여덟 달 동안만 알아 온 시누이를 변호하지 않을 수 없기 때문이었다."], ["It was not that she regretted a word that she had spoken on Lucy’s behalf.", "루시를 위하여 한 말을 한마디라도 아쉬워한다는 것은 아니었다."], ["Chance had thrown her and Lucy together, and, as Lucy was her sister, she should receive from her a sister’s treatment.", "우연이 그녀와 루시를 함께 던져 놓았고, 루시는 그녀의 자매였으니 그녀에게서 자매로서의 대접을 받아야 했다."], ["But she did not the less feel how terrible would be the effect of any disseverance from Lady Lufton.", "그러나 그렇더라도 레이디 러프턴과 갈라서는 일이 있다면 그 결과가 얼마나 끔찍할지를 그녀는 못지않게 느꼈다."]], [["“Oh, Lady Lufton,”", "“아, 레이디 러프턴,”"], ["she said, “do not say that.”", "그녀가 말했다. “그런 말씀은 하지 마십시오.”"]], [["“But, Fanny, dear, I must speak as I find.", "“그렇지만 패니, 얘야, 나는 내가 겪는 대로 말해야 해요."], ["You were talking about clouds just now, and do you think that all this is not a cloud in my sky?", "방금 구름 이야기를 했지요. 그러면 이 모든 것이 내 하늘의 구름이 아니라고 생각해요?"], ["Ludovic tells me that he is attached to Miss Robarts, and you tell me that she is attached to him; and I am called upon to decide between them.", "루도빅은 로바츠 양에게 마음이 붙어 있다고 나에게 말하고, 패니는 그 아가씨가 루도빅에게 마음이 붙어 있다고 나에게 말하지요. 그리고 나는 그 둘 사이를 판결하라는 요구를 받고 있어요."], ["Her very act obliges me to do so.”", "그 아가씨의 그 행동이 바로 나로 하여금 그러지 않을 수 없게 만들어요.”"]], [["“Dear Lady Lufton,”", "“레이디 러프턴,”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts, springing from her seat.", "로바츠 부인이 자리에서 벌떡 일어나며 말했다."], ["It seemed to her at the moment as though the whole difficulty were to be solved by an act of grace on the part of her old friend.", "그 순간 그녀에게는 이 모든 어려움이 옛 벗의 너그러운 처분 하나로 풀리려는 것처럼 여겨졌다."]], [["“And yet I cannot approve of such a marriage,”", "“그런데도 나는 그런 혼인을 승낙할 수 없어요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Robarts returned to her seat, saying nothing further.", "로바츠 부인은 더 아무 말도 하지 않고 제 자리로 돌아갔다."]], [["“Is not that a cloud on one’s horizon?”", "“그것이 사람의 지평 위에 뜬 구름이 아닌가요?”"], ["continued her ladyship.", "마님이 이어 말했다."], ["“Do you think that I can be basking in the sunshine while I have such a weight upon my heart as that?", "“그런 무게를 마음에 얹고 있으면서 내가 햇살을 쬐며 편안히 있을 수 있다고 생각해요?"], ["Ludovic will soon be home, but instead of looking to his return with pleasure I dread it.", "루도빅이 곧 집에 올 텐데, 나는 그 돌아옴을 기쁘게 기다리기는커녕 겁을 내고 있어요."], ["I would prefer that he should remain in Norway.", "나는 그 아이가 노르웨이에 그대로 있어 주었으면 해요."], ["I would wish that he should stay away for months.", "몇 달이고 떠나 있어 주었으면 해요."], ["And, Fanny, it is a great addition to my misfortune to feel that you do not sympathize with me.”", "그리고 패니, 패니가 나를 헤아려 주지 않는다고 느끼는 것은 내 불행에 크게 보태지는 일이에요.”"]], [["Having said this, in a slow, sorrowful, and severe tone, Lady Lufton got up and took her departure.", "느리고 서럽고 매서운 어조로 이 말을 하고 나서 레이디 러프턴은 일어나 떠났다."], ["Of course Mrs. Robarts did not let her go without assuring her that she did sympathize with her,—did love her as she ever had loved her.", "물론 로바츠 부인은 자기가 정말로 마님을 헤아리고 있다고, 여태 사랑해 온 그대로 정말로 사랑하고 있다고 다짐해 두지 않고서는 그분을 보내지 않았다."], ["But wounds cannot be cured as easily as they may be inflicted, and Lady Lufton went her way with much real sorrow at her heart.", "그러나 상처는 입히기만큼 쉽게 나을 수 있는 것이 아니어서, 레이디 러프턴은 마음에 참으로 큰 서러움을 안고 제 길을 갔다."], ["She was proud and masterful, fond of her own way, and much too careful of the worldly dignities to which her lot had called her: but she was a woman who could cause no sorrow to those she loved without deep sorrow to herself.", "그녀는 거만하고 남을 휘두르려 했고, 제 뜻대로 하기를 좋아했고, 제 처지가 불러 준 세상의 위엄에 지나칠 만큼 마음을 썼다. 그러나 그녀는 사랑하는 이들에게 서러움을 주면서 스스로 깊은 서러움을 겪지 않을 수 없는 여인이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLII. TOUCHING PITCH.", "# 제42장. 역청을 만지다"]], [["In these hot midsummer days, the end of June and the beginning of July, Mr. Sowerby had but an uneasy time of it.", "한여름의 이 더운 날들, 곧 유월 끝과 칠월 초에 사워비 씨는 마음 편치 못한 시절을 보냈다."], ["At his sister’s instance, he had hurried up to London, and there had remained for days in attendance on the lawyers.", "누이동생의 권함에 따라 그는 서둘러 런던으로 올라갔고, 거기서 여러 날을 변호사들에게 매여 있었다."], ["He had to see new lawyers, Miss Dunstable’s men of business, quiet old cautious gentlemen whose place of business was in a dark alley behind the Bank, Messrs.", "그는 새 변호사들, 곧 던스터블 양의 법률 대리인들을 만나야 했다."], ["Slow and Bideawhile by name, who had no scruple in detaining him for hours while they or their clerks talked to him about anything or about nothing. It was of vital consequence to Mr.", "은행 뒤편 어두운 골목에 사무소를 둔, 조용하고 늙고 조심스러운 신사들이었고 이름은 슬로 바이더와일 상회였는데, 그들이나 그들의 서기가 무엇이든 이야기를 하거나 아무것도 아닌 것을 이야기하는 동안 그를 몇 시간이나 붙잡아 두는 데 조금도 거리낌이 없었다."], ["Sowerby that this business of his should be settled without delay, and yet these men, to whose care this settling was now confided, went on as though law processes were a sunny bank on which it delighted men to bask easily.", "사워비 씨에게는 이 일이 미루어지지 않고 마무리되는 것이 목숨이 걸린 만큼 중대한 일이었으나, 이 마무리를 이제 맡아 보게 된 이 사람들은 법의 절차란 사람들이 느긋하게 햇볕을 쬐며 즐거워하는 햇살 좋은 언덕이라도 되는 듯이 굴었다."], ["And then, too, he had to go more than once to South Audley Street, which was a worse infliction; for the men in South Audley Street were less civil now than had been their wont.", "그러고서 그는 또 한 번 이상 사우스 오들리 스트리트에 가야 했는데, 그것은 더한 곤욕이었다. 사우스 오들리 스트리트의 사람들이 이제는 전에 하던 버릇보다 덜 공손했기 때문이다."], ["It was well understood there that Mr. Sowerby was no longer a client of the duke’s, but his opponent; no longer his nominee and dependant, but his enemy in the county.", "사워비 씨가 이제는 공작의 의뢰인이 아니라 그의 맞수요, 이제는 그의 추천을 받은 자요 딸린 사람이 아니라 이 주에서 그의 원수라는 것이 거기서는 잘 알려져 있었다."], ["“Chaldicotes,”", "“콜디코츠는,”"], ["as old Mr. Gumption remarked to young Mr.", "늙은 검션 씨가 젊은 게이지비 씨에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["Gagebee; “Chaldicotes, Gagebee, is a cooked goose, as far as Sowerby is concerned.", "“콜디코츠는, 게이지비, 사워비에 관한 한 이미 구워 놓은 거위야."], ["And what difference could it make to him whether the duke is to own it or Miss Dunstable?", "그리고 그것을 공작이 갖게 되든 던스터블 양이 갖게 되든 그에게 무슨 차이가 있단 말인가?"], ["For my part I cannot understand how a gentleman like Sowerby can like to see his property go into the hands of a gallipot wench whose money still smells of bad drugs.", "나로서는 사워비 같은 신사가 어떻게 제 땅이, 그 돈에서 아직도 못된 약 냄새가 나는 약단지 계집의 손에 넘어가는 것을 보고 싶어 할 수 있는지 도무지 알 수가 없어."], ["And nothing can be more ungrateful,” he said, “than Sowerby’s conduct.", "그리고 사워비가 하는 짓만큼 은혜를 모르는 것도 없지.”"], ["He has held the county for five-and-twenty years without expense; and now that the time for payment has come, he begrudges the price.”", "그는 말했다. “그는 스물다섯 해 동안 비용 한 푼 들이지 않고 이 주를 지녀 왔는데, 이제 값을 치를 때가 오니 그 값을 아까워하고 있어.”"], ["He called it no better than cheating, he did not—he, Mr. Gumption.", "그는 그것을 속임질보다 나은 무엇으로도 부르지 않았다, 그러지 않았다 — 그, 검션 씨는."], ["According to his ideas Sowerby was attempting to cheat the duke.", "그의 생각에 따르면 사워비는 공작을 속이려 하고 있는 것이었다."], ["It may be imagined, therefore, that Mr. Sowerby did not feel any very great delight in attending at South Audley Street.", "그러니 사워비 씨가 사우스 오들리 스트리트에 드나드는 일에서 그리 큰 즐거움을 느끼지 못했으리라는 것은 짐작할 만하다."]], [["And then rumour was spread about among all the bill-discounting leeches that blood was once more to be sucked from the Sowerby carcase.", "그러고 나서 어음을 할인하는 온갖 말거머리들 사이에, 사워비의 시체에서 다시 한번 피를 빨아낼 수 있게 되었다는 소문이 퍼졌다."], ["The rich Miss Dunstable had taken up his affairs; so much as that became known in the purlieus of the Goat and Compasses.", "그 부유한 던스터블 양이 그의 일을 떠맡았다는 것, 그만큼은 「염소와 나침반」 근방에 알려졌다."], ["Tom Tozer’s brother declared that she and Sowerby were going to make a match of it, and that any scrap of paper with Sowerby’s name on it would become worth its weight in bank-notes; but Tom Tozer himself—Tom, who was the real hero of the family—pooh-poohed at this, screwing up his nose, and alluding in most contemptuous terms to his brother’s softness.", "톰 토저의 형은 그 여인과 사워비가 혼사를 이루려 하고 있으니 사워비의 이름이 적힌 종이쪽이라면 무엇이든 그 무게만큼의 은행권 값이 나가게 될 것이라고 떠벌렸다. 그러나 그 집안의 진짜 영웅인 톰 토저 자신은 코를 찡그리며 이것을 콧방귀로 물리쳤고, 제 형의 무름을 두고 몹시 업신여기는 말을 늘어놓았다."], ["He knew better—as was indeed the fact.", "그는 더 잘 알고 있었으니, 실로 그것이 사실이었다."], ["Miss Dunstable was buying up the squire, and by jingo she should buy them up—them, the Tozers, as well as others!", "던스터블 양은 그 지주를 사들이고 있었고, 맹세코 그 여자는 그들까지 사들여야 했다 — 그들, 곧 다른 사람들 못지않게 이 토저들까지도!"], ["They knew their value, the Tozers did;—whereupon they became more than ordinarily active.", "그들은 제 값을 알고 있었다, 이 토저들은 알고 있었다. 그리하여 그들은 여느 때보다 더 부지런해졌다."]], [["From them and all their brethren Mr. Sowerby at this time endeavoured to keep his distance, but his endeavours were not altogether effectual.", "이때 사워비 씨는 그들과 그 모든 동류에게서 거리를 두려고 애썼으나, 그 애씀이 온전히 들어맞지는 않았다."], ["Whenever he could escape for a day or two from the lawyers he ran down to Chaldicotes; but Tom Tozer in his perseverance followed him there, and boldly sent in his name by the servant at the front-door.", "하루나 이틀 변호사들에게서 빠져나올 수 있을 때마다 그는 콜디코츠로 달려 내려갔다. 그러나 톰 토저는 그 끈질김으로 거기까지 그를 따라 내려와, 앞문의 하인을 통해 뻔뻔스럽게 제 이름을 들여보냈다."]], [["“Mr. Sowerby is not just at home at the present moment,”", "“사워비 씨는 지금 이 순간 집에 계시지 않습니다요.”"], ["said the well-trained domestic.", "잘 길들여진 하인이 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“I’ll wait about then,”", "“그럼 나는 여기서 기다리고 있겠소.”"], ["said Tom, seating himself on an heraldic stone griffin which flanked the big stone steps before the house.", "톰은 그렇게 말하고, 집 앞의 큰 돌계단 양옆을 지키고 선 문장 무늬의 돌 그리핀에 올라앉았다."], ["And in this way Mr. Tozer gained his purpose.", "그리하여 이런 식으로 토저 씨는 제 뜻을 이루었다."], ["Sowerby was still contesting the county, and it behoved him not to let his enemies say that he was hiding himself.", "사워비는 아직 이 주의 의석을 다투고 있었으므로, 자기가 숨어 있다는 말을 원수들이 하게 두어서는 안 되었다."], ["It had been a part of his bargain with Miss Dunstable that he should contest the county.", "이 주의 의석을 다툰다는 것은 던스터블 양과 맺은 그의 약조의 한 부분이었다."], ["She had taken it into her head that the duke had behaved badly, and she had resolved that he should be made to pay for it.", "공작이 못되게 굴었다는 생각이 그녀의 머리에 박혀 있었고, 그 값을 그가 치르게 하겠다고 그녀는 마음을 굳혔던 것이다."], ["“The duke,”", "“공작이,”"], ["she said, “had meddled long enough;”", "그녀는 말했다. “참견을 충분히 오래 했지요.”"], ["she would now see whether the Chaldicotes interest would not suffice of itself to return a member for the county, even in opposition to the duke.", "이제 그녀는 공작에 맞서서라도 콜디코츠 쪽 이해만으로 이 주의 의원 한 사람을 당선시키기에 넉넉하지 않은지 지켜볼 참이었다."], ["Mr. Sowerby himself was so harassed at the time, that he would have given way on this point if he had had the power; but Miss Dunstable was determined, and he was obliged to yield to her.", "사워비 씨 자신은 그때 어찌나 시달리고 있었던지, 힘이 있었다면 이 점에서는 물러섰을 것이다."], ["In this manner Mr. Tom Tozer succeeded and did make his way into Mr.", "그러나 던스터블 양의 뜻이 굳었으므로 그는 그녀에게 굽히지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Sowerby’s presence—of which intrusion one effect was the following letter from Mr. Sowerby to his friend Mark Robarts:—", "이런 식으로 톰 토저 씨는 뜻을 이루어 사워비 씨의 앞까지 밀고 들어갔으니, 그 밀고 들어옴의 한 가지 결과가 사워비 씨가 제 벗 마크 로바츠에게 보낸 다음 편지였다."]], [["Chaldicotes, July, 185—.", "콜디코츠, 185─년 7월."]], [["MY DEAR ROBARTS,—I am so harassed at the present moment by an infinity of troubles of my own that I am almost callous to those of other people.", "친애하는 로바츠, — 나는 지금 이 순간 나 자신의 끝없는 곤경에 어찌나 시달리고 있는지 남의 곤경에는 거의 무디어져 있습니다."], ["They say that prosperity makes a man selfish.", "사람이 형편이 좋으면 이기적으로 된다고들 하지요."], ["I have never tried that, but I am quite sure that adversity does so.", "나는 그것을 겪어 본 적이 없으나, 형편이 나빠지면 그렇게 된다는 것은 아주 확실히 알고 있습니다."], ["Nevertheless I am anxious about those bills of yours—", "그렇더라도 나는 당신의 그 어음들이 걱정입니다 —"]], [["“Bills of mine!”", "“내 어음이라니!”"], ["said Robarts to himself, as he walked up and down the shrubbery path at the parsonage, reading this letter.", "목사관의 관목 산책길을 오르내리며 이 편지를 읽던 로바츠가 혼자 그렇게 말했다."], ["This happened a day or two after his visit to the lawyer at Barchester.", "이 일은 그가 바체스터의 변호사를 찾아간 지 하루 이틀 뒤에 있었다."]], [["—and would rejoice greatly if I thought that I could save you from any further annoyance about them.", "— 그리고 그 일로 당신이 더 이상 괴롭힘을 겪지 않도록 내가 막아 줄 수 있다고 여겨진다면 나는 크게 기뻐할 것입니다."], ["That kite, Tom Tozer, has just been with me, and insists that both of them shall be paid.", "그 솔개 같은 톰 토저가 방금 나에게 와서 두 장 다 갚아야 한다고 우기고 있어요."], ["He knows—no one better—that no consideration was given for the latter.", "나중 것에는 아무 대가도 주어지지 않았다는 것을 그는 누구보다도 잘 알고 있습니다."], ["But he knows also that the dealing was not with him, nor even with his brother, and he will be prepared to swear that he gave value for both.", "그러나 그 거래가 자기와 된 것이 아니고 자기 형과 된 것조차 아니라는 것 역시 알고 있으니, 그는 두 장 모두에 값을 치렀다고 맹세할 준비가 되어 있을 겁니다."], ["He would swear anything for five hundred pounds—or for half the money, for that matter.", "오백 파운드를 준다면 그는 무엇이든 맹세할 사람이고, 그 절반이라도 그 점은 마찬가지예요."], ["I do not think that the father of mischief ever let loose upon the world a greater rascal than Tom Tozer.", "온갖 악의 아비가 톰 토저보다 더한 악당을 세상에 풀어놓은 적은 없다고 나는 생각합니다."]], [["He declares that nothing shall induce him to take one shilling less than the whole sum of nine hundred pounds.", "그는 구백 파운드 전액에서 단 한 실링이라도 덜 받게 만들 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없다고 못 박고 있습니다."], ["He has been brought to this by hearing that my debts are about to be paid.", "내 빚이 곧 갚아진다는 말을 들은 탓에 이렇게 나온 것이지요."], ["Heaven help me!", "하느님이 나를 도우시기를!"], ["The meaning of that is that these wretched acres, which are now mortgaged to one millionnaire, are to change hands and be mortgaged to another instead.", "그 말의 뜻은, 지금 어느 백만장자에게 저당 잡혀 있는 이 딱한 땅뙈기가 임자를 바꾸어 그 대신 다른 백만장자에게 저당 잡히게 된다는 것뿐입니다."], ["By this exchange I may possibly obtain the benefit of having a house to live in for the next twelve months, but no other.", "이 바꿈으로 내가 얻을 이익이라면 앞으로 열두 달 동안 살 집이 있다는 것뿐이고, 그 밖에는 아무것도 없어요."], ["Tozer, however, is altogether wrong in his scent; and the worst of it is that his malice will fall on you rather than on me.", "그러나 토저는 냄새를 아주 잘못 짚었습니다. 그리고 가장 나쁜 것은 그 앙심이 나에게보다 당신에게 떨어질 것이라는 점입니다."]], [["What I want you to do is this: let us pay him one hundred pounds between us.", "내가 당신에게 바라는 것은 이것입니다. 우리 둘이 나누어 그에게 백 파운드를 갚읍시다."], ["Though I sell the last sorry jade of a horse I have, I will make up fifty; and I know you can, at any rate, do as much as that.", "나에게 남은 마지막 비루먹은 말을 팔더라도 나는 오십을 채우겠습니다. 그리고 당신도 적어도 그만큼은 할 수 있다는 것을 나는 알아요."], ["Then do you accept a bill, conjointly with me, for eight hundred.", "그러고 나서 당신이 나와 함께 팔백 파운드 어음 한 장을 인수해 주세요."], ["It shall be done in Forrest’s presence, and handed to him; and you shall receive back the two old bills into your own hands at the same time.", "그것은 포레스트가 보는 자리에서 하여 그의 손에 넘길 것이고, 당신은 그와 동시에 낡은 어음 두 장을 당신 손에 되돌려 받을 것입니다."], ["This new bill should be timed to run ninety days; and I will move heaven and earth during that time to have it included in the general schedule of my debts which are to be secured on the Chaldicotes property.", "이 새 어음은 구십 일 기한으로 잡아야 해요. 그리고 그 동안에 나는 하늘과 땅을 움직여, 콜디코츠 땅에 담보로 잡히게 될 내 빚의 전체 목록에 그것을 넣도록 하겠습니다."]], [["The meaning of which was that Miss Dunstable was to be cozened into paying the money under an idea that it was part of the sum covered by the existing mortgage.", "그 말의 뜻은, 그 돈이 지금 있는 저당에 들어 있는 액수의 일부라고 여기게 하여 던스터블 양을 속여 그것을 내게 하겠다는 것이었다."]], [["What you said the other day at Barchester, as to never executing another bill, is very well as regards future transactions.", "요전 날 바체스터에서 다시는 어음을 작성하지 않겠다고 당신이 한 말은, 앞으로의 거래에 관해서라면 아주 좋습니다."], ["Nothing can be wiser than such a resolution.", "그런 결심보다 더 지혜로운 것은 없지요."], ["But it would be folly—worse than folly—if you were to allow your furniture to be seized when the means of preventing it are so ready to your hand.", "그러나 그것을 막을 방편이 당신 손에 그토록 가까이 있는데도 당신 가구가 압류되도록 두는 것은 어리석은 일 — 어리석음보다 못한 일일 겁니다."], ["By leaving the new bill in Forrest’s hands you may be sure that you are safe from the claws of such birds of prey as these Tozers.", "새 어음을 포레스트의 손에 남겨 두면 이 토저네 같은 맹금들의 발톱에서 당신이 안전하다는 것을 확신할 수 있어요."], ["Even if I cannot get it settled when the three months are over, Forrest will enable you to make any arrangement that may be most convenient.", "석 달이 지날 때까지 내가 그것을 마무리하지 못한다 해도, 포레스트는 당신에게 가장 편한 어떤 조정이라도 할 수 있게 해 줄 것입니다."]], [["For Heaven’s sake, my dear fellow, do not refuse this.", "부디, 이 사람아, 이것을 거절하지 마세요."], ["You can hardly conceive how it weighs upon me, this fear that bailiffs should make their way into your wife’s drawing-room.", "집행관들이 당신 아내의 응접실까지 들어갈지 모른다는 이 두려움이 나를 어떻게 짓누르는지 당신은 좀처럼 헤아리지 못할 겁니다."], ["I know you think ill of me, and I do not wonder at it.", "당신이 나를 나쁘게 여기는 것을 나도 알고, 그것을 이상하게 여기지도 않아요."], ["But you would be less inclined to do so if you knew how terribly I am punished.", "그러나 내가 얼마나 무섭게 벌을 받고 있는지 안다면 그런 마음이 덜해질 것입니다."], ["Pray let me hear that you will do as I counsel you.", "내가 권하는 대로 하겠다는 말을 부디 들려주세요."]], [["Yours always faithfully,", "언제나 충실한 당신의 벗,"]], [["In answer to which the parson wrote a very short reply:—", "이에 대한 답으로 그 목사는 아주 짧은 답장을 썼다."]], [["Framley, July, 185—.", "프램리, 185─년 7월."]], [["I will sign no more bills on any consideration.", "나는 어떤 이유를 들어도 더는 어음에 내 이름을 적지 않겠습니다."]], [["Yours truly,", "그럼 이만,"]], [["And then having written this, and having shown it to his wife, he returned to the shrubbery walk and paced it up and down, looking every now and then to Sowerby’s letter as he thought over all the past circumstances of his friendship with that gentleman.", "그러고 나서 이것을 써서 아내에게 보인 뒤에, 그는 다시 관목 산책길로 돌아가 그 길을 오르내리며, 그 신사와 벗하여 온 지난 사정들을 두루 되짚어 보면서 이따금 사워비의 편지를 들여다보았다."]], [["That the man who had written this letter should be his friend—that very fact was a disgrace to him.", "이 편지를 쓴 사람이 제 벗이라니 — 바로 그 사실이 그에게는 부끄러움이었다."], ["Sowerby so well knew himself and his own reputation, that he did not dare to suppose that his own word would be taken for anything,—not even when the thing promised was an act of the commonest honesty.", "사워비는 제 자신과 제 소문을 어찌나 잘 알고 있었던지, 제 말이 무엇으로든 받아들여질 것이라고는 감히 짐작조차 하지 못했다. 약속한 것이 가장 흔해빠진 정직에 속하는 일일 때조차 그러했다."], ["“The old bills shall be given back into your own hands,”", "“낡은 어음들은 당신 손에 되돌려 줄 것입니다.”"], ["he had declared with energy, knowing that his friend and correspondent would not feel himself secure against further fraud under any less stringent guarantee.", "그는 힘을 주어 그렇게 못 박아 두었으니, 그만큼 단단한 보증이 아니면 제 벗이요 편지 상대인 사람이 더 이상의 속임수에서 스스로 안전하다고 느끼지 못할 것을 알았기 때문이다."], ["This gentleman, this county member, the owner of Chaldicotes, with whom Mark Robarts had been so anxious to be on terms of intimacy, had now come to such a phase of life that he had given over speaking of himself as an honest man.", "마크 로바츠가 그토록 가까이 사귀고 싶어 했던 이 신사, 이 주 선출 의원, 콜디코츠의 주인은, 이제 자기 자신을 정직한 사람이라고 말하는 일을 아예 그만두어 버린 그런 삶의 국면에까지 이르러 있었다."], ["He had become so used to suspicion that he argued of it as of a thing of course.", "그는 의심받는 일에 어찌나 익숙해졌던지 그것을 으레 있는 일처럼 놓고 이야기했다."], ["He knew that no one could trust either his spoken or his written word, and he was content to speak and to write without attempt to hide this conviction.", "입으로 한 말이든 글로 쓴 말이든 아무도 자기를 믿을 수 없다는 것을 그는 알았고, 이 확신을 감추려는 시도조차 없이 말하고 쓰는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["And this was the man whom he had been so glad to call his friend; for whose sake he had been willing to quarrel with Lady Lufton, and at whose instance he had unconsciously abandoned so many of the best resolutions of his life.", "그런데 이 사람이 바로 그가 제 벗이라고 부르기를 그토록 기뻐했던 사람이었다. 이 사람을 위해서 그는 레이디 러프턴과 다툴 마음까지 있었고, 이 사람의 권함에 따라 제 평생의 가장 좋은 결심들을 그토록 여러 개나 알지 못하는 사이에 버렸다."], ["He looked back now, as he walked there slowly, still holding the letter in his hand, to the day when he had stopped at the school-house and written his letter to Mr. Sowerby, promising to join the party at Chaldicotes.", "이제 그는 편지를 여전히 손에 든 채 그곳을 천천히 걸으면서, 학교 집에 들러 콜디코츠의 모임에 가겠다고 약속하는 편지를 사워비 씨에게 써 보냈던 그날을 되돌아보았다."], ["He had been so eager then to have his own way, that he would not permit himself to go home and talk the matter over with his wife.", "그때 그는 제 뜻대로 하고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 급했던지, 집에 돌아가 아내와 그 일을 놓고 의논하는 것조차 스스로에게 허락하지 않았다."], ["He thought also of the manner in which he had been tempted to the house of the Duke of Omnium, and the conviction on his mind at the time that his giving way to that temptation would surely bring him to evil.", "그는 또 자기가 옴니엄 공작의 집으로 꾀여 들어간 그 방식을, 그리고 그 꾐에 넘어가면 반드시 화를 입게 된다고 그때 마음에 품었던 확신을 생각했다. 그러고 나서 그는 사워비의 침실에서의 그 저녁을 떠올렸다."], ["And then he remembered the evening in Sowerby’s bedroom, when the bill had been brought out, and he had allowed himself to be persuaded to put his name upon it;—not because he was willing in this way to assist his friend, but because he was unable to refuse.", "어음이 꺼내어졌고, 그 위에 이름을 적으라는 설득에 넘어가도록 스스로를 내버려 둔 그 저녁을. 이런 식으로 벗을 돕고 싶은 마음이 있어서가 아니라, 거절할 수가 없어서였다."], ["He had lacked the courage to say, “No,”", "그는 “아니오”라고 말할 용기가 없었다."], ["though he knew at the time how gross was the error which he was committing.", "그때 자기가 저지르고 있는 잘못이 얼마나 큰지 알고 있었는데도."], ["He had lacked the courage to say, “No,” and hence had come upon him and on his household all this misery and cause for bitter repentance.", "그는 “아니오”라고 말할 용기가 없었고, 그리하여 이 모든 참담함과 쓰라린 뉘우침의 까닭이 그와 그의 집안에 닥친 것이었다."]], [["I have written much of clergymen, but in doing so I have endeavoured to portray them as they bear on our social life rather than to describe the mode and working of their professional careers.", "나는 성직자들에 관하여 많이 써 왔으나, 그렇게 하면서 그들의 직업상 행로가 어떤 방식으로 어떻게 굴러가는지를 그리기보다는 그들이 우리 사회 생활에 어떻게 걸려 있는지를 그려 보려고 애썼다."], ["Had I done the latter I could hardly have steered clear of subjects on which it has not been my intention to pronounce an opinion, and I should either have laden my fiction with sermons or I should have degraded my sermons into fiction.", "뒤의 것을 했더라면 나는 의견을 밝힐 뜻이 없었던 주제들을 좀처럼 피해 갈 수 없었을 것이고, 내 소설에 설교를 잔뜩 실었거나 아니면 내 설교를 소설로 떨어뜨려 놓았을 것이다."], ["Therefore I have said but little in my narrative of this man’s feelings or doings as a clergyman.", "그러므로 나는 이 사람이 성직자로서 느끼고 행한 것에 관하여 내 이야기 속에서 거의 말하지 않았다."]], [["But I must protest against its being on this account considered that Mr. Robarts was indifferent to the duties of his clerical position.", "그러나 그 때문에 로바츠 씨가 제 성직의 자리에 딸린 의무에 무심했다고 여겨지는 것에는 나도 항변하지 않을 수 없다."], ["He had been fond of pleasure and had given way to temptation,—as is so customarily done by young men of six-and-twenty, who are placed beyond control and who have means at command.", "그는 즐거움을 좋아했고 꾐에 넘어갔으니, 단속하는 손이 미치지 않는 자리에 놓이고 마음대로 쓸 재물을 가진 스물여섯의 젊은이들이 흔히 그러하는 것과 같았다."], ["Had he remained as a curate till that age, subject in all his movements to the eye of a superior, he would, we may say, have put his name to no bills, have ridden after no hounds, have seen nothing of the iniquities of Gatherum Castle.", "그 나이까지 부목사로 남아 움직임마다 윗사람의 눈 아래에 있었더라면, 우리는 이렇게 말할 수 있으리라, 그는 어떤 어음에도 이름을 적지 않았을 것이고, 사냥개를 따라 말을 달리지도 않았을 것이고, 개서럼 성의 죄악을 하나도 보지 않았을 것이다."], ["There are men of twenty-six as fit to stand alone as ever they will be—fit to be prime ministers, heads of schools, judges on the bench—almost fit to be bishops; but Mark Robarts had not been one of them.", "스물여섯에 이미 앞으로도 더는 나아지지 않을 만큼 홀로 설 만한 사람들이 있다 — 총리도, 학교의 교장도, 법관석의 판사도 될 만한, 거의 주교가 될 만한 사람들이. 그러나 마크 로바츠는 그런 사람들 가운데 하나가 아니었다."], ["He had within him many aptitudes for good, but not the strengthened courage of a man to act up to them.", "그의 안에는 좋은 것을 향한 소질이 많이 있었으나, 그것에 걸맞게 행할 사내의 다져진 용기는 없었다."], ["The stuff of which his manhood was to be formed had been slow of growth, as it is with many men; and, consequently, when temptation was offered to him, he had fallen.", "그의 사내다움을 이룰 재목은 자라는 것이 느렸으니, 많은 사람이 그러한 대로였다. 그리하여 꾐이 그에게 내밀어졌을 때 그는 넘어졌다."]], [["But he deeply grieved over his own stumbling, and from time to time, as his periods of penitence came upon him, he resolved that he would once more put his shoulder to the wheel as became one who fights upon earth that battle for which he had put on his armour.", "그러나 그는 제 넘어짐을 깊이 슬퍼했고, 뉘우침의 시기가 이따금 그에게 찾아올 때마다, 갑주를 입고 나선 그 싸움을 땅에서 싸우는 사람에게 걸맞게 다시 한번 수레바퀴에 어깨를 대겠다고 마음을 굳혔다. 그는 크롤리 씨의 그 말을 몇 번이고 되풀이해 생각했고, 이제 사워비 씨의 편지를 손안에서 구기며 그 길을 오르내리는 동안 다시 그것을 생각했다 — “이것은 무서운 타락입니다."], ["Over and over again did he think of those words of Mr.", "떨어지는 것이 무섭고, 돌아오기가 그토록 어려우니 곱절로 무섭습니다.”"], ["Crawley, and now as he walked up and down the path, crumpling Mr.", "그렇다. 사람이 그 길을 즐거이 달려 내려가는 동안 — 어디를 향하여?"], ["Sowerby’s letter in his hand, he thought of them again—“It is a terrible falling off; terrible in the fall, but doubly terrible through that difficulty of returning.”", "— 무섭게 불어나는 비율로 스스로를 곱해 가는 어려움이 바로 그것이다."], ["Yes; that is a difficulty which multiplies itself in a fearful ratio as one goes on pleasantly running down the path—whitherward?", "그는 이제 돌아갈 수 없는 데까지 와 버린 것인가 — 제 교구의 목자로서 편안한 양심으로 다시는 머리를 들 수 없는 데까지!"], ["Had it come to that with him that he could not return—that he could never again hold up his head with a safe conscience as the pastor of his parish!", "그를 이 참담함으로 이끈 것은 사워비였고, 그에게 이 파멸을 가져온 것도 사워비였던가? 그러나 그렇다면 사워비가 그에게 값을 치르지 않았던가?"], ["It was Sowerby who had led him into this misery, who had brought on him this ruin? But then had not Sowerby paid him?", "그가 지금 바체스터에서 지니고 있는 저 참사회원 자리가 사워비의 선물이 아니었던가?"], ["Had not that stall which he now held in Barchester been Sowerby’s gift?", "그는 이제 가난한 사람이었다 — 곤궁하고 가난에 짓눌린 사람이었다."], ["He was a poor man now—a distressed, poverty-stricken man; but nevertheless he wished with all his heart that he had never become a sharer in the good things of the Barchester chapter.", "그러면서도 그는 바체스터 성당 참사회의 좋은 것들을 나누어 받는 사람이 애초에 되지 않았더라면 하고 온 마음으로 바랐다."]], [["“I shall resign the stall,”", "“그 참사회원 자리를 내놓겠어.”"], ["he said to his wife that night.", "그날 밤 그가 아내에게 말했다."], ["“I think I may say that I have made up my mind as to that.”", "“그것만은 마음을 정했다고 말할 수 있을 것 같아.”"]], [["“But, Mark, will not people say that it is odd?”", "“하지만, 마크, 사람들이 그것을 이상하다고 하지 않겠어요?”"]], [["“I cannot help it—they must say it.", "“어쩔 수 없어 — 사람들이야 그렇게 말하겠지."], ["Fanny, I fear that we shall have to bear the saying of harder words than that.”", "패니, 우리는 그보다 더한 말도 들어야 하게 될까 걱정이야.”"]], [["“Nobody can ever say that you have done anything that is unjust or dishonourable.", "“당신이 옳지 않거나 떳떳하지 못한 일을 했다고는 아무도 말할 수 없어요."], ["If there are such men as Mr. Sowerby—”", "사워비 씨 같은 사람들이 있는 것이라면 —”"]], [["“The blackness of his fault will not excuse mine.”", "“그의 잘못이 검다고 해서 내 잘못이 용서되지는 않아.”"], ["And then again he sat silent, hiding his eyes, while his wife, sitting by him, held his hand.", "그러고 나서 그는 다시 잠자코 앉아 눈을 가렸고, 그의 옆에 앉은 아내는 그의 손을 잡고 있었다."]], [["“Don’t make yourself wretched, Mark.", "“당신을 참담하게 만들지 말아요, 마크."], ["Matters will all come right yet.", "일은 아직 다 잘될 거예요."], ["It cannot be that the loss of a few hundred pounds should ruin you.”", "몇백 파운드를 잃는다고 당신이 망할 수는 없어요.”"]], [["“It is not the money—it is not the money!”", "“돈이 문제가 아니야 — 돈이 문제가 아니야!”"]], [["“But you have done nothing wrong, Mark.”", "“하지만 당신은 잘못한 것이 없어요, 마크.”"]], [["“How am I to go into the church, and take my place before them all, when every one will know that bailiffs are in the house?”", "“집에 집행관이 들어와 있다는 것을 사람마다 다 알고 있는데, 내가 어떻게 교회에 들어가 그들 모두 앞에서 내 자리에 설 수 있겠어?”"], ["And then, dropping his head on to the table, he sobbed aloud.", "그러고 나서 그는 탁자에 머리를 떨구고 소리 내어 흐느꼈다."]], [["Mark Robarts’ mistake had been mainly this,—he had thought to touch pitch and not to be defiled.", "마크 로바츠의 잘못은 주로 이것이었다. 그는 역청을 만지고도 더러워지지 않으리라고 생각했던 것이다."], ["He, looking out from his pleasant parsonage into the pleasant upper ranks of the world around him, had seen that men and things in those quarters were very engaging.", "그는 제 즐거운 목사관에서 제 둘레의 즐거운 상류 계층을 내다보며, 그쪽의 사람과 사물이 아주 마음을 끄는 것을 보았다."], ["His own parsonage, with his sweet wife, were exceedingly dear to him, and Lady Lufton’s affectionate friendship had its value; but were not these things rather dull for one who had lived in the best sets at Harrow and Oxford;—unless, indeed, he could supplement them with some occasional bursts of more lively life?", "다정한 아내가 있는 제 목사관이 그에게 지극히 소중했고, 레이디 러프턴의 애정 어린 벗함에도 그 나름의 값이 있었다. 그러나 해로와 옥스퍼드에서 가장 좋은 패에 끼어 살아 본 사람에게 이런 것들은 좀 심심하지 않았겠는가 — 더 활기찬 삶을 이따금 한바탕씩 곁들여 보탤 수 있는 것이 아니라면?"], ["Cakes and ale were as pleasant to his palate as to the palates of those with whom he had formerly lived at college.", "케이크와 맥주는 전에 대학에서 함께 살던 사람들의 입에 즐거웠던 만큼 그의 입에도 즐거웠다."], ["He had the same eye to look at a horse, and the same heart to make him go across a country, as they.", "말을 보는 눈도 그들과 같았고, 그 말을 몰아 들판을 가로지르게 하는 담도 그들과 같았다."], ["And then, too, he found that men liked him,—men and women also; men and women who were high in worldly standing.", "게다가 그는 사람들이 자기를 좋아한다는 것을 알게 되었다 — 남자들도, 여자들도, 그것도 세상의 지위가 높은 남자와 여자들이."], ["His ass’s ears were tickled, and he learned to fancy that he was intended by nature for the society of high people.", "그의 나귀 귀는 간지럼을 탔고, 그는 자기가 본디 높은 사람들의 자리에 어울리도록 태어났다고 여기는 버릇을 익혔다."], ["It seemed as though he were following his appointed course in meeting men and women of the world at the houses of the fashionable and the rich.", "유행을 아는 사람들과 부자들의 집에서 세상의 남자와 여자들을 만나는 것이 마치 자기에게 정해진 길을 따라가는 것처럼 보였다."], ["He was not the first clergyman that had so lived and had so prospered.", "그렇게 살아서 그렇게 잘된 성직자가 그가 처음은 아니었다."], ["Yes, clergymen had so lived, and had done their duties in their sphere of life altogether to the satisfaction of their countrymen—and of their sovereigns.", "그렇다, 성직자들이 그렇게 살아 왔고, 제 삶의 자리에서 제 나라 사람들의 — 그리고 제 임금들의 — 만족에 온전히 들어맞도록 제 의무를 다해 왔다."], ["Thus Mark Robarts had determined that he would touch pitch, and escape defilement if that were possible.", "그리하여 마크 로바츠는 역청을 만지되 될 수만 있으면 더러워지는 것은 면하겠다고 마음을 굳혔던 것이다."], ["With what result those who have read so far will have perceived.", "그 결과가 어떠했는지는 여기까지 읽어 온 이들이 알아보았을 것이다."]], [["Late on the following afternoon who should drive up to the parsonage door but Mr. Forrest, the bank manager from Barchester—Mr.", "이튿날 늦은 오후에 목사관 문 앞으로 마차를 몰고 온 사람이 다른 누구도 아니고 바체스터의 은행 지배인 포레스트 씨였다 — 사워비가 늘 「데우스 엑스 마키나」로 가리켜 온 그 포레스트 씨, 마땅한 절차로 불러 대기만 하면 그들 모두를 지금의 곤경에서 건져 내고 토저 집안 전부를 물려 보낼 수 있는 사람이었다."], ["Forrest, to whom Sowerby had always pointed as the Deus ex machinâ who, if duly invoked, could relieve them all from their present troubles, and dismiss the whole Tozer family—not howling into the wilderness, as one would have wished to do with that brood of Tozers, but so gorged with prey that from them no further annoyance need be dreaded?", "그 토저 족속을 두고서라면 누구나 그러고 싶었을 대로 울부짖으며 광야로 내쫓는 것이 아니라, 먹이로 배를 그토록 잔뜩 불려 그들에게서 더 이상의 괴롭힘을 두려워할 것이 없게 만드는 식으로 말이다. 이 모든 것을 포레스트 씨는 할 수 있었다."], ["All this Mr. Forrest could do; nay, more, most willingly would do!", "아니, 그보다 더한 것도 아주 기꺼이 하려 했다!"], ["Only let Mark Robarts put himself into the banker’s hand, and blandly sign what documents the banker might desire.", "다만 마크 로바츠가 그 은행가의 손에 스스로를 맡기고, 그 은행가가 바라는 서류에 순순히 이름을 적기만 하면 되었다."]], [["“This is a very unpleasant affair,” said Mr.", "“이것은 아주 불쾌한 일입니다.”"], ["Forrest as soon as they were closeted together in Mark’s book-room.", "마크의 서재에 둘만 들어앉자마자 포레스트 씨가 말했다."], ["In answer to which observation the parson acknowledged that it was a very unpleasant affair.", "이 말에 대한 답으로 그 목사는 그것이 아주 불쾌한 일임을 시인했다."]], [["“Mr. Sowerby has managed to put you into the hands of about the worst set of rogues now existing, in their line of business, in London.”", "“사워비 씨가 손을 써 놓은 덕에 당신은 지금 런던에서 그 장사를 하는 자들 가운데 거의 가장 악질인 악당 패의 손에 들어가 있습니다.”"]], [["“So I supposed; Curling told me the same.”", "“그런 줄 짐작했습니다. 컬링도 같은 말을 했습니다.”"], ["Curling was the Barchester attorney whose aid he had lately invoked.", "컬링은 그가 요즈음 도움을 청한 바체스터의 변호사였다."]], [["“Curling has threatened them that he will expose their whole trade; but one of them who was down here, a man named Tozer, replied, that you had much more to lose by exposure than he had.", "“컬링이 그들의 장사를 전부 폭로하겠다고 위협했습니다. 그런데 이 아래까지 내려와 있던 그들 가운데 한 사람, 토저라는 사람이, 폭로로 잃을 것은 자기보다 당신이 훨씬 많다고 되받았어요."], ["He went further and declared that he would defy any jury in England to refuse him his money.", "그는 더 나아가서, 영국의 어떤 배심이라도 자기 돈을 주지 않을 수 있으면 어디 한번 해 보라고까지 큰소리쳤습니다."], ["He swore that he discounted both bills in the regular way of business; and, though this is of course false, I fear that it will be impossible to prove it so.", "두 어음을 다 여느 장사 방식대로 할인했다고 그는 맹세했어요. 물론 이것은 거짓이지만, 그것이 거짓임을 밝히는 일은 불가능하지 않을까 걱정입니다."], ["He well knows that you are a clergyman, and that, therefore, he has a stronger hold on you than on other men.”", "당신이 성직자라는 것을, 그러니까 다른 사람들보다 당신을 더 세게 붙들 수 있다는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있습니다.”"]], [["“The disgrace shall fall on Sowerby,”", "“그 부끄러움은 사워비에게 떨어질 것입니다.”"], ["said Robarts, hardly actuated at the moment by any strong feeling of Christian forgiveness.", "그 순간 그리스도인의 용서라는 강한 감정에 움직여진 것이라고는 좀처럼 할 수 없는 태도로 로바츠가 말했다."]], [["“I fear, Mr. Robarts, that he is somewhat in the condition of the Tozers.", "“걱정스럽지만, 로바츠 씨, 그는 얼마쯤 토저네와 같은 처지에 있습니다."], ["He will not feel it as you will do.”", "그는 당신처럼 그것을 느끼지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“I must bear it, Mr. Forrest, as best I may.”", "“나는 그것을 견뎌야 하겠지요, 포레스트 씨, 되는 대로 견뎌 내야지요.”"]], [["“Will you allow me, Mr. Robarts, to give you my advice?", "“로바츠 씨, 내 의견을 하나 말씀드려도 되겠습니까?"], ["Perhaps I ought to apologize for intruding it upon you; but as the bills have been presented and dishonoured across my counter, I have, of necessity, become acquainted with the circumstances.”", "이런 것을 들이대는 것을 사과해야 할지도 모르겠어요. 그러나 그 어음들이 내 창구에서 제시되고 부도가 났으니, 나는 어쩔 수 없이 그 사정을 알게 되었습니다.”"]], [["“I am sure I am very much obliged to you,”", "“참으로 대단히 고맙습니다.”"], ["said Mark.", "마크가 말했다."]], [["“You must pay this money, or, at any rate, the most considerable portion of it;—the whole of it, indeed, with such deduction as a lawyer may be able to induce these hawks to make on the sight of the ready money. Perhaps £750 or £800 may see you clear of the whole affair.”", "“당신은 이 돈을 갚아야 합니다. 아니면 적어도 그 가운데 가장 큰 몫을요. 실은 전액을 갚아야 하지요. 다만 변호사가 당장 쥘 돈을 눈앞에 내보여 이 매들에게서 얼마쯤 깎아 낼 수 있다면 그만큼은 덜고요. 750파운드나 800파운드쯤이면 이 일 전부에서 당신을 벗어나게 해 줄지도 모릅니다.”"]], [["“But I have not a quarter of that sum lying by me.”", "“그러나 나에게는 그 액수의 사분의 일도 두고 있는 것이 없습니다.”"]], [["“No, I suppose not; but what I would recommend is this: that you should borrow the money from the bank, on your own responsibility,—with the joint security of some friend who may be willing to assist you with his name.", "“그렇겠지요, 없으실 겁니다. 그러나 내가 권하고 싶은 것은 이것입니다."], ["Lord Lufton probably would do it.”", "당신 자신의 책임으로 은행에서 그 돈을 빌리는 것이지요 — 자기 이름으로 당신을 도와줄 마음이 있는 어떤 벗의 연대 담보를 붙여서요. 러프턴 경이라면 아마 해 주실 겁니다.”"]], [["“No, Mr. Forrest—”", "“아니요, 포레스트 씨 —”"]], [["“Listen to me first, before you make up your mind.", "“마음을 정하기 전에 우선 내 말을 들어 보세요."], ["If you took this step, of course you would do so with the fixed intention of paying the money yourself,—without any further reliance on Sowerby or on any one else.”", "이 걸음을 밟으신다면 당신은 물론 그 돈을 당신 스스로 갚겠다는 굳은 뜻으로 그러시는 것이겠지요 — 사워비에게든 다른 누구에게든 더는 기대지 않고요.”"]], [["“I shall not rely on Mr.", "“나는 다시는 사워비 씨에게 기대지 않겠습니다."], ["Sowerby again; you may be sure of that.”", "그것은 믿으셔도 됩니다.”"]], [["“What I mean is that you must teach yourself to recognize the debt as your own.", "“내 말은 그 빚을 당신 자신의 빚으로 여기도록 스스로를 가르쳐야 한다는 것입니다."], ["If you can do that, with your income you can surely pay it, with interest, in two years.", "그렇게 할 수 있다면 당신 수입으로 이자까지 얹어 이 년 안에 틀림없이 갚을 수 있어요."], ["If Lord Lufton will assist you with his name I will so arrange the bills that the payments shall be made to fall equally over that period.", "러프턴 경이 자기 이름으로 도와주신다면, 나는 갚을 돈이 그 기간에 걸쳐 고르게 떨어지도록 어음들을 짜 놓겠습니다."], ["In that way the world will know nothing about it, and in two years’ time you will once more be a free man.", "그러면 세상은 이 일을 아무것도 모를 것이고, 이 년이 지나면 당신은 다시 자유로운 사람이 됩니다."], ["Many men, Mr. Robarts, have bought their experience much dearer than that, I can assure you.”", "로바츠 씨, 그보다 훨씬 비싼 값으로 경험을 사들인 사람이 많다는 것은 내가 보증할 수 있어요.”"]], [["“Mr. Forrest, it is quite out of the question.”", "“포레스트 씨, 그것은 도무지 말이 되지 않습니다.”"]], [["“You mean that Lord Lufton will not give you his name.”", "“러프턴 경이 자기 이름을 내주지 않을 것이라는 말씀이군요.”"]], [["“I certainly shall not ask him; but that is not all.", "“내가 그에게 청하지 않을 것은 분명합니다. 그러나 그것만이 아닙니다."], ["In the first place my income will not be what you think it, for I shall probably give up the prebend at Barchester.”", "우선 내 수입이 당신이 생각하는 그것이 되지 못할 겁니다. 나는 아마 바체스터의 참사회원직을 내놓을 테니까요.”"]], [["“Give up the prebend!", "“참사회원직을 내놓다니요!"], ["give up six hundred a year!”", "연 육백 파운드를 내놓다니요!”"]], [["“And, beyond this, I think I may say that nothing shall tempt me to put my name to another bill.", "“그리고 그것 말고도, 무엇으로 꾀어도 나는 다른 어음에 내 이름을 적지 않겠다고 말할 수 있을 것 같습니다."], ["I have learned a lesson which I hope I may never forget.”", "나는 결코 잊지 않기를 바라는 교훈을 하나 배웠습니다.”"]], [["“Then what do you intend to do?”", "“그러면 어떻게 하실 생각입니까?”"]], [["“Nothing!”", "“아무것도 하지 않겠습니다!”"]], [["“Then those men will sell every stick of furniture about the place.", "“그러면 그 사람들이 이 집의 가구를 하나도 남기지 않고 팔아 버릴 겁니다."], ["They know that your property here is enough to secure all that they claim.”", "여기 있는 당신 재산이 그들이 청구하는 것 전부를 담보하기에 넉넉하다는 것을 그들은 알고 있어요.”"]], [["“If they have the power, they must sell it.”", "“그럴 힘이 있다면 팔아야 하겠지요.”"]], [["“And all the world will know the facts.”", "“그리고 온 세상이 그 사실을 알게 될 겁니다.”"]], [["“So it must be.", "“그렇게 되어야 하겠지요."], ["Of the faults which a man commits he must bear the punishment.", "사람이 저지른 잘못은 그 벌을 제가 져야 합니다."], ["If it were only myself!”", "나 하나뿐이라면 좋을 것을!”"]], [["“That’s where it is, Mr. Robarts.", "“바로 그것이 문제입니다, 로바츠 씨."], ["Think what your wife will have to suffer in going through such misery as that!", "그런 참담함을 겪는 동안 당신 아내가 무엇을 견뎌야 할지 생각해 보세요!"], ["You had better take my advice.", "내 의견을 따르시는 게 좋습니다."], ["Lord Lufton, I am sure—”", "러프턴 경이라면 틀림없이 —”"]], [["But the very name of Lord Lufton, his sister’s lover, again gave him courage.", "그러나 러프턴 경이라는, 제 누이의 연인이라는 바로 그 이름이 다시 그에게 용기를 주었다."], ["He thought, too, of the accusations which Lord Lufton had brought against him on that night when he had come to him in the coffee-room of the hotel, and he felt that it was impossible that he should apply to him for such aid.", "그는 또 호텔 커피실로 자기를 찾아왔던 그날 밤에 러프턴 경이 자기에게 들이댔던 비난들을 생각했고, 그런 도움을 그에게 청한다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이라고 느꼈다."], ["It would be better to tell all to Lady Lufton!", "차라리 레이디 러프턴에게 모든 것을 말하는 편이 나을 것이었다!"], ["That she would relieve him, let the cost to herself be what it might, he was very sure.", "그녀 자신이 치를 값이 무엇이 되든 그녀가 자기를 건져 주리라는 것은 그가 아주 확실히 알고 있었다."], ["Only this;—that in looking to her for assistance he would be forced to bite the dust in very deed.", "다만 이것뿐이었다. 도움을 얻으려고 그녀에게 눈을 돌린다면 그는 참으로 흙을 씹지 않을 수 없다는 것."]], [["“Thank you, Mr.", "“고맙습니다, 포레스트 씨."], ["Forrest, but I have made up my mind.", "그러나 나는 마음을 정했습니다."], ["Do not think that I am the less obliged to you for your disinterested kindness,—for I know that it is disinterested; but this I think I may confidently say, that not even to avert so terrible a calamity will I again put my name to any bill.", "사심 없는 친절을 베풀어 주신 것에 내가 덜 고마워한다고는 생각하지 마세요 — 그것이 사심 없는 것임을 나는 알고 있으니까요. 그러나 이것만은 자신 있게 말할 수 있을 것 같습니다."], ["Even if you could take my own promise to pay without the addition of any second name, I would not do it.”", "그토록 무서운 재앙을 막기 위해서라 해도 나는 다시 어떤 어음에도 내 이름을 적지 않겠습니다. 두 번째 이름을 덧붙이지 않고 갚겠다는 내 약속만으로 받아 주실 수 있다 해도 나는 그러지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["There was nothing for Mr. Forrest to do under such circumstances but simply to drive back to Barchester.", "이런 형편에서 포레스트 씨가 할 수 있는 일이라고는 그저 마차를 몰아 바체스터로 돌아가는 것밖에 없었다."], ["He had done the best for the young clergyman according to his lights, and perhaps, in a worldly view, his advice had not been bad.", "그는 제 아는 바에 따라 그 젊은 성직자를 위해 할 수 있는 최선을 했고, 어쩌면 세상의 눈으로 보면 그의 의견이 나쁘지 않았다."], ["But Mark dreaded the very name of a bill.", "그러나 마크는 어음이라는 이름 자체를 두려워했다."], ["He was as a dog that had been terribly scorched, and nothing should again induce him to go near the fire.", "그는 무섭게 그슬린 개와 같았으니, 무엇으로도 그를 다시 불 가까이 가게 할 수는 없었다."]], [["“Was not that the man from the bank?”", "“방금 온 이가 그 은행에서 온 사람 아니었어요?”"], ["said Fanny, coming into the room when the sound of the wheels had died away.", "바퀴 소리가 멀어져 사라졌을 때 방으로 들어오며 패니가 말했다."]], [["“Yes; Mr. Forrest.”", "“그래, 포레스트 씨야.”"]], [["“Well, dearest?”", "“그래서요, 여보?”"]], [["“We must prepare ourselves for the worst.”", "“우리는 최악을 맞을 준비를 해야 해.”"]], [["“You will not sign any more papers, eh, Mark?”", "“당신은 이제 어떤 서류에도 이름을 적지 않을 거지요, 그렇죠, 마크?”"]], [["“No; I have just now positively refused to do so.”", "“그래, 방금 딱 잘라 그러지 않겠다고 했어.”"]], [["“Then I can bear anything.", "“그러면 나는 무엇이든 견딜 수 있어요."], ["But, dearest, dearest Mark, will you not let me tell Lady Lufton?”", "그렇지만 여보, 사랑하는 마크, 레이디 러프턴에게 내가 말하게 해 주지 않겠어요?”"]], [["Let them look at the matter in any way the punishment was very heavy.", "그 일을 어느 쪽에서 들여다보아도 그 벌은 몹시 무거웠다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIII. IS SHE NOT INSIGNIFICANT?", "# 제43장. 그 아이는 대수롭지 않은 아이가 아니냐?"]], [["And now a month went by at Framley without any increase of comfort to our friends there, and also without any absolute development of the ruin which had been daily expected at the parsonage. Sundry letters had reached Mr.", "그리고 프램리에서 한 달이 지나갔으나, 그곳의 우리 벗들에게 편안함이 조금이라도 더해진 것은 없었고, 목사관에서 날마다 기다려 온 그 파멸이 실제로 터져 나온 일도 없었다."], ["Robarts from various personages acting in the Tozer interest, all of which he referred to Mr. Curling, of Barchester.", "토저 쪽 이해를 위하여 움직이는 여러 인물에게서 로바츠 씨에게 잡다한 편지가 닿았고, 그는 그것을 모두 바체스터의 컬링 씨에게 넘겼다."], ["Some of these letters contained prayers for the money, pointing out how an innocent widow lady had been induced to invest her all on the faith of Mr. Robarts’ name, and was now starving in a garret, with her three children, because Mr.", "그 편지 가운데 몇 통은 돈을 달라는 애원을 담고 있었으니, 죄 없는 어느 미망인이 로바츠 씨의 이름을 믿고 제 가진 것을 다 넣도록 꾀임을 받았는데 로바츠 씨가 자기가 한 약조를 지키지 않는 탓에 이제 세 아이와 함께 다락방에서 굶고 있다고 늘어놓았다."], ["Robarts would not make good his own undertakings.", "그러나 그 대다수는 위협으로 가득했다."], ["But the majority of them were filled with threats;—only two days longer would be allowed and then the sheriff’s officers would be enjoined to do their work; then one day of grace would be added, at the expiration of which the dogs of war would be unloosed.", "이틀만 더 참아 주겠고 그러고 나면 집행리들에게 저희 일을 하라고 이를 것이라고, 그다음에는 하루의 유예를 더 얹어 주겠고 그것이 다하는 날에는 전쟁의 개들을 풀어 놓을 것이라고 했다."], ["These, as fast as they came, were sent to Mr. Curling, who took no notice of them individually, but continued his endeavour to prevent the evil day.", "이런 것들은 오는 대로 곧 컬링 씨에게 보내졌고, 그는 그것을 하나하나 상대하지는 않으면서도 그 흉한 날을 막으려는 애를 이어 갔다."], ["The second bill Mr. Robarts would take up—such was Mr.", "둘째 어음은 로바츠 씨가 떠맡기로 하겠다는 것이 컬링 씨의 제안이었다."], ["Curling’s proposition; and would pay by two instalments of £250 each, the first in two months, and the second in four.", "그리고 250파운드씩 두 번에 나누어, 첫 번은 두 달 뒤에 두 번째는 넉 달 뒤에 지불하겠다는 것이었다."], ["If this were acceptable to the Tozer interest—well; if it were not, the sheriff’s officers must do their worst and the Tozer interest must look for what it could get.", "이것이 토저 쪽 이해에 받아들여진다면 좋고, 받아들여지지 않는다면 집행리들이 할 수 있는 가장 험한 짓을 해야 할 것이며 토저 쪽 이해는 건질 수 있는 것이나 찾아보아야 할 것이었다."], ["The Tozer interest would not declare itself satisfied with these terms, and so the matter went on.", "토저 쪽 이해는 이 조건에 만족한다고 밝히려 하지 않았고, 그리하여 일은 그대로 이어져 갔다."], ["During which the roses faded from day to day on the cheeks of Mrs. Robarts, as under such circumstances may easily be conceived.", "그동안 로바츠 부인의 두 뺨에서는 장미가 날마다 시들어 갔으니, 그런 사정 아래에서라면 쉽게 그려 볼 수 있는 일이다."]], [["In the meantime Lucy still remained at Hogglestock and had there become absolute mistress of the house. Poor Mrs.", "그러는 동안 루시는 여전히 호글스톡에 남아 그곳에서 그 집의 절대적인 안주인이 되어 있었다."], ["Crawley had been at death’s door; for some days she was delirious, and afterwards remained so weak as to be almost unconscious; but now the worst was over, and Mr.", "가엾은 크롤리 부인은 죽음의 문턱에 있었다. 며칠은 헛소리를 했고 그 뒤로도 거의 정신을 놓은 것처럼 기운이 없었다."], ["Crawley had been informed, that as far as human judgment might pronounce, his children would not become orphans nor would he become a widower.", "그러나 이제 가장 험한 때는 지나갔고, 사람의 판단으로 말할 수 있는 한 그의 아이들이 어미 없는 아이가 되지도 않을 것이고 그가 홀아비가 되지도 않을 것이라는 말이 크롤리 씨에게 전해졌다."], ["During these weeks Lucy had not once been home nor had she seen any of the Framley people.", "이 몇 주 동안 루시는 한 번도 집에 가지 않았고 프램리 사람들을 누구도 만나지 않았다."], ["“Why should she incur the risk of conveying infection for so small an object?”", "“그렇게 조그만 목적을 위하여 전염을 옮길 위험을 무릅쓸 까닭이 무엇인가?”"], ["as she herself argued, writing by letters, which were duly fumigated before they were opened at the parsonage.", "루시 자신이 그렇게 내세우며 편지로만 소식을 주고받았고, 그 편지는 목사관에서 뜯기 전에 절차대로 훈증을 받았다."], ["So she remained at Hogglestock, and the Crawley children, now admitted to all the honours of the nursery, were kept at Framley.", "그리하여 루시는 호글스톡에 남아 있었고, 이제 아기방의 온갖 대접을 다 받게 된 크롤리네 아이들은 프램리에 머물러 있었다."], ["They were kept at Framley, although it was expected from day to day that the beds on which they lay would be seized for the payment of Mr. Sowerby’s debts.", "그 아이들이 누워 자는 침대가 사워비 씨의 빚을 갚기 위하여 압류될 것이라고 날마다 기다려지는 형편이었음에도 아이들은 프램리에 머물러 있었다."]], [["Lucy, as I have said, became mistress of the house at Hogglestock and made herself absolutely ascendant over Mr.", "루시는, 내가 말한 대로, 호글스톡 그 집의 안주인이 되었고 크롤리 씨 위에 완전히 올라섰다."], ["Crawley. Jellies, and broth, and fruit, and even butter, came from Lufton Court, which she displayed on the table, absolutely on the cloth before him, and yet he bore it.", "젤리와 고기 국물과 과일과 심지어 버터까지 러프턴 코트에서 왔고, 루시는 그것을 식탁 위에, 그것도 그의 앞 식탁보 위에 여봐란듯이 펼쳐 놓았는데도 그는 그것을 견뎌 냈다."], ["I cannot say that he partook of these delicacies with any freedom himself, but he did drink his tea when it was given to him although it contained Framley cream;—and, had he known it, Bohea itself from the Framley chest.", "그가 이 맛난 것들을 스스로 거침없이 집어 먹었다고는 말할 수 없다. 그러나 차는 내어 주는 대로 마셨으니, 그 안에는 프램리의 크림이 들어 있었고 — 그가 알았더라면 — 차 자체도 프램리의 차 상자에서 나온 보히차였다."], ["In truth, in these days, he had given himself over to the dominion of this stranger; and he said nothing beyond, “Well, well,”", "사실 이 무렵 그는 이 낯선 사람의 지배에 제 몸을 내주고 있었다."], ["with two uplifted hands, when he came upon her as she was sewing the buttons on to his own shirts—sewing on the buttons and perhaps occasionally applying her needle elsewhere,—not without utility.", "그리고 루시가 그의 셔츠에 단추를 달고 있는 것을 — 단추를 달면서 어쩌면 이따금 바늘을 다른 데에도 대고 있는 것을, 그것도 쓸모 없지 않게 그러고 있는 것을 — 마주쳤을 때에도 두 손을 들어 올리며 “원, 원” 하는 말밖에 하지 않았다."]], [["He said to her at this period very little in the way of thanks.", "이 무렵 그가 루시에게 고맙다는 말을 하는 일은 아주 드물었다."], ["Some protracted conversations they did have, now and again, during the long evenings; but even in these he did not utter many words as to their present state of life.", "긴 저녁 동안 이따금 길게 이어지는 이야기를 나눈 적은 있었다. 그러나 그런 자리에서도 지금 두 사람이 놓인 살림의 형편에 대해서는 많은 말을 하지 않았다."], ["It was on religion chiefly that he spoke, not lecturing her individually, but laying down his ideas as to what the life of a Christian should be, and especially what should be the life of a minister.", "그가 말한 것은 주로 종교였으니, 루시를 따로 붙들고 훈계한 것이 아니라 그리스도인의 삶이란 어떠해야 하는지, 특히 성직자의 삶이란 어떠해야 하는지에 대한 제 생각을 늘어놓았다."], ["“But though I can see this, Miss Robarts,”", "“그러나 이것을 볼 수 있으면서도, 로바츠 양,”"], ["he said, “I am bound to say that no one has fallen off so frequently as myself.", "그가 말했다. “저만큼 자주 떨어져 나간 사람이 없다는 것을 저는 말하지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["I have renounced the devil and all his works; but it is by word of mouth only—by word of mouth only.", "저는 악마와 그 온갖 소행을 끊었습니다. 그러나 그것은 입으로만 한 것입니다 — 입으로만 한 것입니다."], ["How shall a man crucify the old Adam that is within him, unless he throw himself prostrate in the dust and acknowledge that all his strength is weaker than water?”", "사람이 티끌 속에 엎드려 제 힘이라는 것이 다 물보다 약하다고 시인하지 않고서 어떻게 제 안에 있는 옛 아담을 십자가에 못 박을 수 있겠습니까?”"], ["To this, often as it might be repeated, she would listen patiently, comforting him by such words as her theology would supply; but then, when this was over, she would again resume her command and enforce from him a close obedience to her domestic behests.", "이 말이 얼마나 되풀이되든 루시는 참을성 있게 들었고, 제 신학이 내어 줄 수 있는 말들로 그를 위로했다. 그러나 그것이 끝나면 다시 제 지휘를 되찾아 집안일에 관한 제 분부에 그가 빈틈없이 따르도록 몰아붙였다."]], [["At the end of the month Lord Lufton came back to Framley Court.", "한 달이 다 갈 무렵 러프턴 경이 프램리 코트로 돌아왔다."], ["His arrival there was quite unexpected; though, as he pointed out when his mother expressed some surprise, he had returned exactly at the time named by him before he started.", "그가 온 것은 아주 뜻밖의 일이었다. 다만 어머니가 놀라움을 내보였을 때 그가 짚어 말한 대로, 그는 떠나기 전에 자기가 대어 둔 바로 그때에 돌아온 것이었다."]], [["“I need not say, Ludovic, how glad I am to have you,”", "“루도빅, 네가 와 주어서 얼마나 기쁜지는 말할 것도 없구나,”"], ["said she, looking to his face and pressing his arm; “the more so, indeed, seeing that I hardly expected it.”", "마님이 아들의 얼굴을 들여다보며 그 팔을 지그시 누르고 말했다. “더구나 이렇게 될 줄은 좀처럼 생각하지 못했으니 더욱 그렇지.”"]], [["He said nothing to his mother about Lucy the first evening, although there was some conversation respecting the Robarts family.", "첫날 저녁에 그는 어머니에게 루시에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 로바츠 집안에 관한 이야기는 얼마간 있었지만 그랬다."]], [["“I am afraid Mr. Robarts has embarrassed himself,”", "“로바츠 씨가 곤경에 빠진 것 같아 걱정이구나,”"], ["said Lady Lufton, looking very seriously.", "레이디 러프턴이 아주 심각한 얼굴로 말했다."], ["“Rumours reach me which are most distressing.", "“나에게까지 들려오는 소문이 여간 괴로운 것이 아니야."], ["I have said nothing to anybody as yet—not even to Fanny; but I can see in her face, and hear in the tones of her voice, that she is suffering some great sorrow.”", "아직 아무에게도 아무 말 하지 않았다 — 패니에게도 하지 않았어. 그렇지만 그 얼굴을 보면, 그 목소리의 결을 들으면, 그이가 무슨 큰 슬픔을 겪고 있다는 것을 알 수 있구나.”"]], [["“I know all about it,”", "“저는 다 알고 있어요,”"], ["said Lord Lufton.", "러프턴 경이 말했다."]], [["“You know all about it, Ludovic?”", "“네가 다 알고 있다고, 루도빅?”"]], [["“Yes; it is through that precious friend of mine, Mr.", "“예. 콜디코츠의 사워비 씨, 그 귀하신 제 벗 때문이지요."], ["Sowerby, of Chaldicotes.", "그가 사워비의 어음을 인수해 주었어요."], ["He has accepted bills for Sowerby; indeed, he told me so.”", "실은 저에게 그렇게 말했습니다.”"]], [["“What business had he at Chaldicotes?", "“그가 콜디코츠에 무슨 볼일이 있었단 말이냐?"], ["What had he to do with such friends as that?", "그런 벗들과 무슨 상관이 있었단 말이냐?"], ["I do not know how I am to forgive him.”", "내가 그를 어떻게 용서해야 할지 모르겠구나.”"]], [["“It was through me that he became acquainted with Sowerby.", "“그가 사워비를 알게 된 것은 저를 통해서였어요."], ["You must remember that, mother.”", "어머니, 그것을 잊지 마셔야 해요.”"]], [["“I do not see that that is any excuse.", "“그것이 무슨 변명이 되는지 나는 모르겠다."], ["Is he to consider that all your acquaintances must necessarily be his friends also?", "네가 아는 사람이면 다 제 벗도 되어야 한다고 그가 여겨야 하느냐?"], ["It is reasonable to suppose that you in your position must live occasionally with a great many people who are altogether unfit companions for him as a parish clergyman.", "네 처지에서라면 교구 성직자인 그에게는 도무지 걸맞지 않은 동무들과도 이따금 어울려 지내야 하는 것이 마땅한 일이라고 헤아려 볼 만하지 않으냐."], ["He will not remember this, and he must be taught it.", "그가 이것을 기억하려 하지 않으니 그것을 가르쳐 주어야 한다."], ["What business had he to go to Gatherum Castle?”", "그가 개서럼 성에 갈 무슨 볼일이 있었단 말이냐?”"]], [["“He got his stall at Barchester by going there.”", "“그가 바체스터의 참사회원 자리를 얻은 것이 거기 갔기 때문이지요.”"]], [["“He would be much better without his stall, and Fanny has the sense to know this.", "“그 자리가 없는 편이 그에게 훨씬 나았을 것이고, 패니는 그것을 알아볼 만한 분별이 있다."], ["What does he want with two houses?", "집이 둘씩이나 있어서 무엇을 하겠느냐?"], ["Prebendal stalls are for older men than he—for men who have earned them, and who at the end of their lives want some ease.", "참사회원 자리란 그보다 나이 든 사람들의 것이야 — 그것을 얻을 만한 일을 해 온 사람들, 그리고 삶의 끝머리에 이르러 좀 편안하기를 바라는 사람들의 것이지."], ["I wish with all my heart that he had never taken it.”", "그가 그것을 아예 받지 않았더라면 좋았겠다고 나는 온 마음으로 바란다.”"]], [["“Six hundred a year has its charms all the same,”", "“그래도 연 육백 파운드에는 그 나름의 매력이 있지요,”"], ["said Lufton, getting up and strolling out of the room.", "러프턴 경이 일어나 방에서 어슬렁 나가며 말했다."]], [["“If Mark really be in any difficulty,”", "“마크가 정말로 어떤 어려움에 빠져 있다면,”"], ["he said, later in the evening, “we must put him on his legs.”", "그날 저녁 늦게 그가 말했다. “우리가 그를 다시 제 발로 서게 해 주어야 해요.”"]], [["“You mean, pay his debts?”", "“그 빚을 갚아 준다는 말이냐?”"]], [["“Yes; he has no debts except these acceptances of Sowerby’s.”", "“예. 사워비의 어음을 인수해 준 것 말고는 그에게 빚이 없어요.”"]], [["“How much will it be, Ludovic?”", "“얼마나 되겠느냐, 루도빅?”"]], [["“A thousand pounds, perhaps, more or less.", "“천 파운드쯤, 조금 더 되거나 덜 되거나 할 거예요."], ["I’ll find the money, mother; only I shan’t be able to pay you quite as soon as I intended.”", "돈은 제가 마련하겠어요, 어머니. 다만 어머니께 갚는 일이 제가 뜻했던 만큼 이르지는 못하겠지요.”"], ["Whereupon his mother got up, and throwing her arms round his neck declared that she would never forgive him if he ever said a word more about her little present to him.", "그러자 어머니가 일어나 두 팔로 아들의 목을 껴안고, 자기가 아들에게 준 그 조그만 선물에 대해 앞으로 한마디라도 더 입에 올린다면 결코 용서하지 않겠다고 밝혔다."], ["I suppose there is no pleasure a mother can have more attractive than giving away her money to an only son.", "어머니가 누릴 수 있는 즐거움 가운데 외아들에게 제 돈을 내주는 것보다 더 마음을 끄는 것은 없으리라고 나는 생각한다."]], [["Lucy’s name was first mentioned at breakfast the next morning.", "루시의 이름이 처음 입에 오른 것은 이튿날 아침 식사 자리였다."], ["Lord Lufton had made up his mind to attack his mother on the subject early in the morning—before he went up to the parsonage; but as matters turned out Miss Robarts’ doings were necessarily brought under discussion without reference to Lord Lufton’s special aspirations regarding her.", "러프턴 경은 아침 일찍 — 목사관으로 올라가기 전에 — 이 일로 어머니를 몰아붙이기로 마음을 굳혀 두었다. 그러나 일이 되어 가는 형편이, 루시를 두고 러프턴 경이 따로 품은 소망과는 아무 상관 없이 로바츠 양이 한 일을 저절로 이야깃거리로 끌어올렸다."], ["The fact of Mrs. Crawley’s illness had been mentioned, and Lady Lufton had stated how it had come to pass that all the Crawleys’ children were at the parsonage.", "크롤리 부인이 앓는다는 사실이 이야기에 올랐고, 크롤리네 아이들이 모두 목사관에 있게 된 사정을 레이디 러프턴이 밝혔던 것이다."]], [["“I must say that Fanny has behaved excellently,”", "“패니가 참 훌륭하게 처신했다고 말해야겠구나,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“It was just what might have been expected from her.", "“그이에게서 기대할 만한 그대로였지."], ["And indeed,”", "그리고 실은,”"], ["she added, speaking in an embarrassed tone, “so has Miss Robarts.", "마님이 거북한 어조로 덧붙였다. “로바츠 양도 그랬다."], ["Miss Robarts has remained at Hogglestock and nursed Mrs. Crawley through the whole.”", "로바츠 양은 호글스톡에 남아서 그 내내 크롤리 부인을 간호했어.”"]], [["“Remained at Hogglestock—through the fever!”", "“호글스톡에 남았다니 — 그 열병 속에서요!”"], ["exclaimed his lordship.", "나리가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Yes, indeed,”", "“그래, 정말 그랬다,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“And is she there now?”", "“그런데 지금도 거기 있나요?”"]], [["“Oh, yes; I am not aware that she thinks of leaving just yet.”", "“아, 그래. 아직 떠날 생각을 하고 있다는 말은 나도 못 들었다.”"]], [["“Then I say that it is a great shame—a scandalous shame!”", "“그렇다면 저는 그것이 부끄러운 일이라고 말하겠어요 — 망신스럽도록 부끄러운 일이라고요!”"]], [["“But, Ludovic, it was her own doing.”", "“그렇지만 루도빅, 그 아이가 스스로 한 일이야.”"]], [["“Oh, yes; I understand.", "“아, 예. 알겠어요."], ["But why should she be sacrificed?", "그렇지만 왜 그 사람이 제 몸을 바쳐야 합니까?"], ["Were there no nurses in the country to be hired, but that she must go and remain there for a month at the bedside of a pestilent fever?", "이 고을에 삯을 주고 부를 간호인이 하나도 없어서, 그 사람이 가서 한 달을 옮는 열병의 병상 옆에 붙어 있어야 했다는 말인가요?"], ["There is no justice in it.”", "거기에는 의로움이 없어요.”"]], [["“Justice, Ludovic?", "“의로움이라니, 루도빅?"], ["I don’t know about justice, but there was great Christian charity.", "의로움이야 나는 잘 모르겠다만, 거기에는 큰 그리스도인의 사랑이 있었다."], ["Mrs. Crawley has probably owed her life to Miss Robarts.”", "크롤리 부인은 아마 제 목숨을 로바츠 양에게 빚졌을 것이다.”"]], [["“Has she been ill?", "“그 사람이 앓았습니까?"], ["Is she ill?", "지금 앓고 있습니까?"], ["I insist upon knowing whether she is ill.", "앓고 있는지 아닌지 저는 꼭 알아야 하겠어요."], ["I shall go over to Hogglestock myself immediately after breakfast.”", "아침을 먹고 나면 곧 제가 몸소 호글스톡으로 건너가겠습니다.”"]], [["To this Lady Lufton made no reply.", "이 말에 레이디 러프턴은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["If Lord Lufton chose to go to Hogglestock she could not prevent him.", "러프턴 경이 호글스톡에 가기로 정한다면 마님이 그것을 막을 수는 없었다."], ["She thought, however, that it would be much better that he should stay away.", "그러나 아들이 가지 않는 편이 훨씬 나으리라고 마님은 생각했다."], ["He would be quite as open to the infection as Lucy Robarts; and, moreover, Mrs.", "아들도 루시 로바츠와 똑같이 전염에 그대로 열려 있을 것이었다."], ["Crawley’s bedside would be as inconvenient a place as might be selected for any interview between two lovers.", "게다가 크롤리 부인의 병상 옆이란 두 연인이 만나 이야기할 자리로 고를 수 있는 곳 가운데 가장 거북한 곳일 터였다."], ["Lady Lufton felt at the present moment that she was cruelly treated by circumstances with reference to Miss Robarts.", "로바츠 양에 관해서라면 지금 이 순간 사정이 자기를 매정하게 다루고 있다고 레이디 러프턴은 느꼈다."], ["Of course it would have been her part to lessen, if she could do so without injustice, that high idea which her son entertained of the beauty and worth of the young lady; but, unfortunately, she had been compelled to praise her and to load her name with all manner of eulogy.", "물론 의롭지 못한 짓이 되지 않고 그렇게 할 수 있다면, 그 젊은 아가씨의 아름다움과 값에 대해 아들이 품고 있는 저 높은 생각을 깎아내리는 것이 마님의 소임이었을 것이다. 그런데 불행히도 마님은 그 아가씨를 칭찬하고 그 이름에 온갖 종류의 찬사를 얹지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Lady Lufton was essentially a true woman, and not even with the object of carrying out her own views in so important a matter would she be guilty of such deception as she might have practised by simply holding her tongue; but nevertheless she could hardly reconcile herself to the necessity of singing Lucy’s praises.", "레이디 러프턴은 그 바탕이 참된 여인이었으므로, 이토록 중대한 일에서 제 뜻을 이루려는 목적으로도 그저 입을 다물고만 있어서 저지를 수 있는 그런 속임수의 죄를 짓지는 않으려 했다. 그러나 그러면서도 루시를 칭송해야 하는 그 어쩔 수 없음과는 좀처럼 화해할 수가 없었다."]], [["After breakfast Lady Lufton got up from her chair, but hung about the room without making any show of leaving.", "아침을 먹고 나서 레이디 러프턴은 의자에서 일어났으나, 나갈 낌새는 조금도 보이지 않고 방 안을 서성거렸다."], ["In accordance with her usual custom she would have asked her son what he was going to do; but she did not dare so to inquire now.", "늘 하던 대로라면 오늘은 무엇을 할 작정이냐고 아들에게 물었을 것이다. 그러나 지금은 그렇게 물을 엄두가 나지 않았다."], ["Had he not declared, only a few minutes since, whither he would go?", "어디로 갈 것인지를 아들이 겨우 몇 분 전에 밝히지 않았던가?"], ["“I suppose I shall see you at lunch?”", "“점심때에는 너를 보게 되겠지?”"], ["at last she said.", "마침내 마님이 말했다."]], [["“At lunch?", "“점심때요?"], ["Well, I don’t know.", "글쎄요, 모르겠어요."], ["Look here, mother.", "여기 보세요, 어머니."], ["What am I to say to Miss Robarts when I see her?”", "제가 로바츠 양을 만나면 그 사람에게 무슨 말을 해야 합니까?”"], ["and he leaned with his back against the chimney-piece as he interrogated his mother.", "그러면서 그는 어머니를 다잡아 물으며 등을 난로 선반에 기댔다."]], [["“What are you to say to her, Ludovic?”", "“그 아이에게 무슨 말을 해야 하느냐고, 루도빅?”"]], [["“Yes; what am I to say,—as coming from you?", "“예. 제가 무슨 말을 해야 합니까 — 어머니께서 하시는 말로요?"], ["Am I to tell her that you will receive her as your daughter-in-law?”", "어머니가 그 사람을 며느리로 맞으시겠다고 제가 말해야 합니까?”"]], [["“Ludovic, I have explained all that to Miss Robarts herself.”", "“루도빅, 나는 그것을 다 로바츠 양 본인에게 설명해 두었다.”"]], [["“Explained what?”", "“무엇을 설명하셨다는 말입니까?”"]], [["“I have told her that I did not think that such a marriage would make either you or her happy.”", "“그런 혼인이 너를 행복하게 하지도, 그 아이를 행복하게 하지도 않으리라고 내가 생각한다고 말해 주었다.”"]], [["“And why have you told her so?", "“그런데 왜 그런 말씀을 하셨습니까?"], ["Why have you taken upon yourself to judge for me in such a matter, as though I were a child?", "왜 제가 어린아이라도 되는 것처럼 이런 일에서 저를 대신해 판단하는 일을 스스로 떠맡으셨습니까?"], ["Mother, you must unsay what you have said.”", "어머니, 하신 말씀을 거두어 주셔야 합니다.”"]], [["Lord Lufton, as he spoke, looked full into his mother’s face; and he did so, not as though he were begging from her a favour, but issuing to her a command.", "러프턴 경은 이렇게 말하면서 어머니의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다. 그것도 어머니에게 은혜를 몸 낮추어 구하는 사람의 눈길이 아니라 명을 내리는 사람의 눈길이었다."], ["She stood near him, with one hand on the breakfast-table, gazing at him almost furtively, not quite daring to meet the full view of his eye.", "마님은 한 손을 아침 식탁에 얹은 채 아들 가까이 서서, 그 눈을 정면으로 마주할 엄두는 채 내지 못하고 거의 몰래 훔쳐보듯 아들을 바라보았다."], ["There was only one thing on earth which Lady Lufton feared, and that was her son’s displeasure.", "이 세상에 레이디 러프턴이 두려워하는 것은 한 가지뿐이었으니, 그것은 아들의 언짢은 낯빛이었다."], ["The sun of her earthly heaven shone upon her through the medium of his existence.", "마님의 이 땅에 있는 하늘의 해는 아들이라는 존재를 통하여 마님을 비추었다."], ["If she were driven to quarrel with him, as some ladies of her acquaintance were driven to quarrel with their sons, the world to her would be over.", "아는 부인네들 가운데 몇이 저희 아들과 다투는 데까지 밀려갔듯이 마님도 아들과 다투는 데까지 밀려간다면, 마님에게 세상은 끝나 버릴 것이었다."], ["Not but what facts might be so strong as to make it absolutely necessary that she should do this.", "그렇다고 사실이 그렇게 하는 것을 절대로 어쩔 수 없게 만들 만큼 세지 않으리라는 뜻은 아니다."], ["As some people resolve that, under certain circumstances, they will commit suicide, so she could see that, under certain circumstances, she must consent even to be separated from him.", "어떤 사람들이 어떤 사정 아래에서라면 스스로 목숨을 끊겠다고 마음을 정해 두는 것과 마찬가지로, 마님도 어떤 사정 아래에서라면 아들에게서 떨어지는 것에까지 응해야 한다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["She would not do wrong,—not that which she knew to be wrong,—even for his sake.", "마님은 그릇된 일을 — 스스로 그릇되다고 아는 일을 — 아들을 위해서라도 하지 않을 것이었다."], ["If it were necessary that all her happiness should collapse and be crushed in ruin around her, she must endure it, and wait God’s time to relieve her from so dark a world.", "제 행복이 다 허물어져 제 주위에 무너져 내려 짓눌리는 것이 어쩔 수 없는 일이라면 마님은 그것을 견뎌 내고, 그토록 어두운 세상에서 자기를 건져 주실 하느님의 때를 기다려야 했다."], ["The light of the sun was very dear to her, but even that might be purchased at too dear a cost.", "해의 빛은 마님에게 여간 값진 것이 아니었으나, 그것조차 너무 비싼 값으로는 살 수 없는 것이었다."]], [["“I told you before, mother, that my choice was made, and I asked you then to give your consent; you have now had time to think about it, and therefore I have come to ask you again.", "“어머니, 저는 전에도 제 선택이 정해졌다고 말씀드렸고 그때 승낙해 주시기를 청했습니다. 이제 어머니께서 그것을 생각해 보실 틈이 있었으니, 그래서 다시 청하러 왔습니다."], ["I have reason to know that there will be no impediment to my marriage if you will frankly hold out your hand to Lucy.”", "어머니가 루시에게 서슴없이 손을 내밀어 주신다면 제 혼인에 걸림돌이 없을 것이라고 알 만한 까닭이 저에게는 있습니다.”"]], [["The matter was altogether in Lady Lufton’s hands, but, fond as she was of power, she absolutely wished that it were not so.", "이 일은 온전히 레이디 러프턴의 손에 놓여 있었으나, 힘을 좋아하는 사람이면서도 마님은 참으로 그렇지 않기를 바랐다."], ["Had her son married without asking her and then brought Lucy home as his wife, she would undoubtedly have forgiven him; and much as she might have disliked the match, she would, ultimately, have embraced the bride.", "아들이 어머니에게 묻지 않고 혼인해 버린 뒤에 루시를 아내로 삼아 집에 데려왔다면 마님은 틀림없이 아들을 용서했을 것이고, 그 혼사를 아무리 싫어했다 해도 끝내는 그 신부를 품에 안았을 것이다."], ["But now she was compelled to exercise her judgment.", "그런데 이제는 제 판단을 행사하지 않을 수 없게 되었다."], ["If he married imprudently, it would be her doing.", "아들이 지각없이 혼인한다면 그것은 마님이 한 일이 될 것이었다."], ["How was she to give her expressed consent to that which she believed to be wrong?", "스스로 그릇되다고 믿는 일에 어떻게 입으로 밝혀 승낙해 줄 수 있겠는가?"]], [["“Do you know anything against her; any reason why she should not be my wife?”", "“그 사람에게 흠될 것을 무엇이라도 아십니까? 그 사람이 제 아내가 되어서는 안 될 무슨 까닭이라도요?”"], ["continued he.", "그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“If you mean as regards her moral conduct, certainly not,”", "“도덕상의 처신을 두고 하는 말이라면 물론 없다,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."], ["“But I could say as much as that in favour of a great many young ladies whom I should regard as very ill suited for such a marriage.”", "“그렇지만 그런 혼인에 여간 걸맞지 않다고 내가 여길 젊은 아가씨들도 아주 많은데, 나로서는 그이들을 두고도 그만한 말은 해 줄 수 있겠다.”"]], [["“Yes; some might be vulgar, some might be ill-tempered, some might be ugly; others might be burdened with disagreeable connections.", "“예. 어떤 이는 상스러울 수도 있고, 어떤 이는 성미가 나쁠 수도 있고, 어떤 이는 못생겼을 수도 있겠지요."], ["I can understand that you should object to a daughter-in-law under any of these circumstances.", "또 어떤 이는 언짢은 인연을 짐으로 지고 있을 수도 있겠지요. 이런 사정 가운데 어느 하나라도 걸린다면 어머니께서 며느리를 마다하실 만하다는 것은 저도 알 수 있어요."], ["But none of these things can be said of Miss Robarts.", "그렇지만 로바츠 양에게는 이런 것 가운데 어느 하나도 해당되지 않습니다."], ["I defy you to say that she is not in all respects what a lady should be.”", "그 사람이 어느 모로 보나 숙녀가 마땅히 그러해야 할 그대로가 아니라고 어머니께서 말씀하실 수 있다면 해 보십시오.”"]], [["But her father was a doctor of medicine, she is the sister of the parish clergyman, she is only five feet two in height, and is so uncommonly brown!", "그렇지만 그 아이의 아버지는 의사였고, 그 아이는 교구 성직자의 누이이며, 키는 겨우 다섯 피트 두 치밖에 되지 않고, 살빛이 어쩌면 그렇게 검단 말인가!"], ["Had Lady Lufton dared to give a catalogue of her objections, such would have been its extent and nature.", "레이디 러프턴이 제 반대 이유의 목록을 감히 늘어놓았다면 그 폭과 결이 그러했을 것이다."], ["But she did not dare to do this.", "그러나 마님은 그것을 감히 하지 못했다."]], [["“I cannot say, Ludovic, that she is possessed of all that you should seek in a wife.”", "“루도빅, 네가 아내에게서 구해야 할 것을 그 아이가 다 갖추었다고는 나는 말할 수 없다.”"], ["Such was her answer.", "마님의 대답은 그러했다."]], [["“Do you mean that she has not got money?”", "“그 사람에게 돈이 없다는 말씀입니까?”"]], [["“No, not that; I should be very sorry to see you making money your chief object, or indeed any essential object.", "“아니, 그런 것이 아니다. 네가 돈을 으뜸 목적으로, 아니 반드시 있어야 할 목적으로 삼는 것을 보게 된다면 나는 여간 슬프지 않을 것이다."], ["If it chanced that your wife did have money, no doubt you would find it a convenience.", "네 아내에게 어쩌다 돈이 있다면 그것이 편리하다는 것을 너도 물론 알게 되겠지."], ["But pray understand me, Ludovic; I would not for a moment advise you to subject your happiness to such a necessity as that.", "그렇지만 루도빅, 제발 나를 알아들어라. 네 행복을 그런 어쩔 수 없음에 매어 두라고 나는 한순간도 권하지 않겠다."], ["It is not because she is without fortune—”", "그 아이에게 재산이 없기 때문이 아니야 —”"]], [["“Then why is it?", "“그렇다면 무엇 때문입니까?"], ["At breakfast you were singing her praises, and saying how excellent she is.”", "아침 식탁에서는 그 사람을 칭송하시며 얼마나 훌륭한 사람인지를 말씀하셨는데요.”"]], [["“If I were forced to put my objection into one word, I should say—”", "“내 반대를 한마디로 담아야 한다면 나는 이렇게 말하겠다 —”"], ["and then she paused, hardly daring to encounter the frown which was already gathering itself on her son’s brow.", "그러고서 마님은 말을 멈추었으니, 아들의 이마에 이미 모여들고 있는 그 찌푸림을 마주할 엄두가 좀처럼 나지 않았다."]], [["“You would say what?”", "“무엇이라고 말씀하시겠다는 겁니까?”"], ["said Lord Lufton, almost roughly.", "러프턴 경이 거의 거칠게 말했다."]], [["“Don’t be angry with me, Ludovic; all that I think, and all that I say on this subject, I think and say with only one object—that of your happiness.", "“나에게 노여워하지 마라, 루도빅. 이 일에 대해 내가 생각하는 것도, 내가 말하는 것도 다 오직 한 가지 목적으로 생각하고 말하는 것이야 — 네 행복이라는 목적으로."], ["What other motive can I have for anything in this world?”", "이 세상 무슨 일에서든 내가 달리 무슨 동기를 가질 수 있겠느냐?”"], ["And then she came close to him and kissed him.", "그러고서 마님은 아들에게 가까이 다가가 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“But tell me, mother, what is this objection; what is this terrible word that is to sum up the list of all poor Lucy’s sins, and prove that she is unfit for married life?”", "“그렇지만 말씀해 주세요, 어머니. 그 반대가 무엇입니까? 딱한 루시의 죄목을 다 한자리에 몰아 담고 그 사람이 혼인해 사는 삶에 걸맞지 않다는 것을 증명한다는, 그 무서운 한마디가 무엇입니까?”"]], [["“Ludovic, I did not say that.", "“루도빅, 나는 그렇게 말하지 않았다."], ["You know that I did not.”", "내가 그러지 않았다는 것을 너도 알지 않느냐.”"]], [["“What is the word, mother?”", "“그 한마디가 무엇입니까, 어머니?”"]], [["And then at last Lady Lufton spoke it out.", "그리하여 마침내 레이디 러프턴이 그 말을 입 밖에 냈다."], ["“She is—insignificant.", "“그 아이는 — 대수롭지 않아."], ["I believe her to be a very good girl, but she is not qualified to fill the high position to which you would exalt her.”", "나는 그 아이가 아주 착한 아이라고 믿는다. 그렇지만 네가 그 아이를 올려 앉히려는 그 높은 자리를 채울 자격이 그 아이에게는 없어.”"]], [["“Insignificant!”", "“대수롭지 않다니요!”"]], [["“Yes, Ludovic, I think so.”", "“그래, 루도빅. 나는 그렇게 생각한다.”"]], [["“Then, mother, you do not know her.", "“그렇다면 어머니, 어머니는 그 사람을 모르십니다."], ["You must permit me to say that you are talking of a girl whom you do not know.", "어머니가 모르는 처녀를 두고 말씀하고 계신다고 제가 말하는 것을 허락하셔야 합니다."], ["Of all the epithets of opprobrium which the English language could give you, that would be nearly the last which she would deserve.”", "영어라는 말이 어머니께 내어 줄 수 있는 온갖 욕된 딱지 가운데에서, 그것이야말로 그 사람이 들을 만한 것으로는 거의 맨 마지막에 놓일 것입니다.”"]], [["“I have not intended any opprobrium.”", "“나는 욕되게 할 뜻이 없었다.”"]], [["“Insignificant!”", "“대수롭지 않다니요!”"]], [["“Perhaps you do not quite understand me, Ludovic.”", "“네가 나를 온전히 알아듣지 못한 것인지도 모르겠구나, 루도빅.”"]], [["“I know what insignificant means, mother.”", "“대수롭지 않다는 것이 무슨 뜻인지는 저도 압니다, 어머니.”"]], [["“I think that she would not worthily fill the position which your wife should take in the world.”", "“네 아내가 세상에서 차지해야 할 그 자리를 그 아이가 값있게 채우지는 못하리라고 나는 생각한다.”"]], [["“I understand what you say.”", "“무슨 말씀인지 알겠습니다.”"]], [["“She would not do you honour at the head of your table.”", "“네 식탁의 상석에 앉아 네 낯을 세워 주지는 못할 것이다.”"]], [["“Ah, I understand.", "“아, 알겠습니다."], ["You want me to marry some bouncing Amazon, some pink and white giantess of fashion who would frighten the little people into their proprieties.”", "어머니는 제가 껑충하게 큰 아마존 같은 여자, 분홍과 흰빛으로 차린 유행의 여자 거인, 작은 사람들을 겁주어 예절을 지키게 만들 그런 여자와 혼인하기를 바라시는군요.”"]], [["“Oh, Ludovic!", "“아, 루도빅!"], ["you are intending to laugh at me now.”", "이제 나를 놀리려고 하는구나.”"]], [["“I was never less inclined to laugh in my life—never, I can assure you.", "“제 평생 웃고 싶은 마음이 이보다 덜했던 적은 없습니다 — 결코 없어요, 정말입니다."], ["And now I am more certain than ever that your objection to Miss Robarts arises from your not knowing her.", "그리고 이제 저는 로바츠 양에 대한 어머니의 반대가 그 사람을 모르시는 데에서 나온 것이라고 어느 때보다 더 확신합니다."], ["You will find, I think, when you do know her, that she is as well able to hold her own as any lady of your acquaintance;—ay, and to maintain her husband’s position, too.", "어머니가 그 사람을 알게 되시면, 어머니가 아시는 어느 부인에게도 못지않게 그 사람이 제 자리를 지켜 낼 수 있다는 것을 — 그렇습니다, 제 남편의 자리까지 지켜 낼 수 있다는 것을 알게 되실 겁니다."], ["I can assure you that I shall have no fear of her on that score.”", "그 점에서라면 저는 그 사람을 두고 아무 두려움도 없을 것이라고 어머니께 말씀드릴 수 있습니다.”"]], [["“I think, dearest, that perhaps you hardly—”", "“얘야, 어쩌면 네가 좀처럼 —”"]], [["“I think this, mother, that in such a matter as this I must choose for myself.", "“어머니, 저는 이렇게 생각합니다. 이런 일에서는 제가 저를 위하여 골라야 한다고요."], ["I have chosen; and I now ask you, as my mother, to go to her and bid her welcome.", "저는 골랐습니다. 그리고 이제 제 어머니로서 그 사람에게 가시어 어서 오라고 맞아 주시기를 청합니다."], ["Dear mother, I will own this, that I should not be happy if I thought that you did not love my wife.”", "어머니, 이것만은 털어놓겠습니다. 어머니가 제 아내를 사랑하지 않으신다는 생각이 들면 저는 행복하지 못할 것입니다.”"], ["These last words he said in a tone of affection that went to his mother’s heart, and then he left the room.", "이 마지막 말은 어머니의 가슴에 사무치는 애정의 어조로 하고서 그는 방을 나섰다."]], [["Poor Lady Lufton, when she was alone, waited till she heard her son’s steps retreating through the hall, and then betook herself up-stairs to her customary morning work.", "가엾은 레이디 러프턴은 혼자 남게 되자 아들의 발걸음이 현관을 지나 멀어지는 소리가 들릴 때까지 기다렸고, 그러고 나서 늘 하는 아침 일거리를 보러 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["She sat down at last as though about so to occupy herself; but her mind was too full to allow of her taking up her pen.", "마님은 그 일에 몸을 담글 듯이 마침내 자리에 앉았다. 그러나 마음이 너무 가득 차 있어 펜을 들 수가 없었다."], ["She had often said to herself, in days which to her were not as yet long gone by, that she would choose a bride for her son, and that then she would love the chosen one with all her heart.", "마님은 여러 번, 자기 셈으로는 아직 오래 지나지 않은 날들에, 아들의 신부를 자기가 고를 것이고 그러고 나면 고른 그 사람을 온 마음으로 사랑할 것이라고 스스로에게 말한 적이 있었다."], ["She would dethrone herself in favour of this new queen, sinking with joy into her dowager state, in order that her son’s wife might shine with the greater splendour.", "그 새 여왕을 위하여 자기는 왕좌에서 물러날 것이었고, 아들의 아내가 더 큰 영화로 빛나도록 기꺼이 미망인 마님의 자리로 내려앉을 것이었다."], ["The fondest day-dreams of her life had all had reference to the time when her son should bring home a new Lady Lufton, selected by herself from the female excellence of England, and in which she might be the first to worship her new idol.", "마님의 삶에서 가장 애틋한 백일몽은 다 아들이 새 레이디 러프턴을 — 영국의 뛰어난 여인들 가운데에서 마님 자신이 골라낸 사람을 — 집으로 데려올 그때에 걸려 있었고, 그 새 우상을 맨 처음 받들어 모시는 사람이 마님 자신이 되는 것에 걸려 있었다."], ["But could she dethrone herself for Lucy Robarts?", "그러나 루시 로바츠를 위하여 왕좌에서 물러날 수 있겠는가?"], ["Could she give up her chair of state in order to place thereon the little girl from the parsonage?", "목사관에서 온 그 조그만 처녀를 그 위에 앉히려고 제 옥좌를 내줄 수 있겠는가?"], ["Could she take to her heart, and treat with absolute loving confidence, with the confidence of an almost idolatrous mother, that little chit who, a few months since, had sat awkwardly in one corner of her drawing-room, afraid to speak to any one?", "몇 달 전에 마님의 응접실 한구석에 누구에게 말을 걸기도 겁내며 어색하게 앉아 있던 그 조그만 계집애를, 우상을 받들다시피 하는 어머니의 그 믿음으로, 온전히 사랑스러운 믿음을 다 주어 품에 안을 수 있겠는가?"], ["And yet it seemed that it must come to this—to this—or else those day-dreams of hers would in nowise come to pass.", "그러면서도 일이 여기까지 — 여기까지 — 와야 하는 듯싶었고, 그러지 않으면 마님의 그 백일몽은 아무래도 이루어지지 않을 것이었다."]], [["She sat herself down, trying to think whether it were possible that Lucy might fill the throne; for she had begun to recognize it as probable that her son’s will would be too strong for her; but her thoughts would fly away to Griselda Grantly.", "마님은 자리에 앉아, 루시가 그 왕좌를 채우는 것이 될 수 있는 일인지 생각해 보려고 애썼다. 아들의 뜻이 저로서는 너무 세다는 것이 있음직한 일임을 알아보기 시작했기 때문이다."], ["In her first and only matured attempt to realize her day-dreams, she had chosen Griselda for her queen.", "그러나 마님의 생각은 자꾸 그리젤다 그랜틀리에게로 날아갔다. 제 백일몽을 이루려고 처음으로, 또 지금까지 유일하게 무르익도록 해 본 그 시도에서 마님은 그리젤다를 제 여왕으로 골랐다."], ["She had failed there, seeing that the fates had destined Miss Grantly for another throne;—for another and a higher one, as far as the world goes.", "마님은 거기서 실패했으니, 운명이 그랜틀리 양을 다른 왕좌로 정해 놓은 것을 보게 되었기 때문이다 — 세상의 셈으로는 다르고도 더 높은 왕좌로."], ["She would have made Griselda the wife of a baron, but fate was about to make that young lady the wife of a marquis.", "마님이라면 그리젤다를 남작의 아내로 만들었겠지만, 운명은 그 젊은 아가씨를 후작의 아내로 만들려 하고 있었다."], ["Was there cause of grief in this?", "여기에 슬퍼할 까닭이 있었을까?"], ["Did she really regret that Miss Grantly, with all her virtues, should be made over to the house of Hartletop?", "온갖 미덕을 갖춘 그랜틀리 양이 하틀탑 집안으로 넘겨지는 것을 마님은 정말로 애석하게 여겼을까?"], ["Lady Lufton was a woman who did not bear disappointment lightly; but nevertheless she did almost feel herself to have been relieved from a burden when she thought of the termination of the Lufton-Grantly marriage treaty.", "레이디 러프턴은 낙심을 가볍게 견디는 여인이 아니었다. 그러나 그러면서도 러프턴-그랜틀리 혼인 조약이 끝나 버린 것을 생각할 때면 마님은 짐 하나를 벗은 것 같은 느낌을 거의 받았다."], ["What if she had been successful, and, after all, the prize had been other than she had expected?", "만일 성공했는데 그 상이 결국 마님이 기대했던 것과 다른 것이었다면 어떻게 되었겠는가?"], ["She was sometimes prone to think that that prize was not exactly all that she had once hoped.", "그 상이 한때 바랐던 것 그대로는 아니라고 마님은 이따금 생각하는 쪽으로 기울었다."], ["Griselda looked the very thing that Lady Lufton wanted for a queen;—but how would a queen reign who trusted only to her looks?", "그리젤다는 레이디 러프턴이 여왕감으로 바라는 바로 그것처럼 보였다. 그러나 제 겉모습에만 기대는 여왕이 어떻게 군림하겠는가?"], ["In that respect it was perhaps well for her that destiny had interposed.", "그 점에서라면 운명이 끼어든 것이 마님에게 잘된 일이었는지도 몰랐다."], ["Griselda, she was driven to admit, was better suited to Lord Dumbello than to her son.", "그리젤다는 제 아들보다 덤벨로 경에게 더 잘 맞았다고 마님은 시인하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["But still—such a queen as Lucy!", "그렇더라도 — 루시 같은 여왕이라니!"], ["Could it ever come to pass that the lieges of the kingdom would bow the knee in proper respect before so puny a sovereign?", "그토록 조그만 임금 앞에서 이 왕국의 백성들이 마땅한 공경으로 무릎을 굽히는 일이 언제라도 있을 수 있겠는가?"], ["And then there was that feeling which, in still higher quarters, prevents the marriage of princes with the most noble of their people.", "게다가 훨씬 더 높은 자리에서 왕자들이 제 백성 가운데 가장 지체 높은 이와 혼인하는 것을 막는 그 느낌도 있었다."], ["Is it not a recognized rule of these realms that none of the blood royal shall raise to royal honours those of the subjects who are by birth un-royal!", "왕가의 피를 이은 이는 태생으로 왕가가 아닌 신민을 왕가의 영예로 끌어올리지 못한다는 것이 이 나라들에서 인정된 규범이 아닌가!"], ["Lucy was a subject of the house of Lufton in that she was the sister of the parson and a resident denizen of the parsonage.", "루시는 목사의 누이이자 목사관에 사는 거주자라는 점에서 러프턴 집안의 신민이었다."], ["Presuming that Lucy herself might do for queen—granting that she might have some faculty to reign, the crown having been duly placed on her brow—how, then, about that clerical brother near the throne?", "루시 자신이 여왕으로 쓸 만하다고 가정하더라도 — 왕관이 그 이마에 절차대로 놓인 뒤에 다스릴 재간이 얼마간 있을 수 있다고 인정하더라도 — 그러면 왕좌 가까이에 있는 그 성직자 오라비는 어떻게 하는가?"], ["Would it not come to this, that there would no longer be a queen at Framley?", "결국 프램리에 여왕이라는 것이 더 이상 없어지는 데에 이르지 않겠는가?"]], [["And yet she knew that she must yield. She did not say so to herself.", "그러면서도 마님은 자기가 물러서야 한다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["She did not as yet acknowledge that she must put out her hand to Lucy, calling her by name as her daughter.", "스스로에게 그렇게 말하지는 않았다. 루시에게 손을 내밀며 딸이라고 이름 불러야 한다고 아직은 시인하지 않았다."], ["She did not absolutely say as much to her own heart;—not as yet.", "제 마음에게조차 그만큼 딱 잘라 말하지는 않았다 — 아직은 아니었다."], ["But she did begin to bethink herself of Lucy’s high qualities, and to declare to herself that the girl, if not fit to be a queen, was at any rate fit to be a woman.", "그러나 마님은 루시의 높은 자질을 헤아려 보기 시작했고, 그 처녀가 여왕이 되기에 걸맞지 않다 하더라도 아무튼 여인이 되기에는 걸맞다고 스스로에게 밝히기 시작했다."], ["That there was a spirit within that body, insignificant though the body might be, Lady Lufton was prepared to admit.", "그 몸이 아무리 대수롭지 않다 하더라도 그 몸 안에 기개가 있다는 것은 레이디 러프턴도 시인할 준비가 되어 있었다."], ["That she had acquired the power—the chief of all powers in this world—of sacrificing herself for the sake of others; that, too, was evident enough.", "남을 위하여 제 몸을 바치는 힘 — 이 세상 모든 힘 가운데 으뜸인 그 힘 — 을 그 처녀가 얻었다는 것도 넉넉히 뚜렷했다."], ["That she was a good girl, in the usual acceptation of the word good, Lady Lufton had never doubted.", "착하다는 말의 흔한 뜻으로 그 처녀가 착한 아이라는 것은 레이디 러프턴도 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었다."], ["She was ready-witted too, prompt in action, gifted with a certain fire.", "또 재치가 재빨랐고 움직임이 날랬으며 어떤 불기운을 타고났다."], ["It was that gift of fire which had won for her, so unfortunately, Lord Lufton’s love.", "불행하게도 러프턴 경의 사랑을 그 처녀에게 얻어다 준 것이 바로 그 불기운의 타고남이었다. 마님 자신도 루시 로바츠를 사랑하는 것이 넉넉히 될 수 있는 일이었다."], ["It was quite possible for her also to love Lucy Robarts; Lady Lufton admitted that to herself;—but then who could bow the knee before her, and serve her as a queen?", "레이디 러프턴은 그것을 스스로에게 시인했다. 그러나 그러면 누가 그 앞에 무릎을 굽히고 여왕으로 받들 것인가?"], ["Was it not a pity that she should be so insignificant?", "그 아이가 그토록 대수롭지 않은 것이 딱한 일이 아닌가?"]], [["But, nevertheless, we may say that as Lady Lufton sate that morning in her own room for two hours without employment, the star of Lucy Robarts was gradually rising in the firmament.", "그러나 그럼에도 그날 아침 레이디 러프턴이 두 시간을 아무 일도 하지 않고 제 방에 앉아 있는 동안 루시 로바츠의 별이 차츰 하늘에 떠오르고 있었다고 우리는 말할 수 있다."], ["After all, love was the food chiefly necessary for the nourishment of Lady Lufton,—the only food absolutely necessary.", "무엇보다도 레이디 러프턴을 기르는 데 주로 필요한 양식은 사랑이었다 — 절대로 없어서는 안 될 유일한 양식이었다."], ["She was not aware of this herself, nor probably would those who knew her best have so spoken of her.", "마님 자신은 이것을 알지 못했고, 마님을 가장 잘 아는 이들도 아마 마님을 두고 그렇게 말하지는 않았을 것이다."], ["They would have declared that family pride was her daily pabulum, and she herself would have said so too, calling it, however, by some less offensive name.", "그이들은 집안의 자랑이 마님의 나날의 먹이라고 밝혔을 것이고, 마님 자신도 그렇게 말했을 것이다. 다만 그것을 좀 덜 거슬리는 이름으로 불렀을 것이다."], ["Her son’s honour, and the honour of her house!—of those she would have spoken as the things dearest to her in this world.", "아들의 명예, 그리고 제 집안의 명예! 이 세상에서 제게 가장 값진 것으로 마님은 그것을 들어 말했을 것이다."], ["And this was partly true, for had her son been dishonoured, she would have sunk with sorrow to the grave.", "그리고 이것은 얼마쯤 참이었으니, 아들이 명예를 잃었다면 마님은 슬픔에 잠겨 무덤으로 내려앉았을 것이다."], ["But the one thing necessary to her daily life was the power of loving those who were near to her.", "그러나 마님의 나날의 삶에 반드시 있어야 할 한 가지는 제 가까이에 있는 이들을 사랑하는 힘이었다."]], [["Lord Lufton, when he left the dining-room, intended at once to go up to the parsonage, but he first strolled round the garden in order that he might make up his mind what he would say there.", "러프턴 경은 식당을 나서면서 곧바로 목사관으로 올라가려 했으나, 거기서 무슨 말을 할지 마음을 정하려고 우선 정원을 한 바퀴 어슬렁거렸다."], ["He was angry with his mother, having not had the wit to see that she was about to give way and yield to him, and he was determined to make it understood that in this matter he would have his own way.", "그는 어머니에게 노여워하고 있었으니, 어머니가 물러서서 저에게 굽히려 하고 있다는 것을 알아볼 눈치가 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 이 일에서는 제 뜻대로 하겠다는 것을 알아듣게 만들기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["He had learned that which it was necessary that he should know as to Lucy’s heart, and such being the case he would not conceive it possible that he should be debarred by his mother’s opposition.", "루시의 마음에 관하여 알아야 할 것을 그는 알아 두었고, 그러니 어머니의 반대에 막힐 수 있다는 것을 그는 생각조차 하려 하지 않았다. ‘영국에 나만큼 제 어머니를 더 사랑하는 아들은 없다,’ 그가 속으로 말했다."], ["“There is no son in England loves his mother better than I do,”", "‘그렇지만 사람이 참아 낼 수 없는 것들이 있다."], ["he said to himself; “but there are some things which a man cannot stand.", "내가 그러라고 두었더라면 어머니는 나를 저 돌덩이와 혼인시켰을 것이다."], ["She would have married me to that block of stone if I would have let her; and now, because she is disappointed there— Insignificant!", "그런데 이제 그쪽에서 낙심하고 나니 — 대수롭지 않다니! 그토록 어처구니없고, 그토록 사실과 다르고, 그토록 사랑이 없고, 그토록 — 그런 말은 내 평생 들어 본 적이 없다."], ["I never in my life heard anything so absurd, so untrue, so uncharitable, so— She’d like me to bring a dragon home, I suppose.", "어머니는 내가 용을 집에 데려오기를 바라시는 모양이다."], ["It would serve her right if I did,—some creature that would make the house intolerable to her.” “She must do it though,”", "내가 정말 그렇게 한다면 그것이 어머니에게 마땅한 갚음일 것이다 — 어머니에게 이 집을 견딜 수 없는 곳으로 만들어 놓을 무슨 짐승 같은 것을 데려온다면 말이지.’ ‘그래도 어머니는 그것을 하셔야 한다,’ 그가 또 말했다."], ["he said again, “or she and I will quarrel,” and then he turned off towards the gate, preparing to go to the parsonage.", "‘그러지 않으면 어머니와 나는 다투게 될 것이다.’ 그러고서 그는 목사관으로 갈 채비를 하며 문을 향해 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["“My lord, have you heard what has happened?”", "“나리, 무슨 일이 있었는지 들으셨습니까요?”"], ["said the gardener, coming to him at the gate.", "정원사가 문간으로 그에게 다가오며 말했다."], ["The man was out of breath and almost overwhelmed by the greatness of his own tidings.", "그 사람은 숨이 차 있었고 제가 가져온 소식의 엄청남에 거의 짓눌려 있었다."]], [["“No; I have heard nothing.", "“아니, 아무것도 못 들었네."], ["What is it?”", "무슨 일인가?”"]], [["“The bailiffs have taken possession of everything at the parsonage.”", "“집행관들이 목사관의 모든 것을 차지해 버렸습니다요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLIV. THE PHILISTINES AT THE PARSONAGE.", "# 제44장. 목사관에 들이친 블레셋 사람들"]], [["It has been already told how things went on between the Tozers, Mr. Curling, and Mark Robarts during that month. Mr. Forrest had drifted out of the business altogether, as also had Mr. Sowerby, as far as any active participation in it went. Letters came frequently from Mr. Curling to the parsonage, and at last came a message by special mission to say that the evil day was at hand. As far as Mr. Curling’s professional experience would enable him to anticipate or foretell the proceedings of such a man as Tom Tozer, he thought that the sheriff’s officers would be at Framley Parsonage on the following morning. Mr. Curling’s experience did not mislead him in this respect.", "토저네와 컬링 씨와 마크 로바츠 사이에 그 한 달 동안 일이 어떻게 되어 갔는지는 이미 이야기했다. 포레스트 씨는 그 일에서 아주 떠내려가 버렸고, 사워비 씨 또한 적어도 그 일에 손을 걷고 나서는 한에서는 마찬가지였다. 컬링 씨에게서 목사관으로 편지가 자주 왔고, 마침내는 사람을 따로 보내어 흉한 날이 코앞에 왔다는 전갈이 이르렀다. 톰 토저 같은 사내가 어떻게 나올지를 컬링 씨의 직업상 경험으로 미리 짚고 미리 말할 수 있는 한에서, 그는 이튿날 아침에 집행리들이 프램리 목사관에 들이닥칠 것이라고 보았다. 이 점에서 컬링 씨의 경험은 그를 속이지 않았다."]], [["“And what will you do, Mark?”", "“그러면 당신은 어떻게 하시겠어요, 마크?”"], ["said Fanny, speaking through her tears, after she had read the letter which her husband handed to her.", "남편이 건네준 편지를 다 읽고 나서, 패니가 눈물 사이로 말했다."]], [["“Nothing.", "“아무것도 안 해."], ["What can I do?", "내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠어?"], ["They must come.”", "저들은 올 수밖에 없어.”"]], [["“Lord Lufton came to-day.", "“러프턴 경이 오늘 오셨어요."], ["Will you not go to him?”", "그분에게 가 보지 않으시겠어요?”"]], [["“No.", "“아니."], ["If I were to do so it would be the same as asking him for the money.”", "그렇게 하면 돈을 달라고 청하는 것과 같아.”"]], [["“Why not borrow it of him, dearest?", "“그분에게서 빌리면 왜 안 되나요, 여보?"], ["Surely it would not be so much for him to lend.”", "그분이 빌려주기에 그렇게 큰 액수도 아닐 텐데요.”"]], [["“I could not do it.", "“나는 그럴 수 없어."], ["Think of Lucy, and how she stands with him.", "루시를 생각해 봐. 루시가 그와 어떤 처지에 놓여 있는지를."], ["Besides I have already had words with Lufton about Sowerby and his money matters.", "게다가 사워비와 그 돈 문제를 두고 러프턴과는 이미 말이 오간 일이 있어."], ["He thinks that I am to blame, and he would tell me so; and then there would be sharp things said between us.", "그는 내 탓이라고 여기고 있고, 나에게 그렇게 말할 거야. 그러면 우리 사이에 날 선 말이 오가게 되지."], ["He would advance me the money if I pressed for it, but he would do so in a way that would make it impossible that I should take it.”", "내가 다그치면 그는 돈을 대 주기는 할 거야. 그러나 내가 그것을 받을 수 없게 만드는 식으로 대 주겠지.”"]], [["There was nothing more then to be said.", "그러고는 더 할 말이 없었다."], ["If she had had her own way Mrs. Robarts would have gone at once to Lady Lufton, but she could not induce her husband to sanction such a proceeding.", "제 뜻대로 할 수 있었다면 로바츠 부인은 당장 레이디 러프턴에게 갔을 것이나, 그렇게 하도록 남편을 움직일 수는 없었다."], ["The objection to seeking assistance from her ladyship was as strong as that which prevailed as to her son.", "마님에게 도움을 청하는 데 대한 반대는 그 아들에게 청하는 데 대한 반대만큼이나 굳셌다."], ["There had already been some little beginning of ill-feeling, and under such circumstances it was impossible to ask for pecuniary assistance.", "이미 언짢은 감정이 조금 싹터 있었고, 그런 형편에서 돈의 도움을 청하기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Fanny, however, had a prophetic assurance that assistance out of these difficulties must in the end come to them from that quarter, or not come at all; and she would fain, had she been allowed, make everything known at the big house.", "그러나 패니에게는, 이 곤경에서 벗어나게 해 줄 도움이 끝내는 그쪽에서 오거나 아니면 아예 오지 않을 것이라는 예언 같은 확신이 있었다. 그리고 허락만 받았다면 그녀는 기꺼이 큰 집에 모든 것을 알렸을 것이다."]], [["On the following morning they breakfasted at the usual hour, but in great sadness.", "이튿날 아침 그들은 여느 때와 같은 시각에 아침을 먹었으나 크게 침울했다."], ["A maid-servant, whom Mrs. Robarts had brought with her when she married, told her that a rumour of what was to happen had reached the kitchen.", "로바츠 부인이 혼인할 때 데려온 하녀가, 무슨 일이 벌어지려 하는지에 대한 소문이 부엌에까지 이르렀다고 알렸다."], ["Stubbs, the groom, had been in Barchester on the preceding day, and, according to his account—so said Mary—everybody in the city was talking about it.", "마부 스텁스가 전날 바체스터에 가 있었는데, 그의 말로는—메리가 그렇게 전했다—온 도시가 그 이야기를 하고 있다는 것이었다."], ["“Never mind, Mary,” said Mrs.", "“마음 쓰지 말아라, 메리.”"], ["Robarts, and Mary replied, “Oh, no, of course not, ma’am.”", "로바츠 부인이 말했고, 메리는 “아이고, 예, 그럼요, 마님.” 하고 대답했다."]], [["In these days Mrs.", "그즈음 로바츠 부인은 여느 때라면 몹시 바빴다."], ["Robarts was ordinarily very busy, seeing that there were six children in the house, four of whom had come to her but ill supplied with infantine belongings; and now, as usual, she went about her work immediately after breakfast.", "집에 아이가 여섯이나 있었고, 그 가운데 넷은 아기에게 필요한 것들을 변변히 갖추지 못한 채로 그녀에게 온 아이들이었으니 그럴 수밖에 없었다. 그래서 지금도 여느 때처럼 아침을 먹고 나자 곧 일을 하러 나섰다."], ["But she moved about the house very slowly, and was almost unable to give her orders to the servants, and spoke sadly to the children who hung about her wondering what was the matter.", "그러나 그녀는 집 안을 아주 느리게 돌아다녔고, 하인들에게 지시를 내리는 것조차 거의 하지 못했으며, 무슨 일인가 하고 제 곁에 매달리는 아이들에게도 슬픈 목소리로 말했다."], ["Her husband at the same time took himself to his book-room, but when there did not attempt any employment.", "남편은 그와 같은 시각에 서재로 들어갔으나, 들어가서는 아무 일에도 손을 대려 하지 않았다."], ["He thrust his hands into his pockets, and, leaning against the fire-place, fixed his eyes upon the table before him without looking at anything that was on it; it was impossible for him to betake himself to his work.", "그는 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 난로에 기대어 서서, 눈길을 앞의 탁자에 붙박아 두었으나 그 위에 놓인 무엇도 보고 있지 않았다. 제 일에 몸을 붙인다는 것은 그에게 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Remember what is the ordinary labour of a clergyman in his study, and think how fit he must have been for such employment!", "성직자가 서재에서 하는 일이 보통 어떤 것인지를 떠올려 보시라. 그리고 그가 그런 일에 얼마나 알맞은 상태였을지 생각해 보시라!"], ["What would have been the nature of a sermon composed at such a moment, and with what satisfaction could he have used the sacred volume in referring to it for his arguments?", "그런 순간에 지어진 설교가 어떤 것이 되었겠으며, 그가 논거를 찾느라 그 거룩한 책을 뒤적이면서 어떤 흡족함을 느낄 수 있었겠는가? 이 점에서 그는 아내보다 형편이 나빴다."], ["He, in this respect, was worse off than his wife; she did employ herself, but he stood there without moving, doing nothing, with fixed eyes, thinking what men would say of him.", "그녀는 그래도 몸을 놀렸으나, 그는 꼼짝도 않고 아무것도 하지 않은 채 눈길을 붙박고 서서, 사람들이 자기를 두고 무슨 말을 할까만 생각하고 있었다."]], [["Luckily for him this state of suspense was not long, for within half an hour of his leaving the breakfast-table the footman knocked at his door—that footman with whom at the beginning of his difficulties he had made up his mind to dispense, but who had been kept on because of the Barchester prebend.", "그로서는 다행히도 이 애타는 기다림은 길지 않았다. 아침 식탁에서 물러난 지 반 시간도 되지 않아 하인이 그의 방 문을 두드렸으니 말이다. 곤경이 시작될 무렵 그가 내보내기로 마음을 굳혔던 바로 그 하인이었으나, 바체스터 참사회원 자리 때문에 그대로 두고 있던 사람이었다."]], [["“If you please, your reverence, there are two men outside,”", "“목사님, 아룁니다만, 바깥에 사내 둘이 와 있습니다요.”"], ["said the footman.", "하인이 말했다."]], [["Two men!", "사내 둘!"], ["Mark knew well enough what men they were, but he could hardly take the coming of two such men to his quiet country parsonage quite as a matter of course.", "마크는 그들이 어떤 사내들인지 넉넉히 알고 있었으나, 그런 사내 둘이 제 조용한 시골 목사관에 찾아온 것을 아주 당연한 일로 받아들일 수는 없었다."]], [["“Who are they, John?”", "“누구인가, 존?”"], ["said he, not wishing any answer, but because the question was forced upon him.", "그가 말했다. 대답을 바라서가 아니라 물음이 저절로 밀려 나왔기 때문이다."]], [["“I’m afeard they’re—bailiffs, sir.”", "“아무래도—집행관인 것 같습니다요, 나리.”"]], [["“Very well, John; that will do; of course they must do what they please about the place.”", "“되었다, 존. 그만하면 되었다. 물론 저들은 이 집에서 저들 하고 싶은 대로 해야 하겠지.”"]], [["And then, when the servant left him, he still stood without moving, exactly as he had stood before.", "그러고서 하인이 물러난 뒤에도 그는 여전히 꼼짝도 않고, 앞서 서 있던 그대로 서 있었다."], ["There he remained for ten minutes, but the time went by very slowly.", "그는 십 분을 그대로 있었으나 시간은 아주 느리게 갔다."], ["When about noon some circumstance told him what was the hour, he was astonished to find that the day had not nearly passed away.", "정오쯤에 무슨 낌새로 지금이 몇 시인지를 알게 되었을 때, 그는 그날이 아직 지나가기에는 멀었다는 것에 놀랐다."]], [["And then another tap was struck on the door,—a sound which he well recognized,—and his wife crept silently into the room.", "그러고서 또 한 번 문에 두드리는 소리가 났다. 그가 익히 아는 소리였다."], ["She came close up to him before she spoke, and put her arm within his:", "그리고 아내가 소리 없이 방 안으로 들어왔다. 그녀는 말을 하기 전에 그의 바로 앞까지 다가와 그의 팔에 제 팔을 끼웠다."]], [["“Mark,”", "“마크,” 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “the men are here; they are in the yard.”", "“그 사람들이 왔어요. 마당에 있어요.”"]], [["“I know it,”", "“알고 있어.”"], ["he answered gruffly.", "그가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다."]], [["“Will it be better that you should see them, dearest?”", "“당신이 만나 보는 편이 나을까요, 여보?”"]], [["“See them; no; what good can I do by seeing them? But I shall see them soon enough; they will be here, I suppose, in a few minutes.”", "“만나? 아니. 만나서 내가 무슨 도움이 되겠어? 하지만 곧 넉넉히 보게 될 거야. 몇 분 안에 여기로 오겠지.”"]], [["“They are taking an inventory, cook says; they are in the stable now.”", "“요리사 말로는 목록을 뜨고 있대요. 지금은 마구간에 있어요.”"]], [["“Very well; they must do as they please; I cannot help them.”", "“되었어. 저들은 저들 하고 싶은 대로 할 수밖에. 나로서는 어쩔 수 없는 일이야.”"]], [["“Cook says that if they are allowed their meals and some beer, and if nobody takes anything away, they will be quite civil.”", "“요리사 말로는, 저 사람들에게 끼니와 맥주를 주고 아무도 무엇을 들고 나가지만 않으면 아주 점잖게 굴 거라고 해요.”"]], [["“Civil!", "“점잖게라니!"], ["But what does it matter?", "하지만 그게 무슨 상관이야?"], ["Let them eat and drink what they please, as long as the food lasts.", "먹을 것이 남아 있는 동안은 저들 먹고 마시고 싶은 대로 하게 두어."], ["I don’t suppose the butcher will send you more.”", "정육점에서 더 보내 주지는 않을 테니.”"]], [["“But, Mark, there’s nothing due to the butcher,—only the regular monthly bill.”", "“하지만 마크, 정육점에 밀린 것은 없어요. 달마다 치르는 셈뿐이에요.”"]], [["“Very well; you’ll see.”", "“되었어. 보면 알게 될 거야.”"]], [["“Oh, Mark, don’t look at me in that way.", "“아, 마크, 저를 그런 눈으로 보지 마세요."], ["Do not turn away from me.", "저에게서 돌아서지 마세요."], ["What is to comfort us if we do not cling to each other now?”", "이제 서로 붙들지 않는다면 무엇이 우리를 위로해 주겠어요?”"]], [["“Comfort us!", "“우리를 위로한다니!"], ["God help you!", "하느님이 당신을 도우시기를!"], ["I wonder, Fanny, that you can bear to stay in the room with me.”", "패니, 당신이 나와 한 방에 있는 것을 견딜 수 있다는 게 나는 놀라워.”"]], [["“Mark, dearest Mark, my own dear, dearest husband!", "“마크, 우리 마크, 내 귀한, 내 가장 귀한 남편!"], ["who is to be true to you, if I am not?", "제가 아니라면 누가 당신에게 참되겠어요?"], ["You shall not turn from me.", "당신은 저에게서 돌아서면 안 돼요."], ["How can anything like this make a difference between you and me?”", "이런 일이 어떻게 당신과 저 사이를 갈라놓을 수 있겠어요?”"], ["And then she threw her arms round his neck and embraced him.", "그러고서 그녀는 두 팔을 그의 목에 둘러 그를 껴안았다."]], [["It was a terrible morning to him, and one of which every incident will dwell on his memory to the last day of his life.", "그에게는 끔찍한 아침이었고, 그 아침의 모든 일이 목숨이 다하는 날까지 그의 기억에 붙어 있게 될 그런 아침이었다."], ["He had been so proud in his position—had assumed to himself so prominent a standing—had contrived, by some trick which he had acquired, to carry his head so high above the heads of neighbouring parsons.", "그는 제 자리를 그토록 자랑으로 삼아 왔고, 스스로 그토록 두드러진 위치를 차지해 왔으며, 어디서 익힌 재간으로 이웃 목사들의 머리보다 제 머리를 훨씬 높이 쳐들고 다니는 데 성공해 왔다."], ["It was this that had taken him among great people, had introduced him to the Duke of Omnium, had procured for him the stall at Barchester.", "그를 큰 사람들 사이로 데려간 것도, 그를 옴니엄 공작에게 소개한 것도, 그에게 바체스터의 참사회원 자리를 얻어 준 것도 바로 그것이었다."], ["But how was he to carry his head now?", "그러나 이제 무슨 낯으로 머리를 쳐들 것인가?"], ["What would the Arabins and Grantlys say?", "애러빈네와 그랜틀리네는 무슨 말을 할 것인가?"], ["How would the bishop sneer at him, and Mrs. Proudie and her daughters tell of him in all their quarters?", "주교는 어떻게 그를 비웃을 것이며, 프라우디 부인과 그 딸들은 저들이 드는 자리마다 그를 어떻게 입에 올릴 것인가?"], ["How would Crawley look at him—Crawley, who had already once had him on the hip?", "크롤리는 그를 어떤 눈으로 볼 것인가—이미 한 번 그의 덜미를 잡았던 크롤리가?"], ["The stern severity of Crawley’s face loomed upon him now.", "크롤리의 그 냉엄한 얼굴이 이제 그의 앞에 어른거렸다."], ["Crawley, with his children half naked, and his wife a drudge, and himself half starved, had never had a bailiff in his house at Hogglestock!", "아이들은 반쯤 헐벗고 아내는 종처럼 일하고 자신은 반쯤 굶주리면서도, 크롤리는 호글스톡의 제 집에 집행관을 한 번도 들인 적이 없었다!"], ["And then his own curate, Evans, whom he had patronized, and treated almost as a dependant—how was he to look his curate in the face and arrange with him for the sacred duties of the next Sunday?", "그리고 그가 돌보아 주고 거의 딸린 사람처럼 대해 온 제 부목사 에번스는—다음 주일의 거룩한 직무를 두고 그 부목사의 얼굴을 어떻게 마주 보며 의논할 것인가?"]], [["His wife still stood by him, gazing into his face; and as he looked at her and thought of her misery, he could not control his heart with reference to the wrongs which Sowerby had heaped on him.", "아내는 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 여전히 그의 곁에 서 있었다. 그리고 그녀를 보고 그녀의 괴로움을 생각하니, 사워비가 자기 위에 쌓아 올린 그 부당한 짓들에 대해서는 마음을 다스릴 수가 없었다."], ["It was Sowerby’s falsehood and Sowerby’s fraud which had brought upon him and his wife this terrible anguish.", "그와 그의 아내에게 이 끔찍한 고통을 안긴 것은 사워비의 거짓말이요 사워비의 사기였다."], ["“If there be justice on earth he will suffer for it yet,”", "“이 땅에 의로움이 있다면 그는 그 값을 치르게 될 거야.”"], ["he said at last, not speaking intentionally to his wife, but unable to repress his feelings.", "그가 마침내 말했다. 아내에게 들려주려고 한 말은 아니었으나 감정을 누를 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["“Do not wish him evil, Mark; you may be sure he has his own sorrows.”", "“그에게 화가 내리기를 바라지 마세요, 마크. 그에게도 그만의 슬픔이 있을 거예요.”"]], [["“His own sorrows!", "“그만의 슬픔이라니!"], ["No; he is callous to such misery as this.", "아니야. 그는 이만한 괴로움 따위에는 아무 느낌도 없는 사람이야."], ["He has become so hardened in dishonesty that all this is mirth to him.", "부정직에 그토록 무디어져서 이 모든 일이 그에게는 웃음거리일 뿐이야."], ["If there be punishment in heaven for falsehood—”", "거짓말에 하늘의 벌이 있다면—”"]], [["“Oh, Mark, do not curse him!”", "“아, 마크, 그를 저주하지 마세요!”"]], [["“How am I to keep myself from cursing when I see what he has brought upon you?”", "“그가 당신에게 무엇을 가져다주었는지를 보면서 어떻게 저주하지 않을 수 있겠어?”"]], [["“‘Vengeance is mine, saith the Lord,’”", "“‘원수 갚는 것은 내게 있으니, 주께서 이르시느니라.’” 젊은 아내가 대답했다."], ["answered the young wife, not with solemn, preaching accent, as though bent on reproof, but with the softest whisper into his ear.", "꾸짖으려 마음먹은 사람처럼 엄숙한 설교조로가 아니라, 그의 귀에 가장 부드러운 속삭임으로였다. “그 일은 그분께 맡겨요, 마크."], ["“Leave that to Him, Mark; and for us, let us pray that He may soften the hearts of us all;—of him who has caused us to suffer, and of our own.”", "그리고 우리로서는, 그분이 우리 모두의 마음을 부드럽게 해 주시기를 빌기로 해요. 우리를 괴로움에 빠뜨린 그 사람의 마음도, 우리 자신의 마음도요.”"]], [["Mark was not called upon to reply to this, for he was again disturbed by a servant at the door.", "마크는 이 말에 대답할 겨를이 없었다. 다시 문에서 하인이 그를 어지럽혔기 때문이다."], ["It was the cook this time herself, who had come with a message from the men of the law.", "이번에는 요리사가 몸소 와서 법 쪽 사람들의 전갈을 가져왔다."], ["And she had come, be it remembered, not from any necessity that she as cook should do this line of work; for the footman, or Mrs.", "그리고 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 그녀가 요리사로서 이런 일을 해야 할 필요가 있어서 온 것이 아니라는 점이다."], ["Robarts’ maid, might have come as well as she.", "하인이든 로바츠 부인의 몸종이든 그녀만큼 얼마든지 올 수 있었으니 말이다."], ["But when things are out of course servants are always out of course also.", "그러나 일이 제 궤도에서 벗어나면 하인들도 늘 제 궤도에서 벗어난다."], ["As a rule, nothing will induce a butler to go into a stable, or persuade a housemaid to put her hand to a frying-pan.", "여느 때라면 집사를 마구간으로 들여보낼 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없고, 하녀에게 프라이팬을 잡게 할 수 있는 것도 없다."], ["But now that this new excitement had come upon the household—seeing that the bailiffs were in possession, and that the chattels were being entered in a catalogue, everybody was willing to do everything—everything but his or her own work.", "그러나 이제 이 새로운 흥분이 집안에 닥쳐 놓으니—집행관들이 집을 차지하고 앉았고 가재가 하나하나 명부에 올라가고 있으니—누구나 무슨 일이든 하려 들었다. 제 일만 빼놓고 무슨 일이든."], ["The gardener was looking after the dear children; the nurse was doing the rooms before the bailiffs should reach them; the groom had gone into the kitchen to get their lunch ready for them; and the cook was walking about with an inkstand, obeying all the orders of these great potentates.", "정원사는 귀한 아이들을 보고 있었고, 유모는 집행관들이 이르기 전에 방들을 치우고 있었고, 마부는 그들의 점심을 준비하러 부엌에 들어가 있었고, 요리사는 잉크병을 들고 돌아다니며 이 대단하신 권세가들의 분부를 죄다 받들고 있었다."], ["As far as the servants were concerned, it may be a question whether the coming of the bailiffs had not hitherto been regarded as a treat.", "하인들만 두고 말한다면, 집행관들이 온 일이 여태까지는 오히려 즐거운 구경으로 여겨지지 않았을까 물어볼 만하다."]], [["“If you please, ma’am,”", "“마님, 아룁니다만,”"], ["said Jemima cook, “they wishes to know in which room you’d be pleased to have the inmin-tory took fust.", "제마이마 요리사가 말했다. “저 사람들이 어느 방에서 먼저 목로록을 뜨면 좋으시겠는지 알고 싶다고 합니다요."], ["’Cause, ma’am, they wouldn’t disturb you nor master more than can be avoided.", "저이들 말이, 마님이나 나리를 어쩔 수 없는 만큼보다 더 어지럽히지는 않겠다고요."], ["For their line of life, ma’am, they is very civil—very civil indeed.”", "저이들 하는 일치고는요, 마님, 참 점잖습니다요—참말 점잖습니다요.”"]], [["“I suppose they may go into the drawing-room,”", "“응접실에 들어가도 되겠지.”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts, in a sad low voice.", "로바츠 부인이 슬프고 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["All nice women are proud of their drawing-rooms, and she was very proud of hers.", "점잖은 여인이라면 누구나 제 응접실을 자랑으로 삼는 법이고, 그녀도 제 응접실을 크게 자랑으로 삼고 있었다."], ["It had been furnished when money was plenty with them, immediately after their marriage, and everything in it was pretty, good, and dear to her.", "그 방은 그들에게 돈이 넉넉했던 때, 혼인한 바로 뒤에 꾸민 것이어서 그 안의 모든 것이 곱고 좋고 그녀에게 소중했다."], ["O ladies, who have drawing-rooms in which the things are pretty, good, and dear to you, think of what it would be to have two bailiffs rummaging among them with pen and inkhorn, making a catalogue preparatory to a sheriff’s auction; and all without fault or extravagance of your own!", "곱고 좋고 소중한 것들이 놓인 응접실을 가진 부인들이여, 집행관 둘이 펜과 잉크통을 들고 그 사이를 헤집으며 집행 경매에 앞서 명부를 만든다는 것이 어떤 일일지 생각해 보시라. 게다가 그것이 제 잘못도 제 사치도 아닌 탓으로 그리 된다면!"], ["There were things there that had been given to her by Lady Lufton, by Lady Meredith, and other friends, and the idea did occur to her that it might be possible to save them from contamination; but she would not say a word, lest by so saying she might add to Mark’s misery.", "거기에는 레이디 러프턴이 준 것도, 레이디 메러디스가 준 것도, 다른 벗들이 준 것도 있었으니, 그것들만이라도 이 더럽힘에서 건져 낼 수 있지 않을까 하는 생각이 그녀에게 떠오르기는 했다. 그러나 그런 말을 하면 그 말로 마크의 괴로움을 더하게 될까 하여 그녀는 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["“And then the dining-room,”", "“그다음에는 식당이고요.”"], ["said Jemima cook, in a tone almost of elation.", "제마이마 요리사가 거의 신이 난 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Yes; if they please.”", "“그래. 저들이 그러고 싶다면.”"]], [["“And then master’s book-room here; or perhaps the bedrooms, if you and master be still here.”", "“그다음에는 여기 나리 서재이고요. 아니면 마님과 나리께서 그때도 여기 계시면 침실들이고요.”"]], [["“Any way they please, cook; it does not much signify,”", "“저들 하고 싶은 대로 하라지, 요리사. 그것이야 대단할 것도 없으니.”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."], ["But for some days after that Jemima was by no means a favourite with her.", "그러나 그 뒤 며칠 동안 제마이마는 그녀에게 도무지 마음에 드는 사람이 아니었다."]], [["The cook was hardly out of the room before a quick footstep was heard on the gravel before the window, and the hall door was immediately opened.", "요리사가 방에서 나가기가 무섭게 창 앞 자갈길에서 빠른 발소리가 들렸고, 곧 현관문이 열렸다."]], [["“Where is your master?”", "“주인은 어디 있나?”"], ["said the well-known voice of Lord Lufton; and then in half a minute he also was in the book-room.", "익히 아는 러프턴 경의 목소리였다. 그러고서 반 분도 되지 않아 그도 서재에 들어와 있었다."]], [["“Mark, my dear fellow, what’s all this?”", "“마크, 이게 다 무슨 일인가?”"], ["said he, in a cheery tone and with a pleasant face.", "그가 활기찬 목소리로, 밝은 얼굴로 말했다."], ["“Did not you know that I was here?", "“내가 와 있는 것을 몰랐나?"], ["I came down yesterday; landed from Hamburg only yesterday morning.", "어제 내려왔네. 함부르크에서 배를 댄 것이 어제 아침이야."], ["How do you do, Mrs. Robarts?", "그동안 잘 지내셨어요, 로바츠 부인?"], ["This is a terrible bore, isn’t it?”", "참 딱한 일이 되었지요?”"]], [["Robarts, at the first moment, hardly knew how to speak to his old friend.", "로바츠는 첫 순간에는 오랜 벗에게 어떻게 말을 붙여야 할지조차 알지 못했다."], ["He was struck dumb by the disgrace of his position; the more so as his misfortune was one which it was partly in the power of Lord Lufton to remedy.", "제 처지의 부끄러움에 그는 말문이 막혔다. 더구나 제 불운은 러프턴 경이 얼마쯤은 풀어 줄 수 있는 것이었기에 더욱 그러했다."], ["He had never yet borrowed money since he had filled a man’s position, but he had had words about money with the young peer, in which he knew that his friend had wronged him; and for this double reason he was now speechless.", "그는 한 사람의 몫을 하게 된 뒤로 돈을 빌려 본 적이 없었으나, 그 젊은 귀족과 돈을 두고 말이 오간 일이 있었고 그때 벗이 자기에게 부당했다는 것을 알고 있었다. 이 두 겹의 이유로 그는 이제 한마디도 하지 못했다."]], [["“Mr. Sowerby has betrayed him,”", "“사워비 씨가 그이를 배신했습니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts, wiping the tears from her eyes.", "로바츠 부인이 눈물을 닦으며 말했다."], ["Hitherto she had said no word against Sowerby, but now it was necessary to defend her husband.", "여태까지 그녀는 사워비를 두고 한마디도 하지 않았으나, 이제는 남편을 지켜야 했다."]], [["“No doubt about it.", "“두말할 것도 없지."], ["I believe he has always betrayed every one who has ever trusted him.", "그는 자기를 믿은 사람이면 누구든 언제나 배신해 왔다고 나는 보네."], ["I told you what he was, some time since; did I not?", "얼마 전에 그가 어떤 사람인지 자네에게 말했지, 그러지 않았나?"], ["But, Mark, why on earth have you let it go so far as this?", "그런데 마크, 자네는 어쩌자고 일을 이렇게까지 오게 두었나?"], ["Would not Forrest help you?”", "포레스트가 도와주지 않았나?”"]], [["“Mr. Forrest wanted him to sign more bills, and he would not do that,”", "“포레스트 씨는 그이가 어음에 더 서명하기를 바랐지만 그이는 그러려 하지 않았습니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts, sobbing.", "로바츠 부인이 흐느끼며 말했다."]], [["“Bills are like dram-drinking,”", "“어음이란 독한 술을 홀짝이는 것과 같지.”"], ["said the discreet young lord: “when one once begins, it is very hard to leave off.", "사려 깊은 젊은 나리가 말했다. “한번 시작하면 그만두기가 아주 어려운 것이야."], ["Is it true that the men are here now, Mark?”", "마크, 그 사람들이 지금 여기 와 있다는 게 참말인가?”"]], [["“Yes, they are in the next room.”", "“그렇네. 옆방에 있네.”"]], [["“What, in the drawing-room?”", "“뭐, 응접실에?”"]], [["“They are making out a list of the things,”", "“물건 목록을 적고 있습니다.”"], ["said Mrs. Robarts.", "로바츠 부인이 말했다."]], [["“We must stop that at any rate,”", "“그것만은 어떻게든 그만두게 해야지.”"], ["said his lordship, walking off towards the scene of the operations; and as he left the room Mrs.", "나리는 이렇게 말하며 일이 벌어지고 있는 자리로 걸어 나갔다."], ["Robarts followed him, leaving her husband by himself.", "그가 방을 나가자 로바츠 부인도 그를 따라 나가, 남편을 혼자 남겨 두었다."]], [["“Why did you not send down to my mother?”", "“어째서 우리 어머니께 사람을 내려보내지 않았습니까?”"], ["said he, speaking hardly above a whisper, as they stood together in the hall.", "현관에 함께 서서 그가 속삭임보다 겨우 조금 큰 소리로 말했다."]], [["“He would not let me.”", "“그이가 그러지 못하게 했습니다.”"]], [["“But why not go yourself?", "“하지만 부인이 몸소 가시지 않은 것은 왜입니까?"], ["or why not have written to me,—considering how intimate we are?”", "아니면 우리가 이만큼 가까운 사이인데 나에게 편지를 쓰지 않은 것은 왜입니까?”"]], [["Mrs.", "러프턴 경과 루시 사이의 그 특별한 가까움 때문에, 설령 달리 그럴 수 있었다 하더라도 그렇게 할 수는 없었다는 것을 로바츠 부인은 그에게 설명할 수 없었다."], ["Robarts could not explain to him that the peculiar intimacy between him and Lucy must have hindered her from doing so, even if otherwise it might have been possible; but she felt such was the case.", "그러나 그녀는 그것이 사실임을 느꼈다."]], [["“Well, my men, this is bad work you’re doing here,”", "“자, 이 사람들, 여기서 참 못된 일을 하고 있군.”"], ["said he, walking into the drawing-room.", "그가 응접실로 걸어 들어가며 말했다."], ["Whereupon the cook curtseyed low, and the bailiffs, knowing his lordship, stopped from their business and put their hands to their foreheads.", "그러자 요리사는 무릎을 깊이 굽혀 인사했고, 집행관들은 나리를 알아보고 일손을 멈추고 이마에 손을 올렸다."], ["“You must stop this, if you please,—at once.", "“미안하지만 이 일은 그만두어야 하네—당장."], ["Come, let’s go out into the kitchen, or some place outside.", "자, 부엌으로 나가지. 아니면 바깥 어디로든."], ["I don’t like to see you here with your big boots and the pen and ink among the furniture.”", "그 큰 장화를 신고 가구들 사이에서 펜이며 잉크를 들고 있는 걸 보고 싶지 않네.”"]], [["“We ain’t a-done no harm, my lord, so please your lordship,”", "“저희는 아무 해도 끼치지 않았습니다요, 나리, 황송하오나.”"], ["said Jemima cook.", "제마이마 요리사가 말했다."]], [["“And we is only a-doing our bounden dooties,”", "“저희야 마땅히 할 소임을 하고 있을 뿐입니다요.”"], ["said one of the bailiffs.", "집행관 하나가 말했다."]], [["“As we is sworn to do, so please your lordship,”", "“저희가 그리하기로 맹세를 한 대로요, 나리, 황송하오나.”"], ["said the other.", "다른 하나가 말했다."]], [["“And is wery sorry to be unconwenient, my lord, to any gen’leman or lady as is a gen’leman or lady.", "“그리고 신사분이나 숙녀분한테, 참말 신사고 숙녀인 분들한테 불펀을 끼쳐서 참 안됐습니다요, 나리."], ["But accidents will happen, and then what can the likes of us do?”", "하지만 일이란 게 생기고 마는 법인데, 저희 같은 것들이 어쩌겠습니까요?”"], ["said the first.", "앞의 하나가 말했다."]], [["“Because we is sworn, my lord,” said the second.", "“저희는 맹세를 한 몸이니까요, 나리.”"], ["But, nevertheless, in spite of their oaths, and in spite also of the stern necessity which they pleaded, they ceased their operations at the instance of the peer.", "뒤의 하나가 말했다. 그러나 그럼에도, 저들의 맹세에도, 또 저들이 내세운 그 엄한 필요에도 아랑곳없이, 그들은 그 귀족의 말 한마디에 일손을 놓았다."], ["For the name of a lord is still great in England.", "귀족의 이름은 아직도 영국에서 크기 때문이다."]], [["“And now leave this, and let Mrs. Robarts go into her drawing-room.”", "“그러면 이제 이 방을 비우고, 로바츠 부인이 제 응접실에 들어가게 하게.”"]], [["“And, please your lordship, what is we to do?", "“나리, 황송하오나, 그러면 저희는 어떻게 되는 겁니까요?"], ["Who is we to look to?”", "저희는 누구를 바라보아야 합니까요?”"]], [["In satisfying them absolutely on this point Lord Lufton had to use more than his influence as a peer.", "이 점에서 그들을 온전히 만족시키려면 러프턴 경은 귀족으로서의 위세만으로는 모자랐다. 펜과 종이가 있어야 했다."], ["It was necessary that he should have pen and paper.", "그러나 펜과 종이로 그는 그들을 만족시켰다."], ["But with pen and paper he did satisfy them;—satisfy them so far that they agreed to return to Stubbs’ room, the former hospital, due stipulation having been made for the meals and beer, and there await the order to evacuate the premises which would no doubt, under his lordship’s influence, reach them on the following day.", "그들이 스텁스의 방, 곧 전에 병든 말을 두던 칸으로 물러가기로 하는 데까지 만족시킨 것이다. 끼니와 맥주에 관한 약조도 마땅히 붙였고, 거기서 집을 비우라는 명령을 기다리기로 했으니, 그 명령은 나리의 위세로 이튿날 틀림없이 그들에게 이를 것이었다."], ["The meaning of all which was that Lord Lufton had undertaken to bear upon his own shoulder the whole debt due by Mr. Robarts.", "이 모든 것의 뜻은, 러프턴 경이 로바츠 씨가 진 빚 전부를 제 어깨에 짊어지기로 했다는 것이었다."]], [["And then he returned to the book-room where Mark was still standing almost on the spot in which he had placed himself immediately after breakfast.", "그러고서 그는 서재로 돌아왔다. 마크는 아침을 먹은 바로 뒤에 제 몸을 세워 놓았던 그 자리에 거의 그대로 서 있었다."], ["Mrs. Robarts did not return, but went up among the children to counter-order such directions as she had given for the preparation of the nursery for the Philistines.", "로바츠 부인은 돌아오지 않고 아이들에게로 올라갔다."], ["“Mark,”", "블레셋 사람들을 맞으려고 아이 방을 치우라며 내렸던 지시를 거두려는 것이었다."], ["he said, “do not trouble yourself about this more than you can help.", "“마크,” 그가 말했다."], ["The men have ceased doing anything, and they shall leave the place to-morrow morning.”", "“이 일로 어쩔 수 없는 것보다 더 마음을 쓰지 말게. 저 사람들은 손을 놓았고, 내일 아침에는 이 집을 떠날 걸세.”"]], [["“And how will the money—be paid?”", "“그러면 그 돈은—어떻게 치러지는 건가?”"], ["said the poor clergyman.", "딱한 성직자가 말했다."]], [["“Do not bother yourself about that at present.", "“지금은 그런 것으로 속을 끓이지 말게."], ["It shall so be managed that the burden shall fall ultimately on yourself—not on any one else.", "그 짐이 끝내는 자네 자신에게 떨어지도록—다른 누구에게도 아니라—그렇게 되도록 해 놓을 걸세."], ["But I am sure it must be a comfort to you to know that your wife need not be driven out of her drawing-room.”", "하지만 자네 부인이 제 응접실에서 쫓겨나지 않아도 된다는 것은 자네에게도 위로가 될 거라고 나는 믿네.”"]], [["“But, Lufton, I cannot allow you—after what has passed—and at the present moment—”", "“하지만 러프턴, 나는 자네에게 그렇게 하게 할 수 없네—그런 일이 있었는데—게다가 이런 때에—”"]], [["“My dear fellow, I know all about it, and I am coming to that just now.", "“여보게, 나도 다 아네. 그 얘기를 이제 하려던 것이야."], ["You have employed Curling, and he shall settle it; and upon my word, Mark, you shall pay the bill.", "자네는 컬링을 세웠으니 컬링이 마무리할 것이고, 맹세하지만 마크, 그 어음은 자네가 갚는 걸세."], ["But, for the present emergency, the money is at my banker’s.”", "다만 이번 급한 일에는 내 은행에 돈이 있네.”"]], [["“But, Lufton—”", "“하지만 러프턴—”"]], [["“And to deal honestly, about Curling’s bill I mean, it ought to be as much my affair as your own.", "“그리고 정직하게 말하자면, 컬링의 셈 말인데, 그것은 자네 일인 만큼 내 일이기도 해야 하네."], ["It was I that brought you into this mess with Sowerby, and I know now how unjust about it I was to you up in London.", "자네를 사워비와 이 난장판으로 끌어들인 것은 나였고, 런던에서 그 일로 내가 자네에게 얼마나 부당했는지를 이제는 아네."], ["But the truth is that Sowerby’s treachery had nearly driven me wild.", "하지만 사실은 사워비의 배신에 내가 거의 미칠 지경이었던 것이야."], ["It has done the same to you since, I have no doubt.”", "그 뒤로 자네도 같은 일을 당했겠지. 그건 의심할 것도 없네.”"]], [["“He has ruined me,”", "“그는 나를 망쳐 놓았네.”"], ["said Robarts.", "로바츠가 말했다."]], [["“No, he has not done that.", "“아니야, 그가 그렇게까지 하지는 못했네."], ["No thanks to him though; he would not have scrupled to do it had it come in his way.", "그가 봐준 덕은 아니지만. 그럴 기회가 왔다면 그는 거리낌 없이 그렇게 했을 사람이야."], ["The fact is, Mark, that you and I cannot conceive the depth of fraud in such a man as that.", "사실은 말이지 마크, 그런 사람의 사기가 얼마나 깊은지는 자네도 나도 짐작할 수 없어."], ["He is always looking for money; I believe that in all his hours of most friendly intercourse,—when he is sitting with you over your wine, and riding beside you in the field,—he is still thinking how he can make use of you to tide him over some difficulty.", "그는 언제나 돈을 찾고 있네. 벗으로서 가장 다정하게 어울리는 그 모든 시간에도—자네와 함께 술잔을 앞에 놓고 앉아 있을 때에도, 사냥터에서 자네 옆에 나란히 말을 달릴 때에도—그는 여전히 자네를 어떻게 써서 제 어떤 곤경을 넘길까를 생각하고 있다고 나는 믿네."], ["He has lived in that way till he has a pleasure in cheating, and has become so clever in his line of life that if you or I were with him again to-morrow he would again get the better of us.", "그는 그런 식으로 살아온 끝에 남을 속이는 데서 즐거움을 느끼게 되었고, 그 길에 하도 능해져서 자네나 내가 내일 다시 그와 마주 앉는다면 그는 또 우리를 이겨 놓을 걸세."], ["He is a man that must be absolutely avoided; I, at any rate, have learned to know so much.”", "반드시 아주 피해야 할 사람이야. 나는 적어도 그만큼은 알게 되었네.”"]], [["In the expression of which opinion Lord Lufton was too hard upon poor Sowerby; as indeed we are all apt to be too hard in forming an opinion upon the rogues of the world.", "이런 견해를 말하는 데서 러프턴 경은 딱한 사워비에게 너무 매정했다. 사실 우리는 누구나 세상의 악당들에 대한 견해를 세울 때 너무 매정해지기 쉽다."], ["That Mr. Sowerby had been a rogue, I cannot deny.", "사워비 씨가 악당이었다는 것은 나도 부인할 수 없다."], ["It is roguish to lie, and he had been a great liar.", "거짓말을 하는 것은 악당의 짓이고, 그는 거짓말을 크게 하는 사람이었다."], ["It is roguish to make promises which the promiser knows he cannot perform, and such had been Mr. Sowerby’s daily practice.", "하는 사람 자신이 지킬 수 없음을 아는 약속을 하는 것은 악당의 짓이고, 사워비 씨는 날마다 그것을 해 왔다."], ["It is roguish to live on other men’s money, and Mr. Sowerby had long been doing so.", "남의 돈으로 사는 것은 악당의 짓이고, 사워비 씨는 오래도록 그렇게 살아왔다."], ["It is roguish, at least so I would hold it, to deal willingly with rogues; and Mr. Sowerby had been constant in such dealings.", "악당들과 기꺼이 거래하는 것도 악당의 짓이라고—적어도 나는 그렇게 보겠다—사워비 씨는 그런 거래를 한결같이 해 왔다."], ["I do not know whether he had not at times fallen even into more palpable roguery than is proved by such practices as those enumerated.", "여기 늘어놓은 그런 짓들로 드러나는 것보다 더 뚜렷한 악당 짓에 그가 때때로 빠지지 않았는지 어떤지는 나도 모른다."], ["Though I have for him some tender feeling, knowing that there was still a touch of gentle bearing round his heart, an abiding taste for better things within him, I cannot acquit him from the great accusation.", "그의 마음자리에는 아직 신사다운 몸가짐의 기미가 남아 있었고 더 나은 것들에 대한 취향이 그 안에 머물러 있었음을 알기에 나는 그에게 얼마쯤 다정한 마음을 품고 있으나, 그 큰 죄목에서 그를 놓아줄 수는 없다."], ["But, for all that, in spite of his acknowledged roguery, Lord Lufton was too hard upon him in his judgment.", "그러나 그 모든 것에도, 인정된 그의 악당 짓에도, 러프턴 경은 그를 판단하는 데서 너무 매정했다."], ["There was yet within him the means of repentance, could a locus penitentiæ have been supplied to him.", "그의 안에는 아직 회개의 밑거리가 있었으니, 그에게 「로쿠스 페니텐티아이」가 주어질 수만 있었다면 그러했을 것이다."], ["He grieved bitterly over his own ill doings, and knew well what changes gentlehood would have demanded from him.", "그는 제가 저지른 나쁜 짓들을 두고 쓰라리게 슬퍼했고, 신사다움이 제게 무엇을 요구했을지도 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Whether or no he had gone too far for all changes—whether the locus penitentiæ was for him still a possibility—that was between him and a higher power.", "어떤 변화로도 돌이킬 수 없을 만큼 그가 너무 멀리 갔는지 어떤지—그에게 「로쿠스 페니텐티아이」가 아직 있을 수 있는 일인지 어떤지—그것은 그와 더 높은 힘 사이의 일이었다."]], [["“I have no one to blame but myself,”", "“나를 탓할 사람은 나밖에 없네.”"], ["said Mark, still speaking in the same heart-broken tone and with his face averted from his friend.", "마크가 여전히 같은 가슴 무너진 목소리로, 얼굴은 벗에게서 돌린 채로 말했다."]], [["The debt would now be paid, and the bailiffs would be expelled; but that would not set him right before the world.", "빚은 이제 치러질 것이고 집행관들은 쫓겨날 것이었다. 그러나 그것으로 세상 앞에서 그가 바로 세워지지는 않았다."], ["It would be known to all men—to all clergymen in the diocese—that the sheriff’s officers had been in charge of Framley Parsonage, and he could never again hold up his head in the close of Barchester.", "집행리들이 프램리 목사관을 차고 앉아 있었다는 것을 모든 사람이—교구의 모든 성직자가—알게 될 것이고, 그는 바체스터 성당 구내에서 다시는 머리를 쳐들 수 없을 것이었다."]], [["“My dear fellow, if we were all to make ourselves miserable for such a trifle as this—”", "“여보게, 우리가 다 이만한 자잘한 일로 스스로를 비참하게 만든다면—”"], ["said Lord Lufton, putting his arm affectionately on his friend’s shoulder.", "러프턴 경은 벗의 어깨에 다정하게 팔을 얹으며 말했다."]], [["“But we are not all clergymen,”", "“하지만 우리가 다 성직자는 아니지.”"], ["said Mark, and as he spoke he turned away to the window and Lord Lufton knew that the tears were on his cheek.", "마크가 말했다. 그러면서 그는 창가로 돌아섰고, 러프턴 경은 그의 뺨에 눈물이 있음을 알았다."]], [["Nothing was then said between them for some moments, after which Lord Lufton again spoke,—", "그러고서 얼마 동안 두 사람 사이에는 아무 말도 없었다. 그러다 러프턴 경이 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“Mark, my dear fellow!”", "“마크, 여보게!”"]], [["“Well,”", "“왜 그러나.”"], ["said Mark, with his face still turned towards the window.", "마크가 여전히 얼굴을 창 쪽으로 돌린 채로 말했다."]], [["“You must remember one thing; in helping you over this stile, which will be really a matter of no inconvenience to me, I have a better right than that even of an old friend; I look upon you now as my brother-in-law.”", "“한 가지는 기억해 두어야 하네. 이 울짱을 넘게 자네를 도와주는 일은 나로서는 참말 아무 불편도 아니고, 게다가 나에게는 오랜 벗이라는 것보다도 더 나은 자격이 있네. 나는 이제 자네를 처남으로 여기고 있어.”"]], [["Mark turned slowly round, plainly showing the tears upon his face.", "마크가 천천히 돌아섰다. 얼굴 위의 눈물이 그대로 드러났다."]], [["“Do you mean,”", "“그러면”"], ["said he, “that anything more has taken place?”", "하고 그가 말했다. “무슨 일이 더 있었다는 말인가?”"]], [["“I mean to make your sister my wife; she sent me word by you to say that she loved me, and I am not going to stand upon any nonsense after that.", "“나는 자네 누이를 아내로 삼을 작정이네. 그녀가 나를 사랑한다는 말을 자네를 통해 보내왔고, 그 뒤에까지 터무니없는 소리에 매여 있을 생각은 없어."], ["If she and I are both willing no one alive has a right to stand between us; and, by heavens, no one shall.", "그녀와 내가 둘 다 원한다면 살아 있는 누구도 우리 사이에 끼어들 권리가 없네. 맹세코 아무도 그러지 못할 걸세."], ["I will do nothing secretly, so I tell you that, exactly as I have told her ladyship.”", "나는 무엇도 숨어서 하지 않겠네. 그러니 마님께 말씀드린 그대로 자네에게도 이렇게 말해 두는 것이야.”"]], [["“But what does she say?”", "“그런데 마님은 무엇이라 하시는가?”"]], [["“She says nothing; but it cannot go on like that.", "“아무 말씀도 없으시네. 하지만 그렇게 갈 수는 없지."], ["My mother and I cannot live here together if she opposes me in this way.", "어머니가 이런 식으로 나를 막으신다면 어머니와 나는 여기서 함께 살 수 없어. 호글스톡으로 자네 누이를 찾아가서 그녀를 놀라게 하고 싶지는 않네."], ["I do not want to frighten your sister by going over to her at Hogglestock, but I expect you to tell her so much as I now tell you, as coming from me; otherwise she will think that I have forgotten her.”", "하지만 내가 지금 자네에게 말한 만큼은 내가 한 말로 그녀에게 전해 주기를 바라네. 그러지 않으면 그녀는 내가 자기를 잊었다고 여길 테니.”"]], [["“She will not think that.”", "“그렇게 여기지는 않을 걸세.”"]], [["“She need not; good-bye, old fellow.", "“그렇게 여길 까닭이야 없지."], ["I’ll make it all right between you and her ladyship about this affair of Sowerby’s.”", "잘 있게, 이 사람. 이 사워비 건으로 자네와 마님 사이는 내가 다 잘 풀어 놓겠네.”"]], [["And then he took his leave and walked off to settle about the payment of the money.", "그러고서 그는 작별하고 나가, 돈을 치르는 일을 마무르러 걸어갔다."]], [["“Mother,”", "“어머니,”"], ["said he to Lady Lufton that evening, “you must not bring this affair of the bailiffs up against Robarts.", "그가 그날 저녁 레이디 러프턴에게 말했다. “이 집행관 일을 두고 로바츠를 탓하지 마세요."], ["It has been more my fault than his.”", "그의 잘못보다 제 잘못이 더 큽니다.”"]], [["Hitherto not a word had been spoken between Lady Lufton and her son on the subject.", "여태까지 레이디 러프턴과 아들 사이에 이 일을 두고 오간 말은 한마디도 없었다."], ["She had heard with terrible dismay of what had happened, and had heard also that Lord Lufton had immediately gone to the parsonage.", "그녀는 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지를 끔찍한 낙담으로 들었고, 러프턴 경이 곧바로 목사관으로 갔다는 것도 들었다."], ["It was impossible, therefore, that she should now interfere.", "그러니 이제 그녀가 끼어들 수는 없었다."], ["That the necessary money would be forthcoming she was aware, but that would not wipe out the terrible disgrace attached to an execution in a clergyman’s house.", "필요한 돈이야 나오게 될 것을 그녀는 알고 있었으나, 그것으로 성직자의 집에 집행이 들어왔다는 그 끔찍한 수치가 씻기지는 않았다."], ["And then, too, he was her clergyman,—her own clergyman, selected, and appointed, and brought to Framley by herself, endowed with a wife of her own choosing, filled with good things by her own hand!", "게다가 그는 그녀의 성직자였다—그녀 자신의 성직자, 그녀가 골라 세워 프램리로 데려왔고, 그녀가 고른 아내를 붙여 주었고, 그녀 자신의 손으로 좋은 것들을 채워 준 사람이었다!"], ["It was a terrible misadventure, and she began to repent that she had ever heard the name of Robarts.", "그것은 끔찍한 사고였고, 그녀는 로바츠라는 이름을 애초에 들었다는 것 자체를 뉘우치기 시작했다."], ["She would not, however, have been slow to put forth the hand to lessen the evil by giving her own money, had this been either necessary or possible.", "그러나 제 돈을 내어 이 화를 줄이는 일이 필요하거나 될 수 있는 일이었다면 그녀는 손을 내미는 데 결코 더디지 않았을 것이다."], ["But how could she interfere between Robarts and her son, especially when she remembered the proposed connection between Lucy and Lord Lufton?", "그러나 로바츠와 제 아들 사이에 그녀가 어떻게 끼어들 수 있겠는가. 더구나 루시와 러프턴 경 사이에 이야기되던 그 인연을 떠올린다면?"]], [["“Your fault, Ludovic?”", "“네 잘못이라니, 루도빅?”"]], [["“Yes, mother.", "“예, 어머니."], ["It was I who introduced him to Mr.", "그를 사워비 씨에게 소개한 것은 저였습니다."], ["Sowerby; and, to tell the truth, I do not think he would ever have been intimate with Sowerby if I had not given him some sort of a commission with reference to money matters then pending between Mr.", "그리고 사실을 말하자면, 그때 사워비 씨와 저 사이에 걸려 있던 돈 문제를 두고 제가 그에게 무슨 심부름 같은 것을 맡기지 않았다면 그가 사워비와 가까워지는 일도 결코 없었을 겁니다."], ["Sowerby and me. They are all over now,—thanks to you, indeed.”", "그 일들은 이제 다 끝났지요—참말 어머니 덕으로요.”"]], [["“Mr. Robarts’ character as a clergyman should have kept him from such troubles, if no other feeling did so.”", "“달리 붙드는 마음이 없었다 해도, 로바츠 씨는 성직자라는 신분만으로라도 그런 곤경에 빠지지 않았어야 했어.”"]], [["“At any rate, mother, oblige me by letting it pass by.”", "“어떻든 어머니, 이 일은 그냥 넘겨 주시면 제 청을 들어주시는 것이 되겠어요.”"]], [["“Oh, I shall say nothing to him.”", "“아, 그에게는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다.”"]], [["“You had better say something to her, or otherwise it will be strange; and even to him I would say a word or two,—a word in kindness, as you so well know how.", "“부인에게는 무슨 말이든 해 주시는 편이 좋아요. 그러지 않으면 오히려 이상할 테니까요."], ["It will be easier to him in that way, than if you were to be altogether silent.”", "그리고 그에게도 저라면 한두 마디는 하겠어요—어머니가 그토록 잘 아시는 그 다정한 한마디를요. 어머니가 아주 잠잠히 계시는 것보다 그편이 그에게 더 편할 겁니다.”"]], [["No further conversation took place between them at the time, but later in the evening she brushed her hand across her son’s forehead, sweeping the long silken hairs into their place, as she was wont to do when moved by any special feeling of love.", "그때 두 사람 사이에 더 이야기가 이어지지는 않았으나, 저녁이 깊어서 그녀는 손을 아들의 이마에 스쳐 길고 부드러운 머리카락을 제자리로 쓸어 넘겼다. 사랑의 어떤 각별한 마음이 움직일 때 늘 하던 대로였다."], ["“Ludovic,”", "“루도빅,”"], ["she said, “no one, I think, has so good a heart as you.", "그녀가 말했다. “내 생각에는 너만큼 마음이 착한 사람이 없다."], ["I will do exactly as you would have me about this affair of Mr.", "로바츠 씨와 그 돈 문제는 네가 바라는 그대로 하겠다.”"], ["Robarts and the money.” And then there was nothing more said about it.", "그러고서 그 일을 두고 더 이야기된 것은 아무것도 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLV. PALACE BLESSINGS.", "# 제45장. 주교관의 축복"]], [["And now, at this period, terrible rumours found their way into Barchester, and flew about the cathedral towers and round the cathedral door; ay, and into the canons’ houses and the humbler sitting-rooms of the vicars choral.", "그런데 이 무렵 무서운 소문들이 바체스터로 흘러들어, 대성당의 탑들 사이를 날아다니고 대성당 문 앞을 감돌았다. 그렇다, 참사회원들의 집 안에까지, 그리고 성가대 사제들의 더 조촐한 거실에까지 날아들었다."], ["Whether they made their way from thence up to the bishop’s palace, or whether they descended from the palace to the close, I will not pretend to say.", "그것들이 거기에서 주교관으로 올라갔는지, 아니면 주교관에서 구내로 내려왔는지는 내가 감히 말하지 않겠다."], ["But they were shocking, unnatural, and no doubt grievous to all those excellent ecclesiastical hearts which cluster so thickly in those quarters.", "그러나 그 소문들은 사람을 놀라게 하고 사람의 도리에 어긋나는 것이었으며, 그 구역에 그토록 빽빽하게 모여 있는 저 훌륭한 교회의 심정들에게는 의심할 여지 없이 애통한 것이었다."]], [["The first of these had reference to the new prebendary, and to the disgrace which he had brought on the chapter; a disgrace, as some of them boasted, which Barchester had never known before.", "그 가운데 첫째 것은 새 참사회원과 그가 성당 참사회에 안긴 수치에 관한 것이었다. 그들 가운데 몇몇이 자랑삼아 말한 대로, 바체스터가 전에 한 번도 겪어 본 적 없는 수치라는 것이었다."], ["This, however, like most other boasts, was hardly true; for within but a very few years there had been an execution in the house of a late prebendary, old Dr.", "그러나 이 자랑도 다른 대개의 자랑처럼 참이라 하기는 어려웠다."], ["Stanhope; and on that occasion the doctor himself had been forced to fly away to Italy, starting in the night, lest he also should fall into the hands of the Philistines, as well as his chairs and tables.", "불과 몇 해 전에 작고한 참사회원 스탠호프 노박사의 집에 강제 집행이 들어온 일이 있었고, 그때 그 박사 자신도 의자며 탁자와 함께 자기까지 블레셋 사람들의 손에 떨어지지 않도록 밤중에 길을 떠나 이탈리아로 달아나야 했던 것이다."]], [["“It is a scandalous shame,”", "“참으로 망측한 창피요,” 프라우디 부인이 그 노박사를 두고서가 아니라 새로 죄를 지은 사람을 두고 말했다."], ["said Mrs.", "“참으로 망측한 창피지요."], ["Proudie, speaking not of the old doctor, but of the new offender; “a scandalous shame: and it would only serve him right if the gown were stripped from his back.”", "그 사람 등에서 법의를 벗겨 낸다 해도 마땅한 일일 뿐이오.”"]], [["“I suppose his living will be sequestrated,”", "“그 사람 성직록은 압류되겠지요,”"], ["said a young minor canon who attended much to the ecclesiastical injunctions of the lady of the diocese, and was deservedly held in high favour.", "이 교구를 다스리는 부인이 교회 일에 관하여 내리는 지시에 매우 열심히 귀 기울이는 젊은 하급 참사회원 하나가 말했으니, 그는 그만한 대접을 받을 만하게 큰 총애를 누리고 있었다."], ["If Framley were sequestrated, why should not he, as well as another, undertake the duty—with such stipend as the bishop might award?", "프램리가 압류된다면, 남이 아니라 어째서 그가 그 직무를 맡아서는 안 되겠는가—주교가 정해 줄 만한 급여를 받고서?"]], [["“I am told that he is over head and ears in debt,”", "“그 사람은 빚에 목까지 잠겼다고 들었어요,”"], ["said the future Mrs.", "앞으로 티클러 부인이 될 사람이 말했다."], ["Tickler, “and chiefly for horses which he has bought and not paid for.”", "“그리고 대개는 사들이고서 값을 치르지 않은 말 때문이라던데요.”"]], [["“I see him riding very splendid animals when he comes over for the cathedral duties,”", "“그가 대성당 일로 건너올 때 아주 훌륭한 짐승을 타고 오는 것을 저는 봅니다,”"], ["said the minor canon.", "하급 참사회원이 말했다."]], [["“The sheriff’s officers are in the house at present, I am told,”", "“지금 그 집에는 집행리들이 들어와 있다고 들었소,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["“And is not he in jail?”", "“그러면 그이는 감옥에 있지 않은가요?”"], ["said Mrs. Tickler.", "티클러 부인이 말했다."]], [["“If not, he ought to be,” said Mrs.", "“아직 아니라면, 마땅히 그리 되어야지요,”"], ["Tickler’s mother.", "티클러 부인의 어머니가 말했다."]], [["“And no doubt soon will be,”", "“그리고 의심할 것 없이 곧 그리 될 겁니다,”"], ["said the minor canon; “for I hear that he is linked up with a most discreditable gang of persons.”", "하급 참사회원이 말했다. “아주 평판이 좋지 못한 무리와 얽혀 있다고 저는 들었으니까요.”"]], [["This was what was said in the palace on that heading; and though, no doubt, more spirit and poetry was displayed there than in the houses of the less gifted clergy, this shows the manner in which the misfortune of Mr.", "그 제목을 두고 주교관에서 나온 말이 이러했다. 그리고 의심할 것 없이 그곳에서는 재주가 덜한 성직자들의 집보다 더 많은 기개와 시적 재주가 펼쳐졌겠으나, 이것으로 로바츠 씨의 불운이 일반으로 어떻게 이야기되었는지는 드러난다."], ["Robarts was generally discussed. Nor, indeed, had he deserved any better treatment at their hands.", "사실 그가 그들의 손에서 이보다 나은 대접을 받을 자격도 없었다."], ["But his name did not run the gauntlet for the usual nine days; nor, indeed, did his fame endure at its height for more than two.", "그러나 그의 이름은 흔히 그러하듯 아흐레 동안 매질의 열 사이를 지나가지는 않았다. 아니, 그의 명성이 그 절정에 머문 것은 이틀을 넘지 못했다."], ["This sudden fall was occasioned by other tidings of a still more distressing nature; by a rumour which so affected Mrs.", "이 갑작스러운 추락은 훨씬 더 사람을 괴롭게 하는 다른 소식 때문에 일어났다."], ["Proudie that it caused, as she said, her blood to creep.", "프라우디 부인이 하도 마음을 쓴 나머지, 그녀의 말로는 제 피가 스멀거리게 만든 소문 하나 때문이었다."], ["And she was very careful that the blood of others should creep also, if the blood of others was equally sensitive.", "그리고 그녀는 남들의 피도—남들의 피가 그만큼 예민하다면—스멀거리게 하려고 몹시 마음을 썼다."], ["It was said that Lord Dumbello had jilted Miss Grantly.", "덤벨로 경이 그랜틀리 양을 버렸다는 것이었다."]], [["From what adverse spot in the world these cruel tidings fell upon Barchester I have never been able to discover.", "세상의 어느 험한 구석에서 이 잔혹한 소식이 바체스터에 떨어졌는지 나는 끝내 알아낼 수 없었다."], ["We know how quickly rumour flies, making herself common through all the cities. That Mrs.", "소문이라는 것이 얼마나 빠르게 날아가 온 도시에 저를 흔한 것으로 만들어 놓는지는 우리도 알고 있다."], ["Proudie should have known more of the facts connected with the Hartletop family than any one else in Barchester was not surprising, seeing that she was so much more conversant with the great world in which such people lived.", "프라우디 부인이 하틀탑 집안에 얽힌 사실들을 바체스터의 그 누구보다 더 많이 알고 있었다는 것은 놀랄 일이 아니었으니, 그런 사람들이 사는 큰 세상 물정에 그녀가 훨씬 더 통해 있었기 때문이다."], ["She knew, and was therefore correct enough in declaring, that Lord Dumbello had already jilted one other young lady—the Lady Julia Mac Mull, to whom he had been engaged three seasons back, and that therefore his character in such matters was not to be trusted.", "그녀는 덤벨로 경이 이미 다른 젊은 아가씨 하나를 버린 적이 있다는 것을—세 사교철 전에 그와 약혼했던 레이디 줄리아 맥 멀을—알고 있었고, 그러므로 그런 일에서 그의 됨됨이는 믿을 것이 아니라고 선언한 것도 충분히 옳았다."], ["That Lady Julia had been a terrible flirt and greatly given to waltzing with a certain German count, with whom she had since gone off—that, I suppose, Mrs.", "레이디 줄리아가 무서운 희롱꾼이었고 어떤 독일인 백작과 왈츠 추기에 몹시 빠져 있었으며 그 뒤 그 백작과 함께 달아나 버렸다는 것은—큰 세상 물정에 그토록 통해 있었다지만 프라우디 부인이 몰랐던 것이라고 나는 짐작한다."], ["Proudie did not know, much as she was conversant with the great world,—seeing that she said nothing about it to any of her ecclesiastical listeners on the present occasion.", "이번 자리에서 교회에 속한 그 청중 가운데 누구에게도 그 이야기는 한마디도 하지 않았으니 말이다."]], [["“It will be a terrible warning, Mrs.", "“퀴버풀 부인, 이것은 우리 모두에게 무서운 경고가 될 것이오."], ["Quiverful, to us all; a most useful warning to us—not to trust to the things of this world.", "우리에게 아주 유익한 경고지요—이 세상의 것들을 믿지 말라는."], ["I fear they made no inquiry about this young nobleman before they agreed that his name should be linked with that of their daughter.”", "그 사람들이 딸의 이름을 그 젊은 귀족의 이름과 함께 묶기로 합의하기 전에 그 젊은 귀족에 대해 아무런 알아봄도 하지 않았을 것이 나는 걱정이오.”"], ["This she said to the wife of the present warden of Hiram’s Hospital, a lady who had received favours from her, and was therefore bound to listen attentively to her voice.", "이 말은 히램 요양원의 현 워든의 아내에게 한 것이었으니, 그 부인은 그녀에게서 은덕을 입은 바 있어 그 목소리에 주의 깊게 귀 기울여야 할 처지였다."]], [["“But I hope it may not be true,” said Mrs.", "“그러나 사실이 아니었으면 좋겠어요,”"], ["Quiverful, who, in spite of the allegiance due by her to Mrs. Proudie, had reasons of her own for wishing well to the Grantly family.", "퀴버풀 부인이 말했으니, 프라우디 부인에게 마땅히 바쳐야 할 충성이 있음에도 그랜틀리 집안이 잘되기를 바랄 만한 그녀 자신의 이유가 있었던 것이다."]], [["“I hope so, indeed,” said Mrs.", "“나도 진심으로 그러기를 바라오,”"], ["Proudie, with a slight tinge of anger in her voice; “but I fear that there is no doubt.", "프라우디 부인이 목소리에 노여움을 살짝 실으며 말했다. “그러나 의심할 여지가 없는 것이 걱정이오."], ["And I must confess that it is no more than we had a right to expect.", "그리고 이것은 우리가 마땅히 예상해도 좋았던 것에 지나지 않는다고 나는 고백해야 하겠소."], ["I hope that it may be taken by all of us as a lesson, and an ensample, and a teaching of the Lord’s mercy. And I wish you would request your husband—from me, Mrs.", "이것이 우리 모두에게 하나의 가르침으로, 하나의 본보기로, 주님의 자비를 알려 주는 가르침으로 받아들여지기를 나는 바라오."], ["Quiverful—to dwell on this subject in morning and evening lecture at the hospital on Sabbath next, showing how false is the trust which we put in the good things of this world;” which behest, to a certain extent, Mr.", "그리고 부인의 남편께 청해 주었으면 하오—나에게서 나온 말이라고, 퀴버풀 부인—다음 안식일에 요양원의 아침저녁 강론에서 이 주제를 붙들고, 이 세상의 좋은 것들에 두는 믿음이 얼마나 거짓된 것인지를 보여 주시라고.”"], ["Quiverful did obey, feeling that a quiet life in Barchester was of great value to him; but he did not go so far as to caution his hearers, who consisted of the aged bedesmen of the hospital, against matrimonial projects of an ambitious nature.", "이 분부를 퀴버풀 씨는 어느 정도 따랐으니, 바체스터에서 조용히 사는 것이 자기에게 큰 값이 있음을 느꼈던 것이다. 그러나 그 요양원의 늙은 구호 노인들로 이루어진 청중에게 야심 있는 혼인 계획을 조심하라고 경계하는 데까지는 나아가지 않았다."]], [["In this case, as in all others of the kind, the report was known to all the chapter before it had been heard by the archdeacon or his wife.", "이런 경우에 늘 그러하듯이 이 일에서도, 그 소문은 부주교나 그 아내가 듣기 전에 성당 참사회 사람들 모두에게 알려져 있었다. 수석 사제도 그것을 들었으나 귀에 담지 않았다."], ["The dean heard it, and disregarded it; as did also the dean’s wife—at first; and those who generally sided with the Grantlys in the diocesan battles pooh-poohed the tidings, saying to each other that both the archdeacon and Mrs.", "수석 사제의 아내도—처음에는—그러했다. 그리고 교구의 싸움에서 대개 그랜틀리 편에 서는 사람들은 그 소식에 코웃음을 치며, 부주교나 그랜틀리 부인이나 다 제 일은 넉넉히 알아서 처리할 수 있는 사람들이라고 서로 말했다."], ["Grantly were very well able to take care of their own affairs.", "그러나 방울지는 물이 돌을 파낸다."], ["But dripping water hollows a stone; and at last it was admitted on all sides that there was ground for fear,—on all sides, except at Plumstead.", "그리하여 마침내 두려워할 근거가 있다는 것이 온 사방에서 인정되었다—플럼스테드만 빼고 온 사방에서."]], [["“I am sure there is nothing in it; I really am sure of it,”", "“저는 그 말에 아무것도 없다고 확신해요."], ["said Mrs.", "정말로 확신해요,”"], ["Arabin, whispering to her sister; “but after turning it over in my mind, I thought it right to tell you.", "애러빈 부인이 언니에게 속삭이며 말했다. “하지만 마음속으로 이리저리 굴려 본 뒤에 언니에게 말해 두는 것이 옳다고 생각했어요."], ["And yet I don’t know now but I am wrong.”", "그런데 지금은 또 제가 잘못한 것이 아닌가 싶어요.”"]], [["“Quite right, dearest Eleanor,”", "“잘한 거예요, 우리 엘리너,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“And I am much obliged to you.", "“고맙게 생각해요."], ["But we understand it, you know.", "하지만 우리는 그게 어떤 것인지 알지요."], ["It comes, of course, like all other Christian blessings, from the palace.”", "다른 온갖 기독교의 축복과 마찬가지로, 그건 물론 주교관에서 오는 것이에요.”"], ["And then there was nothing more said about it between Mrs. Grantly and her sister.", "그러고서 그랜틀리 부인과 그 동생 사이에 그 일을 두고 더 이야기된 것은 없었다."]], [["But on the following morning there arrived a letter by post, addressed to Mrs. Grantly, bearing the postmark of Littlebath.", "그러나 이튿날 아침 우편으로 편지 한 통이 왔으니, 그랜틀리 부인에게 온 것이었고 리틀배스의 소인이 찍혀 있었다."], ["The letter ran:—", "편지는 이러했다—"]], [["It is known to the writer that Lord Dumbello has arranged with certain friends how he may escape from his present engagement.", "덤벨로 경이 지금의 약혼에서 빠져나갈 방도를 두고 어떤 벗들과 짜 놓았다는 것을 이 글을 쓰는 이는 알고 있습니다."], ["I think, therefore, that it is my duty as a Christian to warn you of this.", "그러므로 이 일을 부인께 알려 드리는 것이 기독교인으로서 저의 의무라고 생각합니다."]], [["Yours truly,", "진심을 담아,"]], [["Now it had happened that the embryo Mrs. Tickler’s most intimate bosom friend and confidante was known at Plumstead to live at Littlebath, and it had also happened—most unfortunately—that the embryo Mrs. Tickler, in the warmth of her neighbourly regard, had written a friendly line to her friend Griselda Grantly, congratulating her with all female sincerity on her splendid nuptials with the Lord Dumbello.", "그런데 마침 알 속의 티클러 부인의 가장 가깝고 속을 털어놓는 벗이 리틀배스에 산다는 것이 플럼스테드에 알려져 있었고, 또 마침—참으로 불행하게도—그 알 속의 티클러 부인이 이웃으로서 품은 따뜻한 마음에서 제 벗 그리젤다 그랜틀리에게 다정한 몇 줄을 적어 보내어, 덤벨로 경과 맺는 그 훌륭한 혼례를 여자다운 온갖 진심으로 축하한 일이 있었던 것이다."]], [["“It is not her natural hand,” said Mrs.", "“이건 그 사람의 본래 글씨가 아니에요,”"], ["Grantly, talking the matter over with her husband, “but you may be sure it has come from her.", "그랜틀리 부인이 남편과 그 일을 이야기하며 말했다. “하지만 그 사람에게서 나온 것임은 틀림없다고 보셔도 돼요."], ["It is a part of the new Christianity which we learn day by day from the palace teaching.”", "이건 우리가 날마다 주교관의 가르침에서 배우는 그 새로운 기독교의 한 부분이지요.”"]], [["But these things had some effect on the archdeacon’s mind.", "그러나 이런 일들은 부주교의 마음에 얼마간 작용을 했다."], ["He had learned lately the story of Lady Julia Mac Mull, and was not sure that his son-in-law—as ought to be about to be—had been entirely blameless in that matter.", "그는 근래에 레이디 줄리아 맥 멀의 이야기를 알게 되었고, 자기 사위가—앞으로 곧 그리 되어야 할 사람이—그 일에서 전혀 나무랄 데 없었다고는 확신하지 못했다."], ["And then in these days Lord Dumbello made no great sign.", "그리고 요즈음 덤벨로 경은 이렇다 할 표시를 크게 보이지 않았다."], ["Immediately on Griselda’s return to Plumstead he had sent her a magnificent present of emeralds, which, however, had come to her direct from the jewellers, and might have been—and probably was—ordered by his man of business.", "그리젤다가 플럼스테드로 돌아오자마자 그는 그녀에게 훌륭한 에메랄드 선물을 보내 왔으나, 그것은 보석상에서 그녀에게 곧바로 온 것이었고 그 집 일 보는 사람이 주문한 것일 수도—아마도 그러했을 것이—있었다."], ["Since that he had neither come, nor sent, nor written.", "그 뒤로는 그가 오지도, 무엇을 보내지도, 편지를 쓰지도 않았다."], ["Griselda did not seem to be in any way annoyed by this absence of the usual sign of love, and went on steadily with her great duties.", "그리젤다는 사랑의 그 흔한 표시가 없는 것을 조금도 마음에 걸려 하는 것 같지 않았고, 제 큰 소임을 꾸준히 해 나갔다."], ["“Nothing,” as she told her mother, “had been said about writing, and, therefore, she did not expect it.”", "그녀가 어머니에게 말한 대로 “편지에 관해서는 아무 말도 없었으니 저는 기다리지 않아요”라는 것이었다."], ["But the archdeacon was not quite at his ease.", "그러나 부주교는 그다지 마음이 편하지 않았다."], ["“Keep Dumbello up to his P’s and Q’s, you know,”", "“덤벨로가 몸가짐을 흐트러뜨리지 않도록 단단히 붙들어 두시게, 아시겠소,”"], ["a friend of his had whispered to him at his club.", "그의 벗 하나가 클럽에서 그에게 귓속말로 그렇게 말했던 것이다."], ["By heavens, yes.", "하늘에 맹세코, 그렇다."], ["The archdeacon was not a man to bear with indifference a wrong in such a quarter.", "부주교는 그런 자리에서 받은 억울함을 아무렇지 않게 견딜 사람이 아니었다."], ["In spite of his clerical profession, few men were more inclined to fight against personal wrongs—and few men more able.", "성직자라는 직분에도 불구하고, 제 몸에 닿은 억울함에 맞서 싸우려는 마음이 그보다 더한 사람은 드물었다—그리고 그보다 더 잘 싸울 수 있는 사람도 드물었다."]], [["“Can there be anything wrong, I wonder?”", "“무엇인가 잘못된 것이 있을 수 있겠소?”"], ["said he to his wife.", "그가 아내에게 말했다."], ["“Is it worth while that I should go up to London?”", "“내가 런던으로 올라가 볼 만한 일이오?”"], ["But Mrs. Grantly attributed it all to the palace doctrine.", "그러나 그랜틀리 부인은 그 모든 것을 주교관의 교리 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["What could be more natural, looking at all the circumstances of the Tickler engagement?", "티클러 약혼의 온갖 사정을 보건대 이보다 더 자연스러운 일이 무엇이겠는가?"], ["She therefore gave her voice against any steps being taken by the archdeacon.", "그러므로 그녀는 부주교가 어떤 조치를 취하는 데 반대표를 던졌다."]], [["A day or two after that Mrs. Proudie met Mrs. Arabin in the close and condoled with her openly on the termination of the marriage treaty;—quite openly, for Mrs. Tickler—as she was to be—was with her mother, and Mrs. Arabin was accompanied by her sister-in-law, Mary Bold.", "그로부터 하루 이틀 뒤에 프라우디 부인이 구내에서 애러빈 부인을 만나 혼사 조약이 끝나 버린 일을 두고 드러내어 위로했다. 참으로 드러내어 그리했으니, 티클러 부인이—앞으로 그리 될 사람이—어머니와 함께 있었고, 애러빈 부인은 그 시누이 메리 볼드와 함께 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“It must be very grievous to Mrs. Grantly, very grievous indeed,”", "“그랜틀리 부인에게는 참 애통한 일일 테지요, 참으로 애통한 일이오,”"], ["said Mrs.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["Proudie, “and I sincerely feel for her.", "“나도 진심으로 그 부인을 딱하게 여기오."], ["But, Mrs. Arabin, all these lessons are sent to us for our eternal welfare.”", "그러나 애러빈 부인, 이 모든 가르침은 우리의 영원한 복을 위하여 우리에게 보내지는 것이오.”"]], [["“Of course,”", "“그렇지요,”"], ["said Mrs. Arabin.", "애러빈 부인이 말했다."], ["“But as to this special lesson, I am inclined to doubt that it—”", "“하지만 이 특별한 가르침에 관해서는, 저는 그것이—”"]], [["“Ah-h!", "“아—!"], ["I fear it is too true.", "너무나 참인 것이 걱정이오."], ["I fear there is no room for doubt.", "의심할 자리가 없는 것이 걱정이오."], ["Of course you are aware that Lord Dumbello is off for the Continent.”", "물론 부인도 덤벨로 경이 대륙으로 떠났다는 것은 알고 있겠지요.”"]], [["Mrs. Arabin was not aware of it, and she was obliged to admit as much.", "애러빈 부인은 그것을 알지 못했고, 알지 못한다고 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“He started four days ago, by way of Boulogne,”", "“나흘 전에 불로뉴 쪽으로 길을 떠났어요,”"], ["said Mrs. Tickler, who seemed to be very well up in the whole affair.", "티클러 부인이 말했으니, 이 일 전체에 아주 밝은 듯했다."], ["“I am so sorry for poor dear Griselda.", "“가엾고 귀한 그리젤다가 참 안타까워요."], ["I am told she has got all her things.", "옷가지는 다 갖추어 놓았다고 들었는데."], ["It is such a pity, you know.”", "참 애석한 일이지요.”"]], [["“But why should not Lord Dumbello come back from the Continent?”", "“하지만 덤벨로 경이 대륙에서 돌아오지 않을 이유가 어디 있나요?”"], ["said Miss Bold, very quietly.", "볼드 양이 아주 조용히 말했다."]], [["“Why not, indeed?", "“그야 없지요."], ["I’m sure I hope he may,”", "그이가 돌아오기를 나도 진심으로 바라오,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["“And no doubt he will, some day.", "“그리고 의심할 것 없이 언젠가는 돌아올 테지요."], ["But if he be such a man as they say he is, it is really well for Griselda that she should be relieved from such a marriage. For, after all, Mrs.", "그러나 그이가 사람들 말대로 그런 사내라면, 그런 혼인에서 그리젤다가 벗어나게 된 것은 참으로 잘된 일이오."], ["Arabin, what are the things of this world?—dust beneath our feet, ashes between our teeth, grass cut for the oven, vanity, vexation, and nothing more!”—well pleased with which variety of Christian metaphors Mrs.", "어차피 애러빈 부인, 이 세상의 것들이 무엇이오?—우리 발밑의 티끌, 우리 이 사이의 재, 가마에 넣으려고 베어 놓은 풀, 헛됨과 괴로움, 그 밖에는 아무것도 아니지요!” 이렇게 여러 가지 기독교다운 비유를 늘어놓은 것에 아주 흐뭇해하며 프라우디 부인은 걸어갔다."], ["Proudie walked on, still muttering, however, something about worms and grubs, by which she intended to signify her own species and the Dumbello and Grantly sects of it in particular.", "그러면서도 지렁이며 구더기에 관하여 무엇인가를 여전히 웅얼거렸으니, 그것으로 그녀는 제가 속한 종족을, 그리고 그 가운데 특히 덤벨로 파와 그랜틀리 파를 가리키려 한 것이었다."]], [["This now had gone so far that Mrs. Arabin conceived herself bound in duty to see her sister, and it was then settled in consultation at Plumstead that the archdeacon should call officially at the palace and beg that the rumour might be contradicted.", "이제 일이 이만큼까지 이르렀으므로 애러빈 부인은 언니를 만나 보는 것이 마땅한 소임이라고 생각했고, 그리하여 플럼스테드에서 의논한 끝에 부주교가 공식으로 주교관을 찾아가 그 소문을 부인해 달라고 청하기로 정해졌다."], ["This he did early on the next morning and was shown into the bishop’s study, in which he found both his lordship and Mrs. Proudie.", "그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 그 일을 했고 주교의 서재로 안내되어 들어갔는데, 그 방에는 주교와 프라우디 부인이 다 있었다."], ["The bishop rose to greet him with special civility, smiling his very sweetest on him, as though of all his clergy the archdeacon were the favourite; but Mrs.", "주교는 일어나 그를 맞이하며 각별한 예를 갖추었고, 제 성직자 가운데 부주교가 가장 마음에 드는 사람이기라도 한 듯이 그에게 가장 다사로운 웃음을 지어 보였다."], ["Proudie wore something of a gloomy aspect, as though she knew that such a visit at such an hour must have reference to some special business.", "그러나 프라우디 부인은 어딘가 어두운 낯빛이었으니, 그런 시각에 그런 방문이 있다면 무엇인가 특별한 용건이 걸려 있을 것을 아는 듯했다."], ["The morning calls made by the archdeacon at the palace in the way of ordinary civility were not numerous.", "부주교가 예의를 차리는 뜻에서 주교관에 아침 방문을 하는 일은 많지 않았다."]], [["On the present occasion he dashed at once into his subject.", "이번 자리에서 그는 곧바로 제 용건으로 뛰어들었다."], ["“I have called this morning, Mrs. Proudie,”", "“프라우디 부인, 오늘 아침 제가 찾아온 것은,”"], ["said he, “because I wish to ask a favour from you.”", "그가 말했다. “부인께 한 가지 청을 드리고 싶어서입니다.”"], ["Whereupon Mrs. Proudie bowed.", "그러자 프라우디 부인이 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“Mrs. Proudie will be most happy, I am sure,”", "“프라우디 부인이 아주 기꺼워할 거예요, 나는 그렇게 믿어요,”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["“I find that some foolish people have been talking in Barchester about my daughter,”", "“바체스터의 어리석은 몇 사람이 제 딸을 두고 이야기하고 있다는 것을 알았습니다,” 부주교가 말했다."], ["said the archdeacon; “and I wish to ask Mrs. Proudie—”", "“그래서 프라우디 부인께 청하고자—”"]], [["Most women under such circumstances would have felt the awkwardness of their situation, and would have prepared to eat their past words with wry faces.", "그런 처지에 놓이면 대개의 여인은 제 자리가 난처하다는 것을 느끼고, 얼굴을 찡그리며 제가 한 말을 되씹어 삼킬 준비를 했을 것이다."], ["But not so Mrs. Proudie.", "그러나 프라우디 부인은 그렇지 않았다."], ["Mrs. Grantly had had the imprudence to throw Mr.", "그랜틀리 부인이 슬로프 씨를 그녀의 얼굴에 던지는 무람없는 짓을 했던 것이다—그것도 그녀 자신의 응접실에서."], ["Slope in her face—there, in her own drawing-room, and she was resolved to be revenged. Mrs.", "그래서 그녀는 앙갚음을 하기로 마음을 굳혀 놓고 있었다."], ["Grantly, too, had ridiculed the Tickler match, and no too great niceness should now prevent Mrs. Proudie from speaking her mind about the Dumbello match.", "게다가 그랜틀리 부인은 티클러 혼사를 비웃기까지 했으니, 이제 지나친 조심 따위가 프라우디 부인이 덤벨로 혼사를 두고 제 속을 말하는 것을 막아설 수는 없었다."]], [["“A great many people are talking about her, I am sorry to say,”", "“애석하지만 아주 많은 사람이 그 아이를 두고 이야기하고 있소,”"], ["said Mrs.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["Proudie; “but, poor dear, it is not her fault.", "“그러나 가엾고 귀한 것, 그건 그 아이 탓이 아니오. 어느 처녀에게든 일어날 수 있는 일이지요."], ["It might have happened to any girl; only, perhaps, a little more care—; you’ll excuse me, Dr. Grantly.”", "다만 조금 더 조심을 했더라면—; 실례하겠소, 그랜틀리 박사.”"]], [["“I have come here to allude to a report which has been spread about in Barchester, that the match between Lord Dumbello and my daughter has been broken off; and—”", "“저는 바체스터에 퍼진 소문, 곧 덤벨로 경과 제 딸 사이의 혼사가 깨졌다는 소문을 두고 말씀드리려고 여기 왔습니다. 그리고—”"]], [["“Everybody in Barchester knows it, I believe,”", "“바체스터의 누구나 다 알고 있는 줄로 나는 아오,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["—“and,”", "—“그리고,”"], ["continued the archdeacon, “to request that that report may be contradicted.”", "부주교가 이어서 말했다. “그 소문을 부인해 주시기를 청하러 왔습니다.”"]], [["“Contradicted!", "“부인이라니요!"], ["Why, he has gone right away,—out of the country!”", "아니, 그이는 아주 떠나 버렸소—이 나라 밖으로 나갔소!”"]], [["“Never mind where he has gone to, Mrs.", "“그 사람이 어디로 갔는지는 상관없습니다, 프라우디 부인."], ["Proudie; I beg that the report may be contradicted.”", "저는 그 소문을 부인해 주시기를 청합니다.”"]], [["“You’ll have to go round to every house in Barchester then,”", "“그러면 바체스터의 집집마다 다 돌아다녀야 할 거요,”"], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“By no means,”", "“결코 그렇지 않습니다,”"], ["replied the archdeacon.", "부주교가 대답했다."], ["“And perhaps it may be right that I should explain to the bishop that I came here because—”", "“그리고 제가 어째서 여기 왔는지를 주교님께 설명해 드리는 것이 옳겠습니다. 그것은—”"]], [["“The bishop knows nothing about it,”", "“주교는 이 일에 관하여 아무것도 모르오,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Nothing in the world,”", "“세상에 아무것도 몰라요,”"], ["said his lordship.", "주교가 말했다."], ["“And I am sure I hope that the young lady may not be disappointed.”", "“그리고 그 젊은 아가씨가 실망하지 않기를 나도 진심으로 바라요.”"]], [["—“because the matter was so distinctly mentioned to Mrs. Arabin by yourself yesterday.”", "—“어제 부인께서 애러빈 부인에게 그 일을 그토록 분명하게 말씀하셨기 때문입니다.”"]], [["“Distinctly mentioned!", "“분명하게 말했다니!"], ["Of course it was distinctly mentioned.", "물론 분명하게 말했지요."], ["There are some things which can’t be kept under a bushel, Dr.", "말 밑에 감추어 둘 수 없는 일도 있는 법이오, 그랜틀리 박사."], ["Grantly; and this seems to be one of them.", "그리고 이것이 그 가운데 하나인 듯하오."], ["Your going about in this way won’t make Lord Dumbello marry the young lady.”", "이런 식으로 돌아다녀 봐야 덤벨로 경이 그 아가씨와 혼인하게 되지는 않을 거요.”"]], [["That was true; nor would it make Mrs. Proudie hold her tongue.", "그것은 참말이었다."], ["Perhaps the archdeacon was wrong in his present errand, and so he now began to bethink himself.", "그러나 그리한다고 프라우디 부인이 입을 다물게 되지도 않을 것이었다. 어쩌면 부주교가 이번 걸음에서 잘못하고 있는 것인지도 몰랐고, 그도 이제 그런 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["“At any rate,”", "“적어도,”"], ["said he, “when I tell you that there is no ground whatever for such a report you will do me the kindness to say that, as far as you are concerned, it shall go no further.", "그가 말했다. “그런 소문에는 아무런 근거도 없다고 제가 말씀드리는 이상, 부인께서 관계하시는 한에서는 그것이 더 퍼져 나가지 않게 하겠다고 말씀해 주시는 친절은 베풀어 주시겠지요."], ["I think, my lord, I am not asking too much in asking that.”", "주교님, 그만한 것을 청하는 것이 지나친 청은 아니라고 저는 생각합니다.”"]], [["“The bishop knows nothing about it,”", "“주교는 이 일에 관하여 아무것도 모르오,”"], ["said Mrs. Proudie again.", "프라우디 부인이 다시 말했다."]], [["“Nothing at all,”", "“전혀 아무것도 몰라요,”"], ["said the bishop.", "주교가 말했다."]], [["“And as I must protest that I believe the information which has reached me on this head,” said Mrs.", "“그리고 이 일에 관하여 나에게 이르러 온 소식을 나는 믿는다고 밝혀야 하는 터이니,”"], ["Proudie, “I do not see how it is possible that I should contradict it.", "프라우디 부인이 말했다. “내가 어떻게 그것을 부인할 수 있겠소."], ["I can easily understand your feelings, Dr. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 박사, 댁의 심정은 나도 쉽게 알 만하오."], ["Considering your daughter’s position the match was, as regards earthly wealth, a very great one.", "댁의 딸의 처지를 생각하면 그 혼사는, 이 땅의 재물로 보아 아주 대단한 것이었지요."], ["I do not wonder that you should be grieved at its being broken off; but I trust that this sorrow may eventuate in a blessing to you and to Miss Griselda.", "그것이 깨진 일로 댁이 슬퍼하는 것을 나는 이상하게 여기지 않소. 그러나 이 슬픔이 댁과 그리젤다 양에게 축복으로 열매 맺기를 바라오."], ["These worldly disappointments are precious balms, and I trust you know how to accept them as such.”", "이 세상의 낙심은 값진 향유이니, 그것을 그렇게 받아들이는 법을 댁이 알고 있으리라고 믿소.”"]], [["The fact was that Dr. Grantly had done altogether wrong in coming to the palace.", "사실 그랜틀리 박사가 주교관에 온 것은 아주 잘못한 일이었다."], ["His wife might have some chance with Mrs. Proudie, but he had none.", "그의 아내라면 프라우디 부인을 상대로 얼마간 승산이 있었겠지만 그에게는 전혀 없었다."], ["Since she had come to Barchester he had had only two or three encounters with her, and in all of these he had gone to the wall.", "그녀가 바체스터에 온 뒤로 그가 그녀와 맞붙은 것은 두세 번뿐이었으나 그 모든 자리에서 그는 벽으로 밀렸다."], ["His visits to the palace always resulted in his leaving the presence of the inhabitants in a frame of mind by no means desirable, and he now found that he had to do so once again. He could not compel Mrs.", "주교관을 찾아가는 일은 언제나 그가 그 집 사람들 앞에서 물러나며 도무지 바랄 것 없는 심정에 잠기는 것으로 끝났고, 이번에도 다시 그렇게 되었음을 그는 알았다."], ["Proudie to say that the report was untrue; nor could he condescend to make counter hits at her about her own daughter, as his wife would have done.", "그는 프라우디 부인에게 그 소문이 사실이 아니라고 말하도록 강제할 수 없었다. 또한 아내라면 했을 것처럼 그녀 자신의 딸을 두고 맞받아 찌르는 짓까지 하려고 몸을 낮출 수도 없었다."], ["And thus, having utterly failed, he got up and took his leave.", "그리하여 아주 실패하고서 그는 일어나 작별했다."]], [["But the worst of the matter was, that, in going home, he could not divest his mind of the idea that there might be some truth in the report.", "그러나 이 일에서 가장 나쁜 것은, 집으로 돌아가면서 그가 그 소문에 얼마간 참이 있을지도 모른다는 생각을 마음에서 떨쳐 낼 수 없었다는 점이다."], ["What if Lord Dumbello had gone to the Continent resolved to send back from thence some reason why it was impossible that he should make Miss Grantly his wife?", "만약 덤벨로 경이 대륙으로 가서 거기에서, 그랜틀리 양을 아내로 삼는 것이 불가능한 이유를 무엇인가 적어 보내리라 작정하고 떠난 것이라면?"], ["Such things had been done before now by men in his rank.", "그런 일은 전에도 그 신분의 사내들이 해 온 바 있었다."], ["Whether or no Mrs. Tickler had been the letter-writing wellwisher from Littlebath, or had induced her friend to be so, it did seem manifest to him, Dr.", "티클러 부인이 리틀배스에서 편지를 쓴 그 ‘잘 바라는 이’였든, 아니면 제 벗을 그렇게 하도록 부추긴 것이었든, 그랜틀리 박사인 그에게 분명하게 보인 것은 프라우디 부인이 그토록 부지런히 퍼뜨리는 그 소문을 정말로 믿고 있다는 사실이었다."], ["Grantly, that Mrs. Proudie absolutely believed the report which she promulgated so diligently.", "바람이 그 생각의 아비였을지도 모른다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["The wish might be father to the thought, no doubt; but that the thought was truly there, Dr. Grantly could not induce himself to disbelieve.", "그러나 그 생각이 참으로 거기 있다는 것은, 그랜틀리 박사가 스스로 믿지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["His wife was less credulous, and to a certain degree comforted him; but that evening he received a letter which greatly confirmed the suspicions set on foot by Mrs.", "그의 아내는 덜 잘 믿는 편이어서 그를 얼마간 위로해 주었다. 그러나 그날 저녁 그는 편지 한 통을 받았으니, 프라우디 부인이 일으켜 놓은 의심을 크게 굳혀 주고 덤벨로 경에 대한 그 아내의 믿음까지 흔들어 놓는 것이었다."], ["Proudie, and even shook his wife’s faith in Lord Dumbello. It was from a mere acquaintance, who in the ordinary course of things would not have written to him.", "그것은 한낱 아는 사람에게서 온 것이었고, 일이 예사롭게 돌아갔다면 그에게 편지를 쓸 만한 사람도 아니었다."], ["And the bulk of the letter referred to ordinary things, as to which the gentleman in question would hardly have thought of giving himself the trouble to write a letter.", "그리고 편지의 대부분은 예사로운 일들에 관한 것이어서, 그런 일로 편지를 쓰는 수고를 스스로 짊어질 생각을 그 신사가 했을 법하지도 않았다."], ["But at the end of the note he said,—", "그러나 그 짧은 글 끝에 그는 이렇게 말했다—"]], [["“Of course you are aware that Dumbello is off to Paris; I have not heard whether the exact day of his return is fixed.”", "“물론 덤벨로가 파리로 떠난 것은 알고 계시겠지요. 돌아올 날이 언제로 정해졌다는 이야기는 저도 듣지 못했습니다.”"]], [["“It is true then,”", "“그러면 사실이군,”"], ["said the archdeacon, striking the library table with his hand, and becoming absolutely white about the mouth and jaws.", "부주교가 서재 탁자를 손으로 치며, 입가와 턱이 아주 하얘지면서 말했다."]], [["“It cannot be,” said Mrs.", "“그럴 수 없어요,”"], ["Grantly; but even she was now trembling.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. 그러나 이제 그녀조차 떨고 있었다."]], [["“If it be so I’ll drag him back to England by the collar of his coat, and disgrace him before the steps of his father’s hall.”", "“만약 그렇다면 그 사내의 상의 목덜미를 잡아 영국으로 끌고 와서 그 아비의 저택 계단 앞에서 망신을 주고 말겠소.”"]], [["And the archdeacon as he uttered the threat looked his character as an irate British father much better than he did his other character as a clergyman of the Church of England.", "그리고 부주교가 그 위협을 입에 올릴 때 그는 영국 국교회의 성직자라는 다른 됨됨이보다 노한 영국인 아버지라는 됨됨이에 훨씬 더 잘 어울려 보였다."], ["The archdeacon had been greatly worsted by Mrs. Proudie, but he was a man who knew how to fight his battles among men,—sometimes without too close a regard to his cloth.", "부주교는 프라우디 부인에게 크게 밀렸으나, 사내들 사이에서 제 싸움을 어떻게 싸워야 하는지는 아는 사람이었다—더러는 제 성직의 옷을 너무 세세히 헤아리지 않고서."]], [["“Had Lord Dumbello intended any such thing he would have written, or got some friend to write by this time,”", "“덤벨로 경이 그런 일을 마음에 두었다면 지금쯤은 편지를 썼거나 벗 가운데 누구를 시켜 쓰게 했을 거예요,”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["“It is quite possible that he might wish to be off, but he would be too chary of his name not to endeavour to do so with decency.”", "“빠져나가고 싶은 마음이야 있을 수 있겠지요. 하지만 그이는 제 이름을 너무 아끼는 사람이라 예를 갖추어 그리하려고 애쓰지 않을 리가 없어요.”"]], [["Thus the matter was discussed, and it appeared to them both to be so serious that the archdeacon resolved to go at once to London.", "이렇게 그 일이 의논되었고, 두 사람에게 다 그것이 하도 중대해 보였으므로 부주교는 곧바로 런던으로 가기로 마음을 정했다."], ["That Lord Dumbello had gone to France he did not doubt; but he would find some one in town acquainted with the young man’s intentions, and he would, no doubt, be able to hear when his return was expected.", "덤벨로 경이 프랑스로 갔다는 것은 그도 의심하지 않았다. 그러나 그 젊은 사내의 뜻을 아는 누군가를 런던에서 찾아낼 것이고, 그리하여 그가 언제 돌아오리라고 예상되는지도 틀림없이 들을 수 있을 것이었다."], ["If there were real reason for apprehension he would follow the runagate to the Continent, but he would not do this without absolute knowledge.", "참으로 걱정할 근거가 있다면 그 도망꾼을 대륙까지 뒤따라가겠지만, 확실히 알지 못하고서는 그리하지 않을 것이었다."], ["According to Lord Dumbello’s present engagements he was bound to present himself in August next at Plumstead Episcopi, with the view of then and there taking Griselda Grantly in marriage; but if he kept his word in this respect no one had a right to quarrel with him for going to Paris in the meantime.", "덤벨로 경이 지금 걸어 놓은 약속대로라면 그는 오는 팔월에 플럼스테드 에피스코피에 몸소 나타나, 그때 거기에서 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 아내로 맞아야 할 처지였다. 그러나 그가 이 점에서 제 말을 지킨다면 그 사이에 파리로 간다고 해서 그를 두고 시비할 권리는 누구에게도 없었다."], ["Most expectant bridegrooms would, no doubt, under such circumstances have declared their intentions to their future brides; but if Lord Dumbello were different from others, who had a right on that account to be indignant with him?", "그런 처지에 놓인 대개의 신랑감이라면 의심할 것 없이 앞으로 제 아내가 될 사람에게 제 뜻을 알려 두었을 것이다. 그러나 덤벨로 경이 남들과 다르다면, 그 때문에 그에게 분개할 권리가 누구에게 있는가?"], ["He was unlike other men in other things; and especially unlike other men in being the eldest son of the Marquis of Hartletop.", "그는 다른 일에서도 남들과 달랐다. 그리고 무엇보다 하틀탑 후작의 맏아들이라는 점에서 남들과 달랐다."], ["It would be all very well for Tickler to proclaim his whereabouts from week to week; but the eldest son of a marquis might find it inconvenient to be so precise!", "티클러 같은 사람이야 주마다 제가 있는 곳을 알려도 다 좋겠지만, 후작의 맏아들에게는 그렇게 꼬박꼬박 하는 것이 거북한 일일 수도 있는 것이다!"], ["Nevertheless the archdeacon thought it only prudent to go up to London.", "그럼에도 부주교는 런던으로 올라가는 것이 그저 조심하는 길이라고 생각했다."]], [["“Susan,”", "“수전,”"], ["said the archdeacon to his wife, just as he was starting;—at this moment neither of them were in the happiest spirits,—“I think I would say a word of caution to Griselda.”", "부주교가 막 떠나려 하면서 아내에게 말했다—이때 두 사람 가운데 어느 쪽도 가장 좋은 심정에 있지는 않았다—“나는 그리젤다에게 조심할 말을 한마디 해 두는 것이 좋겠소.”"]], [["“Do you feel so much doubt about it as that?”", "“그만큼이나 그 일을 의심하시나요?”"], ["said Mrs. Grantly.", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다."], ["But even she did not dare to put a direct negative to this proposal, so much had she been moved by what she had heard!", "그러나 그녀조차 이 제안에 곧바로 아니라고 맞설 엄두를 내지 못했으니, 그만큼 그녀도 자기가 들은 것에 마음이 흔들려 있었던 것이다!"]], [["“I think I would do so, not frightening her more than I could help.", "“나는 그렇게 하겠소. 될 수 있는 대로 그 아이를 겁먹게 하지 않으면서."], ["It will lessen the blow if it be that the blow is to fall.”", "만약 그 매가 떨어질 것이라면, 그렇게 해 두는 것이 매를 덜 아프게 해 줄 것이오.”"]], [["“It will kill me,” said Mrs.", "“저는 죽을 것 같아요,”"], ["Grantly; “but I think that she will be able to bear it.”", "그랜틀리 부인이 말했다. “하지만 그 아이는 견딜 수 있을 거예요.”"]], [["On the next morning Mrs. Grantly, with much cunning preparation, went about the task which her husband had left her to perform.", "이튿날 아침 그랜틀리 부인은 교묘하게 준비를 갖추어 남편이 저에게 남겨 놓고 간 그 일에 나섰다."], ["It took her long to do, for she was very cunning in the doing of it; but at last it dropped from her in words that there was a possibility—a bare possibility—that some disappointment might even yet be in store for them.", "그 일에는 오래 걸렸으니, 그것을 하는 데 그녀가 매우 교묘했기 때문이다. 그러나 마침내 그녀의 입에서, 어쩌면—아주 희미하게나마—그들에게 아직도 어떤 낙심이 닥칠 수도 있다는 말이 새어 나왔다."]], [["“Do you mean, mamma, that the marriage will be put off?”", "“어머니, 혼례가 미루어진다는 말씀이세요?”"]], [["“I don’t mean to say that I think it will; God forbid!", "“그리 될 것 같다는 말은 아니야. 하느님이 막아 주시겠지!"], ["but it is just possible.", "하지만 그럴 수도 있다는 거야."], ["I daresay that I am very wrong to tell you of this, but I know that you have sense enough to bear it.", "이 이야기를 너에게 하는 것이 아주 잘못인지도 모르겠구나. 그러나 나는 네가 그것을 견딜 만한 분별이 있다는 것을 안다."], ["Papa has gone to London and we shall hear from him soon.”", "아버지는 런던으로 가셨으니 곧 소식을 들을 게다.”"]], [["“Then, mamma, I had better give them orders not to go on with the marking.”", "“그럼 어머니, 그 사람들에게 이름 수놓는 일은 더 하지 말라고 일러 두는 게 좋겠어요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLVI. LADY LUFTON’S REQUEST.", "# 제46장. 레이디 러프턴의 청"]], [["The bailiffs on that day had their meals regular,—and their beer, which state of things, together with an absence of all duty in the way of making inventories and the like, I take to be the earthly paradise of bailiffs; and on the next morning they walked off with civil speeches and many apologies as to their intrusion. “They was very sorry,” they said, “to have troubled a gen’leman as were a gen’leman, but in their way of business what could they do?” To which one of them added a remark that, “business is business.” This statement I am not prepared to contradict, but I would recommend all men in choosing a profession to avoid any that may require an apology at every turn;—either an apology or else a somewhat violent assertion of right. Each younger male reader may perhaps reply that he has no thought of becoming a sheriff’s officer; but then are there not other cognate lines of life to which perhaps the attention of some such may be attracted?", "그날 집행관들은 끼니를 어김없이 받아먹었다 — 맥주도 받아 마셨다. 이런 형편에다 재산 목록을 만드는 따위의 일거리가 도무지 없었다는 것까지 겹치니, 나는 그것이 집행관에게는 이 땅의 낙원이라고 여긴다. 그리고 이튿날 아침 그들은 점잖은 말씨와, 남의 집에 들어와 있었던 것을 두고 늘어놓는 숱한 사과와 함께 걸어 나갔다. “참 미안하게 됐수,” 그들은 말했다. “신사다운 신사분을 괴롭혀 놓아서 말이우. 그렇지만 우리 장사가 이런 장사인데 어쩌겠수?” 그 말에 그들 가운데 하나가 “장사는 장사니까요” 하는 소리를 덧붙였다. 이 말을 나는 되받아 반박할 준비가 되어 있지 않다. 그러나 나는 모든 사람에게, 직업을 고를 때에는 걸음마다 사과를 해야 하는 직업은 피하라고 권하고 싶다 — 사과가 아니면 제 권리를 얼마간 거칠게 내세우기라도 해야 하는 직업 말이다. 젊은 남자 독자라면 저마다, 저는 집행리가 될 생각이 없다고 대답할지 모른다. 그렇지만 그와 한 갈래인 다른 생업들, 어쩌면 그런 이들 가운데 몇몇의 마음이 끌릴 만한 생업들이 있지 않은가?"]], [["On the evening of the day on which they went Mark received a note from Lady Lufton begging him to call early on the following morning, and immediately after breakfast he went across to Framley Court.", "그들이 떠난 날 저녁에 마크는 레이디 러프턴에게서 짧은 편지를 받았는데, 이튿날 아침 일찍 들러 달라고 청하는 것이었다. 그래서 그는 아침을 먹고 나자 곧 프램리 코트로 건너갔다."], ["It may be imagined that he was not in a very happy frame of mind, but he felt the truth of his wife’s remark that the first plunge into cold water was always the worst.", "그의 마음이 그리 편치 않았으리라는 것은 짐작할 수 있는 일이다. 그러나 찬물에 처음 뛰어드는 것이 언제나 가장 견디기 어렵다던 아내의 말이 옳다는 것을 그는 느꼈다."], ["Lady Lufton was not a woman who would continually throw his disgrace into his teeth, however terribly cold might be the first words with which she spoke of it.", "그 일을 두고 처음 꺼내는 말이 아무리 무섭게 차갑다 하더라도, 레이디 러프턴은 그의 수치를 두고두고 그의 면전에 들이댈 여인이 아니었다."], ["He strove hard as he entered her room to carry his usual look and bearing, and to put out his hand to greet her with his customary freedom, but he knew that he failed.", "그는 그녀의 방에 들어서면서 여느 때의 낯빛과 몸가짐을 지키려고, 그리고 늘 하던 대로 거침없이 손을 내밀어 인사하려고 몹시 애를 썼다. 그러나 자기가 그러지 못했다는 것을 그는 알았다."], ["And it may be said that no good man who has broken down in his goodness can carry the disgrace of his fall without some look of shame.", "그리고 이렇게 말할 수도 있으리라. 착한 사람으로서 그 착함이 무너져 버린 사람은, 제 넘어짐의 수치를 부끄러운 낯빛 하나 없이 지고 갈 수는 없다고."], ["When a man is able to do that, he ceases to be in any way good.", "사람이 그럴 수 있게 된다면 그는 어느 모로도 착한 사람이 아니게 된다."]], [["“This has been a distressing affair,”", "“참 마음 아픈 일이었어요.”"], ["said Lady Lufton after her first salutation.", "첫인사를 끝내고 레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, indeed,”", "“예, 참으로 그렇습니다.”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“It has been very sad for poor Fanny.”", "“딱한 패니에게는 몹시 슬픈 일이었습니다.”"]], [["“Well; we must all have our little periods of grief; and it may perhaps be fortunate if none of us have worse than this.", "“글쎄요, 우리 모두에게는 저마다 슬픔의 시절이 얼마간 있어야 하는 법이지요. 그리고 우리 가운데 누구도 이보다 더한 것을 겪지 않는다면 어쩌면 그것이 복일지도 몰라요."], ["She will not complain, herself, I am sure.”", "그 사람 자신은 하소연하지 않을 거예요, 틀림없이.”"]], [["“She complain!”", "“그 사람이 하소연을 하겠습니까!”"]], [["“No, I am sure she will not.", "“그럼요, 하지 않을 거예요."], ["And now all I’ve got to say, Mr.", "그러니 이제 내가 할 말은 이것뿐이에요, 로바츠 씨."], ["Robarts, is this: I hope you and Lufton have had enough to do with black sheep to last you your lives; for I must protest that your late friend Mr.", "나는 로바츠 씨와 러프턴이 검은 양을 상대하는 일이라면 평생 쓸 만큼 넉넉히 겪었기를 바라요."], ["Sowerby is a black sheep.”", "로바츠 씨의 지난 벗 사워비 씨는 검은 양이라고 나는 못 박아 두어야 하겠으니까요.”"]], [["In no possible way could Lady Lufton have alluded to the matter with greater kindness than in thus joining Mark’s name with that of her son.", "이렇게 마크의 이름을 제 아들의 이름과 나란히 놓는 것보다 더 다정하게 그 일을 건드릴 방법은 레이디 러프턴에게 있을 수 없었다."], ["It took away all the bitterness of the rebuke, and made the subject one on which even he might have spoken without difficulty.", "그것은 꾸중의 쓴맛을 죄다 걷어내 버렸고, 그로써 그조차도 어렵지 않게 입을 열 수 있는 이야기가 되었다."], ["But now, seeing that she was so gentle to him, he could not but lean the more hardly on himself.", "그러나 이제 그녀가 저에게 그토록 부드럽게 대하는 것을 보고 나니, 그는 그만큼 더 매섭게 저 자신을 몰아붙이지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“I have been very foolish,”", "“저는 몹시 어리석었습니다.”"], ["said he, “very foolish and very wrong, and very wicked.”", "그가 말했다. “몹시 어리석고 몹시 잘못하고 몹시 사악했습니다.”"]], [["“Very foolish, I believe, Mr.", "“몹시 어리석었지요, 로바츠 씨 — 솔직하게, 그리고 이번 한 번으로 끝내려고 하는 말이에요."], ["Robarts—to speak frankly and once for all; but, as I also believe, nothing worse.", "그렇지만 내가 또한 믿는 바로는 그보다 더한 것은 아무것도 없어요."], ["I thought it best for both of us that we should just have one word about it, and now I recommend that the matter be never mentioned between us again.”", "이 일을 두고 우리가 딱 한마디만 나누어 두는 것이 서로에게 가장 좋겠다고 여겼어요. 그리고 이제는 이 일이 우리 사이에서 다시는 입에 오르지 않기를 권해요.”"]], [["“God bless you, Lady Lufton,”", "“하느님께서 마님께 복을 내리시기를 빕니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I think no man ever had such a friend as you are.”", "“마님 같은 벗을 가진 사람은 여태 아무도 없었으리라고 저는 생각합니다.”"]], [["She had been very quiet during the interview, and almost subdued, not speaking with the animation that was usual to her; for this affair with Mr.", "그녀는 이 만남 동안 몹시 조용했고 거의 가라앉아 있었으며, 여느 때 같은 활기로 말하지 않았다."], ["Robarts was not the only one she had to complete that day, nor, perhaps, the one most difficult of completion.", "로바츠 씨와의 이 일이 그날 그녀가 끝내야 하는 유일한 일이 아니었고, 어쩌면 끝내기가 가장 어려운 일도 아니었기 때문이다."], ["But she cheered up a little under the praise now bestowed on her, for it was the sort of praise she loved best.", "그러나 이제 저에게 얹힌 칭찬을 듣고 그녀는 조금 기운을 냈다. 그것이 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 종류의 칭찬이었기 때문이다."], ["She did hope, and, perhaps, flatter herself, that she was a good friend.", "그녀는 자기가 좋은 벗이기를 정말로 바랐고, 어쩌면 그렇다고 스스로 흐뭇해하고 있었다."]], [["“You must be good enough, then, to gratify my friendship by coming up to dinner this evening; and Fanny, too, of course.", "“그러면 오늘 저녁에 저녁을 먹으러 올라와서 내 우정을 흐뭇하게 해 줄 만큼은 마음을 써 주어야 해요. 물론 패니도 함께요."], ["I cannot take any excuse, for the matter is completely arranged.", "무슨 핑계도 받아 주지 않겠어요. 이 일은 벌써 다 정해 놓았으니까요."], ["I have a particular reason for wishing it.”", "나는 그러기를 바라는 데에 남다른 까닭이 있어요.”"], ["These last violent injunctions had been added because Lady Lufton had seen a refusal rising in the parson’s face.", "이 마지막의 거센 분부가 덧붙은 것은, 목사의 얼굴에 마다하는 낯빛이 떠오르는 것을 레이디 러프턴이 보았기 때문이었다."], ["Poor Lady Lufton!", "딱한 레이디 러프턴!"], ["Her enemies—for even she had enemies—used to declare of her, that an invitation to dinner was the only method of showing itself of which her good-humour was cognizant.", "그녀의 원수들은 — 그녀에게도 원수가 있었으니까 — 그녀를 두고 늘 이렇게 단언하곤 했다. 그녀의 좋은 마음이 스스로를 드러낼 줄 아는 방법이라고는 저녁 초대뿐이라고."], ["But let me ask of her enemies whether it is not as good a method as any other known to be extant?", "그렇지만 그 원수들에게 내가 물어보게 해 다오. 그것이 지금 이 세상에 있는 줄로 알려진 다른 어떤 방법에도 못하지 않게 좋은 방법이 아니겠느냐고."], ["Under such orders as these obedience was of course a necessity, and he promised that he, with his wife, would come across to dinner.", "이런 분부 아래에서 순종은 물론 어쩔 수 없는 것이었으므로, 그는 아내와 함께 저녁을 먹으러 건너오겠다고 약속했다."], ["And then, when he went away, Lady Lufton ordered her carriage.", "그러고 나서 그가 물러가자 레이디 러프턴은 마차를 내오라고 일렀다."]], [["During these doings at Framley, Lucy Robarts still remained at Hogglestock, nursing Mrs. Crawley.", "프램리에서 이런 일들이 벌어지는 동안에도 루시 로바츠는 여전히 호글스톡에 남아 크롤리 부인을 간병하고 있었다."], ["Nothing occurred to take her back to Framley, for the same note from Fanny which gave her the first tidings of the arrival of the Philistines told her also of their departure—and also of the source from whence relief had reached them.", "그녀를 프램리로 불러들일 일은 아무것도 생기지 않았다. 블레셋 사람들이 들이닥쳤다는 첫 소식을 전해 준 패니의 짧은 편지가 그들이 물러갔다는 것도 함께 알려 주었기 때문이고 — 구원이 어디에서 그들에게 이르렀는지도 함께 알려 주었기 때문이다."], ["“Don’t come, therefore, for that reason,”", "“그러니 그 일 때문에는 오지 말아요.” 그 편지는 이렇게 말했다."], ["said the note, “but, nevertheless, do come as quickly as you can, for the whole house is sad without you.”", "“그렇지만 그래도 될 수 있는 대로 빨리 와요. 루시가 없으니 온 집이 쓸쓸해요.”"]], [["On the morning after the receipt of this note Lucy was sitting, as was now usual with her, beside an old arm-chair to which her patient had lately been promoted.", "이 편지를 받은 이튿날 아침, 루시는 이제 여느 때처럼 낡은 안락의자 옆에 앉아 있었다. 얼마 전부터 그녀의 병자가 그 의자로 승진해 옮겨 앉게 되었던 것이다."], ["The fever had gone, and Mrs. Crawley was slowly regaining her strength—very slowly, and with frequent caution from the Silverbridge doctor that any attempt at being well too fast might again precipitate her into an abyss of illness and domestic inefficiency.", "열병은 물러갔고 크롤리 부인은 천천히 기운을 되찾고 있었다 — 몹시 천천히였고, 너무 빨리 나으려고 애쓰다가는 병과 집안일을 못 하는 무력의 깊은 구렁으로 다시 곤두박질칠 수도 있다는 실버브리지 의사의 주의를 자주 들으면서였다."]], [["“I really think I can get about to-morrow,”", "“내일이면 정말로 움직일 수 있겠어요.” 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “and then, dear Lucy, I need not keep you longer from your home.”", "“그러면, 루시, 더 이상 루시를 집에서 떼어 놓고 붙들어 둘 일이 없겠지요.”"]], [["“You are in a great hurry to get rid of me, I think. I suppose Mr.", "“나를 떼어 내려고 꽤 서두르시는 것 같아요."], ["Crawley has been complaining again about the cream in his tea.” Mr.", "크롤리 씨가 차에 든 크림을 두고 또 뭐라 하셨나 보군요.”"], ["Crawley had on one occasion stated his assured conviction that surreptitious daily supplies were being brought into the house, because he had detected the presence of cream instead of milk in his own cup.", "크롤리 씨는 한번, 날마다 몰래 먹을 것이 이 집으로 들어오고 있다고 굳게 믿는 바를 밝힌 적이 있었다. 제 잔에 우유가 아니라 크림이 들어 있는 것을 알아냈기 때문이었다."], ["As, however, the cream had been going for sundry days before this, Miss Robarts had not thought much of his ingenuity in making the discovery.", "그러나 크림은 그 전에도 여러 날 들어오고 있었으므로, 그것을 알아낸 그의 밝은 눈을 로바츠 양은 그리 대단하게 여기지 않았다."]], [["“Ah, you do not know how he speaks of you when your back is turned.”", "“아, 루시가 등을 돌리고 있을 때 그이가 루시를 어떻게 말하는지 루시는 모르지요.”"]], [["“And how does he speak of me?", "“그러면 나를 어떻게 말하시나요?"], ["I know you would not have the courage to tell me the whole.”", "다 말해 줄 용기는 없으실 텐데요.”"]], [["“No, I have not; for you would think it absurd coming from one who looks like him.", "“없어요. 그이처럼 보이는 사람의 입에서 나온 말이라면 루시는 어처구니없다고 여길 테니까요."], ["He says that if he were to write a poem about womanhood, he would make you the heroine.”", "여자다움을 두고 시를 쓴다면 루시를 그 여주인공으로 삼겠다고 그이가 그래요.”"]], [["“With a cream-jug in my hand, or else sewing buttons on to a shirt-collar.", "“크림 단지를 손에 들고 있거나, 아니면 셔츠 깃에 단추를 달고 있는 여주인공이겠지요."], ["But he never forgave me about the mutton broth.", "그렇지만 그이는 양고기 국물 일을 두고 나를 끝내 용서하지 않으셨어요."], ["He told me, in so many words, that I was a—storyteller.", "대놓고 그 말 그대로, 내가 — 꾸며 대는 사람이라고 하셨어요."], ["And for the matter of that, my dear, so I was.”", "그리고 그 일로 보자면, 크롤리 부인, 정말 내가 그랬지요.”"]], [["“He told me that you were an angel.”", "“그이는 나에게 루시가 천사라고 했어요.”"]], [["“Goodness gracious!”", "“원 세상에!”"]], [["“A ministering angel.", "“돌보는 천사라고요."], ["And so you have been.", "그리고 정말 그랬어요."], ["I can almost feel it in my heart to be glad that I have been ill, seeing that I have had you for my friend.”", "루시를 벗으로 얻었으니, 내가 앓았던 것이 기쁘다고 할 마음까지도 거의 들어요.”"]], [["“But you might have had that good fortune without the fever.”", "“그렇지만 열병 없이도 그 복은 얻을 수 있었을 텐데요.”"]], [["“No, I should not.", "“아니에요, 얻지 못했을 거예요."], ["In my married life I have made no friends till my illness brought you to me; nor should I ever really have known you but for that.", "혼인해 살아온 동안 나는 벗을 하나도 사귀지 못했어요. 내 병이 루시를 내게 데려다주기까지는요."], ["How should I get to know any one?”", "그 일이 없었다면 루시를 참으로 알게 되는 일도 결코 없었을 거예요. 내가 어떻게 누구를 알게 되겠어요?”"]], [["“You will now, Mrs. Crawley; will you not?", "“이제는 그러시겠지요, 크롤리 부인."], ["Promise that you will.", "그렇지 않아요?"], ["You will come to us at Framley when you are well?", "그러겠다고 약속해 주세요. 몸이 나으면 프램리로 우리를 찾아오시겠지요?"], ["You have promised already, you know.”", "벌써 약속하셨잖아요.”"]], [["“You made me do so when I was too weak to refuse.”", "“내가 너무 힘이 없어서 마다할 수도 없을 때 루시가 그러게 만들었지요.”"]], [["“And I shall make you keep your promise too.", "“그 약속을 지키게도 내가 만들 거예요."], ["He shall come, also, if he likes; but you shall come whether he likes or no.", "그이도 오고 싶으면 오시라고 하세요. 그렇지만 부인은 그이가 좋다 하든 싫다 하든 오셔야 해요."], ["And I won’t hear a word about your old dresses.", "그리고 낡은 옷 이야기는 한마디도 듣지 않겠어요."], ["Old dresses will wear as well at Framley as at Hogglestock.”", "낡은 옷은 호글스톡에서만큼 프램리에서도 잘 입을 수 있어요.”"]], [["From all which it will appear that Mrs.", "이 모든 것으로 보아 크롤리 부인과 루시 로바츠가 이 간병의 기간 동안 몹시 가까워졌음이 드러날 것이다."], ["Crawley and Lucy Robarts had become very intimate during this period of the nursing; as two women always will, or, at least should do, when shut up for weeks together in the same sick room.", "같은 병실에 몇 주씩 함께 갇혀 있게 되면 두 여인은 언제나 그렇게 되는 법이고, 적어도 그렇게 되어야 하는 법이다."]], [["The conversation was still going on between them when the sound of wheels was heard upon the road.", "두 사람 사이에 이야기가 아직 이어지고 있을 때 길 위에서 바퀴 소리가 들려왔다."], ["It was no highway that passed before the house, and carriages of any sort were not frequent there.", "집 앞으로 지나가는 것은 큰길이 아니었고, 어떤 종류의 마차든 그곳에는 자주 다니지 않았다."]], [["“It is Fanny, I am sure,”", "“패니예요, 틀림없이.”"], ["said Lucy, rising from her chair.", "루시가 의자에서 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“There are two horses,” said Mrs.", "“말이 두 마리예요.”"], ["Crawley, distinguishing the noise with the accurate sense of hearing which is always attached to sickness; “and it is not the noise of the pony-carriage.”", "병에는 언제나 딸려 오는 그 정확한 귀로 소리를 가려내며 크롤리 부인이 말했다. “그리고 조랑말 마차 소리도 아니에요.”"]], [["“It is a regular carriage,”", "“제대로 된 마차예요.”"], ["said Lucy, speaking from the window, “and stopping here.", "창가에서 루시가 말했다. “그리고 여기에 서고 있어요."], ["It is somebody from Framley Court, for I know the servant.”", "프램리 코트에서 온 누군가예요. 하인을 내가 알아요.”"]], [["As she spoke a blush came to her forehead.", "그렇게 말하면서 그녀의 이마에 홍조가 올랐다."], ["Might it not be Lord Lufton, she thought to herself,—forgetting at the moment that Lord Lufton did not go about the country in a close chariot with a fat footman.", "러프턴 경이 아닐까, 그녀는 속으로 생각했다 — 러프턴 경이 살진 하인을 딸린 닫힌 마차를 타고 시골을 돌아다니지는 않는다는 것을 그 순간에는 잊고 있었다."], ["Intimate as she had become with Mrs. Crawley she had said nothing to her new friend on the subject of her love affair.", "크롤리 부인과 그토록 가까워졌으면서도 그녀는 제 연애 일을 두고서는 그 새 벗에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["The carriage stopped and down came the footman, but nobody spoke to him from the inside.", "마차가 서고 하인이 내려왔으나 안에서 그에게 말을 거는 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["“He has probably brought something from Framley,”", "“프램리에서 무엇을 가져왔겠지요.”"], ["said Lucy, having cream and such like matters in her mind; for cream and such like matters had come from Framley Court more than once during her sojourn there.", "크림 따위를 머리에 두고 루시가 말했다. 그녀가 거기 머무는 동안 크림 따위가 프램리 코트에서 한두 번 온 적이 있었기 때문이다."], ["“And the carriage, probably, happened to be coming this way.”", "“그리고 마차가 아마 마침 이 길로 오게 되었겠지요.”"]], [["But the mystery soon elucidated itself partially, or, perhaps, became more mysterious in another way.", "그러나 이 알 수 없는 일은 곧 얼마쯤 밝혀졌다. 아니, 어쩌면 다른 쪽으로 더 알 수 없게 되었다."], ["The red-armed little girl who had been taken away by her frightened mother in the first burst of the fever had now returned to her place, and at the present moment entered the room, with awestruck face, declaring that Miss Robarts was to go at once to the big lady in the carriage.", "열병이 처음 터졌을 때 겁먹은 어미가 데려가 버렸던 팔이 붉은 그 어린 하녀가 이제 제자리로 돌아와 있었는데, 그 아이가 바로 이 순간 외경에 사로잡힌 얼굴로 방에 들어와, 로바츠 양이 마차에 있는 큰 마님께 당장 가야 한다고 알렸다."]], [["“I suppose it’s Lady Lufton,”", "“레이디 러프턴이신가 보군요.”"], ["said Mrs. Crawley.", "크롤리 부인이 말했다."]], [["Lucy’s heart was so absolutely in her mouth that any kind of speech was at the moment impossible to her.", "루시의 심장은 어찌나 완전히 목구멍까지 치밀어 올랐던지 그 순간 그녀에게는 어떤 말도 할 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Why should Lady Lufton have come thither to Hogglestock, and why should she want to see her, Lucy Robarts, in the carriage?", "무엇 때문에 레이디 러프턴이 이 호글스톡까지 왔을까, 그리고 무엇 때문에 마차 안에서 저를, 이 루시 로바츠를 보고자 할까?"], ["Had not everything between them been settled?", "두 사람 사이의 일은 다 정해지지 않았던가?"], ["And yet—!", "그런데도 — !"], ["Lucy, in the moment for thought that was allowed to her, could not determine what might be the probable upshot of such an interview.", "루시는 저에게 허락된 그 한순간의 생각으로는 그런 만남의 끝이 어찌 될 법한지 가려낼 수 없었다."], ["Her chief feeling was a desire to postpone it for the present instant.", "그녀의 가장 큰 마음은 그것을 지금 이 순간만이라도 미루고 싶다는 것이었다."], ["But the red-armed little girl would not allow that.", "그러나 팔이 붉은 그 어린 하녀는 그것을 허락하지 않았다."]], [["“You are to come at once,”", "“당장 오셔야 한답니다요.”"], ["said she.", "하녀가 말했다."]], [["And then Lucy, without having spoken a word, got up and left the room.", "그러자 루시는 한마디도 하지 않은 채 일어나 방을 나갔다."], ["She walked downstairs, along the little passage, and out through the small garden, with firm steps, but hardly knowing whither she went, or why.", "그녀는 아래층으로, 좁은 통로를 따라, 그리고 작은 뜰을 지나 밖으로 걸어 나갔다. 걸음은 단단했으나 어디로 가는지, 무엇 하러 가는지는 거의 알지 못했다."], ["Her presence of mind and self-possession had all deserted her.", "마음의 침착도 자기를 다스리는 힘도 다 그녀를 버리고 떠나 버렸다."], ["She knew that she was unable to speak as she should do; she felt that she would have to regret her present behaviour, but yet she could not help herself.", "마땅히 해야 할 대로 말할 수 없다는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 지금 하고 있는 이 몸가짐을 뒤에 뉘우치게 될 것을 그녀는 느꼈으나, 그러면서도 저를 어쩔 수가 없었다."], ["Why should Lady Lufton have come to her there?", "무엇 때문에 레이디 러프턴이 여기까지 저를 찾아왔을까?"], ["She went on, and the big footman stood with the carriage door open.", "그녀는 걸어 나갔고, 큰 하인이 마차 문을 열어 잡고 서 있었다."], ["She stepped up almost unconsciously, and, without knowing how she got there, she found herself seated by Lady Lufton.", "그녀는 거의 저도 모르게 올라섰다. 그리고 어떻게 거기 이르렀는지도 모르는 채, 레이디 러프턴 옆에 앉아 있는 제 몸을 발견했다."]], [["To tell the truth her ladyship also was a little at a loss to know how she was to carry through her present plan of operations.", "사실을 말하자면 마님 또한 이번 작전을 어떻게 끝까지 밀고 나가야 할지 몰라 조금 막막해하고 있었다."], ["The duty of beginning, however, was clearly with her, and therefore, having taken Lucy by the hand, she spoke.", "그러나 먼저 말을 꺼내는 소임은 분명히 그녀에게 있었으므로, 그녀는 루시의 손을 잡고 입을 열었다."]], [["“Miss Robarts,”", "“로바츠 양,”"], ["she said, “my son has come home.", "그녀가 말했다. “내 아들이 집에 왔어요."], ["I don’t know whether you are aware of it.”", "그것을 알고 있는지 모르겠군요.”"]], [["She spoke with a low, gentle voice, not quite like herself, but Lucy was much too confused to notice this.", "그녀는 낮고 부드러운 목소리로, 여느 자기 같지 않게 말했으나, 루시는 너무 어지러워서 그것을 알아차리지 못했다."]], [["“I was not aware of it,”", "“알지 못했습니다.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["She had, however, been so informed in Fanny’s letter, but all that had gone out of her head.", "그러나 그녀는 패니의 편지로 그렇게 전해 들어 알고 있었다. 다만 그 모든 것이 머리에서 빠져나가 버렸던 것이다."]], [["“Yes; he has come back.", "“그래요, 돌아왔어요."], ["He has been in Norway, you know,—fishing.”", "노르웨이에 가 있었지요 — 낚시하러요.”"]], [["“Yes,”", "“예.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“I am sure you will remember all that took place when you came to me, not long ago, in my little room upstairs at Framley Court.”", "“얼마 전에 프램리 코트 위층 그 작은 방으로 나를 찾아왔을 때 있었던 일을 다 기억하고 있을 거예요.”"]], [["In answer to which, Lucy, quivering in every nerve, and wrongly thinking that she was visibly shaking in every limb, timidly answered that she did remember.", "그 말에 루시는, 온몸의 신경이 떨리고 팔다리가 하나하나 눈에 보이게 떨리고 있다고 잘못 생각하면서, 기억한다고 조그맣게 대답했다."], ["Why was it that she had then been so bold, and now was so poor a coward?", "그때는 그토록 담대했는데 지금은 어찌하여 이토록 못난 겁쟁이인가?"]], [["“Well, my dear, all that I said to you then I said to you thinking that it was for the best.", "“그런데 얘야, 그때 내가 한 말은 다 그것이 가장 좋은 길이라고 여기고 한 말이었어요."], ["You, at any rate, will not be angry with me for loving my own son better than I love any one else.”", "내가 다른 어느 누구보다 내 아들을 더 사랑한다고 해서, 적어도 얘야, 그것 때문에 나를 노여워하지는 않겠지요.”"]], [["“Oh, no,”", "“아, 아닙니다.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“He is the best of sons, and the best of men, and I am sure that he will be the best of husbands.”", "“그 아이는 아들 가운데 가장 좋은 아들이고 사내 가운데 가장 좋은 사내예요. 그리고 남편 가운데서도 가장 좋은 남편이 될 것이라고 나는 믿어요.”"]], [["Lucy had an idea, by instinct, however, rather than by sight, that Lady Lufton’s eyes were full of tears as she spoke.", "그렇게 말할 때 레이디 러프턴의 눈에 눈물이 가득했다는 것을 루시는 알아차렸다. 보아서가 아니라 본능으로였다."], ["As for herself she was altogether blinded and did not dare to lift her face or to turn her head.", "루시 자신은 눈이 아주 멀어 버려서 얼굴을 들거나 고개를 돌릴 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["As for the utterance of any sound, that was quite out of the question.", "무슨 소리를 낸다는 것은 아예 있을 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["“And now I have come here, Lucy, to ask you to be his wife.”", "“그리고 이제 나는, 루시, 그 아이의 아내가 되어 달라고 청하러 여기에 왔어요.”"]], [["She was quite sure that she heard the words.", "그 말을 들었다는 것만은 그녀도 아주 분명했다."], ["They came plainly to her ears, leaving on her brain their proper sense, but yet she could not move or make any sign that she had understood them.", "그 말은 뚜렷하게 그녀의 귀에 들어와 그 마땅한 뜻을 그녀의 머리에 남겨 놓았다. 그런데도 그녀는 움직일 수도, 그 말을 알아들었다는 어떤 표시를 낼 수도 없었다."], ["It seemed as though it would be ungenerous in her to take advantage of such conduct and to accept an offer made with so much self-sacrifice.", "그런 처사를 이때다 하고 이용하여, 그토록 큰 자기희생으로 내놓아진 청을 받아들이는 것은 저에게 후하지 못한 일이 될 것처럼 보였다."], ["She had not time at the first moment to think even of his happiness, let alone her own, but she thought only of the magnitude of the concession which had been made to her.", "첫 순간에 그녀에게는 제 행복은 말할 것도 없고 그의 행복까지도 생각할 겨를이 없었다. 그녀는 오직 저에게 베풀어진 양보가 얼마나 큰 것인가만을 생각했다."], ["When she had constituted Lady Lufton the arbiter of her destiny she had regarded the question of her love as decided against herself.", "레이디 러프턴을 제 운명의 심판자로 세울 때 그녀는 제 사랑의 문제가 저에게 불리하게 판결된 것으로 여겼다."], ["She had found herself unable to endure the position of being Lady Lufton’s daughter-in-law while Lady Lufton would be scorning her, and therefore she had given up the game.", "레이디 러프턴이 저를 업신여기고 있는 동안에 그 며느리라는 자리에 있는 것을 견딜 수 없다는 것을 그녀는 알아 버렸고, 그래서 판을 접었다."], ["She had given up the game, sacrificing herself, and, as far as it might be a sacrifice, sacrificing him also.", "그녀는 판을 접으면서 자기를 희생했고, 희생이라 할 수 있는 한에서는 그까지도 희생했다."], ["She had been resolute to stand to her word in this respect, but she had never allowed herself to think it possible that Lady Lufton should comply with the conditions which she, Lucy, had laid upon her.", "이 일에서 제 말을 지키겠다고 그녀는 굳게 마음먹고 있었다. 그러나 그녀 루시가 마님에게 걸어 놓은 조건에 레이디 러프턴이 응하는 일이 있을 수 있다고는 스스로 한 번도 생각해 보지 않았다."], ["And yet such was the case, as she so plainly heard.", "그런데도 사실이 그러하였으니, 그녀가 그토록 뚜렷하게 들은 대로였다."], ["“And now I have come here, Lucy, to ask you to be his wife.”", "“그리고 이제 나는, 루시, 그 아이의 아내가 되어 달라고 청하러 여기에 왔어요.”"]], [["How long they sat together silent, I cannot say; counted by minutes the time would not probably have amounted to many, but to each of them the duration seemed considerable.", "두 사람이 얼마나 오래 말없이 함께 앉아 있었는지 나는 말할 수 없다. 분으로 세자면 아마 여러 분에 이르지도 않았을 것이나, 두 사람에게는 저마다 그 길이가 꽤 되는 것처럼 느껴졌다."], ["Lady Lufton, while she was speaking, had contrived to get hold of Lucy’s hand, and she sat, still holding it, trying to look into Lucy’s face,—which, however, she could hardly see, so much was it turned away.", "레이디 러프턴은 말하는 동안에 어느새 루시의 손을 잡아 두었고, 그것을 여전히 잡은 채 앉아 루시의 얼굴을 들여다보려고 애썼다 — 그러나 얼굴이 어찌나 많이 돌아가 있었던지 그녀는 그것을 거의 볼 수 없었다."], ["Neither, indeed, were Lady Lufton’s eyes perfectly dry.", "사실 레이디 러프턴의 눈도 온전히 말라 있지는 않았다."], ["No answer came to her question, and therefore, after a while, it was necessary that she should speak again.", "그녀의 물음에는 아무 대답도 오지 않았고, 그래서 얼마쯤 지나자 그녀가 다시 말을 해야 했다."]], [["“Must I go back to him, Lucy, and tell him that there is some other objection—something besides a stern old mother; some hindrance, perhaps, not so easily overcome?”", "“내가 그 아이에게 돌아가서, 무슨 다른 반대가 있다고 — 엄한 노모 말고 또 다른 무엇이, 어쩌면 그렇게 쉽게 넘을 수 없는 어떤 걸림돌이 있다고 말해야 하나요, 루시?”"]], [["“No,”", "“아닙니다.”"], ["said Lucy, and it was all which at the moment she could say.", "루시가 말했다. 그것이 그 순간 그녀가 할 수 있는 말의 전부였다."]], [["“What shall I tell him, then?", "“그러면 그 아이에게 무엇이라고 전할까요?"], ["Shall I say yes—simply yes?”", "예라고 — 그저 예라고 말할까요?”"]], [["“Simply yes,”", "“그저 예라고요.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“And as to the stern old mother who thought her only son too precious to be parted with at the first word—is nothing to be said to her?”", "“그러면 하나뿐인 아들이 너무 귀해서 첫마디에 내어 줄 수 없다고 여겼던 그 엄한 노모에게는 — 아무 말도 해 줄 것이 없나요?”"]], [["“Oh, Lady Lufton!”", "“아, 레이디 러프턴!”"]], [["“No forgiveness to be spoken, no sign of affection to be given?", "“용서한다는 말 한마디도, 사랑한다는 표시 하나도 없나요?"], ["Is she always to be regarded as stern and cross, vexatious and disagreeable?”", "그 노모는 언제까지나 엄하고 뒤틀리고 성가시고 마음에 들지 않는 사람으로 여겨져야 하나요?”"]], [["Lucy slowly turned round her head and looked up into her companion’s face.", "루시는 천천히 고개를 돌려 옆에 앉은 이의 얼굴을 올려다보았다."], ["Though she had as yet no voice to speak of affection she could fill her eyes with love, and in that way make to her future mother all the promises that were needed.", "사랑을 말할 목소리는 아직 없었으나 눈만은 사랑으로 가득 채울 수 있었고, 그렇게 하여 앞으로 어머니가 될 이에게 필요한 모든 약속을 다 할 수 있었다."]], [["“Lucy, dearest Lucy, you must be very dear to me now.”", "“루시, 내 귀한 루시, 이제 루시는 나에게 몹시 귀한 사람이어야 해요.”"], ["And then they were in each other’s arms, kissing each other.", "그러고 나서 두 사람은 서로의 품에 안겨 서로에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["Lady Lufton now desired her coachman to drive up and down for some little space along the road while she completed her necessary conversation with Lucy.", "레이디 러프턴은 이제 마부에게 길을 따라 얼마쯤 오르내리며 마차를 몰라고 일렀다. 그동안에 루시와 해 두어야 할 이야기를 끝내려는 것이었다."], ["She wanted at first to carry her back to Framley that evening, promising to send her again to Mrs. Crawley on the following morning—“till some permanent arrangement could be made,”", "그녀는 처음에는 그날 저녁에 루시를 프램리로 데려가고 싶어 하며, 이튿날 아침에 다시 크롤리 부인에게 보내 주겠다고 약속했다 — “무언가 두고 쓸 조처가 마련될 때까지”라고 했는데, 그 말로 레이디 러프턴이 뜻한 것은 앞날의 며느리 자리에 제대로 된 간병인을 대신 들여놓겠다는 것이었다."], ["by which Lady Lufton intended the substitution of a regular nurse for her future daughter-in-law, seeing that Lucy Robarts was now invested in her eyes with attributes which made it unbecoming that she should sit in attendance at Mrs. Crawley’s bedside.", "이제 그녀의 눈에 루시 로바츠는, 크롤리 부인의 병상 옆에 앉아 시중드는 것이 걸맞지 않게 되는 그런 자격을 입고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But Lucy would not go back to Framley on that evening; no, nor on the next morning.", "그러나 루시는 그날 저녁에 프램리로 돌아가려 하지 않았다. 아니, 이튿날 아침에도 그러려 하지 않았다."], ["She would be so glad if Fanny would come to her there, and then she would arrange about going home.", "패니가 여기로 저를 찾아와 준다면 몹시 기쁘겠고, 그러고 나서 집에 돌아가는 일을 정하겠다고 했다."]], [["“But, Lucy, dear, what am I to say to Ludovic?", "“그렇지만 루시, 루도빅에게는 내가 무엇이라고 해야 하나요?"], ["Perhaps you would feel it awkward if he were to come to see you here.”", "그 아이가 여기로 보러 온다면 어쩌면 거북하겠지요.”"]], [["“Oh, yes, Lady Lufton; pray tell him not to do that.”", "“아, 예, 레이디 러프턴. 부디 그러지 마시라고 그분께 일러 주십시오.”"]], [["“And is that all that I am to tell him?”", "“그리고 내가 그 아이에게 전할 말이 그것뿐인가요?”"]], [["“Tell him—tell him—He won’t want you to tell him anything;—only I should like to be quiet for a day, Lady Lufton.”", "“그분께 — 그분께 — 그분은 마님께서 아무것도 전해 주시기를 바라지 않으실 겁니다. 다만 저는 하루만 조용히 있고 싶습니다, 레이디 러프턴.”"]], [["“Well, dearest, you shall be quiet; the day after to-morrow then.—Mind we must not spare you any longer, because it will be right that you should be at home now.", "“그래요, 얘야, 조용히 있게 해 주지요. 그러면 모레예요."], ["He would think it very hard if you were to be so near, and he was not to be allowed to look at you.", "— 그보다 더 오래는 내어 줄 수 없다는 것을 알아 두어요. 이제는 집에 있는 것이 옳은 일이 될 테니까요."], ["And there will be some one else who will want to see you.", "그토록 가까이 있는데 눈으로 볼 수도 없게 된다면 그 아이는 몹시 못 견딜 일로 여길 거예요."], ["I shall want to have you very near to me, for I shall be wretched, Lucy, if I cannot teach you to love me.”", "그리고 루시를 보고 싶어 할 사람이 또 하나 있을 거예요. 나는 루시를 아주 가까이 두고 싶어요."], ["In answer to which Lucy did find voice enough to make sundry promises.", "나를 사랑하도록 가르칠 수 없다면 나는 비참해질 테니까요, 루시.” 그 말에 루시는 여러 가지 약속을 할 만큼의 목소리는 찾아냈다."]], [["And then she was put out of the carriage at the little wicket gate, and Lady Lufton was driven back to Framley.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 작은 쪽문 앞에서 마차에서 내려졌고, 레이디 러프턴은 프램리로 실려 돌아갔다."], ["I wonder whether the servant when he held the door for Miss Robarts was conscious that he was waiting on his future mistress.", "그 하인이 로바츠 양을 위해 문을 잡아 줄 때 자기가 앞날의 안주인을 모시고 있다는 것을 알고 있었을까?"], ["I fancy that he was, for these sort of people always know everything, and the peculiar courtesy of his demeanour as he let down the carriage steps was very observable.", "나는 알고 있었으리라고 짐작한다. 이런 부류의 사람들은 언제나 모든 것을 알고 있는 법이고, 마차 발판을 내려놓을 때의 그 유별난 공손함은 눈에 아주 잘 띄었기 때문이다."]], [["Lucy felt almost beside herself as she returned upstairs, not knowing what to do, or how to look, and with what words to speak.", "루시는 위층으로 돌아가면서 거의 제정신이 아닌 것처럼 느꼈다. 무엇을 해야 할지, 어떤 낯빛을 지어야 할지, 어떤 말로 말을 해야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["It behoved her to go at once to Mrs. Crawley’s room, and yet she longed to be alone.", "곧바로 크롤리 부인의 방으로 가야 하는 것이 마땅했으나, 그러면서도 혼자 있기를 그녀는 몹시 바랐다."], ["She knew that she was quite unable either to conceal her thoughts or express them; nor did she wish at the present moment to talk to any one about her happiness,—seeing that she could not at the present moment talk to Fanny Robarts.", "제 생각을 감추는 것도 드러내는 것도 도무지 할 수 없다는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그리고 지금 이 순간에는 제 행복을 두고 누구와 이야기하기도 바라지 않았다 — 지금 이 순간에 패니 로바츠와 이야기할 수는 없었기 때문이다."], ["She went, however, without delay into Mrs. Crawley’s room, and with that little eager way of speaking quickly which is so common with people who know that they are confused, said that she feared she had been a very long time away.", "그러나 그녀는 지체하지 않고 크롤리 부인의 방으로 들어가서, 자기가 어지러워하고 있다는 것을 아는 사람들에게 흔히 있는 그 조금 다급하게 빨리 말하는 투로, 너무 오래 나가 있었던 것 같아 걱정이라고 말했다."]], [["“And was it Lady Lufton?”", "“그래, 레이디 러프턴이었나요?”"]], [["“Yes; it was Lady Lufton.”", "“예, 레이디 러프턴이었어요.”"]], [["“Why, Lucy; I did not know that you and her ladyship were such friends.”", "“어머, 루시. 루시하고 마님이 그렇게 가까운 사이인 줄은 몰랐어요.”"]], [["“She had something particular she wanted to say,”", "“남달리 하고 싶으신 말씀이 있었어요.”"], ["said Lucy, avoiding the question, and avoiding also Mrs.", "루시가 그 물음을 피하고 크롤리 부인의 눈길도 피하면서 말했다."], ["Crawley’s eyes; and then she sate down in her usual chair.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 여느 때 앉는 의자에 앉았다."]], [["“It was nothing unpleasant, I hope.”", "“언짢은 일은 아니었기를 바라요.”"]], [["“No, nothing at all unpleasant; nothing of that kind.—Oh, Mrs.", "“예, 언짢은 일은 조금도 아니었어요. 그런 종류의 일이 아니었어요."], ["Crawley, I’ll tell you some other time, but pray do not ask me now.”", "— 아, 크롤리 부인, 다음에 언제 말씀드릴게요. 그렇지만 지금은 부디 묻지 말아 주세요.”"], ["And then she got up and escaped, for it was absolutely necessary that she should be alone.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 일어나 달아났다. 혼자 있어야 하는 것이 도무지 어쩔 수 없는 일이었기 때문이다."]], [["When she reached her own room—that in which the children usually slept—she made a great effort to compose herself, but not altogether successfully.", "제 방 — 아이들이 여느 때 자는 방 — 에 이르자 그녀는 마음을 가라앉히려고 크게 애를 썼으나 온전히 되지는 않았다. 그녀는 종이와 압지첩을 꺼냈다."], ["She got out her paper and blotting-book intending, as she said to herself, to write to Fanny, knowing, however, that the letter when written would be destroyed; but she was not able even to form a word.", "스스로에게 말한 대로 패니에게 편지를 쓸 셈이었는데, 다 쓰고 나면 그 편지를 찢어 버리게 될 것을 알면서였다. 그러나 그녀는 한 낱말도 이루어 낼 수 없었다."], ["Her hand was unsteady and her eyes were dim and her thoughts were incapable of being fixed.", "손이 떨리고 눈이 흐리고 생각을 한곳에 붙들어 둘 수가 없었다."], ["She could only sit, and think, and wonder, and hope; occasionally wiping the tears from her eyes, and asking herself why her present frame of mind was so painful to her?", "그녀는 다만 앉아서 생각하고 궁리하고 바랄 수밖에 없었다. 이따금 눈에서 눈물을 닦아 내며, 지금의 이 마음자리가 어찌하여 저에게 이토록 아픈지를 스스로에게 물으면서였다."], ["During the last two or three months she had felt no fear of Lord Lufton, had always carried herself before him on equal terms, and had been signally capable of doing so when he made his declaration to her at the parsonage; but now she looked forward with an undefined dread to the first moment in which she should see him.", "지난 두세 달 동안 그녀는 러프턴 경에 대한 두려움을 조금도 느끼지 않았고, 늘 그와 대등한 자리에서 저를 지켜 왔으며, 그가 목사관에서 저에게 제 마음을 밝혔을 때에도 눈에 띄게 그럴 수 있었다. 그러나 이제 그녀는 그를 보게 될 첫 순간을 두고 무엇이라 이름 붙일 수 없는 두려움으로 앞을 내다보고 있었다."]], [["And then she thought of a certain evening she had passed at Framley Court, and acknowledged to herself that there was some pleasure in looking back to that.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 프램리 코트에서 지낸 어느 저녁을 떠올리고, 그것을 돌아보는 데에 얼마간의 즐거움이 있다는 것을 스스로 인정했다."], ["Griselda Grantly had been there, and all the constitutional powers of the two families had been at work to render easy a process of love-making between her and Lord Lufton.", "그리젤다 그랜틀리가 거기 있었고, 그 아가씨와 러프턴 경 사이에 구애가 순순히 이루어지도록 두 집안의 온갖 헌법적 권능이 나서서 일하고 있었다."], ["Lucy had seen and understood it all, without knowing that she understood it, and had, in a certain degree, suffered from beholding it.", "루시는 그것을 다 보고 다 알아들었으나 제가 알아듣고 있다는 것은 알지 못했으며, 그것을 바라보느라 얼마간 괴로움을 겪었다."], ["She had placed herself apart, not complaining—painfully conscious of some inferiority, but, at the same time, almost boasting to herself that in her own way she was the superior.", "그녀는 저를 한쪽으로 떼어 놓고 하소연하지 않았다 — 무언가 못한 데가 있다는 것을 아프게 의식하면서도, 그러면서 동시에 저 나름으로는 자기가 나은 쪽이라고 스스로에게 자랑하다시피 하면서였다."], ["And then he had come behind her chair, whispering to her, speaking to her his first words of kindness and good-nature, and she had resolved that she would be his friend—his friend, even though Griselda Grantly might be his wife.", "그러고 나서 그가 그녀의 의자 뒤로 와서 그녀에게 속삭이며, 다정하고 마음씨 좋은 첫마디를 그녀에게 건넸고, 그녀는 그의 벗이 되겠다고 마음을 정했다 — 그의 벗이 되겠다고, 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 그의 아내가 될지도 모르는데도."], ["What those resolutions were worth had soon become manifest to her.", "그 마음가짐이 얼마만 한 값이 나가는 것이었는지는 오래지 않아 그녀에게 드러났다."], ["She had soon confessed to herself the result of that friendship, and had determined to bear her punishment with courage.", "그녀는 오래지 않아 그 우정의 결말을 스스로에게 고백했고, 제 벌을 용기로 견디겠다고 작정했다."], ["But now—", "그런데 이제 —"]], [["She sate so for about an hour, and would fain have so sat out the day.", "그녀는 그렇게 한 시간쯤 앉아 있었고, 그대로 하루를 다 앉아 지내고 싶었다."], ["But as this could not be, she got up, and having washed her face and eyes returned to Mrs.", "그러나 그럴 수는 없는 일이었으므로 그녀는 일어나 얼굴과 눈을 씻고 크롤리 부인의 방으로 돌아갔다."], ["Crawley’s room. There she found Mr.", "거기서 그녀는 크롤리 씨까지 있는 것을 보고 크게 기뻐했다."], ["Crawley also, to her great joy, for she knew that while he was there no questions would be asked of her.", "그가 있는 동안에는 저에게 아무 물음도 던져지지 않을 것을 알았기 때문이다. 그는 언제나 그녀에게 몹시 정중했고, 옛 투의 다듬어진 예의로 그녀를 대했다 — 먹을 것을 들여오는 일을 두고 그녀가 거짓을 말했다고 소임의 의식에 밀려 나무라지 않을 수 없었던 그 한 번을 빼고는 그러했다."], ["He was always very gentle to her, treating her with an old-fashioned polished respect—except when compelled on that one occasion by his sense of duty to accuse her of mendacity respecting the purveying of victuals—, but he had never become absolutely familiar with her as his wife had done; and it was well for her now that he had not done so, for she could not have talked about Lady Lufton.", "그러나 그는 제 아내가 그리한 것처럼 그녀와 아주 터놓고 지내는 사이가 된 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그리고 그가 그러지 않았다는 것이 지금 그녀에게는 다행이었으니, 레이디 러프턴을 두고 이야기할 수는 없었을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["In the evening, when the three were present, she did manage to say that she expected Mrs. Robarts would come over on the following day.", "저녁에 세 사람이 함께 있게 되었을 때, 그녀는 이튿날 로바츠 부인이 건너올 것 같다는 말은 겨우 해낼 수 있었다."]], [["“We shall part with you, Miss Robarts, with the deepest regret,”", "“우리는 가장 깊은 애석함으로 로바츠 양을 떠나보내야 하겠습니다.” 크롤리 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Crawley; “but we would not on any account keep you longer.", "“그러나 무슨 일이 있어도 더 오래 붙들어 둘 수는 없습니다."], ["Mrs. Crawley can do without you now.", "이제 내 아내는 로바츠 양 없이도 지낼 수 있습니다."], ["What she would have done, had you not come to us, I am at a loss to think.”", "로바츠 양이 우리에게 와 주지 않았더라면 아내가 어찌 되었을지는 나로서는 헤아릴 길이 없습니다.”"]], [["“I did not say that I should go,”", "“제가 간다고는 말하지 않았어요.”"], ["said Lucy.", "루시가 말했다."]], [["“But you will,”", "“그렇지만 갈 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Crawley.", "크롤리 부인이 말했다."], ["“Yes, dear, you will.", "“그래요, 루시, 갈 거예요."], ["I know that it is proper now that you should return.", "이제는 돌아가는 것이 옳다는 것을 나도 알아요."], ["Nay, but we will not have you any longer.", "아니, 우리는 더 오래 붙들어 두지 않겠어요."], ["And the poor dear children, too,—they may return.", "그리고 딱하고 귀한 아이들도 — 그 아이들도 돌아와도 되겠지요."], ["How am I to thank Mrs. Robarts for what she has done for us?”", "로바츠 부인이 우리에게 해 주신 일을 내가 어떻게 고마워해야 할까요?”"]], [["It was settled that if Mrs.", "이튿날 로바츠 부인이 오면 루시가 함께 돌아가기로 정해졌다."], ["Robarts came on the following day Lucy should go back with her; and then, during the long watches of the night—for on this last night Lucy would not leave the bed-side of her new friend till long after the dawn had broken—she did tell Mrs. Crawley what was to be her destiny in life.", "그러고 나서 그 긴 밤을 지켜 앉아 있는 동안에 — 이 마지막 밤에는 루시가 새벽이 밝고 나서도 한참까지 새 벗의 침상 옆을 떠나려 하지 않았다 — 그녀는 제 앞날의 운명이 어찌 되기로 되어 있는지를 크롤리 부인에게 이야기했다."], ["To herself there seemed nothing strange in her new position; but to Mrs.", "저 자신에게는 제 새 처지에 이상할 것이 아무것도 없어 보였다."], ["Crawley it was wonderful that she—she, poor as she was—should have an embryo peeress at her bedside, handing her her cup to drink, and smoothing her pillow that she might be at rest.", "그러나 크롤리 부인에게는, 저처럼 — 저처럼 가난한 저에게 알 속의 귀족 부인이 병상 옆에 앉아 마실 잔을 건네주고 편히 누우라고 베개를 골라 주고 있다는 것이 놀라운 일이었다."], ["It was strange, and she could hardly maintain her accustomed familiarity.", "이상한 일이었고, 그녀는 여태 지녀 온 그 다정한 터놓음을 지켜 내기가 어려웠다."], ["Lucy felt this, at the moment.", "루시는 그 순간에 그것을 느꼈다."]], [["“It must make no difference, you know,”", "“아무것도 달라져서는 안 돼요.” 그녀가 다급하게 말했다."], ["said she, eagerly; “none at all, between you and me.", "“부인과 나 사이에서는 조금도 달라져서는 안 돼요."], ["Promise me that it shall make no difference.”", "아무것도 달라지지 않겠다고 나에게 약속해 주세요.”"]], [["The promise was, of course, exacted; but it was not possible that such a promise should be kept.", "그 약속은 물론 받아 냈다. 그러나 그런 약속이 지켜지는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["Very early on the following morning—so early that it woke her while still in her first sleep—there came a letter for her from the parsonage.", "이튿날 아침 아주 일찍 — 첫잠에 들어 있는 그녀를 깨울 만큼 일찍 — 목사관에서 그녀에게 편지가 왔다."], ["Mrs. Robarts had written it, after her return home from Lady Lufton’s dinner.", "로바츠 부인이 레이디 러프턴의 저녁 자리에서 집으로 돌아온 뒤에 쓴 것이었다."]], [["The letter said:—", "편지는 이렇게 말했다. —"]], [["How am I to congratulate you, and be eager enough in wishing you joy?", "어떻게 축하를 해야 하고, 어떻게 하면 기쁨을 함께 하겠다는 말을 넉넉할 만큼 열심히 할 수 있을까요?"], ["I do wish you joy, and am so very happy.", "나는 정말로 기쁨을 함께 하겠어요. 그리고 아주 행복해요."], ["I write now chiefly to say that I shall be over with you about twelve to-morrow, and that I must bring you away with me.", "지금 쓰는 것은 무엇보다 이 말을 하려는 것이에요. 내일 열두 시쯤 루시에게 가겠고, 그리고 나는 반드시 루시를 데리고 와야 해요."], ["If I did not some one else, by no means so trustworthy, would insist on doing it.", "내가 그러지 않으면, 도무지 그만큼 믿을 만하지 못한 다른 누군가가 꼭 그러겠다고 나설 테니까요."]], [["But this, though it was thus stated to be the chief part of the letter, and though it might be so in matter, was by no means so in space.", "그러나 이것이 이렇게 편지의 으뜸된 부분이라고 밝혀져 있었고 내용으로는 그러했을지 모르나, 차지한 자리로 보아서는 결코 그렇지 않았다."], ["It was very long, for Mrs. Robarts had sat writing it till past midnight.", "편지는 몹시 길었으니, 로바츠 부인이 자정이 지날 때까지 앉아 그것을 쓰고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["I will not say anything about him [she went on to say, after two pages had been filled with his name], but I must tell you how beautifully she has behaved.", "그분에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않겠어요 [두 장이 그분의 이름으로 채워진 뒤에 그녀는 이렇게 이어 갔다]. 그렇지만 그분이 얼마나 아름답게 처신하셨는지는 말해야 해요."], ["You will own that she is a dear woman; will you not?", "그분이 사랑스러운 여인이라는 것을 루시도 인정하겠지요, 그렇지 않아요?"]], [["Lucy had already owned it many times since the visit of yesterday, and had declared to herself, as she has continued to declare ever since, that she had never doubted it.", "루시는 어제의 그 방문 뒤로 이미 여러 번 그것을 인정했고, 그때부터 지금까지 줄곧 그래 온 대로, 저는 그것을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없다고 스스로에게 단언해 왔다."]], [["She took us by surprise when we got into the drawing-room before dinner, and she told us first of all that she had been to see you at Hogglestock.", "그분은 우리가 저녁 전에 응접실로 들어섰을 때 우리를 놀라게 하셨어요. 다른 무엇보다 먼저, 호글스톡으로 루시를 만나러 다녀오셨다고 우리에게 말씀하신 거예요."], ["Lord Lufton, of course, could not keep the secret, but brought it out instantly.", "러프턴 경은 물론 그 비밀을 지키고 있지 못하고 그것을 그대로 터뜨려 버리셨어요. 러프턴 경이 그것을 어떻게 다 이야기하셨는지는 지금 여기 적을 수 없지만, 있을 수 있는 가장 좋은 투로 하셨다는 것은 루시도 믿을 거예요."], ["I can’t tell you now how he told it all, but I am sure you will believe that he did it in the best possible manner.", "러프턴 경은 내 손을 잡고 여섯 번은 힘주어 쥐셨고, 나는 또 무슨 다른 일까지 하려나 했지요. 그렇지만 하지는 않으셨으니 시샘할 것 없어요."], ["He took my hand and pressed it half a dozen times, and I thought he was going to do something else; but he did not, so you need not be jealous.", "그리고 그분은 마크에게도 어찌나 다정하셨는지, 루시를 칭찬하는 말씀을 그렇게나 하시고, 루시 아버님께는 온갖 좋은 말씀을 다 바치셨어요. 그렇지만 러프턴 경은 루시를 데려오지 않았다고 그분을 대단히 꾸짖으셨어요."], ["And she was so nice to Mark, saying such things in praise of you, and paying all manner of compliments to your father.", "그것은 청승맞고 터무니없는 짓이라고 하셨어요. 그러나 러프턴 경이 그분이 하신 일을 두고 그분을 얼마나 사랑하고 있는지 나는 볼 수 있었어요."], ["But Lord Lufton scolded her immensely for not bringing you.", "그리고 그분도 그것을 볼 수 있었어요. 나는 그분의 투를 아니까요."], ["He said it was lackadaisical and nonsensical; but I could see how much he loved her for what she had done; and she could see it too, for I know her ways, and know that she was delighted with him.", "그분이 러프턴 경을 몹시 흐뭇하게 여기셨다는 것을 나는 알아요."], ["She could not keep her eyes off him all the evening, and certainly I never did see him look so well.", "그분은 저녁 내내 러프턴 경에게서 눈을 떼지 못하셨고, 러프턴 경이 그렇게 좋아 보이는 것은 나도 정말 한 번도 본 적이 없어요."]], [["And then while Lord Lufton and Mark were in the dining-room, where they remained a terribly long time, she would make me go through the house that she might show me your rooms, and explain how you were to be mistress there.", "그러고 나서 러프턴 경과 마크가 식당에 남아 있는 동안 — 무섭게 오래 남아 있었어요 — 그분은 나를 집 안으로 끌고 다니며 루시의 방들을 보여 주고, 루시가 어떻게 그곳의 안주인이 될 것인지를 풀어 놓으셨어요."], ["She has got it all arranged to perfection, and I am sure she has been thinking about it for years.", "그분은 그 모든 것을 나무랄 데 없이 다 갖추어 놓으셨고, 여러 해 동안 그것을 생각해 오셨던 것이 틀림없어요."], ["Her great fear at present is that you and he should go and live at Lufton.", "지금 그분의 가장 큰 두려움은 루시와 러프턴 경이 러프턴으로 가서 거기서 살까 하는 것이에요."], ["If you have any gratitude in you, either to her or me, you will not let him do this.", "루시에게 그분에게든 나에게든 고마운 마음이 조금이라도 있다면, 러프턴 경이 그러도록 두지 말아요."], ["I consoled her by saying that there are not two stones upon one another at Lufton as yet; and I believe such is the case.", "러프턴에는 아직 돌 두 개가 서로 얹혀 있는 곳도 없다고 말해서 나는 그분을 달래 드렸어요. 그리고 정말 그런 형편인 줄로 나는 믿어요."], ["Besides, everybody says that it is the ugliest spot in the world.", "게다가 그곳이 세상에서 가장 보기 흉한 곳이라고 모두들 말하잖아요."], ["She went on to declare, with tears in her eyes, that if you were content to remain at Framley, she would never interfere in anything.", "그분은 눈에 눈물을 담고 말을 이어, 루시가 프램리에 남아 있는 것으로 만족한다면 어떤 일에도 결코 끼어들지 않겠다고 단언하셨어요."], ["I do think that she is the best woman that ever lived.", "그분은 여태 살았던 어느 여인보다도 가장 좋은 여인이라고 나는 정말 그렇게 생각해요."]], [["So much as I have given of this letter formed but a small portion of it, but it comprises all that it is necessary that we should know.", "내가 이 편지에서 옮긴 것은 그 가운데 작은 몫밖에 되지 않으나, 우리가 알아야 할 것은 다 여기에 들어 있다."], ["Exactly at twelve o’clock on that day Puck the pony appeared, with Mrs.", "그날 열두 시 정각에 조랑말 퍽이 나타났으니, 로바츠 부인과 그레이스 크롤리를 뒤에 태우고서였다."], ["Robarts and Grace Crawley behind him, Grace having been brought back as being capable of some service in the house.", "그레이스는 집안에서 얼마쯤 쓸모가 있으리라 하여 다시 데려온 것이었다."], ["Nothing that was confidential, and very little that was loving, could be said at the moment, because Mr.", "그 순간에는 마음속 이야기라 할 것은 아무것도, 정다운 말이라 할 것도 아주 조금밖에 할 수 없었다."], ["Crawley was there, waiting to bid Miss Robarts adieu; and he had not as yet been informed of what was to be the future fate of his visitor.", "크롤리 씨가 거기서 로바츠 양에게 작별을 하려고 기다리고 있었기 때문이고, 그는 제집에 온 손의 앞날이 어찌 되기로 되어 있는지를 아직 전해 듣지 못했기 때문이다."], ["So they could only press each other’s hands and embrace, which to Lucy was almost a relief; for even to her sister-in-law she hardly as yet knew how to speak openly on this subject.", "그래서 두 사람은 서로 손을 힘주어 잡고 껴안을 수밖에 없었는데, 루시에게는 그것이 오히려 마음이 놓이는 일이었다. 올케에게조차도 이 일을 두고 어떻게 터놓고 말해야 할지 그녀는 아직 거의 알지 못했기 때문이다."]], [["“May God Almighty bless you, Miss Robarts,” said Mr.", "“전능하신 하느님께서 로바츠 양께 복을 내리시기를 빕니다.”"], ["Crawley, as he stood in his dingy sitting-room ready to lead her out to the pony-carriage.", "크롤리 씨가 자기의 침침한 거실에 서서, 그녀를 조랑말 마차까지 이끌어 낼 준비를 하고서 말했다. “로바츠 양은 이 집에 햇빛을 들여 주셨습니다."], ["“You have brought sunshine into this house, even in the time of sickness, when there was no sunshine; and He will bless you.", "햇빛이라고는 없던 병의 시절에까지 그리하셨습니다. 그러니 하느님께서 로바츠 양께 복을 내리실 것입니다."], ["You have been the Good Samaritan, binding up the wounds of the afflicted, pouring in oil and balm.", "로바츠 양은 착한 사마리아 사람이 되어, 괴로움을 당한 이의 상처를 싸매고 기름과 향유를 부어 넣어 주셨습니다."], ["To the mother of my children you have given life, and to me you have brought light, and comfort, and good words,—making my spirit glad within me as it had not been gladdened before.", "내 아이들의 어머니에게 로바츠 양은 목숨을 주셨고, 나에게는 빛과 위로와 좋은 말씀을 가져다주셨으니, 여태 흐뭇해진 적이 없던 내 마음속을 흐뭇하게 만들어 주셨습니다."], ["All this hath come of charity, which vaunteth not itself and is not puffed up.", "이 모든 것이 사랑에서 나온 것이니, 사랑은 자랑하지 아니하며 뽐내지 아니합니다."], ["Faith and hope are great and beautiful, but charity exceedeth them all.”", "믿음과 소망도 크고 아름답습니다. 그러나 사랑은 그 모두를 넘어섭니다.”"], ["And having so spoken, instead of leading her out, he went away and hid himself.", "그리고 그렇게 말하고 나서 그는 그녀를 이끌어 내는 대신에 저리로 가서 몸을 숨겼다."]], [["How Puck behaved himself as Fanny drove him back to Framley, and how those two ladies in the carriage behaved themselves—of that, perhaps, nothing further need be said.", "패니가 퍽을 몰아 프램리로 돌아가는 동안 퍽이 어떻게 처신했는지, 그리고 마차에 앉은 그 두 여인이 어떻게 처신했는지 — 그것을 두고서는 아마 더 말할 것이 없을 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVII. NEMESIS.", "# 제47장. 네메시스"]], [["But in spite of all these joyful tidings it must, alas!", "그러나 이 모든 기쁜 소식에도 불구하고, 아아, 이것만은 기억해 두어야 한다."], ["be remembered that Pœna, that just but Rhadamanthine goddess, whom we moderns ordinarily call Punishment, or Nemesis when we wish to speak of her goddess-ship, very seldom fails to catch a wicked man though she have sometimes a lame foot of her own, and though the wicked man may possibly get a start of her.", "공정하되 라다만투스처럼 냉엄한 그 여신 포에나—우리 요즘 사람들은 보통 그를 「벌」이라 부르고, 그의 여신다움을 두고 말하려 할 때에는 「네메시스」라 부른다—는, 제 발이 때때로 절름거린다 해도, 또 악한 사람이 어쩌다 앞질러 달아난다 해도, 그 악한 사람을 붙잡는 데 좀처럼 실패하지 않는다는 것이다."], ["In this instance the wicked man had been our unfortunate friend Mark Robarts; wicked in that he had wittingly touched pitch, gone to Gatherum Castle, ridden fast mares across the country to Cobbold’s Ashes, and fallen very imprudently among the Tozers; and the instrument used by Nemesis was Mr.", "이번 경우에 그 악한 사람은 우리의 불운한 벗 마크 로바츠였다. 알면서도 역청을 만졌고, 개서럼 성에 갔고, 발 빠른 암말들을 몰아 이 고장을 가로질러 코볼즈 애시스까지 달렸고, 그러고서 아주 경솔하게 토저네 사람들 가운데로 떨어졌으니 악했다."], ["Tom Towers of the Jupiter, than whom, in these our days, there is no deadlier scourge in the hands of that goddess.", "그리고 네메시스가 쓴 연장은 《주피터》의 톰 타워스 씨였으니, 요즘 우리 시절에 그 여신의 손에 들린 채찍으로 그보다 더 목숨을 앗을 만한 것은 없다."]], [["In the first instance, however, I must mention, though I will not relate, a little conversation which took place between Lady Lufton and Mr.", "그러나 우선 먼저, 레이디 러프턴과 로바츠 씨 사이에 있었던 짧은 이야기 하나를 말해 두어야겠다. 다만 그 내용을 늘어놓지는 않겠다."], ["Robarts. That gentleman thought it right to say a few words more to her ladyship respecting those money transactions.", "그 신사는 그 금전 거래에 관하여 마님께 몇 마디를 더 아뢰는 것이 옳다고 여겼다."], ["He could not but feel, he said, that he had received that prebendal stall from the hands of Mr. Sowerby; and under such circumstances, considering all that had happened, he could not be easy in his mind as long as he held it.", "자기가 그 참사회원 자리를 사워비 씨의 손에서 받았다는 느낌을 지울 수 없다고, 그리고 그런 사정 아래에서 그동안 벌어진 모든 일을 생각한다면 그 자리를 쥐고 있는 동안에는 마음이 편할 수 없다고 그는 말했다."], ["What he was about to do would, he was aware, delay considerably his final settlement with Lord Lufton; but Lufton, he hoped, would pardon that, and agree with him as to the propriety of what he was about to do.", "자기가 이제 하려는 일이 러프턴 경과의 마지막 정산을 상당히 늦추게 될 것을 자기도 알고 있으나, 러프턴이 그것을 용서해 주고 자기가 하려는 일이 옳다는 데에 함께 뜻을 두어 주기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["On the first blush of the thing Lady Lufton did not quite go along with him.", "이 이야기를 처음 들었을 때 레이디 러프턴은 그와 뜻이 온전히 같지 않았다."], ["Now that Lord Lufton was to marry the parson’s sister it might be well that the parson should be a dignitary of the Church; and it might be well, also, that one so nearly connected with her son should be comfortable in his money matters.", "이제 러프턴 경이 그 목사의 누이와 혼인하기로 되었으니, 그 목사가 교회의 고위 성직자인 것이 좋을 수도 있었다. 또 제 아들과 그토록 가까이 얽힌 사람이 돈 문제에서 편안한 것도 좋을 수 있었다."], ["There loomed also, in the future, some distant possibility of higher clerical honours for a peer’s brother-in-law; and the top rung of the ladder is always more easily attained when a man has already ascended a step or two.", "게다가 앞날에는, 귀족의 처남에게 더 높은 성직의 영예가 올 어떤 먼 가능성도 어렴풋이 비쳤다. 그리고 사닥다리의 맨 윗단은 사람이 이미 한두 단을 올라서 있을 때에 언제나 더 쉽게 닿는 법이다."], ["But, nevertheless, when the matter came to be fully explained to her, when she saw clearly the circumstances under which the stall had been conferred, she did agree that it had better be given up.", "그러나 그럼에도, 이 일이 그녀에게 온전히 설명되어 그 참사회원 자리가 주어진 사정을 똑똑히 보게 되었을 때, 그녀는 그 자리를 내놓는 편이 낫다는 데에 정말로 뜻을 같이했다."]], [["And well for both of them it was—well for them all at Framley—that this conclusion had been reached before the scourge of Nemesis had fallen.", "그리고 이 결론이 네메시스의 채찍이 떨어지기 전에 이르러 있었던 것은 두 사람 모두에게—프램리에 있는 그들 모두에게—다행한 일이었다."], ["Nemesis, of course, declared that her scourge had produced the resignation; but it was generally understood that this was a false boast, for all clerical men at Barchester knew that the stall had been restored to the chapter, or, in other words, into the hands of the Government, before Tom Towers had twirled the fatal lash above his head.", "물론 네메시스는 제 채찍이 그 사임을 이루어 냈다고 언명했다. 그러나 그것이 거짓 자랑이라는 것은 널리 이해되어 있었으니, 톰 타워스가 그 무서운 채찍끈을 제 머리 위로 휘둘러 돌리기 전에 이미 그 참사회원 자리가 성당 참사회에, 다시 말하면 정부의 손에 돌려져 있었다는 것을 바체스터의 성직자라면 모두 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But the manner of the twirling was as follows:—", "그러나 그 휘둘러 돌린 방식은 다음과 같았다.—"]], [["It is with difficulty enough [said the article in the Jupiter], that the Church of England maintains at the present moment that ascendancy among the religious sects of this country which it so loudly claims.", "잉글랜드 국교회가 지금 이 순간 이 나라의 여러 종파 가운데에서 자기가 그토록 큰 소리로 주장하는 그 우위를 지켜 나가는 것은 [《주피터》의 기사는 이렇게 말했다] 어지간히 어려운 일이다."], ["And perhaps it is rather from an old-fashioned and time-honoured affection for its standing than from any intrinsic merits of its own that some such general acknowledgment of its ascendancy is still allowed to prevail.", "그리고 그 우위에 대한 어떤 일반의 인정이 아직도 통하도록 허락되어 있는 것은, 아마도 국교회 자신의 어떤 본디의 값어치 때문이라기보다는 오히려 그 지위에 대한 낡은 방식의, 오래된 애착 때문일 것이다."], ["If, however, the patrons and clerical members of this Church are bold enough to disregard all general rules of decent behaviour, we think we may predict that this chivalrous feeling will be found to give way.", "그러나 만일 이 교회의 추천권을 쥔 이들과 성직에 있는 이들이 점잖은 행실의 일반 규범을 모두 무릅쓰고 무시할 만큼 대담하다면, 우리는 이 의협의 감정도 무너지고 말 것이라고 미리 말할 수 있으리라고 본다."], ["From time to time we hear of instances of such imprudence, and are made to wonder at the folly of those who are supposed to hold the State Church in the greatest reverence.", "우리는 때때로 그런 경솔함의 사례들을 듣게 되고, 국교회를 가장 크게 공경한다고 여겨지는 이들의 어리석음에 놀라게 된다."]], [["Among those positions of dignified ease to which fortunate clergymen may be promoted are the stalls of the canons or prebendaries in our cathedrals.", "운 좋은 성직자들이 올라갈 수 있는 위엄 있고 편안한 자리들 가운데에는 우리 여러 성당의 참사회원 자리가 있다."], ["Some of these, as is well known, carry little or no emolument with them, but some are rich in the good things of this world.", "널리 알려진 대로 그중 어떤 것은 딸린 보수가 거의 없거나 아예 없으나, 어떤 것은 이 세상의 좋은 것들로 넉넉하다."], ["Excellent family houses are attached to them, with we hardly know what domestic privileges, and clerical incomes, moreover, of an amount which, if divided, would make glad the hearts of many a hard-working clerical slave.", "훌륭한 살림집이 그 자리에 딸려 있고, 우리로서는 무엇인지 알기도 어려운 여러 집안의 특권이 함께 붙어 있으며, 게다가 성직에서 나오는 수입이 있으니 그 액수를 나누어 놓으면 힘겹게 일하는 성직의 종들 여럿의 마음을 기쁘게 해 줄 만하다."], ["Reform has been busy even among these stalls, attaching some amount of work to the pay, and paring off some superfluous wealth from such of them as were over full; but reform has been lenient with them, acknowledging that it was well to have some such places of comfortable and dignified retirement for those who have worn themselves out in the hard work of their profession.", "개혁은 이 자리들 사이에서도 부지런히 움직여, 보수에 얼마만큼의 일을 붙여 놓고 너무 넘치는 자리에서는 남는 재물을 얼마쯤 깎아 냈다. 그러나 개혁은 이 자리들에 관대했으니, 제 직업의 힘겨운 일에 몸이 다 닳아 버린 이들을 위하여 그런 편안하고 위엄 있게 물러앉을 자리를 얼마쯤 두는 것이 좋다는 것을 인정한 것이다."], ["There has of late prevailed a taste for the appointment of young bishops, produced no doubt by a feeling that bishops should be men fitted to get through really hard work; but we have never heard that young prebendaries were considered desirable.", "근래에는 젊은 주교를 임명하는 취향이 널리 퍼졌으니, 이는 주교란 정말로 힘겨운 일을 감당할 만한 사람이어야 한다는 느낌에서 나온 것임에 틀림이 없다. 그러나 젊은 참사회원이 바람직하게 여겨진다는 말은 우리가 여태 들어 본 적이 없다."], ["A clergyman selected for such a position should, we have always thought, have earned an evening of ease by a long day of work, and should, above all things, be one whose life has been, and therefore in human probability will be, so decorous as to be honourable to the cathedral of his adoption.", "우리는 그런 자리에 뽑히는 성직자라면 긴 하루의 일로 편안한 저녁을 벌어 놓은 사람이어야 하고, 무엇보다도 그 삶이 여태까지 반듯했으며 따라서 사람의 짐작으로 앞으로도 반듯할 것이어서 자기가 몸을 붙이게 되는 성당에 영예가 될 만한 사람이어야 한다고 늘 생각해 왔다."]], [["We were, however, the other day given to understand that one of these luxurious benefices, belonging to the cathedral of Barchester, had been bestowed on the Rev.", "그런데 우리는 요전 날, 바체스터 성당에 딸린 이 호사스러운 성직록 가운데 하나가 이웃 교구의 교구 사제인 마크 로바츠 목사에게, 성직록과 참사회원 자리를 함께 쥐는 것으로 하여 주어졌다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Mark Robarts, the vicar of a neighbouring parish, on the understanding that he should hold the living and the stall together; and on making further inquiry we were surprised to learn that this fortunate gentleman is as yet considerably under thirty years of age.", "그리고 더 알아본 끝에 이 운 좋은 신사가 아직 서른에도 한참 못 미치는 나이라는 것을 알고 놀랐다. 그러나 우리는 그의 학식과 신앙심과 행실이 자기가 든 성당 참사회에 남다른 빛을 더해 줄 만한 것일지도 모른다고 믿고 싶었으므로, 마음에 걸리기는 했으나 잠잠히 있었다."], ["We were desirous, however, of believing that his learning, his piety, and his conduct, might be of a nature to add peculiar grace to his chapter, and therefore, though almost unwillingly, we were silent.", "그런데 이제 우리 귀에, 아니 실은 온 세상의 귀에, 그 신앙심과 행실이 딱하게도 모자란다는 말이 들려왔다. 그리고 로바츠 씨를 그의 삶과 그가 어울리는 사람들로 미루어 판단한다면, 우리는 그 학식마저 의심하고 싶어진다."], ["But now it has come to our ears, and, indeed, to the ears of all the world, that this piety and conduct are sadly wanting; and judging of Mr. Robarts by his life and associates, we are inclined to doubt even the learning.", "그는 지금 이 순간, 아니 적어도 불과 며칠 전까지는, 런던의 아주 평판 나쁜 어음 할인업자 몇 사람의 소송으로 프램리의 제 목사관에 강제 집행을 들여 놓고 있었다."], ["He has at this moment, or at any rate had but a few days since, an execution in his parsonage house at Framley, on the suit of certain most disreputable bill discounters in London; and probably would have another execution in his other house in Barchester close, but for the fact that he has never thought it necessary to go into residence.", "그리고 아마 바체스터 성당 구내에 있는 그의 또 다른 집에도 강제 집행이 들어갔을 것이나, 그가 거기 거주 근무에 들어갈 필요가 있다고 여긴 적이 한 번도 없었던 까닭에 그것만은 면했다."]], [["Then followed some very stringent, and, no doubt, much-needed advice to those clerical members of the Church of England who are supposed to be mainly responsible for the conduct of their brethren; and the article ended as follows:—", "그다음에는 저희 형제들의 행실에 주로 책임이 있다고 여겨지는 잉글랜드 국교회의 성직자들을 향한, 매우 엄한, 그리고 틀림없이 꼭 필요했을 충고가 이어졌다. 그리고 기사는 다음과 같이 끝을 맺었다.—"]], [["Many of these stalls are in the gift of the respective deans and chapters, and in such cases the dean and chapters are bound to see that proper persons are appointed; but in other instances the power of selection is vested in the Crown, and then an equal responsibility rests on the government of the day.", "이 참사회원 자리 가운데 많은 것은 각 성당의 수석 사제와 참사회가 주는 것이며, 그런 경우에는 마땅한 사람이 임명되도록 살피는 것이 수석 사제와 참사회의 소임이다. 그러나 다른 경우에는 고르는 권한이 왕실에 있으며, 그럴 때에는 그날의 정부에 똑같은 책임이 놓인다."], ["Mr. Robarts, we learn, was appointed to the stall in Barchester by the late Prime Minister, and we really think that a grave censure rests on him for the manner in which his patronage has been exercised.", "우리가 들은 바로 로바츠 씨는 전임 총리의 손으로 바체스터의 참사회원 자리에 임명되었으니, 우리는 그가 자기 추천권을 행사한 방식에 대해 무겁게 책잡혀야 마땅하다고 정말로 생각한다."], ["It may be impossible that he should himself in all such cases satisfy himself by personal inquiry.", "그러한 모든 경우에 그가 몸소 알아보아 스스로 만족하기란 될 수 없는 일일지도 모른다."], ["But our government is altogether conducted on the footing of vicarial responsibility.", "그러나 우리 정부는 처음부터 끝까지 대리 책임의 바탕 위에서 운영된다."], ["Quod facit per alium, facit per se, is in a special manner true of our ministers, and any man who rises to high position among them must abide by the danger thereby incurred.", "「쿠오드 파키트 페르 알리움, 파키트 페르 세」는 우리 각료들에게 특별한 방식으로 참말이며, 그들 가운데에서 높은 자리로 올라가는 사람이라면 누구든 그로써 짊어지는 위험을 감당해야 한다."], ["In this peculiar case we are informed that the recommendation was made by a very recently admitted member of the Cabinet, to whose appointment we alluded at the time as a great mistake.", "이 별난 경우에 우리는 그 추천이 아주 근래에 내각에 들어온 어느 각료의 손으로 이루어졌다고 전해 들었으니, 우리는 그가 임명될 때에 그것을 큰 잘못이라고 빗대어 말한 바 있다."], ["The gentleman in question held no high individual office of his own; but evil such as this which has now been done at Barchester, is exactly the sort of mischief which follows the exaltation of unfit men to high positions, even though no great scope for executive failure may be placed within their reach.", "문제의 그 신사는 제 몫으로 높은 직무를 하나도 쥐고 있지 않았다. 그러나 이제 바체스터에서 저질러진 것과 같은 이런 해악은, 마땅하지 않은 사람들을 높은 자리로 들어 올릴 때 바로 그 뒤에 따라오는 종류의 재앙이니, 행정의 실패를 저지를 큰 여지가 그들 손안에 놓여 있지 않을 때조차 그러하다."]], [["If Mr. Robarts will allow us to tender to him our advice, he will lose no time in going through such ceremony as may be necessary again to place the stall at the disposal of the Crown!", "로바츠 씨가 우리로 하여금 그에게 충고를 내밀도록 허락해 준다면, 그는 그 참사회원 자리를 다시 왕실의 처분에 맡기는 데 필요할 만한 절차를 지체 없이 밟을 것이다!"]], [["I may here observe that poor Harold Smith, when he read this, writhing in agony, declared it to be the handiwork of his hated enemy, Mr.", "여기서 한마디 해 두어도 좋겠다. 가련한 해럴드 스미스는 이 기사를 읽고 괴로움으로 몸을 뒤틀며, 이것이 자기가 미워하는 원수 서플하우스 씨의 솜씨라고 언명했다."], ["Supplehouse.", "그 손을 자기는 안다는 것이었다."], ["He knew the mark; so, at least, he said; but I myself am inclined to believe that his animosity misled him.", "적어도 그는 그렇게 말했다. 그러나 나로서는 그의 앙심이 그를 잘못 이끌었다고 믿고 싶다."], ["I think that one greater than Mr. Supplehouse had taken upon himself the punishment of our poor vicar.", "서플하우스 씨보다 더 큰 이가 우리 가련한 교구 사제를 벌하는 일을 스스로 떠맡았다고 나는 생각한다."]], [["This was very dreadful to them all at Framley, and, when first read, seemed to crush them to atoms. Poor Mrs.", "이것은 프램리에 있는 그들 모두에게 매우 끔찍한 일이었고, 처음 읽었을 때에는 그들을 가루가 되도록 으깨어 놓는 듯했다."], ["Robarts, when she heard it, seemed to think that for them the world was over.", "가련한 로바츠 부인은 그것을 듣고 저희에게는 세상이 다 끝났다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["An attempt had been made to keep it from her, but such attempts always fail, as did this.", "그것을 그녀에게 감추어 두려는 애가 있었으나, 그런 애는 늘 실패하는 법이고 이번에도 그러했다."], ["The article was copied into all the good-natured local newspapers, and she soon discovered that something was being hidden.", "기사는 인정 많은 지방 신문마다 그대로 옮겨 실렸고, 그녀는 곧 무언가가 감추어지고 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 마침내 남편이 그것을 그녀에게 보여 주었고, 그러자 몇 시간 동안 그녀는 아주 무너져 버렸다."], ["At last it was shown to her by her husband, and then for a few hours she was annihilated; for a few days she was unwilling to show herself; and for a few weeks she was very sad.", "며칠 동안은 얼굴을 내밀고 싶어 하지 않았고, 몇 주 동안은 몹시 슬펐다. 그러나 그 뒤로는 세상이 전에 하던 대로 거의 그대로 굴러가는 듯했다."], ["But after that the world seemed to go on much as it had done before; the sun shone upon them as warmly as though the article had not been written; and not only the sun of heaven, which, as a rule, is not limited in his shining by any display of pagan thunder, but also the genial sun of their own sphere, the warmth and light of which were so essentially necessary to their happiness.", "해는 그 기사가 씌어지지 않았을 때만큼이나 따습게 그들 위를 비추었다. 하늘의 해만 그런 것이 아니었으니, 하늘의 해야 대개 이교의 우레를 아무리 늘어놓아도 그 비침이 줄어들지 않는 법이다."], ["Neighbouring rectors did not look glum, nor did the rectors’ wives refuse to call.", "그들 자신의 세계를 비추는 그 다정한 해도 그러했으니, 그 따스함과 빛은 그들의 행복에 그토록 없어서는 안 될 것이었다."], ["The people in the shops at Barchester did not regard her as though she were a disgraced woman, though it must be acknowledged that Mrs.", "이웃 주임 사제들은 뿌루퉁한 얼굴을 하지 않았고, 그 사제들의 부인들이 찾아오기를 마다하지도 않았다. 바체스터의 가게 사람들도 그녀를 수치를 입은 여인처럼 보지 않았다."], ["Proudie passed her in the close with the coldest nod of recognition.", "다만 프라우디 부인이 성당 구내에서 그녀를 지나칠 때 알아보았다는 표시로 가장 차가운 고갯짓을 했다는 것만은 인정해야 한다."]], [["On Mrs. Proudie’s mind alone did the article seem to have any enduring effect.", "오직 프라우디 부인의 마음에만 그 기사가 오래 남는 효험을 내는 듯했다."], ["In one respect it was, perhaps, beneficial; Lady Lufton was at once induced by it to make common cause with her own clergyman, and thus the remembrance of Mr.", "한 가지 점에서는 그것이 어쩌면 이로웠다."], ["Robarts’ sins passed away the quicker from the minds of the whole Framley Court household.", "레이디 러프턴이 그 기사 때문에 곧바로 제 성직자와 한편이 되도록 이끌렸으니, 그리하여 로바츠 씨의 죄에 대한 기억이 프램리 코트 온 집안 사람들의 마음에서 더 빨리 사라져 갔던 것이다."]], [["And, indeed, the county at large was not able to give to the matter that undivided attention which would have been considered its due at periods of no more than ordinary interest.", "그리고 사실 이 주 전체로 보아도, 여느 때만큼의 관심밖에 없는 시절이라면 이 일이 마땅히 받았으리라고 여겨졌을 그 온전한 주목을 이 일에 쏟을 형편이 아니었다."], ["At the present moment preparations were being made for a general election, and although no contest was to take place in the eastern division, a very violent fight was being carried on in the west; and the circumstances of that fight were so exciting that Mr.", "지금 이 순간에는 총선 준비가 이루어지고 있었고, 동부 지구에서는 겨룸이 벌어지지 않게 되어 있었으나 서부에서는 매우 격렬한 싸움이 벌어지고 있었다."], ["Robarts and his article were forgotten before their time. An edict had gone forth from Gatherum Castle directing that Mr.", "그리고 그 싸움의 사정이 어찌나 사람을 들뜨게 하였던지, 로바츠 씨와 그의 기사는 제 시절이 다하기도 전에 잊혀졌다."], ["Sowerby should be turned out, and an answering note of defiance had been sounded from Chaldicotes, protesting on behalf of Mr. Sowerby, that the duke’s behest would not be obeyed.", "사워비 씨를 쫓아내라는 칙령이 개서럼 성에서 나왔고, 그에 맞서는 항명의 나팔 소리가 콜디코츠에서 울려, 사워비 씨를 위하여 공작의 분부에 따르지 않겠다고 항의했던 것이다."]], [["There are two classes of persons in this realm who are constitutionally inefficient to take any part in returning members to Parliament—peers, namely, and women; and yet it was soon known through the whole length and breadth of the county that the present electioneering fight was being carried on between a peer and a woman.", "이 나라에는 헌법상 의원을 뽑아 의회로 보내는 일에 아무런 몫도 할 수 없는 두 부류의 사람이 있다. 곧 귀족과 여자다."], ["Miss Dunstable had been declared the purchaser of the Chace of Chaldicotes, as it were, just in the very nick of time; which purchase—so men in Barsetshire declared, not knowing anything of the facts—would have gone altogether the other way, had not the giants obtained temporary supremacy over the gods.", "그런데도 이번 선거 싸움이 어느 귀족과 어느 여자 사이에서 벌어지고 있다는 것이 곧 이 주의 길이와 너비를 다 통하여 알려졌다. 던스터블 양이 이를테면 바로 그 마지막 고비에 콜디코츠 수렵림의 매수인으로 공표되었던 것이다."], ["The duke was a supporter of the gods, and therefore, so Mr. Fothergill hinted, his money had been refused.", "그리고 이 매입은—바셋주 사람들이 사실은 아무것도 모르면서 언명하기로는—거인들이 신들을 누르고 잠시 우위를 얻지 않았더라면 아주 다른 쪽으로 갔을 것이라고 했다."], ["Miss Dunstable was prepared to beard this ducal friend of the gods in his own county, and therefore her money had been taken. I am inclined, however, to think that Mr.", "공작은 신들을 밀어 주는 사람이었고, 그래서 그의 돈은 거절당했다고 포더길 씨는 은근히 비쳐 말했다."], ["Fothergill knew nothing about it, and to opine that Miss Dunstable, in her eagerness for victory, offered to the Crown more money than the property was worth in the duke’s opinion, and that the Crown took advantage of her anxiety, to the manifest profit of the public at large.", "던스터블 양은 신들의 벗인 이 공작을 그의 제 고을에서 정면으로 맞설 각오가 되어 있었고, 그래서 그녀의 돈은 받아들여졌다는 것이었다. 그러나 나로서는 포더길 씨가 이 일에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다고 생각하고 싶고, 또 던스터블 양이 이기고 싶은 마음이 급한 나머지 공작이 여기는 그 재산의 값보다 더 많은 돈을 왕실에 내밀었으며 왕실이 그녀의 조바심을 이용했으니 온 국민에게는 그것이 뚜렷한 이익이 되었다고 짐작하고 싶다."]], [["And it soon became known also that Miss Dunstable was, in fact, the proprietor of the whole Chaldicotes estate, and that in promoting the success of Mr. Sowerby as a candidate for the county, she was standing by her own tenant.", "그리고 던스터블 양이 사실은 콜디코츠 영지 전체의 임자이며, 사워비 씨를 주의 후보로 밀어 당선시키려 애쓰는 것은 곧 제 소작인의 편에 서 있는 것이라는 사실도 곧 알려졌다."], ["It also became known, in the course of the battle, that Miss Dunstable had herself at last succumbed, and that she was about to marry Dr. Thorne of Greshamsbury, or the “Greshamsbury apothecary,”", "싸움이 이어지는 동안에 던스터블 양 자신이 마침내 항복했다는 것, 그리고 그녀가 그레셤스베리의 손 선생과, 아니 이제 맞은편 패가 즐겨 부르는 이름으로 하면 「그레셤스베리의 약제사」와 혼인할 참이라는 것도 알려졌다."], ["as the adverse party now delighted to call him.", "“그 사람이야 평생 돌팔이나 조금 나은 정도였네.”"], ["“He has been little better than a quack all his life,” said Dr.", "바체스터의 이름난 내과의 필그레이브 박사가 말했다."], ["Fillgrave, the eminent physician of Barchester, “and now he is going to marry a quack’s daughter.”", "“그런데 이제는 돌팔이의 딸과 혼인하려 하네.”"], ["By which, and the like to which, Dr. Thorne did not allow himself to be much annoyed.", "이런 말과 이와 비슷한 말들에 손 선생은 스스로 그다지 시달리도록 내버려 두지 않았다."]], [["But all this gave rise to a very pretty series of squibs arranged between Mr. Fothergill and Mr.", "그러나 이 모든 일에서 포더길 씨와 선거 대리인 클로저스틸 씨 사이에 오간 아주 볼만한 야유 전단들이 잇달아 나왔다."], ["Closerstill, the electioneering agent. Mr.", "사워비 씨는 「그 여인의 애완물」이라 이름 붙었고, 이 애완물을 기르는 여인에 대한 묘사가 나왔는데, 그것은 던스터블 양의 겉모습이며 몸가짐이며 나이를 두고 결코 좋게 말해 주는 것이 아니었다."], ["Sowerby was named “the lady’s pet,” and descriptions were given of the lady who kept this pet, which were by no means flattering to Miss Dunstable’s appearance, or manners, or age.", "그러고 나서 이 주의 서부 지구는 엄숙한 어조로—주와 선거구가 창 없는 담벼락과 헛간 문에 붙는 광고로 물음을 받게 되는 그런 방식으로—어느 여자에게 대표되는 것이 걸맞고 마땅한 일이겠느냐는 물음을 받았다."], ["And then the western division of the county was asked in a grave tone—as counties and boroughs are asked by means of advertisements stuck up on blind walls and barn doors—whether it was fitting and proper that it should be represented by a woman. Upon which the county was again asked whether it was fitting and proper that it should be represented by a duke.", "그러자 이 주는 다시, 어느 공작에게 대표되는 것이 걸맞고 마땅한 일이겠느냐는 물음을 받았다."], ["And then the question became more personal as against Miss Dunstable, and inquiry was urged whether the county would not be indelibly disgraced if it were not only handed over to a woman, but handed over to a woman who sold the oil of Lebanon.", "그러고 나서 물음은 던스터블 양을 겨누어 더 사사로워졌고, 이 주가 어느 여자에게 넘겨지는 것으로도 모자라 레바논 연고를 팔던 여자에게 넘겨진다면 지워지지 않을 수치를 입는 것이 아니겠느냐는 따짐이 거세게 나왔다."], ["But little was got by this move, for an answering placard explained to the unfortunate county how deep would be its shame if it allowed itself to become the appanage of any peer, but more especially of a peer who was known to be the most immoral lord that ever disgraced the benches of the upper house.", "그러나 이 걸음으로 얻은 것은 별로 없었다. 맞대응 벽보가 이 불운한 주를 향하여, 이 주가 스스로 어느 귀족의 봉토가 되도록 내어 준다면, 더구나 상원의 자리를 더럽힌 이들 가운데 가장 부도덕한 귀족으로 알려진 어느 귀족의 봉토가 되도록 내어 준다면 그 수치가 얼마나 깊을 것인지를 풀어 설명했기 때문이다."]], [["And so the battle went on very prettily, and, as money was allowed to flow freely, the West Barsetshire world at large was not ill satisfied.", "그리하여 싸움은 아주 볼만하게 이어져 갔고, 돈이 마음대로 흐르도록 허락되었으므로 서바셋주 세상은 대체로 못마땅하지 않았다."], ["It is wonderful how much disgrace of that kind a borough or county can endure without flinching; and wonderful, also, seeing how supreme is the value attached to the constitution by the realm at large, how very little the principles of that constitution are valued by the people in detail.", "선거구나 주가 그런 종류의 수치를 얼마나 많이 눈 하나 깜박하지 않고 견뎌 낼 수 있는지는 놀라운 일이다. 또한 이 나라가 전체로는 헌법에 얼마나 지극한 값을 매기는지를 볼 때, 그 헌법의 원칙들이 사람들 하나하나에게는 얼마나 값이 나가지 않는지도 놀라운 일이다."], ["The duke, of course, did not show himself.", "물론 공작은 몸을 내보이지 않았다."], ["He rarely did on any occasion, and never on such occasions as this; but Mr.", "그는 어떤 자리에서든 좀처럼 그러지 않았고, 이런 자리에서라면 결코 그러지 않았다."], ["Fothergill was to be seen everywhere.", "그러나 포더길 씨는 어디에서나 눈에 띄었다."], ["Miss Dunstable, also, did not hide her light under a bushel; though I here declare, on the faith of an historian, that the rumour spread abroad of her having made a speech to the electors from the top of the porch over the hotel-door at Courcy was not founded on fact.", "던스터블 양도 제 등불을 말 아래 감추어 두지 않았다. 다만 그녀가 코시의 여관 문 위 현관 지붕 위에 올라 유권자들에게 연설을 했다고 널리 퍼진 소문은 사실에 바탕을 둔 것이 아니었음을, 나는 여기서 사가의 신의를 걸고 언명한다."], ["No doubt she was at Courcy, and her carriage stopped at the hotel; but neither there nor elsewhere did she make any public exhibition.", "그녀가 코시에 있었고 그녀의 마차가 그 여관에 멈추었던 것은 틀림이 없다. 그러나 거기서든 다른 어디서든 그녀는 사람들 앞에 나서서 무엇을 보이는 일을 하지 않았다."], ["“They must have mistaken me for Mrs. Proudie,”", "“저를 프라우디 부인으로 잘못 본 것이겠지요.”"], ["she said, when the rumour reached her ears.", "소문이 그녀의 귀에 닿았을 때 그녀는 그렇게 말했다."]], [["But there was, alas! one great element of failure on Miss Dunstable’s side of the battle.", "그러나 아아, 던스터블 양 쪽의 싸움에는 실패의 큰 요인이 하나 있었다."], ["Mr. Sowerby himself could not be induced to fight it as became a man.", "사워비 씨 자신을 사내답게 싸우도록 이끌 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["Any positive injunctions that were laid upon him he did, in a sort, obey.", "그에게 내려진 뚜렷한 지시라면 그는 어지간히 따르기는 했다."], ["It had been a part of the bargain that he should stand the contest, and from that bargain he could not well go back; but he had not the spirit left to him for any true fighting on his own part.", "그가 이 겨룸에 나선다는 것은 그 흥정의 한 부분이었고, 그 흥정에서 그가 이제 물러날 수도 없었다. 그러나 제 몫으로 참되게 싸워 볼 기운이 그에게는 남아 있지 않았다."], ["He could not go up on the hustings, and there defy the duke. Early in the affair Mr.", "그는 선거 유세장에 올라가 거기서 공작에게 맞설 수가 없었다."], ["Fothergill challenged him to do so, and Mr. Sowerby never took up the gauntlet.", "이 일의 이른 시기에 포더길 씨가 그렇게 해 보라고 걸어 왔으나, 사워비 씨는 그 던져진 장갑을 끝내 집어 들지 않았다."]], [["“We have heard,” said Mr.", "“우리는 들었소.”"], ["Fothergill, in that great speech which he made at the Omnium Arms at Silverbridge—“we have heard much during this election of the Duke of Omnium, and of the injuries which he is supposed to have inflicted on one of the candidates.", "포더길 씨가 실버브리지의 「옴니엄 암스」 여관에서 한 그 대단한 연설에서 말했다."], ["The duke’s name is very frequent in the mouths of the gentlemen,—and of the lady,—who support Mr.", "“우리는 이번 선거 동안에 옴니엄 공작에 대하여, 그리고 각하가 후보 가운데 한 사람에게 입혔다고들 하는 그 해코지에 대하여 많은 말을 들었소."], ["Sowerby’s claims. But I do not think that Mr.", "사워비 씨의 주장을 밀어 주는 신사들—그리고 그 여인—의 입에 공작의 이름이 아주 자주 오르오."], ["Sowerby himself has dared to say much about the duke. I defy Mr.", "그러나 나는 사워비 씨 자신이 공작에 대하여 감히 무슨 말을 많이 했다고는 생각하지 않소."], ["Sowerby to mention the duke’s name upon the hustings.”", "나는 사워비 씨에게 선거 유세장에 올라 공작의 이름을 입에 올려 보라고 걸겠소.”"]], [["And it so happened that Mr. Sowerby never did mention the duke’s name.", "그리고 실제로 사워비 씨는 공작의 이름을 끝내 입에 올리지 않았다."]], [["It is ill fighting when the spirit is gone, and Mr. Sowerby’s spirit for such things was now well nigh broken.", "기운이 다한 뒤의 싸움은 서툰 싸움이 되는 법이고, 그런 일에 쏟을 사워비 씨의 기운은 이제 거의 다 꺾여 있었다."], ["It is true that he had escaped from the net in which the duke, by Mr.", "공작이 포더길 씨의 도움을 얻어 그를 얽어 놓았던 그 그물에서 그가 빠져나온 것은 사실이다."], ["Fothergill’s aid, had entangled him; but he had only broken out of one captivity into another.", "그러나 그는 한 사로잡힘에서 다른 사로잡힘으로 뚫고 나온 것일 뿐이었다. 돈이란 심상치 않은 것이다."], ["Money is a serious thing; and when gone cannot be had back by a shuffle in the game, or a fortunate blow with the battledore, as may political power, or reputation, or fashion.", "그리고 한번 나가 버린 돈은, 정치의 힘이나 명성이나 유행이 그럴 수 있는 것과 달리, 놀음판에서 패를 한번 섞는 것으로도 배틀도어 채로 한번 운 좋게 후려치는 것으로도 되돌려 받을 수 없다."], ["One hundred thousand pounds gone, must remain as gone, let the person who claims to have had the honour of advancing it be Mrs. B.", "십만 파운드가 나갔으면 나간 것으로 남아 있어야 하니, 그것을 앞당겨 내어 드리는 영예를 누렸다고 주장하는 사람이 B 부인이든 C 경이든 마찬가지다."], ["or my Lord C. No lucky dodge can erase such a claim from the things that be—unless, indeed, such dodge be possible as Mr.", "어떤 운 좋은 술수도 그런 청구를 있는 것들 가운데에서 지워 내지 못한다—사워비 씨가 던스터블 양에게 시험해 본 것과 같은 술수가 될 수 있는 경우라면 모르되."], ["Sowerby tried with Miss Dunstable. It was better for him, undoubtedly, to have the lady for a creditor than the duke, seeing that it was possible for him to live as a tenant in his own old house under the lady’s reign.", "그에게는 공작보다 그 여인을 채권자로 두는 편이 틀림없이 나았으니, 그 여인이 다스리는 아래에서 제 옛집에 소작인으로 살아가는 것이 될 수 있는 일이었기 때문이다."], ["But this he found to be a sad enough life, after all that was come and gone.", "그러나 그동안 오고 간 모든 일을 겪고 나서 이것이 어지간히 슬픈 삶이라는 것을 그는 알게 되었다."]], [["The election on Miss Dunstable’s part was lost.", "던스터블 양 쪽에서 이 선거는 지고 말았다."], ["She carried on the contest nobly, fighting it to the last moment, and sparing neither her own money nor that of her antagonist; but she carried it on unsuccessfully.", "그녀는 이 겨룸을 높은 뜻으로 이어 가며 마지막 순간까지 싸웠고, 제 돈도 상대의 돈도 아끼지 않았다. 그러나 그녀가 이어 간 싸움은 이기지 못한 싸움이었다."], ["Many gentlemen did support Mr. Sowerby because they were willing enough to emancipate their county from the duke’s thraldom; but Mr.", "많은 신사들이 정말로 사워비 씨를 밀어 주었으니, 제 주를 공작의 종살이에서 풀어내고 싶은 마음이 넉넉했기 때문이다."], ["Sowerby was felt to be a black sheep, as Lady Lufton had called him, and at the close of the election he found himself banished from the representation of West Barsetshire;—banished for ever, after having held the county for five-and-twenty years.", "그러나 사워비 씨는 레이디 러프턴이 그를 두고 부른 대로 검은 양으로 여겨졌고, 선거가 끝났을 때 그는 서바셋주를 대표하는 자리에서 쫓겨난 자기를 보게 되었다.—스물다섯 해 동안 이 주를 쥐고 있은 끝에 영원히 쫓겨난 것이었다."]], [["Unfortunate Mr. Sowerby!", "불운한 사워비 씨!"], ["I cannot take leave of him here without some feeling of regret, knowing that there was that within him which might, under better guidance, have produced better things.", "나는 얼마간의 아쉬움을 느끼지 않고서는 여기서 그와 작별할 수가 없다. 더 나은 이끌림 아래에서라면 더 나은 것을 내었을 만한 무엇이 그의 안에 있었다는 것을 알기 때문이다."], ["There are men, even of high birth, who seem as though they were born to be rogues; but Mr.", "태생이 높은 사람들 가운데에도 악당이 되려고 태어난 듯이 보이는 사람들이 있다."], ["Sowerby was, to my thinking, born to be a gentleman.", "그러나 사워비 씨는 내 생각으로는 신사가 되려고 태어난 사람이었다."], ["That he had not been a gentleman—that he had bolted from his appointed course, going terribly on the wrong side of the posts—let us all acknowledge.", "그가 신사가 아니었다는 것—그가 제게 정해진 길에서 벗어나 달아나 표주를 끔찍하게 그른 쪽으로 돌아 버렸다는 것—은 우리 모두 인정하기로 하자."], ["It is not a gentlemanlike deed, but a very blackguard action, to obtain a friend’s acceptance to a bill in an unguarded hour of social intercourse.", "사람들과 어울리는 자리의 무심한 한때를 틈타 벗의 어음 인수를 얻어 내는 것은 신사다운 짓이 아니라 아주 불한당다운 짓이다."], ["That and other similar doings have stamped his character too plainly.", "그 일과 그와 비슷한 다른 일들이 그의 사람됨에 너무나 뚜렷하게 도장을 찍어 놓았다."], ["But, nevertheless, I claim a tear for Mr. Sowerby, and lament that he has failed to run his race discreetly, in accordance with the rules of the Jockey Club.", "그러나 그럼에도 나는 사워비 씨를 위해 눈물 한 방울을 청하며, 그가 자키 클럽의 규칙에 맞추어 조심스럽게 제 경주를 달려 내지 못한 것을 애달파한다."]], [["He attempted that plan of living as a tenant in his old house at Chaldicotes and of making a living out of the land which he farmed; but he soon abandoned it.", "그는 콜디코츠의 제 옛집에 소작인으로 살면서 제가 부치는 땅에서 살림을 얻어 내겠다는 그 계획을 시험해 보았다. 그러나 곧 그것을 버렸다."], ["He had no aptitude for such industry, and could not endure his altered position in the county.", "그런 부지런한 일에 타고난 재간이 그에게는 없었고, 이 주에서 달라진 제 처지를 견딜 수도 없었다."], ["He soon relinquished Chaldicotes of his own accord, and has vanished away, as such men do vanish—not altogether without necessary income; to which point in the final arrangement of their joint affairs, Mrs. Thorne’s man of business—if I may be allowed so far to anticipate—paid special attention.", "그는 곧 스스로 콜디코츠를 내놓았고, 그런 사람들이 사라지는 대로 사라져 버렸다.—꼭 있어야 할 수입이 아주 없는 채로 사라진 것은 아니었으니, 두 사람의 얽힌 일을 마지막으로 정리할 때에 이 점에 대해서는—이렇게까지 앞질러 말해도 된다면—손 부인의 사무를 맡아보는 사람이 각별히 마음을 썼기 때문이다."]], [["And thus Lord Dumbello, the duke’s nominee, got in, as the duke’s nominee had done for very many years past.", "그리하여 공작이 낙점한 사람인 덤벨로 경이 당선되었으니, 아주 여러 해 동안 공작이 낙점한 사람이 늘 그러했던 대로였다."], ["There was no Nemesis here—none as yet.", "여기에는 네메시스가 없었다—적어도 아직은 없었다."], ["Nevertheless, she with the lame foot will assuredly catch him, the duke, if it be that he deserve to be caught.", "그럼에도 그 절름거리는 발을 가진 여신은 반드시 그를, 그 공작을 붙잡을 것이다. 그가 붙잡히기에 마땅한 사람이라면 말이다."], ["With us his grace’s appearance has been so unfrequent that I think we may omit to make any further inquiry as to his concerns.", "우리와 함께 있는 자리에 각하가 얼굴을 내민 일이 어찌나 드물었던지, 나는 우리가 각하의 일에 대해서는 더 알아보는 일을 그만두어도 좋겠다고 생각한다."]], [["One point, however, is worthy of notice, as showing the good sense with which we manage our affairs here in England.", "그러나 한 가지 점은 눈여겨볼 만하니, 여기 잉글랜드에서 우리가 우리 일을 얼마나 분별 있게 다스리는지를 보여 주기 때문이다."], ["In an early portion of this story the reader was introduced to the interior of Gatherum Castle, and there saw Miss Dunstable entertained by the duke in the most friendly manner.", "이 이야기의 앞쪽에서 독자는 개서럼 성의 안쪽으로 안내를 받았고, 거기서 던스터블 양이 공작에게 가장 다정한 방식으로 대접받는 것을 보았다."], ["Since those days the lady has become the duke’s neighbour, and has waged a war with him, which he probably felt to be very vexatious.", "그날들 이후로 그 여인은 공작의 이웃이 되었고 그와 전쟁을 벌였으니, 공작으로서는 아마 그것이 몹시 괴로운 일이었을 것이다."], ["But, nevertheless, on the next great occasion at Gatherum Castle, Doctor and Mrs. Thorne were among the visitors, and to no one was the duke more personally courteous than to his opulent neighbour, the late Miss Dunstable.", "그러나 그럼에도, 개서럼 성에서 다음번 큰 자리가 열렸을 때 손 선생과 손 부인은 손님들 가운데 있었고, 공작이 몸소 나서서 그 부유한 이웃, 곧 한때 던스터블 양이던 이보다 더 정중하게 대한 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVIII. HOW THEY WERE ALL MARRIED, HAD TWO CHILDREN, AND LIVED HAPPY EVER AFTER.", "# 제48장. 그들이 모두 결혼하여 아이 둘을 두고 그 뒤로 오래오래 행복하게 살았다는 이야기"]], [["Dear, affectionate, sympathetic readers, we have four couple of sighing lovers with whom to deal in this our last chapter, and I, as leader of the chorus, disdain to press you further with doubts as to the happiness of any of that quadrille. They were all made happy, in spite of that little episode which so lately took place at Barchester; and in telling of their happiness—shortly, as is now necessary—we will take them chronologically, giving precedence to those who first appeared at the hymeneal altar.", "친애하는, 다정한, 마음을 함께 나누어 주는 독자여, 이 마지막 장에서 우리가 다루어야 할 사랑에 한숨짓는 짝은 네 쌍이다. 그리고 나는 합창대의 우두머리로서, 그 카드리유 가운데 어느 한 짝의 행복을 두고 여러분을 더 이상 의심으로 조이는 짓은 하지 않겠다. 그들은 모두 행복하게 되었다. 얼마 전 바체스터에서 있었던 그 조그만 소동에도 그러했다. 그리고 그들의 행복을 이야기하는 데에는—이제 그리해야 하는 대로 짧게—차례를 연월에 따라 잡아, 혼인의 제단에 먼저 나타난 이들에게 앞자리를 주겠다."]], [["In July, then, at the cathedral, by the father of the bride, assisted by his examining chaplain, Olivia Proudie, the eldest daughter of the Bishop of Barchester, was joined in marriage to the Rev. Tobias Tickler, incumbent of the Trinity district church in Bethnal Green.", "그러면 칠월에, 대성당에서, 신부의 아버지가 제 심사 전속 사제의 보좌를 받아, 바체스터 주교의 맏딸 올리비아 프라우디를 베스널 그린의 트리니티 지구 교회 담임 성직자인 토바이어스 티클러 목사와 혼인으로 맺어 주었다."], ["Of the bridegroom, in this instance, our acquaintance has been so short, that it is not, perhaps, necessary to say much.", "이 경우 신랑에 대해서는 우리가 알게 된 지가 하도 짧으므로 많은 말을 할 필요는 아마 없을 것이다."], ["When coming to the wedding he proposed to bring his three darling children with him; but in this measure he was, I think prudently, stopped by advice, rather strongly worded, from his future valued mother-in-law. Mr.", "그는 혼례에 오면서 사랑하는 아이 셋을 데려오겠다고 하였으나, 앞으로 소중한 장모가 될 사람에게서 꽤 강한 어조로 나온 충고에 막혀 그 조치를 그만두었으니, 나는 그것이 지혜로운 일이었다고 생각한다."], ["Tickler was not an opulent man, nor had he hitherto attained any great fame in his profession; but, at the age of forty-three he still had sufficient opportunity before him, and now that his merit has been properly viewed by high ecclesiastical eyes the refreshing dew of deserved promotion will no doubt fall upon him.", "티클러 씨는 넉넉한 사람이 아니었고 그 직업에서 여태 큰 이름을 얻은 바도 없었다. 그러나 마흔셋의 나이에 아직 앞에 놓인 기회가 넉넉했고, 이제 그의 공적이 교회의 높은 눈에 제대로 보이게 되었으니 마땅히 받아야 할 영전의 시원한 이슬이 그에게 내릴 것을 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["The marriage was very smart, and Olivia carried herself through the trying ordeal with an excellent propriety of conduct.", "혼례는 아주 번듯했고, 올리비아는 그 힘든 시련을 훌륭한 몸가짐의 온당함으로 치러 냈다."]], [["Up to that time, and even for a few days longer, there was doubt at Barchester as to that strange journey which Lord Dumbello undoubtedly did take to France.", "그때까지, 아니 그로부터 며칠이 더 지날 때까지도, 덤벨로 경이 프랑스로 정말 떠났던 그 이상한 여행을 두고 바체스터에는 의심이 남아 있었다."], ["When a man so circumstanced will suddenly go to Paris, without notice given even to his future bride, people must doubt; and grave were the apprehensions expressed on this occasion by Mrs.", "그런 처지에 놓인 사내가 앞으로 아내가 될 사람에게조차 알리지 않고 갑자기 파리로 가 버리면 사람들은 의심할 수밖에 없다. 그리하여 이 일을 두고 프라우디 부인은 제 자식의 혼례 조찬 자리에서까지 무거운 걱정을 입에 올렸다."], ["Proudie, even at her child’s wedding-breakfast. “God bless you, my dear children,”", "“하느님이 두 사람에게 복을 내리시기를, 사랑하는 내 아이들이여,”"], ["she said, standing up at the head of her table as she addressed Mr.", "그녀가 제 식탁 상석에 일어서서 티클러 씨와 그 아내에게 말하였다."], ["Tickler and his wife; “when I see your perfect happiness—perfect, that is, as far as human happiness can be made perfect in this vale of tears—and think of the terrible calamity which has fallen on our unfortunate neighbours, I cannot but acknowledge His infinite mercy and goodness.", "“두 사람의 온전한 행복을—곧 이 눈물의 골짜기에서 사람의 행복이 온전해질 수 있는 한에서 온전한 그 행복을—보고, 또 우리 불운한 이웃에게 떨어진 그 무서운 재앙을 생각하면, 나는 그분의 끝없는 자비와 선하심을 인정하지 않을 수 없소."], ["The Lord giveth, and the Lord taketh away.”", "주께서 주시고, 주께서 가져가시느니라.”"], ["By which she intended, no doubt, to signify that whereas Mr. Tickler had been given to her Olivia, Lord Dumbello had been taken away from the archdeacon’s Griselda.", "이 말로 그녀가 뜻한 바는 의심할 것 없이, 티클러 씨는 자기 올리비아에게 주어졌으나 덤벨로 경은 부주교의 그리젤다에게서 거두어졌다는 것이었다."], ["The happy couple then went in Mrs. Proudie’s carriage to the nearest railway station but one, and from thence proceeded to Malvern, and there spent the honeymoon.", "그러고 나서 그 행복한 짝은 프라우디 부인의 마차를 타고 가장 가까운 역에서 한 역 더 간 곳까지 갔고, 거기에서 몰번으로 나아가 신혼여행을 보냈다."]], [["And a great comfort it was, I am sure, to Mrs. Proudie when authenticated tidings reached Barchester that Lord Dumbello had returned from Paris, and that the Hartletop-Grantly alliance was to be carried to its completion.", "그리고 덤벨로 경이 파리에서 돌아왔고 하틀탑-그랜틀리 결합이 끝까지 이루어지게 되었다는 확실한 소식이 바체스터에 닿았을 때, 프라우디 부인에게 그것이 큰 위로가 되었으리라고 나는 믿는다."], ["She still, however, held her opinion—whether correctly or not, who shall say?—that the young lord had intended to escape.", "그러나 그 젊은 나리가 달아날 작정이었다는 제 의견은 그녀가 여전히 버리지 않았으니—그것이 옳았는지 아닌지야 누가 말할 수 있겠는가?"], ["“The archdeacon has shown great firmness in the way in which he has done it,”", "“부주교는 그 일을 해낸 방식에서 대단한 굳셈을 보였소,” 프라우디 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs. Proudie; “but whether he has consulted his child’s best interests in forcing her into a marriage with an unwilling husband, I for one must take leave to doubt.", "“그러나 마음에도 없는 남편과의 혼인으로 제 자식을 밀어 넣으면서 그 아이에게 가장 이로운 것을 헤아렸는지는, 나만은 의심하겠다는 뜻을 밝혀 두어야 하겠소."], ["But then, unfortunately, we all know how completely the archdeacon is devoted to worldly matters.”", "하기야 불행하게도, 부주교가 얼마나 철저하게 세상일에 몸을 바친 사람인지는 우리가 다 알고 있으니.”"]], [["In this instance the archdeacon’s devotion to worldly matters was rewarded by that success which he no doubt desired.", "이 경우에 부주교가 세상일에 바친 그 정성은 그가 의심할 것 없이 바랐던 그 성공으로 갚아졌다."], ["He did go up to London, and did see one or two of Lord Dumbello’s friends.", "그는 실제로 런던으로 올라갔고, 덤벨로 경의 벗 한둘을 실제로 만났다."], ["This he did, not obtrusively, as though in fear of any falsehood or vacillation on the part of the viscount, but with that discretion and tact for which he has been so long noted. Mrs.", "그가 이 일을 한 것은, 그 자작 쪽에서 거짓말이나 흔들림이 있을까 두려워하는 사람처럼 나서서 한 것이 아니라, 그가 그토록 오래 이름을 얻어 온 그 조심스러움과 처신으로 한 것이었다."], ["Proudie declares that during the few days of his absence from Barsetshire he himself crossed to France and hunted down Lord Dumbello at Paris.", "프라우디 부인은 그가 바셋주를 떠나 있던 며칠 동안에 몸소 프랑스로 건너가 파리에서 덤벨로 경을 뒤쫓아 잡아냈다고 밝힌다."], ["As to this I am not prepared to say anything; but I am quite sure, as will be all those who knew the archdeacon, that he was not a man to see his daughter wronged as long as any measure remained by which such wrong might be avoided.", "이에 대해서 나는 아무것도 말할 준비가 없다. 그러나 부주교를 아는 사람이라면 다 그러하듯이, 그런 억울함을 피할 방도가 하나라도 남아 있는 한 그가 제 딸이 억울함을 당하는 것을 보고 있을 사람이 아니었다는 것만은 나도 아주 확실히 안다."]], [["But, be that as it may—that mooted question as to the archdeacon’s journey to Paris—Lord Dumbello was forthcoming at Plumstead on the 5th of August, and went through his work like a man.", "그러나 그것이 어떠했든—부주교의 파리 여행이라는 그 논란거리 문제야 어떠했든—덤벨로 경은 팔월 오일에 플럼스테드에 나타났고, 사내답게 제 할 일을 치러 냈다."], ["The Hartletop family, when the alliance was found to be unavoidable, endeavoured to arrange that the wedding should be held at Hartletop Priory, in order that the clerical dust and dinginess of Barchester Close might not soil the splendour of the marriage gala doings; for, to tell the truth, the Hartletopians, as a rule, were not proud of their new clerical connections.", "하틀탑 집안은 이 결합을 피할 수 없다는 것이 드러나자, 바체스터 구내의 성직자다운 먼지와 그늘진 빛깔이 혼례 잔치의 화려함을 더럽히지 않도록 혼례를 하틀탑 프라이어리에서 치르자고 일을 꾸며 보려 했다. 사실을 말하자면 하틀탑 족속은 대체로 새로 얻은 성직자 인연을 자랑스러워하지 않았던 것이다."], ["But on this subject Mrs.", "그러나 이 문제에서 그랜틀리 부인은 아주 온당하게도 꺾이지 않았다."], ["Grantly was very properly inexorable; nor, when an attempt was made on the bride to induce her to throw over her mamma at the last moment and pronounce for herself that she would be married at the priory, was it attended with any success.", "마지막 순간에 어머니를 버리고 프라이어리에서 혼례를 올리겠다고 스스로 나서서 말하도록 신부를 꾀어 보려는 시도가 있었으나, 그것도 어떤 성과도 얻지 못했다."], ["The Hartletopians knew nothing of the Grantly fibre and calibre, or they would have made no such attempt.", "하틀탑 족속은 그랜틀리 집안의 결과 그릇을 전혀 몰랐던 것이니, 알았더라면 그런 시도를 하지 않았을 것이다."], ["The marriage took place at Plumstead, and on the morning of the day Lord Dumbello posted over from Barchester to the rectory.", "혼례는 플럼스테드에서 치러졌고, 그날 아침 덤벨로 경은 바체스터에서 주임 사제관까지 역마를 갈아 가며 건너왔다."], ["The ceremony was performed by the archdeacon, without assistance, although the dean, and the precentor, and two other clergymen, were at the ceremony.", "예식은 부주교가 보좌 없이 손수 집전했으나, 수석 사제와 선창 사제와 다른 성직자 둘도 그 자리에 있었다. 그리젤다의 몸가짐의 온당함은 올리비아 프라우디의 것과 조금도 다름이 없었다."], ["Griselda’s propriety of conduct was quite equal to that of Olivia Proudie; indeed, nothing could exceed the statuesque grace and fine aristocratic bearing with which she carried herself on the occasion.", "아니, 그날 그녀가 스스로를 지닌 그 조각상 같은 우아함과 훌륭한 귀족다운 태도를 넘어설 것은 아무것도 없었다. 예식이 그녀에게 요구한 서너 마디를 그녀는 편안하고 위엄 있게 말했다."], ["The three or four words which the service required of her she said with ease and dignity; there was neither sobbing nor crying to disturb the work or embarrass her friends, and she signed her name in the church books as “Griselda Grantly” without a tremor—and without a regret.", "그 일을 어지럽히거나 벗들을 난처하게 할 흐느낌도 울음도 없었고, 그녀는 교회의 장부에 “그리젤다 그랜틀리”라고 제 이름을 서명했다—떨림도 없이, 그리고 후회도 없이."]], [["Mrs. Grantly kissed her and blessed her in the hall as she was about to step forward to her travelling carriage, leaning on her father’s arm, and the child put up her face to her mother for a last whisper.", "그랜틀리 부인은 딸이 아버지의 팔에 기대어 여행 마차로 걸어 나가려 할 때 현관에서 딸에게 입을 맞추고 복을 빌어 주었다."], ["“Mamma,” she said, “I suppose Jane can put her hand at once on the moire antique when we reach Dover?”", "그리고 그 아이는 마지막으로 귓속말을 하려고 어머니에게 얼굴을 들었다."], ["Mrs. Grantly smiled and nodded, and again blessed her child.", "“어머니,”"], ["There was not a tear shed—at least, not then—nor a sign of sorrow to cloud for a moment the gay splendour of the day.", "그녀가 말했다. “도버에 닿으면 제인이 무아르 앙티크를 바로 꺼낼 수 있겠지요?”"], ["But the mother did bethink herself, in the solitude of her own room, of those last words, and did acknowledge a lack of something for which her heart had sighed.", "그랜틀리 부인은 웃으며 고개를 끄덕였고, 다시 제 아이에게 복을 빌어 주었다. 눈물 한 방울 흘린 사람이 없었고—적어도 그때는 없었고—그날의 흥겨운 화려함을 한순간이라도 흐려 놓을 슬픔의 낌새도 없었다."], ["She had boasted to her sister that she had nothing to regret as to her daughter’s education; but now, when she was alone after her success, did she feel that she could still support herself with that boast?", "그러나 그 어머니는 제 방에 홀로 있게 되었을 때 그 마지막 말을 되짚어 생각했고, 제 마음이 한숨지어 온 무엇인가가 없다는 것을 인정했다. 그녀는 제 동생에게 딸의 교육에 대해서는 후회할 것이 하나도 없다고 자랑한 바 있었다."], ["For, be it known, Mrs. Grantly had a heart within her bosom and a faith within her heart.", "그러나 이제 제 성공을 얻고 혼자가 되었을 때, 그 자랑으로 여전히 스스로를 버텨 낼 수 있다고 느꼈던가?"], ["The world, it is true, had pressed upon her sorely with all its weight of accumulated clerical wealth, but it had not utterly crushed her—not her, but only her child.", "알려 두어야 할 일이니, 그랜틀리 부인은 가슴에 심장을 지니고 있었고 그 심장 안에 믿음을 지니고 있었다. 세상은, 참으로, 쌓여 온 성직자의 재물이라는 그 온 무게로 그녀를 무겁게 눌러 왔다."], ["For the sins of the father, are they not visited on the third and fourth generation?", "그러나 그것이 그녀를 아주 부서뜨려 놓은 것은 아니었다—그녀를 부서뜨린 것이 아니라 다만 그녀의 아이를 부서뜨렸을 뿐이었다. 아비의 죄는 삼사 대에까지 갚아지지 않는가?"]], [["But if any such feeling of remorse did for awhile mar the fulness of Mrs. Grantly’s joy, it was soon dispelled by the perfect success of her daughter’s married life.", "그러나 그러한 회한의 감정이 한동안 그랜틀리 부인의 기쁨의 그 가득함을 흠집 내었다 하더라도, 그것은 딸의 혼인 생활이 온전하게 성공한 덕에 이내 걷혔다."], ["At the end of the autumn the bride and bridegroom returned from their tour, and it was evident to all the circle at Hartletop Priory that Lord Dumbello was by no means dissatisfied with his bargain.", "가을이 끝날 무렵에 신부와 신랑은 여행에서 돌아왔고, 하틀탑 프라이어리의 온 모임에게는 덤벨로 경이 제가 한 거래를 조금도 못마땅해하지 않는다는 것이 뚜렷했다."], ["His wife had been admired everywhere to the top of his bent.", "그의 아내는 그의 마음이 바랄 수 있는 마지막 한 치까지 어디에서나 찬탄을 받았다."], ["All the world at Ems, and at Baden, and at Nice, had been stricken by the stately beauty of the young viscountess.", "엠스에서도, 바덴에서도, 니스에서도 온 세상이 그 젊은 자작부인의 위엄 있는 아름다움에 마음을 맞았다."], ["And then, too, her manner, style, and high dignity of demeanour altogether supported the reverential feeling which her grace and form at first inspired.", "그리고 또한 그녀의 몸가짐과 품과 태도의 높은 위엄이, 그녀의 우아함과 자태가 처음에 불러일으킨 그 우러르는 마음을 온통 받쳐 주었다."], ["She never derogated from her husband’s honour by the fictitious liveliness of gossip, or allowed any one to forget the peeress in the woman.", "그녀는 남의 소문을 옮기는 꾸며 낸 활기로 남편의 명예를 깎아내리는 일이 없었고, 누구든 그 여인 속의 귀족부인을 잊게 놓아두는 일도 없었다."], ["Lord Dumbello soon found that his reputation for discretion was quite safe in her hands, and that there were no lessons as to conduct in which it was necessary that he should give instruction.", "덤벨로 경은 조심스러움에 대한 제 명성이 그녀의 손에 있으면 아주 안전하다는 것을, 또 그가 몸가짐에 대해 무엇을 가르쳐 주어야 할 일이 하나도 없다는 것을 이내 알게 되었다."]], [["Before the winter was over she had equally won the hearts of all the circle at Hartletop Priory.", "겨울이 다 가기 전에 그녀는 하틀탑 프라이어리의 온 모임의 마음도 똑같이 얻어 놓았다."], ["The duke was there and declared to the marchioness that Dumbello could not possibly have done better.", "공작이 거기에 와 있었고, 후작부인에게 덤벨로가 이보다 더 잘할 수는 도저히 없었을 것이라고 밝혔다."], ["“Indeed, I do not think he could,”", "“참으로, 저도 그럴 수는 없었으리라 생각해요,”"], ["said the happy mother.", "그 행복한 어머니가 말했다."], ["“She sees all that she ought to see, and nothing that she ought not.”", "“그 아이는 보아야 할 것은 다 보고, 보아서는 안 될 것은 하나도 보지 않지요.”"]], [["And then, in London, when the season came, all men sang all manner of praises in her favour, and Lord Dumbello was made aware that he was reckoned among the wisest of his age.", "그리고 사교철이 오자 런던에서 모든 사내가 그녀를 두고 온갖 칭찬을 노래했고, 덤벨로 경은 제가 그 나이의 사람들 가운데 가장 지혜로운 축에 든다고 헤아려지고 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["He had married a wife who managed everything for him, who never troubled him, whom no woman disliked, and whom every man admired.", "그는 모든 일을 제 대신 꾸려 주고, 결코 그를 괴롭히지 않고, 어느 여인도 싫어하지 않고, 모든 사내가 찬탄하는 아내를 얻었던 것이다."], ["As for feast of reason and for flow of soul, is it not a question whether any such flows and feasts are necessary between a man and his wife?", "이성의 잔치와 영혼의 흐름 같은 것이야, 사내와 그 아내 사이에 그런 흐름과 잔치가 꼭 있어야 하는가 하는 것부터가 물어볼 일이 아닌가?"], ["How many men can truly assert that they ever enjoy connubial flows of soul, or that connubial feasts of reason are in their nature enjoyable?", "부부간의 영혼의 흐름을 즐긴다고 참으로 말할 수 있는 사내가 몇이나 되며, 부부간의 이성의 잔치가 그 본성상 즐길 만한 것이라고 말할 수 있는 사내가 몇이나 되겠는가?"], ["But a handsome woman at the head of your table, who knows how to dress, and how to sit, and how to get in and out of her carriage—who will not disgrace her lord by her ignorance, or fret him by her coquetry, or disparage him by her talent—how beautiful a thing it is!", "그러나 제 식탁 상석에 앉은 아름다운 여인이, 옷을 어떻게 입어야 하는지, 어떻게 앉아야 하는지, 마차에 어떻게 오르고 내려야 하는지를 아는 여인이—무지로 제 주인을 망신시키지 않고, 교태로 그를 애태우게 하지 않고, 제 재주로 그를 깎아내리지 않을 여인이—얼마나 아름다운 것인가!"], ["For my own part I think that Griselda Grantly was born to be the wife of a great English peer.", "나로서는 그리젤다 그랜틀리가 위대한 영국 귀족의 아내가 되도록 태어난 사람이라고 생각한다."]], [["“After all, then,”", "“그러니까 결국,”"], ["said Miss Dunstable, speaking of Lady Dumbello—she was Mrs. Thorne at this time—“after all, there is some truth in what our quaint latter-day philosopher tells us—‘Great are thy powers, O Silence!’”", "레이디 덤벨로를 이야기하며 던스터블 양이 말했다—이때 그녀는 손 부인이었다—“결국 우리의 그 별난 요즈음 철학자가 우리에게 일러 주는 말에도 얼마쯤 참이 있는 것이지요—‘오, 침묵이여, 그대의 힘은 위대하도다!’”"]], [["The marriage of our old friends Dr. Thorne and Miss Dunstable was the third on the list, but that did not take place till the latter end of September.", "우리의 오랜 벗 손 선생과 던스터블 양의 혼례는 목록에서 세 번째였으나, 그것은 구월이 다 갈 무렵에야 이루어졌다."], ["The lawyers on such an occasion had no inconsiderable work to accomplish, and though the lady was not coy, nor the gentleman slow, it was not found practicable to arrange an earlier wedding.", "이런 경우에 변호사들이 해내야 할 일이 적지 않았고, 그리하여 그 부인이 내숭을 떤 것도 아니고 그 신사가 느린 것도 아니었으나 더 이른 혼례를 짜 맞추는 것은 될 일이 아니었다."], ["The ceremony was performed at St. George’s, Hanover Square, and was not brilliant in any special degree.", "예식은 하노버 스퀘어의 세인트 조지 교회에서 치러졌고, 어느 특별한 점에서도 빛나지는 않았다."], ["London at the time was empty, and the few persons whose presence was actually necessary were imported from the country for the occasion. The bride was given away by Dr.", "그때 런던은 비어 있었고, 참석이 실제로 꼭 있어야 할 몇 사람만 그 일을 위해 시골에서 실려 올라왔다."], ["Easyman, and the two bridesmaids were ladies who had lived with Miss Dunstable as companions. Young Mr.", "신부를 넘겨주는 소임은 이지먼 선생이 맡았고, 신부 들러리 둘은 던스터블 양의 말벗으로 함께 살아온 부인들이었다."], ["Gresham and his wife were there, as was also Mrs. Harold Smith, who was not at all prepared to drop her old friend in her new sphere of life.", "젊은 그레셤 씨와 그 아내가 그 자리에 있었고, 새로운 삶의 자리에 든 옛 벗을 놓아 버릴 마음이 조금도 없던 해럴드 스미스 부인도 그러했다."]], [["“We shall call her Mrs. Thorne instead of Miss Dunstable, and I really think that that will be all the difference,”", "“우리는 그이를 던스터블 양이 아니라 손 부인이라고 부르게 될 텐데, 참으로 나는 달라지는 것이 그것뿐이리라 생각해요,”"], ["said Mrs. Harold Smith.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다."]], [["To Mrs. Harold Smith that probably was all the difference, but it was not so to the persons most concerned.", "해럴드 스미스 부인에게는 아마 그것이 달라지는 것의 전부였을 터이나, 가장 관계된 두 사람에게는 그렇지 않았다."]], [["According to the plan of life arranged between the doctor and his wife she was still to keep up her house in London, remaining there during such period of the season as she might choose, and receiving him when it might appear good to him to visit her; but he was to be the master in the country.", "손 선생과 그 아내 사이에 마련된 살림의 계획에 따르면, 그녀는 런던의 집을 그대로 지켜 사교철 가운데 제가 고르는 동안은 거기에 머물면서, 그가 그녀를 찾아오는 것이 좋게 여겨질 때에 그를 맞이하기로 되어 있었다. 그러나 시골에서는 그가 주인이 되기로 되어 있었다."], ["A mansion at the Chace was to be built, and till such time as that was completed, they would keep on the old house at Greshamsbury.", "체이스에 저택을 한 채 지을 것이었고, 그것이 다 될 때까지는 그레셤스베리의 옛집을 그대로 두고 쓸 것이었다."], ["Into this, small as it was, Mrs. Thorne,—in spite of her great wealth,—did not disdain to enter.", "그 집이 작기는 했으나, 손 부인은—그 큰 재산에도—거기에 들어가는 것을 낮잡아 보지 않았다."], ["But subsequent circumstances changed their plans. It was found that Mr.", "그러나 그 뒤의 사정이 두 사람의 계획을 바꾸어 놓았다."], ["Sowerby could not or would not live at Chaldicotes; and, therefore, in the second year of their marriage, that place was prepared for them. They are now well known to the whole county as Dr.", "사워비 씨가 콜디코츠에 살 수 없거나 살려 하지 않는다는 것이 드러났고, 그리하여 두 사람이 혼인한 둘째 해에 그곳이 두 사람을 위해 마련되었다."], ["and Mrs. Thorne of Chaldicotes,—of Chaldicotes, in distinction to the well-known Thornes of Ullathorne in the eastern division.", "두 사람은 이제 주 전체에 콜디코츠의 손 선생 부부로 널리 알려져 있다—콜디코츠의 손 선생 부부라 함은, 주의 동부 지구에 있는 그 잘 알려진 울라손의 손 씨네와 구별하기 위한 것이다."], ["Here they live respected by their neighbours, and on terms of alliance both with the Duke of Omnium and with Lady Lufton.", "여기에서 두 사람은 이웃들의 존중을 받으며, 옴니엄 공작과도 레이디 러프턴과도 동맹의 사이를 맺고 살아간다."], ["“Of course those dear old avenues will be very sad to me,” said Mrs.", "“물론 그 정든 옛 가로수 길이야 저에게는 몹시 슬프겠지요,”"], ["Harold Smith, when at the end of a London season she was invited down to Chaldicotes; and as she spoke she put her handkerchief up to her eyes.", "어느 런던 사교철이 끝날 무렵 콜디코츠로 내려오라는 청을 받았을 때 해럴드 스미스 부인이 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하면서 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“Well, dear, what can I do?”", "“글쎄, 이봐요, 내가 어쩌겠어요?”"], ["said Mrs. Thorne.", "손 부인이 말했다."], ["“I can’t cut them down; the doctor would not let me.”", "“나로서는 그것을 베어 낼 수 없어요. 선생님이 그리하게 놓아두지 않을 테니까요.”"]], [["“Oh, no,” said Mrs.", "“아, 그럴 수야 없지요,”"], ["Harold Smith, sighing; and in spite of her feelings she did visit Chaldicotes.", "해럴드 스미스 부인이 한숨지으며 말했다. 그리고 그런 심정에도 그녀는 정말로 콜디코츠를 찾아갔다."]], [["But it was October before Lord Lufton was made a happy man;—that is, if the fruition of his happiness was a greater joy than the anticipation of it.", "그러나 러프턴 경이 행복한 사내가 된 것은 시월에 이르러서였다—다만 그 행복을 손에 쥐고 누리는 것이 그것을 미리 그려 보는 것보다 더 큰 기쁨이었다면 말이다."], ["I will not say that the happiness of marriage is like the Dead Sea fruit,—an apple which, when eaten, turns to bitter ashes in the mouth.", "혼인의 행복이 사해의 열매 같다고—입에 넣으면 입안에서 쓴 재로 변하는 사과 같다고 나는 말하지 않겠다."], ["Such pretended sarcasm would be very false.", "그런 짐짓 꾸민 비꼼은 아주 거짓일 것이다."], ["Nevertheless, is it not the fact that the sweetest morsel of love’s feast has been eaten, that the freshest, fairest blush of the flower has been snatched and has passed away, when the ceremony at the altar has been performed, and legal possession has been given?", "그러나 그럼에도, 제단에서 예식이 치러지고 법이 인정하는 소유가 주어지는 그때에 사랑의 잔치에서 가장 달콤한 한 점은 이미 먹혀 버렸고 그 꽃의 가장 싱싱하고 가장 고운 붉은빛은 이미 낚아채여 지나가 버린 것이 사실이 아닌가?"], ["There is an aroma of love, an undefinable delicacy of flavour, which escapes and is gone before the church portal is left, vanishing with the maiden name, and incompatible with the solid comfort appertaining to the rank of wife.", "사랑에는 어떤 향이, 뭐라 정할 수 없는 섬세한 맛이 있어서, 그것은 교회 문을 벗어나기도 전에 빠져나가 없어져 버린다. 처녀 시절의 이름과 함께 사라져 버리는 것이며, 아내라는 신분에 딸린 그 든든한 편안함과는 함께 있을 수 없는 것이다."], ["To love one’s own spouse, and to be loved by her, is the ordinary lot of man, and is a duty exacted under penalties.", "제 아내를 사랑하고 그 아내에게서 사랑받는 것은 사내에게 주어진 여느 몫이며, 벌을 걸고서 요구되는 의무다."], ["But to be allowed to love youth and beauty that is not one’s own—to know that one is loved by a soft being who still hangs cowering from the eye of the world as though her love were all but illicit—can it be that a man is made happy when a state of anticipation such as this is brought to a close?", "그러나 제 것이 아닌 젊음과 아름다움을 사랑하도록 허락받는 것—제 사랑이 거의 옳지 못한 것이기라도 한 듯 아직 세상의 눈길 앞에서 몸을 옹크리는 그 부드러운 사람에게 사랑받고 있음을 아는 것—이러한 기다림의 처지가 끝나는 때에 사내가 행복해진다고 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["No; when the husband walks back from the altar, he has already swallowed the choicest dainties of his banquet.", "아니다. 남편이 제단에서 걸어 나올 때 그는 이미 제 잔치에서 가장 좋은 진미를 삼켜 버린 것이다."], ["The beef and pudding of married life are then in store for him;—or perhaps only the bread and cheese.", "그때부터 그를 위해 마련되어 있는 것은 혼인 생활의 소고기와 푸딩이다—아니, 어쩌면 빵과 치즈뿐일지도 모른다."], ["Let him take care lest hardly a crust remain,—or perhaps not a crust.", "조심할 것이니, 빵 껍질 한 조각이 겨우 남아 있을 뿐일지도 모르고—아니, 어쩌면 껍질 한 조각조차 없을지도 모른다."]], [["But before we finish, let us go back for one moment to the dainties,—to the time before the beef and pudding were served,—while Lucy was still at the parsonage, and Lord Lufton still staying at Framley Court.", "그러나 끝을 맺기 전에, 잠깐만 그 진미로—소고기와 푸딩이 올라오기 전의 그때로 돌아가 보자. 루시가 아직 목사관에 있었고 러프턴 경이 아직 프램리 코트에 머물고 있던 때로 말이다."], ["He had come up one morning, as was now frequently his wont, and, after a few minutes’ conversation, Mrs. Robarts had left the room,—as not unfrequently on such occasions was her wont.", "그는 요즈음 자주 그러던 대로 어느 아침에 올라왔고, 몇 분 이야기를 나눈 뒤에 로바츠 부인은 방을 나갔다—그런 자리에서 그녀가 드물지 않게 하던 대로였다."], ["Lucy was working and continued her work, and Lord Lufton for a moment or two sat looking at her; then he got up abruptly, and standing before her, thus questioned her:—", "루시는 바늘일을 하고 있었고 그 일을 그대로 이어 갔다. 러프턴 경은 한두 순간 앉아서 그녀를 바라보다가, 문득 일어나 그녀 앞에 서서 이렇게 그녀에게 물었다—"]], [["“Lucy,”", "“루시,”"], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Well, what of Lucy now?", "“네, 루시가 어떻다는 말씀입니까?"], ["Any particular fault this morning?”", "오늘 아침에는 무슨 특별한 잘못이라도 있습니까?”"]], [["“Yes, a most particular fault.", "“그래요, 아주 특별한 잘못이 있어요."], ["When I asked you, here, in this room, on this very spot, whether it was possible that you should love me—why did you say that it was impossible?”", "내가 여기, 이 방에서, 바로 이 자리에서 그대가 나를 사랑하는 것이 있을 수 있는 일이냐고 물었을 때—왜 있을 수 없는 일이라고 말했지요?”"]], [["Lucy, instead of answering at the moment, looked down upon the carpet, to see if his memory were as good as hers.", "루시는 그 자리에서 대답하는 대신, 그의 기억이 제 기억만큼 좋은지 보려고 양탄자로 눈을 내리깔았다."], ["Yes; he was standing on the exact spot where he had stood before.", "그렇다. 그는 전에 서 있던 바로 그 자리에 서 있었다."], ["No spot in all the world was more frequently clear before her own eyes.", "온 세상의 어느 자리도 그녀의 눈앞에 그보다 더 자주 또렷이 떠오르는 곳은 없었다."]], [["“Do you remember that day, Lucy?”", "“그날을 기억해요, 루시?”"], ["he said again.", "그가 다시 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I remember it,”", "“예, 기억합니다,”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Why did you say it was impossible?”", "“왜 있을 수 없는 일이라고 말했지요?”"]], [["“Did I say impossible?”", "“제가 있을 수 없다고 말했습니까?”"]], [["She knew that she had said so.", "그녀는 자기가 그렇게 말했다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["She remembered how she had waited till he had gone, and that then, going to her own room, she had reproached herself with the cowardice of the falsehood.", "그가 가 버릴 때까지 기다렸던 것을, 그러고 나서 제 방으로 올라가 그 거짓말의 비겁함을 스스로 꾸짖었던 것을 그녀는 기억했다. 그때 그녀는 그에게 거짓말을 했다."], ["She had lied to him then; and now—how was she punished for it?", "그런데 이제—그 일로 그녀는 어떤 벌을 받고 있는가?"]], [["“Well, I suppose it was possible,”", "“글쎄요, 있을 수 있는 일이었나 봅니다,”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“But why did you say so when you knew it would make me so miserable?”", "“그러나 그것이 나를 그토록 참담하게 만들 줄 알면서 왜 그렇게 말했지요?”"]], [["“Miserable!", "“참담이라니요!"], ["nay, but you went away happy enough!", "아니지요, 그때는 아주 흐뭇하게 가셨습니다!"], ["I thought I had never seen you look better satisfied.”", "그렇게 만족스러운 얼굴을 하신 것을 저는 여태 본 적이 없다고 생각했는걸요.”"]], [["“Lucy!”", "“루시!”"]], [["“You had done your duty and had had such a lucky escape!", "“할 일을 다 하고 그렇게 운 좋게 빠져나오신 것이었지요!"], ["What astonishes me is that you should have ever come back again.", "저를 놀라게 하는 것은 어쨌든 다시 돌아오셨다는 점입니다."], ["But the pitcher may go to the well once too often, Lord Lufton.”", "하지만 물동이도 우물에 너무 자주 가면 깨지는 법이지요, 러프턴 경.”"]], [["“But will you tell me the truth now?”", "“그러나 이제는 사실을 말해 주겠지요?”"]], [["“What truth?”", "“어떤 사실을요?”"]], [["“That day, when I came to you,—did you love me at all then?”", "“그날, 내가 그대에게 왔을 때—그때 그대는 나를 조금이라도 사랑했나요?”"]], [["“We’ll let bygones be bygones, if you please.”", "“지나간 일은 지나간 일로 두시지요, 그래 주신다면요.”"]], [["“But I swear you shall tell me.", "“그러나 맹세하지만 그대는 나에게 말해야 해요."], ["It was such a cruel thing to answer me as you did, unless you meant it.", "진심이 아니었다면 그렇게 나에게 대답한 것은 참 잔인한 짓이었지요."], ["And yet you never saw me again till after my mother had been over for you to Mrs. Crawley’s.”", "그런데도 그대는 어머니가 크롤리 부인 집으로 그대를 데리러 건너간 뒤가 되도록 나를 다시 보지 않았어요.”"]], [["“It was absence that made me—care for you.”", "“떨어져 있는 것이 저를—경에게 마음을 두게 만든 것입니다.”"]], [["“Lucy, I swear I believe you loved me then.”", "“루시, 맹세하지만 나는 그때 그대가 나를 사랑했다고 믿어요.”"]], [["“Ludovic, some conjuror must have told you that.”", "“루도빅, 어느 요술쟁이가 그것을 일러 주었나 봅니다.”"]], [["She was standing as she spoke, and, laughing at him, she held up her hands and shook her head.", "그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 서 있었고, 그를 보고 웃으며 두 손을 들어 올리고 고개를 저었다."], ["But she was now in his power, and he had his revenge,—his revenge for her past falsehood and her present joke.", "그러나 이제 그녀는 그의 힘 안에 있었고, 그는 앙갚음을 했다—지난날의 그 거짓말과 지금의 그 농담에 대한 앙갚음을."], ["How could he be more happy when he was made happy by having her all his own, than he was now?", "그녀를 온전히 제 것으로 두어 행복해질 때, 그가 지금보다 어떻게 더 행복할 수 있겠는가?"]], [["And in these days there again came up that petition as to her riding—with very different result now than on that former occasion.", "그리고 이 무렵에 그녀가 말을 타는 일에 대한 그 청이 다시 나왔다—그러나 이번에는 그 지난날과는 아주 다른 결말이 되었다."], ["There were ever so many objections, then.", "그때에는 반대가 참으로 많았다."], ["There was no habit, and Lucy was—or said that she was—afraid; and then, what would Lady Lufton say?", "승마복이 없었고, 루시는 겁이 났다—아니면 겁이 난다고 말했다. 그리고 또 레이디 러프턴이 무슨 말을 하겠는가?"], ["But now Lady Lufton thought it would be quite right; only were they quite sure about the horse?", "그러나 이제 레이디 러프턴은 그것이 아주 마땅한 일이라고 생각했다. 다만 그 말에 대해서는 아주 확실한가?"], ["Was Ludovic certain that the horse had been ridden by a lady?", "그 말을 여인이 타 본 적이 있다는 것을 루도빅은 확실히 아는가?"], ["And Lady Meredith’s habits were dragged out as a matter of course, and one of them chipped and snipped and altered, without any compunction.", "그리고 레이디 메러디스의 승마복들이 당연한 일처럼 끌려 나왔고, 그중 한 벌이 아무런 거리낌 없이 잘리고 가위질되고 고쳐졌다."], ["And as for fear, there could be no bolder horsewoman than Lucy Robarts.", "그리고 겁이라면, 루시 로바츠보다 더 담대하게 말을 타는 여인은 있을 수 없었다."], ["It was quite clear to all Framley that riding was the very thing for her.", "말을 타는 것이 그녀에게 딱 맞는 일이라는 것이 온 프램리에 아주 뚜렷했다."], ["“But I never shall be happy, Ludovic, till you have got a horse properly suited for her,”", "“하지만 루도빅, 네가 루시에게 알맞은 말을 제대로 구해 줄 때까지는 나는 마음이 놓이지 않을 게다,”"], ["said Lady Lufton.", "레이디 러프턴이 말했다."]], [["And then, also, came the affair of her wedding garments, of her trousseau,—as to which I cannot boast that she showed capacity or steadiness at all equal to that of Lady Dumbello.", "그리고 또 그녀의 혼례 의복, 곧 그 혼례 옷가지의 일이 다가왔다. 그 일에서 그녀가 레이디 덤벨로만 한 역량이나 뚝심을 보였다고는 내가 자랑할 수 없다."], ["Lady Lufton, however, thought it a very serious matter; and as, in her opinion, Mrs.", "그러나 레이디 러프턴은 그것을 아주 중대한 일로 여겼다."], ["Robarts did not go about it with sufficient energy, she took the matter mainly into her own hands, striking Lucy dumb by her frowns and nods, deciding on everything herself, down to the very tags of the boot-ties.", "그리고 마님이 보기에 로바츠 부인이 넉넉한 기운으로 그 일에 나서지 않았으므로, 마님이 그 일을 대체로 제 손안에 넣어, 눈살과 고갯짓으로 루시의 입을 막아 버리고, 장화 끈의 끝쇠에 이르기까지 모든 것을 스스로 정했다."]], [["“My dear, you really must allow me to know what I am about;”", "“얘야, 내가 무엇을 하고 있는지는 내가 안다고 좀 믿어 주어야 해요.”"], ["and Lady Lufton patted her on the arm as she spoke.", "그러면서 레이디 러프턴은 말하는 동안 그녀의 팔을 다독였다."], ["“I did it all for Justinia, and she never had reason to regret a single thing that I bought.", "“저스티니아 것도 다 내가 했고, 그 아이는 내가 사 준 것 가운데 어느 하나도 후회할 이유가 없었어요."], ["If you’ll ask her, she’ll tell you so.”", "그 아이에게 물어보면 그렇다고 말해 줄 거예요.”"]], [["Lucy did not ask her future sister-in-law, seeing that she had no doubt whatever as to her future mother-in-law’s judgment on the articles in question.", "루시는 앞으로 시누이가 될 그분에게 묻지 않았으니, 문제가 된 그 물건들에 대한 앞으로 시어머니가 될 그분의 판단을 조금도 의심하지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Only the money!", "다만 그 돈이 문제였다!"], ["And what could she want with six dozen pocket-handkerchiefs all at once?", "그리고 여섯 다스나 되는 손수건을 한꺼번에 어디에 쓴다는 말인가?"], ["There was no question of Lord Lufton’s going out as governor-general to India!", "러프턴 경이 인도 총독으로 나갈 일이 있는 것도 아니었다!"], ["But twelve dozen pocket-handkerchiefs had not been too many for Griselda’s imagination.", "그러나 그리젤다의 상상으로는 열두 다스의 손수건도 너무 많은 것이 아니었다."]], [["And Lucy would sit alone in the drawing-room at Framley Court, filling her heart with thoughts of that evening when she had first sat there.", "그리고 루시는 프램리 코트의 응접실에 혼자 앉아, 처음으로 거기에 앉았던 그 저녁의 생각으로 제 마음을 가득 채우곤 했다."], ["She had then resolved, painfully, with inward tears, with groanings of her spirit, that she was wrongly placed in being in that company.", "그때 그녀는 괴로이, 마음속으로 눈물을 흘리며, 제 넋의 신음 소리를 내며, 그런 자리에 있는 것이 제 자리가 아니라고 마음을 정했던 것이다."], ["Griselda Grantly had been there, quite at her ease, petted by Lady Lufton, admired by Lord Lufton; while she had retired out of sight, sore at heart, because she felt herself to be no fit companion to those around her.", "그리젤다 그랜틀리는 아주 편안하게 그 자리에 있어 레이디 러프턴에게 아낌을 받고 러프턴 경에게 찬탄을 받았다. 그러는 동안 그녀는 눈에 띄지 않는 데로 물러나 마음이 아팠으니, 자기가 둘레의 사람들에게 어울리는 벗이 아니라고 느꼈기 때문이었다."], ["Then he had come to her, making matters almost worse by talking to her, bringing the tears into her eyes by his good-nature, but still wounding her by the feeling that she could not speak to him at her ease.", "그러다가 그가 그녀에게 왔고, 그녀에게 말을 걸어 사정을 거의 더 나쁘게 만들었고, 그 다정함으로 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 고이게 했으나, 그에게 편안히 말을 걸 수 없다는 느낌으로 여전히 그녀를 아프게 했다."]], [["But things were at a different pass with her now.", "그러나 이제 그녀의 사정은 다른 데에 이르러 있었다."], ["He had chosen her—her out of all the world, and brought her there to share with him his own home, his own honours, and all that he had to give.", "그가 그녀를 골랐다—온 세상 가운데에서 바로 그녀를 골라, 제 집과 제 영예와 제가 줄 수 있는 모든 것을 함께 나누도록 그녀를 거기로 데려온 것이다."], ["She was the apple of his eye, and the pride of his heart.", "그녀는 그의 눈동자였고 그의 마음의 자랑이었다."], ["And the stern mother, of whom she had stood so much in awe, who at first had passed her by as a thing not to be noticed, and had then sent out to her that she might be warned to keep herself aloof, now hardly knew in what way she might sufficiently show her love, regard, and solicitude.", "그리고 그녀가 그토록 어렵게 여겨 왔던 그 엄한 어머니, 처음에는 눈여겨볼 것도 못 되는 것으로 그녀를 지나쳐 버렸고 그러고 나서는 몸을 멀리 두라는 경고를 받으라고 그녀에게 사람을 보냈던 그 어머니가, 이제는 제 사랑과 아끼는 마음과 애틋한 살핌을 어떤 방식으로 보여야 넉넉할지 거의 알지 못했다."]], [["I must not say that Lucy was not proud in these moments—that her heart was not elated at these thoughts.", "이런 순간에 루시가 자랑스럽지 않았다고—이런 생각에 그녀의 마음이 들뜨지 않았다고 나는 말하지 못하겠다."], ["Success does beget pride, as failure begets shame.", "실패가 부끄러움을 낳듯이 성공은 자랑을 낳는다."], ["But her pride was of that sort which is in no way disgraceful to either man or woman, and was accompanied by pure true love, and a full resolution to do her duty in that state of life to which it had pleased her God to call her.", "그러나 그녀의 자랑은 남자에게든 여자에게든 조금도 부끄러울 것이 없는 종류의 것이었고, 순수하고 참된 사랑과, 또 하느님이 기꺼이 그녀를 부르신 그 삶의 자리에서 제 의무를 다하겠다는 온전한 결심을 함께 지니고 있었다."], ["She did rejoice greatly to think that she had been chosen, and not Griselda.", "그리젤다가 아니라 자기가 뽑혔다는 것을 생각하며 그녀는 참으로 크게 기뻐했다."], ["Was it possible that having loved she should not so rejoice, or that, rejoicing, she should not be proud of her love?", "사랑을 한 사람이 그렇게 기뻐하지 않는 것이, 또 기뻐하면서 제 사랑을 자랑하지 않는 것이 있을 수 있는 일이겠는가?"]], [["They spent the whole winter abroad, leaving the dowager Lady Lufton to her plans and preparations for their reception at Framley Court; and in the following spring they appeared in London, and there set up their staff.", "두 사람은 겨울을 온통 외국에서 보내며, 미망인 마님 레이디 러프턴에게는 프램리 코트에서 두 사람을 맞이할 계획과 준비를 맡겨 두었다. 그리고 이듬해 봄에 두 사람은 런던에 나타나 거기에 살림을 차렸다."], ["Lucy had some inner tremblings of the spirit, and quiverings about the heart, at thus beginning her duty before the great world, but she said little or nothing to her husband on the matter.", "루시는 그렇게 큰 세상 앞에서 제 소임을 시작하는 데에 넋이 떨리고 가슴이 흔들리는 것을 속으로 느꼈으나, 그 일을 두고 남편에게는 거의 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Other women had done as much before her time, and by courage had gone through with it.", "다른 여인들도 그녀보다 앞선 시절에 그만한 일을 해냈고, 용기로 그것을 이겨 냈다."], ["It would be dreadful enough, that position in her own house with lords and ladies bowing to her, and stiff members of Parliament for whom it would be necessary to make small talk; but, nevertheless, it was to be endured.", "제 집에서 귀족 남녀가 자기에게 허리를 굽히고, 딱딱한 하원 의원들에게는 가벼운 이야기를 지어내 주어야 하는 그 자리는 어지간히 끔찍한 일일 것이었다. 그럼에도 그것은 견뎌야 할 일이었다."], ["The time came, and she did endure it.", "그때가 왔고, 그녀는 견뎌 냈다."], ["The time came, and before the first six weeks were over she found that it was easy enough.", "그때가 왔고, 처음 여섯 주가 지나기도 전에 그녀는 그것이 어지간히 쉬운 일임을 알게 되었다."], ["The lords and ladies got into their proper places and talked to her about ordinary matters in a way that made no effort necessary, and the members of Parliament were hardly more stiff than the clergymen she had known in the neighbourhood of Framley.", "귀족 남녀는 제 알맞은 자리를 찾아들어 애쓸 것도 없는 방식으로 그녀에게 여느 일들을 이야기했고, 하원 의원들은 그녀가 프램리 근방에서 알고 지낸 성직자들보다 딱딱할 것이 별로 없었다."]], [["She had not been long in town before she met Lady Dumbello.", "그녀가 도시에 온 지 오래되지 않아 레이디 덤벨로를 만났다."], ["At this interview also she had to overcome some little inward emotion.", "이 만남에서도 그녀는 속으로 얼마쯤 감정을 눌러 이겨 내야 했다."], ["On the few occasions on which she had met Griselda Grantly at Framley they had not much progressed in friendship, and Lucy had felt that she had been despised by the rich beauty.", "프램리에서 그리젤다 그랜틀리를 만난 그 몇 번의 기회에 두 사람은 벗의 사이로 크게 나아간 것이 없었고, 루시는 그 부유한 미인에게 업신여김을 받았다고 느꼈다."], ["She also in her turn had disliked, if she had not despised, her rival.", "그녀 또한 그 맞수를 업신여기지는 않았다 해도 싫어하기는 했다."], ["But how would it be now?", "그러나 이제는 어떻게 될 것인가?"], ["Lady Dumbello could hardly despise her, and yet it did not seem possible that they should meet as friends.", "레이디 덤벨로가 그녀를 업신여기기는 어려울 것이었으나, 그러면서도 두 사람이 벗으로 만나는 일도 있을 수 없어 보였다."], ["They did meet, and Lucy came forward with a pretty eagerness to give her hand to Lady Lufton’s late favourite.", "두 사람이 만났고, 루시는 레이디 러프턴이 한때 아끼던 그 사람에게 손을 내밀려고 사랑스럽게 서둘러 앞으로 나섰다."], ["Lady Dumbello smiled slightly—the same old smile which had come across her face when they two had been first introduced in the Framley drawing-room; the same smile without the variation of a line,—took the offered hand, muttered a word or two, and then receded.", "레이디 덤벨로는 살짝 웃었다—프램리의 응접실에서 두 사람이 처음 인사를 나누었을 때 그 얼굴에 떠올랐던 바로 그 옛 웃음, 선 하나 달라진 것 없는 그 똑같은 웃음이었다. 그러고는 내민 손을 잡고, 한두 마디를 웅얼거리고, 뒤로 물러났다."], ["It was exactly as she had done before.", "그것은 전에 했던 것과 조금도 다름이 없었다."], ["She had never despised Lucy Robarts.", "그녀는 루시 로바츠를 업신여긴 적이 없었다."], ["She had accorded to the parson’s sister the amount of cordiality with which she usually received her acquaintance; and now she could do no more for the peer’s wife.", "그녀는 목사의 누이에게 자기가 알고 지내는 사람들을 여느 때 맞이하는 만큼의 다정함을 베풀어 준 것이었고, 이제 그 귀족의 아내에게도 그보다 더 해 줄 수 있는 것이 없었다."], ["Lady Dumbello and Lady Lufton have known each other ever since, and have occasionally visited at each other’s houses, but the intimacy between them has never gone beyond this.", "레이디 덤벨로와 레이디 러프턴은 그 뒤로 줄곧 서로 아는 사이로 지내며 이따금 서로의 집을 찾기도 했으나, 두 사람 사이의 친함은 결코 이보다 더 나아간 적이 없다."]], [["The dowager came up to town for about a month, and while there was contented to fill a second place.", "미망인 마님은 한 달쯤 도시로 올라왔고, 거기에 있는 동안에는 둘째 자리를 채우는 것으로 만족했다."], ["She had no desire to be the great lady in London.", "마님에게는 런던에서 큰 마님이 되려는 마음이 없었다."], ["But then came the trying period when they commenced their life together at Framley Court.", "그러나 그다음에 두 사람이 프램리 코트에서 함께 살림을 시작하는 그 힘든 시기가 왔다."], ["The elder lady formally renounced her place at the top of the table,—formally persisted in renouncing it though Lucy with tears implored her to resume it. She said also, with equal formality—repeating her determination over and over again to Mrs.", "나이 든 그 부인은 식탁 상석의 제 자리를 격식을 갖추어 내놓았다—루시가 눈물로 다시 앉으라고 애원했음에도 격식을 갖추어 끝까지 내놓았다."], ["Robarts with great energy—that she would in no respect detract by interference of her own from the authority of the proper mistress of the house; but, nevertheless, it is well known to every one at Framley that old Lady Lufton still reigns paramount in the parish.", "마님은 또한 똑같은 격식으로—로바츠 부인에게 큰 기운으로 제 결심을 몇 번이고 되풀이하여 말하며—이 집의 마땅한 안주인이 지닌 권위를 제 간섭으로 어떤 점에서도 깎아내리지 않겠다고 밝혔다. 그러나 그럼에도 늙은 레이디 러프턴이 여전히 그 교구에서 으뜸으로 군림하고 있다는 것은 프램리의 누구에게나 잘 알려진 일이다."]], [["“Yes, my dear; the big room looking into the little garden to the south was always the nursery; and if you ask my advice, it will still remain so.", "“그래요, 얘야. 남쪽 조그만 정원이 보이는 그 큰 방이 언제나 아기방이었어요."], ["But, of course, any room you please—”", "그리고 내 뜻을 묻는다면 앞으로도 그대로일 거예요. 물론, 마음에 드는 방이라면 어느 방이든—”"]], [["And the big room looking into the little garden to the south is still the nursery at Framley Court.", "그리고 남쪽 조그만 정원이 보이는 그 큰 방은 프램리 코트에서 여전히 아기방이다."]]];
